(window.KG_PARTS = window.KG_PARTS || []).push({"group":"epics_minor","chapters":{"ng:1":{"book":"naga_kumara_kaviyam","num":1,"title":"முதல் சருக்கம் 0-19 (1/2)","section":"முதல் சருக்கம்","theme":"The poet invokes the tirthankaras and Vani, apologises for his flawed verse and says his tale comes from the Jain Anga texts; he then places the story in Magadha under King Shrenika, who goes with army and queen to the hill where the lord has come and ends with a hymn praising the lord for conquering karma."},"ng:2":{"book":"naga_kumara_kaviyam","num":2,"title":"முதல் சருக்கம் 20-39 (2/2)","section":"முதல் சருக்கம்","theme":"The king of Magadha, having praised the lord and bowed to Gautama, asks for the Sri Panchami tale; Gautama begins it in Kanakapura, where King Jayandhara falls for a painted woman and marries Prithidevi, who fears the senior queen's envy, takes refuge in the lord of non-killing and is promised a virtuous son."},"ng:3":{"book":"naga_kumara_kaviyam","num":3,"title":"இரண்டாம் சருக்கம் 40-56 (1/2)","section":"இரண்டாம் சருக்கம்","theme":"The queen's temple visit and her dream of a bull and a young sun are read by a sage as the coming of a son who will rule, take up austerity and end his karma; the boy is born, the temple doors open at his foot and a serpent holds him safe in the pond, so he is named Naga Kumaran, and the chapter closes on a courtesan's daughters offered to him."},"ng:4":{"book":"naga_kumara_kaviyam","num":4,"title":"இரண்டாம் சருக்கம் 57-73 (2/2)","section":"இரண்டாம் சருக்கம்","theme":"The prince tames an elephant and a horse and masters learning; his stepmother-like rival's talk leads the king to order him away, and the prince answers by a show of riding, by winning at dice from kings and giving it back, so that the king builds him his own city of Alankarapuram."},"ng:5":{"book":"naga_kumara_kaviyam","num":5,"title":"மூன்றாம் சருக்கம் 74-87 (1/2)","section":"மூன்றாம் சருக்கம்","theme":"The story turns to Uttara Madurai: its king, told by a sage that his sons will serve another, gives them the crown and takes to the forest. The sons reach Pataliputra, marry there, meet Naga Kumara, and Vyala defeats an army sent against him. A minister ends Sridharan's anger, and the prince settles in Vyala's city."},"ng:6":{"book":"naga_kumara_kaviyam","num":6,"title":"மூன்றாம் சருக்கம் 88-101 (2/2)","section":"மூன்றாம் சருக்கம்","theme":"The prince beats the vina-skilled princess Tribhuvanarati, worships at a Jain shrine, frees a hunter's wife from a demon, gains gifts and the service of a yakshi, marries the daughter of Vanaraja and wins him a city, and the section ends on the king Somaprabha who loses his land and takes up the ascetic's yoga to end his deeds."},"ng:7":{"book":"naga_kumara_kaviyam","num":7,"title":"நான்காம் சருக்கம் 102-123 (1/2)","section":"நான்காம் சருக்கம்","theme":"The sage's prophecy to Jayavarma that his sons will serve another comes true when they leave home and join Naga Kumara, who then wins a bride, Gunavati, by relieving her father's city, and, going to the hill of the Vamana temple, sings a hymn that sets out the rewards of worship in rule, Indra's rank and release; a messenger from Kausambi then arrives with a letter and the names of the king's daughters."},"ng:8":{"book":"naga_kumara_kaviyam","num":8,"title":"நான்காம் சருக்கம் 124-144 (2/2)","section":"நான்காம் சருக்கம்","theme":"The hero goes on winning wives by his mantra-rite (Menaki of Ujjayini, Srimati of Madurai, Madanamanjari, Ilakkanai), kills the Vidyadhara Vayuvegan who killed the Prithivitilaka king and seized his five hundred daughters, and at the end meets a sage and asks him the cause of his fortune."},"ng:9":{"book":"naga_kumara_kaviyam","num":9,"title":"ஐந்தாம் சருக்கம் 145-157 (1/2)","section":"ஐந்தாம் சருக்கம்","theme":"The sage tells the hero his past birth as the merchant's son Nagadattan, who took the fast unto death and was reborn a god and then a king; the hero takes the same vow, goes to his father, and the father crowns him and leaves the world."},"ng:10":{"book":"naga_kumara_kaviyam","num":10,"title":"ஐந்தாம் சருக்கம் 158-170 (2/2)","section":"ஐந்தாம் சருக்கம்","theme":"The hero crowns his son and takes the monk's form, conquers his karma and reaches liberation after teaching as a kevali, his companions and wives follow, and the poet closes with a promise to those who hear the tale and a sermon that nothing helps the soul but virtue."},"nk:1":{"book":"neelakesi","num":1,"title":"கடவுள் வாழ்த்து 1-9","section":"கடவுள் வாழ்த்து","theme":"The invocation praises the omniscient one, free of birth and its three companions, whose word ends the suffering of souls, as the only god; the poet then makes his apology to the assembly (his work came in a dream and, like the moon, may carry a spot) and sums up the story to come: the demoness Neelakesi, unable to break a sage's penance, takes the great one's dharma, turns from violence and sets out to answer the schools, beginning with the view that accepts flesh."},"nk:2":{"book":"neelakesi","num":2,"title":"தருமவுரைச் சருக்கம் 10-33 (1/6)","section":"தருமவுரைச் சருக்கம்","theme":"The sermon sarukkam opens with the setting: the rich Panchala land, walked through its tinais and explained as needing no palai, its two kinds of good people (generous householders and those who strive to wear away their deeds), its four varnas, its king Samudrasaran and his capital Pundavarthanam; then, near the town of Palalaiyam, a burning ground of pyres, corpses and demons with a shrine of the demon-mother, where a sage of earth-like patience takes up his dwelling."},"nk:3":{"book":"neelakesi","num":3,"title":"தருமவுரைச் சருக்கம் 34-56 (2/6)","section":"தருமவுரைச் சருக்கம்","theme":"The sage Munichandra stops a goat sacrifice vowed for a child by arguing that the child came from karma, not the goddess, and gives the worshippers a clay substitute and the householder's five abstentions; the demons who lose their offerings bring the goddess Neelakesi, whose terrors fail against his unmoved penance."},"nk:4":{"book":"neelakesi","num":4,"title":"தருமவுரைச் சருக்கம் 57-79 (3/6)","section":"தருமவுரைச் சருக்கம்","theme":"Having failed to terrify the sage Munichandran, Neelakesi takes the form of the princess Kamalekai and tries to seduce him; he sees through her, answers her feigned fears and wiles, and when she weeps over her birth begins a sermon on birth, the four states of birth, the seven hells and the grading of beings by their number of senses."},"nk:5":{"book":"neelakesi","num":5,"title":"தருமவுரைச் சருக்கம் 80-103 (4/6)","section":"தருமவுரைச் சருக்கம்","theme":"Continuing the sermon on the four states of birth, the sage Munichandran describes the five-sensed animals, the five kinds of humans (of the land of enjoyment, of the islands, the divine, the beastlike, and those of the land of deeds, who alone can reach bliss by penance), the five classes of gods, and then, in short lines, the endless pains of hell, where bodies rejoin like water, old enemies fight and killing and flesh-eating are punished in fire; midway he hints to Neelakesi that her own kin are among the demon gods, and she agrees to keep listening."},"nk:6":{"book":"neelakesi","num":6,"title":"தருமவுரைச் சருக்கம் 104-126 (5/6)","section":"தருமவுரைச் சருக்கம்","theme":"The teacher's sermon to Neelakesi finishes the account of suffering in the four states of birth (beasts killed by nature, war, hunt, sacrifice and medicine; humans pained by their own passions or by harms from elements, beings and disease; even the gods coming to an end), then offers its cure: the threefold medicine of knowledge, clear faith and conduct, with the Jain means of knowledge, the limbs of right faith and the grades of conduct, ending in heaven and then release."},"nk:7":{"book":"neelakesi","num":7,"title":"தருமவுரைச் சருக்கம் 127-149 (6/6)","section":"தருமவுரைச் சருக்கம்","theme":"The sermon ends with the sage's teaching on the body: to the woman who came to tempt him he shows that beauty is in the seer, that adornment covers filth and leads to hell, and that one who lives in the burning ground cannot be moved. Neelakesi casts off her disguise, confesses, is forgiven, hymns the sage's endurance in the burning ground, and, asking for atonement, is told to spread the Jina's path, which sends her into the debates."},"nk:8":{"book":"neelakesi","num":8,"title":"குண்டலகேசி வாதச் சருக்கம் 150-170 (1/4)","section":"குண்டலகேசி வாதச் சருக்கம்","theme":"Neelakesi worships at the Jina's shrine, whose doors open on her oath of right faith, and sings eight hymns that praise the omniscient one by paradox and by his victory over deeds, death and birth; she then chooses the Buddha's teaching as the first mean path to crush, sees by clairvoyance Kundalakesi's unanswered challenge-branch at the gate of Kampili, flies there in a woman's form, learns from the gatekeeper why the gate is barred, and plucks up the branch."},"nk:9":{"book":"neelakesi","num":9,"title":"குண்டலகேசி வாதச் சருக்கம் 171-191 (2/4)","section":"குண்டலகேசி வாதச் சருக்கம்","theme":"Before the king of Kampili the debate is staked (honour to the winner, expulsion to the loser); Kundalakesi sets out her teacher, the Buddha who suffered only for others, and the five aggregates and momentary perishing, and Neelakesi attacks the teacher first: his greatness 'from the first', his self-ruin, sufferings that by the Buddhists' own law of karma must be his own sin, a compassion that ends at his release, and his eating of flesh."},"nk:10":{"book":"neelakesi","num":10,"title":"குண்டலகேசி வாதச் சருக்கம் 192-211 (3/4)","section":"குண்டலகேசி வாதச் சருக்கம்","theme":"The third part of the debate with Kundalakesi: Neelakesi mocks her opponent and her teacher, denies the Buddha omniscience and so scriptural authority, reduces the five aggregates to knowing and form, and argues through a run of images (the self-binding thief, the soldier who reports his own death, the garland thread, the scented pot) that momentariness and continuity without a soul cannot stand; Kundalakesi then turns to the Buddha's birth-stories of giving his eyes, body, wife and child, and Neelakesi answers that such gifts are madness, meet no need, and make the testing god ignorant."},"nk:11":{"book":"neelakesi","num":11,"title":"குண்டலகேசி வாதச் சருக்கம் 212-231 (4/4)","section":"குண்டலகேசி வாதச் சருக்கம்","theme":"Neelakesi ends her debate with Kundalakesi by mocking the Jataka tales of the Buddha's gifts of body and life as useless, harmful or false; the king rules the tales fictions, Kundalakesi names her teacher Arkkachandran and is driven out of the city, and Neelakesi refuses every reward, asks the king to keep dharma, and leaves through the sky for Ujjain."},"nk:12":{"book":"neelakesi","num":12,"title":"அர்க்கசந்திர வாதச் சருக்கம் 232-249 (1/2)","section":"அர்க்கசந்திர வாதச் சருக்கம்","theme":"Neelakesi comes to Ujjain, finds the Buddhist teacher Arkkachandra preaching in his monastery, hears his monks recite the Vinaya's rules for eating, shaving and grooming, asks whether that is dharma, and, challenged, opens a catalogue of what she calls the deceits of the Buddhist vows: the bodhisattva's gift of his wives, the denial of a soul alongside a being who wins release, and rules on partners and faults."},"nk:13":{"book":"neelakesi","num":13,"title":"அர்க்கசந்திர வாதச் சருக்கம் 250-266 (2/2)","section":"அர்க்கசந்திர வாதச் சருக்கம்","theme":"The close of the Arkkachandra debate: Neelakesi charges the Buddhist monk with preaching compassion while eating flesh, impermanence while building mansions, and suffering while grumbling over comforts; she refutes his reply that only the mind matters, shows his void release to be no goal at all, teaches him the Jain path of conduct, knowledge and vision, and wins him over, after which he names Mokkalan as his teacher."},"nk:14":{"book":"neelakesi","num":14,"title":"மொக்கல வாதச் சருக்கம் 267-291 (1/8)","section":"மொக்கல வாதச் சருக்கம்","theme":"Neelakesi, having converted Arkkachandra, flies to Padumapuram and opens the Mokkala debate by mocking the Buddhist monks' outward life (the dyed robe, the begging bowl, bodily cleanness, the crafts of a monastery), arguing that restraint lies in the senses and contemplation needs only food; Mokkalan, proud, disputes her victories, recites the Jain categories and calls them the Jina's own invention, and she answers that the Lord's word is truth."},"nk:15":{"book":"neelakesi","num":15,"title":"மொக்கல வாதச் சருக்கம் 292-316 (2/8)","section":"மொக்கல வாதச் சருக்கம்","theme":"In the second run of the debate with Mokkalan, Neelakesi defends the Jain existents of motion and rest, space and time, the soul of the body's size, its bondage, its passage between births and the persistence of things through change, against Buddhist objections she restates and answers; the run ends, in a shorter metre, with her attack on the Buddhist excuses for eating meat killed by others."},"nk:16":{"book":"neelakesi","num":16,"title":"மொக்கல வாதச் சருக்கம் 317-341 (3/8)","section":"மொக்கல வாதச் சருக்கம்","theme":"In the third part of the debate with the Buddhist elder Mokkalan, Neelakesi first defends Jain asceticism against Buddhist mockery (the hardship of the body, the unwashed ascetic, the renouncing death, the vow of silence), then argues that eating meat shares the guilt of killing, using the Buddhists' own worship of the bodhi tree, relics and images to show that acts at a distance bear fruit."},"nk:17":{"book":"neelakesi","num":17,"title":"மொக்கல வாதச் சருக்கம் 342-366 (4/8)","section":"மொக்கல வாதச் சருக்கம்","theme":"Neelakesi presses Mokkalan on eating flesh, answering his defence by intention with the seller who kills for the buyer's price and the teacher whose permission sustains the killing; worsted, he attacks Jain bodily mortification as profitless self-torment, and she answers with the Buddha's own ten perfections and ascetic rules and the bodhisattva's praised sufferings, then attacks the gift of the body as a killing of the lives within it, and begins to defend the Jain view that plants have life."},"nk:18":{"book":"neelakesi","num":18,"title":"மொக்கல வாதச் சருக்கம் 367-390 (5/8)","section":"மொக்கல வாதச் சருக்கம்","theme":"The middle of the Mokkala debate: Neelakesi argues against the Buddhist teacher Mokkalan that trees and the elements have souls; he turns to attack the Jain view that a thing both lasts and perishes, that substance stands with its qualities and that a thing can be called inexpressible; she answers each and turns his dilemma of identity and difference back on the Buddhist aggregates, and they dispute milk turning to curd and gram to mash as examples of transformation."},"nk:19":{"book":"neelakesi","num":19,"title":"மொக்கல வாதச் சருக்கம் 391-414 (6/8)","section":"மொக்கல வாதச் சருக்கம்","theme":"In the Mokkala debate Neelakesi and the Buddhist thera argue over whether a quality and its substance are one or different, how atoms join into matter, and the Jain claim that a thing exists and does not exist in different respects; he attacks it with the gooseberry, the elephant, the bull's horn, the hare's horn and right and left hands, and she answers by separating substance from state, real things from fictions, and naming from existence."},"nk:20":{"book":"neelakesi","num":20,"title":"மொக்கல வாதச் சருக்கம் 415-438 (7/8)","section":"மொக்கல வாதச் சருக்கம்","theme":"In the Mokkala debate Neelakesi and the Buddhist elder Mokkalan argue whether the soul that gives and does penance is the one rewarded in heaven, whether a thing can change yet persist, whether a quality leaves its substance (the flower's scent in a pot), what release is and why the freed do not return, and whether the Jina can know all things in all times at once and speak without karma."},"nk:21":{"book":"neelakesi","num":21,"title":"மொக்கல வாதச் சருக்கம் 439-462 (8/8)","section":"மொக்கல வாதச் சருக்கம்","theme":"The last run of the Mokkala debate: Mokkala attacks the Jain claim of omniscience (infinite objects, past and future things) and mocks the Jain marks of the Lord; Neelakesi answers, declares him defeated with Kundalakesi's old arguments, sings a hymn to the Jina's knowledge, marvels and feet, and explains release as a burnt seed that never sprouts; Mokkala accepts, disowns his teachers, sends her to his own teacher at Kapilapuram, honours her and leaves, and she sets out against the Buddha."},"nk:22":{"book":"neelakesi","num":22,"title":"புத்த வாதச் சருக்கம் 463-487 (1/8)","section":"புத்த வாதச் சருக்கம்","theme":"The Buddha debate opens: Neelakesi travels to the Buddha's city, finds flesh sold in its market and argues that the buyer and eater share the killing, mocking the Buddha's compassion for leaving flesh unforbidden; she then finds the Buddha teaching the three scriptures and the aggregates, charges his doctrines with contradiction, and makes him set out the aggregates, which he begins to expound down to the octads of form and the four elements."},"nk:23":{"book":"neelakesi","num":23,"title":"புத்த வாதச் சருக்கம் 488-512 (2/8)","section":"புத்த வாதச் சருக்கம்","theme":"The Buddha himself sets out the aggregates (feeling, cognition, activity, their momentary arising, the soul as a name for them, and his four truths), and Neelakesi vows to destroy his view and answers that inseparable parts need a bearer, that a thing is known only through its qualities, that the eight constituents of matter put water in fire, and that his accounts of cognition and will contradict themselves or regress into the void."},"nk:24":{"book":"neelakesi","num":24,"title":"புத்த வாதச் சருக்கம் 513-537 (3/8)","section":"புத்த வாதச் சருக்கம்","theme":"Neelakesi debates the Buddha himself: she charges that his store of consciousness is the soul under another name, argues that hearing is by contact and that a momentary mind could not understand a word or know anything, presses the difference between perception and consciousness, and then takes the Buddhist ten bad deeds in order, showing that the rules on coveting, anger, view, lying, slander and harsh or gentle speech do not hold as stated."},"nk:25":{"book":"neelakesi","num":25,"title":"புத்த வாதச் சருக்கம் 538-562 (4/8)","section":"புத்த வாதச் சருக்கம்","theme":"Neelakesi, debating the Buddha in his own city, attacks his account of karma and his first truth: she argues that the rule making killing and theft depend on knowledge and intention would excuse a man who kills, robs or abducts by mistake, while intent without a deed still binds; then she argues that 'all is suffering', 'all is impure' and 'nothing stays' each undo the four noble truths and every reason for conduct."},"nk:26":{"book":"neelakesi","num":26,"title":"புத்த வாதச் சருக்கம் 563-586 (5/8)","section":"புத்த வாதச் சருக்கம்","theme":"In the fifth part of the debate with the Buddha, Neelakesi mocks his former-birth gifts (the rain for worms, the body split open, the wife and children given away), argues that denying the soul refutes itself, and then answers his account of karma without a doer, the seed-and-sprout continuity and the lac-stained pomegranate seed, by pressing that nothing beyond the five aggregates carries a deed to its fruit."},"nk:27":{"book":"neelakesi","num":27,"title":"புத்த வாதச் சருக்கம் 587-610 (6/8)","section":"புத்த வாதச் சருக்கம்","theme":"In the Buddha debate Neelakesi argues against the Buddha that karma cannot lie in the mind alone, since wherever mind is action is, and dismisses his seed-and-flower analogy; the Buddha restates that a deed's power passes hidden through the continuum to its fruit though the doer perishes, and Neelakesi answers that a continuum cannot cross from a dead man to a god, since every example of transmission needs its cause present or an unbroken link."},"nk:28":{"book":"neelakesi","num":28,"title":"புத்த வாதச் சருக்கம் 611-634 (7/8)","section":"புத்த வாதச் சருக்கம்","theme":"In the Buddha debate Neelakesi attacks the Buddhist account of continuity: she rejects the flame, wind and stream as examples of a series, presses the Buddha on how a deed bears fruit across moments, dismantles his doctrine of the trace (vasana) carried like flower scent into a pot, argues that total perishing would make release automatic, and reduces his balance-beam image of simultaneous arising and perishing to nothing."},"nk:29":{"book":"neelakesi","num":29,"title":"புத்த வாதச் சருக்கம் 635-658 (8/8)","section":"புத்த வாதச் சருக்கம்","theme":"The end of the debate with the Buddha: Neelakesi takes apart his balance-beam image of cause and effect and the claim that wholes are only names, asks who reaps a deed's fruit, meets his answer that the reaper is neither the doer nor another but another in the doer's line, like paddy and cane, and argues that what is not the same is other, that his own gifts in former births would then be fruitless, and that the doers reap; he falls silent, assents to the Jain account of things as perishing in one respect and not in another, and she leaves saying that any being who understands the Lord's scripture reaches release."},"nk:30":{"book":"neelakesi","num":30,"title":"ஆசீவக வாதச் சருக்கம் 659-682 (1/3)","section":"ஆசீவக வாதச் சருக்கம்","theme":"Neelakesi, leaving Kapilapuram after defeating the Buddha, sings a hymn claiming that all faultless scripture and every true god belong to the Jina and that he knows without reckoning; she reaches the Ajivikas at Samadandam, where Puranan names Makkali as their silent omniscient Lord, the nine Kathir books as scripture, and five categories of atom (earth, water, fire, wind and soul) that never enter one another; she undertakes to show this contrary to perception and reason, and begins by arguing that a Lord who never spoke left no scripture and that silence cannot make a Lord."},"nk:31":{"book":"neelakesi","num":31,"title":"ஆசீவக வாதச் சருக்கம் 683-706 (2/3)","section":"ஆசீவக வாதச் சருக்கம்","theme":"The middle of the Ajivika debate: Neelakesi argues that a Lord who knows all and keeps silent is lesser than any speaker, that silence proves nothing and a moving body betrays desire, and that a rainbow is a caused thing; she then attacks Puranan's five atom-kinds (earth alone holding the sensible qualities, darkness outside the five, kinds and substances both one and many), his god's freedom from disease, his denial of merit in giving and of sin in killing, and the identity of quality and substance (the ripening date, the plate made a pot, the classes of words), and opens her attack on the doctrine that every outcome is fixed with the example of an infant girl."},"nk:32":{"book":"neelakesi","num":32,"title":"ஆசீவக வாதச் சருக்கம் 707-729 (3/3)","section":"ஆசீவக வாதச் சருக்கம்","theme":"Neelakesi ends her debate with the Ajivika Puranan: she refutes the fixed order (niyati) by the parts a body gains after birth and by the regress of daughters in the womb, attacks the Ajivika account of souls (their colours, numbers, extent and form) and the natural motions of fire, soul, wind and water, then offers him the cure of births as a physician would; he asks to be taught, she shows by a bent finger that a thing both is and is not, he accepts, and she sets him to ascetic penance and gives him the nine categories."},"nk:33":{"book":"neelakesi","num":33,"title":"சாங்கிய வாதச் சருக்கம் 730-748 (1/3)","section":"சாங்கிய வாதச் சருக்கம்","theme":"Neelakesi goes to Hastinapura to the Sankhya teacher Parasaran, draws out his twenty-five principles and his doctrine of a soul that does nothing yet experiences, then attacks it: a non-acting soul could not teach, speak, enjoy, be bound or be carried between births, and his defence of a second, acting soul destroys his own thesis."},"nk:34":{"book":"neelakesi","num":34,"title":"சாங்கிய வாதச் சருக்கம் 749-765 (2/3)","section":"சாங்கிய வாதச் சருக்கம்","theme":"In the middle of her debate with the Sankhya teacher Neelakesi attacks his chain of principles: she asks whether mahat comes from prakriti, mocks the count of eleven organs and the organs of action, sets out the Jain view that the four elements are built of atoms and that space is one formless thing reaching beyond the world, denies that sound belongs to space, and argues that an unchanging soul and an unknowing nature can neither join anew nor produce anything."},"nk:35":{"book":"neelakesi","num":35,"title":"சாங்கிய வாதச் சருக்கம் 766-782 (3/3)","section":"சாங்கிய வாதச் சருக்கம்","theme":"The close of the Sankhya debate: Neelakesi refutes a Lord who is all things and creator, then the pre-existence of effects and the twenty-five principles, and Parasaran concedes and receives right vision."},"nk:36":{"book":"neelakesi","num":36,"title":"வைசேடிக வாதச் சருக்கம் 783-803 (1/2)","section":"வைசேடிக வாதச் சருக்கம்","theme":"Neelakesi reaches Ulokan, the follower of Kanada, who sets out the Vaisheshika's six categories and nine substances; she answers that the four elements are one stuff changing into each other, that sound does not prove ether, that direction is space named relative to an observer, that mind and time are not single substances, and that qualities cannot be separate from their bearer nor joined to it by inherence, offering at the end the Jain triad of arising, perishing and lasting."},"nk:37":{"book":"neelakesi","num":37,"title":"வைசேடிக வாதச் சருக்கம் 804-823 (2/2)","section":"வைசேடிக வாதச் சருக்கம்","theme":"Neelakesi ends the debate with the Vaisheshika teacher Ulokan: she holds quality and action to be both one with and other than their bearer, argues that karma, merit and guilt could not bind or be shed if they were separate from the soul, takes apart generality, particularity and inherence, wins him over by the method of standpoints, and leaves him to worship at the Lord's shrine."},"nk:38":{"book":"neelakesi","num":38,"title":"வேத வாதச் சருக்கம் 824-853","section":"வேத வாதச் சருக்கம்","theme":"In the Vedic debate Neelakesi goes to the Brahmin Bhutikan's Veda school at Kakandi, answers his taunt that a Sudra woman cannot judge the Veda, argues by inference that the Veda was made and contradicts itself on killing and birth, and attacks sacrifice by holding that each deed falls on its doer and not on the gods or ancestors it is offered to; the text breaks at 845-851 and resumes with Bhutikan's concession."},"nk:39":{"book":"neelakesi","num":39,"title":"பூத வாதச் சருக்கம் 854-874 (1/2)","section":"பூத வாதச் சருக்கம்","theme":"The materialist Pisasakan rises after the Vedic debate and, before King Madanasittan's assembly, holds that the five elements combined yield the senses, awareness and pleasure as liquor yields intoxication, and that nothing survives their parting; Neelakesi restates his view and answers that he has no omniscient teacher or scripture, that his own debating presupposes fruit and a debater, that awareness, moral traits, the unity of the senses, memory and a newborn's instinct cannot be effects of the elements."},"nk:40":{"book":"neelakesi","num":40,"title":"பூத வாதச் சருக்கம் 875-894 (2/2)","section":"பூத வாதச் சருக்கம்","theme":"The end of the debate with the materialist Pisasikan: Neelakesi argues that differences of karma, missing senses, memory, feelings and omens cannot come from the elements and that a soul exists; then, to the man who trusts only what he sees, she shows part of her demon form, comforts him as a mother, and he takes the Jain dharma before a marvelling court."},"so:1":{"book":"soolamani","num":1,"title":"பாயிரம் 1-6","section":"பாயிரம்","theme":"The prologue praises the god who conquered the sum of his deeds and frees those who bow at his feet, announces the story of the red-eyed tall one who tore the lion, and asks the good to take the poet's lapses as the moon's spot is taken, naming his patron of the assembly and the old purana whose account he will follow."},"so:2":{"book":"soolamani","num":2,"title":"நாட்டுச் சருக்கம் 7-21 (1/2)","section":"நாட்டுச் சருக்கம்","theme":"The first verses of the Naattu sarukkam describe the land as a place where hills, farms, pastures and shore each give their own sounds and flowers: a country of festival, bees and honey, whose women's beauty and perfumes reach even ascetics."},"so:3":{"book":"soolamani","num":3,"title":"நாட்டுச் சருக்கம் 22-35 (2/2)","section":"நாட்டுச் சருக்கம்","theme":"The country description of the Soolamani's opening section continues with birds on sand banks, lotus ponds and swans, hill-flanks with sleeping elephants, buffaloes and crocodiles, the stored grain and pods of the cropland, the sea-shore's pearls and toddy, the flowers in the women's hair, the music of drums and flutes, and ends by calling the fragrant land like heaven."},"so:4":{"book":"soolamani","num":4,"title":"நகரச் சருக்கம் 36-52 (1/2)","section":"நகரச் சருக்கம்","theme":"The poet's praise of the city of Podanam, the capital of the Surama country, as a serpent's-world rival, a lotus on the earth, a heaven come down, a city of drums, anklets, bees and plenty, ending with the introduction of its king Payapati, gentle to those who approach and hot to foes."},"so:5":{"book":"soolamani","num":5,"title":"நகரச் சருக்கம் 53-69 (2/2)","section":"நகரச் சருக்கம்","theme":"The city's king is praised for a reign in which only Kama's arrows are abroad, who takes his sixth share only as grace and keeps learned counsellors; the verses then turn to his two chief queens, Mikapathi and her sister Sasi, who bind his mind until the work of ruling is set aside and the world sleeps under his one umbrella."},"so:6":{"book":"soolamani","num":6,"title":"குமாரகாலச் சருக்கம் 70-94 (1/2)","section":"குமாரகாலச் சருக்கம்","theme":"The two princes Vijayan and Tivittan are born, grow into youth and set the city's women aflame, and the king rises, is blessed and goes in state to the assembly hall."},"so:7":{"book":"soolamani","num":7,"title":"குமாரகாலச் சருக்கம் 95-118 (2/2)","section":"குமாரகாலச் சருக்கம்","theme":"In the court of the lion throne the poets praise the king, an astrologer is admitted and reads a night's dream as the coming of a Vidyadhara king's daughter and a messenger with a letter, the court affirms it and names Tivittan, and in the council hall the ministers speak of his lordship of the discus and plan a guarded garden for the day."},"so:8":{"book":"soolamani","num":8,"title":"இரதநூபுரச் சருக்கம் 119-142 (1/5)","section":"இரதநூபுரச் சருக்கம்","theme":"The sarukkam turns from the kings of the plain to the Vidyadhara ridge: a hill of gods, groves and lovers, then its golden city Ratanupura-Chakravalam and its king, who has every virtue and learning but lacks grace toward the lowly."},"so:9":{"book":"soolamani","num":9,"title":"இரதநூபுரச் சருக்கம் 143-166 (2/5)","section":"இரதநூபுரச் சருக்கம்","theme":"The Vidyadhara king's long reign gives him a queen, a son Arukkakirti and a daughter Sayampavai, whose beauty is praised verse by verse until kings bow to her; then, as she comes of age, spring opens over the grove with a few sayings on the wealth of the worthless."},"so:10":{"book":"soolamani","num":10,"title":"இரதநூபுரச் சருக்கம் 167-190 (3/5)","section":"இரதநூபுரச் சருக்கம்","theme":"The king rides out to the spring groves, which flatter his women and are drawn as copies of their bodies, then goes to the shrine of the Arhat where he and the descending charanas sing hymns of a god who needs no praise and is loved all the same."},"so:11":{"book":"soolamani","num":11,"title":"இரதநூபுரச் சருக்கம் 191-214 (4/5)","section":"இரதநூபுரச் சருக்கம்","theme":"Sages teach the Vidyadhara king that deeds sprung from a lack of true knowledge bind beings through births until they take refuge at the Lord's feet and hold true seeing, knowledge and conduct, whose end is a state of unfailing bliss; the king goes home, and his young daughter takes the Chakravalam vow and worships the Lord on the hill."},"so:12":{"book":"soolamani","num":12,"title":"இரதநூபுரச் சருக்கம் 215-238 (5/5)","section":"இரதநூபுரச் சருக்கம்","theme":"In the Ratanupura palace the princess hymns the god and brings his flower remnant to her father, who praises her in images of mango, moon and Ganga, then is troubled over whom to give her to and, after weighing love against the treatise, sends for the ministers learned in policy."},"so:13":{"book":"soolamani","num":13,"title":"மந்திரசாலைச் சருக்கம் 239-262 (1/8)","section":"மந்திரசாலைச் சருக்கம்","theme":"The king calls his ministers into the sealed council hall and tells them that kings rule through counsel and the learned, then asks who could match his daughter's worth; the ministers bow, and Sachchutan and others begin to answer in turn with images of gem and sun and sandal."},"so:14":{"book":"soolamani","num":14,"title":"மந்திரசாலைச் சருக்கம் 263-286 (2/8)","section":"மந்திரசாலைச் சருக்கம்","theme":"In the council hall a counsellor presses the king with a theory of kingship: the king's heat or grace is the world's, the king and learning are its eyes, and austerity and rule are two hard burdens. Then, asking leave to speak of a maiden's lord, he turns to a long praise of the Vidyadhara realm as a heaven on the ridge."},"so:15":{"book":"soolamani","num":15,"title":"மந்திரசாலைச் சருக்கம் 287-310 (3/8)","section":"மந்திரசாலைச் சருக்கம்","theme":"The sarukkam's council scene continues. The minister Sachchutan first shows the Vidyadhara king the pleasure-city of Asvagriva, then names Asvagriva's parents, brothers and counsellors, his strength, the tribute he took and gave up, and urges that the girl be given to him; Bhavachutan then rises to speak and begins to name a fault."},"so:16":{"book":"soolamani","num":16,"title":"மந்திரசாலைச் சருக்கம் 311-334 (4/8)","section":"மந்திரசாலைச் சருக்கம்","theme":"In the council hall the counsellors name the princes who might win the maiden, and the sarukkam closes on Pavachchuthan's judgment, the gifts of Suthasagaran and a warning about the danger of the match."},"so:17":{"book":"soolamani","num":17,"title":"மந்திரசாலைச் சருக்கம் 335-358 (5/8)","section":"மந்திரசாலைச் சருக்கம்","theme":"The council of the Vidyadhara king: a first counsellor praises a line of lords and women, then Sumantiri speaks, naming Achuvakandan, a youth firm in austerity and the sage's prophecy that he will reach heaven and then liberation, and ends by saying fate may overturn all plans."},"so:18":{"book":"soolamani","num":18,"title":"மந்திரசாலைச் சருக்கம் 359-382 (6/8)","section":"மந்திரசாலைச் சருக்கம்","theme":"After the council settles that the king should ask about his daughter, he withdraws through a day of palace pool-play, offerings, time-keeping and music, then walks with his guards to the astrologer's mansion, where the astrologer begins to take up the girl's matter."},"so:19":{"book":"soolamani","num":19,"title":"மந்திரசாலைச் சருக்கம் 383-406 (7/8)","section":"மந்திரசாலைச் சருக்கம்","theme":"A teacher tells the king the woman's fortune by reciting the ancient purana: the middle world, the ages, the first lord and the wheel-king Bharata, then the lord's foretelling to Bharata of the line of great persons down to the Kesava Marici, who is the younger prince of the court."},"so:20":{"book":"soolamani","num":20,"title":"மந்திரசாலைச் சருக்கம் 407-430 (8/8)","section":"மந்திரசாலைச் சருக்கம்","theme":"The Vidyadhara king's astrologer foretells that Tivittan will split a lion and that the king will gain both rows of the ridge by giving his daughter; the king tells his queen, the queen answers, the ministers choose Marusi as envoy, and Marusi flies to the grove outside Tivittan's city."},"so:21":{"book":"soolamani","num":21,"title":"தூதுவிடு சருக்கம் 431-454 (1/6)","section":"தூதுவிடு சருக்கம்","theme":"Wandering through a flowering grove, a garlanded man reaches an asoka and a gem slab, where its keeper explains a priest's foretelling that a prince will come, and the king then sends four women, drawn in their ornaments and killing glances, to attend him."},"so:22":{"book":"soolamani","num":22,"title":"தூதுவிடு சருக்கம் 455-478 (2/6)","section":"தூதுவிடு சருக்கம்","theme":"In the envoy sarukkam the young women of the city plague the men with the arts of desire, then turn to serve the Vidyadhara guest, and at the king's bidding Vijayan and Tivittan ride out in procession while the women swoon on the terraces."},"so:23":{"book":"soolamani","num":23,"title":"தூதுவிடு சருக்கம் 479-502 (3/6)","section":"தூதுவிடு சருக்கம்","theme":"The princes ride through the city while its women, shedding shame, pour out their longing, and in the grove under the Ashoka the Vidyadhara envoy Maruci rises, bows, studies the two princes and begins his address."},"so:24":{"book":"soolamani","num":24,"title":"தூதுவிடு சருக்கம் 503-526 (4/6)","section":"தூதுவிடு சருக்கம்","theme":"Tivittan and Vijayan ride with the Vidyadhara envoy into Podanam; in the hall the envoy's letter offering a daughter to the younger prince is read, and when the king sits silent the envoy argues angrily that men of the earth cannot win what is above them, until the king answers that he cannot frame a reply and takes it all as a dream."},"so:25":{"book":"soolamani","num":25,"title":"தூதுவிடு சருக்கம் 527-549 (5/6)","section":"தூதுவிடு சருக்கம்","theme":"Prajapati meets the envoy's claim with the saying that earlier deeds alone decide a man's wealth; Marusi, abashed, answers the doubt that Vidyadharas and men do not marry by saying they are all men, names himself, and tells the story of Nami of Kasi, who sang to a yogi, was given the Vidyadhara lore and the silver mountain by Dharanan, and from whom the Vidyadhara king descends."},"so:26":{"book":"soolamani","num":26,"title":"தூதுவிடு சருக்கம் 550-572 (6/6)","section":"தூதுவிடு சருக்கம்","theme":"At the court of Podanam, the priest answers the Vidyadhara envoy's recital of his king's clan with the Podanam line, telling of the first lord's grace, of the ancestor who gave rule to his son and stood in meditation until anthills and creepers grew round him, and of the kevala he gained; then the king praises Marusi, names the envoy as the king's mouth, defers to the Vidyadhara king's wish, honours him and sends him back."},"so:27":{"book":"soolamani","num":27,"title":"சீயவதைச் சருக்கம் 573-596 (1/11)","section":"சீயவதைச் சருக்கம்","theme":"Marusi returns to the Vidyadhara king, who reads the success of the embassy in his face, and reports in order what he saw in Prajapati's grove, palace and court, how the match was settled and what manner of king the host is."},"so:28":{"book":"soolamani","num":28,"title":"சீயவதைச் சருக்கம் 597-619 (2/11)","section":"சீயவதைச் சருக்கம்","theme":"The Vidyadhara envoy praises the princes and the match before the Podanam king, who rewards him, sends him off and, on his counsellors' word of the month-end prophecy, resolves to send a learned man in return; the poet then turns to the rival king Acuvakanthan, wholly given to pleasure."},"so:29":{"book":"soolamani","num":29,"title":"சீயவதைச் சருக்கம் 620-642 (3/11)","section":"சீயவதைச் சருக்கம்","theme":"The poet turns to the rival king Acuvakanthan, whose temper and pleasures are named; his astrologer Satavintu warns him of pride and neglect and of the younger Podanam prince, and the king, boasting of his own strength, waves the warning away and tells his counsellors to speak."},"so:30":{"book":"soolamani","num":30,"title":"சீயவதைச் சருக்கம் 643-665 (4/11)","section":"சீயவதைச் சருக்கம்","theme":"Acuvakanthan's frightened counsellors urge that a small foe be put down before he grows, Arimanju counsels testing him first by a tribute demand, and the four envoys of the discus-bearer fly to Podanam, are admitted by the gatekeeper and deliver a letter demanding wealth, a thousand dancing women and the marks of a king."},"so:31":{"book":"soolamani","num":31,"title":"சீயவதைச் சருக்கம் 666-688 (5/11)","section":"சீயவதைச் சருக்கம்","theme":"Podanam's king, hearing Asvagriva's demand for tribute, reasons from fate and precedent to submit and gives the envoys women and gifts, until Tivittan learns of it, flares at the word tribute and answers in scorn."},"so:32":{"book":"soolamani","num":32,"title":"சீயவதைச் சருக்கம் 689-711 (6/11)","section":"சீயவதைச் சருக்கம்","theme":"Acuvakanthan's envoys return from the princes, and his minister conjures a magic lion to rouse and ruin the prince; the envoys then taunt Tivittan with the lion, and he vows to split its mouth and goes out alone with Vijayan."},"so:33":{"book":"soolamani","num":33,"title":"சீயவதைச் சருக்கம் 712-734 (7/11)","section":"சீயவதைச் சருக்கம்","theme":"On the mountain Tivittan rouses the lion that has hidden in its cave, tears it apart, and tells Vijayan the harm is gone; then Vijayan, pleased, names for his brother the crystal, lotus pools, elephants and gems that the mountain's beauty offers."},"so:34":{"book":"soolamani","num":34,"title":"சீயவதைச் சருக்கம் 735-757 (8/11)","section":"சீயவதைச் சருக்கம்","theme":"A stretch of sustained description of the mountain where the lion lives: first its perils (cloud, tangle, serpents, bears), then its gem rocks and a long catalogue of flowering trees, ending in lovers' comparisons. No event occurs; it prepares the lion hunt."},"so:35":{"book":"soolamani","num":35,"title":"சீயவதைச் சருக்கம் 758-780 (9/11)","section":"சீயவதைச் சருக்கம்","theme":"Vijayan shows Tivittan the gods' hill and its play, points to the blood of the lion Tivittan has killed and praises the prince's duty to risk his body for the world's creatures and the form of fame that outlasts it, and the two then enter a scorching desert."},"so:36":{"book":"soolamani","num":36,"title":"சீயவதைச் சருக்கம் 781-803 (10/11)","section":"சீயவதைச் சருக்கம்","theme":"On the road to the lion's forest, Vijayan names the wilderness and the guards of the forest, then shows Tivittan the flowering woods, hill-fields and marutham country one by one, in a long series of landscape verses with no action."},"so:37":{"book":"soolamani","num":37,"title":"சீயவதைச் சருக்கம் 804-826 (11/11)","section":"சீயவதைச் சருக்கம்","theme":"The two princes are shown the country and the city on the road, ride into Podanam by night amid the king's army, bow before the king who embraces them, and are asked by him about the forest dust in their hair, which Vijayan answers with a lion's ravage and Tivittan joins in."},"so:38":{"book":"soolamani","num":38,"title":"கல்யாணச் சருக்கம் 827-850 (1/13)","section":"கல்யாணச் சருக்கம்","theme":"After the lion's death the Vidyadhara king resolves to give his daughter to Tivittan, names the eight lords who guard his city, and raises a host of elephants, horses, chariots and foot to escort the bride, as a vimana of gems is conjured at the gate."},"so:39":{"book":"soolamani","num":39,"title":"கல்யாணச் சருக்கம் 851-874 (2/13)","section":"கல்யாணச் சருக்கம்","theme":"The wedding hall is dressed in gems, the lord of the city calls his daughter Sayambavai, who goes through the palace to her mother, is adorned, and sets out on the vimana with her guard, her goods and the king's army."},"so:40":{"book":"soolamani","num":40,"title":"கல்யாணச் சருக்கம் 875-898 (3/13)","section":"கல்யாணச் சருக்கம்","theme":"The guide shows the maiden the hills, rivers, elephants and women of the Vidyadhara host as it crosses the sky, after which the party lands at Podanam, and the poet lays out the camp, its elephants, chariots, palaces and the allied kings around the royal banner."},"so:41":{"book":"soolamani","num":41,"title":"கல்யாணச் சருக்கம் 899-922 (4/13)","section":"கல்யாணச் சருக்கம்","theme":"The maiden-city is built and the Vidyadhara maiden enters it; Maruci reports to the king of Suramai, who orders the city dressed and, on his counsellors' word that the Vidyadhara lord is an equal, has elephants, chariots, horses and soldiers made ready while the prince orders gifts of cloth, jewels, drink and food sent to the Vidyadhara city."},"so:42":{"book":"soolamani","num":42,"title":"கல்யாணச் சருக்கம் 923-946 (5/13)","section":"கல்யாணச் சருக்கம்","theme":"The king has the wedding city perfumed and strewn, calls his princes and the elephant Arasuva, has it dressed in gold and gems, and rides out with the kings in a procession of drums, flags, dust and musth that draws the women's eyes, while Maruci tells the Vidyadhara king, whose host rises to meet him."},"so:43":{"book":"soolamani","num":43,"title":"கல்யாணச் சருக்கம் 947-969 (6/13)","section":"கல்யாணச் சருக்கம்","theme":"The Vidyadhara host comes down from the sky to meet the Podanam king's host, and the two armies mingle like rivers and seas; the kings embrace and bow, Arkakirti is raised onto the king's elephant, and the party enters the city to the Chitrakuta hall, where the king begins to speak."},"so:44":{"book":"soolamani","num":44,"title":"கல்யாணச் சருக்கம் 970-992 (7/13)","section":"கல்யாணச் சருக்கம்","theme":"Sayambavai, watching from her mansion, sees the princes ride in and is taken by Tivittan; the Vidyadhara king and Prajapati exchange greetings and she is given to Prajapati, who declares the match the fruit of Tivittan's austerity, after which she laments her love on the terrace."},"so:45":{"book":"soolamani","num":45,"title":"கல்யாணச் சருக்கம் 993-1015 (8/13)","section":"கல்யாணச் சருக்கம்","theme":"The queen's party is sent to see the bride, and Madhavasenai, who studies the girl in the bride's palace, paints her portrait from memory, wins the queen's gift and praise, and carries it to the dark-hued youth as a rare jewel."},"so:46":{"book":"soolamani","num":46,"title":"கல்யாணச் சருக்கம் 1016-1038 (9/13)","section":"கல்யாணச் சருக்கம்","theme":"A woman shows the young lord a painting of the Vidyadhara king's daughter, Kaman shoots him with love and he burns for the picture, then a long evening scene is drawn and the maiden goes to worship at the Jina's temple."},"so:47":{"book":"soolamani","num":47,"title":"கல்யாணச் சருக்கம் 1039-1061 (10/13)","section":"கல்யாணச் சருக்கம்","theme":"On the eve of her wedding the maiden prays at the Jina's feet, goes to her terrace and sees the stars, and her friends speak of love and the drum of tomorrow; then the night passes over the city, from the setting moon to the sleep of the revellers, spirits and birds."},"so:48":{"book":"soolamani","num":48,"title":"கல்யாணச் சருக்கம் 1062-1084 (11/13)","section":"கல்யாணச் சருக்கம்","theme":"Night ends and the sun rises on the wedding morning, the rites are declared complete, and the royal city and the Vidyadhara guests fill the streets with drums, flags, goods and feasting."},"so:49":{"book":"soolamani","num":49,"title":"கல்யாணச் சருக்கம் 1085-1107 (12/13)","section":"கல்யாணச் சருக்கம்","theme":"The Vidyadhara wedding: the groom and bride are bathed, the priest lays the hall and tends the fire, the bride is given and led round the fire, the priests are paid and the onlookers go away amazed or to the forest."},"so:50":{"book":"soolamani","num":50,"title":"கல்யாணச் சருக்கம் 1108-1130 (13/13)","section":"கல்யாணச் சருக்கம்","theme":"On the wedding night the groom goes up to the mansion with the maid, is drawn to her shy beauty, and speaks his wonder and servitude to her, before he goes down into the play grove where young women sing warnings to the plants about her beauty, and then climbs back up."},"so:51":{"book":"soolamani","num":51,"title":"அரசியற் சருக்கம் 1131-1155 (1/17)","section":"அரசியற் சருக்கம்","theme":"The spy's news reaches Acuvakandan, the discus-lord of the Vidyadhara world, that Tivittan has killed the lion and that the maiden has gone to earth as wed; his rage breaks a diamond pillar and ends in boasts, and he summons his kings, who arrive trembling and wonder at whom the anger is aimed."},"so:52":{"book":"soolamani","num":52,"title":"அரசியற் சருக்கம் 1156-1180 (2/17)","section":"அரசியற் சருக்கம்","theme":"In the council of the angry king, whose rage over a rumoured pledge of the Rathanupura maid to a human sets the court to talk of war, the soldiers, kin and ministers speak in turn, some urging war and some counselling scheming or restraint."},"so:53":{"book":"soolamani","num":53,"title":"அரசியற் சருக்கம் 1181-1205 (3/17)","section":"அரசியற் சருக்கம்","theme":"Under the threat of the discus-lord's army, the Vidyadhara king's council debates what kind of war to fight and the warriors boast in turn; Tivittan takes the war on himself, is given the last art, and receives the envoys' ultimatum for the maid."},"so:54":{"book":"soolamani","num":54,"title":"அரசியற் சருக்கம் 1206-1230 (4/17)","section":"அரசியற் சருக்கம்","theme":"The envoys' insult burns Tivittan, who lets them go by custom and sets a worship-or-die choice; omens and divine word promise his win, and both hosts, the foe's Vidyadhara army, gather with drums and weapons."},"so:55":{"book":"soolamani","num":55,"title":"அரசியற் சருக்கம் 1231-1255 (5/17)","section":"அரசியற் சருக்கம்","theme":"The Vidyadhara war reaches its height: armies of elephants, chariots and archers clash, the hero's arrows blind and rout the foe, and the proud rival king, told his army has broken, answers with a laugh."},"so:56":{"book":"soolamani","num":56,"title":"அரசியற் சருக்கம் 1256-1280 (6/17)","section":"அரசியற் சருக்கம்","theme":"The brothers of Accuvakkiriivan appear and scorn the human host, the two armies meet in a crowded battle of chariots, elephants and horses, and the warriors fight by a code of fearlessness and mercy."},"so:57":{"book":"soolamani","num":57,"title":"அரசியற் சருக்கம் 1281-1306 (7/17)","section":"அரசியற் சருக்கம்","theme":"A field battle of champions in the political sarukkam: Arisenan challenges and is cut down, Gunasenan and Indiran fight and Gunasenan falls, then the archers Varasenan and Kamukan meet."},"so:58":{"book":"soolamani","num":58,"title":"அரசியற் சருக்கம் 1307-1331 (8/17)","section":"அரசியற் சருக்கம்","theme":"The battle of the champions: the danava Arikethanan is cut down by Sarthulakan, Dhumaketu boasts and falls to the Vidyadhara king's younger brother, and Azhalvegan and Devasenan trade taunts."},"so:59":{"book":"soolamani","num":59,"title":"அரசியற் சருக்கம் 1332-1356 (9/17)","section":"அரசியற் சருக்கம்","theme":"The duel of Devasenan and Thazhalvegan ends in Suvarnaketu's arrival, a challenge to single combat, a shower of arrows and illusions, the fall of the golden-banner king, and Srisena's speech urging the kings not to flee."},"so:60":{"book":"soolamani","num":60,"title":"அரசியற் சருக்கம் 1357-1381 (10/17)","section":"அரசியற் சருக்கம்","theme":"The great battle with the Vidyadharas: they are shamed back into the fight, Sri Sena and Sripala join it, a fall of the Vidyadhara king's crown breaks his army, and Kanaka Chitran taunts the kings who flee from fated men."},"so:61":{"book":"soolamani","num":61,"title":"அரசியற் சருக்கம் 1382-1406 (11/17)","section":"அரசியற் சருக்கம்","theme":"The battle with the Vidyadharas continues: Kanaka Chitran taunts the foe and enters the fight, his kin's host rises, and the field fills with the wreck of men, horses and elephants described in a long run of similes."},"so:62":{"book":"soolamani","num":62,"title":"அரசியற் சருக்கம் 1407-1431 (12/17)","section":"அரசியற் சருக்கம்","theme":"The war of the plough-bearer against the Vidyadhara brothers: the young hero is attacked by four, the plough and lion bring his side back, Ratna Greevan's mountain breaks the army until an arrow fells him, and the grieving Asvagriva looses a spell that calls Death and the spirits against the world."},"so:63":{"book":"soolamani","num":63,"title":"அரசியற் சருக்கம் 1432-1456 (13/17)","section":"அரசியற் சருக்கம்","theme":"Tivittan's duel with Asvagriva: the hostile missile's deity shrinks before him, Garuda becomes his mount, the discus king boasts and is answered, and the two answer each other's divine weapons, naga with Garuda, fire with water, sleep with waking."},"so:64":{"book":"soolamani","num":64,"title":"அரசியற் சருக்கம் 1457-1481 (14/17)","section":"அரசியற் சருக்கம்","theme":"The discus of Ayakantan wheels back to Tivittan, who hurls it and kills the king; onlookers draw lessons of impermanence, and the king's wives fall and lament over his body."},"so:65":{"book":"soolamani","num":65,"title":"அரசியற் சருக்கம் 1482-1505 (15/17)","section":"அரசியற் சருக்கம்","theme":"After the discus kills the king of the Vidyadharas, the enemy dead are burned and their wives withdraw, the defeated bow and are shown mercy, the hero meets his father and is bathed and crowned Vasudeva, and the seven royal jewels come to him."},"so:66":{"book":"soolamani","num":66,"title":"அரசியற் சருக்கம் 1506-1530 (16/17)","section":"அரசியற் சருக்கம்","theme":"After the poets' chronicle of the discus-lords, the hero lifts the great gem mountain Kotisilai with his arms while kings and gods acclaim him, then returns in procession to Pothanam, where the city's women come out to him."},"so:67":{"book":"soolamani","num":67,"title":"அரசியற் சருக்கம் 1531-1554 (17/17)","section":"அரசியற் சருக்கம்","theme":"The end of the Arasiyal sarukkam: the king's young daughter is shown to the crowd and adorned, the city's festival darkens the sun, the prince enters the bride's chamber, is crowned beside her, gives her father the Vidyadhara realm and sinks into joy and wealth."},"so:68":{"book":"soolamani","num":68,"title":"சுயம்வரச் சருக்கம் 1555-1578 (1/12)","section":"சுயம்வரச் சருக்கம்","theme":"Opens the Swayamvara sarukkam: Tivittan after his victory, with the world at his feet, gives himself to pleasure; his chief queen asks to see the parijata tree wed to the Kamavalli creeper; and the brahmin jester Madhavan feigns a belly full of sweets, boasts of winning gifts in debate, and runs after fallen fruit in the grove."},"so:69":{"book":"soolamani","num":69,"title":"சுயம்வரச் சருக்கம் 1579-1602 (2/12)","section":"சுயம்வரச் சருக்கம்","theme":"In the grove, Tivittan and the jester walk past fruit, shade and flowers; the jester is afraid of dark, of thieves and of petals, the king teaches him the grove's blooms and cures, the jester jokes about the queen's foot and is struck by a flower, then flees from a spirit that proves to be his own shadow, before the queen arrives."},"so:70":{"book":"soolamani","num":70,"title":"சுயம்வரச் சருக்கம் 1603-1626 (3/12)","section":"சுயம்வரச் சருக்கம்","theme":"The queen overhears the king praise a stranger's footprint, storms out and is won back by the king's bow and embrace; the jester is hunted down, bound in garlands and let loose to sing yamaka songs about fruit; the couple then begin the wedding of the parijata and the Kama creeper and the king is caught again in women's glances."},"so:71":{"book":"soolamani","num":71,"title":"சுயம்வரச் சருக்கம் 1627-1650 (4/12)","section":"சுயம்வரச் சருக்கம்","theme":"Opens the garden sport after the plants' wedding: the groves are dressed, the queens marry the parijata to the Kama creeper, the king hides by art among his women with Madhavan, and the women, sent out by the chief queen, play in leaves and flowers, paint his portrait, sing his fame and complain of their delicacy, until they reach the artificial hill, where guards post watch and the echo mocks them."},"so:72":{"book":"soolamani","num":72,"title":"சுயம்வரச் சருக்கம் 1651-1674 (5/12)","section":"சுயம்வரச் சருக்கம்","theme":"The women play on the gem hill, dressing the stones as grass and flowers and singing the king's deeds; the king has a god take the form of a rutting elephant to frighten them, then comforts them, goes down at noon to the pool, and enters it with them, where the swirling water begins a water-fight."},"so:73":{"book":"soolamani","num":73,"title":"சுயம்வரச் சருக்கம் 1675-1698 (6/12)","section":"சுயம்வரச் சருக்கம்","theme":"Water-sport and the envoy: Tivittan's wives play against him in a garden pool, the play ends with drying, scenting and garlanding, and a Vidyadhara envoy flies down from the sky with a letter that begins to tell of a bride and her suitor."},"so:74":{"book":"soolamani","num":74,"title":"சுயம்வரச் சருக்கம் 1699-1722 (7/12)","section":"சுயம்வரச் சருக்கம்","theme":"Opens the night of Tivittan's household joy: after dismissing the Vidyadhara envoy he spends the night in love-play, ending with the queen, who sees the moon enter her mouth; he reads it as the coming of a son, and the sun and moon give the child form; the son is born, the city feasts, the rites, kin, gifts and praise follow, and the child is named Vijayan."},"so:75":{"book":"soolamani","num":75,"title":"சுயம்வரச் சருக்கம் 1723-1746 (8/12)","section":"சுயம்வரச் சருக்கம்","theme":"A Vidyadhara sky-car lands to bring joy over the birth in Tivittan's house, a Vidyadhara messenger tells how the sun-named lord's wife dreamt of a gem and bore the boy Amitatejas, Tivittan rewards him, and his own son grows, goes to teachers at five, and a younger daughter is born and named amid kin and joy."},"so:76":{"book":"soolamani","num":76,"title":"சுயம்வரச் சருக்கம் 1747-1770 (9/12)","section":"சுயம்வரச் சருக்கம்","theme":"In the Swayamvara sarukkam, Tivittan's daughter grows up and plays ball with her friends, the parents call the tired girl to them, the father weighs her ripeness, takes counsel and has the swayamvara drum proclaimed, and the kings and the Vidyadhara lords gather round the city."},"so:77":{"book":"soolamani","num":77,"title":"சுயம்வரச் சருக்கம் 1771-1793 (10/12)","section":"சுயம்வரச் சருக்கம்","theme":"The swayamvara hall is built and adorned; the kings of Ayodhya, Hastinapura, Kundalapura, Varanasi, Suriyapura and Madurai arrive in state, and the prince Amitatejas outshines them all before the maiden is called in."},"so:78":{"book":"soolamani","num":78,"title":"சுயம்வரச் சருக்கம் 1794-1816 (11/12)","section":"சுயம்வரச் சருக்கம்","theme":"Sayambavai is dressed, guarded and walks in procession to the swayamvara hall, her women praise her, and a herald names the assembled kings to her one by one, praising each by line, deeds, appearance and country, and twice by the merit or austerity that wins his embrace."},"so:79":{"book":"soolamani","num":79,"title":"சுயம்வரச் சருக்கம் 1817-1839 (12/12)","section":"சுயம்வரச் சருக்கம்","theme":"The herald names the last of the kings to the bride, who gives her heart to a young prince before he is named and garlands him; the rejected kings accept it as karma, the wedding is held and the swayamvara closes; a second swayamvara gives the girl Sutara to Srivijayan, and the poet ends the sarukkam by telling all to remember the fruit of deeds."},"so:80":{"book":"soolamani","num":80,"title":"துறவுச் சருக்கம் 1840-1862 (1/10)","section":"துறவுச் சருக்கம்","theme":"Opens the Renunciation sarukkam: King Payapati, delighting in his grandchildren, thinks that his kingship is the fruit of past good deeds and the body is foul and brief, then asks his ministers whether any statecraft can defeat Death; they cannot, and a minister proposes seeking the sages."},"so:81":{"book":"soolamani","num":81,"title":"துறவுச் சருக்கம் 1863-1885 (2/10)","section":"துறவுச் சருக்கம்","theme":"The king orders a drum proclaimed for a festival of the Lord at his golden temple under the Ashoka tree; the poet promises merit and a heavenly body to those who watch, the city and its folk dress and throng the streets, and the king rides out in full royal state while the poet says the onlookers' deeds died."},"so:82":{"book":"soolamani","num":82,"title":"துறவுச் சருக்கம் 1886-1908 (3/10)","section":"துறவுச் சருக்கம்","theme":"Payapati reaches the Jina shrine with flower and water offerings; the Lord appears under an asoka tree with throne, halo, umbrella, whisks, flower rain and heavenly music, and the king bows and sings a hymn praising his feet, his freedom from deeds and his rule above Indra and the dark-blue god."},"so:83":{"book":"soolamani","num":83,"title":"துறவுச் சருக்கம் 1909-1931 (4/10)","section":"துறவுச் சருக்கம்","theme":"After worshipping the Jina, the king bows to a sage, and the sage, at the ministers' plea to turn away births, lists his topics, names the four states and the seven hells, and describes the first hell's pits and the sufferers who entered by their own deeds."},"so:84":{"book":"soolamani","num":84,"title":"துறவுச் சருக்கம் 1932-1954 (5/10)","section":"துறவுச் சருக்கம்","theme":"The sage, answering King Payapati's turn to renunciation, describes the hells in detail: the matched tortures of adulterers, meat-eaters, slanderers and oppressors, then the doubling of pain, size and life span down the layers, and then who falls there."},"so:85":{"book":"soolamani","num":85,"title":"துறவுச் சருக்கம் 1955-1977 (6/10)","section":"துறவுச் சருக்கம்","theme":"The sage ends his account of hell and tells Payapati of the beasts: their one- to five-sensed births and the nigoda below, the tortures men inflict on them, the deceit and hatred that cause a beast's birth, and the deniers sent to the nigoda, then the island peoples counted among animals."},"so:86":{"book":"soolamani","num":86,"title":"துறவுச் சருக்கம் 1978-2000 (7/10)","section":"துறவுச் சருக்கம்","theme":"In the Renunciation sarukkam a sage tells the king which kinds of people are low and how rare is a human birth, shows pleasure as honey over a pit, names the four paths of effort and spends the rest on giving: its three grades, the giver's seven qualities, its fruit in kingship and the harm of giving to the cruel."},"so:87":{"book":"soolamani","num":87,"title":"துறவுச் சருக்கம் 2001-2023 (8/10)","section":"துறவுச் சருக்கம்","theme":"The sage's lesson on austerity continues: the vows and their fruit, the seven doors of merit, compassion and equanimity, knowledge as a lamp, the four kinds of meditation, and the great ones who conquered their deeds, down to the lands of enjoyment won by givers."},"so:88":{"book":"soolamani","num":88,"title":"துறவுச் சருக்கம் 2024-2046 (9/10)","section":"துறவுச் சருக்கம்","theme":"In the Renunciation sarukkam the teacher tells the king of the land of enjoyment where twin children grow, meet, unite and rise to heaven after their span, and then lists the four orders of gods: Bhavana, Vyantara, Jyotishka and the kalpa heavens."},"so:89":{"book":"soolamani","num":89,"title":"துறவுச் சருக்கம் 2047-2068 (10/10)","section":"துறவுச் சருக்கம்","theme":"The teacher finishes his account of the gods: the splendour of the heavens and their pleasures, the powers and spans of the four orders and the kalpa heavens up to thirty-three seas, and the highest gods under no lord; he notes that heaven's wealth, won by austerity, ends, and says that right vision with austerity keeps one from hell and brings endless bliss, as the king bows."},"so:90":{"book":"soolamani","num":90,"title":"முத்திச் சருக்கம் 2069-2089 (1/3)","section":"முத்திச் சருக்கம்","theme":"In the Renunciation sarukkam the teacher closes his account of release, the king resolves to strive for it, his ministers urge him to lay down the crown, and he warns his young sons that both the goddess of wealth and the goddess of the earth are faithless."},"so:91":{"book":"soolamani","num":91,"title":"முத்திச் சருக்கம் 2090-2110 (2/3)","section":"முத்திச் சருக்கம்","theme":"The Renunciation sarukkam continues with King Payapati announcing to the young princes that he has resolved to leave, his queens and ministers taking up austerity with him, his crown cast into the milk sea by the gods, the thousand kings laying down theirs, the sons' grief and its consolation, and the king's arming himself with the five vows, virtues and disciplines against karma."},"so:92":{"book":"soolamani","num":92,"title":"முத்திச் சருக்கம் 2111-2130 (3/3)","section":"முத்திச் சருக்கம்","theme":"In the closing verses of the Release sarukkam, the lord guards the fortress of the self, mounts the elephant of knowledge, draws the sword of meditation, kills the karmic enemies one by one, throws the sword away, enters the city of no return and becomes the crest-jewel of the world, while the gods worship and the princes of the story rule on."},"ud:1":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":1,"title":"பாயிரம் 1-4","section":"பாயிரம்","theme":"The poet's opening: he worships the tirthankara under the pindi tree and Vani, bows to the lord on the lion throne and announces the tale of Udayanan, apologises for his poor words with the image of a gem wrapped in cloth, and ends on the ripening and shedding of karma."},"ud:2":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":2,"title":"உஞ்சைக் காண்டம் 5-28 (1/5)","section":"உஞ்சைக் காண்டம்","theme":"The poem moves from the lord's age and the land to Kausambi, King Sathanikan and Queen Mirukavathi, then tells how a bird carries the queen to her father the sage, how her son Udayanan is born and grows up in the hermitage with Yugi, wins a lute and a divine elephant, and how his uncle Vikraman and then his father Sathanikan hand him a throne and turn ascetic, ending with his crowning and four ministers."},"ud:3":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":3,"title":"உஞ்சைக் காண்டம் 29-51 (2/5)","section":"உஞ்சைக் காண்டம்","theme":"The search for Udayana's vanished divine elephant is set beside Pradyota's court in Ujjain, where, enraged by a tribute-roll and by his ministers' praise of Udayana, the king lets them build a mechanical elephant packed with ninety-six armed men; Udayana ignores omens and Vayantakan's warning, plays his veena before it, and is surrounded."},"ud:4":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":4,"title":"உஞ்சைக் காண்டம் 52-74 (3/5)","section":"உஞ்சைக் காண்டம்","theme":"The capture and its answer: Udayanan's sword wins in the fighting until an elephant's tusk breaks it and he is seized; the dream of Vasavadatta foretells a taker, and Yugi, hearing of the capture, plans a rescue by guile, spreading news of his own death and entering the enemy's city disguised as a madman."},"ud:5":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":5,"title":"உஞ்சைக் காண்டம் 75-97 (4/5)","section":"உஞ்சைக் காண்டம்","theme":"Yugi, disguised as a mad performer, signals Udayanan in prison through music, rouses the king's elephant, which wrecks Ujjain; the ministers advise freeing the captive, and the vina of Udayanan brings the elephant to bow."},"ud:6":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":6,"title":"உஞ்சைக் காண்டம் 98-120 (5/5)","section":"உஞ்சைக் காண்டம்","theme":"Udayanan wins the king of Ujjain's trust by mastering the elephant, teaches Vasavadatta, is granted leave and an army, and flees with her by Yugi's fire-trick on the she-elephant, which dies and is reborn a god, then fends off the hunters and reaches Jayanti."},"ud:7":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":7,"title":"இலாவாண காண்டம் 121-135 (1/2)","section":"இலாவாண காண்டம்","theme":"Settled at Jayanti, Udayanan sinks in pleasure with Vasavadatta and forgets his lost kingdom; Yugi, reading this, plans to part the king from the queen by stratagem, sends a Sankhya woman ascetic with a portrait, and the king, after a sage's prophecy, garlands Virisikai."},"ud:8":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":8,"title":"இலாவாண காண்டம் 136-150 (2/2)","section":"இலாவாண காண்டம்","theme":"Yugi's false fire is played out: the king believes the queen has burned, mourns and wishes to die, is turned by his ministers, and sets out for the Magadha sage on Vayantakan's advice."},"ud:9":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":9,"title":"மகத காண்டம் 151-168 (1/2)","section":"மகத காண்டம்","theme":"Udayanan reaches Magadha still grieving, is deceived by a promise of his lost queen, is joined to Padmavati, and then Urumannuvan, Yugi's model in mind, turns the threat of seven kings into a stratagem of disguise and rumour."},"ud:10":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":10,"title":"மகத காண்டம் 169-185 (2/2)","section":"மகத காண்டம்","theme":"Udayanan wins the war for Tharusakan of Magadha, is given Padumai and a dowry of troops, then wins the Panchala war, enters his city in honour and heals the fallen."},"ud:11":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":11,"title":"வத்தவ காண்டம் 186-204 (1/3)","section":"வத்தவ காண்டம்","theme":"Udayanan, back among his people, rules with gifts, is told by sages that Vasavadatta lives and will bear a world-ruling son, welcomes Urumannuvan and then Vasavadatta and Yugi, hears Yugi's confession of his plot, and, after a sulk, reconciles with Vasavadatta."},"ud:12":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":12,"title":"வத்தவ காண்டம் 205-223 (2/3)","section":"வத்தவ காண்டம்","theme":"Udayanan rewards his ministers, receives Pradyotan's letter owning the snare and asking pardon, sends Yugi to Ujjain where he is honoured and married to two brides, and returns to watch a ball-play at court."},"ud:13":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":13,"title":"வத்தவ காண்டம் 224-241 (3/3)","section":"வத்தவ காண்டம்","theme":"At the end of Vatsa kandam a ball-play contest is won by Mananigai after Dattai's side is beaten; the king marries her, Dattai's anger yields to worship, and he later marries Virisikai by sacrifice, ending as the husband of four chaste queens ruling in peace."},"ud:14":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":14,"title":"நரவாகன காண்டம் 242-262 (1/3)","section":"நரவாகன காண்டம்","theme":"The king flies with the queen in the sky chariot the god's mantra supplies; Naravahanan is born and grows up with four companions, sees and unites with Madanamanjikai, who is carried off by the Vidyadhara Manasavegan, and Manasavegan's sister Vegavati, sent to break her firmness, falls in desire for the prince and takes Madanamanjikai's form."},"ud:15":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":15,"title":"நரவாகன காண்டம் 263-282 (2/3)","section":"நரவாகன காண்டம்","theme":"The youth who joins a Vidyadhara woman is seized by her brother and dropped in the forest, where a sage rescues him and foretells his rule and marriage; he visits his parents, flies to Sridharaloka and meets a Vidyadhara who tells of a king's daughter whose dream a sage reads as a sign of his coming."},"ud:16":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":16,"title":"நரவாகன காண்டம் 283-302 (3/3)","section":"நரவாகன காண்டம்","theme":"The Vidyadhara king gives the youth his daughter at an open gathering of kings, after which the youth gains the wheel and nine treasures through Indra's grace, subdues the Vidyadhara lords, returns with his queens to his father's city, installs a son and is sent off by his father to Indra's city."},"ud:17":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":17,"title":"துறவுக் காண்டம் 303-324 (1/3)","section":"துறவுக் காண்டம்","theme":"The Thuravu Kandam opens: Udayanan, after a life of friendship, love and conquest recalled in verse, turns to a vow and is tempted by women; the passage then breaks into an elephant's rampage in the city and the arrival of wandering Charana ascetics who preach that life is a fearful bondage."},"ud:18":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":18,"title":"துறவுக் காண்டம் 325-346 (2/3)","section":"துறவுக் காண்டம்","theme":"The elephant, calmed by a sage's dharma, comes to Udayanan; the king hears the Jain teaching, learns the elephant was a brahmin ruined by lust and drink, and starts to hear of his own former birth as Gomukan."},"ud:19":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":19,"title":"துறவுக் காண்டம் 347-367 (3/3)","section":"துறவுக் காண்டம்","theme":"The sage explains the elephant's former birth and its seven days; after its death in samadhi the king crowns Gomukhan, takes initiation with his queens and ministers, and on a hill burns the two deeds and reaches perfection while the others are reborn as gods."},"ya:1":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":1,"title":"பாயிரம் 1-4","section":"பாயிரம்","theme":"The poet invokes the omniscient lord, dates his tale in a tirtha lord's age, apologises for flaws with the lamp-and-smoke image and states the plan of the poem as a showing of karma's fruit."},"ya:2":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":2,"title":"முதற் சருக்கம் 5-27 (1/3)","section":"முதற் சருக்கம்","theme":"The poet places the story in Bharata and the city of Rajapuram, where King Maridattan, bewildered by wealth, takes the townspeople's fear of the goddess Chandamari for a duty, goes to her shrine, gathers pairs of animals and resolves to offer a pair of human children; the ascetic Sudattan arrives with his company, and the brother and sister Abhayaruchi and Abhayamati, sent on their round, enter the city in ascetic dress."},"ya:3":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":3,"title":"முதற் சருக்கம் 28-50 (2/3)","section":"முதற் சருக்கம்","theme":"Candakarman, sent by the king for a pair of humans to offer to the goddess, sees the brother and sister who have gone out for alms and seizes them; the brother consoles his frightened sister by teaching that the self is not harmed with the body, that deeds drive the soul through countless births, and that even gods and emperors perish, and the sister takes up the teaching and both resolve to leave the body in the Jinas' refuge."},"ya:4":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":4,"title":"முதற் சருக்கம் 51-73 (3/3)","section":"முதற் சருக்கம்","theme":"On the way to the altar of Chandamari the two youths meditate on the five objects of their reverence, are brought before the king with the sword drawn, bless him while smiling, and, asked why they laugh, tell him that fear is useless and that harm to living bodies bears suffering through births; the king lays down the sword and asks for their story, and the youth begins a sermon on karma, the body's filth and the rejection of those who deny rebirth."},"ya:5":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":5,"title":"இரண்டாஞ் சருக்கம் 74-95 (1/4)","section":"இரண்டாஞ் சருக்கம்","theme":"Ujjayini's king Asokan sees a white hair, argues from the fleeting of youth and the ripening of merit, crowns his son Yasodharan and takes renunciation before Gunadhara; Yasodharan, proud of wealth, forgets dharma and gives himself to conquest and pleasure, and on the night with Amritamati a song from the elephant stable moves her heart."},"ya:6":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":6,"title":"இரண்டாஞ் சருக்கம் 96-117 (2/4)","section":"இரண்டாஞ் சருக்கம்","theme":"The queen, taken by the night song of the elephant-keeper, confesses to her friend Gunavati, who first pleads restraint and recoils from the keeper's ugliness, then goes to him as go-between; he refuses from fear of the king and is brought round, and the queen turns cold to the king and slips away from his bed."},"ya:7":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":7,"title":"இரண்டாஞ் சருக்கம் 118-139 (3/4)","section":"இரண்டாஞ் சருக்கம்","theme":"The king, having followed his queen in the night and seen her with a base lover, spares them in thought and turns from desire; after a hard day of pretence in court he goes to his mother, who tells him to kill a kid to the goddess against his dream, and he refuses to take one life to save his own."},"ya:8":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":8,"title":"இரண்டாஞ் சருக்கம் 140-160 (4/4)","section":"இரண்டாஞ் சருக்கம்","theme":"The king refuses to kill a kid, is pressed into striking a flour cock, which cries out; the queen, pretending to join his renunciation, poisons him and his mother, and the mourners and the teller read the deaths by deeds."},"ya:9":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":9,"title":"மூன்றாஞ் சருக்கம் 161-182 (1/3)","section":"மூன்றாஞ் சருக்கம்","theme":"The king and his mother, dying in attachment, are reborn as peacock and dog, then boar and snake, then fish and crocodile, each birth ending in a violent death at the other's hands, with the poet naming deed as the cause."},"ya:10":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":10,"title":"மூன்றாஞ் சருக்கம் 183-204 (2/3)","section":"மூன்றாஞ் சருக்கம்","theme":"The queen mother and the king, reborn as a goat and a fish, are taken for the rite of the dead by the brahmins and by the king who is their son; the brahmins bless the dead with heaven as they eat him, and the kid, hearing it, recalls his birth, names his city and wealth as the fruit of his deed, and dies into another birth."},"ya:11":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":11,"title":"மூன்றாஞ் சருக்கம் 205-226 (3/3)","section":"மூன்றாஞ் சருக்கம்","theme":"The buffalo killed by the king's men for the loss of a horse is eaten by Amirthamathi, who is then struck by leprosy, which onlookers call the fruit of her deeds; one who understands visits and reproaches her, and the soul is born as a cock that the king has reared, while the chapter closes on the spring."},"ya:12":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":12,"title":"நான்காஞ் சருக்கம் 227-244 (1/2)","section":"நான்காஞ் சருக்கம்","theme":"King Yasomathi sits in his garden among dancers, and his executioner Chandakarman meets the ascetic Akampanan, to whom he says he cut thieves and never found a soul; the sage answers with fire in wood and a conch's sound, names the final state of the soul, and gives the man of the world knowledge, faith and the vows as his way."},"ya:13":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":13,"title":"நான்காஞ் சருக்கம் 245-261 (2/2)","section":"நான்காஞ் சருக்கம்","theme":"In the second half of the sage's reply to Yasomathi, the king's claim that life cannot go on without killing is answered by compassion and by the story of his father and grandmother, who suffered in every birth for killing a flour cock and who now sit as cocks in a cage beside him; they take the vow, are killed by his arrow and pass into human birth, and the chapter ends with Pushpavali's three children and the youngest prince's schooling."},"ya:14":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":14,"title":"ஐந்தாஞ் சருக்கம் 262-284 (1/3)","section":"ஐந்தாஞ் சருக்கம்","theme":"The king, out hunting, turns his killing on the ascetic Sudatta, looses hounds that will not come near him and draws his sword; the merchant Kalyanamitran stops him with the greatness of the ascetic, a former king of Kalinga, and with the rule that lives, even one's own, are not to be taken; the king bows and begs to hear of births, and Sudatta begins by his avadhi knowledge."},"ya:15":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":15,"title":"ஐந்தாஞ் சருக்கம் 285-307 (2/3)","section":"ஐந்தாஞ் சருக்கம்","theme":"The muni Sudattan tells the king the fruits of deeds: Asokan in a god's world, the poisoner Amritamati tortured through the hells and eating her own flesh, and Yasodharan and his mother through beast births to birth as the king's own twin children; the twins remember their births, the king weeps for what his deeds have done and the muni bids him not fear."},"ya:16":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":16,"title":"ஐந்தாஞ் சருக்கம் 308-330 (3/3)","section":"ஐந்தாஞ் சருக்கம்","theme":"The converted king and his companions renounce and he dies in the fast; the twin ascetics also die and are born as gods, and the poem closes by naming dharma, the giving up of anger, knowledge and the vow as what a person should do."}},"verses":{"ng:0":{"book":"naga_kumara_kaviyam","num":1,"t":["மணியுநற் கந்தமுத்து மலிந்த முக்குடை யிலங்க","அணிமலர்ப் பிண்டி யின்கீ ழமர்ந்த நேமீசர் பாதம்","பணியவே வாணி பாதம் பண்ணவர் தமக்கு மெந்தம்","இணைகரஞ் சிரசிற் கூப்பி யியல்புறத் தொழுது மன்றே."],"p":"","r":"The poet opens by joining his hands over his head and worshipping the feet of Lord Nemi, seated under the flowering pindi tree beneath the three umbrellas, and the feet of Vani.","n":"The invocation is not part of the traditional count of 170 and is kept as verse 0. It names Nemi, while the hymn at verses 16-19 praises Sri Vardhamanan; the poem addresses two tirthankaras. Vani is the goddess of speech, named after the lord.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","thiruvadi","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Naga Kumara Kaviyam 0","lb":"NG0","mm":1,"ev":"The poet raises his joined hands and worships the lord's feet.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"ng:1"},"ng:1":{"book":"naga_kumara_kaviyam","num":2,"t":["செந்தளிர்ப் பிண்டி யின்கீழ்ச் செழுமணி மண்ட பத்துள்","இந்திர னினிதி னேத்து மேந்தரி யாச னத்தின்","அந்தமா யமர்ந்த கோவி னருள்புரி தீர்த்த காலங்","கொந்தல ராசன் நாக குமரனற் கதைவி ரிப்பாம்."],"p":"","r":"The poet says he will spread out the tale of the Naga prince, the king who wears blossoms, in the age of the tirtha-lord who sat under the pindi tree.","n":"The tale is dated by the presence of a tirthankara, which sets the whole story inside the Jain teaching age.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","aagamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Naga Kumara Kaviyam 1","lb":"NG1","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"ng:1"},"ng:2":{"book":"naga_kumara_kaviyam","num":3,"t":["திங்கள் முந்நான்கு யோகந் தீவினை யரிய நிற்பர்","அங்கபூ வாதி நூலு ளரிப்பறத் தெளிந்த நெஞ்சிற்","தங்கிய கருணை யார்ந்த தவமுனி யவர்கள் சொன்ன","பொங்குநற் கவிக்க டறான் புகுந்துநீர்த் தெழுந்த தன்றே."],"p":"","r":"The poet says his poem rose from the words of compassionate ascetics who keep twelve-month yoga and have clearly learned the Anga and Purva texts.","n":"The poem takes its authority from named Jain scripture and from ascetics' speech, not from its own invention.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["aagamam","thavam","arivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Naga Kumara Kaviyam 2","lb":"NG2","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"ng:1"},"ng:3":{"book":"naga_kumara_kaviyam","num":4,"t":["புகைக்கொடி யுள்ளுண் டென்றே பொற்புநல் லொளிவிளக்கை","இகழ்ச்சியி னீப்பா ரில்லை யீண்டுநற் பொருளு ணர்ந்தோர்","அகத்தினி மதியிற் கொள்வா ரரியரோ வெனது சொல்லைச்","செகத்தவ ருணர்ந்து கேட்கச் செப்புதற் பால தாமே."],"p":"","r":"The poet argues that people do not discard a bright lamp because of its smoke, so those who grasp the worth of a thing will keep his words in mind.","n":"The lamp-and-soot image is the apology convention of the epics; here the flaw is conceded, not denied.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kutram","sol","saandror"],"pos":[],"nm":"Naga Kumara Kaviyam 3","lb":"NG3","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"ng:1"},"ng:4":{"book":"naga_kumara_kaviyam","num":5,"t":["வெவ்வினை வெகுண்டு வாரா விக்கிநன் கடைக்கும் வாயகள்","செவ்விதிற் புணர்ந்து மிக்க செல்வத்தை யாக்கு முன்னங்","கவ்விய கரும மெல்லாங் கணத்தினி லுதிர்ப்பை யாக்கும்","இவ்வகைத் தெரிவு றுப்பார்க் கினிதுவைத் துரைத்து மன்றே."],"p":"","r":"The poet says that deeds seized earlier can be shed in an instant, and that he tells this sweetly to those who discern it.","n":"This verse, unlike the Naladiyar's, says old deeds can be dropped rather than only borne; the first two lines are hard to parse.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vinai","oozh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Naga Kumara Kaviyam 4","lb":"NG4","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["deeds already gathered can fall away in a moment","jain","asserted",3,"narrator"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"ng:1"},"ng:5":{"book":"naga_kumara_kaviyam","num":6,"t":["நாவலந் தீப நூற்றை நண் ணுதொண் ணூறு கூறில்","ஆவதன் னொருகூ றாகு மரியநற் பரத கண்டம்","பாவலர் தகைமை மிக்கோர் பரம்பிய தரும பூமி","மேவுமின் முகில்சூழ் சோலை மிக்கதோர் மகதநாடு."],"p":"","r":"Bharata land, the Dharma-earth, is a ninetieth part of Jambudvipa, and in it lies the country of Magadha with cloud-wrapped groves.","n":"Dharma-land (தரும பூமி) is the Jain geography: only here can karma be earned and ended.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naadu","aram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Naga Kumara Kaviyam 5","lb":"NG5","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"ng:1"},"ng:6":{"book":"naga_kumara_kaviyam","num":7,"t":["திசைகளெங் கெங்குஞ் செய்யாள் செறிந்தினி துறையு நாட்டுள்","இசையுநற் பாரி சாத வினமலர்க் காவுஞ் சூழ்ந்த","அசைவிலா வமர லோகத் ததுநிக ரான மண்ணுள்","இசையுலா நகர மிக்க விராசமா கிரிய மாமே."],"p":"","r":"In Magadha, where Lakshmi dwells in all directions, stands the city Rajamagiri, rivalling the heavens with its flower groves.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naadu","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Naga Kumara Kaviyam 6","lb":"NG6","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"ng:1"},"ng:7":{"book":"naga_kumara_kaviyam","num":8,"t":["கிடங்கரு கிஞ்சி யோங்கிக் கிளர்முகில் சூடிச் செம்பொன்","கடங்கள்வைத் திலங்கு மாடங் கதிர்மதி சூட்டி னாற்போல்","படங்கிடந் தல்கு லார்கள் பாடலோ டாட லாலே","இடங்கொண்ட வின்ப மும்ப ரிடத்தையு மெச்சு மன்றே."],"p":"","r":"The city's towers, raised above its moat, crowned with moon and cloud, are full of singing and dancing women whose joy rivals that of the gods.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naadu","azhagu","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Naga Kumara Kaviyam 7","lb":"NG7","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"ng:1"},"ng:8":{"book":"naga_kumara_kaviyam","num":9,"t":["பாரித்த தன்மை முன்னம் பாலித்தற் கைம்ம டங்காம்","பூரித்த தார்கள் வேய்ந்த பொற்குடை யெழுந்த மேகம்","வாரித்த திசைந்த ளிக்கும் வண்கையம் பொற்றிண் டோளான்","சீரித்த தலங்கல் மார்பன் சிரேணிக ராசனாமே."],"p":"","r":"The ruler of this land is Shrenika, of the golden umbrella and the generous hand, guarding all who are his.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vendhan","arasu","eegai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Naga Kumara Kaviyam 8","lb":"NG8","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["சிரேணிகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ng:1"},"ng:9":{"book":"naga_kumara_kaviyam","num":10,"t":["ஆறிலொன் றிறைகொண் டாளு மரசன்மா தேவி யன்னப்","பேறுடை நடைவேற் கண்ணாள் பெறற்கருங் கற்பி னாள்போ","வீறுடைச் சாலி னீதா மிடைதவழ் கொங்கை கொண்டை","நாறுடைத் தார ணிந்த நகைமதி முகத்தி னாளே."],"p":"","r":"Chalini, his chief queen, is described as a woman of steady chastity whose face is like the moon.","n":"The virtue named is the ordinary wifely karpu, praised as rare (பெறற்கரும்).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["karpu","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Naga Kumara Kaviyam 9","lb":"NG9","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["சாலினி"],"ec":[],"ch":"ng:1"},"ng:10":{"book":"naga_kumara_kaviyam","num":11,"t":["மற்றுமெண் ணாயி ரம்பேர் மன்னனுக் கினிய மாதர்","வெற்றிவேல் விழியி னாரும் வேந்தனு மினிய போகம்","உற்றுடன் புணர்ந்து வின்பத் துவகையு ளழுந்தி யங்குச்","செற்றவர்ச் செகுத்துச் செங்கோற் செலவிய காலத் தன்றே"],"p":"","r":"Besides the queen, eight thousand beloved wives share his pleasures, and he destroys his foes and rules by the straight sceptre.","n":"Pleasure and just rule are given together as the king's glory, as the epic's first picture of a good king.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sengol","arasu","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Naga Kumara Kaviyam 10","lb":"NG10","mm":1,"ev":"The king enjoys his women and destroys his enemies while ruling.","cl":[],"ag":["சிரேணிகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ng:1"},"ng:11":{"book":"naga_kumara_kaviyam","num":12,"t":["இஞ்சிசூழ் புரத்து மேற்பா லிலங்கிய விபுல மென்னும்","மஞ்சிசூழ் மலையின் மீது வரவீர நாதர் வந்து","இஞ்சிமூன் றிலங்கும் பூமி யேழிறை யிருக்கை வட்டம்","அஞ்சிலம் பார்க ளாட வமரருஞ் சூழ்ந்த வன்றே."],"p":"","r":"On the hill Vipula, west of the walled city, Lord Vira comes, and gods and heavenly women gather round the assembly hall of three walls and seven levels.","n":"The hall of three walls is the samavasarana, the Jain preaching-hall of a tirthankara.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","aram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Naga Kumara Kaviyam 11","lb":"NG11","mm":1,"ev":"The lord Vira arrives on Vipula hill and the gods assemble.","cl":[],"ag":["வரவீர நாதர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ng:1"},"ng:12":{"book":"naga_kumara_kaviyam","num":13,"t":["வனமிகு வதிச யங்கள் வனபாலன் கண்டுவந்து","நனைமது மலர்க ளேந்தி நன்னகர் புகுந்தி ராசன்","மனையது மதிற்க டந்து மன்னனை வணங்கிச் செப்ப","மனமிக மகிழ்ந்தி றைஞ்சி மாமுர சறைக வென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"The forest-keeper sees the wonders of the wood, brings flowers to the king, bows, and reports; the king rejoices and has the great drum beaten.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Naga Kumara Kaviyam 12","lb":"NG12","mm":1,"ev":"The forest-keeper reports the wonders to the king, who has the drum sounded.","cl":[],"ag":["வனபாலன்","சிரேணிகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ng:1"},"ng:13":{"book":"naga_kumara_kaviyam","num":14,"t":["இடிமுர சார்ப்பக் கேட்டு மியம்பிய வத்தி னத்தின்","படுமத யானை தேர்மா வாள்நாற் படையுஞ் சூழக்","கடிமலர் சாந்து மேந்திக் காவலன் றேவி யோடுங்","கொடிநிரை பொன்னே யிற்குக் குழுவுடன் சென்ற வன்றே."],"p":"","r":"At the thunder of the drum the army of elephants, chariots, horses and swords gathers, and the king sets out with his queen to the golden walls.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Naga Kumara Kaviyam 13","lb":"NG13","mm":1,"ev":"The king sets out with his army and queen to the golden walls.","cl":[],"ag":["சிரேணிகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ng:1"},"ng:14":{"book":"naga_kumara_kaviyam","num":15,"t":["பொன்னெயிற் குறுகிக் கைம்மாப் புரவல னிழிந்துட் புக்கு","நன்னிலத் ததிச யங்கள் நரபதி தேவி யர்க்குப்","பன்னுரை செய்து காட்டிப் பரமன்றன் கோயி றன்னை","இன்னியல் வலங்கொண் டெய்தி யீசனை யிறைஞ்சி னானே."],"p":"","r":"The king enters the golden wall, shows the queens the wonders there, circles the lord's hall and bows.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","iraivan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Naga Kumara Kaviyam 14","lb":"NG14","mm":1,"ev":"The king enters, shows the queens the wonders, circles the hall and worships the lord.","cl":[],"ag":["சிரேணிகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ng:1"},"ng:15":{"book":"naga_kumara_kaviyam","num":16,"t":["நிலமுறப் பணிந்தெ ழுந்து நிகரிலஞ் சினையின் முற்றிக்","கலனணி செம்பொன் மார்பன் கால்பொரு கடலிற் பொங்கி","நலமுறு தோத்தி ரங்கள் நாதன்றன் வதன நோக்கிப்","பலமன மின்றி யொன்றிப் பலதுதி செப்ப லுற்றான்."],"p":"","r":"The king falls to the earth, rises, and, welling up like the sea, begins many hymns, gazing on the lord's face with one mind.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Naga Kumara Kaviyam 15","lb":"NG15","mm":1,"ev":"The king bows, rises and begins to praise the lord.","cl":[],"ag":["சிரேணிகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ng:1"},"ng:16":{"book":"naga_kumara_kaviyam","num":17,"t":["பொறியொடுவல் வினைவென்ற புனித னீயே பூநான்கு மலர்ப்பிண்டிப் போத னீயே","புறவிதழ்சேர் மரைமலர்மேல் விரனால் விட்டுப் பொன்னெயிலுண் மன்னியபுங் கவனு நீயே","அறவிபணி பணவரங்கத் தமர்ந்தாய் நீயே ஐங்கணைவில் மன்மதனை யகன்றாய் நீயே","செறிபுகழ்சேர் சித்திநகர் தன்னை யாளும் சிரீவர்த்த மானனெனுந் தீர்த்த னீயே."],"p":"","r":"The king praises the lord as the one who conquered hard deeds with the senses, who sits under the pindi tree, who left Kama, and who now rules the city of release as Sri Vardhamanan.","n":"The first of four hymn verses ending with the refrain on Sri Vardhamanan; the printed eight half-lines were joined into four.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","vinai","veedu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Naga Kumara Kaviyam 16","lb":"NG16","mm":1,"ev":"The king hymns the lord's victory over karma and his seat under the pindi tree.","cl":[["the tirthankara won over the senses and hard karma","jain","asserted",1,"sirenikan"],["he ended the pull of desire in Kama and rules the city of release","jain","asserted",3,"sirenikan"]],"ag":["சிரேணிகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சிரேணிகன்","வரவீர நாதர்"],"ch":"ng:1"},"ng:17":{"book":"naga_kumara_kaviyam","num":18,"t":["கஞ்சமலர் திருமார்பிற் றரித்தாய் நீயே காலமொரு மூன்றுணர்ந்த கடவு ணீயே","பஞ்சாத்தி தானுரைத்த பரம னீயே பரமநிலை யொன்றெனவே பணித்தாய் நீயே","துஞ்சாநல் லுலகுதொழுந் தூய னீயே தொல்வினையெல் லாமெரித்த துறவ னீயே","செஞ்சொற் பாவையை நாவிற் சேர்த்தாய் நீயே சிரீவர்த்த மானெனுந் தீர்த்த னீயே."],"p":"","r":"The king praises the lord for all-knowing in three times, for burning all old deeds, for teaching the five categories, and for placing the tongue under speech.","n":"Karma here is burned by the lord's own renunciation; in the Saiva books it is burned by grace.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","vinai","thuravu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Naga Kumara Kaviyam 17","lb":"NG17","mm":1,"ev":"The king continues the hymn.","cl":[["the lord knows the three times and burned all old deeds","jain","asserted",3,"sirenikan"],["he taught the five categories and said the supreme state is one","jain","asserted",2,"sirenikan"]],"ag":["சிரேணிகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சிரேணிகன்","வரவீர நாதர்"],"ch":"ng:1"},"ng:18":{"book":"naga_kumara_kaviyam","num":19,"t":["அறவனீ யமலனீ யாதி நீயே ஆரியனீ சீரீயனீ யனந்த னீயே","திரிலோக லோகமொடு தேய னீயே தேவாதி தேவனெனுந் தீர்த்த னீயே","எரிமணிநற் பிறப்புடைய யீச னீயே இருநான்கு குணமுடைய யிறைவ னீயே","திரிபுவனந் தொழுதிறைஞ்சுஞ் செல்வ னீயே சிரீவர்த்த மானமெனுந் தீர்த்த னீயே."],"p":"","r":"The king calls him righteous, spotless, ageless, endless, god of gods, the lord of eight qualities, worshipped by the three worlds.","n":"The eight qualities are named only as a number here; the verse does not list them.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","aram","mukkunam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Naga Kumara Kaviyam 18","lb":"NG18","mm":1,"ev":"The king continues the hymn.","cl":[["the liberated lord has eight qualities","jain","asserted",3,"sirenikan"]],"ag":["சிரேணிகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சிரேணிகன்","வரவீர நாதர்"],"ch":"ng:1"},"ng:19":{"book":"naga_kumara_kaviyam","num":20,"t":["முனிவர்தமக் கிறையான மூர்த்தி நீயே மூவா முதல்வனெனு முத்த னீயே","இனிமையா னந்தசுகத் திருந்தாய் நீயே இயலாறு பொருளுரைத்த வீச னீயே","முனிவுமுத லில்லாத முனைவ னீயே முக்குடையின் கீழமர்ந்த முதல்வ னீயே","செனித்திறக்கு மூப்பி றப்புந் தீர்த்தாய் நீயே சிரீவர்த்த மானனெனுந் தீர்த்த னீயே."],"p":"","r":"The king ends the hymn praising the lord as free of anger and of birth, old age and death, who taught the six substances and sits under the three umbrellas.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","piravi","veedu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Naga Kumara Kaviyam 19","lb":"NG19","mm":1,"ev":"The king ends his hymn to the lord.","cl":[["the lord has ended birth, old age and death","jain","asserted",4,"sirenikan"],["he taught the six substances","jain","asserted",2,"sirenikan"]],"ag":["சிரேணிகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சிரேணிகன்","வரவீர நாதர்"],"ch":"ng:1"},"ng:20":{"book":"naga_kumara_kaviyam","num":21,"t":["நவபதநன் னயமாறு நவின்றாய் நீயே நன்முனிவர் மனத்திசைந்த னாத னீயே","உவமையிலா வைம்பதமு முரைத்தாய் நீயே உத்தமர்த மிருதயத்து ளுகந்தாய் நீயே","பவமயமா மிருவினையைப் பகர்ந்தாய் நீயே பரம நிலையமர்ந்த பரமன் நீயே","சிவமயமாய் நின்றதிகழ் தேச னீயே சிரீவர்த்த மானனெனுந் தீர்த்த னீயே."],"p":"","r":"The speaker addresses the lord again and again as 'you': you set out the good way of the nine categories, you spoke the five unequalled ranks, you declared the two deeds that make up becoming, you are the radiant one standing as bliss and the pure ford called Vardhamana.","n":"The verse was printed in eight half-lines and the edition's join into four is the text used. சிவமயமாய் here describes the lord as made of auspiciousness or release, not the Saiva god. The speaker is not named in the verse; the next verse says the king did 'praises' and then went on, so the king is the likely voice.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","vinai","piravi","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Naga Kumara Kaviyam 20","lb":"NG20","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["the lord taught the way of the nine categories, the five ranks and the two deeds from which becoming is made","jain","asserted",1,"magadha_king"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["மன்னன்","சிரீவர்த்த மானன்"],"ch":"ng:2"},"ng:21":{"book":"naga_kumara_kaviyam","num":22,"t":["துதிகள் செய்துபின் றூய்மணி நன்னிலத்","ததிகொள் சிந்தையி னம்பிறப் பணிந்துடன்","நெதியி ரண்டென நீடிய தோளினான்","யதிகொள் பண்ணவர் பாவலன் புக்கதே."],"p":"","r":"After his praises the king, whose long arms are like two treasures, bows with a settled mind toward the pure jewelled ground and goes into the holy place of the lord of ascetics.","n":"Line 4 reads பாவலன் புக்கதே; the sense of பாவலன் (poet, or an epithet of the king) and the object of the entry are not fixed by the verse.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["panivu","vazhipaadu","iraivan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Naga Kumara Kaviyam 21","lb":"NG21","mm":1,"ev":"The king, after praising the lord, bows and enters the place where the lord's teacher sits.","cl":[],"ag":["மன்னன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ng:2"},"ng:22":{"book":"naga_kumara_kaviyam","num":23,"t":["சிறந்து கோட்டத்துச் செல்வக கணதரர்","இறைவ னன்மொழி யிப்பொரு ளுட்கொண்டு","அறைய மர்ந்துயிர்க் கறமழை யைப்பெயுந்","துறவ னற்சரண் டூய்தி னிறைஞ்சினான்."],"p":"","r":"The teacher of the assembly takes in the lord's good word and, seated, pours the rain of dharma on living beings; the king bows at the pure feet of that renunciant.","n":"The teaching is handed on in two steps, lord to ganadhara to hearers, and the verse makes the teacher the one who rains it; the verse does not name him, and 23 names Gautama.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aram","thuravu","panivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Naga Kumara Kaviyam 22","lb":"NG22","mm":1,"ev":"The ganadhara expounds the lord's word to living beings and the king bows to his feet.","cl":[["the teacher takes in the lord's word and sends down dharma on living beings like rain","jain","asserted",3,"narrator"]],"ag":["கௌதமர்","மன்னன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ng:2"},"ng:23":{"book":"naga_kumara_kaviyam","num":24,"t":["மற்றம் மாமுனி யேர்மல ராம்பதம்","உற்று டன்பணிந் தோங்கிய மன்னவன்","நற்ற வர்க்கிறை யானநற் கௌதமர்","வெற்றி நற்சரண் வேந்த னிறைஞ்சினான்."],"p":"","r":"The king, having reached the flower-like feet of the great sage, bows at the victorious feet of Gautama, chief of good ascetics.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["panivu","thavam","thuravu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Naga Kumara Kaviyam 23","lb":"NG23","mm":1,"ev":"The king bows at Gautama's feet.","cl":[],"ag":["மன்னன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ng:2"},"ng:24":{"book":"naga_kumara_kaviyam","num":25,"t":["இருக ரத்தி னிறைஞ்சிய மன்னனும்","பொருக யற்கணிப் பூங்குழை மாதரும்","தரும தத்துவஞ் சனமுனி வர்க்குரை","இருவ ருமியைந் தின்புறக் கேட்டபின்."],"p":"","r":"The king and his queen, who bow with both hands, listen with joy to Gautama's teaching of the nature of dharma, and when they have heard it,","n":"The verse is an unfinished sentence and the next verse supplies its main clause; the claim is thin because the content of the teaching is not given here.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aram","panivu","karpu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Naga Kumara Kaviyam 24","lb":"NG24","mm":1,"ev":"The king and queen hear Gautama teach the true nature of dharma.","cl":[["dharma has a real nature (tattva) that a teacher can set out for lay hearers","jain","asserted",3,"narrator"]],"ag":["மன்னன்","மாதர்","கௌதமர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ng:2"},"ng:25":{"book":"naga_kumara_kaviyam","num":26,"t":["சிரிநற் பஞ்சமி செல்வக் கதையினை","செறிகழல் மன்னன் செப்புக வென்றலும்","அறிவு காட்சி யமர்ந்தொழுக் கத்தவர்","குறியு ணர்ந்ததற் கூறுத லுற்றதே."],"p":"","r":"The king asks that the story of Sri Panchami be told, and the teacher, who dwells in knowledge, vision and conduct, takes his sign and begins to speak.","n":"அறிவு காட்சி ஒழுக்கம் are the three jewels of the Jain path as the verse names them together; the verse does not call them that. The frame sets up everything after it as Gautama's telling of an earlier birth story.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aram","kalvi","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Naga Kumara Kaviyam 25","lb":"NG25","mm":1,"ev":"The king asks Gautama to tell the Sri Panchami story and Gautama begins.","cl":[["the ascetic's life is built on knowledge, right vision and conduct","jain","asserted",3,"narrator"]],"ag":["மன்னன்","கௌதமர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ng:2"},"ng:26":{"book":"naga_kumara_kaviyam","num":27,"t":["நாவலந் தீவி னற்பர தத்திடை","மாவலர் மன்னர் மன்னு மகதநற்","கூவுங் கோகிலங் கொண்மதுத் தாரணி","காவுஞ் சூழ்ந்த கனக புரம்மதே."],"p":"","r":"In the Bharata land of the island of Jambu lies Magadha with its fine cities; Kanakapura, ringed with koel-filled groves, is in it.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naadu","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Naga Kumara Kaviyam 26","lb":"NG26","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"ng:2"},"ng:27":{"book":"naga_kumara_kaviyam","num":28,"t":["அந்ந கர்க்கிறை யான சயந்தரன்","நன்ம னைவிவி சாலநன் னேத்திரை","தன்சு தன்மதுத் தாரணி சீதரன","நன்க மைச்ச னயந்தர னென்பவே."],"p":"","r":"The lord of that city is Jayandhara, whose good wife is Visalanetrai; the verse goes on to name a son, Sridhara, and Nayandhara, with the sense of 'good minister' attached to one of them.","n":"The last two lines run names together (மதுத்தாரணி, சீதரன், நயந்தரன்), so which name belongs to the son and which to the minister cannot be fixed from the Tamil; I read சுதன் as 'son' and நன்கமைச்சன் as the minister.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vendhan","kudi","makkal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Naga Kumara Kaviyam 27","lb":"NG27","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["சயந்தரன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ng:2"},"ng:28":{"book":"naga_kumara_kaviyam","num":29,"t":["மற்றுந் தேவியர் மன்னுமெண் ணாயிரர்","வெற்றி வேந்தன் விழைந்துறு கின்றநாள","பற்ற வாணிகன் பல்பொருள் பொற்கலத்","துற்ற மாதர் படத்துருக் காட்டினான்."],"p":"","r":"While the king enjoys his eight thousand queens, a merchant shows him the form of a woman painted on a gold vessel among his many wares.","n":"The king is unmoved by thousands of wives until a picture arrives; the story starts from a want that none of the existing queens meet.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","azhagu","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Naga Kumara Kaviyam 28","lb":"NG28","mm":1,"ev":"A merchant shows King Jayandhara a portrait of a woman on a gold vessel.","cl":[],"ag":["வாசவன்","சயந்தரன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ng:2"},"ng:29":{"book":"naga_kumara_kaviyam","num":30,"t":["மன்ன னோக்கி மயங்கி மகிழ்ந்தபின்","கின்னரி யோகி ளர்கார் மாதரோ","இன்ன ரூபமிக் காரிது வென்றலும்","மன்னும் வாசவன் வாக்குரை செய்கின்றான்."],"p":"","r":"The king, struck and delighted by the picture, asks whether such a form is of a kinnari, a yogini or a dark woman, and Vasavan the merchant begins to answer.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","mayal","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Naga Kumara Kaviyam 29","lb":"NG29","mm":1,"ev":"The king, bewildered by the picture, asks who she is, and the merchant Vasavan replies.","cl":[],"ag":["சயந்தரன்","வாசவன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ng:2"},"ng:30":{"book":"naga_kumara_kaviyam","num":31,"t":["சொல்ல ரியசு ராட்டிர தேசத்துப்","பல்ச னநிறை பரங்கிரி யாநகர்","செல்வன் சிரீவர்மன் றேவியுஞ் சிரீமதி","நல்சு தையவள் நாமம் பிரிதிதேவி."],"p":"","r":"The merchant says: in the land of Surashtra, in the populous Parangiri, lives Srivarman, whose wife Srimati bears a daughter named Prithidevi.","n":"The speaker is read from the previous verse; the verse itself does not name him.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kudi","makkal","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Naga Kumara Kaviyam 30","lb":"NG30","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["வாசவன்","சயந்தரன்"],"ch":"ng:2"},"ng:31":{"book":"naga_kumara_kaviyam","num":32,"t":["அவ்வ ணிகன வளுடை ரூபத்தைச்","செவ்விதிற் செப்பச் சீருடை மன்னனும்","மௌவ லங்குழன் மாதரைத் தானழைத்துத்","தெய்வ வேள்வியிற் சேர்ந்து புணர்ந்தனன்."],"p":"","r":"The merchant tells of her rightly; the king calls her and, in a rite before the sacred fire, unites with her.","n":"தெய்வ வேள்வி is a rite of the sacred fire, and the verse does not say whether it is a wedding in a Jain form; the epic's affiliation is by tradition only.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","ilvaazhkkai","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Naga Kumara Kaviyam 31","lb":"NG31","mm":1,"ev":"Jayandhara has Prithidevi brought and marries her before the sacred fire.","cl":[],"ag":["வாசவன்","சயந்தரன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ng:2"},"ng:32":{"book":"naga_kumara_kaviyam","num":33,"t":["மன்ன னின்புற்று மாதேவி யாகவே","நன்மைப் பட்ட நயந்து கொடுத்தபின்","மன்னு மாதர்கள் வந்து பணிந்திட","இன்ன வாற்றி னியைந்துடன் செல்லுநாள்"],"p":"","r":"The king, pleased, gives her the rank of chief queen, and when the other wives come to bow to her, they go on in this way.","n":"The verse ends on 'ones who go on together', a clause the next verse finishes.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ilvaazhkkai","arasu","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Naga Kumara Kaviyam 32","lb":"NG32","mm":1,"ev":"Jayandhara makes Prithidevi chief queen and the other consorts come to honour her.","cl":[],"ag":["சயந்தரன்","மன்னு மாதர்கள்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ng:2"},"ng:33":{"book":"naga_kumara_kaviyam","num":34,"t":["வயந்த மாடவே மன்னனு மாதரும்","நயந்து போந்தனர் நன்மலர்க் காவினுட்","பெயர்ந்து பல்லக்கி னேறிப் பிரிதிதேவி","கயந்த னீரணி காண்டற்குச் சென்றநாள்."],"p":"","r":"For the spring game the king and his women go happily to a flower garden, and Prithidevi leaves in a palanquin to watch the water-sport of an elephant.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","azhagu","ilvaazhkkai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Naga Kumara Kaviyam 33","lb":"NG33","mm":1,"ev":"The court goes to the garden and Prithidevi rides out to see the elephant's bathing.","cl":[],"ag":["சயந்தரன்","பிரிதிதேவி"],"ec":[],"ch":"ng:2"},"ng:34":{"book":"naga_kumara_kaviyam","num":35,"t":["வார ணத்தின்முன் மார்க்கத்து நின்றவள்","வாரணி கொங்கை யாரவ ளென்றலும்","ஏர ணிம்முடி வேந்தன்மா தேவியென்று","தார ணிகுழற் றாதி யுரைத்தனள்."],"p":"","r":"A woman standing in the road before the elephant asks who the lady with the belted breasts is, and the nurse answers that she is the king's chief queen.","n":"That the woman at the elephant is Visalanetrai is read from the next verse, not stated here; who asks and to whom the nurse speaks is not fixed by the Tamil.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["ilvaazhkkai","kudi","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Naga Kumara Kaviyam 34","lb":"NG34","mm":1,"ev":"A woman before the elephant asks about Prithidevi and the nurse says she is the king's chief queen.","cl":[],"ag":["விசாலநேத்திரை","தாதி"],"ec":[],"spk":["தாதி",""],"ch":"ng:2"},"ng:35":{"book":"naga_kumara_kaviyam","num":36,"t":["வேல்விழி மாது கேட்டு விசாலநேத் திரையோ வென்னைக்","கான்மிசை வீழ வெண்ணிக் காண்டற்கு நின்றா ளென்று","பான்மொழி யமிர்த மன்னாள் பரம னாலைய மடைந்து","நூன்மொழி யிறைவன் பாதம் நோக்கிநன் கிறைஞ்சி னாளே."],"p":"","r":"The spear-eyed woman hears this and thinks that Visalanetrai stands there to see her fall at the elephant's foot, then goes to the temple and bows to the feet of the lord whose word is scripture.","n":"The fear is the new queen's own supposition and the verse never shows Visalanetrai doing or wishing anything; only the suspicion of rivalry and the turn to the god are given. The reading 'to fall at its feet' (கான்மிசை வீழ) is mine and the elephant is not named in the verse.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["acham","vazhipaadu","azhukkaaru"],"pos":[],"nm":"Naga Kumara Kaviyam 35","lb":"NG35","mm":1,"ev":"Prithidevi, fearing the senior queen means her harm, goes to the temple and bows to the lord.","cl":[],"ag":["பிரிதிதேவி"],"ec":[],"ch":"ng:2"},"ng:36":{"book":"naga_kumara_kaviyam","num":37,"t":["கொல்லாத நல்விரதக் கோமானினைத் தொழுதார்","பொல்லாக் கதியறுத்துப் பொற்புடைய முத்திதனைச்","செல்லற் கெளிதென்றே சேயிழையாள் தான்பரவி","எல்லா வினைசெறிக்கு மியன்முனியைத் தான்பணித்தாள்."],"p":"","r":"Praising the lord who keeps the vow of not killing, she says that those who bow to him cut off the evil birth and reach release easily, and she bids the sage who binds all deeds to teach her.","n":"The god is defined by a vow, not a gift: what he gives is the example; the verse does not say whether the worship or the vow does the cutting. இயன்முனி in line 4 may be a different person from the lord, and the verse does not say.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kollaamai","vinai","veedu","iraivan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Naga Kumara Kaviyam 36","lb":"NG36","mm":1,"ev":"Prithidevi praises the lord of non-killing and asks the sage to teach her.","cl":[["those who worship the lord of the vow of not killing cut off an evil birth and reach release easily","jain","asserted",1,"prithidevi"],["the sage's discipline holds back all deeds","jain","asserted",4,"narrator"]],"ag":["பிரிதிதேவி"],"ec":[],"ch":"ng:2"},"ng:37":{"book":"naga_kumara_kaviyam","num":38,"t":["பணிபவட்கு நன்குரையிற் பரமமுனி வாழ்த்த","அணிபெறவே நற்றவமு மாமோ வெனக்கென்றாள்","கணிதமிலாக் குணச்சுதனைக் கீர்த்தியுட னேபெறுவை","மணிவிளக்க மேபோன்ற மாதவனுந் தானுரைத்தான்,"],"p":"","r":"As she bows, the sage blesses her and she asks whether good penance can be hers; he tells her that she will get a son of countless virtues, with fame.","n":"She asks about tapas and he answers with a son, so the answer is to a different question or the question means 'will my wish for a worthy end be met'; the sense of நற்றவம் ஆமோ is open.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["makkal","thavam","pugazh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Naga Kumara Kaviyam 37","lb":"NG37","mm":1,"ev":"The sage blesses Prithidevi and foretells a famous, virtuous son.","cl":[],"ag":["பிரிதிதேவி","மாதவன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ng:2"},"ng:38":{"book":"naga_kumara_kaviyam","num":39,"t":["நின்றசனந் தன்னுடனே நீடுபோய்த் தவம்பட்டுப்","பின்றை யறவுரைகள் பெருமிதமாய்க் கேட்டுவிதி","வென்ற பரமனடி விமலமாய்த் தான்பணிந்து","அன்றுதான் புத்திரனை யவதரித்தாற் போன்மகிழ்ந்தாள்."],"p":"","r":"She goes on with her people, takes up the penance and hears the teaching of dharma with joy, bows without stain at the feet of the lord who conquered fate, and is glad as if a son were already born.","n":"விதி here is the deed-fate the lord has won over, not an outside power; the verse says tavam is entered but does not say what vow, and 'took penance' may only mean she kept a lay observance.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thavam","vinai","aram","makkal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Naga Kumara Kaviyam 38","lb":"NG38","mm":1,"ev":"Prithidevi takes penance, hears dharma and bows to the lord, delighted as if her son were born.","cl":[["the supreme one has conquered fate, the sum of deeds","jain","asserted",3,"narrator"]],"ag":["பிரிதிதேவி"],"ec":[],"ch":"ng:2"},"ng:39":{"book":"naga_kumara_kaviyam","num":40,"t":["நற்றவ னுரைத்த சொல்லை நறுமலர்க் கோதை கேட்டு","பற்றுட னுணர்ந்து நல்ல பாசிழைப் பரவை யல்குல்","உற்றதன் குழலி னாரோ டுறுதவன் பாதந் தன்னில்","வெற்றியி னிறைஞ்சி வந்து வியன்மனை புகுந்தி ருந்தாள்"],"p":"","r":"Hearing the good ascetic's words, the garlanded woman understands them with attachment and, with her women, bows in triumph at his feet, returns to her great house and sits.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aram","panivu","ilvaazhkkai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Naga Kumara Kaviyam 39","lb":"NG39","mm":1,"ev":"Prithidevi hears the sage, bows with her women and goes home.","cl":[],"ag":["பிரிதிதேவி","மாதவன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ng:2"},"ng:40":{"book":"naga_kumara_kaviyam","num":41,"t":["வனவிளை யாட லாடி மன்னன் றன்மனை புகுந்து","மனமகிழ் கோதை தன்னை மருவிய காத லாலே","புனலினீ யாட லின்றிப் போம்பொருட் புகல்க வென்ன","கனவரை மார்பன் கேட்பக் காரிகை யுரைக்கு மன்றே."],"p":"","r":"Back from sporting in the forest, the king enters his house and the queen, loving, is asked to say what matter is lost for want of water-play; she will speak while he listens.","n":"Lines 3-4 are hard to construe: who asks and what 'the matter that goes' (போம்பொருள்) is are not fixed, and the verse only says the queen will speak.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Naga Kumara Kaviyam 40","lb":"NG40","mm":1,"ev":"The king returns from forest play to the queen's house, and the queen is about to tell him something.","cl":[],"ag":["மன்னன்","பிரிதிவிழ் தேவி"],"ec":[],"ch":"ng:3"},"ng:41":{"book":"naga_kumara_kaviyam","num":42,"t":["இறைவனா லயத்துட் சென்று விறைவனை வணங்கித் தீய","கறையிலா முனிவன் பாதங் கண்டடி பணிந்து தூய","அறவுரை கேட்டே னென்ன வரசன்கேட் டுளம கிழ்ந்து","பிறைநுதற் பேதை தன்னாற் பெறுசுவைக் கடலு ளாழ்ந்தார்."],"p":"","r":"The queen says she went to the god's temple, bowed to the Jina and to the stainless sage, heard pure teaching of virtue, and the king, glad at hearing it, sinks with her into a sea of delight.","n":"The teaching is called அறவுரை but its content is not given; the verse joins temple worship and household pleasure in one scene.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","aram","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Naga Kumara Kaviyam 41","lb":"NG41","mm":1,"ev":"The queen reports her visit to the temple and the sage's sermon; the king rejoices.","cl":[],"ag":["பிரிதிவிழ் தேவி","மன்னன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["பிரிதிவிழ் தேவி","மன்னன்"],"ch":"ng:3"},"ng:42":{"book":"naga_kumara_kaviyam","num":43,"t":["இருவரும் பிரித லின்றி யின்புறு போகந் துய்த்து","மருவிய துயில்கொள் கின்றார் மனோகர மென்னும் யாமம்","இருண்மனை இமிலே றொன்று மிளங்கதிர் கனவிற் றோன்றப்","பொருவிலாட் கண்டெ ழுந்து புரவலர்க் குணர்த்தி னாளே."],"p":"","r":"After shared pleasure the couple sleep, and in the night's watch the queen sees in a dream a bull and a young sun and wakes the king to tell him.","n":"The verse names the omens but not what they mean; the answer waits in verse 44.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kanavu","kurippu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Naga Kumara Kaviyam 42","lb":"NG42","mm":1,"ev":"The queen dreams of a bull and a young sun and wakes the king to tell him.","cl":[],"ag":["பிரிதிவிழ் தேவி","மன்னன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ng:3"},"ng:43":{"book":"naga_kumara_kaviyam","num":44,"t":["வேந்தன்கேட் டினிய னாகி விமலனா லயத்துட் சென்று","சேந்தளிர்ப் பிண்டி யின்கீழ்ச் செல்வனை வணங்கி வாழ்த்தி","காந்திய முனிக்கி றைஞ்சிக் கனாப்பய னுவல வென்றான்","ஏந்திள முலையி னாளு மிறைவனு மிகுந்து கேட்டார்."],"p":"","r":"The pleased king goes to the temple, bows to the Lord under the red-leaved pindi tree and the sage, and asks for the fruit of the dream, which the queen and he hear with joy.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","kanavu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Naga Kumara Kaviyam 43","lb":"NG43","mm":1,"ev":"The king takes the dream to the sage at the temple and asks its meaning.","cl":[],"ag":["மன்னன்","பிரிதிவிழ் தேவி"],"ec":[],"ch":"ng:3"},"ng:44":{"book":"naga_kumara_kaviyam","num":45,"t":["அம்முனி யவரை நோக்கி யருந்துநற் கனவு தன்னைச்","செம்மையி னிருவர் கட்குஞ் சிறுவன்வந் துதிக்கு மென்றுங்","கம்பமின் னிலங்க ளெல்லாங் காத்துநற் றவமுந் தாங்கி","வெம்பிய வினைய றுத்து வீடுநன் கடையு மென்றார்."],"p":"","r":"The sage tells them that the dream means a son will be born to both, who will guard the whole earth, bear great austerity, cut away his heated deeds and reach release.","n":"The sage gives the son two lives, the protector of lands and the ascetic who ends karma; the prophecy sets both before the story of the prince's marriages and later renunciation begins. The word வினை here is the deeds that burn away through his own tapas, not a grace from another.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","thavam","veedu","makkal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Naga Kumara Kaviyam 44","lb":"NG44","mm":1,"ev":"The sage reads the dream: a son will be born who will rule, take up austerity and end his karma in release.","cl":[["austerity cuts off the fierce deeds of past births and so wins final release","jain","asserted",4,"muni"]],"ag":["மாதவன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["மாதவன்","மன்னன்"],"ch":"ng:3"},"ng:45":{"book":"naga_kumara_kaviyam","num":46,"t":["தனையன்வந் துதித்த பின்னைத் தகுகுறிப் புண்டோ வென்று","புனைமல ரலங்கல் மார்பன் புரவலன் மற்றுங் கேட்ப","நினைமினக் குறிக ளுண்டென் னேர்மையிற் கேட்பி ராயின்","தினையனைப் பற்று மில்லாத் திகம்பர னியம்பு கின்றான்."],"p":"","r":"The king asks whether there are signs after the son is born, and a naked sage, free of the smallest attachment, says that he will tell them if they listen with honesty.","n":"திகம்பரன் (sky-clad) is the sage's own title, so the speaker here may be a different monk from the one in 44; the verse does not say.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kurippu","thuravu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Naga Kumara Kaviyam 45","lb":"NG45","mm":1,"ev":"The king asks for signs of the son's coming; a Digambara sage agrees to name them.","cl":[],"ag":["மன்னன்","திகம்பரன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["திகம்பரன்","மன்னன்"],"ch":"ng:3"},"ng:46":{"book":"naga_kumara_kaviyam","num":47,"t":["பொன்னெயிலுள் வீற்றிருக்கும் புனிதன் றிருக்கோயில்","நின்சிறுவன் சரணத்தா னீங்குந் திருக்கதவம்","நன்னாக வாவிதனின் னழுவப் பதமுண்டாம்","மன்னாக மாவினொடு மதமடக்கிச் செலுத்திடுவான்."],"p":"","r":"The sage gives the signs: the doors of the golden-walled Jina's temple will open at the touch of the son's foot, he will rise from the pond of nagas, and with the king of nagas he will curb pride.","n":"Lines 3-4 are obscure (நன்னாக வாவிதனில் அழுவப் பதமுண்டாம், மன்னாக மாவினொடு மதமடக்கி): whether the nagas are the pond's serpents or a naga king, and whose pride is curbed, is not fixed.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kurippu","iraivan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Naga Kumara Kaviyam 46","lb":"NG46","mm":1,"ev":"The sage lists signs by which the son will be known.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["திகம்பரன்","மன்னன்"],"ch":"ng:3"},"ng:47":{"book":"naga_kumara_kaviyam","num":48,"t":["அருள்முனி யருளக்கேட்டு வரசன்றன் றேவிதன்னோ","டிருவரு மிறைஞ்சியேத்தி யெழின்மனைக் கெழுந்துவந்து","பருமுகிற் றவழுமாடப் பஞ்சநல் லமளிதன்னிற்","திருநிகர் மாதுமன்னன் சேர்ந்தினி திருக்குமந்நாள்."],"p":"","r":"The king and queen bow and praise the sage, return to their fine house and live happily on the bed in the upper hall.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inbam","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Naga Kumara Kaviyam 47","lb":"NG47","mm":1,"ev":"The royal couple leave the temple and return to their palace.","cl":[],"ag":["மன்னன்","பிரிதிவிழ் தேவி"],"ec":[],"ch":"ng:3"},"ng:48":{"book":"naga_kumara_kaviyam","num":49,"t":["புண்டவழ் வேற்கண் கோதை பூரண மயற்கைச் சின்னம்","மண்ணினி துண்ண வெண்ணு மைந்தன்பூ வலய மாளும்","பண்ணுகக் கிளவி வாயிற் பரவிய தீருஞ் சேரும்","கண்ணிய மிச்ச மின்னைக் கழித்திடு முறுப்பி தாமே."],"p":"","r":"The queen, in the first mark of pregnancy, longs to eat earth, and the verse says the son will rule the world, with some words of the rest too damaged to read.","n":"The verse is badly corrupt after line 2; only the craving for earth (மண்ணினிது உண்ண) and the son who will rule the world are clear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["makkal","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Naga Kumara Kaviyam 48","lb":"NG48","mm":1,"ev":"The queen shows the craving of pregnancy and the child's rule is foretold.","cl":[],"ag":["பிரிதிவிழ் தேவி"],"ec":[],"ch":"ng:3"},"ng:49":{"book":"naga_kumara_kaviyam","num":50,"t":["திங்க ளொன்பான் நிறைந்து செல்வனற் றினத்திற் றோன்றப்","பொங்குநீர்க் கடல்போல் மன்னன் புரிந்துநல் லுவகை யாகித்","தங்குபொன் னறைதி றந்து தரணியுள் ளவர்க்குச் சிந்திச்","சிங்கநேர் சிறுவ னாமம் சீர்பிரதா பந்த னென்றார்."],"p":"","r":"In the ninth month the son is born on a good day, the king opens his golden store and scatters wealth over the land, and the boy is named Pirathapan.","n":"The child is named Pirathapan (சீர்பிரதாபந்தன்) here, and only after the pond is he called Naga Kumaran in 53.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["makkal","eegai","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Naga Kumara Kaviyam 49","lb":"NG49","mm":1,"ev":"The son is born, the king gives out gold and names him Pirathapan.","cl":[],"ag":["மன்னன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ng:3"},"ng:50":{"book":"naga_kumara_kaviyam","num":51,"t":["பிரிதிவிழ் தேவி யோர்நாள் பெருங்குழுத் தேவி மாரும்","அரியநற் பரமன் கோயி லன்புடன் போக வெண்ணி","விரிநிற மலருஞ் சாந்தும் வேண்டிய பலவு மேந்திப்","பரிவுள தனையற் கொண்டு பாங்கினாற் சென்ற வன்றே."],"p":"","r":"One day the queen and the chief queens decide to go to the Lord's temple, take flowers and sandal and the child with them, and set out.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","makkal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Naga Kumara Kaviyam 50","lb":"NG50","mm":1,"ev":"The queens carry offerings and the baby to the temple.","cl":[],"ag":["பிரிதிவிழ் தேவி"],"ec":[],"ch":"ng:3"},"ng:51":{"book":"naga_kumara_kaviyam","num":52,"t":["சிறுவன்றன் சரணந் தீண்டச் சினாலயங் கதவு நீங்கப்","பிறைநுதற் றாதி தானும் பிள்ளைவிட் டுட்பு குந்தாள்","நறைமலர் வாவி தன்னு ணற்சுதன் வீழக் காணாச்","சிறையழி காதற் றாயுஞ் சென்றுடன் வீழ்ந்தா ளன்றே."],"p":"","r":"As the child's foot touches it, the Jina temple's doors swing open; the queen leaves the child and goes in, and the boy falls into the flower pond, at which the mother, unable to bear it, plunges in after him.","n":"The sage's sign in 46 (doors opening, the pond) is fulfilled; a mother's loving leap into water is given as plain feeling, with no comment on it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["makkal","anbu","kurippu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Naga Kumara Kaviyam 51","lb":"NG51","mm":1,"ev":"The temple doors open at the infant's touch, he falls into the pond, and his mother leaps in after him.","cl":[],"ag":["பிரிதிவிழ் தேவி","நாககுமரன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ng:3"},"ng:52":{"book":"naga_kumara_kaviyam","num":53,"t":["கறைகெழு வேலி னான்றன் காரிகை நீர்மே னிற்பப்","பிறையெயிற் றரவின் மீது பெற்றிருந் தனையற் கண்டு","பறையிடி முரச மார்ப்பப் பாங்கினா லெடுத்து வந்து","இறைவனை வணங்கி யேத்தி யியன்மனை புகுந்தா னன்றே."],"p":"","r":"Seeing the queen standing in the water and the child lying safe on the serpent's hood, the people carry him out to the sound of drums, bow to the Lord and go home.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["makkal","iraivan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Naga Kumara Kaviyam 52","lb":"NG52","mm":1,"ev":"The child is found on a serpent's hood and brought out with drums and worship.","cl":[],"ag":["பிரிதிவிழ் தேவி","மன்னன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ng:3"},"ng:53":{"book":"naga_kumara_kaviyam","num":54,"t":["நாகத்தின் சிரசின் மீது நன்மையிற் றரித்தென் றெண்ணி","நாகநற் குமர னென்று நரபதி நாமஞ் செய்தான்","நாகநே ரகலத் தானை நாமகட் சேர்த்தி யின்ப","நாகவிந் திரனைப் போல நரபதி யிருக்க மந்நாள்."],"p":"","r":"Thinking him held kindly on the serpent's head, the king names him Nagakumaran; the Naga-chested one joins him with Lakshmi and lives like Nagendra, and then comes the day of the next episode.","n":"Line 3 is obscure about who is joined with Nāmakal (நாமகள், the goddess of learning); the king's act of naming is plain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["makkal","arasu","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Naga Kumara Kaviyam 53","lb":"NG53","mm":1,"ev":"The king renames the child Naga Kumaran because the serpent held him.","cl":[],"ag":["மன்னன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ng:3"},"ng:54":{"book":"naga_kumara_kaviyam","num":55,"t":["கின்னரி மனோக ரீயென் கெணிகைநற் கன்னி மாரும்","அன்னவர் தாயும் வந்தே யரசனைக் கண்டு ரைப்பார்","என்னுடைச் சுதையர் கீத மிறைவநின் சிறுவன் காண்க","என்றவள் கூற நன்றென் றினிதுடன் கேட்கின்றாரே."],"p":"","r":"The courtesan girls Kinnari and Manokari and their mother come to the king and she asks him to hear her daughters' music, which he agrees to hear.","n":"Whether கின்னரி and மனோகரி name two girls or a girl and her mother is not clear from the verse.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kalai","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Naga Kumara Kaviyam 54","lb":"NG54","mm":1,"ev":"The courtesans and their mother present their daughters' song before the king and the prince.","cl":[],"ag":["கெணிகை","கன்னிமார்","மன்னன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["கெணிகை","மன்னன்"],"ch":"ng:3"},"ng:55":{"book":"naga_kumara_kaviyam","num":56,"t":["இசையறி குமரன் கேட்டே யிளையவள் கீத நன்றென்","றசைவிலா மன்னன் றானு மதிசய மனத்த னாகித்","திசைவிளக் கனையாள் மூத்தாள் தெரிந்துநீ யென்கொ லென்ன","வசையின்றி மூத்தா டன்னை மனோகரி நோக்கக் கண்டேன்."],"p":"","r":"The music-knowing prince finds the younger girl's song good, and the king, astonished, asks him which of the two is the elder, the answer given that Manokari looked at the elder without fault.","n":"Lines 3-4 give the speakers' turns without marks; who says 'I saw Manokari look at the elder' is a guess.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kalai","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Naga Kumara Kaviyam 55","lb":"NG55","mm":1,"ev":"The prince praises the younger singer; the king asks him about the elder.","cl":[],"ag":["நாககுமரன்","மன்னன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["நாககுமரன்","மன்னன்"],"ch":"ng:3"},"ng:56":{"book":"naga_kumara_kaviyam","num":57,"t":["பலகல மணிந்த வல்குற் பஞ்சநற் சுகந்த னீயும்","துலங்குதன் சுதையர் தம்மை தூய்மணிக் குமரற் கீந்தாள்","அலங்கல்வேற் குமரன் றானு மாயிழை மாதர் தாமும்","புலங்களின் மிகுத்த போகம் புணர்ந்தின்பக் கடலு ளாழ்ந்தார்."],"p":"","r":"The mother gives her daughters to the pure-gemmed prince, and he and the women together sink into a sea of pleasure that fills the senses.","n":"பஞ்சநற் சுகந்தன் is taken as the mother's name or title; the verse does not gloss it.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Naga Kumara Kaviyam 56","lb":"NG56","mm":1,"ev":"The mother gives her daughters to Naga Kumaran, and they enjoy pleasure together.","cl":[],"ag":["கெணிகை","நாககுமரன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ng:3"},"ng:57":{"book":"naga_kumara_kaviyam","num":58,"t":["நாகமிக் கதங்கொண் டோடி நகர்மாட மழித்துச் செல்ல","நாகநற் குமரன் சென்று நாகத்தை யடக்கிக் கொண்டு","வேகத்தின் விட்டு வந்து வேந்தநீ கொள்க வென்ன","வாகுநற் சுதனை நோக்கி யானைநீ கைக்கொள் ளென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"The prince goes after a maddened elephant that has been wrecking the city's mansions, subdues it, lets it go to the king, and the king bids his other son keep it.","n":"The rut that the prince masters is the same maddened state (மதம்) that the sage's prophecy in verse 46 had named, so the feat reads as the fulfilment of a foretold sign. Line 3 and 4 do not make clear which son the king addresses (வாகுநற் சுதன்): the reading follows the word order.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vendhan","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Naga Kumara Kaviyam 57","lb":"NG57","mm":1,"ev":"Naga Kumaran subdues the elephant that has run amok in the city and offers it to the king, who tells his son to take it.","cl":[],"ag":["நாககுமரன்","சயந்தரன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ng:4"},"ng:58":{"book":"naga_kumara_kaviyam","num":59,"t":["மற்றோர்நாட் குமரன் றுட்ட மாவினை யடக்கி மேற்கொண்","டுற்றவூர் வீதி தோறு மூர்ந்துதீக் கோடி யாட்டி","வெற்றிவேல் வேந்தற் காட்ட விழைந்துநீ கொள்க வென்றான்","பற்றியே கொண்டு போகிப் பவனத்திற் சேர்த்தி னானே."],"p":"","r":"On another day the prince masters a vicious horse, rides it through every street with a fire-brand and shows it to the victorious king, who tells him to keep it; he takes it home.","n":"Line 2 (தீக்கோடி ஆட்டி) is not clear; it is read here as a flourish with fire while riding.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vendhan","aanmai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Naga Kumara Kaviyam 58","lb":"NG58","mm":1,"ev":"Naga Kumaran tames a vicious horse, parades it through the streets and presents it to the king, who lets him keep it.","cl":[],"ag":["நாககுமரன்","சயந்தரன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ng:4"},"ng:59":{"book":"naga_kumara_kaviyam","num":60,"t":["அறவுரை யருளிச் செய்த வம்முனி குறித்த நான்குந்","திறவதி னெய்தி நல்ல சீர்கலைக் கடலை நீந்திப்","படுமதக் களிறுந் தேர்மா புகழ்பெற வூர்ந்து மூன்றாம்","பிறையது போல்வ ளர்ந்து பீடுடைக் குமர னானான்."],"p":"","r":"Having gained the four things the sage's teaching of dharma had pointed to, the prince crosses the sea of learning, rides elephants and chariot horses with renown, and grows like the waxing moon into a distinguished youth.","n":"The 'four' (நான்கு) are not named in the verse; they are taken as the four things the sage foretold or set out in the earlier teaching. The verse ties worldly mastery to the sage's words and not to the prince's own effort alone.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kalvi","aram","ilamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Naga Kumara Kaviyam 59","lb":"NG59","mm":1,"ev":"Naga Kumaran grows up, masters the arts and the riding of elephants and horses, and comes of age.","cl":[["a prince who follows the sage's teaching of dharma gains the four ends and mastery of learning","jain","asserted",1,"narrator"]],"ag":["நாககுமரன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ng:4"},"ng:60":{"book":"naga_kumara_kaviyam","num":61,"t":["தூசுநீர் விசாலக் கண்ணி சுதனைக்கண் டினிது ரைப்பாள்","தேசநற் புரங்க ளெங்குந் திகழ்பணி குமரன் கீர்த்திப்","பேசொணா வகையிற் கேட்டேன் பெருந்தவ மில்லை நீயும்","ஏசுற விகழொன் றின்றி யினியுனைக் காக்க வென்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"Visalakanni, seeing her son, tells him kindly that she has heard the prince's fame spread beyond telling through all the lands, that she has no great merit, and that he should so act that no blame touches him and he be protected.","n":"Line 3 (பெருந்தவம் இல்லை நீயும்) can be read as the speaker's own lack of merit or a charge that her son lacks it; the speaker's tone, kindly or envious, is not fixed. The son (சுதன்) is taken as Srithara, because verse 61 turns to him.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kurippu","perumai","manam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Naga Kumara Kaviyam 60","lb":"NG60","mm":1,"ev":"Visalanetri speaks to her son Srithara of Naga Kumaran's spreading fame.","cl":[],"ag":["விசாலநேத்திரை"],"ec":[],"spk":["விசாலநேத்திரை","சிரிதரன்"],"ch":"ng:4"},"ng:61":{"book":"naga_kumara_kaviyam","num":62,"t":["சிரிதரன் கேட்டு நெஞ்சிற் செய்பொரு ளென்னென் றேகி","குறிகொண் டாயி ரத்தினோரைக் கொன்றிடு மொருவ னாகச்","செறியுமைஞ் ஞூறு பேருஞ் சீர்மையிற் கரத்தி னாரை","யறிவினிற் கூட்டிக் கொண்டு வமர்ந்தினி திருக்கு மந்நாள்."],"p":"","r":"Srithara, hearing this, resolves in his heart what to do, and gathers five hundred men of skilled hands, one of whom is to be the killer of a thousand, and stays on happily until the day comes.","n":"The lines on the thousand and five hundred are obscure; whether the men are assassins or a guard is not settled by the verse itself, and the plot is a reading from the next verses.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["soozhchi","vanjam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Naga Kumara Kaviyam 61","lb":"NG61","mm":1,"ev":"Srithara plans against Naga Kumaran and assembles five hundred skilled men.","cl":[],"ag":["சிரிதரன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ng:4"},"ng:62":{"book":"naga_kumara_kaviyam","num":63,"t":["குமரனுநன் மாதருங் குச்சமென்னும் வாவியுள்","மமரநீரி லாடவே வன்னமாலை குங்குமஞ்","சுமரவேந்திப் பட்டுடன் றோழிகொண்டு போகையிற்","சமையுமாட மீமிசைச் சயந்தர னிருந்ததே."],"p":"","r":"The prince and his fair wives bathe in the pond called Kuccam while a maid carries garlands, saffron and silk; meanwhile Jayandhara sits on the upper floor of his mansion.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","mannar"],"pos":[],"nm":"Naga Kumara Kaviyam 62","lb":"NG62","mm":1,"ev":"Naga Kumaran bathes in the pond with his wives while the king watches from the upper floor.","cl":[],"ag":["நாககுமரன்","சயந்தரன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ng:4"},"ng:63":{"book":"naga_kumara_kaviyam","num":64,"t":["வேந்தன்பக் கங்கூறுநல் விசாலநேத் திரையவள்","போந்தனள் மனைவியாற் புணருஞ்சோரன் றன்னிடம்","பூந்தடத்தைச் சுற்றிய பொற்புடைக் கரைமிசை","யேந்திழையா ணிற்பக்கண் டினிச்சுதன் பணிந்ததே."],"p":"","r":"The king's queen Visalanetri comes to the pond's bank and stands there, and the prince bows to her on seeing her.","n":"Line 2 speaks of a thief who couples with another's wife; it suggests a charge laid against the prince but the syntax is broken, so the accusation is only inferred from verses 64 and 65.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["panivu","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Naga Kumara Kaviyam 63","lb":"NG63","mm":1,"ev":"Visalanetri comes to the pond's bank where Naga Kumaran sees her and bows.","cl":[],"ag":["விசாலநேத்திரை","நாககுமரன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ng:4"},"ng:64":{"book":"naga_kumara_kaviyam","num":65,"t":["பொய்யுரை புனைந்தவளைப் புரவலனுஞ் சீறினான்","நையுமிடை மாதரு நாகநற் குமரனும்","செய்யமாலை சாந்துபட்டுச் செம்மையுடன் றாங்கியே","வெய்யவேற்கண் டாயுடன் வியன்மனை யடைந்தனன்."],"p":"","r":"The king rages at the woman who has spun falsehood, while the prince and his slender wife, adorned with garlands and sandal, go back with the attendant to their great house.","n":"The verse says plainly that a lie was fabricated (பொய்யுரை புனைந்தவளை) but not who is accused or who lies; the king's anger may fall on the liar or on the person she slandered.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vaaimai","nindhai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Naga Kumara Kaviyam 64","lb":"NG64","mm":1,"ev":"The king is enraged at the woman's lie; the prince and his wife return to their house.","cl":[],"ag":["சயந்தரன்","நாககுமரன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ng:4"},"ng:65":{"book":"naga_kumara_kaviyam","num":66,"t":["மன்னன்றன் தேவியை மாதேயெங்கு போனதென்","நின்னுடைப் புதல்வனீ ராடற்காணப் போனதென்","நின்னுடன் மனைதனி லீண்டினிதி னாடலென்","நந்நகர்ப் புறத்தனைய னாடனீங்க வென்றனன்."],"p":"","r":"The king asks his queen where she had gone, why she went to watch her son bathe, why he plays with her in the house, and tells that he must go and play outside the city.","n":"The addressee (தேவி) and whose son is meant are not clear; the verse is read as the king sending Naga Kumaran to live outside the capital, which verse 73 then carries out.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["arasu","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Naga Kumara Kaviyam 65","lb":"NG65","mm":1,"ev":"Jayandhara questions the queen and orders the prince out of the city to play.","cl":[],"ag":["சயந்தரன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சயந்தரன்","விசாலநேத்திரை"],"ch":"ng:4"},"ng:66":{"book":"naga_kumara_kaviyam","num":67,"t":["அரசனுரைத் தேகினா னகமகிழ்வு மின்றியே","சிரசிறங்கித் துக்கமாய்ச் சீர்கரத் திருந்தனள்","விரகுநற் குமரனும் வியந்துவந்து கேட்டனன்","அரசனுரை சொல்லக்கேட் டானைமிசை யேறினான்."],"p":"","r":"The king leaves after speaking; the queen bows her head, joyless and grieving; the prince, clever, comes in wonder and asks her, and on hearing the king's word he mounts an elephant.","n":"","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","penvazhi","manam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Naga Kumara Kaviyam 66","lb":"NG66","mm":1,"ev":"The queen grieves at the king's order; Naga Kumaran asks her, hears of the order and mounts an elephant.","cl":[],"ag":["சயந்தரன்","நாககுமரன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ng:4"},"ng:67":{"book":"naga_kumara_kaviyam","num":68,"t":["வாத்திய முழங்கவு மதவாரண மடக்கவும்","ஏத்தரிய வீதிதொறு மீடில்வட்ட சாரியும்","பார்த்தரிய நடனமும் பல்லியங்க ளார்ப்பவே","சீற்றமொ டுலாச்செலச் சீரரசன் கேட்டனன்."],"p":"","r":"With drums sounding, the prince subdues a rutting elephant and displays circling riding in every street to the sound of many instruments, and the king hears of the angry procession.","n":"The prince's riding is a show of his mastery in the face of the order to leave; the verse does not say he is defying it, but the king takes it as such in verse 68.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["perumai","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Naga Kumara Kaviyam 67","lb":"NG67","mm":1,"ev":"Naga Kumaran parades on a rutting elephant through the streets and the king hears of it.","cl":[],"ag":["நாககுமரன்","சயந்தரன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ng:4"},"ng:68":{"book":"naga_kumara_kaviyam","num":69,"t":["நன்னடியார் சொல்லினர் நாகநற் குமரனென்","இன்னுரையை மீறின னினியவன் மனைபுகுந்து","பொன்னணிக ணற்பொரு ணாடிமிக் கவர்கொள","என்றரசன் கூறலு மினப்பொருள் கவர்ந்தனர்."],"p":"","r":"Servants tell the king that the prince has overstepped his sweet word; the king bids them enter his house and take the golden ornaments and goods, and they seize them.","n":"The verse gives no sign that the story judges the stripping unjust or just; it reports the king's order as an act of sceptre, and verses 69 to 72 show the prince turning it round by dice.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kodungol","arasu","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Naga Kumara Kaviyam 68","lb":"NG68","mm":1,"ev":"The king, told that the prince disobeyed, orders his house stripped of ornaments and goods, and the servants take them.","cl":[],"ag":["சயந்தரன்","நன்னடியார்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சயந்தரன்",""],"ch":"ng:4"},"ng:69":{"book":"naga_kumara_kaviyam","num":70,"t":["ஆடுவா ரணமிசை யண்ணல்வந் திழிதர","நீடுமா ளிகையடைய நீர்மைநற்றாய் கூறலும்","ஆடுஞ்சூது மனைபுகுந் தரசர்தம்மை வென்றபின்","கூடுமா பரணமே குமரன் கொண்டி யேகினான்.;"],"p":"","r":"The prince comes down from his elephant, his mother tells him what has happened, and he goes into the house to play at dice, beats the kings, and takes away the ornaments that gather.","n":"The text of line 4 ends in a stray punctuation mark; the order of events is hard to fix.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["soodhu","aanmai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Naga Kumara Kaviyam 69","lb":"NG69","mm":1,"ev":"On his mother's telling Naga Kumaran plays dice with the kings, beats them and carries off their ornaments.","cl":[],"ag":["நாககுமரன்","நற்றாய்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ng:4"},"ng:70":{"book":"naga_kumara_kaviyam","num":71,"t":["அரசர்க ளனைவரு மதிகரா சனைத்தொழ","அரவமணி யாரமு மான முத்து மாலையும்","கரமதிற் கடகமுங் காய்பொற்கே யூரமும்","வெரிமணிக ளிலதைவேந் தென்னவிக் கூற்றென."],"p":"","r":"All the kings, at the great king's sight, bow, with serpent-gem necklaces, pearl garlands, bracelets and armlets, and a cry goes up about the matter.","n":"Line 4 (வேந்தென்ன இக்கூற்றென) is corrupt or obscure; the sense is a guess.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["mannar","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Naga Kumara Kaviyam 70","lb":"NG70","mm":1,"ev":"The kings bow before the king with their jewels gathered.","cl":[],"ag":["அரசர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ng:4"},"ng:71":{"book":"naga_kumara_kaviyam","num":72,"t":["சூதினாற் செயித்துநின் சுதனணிகள் கொண்டனன்","சூதிலாட வென்னுடன் சுதனழைப்ப வந்தபின்","சூதினிற் றுடங்கிநற் சுதனுந்தந்தை யன்பினிற்","சூதிரண்டி லாட்டினுஞ் சுதன்மிகச் செயித்தனன்."],"p":"","r":"A speaker tells the king that by dice he has won the prince's ornaments, that the prince invited him to play and then, out of love for his father, beat him greatly in two games.","n":"The speaker is not named; he is read as one of the kings who played, but the verse could also be the king's own account.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["soodhu","anbu","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Naga Kumara Kaviyam 71","lb":"NG71","mm":1,"ev":"A king reports to Jayandhara that he won the prince's jewels at dice and then lost twice to him.","cl":[],"ag":["நாககுமரன்","அரசர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["அரசர்","சயந்தரன்"],"ch":"ng:4"},"ng:72":{"book":"naga_kumara_kaviyam","num":73,"t":["இனியசூதி லாடலுக் கிசைந்ததேச மன்னரை","இனியதாயப் பொருள்களை யியல்பினாற் கொடுத்துடன்","தனையனும் மனைபுகுந்து தாய்பொருட் கொடுத்தபின்","அணியரச ராரமு மவரவர்க் களித்தனன்."],"p":"","r":"The prince gives each king who had agreed to play the hereditary goods that rightly belong to them, returns home and hands the rest to his mother, and gives the kings their own necklaces back.","n":"The dice are the means by which a prince put out of his goods wins goods back, and the story ends not in keeping them but in giving them away, which the Kural's warning on gambling does not allow for.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","aram","anbu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Naga Kumara Kaviyam 72","lb":"NG72","mm":1,"ev":"Naga Kumaran returns the kings' hereditary property and necklaces, and gives the rest to his mother.","cl":[],"ag":["நாககுமரன்","அரசர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ng:4"},"ng:73":{"book":"naga_kumara_kaviyam","num":74,"t":["மன்னவன்றன் னேவலான் மாநகர்ப் புறத்தினின்","நன்னகர் சமைத்தினிதின் நற்சுத னிருக்கவென்","றன்னகரி னாமமு மலங்கரிய புரமெனத்","தன்னகரின் மேவுங்பொற் றாரணிந்த காளையே."],"p":"","r":"By the king's command a fine city is built outside the great city for the prince to live in, and it is called Alankarapuram; the youth wears a gold garland there.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naadu","vendhan","perumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Naga Kumara Kaviyam 73","lb":"NG73","mm":1,"ev":"Jayandhara orders a city built outside the capital where Naga Kumaran lives, named Alankarapuram.","cl":[],"ag":["சயந்தரன்","நாககுமரன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ng:4"},"ng:74":{"book":"naga_kumara_kaviyam","num":75,"t":["அரிவையர் போகந் தன்னி லானநற் குமரன் றானும்","பிரிவின்றி விடாது புல்லிப் பெருமலர்க் காவு சேர்ந்து","பரிவுட னினிதி னாடிப் பாங்கினாற் செல்லு நாளில்","உரிமையாற் றோழர்வந்து சேர்ந்தது கூற லுற்றேன்."],"p":"","r":"The poet says the prince, bound to his pleasures with women, wanders happily from garden to garden, and then turns to tell what his companion brought to him.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","thunai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Naga Kumara Kaviyam 74","lb":"NG74","mm":1,"ev":"Naga Kumara spends his days in pleasure gardens with women until his friend comes with news.","cl":[],"ag":["நாகநற் குமரன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ng:5"},"ng:75":{"book":"naga_kumara_kaviyam","num":76,"t":["பாரணி சூர சேனம் பண்ணுதற் கரிய நாட்டுள்","ஊரணி கொடிக ளோங்கு முத்தர மதுரை தன்னில்","வாரணி கொங்கை மார்க்கு மாரனேர் செயவர் மாவின்","சீரணி தேவி நாமஞ் செயவதி யென்ப தாகும்."],"p":"","r":"The story shifts to Uttara Madurai in the Surasena land, where the king's queen is named Jayavathi.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naadu","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Naga Kumara Kaviyam 75","lb":"NG75","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"ng:5"},"ng:76":{"book":"naga_kumara_kaviyam","num":77,"t":["வேய்ந்தவெம் முலையாள் பக்கல் வியாளமா வியாள ரென்னுஞ்","சேர்ந்திரு புதல்வர் தோன்றிச் செவ்வியாற் செல்லு நாளில்","காந்திநற் றவத்தோர் வந்தார் கடவுணேர் தூம சேனர்","வேந்தன்வந் தடிவ ணங்கி விரித்தொன்று வினவி னானே."],"p":"","r":"Two sons, Vyala and Mahavyala, are born to the queen, and when the sage Dhumasena comes the king bows and puts a question to him.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["makkal","thavam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Naga Kumara Kaviyam 76","lb":"NG76","mm":1,"ev":"The sage Dhumasena visits the king of Uttara Madurai, who bows and asks him a question.","cl":[],"ag":["செயவர்","தூம சேனர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ng:5"},"ng:77":{"book":"naga_kumara_kaviyam","num":78,"t":["என்னுடையப் புதல்வர் தாமு மினியர சாளு மொன்றோ","அன்னியன் சேவை யொன்றோ வடிகணீ ரருளிச் செய்மின்","துன்னிய புதல்வர் தாமு மொருவனைச் சேவை பண்ணும்","என்றவர் குறியுஞ் சொல்ல யெழின்முடி புதல்வர்க் கீந்தான்."],"p":"","r":"The king asks the sage whether his sons will rule or serve another, hears that they will serve one man, and gives them the crown.","n":"The sage's answer is told only in outline; the verse does not name the man the sons will serve, so the prophecy is open until later verses.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","makkal","soozhchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Naga Kumara Kaviyam 77","lb":"NG77","mm":1,"ev":"Told by the sage that his sons will serve one man, the king crowns his sons.","cl":[["a sage's sign tells what will befall a king's sons","general","asserted",3,"dhumasenar"]],"ag":["செயவர்","தூம சேனர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["செயவர்","தூம சேனர்"],"ch":"ng:5"},"ng:78":{"book":"naga_kumara_kaviyam","num":79,"t":["மன்னன்போய் வனம டைந்து மாமுனி யாகி நிற்பப்","பின்னவ ரமைச்சன் றன்மேற் பெருநிலப் பாரம் வைத்துத்","தன்னிறை தேடிப் போந்தார் தரைமகட் டிலதம் போலும்","பன்னக நகர நேராம் பாடலி புரம தாமே."],"p":"","r":"The king goes to the forest and stands as a great ascetic, while the sons leave the land's burden with a minister and go seeking a lord of their own at Pataliputra.","n":"The king leaves only after he has put the crown on his sons, so renunciation follows the duty of rule rather than replacing it; the sons in turn hand the rule to a minister, and the verse does not say whether the leaving is by choice or by the sage's word.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thuravu","thavam","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Naga Kumara Kaviyam 78","lb":"NG78","mm":1,"ev":"The king becomes a forest ascetic and his sons leave the realm to a minister and travel to Pataliputra.","cl":[["a king who has settled the succession should leave the throne for forest austerity","general","asserted",1,"narrator"]],"ag":["செயவர்","வியாளன்","மாவியாளன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ng:5"},"ng:79":{"book":"naga_kumara_kaviyam","num":80,"t":["நன்னகர்க் கிறைவ னல்ல னாமஞ்சிரீ வர்ம னாகுந்","தன்னவன் றேவி பேருந் தக்கசிரீ மதியா மம்பொற்","கிண்ணம்போல் முலையாள் புத்ரி கேணிகாசுந் தரியென் பாளாம்","விண்ணுறை தேவர் போல வியாளமா வியாளர் வந்தார்."],"p":"","r":"Pataliputra's king is Srivarman, his queen Srimathi and his daughter Kenikasundari, and the two brothers arrive there like gods from heaven.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","naadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Naga Kumara Kaviyam 79","lb":"NG79","mm":1,"ev":"Vyala and Mahavyala reach Pataliputra, whose royal family is introduced.","cl":[],"ag":["வியாளன்","மாவியாளன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ng:5"},"ng:80":{"book":"naga_kumara_kaviyam","num":81,"t":["மன்ன னைக்கண் டிருப்ப மாவியாளன் றகமை கண்டு","தன்னுடையப் புதல்வி தன்னைத் தானவற் கொடுத்துத் தாதி","துன்னிய மகளி தன்னைச் சுந்தரி வியாள னுக்கு","மன்னியற் கொடுப்ப மன்ன ரிருவரு மின்புற் றாரே."],"p":"","r":"The king of Pataliputra, seeing the brother's worth, gives his daughter to one and a maid's daughter Sundari to Vyala, so both kings are glad.","n":"Who receives whom turns on the words தானவற்கு and தாதி துன்னிய மகள், which are not clear in the line; the reading follows the plain order and is not firm.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["perumai","oppuravu","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Naga Kumara Kaviyam 80","lb":"NG80","mm":1,"ev":"Srivarman gives his daughter to Mahavyala and the maid's daughter Sundari to Vyala.","cl":[],"ag":["சிரீவர்மன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ng:5"},"ng:81":{"book":"naga_kumara_kaviyam","num":82,"t":["சிறுதினஞ் சென்ற பின்பு சீருடன் வியாளன் போந்து","நறுமலர்க் கோதை வேலான் நாகநற் குமரற் கண்டு","சிறுமலர் நெற்றிக் கண்ணுஞ் சேரவே மறையக் கண்டு","சிறியன்யா னின்னா னென்றான் செல்வனு மகிழ்வுற் றானே."],"p":"","r":"After a few days Vyala sees Naga Kumara and the small eye on his forehead is hidden, and he declares himself the lesser man, which pleases the prince.","n":"Whether the hidden forehead eye is a mark Vyala recognises, and who says சிறியன் யான், depend on a few ambiguous words; the reading gives Vyala the speech.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["panivu","perumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Naga Kumara Kaviyam 81","lb":"NG81","mm":1,"ev":"Vyala meets Naga Kumara, sees the mark on his forehead and calls himself small, and the prince is glad.","cl":[],"ag":["வியாளன்","நாகநற் குமரன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["வியாளன்","நாகநற் குமரன்"],"ch":"ng:5"},"ng:82":{"book":"naga_kumara_kaviyam","num":83,"t":["செல்வனைக் கொல்வ தென்று சிரீதரன் சேனை வந்து","பல்சன மனையைச் சூழப் பண்புடை வியாளன் கண்டு","வல்லைநீர் வந்த தென்ன வள்ளலை வதைக்க வென்றார்","கொல்களி யானைக் கம்பங் கொண்டுடன் சாடி னானே."],"p":"","r":"Sridharan's soldiers come to kill the lord and surround the house, and Vyala asks why they came and strikes them with an elephant-post.","n":"The killing is done by one who guards a guest and the narrator calls Vyala well-mannered, so the slaughter is praised as loyalty rather than faulted as violence.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kolai","padai","aanmai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Naga Kumara Kaviyam 82","lb":"NG82","mm":1,"ev":"Sridharan's army surrounds the house to kill Naga Kumara and Vyala destroys it with an elephant tether-post.","cl":[],"ag":["சிரீதரன்","வியாளன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ng:5"},"ng:83":{"book":"naga_kumara_kaviyam","num":84,"t":["சேனைதன் மரணங் கேட்டு சிரீதரன் வெகுண்டு வந்தான்","ஆனைமேற் குமரன் றோன்றி யவனும்வந் தெதிர்த்த போது","மானவேன் மன்னன் கேட்டு மந்திரி தன்னை யேவ","கோனவர் குமரற் கண்டு கொலைத் தொழி லொழித்த தன்றே."],"p":"","r":"Hearing of his army's death Sridharan comes in anger, but when the prince appears before him on an elephant the king's minister is sent and the killing stops.","n":"","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vekuli","kolai","amaichu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Naga Kumara Kaviyam 83","lb":"NG83","mm":1,"ev":"Sridharan attacks in anger; Naga Kumara faces him on an elephant and the king sends a minister to end the fighting.","cl":[],"ag":["சிரீதரன்","நாகநற் குமரன்","சிரீவர்மன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ng:5"},"ng:84":{"book":"naga_kumara_kaviyam","num":85,"t":["நாகநற் குமரற் கண்டு நயந்தர னினிய கூறும்","வேகநின் மனைக்குச் சூரன் வெகுண்டவன் வந்தா னென்ன","போகநீ தேசத் தென்று புரவலன் சொன்னா னென்ன","ஆகவே யவன்முன் போகி லவ்வண்ணஞ் செய்வ னென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"The minister Nayantaran gently tells the prince that the angry hero has come to his house and that the king wishes him to leave the land, and the prince says he will do to him as before if he meets him.","n":"Whose command to leave, and who the angry hero is, depend on loose pronouns in lines 2 to 4; the verse leaves the prince's answer ambiguous.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["amaichu","maanam","anjaamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Naga Kumara Kaviyam 84","lb":"NG84","mm":1,"ev":"Nayantaran warns Naga Kumara to leave and the prince refuses to avoid a fight.","cl":[],"ag":["நயந்தரன்","நாகநற் குமரன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["நயந்தரன்","நாகநற் குமரன்"],"ch":"ng:5"},"ng:85":{"book":"naga_kumara_kaviyam","num":86,"t":["நயந்தரன் சென்று ரைப்பா னல்லறி வின்றி நீயே","செயந்தனி லொருவன் கையிற் சேனைதன் மரணங் கண்டும்","நயந்தறி யாத நீயே நன்மனை புகுக வென்றான்","பயந்துதன் சேனை யோடும் பவனத்திற் சென்ற வன்றே."],"p":"","r":"Nayantaran tells the enemy that he lacks sense, having seen his army killed by one man and still not understood, and bids him go home, so he goes frightened with his troops.","n":"The advice is a minister's face-saving retreat for a defeated lord, framed as a rebuke; the verse does not say the lord is wrong to feel shamed.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arivu","acham","pedhaimai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Naga Kumara Kaviyam 85","lb":"NG85","mm":1,"ev":"Nayantaran sends Sridharan home with a rebuke and Sridharan withdraws with his army.","cl":[["one who does not learn from seeing a rival's strength is without good sense","general","reported",1,"nayantaran"]],"ag":["நயந்தரன்","சிரீதரன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["நயந்தரன்","சிரீதரன்"],"ch":"ng:5"},"ng:86":{"book":"naga_kumara_kaviyam","num":87,"t":["தந்தையா லமைச்சன் சொல்லத் தானுந் தன்றாய்க் குரைத்து","தந்திமேன் மாதர் கூடத் தோழனுந் தானு மேறி","நந்திய வியாள னன்னூர் மதுரை யிற்புக் கிருந்து","அந்தமி லுவகை யெய்தி அமர்ந்தினி தொழுகு நாளில்."],"p":"","r":"On the minister's word from the father, the prince tells his mother, rides an elephant with the women and his friend, and settles happily in Vyala's Madurai.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inbam","thunai","naadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Naga Kumara Kaviyam 86","lb":"NG86","mm":1,"ev":"Naga Kumara tells his mother and travels with friend and women to Vyala's city, where they live in joy.","cl":[],"ag":["நாகநற் குமரன்","தோழன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ng:5"},"ng:87":{"book":"naga_kumara_kaviyam","num":88,"t":["மன்னவ குமரனு மன்னனுந் தோழனும்","அந்நகர்ப் புறத்தினி லாடன் மேவலின்","இன்னிசை வீணைவேந் திளையரைஞ் நூற்றுவர்","அன்னவர்க் கண்டுமிக் கண்ண லுரைத்தனன்."],"p":"","r":"The prince, the king and the friend go to play outside the city and come upon five hundred young men with veenas, and the prince speaks to them.","n":"","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["inbam","natpu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Naga Kumara Kaviyam 87","lb":"NG87","mm":1,"ev":"Naga Kumara's party meets five hundred veena-playing youths outside the city.","cl":[],"ag":["நாகநற் குமரன்","தோழன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ng:5"},"ng:88":{"book":"naga_kumara_kaviyam","num":89,"t":["எங்குளிர் யாவர்நீ ரெங்கினிப் போவதென்","றங்கவர் தம்முளே யறிந்தொரு வன்சொலுந்","தங்களூர் நாமமுந் தந்தைதாய் பேருரைத்","திங்கிவ ரென்கையின் வீணைகற் பவர்களே."],"p":"","r":"Questions are put to the newcomers about where they live, who they are and where they are going, and one who knows them names their town and the names of their father and mother, saying they are learners of the vina.","n":"The speakers of the question and of the answer are not named and the grammar of lines 2-4 is loose, so who answers and who is called vina-learners is a guess from the next verse.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kurippu","kalvi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Naga Kumara Kaviyam 88","lb":"NG88","mm":1,"ev":"Strangers are asked who they are and one answers for them, naming their home and parents.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"ng:6"},"ng:89":{"book":"naga_kumara_kaviyam","num":90,"t":["நந்துகாம் பீரநாட்டி னகருங் காம்பீர மென்னு","நந்தன ராசன் றேவி நாமந் தாரணியாம் புத்திரி","கந்தமார் திரிபுவ னாரதி கைவீணை யதனிற் றோற்று","என்தம ரோடுங் கூட வெங்களூர்க் கேறச் சென்றோம்."],"p":"","r":"The speakers say they come from the city Kampiram in the land Nandu-Kampira, whose king Nandana has a queen named Tharani and a daughter Tribhuvanarati, and that they and their kin have come to their own town because the princess's vina skill is boasted of.","n":"Lines 3-4 are hard to construe (கந்தமார்... தோற்று); the sense that the princess is a vina champion whom others come to try is taken from verse 90, which has the prince defeat her on the vina.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kalvi","kudi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Naga Kumara Kaviyam 89","lb":"NG89","mm":1,"ev":"The vina-learners tell the prince that the princess Tribhuvanarati of Kampiram is famed for her vina and that they went to her town.","cl":[],"ag":["வீணைகற்பவர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["வீணைகற்பவர்","நாகநற் குமரன்"],"ch":"ng:6"},"ng:90":{"book":"naga_kumara_kaviyam","num":91,"t":["வெற்றிவேற் குமரன் கேட்டு வியாள னுந் தானுஞ் சென்று","விற்புரு வதனத் தாளை வீணையின் வென்று கொண்டு","கற்புடை யவடன் காமக் கடலிடை நீந்து நாளில்","உற்றதோர் வணிக னைக்கண் டுவந்ததி சயத்தைக் கேட்டான்."],"p":"","r":"The prince hears this, goes with Viyalan, beats the bow-browed princess at the vina and wins her, and while he swims in the sea of love with her he meets a merchant and asks about a wonder the man has seen.","n":"The wager of the vina-contest is not stated in the verse; that defeat wins her is taken from வென்று கொண்டு.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalvi","kaamam","karpu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Naga Kumara Kaviyam 90","lb":"NG90","mm":1,"ev":"Naga Kumara and Viyalan win Tribhuvanarati by defeating her at the vina, and Kumara then questions a merchant about a marvel.","cl":[],"ag":["நாகநற் குமரன்","வியாளன்","திரிபுவனாரதி"],"ec":[],"ch":"ng:6"},"ng:91":{"book":"naga_kumara_kaviyam","num":92,"t":["தீதில்பூந் திலக மென்னுஞ் சினாலய மதனின் முன்னிற்","சோதிமிக் கிரணந் தோன்றுஞ் சூரிய னுச்சி காலம்","ஓதிய குரல னாகி யொருவனின் றலறு கின்றான்","ஏதுவென் றறியே னென்றா னெரிமணிக் கடகக் கையான்."],"p":"","r":"At noon, before the Jain temple named Thilaka, a man with a ringing voice stands crying out, and the arm-braceleted one says he does not know the cause.","n":"The speaker of line 4 is probably the merchant of 90, since he is the one asked about a wonder, but the verse does not say so.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kurippu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Naga Kumara Kaviyam 91","lb":"NG91","mm":1,"ev":"A man is heard crying at the Jain shrine at noon and the merchant says he does not know why.","cl":[],"ag":["வணிகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["வணிகன்","நாகநற் குமரன்"],"ch":"ng:6"},"ng:92":{"book":"naga_kumara_kaviyam","num":93,"t":["குன்றெனத் திரண்ட தோளான் குமரனுங் கேட்டுவந்து","சென்றந்த வால யத்திற் சினவரற் பணிந்து நின்று","வென்றந்த விமலன் மீது விரவிய துதிகள் சொல்லி","முன்னந்த மண்ட பத்தின் முகமலர்ந் தினிதி ருந்தான்."],"p":"","r":"Hearing it, the broad-shouldered prince goes into the shrine, bows to the Jina, praises the pure victor and sits at ease with a bright face in the front hall.","n":"Lines 3-4 give the prince's worship as an act, not an argument; the claim is the worship's premise.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","iraivan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Naga Kumara Kaviyam 92","lb":"NG92","mm":1,"ev":"Naga Kumara enters the Jain temple, worships the Jina and sits in its mandapa.","cl":[["the Jina, the victor, who is free of stain, is worthy of praise and worship","jain","reported",3,"nagakumaran"]],"ag":["நாகநற் குமரன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ng:6"},"ng:93":{"book":"naga_kumara_kaviyam","num":94,"t":["பூசலிட் டொருவன் கூவப் புரவல குமரன் கேட்டு","ஓசனிக் கின்ற தென்ன வொருதனி நின்ற நீயார்","ஆசையென் மனைவி தன்னை யதிபீம வசுரன் கொண்டு","பேசொணா மலைமு ழஞ்சுட் பிலத்தினில் வைத்தி ருந்தான்."],"p":"","r":"As a man cries out, the prince asks who he is, and the man says that the great demon Athibhima has seized his wife and shut her in a cave of burning mountain rock too awful to describe.","n":"ஆசை in line 3 may be the wife's name or the word for desire; verse 94 resolves who speaks.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["ivaral","kolai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Naga Kumara Kaviyam 93","lb":"NG93","mm":1,"ev":"A husband begs help because the demon Athibhima holds his wife in a mountain cave.","cl":[],"ag":["வேடன்","நாகநற் குமரன்","அதிபீமன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["வேடன்","நாகநற் குமரன்"],"ch":"ng:6"},"ng:94":{"book":"naga_kumara_kaviyam","num":95,"t":["இரம்மிய வனத்துள் வாழ்வே னிரம்மிய வேட னன்பேன்","விம்முறு துயர்சொற் கேட்டு வீரனக் குகைகாட் டென்னச்","செம்மையிற் சென்று காட்டச் செல்வனுஞ் சிறந்து போந்து","அம்மலைக் குகைவாய் தன்னி லண்ணலு முவந்து நின்றான்."],"p":"","r":"The man says he lives in the forest as the hunter Rammiya, and at his sorrowful word the prince asks to be shown the cave and, when the hunter has led him, rejoices at the cave mouth.","n":"The hunter's name is split across the line breaks of the edition; the reading Rammiya is from இரம்மிய வேட னன்பேன்.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arul","anbu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Naga Kumara Kaviyam 94","lb":"NG94","mm":1,"ev":"The hunter leads Naga Kumara to the cave where his wife is held.","cl":[],"ag":["வேடன்","நாகநற் குமரன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["வேடன்","நாகநற் குமரன்"],"ch":"ng:6"},"ng:95":{"book":"naga_kumara_kaviyam","num":96,"t":["வியந்தர தேவன் வந்து வந்தனை செய்து நிற்ப","விந்தநற் கிராதன் றேவி தனைவிடு வித்த பின்புச்","சந்திர காந்தி வாளுஞ் சாலமிக் கமளி தானுங்","கந்தநற் காம மென்னுங் கரண்டகங் கொடுத்த தன்றே."],"p":"","r":"A vyantara god comes and worships, the hunter's wife is freed, and the god gives the prince the Chandrakanti sword, a magical couch and a casket called Kamam.","n":"Whether the god or the prince defeats Athibhima is not said; the demon is not named after 93 and the god's gift follows the release.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["arul","eegai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Naga Kumara Kaviyam 95","lb":"NG95","mm":1,"ev":"The vyantara god frees the hunter's wife and gives Naga Kumara a sword, a magic couch and a casket.","cl":[],"ag":["வியந்தரதேவன்","நாகநற் குமரன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ng:6"},"ng:96":{"book":"naga_kumara_kaviyam","num":97,"t":["அங்குநின் றண்ணற் போந்து வதிசயங் கேட்ப வேடன்","இங்குள மலைவா ரத்தி லிரணிய குகையுண் டென்னக்","குங்கும மணிந்த மார்பன் குமரன்கேட் டங்குச் சென்றான்","அங்குள யியக்கி வந்து வடிபணிந் தினிது சொல்வாள்."],"p":"","r":"As the prince goes on and asks about further marvels, the hunter says a Hiranya cave lies in the hill nearby, and the saffron-chested prince goes there, where a yakshi comes, bows at his feet and speaks sweetly.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arul","kurippu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Naga Kumara Kaviyam 96","lb":"NG96","mm":1,"ev":"Naga Kumara goes to the Hiranya cave and a yakshi greets him.","cl":[],"ag":["நாகநற் குமரன்","வேடன்","இயக்கி"],"ec":[],"ch":"ng:6"},"ng:97":{"book":"naga_kumara_kaviyam","num":98,"t":["இனியுனக காள ரானோ மீரி ரண்டா யிரவர்","எனவவள் சொல்ல நன்றென் றினியொரு காரி யத்தின்","நினைவன்யா னங்கு வாவென் னீங்கிநற் குமரன் வந்து","வனசரன் றன்னைக் கண்டு வதிசயங் கேட்பச் சொல்வான்."],"p":"","r":"The yakshi says that four thousand attendants are now at his service, he agrees and says he will think of her when there is a task and she is to come then, and he leaves and asks a forester to tell him of marvels.","n":"ஈரிரண் டாயிரவர் is read as four thousand; the words ஈரிரண்டாயிரவர் could also be two thousands twice over, the same total, and the speaker of lines 2-3 is Kumara.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["ookkam","adimai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Naga Kumara Kaviyam 97","lb":"NG97","mm":1,"ev":"The yakshi offers Naga Kumara her four thousand servants and he promises to call them when needed.","cl":[],"ag":["இயக்கி","நாகநற் குமரன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["இயக்கி","நாகநற் குமரன்"],"ch":"ng:6"},"ng:98":{"book":"naga_kumara_kaviyam","num":99,"t":["வாள்கரஞ் சுழற்றி நிற்பான் வியந்தர னொருவ னென்னக்","காலினைப் பற்றி யீர்ப்பக் கனநிதி கண்டு காவ","லாளெனத் தெய்வம் வைத்து வருகனா லையத்துட் சென்று","தோளன தோழன் கூடத் தொல்கிரி புரத்தைச் சேர்ந்தான்."],"p":"","r":"A forester tells of a vyantara who stands whirling a sword; the prince, dragged by the foot, sees great treasure and a god set there as guard, then goes through the temple with his strong-armed friend to Giripuram.","n":"Who is pulled by the foot, and by whom, is not clear from the lines; the vyantara may be the treasure's guard or the tale told to the prince.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["selvam","kurippu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Naga Kumara Kaviyam 98","lb":"NG98","mm":1,"ev":"Naga Kumara finds a treasure guarded by a vyantara and then travels with his friend to the old city Giripuram.","cl":[],"ag":["நாகநற் குமரன்","வியந்தரன்","வியாளன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["வனசரன்","நாகநற் குமரன்"],"ch":"ng:6"},"ng:99":{"book":"naga_kumara_kaviyam","num":100,"t":["அந்நகர்க் கதிப னான வனராசன் றேவி தானு","மன்னிய முலையி னாள்பேர் வனமாலை மகணன் னாமம்","நன்னுதற் கணைவிழியை நாகநற் குமர னுக்குப்","பன்னரும் வேள்வி தன்னாற் பார்த்திபன் கொடுத்த தன்றே."],"p":"","r":"The king of that city, Vanaraja, and his queen Vanamalai gave their daughter of the arrow-like eyes to Naga Kumara through a great sacrifice.","n":"The daughter's name seems lost from line 2, where வனமாலை is the mother's name and மகள் நன்னாமம் is not followed by a name; the sacrifice (வேள்வி) is named as the means of the gift without comment.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["ilvaazhkkai","aram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Naga Kumara Kaviyam 99","lb":"NG99","mm":1,"ev":"Vanaraja gives his daughter in marriage to Naga Kumara at a sacrifice.","cl":[],"ag":["வனராசன்","வனமாலை"],"ec":[],"ch":"ng:6"},"ng:100":{"book":"naga_kumara_kaviyam","num":101,"t":["தாரணி வனரா சற்குத் தாயத்தா னொருவன் றன்னைச்","சீரணி குமரன் றோழன் சிறந்தணி மாமன் கூடப்","பாரணி வெற்றி கொண்டு புண்டர புரத்தை வாங்கி","யேரணி வனரா சற்கு யெழில்பெறக் கொடுத்த வன்றே."],"p":"","r":"Naga Kumara, with his friend and his uncle, defeats a kinsman of Vanaraja and wins the city Pundarapuram, which he gives to Vanaraja.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vendhan","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Naga Kumara Kaviyam 100","lb":"NG100","mm":1,"ev":"Naga Kumara conquers Pundarapuram from Vanaraja's rival kinsman and hands it to Vanaraja.","cl":[],"ag":["நாகநற் குமரன்","வியாளன்","வனராசன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ng:6"},"ng:101":{"book":"naga_kumara_kaviyam","num":102,"t":["சொல்லரு நாடி ழந்து சோமநற் பிரபன் போகி","யெல்லையிற் குணத்தின் மிக்க யெமதர ரடிவ ணங்கி","நல்லருட் சுரந்த ளிக்கு நற்றவ முனிவ னாகி","யொல்லையின் வினைகள் தீர யோகத்தைக் காத்து நின்றான்."],"p":"","r":"Somaprabha loses his land, bows to the feet of a Jain teacher called Yamadhara, becomes a sage of great mercy and austerity, and stands guarding his yoga so that his old deeds may end.","n":"The verse puts loss of the kingdom and the turn to renunciation side by side but does not say the loss caused the vow; that the vows end vinai is the narrator's own statement.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thuravu","thavam","vinai","yogam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Naga Kumara Kaviyam 101","lb":"NG101","mm":1,"ev":"Somaprabha, who lost his kingdom, takes up the ascetic's life under a Jain teacher.","cl":[["deeds accumulated in the past are made to end by austerity and yoga","jain","asserted",4,"narrator"],["the ascetic who has lost his throne turns to a teacher's feet for release","jain","asserted",2,"narrator"]],"ag":["சோமபிரபன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ng:6"},"ng:102":{"book":"naga_kumara_kaviyam","num":103,"t":["சுப்பிர திட்ட மெனும்புர மாள்பவன்","செப்பு வன்மை செயவர்ம ராசன்றன்","ஒப்பில் பாவையு மோவியம் போற்செம்பொன்","செப்பு நேர்முலை யாணற் செயவதி."],"p":"","r":"The poet introduces Subhrathishta's ruler Jayavarma and his wife Jayavati, whose beauty is likened to a painted figure.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Naga Kumara Kaviyam 102","lb":"NG102","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"ng:7"},"ng:103":{"book":"naga_kumara_kaviyam","num":104,"t":["மக்கட் சேத்திய பேத்திய ரென்றிவர்","மிக்க செல்வத்தின் மேன்மையிற் செல்லுநாள்","பக்க நோன்புடை பரம முனிவரர்","தொக்க ராசன் தொழுதிட் டிறைஞ்சினான்."],"p":"","r":"Jayavarma, living in great prosperity with his children, bows to a supreme ascetic of strict vows.","n":"Line 1 (மக்கட் சேத்திய பேத்திய ரென்றிவர்) is obscure in the printed text; that the king's household and children are meant is inferred from the next verse.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["arasu","thavam","panivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Naga Kumara Kaviyam 103","lb":"NG103","mm":1,"ev":"Jayavarma bows low before a supreme ascetic.","cl":[],"ag":["செயவர்ம ராசன்","பரம முனிவரர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ng:7"},"ng:104":{"book":"naga_kumara_kaviyam","num":105,"t":["இருவ ரென்சுத ரென்னுடை ராச்சிய","மருவி யாளுமோ மற்றொரு சேவையோ","திருவுளம் பற்றித் தேர்ந்தறி விக்கெனத்","திருமுடி மன்ன செப்புவன் கேளென்றார்."],"p":"","r":"The king asks the sage whether his two sons will hold and rule his kingdom or serve another, and the sage answers that he will tell it.","n":"The first three lines are the king's question; the last line gives the ascetic's reply in the poet's voice. The question treats the sons' future as something an ascetic's sight can read.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oozh","arasu","makkal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Naga Kumara Kaviyam 104","lb":"NG104","mm":1,"ev":"Jayavarma asks the ascetic whether his two sons will rule his kingdom or serve another, and the ascetic agrees to say.","cl":[],"ag":["செயவர்ம ராசன்","பரம முனிவரர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["செயவர்ம ராசன்","பரம முனிவரர்"],"ch":"ng:7"},"ng:105":{"book":"naga_kumara_kaviyam","num":106,"t":["புண்டிர மெனும்புரப் புரவ லன்றனைக்","கண்டிறந் துந்திடுங் காவ லன்றனை","யண்டிநற் சேவையா ராவ ராமெனப்","பண்டிறத் தவத்தவர் பண்ணுரை கேட்டபின்."],"p":"","r":"After the ascetic has spoken, the poet reports its sense: the sons will come to serve the protector who overthrows and drives out the ruler of Pundra.","n":"The prediction is given in reported speech, not in the sage's own words, and the line naming who will serve whom is hard (கண்டிறந் துந்திடுங் காவலன்); that the conqueror is Naga Kumara is not said here and is known from the story.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["oozh","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Naga Kumara Kaviyam 105","lb":"NG105","mm":1,"ev":"The ascetic foretells that the king's sons will become servants of the king who defeats the ruler of Pundra.","cl":[],"ag":["பரம முனிவரர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ng:7"},"ng:106":{"book":"naga_kumara_kaviyam","num":107,"t":["மக்கண் மிசைநில மன்னவன் வைத்துடன்","மிக்கு ணத்துவம் வீறுடன் கொண்டுதன்","நிற்கும் செவ்வினை நீங்க நின்றனர்","தக்க புத்திரர் தாரணி யாளுநாள்."],"p":"","r":"The king sets the land on his sons and stands to be rid of his remaining karma while the worthy sons take up the rule.","n":"The abdication follows the prophecy, so the father leaves the sons to the fate the sage read and does not fight it. The words நிற்கும் செவ்வினை நீங்க நின்றனர் are the poet's only statement of why he leaves; செவ்வினை (here read as the deeds still to bear fruit) and the subject of நின்றனர் (the king, or king and queen) are not fixed.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thuravu","vinai","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Naga Kumara Kaviyam 106","lb":"NG106","mm":1,"ev":"Jayavarma hands his kingdom to his sons and takes up the ascetic's life to wear away his karma, and the sons rule.","cl":[["the karma that still remains is removed by giving up rule and standing in penance","jain","asserted",3,"narrator"]],"ag":["செயவர்ம ராசன்","செயவர்மன் மக்கள்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ng:7"},"ng:107":{"book":"naga_kumara_kaviyam","num":108,"t":["நல்ல ருந்தவச் சோமப் பிரபரும்","எல்லை யில்குண விருடிக டம்முடன்","தொல்பு கழ்ப்புரஞ் சுப்பிர திட்டத்தின்","நல்ல காவி னயந்திருந் தார்களே."],"p":"","r":"The great ascetic Somaprabha and endless virtuous sages settle in the fine grove of Subhrathishta.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thavam","samayam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Naga Kumara Kaviyam 107","lb":"NG107","mm":1,"ev":"Somaprabha and other sages take up residence in the grove of Subhrathishta.","cl":[],"ag":["சோமப் பிரபர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ng:7"},"ng:108":{"book":"naga_kumara_kaviyam","num":109,"t":["செயவர் மன்சுதர் சீர்நற் றவர்களை","நயம றிந்துசேர் நன்னடி யைப்பணிந்","தியம்பு மிம்முனி யிப்ப துறந்ததென்","செயந்த ரன்சுதன் சீற்றத்தி னானதே."],"p":"","r":"Jayavarma's sons bow at the sages' feet and say that this sage gave up the world because of the anger of Jayandhara's son.","n":"It is not clear whether lines 3 and 4 are the princes' question (why did this sage renounce?) or an answer given to them, and who the Jayandhara's son is is not told in the verse. The story of the sage's loss is told elsewhere.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thuravu","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Naga Kumara Kaviyam 108","lb":"NG108","mm":1,"ev":"The princes bow to the sages and give anger of Jayandhara's son as the cause of the sage's renunciation.","cl":[],"ag":["செயவர்மன் மக்கள்"],"ec":[],"spk":["செயவர்மன் மக்கள்",""],"ch":"ng:7"},"ng:109":{"book":"naga_kumara_kaviyam","num":110,"t":["என்றவ ருரையைக் கேட்டு இருவருந் துறந்து போந்து","சென்றுநற் குமரன் றன்னைச் சீர்பெற வணங்கிச் சொன்னார்","இன்றுமக் காள ரானோ மென்றவர் கூற நன்றென்","குன்றுசூழ் வனசா லத்துக் குமரன்சென் றிருந்த வன்றே."],"p":"","r":"Having heard that account, the two brothers leave, go to Naga Kumara, bow, and say they are now his servants, which he accepts as he settles in the hill-ringed forest.","n":"This is the story's fulfilment of the prophecy of verses 104 and 105: the sons who might have ruled serve the conqueror. The word துறந்து (leaving) is read here as leaving their home and kingdom, not necessarily taking up a monk's life.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["oozh","panivu","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Naga Kumara Kaviyam 109","lb":"NG109","mm":1,"ev":"The two princes go to Naga Kumara and become his servants, as the ascetic had foretold.","cl":[],"ag":["செயவர்மன் மக்கள்","நாகநற் குமரன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ng:7"},"ng:110":{"book":"naga_kumara_kaviyam","num":111,"t":["அடிமரத் திருப்ப வண்ண லந்நிழற் றிரித லின்றித்","கடிகமழ் மார்பன் றன்னைக் காத்துட னிருப்பப் பின்னும்","விடமரப் பழங்க ளெல்லாம் வியந்து நற்றுய்த் திருந்தார்","கொடிமலர்க் காவு தன்னுட் கோமக னிருந்த போழ்தில்."],"p":"","r":"The princes stay at the tree's foot, never leaving its shade, and guard the prince, eating the fruits of the trees with admiration while he rests in the flowery grove.","n":"விடமரப் பழங்கள் (fruit of poison trees?) is a doubtful reading; if it is right, the verse marks the princes' ease in eating fruit others would avoid.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thunai","panivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Naga Kumara Kaviyam 110","lb":"NG110","mm":1,"ev":"The two princes stay beside Naga Kumara in a grove and guard him, living on the fruit.","cl":[],"ag":["செயவர்மன் மக்கள்","நாகநற் குமரன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ng:7"},"ng:111":{"book":"naga_kumara_kaviyam","num":112,"t":["அஞ்சுநூற் றுவர்கள் வந்தே யடிபணிந் தினிய கூறும்","தஞ்சமா யெங்கட் கெல்லாந் தவமுனி குறியு ரைப்ப","புஞ்சிய வனத்தி ருந்தோம் புரவல னின்னி டத்தின்","நெஞ்சிலிற் குறியன் காணா யெமக்குநீ றிறைவ னென்றார்."],"p":"","r":"Five hundred men come, bow, and say that an ascetic told them the sign, that they have lived in the forest, and that they see his sign and take him as their lord.","n":"The verse's last two lines are hard; the sense that the sage's sign (குறி) points to Naga Kumara as the lord they were promised is inferred.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["panivu","arasu","oozh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Naga Kumara Kaviyam 111","lb":"NG111","mm":1,"ev":"Five hundred men come to Naga Kumara, accept him as their lord, and say a sage had told them his sign.","cl":[],"ag":["அஞ்சுநூற்றுவர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["அஞ்சுநூற்றுவர்","நாகநற் குமரன்"],"ch":"ng:7"},"ng:112":{"book":"naga_kumara_kaviyam","num":113,"t":["அரியநல் லுரையைக் கேட்டு வவ்வணங் களிசிறந்து","உரியநல் லவர்க ளோடு முவந்துட னெழுந்து சென்று","கிரிநகர் தன்னைச்சேரக் கேட்டுநன் நகரைச் சென்றான்","அரிவர னெதிர்க் கொண் டேக யவன்மனை புகுந்தி ருந்தான்."],"p":"","r":"Naga Kumara rises gladly with his people on hearing of the hill city, goes to it, and is received by Harivara and enters his house.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Naga Kumara Kaviyam 112","lb":"NG112","mm":1,"ev":"Naga Kumara goes to the hill city, and its king Harivara receives him at his house.","cl":[],"ag":["நாகநற் குமரன்","அரிவரன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ng:7"},"ng:113":{"book":"naga_kumara_kaviyam","num":114,"t":["அரிவர ராசன் றேவி யருந்ததி யனைய கற்பின்","மிருகலோ சனையென் பாளா மிக்கநன் மகடன் பேருஞ்","சுரிகுழற் கருங்கண் செவ்வாய்த் துடியிடைக் குணவ தீயைப்","பிரவிச் சோதன னிச்சித்துப் பெருநகர் வளைந்த தன்றே."],"p":"","r":"Harivara's queen Mrigalochana, whose chastity was like Arundhati's, has a daughter Gunavati, and Pravichotana, desiring her, besieges the great city.","n":"The queen's chastity is compared to the star Arundhati (the Cintamani uses the same measure at 327); the verse gives the comparison as an ornament and does not argue it. The desire (இச்சித்து) that drives the siege is the poem's cause of war.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["karpu","kaamam","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Naga Kumara Kaviyam 113","lb":"NG113","mm":1,"ev":"Pravichotana, desiring Harivara's daughter Gunavati, surrounds his city with an army.","cl":[],"ag":["பிரவிச்சோதனன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ng:7"},"ng:114":{"book":"naga_kumara_kaviyam","num":115,"t":["நாகநற் குமரன் கேட்டு நாற்படை யோடுஞ் சென்று","வேகநற் போர்க்க ளத்தில் வெற்றிகொண் டவனை யோட்டி","நாகநல் லெருத்தின் வந்து நகர்புகுந் திருப்ப மிக்க","போகமிக் குணவ தீயைப் புரவலன் கொடுத்த தன்றே."],"p":"","r":"Naga Kumara hears of it, marches with his four-fold army, wins the battle, routs the besieger, enters the city on an elephant, and the king gives him Gunavati.","n":"A guest repays the host's welcome of verse 112 by war, and the bride comes as the reward, not as a wooing.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["padai","kaamam","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Naga Kumara Kaviyam 114","lb":"NG114","mm":1,"ev":"Naga Kumara defeats Pravichotana and drives him off, and Harivara gives him his daughter Gunavati.","cl":[],"ag":["நாகநற் குமரன்","அரிவரன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ng:7"},"ng:115":{"book":"naga_kumara_kaviyam","num":116,"t":["வேல்விழி யமிர்தன் னாளை வேள்வியா லண்ண லெய்திக்","கால்சிலம் போசை செய்யக் காமனும் ரதியும் போலப்","போனமும் போக மெல்லாம் பருகியின் புற்று நாளும்","நூனெறி வகையிற் றுய்த்தார் நுண்ணிடை துவள வன்றே."],"p":"","r":"He takes the nectar-eyed girl in the marriage rite and, like Kama and Rati, they enjoy together the pleasures that the rules of the text allow, her anklets sounding.","n":"நூனெறி (the way of the text) sets the limit on pleasure even as the verse compares the pair with the god of love; the verse does not say which text is meant.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","vaazhkkaithunai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Naga Kumara Kaviyam 115","lb":"NG115","mm":1,"ev":"Naga Kumara marries Gunavati and they live in pleasure.","cl":[["pleasure is to be enjoyed within the way set out by the text","general","asserted",4,"narrator"]],"ag":["நாகநற் குமரன்","குணவதி"],"ec":[],"ch":"ng:7"},"ng:116":{"book":"naga_kumara_kaviyam","num":117,"t":["கலையணி யல்குற் பாவை கங்குலும் பகலு மெல்லாஞ்","சிலையுயர்ந் தினிய திண்டோட் செம்மலும் பிரித லின்றி","நிலைபெற நெறியிற் றுய்த்தார் நிகரின்றிச் செல்லு நாளுள்","உலைதலி லுறுவ லீயா னூர்ச்சயந்த கிரிய டைந்தான்."],"p":"","r":"The pair are never apart by night or day and enjoy life by the proper way, until the strong, unfailing prince goes to the hill of Urjayanta.","n":"The hill is named only as ஊர்ச்சயந்த கிரி; that this is the sacred Jain hill and the 'Vamanar' of the next verse is Neminatha is the tradition's identification, not stated in these lines. The words உறுவலி ஈயான் (line 4) are unclear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","pirivu","samayam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Naga Kumara Kaviyam 116","lb":"NG116","mm":1,"ev":"After days of constant union Naga Kumara goes to Urjayanta hill.","cl":[],"ag":["நாகநற் குமரன்","குணவதி"],"ec":[],"ch":"ng:7"},"ng:117":{"book":"naga_kumara_kaviyam","num":118,"t":["வாமனா லையத்து மூன்று வலங்கொண் டுட்புகுந் திறைஞ்சி","தாமமார் மார்பன் மிக்க தக்கநற் பூசை செய்து","சேமமா முக்கு டைக்கீ ழிருந்தரி யாச னத்தின்","வாமனார் துதிகட் சொல்ல வாழ்த்துபு தொடங்கி னானே."],"p":"","r":"He circles the Vamana temple three times, enters, bows, worships well under the three umbrellas, and begins to praise the lord on the lion throne.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","iraivan","paththi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Naga Kumara Kaviyam 117","lb":"NG117","mm":1,"ev":"Naga Kumara worships at the Vamana temple on Urjayanta and begins a hymn to its lord.","cl":[],"ag":["நாகநற் குமரன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ng:7"},"ng:118":{"book":"naga_kumara_kaviyam","num":119,"t":["முத்திலங்கு முக்குடைக்கீழ் மூர்த்தி திருந்தடியை","வெற்றியுடன் பணிந்தவர்கள் விண்ணுலக மாண்டுவந்து","இத்தலமு முழுதாண்டு விருங்களிற் றெருத்தின்மிசை","நித்தில வெண்குடைக்கீழ் நீங்கா திருப்பவரே."],"p":"","r":"Those who bow with victory at the feet of the lord under the pearl umbrellas will rule the heaven, then rule all this earth on a great elephant under a white umbrella.","n":"The promised fruit is rule, in heaven and then on earth, and not release; the hymn builds up in these three verses from kingship (118) to Indra-rank (119) to release (120).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","iraivan","marumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Naga Kumara Kaviyam 118","lb":"NG118","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["worship of the feet of the lord under three umbrellas is repaid by rule of heaven and then of earth","jain","asserted",2,"nagakumaran"]],"ag":["நாகநற் குமரன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["நாகநற் குமரன்",""],"ch":"ng:7"},"ng:119":{"book":"naga_kumara_kaviyam","num":120,"t":["கமலமலர் மீதுறையுங் காட்சிக் கினிமூர்த்தி","யமலமலர்ப் பொற்சரணை யன்பாய்த் தொழுபவர்கள்","இமையவர்க ளுலகத் திந்திரராய்ப் போயுதுதித்து","இமையவர்கள் வந்துதொழ வின்புற் றிருப்பாரே."],"p":"","r":"Those who worship in love the golden feet of the lord who sits on a lotus will go to the gods' world as Indras and be worshipped by the gods.","n":"The lotus on which the lord sits (கமல மலர் மீதுறையும்) is the verse's one image, and the tirthankara is not named.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","iraivan","marumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Naga Kumara Kaviyam 119","lb":"NG119","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["loving worship of the lord's feet is repaid by the rank of Indra in heaven","jain","asserted",3,"nagakumaran"]],"ag":["நாகநற் குமரன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["நாகநற் குமரன்",""],"ch":"ng:7"},"ng:120":{"book":"naga_kumara_kaviyam","num":121,"t":["அரியா சனத்தின்மிசை யமர்ந்த திருமூர்த்தி","பரிவாக வுன்னடியைப் பணிந்து பரவுவர்கள்","திரிலோக முந்தொழவே தேவாதி தேவருமய்","எரிபொன் னுயிர்விளங்கி யினியமுத்தி சேர்பவரே."],"p":"","r":"Those who bow and praise the lord on the lion throne will be gods above gods, worshipped by the three worlds, and reach sweet release with the soul shining.","n":"The worshipper's own soul shines in release (எரிபொன் உயிர் விளங்கி) and the verse does not say the lord gives it by grace; the worshipper's bowing is the stated cause.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["veedu","vazhipaadu","iraivan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Naga Kumara Kaviyam 120","lb":"NG120","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["those who worship the lord on the lion throne become gods and then reach release with the soul shining","jain","asserted",4,"nagakumaran"]],"ag":["நாகநற் குமரன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["நாகநற் குமரன்","வாமனார்"],"ch":"ng:7"},"ng:121":{"book":"naga_kumara_kaviyam","num":122,"t":["இணையிலா யிறைவனை யேத்தியிவ் வகையினாற்","துணையினிய தோழன்மார் சூழ்ந்துட னிருந்தபின்","கணைசிலை பிடித்தொருவன் கண்டொரோலை முன்வைத்து","இணைகரமுங் கூப்பிநின் றினிதிறைஞ்சிக் கூறுவான்."],"p":"","r":"After praising the lord, Naga Kumara sits with his friends, and an archer sets a letter before him, joins his hands, bows, and speaks.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thoothu","natpu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Naga Kumara Kaviyam 121","lb":"NG121","mm":1,"ev":"An archer brings a letter to Naga Kumara, bows, and begins to speak.","cl":[],"ag":["தூதன்","நாகநற் குமரன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ng:7"},"ng:122":{"book":"naga_kumara_kaviyam","num":123,"t":["வற்சையெனு நாட்டினுள் வான்புகழுங் கௌசம்பி","செற்றவரி னும்மிகு சூரன்சுப சந்திரன்","வெற்புநிகர் கற்பினாள் வேந்தன்மகா தேவியும்","நற்சுகா வதியெனு நாமமினி தாயினாள்."],"p":"","r":"The messenger names Kausambi in Vatsa country, its king Subhachandra, fiercer than foes, and his queen Sukavati, chaste like a mountain.","n":"The messenger's account runs across the verses 122 to 123 and goes on after; the speaker is known from 121.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","karpu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Naga Kumara Kaviyam 122","lb":"NG122","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["தூதன்","நாகநற் குமரன்"],"ch":"ng:7"},"ng:123":{"book":"naga_kumara_kaviyam","num":124,"t":["அன்னவர்தம் புத்திரிக ளானவேழு பேர்களாம்","நன்சுயம் பிரபையும் நாகசுப் பிரபையு","இன்பநற் பிரபையும் இலங்குசொர்ண மாலையும்","நங்கைநற் பதுமையு நாகதத்தை யென்பரே."],"p":"","r":"The king and queen's seven daughters are named: Suyambhiraba, Nagasubhaprabha, Inbaprabha, Sornamalai, Padumai and Nagadattai.","n":"The verse says seven daughters but names six; the seventh is lost or named in the next verse.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["makkal","vaazhkkaithunai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Naga Kumara Kaviyam 123","lb":"NG123","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["தூதன்","நாகநற் குமரன்"],"ch":"ng:7"},"ng:124":{"book":"naga_kumara_kaviyam","num":125,"t":["வெள்ளியின் மலையில் மேகவா கனன்றுரந்திடக்","கள்ளவிழ் மாசுகண் டனவன் வந்துடன்","கிள்ளையம் மொழியினாரைக் கேட்டுடன் பெறுகிலன்","வெள்ளையங் கொடிநகர வேந்தனை வதைத்தனன்."],"p":"","r":"A verse whose first half is too corrupt to follow says that someone, after seeing a flaw, came and, failing to get the women he asked for, killed the king of the city of the white banner.","n":"The first two lines are unclear (மேகவா கனன்றுரந்திட, கள்ளவிழ் மாசுகண்டனவன்) and the subject of the killing is not named; whether it is a foe of the hero, an earlier episode retold, or the hero himself cannot be fixed here.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kolai","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Naga Kumara Kaviyam 124","lb":"NG124","mm":1,"ev":"An unnamed figure kills the king of the white-bannered city after failing to obtain the women he asked for.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"ng:8"},"ng:125":{"book":"naga_kumara_kaviyam","num":126,"t":["வேந்தனுக் கிளையனுன்னை வேண்டியோலை யேதர","சேர்ந்தவ னளித்தவோலை வாசகந் தெளிந்தபின்","நாந்தக மயிற்கணை நலம்பெறத் திரித்துடன்","போந்தவனைக் கொன்றனன் பூவலங்கன் மார்பனே."],"p":"","r":"The king's younger brother, wanting the hero, sends a letter; once the hero has understood what the letter says, he turns the peacock-feathered arrow and kills the man who came.","n":"The line breaks and the word நாந்தக (a sword name elsewhere) make the sense of lines 2 to 3 uncertain; who sent the letter and who is killed is read from the sentence order only.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kolai","thoothu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Naga Kumara Kaviyam 125","lb":"NG125","mm":1,"ev":"The hero reads the letter brought to him, reworks an arrow and kills the man who had come with it.","cl":[],"ag":["நாகநற் குமரன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ng:8"},"ng:126":{"book":"naga_kumara_kaviyam","num":127,"t":["அபிசந் திரன்றன்புர மத்தினாக மேகியே","சுபமுகூர்த்த நற்றினஞ் சுபசந்திரன் சுதைகளும்","அபிசந்திரன் றன்மக ளாஞ்சுகண்டன் சுதையுடன்","செபமந்திர வேள்வியாற் செல்வனெய்தி யின்புற்றான்."],"p":"","r":"Going secretly to Abhichandra's city on an auspicious day, the hero marries the daughters of Subhachandra and Abhichandra's own daughter Sukanda by means of a mantra-rite, and is glad.","n":"வேள்வி here is a rite performed with mantras (செபமந்திர வேள்வி) to win the women, not a Vedic sacrifice; the poem uses it again at 131, 139, 141 and 143 as the hero's way of taking brides.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","mandhiram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Naga Kumara Kaviyam 126","lb":"NG126","mm":1,"ev":"The hero goes to Abhichandra's city and marries Subhachandra's daughters and Abhichandra's daughter by a mantra-rite.","cl":[],"ag":["நாகநற் குமரன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ng:8"},"ng:127":{"book":"naga_kumara_kaviyam","num":128,"t":["நங்கைமார்க டன்னுட னாகநற் குமரனும்","இங்கிதக் களிப்பினா லிசைந்தினிப் புணர்ந்துடன்","பொங்குநகர்ப் புறத்தினிற் பூவளவன் மேவியே","திங்கள்சேர் செய்குன் றினுஞ் சேர்ந்தினி தாடுநாள்."],"p":"","r":"With the women as his companions the hero, joyful, takes his pleasure and plays on the pleasure-hill outside the swelling city.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inbam","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Naga Kumara Kaviyam 127","lb":"NG127","mm":1,"ev":"The hero enjoys himself with his wives at a pleasure-hill outside the city.","cl":[],"ag":["நாகநற் குமரன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ng:8"},"ng:128":{"book":"naga_kumara_kaviyam","num":129,"t":["அவந்தியென்னு நாட்டினு ளானவுஞ்சை நீணகர்","உவந்தமன்ன னாமமு மோங்குஞ்செய சேனனாம்","அவன் தனன் மனைவிய ரானநற் செயசிரீயாஞ்","சிவந்தபொன் னிறமகட் சீருடைய மேனகி."],"p":"","r":"The text introduces Ujjayini in the land of Avanti, its king Jayasena, his wife Jayasri and their golden-hued daughter Menaki.","n":"The last line names the daughter without a verb: the sentence is left open and finished in 129 to 131.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vendhan","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Naga Kumara Kaviyam 128","lb":"NG128","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"ng:8"},"ng:129":{"book":"naga_kumara_kaviyam","num":130,"t":["பாடலீ புரத்திருந்த பண்புமா வியாளனு","நாடிவந் திருந்தன னன்குவுஞ்சை நகர்தனில்","சேடிகண்டு மேனகிக்குச் செப்பவந்து கண்டவள்","நாடியவள் போயின ணன்னிதிப் புரிசையே."],"p":"","r":"Mavyalan of Pataliputra has come to Ujjayini; a Vidyadhara woman (சேடி) comes to tell Menaki of him, and she sees him and goes to the garden-wall of the treasure-city.","n":"Who goes to the wall (நன்னிதிப் புரிசை) and why is not clear from the Tamil: நன்னிதி may be a name or an epithet, and the second half may refer to Menaki or to a different woman.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Naga Kumara Kaviyam 129","lb":"NG129","mm":1,"ev":"Mavyalan arrives in Ujjayini, and Menaki, told of him by a Vidyadhara woman, goes to him.","cl":[],"ag":["மாவியாளன்","மேனகி"],"ec":[],"ch":"ng:8"},"ng:130":{"book":"naga_kumara_kaviyam","num":131,"t":["அந்நகர்விட் டேகின னானமா வியாளனும்","சென்றுதன் றமையனைச் சேவடி பணிந்தபின்","நன்றுடன் வணங்கின னாகநற் குமரனை","இன்றிலன்றான் யாரென வென்றம்பியவ னென்னலும்."],"p":"","r":"Mavyalan leaves that city, goes to his elder brother and bows at his feet, and when the younger brother is asked where the hero is, he answers.","n":"Line 3 to 4 make the pairing of 'elder brother' and 'hero' unclear: the Tamil can be read as the hero himself being the elder brother, and the questioner is not named.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["panivu","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Naga Kumara Kaviyam 130","lb":"NG130","mm":1,"ev":"Mavyalan leaves Ujjayini, bows to his elder brother and is asked about Nagakumara.","cl":[],"ag":["மாவியாளன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ng:8"},"ng:131":{"book":"naga_kumara_kaviyam","num":132,"t":["மின்னினிடை நேரிழை மேனகி யெனவொரு","மன்மதனை யிச்சியாள் மாவியாளன் சொல்லலும்","அந்நகரிற் செல்லலு மரிவையர் தரித்திட","மன்னனம்பு வேள்வியான் மன்னிநற் புணர்ந்தனன்."],"p":"","r":"Mavyalan tells of Menaki, slender-waisted and like the love-god's own beauty, and the hero goes to that city and weds her by the mantra-rite.","n":"Mavyalan's words are the report of Menaki's beauty in lines 1 to 2; the marriage in lines 3 to 4 is the narrator's.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","mandhiram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Naga Kumara Kaviyam 131","lb":"NG131","mm":1,"ev":"The hero hears from Mavyalan of Menaki, goes to Ujjayini and marries her by a mantra-rite.","cl":[],"ag":["மாவியாளன்","நாகநற் குமரன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["மாவியாளன்","நாகநற் குமரன்"],"ch":"ng:8"},"ng:132":{"book":"naga_kumara_kaviyam","num":133,"t":["மற்றுமொன் றுரைத்தனன் மதுரைமா நகரியில்","உற்றிருந்த சிரீமதி யோர்ந்துநா டகந்தனில்","வெற்றிமுழ வேழ்வியம்ப வீறுடைய வல்லவன்","பற்றுடன வள்பதியாம் பார்மிசைமே லென்றனன்."],"p":"","r":"Another tale is told of Madurai, where the dancer Srimati, skilled in the drama and its victorious drums, has declared that whoever overcomes her will be her husband.","n":"The speaker of line 1 (மற்றுமொன்று உரைத்தனன்) is not named; he is taken to be Mavyalan from 131. The last line is the sense of a challenge (பதியாம் பார்மிசை) and rests on a damaged word division.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","kalvi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Naga Kumara Kaviyam 132","lb":"NG132","mm":1,"ev":"A tale is told of the Madurai dancer Srimati, who will take as husband the man who defeats her.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"ng:8"},"ng:133":{"book":"naga_kumara_kaviyam","num":134,"t":["அங்குசென்றவ் வண்ணலு மவளைவென்று கொண்டனன்","பொங்குமிக் குழலியர்ப் புணர்ந்துட னிருந்தபின்","வங்கமீது வந்தவோர் வணிகனை வினவுவான்","எங்குள வதிசய மியம்புகநீ யென்றனன்."],"p":"","r":"The hero goes there, beats her and takes her, lives with the women, then asks a merchant come by ship to tell him where a wonder is to be found.","n":"The hero's question is in lines 3 and 4 (எங்குள அதிசயம் இயம்புக நீ).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","kalvi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Naga Kumara Kaviyam 133","lb":"NG133","mm":1,"ev":"The hero defeats Srimati and takes her, then asks an arriving merchant about marvels.","cl":[],"ag":["நாகநற் குமரன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["நாகநற் குமரன்","வணிகன்"],"ch":"ng:8"},"ng:134":{"book":"naga_kumara_kaviyam","num":135,"t":["பொங்குமாழி யுள்ளொரு பூதிலக மாபுரம்","புங்கவன்ற னாலையம் பொங்குசொன்ன வண்ணமுன்","நங்கைமா ரைஞ்நூற்றுவர் நாடொறு மொலிசெய்வார்","அங்கதற்குக் காரணம் யானறியே னென்றனன்."],"p":"","r":"The merchant answers that on the sea stands Bhutilaka, a city whose temple of the Lord has five hundred women singing daily as instructed, and he does not know why.","n":"புங்கவன் (the Lord) is the Jina by the poem's frame (the temple is called அருகனாலயம் in 136), but the verse does not say so.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","iraivan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Naga Kumara Kaviyam 134","lb":"NG134","mm":1,"ev":"The merchant tells of a sea-island city where five hundred women sing daily at a temple.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["வணிகன்","நாகநற் குமரன்"],"ch":"ng:8"},"ng:135":{"book":"naga_kumara_kaviyam","num":136,"t":["தனதுவித்தை தன்னையே தானினைக்க வந்தபின்","மனத்திசைந்த தோழரோடு வள்ளற்றீ பஞ்சென்றுநற்","கனகமய வாலையங் கண்டுவலங் கொண்டுடன்","சினனடி பணிந்துமுன் சிறந்துமிக் கிருந்தனர்."],"p":"","r":"Remembering his own vidya, the hero goes with his chosen friends to the island, sees and circles the golden temple, and bows before the Jina.","n":"வள்ளல் is the hero; the vidya he calls to mind (வித்தை) is the learned flying power by which he crosses the sea, though the verse does not say what it does.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","iraivan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Naga Kumara Kaviyam 135","lb":"NG135","mm":1,"ev":"The hero, with companions, flies to the island, circles the golden temple and worships.","cl":[],"ag":["நாகநற் குமரன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ng:8"},"ng:136":{"book":"naga_kumara_kaviyam","num":137,"t":["ஒருநிரையாய் மங்கைய ரோசைசெய்யக் கேட்டபின்","திருவலங்கன் மார்பினான் சேரவழைத் தவர்களை","யருகனாலை யத்துமுன் னலறுநீங்கள் யாரெனத்","தரணிசுந் தரியவ ளவற்கிதென்று கூறுவாள்."],"p":"","r":"The hero, hearing the women sing in a line, calls them over and asks who they are to cry out before the Arhat temple, and Dharanisundari answers.","n":"தரணிசுந் தரி is split across words by the edition; the speaker form in line 4 is as written.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","arul"],"pos":[],"nm":"Naga Kumara Kaviyam 136","lb":"NG136","mm":1,"ev":"The hero summons the singing women and asks who they are; Dharanisundari begins to answer.","cl":[],"ag":["நாகநற் குமரன்","தரணிசுந்தரி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நாகநற் குமரன்","தரணிசுந்தரி"],"ch":"ng:8"},"ng:137":{"book":"naga_kumara_kaviyam","num":138,"t":["அரியவெள்ளி மாமலை யாடுங்கொடி யேமிடை","பிரிதிவி திலகமெங்கட் பேருடைய நன்னகர்","வரதிரட் சகனேமர் தந்தையை மருகனுக்குக்","கருதியெம்மைக் கேட்டனன் கண்ணவாயு வேகனே."],"p":"","r":"Dharanisundari says they are from the Vidyadhara city Prithivitilaka on the great silver mountain, where Vayuvegan asked their father to give them to his nephew.","n":"மருகன் (nephew or son-in-law) leaves open whether the women were to marry Vayuvegan's nephew; the sentence at line 3 is loosely joined.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","kudi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Naga Kumara Kaviyam 137","lb":"NG137","mm":1,"ev":"Dharanisundari tells where she and the others come from and that Vayuvegan demanded them for his nephew.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["தரணிசுந்தரி","நாகநற் குமரன்"],"ch":"ng:8"},"ng:138":{"book":"naga_kumara_kaviyam","num":139,"t":["எந்தையுங் கொடாமையா லெரியென வெகுண்டனன்","எந்தையை வதைசெய்து வெங்களையும் பற்றியே","இந்தநல் வனத்திருந்தா னென்றவளுங் கூறலும்","அந்தவாயு வேகனை யண்ணல்வதை செய்தனன்."],"p":"","r":"Because their father refused, she says, Vayuvegan raged like fire, killed him, seized their kingdom and settled in the forest, and on hearing this the hero kills Vayuvegan.","n":"The verse treats the hero's killing as the answer to Vayuvegan's, without remark; the poem does not ask whether the Jain rule against killing bears on it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kolai","kodungol"],"pos":[],"nm":"Naga Kumara Kaviyam 138","lb":"NG138","mm":1,"ev":"Vayuvegan, refused, kills the women's father and seizes the kingdom; the hero then kills Vayuvegan.","cl":[],"ag":["நாகநற் குமரன்","வாயுவேகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["தரணிசுந்தரி","நாகநற் குமரன்"],"ch":"ng:8"},"ng:139":{"book":"naga_kumara_kaviyam","num":140,"t":["அஞ்சுநூற்று மங்கையரை யண்ணல்வேள் வியாலெய்தி","நெஞ்சிலன்பு கூரவே நிரந்தரம் புணர்ந்தபின்","அஞ்சுநூற்று வர்படர்க ளாளராகி வந்தனர்","தஞ்சமா யவர்தொழு தகமகிழ்ந்து செல்லுநாள்."],"p":"","r":"The hero wins the five hundred women by the mantra-rite and enjoys them with love, and their five hundred brothers come as his servants and, worshipping him as refuge, live content.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","adimai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Naga Kumara Kaviyam 139","lb":"NG139","mm":1,"ev":"The hero marries the five hundred Vidyadhara women, and their brothers become his attendants.","cl":[],"ag":["நாகநற் குமரன்","அஞ்சுநூற்றுவர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ng:8"},"ng:140":{"book":"naga_kumara_kaviyam","num":141,"t":["கலிங்கமென்னு நாட்டினுட் கனகமய விஞ்சிசூழ்ந்","திலங்கு ரத்னபுர மிந்நகர்க்கு மன்னவன்","துலங்குசந்திர குப்தன் றோகைசந் திரம்மதி","பெலங்கொளிவர் நன்மகட் பேர்மதன மஞ்சிகை."],"p":"","r":"The text introduces Ratnapura in Kalinga, its king Chandragupta, queen Chandramati and their daughter Madanamanjari.","n":"Like 128 and 142, a city-and-king introduction with no verb.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vendhan","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Naga Kumara Kaviyam 140","lb":"NG140","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"ng:8"},"ng:141":{"book":"naga_kumara_kaviyam","num":142,"t":["நாகநற் குமரன் சென்று நன்மந்திர வேள்வியால்","வாகன மினிதினின்று மதன்மஞ் சிகையொடுந்","தாகமிக் குடையனாய்த் தான்லயப் பருகினான்","நாகநற் புணர்ச்சிபோல் நன்குட னிருந்தரோ."],"p":"","r":"By the mantra-rite the hero reaches Madanamanjari, rides in her company and drinks deeply of her, their union like that of a nagas' pair.","n":"The simile of the last line (நாகநற் புணர்ச்சிபோல்) plays on the hero's name Naga; the line 3 phrase தான்லயப் பருகினான் is read as drinking in desire.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","mandhiram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Naga Kumara Kaviyam 141","lb":"NG141","mm":1,"ev":"The hero marries Madanamanjari by a mantra-rite and enjoys her.","cl":[],"ag":["நாகநற் குமரன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ng:8"},"ng:142":{"book":"naga_kumara_kaviyam","num":143,"t":["கங்கைநீ ரணிந்திலங்குங் கங்காளநன் னாட்டினுட்","திங்கடவழ் மாடநற் றிலகபுர மன்னவன்","பொங்குமகு டம்முடி பொற்புவிசை யந்தரன்","இங்கித மனைவிபேர் இயல்விசையை யென்பளே."],"p":"","r":"The text introduces Tilakapura in the land of Kangala, its king Vijayandhara and his wife Iyalvisai.","n":"Another introduction in the series of city-and-king verses (128, 140, 142).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vendhan","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Naga Kumara Kaviyam 142","lb":"NG142","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"ng:8"},"ng:143":{"book":"naga_kumara_kaviyam","num":144,"t":["இலக்கணை யெனுமக ளிலக்கண முடையவள்","மிக்கவண்ண லுஞ்சென்று மெய்ம்மைவேள் விதன்மையால்","அக்கணத் தவனெய்தி யவடன்போகந் துய்த்தபின்","தொக்ககாவு தன்னுளே தொன்முனிவர் வந்தரோ."],"p":"","r":"The hero goes to Ilakkanai, their daughter, wins her at once by the rite and takes her pleasure, when ancient sages come to the grove.","n":"The mantra-rite is called a matter of truth (மெய்ம்மை வேள்வி), the poem's claim that this is a true way of winning a bride.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","thavam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Naga Kumara Kaviyam 143","lb":"NG143","mm":1,"ev":"The hero marries Ilakkanai and enjoys her; ancient sages arrive at the grove.","cl":[],"ag":["நாகநற் குமரன்","முனிவர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ng:8"},"ng:144":{"book":"naga_kumara_kaviyam","num":145,"t":["ஊற்றினைச் செறித்திடு முறுதவனுடைச் சாரணை","நாற்றமிக் குமரனு நன்புறப் பணிந்தபின்","யேற்றவறங் கேட்டுட னிருந்தலக் கணையின்","ஏற்றமோக மென்னென னியன்முனி யுரைப்பரே."],"p":"","r":"The hero bows to the sage of firm austerity, hears the dharma that suits him, and asks what the high pleasure of Ilakkanai has been, which the sage will explain.","n":"The sage's answer is not in this verse (the sentence ends with the promise to teach); the question is about the cause of that fortune in a former birth, as the next verses will show, but this verse says only ஏற்றமோகம் என்ன.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thavam","aram","thuravu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Naga Kumara Kaviyam 144","lb":"NG144","mm":1,"ev":"The hero bows to the Charana sage, hears dharma and asks about his pleasure with Ilakkanai.","cl":[],"ag":["நாகநற் குமரன்","சாரணர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ng:8"},"ng:145":{"book":"naga_kumara_kaviyam","num":146,"t":["நாவலந் தீவு தன்னுள் நன்கயி ராவ தத்தின்","மேவுமின் முகில்சூழ் மாட வீதசோ கப்பு ரத்துக்","காவிநன் விழிமா தற்குக் காமன்விக் கிரம ராசன்","தாவில் சீர் வணிகன் நாமந் தனதத்த னென்ப தாமே."],"p":"","r":"The telling of an earlier birth begins in a city of Jambudvipa, where a merchant named Dhanadattan lives.","n":"The third line (காவிநன் விழிமா தற்குக் காமன்விக் கிரம ராசன்) is not clear: it may name the merchant's wife or give a second name or title, and the city's name is split across lines. The sage is the speaker only because the story is later said (150) to be told by an ascetic; this verse does not say so.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kudi","marumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Naga Kumara Kaviyam 145","lb":"NG145","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["சாரணர்",""],"ch":"ng:9"},"ng:146":{"book":"naga_kumara_kaviyam","num":147,"t":["மனைவிதன் றனதத் தைக்கு மகனாக தத்த னாகும்","வனைமலர் மாலை வேலான் மற்றொரு வணிகன் றேவிப்","புனைமலர்க் கோதை நல்லாட் பொற்புடை வசும திக்கு","மனையினன் மகடன் னாம மியன்நாக வசுவென் பாளம்."],"p":"","r":"Dhanadattan and his wife Dhanadattai have a son, Nagadattan, and another merchant and his wife Vasumati have a daughter, Nagavasu.","n":"Nagadattan is, by the sage's own account in 149 and 150, an earlier birth of the hero; the hero is not named in this verse, so the identification is read from 150.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kudi","makkal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Naga Kumara Kaviyam 146","lb":"NG146","mm":1,"ev":"A son is born to the merchant couple, and the daughter Nagavasu is named in another merchant's house.","cl":[],"ag":["தனதத்தை"],"ec":[],"spk":["சாரணர்",""],"ch":"ng:9"},"ng:147":{"book":"naga_kumara_kaviyam","num":148,"t":["நண்புறு நாக தத்த னாகநல் வசுவென் பாளை","யன்புறு வேள்வி தன்னா லவளுடன் புணர்ந்து சென்றான்","பண்புறு நற்ற வத்தின் பரமுனி தத்த நாமர்","இன்புறும் புறத்தின் வந்தா ரிறைவனா லையத்தி னுள்ளே."],"p":"","r":"Nagadattan marries Nagavasu and goes with her, and a great ascetic named Datta arrives in the king's temple grounds.","n":"வேள்வி (line 2) is read as the marriage rite; the word could also mean a sacrifice, but no sacrifice follows.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","thavam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Naga Kumara Kaviyam 147","lb":"NG147","mm":1,"ev":"Nagadattan, married to Nagavasu, meets the ascetic Datta who has come to the temple.","cl":[],"ag":["நாகதத்தன்","தத்த"],"ec":[],"spk":["சாரணர்",""],"ch":"ng:9"},"ng:148":{"book":"naga_kumara_kaviyam","num":149,"t":["நாகதத் தன்சென் றந்த நன்முனி சரண டைந்து","வாகுநற் றருமங் கேட்டு அனசன நோன்பு கொண்டான்","போகபுண் ணியங்க ளாக்கும் பூரண பஞ்ச மீயில்","ஏகனற் றினத்தி னன்று யிடர்பசி யாயிற் றன்றே."],"p":"","r":"Nagadattan takes refuge with the sage, hears the dharma and takes the fast unto death on the fifth day, when hunger and pain come.","n":"Line 4 (ஏகனற் றினத்தி னன்று) is unclear; it is read as the day on which the pain of hunger came. The sage's teaching is not summarised, only its effect.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thavam","nonbu","vinai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Naga Kumara Kaviyam 148","lb":"NG148","mm":1,"ev":"Nagadattan goes to the sage, hears the teaching and undertakes the fast unto death (அனசன நோன்பு).","cl":[["a fast unto death taken in the sage's presence is the proper close of a householder's life","jain","asserted",2,"charana_munivar"]],"ag":["நாகதத்தன்","தத்த"],"ec":[],"spk":["சாரணர்",""],"ch":"ng:9"},"ng:149":{"book":"naga_kumara_kaviyam","num":150,"t":["தருமநற் றியானந் தன்னாற் றன்னுடை மேனி விட்டு","மருவினா னசோத மத்தின் வானவ னாகித் தோன்றி","வருகயல் விழியாள் நாக வசுவும்வந் தமர னுக்கு","மருவிய தேவி யாகி மயலுறு கின்ற வன்றே."],"p":"","r":"By dharma meditation he leaves his body, is born as a god in the Asodhama heaven, and Nagavasu also becomes a consort of a god.","n":"The heaven is named அசோதமம்; the name is not found in the earlier verses, and the spelling may be corrupt.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["marumai","piravi","thavam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Naga Kumara Kaviyam 149","lb":"NG149","mm":1,"ev":"Nagadattan dies in meditation and is reborn as a god; Nagavasu is reborn as a god's consort.","cl":[["meditation in the fast carries the soul to a heaven after death","jain","asserted",1,"charana_munivar"],["a wife who shares the husband's vow shares his heavenly birth","jain","asserted",3,"charana_munivar"]],"ag":["நாகவசு"],"ec":[],"spk":["சாரணர்",""],"ch":"ng:9"},"ng:150":{"book":"naga_kumara_kaviyam","num":151,"t":["அங்கைந் துபல்ல மாயு வமரனாய்ச் சுகித்து விட்டு","இங்குவந் தரச னானா யினியந்தத் தேவி வந்து","தங்குநின் மனைவி யானாள் தவமுனி யுரைப்பப் பின்னும்","எங்களுக் கந்த நோன்பு யினிதுவைத் தருள வென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"The sage tells the hero that after five palyas as a god he came here as king and that the goddess is now his wife, and the hero asks him to give that vow to them.","n":"Here the work gives the hero's good fortune a cause in his own past conduct, not in grace or chance. The sentence runs from the sage's words into the hero's request, so the speaker is not one voice throughout; the speech is the sage's up to line 3 and the narrator reports the request.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oozh","vinai","thavam","marumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Naga Kumara Kaviyam 150","lb":"NG150","mm":1,"ev":"The sage explains the hero's present kingship and marriage as the fruit of his earlier vow, and the hero asks for the vow.","cl":[["the fruit of a vow kept in one birth is enjoyed as rank and a wife in a later birth","jain","asserted",2,"charana_munivar"]],"ag":["நாகநற் குமரன்","சாரணர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சாரணர்","நாகநற் குமரன்"],"ch":"ng:9"},"ng:151":{"book":"naga_kumara_kaviyam","num":152,"t":["திங்கட் கார்த்திகையி லாதற் சேர்ந்தபங் குனியி லாதற்","பொங்கன லாடி யாதற் பூரண பக்கந் தன்னில்","அங்குறு பஞ்சமியி னனசன நோன்பு கொண்டு","தங்குமாண் டைந்து நோற்றான் றானைந்து திங்க ளன்றே."],"p":"","r":"The vow is set out: taken on the fifth day of the bright fortnight in certain months, kept for five years and five months.","n":"The list of months in lines 1 and 2 is hard to take apart (the words ஆதல், ஆடி, பங்குனி, கார்த்திகை run into a rule whose exact calendar is unclear). The sense of a fixed periodic fast is clear; the exact schedule is not.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["nonbu","thavam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Naga Kumara Kaviyam 151","lb":"NG151","mm":1,"ev":"The sage fixes the days and the length of the vow.","cl":[["the vow of fasting is kept on a fixed fifth day of a bright fortnight over five years and five months","jain","asserted",3,"charana_munivar"]],"ag":["நாகநற் குமரன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சாரணர்",""],"ch":"ng:9"},"ng:152":{"book":"naga_kumara_kaviyam","num":153,"t":["இந்தநற் கிரமந் தன்னி லினிமையி னோன்பு நோற்று","அந்தமி லருகர் பூசை யருண்முனி தானஞ் செய்தால்","இந்திர பதமும் பெற்று இங்குவந் தரச ராகிப்","பந்ததீ வீனையை வென்று பஞ்சம கதியு மாமே."],"p":"","r":"Keeping the fast in this order, worshipping the arhats and giving to ascetics, one reaches Indra's station, then kingship, conquers the binding deed and reaches the fifth state.","n":"The release promised is reached through kingship and gifts: the householder's path is placed before the ascetic's, as the fast and the giving come first and the end of deeds last.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thavam","eegai","veedu","vinai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Naga Kumara Kaviyam 152","lb":"NG152","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["the fast, worship of the arhats and gifts to ascetics bring the rank of Indra, then kingship","jain","asserted",2,"charana_munivar"],["the soul can conquer the binding deeds by vow and reach the final state (பஞ்சம கதி)","jain","asserted",4,"charana_munivar"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["சாரணர்",""],"ch":"ng:9"},"ng:153":{"book":"naga_kumara_kaviyam","num":154,"t":["என்றவ ருரைப்பக் கேட்டு யிறைஞ்சிக் கைக்கொண்டு நோன்பை","சென்றுதன் பவனம் புக்கான் சேயிழை யோடு மன்னன்","நன்றுடன் செல்லு நாளு ணயந்தரன் வந்தி றைஞ்சி","உன்னுடைத் தந்தை யுன்னை யுடன் கொண்டு வருக வென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"The hero accepts the vow and enters his palace with his wife; Nayantaran then bows and tells him his father calls him.","n":"Lines 3-4 report Nayantaran's words; the speech is not set off by a closing particle, so it could be taken as narrator's paraphrase.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thavam","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Naga Kumara Kaviyam 153","lb":"NG153","mm":1,"ev":"The hero takes the vow home; the minister Nayantaran summons him to his father.","cl":[],"ag":["நாகநற் குமரன்","நயந்தரன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ng:9"},"ng:154":{"book":"naga_kumara_kaviyam","num":155,"t":["அமையுநன் கமைச்சன் சொல்லை யருமணி மார்பன் கேட்டு","சமையுநாற் படையுஞ் சூழச் சாலலக் கணையி னோடும்","இமையம்போற் களிற்றினேறி யினியநற் றோழன் மாரும்","இமையவற் கிறைவன் போல வெழில்பெறப் புக்க வன்றே."],"p":"","r":"Hearing the minister, the hero rides out on an elephant with his army, his wife Salalakshana and his friends, like Indra's lord.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Naga Kumara Kaviyam 154","lb":"NG154","mm":1,"ev":"The hero sets out in state, with his army, wife and friends, to join his father.","cl":[],"ag":["நாகநற் குமரன்","சாலலக்கணை"],"ec":[],"ch":"ng:9"},"ng:155":{"book":"naga_kumara_kaviyam","num":156,"t":["தாதையெதிர் கொள்ளவவன் றாழ்ந்தடி பணிந்தான்","ஆதரவி னன்மகனை யன்புற வெடுத்தும்","போதமிகப் புல்லியபின் போந்தனர் மனைக்கே","ஏதமில்சீ ரின்புற வினிதுட னிருந்தார்."],"p":"","r":"The father comes to meet him; the hero bows at his feet, the father lifts and embraces him, and they go home and live happily.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["makkal","anbu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Naga Kumara Kaviyam 155","lb":"NG155","mm":1,"ev":"Father and son meet, embrace and go home.","cl":[],"ag":["நாகநற் குமரன்","செயந்தரன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ng:9"},"ng:156":{"book":"naga_kumara_kaviyam","num":157,"t":["வெற்றியுடன் வேள்விசெய்த வேல்விழியி னாரை","யுற்றுடனே மாதரை யொருங்கழைக்க வந்தார்","சித்திரநற் பாவையரைச் சேர்ந்துட னிருந்தான்","பற்றறச் செயந்தரனும் பார்மகன் வைத்தான்."],"p":"","r":"The women he married with the rite are called to him; he stays with them, and the father, free of attachment, sets the son on the throne.","n":"Line 4 (பற்றறச் செயந்தரனும் பார்மகன் வைத்தான்) is read as the father, without attachment, setting up the son as king; the subject is uncertain, and the order of 156 and 157 overlaps.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Naga Kumara Kaviyam 156","lb":"NG156","mm":1,"ev":"The brides are brought to the hero, and his father arranges to hand over the kingdom.","cl":[],"ag":["நாகநற் குமரன்","செயந்தரன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ng:9"},"ng:157":{"book":"naga_kumara_kaviyam","num":158,"t":["நாககும ரன்றனக்கு நன்மகுடஞ் சூட்டிப்","போகவுப போகம்விட்டுப் புரவலனும் போகி","யாகம னடைக்குமுனி யவரடி பணிந்து","யேகமன மாகியவ னிறைவனுருக் கொண்டான்."],"p":"","r":"The father crowns Naga Kumara, leaves his pleasures, bows at the sage's feet and, one-pointed in mind, takes the form of the Lord.","n":"The father takes the way the sage's prophecy (verse 44) had promised for the son. For the son the prophecy of austerity and release is left open here, since he is crowned.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thuravu","arasu","veedu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Naga Kumara Kaviyam 157","lb":"NG157","mm":1,"ev":"The king crowns his son, leaves the throne and takes up the ascetic's life under a sage.","cl":[["a king should leave pleasures and take the ascetic form once his heir is crowned","jain","asserted",2,"narrator"]],"ag":["செயந்தரன்","நாகநற் குமரன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ng:9"},"ng:158":{"book":"naga_kumara_kaviyam","num":159,"t":["இருவினை கெடுத்தவனு மின்பவுல கடைந்தான்","பிரிதிவிநற் றேவியுந்தன் பெருமகனை விட்டு","சிரிமதி யெனுந்துறவி சீரடி பணிந்து","அரியதவந் தரித்தவளு மச்சுத மடைந்தாள்."],"p":"","r":"The one who destroyed the two deeds reaches the world of bliss, and Prithivi Devi, leaving her great son and bowing at the feet of the sage Sirimati, takes up hard austerity and reaches the Achyuta heaven.","n":"The first line gives the fruit (the world of bliss) for one who has destroyed both karmas, but the verse does not name him; the earlier verses make the king the likeliest, which is read here, not stated. The queen's reward is a heaven (அச்சுதம்), a lesser end than the first line's.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["veedu","thavam","thuravu","vinai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Naga Kumara Kaviyam 158","lb":"NG158","mm":1,"ev":"Prithivi Devi leaves her son, bows to the nun Sirimati, takes austerity and reaches the Achyuta heaven; the earlier king, who has destroyed his karmas, reaches the world of bliss.","cl":[["one who destroys the two kinds of deed reaches the world of bliss","jain","asserted",1,"narrator"]],"ag":["மன்னன்","பிரிதிவிழ் தேவி"],"ec":[],"ch":"ng:10"},"ng:159":{"book":"naga_kumara_kaviyam","num":160,"t":["வேந்தனர்த்த ராச்சியம் வியாளனுக் களித்தான்","ஆய்ந்தபல தோழர்களுக் கவனிக ளளித்துக்","சேர்ந்ததன் மனைவியருள் செயலக் கணைதன்னை","வாய்ந்தமகா தேவிபட்டம் வன்மைபெற வைத்தான்."],"p":"","r":"The king gives half his kingdom to Vyalan, gives gifts to his many friends, and firmly sets Ilakkanai, chief of his wives, in the rank of chief queen.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vendhan","eegai","ilvaazhkkai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Naga Kumara Kaviyam 159","lb":"NG159","mm":1,"ev":"Naga Kumara gives half the kingdom to Vyalan, rewards his companions and installs Ilakkanai as chief queen.","cl":[],"ag":["நாகநற் குமரன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ng:10"},"ng:160":{"book":"naga_kumara_kaviyam","num":161,"t":["இலக்கணையார் தன்வயிற்றி னற்சுதன் பிறந்தான்","மிக்கவன்ற னாமமு மிகுதேவ குமாரன்","தொக்ககலை சிலையியிற் பயின்றுமிகு தொல்தேர்","ஒக்கமிக் களிறுடனே வூர்ந்துதினஞ் சென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"A good son named Devakumaran is born to Ilakkanai, learns the arts and the bow, and daily goes out with the chariot and elephant.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["makkal","kalvi","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Naga Kumara Kaviyam 160","lb":"NG160","mm":1,"ev":"Ilakkanai bears a son, Devakumaran, who is trained in the arts, the bow, chariot and elephant.","cl":[],"ag":["தேவகுமாரன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ng:10"},"ng:161":{"book":"naga_kumara_kaviyam","num":162,"t":["புரிசையெழ நிலத்தின்மிசை பொற்புற விளங்கும்","அரியவரி யாசனத்தி லண்ணல் மிகஏறி","எரிபொன்முடி மன்னர்களெண் ணாயிரவர் சூழ","இருகவரி வீசவினி யெழில்பெற விருந்தான்."],"p":"","r":"The lord, seated on a fine throne in his hall, attended by eight thousand bright-crowned kings and two fly-whisks, sits in splendour.","n":"The splendour here is drawn at full height, just before the next verse turns it to renunciation.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","vendhan","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Naga Kumara Kaviyam 161","lb":"NG161","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["நாகநற் குமரன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ng:10"},"ng:162":{"book":"naga_kumara_kaviyam","num":163,"t":["அரசனினி தியல்பினி னமர்ந்திருக்கு மளவிற்","பரவுமுகின் மாய்ந்திடப் பார்த்திபன் விரத்தி","விரவிமிகு குமரன்மிசை வீறுமுடி சூட்டி","அரியதவந் தாங்கவவ னன்புட னெழுந்தான்."],"p":"","r":"While the king sits at ease, a cloud that has gathered dissolves, and with dispassion arising he crowns the young prince and rises gladly to take up the hard austerity.","n":"The text names the vanishing cloud as the moment of the turn but does not say it is the cause; the verse leaves a quick reader to supply the lesson of impermanence. The crowning of the son comes before the going forth, so the king's duty is discharged first.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thuravu","arasu","nilaiyaamai","thavam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Naga Kumara Kaviyam 162","lb":"NG162","mm":1,"ev":"Seeing a cloud vanish, the king grows dispassionate, crowns his son Devakumaran and leaves to take austerity.","cl":[["the sight of a passing thing turns a king from rule to renunciation","general","asserted",2,"narrator"]],"ag":["நாகநற் குமரன்","தேவகுமாரன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ng:10"},"ng:163":{"book":"naga_kumara_kaviyam","num":164,"t":["அமலமதி கேவலியின் அடியிணை வணங்கி","விமலனுருக் கொண்டனனல் வேந்தர்பலர் கூட","கமலமல ராணிகர்நற் காட்சியிலக் கணையும்","துமிலமனைப் பதுமையெனுந் துறவரடி பணிந்தாள்."],"p":"","r":"The king bows at the feet of the stainless Amalamati, the kevali, and with many kings looking on takes the form of the faultless one, and Ilakkanai and the other wives bow at the feet of the nun Pathumai.","n":"","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thuravu","guru","vesham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Naga Kumara Kaviyam 163","lb":"NG163","mm":1,"ev":"Naga Kumara bows to the kevali Amalamati and takes the monk's form before many kings; Ilakkanai bows to the nun Pathumai.","cl":[],"ag":["நாகநற் குமரன்","இலக்கணை","பதுமை"],"ec":[],"ch":"ng:10"},"ng:164":{"book":"naga_kumara_kaviyam","num":165,"t":["நறுங்குழ லிலக்க ணையு நங்கை மார்தங் கூட","உறுதவந் தரித்துக் கொண்டு வுவந்தவர் செல்லு நாளுள்","மறுவில்சீர் முனிவ னாய னாக குமாரன் றானும்","இறுகுவெவ் வினைகள் வென்று யினிச்சித்தி சேர்ந்த தன்றே."],"p":"","r":"Ilakkanai and her companions take up hard austerity and go on gladly, and in those days the faultless sage Naga Kumara, having overcome the binding deeds, reaches the final perfection.","n":"The verse gives the king's end as the conquest of karma (வினைகள் வென்று), not as a gift of any god; the women's austerity is told but their end is not said here.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thavam","veedu","vinai","siththi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Naga Kumara Kaviyam 164","lb":"NG164","mm":1,"ev":"Ilakkanai and the women take austerity; the monk Naga Kumara conquers his harsh deeds and reaches siddhi.","cl":[["austerity overcomes the binding deeds and brings final perfection","jain","asserted",3,"narrator"]],"ag":["இலக்கணை","நாகநற் குமரன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ng:10"},"ng:165":{"book":"naga_kumara_kaviyam","num":166,"t":["வியாளமா வியாளர் தாமும் விழுத்தவத் தனயை யென்னு","நயாவுயிர் தியானந் தன்னா னாலிரு வினைகள் வென்று","செயத்துதி தேவர் கூறிச் சிறந்தபூ சனையுஞ் செய்ய","மயாவிறப் பிறப்பு மின்றி மருவினார் முத்தி யன்றே."],"p":"","r":"Vyalan and Mavyalan, through meditation on the soul and the supreme austerity, overcome the two deeds, are praised and worshipped by the gods, and reach liberation with no more death or birth.","n":"Meditation on the soul (உயிர் தியானம்) is the named means; no grace or god is given a part, which is the point the Jain account would press against a path of release by a Lord's favour.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["veedu","thiyaanam","vinai","thavam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Naga Kumara Kaviyam 165","lb":"NG165","mm":1,"ev":"Vyalan and Mavyalan conquer their karmas by meditation, are praised by the gods and reach liberation.","cl":[["meditation on the soul destroys both kinds of deed and ends birth and death","jain","asserted",2,"narrator"]],"ag":["வியாளன்","மாவியாளன்","தேவர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ng:10"},"ng:166":{"book":"naga_kumara_kaviyam","num":167,"t":["அருந்தவ யோகந் தன்னா லச்சேத் தியபேத் தியர்தம்","இருவினை தம்மை வென்று வின்புறுஞ் சித்தி சேர்ந்தார்","மருவுநற் றவத்தி னாலே மற்றுமுள் ளோர்க ளெல்லாம்","திருநிறைச் சோத மாதி சேர்ந்தின்பந் துய்த்தா ரன்றே."],"p":"","r":"By hard yoga these indivisible and unbreakable ones overcame the two deeds and reached the joyful perfection, and all the others too, through firm austerity, enjoyed the bliss of heaven.","n":"Two ends are set side by side: those who conquer both deeds reach perfection (சித்தி), while the others by austerity go to a heaven of enjoyment, so austerity alone does not give the same fruit to all.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yogam","thavam","siththi","vinai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Naga Kumara Kaviyam 166","lb":"NG166","mm":1,"ev":"The two brothers reach siddhi by yoga; the rest, by austerity, reach heavenly bliss.","cl":[["austerity and yoga conquer both deeds and bring perfection, and lesser austerity brings heavenly joy","jain","asserted",1,"narrator"]],"ag":["வியாளன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ng:10"},"ng:167":{"book":"naga_kumara_kaviyam","num":168,"t":["நாகநற் குமரற் காயு நான்காண் டைஞ்நூற் றிரட்டி","ஆகுநற் குமார கால மைந்து முப்பத் திரட்டி","போகபூ மியாண்ட பொருவி லெண்ணூ றுவாண்டு","ஆருநற் றவத்தி லாண்டு வறுபத்து நான்க தாமே."],"p":"","r":"The verse counts the years of Naga Kumara's life as the years of youth, of rule in the earthly land and of austerity, which together come to a long span.","n":"The numbers in the Tamil (the first lines are broken into words and units) cannot be fixed cleanly, so the arithmetic is not relied on.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["aayul","kaalam","kurippu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Naga Kumara Kaviyam 167","lb":"NG167","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"ng:10"},"ng:168":{"book":"naga_kumara_kaviyam","num":169,"t":["மறுவறு மனைய வர்க்கு மாதவர் தமக்கு மீந்த","பெறுமிரு நிலங்க ளெங்கும் பெயர்ந்து நற்கே வலியாய்","அறமழை பொழிந்த கால மறுபத்தா றாண்டு சென்றார்","உறுதவர் தேவர் நான்கு முற்றெழு குழாத்தி னோடே."],"p":"","r":"Having moved on as a kevali through both lands he had given to the faultless wives and the great sages, he poured out a rain of dharma for sixty-six years with sages and gods in four groups around him.","n":"Dharma is figured as rain falling on the lands, a teacher's giving without return, and the teacher is already liberated in knowledge before he is freed from the body.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["aram","guru","arul"],"pos":[],"nm":"Naga Kumara Kaviyam 168","lb":"NG168","mm":1,"ev":"As a kevali Naga Kumara teaches dharma for sixty-six years among sages and gods.","cl":[],"ag":["நாகநற் குமரன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ng:10"},"ng:169":{"book":"naga_kumara_kaviyam","num":170,"t":["இதன்கதை யெழுதி யோதி யின் புறக் கேட்ப வர்க்கும்","புதல்வர்நற் பொருளும் பெற்றுப் புரந்தரன் போல வாழ்ந்து","கதமுறு கவலை நீங்கிக் காட்சிநல் லறிவு முன்பாய்ப்","பதமிகு மமர யோகம் பாங்குடன் செல்வ ரன்றே."],"p":"","r":"Those who write and recite this story and those who hear it with joy will gain sons and wealth, live like Indra, shed anger and sorrow, hold right faith and knowledge, and reach the heavens.","n":"This is the usual promise at a poem's close: reward comes from hearing and copying the tale, which is put alongside conduct rather than in place of it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aram","payan","kalvi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Naga Kumara Kaviyam 169","lb":"NG169","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["hearing and copying this story brings children, wealth and heaven","jain","asserted",1,"narrator"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"ng:10"},"ng:170":{"book":"naga_kumara_kaviyam","num":171,"t":["அறமின்றிப் பின்னை யொன்று முயிர்க்கர ணில்லையென்றும்","மறமின்றி யுயிர்க் கிடர்செய் மற்றொன்று மில்லை யென்றும்","திறமிது வுணர்ந்து தேறித் தீக்கதிப் பிறவிக் கஞ்சி","மறமிதை விட்ட றத்தில் வாழுமின் னுலகத் தீரே."],"p":"","r":"Apart from virtue nothing else helps the soul, and nothing else harms it as vice does; knowing this and fearing rebirth in a bad state, the world is told to leave vice and live in virtue.","n":"The closing sermon is a rule, not an event: it puts the whole story's moral in two statements, one about what supports the soul and one about what harms it. Fear of an evil birth is the stated motive, not love of virtue for itself.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aram","maram","naragam","piravi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Naga Kumara Kaviyam 170","lb":"NG170","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["nothing helps the soul but virtue and nothing harms it but vice","general","asserted",1,"narrator"],["fear of an evil rebirth should lead one to leave vice and live by virtue","general","asserted",3,"narrator"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"ng:10"},"nk:1":{"book":"neelakesi","num":1,"t":["நல்லார் வணங்கப் படுவான்பிறப் பாதி நான்கு","மில்லா னுயிர்கட் கிடர்தீர்த்துய ரின்ப மாக்குஞ்","சொல்லான் றருமச் சுடரானெனுந் தொன்மை யினா","னெல்லா முணர்ந்தா னவனேயிறை யாக வேத்தி."],"p":"","r":"The poet praises as god the one whom the good worship, who has none of the four that begin with birth, whose word removes the suffering of souls and makes them rise to joy, the ancient lamp of dharma who knows all things.","n":"The verse does not name the Jina; it defines god by marks (freedom from birth and the three that follow it, omniscience, a teaching that ends suffering) rather than by creation or grace. That this is the Jain omniscient teacher rests on those marks and on the later verses that name the Jina (சினவரன், 148); the claim is tagged Jain on that basis. The god acts through his word (சொல்), not by intervening.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","piravi","aram","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 1","lb":"NK1","ev":"","cl":[["only the omniscient one, free of birth and its companions, whose teaching ends the suffering of souls, is to be praised as god","jain","asserted",4,"narrator"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"nk:1"},"nk:2":{"book":"neelakesi","num":2,"t":["அன்னான் பயந்த வறவாரமிர் துண்டு நின்றார்","இன்னா ரினைய ரெனவேண்டுவ தில்லை யார்க்கும்","பன்னாந் துணையும் பணிந்தாகிய பத்தி யினா","னென்னா லுரைக்கப் படுகின்றதொன் றீங்கு ளதே."],"p":"","r":"Those who have drunk the nectar of dharma he gave need not be named one by one; out of a devotion built by bowing over many days, the poet says, there is one thing he has to tell here.","n":"Line 2 (இன்னார் இனையர் என வேண்டுவது இல்லை) can mean that the saints are too well known to need naming, or that no distinction of persons matters for those who drink the dharma; the reading is not fixed.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["aram","paththi","panivu","amudham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 2","lb":"NK2","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"nk:1"},"nk:3":{"book":"neelakesi","num":3,"t":["பண்டாக மத்துட் பயிலாவுரை யென்று மிக்கார்","விண்டீங் கிதனை வெகுளார்விடல் வேண்டு வன்யான்","தண்டா மரைமே னடந்தான்றடந் தாள்வ ணங்கிக்","கண்டேன் கிடந்தேன் கனவின்னிது கண்ட வாறே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks the learned not to throw his work aside in anger as speech not found in the old Agama: he bowed at the broad feet of the one who walked on cool lotuses, lay down, and saw it in a dream, and tells it as he saw it.","n":"The apology anticipates the charge that a debate poem is a novelty outside scripture, and answers it by vision rather than by citing a text.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kanavu","aagamam","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 3","lb":"NK3","ev":"The poet bows at the lotus-walker's feet, lies down and sees the work in a dream.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"nk:1"},"nk:4":{"book":"neelakesi","num":4,"t":["ஆய்நீல வுண்க ணவளாயடங் காமை செய்யும்","பேய்நீல கேசி பெரியோனறங் கொண்ட பின்னைத்","தீநீல வுள்ளந் திரிந்தேறு திருவத் தளாய்","மாஞால மெல்லா மறமாற்றிய மாட்சி யளா."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi, a demoness in the form of a woman with dark kohl-lined eyes who did unrestrained harm, took the great one's dharma; her dark and evil heart turned, she rose into holiness, and she had the glory of turning the whole wide world from violence.","n":"The verse plays on நீல (dark) three times: her name, the dark eyes, and the dark heart that turns. The conversion is the cause of her public work; the debates are framed as the reform of a world's violence (மறம்), not as a scholastic contest.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aram","maram","anangu","kollaamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 4","lb":"NK4","ev":"Neelakesi the demoness takes the great one's dharma, is transformed, and turns the world from violence.","cl":[["taking the great one's dharma can turn even a demon's evil heart toward holiness","jain","asserted",3,"narrator"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"ch":"nk:1"},"nk:5":{"book":"neelakesi","num":5,"t":["தேவன் னுரைப்பத் தெளிந்தேன்பிற் றெளிந்த வெல்லாம்","மாவென் றுகொண்டேன் மடனேவலி யாக நின்று","நாவல் புலவ ரவைநாப்பண்ணி நாட்ட லுற்றேன்","பாவின் னவென்று பழிப்பாரினி யில்லை யன்றே."],"p":"","r":"The poet says he became clear as the god taught, took all he then understood as great, and, with his own ignorance as his only strength, set out to establish it in the assembly of eloquent poets, where none will now fault the verse.","n":"The last line can be read as confidence (no one will now scorn the verse, since its matter is the god's) or as a hope; மாவென்று கொண்டேன் in line 2 is also open.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thelivu","avai","pedhaimai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 5","lb":"NK5","ev":"The poet, taught by the god, sets out to present his work before the assembly of poets.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"nk:1"},"nk:6":{"book":"neelakesi","num":6,"t":["கண்டிங்கு நாளுங் கடல்வையகங் காதல் செய்யும்","வெண்டிங்க டானும் விமலந்தனக் கில்ல தன்றே","கொண்டென்சொ லெல்லாங் குணனேயெனக்கூறு கென்னே","னுண்டிங்கோர் குற்ற மெனில்யானுமொட் டாமை யுண்டோ."],"p":"","r":"Even the white moon that the sea-girt world beholds and loves every day is not spotless; so the poet will not ask that all his words be taken as merit, and if there is a fault here, he will not refuse to own it.","n":"A conventional apology to the assembly, but turned: the moon's spot is used to claim the right to a fault, not to be ashamed of it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kutram","avai","adakkam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 6","lb":"NK6","ev":"","cl":[["nothing, not even the moon, is free of flaw, so a work may be accepted with its faults acknowledged","general","asserted",2,"narrator"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"nk:1"},"nk:7":{"book":"neelakesi","num":7,"t":["தெள்ளி நரைத்துத் தெருளாதுறு தீமை செய்யும்","புள்ளி னுரையும் பொருளாமெனக் கோட லினா","லெள்ளுந் திறத்த.: துரையென்றிது நீக்க லின்றாய்க்","கொள்ளும் முலகங் குணமாணறம் வேண்டு மென்றால்."],"p":"","r":"Since people take as meaningful even the cry of a bird, the poet argues, the world, if it wants dharma of excellent virtue, should not discard this work as speech fit for scorn but accept it.","n":"Line 1 (தெள்ளி நரைத்துத் தெருளாது உறு தீமை செய்யும்) is obscure: it may describe the bird, or those who, though grey and learned, act without understanding. The argument is a fortiori: if a bird's call is heeded as an omen, a teaching of dharma deserves a hearing.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["aram","avai","igal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 7","lb":"NK7","ev":"","cl":[["those who want dharma should judge a teaching by its worth, not scorn it for its humble source","general","asserted",4,"narrator"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"nk:1"},"nk:8":{"book":"neelakesi","num":8,"t":["நாடும் மநாடா ளரசுந்நக ருந்நகர்சூழ்","காடுங் கடவுள் புகனீக்குதல் கார ணம்மாத்","தேடுஞ் சிறுபேய் பெரும்பேய்த்தியைச் சென்று பற்றும்","பாடும் மவடான் பகைகொண்டுபல் கால்வெ ருட்டி."],"p":"","r":"The lesser demons, who went searching through country, king, city and the forest around it because their god's refuge had been taken away, went and seized on the great demoness, and she, in enmity, tried many times to frighten the sage.","n":"கடவுள் புகல் நீக்குதல் (the removing of refuge in the god) refers back, in the story told later (35 to 45), to the sage stopping the blood offering at the goddess's shrine; the verse compresses that, and the one she frightens is not named here.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["anangu","vazhipaadu","pagai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 8","lb":"NK8","ev":"The lesser demons, deprived of their offerings, seek out Neelakesi, who repeatedly tries to frighten the sage.","cl":[],"ag":["சிறுபேய்","நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"ch":"nk:1"},"nk:9":{"book":"neelakesi","num":9,"t":["தான்கண்ட வன்செய் தவந்தன்னைக் கலக்க கில்லா","மான்கொண்ட நோக்கின் னவளாய்மற மாற்றி யபின்","னூன்கொண்ட காட்சி முதலாக வுடைத்த தெல்லாம்","யான்கண்ட வாறே யுரைப்பன்னவை யார்க் கிதனை."],"p":"","r":"Unable to disturb the penance of the one she had seen, she took the form of a woman with a doe's glance, and after she gave up violence, all that followed, beginning with the view that accepts flesh, the poet will tell to the assembly as he saw it.","n":"ஊன் கொண்ட காட்சி (the view that has taken flesh) names the first debate by its issue, the eating of flesh, which the Kundalakesi debate charges against the Buddhist teaching (163); the poem's polemic is announced through an ethical charge before any metaphysics.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thavam","maram","pulaal","vaadham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 9","lb":"NK9","ev":"Failing to break the sage's penance, Neelakesi takes a woman's form and gives up violence; the poet announces the debates to follow.","cl":[],"ag":["நீலகேசி","முனிச்சந்திரன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"nk:1"},"nk:10":{"book":"neelakesi","num":10,"t":["மாஞ்சோலை பொங்கி மருதங்கிளிப் பிள்ளை கள்வாய்த்","தீஞ்சா றொழுகுந் திணையின்னணி தங்கி யேங்குந்","தாஞ்சால வாழ்நா டளிரீனுந் தகைய துண்டு","பாஞ்சால மென்று பலரும்புகழ் பார்த்தி நாடே."],"p":"","r":"The poet opens on the land praised by many as Panchala, where mango groves swell, parrot chicks in the marutham country drip with sweet juice, and the long-lived country puts out fresh shoots.","n":"பார்த்தி நாடே may name the land as the king's (a form of பார்த்திவன்) or carry a personal name; lines 2 and 3 (திணையின்னணி தங்கி யேங்குந் / தாஞ்சால வாழ்நா டளிரீனுந்) do not divide cleanly, so the reading of the middle is open.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["naadu","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 10","lb":"NK10","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"nk:2"},"nk:11":{"book":"neelakesi","num":11,"t":["வாடா வளத்தான் மலர்ஞால மதிப்பின் மிக்க","நாடாவ திஃதா மதனன்னலஞ் சொன்ன லத்தாற்","கூடா தெனினுஞ் சிலகூறலும் வேண்டு மன்றே","பாடா விருந்தார் பரிவஞ்சும் படிய தன்றே."],"p":"","r":"This land of unfading wealth is the most esteemed in the flowering world; its goodness cannot be told in full, yet some must be said, and it is not a land where guests who come must fear hardship.","n":"The modesty formula (its good cannot be told, but some must be) frames the whole description that follows. Line 4 (பாடா விருந்தார் பரிவஞ்சும் படிய தன்றே) is compressed; taking it as guests not fearing want is one reading.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["naadu","virundhu","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 11","lb":"NK11","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"nk:2"},"nk:12":{"book":"neelakesi","num":12,"t":["வருபுனலன வளவயலிடை மறிவனவின வாளை","மருவினியன மகிழ்தகையன மலர்சிறையன நாரை","கருவரியன கடுநடையன கனைகுரலன கம்புள்","திருவுருவின தெரிகதிரின திசைதிசைதொறு செந்நெல்."],"p":"","r":"In the marutham fields, valai fish leap in the flowing water, herons rest with spread wings, dark-striped waterhens cry as they stride, and red paddy shines with ripe ears in every direction.","n":"Verses 12 to 16 are built on one pattern: each line piles up adjectives in -அன on a single plant or creature and ends on its name, and the five verses walk through the tinais (fields, groves, shore, forest, hills).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naadu","uzhavu","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 12","lb":"NK12","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"nk:2"},"nk:13":{"book":"neelakesi","num":13,"t":["பணைநிலையன கமுகொடுபடு பழமுதிர்வன தெங்க","மிணைநிலையன சுளைகனியிவை யினியனபல வாழை","மணனயர்வன மனையயலன மதுவிரிமணி நீலந்","திணிநிலையன திரளரையன தெரிமலரன மருதம்."],"p":"","r":"Areca palms rise among bamboo, coconuts drop ripe fruit, jack and banana bear sweet segments, fragrant blue lilies open by the houses, and sturdy marutham trees stand in flower.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naadu","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 13","lb":"NK13","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"nk:2"},"nk:14":{"book":"neelakesi","num":14,"t":["கரைதழுவிய கழிமடலின கடிகமழ்வன கைதை","புரைதழுவிய பொதியவிழ்வன பொன்மலர்வன புன்னை","விரைதழுவிய விழைதகையன வெறிமலர்விரி ஞாழல்","நிரைதழுவிய நெறிகழியிடை நிகரலரன நெய்தல்."],"p":"","r":"On the backwater shore, fragrant screwpine, golden-flowered punnai and gnazhal spread their scent, and neythal lilies bloom unmatched along the creeks.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naadu","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 14","lb":"NK14","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"nk:2"},"nk:15":{"book":"neelakesi","num":15,"t":["குருவுடையன கொடிமிடைவொடு குலைவிரிவன கோடல்","தருசுடரன தளவயலின தகைமலரன தோன்றி","யருகுடையன வணியுருவின வயலனவலர் காயா","முருகுடையன முகைவிரிவன முறியலர்வன முல்லை."],"p":"","r":"In the forest country the kodal opens its clusters among the creepers, the thondri glows like flame, the kaayaa blossoms by the fields, and the mullai opens its fragrant buds.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naadu","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 15","lb":"NK15","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"nk:2"},"nk:16":{"book":"neelakesi","num":16,"t":["நனைசினையன நகுவிரையன நலனுடையன நாகம்","வினையுடையன பொழுதிவையென விரிவனகணி வேங்கை","கனைசுடர்விடு கதிர்மணியறை களனயர்வன காந்த","ளினியனபல சுனையயலன விறுவிரையன குறிஞ்சி."],"p":"","r":"On the hills the naagam is in bud and scent, the vengai blooms as if announcing that the time for work has come, the kaanthal stands by the gem-bright rock, and the kurinji flowers beside the springs.","n":"கணி வேங்கை calls the vengai the astrologer: its flowering tells the season (வினையுடையன பொழுது இவை), an old Tamil convention the poet keeps inside a Jain work.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naadu","kaalam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 16","lb":"NK16","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"nk:2"},"nk:17":{"book":"neelakesi","num":17,"t":["ஆடலொடு பாடலவை தாமறுத லின்றிக்","கேடில்புக ழாரவைகள் கண்டுமிசை கேட்டு","மூடலொடு கூடலுணர் வார்கள்புணர் வாராய்ச்","சேடரொடு சேடியருஞ் செல்வமிக நல்கி."],"p":"","r":"The people of the land enjoy dance and song without pause, see the assemblies of the renowned and hear their music, know both the lovers' quarrel and the union that follows, and give wealth freely to their men and women servants.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inbam","oodal","eegai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 17","lb":"NK17","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"nk:2"},"nk:18":{"book":"neelakesi","num":18,"t":["தானமொடு சீலமவை தாங்கிநல மோங்கி","மானமொடு மாயமில ராயமனை யாருங்","கானமொடு கல்லடரு ளில்லிடரு நீங்கி","ஞானமொடு செய்வினைக ணையமுயல் வாரும்."],"p":"","r":"The householders keep giving and right conduct, rise in goodness with honour and without deceit, and others, having left the troubles of home for forest and hill, strive with knowledge to wear away the deeds they have done.","n":"The ideal land holds both lives side by side: the householders' தானம் and சீலம் and the striving of those who aim that their deeds should wither (செய்வினைகள் நைய முயல்வாரும்). Wearing down past karma by knowledge and effort fits Jain teaching, but the verse does not name a school, so no claim is tagged. Line 3 (கானமொடு கல்லடரு ளில்லிடரு நீங்கி) is divided here as forest, rock and leaving the home's troubles; other divisions are possible.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["ilvaazhkkai","thuravu","vinai","gnaanam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 18","lb":"NK18","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"nk:2"},"nk:19":{"book":"neelakesi","num":19,"t":["அந்தணரு மல்லவரு மாகியுட னாய","மந்தமறு நால்வருண மாட்சியின ராகித்","தந்தநெறி யிற்றிரித றானுமில ராகி","நந்திமிசை சேறலுடை நன்மையத நாடே."],"p":"","r":"Brahmins and others together make up the four varnas, flawless and excellent, none straying from the path given to it, and so the land has the good of rising ever higher.","n":"A Jain poet counts the four-varna order, each keeping its own path, among the land's merits. நந்திமிசை சேறல் in line 4 is read as growth upward (நந்தி, flourishing); it could also carry a religious sense that the verse does not make clear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pirappu","andhanar","ozhukkam","naadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 19","lb":"NK19","ev":"","cl":[["a well-ordered land has each of the four varnas keeping to its own given path","general","asserted",3,"narrator"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"nk:2"},"nk:20":{"book":"neelakesi","num":20,"t":["ஞாலமறி நன்மையுடை நாடதென லானு","மாலுமழை மூன்றுமுடை மாதமென லானுங்","காலமவை தாங்கடுமை காண்பருமை யாலும்","பாலைநில மொன்றுமவ ணின்மை பழுதன்றே."],"p":"","r":"Since the land is known for its goodness, since its months each have three rains, and since the harshness of the seasons is hardly seen there, it is no defect that it has no palai country.","n":"The verse closes the tinai catalogue of 12 to 16, which named four landscapes, by explaining away the missing fifth: the dry palai arises from drought and harsh seasons, and this land has neither. The three rains a month, elsewhere a fruit of righteous rule, here are simply the land's own goodness.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naadu","mazhai","kaalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 20","lb":"NK20","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"nk:2"},"nk:21":{"book":"neelakesi","num":21,"t":["இன்ன தன்மையி னாடினி தாளுமம்","மன்ன வன்னவன் யாரெனில் வானிடைச்","சொன்ன நீர்மைச் சுரேந்திரன் போன்றிவட்","டன்ன னாரில் சமுத்திர சாரனே."],"p":"","r":"The king who rules this land happily is Samudrasaran, among men here what Surendra, told of in heaven, is among the gods.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vendhan","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 21","lb":"NK21","ev":"Samudrasaran rules the land.","cl":[],"ag":["சமுத்திரசாரன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"nk:2"},"nk:22":{"book":"neelakesi","num":22,"t":["ஆற்ற லாலரி மாவவ னாணையாற்","கூற்ற மேயெனக் கூறலு மாங்குடி","போற்ற றாயனை யான்பொருந் தார்கண்மேற்","சீற்றத் தாற்றெறு தீத்திர ளேயனான்."],"p":"","r":"In strength he is a lion, in command he is called Death itself, to his people he is a protecting mother, and to his enemies a mass of fire in his anger.","n":"The verse joins the two faces of kingship that the ethics books keep apart, terror (கூற்றம்) and a mother's care (போற்று தாய்), and assigns them to different objects: the first to his command and his foes, the second to the குடி.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vendhan","ali","kootru","pagai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 22","lb":"NK22","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"nk:2"},"nk:23":{"book":"neelakesi","num":23,"t":["தீய தீரத் திருவிளை யாடிய","தேயங் காவல னாய்த்திசை யாவினு","மீய நீண்டகை யேந்த னகர்திசை","போய புண்ட வருத்தன மென்பதே."],"p":"","r":"This guardian of a realm where evils had ended and prosperity played, the lord whose long hands give in every direction, has his city in a quarter called Pundavarthanam.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vendhan","eegai","naadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 23","lb":"NK23","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["சமுத்திரசாரன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"nk:2"},"nk:24":{"book":"neelakesi","num":24,"t":["வளங்கெழு நெடுமதில் வாயில் யாவையு","முளம்புக விழுங்கியிட் டுமிழ்வ வொத்துமேல்","விளங்கிவெண் மதிசெலல் விலக்கி நீள்விசும்","பளந்ததன் றுணைமையு மறிவ தொத்தவே."],"p":"","r":"The gates of its tall, rich walls seem to swallow all who enter and spit them out again, and the walls rise so high that they block the white moon's course and seem to measure the sky.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aran","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 24","lb":"NK24","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"nk:2"},"nk:25":{"book":"neelakesi","num":25,"t":["விரைசெல லிவுளியும் வேழ வீட்டமு","நிரைசெலற் கொடுஞ்சிநன் னேமி யூர்தியு","மரசுடைப் பெருங்கடை நெருங்கு மார்கலி","திரைபொரு கடலொலி யன்ன செம்மற்றே."],"p":"","r":"Swift horses, herds of elephants and rows of fine-wheeled chariots crowd the great gate of the royal palace, with a roar like the sea beating its waves.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["padai","vendhan","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 25","lb":"NK25","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"nk:2"},"nk:26":{"book":"neelakesi","num":26,"t":["அகிற்புகை யளாவியு மணிகொள் வீதியிற்","றுகிற்கொடித் தொகுதியுந் தூய சுண்ணமு","முகிற்றலைக் கலலிவான் மூடி மாநகர்","பகற்கிடை கொடாததோர் பான்மை மிக்கதே."],"p":"","r":"Smoke of agil, clusters of cloth banners on the jewelled streets and pure fragrant powder rise to the clouds and cover the sky, so that the great city gives no room to daylight.","n":"Line 3 (முகிற்றலைக் கலலிவான் மூடி) has a doubtful word (கலலி); the sense of the clouded sky is clear from the rest.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 26","lb":"NK26","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"nk:2"},"nk:27":{"book":"neelakesi","num":27,"t":["ஆங்க மாநக ரணைந்தது பலாலைய மென்னும்","பங்கொள் பேரதவ் வூரது பிணம்படு பெருங்கா","டேங்கு கம்பலை யிரவினும் பகலினு மிகலி","யாங்கு நீர்வையத் தோசையிற் போயதொன் றுளதே."],"p":"","r":"Near that great city is a town called Palalaiyam, and it has a great burning ground where corpses fall, whose clamour, rising night and day, has spread its fame over the water-girt world.","n":"The poem moves straight from the splendid capital to its cremation ground: the prosperity of 10 to 26 is the frame for the place where the story begins. பங்கொள் in line 2 is doubtful.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sudalai","saakkaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 27","lb":"NK27","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"nk:2"},"nk:28":{"book":"neelakesi","num":28,"t":["விண்டு நீண்டன வேய்களும் வாகையும் விரவி","யிண்டு மீங்கையு மிருள்பட மிடைந்தவற் றிடையே","குண்டு கண்ணின பேய்களுங் கூகையுங் குழறிக்","கண்ட மாந்தர்தம் மனங்களைக் கலமலக் குறுக்கும்."],"p":"","r":"Tall bamboos and vaagai trees mingle with thorny indu and eengai so thickly that it is dark, and among them deep-eyed ghouls and owls cry out and throw the minds of those who see it into turmoil.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sudalai","anangu","acham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 28","lb":"NK28","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"nk:2"},"nk:29":{"book":"neelakesi","num":29,"t":["ஈமத் தூமமு மெரியினு மிருளொடு விளக்கா","வூமைக் கூகையு மோரியு முறழுறழ் கதிக்கும்","யாமத் தீண்டிவந் தாண்டலை மாண்பில வழைக்கும்","தீமைக் கேயிட னாயதோர் செம்மலை யுடைத்தே."],"p":"","r":"The smoke and flames of the pyres are its lamps in the dark; owls and jackals howl in turn; at midnight the aandalai birds gather and call; it is a ground given over to evil.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sudalai","anangu","acham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 29","lb":"NK29","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"nk:2"},"nk:30":{"book":"neelakesi","num":30,"t":["வெள்ளின் மாலையும் விரிந்தவெண் டலைகளுங் கரிந்த","கொள்ளி மலையுங் கொடிபடு கூறையு மகலும்","பள்ளி மாறிய பாடையு மெலும்புமே பரந்து","கள்ளி யாரிடைக் கலந்ததோர் தோற்றமுங் கடிதே."],"p":"","r":"Garlands, spread white skulls, charred firebrands, torn cloth from the biers, abandoned biers and bones lie scattered among the cactus, a fearful sight.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sudalai","yaakkai","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 30","lb":"NK30","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"nk:2"},"nk:31":{"book":"neelakesi","num":31,"t":["காக்கை யார்ப்பன கழுதுதங் கிளையொடு கதறித்","தூக்க ளீர்ப்பன தொடர்ந்தபல் பிணங்களுந் தூங்கச்","சேக்கை கொள்வன செஞ்செவி யெருவையு மருவி","யாக்கை கொண்டவர்க் கணைதலுக் கரிதது பெரிதும்."],"p":"","r":"Crows clamour, ghouls shriek with their kin and drag at the hanged, many corpses dangle, red-eared vultures roost there, so that it is very hard for any who have a body to come near.","n":"யாக்கை கொண்டவர்க்கு (for those who have taken a body) names the living by their bodies at the very place where bodies end; the phrase prepares the sage's choice to live there.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sudalai","yaakkai","saakkaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 31","lb":"NK31","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"nk:2"},"nk:32":{"book":"neelakesi","num":32,"t":["கோளி யாலமுங் கோழரை மரங்களும் குழுமித்","தூளி யார்த்தெழு சுடலையு முடலமுந் துவன்றி","மீளி யாக்கைய தாக்கியுண் பேய்க்கண மிகைசூழ்","கூளி தாய்க்கென வாக்கிய கோட்டமொன் றுளதே."],"p":"","r":"Where fierce banyans and thick-trunked trees gather, the dust of the pyres rises and bodies crowd, and swarming hosts of demons that strike down and eat bodies press round, there stands a shrine made for the mother of the kooli demons.","n":"The goddess is not named; கூளி தாய் (mother of the demon hosts) and இறைவி (33) mark her as the burning-ground goddess whom the next verses show receiving a lamb's blood (35). The flesh-eating demon hosts around her shrine set up the poem's first quarrel, over offering flesh.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sudalai","anangu","vazhipaadu","pulaal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 32","lb":"NK32","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"nk:2"},"nk:33":{"book":"neelakesi","num":33,"t":["இறைவி கோட்டத்து ளீரிரு திங்கள தகவை","யுறையு ளாகவவ் வுறையருங் காட்டகத் துறைவான்","பொறையு மாற்றலும் பூமியு மேருவு மனையான் தான்.","சிறைசெய் சிந்தைய னந்தமில் பொருள்களைத் தெரிந்"],"p":"","r":"In the goddess's shrine, taking it as his dwelling for four months, there lived in that forest no one could dwell in a man like the earth in patience and Meru in strength, of restrained mind, who had discerned the endless things...","n":"The sentence runs on into 34, which names him (முனிச்சந்திரன்). உறையருங் காட்டகத்து உறைவான் plays on the word: he dwells in the place no one can dwell, so his first act is a feat of endurance. Four months (ஈரிரு திங்கள்) fits the rains retreat when Jain monks stay in one place, though the verse does not say so.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thavam","porai","thuravu","sudalai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 33","lb":"NK33","ev":"A sage takes the goddess's shrine in the burning ground as his dwelling for four months.","cl":[],"ag":["முனிச்சந்திரன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"nk:2"},"nk:34":{"book":"neelakesi","num":34,"t":["அத்தி காயங்க ளளவைக ளாலளந் தறிவான்","குத்தி யாதிய குணங்களிற் பெரியவ னரிய","பத்தின் மேலிரு தவத்தினிற் பவத்தொட ரறுக்கும்","முத்தின் யான்முனிச் சந்திர னெனும்பெயர் முனிவன்."],"p":"","r":"The narrator introduces the sage named Munichandra: he knows the existents by measuring them with the means of valid knowledge, is great in the virtues that begin with the restraints, and by the twelve kinds of penance cuts the chain of births.","n":"The vocabulary is technical Jain: அத்திகாயங்கள் (the astikayas, the extended existents), குத்தி (gupti, the restraints of mind, speech and body) and பத்தின் மேல் இரு, ten and two, the twelvefold penance. The sage is defined first as one who knows by அளவை, which prepares his later role as teacher of a debater. The opening of line 4 (முத்தின் யான்) does not construe clearly.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thavam","alavai","piravi","arivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 34","lb":"NK34","ev":"","cl":[["the twelvefold penance cuts the chain of births","jain","asserted",3,"narrator"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"nk:3"},"nk:35":{"book":"neelakesi","num":35,"t":["அன்றக் கோட்டத்து ளறிவிலா மறிதலை யறுப்பான்","சென்ற தெய்வதைக் கெனச்சிலர் சிறப்பயர் பொழுதின்","நின்றக் கோண்மின மெனச் சொல்லி நெறியறி வுறுவோ","னொன்றற் பல்வகை யுயிர்க் கொலை யுரைமின மெனவே."],"p":"","r":"At the temple, when some were carrying out the rite of cutting off the head of a goat that knew nothing for the deity, the sage who knew the way told them to stop and to say why they would kill a life for one end.","n":"The sage's first move is a question, not a ban: he asks the worshippers to give their reason, which sets up the exchange of 36 to 40 as a small debate. The goat is called அறிவிலா மறி, the goat without understanding, which makes its killing the taking of a life that cannot consent.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kollaamai","bali","vazhipaadu","kolai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 35","lb":"NK35","ev":"As worshippers prepare to behead a goat for the temple deity, the sage stops them and asks why they kill.","cl":[],"ag":["கொலையாளர்","முனிச்சந்திரன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"nk:3"},"nk:36":{"book":"neelakesi","num":36,"t":["பண்டிந்நின்ற பணைத்தோளி பாலற்பெ றாமையைக்","கண்டியாமிக் கணமோடி தன்பாற்சொன் னோமாக","வுண்டதாயிற் றோர்குழவி யென்னவுவப் பித்தற்குக்","கொண்டுவந்தே மறியறுக்க வென்றார் கொலையாளர்."],"p":"","r":"The killers answer that the broad-shouldered woman standing here had no child, that they made a vow about it to this goddess Modi of the host, that a child was born, and that they have brought the goat to cut it in thanks.","n":"மோடி is a name of the fierce goddess; the sacrifice is a vow repaid (நேர்த்தி in later usage), a gift for a gift. The poem names the worshippers by their act, கொலையாளர், the killers, before they have argued at all.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","bali","makkal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 36","lb":"NK36","ev":"The worshippers explain that the goddess granted a barren woman a child and the goat is the vowed thank-offering.","cl":[["a deity who has granted a child must be repaid with the vowed blood offering","general","refuted",4,"kolaiyaalar"]],"ag":["கொலையாளர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["கொலையாளர்","முனிச்சந்திரன்"],"ch":"nk:3"},"nk:37":{"book":"neelakesi","num":37,"t":["ஊனுடம் போவுயிரோ வுறுகுழவி யாத","றேனொடுங்குங் குழலாட்குத் தேவர்மன னுந்தந்த","தூனுடம் பென்னி லுதிரமா முயிரென்னின்","மானிடமாம் வினைமேலைச் செய்தன்றோ வந்ததென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"The sage asks which became the child, the flesh-body or the life: if the body, it is of the parents' blood; if the life, it came to a human birth by the deed it did before; so what did the god give the woman with honeyed hair?","n":"The argument splits the child into body and soul and finds no share left for the goddess: the body has a natural cause, the soul a karmic one. It denies the gift, not the goddess's existence, so the vow lapses for want of anything to repay.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","piravi","uyir","yaakkai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 37","lb":"NK37","ev":"The sage answers that the child's body came from its parents' blood and its life from its own former deeds.","cl":[["a child's body comes from the parents' blood and its life from its own former deeds, so no deity gave it","jain","asserted",4,"munichandran"]],"ag":["முனிச்சந்திரன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிச்சந்திரன்","கொலையாளர்"],"ch":"nk:3"},"nk:38":{"book":"neelakesi","num":38,"t":["ஏறியானை யிருங்கலைக ணேர்ந்தா ரவையிவையென்","றூறங்கி யுருவுருசெய் தாலுமுவந் தொழிபவான்","மாறுகோ ளிலைமண்ணான் மறியுருசெய் தீந்தக்காற்","பாறினீர்க் கும்மவர்க்கும் பழிபாவ மொன்றிலையே"],"p":"","r":"The sage proposes that, since the gods are content even when what was vowed to them is made in figure, the worshippers make a goat of clay and give it, and then neither they nor the gods will bear blame or sin.","n":"A pastoral compromise: the sage does not ask the worshippers to break their vow but to keep it with an image, and he speaks as if the gods accept this. Lines 1 and 2 (ஏறி யானை இருங்கலைகள் நேர்ந்தார், perhaps vows of an elephant to ride or great stags) do not construe securely; the clay goat of line 3 is plain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kollaamai","vazhipaadu","pazhi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 38","lb":"NK38","ev":"The sage tells them to offer a clay goat in place of the living one.","cl":[["a vowed living offering is discharged without sin by offering its image in clay","jain","asserted",3,"munichandran"]],"ag":["முனிச்சந்திரன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிச்சந்திரன்","கொலையாளர்"],"ch":"nk:3"},"nk:39":{"book":"neelakesi","num":39,"t":["கொன்ற வன்னே கொடியனென வுலகங் கூறு மதனாலு","மொன்ற நூலா ருரைகளோ டொப்ப முடியு மதனாலு","மின்றி னின்று மிதுவொழிதி ராயி னுங்கட் கிருமைக்கு","நன்றி தென்றான் வெந்நரகம் புகுதல் விலக்கு நாவினான்."],"p":"","r":"The sage whose tongue keeps men from the hot hell tells them that the world calls the killer cruel and the words of the scriptures agree, so if they give up this practice from today it will be good for them in both lives.","n":"He argues from two authorities his hearers already accept, common opinion and the scriptures, not from Jain doctrine; the appeal is to a shared ground before the doctrinal list of 40.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kollaamai","naragam","marumai","pazhi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 39","lb":"NK39","ev":"The sage urges the worshippers to give up killing from that day.","cl":[["the world and the scriptures both condemn the killer, and giving up killing benefits this life and the next","general","asserted",1,"munichandran"]],"ag":["முனிச்சந்திரன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிச்சந்திரன்","கொலையாளர்"],"ch":"nk:3"},"nk:40":{"book":"neelakesi","num":40,"t":["கோறல் பொய்த்தல் கொடுங்களவு நீக்கிப் பிறர்ம னைகண்மேற்","சேற லின்றிச் செழும்பொருண்மேற் சென்ற சிந்தை வேட்கையினை","யாறு கிற்பி னமருலக நுங்கட் கடிய தாமென்றான்","நீறு மோடு நிழன்மணியும் பொன்னு நிகரா நோக்குவான்."],"p":"","r":"The sage, who looks on ash, potsherd, shining gem and gold alike, tells them that if they put away killing, lying and theft, do not go after other men's wives, and calm the mind's craving for wealth, the world of the gods will be within their reach.","n":"These are the five lesser vows of the Jain householder (not killing, not lying, not stealing, not going to another's wife, limiting possessions), given in order. The reward named is the world of the immortals, not release: the sage asks of lay worshippers a householder's discipline, while his own epithet (gold and potsherd alike) marks the ascetic's equanimity.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kollaamai","vaaimai","piranil","avaa"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 40","lb":"NK40","ev":"The sage gives the worshippers the five abstentions of the householder.","cl":[["one who avoids killing, lying, theft and others' wives and curbs the craving for wealth reaches the world of the gods","jain","asserted",3,"munichandran"]],"ag":["முனிச்சந்திரன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிச்சந்திரன்","கொலையாளர்"],"ch":"nk:3"},"nk:41":{"book":"neelakesi","num":41,"t":["ஏத்து தற்கேற் றானிரங்கி யின்ன வைசொல் லக்கேட்டுப்","பாத்தி யோயெம் பழவினையும் பாறு கென்று பணிந்துதாம்","யாத்து நின்ற வம்மறியும் மறமு முடனே கொண்டுபோய்க்","காத்து மென்றார் கருவினையு ணீங்கு நல்ல கருத்தினார்."],"p":"","r":"Hearing the compassionate words of the one worthy of praise, the worshippers bowed, prayed that their old deeds too might be undone, and took the tethered goat away with them, promising to keep it safe.","n":"The converts at once think of their own karma (பழவினை), so the sage's argument about the child's karma in 37 has been taken home. Line 3 (அம்மறியும் மறமும்) can be read as taking away the goat and their violence, or the goat and the dharma (அறமும்); either way the goat leaves alive.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","arul","kollaamai","panivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 41","lb":"NK41","ev":"The worshippers bow to the sage, pray to be freed of their old deeds, and lead the goat away alive.","cl":[],"ag":["கொலையாளர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"nk:3"},"nk:42":{"book":"neelakesi","num":42,"t":["ஆய மெல்லா மதுசொல்லிப் போக வவணே வாழ்கின்ற","பேயுங் கூடிப் பெரிதுமகி சூழ்ந்து தம்பெற் றிசொல்லி","னாயு மாக்க ருத்துமில னாவ னிவனங் கட்கென்னிற்","றீயு மன்னென் றேற்றகரு மையாலெனுஞ்சிந் தையிலவாய்"],"p":"","r":"When the whole company had gone away telling of it, the demons who lived in that place gathered in great distress and spoke of their plight, saying that this man had no regard for them at all.","n":"The cost of the conversion falls on the spirits of the place, who lose the offerings they lived on. Lines 3 and 4 are obscure and the demons' words cannot be fixed.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["anangu","bali","idumbai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 42","lb":"NK42","ev":"After the worshippers leave, the demons of the temple gather to lament.","cl":[],"ag":["சிறுபேய்"],"ec":[],"ch":"nk:3"},"nk:43":{"book":"neelakesi","num":43,"t":["நிரந்து வெங்கதி ரெழுதலி னிற்றலை யிலதாய்க்","கரந்த காரிருள் போற்கணங் காண்டலுக் கரிதாய்ப்","பரந்த நாம்பல நாடுகள் பாடிக ணாடி (றெண்ணி)","யிரந்தோர் வன்றெய்வங் கொணர்ந்திவற் கடிதுமென்"],"p":"","r":"The demons say that when the hot sun rises they have no standing, being hidden like dark night and hard to see, and they resolve to roam many lands and settlements and bring back a strong deity to drive this man away.","n":"The demons are weak by day and must borrow strength; the plan of 44 to 46 begins here. The verse is defective: line 3 carries an editor's bracketed (றெண்ணி) and line 4 ends short.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["anangu","pagai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 43","lb":"NK43","ev":"The demons resolve to search for a powerful deity to drive the sage off.","cl":[],"ag":["சிறுபேய்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சிறுபேய்",""],"ch":"nk:3"},"nk:44":{"book":"neelakesi","num":44,"t":["ஆசு மிங்கிருந் தினியென்னை எழுகவென் றயல","காசி நாட்டினுஞ் சேடிய நாட்டினுங் காணா","தேசந் தாம்பல திரியவத் தென்றிசை நீல","கேசி மாதெய்வந் தலைப்பட்டுக் கிளர்ந்தின்ன வுரைக்கும்."],"p":"","r":"Saying there is no use staying, they set out through the Kasi country, the Chedi country and many lands without finding one, and in the southern direction they met the great goddess Neelakesi and eagerly told her this.","n":"Neelakesi is here a மாதெய்வம், a great deity, where the invocation called her a பேய் (4); the poem lets the demons' own estimate of her stand. She is found in the south after the northern lands fail.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["anangu","thunai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 44","lb":"NK44","ev":"The demons search Kasi, Chedi and other lands in vain and find the goddess Neelakesi in the south.","cl":[],"ag":["சிறுபேய்","நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"ch":"nk:3"},"nk:45":{"book":"neelakesi","num":45,"t":["வலிசெய் தெம்மிடம் புகுந்தடு மடையொடு முடைசேர்","பலியு மூட்டுதல் பாவமீ தெனப்பலர்க் குரைத்துக்","கலிகொள் காடுதன் காற்பொடி யாகவுங் கருதா","னலைசெய் தானெமை யாமுனக் கபயமென் றழுத."],"p":"","r":"The demons weep to Neelakesi that the sage forced his way into their place, told many that feeding the bali of cooked food and flesh is sin, does not care what becomes of their clamorous forest, and has tormented them, and they put themselves under her protection.","n":"The sage's teaching reaches us here in the mouth of its victims: what the worshippers heard as a release, the demons report as an invasion (வலிசெய்து புகுந்து). The meaning of line 3 is not secure.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["bali","adaikkalam","anangu","pulaal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 45","lb":"NK45","ev":"The demons complain to Neelakesi that the sage has stopped their offerings and ask her protection.","cl":[],"ag":["சிறுபேய்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சிறுபேய்","நீலகேசி"],"ch":"nk:3"},"nk:46":{"book":"neelakesi","num":46,"t":["அழுவ தென்செய அருந்தவம் வலித்தவ னிருந்து","பொழுது போக்குதல் புரிந்தனன் பொருத்தமஃ துடைத்தே","கழுகு தாமுணக் காட்டுவ னெனக்கைகள் புடையா","வெழுக வென்றுசென் றிடுபிணப் பறந்தலை யிருந்தாள்."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi asks why they weep: let the one who is set on hard penance sit there passing his time, and she will show him to the vultures to eat; beating her hands and crying 'rise', she went and sat in the field where corpses are laid.","n":"She treats the penance as idleness (பொழுது போக்குதல்); the phrase பொருத்தம் அஃது உடைத்தே (that has its fitness) reads as scorn, though its exact turn is not certain.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["anangu","thavam","pagai","saakkaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 46","lb":"NK46","ev":"Neelakesi promises to feed the sage to the vultures and goes to the cremation ground where he sits.","cl":[],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","சிறுபேய்"],"ch":"nk:3"},"nk:47":{"book":"neelakesi","num":47,"t":["இருட்டி ருட்டென நடந்துசென் றெழுந்தெழுந் திருக்கும்","வெருட்ட லன்னினை விழுங்குவ னெனத்தன்னை வியக்கு","மருட்டி றம்மில னறியினி யருவரை நெடுங்கோட்","டுருட்டு வேனென வுயர்தவத் தவன்முன்னை யுரைக்கும்."],"p":"","r":"Going through the dark and rising up again and again, she boasts of herself before the ascetic, saying she will swallow him if she frightens him and that she will roll the high peak of a mountain onto him.","n":"Her threats are self-praise (தன்னை வியக்கும்); the line that seems to say he has no power to delude or to resist (மருட்டிறம் இலன்) is damaged by word division.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["anangu","agandhai","acham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 47","lb":"NK47","ev":"Neelakesi appears before the sage in the dark and threatens to swallow him and crush him with a mountain.","cl":[],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","முனிச்சந்திரன்"],"ch":"nk:3"},"nk:48":{"book":"neelakesi","num":48,"t":["சீல நல்லன சினவரன் றிருமொழி தெளிந்தான்","கால மூன்றினுங் கடையில்பல் பொருளுணர் வுடையான்","மேலு மின்னபல் வியந்தரம் வெருட்டுத லறிவான்","நீல கேசிதன் னெறியின்மை யிதுவென நினைந்தான்."],"p":"","r":"The sage, who had clearly grasped the good precepts and the holy word of the Jina, who knew without limit the many things of the three times, and who knew besides that the vyantara spirits frighten in this way, judged that this was Neelakesi's want of the right path.","n":"வியந்தரம் is the Jain class of wandering demigods (vyantara): the sage meets the goddess with a category, as a known kind of being acting according to its kind, not as a power to be feared. Her violence is read as a lack of நெறி, to be cured, not as wickedness.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arivu","anangu","ozhukkam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 48","lb":"NK48","ev":"The sage recognizes the terrors as the work of a vyantara spirit and judges her to lack the right path.","cl":[["vyantara spirits frighten people with such displays","jain","asserted",3,"narrator"]],"ag":["முனிச்சந்திரன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"nk:3"},"nk:49":{"book":"neelakesi","num":49,"t":["வெருட்டு மாகிலும் வெருட்டுக விகுர்வணை களினாற்","றெருட்டு வேனிவ டிறமின்மை சிறிதிடைப் படலும்","பொருட்டி றங்களைப் புலமையிற் புனைந்துரை பெறுமே","லருட்டி றந்நல வறநெறி பெறுதலு மறிந்தான்."],"p":"","r":"The sage knew that, though she frighten him with her transformations, he would make her understand her own powerlessness, and that if in a little while she gained the arts of meaning through learning, she would come to the good path of dharma and grace.","n":"விகுர்வணை is the Jain term for a deity's power to change form (vikurvana). The sage plans to teach, not to defeat: the line about learning the arts of meaning (பொருட்டிறங்களைப் புலமையில்) foreshadows her later career as a debater.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arul","aram","kalvi","thelivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 49","lb":"NK49","ev":"The sage resolves to bear her terrors and foresees that she will come to the dharma.","cl":[],"ag":["முனிச்சந்திரன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிச்சந்திரன்",""],"ch":"nk:3"},"nk:50":{"book":"neelakesi","num":50,"t":["மாக மேயுற மலையன்ன சிலையொடு சிலையா","மேக மேயென விசும்பிடை வெடிபட விடியா","நாக மேயென நாவினை நீட்டுவ காட்டாப்","பாக மேயெனப் பலவெனச் சிலவென வுலவும்."],"p":"","r":"She rises to touch the sky like a mountain, roars like rock struck on rock, bursts like a cloud thundering through the air, thrusts out her tongue like a serpent, and roams about as many and as few.","n":"The run 50 to 54 is a catalogue of a deity's transformations, each verse built on repeated similes; பலவெனச் சிலவென (as many, as few) shows her changing number as well as shape.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["anangu","acham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 50","lb":"NK50","ev":"Neelakesi takes terrifying forms to frighten the sage.","cl":[],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"ch":"nk:3"},"nk:51":{"book":"neelakesi","num":51,"t":["இலங்கு நீளெயிற் றிடையிடை யழலெழச் சிரியாக்","கலங்கு மார்ப்பொடு சார்ப்படு மழையெனத் தெழியாப்","பிலங்கண் டன்னதன் பெருமுழை வாய்திறந் தழையா","மலங்க நின்றுதன் மடனெடு மயிர்க்கையிட் டுயிர்க்கும்."],"p":"","r":"She laughs with fire flaring between her long gleaming fangs, roars with a din like a storm of rain, opens the great cave of her mouth like a pit and calls, and stands to bewilder him, thrusting her hand into her long hair and panting.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["anangu","acham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 51","lb":"NK51","ev":"Neelakesi takes terrifying forms to frighten the sage.","cl":[],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"ch":"nk:3"},"nk:52":{"book":"neelakesi","num":52,"t":["பொங்கு பூமியுட்பொடிபட வடியிணை புடையாப்","பங்க மேசெய்து படபட வயிறடித் திறுகி","யங்கி போலவீழ்ந் தலறிநின் றுலறியங் காக்கு","மெங்குந் தானென வெரிகொள்ளி வளையெனத் திரியும்."],"p":"","r":"She stamps her feet so the earth is shattered to dust, beats her belly, falls like fire screaming, and whirls everywhere like a circle of burning brands.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["anangu","acham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 52","lb":"NK52","ev":"Neelakesi takes terrifying forms to frighten the sage.","cl":[],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"ch":"nk:3"},"nk:53":{"book":"neelakesi","num":53,"t":["கல்லி னாற்கடுங் கனலினுங் கடுகென வெடிக்கும்","வில்லின் வாய்ப்பெய்து விளங்குவெண் பகழிகள் விடுக்கும்","மல்லி னாற்சென்று மறித்திடு வேனென நெறிக்கும்","பல்லி னாற்பல பிணங்களி னிணங்களைப் பகிரும்."],"p":"","r":"She hurls stones fiercer than fire, sets white arrows to a bow and shoots them, grinds her teeth saying she will close with him in wrestling, and with those teeth divides the fat of many corpses.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["anangu","acham","pulaal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 53","lb":"NK53","ev":"Neelakesi takes terrifying forms to frighten the sage.","cl":[],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"ch":"nk:3"},"nk:54":{"book":"neelakesi","num":54,"t":["ஓடு முட்குடை யுருவுகொண் டருவென வொளிக்கும்","பாடு பாணியிற் பலபல கலகல வொலியா","ஆடு நாடக மரும்பசி களைகென விரும்பி","ஊடு போவனென் றுரைத்துரைத் துள்ளஞ்செய் தொழியும்."],"p":"","r":"She takes a form and then hides as if formless, sings with a clattering din, dances a drama crying that her great hunger be eased, and says again and again that she will pass right through him before she gives over.","n":"அரும்பசி களைக, ease my great hunger: in the middle of the terrors the goddess's own need shows, the hunger left by the offerings the sage has stopped (45). Line 1 (ஓடு முட்குடை) is obscure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["anangu","pasi","bali"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 54","lb":"NK54","ev":"Neelakesi takes terrifying forms to frighten the sage.","cl":[],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"ch":"nk:3"},"nk:55":{"book":"neelakesi","num":55,"t":["குஞ்ச ரம்பெருங் கொடுவரி கடுவிடை கொலைசூ","ழஞ்சு தன்மைய வடலரி யெனவின்ன பிறவும்","வெஞ்சி னம்பெரி துடையன விவையினும் வெருளான்","றஞ்ச மன்றிவன் றவநிறை சுடுமெனத் தவிர்ந்தாள்."],"p":"","r":"Though she became elephant, great striped tiger, fierce bull, killing lion and other beasts of great rage, he did not flinch, and she held back, thinking this was no refuge for her and that the fullness of his penance would burn her.","n":"The sage does nothing: his penance acts by being unmoved, and it is the assailant who imagines it as fire. The story bears her thought out, since she withdraws and in time takes his dharma (4).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thavam","anjaamai","anangu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 55","lb":"NK55","ev":"The sage stays unmoved through her beast forms, and Neelakesi gives up the attack.","cl":[["the fullness of an ascetic's penance burns whoever assails him","general","asserted",4,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"ch":"nk:3"},"nk:56":{"book":"neelakesi","num":56,"t":["அச்ச மேயுறுத் தழிக்குவன் தவமென அறியேன்","விச்சை வேறிலன் விழுக்குண முடையனிவ் விறலோன்","இச்சையாலன்றி யிவன்முன்னை நிலையெனக் கரிதா","நச்சு மெய்யென நடுங்கும் னுடம்பென வொடுங்கி."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi says she had thought to destroy his penance by fear but did not know him: this victor has no other magic but great virtue, and unless he wills it she cannot come before him; and she shrank, her body trembling as if poisoned.","n":"She sets virtue (விழுக்குணம்) against magic (விச்சை): she reads his strength as the absence of the very powers she was using. Line 1's construction (அழிக்குவன் தவம் என அறியேன்) can also mean she did not know he would undo her; the sense of defeat is clear either way.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thavam","panpu","acham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 56","lb":"NK56","ev":"Neelakesi admits she cannot overcome the sage and shrinks back trembling.","cl":[["an ascetic needs no magic: his virtue alone makes him unassailable","general","asserted",2,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி",""],"ch":"nk:3"},"nk:57":{"book":"neelakesi","num":57,"t":["ஆற்றல் சான்றவ னருந்தவ வழலெனை யடுமான்","மாற்று மாறென்கொ லெனநனி மனத்தினு ணினையாச்","சீற்றந் தீர்ந்தென்செய் கருவினை தணிகெனப் பணிந்தாள்","கூற்றம் போல்வதோர்கொடுமையையுடையவள் குறைந்தே."],"p":"","r":"Seeing that the sage's power is such that the fire of his austerity would destroy her and there is no way to counter it, the demoness of Death-like cruelty drops her rage, humbled, and bows, praying that her cruel deeds be quenched.","n":"The verse names the sage by his ஆற்றல் (power) and her by Death, கூற்றம்: the contest is staged as austerity against death. Whether the bow is already pretence is left open; the next verse shows her planning a feigned worship with the same kind of plea.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thavam","vinai","acham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 57","lb":"NK57","ev":"Neelakesi, unable to frighten the sage, ends her rage and bows to him, asking that her cruel karma be quenched.","cl":[],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"ch":"nk:4"},"nk:58":{"book":"neelakesi","num":58,"t":["சிந்தித் தாளிது செறியெயிற் றரிவைய துருவாய்ப்","பந்தித் தாகிய பழவினை கெடுகெனப் படிற்றால்","வந்தித் தியான்கொண்ட வடிவினின் மனநிறை யழித்தா","னொந்தித் தீநிகர் நோன்புகை விடுமிவ னெனவே."],"p":"","r":"She reasons that if she takes the shape of a young woman and, under cover of a feigned worship that prays for her old bound deeds to perish, breaks his inner restraint in that shape, he will abandon his fire-like vow.","n":"The plan turns the language of release from karma (பழவினை கெடுக) into bait: the plea a penitent would make is to be her disguise. Her target is named precisely: not his body but his மனநிறை, the containment of his mind.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vanjam","nirai","nonbu","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 58","lb":"NK58","ev":"Neelakesi resolves to take a young woman's form and break the sage's vow through feigned worship.","cl":[],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி",""],"ch":"nk:4"},"nk:59":{"book":"neelakesi","num":59,"t":["யாம நீங்கலு மரசன்ற னொருமக ளுரைசால்","காம லேகைதன் னுருவொடு திருவெனத் தோன்றித்","தாமஞ் சாந்துதண் மலரின்ன பலகொண்டு துணைசால்","சேமங் காவல சேவடி போற்றெனச் சென்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"At the end of the night she appears in the very form of Kamalekai, the king's only daughter, radiant as the goddess of fortune, and goes with garlands, sandal paste and cool flowers to worship.","n":"She borrows a real person's form, the king's daughter, so the deceit also puts a princess's good name at risk. Line 4's words of praise (சேமம் காவல சேவடி போற்று) may be addressed to the shrine's guardian; who they address is not plain.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vanjam","azhagu","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 59","lb":"NK59","ev":"Neelakesi appears in the form of the princess Kamalekai and sets out with offerings to worship.","cl":[],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"ch":"nk:4"},"nk:60":{"book":"neelakesi","num":60,"t":["வணங்கி வந்திடம் வலங்கொண்டு வழிபடு பொழுதில்","கணங்க டாம்பல கடன்சொல்லிக் கலந்தெடுத் தேத்தித்","துணங்கை யாடத்தன் றுகிலிடை மேகலை துளங்க","வணங்கு மெய்யவ ளருந்தவ னுழைவர நினைந்தாள்."],"p":"","r":"As she bows, circles the place and worships, troops sing the praises due and dance the thunangai while her girdle sways under her garment, and she, in her lovely form, resolves to approach the ascetic.","n":"Who the கணங்கள் are, her own spirit retinue playing attendants or the shrine's troops, is not said.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","vanjam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 60","lb":"NK60","ev":"Neelakesi worships at the shrine while troops sing and dance, then resolves to go near the sage.","cl":[],"ag":["நீலகேசி","கணங்கள்"],"ec":[],"ch":"nk:4"},"nk:61":{"book":"neelakesi","num":61,"t":["காவ லாளகுங் கடையிறந் திவண்வர வொழிக","வேவ லாளரு மிதற்கெய்து மியல்குறை முயல்க","கூலி யான்குறை யுளதெனக் குறுகுமி னமரென்","றோவில் பல்புக ழுறுதவ னறியநின் றுரைக்கும்."],"p":"","r":"Standing where the famed ascetic can hear, she orders the guards to stop at the gate and come no further, the servants to see to what is needed, and her people to come close when she has some want.","n":"The speech is played for the overhearing sage: a princess's orders to her household are her credentials. Line 3 (கூலி யான் குறை உளது) is obscure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vanjam","kurippu","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 61","lb":"NK61","ev":"Neelakesi, as the princess, dismisses her guards and servants within earshot of the sage.","cl":[],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி",""],"ch":"nk:4"},"nk:62":{"book":"neelakesi","num":62,"t":["ஆண்டைக் கோட்டத்தை அணைந்ததோ ரகலிலை யால","மாண்டைக் காயதோர் மரமுத லிருந்தமா தவனைக்","காண்டக் காயென்செய்கருவினை தணிக்கெனப் பணிந்தாள்","வேண்டிக் கொண்டவவ் வியத்தகுவிளங்குரு வுடையாள்"],"p":"","r":"Coming to the shrine, she bows to the great ascetic seated at the foot of a broad-leaved banyan and prays that her cruel deeds be quenched, she who wears the wondrous shining form she had wished for.","n":"Line 3 repeats the plea of verse 57 almost word for word (என்செய் கருவினை தணிக்க), now spoken through a borrowed body as a lure.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vanjam","vinai","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 62","lb":"NK62","ev":"Neelakesi, disguised, bows to the sage under the banyan and asks that her karma be quenched.","cl":[],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"ch":"nk:4"},"nk:63":{"book":"neelakesi","num":63,"t":["வேண்டிய வுருவத னாலும் வேட்கைசெய் யுருவத னாலுங்","காண்டகு மடவர லுருவங் காமுறு வதுநனி தாங்கி","யீண்டிய மிகுகுணத் திறைவ னியல்பினை யெனையது நினையா","நீண்டதோர் கொடியயற் கொடிபோ னிறைதவ வருளென நின்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"Bearing a desired and desire-making form, the beauty of a young woman, and giving no thought at all to the nature of the lord rich in virtues, she stands like a creeper beside a tall creeper and asks the sage of full austerity for his grace.","n":"The simile of a creeper by a creeper puts her as clinging to him; the verse stresses that the form is both chosen (வேண்டிய) and made to rouse desire (வேட்கைசெய்).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","vanjam","azhagu","thavam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 63","lb":"NK63","ev":"Neelakesi stands close beside the sage in her borrowed form and asks for his grace.","cl":[],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"ch":"nk:4"},"nk:64":{"book":"neelakesi","num":64,"t":["உடம்பொடு முயிரிடை மிடைந்த வொற்றுமை வேற்றுமை விகற்பிற்","றொடர்ந்தபல் வினைகளைத் துணிக்குஞ் சுதநெறி முறைமையு மறிவான்","படர்ந்ததன் யோகினை நிறுவிப்பணிந்தவரட் காசிடை மொழிந்தா","னிடம்பக மகளிவள் பெரிது மிராசபுத் திரியல ளெனவே."],"p":"","r":"The sage, who knows the scriptural way that cuts the many deeds bound up in the soul's union with and difference from the body, steadies his yoga and says that this woman is a deceiver and no king's daughter at all.","n":"ஒற்றுமை வேற்றுமை, the body and soul as one and as other, may be the Jain view that they are one in one respect and distinct in another; that is my reading. Line 3, where he speaks (பணிந்தவரட் காசிடை), is obscure, so it is unclear whether he says it to her, to himself or aside.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vinai","uyir","yaakkai","meyyunarvu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 64","lb":"NK64","ev":"The sage, fixed in yoga, perceives that the woman is a deceiver and not the princess.","cl":[["the way of the scripture cuts the many deeds that cling to the soul through its entanglement with the body","jain","asserted",2,"narrator"]],"ag":["முனிச்சந்திரன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிச்சந்திரன்",""],"ch":"nk:4"},"nk:65":{"book":"neelakesi","num":65,"t":["என்னைஈண் டைக்கு வரவென் றருந்தவன் வினவலு மெழிலார்","பொன்னனாள் புடைபெயர்த் திட்ட பொலங்கல மனங்கலக் குவபோன்","மின்னொளி யோடுற மிழற்றமிழற்றுவ கிளியென மொழிந்தாள்","முன்னநான் பரவிய வரங்கண்முடிகுறை கொடுப்பதற் கெனவே."],"p":"","r":"When the sage asks why she has come, the golden lady, her ornaments clinking like a parrot's chatter, says she has come to pay what is due for boons she once prayed for.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","vanjam","sol"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 65","lb":"NK65","ev":"Asked why she has come, Neelakesi says she has come to fulfil vows for boons granted.","cl":[],"ag":["முனிச்சந்திரன்","நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","முனிச்சந்திரன்"],"ch":"nk:4"},"nk:66":{"book":"neelakesi","num":66,"t":["யாதுநீ கொண்ட வரமென் றருந்தவ னியல்பினின் வினவ","வேதினாட் டிறையெங்க ளிறைமே லியல்பின்றி யெழலொழி கெனவே","போதுசாந் தவியொடு புகையும் பொருந்திய பொருந்தெய்வக் கெனலு","மோதிஞா னியிது வாயி னுரையழ கீதென மொழிந்தான்."],"p":"","r":"Asked what boon she sought, she says it was that the king of a hostile land not rise unlawfully against her father, with flowers, sandal, oblation and incense for the god, and the knowing sage answers that if so, her words are fair.","n":"The sage's reply (உரை அழகு ஈது) can be plain assent or a dry remark on a well-made story; he already knows she is not the princess.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","arasu","vanjam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 66","lb":"NK66","ev":"Neelakesi names a vow for her father's safety from a hostile king, and the sage comments on her answer.","cl":[],"ag":["முனிச்சந்திரன்","நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","முனிச்சந்திரன்"],"ch":"nk:4"},"nk:67":{"book":"neelakesi","num":67,"t":["தோடுகொண் டொருசெவி விளங்கத் துளங்குவ மகரமொன் றாடப்","பாடுவண் டோடுசுரும் பரற்றப் பல்கலம் வயிரவில் வீச","வாடுகொம் பனையவ ருரைக்கு மச்சமோ பெரிதுடைத் தடிகள்","காடுகண் டாற்பிறர்க் கறியேன் கவற்றுவ தொக்குமீ தெனக்கே."],"p":"","r":"With a jewel on one ear, a makara earring swinging on the other, bees humming and gems flashing, she says the fear such tender women speak of is great indeed, and that whatever others feel, this forest troubles her.","n":"The demoness of the burning ground claims to be afraid of her own haunt.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["acham","azhagu","vanjam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 67","lb":"NK67","ev":"Neelakesi, in her finery, tells the sage that the forest frightens her.","cl":[],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","முனிச்சந்திரன்"],"ch":"nk:4"},"nk:68":{"book":"neelakesi","num":68,"t":["மணிநகு நெடுமுடி மறவேன் மன்னவன் மகளெனின் மடவாய்","அணிநகை யாயமோ டாடி அரும்பெறற் சுற்றமோ டிருப்பாய்","பிணிமிகு பேய்வன மிதனுட் போதுற லொருதலை பிறவோ","துணிவொடு துறந்தவர்க் கல்லாற் றுன்னதற் கரிதிது பிறர்க்கே."],"p":"","r":"The sage answers that if she were the daughter of the king of the jewelled crown and fierce spear she would be playing with her companions and sitting among her kin, and that this disease-ridden demon forest is hard for any to enter except those who have renounced with resolve.","n":"He tests her story against what a princess's life would be, without accusing her outright.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thuravu","therithal","vanjam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 68","lb":"NK68","ev":"The sage questions why a king's daughter would come into a demon forest that only renouncers approach.","cl":[],"ag":["முனிச்சந்திரன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிச்சந்திரன்","நீலகேசி"],"ch":"nk:4"},"nk:69":{"book":"neelakesi","num":69,"t":["வேணுவோ டினையன பிறவும் வியப்புறு பெருவனம் வினவிற்","பேணுதற் கரிதிது பெரிதும் பிணிதரு பேய்வன மெனவே","வாணுதன் மயிர்குளிர்த் துரைக்கும் மாதவத் தடிகளென் றானுங்","காணுதற் கரியன வுருவங் கண்டறி வனகளு முளவோ."],"p":"","r":"With bamboo and such things, she says, the great forest is a wonder, yet hard to bear, a demon forest full of afflictions; and with her hair on end she asks the great sage whether he has seen any of the forms that are hard to see.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["acham","anangu","vanjam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 69","lb":"NK69","ev":"Neelakesi, feigning fear, asks the sage whether he has seen the spirits of the forest.","cl":[],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","முனிச்சந்திரன்"],"ch":"nk:4"},"nk:70":{"book":"neelakesi","num":70,"t":["புக்கிருந் தொருமனை யுறைவார் போவதும் வருவதுங் கண்டான்","மக்களுந் தாயரந் தம்முள் மருள்வதும் வெருள்வது முளதோ","மிக்கபல் கதிகளு முயிரின் மெய்ம்மையு முணர்ந்தவர்க் கரிதே","ஒக்குமற் றவையுள வேனு முரைப்பது பொருத்தமின் றெமக்கே."],"p":"","r":"The sage answers that those who live together in one house see each other come and go, and mother and children do not take fright at one another; for those who know the many states of birth and the truth of souls such fear is rare, and even if these beings exist it is not fitting for him to speak of them.","n":"The beings he declines to speak of stand before him; his refusal can be read as tact toward one he has already seen through. The housemates image makes all souls kin in one dwelling, across the states of birth.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kathi","uyir","acham","meyyunarvu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 70","lb":"NK70","ev":"","cl":[["one who knows the states of birth and the nature of souls does not fear other beings, who are like housemates and kin","jain","asserted",3,"munichandran"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிச்சந்திரன்","நீலகேசி"],"ch":"nk:4"},"nk:71":{"book":"neelakesi","num":71,"t":["சந்திர முனிவர னுரைப்பத்தளிரியல் சாவுகள் சாரா","மந்திர முளதெனி னடிகள்மனத்தொடு பணிமின மெனவே","யந்தரத்தவர்களும் வணங்குமருந்தவ னவையுனை யடையா","இந்திரன் வேண்டினும் பேய்களென்னமற் றிலங்கிழை மடவோள்;"],"p":"","r":"When the sage has spoken, the tender one asks him to teach her with his whole mind a mantra, if there is one, so that deaths cannot reach her; the ascetic whom even the sky-dwellers worship answers that they will not reach her though Indra himself wished it, so what are demons.","n":"It is unclear whether அவை (line 3) are the deaths she fears or the spirits; either way the assurance is ironic, given to the demoness herself.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["mandhiram","saakkaadu","anangu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 71","lb":"NK71","ev":"Neelakesi asks the sage for a mantra against death, and he tells her no such harm will reach her.","cl":[],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிச்சந்திரன்","நீலகேசி"],"ch":"nk:4"},"nk:72":{"book":"neelakesi","num":72,"t":["துப்படு துவரிதழ் துடிக்குந்துகிலிடை யகலல்கு றுளக்குஞ்","செப்படு வனமுலை செறிக்குஞ்சிதரரி மழைக்கணுஞ் சிறைக்கு","மொப்படு துடியிடை யொசிக்குமுவ்வுறு மதிமுக முழற்று","மிப்படி யவளிவை செயலுமிவையெனை யெமக்கென வுரைத்தான்."],"p":"","r":"She makes her coral lip tremble, loosens the garment at her hips, draws in her breasts, flashes her rain-dark eyes and sways her slender waist, and as she does all this he asks what any of it is to him.","n":"The seduction ends in one line of indifference: he does not argue against desire, he finds nothing in it addressed to him.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","nirai","adakkam","thavam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 72","lb":"NK72","ev":"Neelakesi plays every wile of seduction, and the sage asks what these are to him.","cl":[],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிச்சந்திரன்","நீலகேசி"],"ch":"nk:4"},"nk:73":{"book":"neelakesi","num":73,"t":["காதின கனகப் பைந்தோடுங்கைவெள் வளைகளுங் கழலத்","தாதின வினமலர் பலவுந்தலையன நிலமிசை யுதிரப்","போதன புணரரி நெடுங்கண்புனல்வரப் பூந்துகிற் புடையா","வேதனை பெரிதுடைத் தடிகள்விளிகவிப் பிறப்பென வுரைத்தாள்."],"p":"","r":"Her gold earrings and white bangles slipping, the flowers falling from her head to the ground and tears streaming from her long eyes, she says her anguish is great and cries out that this birth may perish.","n":"The text does not say whether the grief is one more ruse or the first real turn; the sage answers it as real, with a sermon on birth.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["piravi","idumbai","vinai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 73","lb":"NK73","ev":"Neelakesi breaks down weeping and wishes her present birth would end.","cl":[],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","முனிச்சந்திரன்"],"ch":"nk:4"},"nk:74":{"book":"neelakesi","num":74,"t":["பிறவியும் பிறவியுட் பிறக்கும் பிணியுமப் பிணியினைத் துணிக்கு","மறவியின் மருந்துமம் மருந்தின்மாட்சியுங் கேட்குறின் மடவா","யறவிய மனத்தினை யாகி அலங்கழித் தொழிலொழிந் தடங்கி","உறவினை யோம்பினை யிருவென்றுயர்தவ னுரைத்தலு மிருந்தாள்."],"p":"","r":"The high ascetic tells her that if she wishes to hear of birth, of the diseases born within birth, of the medicine of dharma that cuts them and of that medicine's excellence, she should set her mind on dharma, cease her wavering and her actions, grow still and sit, and she sits.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["piravi","noy","marundhu","aram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 74","lb":"NK74","ev":"The sage offers to teach birth and its cure, and Neelakesi sits down to listen.","cl":[["birth is the ground of disease and dharma is the medicine that cuts it","jain","asserted",2,"munichandran"]],"ag":["முனிச்சந்திரன்","நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிச்சந்திரன்","நீலகேசி"],"ch":"nk:4"},"nk:75":{"book":"neelakesi","num":75,"t":["நாற்கதி யுள்ள நரகரை நாஞ்சொல்லின் மூன்றுவகைக்","காற்று வலையங்க ளேந்து நிரையக் கதிநிலந்தா","மேற்ற நிகோதத்தி னிம்ப ரிருளி னிரளிருண்மே","லாற்றப் புகையள றார்மணற் கூர்ம்பர லாய்மணியே."],"p":"","r":"Of the four states of birth he begins with the hell-dwellers: the hell lands, held up by three rings of wind, lie below the nigoda, and are named darkness upon darkness, darkness, smoke, mire, sand, sharp gravel and gems.","n":"The seven names, read upward from the lowest, match the Jain list of seven hell-earths (deepest darkness, darkness, smoke, mud, sand, gravel, gem). How line 3 should be divided (நிகோதத்தின் இம்பர் இருளின் இருள் இருள்) is my reading.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kathi","naragam","piravi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 75","lb":"NK75","ev":"","cl":[["living beings are born into four states of birth, of which hell is one","jain","asserted",1,"munichandran"],["the hell-earths lie below, borne up by three rings of wind","jain","asserted",2,"munichandran"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிச்சந்திரன்","நீலகேசி"],"ch":"nk:4"},"nk:76":{"book":"neelakesi","num":76,"t":["ஏழா யவைவிரிந் தெண்பத்து நான்குநூ றாயிரமாம்","போழா மவற்றப் புரையின் விகற்பமும் பொற்றொடியாய்","கீழா ரலிகண் முழுச்செவி கிண்ணர்க ளெண்ணிகந்த","வூழாம் பிறப்புமுவ் வாதமல் லாருரு வொப்பினரே."],"p":"","r":"These seven spread into eighty-four hundred thousand, with the varieties of their pits; the beings below are sexless and of shapes beyond counting, born there as their lot.","n":"Lines 3 and 4 (முழுச்செவி கிண்ணர்கள், முவ்வாதம் அல்லார்) are hard to construe; I read அலிகள் as the hell-dwellers being neither male nor female, which Jain teaching holds, but the line may list other kinds of being.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["naragam","piravi","oozh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 76","lb":"NK76","ev":"","cl":[["the seven hells open into eighty-four hundred thousand dwellings","jain","asserted",1,"munichandran"],["the beings of hell are neither male nor female","jain","asserted",3,"munichandran"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிச்சந்திரன்","நீலகேசி"],"ch":"nk:4"},"nk:77":{"book":"neelakesi","num":77,"t":["விலங்கின் வகையும் விரிவன யான்சொல்ல வேண்டுதியே","லலங்கலம் பூணா யிருவகை யாமவை யென்கொலென்னின்","நிலங்களி னிற்பவுஞ் செல்பவு மாமென நிற்பனதா","மிலம்பட லின்றியிவ் வையகத் தைந்தா யியன்றனவே."],"p":"","r":"If she wishes him to tell the kinds of animal life too, he says, they are of two sorts, those that stand fixed in the earth and those that move, and the fixed ones are of five kinds throughout this world.","n":"Here 'animal' (விலங்கு) covers the whole state of non-human, non-divine, non-hell life, down to the fixed beings.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kathi","uyir"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 77","lb":"NK77","ev":"","cl":[["beings of the animal state are either fixed or moving, and the fixed are of five kinds","jain","asserted",3,"munichandran"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிச்சந்திரன்","நீலகேசி"],"ch":"nk:4"},"nk:78":{"book":"neelakesi","num":78,"t":["இயங்கு வனவு மிருபொறி யையறி வெல்லையவாய்","மயங்கியிம் மத்திம நல்லுல கத்தின மற்றிவற்று","ணயம்படு நாவின் மூக்கில நந்து முரண்முதலா","வயங்கியங் கோடிய வாயிரண்டாய அறிவினவே."],"p":"","r":"The moving ones, found in this middle world, have from two to five senses; among them the snail and the like, which lack a nose and have only taste beyond touch, are two-sensed.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pori","uyir","kathi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 78","lb":"NK78","ev":"","cl":[["moving beings have from two to five senses, and creatures like the snail have only two","jain","asserted",1,"munichandran"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிச்சந்திரன்","நீலகேசி"],"ch":"nk:4"},"nk:79":{"book":"neelakesi","num":79,"t":["உண்ணி முகுட்டை எறும்பெறி தேண்முத லாவுடைய","வெண்ணில் பல்கோடிய வாயவ் விரண்டொடு மூக்குடைய","கண்ணிய மூவறி வாமவை பெற்றாற் கருணமிலா","நண்ணிய வண்டொடு தேனீ யனையவு நாலறிவே."],"p":"","r":"Lice, mites, ants and the like, countless millions, have those two senses and smell, three in all; those lacking hearing, like the wasp and the honeybee, have four.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pori","uyir"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 79","lb":"NK79","ev":"","cl":[["insects like lice and ants have three senses, and bees and wasps, which lack hearing, have four","jain","asserted",3,"munichandran"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிச்சந்திரன்","நீலகேசி"],"ch":"nk:4"},"nk:80":{"book":"neelakesi","num":80,"t":["இறப்பப்பல் காலின வெட்டி னிரண்டிரண் டேயிழிந்த","பறப்ப நடப்ப தவழ்வன வூர்வன பற்பலவாச்","சிறப்புடை யிந்திய மைந்தென வந்த செவியுடைய","மறப்பில் கடலொடு தீவினு மல்கிய பல்விலங்கே."],"p":"","r":"The sage lists the five-sensed animals, those with many legs, eight, and fewer pair by pair, the flyers, walkers, crawlers and creepers, which have hearing as the fifth sense and fill the sea and the islands.","n":"The verse sorts animals as a Jain classification does, by the number of senses (இந்தியம் ஐந்து) and by legs and gait, not by use to man; the count of legs falls 'two by two' (இரண்டிரண்டே இழிந்த) from eight.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["piravi","uyir"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 80","lb":"NK80","ev":"","cl":[["animals with all five senses, hearing the last, are many in kind by legs and gait and fill sea and islands","jain","asserted",3,"munichandran"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிச்சந்திரன்","நீலகேசி"],"ch":"nk:5"},"nk:81":{"book":"neelakesi","num":81,"t":["வெப்பமுந் தட்பமு மிக்கு விரவிய யோனியவாய்ச்","செப்புவ செப்பில் செய்கைக ளாற்றம செய்வினையைத்","துப்பன போர்த்தும் பொடித்தும் பொரித்து முன்றோன்றுவன","வொப்பவு மொப்பிலுடம்புடம் பேகொண் டுழல்வனவும்."],"p":"","r":"Animals are born from wombs of mixed heat and cold, from membranes, eggs and sprouting, cannot act on what they are told, consume the fruit of the deeds they did, and wander taking one body after another, like and unlike.","n":"Line 2 (செப்புவ செப்பில் செய்கைகள் ஆற்றம்) is hard to divide: it may say the animals cannot do what they are told, or that they cannot do the deeds that would free them; the reading is not fixed. போர்த்தும், பொடித்தும், பொரித்தும் seem to name the modes of birth (in a membrane, by sprouting, by hatching).","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["piravi","vinai","yaakkai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 81","lb":"NK81","ev":"","cl":[["animals arise by several modes of birth and wander from body to body consuming the fruit of their own deeds","jain","asserted",3,"munichandran"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிச்சந்திரன்","நீலகேசி"],"ch":"nk:5"},"nk:82":{"book":"neelakesi","num":82,"t":["நல்லவர் தீயவர் திப்பிய ரொப்பில் குமானுயரோ","டல்லவ ருள்ளுறுத் தாடவ ரைவரு ளாதியினார்","சொல்லுக தன்மையென் பாயெனிற்சொல்லுவன் பல்வகையாற்","புல்லிய போகப் பெருநிலந் தன்னைப் பொருந்தினரே."],"p":"","r":"Asked to tell the kinds of humans, the sage names five, the good, the bad, the divine, the base humans and the rest, and says the first of them dwell in the great land of enjoyment.","n":"சொல்லுக தன்மை என்பாய் எனில் (if you say, tell their nature) shows the sermon as answers to the listener's questions, though she has asked nothing aloud in these verses. The land of enjoyment (போகப் பெருநிலம்) is where pleasure comes without labour; the sage does not praise it, and 86 will place release in the land of deeds instead.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["piravi","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 82","lb":"NK82","ev":"","cl":[["humans are of five kinds, and the first kind live in a land of enjoyment","jain","asserted",4,"munichandran"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிச்சந்திரன்","நீலகேசி"],"ch":"nk:5"},"nk:83":{"book":"neelakesi","num":83,"t":["தீமா னுயர்திறந் தேற்றிடிற் றீவின் சிறுநிலத்தார்","கோமான் முதலார் குணங்களிற் குன்றிய குற்றத்தராய்த்","தாமாம் பெரிய தவந்தலை நிற்பினுந் தன்மைபெறா","ராமான் மடப்பிணை யன்னமென் னோக்கி யவரதிறமே."],"p":"","r":"The bad humans live in the small lands of the islands, faulty and lacking in qualities from their kings down, and even if they hold to great penance they do not reach the higher state; so the sage tells the doe-eyed woman.","n":"The claim limits penance by birth: the same tapas that frees in 86 bears no such fruit here. தன்மை பெறார் may mean they do not gain the state penance gives, or do not gain good nature at all; the first fits the contrast with 86.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thavam","piravi","kutram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 83","lb":"NK83","ev":"","cl":[["humans of the small island lands, wanting in qualities, do not attain the higher state even by great penance","jain","asserted",3,"munichandran"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிச்சந்திரன்","நீலகேசி"],"ch":"nk:5"},"nk:84":{"book":"neelakesi","num":84,"t":["திப்பிய ரென்னப் படுபவர் தீர்த்தந் திறப்பவரு","மப்பிய புண்ணியத் தாழிய ராழிய ரையவரும்","வெப்பிய வான் செலவ் விஞ்சையரெஞ்சலில் வெள்ளியரும்","பப்பிய ரேயவர் பான்மை வினவினும் பைந்தொடியே."],"p":"","r":"The divine humans are those who open the ford, those of heaped-up merit who bear the wheel and their companions, the sky-going Vidyadharas and the unfailing white ones, the sage tells the woman with fresh bangles.","n":"தீர்த்தம் திறப்பவர் (those who open the ford) is the Tamil form of the Tirthankara title; ஆழியர் (wheel-bearers) are world-ruling kings. Who the ஐயவர் and வெள்ளியர் are, and what பப்பியர் in line 4 means, the verse alone does not settle.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["piravi","iraivan","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 84","lb":"NK84","ev":"","cl":[["the highest humans are the founders of the ford, the wheel-bearing kings and the sky-going Vidyadharas","jain","asserted",1,"munichandran"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிச்சந்திரன்","நீலகேசி"],"ch":"nk:5"},"nk:85":{"book":"neelakesi","num":85,"t":["கோலமி னோன்றற் குமானுயர் தம்மையுங் கூறுவன்கேள்","வாலமுங் கோடும் வளைபல்லும் பெற்ற வடிவினராய்ச்","சீலமுங் காட்சியுந் தீண்டலு ரந்தரத் தீவிலுள்ளார்","நீலமும் வேலுங் கயலு நிகர்த்த நெடுங்கண்ணினாய்."],"p":"","r":"The base humans, ugly of form with tails, horns and curved teeth, live in the islands between and do not touch right conduct or right vision.","n":"சீலமும் காட்சியும் (conduct and vision) are two of the Jain three jewels; their absence, not the beastlike form, is what makes these men low. The edition prints தீண்டலு ரந்தரத் where the sense needs தீண்டலர் அந்தரத்.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["piravi","ozhukkam","meyyunarvu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 85","lb":"NK85","ev":"","cl":[["the base humans of the intermediate islands have beastlike forms and are untouched by right conduct and right vision","jain","asserted",3,"munichandran"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிச்சந்திரன்","நீலகேசி"],"ch":"nk:5"},"nk:86":{"book":"neelakesi","num":86,"t":["மானுய ரென்னப் படுபவர் தாமா விதையமென்னுங்","கானுயர் சோலைக் கரும நிலத்தார் கருவினை போய்த்","தானுய ரின்பந் தவத்தாற் றலைப்படுந் தன்மையினார்","வானுயர் தோன்றல் வளர்பிறை யேசிய வாணுதலாய்."],"p":"","r":"Those called humans in the full sense are of the land of deeds, wooded and green, whose nature is that by penance their dark karma departs and they reach the highest bliss.","n":"கரும நிலம் (land of deeds) is set against the land of enjoyment of 82: release is open only where people must act and can do penance. The name of the land in line 1 (ஆவிதையம்) is not clear and may be corrupt.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thavam","veedu","vinai","piravi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 86","lb":"NK86","ev":"","cl":[["only humans of the land of deeds can shed their karma by penance and reach the highest bliss","jain","asserted",3,"munichandran"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிச்சந்திரன்","நீலகேசி"],"ch":"nk:5"},"nk:87":{"book":"neelakesi","num":87,"t":["தூமாண் பவணர் வியந்தரர் சோதிடர் கற்பருப்பால்","வேமானியரென வைவரித் தேவர் விரித்துரைப்பிற்","றீமாண்குமரரோ டீரைவர் முன்னவ ரன்னவர்பின்","பூமாண் புனைகுழ லாய்க்கினிச்சொல்லற் பொல்லா துகொல்லாம்."],"p":"","r":"The gods are of five kinds, Bhavanas, Vyantaras, Jyotishkas, Kalpas and beyond them Vaimanikas, and in detail the first are ten with the fire-princes; the sage wonders whether telling more to the woman with flowered hair is unfitting.","n":"The gods appear as one more class of births in the list that began with hell-beings and animals, ranked and numbered, not as objects of worship. The closing doubt (சொல்லல் பொல்லாது கொல்) prepares the turn in 88.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thevar","piravi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 87","lb":"NK87","ev":"","cl":[["the gods are five classes of embodied beings, the Bhavana gods being ten in kind","jain","asserted",2,"munichandran"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிச்சந்திரன்","நீலகேசி"],"ch":"nk:5"},"nk:88":{"book":"neelakesi","num":88,"t":["இன்குர லார்முத லாநும ரீறா விவருமெண்மர்","பொன்பிதிர்ந் தன்ன பொறிசுணங் காகத்துப் பூண்முலையா","யென்றலு மீரிழு தாரழ லுற்றாங் கினைபவளை","நின்றிறம் பின்னறி வாமறங் கேளென நேர்ந்தனளே."],"p":"","r":"The sage names eight kinds of Vyantara gods, from the sweet-voiced ones to 'your own kin'; at that the woman suffers like wet ghee near fire, and when he says that her nature will be known later and she should hear dharma, she consents.","n":"நுமர் ஈறா (ending with your kin) is the sage letting her know that he sees what she is: the last of the eight Vyantara classes are the demons, and she is a demoness in a princess's form. Lines 1 and 2 are his words; the narrator speaks from என்றலும். He defers the matter (நின் திறம் பின் அறிவாம்) and keeps to the sermon.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thevar","aram","kelvi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 88","lb":"NK88","ev":"The sage hints that her kin are among the demon gods; Neelakesi is stricken, he puts the matter off and bids her hear dharma, and she agrees.","cl":[["the Vyantara gods are of eight kinds","jain","asserted",1,"munichandran"]],"ag":["முனிச்சந்திரன்","நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"ch":"nk:5"},"nk:89":{"book":"neelakesi","num":89,"t":["அந்தர வாழ்க்கையர் சோதிடர் தாங்களு மைவகையர்","சந்திர சூரியர் கோளவர் நாளவ ரல்லவராய்","மந்தர மாமலை தன்னை வலமுறை சூழ்பவருஞ்","சிந்துபு நின்றுசெல் லாதே விளங்குந் திறலவரும்."],"p":"","r":"The Jyotishka gods are also five, moon, sun, planets, constellations and stars; some circle Mount Mandara to the right, and others stand shining without moving.","n":"The sun and moon are here gods of one class among many, ranked below the heaven-dwellers of 90, not supreme lights.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thevar","aadhiththan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 89","lb":"NK89","ev":"","cl":[["the luminary gods are five, some circling the world mountain and some fixed","jain","asserted",3,"munichandran"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிச்சந்திரன்","நீலகேசி"],"ch":"nk:5"},"nk:90":{"book":"neelakesi","num":90,"t":["ஆரண னச்சுதன் சோதம னந்தமு மாதியுமாய்ப்","பாரணை நல்ல பதினறு கற்பத் தவரவர்மே (வோர்","லோரிண ராயமும் மூன்றொன்ப தைந்துக ளுள்ளுறை","வீரியர் வைமா னிகரெனக் கொண்ணீ விளங்கிழையாய்."],"p":"","r":"The sixteen Kalpa heavens run from Saudharma to Arana and Acyuta, and above them, take it, dwell the Vaimanikas in groups of three threes, nine and five.","n":"The verse gives the count of the heavens as plain fact to a listener who is told to take it (கொள் நீ); line 2 of the edition carries a stray bracketed syllable.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thevar","piravi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 90","lb":"NK90","ev":"","cl":[["there are sixteen Kalpa heavens and above them further heavens in groups of nine, nine and five","jain","asserted",2,"munichandran"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிச்சந்திரன்","நீலகேசி"],"ch":"nk:5"},"nk:91":{"book":"neelakesi","num":91,"t":["இப்ப டிப்பி றவியு","ளொப்பி றீய நாரகர்","துப்ப ரிய மாதுயர்","செப்பு வாஞ் சிறிதினி."],"p":"","r":"Now the sage will tell a little of the great, hard-to-bear suffering of the hell-beings, who are the worst in this round of births.","n":"The metre changes here to short lines for the account of hell, which runs to 101. The sage promises only 'a little' (சிறிது).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naragam","idumbai","piravi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 91","lb":"NK91","ev":"","cl":[["the hell-beings are the worst of all births and their suffering is hard to bear","jain","asserted",2,"munichandran"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிச்சந்திரன்","நீலகேசி"],"ch":"nk:5"},"nk:92":{"book":"neelakesi","num":92,"t":["ஈரி ருள்ளி னார்கடம்","பேர ளவ்வைஞ் ஞூறுவி","லோரு மோச னையவை","யூரும் வேத னையரே."],"p":"","r":"The hell-beings of the dark hells are five hundred bows in stature and are beings of pain that spreads over yojanas.","n":"ஈரிருள் and the third line are hard to construe; the size of five hundred bows seems to be that of the beings of the lowest hell.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["naragam","idumbai","yaakkai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 92","lb":"NK92","ev":"","cl":[["hell-beings have bodies of vast measure, five hundred bows tall","jain","asserted",2,"munichandran"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிச்சந்திரன்","நீலகேசி"],"ch":"nk:5"},"nk:93":{"book":"neelakesi","num":93,"t":["காள மான மெய்கடாம்","வாள வாய்க ளாற்பல","கீள வாப வாயினும்","மீளு மேனி நீரினே."],"p":"","r":"Their black bodies, though cut into many pieces by sword-edges, join again like water.","n":"The body that rejoins like water is what makes the pain without end: in hell even dismemberment is not an exit.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naragam","yaakkai","idumbai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 93","lb":"NK93","ev":"","cl":[["bodies in hell cut by blades rejoin like water","jain","asserted",4,"munichandran"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிச்சந்திரன்","நீலகேசி"],"ch":"nk:5"},"nk:94":{"book":"neelakesi","num":94,"t":["மல்ல வர்ம றஞ்செய்துங்","கொல்ல வாவ வல்லமெய்","பல்ல வாவு முள்ளன","சொல்ல வாவ வல்லவே."],"p":"","r":"Even when strong ones do violence to them, their bodies cannot be killed, and there are many such torments that cannot be told.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naragam","yaakkai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 94","lb":"NK94","ev":"","cl":[["the bodies of hell-beings cannot be killed before their time","jain","asserted",2,"munichandran"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிச்சந்திரன்","நீலகேசி"],"ch":"nk:5"},"nk:95":{"book":"neelakesi","num":95,"t":["பண்டை வேரி யர்கடாங்","கண்டு கண்க னல்களாய்","மண்டி மாம றஞ்செய்ப","வெண்ட வப்ப லவுமே."],"p":"","r":"Seeing their enemies of old, their eyes turn to embers, and they rush on and do great violence beyond counting.","n":"The torment here comes from the hell-beings themselves, carrying their old enmity into hell, not from a judge. The first word was repaired from an old encoding; வேரியர் is read as enemies (வைரியர்).","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["naragam","pagai","idumbai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 95","lb":"NK95","ev":"","cl":[["old enemies meet in hell and wound each other without end","jain","asserted",1,"munichandran"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிச்சந்திரன்","நீலகேசி"],"ch":"nk:5"},"nk:96":{"book":"neelakesi","num":96,"t":["பேடி வேத னைபெரி","தோடி யூறு மாதலாற்","சேடி யாடு வன்மையிற்","கூடி யாவ தில்லையே."],"p":"","r":"They are neuter, and since pain runs through them greatly, no pleasure of union comes to them.","n":"பேடி (neuter) fits the Jain teaching that hell-beings have no sex; lines 3 and 4 are obscure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["naragam","idumbai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 96","lb":"NK96","ev":"","cl":[["hell-beings are neuter and know no pleasure of union","jain","asserted",1,"munichandran"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிச்சந்திரன்","நீலகேசி"],"ch":"nk:5"},"nk:97":{"book":"neelakesi","num":97,"t":["கொன்ற பாவ மென்றுமூன்","றின்ற பாவ மென்றுதீச்","சென்று வேவ வாயினு","ணின்று கூவ வாக்குவார்."],"p":"","r":"Naming 'the sin of killing' and 'the sin of eating flesh', the tormentors make the hell-beings go into fire and burn, and make them stand inside its mouth and cry out.","n":"The verse names eating flesh as a sin separate from killing, so one who eats meat he did not kill is not cleared; this is the charge Neelakesi will open the Kundalakesi debate with (163). The tormentors (ஆக்குவார்) are not named, and the verse names no judge. வாயினுள் may be the mouth of the fire or of the hell.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naragam","pulaal","kolai","vinai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 97","lb":"NK97","ev":"","cl":[["killing is a sin punished by burning in hell","jain","asserted",1,"munichandran"],["eating flesh is a sin of its own, punished in hell like killing","jain","asserted",2,"munichandran"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிச்சந்திரன்","நீலகேசி"],"ch":"nk:5"},"nk:98":{"book":"neelakesi","num":98,"t":["உண்ண வாவ நீரெனக்","கண்ண வாவ வாபவர்","நண்ண லாவ வல்லதே","ரெண்ண லாவ தில்லையே."],"p":"","r":"When they long for water to drink, what comes to their eyes and what they can reach is nothing that can be thought pleasant.","n":"","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["naragam","pasi","idumbai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 98","lb":"NK98","ev":"","cl":[["hell-beings who thirst find nothing to relieve them","jain","asserted",1,"munichandran"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிச்சந்திரன்","நீலகேசி"],"ch":"nk:5"},"nk:99":{"book":"neelakesi","num":99,"t":["கரிவ கன்றி நின்றகம்","பொரிவ பொங்கி வீழ்ந்தழைத்","தெரிவ வின்ன மாதுயர்","பரிப வரு மில்லையே."],"p":"","r":"Charred, bruised, burnt inside, boiling up, falling, crying, burning: for such great suffering there is no one who relieves them.","n":"பரிபவரும் இல்லை (there is no one who takes pity or bears it for them) shuts out any rescuer: no god, kin or guru enters this hell.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naragam","idumbai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 99","lb":"NK99","ev":"","cl":[["no one relieves the suffering of the beings in hell","jain","asserted",4,"munichandran"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிச்சந்திரன்","நீலகேசி"],"ch":"nk:5"},"nk:100":{"book":"neelakesi","num":100,"t":["அங்கு வெங்க னலினுட்","டங்கி யுந்த லைத்தலை","பொங்கி யும்பு கைபுக","நுங்கி யுந்நு கர்வவே."],"p":"","r":"There, in the hot fire, sinking, rising head over head, with smoke entering, they are gulped and swallowed and so suffer.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naragam","idumbai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 100","lb":"NK100","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிச்சந்திரன்","நீலகேசி"],"ch":"nk:5"},"nk:101":{"book":"neelakesi","num":101,"t":["ஓச னைய பல்லுயிர்","வீவி னையு றவருங்","காய்சி னக்க டுவிடம்","பேசி னார்க்க ருளுணா."],"p":"","r":"Many beings a yojana in size, hard to bring to death, and burning fierce poison: no grace is food for those spoken of, the verse seems to say of hell's last torments.","n":"The four short lines do not yield a clear sentence; அருளுணா may mean 'grace is not their food' or name a food of hell. The reading is a guess.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["naragam","idumbai","arul"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 101","lb":"NK101","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிச்சந்திரன்","நீலகேசி"],"ch":"nk:5"},"nk:103":{"book":"neelakesi","num":102,"t":["நலங்களில் பிறவியு ணஞ்சுணா ரகர்கள்பின்","விலங்கின்வே தனைகளும் விரிக்கல்வேண் டும்மெனிற்","கலங்கியொன் றொன்றினைக் கண்டுகாற் றென்னப்போ","மலங்கிநின் றும்மனம் மன்னுமஞ் சுங்களே."],"p":"","r":"After the poison-eating hell-beings in their birth without good, the sage turns to the pains of animals: frightened, each flees from another like the wind, and even standing still in confusion their minds are always afraid.","n":"The metre changes again. The animal's pain is fear itself, which never leaves the mind, not torment by others.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["piravi","acham","idumbai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 103","lb":"NK103","ev":"","cl":[["animals live in constant fear of one another","jain","asserted",4,"munichandran"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிச்சந்திரன்","நீலகேசி"],"ch":"nk:5"},"nk:104":{"book":"neelakesi","num":103,"t":["தண்ணென்மா மழையினாற் றாமழிந் துழல்பவும்","புண்ணினா லழியமெய்ப் போரிடைப் புகுத்தவு","முண்ணல்கா ரணத்தினா லோட்டியிட் டொறுக்கவு","மெண்ணில்பல் வலையினு மிழக்குமவ் வுயிர்களே"],"p":"","r":"The teacher goes on with the suffering of beasts: they wander ruined by cold heavy rain, are driven into battle to die of wounds, are chased and tormented for the sake of food, and lose their lives in countless nets.","n":"Most of the harms listed are done by humans (war, the hunt, the net), so the account of the beast's state of birth is also a charge against those who use and kill animals; it leads straight into the next verse's list of killings.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["idumbai","piravi","kollaamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 104","lb":"NK104","ev":"","cl":[["birth as a beast is full of suffering from weather, war, hunting and the snare","jain","asserted",4,"munichandran"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிச்சந்திரன்","நீலகேசி"],"ch":"nk:6"},"nk:105":{"book":"neelakesi","num":104,"t":["வேதவா தியர்கடம் வேள்விவாய விட்டவும்","பூததே வர்கட்கெனாப் புல்லியோர்கள் கொல்லவு","மோதுநோய் மருந்தெனவ் வூட்டுதற் குரைப்பவும்","சாதலால் வரும்மிடர் தாமெனைப் பலவுமே."],"p":"","r":"He adds the deaths beasts suffer when Vedic ritualists offer them in sacrifice, when base men kill them for spirits and gods, and when they are prescribed to be fed as medicine for named diseases: the sufferings that come by death are many.","n":"The verse names the Vedic ritualists (வேதவாதியர்) and their sacrifice outright and counts it from the victim's side, as one more way a beast dies in pain. The Vedic answer is not given here; the charge is argued out with the Vedic disputant in the Veda debate (836-839), where the killing for gods and ancestors is said to bind the killers.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kollaamai","velvi","pulaal","idumbai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 105","lb":"NK105","ev":"","cl":[["killing animals in Vedic sacrifice, for spirits and gods, or as medicine inflicts the suffering of death on them","jain","asserted",1,"munichandran"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிச்சந்திரன்","நீலகேசி"],"ch":"nk:6"},"nk:106":{"book":"neelakesi","num":105,"t":["நடுக்கமுறு நாற்கதியு ணரர்கள்படுந் துன்ப","மெடுக்கிலவை தாமிரண்டு பாகினவு மாகு","மடக்கமிலர்க் காவனவு மன்றிப்பொது வென்றும்","வடுப்பிளவு வாட்பகழி வாட்டியவொண் கண்ணாய்."],"p":"","r":"Addressing her, the teacher says that the suffering humans undergo among the four trembling states of birth is of two parts: what falls to those who lack self-restraint, and what is common to all.","n":"நாற்கதி, the four states of birth (hell, beast, human, god), is the frame of the whole sermon; this verse moves from the beasts of 104-105 to humans and introduces the division that 107-115 work out.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["idumbai","adakkam","piravi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 106","lb":"NK106","ev":"","cl":[["of the four states of birth, the human's suffering is partly the fruit of lacking restraint and partly common to all","jain","asserted",1,"munichandran"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிச்சந்திரன்","நீலகேசி"],"ch":"nk:6"},"nk:107":{"book":"neelakesi","num":106,"t":["தீவினைசெய் வாயிலொடு செற்றமனக் குற்ற","மாவினையி னாம்வெகுளி மானமொடு மாய","மோவினையிற் பற்றவல மச்சமொடு மற்று","மாவனவெ லாமடக்க மில்லவர்த நோவே."],"p":"","r":"The pains of the unrestrained are the gateway of evil deeds, the mind's fault of hatred, anger with pride and deceit, attachment, grief and fear: all of these are the sufferings of those who lack restraint.","n":"Anger (வெகுளி), pride (மானம்), deceit (மாயம்) and attachment (பற்று) together match the four passions that Jain teaching names as the binders of karma; the verse treats them not only as causes of later pain but as present pain. The reading of line 3's பற்று as greed or attachment is mine.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["adakkam","vekuli","pattru","acham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 107","lb":"NK107","ev":"","cl":[["anger, pride, deceit and attachment are themselves the suffering of the unrestrained, as well as the door to evil deeds","jain","asserted",4,"munichandran"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிச்சந்திரன்","நீலகேசி"],"ch":"nk:6"},"nk:108":{"book":"neelakesi","num":107,"t":["இழுக்கலுறு தீக்கதியி லுய்க்குமென வெண்ணார்","விழுக்குலங்கள் மாசுபடு மென்பதனை வேண்டார்","புழுக்குலங்க ளானிறைத்த போர்வையென வோரா","ரழுக்குடம்பிற் கேகெடுவ ராடவர்க ளந்தோ."],"p":"","r":"Men do not reckon that it will drive them into an evil state of birth, do not care that their noble lineage will be stained, do not see that it is a cover filled with swarms of worms, and so perish for the sake of a filthy body, alas.","n":"Desire is not named; the object is only அழுக்குடம்பு, the filthy body, and the verse is in the list of the sufferings of the unrestrained, so lust is the likely sense. It sets three deterrents side by side: the next birth, the family's name, and disgust at the body.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","yaakkai","kudi","piravi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 108","lb":"NK108","ev":"","cl":[["ruin for the sake of the impure body leads to an evil rebirth and stains one's lineage","jain","asserted",1,"munichandran"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிச்சந்திரன்","நீலகேசி"],"ch":"nk:6"},"nk:109":{"book":"neelakesi","num":108,"t":["மதுவொன்றுங் கோதை மலரன்ன கண்ணாய்","பொதுவென்ற நோயும் புணர்ந்திரண்டு பாகா","மிதுவொன் றிடையூ றிரண்டாய் விரியு","மதுவன்றி மெய்ப்பிணியு மூன்றா யலரும்."],"p":"","r":"Then the common affliction: it divides into two, harm from outside (இடையூறு), which itself branches into two, and bodily disease, which opens into three.","n":"The sermon now classifies suffering as a physician classifies diseases; the scheme of 109-113 is what the remedy in 116 answers.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["noy","idumbai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 109","lb":"NK109","ev":"","cl":[["suffering common to all is of two kinds, harm from outside and disease of the body","general","asserted",2,"munichandran"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிச்சந்திரன்","நீலகேசி"],"ch":"nk:6"},"nk:110":{"book":"neelakesi","num":109,"t":["பெடையூடு சாயற் பிணையன்ன நோக்கி","யிடையூ றிரண்டு மினியாவை யென்னி","னடையா வுயிரதுவு மல்லதுவு மன்ன","நடையாய் முதலதுவு நாற்பூத மாமே."],"p":"","r":"He names the two outside harms as those that come from what has life and from what lacks it, and says the first of them, the lifeless, is the four elements.","n":"Line 3 (அடையா உயிரதுவும் அல்லதுவும்) is compressed; that the 'first' is the lifeless kind rests on 111, which lists the elemental harms and ends உயிரல்ல ஊறே.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["idumbai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 110","lb":"NK110","ev":"","cl":[["harm from outside comes either from living beings or from lifeless things, the four elements","general","asserted",4,"munichandran"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிச்சந்திரன்","நீலகேசி"],"ch":"nk:6"},"nk:111":{"book":"neelakesi","num":110,"t":["பெருமழையு நீரும் பெரிதெறியுங் காற்றுங்","கருமலையுங் கல்லுங் கடுநவையு நஞ்சுஞ்","செருமலையும் பல்படையுஞ் செந்தீயும் வந்திங்","குருமிடியு மெல்லா முயிரல்ல வூறே."],"p":"","r":"Heavy rain and flood, the hurling wind, black rockfall and stone, deadly poison, the many weapons of battle, red fire and thunder striking here: all these are harms that come from no living being.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["idumbai","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 111","lb":"NK111","ev":"","cl":[["flood, wind, rockfall, poison, weapons, fire and thunder harm without any living agent","general","asserted",4,"munichandran"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிச்சந்திரன்","நீலகேசி"],"ch":"nk:6"},"nk:112":{"book":"neelakesi","num":111,"t":["செத்துவங்க டாக்கிச் செயிரி னணங்குதலும்","மக்கள் பலவகையின் மன்னு மலைகொலையுங்","துக்கஞ்செய் பல்விலங்கிற் றோன்று மிடையூறு","மொக்கவிவை மூன்று முயிருடைய வூறே."],"p":"","r":"Spirits that strike and afflict in their anger, men's lasting killing in many ways, and the harm that comes from cruel beasts: these three alike are harms that come from living beings.","n":"செத்துவங்கள் in line 1 I take as beings or spirits (from sattva) that afflict; the word is rare and the sense is not certain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["idumbai","anangu","kolai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 112","lb":"NK112","ev":"","cl":[["spirits, men and beasts are the three living sources of outside harm","general","asserted",4,"munichandran"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிச்சந்திரன்","நீலகேசி"],"ch":"nk:6"},"nk:113":{"book":"neelakesi","num":112,"t":["தீர்வனவுந் தீராத் திறத்தனவுஞ் செய்ம்மருந்தி","னூர்வனவும் போலா துவசமத்தி னுய்ப்பனவும்","யார்வினவுங் காலு மவைமூன்று கூற்றவா","நேர்வனவே யாகு நிழறிகழும் பூணாய்."],"p":"","r":"The bodily diseases are three: those that can be cured, those that by their nature cannot, and those that, unlike the ones that yield to applied medicine, pass away by subsiding.","n":"உவசமம் (line 2) looks like upaśama, quieting; the third class may be diseases that settle of themselves, but the contrast with செய்ம்மருந்தின் ஊர்வனவும் is not clear to me.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["noy","marundhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 113","lb":"NK113","ev":"","cl":[["bodily diseases are curable, incurable, or such as pass by subsiding rather than by medicine","general","asserted",3,"munichandran"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிச்சந்திரன்","நீலகேசி"],"ch":"nk:6"},"nk:114":{"book":"neelakesi","num":113,"t":["நல்லாய்நா ரின்றியே நாமுன் விரிசெய்த","வெல்லா விமையோர்க்குமென்று மிறுதி சார்ந்த","தல்லா லகன்றுன்ப மாகா நுமரன்றிப்","பொல்லா தவர்களுறு மல்லைப் புகலுறுங்கால்."],"p":"","r":"He tells her that even all the gods he described before come at last to an end, and that apart from this they have no great suffering, and turns to the darkness that the wicked, other than her own kind, enter.","n":"The sense of lines 3-4 (நுமர் அன்றிப் பொல்லாதவர்கள் உறும் அல்லை) is unsure: நுமர், 'your people', may be the demons of 115, set apart from the other wicked. The gods' end as their only suffering is the clearest point.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","idumbai","naragam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 114","lb":"NK114","ev":"","cl":[["even the gods' heavenly life comes to an end","jain","asserted",2,"munichandran"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிச்சந்திரன்","நீலகேசி"],"ch":"nk:6"},"nk:115":{"book":"neelakesi","num":114,"t":["தீயே யெனவெவர்க்குஞ் செல்லல் பலவாக்கி","வேயே புரைதோளாய் மிக்க விடமெங்கும்","பேயே யெனப்பட்டுப் பேணா தனசெய்வர்","நீயே யெனினல்லை நின்போல்வ ரன்றோ."],"p":"","r":"Those like her, he says, make pain like fire for all, are called demons everywhere and do what no one would cherish; if it is she herself, she is not such now, but those like her are.","n":"The teacher turns the account of suffering onto his listener: she was a பேய் who did harm, and the verse marks her change by exempting her from her own kind. The reading of line 4 (நீயே எனின் அல்லை) as 'not you, now' is mine.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["anangu","innaa"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 115","lb":"NK115","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிச்சந்திரன்","நீலகேசி"],"ch":"nk:6"},"nk:116":{"book":"neelakesi","num":115,"t":["பேர்தற் கரும்பிணி தாமிவை யப்பிணி","தீர்தற் குரியதிரி யோக மருந்திவை","யோர்தற் றெளிவோ டொழுக்க மிவையுண்டார்","பேர்த்த பிணியுட்பிற வார்பெரி தின்பமுற்றே."],"p":"","r":"These are diseases hard to shake off; the medicine fit to cure them is the triple discipline of understanding, clear faith and conduct, and those who take it are not born again into the disease and come to great joy.","n":"The three are the Jain three jewels (right faith, knowledge, conduct), here named திரியோகம் and given as medicine after the classification of disease in 109-113; no god administers them, the soul takes them itself (உண்டார், line 3).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["marundhu","piravi","thelivu","ozhukkam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 116","lb":"NK116","ev":"","cl":[["the threefold remedy of knowledge, clear faith and conduct ends the disease of birth and brings great joy","jain","asserted",4,"munichandran"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிச்சந்திரன்","நீலகேசி"],"ch":"nk:6"},"nk:117":{"book":"neelakesi","num":116,"t":["மானொத்த நோக்கிமருந் தென்றவைமூன் றினுள்ளும்","ஞானத்தி னன்மைநனி கேட்குவை யாயினக்கா","லூனத்தை யின்றியுயி ராதிய வுள்பொருள்க","டானற் குணர்தலிது வாமதன் றத்துவம்மே."],"p":"","r":"Of the three medicines, if she would hear the goodness of knowledge: it is to know well, without defect, the existing realities beginning with the soul; that is its essence.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["gnaanam","meyyunarvu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 117","lb":"NK117","ev":"","cl":[["right knowledge is faultless knowing of the real categories beginning with the soul","jain","asserted",4,"munichandran"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிச்சந்திரன்","நீலகேசி"],"ch":"nk:6"},"nk:118":{"book":"neelakesi","num":117,"t":["காண்டலு மல்லதே யளவை காண்டன்முன்","பூண்டவைம் பொறிமன மவதி புண்ணிய","மாண்டகு மனப்பரி யாயங் கேவல","நாண்டகு மரிவைய ருருவ நண்ணினாய்."],"p":"","r":"Means of valid knowledge are perception and what is other than perception; perception comprises knowledge through the five senses and mind, avadhi, the pure manahparyaya and kevala.","n":"Ranking sense-and-mind knowledge under perception together with the three supersensory kinds (clairvoyance, knowing others' minds, omniscience) is how later Jain logicians arranged the means of knowledge; the older Jain scheme put sense knowledge among the indirect kinds. The verse follows the former. Its perception reaches beyond the senses, which rival schools deny.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["alavai","arivu","gnaanam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 118","lb":"NK118","ev":"","cl":[["valid knowledge is perceptual or non-perceptual, and perception includes, beside sense-and-mind knowledge, the supersensory avadhi, manahparyaya and kevala","jain","asserted",1,"munichandran"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிச்சந்திரன்","நீலகேசி"],"ch":"nk:6"},"nk:119":{"book":"neelakesi","num":118,"t":["நினைவு மீட்டுணர் வூக நேர்தரு","புனைவுசே ரணுமைபொய் யின்மை மெய்யுரை","முனைவர்தம் மாகம மொழியு மாகுமென்","றனையன காட்சியி லளவை யைந்துமே."],"p":"","r":"Memory, recognition, reasoning, inference and the scripture spoken by the truthful sages: these five, he says, are the means of knowledge other than perception.","n":"Memory and recognition count here as valid knowledge; schools that treat memory as no means of knowledge would reject that. அணுமை in line 2 is read as inference (அனுமை).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["alavai","arivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 119","lb":"NK119","ev":"","cl":[["the non-perceptual means of knowledge are five: memory, recognition, reasoning, inference and the word of the sages' scripture","jain","asserted",4,"munichandran"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிச்சந்திரன்","நீலகேசி"],"ch":"nk:6"},"nk:120":{"book":"neelakesi","num":119,"t":["வைப்பு நயனள வைபுகு வாயிலென்றும்","பொய்ப்பி லுயிரே பொருவில்குண மார்க்கணை","செப்பி னிவற்றிற் றிரியாதுள் புகுபவாயி","னொப்பில் பெருமை யுணர்விற்குயர் மாட்சியாமே."],"p":"","r":"Placing, standpoints and the means of knowledge are the doors of entry, and the soul with its qualities is examined through the categories of search; if one enters through these without straying, knowledge rises to incomparable eminence.","n":"வைப்பு, நயன், அளவை and மார்க்கணை look like the Jain technical terms nikshepa, naya, pramana and margana; the line division and syntax of lines 1-3 are hard and the reading is tentative.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["alavai","gnaanam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 120","lb":"NK120","ev":"","cl":[["the soul and its qualities are to be known through placing, standpoints, means of knowledge and the categories of search","jain","asserted",2,"munichandran"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிச்சந்திரன்","நீலகேசி"],"ch":"nk:6"},"nk:121":{"book":"neelakesi","num":120,"t":["காட்சி வகைதான் கடவுண் முதலாய","மாட்சி யமைந்தபொரு ளெட்டு மனத்துவைத்து","மீட்சியில தாய்விரிந் துந்திய வின்பவெள்ள","வேட்கையது வாந்தெளி வென்றனர் வென்றவரே."],"p":"","r":"The kind of right faith, say the victors, is to hold in mind the eight excellent things beginning with God, an unturning longing for the flood of bliss that spreads and surges: that is clear faith.","n":"The verse cites the victors (வென்றவர், the Jinas) as its authority. Which eight things are meant is not spelled out here.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thelivu","iraivan","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 121","lb":"NK121","ev":"","cl":[["right faith is unturning trust in the eight things beginning with God, joined to longing for bliss","jain","asserted",4,"munichandran"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிச்சந்திரன்","நீலகேசி"],"ch":"nk:6"},"nk:122":{"book":"neelakesi","num":121,"t":["முந்துற்ற மூடப் புலிமூன்றும் பிழைத்த பின்னை","யன்பச்ச மாசை யுலகோடிலிங் காத்த ரொப்பு","மென்பெற்று மேத்த லிலராயெண் மயத்து நீங்க","லின்புற்ற காட்சி யுடையார்க்கியல் பாகு மன்றே."],"p":"","r":"Having escaped the three follies that come first, not praising others for love, fear, desire, the world or the outward sign, and standing free of the eight prides: this is the nature of those who have joyful right faith.","n":"The three follies (மூடம்) and eight prides (மயம்) are standard Jain lists of what spoils faith; line 2's division (உலகோடிலிங் காத்த ரொப்பு) is hard and my reading of it is tentative.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thelivu","agandhai","pedhaimai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 122","lb":"NK122","ev":"","cl":[["one of right faith is free of the three follies and the eight prides and praises no false object out of love, fear or desire","jain","asserted",3,"munichandran"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிச்சந்திரன்","நீலகேசி"],"ch":"nk:6"},"nk:123":{"book":"neelakesi","num":122,"t":["ஐயுற்றல் வேட்கை யுவர்ப்பேமயக் கியாது மின்மை","செய்குற்ற நீக்க றிரிந்தாரை நிறுத்த லின்றிப்","பொய்யற்ற காதற் பொருவில்லறங் காட்ட லெட்டுங்","கையுற்ற வாயில் லதுகாட்சியின் மாட்சி யாமே."],"p":"","r":"Freedom from doubt, desire, disgust and delusion, removing faults, steadying those who have strayed, love without falsehood and showing the peerless dharma: these eight are the limbs of right faith in hand, and its excellence.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thelivu","anbu","aram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 123","lb":"NK123","ev":"","cl":[["right faith has eight limbs, from freedom from doubt to steadying the wavering, love of fellow believers and spreading the dharma","jain","asserted",4,"munichandran"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிச்சந்திரன்","நீலகேசி"],"ch":"nk:6"},"nk:124":{"book":"neelakesi","num":123,"t":["நன்றாய காட்சி யுடனாகிய ஞானந் தன்னோ","டொன்றாகி யுள்ளத் தொழியாமை யொழுக்க மென்ப","குன்றாத வொன்றுங் குறைபாட்டதுங் கூறு பவ்வே","வென்றார்த நூலின் விதிமெய்ம்மை யுணர்ந்த வரே."],"p":"","r":"Conduct, say those who know the rule of the victors' books, is that which, one with good faith and knowledge, does not leave the heart; and it is of two kinds, the undiminished and the partial.","n":"The complete and the partial (குன்றாத ஒன்றும் குறைபாட்டதும்) are most likely the full vows of the renouncer and the lesser vows of the householder; the verse itself names only the two grades. Conduct here grows out of faith and knowledge and is not separate from them.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ozhukkam","thelivu","gnaanam","thuravu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 124","lb":"NK124","ev":"","cl":[["conduct joined to faith and knowledge is either complete or partial","jain","asserted",3,"munichandran"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிச்சந்திரன்","நீலகேசி"],"ch":"nk:6"},"nk:125":{"book":"neelakesi","num":124,"t":["போற்றல் செறிவே பொறையாதிய நல்ல றமு","மேற்ற நினைப்போ டிருசார்வி ழுத்த வமு","மாற்றல் பரிசை முதலாகிய வன்ன வெல்லாம்","மாற்ற மறுக்கு மொழுக்கத்தின் மாட்சி யாமே."],"p":"","r":"Guarding, restraint, forbearance and the other good virtues, the twofold penance taken up with resolve, enduring hardships and all such things: these are the excellence of the conduct that refuses change.","n":"இருசார்வு (line 2) I take as penance of two kinds, outer and inner, and பரிசை (line 3) as the hardships to be borne; மாற்றம் மறுக்கும் (line 4) is unclear, perhaps conduct that admits no turning back.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["ozhukkam","porai","thavam","adakkam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 125","lb":"NK125","ev":"","cl":[["conduct is shown in restraint, forbearance and the other virtues, twofold penance and the endurance of hardship","jain","asserted",4,"munichandran"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிச்சந்திரன்","நீலகேசி"],"ch":"nk:6"},"nk:126":{"book":"neelakesi","num":125,"t":["யோக மிவற்றை யுடனுண்ட வுயிர்க ளெல்லாம்","மாக விசும்பி னவர்தம்மொடு மன்ன ரும்மாய்ப்","போக நுகர்ந்து பொருந்தாவினை புல்ல லின்றி","யேகநல் லின்ப மியைந்தாலிழ வில்லை நல்லாய்."],"p":"","r":"All souls that take this discipline together, good one, live with the dwellers of the high sky as their kings and enjoy their pleasures, and then, with no unfitting deed clinging to them, join the one good bliss, where there is no loss.","n":"Heaven is a stage, not the goal: the verse puts the gods' pleasures before release and makes release the end of karma's clinging (வினை புல்லல் இன்றி), which 114's word on the gods' end prepares.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["veedu","vinai","inbam","marumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 126","lb":"NK126","ev":"","cl":[["those who take faith, knowledge and conduct together pass through heavenly enjoyment and, free of clinging karma, reach the single bliss of release","jain","asserted",4,"munichandran"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிச்சந்திரன்","நீலகேசி"],"ch":"nk:6"},"nk:127":{"book":"neelakesi","num":126,"t":["பிறவியா மாறும் பிணியாந் திறமும்","மறவிதா னில்லா மருந்தாம் வகையும்","திறவியாள் கேட்டுத் தெரிந்துள்ளங் கொள்ள","வறவியான் றானு மறவமிர்த மீந்தான்."],"p":"","r":"The sage gives Neelakesi the nectar of dharma, teaching how birth comes about, how it is a disease, and how dharma is the medicine that does not fail, so that she may hear, understand and hold it in her heart.","n":"The verse frames the whole sermon that went before as medicine: birth is the illness (பிணி), dharma the remedy. மறவி தான் இல்லா (line 2) is best taken as 'free of forgetting', a remedy whose effect does not lapse; the phrase is compressed.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aram","piravi","marundhu","amudham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 127","lb":"NK127","ev":"The sage gives Neelakesi his teaching on birth as a disease and dharma as its cure.","cl":[["birth is a disease and dharma is its unfailing medicine","jain","asserted",2,"narrator"]],"ag":["முனிச்சந்திரன்","நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"ch":"nk:7"},"nk:128":{"book":"neelakesi","num":127,"t":["வண்டவாம் வார்குழலும் வாளெயிறும் பூண்முலையும்","தொண்டைவாய் நன்னலமுந் தோளுந் துடியிடையும்","கண்டவாங் காமுகரும் யாமுங் கணநரியும்","விண்டவாக் கொண்டுணரின் வேறுவே றாமன்றோ."],"p":"","r":"The sage says that hair, teeth, breasts, red lips, shoulders and waist, which the lustful see as beauty, are seen otherwise by ascetics like himself and otherwise again by the pack of jackals, so that, rightly understood, the one body is three different things.","n":"The argument is about perception, not anatomy: the body has no single nature that lust reads correctly. The jackals, for whom the same limbs are food, are the third witness, and யாம் (we) places the ascetic between the lover and the scavenger.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","kaamam","meyyunarvu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 128","lb":"NK128","ev":"","cl":[["a woman's beauty is not in the body itself, since lovers, ascetics and jackals see the same body as different things","general","asserted",4,"munichandran"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிச்சந்திரன்","நீலகேசி"],"ch":"nk:7"},"nk:129":{"book":"neelakesi","num":128,"t":["கரையவா வாங்குங் கயமகன் கைத்தூண்டி","லிரையவாப் பன்மீ னிடருறுவ தேபோல்","நுரையவா நுண்டுகிலு மேகலையுஞ் சூழ்ந்த","வரையவாய்ப் பட்டார்க்கு மாழ்துயரே கண்டீர்."],"p":"","r":"Like fish that crave the bait on the hook of a low fisherman on the bank and come to harm, those caught by the one wrapped in foam-fine cloth and a girdle meet only deep sorrow.","n":"The fisherman is கயமகன், a base man, so the bait has a malicious hand behind it; the verse does not say whether that hand is the woman's or desire's own. வரை (line 4) is read as the bounded body girt with cloth and girdle; it could also mean the woman as a 'boundary' or trap.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","idumbai","yaakkai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 129","lb":"NK129","ev":"","cl":[["those caught by desire for a woman's adorned body meet deep sorrow, as fish meet the hook","general","asserted",4,"munichandran"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிச்சந்திரன்","நீலகேசி"],"ch":"nk:7"},"nk:130":{"book":"neelakesi","num":129,"t":["மட்டார் மலர்புனைவும் வாணெடுங்கண் மையணிவும்","பட்டார் கலையுடையும் பல்வளையும் பைந்தோடு","நட்டாரை யெல்லா நரகுக்கே யுய்க்கு நாய்க்","கொட்டார்த்தார் செய்யும் கோலங்கள் வண்ணம்."],"p":"","r":"Flowers in the hair, kohl on the long eyes, silk dress, many bangles and fresh ear-rolls: these adornments lead all who cling to them into hell, the sage says, and the last line likens them to a show put on for a dog.","n":"Line 4 (நாய்க் கொட்டார்த்தார் செய்யும் கோலங்கள் வண்ணம்) is obscure as divided in the text; the comparison to something done for a dog is a guess at it, and the claim rests on line 3 alone.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["naragam","kaamam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 130","lb":"NK130","ev":"","cl":[["a woman's adornments lead those who attach themselves to them into hell","general","asserted",3,"munichandran"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிச்சந்திரன்","நீலகேசி"],"ch":"nk:7"},"nk:131":{"book":"neelakesi","num":130,"t":["ஆடினாய் நான மணிந்தாய் கலன்மாலை","சூடினா யேனுஞ் சுணங்கார் வனமுலையா","யூடினா யாக வொழுக் கூற்றைப் பல்பண்டம்","மூடினாய் தோலின் முகமனுரை யேனே."],"p":"","r":"You have bathed, put on scent, worn jewels and garlands, sulked as lovers sulk, the sage tells the woman before him, but you have only covered with skin a body full of many things that leak filth, and I will not speak flattering words to it.","n":"The sage answers her play (ஊடினாய், the lover's sulk) with a refusal of முகமன், courtly flattery: he declines the lover's role as much as the body. The woman addressed is Neelakesi in the borrowed form of a princess, so the body he strips of its cover is not her own.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","azhagu","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 131","lb":"NK131","ev":"The sage refuses to flatter the adorned woman who has come to tempt him.","cl":[["the adorned body is skin covering a vessel of filth","general","asserted",3,"munichandran"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிச்சந்திரன்","நீலகேசி"],"ch":"nk:7"},"nk:132":{"book":"neelakesi","num":131,"t":["மின்போ னுடங்கிடையும் வேயேய் திரடோளு","மென்றே யிவை மகிழ்ந்தீங் கென்முன்னே வந்தாயாற்","புன்றோலும் பல்லென்பும் போர்த்த புறங்காட்டு","ளன்றே யுறைவ னவற்றான் மருள்வேனோ."],"p":"","r":"You came before me thinking I would delight in a waist like lightning and shoulders like bamboo, the sage says, but I live in the burning ground clothed with poor skin and many bones; would I be deluded by these?","n":"The sage's defence is his dwelling, not a doctrine: one who lives among skin and bones in the புறங்காடு already sees what her body will become. The burning ground is also Neelakesi's own haunt (the demoness of its temple), so he turns her ground against her.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","kaamam","thavam","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 132","lb":"NK132","ev":"The sage tells the tempting woman that, living in the burning ground among bones, he cannot be moved by her beauty.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிச்சந்திரன்","நீலகேசி"],"ch":"nk:7"},"nk:133":{"book":"neelakesi","num":132,"t":["மெழுகுருகு மண்பாவை மேதையான் காய்த்தி","யொழுகுருகு செம்பொன்னா லுண்ணிறைந்த தேபோல்","புழுகுருகு மெய்காட்டிப் பொல்லாத போக்கி","யழகுருவு கொண்டா ளறவமிர்த முண்டாள்."],"p":"","r":"As molten gold fills a clay mould once the wax figure inside has been heated out, she put away her evil, having been shown the worm-ridden truth of the body, took on a form of beauty and drank the nectar of dharma.","n":"The simile is lost-wax casting: the wax (her old nature) melts away and gold takes the same shape, so the change is inward and complete. Line 3 can be taken as 'shown the body as worms' (the sage's lesson) or as her casting off a false body; the reading of line 3 is not secure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["aram","amudham","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 133","lb":"NK133","ev":"Neelakesi casts off her evil and takes in the sage's dharma.","cl":[],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"ch":"nk:7"},"nk:134":{"book":"neelakesi","num":133,"t":["காய்வ செயினுங் குழவிக்கட்கவன்று கழிகண் ணோட்டத்தாற்","றாய்தன் முலையி லமுதூட்டுந்தகைய னறவோன் றானென்று","மாய வுருவ மாறித்தன்மற்றை யுருவ மேகொண்டு","பேயேன் செய்த பிழையெல்லாம்பெரும பொறுவென் றிறைஞ்சினான்."],"p":"","r":"Thinking that the virtuous sage is like a mother who, out of overflowing kindness, nurses her infant with her milk even when it hurts her, she dropped her illusory form, took her own other form, and bowed, saying, 'Lord, forgive all the wrongs I, a demon, have done.'","n":"கழிகண்ணோட்டம் names the Kural's virtue of kindly regard in excess; the mother suckling a biting child makes the sage's forbearance maternal rather than judicial. Her confession calls herself பேயேன், the first time she names her true nature to him.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kannottam","porai","panivu","arul"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 134","lb":"NK134","ev":"Neelakesi sheds the princess's form, takes her own, and begs the sage's forgiveness.","cl":[["the virtuous bear the wrongs of those who hurt them as a mother bears her nursing infant","general","asserted",2,"narrator"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"ch":"nk:7"},"nk:135":{"book":"neelakesi","num":134,"t":["முழங்கு முந்நீர் வையத்து முனிதக் கார்தம் முன்னின்று","வழங்க வாட்ட மொழிவர்நமன்னும் பொறாத வகையுண்டோ","வழுங்க லென்ற வறவோன்றனலர்கொள் பாதம் பெரிதேத்தித்","தொழுங்கை யாளக் குணக்குன்றைத்துதிப்ப னென்று தொடங்கினாள்."],"p":"","r":"The sage tells her not to grieve: in this world the great put away displeasure when those who deserve their anger stand before them and speak, and what is there that such as we cannot bear? She praises his flowering feet and, with joined hands, begins to hymn him as a mountain of virtues.","n":"Lines 1 and 2 are compressed; முனிதக்கார் is read as 'those who deserve anger', so that the great forgive the offender who comes before them. It might instead mean the sages themselves, which would leave the sense close but the grammar different.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["porai","arul","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 135","lb":"NK135","ev":"The sage forgives Neelakesi and she begins her hymn of praise.","cl":[["the great forgive those who wronged them when they come and confess","general","asserted",2,"munichandran"]],"ag":["முனிச்சந்திரன்","நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"ch":"nk:7"},"nk:136":{"book":"neelakesi","num":135,"t":["வெள்ள மாரி தரித்தோய்நீவினையின் வாயி லடைத்தோய்நீ","யுள்ள மாட்சி யுடையோய்நீயுயப்போம் வண்ண முரைத்தோய்நீ","நள்ளென் யாமத் தியான்செய்தநவைக ளெல்லா நனிகண்டு","மெள்ள லில்லாப் பெரியோய்நின்னிணையில் பாத மணைவல்யான்."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi praises the sage as the one who bore the flood rains, who closed the gate of karma, who has a glorious mind, who taught the way to be saved, and who, seeing every harm she did him in the dead of night, showed no contempt; she will come to his peerless feet.","n":"வினையின் வாயில் அடைத்தோய் (closing the doorway of karma) and, in 139, the doorway that burns karma correspond to the Jain pair of stopping karma's inflow and wearing it off; the hymn uses plain Tamil rather than technical names for them.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","thavam","porai","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 136","lb":"NK136","ev":"","cl":[["the ascetic can close the doorway by which karma enters","jain","asserted",1,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","முனிச்சந்திரன்"],"ch":"nk:7"},"nk:137":{"book":"neelakesi","num":136,"t":["மூட மூன்று முரைத்தோய் நீமுரண்செய் தோற்ற முனிந்தோய் நீ","வீடுங் கட்டும் விரித்தோய் நீவினையி னின்பம் வெறுத்தோய் நீ","காடு கிளர்ந்து காட்டியான் கலக்க வொன்றுங் கலங்காத","பாடற் கரிய பெரியோய்நின்பழிப்பில் பாதம் பணிவல்யான்."],"p":"","r":"She hails him as the one who taught the three delusions, scorned the hostile apparition, expounded bondage and release, loathed the pleasure that karma brings, and stayed wholly unshaken when she made the forest rise up to trouble him; she bows to his blameless feet.","n":"மூடம் மூன்று (the three delusions) is the Jain list that the sermon gave at verse 122. முரண்செய் தோற்றம் can be the hostile shapes she raised against him or 'birth' as the source of conflict; the third line makes the first likelier, since she names her own assault.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["veedu","vinai","thavam","meyyunarvu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 137","lb":"NK137","ev":"","cl":[["bondage and release are the two states of the soul that the teacher expounds","jain","asserted",2,"neelakesi"],["the three delusions are to be known and avoided","jain","asserted",1,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","முனிச்சந்திரன்"],"ch":"nk:7"},"nk:138":{"book":"neelakesi","num":137,"t":["அல்லற் பிறவி யகன்றோய்நீஆசை வெவ்வே ரறுத்தோய்நீ","வெல்லற் கரிய வனங்கனைமெய் வெண்ணீ றாக வெகுண்டோய்நீ","கொல்லக் கருதி வந்தேனைக்குணங்க ளாலே வணங்குவித்த","சொல்லற் கரிய பெரியோய் நின்றோமில் பாதந் தொழுவல்யாள்."],"p":"","r":"She hails him as the one who has left painful birth, cut the burning root of desire, angrily reduced the invincible Ananga to white ash, and by his virtues alone made her, who came to kill him, bow; she worships his feet, beyond words to praise.","n":"Burning the god of love to ash is the deed Saiva hymns give to Siva; the Jain hymn gives it to a human ascetic, and keeps even the anger (வெகுண்டோய்). Line 3 states the turn of the whole chapter: she was conquered not by force but by his qualities.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["piravi","avaa","kaamam","panivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 138","lb":"NK138","ev":"","cl":[["an enemy who came to kill is overcome by virtue, not by force","general","asserted",3,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","முனிச்சந்திரன்"],"ch":"nk:7"},"nk:139":{"book":"neelakesi","num":138,"t":["உடம்பின் மெய்ம்மை யுணர்ந்தோய்ந £யுறங்க லார்வ மறுத்தோய்நீ","யிடங்கொ ளின்னா வினையெல்லா மெரிக்கும் வாயில் விரித்தோய்நீ","யடங்க லில்லேற் கருளினாலறங்கூர் மாரி பொழிந்தோய்நின்","றடங்கொள் செந்தா மரையடியென்றலைய வேயென் றலையவே."],"p":"","r":"She hails him as the one who knew the truth of the body, cut away the longing for sleep, laid open the doorway that burns all painful karma, and in grace rained the rain of dharma on her, who had no restraint; may his broad red-lotus feet be on her head.","n":"The grace (அருள்) here is the teacher's compassion in giving the dharma, not a god's power that removes karma; the karma itself is burned by the doorway he showed.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","yaakkai","arul","meyyunarvu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 139","lb":"NK139","ev":"","cl":[["there is a way by which all painful karma is burned away","jain","asserted",2,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","முனிச்சந்திரன்"],"ch":"nk:7"},"nk:140":{"book":"neelakesi","num":139,"t":["தடம்படு மாரி தலைத்தலை நூற","விடம்படு பல்லுயிர் மெய்வழி யேற","வுடம்பொடு வேறெனு மோர்ப்பினை யாகி","யடங்கிய நின்னடி யஞ்சலி செய்வேன்."],"p":"","r":"As the heavy rain beat down again and again and many poisonous creatures crawled up his body, he stood restrained, knowing that he is other than the body; she will worship his feet with joined palms.","n":"உடம்பொடு வேறு எனும் ஓர்ப்பு (the realisation that one is other than the body) is the Jain separation of soul and body made a practice: the ascetic endures because he does not take the body for himself.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["uyir","yaakkai","thavam","porai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 140","lb":"NK140","ev":"","cl":[["the self is distinct from the body","jain","asserted",3,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","முனிச்சந்திரன்"],"ch":"nk:7"},"nk:141":{"book":"neelakesi","num":140,"t":["கல்லுரு கக்கடுங் காற்றெறி போதினி","னல்லிருள் கூர்சுடு காட்டிட மாகப்","பல்வினை யும்பறிப் போய்நின் பாதம்","நல்வினை யிற்றொழு வேனினி நாளும்."],"p":"","r":"In the burning ground of thick darkness, when the harsh wind blew enough to melt stone, he stayed and uprooted all his karmas; she will worship his feet every day as a good deed.","n":"She calls her worship நல்வினை, a good deed, so the hymn itself is placed within the economy of karma the sage is ending.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","thavam","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 141","lb":"NK141","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","முனிச்சந்திரன்"],"ch":"nk:7"},"nk:142":{"book":"neelakesi","num":141,"t":["மங்குன் மழைபொழி மாரிபெந் நாளிற்","கங்குலெண் ணில்லங் கவலைசெய் காட்டு","ளெங்கு மியங்கல னென்றிருந் தோய்நின்","பங்கயம் போல்வன பாதம் பணிவேன்."],"p":"","r":"In the rainy days when clouds poured, through countless nights in the forest that breeds fear, he sat resolved not to move anywhere; she will bow to his lotus-like feet.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thavam","thitpam","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 142","lb":"NK142","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","முனிச்சந்திரன்"],"ch":"nk:7"},"nk:143":{"book":"neelakesi","num":142,"t":["இற்றவர் தம்முட றின்றிட யாமம்","முற்ற நரிமுர லும்முது காட்டுட்","பற்றற வேநினை வோயிரு பாதம்","சுற்றுபு யான்விதி யிற்றொழு வேனே."],"p":"","r":"In the old burning ground where jackals howl all night as they eat the bodies of the dead, he meditates with all attachment cut; she will circle and worship his two feet according to rule.","n":"விதியின் (by rule) shows that the convert's first act is worship in prescribed form, the opposite of the blood offerings her own temple received.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pattru","thavam","nilaiyaamai","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 143","lb":"NK143","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","முனிச்சந்திரன்"],"ch":"nk:7"},"nk:144":{"book":"neelakesi","num":143,"t":["திண்டிறல் சேர்சிறு பேயறை கீறி","வெண்டலை யால்விளை யாடிய காட்டு","ளெண்டுக ளும்மெரிப் போய்நின பாதம்","வண்டறை பூவொடு வந்தனை செய்வேன்."],"p":"","r":"In the forest where fierce little demons tear and play with white skulls, he burns away the eight dusts; she will worship his feet with flowers humming with bees.","n":"எண் துகள் (the eight dusts) is read as the eight kinds of karma of Jain teaching, which cling to the soul like dust; the verse gives only the number and the image.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","thavam","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 144","lb":"NK144","ev":"","cl":[["karma is of eight kinds and is burned away by the ascetic","jain","asserted",3,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","முனிச்சந்திரன்"],"ch":"nk:7"},"nk:145":{"book":"neelakesi","num":144,"t":["பிணங்க ளிடையிடை போரழ லீமத்","தணங்கு துணங்கைசெய் தாடிய காட்டுட்","குணங்க ளுடையன குன்றுத லில்லாய்","வணங்குவ னின்னடி வைகலி னாளும்."],"p":"","r":"In the forest where, among the corpses, the pyre fire blazes and spirits dance the thunangai, he whose virtues never wane stays; she will bow at his feet every day.","n":"The five burning-ground verses (141 to 145) each set the sage in a scene of terror and then name one victory; the terrors are those of Neelakesi's own realm, so the hymn is also her renunciation of it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thavam","anangu","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 145","lb":"NK145","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","முனிச்சந்திரன்"],"ch":"nk:7"},"nk:146":{"book":"neelakesi","num":145,"t":["நுனித்தகு நன்னெறி நோக்கின ளாகி","முனிப்பிறை யோனடி மும்மையி னேத்திப்","பனிக்கட லன்னதொர் பாவமுஞ் செய்தே","னினிச்செய்வ தென்னுரை யாயெனக் கென்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"Looking now to the fine good path, she praised the sage's feet three times and said, 'I have done sin as great as the cold sea; tell me what I must do now.'","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","guru","panivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 146","lb":"NK146","ev":"Neelakesi praises the sage three times and asks him what she must do to answer for her sins.","cl":[],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"ch":"nk:7"},"nk:147":{"book":"neelakesi","num":146,"t":["விலங்கு வெந்நர காதிக டம்முள் விளிந்து தோன் றிவிழு நோயொடு முற்றுக்","கலங்கி யெங்குங் கண்ணில வாகிக் கவலைவெள் ளக்கட லிற்குளித் தாழு","நலங்களில் லாவுயிர் தங்களுக் கெல்லா நடுக்கநீக் கியுயர் நன்னிலை யீயுஞ்","சலங்களில் லாப்பெரி யோன்சரண் கொண்ணீ சனங்கட் கெல்லா மவன்சர ணென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"The sage answers: for all souls that die and are born again among beasts, in fierce hells and the rest, blinded, sunk in the sea of sorrow and without any good, the great one without guile removes their trembling and gives the high state; take refuge in him, for he is the refuge of all people.","n":"The refuge offered is the guileless great one, the Jina, not the sage himself; the sage turns her worship of him to the one he follows. The 'trembling' removed echoes the நாற்கதி (four states) of the earlier sermon, of which beasts and hell are named.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["adaikkalam","piravi","naragam","iraivan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 147","lb":"NK147","ev":"","cl":[["the guileless great one is the refuge of all beings, freeing souls from the births of beasts and hells and giving the high state","jain","asserted",4,"munichandran"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிச்சந்திரன்","நீலகேசி"],"ch":"nk:7"},"nk:148":{"book":"neelakesi","num":147,"t":["உய்தல் வாயுரைத் தாயதன் மேலு முயிருள்ளிட் டபல வுள்பொருள் சொன்னாய்","நைதலில் லாத்தெளி வோடுநன் ஞான நானுங் கொண் டேனுன் னற்குண மெல்லாம்","பெய்துதந் தாய் பிழைத் தேற்கினி தாவோர் பிராயச்சித் தம்பெரி யோயரு ளென்னச்","செய்த தீமை கெடக்கட னாட்டிற் சினவ ரன்னெறி யேதெருட் டென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"She says: you have told me the way to be saved and the realities beginning with the soul; I have taken clear good knowledge that does not wither, and you have filled me with all your good qualities; great one, give me, who erred, an atonement. He answers: to end the evil you did, make the Jina's path clear in the land.","n":"The expiation asked for (பிராயச்சித்தம்) is given not as a rite or a penance but as a mission: she is to teach the Jina's path. This sets up every debate that follows. கடனாட்டில் may be read as 'in the sea-girt land' or 'as a duty'; the command is the same either way.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["piraayachchiththam","gnaanam","aram","kutram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 148","lb":"NK148","ev":"Neelakesi asks the sage for an atonement, and he bids her spread the Jina's path.","cl":[["the evil one has done is atoned for by making the Jina's path clear to others","jain","asserted",4,"munichandran"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி","முனிச்சந்திரன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","முனிச்சந்திரன்"],"ch":"nk:7"},"nk:149":{"book":"neelakesi","num":148,"t":["யாஅ தடிக ளதருளா லருந்துய ரகல்வகை யதனான்","மாஅ துடைஅடி யிவைதா மறவலெ னெனவலங் கொண்டு","வேஎ தடவியன் மலைமேல் விரிகதிர் மணிவிளக் காதி","தீஇ தடுதலை விலங்குஞ் சினகர முவகையிற் சென்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"Saying, 'Lord, by your grace, by which hard suffering is removed, I will never forget these feet', she circled him and went joyfully to the Jina's temple on the mountain, bright with gem-lamps.","n":"The verse is written with lengthened syllables (யாஅ, மாஅ, வேஎ, தீஇ) at the head of each line, and line 3 and 4's description of the temple is only partly clear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","guru","aram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 149","lb":"NK149","ev":"Neelakesi takes leave of the sage and goes to the Jina temple on the mountain.","cl":[],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"ch":"nk:7"},"nk:150":{"book":"neelakesi","num":149,"t":["கொல்லை முல்லைபைங் கோங்குருந்தங் கோடறண் குரவ","நல்ல மல்லிகை நறவம்ஞாழல் தாழைபுன் னாகம்","பல்லி தழ்ப்பனிக் குவளை பானல் பாதிரி பிறவு","மெல்லை யின்மல ரேந்தி றைவன திடவகைக் கெழுந்தாள்."],"p":"","r":"Carrying flowers without limit, from the mullai, konku, kurundham, kanthal, kuravam and jasmine of the garden to the cool many-petalled kuvalai, paanal and paathiri, she rises to go to the Lord's place.","n":"The sarukkam opens with worship, not argument: the debater's first act is to bring flowers, and the list runs through flowers of forest, garden and water alike.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","iraivan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 150","lb":"NK150","ev":"Neelakesi gathers every kind of flower and sets out for the Lord's shrine.","cl":[],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"ch":"nk:8"},"nk:151":{"book":"neelakesi","num":150,"t":["நீட்சி யோக்கமோ டகலநினையநின் றெங்கணு நோக்கி","மாட்சி யால்வலங் கொண்டுமாதவத் திறைவனிற் பிழையாக்","காட்சி யேனெனி லெல்லாக்கதவமுந் திறக்கெனத் திறப்ப","வாட்சி மூவுல குடையவடிகட மடியிணை தொழுதாள்."],"p":"","r":"She takes in the shrine's height, length and breadth, circles it, and declares that if her vision does not stray from the lord of great penance every door shall open; they open, and she bows at the feet of the lord who rules the three worlds.","n":"காட்சி (line 3) is the word for right vision or faith, the first of the three jewels in Jain teaching; the doors open on an oath sworn by it, so the verse makes her right faith, not a priest or a key, the thing that admits her. The verse narrates the oath and its effect; it does not state the efficacy of right faith as a rule.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","meyyunarvu","iraivan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 151","lb":"NK151","ev":"Neelakesi swears by her unswerving faith in the Jina, the shrine's doors open of themselves, and she worships at his feet.","cl":[],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"ch":"nk:8"},"nk:152":{"book":"neelakesi","num":151,"t":["அத்தி யாளியோ டாமானட்ட மங்கல மரிய","பத்தி பாவைபல் பறவைபயில்கொடி திமிசொடு பிறவும்","வித்த கம்பெரி துடையவசித்திர வுருவநன் மலராற்","சித்த நன்னெறி பயந்தான்திருவடிக்கு அருச்சனை செய்தாள்."],"p":"","r":"With fine flowers worked into figures of rare skill, elephants, yalis, wild cattle, the eight auspicious signs, rows of images, birds, creepers and trees, she offers worship at the holy feet of the one who gave the good path of the perfected.","n":"The Jina is named by what he gave, சித்த நன்னெறி, the good path of the siddhas (the released), not by any act of creation or rescue.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 152","lb":"NK152","ev":"Neelakesi worships the Jina's feet with flowers arranged in pictures of animals, birds and the eight auspicious signs.","cl":[],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"ch":"nk:8"},"nk:153":{"book":"neelakesi","num":152,"t":["தூமஞ் சாந்தொடு சுண்ணந்துதியொடு பரவுபு தொழுதே","தாமந் தாழ்தர நாற்றித்தத்துவ தரிசிய துருவே","யாமென றையென வியந்தாங்கன்ன வாயிரத் தோரெண்","ணாம நல்லிசை தொடுத்துநாதகீ தங்களை நவிற்றும்."],"p":"","r":"With incense, sandal and scented powder she praises and bows, hangs garlands, and in wonder at the form of the seer of the tattvas strings good songs on the thousand and eight names and sings them to melody.","n":"ஆயிரத்தோர் எண் நாம (lines 3 and 4) is read as the thousand and eight names, a Jain litany of the Jina as other traditions have theirs; the eight hymns that follow (154 to 161) are presented as these songs. Line 3 (யாமென றையென வியந்து) is obscure in this text and its sense is not fixed.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","thirunaamam","pugazh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 153","lb":"NK153","ev":"Neelakesi offers incense and garlands and sings hymns on the Jina's thousand and eight names.","cl":[],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"ch":"nk:8"},"nk:154":{"book":"neelakesi","num":153,"t":["கன்று காலனைக் கடந்தாய்காதற் காமனைக் கடிந்தாய்","தொன்று மூத்தலைத் துறந்தாய்தோற்ற மாக்கட லிறந்தா","யொன்ற நோய்பகை யொருங்கே யுடைந்து வெங்களத் துதிர","வென்றி ருந்தனை நீயே வீரர்தம் வீரர்க்கும் வீரா."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi sings that the Jina passed beyond angry Death, drove off Kama of desire, renounced ancient aging and crossed the great sea of birth, and that disease and enmity broke together on the battlefield while he stood the victor, hero of heroes.","n":"The victory is told in a battle image (வெங்களத்து, வென்றிருந்தனை, வீரர்தம் வீரர்க்கும் வீரா), but every enemy named is inward or a condition of embodied life; the Jina wins by leaving them behind (கடந்தாய், துறந்தாய், இறந்தாய்), not by striking them.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kootru","kaamam","piravi","veedu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 154","lb":"NK154","ev":"","cl":[["the Jina has passed beyond death, desire, aging and birth","jain","asserted",1,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","அருகந்த பகவர்"],"ch":"nk:8"},"nk:155":{"book":"neelakesi","num":154,"t":["சாத னோய்சரை பிறவிதாஞ்செய் திவினைக் கடலுண்","மாது யருழந் துறுநோய்மறுகு மன்னுயிர்க் கெல்லாந்","தீதி னன்னெறி பயந்துதிரைசெய் நீள்கரை யொருவிப்","போத ரும்புணை படைத்தாய்புலவர்தம் புலவர்க்கும் புலவா."],"p":"","r":"She sings that for all the souls who suffer in the sea of evil deeds they themselves did, through death, disease, aging and birth, the Jina gave a good path free of harm and made the raft that is hard to find, poet of poets.","n":"The sea is தாம் செய் தீவினைக் கடல், the sea of the evil deeds the souls did themselves: no one else is blamed for their suffering, and the Jina's part is to make the raft (புணை படைத்தாய்), not to carry anyone. Line 3 (திரைசெய் நீள்கரை ஒருவி) is read as leaving the wave-beaten shore behind.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","piravi","idumbai","veedu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 155","lb":"NK155","ev":"","cl":[["souls suffer birth, aging, disease and death in a sea made of the evil deeds they themselves did","jain","asserted",1,"neelakesi"],["the Jina's teaching is the raft by which souls cross that sea","jain","asserted",4,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","அருகந்த பகவர்"],"ch":"nk:8"},"nk:156":{"book":"neelakesi","num":155,"t":["அரிய வாயின செய்திட்டமரர் துந்துபி யறைந்து","புரிய பூமழை பொழியப்பொன்னெயில மண்டிலம் புதைந்த","விரிகொ டண்டளிர்ப் பிண்டிமரநிழ லிருந்திரு வினையும்","பிரியும் பெற்றியை யுரைத்தாய்பெரியவர்ப் பெரியவர்ப் பெரியாய்."],"p":"","r":"She sings that after rare wonders, with the gods beating drums and raining flowers, he sat in the shade of the spreading asoka tree within the circle of golden walls and taught the way by which the two kinds of deed part from the soul.","n":"The setting is the Jain preaching assembly: the golden ring of walls (பொன் எயில் மண்டிலம்), the asoka tree (பிண்டி), the drums and the rain of flowers. What is taught is that both deeds (இருவினையும்) part, so good deeds too must go for release; the Jina states the way and the soul must walk it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","veedu","aram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 156","lb":"NK156","ev":"","cl":[["the Jina taught the way by which both good and evil deeds are separated from the soul","jain","asserted",4,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","அருகந்த பகவர்"],"ch":"nk:8"},"nk:157":{"book":"neelakesi","num":156,"t":["பொங்கு சாமரை யேந்திப் புடைபுடை யியக்கார்நின் றிரட்டச்","சிங்க வாசனத் திருந்துதெளிந்தொளி மண்டில நிழற்றத்","திங்கண் முக்குடை கவிப்பத்தேவர்தந் திருந்தவை தெருள","வங்க பூவம தறைந்தாயறிவர்தம் மறிவர்க்கு மறிவா."],"p":"","r":"She sings that with yakshas waving whisks on either side, seated on the lion throne under the halo and the three moon-white umbrellas, he proclaimed the Angas and the Purvas so that the gods' assembly understood, knower of knowers.","n":"அங்க பூவம் names the two classes of Jain canon, the Angas and the Purvas; the verse grounds the scripture's authority in the omniscient speaker, which is the ground the later sarukkams use against the Veda.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","gnaanam","arivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 157","lb":"NK157","ev":"","cl":[["the Anga and Purva scriptures were proclaimed by the omniscient Jina himself","jain","asserted",4,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","அருகந்த பகவர்"],"ch":"nk:8"},"nk:158":{"book":"neelakesi","num":157,"t":["ஊறி யாவது முணராயுறல்வகை யிதுவென வுரைத்தி","கூறு வேனெனக் கூறாய்குரன்முர சனையதோர் குணத்தை","செற லுள்ளமு மில்லையாய்த்திருமலர் மிசையடி யிடுதி","தேறு மாறென்னை நின்னைத்தேவர்தந் தேவர்க்குந் தேவா."],"p":"","r":"She sings a set of paradoxes: he perceives no touch yet tells the kinds of touch, never says 'I will speak' yet has a voice like a drum, has no wish to harm yet sets his feet on flowers; how is she to understand him, god of gods.","n":"Each line sets a lack beside a power: knowledge without sense-contact, speech without intention, walking on flowers without the will to hurt them. The third paradox concerns ahimsa: the Jina's steps fall on flowers, yet no harm is meant. Line 2 is elliptic, and the reading supplies 'yet speaks'.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","arivu","arul"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 158","lb":"NK158","ev":"","cl":[["the omniscient one knows the objects of the senses without perceiving them through the senses","jain","asserted",1,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","அருகந்த பகவர்"],"ch":"nk:8"},"nk:159":{"book":"neelakesi","num":158,"t":["கண்ணி னாலொன்றும் காணாய்காணவு முளபொரு ளொருங்கே","பெண்ணு மல்லவுஞ் சாராய்பிரிதலில் பேரின்ப முடையை","யுண்ணல் யாவது மிலையாயொளிதிக ழுருவமஃ துனதா","லெண்ணில் யார்நினை யுணர்வா ரிறைவர்தம் மிறைவர்க்கு மிறைவா."],"p":"","r":"She sings that he sees nothing with the eye yet sees all things at once, joins with no woman yet has undivided great bliss, eats nothing yet has a radiant form, so that no one who thinks can know him, lord of lords.","n":"Line 3 (உண்ணல் யாவதும் இலையாய்) says the omniscient one does not eat. Whether the omniscient being eats was disputed among Jains themselves, and this verse takes the side that denies it; the hymn states it as praise, not as an argued position. The bliss of line 2 is பிரிதலில் பேரின்பம், bliss that does not part, set against the joy of union with a woman.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","arivu","inbam","undi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 159","lb":"NK159","ev":"","cl":[["the omniscient one sees all existing things together without the eye","jain","asserted",1,"neelakesi"],["the omniscient one takes no food yet keeps a radiant body","jain","asserted",3,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","அருகந்த பகவர்"],"ch":"nk:8"},"nk:160":{"book":"neelakesi","num":159,"t":["சொற்றி யாவதுங் கேளாய்சுதநயந் துணிவுமங் குரைத்தி","கற்றி யாவது மிலையாய்க்கடையில்பல் பொருளுணர் வுடையை","பற்றி யாவது மிலையாய்ப்பரந்தவெண் செல்வமு முடையை","முற்ற யார்நினை யுணர்வார்முனைவர்தம் முனைவர்க்கு முனைவா."],"p":"","r":"She sings that he hears no word yet utters the scripture and its certain conclusions, has learned nothing yet knows endless things, clings to nothing yet has the spread-out eightfold wealth; who can fully know him, first of the first.","n":"சுதநயம் (line 1) joins the Jain terms for scripture (சுதம், sruta) and standpoint (நயம், naya). The 'eightfold wealth' of line 3 is read as the eight splendours that attend the Jina, such as those 157 lists, held without attachment (பற்று).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arivu","kalvi","pattru","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 160","lb":"NK160","ev":"","cl":[["the omniscient one's knowledge of all things is not acquired by hearing or learning","jain","asserted",2,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","அருகந்த பகவர்"],"ch":"nk:8"},"nk:161":{"book":"neelakesi","num":160,"t":["அன்மை யாரவர் தாந்தாமறிந்தன வுரைத்த பொய்யாக்கி","நின்மெ யாகிய ஞானநிகழ்ச்சி நீவிரித் துரைத்த","சொன்மை யாரிடை தெரிந்தார்தொடர்வினை முழுவதுஞ் சுடுநின்","றன்மை யார்பிற ரறிவார்தலைவர்தம் தலைவர்க்குந் தலைவா."],"p":"","r":"She sings that his true knowledge, which he set out in words, proves false what the other teachers each said from what they knew; none of them has discerned it, and who else knows his nature that burns the whole chain of deeds, leader of leaders.","n":"The last of the eight hymns turns from praise to the rival teachers, and so prepares the debate. The burning of deeds belongs to the Jina's own nature and knowledge (நின் ஞானம், நின் தன்மை); the verse does not say he burns the deeds of others.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["gnaanam","vinai","samayam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 161","lb":"NK161","ev":"","cl":[["the other teachers spoke only from what each partly knew, and their teachings are false","jain","asserted",1,"neelakesi"],["the Jina's knowledge burns away the whole chain of deeds","jain","asserted",3,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","அருகந்த பகவர்"],"ch":"nk:8"},"nk:162":{"book":"neelakesi","num":161,"t":["ஆதி யந்தளப் பரிய அருகந்த பகவர்த மறஞ்சால்","சேதி யம்புக்க வர்தந்திருந்தடி களைப்பெருந் துதிசேர்","போதி யிற்பணிந் திருந்தாள்புன்னெறி தாம்பல வவற்றுள்","யாதுகொ றான்மு னென்னாலடர்க்கற் பாலது வென்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"Having bowed at the holy feet of the Arhat, who is beyond measure of beginning and end, with great praise in his shrine full of dharma, she sits and asks which of the many mean paths she should crush first.","n":"She calls the other schools புன்னெறி, mean paths, before hearing any of them; the debates that follow are set up as a campaign (அடர்க்கற்பாலது, to be crushed) begun at the Jina's feet.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["samayam","vaadhu","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 162","lb":"NK162","ev":"After worship Neelakesi sits in the shrine and asks herself which rival path to overthrow first.","cl":[["the other religious paths are base paths to be overcome","jain","asserted",3,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி",""],"ch":"nk:8"},"nk:163":{"book":"neelakesi","num":162,"t":["ஊன்றின்ற லிழுக்கென்னானுயிரினையு முளதென்னா","னோன்றலையு நோன்பென்னானோக்குடைய கணிகையரே","போன்றிருந்து பொதியறுக்கும்புத்தன்றன் புன்னெறியை","யான்சென்றஃ தடிப்படுப்பறைக்கரும மிதுவென்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi decides: the Buddha does not call eating flesh a fault, does not say the soul exists, and does not count enduring austerity as a vow; his mean path robs people like courtesans with alluring eyes, and her task is to go and bring it down.","n":"This is a Jain's summary of Buddhism, and two of its charges are framed as silences (என்னான், he does not say). The work answers them later: on flesh in the Mokkala sarukkam (342, 347), on the soul in Neelakesi's reply to Kundalakesi (194 to 202). The charge about austerity is not answered in the passages read, so it is left as reported. The comparison to courtesans who cut the purse (பொதியறுக்கும்) is polemic; a Buddhist would not accept that the teaching despoils its hearers.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["samayam","pulaal","nonbu","vaadhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 163","lb":"NK163","ev":"Neelakesi chooses the Buddha's teaching as the first she will overthrow.","cl":[["eating flesh is not a fault","buddhist","refuted",1,"neelakesi"],["there is no abiding soul","buddhist","refuted",1,"neelakesi"],["enduring bodily austerity is not a vow","buddhist","reported",2,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி",""],"ch":"nk:8"},"nk:164":{"book":"neelakesi","num":163,"t":["மண்டலத்தி னோக்குவாள்யடுத்ததன தவதியால்","கண்டனடான் காம்பிலிக்காவலன் கடைமுகத்தோர்","தண்ட¨¡ய பொழில்நாவற்சாகைநட் டுரைபெறாக்","குண்டலகே சிப்பெயரைக்குறியாக வேகொண்டாள்."],"p":"","r":"Looking over the land by her far-seeing knowledge, she sees that at the gate of the king of Kampili a naaval branch has been planted in the cool grove and stands unanswered, and she takes the name of Kundalakesi as her mark.","n":"அவதி (line 1) is the Jain term for clairvoyant knowledge, one of the grades of knowledge below omniscience. A naaval (rose-apple) branch planted at a king's gate is the debater's challenge; உரைபெறா says no one has answered it.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vaadhu","arivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 164","lb":"NK164","ev":"By clairvoyance Neelakesi sees the unanswered challenge-branch planted at the gate of the king of Kampili and takes Kundalakesi as her opponent.","cl":[],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"ch":"nk:8"},"nk:165":{"book":"neelakesi","num":164,"t":["தருமத்திற் றிரிவில்லாடயாச்செய்தற் பொருட்டாக","நிருமித்த வகையினதாநெடுநகரை வலஞ்செய்து","திருமுத்தப் பீடிகைக்கட்சித்தரையுஞ் சிந்தித்தோர்","பெருமுத்தப் பெண்ணுருவங்கொண் டியைந்த பெற்றியளாய்."],"p":"","r":"She who never swerves from dharma, so as to do an act of compassion, circles the great city made in the ordained manner, meditates on the perfected ones on the holy pearl seat, and takes the form of a woman like a great pearl.","n":"The purpose of the debate is given as தயா செய்தல், doing compassion: refuting a school is framed as mercy toward those it misleads. The sentence runs on into 166. 'The city made in the ordained manner' (நிருமித்த வகையினதா நெடுநகர்) and the pearl seat may be the Jina's assembly rather than an earthly city; the reading is not fixed.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["arul","aram","vaadhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 165","lb":"NK165","ev":"Neelakesi circles the holy city, meditates on the siddhas and takes a woman's form to go to the debate.","cl":[],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"ch":"nk:8"},"nk:166":{"book":"neelakesi","num":165,"t":["அந்தரமே யாறாச்சென்றழனுதிவே லரசர்கட்","கிந்திரனே போன்றிருந்தவிறைமகன திடமெய்திக்","கந்திருவ மகளேன் யான்காவலனைக் காண்குறுவேன்","வந்திருந்த துரைவிரைந்து வாயிலோ யெனச்சொன்னாள்."],"p":"","r":"Going by the sky, she reaches the palace of the king who is an Indra among spear-bearing kings and tells the gatekeeper she is a Gandharva maiden who wants to see the king, and that he should announce her quickly.","n":"She gives the gatekeeper a false identity (கந்திருவ மகளேன், I am a Gandharva's daughter); the narrative lets the disguise stand without comment.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vaadhu","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 166","lb":"NK166","ev":"Neelakesi flies to Kampili and asks the gatekeeper to announce her to the king as a Gandharva maiden.","cl":[],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","வாயிலோன்"],"ch":"nk:8"},"nk:167":{"book":"neelakesi","num":166,"t":["கருங்களிறுங் களிமாவுங் கந்தோடு பந்தியவே","நெருங்குபுபோய் நீருண்ணாதேர்பண்ணா நெடுங்கடைக்குப்","பெரும்படையுஞ் சாராதிப் பெண்பாவி மரநட்டிங்","கிருந்ததன் றிறத்தினாலெனக்கரிது புகலென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"The gatekeeper replies that elephants and horses stand crowded at their posts and stalls and cannot go to water, chariots are not yoked, and not even the great army comes to the long gate, because of the sinful woman who planted a tree and sits here; so it is hard for him to let her in.","n":"The planted branch closes the king's gate itself: until someone answers the challenge, the palace's elephants, horses and chariots cannot pass. The challenger is called பெண்பாவி, sinful woman, by the gatekeeper, not by the narrator.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","vaadhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 167","lb":"NK167","ev":"The gatekeeper explains that the gate has been closed and the palace traffic stopped since a woman planted a challenge-branch there.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["வாயிலோன்","நீலகேசி"],"ch":"nk:8"},"nk:168":{"book":"neelakesi","num":167,"t":["வாயிலோ னுரைகேட்டு வடிக்கண்ணாண் முகநோக்கி","கோயிலையான் புகவிலக்குங்குறையென்னை முறைதிருத்தும்","பூசலிங் குடையையோபொருளிழவோ வுயிரிழவோ","நீயிலையார் புதனடற்குநிமித்தமிங் கென்னென்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"Hearing him and looking at his face, the sharp-eyed one asks what grievance keeps her from the palace, offers to set things right, and asks whether there is a quarrel, a loss of property or a loss of life here, and what the cause is.","n":"Line 4 (நீயிலையார் புதனடற்கு நிமித்தம்) is hard to divide; the reading takes it only as a question about the cause.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vaadhu","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 168","lb":"NK168","ev":"Neelakesi asks the gatekeeper why the gate is barred and what quarrel lies behind it.","cl":[],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","வாயிலோன்"],"ch":"nk:8"},"nk:169":{"book":"neelakesi","num":168,"t":["என்கருமம் வினவுதியேலிலிங்கியரு ளென்னோடு","நன்குரைப்பார்த் தரவ்வேண்டிநாவற்கொம் பிதுநட்டே","னுன்கரும நீ செய்வாய்நுழைந்தறிவு முடையையேல்","மன்பெரியான் றிருந்தவையுண்மாற்றந்தா வெனச்சொன்னாள்."],"p":"","r":"The words of the woman who planted the branch are given: wanting someone to argue well with her about the grace the robed ascetics teach, she planted this naaval branch; let the gatekeeper do his duty, and if anyone has the wit, let them bring an answer in the great king's assembly.","n":"The verse ends எனச் சொன்னாள், she said, after the gatekeeper's answer of 167, so it is read as Kundalakesi's challenge relayed to Neelakesi; it could also be read as spoken directly by a woman present at the gate. இலிங்கியர் (line 1), those who bear a sect's mark, is read as the Buddhist monks whose teaching she defends; the sense of அருள் here is not fixed.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vaadhu","avai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 169","lb":"NK169","ev":"The gatekeeper reports Kundalakesi's challenge: she planted the branch to call for a debate before the king's assembly.","cl":[],"ag":["குண்டலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["குண்டலகேசி","வாயிலோன்"],"ch":"nk:8"},"nk:170":{"book":"neelakesi","num":169,"t":["அப்படித்தே யெனின்வாயிலடைப்பொழிக யானைதே","ரெப்படியு மியங்குகநும்மிறைமகற்கு மிசைமினென்","றிப்படியா லிவையுரையாவிலைநாவ லிறுத்திட்டா","டுப்போடு கனிதொண்டை துயில்கொண்ட துவர்வாயாள்."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi tells the gatekeeper that if so, the gate need be barred no longer, elephants and chariots may move as they will, and he should tell his king; saying this, she of the coral-red lips plucks up the naaval branch.","n":"Uprooting the branch is the formal acceptance of the debate, and it also frees the palace traffic: the debater's first effect is on public order.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vaadhu","avai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 170","lb":"NK170","ev":"Neelakesi pulls up the challenge-branch, accepting Kundalakesi's challenge and reopening the king's gate.","cl":[],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","வாயிலோன்"],"ch":"nk:8"},"nk:171":{"book":"neelakesi","num":170,"t":["வேந்தனு மதுகேட்டே விம்முயிர்த்த வுவகையனாய்ப்","பூந்தடங்க ணல்லார் புகுதுக வெனப்புகலும்","போந்திருக்க வெனவிருக்கை பொருந்திய வாறவர்கட்","கீந்துலகத் தியற்கையு மினிதினிற் செய்திருந்தான்."],"p":"","r":"Hearing the message, the king, swelling with joy, bids the two women with wide flower-like eyes come in, gives them seats, and performs the customary courtesies graciously.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vendhan","avai","virundhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 171","lb":"NK171","ev":"The king of Kampili admits Neelakesi and Kundalakesi, seats them and receives them with courtesy.","cl":[],"ag":["காம்பிலிக் காவலன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"nk:9"},"nk:172":{"book":"neelakesi","num":171,"t":["முதலவனோ டவனூலுமந்நூலின் முடிபொருளு","நுதலிய பொருணிகழ்வுந்நுங்கோளு மெமக்கறியத்","திதலைமா ணல்குலீர்தெருட்டுமி னெனச்சொன்னா","னதலையும் பெருங்கதவமடைப்பொழித்திட் டலைவேலான்."],"p":"","r":"The king asks the two women to make clear to the court their first teacher, his scripture, the final meaning of that scripture, the matters it treats, and their own tenets.","n":"The king's five heads (முதலவன், நூல், முடிபொருள், நுதலிய பொருள், நுங்கோள்) set the order of the debate: in 178 Neelakesi takes up the first head, the teacher, before the rest.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["avai","samayam","therithal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 172","lb":"NK172","ev":"The king asks the debaters to set out their founder, his scripture, its conclusion, its subject and their own tenets.","cl":[],"ag":["காம்பிலிக் காவலன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["காம்பிலிக் காவலன்","குண்டலகேசி"],"ch":"nk:9"},"nk:173":{"book":"neelakesi","num":172,"t":["நன்றாக வுரைத்தனைநீநரதேவ நின்னவையுள்","வென்க்கோர் விழுப்பொருளும்தோற்றார்க்கோர் பெரந்துயரும்","ஒன்றாக வுரையாக்காலுரையேன்யா னெனச்சொன்னாள்","குன்றாத மதிமுகத்துக்குண்டலமா கேசியே."],"p":"","r":"Kundalakesi praises the king's request but says she will not speak unless he first declares, before his court, a great prize for the winner and great suffering for the losers.","n":"Line 2 is damaged (வென்க்கோர், பெரந்துயரும்); the sense of a stake for winner and loser is clear from 174, where the king answers it.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["avai","pagai","maanam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 173","lb":"NK173","ev":"Kundalakesi refuses to begin until the king sets a prize for the winner and a penalty for the loser.","cl":[],"ag":["குண்டலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["குண்டலகேசி","காம்பிலிக் காவலன்"],"ch":"nk:9"},"nk:174":{"book":"neelakesi","num":173,"t":["அறத்தகைய வரசனுமதுகேட்டாங் கவர்க்குரைப்பான்","சிறப்பயர்வ னன்றாகவென்றார்கட் கின்றேயான்","புறப்படுப்பன் றோற்றாரைப்பொல்லாங்கு செய்தென்றாற்","கிறப்பவும் பெருதுவந்தாரிலங்கிழையா ரிருவருமே."],"p":"","r":"The righteous king promises to honour the winner well and to drive the losers out that very day with ill treatment, and both women rejoice at this.","n":"Line 4 is garbled at its start (கிறப்பவும் பெருதுவந்தார்); 'both rejoiced greatly' is the likely sense. The king's word is kept in 228, where the beaten Kundalakesi is drummed out of the city gate.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","oruththal","avai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 174","lb":"NK174","ev":"The king pledges honour to the winner and expulsion to the loser; both debaters welcome the terms.","cl":[],"ag":["காம்பிலிக் காவலன்","நீலகேசி","குண்டலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["காம்பிலிக் காவலன்","குண்டலகேசி"],"ch":"nk:9"},"nk:175":{"book":"neelakesi","num":174,"t":["வேனிரைத்த விரிதானைவேத்தவையார் வியப்பெய்தக்","கோனுரைத்த வுரைகேட்டே குண்டலமா கேசியுந்","தானுரைத்தாள் தான்வேண்டுந்தலைவனூற் பொருணிகழ்ச்சி","தேனிரைத்த கருங்குழலா டானும்பின றெருட்டினாள்."],"p":"","r":"To the wonder of the royal assembly, Kundalakesi announces the doctrine of the teacher she has chosen, and then the dark-haired woman goes on to set it out.","n":"Line 4's 'the woman of dark tresses swarmed by bees' most likely names Kundalakesi again, since 176 and 177 are her exposition; it could be read as Neelakesi, so the line is marked uncertain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["avai","samayam","puththan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 175","lb":"NK175","ev":"Kundalakesi begins the debate by stating her teacher's doctrine before the court.","cl":[],"ag":["குண்டலகேசி"],"ec":[],"ch":"nk:9"},"nk:176":{"book":"neelakesi","num":175,"t":["ஆதிதான் பெரியனாயறக்கெடு மளவெல்லா","மூதியமே யுணர்ந்தவனுறுதரும மேயுரைத்தான்","யாதனையுந் தான்வேண்டானயலார்க்கே துன்புற்றான்","போதியா னெம்மிறைவன்பொருந்தினா ருயக்கொள்வான்."],"p":"","r":"Kundalakesi says her lord, the one of the Bodhi tree, was great from the first, knew only what is good for the whole time until he came to his end, taught only true dharma, wanted nothing for himself, suffered only for others, and saves those who come to him.","n":"The verse restates in the Neelakesi's metre the invocation of the Kundalakesi itself (கு. 1: முன் தான் பெருமைக்கண் நின்றான் ... பிறர்க்கே உறுதிக்கு உழந்தான்), so the Jain poet puts the rival work's own praise into its heroine's mouth. Each phrase is then attacked in turn: 'from the first' (179-180), 'until the end' (181), 'for others' (183-185). The claim is refuted across 179-190.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["puththan","arul","iraivan","adaikkalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 176","lb":"NK176","ev":"","cl":[["the Buddha, great from the first, wanted nothing for himself, suffered only for others and taught the true dharma until his end","buddhist","refuted",3,"kundalakesi"],["the Buddha saves those who join him","buddhist","refuted",4,"kundalakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["குண்டலகேசி","காம்பிலிக் காவலன்"],"ch":"nk:9"},"nk:177":{"book":"neelakesi","num":176,"t":["முந்துரைத்தான் முந்நூலு மந்நூலின் முடிபொருடா","மைந்துரைப்பி லுருவுழப்பறிவோடு குறிசெய்கை","சிந்தனைகட் செலவோடுவரவுமே நிலையில்லை","தந்துரைப்பி னெரிநுதிபோற்றாங்கேடு நிகழ்வென்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"Kundalakesi says the teacher first taught the three scriptures and their conclusion: the five aggregates (form, feeling, consciousness, perception and formation), and that thoughts going and coming have no permanence, so that things perish of themselves like the tip of a flame.","n":"Her list matches the Manimekalai's own (30: உருவு நுகர்ச்சி குறிப்பே பாவனை உள்ள அறிவு) except that feeling is named உழப்பு, pain or toil, and formation செய்கை. The flame-tip image gives the doctrine of momentary perishing. Neelakesi answers the five aggregates in 194 and momentary perishing in 195-199.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kandham","nilaiyaamai","puththan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 177","lb":"NK177","ev":"","cl":[["what is called a person is five aggregates: form, feeling, consciousness, perception and formation","buddhist","refuted",2,"kundalakesi"],["nothing abides; each thing perishes of itself at once, like the tip of a flame","buddhist","refuted",4,"kundalakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["குண்டலகேசி","காம்பிலிக் காவலன்"],"ch":"nk:9"},"nk:178":{"book":"neelakesi","num":177,"t":["சொல்லியவந் நான்மைமேற்றுணிவினையுந் தான்பெயர்த்து","நல்லவையை மனங்கொளீஇநான்மையின் முதல்வைத்த","வெல்லையில் குணத்தலைவரிலக்கணமென் றெடுத்ததன்மேற்","பல்வகைய பெருங்குற்றம் பதம்பதமாயக் கேளென்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi says she will set aside for now the conclusions on the four heads, and, asking the good assembly to attend, will take up the marks of the leader of boundless virtue placed first among them and show the many great faults in it, point by point.","n":"நான்மை here seems to be the four heads of 172-177 (teacher, scripture, conclusion, subject), not the four noble truths; the teacher is attacked first.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["avai","kutram","therithal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 178","lb":"NK178","ev":"Neelakesi announces that she will refute the account of the teacher before the other heads.","cl":[],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","அவை"],"ch":"nk:9"},"nk:179":{"book":"neelakesi","num":178,"t":["முன்னெனப் படுவதுதான்முதலில்லாத் தடுமாற்றம்","அன்ன தன்கட் பெரியனேலறங்கொண்ட தவமாகும்","பின்னதன்கட் பெரியனேற்பிறழ்வெய்துங் காலச்சொ","லென்னென்றான் பெரியவாறிருமையினுந் திரிந்தென்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi argues that 'the first' can only mean the beginningless round of births: if he was great within that, (his greatness was a fruit of penance, not of a first state); if he became great afterwards, the word of time ('first') goes wrong; either way the claim of greatness turns false.","n":"The target is the opening word of 176 and of the Kundalakesi's own invocation (முன்). Line 2 (அறங்கொண்ட தவமாகும்) and line 4's masculine என்னென்றான் are hard to construe, so the first half of the dilemma is reconstructed.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["piravi","anaadhi","perumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 179","lb":"NK179","ev":"","cl":[["in a round of births without beginning no teacher can be great 'from the first'","jain","asserted",1,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","குண்டலகேசி"],"ch":"nk:9"},"nk:180":{"book":"neelakesi","num":179,"t":["பெருமைமுன் பெற்றனனேற்பின்னைத்தான் முடிப்பதோர்","கருமமிங் கெவனாகுங்காட்டுதியேற் பெற்றிலன்முன்","றருமந்தான் கருதிநீசொன்னாயேற் றலைவரே","யொருமையா லறந்தெளிந்தவுழப்புலையர் முதலானார்."],"p":"","r":"If he had greatness before, she asks, what deed was left for him to complete here; and if he had not and you say he became great by reflecting on dharma, then the toiling outcastes and all others who discerned dharma with single mind would be leaders too.","n":"The second horn assumes that greatness won by learning dharma cannot single out a founder. The outcaste (புலையர்) is the debater's figure for the lowest possible claimant, a social ranking she uses, not one she argues for.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["perumai","aram","pirappu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 180","lb":"NK180","ev":"","cl":[["if a teacher's greatness was only won by coming to know dharma, any who know dharma, even the lowest, would equal him","jain","asserted",4,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","குண்டலகேசி"],"ch":"nk:9"},"nk:181":{"book":"neelakesi","num":180,"t":["தான்கெடினுந் தக்கார்கே டெண்ணற்க வென்பதனை","யூன்கொடுமை யுரைத்தான்ஃதுணர்ந்திலனே யாகாதோ","தான்கெடு மளவெல்லாநினைந்துரைத்த தத்துவந்தான்","மான்கடியு நோக்கினாய்வழியறக்கெட் டொழிவதோ."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi asks whether the teacher who taught the cruelty of flesh did not understand the saying 'even if you are ruined, do not plan the ruin of the worthy', and whether the doctrine he gave while ruining himself to the end must not itself perish utterly.","n":"Line 1 quotes, as a saying (என்பதனை), the opening of Naladiyar 80 (தான்கெடினும் தக்கார்கேடு எண்ணற்க); it is not entered as an echo because the edition's word division leaves only three written words, under the four-word test. Line 3 turns 176's 'until the end' (அறக்கெடும் அளவெல்லாம்) into 'as long as he was ruining himself'. ஊன் கொடுமை (line 2) is taken as the Jain charge that the Buddha allowed flesh-eating (cf. 163, ஊன் தின்றல் இழுக்கு என்னான்); the quatrain's second line also speaks of ஊன், which may be why it was chosen. How exactly the saying convicts him is not spelled out.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pulaal","kedu","saandror"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 181","lb":"NK181","ev":"","cl":[["even when ruined oneself, one should not plan the ruin of the worthy","general","asserted",1,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","குண்டலகேசி"],"ch":"nk:9"},"nk:182":{"book":"neelakesi","num":181,"t":["வழிவாழக் கெடுகின்றார்மாந்தருள் மேலாயார்","பழிபாவ மோராதான்பற்றினார்ப் பாழ்செய்வான்","ஒழிபாவி தலைவனென்றுரைப்பதனை யுலகத்தார்","கிழியோடு மாறாக்காசென்றான்சொற் கேட்பவோ."],"p":"","r":"Those who suffer ruin so that the right way may live are the best of men, she says; but would the world heed one who, heedless of blame and sin, lays waste those who cling to him, and call such a sinner its leader?","n":"Line 4 (கிழியோடு மாறாக் காசு என்றான் சொல்) seems to be a proverb about a coin that will not pass even with its purse; its point is not recoverable.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pazhi","saandror","kedu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 182","lb":"NK182","ev":"","cl":[["one who brings ruin on those who rely on him, heedless of sin, is no leader","general","asserted",3,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","குண்டலகேசி"],"ch":"nk:9"},"nk:183":{"book":"neelakesi","num":182,"t":["நுனைத்தலைய நுண்மயிரைநுனியுறீஇ விதிர்த்திட்டா","லனைத்துணைய தடங்கலுமறக்கிடந்த பிறந்துழப்பு","நினைக்குங்காற் பிறர்க்கேயாமென்றியா னீயன்னா","யனைத்துணைய பெரும்பாவமவன் செய்தா னாகானோ."],"p":"","r":"If one dipped the tip of a fine hair in the wide sea and flicked it off, the births of suffering he lay in were as many as those drops; you say they were all for others, she says, but would he not then have done sin of the same measure?","n":"The image measures the countless lives of the Buddha-to-be; the Jain debater reads their suffering as a ledger of evil deeds of the same size, which 184 makes explicit.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["piravi","idumbai","vinai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 183","lb":"NK183","ev":"","cl":[["suffering through countless births implies evil deeds of the same measure","jain","asserted",4,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","குண்டலகேசி"],"ch":"nk:9"},"nk:184":{"book":"neelakesi","num":183,"t":["துன்பந்தான் றீவினையின் வழித்தோன்றுந் துன்பேயா","மென்பதனை நுமரேடீ யெப்பொழுது முரைப்பவாற்","பின்புந்தான் பிறர்பிறர்க்குப் பிறந்துழப்பே யாக்கினா","லன்பினான் முன்செய்த தருவினையே யாகாதோ."],"p":"","r":"Your own people always say that suffering comes only from evil deeds, she says; so if he afterwards made births of suffering for the sake of others, was that not the fruit of a hard deed he did earlier out of attachment?","n":"She takes a tenet her opponent's school holds (நுமர் உரைப்ப) and turns it against the claim of 176: suffering cannot be chosen for others if all suffering is the fruit of one's own past deed. அன்பினான் is read as attachment, the bond she says caused the deed.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","idumbai","anbu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 184","lb":"NK184","ev":"","cl":[["suffering arises only from one's own evil deeds","buddhist","reported",1,"neelakesi"],["the Buddha's sufferings for others were the fruit of a former deed done out of attachment","jain","asserted",4,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","குண்டலகேசி"],"ch":"nk:9"},"nk:185":{"book":"neelakesi","num":184,"t":["தனக்கொன்றும் பயனின்றித்தளையாளென் றான்வருந்தி","யெனைப்பெருங் குப்பையுமெருச்சுமப்பாற் கண்டக்கால்","நினைப்பதொன் றுடைத்தவன்செய்நெடும்பாவ நிச்சலும்","மனக்கினிதா வவன்றன்னையாள்வார்மாண் புரையாயோ"],"p":"","r":"When we see a bonded labourer who, gaining nothing for himself, toils carrying great heaps of dung, she says, the one thing to think of is the long sin he once did; would you then praise the greatness of those who daily and gladly put him to work?","n":"The simile recasts 'suffered only for others' (176) as bonded labour that pays off a debt, and those who live off it as masters, not as the saved; the second half's target (the disciples) is inferred.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["adimai","vinai","idumbai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 185","lb":"NK185","ev":"","cl":[["one who toils for others with no gain to himself is paying off his own past sin","jain","asserted",3,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","குண்டலகேசி"],"ch":"nk:9"},"nk:186":{"book":"neelakesi","num":185,"t":["அவ்வகையா லுழக்கின்றா னயலார்கள் படுகின்ற","வுய்வகையில் பேரிடரையொழிப்பதன் பொருட்டாக","விவ்வகையா லருள்செய்யு மென்பதனை யெடுத்துரைத்தாள்","கொவ்வையந் துவர்ச் செவ்வாய்க்குண்டலமா கேசியே."],"p":"","r":"Kundalakesi answers that he toils in this way to remove the great suffering others cannot escape, and that this is how his compassion works.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arul","idumbai","puththan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 186","lb":"NK186","ev":"Kundalakesi replies that the Buddha's suffering is compassion acting to end the suffering of others.","cl":[["the Buddha takes on suffering to remove the inescapable suffering of others, and this is his compassion","buddhist","refuted",3,"kundalakesi"]],"ag":["குண்டலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["குண்டலகேசி","நீலகேசி"],"ch":"nk:9"},"nk:187":{"book":"neelakesi","num":186,"t":["அருளினாற் பிறர்க்குழக்குமனனென்ற வவ்வுரையைப்","புரளல்நீ பிறப்பொழியும்பொழுதின்க ணவ்வருளைப்","பொருளன்மை கண்டானோபுற்கலர்தா முலர்ந்தாரோ","தெருளநீ யுரைத்துக்காண்டிருந்தவையா ரிடையென்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi takes up the claim that he toils for others out of compassion: when his births ended, did he find that compassion was no real thing, or had all beings dried up, she asks, and tells her to explain it plainly before the assembly.","n":"புற்கலர் (pudgalas, persons) is a striking word in a Jain's mouth against a school that denies the persisting person; the argument needs only that beings still suffer after the teacher's release.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arul","veedu","uyir"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 187","lb":"NK187","ev":"","cl":[["a compassion that ceases at the teacher's release while beings still suffer was not real compassion","jain","asserted",3,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","குண்டலகேசி"],"ch":"nk:9"},"nk:188":{"book":"neelakesi","num":187,"t":["ஊடுபுக் குயிரடுந் துயரந்தா னொழிக்கின்றான்.","வீடுபெற் றிறந்தனனேல் விளிகவன தருள்பாவி","யோடுகிற் றிலனொன்றுந் தாதையையே யுழப்பித்தோ","னாடைபற் றெனவுரைத்த வவன்போன்றா னாகாதோ."],"p":"","r":"If he who ends the suffering that pierces and kills life has gained release and died out, she says, let his compassion perish: the sinner rescued no one, and is like the man in the saying who made his father toil and then said 'hold my cloth'.","n":"The closing comparison (தாதையையே உழப்பித்தோன் ஆடை பற்று) is a proverb whose story is lost; its drift is a son who burdens his father and then asks more of him.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["veedu","arul","saakkaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 188","lb":"NK188","ev":"","cl":[["a teacher who has passed into release can no longer act in compassion","jain","asserted",2,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","குண்டலகேசி"],"ch":"nk:9"},"nk:189":{"book":"neelakesi","num":188,"t":["அங்கிருவ ருளரன்றோ வறப்போக்கிப் போவாரென்","றிங்கிருந்து நீயஙரைத்தா லிவனருள்யார் தெளிகிற்பார்","அங்கிருவ ருளரெனினு மவரின் முன் னவையீரே","நங்கரும முலைப்பித்து நாம்போது மெனநக்காள்."],"p":"","r":"If you say from here that two others there will come and end suffering, who will trust this one's compassion, she asks; and even if there are two, assembly, before they come we will wear out our own deeds and go, she says, laughing.","n":"The 'two there' are not named; they may be later Buddhas the opponent appeals to. The Jain answer is self-reliance: release comes from wearing out one's own karma (நம் கருமம் உலைப்பித்து), not from another's compassion. Line 2 is corrupt (நீயஙரைத்தா).","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vinai","veedu","muyarchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 189","lb":"NK189","ev":"Neelakesi mocks the appeal to future saviours and laughs.","cl":[["each must wear out his own deeds to reach release; no other's compassion does it","jain","asserted",4,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","அவை"],"ch":"nk:9"},"nk:190":{"book":"neelakesi","num":189,"t":["முன்கொன்றான் றன்றாயைமுழுமெய்யும் போர்த்திருந்து","தின்கின்றான் பிணம்வீடுந்தெருட்டுங்காற் சூனியமே","யென்கின்றா னிவன்போல்வாரிறைவரில் லெனவுரைப்பாய்","தன்கன்று சாக்கறப்பான்றயாப்பிறிதிற் குடையவனோ."],"p":"","r":"He first killed his own mother, she says, eats corpse-flesh with his whole body wrapped in robes, and when he explains release calls it emptiness; and you say there is no lord like him? Does one who milks the cow while its own calf dies have pity for anything else?","n":"Polemic in the Jain's voice: the 'killing' of his mother turns the Buddhist story of Maya dying after his birth into a crime, and 'eats corpse-flesh' is the charge of 163; the Buddhists would accept neither. That release is emptiness is stated only to be mocked. The calf simile: one who takes milk while the calf starves shows no pity, so one who ate flesh cannot claim compassion.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pulaal","arul","soonyam","veedu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 190","lb":"NK190","ev":"","cl":[["release is emptiness","buddhist","refuted",2,"neelakesi"],["one who eats flesh has no compassion for other beings","jain","asserted",4,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","குண்டலகேசி"],"ch":"nk:9"},"nk:191":{"book":"neelakesi","num":190,"t":["கண்ணொடுகா திவையிலள்கரந்தன முலையிரண்டு","முன்னும்வா யுதட்டோடுமூக்கில ளுறுநோய்த்தி","பெண்ணழகிற் கிவள்பிறராற்பேசவும் படுவாளோ","எண்ணுங்கா லென்பேதையெனவுரைக்கு மவனொத்தாள்."],"p":"","r":"Kundalakesi was like a man who calls a woman his darling though she has no eyes or ears, her breasts are shrunken, her teeth, lips and nose are gone and she is diseased: who else would speak of her beauty?","n":"The simile closes the attack on the teacher: praising such a leader is a lover's blindness. Whether these are the narrator's words or Neelakesi's is not certain; 'ஒத்தாள்' in the third person suggests the narrator.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pedhaimai","azhagu","pullarivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 191","lb":"NK191","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"nk:9"},"nk:192":{"book":"neelakesi","num":191,"t":["பருவரலொன் றிலன்றாயைப்பழுப்பறித்தான் தலைவனிவள்","கருவரைமேற் றன்கணவன்காலனையுங் கவிழ்த்திட்டாள்","இருவரையும் போல்வாரிவ்விருநிலத்தின் மேலெங்கும்","பெருவழியார் பேரருளார்பிறர்யாரே யெனநக்காள்."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi laughs that the Buddhists' leader tore open his mother's side at his birth while himself feeling no pain, and that this woman on the dark hill threw down her own husband; who else on earth, she asks, walks so great a path with so great a compassion as these two.","n":"The praise is mockery: பெருவழியார் பேரருளார் is turned against the two whose compassion the Buddhists would claim. The first half refers to a birth that harmed the mother, the second to Kundalakesi's killing of her husband on the hill; the verse names neither story more than this, and காலனையும் (her husband, 'Death' himself?) is hard to fix. The jibe attacks persons, not doctrine, and so carries no claim.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["igal","arul","kolai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 192","lb":"NK192","ev":"Neelakesi mocks Kundalakesi with her teacher's birth and her own killing of her husband.","cl":[],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","குண்டலகேசி"],"ch":"nk:10"},"nk:193":{"book":"neelakesi","num":192,"t":["ஒண்ணுதலா யுன்றலைவனொழிவின்றி யுணர்கலான்","கண்முதலா வுரையவிக்கருவியிற் கண்டுகேட்","டெண்ணியு முணர்தலாவிலைசுமக்கு மொருவன்போ","னுண்ணுணர்வு தனக்கில்லானுரைத்ததுதா னூலாமோ."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi tells Kundalakesi that her leader does not know all things without remainder, since he knew only through the eye and the other organs, by seeing, hearing and reasoning, like a man who merely carries a load, and asks whether the word of one without subtle knowledge can be scripture.","n":"The argument ties a book's authority to its author's omniscience: what the Buddhists count as the means of knowledge (sense and inference) is here made a limit, so a teacher who used them cannot be the all-knower the Jain invocation (verse 1) makes the mark of god. The simile of the load-bearer (இலை சுமக்கும் ஒருவன்) is not fully clear.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["alavai","arivu","puththan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 193","lb":"NK193","ev":"","cl":[["the Buddha did not know all things, since he knew only through the senses, hearing and reasoning","jain","asserted",1,"neelakesi"],["only the word of one with complete and subtle knowledge can count as scripture","jain","asserted",4,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","குண்டலகேசி"],"ch":"nk:10"},"nk:194":{"book":"neelakesi","num":193,"t":["ஐங்கந்த மெனல்பிழைப்பா மறிவினின்வே றாதலாற்","சிங்குந்தன் குறியுழப்புச்செய்கையென் றிவைமூன்று","மிங்கொன்று முருவினோடிரண்டென்னாய் மிகவுரைத்தாய்","சங்கந்தா மல்லவேற்றத்துவமுந் தலைப்பட்டாய்."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi argues that to speak of five aggregates is an error: feeling, perception and volition are not other than knowing and are one with it, so with form there are only two, and in saying more Kundalakesi has stopped speaking of aggregates and arrived at real principles.","n":"The Buddhist list in Manimekalai 30 is form, feeling, perception, formation and consciousness (உருவு நுகர்ச்சி குறிப்பே பாவனை உள்ள அறிவு); the Neelakesi's terms (குறி, உழப்பு, செய்கை) are close but its own, and its reduction to knowing and form mirrors the Jain pair of soul and non-soul. Line 2's சிங்குந்தன் is obscure, so which three are merged is a reading.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kandham","arivu","thaththuvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 194","lb":"NK194","ev":"","cl":[["a person is made of five aggregates","buddhist","refuted",1,"neelakesi"],["feeling, perception and volition are modes of knowing, so the aggregates reduce to knowing and form","jain","asserted",3,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","குண்டலகேசி"],"ch":"nk:10"},"nk:195":{"book":"neelakesi","num":194,"t":["முன்னைத்தன் முழுக்கேடுமுழுக்கேட்டின் வழித்தோன்றும்","பின்னைத்தன் பிறிதறிவும்பெயர்த்துரைத்தல் பெரும்பேதாய்","என்னொக்கு மெனினெருநலிற்புகுந்தா னிடையிராத்","தன்னைத்தந் தெனைக்கொண்டுதான்சென்றா னெனலன்றோ."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi calls it great folly to say that each thing first perishes wholly and that another knowledge arises after that total destruction, likening it to saying that a man who came in yesterday at midnight handed himself over, took me, and went away.","n":"The absurd tale makes one who has ceased the agent of an exchange: if the earlier moment is wholly gone it cannot pass anything to the later one. The joke turns on the same person being both giver and taken.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","arivu","pedhaimai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 195","lb":"NK195","ev":"","cl":[["each moment perishes wholly and a different cognition arises after it","buddhist","refuted",1,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","குண்டலகேசி"],"ch":"nk:10"},"nk:196":{"book":"neelakesi","num":195,"t":["கள்ளனுந் தானேயாய்க்கையாப்புண் டவனேபோ","லுள்ளந்தா னின்றவற்றை யுணர்ந்தவற்றோ டறக்கெட்டிங்","கெள்ளனைத்து மில்லென்றாலிறப்பறித லெவனாகுந்","தெள்ளியாய் தெளிந்திருந்துசிந்தித்துக் காணாயோ."],"p":"","r":"Like a thief who is also the one who binds himself, Neelakesi says, if the mind perishes utterly together with what it knew and not a sesame seed of it remains, how can perishing itself be known; she bids the clear-minded woman think it over calmly.","n":"The thief-and-captor image charges the doctrine with needing the perished mind to witness its own end. தெள்ளியாய் ('clear one') is courteous in form and ironic in force.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","arivu","saakkaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 196","lb":"NK196","ev":"","cl":[["the mind perishes entirely together with what it knew","buddhist","refuted",2,"neelakesi"],["if nothing of the mind remains, no one is left to know its perishing","jain","asserted",3,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","குண்டலகேசி"],"ch":"nk:10"},"nk:197":{"book":"neelakesi","num":196,"t":["கோன்பட்டான் குந்தத்தாற்கத்துண்டா னேனாதி","தான்பட்டான் றளவீரன்தப்பியோ டவனருகே","யான்பட்டே னென்பவன் போல்யாத்திருந்தே சொல்லுதியால்","தான்பட்டான் பட்டார்க்குத் தன்பாட்டை யுரைக்குமோ!"],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi compares Kundalakesi to a soldier who, while king, general and champion fall and he himself flees, says 'I was killed': she speaks while bound, and the verse asks whether one who has fallen tells his own fall to the fallen.","n":"A battlefield report serves as a figure for the momentary self reporting its own extinction: speech about total perishing presupposes a speaker who did not perish.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arivu","nilaiyaamai","saakkaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 197","lb":"NK197","ev":"","cl":[["what has perished cannot report its own perishing","jain","asserted",4,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","குண்டலகேசி"],"ch":"nk:10"},"nk:198":{"book":"neelakesi","num":197,"t":["பிறைப்பிறப்பும் பிள்ளைகடம்பிறப்பினையு மெடுத்துரைப்பின்","மறைபொருள்கள் வெளிப்பட்டாமன்னுந்தாங் கருதுபவால்","குறையென்னை வான்வயிற்றாற்குண்டலமா கேசியித்","தறையகத்துப் பிறப்புரைத்தாள்றத்துவமாக் கொள்வாமோ."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi says that if one cites the birth of the crescent moon and the birth of children, hidden things become plain; she asks what is lacking, and whether the birth Kundalakesi described on this earth should be taken as a principle.","n":"The verse seems to answer an example the Buddhist used for birth without a continuing self (the moon renewed, the child born), but lines 2 and 3 (மறைபொருள்கள் வெளிப்பட்டா, வான்வயிற்றால்) are obscure and the argument cannot be fixed.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["piravi","thaththuvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 198","lb":"NK198","ev":"","cl":[["the birth the Buddhist describes cannot be taken as a real principle","jain","asserted",4,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","குண்டலகேசி"],"ch":"nk:10"},"nk:199":{"book":"neelakesi","num":198,"t":["பின்னசந் தானமும் பிறிதில் சந் தானமு","மின்னவென் றிரண்டுரைத்தெத்துணையோ பொழுதோதிச்","சொன்னதன் பொருளெல்லாஞ்சுவடின்றி யறக்கெடுத்தற்","கன்னதே யெனிலாதனாழிநாட் டாகாதோ."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi says that Kundalakesi has spent long hours expounding two kinds of continuum, the separate and the unseparated, and asks whether, if they serve only to destroy without trace the meaning of all she said, her case does not collapse.","n":"பின்ன சந்தானம் and பிறிதில் சந்தானம் name the Buddhist series of moments as the Neelakesi reports them; the Buddhist sources here do not use these terms. Line 4 (ஆதனாழிநாட் டாகாதோ) is obscure and the conclusion is a guess.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","arivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 199","lb":"NK199","ev":"","cl":[["there are two kinds of continuum, the separate and the unseparated","buddhist","refuted",1,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","குண்டலகேசி"],"ch":"nk:10"},"nk:200":{"book":"neelakesi","num":199,"t":["எண்ணிலாப் பலகந்த மிடையறா வென்றுரைப்பிற்","கண்ணுறா தொன்றுதலாற்கலப்பிலவா மாகவே","திண்ணிதா மிடையறவுதீண்டுமேற் றிரண்டொன்றா","அண்ணறான் முடிந்தறக்கே டரியதே போலுமால்."],"p":"","r":"If the countless aggregates follow without interval, Neelakesi argues, either they never meet and so stay unmixed, or, if their unbroken succession means they touch, two become one; either way the lord's complete extinction becomes hard to hold.","n":"The dilemma is aimed at release as total cessation (அறக்கேடு): a series that either never joins or fuses leaves no clean end. The reading of line 2 (கண்ணுறாது ஒன்றுதலால்) is uncertain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kandham","veedu","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 200","lb":"NK200","ev":"","cl":[["countless aggregates follow one another without interval","buddhist","refuted",1,"neelakesi"],["an unbroken series of aggregates cannot end in the teacher's complete extinction","jain","asserted",4,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","குண்டலகேசி"],"ch":"nk:10"},"nk:201":{"book":"neelakesi","num":200,"t":["வாசனையி னாமெனினும் வழியதனின் முதலதொன்","றாசனைத்து மில்லையே லறிந்துரைப்பு மரிதரோ","பேசினைநீ உளதெனினும் பெருந்தாமத் துண்ணூல்போல்","லோசனையி னெடியதோ ருயிருரைத்தா யாகாயோ."],"p":"","r":"Even if knowledge is carried on by traces, Neelakesi argues, if nothing at all of the first moment is in the one that follows, knowing and speaking become impossible; and if something is there, then like the thread inside a great garland she has described a soul stretched longer than a league.","n":"The garland image is the core of the Jain answer: what runs unbroken through the flowers is the soul the Buddhist denies. The league (ஓசனை) is mockery of a self drawn out across all its moments.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["uyir","nilaiyaamai","arivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 201","lb":"NK201","ev":"","cl":[["memory and knowledge are carried from moment to moment by traces","buddhist","refuted",1,"neelakesi"],["whatever carries the traces through the moments is a soul, as the thread runs through a garland","jain","asserted",4,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","குண்டலகேசி"],"ch":"nk:10"},"nk:202":{"book":"neelakesi","num":201,"t":["பாதிரிப்பூப் புத்தோடு பாழ்ப்பினுந்தான் பல்வழியும்","தாதுரித்தாங் கேடின்மை யென்பதுநுன் றத்துவமோ","போதுரைத்த வோடுநீர் போலுடம்பு பொன்றிடினும்","மூதுரைத்த வாசம்போன் முடிவுயிர்க்கே யாகாதோ."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi asks whether Kundalakesi's principle is that the scent of the pathiri flower stays undestroyed in the new pot even after the flower decays, and argues that then, as the scent outlasts the flower, the soul too should outlast the perishing body.","n":"The image of the new pot scented by pathiri flowers was evidently the Buddhist's example for traces that pass on without a substance; Neelakesi turns it to show something that endures. Line 4 (முடிவு உயிர்க்கே ஆகாதோ) can also be read as a question whether the soul too must end, which would reverse the point.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["uyir","yaakkai","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 202","lb":"NK202","ev":"","cl":[["traces persist as the pathiri scent stays in a new pot after the flower is gone","buddhist","refuted",1,"neelakesi"],["as the scent outlasts the flower, the soul outlasts the body","jain","asserted",4,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","குண்டலகேசி"],"ch":"nk:10"},"nk:203":{"book":"neelakesi","num":202,"t":["சத்திதான் சென்றதே யென்றியே லைந்தன்றிப்","பொத்திநீ யுரைக்கின்ற பொருளோடா றாகாவோ","சத்திதா னதுவன்றி யைந்துமே யாயினும்","பித்தியாய் முழுக்கேடு பேசினா யாகாயோ."],"p":"","r":"If Kundalakesi says that a power passed across, Neelakesi argues, that power is a sixth thing besides the five aggregates; and if it is nothing besides the five, then, mad one, she has after all spoken of total destruction.","n":"The dilemma closes the run on momentariness: either something beyond the aggregates (a self in all but name) or total destruction with nothing to carry deeds or knowledge. பித்தியாய் is a direct insult.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kandham","nilaiyaamai","arivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 203","lb":"NK203","ev":"","cl":[["a power passes from one moment of the aggregates to the next","buddhist","refuted",1,"neelakesi"],["such a power is either a sixth thing beyond the five aggregates or there is total destruction","jain","asserted",2,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","குண்டலகேசி"],"ch":"nk:10"},"nk:204":{"book":"neelakesi","num":203,"t":["அலைபலவே யுரைத்தாளென்றருகிருந்தோர் கருதுதலுந்","தலைவனூல் பொருணிகழ்ச்சிதங்கண்மேற் குற்றங்க","ணிலைபெற வுரைத்தின்மைநிறுத்துவன்யா னென்றுதன்","தலைவனீ பொருள்களேதானாட்ட லுறவினால்."],"p":"","r":"As those sitting near think that Neelakesi has raised many waves of argument, Kundalakesi resolves to establish that the faults laid on her leader's scripture, its meaning and his life have no ground, and sets out to tell what her leader did.","n":"The debate turns here from doctrine to the teacher's life (நிகழ்ச்சி): having lost the argument on momentariness, the Buddhist defends by the founder's conduct. The order of lines 3 and 4 makes the subject of நிறுத்துவன் யான் Kundalakesi speaking.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["avai","guru","puththan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 204","lb":"NK204","ev":"The onlookers judge Neelakesi's case strong, and Kundalakesi undertakes to defend her teacher's scripture and life.","cl":[["the Buddha's scripture, its meaning and his conduct are free of fault","buddhist","refuted",3,"kundalakesi"]],"ag":["அருகிருந்தோர்","குண்டலகேசி"],"ec":[],"ch":"nk:10"},"nk:205":{"book":"neelakesi","num":204,"t":["கண்கொடுத்தான் றடிகொடுத்தான்கயப்புலிக்குத் தற்கொடுத்தான்","பெண்கொடுத்தா னுடம்பினையும் பிளந்திட்டுப் பிறர்க்கீந்தான்","மண்கொடுத்தான் மகக்கொடுத்தான்மன்னுந்தற் சேர்ந்தார்க்கு","விண்கொடுத்தா னவன்கொடுத்த விரித்துரைப்பன் கேளென்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"Kundalakesi tells of her teacher's former gifts: he gave his eyes, gave his flesh, gave himself to a tiger, gave his wife, split open his body and gave it to others, gave land and gave his child, and to those who came to him he gave heaven; she bids Neelakesi listen while she tells them in full.","n":"These are the birth-stories (சாதகம், 206) of the bodhisattva's total giving, offered as proof of compassion. The gift of wife and child is listed with the gifts of the body without comment. Neelakesi answers the catalogue in 207 to 210 by calling such giving madness and its test by Indra empty.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","puththan","piravi","arul"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 205","lb":"NK205","ev":"Kundalakesi recites the Buddha's gifts in former births.","cl":[["the Buddha's gifts in former births of his eyes, body, wife, child and land prove his compassion","buddhist","refuted",1,"kundalakesi"]],"ag":["குண்டலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["குண்டலகேசி","நீலகேசி"],"ch":"nk:10"},"nk:206":{"book":"neelakesi","num":205,"t":["ஏதி லாரிடர் தீர்க்கு மெமவிறை","சாத கம்மிவை யென்று தலைத்தலை","யோகி னாணின் றொருபக லெல்லையுங்","கோதை வார்குழற் குண்டல கேசியே."],"p":"","r":"Saying that these are the birth-stories of 'our lord who removes the distress of strangers', Kundalakesi with long garlanded hair told them one after another for the whole length of a day.","n":"ஏதிலார் இடர் தீர்க்கும் (who ends the distress of those who are nothing to him) names compassion for strangers as the teacher's mark. Line 3 (யோகினாணின்று) is unclear: perhaps she stood in her practice.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","puththan","arul"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 206","lb":"NK206","ev":"Kundalakesi recites the birth-stories for a whole day.","cl":[["the Buddha removes the distress even of those who are strangers to him","buddhist","refuted",1,"kundalakesi"]],"ag":["குண்டலகேசி"],"ec":[],"ch":"nk:10"},"nk:207":{"book":"neelakesi","num":206,"t":["நூலு நாரு மிசைத்தன வொத்தலா","னீல கேசி நெடுங்க ணாள்சொல்லு","மாலும் பேயு முடையவர் செய்கையே","போலு நீ சொன்ன புத்தர் சரிதையை."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi of the long eyes answers that the Buddha's story Kundalakesi told is like the doings of the deluded and the possessed.","n":"The Jain answer does not deny the stories but judges them: gifts of one's own eyes, flesh and family are not compassion but disorder. Line 1 (நூலும் நாரும் இசைத்தன ஒத்தலால்) is unclear; it may say that her words matched as thread and fibre are twisted together.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["eegai","pedhaimai","puththan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 207","lb":"NK207","ev":"Neelakesi dismisses the birth-stories as the deeds of the mad.","cl":[["giving away one's eyes, body, wife and child is the conduct of the deluded and the possessed, not of the compassionate","jain","asserted",3,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","குண்டலகேசி"],"ch":"nk:10"},"nk:208":{"book":"neelakesi","num":207,"t":["போழுங் கண்ணுந் தலையுந் தடிகளுந்","தாழ மின்றி யிவைதம்மி னோவென","வாழு மாந்த ருழைவரு வாரில்லை","கூழன் றன்னுழை யேகொளச் செல்பவோ."],"p":"","r":"People who live their lives do not come asking 'give us slices of flesh, eyes, heads and meat at once'; would anyone go to a gruel-seller to get such things, Neelakesi asks.","n":"The objection is from use: a gift must answer a real need, and no one needs another's eyes or head. கூழன் (the gruel-man) seems to be the ordinary giver of food, set against the giver of his body.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["eegai","yaakkai","pedhaimai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 208","lb":"NK208","ev":"","cl":[["gifts of one's flesh, eyes and head meet no real need, since no living person asks for them","general","asserted",3,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","குண்டலகேசி"],"ch":"nk:10"},"nk:209":{"book":"neelakesi","num":208,"t":["பிளத்த லுள்ளிட்ட வாய்ச்செல் வதிந்திர","னளத்தற் கேலவன் றானறி யும்பிற","னுளத்தை யோரல னேலவன் றேவனாக்","கிளத்த றானோர் கிழமையும் போலுமே."],"p":"","r":"As for the splitting of the body and the rest, Neelakesi says, if Indra came to test the giver he would know the man's heart; if he could not discern another's mind, to speak of him as a god is merely to claim a title.","n":"The birth-stories in which a god comes in disguise to ask for the bodhisattva's body are attacked from the side of the tester: a god who must test does not know, and the test itself proves nothing a true knower needed. The close (ஓர் கிழமையும் போலுமே) is uncertain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["aaraaydhal","arivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 209","lb":"NK209","ev":"","cl":[["a god who must test a giver to learn his heart cannot read minds and is god only in name","jain","asserted",3,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","குண்டலகேசி"],"ch":"nk:10"},"nk:210":{"book":"neelakesi","num":209,"t":["யாவ னாயினு மன்னவ னின்மையிற்","றேவ னென்று தெளியுந் தெளிந்தபின்","சாவ னென்பதோர் சங்கைய மின்றியே","யீவ னென்பதோ ரிச்சையுந் தோன்றுமே."],"p":"","r":"Whoever the asker may be, Neelakesi argues, since no ordinary man would ask such a thing the giver will see that he is a god; and having seen it, the wish to give will arise with no fear that he will die.","n":"If the giver recognises the divine asker, the sacrifice is safe and its merit void: the gift is made in the expectation of no loss. Taking அன்னவன் இன்மையின் as 'since no such man exists' is a reading.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["eegai","saakkaadu","aaraaydhal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 210","lb":"NK210","ev":"","cl":[["a giver who knows the asker is a god gives without risk of death, so the gift is no sacrifice","jain","asserted",3,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","குண்டலகேசி"],"ch":"nk:10"},"nk:211":{"book":"neelakesi","num":210,"t":["உறுதி யல்ல துணர்வடையான்றனக","கிறுதி யேலென்று மிந்திர னெண்ணலன்","மறுதி யின்மையின் மாணிழை நீயெங்குப்","பெறுதி முன்னெடு பின்னியை யாதவே."],"p":"","r":"Indra does not think that one without firm knowledge reaches an end, Neelakesi says, and since there is no answer to this, she asks the jewelled woman where she will find a way to join the earlier and later parts of her story.","n":"Line 1 (உறுதி யல்லதுணர்வடையான்றனக) and line 3 (மறுதி) are hard to construe; the verse seems to say the birth-stories cannot be made consistent with the god's testing. The reading is tentative throughout.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["arivu","aaraaydhal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 211","lb":"NK211","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","குண்டலகேசி"],"ch":"nk:10"},"nk:212":{"book":"neelakesi","num":211,"t":["ஆத னாற்குறந் தாங்கெழு வான்றும்ம","வேத மில்சுட ரேற்றொரு தாமென்றான்","சாத கம்மிவற் றானருள் சாதிப்பா","னோதி னார்க்கு முணர்வொருப் பாயதே."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi sums up against Kundalakesi's catalogue of the Buddha's gifts: the one who proves his compassion by these birth-tales, she says, gives those who recite them only one and the same notion, and the line about a flame being lit seems to refer to one of the tales.","n":"Lines 1 and 2 (ஆதனாற் குறந்தாங்கெழுவான், வேதமில் சுடரேற்று ஒருதாம் என்றான்) do not divide with confidence; only line 3, சாதகம் இவற்றான் அருள் சாதிப்பான், is clear, and it states the position the following verses attack: that the Jataka gifts establish the Buddha's grace.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["saathakam","arul","vaadhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 212","lb":"NK212","ev":"","cl":[["the Buddha's tales of former births establish his compassion","buddhist","refuted",3,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","குண்டலகேசி"],"ch":"nk:11"},"nk:213":{"book":"neelakesi","num":212,"t":["எருது பாலின்மை யெண்ணலன் றும்மலே","கருது மாதனுங் கண் முத லாயின","தருத லல்லது தங்குறை யீதெனார்","மருதின் வாழ்பகை யானவிம் மாந்தரே."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi says the fool who gives eyes and other organs is like one who does not see that an ox has no milk, since those who asked for them never said what lack the gift would meet.","n":"The argument turns the Jataka's praise of the asker-satisfying gift into a question about need: a gift is judged by what it supplies, not by what it costs the giver. மருதின் வாழ் பகை in line 4 is obscure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["eegai","pedhaimai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 213","lb":"NK213","ev":"","cl":[["a gift that meets no stated need of the receiver is folly, not generosity","general","asserted",3,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","குண்டலகேசி"],"ch":"nk:11"},"nk:214":{"book":"neelakesi","num":213,"t":["பாக மேபிளந் தாற்பர காயமொன்","றாகு மேயென வீவஃ தாதன்மை","காக மேயுண்ணுங் கண்ணுமற் றன்னதே","யேக மெய்யும்விண் டாலியை யார்களே."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi asks whether half a body split off becomes one with another's body: thinking so is folly, for the flesh is eaten only by crows, and an eye given is the same, since a body once broken does not join another.","n":"The objection is practical, not moral: flesh and eyes cannot be grafted, so the famous gifts could not have helped anyone. பரகாயம் (another's body) is a borrowed technical word.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","yaakkai","pedhaimai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 214","lb":"NK214","ev":"","cl":[["a part cut from one body cannot become part of another, so a gift of flesh or an eye benefits no one","general","asserted",2,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","குண்டலகேசி"],"ch":"nk:11"},"nk:215":{"book":"neelakesi","num":214,"t":["உள்ளந் தானிரு பாகினு முண்மையாற்","கொள்கின் றானிவ னேகொல்லு வான்றனை","யெள்ளி நேரு மறிவில்லை யேற்பிணங்","கொள்ளென் றீர்ந்து கொடுப்பினுங் கூடுமோ."],"p":"","r":"Since the self is present in both halves of a body cut in two, Neelakesi argues, the one who takes the half is the giver's killer, and if no awareness remains, cutting off a corpse and saying 'take it' is no gift at all.","n":"The premise that the self (உள்ளம்) fills the whole body, so that both halves are alive, is the Jain view of the soul as coextensive with its body; the verse does not name the school, but it is the doctrine the argument needs. The dilemma leaves the receiver either a killer or the taker of a corpse.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["uyir","kolai","eegai","yaakkai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 215","lb":"NK215","ev":"","cl":[["the self is present throughout the body, so whoever takes a living half is the giver's killer","jain","asserted",1,"neelakesi"],["a body without awareness is a corpse, and to give a corpse is no gift","general","asserted",4,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","குண்டலகேசி"],"ch":"nk:11"},"nk:216":{"book":"neelakesi","num":215,"t":["கூறு கூறுசெய் தாலுடம் புள்ளுயிர்","வேறு வேறு செலல்வெளி றாக்கொளாய்","பாறு வாயுரைக் கும்பர மாத்தங்க","டேறு வாருள ரோதெருண் டார்களே."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi calls it folly to hold that when the body is cut into pieces the life within goes off separately with each, and asks whether any clear-minded person would trust the 'ultimate truth' that comes from a vulture's mouth.","n":"பரமார்த்தம் is the Buddhist term for ultimate truth; பாறு வாய் (vulture's mouth) mocks it as carrion talk. What the opponent is said to hold about the divided life is inferred from the gift tales, not quoted from a Buddhist text.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["uyir","yaakkai","pullarivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 216","lb":"NK216","ev":"","cl":[["the life does not divide and go separately when the body is cut into parts","jain","asserted",2,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","குண்டலகேசி"],"ch":"nk:11"},"nk:217":{"book":"neelakesi","num":216,"t":["புத்த னார்வண்ணங் கண்ட புனையிழை","சித்த னேயென்னைச் சேர்மின மென்றலி","னத்த கன்னருள் செய்கல னாய்விடின்","மத்த கம்பிளந் தானென்றன் மாயமே."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi points to the tale of the adorned woman who saw the Buddha's beauty and begged him to unite with her: if he would not grant her that grace, to say he split open his own head for a petitioner is a deceit.","n":"The point is consistency: a compassion that gives the head to one asker but refuses another's desire is not the boundless giving Kundalakesi claimed. Which tale is meant cannot be fixed from the verse.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["arul","kaamam","saathakam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 217","lb":"NK217","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","குண்டலகேசி"],"ch":"nk:11"},"nk:218":{"book":"neelakesi","num":217,"t":["ஆவ தின்மை யறிந்து மவத்தமே","சாவ தேயுங்கள் சத்துவர் சால்பெனிற்","காவல் பூண்ட கணவனோ டீமத்தின்","வேம வட்கும் விழுக்குண மாங்கொலோ."],"p":"","r":"If dying in vain, knowing that nothing will come of it, is the excellence of your Bodhisattvas, Neelakesi asks, would not the woman who burns on the pyre with the husband who guarded her also have high virtue?","n":"A reductio: the wife's death on the pyre is offered as the absurd consequence the Buddhists would have to praise. The phrase ஆவது இன்மை அறிந்தும் (knowing nothing comes of it) is the Jain description; a Buddhist would not grant that the Bodhisattva's death achieved nothing.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["saakkaadu","eegai","vaazhkkaithunai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 218","lb":"NK218","ev":"","cl":[["to die in vain, knowing nothing will come of it, is the excellence of the Bodhisattvas","buddhist","refuted",2,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","குண்டலகேசி"],"ch":"nk:11"},"nk:219":{"book":"neelakesi","num":218,"t":["சாந்தி யாகத் தரும முரைப்புழிக்","காந்தி பாவியைக் கண்டு கலகன்றா","னேந்தி வெம்படை யாலெறிந் தாற்கிடம்","போந்து கொண்டதும் பொய்யினுட் பொய்யன்றோ."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi takes the tale of the patient sage who, while preaching dharma as peace, was cut down by a wicked man with a fierce weapon and gave way to his attacker, and calls it the lie among lies.","n":"காந்தி (patience, Sanskrit kshanti) and கலகன் seem to name the Kshantivadin tale and its king; the reading of lines 2 and 4 (கலகன்றான், இடம் போந்து கொண்டதும்) is uncertain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["porai","kolai","saathakam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 219","lb":"NK219","ev":"","cl":[["the Bodhisattva's patience that yields to the one who strikes him is a mark of his greatness","buddhist","refuted",1,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","குண்டலகேசி"],"ch":"nk:11"},"nk:220":{"book":"neelakesi","num":219,"t":["யானை யுள்ளா செங்குள தங்கெலாம்","வான நின்று வழிபடல் காண்டுமான்","மீனு மல்லவும் வேதனை யெய்துழித்","தான தாதற்றா தாகதர் தன்மையோ."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi appears to ask why, if the gods are seen worshipping where the Tathagata took the form of an elephant, he did not become fish and other creatures when they too were in pain: is that the nature of the Tathagata?","n":"Line 1 (யானை யுள்ளா செங்குள தங்கெலாம்) is probably corrupt; the reading of the whole verse as a charge of selective compassion rests on line 4's ததாகதர் தன்மையோ.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["arul","saathakam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 220","lb":"NK220","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","குண்டலகேசி"],"ch":"nk:11"},"nk:221":{"book":"neelakesi","num":220,"t":["குரங்கு மாயவை கொல்லிய செல்வழி","யிரங்கி யேயுயக் கொண்டது மென்றியாற்","குரங்கு நேர்குதி யாக்குரங் கெங்குள","மரங்கள் பாய்ந்திடு மாண்பின வல்லவோ."],"p":"","r":"Against the tale that, born a monkey, he took pity on his troop when hunters came to kill them and saved them, Neelakesi asks where there are monkeys that cannot leap straight across, since monkeys by nature leap from tree to tree.","n":"The objection is to the tale's need, not its kindness: a bridge of the monkey-king's body was useless to creatures that leap.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arul","saathakam","pedhaimai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 221","lb":"NK221","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","குண்டலகேசி"],"ch":"nk:11"},"nk:222":{"book":"neelakesi","num":221,"t":["சீல நல்லவர் நீள்குவர் சேணெனிற்","கோல மில்குரங் காட்டிக் கொல் வார்களைக்","காலுங் கையு மெழற்கெனக் காண்கிலான்","வாலை நீட்டிக் கிடத்தறன் மாட்சியோ."],"p":"","r":"If it is said that those of good conduct stretch themselves far for others, Neelakesi asks whether it is greatness for a monkey to lie stretching out its tail, not seeing that hands and feet are for rising against those who come to kill.","n":"சீலம் is the Buddhist term for moral conduct. The verse sets resistance or flight, what limbs are for, against the self-offering the tale praises.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["ozhukkam","arul","pedhaimai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 222","lb":"NK222","ev":"","cl":[["those of good conduct stretch themselves out for others even to their own death","buddhist","refuted",1,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","குண்டலகேசி"],"ch":"nk:11"},"nk:223":{"book":"neelakesi","num":222,"t":["தாய்க்கொன் றான்றங்கு செங்குரு திப்புனல்","பேய்க்கொன் றீதல் பெருங்கொடை யென்பதை","வாய்க்கின் றாயினி மானுயர் மாசெலா","நாய்க்கென் றாலிது நல்லற மாங்கொலோ."],"p":"","r":"If you say that giving the red blood of one's own body to a demon is a great gift, Neelakesi asks, would giving all human filth to dogs then be good dharma?","n":"Line 1 (தாய்க்கொன்றான்) does not divide with confidence; the reductio in lines 3 and 4 is clear: a gift is not dharma merely because something of the body is handed to a creature that wants it.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["eegai","aram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 223","lb":"NK223","ev":"","cl":[["giving one's blood to a demon is a great gift","buddhist","refuted",2,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","குண்டலகேசி"],"ch":"nk:11"},"nk:224":{"book":"neelakesi","num":223,"t":["யான்செ யும்பொரு ளென்றங்கொ ரேகாந்தன்","தான்செய் திட்டனன் சாதக கற்பங்கள்","மான்செய் நோக்கி மதிப்பொழி நீயெனக்","கோன்சொ னானிது குண்டல கேசிக்கே."],"p":"","r":"The king tells Kundalakesi that a one-sided teacher, saying 'this is what I will do', made up the fictions of the birth-tales, and bids her, doe-eyed one, give up her esteem for them.","n":"ஏகாந்தன் (one who holds to a single side) carries the Jain charge against other schools of one-sidedness, set against their own many-sided view; கற்பங்கள் here reads as fabrications. The speaker is taken to be the king (கோன்) presiding over the debate, who in 174 promised to expel the loser; the verb சொன்னான் is masculine, so it is not Neelakesi.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["saathakam","vaadhu","samayam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 224","lb":"NK224","ev":"The king gives his verdict to Kundalakesi that the Jataka tales are inventions and tells her to stop esteeming them.","cl":[["the Jataka tales are fabrications of a one-sided teacher","jain","asserted",2,"kampili_vendhan"]],"ag":["காம்பிலிக் காவலன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["காம்பிலிக் காவலன்","குண்டலகேசி"],"ch":"nk:11"},"nk:225":{"book":"neelakesi","num":224,"t":["முயலுரை யிதுவெனெ மூடிக் கொண்டிருக்ந்","தயலார்க் குரைப்பவ ராத ரல்லரோ","புயலிருங் கோந்தலி பொருந்தச் சொல்லினாள்","வியலவ ருரையொடு விரோத மில்லையே"],"p":"","r":"Those who keep the hare's tale under cover and tell it to outsiders as truth are fools, the verse says; the woman with tresses dark as rain-cloud spoke fittingly, and nothing she said ran against the words of the wise.","n":"முயல் உரை is read as the hare tale (the Bodhisattva as a hare offering his body), the last of the Jataka gifts; முயல் could also mean effort. The verse changes metre, and the scribal forms எனெ and இருக்ந் in line 1 are corrupt. Whether lines 1 and 2 continue the king's verdict or are the narrator's is unclear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vaadhu","pedhaimai","arivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 225","lb":"NK225","ev":"The debate closes with Neelakesi judged to have spoken without contradiction.","cl":[],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"ch":"nk:11"},"nk:226":{"book":"neelakesi","num":225,"t":["அரசிறை யிட்ஜுசொலவவை நார்களு","முரைசெறி வுடையன வுரைத்த நீர்மைபூண்","முரைசொடு நெடுங்கொடி முலூங்க நாட்டுக","விரைவொடு படுகென வேந்த னேயினான்."],"p":"","r":"The king commands that the victory of the one whose words were dense with meaning be proclaimed quickly through the land, with the drum and the long banner sounding.","n":"Lines 1 and 3 are corrupt in the edition (இட்ஜுசொலவவை நார்களு, முலூங்க); the gist, a drum proclamation ordered by the king, rests on lines 2 to 4.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vendhan","vaadhu","pugazh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 226","lb":"NK226","ev":"The king orders Neelakesi's victory proclaimed with drum and banner.","cl":[],"ag":["காம்பிலிக் காவலன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["காம்பிலிக் காவலன்",""],"ch":"nk:11"},"nk:227":{"book":"neelakesi","num":226,"t":["இருப்பதென் னினியன்னா யிதுநுமக் குரைத்தார்யார்","சும்க்கினைக் கடிதாகச் சொல்லெளூக் கெனலோடுக்","திருக்கிளர் மதிலுஞ்சை தென்றிசை யகனகரு","ளம்க்கசந் திரனென்னு மவாச்சிய னெளூச்சொன்னாள்"],"p":"","r":"Kundalakesi is asked what remains now and who taught her this doctrine, and is told to say it quickly; she answers that it is the teacher Arkkachandran, who lives in the inner city on the south side of walled Ujjain.","n":"Who asks is not stated: the address அன்னாய் and the plural நுமக்கு suit Neelakesi questioning the defeated debater. The text is corrupt in places (சும்க்கினை, எளூ, ம்க்கசந்திரன் for அர்க்கசந்திரன், அவாச்சியன் for ஆசாரியன்).","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["guru","vaadhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 227","lb":"NK227","ev":"Kundalakesi, questioned, names Arkkachandran of Ujjain as her teacher.","cl":[],"ag":["நீலகேசி","குண்டலகேசி"],"ec":[],"ch":"nk:11"},"nk:228":{"book":"neelakesi","num":227,"t":["கட்டுரை பலசொல்லிக் காவல் நெடுங்கடை நாவலைமுன்","னட்டிவ ணகரிடை நகைசெய்து புகுந்தவிந் நன்னுதலை","வட்டிகொள் பறைகொட்டி வழுவுரை பலசொல்லி (வாரலென்று","பெட்டன பலசெய்து பெருநகர் வாயிலைப் புறப்படுத்தார்."],"p":"","r":"The townspeople, after many harsh words, beat the drum, shout many reproaches telling her not to come back, heap many insults on the fair-browed woman who had laughed as she entered the city after planting her rose-apple branch at the guarded gate, and drive her out of the city's gate.","n":"This is the penalty the king announced before the debate (174): the loser is to be sent out with shame. The நாவல் branch is the challenge Kundalakesi planted at the gate (164, 169). A stray bracket before வாரலென்று in line 3 is an edition mark.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vaadhu","pazhi","maanam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 228","lb":"NK228","ev":"Kundalakesi is driven out of the city gate with drums and insults.","cl":[],"ag":["நகர்","குண்டலகேசி"],"ec":[],"ch":"nk:11"},"nk:229":{"book":"neelakesi","num":228,"t":["புனத்திடை நறுமலர்ப் பூங்கொடி யன்னதோர் பொற்பினளாய்","எனைப்பல நூல்களு மியல்பினி னறிபவ ளேதமில்லாள்","தனக்கினி யான்செயற் பாலதுதானென்னை யெனவுரைத்தான்","இனத்தகை யேற்றரி யிடியுறுமேறெனு மிவற்றை யொப்பான்."],"p":"","r":"The king, like a lion, thunder and a bull, says of Neelakesi, who is beautiful like a flowering creeper in a field, knows many books in their nature and is without fault: what is it fitting for me to do for her now?","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vendhan","kalvi","nanri"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 229","lb":"NK229","ev":"The king asks what reward he should give Neelakesi.","cl":[],"ag":["காம்பிலிக் காவலன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["காம்பிலிக் காவலன்",""],"ch":"nk:11"},"nk:230":{"book":"neelakesi","num":229,"t":["ஆண்டகை அரசிறை அதுசொல்லக்கேட்டவவ் வறத்தகையா","டீண்டல னணிபிற புனைவெனுநினைவிலன் றினையனைத்தும்","வேண்டல னிலனொடு விழுநிதியினையவும் விறற்றகையா","யீண்டினி யறநெறி யுறுகெனவேந்திழை யியம்பினளே."],"p":"","r":"When the king said this, she of dharma, who wanted no jewels, gave no thought to adornment, and desired none of these, neither land nor great treasure, told the king: from now on, hold to the path of dharma here.","n":"The reward is refused item by item (jewels, adornment, land, treasure) and replaced by a charge laid on the king; the winner's prize becomes his conduct. வேந்திழை in line 4 is ஏந்திழை with the glide of the preceding உறுக என.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thuravu","aram","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 230","lb":"NK230","ev":"Neelakesi refuses jewels, land and wealth and asks the king instead to follow the path of dharma.","cl":[["a king should hold to the path of dharma","general","asserted",4,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["காம்பிலிக் காவலன்","நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","காம்பிலிக் காவலன்"],"ch":"nk:11"},"nk:231":{"book":"neelakesi","num":230,"t":["வந்தது மிதுபொருண் மன்னவயானென நன்னுதலா","ளிந்திர னனையநின் னிறைமையினறநெறி யிகழலென்றாங்","கந்தர நெறிசெலற் காயிழையரசனை விடுத்தருக்க","சந்திர னிருந்தவத் திசைமுன்னித்தளிரிய றானெழுந்தாள்."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi tells the king that this is what she came for, and that with a lordship like Indra's he should not scorn the path of dharma; then, leaving him, she rises and sets out through the sky toward the quarter where Arkkachandran lives.","n":"இந்திரன் அனைய repeats the comparison used of this king when Kundalakesi first arrived (166); the sky road is how Neelakesi travels between debates.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aram","arasu","vaadhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 231","lb":"NK231","ev":"Neelakesi takes leave of the king and departs through the sky toward Arkkachandran.","cl":[["a king's power, however great, must not scorn the path of dharma","general","asserted",2,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","காம்பிலிக் காவலன்"],"ch":"nk:11"},"nk:232":{"book":"neelakesi","num":231,"t":["உஞ்சை மாநக ரெய்தின ளாயத","னிஞ்சி மாட்சியு மெல்லையில் செம்மலு","மஞ்சு தோய்நெடு மாடமும் வீதியு","மஞ்சி லோதி யவையவை கண்டபின்"],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi reaches the great city of Ujjain and, once she has looked at its walls, its many great men, its cloud-touching mansions and its streets, the narrative turns to what she found there.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naadu","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 232","lb":"NK232","ev":"Neelakesi arrives at Ujjain and looks over its walls, mansions and streets.","cl":[],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"ch":"nk:12"},"nk:233":{"book":"neelakesi","num":232,"t":["பருக்கை மால்களி யானைப்பல் வேந்தரு","மிருக்க போதக வென்னும் பெருமையான்","றருக்க நீட்டமுந் தன்னிக ரில்லவ","னருக்க சந்திர னென்னு மவாச்சியன்."],"p":"","r":"In the city lived Arkkachandra, a teacher honoured by kings with great elephants, matchless in the reach of his reasoning.","n":"The last word, அவாச்சியன், is not a usual Tamil form; it seems to stand for a title of the teacher (ஆசாரியன் or a like word), but the reading is a guess.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vaadham","arivu","perumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 233","lb":"NK233","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"nk:12"},"nk:234":{"book":"neelakesi","num":233,"t":["போதி சத்துவர் புத்த ரெனப்படு","நீதி யிற்பெரி யாரன நீர்மையா","னோதி நூன்மும்மை யொப்ப வுணர்ந்தவன்","வாதி கட்கோர் வயப்புலி யேறனான்."],"p":"","r":"He was counted great in conduct like the bodhisattvas and buddhas, had mastered the threefold scripture as it should be known, and was a lion among debaters.","n":"நூல் மும்மை, the three-part scripture, fits the Buddhist canon in three baskets; the poet grants the opponent full learning before the debate, so her coming victory is over the best of his school.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vaadham","kalvi","samayam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 234","lb":"NK234","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"nk:12"},"nk:235":{"book":"neelakesi","num":234,"t":["மாடமோங்கி மழைநுழைந் தின்குயில்","பாடு பூம்பொழிற் பாங்கரோர் பள்ளியுட்","பீட மேறிப் பெருந்தகை யார்க்கெலாம்","வீடு பேறும் வினையு முரைப்புழி."],"p":"","r":"In a monastery set in a flowering grove beside high mansions where koels sing, he mounted the seat and was expounding to the assembled worthies release and the deeds that bind.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["veedu","vinai","guru"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 235","lb":"NK235","ev":"Arkkachandra sits on the teaching seat in a monastery and preaches release and karma.","cl":[],"ag":["அர்க்கசந்திரன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"nk:12"},"nk:236":{"book":"neelakesi","num":235,"t":["சென்று தானெய்திச் சிற்பிடத் தாற்புக்குத்","துன்று நீண்மணித் தூணணிந் தெண்ணென","நின்று நீலவைம் பாற்பெய ராளுமங்","கொன்று பல்வகை யோத்துரை கேட்டனள்."],"p":"","r":"She goes there, enters, and standing beside a gem-studded pillar, she whose name means blue tresses hears the various recitations being given.","n":"The poet names her by a paraphrase of her name (நீல ஐம்பால், blue fivefold hair) rather than by நீலகேசி itself.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kelvi","vaadham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 236","lb":"NK236","ev":"Neelakesi enters the monastery and stands by a pillar listening to the recitations.","cl":[],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"ch":"nk:12"},"nk:237":{"book":"neelakesi","num":236,"t":["கொள்ளு மாறுந்தன் கோரகை யுட்கஞ்சி","மொள்ளு மாறு முதுகு நெளித்துண்டு","னள்ளு மாறு மணலெடுத் திட்டவை","மெள்ள மெள்ள விழுங்கு மவைகளும்."],"p":"","r":"What she hears recited are rules: how to receive food in the bowl, how to scoop up gruel, how to bend the back while eating, and how, taking it up in small portions, to swallow it slowly.","n":"Line 3 (மணலெடுத் திட்டவை) may mean taking sand, which makes little sense of eating; it may be a corrupt or rare word for small handfuls, so the detail of the rule is unsure. The verse runs on into 238 and is completed by its வினைய விதியினால்.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["undi","ozhukkam","vesham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 237","lb":"NK237","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["இட்ட தலையர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"nk:12"},"nk:238":{"book":"neelakesi","num":237,"t":["வழிக்கு மாறுந்தம் மண்டையி னுண்டுமன்","ஒழிக்கு மாறும தூட்டு மவைகளும்","மழிக்கு மாறுந் தலைகளை மையிட்டு","விழிக்கு மாறும் வினைய விதியினால்."],"p":"","r":"The list goes on with how to wipe the bowl after eating, how to dispose of and share out leftovers, how to shave the head and how to paint the eyes with collyrium, all by the rules of the Vinaya.","n":"The Jain poet makes the Buddhist rule-book a list of table manners and grooming, which a Buddhist would not accept as its sum; Neelakesi's answer to these rules comes in 239.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vesham","ozhukkam","thuravu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 238","lb":"NK238","ev":"The monks recite Vinaya rules for eating, cleaning the bowl, shaving and painting the eyes.","cl":[["a monk's way of taking food, cleaning his bowl, shaving his head and painting his eyes is fixed by the Vinaya's rules","buddhist","refuted",4,"buddhist_monks"]],"ag":["இட்ட தலையர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"nk:12"},"nk:239":{"book":"neelakesi","num":238,"t":["இனைய வேசொல்லி யிட்ட தலையராய்","வினைய நூலை வியப்பெய்து வார்க்கெலா","மனைய தேநு மறநெறி யென்றனள்","முனைவன் றன்னெறி முன்ன முணர்ந்தவள்."],"p":"","r":"When the shaven-headed monks had recited such things to all who marvelled at the Vinaya book, she who had already learned the way of the Jina asked whether that was what they called the path of dharma.","n":"Her question does not deny that the rules exist but that rules of eating and grooming amount to dharma; முனைவன் தன் நெறி reminds the reader that her standard is the Jain teaching she took in the earlier sarukkams.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aram","koodaozhukkam","samayam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 239","lb":"NK239","ev":"Neelakesi challenges the monks: is such a rule-book the path of dharma?","cl":[["rules for eating and shaving are not themselves the path of dharma","jain","asserted",3,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி","இட்ட தலையர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"nk:12"},"nk:240":{"book":"neelakesi","num":239,"t":["அவ்வு ரையம ரானுய ராசனச்","செவ்வ ரைம்மிசைத் தீத்திரள் போல்பவ","னிவ்வு ரையிவ ணென்னெனச் சொல்லினான்","றெவ்வ ரைத்திறல் வாட்டிய திண்மையான்."],"p":"","r":"Hearing that, the teacher on the high seat, like a mass of fire on a red hill and firm in having withered his opponents' power, demanded what this talk was doing here.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vaadham","avai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 240","lb":"NK240","ev":"Arkkachandra, from the high seat, demands why she speaks so.","cl":[],"ag":["அர்க்கசந்திரன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"nk:12"},"nk:241":{"book":"neelakesi","num":240,"t":["வீரஞ் செய்து விழியல் வினையநூல்","பேர தஃதேல் பெரிது மழகிதே","யோரு மஃதோ ருறுவினை யென்பதைத்","தேரச் சொல்லுநின் றிண்பொரு ளென்றனள்."],"p":"","r":"She replies that if the name of the Vinaya book means the removal of deeds, it is a fine name indeed, and asks him to set out plainly, as his firm doctrine, what that binding deed is.","n":"She seems to play on வினயம் and வினை: a discipline whose name sounds like the undoing of deeds should be able to say which deed binds. Line 1 (வீரஞ் செய்து விழியல்) is obscure, and the pun is my reading.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vinai","vaadham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 241","lb":"NK241","ev":"Neelakesi asks Arkkachandra to explain what deed the Vinaya removes.","cl":[],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","அர்க்கசந்திரன்"],"ch":"nk:12"},"nk:242":{"book":"neelakesi","num":241,"t":["வினைய தாகிய பெற்றி விரித்துநீ","தினையி னேரும் தெருட்டெனக் கென்னவே","அனைய வவ்விர தத்தோ டறிசல","மினைய கேளென் றெடுத்தன சொல்லுமே."],"p":"","r":"Asking him to unfold for her, even a millet-grain's worth, what it is to become deed, she announces that he should now hear the deceits that come with those vows, and takes them up one by one.","n":"சலம் (deceit, sophistry) is the frame word: it opens the catalogue here and closes it at 249 (சலமதோ).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","vanjam","vaadham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 242","lb":"NK242","ev":"Neelakesi begins to list the contradictions in the Buddhist vows.","cl":[],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","அர்க்கசந்திரன்"],"ch":"nk:12"},"nk:243":{"book":"neelakesi","num":242,"t":["தன்னை யீந்ததும் தாரங்க ளீந்தது","மன்ன தன்பொருள் கேட்டறங் கொண்டவன்","மன்னு மில்லயன் மாந்தரைக் காணுமேற்","பின்னைச் செய்வன பேசலு மாகுமோ."],"p":"","r":"She asks: when one who gave away himself and gave away his wives, and took dharma from hearing its meaning, sees the people of other households, can what he would do next even be spoken of?","n":"The giver of self and wives is the bodhisattva of the birth stories, though the verse does not name him; she turns the highest Buddhist gift into a sign of a man for whom no bond holds. A Buddhist would read the same gift as perfected giving.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","vaazhkkaithunai","aram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 243","lb":"NK243","ev":"","cl":[["giving away one's own body and wives is a virtuous gift on the way to buddhahood","buddhist","refuted",1,"neelakesi"],["a man who gives away his wives has shown that no bond restrains what he will do next","general","asserted",4,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","அர்க்கசந்திரன்"],"ch":"nk:12"},"nk:244":{"book":"neelakesi","num":243,"t":["காம மூரிற் கணிகைய ரோடன்ன","தூய்மை யுண்மையிற் றோற்றங் கரந்தவட்","சேம மாவகைச் செல்கமற் றென்பதும்","வாம நூலின் மறைபொரு ளல்லவோ."],"p":"","r":"She asks whether it is not the hidden teaching of their 'left' book that, in the town of pleasure among courtesans, one who hid his true form under purity and truth may be sent off with 'go safely'.","n":"The verse alludes to a story she expects the hearers to know; வாம நூல் may mean a left-handed or crooked book, and the episode cannot be fixed from the Tamil alone. This is a polemical charge, not an account a Buddhist would own.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","kaamam","vanjam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 244","lb":"NK244","ev":"","cl":[["the Buddhist book secretly allows one disguised in purity to frequent courtesans unpunished","buddhist","refuted",4,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","அர்க்கசந்திரன்"],"ch":"nk:12"},"nk:245":{"book":"neelakesi","num":244,"t":["சிங்க தத்த ரெனப்படுந் தேரனார்","சங்க போதியி லாள்கட் டயாச்செய","விங்கி தென்னென வேழாய் தவசிகட்","கெங்கெங் காமி லெனவுரைத் தானரோ."],"p":"","r":"She cites the elder called Simhadatta, who, when asked in the sangha to show compassion to people, said 'what is this?' and gave some answer to seven ascetics.","n":"Line 4 (எங்கெங் காமி லெனவுரைத் தானரோ) cannot be construed with confidence; the point seems to be that a Buddhist elder refused or evaded an act of compassion, which would turn the school's own claim of compassion against it.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["arul","thavam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 245","lb":"NK245","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","அர்க்கசந்திரன்"],"ch":"nk:12"},"nk:246":{"book":"neelakesi","num":245,"t":["யாது மில்லை யுயிரென் றறநெறி","யோதி னானவ் வுயிரிலி தன்னொடு","வேத னைதணிப் பான்வினை வீட்டிற்கும்","சாத னைநிற்குஞ் சத்துவ னாமென்றீர்."],"p":"","r":"She reminds them that they said their teacher taught that there is no soul at all, and yet that a sattva, with that soulless one, relieves suffering and is the means to release from deeds.","n":"The argument is that release and compassion need a someone who suffers and is freed, which the no-soul teaching denies; she does not answer the Buddhist account of a chain without a self, only points to the words.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["uyir","veedu","vinai","vaadham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 246","lb":"NK246","ev":"","cl":[["there is no soul at all","buddhist","refuted",1,"neelakesi"],["a being who relieves others' suffering stands as the means to release from deeds","buddhist","refuted",4,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","அர்க்கசந்திரன்"],"ch":"nk:12"},"nk:247":{"book":"neelakesi","num":246,"t":["சித்த மோடிக் கலங்கித் திரியாத","நத்தம் பெற்றது நற்றவ மேற்கொண்டான்","பத்தின் மேலும் பழிசெய்யு மேற்பள்ளி","வத்தன கண்டீர் வழக்கின்கட் கூரியீர்."],"p":"","r":"She points out, to those so sharp in argument, that one who has taken up good penance and reached a state where the mind no longer runs, stirs and wanders may still, by their rules, commit more than ten faults and keep his place in the monastery.","n":"Lines 2 and 4 (நத்தம், வத்தன) are hard; the reading that the rules tolerate a calmed monk's many faults is a guess from the shape of her catalogue.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thavam","kutram","manam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 247","lb":"NK247","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","அர்க்கசந்திரன்"],"ch":"nk:12"},"nk:248":{"book":"neelakesi","num":247,"t":["போதி யாருரு வெய்திய புற்கலர்","வேதி யாற்கிடந் தாருள ராயினான்","ஞாதி யாரென நாட்டிய கூட்டமும்","ஓதி வைத்ததொன் றுண்மை யுணர்த்துமால்"],"p":"","r":"She adds that the Pudgalas, whose assembly was set up as kin of the Buddha's, recite something that shows one truth: that there is a person after all.","n":"புற்கலர் seem to be the Buddhist school that held a 'person' (pudgala) neither the same as nor other than the aggregates; she uses an inner Buddhist dispute to answer the no-soul claim of 246. Lines 1 and 2 are hard to construe.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["uyir","samayam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 248","lb":"NK248","ev":"","cl":[["there is a person who persists, as one Buddhist assembly recites","buddhist","reported",4,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","அர்க்கசந்திரன்"],"ch":"nk:12"},"nk:249":{"book":"neelakesi","num":248,"t":["ஆரம் பிச்சி யலிவிலங் கவ்வுருச்","சீரிற் கொத்தாள் கணிகை தெருண்டாள்பெண்","ஒரு மில்லா ளுயிரிலி யூமையுந்","தார மாக்கொஃடி ரென்றல் சலமதோ."],"p":"","r":"She closes by asking whether it is not deceit to list as possible wives a hermaphrodite, an animal, a courtesan, a woman, one who has nothing, a lifeless thing and a mute.","n":"The list may twist a monastic rule that names kinds of partner for the gravest sexual offence into a permission; the Tamil does not show which rule she means, and line 3 (ஒரு மில்லா) looks damaged. A Buddhist would not accept this as his teaching.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","vaazhkkaithunai","vanjam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 249","lb":"NK249","ev":"","cl":[["the Buddhist rule allows a man to take as partner a hermaphrodite, an animal, a courtesan or a lifeless thing","buddhist","refuted",4,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","அர்க்கசந்திரன்"],"ch":"nk:12"},"nk:250":{"book":"neelakesi","num":249,"t":["பிறந்த வில்லினுள் வாழ்க்கை பிழைப்பெனு","மறங்கொண் டான்கொண் டவாச்சிய வேடத்தாற்","சிறந்த வல்லன சிங்கின வெங்கணுந்","துறந்த வான்பொருள் சொல்லவும் வல்லையோ."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi asks the monk whether he, wearing the guise taken from one who held that living on in the house of one's birth is a fault, yet full of unworthy things everywhere, can even speak of the high matter of renunciation.","n":"The monk is called by the guise word அவாச்சிய (line 2, after sandhi), which 259 repeats as his title; its sense (perhaps 'one not to be spoken of', perhaps a Buddhist monk's title) cannot be fixed from the verse. Line 3 (சிறந்த வல்லன சிங்கின) is obscure, so the charge of unworthiness is read from the frame of the argument.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["koodaozhukkam","vesham","thuravu","samayam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 250","lb":"NK250","ev":"","cl":[["the Buddha taught that staying in the household is a fault and one must leave home","buddhist","reported",1,"neelakesi"],["one whose life belies the renouncer's guise cannot teach renunciation","general","asserted",4,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","அர்க்கசந்திரன்"],"ch":"nk:13"},"nk:251":{"book":"neelakesi","num":250,"t":["உரைப்ப பேரரு ளுண்பன மீனொடூன்","றிரைப்ப மெல்லனை செய்வ விழுத்தவம்","கரைப்ப தீவினை கண்டது சூனியம்","புரைப்பின் மார்க்கம் பொருத்த முடைத்தரோ."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi lists the monks' contradictions: they preach great compassion but eat fish and flesh, spread soft beds yet claim lofty penance, claim to dissolve evil deeds, and what they have found is the void; she asks whether so faulty a path holds together.","n":"The verse is built as a run of paired verbs (உரைப்ப/உண்பன, திரைப்ப/செய்வ), each pair setting a word against a deed. The void (சூனியம்) is named here as the Buddhist goal; the charge that it is empty is answered at length in 262 and 263.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pulaal","arul","koodaozhukkam","soonyam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 251","lb":"NK251","ev":"","cl":[["preaching great compassion while eating fish and flesh is self-contradictory","jain","asserted",1,"neelakesi"],["the truth the Buddha saw is the void","buddhist","refuted",3,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","அர்க்கசந்திரன்"],"ch":"nk:13"},"nk:252":{"book":"neelakesi","num":251,"t":["எல்லா மசுசியு மென்ப வனவா","லல்லா லழுக்குற் றவனடிக் கேத்தலர்","சொல்லார் சுகமுஞ் சுகத னவனென்று","பல்லார் வருத்தம் பழுதெனப் பண்ணுப."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi says they call everything impure, yet bow only at the feet of their own defiled master, call him Sugata though they speak of no bliss, and declare the toil of others' austerities to be worthless.","n":"சுகதன் (Sugata, 'the one gone well') is played against சுகம் (bliss): the title promises the joy the doctrine is said to deny. Lines 2 and 4 do not divide with certainty (அழுக்குற் றவனடிக் கேத்தலர், பல்லார் வருத்தம்), so the reading is tentative.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["samayam","thavam","koodaozhukkam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 252","lb":"NK252","ev":"","cl":[["all things are impure","buddhist","reported",1,"neelakesi"],["the hardship of bodily austerity is futile","buddhist","reported",4,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","அர்க்கசந்திரன்"],"ch":"nk:13"},"nk:253":{"book":"neelakesi","num":252,"t":["நிலையா வெனெச்சொல்லி நேர்ப்ப பொருடூயே","மலையோ ரனையந்ன் மாட மெடுப்ப","விலையே யுயிரென் றிறந்த நினைப","புலைசே யமர்ந்தவர் புத்தியின் வண்ணமே"],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi mocks them as people who say all things do not last yet accept goods, raise mansions like mountains, and treat life as a thing with a price: such is the understanding of those who sit in baseness.","n":"The doctrine of impermanence is turned against the monastery: those who teach that nothing stays build as if to stay. Line 2 (மலையோ ரனையந்ன்) and line 3 (விலையே யுயிரென் றிறந்த நினைப) are corrupt or hard to divide.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","koodaozhukkam","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 253","lb":"NK253","ev":"","cl":[["all things are impermanent","buddhist","reported",1,"neelakesi"],["those who teach impermanence while building great dwellings belie their teaching","general","asserted",2,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","அர்க்கசந்திரன்"],"ch":"nk:13"},"nk:254":{"book":"neelakesi","num":253,"t":["மயித்திரம் பாவித்து மற்றவற் றூனை","யசிப்பன வேபோ லமர்ந்ததிருந்த துண்ணுஞ்","சயித்தியங் காணித் தலையினை முட்டும்","பயித்தியங் கொண்டவர் பண்புமாஃ தொக்கும்"],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi says they cultivate friendliness toward creatures and then sit eating those very creatures' flesh as if devouring them, and knock their heads before a shrine; the ways of madmen are just like this.","n":"மயித்திரம் (maitri) and சயித்தியம் (caitya) are Sanskrit-derived Buddhist terms used precisely, and பயித்தியம் (madness) rhymes on them; the mockery is in the sound as well as the charge.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pulaal","arul","vazhipaadu","koodaozhukkam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 254","lb":"NK254","ev":"","cl":[["monks cultivate friendliness toward all beings","buddhist","reported",1,"neelakesi"],["one who meditates on friendliness toward beings and then eats their flesh acts like a madman","jain","asserted",4,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","அர்க்கசந்திரன்"],"ch":"nk:13"},"nk:255":{"book":"neelakesi","num":254,"t":["புத்த ருருவுக்கும் போலிக்கும் போலியை","மத்தகத் தேத்தி வணங்கி வழிபடுஞ்","செத்த பொழுதினச் செந்தடி மென்றிடு","மத்த னுடைய வருள்வகை வண்ணம்."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi says they set on their heads and worship a copy of a copy of the Buddha's form, and when a creature has died they chew its red flesh: such is the kind of grace their master taught.","n":"செத்த பொழுதின் (when it has died) points at the defence that flesh of an animal not killed for the eater is allowed; the verse only sneers at it, and 260 gives the argument against it. The image worship is mocked as twice removed from its original (போலிக்கும் போலி).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pulaal","vazhipaadu","arul"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 255","lb":"NK255","ev":"","cl":[["flesh of a creature that has already died may be eaten","buddhist","refuted",3,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","அர்க்கசந்திரன்"],"ch":"nk:13"},"nk:256":{"book":"neelakesi","num":255,"t":["பேனறாக் கூறை பெருமுடுகுநாறுமேற் றுக்கந் துக்கம்","மானறா நோக்கி மணற்சுமையுந்தான்பெரிதாற் றுக்கந் துக்கந்","கூனிறாக் கண்டாலுங் கொள்ளமுடியாதேற் றுக்கந் துக்கந்","தானறாப் பறொழிலுந் தான்றுக்கமாதலாற் சருவ்வந் துக்கம்."],"p":"","r":"In a mocking song, Neelakesi has the monk cry 'suffering, suffering' when his lice-ridden robe stinks, when the load is heavy, when he sees a prawn and cannot have it, and conclude that since all his endless tasks are suffering, all is suffering.","n":"The refrain துக்கம் துக்கம் and the close சருவம் துக்கம் parody the Buddhist teaching that all is suffering by reducing it to a greedy monk's petty discomforts; a Buddhist would not accept this as the teaching (compare Manimekalai 30, where suffering is traced to clinging). Line 2 (மானறா நோக்கி மணற்சுமையுந்) is unclear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["idumbai","samayam","koodaozhukkam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 256","lb":"NK256","ev":"","cl":[["all is suffering","buddhist","refuted",4,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","அர்க்கசந்திரன்"],"ch":"nk:13"},"nk:257":{"book":"neelakesi","num":256,"t":["பொய்பொத்திச் சொல்லினவும்போங்கூலி கொண்டனவும்","வையத்தஞ் சுட்டனவும்வாழ்மருது கொன்றனவும்","கையத்தி னூனுக்கேகன்றிக் கலாய்த்தனவு","மையத்தை யின்றியடுப வாலோவழல்நரகத் துள்ளேயடுப வாலோ."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi warns that the lies told under cover, the wages taken, the burnings, the felling of living marutham trees, and the quarrels in anger over meat in the hand will without doubt torment them in the burning hell.","n":"The list joins speech (lies), livelihood (wages), harm to living plants and greed for meat; the killing of trees counts plants as lives, a Jain concern. Line 2 (வையத்தஞ் சுட்டனவும்) is uncertain in its object.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["naragam","vinai","pulaal","vaaimai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 257","lb":"NK257","ev":"","cl":[["lying, harming living trees and quarrelling over meat lead without doubt to the burning hell","jain","asserted",4,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","அர்க்கசந்திரன்"],"ch":"nk:13"},"nk:258":{"book":"neelakesi","num":257,"t":["பற்றே மிகப்பெருக்கிப் பஃறொடர்ப்பா டேயாக்கி","யற்றீர் போற் காட்டி யடைக்கலமே வவ்வுநீர்","பெற்றீரே பேயுடம் பன்றேற் பெரும்பாலு","மெற்றே யிருணரகிற் கீர்க்கு மாலோ","விரக்கமொன் றில்லீரை யீர்க்கு மாலோ."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi tells them that, swelling attachment and multiplying ties while showing themselves free of it and seizing what was left in their keeping, they will take a demon's body or else be dragged, merciless as they are, into the dark hell.","n":"அடைக்கலமே வவ்வுநீர் charges the monks with taking deposits entrusted to them; the punishment is graded, a ghost's birth or, more often, hell. The refrain ஈர்க்குமாலோ closes the song begun at 256.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pattru","koodaozhukkam","naragam","piravi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 258","lb":"NK258","ev":"","cl":[["those who swell attachment while appearing free of it and seize deposits are reborn as ghosts or dragged into hell","jain","asserted",3,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","அர்க்கசந்திரன்"],"ch":"nk:13"},"nk:259":{"book":"neelakesi","num":258,"t":["ஆங்கவ ளறங்கூறக்கேட்ட வவாச்சியன்றான்","றேங்கம ழொலிகோதாய்சித்தமே யல்லதில்லை","தீங்கொழுக் கென்றதெல்லாந்தீவினையென் னல்வேண்டார்","பூங்கமழ் காராடைபோர்த்தவெம் புத்தரென்றான்"],"p":"","r":"Hearing her teaching, the monk replies to the lady of fragrant hair that there is nothing but the mind, and that our dark-robed Buddhas would not call all that is called evil conduct evil deed.","n":"The monk's defence of the meat charge is that karma lies in intention alone (சித்தமே அல்லதில்லை). This is the Neelakesi's version of the Buddhist view; Manimekalai 30 lists bodily deeds such as killing and theft among the ten evil deeds, so a Buddhist text itself would not accept that conduct is never evil karma.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["manam","vinai","samayam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 259","lb":"NK259","ev":"Arkkachandran answers Neelakesi's charges by saying that only the mind matters.","cl":[["there is nothing but mind","buddhist","refuted",2,"arkkachandran"],["what is called evil conduct is not evil karma when the mind is pure","buddhist","refuted",3,"arkkachandran"]],"ag":["அர்க்கசந்திரன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["அர்க்கசந்திரன்","நீலகேசி"],"ch":"nk:13"},"nk:260":{"book":"neelakesi","num":259,"t":["துத்தலே வேண்டிநின்றுதோந்தொடர்ப் பாடுநீக்காய்","சித்தமே நல்லதென்றாற்றேற்றலு மாவதுண்டோ","கத்திகொண்டில் லில்வாழ்பேய்காறலை வேறுசெய்து","குத்தவதின் னும்போழ்திற்கூடுமோகன் மையேடா."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi retorts that he wants only to eat and will not cut off fault and entanglement; if he says the mind is good, who can believe it, and when a butcher with a knife cuts off leg and head and stabs, will karma not attach when he eats?","n":"This answers 259 and 255: the eater who did not kill still shares the killing. The phrase இல்லில்வாழ்பேய் (the demon that lives in the house) is read here as a figure for the household butcher, but that is uncertain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pulaal","vinai","manam","kolai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 260","lb":"NK260","ev":"","cl":[["eating flesh that another has killed binds karma to the eater whatever his intention","jain","asserted",4,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","அர்க்கசந்திரன்"],"ch":"nk:13"},"nk:261":{"book":"neelakesi","num":260,"t":["உள்ளமும் பாயிரம்மு மொக்குமேல் வீடுமுண்டாம்","கொள்ளுமேற் குற்றமஃதாக் கூடுமே பற்றுமாங்கண்","விள்ளுமேல் வேறதாய வேடமு மன்னதேயாங்","கள்ளமே சொல்லிநின்று கன்றினாற் காட்டலாமோ."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi says that if the inner mind and outer conduct agree there may be release, but if one takes flesh the fault comes with it and attachment there, and if the two part, the outward guise is just as false: can he prove otherwise by speaking deceit and growing angry?","n":"பாயிரம் is taken as the outward (Sanskrit bāhya) against உள்ளம், the inward; that reading carries the verse and is not certain. The verse answers 259 by refusing to split intention from deed.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["veedu","manam","ozhukkam","vesham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 261","lb":"NK261","ev":"","cl":[["release comes only when the inner mind and outward conduct agree","jain","asserted",1,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","அர்க்கசந்திரன்"],"ch":"nk:13"},"nk:262":{"book":"neelakesi","num":261,"t":["புனைந்துநீ சொல்லும் வீடும்போகவுண் டாக தந்தே","நினைந்துநாங் காணி னெல்லாநின்றதொன் றில்லை யென்றாற்","றுனைந்துதா னுண்மை நன்றுசூனிய மாதற் கென்றாட்","கினைந்தினைந் தேங்கி நல்லாயென்செயற் பால தென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"When she tells him that, if on reflection nothing that is stands, the release he dresses up comes to this, that the truth is to become void, the monk, grieving and sighing, asks her, good lady, what he should do.","n":"The verse turns on என்றாட்கு (to her who said): lines 1 to 3 report her reductio, line 4 his surrender. Line 1 (போகவுண் டாக தந்தே) is hard to divide.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["veedu","soonyam","samayam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 262","lb":"NK262","ev":"Arkkachandran, unable to answer her, asks Neelakesi what he must do.","cl":[["on reflection nothing endures","buddhist","refuted",2,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி","அர்க்கசந்திரன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"nk:13"},"nk:263":{"book":"neelakesi","num":262,"t":["செத்தவ ரப்பொழுதே தேவருட் செல்பவேனு","மத்தலை யின்பநோக்கா ரஞ்சுவ மாக்களந்தோ","தொத்துள வாகவென்னான் சூனிய வீடுசொன்ன","புத்தனை நோதுமத்த புலம்பனீ போகவென்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi says that even if the dead went straight among the gods, people would not look to that joy but would fear death; so we grieve for the Buddha who taught a void release without saying there would be anything to hold to, and she bids the lamenting man leave it.","n":"The argument is from fear: if even death that leads to heaven is dreaded, a release that is only voidness can attract no one. தொத்து (something to cling to) names what the void release lacks. The close (அத்த புலம்பனீ போக) can be read as a dismissal of the Buddha or a bidding to the monk to stop lamenting.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["veedu","soonyam","acham","marumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 263","lb":"NK263","ev":"","cl":[["release is a void","buddhist","refuted",3,"neelakesi"],["a release that is mere void can be no goal, since people fear even a death that leads to heaven","jain","asserted",2,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","அர்க்கசந்திரன்"],"ch":"nk:13"},"nk:264":{"book":"neelakesi","num":263,"t":["புன்னெறி யவைகளெல்லாம்போக்கிய பாக்கியத்தாய்","நன்னெறி நன்ஞானங்காட்சியு நன்குகொண்டென்","சொன்னெறி திரிவாயேற்சோர்வில்பே ரின்பமெய்தி","மன்னுதி யென்றுமற்றுங்கூறினாண் மாதராளே."],"p":"","r":"The woman further tells him that, fortunate in having cast off all base paths, if he takes up well the good path, good knowledge and right vision and walks by her word, he will reach unfailing great bliss and abide forever.","n":"நன்னெறி, நன்ஞானம், காட்சி are the Jain three jewels (conduct, knowledge, vision), given here as the path that replaces the void release.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["gnaanam","ozhukkam","veedu","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 264","lb":"NK264","ev":"Neelakesi teaches Arkkachandran the path of right conduct, knowledge and vision.","cl":[["right conduct, right knowledge and right vision lead to lasting, unfailing bliss","jain","asserted",2,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","அர்க்கசந்திரன்"],"ch":"nk:13"},"nk:265":{"book":"neelakesi","num":264,"t":["காட்டுழல் களிநல் யானைகால்கையி னோர்ப்பித் தேறித்","தோட்டியிட் டூர்வ தேபோற்சூரிய சோமன் றானும்","வாட்டடங் கண்ணி நல்லாள்வாக்கெனுந் தூக்க யிற்றாற்","பூட்டுபு கொள்ளப் பட்டான்போதியார்க் காதி யன்னான்."],"p":"","r":"As a rutting wild elephant from the forest is bound hand and foot, mounted and driven with the goad, so Suriya Soman, first among the Buddha's followers, was bound and taken by the rope of the good lady's words.","n":"சூரிய சோமன் (sun-moon) is a Tamil rendering of the name Arkkachandra (அருக்க, sun; சந்திரன், moon), which 266 gives directly.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["solvanmai","samayam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 265","lb":"NK265","ev":"Arkkachandran is won over to the Jain dharma by Neelakesi's speech.","cl":[],"ag":["அர்க்கசந்திரன்","நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"ch":"nk:13"},"nk:266":{"book":"neelakesi","num":265,"t":["அருக்கமா சந்திரனையறங்கொளீஇ யாங்கவனை","யிருக்கும்வா யொருப்படுத்திங்கீதுநுனக் குரைத்தாரைப்","பொருக்கநீ சொல்லென்னப்புத்தனார் முதன்மாணி","முருக்குவாய்சென் றவனாம்மொக்கல னெனச்சொன்னான்."],"p":"","r":"Having made Arkkachandra take up the dharma, she bade him say quickly who had taught him these things, and he said it was Mokkalan, the Buddha's chief disciple, who had gone to Murukkuvay.","n":"முருக்குவாய் is taken as a place name; the line could also be read otherwise. The naming of the teacher sends Neelakesi on to the Mokkala debate of the next sarukkam.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["samayam","guru"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 266","lb":"NK266","ev":"Arkkachandran, now converted, names Mokkalan, the Buddha's chief disciple, as his teacher.","cl":[],"ag":["நீலகேசி","அர்க்கசந்திரன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"nk:13"},"nk:267":{"book":"neelakesi","num":266,"t":["நீவருத லொழியென்றுநிறைபதும புரத்துக்கே","மாதிரந்தா னெறியாகமனம்போலச் சென்றெய்தி","மூதுரையுங் காரணமுமுழுதெழுதி யழகிதாய்ப்","போதுகளும் பொன்மணலும்புனைந்தினிய பொலிவிற்றாய்."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi tells Arkkachandra not to come with her, goes to Padumapuram along the sky as swift as thought, and reaches a vihara where old sayings and their reasons are written out in full, its ground strewn with flowers and golden sand.","n":"The verse runs on into 268 and has no main verb of its own; the place it describes is named as the பள்ளி only in 268. That the vihara's walls carry written sayings with their reasons (மூதுரையும் காரணமும்) fits a Buddhist house of teaching, but that is a reading of a compressed line.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["samayam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 267","lb":"NK267","ev":"Neelakesi leaves Arkkachandra behind and flies through the sky to Padumapuram.","cl":[],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"ch":"nk:14"},"nk:268":{"book":"neelakesi","num":267,"t":["கொடிமகரக் கோபுரமும்நெடுமதிலுங் குடிஞைகளும்","தொடிமகரத் தூணிரையுஞ்சொலற்கரிதாய்ச் சுவர்க்கத்தின்","படிமகரப் படிமையதப்பள்ளிகண் டளியள்போய்க்","கடிமகரக் கடல்கடந்துகலந்தந்த நலமென்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"Seeing the vihara with its makara-bannered gateway, high walls, rows of carved pillars and images like heaven's, she remarks with pity that this splendour is the wealth that ships crossing the shark-filled sea have brought in.","n":"Her first word is a jibe: the monastery's beauty is bought with sea-trade profit, so the renouncers live off merchants' wealth. அளியள் (pitying) sets her tone before any argument begins. குடிஞை (owl, or a carved ornament) is not certain here.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["selvam","azhagu","samayam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 268","lb":"NK268","ev":"Neelakesi sees the vihara at Padumapuram and comments on where its wealth came from.","cl":[],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி",""],"ch":"nk:14"},"nk:269":{"book":"neelakesi","num":268,"t":["ஒழுக்கமுங் கல்விகளுமுரைத்தனவே யொப்பனகள்","இழுக்கில்லாப் பெருந்தவத்திலிங்கிகளைத் தான்கண்டு","முழுத்தாள தாய்ப்பள்ளிமுற்றத்தோ ரரைமரத்தின்","குழுக்கொம்பர் பிடித்தொருகாற்குஞ்சித்து நின்றுதான்."],"p":"","r":"Seeing the robed monks, whose conduct and learning match what is said of them and who are called faultless in great penance, she stands in the vihara courtyard holding a cluster of branches of the arasu tree, one leg bent.","n":"இலிங்கிகள் names the monks by their outward sign (இலிங்கம், the mark of an order), which is the theme of her whole attack. The praise in lines 1 and 2 may be ironic. Standing with a hand on the arasu (bodhi) branch inside a Buddhist courtyard is a challenger's pose, as Kundalakesi planted the naaval branch in 228.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thavam","ozhukkam","vesham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 269","lb":"NK269","ev":"Neelakesi stands in the courtyard of the vihara, holding a branch of its arasu tree.","cl":[],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"ch":"nk:14"},"nk:270":{"book":"neelakesi","num":269,"t":["துன்னஞ்செய் தாடையைத்துவர்தோய்த்துக் கொட்டியும்","பொன்னஞ்செய் புத்தங்கப்புகையூட்டிக் கைசெய்து","தன்னமு மளித்தாயதலைசொறியு மிடையிலையா","லென்னவற்றி னாம்பயனையெனக்கறிய வுரையென்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"She asks the monks what good comes of their busy work: stitching cloth, dyeing it in ochre and beating it, perfuming it with incense, handling food, with no leisure even to scratch their heads.","n":"The question turns the monks' care for the robe into a householder's chores; the following verses press the same point. Line 3 (தன்னமு மளித்தாய) is not clear; it may refer to giving out food or to something else in the routine.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vesham","thuravu","payan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 270","lb":"NK270","ev":"Neelakesi asks the monks what fruit their work on the robe brings.","cl":[["the labour of sewing, dyeing and perfuming robes yields no fruit for one who has renounced","jain","asserted",4,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","இட்ட தலையர்"],"ch":"nk:14"},"nk:271":{"book":"neelakesi","num":270,"t":["ஆங்கவ ளதுவுரைப்பவதற்குரிய மறுமாற்றந்","தாங்களு மரைக்கில்லார் தலைசாய்த்தங் கிருந்தார்","மூங்கைமையான் மொழிகொண்டேன்மொக்கலநற் றேரயான்","பாங்கினால் வினவுவன்படிறின்றி யுரையென்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"When the monks have no answer and sit with bowed heads, she takes their silence as their reply and turns to Mokkalan, the good elder, saying she will question him properly and he must answer without deceit.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vaadhu","sol"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 271","lb":"NK271","ev":"The monks fall silent before Neelakesi, and she challenges Mokkalan to answer.","cl":[],"ag":["இட்ட தலையர்","நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","மொக்கலன்"],"ch":"nk:14"},"nk:272":{"book":"neelakesi","num":271,"t":["வீடிற்கே யெனின் ஞானம்வேண்டாதே முடியுமாற்","பீடிற்கே யெனினின்னிற்பெருஞ்செல்வர் திருந்தினார்","மூடிற்றின் பயனென்னையெனவினவ மொக்கலன","மூடிற்றுஞ் சிறிதுளதாலுருவறிதற் கெனமொழிந்தான்."],"p":"","r":"She asks whether the robe is for release, which would then come without knowledge, or for dignity, in which the wealthy outdo him, and what use covering is; Mokkalan replies that covering has a small use, that the form may be known.","n":"Her dilemma leaves the robe only two possible ends, release or prestige, and Mokkalan answers with a third, recognition, and concedes the use is small (சிறிது). The next verses attack that answer.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vesham","veedu","perumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 272","lb":"NK272","ev":"Neelakesi asks what the robe is for, and Mokkalan answers that it makes the monk known.","cl":[["if the robe brought release, release would come without knowledge","jain","asserted",1,"neelakesi"],["the robe has a small use: it lets the monk's form be known","buddhist","refuted",4,"mokkalan"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி","மொக்கலன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","மொக்கலன்"],"ch":"nk:14"},"nk:273":{"book":"neelakesi","num":272,"t":["படைப்பெளிதாற் கேடறிதாற்பலகள்வர் நவையாரா","லுடைக்கியைந்த வொலியற்றாலூன்றருவார்க் குணர்த்துமால்","விடக்கமர்ந்த வுள்ளத்தாய்வேடமு மறிவிக்குந்","தொடர்ப்பாடும் பெரிதன்றாற்றொட்டைந் பூணியோ."],"p":"","r":"She answers that robes are easy to make and hard to keep from harm and thieves, and that the garb serves mainly to tell flesh-givers who you are, you whose heart delights in meat.","n":"விடக்கமர்ந்த உள்ளத்தாய் (you whose heart is set on flesh) carries the Jain charge that Buddhist monks accept meat given to them; the Buddhist side would not accept that the robe exists to solicit it. Lines 1, 2 and 4 are compressed, and the last words (தொட்டைந் பூணியோ) are probably corrupt.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vesham","pulaal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 273","lb":"NK273","ev":"","cl":[["the Buddhist monk's garb serves to make him known to those who give flesh, and he desires flesh","jain","asserted",3,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","மொக்கலன்"],"ch":"nk:14"},"nk:274":{"book":"neelakesi","num":273,"t":["பொன்கொண்டா ராயினும் போர்வைபூச் செனிற்புலையன்","வன்கண்மை யாற்செய்தவஞ்சமே யெனவளைப்பர்","தன்றன்மை யாகியதான்பழிப்பார் தாமுளரோ","வெண்கண்டு வந்திங்கணிதுகொண்டா யெனச்சொன்னாள்."],"p":"","r":"She says that a man in such a covering who takes gold will be cornered as a cheat with a low man's hardness, that no one faults his own kind, and asks what he saw that made him take this up.","n":"The verse is obscure: போர்வை பூச்செனின் and வெண்கண்டு do not divide with confidence, and the reading given is one possibility. Its drift fits the verses around it, that the garb invites suspicion rather than trust.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vesham","vanjam","pazhi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 274","lb":"NK274","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","மொக்கலன்"],"ch":"nk:14"},"nk:275":{"book":"neelakesi","num":274,"t":["உண்ணன்மை தவமென்றங்குறுப்பெல்லா மறைக்கின்றாய்","திண்ணென்ற மனமிலைநீசிறைபலவுஞ் செய்தலால்","பெண்ணென்றும் பிறவென்றுந்தானோக்கிப் பெரும்பேதாய்","கண்ணன்றோ வுள்ளத்தைக்கலக்குவன வவைகாவாய்."],"p":"","r":"She tells him that he hides all his limbs calling it penance because his mind is not firm, and builds many enclosures around himself; it is the eyes, looking at women and other things, that stir the mind, so he should guard them.","n":"Behind the charge stands the Jain (Digambara) practice of going unclothed: covering the body is read as a confession of weak control, and restraint is placed in the senses rather than in cloth. The verse does not name nudity. உண்ணன்மை in line 1 is unclear; it may be 'not eating' or a corrupt form.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["adakkam","vesham","manam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 275","lb":"NK275","ev":"","cl":[["covering the body is a sign of an unfirm mind; restraint lies in guarding the eyes, which disturb the mind","jain","asserted",4,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","மொக்கலன்"],"ch":"nk:14"},"nk:276":{"book":"neelakesi","num":275,"t":["பெண்பாலார் கண்டக்காற் பேதுறுவ ரெனவுரைப்பாய்","திண்பான்மை யவர்க்கழியச்சிதையுநின் றவமாயின்","மண்பாலா ரவருள்ளமாண்புளதா யுரையாரா","லெண்பாலும் படாதாகியிழுக்குநின் குணமந்தோ."],"p":"","r":"If, as he says, women would be disturbed on seeing him, and his penance would crumble through their nature, then people will not say there is dignity in his mind, and his virtue falls under no head at all.","n":"She turns his excuse for covering (that women would be troubled) back on him: a penance that a woman's look can break is no penance.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thavam","nirai","pazhi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 276","lb":"NK276","ev":"","cl":[["penance that can be broken by being seen by women is not firm penance","jain","asserted",2,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","மொக்கலன்"],"ch":"nk:14"},"nk:277":{"book":"neelakesi","num":276,"t":["இழுக்கினு மிழத்தியாலிடறினு மதுவேயால்","விழுக்கலமால் வினைபெரிதால்வினைக்கேடாந் தொழிறருமா","லொழுக்கிற்கு முரித்தன்றூணோரிடையூ றுடன்கொடுக்கும்","வழுக்கின்றித் தவஞ்செய்யின்மண்டையாற் பயனென்னோ."],"p":"","r":"She attacks the begging bowl: slip and it is lost, stumble and the same, it is a costly vessel that brings much labour and harm, it is not fit for right conduct and hinders the meal, and if one does penance without fault, what is the bowl for?","n":"The bowl (மண்டை) is the Buddhist monk's mark of begging. The Jain ascetic of the Digambara rule eats from the hollow of his hands, which is the unstated contrast. Line 2 (வினை பெரிதால் வினைக்கேடாந் தொழில்) may mean much work, or much karma, from the bowl; both fit her charge.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["iravu","thavam","undi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 277","lb":"NK277","ev":"","cl":[["one who does penance without fault has no use for a begging bowl","jain","asserted",4,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","மொக்கலன்"],"ch":"nk:14"},"nk:278":{"book":"neelakesi","num":277,"t":["நிறந்தூய்தா நீரினால்வாய்தூய்தாம் பாகாற்","பறைந்துபோய் மெல்கோலாற்பல்லெலாந் தூயவாம்","புறந்தூய்மை செய்தக்கால்புரிவள்ளந் தூய்தாமே","லறந்தூய்மை கணிகையர்க்கேயாற்றவு முளதாமால்."],"p":"","r":"Water makes the skin clean, betel the mouth, the chewing stick the teeth; if cleaning the outside made the mind pure, she says, then courtesans would have purity of dharma in plenty.","n":"The reductio picks the figure most given to bathing and adornment to show that bodily care says nothing of the mind. It answers the monks' care for clean, dyed and perfumed robes in 270.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thooimai","manam","varaivinmagalir"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 278","lb":"NK278","ev":"","cl":[["outer cleanness does not make the mind pure","general","asserted",3,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","மொக்கலன்"],"ch":"nk:14"},"nk:279":{"book":"neelakesi","num":278,"t":["சவருடைய மனைவாழ்க்கையெனப்போந்து தவம்புரிந்தாய்","பவருடைய விறகிறுத்துப்பலகலங்க ளொருப்படுத்துற்","றுவரோடு பல்கூறையுடன்புழுக்கி யொலித்திடு நீ","துவரடுதி பூவடுதிசோறடலே முனிந்தாயோ."],"p":"","r":"He left home life as a burden and took up penance, she says, yet he splits firewood, gathers pots, boils cloths with dye and beats them; he cooks dye and cooks flowers: was it only cooking rice he gave up?","n":"The joke is in அடுதி (you cook): the monk still keeps a kitchen, only for dye. சவருடைய in line 1 is unclear; it is taken here as 'troublesome'.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thuravu","ilvaazhkkai","koodaozhukkam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 279","lb":"NK279","ev":"","cl":[["a renouncer who keeps the household's labours for his robes has not renounced household life","jain","asserted",4,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","மொக்கலன்"],"ch":"nk:14"},"nk:280":{"book":"neelakesi","num":279,"t":["வண்ணாரம் துன்னாரம் மச்சிகமே கச்சாரஞ்","சண்ணார மெனப்பிறவுந் தவத்துள் நீ கற்றனவா","லெண்ணார்ந்த காரங்க ளில்லகத்தே பயின்றாயே","லுண்ணாயே வயிறார வோர்ப்பொன்று மிலையேகாண்."],"p":"","r":"She lists the crafts he has learned in his penance, dyeing, tailoring and others, and says that had he practised so many trades at home he would at least have eaten his fill; of contemplation there is none.","n":"The names of the crafts in lines 1 and 2 (மச்சிகம், கச்சாரம், சண்ணாரம்) are rare and their sense is not sure. ஓர்ப்பு (reflection) in line 4 sets up 281, where the renouncer's duty is said to be contemplation.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thuravu","koodaozhukkam","ilvaazhkkai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 280","lb":"NK280","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","மொக்கலன்"],"ch":"nk:14"},"nk:281":{"book":"neelakesi","num":280,"t":["சிறந்தாய்க்கீ துரைக்கலாஞ்சிந்தனையை முடிப்பதே","துறந்தார்க்குக் கடனாகிற்சோறலாற் பிறவேண்டா","லிறந்தார்க்கு மெதிரார்க்குமிவட்காலத் துள்ளார்வான்","பிறந்தார்க்கு மிதுவன்றிப்பிறிதொன்று சொல்லாயோ."],"p":"","r":"She says that if the renouncer's duty is to bring contemplation to completion, he needs nothing but food, and asks whether he has nothing else to teach the dead, those yet to come, those now living and those born in heaven.","n":"Line 3 and 4 are hard to construe; one reading is that his teaching for all beings in all times comes down to this robe-work. The first half is clearer: contemplation needs only food to sustain it, not the crafts of 280.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thuravu","thiyaanam","undi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 281","lb":"NK281","ev":"","cl":[["the renouncer's duty is to complete contemplation, and for that he needs nothing but food","jain","asserted",2,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","மொக்கலன்"],"ch":"nk:14"},"nk:282":{"book":"neelakesi","num":281,"t":["உண்டியா லுடம்புளதாலுடம்பினா லுணர்வாமென்","றெண்டிசையும் பரந்திசைப்பவீதுனக்கே தெரியாதோ","தண்டியாய்க் கழியாதுதவஞ்செய்த லுறுதியேற்","பண்டியாற் போக்குநின்பஃறொடர்ப்பா டெனச்சொன்னாள்."],"p":"","r":"She asks whether he alone does not know what all eight directions proclaim, that the body lives by food and awareness by the body, and tells him that if penance without wasting away is what matters, he should end his many entanglements by the belly alone.","n":"The chain food, body, awareness is cited as common knowledge, not as a school's doctrine. It concedes that the body must be fed while denying that anything more (the robe's crafts) is needed. பண்டி (belly) and the last line are not certain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["undi","yaakkai","arivu","thavam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 282","lb":"NK282","ev":"","cl":[["the body exists by food and awareness by the body","general","asserted",1,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","மொக்கலன்"],"ch":"nk:14"},"nk:283":{"book":"neelakesi","num":282,"t":["அருளுடையா ளுரைப்பக்கேட்டாங்காரித் தவனுந்தன்","பொருளுடைமைத் தருக்கினும்புன்ஞானக் களிப்பினு","மருளுடையார் மதிப்பினுமாற்றந்தான் செயற்பொருட்டா","லிருளுடைந்த கூந்தலாளிட்டத்தை யெண்ணுவான்."],"p":"","r":"Hearing the compassionate woman, Mokkalan, swollen with pride in his possessions, the intoxication of petty learning and the esteem of the deluded, considers what she intends so as to frame a reply.","n":"The narrator judges him before he speaks: the three sources of his pride (wealth, petty knowledge, the regard of fools) answer the opening jibe of 268 about the vihara's wealth. ஆங்காரித்து is taken as 'puffed with ahamkara'.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["agandhai","pullarivu","vaadhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 283","lb":"NK283","ev":"Mokkalan, proud, weighs Neelakesi's purpose before answering.","cl":[],"ag":["மொக்கலன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"nk:14"},"nk:284":{"book":"neelakesi","num":283,"t":["தரணென்று நன்றென்றாடன்றன்மை யுருவென்றாள்","அரணென்னத் தெளிந்ததுதானாருகத மேமன்னு","முரணின்ற துண்மையான்மொக்கலனு முனிந்துரைப்பா","னிரணியனைப் போல்வந்திங்கிடர்ப்பட்டா யென்றானே."],"p":"","r":"Since what she held to as refuge was the Arhat's teaching and the clash between them was real, Mokkalan answers in anger that she has come here like Hiranya and met with trouble.","n":"Line 1 (தரணென்று நன்றென்றாடன்றன்மை யுருவென்றாள்) is hard to divide; தரண் may be சரண் (refuge). The comparison with Hiranya, the proud king destroyed for defying a god, is a threat, and one a Buddhist speaker borrows from Vaishnava story.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vaadhu","samayam","adaikkalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 284","lb":"NK284","ev":"Mokkalan, angered, warns Neelakesi that she has come to her ruin like Hiranya.","cl":[],"ag":["மொக்கலன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["மொக்கலன்","நீலகேசி"],"ch":"nk:14"},"nk:285":{"book":"neelakesi","num":284,"t":["என்னாலும் வெலப்பட்டாரிருவருள ரிங்கவரைச்","சொன்னாலு மறிதிநீதுடிகடியு மிடையுடைய","கொன்னாணு நெடுவேற்கட்குண்டலமா கேசியு","மன்னாளுக் கறமுரைத்தவருக்கமா சந்திரனும்."],"p":"","r":"She replies that there are two here already defeated by her, whom he will know when she names them: Kundalakesi, with waist like a drum and long spear-eyes, and Arkkachandra, who once taught that woman dharma.","n":"Arkkachandra is called the one who taught Kundalakesi (அன்னாளுக்கு அறம் உரைத்த), which matches 227, where Kundalakesi names him as her teacher.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vaadhu","pugazh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 285","lb":"NK285","ev":"Neelakesi tells Mokkalan she has already defeated Kundalakesi and Arkkachandra.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","மொக்கலன்"],"ch":"nk:14"},"nk:286":{"book":"neelakesi","num":285,"t":["என்றாளை முகநோக்கியிதுபெரிதும் பொய்த்தனைநீ","யொன்றாத கொள்கையாருலகினுள் யாவரையும்","வென்றாள்மள் றிவள்சம்புவிரதியாய்த் திரிந்தெங்கும்","நன்றாரம் பிறர்க்கீந்தான்றருமங் கொண்டென்றானாய்."],"p":"","r":"Looking her in the face, Mokkalan says this is a great lie: Kundalakesi defeated everyone of opposing creeds in the world, wandering everywhere under the vow of the naaval branch and giving dharma to others.","n":"சம்புவிரதி names the challenger's vow of the jambu (naaval) branch that Kundalakesi planted in 228. Line 3 has a doubtful form (வென்றாள்மள்) and line 4 is unclear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vaadhu","pugazh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 286","lb":"NK286","ev":"Mokkalan calls Neelakesi's claim a lie and praises Kundalakesi as undefeated.","cl":[],"ag":["மொக்கலன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["மொக்கலன்","நீலகேசி"],"ch":"nk:14"},"nk:287":{"book":"neelakesi","num":286,"t":["வேதியரை முதலாகவெலப்பட்டா ரிவரிவரென்","றோதியாங் கவையவைதா மிவையிவையென் றுரைப்பக்கேட்","டாதிகா லாவணத்துளார்கதரை வென்றதனை","நீதியா லுரைத்தியேனின்னையான் வெல்லேனோ."],"p":"","r":"She says that after hearing him list those defeated, beginning with the Vedic brahmins, and name the creed of each, if he can tell rightly how the Arhat's followers were defeated in the first hall, she will surely defeat him.","n":"Who speaks is not marked, and the verse can also be read as Mokkalan's boast; 288 (எனக் கேட்டு) only says that someone heard it. ஆதிகால் ஆவணத்துள் (in the first hall, or at the first time) is unclear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vaadhu","vedham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 287","lb":"NK287","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","மொக்கலன்"],"ch":"nk:14"},"nk:288":{"book":"neelakesi","num":287,"t":["எனக்கேட்டாங் கெடுத்துரைப்பானிந்திரர்க டொழப்படுவான்","றனக்காய தர்மமுமதர்மமுங் காலமுங்","கனப்பாட்டிற் காயமேயுயிருருவே புண்ணியமே","நினைக்குங்காற் பாவமேகட்டுவீ டெனநிறுத்தி."],"p":"","r":"Hearing this, Mokkalan sets out what the Lord worshipped by the Indras taught: dharma, adharma, time, space, the soul, matter, merit, sin, bondage and release.","n":"The list is the Jain categories as the Manimekalai's Jain gives them (27:171-182), here put in a Buddhist's mouth so that he can mock them in 289. காயம் stands for space (ஆகாயம்) and உருவு for matter, both as the line reads them.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","vinai","veedu","uyir"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 288","lb":"NK288","ev":"Mokkalan recites the Jain categories.","cl":[["the Lord worshipped by the Indras taught dharma, adharma, time, space, soul, matter, merit, sin, bondage and release","jain","reported",2,"mokkalan"]],"ag":["மொக்கலன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["மொக்கலன்","நீலகேசி"],"ch":"nk:14"},"nk:289":{"book":"neelakesi","num":288,"t":["இப்பொருட்க ணிகழ்ச்சியு மிவையிவையா மெனவிரித்துச்","செப்பினா னாதன்றன் சிந்தைக் கெழுந்தவா","றப்பொருளு மந்நிகழ்வு மவையவையா வறியாதை","வப்பிள வனமுலையார் மணல்விளையாட் டதுவேபோல்."],"p":"","r":"He spells out what happens in each of these categories and says that the Lord told them as they arose in his own mind, without knowing the things and their workings as they are, like girls building in the sand at play.","n":"The charge is that the Jina's categories are a mental construction, not knowledge of what is; Neelakesi answers it in 290 and 291.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nindhai","samayam","pullarivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 289","lb":"NK289","ev":"","cl":[["the Jain Lord's categories arose from his own mind and do not match things as they are","buddhist","refuted",3,"mokkalan"]],"ag":["மொக்கலன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["மொக்கலன்","நீலகேசி"],"ch":"nk:14"},"nk:290":{"book":"neelakesi","num":289,"t":["மொக்கலனு மிதுகூற முல்லைநா றிருங்குழலா","ணக்கனளா யிதுகூறு நாதன தியல்பறியா","யிக்கிரமத் திந்திர னிருடிகளைத் தேவியரைத்","தக்கதாத் தொழுதக்கா லவர்தலைவ ரெனலாமோ."],"p":"","r":"When Mokkalan has said this, she laughs and says he does not know the Lord's nature: if Indra worships sages and goddesses as is fitting, can they be called his masters?","n":"Her answer seems aimed at the epithet he used in 288 (worshipped by the Indras): worship by Indra does not of itself make one supreme, so the Lord's authority rests on his nature, which 291 sets out. The construal of lines 3 and 4 is not certain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","vazhipaadu","vaadhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 290","lb":"NK290","ev":"Neelakesi laughs at Mokkalan and begins her answer.","cl":[["being worshipped by Indra does not by itself make one the supreme Lord","jain","asserted",4,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["மொக்கலன்","நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","மொக்கலன்"],"ch":"nk:14"},"nk:291":{"book":"neelakesi","num":290,"t":["எந்தலைவ ரியல்பொடுநூ லின்னணமென் றறியாதாய்","சிந்தனைக்க ணாயினுந் தீமையு முரைத்திலையாற்","றந்துரைத்த தலைவனூற் றத்துவமா மாகவே","முந்துரைத்த பொருணிகழ்வு பிழைப்பின்மை முடியாவோ."],"p":"","r":"She tells him that, not knowing her Lord's nature and scripture, he has not shown a single fault in them even in thought; since what the Lord taught is the truth, are not the categories and their workings stated before established as without error?","n":"The argument rests on the Lord's nature: his teaching is தத்துவம் (truth itself), so the categories need no further proof once no fault is found. Mokkalan's 289 named no fault, only an origin.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","meyyunarvu","vaadhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 291","lb":"NK291","ev":"","cl":[["since the Lord's teaching is truth, the categories and their workings stand without error","jain","asserted",3,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","மொக்கலன்"],"ch":"nk:14"},"nk:292":{"book":"neelakesi","num":291,"t":["அத்தியைந் தெனினல்லவறுபொருளு மவையாகா","வுத்தியா வெடுத்தோதுமொன்பதனோ டொட்டலவாற்","குதிய பல்குறையே யன்றியுமிப் பொருளெல்லாம்","பொத்தியுங் காட்டுவாய்பொருளியைவோ பெரிதென்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi sets out the charge that the Jain counts do not agree with one another (if the existents are five, the six substances are not they, and neither tallies with the nine categories recited), and closes by asking whether the opponent, who hides and shows his own things at will, has an account that holds together any better.","n":"The five அத்தி (astikayas), the six substances and the nine categories are three standard Jain lists that overlap rather than compete; the charge treats them as rival totals. The answer is the run of verses that follows (293 to 296), which explains dharma and adharma, two of the five, and turns on the Buddhist's own list. Who frames the charge is not fully clear from the verse alone, though it closes with என்றாள்.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["aththikaayam","vaadhu","samayam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 292","lb":"NK292","ev":"","cl":[["the Jain counts of five extended existents, six substances and nine categories contradict one another","buddhist","refuted",1,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","மொக்கலன்"],"ch":"nk:15"},"nk:293":{"book":"neelakesi","num":292,"t":["சலம்படவே யுரைத்தனைநீதருமத்திற் செல்லுதுமென்","றிலம்படுமே லியக்கில்லையென்பதெம் முரையென்போம்","கலஞ்செல்லுங் கடலதனைக்காற்றேபோ லுந்தாதாம்","பலம்படு முரைநினக்குப்மாம்புண்ட பாலேபோல்."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi says the opponent twisted the Jain teaching into 'we move by dharma': the Jains say only that where dharma is lacking there is no motion, for dharma lets moving things go as the sea lets a ship go, without pushing it as the wind does, and good teaching turns in him to poison like milk a snake has drunk.","n":"The refuted claim is the opponent's version of the Jain doctrine, which Neelakesi calls a quibble (சலம்); the Jain view as she gives it separates a condition of motion (the sea) from its cause (the wind).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aththikaayam","vaadhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 293","lb":"NK293","ev":"","cl":[["dharma is the force that drives moving things","jain","refuted",1,"neelakesi"],["dharma is the medium without which nothing moves, not the mover itself","jain","asserted",2,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","மொக்கலன்"],"ch":"nk:15"},"nk:294":{"book":"neelakesi","num":293,"t":["அல்லதற்க மப்படியேயாமென்ற லதுகொள்ளாய்","செல்லவுஞ் செலுத்தவுநில்லவு நிறுத்தவுஞ்","சொல்லியவாய் தேய்க்குறுவாய்சொல்லிக்கொள் வலியதனால்","பல்லொடும் படத்தேய்த்தாற்பயம்பெரிதும் படுமன்றோ."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi says the opponent will not accept that adharma stands to rest as dharma stands to motion, and mocks him for wearing out his mouth repeating 'going and making go, staying and making stay' until he rubs his teeth too.","n":"அல்லதற்கம் stands for adharma (the existent of rest). The pairs செல்லவும் செலுத்தவும், நில்லவும் நிறுத்தவும் are the opponent's confusion of a thing's own going or staying with being made to go or stay.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aththikaayam","vaadhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 294","lb":"NK294","ev":"","cl":[["adharma is the medium of rest as dharma is of motion, not the cause that makes things stay","jain","asserted",1,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","மொக்கலன்"],"ch":"nk:15"},"nk:295":{"book":"neelakesi","num":294,"t":["கடனிலமா காயமேயமையாவோ விவையிரண்டு","முடனில்லை யாயினுமூனமிங் கெவனென்பாய்","மடனுடையை நீபெரிதுமன்னுயிர்க்கும் புற்கலக்கு","மிடனெல்லா வுலகி னெல்லையும் புறப்படுமோ."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi answers the opponent's question why sea, land and space are not enough, and what is lost if dharma and adharma do not exist: if they did not, the world would have no edge, and the room of souls and matter would spill past every bound.","n":"புற்கலம் is pudgala, matter. The Jain argument is cosmological: space is unbounded, so something other than space must mark where the inhabited world ends.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aththikaayam","aakaasam","uyir"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 295","lb":"NK295","ev":"","cl":[["dharma and adharma are superfluous, since sea, land and space suffice for things to move and rest","buddhist","refuted",1,"neelakesi"],["dharma and adharma fix the limit of the world within which souls and matter can move","jain","asserted",3,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","மொக்கலன்"],"ch":"nk:15"},"nk:296":{"book":"neelakesi","num":295,"t":["பலசொல்லிக் குறையென்னைப்பஞ்சமா கந்தமே","யலகில்லாப் பெரும்பரப்பினாகாய நினக்கில்லை","நிலைசெலவிற் கிவை வேண்டாநின்பொருளு மிவையல்லா","வுலகெல்லை யுரைப்பான்புக்குணர்வினையே வருத்துதியால்."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi says the opponent needs no long speech to be shown short: he admits only the five great aggregates, has no space of measureless extent, needs no conditions of rest and motion, and so, setting out to state the world's limit, only strains his own understanding.","n":"பஞ்சமா கந்தம் is the Buddhist list of five aggregates. That the Buddhist denies space as an entity is the Jain's report; the answer is that without space and the existents of motion and rest he cannot say where the world ends.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kandham","aakaasam","aththikaayam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 296","lb":"NK296","ev":"","cl":[["only the five aggregates are real, and space is no entity","buddhist","refuted",1,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","மொக்கலன்"],"ch":"nk:15"},"nk:297":{"book":"neelakesi","num":296,"t":["காலநீ வேண்டாயாய்க்கணிகமுங் கற்பமும்","சாலமும் புனைந்துரைத்தி சமழ்ப்பென்னு மிலையாகிப்","பாலமா பண்டிதனே பழநோன்பி யிவனென்பாய்","மாலுமிங் குடையையோமயக்குவதொன் றுண்டனையோ."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi mocks the opponent for denying time while speaking without shame of moments, aeons and ages, and of a man as child, scholar and old ascetic in turn, and asks whether he is deluded or has eaten something that dazes him.","n":"காலம் here is time as one of the Jain substances, not the Kural's right season. கணிகம், the moment, is the Buddhist's own word for momentariness, so the barb is that his central doctrine presupposes the time he denies.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaalam","vaadhu","pedhaimai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 297","lb":"NK297","ev":"","cl":[["time is not a real thing","buddhist","refuted",1,"neelakesi"],["one who denies time cannot speak of moments, aeons or the stages of a life","jain","asserted",2,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","மொக்கலன்"],"ch":"nk:15"},"nk:298":{"book":"neelakesi","num":297,"t":["இக்கோட்க ளெழனோக்கியிவையிவையோ யாமென்றா","லக்கோட்க ளெழனோக்கியவையவையாக் கண்டிருந்","தெக்கோளு மில்லென்பாயாண்டெண்ணி யேத்துதியான்","மெய்க்கோளா லென்றியான்மிகைதெருட்டுந் திறங்காணேன்."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi says that where the Jains, watching the heavenly bodies rise, take them as marking time, the opponent watches the same bodies, sees them only as themselves and says there is no time at all, though he counts and praises years by them; she sees no way to correct such excess by sound doctrine.","n":"கோள் may mean the planets or the tenets of the two sides; the reading as heavenly bodies fits the counting of years (ஆண்டு எண்ணி).","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaalam","vaadhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 298","lb":"NK298","ev":"","cl":[["there is no time beyond the risings of the heavenly bodies","buddhist","refuted",3,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","மொக்கலன்"],"ch":"nk:15"},"nk:299":{"book":"neelakesi","num":298,"t":["கருத்தினாற் பெற்றாமோ கண்கூடாக் கண்டோமோ","பொருத்தனையென் றுரைக்கின்றாயுறுநோயைத் தீர்ப்பதோர்","மருத்துநூ லில்லையான்மயங்கியே சொல்லாது","திருத்தியநின் னுணர்வின்மைதெருட்டிக்கா ணெனச் சொன்னாள்."],"p":"","r":"To the opponent's charge that the Jains fabricate what they neither inferred nor saw, Neelakesi answers that on that rule there could be no medical treatise to cure disease, and tells him to stop speaking in confusion and correct his own want of understanding.","n":"கருத்து is inference and கண்கூடு perception, the two means of knowledge the Buddhist logicians allow; Neelakesi's counter-instance is a body of knowledge that rests on texts.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["alavai","marundhu","noy"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 299","lb":"NK299","ev":"","cl":[["only what is perceived directly or inferred is known; anything else is fabrication","buddhist","refuted",1,"neelakesi"],["an authoritative text is a means of knowledge, as the medical treatise that cures disease shows","jain","asserted",3,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","மொக்கலன்"],"ch":"nk:15"},"nk:300":{"book":"neelakesi","num":299,"t":["பொறியுணர்வின் புலமாயபுற்கலமே யுயிரறியு","மறிவினா லறியாதேயாமாகா தெனவுரைப்பாய்","நெறியென்னை யிந்திரன்றன்நெடுநகரக் கவன்றேவி","குறியளோ நெடியளோநூலொழிப்பாய் கூறிக்காண்."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi takes up the opponent's rule that the soul knows only matter within the senses' range and that what such knowing does not reach cannot exist, and asks him to say, without scripture, whether Indra's queen in his far city is short or tall.","n":"The taunt works because the Buddhist too speaks of Indra and his heaven, which no sense has reached; Neelakesi turns his own cosmology into testimony he relies on.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pori","alavai","uyir"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 300","lb":"NK300","ev":"","cl":[["the soul knows only matter that the senses reach, and what they do not reach does not exist","buddhist","refuted",1,"neelakesi"],["what lies beyond the senses is known only through scripture","jain","asserted",4,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","மொக்கலன்"],"ch":"nk:15"},"nk:301":{"book":"neelakesi","num":300,"t":["மெய்யளவிற் றுயிரென்றுமெய்யகத் தடக்குரைத்தல்","பொய்யளவைக் குடங்குடத்திற்புகலருமை போலென்பாய்","மெய்யளவின் மெய்யுணர்வைமெய்யகத் தடக்குரைத்தி","யையனையே யடங்கானென்றதுவாதன் வண்ணக்கால்."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi takes up the objection that a soul of the body's size cannot be held inside the body, any more than one pot fits inside another of its measure, and answers that the opponent himself holds that consciousness, of the body's extent, is held within the body, so to deny the same of the self is a fool's way.","n":"The Jain soul takes the size of the body it occupies. ஆதன் (fool) and the pot image (அளவைக் குடம்) are both slightly obscure; the parity argument with consciousness is clear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["uyir","yaakkai","arivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 301","lb":"NK301","ev":"","cl":[["a soul as large as the body cannot be contained within the body","buddhist","refuted",1,"neelakesi"],["the soul extends through the whole body and is contained within it","jain","asserted",3,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","மொக்கலன்"],"ch":"nk:15"},"nk:302":{"book":"neelakesi","num":301,"t":["அருவாத லாலடங்குமுணர்வுதா னங்கென்னிற்","பெருவாத மங்கில்லைபெற்றியொன் றறியாத","திருவாள னுரைவண்ணந்தீட்டொட்டுக் கலப்பியாப்","புருவாய வுடம்பினோடுணர்வினுக் குளதாமோ."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi says that if the opponent explains consciousness as contained in the body because it is formless, there is no great dispute, since the Jains say the same of the soul, but asks how on his account formless consciousness can have contact, union, mixture or binding with a body that has form.","n":"திருவாளன் is ironic. The verse turns the opponent's problem (how is a formless soul bound?) back on his own account of consciousness and body.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["uyir","arivu","yaakkai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 302","lb":"NK302","ev":"","cl":[["being formless, the soul is held in the body as formless consciousness is","jain","asserted",1,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","மொக்கலன்"],"ch":"nk:15"},"nk:303":{"book":"neelakesi","num":302,"t":["யாப்புண்டா லுழப்பதவ்வுயிரென்றேற் கதுவன்று","போய்ப்பிண்டத் துழப்புழப்பப்புலம்புவ தென்செயலென்பா","யேப்புண்பட் டான்படநோயேதிலர்க்காய்ச் சோமாகிச்","சாப்புண்பட் டேனென்றுசாற்றுவதுன் றத்துவமோ."],"p":"","r":"When Neelakesi says that if there is bondage it is the soul that suffers, the opponent answers that when the body-mass suffers there is no 'I' to lament; she asks whether his doctrine is then that a man struck by an arrow cries 'I am wounded to death' for a pain that belongs to strangers.","n":"பிண்டம் is the lump of the body, the Buddhist's bundle without a self. Line 3 (ஏதிலர்க்காய்ச் சோமாகி) does not construe cleanly.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["uyir","yaakkai","idumbai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 303","lb":"NK303","ev":"","cl":[["where there is bondage it is the soul that suffers","jain","asserted",1,"neelakesi"],["suffering belongs to the bodily aggregate and to no self","buddhist","refuted",2,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","மொக்கலன்"],"ch":"nk:15"},"nk:304":{"book":"neelakesi","num":303,"t":["உழப்புழப்பச் செய்கையானுறுதுயருற் றேனென்றல்","பிழைப்பதுவாக் கருதாதேபெருவழியு ளிடறுதியா","லுழப்பறிவு குறிசெய்கையொருவனவே யெனச்சொன்னார்க்","கிழிக்குவதிங் கில்லாமையிதனாலே யறியனென்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi says the opponent stumbles on the high road by not seeing that 'by my acts I came to grief' is an error on his own terms, and that those who hold feeling, cognition, perception and action to belong to one being have nothing here to answer for.","n":"உழப்பு, அறிவு, குறி, செய்கை are four of the five Buddhist aggregates (feeling, consciousness, perception, formations), the non-bodily ones. Neelakesi does not deny the list; she gives all four one owner, which is what the Buddhist denies.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kandham","uyir","arivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 304","lb":"NK304","ev":"","cl":[["feeling, cognition, perception and volition belong to a single subject","jain","asserted",3,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","மொக்கலன்"],"ch":"nk:15"},"nk:305":{"book":"neelakesi","num":304,"t":["அருவாயில் யாப்பில்லையன்றாயிற் குறைபடூஉ","மிருவாறின் கூட்டமுந்தீதென்ப தெம்மிடமே","மருவாதா யுரைத்ததனைமனங்கொள்ளா யதுவன்றிப்","பொருவாறொன் றுரைத்தாலுமொருவாறு முணராயால்."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi repeats the dilemma the opponent levels at the Jains (a formless soul cannot be bound, and a soul with form would be diminished, so either way fails) and says he has not taken in what was said to him and grasps no comparison however it is put.","n":"The answer she says he ignores is the parity argument of 302: his own formless consciousness is held in the body.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["uyir","vaadhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 305","lb":"NK305","ev":"","cl":[["a formless soul cannot be bound and a soul with form would be diminished, so the soul is untenable either way","buddhist","refuted",1,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","மொக்கலன்"],"ch":"nk:15"},"nk:306":{"book":"neelakesi","num":305,"t":["அறிவெழுந் தவலிக்குமென்பதூஉ மதுவெழப்","பிறிதொன்று பேதுறுமங்கென்பதூஉம் பெரும்பேதாய்","குறிகொண்டா ருரையன்றாற்குற்றமே கொளலுறுவாய்","பொறிகொண்டு காற்றினையும்போகாமற் சிமிழாயோ."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi calls it great folly, not the word of those who know, to say that cognition arises and itself suffers and that as it arises something else is bewildered, says the opponent seizes only on faults, and taunts him to trap the wind in a machine.","n":"The target seems to be a momentary cognition taken as subject of suffering; what பிறிதொன்று names is unclear, and so is the point of the wind-trap taunt.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["arivu","pedhaimai","vaadhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 306","lb":"NK306","ev":"","cl":[["a cognition arises and is itself what suffers, while another thing is confused","buddhist","refuted",1,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","மொக்கலன்"],"ch":"nk:15"},"nk:307":{"book":"neelakesi","num":306,"t":["பிறன்சுமவான் றானடவான்பெருவினையு முய்க்கில்லா","வறஞ்செய்தா னமருலகிற்செல்லும்வா யரிதென்று","புறம்புறம்பே சொல்லியெம்பொருணிகழ்ச்சி யறியாயாற்","கறங்குகளி மல்லனவுங்காற்றெறியத் திரியாவோ."],"p":"","r":"To the objection that no one carries the soul, it does not walk, and deeds cannot convey it, so the doer of good can hardly reach the world of the gods, Neelakesi answers that he speaks from outside the doctrine: does not the whirligig turn when the wind strikes it?","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","marumai","uyir"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 307","lb":"NK307","ev":"","cl":[["since nothing carries the soul, the doer of good cannot travel to the world of the gods","buddhist","refuted",1,"neelakesi"],["deeds move the soul to its next birth as the wind turns a whirligig","jain","asserted",4,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","மொக்கலன்"],"ch":"nk:15"},"nk:308":{"book":"neelakesi","num":307,"t":["மகனேயாய்ப் பிறப்பினு மாதுயரங் கேடில்லை","யவனாகா னாயினு மறஞ் செய்த லவமாகு","மெவனாகு மென்றெம திட்டமே யுரைத்தியா","னகைநாணி நீநின்னை நன்பகலே மறைக்கின்றாய்."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi takes up the dilemma about the one reborn (if he is the same man his great sorrow has no end, and if he is not, the good he did is wasted) and says the question 'who then becomes?' only states the Jain position, mocking the opponent for hiding himself in broad daylight to escape the laughter.","n":"The dilemma suits a Buddhist attack on a persisting soul, and the reply that it states the Jain view suggests the Jain answer of a self both same and changed (see 312); but whose dilemma it is and how எமது இட்டமே is meant are not certain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["piravi","aram","vinai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 308","lb":"NK308","ev":"","cl":[["if the one reborn is the same man his sorrow never ends, and if not, the good he did is wasted","buddhist","refuted",1,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","மொக்கலன்"],"ch":"nk:15"},"nk:309":{"book":"neelakesi","num":308,"t":["வீயுடம்பிட் டுயிர்சென்று வினையுடம்பு முளகாகத்","தாயுடம்பி னகத்துடம்பு தான்வைத்த தின்றியே","நீயுடம்பு பெற்றவா றுரையென்பாய் நிழல்போலும்","டேயுடம்பு பிறிதுடம்பிற் புகல்பேதாய் காணாயோ."],"p":"","r":"To the opponent's demand that she say how a body is gained if the soul leaves the dead body with only its karma-body and no body has been laid in the mother's, Neelakesi answers that he fails to see the fiery body, like a shadow, entering another body.","n":"வினையுடம்பு and தேயுடம்பு are the Jain karmic and fiery (taijasa) bodies that go with the soul between births. Line 4 begins with டேயுடம்பு where தேயுடம்பு is expected.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["piravi","karu","vinai","uyir"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 309","lb":"NK309","ev":"","cl":[["a soul leaving the dead body with only its karma-body cannot gain a new body unless one is set in the mother's","buddhist","refuted",1,"neelakesi"],["a subtle fiery body enters the new body as a shadow does","jain","asserted",3,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","மொக்கலன்"],"ch":"nk:15"},"nk:310":{"book":"neelakesi","num":309,"t":["எப்பொருளு மொன்றொன்றிற்கிடங்கொடுத்த விரும்புண்ணீர்","புககிடங்கொண் டடங்குதலேபோலவும் தந்தைதாய்","சுக்கிலமுஞ் சோணிதமுந்தழீஇச்சுதையு ணெய்யனைத்தா","யொத்துடம்பி னகத்தடங்கியுடன்பெருக மெனவுரைத்தாள்."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi explains that things give room to one another as heated iron takes in water, and that the soul, embracing the father's seed and the mother's blood, settles within the body like ghee in plaster and grows along with it.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["karu","uyir","yaakkai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 310","lb":"NK310","ev":"","cl":[["the soul takes up the parents' seed and blood, settles within the body and grows with it","jain","asserted",2,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","மொக்கலன்"],"ch":"nk:15"},"nk:311":{"book":"neelakesi","num":310,"t":["செய்வினைதா னிற்பவே பயனெய்து மென்பதூஉ","மவ்வினை யறக்கெட்டா லதுவிளையு மென்பதூஉ","மிவ்விரண்டும் வேண்டுத லெமக்கில்லை யெடுத்துரைப்பி","னைவினையி னிலைதோற்ற நாசந்தா னாட்டுங்கால்."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi says the Jains need neither of the opponent's alternatives, that a deed bears fruit only while it stands or ripens only when it has wholly perished, because a deed, when explained, has persistence, arising and destruction at once.","n":"நிலை, தோற்றம், நாசம் is the Jain triad of persistence, origination and destruction. ஐவினை in line 4 is obscure (fine, subtle, or a division error).","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vinai","payan","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 311","lb":"NK311","ev":"","cl":[["a deed must yield its fruit either while it persists or after it has wholly perished","buddhist","refuted",1,"neelakesi"],["a deed, like every existent, has persistence, arising and destruction together","jain","asserted",4,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","மொக்கலன்"],"ch":"nk:15"},"nk:312":{"book":"neelakesi","num":311,"t":["பைம்பொன்செய் குடமழித்து;பன்மணிசேர் முடிசெய்தாற்","செம்பொன்னா னிலையுதலுஞ்சிதைவாக்க மவைபெறலு","நம்பான்றிங் கிவைபோலநரர்தேவ ருயிர்களையும்","வம்பென்று கருதனீவைகலும்யா முரையாமோ."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi gives the example of a gold pot broken and made into a jewelled crown, where the gold persists while one form is lost and another made, and says that men and gods are souls in the same way, so the opponent should not think it idle talk that the Jains repeat daily.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["uyir","piravi","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 312","lb":"NK312","ev":"","cl":[["as gold persists while the pot perishes and the crown arises, the soul persists through births as man or god","jain","asserted",1,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","மொக்கலன்"],"ch":"nk:15"},"nk:313":{"book":"neelakesi","num":312,"t":["கொன்ற பாவமுண் டாயின் குறட்கண்ணும்","ஒன்று மேயென் றுரைப்பனெப் பாரியார்","பொன்றினும் புத்த ரேநீவிர் சொல்லின","சென்று சேர்தலைச் சித்தம தின்மையால்."],"p":"","r":"In a shorter metre, Neelakesi tells the Buddhists that if killing is a sin, it reaches the eater too, and rejects their claim that sin goes and joins only where there is intention.","n":"The verse addresses the Buddhists in the plural (புத்தரே நீவிர்). குறட்கண்ணும் in line 1 is obscure, and the eater is supplied from the argument of 314 rather than named here.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kolai","pulaal","kollaamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 313","lb":"NK313","ev":"","cl":[["the sin of killing does not attach where there is no intention to kill","buddhist","refuted",3,"neelakesi"],["if killing is a sin, it reaches also the one who eats what was killed","jain","asserted",1,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","மொக்கலன்"],"ch":"nk:15"},"nk:314":{"book":"neelakesi","num":313,"t":["சொன்ன சூனைத் துறந்தவற் றட்டன","பின்னை யுண்டல் பிழைப்புடைத் தென்றியா","னன்னு தல்லைத் துறந்தவ ளட்டது","தன்னை யுண்டுந் தவசியை யல்லையோ"],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi takes up the opponent's rule about eating, after renunciation, what others have cooked, and asks whether he does not count himself an ascetic while eating what a woman he has renounced has cooked.","n":"சூனை (slaughter, or the place of killing and cooking) and the construction of lines 1 and 2 are uncertain; the argument appears to be that the Buddhist ascetic eats meat cooked by householders and denies any share in the killing.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pulaal","thuravu","kolai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 314","lb":"NK314","ev":"","cl":[["a renouncer who eats what others have prepared does not share the fault of its preparing","buddhist","refuted",1,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","மொக்கலன்"],"ch":"nk:15"},"nk:315":{"book":"neelakesi","num":314,"t":["கொன்ற பாவங் கெடுகெனக் கையிட்டு","நின்ற தென்பது நீயுரைப் பாயெனி","னன்று துன்னின தாதன்மை யாற்சொன்னாய்","சென்றும் வந்துந் தியானம் புகலென்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi says that if the opponent claims the sin of killing is undone by raising the hand and saying 'let it perish', he says it as a fool, and tells him to go back and forth into his meditation.","n":"The last line is sarcasm about Buddhist meditation as a cure for a killing that has already happened. Whether the gesture is a real Buddhist rite or the debater's caricature the verse does not show.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kolai","piraayachchiththam","thiyaanam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 315","lb":"NK315","ev":"","cl":[["the sin of killing is removed by raising the hand and declaring it gone","buddhist","refuted",1,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","மொக்கலன்"],"ch":"nk:15"},"nk:316":{"book":"neelakesi","num":315,"t":["இன்ப துன்ப மிருவினைக் காரிய","மென்ப வர்க்கென்னை யேதமுண் டென்றியேற்","பின்பு பேணுந் தவத்தினி னாகிய","துன்ப வர்க்குந் துதாங்கனத் தொன்றுமே."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi takes up the question what fault there is in holding that pleasure and pain are the effects of the two kinds of deeds, and answers that on that view the suffering of the penance one later undertakes falls under the same rule.","n":"The verse can be read either as the Buddhist objection that penance's pain is only old karma ripening, which would make penance pointless, or as Neelakesi turning that objection; line 4 (துதாங்கனத் தொன்றுமே) is corrupt and the answer is not given here.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vinai","thavam","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 316","lb":"NK316","ev":"","cl":[["pleasure and pain are the effects of good and bad deeds","jain","reported",1,"neelakesi"],["on that view the pain of penance undertaken later is itself only the fruit of deeds","buddhist","reported",3,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","மொக்கலன்"],"ch":"nk:15"},"nk:317":{"book":"neelakesi","num":316,"t":["செய்த தீவினை சென்றின்ப மாக்குமென்","றிஃது ரைப்பவ ரீங்கில்லை யாயினும்","பொய்கள் சொல்லிப் புலைமக னேயெம்மை","வைதல் காரண மாநின்று வைதியோ."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi tells Mokkalan that no one among her people says an evil deed once done turns into pleasure, and asks whether, base fellow, he reviles them with lies only to have a reason for reviling.","n":"The verse answers a charge, apparently that the Jains' bodily hardship is itself an evil deed they expect to ripen as bliss; she denies anyone holds it and calls the charge slander (வைதல்). The charge itself is known only from her denial.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","nindhai","vaadhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 317","lb":"NK317","ev":"","cl":[["no Jain holds that an evil deed once done turns into pleasure","jain","asserted",1,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","மொக்கலன்"],"ch":"nk:16"},"nk:318":{"book":"neelakesi","num":317,"t":["இந்திரி யங்களை வென்றற் பொருட்டென","வந்து டம்பு வருத்தல் பழுதென்பாய்","தந்து ரைத்த தலைமழி யாதிய","சிந்த னைக்கிவை செய்வதெ னோசொல்லாய்."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi turns on Mokkalan's view that tormenting the body to conquer the senses is a fault: then why, she asks, do his own people practise the shaving of the head and the other rules they themselves laid down?","n":"The answer is a tu quoque, not a defence of austerity on its merits: the Buddhist rejection of self-mortification is met by pointing to the monk's shaven head (தலைமழி).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thavam","yaakkai","vesham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 318","lb":"NK318","ev":"","cl":[["tormenting the body in order to conquer the senses is a fault","buddhist","refuted",2,"neelakesi"],["the Buddhists' own tonsure and rules are bodily discipline of the same kind","jain","asserted",3,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","மொக்கலன்"],"ch":"nk:16"},"nk:319":{"book":"neelakesi","num":318,"t":["புனைவு வேண்டலர் போக நுகர்விலர்","நினைவிற் கேயிடை கோளென நேர்தலா","லினைய வும்மல மேறினு மென்செய","மனைய தான்மக்கள் யாக்கையின் வண்ணமே."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi says the Jain ascetics want no adornment and enjoy no pleasures because they accept these as hindrances to meditation, so if dirt gathers on them it does no harm: that is simply what a human body is like.","n":"The verse answers the stock mockery of the unwashed Jain ascetic (மலம் ஏறினும்); it does not praise dirt but makes the body's filth a fact not worth correcting. Line 4's மனையதான் is hard to divide.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","thuravu","thavam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 319","lb":"NK319","ev":"","cl":[["adornment and enjoyment hinder meditation, so an ascetic need not clean away the dirt of the body","jain","asserted",2,"neelakesi"],["filth belongs to the nature of the human body","jain","asserted",4,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","மொக்கலன்"],"ch":"nk:16"},"nk:320":{"book":"neelakesi","num":319,"t":["பாவந் துய்த்துமென் றோமல்ல துய்ப்பினு","மாவ தின்மைக் கரசுரைத் தாயன்றோ","வோவ லின்பந் தருமெ னுயிரென்பாய்","தேவ னாகித் திரிந்துதான் காட்டிக்காண்."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi says her side never claimed to enjoy sin, that Mokkalan has proclaimed that undergoing hardship gains nothing, and that if he says giving it up brings bliss to the soul, he should become a god, go about, and show it.","n":"The answer is a challenge to show the promised bliss rather than an argument, so the claim is marked reported. ஓவல் (ceasing, giving up) may mean giving up austerity or the final cessation; the reading turns on it.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vinai","thavam","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 320","lb":"NK320","ev":"","cl":[["undergoing bodily hardship yields nothing, and leaving it brings the soul bliss","buddhist","reported",2,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","மொக்கலன்"],"ch":"nk:16"},"nk:321":{"book":"neelakesi","num":320,"t":["அழிவு காலத் தறத்தொடர்ப் பாடெலா","மொழியல் வேண்டுமென் றொற்றுமை தாங்கொளீஇ","வழியுங் காட்டுமம் மாண்புடை யார்கண்மேற்","பழியிங் கிட்டுரைத் தாற்பய னென்னையோ."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi asks what Mokkalan gains by heaping blame on the noble ones who teach that at the time of dying even every tie to dharma must be given up, and who show that path.","n":"The verse seems to defend the Jain practice of a chosen, renouncing death, which the opponent blames; it names no fasting, so vadakkiruththal is not tagged. ஒற்றுமை தாங்கொளீஇ in line 2 is not clear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thuravu","pattru","saakkaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 321","lb":"NK321","ev":"","cl":[["at the time of death every bond, even the bond to dharma, must be let go","jain","asserted",1,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","மொக்கலன்"],"ch":"nk:16"},"nk:322":{"book":"neelakesi","num":321,"t":["சிந்த னையினுந் தீவினை யாமென்பார்க்","கைந்திற் காம மமையுமென் றீரென்பாய்","சுந்த மாகச் சுவடறு வீரென","வந்தி தோறும் புடைக்க வமையுமோ."],"p":"","r":"The verse quotes Mokkalan as saying that the Jains, who hold that evil comes even from thought, allow desire through the five senses, and answers with a question whether one may then strike at every turn, saying the victim will be wiped out without trace.","n":"The sense is unsure: சிந்தனையினும் தீவினை ஆம் may describe the Buddhist teaching that the deed lies in intention rather than the Jain view, and சுந்தமாக and வந்தி தோறும் in lines 3-4 are obscure, so no claim is recorded.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vinai","kaamam","manam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 322","lb":"NK322","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","மொக்கலன்"],"ch":"nk:16"},"nk:323":{"book":"neelakesi","num":322,"t":["பெண்ம கள்ளிர் பிறகிட வுண்பவர்","கண்ணி னாலில்லுட் கந்தியைக் காணினு","முண்ண லம்மெனும் மோத்துடை யார்களைத்","திண்ண தாவைது தீவினை கோடியோ."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi asks whether, by firmly reviling those whose scripture says they will not eat if they so much as see a கந்தி within the house, though they eat what the women of the house set before them, Mokkalan is not taking on evil karma.","n":"கந்தி is read here as a woman ascetic, as in Jain usage, but that and the phrase பிறகிட in line 1 are not certain; the rule of alms described cannot be fixed from the verse alone.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["nindhai","vinai","ozhukkam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 323","lb":"NK323","ev":"","cl":[["reviling ascetics who keep a strict rule of alms brings evil karma on the reviler","jain","asserted",4,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","மொக்கலன்"],"ch":"nk:16"},"nk:324":{"book":"neelakesi","num":323,"t":["பிள்ளை பெண்ணலி யாயினும் மாண்வயிற்","றுள்ள தேயென் றொழுக்கங் கொடுத்தியாற்","பிள்ளை பெண்ணலி யன்மையை யாதினா","லுள்ளங் கொண்டிழ வூசி யுரைப்பதே."],"p":"","r":"The verse takes up a rule of conduct that turns on whether a child is female or neuter, apparently in the womb, and asks by what means one could know that it is not, setting this against Mokkalan's teaching.","n":"The verse is too obscure to construe with confidence (ஆண்வயிற்று, இழவூசி); which rule is meant and whose it is are not clear, so no claim is recorded.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["ozhukkam","samayam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 324","lb":"NK324","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","மொக்கலன்"],"ch":"nk:16"},"nk:325":{"book":"neelakesi","num":324,"t":["மோனம் பொய்யஞ்சிக் கொண்டவன் மெய்யுரைக்","கூனந் தோன்றி லுரைத்தன னென்றியேற்","றானம் யாவர்க்குஞ் செய்வது நன்றனென்பா","யீன மென்னோ தெருச்சுமக் கிற்றியோ."],"p":"","r":"The verse turns to the vow of silence: if Mokkalan objects that one who took silence for fear of lying spoke when truth was about to be harmed, Neelakesi answers that he himself says giving to everyone is good, and asks what shame there is in that, taunting him whether he will carry the street too.","n":"The connection between the Jain vow of silence (மோனம்) and the Buddhist praise of giving to all is not clear; the taunt தெருச் சுமக்கிற்றியோ is obscure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["mounam","vaaimai","eegai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 325","lb":"NK325","ev":"","cl":[["giving to everyone alike is good","buddhist","reported",3,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","மொக்கலன்"],"ch":"nk:16"},"nk:326":{"book":"neelakesi","num":325,"t":["உய்யக் கொள்வ னெனச் சொல்லி யுள்ளத்தாற்","கையிற் காட்டல் கரவுள தாமெனிற்","பொய்சி தைத்ததென் சொல்லிப் பெயர்ந்துரை","பொய்யு ரைத்தில னென்றல் பொருந்துமோ."],"p":"","r":"Continuing on the silent ascetic, the verse asks whether a sign of the hand that says 'I will save you', made from the heart without speech, is concealment, and challenges Mokkalan to say what lie it broke, or whether it is fitting to say no lie was told.","n":"Who makes the gesture is not certain: it may be the silent Jain ascetic answering by signs, or an image of the Buddha with the hand raised in protection. The edition's line 4 needed repair.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["mounam","vaaimai","karavu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 326","lb":"NK326","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","மொக்கலன்"],"ch":"nk:16"},"nk:327":{"book":"neelakesi","num":326,"t":["கொல்வினை யஞ்சிப் புலால் குற்ற மென்பதை","நல்வினை யேயென நாட்டலு மாமென்னை","வில்லினை யேற்றிநும் மெய்ம்மை கொளீஇயது","சொல்லினை யாதலிற் சொல்வன் யானே."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi announces that she can establish, as a good deed, the teaching that eating flesh is a fault because one fears the deed of killing, and since Mokkalan has drawn his bow to have his truth accepted, she will now speak.","n":"The verse opens the argument on flesh that runs to the end of the chapter; the bow (வில்லினை ஏற்றி) is the debate itself.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pulaal","kollaamai","vinai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 327","lb":"NK327","ev":"","cl":[["refusing flesh out of fear of the deed of killing is a good deed","jain","asserted",1,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","மொக்கலன்"],"ch":"nk:16"},"nk:328":{"book":"neelakesi","num":327,"t":["புத்தர்கட் பத்தியிற் போதி மரந்தொழிற்","புத்தர்கட் பத்தரை யேதொழு புத்தர்கட்","பத்தியை யாக்கு மதுவெனிற் (பற்றிய)","பத்தங் குடைசெருப் புந்தொழு பாவீ."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi argues that if the Buddhists worship the bodhi tree out of devotion to the Buddhas, they should rather worship the Buddhas' devotees, and if they say the tree breeds devotion to the Buddhas, then the sinner should worship their bowl, umbrella and sandals too.","n":"The reductio makes any object associated with the Buddhas equally worthy, so tree-worship proves too much. It also prepares the argument of 330-331: if an indirect act toward the Buddha gains merit, an indirect part in killing gains sin. பத்தம் in line 4 is read as the bowl; பற்றிய is the edition's bracketed supply.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","paththi","puththan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 328","lb":"NK328","ev":"","cl":[["worshipping the bodhi tree is right because it breeds devotion to the Buddhas","buddhist","refuted",3,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","மொக்கலன்"],"ch":"nk:16"},"nk:329":{"book":"neelakesi","num":328,"t":["ஆங்கவர் போல வருள்செய் பவர்களை","நீங்குமி னென்பது நீர்மை யெனினது","வீங்கிதற் கெய்தா விடினிலை போதிக்கும்","தீங்கே நுமர்செய்கை தேரமற் றென்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi says that if Mokkalan holds it right to tell people to leave those who show grace as the Buddhas did, then what his people do teaches only harm, and with that she ends this point.","n":"The verse ends with என்றாள், closing a turn of the debate. Lines 2-3 (நீங்குமின், வீங்கிதற் கெய்தா விடின்) do not yield a clear argument; தேரமற்று may hold the address தேர (elder), the title of a Buddhist monk.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["arul","samayam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 329","lb":"NK329","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","மொக்கலன்"],"ch":"nk:16"},"nk:330":{"book":"neelakesi","num":329,"t":["பல்லுடை யான்றன்னைப் பண்டுகண் டேத்தினுந்","தொல்லுரை கேட்டுறுப் பேதொழு தாலும்பி","னல்வினை யாமென்று நாட்டுதி யாய்விடிற்","கொல்வதுந் தின்பதுங் குற்றமற் றென்னாய்."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi argues that if Mokkalan establishes that praising, long ago, the one who left a tooth, or worshipping a bodily relic after hearing old stories, becomes a good deed afterwards, then he cannot say that killing and eating are without fault.","n":"பல்லுடையான் is read as the one whose tooth is kept, a tooth relic; உறுப்பே தொழுதாலும் supports a relic cult. The argument is by parity: the Buddhist cannot accept merit at a distance and deny sin at a distance.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","kolai","pulaal","vinai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 330","lb":"NK330","ev":"","cl":[["worshipping a relic of the Buddha's body, on hearing his story, becomes merit later","buddhist","refuted",2,"neelakesi"],["if acts toward a body that one did not make bear fruit, killing and eating also bear fruit","jain","asserted",4,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","மொக்கலன்"],"ch":"nk:16"},"nk:331":{"book":"neelakesi","num":330,"t":["ஏத்தின ரேத்துக வென்றிறை போல்வன","பாத்தில பைம்பொற் படிமைசெய் தாலவை","யேத்துநர் பெய்தவ ரெய்துவ நன்றெனில்","வீத்தவர் தின்பவர் வெவ்வினைப் பட்டார்."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi argues that if, when someone makes golden images like the lord so that worshippers may praise him, both the worshippers and the makers gain good, then those who kill and those who eat both fall into cruel karma.","n":"The Buddhist premise is granted, not answered, and turned into the Jain conclusion: merit shared between maker and worshipper means sin shared between killer and eater.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","pulaal","kolai","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 331","lb":"NK331","ev":"","cl":[["making images of the Buddha earns merit for both the makers and the worshippers","buddhist","reported",3,"neelakesi"],["the killer and the eater both incur the cruel deed","jain","asserted",4,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","மொக்கலன்"],"ch":"nk:16"},"nk:332":{"book":"neelakesi","num":331,"t":["வெற்றுடம் புண்பதும் வேலின் விளிந்தவை","தெற்றென வுண்பதுந் தீமை தருமென்னை","யொற்றைநின் றாடுணை யூறு படுத்தவட்","குற்றமன் றோசென்று கூடுவ தேடா."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi says that eating the body of a creature that simply died and eating what was killed by the spear both bring evil, and compares it to going to a woman left alone after her companion was struck down: is not that union a fault, fellow?","n":"The simile seems to say that one who did not do the harm still joins in its gain and so shares the fault; who struck the companion is not said, so the reading of line 3 is tentative.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pulaal","vinai","kutram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 332","lb":"NK332","ev":"","cl":[["eating a creature that died of itself and eating one killed by another both bring evil","jain","asserted",1,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","மொக்கலன்"],"ch":"nk:16"},"nk:333":{"book":"neelakesi","num":332,"t":["பிடிப்பது பீலி பிறவுயி ரோம்பி","முடிப்ப தருளது போன்முடை தின்று","கடிப்ப தெலும்பதன் காரண மேனி","தடிப்பத லாலரு டானுனக் குண்டோ."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi says that the Jains carry the peacock feather and guard other lives, and what they fulfil is compassion, whereas Mokkalan eats stinking flesh and gnaws bones so that his body grows fat, and asks whether, apart from that, he has any compassion.","n":"The peacock feather (பீலி) is the Jain ascetic's whisk for brushing small creatures from his path; the contrast is between an ascetic's tool for sparing lives and a monk who eats lives.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arul","pulaal","yaakkai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 333","lb":"NK333","ev":"","cl":[["one who eats flesh to fatten his body has no compassion","jain","asserted",4,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","மொக்கலன்"],"ch":"nk:16"},"nk:334":{"book":"neelakesi","num":333,"t":["ஆட்டொரு கான்மயிற் பீலி யுகமவை","ஈட்டுதல் போலுதிர்ந் துக்க விறைச்சியைக்","காட்டியுந் தின்னுங் கருத்திலை நீ தசை","வேட்டுநின் றேயழைத் தீவினை யாளோ."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi says that as peacock feathers drop at a shake and are gathered, so Mokkalan has no thought of eating only flesh that has fallen of itself: he stands craving meat and calls for it, doer of evil.","n":"The verse meets an implied counter, that the Jains too use what comes from animals: their feathers are shed, while the monk's meat is sought.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pulaal","avaa","vinai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 334","lb":"NK334","ev":"","cl":[["the Jain ascetic's feathers are gathered after they fall, harming no bird, which is unlike eating flesh","jain","asserted",1,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","மொக்கலன்"],"ch":"nk:16"},"nk:335":{"book":"neelakesi","num":334,"t":["மானொடு மீனில மன்னு முடம்பட","லூனடு வாரிடு வாரை யொளித்தலிற்","றானடை யாவினை யாமென்ற றத்துவந்","தீனிடை நீபட்ட தீச்செய்கை யென்னோ."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi asks what evil deed Mokkalan has fallen into, in saying that because those who kill and cook deer, fish and other creatures and those who give the meat are hidden from him, the deed of killing does not reach him.","n":"This is a Jain's account of the Buddhist rule that meat is allowed when the monk did not see, hear or suspect that it was killed for him; the verse states only the hiding of killer and giver.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pulaal","vinai","kolai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 335","lb":"NK335","ev":"","cl":[["the deed of killing does not reach one who eats meat when the killer and the giver are hidden from him","buddhist","refuted",3,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","மொக்கலன்"],"ch":"nk:16"},"nk:336":{"book":"neelakesi","num":335,"t":["குறிக்கப் படாமையிற் கொல்வினை கூடான்","பறித்துத் தின்பானெனிற் பாவமாம் பூப்போற்","செறிக்கப் படுமுயிர் தீவினை பின்னு","நெறிக்கட் சென்றாறலைப் பாரொப்ப னேர்நீ."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi says that if the monk claims he does not share in the killing because the animal was not marked out for him, he who plucks it up and eats still sins, and he stands level with highway robbers who go out on the road and waylay travellers.","n":"பூப்போல் செறிக்கப்படும் உயிர் in line 3, lives crowded like flowers, may mean the many small lives harmed with the meat; the line is not clear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pulaal","kolai","vinai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 336","lb":"NK336","ev":"","cl":[["a monk does not share in a killing when the animal was not marked out for him","buddhist","refuted",1,"neelakesi"],["the eater of flesh is the equal of the robber on the road","jain","asserted",4,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","மொக்கலன்"],"ch":"nk:16"},"nk:337":{"book":"neelakesi","num":336,"t":["விலையறம் போலு மெனின்வினை யாக்க","நிலையுமீ றென்பது நேர்குவை யாயின்","வலையினின் வாழ்நர்க்கும் வைகலு மீந்தாற்","கொலையென்றும் வேண்டலன் றோகுண மில்லாய்."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi argues that if Mokkalan says paying a price for meat is like a fair act, then by giving daily to those who live by the net he is always wanting killing, man without virtue.","n":"Line 2 (வினையாக்க நிலையுமீறு, the growth, stay and end of karma) seems to name a Buddhist concession that the argument uses, but it is not clear how.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pulaal","kolai","kollaamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 337","lb":"NK337","ev":"","cl":[["paying a price for meat is a fair transaction and brings no sin","buddhist","refuted",1,"neelakesi"],["the buyer who pays the fisherman every day wants the killing","jain","asserted",3,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","மொக்கலன்"],"ch":"nk:16"},"nk:338":{"book":"neelakesi","num":337,"t":["நும்பள்ளிக் கீபொரு ளாலுணர் வில்லவ","ரெம்பள்ளி தாஞ்சென் றெடுப்ப வெனினது","கம்பலை யாம்வினை யில்கறிக் கீபொருள்","செம்பக லேகொலை யாளரிற் சேரும்."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi argues that if, with money given to the Buddhist monastery, people without understanding go and fetch meat, that is a scandal, and money given for a meat curry joins its giver to the killers in broad daylight.","n":"எம்பள்ளி in line 2 (our monastery) is odd in her mouth and may be the Buddhists' words quoted.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pulaal","kolai","eegai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 338","lb":"NK338","ev":"","cl":[["money given to buy meat makes the giver one of the killers","jain","asserted",3,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","மொக்கலன்"],"ch":"nk:16"},"nk:339":{"book":"neelakesi","num":338,"t":["நாவின்கண் வைத்த தசைபய னேயென","வேவினை நீயுமற் றின்பமஃ தாதலிற்","றேவன்கண் வைத்த சிரத்தை செயலன்று","தூவென வெவ்வினை யைத்துடைத் தாயால்."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi charges that Mokkalan holds the flesh set on the tongue to be the real gain, since its pleasure is his, and that, not by faith placed in his god, he wipes away the cruel deed by calling the meat pure.","n":"The claim is the Jain's version of the Buddhist rule of permitted, 'pure' meat, and the school would not put it as a wiping-away of guilt. வேவினை and சிரத்தை செயலன்று are hard to fit together.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pulaal","thooimai","vinai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 339","lb":"NK339","ev":"","cl":[["calling meat pure removes the deed of eating it","buddhist","refuted",4,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","மொக்கலன்"],"ch":"nk:16"},"nk:340":{"book":"neelakesi","num":339,"t":["கன்றிய காமந்துய்ப் பான்முறைக் கன்னியை","யென்றுகொ லெய்துவ தோவெனுஞ் சிந்தையன்","முன்றினப் பட்ட முயன்முத லாயின","நின்றன வுந்தின நேர்ந்தனை நீயே."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi says that, like a man of ripened lust who keeps thinking when he will get the maiden of his kin, Mokkalan has agreed to eat the hare and the other creatures already eaten, and those still living too.","n":"The simile makes craving for meat like lust that cannot wait. முன்தினப்பட்ட முயல் may only mean animals usually eaten, or may glance at the birth story of the hare that gave itself as food; the verse does not decide it.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pulaal","avaa","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 340","lb":"NK340","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","மொக்கலன்"],"ch":"nk:16"},"nk:341":{"book":"neelakesi","num":340,"t":["தூய்மையி லாமுடை சுக்கில சோணித","மாமது போன்மெனி னான்முலைப் பாலன்ன","தூய்மைய தன்றது சொல்லுவன் சோர்வில","வாம னுரைவையந் தன்னொடு மாறே."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi answers the comparison of meat with the four-teated cow's milk: flesh, made of stinking semen and blood, is not pure like milk, she will say it without fault, and the word of Vaaman is at odds with the world itself.","n":"வாமன் is taken as the Buddha, as the opponent's teacher; the name is also used of the Jina elsewhere, so the identification rests on the context. The verse assumes, as the world does, that milk is pure food and flesh is not.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pulaal","thooimai","yaakkai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 341","lb":"NK341","ev":"","cl":[["meat is no more impure than cow's milk","buddhist","refuted",2,"neelakesi"],["flesh, made of semen and blood, is impure as milk is not","jain","asserted",1,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","மொக்கலன்"],"ch":"nk:16"},"nk:342":{"book":"neelakesi","num":341,"t":["மேன்மக்க ணஞ்சொடு கள்வரைந் தாரது","போன்மக்க ளாரும் புலால்வரை யாரெனிற்","றான்மெய்க்க ணின்ற தவசிமற் றெங்குள","னூன்மெய்க்கொண் டுண்பவ னுன்னல தென்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi argues that the noble, who shun toddy as they shun poison, ought by the same reasoning to shun flesh, and asks where an ascetic firm in truth is to be found when the one who takes flesh and eats it is none other than Mokkalan.","n":"The argument works from a ban the Buddhists already keep: the monk renounces toddy, so the debater asks why the same judgement stops short of meat. The last line turns the general question into a charge against the elder himself.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pulaal","kal","thavam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 342","lb":"NK342","ev":"","cl":[["one who shuns intoxicating liquor as harmful must for the same reason shun flesh","jain","asserted",2,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","மொக்கலன்"],"ch":"nk:17"},"nk:343":{"book":"neelakesi","num":342,"t":["பார்ப்பனி யோத்துநின் னோத்தும் பயமெனி","னீப்பவுங் கொள்பவு நேர்து மவையவை","தூப்பெனு மில்லன வேசொல்லி நிற்குமோர்","கூர்ப்பினை நீயென்றுங் கோளிலை யென்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"She says that if the brahmins' scripture and his own are both claimed to bring benefit, she can grant what each tells one to give up and to take, but since both speak only of things that are not clean, he has never had a sharp judgement.","n":"The verse pairs the Buddhist scripture with the brahmins' (பார்ப்பனி ஓத்து) as alike in allowing what is unclean, which in context means flesh. The middle lines are hard to construe and the reading given is tentative.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["samayam","thooimai","arivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 343","lb":"NK343","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","மொக்கலன்"],"ch":"nk:17"},"nk:344":{"book":"neelakesi","num":343,"t":["தூவினி னுண்புழுத் துய்ப்பனென் னாமையிற்","றீவினை சேர்ந்திலன் றின்பவ னென்னினு","மோவெனு முன்விலை வாணிக ரென்றினர்","மேவினர் தாம்விலை யேவினை வேண்டார்."],"p":"","r":"She grants, for argument, his plea that the eater incurs no evil deed because he never thought of consuming the small creatures in the flesh, and answers that the sellers of meat want only the price, not the deed, so the killing is done for the buyer.","n":"This answers the Buddhist defence by intention: the eater did not will the death. The reply moves the question from the eater's mind to the market, where the merchant's motive is the price the eater pays. Line 3 (ஓவெனு முன்) is obscure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pulaal","vinai","kollaamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 344","lb":"NK344","ev":"","cl":[["one who eats flesh without intending harm to any creature incurs no evil deed","buddhist","refuted",2,"neelakesi"],["the seller kills for the buyer's price, so the eater's lack of intent does not free him from the killing","jain","asserted",4,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","மொக்கலன்"],"ch":"nk:17"},"nk:345":{"book":"neelakesi","num":344,"t":["அடங்கிய வம்பு பறித்தன் முதலா","வுடங்குசெய் தார்வினை யொட்டல ரென்பாய்","மடங்கினர் வாழ்க வெனுமாற் றார்போற்","கடஞ்சொல்லித் தின்பதிங் கியார்கட் டயாவோ."],"p":"","r":"She recalls his saying that those who join in acts such as pulling out an arrow already lodged in a beast are not touched by the deed, and asks who here shows compassion by eating while pleading such excuses.","n":"The example of the lodged arrow seems to stand for every share in a death that comes after the blow; the debater treats it as a loophole. Line 3 and the word கடம் in line 4 are unclear, so the sense of the comparison is uncertain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vinai","arul","pulaal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 345","lb":"NK345","ev":"","cl":[["those who only take part after the killing, as by drawing out the arrow, are not bound by its deed","buddhist","refuted",2,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","மொக்கலன்"],"ch":"nk:17"},"nk:346":{"book":"neelakesi","num":345,"t":["தின்னு மனமுடைப் பேயெய்துந் தீவினை","மன்னு மிகவுடைத் தாய்வினைப் பட்டில்லா","ளென்னு முரைபெரி தேற்கு மிகழ்ச்சி","தன்னை வினைப்பட நீசொல்லி னாயால்."],"p":"","r":"She turns his own teaching back on him: if, as he says, a demon with a mind set on eating earns a lasting evil deed, then by his own words the will to eat is what binds, and the one who eats is bound.","n":"The Buddhist emphasis on intention, used in 344 to excuse the eater, is here made to condemn him: the desire to eat is an intention too. The figure of the demon (பேய்) may glance at Neelakesi's own past as a flesh-taking demoness. Lines 2 and 3 do not construe clearly.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vinai","manam","pulaal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 346","lb":"NK346","ev":"","cl":[["a mind set on eating flesh is itself enough to earn evil deed","buddhist","reported",1,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","மொக்கலன்"],"ch":"nk:17"},"nk:347":{"book":"neelakesi","num":346,"t":["அறஞ் சொல்லக் கொள்ளு மறமென் றறிந்தாங்","கறஞ்சொல்லி னார்க்கற மாமென் றறியாய்","புறஞ்சொல்லி தன்று புலால்குற்ற மென்று","துறந்தொழிந் தாற்கொலை துன்னினர் யாரோ."],"p":"","r":"She says he knows that whatever is taught as dharma is taken up as dharma, yet does not see that teaching it earns dharma for the teacher; that flesh is a fault is no outsider's slander, and if all renounced it, who would go near killing?","n":"The verse joins two points: people do what their teachers call dharma, and if all gave up flesh the killing would end. Together they make the teacher, not only the buyer, answerable for the slaughter his permission sustains.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pulaal","kollaamai","aram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 347","lb":"NK347","ev":"","cl":[["if everyone renounced eating flesh, no one would kill for it","jain","asserted",4,"neelakesi"],["those who teach dharma gain dharma by the teaching","general","asserted",2,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","மொக்கலன்"],"ch":"nk:17"},"nk:348":{"book":"neelakesi","num":347,"t":["அறந்தலை நின்றாங் கருளொடு கூடித்","துரந்தனள் யானென்னுஞ் சொல்லு முடையாய்","மறங்கொண்டி துண்டென்னை மன்னுயிர்க் காமே","சிறந்ததுண் டோவிது சிந்தித்துக் காணாய்."],"p":"","r":"She reminds him that he has even her own word, that she, standing at the head of dharma and joined to grace, drove violence away; what then does holding to cruelty gain him, and is anything more precious than the lasting life of beings? Let him think and see.","n":"துரந்தனள் யான் is a woman speaking of herself, which suggests Neelakesi cites her own turning from the bloodshed of her shrine (9) as the example of a mind that gave up violence; this is a reading, not certain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["arul","kollaamai","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 348","lb":"NK348","ev":"","cl":[["nothing is more precious than the life of a living being","jain","asserted",4,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","மொக்கலன்"],"ch":"nk:17"},"nk:349":{"book":"neelakesi","num":348,"t":["பேயொப்ப நின்று பிணங்கிக்கண் டார்க்கெனு","மாயத்தி னூனுண்ண மன்னு மருமையி","னாயொப்பச் சீறி நறுநுத லாளொடு","காயக் கிலேசத்திற் கட்டுரைக் கின்றான்."],"p":"","r":"Finding that his defence of eating flesh can no longer stand, Mokkalan, snarling like a dog, turns to argue with the fair-browed woman against the mortification of the body.","n":"காயக் கிலேசம், torment of the body, names the Jain outer austerities; the debate shifts from meat to penance because the Buddhist now attacks rather than defends. The narrator's simile of the dog shows where the poem's sympathy lies.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vaadham","vekuli","thavam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 349","lb":"NK349","ev":"Worsted on eating flesh, Mokkalan angrily turns to attack the Jain practice of bodily mortification (காயக் கிலேசம்).","cl":[],"ag":["மொக்கலன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"nk:17"},"nk:350":{"book":"neelakesi","num":349,"t":["வெயிறெறவ் வுணங்கியும் வெள்ளிடைந் நனைந்துமூன்","டயிறலிற் பட்டினிகள் விட்டுமின்ன கட்டமாய்த்","துயிறுறந் திராப்பகற் றுன்பவெங் கடலினார்க்","கயிறெறுந் நெடுங்கணா யாவதில்லை யல்லதும்."],"p":"","r":"Mokkalan tells her that drying in the sun, soaking in the open, giving up food in fasts and forsaking sleep night and day in a hot sea of suffering brings no gain at all, O long-eyed one.","n":"The list is of the Jain outer austerities: exposure, fasting, wakefulness. The claim is answered in 354 to 358, where Neelakesi shows that the Buddhists' own ascetic practices and their praise of the bodhisattva's sufferings are hardships of the same kind.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thavam","yaakkai","idumbai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 350","lb":"NK350","ev":"","cl":[["hardships such as exposure, fasting and sleeplessness bring no benefit","buddhist","refuted",4,"mokkalan"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["மொக்கலன்","நீலகேசி"],"ch":"nk:17"},"nk:351":{"book":"neelakesi","num":350,"t":["காயம்வாட்டி யுய்த்தலிற் கண்டநன்மை யுண்டெனின்","தீயினாற் சுடுதலுந் தெற்றியேறி வீழ்தலு","நோயினாற் றிரங்கலுந் நோன்மையென்ன லாம்பிற","நீயனா யிதற்கினி நேமியென்று சொல்லென."],"p":"","r":"He argues that if some good were to be seen in wasting the body away, then burning in fire, throwing oneself from a height and wasting with disease could all be called penance, and bids her say why not.","n":"A reductio: the Jain must either say what separates his fasting from self-destruction or accept the absurd conclusion. The argument assumes that pain itself is what the Jain values, which the Jain reply in 358 and 359 does not grant.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thavam","yaakkai","saakkaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 351","lb":"NK351","ev":"","cl":[["if wasting the body were meritorious, burning oneself, leaping from a height or wasting with disease would count as penance","buddhist","refuted",1,"mokkalan"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["மொக்கலன்","நீலகேசி"],"ch":"nk:17"},"nk:352":{"book":"neelakesi","num":351,"t":["புண்ணினைத் தடிதலும் போழவாற்றி நிற்றலும்","கண்ணினைக் கழிகள்ளான் மிண்டிக்கொண்டு நீட்டலும்","விண்ணுயர் நெடுவரைவ் வீற்றுவீற்று வீழ்தலும்","அண்ணலார்தஞ் செய்கையு மாவதில்லை யல்லதும்."],"p":"","r":"He adds that cutting one's own wounds and enduring being split, forcing open the eyes with sticks, throwing oneself from a lofty mountain, and the practices of the 'lords' who do such things, are of no use.","n":"அண்ணலார் (the lords) is likely ironic, aimed at the ascetics who are honoured for such feats. The list mixes known ascetic exploits with plain self-harm to make the line between them vanish.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thavam","yaakkai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 352","lb":"NK352","ev":"","cl":[["self-wounding and other extreme mortifications are of no use","buddhist","refuted",4,"mokkalan"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["மொக்கலன்","நீலகேசி"],"ch":"nk:17"},"nk:353":{"book":"neelakesi","num":352,"t":["தூக்கடம்மை யாக்கலே தொல்லைநல் லறம்மெனின்","நாக்களைப் பறித்தலுந் நான்றுவீழ்ந்து பொன்றலுந்","தீக்கள் பாய்ந்துசாதலுந் தீயசெங் கழுவ்வின்மேன்","மேக்கினைக்கொண் டேறலு மேன்மையென்ன லாம்பிற."],"p":"","r":"He says that if tormenting oneself were the old and good dharma, then tearing out tongues, hanging oneself to die, leaping into fire to perish and climbing the cruel red stake would all be excellent.","n":"The closing image of the impaling stake (கழு) is the death of a criminal, so the argument ranks the ascetic with the condemned man. The verse ends the attack that 354 begins to answer.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thavam","yaakkai","saakkaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 353","lb":"NK353","ev":"","cl":[["if self-torment were dharma, self-mutilation, hanging, burning and impalement would be excellent","buddhist","refuted",1,"mokkalan"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["மொக்கலன்","நீலகேசி"],"ch":"nk:17"},"nk:354":{"book":"neelakesi","num":353,"t":["தானஞ்சீல மும்பொறை தக்கதாய வீரியம்","மூனமில் தியானமே யுணர்ச்சியோ டுபாயமும்","மானமில் லருளினைவ் வைத்தலேவ லிம்மையுஞ்","ஞானமீரைம் பாரமீதை நாடுங்கா லிவைகளும்."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi replies by naming what his own teacher laid down: giving, conduct, forbearance, fitting vigour, flawless meditation, understanding with skill in means, the firm resolve of compassion, strength and knowledge: these are the ten perfections.","n":"ஈரைம் பாரமீதை, the twice-five perfections, is the tenfold list in which skill in means, resolve, strength and knowledge follow the six of giving, conduct, forbearance, vigour, meditation and wisdom. Here the Jain debater gives the list fairly, since her point needs it to be the Buddhists' own.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["paaramithai","eegai","porai","thiyaanam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 354","lb":"NK354","ev":"","cl":[["the Buddha taught ten perfections, from giving and conduct to strength and knowledge","buddhist","reported",4,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","மொக்கலன்"],"ch":"nk:17"},"nk:355":{"book":"neelakesi","num":354,"t":["விருக்கமூலி யாகலும் வெள்ளிடை யுறைதலும்","மிருத்தனிற்ற லன்றியு மிட்டகூறை யெய்தலும்","மருக்கையின் மயானத்துட் சேக்கையும் மனைகளை","வருச்சியார் புகுதலும் மற்றவற்றொ டுண்டலும்."],"p":"","r":"She goes on with the ascetic rules his teacher gave: living at the foot of a tree, dwelling in the open, sitting and standing without lying down, wearing cast-off cloth, sleeping in the burning ground, entering houses in turn without passing any by, and eating with the rest.","n":"The items match the Buddhist ascetic observances (துதாங்கம், named in 356): the debater lists them to show that Buddhism too has practices of hardship. வருச்சியார் புகுதல் seems to mean going for alms without skipping houses; the last item is unclear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thuravu","thavam","undi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 355","lb":"NK355","ev":"","cl":[["the monk may take up ascetic rules such as living at a tree's foot, in the open and in the burning ground, and wearing cast-off cloth","buddhist","reported",1,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","மொக்கலன்"],"ch":"nk:17"},"nk:356":{"book":"neelakesi","num":355,"t":["அத்திட்டாடை கோடலும்மமையுமென்ன நீங்கலும்","பெற்றதன்னிற் சேக்கையும்பேர்த்துண்ணா தொழிதலும்","குற்றமென்னப் பிச்சையுங்குறித்துழிப் புகாதுதான்","றுற்றியுய்த்த றன்னொடுதுதாங்கென்றாத்தர் சொன்னவே."],"p":"","r":"The list ends with taking only three robes, stopping the meal at the word 'enough', sleeping on whatever place is given, not eating a second time, treating alms as a fault (?) and not going where one had planned: these, she says, are the ascetic practices the Buddha spoke.","n":"துதாங்கு is the Buddhist term for these voluntary austerities and ஆத்தர் (the trustworthy one) names the Buddha as their author, so the list is presented as his own word. The fourth line is badly corrupted.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thuravu","undi","thavam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 356","lb":"NK356","ev":"","cl":[["the Buddha prescribed the ascetic practices called dhutanga","buddhist","reported",4,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","மொக்கலன்"],"ch":"nk:17"},"nk:357":{"book":"neelakesi","num":356,"t":["பாரமீ துதாங்கொடு பற்பல கிலேசமும்","நேருமனையி லுண்மையா னீரும்வேண்டி னீரெனக்","கூரிமம் வெயில்பசி கூடலுங் கூடினாற்","சேர்தலில்லை நல்லறஞ் சிந்தையென்று செப்பலும்."],"p":"","r":"With the ten perfections and the ascetic practices come many hardships, she says, yet he declares that when sharp cold, sun and hunger come together, good dharma cannot join the mind.","n":"She sets the Buddhists' own hard practices beside their claim that hardship keeps dharma out of the mind: the claim is answered by their own rule-book. Line 2 does not construe cleanly.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["idumbai","thavam","paaramithai","pasi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 357","lb":"NK357","ev":"","cl":[["when cold, heat and hunger beset the body, the mind cannot hold to dharma","buddhist","refuted",4,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","மொக்கலன்"],"ch":"nk:17"},"nk:358":{"book":"neelakesi","num":357,"t":["அருந்தடிக ளீரவும் மறஞ்செய்வாளிற் போழவும்","வருந்தவான துள்ளநீ மாட்சிநன்று மென்றியா","லிருந்துநின்று நன்னெறிக் கிடைப்படாத சிந்தையாற்","பெருந்தவங்கள் செய்ந்நரைப் பேசுவாயோர் பேதையே."],"p":"","r":"She says he calls it glorious that the bodhisattva endured having slices of his flesh cut and his body split by the sword in the doing of dharma, yet with a mind that never stays on the good path he speaks against those who do great penance: a fool.","n":"The bodhisattva is not named; the cutting of flesh and splitting by the sword recall the birth stories of self-giving, which the debate attacks elsewhere (214, 243, 360). Here the stories are used only to show inconsistency: the Buddhist praises bodily suffering when his own hero bears it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thavam","yaakkai","pedhaimai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 358","lb":"NK358","ev":"","cl":[["one who praises the bodhisattva's endurance of cutting cannot consistently condemn the hardships of penance","jain","asserted",4,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","மொக்கலன்"],"ch":"nk:17"},"nk:359":{"book":"neelakesi","num":358,"t":["புத்தராகு மாண்பினா போதிகத்து வர்கட்காம்","பத்துமாய பாரமீதை பாரவட்ட மென்றலும்","சித்தராகு மாண்பினாற் சீலமும் வதங்களும்","மெத்துணையும் மாயிரம்மா மென்றுமியாமு மென்றனள்."],"p":"","r":"She says that as he holds the ten perfections to be the burden by which bodhisattvas become Buddhas, so the Jains too hold that conduct and vows are what make one a Siddha, the perfected one.","n":"பாரவட்டம் and the last line are obscure. The parallel is the point: each school has a discipline that leads to its perfected state, so the Buddhist cannot call the Jain discipline mere torment without saying the same of his own.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["paaramithai","ozhukkam","veedu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 359","lb":"NK359","ev":"","cl":[["the ten perfections are what bring bodhisattvas to buddhahood","buddhist","reported",2,"neelakesi"],["conduct and vows are the means by which one becomes a Siddha","jain","asserted",3,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","மொக்கலன்"],"ch":"nk:17"},"nk:360":{"book":"neelakesi","num":359,"t":["உடம்பினுள்ள பல்லுயிர் சாவவூனுண் மானுக்குத்","தடங்கொண்மா வரைமிசைத் தன்னையீத னன்மையேற்","படம்புனைந்த வர்கடாம் பலருமுண்ணு நீரினுள்","விடம்பெய்தாற்கு நன்றுகொல் வியாதியாளன் றீர்கென."],"p":"","r":"She asks: if it is good that the bodhisattva gave himself on the great mountain to a beast hungry for flesh, though the many lives within his body die, is it good also to poison the water that many drink, saying 'may the sick man be cured'?","n":"The birth story of the body given to a hungry beast is alluded to, not told (மா is any beast). The argument rests on the Jain view that a body teems with small lives, so one act of giving is many acts of killing; a Buddhist would weigh the gift by its intent, which the verse does not consider.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","kollaamai","yaakkai","uyir"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 360","lb":"NK360","ev":"","cl":[["giving one's body to be eaten kills the many lives within it, and is no better than poisoning common water to cure one man","jain","asserted",4,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","மொக்கலன்"],"ch":"nk:17"},"nk:361":{"book":"neelakesi","num":360,"t":["அல்லவர்கள் சாதலை அறிந்தனன் னவனெனில்","நல்லதில்லை நஞ்சினா லென்றுநாட்டு வாயெனி","னெல்லையில்ல பல்லுயிர் தன்கணுள்ள வெஞ்சலுங்","கொல்லவந்த வூன்களும் குற்றமென்ற வாறுகொல்."],"p":"","r":"If he answers that the giver knew the others would not die, or that poison is plainly not good, she asks how the countless lives in the giver's own body and the flesh that came to be killed are not then counted a fault.","n":"The verse anticipates two replies and turns both: whatever the giver knew, the deaths in his body follow from his act. Line 1 is hard to read and the first reply is uncertain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kollaamai","uyir","kutram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 361","lb":"NK361","ev":"","cl":[["a gift of the body that destroys the lives within it is a fault","jain","asserted",4,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","மொக்கலன்"],"ch":"nk:17"},"nk:362":{"book":"neelakesi","num":361,"t":["நீட்சி திரிவா மயிருகிர் காட்டினை","மாட்சியில் லாமயிர் மன்னுயி ருள்வழித்","தாட்க ணிமிருந் தலைநிமி ராவெழல்","காட்சி மரத்திற்குக் காறலை யெங்கம்."],"p":"","r":"Answering his example of hair and nails, which grow and change yet have no life, she says that hair grows only where the living soul abides in the body, and that the rising growth seen in a tree is of another kind.","n":"This opens the argument on whether plants have life, a Jain tenet (trees are one-sensed beings) that the Buddhist denies. The text was repaired at line 1, and lines 3 and 4 do not construe cleanly.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["uyir","vaadham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 362","lb":"NK362","ev":"","cl":[["hair and nails grow though they have no life, so growth does not prove that trees live","buddhist","refuted",1,"neelakesi"],["hair grows only while the soul dwells in the body","jain","asserted",2,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","மொக்கலன்"],"ch":"nk:17"},"nk:363":{"book":"neelakesi","num":362,"t":["மரங்கள் வளருமென மன்னுங் கூம்பி","விரிந்த விலையின் வேற்றுமை சொன்னாய்","பொருந்து மிவையு மல்லவு மன்றோ","வொருங்கிவ் வுலகத் துயிர்களு மென்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"You spoke of trees growing, folding up and differing in their spreading leaves, she says; these and the rest are found together in the living beings of this world as well.","n":"The argument is from shared marks: what grows, closes at night and puts out leaves of different shapes behaves as the animals do. It is an inference by likeness, which the Buddhist answers with lifeless things that change (365, 366).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["uyir","vaadham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 363","lb":"NK363","ev":"","cl":[["trees grow, fold and change as living beings do, and so they have life","jain","asserted",4,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","மொக்கலன்"],"ch":"nk:17"},"nk:364":{"book":"neelakesi","num":363,"t":["வயாத்திரு வாக்கி வளர்பூ சணிக்குத்","தயார்செய்கை தீதென்னுந் தத்துவங் கண்டா","யுயாப்பிழைத் தாய்மெழு கூனொடு பட்ட","வயாவதற் கீண்டுப் பயத்தலி லன்றே."],"p":"","r":"She takes up the 'craving' (வயா) of the growing pumpkin, a mark of life in plants, and tells him that he erred in his account of it, but the lines that give his account are obscure.","n":"வயா, the craving of pregnancy, is ascribed to plants to argue that they feel. What the Buddhist said about the pumpkin and the wax and flesh of line 3 cannot be made out.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["uyir","vaadham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 364","lb":"NK364","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","மொக்கலன்"],"ch":"nk:17"},"nk:365":{"book":"neelakesi","num":364,"t":["யாதினு மாழ்குமம் மாழ்கியு மென்றுழி","நீதின்னுந் தோலை நெருப்பொடு கூட்டத்தி","னோதினை தேறுற நீர்க்குரைத் தாய்மற்றுஞ்","சேதனை யில்லாய் திரிவென்னை வண்ணம்."],"p":"","r":"She recalls that when it was urged that plants wilt at every touch, he answered that the hide one eats also shrinks when brought to the fire, and she asks how the change of a thing without awareness can be the same.","n":"The hide example is answered again at 369, where the hide is said not to be like the living plant. சேதனை இல்லாய் may address Mokkalan as lacking sense, or describe the hide as lacking awareness.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["uyir","vaadham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 365","lb":"NK365","ev":"","cl":[["lifeless things such as hide also shrink and change, so wilting does not prove that plants live","buddhist","refuted",2,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","மொக்கலன்"],"ch":"nk:17"},"nk:366":{"book":"neelakesi","num":365,"t":["அரும்பு மலரு மரும்பிணி தீர்வு","மொருங்குதங் காரணத் தாக்க முணர்த்து","மரங்களு மன்னுயி ரெய்தின வென்ன","விரும்பொடு காந்த மியைவி றிரிவே."],"p":"","r":"She sets out his further reply: if trees are said to have life because their budding, flowering and power to cure grave disease show growth from their own cause, then the iron and the magnet drawing together would also be such a change.","n":"The magnet is the Buddhist counter-example to the Jain argument from plants' responsiveness. Her answer to it lies beyond this chapter, so the claim is left as reported here.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["uyir","vaadham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 366","lb":"NK366","ev":"","cl":[["a magnet moves iron without life, so change from a cause does not prove that plants live","buddhist","reported",4,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","மொக்கலன்"],"ch":"nk:17"},"nk:367":{"book":"neelakesi","num":366,"t":["ஒப்ப மரங்கட் குயிருண்மை யாமினி","யிப்படித் தோன்று மிருதுக்கள் சார்ந்தெனச்","செப்பிய வேதுத் திரிவெனக் காட்டிய","வெப்பங் குளிரவை தாமவை யேயால்."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi holds that trees have life by the same mark as we do, since they change with the seasons, and answers that the heat and cold Mokkalan offered to show her reason faulty work on us in just the same way.","n":"The verse picks up an exchange already under way: Mokkalan has called her reason (ஏது) fallacious by saying heat and cold, not life, make trees change; she turns the objection back on the human body.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["uyir","vaadhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 367","lb":"NK367","ev":"","cl":[["trees are living beings, as their changes with the seasons show","jain","asserted",1,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","மொக்கலன்"],"ch":"nk:18"},"nk:368":{"book":"neelakesi","num":367,"t":["மரங்களை யொப்புமை யாலுயி ரென்னக்","கிரந்தியும் வெப்பங் கிளக்குவை யாயி","னிரந்த வுடம்பின் விகார நினக்குப்","பரந்துண ருண்மையைப் பார்ப்படுத் தாயால்."],"p":"","r":"If Mokkalan rejects the analogy by pointing to knots and heat in trees as mere alterations, she says, he has thrown down the truth that the changes of an embodied body are felt.","n":"கிரந்தி is taken as a knot or swelling in the wood; the sense of the second half turns on that and on reading பார்ப்படுத்தாய் as 'cast to the ground'.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["uyir","yaakkai","vaadhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 368","lb":"NK368","ev":"","cl":[["swellings and heat in a tree are changes of a living body, as they are in ours","jain","asserted",2,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","மொக்கலன்"],"ch":"nk:18"},"nk:369":{"book":"neelakesi","num":368,"t":["வாட்டங்க ளுண்மையின் வாழ்மரஞ் சேர்ந்தவை","நாட்டிய வாதலி னல்லுயி ரோவெனக்","காட்டிய தோலொத் திராமையும் வாடுமத்","தோட்டஞ்செய் சேம்புயிர் தொன்முடி வன்றோ."],"p":"","r":"She meets the objection that withering does not prove life: a tortoise whose skin is unlike ours also withers, and the taro of the garden comes to its natural end, so withering belongs to the living.","n":"The tortoise clause (தோலொத்திராமையும்) is hard to divide; the reading that it is an animal unlike us which still withers is a guess.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["uyir","vaadhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 369","lb":"NK369","ev":"","cl":[["withering and coming to an end are marks of life in plants as in animals","jain","asserted",4,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","மொக்கலன்"],"ch":"nk:18"},"nk:370":{"book":"neelakesi","num":369,"t":["அற்ற வுடம்புக ளாறுத லான்மரந்","தெற்ற வுயிருண்மை செப்பத்தி னாமெனப்","பெற்ற பிழைசொல்லிப் பித்தெழுந் தாரொப்பக்","குற்ற மிவையெனக் கூறிதி யன்றோ."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi says that when the Jains argue trees have life because cut trees heal as cut bodies do, Mokkalan calls this an error and lists it among faults, like men risen in madness.","n":"She reports his charge of fault only to mock it; the comparison to the mad is aimed at him.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["uyir","yaakkai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 370","lb":"NK370","ev":"","cl":[["a tree that heals where it is cut shows life as a wounded body does","jain","asserted",1,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","மொக்கலன்"],"ch":"nk:18"},"nk:371":{"book":"neelakesi","num":370,"t":["காட்டின மண்ணை முதலா வுடையன","வோட்டி யுரைத்த வுயிரென வொட்டலர்","நாட்டினுள் வாழ்பவ ரின்னரென் றாவந்த","நாட்டை யவரென்ன நாட்டிய வாறே."],"p":"","r":"She compares his refusal to accept the souls her scripture names, the beings that have earth and the rest for body, to calling a land's people by the land's name, a confusion of the thing with what it lives in.","n":"The simile of the land and its dwellers is compressed and its direction is not certain; the claim follows the Jain doctrine that earth, water, fire, wind and plants are bodies of one-sensed souls, set out in the sermon (77).","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["uyir","vaadhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 371","lb":"NK371","ev":"","cl":[["earth and the other elements are the bodies of souls","jain","asserted",1,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","மொக்கலன்"],"ch":"nk:18"},"nk:372":{"book":"neelakesi","num":371,"t":["தாவர மாய மரமிவை தாமென","யாவருஞ் சொல்லுப வஃது மறிந்திலை","நீவி ரெவர்சொல்லச் சொல்லினீ ரென்றுநின்","சீவரம்போற் கட்டில் செப்புவ தென்னோ."],"p":"","r":"Everyone calls trees the fixed beings (தாவரம்), she says, and he does not know even that; why does he ask 'on whose word do you say it' as if tying his robe?","n":"The last line's jibe about the Buddhist robe (சீவரம்) and its knot is obscure; it seems to mock his demand for an authority.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["uyir","samayam","vaadhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 372","lb":"NK372","ev":"","cl":[["trees are among the fixed beings, as common usage itself says","jain","asserted",1,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","மொக்கலன்"],"ch":"nk:18"},"nk:373":{"book":"neelakesi","num":372,"t":["மக்களுட் டோன்றிய போழ்த மரவுயிர்க்","கொப்ப வுடம்பறி வன்றியொன் றில்லெனிற்","றக்கதன் றன்மையுடைப் போதி சத்துவன்","மிக்கதென் னோதிக்கு வேற்றுமை வேண்டார்."],"p":"","r":"If a human-born being had no more than a tree-soul's knowledge of the body, she asks, why would those who revere the Bodhisattva of that nature refuse to make a distinction where he is greater?","n":"The verse seems to turn the Buddhist's own Bodhisattva, who passed through many births, against his denial that trees have souls, but the syntax of lines 3 and 4 cannot be fixed.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["uyir","piravi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 373","lb":"NK373","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","மொக்கலன்"],"ch":"nk:18"},"nk:374":{"book":"neelakesi","num":373,"t":["நாண முடைய மரமுத லியாவையும்","மூணின வாழ்ந்துமுண் ணாவிடிற் சாதலைக்","காணவும் பட்டது கஞ்சியோ டல்லதை","யாண மிலாப்பொரு ளாட்சியர் போன்றே."],"p":"","r":"Trees and the rest, even the shy plant that shrinks, live by feeding and die when they are not fed, as plainly as stingy rich men live on gruel.","n":"நாணமுடைய மரம் is read as the plant that shrinks at a touch; the comparison in line 4 with misers who live on gruel is uncertain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["uyir","undi","saakkaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 374","lb":"NK374","ev":"","cl":[["plants take food and die without it, which only living things do","jain","asserted",2,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","மொக்கலன்"],"ch":"nk:18"},"nk:375":{"book":"neelakesi","num":374,"t":["மயக்குடை மாட்சியி னார்க்கு மரங்கட்கு மன்னுயிர்தாம்","பயப்பட வொக்கு மெனவே யெனமன்னும் பற்றிலனாய்","வியப்புடை யாகம மீதென நீயும் விரித்துரைக்கு","நயப்பிர மாணங்கண் மேற்குற்ற நாடுவன் யானெனவே."],"p":"","r":"Shaken in his hold on his view that trees and the deluded alike lack lasting souls, Mokkalan tells her to set out her scripture, and he will search out the faults in its nayas and its means of knowledge.","n":"The first two lines are hard; whatever their exact sense, the verse marks the debate's turn from the souls of plants to the Jain theory of knowledge.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vaadhu","alavai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 375","lb":"NK375","ev":"Mokkalan, unable to hold his ground on the souls of trees, challenges Neelakesi to lay out her scripture so that he can attack its nayas and pramanas.","cl":[],"ag":["மொக்கலன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"nk:18"},"nk:376":{"book":"neelakesi","num":375,"t":["நிற்றலுங் கேட்டினோ டுண்மையு மின்மையு நேர்தலினு","மொற்றுமை வேற்றுமை தம்மையு மொட்டப் படுதலினுங்","குற்ற மிவையிவை யாதலைக் கேளெனக் கூறினனே","முற்ற மவளது பக்க மறிதலில் மொக்கலனே."],"p":"","r":"Mokkalan tells her to hear the faults that follow from holding a thing both to stand and to perish, to be and not to be, to be one and to be different, the narrator noting that he did not know her side fully.","n":"The narrator's remark that he spoke without knowing all her position (முற்ற மவளது பக்க மறிதலில்) is the work's verdict in advance on the attack that follows.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vaadhu","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 376","lb":"NK376","ev":"Mokkalan announces the faults he will find in the Jain view of things as both lasting and perishing.","cl":[],"ag":["மொக்கலன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"nk:18"},"nk:377":{"book":"neelakesi","num":376,"t":["வேயொத்த தோளி நிலையுதல் வேண்டப் படுதலினாற்","காயத்தின் றன்மைய வாயெக் கருமமுங் காண்பரிதா","நாசத் தவமெனிற் றோன்றுவ தாமு நவநவமாம்","தோசத்த வாநின் பொருளெனக் கேட்டிது சொல்லினனே."],"p":"","r":"Mokkalan argues that if a thing must stay the same it can do no work, and if it perishes then what appears is new each time, so her thing is faulty either way.","n":"Neelakesi answers in 378 that these faults belong to one-sided views. Line 2 (காயத்தின் றன்மைய) is not clear in its detail.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","vaadhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 377","lb":"NK377","ev":"","cl":[["a thing that lasts cannot act, and a thing that perishes is replaced by a new one, so a thing cannot be both","buddhist","refuted",2,"mokkalan"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["மொக்கலன்","நீலகேசி"],"ch":"nk:18"},"nk:378":{"book":"neelakesi","num":377,"t":["நின்றன வேயென்று நில்லலவேயென்று நேர்பவர்க்கு","மொன்றென வேயும்பின் வேறெனவேயுந்தம் முண்மையின்கட்","சென்றன வேயென்றுஞ் செல்லலவேயென்றுஞ் செப்பினர்க்கே","அன்றென லாமோ வறைந்தபல்குற்ற மவையவையே."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi answers that the faults he listed cannot be charged against those who hold that things both stand and do not stand, are one and then different, have gone and have not gone, each in its own respect.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","vaadhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 378","lb":"NK378","ev":"","cl":[["a thing that both lasts and changes, and is one and different in different respects, escapes the faults of either one-sided view","jain","asserted",4,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","மொக்கலன்"],"ch":"nk:18"},"nk:379":{"book":"neelakesi","num":378,"t":["நின்ற குணங்களி னித்தியமென்று நிலையிலவா","மென்ற குணங்க ளநியத மென்று மியம்புதலாற்","சென்ற குணங்க ளிருமையு மல்ல தவற்றினிற்றீர்ந்","தொன்றங்கு நின்ற பொருளுள்ள தேலா தெனவுரைத்தான்."],"p":"","r":"Mokkalan says that since she calls the lasting qualities eternal and the passing ones non-eternal, a thing standing apart from both kinds of quality cannot be accepted.","n":"Neelakesi answers in 380 that no one held the substance to stand apart from its qualities.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["gunam","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 379","lb":"NK379","ev":"","cl":[["there is no substance standing apart from its qualities","buddhist","refuted",4,"mokkalan"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["மொக்கலன்","நீலகேசி"],"ch":"nk:18"},"nk:380":{"book":"neelakesi","num":379,"t":["கேடில வாய குணத்தி னிலையுங் கெடுங்குணத்தி","னீடில வென்பது நேர்ந்தினி யப்பொரு ணேர்தலில்லாய்","மூடலை யாவதன் காரண மென்னை முடிகுணத்திற்","கூடல தாய குணிப்பொருள் கூறினர் யாவரென்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi asks why, having granted that the lasting qualities endure and the perishing ones pass, he will not grant the substance, and who ever said that the substance bearing them was something not joined to them.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["gunam","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 380","lb":"NK380","ev":"","cl":[["the substance is the bearer of its lasting and passing qualities and is not apart from them","jain","asserted",4,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","மொக்கலன்"],"ch":"nk:18"},"nk:381":{"book":"neelakesi","num":380,"t":["குணங்களல் லாற்பொருள் வேறில்லை யாயிற் குறிப்பொருளாம்","பிணங்கல வாகிப் பிறபிற வாயிற பிறபொருளா","முணர்ந்தன தாமிகு சொல்லினு மொன்றெனி னொன்றவையா","நுணங்கிய கேள்வியி னாயொன் றுரையென நோக்கினனால்."],"p":"","r":"Mokkalan presses a dilemma: if there is no thing besides the qualities the thing is only a label; if the qualities are distinct, they are distinct things; if they are one, they are one; she must say which.","n":"Neelakesi turns the same dilemma back on him in 382 and 383.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["gunam","vaadhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 381","lb":"NK381","ev":"","cl":[["substance and quality must be either the same or different, and either answer undoes the Jain account","buddhist","refuted",1,"mokkalan"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["மொக்கலன்","நீலகேசி"],"ch":"nk:18"},"nk:382":{"book":"neelakesi","num":381,"t":["நிலையா தெனவு முயிரில்லையென்று நெறிமையினாற்","றொலையாத் துயரொடு தூய்தன்மையென்றின்ன தொக்குளவாக்","கலையா விழுப்பொருட் கந்தங்களைந்திற்கும் காட்டுதலான்","மலையா திதுநுங்கண் மார்க்கத்தொடென்றனள் மாணிழையே."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi replies that his own school lays impermanence, no-soul, endless suffering and impurity on all five aggregates, so her position does not clash with his path.","n":"தூய்தன்மை is read as தூய்து அன்மை, 'not pure', which makes the four the Buddhist marks the Manimekalai also lists (அநித்தம் துக்கம் அநான்மா அசுசி). Her point is that the Buddhist too ascribes qualities to a bearer.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kandham","nilaiyaamai","uyir"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 382","lb":"NK382","ev":"","cl":[["the five aggregates are impermanent, without self, suffering and impure","buddhist","reported",3,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","மொக்கலன்"],"ch":"nk:18"},"nk:383":{"book":"neelakesi","num":382,"t":["ஆரிய சத்தைய லாற்கந்தம்வேறில்லை யேற்குறியா","மாரிய சத்தையுங் கந்தமும்வேறெனின் வேறவையாம்","பேரிவை தாமிரண் டொன்றினுக்கேயெனி னொன்றவையாங்","கூரிய சிந்தையி னாயொன்றுசொல்லென்று கூறினளே."],"p":"","r":"She turns his dilemma on him: if there is no aggregate besides the noble truth it is a mere name, if they are different they are two things, and if both are names of one they are one; let him say which.","n":"ஆரிய சத்தை is read as the noble truth (ārya-satya); if it means 'noble existence' the retort is the same in form. The speaker is named by the verb கூறினளே.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kandham","vaadhu","meyyunarvu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 383","lb":"NK383","ev":"","cl":[["the Buddhist relation of the truths to the aggregates meets the same dilemma of identity and difference","jain","asserted",4,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","மொக்கலன்"],"ch":"nk:18"},"nk:384":{"book":"neelakesi","num":383,"t":["சொல்லலன் யானெனச் சொல்லுவையாயினுஞ் சொன்மலைவாம்","சொல்லல னென்ன வினவினுமென்னினுஞ் சொல்லிலையாம்","சொல்லுவ னல்லன் ஒருவகையாச் சொலினவ் வகையாற்","சொல்லிய குற்றங்க டுன்னுமெனவது சொல்லினனே."],"p":"","r":"Mokkalan argues that if she says she will not say, she contradicts herself in saying it, and if she says it one way the faults already named attach to her.","n":"The target is the Jain mode of the inexpressible; Neelakesi answers in 385. The second line's turns on சொல்லலன் are hard to follow exactly.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sol","vaadhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 384","lb":"NK384","ev":"","cl":[["to call a thing inexpressible is itself to express it, so the claim defeats itself","buddhist","refuted",1,"mokkalan"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["மொக்கலன்","நீலகேசி"],"ch":"nk:18"},"nk:385":{"book":"neelakesi","num":384,"t":["தன்மையி னன்மையுந் தன்னல்பொருள்களி னுண்மையுந்தம்","பன்மை யுடையவப் பண்புகளெல்லா முடனுரையுஞ்","சொன்மை யுணரா தவர்கட்குத்தான்சொலற் பாடின்மையாற்","புன்மை யுடைய புறத்தீருரைக்கு முரையுமென்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi answers that a thing is not in another's nature and is in its own, and that its many qualities cannot be stated at once; that is the only sense in which it cannot be spoken, which those who do not understand words miss.","n":"Line 4's 'outsiders' speech' (புறத்தீர் உரை) is not clear; it seems to say the charge is the outsiders' misunderstanding.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sol","gunam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 385","lb":"NK385","ev":"","cl":[["a thing exists in its own nature and not in another's, and is inexpressible only in that all its aspects cannot be said at once","jain","asserted",3,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","மொக்கலன்"],"ch":"nk:18"},"nk:386":{"book":"neelakesi","num":385,"t":["சேற்பொருள் போலரி சிந்தியகண்ணாய் சிதர்ந்துரைக்கு","நூற்பொரு டாம்பரி ணாமத்திரிவென நோக்குதியேற்","பாற்பொரு டான்றயி ராயபொழுதின்கட் பாழ்த்திலதேற்","பாற்பொரு ளேயின் றயிரெனச்சொல்லப் பழுததென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"Mokkalan objects that if her scripture means transformation, then when milk becomes curd and the milk has not perished, the curd ought still to be called milk, which is absurd.","n":"He addresses her by her eyes streaked red like a fish (சேல்). Neelakesi answers in 387.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["parinaamam","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 386","lb":"NK386","ev":"","cl":[["if milk persists in the curd, the curd should be called milk","buddhist","refuted",4,"mokkalan"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["மொக்கலன்","நீலகேசி"],"ch":"nk:18"},"nk:387":{"book":"neelakesi","num":386,"t":["உருவப் பிழம்பப் பொருளென்றுரைப்பனிப் பாறயிர்மோர்","பருவத்தி னாம்பரி யாயப்பெயரென்பன் பாலழிந்து","தருவித் துரைத்த தயிருருவாய்மும்மைத் தன்மையதாந்","திருவத்த தென்பொரு ளாதலைத்தேர தெளியிதென்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi says the substance is the mass of form, while milk, curd and buttermilk are names of its stages; the milk perishes and curd arises, so the thing has a threefold nature.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["parinaamam","nilaiyaamai","gunam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 387","lb":"NK387","ev":"","cl":[["a substance persists while its modes, like milk, curd and buttermilk, arise and perish, so it has the threefold nature of arising, perishing and lasting","jain","asserted",4,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","மொக்கலன்"],"ch":"nk:18"},"nk:388":{"book":"neelakesi","num":387,"t":["பெற்றது தானுங்கும் மாயத்திரிபு பயற்றியல்டே","யிற்ற திதுவென திட்டமென்பாயிவ் விரும்மையினுந்","தெற்றெனத் தீர்ந்தோர் பொருளென்னைதேற்றினித் தேற்றிலையேன்","மற்றது வாமை மயிரெனச்சொல்லுவன் மன்னுமென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"Mokkalan says she holds the change of gram into a cooked mash as her doctrine, and demands that she show plainly some one thing apart in both states, or he will call it a turtle's hair.","n":"'Turtle's hair' (ஆமை மயிர்) is the stock example of the non-existent. Line 1's பயற்றியல்டே is corrupt. Neelakesi answers in 389.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["parinaamam","vaadhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 388","lb":"NK388","ev":"","cl":[["no thing persisting through the change of gram into mash can be shown, so it does not exist","buddhist","refuted",3,"mokkalan"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["மொக்கலன்","நீலகேசி"],"ch":"nk:18"},"nk:389":{"book":"neelakesi","num":388,"t":["கெட்ட திரிட்சியுந் தோன்றியசாந்தும் பொருளெனவும்","பட்டன வப்பொருள் பையைகளேஎன்னும் பான்மையினால்","விட்ட திரள்வினுந் தோன்றியசாந்தினும் வேற்றுமையாம்","நட்டமுந் தோற்றமு நாட்டேனுருவிற்கு நானுமென்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi says the lump that perished and the paste that arose are both the thing in its modes, which differ from each other, and she too affirms loss and arising for the form.","n":"பையைகளேஎன்னும் in line 2 looks corrupt; it is read as naming the modes.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["parinaamam","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 389","lb":"NK389","ev":"","cl":[["loss and arising belong to the modes of a substance, which differ while the substance remains","jain","asserted",4,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","மொக்கலன்"],"ch":"nk:18"},"nk:390":{"book":"neelakesi","num":389,"t":["திரியும் பொருள்க டிரிந்தாம்பயறுகும் மாயமுமாய்","விரியும் மெனவது வேண்டுகின்றாயறக் கேட்டமைக்கேற்","கரியும் முடையன் பயறொடுநீருங் கலந்துபெய்தா","லெரியுறு கின்றதன் றேயிதுவோவொப்ப விற்றதென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"Mokkalan retorts that if she wants changing things to become gram and mash by change, then gram poured with charcoal and water should also burn, and asks whether that is the same.","n":"The verse is obscure and the answer falls in the next chapter, so the claim is marked reported here; the reading of கரியும் as charcoal is a guess.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["parinaamam","vaadhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 390","lb":"NK390","ev":"","cl":[["if the persisting material makes things the same, mixtures with other matter would share each other's nature","buddhist","reported",4,"mokkalan"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["மொக்கலன்","நீலகேசி"],"ch":"nk:18"},"nk:391":{"book":"neelakesi","num":390,"t":["பருமை யுடைய பயற்றின்வழியொன்று பாவியுண்டா","யருமை யுடையவந் நீருக்குமாவியன் றோவதன்றி","யிருமையுங் கெட்டுட னாயிற்கும்மாயமு மில்லற்கனும்","பெருமையி னாலொன்று பெற்றொன்றுபேறின்மை பேதைமையே."],"p":"","r":"Answering the cooked-pulse example, Neelakesi says the gross pulse changes in its way and the water too passes off as vapour, so that when both are spent together into the mash it is folly to grant a change to one and deny it to the other.","n":"The verse replies to 390, where the Buddhist mocks pulse and water boiled together into கும்மாயம்; the example is the stock Jain one for a substance that persists through change. Line 1 (பாவியுண்டா) is hard to construe, and the speaker is inferred from the alternation of என்றான் and என்றாள்.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["gunam","vaadhu","pedhaimai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 391","lb":"NK391","ev":"","cl":[["when pulse and water are cooked into one dish both undergo change, so neither alone can be said to persist unchanged","jain","asserted",4,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","மொக்கலன்"],"ch":"nk:19"},"nk:392":{"book":"neelakesi","num":391,"t":["கெடுவன தோன்றுவ நிற்பன தாமுங் குணமென்றியேற்","கெடுவன தோன்றுவ நிற்பன தாங்குண மாயினற்காற்","கெடுவது தோற்ற நிலையுத றானப் பொருளெனவும்","படுவஃ தாக வுரைப்ப தியாதின் பவத்ததென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"The Buddhist objects that if perishing, arising and persisting are qualities, then the thing that perishes, arises and persists would have to be some further substance, and asks what that could be.","n":"He takes the Jain triad of origination, persistence and destruction and tries to force it into a split between qualities and a bearer that is never found, which suits his own view that there is no lasting substance behind momentary events.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["gunam","vaadhu","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 392","lb":"NK392","ev":"","cl":[["if arising, persisting and perishing are qualities, the substance that bears them must be something else that cannot be named","buddhist","refuted",3,"mokkalan"]],"ag":["மொக்கலன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["மொக்கலன்","நீலகேசி"],"ch":"nk:19"},"nk:393":{"book":"neelakesi","num":392,"t":["கூறிய தெக்குண மக்குணந்தானக் குணிப்பொருளே","தேறிய தெக்குணி யக்குணிதீர்ந்தில பல்குணமும்","வேறென வொன்றென வில்வகைவேண்டுகின் றேற்கவைதா","மாறென்னுங் கொள்ளா முடிபுமொழிநின் மயக்கமென்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi replies that the quality spoken of is the qualified substance itself and the substance is never apart from its many qualities, so she holds them both different and one, and his confusion is to treat these as contradictory.","n":"This is the Jain position of difference-in-identity (வேறென ஒன்றென) between quality and substance, stated against the Buddhist demand for one or the other; the Jain refuses the either-or rather than choosing a side.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["gunam","vaadhu","meyyunarvu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 393","lb":"NK393","ev":"","cl":[["quality and qualified substance are both different and one, and holding both is no contradiction","jain","asserted",3,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","மொக்கலன்"],"ch":"nk:19"},"nk:394":{"book":"neelakesi","num":393,"t":["புற்கல மாய முதற்பொருடத்தமுட் புல்லினவாய்க்","கற்களு நீருந் நிலத்தொடுகற்றழ லென்றினைய","பற்பல கூற்றாற் பிறங்கிப்பரக்குந் தியமென்னையோ","வுற்றவை யொன்றொன்றி னுட்புகுமோத்துடை யாய்க்கெனலும்."],"p":"","r":"The Buddhist asks the woman who holds the scripture how matter (புற்கலம்), the primary substance, joining with itself, spreads out as stones, water, earth and fire, and whether the things that meet enter into one another.","n":"புற்கலம் here is the Jain pudgala, matter, not the Buddhist Pudgalavada person of 248. தியம் in line 3 is not a word I can construe; the sense of the question is clear from 395 to 397.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["anu","vaadhu","aagamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 394","lb":"NK394","ev":"","cl":[["atoms that combine into gross things must either interpenetrate or stay apart, and either way the Jain account fails","buddhist","refuted",4,"mokkalan"]],"ag":["மொக்கலன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["மொக்கலன்","நீலகேசி"],"ch":"nk:19"},"nk:395":{"book":"neelakesi","num":394,"t":["யாத்தற் கமைந்த குணத்தின வாய வணுப்பொருள்க","ணீத்தற் கரியன நீத்த வருக்க நெறிமையினா","லேத்தற் கியைந்த விரண்டணு வாதியி னின்னணமா","மோத்திற்கிடந்த வகையிது கேளென் றுரைத்தனளே."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi tells him to hear what the scripture lays down: the atoms have qualities fit for binding, are hard to separate once joined, and, setting aside the classes that do not bind, build up from the two-atom compound onward.","n":"The appeal is to ஓத்து, the Jain canon; the rule of excluded classes (நீத்த வருக்க நெறிமை) seems to be the doctrine that only atoms of certain degrees of quality bind, but the line is compressed.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["anu","aagamam","gunam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 395","lb":"NK395","ev":"","cl":[["atoms bind by qualities of their own and build compounds upward from the two-atom unit","jain","asserted",1,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","மொக்கலன்"],"ch":"nk:19"},"nk:396":{"book":"neelakesi","num":395,"t":["இரண்டணு வாதியி னின்னணமேறுநின் கந்தமெனிற்","றிரண்டன வாய்த்தம்முட் சென்றுடன்றீண்டு மிடத்தவைதா","முருண்டன தாமொன்றி னுள்ளும்புடையு முடைமையினாற்","றெருண்டனம் பாகுபா டுற்றற்குமென்றனன் றேரனுமே."],"p":"","r":"The thera argues that if the aggregate (கந்தம்) rises from the dyad upward, then atoms that crowd together and touch must, being round, have an inside and sides, and so are seen to be divisible.","n":"கந்தம் here is the aggregate of atoms, not the Buddhist five aggregates; the argument, that contact implies parts and parts deny indivisibility, is the Buddhist objection to atoms in general.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["anu","vaadhu","therithal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 396","lb":"NK396","ev":"","cl":[["an atom that touches another must have an inside and sides, and so cannot be indivisible","buddhist","refuted",3,"mokkalan"]],"ag":["மொக்கலன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["மொக்கலன்","நீலகேசி"],"ch":"nk:19"},"nk:397":{"book":"neelakesi","num":396,"t":["ஓரிட மாய முதற் பொருட் குள்ளும் புடையுஞ்சொல்லிப்","பேரிட மாக்கிப் பிளப்ப னெனவும் பிதற்றுகின்றா","யாரிட மாய வறிவிற்கு மின்னண மாதலினா","னேரிடத் தாற்பன்மை யெய்தி யுருவா நெறியுமென்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi says he prattles in giving an inside and sides to a primary substance that holds one place, enlarging it to split it, and answers that by sharing the same place atoms reach plurality and become form, as happens even with cognition.","n":"The reply seems to rest on the Jain view that many atoms can occupy one point of space, so that combination does not require parts; the comparison with அறிவு in line 3 is obscure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["anu","vaadhu","arivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 397","lb":"NK397","ev":"","cl":[["atoms that occupy one and the same place can become many and take form without being divided","jain","asserted",4,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","மொக்கலன்"],"ch":"nk:19"},"nk:398":{"book":"neelakesi","num":397,"t":["வண்டாயுங் கோதாய் வரைநெல்லியின் காய தங்கை","யுண்டாய போதே யுறையூரகத் தில்லை யென்பாய்","கண்டாயிம் மெய்ம்மை பிறர்காண்டற் கரிய தென்றான்","பெண்டான மீயு மறங்கொண்ட பெருமை யினான்."],"p":"","r":"The Buddhist, whom the narrator calls great in the dharma that gives away women, mocks her: the hill gooseberry is in her hand, and at that very moment she will say it is not in Uraiyur, as if this were a truth hard for others to see.","n":"He parodies the Jain claim that a thing exists from its own place and does not exist from another as a truism. The narrator's epithet (பெண்டானம் ஈயும் அறம்) turns Neelakesi's earlier charge about the bodhisattva who gave his wives into a sneer at the speaker.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vaadhu","pullarivu","meyyunarvu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 398","lb":"NK398","ev":"","cl":[["that a fruit held here does not exist in a distant town is a truism, not a hidden truth","buddhist","refuted",3,"mokkalan"]],"ag":["மொக்கலன்","நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["மொக்கலன்","நீலகேசி"],"ch":"nk:19"},"nk:399":{"book":"neelakesi","num":398,"t":["வெய்தாய தீயுங் குளிராகிய நீரும் விண்டோய்ந்","தைதாய காற்று மவையாரு மறிப வென்றாற்","பொய்யாகு மென்னா யவைபுத்த வசன மென்பாய்","செய்தாய் முழுக்கூ ழதுபோலச் சிதைக்க வென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"He goes on that everyone knows fire is hot, water cold and wind thin, and says that if she says these are Buddha's words she will not call them false, yet she has spoiled them like a whole pot of gruel.","n":"The run of lines 3 and 4 is unclear: who calls what false and what the gruel (முழுக்கூழ்) stands for cannot be fixed from the verse alone.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vaadhu","meyyunarvu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 399","lb":"NK399","ev":"","cl":[["the plain natures everyone perceives, such as the heat of fire and the cold of water, need no qualification","buddhist","reported",1,"mokkalan"]],"ag":["மொக்கலன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["மொக்கலன்","நீலகேசி"],"ch":"nk:19"},"nk:400":{"book":"neelakesi","num":399,"t":["கந்தின்கட்ட காணாய் களியானையை யில்லை யென்பாய்","வந்திங்க ணின்ற பொழுதுண்மை மறுக்க லாமோ","தந்திங் குரைத்த வுரைதானுங் கெடுக வென்றான்","வெந்திங்கு வித்தின் னனைத்தாகிய வீடு கண்டான்."],"p":"","r":"He adds that she would call the rutting elephant tied to the post non-existent, though when it has come and stands here its existence cannot be denied, and curses her words; the narrator names him as one who has seen a release like a burnt seed.","n":"The closing epithet (வெந்து ... வித்தின் அனைத்தாகிய வீடு) gives the Buddhist release as a seed burnt so it cannot sprout again; whether the narrator means it as praise or as the Jain view of nirvana as extinction is not said.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vaadhu","veedu","meyyunarvu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 400","lb":"NK400","ev":"","cl":[["what is plainly present before one cannot be called non-existent","buddhist","refuted",2,"mokkalan"]],"ag":["மொக்கலன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["மொக்கலன்","நீலகேசி"],"ch":"nk:19"},"nk:401":{"book":"neelakesi","num":400,"t":["ஆண்டில்லை யென்பன் னதுவுள்வழி யுண்டு மென்ப","னீண்டின்மை யுண்மை யிவையாக விசைத்து நின்றேன்","வேண்டி யனவே முடிப்பாய்விரி பொன்னெ யிலு","ளீண்டி யிமையோர் தொழுவானெம் மிறையு மென்னாய்."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi answers that she called the thing non-existent there and existent where it is, stating non-existence and existence together, and that he finishes only the arguments he wants and will not also say the same of her Lord whom the gods throng to worship in the golden fort.","n":"This answers 398 to 400: the Jain claim is not that the gooseberry or the elephant is absent, but that existence and non-existence each hold in their own respect. The point of line 4 about the Lord in the golden fort is unclear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vaadhu","iraivan","meyyunarvu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 401","lb":"NK401","ev":"","cl":[["a thing is non-existent from another place and existent where it is, and both are said together","jain","asserted",2,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","மொக்கலன்"],"ch":"nk:19"},"nk:402":{"book":"neelakesi","num":401,"t":["கொல்லேற்றின் கோடு குழக்கன்றது வாயி னக்கா","லில்லாகு மென்றி யிவையிங்ஙன முண்மை யின்மை","சொல்லேனு மல்லே னதுசொல்லுவன் யானு மன்னாய்","கொல்லேற தாகப் பொழுதேயுடன் கூறு கென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"The Buddhist says that if the horn of the killing bull is non-existent in the young calf, then, since she speaks of existence and non-existence together, she should say the calf is the killing bull at that very time.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vaadhu","pullarivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 402","lb":"NK402","ev":"","cl":[["if existence and non-existence hold of one thing at once, the calf must already be the bull","buddhist","refuted",4,"mokkalan"]],"ag":["மொக்கலன்","நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["மொக்கலன்","நீலகேசி"],"ch":"nk:19"},"nk:403":{"book":"neelakesi","num":402,"t":["ஏறாய காலத் தெழினல்லது வத்து பேதங்","கூறாரெ ழாத குழக்கன்றினுக் கின்மை முன்னா","வீறாகி நிற்கும் முதலுண்மையிற் கின்மை யெங்கு","மாறியாது மில்லை கலைக்குண்மையு மற்றுமென்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi replies that a difference of thing is spoken of only when the bull has arisen; for the calf not yet grown, non-existence comes first while the existence of the substance stands firm, and nowhere does either change.","n":"She separates the persisting substance from its states: the horn is a state not yet arisen, so its absence in the calf is prior non-existence and not the absence of the animal. கலைக்கு in line 4 is unclear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vaadhu","gunam","meyyunarvu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 403","lb":"NK403","ev":"","cl":[["the horn's absence in the calf is the prior non-existence of a state, while the substance persists throughout","jain","asserted",2,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","மொக்கலன்"],"ch":"nk:19"},"nk:404":{"book":"neelakesi","num":403,"t":["கன்று முயலுங் கழுதைப்பெயர் பெற்ற னவுங்","குன்றுந் தலையுட் பெறப்பாடெய்தல் கோடு றுப்பா","வென்றும் மவற்றுக் கெழலில்லைநின் பேத மென்றாற்","சென்றுஞ் சிலவிற் சிலவின்மையு மாகு மென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"He counters that a calf, a hare, the beast called donkey and a hill never come to have a horn on the head as a limb, so her distinction fails, and some things are simply non-existent in some respects.","n":"The hare's and donkey's horns are the stock examples of what never exists; why கன்று (calf) appears in this list, after the calf of 402 that does grow horns, is unclear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vaadhu","meyyunarvu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 404","lb":"NK404","ev":"","cl":[["some things, like the horn of a hare or donkey, never arise, so their non-existence is not a matter of standpoint","buddhist","refuted",3,"mokkalan"]],"ag":["மொக்கலன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["மொக்கலன்","நீலகேசி"],"ch":"nk:19"},"nk:405":{"book":"neelakesi","num":404,"t":["இல்லாத கோட்டை யுளதாக வெடுத்து மென்று","சொல்லார்கள் பேதம் சொலவேண்டுவை யாயி னக்காற்","புல்லாது நில்லாப் பொருடங்களுக் குண்மைக் கின்மை","கல்லாது நீயுங் கழுதைக்கருள் செய்தி யென்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi says no one speaks of distinctions by taking up a horn that does not exist as if it did; if he wants distinctions he must learn the existence and non-existence of things that cannot stand without joining, or else he too is granting the donkey a horn.","n":"Her reply limits the scope of her own doctrine: existence and non-existence in respects are said of real substances and their states, not of fictions.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vaadhu","pullarivu","meyyunarvu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 405","lb":"NK405","ev":"","cl":[["existence and non-existence in respects are said only of real things, not of fictions like a hare's horn","jain","asserted",3,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","மொக்கலன்"],"ch":"nk:19"},"nk:406":{"book":"neelakesi","num":405,"t":["இல்லை வலக்கை யிடக்கைவகை யால தென்றுஞ்","சொல்லின் னதற்கு மதுவேயெனுஞ் சூழ்ச்சி மிக்கா","யொல்லை யிரண்டு முளவாக வுணர்ந்தனை நீ","நல்லை பெரிதும் மெனமொக்கல னக்க னனே."],"p":"","r":"Mokkalan laughs: there is no right or left hand except by relation, she says the same of that, she of great cunning has quickly understood both to exist, and she is very good indeed.","n":"The praise is sarcasm; his point is that relational terms like right and left have no being of their own.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vaadhu","pullarivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 406","lb":"NK406","ev":"Mokkalan laughs at Neelakesi.","cl":[["right and left exist only by relation and have no being of their own","buddhist","refuted",1,"mokkalan"]],"ag":["மொக்கலன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["மொக்கலன்","நீலகேசி"],"ch":"nk:19"},"nk:407":{"book":"neelakesi","num":406,"t":["இக்கை வகையா லதுதானுள தாயி னக்காற்","றொக்க விரண்டும் முடனாதலிற் றூய்தொ ருபால்","பக்கம் மதுவும் படுபாழினிக் காலு மற்றாய்ச்","செக்கின்கணைபோன்றினிச் சென்றுருள் சேம மென்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi answers that if a thing exists only by this relation, both sides arise together, so each side becomes void and the limbs too, and tells him to go round now like the shaft of an oil press, which is his safety.","n":"The oil-press image mocks an argument that goes in circles; the middle of the verse is compressed.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vaadhu","pullarivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 407","lb":"NK407","ev":"","cl":[["if relation alone gave things their being, both relata and everything else would become empty","jain","asserted",3,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","மொக்கலன்"],"ch":"nk:19"},"nk:408":{"book":"neelakesi","num":407,"t":["கைகால் வகையால் பெறப்பாடிலை காலு மற்றாய்","மெய்தா மொழிய வவைபாறெய்தல் வேண்டு தலாற்","கொய்தார் நறும்பூங் குழலாய்குழ மண்ணர் களாச்","செய்தா யுலகிற் சிறுமானுயர் தம்மை யென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"He replies that hands and feet do not get their being from relation, since the body must have the parts named, and says she has made the little humans of the world into lumps of clay.","n":"குழமண்ணர்களா in line 3 is obscure and the turn of his argument is hard to follow; he seems to concede that limbs are not merely relational.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vaadhu","yaakkai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 408","lb":"NK408","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["மொக்கலன்","நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["மொக்கலன்","நீலகேசி"],"ch":"nk:19"},"nk:409":{"book":"neelakesi","num":408,"t":["கால்கால் வகையா லுளகைகளுங் கையி னற்றாய்ப்","பாலாய் முடியு மவைபண்டை யியல்பி னாலே","யேலா திவைதா முளவெத்திறத் தானு மென்னி","னாலாவ தான முடிவி னாயொடு நண்டு மொத்தாய்.."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi says the hands, which exist by relation to the feet, also come to be by their own former nature, and that if he asks in what way they exist, his conclusion makes him like the dog and the crab.","n":"The dog and crab comparison is an insult whose point is lost; the doctrine seems to be that a thing has both its own nature and a relational one.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vaadhu","pullarivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 409","lb":"NK409","ev":"","cl":[["a limb has both its own prior nature and a nature from relation","jain","asserted",2,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","மொக்கலன்"],"ch":"nk:19"},"nk:410":{"book":"neelakesi","num":409,"t":["அல்லென் றுரைத்த வுரைதானுமெம் மாக மத்து","ளில்லென்ற வாறென் றிவையிங்ஙனம் வேண்டு கின்றாய்","சொல்லன்று நாயைந் நரிதானென்னச் சொல்லு கின்றா","னில்லென்ற வாறோ நரிதன்னையு மென்ற னனே."],"p":"","r":"The Buddhist says she demands that the word 'is not' (அல்) in his scripture mean 'does not exist' (இல்), and asks whether one who says 'that is not a dog, it is a jackal' is saying the jackal does not exist.","n":"He separates negation of identity (அல்) from negation of existence (இல்), a distinction Tamil grammar itself draws.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sol","vaadhu","aagamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 410","lb":"NK410","ev":"","cl":[["saying a thing is not this is not saying it does not exist","buddhist","refuted",3,"mokkalan"]],"ag":["மொக்கலன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["மொக்கலன்","நீலகேசி"],"ch":"nk:19"},"nk:411":{"book":"neelakesi","num":410,"t":["நாய்கொன் னரிகொல் லெனத்தோன்றுமுணர்வு நண்ணி","யாய்சொல் லிரண்டின் னுணர்ந்தல்லதுவன்மை யென்றாய்","நீசொல் லறியா யறிவார்நெறிநேடு கில்லாய்","பேய்சொல் லுபவே பலசொல்லிப்பிதற்ற லென்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi says that when the cognition 'dog or jackal?' arises he claimed that 'not' is known only after examining both words, that he does not know words or seek the way of those who know, and that prattling many words is what ghosts do.","n":"Line 2 seems to report his position before she dismisses it; her answer is mostly scorn, not a counter-argument.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sol","pullarivu","vaadhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 411","lb":"NK411","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","மொக்கலன்"],"ch":"nk:19"},"nk:412":{"book":"neelakesi","num":411,"t":["பேரும் உணர்வும் பொருளில்லதற் கில்லை யென்றி","சார்வும் மகல்வுந் தலைப்பெய்தலோ டுள்ள மின்மை","நேரிங் கிவையு முணராமையிற் கென்ற னனாய்த்","தேரன் சிறிதே தெரிகோதையை நக்க னனே."],"p":"","r":"The thera, smiling a little at the woman with the chosen garland, says she claims a thing that does not exist has neither name nor cognition, and asks whether dependence, separation, meeting and the absence of a mind are not named so only for want of knowing.","n":"சார்வு is the Buddhist term for dependent arising; the list in line 2 seems meant as names for absences or relations that have no substance, but the force of line 3 (உணராமையிற்கு) is uncertain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sol","saarbu","vaadhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 412","lb":"NK412","ev":"The Buddhist thera smiles at Neelakesi.","cl":[["names and cognitions do occur for what does not exist","buddhist","refuted",1,"mokkalan"]],"ag":["மொக்கலன்","நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["மொக்கலன்","நீலகேசி"],"ch":"nk:19"},"nk:413":{"book":"neelakesi","num":412,"t":["ஆத்தன் னுரைத்த பொருடன்னையவ் வாக மத்தாற்","சாத்தன் பயின்றா லறியாவிடுந் தன்மை யுண்டோ","வீர்த்திங் குரைத்த பலதம்முளொன் றின்ன தென்னா","யோத்தின் வகையாற் பெயரோடுணர் வின்மைக் கென்றாள்"],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi asks whether anyone, even a common man, who studied the meaning the Trustworthy One gave in that scripture could fail to know, and says that of all he has dragged in he cannot name one by the scripture's account that has a name and a cognition but no existence.","n":"ஆத்தன் is the Apta, the trustworthy teacher, the Jain title for the Jina; சாத்தன் stands for anyone at all. The appeal is to scripture, but made as a debating challenge.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aagamam","vaadhu","arivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 413","lb":"NK413","ev":"","cl":[["one who studies the meaning the Trustworthy One gave in the scripture cannot fail to know","jain","asserted",1,"neelakesi"],["nothing that has a name and a cognition is wholly non-existent","jain","asserted",4,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","மொக்கலன்"],"ch":"nk:19"},"nk:414":{"book":"neelakesi","num":413,"t":["ஒன்றி னியற்கை யொருவான்பொருட் கில்லை யென்றே","யென்று முரைத்தி யிரும்பெய்திய வெம்மை யந்நீர்","சென்றும் மறுகித் தீக்குணஞ் சேர்ந்த தற்றேற்","குன்றும் பிறவோ வினிநீண்ட கோளு மென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"The Buddhist says she always holds that the nature of one thing does not belong to another, yet water heated as iron is heated stirs and takes on fire's quality, and if so her long thesis is diminished.","n":"He turns a Jain thesis against itself with heated water. The reply is not in this chapter: 415 turns to the soul in the body, which may be her answer by analogy, but the text does not say so.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["gunam","vaadhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 414","lb":"NK414","ev":"","cl":[["the quality of one thing passes into another, as heated water takes on fire's heat","buddhist","reported",3,"mokkalan"]],"ag":["மொக்கலன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["மொக்கலன்","நீலகேசி"],"ch":"nk:19"},"nk:415":{"book":"neelakesi","num":414,"t":["கொண்ட வுடம்போடுயிர் தானுடன் கூடி நின்றாற்","கண்டு முணர்ந்து மவையாவதென் கல்வி யில்லா","யுண்டங்க ணின்ற வுயிர்க்காக வுரைப்ப தொக்கும்","பிண்டந் நிகழ்ச்சி பிழைப்பாகு நினக்கு மென்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi argues that if the soul stays joined with the body it has taken, seeing and knowing are rightly said to belong to the soul that is there, and calls Mokkalan unlearned: for him, who has only the heap of the body, the working of that heap goes wrong.","n":"பிண்டம் (the lump or heap) is her word for what the Buddhist puts in place of a soul; the point is that perception needs a perceiver dwelling in the body, not a mere aggregate.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["uyir","yaakkai","arivu","vaadhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 415","lb":"NK415","ev":"","cl":[["seeing and knowing belong to the soul that abides joined with the body","jain","asserted",3,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","மொக்கலன்"],"ch":"nk:20"},"nk:416":{"book":"neelakesi","num":415,"t":["மெச்சி யிடத்தாற் பிறிதின்மை விளம்பு கின்றாய்","பிச்சை முதலாப் பெரிதாவறஞ் செய்த வன்றா","னச்செல் கதியுள் ளமரன்னெனப் பாடு மின்றே","விச்செய்கை யெல்லா மிகழ்வாம்பிற வென்ற னனே."],"p":"","r":"Mokkalan replies that she holds the soul to be not other than the body in place, and objects that then no one can say today that the man who did great dharma, beginning with alms, becomes a god in the course he goes to, so all such deeds would be despised.","n":"The objection takes the Jain teaching that the soul fills the body it occupies and argues that a soul bound to this body's place cannot be the one rewarded in heaven; 417 answers it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","marumai","thevar","uyir"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 416","lb":"NK416","ev":"","cl":[["a soul not other than the body in place cannot be the one that reaches heaven for its alms, so meritorious deeds lose their point","buddhist","refuted",1,"mokkalan"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["மொக்கலன்","நீலகேசி"],"ch":"nk:20"},"nk:417":{"book":"neelakesi","num":416,"t":["ஊனத்தை யின்றி வழங்காவுழல் கின்ற போழ்து","மானத்தி னீங்கி வதங்காத்து வருந்தும் போழ்தும்","வானத்த தாய பொழுதுமன் னுயிர தென்றா","டானத்தி னுண்மை யிதுதத்துவ மாக்கொ ளென்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi answers that the same abiding soul is there when it goes about giving without stint, when it leaves pride and suffers to keep its vows, and when it is in heaven, and tells him to take this as the truth of giving.","n":"She grounds the worth of giving (தானம்) on the identity of the giver across lives: the giver and the one in heaven are one soul. The value of the gift is made to rest on the reward reaching its doer.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","uyir","marumai","nonbu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 417","lb":"NK417","ev":"","cl":[["the soul that gives and keeps vows is the same soul that later dwells in heaven, which is what makes giving true","jain","asserted",3,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","மொக்கலன்"],"ch":"nk:20"},"nk:418":{"book":"neelakesi","num":417,"t":["காலம் பிறிதிற் பொருளில்லெனக் காட்டு கின்றாய்","ஞால மறியத் தவஞ்செய்தவ னல்லு யிர்தா","னேலங்கொள் கோதா யெதிர்காலத்தி னின்மை யாமேற்","சீலங்கள் காத்தல் வருத்தஞ்சிதை வாக வென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"Mokkalan says she shows that a thing has no being at a time other than its own, and argues, addressing her as the woman with scented tresses, that then the good soul of a world-famed ascetic would not exist in the time to come, and keeping the precepts would be wasted toil.","n":"The first line can be construed in more than one way; read here as his statement of her view that each state of a thing exists in its own time, turned against continuity of reward.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thavam","uyir","ozhukkam","vaadhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 418","lb":"NK418","ev":"","cl":[["if a thing exists only in its own time, the ascetic's soul will not exist in the future and keeping precepts is vain","buddhist","refuted",3,"mokkalan"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["மொக்கலன்","நீலகேசி"],"ch":"nk:20"},"nk:419":{"book":"neelakesi","num":418,"t":["ஆற்ற வருந்தித் தவஞ்செய்து மரிய காத்துந்","நோற்றும் பெரிதுந் நுணுகாநின்ற பொழுதி னானும்","மேற்ற முடைய விமையானெனப் பட்ட போழ்துஞ்","சாற்றி னுயிர்தன் பொழுதே யுண்மை தங்கு மென்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi answers that whether the soul is suffering in austerity, guarding hard vows, fasting and wasting away, or is called an exalted unblinking god, its being stays with it in each of its times.","n":"இமையான், the one who does not blink, is a god; the verse places the ascetic and the god he becomes on one line of a single soul's existence.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["uyir","thavam","thevar","nonbu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 419","lb":"NK419","ev":"","cl":[["the soul's existence abides through each of its states, from austerity to divine birth","jain","asserted",4,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","மொக்கலன்"],"ch":"nk:20"},"nk:420":{"book":"neelakesi","num":419,"t":["நூறா னிரும்பாய் நிகழாமை நொடிதி யாங்கே","பாறான் றயிரா மெனநின்று பயிற்று தியான்","மாறா னுடையா ருரையொக்குநின் மாற்ற மென்னாத்","தேறார் தெருண்டா ரெனச் சொல்லினன் றேர னும்மே."],"p":"","r":"Mokkalan charges that she says one thing does not become another, and in the same breath teaches that milk becomes curd, so her words are like those of people who contradict themselves; the thera says those who do not understand think they are clear.","n":"The first half-line (நூறான் இரும்பாய் நிகழாமை) seems to give her example of what does not become something else; its exact sense is not clear. The charge is against the Jain view that a thing both persists and changes.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vaadhu","arivu","pedhaimai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 420","lb":"NK420","ev":"","cl":[["to hold that a thing does not become another and yet that milk becomes curd is self-contradiction","buddhist","refuted",3,"mokkalan"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["மொக்கலன்","நீலகேசி"],"ch":"nk:20"},"nk:421":{"book":"neelakesi","num":420,"t":["தத்தந் நிமித்தந் தலைப்பெய்துதங் காரி யம்மா","யொத்த பொருள்க ணிகழ்வாக்க முரைத்து நின்றேன்","பித்தனி னோப்பப் பிறிதிற்பிறி தாமென் பனோ","வித்தின் வழியா னுரைநீயும்வெள் யானை யென்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi replies that she said suitable things become their effects by meeting their own causes, and asks whether she, like a madman, would say anything becomes anything; she tells him to speak along the line of the seed, and calls him a white elephant.","n":"The seed (வித்து) is the model of change by proper cause. வெள் யானை may be a jibe at the white elephant of the Buddha's birth story, or simply an insult for a dull one; the text does not settle it.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vaadhu","meyyunarvu","arivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 421","lb":"NK421","ev":"","cl":[["a thing becomes its effect only through its own proper causes, not anything from anything","jain","asserted",1,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","மொக்கலன்"],"ch":"nk:20"},"nk:422":{"book":"neelakesi","num":421,"t":["கூடா பொருள்கள் பிறிதின்குணத் துண்மை யென்பாய்","பாடாஅலப் புட்பத் தனவாகிய பண்பு நாற்றம்","மோடாவ தெய்திற் றெனவையமுரைக் கின்ற தஃதா","னாடாது சொன்னா யதனன்மை யொழிக வென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"Mokkalan says she holds that a quality cannot exist in a thing other than its own, and counters that the world says the scent which is the quality of the patala flower has passed into the potsherd; she spoke without examining, so let her claim go.","n":"He appeals to common usage (வையம் உரைக்கின்றது) as evidence that a quality can move from its substance into another.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vaadhu","alavai","meyyunarvu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 422","lb":"NK422","ev":"","cl":[["a quality can pass from its own substance into another, as a flower's scent into a pot","buddhist","refuted",3,"mokkalan"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["மொக்கலன்","நீலகேசி"],"ch":"nk:20"},"nk:423":{"book":"neelakesi","num":422,"t":["போதுக்க வாசம் புதுவோட்டைப் பொருந்தினாலும்","மேதக்க நாற்ற மிதுபூவின தென்ப மிக்கார்","தாதுக்க நின்று மவைபோக்குந் ததாக தற்கென்","றேதுக்கள் காட்டி முடித்தாளிணை யில்ல நல்லாள்."],"p":"","r":"The narrator tells how the peerless woman ended her answer by giving reasons to the Tathagata's man: even when a flower's scent sits in a new sherd, the wise say the fine scent is the flower's, for the flower's particles stay there and carry it.","n":"தாதுக்கள் here are read as the flower's fine particles: the scent never leaves its substance, since bits of the flower lodge in the pot. ததாகதற்கு names the opponent by his master.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vaadhu","alavai","meyyunarvu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 423","lb":"NK423","ev":"Neelakesi closes her reply on the flower's scent with reasons.","cl":[["a scent in a pot remains the flower's, carried by particles of the flower","jain","asserted",3,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"ch":"nk:20"},"nk:424":{"book":"neelakesi","num":423,"t":["வீட்டிட மென்று நின்னால்வேண்டவும் பட்ட தன்னை","நாட்டுவ னதுவு நாயிற்கென்றுநன் றென்றி யாயிற்","சூட்டடு நரகந் தானுஞ்சுடர்ந்தநற் சுவர்க்கந் தானும்","பூட்டின முரைத்த வக்காற்போந்ததங் கென்னை யென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"Mokkalan takes up the place of release she desires, and asks, if she calls it good, what went there in the way the burning hell and the bright heaven, which they named together, are places one goes to.","n":"The second line (நாயிற்கு) is obscure. The gist seems to be that a release located in a place is only one more destination like hell and heaven.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["veedu","naragam","marumai","vaadhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 424","lb":"NK424","ev":"","cl":[["a release that is a place would be one more destination like hell and heaven","buddhist","refuted",1,"mokkalan"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["மொக்கலன்","நீலகேசி"],"ch":"nk:20"},"nk:425":{"book":"neelakesi","num":424,"t":["கதியின வகைய வாறுங்கந்தபிண் டங்கள் சொன்னான்","பதியின வென்ன நின்றாய்பாக்கனாய் காட்டு தீயால்","விதியினின் விளையட் டார்தம்வீட்டிட மின்ன தென்றாற்","கதுவென்னை யென்னச் சொன்னாலாகம மல்ல தாமோ."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi retorts that his teacher spoke of six kinds of destinies and of heaps of aggregates as if they were places, and he shows them; if one says the place of release of those who have destroyed karma's fruit by rule is such, and he asks what it is, is the answer not scripture?","n":"The verse has no closing என்றாள், so the speaker is inferred from the alternation. ஆகமம் here is the Jain scripture, not the Saiva Agamas. பாக்கனாய் is an obscure term of address.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["veedu","kathi","kandham","puththan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 425","lb":"NK425","ev":"","cl":[["the place of the released is known from scripture, as the Buddha himself named six destinies","jain","asserted",4,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","மொக்கலன்"],"ch":"nk:20"},"nk:426":{"book":"neelakesi","num":425,"t":["பேர்த்தினவண் வார லில்லாப்பிறவியாந் தான மென்னிற்","றீர்த்திவண் வார லின்மைசேர்விடக் குண்மை யாமோ","கூர்த்தலில் வினையி னின்மைகூறுவ னென்றி யாயி","னார்த்துள னவனே யாயினண்ணுமே வினையு மென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"Mokkalan asks whether, if release is a birth-place from which none come back, the not-coming-back belongs to the place reached; and if she explains it by the absence of karma, he argues that if the freed one is the same who was bound, karma will reach him again.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["veedu","vinai","piravi","vaadhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 426","lb":"NK426","ev":"","cl":[["if the released soul is the same one that was bound, karma can bind it again, so release by a lasting soul is not final","buddhist","refuted",4,"mokkalan"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["மொக்கலன்","நீலகேசி"],"ch":"nk:20"},"nk:427":{"book":"neelakesi","num":426,"t":["பிறப்பதை வீடு மென்னேனவ்விடம் பேர்ப்பின் றென்னே","னுறத்தகு வினைக டாமுமுண்மையா லொட்டு மென்னேன்","மறத்தலில் யோக பாவமாசதா மீட்டு மென்ப","திறப்பவும் வேண்டு கின்றேற்கெய்தல நின்சொ லென்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi says she does not call release a birth, nor say the place itself prevents return, nor that karmas cling merely because they exist; she holds that it is the unforgetting yoga activity that brings back the stain, and his words do not reach her.","n":"யோக பாவம் is the Jain sense of yoga: the activity of body, speech and mind through which karma flows in. With that activity gone, nothing draws karma to the freed soul. Not the Saiva or Siddhar yoga of union.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["veedu","vinai","piravi","meyyunarvu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 427","lb":"NK427","ev":"","cl":[["karmic stain returns only through the activity (yoga) of body, speech and mind; the freed soul without it is not bound again","jain","asserted",3,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","மொக்கலன்"],"ch":"nk:20"},"nk:428":{"book":"neelakesi","num":427,"t":["பிறக்குந்தன் ஞானத் தாலும்பின்னுந்தன் னுண்மை யாலும்","புறப்பொருள் கொண்டு நின்றுபுல்லிய சிந்தை யாலுஞ்","சிறப்புடை வீடி தென்றுசெப்புநீ தீவி னையைத்","துறக்குமா றில்லை நல்லாய்சொல்லுநீ வல்ல தென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"Mokkalan argues that she calls this the excellent release while the soul still has knowledge that arises, continued being, and a clinging thought that takes outer objects; by such a soul there is no way to give up evil karma, good woman, and he challenges her to answer.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["veedu","vinai","gnaanam","pattru"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 428","lb":"NK428","ev":"","cl":[["a released soul that still knows, persists and takes outer objects keeps the grounds of evil karma","buddhist","refuted",3,"mokkalan"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["மொக்கலன்","நீலகேசி"],"ch":"nk:20"},"nk:429":{"book":"neelakesi","num":428,"t":["நன்றியில் கார ணங்கணாட்டிநீ காட்டி னவ்வு","மொன்றுநா னொட்டல் செல்லேன்யோடுகொடு பாவ நின்றாற்","குன்றினிற் கூர்ங்கை நாட்டாற்கூடுநோ யாதிற் குண்டோ","வொன்றுநீ யுணர மாட்டாயொழிகநின் னுரையு மென்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi rejects every one of his unprofitable reasons, arguing that harm comes only where the binding activity stands, and that he understands nothing; let his speech end.","n":"Lines 2 and 3 are obscure (யோடுகொடு, குன்றினில் கூர்ங்கை நாட்டால்); the reading takes them as an image of something that cannot harm where there is nothing to take hold, in line with 427.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vinai","veedu","vaadhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 429","lb":"NK429","ev":"","cl":[["knowing objects does not bind karma unless the binding activity is present","jain","asserted",2,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","மொக்கலன்"],"ch":"nk:20"},"nk:430":{"book":"neelakesi","num":429,"t":["கருவிதா னொன்று மின்றிக்கடையிலாப் பொருளை யெல்லா","மருவிய ஞானந் தன்னாலறியுமெம் மிறைவ னென்பாய்","கருவிதா னகத்தி னாயகடையிலா ஞான மன்றோ","மருவியார்க் கமிர்த மொப்பாய்மாற்றந்தா விதனுக் கென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"Mokkalan says she holds that her Lord knows all endless things by knowledge without any instrument, and objects that endless knowledge itself is an inner instrument; he asks her, nectar to those who join her, to answer.","n":"The Jain claim of omniscience without sense organs is stated here by the opponent; the objection is his.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","gnaanam","alavai","vaadhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 430","lb":"NK430","ev":"","cl":[["the Jina's endless knowledge is itself an inner instrument, so his knowing is not free of instruments","buddhist","refuted",3,"mokkalan"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["மொக்கலன்","நீலகேசி"],"ch":"nk:20"},"nk:431":{"book":"neelakesi","num":430,"t":["வினையுமவ் வினையி னாயவிகலஞா னங்க டாமு","மினையவே கருவி யென்றாலிங்குநின் னுள்ளம் வையாய்","முனைவனாய் மூர்த்தி யல்லான்மூடுமே மாசு மென்பாய்","கனைகட லென்லை காணுங்காக்கையொத் தாங்கொ லென்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi tells him to fix his mind on this: what are called instruments are karma and the partial knowledges that karma produces; impurity covers all but the Lord, and to think otherwise is like a crow trying to see the end of the roaring sea.","n":"விகல ஞானங்கள் are the defective, sense-bound knowings that karma gives; the Lord's knowledge is not of that kind. The crow and the sea mock a limited knower claiming the whole.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["gnaanam","vinai","iraivan","malam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 431","lb":"NK431","ev":"","cl":[["instruments of knowing are karma and the partial knowledges karma produces; the Lord's complete knowledge needs none","jain","asserted",1,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","மொக்கலன்"],"ch":"nk:20"},"nk:432":{"book":"neelakesi","num":431,"t":["கொண்டதன் கரணந் தானுமில்லையேற் கூற்று மில்லை","மண்டினர் வினவு வார்க்குமலைச்சிலம் பனைய னென்றா","லுண்டுதன் கரணந் தானுமுரைக்குநர்க் குறுவ னென்னிற்","பண்டுசெய் நல்வி னையைப்பகவனே யென்று மென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"Mokkalan sets a dilemma: if the Lord has no organ, he has no speech either and is only like a mountain echo to those who throng and ask; if he has an organ and comes to those who ask, she will be calling good karma done long ago the Lord.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","vinai","sol","vaadhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 432","lb":"NK432","ev":"","cl":[["an omniscient Lord without organs cannot speak, and one with organs is only the fruit of past good karma","buddhist","refuted",4,"mokkalan"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["மொக்கலன்","நீலகேசி"],"ch":"nk:20"},"nk:433":{"book":"neelakesi","num":432,"t":["தனுவெனுங் கருவி தன்னாற்றன்னடைந் தார்க டன்னை","வினவின வுணர்ந்து சொல்லும்வினையினுக் கின்ன துண்டோ","சினவினுந் தேர வொன்றுசெப்புவன் செல்க தீயுட்","கனவினு நின்ன னாரைக்காணல னாக வென்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi answers that by the instrument called the body the Lord knows and tells what those who come to him ask, and asks whether karma can do that; then, though he may be angry, she tells the thera to go into the fire and wishes never to see his like even in a dream.","n":"The curse in lines 3 and 4 marks the debate's heat; it is not an event, only speech.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","yaakkai","sol","vaadhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 433","lb":"NK433","ev":"","cl":[["the Lord speaks and answers through a body, which karma by itself cannot do","jain","asserted",1,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","மொக்கலன்"],"ch":"nk:20"},"nk:434":{"book":"neelakesi","num":433,"t":["முறையினா லறிய லன்னேன் மூத்தலே யிளமை சாக்கா","டுறையல வொருவன் கண்ணே யுடனவை யாக வொட்டி","னிறைவனா ருணர்வு தானுமின்மைமே லெழலும் வேண்டி","யறைதுநா மன்ன மன்னா யன்னண மாக வென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"Mokkalan argues that if the Lord knows not in order but all at once, then old age, youth and death, which never dwell together in one man, would be together in his knowledge, and his knowledge would rise upon what does not exist; let it be so then, swan-like one.","n":"The objection is that knowing past and future at once means knowing what is not now real. The word division of line 4 (அன்ன மன்னாய்) is taken as அன்னம் அன்னாய்.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","gnaanam","ilamai","vaadhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 434","lb":"NK434","ev":"","cl":[["knowledge of all times at once would be knowledge of what does not exist","buddhist","refuted",3,"mokkalan"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["மொக்கலன்","நீலகேசி"],"ch":"nk:20"},"nk:435":{"book":"neelakesi","num":434,"t":["சீலவான் றெய்வ யாக்கைதிண்ணிதா வெய்தி நின்றார்","காலமூன் றானு முய்த்துக்காட்டலுங் காண்டு மன்றோ","ஞாலமூன் றானு மிக்கஞானவா னான நாதன்","போலுமென் றோர்தல் செல்லாய்போர்த்தனை யகமு மென்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi answers that we see those of virtuous conduct who have won firm divine bodies show forth the three times, and he will not grasp that the Lord, wiser than the three worlds, knows them all the more; he has covered his mind.","n":"The argument is a fortiori: if gods who reached heaven by virtue can show the three times, the Lord greater than they can.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","gnaanam","thevar","ozhukkam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 435","lb":"NK435","ev":"","cl":[["if virtuous gods can show the three times, the Lord wiser than the three worlds knows them","jain","asserted",4,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","மொக்கலன்"],"ch":"nk:20"},"nk:436":{"book":"neelakesi","num":435,"t":["நாளெல்லா மாகி நின்றநன்பொரு டம்மை யெல்லாங்","கோளெல்லாந் தானொ ருங்கேகொள்ளுமே லீர்ங்கு வள்ளைத்","தாளெல்லாந் தானொ ருங்கேதானுநல் லானோர் நல்ல","வாளினா லேறு முண்டேல்வாய்க்குநின் னுரையு மென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"Mokkalan argues that a knowledge grasping at once all good things of all days in all their aspects would be like a good man with a good sword cutting all the stalks of the wet water creeper at one stroke; only if that can be would her word hold.","n":"The image seems to be the cutting of many stalks that looks like one act but is a succession of cuts; the lines are hard to construe.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["gnaanam","iraivan","vaadhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 436","lb":"NK436","ev":"","cl":[["no knowing grasps all things in one act; what looks simultaneous is successive","buddhist","refuted",2,"mokkalan"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["மொக்கலன்","நீலகேசி"],"ch":"nk:20"},"nk:437":{"book":"neelakesi","num":436,"t":["நீருநீர் தோறு முவ்வாநலையிற்றே திங்க ளென்று","மூரினூர் தோறு மொவ்வாவொளியிற்றே ஞாயி றென்றும்","யாரின்யார் கேட்ட றீவாரன்னனே யண்ண லென்றார்","தேரனீ சொன்ன தன்னம்சேரல வாக வென்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi asks who ever learned from anyone that the moon is different in each pool of water or the sun has a different light in each town, and says the wise called the Lord such; what the thera said does not hold.","n":"The moon one in all waters and the sun one over all towns answer 436: one undivided knowing is present to all things at once. அன்னம் in line 4 is hard to construe.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","gnaanam","oli"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 437","lb":"NK437","ev":"","cl":[["the Lord's knowing is one and present to all things at once, as one moon is in every water","jain","asserted",3,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","மொக்கலன்"],"ch":"nk:20"},"nk:438":{"book":"neelakesi","num":437,"t":["அளவிலாப் பல்பொ ருள்கட்காகுபண் பாகி நின்ற","வுளவெலாப் பொதுக்கு ணத்தானொருங்குகோ ளீயுமென்னிற்","பிளவெலா மாகு மன்றேபெற்றிதா மொத்த லில்லேற்","கொளவெலா ஞானந் தானுங்கொள்ளுமா றெவன்கொ லென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"Mokkalan asks: if the Lord's knowledge grasps the countless things at once through a common quality present in all, then all their differences would be lost; and if their natures are not alike, how could knowledge take them all?","n":"Neelakesi's reply comes in 439, the first verse of the next run, where she answers that things are grasped by being both alike and unlike.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["gnaanam","iraivan","vaadhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 438","lb":"NK438","ev":"","cl":[["a single grasp of all things either erases their differences through a common quality or cannot hold unlike things together","buddhist","refuted",2,"mokkalan"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["மொக்கலன்","நீலகேசி"],"ch":"nk:20"},"nk:439":{"book":"neelakesi","num":438,"t":["ஒன்றல்லாப் பலபொ ருளுமொத்தொவ்வாப் பெற்றி யாலே","நின்றுகோட் செய்யு மென்றானீடிய குற்ற மாகா","தென்றலா லின்ன தன்மையிறைவன தறிவு மெய்ம்மை","யின்றெலாங் கேட்டு மோராயேடனீ யென்று சொன்னாள்."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi says there is no lasting fault in holding that the Lord's knowledge stands and grasps the many different things by the ways they are alike and unlike, and rebukes Mokkala for not taking in, after all he has heard today, the truth of such knowledge.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arivu","iraivan","vaadham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 439","lb":"NK439","ev":"Neelakesi answers Mokkala's objection to the omniscience of her Lord and rebukes him for not grasping it.","cl":[["the omniscient's knowledge grasps many distinct things through their likeness and difference without fault","jain","asserted",1,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","மொக்கலன்"],"ch":"nk:21"},"nk:440":{"book":"neelakesi","num":439,"t":["எல்லையில் பொருள்க டம்மை எல்லையி லறிவி னாலே","யெல்லையின் றறியு மெங்க ளெல்லையி லறிவ னென்பாய்","எல்லையில் பொருள்க டம்மை யெல்லையின் றறியி னின்ற","வெல்லையி லறிவு தானு மெங்ஙன மெய்து மென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"Mokkala objects that if the boundless knower knows boundless things without limit by boundless knowledge, that knowledge could never reach the end of them.","n":"The verse turns on the word எல்லை, used seven times: an object without end, he argues, cannot be known to its end, so a knowledge that knows it completely contradicts itself.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arivu","vaadham","iraivan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 440","lb":"NK440","ev":"Mokkala objects that infinite objects cannot be known completely.","cl":[["a knowledge of infinitely many things can never be completed, so no one knows all things","buddhist","refuted",4,"mokkalan"]],"ag":["மொக்கலன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["மொக்கலன்","நீலகேசி"],"ch":"nk:21"},"nk:441":{"book":"neelakesi","num":440,"t":["துளக்கில்லாப் பலபொ ருளுந்தொக்கதன் றன்மை யெல்லாம்","விளக்குமே ஞாயி றொப்பவென்பது மேலுஞ் சொன்னேற்","களக்கிவர் தன்ன மன்னாயாத்தன தறிவு மென்றென்","றிளக்கிநீ யின்னு மஃதேசொல்லுதி யேழை யென்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi replies that she has already said the Lord's knowledge lights up the whole nature of the many gathered things at once as the sun does, and calls him a simpleton for repeating the same objection.","n":"Line 3 is hard to divide (களக்கிவர் தன்ன மன்னாய் ஆத்தனது அறிவும்); the image there is unclear, so only the sun simile and the rebuke are read with confidence.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["arivu","iraivan","pedhaimai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 441","lb":"NK441","ev":"Neelakesi repeats that the Lord's knowledge reveals all things like the sun and rebukes Mokkala.","cl":[["the omniscient's knowledge reveals the nature of all things together as the sun lights everything","jain","asserted",2,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","மொக்கலன்"],"ch":"nk:21"},"nk:442":{"book":"neelakesi","num":441,"t":["ஓதலி லுணர்வு மின்றேலூறவற் குண்டு மாகு","மோதலி லுணர்வு முண்டேலொன்றுமே பலவும் வேண்டா","மோதலி லுணர்பொ ரூடாமுள்ளவும் மில்ல வும்மே","லேதமா மில்பொ ருண்மேனிகழ்ச்சிதா னிறைவற் கென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"Mokkala presses a dilemma: if the Lord has no knowledge of what is not present he is defective, if he has it then nothing more is needed, and since the things so known include what does not exist, his knowledge would be working on non-existent things, which is a fault.","n":"The repeated ஓதலில் is the crux; I take it as knowledge of what is not before him (past and future things), which 443's answer about gone and coming qualities supports, but the word could be read otherwise.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["arivu","vaadham","iraivan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 442","lb":"NK442","ev":"Mokkala argues that knowing past and future things would mean knowing what does not exist.","cl":[["to know past and future things is to know what does not exist, which is a fault in knowledge","buddhist","refuted",4,"mokkalan"]],"ag":["மொக்கலன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["மொக்கலன்","நீலகேசி"],"ch":"nk:21"},"nk:443":{"book":"neelakesi","num":442,"t":["சென்றவக் குணங்க டாமுஞ்செல்லுமக் குணங்க டாமு","மன்றையக் குணங்க டாமுமப்பொருட் டன்மை யாலே","நின்றதன் ஞானந் தன்னானிருமல னுணரு மென்றாற்","பொன்றின வெதிர்வ வென்றல்பொருள்களுக் கில்லை யென்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi answers that the stainless one knows by his abiding knowledge the qualities that have gone, those that will come and those of today through the nature of the substance itself, so past and future are not lacking in things.","n":"The answer rests on the Jain view that a substance carries its past and future modes, so the omniscient does not know a non-existent but an aspect of what is; it meets 442 directly.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arivu","iraivan","kaalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 443","lb":"NK443","ev":"","cl":[["past and future qualities belong to the nature of a substance, so the omniscient knows them without knowing non-existents","jain","asserted",4,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","மொக்கலன்"],"ch":"nk:21"},"nk:444":{"book":"neelakesi","num":443,"t":["பிறவிதா னொன்று மில்லான்பெரியனே யென்று நின்றான்","மறவிதா னில்லை யோனிமன்னுநான் கென்னு மில்லா","னறவியா யுந்த நூலுள் ளாத்தனா மாயி னக்காற்","புறவினிற் புரளுங் கல்லும்புண்ணிய னாக வென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"Mokkala mocks the Jain marks of the Lord: if one is the Aptha in your scripture because he has no birth, no forgetting and none of the four wombs, then the stone that rolls about in the open would be a holy one too.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","piravi","vaadham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 444","lb":"NK444","ev":"Mokkala argues that the Jain marks of lordship, being absences, would make a stone a lord.","cl":[["a lord defined by having no birth and no forgetting is no better than a stone","buddhist","refuted",4,"mokkalan"]],"ag":["மொக்கலன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["மொக்கலன்","நீலகேசி"],"ch":"nk:21"},"nk:445":{"book":"neelakesi","num":444,"t":["பிறத்தலே தலைமை யாயிற்பிள்ளைக ளல்ல தென்னை","யறக்கெட றான தென்னிலட்டக வித்து வெந்தாம்","புறப்படும் போர்வை யாலேற்புண்டொழு நோய ராகச்","சிறப்புடை யண்ண றன்னைக்கல்லெனச் சொல்லு வாய்க்கே."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi retorts that if being born made one great, children would be greatest; the Lord is great because the eightfold seeds are utterly burnt; and she wishes on those who call the excellent Lord a stone that they become lepers with sores breaking through the skin.","n":"The eightfold seeds (அட்டக வித்து) are read as the eight kinds of karma, so lordship is defined by what has been destroyed, not by mere absence of birth. Lines 3 and 4 read as a curse on the mocker, but the syntax is loose and the curse is my reading.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","vinai","piravi","kadunjol"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 445","lb":"NK445","ev":"Neelakesi rebuts Mokkala's stone argument and curses those who call her Lord a stone.","cl":[["the Lord's greatness is that the eight seeds of karma are burnt away, not mere absence of birth","jain","asserted",2,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","மொக்கலன்"],"ch":"nk:21"},"nk:446":{"book":"neelakesi","num":445,"t":["அடைவிலா யோனி யானாயாருமொப் பாரு மின்றிக்","கடையிலா ஞான மெய்திக்கணங்கணான் மூன்றுஞ் சூழ்ந்து","புடையெலாம் போற்றி யேத்தப்பொன்னெயிற் பிண்டி மூன்று","குடையினா னிறைவ னென்றாற்குற்றமிங் கென்னை யென்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi asks what fault there is in calling Lord the one who has no womb to enter, has no equal, has reached endless knowledge, and is praised on every side by the twelve assemblies under the pindi tree within golden walls, beneath the triple umbrella.","n":"The golden walls, the pindi (asoka) tree and the three umbrellas are the marks of the Jina's preaching hall; she answers the stone jibe by adding positive marks to the negative ones.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","arivu","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 446","lb":"NK446","ev":"","cl":[["the Lord is the one free of rebirth who has gained endless knowledge and is worshipped by the twelve assemblies","jain","asserted",2,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","மொக்கலன்"],"ch":"nk:21"},"nk:447":{"book":"neelakesi","num":446,"t":["கோதியிட் டுள்ள தெல்லாங்குண்டல கேசி யென்பா","ளாதிசா லாவ ணத்துளார்கதர் தம்மை வென்ற","வீதியீ தென்று சொல்லிவீழ்ந்தனை நீயு மென்றா","ணீதியாற் சொல்லி வென்றநீலமா கேசி நல்லாள்."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi, who won by speaking with justice, tells Mokkala that what he has sifted and put forward is the line by which Kundalakesi once defeated the Jains in the great hall, and that he too has fallen.","n":"Lines 1 and 2 (கோதியிட்டு, ஆதிசாலாவணத்துள் ஆர்கதர்) are hard; I read ஆர்கதர் as the Arhat's followers and the place as a hall or market of debate, which makes Mokkala's arguments a reuse of Kundalakesi's; the verse may instead say Kundalakesi was defeated there.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vaadham","samayam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 447","lb":"NK447","ev":"Neelakesi declares Mokkala defeated, saying his arguments were the ones Kundalakesi had used.","cl":[],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","மொக்கலன்"],"ch":"nk:21"},"nk:448":{"book":"neelakesi","num":447,"t":["பேதைக ளுரைப்பன வேசொல்லிப்பெரிதலப் பாட்டினைநீ","பேதைமற் றிவன்பெரி தெனப்படும்கருத்துடை மிகுதியினாய்","தாதையைத் தலைவனைத் தத்துவதரிசியைத் தவநெறியி","னீதியை யருளிய நிருமலன்றகைநினக் குரைப்ப னென்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi tells Mokkala that he has babbled at length only what fools say and has a mind so swollen that men call him a great fool, and announces that she will tell him the worth of the stainless one, the father, the leader, the seer of reality who taught the justice of the path of tapas.","n":"The metre changes here, and the next seven verses are a hymn to the Jina in short four-syllable feet.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","pedhaimai","thavam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 448","lb":"NK448","ev":"Neelakesi begins a hymn on the qualities of her Lord.","cl":[],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","மொக்கலன்"],"ch":"nk:21"},"nk:449":{"book":"neelakesi","num":448,"t":["பகைபசி பிணியொடு பரிவின பலகெட","முகைமலர் தளிரொடு முறிமரம் வெறிசெய","மிசைநிலம் விளைவெய்த விழைவொடு மகிழ்வன","திசைதொறு மிவைபிற சுகதன செலவே."],"p":"","r":"The hymn says that wherever the Lord goes, enmity, hunger, disease and the many sorrows perish, trees burst into bud, flower, shoot and fragrance, the land yields its harvest, and all rejoice in every direction.","n":"The Lord is called சுகதன், a name usually given to the Buddha; either the hymn claims the title for the Jina or line 4 says these marvels are other than the Buddha's, and I cannot settle which.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","pasi","pagai","noy"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 449","lb":"NK449","ev":"","cl":[["the Jina's going through the land ends enmity, hunger and disease","jain","asserted",1,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","மொக்கலன்"],"ch":"nk:21"},"nk:450":{"book":"neelakesi","num":449,"t":["குழுவன பிரிவன குறைவில நிலையின","எழுவன விழுவன விறுதியி லியல்பின","வழுவலில் பொருள்களை மலர்கையின் மணியென","முழுவது முணருமெ முனைவர னறிவே."],"p":"","r":"The hymn says our Lord's knowledge knows wholly, like a gem in the open palm, all the unerring things that combine and separate, that never fail in their permanence, that rise and fall, and whose nature is without end.","n":"Things are described at once as abiding (நிலையின) and as rising and falling (எழுவன விழுவன): the Jain view that a thing is permanent as substance and changing as mode, set against the Buddhist claim of momentariness.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arivu","iraivan","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 450","lb":"NK450","ev":"","cl":[["things are both permanent and arising and perishing, and the omniscient knows them all as plainly as a gem in the palm","jain","asserted",4,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","மொக்கலன்"],"ch":"nk:21"},"nk:451":{"book":"neelakesi","num":450,"t":["நிறைபொறி யுளவவை யறிதலி னெறிமைய","முறைபொரு ணிகழினு முறைபடு மறிவிலன்","மறைபொரு ளுளவவ னறிவினை மறையல","விறைபொருண் முழுவது மறிதிற மிதுவே."],"p":"","r":"The hymn says those with full senses know through them and in order, but the Lord is not one whose knowledge goes by order even when things happen in order; hidden things exist but do not hide from his knowledge, and this is how he knows all.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arivu","iraivan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 451","lb":"NK451","ev":"","cl":[["the omniscient's knowledge does not depend on the senses and does not proceed in sequence","jain","asserted",2,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","மொக்கலன்"],"ch":"nk:21"},"nk:452":{"book":"neelakesi","num":451,"t":["பிணிதரு பிறவிய மறுசுழி யறுவதொர்","துணிவிது வெனநம துயர்கெடு முறைமையு","மணிதரு சிவகதி யடைதலு மருளுதல்","பணிதரு பரமன தருள்படு வகையே."],"p":"","r":"The hymn says the grace of the supreme one to whom all bow shows the sure way to cut the whirlpool of the sea of disease-bringing births, the way our sorrow ends, and the attainment of the jewel-like state of Siva.","n":"சிவகதி here is the auspicious state of the released soul, not union with Siva; the hymn shares the Saiva words அருள், பரமன், சிவகதி while giving them Jain sense.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["veedu","piravi","arul"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 452","lb":"NK452","ev":"","cl":[["the supreme one's teaching shows the way to cut the cycle of births and reach the auspicious state","jain","asserted",4,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","மொக்கலன்"],"ch":"nk:21"},"nk:453":{"book":"neelakesi","num":452,"t":["சொரிவன மலர்மழை துளிகளு நறுவிரை","புரிவன வமரர்கள் புகழ்தகு குணமிவை","விரிவன துதியொலி விளைவது சிவகதி","எரிவன மணியிதெ மிறைவன திடமே."],"p":"","r":"The hymn names the signs of the Lord's presence: showers of flowers and drops of fragrance, the deeds of the gods, the praiseworthy qualities, the spreading sound of praise from which the state of Siva follows, and the blazing gem-set seat.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","iraivan","veedu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 453","lb":"NK453","ev":"","cl":[["the praise of the Lord brings about the auspicious state","jain","asserted",3,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","மொக்கலன்"],"ch":"nk:21"},"nk:454":{"book":"neelakesi","num":453,"t":["அரசரு மமரரு மமர்வனர் வினவலின்","வரைவில பிறர்களு மனநிலை மகிழ்வெய்த","உரைபல வகையினு முளபொரு ளுணரவொர்","முரைசென வதிருமெ முனைவரன் மொழியே."],"p":"","r":"The hymn says that when kings, gods and countless others sit and ask, our Lord's speech thunders like a drum so that all of them, rejoicing, understand in many ways the things that are.","n":"The speech heard by every listener in his own way is the Jina's divine sound, one of his marks.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","sol","kelvi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 454","lb":"NK454","ev":"","cl":[["the Lord's single speech is understood by every hearer according to his kind","jain","asserted",3,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","மொக்கலன்"],"ch":"nk:21"},"nk:455":{"book":"neelakesi","num":454,"t":["வினையிரு ளடுவன விரிகதி ரியல்பொடு","கனையிருள் கடிவன கடுநவை யடுவன","மனையிரு ணெறிபெற மதிகெட வடைவன","வினையமெ யிறையவ னிணையடி யிவையே."],"p":"","r":"The hymn says these are the twin feet of our Lord, which with spreading rays destroy the darkness of deeds, drive away dense darkness, defeat cruel faults, and are reached by those who seek the path.","n":"Line 3 (மனையிருள் நெறிபெற மதிகெட அடைவன) is obscure; and வினையம் in line 4 may be 'discipline' or a play on வினை, deeds.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thiruvadi","vinai","iraivan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 455","lb":"NK455","ev":"","cl":[["the Lord's feet destroy the darkness of karma","jain","asserted",1,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","மொக்கலன்"],"ch":"nk:21"},"nk:456":{"book":"neelakesi","num":455,"t":["ஆத்த னிவனென் றடிக ளிடமிசைப்","பூத்தனைத் தூவிப் பொருந்து துதிகளி","னேத்துநர் கண்டா யிருவினை யுங்கெடப்","பாத்தில் சிவகதிப் பான்மைய ரென்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi ends the hymn: those who, saying this is the Aptha, strew flowers on the place of his feet and praise him with fitting hymns lose both kinds of deed and come to the undivided state of Siva.","n":"Both kinds of deed (இருவினை), good and bad, must perish; the verse makes worship of the Aptha a cause of that, a stronger claim for devotion than the debate verses make.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","vinai","veedu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 456","lb":"NK456","ev":"Neelakesi closes her hymn by telling what reward the Aptha's worshippers receive.","cl":[["those who worship the Aptha with flowers and praise lose both kinds of deed and reach release","jain","asserted",3,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","மொக்கலன்"],"ch":"nk:21"},"nk:457":{"book":"neelakesi","num":456,"t":["ஏந்த றிறங்க ளிவையே லமைந்தன","போந்த வகையாற் பொருளும் பிழைப்பில","வீந்த விவற்றினின் வேற்றுமை வீட்டிற்கு","மாய்ந்த வகையா லறிவிமற் றென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"Mokkala grants that if these are the Lord's powers they are fitting and the matters as set out are without error, and asks her to explain, as she has examined it, how release differs from what has simply perished.","n":"The question seems to be whether Jain release is anything other than extinction; the reading of வீந்த இவற்றின் வேற்றுமை is doubtful.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["veedu","vaadham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 457","lb":"NK457","ev":"Mokkala concedes the Lord's powers and asks what release is.","cl":[],"ag":["மொக்கலன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["மொக்கலன்","நீலகேசி"],"ch":"nk:21"},"nk:458":{"book":"neelakesi","num":457,"t":["வித்தென்றும் வெந்தால் முளையல தாயெண்மை","யொத்தினி துண்டா முயிரும் பிறப்பின்றிச்","சித்தி யகத்துச் சிதைவிலெண் டன்மையி","னித்திய மாகி நிலையுள தென்னாய்."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi answers that as a burnt seed never sprouts, the soul, having become the same in the eight qualities and free of birth, abides permanently in the state of perfection with its eightfold nature intact.","n":"The burnt seed answers 457: release is not perishing, since the soul remains; what is burnt is only the capacity to be born.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["veedu","uyir","piravi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 458","lb":"NK458","ev":"","cl":[["the released soul, like a burnt seed, is never born again and abides eternally in perfection with eight qualities","jain","asserted",4,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","மொக்கலன்"],"ch":"nk:21"},"nk:459":{"book":"neelakesi","num":458,"t":["ஒக்கு மிதுவென வுள்ளங் குளிர்ந்தின","மொக்கலன் சொல்லுமிம் மோக்கத்தைப் பாழ்செய்த","தக்கில தாகுந் தலைவ ரியல்பென","நக்கன னாய்க்கென்று நன்னுத லென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"Mokkala, his heart cooled, says this is right, and that the nature of his leaders who ruined this release is unworthy, and he laughs at himself.","n":"Line 4 (நக்கனனாய்க்கென்று நன்னுதல் என்றான்) is obscure; நக்கனன் may be 'he laughed' or a form of நக்கன், the naked Jain ascetic, so it is unclear whether he laughs or addresses Neelakesi.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["veedu","samayam","thelivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 459","lb":"NK459","ev":"Mokkala accepts the Jain account of release and disowns his own teachers' doctrine.","cl":[["the Buddhist teachers' doctrine ruined the idea of release","jain","asserted",3,"mokkalan"]],"ag":["மொக்கலன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["மொக்கலன்","நீலகேசி"],"ch":"nk:21"},"nk:460":{"book":"neelakesi","num":459,"t":["பண்டே யெனக்கிம் மயக்கம் பயந்தவன்","கண்டார் மயங்குங் கபில புரமென்ப","துண்டாங் கதனகத் தோத்துரைக் கின்றனன்","றண்டா தவனொடு தாக்கெனச் சொல்லி."],"p":"","r":"Mokkala tells her that the one who first gave him this delusion is in Kapilapuram, the city that bewilders those who see it, teaching his doctrine there, and bids her go and fight him without flinching.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vaadham","guru","mayal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 460","lb":"NK460","ev":"Mokkala sends Neelakesi to Kapilapuram to debate the teacher who taught him.","cl":[],"ag":["மொக்கலன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["மொக்கலன்","நீலகேசி"],"ch":"nk:21"},"nk:461":{"book":"neelakesi","num":460,"t":["சிறப்பின தென்பதைச் செப்பலுந் தெற்றெனப்","பிறப்பறுத் தின்பெய்தும் பெற்றியின் மிக்க","வறப்புணை யாகிய வாயிழை யாயான்","மறப்பில னென்று வலஞ்செய் தொழிந்தான்."],"p":"","r":"As soon as she declared the excellence of the Jain way, Mokkala said at once that he would never forget her, the jewelled woman who is a raft of dharma with the power to cut off birth and bring bliss, and he circled her and departed.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aram","piravi","panivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 461","lb":"NK461","ev":"Mokkala honours Neelakesi by circling her and takes his leave.","cl":[["the dharma she teaches is a raft that cuts off birth and brings bliss","jain","asserted",2,"mokkalan"]],"ag":["மொக்கலன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["மொக்கலன்","நீலகேசி"],"ch":"nk:21"},"nk:462":{"book":"neelakesi","num":461,"t":["அருளே யுடைய னறனே யறிவா","டெருளா தவரைத் தெருட்டல் லதுவே","பொருளா வுடையாள் புலனே நிறைந்தாள்","இருடீர் சுடர்போ லெழுந்தா ளவன்மேல்."],"p":"","r":"She who has grace, whose knowledge is dharma, whose only wealth is enlightening those who do not understand, and whose senses are full, rose like a flame that ends darkness and went against him.","n":"அவன்மேல் at the end points forward to the Buddha in Kapilapuram named in 460, and closes the sarukkam.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arul","aram","arivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 462","lb":"NK462","ev":"Neelakesi rises and sets out against the Buddha.","cl":[],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"ch":"nk:21"},"nk:463":{"book":"neelakesi","num":462,"t":["அணிநா டிவைதா மறல்யா றிவைதாம்","பிணிநா டிவைதாம் பெருங்கா டிவைதாம்","மணிமா மலைதா மெனவே வருவாள்","அணியார் சுகதன் நகரெய் தினளே."],"p":"","r":"Naming the fair lands, the rivers, the lands of sickness, the great burning grounds and the jewelled mountains as she passes them, Neelakesi reaches the beautiful city of Sugata, the Buddha.","n":"The journey is told as her own pointing speech (இவைதாம் repeated), so the land is seen through the traveller's eyes; சுகதன் (line 4) names the Buddha by the title Sugata, which marks the town as his own.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naadu","puththan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 463","lb":"NK463","ev":"Neelakesi travels through lands, rivers, forests and mountains and arrives at the Buddha's city.","cl":[],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"ch":"nk:22"},"nk:464":{"book":"neelakesi","num":463,"t":["பறையின் னொலியும் படுகண் டிகையுஞ்","சிறையின் மிகுமா லிதுசெம் படர்கள்","இறைவன் னுறையு மிடமா மெனலும்."],"p":"","r":"Amid the sound of drums and the ringing of bells, someone says that this, greater than its walls, is the place where the lord of the red-robed ones dwells.","n":"The verse has only three lines and the speaker of எனலும் is not named; whether it is Neelakesi herself or someone on the road who points out the town cannot be fixed. செம்படர்கள் (line 2) seems to name the ochre-robed monks, but the sense of சிறையின் மிகுமால் is unclear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["puththan","idam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 464","lb":"NK464","ev":"Someone points out the Buddha's dwelling place as Neelakesi approaches.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"nk:22"},"nk:465":{"book":"neelakesi","num":464,"t":["மழைசேர் நகரம் மலைபோன் றனவே","கழைசேர் கொடியுங் கதலிவ் வனமே","விழைதா ரவரும் விரிகோ தையரும்","முழைவாழ் புலியே மயிலே மொழியின்."],"p":"","r":"The description of the city goes on: buildings like rain-clouded mountains, flags on bamboo poles, groves of plantain, and men wearing garlands and women with spread tresses who, if one must name them, are tigers of the caves and peacocks.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naadu","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 465","lb":"NK465","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"nk:22"},"nk:466":{"book":"neelakesi","num":465,"t":["நெடுவெண் டிரைமே னிமிருந் திமிலுங்","கடுவெஞ் செலவின் னுலவுங் கலனும்","படுவண் டறையும் பொழிலும் மெழிலார்","மடுவுந் திடரும் மணல்வார் புறவும்"],"p":"","r":"The catalogue of the land continues with the coast: fishing boats riding the tall white waves, swift ships moving, groves loud with bees, and fair ponds, mounds and sandy jungle tracts.","n":"The verse has no main verb; its list runs on into 467, where எனவும் closes it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naadu","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 466","lb":"NK466","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"nk:22"},"nk:467":{"book":"neelakesi","num":466,"t":["கயன்மீ னிரியக் கழுநீர் விரியும்","வயன்மாண் புடைய வளமைத் தெனவும்","முயன்மீ னெறியு முறியுங் கறியா","தயன்மே யுறையா தணியிற் றெனவும்."],"p":"","r":"It is said too to be a land of rich fields where kayal fish leap and water lilies open, and a land adorned in ways the following lines describe obscurely.","n":"Lines 3 and 4 (முயன்மீ னெறியு முறியுங் கறியா தயன்மே யுறையா தணியிற்று) resist parsing; they seem to praise the land's order or plenty, but no single sense can be fixed.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["naadu","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 467","lb":"NK467","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"nk:22"},"nk:468":{"book":"neelakesi","num":467,"t":["கழுகின் னினமும் கழுதின் னினமும்","முழுதும் மறுவை பலமூ டினரும்","கொழுதின் னிணனும் பிணனுங் குலவி","இழுதென் னெலும்பா ரிடுகா டெனவும்."],"p":"","r":"It is said also to hold a burning ground where flocks of vultures and demons gather, shrouded corpses lie, fat and bodies mingle, and bones are heaped up.","n":"The burning ground stands in the same list as the coast and the fields, without comment; in a work whose heroine was a demon of the burning ground (the opening sarukkams), the place is part of the ordinary landscape rather than a horror.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sudalai","yaakkai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 468","lb":"NK468","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"nk:22"},"nk:469":{"book":"neelakesi","num":468,"t":["சாதியே மிக்க தடுமாற்ற வெந்துயர","மோதியே வைத்தாரவ் வோத்தெலா மீக்கிடந்த","வீதியே காணலா மென்றாளா னின்றாரும்","போதியா ரீண்டைப் புலால்பழியா ரென்றலும்."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi says that the very street shows what became of the scriptures that taught the cruel sorrow of wandering through births, and those standing there answer that the Buddhists here do not condemn flesh.","n":"The verse turns on the reading of ஆனின்றாரும் (line 3) as 'those standing there', which makes line 4 a bystanders' report; it could also be Neelakesi's own sarcasm. The charge that flesh-eating is allowed is answered at once in 470 to 474.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["piravi","pulaal","samayam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 469","lb":"NK469","ev":"In the Buddha's town Neelakesi remarks on the street, and bystanders tell her the Buddhists there do not condemn meat.","cl":[["the Buddhist scriptures teach the great sorrow of wandering through births","buddhist","reported",1,"neelakesi"],["eating flesh bought in the market is not blameworthy","buddhist","refuted",4,"onlookers"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி","அருகிருந்தோர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","அருகிருந்தோர்"],"ch":"nk:22"},"nk:470":{"book":"neelakesi","num":469,"t":["அங்காடிப் பண்டவூன்றின்ன வறமுரைத்தார்க்","கிங்காடி வாழ்வனவுமூனாய்வந் தீண்டியவாற்","கொங்காடத் தேனறையுங்கொய்ம்மருதம் பூவணிந்த","பொங்காடை போர்த்தார்க்குப்பொல்லாதே யென்னீரோ."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi asks the bystanders whether they will not call it evil for the robed ones who preached dharma to eat flesh from the market, when creatures living all around have come there as meat.","n":"The robe is described lavishly (dyed like marutham flowers where bees hum), so the finery of the dress is set against the stall of meat; the charge is about the gap between preaching dharma and buying flesh, not only about diet.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pulaal","arul","koodaozhukkam","vesham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 470","lb":"NK470","ev":"","cl":[["those who preach dharma yet eat flesh bought in the market do evil, since the creatures were killed for that market","jain","asserted",4,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","அருகிருந்தோர்"],"ch":"nk:22"},"nk:471":{"book":"neelakesi","num":470,"t":["விலைபடைத்தா ரூன்வேண்ட வவ்விலைதான் வேண்டி","வலைபடைத்தார்க் கெம்முயிரை வைக்கின்றா மின்ன","கொலைபடைத்தா னோகொடிய னென்பனவே போலத்","தலையெடுத்து வாய்திறப்ப தாமிவையோ காணீர்."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi points to the animals in the market, which seem to lift their heads and open their mouths to say that because buyers with money want flesh the netters lay down their lives for that price, so is the killer alone the cruel one?","n":"The argument is given to the victims: the slaughtered beasts themselves seem to blame the buyer, which turns an economic argument (price draws the net) into an accusation spoken by the dead.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pulaal","kolai","kollaamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 471","lb":"NK471","ev":"","cl":[["the buyers' demand for flesh is what makes the hunter kill, so the killer is not the only culprit","jain","asserted",1,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","அருகிருந்தோர்"],"ch":"nk:22"},"nk:472":{"book":"neelakesi","num":471,"t":["தன்றார மீந்தான் றனக்குறுதி யாவதனை","யொன்றானும் வேண்டான் பிறர்க்கே யுழந்தானூன்","தின்றானுந் தீவினையைச் சேருமெனச் சொன்னாற்","பொன்றாவாய்ப் பல்விலங்கும் பூமிமேல் வாழாவோ."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi says that the teacher who gave away his own wife and laboured only for others, wanting nothing for himself, had only to say that the one who eats flesh also gathers evil karma, and the many animals on earth would have lived without dying.","n":"The Buddha's legendary self-giving (the gift of his wife, a former-birth story) is granted in full, and then used against him: his compassion stopped short of the one word that would have saved the animals. The praise is the weapon.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pulaal","vinai","eegai","puththan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 472","lb":"NK472","ev":"","cl":[["the Buddha in a former life gave away his own wife and toiled only for others","buddhist","reported",1,"neelakesi"],["the one who eats flesh, not only the one who kills, incurs evil karma","jain","asserted",3,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","அருகிருந்தோர்"],"ch":"nk:22"},"nk:473":{"book":"neelakesi","num":472,"t":["உரிதா வுணர்ந்தானொன் றோரா துரையான்","பரிவே யிதுவுந்தன் பாலரோ டெல்லா","மெரிதோய் நரகம்பா ழேற்றுவா னேயாம்","பெரிதா மளியன் பெருந்தகைய னேகாண்."],"p":"","r":"With irony Neelakesi calls the Buddha a knower who would not speak without reflection, and says this too is his kindness, that he will lift all, together with his own children, into the burning hell: truly a most gracious and noble one.","n":"Every term of praise (பெரிதாம் அளியன், பெருந்தகையன்) is meant in reverse; the verse says the teacher's permission of flesh is a fault of knowledge, not a lapse, since he 'knew rightly'.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naragam","pulaal","arul","puththan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 473","lb":"NK473","ev":"","cl":[["a teacher who permits flesh-eating leads himself and his disciples into the fiery hell","jain","asserted",3,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","அருகிருந்தோர்"],"ch":"nk:22"},"nk:474":{"book":"neelakesi","num":473,"t":["கொடைக்கொட்டி விற்பானுங் கொள்வானு மன்றி","யிடைச்செட்டி னாற்பொருளை யெய்துவான் போல","முடைக்கொட்டி முத்துரைத்து மூடிக்கொண் டேகுங்","குடைச்சிட்ட னாருயிர்க்கோர் கூற்றமே கண்டீர்."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi says that, like a middleman who neither sells nor buys outright but takes his profit between them, the umbrella-bearing monk who piles up foul meat, recites his threefold excuse and goes off covered in his robe is a very Death to living beings.","n":"முத்துரைத்து (line 3), 'speaking three', is read here as the threefold test by which meat is pure for a monk (not seen, heard or suspected to be killed for him); that reading is likely but not certain from the verse. The simile matters: the middleman profits from a trade he does not perform, as the eater profits from a killing he does not do.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pulaal","kolai","kootru"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 474","lb":"NK474","ev":"","cl":[["meat is pure for a monk when three conditions show it was not killed for him","buddhist","refuted",3,"neelakesi"],["the eater who neither kills nor sells still shares the killing, like a middleman who profits from both sides","jain","asserted",2,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","அருகிருந்தோர்"],"ch":"nk:22"},"nk:475":{"book":"neelakesi","num":474,"t":["ஆடுவார் காண்பா ரவரருகே தான்சென்று","தோடுவார்ந் தாலொப்பச் சொல்விரிப்பான் போற்பாவம்","கூடுவார் கூடாதார் கொன்றார்தின் றாரென்னும்","சேடனார்க் காண்டுநா மென்றுதான் சென்றாளே."],"p":"","r":"Saying 'let us go and see the master who, like one who comes up beside dancers and watchers and spins out matching words, sorts out who incurs sin and who does not, the killer or the eater', Neelakesi goes on.","n":"The teacher is mocked as a talker who fits his words to whoever is listening; the claim she means to test, that the sin is the killer's and not the eater's, has already been answered in 471 and 474.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pulaal","vinai","kolai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 475","lb":"NK475","ev":"Neelakesi resolves to go and confront the Buddha himself and sets out.","cl":[["the sin of killing falls on the one who killed, not on the one who eats","buddhist","refuted",3,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","அருகிருந்தோர்"],"ch":"nk:22"},"nk:476":{"book":"neelakesi","num":475,"t":["அணிசெய் கோழரை யரைநிழ லழகனைப் பொருந்தி","மணிக டாம்பல கதிர்விடு மலருடை மணைமேற்","றுணிவு தோற்றினை யெனச்சிலர் துதியொடு தொழுது","பணிய யாதுமோர் பரிவிலன் படம்புதைத் திருந்தான்."],"p":"","r":"Under the shade of the fair peepal the handsome teacher sits on a flowered seat that sheds the rays of many gems; some praise and worship him, saying he has shown resolve, but he sits wrapped in his robe, moved by nothing.","n":"பரிவிலன் (line 4) can be read as calm detachment or as coldness to his worshippers; the narrator does not say which, and the robe 'buried' around him (படம் புதைத்து) keeps the picture ambiguous.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["puththan","vazhipaadu","pattru"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 476","lb":"NK476","ev":"Devotees worship the Buddha seated under the peepal tree, and he sits unmoved.","cl":[],"ag":["புத்தனார்","சிலர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"nk:22"},"nk:477":{"book":"neelakesi","num":476,"t":["ஆத்த னேயெனத் தெளிந்தவ ணமர்ந்திருந் தவர்க்குச்","சூத்தி ரம்மிது வினையமு மிதுவிது பிறிதாஞ்","சாத்தி ரம்மிவை மூன்றென வன்றவத் தோன்றல்","பாத்து ரைக்குந்தன் பதப்பொருள் பலவகைப் படவே."],"p":"","r":"To those who sit there sure that he is the trustworthy teacher, the noble one divides his doctrine's meaning many ways, saying this is the Sutra, this the Vinaya, this the other, the Shastra, these three.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["puththan","guru","samayam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 477","lb":"NK477","ev":"The Buddha expounds his teaching to the assembled devotees.","cl":[["the teaching is set out in three scriptures, the Sutra, the Vinaya and the Shastra","buddhist","reported",2,"buddha"]],"ag":["புத்தனார்","சிலர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"nk:22"},"nk:478":{"book":"neelakesi","num":477,"t":["கந்த மைந்திவை கணிகத்த வாமெனக் கரைந்து","முந்தி நாடினோ ருணரவல்ல தில்லையென் றுரைத்தும்","புந்தி யாலங்கோர் புற்கல னுளனெனப் புணர்த்து","மந்தி லாற்சொலாப் பாட்டினோ டியாதுமில் லெனவும்."],"p":"","r":"He goes on to declare that the five aggregates are momentary, that there is nothing that knows having first examined, and yet joins by reasoning that there is a person (pudgala), and also that apart from what cannot be spoken there is nothing.","n":"The verse is built to show the doctrines at odds: momentary aggregates and no lasting knower set against a person that is somehow there. Line 4 (மந்திலாற் சொலாப் பாட்டினோடு) is obscure; 'the unsayable' is read from 480's அவாச்சியம். This is the Jain poet's account of Buddhist teaching; a person held to be inexpressible belongs to one Buddhist school, not to all. The claims are answered in 479 to 482.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kandham","nilaiyaamai","uyir","vaadhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 478","lb":"NK478","ev":"","cl":[["the five aggregates are momentary","buddhist","refuted",1,"buddha"],["there is no knower that persists from one moment to the next","buddhist","refuted",2,"buddha"],["there is nonetheless a person (pudgala)","buddhist","refuted",3,"buddha"]],"ag":["புத்தனார்"],"ec":[],"ch":"nk:22"},"nk:479":{"book":"neelakesi","num":478,"t":["தத்து வம்மிவை தாமெனத் தமர்களுக் குரைக்கும்","புத்த னார்தம்மை புயலிருங் கூந்தலி பொருந்திப்","பித்தர் போற்பல பிதற்றினீர் பிதற்றிய விவைதா","மெத்தி றத்தினு மிசையின்மை யிசைக்குவ னென்னெவெ"],"p":"","r":"The dark-haired one comes up to the Buddha as he tells his followers that these are the true principles, and says: you babbled many things like a madman, and I will show that what you babbled agrees in no way.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vaadhu","puththan","thaththuvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 479","lb":"NK479","ev":"Neelakesi approaches the Buddha and challenges his teaching.","cl":[["the Buddha's doctrines contradict one another in every way","jain","asserted",4,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி","புத்தனார்"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","புத்தனார்"],"ch":"nk:22"},"nk:480":{"book":"neelakesi","num":479,"t":["ஓதி னீர்சொன்ன கந்தமைந் துளவெனி னுருவே","வேத னையொடு குறிசெய்கை யிலவென விரிப்பும்","போதியா லங்கோர் புற்கல னுளனெனப் புணர்ப்பும்","யாது மின்மையோ டவாச்சிய மறும்பிற வெனவே."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi lists the contradictions: he says the five aggregates exist, yet expounds that form with feeling, perception and formation does not exist; he joins a person by reasoning; and he speaks of nothingness and of the inexpressible besides.","n":"Line 3 repeats 478's line 3 almost word for word but with போதியால் for புந்தியால்; one is likely a copyist's slip.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kandham","vaadhu","uyir"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 480","lb":"NK480","ev":"","cl":[["one cannot hold both that the aggregates exist and that form, feeling, perception and formation do not","jain","asserted",2,"neelakesi"],["there is a person (pudgala) besides the aggregates","buddhist","refuted",3,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","புத்தனார்"],"ch":"nk:22"},"nk:481":{"book":"neelakesi","num":480,"t":["ஐந்தில் யான்சொன்ன பலகளு மமைவில வெனினும்","கந்த முண்மைக்கட் கருத்துள தாம்பிற வதனாற்","சிந்த மாயவு முளவெனத் தெளியினி யெனலும்","நந்த னார்க்கற முரைத்திர்நீ ரோவென நக்காள்."],"p":"","r":"The Buddha answers that even if many of the things he said of the five do not fit, there is a point in the reality of the aggregates, so the mental states too exist and she should be clear on it; Neelakesi laughs and asks whether he is the one who preached dharma to Nanda.","n":"The Buddha concedes ground (அமைவில எனினும்) before he holds it, which the poet means as a first retreat. Neelakesi's laugh names Nanda, the Buddha's kinsman whom tradition says he converted, as if to ask how such a muddled teacher won anyone.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kandham","vaadhu","puththan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 481","lb":"NK481","ev":"The Buddha partly concedes and defends the reality of the aggregates; Neelakesi laughs at him.","cl":[["though some of its divisions fail, the aggregates are real and so the mental states exist","buddhist","refuted",2,"buddha"]],"ag":["புத்தனார்","நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["புத்தனார்","நீலகேசி"],"ch":"nk:22"},"nk:482":{"book":"neelakesi","num":481,"t":["இட்ட நீபல வுரைத்தனை யிவற்றுளொன் றொழிய","நட்ட மாயினு நன்மையை நின்வயிற் றருவோய்","குட்ட மேமுழு மெய்யினு மெழுந்தவன குடுமி","தொட்டி யானெனி னுந்தூய னோவது மாமோ."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi says that he has spoken many tenets of his choosing, and if all but one were lost and he claimed goodness from that one, it would be like a man covered head to foot in leprosy who touches only his clean tuft of hair: is he pure?","n":"The simile answers 481's concession: one sound tenet cannot clean a doctrine that is sick everywhere else.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vaadhu","kutram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 482","lb":"NK482","ev":"","cl":[["one sound tenet does not make a doctrine sound when the rest of it is faulty","general","asserted",3,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","புத்தனார்"],"ch":"nk:22"},"nk:483":{"book":"neelakesi","num":482,"t":["நல்ல வேயென நாட்டிய கந்தமிவ் வைந்து","மில்ல வேயெனத் தெருட்டுவ னெடுத்துரை யெனத்தான்","சொல்ல வேதுவ ராடைகண் மூடிய சேடன்","மெல்ல வேயிவை கேளென விரித்தவ னுரைக்கும்."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi tells him to set out the five aggregates he established as good, and she will make clear that they are not; the elder in the ochre robe then begins slowly to expound them, bidding her listen.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vaadhu","kandham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 483","lb":"NK483","ev":"Neelakesi challenges the Buddha to state the aggregates, and he begins to expound them.","cl":[["the five aggregates as the Buddha teaches them do not exist","jain","asserted",2,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி","புத்தனார்"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","புத்தனார்"],"ch":"nk:22"},"nk:484":{"book":"neelakesi","num":483,"t":["உருவே திரிவே தனையா றுணர்வும்","மருவா தனமாண் குறியத் துணைசெய்","இருவே றவைசெய் கையிரு பதுமாந்","திருவே யிவையெம் பொருடே ரெனவும்."],"p":"","r":"The Buddha lists the aggregates with their divisions: form; feeling of three kinds; consciousness of six; perception in like number; and formation of twenty in two kinds; these, he says, are our wealth.","n":"The counts (three feelings, six consciousnesses, twenty formations) are read from compressed lines and line 2 (மருவா தனமாண் குறியத் துணைசெய்) is hard to divide. The list is finer than the bare five names of the Manimekalai; it is answered from 494, where Neelakesi vows to burn the aggregates like a hunter's hut.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kandham","arivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 484","lb":"NK484","ev":"","cl":[["the aggregates are form, three kinds of feeling, six kinds of consciousness, perception and twenty formations","buddhist","refuted",1,"buddha"]],"ag":["புத்தனார்"],"ec":[],"spk":["புத்தனார்","நீலகேசி"],"ch":"nk:22"},"nk:485":{"book":"neelakesi","num":484,"t":["முறைசெப் பியவைந் தினுண்முன் னையுரு","வரையிற் பலவட் டகமுள் ளுறுத்த","விறைபட் டனவெட் டெனவொட் டினகே","ணறையிற் பொலிகோ தைநறுந் நுதலே."],"p":"","r":"The Buddha tells her, fair-browed one with fragrant garland, that of the five in order the first, form, contains within its bounds many octads, each fixed as eight joined together.","n":"The octad (அட்டகம்) is the smallest unit of matter as eight inseparable things together; 495 answers it, arguing that if the mass is inseparable its parts become mere qualities of one bearer, or else must be separate things.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kandham","anu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 485","lb":"NK485","ev":"","cl":[["material form is made of octads, units of eight things joined inseparably","buddhist","refuted",3,"buddha"]],"ag":["புத்தனார்"],"ec":[],"spk":["புத்தனார்","நீலகேசி"],"ch":"nk:22"},"nk:486":{"book":"neelakesi","num":485,"t":["நிலநீ ரெரிகாற் றோடுரு விரதந்","நலமா கியநாற் றமொடூ றிவைதா","மிலவே யவையெட் டினும்விட் டதிறஞ்","சொலவா முடன்கேட் டொடுதோற் றமுமாம்."],"p":"","r":"He names the eight: earth, water, fire and wind, with colour, taste, fine smell and touch; and says that what is left out of the eight can be told, with sound and appearance.","n":"Lines 3 and 4 (இலவே யவையெட் டினும்விட் டதிறம் ...) are hard to construe; the reading that sound is added outside the eight is a guess.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kandham","anu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 486","lb":"NK486","ev":"","cl":[["the eight of the octad are the four elements with colour, taste, smell and touch","buddhist","reported",1,"buddha"]],"ag":["புத்தனார்"],"ec":[],"spk":["புத்தனார்","நீலகேசி"],"ch":"nk:22"},"nk:487":{"book":"neelakesi","num":486,"t":["வலிதா நிலமை யதுநீர் வெய்துதீக்","கலியே தருகாற் றியக்கம் கருமப்","பொலிவேற் பொறையயர்த் தல்புலர்த் துளர்த்தன்","மலிபூ தங்கணான் கின்மாண் பின்னவையே."],"p":"","r":"He gives the marks and works of the four great elements: earth is hard, water moist, fire hot and wind brings motion; and their functions are bearing, holding together, drying and stirring.","n":"Each element has a nature and a function: earth bears, water binds, fire dries or ripens, wind stirs. The reading of அயர்த்தல் as 'holding together' is from the pattern of the list.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["anu","thaththuvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 487","lb":"NK487","ev":"","cl":[["the four great elements are known by hardness, moisture, heat and motion, and act by bearing, binding, drying and stirring","buddhist","reported",1,"buddha"]],"ag":["புத்தனார்"],"ec":[],"spk":["புத்தனார்","நீலகேசி"],"ch":"nk:22"},"nk:488":{"book":"neelakesi","num":487,"t":["இனிவே தனையா வனவின் படுமொடு","துனிவே தருதுன் பமுமா மிடையுன்","நனிதா நலதீ வினையன் மையினாம்","பனிவே யிணைபன் னியதோண் மடவாய்."],"p":"","r":"The Buddha tells Neelakesi that the feelings are three: pleasure, the pain that brings distress, and a middle feeling that comes from what is neither good nor evil deed.","n":"This opens the Buddha's own account of the aggregates in the sarukkam, with feeling (வேதனை) first; the neutral feeling is tied here to deed that is neither good nor evil (line 3), a causal link the verse states in passing. Neelakesi answers the separateness of feeling in 505.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kandham","inbam","idumbai","vinai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 488","lb":"NK488","ev":"","cl":[["feeling is of three kinds: pleasant, painful and neither","buddhist","reported",1,"buddha"],["the neutral feeling arises from deed that is neither good nor evil","buddhist","reported",3,"buddha"]],"ag":["புத்தனார்"],"ec":[],"spk":["புத்தனார்","நீலகேசி"],"ch":"nk:23"},"nk:489":{"book":"neelakesi","num":488,"t":["அறிவா வனதா மினியா வெனினைம்","பொறியோ டுமனம் மிவைபுல் லினவேற்","குறிதா மிவையா றினுங்கூறுவதென்","னெறியா மிவைநீ லநிகர்த் தகணாய்."],"p":"","r":"The Buddha says that cognitions arise when the five senses and the mind meet their objects, and that perceptions are what is named in these six; this, he says, is his way.","n":"Cognition (அறிவு) and perception (குறி) are both defined by the six doors, with no knower behind them; 507 to 509 attack exactly this, asking how six cognitions can be counted if only one occurs at a time.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kandham","arivu","manam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 489","lb":"NK489","ev":"","cl":[["cognition is sixfold, arising when the five senses and the mind meet their objects, with no knower besides","buddhist","refuted",1,"buddha"]],"ag":["புத்தனார்"],"ec":[],"spk":["புத்தனார்","நீலகேசி"],"ch":"nk:23"},"nk:490":{"book":"neelakesi","num":489,"t":["குயலா குயலம் மெனக்கூ றும்வினைப்","பயனாற் பலபா கெனப்பட் டவைதா","மயலா மனமே வசிகா யத்தினா","மயலா ரிதாவை மினியா ரறிவார்."],"p":"","r":"The Buddha says that the activities called wholesome and unwholesome are divided into many kinds by the fruit of deeds and are done by mind, speech and body, and asks who among the deluded could know them.","n":"குயலா குயலம் is the Pali-Sanskrit pair kusala and akusala, and வசி and காயம் are speech and body; the verse names the formations by the three doors of action. Lines 3 and 4 (மயலா மனமே, மயலா ரிதாவை) are hard to divide, so the closing question is a reading.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vinai","kandham","manam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 490","lb":"NK490","ev":"","cl":[["deeds are wholesome or unwholesome and are done by mind, speech and body","buddhist","reported",3,"buddha"]],"ag":["புத்தனார்"],"ec":[],"spk":["புத்தனார்","நீலகேசி"],"ch":"nk:23"},"nk:491":{"book":"neelakesi","num":490,"t":["கடனா கியகந் தமிவைந் துகளும்","உடனே யவைதோன் றியொரு கணத்துட்","கெடுமே யவைகேட் டினும்வாட் கணல்லாய்","சுடர்மே யசுடர்ந் நுதிபோன் மெனவும்."],"p":"","r":"The Buddha says the five aggregates arise together and perish within a single moment, as the tip of a flame on a lamp does.","n":"The flame tip (சுடர் நுதி) is the image Kundalakesi used for the same doctrine in 177 (எரிநுதி). The claim that all five arise at once is what 506 turns against him, by giving the aggregates a causal order.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kandham","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 491","lb":"NK491","ev":"","cl":[["the five aggregates arise together and perish within one moment","buddhist","refuted",2,"buddha"]],"ag":["புத்தனார்"],"ec":[],"spk":["புத்தனார்","நீலகேசி"],"ch":"nk:23"},"nk:492":{"book":"neelakesi","num":491,"t":["கவையொப் பனவை விரலைந்து களும்","இவையிப் படிக்கைப் படியென் றதுபோ","லவையப் படிக்கந் தங்களைந் துகளும்","நவையைப் படுநல் லுயிரா மெனவும்."],"p":"","r":"The Buddha says that as five fingers placed together are called a hand, so the five aggregates together are what is called a living soul.","n":"The soul becomes a collective name, not a thing; 495 takes the same fingers (there a fist, முட்டி) and argues that what holds them together must be a bearer of qualities.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["uyir","kandham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 492","lb":"NK492","ev":"","cl":[["a soul is only the name for the five aggregates taken together, as 'hand' is for five fingers","buddhist","refuted",3,"buddha"]],"ag":["புத்தனார்"],"ec":[],"spk":["புத்தனார்","நீலகேசி"],"ch":"nk:23"},"nk:493":{"book":"neelakesi","num":492,"t":["அவைதா நிலையா துயரா மசுவம்","நவையா ருயிர்நாட் டிலங்காட் டமிலை","யிவைநான் மையென்வாய் மையிவ்வா றுணர்வா","ரெவையே செய்துமெய் துபவீ டுமென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"The Buddha ends his account: the aggregates are impermanent, painful and impure, and no soul is to be established in them; these four are his truth, and those who know them so reach release whatever they do.","n":"The four named here are impermanence, suffering, impurity (அசுவம், asubha) and no soul, which is a Jain poet's version of the Buddha's 'four truths'; the Buddhist Manimekalai (30) gives the four truths as suffering, its cause, release and its cause. எவையே செய்தும் ('whatever they do') may be the poet's barb that knowledge alone, not conduct, is said to free, the point the monk made in 259. Line 2 (நாட்டிலங்காட்டமிலை) is hard to divide.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","uyir","veedu","puththan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 493","lb":"NK493","ev":"The Buddha finishes setting out his doctrine of the aggregates to Neelakesi.","cl":[["the aggregates are impermanent, painful and impure, and there is no soul","buddhist","refuted",1,"buddha"],["those who understand these four truths reach release whatever they do","buddhist","reported",4,"buddha"]],"ag":["புத்தனார்"],"ec":[],"spk":["புத்தனார்","நீலகேசி"],"ch":"nk:23"},"nk:494":{"book":"neelakesi","num":493,"t":["பல்லியல் பாகிப் பரந்தவைங் கந்தமுங் கந்தங்கடாம்","புல்லிய வொற்றுமை யிற்குறி யாகிய பொய்யுயிருஞ்","சொல்லிய கேட்டவள் வேட்டக் குரம்பை சுடுபவர்போ","லல்லியங் கோதைநின் காட்சி யழித்திடு வேனெனத்தான்."],"p":"","r":"Having heard of the many-natured five aggregates and of the false soul that is a mere name for their loose unity, the lily-garlanded Neelakesi says she will destroy his view, like those who burn down a hut.","n":"பொய்யுயிர் ('false soul') is the narrator's summary of the Buddha's position, and காட்சி ('view', line 4) is the word Jains use for right or wrong faith, so destroying his காட்சி is a matter of doctrine, not only of debate. The simile வேட்டக் குரம்பை சுடுபவர் is unclear: the hut of hunters, or a hut one has cherished.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vaadhu","kandham","uyir"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 494","lb":"NK494","ev":"Neelakesi hears the Buddha out and vows to destroy his view.","cl":[],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"ch":"nk:23"},"nk:495":{"book":"neelakesi","num":494,"t":["பிண்டம் பிரிவில வேயெனச்சொல்லுத லாலவற்றுக்","குண்டங் கொருகுணி யாங்கவை தாங்குண மாகுமன்றேல்","விண்டங் கவையவை வேறுளதாதலும் வேண்டுமன்றோ","பண்டங்கு நீசொன்ன முட்டியிற்பஞ்சாங் குலிகளும்போல்."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi argues that since he calls the aggregate inseparable, there must be one bearer whose qualities they are; otherwise, split apart, each must be a separate thing, as the five fingers in the fist he cited are.","n":"The Buddha's example cuts both ways: either the aggregates are one thing's qualities (குணம் of a குணி, the Jain soul) or they are five things and no 'person' at all. The Buddha said 'hand' in 492 and Neelakesi says 'fist', so the example may have stood in a lost or differently worded verse.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["uyir","gunam","kandham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 495","lb":"NK495","ev":"","cl":[["the aggregates form one inseparable whole","buddhist","refuted",1,"neelakesi"],["parts that are inseparable must be the qualities of one bearer, and that bearer is the soul","jain","asserted",2,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","புத்தனார்"],"ch":"nk:23"},"nk:496":{"book":"neelakesi","num":495,"t":["பூதன்கும் புகுந்தே தெருல்லலுற் றயவற்றுக்","கேதமி றன்மை கரும மிரண்டா வியைந்தவை","பேதமு மாமென்ற பெற்றியி னாற்பொரு ளிற்றென்றலாற்","சாத மாமுயிர் தன்மையிற் றேற்ற தவறெவனோ"],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi argues that things have both their nature and their action, joined yet distinct, and asks what fault there is in establishing the soul from its nature.","n":"Line 1 is corrupt and the argument is read from lines 2 to 4; தன்மை and கருமம் (nature and function) return in 501.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["uyir","gunam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 496","lb":"NK496","ev":"","cl":[["a thing has both a nature and an action, so the soul can be established from its nature","jain","asserted",4,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","புத்தனார்"],"ch":"nk:23"},"nk:497":{"book":"neelakesi","num":496,"t":["நின்சொல்லப் பட்ட வலிப்புந் தரிப்பு நிலத்தினவே","லென்சொல்லப் பட்ட வுணர்வொடு காட்சி யுயிரனவா","முன்சொல்லப் பட்ட நிலந்தா முடியின் முடிவுளதாம்","பின்சொல்லப் பட்ட வுயிரும் பெரிய பிரச்சையினாய்."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi says that as he gives hardness and support as the marks of earth, she gives knowing and seeing as the marks of the soul; if earth is established, so is the soul, man of great wisdom.","n":"உணர்வு and காட்சி are the Jain pair of knowledge and perception that define the soul; the argument is one of parity, using the Buddhist's own way of defining an element by its marks. பெரிய பிரச்சையினாய் (great prajna) is ironic.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["uyir","arivu","gunam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 497","lb":"NK497","ev":"","cl":[["earth is known by its marks of hardness and support","buddhist","reported",1,"neelakesi"],["if earth is established by its marks, the soul is established by its marks of knowing and seeing","jain","asserted",2,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","புத்தனார்"],"ch":"nk:23"},"nk:498":{"book":"neelakesi","num":497,"t":["வற்பமல் லானில மில்லெனச் சொல்லுவ னாங்கதுபோற்","பொற்ப மிலாவுயிர் தானுமில் புத்திய லாலெனலும்","வற்பமலா நிலமன்னுந் தரிக்குமென் பாயல்லையோ","கற்பமெல் லாம்பிறர்க் கேநின் றுழந்த கருணையினாய்."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi says he holds there is no earth apart from hardness and no soul apart from cognition, yet says that earth persists and bears things; she calls him the compassionate one who toiled for others through every aeon.","n":"The address in line 4 repeats the praise Kundalakesi gave her teacher in 176 (அயலார்க்கே துன்புற்றான்), now spoken to the Buddha himself in a verse that charges him with contradiction.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["uyir","gunam","puththan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 498","lb":"NK498","ev":"","cl":[["there is no earth apart from hardness and no soul apart from cognition","buddhist","refuted",1,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","புத்தனார்"],"ch":"nk:23"},"nk:499":{"book":"neelakesi","num":498,"t":["உரிய வலிமையல் லானில மோரல னென்றிருந்தாற்","பெரிய நிலத்தை யறிவிக்கும் பெற்றியில் லாய்பெரிது","மரிய வுயிரை யறிவுறக் காட்டென்றி யெப்பொருளும்","தெரியக் குணமுகத் தாலன்றி யென்றுந் தெருட்டுளதோ."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi argues that if earth is nothing but hardness he cannot make great earth known, and yet he demands to be shown the soul; has any thing ever been made clear except through its qualities?","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["uyir","gunam","arivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 499","lb":"NK499","ev":"","cl":[["nothing is ever made known except through its qualities, so the soul need not be shown apart from them","jain","asserted",4,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","புத்தனார்"],"ch":"nk:23"},"nk:500":{"book":"neelakesi","num":499,"t":["விருத்த நிலைமையும் வேண்டலம் யாமென வேண்டுகின்றீ","ரருத்த மெனக்கொண்ட வட்டகம் யாவையும் விட்டில","றிருத்தமி றீவயி னீருறை திட்ட விரோதமன்றோவாற்","பொருத்தம லாதன வேசொல்லும் புத்தநின் புத்தியிதோ."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi says that he claims to accept no contradiction, yet has not given up the eight he took as real, which puts water in fire unchanged, a contradiction plain to sight; is this, Buddha, the wisdom that says only incoherent things?","n":"அட்டகம் ('the eight') is read as the eight constituents that the Buddhist account holds are always found together in matter, so that water is present even in fire; the verse names the eight only by number. திட்ட விரோதம் is the logician's term for a claim contradicted by what is seen.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vaadhu","thaththuvam","puththan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 500","lb":"NK500","ev":"","cl":[["the eight constituents of matter are always found together","buddhist","refuted",2,"neelakesi"],["a doctrine that puts water within fire is contradicted by what is seen","general","asserted",3,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","புத்தனார்"],"ch":"nk:23"},"nk:501":{"book":"neelakesi","num":500,"t":["தன்மை கரும மவற்றன வேயென்ற றானென்னைவே","றின்மை முடியினெ னிட்டமுரைப்பினுங் கெட்டதென்னோ","மன்னுமந் நான்கு மறுதலை தத்தமு ளாதலினா","லென்னு மியல்பும் பயனு மழிக்குமென் பேனல்லனோ?"],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi argues that if nature and function belong to the elements, then since the four elements are opposites present within one another, each would destroy the other's nature and use.","n":"Lines 1 and 2 are obscure (தானென்னைவே றின்மை), so the step from the first half to the conclusion is a reading.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thaththuvam","gunam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 501","lb":"NK501","ev":"","cl":[["if the four opposed elements are present in each other, each loses its nature and its use","jain","asserted",3,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","புத்தனார்"],"ch":"nk:23"},"nk:502":{"book":"neelakesi","num":501,"t":["ஊரிது காடிது தானென லென்னை யொருங்குளவே","னீரிது தீயிது தானென லாமோ நிகழ்வுடனே","யாரிவை கேட்டறி வாரவை யட்டக மென்னினலாற்","பேரிது வேயெனச் சொ;லுத றானும் பிழைக்குங்கொலோ."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi asks why one says 'this is a town, this a forest', and how, if all are present together, one could say 'this is water, this is fire'; if the eight are everywhere, even giving a name goes wrong.","n":"The town and forest are the ordinary case of naming by what is there; the octad, she argues, leaves no ground for naming one element rather than another.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thaththuvam","vaadhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 502","lb":"NK502","ev":"","cl":[["if all eight constituents are present everywhere, nothing can be named as one element rather than another","jain","asserted",2,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","புத்தனார்"],"ch":"nk:23"},"nk:503":{"book":"neelakesi","num":502,"t":["தொக்கவை யாயுட னேயவைநிற்பினு மாங்க வற்றுண்","மிக்கத னாற்பெயர் சொல்லுவன்யானென்று வேண்டுதியேற்","புக்கன தாம்பொறி யானின்புலமன்றிப் பொய்யெனத்தா","னக்கனர் சாக வெனுநீயுரைக்கு நயங்க ளென்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi takes up his answer that though the constituents stand together he names a thing by the one that predominates, and dismisses the reasonings he offers; with this she ends a stretch of her reply.","n":"Lines 3 and 4 (புக்கனதாம் பொறியானின்புலமன்றிப் பொய்யெனத்தா னக்கனர் சாக) are obscure; they seem to say that what enters through the senses is called false, and that others laugh at such methods, but the sense cannot be fixed. என்றாள் closes a speech, though 504 continues in her voice.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vaadhu","thaththuvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 503","lb":"NK503","ev":"","cl":[["a compound is named after the constituent that predominates in it","buddhist","refuted",2,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","புத்தனார்"],"ch":"nk:23"},"nk:504":{"book":"neelakesi","num":503,"t":["உருவுடை யட்டக மன்றியுமைந்தினுட் பட்டவெல்லாம்","மருவொடு கூடியுந் தீண்டியுமாக்குந் திறமரிதான்","மருவுடை யார்களை மாயங்கள்சொல்லி மருட்டியண்ணுந்","திருவுடை யாயவ காயத்துத்தேரை யடித்தெருட்டாய்."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi says that apart from the eight with form, it is impossible for all that falls within the five aggregates to join, touch and produce one another; deluding the attached with illusions, she says, he drives his chariot through empty space.","n":"அவகாயத்துத் தேரை ('a chariot in empty space') is read as an image of an argument that moves without ground; the verse is hard and the reading is offered tentatively.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kandham","vaadhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 504","lb":"NK504","ev":"","cl":[["the formless aggregates cannot combine by contact as material things do","jain","asserted",2,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","புத்தனார்"],"ch":"nk:23"},"nk:505":{"book":"neelakesi","num":504,"t":["திரிவே தனைகளுந் தீராநுமக்குள வாதலன்றே","கரிவே தனையவர் காமுறுகாமங் கடிந்ததுதான்","பரிவே பெரிதுடை யீர்முன்னுரைத்தபல் செய்கைகளின்","விரிவே யவையோரின் வேதனைவேறில்லை யாம்பிறவே."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi argues that the three feelings remain in him too, and that if the many activities he named before are examined, feeling is only their outgrowth and nothing apart from them.","n":"She makes the move she made against Kundalakesi in 194, folding feeling into activity, and so denies feeling the standing of a separate aggregate. Line 2 (கரிவே தனையவர் காமுறு காமம்) is obscure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kandham","vinai","arivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 505","lb":"NK505","ev":"","cl":[["feeling is not a separate aggregate but an outgrowth of activity","jain","asserted",4,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","புத்தனார்"],"ch":"nk:23"},"nk:506":{"book":"neelakesi","num":505,"t":["உணர்ச்சியி னாற்செய்கை யாக்கியச்செய்கையி னாலுழப்பாம்","உணர்ச்சியி னாங்கோர் பொருட்சுவடுள்ளது போல்கின்றதா","லுணர்ச்சியொ டல்லன வொன்றொன்றில்நோக்கில வாமெனினு","மிணர்ச்சி யிழந்து பிறபிறவாகிப் பெறலிலவே"],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi argues that activity is made by cognition and feeling by activity, and that cognition bears a trace of its object; so the aggregates stand in an order of cause and effect, and things that lose their connection become other and other and cannot be had.","n":"Read against 491, the causal order cognition, activity, feeling tells against the claim that all five arise in one moment. Lines 3 and 4 are hard to construe.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kandham","arivu","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 506","lb":"NK506","ev":"","cl":[["cognition produces activity and activity produces feeling, so the aggregates are cause and effect, not simultaneous","jain","asserted",1,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","புத்தனார்"],"ch":"nk:23"},"nk:507":{"book":"neelakesi","num":506,"t":["ஆறினி னொறே நிகழும்பொழுதினல் லாவுணர்வு","தேறின வாறவற் றுண்மையெனக்குத் தெருட்டனலிந்","தாறின வோவில்ல தாழ்ச்சியினாலுள தாமெனினீ","கூறிய வாற்றா லுயிருண்மைகூறலுங் குற்றமென்னோ."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi asks how, when only one of the six cognitions occurs at a time, he knows that the other five exist; and if he says they exist though sunk from view, what fault is there in affirming the soul by the same method?","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["uyir","arivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 507","lb":"NK507","ev":"","cl":[["only one of the six cognitions occurs at a time","buddhist","refuted",1,"neelakesi"],["if cognitions can exist without appearing, the soul can be affirmed by the same reasoning","jain","asserted",4,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","புத்தனார்"],"ch":"nk:23"},"nk:508":{"book":"neelakesi","num":507,"t":["ஓருணர் வுள்ள பொழுதினொழிந்த வுணர்வுகடம்","பேரு முணரப் படாமைபெற்றாமென்னும் பேச்சுமுண்டா","லீருணர் வில்லை யிருமூன்றொருங்குள வென்றுரைக்கு","மோருணர் வேயுடை யீர்சொற்களொன்றொன் றழிப்பனவே."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi says that he holds that when one cognition is present the departed ones cannot be perceived even by name, that no two cognitions exist at once, and also that the six exist together; his words, she says, destroy one another.","n":"ஓருணர்வே உடையீர் ('you who have only one cognition') turns his doctrine into an insult: a mind that holds one thing at a time cannot see its own contradiction.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arivu","vaadhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 508","lb":"NK508","ev":"","cl":[["there are never two cognitions at once","buddhist","refuted",3,"neelakesi"],["the six cognitions exist together","buddhist","refuted",3,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","புத்தனார்"],"ch":"nk:23"},"nk:509":{"book":"neelakesi","num":508,"t":["ஒன்றே யுணர்வாய் நிகழற்குக்காரண மவ்வுணர்வாய்","நின்றே யறிவான் றனிமையினாலெனத் தேரினிநீ","யன்றே யெனினவை யாறுந்தமுண்மையின் வேறென்னலாற்","சென்றே புலந்தலைப் பெய்தலறிவுடன் சீர்க்குமன்றே."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi takes up the answer that cognition occurs as one because the knower stands alone in it, and argues that if the six are distinct by their own existence, going out to their objects and meeting them would suit a knower.","n":"The verse names a knower (அறிவான், line 2), which is what the Jain wants the Buddhist to concede; the construal of lines 3 and 4 is uncertain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["arivu","uyir"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 509","lb":"NK509","ev":"","cl":[["cognitions that occur one at a time point to a single knower","jain","asserted",2,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","புத்தனார்"],"ch":"nk:23"},"nk:510":{"book":"neelakesi","num":509,"t":["இச்சையில் லாமையி னெல்லாமொருங்குண ராவெனினு","மிச்சையெல் லாத்திற்கும் வேறேலொருங்குட னெய்துமன்றி","யிச்சையெல் லாத்திற்கு மொன்றேற்குணிப்பொரு ளிச்சையென்றாய்","தச்சனஞ் சிச்சா ரகழ்கள்வனென்கின்ற தன்மையினாய்."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi argues that if he says all things are not known at once for lack of will, then a separate will for each would bring them all together, and one will for all makes will the bearer of qualities; he is, she says, like the carpenter who in fear cries 'thief'.","n":"A single will that governs every cognition is a soul under another name, which is why the carpenter of the proverb, who cries out and so gives himself away, fits; the proverb itself (தச்சனஞ் சிச்சா ரகழ்கள்வன்) is obscure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["uyir","gunam","arivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 510","lb":"NK510","ev":"","cl":[["cognitions do not all arise at once because will is lacking","buddhist","refuted",1,"neelakesi"],["a single will governing all cognitions would be a bearer of qualities, a soul under another name","jain","asserted",3,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","புத்தனார்"],"ch":"nk:23"},"nk:511":{"book":"neelakesi","num":510,"t":["பிறந்து பிறந்துநி னிச்சைகெடலன்றிப் பின்னொன்றின்மேற்","சிறந்து சிறந்தாங் குணர்ச்சிவிரியுந் திறமரிதாற்","கறந்த கறந்த கலஞ்சுவைத்திட்டாற் கறைக்கலங்க","ணிறைந்த நிறைந்தவை பாறயிர்மோரெனத் தானென்னையோ."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi argues that if his will perishes at every arising, knowledge cannot grow from excellence to excellence; if each pot just milked is tasted, how could the vessels be full of milk, curd and buttermilk?","n":"Milk, curd and buttermilk are one substance through its changes; the image asks for something that lasts through change, which momentary perishing denies. The phrasing of lines 3 and 4 is hard and the image's exact point is a reading.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","arivu","uyir"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 511","lb":"NK511","ev":"","cl":[["if every moment perishes wholly, knowledge cannot grow by accumulation","jain","asserted",2,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","புத்தனார்"],"ch":"nk:23"},"nk:512":{"book":"neelakesi","num":511,"t":["எல்லா வுணர்ச்சியு மிச்சைவழியா லெழுமெனலா","லில்லாம் பிறநுன திச்சையுமிச்சைமு னின்மையினால்","வல்லா யிதற்குமுண் டாயின்வரம்பின்மை யாமதனாற்","சொல்லா விடுந்திற மென்னோவிரிவிற்குச் சூனியமே."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi argues that if every cognition arises by way of will, and his will has no will before it, positing one for it too makes a regress without end; so there is no escaping it: his expansion ends in the void.","n":"The void (சூனியம்) here is the end of a failed argument, not a goal; it echoes Neelakesi's charge in 263 that the Buddha taught a void release.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["soonyam","arivu","vaadhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 512","lb":"NK512","ev":"","cl":[["all cognitions arise by way of will","buddhist","refuted",1,"neelakesi"],["if every will needs a prior will, there is an infinite regress","jain","asserted",3,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","புத்தனார்"],"ch":"nk:23"},"nk:513":{"book":"neelakesi","num":512,"t":["வெளிப்பட்டு நின்றதொன் றன்றியொழிந்தவிஞ் ஞானங்கடாங்","குளித்தன தாங்கொள்ளப் பாடின்மையாலின்மை கூறிநின்றேற்","களித்தவை தங்களுக் காலயமாலய மாமெனநீ","யொளித்தனை கொள்ளலுற் றாயுயிர்தன்னையோர் பேருரைத்தே."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi says that when she argued the consciousnesses other than the one now manifest are gone, since nothing can be grasped of them, the Buddha answered that they rest in a store (ஆலயம்); by that name he has quietly taken back the soul.","n":"The word ஆலயம் (line 3, doubled) answers to the Buddhist store-consciousness: Neelakesi's point is that a lasting receptacle of past knowings is the soul in all but name, the same move she made against Kundalakesi's carried-over power (203). Whether the Buddhists would accept the store as a soul is exactly what the Jain account denies them.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["uyir","arivu","kandham","samayam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 513","lb":"NK513","ev":"","cl":[["the consciousnesses not now manifest are kept in a store-consciousness","buddhist","refuted",3,"neelakesi"],["a lasting store of consciousness is the soul under another name","jain","asserted",4,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","புத்தனார்"],"ch":"nk:24"},"nk:514":{"book":"neelakesi","num":513,"t":["இருளுடை மாலைக்கண் டோன்றாதெனக்கென நண்பகலே","பொருளுடை யார்பொருள் கொள்வானகழுநன் போன்றிலையோ","வருளுடை யார்சொல்லு மாருயிராலய மென்றிருக்கும்","மருளுடை யாய்நின் மாண்பழிந்தெற்றான் மயங்கினையோ."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi likens the Buddha to a burglar who, saying he cannot be seen in the dark evening, digs into a rich man's house at broad noon: he keeps as his 'store' the precious soul the compassionate teach, and has lost his dignity in confusion.","n":"The simile turns doctrine into theft: the soul belongs to அருளுடையார் (the compassionate, line 3, the Jains as Neelakesi presents them), and taking it in daylight under a new name is a theft everyone can see.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["uyir","kalavu","samayam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 514","lb":"NK514","ev":"","cl":[["the store the Buddhist posits is the soul the Jain teachers teach","jain","asserted",3,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","புத்தனார்"],"ch":"nk:24"},"nk:515":{"book":"neelakesi","num":514,"t":["மூக்கொடு நாமெய்யும் மூன்றுந்தம்மூன்று புலன்களையும்","தாக்கிய போழ்தே யறிதலுந்தத்துவ மாமென்றியா","லாக்கிய மூன்றி லறிவுமரூவா லவையொருவாப்","பாக்கியஞ் செய்தாய் பரிசங்கள்கொள்ளும் பரிசென்னையோ."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi takes up the Buddhist rule that nose, tongue and body know their three objects only when they strike against them, and asks how, if the knowing in these three is formless, it can take touches at all.","n":"The Buddhist position reported is that smell, taste and touch are known by contact; the objection is that a formless knower cannot be struck. Line 3 (யாலாக்கிய, ஒருவாப் பாக்கியம்) is hard to construe, so the shape of the objection is read from lines 1-2 and 4.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pori","arivu","thaththuvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 515","lb":"NK515","ev":"","cl":[["nose, tongue and body know their objects only on contact with them","buddhist","reported",2,"neelakesi"],["a formless knowing cannot receive contact","jain","asserted",4,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","புத்தனார்"],"ch":"nk:24"},"nk:516":{"book":"neelakesi","num":515,"t":["உற்றில வாயொலி கொள்ளுஞ்செவியென வோதுகின்றாய்","கற்றிலை மெய்ம்மைநீ கட்புலந்தன்னோடோர் காலத்தினாற்","பெற்றில நாமதன் பின்கொளறானும் பெருந்தவத்தாய்","மற்றிது தான்றன் பொறியுறுகாறும் வரலினன்றே."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi says the Buddha teaches that the ear takes sound without its reaching it, but he has not learned the truth: we do not hear a thing at the moment we see it but afterwards, because sound must travel until it reaches its organ.","n":"The argument is from experience: sight and sound of one event come apart in time, so the ear must wait for sound to arrive. பெருந்தவத்தாய் (line 3), 'man of great penance', is said in mockery.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pori","arivu","therithal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 516","lb":"NK516","ev":"","cl":[["the ear grasps sound without contact","buddhist","refuted",1,"neelakesi"],["sound is heard after the thing is seen because it must travel to the ear, so hearing is by contact","jain","asserted",4,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","புத்தனார்"],"ch":"nk:24"},"nk:517":{"book":"neelakesi","num":516,"t":["வாய்த்துரை யீதென வாமனிதுசொல்லும் வந்துறுமேற்","சேய்த்தெனக் கோடலுஞ் சேராதொலிசெவிக் கண்ணதெனி","னீத்தன தாமல வாயினுநீசொல்லு முற்றறிவின்","றீத்தனைப் போலவுந் தேறென்றவனைத் தெருட்டினளே."],"p":"","r":"The Buddha replies that if sound came and touched the ear it could not be heard as far off; Neelakesi answers that sound in the ear may still be known as from afar, and bids him understand.","n":"Lines 3-4 are obscure: the comparison with the 'fire' of all-knowing knowledge (முற்றறிவின் தீத்தனை) cannot be fixed, so the shape of her answer is uncertain. The verse closes in the narrator's voice (தெருட்டினளே).","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pori","arivu","vaadhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 517","lb":"NK517","ev":"The Buddha objects that a sound heard as distant cannot touch the ear, and Neelakesi answers him.","cl":[["if sound touched the ear it could not be heard as far away","buddhist","refuted",2,"buddha"]],"ag":["புத்தனார்","நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"ch":"nk:24"},"nk:518":{"book":"neelakesi","num":517,"t":["பெற்ற வெழுத்தேற் பிரிவின்கணாறாம் பெயர் நுனது","முற்ற வுணர்ந்து முடித்துரையென்னை முதலெழுத்துப்","பற்றின சித்தம் பலவுணர்ந்தேயவை பாழ்படலாற்","கற்றினி யெல்லாக் கணக்குங்கலந்துரை காண்பென்னையோ."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi asks how, if the mind that grasped the first letter of a word perishes, anyone can understand a word whole and speak it, or learn and put together any reckoning at all.","n":"The objection to momentariness is tested on the smallest case, a single word heard letter by letter. Line 1 (பிரிவின்கணாறாம் பெயர் நுனது, perhaps 'your name, its letters parted') is obscure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","arivu","sol"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 518","lb":"NK518","ev":"","cl":[["the mind that grasps each letter perishes at once","buddhist","refuted",3,"neelakesi"],["understanding a word or a reckoning needs a knower that lasts across its parts","jain","asserted",4,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","புத்தனார்"],"ch":"nk:24"},"nk:519":{"book":"neelakesi","num":518,"t":["ஓதிய கந்தங்க ளொற்றுமையாலுயி ரென்றதுபோற்","போதிய னாய்நின்னைப் புத்தனென்றாலது பொய்பிறவோ","வாதியி னாம்புத்தி யாவதல்லாலந்தத் தன்களையா","நீதியி னாற்சொல்லி நின்றுநின்பேறு முணர்விலையே."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi argues that if the aggregates are called a soul only by their union, then to call him 'Buddha' for his awakening is a lie too, since the awakening belonged only to its first moment; he does not even know what he has attained.","n":"She turns the Buddhist account of the self as a conventional name against the Buddha's own title: on that account no one persists to be the awakened one.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kandham","puththan","uyir"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 519","lb":"NK519","ev":"","cl":[["the soul is only a name for the union of the aggregates","buddhist","refuted",1,"neelakesi"],["if the self is only a name, the title Buddha names no one who awoke","jain","asserted",2,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","புத்தனார்"],"ch":"nk:24"},"nk:520":{"book":"neelakesi","num":519,"t":["அன்றியு நின்சொ லறிபொருடானில வாதலினா","லொன்றி யுரைத்த வுனக்குமெனைத்து முணர்வருமை","யென்றினி யாமுந் தெளிந்தோமிதனா லெனவுரைத்தாள்","வென்றி யுடையன வல்லதுசொல்லா விரிகுழலே."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi of the spreading hair, who speaks nothing but winning words, concludes that since the Buddha's words have no real things for their objects, she is now clear that he who spoke them has no knowledge at all.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sol","arivu","vaadhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 520","lb":"NK520","ev":"","cl":[["words have no real objects","buddhist","refuted",1,"neelakesi"],["one whose words refer to nothing real has no knowledge","jain","asserted",2,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","புத்தனார்"],"ch":"nk:24"},"nk:521":{"book":"neelakesi","num":520,"t":["ஆறுகுறி யாவனவு மாயபுலந் தாமும்","கூறுகுறி மாறுமவை கொள்ளும்வகை தாமும்","மாறுகுறி மாறிக்குறி யென்றுமயக் காதே","வேறுகுறி தாமுணர்வின் விள்ளுவ லினிக்கேள."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi announces that she will now explain the six perceptions (குறி), their objects and how they take them, and how perception differs from consciousness, without confusing the terms.","n":"குறி here is the perception aggregate, not a sign: the next three verses press whether it can be told apart from knowing (உணர்வு), the point she made against Kundalakesi in 194.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kandham","arivu","pori"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 521","lb":"NK521","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","புத்தனார்"],"ch":"nk:24"},"nk:522":{"book":"neelakesi","num":521,"t":["கண்ணின்குறி மூக்கின்குறி மெய்யின்குறி செவியி","னண்ணுங்குறி நாவின்குறி காடின் மனத்தோடு","மெண்ணுங்குறி யாவனவிவ் வாறுமெனக் கொண்ணீ","பண்ணின்குறி யேசுமொழிப் பாவையெனச் சொல்லும்."],"p":"","r":"The woman whose speech outdoes a tune names the six perceptions: of eye, nose, body, ear, tongue and, with the mind, the perception that thinks.","n":"காடின் (line 2) gives no sense and is probably corrupt; the sixth perception is read as the mind's.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kandham","pori","manam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 522","lb":"NK522","ev":"","cl":[["perception is of six kinds, one for each of the five senses and the mind","buddhist","reported",3,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","புத்தனார்"],"ch":"nk:24"},"nk:523":{"book":"neelakesi","num":522,"t":["அறிவுகுறி யென்பனவி னாயபுல மொன்றே","லறிவுகுறி யென்பனவு மாகுமவை யொன்றே","பிறிதுகொளு புலமுள்ள தாகுமெனிற் பேதம்","சிறிதுநெறி காட்டினது செல்லுமெனச் சொல்லும்."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi argues that if consciousness and perception have one and the same object they are one thing, and if each takes another object the difference can be shown; so she says.","n":"The opening horn of a dilemma: two aggregates with a single object collapse into one, as she argued in 194 that feeling, perception and volition are not other than knowing.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kandham","arivu","thaththuvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 523","lb":"NK523","ev":"","cl":[["two faculties that take the same object are not two","jain","asserted",2,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","புத்தனார்"],"ch":"nk:24"},"nk:524":{"book":"neelakesi","num":523,"t":["உண்மைகுறி கொள்ளுமுணர் வின்புலம தெய்தா","தெண்ணினுணர் வோடுகுறி யிவ்வகைய வென்னி","லுண்மையுணர் வின்புலமோ டொன்றெனில தொன்றே","நண்ணலில வேலுணர்வி னாயபுல னின்றே."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi presses the dilemma: if perception reaches its real object while consciousness does not, then either their object is one and they are one, or, if consciousness never meets it, consciousness has no object at all.","n":"Lines 1-2 are compressed and the sense of உண்மைகுறி is not secure; the conclusion in line 4 is clear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kandham","arivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 524","lb":"NK524","ev":"","cl":[["if consciousness does not reach the object perception takes, consciousness has no object","jain","asserted",4,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","புத்தனார்"],"ch":"nk:24"},"nk:525":{"book":"neelakesi","num":524,"t":["ஒருங்குகுறி யோடுணர்வுதோன்றியுடன் கொள்க","வொருங்குபுலந் தோன்றியவையொத்தகெட லானா","லொருங்குகுறி யோடுணர்வுதோன்றலில வாமே","லொருங்குபுலந் தோன்றுதலினொன்றுமுணர் வில்லை."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi argues that if perception and consciousness arise with their object at one instant and perish together, nothing is known, and if they do not arise together, then since the object arises at once there is still no knowing.","n":"This is the momentariness objection in its sharpest form: a knowing that lasts no longer than its object has no time in which to know it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","arivu","kandham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 525","lb":"NK525","ev":"","cl":[["object, perception and consciousness arise together and perish together","buddhist","refuted",2,"neelakesi"],["on that account nothing is ever known","jain","asserted",4,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","புத்தனார்"],"ch":"nk:24"},"nk:526":{"book":"neelakesi","num":525,"t":["உள்ளுஞ் சொல்லுடம் பென்றிவை மூன்றினாற்","கொள்ளு நுங்குச லாகுச லங்கடா","மெள்ளி னேரு மிவையின்மை காட்டுவே","னுள்ளக் காக வுரைத்தவம் மூன்றினுள்."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi turns to the Buddhists' wholesome and unwholesome deeds, which they class by mind, speech and body, and says she will show that they do not hold even a sesame seed's worth, beginning with the three of the mind.","n":"குசல அகுசலம் (line 2) are the Buddhist terms. From here to 537 she takes the ten bad deeds in order: the three of the mind (527-530), then the four of speech (531-537), the list the Manimekalai's teacher gives (Manimekalai 30, lines 64-70).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","aram","manam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 526","lb":"NK526","ev":"","cl":[["good and bad deeds are done by mind, speech and body","buddhist","reported",1,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","புத்தனார்"],"ch":"nk:24"},"nk:527":{"book":"neelakesi","num":526,"t":["வேண்ட லேயென்றும் வெஃகுத லாயினா","லீண்டி நின்றநி னித்தொடைப் பாடெலாந்","தீண்ட லன்னெனத் தீண்டிய வந்தவோ","வீண்டை யேநிற்ப விஃது மறிகிலை."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi argues that if all wanting is coveting, then the whole garland of verses the Buddha strung together is coveting too, and he does not see even this.","n":"The target is the first mental fault, வெஃகல். Lines 2-3 (தீண்டல் அன்ன, தீண்டிய வந்தவோ) are hard to construe; the charge read here, that the teacher's own wish to teach is a wanting, rests on line 2.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vehkaamai","avaa","vinai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 527","lb":"NK527","ev":"","cl":[["all wanting is coveting, a fault of the mind","buddhist","refuted",1,"neelakesi"],["if every wanting is coveting, the teacher's own composing of his teaching is coveting","jain","asserted",2,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","புத்தனார்"],"ch":"nk:24"},"nk:528":{"book":"neelakesi","num":527,"t":["நன்மை வெஃகுதல் நன்றெனச் சொல்லுலு","மனமை வெஃகுத லன்றெனக் கூறலு","மின்மை யால்வெஃக லென்றதுந் தீமையி","னின்மை யாய நெறிமொழி நீயினி."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi says that since the Buddha does not declare that coveting the good is good and coveting what is not good is not, he must now state a rule under which his word 'coveting' is free of evil.","n":"She asks for coveting to be judged by its object. மனமை (line 2) is probably a slip for அன்மை, which the parallel with நன்மை requires.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vehkaamai","aram","avaa"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 528","lb":"NK528","ev":"","cl":[["wanting what is good is not a fault; coveting must be judged by its object","jain","asserted",1,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","புத்தனார்"],"ch":"nk:24"},"nk:529":{"book":"neelakesi","num":528,"t":["தீய வாய வெகுளியை யொப்பன","மாய மான முலோப மனத்தன","காய்வு செய்திலை கண்டுநின் கள்ளமே","நீய வையவை நேர்தலி னாம்பிற."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi says deceit, pride and greed, which are of the mind, are as evil as anger, yet the Buddha, though he saw them, did not condemn them; that is his fraud, and once these are admitted others follow.","n":"மாயம் மானம் உலோபம் with வெகுளி (lines 1-2) make the Jain four passions: anger, pride, deceit and greed. The charge is that the Buddhist list of mental faults (coveting, anger, wrong view) leaves three of them out.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vekuli","vanjam","avaa","kutram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 529","lb":"NK529","ev":"","cl":[["anger is a fault of the mind","buddhist","reported",1,"neelakesi"],["deceit, pride and greed are faults of the mind as evil as anger","jain","asserted",2,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","புத்தனார்"],"ch":"nk:24"},"nk:530":{"book":"neelakesi","num":529,"t":["காட்சி நல்லன் காண்ட லனைத்தினு","மாட்சி யாமெனின் மன்னுமஃ தொட்டுவேன்","வேட்கை யாலிது நன்றென வேண்டினு","மாட்சி யொட்டி வழித்தன னல்லனோ."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi grants that right view is the excellence above all seeing, but argues that whoever wants it, judging it good, wants it from desire, and so by the Buddhist rule falls while assenting to it.","n":"காட்சி is the third mental item (பொல்லாக் காட்சி, wrong view, in the Buddhist list), and also the Jain right vision. Line 4 (வழித்தனன்) is obscure, so the conclusion is read from the run of argument in 527-528.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["meyyunarvu","avaa","vehkaamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 530","lb":"NK530","ev":"","cl":[["right view is the highest excellence of seeing","jain","asserted",2,"neelakesi"],["wanting right view is itself a wanting, so the Buddhist rule on coveting condemns it","jain","asserted",3,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","புத்தனார்"],"ch":"nk:24"},"nk:531":{"book":"neelakesi","num":530,"t":["பொய்கு றள்ளே கடுஞ்சொற் பயனில்கொ","னைத லில்லன நான்கிவை நாவினாஞ்","செய்கை தீயன வாமெனச் செப்பினீ","ரைய நன்மையென் றீரவற் றப்புடை."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi recalls that the Buddhists declared lying, slander, harsh words and idle talk to be the four evil deeds of the tongue, and their opposites good.","n":"The list is the Buddhist one as the Manimekalai gives it, almost word for word (பொய்யே குறளை கடுஞ்சொல் பயன் இல், Manimekalai 30, line 68). குறள்ளே and கொனைதல் (lines 1-2) are probably faulty.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vaaimai","purangooral","kadunjol","payan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 531","lb":"NK531","ev":"","cl":[["lying, slander, harsh words and idle talk are the four evil deeds of speech","buddhist","reported",2,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","புத்தனார்"],"ch":"nk:24"},"nk:532":{"book":"neelakesi","num":531,"t":["மண்டை கெண்டிகை மாட மடாமனை","கொண்டை குண்டிகை கூறை குடங்கடை","யுண்டி கட்டி லுடம்புயி ராதிய","பிண்ட மாய பிறபிற யாவையும்."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi lists composite things: begging bowl, water pot, mansion, monastery, house, topknot, ascetic's vessel, robe, pot, shop, food, bed, body and soul, and all other things made of parts.","n":"The list runs straight into 533, and mixes the monk's own goods (மண்டை, குண்டிகை, கூறை) with the body and soul, so that his daily speech is caught by the argument.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kandham","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 532","lb":"NK532","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","புத்தனார்"],"ch":"nk:24"},"nk:533":{"book":"neelakesi","num":532,"t":["கூட்ட மான குறியெனி னலதை","பூட்ட லங்கோர் பொருளின்மை வேண்டியு","மீட்டு மீட்டிவை சொல்லின மெய்யுரை","நாட்டு மாறென்கொ லோவிளி நாசநீ."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi argues that if all these are only names for collections with no single thing there, yet the Buddhist names them again and again, he cannot establish true speech, and his own rule against lying condemns him.","n":"This is where the two halves of the sarukkam meet: the doctrine that composites are only names (532) is set against the rule that lying is a bad deed of speech (531). The vocative விளி நாச (line 4) is obscure.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vaaimai","sol","kandham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 533","lb":"NK533","ev":"","cl":[["composite things are only names for collections, with no single thing there","buddhist","refuted",1,"neelakesi"],["one who names what he holds unreal speaks falsely every time he names it","jain","asserted",3,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","புத்தனார்"],"ch":"nk:24"},"nk:534":{"book":"neelakesi","num":533,"t":["நிலம்பொய் நீர்பொய் நெடுநகர் தாமும்பொய்","கலம்பொய் காற்றொடு தீயும்பொய் காடும்பொய்","குலம்பொய் யேயெனக் கூறுஞ்செங் கூறையாய்","சலம்பொய் யன்றிதொன் றேநுங்கள் சத்தையே."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi mocks the red-robed one who says earth, water, the great city, vessels, wind, fire, forest and clan are all false, saying that of all this only his own deceit is not false.","n":"The list of elements and goods called பொய் is the Jain account of Buddhist teaching; the Buddhists would say such things exist by convention, not that they are lies. Line 4 (சலம் பொய் அன்று, சத்தை) is read as sarcasm, but its exact turn is uncertain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vaaimai","nilaiyaamai","samayam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 534","lb":"NK534","ev":"","cl":[["earth, water, fire, wind and all made things are unreal","buddhist","refuted",3,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","புத்தனார்"],"ch":"nk:24"},"nk:535":{"book":"neelakesi","num":534,"t":["குற்ற மென்ற குறளையே யொப்பன","மற்றுஞ் சால வுளபிற மாண்பில","வொற்றைப் பெண்ணுரை யாடுதல் போகங்கள்","கற்ற லேகடுஞ் சொல்லின்ன காத்தலும்."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi says that besides slander, which the Buddhists call a fault, there are many other unworthy kinds of speech, such as talking alone with a woman and teaching the arts of pleasure, and harsh words must be guarded against as well.","n":"The objection is that the four-item list is too short; her examples come from a renouncer's code of speech rather than from the Buddhist list.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sol","kutram","kadunjol"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 535","lb":"NK535","ev":"","cl":[["the faults of speech are many more than four, including private talk with a woman and teaching pleasures","jain","asserted",3,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","புத்தனார்"],"ch":"nk:24"},"nk:536":{"book":"neelakesi","num":535,"t":["பையச் சொல்லுத னல்வினைப் பாலென்றா","லெய்யக் குற்ற வெறியப்புணர்க்குங்கால்","வையத் தீயாவரு மந்திர மாமவை","செய்யச் சொல்லுநர் செவ்விய ராபவோ."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi argues that if speaking gently is a good deed, then those who softly lay plans to wound and kill, and the whispered counsels that urge such deeds, would be upright.","n":"She refutes the rule that soft speech is good by the gentle-voiced plotter: the goodness of speech lies in what it brings about, not its tone. மந்திரம் (line 3) is counsel, not a sacred formula. Line 2 is compressed.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inchol","vanjam","kolai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 536","lb":"NK536","ev":"","cl":[["gentle speech is a good deed","buddhist","refuted",1,"neelakesi"],["soft words that plot harm are not good, so speech is not good by its gentleness","jain","asserted",4,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","புத்தனார்"],"ch":"nk:24"},"nk:537":{"book":"neelakesi","num":536,"t":["கடுக்கத் தாமடி கள்ளெனக் காய்ஞர்யா","ரொடுக்கச் சாம்பிவை தாலுவப் பாரில்லை","கொடுக்க வென்றல் குசலமன் றென்பதோ","படுக்க லுற்ற பதகநின் பாடமே."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi asks who would condemn as harsh the stern words that restrain, and whether saying 'give' is not wholesome, and calls the Buddhist teaching a wicked lesson that brings its holder down.","n":"Lines 1-2 are obscure (கடுக்கத் தாமடிகள், ஒடுக்கச் சாம்பிவை); the reading that a harsh word may restrain, as 'give' is good whatever its tone, is tentative.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kadunjol","eegai","vinai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 537","lb":"NK537","ev":"","cl":[["urging another to give is a wholesome deed of speech","jain","asserted",3,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","புத்தனார்"],"ch":"nk:24"},"nk:538":{"book":"neelakesi","num":537,"t":["களபாவ மாம்பய னில்லன சொல்லலேற்","பூவ மேபொருள் கைக்கொண்டு புற்கலர்","சாவச் சொல்லுவ தத்துவ மாம்பிற","வேவ னன்றென்றி யொப்பவொன் றில்லையேல்."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi argues that if the Buddha will not speak of things that have no fruit, then what his followers say about persons (புற்கலர்) dying could count as truth only if something were held to persist, and nothing in his teaching matches that.","n":"Line 1 (களபாவமாம்) and line 4 (ஏவனன்றென்றி) resist division; the reading rests on புற்கலர் (pudgala, the 'person') and சாவ (dying) in lines 2 and 3, and on the frame of the argument against a doctrine with no lasting self.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kandham","nilaiyaamai","samayam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 538","lb":"NK538","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","புத்தன்"],"ch":"nk:25"},"nk:539":{"book":"neelakesi","num":538,"t":["காயத் தாற்கொலை காமங் களவென","நீயத் தாசொன்ன நேருந் திறமென்னோ","மாயத் தான்மன மின்றி யவையவை","தேயத் தியாருளர் செய்பவை ரென்பவே."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi takes up the Buddha's teaching that killing, lust and theft are deeds of the body and asks how that can be, when without a mind no one is there in any place to do such deeds.","n":"The Buddhist grouping is by the door a deed goes out through (body, speech, mind, as 526 set out); Neelakesi reads it as if the body were said to act alone. The same three bodily deeds open the Manimekalai teacher's list of ten evil deeds.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","manam","kolai","kalavu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 539","lb":"NK539","ev":"","cl":[["killing, lust and theft are the deeds that arise in the body","buddhist","refuted",1,"neelakesi"],["no deed of the body is done without a mind that does it","jain","asserted",3,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","புத்தன்"],"ch":"nk:25"},"nk:540":{"book":"neelakesi","num":539,"t":["வழிமுள் ளூன்றன் மனைசுடன் மாந்தரைக்","குழியு ளுந்துதல் கோயிற் கலஞ்செய்த","லொழிவில் யானைமுன் னோட்டலோ டின்னவும்","பழியும் பாவமு மாக்குவ வல்லவோ."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi lists deeds that harm without a chosen victim (planting thorns on a path, burning a house, pushing people into a pit, despoiling temple vessels, driving an elephant at people) and asks whether these do not bring blame and sin.","n":"The list prepares the attack on the five conditions of killing in 541: in none of these deeds does the doer know and intend a particular life. கோயிற் கலஞ்செய்தல் may mean damaging the temple's vessels or something else done to them; the sense of harm is clear from the frame.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","pazhi","innaa","kolai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 540","lb":"NK540","ev":"","cl":[["deeds that harm whoever happens to meet them bring blame and sin though no particular victim was intended","jain","asserted",4,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","புத்தன்"],"ch":"nk:25"},"nk:541":{"book":"neelakesi","num":540,"t":["ஒன்றி நின்ற வுயிரை யுயிரிது","வென்று சிந்தித் தழிப்ப னெனவெண்ணி","நன்றி யிஃறொழி றேற்ற நவையினாற்","கொன்ற தேகொலை யென்றைந்திற் கூறினாய்."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi restates the Buddha's rule that a killing is a killing only when five things meet: a life joined to a body, the thought 'this is a life', the intention 'I will destroy it', the deed done with that fault, and the death.","n":"The rule is stated here in order to be answered: 543 to 550 bring cases of mistaken identity in which, by this rule, a man would die or be robbed with no sin incurred. ஐந்தில் (line 4) names the five; their list is read from lines 1 to 4.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kolai","manam","vinai","kollaamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 541","lb":"NK541","ev":"","cl":[["a killing counts as killing only when there is a living being, knowledge that it lives, the intention to kill, the act, and death","buddhist","refuted",4,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","புத்தன்"],"ch":"nk:25"},"nk:542":{"book":"neelakesi","num":541,"t":["தேவ னையிவன் றேவ னெனவெண்ணிப்","பாவனையிற் பணியு மனத்தனாய்த்","தூவென மும்மையே தோற்றித் தொழானங்கோர்","தாவ னைதொழு தான்றவ றெய்துமோ."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi tests the intention rule from the side of worship: if a man, thinking 'this is the god', bows in devotion but has in fact bowed to something other than the god, does he incur fault or not?","n":"The verse is a question whose answer is left for the hearer; தாவனை in line 4 (what was wrongly worshipped) and தூவென மும்மையே தோற்றி in line 3 are hard to fix, so the exact case is uncertain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","manam","vinai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 542","lb":"NK542","ev":"","cl":[["whether an act brings merit or fault depends on what the mind took its object to be","buddhist","refuted",1,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","புத்தன்"],"ch":"nk:25"},"nk:543":{"book":"neelakesi","num":542,"t":["ஒத்த வன்றனை யுறுபகை யேயெனக்","குத்தி னாற்குங் கொலைவினை யில்லைனப்","புத்த னீருரைத் தீரங்கோர் புற்கலன்","செத்த வாறது சிந்திக்கற் பாலதே."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi recalls that the Buddhists say a man who stabs someone resembling his enemy, taking him for that enemy, incurs no killing-karma, and bids them consider how the person stabbed nonetheless died.","n":"The answer is the plain fact of line 4 (செத்தவாறு): a man is dead, and the rule of intention cannot make him alive. The verse sets the pattern of resemblance (ஒத்தவன்) that 549 repeats for theft (ஒப்பது).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kolai","vinai","manam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 543","lb":"NK543","ev":"","cl":[["one who kills a man mistaking him for his enemy incurs no karma of killing","buddhist","refuted",2,"neelakesi"],["a death that one's deed causes is a killing whatever the doer took the victim to be","jain","asserted",4,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","புத்தன்"],"ch":"nk:25"},"nk:544":{"book":"neelakesi","num":543,"t":["கொன்ற பாவமுண் டாயின் குறட்கண்ணும்","ஒன்று மேயென் றுரைப்பனெப் பாரியார்","பொன்றினும் புத்த ரேநீவிர் சொல்லின","சென்று சேர்தலைச் சித்தம தின்மையால்."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi says that, by the Buddhists' own words, even when someone dies no sin of killing follows, because the doer's mind did not go out and reach that victim.","n":"Line 1 to 2 (குறட்கண்ணும் ஒன்றுமே ... எப்பாரியார) are obscure; the verse repeats 313 of the Mokkala debate word for word, so the editor or the tradition placed the same argument in both debates.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["kolai","manam","vinai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 544","lb":"NK544","ev":"","cl":[["the sin of killing follows only when the killer's mind reached the one who died","buddhist","refuted",4,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","புத்தன்"],"ch":"nk:25"},"nk:545":{"book":"neelakesi","num":544,"t":["பட்டி லன்பகை யாலெய்து பாவமங்","கொட்டி நீயவ் வுயிர்க்கொலை யாலென்னில்","துட்டனைத் தொழு தோன்றுறந் தானெனக்","கெட்ட னன்னிது கேட்க வினைநிலை."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi argues that where the intended enemy did not die, the Buddhist must either deny the sin of the hostile intention or charge it to the other life that was killed, and she bids him hear how karma truly works, citing one who worshipped a wicked man and was ruined.","n":"பட்டிலன் (line 1, 'he did not die') fixes the case as a failed or misdirected killing; line 3 (துட்டனைத் தொழுதோன் துறந்தான்) alludes to a story the verse does not tell, and the reading of lines 2 and 3 is a guess.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vinai","manam","kolai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 545","lb":"NK545","ev":"","cl":[["a hostile intention binds karma even when the deed falls on someone other than its target","jain","asserted",4,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","புத்தன்"],"ch":"nk:25"},"nk:546":{"book":"neelakesi","num":545,"t":["ஆய்ந்த வைந்தினு மாம்வினை யார்ப்பெனின்","வீய்ந்த தின்மையின் வெய்ய வவீசிதான்","காந்தி பாலி யிரங்கக் கலகனைப்","போழ்ந்து கோடல் பொருந்தல தொக்குமே."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi argues that if karma binds only when all five conditions are met, then in a case the Buddhists themselves tell, where no one died, it would be unfitting that the doer went to the fierce Avici hell for splitting Kalakan.","n":"The verse turns on a story it only names (அவீசி, the hell; காந்தி, பாலி and கலகன், unidentified here); that the case is a Buddhist one in which no death occurs is read from வீய்ந்ததின்மையின் (line 2) and the argument.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["naragam","vinai","kolai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 546","lb":"NK546","ev":"","cl":[["karma binds only when all five conditions of the deed are met","buddhist","refuted",1,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","புத்தன்"],"ch":"nk:25"},"nk:547":{"book":"neelakesi","num":546,"t":["கொல்வ னென்றவன் கூர்ம்படை குன்றினுஞ்","செல்வ னென்றய லார்மனைச் சேரினுங்","கல்லு வானொ டெல்லார்க்குங் கருவினை","சொல்லு வானோ டுலகமுஞ் சுற்றமே."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi says that the man who sets out to kill though his sharp weapon fails, the man who sets out for another's house, and the burglar all carry the dark deed of their intent, and the world and kin are bound with the one who speaks it.","n":"Here Neelakesi grants intention its weight; her case against the Buddha is not that the mind does not matter but that mind and deed both bind, so a failed intent and an unintended death each carry karma. Line 4 (சொல்லுவானோடு உலகமுஞ் சுற்றமே) is unclear.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","manam","kolai","kalavu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 547","lb":"NK547","ev":"","cl":[["the intention to kill, to go to another's wife or to steal binds evil karma even when the act fails","jain","asserted",3,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","புத்தன்"],"ch":"nk:25"},"nk:548":{"book":"neelakesi","num":547,"t":["துப்பி னால்வினை சொல்லலன் யானென்று","வப்பி னார்முலை காய்வது வாமனீ","யிப்ப லாரிடை யென்னையி தென்பதோ","செப்பி னாலுஞ் சிதைகின்ற தில்லையால்."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi presses the Buddha on a case involving contact with women, saying that his claim not to reckon karma from such contact does not survive even being stated.","n":"Nearly every line is obscure (துப்பினால் in line 1, வப்பினார் முலை காய்வது in line 2); the Buddha is addressed as வாமன் as in 517. The claim is read from the frame of 541 to 550.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","vinai","manam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 548","lb":"NK548","ev":"","cl":[["contact without intention brings no karma","buddhist","refuted",1,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","புத்தன்"],"ch":"nk:25"},"nk:549":{"book":"neelakesi","num":548,"t":["பண்டு தான்வைத்தப் பண்டத்தை யொப்பதொன்","றுண்ட தாயி னதுவது வேயெனக்","கொண்டு போகினுங் கொள்ளினுங் குற்றமில்","கண்ட போழ்துங் களவன்ற தென்றலால்."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi states the Buddhist rule on theft: if a man takes a thing that resembles what he once deposited, believing it his own, there is no fault whether he carries it off or keeps it, for even when seen it is not theft.","n":"The rule is answered by the satire of 550, which applies the same reasoning to another man's wife.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalavu","manam","vinai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 549","lb":"NK549","ev":"","cl":[["taking another's thing in the belief that it is one's own is not theft","buddhist","refuted",4,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","புத்தன்"],"ch":"nk:25"},"nk:550":{"book":"neelakesi","num":549,"t":["பஞ்சி மெல்லடி நோவப் பகனடந்","தஞ்சி லோதியு மல்க வவளெனத்","துஞ்சு மில்லுடை யாளைச் சுமந்துபோய்","வஞ்சி யான்கொள்க வாழ்கபுத் தன்னென."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi mocks the rule: let a man carry off a householder's wife asleep at home, telling himself 'this is she' (his own wife, whose soft feet ached from walking by day), and take her by deceit, crying 'long live the Buddha'.","n":"The verse is a reductio: it adds nothing to the rule of 549 but its consequence. The cry வாழ்க புத்தன் puts the Buddha's name on the abduction.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["piranil","kaamam","manam","puththan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 550","lb":"NK550","ev":"","cl":[["if mistaken belief excused the deed, a man could carry off another's wife without fault","jain","asserted",4,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","புத்தன்"],"ch":"nk:25"},"nk:551":{"book":"neelakesi","num":550,"t":["கந்த மாவன காகதந் தம்மெனப்","பந்த மின்மையிற் பாழ்செய்திட் டேனினி","யந்தி னீசொன்ன வாரிய சத்தையுஞ்","சிந்தை யுஞ்சிதைப் பேன்சில சொல்லினால்."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi says that, having laid waste the aggregates by showing them to be as unreal as a crow's teeth since nothing binds them into one, she will now shatter with a few words the noble truths he taught and the mind as well.","n":"காகதந்தம் (crow's teeth) is a stock example of a thing that does not exist. ஆரிய சத்தை (line 3) names the noble truths, which 553 to 562 take up.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kandham","meyyunarvu","samayam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 551","lb":"NK551","ev":"Neelakesi turns from the aggregates to attack the Buddha's noble truths.","cl":[["the five aggregates, with nothing binding them into one, are as unreal as a crow's teeth","jain","asserted",1,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","புத்தன்"],"ch":"nk:25"},"nk:552":{"book":"neelakesi","num":551,"t":["பிண்டமொன் றாயினும் பிரியநோக் கின்னது","கொண்டுநின் றாம்பிற கூறினைந் தேயெனக்","கண்டநா மெய்ம்மையுங் காட்டுவா யீங்கெனி","னுண்டுதா மாகுல முணர்வுதான் கூறுவோம்."],"p":"","r":"The verse gives the Buddhist account that the body-heap, though one, is viewed apart as five, and a challenge to show here the truth that was seen; Neelakesi answers that she will speak of suffering and of awareness.","n":"The speaker of lines 1 to 3 may be the Buddha (கொண்டுநின்றாம், கண்டநா மெய்ம்மை, 'we held', 'the truth we saw') and line 4 Neelakesi's reply (கூறுவோம்); the verse has no speech marker but எனின், and ஆகுலம் in line 4 is read as distress.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kandham","idumbai","arivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 552","lb":"NK552","ev":"","cl":[["the one heap of the body is analysed into five aggregates","buddhist","reported",2,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","புத்தன்"],"ch":"nk:25"},"nk:553":{"book":"neelakesi","num":552,"t":["முழுதுந்துன்பம்மென மொழியினஃ தாகிய","பொழுதினா னல்லவும் புல்லுமா மாதலாற்","பழுதுதா னவ்வுரை பன்மைதா னின்மையில்","இழுதையா னான்குள வென்றுசொன் னாயென."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi argues that if all is suffering, then good things too become worthless the moment one says so; the teaching is therefore faulty, and since suffering admits no plurality, the fool was wrong to say there are four truths.","n":"The argument needs the Buddhist to hold that the third and fourth truths, cessation and the path, are themselves suffering; the Manimekalai's teacher gives release as joy (இன்பம் வீடே), which the reductio does not meet.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["idumbai","meyyunarvu","samayam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 553","lb":"NK553","ev":"","cl":[["all is suffering","buddhist","refuted",1,"neelakesi"],["if everything is suffering there can be only one truth, not four","jain","asserted",4,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","புத்தன்"],"ch":"nk:25"},"nk:554":{"book":"neelakesi","num":553,"t":["உழப்புமூன் றும்முட னொக்கநோக் கின்னது","வழுக்கில்லா வாய்மையேல் வாய்மை சொல்லியினி","யிழுக்கினாய் நீபிறர்க் கின்பமீந் தேனெனல்","பழுக்களே காய்வது ண்டுமுண் டேபிற."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi argues that if the flawless truth is to see the three kinds of suffering together, then the Buddha, having spoken that truth, erred in saying he gave joy to others.","n":"உழப்பு மூன்றும் (line 1) is read as the three kinds of suffering; line 4 (பழுக்களே காய்வது ...) is corrupt or obscure and is left unread.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["idumbai","vaaimai","arul"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 554","lb":"NK554","ev":"","cl":[["seeing the three kinds of suffering together is the noble truth","buddhist","refuted",1,"neelakesi"],["one who teaches that all is suffering cannot claim to give others joy","jain","asserted",3,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","புத்தன்"],"ch":"nk:25"},"nk:555":{"book":"neelakesi","num":554,"t":["துக்கமே யாயினாற் றொழிலுமொன் றாய்ப்பய","மொக்கவே வேண்டுமா லுயர்விலாக் கீழ்க்கதி","புக்குவீழ்ந் தார்பிறர் பொங்கிநின் றாரெமர்","மிக்கதே யென்னெறி யென்றுவேண் டல்லெவன."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi argues that if all is suffering, every deed is one and its fruit must be equal, so the Buddhists cannot say that others have fallen into low births while their own people rise, or that their path is the highest.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["idumbai","vinai","piravi","samayam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 555","lb":"NK555","ev":"","cl":[["if all is suffering, deeds and their fruits are all alike and no path is higher than another","jain","asserted",4,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","புத்தன்"],"ch":"nk:25"},"nk:556":{"book":"neelakesi","num":555,"t":["உறுதிகூ றல்லொழி யொழுக்கங்காத் தல்லொழி","யிறுதியிஃ றுன்பமே யின்பமில் லாயினாற்","சிறிதுநீ தீப்புகாய் சேர்வதென் னைந்நிழல்","இறுதியில் லாத்துய ரின்னசெய் யாயினி."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi tells the Buddha that if the end of everything is only suffering with no joy, he should stop giving counsel and guarding conduct, go a little into fire, and not seek shade, since every act only adds to endless suffering.","n":"The verse drives 'all is suffering' to its practical end: if there is no joy anywhere, fire and shade are alike, and so are virtue and vice.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["idumbai","ozhukkam","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 556","lb":"NK556","ev":"","cl":[["a teaching that all is suffering with no joy leaves no reason to teach or keep conduct","jain","asserted",2,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","புத்தன்"],"ch":"nk:25"},"nk:557":{"book":"neelakesi","num":556,"t":["தூய்மை யில்லை முழுவது மென்பதை","வாயும் நீசொல்லும் வாய்மைய தாயினாற்","றாமஞ் சாந்தம் புனைபவர் தாமெலாம்","ஏம நன்னெறி கண்டில ரேபிற."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi argues that if his word that nothing at all is pure is true, then all who wear garlands and sandal paste have failed to find the safe good path.","n":"The verse does not say whose garlands are meant; it may point at the Buddhists' own worship with garland and sandal, or at all householders, so the force of the consequence is uncertain. Naladiyar 48, a Jain quatrain, says just this of those who praise sandal and garland.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thooimai","yaakkai","samayam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 557","lb":"NK557","ev":"","cl":[["nothing at all is pure","buddhist","refuted",1,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","புத்தன்"],"ch":"nk:25"},"nk:558":{"book":"neelakesi","num":557,"t":["ஓது மோத்து மொழுக்குநின் னுண்டியுங்","கோதில் தூய்மைய வாமெனக் கூறியும்","யாது நீயசு வாமெனச் சொல்லுவாய்","நாத னீபிற நன்கறிந் தாயவை."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi points out that the Buddhist says his recitation, conduct and food are flawless and pure, and asks what, then, he calls impure; the Lord, she says, knew other things very well.","n":"நாதன் நீ பிற நன்கறிந்தாய் (line 4) is irony.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thooimai","koodaozhukkam","samayam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 558","lb":"NK558","ev":"","cl":[["the monks' recitation, conduct and food are pure","buddhist","refuted",2,"neelakesi"],["one who calls all things impure cannot call his own practice pure","jain","asserted",3,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","புத்தன்"],"ch":"nk:25"},"nk:559":{"book":"neelakesi","num":558,"t":["நீயி னேசொன்ன மெய்ம்மையை நோக்கிலார்","தூய்மை யாம்பிறர் தூய்மை யிலரென்று","காய்ப வேகவி மண்டைகள் என்றனள்","வேயி னன்மை விலக்கிய தோளினாள்."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi, whose shoulders surpass the bamboo, says that the bowl-carrying monkeys who ignore the truth the Buddha spoke burn with anger, saying 'we are pure and others are impure'.","n":"கவி மண்டைகள் (line 3) joins monkey and begging bowl as an insult to the monks; the narrator's line names Neelakesi as the speaker by her shoulders.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thooimai","vekuli","koodaozhukkam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 559","lb":"NK559","ev":"","cl":[["monks who call themselves pure and others impure have ignored their own teacher's truth","jain","asserted",2,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","புத்தன்"],"ch":"nk:25"},"nk:560":{"book":"neelakesi","num":559,"t":["சென்ற சென்றுன செந்நெறி கண்டவர்","பின்றைத் தூய்மை பெறுவது மில்லையே","லின்று மின்று மியல்வது வாக்கொள்வாய்க்","கின்றித் தூய்தன்மை யென்னை யிழைத்ததோ."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi argues that if even those who saw the right path long ago gain no purity afterward, then for one who takes whatever arises day by day as its nature, there is nothing that could have made things impure.","n":"Lines 3 and 4 (இன்றுமின்று மியல்வது வாக்கொள்வாய்க்கு இன்றித் தூய்தன்மை) are hard to divide; the reading joins the momentariness of 562 to the purity argument of 557 to 561.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thooimai","nilaiyaamai","meyyunarvu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 560","lb":"NK560","ev":"","cl":[["whatever arises moment by moment is simply its own nature","buddhist","refuted",3,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","புத்தன்"],"ch":"nk:25"},"nk:561":{"book":"neelakesi","num":560,"t":["அழுகு பூசுமி னங்கண மாடுமின்","கழுகு ணூத்தையோ டேனவுங் கவ்வுமின்","மெழுகு மின்னிடை மெச்சிய மல்லதின்","முழுதுந் தூய்தன்மை சொல்லிய மூடர்கள்."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi tells the fools who said all is impure to smear themselves with rot, bathe in the gutter, and gulp the filth that vultures and pigs eat, since by their teaching none of it differs from anything else.","n":"தூய்தன்மை in line 4 is தூய்து அன்மை, 'not being pure'. Line 3 (மெழுகுமின்னிடை மெச்சியமல்லதின்) is obscure.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thooimai","pedhaimai","samayam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 561","lb":"NK561","ev":"","cl":[["if all things are impure there is no ground to choose clean over foul","jain","asserted",4,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","புத்தன்"],"ch":"nk:25"},"nk:562":{"book":"neelakesi","num":561,"t":["நில்லா வென்னி னில்லன்மையாலும் நில்லாவா","யில்லா மென்ற லின்புறு மேற்கோ ளிழுக்காகு","மெல்லாந் தானே யென்றலி னேன் விழவெய்தி","நல்லாய் சொன்ன நான்மையை நாட்டுந் திறமென்னோ."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi argues that if he says things do not stay, the saying itself does not stay, and if he says 'we are not', his own happy thesis is spoiled; since all is the same as itself, she asks, good one, how will he establish the four truths he spoke.","n":"The verse is in a longer metre than its neighbours. நல்லாய் (line 4) addresses the opponent as 'good one', ironically. Line 3 (எல்லாந் தானே யென்றலினேன் விழவெய்தி) is not secure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","meyyunarvu","samayam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 562","lb":"NK562","ev":"","cl":[["nothing endures beyond the moment","buddhist","refuted",1,"neelakesi"],["a doctrine of momentariness cannot establish any thesis, including the four truths","jain","asserted",4,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","புத்தன்"],"ch":"nk:25"},"nk:563":{"book":"neelakesi","num":562,"t":["உழுவார் வணிக ரென்றிவ ருள்ளிட்டுலகத்துள்","வழுவார்க் கீய வான்பொருள் வேறாய் மறியும்மே","வழுவார் தமையுங் காண்டுமஃ தாமாறுரையீரோ","புழுவாழ் கென்று புனன்மழை தந்த புகழுள்ளீர்."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi asks the Buddha, whom she hails as the one famed for sending rain so that worms might live, to explain how gifts of great wealth made by farmers, merchants and others to the faultless are said to turn out differently, since faultless people are to be seen too.","n":"The closing epithet turns a former-birth tale of compassion (rain sent for worms) into mockery; the sense of lines 1-3, whether the fruit of a gift is said to differ by its receiver, rests on a compressed construction.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["eegai","puththan","payan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 563","lb":"NK563","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","புத்தன்"],"ch":"nk:26"},"nk:564":{"book":"neelakesi","num":563,"t":["தெய்வத மென்று தேறினர் செய்யுஞ் சிறப்பென்றும்","கையது வீயக் காமுறு தானக் கலப்பென்று","மெய்தல் ரேயின் னிவையிவை யெல்லா மிழுக்காவோ","மெய்பிளந் திட்டு வேண்டுநர்க் கீயும் விழுமிய்யீர்."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi asks whether worship paid by those convinced that something is a god, and giving mixed with the wish to be rid of what is in one's hand, are not all faults when examined, addressing the Buddha as the noble one who split his own body to give to those who asked.","n":"Line 3 (மெய்தல் ரேயின்) is damaged; reading it as 'if one examines truly' makes the verse a charge that the Buddha's body-gifts fall under faults his own side names.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["eegai","vazhipaadu","kutram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 564","lb":"NK564","ev":"","cl":[["worship offered from belief in a god and giving driven by desire are faults","buddhist","reported",3,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","புத்தன்"],"ch":"nk:26"},"nk:565":{"book":"neelakesi","num":564,"t":["ஒள்ளிழை யாரே யுறுபொருள் பிச்சைக் குரியார்கள்","தள்ளின போழ்தின் னவையவை தன்னைத் தலைநிற்பார்","கள்ளர்க ளன்மை காட்டலு மாமோ கருணையாற்","பிள்ளைக ளேங்கப் பிறர்களுக் கீந்த பெருமைய்யீர்."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi asks whether jewelled women are goods fit to be given as alms, and whether those who take charge of them once they are handed over can be shown not to be thieves, mocking the Buddha's greatness in giving to others out of compassion while his own children wailed.","n":"The objection shifts ground from earlier attacks on the body-gifts: here the wrong is that persons are not the giver's property, so the gift turns the receiver into a thief. The word கருணையால் is used against the compassion it names.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","puththan","makkal","arul"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 565","lb":"NK565","ev":"","cl":[["wives and children are not goods that may be given away, and whoever takes them so is a thief","general","asserted",3,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","புத்தன்"],"ch":"nk:26"},"nk:566":{"book":"neelakesi","num":565,"t":["நில்லா தாகக் கூறுத றன்னை நெறியென்றீர்","பல்வகை யானும் பாழ்செய்து பின்னே பரிகாரஞ்","சொல்லுவி ராயிற் சொல்லிய மெய்ம்மை துறவாமன்","கொல்சின வேழங் குறிநிலை செய்த குணத்தின்னீர்."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi says that the Buddha made the teaching that nothing endures his path, and that if after ruining everything in many ways he then offers a remedy he cannot keep to the truth he declared, hailing him as the one whose virtue stopped the murderous wrathful elephant.","n":"The epithet in line 4 recalls the taming of a raging elephant; the argument that a remedy presupposes something lasting is a reading of the compressed lines 2-3.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","puththan","vaaimai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 566","lb":"NK566","ev":"","cl":[["nothing endures, and to teach this is the path","buddhist","refuted",1,"neelakesi"],["one who denies all endurance cannot consistently offer a remedy afterwards","jain","asserted",3,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","புத்தன்"],"ch":"nk:26"},"nk:567":{"book":"neelakesi","num":566,"t":["ஓதி யாது முயிரில்லை யென்ப துரைத்து நின்றாயேற்","கோதி லங்கோர் குறியுயிரே கொள்ளி னுங்கோளழிவாம","வேதி லார்சொல் பரிகற்ப னையி னாலின் மைசொல்லி","னீதி யாநின் கருத்தினா லுண்மை யுந்நேர்ந் தாயன்றோ."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi argues that if the Buddha holds there is no soul at all, then admitting even one faultless mark as soul destroys his thesis, and if he denies the soul only through a constructed fiction (பரிகற்பனை) he has by his own view conceded that it exists.","n":"Line 3's ஏதிலார் சொல் may mean 'the word of strangers' or 'unrelated words'; either way the move is that a denial made by mere construction admits the thing denied.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["uyir","samayam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 567","lb":"NK567","ev":"","cl":[["there is no soul at all","buddhist","refuted",1,"neelakesi"],["to deny a thing only by a constructed designation is to concede that it exists","jain","asserted",4,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","புத்தன்"],"ch":"nk:26"},"nk:568":{"book":"neelakesi","num":567,"t":["ஒன்று மில்லை யுயிரென்றுரைத்தநீ யுண்மை யின்மை","நின்ற வாறே நெறியாகநேர்கின் றாய்நீ யாவனோ","வன்றி முன்சொன் னவத்திறமெத்திறத்தா னும்மா காமையா","லின்றுந் நின்றுந் நீமொழிந்தாயெம்மிறை யேயிறை யாகவே."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi asks the Buddha, who said the soul is nothing, who then he is that accepts being and non-being standing as they are as the path, and says that since the nine heads he spoke of before cannot hold by any means, he has today taken her Lord as the Lord.","n":"The question நீ யாவனோ turns the no-self thesis on the speaker. What the nine (நவத்திறம்) are is not said in this verse.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["uyir","iraivan","samayam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 568","lb":"NK568","ev":"","cl":[["one who denies the soul cannot say who it is that holds a view","jain","asserted",2,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","புத்தன்"],"ch":"nk:26"},"nk:569":{"book":"neelakesi","num":568,"t":["போற லானு மதுபோ லாதென்றும்பு னைவினாலும்","வேறல் லதில் லையெனவும் வினைவலியும்யோ கின்னாலும்","தேறி நின்ற பொழுதோடிவ்வேழாந்திறத்தி னாலுங்","கூற லாமோ மீட்டுணர்வுகொண்டு ணர்வா னில்லாக்கால்."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi asks whether, if there is no knower who recollects, the Buddhist can explain knowing by any of seven ways: by likeness, by unlikeness, by construction, by 'nothing other than it', by the strength of deeds, by yoga, or by the moment of clear understanding.","n":"The list is compressed and the reading of some items (புனைவு, யோகு) is tentative; the point is that recollection (மீட்டுணர்வு) needs one who knows across time.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["arivu","ninaivu","uyir"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 569","lb":"NK569","ev":"","cl":[["without an enduring knower, recollection cannot be accounted for by likeness, karma, yoga or any other means","jain","asserted",4,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","புத்தன்"],"ch":"nk:26"},"nk:570":{"book":"neelakesi","num":569,"t":["நாம சீவன் முதலாய நான்மை களின்முன் மூன்றிலுந்","தூய்மை சீவனுடைத்தாகு மன்றே னின்சொன் மாறுமாந்","தாம சீவன் முழுவது மென்று தருக்கு கின்றாய்","வாம சீவ னிவற்றினா லுண்மை மறுக்க லாகுமோ."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi appeals to the four ways of designating the soul that begin with the soul-in-name, says the Buddha's word changes if purity belongs to the soul in the first three, and asks whether by these he can deny the soul's existence.","n":"நாம சீவன் முதலாய நான்மை looks like the Jain fourfold placing of a word (by name, by representation, by substance, by state); தாம சீவன் and வாம சீவன் in lines 3-4 are obscure and may be corrupt.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["uyir","thaththuvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 570","lb":"NK570","ev":"","cl":[["the soul is spoken of in four ways beginning with the soul in name, and these do not allow its existence to be denied","jain","asserted",1,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","புத்தன்"],"ch":"nk:26"},"nk:571":{"book":"neelakesi","num":570,"t":["அறியும் மெய்ம்மை யும்மைங்கந்தம்மாட சிய்யு மல்லவையுஞ்","சிறிதும் மென்முன் னிலையின்மைகாட்டிச் சிதைத்தே னாலினிப்","பிறிதொன் றுண்டே லுரைய்யெனவுரைத்தாள் புத்தன் றான்பெரிதும்","மெறிபட் டென்னெ றியினாலேயடக்கு வன்னி னையுமென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi says she has shattered the knower, truth, the five aggregates and everything else by showing that none of them stands, and bids the Buddha name anything left; the Buddha answers that he will subdue her by his own path, and bids her consider.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vaadhu","kandham","puththan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 571","lb":"NK571","ev":"Neelakesi declares the Buddha's categories refuted and challenges him to name another; the Buddha says he will subdue her by his own path.","cl":[],"ag":["நீலகேசி","புத்தன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"nk:26"},"nk:572":{"book":"neelakesi","num":571,"t":["ஆதி யலாத வகன்றடுமாற்றமுஞ்","சாதி யறுக்குந் தகையு மிவையென","வோதி வினைப்பயத் தொப்புரைப் பார்க்கன்றி","நீதி யிலாநெறி நேரல னென்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi says she will not accept a path without reason except from those who teach the wide wandering that has no beginning, the means that cuts off birth, and the fit between a deed and its fruit.","n":"The verse sets her terms for the reply that follows: any account the Buddha gives must show how a deed's fruit reaches its doer. That ஒப்பு means the deed-fruit correspondence is a reading.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["piravi","vinai","veedu","anaadhi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 572","lb":"NK572","ev":"","cl":[["the round of births has no beginning","jain","asserted",1,"neelakesi"],["a path is acceptable only if it shows how birth is cut off and how a deed's fruit fits the deed","jain","asserted",3,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","புத்தன்"],"ch":"nk:26"},"nk:573":{"book":"neelakesi","num":572,"t":["வித்தின் வழிவழித் தோன்றும் முளைகிளை","சத்தியி னாயசந் தானத்தை மாற்றென்பன்","வெத்தவ வித்தினின் வேறன்று வீடிது","தத்துவ மாக்கொ டளிரிய லாயே."],"p":"","r":"The Buddha says that as sprout and branch appear in succession from a seed, he calls the continuity arising by potency a change, that release is not other than this, and bids Neelakesi take it as the truth.","n":"Neelakesi answers the seed image in 579-583, where she argues the analogy carries nothing across for the aggregates. The account is given in a Jain work.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kandham","veedu","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 573","lb":"NK573","ev":"","cl":[["a person is a continuity (சந்தானம்) arising by potency, as sprout and branch from a seed","buddhist","refuted",2,"puththan"],["release is not other than the course of this continuity","buddhist","refuted",3,"puththan"]],"ag":["புத்தன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["புத்தன்","நீலகேசி"],"ch":"nk:26"},"nk:574":{"book":"neelakesi","num":573,"t":["வினையு முளது பயனு முளது","தினையனைத் தாயினுஞ் செய்ஞ்ஞன மில்லை","யெனையவுங் கந்த மருகண நில்லா","நினையின்மற் றென்றா னெறிபயந் தானே."],"p":"","r":"The Buddha says that the deed exists and its fruit exists, but there is not a millet-grain's worth of a doer, and that none of the aggregates stands for even a moment.","n":"Neelakesi answers at once in 575 and again in 584. The thesis matches the Buddhist teacher's own words in Manimekalai 30 (the chain has no maker), so here the polemic attacks a view the school holds.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","payan","kandham","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 574","lb":"NK574","ev":"","cl":[["deeds and their fruits exist, but there is no doer","buddhist","refuted",1,"puththan"],["none of the aggregates lasts beyond a moment","buddhist","refuted",3,"puththan"]],"ag":["புத்தன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["புத்தன்","நீலகேசி"],"ch":"nk:26"},"nk:575":{"book":"neelakesi","num":574,"t":["குணம்பொரு ளென்றி கொடைபொரு ளென்றி","யுணர்ந்துசெய் துண்பா னொருவனி லென்றி","கணந்தனி லேநிலை கந்தமு மாயக்காற்","பிணங்குவ தொக்குநின் பேரெமக் கென்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi answers that the Buddha calls virtue a real thing and giving a real thing, yet says there is no one who knows, acts and enjoys, and that if the aggregates last only a moment even his own name quarrels with him.","n":"The jab at his name is a reading of பிணங்குவது ஒக்கும் நின் பேர்: the 'awakened one' cannot be a knower if there is no knower.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","uyir","eegai","kandham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 575","lb":"NK575","ev":"","cl":[["there is no one who knows, acts and enjoys the fruit","buddhist","refuted",2,"neelakesi"],["merit and giving make no sense without one who knows, acts and enjoys","jain","asserted",1,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","புத்தன்"],"ch":"nk:26"},"nk:576":{"book":"neelakesi","num":575,"t":["தீதுள்ள மேலது தீயழப் பேசெய்யும்","யாதுள்ள மாண்புள மேலின்ப மாமென்னை","மாதுளம் பீசமுண் மாணரக் கின்னிறம்","போதுள்ளங் காண்பது போலமற் றென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"The Buddha says an evil mind brings the suffering of burning and a mind of worth brings bliss hereafter, as when a pomegranate seed is steeped in lac its red is seen in the flower.","n":"The lac-and-pomegranate image is the Buddhist answer to how a deed reaches its fruit without a lasting self; Neelakesi takes it apart in 579-585.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","naragam","inbam","payan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 576","lb":"NK576","ev":"","cl":[["an evil mind leads to burning suffering and a worthy one to bliss, as lac on a pomegranate seed reddens its flower","buddhist","refuted",1,"puththan"]],"ag":["புத்தன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["புத்தன்","நீலகேசி"],"ch":"nk:26"},"nk:577":{"book":"neelakesi","num":576,"t":["எட்டி னியன்ற விரண்டினு ளாங்கவை","யட்ட வரத்தமு மல்லது மாய்ப்பய","மாட்டார் மலர்க்கண்ணுஞ் செம்மையு மற்றுமா","விட்டமுங் காடம்டுவ னீங்கிது போல."],"p":"","r":"The Buddha goes on that the seed and the lac are each made of eight constituents, and that the red and the other marks appear in the flower's eye and colour, and so it is here.","n":"எட்டின் இயன்ற reads as the Buddhist octad of matter; line 4 (காடம்டுவ) is damaged and the second half of the verse is hard to construe.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["payan","kandham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 577","lb":"NK577","ev":"","cl":[["seed and lac are each made of eight material constituents","buddhist","refuted",1,"puththan"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["புத்தன்","நீலகேசி"],"ch":"nk:26"},"nk:578":{"book":"neelakesi","num":577,"t":["ஐந்தி னியன்றவர் பிண்டத்த ராகிய","மைந்த ரிருவர் குசலா குசலத்தர்","சிந்தையி னல்லவன் றன்வழித் தேவனும்","வெந்தொழி லான்வழி வீறி னரகனும்."],"p":"","r":"The Buddha gives his parallel: two youths made of the five aggregates, one of wholesome and one of unwholesome deeds, so that from the good-minded one comes a god and from the one of cruel deeds a hell-dweller.","n":"The terms குசல and அகுசல are the Buddhist words for wholesome and unwholesome action; Neelakesi answers the parallel in 579-580.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kandham","vinai","naragam","piravi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 578","lb":"NK578","ev":"","cl":[["a being of five aggregates continues as a god or a hell-dweller according to wholesome or unwholesome deeds","buddhist","refuted",2,"puththan"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["புத்தன்","நீலகேசி"],"ch":"nk:26"},"nk:579":{"book":"neelakesi","num":578,"t":["பீச மெனப்பட்ட தெட்டே பிறிதங் கொன்","றாசொன்று மில்லைய வைந்திற்கு மன்னது","நீ சொன்ன வாறிது நேருந் திறமென்னை","யேசுவன் கேள்யா னெடுத்தினி யென்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi says the seed is only the eight and nothing else, and the five aggregates are likewise, so she asks how his example fits and bids him hear while she takes it up and rebukes it.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kandham","vaadhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 579","lb":"NK579","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","புத்தன்"],"ch":"nk:26"},"nk:580":{"book":"neelakesi","num":579,"t":["அயலரக் கட்டக பீசமுண் டாங்கு","வியலகத் தைந்திற்கும் வேறொன்று காட்டாய்","குயலமு மல்லது மாயினன் றாகும்","மயல்படைத் தாயொழி மாதுளங் காட்டல்."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi argues that the lac is something added to the eightfold seed, but he shows no such further thing for the five aggregates, and tells the deluded Buddha to stop showing the pomegranate.","n":"This is the same dilemma she put to Kundalakesi in 203: whatever carries the deed across is a further thing beyond the five, which is what the Jain calls a soul.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kandham","vinai","uyir"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 580","lb":"NK580","ev":"","cl":[["the lac analogy fails because the lac is a thing beyond the seed, and nothing beyond the five aggregates is admitted to carry a deed","jain","asserted",2,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","புத்தன்"],"ch":"nk:26"},"nk:581":{"book":"neelakesi","num":580,"t":["பூவின்கட் காட்டல் பொருந்தா ததன்வழி","மாவின்க ணாக மகன்செய் வினைப்பயன்","தேவன்கட் போலத் திருந்திய மாதுளஞ்","சாவின்கட் செய்கையுஞ் சாங்களைந் தாயோ."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi says that showing the colour in the flower does not fit, since a man's deed bears fruit in the god he becomes, and asks whether the Buddha has set aside that the act too perishes when the pomegranate dies.","n":"Lines 2-4 are compressed (மாவின்கண் may be 'in the mango' or a place-word); the reading that the flower is the wrong place for the fruit of a deed is tentative.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vinai","payan","saakkaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 581","lb":"NK581","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","புத்தன்"],"ch":"nk:26"},"nk:582":{"book":"neelakesi","num":581,"t":["வித்தொடு பூவின்கண் வேற்றுமை காட்டினும்","துத்தல் குழவி கிழவன்கட் சொல்லென்பன்","பித்துடை யார்போற் பிதற்றி வினைப்பய","மெத்திறத் தின்னு மியைத் துரைக் கில்லாய."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi says that even if he shows a difference between seed and flower, he must speak of the infant and the old man, and that babbling like the mad he cannot join a deed to its fruit in any way.","n":"The infant and the old man are one person at two times: Neelakesi moves the question from a plant's series to the life of one human, where the doer and the enjoyer should be the same.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","payan","pedhaimai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 582","lb":"NK582","ev":"","cl":[["on the Buddhist account a deed cannot be joined to its fruit","jain","asserted",4,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","புத்தன்"],"ch":"nk:26"},"nk:583":{"book":"neelakesi","num":582,"t":["அங்குரந் தன்கண்ணுஞ் செல்லா தரக்கொடு","மங்கின பீசத் துருவ மலரின்கட்","டங்கின வென்னுஞ்சொற் றத்துவ மாக்கொண்ட","வங்குலி மாரனை யாதன்மற் றென்னாய்."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi asks: if the seed's form, faded with the lac, skips the sprout and settles in the flower, what would he say of Angulimala?","n":"Angulimala, the killer who became the Buddha's disciple, is named without his story; how his case bears on the argument (whether his killings bore fruit) is not spelled out here.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vinai","payan","kolai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 583","lb":"NK583","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","புத்தன்"],"ch":"nk:26"},"nk:584":{"book":"neelakesi","num":583,"t":["அப்படி யாலரக் காமது போன்மெனிற்","றப்படை யானுழப் பெய்வழித் தங்குத","றுப்புடை யான்சுர னாகிய வன்றுய்க்க","லிப்படி யாயினீ யென்றுரை யாயோ."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi asks whether, if he says the lac comes to be like that by such a course, he will also say that one man toils at the deed while another, born a god, enjoys its fruit.","n":"The objection is that without a lasting self the one who acts and the one who enjoys are two; line 2 (தப்படையான் உழப் பெய்வழி) is obscure and the reading rests on the sense of the whole.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vinai","payan","piravi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 584","lb":"NK584","ev":"","cl":[["without a lasting self the doer of a deed and the enjoyer of its fruit would be different persons","jain","asserted",3,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","புத்தன்"],"ch":"nk:26"},"nk:585":{"book":"neelakesi","num":584,"t":["எம்மை யுவப்ப வினைவழித் துப்பெனின்","¦ச்மை வழியது தீஞ்சுவை யென்றில்லை","யெம்மை வினைவினை யாக்குநின் பூவுரை","யிம்மையோ டும்மை யிவையிலை யாலோ."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi argues that his flower example cannot carry a deed's sweet fruit from one birth to the next, so by his account there would be neither this birth nor the next.","n":"Line 2 is damaged (a non-Tamil character stands for its first letter), and line 1 is hard to construe.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["marumai","vinai","piravi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 585","lb":"NK585","ev":"","cl":[["on the Buddhist account there would be neither this birth nor the next","jain","asserted",4,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","புத்தன்"],"ch":"nk:26"},"nk:586":{"book":"neelakesi","num":585,"t":["உண்டது போலு முறுபயன் பன்மலர்","கண்டது காரண மாகக் கருதினு","மண்டையா மான்றசை மீன்றடி தோன்றிய","வண்டுண வாகலு மாமத னாலே."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi draws a consequence: if the fruit of many flowers is reckoned as eaten because they were seen as its cause, then deer flesh and fish meat in the begging bowl could pass for bees' food.","n":"The construction is obscure; that the absurd consequence aims at the Buddhist monk's eating of meat (மண்டை, the bowl) is a reading.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pulaal","pedhaimai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 586","lb":"NK586","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","புத்தன்"],"ch":"nk:26"},"nk:587":{"book":"neelakesi","num":586,"t":["தீயுழப் பேசெயுந் தீவினை யென்பது","வாயுழப் பாம்வழி யேபுகுந் தாயினி","நோயுழப் பாகிய நுஞ்செய்கை யாவையும்","நீயுழப் பாய்பிறர்க் கேயுழந் தாயால்."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi takes the Buddha's own account of the evil deed as what brings burning pain and turns it on him: all his own deeds that were pain endured for others he must himself suffer, since he laboured only for others.","n":"The last words, பிறர்க்கே உழந்தாய், repeat the praise of the Buddha in Kundalakesi's invocation and in Neelakesi 472 (பிறர்க்கே உழந்தான்); here the praise becomes the charge. வாயுழப்பாம் வழி (line 2) is compressed, and whether the pain meant is the giver's own or that of those he gave away (his wife and children, 472, 565) is not settled by the verse.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vinai","eegai","idumbai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 587","lb":"NK587","ev":"","cl":[["by the Buddhist definition, a deed that brings pain is an evil deed whose fruit the doer must suffer, so the Bodhisattva's painful self-giving binds him","jain","asserted",4,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","புத்தனார்"],"ch":"nk:27"},"nk:588":{"book":"neelakesi","num":587,"t":["உள்ளம் வினையென வோதினை யேற்செய்கை","யெள்ளின் றுணையுமஃ தின்மை யினைந்தெனக்","கொள்ளுந் திறமென்னை கூறாய் குணந்தினிக்","கள்ளமல் லாலென்றுங் கட்டுரை யாயால்."],"p":"","r":"If the Buddha teaches that the mind is the deed, Neelakesi asks how he can hold that bodily action is not karma even to the size of a sesame seed, and says he never states a quality without deceit.","n":"The Buddhist position under attack is that karma is intention (உள்ளம் வினை); Neelakesi answers it in this verse and the next seven by arguing that action cannot be left out of karma.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","manam","vaadhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 588","lb":"NK588","ev":"","cl":[["the mind (intention) alone is karma, and the bodily act is not karma","buddhist","refuted",1,"neelakesi"],["bodily action is karma too, not only the mind behind it","jain","asserted",2,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","புத்தனார்"],"ch":"nk:27"},"nk:589":{"book":"neelakesi","num":588,"t":["நின்னுடை யுள்ளமுஞ் செய்கையு மொன்றெனிற்","றன்னிடை யெய்துந் தரும தருமிமற்","றென்னிடைக் கொண்டிலை யெங்குப்பெற் றாயிது","முன்னுடம் பாட்டின் முரணுள தாமால்."],"p":"","r":"If mind and action are one for the Buddha, Neelakesi asks where he got the merit and demerit that arise within the doer, since it contradicts what he agreed before.","n":"தரும அதருமி (line 2) is read as merit and demerit settling in the self; the verse uses the Buddhist's earlier concession (முன்னுடம்பாடு) against him, a debate move rather than a doctrine of its own.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vinai","manam","vaadhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 589","lb":"NK589","ev":"","cl":[["if mind and action are identical, the merit and demerit that settle in the doer cannot be accounted for","jain","asserted",2,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","புத்தனார்"],"ch":"nk:27"},"nk:590":{"book":"neelakesi","num":589,"t":["சித்தமுஞ் செய்கையும் வேறென்றி யேயெனி","லொத்த வினையுடன் பாடின்றி யாமினித்","துத்த லையாதின் வழித்தெனச் சொல்லுதி","யொத்திய வல்ல துரையலை யாயால்."],"p":"","r":"If instead the Buddha says mind and action are different, Neelakesi says that without an agreed account of the deed he cannot say from what the eating of its fruit follows, and he speaks what does not fit.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","manam","vaadhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 590","lb":"NK590","ev":"","cl":[["if mind and action are separate, the Buddhist cannot say what the experience of a deed's fruit follows from","jain","asserted",3,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","புத்தனார்"],"ch":"nk:27"},"nk:591":{"book":"neelakesi","num":590,"t":["கண்டுணர்ந் தார்வத்தி னாற்செய்கை யாதலை","யுண்டெனி னாற்குண மொன்றினுக் கொட்டினை","பிண்டிநீ ழலவன் பேரறஞ் செர்தலிற்","கொண்டநின் கோளின்கட் குற்றமுண் டாமோ."],"p":"","r":"If the Buddha grants that action arises from seeing, knowing and desire, Neelakesi says he has conceded several qualities to one bearer, and asks what fault there is in his view now that he has come to the great dharma of the one under the pindi tree.","n":"பிண்டி நீழலவன் is the Jina under the asoka tree: Neelakesi claims that the Buddha's own chain of seeing, knowing, desire and action presupposes one lasting bearer, and so lands him in the Jain teaching. செர்தலிற் (line 3) is read as சேர்தலின்.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vinai","samayam","vaadhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 591","lb":"NK591","ev":"","cl":[["an action that arises from seeing, knowing and desire implies one bearer of all these qualities","jain","asserted",2,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","புத்தனார்"],"ch":"nk:27"},"nk:592":{"book":"neelakesi","num":591,"t":["விளைவத னால்வினை யாக்குமென் பார்சொ","லுளைவதிங் கென்செய வோவுணர் வில்லாய்","தளைபெய்து வைத்தென்னைத் தம்பொறி யெல்லா","மளைவது நன்றிது தானற மாமேல்."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi asks why the words of those who say a deed makes karma by what it brings about should trouble him, and says that if his teaching were dharma, it would be good to bind a person in fetters and handle all their senses.","n":"The second half is read as a reductio: if only the mind makes karma, an act done to a bound and unwilling body would carry no fault. Whose senses are meant (தம் பொறி) and who is bound (என்னை) are not certain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vinai","manam","kolai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 592","lb":"NK592","ev":"","cl":[["a deed makes karma by what it brings about, not by the intention alone","jain","asserted",1,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","புத்தனார்"],"ch":"nk:27"},"nk:593":{"book":"neelakesi","num":592,"t":["ஆர்வத்தி னால்வினை யாக்கு மெனச்சொல்லி","னார்வத்தைச் சித்தமென் றாருரைப் பாரினிச்","சேர்வித்த துப்பினிற் செய்கையு மாதலை","நேர்வித்த வாறது நீயறி யாயால்."],"p":"","r":"If the Buddha says karma is made by desire, Neelakesi asks who would call desire the mind, and says that action too arises from the experience that desire has joined, a link he does not know.","n":"ஆர்வம் (desire) and சித்தம் (mind) are kept apart here; துப்பு, used through 593 to 596, is read as experience or enjoyment of objects.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vinai","avaa","manam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 593","lb":"NK593","ev":"","cl":[["desire is not the same as mind, and action arises from the experience that desire joins","jain","asserted",2,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","புத்தனார்"],"ch":"nk:27"},"nk:594":{"book":"neelakesi","num":593,"t":["உணர்வினை ஆர்வ மெனவுரைப் பாயேற்","புணரும் பிறர்கடம் பொற்றொடி யார்மே","லுணர்வன்ற தார்வ முழப்பெனச் சொல்லி","னிணர்பிரி யாத்துப்பி னால்வினை யன்றாய்."],"p":"","r":"If the Buddha calls awareness desire, Neelakesi says, then desire for other men's women would be awareness; and if he says such desire is pain and not awareness, the experience inseparable from it would no longer be karma.","n":"The example of desire for others' wives (பிறர்கள் தம் பொற்றொடியார்) tests the Buddhist equation of desire with awareness; line 4 (இணர் பிரியாத் துப்பு) is obscure and the conclusion is reconstructed.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vinai","kaamam","manam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 594","lb":"NK594","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","புத்தனார்"],"ch":"nk:27"},"nk:595":{"book":"neelakesi","num":594,"t":["சித்திமுடைச் செய்கை செய்வினை யாதலின்","சித்த முடைத்துப்புஞ் செய்வினை யாம்பிற","சித்தம் வினையெனச் செப்புத லாலெங்குச்","சித்தமுண் டவ்வழிச் செய்கையு முண்டே."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi argues that since action done with mind is a deed, experience done with mind is a deed too, and since the Buddha calls mind karma, wherever there is mind there is also action.","n":"The argument turns the Buddhist premise into its opposite: if intention is karma and intention never comes without doing, action can never be left out. சித்திமுடை (line 1) is read as சித்தமுடை.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","manam","vaadhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 595","lb":"NK595","ev":"","cl":[["wherever there is mind there is action, so action cannot be excluded from karma","jain","asserted",4,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","புத்தனார்"],"ch":"nk:27"},"nk:596":{"book":"neelakesi","num":595,"t":["செய்கையி னாற்றுப்பு மாக்கியத் துப்பினிற்","செய்கையு மாம்வகை செப்புவித் தேனினிப்","பொய்கைசெய் தேசொன்ன பூவொடு வித்துரை","வைகவென் றாண்மல ருண்கண் மடவாள்."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi says she has made the Buddha state how action produces experience and experience action, and tells him to set aside his tale of seed and flower, made up as a lie.","n":"பொய்கை செய்து (line 3) puns on பொய் (a lie): the seed-and-flower analogy of 576 is called an invention. The poet names the speaker by an epithet, the young woman with flower-like eyes.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","vaadhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 596","lb":"NK596","ev":"Neelakesi closes her argument on mind and action and dismisses the Buddha's seed-and-flower analogy.","cl":[["action gives rise to experience and experience to action, so neither is karma without the other","jain","asserted",1,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","புத்தனார்"],"ch":"nk:27"},"nk:597":{"book":"neelakesi","num":596,"t":["இருவகைப் பீசத் தியல்வு மழித்துத்","திருவகைத் தேவொடு பூவுஞ் சிதைத்தே","மருவுகை யாய்நின் மதுரஞ்செய் மாவும்","தருவனை யாயிற் றகரு மதுபோல்."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi says that, having destroyed the nature of both kinds of seed and broken his two kinds of god and his flower, the sweetened mango he offers will break in the same way if he brings it.","n":"The two seeds and the two kinds of deva look back to the Buddha's analogies in 576 to 578 (the lac-stained pomegranate seed; the good and evil sons who become god and hell-being); the sweetened mango is a further analogy she pre-empts. The lines are compressed and the reading is tentative.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["piravi","vaadhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 597","lb":"NK597","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","புத்தனார்"],"ch":"nk:27"},"nk:598":{"book":"neelakesi","num":597,"t":["வலிசெய்து பீசத்தின் மாண்பு மழித்திட்","டலிசெய்து விட்டே னமையு மதன்மேற்","பலிசையி னீசொல்லும் பாடங்க ளெல்லாம்","நலிவனொன் றொன்றா நடுவுணர்ந் தென்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi says she has by force stripped the seed of its worth and left it barren, and that she will crush one by one, judging fairly, all the texts he cites in his boasting.","n":"அலிசெய்து (made sterile) completes the seed image. பலிசையின் (line 3) is obscure; 'boasting' is a guess. நடுவுணர்ந்து claims impartiality for the Jain side.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vaadhu","naduvu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 598","lb":"NK598","ev":"Neelakesi declares the seed analogy defeated and promises to refute the Buddha's other texts.","cl":[],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","புத்தனார்"],"ch":"nk:27"},"nk:599":{"book":"neelakesi","num":598,"t":["பிறந்தவப் பிண்டம் வினையினோ டாங்கே","யிறந்தன வெத்திறத் தின்னுமற் றென்றாற்","சிறந்தவத் தேவெய்திச் சேர்தலுஞ் சீரா","தறஞ்செய் தறானு மவம்பிற வன்றே."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi says that the aggregate that was born perished there along with its deed, so it cannot eat its fruit afterwards; then reaching that excellent heaven does not hold, and doing dharma is itself in vain.","n":"The argument is the Jain charge that momentariness destroys moral reward: if the doer ends with the act, heaven earned by merit has no recipient and virtue loses its point. பிண்டம் is read as the aggregate person of the Buddhist account.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","marumai","kandham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 599","lb":"NK599","ev":"","cl":[["if the person who acted perishes with the act, no one remains to receive its fruit, and doing dharma is in vain","jain","asserted",4,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","புத்தனார்"],"ch":"nk:27"},"nk:600":{"book":"neelakesi","num":599,"t":["உரந்தனை யாது மொடுக்ககி லாகிச்","சுரந்தபல் குற்றஞ் சொலக்கேட் டிருந்தான்","பரந்தினி நீசொன்ன பல்வழி யெல்லாங்","கரந்தனை போதலைக் காட்டுவன் கேணீ."],"p":"","r":"The Buddha, unable to hold back his strength, sits hearing the many faults she pours out, and then says he will show her how she has hidden the truth in all the many paths she has laid out, and bids her listen.","n":"Lines 1 and 2 are the narrator's; lines 3 and 4 are the Buddha's speech. This is one of the few places the narrator shows the Buddha's state of mind, and it is not flattering.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vaadhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 600","lb":"NK600","ev":"The Buddha listens to Neelakesi's charges and begins his reply.","cl":[],"ag":["புத்தனார்"],"ec":[],"spk":["புத்தனார்","நீலகேசி"],"ch":"nk:27"},"nk:601":{"book":"neelakesi","num":600,"t":["அரக்கொடு பீச மறக்கெட்ட வாற்றல்","கரப்பது போலிடைக் காண்பரி தாகி","மரத்திடை சென்று மலரின்கட் டோன்றிப்","பரக்குமென் றேன்பயம் பைந்தொடி யென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"The Buddha says, addressing her as the woman of fresh bangles, that he meant the fruit is like the power of a seed treated with lac: it hides between, hard to see, passes through the tree and appears and spreads in the flower.","n":"This is the Buddha's restatement of the lac-and-seed analogy of 576, answering Neelakesi's objection in 583 that the lac's colour is not seen even in the sprout. Neelakesi answers it in 603 to 610 (the gap in the branch, 610).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","santhaanam","piravi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 601","lb":"NK601","ev":"","cl":[["a deed's power passes unseen through the continuum, as a seed's lac colour passes hidden through the tree to the flower","buddhist","refuted",4,"buddha"]],"ag":["புத்தனார்"],"ec":[],"spk":["புத்தனார்","நீலகேசி"],"ch":"nk:27"},"nk:602":{"book":"neelakesi","num":601,"t":["அன்னணஞ் செய்தா னறங்கெட் டவன்வழித்","துன்னுஞ் சந்தானத் தொடர்ச்சி நிகழ்ச்சியிற்","பின்னை யதுபெறு மாதலின் யான்கண்ட","நன்னெறி நின்னா லறிவரி தென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"The Buddha says that, in the same way, though the doer and his deed have perished, the continuity of the continuum that runs on from him later receives the fruit, so the good path he saw is hard for her to know.","n":"The Buddha concedes that the doer perishes (அறங்கெட்டவன், line 1) and puts the fruit in the continuum (சந்தானத் தொடர்ச்சி); Neelakesi attacks this continuum in 603 to 610.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["santhaanam","vinai","marumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 602","lb":"NK602","ev":"","cl":[["the doer perishes, but the continuum running on from him receives the fruit of his deed","buddhist","refuted",2,"buddha"]],"ag":["புத்தனார்"],"ec":[],"spk":["புத்தனார்","நீலகேசி"],"ch":"nk:27"},"nk:603":{"book":"neelakesi","num":602,"t":["கந்தங்க ளெல்லாங் கடையறக் கெட்டக்க","ணந்தமி லாக்குற்ற மாமெனச் சொல்லுஞ்","சந்தங்க டம்முட் சவலைச் சந்தானமும்","வந்தஃ தெவன் செய்யும் வாமமாற் றென்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi asks what, once all the aggregates have perished without remainder, a feeble continuum arising among them could do, and bids the Buddha answer.","n":"வாம (line 4) is read as a vocative of வாமன், a name of the Buddha (Manimekalai 30 calls him வாமன்): 'answer, Vaman'. Line 2 (அந்தமிலாக் குற்றமாம் எனச் சொல்லும்) is obscure: it may report a Buddhist saying that the aggregates' total perishing would be an endless fault.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kandham","santhaanam","vinai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 603","lb":"NK603","ev":"","cl":[["a continuum left after the aggregates have wholly perished can carry nothing","jain","asserted",4,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","புத்தனார்"],"ch":"nk:27"},"nk:604":{"book":"neelakesi","num":603,"t":["ஆற்ற மகன்கெட்ட போழ்தே யமரிற்","றோற்றமு மென்னை துடித விமானத்து","ளேற்ற தவத்தவன் றேவென லென்கொல்","சாற்றுஞ்சந் தானத்தைச் சந்தித்துக் காட்டாய்."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi asks how, when the man who did the deeds perished, there can be an appearing among the gods, and why the ascetic is said to have become a god in the Tushita palace, and tells the Buddha to show the continuum he proclaims by joining the two.","n":"துடித விமானம் is the Tushita heaven, where Buddhist tradition places the Bodhisattva's last birth before he came down as the Buddha; Neelakesi turns the Buddha's own story into a test case for rebirth without a self.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["santhaanam","thevar","piravi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 604","lb":"NK604","ev":"","cl":[["the Bodhisattva who perished was reborn as a god in the Tushita heaven","buddhist","refuted",3,"neelakesi"],["rebirth as a god cannot be claimed unless the continuum joining the dead man and the god is shown","jain","asserted",4,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","புத்தனார்"],"ch":"nk:27"},"nk:605":{"book":"neelakesi","num":604,"t":["மக்க ளுடம்பொடு தேவ ருடம்பிடை","மிக்க விடம்பெரு வெள்ளிடை யாலிது","புக்க தொடர்வில்லை யாதலி னீகொண்ட","பக்க முடன்கெடு மாலென்னை பாவம்."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi says there is a vast empty space between a human body and a god's body, so no connection enters across it, and the side the Buddha has taken perishes with that; what a pity, she says.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["santhaanam","thevar","yaakkai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 605","lb":"NK605","ev":"","cl":[["a continuum cannot cross the gap between a human body and a god's body","jain","asserted",2,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","புத்தனார்"],"ch":"nk:27"},"nk:606":{"book":"neelakesi","num":605,"t":["புளிபொறி வாழ்நிழ றண்மதி யின்ன","தெளிவுள தாஞ்செல வின்முடி வென்னில்","விளிபவ னுளபொழு தேவினை துய்க்கு","மொளிகிளர் தேவ னுளமென்னு மாறோ."],"p":"","r":"If the Buddha says that examples like the tamarind, the seal, the shadow of a living thing and the cool moon make plain how transmission ends, Neelakesi asks whether the radiant god who eats the deed exists while the dying man still exists.","n":"The four examples are read as the Buddhist illustrations of a cause producing an effect without passing into it (sourness felt at the sight of tamarind, a seal and its impression, a body and its shadow, the moon and its reflection); Neelakesi's point is that in each the cause is present while the effect arises, so the dead man would have to coexist with the god. The identification of புளி and பொறி is a reading.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["santhaanam","piravi","vaadhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 606","lb":"NK606","ev":"","cl":[["rebirth can be explained by examples where an effect arises at a distance without anything passing over","buddhist","refuted",2,"neelakesi"],["in each such example the cause still exists while the effect appears","jain","asserted",3,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","புத்தனார்"],"ch":"nk:27"},"nk:607":{"book":"neelakesi","num":606,"t":["சென்றில தேலிடை யற்றுழிக் தோன்றுக","வென்றலு மிங்குள தோவது தானெனி","னொன்றல பல்பொரு டாமொளி யாதிய","நின்றல வோவுல கெங்கும் நிறைந்தே."],"p":"","r":"If nothing travels across and the effect simply appears where there is a gap, Neelakesi asks whether that is ever seen here, for then light and many other things would stand filling the whole world.","n":"The reductio is that an effect arising across a gap with nothing crossing would let anything appear anywhere. Line 1 (யற்றுழிக் தோன்றுக) has a doubtful consonant, and the sense of lines 3 to 4 is reconstructed.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["santhaanam","vaadhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 607","lb":"NK607","ev":"","cl":[["an effect that arises across a gap with nothing passing over would appear everywhere at once","jain","asserted",4,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","புத்தனார்"],"ch":"nk:27"},"nk:608":{"book":"neelakesi","num":607,"t":["தண்மதி கெட்டதன் சாயை யுடன்பெற","லுண்மை யுண்டாயினி னொத்துரை யொட்டுவன்","கண்மதி யாததெங் காரிய மேயின்ன","வெண்மதி யாயை விலக்குநர் யாரோ."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi says that if the cool moon perished and its reflection still came, she would accept his word; what the eye does not see is no business of hers, and who could stop one so simple-minded.","n":"வெண்மதியாய் (line 4) puns on the white moon of the example and a blank mind: the Buddha is mocked with his own image.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["santhaanam","vaadhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 608","lb":"NK608","ev":"","cl":[["a reflection never remains after its original has perished, so the moon example cannot show rebirth after death","jain","asserted",1,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","புத்தனார்"],"ch":"nk:27"},"nk:609":{"book":"neelakesi","num":608,"t":["மதியென்று மில்லெனி னில்லொளி தானும்","புதியதும் பாழது பொன்றிய போழ்தே","கதியினை நாட்டிய காட்டி யெமக்கிங்","கதுவென்னை சொல்லிய வாறுரை யாத்தா."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi says that if the moon is not there, its light is not there either, and the new thing is empty once the old has perished; she bids the one who set up the destinies of rebirth show how that is said.","n":"ஆத்தா (line 4) is read as a vocative of ஆப்தன், the trustworthy teacher, used here with irony; கதியினை நாட்டிய refers to the Buddha's teaching of the destinies into which beings are born.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["santhaanam","piravi","vaadhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 609","lb":"NK609","ev":"","cl":[["when the source has perished, what arises after it has nothing in it","jain","asserted",2,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","புத்தனார்"],"ch":"nk:27"},"nk:610":{"book":"neelakesi","num":609,"t":["அடியொடு பூவி னிடையற வின்றி","நெடியதோர் கொம்பினை நீமறந் தாயோ","மடியிலார் செய்த மானுயர் தெய்வப்","படியிது வென்றிடை பாழ்செய்திட் டாயால்."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi asks whether the Buddha has forgotten the long branch that joins root to flower without a gap, since in calling a man's becoming a god the work of the unwearying he has left an emptiness in between.","n":"She grants his seed-to-flower analogy only to show what it needs: an unbroken branch. Between death and the god's birth there is none. மானுயர் தெய்வப் படி (line 3) is read as the pattern of a human become a god; மடியிலார் may be the Buddhas or the Buddha himself.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["santhaanam","piravi","vaadhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 610","lb":"NK610","ev":"","cl":[["the seed-to-flower continuity holds only because an unbroken branch joins them, and no such link joins a dead man to a god","jain","asserted",1,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","புத்தனார்"],"ch":"nk:27"},"nk:611":{"book":"neelakesi","num":610,"t":["தோற்ற மிடையற வில்லாத் தொடர்ச்சியைச்","சாற்றுதும் யாமுஞ்சந் தான மெனவென்னிற்","காற்றுஞ் சுடருங் கரகத்துட் டாரையு","மேற்றன வல்ல விரண்டுண்மை யாலே."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi takes up the Buddha's plea that his school too speaks of an unbroken continuity of arisings, called santana, and answers that the wind, the flame and the stream poured from a water-pot will not serve as examples of it, since in each two things are real.","n":"The 'two' of line 4 is taken as a lasting stuff and its passing states (air, oil or water beside the gust, the tongue of fire, the falling stream), so each of the Buddhist's own examples is turned into a case of something that persists; the verse does not spell the two out.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["santhaanam","nilaiyaamai","vaadhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 611","lb":"NK611","ev":"","cl":[["the arisings of a thing form an unbroken continuity (santana), as a flame, the wind or a stream does","buddhist","refuted",2,"buddha"],["the flame, the wind and the stream are not examples of bare succession, since something real persists in each","jain","asserted",4,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","புத்தனார்"],"ch":"nk:28"},"nk:612":{"book":"neelakesi","num":611,"t":["பித்தொடு கனவிடைப் பேயின் மயங்குநர்க்","கொத்த வுணர்வுண்மை யொட்டல தென்செய","தத்துவ மாயசந் தான முளதெனிற்","புத்தநின் சொற்கள் பொருளில வேயால்."],"p":"","r":"She puts a dilemma: if the continuity is only a notion, it is like the knowing of the mad, the dreamer or the possessed, which does not answer to anything; if it is a real thing, then, Buddha, his words have no meaning.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["santhaanam","vaadhu","meyyunarvu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 612","lb":"NK612","ev":"","cl":[["if the continuity is a real thing, the teaching that nothing lasts loses its meaning","jain","asserted",3,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","புத்தனார்"],"ch":"nk:28"},"nk:613":{"book":"neelakesi","num":612,"t":["மூத்தல் வகையு முதலத னைம்மையு","மாத்த கணந்தோ றலர்ந்த நிகழ்ச்சியுஞ்","சாத்திரத் தாலத் தவிரன்கட் டாழ்ச்சியும்","போத்தந்து காட்டுந் திறமென்னை புத்தா."],"p":"","r":"She asks the Buddha how, on his account, he can show the stages of ageing, the fine smallness of a thing's beginning, the growth that opens out moment by moment, and its decline.","n":"Ageing and growth by degrees need something that grows and ages; the point is implied by the question rather than stated. The third line (சாத்திரத்தால் அத் தவிரன்கட் டாழ்ச்சியும்) is obscure and its sense is guessed.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kanam","nilaiyaamai","puththan","vaadhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 613","lb":"NK613","ev":"","cl":[["ageing, growth and decline by degrees cannot be shown if each moment perishes wholly","jain","asserted",4,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","புத்தனார்"],"ch":"nk:28"},"nk:614":{"book":"neelakesi","num":613,"t":["கப்பம் பலபல கண்ணிமை யானுக்குத்","துப்பு முரைத்தென்னை தோன்றிய வக்கணத்","தொக்க விளைவுரைப் பாய்பின் வழிவழிச்","செப்புதி யேற்செய்கை சென்றுசென் றுண்டே."],"p":"","r":"What use, she asks, is telling of the many aeons and the merit of the unblinking one, if he says the fruit comes in the very moment a thing arises; and if he says it comes down the line afterwards, then the deed goes on and on and exists.","n":"The aeons (கப்பம்) are the Bodhisattva's long striving told in the birth stories; she makes them pointless under momentariness, or else makes the deed a lasting thing.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","kanam","nilaiyaamai","vaadhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 614","lb":"NK614","ev":"","cl":[["the fruit of a deed arises in the same moment as the deed","buddhist","refuted",3,"buddha"],["if a deed's fruit comes later down the series, the deed persists until then","jain","asserted",4,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","புத்தனார்"],"ch":"nk:28"},"nk:615":{"book":"neelakesi","num":614,"t":["காரண மென்னினுஞ் சென்றின்மை யாலில்லை","ஏரண தேவ விழுதைமை சொல்லுதி","யூருணி நீர்நிறை வுண்டோ யுறுபுனல்","வாரண வாய்க்கால் வரவில்லை யாக்கால்."],"p":"","r":"Even if he calls the earlier moment a cause, she says, it is no cause, since nothing of it goes into the next; mocking him as a god of logic who speaks folly, she asks whether the village tank fills when no water comes down the channel.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vaadhu","nilaiyaamai","pedhaimai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 615","lb":"NK615","ev":"","cl":[["a cause must pass something into its effect, as water must flow down the channel to fill a tank","jain","asserted",1,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","புத்தனார்"],"ch":"nk:28"},"nk:616":{"book":"neelakesi","num":615,"t":["நில்லா தறக்கெடுந் தோன்றிற்றுந் தோற்றமு","மெல்லா வகையினு மில்லதற் கேலில்லை","சொல்லாய் தொடர்ச்சி தொடர்ச்சியென் றேநின்று","பல்லார் களையும் படுத்துண்ணும் பண்பா."],"p":"","r":"What has arisen perishes utterly without staying, and the wholly non-existent cannot arise, she says, so his endless talk of continuity is only words, and she calls him one whose way is to lay many low and feed on them.","n":"The last line is abuse: it charges the Buddhist teaching with deceiving people for a living, not only with error.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["santhaanam","nilaiyaamai","vaadhu","vanjam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 616","lb":"NK616","ev":"","cl":[["what is wholly non-existent cannot come to arise","jain","asserted",2,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","புத்தனார்"],"ch":"nk:28"},"nk:617":{"book":"neelakesi","num":616,"t":["சுடருடைத் தோற்றத் தொடர்ச்சியைச் சொல்லி","யிடருடைத் தாக விவையிவை காட்டி","யடர்படுத் திட்டாட் கதற்கொன்று நாடிச்","சடருடை வாசனை சாதிக்க லுற்றான்."],"p":"","r":"The narrator tells that when the Buddha had argued the continuity of arising by the flame and she had shown each point faulty and pressed him hard, he searched for an answer and set out to establish the doctrine of vasana, the trace that carries over.","n":"The word before வாசனை (சடருடை) is doubtful; the sense that he now turns to the trace is clear from the verses that follow.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vaasanai","santhaanam","vaadhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 617","lb":"NK617","ev":"Pressed on the flame example, the Buddha turns to defend continuity by the doctrine of the trace (vasana).","cl":[],"ag":["புத்தனார்","நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"ch":"nk:28"},"nk:618":{"book":"neelakesi","num":617,"t":["கெட்டபி னாற்றல்க ளெல்லாம் வழியதற்","கொட்டு நறுமலர் வாசமோ டோட்டின்கட்","சுட்டுவ தொப்ப வெனச்சொல்லு நீகண்ட","விட்ட மிவற்றோ டியையல தென்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi states his new claim, that after a moment perishes all its powers cling to what follows as the scent of fragrant flowers is found in a pot, and says that the tenet he holds does not fit these examples.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vaasanai","nilaiyaamai","vaadhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 618","lb":"NK618","ev":"","cl":[["after a moment perishes its powers pass into the next, as flower scent stays in a pot","buddhist","refuted",2,"buddha"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","புத்தனார்"],"ch":"nk:28"},"nk:619":{"book":"neelakesi","num":618,"t":["முன்னைய துள்ள பொழுதத்து மற்றதன்","பின்னைய தில்லை யதுபெற்ற போழ்து","தன்னையு மில்லை யெனச்சொல்லின் வாசனை","யென்னை யியையுந் திறமிவை தானே."],"p":"","r":"While the earlier moment exists the later does not yet, and when the later is had the earlier is gone, she argues, so in what way can a trace join the two.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vaasanai","kanam","vaadhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 619","lb":"NK619","ev":"","cl":[["a trace cannot pass between two moments that never exist together","jain","asserted",4,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","புத்தனார்"],"ch":"nk:28"},"nk:620":{"book":"neelakesi","num":619,"t":["ஓடு மலரு மொருங்குள வாதலிற்","கூடும் வாசமுங் குற்றமொன் றில்லெனிற்","கேடும் பிறப்பு முடனில்லை யாதலிற்","கூடல வென்பது கூறின மன்றே."],"p":"","r":"If he answers that pot and flower exist together and so the scent can join them without fault, she replies that perishing and birth are never together, which is just why she said the moments do not join.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vaasanai","kanam","vaadhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 620","lb":"NK620","ev":"","cl":[["scent passes because pot and flower are present together","buddhist","refuted",2,"buddha"],["the perishing of one moment and the birth of the next are not simultaneous, so nothing joins them","jain","asserted",3,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","புத்தனார்"],"ch":"nk:28"},"nk:621":{"book":"neelakesi","num":620,"t":["கெடுவத னுண்ணின்று தோன்றுவ தன்கண்","வடுவறச் செல்வது வாசனை யென்னப்","படுவதொன் றன்றுநின் பக்கத்தை யெல்லாஞ்","சுடுவதோர் கொள்ளி சுவடித்த வாறே."],"p":"","r":"Something that goes whole from within the perishing moment into the arising one cannot be called a mere trace, she says; posited so, it is a firebrand that burns his whole side.","n":"If whatever crosses over crosses entire, it is a lasting thing, which is the Jain's point; the firebrand turns the Buddhist's own device against him.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vaasanai","nilaiyaamai","vaadhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 621","lb":"NK621","ev":"","cl":[["what passes entire from one moment to the next is a persisting thing, not a trace","jain","asserted",2,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","புத்தனார்"],"ch":"nk:28"},"nk:622":{"book":"neelakesi","num":621,"t":["போதின்க ணின்றும்புத் தோட்டிற் பொருந்திய","தாதின நாற்றந்தண் ணீருக்குச் சொல்லுதி","யோதன கந்த முடன்கெடின் வாசனை","யாதின தாகு மறிந்துரை யேழாய்."],"p":"","r":"He speaks of the pollen's smell that came from the blossom into the new pot as passing to the cool water, she says; but if the scent perishes along with the flower, whose would the trace be? She tells him, as one who does not know, to answer with understanding.","n":"The image is the old one of the new pot that perfumes the water put in it. ஓதன in line 3 is unclear; it is read as 'the scent that is spoken of'.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vaasanai","vaadhu","pedhaimai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 622","lb":"NK622","ev":"","cl":[["a scent must belong to some bearer; if the flower's scent perished with it, the trace in the water belongs to nothing","jain","asserted",4,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","புத்தனார்"],"ch":"nk:28"},"nk:623":{"book":"neelakesi","num":622,"t":["வாசனை வாசனை யென்று வழிவழி","நீசொன்ன நீசொன்ன நேர்வதிங் கென்செய்ய","பாசன மாகிய பாதிரிப் பூவினைப்","பூசின தெப்பொருள் போத்தந்து காட்டாய்."],"p":"","r":"He keeps saying 'trace, trace' along the line, she says, and assenting to it achieves nothing; let him come and show what thing it is that the pathiri flower, kept in the vessel, smeared with its scent.","n":"பாசனம் is taken as the vessel (Sanskrit bhajana): she demands the lasting receiver that a scent needs.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vaasanai","vaadhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 623","lb":"NK623","ev":"","cl":[["a scent is received by some thing, and the Buddhist must name what receives the trace","jain","asserted",4,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","புத்தனார்"],"ch":"nk:28"},"nk:624":{"book":"neelakesi","num":623,"t":["பூவின்கட் கேடும்புத் தோட்டின்கட் டோன்றியு","மேவிய வாசனை யெங்குமுண் டாமெனின்","சோபன மாகச் சுவாகதம் போந்ததென்","றேவன் றிருந்தடி சிந்திமற் றென்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"If he says the trace, perishing in the flower and arising in the new pot, is found everywhere, she says in mockery, then let him hail it as an auspicious arrival and think on the fine feet of his god.","n":"The second half is hard: சுவாகதம் is taken as 'welcome' and the line as sarcasm that a trace found everywhere would be a lasting, all-pervading thing fit to be worshipped; the word division of தேவன் or ஏவன் is not certain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vaasanai","vaadhu","nindhai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 624","lb":"NK624","ev":"","cl":[["the trace perishes in the flower and arises in the pot, and is found everywhere","buddhist","refuted",2,"buddha"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","புத்தனார்"],"ch":"nk:28"},"nk:625":{"book":"neelakesi","num":624,"t":["அதற்கு மறக்கே டுளதெனின் வாச","மதற்குமுண் டாயினி யெல்லையின் றோடு","மிதற்கெண்ணு மில்லெனி னில்லை யதற்கு","முதற்சொன்ன குற்றம் முடிந்தன வாமே."],"p":"","r":"If the trace too perishes utterly, she argues, it must have a trace of its own and the series runs on without end; if he says no such reckoning applies to it, then none applies to the first either, and the faults she named at the start stand.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vaasanai","vaadhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 625","lb":"NK625","ev":"","cl":[["a trace that itself perishes needs a further trace, without end","jain","asserted",2,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","புத்தனார்"],"ch":"nk:28"},"nk:626":{"book":"neelakesi","num":625,"t":["சென்றதென் றாலதன் கேடின்மை யாஞ்செல","வின்றதென் றாலத னாற்றமு மில்லற்க","நன்றிதென் றேசொல்ல நல்லது நாமறிந்","தன்றிதென் றென்முனே யாருரைக் கிற்பார்."],"p":"","r":"If he says the scent went over, it did not perish; if he says it did not go, the smell too is absent; and she boasts that when she, knowing the good, declares this right, no one can say before her that it is not.","n":"The second line's ending (இல்லற்க) is odd; the dilemma of the first two lines is clear.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vaasanai","vaadhu","kedu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 626","lb":"NK626","ev":"","cl":[["either the scent went over and so did not perish, or it did not go and the smell is not there","jain","asserted",1,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","புத்தனார்"],"ch":"nk:28"},"nk:627":{"book":"neelakesi","num":626,"t":["கேடது தான்முழு தாதலி னீசொல்லும்","வீடது வாகலும் வேண்டும் விழுத்தவம்","கூடு பவர்க்குங் குணமில் லவர்க்கு","நீடல தேயுள தாநிரு வாணம்."],"p":"","r":"Since he makes perishing total, she argues, the release he speaks of must be that very perishing, so nirvana would come alike, and briefly, to those who join in great austerity and to those without virtue.","n":"The argument makes Buddhist release a by-product of momentariness, which the Buddhist would deny: the Manimekalai's teacher places release in seeing impermanence and the cessation of craving, not in the perishing of moments. நீடலதே is read as 'not lasting'.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["veedu","thavam","nilaiyaamai","vaadhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 627","lb":"NK627","ev":"","cl":[["if every moment perishes entirely, release is that perishing and comes alike to the ascetic and the vicious","jain","asserted",4,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","புத்தனார்"],"ch":"nk:28"},"nk:628":{"book":"neelakesi","num":627,"t":["தோற்றம் வழியென்று சொல்லுத லாற்றட","மாற்ற மதுவிதன் மார்க்கத்து மாண்பெனச்","சாற்றுதி யேலுந் சவறது வாதலைத்","தேற்றுவன் கேணீ தெருண்டிருந் தென்றாள்"],"p":"","r":"If he declares that, since arising follows down a line, this reply is the excellence of his path, she says she will make plain that it is faulty, and bids him listen with a clear head.","n":"The first two lines are compressed and the exact claim she quotes is uncertain; சவறு is taken as 'fault'.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["santhaanam","vaadhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 628","lb":"NK628","ev":"","cl":[["arising by succession down a line answers the objection and is the strength of the Buddha's path","buddhist","refuted",3,"buddha"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","புத்தனார்"],"ch":"nk:28"},"nk:629":{"book":"neelakesi","num":628,"t":["தன்வழித் தோற்றிக் கெடுமோ வதுகெடப்","பின்வழித் தோற்றம் பெறுமோ விரண்டனு","ளென்வழித் தோற்றமஃ தாமா றிருளற","நின்வழித் தோற்றத்தை நீயுரை யென்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"Does a moment bring forth its successor and then perish, she asks, or does the later arising come when it perishes; of the two, which is his way of arising? Let him say it plainly, without darkness.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kanam","santhaanam","vaadhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 629","lb":"NK629","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","புத்தனார்"],"ch":"nk:28"},"nk:630":{"book":"neelakesi","num":629,"t":["வழியொன்று தோற்றிக் கெடுமேன் மகப்பெற்","றழிகின் றவள்போ லணைவுள தாகு","மொழிகின்ற தேயது காணல தென்னிற்","கழிகின்ற கன்னிக்கோர் காதலற் காட்டாய்."],"p":"","r":"If a moment produces the next and then perishes, she says, there is contact between them, as with a woman who bears a child and dies; if he says the perishing one never meets what follows, let him show a son to a maiden who has died.","n":"A dead virgin has no son: the image makes a cause that never meets its effect as impossible as a child without a mother.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kanam","vaadhu","santhaanam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 630","lb":"NK630","ev":"","cl":[["a cause that never meets its effect cannot produce it","jain","asserted",4,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","புத்தனார்"],"ch":"nk:28"},"nk:631":{"book":"neelakesi","num":630,"t":["பிறப்பித்துக் கேடும்பின் றோற்றக் கெடலும்","இறப்பவுங் கூடா திருதிறத் தானுந்","துறப்பித்த வாறிது தூவெனக் கேட்டை","மறப்பித் துரைத்ததுண் டோசொல்லு வாம."],"p":"","r":"Both ways fail, she says, perishing after giving birth and perishing as the next arises, so he is made to give up both; she then asks him, as Vaman, whether he ever spoke of perishing so as to make it forgotten.","n":"வாம is read as the vocative of வாமன், a name for the Buddha. The second half is obscure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kanam","vaadhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 631","lb":"NK631","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","புத்தனார்"],"ch":"nk:28"},"nk:632":{"book":"neelakesi","num":631,"t":["சுலாப்பல சொல்லிச் சுழன்று விடாதே","துலாத்தலைத் தூக்கமு மோக்கமுங் காட்டி","னிலாத்தலை யல்லா விரண்டு ளொன்றுண்டா","யிலாத்தலை யில்லே லமைவது மன்றே."],"p":"","r":"She tells him not to spin round with many evasions: if he shows the rising and sinking of the two ends of a balance as the arising and perishing of moments, both ends must be there, and if the end that is gone does not exist, the image does not hold.","n":"The balance-beam is the Buddhist's figure for a cause perishing at the same instant as its effect arises; her reply turns on both pans being present together. The third line is read loosely.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kanam","vaadhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 632","lb":"NK632","ev":"","cl":[["the cause's perishing and the effect's arising are simultaneous, like the two ends of a balance beam","buddhist","refuted",2,"buddha"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","புத்தனார்"],"ch":"nk:28"},"nk:633":{"book":"neelakesi","num":632,"t":["இல்லதற் கோயில்லை கேடென்னை காக்கையின்","பல்லதற் கோதார் பருமையு நுண்மையுஞ்","சொல்லிதற் காவதுண் டேலெனச் சொல்லின","ணல்லதற் கல்லது நாப்பெய ராதாள்."],"p":"","r":"For what does not exist there is no perishing, she says, just as no one speaks of the size or fineness of a crow's tooth; the narrator adds that she, whose tongue moves only for the good, asked him whether any word could be said of such a thing.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kanam","vaadhu","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 633","lb":"NK633","ev":"","cl":[["what does not exist cannot perish, as a crow's tooth has no size","jain","asserted",1,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","புத்தனார்"],"ch":"nk:28"},"nk:634":{"book":"neelakesi","num":633,"t":["துலாமில்லை யட்டக மேயது தானு","மிலாமிரு பாற்றலை யேதலை தாமு","நிலாம்வகை யில்லுயர் வோடிழி வெங்கும்","கலாமவை யாய்க்கடை கண்டது பாழே."],"p":"","r":"On his view there is no balance, she concludes: the beam too is absent, the two ends have no way to stand, rising and falling are mixed everywhere, and what is seen at the end is void.","n":"அட்டகம் is taken as the beam or frame of the balance; if it too is momentary, the image collapses. The verse turns the Buddhist figure into emptiness (பாழ்) as a reproach, not a doctrine.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kanam","vaadhu","paazh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 634","lb":"NK634","ev":"","cl":[["if every part is momentary, the balance and its two ends do not exist and the image ends in nothing","jain","asserted",4,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","புத்தனார்"],"ch":"nk:28"},"nk:635":{"book":"neelakesi","num":634,"t":["ஓல குளவி குருட்டெண் ணெய் வாணிச்சி","கோலஞ் சிதையுமென் றெண்ணெயட் டாளென்னும்","பாலகர் பாட்டுரை போன்றது மற்றுநின்","நூலுரை யெல்லா நுழைந்துணர் வார்க்கே."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi tells the Buddha that to anyone who enters into it, all his scripture is like a children's nonsense rhyme about a wasp and a blind oil-woman who poured out her oil for fear her looks would be spoiled.","n":"The rhyme is quoted as a sample of nonsense, not for its content; the insult opens the last round of the debate by calling the whole scripture incoherent rather than one tenet false.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vaadhu","samayam","pedhaimai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 635","lb":"NK635","ev":"","cl":[["the Buddha's scripture, examined closely, does not hold together","jain","asserted",4,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","புத்தனார்"],"ch":"nk:29"},"nk:636":{"book":"neelakesi","num":635,"t":["கோற்றிர ளொன்றா யதன்றலைக் கேயுட","னாற்றியு மோக்கமு நாட்டினை யஃதொப்பத்","தோற்றமுங் கேடுந் தொகுபிண்ட மொன்றிற்குச்","சாற்றுதி யாய்விடிற் றக்கதென் னேனோ."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi takes up the Buddha's image of the balance beam, whose two ends sink and rise at once, and says that if he granted arising and perishing to one gathered whole in the same way she would call it fitting.","n":"The beam is the Buddhist image for a cause ceasing as its effect arises; her point is that the image needs one beam, a single thing that bears both motions, which is what the doctrine of momentary aggregates denies.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vaadhu","kandham","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 636","lb":"NK636","ev":"","cl":[["the cause ceases as the effect arises, like the two ends of a balance beam","buddhist","refuted",2,"neelakesi"],["arising and perishing need one continuing thing to happen in, as the beam's ends need the beam","jain","asserted",3,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","புத்தனார்"],"ch":"nk:29"},"nk:637":{"book":"neelakesi","num":636,"t":["தலையன தாழ்ச்சியு மெழுச்சியு மன்றி","நிலையிலை கோலிடை நீக்கலு நீங்கு","மிலையென வெட்டி னிடுகுறி யொன்றெனிற்","பலவினி யிங்குப் படுவன கேணீ."],"p":"","r":"If he answers that the beam has no standing of its own apart from the sinking and rising of its ends, and that its oneness is only a name given by convention, she tells him to hear the many faults that follow.","n":"இடுகுறி, an assigned name, is the Buddhist account of every whole as a label for parts; the faults are given in 638 and 639.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vaadhu","sol","kandham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 637","lb":"NK637","ev":"","cl":[["the beam is one only by an assigned name; nothing stands besides the motions of its ends","buddhist","refuted",3,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","புத்தனார்"],"ch":"nk:29"},"nk:638":{"book":"neelakesi","num":637,"t":["கோலுந் தலையு முடனில்லை யாமெனின்","மேலு முரைத்தன மேயதன் மேற்பழி","வாலுந் தலையு நடுவு மவையின்றிப்","பாலும் படுவதோ ராவுள தென்னாய்."],"p":"","r":"If beam and ends do not exist together, she says, she has answered that already, and a further fault follows: he would have to say there is a cow that gives milk without tail, head or middle.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vaadhu","kandham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 638","lb":"NK638","ev":"","cl":[["the whole and its parts do not exist together","buddhist","refuted",1,"neelakesi"],["a whole that does work cannot be denied while its parts are granted","jain","asserted",4,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","புத்தனார்"],"ch":"nk:29"},"nk:639":{"book":"neelakesi","num":638,"t":["புணரிய தாமவை பொய்யெனச் சொன்னேற்","குணர்வதங் குண்மையு மொட்டுவை யாயிற்","குணகுணி யாயு மவயவி யாயும்","பிணபிணக் கெய்துமப் பேரிடர் செய்தாய்."],"p":"","r":"If he says compounded things are unreal yet grants that the awareness of them is real, she argues, he falls into the tangle of quality and bearer, part and whole, and has brought that great trouble on himself.","n":"Line 1's சொன்னேற்கு is first person and does not fit the sense well; the reading takes the line as stating his position.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vaadhu","gunam","arivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 639","lb":"NK639","ev":"","cl":[["compounded things are unreal though the cognition of them is real","buddhist","refuted",1,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","புத்தனார்"],"ch":"nk:29"},"nk:640":{"book":"neelakesi","num":639,"t":["இருபிண்ட முப்பிண்ட மெண்ணில்பல் பிண்டம்","வருபிண்ட மேற்பல மற்றவை நீங்க","வொருபிண்டங் கொண்டாங் குயிர்க்குறுதி யிட்டுத்","திருவுண்ட செய்கையிற் செய்தவ னாக."],"p":"","r":"Two masses, three masses, countless masses, and more to come: she bids him leave them, take one whole and set the soul's good in it, and so act as one blessed.","n":"பிண்டம் is a lump or aggregate; she mocks the multiplying of momentary heaps and urges a single bearer of the self's welfare. The last line is hard to construe.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["uyir","kandham","vaadhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 640","lb":"NK640","ev":"","cl":[["the soul's good needs one enduring bearer, not a series of aggregates","jain","asserted",3,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","புத்தனார்"],"ch":"nk:29"},"nk:641":{"book":"neelakesi","num":640,"t":["வினைசெய்தான் றுய்க்குமோ வேறொருவ னேயோ","வனைவரு மோவில்லை யோசொல்லா யோவென்னக்","கனைகடலி னுண்மணலிற் கண்ணினையு மீந்தா","னினைவகைய கேளென் றெடுத்துரைக்க லுற்றான்."],"p":"","r":"When Neelakesi asks whether the doer of a deed enjoys its fruit, or another, or all, or no one, the one who had given his eyes more times than the sea has grains of sand begins to answer.","n":"The Buddha is named only by his gifts in former births (eyes given beyond counting), the very gifts she will turn against him in 649 and 653.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","payan","saathakam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 641","lb":"NK641","ev":"Neelakesi asks the Buddha who reaps the fruit of a deed, and he begins his answer.","cl":[],"ag":["நீலகேசி","புத்தனார்"],"ec":[],"ch":"nk:29"},"nk:642":{"book":"neelakesi","num":641,"t":["அவனேயு மென்னேன் பிறனேயு மென்னே","னவனும் பிறனு மவரேயு மென்னே","னெவனேயு மெய்தா னெனலேயு மாகா","வெவனோ விதுதுய்ப்பா னின்னணங்கே ளென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"The Buddha says he does not call the reaper the doer, nor another, nor both, nor can it be said no one reaches the fruit, and bids her hear who it is.","n":"A fourfold denial; Neelakesi answers it in 645 to 651 by holding that what is not the same must be other.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","payan","kandham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 642","lb":"NK642","ev":"","cl":[["the one who reaps a deed's fruit is neither the doer, nor another, nor both, nor no one","buddhist","refuted",1,"buddha"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["புத்தனார்","நீலகேசி"],"ch":"nk:29"},"nk:643":{"book":"neelakesi","num":642,"t":["அதுவேயு மென்னேன் பிறிதேயு மென்னேன்","அதுவும் பிறிது மவையேயு மென்னேன்","அதுபோன்ற வல்ல ததன்வழியின் மற்றொன்","றிதுபோல வென்பே னெடுத்துரையுங் கேணீ."],"p":"","r":"He says it is neither that, nor other, nor both, nor like it, but another thing arising in its line, and offers an example.","n":"The key words are அதன் வழியின் மற்றொன்று, another in its line: the doctrine of a continuity without identity, which 646 and 651 attack.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kandham","piravi","vinai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 643","lb":"NK643","ev":"","cl":[["what bears the fruit is neither the same nor other but another in the same line of continuity","buddhist","refuted",3,"buddha"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["புத்தனார்","நீலகேசி"],"ch":"nk:29"},"nk:644":{"book":"neelakesi","num":643,"t":["இடுபீச மன்றா லிறுங்காதி யன்றால்","வடுவாய வவ்விரண்டும் வந்தனவு மன்றால்","நெடிதாய தீங்கரும்பு நெல்லுமே போல","நடுவாக நோக்காய் நறுநுதலா யென்ன."],"p":"","r":"It is not the seed sown, nor the sprout, nor what came from those two, he says, but as with tall sweet sugarcane and paddy; and he bids the fair-browed woman look at it from the middle.","n":"நடுவாக நோக்காய் asks her to look from the middle, the Buddhist middle between identity and difference; Neelakesi takes his own paddy and cane apart in 650 and 651.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kandham","piravi","vaadhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 644","lb":"NK644","ev":"","cl":[["as with paddy and cane, the later growth is neither the seed nor other than its line","buddhist","refuted",3,"buddha"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["புத்தனார்","நீலகேசி"],"ch":"nk:29"},"nk:645":{"book":"neelakesi","num":644,"t":["ஆத்தாவ தன்றேற் பிறிதா மரும்பெற","னாத்தான் வருத்த நீ நாட்டியதியாங் காணேன்","போத்தாகா தேற்குதிரை பெட்டையது போலென்","சாத்தாகு மாகாமை சாதிக்க வல்லையோ."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi says that if it is not the same it is other, and she sees nothing he has established; as a horse that is not male is a mare, can he prove anything that both is and is not so?","n":"She presses the excluded middle against his tetralemma; the first two lines are hard to divide.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vaadhu","arivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 645","lb":"NK645","ev":"","cl":[["what is not the same must be other; there is no third case","jain","asserted",1,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","புத்தனார்"],"ch":"nk:29"},"nk:646":{"book":"neelakesi","num":645,"t":["அவன்போற லானு மவனன்மை யானு","மவன்றன் வழியா னவனாத லானு","மவன்றான் பிறனே யவனென்ற லானு","மவன்றான் பிறனே யெனவழிவ துண்டோ."],"p":"","r":"Whether by resembling the doer, by not being him, by coming in his line, or by being called other, she asks, is there any way the reaper ceases to be another?","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vaadhu","vinai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 646","lb":"NK646","ev":"","cl":[["resemblance and descent do not make the reaper the doer; he remains another","jain","asserted",4,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","புத்தனார்"],"ch":"nk:29"},"nk:647":{"book":"neelakesi","num":646,"t":["கண்போலக் கயலெனினுங்","காடன்றித் தூறெனினும்","பெண்பாலாற் பல்கியும்","எல்லாம் பிறவேயாம்."],"p":"","r":"Though a carp is like an eye, though a thicket is not a forest, though things multiply through the female, all of them are other things.","n":"Three examples answer his three ways in 646: likeness (the carp and the eye), being less than the whole (thicket and forest) and descent (offspring); each still yields another thing. The verse is in short lines, unlike its neighbours.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vaadhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 647","lb":"NK647","ev":"","cl":[["likeness, partiality and descent each leave the thing another","jain","asserted",4,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","புத்தனார்"],"ch":"nk:29"},"nk:648":{"book":"neelakesi","num":647,"t":["அயலானே துய்க்கு மவன்றுவ்வா னென்பாய்","முயலானு மில்லான் முகஞ்சிறிதே யொப்பான்","வயலாமை போல்வதோர் மக்குளி யிலானென்","றியலா தனவே யெடுத்துரைத்தி யென்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"She says he holds that a stranger reaps and the doer does not, and calls this a heaping up of impossible things, like describing a creature that is no hare, faintly like one in the face, like a field-tortoise.","n":"The run of comparisons in lines 2 and 3 is obscure (மக்குளி is not clear); their sense as a list of impossibles is taken from line 4.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vinai","payan","vaadhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 648","lb":"NK648","ev":"","cl":[["on the Buddhist account another reaps what the doer did, and the doer never reaps it","jain","asserted",1,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","புத்தனார்"],"ch":"nk:29"},"nk:649":{"book":"neelakesi","num":648,"t":["அயற்பிறனு மல்ல னவன்றானு மல்லன்","புயற்றிறலைங் கூந்த லிதன்போலப் பிறனுக்கேல்","முயற்பிறவி மேயினீர் முன்செய்தா னங்கோர்","பயப்பெறுவ தில்லையேற் பாழ்பயனி தென்னோ."],"p":"","r":"If the reaper is neither another nor himself, and so is another after all, she asks the Buddha who took birth as a hare: if the one who did the deed gets no fruit of it, what is that fruit but waste?","n":"The hare birth is the tale of the hare that gave itself as food; she does not deny the tale here but uses it: on his doctrine its fruit could never have reached the Buddha. Line 2's address to a woman of cloud-dark tresses suggests line 1 quotes his formula back at her.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vinai","payan","saathakam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 649","lb":"NK649","ev":"","cl":[["if the doer gets no fruit, the Bodhisattva's self-giving in former births gained him nothing","jain","asserted",4,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","புத்தனார்"],"ch":"nk:29"},"nk:650":{"book":"neelakesi","num":649,"t":["தற்பிறிதே யாகிப் பிறபிறிது தானல்லா","நெற்பிறிது போன்மெனவு நீயுரைத்தா யஃதொழிந்து","சொற்பிறிது சொன்னாய் சுவர்க்கத் தவர்க்குரைப்","பிற்பிறனே யாகிப் பெறப்பிறனே யாகானோ."],"p":"","r":"He likened it to paddy, other than itself yet not other, but then said something else; when he tells of those in heaven, she asks, is not the one who gains heaven another than the one who earned it?","n":"Line 1 is hard to construe; the force is that his own preaching of heaven as a reward needs the one rewarded to be the one who did well.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["marumai","vinai","vaadhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 650","lb":"NK650","ev":"","cl":[["on the Buddhist account the one who reaches heaven is other than the one who earned it","jain","asserted",4,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","புத்தனார்"],"ch":"nk:29"},"nk:651":{"book":"neelakesi","num":650,"t":["நெல்லின் வழிக்கரும்பு நீள்கரும்பி னானெல்லுஞ்","சொல்லுநீ சொல்லாயேற் சொல்லியநின் சொல்லெய்தா","வல்லனாய்ச் செய்த மகன்வழியி னான்மகன்றா","னல்லனாய்ச் சொல்லின் னுலங்கரு வாமே."],"p":"","r":"Let him say that sugarcane comes in the line of paddy and paddy from cane; if he cannot, his example fails, and if he says the son in the line of the father who did the deed is not the father, his account comes apart.","n":"She turns his paddy against him: paddy gives only paddy, so the example shows a kind that persists, not a 'neither same nor other'. The last words (உலங்கருவாமே) are obscure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vaadhu","vinai","makkal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 651","lb":"NK651","ev":"","cl":[["seed gives its own kind, so the paddy example proves sameness of kind, not a reaper who is neither same nor other","jain","asserted",1,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","புத்தனார்"],"ch":"nk:29"},"nk:652":{"book":"neelakesi","num":651,"t":["செய்தானுஞ் செய்தானே துப்பானுந் துப்பானே","யிஃதாலென் மெய்ம்மை கிடந்தவா றென்றானுக்","கெய்தார் பிறவோ விருசார் வினைப்பயனுஞ்","செய்தார்க டாமெனலுஞ் சிந்தித் திருந்தானால்"],"p":"","r":"The Buddha says the doer is the doer and the reaper the reaper and that this is how his truth lies; Neelakesi asks whether others reach the fruit, saying the fruit of both kinds of deed belongs to the doers themselves, and he sits silent in thought.","n":"His new formula keeps doer and reaper as two terms, which grants her the separation she has been pressing; her answer, செய்தார்கள் தாம், makes the doer the reaper. His falling silent is the turn of the debate.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","payan","vaadhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 652","lb":"NK652","ev":"The Buddha restates that doer is doer and reaper is reaper; Neelakesi answers that the doers reap, and he falls silent.","cl":[["the doer is only the doer and the reaper only the reaper","buddhist","refuted",1,"buddha"],["the fruit of good and bad deeds alike falls to those who did them","jain","asserted",4,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["புத்தனார்","நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"ch":"nk:29"},"nk:653":{"book":"neelakesi","num":652,"t":["கண்ணுந் தலையும் பிறவுங் கருந்தடியும்","பெண்ணுங் கொடுத்துப் பிறர்க்கே யுழந்தாய்முன்","னெண்ணுங்கா லின்னுநீ யெவ்வினையுஞ் செய்தாரை","யுண்ணுந் திறமொன்று மோதா தொழிந்தாயோ."],"p":"","r":"She reminds him that in former times he toiled only for others, giving eyes, head, flesh and wife, and asks whether, even now, he has failed to teach any way by which the doers of a deed come to eat its fruit.","n":"The gifts Kundalakesi praised (205) become evidence against the Buddha: they were made in hope of fruit, and his teaching leaves no one to receive it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","saathakam","vinai","payan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 653","lb":"NK653","ev":"","cl":[["a teacher who gave his body in former births for its merit must account for how the doer reaps","jain","asserted",4,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","புத்தனார்"],"ch":"nk:29"},"nk:654":{"book":"neelakesi","num":653,"t":["எனைத்துணையு நீவருந்தி யெத்துணையோர் கால","நினைத்திருப்பி னல்லது நின்காட்சி தன்னால்","வினைப்பயத்தின் கூட்டம் விரித்துரைப்ப னென்னிற்","றினைத்துணையு மாகாமை தேரிதுநீ யென்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"However much he strains and however long he ponders, she says, if he claims by his own vision to explain how a deed joins its fruit, let him see that it comes to not even a millet grain.","n":"காட்சி may mean his view or doctrine as well as his seeing; either way the verdict is that the doctrine cannot join deed to fruit.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","payan","vaadhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 654","lb":"NK654","ev":"","cl":[["the Buddhist doctrine cannot join a deed to its fruit at all","jain","asserted",3,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","புத்தனார்"],"ch":"nk:29"},"nk:655":{"book":"neelakesi","num":654,"t":["முழுக்கேடு வேண்டாயேன் முன்னின்ற துண்டாம்","வழுக்குமே லவ்வுரைக்கு மாறுகோ ளிஃதா","மிழுக்காமை புத்த£ ரெனைப்பலருங் கூடிக்","குழுக்களாய் வந்துநுங் கோளிறுமி னென்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"Unless he wants total destruction, what stood before must continue, and if it slips away that contradicts his own word; she challenges the Buddhists, however many, to gather in their companies and defend their tenet.","n":"The choice she gives is annihilation or persistence; the challenge to all Buddhists widens the debate from the Buddha to his order.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vaadhu","nilaiyaamai","samayam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 655","lb":"NK655","ev":"Neelakesi challenges all the Buddhists to come together in companies and defend their tenet.","cl":[["unless one accepts total annihilation, what existed before must persist","jain","asserted",1,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","புத்தர்"],"ch":"nk:29"},"nk:656":{"book":"neelakesi","num":655,"t":["நின்றே நிலையுமெனி னித்தியமே யாயொழியு","மன்றேயஃ தாயி லநித்தியமா மவ்விரண்டு","மென்றே யுரைக்கிலிரு வழிக் குற்றமுமா","மென்றே யுரைக்கின்றா யேகாந்த னாகுதியோ."],"p":"","r":"Quoting his objection that a thing which stays is eternal, one which ceases is not, and one called both has the faults of both, she asks whether by saying so he makes himself a one-sided thinker.","n":"ஏகாந்தன் is the Jain word for one who holds a single-sided view; the charge turns his objection to the Jain 'both' into evidence of his own error. The answer is given in 657.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vaadhu","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 656","lb":"NK656","ev":"","cl":[["to call a thing both lasting and passing incurs the faults of both","buddhist","refuted",3,"neelakesi"],["refusing a thing's being both lasting and passing in different respects is one-sidedness","jain","asserted",4,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","புத்தனார்"],"ch":"nk:29"},"nk:657":{"book":"neelakesi","num":656,"t":["உச்சேதந் தானு மொருவகையா லவ்வகையே","யச்சேத மின்மையு மவ்விரண்டு மாய்நிகழ்ந்து","பொய்ச்சேத மல்லாப் பொருண் முடிபொன் றுண்டாக","மெச்சாயே நீயெனயான் மெச்சுவனே யென்றான ."],"p":"","r":"When she asks whether he will not praise a conclusion in which a thing is cut off in one way, not cut off in that way too, and both, a conclusion that is no false cutting, the Buddha says he will praise it.","n":"உச்சேதம் is annihilation; the Buddha here assents to the Jain many-sided account he attacked in 656. Whether line 4 gives her question and his assent, or his challenge and her assent, turns on the masculine என்றான், which favours the Buddha as speaker.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vaadhu","nilaiyaamai","meyyunarvu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 657","lb":"NK657","ev":"The Buddha agrees to praise the Jain conclusion that a thing perishes in one respect and not in another.","cl":[["a thing is annihilated in one respect, not annihilated in another, and both","jain","asserted",1,"buddha"]],"ag":["புத்தனார்"],"ec":[],"spk":["புத்தனார்","நீலகேசி"],"ch":"nk:29"},"nk:658":{"book":"neelakesi","num":657,"t":["இறைவன்ற னூலுணரி னெவ்வுருவி னாரு","முறையினா லெய்துவர்தா முன்னிய வீடென்றாள்","மறையினா லாயினு மற்றொருவா றேனு","நிறையினாற் செல்லென்று நோழையுஞ் சென்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi says that those of any form who understand the Lord's scripture reach in due order the release they seek; bidden to go on with steadfastness, by scripture or another way, she departs.","n":"எவ்வுருவினாரும், those of any form, includes the speaker herself, a demoness converted (4). முறையினால் makes release gradual, by order, not sudden. Who says செல் in line 4 is not stated; the Buddha is the likeliest.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["veedu","iraivan","nirai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 658","lb":"NK658","ev":"Neelakesi tells the Buddha that anyone who understands the Lord's scripture reaches release, and having won, she leaves.","cl":[["whoever understands the Lord's scripture, of whatever form, attains release in due order","jain","asserted",1,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி","புத்தனார்"],"ec":[],"ch":"nk:29"},"nk:659":{"book":"neelakesi","num":658,"t":["கண்ணார் சிறப்பிற் கபில புரங்கடந்து","விண்ணாறு செல்வாள் வியன்மலைபோற் றோற்றத்தாள்","உண்ணால் வினையு மொருவி யொளிர்மேனி","யெண்ணா துணர்ந்தானை யேத்தத் தொடங்கினாள்."],"p":"","r":"Leaving Kapilapuram behind, Neelakesi travels by the sky path, vast as a mountain, and begins to praise the one of shining body who shed the inner four deeds and knows without having to reckon.","n":"எண்ணாது உணர்ந்தான், 'he who knew without reckoning', is the mark she earlier denied the Buddha, who she said knew by seeing, hearing and reckoning (193). உண்ணால் வினை is read as உள் நால் வினை, the four inner deeds, which fits the Jain account of the omniscient but is a reading of a joined form.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","arivu","vinai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 659","lb":"NK659","ev":"Neelakesi leaves Kapilapuram by the sky and begins a hymn to the Jina.","cl":[["the Lord, rid of the inner deeds, knows all things without reasoning his way to them","jain","asserted",4,"narrator"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"ch":"nk:30"},"nk:660":{"book":"neelakesi","num":659,"t":["அங்கம் பயந்தா னறைந்த சுதக்கடலுள்","பங்கங்கள் சாராப் பரசமையர் சொல்லுளவோ","பங்கங்கள் சாராப் பரசமையர் சொல்லேபோற்","புங்கவன்றன் சேவடியைச் சேராத பூவுளவோ."],"p":"","r":"She asks whether any word of the other schools free of fault lies outside the ocean of scripture told by the one who gave the Angas, any more than there is a flower that does not come to rest at the Lord's red feet.","n":"The verse turns on a rhetorical question whose force can be read two ways: that no rival school speaks without fault, or that whatever is faultless in them is already in the Jina's scripture; the parallel with the flowers that all end at the Lord's feet favours the second.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["aagamam","samayam","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 660","lb":"NK660","ev":"","cl":[["whatever is faultless in the other schools' words is already contained in the Jina's scripture","jain","asserted",2,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி",""],"ch":"nk:30"},"nk:661":{"book":"neelakesi","num":660,"t":["பூர்ப்பம் பயந்தான் புகன்ற சுதக்கடலுள்","சார்த்திப் பிறவாத் தவநெறிக டாமுளவோ","சார்த்திப் பிறவாத் தவநெறிக டம்மேபோற்","றீர்த்தன் றிருநாமங் கொள்ளாத தேவுளவோ."],"p":"","r":"She asks whether there is any penance-path that ends birth not found in the scripture-ocean of the one who gave the Purvas, and likewise whether there is any god who does not bear the Tirthankara's holy name.","n":"The second half folds every god of every school into the Jina: whatever is rightly worshipped as a god is a name of the Tirthankara.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aagamam","thavam","piravi","iraivan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 661","lb":"NK661","ev":"","cl":[["every path of penance that ends birth is taught in the scripture of the Purvas","jain","asserted",2,"neelakesi"],["every true god is a name of the Tirthankara","jain","asserted",4,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி",""],"ch":"nk:30"},"nk:662":{"book":"neelakesi","num":661,"t":["புலவ னுரைத்த புறக்கேள்வி சாரா","துலக நவின்றுரைக்கு மோத்தெங் குளதோ","வுலக நவின்றுரைக்கு மோத்தேயு மன்றிப்","பலவும் பகர்வாப் பயந்தனவே யன்றோ."],"p":"","r":"She asks where there is any scripture the world recites that does not draw on the learning the Poet uttered, and says that the world's scriptures and much else besides were born of what he said.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aagamam","kelvi","samayam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 662","lb":"NK662","ev":"","cl":[["the scriptures other peoples recite are derived from the Jina's teaching","jain","asserted",4,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி",""],"ch":"nk:30"},"nk:663":{"book":"neelakesi","num":662,"t":["அலரோடு சாந்த மணிந்தெம் மிறைவர்","மலரடியை யல்லதியா மற்றறிவ தில்லை","மற்றறிவ தில்லாத வெம்மை மலரடிகண்","முற்றவே செய்து முடிவிற்க மன்றே."],"p":"","r":"Adorned with flowers and sandal, she says she knows nothing but her Lord's flower feet, and asks that those feet bring her, who knows nothing else, through to the end.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thiruvadi","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 663","lb":"NK663","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி",""],"ch":"nk:30"},"nk:664":{"book":"neelakesi","num":663,"t":["புனையுலகிற் காதிய புங்கவ னார்த","மிணையடியை யல்லதியா மின்புறுவ தில்லை","இன்புறுவ தில்லாத வெம்மை யிணையடிக","டுன்புறவி லக்கதியுட் டோற்றுவிக்கு மன்றே."],"p":"","r":"She has no joy except in the paired feet of the Lord who was first in the world, and trusts those feet to bring her, who has no other joy, to birth in the state where suffering is put away.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thiruvadi","idumbai","veedu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 664","lb":"NK664","ev":"","cl":[["devotion to the Jina's feet brings one to the state where suffering is removed","jain","asserted",4,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி",""],"ch":"nk:30"},"nk:665":{"book":"neelakesi","num":664,"t":["இரவிடைநன் மணிபோலு மண்ணா துணர்ந்தான்","திருவடியே யல்லதென் சிந்தனையி லில்லைச்","சிந்தனையொன் றில்லாத வெம்மைத் திருவடிக","ளந்திணையில் பேராற்ற லாக்குவிக்கு மன்றே."],"p":"","r":"Nothing is in her mind but the holy feet of the one who knows without approaching, like a fine gem shining in the night, and she trusts those feet to give her, whose mind holds nothing else, great power.","n":"அண்ணாது உணர்ந்தான, 'who knew without coming near', repeats the claim of 659 that the Lord's knowing does not go through the senses' contact with things.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thiruvadi","arivu","manam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 665","lb":"NK665","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி",""],"ch":"nk:30"},"nk:666":{"book":"neelakesi","num":665,"t":["தொக்குட னாயவென் றொல்வினை தீர்கென","முக்குடை யானடி மூன்றினும் வந்தித்துக்","குக்குட மாநகர் நின்று கொடிமினிற்","றக்கதிற் றான்போய்ச் சமதண்டம் புக்காள்."],"p":"","r":"Praying that the old deeds gathered on her may end, she worships the feet of the Lord of the triple umbrella in thought, word and body, and from the great city of Kukkuta goes like a flag of lightning into Samadandam.","n":"The convert who once begged the sage to quench her cruel deeds (62) now asks the Jina's feet for the same thing; the prayer is hers, and the verse does not say how the feet are to end the deeds.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","thiruvadi","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 666","lb":"NK666","ev":"Neelakesi worships the Jina's feet, praying to be rid of her old deeds, and goes from Kukkuta city to Samadandam.","cl":[],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"ch":"nk:30"},"nk:667":{"book":"neelakesi","num":666,"t":["ஈண்டி யிருந்த விலிங்கியர் தங்கட்கு","மாண்ட துகிலல்குல் மாதரிது சொல்லும்","காண்டற் கினிதே கடிமலர்ப் பூம்பள்ளி","யீண்டுறை வாரிவர் யாவர்கொ லென்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"To the ascetics gathered there she says that the grove of fragrant flowers is lovely to see and asks who these are that dwell in it.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["samayam","vaadhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 667","lb":"NK667","ev":"Neelakesi comes on the Ajivika ascetics in their flowering grove and asks who they are.","cl":[],"ag":["நீலகேசி","ஆசீவகர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","ஆசீவகர்"],"ch":"nk:30"},"nk:668":{"book":"neelakesi","num":667,"t":["காரணம் வேண்டாக் கடவுட் குழாந்தன்னிற்","பேருணர் வெய்திப் பெரிதும் பெரியவன்","பூரண னென்பான் பொருவறக் கற்றவ","னாரணங் கன்னாட் கறிய வுரைக்கும்."],"p":"","r":"From that throng of godlike ones who want no cause, Puranan, greatly learned and greatest of them, speaks so that the woman may know.","n":"காரணம் வேண்டாக் கடவுட் குழாம், 'the god-throng that needs no cause', may be a Jain's barb at the Ajivika teaching that things come about without cause; the phrase can also be plain praise of ascetics who want nothing.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["samayam","kalvi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 668","lb":"NK668","ev":"Puranan, the most learned of the Ajivikas, steps forward to answer her.","cl":[],"ag":["பூரணன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"nk:30"},"nk:669":{"book":"neelakesi","num":668,"t":["புயலிருங் கூந்தற் பொலங்கொடி யன்னா","யயலியர் தாமல்ல வாசீ வகர்கள்","வியலிடத் தியாரும் வியக்குந் தகையார்","மயலறு காட்சியிம் மாதவ ரென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"Puranan tells her that these are not strangers but Ajivikas, great ascetics of undeluded vision at whom all the wide world marvels.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["samayam","thavam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 669","lb":"NK669","ev":"Puranan names his company as the Ajivikas.","cl":[],"ag":["பூரணன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["பூரணன்","நீலகேசி"],"ch":"nk:30"},"nk:670":{"book":"neelakesi","num":669,"t":["ஆத்தனு நூலும் பொருளு நிகழ்ச்சியும்","பாத்தன சொல்லப் பயம்பெரி தாகலி","னோத்துரை யேயிங் குரையென் றுரைத்தனள்","சாத்திரம் யாவையுந் தன்னிக ரில்லாள்."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi, unequalled in every science, tells him that much is gained by setting out in order the teacher, the scripture, the categories and how they come about, and asks him to state his scripture here.","n":"Her four heads (ஆத்தன், நூல், பொருள், நிகழ்ச்சி) are the questions the king of Kampili put at the first debate (172), now in her own mouth: she has become the examiner.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vaadhu","aagamam","alavai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 670","lb":"NK670","ev":"Neelakesi asks Puranan to set out his teacher, scripture, categories and their workings.","cl":[],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","பூரணன்"],"ch":"nk:30"},"nk:671":{"book":"neelakesi","num":670,"t":["என்றலு மற்கலி தானே யிறையினி","ஒன்பது வாங்கதிர் நூல்யா முடையன","மன்பெறு நுண்பொரு ளைந்தியல் பாயவை","யென்ப நிகழ்ச்சியுங் காழ்ப்பா டெனச்சொல."],"p":"","r":"Puranan answers that Makkali himself is the Lord, that their scripture is the nine books called Kathir, that the subtle categories are five, and that their working is fixedness (காழ்ப்பாடு).","n":"Neelakesi answers each head in turn: the authority of the books in 680-681, the five categories in 687-693, and the காழ்ப்பாடு in 694, where she calls it a fixity of bewilderment. காழ்ப்பாடு, 'hardening', is read as the fixed course of things; the word is rare and its exact sense is not secure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","aagamam","anu","aaseevagam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 671","lb":"NK671","ev":"Puranan names Makkali as the Ajivikas' god and states their scripture and categories.","cl":[["Makkali is the Lord","ajivika","refuted",1,"puranan"],["the Ajivika scripture is the nine books called Kathir","ajivika","refuted",2,"puranan"],["the subtle categories are five","ajivika","refuted",3,"puranan"],["the working of the categories is fixed","ajivika","refuted",4,"puranan"]],"ag":["பூரணன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["பூரணன்","நீலகேசி"],"ch":"nk:30"},"nk:672":{"book":"neelakesi","num":671,"t":["அறிந்தா னிறைவ னவனா குதலாற்","செறிந்தான் பெரிதுஞ் செறியா துரைப்பி","னெறிந்தா னனைய வியல்பா குதலான்","மறிந்தான் றடுமாற் றகத்தே மயங்கி."],"p":"","r":"Puranan says the Lord is Lord because he knew, and kept himself closed; were he to speak without restraint, he would be like one who has thrown something and would fall back, bewildered, into the round of wandering.","n":"This is the Ajivika ground for a silent omniscient: to speak is to be moved, and to be moved is to fall back into birth. Neelakesi takes the verse up almost word for word in 682 to answer it. The simile எறிந்தான் அனைய (like one who threw) is compressed and its exact point is not certain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","mounam","piravi","aaseevagam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 672","lb":"NK672","ev":"","cl":[["the omniscient Lord stays silent, since speaking would draw him back into the round of births","ajivika","refuted",2,"puranan"]],"ag":["பூரணன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["பூரணன்","நீலகேசி"],"ch":"nk:30"},"nk:673":{"book":"neelakesi","num":672,"t":["உரையா னிறைவ னுணலு மிலனாய்த்","திரையா னரையான் றெரிவில் லுருவம்","வரையா வகைவா னிடுவில் லனையன்","புரையா வறிவிற் புகழ்பூ ரணனே."],"p":"","r":"Puranan describes the Lord as one who neither speaks nor eats, has no wrinkle or grey hair, and has a form that cannot be seen, like a rainbow in the sky that no bound contains.","n":"The last words, புகழ் பூரணனே, may close the speech with the speaker's name or may call the Lord the Full One (பூரணன்); the division of line 4 allows either. Neelakesi turns the rainbow simile against him in 684.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","yaakkai","aaseevagam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 673","lb":"NK673","ev":"","cl":[["the Lord neither speaks nor eats nor ages, and his form is unseen like a rainbow","ajivika","refuted",1,"puranan"]],"ag":["பூரணன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["பூரணன்","நீலகேசி"],"ch":"nk:30"},"nk:674":{"book":"neelakesi","num":673,"t":["அடங்கல் குறிக்கோண் முதலா யினவாய்க்","கிடந்த கதிருட் கிளந்த பொருளுந்","தொடங்கி யுரையாந் தொகையா குவதே","யுடங்கே யுணுவைந் துருவா யுளவே."],"p":"","r":"Puranan says the matters spoken of in the Kathir, beginning with restraint and the goal, when gathered come to this: there are atoms, and they are of five forms.","n":"உணு in line 4 is read as அணு.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["anu","aagamam","aaseevagam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 674","lb":"NK674","ev":"","cl":[["all that the scripture treats comes down to atoms of five kinds","ajivika","refuted",4,"puranan"]],"ag":["பூரணன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["பூரணன்","நீலகேசி"],"ch":"nk:30"},"nk:675":{"book":"neelakesi","num":674,"t":["நிலநீ ரெரிகாற் றுயிரி னியல்பும்","பலநீ ரவற்றின் படுபா லவைதாம்","புலமா கொலியொன் றொழிய முதற்காஞ்","சலமா யதுதண் மையையே முதலாம்."],"p":"","r":"Puranan names the five as earth, water, fire, wind and soul, each with its many qualities, and says that, sound apart, coolness is the first quality of water.","n":"Soul counted as the fifth beside the four elements is the same five the Manimekalai's Ajivika gives. Line 3 is hard to divide, and which quality is assigned to earth is not clear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["anu","uyir","aaseevagam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 675","lb":"NK675","ev":"","cl":[["the five categories are earth, water, fire, wind and soul, each with its own qualities","ajivika","refuted",1,"puranan"]],"ag":["பூரணன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["பூரணன்","நீலகேசி"],"ch":"nk:30"},"nk:676":{"book":"neelakesi","num":675,"t":["எறித்தன் முதலா யினதீ யினவாம்","செறித்த லிரையோ டிவைகாற் றினவா","மறித்தல் லறிதல் லவைதா முயிராம்","குறித்த பொருளின் குணமா லிவையே."],"p":"","r":"Puranan says burning and the like belong to fire, compacting and feeding to wind, turning back and knowing to soul, and that these are the qualities of the substances named.","n":"Knowing is listed as one quality among the others, the soul's as burning is fire's; Neelakesi's attack on the five classes begins at 687.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["anu","uyir","arivu","aaseevagam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 676","lb":"NK676","ev":"","cl":[["knowing is the quality proper to soul, as burning is to fire","ajivika","refuted",3,"puranan"]],"ag":["பூரணன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["பூரணன்","நீலகேசி"],"ch":"nk:30"},"nk:677":{"book":"neelakesi","num":676,"t":["அணுமே யினவைந் தவைதா மனைத்துங்","குணமே யிலவாங் குழுவும் பிரியு","முணன்மே யினுமுள் புகுதல் லுரையேங்","கணமே யெனினும் மொருகா லமிலை."],"p":"","r":"Puranan says the five kinds of atom all combine and separate, but that one does not enter into another, not even for an instant.","n":"Neelakesi answers this in 698, where she presses him that, if nothing gives room to anything else, ghee cannot become flame. Line 2's குணமே இலவாம் seems to deny the atoms qualities just after 676 gave them; the reading is unsure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["anu","aaseevagam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 677","lb":"NK677","ev":"","cl":[["atoms combine and part but never enter one another","ajivika","refuted",3,"puranan"]],"ag":["பூரணன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["பூரணன்","நீலகேசி"],"ch":"nk:30"},"nk:678":{"book":"neelakesi","num":677,"t":["இவையே பொருள்கள் இவற்றி னியல்பும்","சவையே அறியச் சிலசாற் றுவன்கேள்","சுவையே யுடையம் மெனநீ யிகழல்","லவையே பிறராலழிதற் கரிய."],"p":"","r":"Puranan says these are the categories and he will tell something of their nature for the assembly, warns her not to scorn the Ajivikas as caring only for taste, and claims the categories cannot be overthrown by others.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vaadhu","aaseevagam","avai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 678","lb":"NK678","ev":"","cl":[["the Ajivika categories cannot be overthrown by rival schools","ajivika","refuted",4,"puranan"]],"ag":["பூரணன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["பூரணன்","நீலகேசி"],"ch":"nk:30"},"nk:679":{"book":"neelakesi","num":678,"t":["அண்ணலு நூலும் பொருளுநிகழ்வு மிவையெனலு","மெண்ணினு மேனை யெழுத்தினுமிக்காங் கிருந்தவர்முன்","கண்ணினு மன்றிக் கருத்தினும்வேறெனக் காட்டலுற்றுப்","பண்ணலங் கொண்டசொல் லாளவைபேர்த்தும் பகர்ந்தனளாய்."],"p":"","r":"When he has named the Lord, scripture, categories and their working, Neelakesi, of skilled speech, sets out before the learned company to show that all of it is contrary both to sight and to reason, and speaks again.","n":"கண்ணினும் அன்றிக் கருத்தினும் வேறு: she will test his account by perception and by inference, the two means both sides grant, not by her own scripture.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vaadhu","alavai","avai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 679","lb":"NK679","ev":"Neelakesi begins her answer, undertaking to show the Ajivika account contrary to perception and reason.","cl":[],"ag":["நீலகேசி","பூரணன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"nk:30"},"nk:680":{"book":"neelakesi","num":679,"t":["முற்ற வறிந்துரை யாதவன் மோனாந் திருந்தனனேற்","செற்றம் பெரிது முடையனச் சீவன்க டம்மொடெல்லா","மற்ற முடையவர் சொல்லின வாகம மன்மையினாற்","பெற்ற வகையென்னை பேதாயதனைப் பெயர்த்தெனவே."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi argues that if the one who knew everything kept silence and never spoke, then the books were spoken by souls full of hatred and other faults, so they are not scripture, and she asks him, fool, how he came by them.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["mounam","aagamam","vaadhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 680","lb":"NK680","ev":"","cl":[["if the omniscient never spoke, the books in his name are the words of passion-bound souls and have no authority","jain","asserted",3,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","பூரணன்"],"ch":"nk:30"},"nk:681":{"book":"neelakesi","num":680,"t":["ஒக்கலி யோகலி யென்றிரு தெய்வ முரைத்தனவேன்","மற்கலி யார்போ லறிந்தன வாயிற் செறிந்தனவாம்","தக்கில வேயறி யாதன சொல்லுத றத்துவத்தை","யிக்கலி யாள ருரைத்தவு மேதமெ னாய்பிறவோ."],"p":"","r":"She goes on that if he says the two gods Okkali and Yokali told the books, then had they known as Makkali knew they too would have kept silent, and to speak of the truth without knowing it is unfit, so what these teachers said is faulty.","n":"Okkali and Yokali are otherwise unknown in the corpus; the verse treats them as the Ajivika's fallback authors. இக்கலியாளர் may pun on Makkali's name and on கலி, the dark age.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["aagamam","arivu","vaadhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 681","lb":"NK681","ev":"","cl":[["one who speaks of the truth without knowing it is not a fit authority","jain","asserted",3,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","பூரணன்"],"ch":"nk:30"},"nk:682":{"book":"neelakesi","num":681,"t":["அறிந்தா னறிந்தன தான்சொல்லினார்வச் சினத்தனனா","யெறிந்தா னனையதோ ரேதத்தையெய்துமவ் வேதத்தினான்","மறிந்தா னகன்றடு மாற்றத்தகத்தெனின் மாண்புணர்ந்தாய்","செறிந்தாங் கிருக்கிற்பி னீயுஞ்சிற்றாத்தனை யாகிற்றியே"],"p":"","r":"Taking up his words, she says that if a knower who speaks what he knows becomes subject to longing and anger and falls back into wandering by that fault, then, by his own reasoning, he too would become a lesser Apta by sitting silent.","n":"The verse reuses the words of 672 (அறிந்தான், எறிந்தான் அனைய, மறிந்தான், தடுமாற்று) to turn them: if silence is what makes the Lord, any silent man is one. The last line is compressed and its turn is a reading.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["mounam","arivu","iraivan","vaadhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 682","lb":"NK682","ev":"","cl":[["silence alone cannot be what makes one an omniscient Lord","jain","asserted",4,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","பூரணன்"],"ch":"nk:30"},"nk:683":{"book":"neelakesi","num":682,"t":["ஆத்த னறிந்தன யாவையுஞ்சொல்லல னாய்விடினிச்","சாத்தனும் யானு மவன்றன்னிற்சால விசையுடைய","நாத்தனை யாட்டியோர் நன்மைகண்டாலு நினக்குரைத்து","மீத்தன முண்டு மிருமைக்குமேத மிலம்பிறவோ."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi argues that if the Ajivika Lord did not tell all that he knew, then any common man and she herself, who wag their tongues to tell others whatever good they see, are greater than he and stand free of fault in this world and the next.","n":"சாத்தன் in line 2 is the stock name for any man at all; the argument ranks the silent omniscient below the most ordinary speaker. The silence answered is the one Puranan claimed for his Lord in 673 (உரையான் இறைவன்).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arivu","iraivan","sol","vaadhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 683","lb":"NK683","ev":"Neelakesi answers the claim that the Ajivika Lord keeps silent and says that ordinary speakers would then surpass him.","cl":[["an omniscient who does not tell what he knows is lesser than an ordinary man who shares the good he sees","jain","asserted",1,"neelakesi"],["the supreme knower keeps silent and does not teach what he knows","ajivika","refuted",1,"puranan"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","பூரணன்"],"ch":"nk:31"},"nk:684":{"book":"neelakesi","num":683,"t":["வானிடு வில்லின் வரவறி யாத வகையனென்பாய்","தானுடம் போடு பொறியின னாதலிற் சாதகனா","மீனடைந் தோடும் விடுசுட ரான்கதிர் வீழ்புயன்மேற்","றானடைந் தாற்றனு வாமிது வாமதன் றத்துவமே."],"p":"","r":"Taking up Puranan's comparison of the Lord to the rainbow, Neelakesi says that one with a body and senses is a born being, and that a rainbow is no uncaused marvel but what appears when the sun's rays fall on the rain-cloud: that is its whole nature.","n":"The word சாதகன் in line 2 is read here as 'one who is born'; it could carry another sense, and the division of line 4 (தனுவாம் as 'becomes a bow') is my own.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","yaakkai","vaadhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 684","lb":"NK684","ev":"Neelakesi turns the rainbow simile for the Ajivika Lord against him.","cl":[["a being with a body and sense organs is born and caused, like a rainbow that arises from sunlight on rain","jain","asserted",2,"neelakesi"],["the Lord's form is like a rainbow that cannot be grasped","ajivika","refuted",1,"puranan"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","பூரணன்"],"ch":"nk:31"},"nk:685":{"book":"neelakesi","num":684,"t":["முற்ற வறிந்தனன் யானென்றுமோனங்கொண் டேயிருந்தா","னற்ற மகலவென் றானீயறிந்தமை யாதினினாம்","பெற்ற வகையெனப் பேச்சின்மையாலெனிற் பிள்ளைகளும்","மற்றிம் மரமு மலையுமம்மாண்பின வாம்பிறவோ."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi asks how Puranan knows that his Lord, who said 'I know all' and then kept silent, really knew; if the proof is that he did not speak, then infants, this tree and the mountain have the same greatness.","n":"Line 2 (னற்ற மகலவென் றானீ) is hard to divide; the sense taken is that the Lord fell silent so that fault would leave him, which matches 682's claim that speaking would bring passion.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["mounam","arivu","iraivan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 685","lb":"NK685","ev":"Neelakesi mocks the Ajivika proof of the Lord's omniscience from his silence.","cl":[["silence is no evidence of knowledge, since infants, trees and mountains are silent too","jain","asserted",3,"neelakesi"],["the Lord's silence after declaring that he knows all shows his perfect knowledge","ajivika","refuted",1,"puranan"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","பூரணன்"],"ch":"nk:31"},"nk:686":{"book":"neelakesi","num":685,"t":["முடக்கு மெனினு நிமிர்க்குமெனினுந்தன் மூக்குயிர்த்து","நடக்கு மெனினு மிருக்குமெனினுந்த னல்லுறுப்பி","னடக்கு மியல்பல்ல னன்னவற்றார்வத்த னாகுமன்றி","யுடக்கு மிவையில்லை யேலுயிர்தானுண்மை யொட்டுவனோ."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi argues that the Lord who bends and stretches his limbs, breathes, walks and sits has not stilled his body, so by Puranan's own reasoning he acts from desire; and if he did none of these, who would grant he had life at all?","n":"The verse turns the Ajivika defence of 682 (that to speak would make the Lord a man of desire and anger) back on him: silence of the tongue leaves the rest of the body in motion.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["avaa","mounam","uyir"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 686","lb":"NK686","ev":"Neelakesi argues that the silent Lord's bodily movements betray desire as much as speech would.","cl":[["if speaking proves passion, every bodily movement proves it too, and a being without movement could not be called alive","jain","asserted",3,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","பூரணன்"],"ch":"nk:31"},"nk:687":{"book":"neelakesi","num":686,"t":["நிலப்பாலு நீர்ப்பாலுந் தீப்பாலும் காற்றின்","புலப்பாலு நெட்டுயிரின் போக்கில்லாப் பாலும்","சொலற்பால வல்லாத சொல்லுதலால் யானு","மலப்பா தொழியேனிவ் வாசீ வகனை","யருகிருந்தார் தாமறிய வாசீ வகனை."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi declares that since the Ajivika has spoken what should not be spoken, the earth-kind, water-kind, fire-kind, the wind-kind and the kind of life, she will not stop harrying him until all who sit near have seen it.","n":"The metre changes here to five-line verses whose last line repeats the fourth with a varied opening; the refrain carries the taunt. The five பால் are the five atom-kinds Puranan set out in 674-677.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vaadhu","anu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 687","lb":"NK687","ev":"Neelakesi announces before the onlookers that she will confute the Ajivika's five kinds.","cl":[["the five kinds of earth, water, fire, wind and life are the whole of what exists","ajivika","refuted",1,"puranan"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","பூரணன்"],"ch":"nk:31"},"nk:688":{"book":"neelakesi","num":687,"t":["வண்ணாதி யெல்லாம் வகுப்பின் னிலப்பாலாம்","நண்ணாத மூன்றிற்கு நன்பால் பிறவாகிக்","கண்ணாதி யாலவற்றைக் காணப்பா டில்லையா","யெண்ணாதே யிந்தியக்கோ ளெய்தாமை வேண்டும்","எனைத்தும் பெறப்பாடு மில்லாத வேண்டும்."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi argues that if colour and the other sensible qualities all belong to the earth-kind, the other three elements, having none, could never be seen by eye or any sense, and so could never be known by any means at all.","n":"The target is 675's claim that every sense-object but sound is earth's; water keeps only its coolness, fire its burning. The objection is that this leaves water, fire and wind unseen.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["anu","alavai","gunam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 688","lb":"NK688","ev":"Neelakesi attacks the Ajivika assignment of the sensible qualities to earth alone.","cl":[["all sensible qualities except sound belong to the earth-kind","ajivika","refuted",1,"puranan"],["an element with no sensible quality could be known by no means of knowledge","jain","asserted",4,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","பூரணன்"],"ch":"nk:31"},"nk:689":{"book":"neelakesi","num":688,"t":["நீர்ப்பாலுந் தீப்பாலு நில்லா வளிப்பாலும்","பேர்ப்பாலே பற்றிப் பிறப்பிறவா நீபெருக்கி","யோர்ப்பி யாதுஞ்செய்யா துரைத்தா யுரைத்தமையிற்","கூர்ப்பியாது மின்றிநின் கோளழியு மன்றே","கொணர்ந்துநீ யைந்தென்ற கோளழியு மன்றே."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi says the Ajivika has multiplied the kinds of water, fire and restless wind by holding only to their names, without examining anything, and so his doctrine, the five he brought forward, collapses.","n":"பிறப்பிறவா in line 2 is not clear to me; it may mean 'without passing beyond birth' or be a corrupt reading.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["anu","vaadhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 689","lb":"NK689","ev":"Neelakesi declares the Ajivika's doctrine of five kinds overthrown.","cl":[["kinds distinguished only by name and not by examination give no ground for a doctrine","jain","asserted",2,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","பூரணன்"],"ch":"nk:31"},"nk:690":{"book":"neelakesi","num":689,"t":["பொருடாமி வைந்தொழியப் போத்தந் துரைப்பா","யிருடாமி வைந்தனு ளெக்கூற்ற தாமோ","விருடாமி வைந்தனுளு மெக்கூற்று மில்லை","லருடாழ்ந்து நீயிருப்ப தியாதின்பா லாமோ","அணுமயமாங் கந்தங்க டாமனந்த மன்றோ."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi asks, if things are only these five, to which of the five darkness belongs, and if to none, to which the state the Ajivika himself abides in belongs; and are not the compounds made of atoms beyond number?","n":"அருடாழ்ந்து in line 4 may be அருள் தாழ்ந்து, 'sunk in grace' or 'fallen from compassion'; the jibe is unclear. கந்தம் here is the atom-compound of Jain usage, not the Buddhist aggregate.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["anu","vaadhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 690","lb":"NK690","ev":"Neelakesi challenges the Ajivika to place darkness among his five kinds.","cl":[["darkness falls under none of the five kinds, so the five do not exhaust what exists","jain","asserted",2,"neelakesi"],["compounds made of atoms are infinite in number","jain","asserted",5,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","பூரணன்"],"ch":"nk:31"},"nk:691":{"book":"neelakesi","num":690,"t":["பலவாக நீசொன்ன பாலெல்லாந் தம்முட்","கலவாவா யப்பொருளே யாதலையுங் கண்டா","லுலவாதோ வொற்றுமையும் வேற்றுமையு மென்றாற்","சலவாதி யொன்றுஞ் சமழலையே கண்டாய்","சமத்திடை யொன்றுஞ் சமழலையே கண்டாய்."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi argues that if the many kinds he named do not mix and yet are all one substance, he has both oneness and difference at once, and says he has shown no shame at all before the assembly.","n":"சலவாதி may be 'deceitful debater'; சமழலையே is read as 'not ashamed' (சமழ்). Both readings are mine.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["anu","vaadhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 691","lb":"NK691","ev":"Neelakesi charges the Ajivika with holding both identity and difference of his kinds.","cl":[["the kinds are many and unmixed yet are the one substance","ajivika","refuted",1,"puranan"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","பூரணன்"],"ch":"nk:31"},"nk:692":{"book":"neelakesi","num":691,"t":["பாறாம் பலவாகிப் பாலாகு மப்பொருளே","வேறாது மில்லை யெனவே விளம்புவாய்","நீறாக நின்ற நிலப்பால் பெறவேலா","நாறா வகையெனக்கு நன்குரைக்கல் வேண்டும்","நலிந்தாற் பிறபொருட்கு நாட்டலே வேண்டும்."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi presses: if the one substance becomes the many kinds and there is nothing besides, he must explain how the earth-kind that has turned to ash gives off no smell, and if he is cornered he must establish the same for the other substances.","n":"Line 3's பெறவேலா is unclear; the challenge read is that ash, still earth, has lost earth's smell, so the quality cannot be the substance itself.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["anu","gunam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 692","lb":"NK692","ev":"Neelakesi asks the Ajivika why ash, though of the earth-kind, has no smell.","cl":[["a substance and its kinds are one with nothing besides","ajivika","refuted",2,"puranan"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","பூரணன்"],"ch":"nk:31"},"nk:693":{"book":"neelakesi","num":692,"t":["இன்றேய தாயி னிவைபா லிவைபொருள்க","ளென்றே பலவா வெடுத்துரைப்ப தென்செய்யக்","குன்றோ மலையோ குவடோ வடுக்கலோ","வன்றோவ தன்றாலஃ தியாப்பாதல் வேண்டூம்","அவையவையே சொன்னாலஃ தியாப்பாதல் வேண்டும்."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi says that if kinds and substances are not different, listing 'these are kinds, these are substances' as many is like calling one thing hill, mountain, peak and crag: a mere arrangement of words.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sol","anu","vaadhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 693","lb":"NK693","ev":"Neelakesi mocks the Ajivika's lists as synonyms for one thing.","cl":[["if kinds are not different from substances, naming them separately is only synonymy","jain","asserted",2,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","பூரணன்"],"ch":"nk:31"},"nk:694":{"book":"neelakesi","num":693,"t":["நோயில்லை வாழி கடவு ளெனவுரைத்தா","னாயினோ யின்மையினேர்ந் தாய வழியொருநாட்","டீயினும் வெய்யநோய் சேர்தலையுங் காண்டுநீ","சாயினும் தத்துவத்தைச் சாராதா யன்றோ","தடுமாற்றக் காழ்ப்பாடந் தாமுளவே யன்றோ."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi recalls that the Ajivika said the god has no disease, yet one day a sickness hotter than fire came upon him; even dying, she says, Puranan will not come to the truth, so full is his doctrine of wavering and fixed dogma.","n":"The 'sickness hotter than fire' may recall the tradition that Makkali died of a burning fever; the verse names neither the god nor the occasion, and whether கடவுள் here is the founder or the ascetics (கடவுட் குழாம், 668) is not certain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["noy","iraivan","yaakkai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 694","lb":"NK694","ev":"Neelakesi cites the burning sickness that struck the Ajivika god against the claim that he is free of disease.","cl":[["the Ajivika god is free of disease","ajivika","refuted",1,"puranan"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","பூரணன்"],"ch":"nk:31"},"nk:695":{"book":"neelakesi","num":694,"t":["கடுங்கதிரோன் மீதூரக் காணாக்கோ ளெல்லாம்","படும்பொழுது மெழுச்சியினுந் தம்பயனே செய்யு","நெடுங்காலம் பல்பிறவி நின்றன வெல்லா","மொடுங்காதே மேய்ந்துண் டுழிதரலே வேண்டும்","உதவாத வார்தலையு மொட்டலே வேண்டும்."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi argues that as the hidden planets, though outshone by the sun, still yield their effects at rising and setting, so if all is fixed the beings that have stood through long ages of many births must roam grazing and eating without end, and the Ajivika must accept even fruitless feeding.","n":"The planet simile and the force of உதவாத ஆர்தல் are hard to fix; the reading that the verse attacks the fixed course of births is mine.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["niyatham","piravi","oozh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 695","lb":"NK695","ev":"Neelakesi draws an absurd consequence from the Ajivika's fixed course of births.","cl":[["souls pass through a long fixed course of births that runs its course regardless of their acts","ajivika","refuted",3,"puranan"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","பூரணன்"],"ch":"nk:31"},"nk:696":{"book":"neelakesi","num":695,"t":["எப்பாலுந் தான்கெடா வில்லனவுந் தோன்றாவென்","றொப்பியாது மில்ல துரைத்தளியின் றானுண்ணும்","துப்பாயதூச் சோற்றுத் தூய்தல்லா தாழ்ந்துளதென்","றிப்பாவி செய்யு மிழிதகவி தென்னோ","விழுதைதான் செய்யு மிழிதகவி தென்னோ."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi asks what this low act of the sinner and fool is: he says that what exists never perishes and what is not never comes to be, yet he says the pure rice he eats in the alms hall has sunk to impurity.","n":"The charge is that calling pure food impure admits a change that the doctrine of 'nothing arises, nothing perishes' forbids; அளி in line 2 is read as the hall where food is given.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["undi","thooimai","vaadhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 696","lb":"NK696","ev":"Neelakesi points out the Ajivika's inconsistency over impure food.","cl":[["nothing that exists perishes and nothing that does not exist comes to be","ajivika","refuted",1,"puranan"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","பூரணன்"],"ch":"nk:31"},"nk:697":{"book":"neelakesi","num":696,"t":["நின்றீக கொண்டீக வுண்டீக தின்றீக","வென்றிவைகள் கூறி யிடுவார்க் கறம்வேண்டான்","கொன்றீகை தீதென்றுங் கொல்பாவ மில்லென்றுந்","தன்றீகை யுண்ணாதான் றான்கண்ட தென்னோ","தவத்தினு மில்வாழ்க்கை தான்கண்ட தென்னோ."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi says the Ajivika wants no merit for those who give in every manner, standing, receiving or eating, and holds both that a gift bought by killing is evil and that killing carries no sin; she asks what one who denies the fruit of his own gift has found, and what he has found in austerity above the household life.","n":"Line 3 sets two Ajivika sayings side by side (கொன்றீகை தீது, கொல்பாவம் இல்) as a contradiction. The Neelakesi gives the Ajivika a denial of the fruit of acts; whether the school would accept that account the verse does not show. Line 5 may instead ask what he saw in household life rather than austerity.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["eegai","kollaamai","aram","thavam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 697","lb":"NK697","ev":"Neelakesi charges the Ajivika with denying the merit of giving and the sin of killing at once.","cl":[["no merit comes to the giver","ajivika","refuted",2,"puranan"],["killing carries no sin","ajivika","refuted",3,"puranan"],["one who denies the fruit of deeds has no reason to prefer austerity to the household life","jain","asserted",5,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","பூரணன்"],"ch":"nk:31"},"nk:698":{"book":"neelakesi","num":697,"t":["இல்லாத தோன்றா கெடாவுள் ளனவென்பாய்","சொல்லாயே நெய்சுடராய்ச் சுட்டிடுமா றென்றேனுக்","கல்லாந் தயிர்த்தோடி யாழ்மிதப்புச் சொல்லுதியா","லெல்லாமொன் றொன்றிற் கிடங்கொடா வன்றே","யிழிவுயர்ச்சிக் காரணமு மில்லாதா யன்றே."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi recalls that when she asked him how ghee turns to flame and burns, if what is not cannot arise and what is cannot perish, he answered only that stones sink and other things float; then, she says, nothing gives room to anything, and he has no cause for sinking and rising.","n":"என்றேனுக்கு in line 2 makes the verse a report of an exchange: her question and his answer. Line 3 (கல்லாந் தயிர்த்தோடி யாழ்மிதப்பு) is hard to divide; the sense of stone sinking and things floating is a guess.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["anu","vaadhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 698","lb":"NK698","ev":"Neelakesi reports the Ajivika's answer about sinking and floating and rejects it as giving no cause.","cl":[["atoms never enter one another, and each sinks or rises by its own nature","ajivika","refuted",4,"puranan"],["a doctrine that admits no change gives no cause for sinking and rising","jain","asserted",5,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி","பூரணன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","பூரணன்"],"ch":"nk:31"},"nk:699":{"book":"neelakesi","num":698,"t":["ஓட்டுங் குதிரையு மொன்றே யெனிற்குதிரை","யூட்டம் பொழுதொடுதான் புல்லுண்ணும் போழ்தின்கா","னாட்டிய வீதி யதிசயத்தை நீயெமக்குக்","காட்டி யுரைப்பினின் காட்சியைக் கோடும்","கடவுட் குழாத்தார்தம் காழ்ப்பெலாங் கோடும்."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi says that if the running horse and the horse that eats grass at feeding time are one unchanged thing, let him show her that marvel and she will accept his view and every dogma of his band of 'gods'.","n":"கடவுட் குழாம் is Puranan's own name for the Ajivika ascetics (668), used here in mockery. The point of காநாட்டிய வீதி is not clear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["gunam","parinaamam","vaadhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 699","lb":"NK699","ev":"Neelakesi challenges the Ajivika to show a thing unchanged through its states.","cl":[["a thing stays one and unchanged through all its states","ajivika","refuted",1,"puranan"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","பூரணன்"],"ch":"nk:31"},"nk:700":{"book":"neelakesi","num":699,"t":["வண்ண முதலா வுடைய குணமெல்லாம்","எண்ணுங்கா லப்பொருளே லீந்தி னிளங்காய்க்கட்","கண்ணினாற் கண்ட பசுமை கனிக்கண்ணுந்","திண்ணிதாக் காட்டிற் றெருண்டாயே யென்றும்","திரிந்தொழிந்த காட்டினாற் றேவனே யென்றும்."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi says that if colour and all the qualities are the substance itself, he should show the greenness seen in the young date-fruit still firm in the ripe fruit; she would call him wise if he could, and a god if he can do it with a thing that has changed.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["gunam","parinaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 700","lb":"NK700","ev":"Neelakesi tests the identity of quality and substance with the ripening date.","cl":[["qualities such as colour are the substance itself","ajivika","refuted",1,"puranan"],["a quality changes while its substance remains, so the two are not identical","jain","asserted",3,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","பூரணன்"],"ch":"nk:31"},"nk:701":{"book":"neelakesi","num":700,"t":["வட்ட முதலா வுடைய பொருளெல்லா","மொட்டிநீ யப்பொருளே யொன்றும்வே றில்லென்பாய்","தட்ட மழித்தோடஞ் செய்தா லதன்கண்ணும்","விட்ட வடிவு விரித்துநீ காட்டாய்","விகார மனைத்தும் விரித்துநீ காட்டாய்."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi says that since he holds that shape and the like are the substance itself and nothing else, he should show the old shape still present when a plate is broken down and made into a vessel, and show all its changes.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["gunam","parinaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 701","lb":"NK701","ev":"Neelakesi asks the Ajivika to show the plate's shape in the pot made from it.","cl":[["shape is the substance itself and nothing other","ajivika","refuted",2,"puranan"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","பூரணன்"],"ch":"nk:31"},"nk:702":{"book":"neelakesi","num":701,"t":["மிதப்பனவு மாழ்வனவும் வேண்டுவனயா னென்னிற்","பதப்பொரு டானான்கின் பன்மைமுடித் தாயா","மிதப்பனவே யாழ்வனதாம் வேறியாது மில்லே","லுதப்பேனு நின்சொ லுதவலவே கண்டா","லுடனேநின் பக்க முடைத்திட்டாய் கண்டாய்."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi says that when she asks for the things that float and sink, he concludes a plurality of four categories; but if what floats is what sinks and there is nothing else, his words serve nothing, and he has broken his own side at once.","n":"பதப்பொருள் நான்கு in line 2 may be four categories of meaning; what they are is not stated. உதப்பேனும் in line 4 is obscure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sol","vaadhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 702","lb":"NK702","ev":"Neelakesi argues that the Ajivika's own distinctions break his doctrine of identity.","cl":[["whoever distinguishes floating from sinking things has already given up their identity","jain","asserted",3,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","பூரணன்"],"ch":"nk:31"},"nk:703":{"book":"neelakesi","num":702,"t":["தொழிற்சொற் குணச்சொல் வடிவுச்சொன் மூன்றும்","பிழைப்பில் பதமாப் பிரிவிடத்துக் காண்டு","மிழுக்கில் பொருளோ டியைத்தக்காற் சந்தி","யெழுத்தியலிற் கூட்டமு மெப்பொழுதுங் காண்டு","மிலக்கண நின்சொ லியையலவே கண்டாய்."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi says that words of action, quality and form are seen distinct when words are separated without error, and joined with their sense they are seen combined by sandhi, but the Ajivika's words fit no rule of grammar.","n":"The argument from grammar is that the three classes of word stay distinct even when joined, as the qualities stay distinct in a thing; the Ajivika's speech is said to break that rule.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sol","gunam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 703","lb":"NK703","ev":"Neelakesi appeals to grammar against the Ajivika's denial of distinct qualities.","cl":[["language distinguishes action, quality and form words, so the things they name are distinct","jain","asserted",1,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","பூரணன்"],"ch":"nk:31"},"nk:704":{"book":"neelakesi","num":703,"t":["அதுவா வதுவு மதுவாம் வகையு","மதுவாந் துணையு மதுவாம் பொழுதுஞ்","சதுவா நியதத் தனவா வுரைத்தல்","செதுவா குதலுஞ் சிலசொல் லுவன்யான்."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi announces that she will say a few words on the Ajivika claim that what comes to be, the way it comes, the measure to which it comes and the time when it comes are all fixed, and on how that claim falls.","n":"The metre changes again to short four-line verses. The refutation follows in 705 to 707 (the infant girl).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["niyatham","oozh","vaadhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 704","lb":"NK704","ev":"Neelakesi turns to the Ajivika doctrine that everything is fixed.","cl":[["what comes to be, how, how much and when are all fixed beforehand","ajivika","refuted",3,"puranan"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","பூரணன்"],"ch":"nk:31"},"nk:705":{"book":"neelakesi","num":704,"t":["அரிவை யவளாங் குழவி யவளை","யுரிய வகையா லுவந்தாங் கெடுத்தா","லரிய முழமூன் றளவாம் பொழுதும்","வரிசை யுரைத்த வருட மதன்பின்."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi sets up her case: take up with proper care the infant girl who is to become a woman, and consider her when she is three cubits tall and when the years stated in their order have passed.","n":"The verse only sets up the example; its point comes in 706 and 707, where the woman's breasts, child and hair would have to be present already in the infant if all is fixed.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["niyatham","ilamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 705","lb":"NK705","ev":"Neelakesi begins an example of a girl child to test the doctrine of fixed outcome.","cl":[],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","பூரணன்"],"ch":"nk:31"},"nk:706":{"book":"neelakesi","num":705,"t":["குழவித் திறமுந் துறவா ளவளும்","முழுவித் ததுவும் முளையா துளதா","மிழவெத் துணையு மியல்பேன் முடியா","தழிவித் திடுவே னயநீ விரையல்."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi says that, on his view, the nature of the child and the woman she will be, the whole of the seed, already exist without sprouting, and tells him not to hurry, for she will undo his view before it can complete its course.","n":"துறவாளவளும் in line 1 is unclear (perhaps 'she who will not leave it' or 'the renunciant'); the reply is in 707, where breasts, child and hair absent from the infant overturn the view.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["niyatham","vaadhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 706","lb":"NK706","ev":"Neelakesi presses the infant example against the fixed-outcome doctrine.","cl":[["the later states of a being already exist, unsprouted, in its beginning","ajivika","refuted",2,"puranan"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","பூரணன்"],"ch":"nk:31"},"nk:707":{"book":"neelakesi","num":706,"t":["முலையும் மகவும் முறுவல் லவையும்","தலையுண் மயிரும் முகிரும் முடனே","நிலையில் லமையு மிலதா மெனினே","யலையுந் நினகோ ளுடனே யெனலும்."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi argues that breasts, children, smiles (the teeth), the hair of the head and the nails appear only later; if they had no standing existence before, the Ajivika's doctrine that what does not exist cannot arise is shaken.","n":"The verse continues the test begun at 704-706, where the Ajivika's fixed order (நியதம்) was set against a woman and the infant she bears; the parts named are exactly those a newborn lacks. Line 3's நிலையில்லாமை is read here as 'not existing beforehand', which is the sense the argument needs.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oozh","karu","yaakkai","vaadhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 707","lb":"NK707","ev":"","cl":[["whatever a being will have is already present in it, since what does not exist cannot come to be","ajivika","refuted",4,"neelakesi"],["bodily parts such as breasts, teeth, hair and nails come into being later and were not there before","jain","asserted",3,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","பூரணன்"],"ch":"nk:32"},"nk:708":{"book":"neelakesi","num":707,"t":["உளவே யெனின்முன் னுரைத்தந் தியதங்","களவே யெனலாங் கடையா மெனநீ","கிளவா தொழியாய் கிளந்த குழவிக்","களவே முழமா வவைதாம் பலவால்."],"p":"","r":"If the Ajivika answers that these things do exist in the infant already, Neelakesi says, his talk of a fixed later time is a sham, and he cannot keep saying they come at the end, for the infant he spoke of is a cubit long while the things to come are many.","n":"களவே (line 2) is taken as 'deceit': if everything is already there, calling its appearance 'fixed for later' only hides the problem. The division of lines 2-4 is hard to fix.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["oozh","karu","vaadhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 708","lb":"NK708","ev":"","cl":[["the infant already contains all it will later have","ajivika","refuted",1,"neelakesi"],["a cubit-long infant cannot hold the many parts and powers it will later have","jain","asserted",4,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","பூரணன்"],"ch":"nk:32"},"nk:709":{"book":"neelakesi","num":708,"t":["உடையள் ளிவடன் னுதரத் தொருபெண்","ணடையு மவளுக் கவளவ் வகையாற்","கடையில் குழவி யவைதன் னியல்பா","நடையு மதுவே னகையாம் பிறவோ."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi draws the regress: this woman holds a girl in her womb, that girl in the same way holds her own, and so on without end, so that endless infants would lie one within another by nature, which is laughable.","n":"This is a reductio of preformation: the generations to come must all be packed in the first womb. The Ajivika claim answered is that of 707-708.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["karu","oozh","vaadhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 709","lb":"NK709","ev":"","cl":[["if all that is to come is already present, a woman's unborn daughter must already hold her own daughter, and so without end, which is absurd","jain","asserted",1,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","பூரணன்"],"ch":"nk:32"},"nk:710":{"book":"neelakesi","num":709,"t":["இனியாம் வகையு மிசைத்தி யெனினுந்","நனிகா ரணமாய் நடுக்கு நினகோட்","டனிகா ரியமும் முளதேற் றவறா","முனிலா மொருவன் பொழுதும் முடிவாம்."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi says that even if the Ajivika states how things will come to be, his doctrine trembles once the fixed order is made the cause, for if a separate effect also exists it is a fault, and one man's time and end would then be settled.","n":"The line division (நனிகாரணமாய் நடுக்கு நினகோட்டனிகாரியமும்) allows several readings; the gist taken here is that a fixed order leaves nothing for a person's own deeds to cause.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["oozh","vinai","vaadhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 710","lb":"NK710","ev":"","cl":[["if the fixed order is the sole cause, there is no room for any separate effect of one's own doing","jain","asserted",3,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","பூரணன்"],"ch":"nk:32"},"nk:711":{"book":"neelakesi","num":710,"t":["நியதந் நிகழ்ச்சிந் நியதா வுரைப்ப","தயதி யெனினீ யமையுஞ் சலமேல்","வியதி யெனினும் வெகுளல் இழுதை","பயதி யெனினு நினக்கோர் பயனே."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi plays on the Ajivika's word niyati: to say every occurrence is fixed is niyati; if he says 'ayati' he settles in falsehood, if 'viyati' his anger is foolish, and if 'payati' that too gives him something.","n":"அயதி, வியதி and பயதி look like Sanskrit-sounding variations on நியதி used to mock the opponent, but what each means here cannot be fixed from the Tamil alone.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["oozh","vaadhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 711","lb":"NK711","ev":"","cl":[["every occurrence is fixed by niyati","ajivika","refuted",1,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","பூரணன்"],"ch":"nk:32"},"nk:712":{"book":"neelakesi","num":711,"t":["பாலைப் பழுத்தி னிறத்தன வாய்ப்பல மாட்டொடுகண்","ணாலெத் துணையு மகன்றைந்து நூறாம் புகையுயர்ந்து","ஞாலத் தியன்றன நல்லுயி ரென்பது நாட்டுகின்றாய்","மாலித் துணையுள வோநீ பெரிதும் மயங்கினையோ."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi mocks the Ajivika's account of souls, which gives them colours like ripe fruit and counts them in great numbers rising like smoke through the world, asking whether there can be so many delusions and whether he is not greatly confused.","n":"The colours seem to answer to the Ajivika grades of birth by colour (the Manimekalai's Ajivika gives six, black to white); the numbers in lines 1-2 (எத்துணையும் அகன்று ஐந்து நூறாம்) are unclear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["uyir","samayam","vaadhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 712","lb":"NK712","ev":"","cl":[["souls are of distinct colours and fixed great numbers","ajivika","refuted",3,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","பூரணன்"],"ch":"nk:32"},"nk:713":{"book":"neelakesi","num":712,"t":["ஒன்றினு ளொன்று புகலிலவென்ற வுயிர்களெல்லா","நின்றன தந்த மகலமுநீளமும் பெற்றனவாய்","நன்றுநீ சொல்லுதி நாந்தொக்கிருந்துழி நல்லுயிர்க","டுன்றின வென்பது சொல்லாதினியென்ன சொல்லுதியோ."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi turns the Ajivika's claim that no soul enters another and that each has its own breadth and length against him: when we sit crowded together, souls would have to press one upon another, and he has no answer to that.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["uyir","anu","vaadhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 713","lb":"NK713","ev":"","cl":[["souls never enter one another and each has its own length and breadth","ajivika","refuted",1,"neelakesi"],["if souls cannot share room, beings crowded together would leave their souls no place","jain","asserted",3,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","பூரணன்"],"ch":"nk:32"},"nk:714":{"book":"neelakesi","num":713,"t":["தானுள தாய வழியதன் றன்பா லியல்பெனலா","மூனுள தாய வுயிர்ப்பிர தேச முணர்வதுபோல்","வானுளம் போயுழி மன்னு மறிவிலை யேலதனை","நானுள தென்றுரை யேனதற் கியாரினி நாட்டுகிற்பார்."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi argues that a thing's nature is in itself where it exists: as the soul's extent within the flesh perceives, so if the soul knows nothing once it has gone above, she would not call it existent at all, and none could then establish it.","n":"பிரதேசம் (line 2) is the Jain term for the soul's spatial units spread through the body. Which Ajivika claim the verse answers (a released soul without knowledge, perhaps) is not stated.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["uyir","arivu","veedu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 714","lb":"NK714","ev":"","cl":[["knowing belongs to the soul's own nature wherever the soul is","jain","asserted",1,"neelakesi"],["a soul that knows nothing after it departs could not be shown to exist","jain","asserted",4,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","பூரணன்"],"ch":"nk:32"},"nk:715":{"book":"neelakesi","num":714,"t":["ஒன்றென நின்ற உயிர்தானுருவின தாதலினாற்","பொன்றுந் துணையும்பல் போழெய்தும்பூசணிக் காயினைப்போ","லின்றெனி னாகம மாறதுவாமினி யவ்விரண்டு","மின்றெனிற் சால வெளிதாம்பிறவத னின்மையுமே."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi says that if the one soul has form, it would take many slices like a pumpkin until it perished; if the Ajivika denies that, his own scripture is contradicted; and if he gives up both, it becomes very easy to show the soul does not exist.","n":"The trilemma leaves the opponent three exits, each fatal: a cuttable soul, a contradicted scripture (ஆகமம்), or no soul at all.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["uyir","aagamam","vaadhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 715","lb":"NK715","ev":"","cl":[["the soul has bodily form","ajivika","refuted",1,"neelakesi"],["what has form can be cut into parts and destroyed","jain","asserted",2,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","பூரணன்"],"ch":"nk:32"},"nk:716":{"book":"neelakesi","num":715,"t":["எண்டனை யாக்கி யிடவகை யுட்பொரு ளீறுசொல்லி","மண்டல மாக்கி மறுத்துங் கொணரு மனத்தினையேற்","கண்டிலை நீமெய்ம்மை காழ்ப்பட்டு நின்ற கனவுயிர்க்கெண்","ணுண்டெனி னில்லை யகன்றடுமாற்ற முலப்பின்மைபோல்."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi says the Ajivika, who counts souls by a number, sets out their places and their end and makes a great circle that brings them round again, has not seen the truth; if souls are of fixed count, the wandering would not be endless.","n":"மண்டலம் (line 2) is read as the Ajivika's great cycle of births after which all reach the end; the text's அகன்றடுமாற்றம் may be அகன்ற தடுமாற்றம் (the wide wandering). Line 3 is obscure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["piravi","uyir","oozh","vaadhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 716","lb":"NK716","ev":"","cl":[["souls are of fixed number and pass through a fixed great cycle to their end","ajivika","refuted",1,"neelakesi"],["if souls were finite in number the round of births could not be without end","jain","asserted",4,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","பூரணன்"],"ch":"nk:32"},"nk:717":{"book":"neelakesi","num":716,"t":["மேற்சீர தீயோ டுயிர்காற்று விலங்கு சீராம்","பாற்சீர நீரு நிலந்தானும் பணிந்த சீரா","மேற்சீர மேற்போம் விலங்கோடு விலங்கு சீர்கீ","ழாற்சீர வீழும் மவையென்னினு மாவ தென்னோ."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi sets out the Ajivika teaching of natural motions: fire and the soul move upward, wind moves sideways, water and earth move downward, each falling or rising by its kind, and asks what follows even if that is so.","n":"The answer follows in 718-720: a soul that rises by nature could not stay in a body, and wind and water change direction.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["uyir","anu","thaththuvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 717","lb":"NK717","ev":"","cl":[["fire and the soul by nature move upward, wind sideways, water and earth downward","ajivika","refuted",1,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","பூரணன்"],"ch":"nk:32"},"nk:718":{"book":"neelakesi","num":717,"t":["தீயு முயிருந் தமக்காய திசையி னாலே","போயு மொழியா திவணிற்றல் பொருத்த மன்றால்","வீயும் வகையும் வினையாக்கும் திறமு மெல்லாம்","நீயு மவற்றை நினைவாயுள வாக வன்றோ."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi argues that if fire and soul go by their own direction, it is not fitting that the soul should stay here at all; yet the Ajivika himself holds that dying and the working of deeds exist.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["uyir","vinai","vaadhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 718","lb":"NK718","ev":"","cl":[["a soul that rises by its own nature could not remain bound in a body to die and do deeds","jain","asserted",2,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","பூரணன்"],"ch":"nk:32"},"nk:719":{"book":"neelakesi","num":718,"t":["தென்றை யதுளையத் திசைதானுறப் போய காற்றேற்","பின்றை யொருநாட் பெயராததோர் பெற்றி யஃதான்","முன்றை தழுவி முனிவாக்கும் வடந்தை யத்தா","வின்றைப் பகலே யிதன்மெய்ம்மை யிசைக்கிற்றியோ."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi says that the wind which went south until it reached its quarter will one day turn back, and asks the ascetic whether he can now state in daylight the truth of his doctrine of fixed direction.","n":"வடந்தை (line 3) is the north wind; the point seems to be that the same wind blows from south and from north. Lines 3-4 are obscure, and line 2 has a repaired letter.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thaththuvam","vaadhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 719","lb":"NK719","ev":"","cl":[["the elements do not keep one fixed direction of motion","jain","asserted",1,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","பூரணன்"],"ch":"nk:32"},"nk:720":{"book":"neelakesi","num":719,"t":["முன்சென்று வீழுந் நிலநீரை முகிலு ணின்று","பின்சென்று பெய்யுந் துளிதானும் பெருந்த வத்தா","யென்சென்ற தெய்துந் திறந்தன் னையெனக் குணர","நின்சென்ற வாற்றா லுரைத்தானெறி யாற்ற நன்றே."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi says, mocking, that the Ajivika has explained well how water, which first falls to the ground and then comes down again as drops from the clouds, reaches where it goes.","n":"நன்றே (line 4) is read as irony: water that has gone up into the cloud breaks the rule that water only sinks.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thaththuvam","mazhai","vaadhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 720","lb":"NK720","ev":"","cl":[["water that rises into cloud and falls again does not simply move downward by nature","jain","asserted",1,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","பூரணன்"],"ch":"nk:32"},"nk:721":{"book":"neelakesi","num":720,"t":["பாலெங்கு மோதப் படுகின்ற பதப்பொ ருட்குக்","காலங்கள் சொல்லா யதுதானுன் கணக்கு மென்றாற்","சீலங்கள் காத்துக் குணனின்மையைச் செப்பு கின்றாய்","மாலிங் குடையை யதுதீர்க்கு மருந்து முண்டோ."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi says the Ajivika does not give the times of the categories that are recited everywhere, yet guards precepts while proclaiming that there are no qualities, and asks whether any medicine can cure such madness.","n":"மால் (line 4) turns the debate toward the medical images of 722-724. What 'times' and 'qualities' refer to is not settled by the Tamil.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["gunam","noy","marundhu","vaadhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 721","lb":"NK721","ev":"","cl":[["things (or the soul) are without qualities","ajivika","refuted",3,"neelakesi"],["keeping precepts makes no sense for one who denies qualities","jain","asserted",3,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","பூரணன்"],"ch":"nk:32"},"nk:722":{"book":"neelakesi","num":721,"t":["நோயுற்ற நுன்போற் குணமொன்றில னாய யானும்","பேய்மற் றிவடா னெனக்கண்டோர் பெரிய வன்றா","னீமற்றி துண்ணென் றறநல்க விளங்கப் பெற்றேன்","வாமத்து ணீயும் மதுபோலு மருந்தில் லையே."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi tells the Ajivika that she too was once like him, sick and without any good quality, so that those who saw her called her a demon, until a great one gave her dharma saying 'take this' and she grew bright; there is no medicine like it.","n":"She speaks of her own past: the demoness of the burning ground whom the sage Munichandra taught (ch. 3-7). The debater offers the opponent the cure she herself received. The opening of line 4 (வாமத்துணீயும்) is unclear.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["marundhu","noy","aram","guru"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 722","lb":"NK722","ev":"Neelakesi tells the Ajivika how she, once a demon, was cured by the dharma a great teacher gave her.","cl":[["the dharma taught by a teacher cures a being as medicine cures the sick","jain","asserted",3,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","பூரணன்"],"ch":"nk:32"},"nk:723":{"book":"neelakesi","num":722,"t":["நோயைத் துணிந்தே யுறுநோய்முத னாடியந்நோய்க்","காய மருந்தே யறிந்தூட்டுமஃ துண்டு காட்டிற்","பாய மறுக்கும் படியாமது பல்லு யிர்க்கும்","கூயத்தி னென்னை குரவருப தேச மென்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi says the teachers' instruction is like a physician's: it settles what the disease is, searches out its root, knows the medicine fit for it and gives it, and so cuts away the suffering of many beings.","n":"The order of line 1 (நோய், நோய்முதல் நாடி) is the order of Kural 948, but the shared run is under four words, so it is linked, not marked as an echo. கூயத்தின் (line 4) is unclear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["marundhu","noy","guru","arivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 723","lb":"NK723","ev":"","cl":[["a teacher's instruction, like a physician's, finds the disease, its root and its fitting medicine","jain","asserted",1,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","பூரணன்"],"ch":"nk:32"},"nk:724":{"book":"neelakesi","num":723,"t":["சாதி முதலாப் பிணிதாமிவை யப்பி ணியிற்","காதி யறியில் லவைதீவினை யூண தனாற்","றீதின் றிதனில் லழுந்தல்திரி தோடத் தினால்","வேத னையது தீர்ப்பது மெய்யுணர் வாமே."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi names birth and the rest as the diseases; their cause, known rightly, is the eating of bad deeds; sinking in them comes from the threefold fault; and what ends the suffering is true understanding.","n":"திரிதோடம் (line 3) is the physician's three humours, carried over to the soul; the Jain triple fault is probably meant, but the verse does not name its members.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["piravi","noy","vinai","meyyunarvu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 724","lb":"NK724","ev":"","cl":[["birth and its sequels are diseases caused by eating the fruit of bad deeds","jain","asserted",1,"neelakesi"],["true understanding is the cure that ends the suffering of births","jain","asserted",4,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","பூரணன்"],"ch":"nk:32"},"nk:725":{"book":"neelakesi","num":724,"t":["மானின்ற நோக்கின் மறவேனெடுங் கண்ணி னல்லாய்","மேனின்ற வெல்லா மிகநல்லவிம் மெய்யுணர்ச்சி","தானின்ற தன்மை தவிராதுரைக் கிற்றி யேனின்","னூனின்ற வாறே பொருணோக்குவன் யானு மென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"The Ajivika answers Neelakesi, praising her deer-like gaze and long eyes: he will not forget; if she will state without fail how this true understanding, best of all, stands, he too will look at things the way she does.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["meyyunarvu","guru","vaadhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 725","lb":"NK725","ev":"The Ajivika Puranan asks Neelakesi to teach him true understanding and promises to see things her way.","cl":[],"ag":["பூரணன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["பூரணன்","நீலகேசி"],"ch":"nk:32"},"nk:726":{"book":"neelakesi","num":725,"t":["நின்ற விரலுந் நிலையாழ்ந்து முடங்க லாயிற்","சென்றவ் விரலும் மெனத்தானின் கூற்ற தாயிற்","றொன்றவ் விரலே யுறலுண்மையு மின்மை யும்மா","மென்றவ் விரலே யிதுவென்றனள் வேற்க ணல்லாள்."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi shows him a finger: if the straight finger bends and he says 'that finger has gone', then by his own words that one finger both is and is not; this, she says, is the point.","n":"The finger that remains a finger while its straightness ends is the Jain way of holding that a thing persists and changes at once, against the Ajivika's 'nothing arises, nothing perishes' (698).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["meyyunarvu","arivu","vaadhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 726","lb":"NK726","ev":"Neelakesi bends a finger before Puranan to show that a thing both is and is not.","cl":[["one and the same thing both is and is not, in different respects","jain","asserted",3,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","பூரணன்"],"ch":"nk:32"},"nk:727":{"book":"neelakesi","num":726,"t":["ஆழ்ச்சி யொருபா லதுவல்லன தம்மோ டாழா","தேழ்ச்சி யொன்பா லதுதன்னொடு மின்ன தென்னச்","சூழ்ச்சி யமைந்த துணைத்தோளியர் சொற்க ளென்று","தாழ்ச்சி மனத்தா லிதுதத்துவ மென்ற னன்னா."],"p":"","r":"The narrator says that hearing her words, that a thing sinks in one respect and not with others, and rises in one respect, the Ajivika, with a humbled mind, said 'this is the truth'.","n":"ஆழ்ச்சி and ஏழ்ச்சி (sinking and rising) take up the natural-motion debate of 717-720 in the new key of respects.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thaththuvam","panivu","vaadhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 727","lb":"NK727","ev":"Puranan, humbled, accepts Neelakesi's teaching as the truth.","cl":[["a thing sinks in one respect and rises in another","jain","asserted",1,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["பூரணன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"nk:32"},"nk:728":{"book":"neelakesi","num":727,"t":["பொய்ந்நின்ற வெல்லாம் புரைத்தாயினிப் பூர ணன்னே","மெய்ந்நின்ற பெற்றி யறிந்தாயிதன் மேலு நன்றாக்","கைந்நின்று முண்டுங் கடைப்பள்ளி வழியு மாக்கிச்","செய்ந்நின்று நீசெய் தவந்தானெனச் செப்பி னளே."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi tells Puranan that he has cleared away all falsehood and come to know the truth; beyond this, eating from the hand and taking the last resting place, he should do the penance proper to him.","n":"கைந்நின்று உண்டும் is read as the Jain ascetic's eating from the palms, and கடைப்பள்ளி as the last bed, the vowed death; both readings are probable but not certain.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thavam","thuravu","undi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 728","lb":"NK728","ev":"Neelakesi tells the converted Puranan to take up ascetic penance.","cl":[["one who has seen the truth should take up the renouncer's discipline of eating from the hand and the final fast","jain","asserted",3,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி","பூரணன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","பூரணன்"],"ch":"nk:32"},"nk:729":{"book":"neelakesi","num":728,"t":["கல்லா தறிந்த கடவுள்ளிறை யாகு மெய்ந்நூல்","சொல்லானு மல்ல னவன்சொல்லின தாகு மும்மூன்","றெல்லாப் பொருளுந் தம்பான்மை யியல்பு மேன்று","பொல்லாத போக்கி யினிப்பூரண சென்மி னென்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi gives Puranan the true scripture of the god who knew without learning, the three-times-three categories and the nature of all things, bids him put off the wrong paths, and sends him away.","n":"மும்மூன்று (lines 2-3) is read as the nine categories the Jains recite. Line 2 (சொல்லானும் அல்லன்) seems to say the lord is not a mere speaker but the source whose word it is; the sense is not secure.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","thaththuvam","aagamam","guru"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 729","lb":"NK729","ev":"Neelakesi gives Puranan the nine categories and dismisses him.","cl":[["the true scripture is the word of the lord who knew without being taught","jain","asserted",1,"neelakesi"],["all things are comprised in the nine categories","jain","asserted",2,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி","பூரணன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","பூரணன்"],"ch":"nk:32"},"nk:730":{"book":"neelakesi","num":729,"t":["ஆங்கவன் சொல்லவவ் வத்தின புரத்து","ளோங்க வொருகொடி நட்டுரைக் கிற்பவ","னாங்க ணெவர்க்கு மறையென் றிருந்தவச்","சாங்கியன் றன்னைத் தலைப்பெயச் சென்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"When the defeated Ajivika had told her of him, Neelakesi went to Hastinapura to confront the Sankhya teacher who had planted a tall challenge flag there and was held by everyone as their authority.","n":"ஆங்கவன் சொல்ல picks up the end of the Ajivika sarukkam (729), where she sends Puranan away; the flag planted to dare all comers (கொடி நட்டு) is the formal opening of a debate. எவர்க்கும் மறை என்று இருந்த is read here as 'regarded by all as their scripture or authority'; it could also mean he was hidden from or closed to everyone.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vaadhu","samayam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 730","lb":"NK730","ev":"On Puranan's word Neelakesi goes to Hastinapura to meet the Sankhya teacher who has planted a challenge flag.","cl":[],"ag":["பூரணன்","நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"ch":"nk:33"},"nk:731":{"book":"neelakesi","num":730,"t":["சென்றா ளவன்றன் சிரத்தையைக் கண்டோத","நின்றா ளவன்றா னெறிபகர் கின்றனன்","பின்றான் பிரளையத் தாக்கமும் பேர்ச்சியு","மொன்றா வகையா லுரைகளை யொட்டா"],"p":"","r":"Arriving, she saw his devotion and stood to hear him recite, and he set out his path, then the coming forth and the drawing back of things at the world's dissolution.","n":"பிரளையத்து ஆக்கமும் பேர்ச்சியும, the emergence and the withdrawal, is the Sankhya cycle of manifestation and reabsorption into nature. Line 4 (ஒன்றா வகையால் உரைகளை ஒட்டா) breaks off and seems to run on into 732; its sense is not clear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pakuthi","thaththuvam","samayam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 731","lb":"NK731","ev":"Neelakesi listens as the Sankhya teacher expounds his path and the cycle of emergence and dissolution.","cl":[["the world comes forth from nature and is drawn back into it at dissolution","vedic","reported",3,"paraasaran"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி","பராசரன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"nk:33"},"nk:732":{"book":"neelakesi","num":731,"t":["பாலொத்து நின்ற பரமாத் துமனொடு","மூலப் பகுதியு மல்லாப் பகுதியு","மேலொத் தியன்ற விதிவிகற் போடு","நூலிற் கிடந்தவ நுண்பொரு ளன்றே."],"p":"","r":"The narrator sums up what he expounded: the supreme self, root nature and the nature that is not the root, with the rules and divisions that belong to them, the subtle matters laid down in his treatise.","n":"மூலப் பகுதி and அல்லாப் பகுதி render the Sankhya distinction between primal nature and its evolutes. பாலொத்து நின்ற is obscure; it may liken the self to milk, or mean 'standing on its own side'. The supreme self (பரமாத்துமன்) returns in 746-747, where she asks how it relates to the many souls.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pakuthi","thaththuvam","uyir"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 732","lb":"NK732","ev":"","cl":[["the real things are the supreme self, root nature and the evolved nature that is not the root","vedic","reported",2,"paraasaran"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"nk:33"},"nk:733":{"book":"neelakesi","num":732,"t":["ஒருங்கிருந் தார்கட் குடனவை யெல்லாம்","பருங்கினன் மெய்யும் பராசரன் றன்னை","விரும்பினள் போல வினவின ளன்றே","யருங்கல மாய வறிவினுள் மிக்காள்."],"p":"","r":"He laid all this out before the gathered assembly, and she, the foremost in knowledge, questioned Parasaran as if she too longed for the truth.","n":"விரும்பினள் போல, 'as though she desired it', tells the reader her questions are a debater's feint: she plays the eager pupil to draw out his doctrine before attacking it. பருங்கினன் in line 2 is an unusual form, perhaps for a verb of setting out or teaching.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vaadhu","arivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 733","lb":"NK733","ev":"Before the assembly Neelakesi questions the Sankhya teacher Parasaran, feigning a learner's eagerness.","cl":[],"ag":["பராசரன்","நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"ch":"nk:33"},"nk:734":{"book":"neelakesi","num":733,"t":["என்னைப் பயந்தீரிஃ தென்னெனக் கேட்டன","ணன்னுத லாயிது வென்னெறி யென்றன","னன்னனஃ தாயி னறிவி யெனச்சொல","வின்னன கேளென் றெடுத்தனன் சொல்லும்."],"p":"","r":"She asks him, as a disciple asks the one who begot her, what this teaching is; he says it is his path, she asks to be taught it, and he begins: hear these things.","n":"என்னைப் பயந்தீர், 'you who gave me birth', is the address of a pupil to a teacher as spiritual parent, and continues the feint of 733; நன்னுதலாய், 'fair-browed one', is his condescending address to a woman.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vaadhu","samayam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 734","lb":"NK734","ev":"Neelakesi, posing as a pupil, asks to be taught the Sankhya path, and the teacher begins.","cl":[],"ag":["நீலகேசி","பராசரன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"nk:33"},"nk:735":{"book":"neelakesi","num":734,"t":["மன்னுயிர் தெற்றென வில்லது மான்செருக்","கென்னவு மிந்திய மைந்தைந் தொருமன","மன்னதன் மாத்திரை யைந்தைந்து பூதமும்","பன்னிய வையைம் பதப்பொரு ளென்றான்"],"p":"","r":"The Sankhya teacher lists his twenty-five categories: the abiding soul, the unmanifest, the great principle, the sense of self, the five and five senses, the one mind, the five subtle elements and the five gross elements.","n":"The count works out: soul, the unmanifest (தெற்றென இல்லது, 'what is not clearly there'), மான் (mahat), செருக்கு (ahamkara), ten senses, mind, five மாத்திரை (tanmatras) and five பூதம் make twenty-five. Neelakesi pronounces these categories faulty in 739 and attacks the soul among them from 740 on.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thaththuvam","pakuthi","uyir"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 735","lb":"NK735","ev":"","cl":[["reality is exhausted by twenty-five categories, from the soul and the unmanifest down to the five elements","vedic","refuted",4,"paraasaran"]],"ag":["பராசரன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["பராசரன்","நீலகேசி"],"ch":"nk:33"},"nk:736":{"book":"neelakesi","num":735,"t":["எத்திறத் தின்னுஞ்செய் யான்குண மொன்றிலன்","றத்துவ னின்பன பேதன லேபக","னித்திய னெங்கு முளனெடுங் காட்சியன்","றுத்த லுடையனென் றோன்றலு மென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"He describes the soul: it does nothing in any way, has none of the qualities, is a principle of its own, unstained, eternal, present everywhere, the far-seeing witness, and yet the one who experiences.","n":"Line 2 (இன்பன பேதனலே பகன்) is hard to divide; அலேபகன், 'unstained', can be made out, but the rest is uncertain. The pairing in line 4 of the non-acting soul with துத்தல் உடையன், 'one who experiences or eats', is the joint Neelakesi presses in 740-745: how can what does nothing enjoy?","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["uyir","mukkunam","thaththuvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 736","lb":"NK736","ev":"","cl":[["the soul does no action of any kind and is without the qualities","vedic","refuted",1,"paraasaran"],["the soul is eternal, all-pervading, a seer and an experiencer","vedic","refuted",3,"paraasaran"]],"ag":["பராசரன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["பராசரன்","நீலகேசி"],"ch":"nk:33"},"nk:737":{"book":"neelakesi","num":736,"t":["நின்று பரந்தரு வாய்ப்பொறி யேதுமிக்","கொன்று கிரிகையின் றப்பியத் தம்மது","சென்று செய் மானிற் செருக்கத்தி னீரெட்டு","மன்றியு மைவகைப் பூதமு மன்றே."],"p":"","r":"He describes the unmanifest as standing, spread out and formless, with no sense organ and no action, and from it the great principle, from the sense of self the sixteen, and besides them the five elements.","n":"ஈரெட்டு, 'twice eight', are the sixteen products of the sense of self (the eleven organs with mind and the five subtle elements), with the five gross elements beyond them, the classical Sankhya order of evolution. Line 1's இக்கு and line 2's division are unclear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pakuthi","thaththuvam","mukkunam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 737","lb":"NK737","ev":"","cl":[["from the formless unmanifest arise the great principle, the sense of self, its sixteen products and the five elements","vedic","reported",3,"paraasaran"]],"ag":["பராசரன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["பராசரன்","நீலகேசி"],"ch":"nk:33"},"nk:739":{"book":"neelakesi","num":737,"t":["கதக்களி யானைமுன் கல்லெறிந் தாற்போற்","பதப்பொரு டம்மைப் பழுதென் றுரைப்ப","மதத்தினின் மிக்கவன் மாதரை நோக்கி","யுதப்பென்னுங் குற்ற முரையெனக் கென்ன."],"p":"","r":"Like one throwing a stone before a raging rutting elephant she declared his categories faulty, and he, swollen with pride, turned to the woman and told her to name the fault.","n":"மதம் is both an elephant's rut and a school's doctrine, so மதத்தினின் மிக்கவன் is the man swollen with rut and with his creed; the simile makes her challenge brave rather than rash. உதப்பு is a rare word, taken here as the fault or flaw he demands she show.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vaadhu","thaththuvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 739","lb":"NK739","ev":"Neelakesi declares the Sankhya categories faulty, and the angry teacher demands that she name the fault.","cl":[["the Sankhya's twenty-five categories are faulty","jain","asserted",2,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி","பராசரன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"nk:33"},"nk:740":{"book":"neelakesi","num":738,"t":["செய்யா துயிரெனச் செப்புகின் றாய்நின்னை","வையா யுயிருள தன்றெனின் வாக்கிவை","மெய்யாம் பிறசெய்கை யாதலி னாலிவை","மையா மினிநின்றன் மார்க்கமு மன்றே."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi argues that if he says the soul does not act, then his speaking cannot be his own: these words, being the work of something else, are flawed, and the path is not even his.","n":"The argument turns the doctrine on the speaker: a teacher whose self does nothing cannot claim the teaching he is uttering. Lines 1-2 (நின்னை வையாய் ... உளது அன்றெனின்) are hard to construe, and the reading of their steps is tentative.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["uyir","vaadhu","sol"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 740","lb":"NK740","ev":"","cl":[["if the soul does nothing, the speaker's own words are not his doing and his doctrine is not his","jain","asserted",3,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","பராசரன்"],"ch":"nk:33"},"nk:741":{"book":"neelakesi","num":739,"t":["எத்திறத் தின்னுஞ்செய் யானவ னென்றலிற்","றத்துவஞ் சொல்லுந் தலைமக னாகிய","சித்தியு மில்லாந் திருட்ட விரோதமும்","பொய்த்தலுள் ளிட்டவும் புல்லுமற் றென்ன."],"p":"","r":"She goes on: since he holds the soul does nothing in any way, even his standing as the master who teaches the principles fails, and contradiction with what is seen and falsehood cling to the doctrine.","n":"Line 1 repeats 736's own words (எத்திறத்தின்னுஞ்செய்யான்) so the refutation quotes the thesis. திருட்ட விரோதம், contradiction with what is seen, is a named fault of the debate manuals: everyone sees the soul acting.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["uyir","alavai","vaadhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 741","lb":"NK741","ev":"","cl":[["a soul that does nothing could not be the teacher of the principles, and the claim contradicts what is seen","jain","asserted",3,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","பராசரன்"],"ch":"nk:33"},"nk:742":{"book":"neelakesi","num":740,"t":["பெருமை யுயிர்க்குரை யேன்செய்கை பின்னும்","இருமை யுயிரென தாமிடை யொன்றுங்","கரும வுயிரு மிவற்றினி னன்றே","யருமை யுடைச்செய்கை யாக்கமு மென்றான்"],"p":"","r":"The Sankhya replies that the soul is of two kinds, and that apart from the soul that does nothing there is a soul bound up with action, from which the work of the world proceeds.","n":"This is his escape from 740-741: action belongs to a karmic soul (கரும உயிர்), not to the pure one. Line 1 (பெருமை உயிர்க்குரையேன்) and the middle term in line 2 (இடை ஒன்றும்) are obscure. Neelakesi answers at once in 743.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["uyir","vinai","thaththuvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 742","lb":"NK742","ev":"","cl":[["there are two kinds of soul, and action comes from the soul bound with deeds","vedic","refuted",3,"paraasaran"]],"ag":["பராசரன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["பராசரன்","நீலகேசி"],"ch":"nk:33"},"nk:743":{"book":"neelakesi","num":741,"t":["செய்யு முயிர்களுஞ் செய்யா வுயிர்கனும்","மெய்யி னுளவெனின் மேற்கோ ளழிதலும்","பொய்யும் பொதியறை மையுமற் றல்லவு","மெய்து மிதற்கினி யென்செய்தி யென்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"She replies that if acting souls and non-acting souls both truly exist, his thesis is destroyed and falsehood and the other faults come upon it; what will he do now?","n":"மேற்கோள் is the formal thesis of a debate; to grant an acting soul is to contradict the thesis that the soul does not act, so the defence of 742 sinks the position it defends.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["uyir","vaadhu","alavai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 743","lb":"NK743","ev":"","cl":[["granting acting souls beside non-acting ones destroys the thesis that the soul does not act","jain","asserted",2,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","பராசரன்"],"ch":"nk:33"},"nk:744":{"book":"neelakesi","num":742,"t":["சோம்பன் குணமிலன் றோன்றா வொளிப்பின","னோம்பற் கருமையி னுண்ணுமாற் றானுயி","னாம்பின்னைச் செய்ததெ னன்கவ னின்றியும்","போம்பொழு தேலவ னாற்பொரு ளென்னோ."],"p":"","r":"She asks what an idle soul without qualities, hidden and unmanifest, actually does: if it only enjoys, how does it come to enjoy, and if everything goes on without it, what is it for?","n":"சோம்பன், 'the idler', is her mocking name for the inactive soul. Line 2 (ஓம்பற்கு அருமையின் உண்ணுமாற்றான்) is hard to construe; the question 'how does it eat' answers to the experiencer of 736.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["uyir","vinai","mukkunam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 744","lb":"NK744","ev":"","cl":[["a soul that does nothing and without which things go on serves no purpose, and cannot be the enjoyer","jain","asserted",4,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","பராசரன்"],"ch":"nk:33"},"nk:745":{"book":"neelakesi","num":743,"t":["ஆண்டரு வாய்த்தொழில் யாதுமில் லாயின்","வேண்டின்மெய் யாகி விகிர்தி விகற்பொடு","தீண்டலு மொட்டலுந் தேரினி லாதவன்","காண்டற்குந் துத்தற்குங் காரண மென்னோ."],"p":"","r":"She asks how a soul that is formless and does nothing, and that on examination neither touches nor joins nature with its forms and modifications, can be the cause of its own seeing and experiencing.","n":"விகிர்தி விகற்பொடு names nature's modifications and their varieties. The objection is the one she pressed against the Buddhist on how formless consciousness can be joined to a body (302); a Jain soul, of the body's size, is in contact with what it knows.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["uyir","pakuthi","yaakkai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 745","lb":"NK745","ev":"","cl":[["a soul with no action and no contact with nature cannot be the cause of seeing and experiencing","jain","asserted",4,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","பராசரன்"],"ch":"nk:33"},"nk:746":{"book":"neelakesi","num":744,"t":["முத்தாத் துமனை முனிந்தோ வதுவன்றிப்","புத்தாத்து மாக்கட் புரிந்தோ விரிந்தெங்குஞ்","சித்தாத் துமனாய்த் திரிவின்றி நின்ற","சுத்தாத் துமைச் சுழற்றுவ தென்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"She asks whether, if the soul stands pure, conscious, pervading everything and unchanging, nature makes it whirl in the round of births out of hatred for the released soul or love of the bound ones.","n":"முத்தாத்துமன் and புத்தாத்துமாக்கள் are the released and the bound souls (புத்த for baddha, 'bound', not the Buddha). The mock alternatives, hatred or desire, are her point: a changeless pure soul gives nature no reason to bind it, so Sankhya bondage has no cause.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["uyir","piravi","veedu","pakuthi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 746","lb":"NK746","ev":"","cl":[["nothing could bind in the round of births a soul that is pure, pervading and changeless","jain","asserted",3,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","பராசரன்"],"ch":"nk:33"},"nk:747":{"book":"neelakesi","num":745,"t":["பரமாத் துமனைப் பளிங்கடை போல","வருமாத்து மாக்களின் மன்னும் விகாரம்","தருமாத் திரையன்றித் தக்கதொன் றாக","வொருமாத் துமனை யுரைத்திடு கென்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"She demands that, instead of saying the supreme soul takes on the souls' modifications only as crystal takes colour from what is beside it, he state properly what the one soul is.","n":"பளிங்கு அடை போல, 'like crystal by what lies near it', is the Sankhya image of a soul that only seems coloured by nature's changes. She treats the image as an evasion. மாத்திரை in line 3 may mean 'a mere measure' (only an appearance) and is uncertain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["uyir","thaththuvam","pakuthi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 747","lb":"NK747","ev":"","cl":[["the soul is only apparently modified, as crystal takes the colour of what is beside it","vedic","refuted",1,"neelakesi"],["the crystal image explains only an appearance and does not say what the one soul is","jain","asserted",4,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","பராசரன்"],"ch":"nk:33"},"nk:748":{"book":"neelakesi","num":746,"t":["செல்லு மெனினுஞ் சிதையுஞ் செல்லானென்று","சொல்லு மெனினுமுன் சொல்லிய தாமெய்தும்","பல்லுநுன் னாவும் பதையா துரையன்றி","யெல்லுந் துணையு மிருவினை யென்றே."],"p":"","r":"She closes with a dilemma: if he says the soul goes from birth to birth, his doctrine breaks; if he says it does not go, the earlier objections return; what accompanies the soul is its twofold deeds, and his speech is mere fluency of teeth and tongue.","n":"Line 3 (பல்லும் நுன் நாவும் பதையாது உரை) mocks talk that runs on without the tongue faltering. எல்லும் துணையும் இருவினை is read as 'the twofold deeds are its light and companion', the Jain answer to what carries a soul between births; the line is compressed and the reading is tentative.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vinai","uyir","piravi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 748","lb":"NK748","ev":"","cl":[["whether the soul is said to move between births or not, the Sankhya account fails","jain","asserted",1,"neelakesi"],["the soul's own twofold deeds accompany it through births","jain","asserted",4,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","பராசரன்"],"ch":"nk:33"},"nk:749":{"book":"neelakesi","num":747,"t":["யானென தென்னுஞ் செருக்கினை யீன்பது","மானெனப் பட்டது மன்னுமோர் சேதனை","தானினை யாக்குத றக்கின்ற சேதனை","மேனினைத் தானுரைத் தானல்ல னென்றாள்"],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi takes the Sankhya's mahat, the principle said to give birth to the pride of 'I' and 'mine', and argues that whatever brings a thought into being must be a sentient thing, which her opponent never considered.","n":"The Sankhya puts mahat on the side of unconscious nature; the objection turns that against him: producing the sense of self is a work of thought, and thought belongs to the sentient. The reading of சேதனை twice (lines 2 and 3) as the sentient is mine and the lines are compressed.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thaththuvam","agandhai","uyir","vaadhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 749","lb":"NK749","ev":"","cl":[["mahat is what gives rise to the sense of 'I' and 'mine'","vedic","refuted",1,"neelakesi"],["only a sentient thing can bring a thought into being","jain","asserted",3,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","சாங்கியன்"],"ch":"nk:34"},"nk:750":{"book":"neelakesi","num":748,"t":["மான்றான் பகுதி வழித்தோ வழித்தன்றித்","தான்றான் பிறிதோர் பொருளோ யிரண்டொடு","மூன்றாவ தொன்றினி யின்றா விருந்தவிச்","சான்றா ரறியவுஞ் சாற்றினி யென்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"She asks whether mahat comes forth from prakriti or is a separate thing in its own right, says there is no third possibility, and bids him answer so that the learned may judge.","n":"A dilemma: each horn is answered in the verses that follow (751 and 761). சான்றார் appeals to the assembly of the wise who judge a debate.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pakuthi","thaththuvam","vaadhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 750","lb":"NK750","ev":"","cl":[["mahat comes forth from prakriti","vedic","refuted",1,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","சாங்கியன்"],"ch":"nk:34"},"nk:751":{"book":"neelakesi","num":749,"t":["முதற்பொரு ளேயாங் குணமது வாகின்","அதற்பிறி தென்னி னதுமனனு முண்டா","லிதற்கினி நீசொலற் பாலதென் னென்றாள்","சுதப்பொருண் மேனன்றுஞ் சொல்லுதல் வல்லாள்."],"p":"","r":"She presses the dilemma: if the quality is the first substance itself, nothing new has come forth, and if it is other than it, a further thing exists; she, skilled in speaking on scripture, asks what he can say to this.","n":"Line 2 (அதற்பிறி தென்னி னதுமனனு முண்டா) is hard to construe; the sense that a distinct product would be a new thing beside prakriti is my reading. சுதப்பொருள் (line 4) praises her command of scripture.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pakuthi","gunam","vaadhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 751","lb":"NK751","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","சாங்கியன்"],"ch":"nk:34"},"nk:752":{"book":"neelakesi","num":750,"t":["அருவாய காரண மாயவ் வியத்த","முருவா மறுதலை யொப்பிக்கு மென்பாய்","மருவாத சொல்லினை மாதிரந் தானே","பருவாய்ப் பதக படைத்திடு மென்னாய்."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi says that if the formless, unmanifest cause can bring forth its opposite, the world with form, he may as well say that empty space by itself creates gross things, and calls his words incoherent.","n":"A reductio: the Sankhya's unmanifest (அவ்வியத்தம்) is formless, and if formless can yield form, any formless thing, space included, could. In 759 she gives the Jain view of space as formless and producing nothing.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pakuthi","aakaasam","thaththuvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 752","lb":"NK752","ev":"","cl":[["the formless unmanifest cause brings forth the world with form","vedic","refuted",1,"neelakesi"],["what is formless cannot produce gross things with form","jain","asserted",4,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","சாங்கியன்"],"ch":"nk:34"},"nk:753":{"book":"neelakesi","num":751,"t":["பகைக்குண மாகிய பகுதி விகுதி","மிகைக்குணந் தோன்றுநின் மேற்கோ ளழித்துத்","தொகைக்கணம் யாவையுஞ் சூனிய மாமா","நகைக்குண மல்லது நம்பலை யென்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"She says prakriti made of mutually hostile qualities, and products in which one quality comes to exceed the others, destroy his own thesis and leave all his classes empty, so his doctrine is only laughable and she will not believe it.","n":"பகைக்குணம் (line 1) names the three qualities as enemies of one another; the point is that opposed qualities cannot form one stable cause, and if a product comes by one exceeding the rest, the balanced cause is already lost.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["mukkunam","pakuthi","vaadhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 753","lb":"NK753","ev":"","cl":[["prakriti is made of three opposed qualities, and its products appear when one exceeds the others","vedic","refuted",1,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","சாங்கியன்"],"ch":"nk:34"},"nk:754":{"book":"neelakesi","num":752,"t":["புத்தேந் திரியமுங் கம்மேந் திரியமும்","பத்தேந் திரியத்தோ டொன்றாய்ப் படைத்தனை","பித்தேந் திரியமும் பேயேந் திரியமும்","குத்தேந் திரியமுங் கொண்டிலை யன்றே."],"p":"","r":"Mocking the count of organs, she says he made five organs of knowing and five of action, ten and one more, yet left out an organ of madness, an organ of possession and an organ of punching.","n":"The joke rests on the Sanskrit names புத்தேந்திரியம் and கம்மேந்திரியம்: if every power of acting counts as an organ, there is no end to the list. The 'one' added to the ten is the mind.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pori","thaththuvam","vaadhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 754","lb":"NK754","ev":"","cl":[["there are five organs of knowing, five of action and the mind, eleven in all","vedic","refuted",2,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","சாங்கியன்"],"ch":"nk:34"},"nk:755":{"book":"neelakesi","num":753,"t":["தந்திர மாவன தாமிடைத் தோன்றுவ","வந்தர வாத்துவ னைந்தென வேண்டினை","சிந்தனை யுள்ளிட்டுச் சீவன் குணமெனி","னிந்திரன் றானு மிணைநுனக் காமோ."],"p":"","r":"She turns to the five subtle elements that he says arise in between, and says that if he counts thought and the rest as qualities of the soul, not even Indra could match him.","n":"The sarcasm of line 4 suggests that counting thought as the soul's quality would give away the Sankhya case, for which thought belongs to nature, and take the Jain side. Lines 1 and 2 (தந்திர மாவன ... வந்தர வாத்துவன்) are obscure and the reading of தந்திரம் as the subtle elements is a guess.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thaththuvam","uyir","arivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 755","lb":"NK755","ev":"","cl":[["five subtle elements arise between the principle of self and the gross elements","vedic","refuted",1,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","சாங்கியன்"],"ch":"nk:34"},"nk:756":{"book":"neelakesi","num":754,"t":["கைகளுங் காலு மிருசா ரிடக்கரு","மெய்திய வாக்குமற்றிந்திய மாமெனிற்","செய்யும் புலனு மறியு மறிவுமற்","றெய்த வுணர்ந்திங் கெடுத்துரை யென்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"She asks: if hands, feet, the two organs of excretion and speech are organs, then let him show, having understood it, what sense object they act on and what knowledge they know.","n":"A sense organ, for her, is defined by an object and a knowing; the five organs of action have neither, so calling them organs is a misuse of the word.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pori","arivu","thaththuvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 756","lb":"NK756","ev":"","cl":[["hands, feet, the two excretory organs and speech are organs","vedic","refuted",2,"neelakesi"],["an organ is that which has an object and a knowledge of it","jain","asserted",3,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","சாங்கியன்"],"ch":"nk:34"},"nk:757":{"book":"neelakesi","num":755,"t":["காட்டிய வாறுங் கருமத்தி னாமெனி","னூட்டு முலையு முதடும் புருவமு","மாட்டுங் கவுளு மறமெல்லும் பற்களும்","கூட்டி மிடறுங் கொளக்குற்ற மென்னோ."],"p":"","r":"If he says they are counted as organs because they act, she asks what fault there would be in adding the suckling breast, the lips, the eyebrows, the moving cheeks, the chewing teeth and the throat.","n":"The reply to a defence the opponent might give (acting, கருமம்): the criterion of action would make every working part of the body an organ.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pori","yaakkai","vaadhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 757","lb":"NK757","ev":"","cl":[["the organs of action are counted as organs because they act","vedic","refuted",1,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","சாங்கியன்"],"ch":"nk:34"},"nk:758":{"book":"neelakesi","num":756,"t":["ஐந்துதன் மாத்திரை தாமணுவாற் றொடர்","கந்தங்க ணாதியிற் காம்புல னேயவை","வந்துபெருகி வரிசையி னான்மிகும்","புந்தியி னால்வகைப் பூதமு மென்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"She sets out the Jain account: the five subtle qualities, smell and the rest, belong to matter bound up in atoms; they are what the senses grasp, and as atoms come together and grow in order they make the four kinds of elements, as reason shows.","n":"Against the Sankhya's five elements unfolding from subtle elements, she counts four elements built of atoms, with smell and the other qualities as their sensed properties; space is kept apart and treated in 759. The construal of lines 1 and 4 is mine.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["anu","pori","gunam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 758","lb":"NK758","ev":"","cl":[["the four elements are built up from atoms, and smell and the other sense qualities belong to them","jain","asserted",1,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","சாங்கியன்"],"ch":"nk:34"},"nk:759":{"book":"neelakesi","num":757,"t":["ஒன்றாய்ப் பரந்திவ் வுலகு மலோகமுஞ்","சென்றாய்க் கிடந்த தசேதனை தானென்று","மன்றா யருவா யதுவவ்வா காயமு","மென்றா ளெழினெடுங் கண்ணிணை நல்லாள்."],"p":"","r":"Space, says the lady of beautiful long eyes, is one, extends through this world and the non-world beyond it, and is non-sentient and without form.","n":"உலகு and அலோகம் (line 1) are the Jain world and non-world: space alone runs on beyond the world into the empty non-world, which is why space cannot be one of the elements that unfold from prakriti.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aakaasam","anu","thaththuvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 759","lb":"NK759","ev":"","cl":[["space is one, formless and non-sentient, and extends through the world and the non-world","jain","asserted",1,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","சாங்கியன்"],"ch":"nk:34"},"nk:760":{"book":"neelakesi","num":758,"t":["தானரு வாய பொருளது வாமொலி","மேன்மரு வாதுரு வாதலி னான்மெய்ம்மை","நூன்மரு வாதுசொன் னாயிது வென்றனள்","மான்மரு வாவந்த நோக்கு மரியாள்."],"p":"","r":"She says sound has form and so cannot rest in space, which is formless; in making sound belong to space he spoke against true teaching, says she of the deer-like gaze.","n":"In the Jain view sound is matter, and so it has form; the Sankhya derives ear and sound from space. The epithet of line 4 is read as மான் மருவா வந்த நோக்கு, a gaze that came as a deer's.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aakaasam","pori","thaththuvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 760","lb":"NK760","ev":"","cl":[["sound belongs to space","vedic","refuted",1,"neelakesi"],["sound has form and so cannot belong to formless space","jain","asserted",2,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","சாங்கியன்"],"ch":"nk:34"},"nk:761":{"book":"neelakesi","num":759,"t":["பகுதியின் மானில்லை யிஃதினு மஃதில்லை","தொகுதிசெய் பல்குணந் தோற்றமு மில்லா","மிகுதிசெய் பூதத்து மெய்ம்மை பெறாமற்","றகுதியின் றத்தநின் றத்துவ மென்ன."],"p":"","r":"She sums up: mahat does not come from prakriti, nor the next principle from mahat; the many qualities that would compose them have no arising, and since the elements gain no reality, his principles are unfit.","n":"This answers the dilemma of 750 by denying both horns: the chain of principles fails at its first link. அத்த (line 4) addresses the opponent as master, likely with irony.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thaththuvam","pakuthi","gunam","vaadhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 761","lb":"NK761","ev":"","cl":[["the principles unfold in a chain from prakriti through mahat to the elements","vedic","refuted",1,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","சாங்கியன்"],"ch":"nk:34"},"nk:762":{"book":"neelakesi","num":760,"t":["குருடனும் பங்குவுங் கூட்டத்திற் கூட்டிப்","பொருடம் தாக்கமும் போத்தந் துரைப்பிற்","றெருட லிலையவர் செய்கையிற் செய்கை","யிருடன்னை யின்றி யிவையெய்து மென்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"She says that if he joins the blind man and the lame man together to explain how soul and nature combine, the comparison does not make it clear, for those two act by their own understanding, while here the work is said to happen with no knower in it.","n":"The blind man carrying the lame is the Sankhya's own image for nature that acts without seeing and the soul that sees without acting. Her objection, as I read lines 3 and 4, is that both men in the image are conscious and agree, which nature cannot do. The lines are terse and the reading is not secure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pakuthi","uyir","arivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 762","lb":"NK762","ev":"","cl":[["soul and nature combine like a lame man riding a blind one, the one seeing and the other acting","vedic","refuted",1,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","சாங்கியன்"],"ch":"nk:34"},"nk:763":{"book":"neelakesi","num":761,"t":["எவ்வகை யின்னும் விகார மிலாப்பொருட்","கிவ்வகை தம்மை யெடுத்துரை யென்செயும்","மெய்வகை யாலொப்பின் மேற்கோண் முதலிய","வவ்வகை யெல்லா மழிவுள தாமே."],"p":"","r":"She asks what good it does to set out these classes of principles for a soul that never changes in any way, and says that if the comparison is pressed truly, his thesis and every part of his proof collapse.","n":"If the soul is wholly unchanging (விகாரம் இலா), knowing the principles cannot change its state, so the teaching of the twenty-five loses its purpose; மேற்கோள் is the thesis of a formal proof.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["uyir","thaththuvam","veedu","vaadhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 763","lb":"NK763","ev":"","cl":[["the soul is without any change","vedic","refuted",1,"neelakesi"],["teaching the principles can do nothing for a soul that cannot change","jain","asserted",2,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","சாங்கியன்"],"ch":"nk:34"},"nk:764":{"book":"neelakesi","num":762,"t":["கூடியு மாகாக் குணத்தின் நீயவட்","பாடி யுரைத்த வுயிரும் பகுதியும்","பேடிகள் சாரினும் பிள்ளை பெறாமையை","நாடியுங் காணென்று நன்னுத னக்காள்."],"p":"","r":"Laughing, the lady of fair brow tells him to look and see: the soul and prakriti he praised, being of a kind that cannot produce even when joined, are like eunuchs who bear no child however they consort.","n":"The image answers the Sankhya union of soul and nature: one does not act and the other does not know, so their joining yields nothing. Line 1 (குணத்தின் நீயவட்) is unclear; அவள் may be prakriti spoken of as female.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["uyir","pakuthi","vaadhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 764","lb":"NK764","ev":"","cl":[["the world comes forth from the union of soul and prakriti","vedic","refuted",2,"neelakesi"],["two things that can neither act and know together cannot produce by joining","jain","asserted",3,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","சாங்கியன்"],"ch":"nk:34"},"nk:765":{"book":"neelakesi","num":763,"t":["இல்லுளி யின்றிமற் றெங்கு மிவைமுன்னும்","புல்லின வேயாற் புணர்ச்சி புதிதெனச்","சொல்லின தென்செயத் தோற்றப் படுபொருள்","பல்லன தாமவை பண்டு முளவே."],"p":"","r":"She argues that if soul and nature were joined everywhere even before, calling their union new means nothing, and the many things to be produced would have existed before as well.","n":"Both soul and nature are said to pervade all, so they were never apart, and a first union that starts the world cannot happen; and if effects already exist in their cause, nothing is newly produced. The opening இல்லுளி is obscure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pakuthi","uyir","thaththuvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 765","lb":"NK765","ev":"","cl":[["the world begins when soul and nature come together","vedic","refuted",2,"neelakesi"],["effects exist in their cause before they appear","vedic","refuted",3,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","சாங்கியன்"],"ch":"nk:34"},"nk:766":{"book":"neelakesi","num":764,"t":["ஆட லழித்தல் படைத்த லடங்குதல்","வீடுபெற் றாங்கண் விளங்க நிலைமையுங்","கூடிய வைந்து குணத்தின னாதலி","னாடிய குற்றங்க ணண்ணல வென்ன."],"p":"","r":"The opponent holds that because his Lord has the five qualities (dancing, destroying, creating, withdrawing, and the state that shines when release is won), none of the faults Neelakesi has examined touch him.","n":"The five named here are the Saiva five acts, with the dance (ஆடல்) first, though the sarukkam is named for the Sankhya; the verse does not say whether Parasaran now speaks for a lord added to the Sankhya scheme or the debate has turned to a theist. The view is answered at once in 767-775, which is why it is marked refuted.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["aindhozhil","iraivan","samayam","vaadhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 766","lb":"NK766","ev":"","cl":[["the Lord who dances, destroys, creates, withdraws and grants release is beyond the faults charged against him","saiva","refuted",3,"parasaran"]],"ag":["பராசரன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["பராசரன்","நீலகேசி"],"ch":"nk:35"},"nk:767":{"book":"neelakesi","num":765,"t":["ஓதிய வெல்லா மொருவனி னங்கொரு","நீதி வகையா னெறிமைப் படுதலும்","வாதுசெய் வார்கள் பிறராய் வருவது","மூதிய மில்லை யொழியென் றுரைத்தாள்."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi answers that if everything spoken proceeds from one being by a single rule, then even the debaters who come forward as others are he, and there is no gain in the dispute; give it up, she says.","n":"The objection turns the debate itself against the doctrine: if the one Lord is all, Parasaran is arguing with himself.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","vaadhu","samayam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 767","lb":"NK767","ev":"","cl":[["everything that is spoken proceeds from one Lord by one rule","saiva","refuted",1,"neelakesi"],["if all is one being, a debate between opponents has no point","jain","asserted",3,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","பராசரன்"],"ch":"nk:35"},"nk:768":{"book":"neelakesi","num":766,"t":["யானை குதிரை முதலாப் படைகுடி","யேனைய தாங்களு மெல்லா மவனெனிற்","றானென்று மாள்வது தன்னை யெனினங்கள்","கோனிவ னாமெனக் கூறினார் யாரோ."],"p":"","r":"She asks: if elephants, horses, armies, subjects and everything else are he, then he forever rules only himself, so who could ever say 'this one is our king'?","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","arasu","vaadhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 768","lb":"NK768","ev":"","cl":[["if all beings are the one Lord, the difference of ruler and ruled vanishes","jain","asserted",2,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","பராசரன்"],"ch":"nk:35"},"nk:769":{"book":"neelakesi","num":767,"t":["என்னை யொழித்தினி யெல்லா மவனெனச்","சொன்ன முறைமைய னாகு மவனெனிற்","றன்னை யொழித்துத் தபுத்துடன் றின்றிடிற்","பின்னை யவனையோர் பித்தனென் னாமோ"],"p":"","r":"She argues that if all but herself is he, and he destroys, fights and consumes himself, people would have to call him a madman.","n":"பித்தன் (line 4), 'madman', is also a common name of Siva; the verse may be turning the epithet against its bearer, though it does not say so.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","pedhaimai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 769","lb":"NK769","ev":"","cl":[["a Lord who is everything and destroys his creatures destroys himself, which is madness","jain","asserted",3,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","பராசரன்"],"ch":"nk:35"},"nk:770":{"book":"neelakesi","num":768,"t":["தன்கையிற் றன்கண்ணைத் தானே பொதக்குத்தி","யென்செயக் குத்தினை யென்பார் பிறரில்லை","தன்கையிற் றன்கண்ணைத் தான்பொதக் குத்துவ","தென்செய வோவிதன் காரணஞ் சொல்லாய்."],"p":"","r":"She compares such a god to a man who stabs his own eye with his own hand, where there is no one else even to ask why, and demands the reason.","n":"The repeated line (1 and 3) makes the image the argument: harm done to creatures by a god who is the creatures has no motive at all.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","kollaamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 770","lb":"NK770","ev":"","cl":[["harm a god who is all things does to beings is harm without reason done to himself","jain","asserted",3,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","பராசரன்"],"ch":"nk:35"},"nk:771":{"book":"neelakesi","num":769,"t":["தன்னைப் படைப்பின்முன் றானின்மை யாலில்லை","பின்னைப் படைக்கிற் படைக்கப் படுவதின்","முன்னைப் படைப்பென் முடிவில்லை மூடனே","நுன்னைப் படைத்தவர் யாரினி நோக்காய்."],"p":"","r":"She argues that the creator could not have made himself, since before the making he was not, and if another made him there is no end of makers; 'who made you, fool?' she asks.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","pedhaimai","vaadhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 771","lb":"NK771","ev":"","cl":[["a creator can neither create himself nor be created without an endless regress of creators","jain","asserted",3,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","பராசரன்"],"ch":"nk:35"},"nk:772":{"book":"neelakesi","num":770,"t":["கொன்றுகொன் றிட்டுத் தவஞ்செய்யி னத்தவம்","பொன்றுமற்றாதலி னஃதும் பொருத்தமின்","றின்றிதின் றிட்டுப் படைப்பிற் றெருட்சிமற்","றென்று மிலன்பெரி தேழையு மாமே"],"p":"","r":"She says that penance done after killing upon killing perishes, so that too is unfitting, and a god who makes things after destroying them has no understanding at all and is utterly foolish.","n":"The verse assumes the Jain rule that killing undoes penance and applies it to the god himself; line 3 (இன்றிதின் றிட்டுப் படைப்பின்) is read as destroying before creating.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kollaamai","thavam","iraivan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 772","lb":"NK772","ev":"","cl":[["penance done by one who kills is destroyed by the killing","jain","asserted",1,"neelakesi"],["a god who destroys and then creates shows no wisdom","jain","asserted",3,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","பராசரன்"],"ch":"nk:35"},"nk:773":{"book":"neelakesi","num":771,"t":["ஓர்ப்பவன் சொல்லவ னூனவன் றீனவன்","றீர்ப்பவ னோயவன் சீறு மவனுயிர்","நீப்பவன் சாபவ னீப்பவ னேற்பவன்","பேர்ப்பவ னாயும் பெறுகின்ற தென்னோ."],"p":"","r":"She lists what the one god must be: thinker and speaker, flesh and food, healer and disease, the angry one, the killer and the one who dies, giver and receiver, the one who removes; and asks what he gains by any of it.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","uyir"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 773","lb":"NK773","ev":"","cl":[["a god who is both doer and sufferer of every act gains nothing by acting","jain","asserted",4,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","பராசரன்"],"ch":"nk:35"},"nk:774":{"book":"neelakesi","num":772,"t":["நாயாய்க் கடிக்கு நரியாய்ப் பலகொல்லும்","பேயாய்ப் புடைத்துண்ணும் பெற்றமு மாய்க்குத்து","மீயாய் நலியு மெறும்பாய்த் தெறுமெங்கும்","தீயா னொருவனின் றேவனு மென்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi concludes that the god who bites as the dog, kills as the jackal, beats and eats as the demon, gores as the bull, torments as the fly and stings as the ant is everywhere one evil god.","n":"The catalogue of small cruelties is the Jain's moral test of the doctrine that God is all: whatever harms a living thing would be God harming.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","kollaamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 774","lb":"NK774","ev":"","cl":[["if the one god is every creature, he is the doer of all their harms and so an evil god","jain","asserted",4,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","பராசரன்"],"ch":"nk:35"},"nk:775":{"book":"neelakesi","num":773,"t":["வீடு தலைபெற வெந்துநெஞ் சாண்டிடத்","தாடி தவஞ்செய்த தன்கா லழித்திடப்","பாடிய கையிற் படைக்கு மிவனெனின்","மூடர்கட் டேற்ற முடிவுமுண் டாமோ."],"p":"","r":"She asks whether, if this god who burns, dances and destroys with his foot is also the one who creates with his hand, the confusion of fools could ever end.","n":"Lines 1-2 seem to recall deeds told of Siva (a burning, the dance, a foot that destroys, one who did penance), but the words are compressed and the episodes cannot be fixed from them.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","pedhaimai","veedu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 775","lb":"NK775","ev":"","cl":[["a god who both destroys and creates gives no ground for clear understanding","jain","asserted",4,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","பராசரன்"],"ch":"nk:35"},"nk:776":{"book":"neelakesi","num":774,"t":["நித்திய மாய பொருணின வாதலின்","வித்தினு ளுண்டென வேண்டுதி நீள்பனை","யெத்துணை யோவது வென்னினு நுன்கைக்கு","மித்துணை யுண்டென்ப தென்னைகொ லேழாய்."],"p":"","r":"Turning to his doctrine that things are eternal, she says he must hold that the tall palm is already in its seed; then however large it is, why not say that much is also in the hand that holds the seed, fool?","n":"The verse attacks the Sankhya teaching that the effect already exists in its cause; the reductio is the Jain's, and Parasaran's own wording of the doctrine is not given.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thaththuvam","parinaamam","pedhaimai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 776","lb":"NK776","ev":"","cl":[["since things are eternal, the effect already exists in its cause, as the palm in its seed","vedic","refuted",2,"neelakesi"],["if the tree were already in the seed, the hand holding the seed would hold the tree","jain","asserted",3,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","பராசரன்"],"ch":"nk:35"},"nk:777":{"book":"neelakesi","num":775,"t":["இப்பொழு தில்லை யெதிரதற் குண்டெனி","னப்பொழு துண்மையு மின்மையு மாக்கி","னெப்பொரு டாங்களு மின்னன வேயெனச்","செப்பினள் தத்துவஞ் சேயரிக் கண்ணாள்."],"p":"","r":"When he says the tree is not there now but will be later, she answers that he then makes it non-existent at one time and existent at another, and so all things are both; thus the red-streaked-eyed one set out the truth.","n":"The opponent's retreat ('not now, but later') is taken as granting the Jain position that a thing is existent and non-existent in different respects; the verse ends the satkarya argument by absorbing it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thaththuvam","parinaamam","vaadhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 777","lb":"NK777","ev":"","cl":[["the effect is not now but exists later","vedic","refuted",1,"neelakesi"],["every thing is both existent and non-existent according to its time","jain","asserted",3,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","பராசரன்"],"ch":"nk:35"},"nk:778":{"book":"neelakesi","num":776,"t":["உருவோ டருவமா காயமு மூன்று","மிருபதின் மேலுமைந் தாக விசைத்தனை","யருவோ டலோக மசேதன மூன்றிற்","செருவோ டுரிமையிற் சேர்பவு மன்றே"],"p":"","r":"She says he has spread three things (the formed, the formless and space) into twenty-five, while the formless, the non-world and the insentient are three that belong together of their own right.","n":"அலோகம் (line 3), the space beyond the world, is a Jain category, so she sets her own scheme against his count; line 4 (செருவோ டுரிமையிற் சேர்பவு மன்றே) is hard to construe.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thaththuvam","aththikaayam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 778","lb":"NK778","ev":"","cl":[["the principles of reality are twenty-five","vedic","refuted",2,"neelakesi"],["the formless, the non-world and the insentient are categories of their own","jain","asserted",3,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","பராசரன்"],"ch":"nk:35"},"nk:779":{"book":"neelakesi","num":777,"t":["ஒன்றொன்றி னொன்றி யுலகுள் வழியெங்குஞ்","சென்றவ னுண்மை பகுதி யிதுமன்னு","மென்றனை யெண்முறை யறிமற் றீண்டுபு","நன்றன வீறா நிலமுத னான்கே."],"p":"","r":"She recalls that he said the person who pervades the whole world is real and that primal nature endures, and tells him to learn by the right count that here earth and the other three come first.","n":"Lines 3-4 are obscure; the sense that the four elements are taken as basic is a guess from நிலமுத னான்கே.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thaththuvam","uyir"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 779","lb":"NK779","ev":"","cl":[["the person pervades the whole world, and primal nature lasts","vedic","refuted",2,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","பராசரன்"],"ch":"nk:35"},"nk:780":{"book":"neelakesi","num":778,"t":["பத்தனை யாய்நின் பரமாத் துமனையுஞ்","சித்தனை யாகக் கருதியல் சீவன்கட்","கத்தன்மை ஞானமு மென்னா யவனுக்குத்","துத்தலுங் காண்டலுஞ் சொல்லினை யன்றோ."],"p":"","r":"She tells him that he, a bound soul, should not take his supreme self alone to be perfected while denying that state and knowledge to souls, since he himself said that self enjoys and sees.","n":"Sankhya's person is inactive yet a seer and enjoyer; Neelakesi takes the two verbs (துய்த்தல், காண்டல்) as admitting what the Jain soul has, knowledge and experience, and so as giving every soul the perfected state.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["uyir","arivu","veedu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 780","lb":"NK780","ev":"","cl":[["the supreme self is the one that enjoys and sees","vedic","refuted",4,"neelakesi"],["perfection and knowledge belong to every soul, not to one supreme self","jain","asserted",2,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","பராசரன்"],"ch":"nk:35"},"nk:781":{"book":"neelakesi","num":779,"t":["வண்டார் குழற்பெயர் மாணிழை யிற்றெனக்","கொண்டே னெனவவன் கூறினன் கூறலும்","பண்டே லறியெனப்பராசர நீயினிக்","கண்டா யெனச் சொல்லிக் காட்சி கொடுத்தாள்."],"p":"","r":"Parasaran says he has taken this from the jewelled one whose name is her bee-filled hair, and when he says it she tells him he did not know before and now sees, and gives him right vision.","n":"குழற்பெயர் (line 1), 'named for her hair', plays on Neelakesi's name (the dark-haired one). காட்சி is the Jain jewel of right vision, which she gives the defeated as in the earlier sarukkams.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["meyyunarvu","vaadhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 781","lb":"NK781","ev":"Parasaran concedes that he has taken Neelakesi's teaching, and she gives him right vision.","cl":[],"ag":["பராசரன்","நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"ch":"nk:35"},"nk:782":{"book":"neelakesi","num":780,"t":["ஐந்து மிருபது மாகிய சொற்பொரு","டந்திவை யல்லது தத்துவ மில்லென்ற","சிந்தை யொழித்துச் சினவரன் சேவடி","வந்தனை யேசெய்து வாழிநீ யென்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"She tells him to give up the thought that there is no principle beyond the twenty-five, to worship the red feet of the Jina, and to live well.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thaththuvam","vazhipaadu","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 782","lb":"NK782","ev":"Neelakesi bids Parasaran leave the twenty-five principles and worship the Jina's feet.","cl":[["there is no principle beyond the twenty-five","vedic","refuted",2,"neelakesi"],["one should worship the feet of the Jina","jain","asserted",3,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","பராசரன்"],"ch":"nk:35"},"nk:783":{"book":"neelakesi","num":781,"t":["ஆட்டினா ளவனையு மாக்கிச் செல்பவள்","வீட்டினார் நெறியென விரித்த மேலையோர்","காட்டினார் பலருளுங் கணாத னேயெனு","மீட்டினா னுலோகன திடத்தை யெய்தினாள்."],"p":"","r":"Having won over the previous opponent as well, Neelakesi goes on and, among the many ancients who taught their ways as the path to release, reaches the place of Ulokan, who had stored up the teaching that Kanada is the master.","n":"The sarukkam opens by naming Kanada (கணாதன்) as the founder whose teaching the opponent holds; the opponent himself is called உலோகன், a name that recalls the school's other title for its founder, though the verse uses it of the debater. The word division of line 4 (ஈட்டினான் உலோகனது இடத்தை) is the reading assumed here.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vaadhu","samayam","veedu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 783","lb":"NK783","ev":"Neelakesi, having converted her last opponent, travels on to the dwelling of Ulokan, the follower of Kanada.","cl":[],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"ch":"nk:36"},"nk:784":{"book":"neelakesi","num":782,"t":["வனப்புடை மாதரைக் கண்டு மாதவன்","சினப்புடைக் கருத்தின ளென்னச் சிந்தித்தே","நினக்கினி நெறிவயி னின்ற மெய்ம்மையை","மனக்கொளக் கிளக்குவேன் மன்னுங் கேளென."],"p":"","r":"The great ascetic sees the beautiful woman, takes her for one come in a contentious mood, and tells her to listen while he sets out, for her to take to heart, the truth that stands on the right path.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vaadhu","meyyunarvu","thavam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 784","lb":"NK784","ev":"Ulokan sees Neelakesi, judges her ready to argue, and offers to expound his doctrine.","cl":[],"ag":["உலோகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"nk:36"},"nk:785":{"book":"neelakesi","num":783,"t":["நெறியெனப் படுவது நின்ற மெய்ம்மையங்","கறிதலுக் கரியன வாறு சொற்பொருள்","செறியயான் சொலிற் றிரப்பியங் குணந்தொழில்","பொறியினாய் பொதுசிறப் புடன் புணர்ப்பதே."],"p":"","r":"The Vaisheshika says the path is the abiding truth, and that the categories, hard to know, are six: substance, quality, action, the general, the particular, and the joining of them.","n":"The six are given in Tamil and Sanskrit mixed: திரப்பியம் for substance, குணம், தொழில், then பொது and சிறப்பு, and inherence only as the act of joining (புணர்ப்பது). Neelakesi answers the substances in 791 to 797 and the separation of quality from substance and the joining in 798 to 802.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thaththuvam","gunam","meyyunarvu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 785","lb":"NK785","ev":"","cl":[["reality is set out in six categories: substance, quality, action, generality, particularity and inherence","vedic","refuted",3,"ulokan"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["உலோகன்","நீலகேசி"],"ch":"nk:36"},"nk:786":{"book":"neelakesi","num":784,"t":["பூதமைந் தொடுதிசை மனம்பொ ழுதுயி","ரோதினப் பொருள்கடா மொன்ப தாமவை","நீதியிற் குணமவற் றியல்பு செய்கையும்","போதரும் பொருட்புடை பெயர்ச்சி யாகுமே."],"p":"","r":"He names the substances as nine, the five elements with direction, mind, time and soul; qualities are their nature, and action is the moving of the substances from place to place.","n":"Action is defined as புடைபெயர்ச்சி, shifting position, so action is motion only. Each of the nine is attacked in turn from 791: the elements, ether (793), direction (794 to 796), mind and time (797).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thaththuvam","thisai","kaalam","uyir"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 786","lb":"NK786","ev":"","cl":[["there are nine substances: five elements, direction, mind, time and soul","vedic","refuted",1,"ulokan"],["action is the movement of a substance","vedic","reported",4,"ulokan"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["உலோகன்","நீலகேசி"],"ch":"nk:36"},"nk:787":{"book":"neelakesi","num":785,"t":["பெரியதும் பின்னது மாய தப்பொது","உரிதினிற் பொருள்களைச் செலுத்து மொற்றுமை","தெரிவுற வருவது சிறப்ப தாங்குணக்","கிரியைக ளிதற்கெனக் கிளத்தல் கூட்டமே."],"p":"","r":"He defines the general as the higher and lower sameness that gathers things into their kinds, the particular as what makes a thing known as distinct, and the joining as what lets one say that these qualities and actions belong to this thing.","n":"The joining is defined by what it licenses one to say (கிளத்தல், line 4), and that is where Neelakesi presses in 800 to 802: if a quality is other than its bearer, nothing makes it 'its'.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thaththuvam","gunam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 787","lb":"NK787","ev":"","cl":[["generality, higher and lower, gathers things into classes and particularity marks each as distinct","vedic","reported",1,"ulokan"],["inherence is what makes qualities and actions belong to their substance","vedic","refuted",4,"ulokan"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["உலோகன்","நீலகேசி"],"ch":"nk:36"},"nk:788":{"book":"neelakesi","num":786,"t":["ஆறின் முதன்மூன் றத்தி மற்றவற்","றீறின் மும்மையு மின்மையை யெய்தின","கூறின பொருள்களுங் குணனுஞ் செய்கையும்","வேறென விரித்தனன் விசேட வாதியே."],"p":"","r":"The narrator sums up: the Vaisheshika taught that of the six the first three have being and the last three do not, and that substances, qualities and actions are each separate from one another.","n":"The verse says the last three reached இன்மை, non-being; this is the Jain poem's wording, and the school would more likely say that generality, particularity and inherence lack the universal of being rather than that they do not exist. The separateness of substance, quality and action is the claim the rest of the chapter attacks (798 to 802).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thaththuvam","gunam","samayam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 788","lb":"NK788","ev":"","cl":[["of the six categories the first three possess being and the last three do not","vedic","reported",1,"ulokan"],["substance, quality and action are separate from each other","vedic","refuted",4,"ulokan"]],"ag":["உலோகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"nk:36"},"nk:789":{"book":"neelakesi","num":787,"t":["தத்துவ மிவையெனத் தலையுந் தூக்கினாட்","கொத்ததன் றோவென வுரைநல் லாயினி","யித்தவ மிப்பொரு டேறி யான்செயிற்","பொய்த்தவ மாதலிற் போவ னென்னவே."],"p":"","r":"When he lifts his head and asks whether these principles are not right, Neelakesi answers that to practise penance on such principles would be false penance, and she will go.","n":"The verse runs his question and her answer together; the division assumed here gives lines 1 and 2 (to ஒத்ததன்றோ, with உரை நல்லாய் as his address to her) to him and lines 3 and 4 to her.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thavam","samayam","vaadhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 789","lb":"NK789","ev":"Ulokan asks proudly whether his principles are not sound; Neelakesi says penance on them is false and makes to leave.","cl":[["penance founded on the Vaisheshika principles is false penance","jain","asserted",4,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["உலோகன்","நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"ch":"nk:36"},"nk:790":{"book":"neelakesi","num":788,"t":["மெய்ந்நெறி யிதுவென விரிப்பக் கேட்டிருந்","திந்நெறி யமைதியின் றென்னுஞ் சொல்லினாய்","பொய்ந்நெறி யாதலைத் தேற்றிப் போகெனக்","கைந்நிறுத் தாளது காட்டக் கேளென."],"p":"","r":"He tells her that since she heard him expound the true path and calls it unsound, she must prove it a false path before she leaves; she stops him with a raised hand and bids him listen while she shows it.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vaadhu","meyyunarvu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 790","lb":"NK790","ev":"Ulokan challenges Neelakesi to prove his doctrine false before leaving, and she halts him and begins her refutation.","cl":[],"ag":["உலோகன்","நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"ch":"nk:36"},"nk:791":{"book":"neelakesi","num":789,"t":["நிலமுதற் பூதமாய் நின்ற நான்மையுங்","குலமுதன் மூர்த்தியாய்க் கூறி னொன்றவை","யலமுதற் பொருள்களாய்ப் பன்னி னாயவை","வலமுறை யிடமுறை வருதல் காட்டுகோ."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi says the four elements from earth onward are one if their origin and form are considered, though he recites them as separate primary substances, and offers to show how they pass into one another in either order.","n":"Her point is that the four are not four kinds of stuff but one stuff in four states, which turn into each other forwards and backwards (வலமுறை இடமுறை); the examples follow in 792.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thaththuvam","parinaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 791","lb":"NK791","ev":"","cl":[["earth, water, fire and air are four distinct substances","vedic","refuted",3,"ulokan"],["the four elements are of one kind and change into one another","jain","asserted",2,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","உலோகன்"],"ch":"nk:36"},"nk:792":{"book":"neelakesi","num":790,"t":["உரியதோர் நீரணைந் துப்ப தாகுநெய்","யெரியெழு முளர்ச்சியா லிரைக்குங் காற்றதாம்","பெரியதோ ருருமுநீர்ப் பிண்டம் வந்துநீர்","வரிசையிற் படலமாய் வந்த வல்லவோ."],"p":"","r":"She gives examples of the elements turning into one another: water joined to the right thing becomes salt, ghee blazes up as fire, wind roars from agitation, and a great hailstone is a lump of water that came from water layer by layer.","n":"Each example is a change across the Vaisheshika's boundaries between elements: water to earthy salt, a liquid to fire, water to a solid. Line 3 was repaired in the text (see source_issues) and the details of lines 2 and 3 are uncertain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["parinaamam","thaththuvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 792","lb":"NK792","ev":"","cl":[["one element is seen to turn into another, as water into salt and into hail","jain","asserted",1,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","உலோகன்"],"ch":"nk:36"},"nk:793":{"book":"neelakesi","num":791,"t":["ஒலியதன் குணமென வுரைத்தி யாதலால்","வலியுடை நிலையில மற்றெப் பூதமும்","ஒலியொடு முதறம்முட் புல்ல லின்மையாற்","கலிசெய லொழிகநின் காய மென்றனள்."],"p":"","r":"She says that since he makes sound the quality of ether, and no element stands firm on its own or is joined with sound among the primary ones, he should give up the clamour of his ether.","n":"What she dismisses is 'your' ether (நின் காயம்), the one defined by sound; in 794 she herself folds direction into space (காயம்), so the attack is on the Vaisheshika's proof of ether, not on space as such. Line 3 is obscure and the middle of the argument is uncertain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["oli","aakaasam","gunam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 793","lb":"NK793","ev":"","cl":[["sound is the quality of ether","vedic","refuted",1,"ulokan"],["sound gives no ground for positing the Vaisheshika's ether","jain","asserted",4,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","உலோகன்"],"ch":"nk:36"},"nk:794":{"book":"neelakesi","num":792,"t":["சுட்டின திசைத்திறஞ் சொல்லிற் சூரியன்","பட்டது மெழுந்ததும் பற்றி நான்குமாய்","யெட்டெனப் பலவென வின்ன தன்மையாற்","கட்டினர் வழங்கினுங் காய மெய்துமே."],"p":"","r":"She says direction is only pointing: from where the sun set and rose people make four, eight or many quarters and use them by convention, and even so it comes down to space.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thisai","aakaasam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 794","lb":"NK794","ev":"","cl":[["direction is space named by convention from the sun's rising and setting, not a substance of its own","jain","asserted",1,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","உலோகன்"],"ch":"nk:36"},"nk:795":{"book":"neelakesi","num":793,"t":["மண்டிலம் பலரையு நிறுவி மத்திமங்","கொண்டுநின் றான்றிசை கூறு மின்னென","வெண்டிசை யவன்வயிற் பிறக்கு மென்பவேற்","கண்டில நின்பொருள் காட்ட வல்லையோ."],"p":"","r":"She proposes setting many people in a circle with one in the middle and asking him to name the directions: if the eight directions are born from where he stands, she sees no thing of his called direction, and asks him to show it.","n":"The thought experiment makes direction relative to an observer: the same spot is a different quarter for each person in the ring.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thisai","aakaasam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 795","lb":"NK795","ev":"","cl":[["the directions arise relative to an observer, so no substance called direction is ever seen","jain","asserted",3,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","உலோகன்"],"ch":"nk:36"},"nk:796":{"book":"neelakesi","num":794,"t":["அத்திசை யவனுமா யல்ல னும்மென","வெத்திசை யவர்களு மிசைப்பி னேகநீ","பத்திசெய் தினியென்னைப் பரமன் பாதமே","சித்தியு முடிவெனச் சேர்ந்து வாழ்தியே."],"p":"","r":"She says that if a quarter is that direction for one person and not for another, and people at every point can say the same, he should go, now devoted, and live holding the feet of the Supreme as attainment and the end.","n":"The verse closes the case on direction with an appeal to conversion; the Supreme (பரமன்) is in the Jain poem the Arhat. The syntax of lines 2 and 3 (ஏக நீ, இனி என்னை) is uncertain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thisai","iraivan","veedu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 796","lb":"NK796","ev":"","cl":[["a direction is one quarter for one person and another for the next, so it has no nature of its own","jain","asserted",1,"neelakesi"],["the feet of the Supreme are attainment and the final end","jain","asserted",3,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","உலோகன்"],"ch":"nk:36"},"nk:797":{"book":"neelakesi","num":795,"t":["மன்னுமம் மனமெனப் படுவ தாவதே","யின்னுயி ருருவினோ டியைந்த வொற்றுமை","யின்னுமக் காலமு மிருமைத் தாகலி","னின்னுடைப் பொழுதவ ணிற்ற லில்லையே."],"p":"","r":"She says what is called mind is the union of the soul with bodily form, and that time is of two kinds, so his time does not stand as a substance there.","n":"She does not deny mind or time outright: mind is placed in the soul's union with the body, and time is split in two (இருமைத்து), against the Vaisheshika's single substance. What the two kinds are the verse does not say.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["manam","kaalam","uyir","yaakkai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 797","lb":"NK797","ev":"","cl":[["mind is the soul joined with bodily form, not a separate substance","jain","asserted",2,"neelakesi"],["time is twofold, so it is not a single substance","jain","asserted",3,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","உலோகன்"],"ch":"nk:36"},"nk:798":{"book":"neelakesi","num":796,"t":["குணங்களுந் தொழில்களுங் கூறி வேறெனிற்","பிணந்தனைச் சீவனாய்ப் பெற்ற தென்னையோ","வுணர்ந்தில னுரையுமொன் றிலனெனி னுறுதிநாம்","புணர்ந்தில மவன்வயிற் போந்த தில்லையே."],"p":"","r":"She asks that if qualities and actions are other than their substance, how a corpse ever came to be a living soul; if the soul in itself neither knows nor speaks, nothing ever reached it and we were never joined to it.","n":"The target is the separation of knowing from the knower: if awareness is only a quality added to the soul, the soul by itself is as inert as a corpse.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["gunam","uyir","arivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 798","lb":"NK798","ev":"","cl":[["qualities and actions are other than the substance that has them","vedic","refuted",1,"ulokan"],["a soul without knowing of its own would be no different from a corpse","jain","asserted",2,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","உலோகன்"],"ch":"nk:36"},"nk:799":{"book":"neelakesi","num":797,"t":["எப்பொரு ளெக்குணத் தானு மில்லையே","லப்பொரு ளக்குணத் தயல தாதலாற்","செப்பிலக் குளிரினாற் றீய துண்மைபோற்","றுப்பெனப் பொருள்களுந் தோற்ற மில்லையே."],"p":"","r":"She argues that if a thing has no quality of its own, it is other than any quality, and as surely as fire cannot be shown by its coldness, such things would never appear at all.","n":"The simile of line 3 (fire and cold in a vessel) is compressed and its exact turn uncertain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["gunam","thaththuvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 799","lb":"NK799","ev":"","cl":[["a thing taken apart from its qualities could never appear","jain","asserted",4,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","உலோகன்"],"ch":"nk:36"},"nk:800":{"book":"neelakesi","num":798,"t":["குணத்தொடு குணிகளைக் கூறி வேறெனப்","புணர்ப்பதோர் பொருளினை வேண்டிற் பொய்யெனிற்","குணத்தொடு குணிகளுங் கூட்ட மின்மையா","லுணர்த்துதற் கரிதவை யுளவு மல்லவே."],"p":"","r":"She says that if, having called quality and bearer separate, he needs a further thing to join them, that is false: since quality and bearer have no joining apart from each other, neither can be made known and neither exists.","n":"This is the answer to the joining of 787: a link between two things that are never apart is either needless or, if they are truly apart, unable to make either known.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["gunam","thaththuvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 800","lb":"NK800","ev":"","cl":[["a separate inherence is needed to join qualities to their bearers","vedic","refuted",2,"ulokan"],["quality and bearer need no third thing to join them, and taken apart neither exists","jain","asserted",3,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","உலோகன்"],"ch":"nk:36"},"nk:801":{"book":"neelakesi","num":799,"t":["ஒன்பதுந் தத்தும துண்மை யாற்பல","வென்பது மெனைத்தென வெண்ணப் பட்டதும்","வன்பிதன் குணமிது வென்னப் பெற்றது","மன்பதற் குடைமையி னறியப் பட்டதே."],"p":"","r":"She says the nine substances are many by their own being, and that their number, their hardness and each quality are known only because they belong to the thing.","n":"The verse could also be read as the Vaisheshika's reply that qualities are known through their relation of belonging; the reading here takes it as Neelakesi's, leading into 802.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["gunam","arivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 801","lb":"NK801","ev":"","cl":[["a thing's number and qualities are known only as belonging to it","jain","asserted",4,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","உலோகன்"],"ch":"nk:36"},"nk:802":{"book":"neelakesi","num":800,"t":["அதனது குணமதற் கயல தாதலா","லிதனது குணமென விழுக்கிற் றென்னையோ","உதனமு முணர்விலை யொன்ற தென்றக்கால்","விதனமும் படாயது மெய்யு மாகுமே."],"p":"","r":"She asks how, if a thing's quality is other than it, he can slip into calling it this thing's quality; and if the soul is one thing without knowing, it could not even suffer, and that would have to be true.","n":"Line 3's உதனமு is obscure; the reading of lines 3 and 4 as an absurd consequence (no knowing, so no suffering) is a guess.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["gunam","arivu","uyir"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 802","lb":"NK802","ev":"","cl":[["a quality other than its thing cannot be called that thing's quality","jain","asserted",1,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","உலோகன்"],"ch":"nk:36"},"nk:803":{"book":"neelakesi","num":801,"t":["கெடக்கெடும் பொருளெனிற் கேடு முண்டெனப்","படப்பெறு மதற்குநி பரிவ தென்னையோ","வடக்குந்தன் றோற்றமு மொட்டி மும்மைய","மடக்கிலு மதுபெரி தழகி தாகுமே."],"p":"","r":"She says that if he grants that a thing perishes when its state perishes, then destruction is admitted, and there is nothing to regret: if he also admits its arising and takes in the three together, that would be very fine.","n":"The three (மும்மை) are arising, perishing and lasting, the Jain triad she set out against Mokkalan (387). Here she offers it as the settlement for a school that held the substances eternal.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["parinaamam","thaththuvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 803","lb":"NK803","ev":"","cl":[["every real thing has arising, perishing and lasting together","jain","asserted",3,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","உலோகன்"],"ch":"nk:36"},"nk:804":{"book":"neelakesi","num":802,"t":["குணங்கடாங் குணியெனுங் கூற்று முண்மையிற்","பிணங்கலாய்ப் பொருள்வயிற் பேறு முள்ளதே","யிணங்கலா யிருமைய தின்மை யுண்மையும்","வணங்கலாம் வகையதோர் மாட்சி மிக்கதே."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi states her own position: the saying that the qualities are the qualified thing also holds, without contradiction the qualities are found in the substance, and the being and not-being of their twoness (one and different) together make a doctrine worth revering.","n":"Line 3's இருமைய தின்மை உண்மையும் is the Jain both-ways answer: the quality is one with and other than its bearer. Against the Buddhist in 393 she defended the bearer; here, against a school that makes quality a separate category, she defends the oneness.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["gunam","samayam","meyyunarvu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 804","lb":"NK804","ev":"","cl":[["qualities are both one with and different from the substance that bears them","jain","asserted",3,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","உலோகன்"],"ch":"nk:37"},"nk:805":{"book":"neelakesi","num":803,"t":["பண்பினாற் பொருள்களுக் காய பல்பயஞ்","செண்பினா னறிவினான் செறிவி னானென","மண்பொனாற் குணநிலைக் காய மாட்சியா","நண்பினா னல்லது நடத்த லில்லையே."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi says the many uses things have come through their qualities, by knowing and by closeness, as clay and gold serve through the qualities that stand in them; nothing works except through that intimate union.","n":"Line 2's செண்பினான் is obscure; the clay and gold of line 3 are the stock examples of a substance that keeps its kind through changing forms.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["gunam","payan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 805","lb":"NK805","ev":"","cl":[["a thing serves any use only through the qualities joined in it","jain","asserted",4,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","உலோகன்"],"ch":"nk:37"},"nk:806":{"book":"neelakesi","num":804,"t":["விலைபெறு நன்மையால் வெறுப்ப தீமையால்","கொலைபெறுங் களவினாற் குணத்தி னக்குண","நிலைபெறும் பொருளினா னின்ற வொற்றுமை","யலைபெறும் வேறெனி னாவ தில்லையே."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi argues that the goodness that fetches a price, the evil that is loathed, and the guilt of killing and theft stay as qualities in the person who bears them, a settled oneness; call them separate and nothing would follow.","n":"The metaphysical point is pressed through ethics: if virtue and the guilt of killing and stealing were not the doer's own qualities, praise and blame could not reach the doer.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["gunam","kolai","kalavu","aram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 806","lb":"NK806","ev":"","cl":[["virtue and the guilt of killing and theft belong to the doer as his own qualities","jain","asserted",3,"neelakesi"],["qualities are separate from the substance that bears them","vedic","refuted",4,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","உலோகன்"],"ch":"nk:37"},"nk:807":{"book":"neelakesi","num":805,"t":["நல்வினைப் படுதலுந் தீவினைப் படுதலும்","பல்வினைப் பாகினாற் பயங்க ளெய்தலு","மில்வினைக் குணங்கடா மென்றும் வேறெனிற்","சொல்லில சுழற்சியும் வீடுந் தோற்றமே."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi says that falling into good deeds and bad, and reaping fruits as many deeds ripen, belong to the soul as its qualities; if those qualities were ever separate from it, the round of births and release would be empty words.","n":"The stake of the whole quality debate is named here: if karma's fruits do not inhere in the soul, nothing is bound and nothing is freed. Both good and bad deeds (நல்வினை, தீவினை) are counted as binding.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","piravi","veedu","uyir"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 807","lb":"NK807","ev":"","cl":[["good and bad deeds and their ripened fruits are qualities of the soul itself","jain","asserted",3,"neelakesi"],["if deeds were separate from the soul, rebirth and release would be meaningless","jain","asserted",4,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","உலோகன்"],"ch":"nk:37"},"nk:808":{"book":"neelakesi","num":806,"t":["ஆட்டுடை யாடன வாட றாங்களு","மோட்டுடைக் குதிரையு மோட்டு மென்றின்ன","கூட்டிய வப்பொருட் கொடைய வாதலான்","மீட்டவை யொன்றென வேண்டல் வேண்டுமே."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi argues that the dancer's dancing and the running horse's running belong to the one combined thing, so action too must be granted to be one with its bearer.","n":"This answers the Vaisheshika category of action (கருமம், தொழில்) as she answered quality: the dance is not a thing that could be found apart from the dancer.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["gunam","thaththuvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 808","lb":"NK808","ev":"","cl":[["action is one with the substance that acts","jain","asserted",4,"neelakesi"],["action is a category separate from substance","vedic","refuted",3,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","உலோகன்"],"ch":"nk:37"},"nk:809":{"book":"neelakesi","num":807,"t":["கூத்த ராடலுங் குதிரை யோடலு","மோத்துரை யுள்பட வொழிந்த யாவையு","நீத்தன வேயல்ல நிலையு முண்மையிற்","போத்தரல் வேண்டினப் பொழுதி னாகுமே."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi says the dancers' dancing, the horse's running, recitation of scripture and every other act that has stopped are not lost; they persist in their bearer and come forth again when called for.","n":"Action is treated as a capacity that stays latent in the substance, not as an event that perishes when it ceases; this is how she can call it one with its bearer.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["gunam","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 809","lb":"NK809","ev":"","cl":[["an action that has ceased persists as a capacity in its bearer and returns when needed","jain","asserted",3,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","உலோகன்"],"ch":"nk:37"},"nk:810":{"book":"neelakesi","num":808,"t":["பிணங்கல வாய்த்தம்முட் பிறக ளாகிய","குணங்களுந் தொழில்களுங் குழுமிக கெட்டன","புணர்ந்துடன் பொருள்வயிற் போந்த வாறுநீ","யுணர்ந்திலை யதுவுநின் னுடைய தேபிற."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi tells Ulokan that he has not grasped how qualities and actions, distinct yet not at odds, gather, pass away and come to reside together in the substance, and that the fault lies with him.","n":"Line 2 reads குழுமிக கெட்டன without the doubled consonant, and line 4's அதுவும் நின்னுடையதே பிற is hard to construe; the reading follows the drift of the argument.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["gunam","vaadhu","arivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 810","lb":"NK810","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","உலோகன்"],"ch":"nk:37"},"nk:811":{"book":"neelakesi","num":809,"t":["பாதுவெலாப் பொருளொடும் பொருந்தி நின்றதே","லதுவெலாப் பொருள்களை யாக்கு மொற்றுமை","யிதுவலா லவைதம்மு ளியைத லில்லையேல்","செதுவலாம் பிறவது சென்ற தென்றலே."],"p":"","r":"Turning to generality, Neelakesi argues that if the universal stands joined to every thing it would be a oneness making all things one, and if it does not join them, saying that it went into them is empty.","n":"Line 1 is printed பாதுவெலா, plainly for பொதுவெலா (the universal, பொது); line 4's செதுவலாம் is obscure. The dilemma itself (all one, or not joined at all) is clear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thaththuvam","vaadhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 811","lb":"NK811","ev":"","cl":[["a single generality resides in every thing of its kind","vedic","refuted",1,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","உலோகன்"],"ch":"nk:37"},"nk:812":{"book":"neelakesi","num":810,"t":["அன்றியும் பொதுவது வந்தமில் பொருட்","சென்றதே யென்றலாற் சிதர்ந்து பன்மையாய்க்","குன்றியுங் கூடியு நின்றுங் கொள்பய","மின்றியும் போதலா லென்னை யாயதோ."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi presses further: since he says the universal has entered endless things, it must be scattered into many, shrinking, joining and staying, and going without any use; what then has it become?","n":"","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thaththuvam","vaadhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 812","lb":"NK812","ev":"","cl":[["one generality cannot be present whole in endless separate things","jain","asserted",2,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","உலோகன்"],"ch":"nk:37"},"nk:813":{"book":"neelakesi","num":811,"t":["பொதுவெனப் படுவதோர் போலி யாதலாற்","பொதுவெனப் படுவன போன்ற தாங்களே","யதுவென மீட்டிருந் தாறென் றெண்ணுவா","யிதுவென வென்னையிங் கிதனோ டேழெனாய்."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi says that what is called the universal is only likeness, so the things called common are simply things that resemble one another; and she asks why, if he counts such a thing again among six categories, he does not add this one and make seven.","n":"போலி (line 1) gives the Jain account of the universal as similarity among things, not a separate entity. The jibe about counting seven turns on the Vaisheshika list of six categories (ஆறு, line 3).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thaththuvam","samayam","vaadhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 813","lb":"NK813","ev":"","cl":[["what is called the universal is only the likeness of things to one another","jain","asserted",1,"neelakesi"],["the categories are six","vedic","refuted",3,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","உலோகன்"],"ch":"nk:37"},"nk:814":{"book":"neelakesi","num":812,"t":["சிறப்பெனப் படுவது தெரியி னப்பொரு","ளறப்பெற நின்றவக் குணம தாதலின்","இறப்பவு மிதுதன தின்மை யேயினாய்","புறப்படுத் திடுவனுன் பொருள ளெண்ணலே."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi says that particularity, examined, is just the quality that stands fully in the thing itself, so it too has no being of its own, and she will throw out his count of categories.","n":"Line 4 reads பொருள ளெண்ணலே with a doubled ள.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thaththuvam","gunam","vaadhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 814","lb":"NK814","ev":"","cl":[["particularity is a separate category","vedic","refuted",1,"neelakesi"],["particularity is only the thing's own quality","jain","asserted",2,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","உலோகன்"],"ch":"nk:37"},"nk:815":{"book":"neelakesi","num":813,"t":["கூட்டினா லல்லது கூட்ட மில்லையேற்","கூட்டுவான் செல்வதுங் கூட்ட மில்லையே","பாட்டினாற் பலவுமாம் பயமி லாதன","மூட்டினா லுரைத்தியோ முனிவு போக்கிதோ."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi argues against inherence: if there is no joining except by a joiner, then the inherence that goes to join must itself be joined by another, and so on; she asks whether he piles up many useless things as if reciting verses.","n":"The regress is clear in lines 1-2; line 4's முனிவு போக்கிதோ is hard to construe, and the sense of பாட்டினால் (line 3) is a guess.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thaththuvam","vaadhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 815","lb":"NK815","ev":"","cl":[["quality and substance are joined by a separate relation of inherence","vedic","refuted",1,"neelakesi"],["a relation that joins things must itself be joined, so inherence leads to endless regress","jain","asserted",2,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","உலோகன்"],"ch":"nk:37"},"nk:816":{"book":"neelakesi","num":814,"t":["கொக்கொடக் கருமையைக் கூட்டு வித்தலுஞ்","சுக்கொடத் துவர்ப்பினைத் துன்னு வித்தலுஞ்","சிக்கென வேற்றுமை தீர்ப்பி நீயென","நக்கன ளளியனோ நயவ னென்னவே."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi dares Ulokan to make the heron take blackness and dry ginger take astringency, and so do away with difference once and for all, and laughs that this learned man is to be pitied.","n":"If inherence could join any quality to any substance, nothing would stop black from inhering in the white heron; the absurd test shows a quality belongs to its own bearer by nature.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["gunam","vaadhu","igal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 816","lb":"NK816","ev":"Neelakesi challenges Ulokan to join blackness to a heron and laughs at him.","cl":[["if inherence joined qualities from outside, any quality could be joined to any thing","jain","asserted",1,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","உலோகன்"],"ch":"nk:37"},"nk:817":{"book":"neelakesi","num":815,"t":["என்றுமப் பொருள்களு மியல்புந் தங்களோ","டொன்றென வருதலா லொன்று மாகுமே","யென்றலி லிருமையுந் தெரியி னின்மையா","லின்றினிக் கூட்டுவ தில்லை யில்லதே."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi concludes that since things and their natures always arrive together as one, they are one, and since the twoness he needs is not found, there is nothing left for inherence to join.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["gunam","thaththuvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 817","lb":"NK817","ev":"","cl":[["a thing and its nature always come together as one, leaving nothing for inherence to join","jain","asserted",2,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","உலோகன்"],"ch":"nk:37"},"nk:818":{"book":"neelakesi","num":816,"t":["ஒன்றுநற் பொருள்கடாங் குணங்க டாம்பல","வென்றுநீ யேகம் வேறென்ப தென்னெனப்","பொன்றுமக் குணமெனிற் பொருட்கு மாமென","நன்றினிக் குணமுண்மை நாட்ட மாமென."],"p":"","r":"An exchange: Ulokan asks why she calls substance one and qualities many and yet says they are both one and different; she answers that if a quality perishes it perishes for the substance too; and it is said that the reality of the quality is now established.","n":"The verse is a run of quoted speeches each closed by என; who says the last (நன்று...) is not stated, and the reading of line 3 is tentative.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["gunam","vaadhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 818","lb":"NK818","ev":"Ulokan questions Neelakesi's one-and-different thesis and she answers him.","cl":[["when a quality perishes the substance changes with it","jain","asserted",3,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["உலோகன்","நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"ch":"nk:37"},"nk:819":{"book":"neelakesi","num":817,"t":["குணிக்கணா னோக்கினாற் குணங்க ளில்குணத்","தணிக்கணா னோக்கினா லதுவு மன்னதே","பிணிக்கலாம் பிரிக்கலாம் பெற்ற நான்மையிற்","றுணிக்கலாந் துருநெறி துன்னு நன்மையே."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi explains that seen from the side of the bearer there are no qualities apart, and seen from the side of the qualities the same holds; so one may join or separate them, and with the fourfold reckoning so gained one may decide, and the good of the path is reached.","n":"Lines 1-2 set the two standpoints, of the substance and of the quality, from which the same thing is called one or many; நான்மை (line 3) and துருநெறி (line 4) are not clear, and the fourfold may be the four alternatives of one, different, both and neither.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["nayam","gunam","meyyunarvu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 819","lb":"NK819","ev":"","cl":[["from the standpoint of the bearer qualities are not separate, and from the standpoint of the quality the same holds","jain","asserted",1,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","உலோகன்"],"ch":"nk:37"},"nk:820":{"book":"neelakesi","num":818,"t":["இல்லையக் குணங்குணிக் குண்மை தானெனச்","சொல்லினக் குணிகுணத் தொன்று மாதலா","லல்லதக் குணங்களு மவைக ளாமென","நல்லதித் துணிவென நயத்தி லெய்தினான்."],"p":"","r":"The narrator tells how Ulokan, through the method of standpoints, came to the conclusion that the quality has no reality apart from its bearer, that the bearer is one with its quality, and that the qualities are those things themselves, and judged it good.","n":"எய்தினான் (line 4) is masculine: the opponent himself reaches the conclusion, by நயம், the standpoint method. The defeat is recorded as a conversion of judgment, not a rout.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nayam","gunam","vaadhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 820","lb":"NK820","ev":"Ulokan accepts Neelakesi's conclusion by the method of standpoints.","cl":[["the quality has no reality apart from its bearer, and the bearer is one with its quality","jain","asserted",1,"ulokan"]],"ag":["உலோகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"nk:37"},"nk:821":{"book":"neelakesi","num":819,"t":["பொருளொடக் குணந்தொழிற் குண்மை யொன்றெனத்","தெருள்வது மும்மையிற் றெரிய வைப்பது","மருளுடை யறநெறி யண்ணல் சேவடி","யிருள்கெட நினைத்தலு மினையை யாகென."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi bids Ulokan to understand that substance, quality and action have one reality, to make it clear in the threefold path, and to meditate on the red feet of the lord of the path of compassion so that darkness perishes, and so to be.","n":"மும்மை (line 2) is most likely the three jewels of right faith, knowledge and conduct. Darkness ends by the disciple's own understanding and remembering of the lord, not by the lord's act.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","arul","meyyunarvu","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 821","lb":"NK821","ev":"Neelakesi instructs the defeated Ulokan in the Jain path.","cl":[["substance, quality and action have one reality","jain","asserted",1,"neelakesi"],["meditating on the feet of the compassionate lord dispels darkness","jain","asserted",3,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","உலோகன்"],"ch":"nk:37"},"nk:822":{"book":"neelakesi","num":820,"t":["ஓம்படுத் துலோகனை யொழியச் சொல்லியான்","காம்புடைக் கடநெறி கடப்ப னென்னவே","பேம்படுப் பவரொடும் பிரிவின் னாமையைத்","தேம்படு கிளவிநீ சிந்தி யென்னவே."],"p":"","r":"Having given Ulokan her parting charge, Neelakesi says she will cross the bamboo-filled forest road, and he answers by asking the sweet-spoken one to think of the pain of parting.","n":"The division of speakers is inferred from the two என்னவே; பேம்படுப்பவரொடும் (line 3) is hard, perhaps parting even from those who once caused fear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","vaadhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 822","lb":"NK822","ev":"Neelakesi takes leave of Ulokan, who speaks of the pain of parting.","cl":[],"ag":["உலோகன்","நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"ch":"nk:37"},"nk:823":{"book":"neelakesi","num":821,"t":["ஒக்குமவ் வுரையென வுள்ள தேயென","நக்கன ளாகிய நாதன் சேதியஞ்","சிக்கென வேத்துதல் சிறந்த தென்னவே","தக்கதென் றவன் சொலத் தானு நீங்கினாள்."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi smiles that his words are fitting and true, and tells him that steadily praising the Lord's shrine is the best course; when he says it is right, she leaves.","n":"சேதியம் (line 2) is the caitya, the shrine of the Jina. நக்கனளாகிய may be read as 'she smiled' or as நக்கன் (the unclothed one) with the Lord; the reading takes the first.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","iraivan","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 823","lb":"NK823","ev":"Neelakesi tells Ulokan to worship at the Lord's shrine; he agrees and she departs.","cl":[["steady praise of the Lord's shrine is the best course","jain","asserted",3,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி","உலோகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"nk:37"},"nk:824":{"book":"neelakesi","num":822,"t":["காதம் பலவும் கடந்தபின்காகந்திக் கடிநகருள்","வேதமு மங்கமும் விச்சைகணிலைமையும் வேண்டுநர்கட்","கோதவுங் கேட்பவு முரைத்தலினுலகினு ளறியப்பட்டான்","பூதிக னெனப்படு மந்தணனோத்திடம் புக்கனளே."],"p":"","r":"After crossing many kaathams Neelakesi enters the guarded city of Kakandi and goes into the Veda school of the Brahmin Bhutikan, known in the world for teaching and expounding the Veda, its limbs and the other sciences to all who seek them.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vedham","andhanar","vaadhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 824","lb":"NK824","ev":"Neelakesi reaches Kakandi and enters the Veda school of the Brahmin Bhutikan.","cl":[],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"ch":"nk:38"},"nk:825":{"book":"neelakesi","num":823,"t":["என்னை யிங்குநும் பொருளெனவினவலு மிவ்விருந்த","வன்னைதன் வரவிதே லாதியிலருமறை யதுமுதலாப்","பின்னைவந் தனகளு மிவையெனப்பையவே யெர்த்துரைத்தான்","முன்னமங் கிருந்தவோர் முதுமகனவைதன் முறைமையினே."],"p":"","r":"When she asks what their subject is, an old man seated there, taking this to be the lady's errand, sets out gently and in order the rare Veda from the beginning and the works that came after it.","n":"Whether the elder (முதுமகன்) is Bhutikan himself is not said; the next verse has Bhutikan answer her in anger. எர்த்துரைத்தான் in line 3 looks like a slip for எடுத்துரைத்தான்.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vedham","kalvi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 825","lb":"NK825","ev":"Neelakesi asks what is taught there, and an elder of the school lists the Veda and the works that follow it.","cl":[],"ag":["நீலகேசி","முதுமகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"nk:38"},"nk:826":{"book":"neelakesi","num":824,"t":["நாத்திக மல்லது சொல்லலையாயின்மு னான்பயந்த","சாத்திர மாவது வேதமன்றோவது தான்சயம்பு","சூத்திரி நீயது வல்லையலாமையிற் சொல்லுகிறாய்","போத்தந்தி யோவதன் றீமையென்றான்பொங்கிப் பூதிகனே."],"p":"","r":"Bhutikan, flaring up, tells her that if all she speaks is unbelief, the scripture brought forth of old is the Veda, which is self-existent, and that she, a Sudra woman, talks so only because she has no competence in it.","n":"The Brahmin answers her with her birth before her argument: the charge of being a Sudra woman (சூத்திரி) is the point she takes up in 827 and that 830 and 834 answer. Line 1 (முன் நான் பயந்த) and line 4 (போத்தந்தியோ) are hard to divide; the sense 'the scripture of old is the Veda, self-born' is clear from சயம்பு.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vedham","pirappu","vaadhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 826","lb":"NK826","ev":"Bhutikan angrily calls her an unbeliever and a Sudra woman unfit to judge the Veda.","cl":[["the Veda is self-existent, made by no one","vedic","refuted",2,"bhuthikan"],["one of Sudra birth has no competence to speak on the Veda","vedic","refuted",3,"bhuthikan"]],"ag":["பூதிகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["பூதிகன்","நீலகேசி"],"ch":"nk:38"},"nk:827":{"book":"neelakesi","num":825,"t":["பூதிகன் றானது சொல்லலும் யானது வல்லனெல்லாம்","சாதிகண் டாயெனத் தான்றள ராது சாற்றுகென்றாட்","காதியென் றானுமோ ரந்தமென்றானுமுண் டேலதற்கு","நீதியி னாலுரை நீயினி யானது நேர்வ னென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi, unshaken, says she is able in all of it and that he has looked only at her birth, and bids him state his case; he answers that if the Veda has any beginning or any end she should show it by reasoning, and he will concede.","n":"The terms of the debate are set here: the Brahmin stakes the Veda's authority on its having no origin, and the next five verses are her inferences that it was made. Read with the division சாற்றுக என்றாட்கு ஆதி: lines 1-2 are hers and lines 3-4 his.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vedham","vaadhu","pirappu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 827","lb":"NK827","ev":"Neelakesi rejects the slur on her birth, and Bhutikan challenges her to prove the Veda has a beginning or end.","cl":[["the Veda has neither beginning nor end","vedic","refuted",3,"bhuthikan"]],"ag":["பூதிகன்","நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"ch":"nk:38"},"nk:828":{"book":"neelakesi","num":826,"t":["செய்கையும் புதுமையு முடைமையிற்றிருட்டத்தின் மறுதலையிற்","பொய்யொடும் பொருளொடுங் குவகொடுஞ்சாலவும் பொருந்துதலின்","மையறு மயக்கமு மாற்றொடுகொலைமன்னு மருவுதலின்","ஐயமி றீக்கதிச் செலுத்துவததுவென்னை யாவதென்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi argues that the Veda is a made thing, since it involves action and novelty, goes against what is seen, mixes falsehood with sense, and is bound up with confusion, contradiction and killing, and so surely drives its followers to an evil birth.","n":"She stacks several reasons (ஏது) in the form of a formal inference, the logicians' own method turned on the Veda. Line 2 குவகொடும் is unclear and may be corrupt.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vedham","kathi","kolai","vaadhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 828","lb":"NK828","ev":"","cl":[["the Veda is a made work, not eternal, because it shows action and novelty","jain","asserted",1,"neelakesi"],["because it is joined with killing, the Veda leads its followers to an evil state of birth","jain","asserted",4,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","பூதிகன்"],"ch":"nk:38"},"nk:829":{"book":"neelakesi","num":827,"t":["யாரது செய்தவ ரறியிலிங்குரையெனி லங்கொருவ","னூரது நடுவணொ ருஐறயுளில்மலம்பெய்திட் டொளித்தொழியிற்","பேரினு முருவினும் பெறலிலனாதலின் றாக்குறித்துத்","தேரினு மினியது செய்தவரில்லெனச் செப்புவவே."],"p":"","r":"To the question who made it, if anyone knows, she answers that when a man defiles a room in the middle of the town and slips away, no one concludes on inquiry that nobody did it merely because his name and form cannot be found.","n":"The coarse example is chosen to insult as well as to argue: the authorless Veda is likened to filth left by a man who hid. Line 2 ஒருஐறயுள் is garbled (perhaps ஓர் உறையுள்).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vedham","vaadhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 829","lb":"NK829","ev":"","cl":[["that a work's author is not remembered does not show it has no author","jain","asserted",3,"neelakesi"],["no one is known to have made the Veda, so it has no maker","vedic","refuted",1,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","பூதிகன்"],"ch":"nk:38"},"nk:830":{"book":"neelakesi","num":828,"t":["தோற்றமு நாற்றமுஞ் சுவையுடனூறிவற் றாற்றொடங்கி","யாற்றவு மாயிரு வேதம்வல்லார்களஃ தறிந்துரைப்ப","மேற்குலத் தாரோ டிழிந்தவரென்பது மெய்ம்மைபெறா","நூற்றிறஞ் செய்தவ ரறிகுவர்நுழைந்தறி வுடையவரே."],"p":"","r":"She argues that when appearance, smell, taste and touch are examined in full, the claim that some are of high birth and others low does not hold true, as those versed in the two Vedas, the makers of treatises and the penetrating know.","n":"The argument against caste is from perception: no sense can find the difference that birth is said to make. It also answers the taunt of 826. The force of இரு வேதம் (two Vedas) and of line 1's list is not certain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pirappu","kudi","arivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 830","lb":"NK830","ev":"","cl":[["high and low birth is not a real difference, since the senses find none between persons","jain","asserted",3,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","பூதிகன்"],"ch":"nk:38"},"nk:831":{"book":"neelakesi","num":829,"t":["முயற்சியி னிசைத்தலி னெழுத்தினிற் பதத்தினின் முடிவதனால்","செயற்பட லுடையத னியற்கையிற்செய்தவர் பெயர்பெறலா","லியற்கைய தன்றுநின் வேதமென்றேதுவி னெடுத்துரைத்தாள்","புயற்றிற லிகலிய கூந்தலின்பெயருடைப் புலமையினாள்."],"p":"","r":"She argues from reasons that the Veda is not a thing of nature, since it is completed by the effort of utterance in letters and words, has the character of something produced, and its makers give their names to its parts; so speaks the learned one whose name tells of hair that rivals the rain-cloud.","n":"The poet names Neelakesi here by a gloss on her name (நீல-கேசி, dark hair) joined to புலமை, learning, just after Bhutikan has called her an incompetent Sudra.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vedham","vaadhu","kalvi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 831","lb":"NK831","ev":"","cl":[["speech is produced by effort through letters and words, so the Veda is not natural or eternal","jain","asserted",1,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","பூதிகன்"],"ch":"nk:38"},"nk:832":{"book":"neelakesi","num":830,"t":["கதியவர் தம்பெய ரின்னவைசுட்டின காட்டலினு","முதியவர் நாள்களொ டொப்பிலவிப்பொழு தொத்தலினும்","விதியது வாதலின் வேதத்தையாஞ்சொல்லுங் கீதத்தைப் போற்","புதியது வேயெனச் சொல்லுதுநாமது பொருந்து மென்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"She adds that since the Veda names particular persons and matches the present age rather than the days of the ancients, and since it is a body of injunctions, we call it new like the songs we sing, and that is fitting.","n":"Line 1 கதியவர் is unclear; the sense 'it names persons' rests on பெயர் இன்னவை சுட்டின.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vedham","vaadhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 832","lb":"NK832","ev":"","cl":[["the Veda names persons and fits the present age, so it is recent like any song","jain","asserted",4,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","பூதிகன்"],"ch":"nk:38"},"nk:833":{"book":"neelakesi","num":831,"t":["கொல்வது தீதெனப் பொருள்வழிவேள்வியிற் கொலப்படுவ","வெல்லையொன் றிலதென்ப விணைவிழைச்சொழிகென்ப வம்முகத்தாற்","செல்கதி யுளதென்ப தீர்த்துகநெறியென்றுந் தீயவென்று","பல்லவர் துணிவுமெம் வேதத்தினுளவெனப் பயின்றுரைப்ப."],"p":"","r":"She lists the Veda's contradictions as its own reciters state them: it says killing is evil yet sets no limit to what may be killed in sacrifice for gain, says to give up sexual union and that a heaven is reached by it, and says the holy fords are the path and also that they are evil.","n":"The sacrificial claim is answered in 836 to 844. The account of what the Veda teaches is the Jain debater's, and a Vedic school would not grant that its rules contradict each other.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kollaamai","velvi","vedham","vaadhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 833","lb":"NK833","ev":"","cl":[["the Veda contains precepts that contradict each other","jain","asserted",4,"neelakesi"],["killing in the sacrifice has no limit and brings gain","vedic","refuted",1,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","பூதிகன்"],"ch":"nk:38"},"nk:834":{"book":"neelakesi","num":832,"t":["சாதிக்கட் பயவா தவப்பயந்தருமெனத் தந்துரைப்ப","வாதிக்க ணான்வழி நால்வரதமைதியை யமர்ந்துரைப்ப","சூதித்த தோற்றமும் பிழைப்பெனச்சூத்திரப் பிறவிகொள்ளார்","வாதித்த வாறென்று தெருண்டவர்க்கிவையிவை மயாமயக்கே."],"p":"","r":"She goes on: they say birth yields nothing and austerity yields fruit, yet they set out with approval the order of the four classes from the beginning and will not grant a Sudra birth; to the clear-sighted these are confusions on confusions.","n":"She does not argue that either Vedic teaching is false here, only that they cannot both stand. Line 3 சூதித்த தோற்றமும் பிழைப்பென is obscure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pirappu","thavam","vedham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 834","lb":"NK834","ev":"","cl":[["birth yields nothing and austerity yields fruit","vedic","reported",1,"neelakesi"],["the four classes are ordained from the beginning","vedic","reported",2,"neelakesi"],["a scripture that says both of these is confused","jain","asserted",4,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","பூதிகன்"],"ch":"nk:38"},"nk:835":{"book":"neelakesi","num":833,"t":["மறுதலை தத்தமு ளாக்கிமயக்கமுஞ் செய்தமையாற்","பெறுதலை யென்னைகொ றத்துவந்தனையன்று பறுமுண்டே","யுறுதியுஞ் சால்பு முடையனயாவையு முண்மையினாற்","செறுதலை யேவில்லை சீர்த்தனசெய்விக்குஞ் சிட்டிதுவே."],"p":"","r":"Because it has made contradictions within itself and caused confusion, she asks what is gained by it, and says that a teaching that is firm and worthy, true in all it says, has no conflict within, and that such a teaching is what makes people do excellent things.","n":"Line 2 (பெறுதலை யென்னைகொ றத்துவந்தனையன்று பறுமுண்டே) is badly corrupt and cannot be divided with confidence; only the drift of lines 1, 3 and 4 is clear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vedham","vaaimai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 835","lb":"NK835","ev":"","cl":[["a true teaching has no inner contradiction","jain","asserted",3,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","பூதிகன்"],"ch":"nk:38"},"nk:836":{"book":"neelakesi","num":834,"t":["வசுக்களொ டுருத்திரர் பிதிரரோடிவர்முத லாப்பலர்க்கும்","பசுக்களோ டெருமைகள் குதிரைகள்புலியொடு நாய்முதலா","விசுக்கிழிந் தனபல கொலைகளுமிரங்கலிர் கொன்றவரை","யசிப்பவர் போன்றனி ராயினுமருவினை யாநுமக்கே."],"p":"","r":"She charges that for the Vasus, Rudras, ancestors and many others they kill cows, buffaloes, horses, tigers, dogs and more without pity, and that even if they behave as mere eaters of what others killed, the heavy deed is theirs.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kollaamai","velvi","vinai","thevar"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 836","lb":"NK836","ev":"","cl":[["killing in sacrifice to gods and ancestors brings a heavy deed upon the sacrificers","jain","asserted",4,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","பூதிகன்"],"ch":"nk:38"},"nk:837":{"book":"neelakesi","num":835,"t":["தேவரும் பிதிரரும் நுதலிய கொலைகளிற் றீவினைதா","மேவர வல்லன வேண்டுவல் யானென வேண்டுதியேல்","யாவரையு நுதலியு மவரவர் செய்தன வவரவர்க்கே","யாவரி னடையுமவ் வருவினை நுமக்கறி வரியதென்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"If he says that in killings meant for the gods and ancestors he wishes the evil deed to fall on them, she answers that whoever is intended, what each did comes to each, and that deed reaches him in ways he cannot know.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","velvi","thevar"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 837","lb":"NK837","ev":"","cl":[["the sin of a killing done for the gods passes to the gods","vedic","refuted",2,"neelakesi"],["each person's deed comes back to that person, whoever it was meant for","jain","asserted",3,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","பூதிகன்"],"ch":"nk:38"},"nk:838":{"book":"neelakesi","num":836,"t":["ஊட்டுதும் யாமென் றுமர்களைநுதலியோர் சாலைவைத் தால்","வீட்டினங் கிடலின்றி வினைநிலைநுமக்கறி வரியதுபோல்","கூட்டிமற் றவர்களை நுதலியகொலைவினை தங்களையுங்","காட்டுகில் லாரவர் தாமவையறிவதோர் கணக்கிலரே."],"p":"","r":"She argues that just as one who sets up a feeding-hall for his kin cannot know how its merit stands, so the gods for whom killing is done do not even show that the deed has reached them, and have no reckoning by which to know it.","n":"The comparison in lines 1-2 is compressed and its point is not certain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vinai","thevar","eegai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 838","lb":"NK838","ev":"","cl":[["a deed done in another's name does not pass to that other","jain","asserted",3,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","பூதிகன்"],"ch":"nk:38"},"nk:839":{"book":"neelakesi","num":837,"t":["சிறந்தவர் தங்களுக் கெய்துகசென்றென்னுஞ் சிந்தையரா","யறம்பல செய்தவர்க் கல்லதங்கவர்களுக் காகுமென்றாற்","றுறந்தவர் வீடுபெற்றார்களைநுதலிய தொடர்வினையும்","பிறங்கியிப் பிறவியிற் போக்குமற்றிவையென்ன பேதைமையே."],"p":"","r":"If merit done with the wish that it reach the eminent goes to them rather than the doer, she says, then a deed aimed at renouncers who have already won release would drag them back into this birth: what folly is that.","n":"A reductio: the doctrine of transferred merit would let anyone's deed bind even the liberated, which neither side will grant.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","veedu","thuravu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 839","lb":"NK839","ev":"","cl":[["merit done in the name of others reaches them and not the doer","vedic","refuted",2,"neelakesi"],["if deeds passed to those they were aimed at, the released could be bound again","jain","asserted",3,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","பூதிகன்"],"ch":"nk:38"},"nk:840":{"book":"neelakesi","num":838,"t":["நண்பரை நுதலியும் பகைவரைநுதலியு மமிர்தொடுநஞ்","சுண்பவர்க் கல்லதற் கவர்களுக்காமென வுரைக்குநர்யார்","பண்பிலி தேவரை றுதலியகொலையினிற் பல்வினைதா","னுண்பல வகையினி னடைந்தவைவிளையுங்க ணுமக்குமென்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"Who would say, she asks, that nectar or poison eaten with a friend or an enemy in mind goes to them rather than to the eater; so the many deeds of killing aimed at the gods ripen in many ways in you, faithless one.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","kollaamai","thevar"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 840","lb":"NK840","ev":"","cl":[["the fruit of a deed falls on its doer as poison works on the one who eats it","jain","asserted",2,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","பூதிகன்"],"ch":"nk:38"},"nk:841":{"book":"neelakesi","num":839,"t":["கொன்றவர்க் கல்லது நுதலப்பட் டார்களைக் கூடலவேற்","றின்றவர்க கியாவையுந் தீவினைசேரல தேவர்க்குப்போ","லென்றுரைப் பாய்க்கெய்து மேழைமையுண்குவ வேலிமையார்க்","கொன்றுவி யேனல னோவினையூன்றின்பவர்க் கொப்பவென்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"If he says the evil deed joins only the killers and not those for whom the killing was done, nor the eaters, as it does not join the gods, she calls it foolishness and says the deed falls alike on those who eat the flesh with pleasure.","n":"Lines 2-4 are hard to divide (றின்றவர்க கியாவையும், கொன்றுவி யேனல னோ), so the exact shape of the objection is uncertain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pulaal","vinai","kollaamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 841","lb":"NK841","ev":"","cl":[["only the killer, not the eater of sacrificial flesh, bears the sin","vedic","refuted",1,"neelakesi"],["those who eat the flesh share the killer's deed","jain","asserted",4,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","பூதிகன்"],"ch":"nk:38"},"nk:842":{"book":"neelakesi","num":840,"t":["ஈகளு நாய்களுங் கொன்றவரீவகண் டின்புறலிற்","றீயவை யேசெய்யுந் தேவரத்தீவினை தீர்க்கிற்பவோ","நோய்களும் பேய்களு மொழிக்குவமெனினவை நுங்களுக்கு","மாய்விடி னுணரினஃ தாம்வினையகற்றுதற் கரியதென்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"Can gods who rejoice when killers of flies and dogs give to them, and who do only evil, remove that evil deed; and if they say the rite drives off diseases and demons, she answers that when death comes they will learn how hard karma is to remove.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thevar","vinai","velvi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 842","lb":"NK842","ev":"","cl":[["sacrifice drives away disease and demons","vedic","refuted",3,"neelakesi"],["gods who delight in killing cannot remove the deed of the killer","jain","asserted",2,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","பூதிகன்"],"ch":"nk:38"},"nk:843":{"book":"neelakesi","num":841,"t":["நம்முறு துன்பங்க ணாமொழிக்கல்லலம் பிறருறுப","வெம்முறை யாயினும் போக்குதற்கரியவிங் கிவர்களைப்போற்","றம்முறு துன்பமும் தாமொழிக்கில்லலர் பிறர்களையே","லெம்முறை நோய்களுஞ் செய்குபவவரென விகழ்ந்தனளே."],"p":"","r":"She mocks: we cannot end our own sufferings and others' are harder still to remove, and these gods likewise cannot end their own, yet they are said to bring every kind of disease on others.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thevar","idumbai","nindhai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 843","lb":"NK843","ev":"Neelakesi mocks the gods of the sacrifice.","cl":[["gods who cannot end their own suffering cannot end that of others","jain","asserted",3,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","பூதிகன்"],"ch":"nk:38"},"nk:844":{"book":"neelakesi","num":842,"t":["நாங்கொன்று கொடுக்குமவ் விலங்கினைநலிவதோர் பசியினரேற்","றாங்கொன்று தின்குவ ராய்விடினவர்களைத் தவிர்க்குநர்யார்","தீங்கொன்று முரையன்மின் தேவர்தம்மூணினைச் சேணின்றுதாம்","வாங்குத லல்லது முடையொடுசோறுண்ணும் வயிற்றினரே."],"p":"","r":"If the gods were hungry beings who tormented the beast we kill and give, she says, they would kill and eat it themselves and none could stop them; so speak no ill of them: the gods take their food from afar and have no bellies that eat rice with flesh.","n":"She turns piety against the rite: to say the gods eat flesh is itself to slander them (தீங்கொன்றும் உரையன்மின்).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thevar","pulaal","velvi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 844","lb":"NK844","ev":"","cl":[["the gods need and eat the flesh offered in sacrifice","vedic","refuted",1,"neelakesi"],["the gods have no bodily hunger for meat, so the slaughter offered to them is pointless","jain","asserted",4,"neelakesi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","பூதிகன்"],"ch":"nk:38"},"nk:852":{"book":"neelakesi","num":843,"t":["பொய்த்துரை யாநன்மை போதுவ தேலில்லை பூதிகனே","சத்திய மேயுரை நீயெனத் தானுமஃ தேயுரைத்தா","னெத்திசை யார்களு மேத்துதற் கேற்றன னிவனுமென்றார்","தத்துவ ரேநின்று தத்துவ ரெனப்படுந் தன்மையினார்."],"p":"","r":"Telling Bhutikan that no good comes of speaking falsely and bidding him speak the truth, she hears him speak it too, and the people say that he also is fit to be praised by all quarters, men of the nature called true who stand in the truth.","n":"Verses 845 to 851 are missing from the text, so the turn between her attack on sacrifice and his concession is lost; 852 opens on her last plea.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vaaimai","vaadhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 852","lb":"NK852","ev":"Bhutikan, urged to speak the truth, concedes, and the onlookers praise him.","cl":[["no good comes of speaking falsely","general","asserted",1,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி","பூதிகன்","நகரத்துள்ளார்"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","பூதிகன்"],"ch":"nk:38"},"nk:853":{"book":"neelakesi","num":844,"t":["நன்பொரு ளாவன விவையெனவவனோடு நகரத்துள்ளா","ரின்புறும் வகையினி னெடுத்தன ளுரைத்தபின் விடுக்கலுற்றாட்","கன்புபட் டவர்களு மறநெறியறிவித்த வார்வத்தினாற்","பின்புசென் றொழிதுமென் றதுசெய்துவலங்கொண்டு பெயர்ந்தனரே."],"p":"","r":"After she has expounded to him and the citizens, to their delight, which teachings are good and prepares to leave, those bound to her in love, eager because she made the path of dharma known, follow her a little way, circle her and turn back.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aram","anbu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 853","lb":"NK853","ev":"Neelakesi teaches Bhutikan and the townspeople, then leaves; her new followers escort and circumambulate her.","cl":[],"ag":["நீலகேசி","பூதிகன்","நகரத்துள்ளார்"],"ec":[],"ch":"nk:38"},"nk:854":{"book":"neelakesi","num":845,"t":["வேத வாதம் வெளிறுசெய் தாளங்கோர்","பூத வாதியும் பொங்கினன் மேற்செல","வாத வாதி யிவனை யடக்கினா","லேத வூதிய மில்லென வெண்ணித்தான்."],"p":"","r":"After Neelakesi has left the Vedic debater pale, a materialist rises in anger to go against her, reckoning that to subdue so practised a debater would be no small gain.","n":"பூத வாதி names the opponent by his doctrine, that only the elements are; his motive is the prize of beating a famous debater, not a question he wants answered.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vaadhu","boothavaadham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 854","lb":"NK854","ev":"A materialist rises to challenge Neelakesi after her defeat of the Vedic debater.","cl":[],"ag":["பிசாசகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"nk:39"},"nk:855":{"book":"neelakesi","num":846,"t":["நில்லப் பாவினி நீகண்ட தத்துவஞ்","சொல்லற் பாலையிங் கென்னலுஞ் சொல்லுவான்","மல்லற் றானை மதனசித் தன்னெனுங்","கல்லொத் தோங்கிய தோட்களி யானையான்."],"p":"","r":"When someone bids the man stop and state here the truth he has seen, the king Madanasittan, with his great army, stone-like shoulders and rutting elephants, is about to speak.","n":"Line 1 can be divided நில் அப்பா இனி (a courteous 'stay now, sir') or நில் பாவி நீ ('stop, sinner'); the first fits a presiding king inviting the materialist to state his case, the second Neelakesi challenging him. The description of line 3-4 is of a king, and he seems to be the one who then opens the hearing (856).","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vaadhu","avai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 855","lb":"NK855","ev":"The materialist is called on to state his doctrine before King Madanasittan.","cl":[],"ag":["மதனசித்தன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"nk:39"},"nk:856":{"book":"neelakesi","num":847,"t":["அணிகொ ளாரத் தரசவை கேட்கெனப்","பிணிகொள் மூஞ்சிப் பிசாசகன் சொல்லுவான்","குணிகு ணம்மெனுங் கூற்றில னாலதென்","றுணிவைம் பூதங்க ளேதொழில் சொல்லுவேன்."],"p":"","r":"Told to let the royal assembly hear, the materialist Pisasakan, of the diseased face, says he will make no talk of substance and quality but will set out, as settled, only the workings of the five elements.","n":"பிசாசகன், a name that sounds like பிசாசு (a demon), is given with an insult (பிணிகொள் மூஞ்சி); the end of the sarukkam turns the name into a joke (892: 'your very name is Pisasikan'). Refusing the substance-quality frame that the Vaisheshika and Jain debates turned on is his first move.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["boothavaadham","gunam","vaadhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 856","lb":"NK856","ev":"Pisasakan begins his case before the king's assembly.","cl":[["only the workings of the five elements need be stated, without any distinction of substance and quality","lokayata","reported",4,"pisasakan"]],"ag":["பிசாசகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"nk:39"},"nk:857":{"book":"neelakesi","num":848,"t":["தண்ணென் றீநில நீர்வளி காயத்தாற்","கண்ணு மூக்கொடு நாமெய் செவிகளாய்","வண்ண நாற்றஞ் சுவையினொ டூறொலி","யெண்ணுங் காலை யியைந்துழி யெய்துமே."],"p":"","r":"Pisasakan says that from fire, earth, water, wind and space come eye, nose, tongue, body and ear, and colour, smell, taste, touch and sound, which arise where the elements are joined.","n":"He counts five elements, space (காயம்) among them; the materialists are usually said to count four. Each sense and its object is derived from the elements; nothing is left over for a soul.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["boothavaadham","pori"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 857","lb":"NK857","ev":"","cl":[["the five senses and their five objects arise from the five elements joined together","lokayata","refuted",4,"pisasakan"]],"ag":["பிசாசகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["பிசாசகன்","அவை"],"ch":"nk:39"},"nk:858":{"book":"neelakesi","num":849,"t":["ஐந்துங் கூடிய றிவின்ப மாதியாய்","வந்து தோன்றி மதுமயக் காற்றலி","னந்தி நாளுங் குடஞ்சுடர் நாட்டம்போற்","சிந்தி னாலவை சென்றினஞ் சேருமே."],"p":"","r":"He says that when the five come together, knowing, pleasure and the rest appear, as the power to intoxicate appears in liquor and as a lamp's light in a pot, and that when the combination scatters each part goes back to join its own kind.","n":"Consciousness is a new power of the mixture, as drunkenness is of fermented liquor, and it ends with no remainder when the parts return to their elements. Neelakesi answers the liquor figure in 866 and the claim that a mixture yields what its parts lack in 868. The lamp-in-a-pot comparison of line 3 is compressed and its point is a reading.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["boothavaadham","arivu","kal","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 858","lb":"NK858","ev":"","cl":[["awareness and pleasure arise from the combined elements as intoxication arises in liquor","lokayata","refuted",2,"pisasakan"],["when the combination breaks up, each element returns to its own kind and nothing survives","lokayata","refuted",4,"pisasakan"]],"ag":["பிசாசகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["பிசாசகன்",""],"ch":"nk:39"},"nk:859":{"book":"neelakesi","num":850,"t":["உலகெ லாமவை யேயுயி ருண்டெனச்","சொலவ லாரன சொற்றெளிந் தேநின்று","பலக லாங்களுஞ் செய்வ பயனிலார்","புலவ ராவதன் றோவங்குப் போந்ததே."],"p":"","r":"He says the whole world is these elements alone, and that those who stand convinced by the clever talk of a soul only make many fruitless quarrels and end up as mere scholars.","n":"The sting is that belief in a soul produces argument and nothing else; Neelakesi turns the same charge back in 864, asking what his own debating is for if nothing bears fruit.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["boothavaadham","uyir","samayam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 859","lb":"NK859","ev":"","cl":[["the world is nothing but the elements, and talk of a soul yields only fruitless dispute","lokayata","refuted",1,"pisasakan"]],"ag":["பிசாசகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["பிசாசகன்",""],"ch":"nk:39"},"nk:860":{"book":"neelakesi","num":851,"t":["சென்ற காலத்துஞ் செல்கின்ற காலத்து","நின்ற காலத்தி லும்மிந் நிகழ்ச்சியே","யென்று மிவ்வுல கித்தன்மைத் தேயிது","வன்ற தென்றுரைப் பாரயர்ப் பார்களே."],"p":"","r":"He ends that in time past, present and to come this combining alone is what happens, the world is always of this nature, and those who say otherwise are deluded.","n":"Answered in this chapter's arguments for the soul (865-874) and conceded by him at the sarukkam's end (893).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["boothavaadham","samayam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 860","lb":"NK860","ev":"","cl":[["in all three times the world is only the combining of the elements","lokayata","refuted",3,"pisasakan"]],"ag":["பிசாசகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["பிசாசகன்",""],"ch":"nk:39"},"nk:861":{"book":"neelakesi","num":852,"t":["இட்ட மாவ திதுவெனக் கேட்டவள்","சிட்ட ரன்றியுஞ் சென்றிருந் தார்க்கெலா","மொட்டி மீட்டு முரைத்துளந் தான்கொளீஇக்","கட்டு ரைய்யெடுத் தாள்கயற் கண்ணினாள்."],"p":"","r":"Having heard that this is his settled tenet, the carp-eyed Neelakesi repeats it for the learned and for everyone sitting there until they hold it clearly, and then takes up her reply.","n":"She restates the opponent's case to the whole audience before answering it, the debate's rule that the view refuted must first be granted and understood.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vaadhu","avai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 861","lb":"NK861","ev":"Neelakesi restates the materialist's position to the assembly and begins her refutation.","cl":[],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"ch":"nk:39"},"nk:862":{"book":"neelakesi","num":853,"t":["வினையி னீங்கி விளங்கிய ஞானத்தோர்","முனைவ னின்மையி னான்முதல னூலில்லை","யனைய மாண்பின தாகம மாதலாற்","புனைவ னின்னுரை பொய்யெனச் சொல்லியே."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi says that since he has no teacher shining with knowledge freed from deeds, he has no first book, and lacking scripture of that worth his words are invention and false.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aagamam","guru","vinai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 862","lb":"NK862","ev":"","cl":[["a doctrine with no teacher freed from deeds has no first scripture, and so is invention","jain","asserted",2,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி",""],"ch":"nk:39"},"nk:863":{"book":"neelakesi","num":854,"t":["கவைத்த கோலொடு கட்டில் கடிஞைகாத்","தவத்திற் கொத்தன தாங்கினித் தாபதம்","அவத்த மேபிற வாருயி ரில்லையேற்","சுதத்தி னாலுய்த்தல் சூதது வாகுமே."],"p":"","r":"She says that if there is no soul besides the elements, then the forked staff, the bowl and the other gear fit for penance, and the ascetic's state itself, are wasted, and guiding people by teaching is a cheat.","n":"The argument is that ascetic practice only makes sense if something survives the body; it may also hit the materialist's own ascetic garb, which the verse implies he wears. கட்டில் in line 1 and சுதத்தி in line 4 are doubtful forms.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thavam","vesham","uyir"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 863","lb":"NK863","ev":"","cl":[["without a soul beyond the elements, penance and its gear are futile and teaching is deception","jain","asserted",3,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி",""],"ch":"nk:39"},"nk:864":{"book":"neelakesi","num":855,"t":["பூத மல்லது பொய்பிற நூலென்று","வாதஞ் செய்து வருந்தி முடிப்பதென்","நாத னன்னெறி நல்வினை நற்பயன்","யாது மில்லை யெனினஃ தவத்தமே."],"p":"","r":"She asks why he labours in debate to prove that only the elements are and other books are false, when on his view there is no Lord's good path, no good deed and no good fruit, so that the effort itself is waste.","n":"A performative objection: arguing to win presupposes that some outcome is worth reaching, which his denial of fruit undercuts. It returns the 'fruitless quarrel' charge of 859.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["payan","vinai","vaadhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 864","lb":"NK864","ev":"","cl":[["if there is no path, no merit and no fruit, arguing for that view is itself pointless","jain","asserted",3,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி",""],"ch":"nk:39"},"nk:865":{"book":"neelakesi","num":856,"t":["யாது மில்லை யுயிரிவை யாஞ்சொல்லும்","பூத மேயெனப் போந்திருந் தென்னொடு","வாதஞ் செய்கின்ற பூதமவ் வாதமோ","யாதைம் பூதங்க டம்முள்ளு மஃதினி."],"p":"","r":"She asks: if nothing is soul and only what he calls elements exist, then which of the five is the element that has come and sits debating with her, and which is that debate?","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["uyir","boothavaadham","vaadhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 865","lb":"NK865","ev":"","cl":[["the one who debates cannot be identified with any of the five elements","jain","asserted",3,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி",""],"ch":"nk:39"},"nk:866":{"book":"neelakesi","num":857,"t":["அளித்த வைந்திற் கறிவின்ப மாக்குவான்","களித்தற் காற்ற லுடையன காட்டினாய்","களித்தற் காரண காரிய மூர்த்தியா","லொளித்து நின்ற வுணர்வுரு வென்றியோ."],"p":"","r":"She says he has offered the intoxicating power of liquor to show how the five yield knowing and pleasure, and asks whether, since in liquor both cause and effect are material, he means that awareness lay hidden as a material form.","n":"The reply to 858: drunkenness is a change in matter produced by matter, so the comparison either makes awareness material or fails. Lines 3-4 are compressed and the turn of the argument is a reading.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["arivu","kal","boothavaadham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 866","lb":"NK866","ev":"","cl":[["the liquor analogy cannot explain awareness, since in liquor cause and effect are both material","jain","asserted",3,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி",""],"ch":"nk:39"},"nk:867":{"book":"neelakesi","num":858,"t":["உருவின் காரிய மேலுரு வென்றுண","ரருவின் காரிய மேலவா காயக்கா","மொருவன் காரிய மன்றுணர் வென்கின்றாய்","மருவுங் காரண காரிய மற்றெனோ."],"p":"","r":"She says the effect of what has form is form and the effect of the formless is formless, as with space, yet he says awareness is not the effect of any one thing; what then is its matching cause?","n":"The principle is that an effect shares the kind of its cause, so formless awareness cannot be the product of elements with form. Line 2's ஆகாயக்கு ஆம் is a division of a joined form.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["arivu","boothavaadham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 867","lb":"NK867","ev":"","cl":[["an effect is of the same kind as its cause, so formless awareness cannot come from elements with form","jain","asserted",1,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி",""],"ch":"nk:39"},"nk:868":{"book":"neelakesi","num":859,"t":["வையு மண்ணு மயிரு மலமுமோர்","பையுள் வைக்கப் பளிங்கும் பயக்குமோ","பொய்யைம் பூதம் புணர்ந்துணர் வோடின்பம்","செய்யு மென்பது சிந்திக்கற் பாலதோ."],"p":"","r":"She asks whether straw, earth, hair and dung put in a bag will yield crystal, and whether the claim that the five false elements joined make awareness and pleasure deserves a thought.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["boothavaadham","arivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 868","lb":"NK868","ev":"","cl":[["a mixture cannot produce a thing of a nature none of its parts has","jain","asserted",2,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி",""],"ch":"nk:39"},"nk:869":{"book":"neelakesi","num":860,"t":["கள்ளப் பூதமுங் காமிக்கும் பூதமும்","வள்ளற் பூதமும் மல்லவு மல்லவா","லுள்ளப் பூதமொன் றாக்குவ துண்மையைக்","கொள்ளப் பூதக் குணமவை யல்லவே."],"p":"","r":"She says there is no thieving element, lusting element or generous element, so to say that some mind-element produces these traits is false: they are not qualities of the elements.","n":"Moral character is her evidence that mind is not material: no element carries theft, desire or bounty as a quality, so these must belong to something else.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["gunam","manam","boothavaadham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 869","lb":"NK869","ev":"","cl":[["dispositions such as theft, lust and generosity are not qualities of any element","jain","asserted",4,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி",""],"ch":"nk:39"},"nk:870":{"book":"neelakesi","num":861,"t":["பொறியைந் தாலைந்து பூதத்தி னாகிய","வறிவைந் தாலைந்து மவ்வைந்திற் காகுமோ","பிறிதொன் றோபொருட் பெற்றிமற் றிற்றெனக்","குறிகொண் டாயொன்று கூறெனக் கென்னவே."],"p":"","r":"She asks him to mark the matter and say one thing: are the five knowings that come through the five senses, made of the five elements, the property of those five, or of something other?","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pori","arivu","uyir"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 870","lb":"NK870","ev":"","cl":[["the five kinds of sense-knowing must belong either to the elements severally or to a further knower","jain","asserted",3,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி",""],"ch":"nk:39"},"nk:871":{"book":"neelakesi","num":862,"t":["ஒன்றன் காரிய மொன்றென வொட்டினுஞ்","சென்றெ லாமவை சேதனை யாகுமே","யன்றெ லாமவை யாக்கமொன் றேயினி","யென்ற லானுமஃ தின்னுயி ரெய்துமே."],"p":"","r":"She says that even if one grants that each element's effect is its own sense-knowing, all of them would then be sentient; and if instead they all make up one product, the argument arrives at the soul.","n":"A dilemma: either every element is conscious (which he denies of dead matter) or the unity of knowing needs a single subject, which is the soul. The second horn's wording (யாக்கம் ஒன்றே) is compressed.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["uyir","arivu","boothavaadham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 871","lb":"NK871","ev":"","cl":[["either every element is conscious, or the unity of knowing requires a soul","jain","asserted",2,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி",""],"ch":"nk:39"},"nk:872":{"book":"neelakesi","num":863,"t":["அனைத்துப் பூதமு மேயறி வாக்கினான்","மனத்துக் கின்னுமோர் பூதத்தை மன்னுநீ","நினைத்துக் காணன்றி நேடியுங் காணையா","வெனக்கு நீசெய்வ தித்துணை யேயினி."],"p":"","r":"She says that if he makes all the elements into knowing, he must still posit another element for the mind; let him think it over or search it out, for that is all he can do against her now.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["manam","boothavaadham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 872","lb":"NK872","ev":"","cl":[["the materialist would need a further, sixth element to account for mind","jain","asserted",2,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி",""],"ch":"nk:39"},"nk:873":{"book":"neelakesi","num":864,"t":["பிண்ட மாகிப் பிறந்தன யாவையும்","உண்டுங் கண்டு முணர்ந்தவுஞ் செய்தவுங்","கொண்டு மீட்டவை கூறுதல் கூறுங்கால்","கண்ட பூதத்துக் காரிய மென்றியோ."],"p":"","r":"She asks whether, when beings born in a body later recall what they ate, saw, understood and did, he will say that this recollection is the effect of an element that can be seen.","n":"Memory is the evidence here: recollection carries the past into the present, which no perceptible element is seen to do.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["uyir","arivu","boothavaadham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 873","lb":"NK873","ev":"","cl":[["memory of past experience is not the effect of any perceptible element","jain","asserted",4,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி",""],"ch":"nk:39"},"nk:874":{"book":"neelakesi","num":865,"t":["பிறந்த நாள்களுட் பிள்ளையு மல்லவு","மறிந்து தாய்முலை யோடல்ல வுண்டிக","ளறிந்த வாறென்கொ லோவைந்து பூதமுஞ்","செறிந்த நாள்களுட் செய்வன வல்லவால்."],"p":"","r":"She asks how, in the first days after birth, the infant and other young know to run to the mother's breast and not to other food, since the five elements just come together in those days cannot do that.","n":"Instinct as proof of a prior knower: the newborn's seeking of the breast is knowledge it was not taught in this body. The verse does not say 'former birth', but the argument points there, and the materialist concedes birth at 893.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["uyir","arivu","piravi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 874","lb":"NK874","ev":"","cl":[["a newborn's knowing to seek the breast shows knowledge not produced by the elements of this birth","jain","asserted",3,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி",""],"ch":"nk:39"},"nk:875":{"book":"neelakesi","num":866,"t":["புத்தி யானின்றிப் பூதத்தி னாயவேற்","பத்து மல்லவும் பன்றிக்கு நாய்கட்கு","மொத்த தன்மைய பன்மைய குட்டிகள்","வித்தி னாய வினைவிகற் பாம்பிற."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi argues that if beings came from the elements alone, with no knowing principle, the many pups of one litter of pigs or dogs would all be alike; their differences come from deeds, as from a seed.","n":"Same parents, same food, same elements, yet the litter differs: the Jain answer puts the difference in each soul's own karma. The materialist's view is given as the Jain poem states it, not in a materialist's own words. The construal of புத்தி யானின்றி (line 1) as 'without a knowing principle' is mine.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vinai","uyir","boothavaadham","piravi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 875","lb":"NK875","ev":"","cl":[["living beings are produced by the elements alone, with no knowing principle","lokayata","refuted",1,"neelakesi"],["the differences among the young of one litter come from their own deeds","jain","asserted",4,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","பிசாசிகன்"],"ch":"nk:40"},"nk:876":{"book":"neelakesi","num":867,"t":["குறைந்து பூதங்கள் கூட்டமுண் டாமவ","ணுறைந்த பூதத் துணர்வல்ல தின்மையா","லறைந்த பூதங்க ளைந்துமங் கில்லெனின்","மறைந்த பூதத்தி லுண்மைவந் தெய்துமால்."],"p":"","r":"She presses that if an aggregate of elements can form with some elements lacking, and awareness is only the elements' own, then where the five she names are not all present the materialist must bring in a hidden element to supply the awareness.","n":"A reductio: the doctrine that admits only the seen elements would need an unseen one. The verse is compressed and the steps are my reconstruction.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["boothavaadham","arivu","vaadhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 876","lb":"NK876","ev":"","cl":[["awareness is nothing but a property of the elements gathered together","lokayata","refuted",2,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","பிசாசிகன்"],"ch":"nk:40"},"nk:877":{"book":"neelakesi","num":868,"t":["நீருங் காற்றுமல் லானில மில்லையோ","வூருஞ் சங்கினோ டூர்மச்சி மூக்சில","தேரி யுண்டலிற் றீயுமுண் டில்லைகண்","காரி யஞ்செவி காணலங் காயக்கென்."],"p":"","r":"Neelakesi asks how creatures such as the crawling conch, which have earth, water and wind in their bodies and fire too since they eat, still lack the eye and the ear, if each sense were made by an element.","n":"She turns the Jain grading of beings by the number of their senses against the materialist's scheme in 857, where each element yields its sense: the lower creatures have the elements but not the senses. Line 2 (ஊர் மச்சி மூக்சில) is hard to divide; 'lacking the nose' is a guess.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pori","boothavaadham","uyir"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 877","lb":"NK877","ev":"","cl":[["each of the five senses is made from one of the five elements","lokayata","refuted",4,"neelakesi"],["some beings, like the conch, have fewer senses though their bodies hold all the elements","jain","asserted",2,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","பிசாசிகன்"],"ch":"nk:40"},"nk:878":{"book":"neelakesi","num":869,"t":["ஒன்றொன் றாக வுணர்ச்சி முறைமையாற்","சென்று பூதங்கள் சேர்வதற் கேதுவென்","குன்றித் தத்தமுள் யாவையுங் கூடுமே","னின்ற மெய்ம்மை நினதென நேர்வல்யான்."],"p":"","r":"She asks what cause brings the elements together one by one in the order of awareness, and says mockingly that if they can shrink and all merge into one another she will grant that his truth stands.","n":"The objection is that combination needs a cause the materialist does not name; the closing concession is ironic.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["boothavaadham","vaadhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 878","lb":"NK878","ev":"","cl":[["the elements come together of themselves to make awareness","lokayata","refuted",1,"neelakesi"],["the joining of the elements needs a cause beyond the elements","jain","asserted",2,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","பிசாசிகன்"],"ch":"nk:40"},"nk:879":{"book":"neelakesi","num":870,"t":["ஐந்து பூத மவற்றவற் றாலைந்து","மிந்தி யங்க ளியையு மிவையெனுஞ்","சிந்த னையொழி நீயெனச் செப்பினா","ளுய்ந்து வாழு முயிருண்மை யொட்டினாள்."],"p":"","r":"Telling him to give up the thought that the five senses each arise from one of the five elements, Neelakesi establishes the reality of the soul that lives on.","n":"The narrator's ஒட்டினாள் (line 4) reports the outcome as won: the soul's existence is set up against the materialist's reduction of sense to element in 857.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["uyir","pori","boothavaadham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 879","lb":"NK879","ev":"","cl":[["the five senses arise each from one of the five elements","lokayata","refuted",1,"neelakesi"],["a living soul exists","jain","asserted",4,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","பிசாசிகன்"],"ch":"nk:40"},"nk:880":{"book":"neelakesi","num":871,"t":["உணர்வு மின்பமு மோரறி வாதிக்கட்","புணரு மேயெனிற் பொய்தொகைக் கண்ணென","லுணர்வு மின்பமு முள்வழி யேயுயிர்","புணரு மென்றனள் போதன கண்ணினாள்."],"p":"","r":"She says that if awareness and pleasure are granted even to the one-sensed beings, the claim that they are a mere aggregate is false: wherever awareness and pleasure are, a soul is joined there.","n":"ஓரறிவு (line 1) is the Jain term for the one-sensed beings, plants and the like; awareness in the lowest form of life is made the mark of a soul.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["uyir","arivu","pori"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 880","lb":"NK880","ev":"","cl":[["wherever there is awareness and pleasure, a soul is joined there","jain","asserted",3,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","பிசாசிகன்"],"ch":"nk:40"},"nk:881":{"book":"neelakesi","num":872,"t":["காற்றி னாலுடம் பாமெனிற காற்றினே","தோற்றி னாலுயிர் தான்றொகை யென்செயு","மாற்றிவ் வைந்தினு மாருயி ராமெனு","மாற்ற மாயின் மனங்கொளற் பாலதே."],"p":"","r":"If the body comes from wind, she argues, what work is left for the aggregate in making the soul; but if he says the soul is something apart from these five, that would be worth accepting.","n":"Taking மாற்றி (line 3) as 'apart from' makes the verse an opening for the opponent to concede; the sense of line 2 is uncertain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["uyir","yaakkai","boothavaadham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 881","lb":"NK881","ev":"","cl":[["the body and its life come from wind","lokayata","refuted",1,"neelakesi"],["the soul is other than the five elements","jain","asserted",3,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","பிசாசிகன்"],"ch":"nk:40"},"nk:882":{"book":"neelakesi","num":873,"t":["வேறு வேறைந்து பூதமு மெய்ம்மையா","லீறுந் தோற்றமு மில்லுயி ராயின","பாறி யாவையும் பாழ்த்தொழி யும்மெனக்","கூறி னாயது குற்ற முடைத்தரோ."],"p":"","r":"She calls faulty his claim that, since the five elements are real and separate and there is no soul without end or origin, everything falls apart and comes to nothing when they part.","n":"This is the materialist's account of death: the elements disperse and nothing survives. Here the reply is only 'it has a fault'; the following verses give the reasons.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["boothavaadham","saakkaadu","uyir"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 882","lb":"NK882","ev":"","cl":[["when the elements part at death all is scattered and nothing remains","lokayata","refuted",3,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","பிசாசிகன்"],"ch":"nk:40"},"nk:883":{"book":"neelakesi","num":874,"t":["குழவிக் காலத்துக் கூறின யாவையுங்","கீழவுக் காலத்துக்கண்ணவை கேட்டலா","லிழவெக் காலு மிலானினி யார்களைத்","தொழுவிப் பானங்கோர் தோன்றலுந் தோன்றுமே."],"p":"","r":"She argues that what is heard in infancy is remembered in old age, so there is a knower that never loses it, and asks who else would make men bow in worship.","n":"The argument from memory: the body's elements change from childhood to old age, but what was learned stays, so the one who holds it is not the elements. Lines 3 and 4 are obscure and the second half of the reading is a guess; கீழவு (line 2) reads as கிழவு, old age.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["uyir","arivu","boothavaadham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 883","lb":"NK883","ev":"","cl":[["memory carried from infancy to old age shows a lasting knower","jain","asserted",2,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","பிசாசிகன்"],"ch":"nk:40"},"nk:884":{"book":"neelakesi","num":875,"t":["துன்பந் தூய்மையுந் துட்கென வுட்கலும்","அன்பு மானமு மாயமு மாண்மையு","மின்பு மென்றிவை யாக்கிய தீதென","முன்பு நின்று முறுக்கவும் வல்லையோ."],"p":"","r":"She challenges him to stand before her and insist that pain, purity, fear and dread, love, honour, deceit, courage and pleasure are made by fire.","n":"The list mixes feelings with moral qualities (purity, honour, deceit): the point is that the moral life cannot be credited to an element.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["manam","uyir","boothavaadham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 884","lb":"NK884","ev":"","cl":[["feelings and moral qualities are not made by the material elements","jain","asserted",3,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","பிசாசிகன்"],"ch":"nk:40"},"nk:885":{"book":"neelakesi","num":876,"t":["கனவு மந்திரம் சிந்தனை வாழுநாள்","வினவு சோதிடங் கேட்டுரை புட்குரல்","அனகள் யாவையு மென்னைநின் பூதங்க","ளனகொ னீயிங் கறிந்தனை சொல்லென."],"p":"","r":"She asks whether dream, mantra, thought, the span of life, the astrology people consult and the omen of bird cries are his elements, and bids him say if he knows.","n":"She counts omens, astrology and mantra as real things the elements cannot explain: the debater argues from what the world already trusts, not from Jain doctrine alone.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kanavu","mandhiram","boothavaadham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 885","lb":"NK885","ev":"","cl":[["dream, mantra, thought, life-span, astrology and bird-omen are real yet are not elements","jain","asserted",3,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","பிசாசிகன்"],"ch":"nk:40"},"nk:886":{"book":"neelakesi","num":877,"t":["வைத்த வத்து மறுபிறப் பிற்றமர்க்","குய்த்துக் காட்டுத லேலுல கொட்டுமா","லெத்தி றத்தினு மில்லுயு ரென்றிநீ","செத்து வம்மெய்திற் சில்லைமை செய்பவால்."],"p":"","r":"She says the world accepts it when one reborn leads his kin to the thing he had laid away in his former life, and that though he says there is no soul in any form, the dead come back and do mischief.","n":"The reborn man finding his own hidden treasure is offered as evidence outside the claimant's own mind. Line 4 (செத்து வம்மெய்திற் சில்லைமை செய்பவால்) is hard to construe; 'the dead come back' is my reading. உலகு ஒட்டும் (line 2) makes common acceptance the test.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["piravi","marumai","uyir","anangu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 886","lb":"NK886","ev":"","cl":[["one reborn can show his kin what he hid in a former life","jain","asserted",1,"neelakesi"],["there is no soul in any form","lokayata","refuted",3,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","பிசாசிகன்"],"ch":"nk:40"},"nk:887":{"book":"neelakesi","num":878,"t":["பேயு மில்லை பிறப்பது மில்லென்பாய்","வாயுங் கிள்ளிப் புடைப்ப வருவதென்","மாயத் தாலன்றி மந்திரத் தாற்றெய்வங்","கூயக் காலறங் கோடலை யொட்டென."],"p":"","r":"She asks the man who says there are no ghosts and no rebirth why then a possessed person pinches his mouth and beats himself, and tells him to grant that a deity called by mantra, and not by trickery, comes and seizes.","n":"Possession is offered as a public sign of unseen beings. The phrase அறங்கோடல் (line 4) is unclear; reading it as the deity's seizing is a guess.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["anangu","mandhiram","piravi","vaadhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 887","lb":"NK887","ev":"","cl":[["there are no ghosts and no rebirth","lokayata","refuted",1,"neelakesi"],["a deity summoned by mantra truly comes and possesses","jain","asserted",3,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","பிசாசிகன்"],"ch":"nk:40"},"nk:888":{"book":"neelakesi","num":879,"t":["ஒட்டி னேனென் றுரைப்ப வுணர்விலா","முட்டை காண்கென முனகை முறுக்கியே","சுட்டி னாளங்குத் தோற்றமு நோக்கென","விட்டுத் தான்றன் விகுர்வணை காட்டுவாள்."],"p":"","r":"When he says he will accept it, she points and bids him look, and putting aside her assumed form she shows him her power of transformation.","n":"The materialist who trusts only the seen is given something to see: the demoness who has argued all the debates in a woman's form reveals what she is. விகுர்வணை (line 4) is the Jain word for this power of changing form. Line 2 (உணர்விலா முட்டை காண்கென முனகை முறுக்கியே) is obscure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["anangu","meyyunarvu","vaadhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 888","lb":"NK888","ev":"Pisasikan says he will accept the claim if shown; Neelakesi points and, dropping her assumed form, shows him her transformation.","cl":[],"ag":["பிசாசிகன்","நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"ch":"nk:40"},"nk:889":{"book":"neelakesi","num":880,"t":["கழுதுங் காணலராகிக் கலங்கியே","யழுதுஞ் சாப வகலிடத் தாரிவன்","முழுதுங் காணின் முடியு மெனமுன்னி","வழுவில் வாயும் வளைபல்லுந் தோற்றலும்."],"p":"","r":"Knowing that even spirits and the people of the wide world weep and die at that sight, and that he would die if he saw it whole, she shows him only her mouth and her curved teeth.","n":"Her restraint is the first act of care in the scene: she measures out the proof so as not to kill the man she is converting.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["anangu","acham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 889","lb":"NK889","ev":"Neelakesi, judging that the full sight would kill him, shows Pisasikan only her mouth and curved teeth.","cl":[],"ag":["நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"ch":"nk:40"},"nk:890":{"book":"neelakesi","num":881,"t":["கண் புதைத்துக் கவிழ்ந்தனன் வழவே","திண் பதத்திற் றெருட்டி யெடுத்திரீஇப்","பண்பு தக்கன சொல்லிப் பரியனின்","நண்ப தென்று நடுக்கமுந் தீர்த்தபின்."],"p":"","r":"He covers his eyes and falls face down; she lifts him, seats him, speaks fitting gentle words, tells him she is his friend, and calms his trembling.","n":"Line 1's வழவே is unclear; the gesture sequence is plain.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["acham","anangu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 890","lb":"NK890","ev":"Pisasikan covers his eyes and falls; Neelakesi raises him, speaks kindly as his friend, and stills his fear.","cl":[],"ag":["பிசாசிகன்","நீலகேசி"],"ec":[],"ch":"nk:40"},"nk:891":{"book":"neelakesi","num":882,"t":["பேய்கண் டாயதன் பெற்றி யுரையென","வாய்கண் டேனென்னை வாழ்க்கை வலியன்மின்","நோய்கொண் டேனென வஞ்ச னுனக்கவ","டாய்கண் டாயென்றுஞ் சாதலில் லையென."],"p":"","r":"Asked to tell what the ghost he saw was like, he says he saw its mouth, begs not to be pressed, and says he is sick with it; she tells him not to fear, that she is a mother to him, and that there is never any death.","n":"என்றும் சாதலில்லை (line 4) carries the doctrinal point of the scene into comfort: what he fears ends nothing. Who says each phrase is fixed from the dialogue, and the reading of line 3's அவள் தாய் as 'a mother to you' is a guess.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["anangu","acham","saakkaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 891","lb":"NK891","ev":"Neelakesi asks Pisasikan to describe the ghost; he says he saw its mouth and is ill with fear, and she reassures him.","cl":[["there is never death for the soul","jain","asserted",4,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி","பிசாசிகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"nk:40"},"nk:892":{"book":"neelakesi","num":883,"t":["பெற்ற பேரும் பிசாசிக னென்பதே","யற்ற மின்றி யவட்கு மகனைநீ","குற்ற மில்லறங் கொள்ளின்மற் றெம்மொடு","சுற்ற மாதலிற் சொல்லெனச் சொல்லுவான்."],"p":"","r":"She tells him that the very name he bears, Pisasikan, makes him without question the ghost's son, and that if he takes the faultless dharma he is kin to her and her own, and bids him speak; and he speaks.","n":"A pun: பிசாசிகன் means 'the ghost's one', so the denier of ghosts was named for them; the demoness adopts him through dharma. Taking the dharma makes him kin (சுற்றம்) of those who keep it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aram","anangu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 892","lb":"NK892","ev":"Neelakesi tells Pisasikan his name makes him her son and that taking the dharma makes him her kin, and asks him to speak.","cl":[["one who takes the faultless dharma becomes kin of those who keep it","jain","asserted",3,"neelakesi"]],"ag":["நீலகேசி","பிசாசிகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலகேசி","பிசாசிகன்"],"ch":"nk:40"},"nk:893":{"book":"neelakesi","num":884,"t":["பிறப்பும் பேயு முதலாப் பிறகளுந்","திறத்தி னீசொன்ன யாவையுந் தேறிநின்","றறத்தை யானு மமைவரக் கொண்டனென்","மறக்க லேனினி மன்னுமை யானென."],"p":"","r":"He says he has understood and holds firm all she told him, birth, ghosts and the rest, has taken the dharma as it should be taken, and will not forget it now.","n":"The convert names first what he had denied (பிறப்பு, பேய்): the debate ends not with silence but with assent to its two disputed facts.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aram","thelivu","piravi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 893","lb":"NK893","ev":"Pisasikan accepts rebirth and ghosts and takes the dharma.","cl":[["rebirth and ghosts are real","jain","asserted",1,"pisasikan"]],"ag":["பிசாசிகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["பிசாசிகன்","நீலகேசி"],"ch":"nk:40"},"nk:894":{"book":"neelakesi","num":885,"t":["பேந்தரு தோற்றப் பிசாசிக னிற்றென","வேந்துமவ் வேத்தவை யாரும் வியப்பெய்தி","யாய்ந்த கேள்வியி னாளை யையா யென","மாந்தர் யாரு மதித்தன ரென்பவே."],"p":"","r":"The narrator says that when Pisasikan was thus won, the king and all that royal assembly marvelled, and all the people honoured the woman of searching learning as their lady.","n":"This closes the last debate of the work. பேந்தரு தோற்றம் (line 1) is obscure; it may describe the fear-struck look of the converted man.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vaadhu","avai","perumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Neelakesi 894","lb":"NK894","ev":"The king and his assembly marvel at Pisasikan's conversion, and all honour Neelakesi.","cl":[],"ag":["பிசாசிகன்","மதனசித்தன்","அவை"],"ec":[],"ch":"nk:40"},"so:1":{"book":"soolamani","num":1,"t":["வென்றான் வினையின் தொகையாய விரிந்து தன்கண்","ஒன்றாய்ப் பரந்த வுணர்வின்னொழி யாது முற்றும்","சென்றான் திகழுஞ் சுடர்சூழொளி மூர்த்தி யாகி","நின்றா னடிக்கீழ் பணிந்தார் வினை நீங்கி நின்றார்."],"p":"","r":"The poet praises the one who conquered the whole sum of his deeds, whose knowledge spread wide, filled everything and lost nothing, and who now stands as a shining form in a ring of light; those who bowed at his feet were freed of their deeds and stand released.","n":"The verse says that the devotees' deeds left them after they bowed (வினை நீங்கி நின்றார்) but does not say whether the bowing or the god's example does it; the god is praised for what he conquered, not for any giving. The verse does not name him; the Jain Arhat is read from வென்றான் வினையின் தொகை and from the next verse's link to the story's hero.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","iraivan","thiruvadi","veedu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1","lb":"SO1","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["the god conquered the whole sum of karma by a knowledge that spread through all things","jain","asserted",1,"narrator"],["those who bow at his feet are freed of their karma","jain","asserted",4,"narrator"]],"ag":["வென்றான்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:1"},"so:2":{"book":"soolamani","num":2,"t":["அங்கண் ணுலகிற் கணிவான்சுட ராகி நின்றான்","வெங்கண் வினைபோழ்ந் திருவச் சரண் சென்ற மேனாள்","பைங்கண் மதர்வைப் பகுவாயரி யேறு போழ்ந்த","செங்கண் ணெடியான் சரிதம்மிது செப்ப லுற்றேன்."],"p":"","r":"The poet says the god stood as a light of the world above, having split fierce deeds, and that he begins the story of the red-eyed tall one, who in an earlier day went for refuge to him and who tore open the green-eyed lion with gaping mouth.","n":"Line 1 அணிவான்சுடர் reads கணிவான்சுடர் in this text and may be a misprint. The grammar that joins the refuge (சரண் சென்ற மேனாள்) to the red-eyed tall one in line 4 is loose, so the hero's earlier refuge is the likely reading, not a fixed one. The hero is named only by epithet here; the Jain Vasudeva of the story is Tivittan.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","vinai","oli"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 2","lb":"SO2","mm":1,"ev":"Tivittan, the red-eyed tall one, tears open the lion (recalled as a feat of his past); the poet announces that he will tell his story.","cl":[["the god split the fierce karma and stands as a light of the world above","jain","asserted",2,"narrator"]],"ag":["செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:1"},"so:3":{"book":"soolamani","num":3,"t":["கொற்றங்கொ ணேமி நெடுமால்குணங் கூற விப்பால்","உற்றிங்கொர் காதல் கிளரத்தமிழ் நூற்க லுற்றேன்","மற்றிங்கொர் குற்றம் வருமாயினு நங்கள் போல்வார்","அற்றங்கள் காப்பா ரறிவிற்பெரி யார்க ளன்றே."],"p":"","r":"The poet says that, to tell the qualities of the tall one with the victorious discus, a desire has risen in him to spin a Tamil work here, and that if a flaw should come, great ones of knowledge are those who guard the lapses of people like himself.","n":"நேமி நெடுமால் is the discus-bearing tall one, taken as the same hero as the செங்கண் நெடியான் of verses 2 and 5, and not as a god of worship; the Tamil does not say so here. அற்றங்கள் காப்பார் is guard the lapses, which is a different act from the Cintamani's washing of the flaw.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thamizh","kutram","saandror"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 3","lb":"SO3","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["the wise guard the lapses of lesser people rather than exposing them","general","asserted",4,"narrator"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:1"},"so:4":{"book":"soolamani","num":4,"t":["நாமாண் புரைக்குங் குறையென்னிது நாம வென்வேல்","தேமா ணலங்கற் றிருமால்நெடுஞ் சேந்த னென்னும்","தூமாண் தமிழின் கிழவன்சுட ரார மார்பின்","கோமா னவையுட் டெருண்டார்கொளப் பட்ட தன்றே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks what fault there is in this speaking of excellence, since it was accepted by the discerning in the assembly of the king, Tirumal Nedunjendan, called lord of pure Tamil, with his victorious spear and cool garland.","n":"Whether Tirumal Nedunjendan is the poet's patron, the king in whose assembly the work was first heard, or both is not fixed by the verse; கோமான் அவை may be his assembly or a king's assembly he belongs to. No claim rests on the identity.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thamizh","avai","kutram","saandror"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 4","lb":"SO4","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:1"},"so:5":{"book":"soolamani","num":5,"t":["செங்கண் ணெடியான் றிறம்பேசிய சிந்தை செய்த","நங்கண் மறுவும் மறுவன்றுநல் லார்கண் முன்னர்","அங்கண் விசும்பி நிருள்போழ்ந்தகல் வானெ ழுந்த","திங்கண் மறுவுஞ் சிலர்கைதொழச் செல்லு மன்றே."],"p":"","r":"The poet says that even his fault, in the thought that told the story of the red-eyed tall one, is no fault before the good, as the moon's spot does not stop some from raising hands to it when it rises through the dark sky.","n":"The moon's spot is used as an excuse in the same way in Neelakesi 6 and Civaka Cintamani 4; here it is the moon's rising before worshippers that matters, so the blemish is overlooked and not cleaned off.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kutram","saandror","perumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 5","lb":"SO5","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["the faults of a poet's work do not count before the good, as the moon's spot does not stop its worship","general","asserted",3,"narrator"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:1"},"so:6":{"book":"soolamani","num":6,"t":["விஞ்சைக் கிறைவன் விரைசூழ்முடி வேந்தன் மங்கை","பஞ்சிக் கனுங்குச் சிலம்பாரடிப் பாவை பூவார்","வஞ்சிக் கொடிபோல் பவள்காரண மாக வந்த","செஞ்சொற் புராணத் துரையின்வழிச் சேறு மன்றே."],"p":"","r":"The poet says that he will go by the account of the Purana of fair words, which came about because of the daughter of the Vidyadhara king, who is like a vanji creeper with anklets on her feet.","n":"The verse gives the poem's source as an older purana and not as a tale of his own invention; the Vidyadhara king's daughter is the figure through whom that purana is said to have arisen, and is not an agent here. The verse names no purana.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sol","thamizh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 6","lb":"SO6","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:1"},"so:7":{"book":"soolamani","num":7,"t":["மஞ்சுசூழ் மணிவரை யெடுத்த மாலமர்","இஞ்சிசூ ழணிநக ரிருக்கை நாடது","விஞ்சைந்ண ளுலகுடன் விழாக்கொண் டன்னது","துஞ்சுந்ணணள் நிதியது சுரமை யென்பவே."],"p":"","r":"The country is a city set round with a jewelled hill, rivalling the Vidyadhara world in festival, and its wealth is said never to sleep; the name Surumai ends the verse.","n":"The last line's wealth-that-sleeps and the name சுரமை are hard to construe after the edition's repair of line 4.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["naadu","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 7","lb":"SO7","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:2"},"so:8":{"book":"soolamani","num":8,"t":["பங்கயங் காடுகொண் டலர்ந்த பாங்கெலாம்","செங்கய லினநிரை திளைக்குஞ் செல்வமும்","மங்கையர் முகத்தன மதர்த்த வாளரி","அங்கயர் பிறழ்ச்சியு மறாத நீரவே."],"p":"","r":"Lotus beds, shoals of red carp and the women's faces and eyes are set side by side, so the land's waters and its women share one beauty.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naadu","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 8","lb":"SO8","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:2"},"so:9":{"book":"soolamani","num":9,"t":["ஆங்கவ ரணிநடை யன்னத் தோட்டன","தீங்குரன் மழலையாற் சிலம்புந் தண்பணை","வீங்கிள முலையவர் மெல்லென் சீறடி","ஓங்கிருஞ் சிலம்பினாற் சிலம்பு மூர்களே."],"p":"","r":"Women's swan-like walk and soft speech make the cool fields ring, and the villages ring in turn with the anklets on their small feet.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naadu","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 9","lb":"SO9","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["மங்கையர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:2"},"so:10":{"book":"soolamani","num":10,"t":["நிழலகந் தவழ்ந்துதே னிரந்து தாதுசேர்","பொழிலகம் பூவையுங் கிளியும் பாடுமே","குழலகங் குடைந்துவண் டுறங்குங் கோதையர்","மழலையும் யாழுமே மலிந்த மாடமே."],"p":"","r":"Bees and parrots fill the groves while the houses are full of the speech and lutes of women.","n":"Line 2 has a repaired letter; the sense is plain.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naadu","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 10","lb":"SO10","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["மங்கையர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:2"},"so:11":{"book":"soolamani","num":11,"t":["காவியும் குவளையு நெகிழ்ந்து கள்ளுமிழ்","ஆவியுண் டடர்த்ததே னகத்து மங்கையர்","நாவியுங் குழம்பு முண் ணகில நற்றவம்","மேவிநின் றவரையு மெலிய விம்முமே."],"p":"","r":"Scented water-flowers and women's perfumes are so heavy that they weaken even the ascetics who stand in good austerity.","n":"The poet lets the land's sweetness reach the austere: a quiet admission that the senses test the renouncer, in a work whose later sarukkams praise renunciation.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","thavam","naadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 11","lb":"SO11","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["மங்கையர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:2"},"so:12":{"book":"soolamani","num":12,"t":["வானிலங் கருவிய வரையு முல்லைவாய்த்","தேனிலங் கருவிய திணையுந தேரல்சேர்","பானலங் கழனியுங் கடலும் பாங்கணி","நானிலங் கலந்துபொன்னரலு நாடதே."],"p":"","r":"Mountain, forest, farmland and shore, the four regions, are all found together in this one land.","n":"Line 2 reads திணையுந தேரல்சேர் in the edition (a likely misprint) and line 4 ends oddly; the four-region claim is clear.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naadu","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 12","lb":"SO12","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:2"},"so:13":{"book":"soolamani","num":13,"t":["முன்றி லெங்கு முருகயர் பாணியும்","சென்று வீழரு வித்திர ளோசையும்","வென்றி வேழ முழக்கொடு கூடிவான்","ஒன்றி நின்றதி ரும்மொரு பாலெலாம்."],"p":"","r":"Festival drums, falling streams and the roar of elephants together fill the air on one side of the land.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naadu","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 13","lb":"SO13","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:2"},"so:14":{"book":"soolamani","num":14,"t":["ஏறு கொண்டெறி யும்பணைக் கோவலர்","கூறு கொண்டெழு கொன்றையந் தீங்குழல்","காறு கொண்டவர் கம்பலை யென்றிவை","மாரு கொண்டுசி லம்புமொர் மாடெலாம்."],"p":"","r":"On one side of the land the cowherds' drums, their sweet konrai-flutes and their shouting fill the pastures.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naadu","uzhavu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 14","lb":"SO14","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["கோவலர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:2"},"so:15":{"book":"soolamani","num":15,"t":["அணங்க னாரண வாடல் முழவமும்","கணங்கொள் வாரணக் கம்பலைச் செல்வமும்","மணங்கொள் வார்முர சும்வய லோதையும்","இணங்கி யெங்கு மிருக்குமொர் பாலெலாம்."],"p":"","r":"On another side the sound of festival drums, flocks of birds and the plough-song of the fields is joined.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naadu","uzhavu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 15","lb":"SO15","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:2"},"so:16":{"book":"soolamani","num":16,"t":["கலவ ரின்னிய முங்கட லச்சிறார்","புலவு நீர்ப்பொரு பூணெறி பூசலும்","நிலவு வெண்மண னீளிருங் கானல்வாய்","உலவு மோதமு மோங்குமொர் பாலெலாம்."],"p":"","r":"On the coast the music of the fishers, the children's play, the shore's noise and the surge of waves are all heard.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naadu","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 16","lb":"SO16","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["சிறார்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:2"},"so:17":{"book":"soolamani","num":17,"t":["கைவி ரிந்தன காந்தளும் பூஞ்சுனை","மைவி ரிந்தன நீலமும் வான்செய்நாண்","மெய்வி ரிந்தன வேங்கையும் சோர்ந்ததேன்","நெய்வி ரிந்தன நீளிருங் குன்றெலாம்."],"p":"","r":"Kandal, blue lilies and vengai flower on the hills, and the honey of the high slopes runs thick like ghee.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naadu","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 17","lb":"SO17","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:2"},"so:18":{"book":"soolamani","num":18,"t":["கொன்றை யுங்குருந் துங்குலைக் கோடலும்","முன்றி லேறிய முல்லையம் பந்தரும்","நின்று தேன்நிரந் தூதவி ரிந்தரோ","மன்றெ லாமண நாரும ருங்கினே."],"p":"","r":"Konrai, kurundu, kotal and the jasmine bowers fill the village commons with scent and drip honey.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naadu","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 18","lb":"SO18","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:2"},"so:19":{"book":"soolamani","num":19,"t":["நாற விண்டன நெய்தலு நாண்மதுச்","சேறு விண்டசெந் தாமரைக் கானமும்","ஏறி வண்டின மூன்றவி ழிந்ததேன்","ஊறி வந்தொழு கும்மொரு பாலெலாம்."],"p":"","r":"The seashore's lilies, red lotus beds and the bees on them pour out honey that flows everywhere on one side of the land.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naadu","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 19","lb":"SO19","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:2"},"so:20":{"book":"soolamani","num":20,"t":["கோடு டைந்தன தாழையுங் கோழிருள்","மோடு டைந்தன மூரிக் குவளையும்","தோடு டைந்தன சூகமுங் கற்பகக்","காடு டைந்தன போன்றுள கானலே."],"p":"","r":"The seaside groves, with their screwpine, blue lilies and parrots, look like a wishing-tree forest.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naadu","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 20","lb":"SO20","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:2"},"so:21":{"book":"soolamani","num":21,"t":["நீல வால வட்டத்தி னிறங்கொளக்","கோலும் பீலிய கோடுயர் குன்றின்மேல்","ஆலு மாமழை நீள்முகி லார்த்தொறும்","ஆலு மாமயி லாலுமொர் பாலெலாம்."],"p":"","r":"On the high hills, peacocks dance whenever the great clouds roar over the peaks.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naadu","mazhai","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 21","lb":"SO21","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:2"},"so:22":{"book":"soolamani","num":22,"t":["நக்க முல்லையு நாகிளங் கொன்றையும்","உக்க தாதடர் கொண்டொலி வண்டறா","எக்க ரீர்மணற் கிண்டியி ளம்பெடைப்","பக்க நோக்கும் பறவையொர் பாலெலாம்."],"p":"","r":"On the sandy banks the jasmine and the young konrai drop their pollen thick under ceaselessly humming bees, and in every such spot the birds scratch the damp sand and look to their young mates beside them.","n":"Pure scene-setting inside the country description (நாட்டுச் சருக்கம்): the birds are shown as pairs with their young mates, a quiet picture of ease with no event or claim.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naadu","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 22","lb":"SO22","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:3"},"so:23":{"book":"soolamani","num":23,"t":["துள்ளி றாக்கவுட் கொண்டு சுரும்பொடு","கள்ள றாதசெந் தாமரைக் கானகத்","துள்ள றாதுதைந் தோகை யிரட்டுறப்","புள்ள றதுபு லம்பின பொய்கையே."],"p":"","r":"A pond lies thick with red lotus whose honey never fails, and with bees and birds crowding its thicket the water-birds cry out over it without pause.","n":"The first two lines are hard (துள்ளிறாக்கவுட்கொண்டு, உள்ளறாதுதைந்தோகை) and line 4 reads புள்ளறது, which looks like a misprint for புள் அறாது 'the birds without ceasing'; the general picture of a bird-filled lotus pond is clear, the word-by-word sense is not.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["naadu","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 23","lb":"SO23","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:3"},"so:24":{"book":"soolamani","num":24,"t":["வெண்மு ளைப்பசுந் தாமரை மென்சுருள்","முண்மு ளைத்திர ளோடு முனிந்துகொண்","டுண்மு ளைத்திள வன்ன முழக்கலால்","கண்மு ளைத்த தடத்த கழியெலாம்."],"p":"","r":"Young swans, stirring angrily among the white-shooted green lotus and its thorny stems, churn the roots and make the backwaters of the tank seem to sprout eyes.","n":"The sense of the last line (கண்முளைத்த தடத்த கழியெலாம்) is not fixed: it may say the backwaters are full of the bud-eyes of the lotus, or that they are alive with the swans' sprouting wakefulness; the angry swans (முனிந்து) are the one sure action.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["naadu","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 24","lb":"SO24","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:3"},"so:25":{"book":"soolamani","num":25,"t":["காந்த ளங்குலை யாற்களி வண்டினம்","கூந்தி ளம்பிடி வீசக்கு ழாங்களோ","டேந்து சந்தனச் சர லிருங்கைமா","மாந்தி நின்றுறங் கும்வரை மாடெலாம்,"],"p":"","r":"On the hill-flanks, where bees hum over the kanthal clusters and the she-elephants fan their herd, great elephants with raised trunks drink the sandal-scented flow and stand asleep.","n":"Line 3 and the grammar linking the elephants to the sandal and to the fanning are obscure (ஏந்து சந்தனச் சரல் இருங்கை), and the verse ends in a comma in the text; it is the fullest hill-country picture of the chapter but its syntax is partly guessed.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["naadu","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 25","lb":"SO25","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:3"},"so:26":{"book":"soolamani","num":26,"t":["தார்செய் கொன்றை தளித்ததண் டேறலுண்","டேர்செய் கின்ற விளம்பு லிருங்குழைக்","கார்செய் காலை கறித்தொறு மெல்லவே","போர்செய் மாவினம் பூத்தண்பு றணியே."],"p":"","r":"On the cool outskirts of the hills the herds drink the cool honey that the konrai garlands drop, then in the rainy season browse the tender dark leaves of the young tamarind, while the mango blossoms.","n":"The subject of கறித்தொறு and of போர்செய் மாவினம் (fighting herds of beasts, or of cattle) is not named; the verse may be about grazing cattle and deer or about fighting animals, and the sense given here is the likelier of the two.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["naadu","mazhai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 26","lb":"SO26","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:3"},"so:27":{"book":"soolamani","num":27,"t":["அள்ளி லைககுவ ளைத்தடம் மேய்ந்தசைஇக்","கள்ள லைத்தக வுட்கரு மேதிபால்","உள்ள லைத்தொழு கக்குடைந் துண்டலால்","புள்ள லைத்த புனலபு லங்களே."],"p":"","r":"Black buffaloes graze the kuvalai pool until they tire, then wade in so that their milk runs into the water, and the birds are driven off the pools of the fields.","n":"The last line (புள்ளலைத்த புனலபுலங்களே) is corrupt or badly divided in the text, and the verb for the milk (உள்ளலைத்தொழுக) is uncertain; only the buffaloes grazing in the kuvalai pool and the milk flowing into water can be read with confidence.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["naadu","uzhavu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 27","lb":"SO27","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:3"},"so:28":{"book":"soolamani","num":28,"t":["கெண்டை யஞ்சினை மேய்ந் து கிளர்ந்துபோய்","முண்ட கத்துறை சேர்ந்த முதலைமா","வண்டல் வார்கரை மாமக ரக்குழாம்","கண்டு நின்று கனலும் கழியெலாம்."],"p":"","r":"The crocodile that has fed on the spawn of the carp returns to the lotus ford, and the herd of great makara on the silty bank glare at it in anger along the whole backwater.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 28","lb":"SO28","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:3"},"so:29":{"book":"soolamani","num":29,"t":["கண்ணி லாங்கழை யின்கதிர்க் கற்றையும்","மண்ணி லாங்குரல் வார்தினை வாரியும்","எண்ணி லாங்கவி ளைவன வீட்டமும்","உண்ணி லாங்குல வாமை யுயர்ந்தவே."],"p":"","r":"Heaps of bamboo seed, of the long-eared millet, and of numberless other grains rise high in the houses, and do not grow less however much is eaten.","n":"The verse is about yield at rest: grain is already gathered and stored, with no farmer in sight, and the last clause (உண்ணிலாங் குலவாமை) says the store does not shrink as it is eaten, which is the poet's way of calling the land inexhaustible. The reading of line 1 (கண்ணிலாங்கழை) is uncertain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["uzhavu","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 29","lb":"SO29","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:3"},"so:30":{"book":"soolamani","num":30,"t":["பேழ்த்த காயின பேரெட் பிறங்கிணர்","தாழ்த்த காயின தண்ணவ ரைக்கொடி","சூழ்த்த காய்த்துவ ரைவர கென்றிவை","மூழ்த்த போன்றுள முல்லை நிலங்களே."],"p":"","r":"The mullai tracts seem overspread with the large pods of sesame in shining clusters, the hanging cool pods of the avarai, and the pods of the tuvarai crowded on their climbers.","n":"The mullai here is a farmed land of pod-crops, not the wild forest of the herdsmen's land; the verse names no person.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["uzhavu","naadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 30","lb":"SO30","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:3"},"so:31":{"book":"soolamani","num":31,"t":["மோடு கொண்டெழு மூரிக் கழைக்கரும்","பூடு கொண்ட பொதும்பரொ டுள்விராய்த்","தோடு கொண்டபைங் காய்துவள் செந்நெலின்","காடு கொண்டுள கண்ணக னாடெலாம்."],"p":"","r":"The wide lands are full of tall sugarcane rising in clumps, groves thick with shrubs, and red paddy bending under its green-filled ears.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["uzhavu","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 31","lb":"SO31","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:3"},"so:32":{"book":"soolamani","num":32,"t":["சங்கு நித்தில முத்தவ ழிப்பியும்","தெங்கந் தீங்குலை யூறிய தேறலும்","வங்க வாரியும் வாரலை வாரியும்","தங்கு வாரிய தண்கட னாடெலாம்."],"p":"","r":"The cool sea-lands are full of conches, pearls and pearl-oysters, the sweet toddy that wells in the coconut spathe, and the goods that ships and waves bring in.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","naadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 32","lb":"SO32","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:3"},"so:33":{"book":"soolamani","num":33,"t":["கொடிச்சியர் புனத்தயல் குறிஞ்சி நெய்பகர்","இடைச்சியர் கதுப்பயர் கமழு மேழையம்","கடைச்சியர் களையெறி குவளை கானல்வாய்த்","தொடுத்தலர் பிணையலார் குழலுட் டோ ன்றுமே."],"p":"","r":"The flowers of each tract are seen in the women's hair: the hill women by the millet field, the herdswomen selling ghee, and the farm women who weed out the kuvalai, with garlands strung in the shore grove.","n":"The three kinds of women (கொடிச்சியர், இடைச்சியர், கடைச்சியர்) are named by the tract they belong to, so the verse sorts the country by its peoples as well as by its plants; the syntax of the second half is loose, so which flower belongs to which woman is only partly clear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["naadu","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 33","lb":"SO33","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:3"},"so:34":{"book":"soolamani","num":34,"t":["கலவர்தஞ் சிறுபறை யிசையிற் கைவினைப்","புலவர்தேம் பிழிமகிழ் குரவை பொங்குமே","குலவுகோற் கோவலர் கொன்றைத் தீங்குழல்","உலவுநீ ளசுணமா வுறங்கு மென்பவே."],"p":"","r":"The small drum of the craftsmen swells the kuravai dance of those drinking honey-wine, and the herdsmen with their crooks play sweet konrai flutes, so that, they say, the long-bodied asunam beast falls asleep.","n":"The asunam (அசுணம்) is the animal the tradition says sleeps to sweet music; the verse uses it to show the flute as soothing. கலவர் in line 1 is a doubtful word for the people who beat the small drum, and whether the following kuravai belongs to the same group is not clear from the text.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naadu","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 34","lb":"SO34","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:3"},"so:35":{"book":"soolamani","num":35,"t":["மாக்கொடி மாணையு மெளவற் பந்தரும்","கார்க்கொடி முல்லையுங் கலந்து மல்லிகைப்","பூக்கொடிப் பொதும்பரும் பொன்ன ஞாழலும்","தூக்கடி கமழ்ந்துதான் றுறக்க மொக்குமே."],"p":"","r":"Mango vines, jasmine arbours, the dark-leaved mullai creeper, mallikai thickets and golden naazhal all breathe such strong fragrance that the land is like heaven.","n":"This last verse of the country description ends in a comparison (துறக்கம் ஒக்குமே, 'is like heaven') that no earlier verse of the section makes; the poet offers the land's scent as proof of its excellence, with no doctrinal claim.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naadu","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 35","lb":"SO35","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:3"},"so:36":{"book":"soolamani","num":36,"t":["சொன்னநீர் வளமைத் தாய சுரமைநாட் டகணி சார்ந்து","மன்னன்வீற் றிருந்து வைக நூலவர் வகுக்கப் பட்ட","பொன்னவிர் புரிசை வேலிப் போதன மென்ப துண்டோர்","நன்னகர் நாக லோக நகுவதொத் தினிய தொன்றே."],"p":"","r":"The city called Podanam stands inside a golden wall on the throne-ground of the Surama country, a fine town that seems to laugh at the world of the serpents.","n":"Line 1 is a loose construction (the land's fame and the capital seated in it); the sense is the praise of the country before the city, and the comparison is with the Naga world below.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naadu","azhagu","perumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 36","lb":"SO36","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:4"},"so:37":{"book":"soolamani","num":37,"t":["சங்கமேய் தரங்க வேலித் தடங்கடற் பொய்கை பூத்த","அண்கண்மா ஞால மென்னுந் தாமரை யலரி னங்கேழ்ச்","செங்கண்மால் சுரமை யென்னுந் தேம்பொகுட் டகத்து வைகும்","நங்கையர் படிவங் கொண்ட நலத்தது நகர மன்றே."],"p":"","r":"The sea is a pond and the wide earth a lotus in it; in the lotus's honeyed centre lives red-eyed Tirumal in Surama, and the city takes the beauty of women's forms.","n":"The poet builds the comparison in three nested steps (sea as pond, earth as lotus, Surama as its seed-vessel), and the last line turns the city into a woman's body, the poem's usual measure of beauty.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","naadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 37","lb":"SO37","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:4"},"so:38":{"book":"soolamani","num":38,"t":["செஞ்சுடர்க் கடவு டிண்டே ரிவுளிகா றிவள வூன்றும்","மஞ்சுடை மதர்வை நெற்றி வானுழு வாயின் மாடத்","தஞ்சுட ரிஞ்சி , யாங்கோ ரழகணிந் தலர்ந்த தோற்றம்","வெஞ்சுடர் விரியு முந்நீர் வேதிகை மீதிட் டன்றே."],"p":"","r":"The city's sky-reaching mansions, where the sun's horses graze and the clouds are caught on the frontage, seem a flower opened by the sea and set on the altar of the great ocean.","n":"Lines 1-3 are hard to construe; the image of the city as a flower laid on the ocean's altar rests on line 4.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","perumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 38","lb":"SO38","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:4"},"so:39":{"book":"soolamani","num":39,"t":["இரும்பிடு தொடரின் மாவி னெழுமுதற் பிணித்த யானைக்","கரும்பிடு கவள மூட்டும் கம்பலை கலந்த காவின்","அரும்பிடை யலர்ந்த போதி னல்லியுண் டரற்று கின்ற","கரும்பொடு துதைந்து தோன்றுஞ் சூழ்மதில் இருக்கை யெல்லாம்."],"p":"","r":"Beyond the groves, elephants chained at the roots of mango trees are fed sugar-cane, and bees drink pollen from the buds, all around the city's walled dwellings.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","naadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 39","lb":"SO39","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:4"},"so:40":{"book":"soolamani","num":40,"t":["மானளா மதர்வை நோக்கின் மையரி மழைக்க ணார்தம்","தேனளா முருவக் கண்ணிச் செல்வர்தோ டிளைக்கு மாடங்","கானளாங் காம வல்லி கற்பகங் கலந்த கண்ணார்","வானளாய் மலர்ந்து தோன்று மணிவரை யனைய தொன்றே."],"p":"","r":"The mansions rise like a jewelled mountain, where women with rain-dark painted eyes and lords in honeyed garlands play, and the creeper of desire is mixed with the wishing tree.","n":"The mansion's pleasure is shown as love meeting wealth, with the creeper and the wishing tree the poet's own figures for desire and its fulfilment.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","kaamam","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 40","lb":"SO40","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:4"},"so:41":{"book":"soolamani","num":41,"t":["அகிலெழு கொழும்புகை மஞ்சி னாடவும்","முகிலிசை யெனமுழா முரன்று விம்மவும்","துகிலிகைக் கொடியனார் மின்னிற் றோன்றவும்","இகலின மலையொடு மாட மென்பவே."],"p":"","r":"The mansions are said to rival the mountains: incense smoke is the cloud, drums rumble like thunder, and women like banners show up like lightning.","n":"Each part of the mansion has a mountain's counterpart (smoke-cloud, drum-thunder, banner-lightning); the poet is showing the whole built city as a weather.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 41","lb":"SO41","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:4"},"so:42":{"book":"soolamani","num":42,"t":["கண்ணெலாங் கவர்வன கனக கூடமும்","வெண்ணிலாச் சொரிவன வெள்ளி வேயுளும்","தண்ணிலாத் தவழ்மணித் தலமுஞ் சார்ந்தரோ","மண்ணினா லியன்றில மதலை மாடமே."],"p":"","r":"The golden halls, the silver bamboo-like pillars and the jewelled floors that gleam under the moon are said not to be made of earth.","n":"The closing claim that the mansions are not earthen is praise by exaggeration, not a doctrine about the city.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 42","lb":"SO42","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:4"},"so:43":{"book":"soolamani","num":43,"t":["மாடவாய் மணிமுழா விசையு மங்கையர்","ஆடுவார் சிலம்பிணை யதிரு மோசையும்","பாடுவார் பாணியும் பயின்று பல்கலம்","முடிமா ணகரது முரல்வ தொக்குமே."],"p":"","r":"The jewelled drums, the anklets of dancing women and the songs of singers, all heard from the mansions, make the city sound like a hall of many instruments.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inbam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 43","lb":"SO43","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:4"},"so:44":{"book":"soolamani","num":44,"t":["தாழிவாய்க் குவளையுந் தண்ணெ னோதியர்","மாழைவா ணெடுங்கணு மயங்கி வந்துசென்","றியாழவா மின்குர லாலித் தார்த்தரோ","ஏழைவாய்ச் சுரும்பின மிளைக்கு மென்பவே."],"p":"","r":"The bees, drawn by the water-lilies and by the women's long eyes, mistake the eyes for flowers and, bewildered, grow weary buzzing among the dwellings.","n":"The mistake of bees for flowers is the poet's stock image for the women's eyes, and the verse is praise of their beauty, not a scene.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 44","lb":"SO44","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:4"},"so:45":{"book":"soolamani","num":45,"t":["பளிங்குபோழ்ந் தியற்றிய பலகை வேதிகை","விளிம்புதோய் நெடுங்கடை வீதி வாயெலாம்","துளங்குபூ மாலையுஞ் சுரும்புந் தோன்றலால்","வளங்கொள்பூங் கற்பக வனமும் போலுமே."],"p":"","r":"The crystal-slabbed benches along the shop-lined streets, with swaying garlands and bees, look like a grove of wishing trees.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 45","lb":"SO45","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:4"},"so:46":{"book":"soolamani","num":46,"t":["காவிவாய்க் கருங்கணார் காமர் பூஞ்சிலம்","பாவிவாய் மாளிகை யதிரக் கேட்டொறும்","தூவிவான் பெடைதுணை துறந்த கொல்லென","வாவிவா யிளவனம் மயங்கு மென்பவே."],"p":"","r":"The sound of anklets of dark-eyed women echoing in the mansions makes the swans in the ponds restlessly wonder whether their mates have left them.","n":"The swans' fear of parting is the poet's own touch, an image of separation set inside a description of plenty.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","kaamam","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 46","lb":"SO46","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:4"},"so:47":{"book":"soolamani","num":47,"t":["விலத்தகைப் பூந்துணர் விரிந்த கோதையர்","நலத்தகைச் சிலம்படி நவில வூட்டிய","அலத்தகக் குழம்புதோய்ந் தரச வீதிகள்","புலத்திடைத் தாமரை பூத்த போலுமே."],"p":"","r":"The royal streets, stained with the red lac that women's anklet-sounding feet leave, look like lotuses blooming in a field.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 47","lb":"SO47","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:4"},"so:48":{"book":"soolamani","num":48,"t":["கண்ணிலங் கடிமலர்க் குவளைக் கற்றையும்","வெண்ணிலாத் திரளென விளங்கு மாரமும்","வண்ணவான் மல்லிகை வளாய மாலையும்","அண்ணன்மா நகர்க்கவைக் கரிய அல்லவே."],"p":"","r":"Lotuses, pearl strings that gleam like moonlight and jasmine garlands are not rare in the great city, so the speaker says there is no scarcity of such adornments.","n":"The verse closes on the negative (not rare), so the sense is that adornments are plentiful; line 4 is short of a clear subject.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["selvam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 48","lb":"SO48","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:4"},"so:49":{"book":"soolamani","num":49,"t":["தேம்பழுத் தினியநீர் மூன்றுந் தீம்பலா","மேம்பழுத் தளிந்தன சுளையும் வேரியும்","மாம்பழக் குவைகளூ மதுத்தண் டீட்டமும்","தாம்பழுத் துளசில தவள மாடமே."],"p":"","r":"The city's white mansions overflow with ripe fruits, the sweet water of three kinds, jackfruit, mango heaps and honey, as if the whole place were ripened.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","naadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 49","lb":"SO49","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:4"},"so:50":{"book":"soolamani","num":50,"t":["மைந்தரு மகளிரு மாலை காலையென்","றந்தரப் படுத்தவ ரறிவ தின்மையால்","சுந்தரப் பொற்றுக டுதைந்த பொன்னக","ரிந்திர வுலகம்வந் திழிந்த தொக்குமே."],"p":"","r":"Its men and women, caught up in joy, know no difference between evening and morning, so that the golden city seems an Indra's heaven come down to earth.","n":"The unawareness of time is praise of the city's pleasure; it contrasts with the Jain teaching of impermanence that the epic later brings in, but this verse does not make that contrast.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inbam","azhagu","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 50","lb":"SO50","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:4"},"so:51":{"book":"soolamani","num":51,"t":["மற்றமா நகருடை மன்னன் றன்னுயா","ஒற்றைவெண் குடைநிழ லுலகிற் கோருயிர்ப்","பொற்றியான் பயாபதி யென்னும் பேருடை","வெற்றிவேல் மணி முடி வேந்தர் வேந்தனே."],"p":"","r":"The king of that great city, the single protector of the world under one white parasol, is the crowned, victorious king of kings called Payapati.","n":"Line 2 is the common figure of a single parasol shading the world; the verse names the king only here, so who he is must come from the lines that follow.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vendhan","arasu","perumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 51","lb":"SO51","mm":1,"ev":"Payapati, the city's king, is named and praised as sole protector under the white parasol.","cl":[],"ag":["பயாபதி"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:4"},"so:52":{"book":"soolamani","num":52,"t":["எண்ணின ரெண்ணகப் படாத செய்கையான்","அண்ணிய ரகன்றவர் திறத்து மாணையான்","நண்ணுநர் பகைவரென் றிவர்க்கு நாளினும்","தண்ணியன் வெய்யனந் தானை மன்னனே."],"p":"","r":"He is a king beyond reckoning in his works, whose command holds even the distant, gentle to those who come near and hot to enemies, with a great army.","n":"The king is described as cool to those who come to him and fierce to foes, a double character that the praise treats as kingly, not as a flaw.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","pagai","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 52","lb":"SO52","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["பயாபதி"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:4"},"so:53":{"book":"soolamani","num":53,"t":["நாமவே னரபதி யுலகங் காத்தநாட்","காமவேள் கவர்கணை கலந்த தல்லது","தாமவேல் வயவர்தந் தழலங் கொல்படை","நாமநீர் வரைப்பக நலித தில்லையே."],"p":"","r":"In the days this king guarded the world nothing but Kama's arrows mingled in the land; no fierce, fire-like killing force of spear-bearing warriors troubled his sea-bounded realm.","n":"The praise of a bloodless reign names Kama's arrows as the only weapon abroad, which sets up the verses that follow, where the same arrows work inside the palace.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","kaamam","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 53","lb":"SO53","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["நரபதி"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:5"},"so:54":{"book":"soolamani","num":54,"t":["ஆறிலொன் றறமென வருளி னல்லதொன்","றூறுசெய் துலகினி னுவப்ப தில்லையே","மாறிநின் றவரையும் வணக்கி னல்லது","சீறிநின் றெவரையுஞ் செகுப்ப தில்லையே."],"p":"","r":"The king takes his sixth share only as a duty of grace, rejoices in no harm done to the world, and brings even enemies low by making them bow, never by slaying them in anger.","n":"The reading takes ஆறிலொன்று as the royal share of the produce and அறம் as its name; the verse says nothing of a rival measure.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aram","arul","kolai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 54","lb":"SO54","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["a king's one-sixth share is his dharma, to be taken only with grace and without oppression","general","asserted",1,"narrator"],["a king should subdue foes by making them bow, not by killing in wrath","general","asserted",3,"narrator"]],"ag":["நரபதி"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:5"},"so:55":{"book":"soolamani","num":55,"t":["அடிநிழ லரசரை யளிக்கு மாய்கதிர்","முடிநிழன் முனிவரர் சரண முழ்குமே","வடிநிழல் வனைகதி ரெஃகின் மன்னவன்","குடைநிழ லுலகெலாங் குளிர நின்றதே."],"p":"","r":"Kings find shelter in the shade at the king's feet, sages find refuge in the shade of his crown, and the world stands cool under the shade of his umbrella.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 55","lb":"SO55","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:5"},"so:56":{"book":"soolamani","num":56,"t":["மன்னிய பகைக்குழா மாறும் வையகம்","துன்னிய வரும்பகைத் தொகையும் மின்மையால்","தன்னையுந் தரையையுங் காக்கு மென்பதம்","மன்னவன் றிறத்தினி மருள வேண்டுமோ."],"p":"","r":"With neither lasting foes nor a ring of hostile neighbours, the king guards himself and the earth, so there is nothing here to puzzle over.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","pagai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 56","lb":"SO56","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["நரபதி"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:5"},"so:57":{"book":"soolamani","num":57,"t":["மேலவர் மெய்ப்பொருள் விரிக்கும் வீறுசால்","நூலினாற் பெரியவர் நுழைந்த சுற்றமா","ஆலுநீ ரன்னமோ டரச வன்னமே","போலநின் றுலகினைப் பொதுமை நீக்கினான்."],"p":"","r":"The king takes as kin men great in the books that unfold the truth and, like the swan that parts milk from water, keeps the world free of the common.","n":"பொதுமை நீக்கினான் is the hard phrase; it may mean he put aside what is common, or what is shared, in his rule, and the sense is not fixed.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["saandror","thunai","kalvi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 57","lb":"SO57","mm":1,"ev":"The king chooses learned counsellors as his kinsmen.","cl":[],"ag":["நரபதி"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:5"},"so:58":{"book":"soolamani","num":58,"t":["கொதிநுனைப் பகழியான் குறிப்பி னல்லதொன்","றிதுநமக் கிசைக்கென வெண்ணு மெண்ணிலா","நொதுமலர் வெருவுறா நுவற்சி யாளர்பின்","அதுவவன் பகுதிக ளமைதி வண்ணமே."],"p":"","r":"Whatever the king does not hint at, none of the countless strangers dares to propose, and the speakers around him stay unafraid; his divisions follow in orderly array.","n":"Lines 2 and 3 are hard to construe; the verse seems to say that nothing is said at court except at the king's hint, and that his forces fall in as he arranges.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["arasu","amaichu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 58","lb":"SO58","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:5"},"so:59":{"book":"soolamani","num":59,"t":["மற்றவன் றேவியர் மகர வார்குழைக்","கொற்றவர் குலங்களை விளக்கத் தோன்றினார்","இற்றதிம் மருங்குலென் றிரங்க வீங்கிய","முற்றுறா முலையினார் கலையின் முற்றியார்."],"p":"","r":"His queens appear to light the royal clans, with swelling breasts whose weight bends their waists and a mastery of the arts.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 59","lb":"SO59","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["தேவியர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:5"},"so:60":{"book":"soolamani","num":60,"t":["பஞ்சனுங் கடியினார் பரந்த வல்குலார்","செஞ்சுணங் கிளமுலை மருங்கு சிந்தினார்","வஞ்சியங் குழைத்தலை மதர்வைக் கொம்புதம்","அஞ்சுட ரிணர்க்கொசிந் தனைய வைம்மையார்."],"p":"","r":"The queens are slender-waisted, broad-hipped women with reddish spots of beauty on the breast, bending like a vanci creeper with a bough of five bright flowers.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 60","lb":"SO60","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:5"},"so:61":{"book":"soolamani","num":61,"t":["காமத்தொத் தலர்ந்தவர் கதிர்த்த கற்பினார்","தாமத்தொத் தலர்ந்துதாழ்ந் திருண்ட கூந்தலார்","தூமத்துச் சுடரொளி துளும்பு தோளினார்","வாமத்தின் மயங்கிமை மதர்த்த வாட்கணார்."],"p":"","r":"The queens are full of desire and of shining chastity, with dark hair heavy with flowers, shoulders bright as smoke-lit flame and broad dark eyes.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","karpu","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 61","lb":"SO61","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:5"},"so:62":{"book":"soolamani","num":62,"t":["ஆயிர ரவரவர்க் கதிகத் தேவியர்","மாயிரு விசும்பினி னிழிந்த மாண்பினார்","சேயிருந் தாமரைத் தெய்வ மன்னர் என்","றேயுரை யிலாதவ ரிருவ ராயினார்."],"p":"","r":"Besides the thousand wives, two stand above the rest, said to have come down from the sky and to be like lotus goddesses, beyond any words.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","karpu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 62","lb":"SO62","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["தேவியர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:5"},"so:63":{"book":"soolamani","num":63,"t":["நீங்கரும் பமிழ்த மூட்டித் தேனளாய்ப் பிழிந்த போலும்","ஓங்கிருங் கடலந் தானை வேந்தணங் குறுக்கு மின்சொல்","வீங்கிருங் குவவுக் கொங்கை மிகாபதி மிக்க தேவி","தாங்கருங் கற்பின் றங்கை சசிஎன்பாள் சசியோ டொப்பாள்."],"p":"","r":"The chief queen Mikapathi, sweet of speech, is joined by her younger sister Sasi, whose chaste constancy matches Indra's queen Sachi.","n":"தங்கை marks Sasi as the younger sister, and the verse praises both for a chastity hard to bear; the poet's own voice carries the praise.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["karpu","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 63","lb":"SO63","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["மிகாபதி","சசி"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:5"},"so:64":{"book":"soolamani","num":64,"t":["பூங்குழை மகளிர்க் கெல்லாம் பொன்மலர் மணிக்கொம் பன்ன","தேங்குழல் மங்கை மார்கள் திலகமாய்த் திகழ நின்றார்","மாங்கொழுந் தசோக மென்றாங் கிரண்டுமே வயந்த காலத்","தாங்கெழுந் தவற்றை யெல்லா மணிபெற வலரு மன்றே."],"p":"","r":"The two women shine as the diadem of all flower-eared women, like the mango sprout and the asoka that bloom together in spring.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 64","lb":"SO64","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["தேவியர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:5"},"so:65":{"book":"soolamani","num":65,"t":["பெருமக னுருகும் பெண்மை மாண்பினும் பேணி நாளும்","மருவினும் புதிய போலும் மழலையங் கிளவி யாலும்","திருமகள் புலமை யாக்குஞ் செல்விஎன் றிவர்கள் போல","இருவரு மிறைவ னுள்ளத் தொருவரா யினிய ரானார்."],"p":"","r":"Cherished by the king and fresh in their prattle every day, the two become single in his heart, as dear as the goddess of wealth and the goddess of learning are to a great man.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["anbu","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 65","lb":"SO65","mm":1,"ev":"The two queens come to hold one place in the king's heart.","cl":[],"ag":["தேவியர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:5"},"so:66":{"book":"soolamani","num":66,"t":["மன்னவ னாவி யாவார் மகளிரம் மகளிர் தங்கள்","இன்னுயி ராகி நின்றா னிறைமக னிவர்க டங்கட்","கென்னைகொ லொருவர் தம்மே லொருவர்க்கிங் குள்ள மோட","முன்னவன் புணர்த்த வாறம் மொய்ம்மலர்க் கணையி னானே."],"p":"","r":"The king is the life of the women and they are his; the poet asks what makes one heart run toward another, and gives the answer in the way the flower-arrowed god had ordained.","n":"The verse treats the love among the three as set by Kama's design, and does not say whether the poet holds it a bond to be praised or one that binds.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","anbu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 66","lb":"SO66","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["தேவியர்","நரபதி"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:5"},"so:67":{"book":"soolamani","num":67,"t":["சொற்பகர்ந் துலகங் காக்குந் தொழில்புறத் தொழிய வாங்கி","மற்பக ரகலத் தானை மனத்திடைப் பிணித்து வைத்தார்","பொற்பகங் கமழப் பூத்த தேந்துணர் பொறுக்க லாற்றாக்","கற்பகக் கொழுந்துங் காம வல்லியங் கொடியு மொப்பார்."],"p":"","r":"Setting aside the work of speaking justice and guarding the world, the queens bind the king's mind, like a kalpaka sprout and a creeper of desire that cannot bear their load of flowers.","n":"The verse says the king's ruling work is pushed outside; the narrator does not blame the queens or the king here.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","arasu","nenju"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 67","lb":"SO67","mm":1,"ev":"The queens draw the king's mind from the work of ruling.","cl":[],"ag":["தேவியர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:5"},"so:68":{"book":"soolamani","num":68,"t":["மங்கைய ரிருவ ராகி மன்னவ னொருவ னாகி","அங்கவ ரமர்ந்த தெல்லா மமர்ந்தருள் பெருகி நின்றான்","செங்கயல் மதர்த்த வாட்கட் டெய்வமா மகளிர் தோறும்","தங்கிய வுருவந் தாங்குஞ் சக்கரன் றகைமை யானான்."],"p":"","r":"Two women, one king: he gives himself wholly in each, taking the form of Indra among goddess-like women.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","inbam","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 68","lb":"SO68","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["நரபதி","தேவியர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:5"},"so:69":{"book":"soolamani","num":69,"t":["முற்றுநீர் வளாக மெல்லா முழுதுட னிழற்று மூரி","ஒற்றைவெண் குடையி னீழ லுலகுகண் படுப்ப வோம்பிக்","கொற்றவ னெடுங்க ணார்தங் குவிமுலைத் தடத்து மூழ்கி","மற்றவற் கரசச் செல்வ மின்னண மமர்ந்த தன்றே."],"p":"","r":"His sea-girdled earth sleeps under the shade of one white umbrella, while the king bathes in his queens' breasts, and such was the wealth of his rule.","n":"The closing verse sets the single umbrella of rule beside the delight of the palace and lets them stand together as the king's 'rule-wealth' (அரசச் செல்வம்), without comment.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","vendhan","selvam","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 69","lb":"SO69","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["நரபதி"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:5"},"so:70":{"book":"soolamani","num":70,"t":["ஆங்கவர் திருவயிற் றமரர் கற்பமாண்","டீங்குட னிழிந்துவந் திருவர் தோன்றினார்","வாங்குநீர்த் திரைவளர் வளையு மக்கடல்","ஓங்குநீர் நிழலுமொத் தொளிரு மூர்த்தியார்."],"p":"","r":"Two beings come down together from the heaven of the kalpa into the queens' wombs and are born, shining like the sea's deep water.","n":"The two are heavenly beings born here, so the story opens their human lives with a rebirth, not a beginning; line 1's திருவயிற்று (holy wombs, plural) shows two mothers.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["piravi","marumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 70","lb":"SO70","mm":1,"ev":"Two beings descend from a heavenly kalpa and are born as the two sons of the king.","cl":[],"ag":["விசயன்","செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:6"},"so:71":{"book":"soolamani","num":71,"t":["பெண்ணிலாந் தகைப்பெருந் தேவி பேரமர்க்","கண்ணிலாங் களிவள ருவகை கைம்மிகத்","தண்ணிலா வுலகெலாந் தவழந்து வான்கொள","வெண்ணிலா சுடரொளி விசயன் றோன்றினான்."],"p":"","r":"The great queen's joy overflows as Vijayan is born, bright as a white moon that fills the cool world.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["makkal","pirappu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 71","lb":"SO71","mm":1,"ev":"Vijayan is born to the queen.","cl":[],"ag":["விசயன்","தேவி"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:6"},"so:72":{"book":"soolamani","num":72,"t":["ஏரணங் கிளம்பெருந் தேவி நாளுறச்","சீரணங் கவிரொளித் திவிட்டன் றோன்றினான்","நீரணங் கொளிவளை நிரந்து விம்மின","ஆரணங் கலர்மழை யமரர் சிந்தினார்."],"p":"","r":"In due time the second queen bears Tivittan, whose radiance outshines the jewel-lights, and the gods rain flowers.","n":"Line 3 has the sea's conches swelling, so earth and heaven both mark the birth.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["makkal","pirappu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 72","lb":"SO72","mm":1,"ev":"Tivittan is born to the second queen, and the gods shower flowers.","cl":[],"ag":["செங்கண் ணெடியான்","தேவி","அமரர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:6"},"so:73":{"book":"soolamani","num":73,"t":["திசையெலாத் தெளிந்தன தேவர் பொன்னகர்","இசையெலாம் பெருஞ்சிறப் பியன்ற வேற்பவர்","நசையெலா மவிந்தன நலியுந் தீவினைப்","பசையெலாம் பறந்தன பலர்க்கு மென்பவே."],"p":"","r":"At their birth the quarters grow clear, the gods' city rings with praise, wishes are fulfilled and the clinging moisture of afflicting evil deeds dries up for many, the poet reports.","n":"The closing என்பவே (they say) gives the claim as report. தீவினைப் பசை pictures karma as a moisture that makes deeds stick; the verse does not say whose deeds, only that it dries for many.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","piravi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 73","lb":"SO73","mm":1,"ev":"At the princes' birth the quarters clear and the hold of evil karma is said to dissolve.","cl":[["the birth of a great being dries up the clinging residue of harmful deeds for many","general","reported",3,"narrator"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:6"},"so:74":{"book":"soolamani","num":74,"t":["செய்தமா ணகரியிற் சிறந்து சென்றுசென்று","எய்தினார் குமரராம் பிராய மெய்தலும்","மைதுழாம் நெடுங்கணார் மனத்துட் காமனார்","ஐதுலாங் கவர்கணை யரும்பு வைத்தவே."],"p":"","r":"The two grow up in their splendid city and reach the age of princes, and the love-god sets his arrow-buds in the hearts of dark-eyed women.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ilamai","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 74","lb":"SO74","mm":1,"ev":"The princes come of age and Kaman plants his arrows in the women's hearts.","cl":[],"ag":["விசயன்","செங்கண் ணெடியான்","காமனார்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:6"},"so:75":{"book":"soolamani","num":75,"t":["காமரு வலம்புரி கமழு மேனியன்","தாமரை யகவிதழ் தடுத்த கண்ணினன்","தூமரு ளிருடுணர்ந் தனைய குஞ்சியன்","பூமரு பொலங்குழை புரளுங் காதினன்."],"p":"","r":"A prince is described: fragrant with conch-white, lotus-petal eyes, a crest like dark night, gold earrings swinging at his ears.","n":"The sequence of 75 and 76 describes the elder, as 78 later names the other as his younger.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","ilamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 75","lb":"SO75","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:6"},"so:76":{"book":"soolamani","num":76,"t":["வாடலில் கண்ணியன் மலர்ந்த மார்பினன்","தாடவழ் தடக்கையன் றயங்கு சோதியன்","கோடுயர் குன்றெனக் குலவு தோளினன்","பீடுடை நடையினன் பெரிய நம்பியே."],"p":"","r":"The elder prince has an unwithering garland, broad chest, long arms, shining body, shoulders like hill peaks and a stately walk.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","perumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 76","lb":"SO76","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:6"},"so:77":{"book":"soolamani","num":77,"t":["பூவயம் புதுமலர் புரையு மேனியன்","தூவிரி தாமரை தொலைத்த கண்ணினன்","தீவிரி யாம்பலிற் சிவந்த வாயினன்","மாவிரி திருமறு வணிந்த மார்பினன்."],"p":"","r":"The younger prince is described: body like a fresh flower, eyes surpassing lotus, red lips like the ambal, and the holy mark on his chest.","n":"திருமறு is the mark on the chest; the verse gives it as a feature of the person without comment.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 77","lb":"SO77","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:6"},"so:78":{"book":"soolamani","num":78,"t":["சங்கியல் வலம்புரி திகிரி யென்றிவை","தங்கிய வங்கைய னடித்தண் போதினன்","மங்கல மழகளி றனைய செல்கையன்","இங்குமுன் மொழிந்தவற் கிளய நம்பியே."],"p":"","r":"The younger prince bears the emblems of conch and discus on his palm, walks like a splendid elephant, and is the younger of the one just described.","n":"Conch (சங்கு) and discus (திகிரி) on the palm are given as body marks. 109 later has the court declare Tivittan a guardian of the whole world, which makes these marks read as signs of rule.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","perumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 78","lb":"SO78","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:6"},"so:79":{"book":"soolamani","num":79,"t":["திருவிளைத் துலகுகண் மலரத் தெவ்வர்தம்","யுரிவளை நன்னகர்ச் செல்வம் புல்லென","வரிவளைத் தோளியர் மனத்துட் காமநோய்","எரிவளைத் திடுவதோ ரிளமை யெய்தினார்."],"p":"","r":"As the princes enter a youth that sets fire in the hearts of bangled women, their splendour dulls their rivals' wealth.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ilamai","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 79","lb":"SO79","mm":1,"ev":"The princes reach an age that kindles love-sickness in women.","cl":[],"ag":["குமரர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:6"},"so:80":{"book":"soolamani","num":80,"t":["உவர் விளை கடற்கொடிப் பவள மோட்டிய","துவரிதழ் வாயவர் துளங்கு மேனியர்","அவர்கட மருள்கொலோ வனங்க னாய்மலர்","கவர்கணை கடைக்கணித் துருவு காட்டினார்."],"p":"","r":"The coral-lipped women turn glances at them, as if the love-god's flower-arrows had taken shape in sidelong looks.","n":"அவர்கடம் மருள்கொலோ (is it their bewilderment?) leaves open whether the glance is love or the princes' charm striking them.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","kurippu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 80","lb":"SO80","mm":1,"ev":"The women show their love by glances, which the poet likens to Kaman's arrows.","cl":[],"ag":["மடவரலார்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:6"},"so:81":{"book":"soolamani","num":81,"t":["கடலொளி மணிவணன் கனவில் வந்தெம","துடலகம் வெருவிதா யுள்ளம் வவ்வினான்","விடலில னெம்முயிர் விடுக்குங் கொல்லென","மடவர லவர்குழா மயக்க முற்றதே."],"p":"","r":"The women swoon with fear: the dark-gemmed one has come in a dream, taken their hearts and will not let go; will he take their lives?","n":"The dark one of the sea's colour (81) and the conch-coloured one (82) are likely the two princes, as 106 later calls the younger கடல் வண்ணன்; the verse itself names neither.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kanavu","kaamanoy"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 81","lb":"SO81","mm":1,"ev":"The women, dazed, say a prince seen in dream has taken their hearts.","cl":[],"ag":["மடவரலார்"],"ec":[],"spk":["மடவரலார்",""],"ch":"so:6"},"so:82":{"book":"soolamani","num":82,"t":["வார்வளை வண்ணனென் மனத்து ளான்பிறர்","ஏர்வளர் நெடுங்கணுக் கிலக்க மல்லனாற்","கார்வளர் கொம்பனா ரிவர்கள் காமநோய்","ஆர்வளர்த் தவர்கொலென் பருவ மாயினார்."],"p":"","r":"One woman says the conch-coloured prince is in her heart and not a target for other women's eyes; the poet asks who raised this heart-sickness, and answers that it is their age.","n":"Lines 1-2 are the woman's thought, lines 3-4 the poet's question, so the speaker changes within the verse; 82's assignment is uncertain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamanoy","ilamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 82","lb":"SO82","mm":1,"ev":"A woman declares that the fair prince lives in her heart.","cl":[],"ag":["மடவரலார்"],"ec":[],"spk":["மடவரலார்",""],"ch":"so:6"},"so:83":{"book":"soolamani","num":83,"t":["கண்ணிலாங் கவினொளிக் காளை மார்திறத்","துண்ணிலா வெழுதரு காம வூழெரி","எண்ணிலாச் சுடர்சுட விரிந்து நாண்விடாப்","பெண்ணலாற் பிறிதுயிர் பெரிய தில்லையே."],"p":"","r":"For the women the fire of desire rises within for these radiant youths and burns with countless flames, and no creature but shame-bound women bears it more heavily.","n":"காம வூழெரி joins desire and ஊழ், a fire from fate; the last line reads as a claim that women bound by shame suffer most, but its sense is unsure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","naanam","oozh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 83","lb":"SO83","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:6"},"so:84":{"book":"soolamani","num":84,"t":["திருவளர் செல்வர்மேற் சென்ற சிந்தைநோய்","ஒருவரி னொருவர்மிக் குடைய ராதலால்","உருவளர் கொம்பனா ருள்ளங் காய்வதோர்","எரிவளர்த் திடுவதோ ரிளமை யெய்தினார்."],"p":"","r":"Because the heart-sickness that goes out to the wealthy lords grows stronger in each woman than the other, the princes reach the age that makes a fire burn in their hearts.","n":"This verse repeats 79's closing line, now attaching the fire to the lords' wealth (திருவளர் செல்வர்) as well as to their youth.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","ilamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 84","lb":"SO84","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:6"},"so:85":{"book":"soolamani","num":85,"t":["மற்றொர்நா ளமரிகைக் கொடிகொண் மாமணிச்","சுற்றுவான் சுடரொளி தழுவிச் சூழ்மலர்","முற்றிவண் டினம்விடா முடிகொள் சென்னியக்","கொற்றவ னிளையவர் குழைய வைகினான்."],"p":"","r":"On another day the crowned king, his head ringed with flowers and bees, passes his rest among the young women of his court.","n":"The sense of இளையவர் குழைய (the young [women/men] drooping) is not clear from the verse.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["arasu","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 85","lb":"SO85","mm":1,"ev":"The king rests in his palace among the young women.","cl":[],"ag":["பிரசாபதி"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:6"},"so:86":{"book":"soolamani","num":86,"t":["மஞ்சுடை மணிநகு மாலை மண்டபத்","தஞ்சுட ரகிற்புகை யளைந்து தேனளாய்ப்","பஞ்சுடை யமளிமேற் பள்ளி யேற்பவன்","செஞ்சுட ரிரிவதோர் திறத்த னாயினான்."],"p":"","r":"In the garland-hall amid the smoke of aloe and honey, the king on a cotton-stuffed couch gives himself to rest until the red light changes.","n":"செஞ்சுடர் இரிவதோர் திறத்தனாயினான் is hard; it is read here as the king stirring as the red light moves.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 86","lb":"SO86","mm":1,"ev":"The king sleeps in the garland-hall until dawn.","cl":[],"ag":["பிரசாபதி"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:6"},"so:87":{"book":"soolamani","num":87,"t":["மன்னவன் றுயில்விடுத் தருள மைந்தர்பொன்","றுன்னிய வுடையினர் துதைந்த கச்சையர்","பின்னிய ஞாணினர் பிடித்த வில்லினர்","அன்னவ ரடிமுதற் காவ னண்ணிணார்."],"p":"","r":"The king wakes and signals, and young attendants, in gold clothes, tight girdles and with bows in hand, come to take their post at his feet.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 87","lb":"SO87","mm":1,"ev":"Armed young attendants come to guard the awakened king.","cl":[],"ag":["பிரசாபதி","மைந்தர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:6"},"so:88":{"book":"soolamani","num":88,"t":["தங்கிய தவழழொளி தடாவி வில்லிட","மங்கல வுழக்கல மருங்கு சேர்ந்தன","அங்கவன் கண்கழூஉ வருளிச் செய்தனன்","பங்கய முகத்தர்பல் லாண்டு கூறினார்."],"p":"","r":"Ceremonial articles gather by him, he washes his eyes, and the lotus-faced women call out their wish for him to live many years.","n":"Line 1's தவழழொளி தடாவி is a doubtful reading.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 88","lb":"SO88","mm":1,"ev":"The king washes his eyes and the women wish him long life.","cl":[],"ag":["பிரசாபதி","கன்னியர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:6"},"so:89":{"book":"soolamani","num":89,"t":["அந்தண ராசிடை கூறி யாய்மலர்ப்","பைந்துணர் நெடுமுடி பயில வேற்றினார்","செந்துணர் நறுமலர் தெளித்துத் தேவர்மாட்","டிந்திர னனையவ னிறைஞ்சி யேத்தினான்."],"p":"","r":"Brahmins bless him, set the flower-crown on his head, and he bows to the gods and praises them like Indra.","n":"The verse puts brahmin blessing and the king's worship before the day's rule begins, as part of the morning rite.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 89","lb":"SO89","mm":1,"ev":"Brahmins bless and crown the king, who then worships the gods.","cl":[],"ag":["அந்தணர்","பிரசாபதி"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:6"},"so:90":{"book":"soolamani","num":90,"t":["விரையமர் கோதையர் வேணுக் கோலினர்","உரையமர் காவல்பூண் கடையி னூடுபோய்","முரசமர் முழங்கொலி மூரித் தானையன்","அரசவை மண்டப மடைவ தெண்ணினார்."],"p":"","r":"Women with garlands and staves go before the king through the palace gate, with the drum's roar, and he plans to reach the assembly hall.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 90","lb":"SO90","mm":1,"ev":"The king, escorted, sets out for the assembly hall.","cl":[],"ag":["பிரசாபதி"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:6"},"so:91":{"book":"soolamani","num":91,"t":["பொன்னவிர் திருவடி போற்றி போற்றிஎன்","றன்னமென் னடையவர் பரவ வாய்துகிற்","கன்னியர் கவரிகா லெறியக் காவலன்","முன்னிய நெடுங்கடை முற்ற முன்னினான்."],"p":"","r":"Girls praise his golden feet, fan him with chowries and clothing, and the king reaches the great outer court.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 91","lb":"SO91","mm":1,"ev":"The king, praised and fanned by girls, reaches the outer court.","cl":[],"ag":["பிரசாபதி","கன்னியர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:6"},"so:92":{"book":"soolamani","num":92,"t":["மஞ்சிவர் வளநகர் காக்கும் வார்கழல்","நஞ்சிவர் வேனர பதியை யாயிடை","வெஞ்சுடர் வாளினர் விசித்த கச்சையர்","கஞ்சுகி யவர்கண்மெய் காவ லோம்பினார்."],"p":"","r":"Chamberlains with swords and girdles guard the king, lord of the Naga-poisoned (poison-spear-bearing) city, as he goes.","n":"நஞ்சிவர் வேனர பதியை in line 2 is hard; it is read as an epithet of the king.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 92","lb":"SO92","mm":1,"ev":"Chamberlains guard the king as he passes.","cl":[],"ag":["கஞ்சுகியவர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:6"},"so:93":{"book":"soolamani","num":93,"t":["வாசநீர் தெளித்தலர் பரப்பி வானகம்","எசுநீ ளிருக்கைய விலங்கு சென்னிய","மூசுதே னெடுங்கடை மூன்றும் போய்ப்புறத்","தோசைநீள் மண்டப முவந்த தெய்தினான்."],"p":"","r":"The king passes the three gates, scented with water and strewn with flowers, and reaches the pavilion outside.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 93","lb":"SO93","mm":1,"ev":"The king passes three gates and reaches the outer pavilion.","cl":[],"ag":["பிரசாபதி"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:6"},"so:94":{"book":"soolamani","num":94,"t":["பளிங்கொளி கதுவப் போழ்ந்த பலகைகண் குலவச் சேர்த்தி","விளங்கொளி விளிம்பிற் செம்பொன் வேதிகை வெள்ளி வேயுட்","டுளொங்கொளி பவளத் திண்காற் சுடர்மணி தவழும் பூமி","வளங்கவின் றனைய தாய மண்டப மலிரப் புக்கான்."],"p":"","r":"The king enters the great hall of crystal, gold and coral pillars that shines like heaven's wealth.","n":"Line 3 begins டுளொங்கொளி, an apparent misprint for துளங்கொளி.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 94","lb":"SO94","mm":1,"ev":"The king enters the assembly hall.","cl":[],"ag":["பிரசாபதி"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:6"},"so:95":{"book":"soolamani","num":95,"t":["குஞ்சரக் குழவி கவ்விக் குளிர்மதிக் கோடு போலும்","அஞ்சுட ரெயிற்ற வாளி யணிமுக மலர வூன்றிச்","செஞ்சுட ரணிபொற் சிங்கா சனமிசைச் சேர்ந்த செல்வன்","வெஞ்சுட ருதயத் துச்சி விரிந்த வெய் யவனோ டொத்தான்."],"p":"","r":"The throne is described, with a fanged beast carved on it gripping an elephant calf, and the lord who takes his seat on it is likened to the fierce sun spreading over the top of the rising hill.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 95","lb":"SO95","mm":1,"ev":"The lord takes his seat on the lion throne.","cl":[],"ag":["வேந்தன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:7"},"so:96":{"book":"soolamani","num":96,"t":["பூமரு விரிந்த நுண்ணூற் புரோகிதன் பொறிவண் டார்க்கும்","மாமல ரணிந்த கண்ணி மந்திரக் கிழவர் மன்னார்","ஏமரு கடலந் தானை யிறைமகன் குறிப்பு நோக்கித்","தாமரைச் செங்கண் டம்மாற் பணித்ததா னத்த ரானார்."],"p":"","r":"The priest, the garlanded counsellors and the kings of the sea-like army take the places assigned to them, watching the sign of the lord and at the order of the lotus-eyed one.","n":"இறைமகன் (line 3) and தாமரைச் செங்கண் தம்மான் (line 4) are not named; they may be one person, the prince, or the lord on the throne, so who sets the seating is not fixed here.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["avai","amaichu","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 96","lb":"SO96","mm":1,"ev":"Priest, counsellors and kings take their appointed seats at the lord's sign.","cl":[],"ag":["புரோகிதன்","மந்திரக் கிழவர்","மன்னர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:7"},"so:97":{"book":"soolamani","num":97,"t":["முன்னவ ரிருந்த பின்னை மூரிநீ ருலகங் காக்கும்","மன்னவன் கழலைத் தங்கண் மணிமுடி நுதியிற் றீட்டிப்","பின்னவன் பணித்த தானம் பெறுமுறை வகையிற் சேர்ந்தார்","மின்னிவர் கடகப் பைம்பூண் வென்றிவேல் வேந்த ரெல்லாம்."],"p":"","r":"After the elder one is seated, the victorious kings touch their crowns to the king's anklets and take the places that the younger one assigns them in due order.","n":"முன்னவர் and பின்னவன் are not named; they read as the elder and younger prince, and the younger is likely Tivittan, who is named at 109, but the verse does not say so.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["mannar","panivu","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 97","lb":"SO97","mm":1,"ev":"The kings bow their crowned heads at the king's feet and take the seats the younger prince assigns.","cl":[],"ag":["மன்னர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:7"},"so:98":{"book":"soolamani","num":98,"t":["வழிமுறை பயின்று வந்த மரபினார் மன்னர் கோமான்","விழுமல ரடிக்கண் மிக்க வன்பினார்; வென்றி நீரார்;","எழுவளர்த் தனைய தோளா ரிளையவ ரின்ன நீரார்","உழையவ ராக வைத்தா னோடைமால் களிற்றி னானே."],"p":"","r":"Men of hereditary line, loving the king's feet, proud in victory and strong of arm, are what the king of the frontleted war-elephant keeps at his side.","n":"இளையவர் (line 3) may mean young warriors or the younger princes; the sentence is read as describing the king's attendants.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["arasu","sutram","anbu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 98","lb":"SO98","mm":1,"ev":"The king places loyal men of old families beside him as attendants.","cl":[],"ag":["வேந்தன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:7"},"so:99":{"book":"soolamani","num":99,"t":["காவல னென்னுஞ் செம்பொற் கற்பகங் கவின்ற போழ்தில்","நாவல ரென்னும் வண்டு நகைமுகப் பெயரி னாய","பூவலர் பொலிவு நோக்கிப் புலமயங் களிப்ப வாகிப்","பாவல ரிசையிற் றோன்றப் பாடுபு பயின்ற வன்றே."],"p":"","r":"When the king stands in full bloom like a golden wishing tree, the poets, like bees, gather to his bright face and begin to sing in measured melody.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pugazh","kalvi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 99","lb":"SO99","mm":1,"ev":"The poets gather and begin to sing the king's praise.","cl":[],"ag":["புலவர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:7"},"so:100":{"book":"soolamani","num":100,"t":["பண்ணமை மகர நல்யாழ்ப் பனுவனூற் புலவர் பாடி","மண்ணமர் வளாக மெல்லாம் மலர்ந்ததின் புகழோ டொன்றி","விண்ணகம் விளங்கு திங்கள் வெண்குடை நிழலின் வைகிக்","கண்ணம ருலகம் காக்கும் கழலடி வாழ்க வென்றார்."],"p":"","r":"The poets, skilled in the books of the lute and song, sing that the king's fame fills the earth, and wish long life to the feet that guard the world under the moon-like white umbrella.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pugazh","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 100","lb":"SO100","mm":1,"ev":"The poets sing and wish the king long life.","cl":[],"ag":["புலவர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["புலவர்","வேந்தன்"],"ch":"so:7"},"so:101":{"book":"soolamani","num":101,"t":["மஞ்சுடை மலையின் வல்லி தொடரவான் வணங்க நின்ற","அஞ்சுடர்க் கடவுள் காத்த வருங்குல மலரத் தோன்றி","வெஞ்சுட ரெஃக மொன்றின் வேந்துகண் ணகற்றி நின்ற","செஞ்சுடர் முடியி னாய்நின் கோலிது செல்க வென்றார்."],"p":"","r":"The poets praise the king as the one who rose from a line guarded by a bright god, who turned rival kings' eyes away with one fierce spear, and bid his rule go on.","n":"The verse credits the line's survival to a god of bright lustre; who is meant is not named.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sengol","pugazh","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 101","lb":"SO101","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["புலவர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["புலவர்","வேந்தன்"],"ch":"so:7"},"so:102":{"book":"soolamani","num":102,"t":["இன்னணம் பலரு மேத்த வினிதினங் கிருந்த வேந்தன்","பொன்னணி வாயில் காக்கும் பூங்கழ லவனை நோக்கி","என்னவ ரேனு மாக நாழிகை யேழு காறும்","கன்னவி றோளி னாய்நீ வரவிடு காவ லென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"The king, seated among the praise, tells the doorkeeper to lift the guard and let in whoever comes until seven nazhikai have passed.","n":"காவல் வரவிடு can mean opening the gate or letting the guard down; either way the order is that the court is open to any caller for seven nazhikai.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 102","lb":"SO102","mm":1,"ev":"The king orders the doorkeeper to admit anyone for seven nazhikai.","cl":[],"ag":["வேந்தன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["வேந்தன்","வாயில் காக்கும்"],"ch":"so:7"},"so:103":{"book":"soolamani","num":103,"t":["ஆயிடை யலகின் மெய்ந்நூ லபவுசென் றடங்கி நின்றான்","சேயிடை நிகழ்வ தெல்லாஞ் சிந்தையிற் றெளிந்த நீரான்","மேயிடை பெறுவ னாயின் வேந்துகாண் குறுவன் கொல்லோ","நீயிடை யறிசொல் லென்றோர் நிமித்திக னெறியிற் சொன்னான்."],"p":"","r":"A learned astrologer, who knows by thought what happens far off, tells the doorkeeper that if he finds an opening he would see the king, and bids the man judge the moment.","n":"Line 1 அலகின் மெய்ந்நூல் அபவு சென்று is obscure; the sense that he is a master of an exact science and waits at the gate is clear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kalvi","kaalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 103","lb":"SO103","mm":1,"ev":"The astrologer asks the doorkeeper whether he may see the king.","cl":[["one trained in the exact science knows what happens far away by thought","general","reported",2,"narrator"]],"ag":["நிமித்திகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["நிமித்திகன்","வாயில் காக்கும்"],"ch":"so:7"},"so:104":{"book":"soolamani","num":104,"t":["ஆங்கவ னரசர் கோமான் குறிப்பறிந் தருளப் பட்டீர்","ஈங்கினிப் புகுமி னென்றா னிறைவனை யவனுஞ் சேர்ந்தான்","வீங்கிருந் தானை யானும் வெண்மலர் பிடித்த கையால்","ஓங்கிருந் தானங் காட்டி யுவந்தினி திருக்க வென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"The doorkeeper, knowing the king's mind, tells the astrologer he has leave to enter; the king, a white flower in his hand, shows him a high seat and bids him sit at ease.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","kurippu","virundhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 104","lb":"SO104","mm":1,"ev":"The doorkeeper admits the astrologer and the king offers him a seat.","cl":[],"ag":["வாயில் காக்கும்","நிமித்திகன்","வேந்தன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:7"},"so:105":{"book":"soolamani","num":105,"t":["உற்றத னொழுக்கிற் கேற்ப வுலகுப சார நீக்கிக்","கொற்றவன் குறிப்பு நோக்கி யிருந்தபின் குணக்குன் றொப்பான்","முற்றிய வுலகின் மூன்று காலமூ முழுது நோக்கிக்","கற்றநூற் புலமை தன்னைக் காட்டுதல் கருதிச் சொன்னான்."],"p":"","r":"Having laid aside worldly courtesies as his discipline asks, the astrologer, who has looked fully at the three times, waits for the king's sign and then speaks, wishing to show the mastery of the books he has learned.","n":"The narrator gives his motive as showing his learning (line 4), which colours the prophecy that follows; Naladiyar 52 treats the study of astrology as folly.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalvi","kurippu","kaalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 105","lb":"SO105","mm":1,"ev":"The astrologer, after waiting on the king's sign, begins to speak.","cl":[["a man learned in the books can see all three times, past, present and to come","general","reported",3,"narrator"]],"ag":["நிமித்திகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:7"},"so:106":{"book":"soolamani","num":106,"t":["கயந்தலைக் களிற்றி னாயோர் கனாக்கண்ட துளது கங்குல்","நயந்தது தெரியி னம்பி நளிகடல் வண்ணன் றன்னை","விசும்பகத் திழிந்து வந்தோர் வேழம்வெண் போது சேர்ந்த","தயங்கொளி மாலை சூட்டித் தன்னிட மடைந்த தன்றே."],"p":"","r":"The astrologer tells the king that a dream was seen in the night: an elephant came down from the sky, set a shining garland of white flowers on the sea-hued young lord, and went to its own place.","n":"The Tamil does not say whose dream; 111 suggests the king's. The identification of the sea-hued young lord with Tivittan rests on 109.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kanavu","oozh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 106","lb":"SO106","mm":1,"ev":"The astrologer relates the night's dream of the sky-descending elephant that garlands the young lord.","cl":[],"ag":["நிமித்திகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["நிமித்திகன்","வேந்தன்"],"ch":"so:7"},"so:107":{"book":"soolamani","num":107,"t":["மன்மலர்ந் தகன்ற மார்ப மற்றதன் பயனுங் கேண்மோ","நன்மலர் நகைகொள் கண்ணி நம்பித னாம மேத்தி","மின்மலர்ந் திலங்கு பைம்பூண் விஞ்சைவேந் தொருவன் வந்து","தன்மக ளொருத்தி தன்னைத் தந்தனன் போகு மென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"The astrologer gives the dream's fruit: a Vidyadhara king in shining jewels will come, praise the young lord's name, give him one of his daughters and go.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kanavu","oozh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 107","lb":"SO107","mm":1,"ev":"The astrologer foretells that a Vidyadhara king will give his daughter to the young lord.","cl":[["a dream of an elephant garlanding a youth foretells a bride given by a king of the Vidyadharas","general","reported",4,"nimittikan"]],"ag":["நிமித்திகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["நிமித்திகன்","வேந்தன்"],"ch":"so:7"},"so:108":{"book":"soolamani","num":108,"t":["கட்பகர் திவலை சிந்துங் கடிகமழ் குவளைக் கண்ணித்","திட்பமாஞ் சிலையி னாய்! நீ தெளிகநா னேளு சென்றால்","ஒட்பமா யுரைக்க வல்லா னொருவனோ ரோலை கொண்டு","புட்பமா கரண்ட மென்னும் பொழிலகத் திழியு மென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"The astrologer adds that when seven have passed, a man able to speak well will come down with a letter into the garden called Pushpakarandam.","n":"நானேளு (line 2) looks like a misprint for நா னேழு; the unit of seven is not named and may be the seven nazhikai of 102.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thoothu","kaalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 108","lb":"SO108","mm":1,"ev":"The astrologer foretells the arrival of a messenger with a letter in the Pushpakarandam garden.","cl":[["a messenger with a letter will descend into the Pushpakarandam garden after seven have passed","general","reported",4,"nimittikan"]],"ag":["நிமித்திகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["நிமித்திகன்","வேந்தன்"],"ch":"so:7"},"so:109":{"book":"soolamani","num":109,"t":["என்றவ னியம்பக் கேட்டே யிருந்தவர் வியந்து நோக்கிச்","சென்றுயர் திலகக் கண்ணித் திவிட்டனித் திறத்த னேயாம்","ஒன்றிய வுலக மெல்லா மொருகுடை நீழற் காக்கும்","பொன்றிக ழலங்கன் மார்ப போற்றிபொய் யன்றி தென்றார்."],"p":"","r":"Those seated marvel and answer that Tivittan is indeed of this kind, the one who will protect the whole joined world under one umbrella, and say the prophecy is not false.","n":"The vocative in lines 3-4 addresses the one who will rule under one umbrella, and the court is taken to speak of Tivittan; they confirm the astrologer without any test.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","oozh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 109","lb":"SO109","mm":1,"ev":"The assembly names Tivittan as the one foretold and affirms the prophecy.","cl":[["Tivittan will guard the whole world under a single umbrella","general","reported",3,"sabhaiyor"]],"ag":["இருந்தவர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["இருந்தவர்",""],"ch":"so:7"},"so:110":{"book":"soolamani","num":110,"t":["உரையமைந் திருப்ப விப்பா லோதுநா ழிகையொன் றோட","முரசமொன் றதிர்த்த தோங்கி யதிர்தலு முகத்தி னாலே","அரசவை விடுத்த வேந்த னகத்தநூ லவரை நோக்கி","வரையுயர் மாடக் கோயின் மந்திர சாலை சேர்ந்தான்."],"p":"","r":"After about one nazhikai a drum sounds; the king dismisses the court with a look, turns to his counsellors and goes to the council hall of his tall mansion.","n":"அகத்தநூலவர் is read as those learned in the counsel-books, the ministers (compare மந்திரத்துளார் in 113).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["avai","soozhchi","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 110","lb":"SO110","mm":1,"ev":"The king ends the audience and withdraws to the council hall with his counsellors.","cl":[],"ag":["வேந்தன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:7"},"so:111":{"book":"soolamani","num":111,"t":["கங்குல்வாய்க் கனவவன் கருதிச் சொற்றதும்","மங்கலப் பெரும்பயன் வகுத்த வண்ணமும்","கொங்கலர் தெரியலான் கூறிக் கொய்ம்மலர்த்","தொங்கலார் நெடுமுடி சுடரத் தூக்கினான்."],"p":"","r":"In the council hall the king recounts the night's dream as the astrologer interpreted it and the fortunate fruit he assigned, and lifts his crowned head.","n":"தூக்கினான் is read as raising his head; it could also mean weighing the matter.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["soozhchi","kanavu","amaichu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 111","lb":"SO111","mm":1,"ev":"The king repeats the dream and its fruit to his ministers.","cl":[],"ag":["வேந்தன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:7"},"so:112":{"book":"soolamani","num":112,"t":["சூழுநீ ருலகெலாந் தொழுது தன்னடி","நீழலே நிரந்துகண் படுக்கு நீர்மையான்","ஆழியங் கிழவனா யலரு மென்பது","பாழியந் தோளினாய் பண்டுங் கேட்டுமே."],"p":"","r":"The ministers answer the king that he whom the whole encircled world will worship and lie down in the shade of will bloom as lord of the discus, which they had heard before.","n":"பண்டுங் கேட்டுமே says they had heard this before; an earlier prophecy about the prince is not shown here.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","oozh","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 112","lb":"SO112","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["the one before whom the whole world bows will become lord of the discus, a universal ruler","general","reported",3,"amaichar"]],"ag":["மந்திரக் கிழவர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["மந்திரக் கிழவர்","வேந்தன்"],"ch":"so:7"},"so:113":{"book":"soolamani","num":113,"t":["நற்றவ முடையனே நம்பி யென்றுபூண்","விற்றவழ் சுடரொளி விளங்கு மேனியக்","கொற்றவன் குறிப்பினை யறிந்து கூறிய","மற்றவர் தொடங்கினார் மந்தி ரத்துளார்."],"p":"","r":"The ministers, knowing the king's mind and saying the young lord must have good austerity, begin their counsel.","n":"The verse does not say that the austerity was of a past birth, and no school's doctrine of merit is named.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["soozhchi","thavam","oozh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 113","lb":"SO113","mm":1,"ev":"The ministers begin to give counsel.","cl":[["the young lord's rising fortune comes from good austerity","general","reported",1,"amaichar"]],"ag":["மந்திரக் கிழவர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:7"},"so:114":{"book":"soolamani","num":114,"t":["சங்க லேகையுஞ் சக்கர லேகையும்","அங்கை யுள்ளன வையற் காதலால்","சங்க பாணியான் சக்க ராயுதம்","அங்கை யேந்துமென் றறையல் வேண்டுமே."],"p":"","r":"The ministers say that the conch-line and the discus-line are on the young lord's palm, so he will hold the discus weapon in his hand like the conch-handed lord.","n":"The comparison borrows the image of Vishnu, the conch-handed; the verse does not call the prince Vishnu.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kurippu","oozh","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 114","lb":"SO114","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["conch and discus lines on the palm show a man who will wield the discus","general","reported",2,"amaichar"]],"ag":["மந்திரக் கிழவர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["மந்திரக் கிழவர்","வேந்தன்"],"ch":"so:7"},"so:115":{"book":"soolamani","num":115,"t":["விஞ்சைய ருலகுடை வேந்தன் றன்மகன்","வஞ்சியங் கொடியிடை மயிலஞ் சாயலான்","எஞ்சலின் றியங்கிவந் திழியு மாய்விடில்","அஞ்சிநின் றவ்வுல காட்சி செல்லுமே."],"p":"","r":"The ministers say that if the Vidyadhara king's slender, peacock-gaited daughter comes down without fail, the rule of that world will follow.","n":"Line 4 அஞ்சிநின் றவ்வுலகாட்சி செல்லுமே is hard to construe; the sense that the Vidyadhara realm's rule is tied to the bride's descent is not certain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["arasu","oozh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 115","lb":"SO115","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["மந்திரக் கிழவர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["மந்திரக் கிழவர்","வேந்தன்"],"ch":"so:7"},"so:116":{"book":"soolamani","num":116,"t":["நாமினி மற்றவன் மொழிந்த நாளினால்","தேமரு சிலாதலந் திருத்தித் தெய்வமாம்","தூமரு மாலையாய் துரும காந்தனைக்","காமரு பொழிலிடைக் காவல் வைத்துமே."],"p":"","r":"The ministers propose that, for the day the astrologer named, they set the fragrant stone platform in order and post Turumakanthan as guard in the lovely garden.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["soozhchi","amaichu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 116","lb":"SO116","mm":1,"ev":"The ministers propose to prepare the stone platform and set Turumakanthan to guard the garden.","cl":[],"ag":["மந்திரக் கிழவர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["மந்திரக் கிழவர்","வேந்தன்"],"ch":"so:7"},"so:117":{"book":"soolamani","num":117,"t":["என்றவர் மொழிந்தபி னிலங்கு பூணினான்","நன்றது பெரிதுமென் றருளி நாடொறும்","சென்றவன் காக்கென மொழிந்து தேங்குழல்","இன்றுணை யவர்கடங் கோயி லெய்தினான்."],"p":"","r":"The king approves the plan as very good, orders Turumakanthan to go and guard daily, and goes to the palace of his dear consorts.","n":"இன்றுணையவர் கடங் கோயில் is read as the palace of his consorts.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","soozhchi","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 117","lb":"SO117","mm":1,"ev":"The king approves the plan, charges the guard and goes to the consorts' palace.","cl":[],"ag":["வேந்தன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:7"},"so:118":{"book":"soolamani","num":118,"t":["எரிபடு விரிசுட ரிலங்கு பூணினான்","திருவடி தொழுதுசெல் துரும காந்தனும்","வரிபடு மதுகர முரல வார்சினைச்","சொரிபடு மதுமலர்ச் சோலை நண்ணினான்."],"p":"","r":"Turumakanthan bows at the king's feet and goes to the flower garden where bees hum and honey drips from the long branches.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["panivu","aalvinai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 118","lb":"SO118","mm":1,"ev":"Turumakanthan goes to the garden to keep watch.","cl":[],"ag":["துரும காந்தன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:7"},"so:119":{"book":"soolamani","num":119,"t":["புரிசை நீண்மதிற் போதன மாநகர்","அரசர் வார்த்தையவ் வாறது நிற்கவே","விரைசெய் வார்பொழில் விஞ்சையர் சேடிமேல்","உரையை யாமுரைப் பானுற நின்றதே."],"p":"","r":"The poet leaves the kings' talk where it stands and promises to tell of the Vidyadhara country on the ridge.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naadu","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 119","lb":"SO119","mm":1,"ev":"The poet turns from the kings of the plain to the city of the Vidyadharas.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:8"},"so:120":{"book":"soolamani","num":120,"t":["நிலவு வெண்சுடர் பாய்நில மொப்பநீண்","டுலவு நீள்கட றீண்டியு யர்ந்துபோய்","இலகு வின்மணி வானியன் மாடெலாம்","விலக நின்றது விஞ்சையர் குன்றமே."],"p":"","r":"The Vidyadhara hill, pale as moonlit ground, stretches to the sea and rises so high that the jewelled mansions of the sky stand aside for it.","n":"Lines 3-4 are hard to construe; the sense of the hill outreaching the sky's mansions is the likely one.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["naadu","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 120","lb":"SO120","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:8"},"so:121":{"book":"soolamani","num":121,"t":["தொக்க வானவர் சூழ்குழ லாரொடும்","ஒக்க வாங்குள ராய்விளை யாடலால்","உக்க சோதிகள் சோலையி னூடெலாம்","செக்கர் வானக முஞ்சிறி தொக்குமே."],"p":"","r":"Gods gather with their women to play in the groves, and the sparks that fall from their jewels make the groves look like the red evening sky.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 121","lb":"SO121","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:8"},"so:122":{"book":"soolamani","num":122,"t":["அவிழுங் காதல ராயர மங்கையர்","பவழ வாயமு தம்பரு கிக்களி","தவழு மென்முலை புல்லத் ததைந்ததார்","கமழு நின்றன கற்பகச் சோலையே."],"p":"","r":"Heavenly women and their loving mates drink the coral-lipped nectar and embrace, so crushed garlands scent the wishing-tree groves.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 122","lb":"SO122","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:8"},"so:123":{"book":"soolamani","num":123,"t":["கிளருஞ் சூழொளிக் கின்னர தேவர்தம்","வளரும் பூண்முலை யாரொடு வைகலால்","துளருஞ் சந்தனச் சோலைக ளூடெலாம்","நளிருந் தெய்வ நறுங்குளிர் நாற்றமே."],"p":"","r":"Kinnara gods dwell with their women in the sandal groves, which are full of a cool divine fragrance.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inbam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 123","lb":"SO123","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:8"},"so:124":{"book":"soolamani","num":124,"t":["மங்குல் வாடைமந் தார்வன மீதுழாய்ப்","பொங்கு தாதொடு பூமதுக் கொப்பளித்","தங்க ராகம ளாயர மங்கையர்","கொங்கை வாரிகள் மேற்குதி கொள்ளுமே."],"p":"","r":"A cool wind stirs the mandara grove and the tulasi, and pollen and flower-honey spill over the breasts of the heavenly women like water.","n":"The verb in line 4 can also mean the scent leaps upon them; the image of pollen and honey as bath-water is the likelier.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 124","lb":"SO124","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:8"},"so:125":{"book":"soolamani","num":125,"t":["தேன கத்துறை யுஞ்செழுஞ் சந்தனக்","கான கத்தழை யின்கமழ் சேக்கை மேல்","ஊன கத்தவர் போகமு வந்தரோ","வான கத்தவர் வைகுவர் வைகலே."],"p":"","r":"In the sandal bowers beds of leaves are set; those who still bear flesh delight in pleasure there, while those of the sky stay day after day.","n":"The contrast between the fleshly and the sky-dwellers in lines 3-4 depends on a rare compound and may be read as two groups of lovers or as men against gods.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["inbam","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 125","lb":"SO125","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:8"},"so:126":{"book":"soolamani","num":126,"t":["மஞ்சு தோய்வரை மைந்தரொ டடிய","அஞ்சி லோதிய ராரள கப்பொடி","பஞ்ச ராகம்ப தித்தப ளிக்கறைத்","துஞ்சு பாறைகண் மேற்று தை வுற்றதே."],"p":"","r":"Women of fine hair play with the hill's youths, and the scented powder of their play falls and stains the sleeping rocks.","n":"The edition's word division in lines 3-4 is doubtful.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 126","lb":"SO126","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:8"},"so:127":{"book":"soolamani","num":127,"t":["மாத ரார்நடை கற்கிய வானிழிந்","தாது வண்டுண வூழடி யூன்றிய","பாத ராகம்ப தித்தப ளிக்கறை","காத லார்தம கண்கவர் கின்றவே."],"p":"","r":"The red dye of women's feet, learning a walk of their own, marks the rocks and draws the eyes of their lovers.","n":"Lines 1-2 are uncertain in wording; the footprint-dye is certain from line 3.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 127","lb":"SO127","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:8"},"so:128":{"book":"soolamani","num":128,"t":["ஆகு பொன்னறை மேலரு வித்திரள்","நாக கன்னிய ராடலின் ஞால்கைம்மா","வேக மும்மத வெள்ளம ளாவிய","போக மல்லிகை நாறும்பு னல்களே."],"p":"","r":"Streams run down golden rocks where naga maidens bathe, mixing with the rut of the elephants and smelling of jasmine.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","naadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 128","lb":"SO128","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:8"},"so:129":{"book":"soolamani","num":129,"t":["பூக்க ளாவன பொன்மரை பூம்பொழில்","காக்க ளாவன கற்பகச் சோலைகள்","வீக்கு வார்கழல் விஞ்சையர் சேடிமே","லூக்கி யமுரைக் கின்றதிங் கென்கொலோ."],"p":"","r":"The flowers are golden lotuses and the groves are wishing-tree gardens, so the poet asks what more need be told of the Vidyadhara ridge.","n":"The question of line 4 is a figure of speech that cuts the description short; its exact force is not settled.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","naadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 129","lb":"SO129","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:8"},"so:130":{"book":"soolamani","num":130,"t":["வரையின் மேன்மதி கோடுற வைகிய","திருவ நீளொளித் தென்றிசைச் சேடிமே","லிரத நூபுரச் சக்கர வாளமென்","றுரைசெய் பொன்னக ரொன்றுள தென்பவே."],"p":"","r":"On the southern ridge of the Vidyadharas, where the moon rests on the hill, a golden city called Ratanupura-Chakravalam is said to stand.","n":"The city's name is given here for the first time; the title of the sarukkam comes from it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naadu","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 130","lb":"SO130","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:8"},"so:131":{"book":"soolamani","num":131,"t":["அம்பொன் மாலையார்க ளித்த லத்தெ ழுந்த ரத்தவாய்க்","கொம்பா னார் கொடுத்த முத்த நீர வாய கோழரைப்","பைம்பொன் வாழை செம்பொ னேப ழுத்து வீழ்ந்த சோதியால்","வம்பு பாய்ந்து வந்தொ சிந்து சாறு சோர்வ மானுமே."],"p":"","r":"Gold plantains ripen red-gold, and their gleam seems to drip as juice on the garland-wearing women's pearl-lipped talk.","n":"The wording of lines 1-2 is corrupt or very compressed; only the gold fruit and its glow are firm.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 131","lb":"SO131","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:8"},"so:132":{"book":"soolamani","num":132,"t":["வேய்தி ழன்னி லாவி லங்கு வெள்ளி விம்மு பாளைவாய்ப்","பாய்நி ழற்ப சுங்க திர்ப்ப ரூஉம ணிக்கு லைகுலாய்ச்","சேய்நி ழற்செ ழும்பொ னாற்றி ரண்ட செம்ப ழத்தவாய்ப்","போய்நி ழற்பொ லிந்து வீழ்வ போன்ற பூக ராசியே."],"p":"","r":"Heaps of areca nut under the moonlit silver spathes look like fallen gems and ripe gold clusters.","n":"The sense of line 1 turns on a doubtful word; the image of the nuts as fallen jewels is clear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 132","lb":"SO132","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:8"},"so:133":{"book":"soolamani","num":133,"t":["காந்தி நின்ற கற்ப கந்தி ழற்க லந்து கையறப்","பாய்ந்தெ ரிந்த போல்வி ரிந்து பாரி சாத மோர்செய","வாய்ந்தெ ரிந்த பொன் மாட வாயி லாறு கண்கொளப்","போந்தெ ரிந்த போன்ம ரம்பு றம்பொ லிந்தி லங்குமே."],"p":"","r":"The wishing-trees' radiance and scattered parijata flowers look like scattered fire, and the golden mansions' doors shine behind the trees.","n":"Lines 1-2 are hard to construe.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 133","lb":"SO133","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:8"},"so:134":{"book":"soolamani","num":134,"t":["மாசில் கண்ணி மைந்த ரோடு மங்கை மார்தி ளைத்தலில்","பூசு சாந்த ழித்தி ழிந்த புள்ளி வேர்பு லர்த்தலால்","வாச முண்ட மாரு தந்தென் வண்டு பாட மாடவாய்","வீச வெள்ளி லோத்தி ரப்பொ தும்பர் பாய்ந்து விம்முமே."],"p":"","r":"Lovers sport together, ruining their sandal paste and sweating, and a wind scented with the groves and bees makes the flowers sway.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 134","lb":"SO134","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:8"},"so:135":{"book":"soolamani","num":135,"t":["ஆந்து ணர்த்த மால மும்ம சோக பல்ல வங்களும்","தாந்து ணர்த்த சந்த னத்த ழைத்த லைத்த டாயின","மாந்து ணர்ப்பொ தும்பர் வந்து வைக மற்ற தூன்றலால்","தேந்து ணர்ச்சு மந்தொ சிந்த சைந்த தேவ தாரமே."],"p":"","r":"Mango, ashoka and sandal sprouts in flower let their honey drop, and the swaying devadaru drops it too as the wind bears the scent.","n":"","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","naadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 135","lb":"SO135","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:8"},"so:136":{"book":"soolamani","num":136,"t":["தெய்வ யாறு காந்த ளஞ்சி லம்பு தேங்கொள் பூம்பொழில்","பெளவ முத்த வார்ம ணற்ப றம்பு மெளவன் மண்டபம்","எவ்வ மாடு மின்ன போலி டங்க ளின்ப மாக்கலால்","கவ்வை யாவ தந்த கர்க்கு மார னார்செய் கவ்வையே."],"p":"","r":"The divine river, groves and flowering bowers of this place are such pleasant haunts that all the noise they make is the love-god's own tumult.","n":"The place is made an arena of Kama's play, so the setting prepares the later court scenes of desire among the Vidyadharas.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 136","lb":"SO136","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:8"},"so:137":{"book":"soolamani","num":137,"t":["மற்ற மாந கர்க்கு வேந்தன் மான யானை மன்னர் கோன்","அற்ற மின்றி நின்ற சீர ழற்பெ யர்ப்பு ணர்ச்சியான்","முற்று முன்ச டிப் பெ யர்சொன் மூன்று லஃகு மான்றெழப்","பெற்று நின்ற பெற்றியான் பீடி யாவர் பேசுவார்."],"p":"","r":"The king of that city, lord of war-elephant kings, is faultless and named by a riddle of fire and syllables that the poet leaves to the reader; who could describe him?","n":"The name is given as a puzzle in lines 2-3 and cannot be fixed with certainty from the verse alone.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vendhan","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 137","lb":"SO137","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["விஞ்சையர் வேந்தன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:8"},"so:138":{"book":"soolamani","num":138,"t":["இங்கண் ஞால மெல்லை சென்றி லங்கு வெண்கு டைந்நிழல்","வெங்கண் யானை வேந்தி றைஞ்ச வென்றி யின்வி ளங்கினான்","கொங்கு கொண்டு வண்ட றைந்து குங்கு மக்கு ழம்பளாய்","அங்க ராக மங்க ணிந்த லர்ந்த வார மார்பினான்."],"p":"","r":"His white umbrella's shade reaches the world's end, fierce-eyed elephant kings bow to him, and he shines in victory with a saffron-smeared, garlanded chest.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vendhan","perumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 138","lb":"SO138","mm":1,"ev":"The Vidyadhara king receives the homage of rival elephant kings.","cl":[],"ag":["விஞ்சையர் வேந்தன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:8"},"so:139":{"book":"soolamani","num":139,"t":["விச்சை யாய முற்றினான் விஞ்சையார்க ளஞ்சநின்","றிச்சை யாய வெய்தினா னேந்து செம்பொ னீண்முடிக்","கச்சை யானை மானவேற் கண்ணி லங்கு தாரினான்","வெச்ச னுஞ்சொ லொன்றுமே விடுத்து மெய்ம்மை மேயினான்."],"p":"","r":"He has mastered every branch of knowledge, the Vidyadharas fear him, his wishes are met, and he lets no false word pass but keeps to truth.","n":"The word வெச்சனும் in line 4 is obscure; the claim of truthfulness rests on மெய்ம்மை.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["arasu","vaaimai","kalvi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 139","lb":"SO139","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["a king should keep to truth and let no falsehood pass his lips","general","asserted",4,"narrator"]],"ag":["விஞ்சையர் வேந்தன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:8"},"so:140":{"book":"soolamani","num":140,"t":["வெற்றி வெண்குடை விஞ்சையர் வேந்தவ","னொற்றை யந்தனிக் கோலுல கோம்புநாள்","குற்ற மாயதொன் றுண்டு குணங்களா","லற்ற கீழுயிர் மேலரு ளாமையே."],"p":"","r":"In the days when this Vidyadhara king guards the world under his one sceptre, there is a single fault among his virtues: he lacks grace toward the lowly lives beneath him.","n":"The poet praises the king's virtues in the previous verses and then names one flaw; compassion toward lower creatures is the Jain test of a ruler's goodness, though the verse itself names no school.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","arul","kutram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 140","lb":"SO140","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["a ruler with every virtue is still at fault if he lacks grace toward the lowest living beings","general","asserted",4,"narrator"]],"ag":["விஞ்சையர் வேந்தன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:8"},"so:141":{"book":"soolamani","num":141,"t":["செம்பொ னீண்முடி யான்சொரு வின்றலை","வெம்பு வேலவன் விஞ்சையர் மண்டிலம்","நம்பி யாள்கின்ற நாளி னடுங்கின","கம்ப மாடக் கதலிகை போதுமே."],"p":"","r":"While the golden-crowned spear-bearing Vidyadhara king rules the realm, the plantain-like banners tremble.","n":"Whether the trembling banners are an omen of the fault just named or simply a sign of his might is not clear in the Tamil.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["arasu","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 141","lb":"SO141","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["விஞ்சையர் வேந்தன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:8"},"so:142":{"book":"soolamani","num":142,"t":["மின்னு வார்ந்தமந் தாரவி ளங்கிணார்","துன்னு தொன்முடி யானொளி சென்றநாள்","மன்னு மாடவர் மேல்வளைந் திட்டன","பொன்னு னார்புரு வச்சிலை போலுமே."],"p":"","r":"In the days when his bright old crown shines, the brows of the women press on the men like bent bows.","n":"Lines 1-3 are hard to construe; the closing bow-like brow image is firm.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 142","lb":"SO142","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:8"},"so:143":{"book":"soolamani","num":143,"t":["வெண்ணி லார்ச்சுட ருந்தனி வெண்குடை","எண்ணி லாப்புக ழானினி தாண்டநா","ளுண்ணி லாப்பல வாயுள வாயின","கண்ண னாரொடு காமக்க லங்களே."],"p":"","r":"Under his white umbrella the king of endless fame rules happily, and the days of his reign run to many, spent among loving women and the ornaments of desire.","n":"Line 4 is hard to construe: the word division makes it unclear whether the women and the love-ornaments are the days' company or something counted among them.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vendhan","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 143","lb":"SO143","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:9"},"so:144":{"book":"soolamani","num":144,"t":["மாக்கண் வைய மகிழ்ந்துதன் றாணிழல்","நோக்கி வைக நுனித்தவ னாண்டநாள்","தாக்க ணங்கனை யார் தம தாயரால்","வீக்கப் பட்டன மென்முலை விம்முமே."],"p":"","r":"While the king keeps the wide earth gladly under the shade of his feet, the young girls' swelling breasts are bound by their mothers.","n":"Lines 3-4 are compressed; the binding of the girls' breasts by their mothers is the likely sense, shown as a sign of peace in his time.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vendhan","ilamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 144","lb":"SO144","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:9"},"so:145":{"book":"soolamani","num":145,"t":["வடிய வாளவ னாளவும் வாய்களில்","கடிய வாயின கள்ளவிழ் தேமல","ரடிய வாய்ப்பயப் பட்டடங் காவலர்க்","கொடிய வாயின கொங்கவிழ் சோலையே."],"p":"","r":"His keen sword is feared; kings who submit in dread of it find even the honey-dripping groves turned harsh to them.","n":"The sense of line 4 turns on a word division; the reading is uncertain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vendhan","acham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 145","lb":"SO145","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:9"},"so:146":{"book":"soolamani","num":146,"t":["மாய மாயநின் றான்வரை மார்பிடை","மேய பூமகள் போல விளங்கினாள்","தூய வாமுறு வற்றுவர் வாயவள்","வாயு வேகையென் பாள்வளர் கொம்பனாள்."],"p":"","r":"His queen Vayuvegai, a young vine of pure birth, shines on his chest like the flower-goddess on the chest of the dark Lord.","n":"The simile compares the king with the Lord who carries Lakshmi on his chest, which a Jain poet uses for royal splendour, not worship.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","vaazhkkaithunai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 146","lb":"SO146","mm":1,"ev":"The king takes Vayuvegai as his queen.","cl":[],"ag":["வாயுவேகை"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:9"},"so:147":{"book":"soolamani","num":147,"t":["பைம்பொற் பட்ட மணிந்த கொல் யானையான்","அம்பொற் பட்ட நறுங்குழ லார்க்கெலாம்","செம்பொற் பட்டஞ் செறிந்த திருநுதல்","அம்பொற் பட்டுடை யாளணி யாயினான்."],"p":"","r":"The king whose elephant wears a golden frontlet becomes the adornment of his queen, who is dressed in golden silk.","n":"","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vaazhkkaithunai","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 147","lb":"SO147","mm":1,"ev":"The king dotes on his queen.","cl":[],"ag":["விஞ்சையர் வேந்தன்","வாயுவேகை"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:9"},"so:148":{"book":"soolamani","num":148,"t":["கோவை வாய்குழ லங்குளிர் கொம்பனாள்","காவி வாணெடுங் கண்ணியக் காவலற்","காவி யாயணங் காயமிழ் தாயவன்","மேவு நீர்மைய ளாய்விருந் தாயினாள்."],"p":"","r":"The vine-like queen with lotus eyes becomes nectar to the king and, matching his nature, a feast to him.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["virundhu","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 148","lb":"SO148","mm":1,"ev":"The queen becomes the king's delight.","cl":[],"ag":["வாயுவேகை","விஞ்சையர் வேந்தன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:9"},"so:149":{"book":"soolamani","num":149,"t":["முருக்கு வாயவண் முள்ளெயிற் றேர்நகை","யுருக்க வேந்த னொருங்குறை கின்றநாள்","பெருக்க மாகப் பிறந்தனன் பெய்கழல்","அருக்க கீர்த்தி யென் பானலர் தாரினான்."],"p":"","r":"While the king lives with her many days, a son is born, Arukkakirti, wearing a garland and anklets.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["makkal","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 149","lb":"SO149","mm":1,"ev":"A son, Arukkakirti, is born to the king.","cl":[],"ag":["அருக்ககீர்த்தி"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:9"},"so:150":{"book":"soolamani","num":150,"t":["நாம நள்லொளி வேனம்பி நங்கையா","யேம நல்லுல கின்னிழிந் தந்நகைத்","தாம மல்லிகை மாலைச் சயம்பவை","காம வல்லியுங் காமுறத் தோன்றினாள்."],"p":"","r":"A daughter, Sayampavai, is born, as if come down from the good world; she is so lovely that even the vine of love desires her.","n":"The verse says she came down (இழிந்து) from the good world, which gives her birth a heavenly origin; it does not say which birth came before.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["makkal","piravi","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 150","lb":"SO150","mm":1,"ev":"A daughter, Sayampavai, is born to the king.","cl":[],"ag":["சயம்பவை"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:9"},"so:151":{"book":"soolamani","num":151,"t":["கங்கை நீரன ஞான்ற கதிரிளந்","திங்க ளாற்றெழப் பட்டது செக்கர்வான்","மங்கை மார்பிறப் பும்மட மாதரிந்","நங்கை யாற்றெழப் பாடு நவின்றதே."],"p":"","r":"The red evening sky and the young moon are set beside her as measures of her radiance, and other women bow before her.","n":"Lines 1-2 and the verb in line 4 are hard to construe; the sense that her radiance outdoes the moon and the sky is the likely one.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 151","lb":"SO151","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:9"},"so:152":{"book":"soolamani","num":152,"t":["வண்டு சூழ்மலர் போன்றள கக்கொடி","கொண்டு சூழ்ந்தது குண்டல வாண்முகங்","கெண்டை கண்கிள ரும்புரு வஞ்சிலை","உண்டு கொல்லென வுண்டும ருங்குலே."],"p":"","r":"Her hair is like a flower swarmed by bees, her eyes like carp, her brows like bows, and her waist so slight that one asks whether it is there.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","nalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 152","lb":"SO152","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:9"},"so:153":{"book":"soolamani","num":153,"t":["காதின் மீதணி கற்பகத் தொத்திணர்","ஊது தேனிற கூன்றியி ருத்தொறும்","போது தேர்முகத் தும்புரு வக்கொடி","நோத லேகொல்நொ சிந்துள வாங்களே."],"p":"","r":"Bees feed on the wishing-tree flowers at her ear while the vines of her brows seem to bend as if pained.","n":"The sense of lines 3-4 is not secure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 153","lb":"SO153","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:9"},"so:154":{"book":"soolamani","num":154,"t":["விண்ண ணங்க விழித்துவி ளங்கொளி","மண்ண ணங்குற வேவளர் வெய்திய","பெண்ண ணங்கிது தோன்றிய பின்கொலோ","கன்ன ணங்குறு காரிகை கண்டதே."],"p":"","r":"Sky and earth marvel at the radiance of her eyes, as though a goddess had been born; the maiden is gazed upon with awe.","n":"The verb அணங்க is doubtful here; it may mean 'stand in awe' or 'ache'.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 154","lb":"SO154","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:9"},"so:155":{"book":"soolamani","num":155,"t":["கொங்கு போதரு வான்குமிழ் கின்றன","அங்க ராகம ணிந்ததை யன்றியும்","நங்கை நாகரி கம்பொறை நாண்மதுத்","தங்கு வார்கொடி யிற்றளர் வித்ததே."],"p":"","r":"Her fragrance rises beyond the body-paste she wears, and her grace and modesty make the honey-dripping flower-vine droop in shame.","n":"","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","naanam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 155","lb":"SO155","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:9"},"so:156":{"book":"soolamani","num":156,"t":["மங்கு றோய்வரை மன்னவன் றொல்குடி","நங்கை போற்றியென் றேத்தி நறுங்குழல்","மங்கை மார்பலர் காப்ப வளர்ந்துதன்","கொங்கை யாற்சிறி தேகுழை வெய்தினாள்."],"p":"","r":"Tended by her attendants, who hail her as the lady of the hill-king's ancient line, she grows, her breasts a little heavy.","n":"Whether மங்கை in line 3 names the attendants or describes her is not clear from the word division.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["ilamai","makkal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 156","lb":"SO156","mm":1,"ev":"Sayampavai grows up in her attendants' care.","cl":[],"ag":["சயம்பவை","மைந்தர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:9"},"so:157":{"book":"soolamani","num":157,"t":["வாம வாணொடு நோக்கிம டங்கனி","தூம வார்குழ லாடுவர் வாயிடை","நாம நள்லொளி முள்ளெயி றுள்ளெழு","காம னாளரும் பிற்கடி கொண்டவே."],"p":"","r":"Her sidelong looks and shining teeth are called love's own guard, and her curling hair hangs loose as she looks and moves.","n":"","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 157","lb":"SO157","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:9"},"so:158":{"book":"soolamani","num":158,"t":["மஞ்சு தோய்வரை யாரஞ்சு மாண்பினால்","அஞ்சி லோதிநி னைப்பின கத்தவாய்","விஞ்சை தாம்பணி செய்தல்வி ரும்பினன்","எஞ்சி லாவகை யாலிணர் கொண்டவே."],"p":"","r":"Awed by her grace, the Vidyadharas think of her and long to serve her, bringing flower-clusters without stint.","n":"Line 3 is hard to construe.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","panivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 158","lb":"SO158","mm":1,"ev":"Vidyadharas long to serve Sayampavai.","cl":[],"ag":["விஞ்சையர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:9"},"so:159":{"book":"soolamani","num":159,"t":["நங்கை தோன்றிய பின்னகை வேலினாற்","கங்கண் ஞாமல மர்ந்தடி மைத்தொழில்","தங்க நீண்முடி யாற்றலை நின்றனர்","வெங்கண் யானைவி ளங்கொளி வேந்தரே."],"p":"","r":"Once she is born, the kings with fierce-eyed elephants bow with their long crowns and take up service at her feet, won by the lance of her smile.","n":"Her smile is likened to a spear, so kings who bow to the father's sword in verse 145 bow here to the daughter's beauty.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vendhan","adimai","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 159","lb":"SO159","mm":1,"ev":"Kings submit to Sayampavai's beauty as if to a conqueror.","cl":[],"ag":["மன்னர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:9"},"so:160":{"book":"soolamani","num":160,"t":["நங்கையாள் வளர்ந்து காம நறுமுகை துணர வைத்து","மங்கையாம் பிராய மெய்தி வளரிய நின்ற நாளும்","பைங்கண்மால் யானை யாற்குப் பருவம்வந் திறுத்த தென்றாள்","வங்கவாய்ப் பவழச் செவ்வாய் வயந்தமா திலகை யென்பாள்."],"p":"","r":"As she grows to a woman, love's bud swelling in her, Vayantamatilakai says that the season has come for the elephant-lord.","n":"The elephant-lord is not named; whether he is a suitor or the king is not clear. Whether பருவம் means the spring or the girl's age is left open; verse 161 gives spring.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["ilamai","kaalam","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 160","lb":"SO160","mm":1,"ev":"Vayantamatilakai tells that the season of love has come.","cl":[],"ag":["வயந்தமா திலகை"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:9"},"so:161":{"book":"soolamani","num":161,"t":["தேங்குலா மலங்கன் மாலைச் செறிகழன் மன்னர் மன்ன!","பூங்குலாய் விரிந்த சோலைப் பொழிமதுத் திவலை தூவக்","கோங்கெலாங் கமழ மாட்டாக் குணமிலார் செல்வ மேபோல்","பாங்கெலாஞ் செம்பொன் பூப்ப விரிந்தது பருவ மென்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"Vayantamatilakai tells the king of kings that the season has come, and the golden kongu flowers bloom as the wealth of the worthless does, which spreads no scent.","n":"The king of kings addressed is not named in the Tamil; he may be the Vidyadhara king. The simile faults wealth that lacks worth, not wealth itself.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaalam","selvam","naadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 161","lb":"SO161","mm":1,"ev":"Vayantamatilakai tells the king of kings that spring has come.","cl":[["the wealth of those without virtue spreads no good, as the kongu tree in bloom spreads no scent","general","asserted",3,"vayantamatilakai"]],"ag":["வயந்தமா திலகை"],"ec":[],"spk":["வயந்தமா திலகை",""],"ch":"so:9"},"so:162":{"book":"soolamani","num":162,"t":["வேய்ந்திண ரொசிந்த சோலை வேனிலான் வென்றி யோகைத்","தேந்துணர் கொடுப்ப மூழ்கித் தேறல் வாய் நெகிழ மாந்தித்","தாந்துணர் துணையோ டாடிச் சாறுகொண் டூறு மேரார்","மாந்துண ரொசிய வேறி மதர்த்தன மனிவண் டெல்லாம்."],"p":"","r":"Honey-drunk bees in the flowering grove dive into the nectar, bathe in it and play with their mates in the spring king's victory.","n":"","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["inbam","naadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 162","lb":"SO162","mm":1,"ev":"Bees feast on nectar in the spring grove.","cl":[],"ag":["வண்டு"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:9"},"so:163":{"book":"soolamani","num":163,"t":["கடிமலர்க் கணையி னான்றன் கழலடி பரவிக் காமர்","படிமலர்த் தும்பி யென்னும் பாண்படை தொடர்ந்து பாடக்","கொடிவளர் மகளிர் பூங்கட் குடைந்துநீர் குடிமி னென்று","வடிமலர் வள்ளத் தேந்த வாய்மடுத் திட்ட வன்றே."],"p":"","r":"Bees are shown as bards singing at the feet of the god of love, while flower-vines like women hold out honey cups and bid them drink.","n":"The flowers pour as women do, so the grove acts out a feast of the god of love.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inbam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 163","lb":"SO163","mm":1,"ev":"Bees praise Kaman and drink nectar offered by blossoms.","cl":[],"ag":["வண்டு"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:9"},"so:164":{"book":"soolamani","num":164,"t":["அஞ்சுடர் முருக்கி னங்கே ழணிமல ரணிந்து கொம்பர்த்","துஞ்சிடை பெறாது தும்பி துவன்றிமேற் றுகைக்குந் தோற்றம்","செஞ்சுட ரிலங்குஞ் செந்தீக் கருஞ்சுடர்க் கந்துள் சிந்தி","மஞ்சுடை மயங்கு கானம் மண்டிய வகையிற் றன்றே."],"p":"","r":"Bees swarm over the flame-red murukku blossoms, and the sight is like fire fallen upon a dark smoky forest.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 164","lb":"SO164","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:9"},"so:165":{"book":"soolamani","num":165,"t":["அந்தழை யசோகம் பூத்த வழகுகண் டவாவி னோக்கி","வெந்தழற் பிறங்க லென்று வெருவிய மறுவி றும்பி","கொந்தவிழ்ந் துமிழப் பட்ட குளிர்மதுத் திவலை தூவச்","செந்தழற் பிறங்க லன்மை தெளிந்துசென் றடைந்த வன்றே."],"p":"","r":"A bee afraid that the red ashoka blossom is a blaze finds when pollen drops that it is no fire, and goes to it.","n":"The bee's fear and relief show a mistaken seeing set right by a nearer look.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","therithal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 165","lb":"SO165","mm":1,"ev":"A bee fears a blossom as fire, finds out it is not, and goes to it.","cl":[],"ag":["வண்டு"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:9"},"so:166":{"book":"soolamani","num":166,"t":["மாஞ்சினை கறித்த துண்டந் துவர்த்தலின் மருங்கு நீண்ட","பூஞ்சினை முருக்கஞ் சோலைப் பூக்கள்வா யார மாந்தித்","தீஞ்சுவை மிழற்று கின்ற சிறுகுயில் செல்வ ரேனும்","தாஞ்சுவை திரிந்த பிஇன்றைச் சார்பவ ரில்லை யன்றே."],"p":"","r":"The cuckoo, whose mouth is sour from nibbling mango, drinks murukku flowers and sings; even the rich, once their sweetness turns, find none to come near them.","n":"The likeness (செல்வரேனும்) is drawn only in the last two lines; whose sweetness turns, the wealth or the person, is not said.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","nilaiyaamai","natpu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 166","lb":"SO166","mm":1,"ev":"A cuckoo leaves sour mango for sweet murukku flowers.","cl":[["those who lose their sweetness lose their followers, even the rich","general","asserted",4,"narrator"]],"ag":["குயில்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:9"},"so:167":{"book":"soolamani","num":167,"t":["கோவைவண் டூது கின்ற குரவெனுங் குரைகொண் மாதர்","பாவைகொண் டாடு கின்ற பருவத்தே பயின்ற காமன்","ஆவிகொண் டிவளிக் கைவிட் டகலுமோ வென்று தத்தம்","பூவையுங் கிளியுங் கொண்டு புலம்பின பொழில்க ளெல்லாம்."],"p":"","r":"Girls at doll-play, already touched by love's god, fear he will take their lives and leave; they carry their parrots and mynas and wail through all the groves.","n":"The syntax of lines 1 to 3 is compressed in the edition's word division; the sense of the girls' fear is held with some doubt.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 167","lb":"SO167","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:10"},"so:168":{"book":"soolamani","num":168,"t":["வயந்தமாங் குணர்த்தக் கேட்டே மன்னவன் மக்க ளோடு","முயர்ந்ததன் னுரிமை யோடு முரிமைகாப் பவர்க ளோடும்","கயந்தலைக் களிருந் தேரும் வையமுங் கவின வேறி","நயந்தன னகரி னீங்கி னோவன நண்ணி னானே."],"p":"","r":"Told of spring, the king leaves the city with his children, his household and the keepers of his household, riding elephant and chariot, and comes to the pleasure groves.","n":"The first line's வயந்தம் may be spring personified as the announcer; the edition's division leaves this open.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["arasu","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 168","lb":"SO168","mm":1,"ev":"The king sets out from the city with his children and retinue for the spring groves.","cl":[],"ag":["வேந்தன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:10"},"so:169":{"book":"soolamani","num":169,"t":["கோமான்சென் றணைதலுமே கொங்கணிந்த மலர்தூவித்","தேமாநின் றெதிர்கொள்ளச் சிறுகுயில்போற் றிசைத்தனவே","வாமான்றேர் மன்னற்கு மங்கலஞ்சொன் மகளிரைப்போற்","றூமாண்ட விளங்கொடிதந் தளிர்க்கையாற் றொழுதனவே."],"p":"","r":"As the king arrives, the groves greet him: flowers scatter, koel birds sing a blessing, and slender creepers lift their buds in salutation like women who speak good omens.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 169","lb":"SO169","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:10"},"so:170":{"book":"soolamani","num":170,"t":["கடிவாச மலர்விண்ட கமழ்தாது கழலவற்கு","வடிவாசப் பொடியாக வனவல்லி சொரிந்தனவே","புடைவாசங் கொள மாலம் பூங்கவரி யெடுத்தெறியக்","குடைமாக மெனவேந்திக் கோங்கம்போ தவிழ்ந்தனவே."],"p":"","r":"The woodland honours him further: creepers pour pollen as scented powder, boughs wave like fly-whisks, and the kongu buds open as umbrellas.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 170","lb":"SO170","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:10"},"so:171":{"book":"soolamani","num":171,"t":["கொடியாடு நெடுநகரக் கோமான்றன் குணம்பரவி","அடிபாடு மவர்களென வணிவண்டு முரன்றனவே","வடிவாய வேலவற்கு மலர்ச்சின்னஞ் சொரிவனபோல்","கொடுவாய கிளிகோதிக் குளிர்நறும்போ துகுத்தனவே."],"p":"","r":"Bees hum like bards singing his virtue, and parrots shake down cool flowers as though strewing the tokens of homage before the spear-bearing lord.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","pugazh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 171","lb":"SO171","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:10"},"so:172":{"book":"soolamani","num":172,"t":["குரவகத்து குடைந்தாடிக் குளிர்நறவங் கொப்பளித்தார்த்","தரவவண்டின் னிசைபாட வருவிநீ ரளைந்துராய்","விரைமலர்ந்த துணர்வீசி விரைஞாற வருதென்றல்","புரவலன்றன் றிருமுடிமேற் போதலர வசைத்ததே."],"p":"","r":"A breeze bathes in the creepers' honey and the stream, sets the bees singing, and loosens flowers on the king's crown.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 172","lb":"SO172","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:10"},"so:173":{"book":"soolamani","num":173,"t":["இன்னவா றிளவேனி லெதிர்கொள்ள வெழில்யானை","மன்னவாந் தனிச்செங்கோன் மறவேல்வை யகவேந்தன்","தன்னவா மடவாரைத் தானுவந்து பொழில்காட்டி","மின்னவா மிடைநோவ விளையாட வருளினான்."],"p":"","r":"With spring thus welcoming him, the king of the earth takes pleasure in showing his women the groves and bids them play till their waists ache.","n":"The poet names him with the sceptre (செங்கோன்) in passing; the verse makes no claim about justice and links no sceptre to the play.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","sengol","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 173","lb":"SO173","mm":1,"ev":"The king shows the groves to his women and bids them play.","cl":[],"ag":["வேந்தன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:10"},"so:174":{"book":"soolamani","num":174,"t":["எரியணிந்த விளம்பிண்டி யிணரார்ந்த விடமெல்லாம்","பொரியணிந்த புன்குதிர்ந்து பூநாறுந் துறையெல்லாம்","வரியணி ந்து வண்டூத வளர்கின்ற விளவேனில்","புரியணிந்த குழலீர்நுஞ் செல்வம்போற் பொலிந்ததே."],"p":"","r":"The mango and the flaming ashoka, the punnai with its scattered pollen, and the bee-hummed banks show spring shining like the wealth of the women addressed.","n":"The comparison of spring's bloom to the women's wealth (செல்வம்) turns the scene's praise toward the women, not the season.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 174","lb":"SO174","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:10"},"so:175":{"book":"soolamani","num":175,"t":["காரணிந்த குழலீர்நுங் கைத்தலங்க டகைநோக்கிச்","சீரணிந்த செழும்பிண்டி தளிரீன்று திகழ்ந்தனவே","வாரணிந்த முலையீர்நும் மருங்குறனின் வகைநோக்கி","ஏரணிந்த குருக்கத்தி யிளங்கொடித்தா யீன்றனவே."],"p":"","r":"The shoots of the ashoka and the young creepers are said to have taken their forms from the women's hands and waists, so nature is made to borrow from the body.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 175","lb":"SO175","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:10"},"so:176":{"book":"soolamani","num":176,"t":["மாந்தளிரிங் கிவைநுமது நிறங்கொண்டு வளர்ந்தனவே","ஏந்திளந்தீங் குயிலிவைநுஞ் சொற்கற்பா னிசைந்தனவே","தேந்தளங்கு குழலீர்நுஞ் செவ்வாயி னெழினோக்கித்","தாந்தளிர்மென் முருக்கினிய தாதொடு ததைந்தனவே."],"p":"","r":"Mango shoots take the women's colour, koels learn their speech, and flowering trees gather their lips' red: spring is drawn as a copy of women.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 176","lb":"SO176","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:10"},"so:177":{"book":"soolamani","num":177,"t":["காவியுஞ் செங் கழுநீருங் கமலமுங் கண் விரிந்துநளி","வாவியு மண் டபமுமெழின் மதனனையு மருட்டுமே","தூதுயருங் கிளியன்ன சொல்லினீர் துணையில்லார்","ஆவியுய்ந் துள்ளாராத லரிதேயிவ் விள வேனில்."],"p":"","r":"Lotus and water-lily ponds and pavilions would dazzle even the love-god, but women without mates, with sweet parrot speech, will hardly live through this spring.","n":"The verse turns the season's beauty into a danger for the unpartnered, as the Kural's love chapters do for the parted.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamanoy","pirivu","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 177","lb":"SO177","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:10"},"so:178":{"book":"soolamani","num":178,"t":["இன்னண மிளையவர் மருள வீண்டுசீர்","மன்னவன் வயந்தமாட் டருளி மாமணிக்","கன்னவில் புரிசையுட் கடவுட் காக்கிய","பொன்னவி றிருநகர் பூவொ டெய்தினான்."],"p":"","r":"Having thus delighted the young women, the king takes leave of the spring, and goes with flowers to the golden city of the temple shrine.","n":"மன்னவன் வயந்தமாட் டருளி is difficult; the sense of taking leave of spring is a reading, not fixed.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 178","lb":"SO178","mm":1,"ev":"The king leaves the spring grove and goes to the city where the god's shrine stands.","cl":[],"ag":["வேந்தன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:10"},"so:179":{"book":"soolamani","num":179,"t":["உலமுறை தோளினா னுவகை கூர்ந்தனன்","குலமுறை வழிபடுந் தெய்வக் கோயிலை","வலமுறை வந்தனன் வரலு மாமணிக்","கலமுறை கதிர்நகைக் கபாடம் போழ்ந்ததே."],"p":"","r":"Glad, the king walks round the shrine of the clan's god from right to left, and its gem-set doors burst open with bright light.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","iraivan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 179","lb":"SO179","mm":1,"ev":"The king circles the shrine and its jewelled doors open.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:10"},"so:180":{"book":"soolamani","num":180,"t":["பிணிநிலை பெயர்ப்பன பிறவி தீர்ப்பன","மணிநிலை விசும்பொடு வரங்க ளீவன","கணிநிலை யிலாத்திறற் கடவுட் டானகம்","மணிநிலைச் சுடரொளி மலர்ந்து தோன்றவே."],"p":"","r":"The shrine of the god of unmeasured might shines: it is said to drive out ills, end births, and give boons to heaven.","n":"The verse credits the shrine and its god with ending births (பிறவி தீர்ப்பன) without saying whose own effort does it; Jain doctrine elsewhere leaves release to the self's own work.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","piravi","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 180","lb":"SO180","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["the shrine of the Arhat removes ills, ends births and gives boons","jain","asserted",1,"narrator"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:10"},"so:181":{"book":"soolamani","num":181,"t":["மெய்ம்மயி ரெறிந்தொளி துளும்பு மேனியன்","கைம்முகிழ் முடித்தடங் கதழச் சேர்த்தினான்","வெம்மைசெய் வினைத்துகள் விளிய வென்றவன்","செம்மலர்த் திருந்தடி சீரி னேத்தினான்."],"p":"","r":"The king, with hair standing on end and radiant, joins his hands and praises well the red lotus feet of him who conquered the harsh dust of karma.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","vinai","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 181","lb":"SO181","mm":1,"ev":"The king bows in worship before the conqueror of karma.","cl":[["the one worthy of praise is he who has conquered the karma that torments","jain","asserted",3,"narrator"]],"ag":["வேந்தன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:10"},"so:182":{"book":"soolamani","num":182,"t":["அணியாது மொளிதிகழு மாரணங்கு திருமூர்த்தி","கணியாது முழுதுணர்ந்த கடவுளென் றறையுமே","கணியாது முழுதுணர்ந்த கடவுளென் றறைந்தாலும்","அணிஞால முடையாயை யறிவாரோ வரியரே."],"p":"","r":"They say the god of pure body and bright limbs is called the knower of all; yet even called so, the world-holder is hard to truly know.","n":"The refrain repeats the line, an old pattern of hymn that turns each epithet back on itself; the claim is about the limits of naming.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","arivu","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 182","lb":"SO182","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["the Arhat is omniscient but cannot be fully known even by one who names him so","jain","asserted",4,"arasan"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["வேந்தன்","இறைவன்"],"ch":"so:10"},"so:183":{"book":"soolamani","num":183,"t":["பகைநாறு மயிற்படைகள் பயிலாத திருமூர்த்திறை","இகன்மாற வென்றுயர்ந்த விறைவனென் றறையுமே","இகன்மாற வென்றுயர்ந்த விறைவவென் றறைந்தாலும்","அகன்ஞால முடையாயை யறிவாரோ வரியரே."],"p":"","r":"They say the god who has no bird-borne weapon and has conquered all strife is the Lord; yet even so named, he is hard to know.","n":"The unarmed god may be set against gods who bear weapons; the verse does not name any rival.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 183","lb":"SO183","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["the Arhat has conquered strife and carries no weapon, but even so he is hard to know","jain","asserted",4,"arasan"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["வேந்தன்","இறைவன்"],"ch":"so:10"},"so:184":{"book":"soolamani","num":184,"t":["திருமறுவு வலனணிந்து திகழ்கின்ற திருமூர்த்தி","ஒருமறுவு மிலையென்ப தொழியாம லுணர்த்துமே","ஒருமறுவு மிலையென்ப தொழியாம லுணர்த்துகினும்","அருமறையை விரித்தாயை யறிவாரோ வரியரே."],"p":"","r":"They say the god with the auspicious mark on his right is without any blemish; even so told, the one who unfolded the great scripture is hard to know.","n":"","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","kalvi","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 184","lb":"SO184","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["the Arhat is without blemish and is the source of the scripture","jain","asserted",2,"arasan"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["வேந்தன்","இறைவன்"],"ch":"so:10"},"so:185":{"book":"soolamani","num":185,"t":["இன்னண மிறைவனை யேத்தி யேந்தறன்","சென்னியுட் சேர்த்திய சேடப் பூவினன்","கன்னவி றிருமனிக் கபாடந் தாழுறீஇ","மின்னிய திருநகர் முற்ற முன்னினான்."],"p":"","r":"Having praised the Lord thus, the king bows his head with the flower of the offering, shuts the gem doors, and walks to the courtyard of his shining palace.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 185","lb":"SO185","mm":1,"ev":"The king ends his worship, closes the shrine doors and goes to his palace courtyard.","cl":[],"ag":["வேந்தன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:10"},"so:186":{"book":"soolamani","num":186,"t":["ஆரணங் கவிரொளி யெரிய வாயிடைச்","சாரணர் விசும்பினின் றிழிந்து தாதைதன்","ஏரணி வளநகர் வலங்கொண் டின்னணம்","சீரணி மணிக்குரல் சிலம்ப வாழ்த்தினார்."],"p":"","r":"The charanas, heavenly sages, come down from the sky as the scripture's flame burns, circle the father's rich city and bless him with bells ringing.","n":"","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","iraivan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 186","lb":"SO186","mm":1,"ev":"The charanas descend and circle the city, singing.","cl":[],"ag":["சாரணர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:10"},"so:187":{"book":"soolamani","num":187,"t":["விரைமணந்த தாமரைமேல் விண்வணங்கச் சென்றாய்","உரைமணந்தி யாம்பரவ வுண்மகிழ்வா யல்லை","யுண்மகிழ்வா யல்லை யெனினு முலகெல்லாங்","கண்மகிழ நின்றாய்கட் காத லொழியோமே."],"p":"","r":"Their song addresses the one who walked above a lotus, saying that though he is not pleased by their praise, they will not give up love for him who stood so that all the world rejoiced.","n":"The pattern repeats in 187 to 189; each time the praise concedes that he feels no delight, then keeps the love anyway.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","vazhipaadu","anbu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 187","lb":"SO187","mm":1,"ev":"The charanas sing to the Arhat.","cl":[["the Arhat is untouched by praise, yet merits love for the joy he brought the world","jain","asserted",2,"charanar"]],"ag":["சாரணர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சாரணர்","இறைவன்"],"ch":"so:10"},"so:188":{"book":"soolamani","num":188,"t":["முருகணங்கு தாமரையின் மொய்ம்மலர்மேற் சென்று","யருகணங்கி யேத்தி யதுமகிழ்வா யல்லை","யதுமகிழ்வா யல்லை யெனினும் பெயராக்","கதிமகிழ நின்றாய்கட் காத லொழியோமே."],"p":"","r":"They say again that though he does not take joy in being worshipped beside the lotus, they will not give up love for him who stood in a state that cannot be reversed, so the path rejoices.","n":"","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","vazhipaadu","anbu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 188","lb":"SO188","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["the Arhat takes no joy in worship, yet his unreturning state earns love","jain","asserted",2,"charanar"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["சாரணர்","இறைவன்"],"ch":"so:10"},"so:189":{"book":"soolamani","num":189,"t":["மணமயங்கு தாமரைமேல் வான்வணங்கச் சென்றாய்","குணமயங்கி யாம்பரவக் கொண்டுவப்பா யல்லை","கொண்டுவப்பா யல்லை யெனினுங் குளிர்ந்துலகம்","கண்டுவப்ப நின்றாய்கட் காத லொழியோமே."],"p":"","r":"They say a third time that he is not glad when the heavens bow and they praise, yet the cool world rejoices at his standing, so they will not leave their love.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","vazhipaadu","anbu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 189","lb":"SO189","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["the Arhat takes no joy in praise, yet the world rejoices in him","jain","asserted",2,"charanar"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["சாரணர்","இறைவன்"],"ch":"so:10"},"so:190":{"book":"soolamani","num":190,"t":["தீதறு முனிவர்தஞ் செல்வன் சேவடிக்","காதலி னெழுவிய காம ரின்னிசை","யேதமின் றெவ்வள விசைத்த தவ்வள","வோதிய வுயிர்க்கெலா முறுகண் டீர்ந்தவே."],"p":"","r":"The sweet song of the lovers of the sage's lord ends, and all lives that heard it were freed from sorrow, as long as it lasted.","n":"The poet closes the hymn with the claim that hearing it soothes lives; the bound of the verse's last two lines is hard to fix.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","vazhipaadu","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 190","lb":"SO190","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:10"},"so:191":{"book":"soolamani","num":191,"t":["இறைவனை யின்னண மேத்தித் தந்தொழில்","குறைவிலா முடிந்தபின் குணக்குன் றாயினார்","மறமலி மன்னனை நோக்கி மற்றவற்","கறமழை பொழிவதோ ரார்வ மெய்தினார்."],"p":"","r":"The sages, their own duties fully done and now mountains of virtue, look to the war-proud king and are eager to pour the rain of dharma on him.","n":"The rain-of-dharma image has the teaching fall on the hearer unasked by any gift from him; the sages' eagerness, not the king's request, begins the sermon (the king asks in 197).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thavam","aram","arul"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 191","lb":"SO191","mm":1,"ev":"The sages, having finished their rite, turn to the king wishing to teach him dharma.","cl":[],"ag":["முனிவர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:11"},"so:192":{"book":"soolamani","num":192,"t":["தென்றலுஞ் செழுமதுத் திவலை மாரியும்","என்றுநின் றறாததோ ரிளந்தண் பிண்டியும்","நின்றொளி திகழ்வதோர் நிலாக்கல் வட்டமும்","சென்றவ ரமர்ந்துழித் திகழ்ந்து தோன்றுமே."],"p":"","r":"Wherever these sages sit there appear the south wind, a fine drizzle of honey, an ever-fresh cool ashoka tree and a shining disc of moonstone.","n":"The verse lists the signs that mark a sage's seat; the ashoka (பிண்டி) is the Jain holy tree in the other Jain epics, but the verse itself does not name any faith.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thavam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 192","lb":"SO192","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:11"},"so:193":{"book":"soolamani","num":193,"t":["வென்றவன் றிருநகர் விளங்கு வேதிகை","மூன்றில்சேர்ந் திருந்தனர் முனிவ ராதலும்","மின்றவழ் விளங்குவேல் வென்றி வேந்தனும்","சென்றவர் திருந்தடி முடியிற் றீட்டினான்."],"p":"","r":"When the sages take their place on the three-tiered platform of the Victor's shining city, the spear-bearing king goes to them and sets their feet upon his head.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["panivu","vazhipaadu","thavam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 193","lb":"SO193","mm":1,"ev":"The king goes to the sages on the three-tiered platform and lays their feet on his crown.","cl":[],"ag":["முனிவர்","விஞ்சையர் வேந்தன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:11"},"so:194":{"book":"soolamani","num":194,"t":["பாசிடைப் பரப்புடைப் பழன நாடனை","ஆசிடை கொடுத்தவ ரிருக்க வென்றலும்","தூசுடை மணிக்கலை மகளிர் சூழ்தர","ஏசிடை யிலாதவ னிருக்கை யெய்தினான்."],"p":"","r":"The sages bless the lord of the water-rich land and bid him sit, and he takes his seat amid women in fine cloth and jewelled girdles.","n":"The king is called by his land (பழன நாடன்) and not by name; line 4 calls him faultless (ஏசிடை இலாதவன்) just as he comes to hear teaching on faults.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["panivu","arasu","aram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 194","lb":"SO194","mm":1,"ev":"The sages bless the king and tell him to be seated; he sits among his attendant women.","cl":[],"ag":["முனிவர்","விஞ்சையர் வேந்தன்","கன்னியர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:11"},"so:195":{"book":"soolamani","num":195,"t":["தாளுயர் தாமரைத் திருவுந் தண்கதிர்","நீளெழி லாரமு நிழன்ற கண்குலாம்","தோளிணை செவ்வியோ வென்னச் சூழொளி","வாளவன் மணிமுடி வணங்கி வாழ்த்தினான்."],"p":"","r":"The sword-bearing king bows his jewelled crown to the sages and blesses them.","n":"Lines 1-3 compare his shoulders to the lotus-seated goddess and a long necklace; the construction is hard and the sense of the comparison is a guess.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["panivu","vazhipaadu","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 195","lb":"SO195","mm":1,"ev":"The king bows his crown to the sages and gives praise.","cl":[],"ag":["விஞ்சையர் வேந்தன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:11"},"so:196":{"book":"soolamani","num":196,"t":["முனிவருட் பெரியவன் முகத்து நோக்கியொன்","றினிதுள துணர்த்துவ தடிக ளென்றலும்","பனிமலர்த் தாமரைப் பழன நாடனைக்","கனியமற் றின்னணங் கடவுள் கூறினான்."],"p":"","r":"The king looks at the chief of the sages and says he has one sweet thing to ask of him, and the sage answers him kindly.","n":"Who says 'அடிகள்' and who asks the favour in lines 1-2 is not marked in the text; reading the king as the one who speaks first fits the sage's reply in 197.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["guru","kalvi","arul"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 196","lb":"SO196","mm":1,"ev":"The king addresses the chief sage, who replies gently.","cl":[],"ag":["விஞ்சையர் வேந்தன்","முனிவருட் பெரியவன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:11"},"so:197":{"book":"soolamani","num":197,"t":["துன்னிய வினைப்பகை துணிக்குந் தொன்மைசா","லின்னுரை யமிழ்தெமக் கீமி னென்பதாம்","மன்னநின் மனத்துள தென்ன மாமணிக்","கன்னவில் கடகக்கை கதழக் கூப்பினான்."],"p":"","r":"The sage says what is in the king's mind: he wants the old sweet word that cuts the bond of deeds, and the king folds his hands in eager assent.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","guru"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 197","lb":"SO197","mm":1,"ev":"The sage names the king's wish for the teaching, and the king joins his hands.","cl":[["the ancient teaching is a sweet word that cuts the enemy-deeds that cling","jain","asserted",1,"peru_munivan"]],"ag":["விஞ்சையர் வேந்தன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிவருட் பெரியவன்","விஞ்சையர் வேந்தன்"],"ch":"so:11"},"so:198":{"book":"soolamani","num":198,"t":["மெய்யறி விலாமை யென்னும் வித்தினிற் பிறந்து வெய்ய","கையறு வினைகள் கைபோய்க் கடுந்துயர் விளைத்த போழ்தில்","மையுற வுழந்து வாடும் வாழுயிர்ப் பிறவி மாலை","நெய்யுற நிழற்றும் வேலோ யினைத்தென நினைக்க லாமோ."],"p":"","r":"From the seed of lacking true knowledge there spring cruel helpless deeds which, when they ripen into harsh pain, leave living beings weary through a garland of births too long to reckon.","n":"The verse names the seed as absence of true knowledge (மெய்யறிவிலாமை), not craving; lines 3-4 are tightly packed and the exact construction of the last line is uncertain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vinai","piravi","arivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 198","lb":"SO198","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["lack of true knowledge is the seed from which harmful deeds grow","jain","asserted",1,"peru_munivan"],["the births that deeds produce form a chain of suffering whose length cannot be reckoned","jain","asserted",4,"peru_munivan"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிவருட் பெரியவன்","விஞ்சையர் வேந்தன்"],"ch":"so:11"},"so:199":{"book":"soolamani","num":199,"t":["சூழ்வினை துரப்பச் சென்று சூழ்வினைப் பயத்தினாலே","வீழ்வினை பிறிது மாக்கி வெய்துற விளிந்து தோன்றி","ஆழ்துய ருழக்கு மந்தோ வளியற்ற வறிவில் சாதித்","தாழ்வினை விலக்குஞ் சார்வு தலைப்படா வளவு மென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"Driven by their deeds, beings do further deeds from the fruit of the first, die in pain and are born again, and sink in suffering until they reach the refuge that bars the low births.","n":"The refuge (சார்வு) is not yet named here; the next verse supplies it. The word is not the Buddhist சார்பு (dependent arising) of the Manimekalai.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","piravi","kutram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 199","lb":"SO199","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["deeds compel new action, and the fruit of one deed breeds further deeds","jain","asserted",1,"peru_munivan"],["beings keep suffering through births until they reach a refuge that stops the fall","jain","asserted",3,"peru_munivan"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிவருட் பெரியவன்","விஞ்சையர் வேந்தன்"],"ch":"so:11"},"so:200":{"book":"soolamani","num":200,"t":["காதியங் கிளைகள் சீறுங் காமரு நெறிக்குங் கண்ணாய்ப்","போதியங் கிழவர் தங்க டியானத்துப் புலங்கொண் டேத்தி","யாதியந் தகன்று நின்ற வடிகளே சரணங் கண்டாய்","மாதுய ரிடும்பை தீர்க்குஞ் சரணெனப் படுவ மன்னா."],"p":"","r":"The sage names the refuge: the feet of the one who stands beyond all beginning, whom the lords of enlightenment praise in meditation, as the cure for great suffering.","n":"Lines 1-2 are hard: காதியம் looks like the four destroying deeds of Jain teaching, and போதியங் கிழவர் is a phrase other traditions use for those of the bodhi tree, here read as masters of enlightenment.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","thiruvadi","vinai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 200","lb":"SO200","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["the feet of the Lord who stands beyond beginning are the one refuge that ends great suffering","jain","asserted",3,"peru_munivan"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிவருட் பெரியவன்","விஞ்சையர் வேந்தன்"],"ch":"so:11"},"so:201":{"book":"soolamani","num":201,"t":["மெய்ப்பொரு டெரிதல் மற்றப் பொருண்மிசை விரிந்த ஞான","மப்பொருள் வழாத நூலி னருந்தகை யொழுக்கந் தாங்கி","யிப்பொருள் ளிவைகள் கண்டா யிறைவனால் விரிக்கப் பட்ட","கைப்பொரு ளாகக் கொண்டு கடைப்பிடி கனபொற் றாரோய்."],"p":"","r":"The sage tells the king to hold fast to what the Lord laid out: discerning true things, the knowledge that grows on them and conduct kept faithful to the text.","n":"The verse names three things in turn; whether the poet means the Jain triad of right faith, knowledge and conduct is a reading, as the verse does not number them. Verse 203 speaks of three nectars.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["meyyunarvu","arivu","ozhukkam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 201","lb":"SO201","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["true seeing, knowledge and conduct faithful to the scripture are the means the Lord taught","jain","asserted",1,"peru_munivan"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிவருட் பெரியவன்","விஞ்சையர் வேந்தன்"],"ch":"so:11"},"so:202":{"book":"soolamani","num":202,"t":["உற்றடு பிணியு மூப்பு மூழுறு துயறு நீக்கிச்","சுற்றிநின் றுலக மேத்துஞ் சுடரொளி யுருவந் தாங்கிப்","பெற்றதோர் வரம்பி லின்பம் பிறழ்விலா நிலைமை கண்டாய்","மற்ரவை நிறைந்த மாந்தர் பெறப்படு நிலைமை மன்னா."],"p":"","r":"The end of these is a state free of disease, age and the pain of births, with a shining form the world praises and a limitless, unfailing bliss, which those who are full of these things reach.","n":"The verse says the released keep a form (உருவம்) and bliss; it does not say they join a god. Line 4 reads மற்ரவை, likely a slip for மற்றவை.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["veedu","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 202","lb":"SO202","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["release is a state without disease, age or pain, with a shining form and unfailing bliss","jain","asserted",3,"peru_munivan"],["those who fulfil the three means attain it","jain","asserted",4,"peru_munivan"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிவருட் பெரியவன்","விஞ்சையர் வேந்தன்"],"ch":"so:11"},"so:203":{"book":"soolamani","num":203,"t":["அருந்துய ரறுக்கு மாண்பி னாரமிர் தவைகண் மூன்றும்","திருந்தநன் குரைப்பக் கேட்டே தீவினை யிருள்கள் போழும்","விரிந்தநல் லறிவின் சோதி விளங்கலிற் சனங்க ளெல்லாம்","பரிந்தகங் கழுமத் தேறிப் பாவம் பரிந் தவர்க ளொத்தார்."],"p":"","r":"Hearing the three nectars that cut hard suffering taught well, the people, lit by wide knowledge that tears the darkness of evil deeds, grow clear in heart as if freed from sin.","n":"The 'three nectars' (அமிர்தவைகள் மூன்றும்) most likely refer back to the three things of verse 201, but the verse does not say so. The verse says the people were as if without sin (ஒத்தார்), not that they were.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arivu","vinai","thelivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 203","lb":"SO203","mm":1,"ev":"The assembled people hear the teaching and are cleared of heart as if of sin.","cl":[["hearing the true teaching lets the light of knowledge split the darkness of evil deeds","jain","asserted",2,"narrator"]],"ag":["சனங்கள்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:11"},"so:204":{"book":"soolamani","num":204,"t":["மன்னிய முனிவன் வாயுண் மணிகொழித் தனைய வாகிப்","பன்னிய பவங்க டீர்க்கும் பயங்கெழு மொழிக டம்மால்","கன்னவில் கடகத் தோளான் காட்சியங் கதிர்ப்புச் சென்றான்","பின்னவ னுரிமை தானும் பெருவத மருவிற் றன்றே."],"p":"","r":"The sage's jewel-bright words, which end the sins they tell of, bring the king to right vision, and his own one too takes up a great vow.","n":"காட்சி (sight, vision) is read here as the right seeing of the Jain triad. உரிமை (one's own, dear one) is taken as the daughter of 205, but the verse does not say who it is.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["arivu","vinai","thuravu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 204","lb":"SO204","mm":1,"ev":"The king reaches clear sight through the sage's words and a dear one of his takes a great vow.","cl":[["a true teacher's words remove the sins they speak of and bring right seeing","jain","asserted",2,"narrator"]],"ag":["விஞ்சையர் வேந்தன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:11"},"so:205":{"book":"soolamani","num":205,"t":["மன்னவன் மடமகள் வணங்கி மற்றவ","ரின்னுரை யமுதமுண் டெழுந்த சோதியள்","பன்னியொர் நோன்பு மேற் கொண்டு பாங்கினால்","பின்னது முடிப்பதோர் பெருமை யெண்ணினாள்."],"p":"","r":"The king's young daughter bows, rises shining after drinking their sweet word, resolves on a vow and thinks how to finish it with greatness.","n":"The verse does not name her; she is the girl born as Sayampavai in verse 150, which this chapter does not repeat.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nonbu","thavam","thuravu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 205","lb":"SO205","mm":1,"ev":"The king's young daughter bows to the sages and takes a vow.","cl":[],"ag":["சயம்பவை"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:11"},"so:206":{"book":"soolamani","num":206,"t":["முனிவரர் திருந்தடி வணங்கி மூசுதேன்","பனிமலர் விரவிய படலை மார்பினான்","கனிவளர் பொழிலிடைக் கடவு ணன்னகர்","இனிதினின் வலமுறை யெய்தி யேகினான்."],"p":"","r":"Wearing a garland of honeyed cool flowers, the king bows to the sages' feet, circles the god's shining city in the right-hand way and goes.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","panivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 206","lb":"SO206","mm":1,"ev":"The king bows to the sages, circles the shrine city and leaves.","cl":[],"ag":["விஞ்சையர் வேந்தன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:11"},"so:207":{"book":"soolamani","num":207,"t":["வாமமே கலையவர் மனத்தில் வார்பொழில்","காமவே ளிடங்கொள வருளிக் கண்ணொளிர்","தாமவே லிளையவர் காப்பத் தாழ்கதிர்","நாமவே னரபதி நகர நண்ணினான்."],"p":"","r":"The king, guarded by young men with spears, goes back to his own city.","n":"Lines 1-2 say something of women's minds and the love-god's place in them, but the construction is not clear and the link to the king's return is a guess.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["arasu","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 207","lb":"SO207","mm":1,"ev":"The king goes back to his own city.","cl":[],"ag":["விஞ்சையர் வேந்தன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:11"},"so:208":{"book":"soolamani","num":208,"t":["அகநக ரரைசரோ டரைசன் சென்றபின்","சகதபி நந்தன ரென்னுஞ் சாரணர்","மிகநவின் றிறைவனை வணங்கி விண்ணிடைப்","பகனகு கடரொளி படர வேகினார்."],"p":"","r":"After the king and the lords of the inner city have gone, the sky-travelling sages called Jagadabhinandana praise the Lord again and leave through the sky in a blaze of light.","n":"The sages named here are likely the same ones who taught the king, but the verse does not say so.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","thavam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 208","lb":"SO208","mm":1,"ev":"The sky-travelling sages worship the Lord and depart through the sky.","cl":[],"ag":["சாரணர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:11"},"so:209":{"book":"soolamani","num":209,"t":["அழற்கொடி யெறித்தொறுஞ் சுடரு மாடக","நிழற்கொடி யதுவென நிறைந்த காரிகைக்","குழற்கொடி யனையவள் கொண்ட நோன்பினால்","எழிற்கொடி சுடர்வதோ ரியற்கை யெய்தினாள்."],"p":"","r":"By the vow she has taken, the creeper-haired girl comes to a flame-like glow that shines, like a banner of fire on a mansion.","n":"Lines 1-2 pile images (a flame-banner, the shadow of mansion banners) and the comparison is hard to resolve; the main sense, that the vow makes her glow, is clear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["nonbu","thavam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 209","lb":"SO209","mm":1,"ev":"The king's daughter shines with a new glow from her vow.","cl":[],"ag":["சயம்பவை"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:11"},"so:210":{"book":"soolamani","num":210,"t":["முகைத்தவார் முல்லையை முருக்கு மெல்லியல்","நகைத்தவார் குழலவ டன்மை யாயினும்","வகுத்தவா றுயர்ந்தன நோன்பு மாசிலா","வகத்துமாண் புடையவர்க் கரிய தில்லையே."],"p":"","r":"Though the girl is as soft as she is, a vow set out as it should be is high, and nothing is hard for those who are spotless within.","n":"The verse says it of her tenderness: a delicate girl can bear a hard vow, because what holds it up is a clean mind, not strength of body.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nonbu","thavam","manam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 210","lb":"SO210","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["nothing is hard to those of spotless inner virtue","general","asserted",4,"narrator"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:11"},"so:211":{"book":"soolamani","num":211,"t":["இந்திர வுலகமும் வணக்கு மீடுடைத்","தந்திர நோன்பொளி தவழத் தையலாள்","மந்திர நறுநெய்யால் வளர்ந்து மாசிலா","வந்தர வழற்கொடி யனைய ளாயினாள்."],"p":"","r":"Honoured even by Indra's world, her vow sheds its light and she grows, nourished as if by mantra and sweet ghee, into something like a spotless flame-banner in the sky.","n":"The mantra and ghee image can be read as a figure for her glow rather than a literal rite; the verse does not describe a fire-offering.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["nonbu","thavam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 211","lb":"SO211","mm":1,"ev":"The king's daughter keeps her vow and glows like a flame-banner.","cl":[],"ag":["சயம்பவை"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:11"},"so:212":{"book":"soolamani","num":212,"t":["தாங்கருஞ் சுடொரொளி சக்கர வாளமென்","றோங்கிரும் பெயர்கொணோன் புயர நோற்றபின்","றீங்கரும் பனையசொற் சிறுமி தெய்வதக்","காங்கொரு பெருஞ்சிறப் பயர்தல் மேயினாள்."],"p":"","r":"After she has kept the vow named Chakravalam well, the sweet-voiced girl comes to wish a great special worship of the god.","n":"The vow's name matches the city's in verse 130, and the text does not link them. Line 1 reads சுடொரொளி, probably for சுடரொளி.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nonbu","thavam","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 212","lb":"SO212","mm":1,"ev":"The girl completes her vow and sets out to hold a great worship.","cl":[],"ag":["சயம்பவை"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:11"},"so:213":{"book":"soolamani","num":213,"t":["தண்ணவிர் நிலாச்சுடர் தவழு மவ்வரைக்","கண்ணவிர் சென்னிமேற் கடவுட் டானமஃ","தண்ணலங் கோமக ளருச்சித் தாயிடை","விண்ணவ ருலகமூம் வியப்ப வேத்தினாள்."],"p":"","r":"On the moonlit hill, over the crest, the king's daughter worships the god's shrine and praises it so that the gods' world marvels.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","thavam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 213","lb":"SO213","mm":1,"ev":"The king's daughter worships at the hilltop shrine and praises the god.","cl":[],"ag":["சயம்பவை"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:11"},"so:214":{"book":"soolamani","num":214,"t":["ஆதியங் கடவுளை யருமறை பயந்தனை","போதியங் கிழவனை பூமிசை யொதுங்கினை","போதியங் கிழவனை யொதுங்கிய","சேதியஞ் செல்வநின் றிருவடி வணங்கினம்."],"p":"","r":"The hymn addresses the Lord as the primal god who gave the rare scripture, the lord of the bodhi, who stood on the flower, and bows to his holy feet.","n":"Line 3 repeats line 2's போதியங் கிழவனை and breaks the metre of the sense; the line may be damaged. The words பூமிசை ஒதுங்கினை are close in sense to Kural 3's மலர்மிசை ஏகினான், but share no words with it, so this is a link, not an echo. The speaker is taken to be the girl, from 213.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","thiruvadi","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 214","lb":"SO214","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["the primal Lord gave the rare scripture","jain","asserted",1,"sayampavai"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["சயம்பவை","வென்றான்"],"ch":"so:11"},"so:215":{"book":"soolamani","num":215,"t":["காமனைக் கடிந்தனை காலனைக் காய்ந்தனை","தேமலர் மாரியை திருமறு மார்பனை","தேமலர் மாரியை திருமறு மார்பனை","மாமலர் வண்ணநின் மலரடி வணங்கினம்."],"p":"","r":"The king's daughter hymns the god as the one who cut off desire and was wrathful at Death, the lord with the bright mark on his breast, whose flower-like feet she and her women bow to.","n":"Line 3 repeats line 2 word for word in the edition; the repetition is kept as a text problem rather than a second statement.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","kaamam","kootru","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 215","lb":"SO215","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["the god has overcome desire and Death","jain","asserted",1,"princess"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["மங்கை","வென்றான்"],"ch":"so:12"},"so:216":{"book":"soolamani","num":216,"t":["ஆரருள் பயந்தனை யாழ்துய ரவித்தனை","யோரரு ளாழியை யுலகுடை யொருவனை","யோரரு ளாழியை யுலகுடை யொருவனை","சீரருண் மொழியநின் றிருவடி தொழுதனம்."],"p":"","r":"The hymn goes on: the god gave grace, ended deep sorrow, is the one lord of the wheel and of the world, and she bows at his feet to speak of his graceful words.","n":"Line 3 repeats line 2 in the edition, as in the verse before.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","thiruvadi","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 216","lb":"SO216","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["the god's grace ends deep sorrow","jain","asserted",1,"princess"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["மங்கை","வென்றான்"],"ch":"so:12"},"so:217":{"book":"soolamani","num":217,"t":["கருவடி நெடுநல்வேற் கண்ணி யின்னணம்","வெருவுடை வினைப்பகை விலக்கும் வீறுசால்","மருவுடை மொழிகளாற் பரவி வாமன","திருவடிச் சேடமுந் திகழச் சூடினாள்."],"p":"","r":"The spear-eyed girl, having praised the god in such words that keep off the fearsome enemy of deeds, wears the remnant of his holy feet's flowers.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","vinai","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 217","lb":"SO217","mm":1,"ev":"The princess ends her hymn and puts on the flower remnant from the god's feet.","cl":[["praise of the god keeps off the fearsome enemy of deeds","jain","asserted",2,"narrator"]],"ag":["மங்கை"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:12"},"so:218":{"book":"soolamani","num":218,"t":["வானுயர் கடவுளை வயங்கு சேவடித்","தேனுயர் திருமலர்ச் சேடங் கொண்டபின்","மானுயர் நோக்கியர் பரவ மங்கைதன்","கோனுயர் வளநகர்க் கோயின் முன்னினாள்."],"p":"","r":"Having taken the honey-rich flower remnant from the god's feet, the girl walks, praised by her doe-eyed companions, to the temple-like palace of her father's rich city.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","kudi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 218","lb":"SO218","mm":1,"ev":"The princess, praised by her companions, goes to her father's palace.","cl":[],"ag":["மங்கை"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:12"},"so:219":{"book":"soolamani","num":219,"t":["வெஞ்சுடர் வேலவர்க் குணர்த்தி மெல்லவே","பஞ்சுடைச் சேவடி பரவச் சென்றுகன்","னஞ்சுடர் மெல்விரல் சிவப்ப வாழியின்","செஞ்சுட ரங்கையிற் சேட நீட்டினான்."],"p":"","r":"Someone tells the king, goes gently up with feet that praise, and holds out the flower remnant in a red palm whose soft fingers turn red.","n":"The final verb is masculine (நீட்டினான்) though the princess has been the actor in the two verses before; the sense of who offers the remnant, and the words ending line 2 and starting line 3, cannot be fixed.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 219","lb":"SO219","mm":1,"ev":"The god's flower remnant is held out to the king in a palm.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:12"},"so:220":{"book":"soolamani","num":220,"t":["அல்லியி னரவண் டிரிய வாய்மலர்","வல்லியின் வணங்கிய மகளை மன்னவன்","முல்லையஞ் சிகழிகை முச்சி மோந்திவை","சொல்லிய தொடங்கினான் சுடரும் வேலினான்."],"p":"","r":"The king smells the crown of the hair of his daughter, who bows like a creeper while bees leave her lily-like flowers, and begins to say these things.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["makkal","anbu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 220","lb":"SO220","mm":1,"ev":"The king smells his daughter's hair-crown and begins to speak to her.","cl":[],"ag":["விஞ்சையர் வேந்தன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:12"},"so:221":{"book":"soolamani","num":221,"t":["தேந்துணர் பலவுள வேனுஞ் செங்குழை","மாந்துணர் வயந்தனை மலரத் தோன்றுமே","பூந்துண ரோதிநீ பிறந்து பொன்செய்தார்","வேந்துவந் திறைஞ்சயான் விளங்கு கின்றதே."],"p":"","r":"The king tells her that though many bunches of nectar flowers stand, only the mango cluster makes spring appear, and he shines because she was born to him.","n":"Her birth is praised as what lights the father and the house, a view of a daughter that the following four verses repeat in other images.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["makkal","perumai","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 221","lb":"SO221","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["விஞ்சையர் வேந்தன்","மங்கை"],"ch":"so:12"},"so:222":{"book":"soolamani","num":222,"t":["கங்கைநீர் பாய்ந்துழிக் கடலுந் தீர்த்தமா","மங்கணீ ருலகெலா மறியப் பட்டது","நங்கைநீ பிறந்ததற் பின்னை நங்குடி","வங்கநீர் வரைப்பெலாம் வணக்கப் பட்டதே."],"p":"","r":"The king says that where Ganga water flows even the sea becomes sacred, and that since her birth his family has been made known to the world and the seas' neighbours bowed.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["makkal","perumai","pugazh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 222","lb":"SO222","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["விஞ்சையர் வேந்தன்","மங்கை"],"ch":"so:12"},"so:223":{"book":"soolamani","num":223,"t":["போதுலாந் தாமரை பூத்த பொய்கையைத்","தீதுலாங் கீழுயிர் தீண்டச் செல்லல","மாதுலா மடந்தைநீ பிறந்திம் மண்டில","மேதிலா ரிடைதிற மிகந்து நின்றதே."],"p":"","r":"The king says that, as the base creatures do not touch a lotus pool, the whole region has fallen away from lowness since her birth.","n":"Lines 2 to 4 are hard to construe; the king's claim may be that lowly people stay clear of the realm because of her.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["makkal","perumai","kudi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 223","lb":"SO223","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["விஞ்சையர் வேந்தன்","மங்கை"],"ch":"so:12"},"so:224":{"book":"soolamani","num":224,"t":["வானகத் திளம்பிறை வளர வையகம்","ஈனகத் திருள்கெட வின்ப மெய்துமே","நானகக் குழலிநீ வளர நங்குடி","தானகத் திருள்கெடத் தயங்கு கின்றதே."],"p":"","r":"The king says that as the young moon growing in the sky makes the world glad and dispels darkness, so her growth dispels the darkness in the house.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["makkal","azhagu","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 224","lb":"SO224","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["விஞ்சையர் வேந்தன்","மங்கை"],"ch":"so:12"},"so:225":{"book":"soolamani","num":225,"t":["கண்பகர் மல்லிகை கமழக் காதலால்","சண்பகத் தனிவனந் தும்பி சாருநீ","பெண்பகர் திருவனாய் பிறந்து நங்குடி","மண்பக ருலகெலா மகிழச் செல்லுமே."],"p":"","r":"The king says that as a bee comes in love to the jasmine and the lone champak grove, she is a fortune among women, whose house makes the whole earth glad.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["makkal","azhagu","kudi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 225","lb":"SO225","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["விஞ்சையர் வேந்தன்","மங்கை"],"ch":"so:12"},"so:226":{"book":"soolamani","num":226,"t":["கொவ்வையந் துவரிதழ்க் கோல வாயவட்","கிவ்வகை யணியன கூறி யீண்டுநும்","மவ்வைதன் கோயில்புக் கடிசி லுண்கென","மவ்வலங் குழலியை மன்ன னேயினான்."],"p":"","r":"Having said such praise of her ornaments to the red-lipped girl, the king tells her to go into her mother's house and eat the meal.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["makkal","anbu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 226","lb":"SO226","mm":1,"ev":"The king bids his daughter go to her mother's house and eat.","cl":[],"ag":["விஞ்சையர் வேந்தன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:12"},"so:227":{"book":"soolamani","num":227,"t":["பல்கலம் பெரியன வணியிற் பாவைத","னல்குனோ மெனச்சிலம் பணிந்து மெல்லவே","செல்கவென் றிருமக ளென்று செம்பொனான்","மல்கிய முடியினான் மகிழ்ந்து நோக்கினான்."],"p":"","r":"Telling her to wear anklets, not heavy ornaments, and walk gently, the golden king looks fondly on his daughter.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["makkal","anbu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 227","lb":"SO227","mm":1,"ev":"The king sends her off wearing anklets and watches her go.","cl":[],"ag":["விஞ்சையர் வேந்தன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:12"},"so:228":{"book":"soolamani","num":228,"t":["மண்ணருங் கலமெலாம் வலிதின் வவ்வினும்","விண்ணருங் கலமெலாம் விதியி னெய்தினும்","பெண்ணருங் கலமிது பெறுதன் மானுடர்க்","கெண்ணருந் தகைத்தென விறைவ னெண்ணினான்."],"p":"","r":"The king reflects that even if mortals seized every earthly jewel by force and won every heavenly one by fate, a girl as a jewel is hard to come by, a thing past counting.","n":"The edition's word division makes the speaker's own judgement of her worth hard to separate from the narrator's report that he thought so (line 4).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["makkal","selvam","ilvaazhkkai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 228","lb":"SO228","mm":1,"ev":"The king ponders his daughter's worth beyond all treasure.","cl":[["a daughter is a jewel beyond every treasure of earth and heaven","general","asserted",3,"vinjaiyar_vendhan"]],"ag":["விஞ்சையர் வேந்தன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["விஞ்சையர் வேந்தன்",""],"ch":"so:12"},"so:229":{"book":"soolamani","num":229,"t":["மையணி வரையின்வாழ் மன்னர் தொல்குடிக்","கையணி நெடுநல்வேற் காளை மார்களுள்","நெய்யணி குழலிவட் குரிய நீர்மையான்","மெய்யணி பொறியவ னெவன்கொல் வீரனே."],"p":"","r":"The king asks which youth among the spear-bearing sons of the ancient mountain-kings is fitted by quality for her, which marked hero of the body and brave.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ilvaazhkkai","kudi","vaazhkkaithunai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 229","lb":"SO229","mm":1,"ev":"The king wonders who among the Vidyadhara youths is worthy of his daughter.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["விஞ்சையர் வேந்தன்",""],"ch":"so:12"},"so:230":{"book":"soolamani","num":230,"t":["பொலங்கலக் குரியவாம் பொருவின் மாமணி","யிலங்கல மென்மை வீயஞ் சேர்த்தினும்","குலங்கலந் தில்வழிக் குரவர் கூட்டினும்","மலங்கலங் குழலிய ரன்றென் கிற்பவோ."],"p":"","r":"The king reasons that though fine jewels may be set with soft flowers, and elders may join girls where families do not mingle, women with sad curls would not say no.","n":"The sense of lines 3 and 4 turns on குலம் கலந்தில்வழி and on the sandhi of அன்றென்கிற்பவோ; the verse may mean that a daughter would not refuse an ill-matched union.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["ilvaazhkkai","kudi","vaazhkkaithunai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 230","lb":"SO230","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["a match of unmingled families made by elders leaves the girl unable to refuse","general","reported",3,"vinjaiyar_vendhan"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["விஞ்சையர் வேந்தன்",""],"ch":"so:12"},"so:231":{"book":"soolamani","num":231,"t":["அந்தைதா முறுவது கருதி யாருயிர்த்","தந்தைதா யென்றிவர் கொடுப்பிற் றையலார்","சிந்தைதா யிலாதவர் திறத்துஞ் செவ்வனே","நொந்துதாம் பிறிதுரை நொடிய வல்லரோ."],"p":"","r":"The king thinks that if parents give girls away with only the groom's side's gain in mind, girls whose hearts are not in it can only grieve and speak otherwise.","n":"The word அந்தை (line 1) is rare and the grammar compressed; the reading of lines 1 and 2 is provisional.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["ilvaazhkkai","makkal","vaazhkkaithunai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 231","lb":"SO231","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["parents who give a girl only for the groom's side's sake leave her grieving","general","reported",2,"vinjaiyar_vendhan"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["விஞ்சையர் வேந்தன்",""],"ch":"so:12"},"so:232":{"book":"soolamani","num":232,"t":["காதலா லறிவது காமங் காதலே","யேதிலா ருணர்வினா லெண்ண லாவதன்","றாதலான் மாதரா டிறத்தி னாணைநூ","லோதினா ருரைவழி யொட்டற் பாலதே."],"p":"","r":"The king concludes that love is known only through love, outsiders cannot weigh a woman's desire by their own judgement, so in her matter he should follow the words of those who recite the treatise.","n":"The verse gives two guides in tension: the girl's own love, which no outsider can judge, and the treatise's ruling, which the king chooses to follow; it does not say whether her love will be asked.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","ilvaazhkkai","kalvi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 232","lb":"SO232","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["a girl's desire is known only by love, not by an outsider's reckoning","general","reported",1,"vinjaiyar_vendhan"],["in a woman's marriage one should follow the way of those versed in the treatise","general","asserted",4,"vinjaiyar_vendhan"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["விஞ்சையர் வேந்தன்",""],"ch":"so:12"},"so:233":{"book":"soolamani","num":233,"t":["தன்னுணர் பொறிபிறர் தங்கண் கூட்டென","வின்னண மிருவகைத் திறைவர் வாழ்க்கையே","தன்னுணர் பொறிப்புலந் தன்னி னாம்பிறி","தின்னணா மியற்றுகென் றமைச்ச ரேவுவார்."],"p":"","r":"The king reasons that a ruler's life is of two kinds, his own senses and those joined to others', and so ministers urge him to act on his own judgement.","n":"The syntax of lines 1 and 2 is compressed, and whether the ministers urge the king or are urged by him cannot be fixed from the Tamil.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["arasu","amaichu","arivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 233","lb":"SO233","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["விஞ்சையர் வேந்தன்",""],"ch":"so:12"},"so:234":{"book":"soolamani","num":234,"t":["தண்ணிய தடத்தவே யெனினுந் தாமரை","விண்ணியல் கதிரினால் விரியும் வேந்தரும்","புண்ணியப் பொதும்பரே புரிந்து வைகினும்","கண்ணிய புலவரா லலர்தல் காண்டுமே."],"p":"","r":"The king reflects that though lotuses stand in cool pools they open by the sun's rays, and kings dwelling among virtue's groves likewise open by those who consider with learning.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","kalvi","pugazh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 234","lb":"SO234","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["a king flourishes only through learned poets and counsellors","general","asserted",4,"vinjaiyar_vendhan"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["விஞ்சையர் வேந்தன்",""],"ch":"so:12"},"so:235":{"book":"soolamani","num":235,"t":["மாமலர் நெடுங்கடன் மதலை மாசிலாக்","காலமைந் தொழுகுமேற் கரையுங் காணுமே","நூலவர் நுழைவொடு நுழைந்து செல்லுமேல்","மேலவ ரொழுக்கமும் வேலை காணுமே."],"p":"","r":"The king says that a boat on the great flower-sea reaches its shore if it sails true to the five times, and that those who enter with the learned's skill will find the sea of the elders' conduct.","n":"Lines 1 and 2 are hard to construe in the edition's word division, including the 'five' of line 2.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kalvi","ozhukkam","arivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 235","lb":"SO235","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["going with the learned's depth lets one reach the limit of the elders' conduct","general","asserted",3,"vinjaiyar_vendhan"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["விஞ்சையர் வேந்தன்",""],"ch":"so:12"},"so:236":{"book":"soolamani","num":236,"t":["ஒன்றுநன் றென உணர்ந் தொருவன் கொள்ளுமே","லன்றதென் றொருவனுக் கறிவு தோன்றுமே","நின்றதொன் றுண்டினி நீதி நூலினோ","டொன்றிநின் றவருரை யுலக மொட்டுமே."],"p":"","r":"The king notes that what one takes for good another finds not so, but one thing stands: the world accepts what accords with the treatise of policy.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","kalvi","arivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 236","lb":"SO236","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["the world accepts what accords with the treatise of policy","general","asserted",4,"vinjaiyar_vendhan"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["விஞ்சையர் வேந்தன்",""],"ch":"so:12"},"so:237":{"book":"soolamani","num":237,"t":["அந்தண ரொழுக்கமு மரைசர் வாழ்க்கையும்","மந்திர மில்லையேன் மலரு மாண்பில","இந்திர னிறைமையு மீரைஞ் ஞாற்றுவர்","தந்திரக் கிழவர்க டாங்கச் செல்லுமே"],"p":"","r":"The king says that brahmin conduct and kings' lives, without counsel, do not flower, and that even Indra's lordship is borne up by the thousand masters of the treatise.","n":"The Indra and the thousand 'masters of the treatise' (ஈரைஞ்ஞாற்றுவர் தந்திரக் கிழவர்) are brought in as a Vedic and Brahmanical example in the king's own argument; the verse makes no Jain claim.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","amaichu","kalvi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 237","lb":"SO237","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["brahmin conduct and kings' lives need counsel to flourish","general","asserted",2,"vinjaiyar_vendhan"],["even Indra's rule is borne by masters of the treatise","vedic","asserted",3,"vinjaiyar_vendhan"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["விஞ்சையர் வேந்தன்",""],"ch":"so:12"},"so:238":{"book":"soolamani","num":238,"t":["என்றுதன் மனத்தினா னெண்ணி யீண்டுசீர்","நின்றநூற் கிழமையி னீதி மாக்களை","யொன்றிநீர் தருகென வுழைக்குற் றேவலார்","சென்றவர்க் கருளிது வென்று செப்பினார்"],"p":"","r":"Having so reasoned in his mind, the king sends his men to summon the ministers who are masters of the treatise of policy, and they go to say what he has commanded.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","amaichu","kalvi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 238","lb":"SO238","mm":1,"ev":"The king sends messengers to bring his ministers learned in policy to counsel him on his daughter's marriage.","cl":[],"ag":["விஞ்சையர் வேந்தன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:12"},"so:239":{"book":"soolamani","num":239,"t":["செஞ்சினைத் தெரியலா னருளிச் செய்தது","தஞ்செவிக் கிசைத்தலுந் தணப்பில் கேள்வியா","ரஞ்சினர் நடுங்கின ராகி யாயிடை","நஞ்சிவர் வேலினான் பாத நண்ணினார்"],"p":"","r":"The king's word reaches the learned counsellors, who tremble at it and come to his feet.","n":"The words that open the verse name the king only by an epithet, and the poison-bright spear in line 4 names him again; that both are the same king, who spoke at the end of the last section, is the likely reading.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["soozhchi","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 239","lb":"SO239","mm":1,"ev":"The king's summons reaches his ministers, who come in fear to the king.","cl":[],"ag":["விஞ்சையர் வேந்தன்","அமைச்சர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:13"},"so:240":{"book":"soolamani","num":240,"t":["உள்ளுணின் றொலிபுறப் படாத தொண் சிறைப்","புள்ளுமல் லாதவும் புகாத நீரது","வெள்ளிவெண் விளிம்பினால் விளங்கு வேதிகை","வள்ளறன் மந்திர சாலை வண்ணமே"],"p":"","r":"The poet describes the council hall as a closed place with a silver-rimmed platform, where no sound leaves and neither bird nor anything else comes in.","n":"Line 2 names only birds and 'what are not birds' as barred; the poet gives no reason, and the closing of the hall to sound is the plain point.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["soozhchi","avai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 240","lb":"SO240","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:13"},"so:241":{"book":"soolamani","num":241,"t":["ஆங்கமர்ந் தமைச்சரோ டரைசர் கோமகன்","பூங்கமழ் மண்டபம் பொலியப் புக்கபின்","வீங்கொளி மணிக்குழை மிளிர்ந்து வில்லிட","வீங்கிவை மொழிந்தன னிறைவ னென்பவே"],"p":"","r":"The king and his ministers go into the fragrant hall and sit, and the king, earrings flashing, begins to speak.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","amaichu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 241","lb":"SO241","mm":1,"ev":"The king and his ministers enter the hall and sit, and the king begins to speak.","cl":[],"ag":["விஞ்சையர் வேந்தன்","அமைச்சர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:13"},"so:242":{"book":"soolamani","num":242,"t":["மண்ணியல் வளாகங் காக்கு மன்னவர் வணக்க லாகப்","புண்ணிய நீர ரேனும் புலவராற் புகலப் பட்ட","நுண்ணிய நூலி னன்றி நுழை பொரு ளுணர்த்த றேற்றா","ரெண்ணிய துணிந்து செய்யுஞ் சூழ்ச்சியு மில்லை யன்றே"],"p":"","r":"The king says that even kings of good nature can grasp subtle meanings only through the fine treatises the learned have composed, and that none decides on his own thought.","n":"The verse ranks the treatise above the king's own discernment; it does not say that books alone suffice for acting.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalvi","soozhchi","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 242","lb":"SO242","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["a king, however virtuous, can understand subtle matters only through the treatises of the learned and has no counsel of his own to decide by","general","asserted",2,"vinjaiyar_vendhan"]],"ag":["விஞ்சையர் வேந்தன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["விஞ்சையர் வேந்தன்","அமைச்சர்"],"ch":"so:13"},"so:243":{"book":"soolamani","num":243,"t":["வால்வளை பரவி மேயும் வளர்திரை வளாக மெல்லாம்","கோல்வளை வுறாமற் காக்குங் கொற்றவ னெடிய னேனும்","மேல்விளை பழியும் வெய்ய வினைகளும் விலக்கி நின்றார்","நூல்விளை புலவ ரன்றே நுணங்குபோ தணங்கு தாரீர்"],"p":"","r":"The king says that though he guards the whole sea-ringed earth with an unbent sceptre, it is the learned of the books who stand to keep later blame and fierce deeds away from him.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["amaichu","sengol","pazhi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 243","lb":"SO243","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["the learned guard even a just king from later blame and from cruel deeds","general","asserted",3,"vinjaiyar_vendhan"]],"ag":["விஞ்சையர் வேந்தன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["விஞ்சையர் வேந்தன்","அமைச்சர்"],"ch":"so:13"},"so:244":{"book":"soolamani","num":244,"t":["சுற்றுநின் றெரியுஞ் செம்பொன் மணிமுடி சுடரச் சூட்டி","வெற்றிவெண் குடையி னீழல் வேந்தன்வேற் றிருக்கு மேனு","மற்றவன் மனமுங் கண்ணும் வாழ்க்கையும் வலியுஞ் சால்பு","மற்றமி லரசுங் கோலு மாபவ ரமைச்ச ரன்றே"],"p":"","r":"The king says that though he sits crowned under the white umbrella, his mind, his eye, his living, his strength, his worth and his rule and sceptre belong to his ministers.","n":"Line 4 gives the ministers an epithet, மாபவர், that can be read as 'of great penance' or as 'great ones'; either way the weight falls on their share in his rule.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["amaichu","arasu","sengol"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 244","lb":"SO244","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["a king's mind, sight, strength, rule and sceptre depend on his ministers","general","asserted",3,"vinjaiyar_vendhan"]],"ag":["விஞ்சையர் வேந்தன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["விஞ்சையர் வேந்தன்","அமைச்சர்"],"ch":"so:13"},"so:245":{"book":"soolamani","num":245,"t":["வீங்குநீர் ருலகங் காக்கும் விழுநுக மொருவ னாலே","தாங்கலாந் தன்மைத் தன்று தளையவிழ் தயங்கு தாரீர்","பாங்கலார் பணியச் சூழு நூலவர் பாக மாகப்","பூங்குலா மலங்கன் மாலைப் புரவலன் பொறுக்கு மன்றே"],"p":"","r":"The king says a single yoke cannot carry the whole watery world, but it can be borne when learned counsellors, who plan how rivals will bow, share the load with the ruler.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["amaichu","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 245","lb":"SO245","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["a king cannot bear the world alone and bears it with the learned beside him","general","asserted",2,"vinjaiyar_vendhan"]],"ag":["விஞ்சையர் வேந்தன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["விஞ்சையர் வேந்தன்","அமைச்சர்"],"ch":"so:13"},"so:246":{"book":"soolamani","num":246,"t":["அற்றமின் றுலகங் காக்கு மருந்தொழில் புரிந்து நின்றான்","கற்றவர் மொழிந்த வாறு கழிப்பது கடன தாகு","மற்றவற் குறுதி நோக்கி வருபழி வழிகள் தூரச்","செற்றவர்ச் செருக்குஞ் சூழ்ச்சி தெருண்டவர் கடவ வன்றே"],"p":"","r":"The king says that a king who has taken up the hard work of guarding the world must pass it as the learned direct, and that those who see his good, keep blame far off and break proud foes are the wise in counsel.","n":"The last two lines are compressed and the link between the 'good' sought for him and the foes' pride is not spelled out.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["amaichu","soozhchi","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 246","lb":"SO246","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["a king's duty is to carry out his rule as the learned have taught","general","asserted",2,"vinjaiyar_vendhan"],["the counsellor's task is to seek the king's good, keep blame from him and break proud foes","general","asserted",3,"vinjaiyar_vendhan"]],"ag":["விஞ்சையர் வேந்தன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["விஞ்சையர் வேந்தன்","அமைச்சர்"],"ch":"so:13"},"so:247":{"book":"soolamani","num":247,"t":["செறிந்தவர் தெளிந்த நூலார் சிறந்தவை தெரிந்து சொன்னால்","அறிந்தவை யமர்ந்து செய்யு மமைதியா னரச னாவான்","செறிந்தவர் தெளிந்த நூலார் சிறந்தவை தெரிந்து கூறி","அறிந்தவை யியற்று கிற்கு மமைதியா ரமைச்ச ராவார்"],"p":"","r":"The king says he is a king who calmly acts on what well-read learned men choose and tell him, and that ministers are those who tell him so and then carry it out.","n":"Lines 1-2 and 3-4 say nearly the same in the same words, one for the king and one for the minister; the poem sets the two offices side by side.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","amaichu","soozhchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 247","lb":"SO247","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["a king is one who acts calmly on what the learned advise","general","asserted",2,"vinjaiyar_vendhan"],["a minister is one who both advises and carries out what the learned choose","general","asserted",4,"vinjaiyar_vendhan"]],"ag":["விஞ்சையர் வேந்தன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["விஞ்சையர் வேந்தன்","அமைச்சர்"],"ch":"so:13"},"so:248":{"book":"soolamani","num":248,"t":["வாள்வலித் தடக்கை மன்னர் வையகம் வணக்கும் வாயில்","தோள்வலி சூழ்ச்சி யென்றாங் கிருவகைத் தொகையிற் றாகும்","ஆள்வலித் தானை யார்கட் காதிய தழகி தேனும்","கோள்வலிச் சீய மொப்பீர் சூழ்ச்சியே குணம தென்றான்"],"p":"","r":"The king says that kings win the world by two powers, strength of arm and counsel, and that though the armed host is fine, he tells lion-like ministers that counsel is the quality that counts.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["soozhchi","padai","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 248","lb":"SO248","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["counsel is a better power than strength of arm for a king who would win the world","general","asserted",4,"vinjaiyar_vendhan"]],"ag":["விஞ்சையர் வேந்தன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["விஞ்சையர் வேந்தன்","அமைச்சர்"],"ch":"so:13"},"so:249":{"book":"soolamani","num":249,"t":["ஊழ்வர வன்ன தேனு மொருவகைக் கரும மெல்லாம்","சூழ்பவர் சூழ்ந்து சூழுஞ் சூழ்ச்சியுட் டோ ன்று மன்றே","யாழ்பகர்ந் தினிய தீஞ்சொ லமிர்தனா ரேனுஞ் சூழ்ச்சி","வாழ்பவர் வல்ல ராயின் மன்னராய் மலர்ப வன்றே"],"p":"","r":"The king says that even what comes like fate shows itself within the counsel of those who take counsel, and that those who live by counsel, if skilled, flourish as kings.","n":"ஊழ் வர அன்னது is condensed. The sense may be 'even an act that fate has set is shown through counsel', not that counsel overturns fate; the verse does not say a former deed is meant.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["oozh","soozhchi","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 249","lb":"SO249","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["even what arrives like fate takes shape in deliberation, and those skilled in counsel rise as kings","general","asserted",2,"vinjaiyar_vendhan"]],"ag":["விஞ்சையர் வேந்தன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["விஞ்சையர் வேந்தன்","அமைச்சர்"],"ch":"so:13"},"so:250":{"book":"soolamani","num":250,"t":["ஆற்றன்மூன் றோதப்பட்ட வரசர்கட் கவற்றின் மிக்க","ஆற்றறான் சூழ்ச்சி யென்ப தாதலா லதனை யாயும்","ஆற்றலா ரமைச்ச ராக வமைச்சரோ டமர்ந்து செல்லும்","ஆற்றலா னரச னாகி னரியதொன் றில்லை யன்றே"],"p":"","r":"The king says that of the three powers taught for kings, counsel is the highest, so a king who sits and goes with able ministers meets nothing hard.","n":"The verse does not name the three powers.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["soozhchi","amaichu","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 250","lb":"SO250","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["counsel is the highest of a king's three powers, and a king guided by able ministers finds nothing too hard","general","asserted",2,"vinjaiyar_vendhan"]],"ag":["விஞ்சையர் வேந்தன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["விஞ்சையர் வேந்தன்","அமைச்சர்"],"ch":"so:13"},"so:251":{"book":"soolamani","num":251,"t":["வடந்திகழ் முலையி னார்தங் காமத்தின் மதர்த்த மன்னர்க்","கடைந்தவர் மாண்பு மாங்கொன் றில்லையே லரசர் வாழ்க்கை","கடந்தவழ் கடாத்த வேழங் களித்தபின் கல்வி மாணா","மடந்தவ ழொருவன் மேல்கொண் டன்னதோர் வகையிற் றாமே"],"p":"","r":"The king says that kings maddened with love of the women who wear necklaces, if they have no learned men to join them, are like a rutting elephant flushed with pride and ridden by a fool without learning.","n":"Line 4's image is hard to construe: the rider is read here as a fool who has mounted the elephant.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","amaichu","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 251","lb":"SO251","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["a king who gives himself to women and has no learned counsellors is out of control like a rutting elephant under an ignorant rider","general","asserted",4,"vinjaiyar_vendhan"]],"ag":["விஞ்சையர் வேந்தன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["விஞ்சையர் வேந்தன்","அமைச்சர்"],"ch":"so:13"},"so:252":{"book":"soolamani","num":252,"t":["சுந்தரச் சுரும்புண் கண்ணிச் சூழ்கழ லரசர் வாழ்க்கை","தந்திர மறிந்து சூழ்வான் சூழ்ச்சிசார்ந் தமையல் வேண்டும்","மந்திரம் வழுவு மாயின் வாளெயிற் றரவு காய்ந்து","தந்திரந் தப்பி னாற்போற் றன்னையே தபுக்கு மன்றே"],"p":"","r":"The king says that kings must lean on counsel, and that if counsel slips, it turns on the king like an angry fanged snake whose charmer's art has failed.","n":"தந்திரம் in line 4 could be the charmer's art or a rule of statecraft; the snake image fits the first better.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["soozhchi","arasu","kedu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 252","lb":"SO252","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["a king must rely on counsel, and failed counsel destroys him","general","asserted",3,"vinjaiyar_vendhan"]],"ag":["விஞ்சையர் வேந்தன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["விஞ்சையர் வேந்தன்","அமைச்சர்"],"ch":"so:13"},"so:253":{"book":"soolamani","num":253,"t":["எடுத்தன னிலங்கு சாதி யெழிலொடு திகழு மேனு","மடுத்தன நிறத்த தாகு மணிகிளர் பளிங்கு போல","வடுத்தவ மலர்ந்து நுண்ணூன் மதியவர் வினையின் மாட்சி","கொடுத்தவா நிலைமை மன்னன் குணங்களாக் கொள்ப வன்றே"],"p":"","r":"The king says that a jewel of fair birth takes the colour of what it lies on like a crystal, and that so the good deeds of the learned are counted as the king's own virtues.","n":"Lines 1-3 are knotted; the verse is read here as a crystal taking its hue from what lies beside it, not as a claim that the ministers are flawless.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["amaichu","arasu","pugazh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 253","lb":"SO253","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["a king is credited with the virtues shown in his ministers' work","general","asserted",4,"vinjaiyar_vendhan"]],"ag":["விஞ்சையர் வேந்தன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["விஞ்சையர் வேந்தன்","அமைச்சர்"],"ch":"so:13"},"so:254":{"book":"soolamani","num":254,"t":["மன்னுநீர் வளாக மெல்லாம் வணக்குதல் வல்லீ ராய","பன்னுநூற் புலவீர் முன்னர்ப் பலபகர்ந் துரைப்ப தென்னை","யென்னைநீ ரிறைவ னாக்கி யிராப்பக லியற்ற வன்றே","யின்னநீ ரின்ப வெள்ள மியைந்தியா னுயர்ந்த தென்றான்"],"p":"","r":"The king tells the ministers it is useless to say more, since they made him king by their daily work, and that through them he has risen into a flood of joy.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["amaichu","arasu","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 254","lb":"SO254","mm":1,"ev":"The king thanks the ministers and says he owes his kingship and his joy to their work.","cl":[],"ag":["விஞ்சையர் வேந்தன்","அமைச்சர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["விஞ்சையர் வேந்தன்","அமைச்சர்"],"ch":"so:13"},"so:255":{"book":"soolamani","num":255,"t":["கொங்குடை வயிரக் குன்றின் கொழுஞ்சுடர் விளக்கிட் டாங்கு","நங்குடி விளங்க வந்த நங்கைதன் னலத்திற் கொத்தான்","தங்குடி விளங்க நின்ற தன்மையா னெவன்கொ லென்றான்","சங்குடைந் தனைய தாழைத் தடமலர்த் தொடைய லானே"],"p":"","r":"The king asks who could match the worth of the maiden born to light his house like a lamp on a diamond hill, and in what way he must stand so that his own house shines.","n":"The verse names no marriage; the question of who is her match is read here from the lamp image and the word நங்கை, 'the woman', and the poem's turn to a Vidyadhara match elsewhere in the section.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kudi","azhagu","soozhchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 255","lb":"SO255","mm":1,"ev":"The king puts to the ministers the question of the fit husband for the maiden who lights their house.","cl":[],"ag":["விஞ்சையர் வேந்தன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["விஞ்சையர் வேந்தன்","அமைச்சர்"],"ch":"so:13"},"so:256":{"book":"soolamani","num":256,"t":["இறையிவை மொழியக் கேட்டே யிருந்தவ ரிறைஞ்சி யேத்தி","யறைகழ லரவத் தானை யணிமுடி யரச ரேறே","நிறைபுக ழுலகங் காத்து நிலாகநின் னிறைமை யென்று","முறைமுறை மொழிய லுற்று முன்னிய முகத்த ரானார்"],"p":"","r":"The ministers hear the king, bow and praise him, wish him a long, widely praised rule over the earth, and each in turn prepares to speak.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["amaichu","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 256","lb":"SO256","mm":1,"ev":"The ministers bow, praise the king and prepare to speak in turn.","cl":[],"ag":["அமைச்சர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:13"},"so:257":{"book":"soolamani","num":257,"t":["பணிந்துமற் றேனை யார்பாங் கிருப்பநூல் பலவு நோக்கித்","துணிந்துதன் புலைமை தோன்றச் சச்சுதன் சொல்ல லுற்றான்","இணந்துநின் றுலவுந் தும்பி யிடையிடை யிருண்டு தோன்ற","அணிந்துநின் றலரும் பைந்தா ரணிமணி முடியி னாற்கே"],"p":"","r":"One minister, Sachchutan, bows while the others sit aside, studies the many treatises, and begins to speak with humility before the king in his gem crown and green garland.","n":"Line 2 says he speaks out of 'his own lowliness'; the humility is a convention of address.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["amaichu","kalvi","panivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 257","lb":"SO257","mm":1,"ev":"The minister Sachchutan bows to the king and begins to speak.","cl":[],"ag":["சச்சுதன்","அமைச்சர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:13"},"so:258":{"book":"soolamani","num":258,"t":["பொற்கதிர் பரப்பி வந்து பொங்கிருள் புதைய நூறுந்","தொழிற்கதிர்க் கடவு டோ ன்றச் சூரிய காந்தமென்னும்","எழிற்கதிர்ப் பிறங்கல் வட்ட மெரியுமிழ்ந் திடுவ தன்றே","அழற்சதி ரிலங்குஞ் செவ்வே லதிர்கழ லரசர் கோவே"],"p":"","r":"Sachchutan tells the king that the sun-gem spits fire when the sun appears and scatters the night's dark, and addresses him as the king of kings with the blazing spear.","n":"The verse gives only the image; the application to the king and his ministers is drawn in verse 259.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 258","lb":"SO258","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["சச்சுதன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சச்சுதன்","விஞ்சையர் வேந்தன்"],"ch":"so:13"},"so:259":{"book":"soolamani","num":259,"t":["கோணைநூற் றடங்க மாட்டக் குணமிலார் குடர்க ணைய","ஆணைநூற் றடங்கக் காக்கு மரசர்த மருளி னாலே","பேணுநூற் புலவர் மாண்பும் பெருகுவ துருவத் தார்மேல்","பூணுநூற் பொலிந்து தோன்றும் பொன்வரை மார்ப வென்றான்"],"p":"","r":"Sachchutan says that by the grace of kings who guard the order of command and treatise, the worth of the learned poets grows, as the sacred thread shows bright on the chest of the golden-chested king.","n":"Lines 1-2 are dense; the sense taken is that kings who keep the rule of treatise draw out the worth of those who follow it, as the sun draws fire from the sun-gem in 258.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["amaichu","kalvi","arul"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 259","lb":"SO259","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["the standing of the learned grows through the grace of kings who guard the law of command and treatise","general","asserted",3,"sachchutan"]],"ag":["சச்சுதன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சச்சுதன்","விஞ்சையர் வேந்தன்"],"ch":"so:13"},"so:260":{"book":"soolamani","num":260,"t":["சூழ்கதிர் தொழுதி மாலைச் சுடர்பிறைக் கடவு டோ ன்றித்","நாழ்கதிர் சொரிந்த போழ்திற் சந்திர காந்த மென்னும்","வீழ்கதிர் விளங்கு வட்டம் வெள்ளநீர் விரியு மன்றே","போழ்கதிர் பொழிந்து பொங்கிப் புலானிணம் பொழியும் வேலோய்"],"p":"","r":"A minister tells the king that the moon-gem pours a flood of water when the moon god rises and sheds cool rays, and addresses him as the spear-bearer who pours out flesh and fat.","n":"Line 4 turns from the cool image to the spear that sheds the flesh and fat of foes. The speaker is the next minister in the turn announced in 256, and is not named.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["arasu","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 260","lb":"SO260","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["அமைச்சர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["அமைச்சர்","விஞ்சையர் வேந்தன்"],"ch":"so:13"},"so:261":{"book":"soolamani","num":261,"t":["கண்ணளித் துலக மெல்லாங் கவின்பெறக் காவல் பூண்டு","தண்ணளித் தயங்கு செங்கோற் றாரவர் தவத்தி னாலே","மண்ணளித் தினிய நூலோர் மந்திர மலரு மென்றான்","விண்ணளித் திலங்கும் வெள்ளி விரிந்தவெண் குடையி னாற்கே"],"p":"","r":"The minister says that by the merit of kings who guard the world with a gracious straight sceptre, the counsel of learned men blossoms, and he speaks it to the king under the white umbrella.","n":"தவம் here may mean the austerity or merit of the king, not a former deed; the verse does not say.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sengol","thavam","amaichu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 261","lb":"SO261","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["the counsel of the learned flowers through the merit of kings who rule with a straight sceptre","general","asserted",2,"vinjaiyar_amaichar"]],"ag":["அமைச்சர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["அமைச்சர்","விஞ்சையர் வேந்தன்"],"ch":"so:13"},"so:262":{"book":"soolamani","num":262,"t":["கண்ணிய கடாத்த வேழங் கவுளினா னுரிஞப் பட்டுந்","தண்ணிய தன்மை நீங்காச் சந்தனச் சாதி போலப்","புண்ணிய கிழவர் கீழோர் பிழைத்தன பொறுப்ப வாயின்","மண்ணியல் வளாக மெல்லாம் வழிநின்று வணங்கு மன்றே"],"p":"","r":"The speaker says that as sandal keeps its coolness though a rutting elephant rubs against it, so if the virtuous masters bear the lapses of the lowly, the whole earth follows and bows to them.","n":"This verse has no closing என்றான் and may belong to a third minister's speech; the speaker is read here as the same unnamed minister as 260-261.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["porai","arasu","arul"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 262","lb":"SO262","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["lords who bear the lapses of the lowly are followed and honoured by the whole earth","general","asserted",3,"vinjaiyar_amaichar"]],"ag":["அமைச்சர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["அமைச்சர்","விஞ்சையர் வேந்தன்"],"ch":"so:13"},"so:263":{"book":"soolamani","num":263,"t":["நிறந்தலை மயங்க வெம்பி நெடுங்கடல் சுடுவ தாயின்","இறந்தலை மயங்கு நீர்வா ழுயிர்க்கிட ரெல்லை யுண்டோ ?","மறந்தலை மயங்கு செவ்வேன் மன்னவன் வெய்ய னாயின்","அறந்தலை மயங்கி வைய மரும்பட ருழக்கு மன்றே"],"p":"","r":"The counsellor says that if the sun scorched the great sea there would be no end to the misery of the creatures that live in its water, and so, when a king grows cruel and fierce, aram goes astray and the world suffers hard pain.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kodungol","aram","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 263","lb":"SO263","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["a cruel king throws aram into confusion and the whole world into suffering","general","reported",3,"amaichan"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["மந்திரக் கிழவன்","வேந்தன்"],"ch":"so:14"},"so:264":{"book":"soolamani","num":264,"t":["மண்குளிர் கொள்ளக் காக்கு மரபொழிந் தரசர் தங்கள்","விண்குளிர் கொள்ள வோங்கும் வெண்குடை வெதும்பு மாயிற்","கண்குளிர் கொள்ளப் பூக்குங் கடிகயத் தடமுங் காவும்","தண்குளிர் கொள்ளு மேனுத் தாமிக வெதும்பு மன்றே"],"p":"","r":"If kings give up the old way of guarding the earth's coolness and their white parasols grow hot, then even the cool ponds and groves, which flower to please the eye, will be scorched with them.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","kodungol","sengol"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 264","lb":"SO264","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["when the king abandons his duty to guard, the cool places of the land are scorched along with him","general","reported",1,"amaichan"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["மந்திரக் கிழவன்","வேந்தன்"],"ch":"so:14"},"so:265":{"book":"soolamani","num":265,"t":["தீயினர் படர்ந்து வேந்தன் செறுவதே புரியு மாயிற்","போயினம் படர்ந்து வாழும் புகலிட மின்மை யாலே","வேயினம் படர்ந்த சாரல் வேங்கையை வெருவிப் புல்வாய்","மாயினம் படர்ந்த தெல்லாம் வையகம் படரு மன்றே"],"p":"","r":"If the king bent on evil will do nothing but destroy, the people have no refuge to go to; as the deer of the bamboo slopes fear the tiger, so the whole world is driven out.","n":"The last two lines compare the people to deer driven from a tiger-haunted hill; the exact turn of line 4 is hard to fix.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kodungol","arasu","kudi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 265","lb":"SO265","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["a king who only destroys leaves his people no refuge","general","reported",1,"amaichan"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["மந்திரக் கிழவன்","வேந்தன்"],"ch":"so:14"},"so:266":{"book":"soolamani","num":266,"t":["மறந்தலை மயங்கி வையத் தொருவரை யொருவர் வாட்ட","விரந்தலை யுறாமை நோக்கி யின்னுயிர் போலக் காக்கும்","அறந்தலை நின்ற வேந்த ரடிநிழ லன்றி யார்க்கும்","சிறந்ததொன் றில்லை கண்டாய் திருமணி திகழும் பூணோய்"],"p":"","r":"When men wound one another, the only best thing for anyone is the shade at the feet of a king who stands in aram and guards the people like his own sweet life, watching that they not come to begging.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aram","sengol","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 266","lb":"SO266","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["nothing is better for any people than the shelter of a king who stands in aram and guards them like his own life","general","reported",3,"amaichan"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["மந்திரக் கிழவன்","வேந்தன்"],"ch":"so:14"},"so:267":{"book":"soolamani","num":267,"t":["ஒருமையாற் றுன்ப மெய்து மொருவனை யும்மை யாலே","திருமையான் முயங்குஞ் செல்வச் செருக்கொடு திளைப்ப நோக்கி","இருமையு மொருமை யாலே யியற்றலி னிறைவன் போலப்","பெருமையை யுடைய தெய்வம் பிறிதினி யில்லை யன்றே"],"p":"","r":"The counsellor says that, as the king orders a man's lot for both worlds in one act, there is no god so great as he, and calls him god-like on that ground; how the man's suffering and wealth are meant is not clear.","n":"The word உம்மை (a former birth) is in line 1, so the verse seems to set a king's power beside the working out of earlier deeds, but the grammar of lines 1-2 allows more than one construction.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["arasu","iraivan","vinai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 267","lb":"SO267","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["a king who shapes the lot of both worlds for his subjects is as great as a god, and no other deity is greater","general","reported",3,"amaichan"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["மந்திரக் கிழவன்","வேந்தன்"],"ch":"so:14"},"so:268":{"book":"soolamani","num":268,"t":["கண்ணெனப் படுவ மூன்று காவலன் கல்வி காமர்","விண்ணினைச் சுழல வோடும் வெய்யவ னென்னும் பேரார்","எண்ணினுட் டலைக்கண் வைத்த கண்ணஃ தில்லை யாயின்","மண்ணினுக் கிருளை நீக்கும் வகைபிறி தில்லை மன்னா"],"p":"","r":"The counsellor names three eyes, the king, learning and the sun that circles the sky, and says that without the one set first among them nothing else could drive the darkness from the earth.","n":"Which eye is meant by the one 'set at the head' (line 3) is not stated; the speaker's praise of the king in the preceding verses suggests the king, but it may also be learning.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["arasu","kalvi","arivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 268","lb":"SO268","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["the king, learning and the sun are the three eyes of the world","general","reported",1,"amaichan"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["மந்திரக் கிழவன்","வேந்தன்"],"ch":"so:14"},"so:269":{"book":"soolamani","num":269,"t":["குடிமிசை வெய்ய கோலுங் கூற்றமும் பிணியு நீர்சூழ்","படிமிசை யில்லை யாயின் வானுளயார் பயிறு மென்பார்","முடிமிசைத் திவள வேந்தர் முறைமுறை பணிய விம்மி","அடைமிசை நரலுஞ் செம்பொ னதிர்கழ லரச ரேறே"],"p":"","r":"The counsellor asks what the dwellers of the sky would say if the harsh rod, death and disease did not lie upon the people of the earth, and addresses the king as the lord of kings whom rulers bow to in turn.","n":"The question in lines 1-2 is cut short and the sense of பயிறும் (practise, serve) is not fixed, so the point of the question is unclear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kodungol","kootru","noy"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 269","lb":"SO269","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["மந்திரக் கிழவன்","வேந்தன்"],"ch":"so:14"},"so:270":{"book":"soolamani","num":270,"t":["தண்சுடர் கடவுள் போலத் தாரகைக் குழாங்க டாமே","விண்சுடர் விளக்க மாக விளங்கல வேந்தர் போல","மண்சுடர் வரைப்பின் மிக்க மக்களு மில்லை கண்டாய்","கண்சுடர் கனலச் சீறுங் கமழ்கடாக் களிற்று வேந்தே"],"p":"","r":"Not even the stars, shining like the cool moon in the sky, outshine men as kings do; no people in the world's bright round are greater than kings, says the counsellor to the angry elephant of a king.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","perumai","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 270","lb":"SO270","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["no people on earth are greater than kings","general","reported",3,"amaichan"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["மந்திரக் கிழவன்","வேந்தன்"],"ch":"so:14"},"so:271":{"book":"soolamani","num":271,"t":["அருந்தவ மரைச பார மிரண்டுமே யரிய தம்மை","வருந்தியு முயிரை யோம்பி மனத்தினை வணக்கல் வேண்டும்","திருந்திய விரண்டுந் தத்தஞ் செய்கையிர் றிரியு மாயிற்","பெருந்துயர் விளைக்கு மன்றே பிறங்குதார் நிறங்கொள் வேலோய்"],"p":"","r":"The two burdens of a king, hard austerity and the weight of rule, are each hard to bear and must be borne by hardship to himself, guarding life and bowing the mind; if either strays from its own work it brings great sorrow.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thavam","arasu","adakkam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 271","lb":"SO271","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["a king must bear both tapas and the burden of rule, each held to its proper work, or great sorrow follows","general","reported",1,"amaichan"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["மந்திரக் கிழவன்","வேந்தன்"],"ch":"so:14"},"so:272":{"book":"soolamani","num":272,"t":["அந்தரந் திரியுஞ் செய்கை யமரர்தம் மரசு வேண்டி","இந்திர வுலகங் காணு நெறியவை யாவை யென்னின்","மந்திரம் வழாத வாய்மை மாதவம் முயற லன்றேல்","தந்திரந் தழுவிச் செங்கோ றளர்விலன் றரித்த லென்றான்"],"p":"","r":"Asked what ways lead to the rule of the gods and the sight of Indra's world, the counsellor says that the true word that never fails the mantra and great austerity are the ways, or else a king holding the sceptre in policy without slackening.","n":"Two roads to the same world are put together: the ascetic's and the king's. The verse gives no word of grace; both depend on the doer's effort.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thavam","sengol","marumai","vaaimai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 272","lb":"SO272","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["the world of the gods is reached through truth faithful to the mantra and great austerity, or through unslackening just rule","general","reported",3,"amaichan"]],"ag":["மந்திரக் கிழவன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["மந்திரக் கிழவன்","வேந்தன்"],"ch":"so:14"},"so:273":{"book":"soolamani","num":273,"t":["மரந்தலை யிணங்கி வான்றோய் மணிவளர் வயிரக் குன்றம்","உரந்தனக் குயர வேந்தி யுய்த்திடு மொருவற் கேனும்","அருந்தவ மரசை பார மவைபொறை யரிது கண்டாய்","இரந்தவர்க் கீட்டப் பட்ட விருநிதிக் கிழவ வென்றான்"],"p":"","r":"Even a man who could lift a diamond hill on his strength to carry it far would find the two burdens, austerity and rule, hard to bear, the counsellor tells the lord of the great treasures given to beggars.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thavam","arasu","porai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 273","lb":"SO273","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["austerity and the burden of rule are harder to bear than lifting a mountain","general","reported",3,"amaichan"]],"ag":["மந்திரக் கிழவன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["மந்திரக் கிழவன்","வேந்தன்"],"ch":"so:14"},"so:274":{"book":"soolamani","num":274,"t":["உரிதினி னொருவன் செய்த வூழ்வினை யுதயஞ் செய்து","விரிதலி னதன துண்மை விளங்கினாற் போல வேந்தர்","கருதிய கருமச் சூழ்ச்சிப் பயத்தினாற் கருதும் வண்ணம்","எரிதவழ்ந் திலங்கும் வேலோய் என்ணுவ தென்ண மென்றான்"],"p":"","r":"The counsellor says that, as a deed done by a man in an earlier birth rises into fruit and shows that it was real, the fruit of the counsel kings take comes out as they intend, and asks the king to say what he is thinking.","n":"Karma is used as a comparison for counsel, not argued; the speaker treats it as known.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oozh","vinai","soozhchi","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 274","lb":"SO274","mm":1,"ev":"The counsellor asks the king to tell the plan he has in mind.","cl":[["a deed of a former birth rises into fruit and shows that it was real","general","reported",1,"amaichan"]],"ag":["மந்திரக் கிழவன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["மந்திரக் கிழவன்","வேந்தன்"],"ch":"so:14"},"so:275":{"book":"soolamani","num":275,"t":["பஞ்சிநன் றூட்டப் பட்ட மாதுளம் பருவ வித்து","மஞ்சிநின் றகன்ற சாகை மலரிடை வடிவு காட்டும்","அஞ்சிநின் றனலும் வேலோய் சூழ்ச்சியு மன்ன தேயால்","வெஞ்சொலொன் றுரைக்க மாட்டா விடுசுடர் விளங்கு பூணோய்"],"p":"","r":"Counsel, says the speaker, is like a pomegranate seed fed with cotton, whose form shows again in the blossom of its branch; he adds that he cannot say one harsh word.","n":"The comparison is clear in outline, but how the seed's form returning in the flower fits counsel, and who is the subject of line 4, is not certain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["soozhchi","amaichu","panivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 275","lb":"SO275","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["மந்திரக் கிழவன்","வேந்தன்"],"ch":"so:14"},"so:276":{"book":"soolamani","num":276,"t":["கொற்றவேன் மன்னர்க் கோதுங் குணமெலாங் குழுமி வந்து","முற்றுநின் றுருவு கொண்ட மூர்த்திநின் முன்னர் யாங்கள்","இற்றென வுரைக்கு நீதி யோதுநூ லெல்லை காணக்","கற்றவர் முன்னை யேனோர் கதையொத்துக் காட்டு மன்றே"],"p":"","r":"The counsellor says that all the virtues told of kings gather and take form in the king before him, so whatever the counsellors say of the ways of policy will seem to men who have learned the books to their end like an old tale.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["amaichu","panivu","kalvi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 276","lb":"SO276","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["மந்திரக் கிழவன்","வேந்தன்"],"ch":"so:14"},"so:277":{"book":"soolamani","num":277,"t":["தேன்மகிழ் தெரிய லாய்நின் றிருக்குலந் தெளிப்ப வந்த","பான்மகிழ்ந் தனைய தீஞ்சொற் பவழவாய்ப் பரவை யல்கும்","வான்மகிழ் மணங்கொண் மேனி யணங்கினுக் குரிய கோனை","யான்மகிழ் துணர்த்தக் கேட்பி னிடைசிறி தருளு கென்றான்"],"p":"","r":"The counsellor asks the king, if he will hear what he tells gladly of the lord fit for the maiden, to grant a little time.","n":"Lines 1-3 name a maiden of sweet speech and coral lips and a lord fit for her; who is meant is not stated until later verses, and the reading of the clause is therefore open.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["amaichu","solvanmai","kudi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 277","lb":"SO277","mm":1,"ev":"The counsellor asks for a little time to speak about the lord fit for the maiden.","cl":[],"ag":["மந்திரக் கிழவன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["மந்திரக் கிழவன்","வேந்தன்"],"ch":"so:14"},"so:278":{"book":"soolamani","num":278,"t":["மஞ்சிவர் மால்வரைச் சென்னி வடமலை","விஞ்சையர் வாழும் விழாவணி நல்லுல","கஞ்சியல் வில்லோ யதுமற் றமரர்கள்","துஞ்சிய வில்லாத் துறக்க மனைத்தே"],"p":"","r":"The counsellor begins to describe the northern hill, adorned for festival, where the Vidyadharas live, and says it is like a heaven in which the gods are never gone.","n":"The speaker calls the hill the northern mountain (வடமலை); verse 130 places the city on the southern ridge.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naadu","azhagu","marumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 278","lb":"SO278","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["மந்திரக் கிழவன்","வேந்தன்"],"ch":"so:14"},"so:279":{"book":"soolamani","num":279,"t":["மண்ணியல் வாழ்நர்க்கும் வானுல கொப்பது","புண்ணிய மில்லார் புகுதற் கரியது","கண்ணிய கற்பகக் கானங் கலந்தது","வின்ணிய லின்பம் விரவிற் றினிதே"],"p":"","r":"The counsellor says this realm is like the sky-world to the people of earth but hard for those without merit to enter, full of wishing-tree groves, and rich in heaven's pleasures.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naadu","inbam","marumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 279","lb":"SO279","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["those without merit cannot enter such a world","general","reported",2,"amaichan"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["மந்திரக் கிழவன்","வேந்தன்"],"ch":"so:14"},"so:280":{"book":"soolamani","num":280,"t":["எல்லா விருதுவு மீனும் பொழிலின்","தெல்லா நிதியு மியன்ற விடத்தின","தெல்லா வமரர் கணமு மிராப்பகல்","எல்லாப் புலமு நுகர்தற் கினிதே"],"p":"","r":"The groves of the realm bear fruit in every season, wealth of every kind is gathered in it, and all troops of gods can enjoy all the senses there day and night.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naadu","selvam","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 280","lb":"SO280","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["மந்திரக் கிழவன்","வேந்தன்"],"ch":"so:14"},"so:281":{"book":"soolamani","num":281,"t":["பொன்னிதழ்த் தாமரை பொய்கையுட் பூப்பன","பொன்னிதழ்த் தாமம் பொழில்வா யவிழ்ப்பன","பொன்னிதழ்த் தாது மணிநிலம் போர்ப்பன","பொன்னிதழ்த் தாது துகளாய்ப் பொலிவன"],"p":"","r":"Lotuses with golden petals bloom in its ponds, golden garlands open in its groves, golden pollen covers its jewelled ground, and golden dust shines in the air.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naadu","azhagu","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 281","lb":"SO281","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["மந்திரக் கிழவன்","வேந்தன்"],"ch":"so:14"},"so:282":{"book":"soolamani","num":282,"t":["கானங்க ளாவன கற்பகங் காமுகர்","தானங்க ளாவன சந்தனத் தாழ்பொழில்","நானங்க ளாவன நாவி நருவிரை","வானங்க ளாம்வகை மற்றுமொன் றுண்டோ ?"],"p":"","r":"The groves are wishing-trees, the lovers' places are sandal bowers and the perfumes are musk and civet; the counsellor asks whether anything else could be called heavens.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naadu","inbam","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 282","lb":"SO282","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["மந்திரக் கிழவன்","வேந்தன்"],"ch":"so:14"},"so:283":{"book":"soolamani","num":283,"t":["மணிக்கற் படாதன மண்டபம் செம்பொன்","குணிக்கப் படாதன குளிர்புனல் நீத்தம்","கணிக்கப் படாத கதிர்மணிக் குன்றம்","பிணிக்கப் படாதவர் யாரவை பெற்றால்"],"p":"","r":"No hall there lacks jewels, its golden streams are past counting, and its gem hills cannot be numbered; the counsellor asks who would not be bound if he got them.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","pattru","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 283","lb":"SO283","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["no one stays free of attachment once he has gained such riches","general","reported",4,"amaichan"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["மந்திரக் கிழவன்","வேந்தன்"],"ch":"so:14"},"so:284":{"book":"soolamani","num":284,"t":["ஆங்கதன் மேல வறுபது மாநகர்","தீங்கதிர் மண்டிலஞ் சேர்ந்து திளைப்பன","நீங்கரு மாநகர் தம்மு ணிலாவிரிந்","தோங்கிய சூளா மணியி னொளிர்வது"],"p":"","r":"Above it, the counsellor says, sixty great cities touch the sun's bright disk, and among the cities that cannot be left one shines like a crest-jewel under spreading moonlight.","n":"சூளாமணி, the crest-jewel, is the name of the whole work; here the word is a comparison for one city.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naadu","azhagu","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 284","lb":"SO284","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["மந்திரக் கிழவன்","வேந்தன்"],"ch":"so:14"},"so:285":{"book":"soolamani","num":285,"t":["மரத்தினு மண்ணினு மாடங்கள் யாவும்","திருத்தின வில்லது செம்பொ னுலகில்","புரத்தினை வெல்வது பொன்னகர் பூந்தண்","ணிரத்தின பல்லவ மென்பதொன் றுண்டே"],"p":"","r":"None of the mansions of that golden world is built of wood or clay, and a golden city called Ratna-pallavam surpasses the city of the old gods.","n":"The initial of line 4 is garbled, and the identity of the city named (and its relation to the city of verse 130) is not clear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["naadu","azhagu","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 285","lb":"SO285","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["மந்திரக் கிழவன்","வேந்தன்"],"ch":"so:14"},"so:286":{"book":"soolamani","num":286,"t":["வளைத்தகை மங்கையர் மைந்தரொ டாடி","முளைத்தெழு காம முடிவில ராகித்","திளைத்தலி னின்னகர் தெய்வ வுலகம்","களித்திழிந் தன்னதோர் கவ்வை யுடைத்தே"],"p":"","r":"Women with bangled hands play with their men, desire sprouts endlessly in them, and the city seems a gods' world come down in joy, loud with its noise.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","inbam","marumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 286","lb":"SO286","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["மந்திரக் கிழவன்","வேந்தன்"],"ch":"so:14"},"so:287":{"book":"soolamani","num":287,"t":["ஆடவர் கொம்பனை யாரிளை யாரவர்","பாடக மெல்லோர் பரவிய சீறடி","தோடலர் தொங்கலங் குஞ்சியுட் டோ யவைத்","தூட லுணர்த்துந் தொழிலதொன் றுண்டே"],"p":"","r":"Young women, creeper-slender to men and praised for their small anklet-feet, have a custom of touching those feet to the flower-garlanded hair of their lovers, which tells of the lovers' quarrel.","n":"Lines 3 and 4 are hard to construe: தோய வைத்து ஊடல் உணர்த்தும் தொழில் may mean the woman sets her foot in the man's hair to show or to end her sulk. The reading follows the nearer sense and is held loosely.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","oodal","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 287","lb":"SO287","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["சச்சுதன்","விஞ்சையர் வேந்தன்"],"ch":"so:15"},"so:288":{"book":"soolamani","num":288,"t":["சிலைத்தடந் தோளவர் செஞ்சாந் தணிந்த","மலைத்தட மார்பிடை மைமதர்க் கண்ணார்","முலைத்தடம் பாய முரிந்து முடவண்","டிலைத்தடத் தேங்கு மிரக்க முளதே"],"p":"","r":"The broad chests of strong-armed men, smeared with red sandal, are pressed by the breasts of dark-eyed women, and the bees caught in the crushed paste cling and cry out.","n":"முடவண் டிலைத்தடத் தேங்கும் இரக்கம் (line 3-4) is obscure; முடவண்டு could be a bee with a crushed wing, and the pity it draws is read here as a figure for the crushed paste.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 288","lb":"SO288","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["சச்சுதன்","விஞ்சையர் வேந்தன்"],"ch":"so:15"},"so:289":{"book":"soolamani","num":289,"t":["வனைத்தன போலும் வளர்ந்த முலையார்","இனைந்துதங் காதல ரின்பக் கனிகள்","கனிந்து களித்தகங் காமங் கலந்துண","முனிந்து புருவ முரிவ பலவே"],"p":"","r":"Women of full breasts, wearied, taste with their lovers the ripe fruits of love's joy, and many lift a curved brow in mock anger as desire mingles within them.","n":"The ripe fruits (இன்பக் கனிகள்) are the pleasure itself, and the frowning brow is the sulk of lovers' play, not a real quarrel.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","inbam","oodal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 289","lb":"SO289","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["சச்சுதன்","விஞ்சையர் வேந்தன்"],"ch":"so:15"},"so:290":{"book":"soolamani","num":290,"t":["செவ்வாய்ப் பவழக் கடிகைத் திரளெனும்","அவ்வா யமிர்தமுண் டார்பல ராடவர்","ஒவ்வா திளைப்ப ரொசிந்தன ரோடரி","மைவா ணெடுங்கண் மலக்கம் பெரிதே"],"p":"","r":"Many men have drunk the nectar of coral lips and cannot match the women's appetite, so they tire, and the dark long-eyed women's languor is great.","n":"The verse says men tire because they are not equal to the women (ஒவ்வாது இளைப்பர்), which turns the usual picture of the weary woman about.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 290","lb":"SO290","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["சச்சுதன்","விஞ்சையர் வேந்தன்"],"ch":"so:15"},"so:291":{"book":"soolamani","num":291,"t":["வளர்வன போலு மருங்குல்க ணோவத்","தளர்வன போல்பவர் தாமக் குழன்மேற்","கிளர்வன போதிள வாசங் கிளைத்துண்","டுளர்வன போதரு மூதை யுளதே"],"p":"","r":"Waists seem to ache and droop, bees wander eating the fresh scent of buds in the women's wreathed hair, and a breeze moves through.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 291","lb":"SO291","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["சச்சுதன்","விஞ்சையர் வேந்தன்"],"ch":"so:15"},"so:292":{"book":"soolamani","num":292,"t":["பஞ்சா ரகலல்குற் பாவையர் பூண்முலைச்","செங்சாந் தணிந்து திகழ்ந்த மணிவண்டு","மஞ்சார் பொழிலுள் வளர்பெடை கண்டதற்","கஞ்சா வொளிக்கு மயல ததுவே"],"p":"","r":"A gem-dark bee, dyed by the red sandal of the women's breasts, sees its growing mate in the cloud-high grove and, unafraid, hides nearby.","n":"The last two lines are compressed: அஞ்சா ஒளிக்கும் அயலது அதுவே is taken as the bee hiding unafraid beside its mate, a pairing that echoes the lovers.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 292","lb":"SO292","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["சச்சுதன்","விஞ்சையர் வேந்தன்"],"ch":"so:15"},"so:293":{"book":"soolamani","num":293,"t":["பாசிலை மென்றழைப் பள்ளியுட் பாவையர்","தூசினு ணின்று சொரிமணிக் கோவையும்","பூசின சாந்தும் பிணையலும் போர்த்திடை","மூசின வண்டின மொய்ப்பொழி லெல்லாம்"],"p":"","r":"On beds of soft leaves in the grove, strings of gems slipped from the women's clothing, sandal paste and garlands lie in a heap over which swarms of bees press.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 293","lb":"SO293","mm":1,"ev":"Lovers leave their gems, paste and garlands scattered over the leaf beds of the grove, and bees swarm over them.","cl":[],"ag":["காதலர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சச்சுதன்","விஞ்சையர் வேந்தன்"],"ch":"so:15"},"so:294":{"book":"soolamani","num":294,"t":["காம விலேகையுங் கற்பக மாலையும்","சேம மணிநகைச் செப்பினு ளேந்துபு","தூமக் குழலவர் தூது திரிபவர்","தாமத் தெருவிடை தாம்பலர் கண்டாய்"],"p":"","r":"Go-between women with love-letters and wishing-tree garlands in jewelled caskets roam the garland street, and the speaker bids the king see how many there are.","n":"கண்டாய் (line 4) is the speaker's own pointing-out: the sight is offered to the king as proof of the city's life.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","thoothu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 294","lb":"SO294","mm":1,"ev":"Go-between women carry love-letters and garlands through the garland street.","cl":[],"ag":["தூது திரிபவர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சச்சுதன்","விஞ்சையர் வேந்தன்"],"ch":"so:15"},"so:295":{"book":"soolamani","num":295,"t":["தாமத் தொடையல் பரிந்து தமனிய","வாமக் கலங்கள் புலம்ப மகளிர்கள்","காமக் கடலைக் கலக்குங் கழலவர்","தீமைத் தொழிலவை தேர்ந்துள வன்றே"],"p":"","r":"Garlands snap and golden vessels ring out as anklet-wearing men churn the sea of desire among the women; their doings are marked as wicked.","n":"தீமைத் தொழிலவை தேர்ந்துள வன்றே (line 4) can be read as an aside that these doings are faulty, or as the poet's tag for such deeds being ready to hand; the sense is not fixed, and no claim is built on it.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","kutram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 295","lb":"SO295","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["சச்சுதன்","விஞ்சையர் வேந்தன்"],"ch":"so:15"},"so:296":{"book":"soolamani","num":296,"t":["பொன்னகர் தன்னை யாள்வான் புரந்தர னணைய மாண்பின்","மன்னவன் மயூர கண்டன் மற்றவன் தேவி மாருள்","மின்னவிர் மருங்கு னீலாங் கனையென விளங்கி நின்றா","ளன்னவள் புதல்வன் கண்டா யச்சுவக் கிரீவ னென்பான்"],"p":"","r":"The speaker names Asvagriva's line: his father Mayurakandan, a king as majestic as Indra over the golden city, and his mother Nilanganai, a chief queen who shines among the wives.","n":"This opens the minister's turn from the city to its lord; the earlier verses of the sarukkam give no speaker name, and the speaker is Sachchutan only from 307.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","pirappu","kudi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 296","lb":"SO296","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["சச்சுதன்","விஞ்சையர் வேந்தன்"],"ch":"so:15"},"so:297":{"book":"soolamani","num":297,"t":["அதிர்கழ லலங்கல் வேலோ யச்சுவக் கிரீவ னென்னும்","பொதியவிழ் பொலங்கொள் பைந்தார்ப் புரவலன் றிகிரி யெய்தி","மதிதவழ் குன்ற மெல்லாம் வணக்கிய பின்றை மண்ணும்","கொதிதவழ் வேலி னான்றன் குறிப்பொடு கூடிற் றன்றே"],"p":"","r":"Asvagriva, lord of the garlanded spear, took up the discus, bowed the mountains that moon-skim, and the earth fell in with his will.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vendhan","perumai","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 297","lb":"SO297","mm":1,"ev":"Asvagriva wins the discus and brings the hill-kings, and then the land, under his will.","cl":[],"ag":["அச்சுவக் கிரீவன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சச்சுதன்","விஞ்சையர் வேந்தன்"],"ch":"so:15"},"so:298":{"book":"soolamani","num":298,"t":["சுற்றமாண் புடைமை யாலுஞ் சூழ்கதிர்த் திகிரி யாளுங்","கொற்றமாங் குடைமை யாலுங் குலத்தது பெருமை யாலுங்","சுற்றமாண் விஞ்சை யாலுங் கருதிய முடித்த லாலும்","வெற்றிவே லவனோ டொப்பார் வேந்தர்மற் றில்லை வேந்தே"],"p":"","r":"No king is his equal in strength of kin, in the discus he rules by, in the parasol of victory, in lineage, in learned vidya and in finishing what he plans.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","perumai","sutram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 298","lb":"SO298","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["a king's standing rests on kin, lineage, learning, the discus and the finishing of what he plans","general","reported",1,"sachchutan"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["சச்சுதன்","விஞ்சையர் வேந்தன்"],"ch":"so:15"},"so:299":{"book":"soolamani","num":299,"t":["தம்பியர் நீலத் தேரோன் றயங்குதார் நீல கண்டன்","வம்புயர் மகரப் பேழ்வாய் வயிரமா கண்டன் வண்டும்","தும்பியுந் துவைக்குந் தொங்கற் சுகண்டனென் றிவர்கள் கண்டாய்"],"p":"","r":"The speaker names Asvagriva's three younger brothers: Nilakandan, Vairamakandan and Sukandan.","n":"The edition gives this verse three lines; a fourth is lost or was never cut.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sutram","kudi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 299","lb":"SO299","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["சச்சுதன்","விஞ்சையர் வேந்தன்"],"ch":"so:15"},"so:300":{"book":"soolamani","num":300,"t":["படையின தமைதி கூழின் பகுதியென் றிவற்றின் பன்மாண்","புடையவ ரவனொ டொப்பா ரொருவர்மற் றில்லை வேந்தே","விடயமொன் றின்றி வென்ற விடுசுடை ராழி யாளும்","நடையவ னுவப்பின் ஞாலம் பிறருழை நடப்ப தென்றான்"],"p":"","r":"In the army, its calm order and the share of food, and in other manifold things, none equals him; the discus he rules by is held without a country to answer to, and the speaker says that if he is pleased the world would pass to others.","n":"The army and the food are two of the limbs the Kural counts (381). நடையவன் உவப்பின் ஞாலம் பிறருழை நடப்ப தென்றான் (line 4) is hard to place and may mean that the world will go over to others' side if he is pleased.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["arasu","padai","perumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 300","lb":"SO300","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["சச்சுதன்","விஞ்சையர் வேந்தன்"],"ch":"so:15"},"so:301":{"book":"soolamani","num":301,"t":["ஆணைநூ லமைச்ச னாவா னரிமஞ்சு வவன தற்றல்","கோணை நூற் பவரைத் தன்சொற் குறிப்பின்மே னிறுத்த வல்லான்","பேணுநூ னிமித்தம் வல்லான் சதவிந்து பெரிய நீரான்","காணுநூற் புலமை யாருங் காண்பவரில்லை கண்டாய்"],"p":"","r":"Arimanju is his minister of the law-treatise, able to bring learned men round to his word; Satavindu, great-natured, is skilled in the omen-books; no learned man sees his equal.","n":"The minister's mastery is told wholly in books (ஆணைநூல், நூல், நிமித்தம்), with no word for native wit.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["amaichu","kalvi","arivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 301","lb":"SO301","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["சச்சுதன்","விஞ்சையர் வேந்தன்"],"ch":"so:15"},"so:302":{"book":"soolamani","num":302,"t":["தன்னலாற் றெய்வம் பேணார் சார்ந்தவர் தானுஞ் சார்ந்தார்க்","கென்னலா லிவருக் குற்றா ரில்லையென் றிரங்கு நீரான்","பொன்னெலா நெதிய மாரப் பொழிந்திடு கின்ற பூமி","மன்னெலா மவனை யன்றி வணங்குவ தில்லை மன்னா"],"p":"","r":"A man of pity says that those who lean on him honour no god but him and have no kin but him; the earth rains wealth for him, and all kings bow to none other.","n":"The subject of lines 1 and 2 is unclear: it could be Satavindu of 301 or Asvagriva himself, and தன்னலால் தெய்வம் பேணார் (line 1) may mean they honour no god other than him.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["arasu","arul","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 302","lb":"SO302","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["சச்சுதன்","விஞ்சையர் வேந்தன்"],"ch":"so:15"},"so:303":{"book":"soolamani","num":303,"t":["குளிருவா ளுழுவை யன்னான் குமாரகா லத்து முன்னே","களிருநூ றெடுக்க லாகக் கற்றிரள் கடகக் கையால்","ஒளிறுவா ளுழவ னேந்தி யுருட்டிவட் டாட வன்றே","வெளிறிலாக் கேள்வி யானை விஞ்சைய ரஞ்சி யிட்டார்"],"p":"","r":"In his youth the sword-bearer lifted and rolled like a toy a heap of rock that two hundred elephants might have raised, so that the faultless-learned Vidyadharas took fright.","n":"The subject is named only by the title of the sword-bearer, குமாரகாலம், his youth; the verse is read as about Asvagriva.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["perumai","vali","anjaamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 303","lb":"SO303","mm":1,"ev":"The young Asvagriva lifts and rolls a mass of rock that two hundred elephants could raise, and the Vidyadharas fear him.","cl":[],"ag":["அச்சுவக் கிரீவன்","விஞ்சையர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சச்சுதன்","விஞ்சையர் வேந்தன்"],"ch":"so:15"},"so:304":{"book":"soolamani","num":304,"t":["முற்றவ முடைமை யாலே மூரிநீ ருலக மெல்லாம்","மற்றவ னேவல் கேளா மன்னவ ரில்லை மன்னா","செற்றவ னலித லஞ்சித் திறைகொடுத் தறிவித் தன்றே","நற்றவ நங்கை தோன்றா முன்னநா மாண்ட தெல்லம்"],"p":"","r":"Because of his full austerity no king of the sea-girt earth fails to heed his command; fearing his anger they paid tribute and sent word, and all that was ours was lost before the girl of good austerity appeared.","n":"நாம் மாண்டது எல்லாம் (line 4) may mean everything we lost, or all that passed before her birth; the sense is not fixed. The claim in line 1 is a courtier's, put to win the king, and the work does not here answer it.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thavam","arasu","vinai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 304","lb":"SO304","mm":1,"ev":"The kings, fearing Asvagriva's anger, pay tribute and send word to him.","cl":[["complete austerity in a past life brings a ruler the obedience of all kings","general","reported",1,"sachchutan"]],"ag":["மன்னர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சச்சுதன்","விஞ்சையர் வேந்தன்"],"ch":"so:15"},"so:305":{"book":"soolamani","num":305,"t":["ஈங்குநங் குலக்கொம் பொப்பாள் பிறந்தபி னினிய னாகித்","தேங்கம ழலங்கல் வேலோன் றிறைகொள லொழிந்து செல்லு","மாங்கவன் றிறங்க ளெல்லா மறிதியா லாணை வேந்தே","தீங்கியா னுணர்த்திற் றுண்டோ திருவடி தெளிக வென்றான்"],"p":"","r":"Since the girl, the creeper of the speaker's own family, was born, Asvagriva has grown kind and stopped taking tribute, and the speaker asks the king to say whether he has told anything untrue.","n":"The speaker's own reckoning is that the girl's birth is why Asvagriva softened; the verse does not say whether the girl's beauty or her line is the cause.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","thunai","vaaimai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 305","lb":"SO305","mm":1,"ev":"After the girl's birth Asvagriva gives up taking tribute from the Vidyadharas.","cl":[],"ag":["அச்சுவக் கிரீவன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சச்சுதன்","விஞ்சையர் வேந்தன்"],"ch":"so:15"},"so:306":{"book":"soolamani","num":306,"t":["மற்றவற் குரிய ணங்கை யென்பதன் மனத்தி னோடு","முற்றுவந் துளது சால வுறுதியு முடைய தொக்கும்","வெற்றிவேல் விஞ்சை யாரு மஞ்சுவர் மின்செய் பைம்பூண்","கொற்றவ குறிப்புண் டாயிற் கொடுப்பது குணங்கொ லென்றான்"],"p":"","r":"The speaker says the girl is fit for Asvagriva, that his mind is set and firm, that the Vidyadharas fear him, and asks whether, if the king is willing, giving her would not be good.","n":"Bhavachutan answers in 308 to 310, but the argument is not finished in this chapter, so the claim is left as reported, not refuted.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","kurippu","panivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 306","lb":"SO306","mm":1,"ev":"The minister urges the Vidyadhara king to give his daughter to Asvagriva.","cl":[["it is wise and good to give the girl to the suitor whom all fear","general","reported",4,"sachchutan"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["சச்சுதன்","விஞ்சையர் வேந்தன்"],"ch":"so:15"},"so:307":{"book":"soolamani","num":307,"t":["சுடர்மணி மருங்குற் பைங்கட் சுளிமுகக் களிதல் யானை","யடர்மணிக் கதிரும் பைம்பொன் மாலையு மணிந்த சென்னித்","தொடர்மணிப் பூணி னாற்குச் சச்சுதன் சொல்லக் கேட்டே","படர்மணிப் படலை மாலைப் பவச்சுதன் பகர லுற்றான்"],"p":"","r":"When Sachchutan, whom the king of the golden-mounted elephant honours, has spoken, Bhavachutan rises to speak.","n":"This verse names the two ministers, and so fixes that the speaker of 277 to 306 was Sachchutan.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["amaichu","avai","solvanmai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 307","lb":"SO307","mm":1,"ev":"Bhavachutan takes up the debate after Sachchutan has spoken.","cl":[],"ag":["சச்சுதன்","பவச்சுதன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:15"},"so:308":{"book":"soolamani","num":308,"t":["நூலா ராய்ந்து நுண்பொறி கண்ணு நொடிவல்லான்","மேலா ராயு மேதமை யாலு மிகநல்லான்","தோலா நாவிற் சச்சுதன் சொல்லும் பொருளெல்லாம்","வேலார் கையாய் மெய்ம்மைய வன்றே மிகையாலோ"],"p":"","r":"Bhavachutan says that Sachchutan, learned and quick to judge, wise in sifting and strong of tongue, says nothing that is not true, and that nothing he has said is excess.","n":"He first grants his colleague's learning and truthfulness, and turns in 309.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["amaichu","vaaimai","kalvi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 308","lb":"SO308","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["பவச்சுதன்","விஞ்சையர் வேந்தன்"],"ch":"so:15"},"so:309":{"book":"soolamani","num":309,"t":["தேனும் வண்டுந் தீதில பாடுஞ் செறிதாரோய்!","யானுங் கண்டே னச்சுவ கண்டேன் றிறமஃதே","மானங் கொண்ட மாரதர் போரே றனையாயோ","ரூனங் கண்டே னொட்டினு மொட்டே னுரைசெய்கேன்"],"p":"","r":"Bhavachutan says he too saw Asvagriva's worth, but he saw one fault in this proud warrior, and he will not join in without speaking it out.","n":"The fault itself is named only after this chapter. ஒட்டினும் ஒட்டேன் (line 4) is read as refusing to join the colleague's view while holding back this point.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["amaichu","vaaimai","thelivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 309","lb":"SO309","mm":1,"ev":"Bhavachutan declares that he has found a fault in Asvagriva and will state it.","cl":[["a counsellor must voice a fault he has seen, even against a colleague's agreed praise","general","reported",4,"bavachutan"]],"ag":["பவச்சுதன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["பவச்சுதன்","விஞ்சையர் வேந்தன்"],"ch":"so:15"},"so:310":{"book":"soolamani","num":310,"t":["மானக் கோதை மாசறு வேலோய் வரவெண்ணி","நானக் கோதை நங்கை பிறந்த நாளானே","வானக் கோளின் மாண்புணர் வார்கண் மறுவில்லாத்","தானக் கோளிற் சாதக வோலை தலைவைத்தார்"],"p":"","r":"When the lady of the wreath was born, those who know the excellence of the sky's planets, faultless in their craft, laid the birth-horoscope leaf in the place of the giving-planet.","n":"தானக் கோளிற் சாதக வோலை தலைவைத்தார் (line 4) is read as placing the leaf under the planet of giving; the horoscope's content is not told here.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaalam","arivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 310","lb":"SO310","mm":1,"ev":"On the day the girl is born, astrologers draw up her birth horoscope.","cl":[],"ag":["மாண்புணர் வார்"],"ec":[],"spk":["பவச்சுதன்","விஞ்சையர் வேந்தன்"],"ch":"so:15"},"so:311":{"book":"soolamani","num":311,"t":["காவிப் பட்டங் கள்விரி கானற் கடனாடன்","மேவிப் பட்டம் பெற்றவன் காதன் மேயனால்","ஏவிப் பட்ட மீந்தவ ரெல்லா மினிதேத்தும்","தேவிப் பட்டஞ் சேர்பவ ளன்றே திருவன்னாள்"],"p":"","r":"The speaker says the woman who takes the queen's crown beside the lord of the flowered shore is like Lakshmi herself, praised by all the others sent to wear the title.","n":"The grammar of lines 1-3 is compressed and the queen meant is not named; the verse is read as praise of a chief queen at a court.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","perumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 311","lb":"SO311","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["மந்திரக் கிழவர்",""],"ch":"so:16"},"so:312":{"book":"soolamani","num":312,"t":["நங்கோ னங்கை நன்மக னாகி நனிவந்தான்","தங்கோ னேவத் தானிள வேந்தாய்த் தலைநின்றான்","எங்கோ னென்றே யிவ்வுல கேத்து மியறன்னால்","செங்கோ லின்பஞ் சேர்பவ னன்றே செருவேலோன்"],"p":"","r":"The speaker says the son who came as the good child of our lord and, by his lord's order, became the young king, is praised by the world as a lord of the just sceptre who enjoys its pleasure.","n":"Who the young king is cannot be fixed from these lines; the 'just sceptre' praise is the speaker's.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["arasu","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 312","lb":"SO312","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["மந்திரக் கிழவர்",""],"ch":"so:16"},"so:313":{"book":"soolamani","num":313,"t":["என்றா லன்றச் சாதக வோலை யெழுதிற்றால்","குன்றா வென்றிக் குன்றுறழ் யானைக் கொலைவேலோய்","நன்றா நங்கைக் கொன்றிய காமப் பருவத்தால்","நின்றா னன்றே யின்றுணை யாகுந் நிலைமேயான்"],"p":"","r":"The speaker says that a palm-leaf horoscope was written for the maiden, and that, now she has reached the age of desire, the prince who will be her helper stands near.","n":"The leaf is the first sign in the chapter that her match is fixed by a written fate and not by choice; whether the leaf names a man is not stated.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","oozh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 313","lb":"SO313","mm":1,"ev":"A horoscope leaf written for the maiden is recalled, and the one who is to be her partner is said to be at hand.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["மந்திரக் கிழவர்",""],"ch":"so:16"},"so:314":{"book":"soolamani","num":314,"t":["ஆழிக் கோமா னச்சுவ கண்ட னவனுக்கே","ஊழிக் கால மோடின வென்னு முரையாலும்","தாழிக் கோலப் போதன கண்ணா டகுவாளோ","சூழிக் கோலச் சூழ்களி யானைச் சுடர்வேலோய்"],"p":"","r":"The speaker says that, by the saying that the aeon's time has run out for Achuvakandan the lord of the sea-girt earth, the maiden with the flowery eyes will not be found for any other.","n":"ஊழிக் காலம் ஓடின can be read as a foretelling of the king's end; it returns at 333 where the words said of him are matched with old saying. The sense is held open.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["oozh","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 314","lb":"SO314","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["மந்திரக் கிழவர்",""],"ch":"so:16"},"so:315":{"book":"soolamani","num":315,"t":["கண்ணார் கோதைக் காமரு வேய்த்தோட் கனகப்பேர்","மண்ணார் சீர்த்திச் சித்திரை யென்னு மடமாதின்","றெண்ணா ரின்பக் காதலி யாகி யியல்கின்றாள்","பெண்ணார் சாயல் பெற்றன டேவிப் பெறுபட்டம்"],"p":"","r":"The speaker names the king's beloved Sittirai as the maiden of golden fame, who holds the queen's crown with the grace of her gentle beauty.","n":"'Sittirai' is read as a woman's name; the line is compressed.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 315","lb":"SO315","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["மந்திரக் கிழவர்",""],"ch":"so:16"},"so:316":{"book":"soolamani","num":316,"t":["வானோ ருட்கு மக்களோ ரைஞ்ஞூற் றுவர்தம்முள்","ஈனோ ருட்கு மிரத்தின கண்ட னெனநின்றான்","ஏனோ ருட்கு மின்னிள வேந்தா யியல்கின்றான்","ஊனோ ருட்கு மொண்சுடர் நஞ்சூ றொளிவேலோய்"],"p":"","r":"The speaker says that among the five hundred sons of the king, Ratnakandan stands before them and is the young king of all the others, bright as a shining spear.","n":"The last line is tangled; the praise of Ratnakandan is the sure part.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vendhan","perumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 316","lb":"SO316","mm":1,"ev":"Ratnakandan is named the chief among five hundred princes.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["மந்திரக் கிழவர்",""],"ch":"so:16"},"so:317":{"book":"soolamani","num":317,"t":["அன்னா னாயி னாதலி னன்றே யவனன்னால்","என்னா னாவா னென்றனன் வேந்த னெனலோடும்","இன்னா னின்னா னிந்நக ராள்வா னிவனென்றே","அன்னா னன்னாற் கந்நக ரெல்லா மறிவித்தான்"],"p":"","r":"The king declares that this one is who he will be to him and rules this city, and so he makes known to the whole city who the heir is.","n":"","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["arasu","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 317","lb":"SO317","mm":1,"ev":"The king announces the heir to the whole city.","cl":[],"ag":["வேந்தன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:16"},"so:318":{"book":"soolamani","num":318,"t":["மையார் சென்னி மால்வரை யாளும் வயமொய்ம்பிற்","கையா ரெஃகிற் காளைக டம்முட் கமழ்கோதை","மெய்யா மேவு மேதகு வானை மிகவெண்ணிக்","கொய்யா விம்முங் கொங்கலர் தாரோய் கொடுவென்றான்"],"p":"","r":"A speaker addressing the chief of the great hill asks that the lady be given to the one among the youths with sharp spears who is truly worthy of her.","n":"Verses 318-329 are a list of names and lands: the speaker is read as a counsellor, but the Tamil gives no word for his office.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["therithal","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 318","lb":"SO318","mm":1,"ev":"The speaker begins to name the princes and asks that the maiden be given to the worthy one.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["மந்திரக் கிழவர்",""],"ch":"so:16"},"so:319":{"book":"soolamani","num":319,"t":["கேடிலிம் மலையின் மேலாற் கின்னர கீத மாளும்","தோடிலங் குருவத் தொங்கற் சுடர்முடி யரசன் செம்மல்","பாடல்வண் டிமிரும் பைந்தார்ப் பவனஞ்ச னென்ப பாரித்","தாடலம் புரவி வல்ல அரசிளங் குமர னென்றான்"],"p":"","r":"The counsellor names Pavananjan, son of the king on the Kinnara hill and a master of horses, as a young prince for her to consider.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","kalvi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 319","lb":"SO319","mm":1,"ev":"A prince of the hill, Pavananjan, is put forward.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["மந்திரக் கிழவர்",""],"ch":"so:16"},"so:320":{"book":"soolamani","num":320,"t":["அளந்தறி வரிய செய்கை யமிழ்தமா பதியை யாளும்","வளந்தரு வயிரப் பைம்பூண் மன்னவன் சிறுவன் வண்டார்","விளங்கொளி யுருவத் திண்டோள் வேகமா ரதனை யன்றே","இளங்களி யுழுவை யாக விருநிலம் புகழ்வ தென்றான்"],"p":"","r":"The counsellor puts forward the son of the king of Amirthamapati, Vegaman of firm shoulders, whom the whole earth praises as a young lion.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","pugazh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 320","lb":"SO320","mm":1,"ev":"Vegaman is put forward.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["மந்திரக் கிழவர்",""],"ch":"so:16"},"so:321":{"book":"soolamani","num":321,"t":["வேழத்தாற் பொலிந்த சோலை மேகமா புரம தாளும்","ஆழித்தே ரரவத் தானை யரசர்கோன் புதல்வ னந்தார்ப்","பாழித்தோ ளுருவச் செங்கட் பதுமத்தேர்ப் பெயரி னானை","ஊழித்தீ யென்று வேந்த ருட்குவ துருவத் தாரோய்"],"p":"","r":"The counsellor names the son of the king of Megamapuram, Pathumatheran, whom kings fear as the fire of the aeon.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","pugazh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 321","lb":"SO321","mm":1,"ev":"Pathumatheran is put forward.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["மந்திரக் கிழவர்",""],"ch":"so:16"},"so:322":{"book":"soolamani","num":322,"t":["இலங்கொளி மாடவீதி யிரத்தின புரம தாளும்","உலங்கெழு வயிரத் திண்டோ ளொளிமுடி யரசன் செம்மல்","அலங்கலம் புரவித் தானை யருங்கலத் தேரின் பேரன்","குலங்கெழு குரிசில் கண்டாய் கொண்டல்வா னுருமோ டொப்பான்"],"p":"","r":"The counsellor names the grandson of the king of Rathinapuram, a noble youth who is like thunder under cloud.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","pugazh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 322","lb":"SO322","mm":1,"ev":"A grandson of the king of Rathinapuram is put forward.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["மந்திரக் கிழவர்",""],"ch":"so:16"},"so:323":{"book":"soolamani","num":323,"t":["நங்கண்மால் வரையின் மேலோன் நன்னகர் கீத மென்னும்","திங்கண்மால் புரிசை வேலிச் செழுநக ரரசன் செம்மல்","அங்கண்மா ஞால மாளு மரிகண்ட னவனை விண்மேல்","செங்கண்மான் முனியு மேனுஞ் செய்வதொன் றில்லை கண்டாய்"],"p":"","r":"The counsellor says that Arikandan, son of the king of the city Keetham, rules a great earth, and not even the red-eyed Tirumal in anger could do him harm.","n":"செங்கண்மான் is the epic's own epithet for its hero (verse 2, the red-eyed tall one), so the boast sits beside the story's turn.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","perumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 323","lb":"SO323","mm":1,"ev":"Arikandan is put forward and said to be unassailable.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["மந்திரக் கிழவர்",""],"ch":"so:16"},"so:324":{"book":"soolamani","num":324,"t":["சேந்தெரி செம்பொன் வீதித் திரிபுர மதனை யாளும்","வாய்ந்தெரி வயிரப் பைம்பூண் மன்னவன் புதல்வன் மல்லா","டேந்துதோ ளரசர் போரே றிவனளி தாங்க னென்பன்","காய்ந்தெரி கனலின் வெய்யோன் கல்வியாற் கடலோ டொப்பான்"],"p":"","r":"The counsellor names the son of the king of Thiripuram, Alithangan, as a bull among warrior kings, hot as fire and deep in learning as the sea.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalvi","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 324","lb":"SO324","mm":1,"ev":"Alithangan is put forward.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["மந்திரக் கிழவர்",""],"ch":"so:16"},"so:325":{"book":"soolamani","num":325,"t":["செந்தளிர் புதைந்த சோலைச் சித்திர கூட மாளும்","அந்தளி ரலங்கன் மாலை யரசர்கோன் சிறுவ னந்தார்","இந்திரன் புதல்வ னன்னா னேந்தலே மாங்க தற்கிம்","மந்திர வுலகின் வாழு மன்னர்மா றில்லை மன்னா"],"p":"","r":"The counsellor says the son of the king of Chitrakuta is like Indra's son, so that the kings who live in this world of mantra are no match for him.","n":"The name of the prince in line 3 is read from compressed grammar; the Indra comparison is clear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["arasu","perumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 325","lb":"SO325","mm":1,"ev":"Mangathan is put forward.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["மந்திரக் கிழவர்",""],"ch":"so:16"},"so:326":{"book":"soolamani","num":326,"t":["அருமணி யடுத்த வீதி யச்சுவ புரம தாளும்","திருமணி திகழும் பூணான் பெருமகன் சிறைவண் டென்னும்","கருமணி துதைந்த பைந்தார்க் கனகசித் திரனை யன்றே","ஒருமணி திலத மாக வுடையது நிலம தென்றான்"],"p":"","r":"The counsellor names Kanakachitran, son of the king of Achuvapuram, and says that the earth owns him as its one jewel for the forehead.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","pugazh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 326","lb":"SO326","mm":1,"ev":"Kanakachitran is put forward.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["மந்திரக் கிழவர்",""],"ch":"so:16"},"so:327":{"book":"soolamani","num":327,"t":["சீரணி முழவ மோவாச் சிரீநிலை யதனை யாளும்","காரணி தடக்கை வேந்தன் கான்முளை கனபொ னார்ந்த","தேரணி யரவத் தானைச் சித்திரத் தேரின் பேரான்","தாரணி மார்ப னன்றே தரணிக்கோர் திலத மாவான்"],"p":"","r":"The counsellor names the grandson of the king of Sirinilai, Chitrattheran, as one who will be the single mark of beauty on the whole earth.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","pugazh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 327","lb":"SO327","mm":1,"ev":"A prince of Sirinilai is put forward.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["மந்திரக் கிழவர்",""],"ch":"so:16"},"so:328":{"book":"soolamani","num":328,"t":["கற்றவர் புகழுங் சீர்த்திக் கனகபல் லவத்தை யாளும்","கொற்றவன் சிறுவன் கோலக் குங்குமக் குவவுத் தோளான்","செற்றவர்ச் செருக்குஞ் செய்கை செருவல்லான் சிங்க கேது","மற்றவன் பிறந்த பின்னா மண்மகள் மகிழ்ந்த தென்றான்"],"p":"","r":"The counsellor names Singakethu, son of the king of Kanakapallavam, who crushes the pride of his foes, and says the earth rejoiced after his birth.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","pugazh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 328","lb":"SO328","mm":1,"ev":"Singakethu is put forward.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["மந்திரக் கிழவர்",""],"ch":"so:16"},"so:329":{"book":"soolamani","num":329,"t":["இஞ்சிசூழ் ழெரிபொன் மாடத் திந்திரன் மிசைந்த நாமச்","சஞ்சய முடைய கோமான் றாண்முளை தரணி யெல்லாம்","அஞ்சுநீ ரலங்கல் வேலா னருஞ்சய னவனை நங்கண்","மஞ்சுசூழ் மலைக்கோர் சூளா மணியெனக் கருது மன்னா!"],"p":"","r":"The counsellor names Arunjayan, son of Sanjayan, before whom all the earth trembles, and says he is to the hills as the crest-jewel.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","pugazh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 329","lb":"SO329","mm":1,"ev":"Arunjayan, son of Sanjayan, is put forward; the roll of suitors ends.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["மந்திரக் கிழவர்",""],"ch":"so:16"},"so:330":{"book":"soolamani","num":330,"t":["எங்கிவர் தம்முள் யாவ ரிலங்கிரும் பவழச் செவ்வாய்க்","கோங்கிவர் குவிமென் கொங்கைக் கொம்பினுக் குரிய காளை","ஆங்கவன் றன்னை யாராய்ந் தறிந்தருள் செய்க வென்றான்","வாங்கிரும் பரவை முந்நீர் மணிகொழித் தனைய சொல்லான்"],"p":"","r":"A speaker asks that the young man worthy of the maiden be sought out from among these princes, examined and told to him, in words like gems cast up by the sea.","n":"The speaker is not named; he may be the king or the one who has just read the roll.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["therithal","thunai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 330","lb":"SO330","mm":1,"ev":"The speaker asks that the worthy prince be examined and chosen from the list.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["மந்திரக் கிழவர்",""],"ch":"so:16"},"so:331":{"book":"soolamani","num":331,"t":["மன்னர் நீண்முடி மென்மணித் தொத்தொளி","துன்னு சேவடி யாற்குச் சுருங்கவே","பன்னு கேள்விப் பவச்சுதன் சொல்லலும்","அன்னதே யென்றெல் லார்களு மொட்டினார்"],"p":"","r":"Pavachchuthan of wide learning speaks briefly to the one whose feet are served by kings' crowns, and all present agree that it is so.","n":"What Pavachchuthan said is not given in the verse.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["therithal","arivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 331","lb":"SO331","mm":1,"ev":"Pavachchuthan gives the judgment and the whole company agrees to it.","cl":[],"ag":["பவச்சுதன்","இருந்தவர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["பவச்சுதன்",""],"ch":"so:16"},"so:332":{"book":"soolamani","num":332,"t":["அல்லி நாண்மலர்த் தாருமுத் தாரமும்","வல்லி யாங்கனி சாந்தும் வனைந்துராய்","மல்லி னான்மலி மார்பற்கு மற்றிவை","சொல்லி னான்சுத சாகர னென்பவே"],"p":"","r":"Suthasagaran brings a flower garland, a string of pearls, a fruit-bearing creeper and sandal paste, and says something to the wrestler of the broad chest.","n":"The words are not quoted; that these are betrothal gifts is likely but is the reader's inference.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 332","lb":"SO332","mm":1,"ev":"Suthasagaran speaks to the chosen man and gives him garlands, pearls and sandal.","cl":[],"ag":["சுத சாகரன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சுத சாகரன்",""],"ch":"so:16"},"so:333":{"book":"soolamani","num":333,"t":["ஆழி யாள்கின்ற வச்சுவ கண்டன்மேல்","பாழி யாகின்ற திண்டோட் பவச்சுதன்","சூழி யானையி னாய் சொலப் பட்டன","ஊழி யாருரை யும்மொத் துள கண்டாய்"],"p":"","r":"The speaker tells the lord with the crested elephant that the words said about Achuvakandan, who rules the sea-girt earth, and Pavachchuthan, the strong-shouldered, are like the old words of the aeon.","n":"ஊழியார் உரை recalls 314 (ஊழிக் காலம் ஓடின). Whether it means the elders' fate-lore or a prophecy is not fixed.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["oozh","therithal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 333","lb":"SO333","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["மந்திரக் கிழவர்",""],"ch":"so:16"},"so:334":{"book":"soolamani","num":334,"t":["ஆயி னுஞ்சிறி துண்டறி வண்டினம்","பாயி னும்பனிக் கும்படர்க் கோதைதன்","வேயி னும்பணைக் கின்றமென் றோள்பிறர்","தோயி னும்பகை யாஞ்சுடர் வேலினாய்"],"p":"","r":"The speaker says there is a little knowledge in what he reports, but that even the soft arms of the flower-garlanded girl would turn to enmity if others touched them, and so the bright-speared one should take care.","n":"This verse ends the section on a warning that undoes the praise of the list; the line has hard compounds.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","therithal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 334","lb":"SO334","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["மந்திரக் கிழவர்",""],"ch":"so:16"},"so:335":{"book":"soolamani","num":335,"t":["வண்ட வாமுடி மன்னருண் மற்றவன்","தண்ட மாற்றுநர் தாமிலை யாற்சிறி","துண்டி யானுரைப் பானுறு கின்றது","விண்டு வாழுநர் மேனகு வேலினாய்"],"p":"","r":"The speaker begins by saying that, among the crowned kings, one has no one to turn aside his rod, and that he will tell the king a little of what now presses on them.","n":"Who 'he' is in lines 1-2 is not fixed by the verse; the sense is left open.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["arasu","soozhchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 335","lb":"SO335","mm":1,"ev":"The first counsellor opens his speech to the king.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முதல் மந்திரி","விஞ்சையர் வேந்தன்"],"ch":"so:17"},"so:336":{"book":"soolamani","num":336,"t":["போக மாண்டவிச் சேடியோர் பொன்னகர்க்","கேக நாயக னாயினி தாள்பவன்","மேக வாகன னென்றுளன் வீழ்மத","வேக மால்களி றும்மிகு வேலினான்"],"p":"","r":"The speaker names a lord of the golden city of the ridge, Megavahana, who rules it in comfort with his proud rutting elephants and bright spear.","n":"The ridge city is the Vidyadhara realm of the sarukkam before; the verse names the lord and his elephants only.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["arasu","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 336","lb":"SO336","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முதல் மந்திரி","விஞ்சையர் வேந்தன்"],"ch":"so:17"},"so:337":{"book":"soolamani","num":337,"t":["நாக மாலைகண் மேனகு வண்டினம்","ஏக மாலைய வாயிசை கைவிடாத்","தோகை மாமயில் போற்சுரி கூந்தலாள்","மேக மாலினி யென்றுரை மிக்குளாள்"],"p":"","r":"A woman with curling hair like a peacock's tail, hummed over by bees, is named Megamalini.","n":"Her tie to Megavahana is not stated in the verse; a daughter is the likely sense.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","penvazhi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 337","lb":"SO337","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முதல் மந்திரி","விஞ்சையர் வேந்தன்"],"ch":"so:17"},"so:338":{"book":"soolamani","num":338,"t":["தேவி மற்றவ டெய்வம் வழிபட","மேவி வந்தனன் விச்சுவ னென்பவன்","ஓவி றொல்புக ழானுளன் கூற்றமும்","ஏவி நின்றினி தாண்டிடு மீட்டினான்"],"p":"","r":"A man named Vichuvan, of old renown, is said to have come to worship her goddess and to rule happily, even commanding Death.","n":"The verse says he 'came to worship her god' (தெய்வம் வழிபட மேவி வந்தனன்) without saying where; the last line's claim that he commands Death is boast-like praise.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vendhan","pugazh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 338","lb":"SO338","mm":1,"ev":"Vichuvan comes to worship.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முதல் மந்திரி","விஞ்சையர் வேந்தன்"],"ch":"so:17"},"so:339":{"book":"soolamani","num":339,"t":["மையில் வானுல காண்டுமண் ணோர்களுக்","குய்யும் வாயி லாணுர்த்திய தோன்றிய","ஐய னற்பிற வாரஞர் நீங்கியிவ்","வைய மாயதெல் லாம்வளர் கின்றதே"],"p":"","r":"The speaker says that one born to rule the sky-world and be a door of rescue for earthly beings has appeared, and the world grows once its griefs have gone.","n":"The person praised is not named; the lines are hard to construe.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["perumai","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 339","lb":"SO339","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முதல் மந்திரி","விஞ்சையர் வேந்தன்"],"ch":"so:17"},"so:340":{"book":"soolamani","num":340,"t":["மங்குல் வானுல காண்டு வரத்தினால்","இங்கு வந்தென னீணண்டளி யீந்தபின்","திங்கள் வானொளி யிற்றிகழ் சோதியாய்த்","தங்கு வானுல கிற்றகை சான்றதே"],"p":"","r":"The same lord, having ruled the sky-world by a boon, came here, gave long grace, and then became a light bright as the moon and fit for the world above.","n":"","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["arul","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 340","lb":"SO340","mm":1,"ev":"He gives grace and then takes his place above.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முதல் மந்திரி","விஞ்சையர் வேந்தன்"],"ch":"so:17"},"so:341":{"book":"soolamani","num":341,"t":["தன்னி னாய்விளை வித்திரு ளைத்தவிர்த்","தின்ன னாகவென் றெண்ணிய வெண்ணமோ","டன்ன னாதலி னாலவன் மேற்பிறர்","என்ன ரேனுமின் னாதன வெண்ணிலார்"],"p":"","r":"The speaker says that, with the thought of ending others' darkness through himself, he is such a one that no other would think ill of him.","n":"","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["arul","thavam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 341","lb":"SO341","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முதல் மந்திரி","விஞ்சையர் வேந்தன்"],"ch":"so:17"},"so:342":{"book":"soolamani","num":342,"t":["காம்பின் வாய்ந்தமென் றோளியக் காதலன்","தீம்பன் மாலைநன் மார்பகஞ் சேருமேல்","ஆம்பன் மாலையு மாய்கதிர்த் திங்களும்","தாம்பன் மாலையுஞ் சார்ந்த தனைத்தரோ"],"p":"","r":"If the girl's lover comes to the chest of the garland-wearer, then evening, moon and daylight all seem to meet in one.","n":"The lines are in the style of lovers' praise; the exact comparison is hard to fix.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 342","lb":"SO342","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முதல் மந்திரி","விஞ்சையர் வேந்தன்"],"ch":"so:17"},"so:343":{"book":"soolamani","num":343,"t":["நம்பி தங்கை நகைமலர்க் கற்பகக்","கொம்பி னன்னவன் கொங்கணி கூந்தலாள்","அம்பி னீண்டரி சிந்திய மாக்கயல்","வம்பி னீண்டமை வாணெடுங் கன்ணினாள்"],"p":"","r":"The sister of the lord is praised as a wishing-tree branch with fragrant hair and carp-long eyes.","n":"","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","penvazhi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 343","lb":"SO343","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முதல் மந்திரி","விஞ்சையர் வேந்தன்"],"ch":"so:17"},"so:344":{"book":"soolamani","num":344,"t":["கோதின் மாலைகள் மேற்குதி கொண்டெழு","கீத மாலைய கின்னர வண்டினம்","ஊதி மாலைய வாயுறை யுங்குழல்","சோதி மாலையென் பாள்சுடர்ப் பூணினாள்"],"p":"","r":"A woman wearing bright jewels, whose hair is hummed over by kinnara bees, is named Jothimalai.","n":"","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","penvazhi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 344","lb":"SO344","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முதல் மந்திரி","விஞ்சையர் வேந்தன்"],"ch":"so:17"},"so:345":{"book":"soolamani","num":345,"t":["வெம்பு மால்களி யானை விலக்குநீர்","நம்பி ஞாயிறு சேர்பெய ராற்கணி","அம்பி னீளரி வாணெடுங் கண்ணவள்","வம்பு சேர்முலை வாரி வளாகமே"],"p":"","r":"A lord whose name joins the sun's is set beside a long-eyed woman whose breast is likened to the whole earth.","n":"Lines 2-4 are hard to construe; the lord's name is not spelled out.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","penvazhi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 345","lb":"SO345","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முதல் மந்திரி","விஞ்சையர் வேந்தன்"],"ch":"so:17"},"so:346":{"book":"soolamani","num":346,"t":["இன்ன வாறிசை யப்பெறின் யாவரும்","என்ன வாறு மிகப்பவ ரின்மையால்","அன்ன வாறரு ளுண்டெனி லாய்ந்தியான்","சொன்ன வாறுகொண் டீசுடர் வேலினோய்"],"p":"","r":"The speaker ends by asking the king, if grace is in him, to weigh what he has said and act on it.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["soozhchi","arul"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 346","lb":"SO346","mm":1,"ev":"The first counsellor closes his advice and asks the king to act.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முதல் மந்திரி","விஞ்சையர் வேந்தன்"],"ch":"so:17"},"so:347":{"book":"soolamani","num":347,"t":["கொங்குவண் டலைந்த தாரான் குறிப்பறிந் திவைக ளெல்லாம்","அங்கவன் மொழிந்த பின்னை யவனையு மமைதி கூறி","நங்கைதன் றாதை தோழர் நால்வரு ணால்வ னாவான்","தொங்கலந் துணர்கொள் மார்பிற் சுமந்திரி சொல்ல லுற்றான்"],"p":"","r":"The poet says that the garland-wearer, having heard all this and understood the intent, spoke calming words to the first speaker, and Sumantiri, the fourth of the girl's father's four companions, began to speak.","n":"Whose heart 'the garland-wearer' is understood to read is not quite clear from the lines.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["amaichu","soozhchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 347","lb":"SO347","mm":1,"ev":"The first counsellor finishes, and Sumantiri, the fourth counsellor, takes up the counsel.","cl":[],"ag":["முதல் மந்திரி","சுமந்திரி"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:17"},"so:348":{"book":"soolamani","num":348,"t":["அண்ணலங் களிகொள் யானை யச்சுவ கண்டன் மூத்தாற்","கெண்ணலுந் தகுவ தன்றா லிவன்பணி யகற்ற லாற்றாக்","கண்ணலங் கவரும் வேலோர்க் கீயினுங் கரும மன்றால்","பெண்ணலங் கனிந்த பேதை யிருப்பதும் பெருமை யன்றே"],"p":"","r":"Sumantiri says it is not right to weigh the claim of the elder Achuvakandan, whose order none can turn aside, nor to give the maiden to spear-bearers, and that she stay unwed is no honour.","n":"The chain of reasons in lines 1-4 is hard to follow; whether he refuses or urges Achuvakandan's claim is uncertain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["amaichu","magatpaal","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 348","lb":"SO348","mm":1,"ev":"Sumantiri begins by naming Achuvakandan and the maiden's unwed state.","cl":[],"ag":["சுமந்திரி"],"ec":[],"spk":["சுமந்திரி","விஞ்சையர் வேந்தன்"],"ch":"so:17"},"so:349":{"book":"soolamani","num":349,"t":["சூழ்கதிர்ப் புரிசை வேலிச் சுரேந்திர மாளும்","தாழ்கதி ரார மார்பிற் றமனியக் குழையி னான்றன்","போழ்கதிர்க் கடவுள் போலும் புதல்வனுக் குரிமை செய்ய","தாழ்கதிர் விலங்க லாளு மரசவஃ தரிது கண்டாய்"],"p":"","r":"Sumantiri says that for the son, bright as the sun-god, of the golden-eared ruler of the walled city of Surendra, ruling the hill-realm by right would be a hard thing.","n":"The ruler and the son are unnamed; it is unclear which side the verse favours.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["arasu","soozhchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 349","lb":"SO349","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["சுமந்திரி","விஞ்சையர் வேந்தன்"],"ch":"so:17"},"so:350":{"book":"soolamani","num":350,"t":["மங்கையர் முகத்தி னீண்டு மைகடை மதர்ப்ப மாந்தி","அங்கயல் பிறழ்வ போலு மையரி யடர்த்த வாட்கண்","பங்கயச் செங்க ணான்மேற் படைத்தொழில் பயின்ற போழ்தும்","தங்கிய மனத்த னாகித் தளர்விலன் றவத்தின் மிக்கான்"],"p":"","r":"Though the sword-like glances of women, dark-lined and flickering like carp, strike at him as weapons, the young man keeps a steady mind and stands high in austerity.","n":"The youth is not named until verse 353; verses 350-353 are read as describing Vijayapathiran. The weapons are the women's eyes, read as a figure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thavam","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 350","lb":"SO350","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["a youth firm in austerity is not shaken by women's glances","general","asserted",4,"sumanthiri"]],"ag":["விசயபத்திரன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சுமந்திரி","விஞ்சையர் வேந்தன்"],"ch":"so:17"},"so:351":{"book":"soolamani","num":351,"t":["மண்கனி முழவச் சீரு மடந்தையர் தூக்கு மற்றும்","பண்கனி பாட லாடற் பாணியும் பயின்று மேவான்","விண்கனிந் தனைய வின்ப வெள்ளமும் வெறுத்து நின்றான்","கண்கனி யுருவக் காளை கடவுளர் தகையன் கண்டாய்"],"p":"","r":"The youth does not take to the beat of drums, the songs and dances of women, and turns from heavenly pleasures, a handsome man like one of the gods.","n":"The Jain frame is inferred from the later naming of the sage Asodharan; the verse itself only describes the youth's distaste.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thuravu","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 351","lb":"SO351","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["turning away from sensual pleasure marks one fit for release","jain","reported",3,"sumanthiri"]],"ag":["விசயபத்திரன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சுமந்திரி","விஞ்சையர் வேந்தன்"],"ch":"so:17"},"so:352":{"book":"soolamani","num":352,"t":["செறிகழ லவற்குத் தாதை சித்திர கூட மென்னும்","அறிவரன் கோயி லெய்தி யணிவிழ வயர்த்த காலை","இறுதியி லவதி ஞானி யசோதர னென்னும் பேர","உறுவனை வணங்கிக் கேட்டான் மகன்றிற முலங்கொ டோ ளான்"],"p":"","r":"His father, in a festival at the shrine of the sage of Chitrakuta, bows to the avadhi-knower named Yasodhara and asks about his son's future.","n":"The father is named Jayasenan only in verse 353.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thavam","kaalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 352","lb":"SO352","mm":1,"ev":"The father goes to Asodharan at Chitrakuta and asks about his son.","cl":[],"ag":["சயசேனன்","யசோதரன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சுமந்திரி","விஞ்சையர் வேந்தன்"],"ch":"so:17"},"so:353":{"book":"soolamani","num":353,"t":["பங்கயப் பழன வேலிப் பவகிரி யரசன் பைந்தார்","தங்கிய தடங்கொண் மார்பன் சயசேன னவற்குத் தேவி","செங்கய னெடுங்கட் செவ்வாய்ப் பிரீதிமதி பயந்த காளை","வெங்களி யானை வல்ல விசயபத் திரனென் பானே"],"p":"","r":"Sumantiri names the youth: Vijayapathiran, son of Jayasenan, king of Bhavagiri, and his queen Pritimati.","n":"","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kudi","makkal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 353","lb":"SO353","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["சுமந்திரி","விஞ்சையர் வேந்தன்"],"ch":"so:17"},"so:354":{"book":"soolamani","num":354,"t":["மந்திரத் தரசர் கோவே மற்றவன் வையங் காக்கும்","தந்திரந் துறந்து நோற்று மறைந்தசா சார மென்னும்","இந்திர வுலக மெய்தி யேழொடீ ரைந்து முன்னீர்","அந்தர காலந் தேவர்க் கரசனா யாண்டு வந்தான்"],"p":"","r":"The youth will give up the rule that protects the world, practise austerity, reach Indra's world Sasara, and rule there as lord of the gods for seventeen oceans of time.","n":"The lines are the sage's prophecy as Sumantiri reports it; the later 'we heard' (355) shows it is reported speech.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thavam","thuravu","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 354","lb":"SO354","mm":1,"ev":"The sage foretells the youth's rebirth as a god.","cl":[["a king who gives up rule for austerity gains the lordship of the gods","jain","asserted",2,"sumanthiri"]],"ag":["விசயபத்திரன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சுமந்திரி","விஞ்சையர் வேந்தன்"],"ch":"so:17"},"so:355":{"book":"soolamani","num":355,"t":["ஆதலா லமர போக நுகர்ந்தவ னரைசர் செல்வம்","போதுலா மலங்கன் மார்ப பொருளென மருளல் செல்லான்","தீதெலா மகல நோற்றுச் சிவகதி சேரு மென்றக்","கோதிலா முனிவன் சொன்ன வுரையிவை கூறக் கேட்டாம்"],"p":"","r":"So, Sumantiri says, having enjoyed divine pleasures he will not be dazzled into taking kings' wealth for substance, and will reach Sivagati by austerity that ends all evil: this was the sage's word.","n":"Sivagati here is the Jain word for liberation; it need not mean Siva's grace.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["veedu","thavam","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 355","lb":"SO355","mm":1,"ev":"Sumantiri closes his report of the sage's words.","cl":[["austerity ends all evil and brings one to liberation","jain","asserted",3,"sumanthiri"],["kings' wealth is not a real thing to rely on","general","asserted",2,"sumanthiri"]],"ag":["விசயபத்திரன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சுமந்திரி","விஞ்சையர் வேந்தன்"],"ch":"so:17"},"so:356":{"book":"soolamani","num":356,"t":["அம்மையாற் றவங்க டாங்கி யலர்ந்தநல் லறிவி னாலும்","இம்மையா னுடம்பு நீங்கி யிகந்துபோ மியற்கை யாலும்","செம்மையாற் கடவுட் டானஞ் சேர்வதே சிந்தை யாற்கு","மெய்ம்மையாற் கருமச் சுற்றம் வேண்டுவ தில்லை வேந்தே"],"p":"","r":"Sumantiri says that through austerity, wide knowledge and the body's nature of leaving in this life, the thoughtful reach the place of the god, and truly have no need of kin of karma.","n":"கருமச் சுற்றம் is read as kin bound by deed or duty; the sense turns on that phrase.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thavam","nilaiyaamai","sutram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 356","lb":"SO356","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["austerity and clear knowledge, with the body's passing, lead the mind to the divine seat","jain","asserted",3,"sumanthiri"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["சுமந்திரி","விஞ்சையர் வேந்தன்"],"ch":"so:17"},"so:357":{"book":"soolamani","num":357,"t":["வாரணி முரச மார்ப்ப வயிரொடு வளைக ளேங்கத்","தாரவர் குழாங்க ளீண்டச் சயமர மறைது மேனும்","ஆரவி ராழி யனை யஞ்சுது மறிய லாகா","காரவி தடக்கை வேந்தே கழலவர் கரும மென்றான்"],"p":"","r":"Sumantiri says that even if a swayamvara were proclaimed with drums and conches and crowds, they cannot tell whether they dare face Achuvakandan, king, for that is a matter for the brave.","n":"Lines 2-3 are hard; the swayamvara is read as a conditional.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["amaichu","soozhchi","maanam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 357","lb":"SO357","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["சுமந்திரி","விஞ்சையர் வேந்தன்"],"ch":"so:17"},"so:358":{"book":"soolamani","num":358,"t":["ஒன்றுநாங் கருதிச் சூழி னூழது விளைவு தானே","கன்றிநாங் கருதிற் றின்றி மற்றோர்வா றாக நண்ணும்","என்றுநாந் துணிந்த செய்கை யிதன்றிறத் தென்ன மாட்டாய்","இன்றுநாந் துணிது மாயி னினிச்சிறி துரைப்ப னென்றான்"],"p":"","r":"Sumantiri says that when they plan one thing, fate decides the result, and a plan pressed hard may end otherwise, so none can say the course is certain; if they decide today he will say a little more.","n":"The fate claim is used as a counsellor's caution, not as a reason to give up counsel; he still offers to speak further.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oozh","soozhchi","vinai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 358","lb":"SO358","mm":1,"ev":"Sumantiri warns that fate may overturn their plans and offers more counsel.","cl":[["fate decides the outcome of human planning, and plans may end otherwise than intended","general","reported",1,"sumanthiri"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["சுமந்திரி","விஞ்சையர் வேந்தன்"],"ch":"so:17"},"so:359":{"book":"soolamani","num":359,"t":["வீழ்புரி விளங்கு நூலோய் மேலுநங் குலத்து ளார்கட்","கூழ்புரிந் துறுதி கூறு முயர்குல மலர நின்றான்","தாழ்புரி தயங்கு நுண்ணூற் சதவிந்து மொழிந்த வாற்றால்","யாழ்புரி மழலை யாள் கண் ணாவதை யறிது மென்றான்"],"p":"","r":"A counsellor says that, by what Satavindu said, the council will learn what is to befall the girl of the yazh-soft voice, while the king stands for the good of the high clan.","n":"The speaker and the one addressed as wearer of the shining thread are not named in the verse; Satavindu is named but his role is left to the sarukkam's earlier verses.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["amaichu","soozhchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 359","lb":"SO359","mm":1,"ev":"The council opens on the question of the king's daughter.","cl":[],"ag":["மந்திரக் கிழவர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["மந்திரக் கிழவர்",""],"ch":"so:18"},"so:360":{"book":"soolamani","num":360,"t":["என்றவன் மொழிந்த போழ்தி னேனையா ரினிதி னோக்கி","மின்றவழ் விளங்கு வேலோய் மெய்யினு மேவல் வேண்டும்","சென்றவன் மனையு ணீயே வினவெனச் சேனை வேந்தன்","நன்றவர் மொழிந்த வெல்லா நல்லவா நயந்து கேட்டான்"],"p":"","r":"The others look on kindly and tell the king the matter must be true and he should go to the man's house and ask himself, and the king listens gladly to all they say.","n":"","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["amaichu","soozhchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 360","lb":"SO360","mm":1,"ev":"The counsellors urge the king to go and ask in person, and he agrees.","cl":[],"ag":["மந்திரக் கிழவர்","விஞ்சையர் வேந்தன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["மந்திரக் கிழவர்","விஞ்சையர் வேந்தன்"],"ch":"so:18"},"so:361":{"book":"soolamani","num":361,"t":["இந்திர னனைய நீரோ யினிப்பிறி தெண்ணல் வேண்டா","மந்திர நீளு மாயின் வருவன வறிய லாகா","சந்திரன் றவழ நீண்ட தமனியச் சூல நெற்றி","அந்தரந் திவளு ஞாயிற் கோயில்புக் கருளு கென்றார்"],"p":"","r":"The counsellors tell the Indra-like king to think of nothing else, since delay in counsel hides what is coming, and ask him to enter the palace that touches the sun.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["amaichu","kaalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 361","lb":"SO361","mm":1,"ev":"The counsellors press the king to enter the palace at once.","cl":[],"ag":["மந்திரக் கிழவர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["மந்திரக் கிழவர்","விஞ்சையர் வேந்தன்"],"ch":"so:18"},"so:362":{"book":"soolamani","num":362,"t":["மந்திரக் கிழவர் தம்மை மனைபுக விடுத்து மன்னன்","சுந்தரச் சுரும்புந் தேனுஞ் சூழ்கழ னிரையு மார்ப்ப","வந்தர மகளிர் போல்வார் வரன்முறை கவரி வீச","அந்தரக் கடைக ணீங்கி யகனக ரருளிப் புக்கான்"],"p":"","r":"The king sends his counsellors to their homes and walks into the great city's palace past the outer gates, fanned by women like celestials while bees and anklets sound.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vendhan","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 362","lb":"SO362","mm":1,"ev":"The king dismisses the counsellors and enters the palace.","cl":[],"ag":["விஞ்சையர் வேந்தன்","மந்திரக் கிழவர்","அந்தர மகளிர் போல்வார்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:18"},"so:363":{"book":"soolamani","num":363,"t":["மிகுகதிர் விலங்கலார் வேந்தன் றேனுடைந்","துகுகதிர் மண்டப மொளிர வேறலும்","தொகுகதிர் சுடுவன பரப்பிச் சூழொளி","நகுகதிர் மாண்டில நடுவ ணின்றதே"],"p":"","r":"As the king of the hill-dwellers climbs into the hall, the honey-broken, sun-bright rays shine and a ring of light stands in the hall's middle.","n":"Lines 3-4 are compressed; the ring of light is read as a sun-bright disc on the floor.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vendhan","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 363","lb":"SO363","mm":1,"ev":"The king mounts the radiant hall.","cl":[],"ag":["விஞ்சையர் வேந்தன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:18"},"so:364":{"book":"soolamani","num":364,"t":["கண்டிரள் கழைவளர் கரும்பு கைமிகுத்","தொண்டிரள் வெள்ளிலை யுரிஞ்சு மோடைமா","வெண்டிரண் மணிபுடை சிலம்ப விட்டன","வண்டிரள் கிளையொடு வளைக ளார்த்தவே"],"p":"","r":"Elephants with face-plates, fed cane and betel, ring their bells at their sides while swarms of bees and bangles sound.","n":"The grammar of lines 1-2 is dense; the elephants' feeding is the likely sense.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["selvam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 364","lb":"SO364","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:18"},"so:365":{"book":"soolamani","num":365,"t":["ஒலிவிழா வண்டின மூத வூறுதேன்","மலிவிழாப் பிணையலு மணங்கொள் சாந்தமும்","பலிவிழாப் பதாகையும் பரந்து பாடுவார்","கலிவிழாக் கழுமின கடவுட் டானமே"],"p":"","r":"The hall of divine offerings fills with festival: humming bees, honey-dripping garlands, scented paste, offering-banners and singers.","n":"கடவுட் டானம் is read as the place where offerings are made; the compound is not fixed.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 365","lb":"SO365","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:18"},"so:366":{"book":"soolamani","num":366,"t":["குண்டுநீர்க் குழுமலர்க் குவளைப் பட்டமும்","மண்டுநீர் மரகத மணிக்கல் வாவியும்","கொண்டுநீ ரிளையவர் குடையக் கொங்கொடு","வண்டுநீர்த் திவலையின் மயங்கி வீழ்ந்தவே"],"p":"","r":"Young bathers dip in pools of blue lilies and emerald-stepped ponds, and bees fall mingled with the drops.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inbam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 366","lb":"SO366","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["இளையவர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:18"},"so:367":{"book":"soolamani","num":367,"t":["பங்கயத் துகள்படு பழன நீர்த்திரை","மங்கையர் முலையொடு பொருத வாவிகள்","அங்கவ ரரிசன மழித்த சேற்றினும்","குங்குமக் குழம்பினுங் குழம்பு கொண்டவே"],"p":"","r":"Waves of the lotus-pollened ponds strike the women's breasts, and the pools grow thick with washed face-paste and saffron paste.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","azhagu","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 367","lb":"SO367","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["மங்கையர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:18"},"so:368":{"book":"soolamani","num":368,"t":["அங்கவள்வாய்க் கயம்வல ராம்ப றூம்புடைப்","பொங்குகா டேர்பட ஞெறித்துப் பூவொடு","கொங்கைவாய்க் குழலவர் குளிப்ப விட்டன","திங்கள் வாண் முகவொளி திளைப்ப விண்டவே"],"p":"","r":"As women with long hair bathe, they crush the lotus and ambal beds and the bright light of their moon-faces mingles with the water.","n":"Lines 1-2 are hard to construe.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 368","lb":"SO368","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["மங்கையர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:18"},"so:369":{"book":"soolamani","num":369,"t":["மாயிரும் பனித்தடம் படிந்து மையழி","சேயரி நெடுமலர்க் கண்கள் சேந்தெனத்","தாயரை மறைக்கிய குவளைத் தாதுதேன்","பாயமோந் திறைஞ்சினார் பாவை மார்களே"],"p":"","r":"Women whose kohl has washed off in the great cool pool have eyes as red as long flowers and bow before the lily pollen and honey that spray over them.","n":"The sense of line 3 (hiding from nurses, or the flowers hiding the girls) is uncertain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 369","lb":"SO369","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["மங்கையர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:18"},"so:370":{"book":"soolamani","num":370,"t":["சந்தனத் துளித்தலை ததும்பச் சாந்தளைந்","தந்தரத் தசைப்பன வால வட்டமு","மெந்திரத் திவலையு மியற்றி யீர்மணல்","பந்தருட் பாலிகைக் குவளை பாய்த்தினார்"],"p":"","r":"On wet sand under a pavilion the women hang fans and set up spray from machines, sprinkle sandal drops and paste and scatter blue lilies on the seed-pots.","n":"The device in line 3 is read as a spray-machine; the sprinkling and scattering are the likelier sense.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["inbam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 370","lb":"SO370","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["மங்கையர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:18"},"so:371":{"book":"soolamani","num":371,"t":["குருமணித் தாமரைக் கொட்டை சூடிய","திருமணிப் பீடமுஞ் செதுக்க மாயவும்","பருமணிப் பளிங்கென விளங்கு வான்பலி","அருமணிக் கொம்பனா ரலர வூட்டினார்"],"p":"","r":"Women like jewelled twigs lay out a flower offering on a gem seat carved to look like a lotus-pod, bright as crystal.","n":"The rare compounds in lines 1-3 leave the exact offering uncertain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 371","lb":"SO371","mm":1,"ev":"Women set out a flower offering on a jewelled seat.","cl":[],"ag":["மங்கையர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:18"},"so:372":{"book":"soolamani","num":372,"t":["அன்னரும் பொழுதுகண் ணகற்ற வாயிடைப்","பன்னருங் காலநூல் பயின்ற பண்புடைக்","கன்னலங் கருவியோர் கழிந்த நாழிகை","மன்னவ னடிமுத லுணர்த்தி வாழ்த்தினார்"],"p":"","r":"Skilled time-keepers who know the books of the hours tell the king, at his feet, how many ghatikas have passed and bless him.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaalam","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 372","lb":"SO372","mm":1,"ev":"The time-keepers report the passing hours to the king and bless him.","cl":[],"ag":["கன்னலங் கருவியோர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:18"},"so:373":{"book":"soolamani","num":373,"t":["வாரணி முலையவர் பரவ மன்னவன்","ஈரணிப் பள்ளிபுக் கருளி னானிரந்","தோரணி யின்னிய மிசைத்த வின்பமோ","டாரணி தெரியலா னமிர்த மேயினான்"],"p":"","r":"With women praising him the king enters his twin-adorned chamber, where he enjoys the nectar to the sound of fine music.","n":"அமிர்தம் can be a drink or simply sweetness; the verse does not say which.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inbam","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 373","lb":"SO373","mm":1,"ev":"The king retires to his chamber for music and pleasure.","cl":[],"ag":["விஞ்சையர் வேந்தன்","அந்தர மகளிர் போல்வார்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:18"},"so:374":{"book":"soolamani","num":374,"t":["வெள்ளிழை பொலிந்தொளி துளும்பு மேனியன்","வள்ளிதழ் மல்லிகை மலர்ந்த மாலையான்","அள்ளிதழ்ப் புதுமல ரடுத்த வீதிமேல்","கள்ளிதழ்க் கண்ணியான் காலி னேகினான்"],"p":"","r":"Bright in his white-threaded body and a jasmine garland, the king walks on foot along a street strewn with fresh flowers.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vendhan","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 374","lb":"SO374","mm":1,"ev":"The king walks along the flowered street.","cl":[],"ag":["விஞ்சையர் வேந்தன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:18"},"so:375":{"book":"soolamani","num":375,"t":["பொன்னலர் மணிக்கழல் புலம்பத் தேனினம்","துள்ளலர் தொடையலிற் சுரும்போ டார்த்தெழ","மன்னவன் னடத்தொறு மகர குண்டலம்","மின்மலர்த் திலங்குவில் விலங்க விட்டவே"],"p":"","r":"His golden anklets ring, bees and honey-drops follow his garland, and at every step his shark-shaped earrings throw lightning-like gleams.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vendhan","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 375","lb":"SO375","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["விஞ்சையர் வேந்தன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:18"},"so:376":{"book":"soolamani","num":376,"t":["நெய்யிலங் கெஃகினர் நிறைந்த விஞ்சையர்","கையிலங் கீட்டியர் கழித்த வாளினர்","மெய்யிலங் குறையினர் விசித்த கச்சையர்","வையகங் காவலன் மருங்கு சுற்றினார்"],"p":"","r":"Vidyadhara guards with oiled spears, lances, drawn swords and girded belts close round the king.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vendhan","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 376","lb":"SO376","mm":1,"ev":"The Vidyadhara guards encircle the king.","cl":[],"ag":["விஞ்சையர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:18"},"so:377":{"book":"soolamani","num":377,"t":["சுரும்புசூழ் பிணையலுஞ் சுண்ண மாரியும்","கரும்புசூழ் கிளவியர் சொரிந்து கைதொழ","நிரம்புநூ னிமித்திகன் மாட நீள்கடை","அரும்புசூழ் தெரியலா னருளி னெய்தினான்"],"p":"","r":"Women pour garlands and powder over him with folded hands as the king reaches, in his grace, the high gate of the astrologer's mansion.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vendhan","kurippu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 377","lb":"SO377","mm":1,"ev":"The king reaches the astrologer's mansion.","cl":[],"ag":["விஞ்சையர் வேந்தன்","அந்தர மகளிர் போல்வார்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:18"},"so:378":{"book":"soolamani","num":378,"t":["எங்குலம் விளங்கவிக் கருளி வந்தவெங்","கொங்கலர் தெரியலாய் கொற்றங் கொள்கென","மங்கல வுழைக்கலம் பரப்ப மன்னனுக்","கங்கலர் கேள்வியா னாசி கூறினான்"],"p":"","r":"The learned man blesses the king as the clan's glory who has come, bidding him take victory, while the auspicious articles are spread.","n":"","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vendhan","kalvi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 378","lb":"SO378","mm":1,"ev":"The astrologer greets the king with a blessing.","cl":[],"ag":["நிமித்திகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["நிமித்திகன்","விஞ்சையர் வேந்தன்"],"ch":"so:18"},"so:379":{"book":"soolamani","num":379,"t":["கொண்டமர்ந் தகிற்புகை கழுமிக் கோதைவாய்","விண்டமர்ந் தொழுகுவ மதுக்கள் வீழ்ந்துராய்","வண்டமர்ந் தொலிசெய மருங்குல் கொண்டதோர்","மண்டப மணித்தல மன்ன னெய்தினான்"],"p":"","r":"The king enters a gem-floored hall thick with aloe smoke and fallen honey as bees hum.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vendhan","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 379","lb":"SO379","mm":1,"ev":"The king enters the hall.","cl":[],"ag":["விஞ்சையர் வேந்தன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:18"},"so:380":{"book":"soolamani","num":380,"t":["தழையவிழ் சந்தனப் பொதும்பு போன்மது","மழைதவழ் மண்டப மலிர வீற்றிருந்","துழையவர் குறிப்பறிந் தகல வொண்சுடர்க்","குழையவன் குமரிதன் கரும மென்னினான்"],"p":"","r":"Seated in the hall, as the attendants read his sign and leave, the bright-eared one says the matter is the girl's affair.","n":"Which of the two is meant by 'the bright-eared one' turns on the sarukkam's context, not on the line.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["soozhchi","kurippu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 380","lb":"SO380","mm":1,"ev":"The attendants withdraw and the king states his errand.","cl":[],"ag":["விஞ்சையர் வேந்தன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["விஞ்சையர் வேந்தன்","நிமித்திகன்"],"ch":"so:18"},"so:381":{"book":"soolamani","num":381,"t":["கனைத்தெதிர் கதிர்மணிக் கடகஞ் சூடிய","பனைத்திர ளனையதோட் படலை மாலையான்","மனத்தினை மறுவினூல் வாயி னாற்சொல","நினைத்திவை விளம்பினா னிமித்த நீதியான்"],"p":"","r":"Bright with bracelet and garland, the astrologer thinks, then begins to speak from his flawless book what he foresees.","n":"","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["oozh","kalvi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 381","lb":"SO381","mm":1,"ev":"The astrologer prepares to read the omen.","cl":[],"ag":["நிமித்திகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:18"},"so:382":{"book":"soolamani","num":382,"t":["மணங்கமழ் மதுமல ரலங்கன் மாலைபோல்","வணங்கெழி னுடங்கிடை மாழை நோக்கிநங்","கணங்குழை கருமமாங் கருதிற் றென்றனன்","அணங்கெழில் விரிந்தநூ லலர்ந்த நாவினான்"],"p":"","r":"Looking at the gold-bright, bending-waisted girl, the astrologer says that the jewelled daughter's matter has come to be weighed.","n":"The verse gives only the opening of his words; the content of the reading is not yet stated.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["oozh","soozhchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 382","lb":"SO382","mm":1,"ev":"The astrologer takes up the king's question.","cl":[],"ag":["நிமித்திகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["நிமித்திகன்","விஞ்சையர் வேந்தன்"],"ch":"so:18"},"so:383":{"book":"soolamani","num":383,"t":["வெண்ணிலா விரிந்தென விளங்கு மாலையள்","கண்ணிலாங் கவர்தகைக் கண்ணி மன்னனை","மண்ணிலா மறுகிடை வலங்கொண் டெய்தினாள்","எண்ணிலாங் கதுதிரு வெதிர்ந்த வன்ணமே"],"p":"","r":"A woman shining like a garland spread out as moonlight, her eyes unlike any other, walks round the king in the street and reaches him, as fortune was meant to meet him.","n":"Lines 3-4 are compressed; the verse does not name the woman, and she is taken here to be the Vidyadhara king's daughter of the opening verses.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kurippu","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 383","lb":"SO383","mm":1,"ev":"The woman circles the king in the street and comes before him.","cl":[],"ag":["விஞ்சையர் மகள்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:19"},"so:384":{"book":"soolamani","num":384,"t":["பொன்சுலாஞ் சுடரிழை பொறுத்த பூண்முலை","மின்சுலா நுடங்கிடை மெல்லி யாடிறம்","என்சொலா லின்றியா னியம்பு நீரதோ","மன்சுலா வகலநின் றலரும் வாளினாய்"],"p":"","r":"A speaker says he cannot tell in words the beauty of the slender woman with golden jewels, and addresses a king whose sword gleams widely.","n":"The speaker is not named in the verse; the teacher of verse 386 is taken as the voice, and the one addressed as a king by the sword epithet.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 384","lb":"SO384","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["ஆரியன்","வேந்தன்"],"ch":"so:19"},"so:385":{"book":"soolamani","num":385,"t":["ஆதிநா ளறக்கதி ராழி தாங்கிய","சோதியான் றிருமொழி விளக்கித் தோன்றுமால்","போதுவார் புரிகுழற் பொலங்கொம் பன்னவிம்","மாதராள் வனமுலைக் குரிய மைந்தனே"],"p":"","r":"The speaker says that what shines forth will make clear the holy word of the light who once bore the wheel of dharma, and calls the youth the rightful lord of the woman's breasts.","n":"Who is called மைந்தன் (youth or hero) is not fixed; he may be the king's son whom the woman is meant for.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","aram","aazhi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 385","lb":"SO385","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["the first lord's word, borne with the wheel of dharma, makes clear what is to come","jain","asserted",1,"aariyan"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["ஆரியன்","வேந்தன்"],"ch":"so:19"},"so:386":{"book":"soolamani","num":386,"t":["அம்மயி லனையவ டிறத்தி னாரியன்","செம்மையில் விளம்பிய செல்வங் கேட்டலும்","மெய்ம்மையிற் றெரிந்தொளி துளும்பு மேனியன்","பொய்ம்மையில் புகழவன் பொலிந்து தோன்றினான்"],"p":"","r":"Hearing the noble teacher's account of the woman's fortune, the king, whose fame has no falsehood in it, shone with the radiance of one who had seen the truth.","n":"","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pugazh","arul"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 386","lb":"SO386","mm":1,"ev":"The king hears the teacher's account of the woman's fortune and is lit with joy.","cl":[],"ag":["ஆரியன்","வேந்தன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:19"},"so:387":{"book":"soolamani","num":387,"t":["முன்னிய வுலகுகண் விடுத்த மூர்த்தியான்","மன்னிய திருமொழி யகத்து மாதராள்","என்னைகொல் விரிந்தவா றெனலு மன்னனுக்","கன்னவ னாதிமா புராண மோதினான்"],"p":"","r":"When the king asks how the woman's lot spreads in the holy word of the one who sent his eyes over the whole world, the teacher recites the ancient great purana to him.","n":"","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["puraanam","iraivan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 387","lb":"SO387","mm":1,"ev":"The king asks how the woman's fortune lies in the lord's word, and the teacher answers with the old purana.","cl":[],"ag":["வேந்தன்","ஆரியன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:19"},"so:388":{"book":"soolamani","num":388,"t":["மூவகை யுலகினு ணடுவண் மூரிநீர்த்","தீவின தகலமுஞ் சிந்து வட்டமும்","ஓவல வொன்றுக்கொன் றிரட்டி கண்ணறை","ஏவலாய் விரிந்தவை யெண்ணி றந்தவே"],"p":"","r":"The purana begins with the shape of the middle world: a continent in the great sea and a ring of ocean around it, each double the last, in numbers past counting.","n":"The verse gives measures only as doubling; no figure is stated, so the structure is all it teaches.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaalam","aram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 388","lb":"SO388","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["the middle world is a continent ringed by oceans and further rings, each twice the width of the one before","jain","asserted",1,"aariyan"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["ஆரியன்","வேந்தன்"],"ch":"so:19"},"so:389":{"book":"soolamani","num":389,"t":["மந்தர நெடுமலை நடுவின் வாய்ந்தது","சுந்தர வேதிகை மருங்கு சூழ்ந்தது","நந்திய நளிசினை நாவன் மாமரம்","அந்தரத் துடையதிவ் வவனி வட்டமே"],"p":"","r":"At the centre stands the long mountain Mandara, ringed by a fair terrace; the great jambu tree stands in the midst of the earth-disc, which hangs in the air.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kurippu","andam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 389","lb":"SO389","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["the earth is a disc hung in the air with Mandara at its centre and the jambu tree on it","jain","asserted",4,"aariyan"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["ஆரியன்","வேந்தன்"],"ch":"so:19"},"so:390":{"book":"soolamani","num":390,"t":["குலகிரி யாறுகூர் கண்ட மேழ்குலாய்","மலைதிரை வளர்புன லேழி ரண்டதாய்க்","கொலைதரு வேலினாய் கூறப் பட்டதிவ்","வலைதிரை நெடுங்கட லவனி வட்டமே"],"p":"","r":"The disc is told as six great mountains, seven lands and fourteen rivers, a world ringed by the sea's nets of waves.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kurippu","naadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 390","lb":"SO390","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["the disc has six mountain ranges, seven lands and fourteen rivers","jain","asserted",1,"aariyan"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["ஆரியன்","வேந்தன்"],"ch":"so:19"},"so:391":{"book":"soolamani","num":391,"t":["மாற்றறு மண்டில மதனு ளூழியால்","ஏற்றிழி புடையன விரண்டு கண்டமாம்","தேற்றிய விரண்டினுந் தென்மு கத்தது","பாற்றரும் புகழினாய் பரத கண்டமே"],"p":"","r":"In this disc, by the turning of ages, two lands rise and fall; of the two, the southern one is Bharata-khanda.","n":"The turning ages are only named by ஊழி here; the doctrine of a rising and a falling half-cycle is read from line 2 and the later verses.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaalam","naadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 391","lb":"SO391","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["ages turn so that a land rises and falls in them","jain","asserted",2,"aariyan"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["ஆரியன்","வேந்தன்"],"ch":"so:19"},"so:392":{"book":"soolamani","num":392,"t":["மற்றது மணிமய மாகிக் கற்பகம்","பொற்றிர ளணிபொழிற் போக பூமியாய்","முற்றிய வூழிமூன் றேறி மீள்வழிப்","பிற்றகை யூழிவட் பிரமர் தோன்றினார்"],"p":"","r":"That southern land, jewelled and with wish-giving trees and golden groves, was an enjoyment land for three ages, and in the following age the first patriarchs were born.","n":"பிரமர் is read here as the patriarchs of the following age; the word is not glossed in the verse.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaalam","naadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 392","lb":"SO392","mm":1,"ev":"In the age after the three enjoyment ages, the first patriarchs are born.","cl":[["the land passed through three ages of effortless enjoyment before the patriarchs came","jain","asserted",3,"aariyan"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["ஆரியன்","வேந்தன்"],"ch":"so:19"},"so:393":{"book":"soolamani","num":393,"t":["வெங்கதிர்ப் பரிதியும் விரைவு தண்பனி","அங்கதிர் வளையமு மாதி யாயின","இங்கிவர் படைத்தன ரிழிந்த திவ்வகை","பொங்கிய புரவியாய் போக காலமே"],"p":"","r":"The patriarchs began the sun, the cool moon's ring and the other things the age lacked; so the enjoyment age ended.","n":"Line 3 says these ones 'made' (படைத்தனர்); whether the sun and moon were newly set or newly seen is not decided by the Tamil.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaalam","andam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 393","lb":"SO393","mm":1,"ev":"The patriarchs appear and set up the sun, moon and other things of the new age.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["ஆரியன்","வேந்தன்"],"ch":"so:19"},"so:394":{"book":"soolamani","num":394,"t":["ஊழிமூன் றாவதோய்ந் திறுதி மன்னுயிர்","சூழ்துயர் பலகெடச் சோதி மூர்த்தியாய்","ஏழுய ருலகுடன் பரவ வீண்டருள்","ஆழியங் கிழமையெம் மடிக டோ ன்றினாய்"],"p":"","r":"When the third age ended, the lord appeared as a form of light to end the many sorrows of living beings, to be praised by the seven worlds.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","kaalam","arul"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 394","lb":"SO394","mm":1,"ev":"At the end of the third age the first lord is born to end beings' sorrow.","cl":[["a lord appears to remove the sorrows of living beings and is praised by all worlds","jain","asserted",2,"aariyan"]],"ag":["இறைவன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["ஆரியன்","வேந்தன்"],"ch":"so:19"},"so:395":{"book":"soolamani","num":395,"t":["ஆரரு டழழுவிய வாழிக் காதியாம்","பேரருண் மருவிய பிரான்றன் சேவடி","காரிருள் கழிதரக் கண்க வின்றரோ","சீரருள் சரணென வுலகஞ் சேர்ந்ததே"],"p":"","r":"The first lord's red feet drove out the dark, and the world took refuge in his grace as the eyes opened.","n":"The verse uses the wheel's name ஆழி for the first lord's emblem; the dark is darkness of the eyes, and the eyes' opening is the gain.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thiruvadi","arul","iraivan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 395","lb":"SO395","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["refuge at the first lord's feet and grace ends the darkness of beings","jain","asserted",3,"aariyan"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["ஆரியன்","வேந்தன்"],"ch":"so:19"},"so:396":{"book":"soolamani","num":396,"t":["அலந்தவ ரழிபசி யகற்றும் வாயிலும்","குலங்களுங் குணங்களுங் கொணார்ந்து நாட்டினான்","புலங்கிளர் பொறிநுகர் விலாத புண்ணியன்","நலங்கிளர் திருமொழி நாத னென்பவே"],"p":"","r":"The lord, free of the senses' enjoyment and full of merit, opened the doors that ease the hunger of the afflicted, and set up the orders of men and their qualities.","n":"The verse is cautious: it closes with 'they say', so the account is given as a received tradition, not as the poet's own report.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arul","eegai","aram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 396","lb":"SO396","mm":1,"ev":"The first lord ends the hunger of the afflicted and establishes the orders and qualities of men.","cl":[["the one who has no taste of the senses is the giver who ends the hunger of the afflicted","jain","asserted",3,"aariyan"],["the lord set up the orders of people and their qualities","jain","asserted",2,"aariyan"]],"ag":["இறைவன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["ஆரியன்","வேந்தன்"],"ch":"so:19"},"so:397":{"book":"soolamani","num":397,"t":["ஆங்கவன் றிருவரு ளலரச் சூடிய","வீங்கிய விரிதிரை வேலி காவலன்","ஓங்கிய நெடுங்குடை யொருவ னாயினான்","பாங்குயர் பரிதிவேற் பரத னென்பவே"],"p":"","r":"By that lord's grace a king of the sea-bound earth, with a single great umbrella, became Bharata, the king of the spear.","n":"The verse closes with 'they say', so the sovereignty is told as received tradition.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","vendhan","arul"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 397","lb":"SO397","mm":1,"ev":"By the first lord's grace Bharata becomes the sole king of the earth.","cl":[["a king's sole sovereignty arises by the grace of the first lord","jain","asserted",1,"aariyan"]],"ag":["பரதன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["ஆரியன்","வேந்தன்"],"ch":"so:19"},"so:398":{"book":"soolamani","num":398,"t":["ஆழியா லகலிடம் வணக்கி யாண்டவன்","பாழியா நவின்றதோட் பரத னாங்கொர்நாள்","ஊ ழியா னொளிமல ருருவச் சேவடி","சூழிமால் யானையான் றொழுது வாழ்த்தினான்"],"p":"","r":"Bharata, who bowed the earth with his wheel and ruled it, one day came to the first lord's feet, worshipped and praised them.","n":"Bharata rules by a discus of war yet bows at the feet of the lord of the dharma-wheel; the verse sets the two wheels side by side without comment.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","vendhan","aazhi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 398","lb":"SO398","mm":1,"ev":"Bharata, ruler by the wheel, worships and praises the first lord.","cl":[],"ag":["பரதன்","இறைவன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["ஆரியன்","வேந்தன்"],"ch":"so:19"},"so:399":{"book":"soolamani","num":399,"t":["கதிரணி மணிமுடி வணங்கிக் காவலன்","எதிரது வினவினா னிறைவன் செப்பினான்","அதிர்தரு விசும்பிடை யமிர்த மாரிசோர்","முதிர்தரு முகிலிடை முழங்கிற் றென்னவே"],"p":"","r":"Bharata bowed his crowned head and asked what lay ahead; the lord answered, his voice like thunder that sheds nectar rain from a rain-heavy cloud.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","iraivan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 399","lb":"SO399","mm":1,"ev":"Bharata asks the lord what is to come, and the lord answers.","cl":[],"ag":["பரதன்","இறைவன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["ஆரியன்","வேந்தன்"],"ch":"so:19"},"so:400":{"book":"soolamani","num":400,"t":["என்முத லிருபத்தீ ரிருவர் நாதர்கள்","நின்முத லீரறு வகையர் நேமியர்","மன்முதல் பலவர்கே சவர்கண் மாற்றவர்","தொன்முத லவர்தொகை யொன்ப தொன்பதே"],"p":"","r":"The lord tells him the count: twenty-four lords beginning with himself, twelve wheel-kings beginning with Bharata, and nine each of the Kesavas and their rivals.","n":"The verse lists three classes of great persons (lord, wheel-king, Kesava with rival); the text only names them in the classes and counts, not by name.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kurippu","kaalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 400","lb":"SO400","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["each age holds twenty-four lords, twelve wheel-kings, nine Kesavas and nine rivals","jain","asserted",4,"adinatha"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["இறைவன்","பரதன்"],"ch":"so:19"},"so:401":{"book":"soolamani","num":401,"t":["மன்னவ நின்மகன் மரிசி மாற்றிடைப்","பொன்னவிர் போதன முடைய பூங்கழல்","கொன்னவில் வேலவன் குலத்துட் டோ ன்றினான்","அன்னவன் கேசவர்க் காதி யாகுமே"],"p":"","r":"The lord says to the king that his son Marici will be born in the family of the hero of the golden anklet and be the first of the Kesavas.","n":"The identification of Marici's line with Podanapura is not in this verse; read with 406.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["oozh","kurippu","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 401","lb":"SO401","mm":1,"ev":"The lord foretells that Bharata's son Marici will be born as the first of the Kesavas.","cl":[["the line of great persons is foretold, and a particular man's birth as a Kesava is fixed beforehand","jain","asserted",4,"adinatha"]],"ag":["மரிசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["இறைவன்","பரதன்"],"ch":"so:19"},"so:402":{"book":"soolamani","num":402,"t":["கேசவ னார்திறங் கிளப்பின் வெண்மலை","காசறு வனப்பினோர் கன்னி யேதுவால்","ஆசர வச்சுவக் கிரீவ னாவியும்","தேசறு திகரியுஞ் செவ்வன வெளவுமே"],"p":"","r":"Telling of that Kesava: the lord says that because of a girl of silver-hill beauty he will take the life and the discus of Ashvagriva.","n":"Line 3's spelling of the rival's name is corrupt (ஆசரவச்சுவக்கிரீவன்); it is read as Ashvagriva.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["oozh","kurippu","aazhi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 402","lb":"SO402","mm":1,"ev":"The lord foretells that the Kesava will take Ashvagriva's life and discus.","cl":[["a future hero's victory over his rival is foretold","jain","asserted",4,"adinatha"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["இறைவன்","பரதன்"],"ch":"so:19"},"so:403":{"book":"soolamani","num":403,"t":["தேரணி கடற்படைத் திவிட்டன் சென்றுபின்","ஆரணி யறக்கதி ராழி நாதனாம்","பாரணி பெரும்புகழ்ப் பரத வென்றனன்","சீரணி திருமொழித் தெய்வத் தேவனே"],"p":"","r":"The lord says that the Kesava, going afterwards with his sea of an army, will become the lord of the wheel and conquer.","n":"The verse does not name the Kesava; the vocative பரத makes the lord's address to Bharata plain, and line 4's final word is read as a closing epithet.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["arasu","aazhi","kurippu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 403","lb":"SO403","mm":1,"ev":"The lord foretells the Kesava's march with his army and his lordship of the discus.","cl":[],"ag":["செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ec":[],"spk":["இறைவன்","பரதன்"],"ch":"so:19"},"so:404":{"book":"soolamani","num":404,"t":["ஆதியு மந்தமு நடுவு நம்மதே","ஓதநீ ருலகுடை யுரிமை யென்றரோ","காதுவே லரசர்கோக் களிப்புற் றானிது","போதுசே ரலங்கலாய் புராண நீர்மையே"],"p":"","r":"The lord says that beginning, end and middle are theirs, the sea-ringed world's right; the king of kings rejoiced, and the teacher closes: such is the way of the purana.","n":"Lines 1-2 read as the lord's words; line 3 narrates Bharata's joy; line 4 is the teacher closing the story to his own king. The break between voices is uncertain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaalam","arasu","puraanam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 404","lb":"SO404","mm":1,"ev":"Bharata rejoices at the lord's words about the sea-ringed world.","cl":[],"ag":["பரதன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["இறைவன்","பரதன்"],"ch":"so:19"},"so:405":{"book":"soolamani","num":405,"t":["அன்னணம் புராணநூ லகத்துத் தோன்றிய","கன்னவி விலங்குதோட் காளை யானவன்","மின்னவில் விசும்பின் றிழிந்து வீங்குநீர்","மன்னிய வரைப்பக மலிரத் தோன்றினான்"],"p":"","r":"So, as the purana's book tells, the bull-armed hero came down from the shining sky and was born on the earth ringed by the swelling sea.","n":"The hero is not named; he is taken as Tivittan whose rise Marici's line foretold in verses 401-403.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["piravi","puraanam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 405","lb":"SO405","mm":1,"ev":"The purana's hero descends from the sky and is born on earth.","cl":[["a being descends from heaven and takes birth on earth as the purana foretold","jain","asserted",3,"aariyan"]],"ag":["செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ec":[],"spk":["ஆரியன்","வேந்தன்"],"ch":"so:19"},"so:406":{"book":"soolamani","num":406,"t":["திருவமர் சுரமைநா டணிந்து செம்பொனால்","பொருவரு போதன முடைய பூங்கழல்","செருவமர் தோளினான் சிறுவ ராகிய","இருவரு ளிளையவ னீண்டந் நம்பியே"],"p":"","r":"Of the two sons, born in golden Podanapura of Surama land, the younger is the youth, named Nambi, whom the teacher has been describing.","n":"This verse ties the purana to the two princes of earlier chapters: the elder is Vijayan and the younger Tivittan, who bears the discus marks of verse 78.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["piravi","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 406","lb":"SO406","mm":1,"ev":"The teacher names the younger of the king's two sons as the hero of the purana.","cl":[],"ag":["செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ec":[],"spk":["ஆரியன்","வேந்தன்"],"ch":"so:19"},"so:407":{"book":"soolamani","num":407,"t":["கானுடை விரிதிரை வையங் காக்கிய","மானுட வுடம்பினான் மறைந்து வந்தவத்","தேனுடை யலங்கலான் றெய்வ மார்பகம்","தானடைந் தமர்வதற் குரிய டையலே"],"p":"","r":"The speaker says that the woman is fit to come and rest on the breast of the god-like lord who guards the sea-girt earth in a human body, wearing a honeyed garland.","n":"The grammar is clipped and the speaker is not named here; line 410 calls the whole run the astrologer's words, which is the basis for the speaker given.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["oozh","kanavu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 407","lb":"SO407","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நிமித்திகன்","விஞ்சையர் வேந்தன்"],"ch":"so:20"},"so:408":{"book":"soolamani","num":408,"t":["ஆங்கவற் கீந்தபி னாழி தாங்கிய","ஈங்கவற் கொன்றுனக் கிரண்டு சேடியும்","தாங்கிய திருவினாற் றருமற் றென்றலும்","வீங்கிய வுவகையன் வேந்த னாயினான்"],"p":"","r":"After the girl is given to the discus-bearer, the speaker says, one daughter goes to him and the two Vidyadhara rows of the ridge, with their wealth, come to the king; at this word the king swells with joy and becomes sovereign.","n":"The wording of line 2 is compressed; the sense that one is given to the lord and two rows come to the king is the likely one.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["oozh","arasu","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 408","lb":"SO408","mm":1,"ev":"The Vidyadhara king, told that giving his daughter wins him both rows of the ridge, is filled with joy.","cl":[],"ag":["விஞ்சையர் வேந்தன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["நிமித்திகன்","விஞ்சையர் வேந்தன்"],"ch":"so:20"},"so:409":{"book":"soolamani","num":409,"t":["கொங்கலர் தெரியலான் றிறத்திற் கொள்குறி","இங்கியா னிசைத்ததே யமையு மல்லதோர்","திங்கணா ளகவையிற் றிவிட்ட னாங்கொரு","சிங்கம்வாய் பிளந்திடுந் தெளியீ தென்னவே"],"p":"","r":"The speaker says that the sign taken about the garlanded one needs nothing more than this: within a month's days Tivittan will split a lion's mouth.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kurippu","oozh","kaalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 409","lb":"SO409","mm":1,"ev":"The astrologer foretells that Tivittan will tear open a lion within a month.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நிமித்திகன்","விஞ்சையர் வேந்தன்"],"ch":"so:20"},"so:410":{"book":"soolamani","num":410,"t":["நிமித்திக னுரைத்தது நிறைந்த சோதியான்","உமைத்தகை யிலாததோ ருவகை யாழ்ந்துகண்","இமைத்ததில னெத்துணைப் பொழுது மீர்மலர்ச்","சுமைத்தகை நெடுமுடி சுடரத் தூக்கினான்"],"p":"","r":"The astrologer's words sink the radiant king in a joy beyond bearing; he does not blink for a long while and lifts his crowned head so that it blazes.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kurippu","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 410","lb":"SO410","mm":1,"ev":"The Vidyadhara king receives the astrologer's word with joy.","cl":[],"ag":["விஞ்சையர் வேந்தன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:20"},"so:411":{"book":"soolamani","num":411,"t":["இருதிலத் தலைமக னியன்ற நூற்கடல்","திருநிதிச் செல்வனச் செம்பொன் மாரியாச்","சொரினிதிப் புனலுடைச் சோதி மாலையென்","றருநிதி வளங்கொணா டாள நல்கினான்"],"p":"","r":"The king gives the learned astrologer a golden rain of treasure and a bright garland, speaking of Jyotimalai, so that he might rule his land with the rare wealth.","n":"Lines 1 to 3 are hard to construe. It is not certain whether Jyotimalai is the daughter's name or a garland of light, whether the astrologer or another man receives the gift, or who rules the land.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["eegai","selvam","kalvi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 411","lb":"SO411","mm":1,"ev":"The king rewards the astrologer with wealth.","cl":[],"ag":["விஞ்சையர் வேந்தன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:20"},"so:412":{"book":"soolamani","num":412,"t":["மன்னவன் பெயர்ந்துபோய் வாயு வேகைதன்","பொன்னகர் புக்கனன் பொழுதுஞ் சென்றது","கன்னிதன் பெருமையுங் கருமச் சூழ்ச்சியும்","அன்னமென் னடையவட் கறியக் கூறினான்"],"p":"","r":"The king goes to Vayuvegai's golden city as the day wears on and tells her, so that she of the swan gait will know, the girl's greatness and the plan to be carried out.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["soozhchi","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 412","lb":"SO412","mm":1,"ev":"The Vidyadhara king travels to Vayuvegai's city and tells her of the girl's greatness and the plan.","cl":[],"ag":["விஞ்சையர் வேந்தன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:20"},"so:413":{"book":"soolamani","num":413,"t":["தொக்கின மலர்த்துதை விலாத சோலையும்","புக்கிளந் தாமரை நகாத பொய்கையும்","மிக்கிளம் பிறைவிசும் பிலாத வந்தியும்","மக்களை யிலாததோர் மனையு மொக்குமே"],"p":"","r":"The king says that a house without children is like a grove with no blossom, a pool with no young lotus, and an evening with no crescent moon.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["makkal","ilvaazhkkai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 413","lb":"SO413","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["a house without children is empty, like a grove without flowers","general","reported",1,"vinjaiyar_vendhan"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["விஞ்சையர் வேந்தன்","வாயு வேகை"],"ch":"so:20"},"so:414":{"book":"soolamani","num":414,"t":["தலைமகள் றாடனக் காகச் சாகைய","நிலைமைகொண் மனைவியர் நிமிர்ந்த பூந்துணர்","நலமிகு மக்களா முதியர் தேன்களாக்","குலமிகு கற்பகங் குளிர்ந்து தோன்றுமே"],"p":"","r":"The king says that when a first daughter is born, wives become the branches, children the flower clusters and elders the honey of the wishing-tree of the family, which then stands cool and fair.","n":"","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["makkal","kudi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 414","lb":"SO414","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["a house is made whole by wives, children and elders together","general","reported",1,"vinjaiyar_vendhan"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["விஞ்சையர் வேந்தன்","வாயு வேகை"],"ch":"so:20"},"so:415":{"book":"soolamani","num":415,"t":["சூழிநீண் முகத்தன துளைக்கைம் மாவொடு","மாழைநீண் மணியிவை யெளிய மாண்பினால்","வாழுநீர் மக்களைப் பெறுதன் மாதரார்க்","காழிநீர் வையகத் தரிய தாவதே"],"p":"","r":"The king says that for women, bearing children is harder to gain in this sea-girt world than elephants with trunks or gold and gems.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["makkal","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 415","lb":"SO415","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["bearing children is rarer for women than wealth","general","reported",3,"vinjaiyar_vendhan"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["விஞ்சையர் வேந்தன்","வாயு வேகை"],"ch":"so:20"},"so:416":{"book":"soolamani","num":416,"t":["தகளிவாய்க் கொழுங்சுடர் தனித்துங் கோழிருள்","நிகளவாய்ப் பிளந்தகஞ் சுடர நிற்குமே","துகளிலாச் சுடர்மணி துளும்பு பூணினாய்","மகளெலாத் திசைகளு மலிர மன்னினாள்"],"p":"","r":"The king says that as a lamp in a dark place makes the cave shine, so the daughter now shines in every direction, and he calls her mother, rich in jewels, to see it.","n":"Lines 1 and 2 are corrupt or lean in their word division; the lamp comparison is the likely sense.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["makkal","oli","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 416","lb":"SO416","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["விஞ்சையர் வேந்தன்","வாயு வேகை"],"ch":"so:20"},"so:417":{"book":"soolamani","num":417,"t":["வலம்புரி வயிற்றிடைப் பிறந்த மாமணி","நலம்புரி பவித்திர மாகு நாமநீர்","பொலம்புரி மயிலனாய் பயந்த பூங்கொடி","குலம்புரிந் தவர்க்கெலாங் கோல மாகுமே"],"p":"","r":"The king calls the daughter born to her, like a gem from a right-whorled conch, a pure and flowering creeper who will be the glory of the whole family that bears her.","n":"Line 2 is unclear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["makkal","kudi","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 417","lb":"SO417","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["விஞ்சையர் வேந்தன்","வாயு வேகை"],"ch":"so:20"},"so:418":{"book":"soolamani","num":418,"t":["மக்களை யிலாதவர் மரத்தொ டொப்பவென்","றொக்கநின் றுரைப்பதோ ருரையு மூய்த்துநீர்","நக்கவா னிளம்பிறை வளர்ந்த நாட்கதிர்ச்","செக்கர்வா னனையதோர் திருவு மெய்தினாய்"],"p":"","r":"The king reminds her of the saying that the childless are like trees, and says she has now gained a wealth like the red evening sky of the waxing crescent.","n":"The king gives the saying as a common one and does not argue for it; he uses it to praise what she has gained.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["makkal","selvam","kurippu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 418","lb":"SO418","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["the childless are like trees","general","reported",1,"vinjaiyar_vendhan"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["விஞ்சையர் வேந்தன்","வாயு வேகை"],"ch":"so:20"},"so:419":{"book":"soolamani","num":419,"t":["மாவினை மருட்டிய நோக்கி நின்மகள்","பூவினுண் மடந்தைபொற் பூவை நாளொளித்","தேவனுக் கமிர்தமாந் தெய்வ மாமென","ஓவினூற் புரோகித னுணர வோதினான்"],"p":"","r":"The king tells her that the priest, learned in lore, said that her daughter, with a look that dazzles deer, will be the lotus-goddess to the god, a nectar to him.","n":"The priest here is reported by the king; he does not speak in the scene.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["oozh","amudham","kurippu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 419","lb":"SO419","mm":1,"ev":"The priest, in the king's report, declares that the daughter is destined for Tivittan.","cl":[],"ag":["புரோகிதன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["விஞ்சையர் வேந்தன்","வாயு வேகை"],"ch":"so:20"},"so:420":{"book":"soolamani","num":420,"t":["மத்தவார் மதகளிற் றுழவன் மற்றிவை","ஒத்தவா றுரைத்தலு மூவகை கைம்மிக","முத்தவாண் முகிழ்நகை யடக்கி மொய்குழல்","தொத்துவார் பிணையலா டொழுது சொல்லினாள்"],"p":"","r":"At the king's words the queen hides her wide smile in great joy and speaks, bowing with garlanded hair.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inbam","panivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 420","lb":"SO420","mm":1,"ev":"The queen suppresses a smile and bows to answer the king.","cl":[],"ag":["வாயு வேகை"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:20"},"so:421":{"book":"soolamani","num":421,"t":["மின்னவிர் மணிமுடி வேந்தர் வேந்தவிக்","கன்னிநின் னருளினே கருதப் பட்டனள்","மன்னவ ரருளில ராயின் மக்களும்","பின்னவர் பெறுவதோர் பெருமை யில்லையே"],"p":"","r":"The queen tells the king of kings that the girl was valued through his grace, and that if kings are without grace, children have no worth among later folk.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arul","makkal","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 421","lb":"SO421","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["children win worth only through the grace of kings","general","reported",3,"vayuvekai"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["வாயு வேகை","விஞ்சையர் வேந்தன்"],"ch":"so:20"},"so:422":{"book":"soolamani","num":422,"t":["பிடிகளை மகிழ்களிற் றரசர் பெய்ம்மலர்","முடிகளின் மணிபொர முரலு மொய்கழல்","அடிகள தருளினா லம்பொன் சாயலிக்","கடிகமழ் குழலினாள் கவினு மெய்தினாள்"],"p":"","r":"The queen says that by the grace of the feet of the elephant-riding kings, whose crowns bear the bees of strewn flowers, the girl gained her beauty and fair hair.","n":"Which kings the grace belongs to is not stated; the sense is that the match with a king's son is what gives the girl standing.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["arul","azhagu","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 422","lb":"SO422","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["வாயு வேகை","விஞ்சையர் வேந்தன்"],"ch":"so:20"},"so:423":{"book":"soolamani","num":423,"t":["திருமனைக் கிழத்திதன் றேங்கொள் சின்மொழி","மருமணி முடியினான் மகிழ்ந்து மற்றவள்","பருமணிப் பூண்முலை பாய மார்பிடை","அருமணித் தெரியறே னழிய வைகினான்"],"p":"","r":"The king, pleased at the queen's few sweet words, lies with her until his jewelled garland is crushed against her breast.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inbam","ilvaazhkkai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 423","lb":"SO423","mm":1,"ev":"The king and queen lie together, pleased with each other.","cl":[],"ag":["விஞ்சையர் வேந்தன்","வாயு வேகை"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:20"},"so:424":{"book":"soolamani","num":424,"t":["மற்றைநாண் மகனையு மமைச்சர் தம்மையும்","கொற்றவாட் டடக்கையான் கூவிக் கொண்டிருந்","திற்றியான் கருதிய தென்று தொல்லைநூல்","கற்றநா வலனது கதையுஞ் சொல்லினான்"],"p":"","r":"The next day the king calls his son and his ministers, tells them his plan and tells the tale of the learned astrologer.","n":"The king's agent form here is pointing to the ministers; no word names him on the line, so he is marked unnamed.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["soozhchi","amaichu","kalvi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 424","lb":"SO424","mm":1,"ev":"The king summons his son and ministers and recounts the astrologer's prophecy.","cl":[],"ag":["விஞ்சையர் வேந்தன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:20"},"so:425":{"book":"soolamani","num":425,"t":["வீங்கிய முலையவ டிருவும் வெம்முலைக்","கோங்கிய முகிலவ னுரிய பெற்றியுந்","தாங்கிய புகழவன் மொழியத் தாரவர்","தேங்கிய வுவகையர் தெரிந்து சொல்லினார்"],"p":"","r":"When the king speaks of the girl's wealth and the lord's due, the garlanded ministers, joyful, understand it and answer him.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["amaichu","soozhchi","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 425","lb":"SO425","mm":1,"ev":"The ministers, once told the plan, reply with joy.","cl":[],"ag":["மந்திரக் கிழவர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["மந்திரக் கிழவர்","விஞ்சையர் வேந்தன்"],"ch":"so:20"},"so:426":{"book":"soolamani","num":426,"t":["தெய்வமே திரிகுழற் சிறுமி யாவதற்","கையமே யொழிந்தன மனலும் வேலினாய்","செய்யதோர் தூதினித் திவிட்டன் றாதையாம்","வெய்யவே லவனுழை விடுத்தும் வேந்தனே"],"p":"","r":"The ministers tell the king that doubt about the girl's marriage is gone and that a messenger should now be sent to Tivittan's father, the king of the burning spear.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["amaichu","thoothu","soozhchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 426","lb":"SO426","mm":1,"ev":"The ministers advise sending an envoy to Tivittan's father.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["மந்திரக் கிழவர்","விஞ்சையர் வேந்தன்"],"ch":"so:20"},"so:427":{"book":"soolamani","num":427,"t":["கற்றவன் கற்றவன் கருதுங் கட்டுரைக்","குற்றன வுற்றவுய்த் துரைக்கு மாற்றலான்","மற்றவன் மருசியே யவனை நாம்விடச்","சுற்றமுங் கருமமுஞ் சொல்ல வல்லனே"],"p":"","r":"The ministers name Marusi, a learned man who can carry what he has been taught and say what is fit, as the one to send, since he can put kin and the matter in words.","n":"","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thoothu","kalvi","solvanmai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 427","lb":"SO427","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["an envoy should be learned and able to speak both of kin and of the business","general","reported",3,"amaichar"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["மந்திரக் கிழவர்","விஞ்சையர் வேந்தன்"],"ch":"so:20"},"so:428":{"book":"soolamani","num":428,"t":["காரியந் துணிந்தவர் மொழியக் காவலன்","மாரியந் தடக்கையான் வருக வென்றொரு","சீரிய திருமுகஞ் சிறப்பொ டீந்தனன்","ஆரியன் கழலடி யவனும் வாழ்த்தினான்"],"p":"","r":"When the ministers have spoken the king calls Marusi and gives him a fine letter with honour; Marusi blesses the king's feet.","n":"The Vidyadhara king, as the passage's one king, is understood to be the giver of the letter; the verse does not name Marusi by the word at line 4.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thoothu","arasu","panivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 428","lb":"SO428","mm":1,"ev":"The king gives Marusi a letter for Tivittan's father, and Marusi blesses him.","cl":[],"ag":["விஞ்சையர் வேந்தன்","மருசி"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:20"},"so:429":{"book":"soolamani","num":429,"t":["மன்னவன் பணியொடு மருசி வானிடை","மின்னவிர் முகிற்குழா முழங்கும் வீதிபோய்த்","துன்னினன் சுரமைநாட் டகணி சூடிய","பொன்னகர் புறத்ததோர் பொழிலி னெல்லையே"],"p":"","r":"At the king's order Marusi crosses the sky, past the roaring clouds, and reaches the edge of a grove outside the golden city of the Surumai land.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thoothu","naadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 429","lb":"SO429","mm":1,"ev":"Marusi flies to the land of Surumai and reaches a grove beside its golden city.","cl":[],"ag":["மருசி"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:20"},"so:430":{"book":"soolamani","num":430,"t":["புதுமலர்ப் புட்பமா கரண்ட மென்னுமப்","பொதுமலர்ப் பூம்பொழில் புகலும் பொம்மென","மதுமலர் பொழிதர மழலை வண்டினம்","கதுமல ரினையொடு கலவி யார்த்தவே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes the grove, Pushpakarandam, where bees murmur and drip honey and pair with fresh blossoms.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naadu","azhagu","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 430","lb":"SO430","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:20"},"so:431":{"book":"soolamani","num":431,"t":["மருவினியன மதுவிரிவன மலரணிவன வகுளம்","திருமருவிய செழுநிழலன செங்குழையன தேமா","வரிமருவிய மதுகரமுண மணம்விரிவன நாகம்","பொரிவிரிவன புதுமலரென புன்குதிர்வன புறனே"],"p":"","r":"A catalogue of grove trees in bloom: bakula and mango with honey-drinking bees, naga and punnai dropping flowers on the ground beside them.","n":"The verse is a list in the edition's compressed word division, with its words run together; the trees are named by their usual Tamil names but several identifications are held with doubt.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","naadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 431","lb":"SO431","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:21"},"so:432":{"book":"soolamani","num":432,"t":["நிழனகுவன நிமிர்தழையன நிறைகுளிர்வன சாந்தம்","எழினகுவன விளமலரென வெழுசண்பக நிகரம்","குழனகுவன மதுகரநிரை குடைவனபல குரவம்","அழனகுவன வலர்நெரிதர வசை நிலையவ சோகம்"],"p":"","r":"The grove list continues: shady sandal, champak, kurava and asoka, each described by what it spreads, its cool and its bees.","n":"Continues the list of 431; the repeated verb endings give a litany of trees rather than a sentence.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 432","lb":"SO432","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:21"},"so:433":{"book":"soolamani","num":433,"t":["எழுதுருவின வெழுதளிரென விணரணிவன விரதம்","இழுதுருவின கொழுமலரிடை யெழில்பொலிவன மதுகம்","கழுதுருவின கஞலிலையன கழிமடலின் கைதை","பொழுதுருவின வணிபொழிலின பொழி தளிரென புன்னை"],"p":"","r":"More trees of the grove are named by the look of their shoots and flowers: iluppai, madhuka, kaithai and punnai among them.","n":"Two or three tree names here are doubtful in the edition's word division.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 433","lb":"SO433","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:21"},"so:434":{"book":"soolamani","num":434,"t":["வளர்கொடியன மணம்விரிவன மல்லிகையொடு மெளவல்","நளிர்கொடியன நறுவிரையக நறுமலரன நறவம்","குளிர்கொடியன குழைமாதவி குவிமுகையன கொகுடி","ஒளிர்கொடியன வுயர்திரளினோ டொழு கிணரன வோடை"],"p":"","r":"Creepers and climbing flowers are listed: jasmine, mullai, madhavi and kokudi with buds and clusters, and a flowering pond among them.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 434","lb":"SO434","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:21"},"so:435":{"book":"soolamani","num":435,"t":["குடையவிவன கொழுமலரின் குளிர்களின கோங்கம்","புடையவிழ்வன புதுமலரன புன்னாகமொ டிலவம்","கடியவிழ்வன கமழ் பாதிரி கலிகளிகைய சாகம்","இடையவிழ்வன மலரளவில விதுபொழிலின தியல்பே"],"p":"","r":"The grove's open flowers are listed again: kongu, punnaga, ilavam and patiri, whose petals open beyond counting.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 435","lb":"SO435","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:21"},"so:436":{"book":"soolamani","num":436,"t":["மதுமகிழ்வன மலர்குடைவன மணிவண்டொடு தும்பி","குதிமகிழ்வன குவிகுடையன நுகிகோதுபு குயில்கள்","புதுமகிழ்வன பொழிலிடையன புணர்துணையன பூவை","விதிமகிழ்பவர் மதிமகிழ்வுற விரவுற்றன விரிவே"],"p":"","r":"In the grove's flowers bees, koels and mynas take their pleasure, and the verse ends by saying the grove's blossoming was such as to delight those who delight in fate and mind.","n":"The last line, which seems to say the sight pleased minds that delight in fate, is read with doubt; விதி மகிழ்பவர் is not clear from the edition's division.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 436","lb":"SO436","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:21"},"so:437":{"book":"soolamani","num":437,"t":["அதுவழகுத கைமகிழ்வுற வலர்தாரவ னடைய","இதுவழகிய திவண்வருகென வெழுபுள்ளொலி யிகவா","விதிவழகுடை விரியிலையிடை வெறிவிரவிய வேரிப்","பொதியவிழ்வன புதுமலரணி பொய்கைக்கரை புக்கான்"],"p":"","r":"A garlanded man, pleased at the sight, hears birds call him to come here, and enters the bank of a pond where flowers open.","n":"The man is named only by his garland (அலர்தாரவன்) and by the verb; the story elsewhere gives him a name this verse does not.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 437","lb":"SO437","mm":1,"ev":"The garlanded man enters the bank of the flowering pond.","cl":[],"ag":["அலர்தாரவன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:21"},"so:438":{"book":"soolamani","num":438,"t":["புணர்கொண்டெழு பொய்கைக்கரை பொரு திவலைகள் சிதறாத்","துணர்கொண்டன கரைமாநனி தூறுமலர்பல தூவா","வணர்கொண்டன மலலுற்றலை வளர்வண்டின மெழுவா","இணர்கொண்டெதி ரெழுதென்றலி னெதிர்கொண்டதவ் விடமே"],"p":"","r":"The pond's bank, wet with spray, is heavy with flowers and rising bees, and the south wind comes to meet the place.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 438","lb":"SO438","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:21"},"so:439":{"book":"soolamani","num":439,"t":["புனல்விரவிய துளிர்பிதிர்வது புரிமுத்தணி மணல்மேல்","மினல்விரவிய சுடர்பொன்னொளிர் மிளிர்வேதிகை மிகையொண்","கனல்விரவிய மணியிடைகன கங்கணியணி திரளின்","அனல்விரவிய வலரணியதொ ரசோகம்மது கண்டான்"],"p":"","r":"Where the water, the pearl-strewn sand, a gold-bright altar and bright fire-like flowers meet, the man sees an asoka tree.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 439","lb":"SO439","mm":1,"ev":"The man sees an asoka tree beside a shining altar.","cl":[],"ag":["அலர்தாரவன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:21"},"so:440":{"book":"soolamani","num":440,"t":["அதனின்னிழ லவனடைதலு மதுகாவல னாவான்","பொதியின்னவிழ் மலர்சிதறுபு பொலிகென் றுரை புகலா","மதீயின்னொளி வளர்கின்றதொர் மணியின்சிலை காட்ட","இதுவென்னென இதுவென்னென வினையன்பல சொன்னான்"],"p":"","r":"As he reaches its shade, the keeper of it tells the flowers to scatter and bloom, and shows a gem-bright slab, saying many things in wonder at what it is.","n":"Who is meant by the one who says 'what is this' (line 4) is not settled by the verse.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 440","lb":"SO440","mm":1,"ev":"The keeper welcomes the man and shows him the gem slab under the asoka.","cl":[],"ag":["அலர்தாரவன்","காவலன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["காவலன்",""],"ch":"so:21"},"so:441":{"book":"soolamani","num":441,"t":["மினற்கொடி விலங்கிய விலங்கன்மிசை வாழும்","புனற்கொடி மலர்த்தொகை புதைத்தபொலி தாரோய்","நினக்கென வியற்றிய நிலாநிழன் மணிக்கல்","மனக்கினிதி னேறினை மகிழ்ந்திருமி னென்றான்"],"p":"","r":"The keeper tells him, wearer of the garland, that this gem-stone, made to be a shaded seat for him, is pleasing to climb, and bids him sit gladly.","n":"The speaker is taken from the keeper of 440 and the addressee from the vocative 'wearer of the garland' (தாரோய்); the verse does not name either.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 441","lb":"SO441","mm":1,"ev":"The keeper offers the garlanded man the gem slab as his seat.","cl":[],"ag":["காவலன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["காவலன்","அலர்தாரவன்"],"ch":"so:21"},"so:442":{"book":"soolamani","num":442,"t":["அழற்கதி ரியங்கற வலங்கிண ரசோகம்","நிழற்கதிர் மரத்தகைய தாக நினை கில்லேன்","பொழிற்கடவுள் பொன்னிடமி தென்னைபுகு மாறென்","றெழிற்கதிர் விசும்பிடை யிழிந்தவன் மொழிந்தான்"],"p":"","r":"The one who came down from the shining sky says he cannot think how a tree of fire-bright flowers could cast a shade, and asks whose golden place it is in the grove-god's precinct and how he came to enter it.","n":"Whether the descender is the garlanded man of 437 or another is held open by the edition's compression.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["arivu","iraivan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 442","lb":"SO442","mm":1,"ev":"The man who has come down from the sky asks what the place is and how he came to enter.","cl":[],"ag":["அலர்தாரவன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["அலர்தாரவன்","காவலன்"],"ch":"so:21"},"so:443":{"book":"soolamani","num":443,"t":["நிலாவளர் நிழற்கதிர் நிமிர்ந்தொளி துளும்பும்","சிலாதல மிதற்குரிய தெய்வமெனல் வேண்டா","அலாதவ ரிதற்குரிய ரல்லரவ ராவிர்","உலாவிய கழற் றகையி னீரென வுரைத்தான்"],"p":"","r":"The keeper answers that the slab is not to be thought of as a god's; no one but the anklet-wearers addressed is fit for it.","n":"The plural 'you with anklets' (கழற்றகையினீர்) shows more than one hearer; the verse does not say who they are.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["arivu","oozh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 443","lb":"SO443","mm":1,"ev":"The keeper says the slab belongs by right to those he addresses.","cl":[],"ag":["காவலன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["காவலன்","அலர்தாரவன்"],"ch":"so:21"},"so:444":{"book":"soolamani","num":444,"t":["என்னவிது வந்தவகை யென்னினிது கேண்மின்","நன்னகரி தற்கிறைவன் முன்னநனி நண்ணித்","தன்னிகரி கந்தவ னங்கத னெனும் பேர்ப்","பொன்னருவி நூல்கெழுபு ரோகித னுரைத்தான்"],"p":"","r":"The keeper begins to say how the slab came to be, and reports that a learned house-priest of the city's lord had once spoken of it.","n":"The priest's name is garbled in the edition's division (பொன்னருவி and an unclear second name).","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["oozh","kalvi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 444","lb":"SO444","mm":1,"ev":"The keeper reports that the king's priest foretold the slab.","cl":[],"ag":["புரோகிதன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["காவலன்","அலர்தாரவன்"],"ch":"so:21"},"so:445":{"book":"soolamani","num":445,"t":["மின்னவிர் விளங்குசுடர் விஞ்சையுல காளும்","வின்னவி றடக்கைவிறல் வேலொருவன் வேண்டி","மன்னநின் மகற்கொரு மகட்கரும முன்னி","இன்னவ னினைப்பகலு ளீண்டிழியு மென்றான்"],"p":"","r":"The priest had said that a fierce, spear-carrying lord of the Vidyadhara world would come seeking a bride for the king's son and descend here this very day.","n":"The report is relayed by the keeper and the speaker of 'என்றான்' is the priest only by the story so far.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["oozh","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 445","lb":"SO445","mm":1,"ev":"The priest foretells a Vidyadhara lord's coming to seek a bride for the king's son.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["புரோகிதன்","வேந்தன்"],"ch":"so:21"},"so:446":{"book":"soolamani","num":446,"t":["மடங்கலை யடுந்திற னெடுந்தகைதன் மாறாய்","அடங்கல ரடங்கவடு மாழியஃ தாள்வான்","உடங்கவ னுடன்றெரி துளும்பவரும் வந்தால்","நடந்தவ னடுங்கவடு நம்பியிவ னென்றான்"],"p":"","r":"The priest added that the youth would destroy him, the foe of a lion-slaying man, when he came blazing in anger with his discus, so that the one he faced would tremble.","n":"The enemy and the youth are not named; the identification with the story's hero and his foe is from the larger book, not this verse.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["oozh","kodungol"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 446","lb":"SO446","mm":1,"ev":"The priest foretells the youth will overcome the discus-bearing enemy.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["புரோகிதன்","வேந்தன்"],"ch":"so:21"},"so:447":{"book":"soolamani","num":447,"t":["ஆங்கவன் மொழிந்தபி னடங்கலரை யட்டான்","தேங்கமழ் பொழிற்றிகழ் சிலாதலமி தாக்கி","ஈங்கவ னிழிந்தபி னெழுந்தெதிர்கொ ளென்ன","நீங்கல னிருந்தன னெடுந் தகையி தென்றான்"],"p":"","r":"After he has killed his enemies, the youth will make this shaded slab, and when he descends is to be received, so the priest said; the keeper has stayed to wait.","n":"The line 'நீங்கலன் இருந்தனன்' makes the keeper's waiting part of the fulfilment.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["oozh","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 447","lb":"SO447","mm":1,"ev":"The keeper has waited at the slab as the priest foretold, to greet the one who descends.","cl":[],"ag":["காவலன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["புரோகிதன்","வேந்தன்"],"ch":"so:21"},"so:448":{"book":"soolamani","num":448,"t":["என்றவன் மொழிந்தபி னருந்தன னிருப்பச்","சென்றவன் வழிச் சிரமை தீர்மினென நால்வர்","பொன்றவழ் பொருந்திழை யணங்கினனை யாரை","மின்றவழ் விளங்குகொடி வேந்தனும் விடுத்தான்"],"p":"","r":"When the keeper has finished, the king sends four goddess-like women after the man who has eaten and sits there, to ease his journey's weariness.","n":"Whether the guest has eaten what the keeper offered or merely rested is not clear from அருந்தனன் in line 1.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["arasu","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 448","lb":"SO448","mm":1,"ev":"The king sends four women to attend the seated guest.","cl":[],"ag":["வேந்தன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:21"},"so:449":{"book":"soolamani","num":449,"t":["பொன்னவிர் மணிக்கலை சிலம்பொடு புலம்ப","மின்னவிர் மணிக்குழை மிளிர்ந்தொளி துளும்பச்","சின்னமலர் துன்னுகுழ றேறலொடு சோர","அன்னமென வல்லவென வன்னண நடந்தார்"],"p":"","r":"The four women walk like swans, their gem girdles and anklets ringing, ear-ornaments glinting, and flowers slipping loose in their hair.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 449","lb":"SO449","mm":1,"ev":"The four women walk toward the guest.","cl":[],"ag":["அணங்கினனையார்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:21"},"so:450":{"book":"soolamani","num":450,"t":["நலங்கனி மடந்தையர் நடத்தொறு மிணர்ப்போ","தலங்கலள கக்கொடி யயற்சுடர வோடி","விலங்குபுரு வக்கொடி முரிந்துவெரு வெய்த","மலங்கின விலங்கின மதர்த்தவவர் வாட்கண்"],"p":"","r":"As the women walk, their arm-garlands swing, their brow-bows rise in alarm and their sword-sharp eyes outdo the deer's in pride.","n":"The brow-as-bow and eye-as-sword images are the poet's description of the women's bodies, not an event.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","kan","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 450","lb":"SO450","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["அணங்கினனையார்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:21"},"so:451":{"book":"soolamani","num":451,"t":["அலத்தக மலைத்தன வடித்தல மரற்றும்","கலைத்தலை மலைத்து விரி கின்றகடி யல்குல்","முலைத்தலை முகிழ்த்தொளி துளும்பி யுள முத்தம்","மலைத்தலை மயிற்கண் மருட்டுவர் சாயல்"],"p":"","r":"The women's red-dyed feet and ringing anklets, their girdled hips, pearl-hung breasts and gait confuse the peacock in the hills.","n":"The comparison is written as if nature were baffled by them; the grammar of lines 3 and 4 is read with doubt.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 451","lb":"SO451","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["அணங்கினனையார்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:21"},"so:452":{"book":"soolamani","num":452,"t":["கணங்கெழு கலாவமொளி காலுமக லல்குல்","சுணங்கெழு தடத்துணை முலைசுமை யிடத்தாய்","வணங்கியும் நுணங்கியும் வருந்திய மருங்கிற்","கிணங்குதுணை யாய்ஞிமி றிரங்கின வெழுந்தே"],"p":"","r":"Their peacock-tail girdles, spotted pairs of breasts and bending waists make a busy sight, and bees cry out and rise from them.","n":"The verse is a compressed description of the women's bodies; its last line reads as the bees' lament.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 452","lb":"SO452","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:21"},"so:453":{"book":"soolamani","num":453,"t":["முலைத்தொழில் சிலைத்தொழிலி னாருயிர் முருக்கும்","நிலைத்தொழில் வென்றுள நினைத்தொழுக வின்பக்","கலைத்தொழில்கள் காமனெய் கணைத்தொழில்க ளெல்லாம்","கொலைத்தொழில்கொள் வாட்கணி னகத்தகுறி கண்டீர்"],"p":"","r":"The power of their breasts and brows, of their lives' undoing and of every art of love is said to be marked in the killing look of their sword-like eyes.","n":"The verse treats the eyes as the sign of the love-god's whole equipment, at which breasts and bow-brows are only instruments.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","kan","kootru"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 453","lb":"SO453","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:21"},"so:454":{"book":"soolamani","num":454,"t":["துடித்ததுவர் வாயொடுது ளும்புநகை முத்தம்","பொடித்தவியர் நீரொடுபொ லிந்தசுட ரோலை","அடுத்ததில கத்தினொட ணிந்தவள கத்தார்","வடித்தசிறு நோக்கொடுமு கத்தொழில்வ குத்தார்"],"p":"","r":"Their lips tremble and their pearl-teeth smile, sweat beads their foreheads, earrings shine and their curled hair meets their brow-marks, as they shape their faces' work with a thin glance.","n":"The verse ends the chapter on the women's approach, with the message they are sent to deliver not yet spoken.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","azhagu","kan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 454","lb":"SO454","mm":1,"ev":"The four women shape their faces' play toward the guest.","cl":[],"ag":["அணங்கினனையார்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:21"},"so:455":{"book":"soolamani","num":455,"t":["பூவிரிகு ழற்சிகைம ணிப்பறவை போகா","வாவிகொள கிற்புகையுள் விம்மியவ ரொண்கண்","காவியென வூதுவன கைத்தலம் விலங்க","மேவியிவை காந்தளென வீழமிக நொந்தார்"],"p":"","r":"The poet says that in the smoke of incense bees take the women's bright eyes for blue lilies and their hands for kanthal flowers and swarm at them, and the women are sorely pained.","n":"The grammar of lines 1-2 is compressed; the women are taken as the sufferers, but the verse could also be read of the men pained at the sight.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","kan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 455","lb":"SO455","mm":1,"ev":"Bees mistake the women's eyes and hands for flowers and the women are pained by them.","cl":[],"ag":["மங்கையர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:22"},"so:456":{"book":"soolamani","num":456,"t":["சுரும்பொடு கழன்றுள குழற்றொகை யெழிற்கை","கரும்பொடு கலந்துள களித்தவவர் தீம்பண்","நரம்பொடு நடந்துள விரற்றலை யெயிற்றேர்","அரும்பொடு பொலிந்ததுவர் வாயமிர்த மன்றே"],"p":"","r":"The poet pairs the women's looks with things that delight: hair with bees, hands with sugar cane, sweet songs with lute strings, the nectar of the lips with the bud-like teeth.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 456","lb":"SO456","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:22"},"so:457":{"book":"soolamani","num":457,"t":["கணங்குழை மடந்தையர் கவின்பிற ழிருங்கண்","அணங்குர விலங்குதொ றகம்புலர வாடி","மணங்கம ழலங்கலுடை மைந்தர்த மனந்தாழ்","வணங்கிடை வணங்குதொ றணங்கென வணங்கும்"],"p":"","r":"As men in fragrant garlands bow their hearts to the women, the women's wide eyes, struck by desire, dry within and wilt, and they bow back as if before a goddess.","n":"The pairing of bowing in line 4 is hard to place; it may be the men who bow to the women as to a goddess.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","kan","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 457","lb":"SO457","mm":1,"ev":"The men bow to the women and the women bow back, both pained by desire.","cl":[],"ag":["மைந்தர்","மங்கையர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:22"},"so:458":{"book":"soolamani","num":458,"t":["நெய்யலர் குழற்றொகை நெருப்பினடு மென்பார்","மையலர் நெடுங்கணிவை வல்லகொலை யென்பார்","தொய்யலிள மென்முலையி னீர்சுடுதி ராயின்","உய்யல மெனத்தொழுது மைந்தர்க ளுடைந்தார்"],"p":"","r":"The poet says the men, saying the oiled hair of the women would burn them like fire and their long dark eyes can kill, worship them and break down, begging to be spared if the women should scorch them with their breasts.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","kan","kaamanoy"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 458","lb":"SO458","mm":1,"ev":"The men worship the women and collapse, saying their hair burns and their eyes kill.","cl":[],"ag":["மைந்தர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:22"},"so:459":{"book":"soolamani","num":459,"t":["நாம நூற்கலை விச்சை யினன்னெறி யிவைதாம்","தாம நீள்குழற் றளர்நடை யுருவுகொண் டனையார்","வாம மேகலை மடவர லிவர்களை வளர்த்தார்","காம நூலினுக் கிலக்கியங் காட்டிய வளர்த்தார்"],"p":"","r":"The poet says these women were reared as the living pattern of the lore of love, with trailing hair and the forms of garlanded creepers, and shown as proof of the Kama manual.","n":"The first line names the lore and the arts of learning as a good path; line 4 makes the women its example.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 459","lb":"SO459","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:22"},"so:460":{"book":"soolamani","num":460,"t":["இனிய வீங்கிய விளமுலை யிவர்களை வளர்த்தார்","பனியின் மென்மல ரலர்ந்தன வுவகையிற் பயில்வார்","கனிப வேலிவர் கடல்விளை யமிர் தெனக் கனிவார்","முனிப வேலிவ ரனங்கனைங் கணையென முனிவார்"],"p":"","r":"The poet says the women, reared with swelling breasts, bloom like cool flowers for those they love, melt like fruit sweet as sea-ambrosia for them, and turn in anger like Kama's five arrows.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 460","lb":"SO460","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:22"},"so:461":{"book":"soolamani","num":461,"t":["புலவி தானுமோர் கலவியை விளிப்பதோர் புலவி","கலவி தானுமோர் புலவியை விளைப்பதோர் கலவி","குலவுவார் சிலை மதனனைங் கணையொடு குலவி","இலவு வாயுடை யிளையவ ருடையன விவையே"],"p":"","r":"The poet says their sulks call for union and their unions breed sulks, so that these young women with red lips go about holding Kama's five arrows.","n":"The play of sulk and union is the stock pairing of love poetry; here it is cast as the work of Kama's arrows.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","oodal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 461","lb":"SO461","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:22"},"so:462":{"book":"soolamani","num":462,"t":["மன்னு வார்சிலை மதனனோர் வடிவுகொண் டிலாதான்","தன்னை நாமுமோர் தகைமையிற் றணத்துமென் றிருப்பார்","என்னை பாவமிங் கிவர்களைப் படைத்தன னிதுவால்","பின்னை யாங்கவன் பிறவிக்கு முதல் கண்ட வகையே."],"p":"","r":"The men, seeing the bodiless Kama only through these women, say they will somehow bear him, and cry out in wonder at what sin the Creator committed in making them, since here they have found the root of his birth.","n":"The speaker of lines 3-4 is not named; they are read as the men of the scene, and the Creator is only exclaimed against, not argued with.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 462","lb":"SO462","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["women's beauty is where desire is seen to take birth, and so the Creator's making of them is blamed","general","reported",3,"maindhar"]],"ag":["மைந்தர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["மைந்தர்",""],"ch":"so:22"},"so:463":{"book":"soolamani","num":463,"t":["வாம மேகலை முதனின்று வயிற்றிடை வளைர்ந்த","சாம லேகைகண் மயிர்நிரை யலதல மீது","காம நீரெரி யகத்து கனன்றெழ நிமிர்ந்த","தூம லேகைகள் பொடித்தன துணை முலை யுறவே"],"p":"","r":"The poet says that as the fire of desire blazed within them, fine lines of hair rose from the girdle across the belly, like smoke-lines, toward the breasts.","n":"The body is read as the women's; the 'smoke' is the line of fine hair, which the verse makes the sign of fire within.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","azhagu","yaakkai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 463","lb":"SO463","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["மங்கையர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:22"},"so:464":{"book":"soolamani","num":464,"t":["சனங்க டாஞ்சில தவங்களைத் தாங்குது மெனப்போய்","வனங்கள் காப்பவ ருளரென முனிவமற் றன்றேல்","தனங்க டாழ்ந்தவழ் சந்தனக் குழம்பிடை வளர்ந்த","கனங்கொள் வெம்முகங் கறுப்பதென் காரண முரையீர்"],"p":"","r":"The men ask the women why their faces darken, and say that if they were gone to the forest to bear small austerities then to be angry at others would be understood, but they linger in sandal paste.","n":"The verse is a question put to the women; its first lines are hard to construe and the sense of vanavar or ascetic guardians is read loosely.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","kan","kaamanoy"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 464","lb":"SO464","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["மைந்தர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["மைந்தர்","மங்கையர்"],"ch":"so:22"},"so:465":{"book":"soolamani","num":465,"t":["தூம மென்புகை துழாவிவண் டிடை யிடை துவைக்கும்","தாம வோதியர் தம்முகத் தனபிறர் மனத்த","காம நீள்சிலை கணையொடு குனிவகண் டாலும்","யாமெ மின்னுயி ருடையமென் றிருப்பதிங் கெவனோ"],"p":"","r":"The men say that when they see Kama's long bow, with arrows, bent on the faces of these women, it is hard to say why they should still hold on to their sweet lives.","n":"The bow is read as the women's brows set on their faces; the poet leaves the image unexplained.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","kaamanoy","uyir"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 465","lb":"SO465","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["மைந்தர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["மைந்தர்","மங்கையர்"],"ch":"so:22"},"so:466":{"book":"soolamani","num":466,"t":["என்று மைந்தர்க ளிடருற வெழுதிய கொடிபோற்","சென்று கற்பக வனமன செறிபொழி லடைந்தார்","இன்று காமுகர் படையினை யிடர்பட நடந்த","வென்றி காமனுக் குரைத்துமென் றிரைத்தளி விரைந்த"],"p":"","r":"After the men's cry the women go off like creepers drawn on cloth to the Kalpaka grove, hurrying to tell Kama of their victory over the army of lovers.","n":"","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 466","lb":"SO466","mm":1,"ev":"The women leave the men in pain and go to the Kalpaka grove to report their conquest to Kama.","cl":[],"ag":["மங்கையர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:22"},"so:467":{"book":"soolamani","num":467,"t":["ஆடைகைத் தலத்தொருத்தி கொண்டதங் கடைப்பைதன்","மாடுகைத் தலத்தொருத்தி கொண்டது மணிக்கலம்","சேடிகைத் தலத்தன செறிமணித் திகழ்வசெங்","கோடிகைத் தலத்தன குளிர்மணிப் பிணையலே"],"p":"","r":"The poet lists what the women carry: one a cloth, one a jewelled vessel, the attendants strings of gems, others garlands of cool gems in their hands.","n":"","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 467","lb":"SO467","mm":1,"ev":"Women carry cloths, jewel vessels and garlands.","cl":[],"ag":["மங்கையர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:22"},"so:468":{"book":"soolamani","num":468,"t":["வண்ணச் சந்தங்க ணிறைந்தன மணிச்செப்பு வளர்பூங்","கண்ணிச் சந்தங்க ணிறைந்தன கரண்டகங் கமழ்பூஞ்","சுண்ணச் சந்தங்க ணிறைந்தன சுடர்மணிப் பிரப்போ","டெண்ணச் சந்தங்கள் படச்சுமந் திளையவ ரிசைந்தார்"],"p":"","r":"The poet lists what the women bear: caskets of colour, flower-garlands, perfumed powder and trays of gems, with all that was wished, and the young women go in step.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 468","lb":"SO468","mm":1,"ev":"The young women carry colours, garlands and powders in jewelled caskets.","cl":[],"ag":["மங்கையர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:22"},"so:469":{"book":"soolamani","num":469,"t":["தகளி வெஞ்சுட ரெனத்திகழ் மணிக்குழை தயங்க","மகளிர் மங்கல வுழைக்கலஞ் சுமந்தவர் பிறரோ","டுகளு மான்பிணை யனையவ ருழைச் செல வொளிர்தார்த்","துகளில் விஞ்சையன் றுணிந்தனன் றுறக்கமீ தெனவே"],"p":"","r":"As the women with golden vessels of omen go to the man, followed by others, the faultless Vidyadhara in his bright garland decides that this must be heaven.","n":"That the guest is the Vidyadhara envoy of the sarukkam's title is read from the sarukkam, not stated in the verse.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","virundhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 469","lb":"SO469","mm":1,"ev":"The women go with auspicious vessels to the Vidyadhara, who judges the place to be heaven.","cl":[],"ag":["மங்கையர்","விஞ்சையன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:22"},"so:470":{"book":"soolamani","num":470,"t":["துறக்கம் புக்கவர் பெறுவன விவையெனத் துணியா","வெறிக்கண் விம்மிய விரைவரி தாரவ னிருப்பச்","சிறைக்க ணோக்கமுஞ் சிறுநகைத் தொழில்களுஞ் சுருக்கி","அறைக்கண் மாந்தனுக் கதிதியந் தொழிலினி லமைந்தார்"],"p":"","r":"The Vidyadhara sits and thinks these are what those who reach heaven gain; the young women set aside their sidelong looks and little smiles and devote themselves to the guest-duty owed to the man in the room.","n":"The women keep their looks for the lovers of verse 466 and turn them off for the guest; the verse makes hospitality a duty that cuts across the arts of desire.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["virundhu","kaamam","aram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 470","lb":"SO470","mm":1,"ev":"The women put away their glances and smiles and serve the guest.","cl":[],"ag":["மங்கையர்","விஞ்சையன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:22"},"so:471":{"book":"soolamani","num":471,"t":["ஆட்டி னார்வெறி கமழ்வன வணிகிளர் நறுநீர்","தீட்டி னார்நறுஞ் சாந்தமுஞ் சிறிதுமெய் கமழச்","சூட்டி னார்சிலர் நறுமல ரறுசுவை யடிசில்","ஊட்டி னாரவ னமரரு ளொருவனொத் தொளிர்ந்தான்"],"p":"","r":"The women bathe him, anoint him with sandal paste, garland him and feed him a meal of six tastes, so that he shines as one of the gods.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["virundhu","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 471","lb":"SO471","mm":1,"ev":"The women bathe, anoint, garland and feed the guest.","cl":[],"ag":["மங்கையர்","விஞ்சையன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:22"},"so:472":{"book":"soolamani","num":472,"t":["வயந்த முன்னிய திலகைகல் லியாணிகை வடிவார்","வியந்த சேனைமென் கமலமா லதையென விளம்பும்","இயங்கு பூங்கொடி யனையவ ரியல்புக ணினையா","வயங்கு தொல்புக ழம்பர சரன்மகிழ்ந் திருந்தான்"],"p":"","r":"Women named Tilakai, Kalyanikai, Senai and Kamalamalai, who are like moving flower creepers, attend him, and the old-famed lord of the sky is glad.","n":"The four names are clear from the Tamil but the word ambarasaran at the end is split in the text and read as the guest.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["virundhu","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 472","lb":"SO472","mm":1,"ev":"Four named women attend the guest, who is pleased.","cl":[],"ag":["மங்கையர்","விஞ்சையன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:22"},"so:473":{"book":"soolamani","num":473,"t":["ஆங்கெழிற் பொலிந்தவன் னிருந்தபின் னலங்குதார்","வீங்கெழிற் பொலிந்தானை வேந்தனேவ வீவில்சீர்ப்","பூங்கழற் பொலங் குழைந் திவிட்டனோடு போர்க்கதந்","தாங்கெழிற் பெருங்கையானை சங்க வண்ண னேறினான்"],"p":"","r":"At the king's bidding Vijayan, called the conch-coloured one, mounts the great trunked elephant together with Tivittan, ready for battle.","n":"Whether the verse sends the princes to war or to a procession is not stated; the sarukkam's theme of sending an envoy points to a march out.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vendhan","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 473","lb":"SO473","mm":1,"ev":"At the king's order Vijayan and Tivittan mount a war elephant.","cl":[],"ag":["வேந்தன்","விசயன்","செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:22"},"so:474":{"book":"soolamani","num":474,"t":["தம்பியோடு ங்கவிசய திவிட்டர்கள் ஏறிய சிறப்பு","பைம்பொ னோடை வீழ்மணிப் பகட்டெருத்த மேறினான்","செம்பொன்மா மலைச்சிகைக் கருங்கொண்மூவி னோடெழூஉம்","வம்பவெண்ணி லாவிங்கு திங்கள்போல மன்னினான்"],"p":"","r":"The poet says the elder brother mounts the elephant with a golden brow-band and a gem on its neck, and sits there like a white moon rising with a dark cloud above the red-gold mountain.","n":"Line 1 reads as a headline for the verse, not as a line of the metre, and is damaged.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","perumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 474","lb":"SO474","mm":1,"ev":"Vijayan and Tivittan mount the elephant.","cl":[],"ag":["விசயன்","செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:22"},"so:475":{"book":"soolamani","num":475,"t":["ஆர்த்தபல்லி யக்குழா மதித்தகுஞ்ச ரக்குழாம்","தேர்த்தவீரர் தேர்க்குழாந் திசைத்தபல்ச னக்குழாம்","போர்த்தசா மரக்குழாம் புதைத்தவெண் கொடிக்குழாம்","வேர்த்தவேந்தர் பல்குழாம் விரைந்தகூந்தல் மாக்குழாம்"],"p":"","r":"The poet lists the crowd that gathers: musical instruments, elephants, chariots with warriors, people, fly-whisks, white banners, kings in sweat and the women with flowing hair.","n":"The verse is a single run of nouns with no verb; it describes the procession.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["perumai","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 475","lb":"SO475","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:22"},"so:476":{"book":"soolamani","num":476,"t":["பாடுவார்வ ணங்குவார்ப லாண்டுகூறி வாழ்த்துவார்","ஆடுவாரோ டார்வமாந்த ரன்னரின்ன ராயபின்","சூடுமாலை சோரவுந் தொ டாரமாலை வீழவும்","மாடவாயின் மேலெலாம டந்தைமார்ம யங்கினார்"],"p":"","r":"Singers, bowers, those who bless with long life and dancers join the throng, and women in the mansions faint, their garlands slipping and their breast-ornaments falling.","n":"","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 476","lb":"SO476","mm":1,"ev":"The women on the mansion terraces faint as the princes pass.","cl":[],"ag":["மங்கையர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:22"},"so:477":{"book":"soolamani","num":477,"t":["கொண்டலார்ந்த பொன்னொளிக் குழற்கொடிக்கு ழாமனார்","மண்டலந்நி றைந்ததிங்கள் வட்டமொத்த வாண்முகம்","குண்டலங்கொ ழும்பொனோலை யென்றிரண்டு கொண்டணிஇ","வண்டலர்ந்து மாலைதாழ்ந்து மாடவாய்ம றைந்தவே"],"p":"","r":"The women with cloud-like hair, faces like the full moon, earrings and golden ear-leaves lean out, with garlands swaying, and then are hidden in the mansion windows.","n":"","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 477","lb":"SO477","mm":1,"ev":"The women look out from the mansion windows and withdraw.","cl":[],"ag":["மங்கையர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:22"},"so:478":{"book":"soolamani","num":478,"t":["கூடுதும்பி யூடுதோய்கு ழற்றொகைத்து ணர்துதைந்","தோடுமேலெ ருத்திடைக்கு லைந்தகோதை யோடுலாய்","மாடவாயின் மாலைஞால மாடமேறு மாதரார்","ஆடுமஞ்ஞை கோடுகொள்வ தென்னலாவ தாயினார்"],"p":"","r":"The women on the upper storeys, hair clustered with bees and garlands slipping, look like dancing peacocks.","n":"","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 478","lb":"SO478","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["மங்கையர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:22"},"so:479":{"book":"soolamani","num":479,"t":["தொண்டைவாய் மடந்தைமார்கள் சுடிகைவட்ட வாண்முகம்","கொண்டகோல நீரவாய கோடிமாட மேலெலாம்","வண்டுசூழ்ந்த பங்கயம லர்க்குழாமி ணைப்படூஉக்","கெண்டையோடு ந்ன்றலைந்த கேழவாய்க்கி ளர்ந்தவே"],"p":"","r":"The poet shows the coral-lipped women of the city on its many-storeyed terraces, their bright faces and carp-like eyes playing among clusters of lotus.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 479","lb":"SO479","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:23"},"so:480":{"book":"soolamani","num":480,"t":["மாலைதாழு மாடவாய் நிலத்தகத்து மங்கைமார்","வேலவாய நெடியகண் விலங்கிநின் றிலங்கலால்","சாலவாயி றாமெலாமொர் தாமரைத் தடத்திடை","நீலமாம லர்க்குழாநி ரந்தலர்ந்த நீரவே"],"p":"","r":"The women stand along the garlanded terraces with long spear-like eyes slanting, so that the roofs look like a lotus pool in which blue lilies have opened together.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 480","lb":"SO480","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:23"},"so:481":{"book":"soolamani","num":481,"t":["சுண்ணமாரி தூவுவார் தொடர்ந்துசேர்ந்து தோழிமார்","வண்ணவார வளைதயங்கு முன்கைமேல்வ ணங்குவார்","நண்ணிநா ணொழிந்துசென்று நம்பிமார்கள் முன்னரே","கண்ணிதம்மி னென்றிரந்து கொண்டுந்ன்று கண்ணுவார்"],"p":"","r":"The women with their companions shower perfumed powder, bow with bangled forearms, lose their shame and go before the princes to beg for a garland and a glance.","n":"The word கண்ணிதம்மின் is read as a plea for the princes' garland; the verse does not say it is given.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","naanam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 481","lb":"SO481","mm":1,"ev":"The city's women scatter perfumed powder and beg the princes for their garlands.","cl":[],"ag":["மடவரலார்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:23"},"so:482":{"book":"soolamani","num":482,"t":["பாடுவார்மு ரன்றபண்ம றந்தொர்வாறு பாடியும்","ஆடுவார்ம றந்தணிம யங்கியர்மை யாடியும்","சூடுவான்றொ டுத்த கோதை சூழ்குழன்ம றந்துகண்","நாடுவாய்நி ழற்கணிந்து நாணுவாரு மாயினார்"],"p":"","r":"In their longing the women sing and forget the tune, dance and forget the beat, leave their hair garlands unworn, and stand shy in the shade.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","naanam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 482","lb":"SO482","mm":1,"ev":"The women sing and dance in confusion before the princes and then draw back, shy.","cl":[],"ag":["மடவரலார்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:23"},"so:483":{"book":"soolamani","num":483,"t":["இட்டவில்லி ரட்டையுமி ரண்டுகெண்டை போல்பவும்","விட்டிலண்க்கு தொண்டையங் கனிப்பிழம்பொ டுள்விராய்ச்","சுட்டிசூட்ட ணிந்துசூளி மைமணிசு டர்ந்துனீள்","பட்டம்வேய்ந்த வட்டமல்ல தில்லைநல்ல பாங்கெலாம்"],"p":"","r":"The poet praises the women's brows like two strung bows, eyes like two carp, red lips and jewelled forehead bands, and says their beauty is not only in these.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 483","lb":"SO483","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:23"},"so:484":{"book":"soolamani","num":484,"t":["அலத்தகக்கு ழம்புதம்ம டித்தலத்தொர் பாகமா","நிலத்தலத்தொர் பாகமா நீடுவாயில் கூடுவார்","கலைத்தலைத்தொ டுத்தகோவை கண்ணெகிழ்ந்து சிந்தலான்","மலைத்தலைத்த ழற்சிதர்ந்த போன்றமாட வாயெலாம்"],"p":"","r":"The women gather at the long gate with lac-reddened soles and slipping girdles, so that the terraces look like a mountain where fire has scattered.","n":"The comparison of the terraces to a fire-scattered mountain is hard in lines 3 and 4 and is read loosely as an image of their glow.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 484","lb":"SO484","mm":1,"ev":"The women gather at the gate of the mansions, their girdles slipping.","cl":[],"ag":["மடவரலார்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:23"},"so:485":{"book":"soolamani","num":485,"t":["பாடகந்து ளங்கவும்பு சும்பொனோலை மின்னவும்","சூலகந்து ளும்பவஞ் சு ரும்புகழ்ந்து பாடவும்","ஊடகங்க சிந்தொசிந்து நின்றுசென்று வந்துலாய்","நாடகங்க ணன்னர்க்க ணங்கைமார்ந விற்றினார்"],"p":"","r":"With anklets ringing, gold ear-rolls shining and others praising them, the women move about the gate and perform a dance for the princes.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 485","lb":"SO485","mm":1,"ev":"The women dance before the princes at the gate of the mansions.","cl":[],"ag":["மடவரலார்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:23"},"so:486":{"book":"soolamani","num":486,"t":["மாலையால்வி ளங்கியும்பொன் வாசச்சுண்ணம் வீசியும்","சாலவாயி லாறுசந்த னக்குழம்பு சிந்தியும்","நீலவாணெ டுங்கணார்நி ரந்து நெஞ்சு தாழொரீஇ","ஞாலமாளு நம்பிமாரின் மாலுமாகி நண்ணினார்"],"p":"","r":"Wearing garlands, scattering gold powder and sandal paste, the blue-eyed women give up their hearts and join the princes who rule the earth in a mist of infatuation.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 486","lb":"SO486","mm":1,"ev":"The women, giving up their hearts, come to the princes.","cl":[],"ag":["மடவரலார்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:23"},"so:487":{"book":"soolamani","num":487,"t":["வேய்மறிந்த தோள்விளர்த்து வெவ்வுயிர்ப்பொ டுள்விராய்த்","தோமறிந்த சூழ்துகின்னெ கிழ்ந்துடுத்து வீழ்ந்தசைஇப்","பூமறிந்த தேங்குழன் முடிப்பொதிந்து வீழ்த்துலாய்த்","தாமறிந்த முல்லைவாய தாதுகுத்து டங்கினார்"],"p":"","r":"The women's shoulders grow pale, they sigh, their clothes slip and flowers fall from their hair, and they begin to languish as jasmine pollen scatters.","n":"The verse pictures the languor of love at first sight, with no absence or parting yet; the women pine for men who have only passed.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","pasalai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 487","lb":"SO487","mm":1,"ev":"The women, in love, grow pale and sigh.","cl":[],"ag":["மடவரலார்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:23"},"so:488":{"book":"soolamani","num":488,"t":["கொங்குவார்ம லர்த்தடத்த மர்ந்தகோதை மார்களோ","டங்கராகம் வீற்றிருந்த ணிந்தவார மாகுலாய்","மங்கைமார்கள் கண்ணும்வண்டு மாலையும னங்களும்","தங்குமார்பி னம்பிமார்க டானைசோலை சார்ந்ததே"],"p":"","r":"The troop of the princes, whose chests hold the women's eyes, bees, garlands and minds, arrives at the grove.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 488","lb":"SO488","mm":1,"ev":"The troop of the princes reaches the grove.","cl":[],"ag":["குமரர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:23"},"so:489":{"book":"soolamani","num":489,"t":["மானளாய நோக்கினார்ம னங்கலந்து பின்செல","வானளாய சோலைவாயின் மன்னவீரர் துன்னலலும்","கானளாய போதணிந்து காவிவிம்மு கள்ளளைஇத்","தேனளாவு வண்டுகொண்டு தெறல்சென் றெழுந்ததே"],"p":"","r":"The women follow in heart as the kings and heroes enter the sky-high grove of flowers, and the bees, drunk on honey, rise in a swarm.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 489","lb":"SO489","mm":1,"ev":"The princes and their heroes enter the grove, with the women's hearts following.","cl":[],"ag":["குமரர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:23"},"so:490":{"book":"soolamani","num":490,"t":["செம்முகப்ப சும்பொ னோடை வெண்மருப்பி ணைக்கரு","வெம்முகத்து வீழ்கடாத்து வேழநின்றி ழிந்தபின்","கைம்முகத்து வேலிலங்கு காமர் தாங்கொ லென்றுசென்","றம்முகத்து தும்பிவண்டு தேனொடாடி யார்த்தவே"],"p":"","r":"After the elephants with golden brow-plates and white tusks are left, the bees and beetles hum at the princes as if asking whether these are the spear-bearing lovers.","n":"Whether the princes dismount or the elephants simply stop is not clear from இழிந்த; read here as the princes alighting.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 490","lb":"SO490","mm":1,"ev":"The princes leave their elephants and the bees gather round them.","cl":[],"ag":["குமரர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:23"},"so:491":{"book":"soolamani","num":491,"t":["தாதுநின்ற தேறனீர் தளித்திவற்றின் மேலளி","கோதுகின்ற போதுகொண்டு சிந்திநம்பி மார்களை","மாதுநின்ற மாதவிக் கொடிகடந் தளிர்க்கையால்","போதுகென்றி டங்கள்காட்டு கின்றபோற்பொ லிந்தவே"],"p":"","r":"In the grove the madhavi creepers, as if with soft hands, show the princes the places among the flowers, while bees scatter pollen-water over them.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 491","lb":"SO491","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:23"},"so:492":{"book":"soolamani","num":492,"t":["போதுலாய வேரிமாரிஇ சாரலாய்ப்பொ ழிந்துதேன்","கோதலா னெரிந்துதாது கால்குடைந்து கொண்டுறீஇ","மாதுலாய வண்டிரைத்து மங்குல்கொண்டு கண்மறைத்","தேதிலார்க்கி யங்கலாவ தன்றுசோலை வண்ணமே"],"p":"","r":"The poet says the grove's colour cannot be told: flowers rain honey, bees hum, and clouds of blossoms hide the sky.","n":"Lines 2 to 4 are in compressed grammar and are read only for their general sense of a grove thick with bloom.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 492","lb":"SO492","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:23"},"so:493":{"book":"soolamani","num":493,"t":["போதுலாய பூம்பொதும்பர் மேலதென்றல் வீசலால்","தாதுலாய போதணிந்து தாழ்ந்துதாம வார்குழல்","மாதரார்கள் போலவல்லி மார்புபுல்லி மைந்தரைக்","காதலால்வ ளைப்பபோன்று காவினுட்க லந்தவே"],"p":"","r":"As the southern breeze blows through the flowering grove the vines clasp the trees, like women with garlanded hair embracing the young men they love.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 493","lb":"SO493","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:23"},"so:494":{"book":"soolamani","num":494,"t":["புல்லிவண்ட மர்ந்துகங்கு பூந்தழைப்பொ தும்பிடை","மல்லிகைக்கொ டிக்கலந்து மெளவல்சூட வெளவுநீர்","வல்லிமண்ட பங்கள்சென்று மாதவிக் கொழுந்தணி","அல்லிமண்ட பத்தயல சோகமாங்க ணெய்தினார்"],"p":"","r":"The princes pass the jasmine bowers and the madhavi arbours and reach the Ashoka tree beside the lotus bower.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","naadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 494","lb":"SO494","mm":1,"ev":"The princes walk through the grove's bowers and reach the Ashoka tree.","cl":[],"ag":["குமரர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:23"},"so:495":{"book":"soolamani","num":495,"t":["பஞ்சிலங்கு மல்குலார்ப லாண்டுகூற வாண்டுபோய்","மஞ்சிலங்க சோகநீழன் மன்னவீரர் துன்னலும்","விஞ்சையன்ம கிழ்ந்தெழுந்து வென்றிவீரர் தங்களுக்","கஞ்சலித்த டக்கைகூப்பி யார்வமிக்கி றைஞ்சினான்"],"p":"","r":"While the women call blessings, the princes reach the Ashoka shade and the Vidyadhara rises gladly, joins his hands and bows to them in eager love.","n":"The verse says only the Vidyadhara; he is taken as Maruci, the envoy the king chose at 427 and who reached the grove at 429.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["panivu","thoothu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 495","lb":"SO495","mm":1,"ev":"Maruci, the Vidyadhara envoy, rises and bows with joined hands to the princes under the Ashoka.","cl":[],"ag":["மருசி","குமரர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:23"},"so:496":{"book":"soolamani","num":496,"t":["ஆங்கவனி றைஞ்சலு மலர்ந்ததிங்க ணீளொளிப்","பூங்கழற்பொ லங்குழைப்பொ லிந்திலங்கு தாரினான்","நீங்கருங்கு குணத்தினீவிர் நீடுகுரவ ராதலில்","ஈங்கெமக்கு நீர்பணிந்த தென்னையென்றி யம்பினான்"],"p":"","r":"At his bow a bright-faced prince in earrings and garland asks him why he bows to them, since they should be the ones to bow to elders of faultless virtue.","n":"The verb is singular, so one prince speaks, and the verse does not say which; the id princes is used for the pair.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["panivu","kurippu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 496","lb":"SO496","mm":1,"ev":"A prince asks Maruci why he has bowed to them.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["குமரர்","மருசி"],"ch":"so:23"},"so:497":{"book":"soolamani","num":497,"t":["பானிறக்க திர்நகைப ரந்தசோதி யானையும்","நீனிறக்க ருங்கட னிகர்க்குமேனி யானையும்","வானெறிக்கண் வந்தவன்ம கிழ்ந்துகண்ம லர்ந்துதன்","நூனெறிக்கண் மிக்கநீர்மை யொக்கநின்று நோக்கினான்"],"p":"","r":"The Vidyadhara, come by the sky road, looks glad-eyed at one prince of milk-white radiance and one with a body like the dark sea, matching what he sees to the rules of his lore.","n":"நூல்நெறி is read as the lore by which marks of a person are judged; the verse does not name the marks.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kurippu","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 497","lb":"SO497","mm":1,"ev":"Maruci looks closely at the two princes, one fair and one dark.","cl":[],"ag":["மருசி"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:23"},"so:498":{"book":"soolamani","num":498,"t":["வேல்கொடானை வீரர்தம்மை விஞ்சையன் வியந்துநீள்","நூல்கொள்சிந்தை கண்கடாவ நோக்கிநோக்கி யார்காலன்","கால்கள்கொண்டு கண்ணிகாறு முண்மகிழ்ந்து கண்டுகண்","மால்கொள்சிந்தை யார்கள்போல மற்றுமற்று நோக்கினான்"],"p":"","r":"The Vidyadhara gazes at the spear-bearing heroes again and again, from their feet up to their garlands, his heart sunk in love like that of one who is lovesick.","n":"The reading of காலன்கால்கள்கொண்டு கண்ணிகாறும் as 'from the feet to the garland' is uncertain; the simile of the lovesick is the poet's.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","kurippu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 498","lb":"SO498","mm":1,"ev":"Maruci stares at the two princes from feet to garland.","cl":[],"ag":["மருசி"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:23"},"so:499":{"book":"soolamani","num":499,"t":["வேரிமாலை விம்மவும்வி ளங்குபூண்டு ளும்பவுந்","தாரொடார மின்னவுந்த யங்குசோதி கண்கொள","வாரநோக்க கில்லனன்ன னரசநம்பி மார்களைச்","சாரவாங்கொர் கற்றலத்தி ருந்துகான்வி ளம்பினான்"],"p":"","r":"With garlands and jewels gleaming so that the eye cannot take them in, the Vidyadhara draws near the princes and, sitting on a rock beside them, begins to speak.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thoothu","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 499","lb":"SO499","mm":1,"ev":"Maruci sits on a rock near the princes and begins to speak.","cl":[],"ag":["மருசி"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:23"},"so:500":{"book":"soolamani","num":500,"t":["செம்பொன்வான கட்டிழிந்து தெய்வ யானை யுண்மறைஇ","வம்புநீர்வ ரைப்பகம்வ ணக்கவந்த மாண்புடை","நம்பிமீர்க ணுங்கள்பாத நண்ணிநின் றி றைஞ்சுவார்","அம்பொன்மாலை மார்பினீர ருந்தவஞ்செய் தார்களே"],"p":"","r":"Maruci addresses the worthy princes who came down from the golden heights, hiding the divine elephant, to humble the sea-girt earth, and says those who bow at their feet have done hard penance.","n":"The speaker names no school; the claim is the envoy's courtesy. Its logic, that reaching the great is a fruit of earlier tapas, is the epic's own karma-thought, but the verse does not use the word for karma.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thavam","panivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 500","lb":"SO500","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["those who come to bow at the feet of the great must have done hard penance in the past","general","reported",4,"maruci"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["மருசி","குமரர்"],"ch":"so:23"},"so:501":{"book":"soolamani","num":501,"t":["திங்கள்வெண் கதிர்ச்சுடர்த் திலதவட்ட மென்றிரண்","டிங்கண்மா லுயிர்க்கெலாமெ ளிய்யவென்று தோன்றலும்","தங்கள்சோதி சாரலாவ வல்லவன்ன நீரவால்","எங்கண்முன்னை நுடங்கடன்மை யென்றுபின்னை யேத்தினான்"],"p":"","r":"Maruci praises the princes' radiance and the way it makes the world's beings feel gladly drawn to them, saying it cannot be matched, and goes on to hymn them.","n":"Lines 1 to 3 are in compressed grammar; the sense of praise is plain but the words that carry it are not fixed.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pugazh","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 501","lb":"SO501","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["மருசி","குமரர்"],"ch":"so:23"},"so:502":{"book":"soolamani","num":502,"t":["இமைகள்விட்ட நோக்கமேற வின்னபோல்வ சொல்லலு","மமைகமாற்றம் நூம்மை யெங்க ளடிகள்காண வேகுவாம்","சுமைகொண்மாலை தொடுகளிற்றெ ருத்தமேறு கென்றனர்","சிமைகொடேவர் போலநின்று திகழுகின்ற சோதியார்"],"p":"","r":"The princes, their gaze bright, say that talk is enough, that they will go to see their lord, and tell him to mount the elephant.","n":"The word அடிகள் may name the princes' father or another lord; the verse does not say which, and the one invited to ride is read as Maruci.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thoothu","panivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 502","lb":"SO502","mm":1,"ev":"The princes tell Maruci that they will go to see their lord and ask him to ride.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["குமரர்","மருசி"],"ch":"so:23"},"so:503":{"book":"soolamani","num":503,"t":["அம்பொன்மாலை கண் கவர்ந்த லர்ந்தசெல்வ வெள்ளமேய்","வெம்புமால்க ளிற்றெருத்தம் விஞ்சையாளன் மேல்கொளப்","பைம்பொன்மாலை வார்மதப்ப ரூஉக்கை யீரு வாக்கண்மீச்","செம்பொன்மாலை மார்பசேர்ந்து தேவரிற்று ளும்பினார்"],"p":"","r":"The two princes, with the Vidyadhara beside them, ride the neck of a rutting elephant in a flood of splendour that holds every eye, and shine like gods.","n":"Who sits where on the elephant turns on a compressed second line, so the sense is the likely one only.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["selvam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 503","lb":"SO503","mm":1,"ev":"Tivittan and Vijayan ride a rutting elephant with the Vidyadhara envoy toward Podanam.","cl":[],"ag":["செங்கண் ணெடியான்","விசயன்","மருசி"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:24"},"so:504":{"book":"soolamani","num":504,"t":["கதிர்நகைக் கபாட வாயிற் கதலிகைக் கனக நெற்றி","மதிநக வுரிஞ்சு கோட்டு மாளிகை நிரைத்த வீதிப்","புதுநக ரிழைத்து முத்து பொலங்கலத் தொகையும் பூவும்","எதிர்நகைத் துகைத்து மாத ரெதிர்கொள நகரஞ் சேர்ந்தார்"],"p":"","r":"The city has been decked with gates, plantain-flags, gold-topped mansions that rub the moon, and the women meet the princes with pearls, gold vessels and flowers as they enter.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","virundhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 504","lb":"SO504","mm":1,"ev":"The princes enter the decked city while women welcome them with pearls, gold vessels and flowers.","cl":[],"ag":["செங்கண் ணெடியான்","விசயன்","மாதர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:24"},"so:505":{"book":"soolamani","num":505,"t":["விரைக்கதி ரலங்கற் செங்கேழ் விண்ணியங் கொருவ னோடும்","வரைக்கெதிர்ந் திலங்கு மார்பின் மன்னவ குமரச் செல்வர்","எரிக்கதி ரேற்றைக் கால மெழுநிலாப் பருவ மேக","நிரைத்தெழு மிருது மன்று நிரந்ததோர் சவிய ரானார்"],"p":"","r":"With the one who travels the sky, the sons of the kings, broad-chested and garlanded, take on a radiance that spreads like the bright season of the rising moon and the rows of clouds.","n":"The comparison in the last two lines is compressed and its season-words are hard to fix.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 505","lb":"SO505","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["செங்கண் ணெடியான்","விசயன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:24"},"so:506":{"book":"soolamani","num":506,"t":["வார்கலந் திலங்கு கொம்மை வனமுலை மகளி ரிட்ட","ஏர்கலந் தெழுந்த தூம வியன்புகை கழுமி நான","நீர்கலந் துகுத்த மாலை நிறமதுத் திவலை சிந்தக்","கார்கலந் திருண்ட போலுங் கண்ணகன் தெருவுட் சென்றார்"],"p":"","r":"The princes pass down the broad street where incense smoke from women's bowls and drops of honey from garlands soaked in perfume make the air as dark as a gathering cloud.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","virundhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 506","lb":"SO506","mm":1,"ev":"The princes ride down the wide street through incense smoke and dripping garlands.","cl":[],"ag":["செங்கண் ணெடியான்","விசயன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:24"},"so:507":{"book":"soolamani","num":507,"t":["தெளிர்முத்த மணலுஞ் செம்பொற் சுண்ணமுஞ் சிதர்ந்து தீந்தேன்","தளிர்முத்த மலரும் போதும் சாந்தமு வண்டார்","ஒளிர்முத்த முறுவ லார்த முழைக்கலங் கலந்து மாலைக்","குளிர்முத்த நிழற்றுங் கோயிற் பெருங்கடை குறுகச் சென்றார்"],"p":"","r":"Over pearl sand, gold powder, flowers and sandal strewn by bright-toothed women, they come close to the great gate of the palace, which is hung with cool pearl garlands.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 507","lb":"SO507","mm":1,"ev":"The princes reach the palace's great gate.","cl":[],"ag":["செங்கண் ணெடியான்","விசயன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:24"},"so:508":{"book":"soolamani","num":508,"t":["மற்றவை ரடைந்த போழ்கின் வாயிலோ ருணர்த்தக் கேட்டு","கொற்றவ னருவி தூங்குங் குளிர்மணிக் குன்றம் போல","முற்றிநின் றிலங்குஞ் செம்பொன் முடிமிசை முத்த மாலைக்","கற்றைகள் தவழச் சென்றோர் கனககூ டத்தி ருந்தான்"],"p":"","r":"When the gatekeepers announce them, the king, like a jewelled mountain crowned with a falling stream, goes in with strands of pearl lying over his gold crown and sits in the golden hall.","n":"The king is not named here; he is read as Payapati from 509's 'king who gave kindness to the world' and the sarukkam's earlier verses.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","avai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 508","lb":"SO508","mm":1,"ev":"The gatekeepers announce the arrivals and the king takes his seat in the golden hall.","cl":[],"ag":["வாயிலோர்","வேந்தன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:24"},"so:509":{"book":"soolamani","num":509,"t":["மன்னவ குமர ரோடும் விஞ்சையன் மகிழ்ந்து வையத்","தின்னருள் புரிந்த வேந்த னிடையறிந் தினிதி னெய்திக்","கன்னவில் தோளி னான்றன் கழலடி தொழுது நின்றான்","அன்னவர்க் கிருக்கைத் தான மரசனு மருளிச் செய்தான்"],"p":"","r":"The Vidyadhara, glad, enters with the princes at the right moment and bows at the king's feet, and the king graciously gives them places to sit.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["panivu","virundhu","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 509","lb":"SO509","mm":1,"ev":"The envoy bows to the king, who offers seats to the envoy and the princes.","cl":[],"ag":["மருசி","வேந்தன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:24"},"so:510":{"book":"soolamani","num":510,"t":["வீரியக் குமர ரொடும் விஞ்சையஞ் செல்வ னோடும்","காரியக் கிழவர் சூழக் கவின்றுகண் குளிரத் தோன்றி","ஆரியன் னலர்ந்த சோதி யருங்கலப் பீட நெற்றிக்","தாரகை யணிந்து தோன்றுஞ் சந்திர சவிய னானன்"],"p":"","r":"The king shows himself with the brave princes, the Vidyadhara and his counsellors around him, as the moon gleams among stars on the top of the jewelled seat.","n":"The simile of line 4 makes the king the moon; the rest of the verse is a compressed list of those who sit around him.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["arasu","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 510","lb":"SO510","mm":1,"ev":"The king takes his place among princes, envoy and counsellors.","cl":[],"ag":["வேந்தன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:24"},"so:511":{"book":"soolamani","num":511,"t":["அலகையில் தானை வேந்த னம்பர சரனை நோக்கி","உலகுப சார மாற்ற முரைத்தலுக் குரிய கூறி","விலகிய கதிர வாகி விளங்கொளிக் கடகக் கையான்","மலரகங் கழுமப் போந்து மனமகிழ்ந் திருந்த போழ்தின்"],"p":"","r":"The king, looking at the sky-goer, speaks the courtesies that fit the occasion, and the bracelet-armed envoy sits in joy as the grove of the hall fills with his presence.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["virundhu","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 511","lb":"SO511","mm":1,"ev":"The king greets the envoy with courtesies and the envoy sits pleased.","cl":[],"ag":["வேந்தன்","மருசி"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:24"},"so:512":{"book":"soolamani","num":512,"t":["விஞ்சைய னெழுந்து தங்கோன் வெள்ளிவே தண்ட நோக்கி","அஞ்சலித் தடக்கை கூப்பி யரக்கிலச் சினையின் வைத்த","எஞ்சலி லோலை காட்ட விறைமகன் குறிப்பு நோக்கி","வஞ்சமில் வயங்கு கேள்வி மதிரவன் கரத்தில் வாங்கி"],"p":"","r":"The envoy rises, faces his own lord's ridge, folds his hands and shows the letter laid in a lac seal; reading the prince's sign, a learned counsellor takes it from his hand.","n":"Whether 'the lord's son' of line 3 is Tivittan, whose sign the counsellor reads, or another prince is not fixed by the verse.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thoothu","kurippu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 512","lb":"SO512","mm":1,"ev":"The envoy shows the sealed letter and the counsellor Mathiravan takes it.","cl":[],"ag":["மருசி","மதிரவன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:24"},"so:513":{"book":"soolamani","num":513,"t":["நிகரிகந் தழகி தாகி நெரிவடுப் படாத வேழப்","புகர்முகப் பொறிய தாய புகழ்ந்தசொல் லகத்துப் போகா","மகரவாய் மணிகட் செப்பின் மசிகலந் தெழுதப் பட்ட","பகரரும் பதங்கள் நோக்கிப் பயின்று பின் வாசிக் கின்றான்"],"p":"","r":"The counsellor studies the ink-written words from the jewelled casket, lines that are fair, unmatched and unbroken by creases, and then reads them aloud.","n":"Lines 1 and 2 are hard to construe; the description of the leaf and the casket is the likely sense.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thoothu","kalvi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 513","lb":"SO513","mm":1,"ev":"Mathiravan examines the letter's writing and begins to read it aloud.","cl":[],"ag":["மதிரவன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:24"},"so:514":{"book":"soolamani","num":514,"t":["போதனத் திறைவன் காண்க விரதநூ புரத்தை யாளும்","காதுவேன் மன்ன னோலை கழலவன் றனக்கு நாளும்","ஆதிய வடிசி லொண்கே ழஞ்சன முள்ளிட் டெல்லாம்","தீதுதீர் காப்புப் பெற்றுச் செல்கென விடுத்த தன்றே"],"p":"","r":"The letter of the king who rules Rathanupura addresses the lord of Podanam, and sends for the one of the warrior anklets food and ointment and the rest, free of harm, as his protection for the journey.","n":"The name of the writer in line 2 and the sense of line 4 are doubtful; the verse is read as the opening of the letter's words.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thoothu","virundhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 514","lb":"SO514","mm":1,"ev":"The Vidyadhara king's letter, read aloud, greets the lord of Podanam and sends gifts.","cl":[],"ag":["விஞ்சையர் வேந்தன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["விஞ்சையர் வேந்தன்","வேந்தன்"],"ch":"so:24"},"so:515":{"book":"soolamani","num":515,"t":["அல்லதூஉங் கரும தலங்குதா ரிவுளித் திண்டேர்","வல்லக னிளைய நம்பிக் குரியளா வழங்கப் பட்டாள்","மல்லக மார்பி னன்றான் மருமக ளிவளைக் கூவி","வல்லிதிற் கொடுக்க மன்னன் வாழ்கதன் கண்ணி மாதோ"],"p":"","r":"The letter says that the girl is given as the younger prince's own and asks the strong-chested king to call her his daughter-in-law and take her, wishing him and his garland long life.","n":"The offer is in the letter's voice, and the letter is the speech of the absent Vidyadhara king; who 'gives' and who 'takes' in line 4 is not made plain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["makkal","kudi","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 515","lb":"SO515","mm":1,"ev":"The letter offers the Vidyadhara king's daughter to the younger prince.","cl":[],"ag":["விஞ்சையர் வேந்தன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["விஞ்சையர் வேந்தன்","வேந்தன்"],"ch":"so:24"},"so:516":{"book":"soolamani","num":516,"t":["என்றவ னோலைவாசித் திருந்தன னிறைவன் கேட்டு","வென்றியம் பெருமை விச்சா தரரென்பா ரெம்மின் மிக்கார்","இன்றிவன் விடுத்த திவ்வா றென்கொலோ வென்று சிந்தித்","தொன்றுமற் றுரைக்க மாட்டா திருந்தன னுரங்கொ டோ ளான்"],"p":"","r":"The reading done, the king, thinking that Vidyadharas are greater than his own kind, wonders what lies behind this message and sits unable to say a word.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maanam","thelivu","perumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 516","lb":"SO516","mm":1,"ev":"The king, puzzled, sits silent after the letter is read.","cl":[["Vidyadharas are greater than men of the earth","general","reported",2,"arasan"]],"ag":["மதிரவன்","வேந்தன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:24"},"so:517":{"book":"soolamani","num":517,"t":["தீட்டருந் திலதக் கண்ணிச் செறிகழ லரசர் கோமான்","மீட்டுரை கொடாது சால விம்மலோ டிருப்ப நோக்கி","வாட்டரும் பெருமை யெங்கோ னோலையை மதியா வாறென்","றோட்டருங் கதத்த னாகிக்கேசர னுரைக்க லுற்றான்"],"p":"","r":"Seeing the king sit sighing without a reply, the envoy, unstoppable in his anger, begins to say that he cannot see why a letter of his unquenchable lord is not honoured.","n":"The envoy is named by the sky-goer's title; the identification with Marusi rests on 427 to 429 and on the envoy's arrival at 505 and not on a name in this verse.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vekuli","thoothu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 517","lb":"SO517","mm":1,"ev":"The envoy angrily asks the silent king why he does not honour his lord's letter.","cl":[],"ag":["மருசி","வேந்தன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["மருசி","வேந்தன்"],"ch":"so:24"},"so:518":{"book":"soolamani","num":518,"t":["முன்னமோர் கருமம் வேண்டி மொழிபவேல் மனிதர் தம்மால்","என்னவ ரேனு மாக விகழ்ந்திடப் படுப போலாம்","அன்னதே யுலக வார்த்தை யாவதின் றறியும் வண்ணம்","மின்னவின் றிலங்கும் வேலோய் நின்னுழை விளங்கிற் றன்றே"],"p":"","r":"The envoy says that the world's saying is shown true in the king: when a man speaks wanting something, whoever he may be, he is slighted.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sol","pazhi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 518","lb":"SO518","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["one who comes asking for something is slighted, whoever he is","general","reported",1,"maruci"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["மருசி","வேந்தன்"],"ch":"so:24"},"so:519":{"book":"soolamani","num":519,"t":["பூவிரி யுருவக் கண்ணிப் பொலங்குழை யிலங்கு சோதித்","தேவரே யெனினுந் தோறச் சில்பகல் செல்ப வாயில்","ஏவரே போல நோக்கி யிகழ்ந்துரைத் தெழுவ தன்றே","மாவிரி தானை மன்னா மனிதர தியற்கை யென்றான்"],"p":"","r":"He adds that even gods, once they fall and a few days pass, are looked on and scorned like anyone, and calls this the way of men.","n":"Line 2's wording is hard, so the sense of falling and slighting is the likely one; the envoy speaks this as an accusation of the king's manner, not as a rule he lays down.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","manam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 519","lb":"SO519","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["even gods, when they fall, are scorned like anyone; this is human nature","general","reported",4,"maruci"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["மருசி","வேந்தன்"],"ch":"so:24"},"so:520":{"book":"soolamani","num":520,"t":["வரைமலி வயங்கு தோளாய் வியாதியான் மயங்கி னார்க்குச்","சுரைமலி யமிர்தத் தீம்பால் சுவைதெரிந் துண்ண லாமோ","விரைமலி விளங்கு பைந்தார் விஞ்சையர் செல்வந் தானும்","நுரைமலி பொள்ளல் யாக்கை மனித்தர்க்கு நுகர லாமோ"],"p":"","r":"The envoy asks whether a man sick with disease can taste the sweet milk-nectar, or men whose body is a frothy hollow can enjoy the wealth of the Vidyadharas.","n":"The body-as-froth image is the one the Naladiyar uses to teach detachment; here it is used as an insult in a rank argument, and no doctrine of release is stated.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","selvam","perumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 520","lb":"SO520","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["the Vidyadharas' wealth cannot be enjoyed by men, whose bodies are frothy hollows","general","reported",3,"maruci"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["மருசி","வேந்தன்"],"ch":"so:24"},"so:521":{"book":"soolamani","num":521,"t":["அறவிய மனத்த ரன்றி யழுங்குத லியல்பி னார்க்குப்","பிறவியை யறுக்குங் காட்சிப் பெருநிலை யெய்த லாமோ","வெறிமயங் குருவக் கண்ணி விஞ்சையர் விளங்கு தானம்","மறவியின் மயங்கி வாழும் மனித்தர்க்கு நிகழ்த்த லாமோ"],"p":"","r":"He asks whether those who lack a mind of dharma and are bent on wasting can reach the great state of insight that cuts birth, and whether Vidyadharas' place can be told to men living in forgetfulness.","n":"The birth-cutting vision is used only as an analogy for what men cannot reach; the verse does not say which school's insight it means, and it is spoken by a Vidyadhara in an argument about rank.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["piravi","aram","sirumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 521","lb":"SO521","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["the great state of insight that cuts birth cannot be won by those without a mind of dharma","general","reported",1,"maruci"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["மருசி","வேந்தன்"],"ch":"so:24"},"so:522":{"book":"soolamani","num":522,"t":["அருங்கடி கமழுந் தாரை யழிமதக் களிற்றி னாற்றல்","மரங்கெடத் தின்று வாழுங் களபக்கு மதிக்க லாமோ","இரங்கிடு சிறுபுன் வாழ்க்கை யிந்நிலத் தவர்கட் கென்றும்","வரங்கிடந் தெய்த லாமோ மற்றெமர் பெருமை மன்னா"],"p":"","r":"He asks whether a young elephant that lives by eating trees can be set beside the strength of a rutting elephant, and whether men of this earth, of a pitiable small life, can win by waiting the greatness of his people.","n":"The word in line 2 for the animal is not clear (களபக்கு); the comparison is the likely sense.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sirumai","perumai","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 522","lb":"SO522","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["men of this earth, of a small life, cannot win the greatness of the Vidyadharas by waiting for a boon","general","reported",3,"maruci"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["மருசி","வேந்தன்"],"ch":"so:24"},"so:523":{"book":"soolamani","num":523,"t":["உள்ளிய மரங்கொள் சோலை மண்மிசை யுறையு மாந்தர்","ஒள்ளிய ரேனுந் தக்க துணர்பவ ரில்லை போலாம்","வெள்ளியஞ் சிலம்பி னென்கோன் விடுத்தே யேது வாக","எள்ளியோ ருரையு மீயா திருந்தனை யிறைவ வென்றான்"],"p":"","r":"He says that men who live on earth, even the clever, seem not to know what is fitting, and rebukes the king for not giving even a word of answer after his lord on the silver ridge sent the letter.","n":"Lines 3 and 4 are compressed; the rebuke is for the king's silence.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pedhaimai","sirumai","sol"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 523","lb":"SO523","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["men on earth, even the clever, do not know what is fitting","general","reported",2,"maruci"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["மருசி","வேந்தன்"],"ch":"so:24"},"so:524":{"book":"soolamani","num":524,"t":["ஆங்கவ னுரைப்பக் கேட்டே யம்பர சரனை நோக்கித்","தேங்கம ழலங்கன் மார்ப சிவந்துரை யாடல் வேண்டா","ஓங்கிய வோலை மாற்றக் குரியவா றுரைக்க மாட்டா","தீங்கியா னிருந்த தென்றா னெரிசுடர் வயிரப் பூணான்"],"p":"","r":"The king, hearing him, turns to the sky-goer and asks him not to speak in anger, saying that he has stayed here because he cannot say what a fitting reply to so grand a letter would be.","n":"The speaker is not named in the verse; the diamond-ornamented man of line 4 is read as the king whose silence is the cause of 516 and 517.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vekuli","panivu","sol"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 524","lb":"SO524","mm":1,"ev":"The king asks the envoy not to be angry and says he cannot frame a fitting reply.","cl":[],"ag":["வேந்தன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["வேந்தன்","மருசி"],"ch":"so:24"},"so:525":{"book":"soolamani","num":525,"t":["வெஞ்சுடர் தெறுதீ விச்சா தரரென்பார் மிக்க நீரார்","செஞ்சுடர்த் திலதக் கண்ணித் தேவரே தெரியுங் காலை","மஞ்சிடை மண்ணுள் வாழும் மக்களுக் கவர்க டம்மோ","டெஞ்சிய தொடர்ச்சி இன்ப மெய்துதற் கரிது மாதோ"],"p":"","r":"The king says that the Vidyadharas are fiery, great, and no less than gods when examined, so that for men who live under the clouds on the earth a bond and joy with them is hard to gain.","n":"The king grants the premise of the envoy's pride without agreeing to the match; the work leaves the claim unanswered here.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["perumai","sirumai","samanilai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 525","lb":"SO525","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["a bond with Vidyadharas, who are god-like, is hard for men of the earth to gain","general","reported",3,"arasan"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["வேந்தன்","மருசி"],"ch":"so:24"},"so:526":{"book":"soolamani","num":526,"t":["ஈட்டிய வூன்செய் யாக்கை யெம்முழை யின்ன வாறு","வாட்டமில் வயங்கு கண்ணி மணிமுடி மன்ன னோலை","காட்டிநீ யுரைத்த வெல்லாங் கனவெனக் கருதி னல்லான்","மீட்டது மெய்ம்மை யாக வியந்துரை விரிக்க லாமோ"],"p":"","r":"The king says that this body made of flesh is as it is, and that he would take all that the envoy has said, showing the letter of the garlanded king, to be a dream, since he cannot rightly marvel at it and speak it out as true.","n":"The dream is used for what is too good to be believed, though the court has just treated a dream as a true sign (106 to 112).","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kanavu","manam","thelivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 526","lb":"SO526","mm":1,"ev":"The king tells the envoy he can only take the offer as a dream.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["வேந்தன்","மருசி"],"ch":"so:24"},"so:527":{"book":"soolamani","num":527,"t":["இன்னவ னின்ன நீரா னின்னவே யெய்து கென்று","முன்னவன் செய்த மொய்ம்பின் வினைகளே முயல்வ தல்லால்","பின்னவன் பிறந்து தன்னாற் பெறுதலுக் குரிய வாய","துன்னுவ தென்றுக் கான்று துணியுமோ சொல்ல வென்றான்"],"p":"","r":"The king says that only the strong deeds a man did in an earlier birth strive to bring each person his lot, and asks whether one born later could by his own doing gain what is not due to him.","n":"The king's use of karma here is a way to hold the Vidyadharas' wealth out of reach of men; Marusi gives way in 529, and then argues in 531 that Vidyadharas are men, so the objection is met by another route and not by denying karma. Speaker taken from 529, which names the king's words; lines 3 and 4 are hard to construe.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["oozh","vinai","muyarchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 527","lb":"SO527","mm":1,"ev":"The king answers Marusi by saying that earlier deeds alone decide what each man reaches.","cl":[["the deeds of a former birth alone work out what each person gains, and effort in the later birth does not bring what they have not earned","general","asserted",2,"arasan"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["வேந்தன்","மருசி"],"ch":"so:25"},"so:528":{"book":"soolamani","num":528,"t":["மெய்ப்புடை தெரிந்து மேலை விழுத்தவம் முயன்று நோற்றார்க்","கொப்புடைத் துங்கள் சேரி யுயர்நிலைச் செல்வ மெல்லாம்","எப்படி முயறு மேனு மெங்களுக் கெய்த லாகா","தப்படி நீயு முன்னர் மொழிந்தனை யன்றே யென்றான்"],"p":"","r":"The king says that the high-state wealth of the Vidyadhara quarter belongs to those who understood the truth and practised higher austerity, that no effort of ours could reach it, and that Marusi had said so himself earlier.","n":"The reference to Marusi's own earlier words points to verses before this chapter's first; they are not in this chapter, so what he said is not checked here. The word எங்களுக்கு is read as men in general.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thavam","oozh","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 528","lb":"SO528","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["the great wealth of the high state falls to those who earlier practised austerity and cannot be won by later effort","general","asserted",1,"arasan"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["வேந்தன்","மருசி"],"ch":"so:25"},"so:529":{"book":"soolamani","num":529,"t":["இறைவனாங் குரைத்த சொற்கேட் டென்னைபா வம்பொ ருந்தாக்","கறையவா மொழிகள் சொன்னேன் காவலன் கருதிற் றோரேன்","பொறையினாற் பெரியன் பூபன் சிறியன்யா னென்று நாணி","அறிவினாற் பெரிய நீரா னவிந்தன கதத்த னானான்"],"p":"","r":"Hearing the king's words, Marusi is ashamed that he spoke unfit words without grasping the king's mind, calls the king great in forbearance and himself small, and his anger dies down.","n":"Who it is that is angry and whose words are called unfit are not stated outright, and the sentence in lines 1 to 2 is compressed; the reading follows 527 and 528 as the king's rebuke and Marusi's yielding.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["porai","naanam","panivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 529","lb":"SO529","mm":1,"ev":"Marusi, abashed by the king's reply, gives up his anger.","cl":[["a king is made great by forbearance","general","reported",3,"maruci"]],"ag":["மருசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["மருசி",""],"ch":"so:25"},"so:530":{"book":"soolamani","num":530,"t":["கிளர்ந்தொளி துளும்பும் மேனிக் கேசர ரோடு மண்மேல்","வளர்ந்தொளி திவளும் பூணோர் மணவினை முயங்க லில்லென்","றளந்தறி வரிய சீரோற் கையமீ தகற்று கென்றாங்","குளர்ந்துன னுணர்வி னூக்கி யுரைக்கிய வெடுத்துக் கூறும்"],"p":"","r":"The doubt is stated that Vidyadharas of shining bodies and the ornamented men of the earth do not join in marriage, and that this doubt be removed for the one of unmeasured glory; Marusi, stirring his understanding, sets out to answer.","n":"The claim is put in order to be answered, and 531 answers it by saying that Vidyadharas are men. The verse does not say who holds the doubt; the king is the likely holder, since it is he who must be won over.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["therithal","thelivu","thoothu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 530","lb":"SO530","mm":1,"ev":"The objection that Vidyadharas and men do not marry is raised, and Marusi begins his reply.","cl":[["Vidyadharas and men of the earth do not join in marriage","general","refuted",2,"arasan"]],"ag":["மருசி"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:25"},"so:531":{"book":"soolamani","num":531,"t":["மஞ்சிவர் மணங்கொள் சோலை மணிவரைச் சென்னி வாழும்","விஞ்சையர் விச்சை யாலே விழுமிய ரென்ப தல்லால்","அஞ்சலில் தானை வேந்தே மனிதரே யவரும் யாதும்","வெஞ்சுடர் விளங்கு வேலோய் வேற்றுமை யின்மை கேண்மோ"],"p":"","r":"Marusi tells the fearless king that the Vidyadharas of the jewelled mountain are excellent only by their lore, and are otherwise just men, so that there is no difference between them and the king's people.","n":"The verse says where the difference lies, in lore, and that lore is not a difference of birth; it does not say what makes the claim true beyond Marusi's word, and the work later has the marriage go forward.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalvi","samanilai","pirappu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 531","lb":"SO531","mm":1,"ev":"Marusi answers the doubt by saying Vidyadharas are men, set apart only by their learning.","cl":[["Vidyadharas are not a different kind from men, and differ only in their lore","general","asserted",2,"maruci"]],"ag":["மருசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["மருசி","வேந்தன்"],"ch":"so:25"},"so:532":{"book":"soolamani","num":532,"t":["மண்ணவில் முழவின் மாநீர்ப் பவபுர முடைய மன்னன்","பண்ணவில் களிதல் யானைப் பவனவே கற்குத் தேவி","கண்ணவில் வடிவிற் காந்தி மதியவள் பாவை","வண்ணவிற் புருவ வாட்கண் வாயுமா வேகை யென்பான்"],"p":"","r":"Marusi begins to name his own king's house: the lord of the drum-sounding, water-ringed city of Pavapuram, his queen and a woman of dazzling beauty whose name is given as Vayuvegai.","n":"The word division makes it unclear which name belongs to the king, which to the queen and which to the daughter; the same name Vayuvegai is the queen's in the earlier chapters.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thoothu","kudi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 532","lb":"SO532","mm":1,"ev":"Marusi introduces the Vidyadhara king's city and royal house.","cl":[],"ag":["மருசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["மருசி","வேந்தன்"],"ch":"so:25"},"so:533":{"book":"soolamani","num":533,"t":["மற்றவ ளோடும் வந்தேன் மன்னன்யான் மருசி யென்பேன்","அற்றமில் கேள்வி யெந்தை யஞ்சுமா னென்னும் பேரான்","பெற்றதா யருசி மாலை பெருமக னருளினால் யான்","கற்றநூல் பல்ல வாகுங் கருமணிக் கடகக் கையான்"],"p":"","r":"Marusi says that he came with her, gives his name, his learned father Anjuman and his mother Arusimalai, and says that by the great lord's grace the books he learned are many.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thoothu","kalvi","kudi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 533","lb":"SO533","mm":1,"ev":"Marusi names himself and his parents to the king.","cl":[],"ag":["மருசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["மருசி","வேந்தன்"],"ch":"so:25"},"so:534":{"book":"soolamani","num":534,"t":["அலகைசா லாதி காலத் தரசர்கள் தொடர்ச்சி யெல்லாம்","உலகநூல் பலவு மோதி யுணர்ந்தன னுரைப்பக் கேண்மோ","விலகிய கதிர வாகி விடுசுடர் வயிரக் கோலத்","திலகம்வீற் றிருந்த கண்ணித் திருமுடிச் செல்வ என்றான்"],"p":"","r":"Marusi says he has read in many world-books the whole succession of kings of the earliest times, and asks the crowned king to hear it.","n":"The account that follows is his own recital of what books tell, so its traditions are reported through him.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalvi","thoothu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 534","lb":"SO534","mm":1,"ev":"Marusi announces that he will recount the descent of kings from the books he has studied.","cl":[],"ag":["மருசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["மருசி","வேந்தன்"],"ch":"so:25"},"so:535":{"book":"soolamani","num":535,"t":["ஆதிநா ளரசர் தங்க ளருங்குல மைந்து மாக்கி","ஓதநீ ருலகின் மிக்க வொழுக்கமுந் தொழிலுந் தோற்றித்","தீதுதீர்ந் திருந்த பெம்மான் திருவடி சாரச் சென்று","நீதி நூற் றுலகம் காத்து நிலத்திரு மலர நின்றான்"],"p":"","r":"Marusi says that in the first days the first lord set up the five great royal lineages, made known their conduct and occupations, then went to the feet of the faultless lord and guarded the world by the book of justice.","n":"The verse does not name the first lord; it is taken as the same figure the earlier chapter calls the first lord, and the sentence does not make clear whether the faultless lord to whom he goes is the same person or another.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kudi","ozhukkam","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 535","lb":"SO535","mm":1,"ev":"The first king establishes the five lineages, with their conduct and occupations, and then rules justly.","cl":[["the first lord ordained the five royal lineages together with their conduct and occupations","jain","reported",2,"maruci"],["a king guards the world by the book of justice","general","asserted",4,"maruci"]],"ag":["இறைவன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["மருசி","வேந்தன்"],"ch":"so:25"},"so:536":{"book":"soolamani","num":536,"t":["முசிநாச் சுரும்பு பாய முருகுடைத் துருக்குஞ் சோலைக்","காசிநாட் டரசன் செங்கோற் கதிர்முடிக் கச்ச னென்பான்","மாசினாற் கடலந் தானை மன்னவற் றவற்குத் தேவி","தூசினாற் றுளும்பு மல்குல் சுதஞ்சனை சுடரும் பூணாய்"],"p":"","r":"Marusi names Kachchan, the Kasi king of the just sceptre, whose queen is Suthanjanai of the jewelled ornaments.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kudi","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 536","lb":"SO536","mm":1,"ev":"Marusi names the king of Kasi and his queen.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["மருசி","வேந்தன்"],"ch":"so:25"},"so:537":{"book":"soolamani","num":537,"t":["வேய்ந்தக நிழற்றுங் கோதை மிளிர்மணிக் கலாப வட்டம்","போந்தகந் திகழ்ந்து மின்னுப் பூந்துகில் பொலிந்த வல்குல்","வாய்ந்தகங் கமழுங் கோதை யவள்பெற்ற வரச சிங்க","நாந்தகக் கிழவர் கோவே நமியென்பான் நலத்தின் மிக்கான்"],"p":"","r":"Marusi says that their son was Nami, a prince of the Nandaka-sword line, excelling in goodness.","n":"The phrase நாந்தகக் கிழவர் is read as the swordsmen's line; the sense is clear but the epithet is rare.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kudi","perumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 537","lb":"SO537","mm":1,"ev":"Marusi names Nami as the son of the king of Kasi.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["மருசி","வேந்தன்"],"ch":"so:25"},"so:538":{"book":"soolamani","num":538,"t":["அங்கவ னரசு வேண்டா னற்கடல் படைத்த நாதன்","பங்கயங் கமழு மேனி பவித்திர பரம யோகி","தங்கிய தியானப் போழ்தி றாழ்ந்துதன் றடக்கை கூப்பிப்","பொங்கிய காதல் கூரப் பாடினன் புலமை மிக்கான்"],"p":"","r":"There Nami, who is said not to want a kingdom, bowed his head in the pure supreme yogi's steady meditation, joined his broad hands and, swelling with love, sang, being rich in learning.","n":"The line அரசு வேண்டான் says he did not want a kingdom, yet in 547 he asks for wealth; either the first words mean he did not ask the kingdom of the yogi, or the line is not sound. The yogi is not named.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","panivu","thiyaanam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 538","lb":"SO538","mm":1,"ev":"Nami bows before the meditating yogi and sings to him.","cl":[],"ag":["நமி"],"ec":[],"spk":["மருசி","வேந்தன்"],"ch":"so:25"},"so:539":{"book":"soolamani","num":539,"t":["அலகிலா ஞானத் தகத்தடங்க நுங்கி","உலகெலாம் நின்று னொளித்தாயு நீயே","ஒளித்தாயு நீயே யுயிர்க்கெலாங் கண்ணா","யளித்தாயுங் காத்தாயு நீயேவாழி யறவேந்தே"],"p":"","r":"Nami sings that the Lord took in the whole world within his unlimited knowledge, hid it and is its guardian and protector, eyes to all lives, and that he is the king of dharma.","n":"The hymn's refrain is நீயே, you yourself; its second and third lines repeat each other's words.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","arivu","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 539","lb":"SO539","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["the Lord holds the whole world within his unbounded knowledge and guards it as the king of dharma","jain","reported",1,"nami"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நமி","இறைவன்"],"ch":"so:25"},"so:540":{"book":"soolamani","num":540,"t":["அழனாறும் வெங்கதிரோ னாண வலராது","நிழனாறு மூர்த்தியாய் நின்றாயு நீயே","நின்றாயு நீயே நிறைபொரு ளெல்லைக்கட்","சென்றாயும் வென்றாயு நீயேவாழி திருமாலே"],"p":"","r":"Nami sings that the Lord stood so that the hot-rayed sun was put to shame, that he has gone to the limit of all things and conquered, and that he is Tirumal.","n":"The name Tirumal is used in praise of the Jain Lord, as the epic uses it for Tivittan elsewhere; the verse does not equate the two. Line 1 is hard to construe.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 540","lb":"SO540","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["the Lord has conquered and reached the limit of all things","jain","reported",3,"nami"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நமி","இறைவன்"],"ch":"so:25"},"so:541":{"book":"soolamani","num":541,"t":["நிறைதரு கேவலத்தோய் நின்னடியார்க் கெல்லாங்","குறைதலி லின்பங் கொடுப்பயு நீயே","கொடுப்பயு நீயேயெங் குற்றவேல் வேண்டாய்","விடுத்தாயு நீத்தாயு நீயேவென்ற பெருமானே"],"p":"","r":"Nami sings to the one of full kevala knowledge that he gives his servants unfailing bliss, and that he put away and cast off the cruel spear and every desire.","n":"The Lord is praised as having no wish and yet as the giver, which Kural 4 does not allow its lord; the phrase எங்குற்றவேல் in line 3 is obscure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","inbam","thuravu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 541","lb":"SO541","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["the Lord gives unfailing bliss to those who serve his feet","jain","reported",2,"nami"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நமி","இறைவன்"],"ch":"so:25"},"so:542":{"book":"soolamani","num":542,"t":["என்றவன் பாடக் கேட்டே யிறஞ்சின குறிஞ்சி யேகா","நின்றன விலங்கு சாதி நிலங்கொண்ட பறவை எல்லா","மன்றுமெய் மறந்து சேர்ந்தார் கின்னர ரமரர் தாழ்ந்தார்","வென்றவன் றியானத் துள்ளான் வியந்திலன் சிறிதும் வேந்தே"],"p":"","r":"At Nami's singing the hills bowed, the animals and birds and the Kinnaras and gods gathered and bent, but the victor in meditation was not at all astonished.","n":"The yogi does not answer the song; the reply to Nami comes from Dharanan, the serpent king, in the verses that follow.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thiyaanam","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 542","lb":"SO542","mm":1,"ev":"All creatures and the gods bow to Nami's song, while the yogi stays in meditation unmoved.","cl":[["the one who has conquered is not moved by praise","jain","reported",4,"maruci"]],"ag":["விலங்கு சாதி","இறைவன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["மருசி","வேந்தன்"],"ch":"so:25"},"so:543":{"book":"soolamani","num":543,"t":["மணநிரைத் திலங்குந் தாரோய் மற்றவ னுலோக நாதன்","குணநிரைத் திசைத்த கீதங் கேட்டலு மணிகொள் கோவைக்","கணநிரைத் திலங்குங் காய்பொன் முடிமிசை யீரைஞ்ஞாறு","பணநிரைத் திலங்கப் புக்கான் பணதர ரரச னன்றே"],"p":"","r":"On hearing the song of Nami to the lord of the world, Dharanan, the king of the serpent lords, came with his thousand hoods in a gleaming row.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","perumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 543","lb":"SO543","mm":1,"ev":"Dharanan, drawn by the song, comes in with his thousand hoods.","cl":[],"ag":["பணதரன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["மருசி","வேந்தன்"],"ch":"so:25"},"so:544":{"book":"soolamani","num":544,"t":["பன்னக ருலகங் காக்கும் பாய்கதிர்ப் பசும்பொன் மேனி","மின்னவிர் வயிரச் சூட்டு விடுசுடர் மணிப்பொற் பூணான்","தன்னிக ரிகந்த தோன்றல் சரணெனப் பரமன் பாதம்","மன்னர்கட் கரசன் முன்னை வலங்கொடு வணக்கங் செய்தான்"],"p":"","r":"Dharanan, ruler of the serpent world, of body like fresh gold and with a crest of diamond and gems, circled the Lord's feet to the right and made his reverence, saying that he took the Lord as his refuge.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","panivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 544","lb":"SO544","mm":1,"ev":"Dharanan circles the Lord's feet to the right and bows.","cl":[],"ag":["பணதரன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["மருசி","வேந்தன்"],"ch":"so:25"},"so:545":{"book":"soolamani","num":545,"t":["தேந்துண ரிலங்கு கண்ணித் தேவனத் தேவர் கோனைத்","தீந்தொடை நரம்பின் றெய்வச் செழுங்குரல் சிலம்ப வேத்தப்","பூந்துணர்க் கற்ப லோகம் புடைபெயர்ந் திட்ட போற்றா","வேந்துடை மான மெல்லாம் வேலினால் விடுத்த வேந்தே"],"p":"","r":"Dharanan says to Nami that his song to the lord of the gods, in the strings' divine voice, set the kalpa heaven shaking, and calls him the king who by his spear cast down the pride of kings who did not worship.","n":"The lines are difficult; it is not certain that Dharanan is speaking or that the vocative at the end is Nami, though 546 and 547 make this the likelier reading.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","perumai","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 545","lb":"SO545","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["பணதரன்","நமி"],"ch":"so:25"},"so:546":{"book":"soolamani","num":546,"t":["மாண்டதன் நிலைமை யுள்ளி வருபொருண் மெய்ம்மைநோக்கித்","தூண்டிய சுடரி னின்ற தியானத்தைத் துளங்கு வாய்போ","லீண்டுவந் திசைக்குற் றேவ லெம்மிறை யடிக்கட் செய்தாய்","வேண்டுவ தெவன்கொ லென்றான் மிடைமணிப் பூணினானே"],"p":"","r":"Dharanan asks Nami what he wants, having come and done service at the Lord's feet, like a flame that shakes in meditation.","n":"The first two lines are obscure, and the simile of the lamp may describe Nami's meditation or his song.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["arul","therithal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 546","lb":"SO546","mm":1,"ev":"Dharanan asks Nami what he wishes.","cl":[],"ag":["பணதரன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["பணதரன்","நமி"],"ch":"so:25"},"so:547":{"book":"soolamani","num":547,"t":["பண்மிசைப் படர்ந்த சிந்தைப் பணதரற் பணிந்து மாற்றத்","துண்மிசைத் தொடர்பு நோக்கி யுறுவலி யதனைக் கேளா","விண்மிசை யவர்கள் போல வேண்டிய விளைக்குஞ் செல்வ","மண்மிசைப் பெறுவ னாக மற்றிதென் மனத்த தென்றான்"],"p":"","r":"Nami bows to Dharanan and says that what is in his mind is to obtain on the earth the wealth that gives every wish, as the dwellers in the sky have it.","n":"Nami asks the serpent king, not the yogi he has praised, and asks for wealth rather than release; the verse offers no judgment of the wish.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["avaa","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 547","lb":"SO547","mm":1,"ev":"Nami asks the serpent king for wealth that grants every wish.","cl":[],"ag":["நமி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நமி","பணதரன்"],"ch":"so:25"},"so:548":{"book":"soolamani","num":548,"t":["இச்சையங் குரைப்ப கேட்டாங் கிமைய வரியற்கை யெய்தும்","வீச்சையுந் துணையும் வெள்ளி விலங்கலுங் கொடுத்து வேந்தாய்","நிச்சமு நிலாக வென்று நிறுவிப்போய் நிலத்தின் கீழ்த்த","னச்சமி லுலகஞ் சேர்ந்தா னாயிரம் பணத்தி னானே"],"p":"","r":"Hearing his wish, Dharanan gives him the sky-going lore, a companion and the silver mountain, tells him to be king always, sets him there and goes down to the fearless world below the earth.","n":"The word துணை is read as a companion, who is not named; the silver mountain is taken as the ridge on which the Vidyadharas live, as in earlier chapters.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["eegai","arasu","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 548","lb":"SO548","mm":1,"ev":"Dharanan gives Nami the lore of the sky-dwellers, a companion and the silver mountain, and returns below the earth.","cl":[],"ag":["பணதரன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["மருசி","வேந்தன்"],"ch":"so:25"},"so:549":{"book":"soolamani","num":549,"t":["ஆங்கவன் குலத்து ளானெம் மதிபதி யவனோ டொப்பா","யோங்கிய குலமுஞ் செல்வப் பெருமையு முடைய நீயு","மீங்கிரு குலத்து ளீர்க்குங் கருமம்வந் திசைத்த போழ்தி","னீங்கரு நறுநெய் தீம்பால் சொரிந்தோர் நீர்மைத் தென்றான்"],"p":"","r":"Marusi closes: the one of Nami's line is our overlord, and for the king, of high lineage and wealth like his, a matter that joins the two houses will be like pure ghee and sweet milk poured together.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kudi","thoothu","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 549","lb":"SO549","mm":1,"ev":"Marusi ends by saying the Vidyadhara king descends from Nami and that a union of the two houses would suit them.","cl":[["houses equal in lineage and wealth should be joined in marriage","general","asserted",2,"maruci"],["the Vidyadhara king descends from Nami, son of the king of Kasi","general","reported",1,"maruci"]],"ag":["மருசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["மருசி","வேந்தன்"],"ch":"so:25"},"so:550":{"book":"soolamani","num":550,"t":["தங்குலத் தொடர்ச்சி கூறித் தானவ னிருந்த போழ்தி","னுங்குல நிலைமை யெல்லா நூலினீ யுரைத்த வாறே","எங்குல நிலைமை யானு முரைப்பனென் றெடுத்துக் கொண்டு","பொங்கலர்ப் பிணைய லான்றன் புரோகிதன் புகல லூற்றான்"],"p":"","r":"When the envoy has finished telling the line of his own king, the priest says he will give the line of his own lord in the same manner and begins.","n":"The exchange of clan recitals before any message is delivered is the epic's way of staging the envoy's first test; the priest speaks 551 to 561.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kudi","thoothu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 550","lb":"SO550","mm":1,"ev":"Marusi ends the recital of his own clan, and the priest of Podanam begins to recite his king's clan in return.","cl":[],"ag":["மருசி","புரோகிதன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:26"},"so:551":{"book":"soolamani","num":551,"t":["யாவனாற் படைக்கப் பட்ட துலகெலாம் யாவன் பாத்த","தேவனால் வணக்கப்பட்ட தியாவன தகலஞ் சேர்ந்து","பூவினாள் பொறியொன் றானாள் புண்ணிய வுலகங் கான","ஏவினான் யாவ னம்மை யாவன துலக மெல்லாம்"],"p":"","r":"The speaker asks in a run of questions by whom the world was ordered, whom the gods bowed to, and whose is the whole world, leading into the account of the clan's first lord.","n":"The verb for what the first lord did is 'created' (படைக்கப்பட்டது), but the lines are a rhetorical build-up and the next verse names only a descendant by his grace; the Jain tradition's first lord is a founder of the order of life, not a creator, and the verse does not decide this.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","kudi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 551","lb":"SO551","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["புரோகிதன்","மருசி"],"ch":"so:26"},"so:552":{"book":"soolamani","num":552,"t":["மற்றவ னருளின் வந்தான் மரகத மணிக்குன் றொப்பச்","சுற்றி நின் றிலங்கு சோதித் தோள்வலி யெனும்பே ரானக்","கொற்றவ னுலகங் காத்த கோன்முறை வேண்டி யன்றே","கற்றவ ரின்று காறுங் காவனூல் கற்ப தெல்ல்லம்"],"p":"","r":"By the first lord's grace there came one named Tolvali, like an emerald hill, whose guarding of the world is the reason the learned still study the books of protection.","n":"The verse names only 'arm-strength' (தோள்வலி); the traditional identification with Bahubali, the Podanam king of the old line, is not in the Tamil here.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","kalvi","kudi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 552","lb":"SO552","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["the books of protection are studied because kings must guard the world","general","asserted",3,"purohitan"]],"ag":["தோள்வலி"],"ec":[],"spk":["புரோகிதன்","மருசி"],"ch":"so:26"},"so:553":{"book":"soolamani","num":553,"t":["கொடிவரைந் தெழுதப் பட்ட குங்குமக் குவவுத் தோளான்","இடிமுர சதிருந் தானை யிறைத்தொழில் மகனுக் கீந்து","கடிமண் மனுக்குந் தெய்வக் கழலடி யரசர் தங்கள்","முடிபொர முனிவிற் றான்போய் முனிவன முன்னினானால்"],"p":"","r":"A king of the line gives the work of rule to his son, while kings crowd at his feet, and sets his mind on the sage's way.","n":"Nothing in the line says the king of 553 is the same as Tolvali of 552, but 554 to 560 run on as one life and are read so.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thuravu","arasu","thavam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 553","lb":"SO553","mm":1,"ev":"The king hands the kingship to his son and goes out to renounce.","cl":[["a king may hand his rule to his son and leave for austerity","general","asserted",2,"purohitan"]],"ag":["தோள்வலி"],"ec":[],"spk":["புரோகிதன்","மருசி"],"ch":"so:26"},"so:554":{"book":"soolamani","num":554,"t":["விண்ணுயர் விளங்கு கோட்டு விடுசுடர் விளங்க மாட்டாக்","கண்ணுயர் கதலி வேலிக் கார்க்கயி லாய நெற்றிப்","புண்ணியக் கிழவன் போகிப் பொலங்கலம் புலம்ப நீக்கித்","திண்ணிய தியானச் செந்தீச் செங்சுடர் திகழ நின்றான்"],"p":"","r":"On the Kailasa hill's brow the holy king puts off his gold ornaments and stands, his firm meditation shining as a red flame.","n":"Meditation is imaged as a fire, so the ornaments left behind and the flame within are set against each other.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thavam","thiyaanam","thuravu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 554","lb":"SO554","mm":1,"ev":"The king strips off his ornaments and stands in meditation on Kailasa.","cl":[],"ag":["தோள்வலி"],"ec":[],"spk":["புரோகிதன்","மருசி"],"ch":"so:26"},"so:555":{"book":"soolamani","num":555,"t":["கழலணிந் திலங்கு பாதங் கலந்தன கருங்கட் புற்றத்","தழலணிந் தெழுந்த வைவா யருமணி யாடு நாகம்","பொழிலணிந் தெழுந்த வல்லி புதைந்தன பூமி நாதன்","குழலணிந் தெழுந்த குஞ்சி குடைந்தன குருவிக் கூட்டம்"],"p":"","r":"As he stands, serpents rise from the anthills round his feet, creepers cover the earth-lord's body, and flocks of small birds nest in his hair.","n":"The stillness is shown by what the forest does to the standing man; the verse says nothing of his pain.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thavam","thiyaanam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 555","lb":"SO555","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["புரோகிதன்","மருசி"],"ch":"so:26"},"so:556":{"book":"soolamani","num":556,"t":["அருமுடி யரசர் தாழ்ந்த வடிமிசை யரவ மூரக்","கருவடி நெடுங்க ணல்லார் கலந்த தோள் வல்லி புல்ல","மருவுடை யுலகம் பாடல் வனத்திடைப் பறவை பாடத்","திருவுடை யடிக ணிண்ற திறமிது தெரிய லாமோ"],"p":"","r":"The speaker says that kings bow to his feet with snakes crawling over them, the vines clasp his arms as women would, and the forest birds sing for him, so that his state cannot be known.","n":"The kings who bow are the rank he left; the verse sets their worship beside creeper and serpent.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thavam","panivu","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 556","lb":"SO556","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["the ascetic's state is beyond the understanding of the people who bow","general","asserted",4,"purohitan"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["புரோகிதன்","மருசி"],"ch":"so:26"},"so:557":{"book":"soolamani","num":557,"t":["வெண்டவாங் குவளைக் கண்ணி மன்னர்தம் மகுட கோடி","விண்டவாம் பிணைய லுக்க விரி மதுத் துவலை மாரி","உண்டவான் கழல்கள் சூழ்ந்த திருவடி யரவ மூரக்","கண்டவா றிங்க ணார்க்குங் கருதுவ தரிது கண்டாய்"],"p":"","r":"In the same tableau the garlands of bowing kings drip honey on his feet, and the speaker says that what is thought of those who watch this is hard to say.","n":"Line 3 reads the serpents as moving over the feet already crowded by kings' crowns, so the passage presses worship and the wild together.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["panivu","thavam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 557","lb":"SO557","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["புரோகிதன்","மருசி"],"ch":"so:26"},"so:558":{"book":"soolamani","num":558,"t":["அடுக்கிய வனிச்சப் பூவி னளிமே லரத்தச் செவ்வாய்","வடிக்கயல் நெடுங்க ணார்தம் வளிக்கையால் வளித்த மார்பில்","தொடுக்கிய தொடுத்த போலுந் துறுமலர்க் கத்தி மாதர்","கொடிக்கையா லிடுக்க றன்மேல் கொற்றவன் குலவப் பட்டன்"],"p":"","r":"A king reclines, his chest fanned by women of fish-like eyes and red lips and bound by their flowered arms, in the setting of a garland-wearing sensual life.","n":"Whether this describes the same king before he renounced or a separate sensual figure set beside the ascetic is not fixed by the Tamil.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","pattru"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 558","lb":"SO558","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["புரோகிதன்","மருசி"],"ch":"so:26"},"so:559":{"book":"soolamani","num":559,"t":["புல்லிய பொலங்கொம் பொப்பார் புலவியுட் கலவி சென்று","மெல்லிய மாலை தம்மால் விசித்தலை விடுத்து மீட்டு","மல்லுய ரலர்ந்த மார்பின் மாதவிப் பேதை யார்த்த","வல்லிகள் விடுக்க மாட்டா மனத்தினன் மன்ன னானான்"],"p":"","r":"The women free the king's chest of the garlands they had tied in love's quarrel, but the king's mind cannot let go of the vines of a girl called Madhavi.","n":"The word மாதவி may be a flower (the madhavi creeper) or a girl's name; both readings keep the picture of a mind that will not release its hold, set against the clasp of 556.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pattru","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 559","lb":"SO559","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["புரோகிதன்","மருசி"],"ch":"so:26"},"so:560":{"book":"soolamani","num":560,"t":["ஓவலில் குணங்க ளென்னு மொளிர்மணிக் கலங்க டாங்கித்","தேவர்க ளுலக மெல்லாஞ் செழுமண மயர்ந்து கூட்டக்","கேவலப் பெண்ணென் பாளோர் கிளரரொளி மடந்தை தன்னை","ஆவியு ளடக்கிப் பின்னை யமரர்க்கு மரிய னானான்"],"p":"","r":"He wears a set of jewels called his unfailing virtues, and when the gods of all the worlds hold a rich wedding he takes the lady called Kevala, the woman of rising light, into his soul and then becomes rare even to the gods.","n":"The ornaments are his own virtues and the bride his own full knowledge; the marriage image of the poem is turned inward.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["veedu","thuravu","arivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 560","lb":"SO560","mm":1,"ev":"The king takes in the maiden Kevala, whom the gods wed to him, and rises above the gods.","cl":[["kevala, the soul's full knowledge, is gained by the renouncer and puts him above the gods","jain","asserted",3,"purohitan"]],"ag":["தோள்வலி"],"ec":[],"spk":["புரோகிதன்","மருசி"],"ch":"so:26"},"so:561":{"book":"soolamani","num":561,"t":["எங்கள்கோ னிவன்க ணின்று மிக்குயர் குலத்து வேந்தர்","தங்களோர் புறஞ்சொல் வாராத் தன்மையா லுலகங் காத்தார்","அங்கவர் வழிக்கண் தோன்றி யகலிடம் வணங்க நின்ற","இங்கிவன் பெருமை நீயுமறிதியா லேந்த லென்றான்"],"p":"","r":"The speaker says that the line of high clan guarded the world so that no reproach ever came on it, and that the man who stands honoured among them now carries the same greatness.","n":"It is the priest's own king who is praised through descent; the unit is the end of his recital.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kudi","arasu","pazhi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 561","lb":"SO561","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["kings of the high line guard the world without a word of reproach","general","asserted",2,"purohitan"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["புரோகிதன்","மருசி"],"ch":"so:26"},"so:562":{"book":"soolamani","num":562,"t":["குடித்தொட ரிரண்டுங் கேட்டே குறுமயி ரெறிந்து கண்ணுள்","பொடித்தநீர்த் திவலை சிந்தப் புகழ்ந்தன ரிருந்த வேந்தர்","அடுத்தெரி யலர்ந்த செம்பொ னணிமணி முடியி னானங்","கெடுத்துரை கெடாத முன்னக் கேசர னிதனைச் சொன்னான்"],"p":"","r":"Hearing both lines recited, the seated kings weep and praise them, and Marusi, called Kesaran, speaks before the moment fades.","n":"The envoy is named Kesaran, the sky-goer, here, and Marusi elsewhere; the same man is meant.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kudi","thoothu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 562","lb":"SO562","mm":1,"ev":"The kings who hear the two clan recitals weep and praise them, and Marusi answers.","cl":[],"ag":["மன்னர்","மருசி"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:26"},"so:563":{"book":"soolamani","num":563,"t":["இப்படித் தாயிற் பண்டை யிசைந்தது சுற்ற மென்னை","அப்படி யரிய செய்த வடிகளெம் மரச னாய","கைப்புடை யிலங்கு செவ்வேற் கச்சற்கு மருக னாரென்","றொப்புடைப் புராண நன்னூ லுரைப்பதியா னறிவ னென்றான்"],"p":"","r":"The envoy says that so old a bond must be fitting, that the lord who did those rare deeds is the king of his own people and the grandson of Kacchan with the red spear, and that the old purana tells it.","n":"Kacchan's name is traditionally that of the father of Nami and Vinami, the Vidyadhara line's founders; the verse itself gives only the name and the purana as authority.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kudi","thoothu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 563","lb":"SO563","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["the Vidyadhara king descends from Kacchan and shares an old kinship with the Podanam line","general","asserted",3,"maruci"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["மருசி",""],"ch":"so:26"},"so:564":{"book":"soolamani","num":564,"t":["மன்னவன் மனத்தி னாற்ற மிறைவனை வணங்கி வாழ்த்திப்","பின்னவன் ரன்னை நோக்கிப் பேசினன் பிறங்கு தாரோய்","முன்னிய வுலக நூலுங் குலங்களு முறையு முள்ளிட்","டின்னவா றறியு நீரோ ரில்லை நின் போல வென்றான்"],"p":"","r":"The king worships the lord, turns to the envoy and says no one is like him in knowing the world's books, the clans and the right order.","n":"This praise follows the recitals just given, so the praise is of his measured performance there.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thoothu","kalvi","kudi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 564","lb":"SO564","mm":1,"ev":"The king worships the Arhat and then praises the envoy's learning.","cl":[],"ag":["பிரசாபதி"],"ec":[],"spk":["பிரசாபதி","மருசி"],"ch":"so:26"},"so:565":{"book":"soolamani","num":565,"t":["மந்திரக் கிழவர் கண்ணா மக்கடன் றாள்க ளாகச்","சுந்தர வயிரத் திண்டோ டோ ழராச் செவிக ளொற்றா","அந்தர வுணர்வ நூலா வரசெனு முருவு கொண்ட","எந்திர மிதற்கு வாயாத் தூதுவ ரியற்றப் பட்டார்"],"p":"","r":"The king says that ministers are his eyes, children his feet, strong shoulders his friends, spies his ears, and the book of knowledge his sense, and that for the voice of this machine called king the envoys are made.","n":"The king is called a machine (எந்திரம்); the limbs of the state are its parts, and the envoy is the mouth.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thoothu","arasu","amaichu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 565","lb":"SO565","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["a king sees through ministers, hears through spies and speaks through envoys","general","asserted",4,"pirasapathi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["பிரசாபதி","மருசி"],"ch":"so:26"},"so:566":{"book":"soolamani","num":566,"t":["ஆதிநூ லமைச்சர்க் கோது மாண்பொலா மமைந்து நின்றான்","தூதனாச் சொல்லிற் சொல்லாச் சூழ்பொரு ளில்லை போலா","மேதிலார்க் காவ துண்டோ வின்னன புகுந்த போழ்திற்","கோதிலாக் குணங்க டேற்றிக் கொழித்துரை கொளுத்த லென்றான்"],"p":"","r":"The king says the envoy has all the virtues the old book gives for ministers, that nothing in policy lies beyond the envoy's speech, and bids him show faultless virtues in sifted words when such a matter comes.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thoothu","solvanmai","kalvi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 566","lb":"SO566","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["no matter of policy is beyond what an envoy can say","general","asserted",2,"pirasapathi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["பிரசாபதி","மருசி"],"ch":"so:26"},"so:567":{"book":"soolamani","num":567,"t":["மற்றிம்மாண் புடைய நின்னை யுடையவம் மன்னர் மன்னன்","எற்றைநூற் றெய்த மாட்டா னிதன்றிற நிற்க வெம்மைச்","சுற்றமா நினைந்து நின்னைத் தூதனா விடுத்துச் செல்லப்","பெற்றியாம் பிறவி தன்னாற் பெறும்பயன் பெற்ற தென்றான்"],"p":"","r":"The king says that the Vidyadhara king, who has such a man, needs no other text, and that sending him as envoy shows that king counted Podanam as kin, so that the birth's fruit has been gained.","n":"Line 1 to 2 can be read two ways, as praise of Marusi's king for owning him or as the Podanam king's own claim; the first is taken.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thoothu","sutram","natpu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 567","lb":"SO567","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["பிரசாபதி","மருசி"],"ch":"so:26"},"so:568":{"book":"soolamani","num":568,"t":["இன்றியா னின்னை முன்வைத் தினிச்சில வுரைக்கல் வேண்டா","ஒன்றியா னுரைக்கற் பால வுரையையு முணர்த்தி நீயே","வென்றியால் விளங்கு தானை விஞ்சையங் கிழவன் கண்ணா","நின்றியான் வாழ்வ தல்லா னினைப்பினி யில்லை மன்னோ"],"p":"","r":"The king says that with the envoy before him he needs no further words, that Marusi should speak what must be said, and that he has no thought but to live with the Vidyadhara king as his own eye.","n":"The king puts himself in the Vidyadhara king's care in a way that reads as consent, but he does not yet name the girl or the match.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thoothu","natpu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 568","lb":"SO568","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["பிரசாபதி","மருசி"],"ch":"so:26"},"so:569":{"book":"soolamani","num":569,"t":["கொற்றவன் குறிப்பி தாயிற் கூவித்த னடியன் மாரை","உற்றதோர் சிறுகுற் றேவற் குரியராய்க் கருதித் தானே","அற்றமி லலங்கல் வேலோ னறிந்தருள் செய்வ தல்லான்","மற்றியா னுரைக்கு மாற்ற முடையனோ மன்னற் கென்றான்"],"p":"","r":"The king says that if this is the Vidyadhara king's wish, the lord of the flower garland will know and grant it, since he himself has no standing to speak to that king.","n":"The king answers the envoy as if he were a servant of the Vidyadhara king, and the verse stops short of saying yes.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["arasu","panivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 569","lb":"SO569","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["பிரசாபதி","மருசி"],"ch":"so:26"},"so:570":{"book":"soolamani","num":570,"t":["தூதன்மற் றதனைக் கேட்டே தொழுதடி வணங்கிச் செங்கோல்","ஏதமில் புகழி னாயானடிவலங் கொள்வ னென்னப்","போதுசே ரலங்க லானும் பொலங்கலம் பொறுக்க லாகாச்","சோதிய சுடரச் சேர்த்திப் பெருஞ்சிறப் பருளிச் செய்தான்"],"p":"","r":"Hearing this, the envoy bows at his feet and says he will walk round the feet of the king of the faultless sceptre and fame, and the garlanded king rewards him with a gold ornament and great honour.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["panivu","eegai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 570","lb":"SO570","mm":1,"ev":"Marusi bows and offers to circle the king's feet, and the king honours him with an ornament and gifts.","cl":[],"ag":["மருசி","பிரசாபதி"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:26"},"so:571":{"book":"soolamani","num":571,"t":["அற்றைநா ளங்குத் தாழ்ப்பித் தகனகர்ச் செல்வந் தன்னோ","டுற்றவ னுவப்பக் கூறி யுரிமைநா டகங்கள் காட்டிப்","பிற்றைநாட் குரவர் தம்மைப் பின்சென்று விடுமி னென்று","மற்றவர்க் கருளிச் செய்தான் மருசியுந் தொழுது சென்றான்"],"p":"","r":"The king keeps him there for the day, speaks to please him of the city's wealth, shows him the court plays, and orders that he be sent on next day with the elders, and Marusi bows and goes.","n":"The lines on the city and the plays are compressed; the guest-rite of a day's welcome is the likely sense.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["virundhu","thoothu","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 571","lb":"SO571","mm":1,"ev":"The king detains Marusi a day with talk of the city and plays, arranges his sending off, and Marusi leaves.","cl":[],"ag":["பிரசாபதி","மருசி"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:26"},"so:572":{"book":"soolamani","num":572,"t":["உலனல னடுதிண்டோ ளூழிவே லோடை யானைச்","சலநல சடியென்பேர்த் தாமரைச் செங்க ணான்றன்","குலநல மிகுசெல்கைக் கோவொடொப் பார்கள் வாழு","நலனமர் நளிசும்மைத் தொன்னகர் நண்ணி னானே"],"p":"","r":"Marusi reaches the old, crowded city of a lotus-eyed king whose strong arms and war-elephants are praised, the city where those like the king live.","n":"Lines 1 to 2 are garbled, so which city and which king are meant is a guess; it is read as the Vidyadhara king's city.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thoothu","naadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 572","lb":"SO572","mm":1,"ev":"Marusi arrives at the old city.","cl":[],"ag":["மருசி"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:26"},"so:573":{"book":"soolamani","num":573,"t":["மற்ற மாநகர் மருசி புக்கபின்","கொற்ற வேலவன் கோயின் மாநெதி","முற்று வான்கடை மூன்றுஞ் சென்றுகோன்","சுற்று வார்கழ றெழுது துன்னினான்"],"p":"","r":"After Marusi has entered the great city he passes the three outer gates of the spear-bearing king's palace and comes close to the king, worshipping at his anklets.","n":"The word before the last in line 4 reads as எழுது in the text; தொழுது (worshipping) is the likely sense, and the palace is the Vidyadhara king's because verse 577 has Marusi report his errand done.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thoothu","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 573","lb":"SO573","mm":1,"ev":"Marusi returns to the Vidyadhara palace and approaches the king.","cl":[],"ag":["மருசி"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:27"},"so:574":{"book":"soolamani","num":574,"t":["விலங்கு வார்குழை மிளிர்ந்து வில்லிடக்","கலந்து மாமணிக் கடக மின்செய","அலங்கல் வேலினா னங்கை யாலவற்","கிலங்கு மாநிலத் திருக்கை யேவினான்"],"p":"","r":"The king, with swaying earrings, gem-set bracelets and a garlanded spear, directs with his hand that Marusi sit on the broad floor.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","thoothu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 574","lb":"SO574","mm":1,"ev":"The Vidyadhara king bids the returned envoy sit.","cl":[],"ag":["விஞ்சையர் வேந்தன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:27"},"so:575":{"book":"soolamani","num":575,"t":["தொகுத்த மாண்புடைத் தூதன் மன்னவன்","வகுத்த மாமணித் தலத்தின் மேன்மனத்","தகத்தி னாலமர்ந் திருப்ப வாங்கவன்","முகத்தி னாற்பொருண் முடிவு கண்ணினான்"],"p":"","r":"Marusi sits composed on the gem floor the king has laid out, and the king reads from his face how the errand ended.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kurippu","thoothu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 575","lb":"SO575","mm":1,"ev":"The king reads the outcome of the embassy from the envoy's face before he speaks.","cl":[],"ag":["மருசி","விஞ்சையர் வேந்தன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:27"},"so:576":{"book":"soolamani","num":576,"t":["தூத னின்முகப் பொலிவி னாற்சுடர்க்","காது வேலினான் கரும முற்றுற","ஓதி ஞானிபோ லுணர்ந்த பின்னது","கோதில் கேள்வியான் றொழுது கூறினான்"],"p":"","r":"From the brightness of the envoy's face the king grasps, like a sage, that the task is done, and the faultlessly learned Marusi bows and speaks.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kurippu","kelvi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 576","lb":"SO576","mm":1,"ev":"Seeing success in Marusi's face, the king waits as Marusi bows and begins his report.","cl":[],"ag":["விஞ்சையர் வேந்தன்","மருசி"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:27"},"so:577":{"book":"soolamani","num":577,"t":["வெல்க வாழிநின் வென்றிவார்கழல்","செல்க தீயன சிறக்க நின்புகழ்","மல்க நின்பணி முடித்து வந்தனன்","பில்கு மும்மதப் பிணர்க்கை யானையாய்"],"p":"","r":"Marusi greets the king, wishing his victorious anklets to live, evil to leave and his fame to grow, and says he has finished the king's errand and returned.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thoothu","pugazh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 577","lb":"SO577","mm":1,"ev":"Marusi announces that the errand is done.","cl":[],"ag":["மருசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["மருசி","விஞ்சையர் வேந்தன்"],"ch":"so:27"},"so:578":{"book":"soolamani","num":578,"t":["இங்கு நின்றுபோ யிழிந்த சூழலும்","அங்கு வேந்தனை யணைந்த வாயிலும்","பொங்கு தானையான் புகன்ற மாற்றமுந்","தொங்கன் மார்பினாய் சொல்லு கேனெனா"],"p":"","r":"Marusi promises to tell the circumstances of his descent from here, how he came before the king there, and the words the army-bearing king spoke.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thoothu","sol"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 578","lb":"SO578","mm":1,"ev":"Marusi undertakes a full report of the embassy.","cl":[],"ag":["மருசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["மருசி","விஞ்சையர் வேந்தன்"],"ch":"so:27"},"so:579":{"book":"soolamani","num":579,"t":["அள்ளி லைச்செழும் பலவி னார்சுளை","முள்ளு டைக்கனி முறுகி விண்டெனக்","கள்ளு றைத்தொறுங் கழுமி யூற்றறா","வள்ளி லைப்பொழின் மகிழ்ந்து புக்கதும்"],"p":"","r":"Marusi tells how he entered the grove of jackfruit whose ripe, thorny fruit split and let their juice and honey run.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 579","lb":"SO579","mm":1,"ev":"Marusi enters the grove outside the city.","cl":[],"ag":["மருசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["மருசி","விஞ்சையர் வேந்தன்"],"ch":"so:27"},"so:580":{"book":"soolamani","num":580,"t":["முள்ள ரைப்பசு முளரி யந்தடத்","துள்ளி ரைத்தெழு மொலிசெய் வண்டினம்","கள்ளி ரைத்துகக் கண்டு வண்சிறைப்","புள்ளி ரைப்பதோர் பொய்கை சார்ந்ததும்"],"p":"","r":"Marusi tells how he came to a pool where bees swarm the lotuses and flocks of birds hunt the water.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 580","lb":"SO580","mm":1,"ev":"Marusi reaches the pool in the grove.","cl":[],"ag":["மருசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["மருசி","விஞ்சையர் வேந்தன்"],"ch":"so:27"},"so:581":{"book":"soolamani","num":581,"t":["நித்தி லம்மணி நிரந்து வெள்ளிவேய்","பத்தி சித்திரப் பலகை வேதிகை","சித்தி ரங்களிற் செறிந்து காமனார்","அத்தி ரம்மென அசோகங் கண்டதும்"],"p":"","r":"Marusi tells how he saw an asoka tree set about with jewelled and silver-patterned platforms, looking like the love-god's own weapon.","n":"The asoka's flower is one of the love-god's arrows, which is why the tree is called his weapon.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 581","lb":"SO581","mm":1,"ev":"Marusi sees the asoka tree.","cl":[],"ag":["மருசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["மருசி","விஞ்சையர் வேந்தன்"],"ch":"so:27"},"so:582":{"book":"soolamani","num":582,"t":["தன்ணி ழற்சுடர்த் தமனி யத்தினான்","மன்ணி ழற்கொள மருங்கு சுற்றிய","வெண்ணி ழற்சுடர் விளங்கு கற்றலங்","கண்ணி ழற்கொளக் கண்ட காட்சியும்"],"p":"","r":"Marusi tells of a white stone slab ringed by gold that shines of itself, and of the sight it made to the eye.","n":"The word division of lines 1 to 3 is run together and the construction is hard, so the sense of the slab's light is only the likely one.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 582","lb":"SO582","mm":1,"ev":"Marusi sees the stone slab in the grove.","cl":[],"ag":["மருசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["மருசி","விஞ்சையர் வேந்தன்"],"ch":"so:27"},"so:583":{"book":"soolamani","num":583,"t":["சுரிந்த குஞ்சியன் சுடரு மேனியன்","எரிந்த பூணின னிலங்கு தாரினன்","வரிந்த கச்சைய னொருவன் வந்துவண்","டிரிந்து பாயவிங் கேறு கென்றதும்"],"p":"","r":"Marusi tells how a man with curled forelock, shining body, blazing ornaments and a tight girdle came while the bees scattered, and bade him climb up.","n":"The man is not named. Verse 116 posted Turumakanthan as guard in this garden for the day the astrologer named, and he is the likely figure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thoothu","kurippu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 583","lb":"SO583","mm":1,"ev":"A man of the garden comes to Marusi and bids him come up.","cl":[],"ag":["மருசி","துரும காந்தன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["மருசி","விஞ்சையர் வேந்தன்"],"ch":"so:27"},"so:584":{"book":"soolamani","num":584,"t":["மற்ற வன்றனக் குரைத்த மாற்றமுங்","கொற்ற வன்விடக் கொம்ப னார்சிலர்","உற்ற மங்கலக் கலங்க ளோடுடன்","முற்ற வூண்டொழின் முடிந்த பெற்றியும்"],"p":"","r":"Marusi tells what was said to him there, and how at the king's sending some women with auspicious vessels finished serving him a meal.","n":"The king named is the host, Tivittan's father, taken from the sending of the envoy in verses 426-428.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["virundhu","thoothu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 584","lb":"SO584","mm":1,"ev":"The host king sends women who serve the envoy a meal.","cl":[],"ag":["மருசி","பிரசாபதி","கொம்பனார்"],"ec":[],"spk":["மருசி","விஞ்சையர் வேந்தன்"],"ch":"so:27"},"so:585":{"book":"soolamani","num":585,"t":["பங்கய யத்தலர்ச் செங்கண் மாமுடித்","திங்கள் வண்ணனுஞ் செம்பொ னீள்குழைப்","பொங்கு வெண்டிரைப் புணரி வண்ணனும்","அங்கு வந்தது மவர்கள் சொற்றதும்"],"p":"","r":"Marusi tells how the moon-hued prince with red lotus eyes and the sea-hued prince in long golden earrings came there, and what they said.","n":"The princes are named by colour only; moon-hued is taken as Vijayan and sea-hued as Tivittan, the two sons of Pirasapathi.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thoothu","sol"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 585","lb":"SO585","mm":1,"ev":"The two princes come to the envoy and speak with him.","cl":[],"ag":["விசயன்","செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ec":[],"spk":["மருசி","விஞ்சையர் வேந்தன்"],"ch":"so:27"},"so:586":{"book":"soolamani","num":586,"t":["நற்பு றத்தன நாற்ப தாம்வய","திப்பு ரத்தன விளங்க ருங்கைம்மா","மொய்ப்பு றத்துமேன் முழங்கு தானையோ","டப்பு றத்தர சவைய டைந்ததும்"],"p":"","r":"Marusi tells of the host king's court, with the great elephants and the roaring army drawn up outside, and how the others came to the assembly.","n":"Line 1 and 2 are compressed and the number in the first line is hard to place, so only the general picture of the court is safe.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["arasu","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 586","lb":"SO586","mm":1,"ev":"Marusi enters the host king's crowded assembly.","cl":[],"ag":["மருசி","பிரசாபதி"],"ec":[],"spk":["மருசி","விஞ்சையர் வேந்தன்"],"ch":"so:27"},"so:587":{"book":"soolamani","num":587,"t":["மன்ன வன்கழல் வணங்கி நின்றதும்","பின்ன வன்றனா லிருக்கை பெற்றதும்","பொன்னி றப்பொறி புகழ்ந்த சாதகந்","துன்னி வாசகந் தொழுது கொண்டதும்"],"p":"","r":"Marusi tells how he bowed at the king's anklets, received a seat through the younger prince, and how the horoscope with its golden marks was praised and the letter taken with reverence.","n":"Whose hand takes the letter is not stated; the king is the likely receiver.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thoothu","kurippu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 587","lb":"SO587","mm":1,"ev":"Marusi bows to the host king, is seated at the younger prince's word, and the horoscope and letter are received.","cl":[],"ag":["மருசி","பிரசாபதி"],"ec":[],"spk":["மருசி","விஞ்சையர் வேந்தன்"],"ch":"so:27"},"so:588":{"book":"soolamani","num":588,"t":["ஓட்டி றானையா னோலை வாசகங்","கேட்ட மன்னவன் கிளர்ந்த சோதியான்","மீட்டொர் சொற்கொடா விம்மி தத்தனா","யீட்டு மோனியா யிருந்த பெற்றியும்"],"p":"","r":"Marusi tells how the king, hearing the letter of the lord of the army, was struck with wonder and sat silent, giving no other word.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","manam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 588","lb":"SO588","mm":1,"ev":"The host king hears the letter and falls silent without replying.","cl":[],"ag":["பிரசாபதி"],"ec":[],"spk":["மருசி","விஞ்சையர் வேந்தன்"],"ch":"so:27"},"so:589":{"book":"soolamani","num":589,"t":["இருந்த மன்னன்மே லெடுத்த மாற்றமும்","வருந்தி மற்றவன் மறுத்த வன்ணமும்","புரிந்து தொல்குலம் புகன்ற பெற்றியு","மருந் தகைத் தொடர் பமைந்த வாக்கமும்"],"p":"","r":"Marusi tells of words raised against the seated king, of how someone grieved and refused, of what the old clan said, and of the rare kinship that was finally joined.","n":"The speakers of the objection and the refusal are not named and the grammar of all four lines is compressed.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kudi","sutram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 589","lb":"SO589","mm":1,"ev":"An objection to the match is raised and answered and the kinship is settled.","cl":[],"ag":["பிரசாபதி"],"ec":[],"spk":["மருசி","விஞ்சையர் வேந்தன்"],"ch":"so:27"},"so:590":{"book":"soolamani","num":590,"t":["பின்னை மன்னவன் பேணி நன்மொழி","சொன்ன வண்ணமுஞ் சுற்ற மாயதும்","பொன்ன கைக்குலம் பொலிந்து கண்கொள","வின்ன கைச்சிறப் பருளி யீந்ததும்"],"p":"","r":"Marusi tells how the king then spoke kind words, became kin, and bestowed gifts and honours as the gold-adorned clans looked on.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sutram","eegai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 590","lb":"SO590","mm":1,"ev":"The host king accepts the match and gives gifts.","cl":[],"ag":["பிரசாபதி"],"ec":[],"spk":["மருசி","விஞ்சையர் வேந்தன்"],"ch":"so:27"},"so:591":{"book":"soolamani","num":591,"t":["அருங்கல லக்குழாத் தரசன் றேவிமார்","பெருங்கு லத்தவர் பெயர்ந்து கண்டது","மொருங்கு மற்றுளோ ருரைத்த வார்த்தையுஞ்","சுருங்கில் கேள்வியான் றொழுது சொல்லினான்"],"p":"","r":"Marusi tells what the king's queens and great kin saw as they came forward and what the others said, and then, bowing, goes on to speak further.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sutram","sol"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 591","lb":"SO591","mm":1,"ev":"Marusi finishes his account of the court and bows to add a further word.","cl":[],"ag":["மருசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["மருசி","விஞ்சையர் வேந்தன்"],"ch":"so:27"},"so:592":{"book":"soolamani","num":592,"t":["சொன்ன வார்த்தையிஃ திருக்கச் சொல்லுவ","தின்ன மொன்றுள வடிகள் யான்பல","மன்னர் தங்களை மகிழ்ந்து கண்டன","னன்ன னீர்மையா ரரச ரில்லையே"],"p":"","r":"Marusi says that beyond all that has been told there is one more thing: he has gladly seen many kings and none is of such a nature.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","pugazh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 592","lb":"SO592","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["மருசி","விஞ்சையர் வேந்தன்"],"ch":"so:27"},"so:593":{"book":"soolamani","num":593,"t":["கற்ற நூல்பிறர் கற்ற நூலெலா","முற்ற நோக்கினு முற்ற நோக்கல","வுற்ற நூலெலா முற்ற நூல்களாய்ப்","பெற்ற நூலவன் பெற்றி வண்ணமே"],"p":"","r":"Marusi says that all the books the king has learned stand against those learned by others and are complete beyond them, so that the books he has gained are the measure of his character.","n":"The clauses of lines 1 and 2 are hard to divide and the comparison is read as the king's learning outstripping all others.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kalvi","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 593","lb":"SO593","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["a king's worth shows in a learning that takes in and surpasses the learning of others","general","reported",1,"maruci"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["மருசி","விஞ்சையர் வேந்தன்"],"ch":"so:27"},"so:594":{"book":"soolamani","num":594,"t":["எரியு மாணையான் குளிரு மீகையான்","பெரியன் பெற்றியாற் சிறிய னண்பினா","னரியன் வேந்தர்கட் கெளிய னாண்டையார்க்","குரிய னோங்குதற் கோடை யானையான்"],"p":"","r":"Marusi describes the king as fiery in command and cool in giving, great by nature yet modest in friendship, hard for kings to reach, easy for those who serve him.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","eegai","panpu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 594","lb":"SO594","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["a ruler should be fierce in command yet cool in giving, great in nature yet modest with friends","general","reported",1,"maruci"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["மருசி","விஞ்சையர் வேந்தன்"],"ch":"so:27"},"so:595":{"book":"soolamani","num":595,"t":["எல்லை நீருல கினிது கண்பட","வெல்லும் வேலவன் விளங்கு தண்ணளி","யில்லை யேலுல கில்லை யாமென","நல்ல னேயவ னாம வேலினாய்"],"p":"","r":"Marusi says that if the king's gentle grace were absent the world itself would be absent, so good is he.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arul","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 595","lb":"SO595","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["the world stands only because of a king's gentle grace","general","reported",3,"maruci"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["மருசி","விஞ்சையர் வேந்தன்"],"ch":"so:27"},"so:596":{"book":"soolamani","num":596,"t":["கற்ற நூலினார் கலந்த காதலா","லுற்ற போழ்துயிர் கொடுக்கு மாற்றலாற்","கொற்ற வேலவன் குடையி னீழலார்","சுற்ற மாண்பினர் சுடரும் வேலினாய்"],"p":"","r":"Marusi says that those who live in the shade of the king's umbrella are men of learning joined by love who give their lives when the time comes.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalvi","natpu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 596","lb":"SO596","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["a king's learned retainers, bound to him by love, give their lives when need comes","general","reported",2,"maruci"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["மருசி","விஞ்சையர் வேந்தன்"],"ch":"so:27"},"so:597":{"book":"soolamani","num":597,"t":["கோதிலார் குல மக்கண் மாக்கண்மற்","றேதி லாரென வியைந்த தின்மையார்","ஆத லாற்றமர் பிறர்க ளாவதங்","கோதி லாரவர்க் குள்ளஃ தில்லையே"],"p":"","r":"The speaker says that among children of faultless clans none counts as a stranger, so kin and others alike have no fault in them.","n":"The compressed grammar and the doubled word for children make the sense uncertain; the speaker is taken to be the envoy opening his address to the king.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sutram","kudi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 597","lb":"SO597","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["மருசி","பிரசாபதி"],"ch":"so:28"},"so:598":{"book":"soolamani","num":598,"t":["வைய மின்புறின் மன்ன னின்புறும்","வெய்ய தொன்றுறிற் றானும் வெய்துறுஞ்","செய்ய கோலினாய் செப்ப லாவதன்","றைய தாரினா னருளின் வண்ணமே"],"p":"","r":"The speaker tells the king that when the world is glad the king is glad, and when something harsh touches it he too suffers, as the lord of the garland's grace shows.","n":"The last two lines are clipped and the speaker is not named; the envoy is assumed from 604 and 605.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["arasu","arul"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 598","lb":"SO598","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["a king shares the joy and the pain of the people he rules","general","reported",1,"maruci"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["மருசி","பிரசாபதி"],"ch":"so:28"},"so:599":{"book":"soolamani","num":599,"t":["வீவில் வீங்குநீர் வேலி வாழ்பவர்க்","காவி யாபவ ரரச ராதலாற்","காவ லோவுங்கொ லென்று கண்படான்","மாவ றானையம் மன்னர் மன்னனே"],"p":"","r":"The speaker says that because kings are the life of those who live within the sea's fence, the king of kings with the great army cannot sleep, wondering whether protection will fail.","n":"Set against verses 611 to 619, where the rival king sleeps and wakes in the women's company, this verse gives the standard those verses fall short of.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["arasu","sengol"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 599","lb":"SO599","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["a king's life is the guarding of his people, and the duty costs him his sleep","general","reported",2,"maruci"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["மருசி","பிரசாபதி"],"ch":"so:28"},"so:600":{"book":"soolamani","num":600,"t":["மங்குல் மாமழை மாரி வண்கையான்","பொங்கு காதலால் புதல்வர் தாமுமற்","றிங்கண் வேந்தர்கட் கேனை மான்கண்முன்","சிங்க வேறெனச் செப்பு நீரரே"],"p":"","r":"The speaker says the king, generous as rain-cloud and fond of his sons, is the sort who would tell of them as a lion among deer before the other kings.","n":"Whether the verse asks the king to speak of his sons or says that he will is not settled by the grammar.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["makkal","pugazh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 600","lb":"SO600","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["மருசி","பிரசாபதி"],"ch":"so:28"},"so:601":{"book":"soolamani","num":601,"t":["கைய வாச்சிலைக் காம னிங்கிரு","மெய்யி னால் வெளிப் பட்ட நீரதால்","வைய மாள்பவன் புதல்வர் வார்கழ","லையன் மார்கடம் மழகின் வண்ணமே"],"p":"","r":"The speaker says the love-god's bow has shown itself in the two bodies and that the sons of the world's ruler, bright with long anklets, are the beauty of their line.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","makkal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 601","lb":"SO601","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["குமரர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["மருசி","பிரசாபதி"],"ch":"so:28"},"so:602":{"book":"soolamani","num":602,"t":["சங்க வண்ணனார் தம்பி தானுநீர்","பொங்கு கார்முகில் புரையு மேனிய","னங்க ணிவ்வுல காள நாட்டிய","மங்க லப்பொறி மன்ன காண்டியால்"],"p":"","r":"The speaker says the conch-hued elder and the younger, dark as a swelling cloud, bear the auspicious mark set for ruling this world, which the king may see for himself.","n":"The mark is read as a sign of rule over the world; the verse gives no other detail.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","kurippu","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 602","lb":"SO602","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["குமரர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["மருசி","பிரசாபதி"],"ch":"so:28"},"so:603":{"book":"soolamani","num":603,"t":["செங்கண் மாலவன் தெய்வ மார்பகம்","பங்க யத்துமேற் பாவை தன்னுட","னங்கு லக்கொடி நங்கை சேர்வதற்","கிங்கன் மாதவ மெவன்கொல் செய்ததே"],"p":"","r":"The speaker asks what austerity the vine-slender woman did to be joined with the red-eyed one on whose divine breast the lotus-lady already rests.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thavam","azhagu","oozh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 603","lb":"SO603","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["a union so fitting must have been earned by austerity in a former life","general","reported",4,"maruci"]],"ag":["மங்கை"],"ec":[],"spk":["மருசி","பிரசாபதி"],"ch":"so:28"},"so:604":{"book":"soolamani","num":604,"t":["நங்கை யங்கவ னலத்திற் கொப்பவ","ளிங்கி வட்குவ றேந்த லில்லிவர்","பொங்கு புண்ணியம் புணர்த்த வாறிது","வெங்கண் யனையாய் வியக்கு நீரதே"],"p":"","r":"The speaker says the woman equals the lord in beauty, that nothing is wanting between them, and that their growing merit joined them, so the king should marvel.","n":"The verse says merit joined them but not the kind of former birth; the Jain frame is the work's, not stated here.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","oozh","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 604","lb":"SO604","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["the merit of former deeds is what joins this pair","general","reported",3,"maruci"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["மருசி","பிரசாபதி"],"ch":"so:28"},"so:605":{"book":"soolamani","num":605,"t":["என்று கூறலு மேந்து நீண்முடி","வென்றி நீள்புகழ் வேக யானையா","னன்று மற்றவற் கருளி யீந்தன","னின்று மின்சுடர் நிதியின் நீத்தமே"],"p":"","r":"On hearing this the crowned king of the swift elephants graciously gives the speaker a flood of shining wealth.","n":"The king is named by the epithet for swift elephants; he is taken to be the same Prajapati who sits on the throne in the earlier chapters.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 605","lb":"SO605","mm":1,"ev":"The Podanam king rewards the envoy with great wealth after the praise of his sons.","cl":[],"ag":["பிரசாபதி"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:28"},"so:606":{"book":"soolamani","num":606,"t":["மற்ற வன்றனை மனைபு கப்பணித்","துற்ற மந்திரத்தவர்க ளோடிருந்","தெற்று நாமினிச் செய்வ தென்றனன்","வெற்றி நீள்குடை வேந்தர் வேந்தனே"],"p":"","r":"The king sends the envoy off to his house and, with the counsellors who have joined him, asks what is now to be done.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["amaichu","soozhchi","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 606","lb":"SO606","mm":1,"ev":"The king sends the envoy to rest and asks his counsellors what to do next.","cl":[],"ag":["பிரசாபதி","மருசி","மந்திரக் கிழவர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:28"},"so:607":{"book":"soolamani","num":607,"t":["செங்க ணீன்முடிச் செல்வ சென்றொரு","திங்கள் நாளினுட் டிவிட்ட னாங்கொரு","சிங்கம் வாய் பகத் தெறுவ னென்பது","தங்கு கேள்வியான் றான்முன் சொன்னதே"],"p":"","r":"The speaker, calling the king lord of the red-eyed crown, reminds him that the astrologer said earlier that within a month Tivittan would split a lion's mouth.","n":"The verse names the reporter only as one of settled learning; whether the counsellors or the astrologer speak is not shown, and the counsellors are taken for the speaker.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["oozh","kaalam","kurippu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 607","lb":"SO607","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["மந்திரக் கிழவர்","பிரசாபதி"],"ch":"so:28"},"so:608":{"book":"soolamani","num":608,"t":["ஆதலா லஃதறியும் வாயிலா","வோது மாண்பினா னொருவ னெற்றனாய்த்","தீதி றானையாய் செல்ல வைப்பதே","நீதி யாமென நிகழ்த்தி னாரரோ"],"p":"","r":"Therefore, the speakers said, it is right to send a man of learned excellence who knows the matter, and the king is told so.","n":"The reply envoy is not named here; the verse says only that one is to be sent to the Vidyadhara king.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thoothu","amaichu","soozhchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 608","lb":"SO608","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["a learned man of knowledge is the proper one to send as envoy","general","reported",2,"amaichar"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["மந்திரக் கிழவர்","பிரசாபதி"],"ch":"so:28"},"so:609":{"book":"soolamani","num":609,"t":["உய்த்து ணர்ந்தவ ருரைத்த நீதிமேல்","வைத்த வொற்றினன் மன்ன னானபி","னத்தி றத்தனே யமர்ந்த சிந்தைய","னொத்த சுற்றமோ டுவகை யெய்தினான்"],"p":"","r":"The king, who rested his trust on spies and on the counsel thoughtful men had given, accepts it and rejoices with his kin.","n":"The wording that makes the king one trusting spies rests on a compressed line and may be read otherwise.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["arasu","otru","sutram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 609","lb":"SO609","mm":1,"ev":"The king accepts the counsel and rejoices with his kin.","cl":[],"ag":["பிரசாபதி"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:28"},"so:610":{"book":"soolamani","num":610,"t":["இத்தி சைக்கணிவ் வாறிது செல்லுநா","ளத்தி சைக்கணஞ் சப்படு மாழியா","னெத்தி சைக்கும் வெய் யோனியன் முன்னுற","வைத்தி சைத்தன மற்றதுங் கூறுவாம்"],"p":"","r":"The poet says that as days go on thus, the feared discus-bearer stays in the other quarter, and he now turns to tell of the fierce one set first before them.","n":"The verse moves the telling from Podanam to the rival king without naming him; only verse 619 gives his name.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["soozhchi","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 610","lb":"SO610","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:28"},"so:611":{"book":"soolamani","num":611,"t":["பஞ்சி மேன்மிதிக் கிற்பனிக் குந்தகை","யஞ்சி லோதிய ரம்முலை நாஞ்சிலா","மஞ்சு தோய்வரை மார்ப மடுத்துழத்","துஞ்ச லோவுந் தொழிலின னாயினான்"],"p":"","r":"The ruler who could not bear to tread on cotton bed for the coolness of it is shown lying on the breasts of delicate women, so absorbed in them that he never wearies.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","inbam","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 611","lb":"SO611","mm":1,"ev":"The ruler spends his hours lying with women of the palace.","cl":[],"ag":["அச்சுவ கண்டன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:28"},"so:612":{"book":"soolamani","num":612,"t":["முத்த வாணகை மோய்பவ ளத்துணி","யொத்த வாயமு தொண்கடி கைத்திரள்","வைத்த வாயின னாய்மட வார்கடஞ்","சித்த வாரிக ளுட்சென்று தங்கினான்"],"p":"","r":"The ruler holds in his mouth the red-coral-like lips and the sweet nectar of the women, and sinks into their hearts.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 612","lb":"SO612","mm":1,"ev":"The ruler takes the women's lips and sinks into love with them.","cl":[],"ag":["அச்சுவ கண்டன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:28"},"so:613":{"book":"soolamani","num":613,"t":["ஆரந் தங்கிய மார்பனு மந்தளிர்க்","காருங் கொம்பனை யாருங் கலந்துழித்","தாருங் கொங்கை ளும்பொரத் தாஞ்சில","வாரம் பட்டணி வண்டின மார்த்தவே"],"p":"","r":"When the garlanded man and the women mingle, the garlands and breasts strike one another and the bees on them hum together.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 613","lb":"SO613","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["அச்சுவ கண்டன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:28"},"so:614":{"book":"soolamani","num":614,"t":["வண்டு தோய்மது வாக்கிவள் ளத்தினுட்","கொண்டு கொம்பனை யார்கள் கொடுப்பவஃ","துண்டு மற்றவ ரொண்டுவர் வாயொளித்","தொண்டை யங்கனி யின்சுவை யெய்தினான்"],"p":"","r":"The women gather honey from the garlands and offer it, and the ruler takes it and also enjoys the sweetness of the lips of the red fruit.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 614","lb":"SO614","mm":1,"ev":"The women give the ruler honey and he drinks it with the taste of their lips.","cl":[],"ag":["அச்சுவ கண்டன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:28"},"so:615":{"book":"soolamani","num":615,"t":["தாம மென்குழ லார்தடங் கண்ணெனுந்","தேம யங்கிய செங்கழு நீரணி","காம மென்பதொர் கள்ளது வுண்டரோ","யாம மும்பக லும்மயர் வெய்தினான்"],"p":"","r":"The women's flower-hung hair and eyes are the honey-drenched lilies, desire is the toddy they offer, and the ruler is lost in it through day and night.","n":"The poet makes desire the drink, and so turns the women's eyes into the cup; the verse reports it without comment.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","kal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 615","lb":"SO615","mm":1,"ev":"The ruler, drunk on desire, loses track of day and night.","cl":[],"ag":["அச்சுவ கண்டன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:28"},"so:616":{"book":"soolamani","num":616,"t":["சுற்று வார்முலை யார்தந் துகிற்றட","முற்று மூழ்கும் பொழுது முனிவவ","ருற்றபோழ் துணர்த் தும்பொழு தும்மலான்","மற்றொர் போழ்திலன் மன்னவ னாயினான்"],"p":"","r":"Whether he is plunged in the women's soft clothes or called awake by a sage, he never, at any other hour, behaves as a king.","n":"The first lines of the verse are hard to read, and the sage's part is uncertain; the sense taken is that his only role is as lover.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["arasu","kaamam","pochchaappu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 616","lb":"SO616","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["a king who gives all his hours to pleasure no longer rules","general","reported",3,"narrator"]],"ag":["அச்சுவ கண்டன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:28"},"so:617":{"book":"soolamani","num":617,"t":["மண்க னிந்த முழவின் மடந்தையர்","கண்க னிந்திடு நாடகக் காட்சியும்","பண்க னிந்தவின் றீங்குரற் பாடலும்","விண்க னிந்திட வேவிழை வேய்தினான்"],"p":"","r":"He takes his pleasure in the women's dance on the drum-filled floor, in their song of sweet notes, and he longs to be in heaven's company.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 617","lb":"SO617","mm":1,"ev":"The ruler takes pleasure in dance and song.","cl":[],"ag":["அச்சுவ கண்டன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:28"},"so:618":{"book":"soolamani","num":618,"t":["வாவி யும்மது மண்டபச் சோலையுந்","தூவி மஞ்ஞை துதைந்தசெய் குன்றமும்","பாவும் வெண்மண லும்புனற் பட்டமு","மேவு நீர்மைய னாய்விளை யாடினான்"],"p":"","r":"He takes his play in pools, wine-halls, gardens, peacock hills, white sand and water-sport.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inbam","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 618","lb":"SO618","mm":1,"ev":"The ruler spends his time in pleasure grounds and water-play.","cl":[],"ag":["அச்சுவ கண்டன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:28"},"so:619":{"book":"soolamani","num":619,"t":["மின்னுஞ் செங்கதிர் மண்டிலம் வெய்தொளி","துன்னுஞ் திங்கட் பனிச்சுடர் தண்ணிது","என்னு மித்துணை யும்மறி யான்களித்","தன்ன னாயின னச்சுவ கண்டனே"],"p":"","r":"He is so given over that he knows neither the sun's heat nor the moon's cool, and the verse names him Acuvakanthan.","n":"Only here is the rival king named; the earlier verses of the chapter, about a discus-bearer, are read as his.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","pochchaappu","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 619","lb":"SO619","mm":1,"ev":"Acuvakanthan loses all awareness in pleasure.","cl":[["pleasure that blots out awareness is a king's blindness","general","reported",2,"narrator"]],"ag":["அச்சுவ கண்டன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:28"},"so:620":{"book":"soolamani","num":620,"t":["சீறிற் றேந்துணர் வின்றிச் செகுத்திடு","மாறுகண் டென்பதோர் மாற்றம் பொறான்மனந்","தேறின் யாரையும் தேறுஞ் செருக்கொடிவ்","வாறு சென்ற தவற்கர சென்பவே"],"p":"","r":"The poet says that a king who destroys in anger without weighing a matter and cannot bear an opposing word, and who, whenever his mind clears, thinks he can see through everyone, is carried along by his pride.","n":"The poet opens the rival's portrait with his temper, ahead of the scene that shows it; the closing phrase of the verse is read as the poet's own name for such a king.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["agandhai","vekuli","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 620","lb":"SO620","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["a king who strikes in anger without examining the matter is carried off by his own pride","general","reported",1,"narrator"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:29"},"so:621":{"book":"soolamani","num":621,"t":["தோடு மல்கு சுரும்பணி கோதையர்","கோடி மென்றுகிற் குய்யத் தடம்படித்","தாடித் தன்னணை யாமையிற் பூமகள்","ஊட லுற்றிடம் பார்த்துள ளாயினாள்"],"p":"","r":"While the king plays with the garlanded women in the pond, the goddess of fortune, left unembraced, takes offence and looks for a place to go.","n":"The verse says only that she looks for a place, not that she has gone; it is read as a forecast of loss, and sets the pond scene against the sleeplessness the envoy praised in 599.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","kaamam","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 621","lb":"SO621","mm":1,"ev":"The king's pleasure in the pond costs him the favour of the goddess of fortune, who begins to look elsewhere.","cl":[["a king's wealth leaves him when he neglects it for pleasure","general","reported",3,"narrator"]],"ag":["அச்சுவ கண்டன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:29"},"so:622":{"book":"soolamani","num":622,"t":["ஆங்கொர் நாளிறை பெற்றறி வின்கடல்","தாங்கி னான்சத விந்துவென் பானுளன்","நீங்க லாப்புக ழான்ற னிமித்திகன்","வீங்கு வெல்கழ லார்கு விளம்பினான்"],"p":"","r":"One day the king's astrologer Satavintu, a sea of learning with unfading fame, speaks to the king of the strong anklets.","n":"This is the second court in the poem to hear an astrologer; in 103 to 109 the Podanam king's astrologer reads a dream, and here the rival's astrologer reads the same fortune from the other side.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalvi","kurippu","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 622","lb":"SO622","mm":1,"ev":"Satavintu, the astrologer of the rival king, begins to speak to him.","cl":[],"ag":["சதவிந்து"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:29"},"so:623":{"book":"soolamani","num":623,"t":["மன்ன கேள்வளை மேய்திரை மண்டிலந்","தன்னை யாள்பவர்க் கோதின் தங்கணே","பன்னி னாறு பகைக்குல மாமவை","முன்னம் வெல்கவென் றான்முகம் நோக்கினான்"],"p":"","r":"The astrologer addresses the king and says that among those who rule the sea-ringed land there are said to be six hostile groups, which must be conquered first.","n":"The verse does not say whether the six are outer rivals or inner faults; the next verse, which speaks of one who has conquered himself, is the only hint that the poet means the latter.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["arasu","pagai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 623","lb":"SO623","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["a ruler must first overcome six classes of foe","general","reported",3,"sathavindhu"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["சதவிந்து","அச்சுவ கண்டன்"],"ch":"so:29"},"so:624":{"book":"soolamani","num":624,"t":["தன்னை வென்றதண் டார்வய வேந்தனைப்","பின்னை வேறல பிறர்க்கரி தாதலான்","மன்ன மற்றவ னாளும் வரைப்பகம்","பொன்னின் மாரி பொழிந்திடு நன்றரோ"],"p":"","r":"The astrologer says that a king who has conquered himself is hard for others to conquer, and that gold rains in the land where he rules.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","adakkam","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 624","lb":"SO624","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["a king who has conquered himself is hard for others to conquer","general","reported",1,"sathavindhu"],["the land of a self-mastering king is rich, as if gold rained on it","general","reported",4,"sathavindhu"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["சதவிந்து","அச்சுவ கண்டன்"],"ch":"so:29"},"so:625":{"book":"soolamani","num":625,"t":["மாசி றண்டன்ன தோண்மன்ன மன்னிய","கோசி றண்டத்த னாய்விடிற் கொற்றவ","னேசி றண்டம் பரவவின் வையக","மாசி றண்டத்த னாயினி தாளுமே"],"p":"","r":"The astrologer says that if the king stays with a sceptre without fault he will rule gladly, and the other kings, praising him, will serve under him.","n":"The words for the sceptre and the rod are doubled and the grammar is clipped, so the sense of the condition is a best reading.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["arasu","sengol"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 625","lb":"SO625","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["a king ruling with a faultless sceptre rules in ease and wins the praise of other kings","general","reported",4,"sathavindhu"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["சதவிந்து","அச்சுவ கண்டன்"],"ch":"so:29"},"so:626":{"book":"soolamani","num":626,"t":["பெற்ற தன்முத லாப்பின் பெறாததுஞ்","சுற்றி வந்தடை யும்படி சூழ்ந்துசென்","றுற்ற வான்பொருள் காத்துய ரீகையுங்","கற்ற வன்பிறர் காவல னாகுவான்"],"p":"","r":"The astrologer says the learned man who keeps what he has earned, draws in what he has not yet, guards the great wealth and gives it away becomes the guardian of others.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","kalvi","eegai","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 626","lb":"SO626","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["a learned king who earns, guards and gives wealth becomes the protector of others","general","reported",3,"sathavindhu"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["சதவிந்து","அச்சுவ கண்டன்"],"ch":"so:29"},"so:627":{"book":"soolamani","num":627,"t":["அருக்கன் றன்னறி வாக வலர்ந்தநீர்த்","திருக்க வின்றசெல் வச்செழுந் தாமரை","செருக்கெ னப்படுந் திண்பனி வீழுமேல்","முருக்கு மற்றத னைமுகத் தாரினாய்"],"p":"","r":"The astrologer says that pride is the dense frost which, if it falls, destroys the lotus of wealth that blooms in the water of one's own understanding.","n":"The sun in the first line is the king's own knowledge, so the image makes understanding both the cause of wealth and the thing pride darkens.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["agandhai","selvam","kedu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 627","lb":"SO627","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["pride destroys the wealth that understanding has built","general","reported",3,"sathavindhu"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["சதவிந்து","அச்சுவ கண்டன்"],"ch":"so:29"},"so:628":{"book":"soolamani","num":628,"t":["இகழ்ச்சி யிற்கெடு வார்களை யெண்ணுக","மகிழ்ச்சி யுண்மதி மைந்துறும் போதெனப்","புகழ்ச்சி நூலுட் புகன்றனர் பூவினுட்","டிகழ்ச்சி செல்பொன் மணிமுடி மன்னனே"],"p":"","r":"The astrologer says the king should think of those ruined by negligence, and be watchful in the hour of his own strength, as the books of praise say.","n":"The counsel keeps close to Kural 539 in sense and in the pairing of joy and ruin, but the words are not those of the couplet, so no echo is marked; the verse names its source as the books of praise.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pochchaappu","kedu","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 628","lb":"SO628","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["those ruined by negligence should be remembered when one is strong and glad","general","reported",1,"sathavindhu"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["சதவிந்து","அச்சுவ கண்டன்"],"ch":"so:29"},"so:629":{"book":"soolamani","num":629,"t":["நெறியி னீதிக்க னேரிவை யொப்பவு","மறிதி நீயவை நிற்க வழன்று நீ","செறுதி யேனுஞ்செம் பொன்முடி மன்னவோர்","உறுதி யானுரைப் பானுறு கின்றதே"],"p":"","r":"The astrologer says the king already knows the even-handed justice of the right way, but that, though the king may be angered, he will speak what is for the king's good.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","vekuli"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 629","lb":"SO629","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["a counsellor must say what is for the king's good even at the risk of his anger","general","reported",3,"sathavindhu"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["சதவிந்து","அச்சுவ கண்டன்"],"ch":"so:29"},"so:630":{"book":"soolamani","num":630,"t":["என்ற லும்மிணர் வேய்முடி மாலையா","னன்று சொல்லுக வென்று நகைமணிக்","குன்ற மன்னதிண் டோ ண்மிசைக் குண்டலஞ்","சென்று மின்சொரி யச்செவி தாழ்த்தினான்"],"p":"","r":"On hearing him the king, wreathed in flowers, says he should speak well and bends his ear, the earring on his strong shoulder shedding light.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kelvi","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 630","lb":"SO630","mm":1,"ev":"The king tells the astrologer to speak and bends his ear to listen.","cl":[],"ag":["அச்சுவ கண்டன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:29"},"so:631":{"book":"soolamani","num":631,"t":["பூமி மேற்புரி சைம்மதிற் போதன","நாம நன்னக ராளு நகைமலர்த்","தாம நீண்முடி யான்றன் புதல்வர்கள்","காம வேளனை யாருளர் காண்டியால்"],"p":"","r":"The astrologer tells the king to look at the sons of the king who rules the great city of Podanam, men like the god of love.","n":"The verse names the princes' beauty and not yet the danger; the warning is in 632.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kurippu","azhagu","makkal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 631","lb":"SO631","mm":1,"ev":"Satavintu points the king to the princes of Podanam as the matter of his warning.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["சதவிந்து","அச்சுவ கண்டன்"],"ch":"so:29"},"so:632":{"book":"soolamani","num":632,"t":["ஏந்து தோளவ ருள்ளிளை யானமக்","காய்ந்த தொல்பகை யாகுமென் றேயுறப்","போந்தொர் புன்சொ னிமித்தம் புறப்பட","வேந்த யான்மனத் தின்மெலி கேனரோ"],"p":"","r":"The astrologer says the youngest of those strong-shouldered princes will become our ancient enemy, and asks whether a petty word of omen should make him weaken at heart.","n":"Who speaks the second half, and whether the closing question is the astrologer's own or his report of the king's coming mood, is not clear from the Tamil.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kurippu","pagai","makkal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 632","lb":"SO632","mm":1,"ev":"Satavintu names the younger Podanam prince as the foe the omen points to.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["சதவிந்து","அச்சுவ கண்டன்"],"ch":"so:29"},"so:633":{"book":"soolamani","num":633,"t":["முத்த நிண்முடி யான்மூன்ன மற்றதற்","கொத்த வாறுணர்ந் தீயென வென்செயு","மைத்த கைமனத் தன்மணித் தன்னெனக்","கைத்த லங்கதிர் வீச மறித்தனன்"],"p":"","r":"The crowned king, told to weigh a fit course, waves the matter away with the shining palm of his hand.","n":"The first two lines are hard to parse; the gesture of the palm is clear, and the rest is read as the astrologer's urging.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["agandhai","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 633","lb":"SO633","mm":1,"ev":"The king waves off the astrologer's advice with his hand.","cl":[],"ag":["அச்சுவ கண்டன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:29"},"so:634":{"book":"soolamani","num":634,"t":["மிகையின் வந்தவிச் சாதர வேந்தர்தந்","தொகையை வென்றவென் றோளுள வாப்பிற","பகையி னிப்படர்ந் தென்செயு மென்றன","னகைகொ ணீண்முடி நச்சர வம்மனான்"],"p":"","r":"The king smiles and says that his shoulders have conquered the whole host of Vidyadhara kings, so what can other foes do against him.","n":"The boast is the very pride that 627 warned of, spoken by the one warned, and 635 to 640 continue it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["agandhai","vali","pagai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 634","lb":"SO634","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["a king who has beaten many kings need not fear lesser foes","general","reported",2,"acuvakanthan"]],"ag":["அச்சுவ கண்டன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["அச்சுவ கண்டன்",""],"ch":"so:29"},"so:635":{"book":"soolamani","num":635,"t":["மாசி லாலவட் டத்தெழு மாதரும்","வீச விண்டொடு மேருத் துளங்குமோ","பேசின் மானிடப் பேதைக ளாற்றலா","லாசி றோளிவை தாமசை வெய்துமோ"],"p":"","r":"The king asks whether Mount Meru would shake at the fanning of a palm leaf, and so whether the strength of mere humans can disturb his faultless shoulders.","n":"The boast holds that his own strength is what stands in the way, and that mortal men are the leaf beside the mountain.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vali","agandhai","pagai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 635","lb":"SO635","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["அச்சுவ கண்டன்",""],"ch":"so:29"},"so:636":{"book":"soolamani","num":636,"t":["வேழத் தின்மருப் புத்தடம் வீறுவ","வாழைத் தண்டினு ளூன்ற மழுங்குமோ","வாழித் தானவர் தானையை யட்டவென்","பாழித் தோள்மனித் தர்க்குப் பணியுமோ"],"p":"","r":"The king asks whether a great elephant's tusk blunts on a plantain stem, and so whether his shoulders, which slew the armies of the Danavas, would bend to men.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vali","agandhai","pagai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 636","lb":"SO636","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["அச்சுவ கண்டன்",""],"ch":"so:29"},"so:637":{"book":"soolamani","num":637,"t":["வேக மாருதம் வீசவிண் பாற்சிறு","மேக சாலம் விரிந்தெதிர் செல்லுமோ","வேக மாயவென் சீற்றமஞ் சாதெதி","ராக மானுடர் தாமசை கிற்பவோ"],"p":"","r":"The king asks whether a handful of small clouds can march against a fast wind, and so whether men, as his foes, could stir against his fury.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vali","agandhai","pagai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 637","lb":"SO637","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["அச்சுவ கண்டன்",""],"ch":"so:29"},"so:638":{"book":"soolamani","num":638,"t":["குலிச மிந்திரன் கொண்டு பணிக்கு மேன்","மலையின் மாசிக ரங்களும் வீழ்த்திடு","நிலைய வெஞ்சுட ராழி நினைப்பனேற்","றொலைவில் வானவர் தோளுந் துணிக்குமே"],"p":"","r":"The king says that if Indra's thunderbolt were set against him it would still fall short, and that if he turned his mind to his fierce shining discus he could cut down the shoulders of the gods.","n":"The discus is the weapon 402 says Tivittan will take from him with his life; the boast is placed so that the reader hears that prophecy beneath it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vali","padai","agandhai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 638","lb":"SO638","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["அச்சுவ கண்டன்",""],"ch":"so:29"},"so:639":{"book":"soolamani","num":639,"t":["விச்சை மற்றவர் தம்மை விடுப்பதோ","ரிச்சை யென்கணுண் டாமெனின் யாவரே","யச்ச மின்றி நிற் பாரந் நிமித்த நூல்","பொச்ச லாங்கொல் புலந் தெழு நீர்மையாய்"],"p":"","r":"The king asks whether, if he wished to send a spell against them, anyone could stand fearless, unless the book of omens is a deceit, and he calls the speaker one of angry nature.","n":"The grammar of the question can be read as a challenge to the astrologer or as the king's wavering over the omen; the second reading is the more cautious.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kurippu","acham","vali"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 639","lb":"SO639","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["the book of omens may be false","general","reported",3,"acuvakanthan"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["அச்சுவ கண்டன்","சதவிந்து"],"ch":"so:29"},"so:640":{"book":"soolamani","num":640,"t":["புலவர் சொல்வழி போற்றில னென்பதோ","ரலகிற் புன்சொலுக் கெஞ்சுவ னல்லதே","லுலக மொப்ப வுடன்றெழு மாயினு","மலைவன் மற்றதன் கண்மதிப் பில்லையே"],"p":"","r":"The king says he has not been one to obey the word of the learned, and that he would fear only a petty word; even if the whole world rose together in anger he would fight it, and he does not value such an omen.","n":"The verse is the king's refusal to be warned, and his own claim that the sages' word counts for little; the epic does not endorse it.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["agandhai","kurippu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 640","lb":"SO640","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["a brave king need not heed a petty word of omen","general","reported",2,"acuvakanthan"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["அச்சுவ கண்டன்",""],"ch":"so:29"},"so:641":{"book":"soolamani","num":641,"t":["ஆத லாலாதற் கேற்ற தமைச்சர்க","ளோதி யாங்குணர்ந் தீகவென் றொட்டினான்","யாதுந் தன்கணல் லார்செயற் கேன்றதோ","ரேத முண்டெனு மெண்ணமில் லாதவன்"],"p":"","r":"The narrator says that the king, who saw no fault in any good man's deed, told fit counsellors to speak and let it be understood.","n":"He calls counsel only after saying in 634 to 640 that he would fight regardless, so the order may be formal; the last two lines are read in this way.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["amaichu","soozhchi","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 641","lb":"SO641","mm":1,"ev":"The king orders his counsellors to speak and give their counsel.","cl":[],"ag":["அச்சுவ கண்டன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:29"},"so:642":{"book":"soolamani","num":642,"t":["அலங்க லாழியி னானது கூறலுங்","கலங்கு நூற்கரு மத்தொழின் மாக்கடம்","புலங்கொள் சூழ்ச்சிய ராகிப் புகன்றன","ருலங்கொள் தோளவ னுக்குணர் வாயினார்"],"p":"","r":"When the king of the garland and discus had said this, his learned counsellors, skilled in the work of the books, spoke to him with their own plans and gave him understanding.","n":"The phrase for the counsellors' learning is hard to read, and the content of the advice is left to the following verses.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["amaichu","soozhchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 642","lb":"SO642","mm":1,"ev":"The counsellors answer the king with their own advice.","cl":[],"ag":["கருமத்தொழின் மாக்கள்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:29"},"so:643":{"book":"soolamani","num":643,"t":["எரியுந் தீத்திர ளெட்டுணைத் தாயினுங்","கரியச் சுட்டிடுங் காந்திக் கனலுமேற்","றெரியிற் றொல்பகை தான்சிறி தாயினும்","விரியப் பெற்றபின் வென்றிடு கிற்குமே"],"p":"","r":"The speaker warns that a fire, however small its spark, blackens what it touches, and that a foe of old, though small, can win once he is allowed to spread.","n":"The speakers are not named; they are read as the frightened ones whom verse 646 says spoke before Arimanju. The rival king is not named in the verse.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pagai","sirumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 643","lb":"SO643","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["a small enemy left alone until he has spread can come to conquer","general","reported",3,"acuva_amaichar"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["கருமத்தொழின் மாக்கள்","அச்சுவ கண்டன்"],"ch":"so:30"},"so:644":{"book":"soolamani","num":644,"t":["முட்கொணச்சு மரமுளை யாகவே","யுட்கி நீக்கி னுகிரினுங் கொல்லலாம்","வட்கி நீண்டதற் பின் மழு வுந்தறு","கட்கு டாரமுந் தாங்களை கிற்பவோ"],"p":"","r":"The speaker says a poisonous thorny tree can be killed at the sprout, even with a nail, but once it has grown tall neither axe nor hatchet will master it.","n":"The first line is compressed (the words for the poisonous thorn tree run together), and the sense of the second line is taken from the whole image.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pagai","kaalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 644","lb":"SO644","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["an enemy must be uprooted while he is young, since once grown he cannot be cut down","general","reported",3,"acuva_amaichar"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["கருமத்தொழின் மாக்கள்","அச்சுவ கண்டன்"],"ch":"so:30"},"so:645":{"book":"soolamani","num":645,"t":["சிறிய வென்றிக ழார்பகை சென்றுசென்","றறிய லாவவன் றாலணி மாமலர்","வெறியும் வேரியும் விம்மி விரிந்துதேன்","செறியுந் தொங்கற்செம் பொன்முடி மன்னனே"],"p":"","r":"The speaker urges the king not to despise the foe as small but to go again and again and learn of him, a king with a honeyed garland and a crown of red gold.","n":"The syntax is thin: whether the last line names the foe or only addresses the king is not fixed by the words, and it is read here as the foe.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pagai","aaraaydhal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 645","lb":"SO645","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["a foe must not be scorned as small but must be known by going to see","general","reported",1,"acuva_amaichar"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["கருமத்தொழின் மாக்கள்","அச்சுவ கண்டன்"],"ch":"so:30"},"so:646":{"book":"soolamani","num":646,"t":["அஞ்சி நின்றவர் கூறிய பின்னரி","மஞ்சு வென்பவன் சொல்லுமற் றாங்கவன்","செஞ்செ வேபகை யாமெனிற் றேர்ந்துகண்","டெஞ்சி றொல்புக ழாய்பின்னை யெண்ணுவாம்"],"p":"","r":"After the frightened speakers have spoken, a man named Arimanju says that if the foe proves truly an enemy, the king should first examine and see, and only then take counsel.","n":"The name is read as Arimanju because the end of line 1 runs into it; the verse opens as the poet's link and passes into his speech.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["aaraaydhal","amaichu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 646","lb":"SO646","mm":1,"ev":"Arimanju steps in after the frightened counsellors and urges the king to examine the foe before he plans against him.","cl":[["a foe is to be tested and known before action is planned against him","general","reported",3,"arimanju"]],"ag":["மஞ்சு"],"ec":[],"spk":["மஞ்சு","அச்சுவ கண்டன்"],"ch":"so:30"},"so:647":{"book":"soolamani","num":647,"t":["பகைய லாதவ ரைப்பகை யாக்கலும்","நகையி றீமனத் தாமரை நண் பெண்ணலு","முகையின் வேய்ந்தமென் மொய்ம்மலர்க் கண்ணியாய்","மிகையின் மற்றவை பின்னை வெதுப்புமே"],"p":"","r":"Arimanju says that making enemies of those who are not enemies, and counting as friends those of an ill heart, will both torment later through excess.","n":"This sets Arimanju's caution against the counsellors' haste in 643 and 644. The word in line 2 for the ill-hearted one is corrupt and is read as the ill-hearted person.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pagai","natpu","therithal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 647","lb":"SO647","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["making foes of the harmless and friends of the ill-minded both bring later pain","general","reported",1,"arimanju"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["மஞ்சு","அச்சுவ கண்டன்"],"ch":"so:30"},"so:648":{"book":"soolamani","num":648,"t":["அறியத் தேறுந் திறத்ததெவ் வாறெனிற்","றிறையிற் கென்று விடுதும்விட் டாற்றிறை","முறையிற் றந்து முகமன் மொழிந்தெதிர்","குறையிற் கொற்றவ குற்றமங் கில்லையே"],"p":"","r":"Arimanju proposes to learn the foe's mind by sending to him for tribute: if he gives it in due order with courteous words, well; if he falls short, no fault will lie with the king.","n":"The test is built to fail: a king who pays acknowledges the overlord, and one who does not has put himself in the wrong. The last line is compressed.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["aaraaydhal","soozhchi","pagai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 648","lb":"SO648","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["a demand for tribute is a fair test of a rival, and his refusal clears the demander of fault","general","reported",4,"arimanju"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["மஞ்சு","அச்சுவ கண்டன்"],"ch":"so:30"},"so:649":{"book":"soolamani","num":649,"t":["என்ற லும்மிது நன்றென வேந்தொளி","நின்ற நீண்முடி நீடிணர்க் கண்ணியான்","சென்று தூதுவர் தாந்திறை கொள்கென","வென்றி வேலவன் மேல்விடை யேயினான்"],"p":"","r":"On hearing this the crowned king calls it good and sends messengers to the spear-bearing king with the order to take the tribute.","n":"Neither king is named; the crowned king is read as Acuvakanthan and the spear-bearer as the king of Podanam, from the arrival in 651.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thoothu","soozhchi","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 649","lb":"SO649","mm":1,"ev":"Acuvakanthan accepts Arimanju's plan and sends envoys to the victorious-spear king to demand tribute.","cl":[],"ag":["அச்சுவ கண்டன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:30"},"so:650":{"book":"soolamani","num":650,"t":["ஊட்ட ரக்குண்ட கோலரொண் கோலத்த","ரோட்ட ரும்பொறி யொற்றிய வோலையர்","நாட்டி யம்முணர் வாரொரு நால்வர்சேண்","மோட்டெ ழின்முகில் சூழ்நெறி முன்னினார்"],"p":"","r":"Four envoys in bright dress, bearing letters pressed with an unbroken seal, set out together along a path through the clouds.","n":"Several words in lines 1 and 3 are obscure (the ones for the red-lac staves and for their knowledge of the dance), so the description of the four is partly guessed.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thoothu","soozhchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 650","lb":"SO650","mm":1,"ev":"Four envoys with sealed letters set out through the clouds.","cl":[],"ag":["தூதுவர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:30"},"so:651":{"book":"soolamani","num":651,"t":["தீதறு தென்மலை மாதிர முன்னுபு","தூதுவர் சூழ்சுடர் சூடிய சூளிகை","யோதின ரோதி யுலப்பற வோங்கிய","போதன மாநகர் புக்கன ரன்றே"],"p":"","r":"The envoys make for the southern quarter and its hills and, speaking on and on, enter the great city of Podanam that rises high.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thoothu","naadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 651","lb":"SO651","mm":1,"ev":"The envoys enter the great city of Podanam.","cl":[],"ag":["தூதுவர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:30"},"so:652":{"book":"soolamani","num":652,"t":["செஞ்சுடர் மின்னொளி சென்று பரந்திட","மஞ்சொடு வைகிய மாமணி மாளிகை","வெஞ்சுடர் வீதி விலக்குவ கண்டுதம்","விஞ்சையர் செல்வம் வெறுத்தன ரன்றே"],"p":"","r":"The mansions, lit like lightning among the clouds, block the sun's street, and seeing them the envoys turn in disgust from the wealth of their own Vidyadhara kind.","n":"The envoys are taken as Vidyadharas because the verse speaks of the Vidyadhara wealth they leave behind; the verse does not call them so.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","perumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 652","lb":"SO652","mm":1,"ev":"The envoys, seeing the mansions of Podanam, come to disdain the wealth of their own people.","cl":[],"ag":["தூதுவர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:30"},"so:653":{"book":"soolamani","num":653,"t":["முரி முழாவொலி விம்மி முரன்றெழு","காரி மிழார்கலி யான் மயி லாலுவ","சோரி முழாவிழ விற்றெரு துற்றபின்","சீரி மிழாற்பொலி வெய்தினர் சென்றே"],"p":"","r":"The swelling sound of drums, the peacocks that dance to it as to thunder and the festive streets give the envoys, as they pass, a glow of splendour.","n":"The wording is dense and the last two lines are read only for their general sense, the subject of the verb for glory being supplied.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 653","lb":"SO653","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:30"},"so:654":{"book":"soolamani","num":654,"t":["சூளிகை சூடிய சூல விலைத்தலை","மாளிகை மேன்மழை மாமுகில் போழ்தலின்","னீளிய நீரரு வித்திரள் வீழ்வன","காளைக டாதைந கர்ப்பல கண்டார்"],"p":"","r":"Cascades fall from the clouds that the mansions' spire-tops cleave, and the envoys see the many streets of the city.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","naadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 654","lb":"SO654","mm":1,"ev":"The envoys walk the streets of Podanam under mansion-cascades.","cl":[],"ag":["தூதுவர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:30"},"so:655":{"book":"soolamani","num":655,"t":["கூடுநர் கோவை மணிக்கலை யுக்கவு","மூடினர் சிந்திய வொண்சுடர் மாலையு","மூடிய மூரி நெடுந்தெரு வொப்பவு","மூடு செலற்கரி தாயிட ருற்றார்"],"p":"","r":"The long streets lie covered with the gem girdles of lovers and the garlands flung by those in sulks, so that the envoys find passage hard.","n":"The picture of lovers' quarrels is read from the words for the girdles and garlands; the sentence is long and compressed.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","kaamam","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 655","lb":"SO655","mm":1,"ev":"The envoys struggle to pass through streets strewn with ornaments.","cl":[],"ag":["தூதுவர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:30"},"so:656":{"book":"soolamani","num":656,"t":["மூரிநடைக்களி யானை மதத்தினொ","டேரி னடைக்கலி மாதம் விலாழியு","மோரி நுரைப்ப வுகுத்த பெருங்கடை","வேரி வெறிக்கள மொப்பது கண்டார்"],"p":"","r":"The street is mired with the rut and spittle of the great elephants, so that the envoys take it for a field of fragrant toddy-mad ground.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 656","lb":"SO656","mm":1,"ev":"The envoys see the streets of rut-wet ground.","cl":[],"ag":["தூதுவர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:30"},"so:657":{"book":"soolamani","num":657,"t":["வண்டு படக்குவ ளைப்பிணை நக்கலர்","விண்ட மதுப்பரு கிக்களி யின்மதர்","கொண்டு நடைக்களி யன்ன மிரைப்பதொர்","மண்டு புனற்புரி சைப்பதி சார்ந்தார்"],"p":"","r":"The envoys come to the walled city that lies behind a rushing moat, where bees cluster on lilies and swans cry out.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aran","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 657","lb":"SO657","mm":1,"ev":"The envoys reach the walled royal town.","cl":[],"ag":["தூதுவர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:30"},"so:658":{"book":"soolamani","num":658,"t":["கோயின் முகத்தது கோடுயர் சூளிகை","வேயின் முகத்ததின் மாமழை வீழ்வது","ஞாயின் முகத்த நகைத்திரண் முத்தணி","வாயின் முகத்து மடுத்திது சொன்னார்"],"p":"","r":"Standing before the palace gate with its high turrets, they speak to the guard.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thoothu","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 658","lb":"SO658","mm":1,"ev":"The envoys, at the palace gate, address the guard.","cl":[],"ag":["தூதுவர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:30"},"so:659":{"book":"soolamani","num":659,"t":["வேய்தலை நீடிய வெள்ளி விலங்கலி","னோய்தலி லொண்சுட ராழியி னான்றமர்","வாய்தலி னின்றனர் வந்தென மன்னன்முன்","நீதலை சென்றுரை நீள்கடை காப்போய்"],"p":"","r":"The envoys tell the gatekeeper to go before the king and say that the men of the bright discus-bearer, come from the silver ridge, stand at the gate.","n":"The envoys name only their master's discus and do not name him; the ridge of silver is read as the Vidyadhara ridge.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thoothu","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 659","lb":"SO659","mm":1,"ev":"The envoys have the gatekeeper announce them as the men of the discus-bearer.","cl":[],"ag":["தூதுவர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["தூதுவர்","வாயில் காக்கும்"],"ch":"so:30"},"so:660":{"book":"soolamani","num":660,"t":["என்றவர் கூற விருங்கடை யானடி","னான்றென நாறொளி நீண்முடி யானடி","மன்ற வணங்கி மொழிந்தனன் மன்னனும்","ஒன்றிய போதக என்ப துரைத்தான்"],"p":"","r":"At their words the gatekeeper bows to the king's feet and tells him, and the king says that they may come in.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","thoothu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 660","lb":"SO660","mm":1,"ev":"The gatekeeper reports the envoys, and the king bids them enter.","cl":[],"ag":["வாயில் காக்கும்","பிரசாபதி"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:30"},"so:661":{"book":"soolamani","num":661,"t":["பொன்னவிர் நீள்கடை காவலன் போதக","வென்னலி னெய்தி யிலங்கொளி நீண்முடி","மன்னவன் வார்கழல் வாழ்த்திமடக்கிய","சொன்னவி லோலைகை தொழுதன ரீந்தார்"],"p":"","r":"The gatekeeper tells the envoys to come in, and they approach the king, bless his anklets and, with folded hands, give him the letter they had folded.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thoothu","panivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 661","lb":"SO661","mm":1,"ev":"The envoys enter, bless the king and give him the folded letter.","cl":[],"ag":["வாயில் காக்கும்","தூதுவர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:30"},"so:662":{"book":"soolamani","num":662,"t":["வாசகன் மற்றது வாசினை செய்தபின்","மாசக னீள்முடி மன்னவன் முன்னிவை","தேசக மூசிய வாழியன் சீர்த்தம","ரோசைக ளோலை கொடொப்ப வுரைத்தர்"],"p":"","r":"After the reader has read the letter before the king, the discus-king's men repeat in matching words what it says.","n":"The last word (உரைத்தர்) is short of a syllable and the sense of line 3 is clipped; the verse is taken as a link to the demand in 663 to 665.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thoothu","sol"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 662","lb":"SO662","mm":1,"ev":"A reader reads the discus-king's letter aloud and his men repeat its demand.","cl":[],"ag":["வாசகன்","தூதுவர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:30"},"so:663":{"book":"soolamani","num":663,"t":["ஊடக மோடி யெரிந்தொளி முந்தூறு","மாடக மாயிர கோடியு மல்லது","சூடக முன்கையர் தோடக மெல்லடி","நாடக ராயிர நாரியர் தம்மையும்"],"p":"","r":"The speaker begins the list of what must be given: gleaming mansions by the thousand crore and a thousand dancing women with bangled arms and ear-rolls.","n":"The sentence runs on through 664 and 665 and ends there. The word in line 2 for the first thing demanded is obscure, and 'mansions' is a guess.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["selvam","pagai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 663","lb":"SO663","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["தூதுவர்","பிரசாபதி"],"ch":"so:30"},"so:664":{"book":"soolamani","num":664,"t":["தெண்டிரை சிந்திய சங்கொடு செங்கதி","ரெண்டர னம்பவ ழக்கொடி யீட்டமும்","கண்டிரள் முத்தொடு காழகி லந்துகில்","பண்டரு நீரன வும்பல் பண்டமும்"],"p":"","r":"The list goes on: conches thrown up by the sea, coral, heaps of pearl, aloe wood, silk and the many goods that come with the waters.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 664","lb":"SO664","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["தூதுவர்","பிரசாபதி"],"ch":"so:30"},"so:665":{"book":"soolamani","num":665,"t":["வெண்கதிர் முத்தகில் வேழ மருப்பொடு","கண்கவர் சாமரை வெண்மயி ரின்கணம்","தண்கதிர் வெண்குடை யாய்தரல் வேண்டுமி","தொண்சுட ராழியி னானுரை யென்றார்"],"p":"","r":"The list ends with white-rayed pearls, aloe, elephant tusks, whisks of white yak-hair and the cool white umbrella, all of which must be given, says the speech of the bright-discus king.","n":"The white umbrella and the yak-hair whisks are the marks of a king, so the demand goes past tribute toward giving up his standing. The verse does not say Podanam yields or refuses.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","vendhan","pagai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 665","lb":"SO665","mm":1,"ev":"The envoys finish reading the demand for wealth, women and royal insignia.","cl":[["a lesser king owes an overlord, on demand, tribute of treasure, women and the marks of a king","general","reported",3,"thoothar"]],"ag":["தூதுவர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["தூதுவர்","பிரசாபதி"],"ch":"so:30"},"so:666":{"book":"soolamani","num":666,"t":["வேந்தன்மற் றதனைக் கேட்டே வெற்றுவ னெறிந்த கல்லைக்","காந்திய கந்த தாகக் கவுட்கொண்ட களிரு போலச்","சேந்தவ ருரைத்த மாற்றஞ் சிந்தையு ளடக்கி வைத்து","நாந்தகக் கிழவர் கோமா னயந்தெரி மனத்த னானான்"],"p":"","r":"The king, having heard the envoys' demand, keeps their angry words inside his mind as an elephant keeps a stone thrown by a vain man in its cheek, and turns to a mind that weighs gentle courses.","n":"The simile is hard: the thrown stone and its being held in the cheek as if it were a scent-ball are read as swallowed insult; the verse does not say outright that the king hides his anger.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["arasu","adakkam","soozhchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 666","lb":"SO666","mm":1,"ev":"The king hears the demand and swallows the insult without showing it.","cl":[],"ag":["பிரசாபதி"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:31"},"so:667":{"book":"soolamani","num":667,"t":["கருத்துமாண் குலனுந் தேசுங் கல்வியும் வடிவுந் தம்மில்","பொருத்தினாற் பழிக்க லாகாப் புலைமைமிக் குடைய ரேனு","மொருத்தனுக் கொருத்தன் கூறக்கேட்டுற்றுச் செய்து வாழத்","திருத்தினா னிறைவ னேகாண் செய்வினைக் கிழவ னென்பான்"],"p":"","r":"The speaker says that, however a man's birth, learning and form stand, each is made to hear another's word, act on it and live by it by the lord called master of deeds.","n":"The first three lines turn on a hard clause about baseness that cannot be blamed; the sense of the verse as a whole is held loosely. The king is taken as the one reflecting, from 671, where his thought is said to end.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vinai","oozh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 667","lb":"SO667","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["a lord called master of deeds orders that men hear one another's word, act on it and so live","general","reported",4,"pirasapathi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["பிரசாபதி",""],"ch":"so:31"},"so:668":{"book":"soolamani","num":668,"t":["மதியினை மலரச் சூழ்ந்து வருந்தித்தாம் படைக்கப் பட்ட","நிதியினை நுகர்ந்து மென்று நினைத்தினி திருந்த போழ்திற்","பதியினைக் கலக்கிச் சென்று பறித்துத்தாம் பிறர்க்கு நீட்டும்","விதியினை விலக்க மாட்டா மெலிபவால் வெளிய நீரார்"],"p":"","r":"The speaker says that men who plan to enjoy the wealth they gained with effort are weakened by fate, which upsets the city, takes what they hold and hands it to others, and no one can bar it.","n":"Set beside 680 to 683, the king's fate-talk is a reason offered for giving way; the verse does not say that the loss has already come, only that fate can cause it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oozh","selvam","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 668","lb":"SO668","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["fate takes what a man has gathered and gives it to others, and nothing can hold it back","general","reported",3,"pirasapathi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["பிரசாபதி",""],"ch":"so:31"},"so:669":{"book":"soolamani","num":669,"t":["ஓளியினாற் பெரிய னாய வொருவனுக் குவப்பச் செய்தோ","ரளியினால் வாழ்து மென்னும் மவாவினு ளழுந்து கின்றாம்","தெளியநா மிதனைக் கண்டும் செய்வினைத் திறங்க ளோரா","மளிய மோ வளியஞ் சால வறிவினாற் பெரிய மேகாண்"],"p":"","r":"The speaker says that those who live by the favour of a man great in splendour sink in the wish to live by his grace, and though they see it plainly they do not weigh the workings of deeds.","n":"The speaker takes the knowledge of deeds as the knowledge he and his own lack; the verse blames desire for favour, not the overlord.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vinai","avaa","panivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 669","lb":"SO669","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["those who live on a greater man's favour forget how deeds work","general","reported",3,"pirasapathi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["பிரசாபதி",""],"ch":"so:31"},"so:670":{"book":"soolamani","num":670,"t":["அன்றுநா முயலப் பட்ட வினைகள்மற் றனைய வானா","லின்று நா மவலித் தென்னை யினிச்செய்வ தெண்ணி னல்ல","வென்றியான் விளங்கு மாழி யவர்கட்கு மேலை வேந்த","ரொன்றியாங் குவப்பித் தாண்ட துரைப்பக்கேட் டுணர்ந்தா மன்றே"],"p":"","r":"The speaker says that since the deeds done earlier are of this kind, there is nothing to gain by fretting now, and that he has heard how the earlier kings of the sea-ringed earth joined and pleased the overlord and ruled.","n":"This is where the king's reasoning from deeds becomes a decision: the fruit of deeds is read as a reason to pay. Verse 685 gives the opposite answer to the same demand.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","arasu","soozhchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 670","lb":"SO670","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["present helplessness is the fruit of deeds undertaken earlier","general","reported",1,"pirasapathi"],["earlier kings ruled by pleasing the overlord, and that is the precedent","general","reported",4,"pirasapathi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["பிரசாபதி",""],"ch":"so:31"},"so:671":{"book":"soolamani","num":671,"t":["என்றுதன் மனத்தி னெண்ணி யிலங்குகோற் கைய ராகி","நின்றகே சரரை நோக்கி நிலமன்ன னனைய சொன்னர்க்","கொன்றியா மிங்க ணுள்ள தொருப்படுத் துய்ப்பக் கொண்டு","சென்றுறு மிறவர்க் கெம்வா யின்னுரை தெரிமி னென்றான்"],"p":"","r":"Having thought thus, the king looks at the sceptre-bearing sky-goers and tells them that he will gather what is here and send it with them, and bids them carry his courteous word to their lord.","n":"The envoys are called by a name for the sky-goers; taking them to be Asvagriva's Vidyadhara envoys rests on 678 and 680.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thoothu","arasu","eegai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 671","lb":"SO671","mm":1,"ev":"The king tells Asvagriva's envoys he will gather what is due and send it back with them.","cl":[],"ag":["பிரசாபதி","கேசரர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:31"},"so:672":{"book":"soolamani","num":672,"t":["ஆளிகட் கரச னன்ன வரசர்கோ னதனைக் கூறி","வாளிவிற் றடக்கை வெம்போர் மணிவரை யனைய மார்பிற்","காளைக ளிதனைக் கேட்பிற் கனல்பவா லவரை யின்னே","மீளுமா றமைப்ப னென்று வேண்டுவ விதியி னீந்தான்"],"p":"","r":"The king says to himself that the young men will burn if they hear this and that he will send the envoys back at once, and then gives them what they ask by custom.","n":"The young men are taken to be the princes Vijayan and Tivittan; the verse says only that fierce youths would flare at the news.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["arasu","soozhchi","eegai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 672","lb":"SO672","mm":1,"ev":"The king hides the matter from the princes and gives the envoys what they ask, to send them away quickly.","cl":[],"ag":["பிரசாபதி"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:31"},"so:673":{"book":"soolamani","num":673,"t":["செய்யவாய்ப் பசும்பொ னோலைச் சீறடிப் பரவை யல்கு","லையநுண் மருங்கு நோவ வடிக்கொண்ட குவவுக் கொங்கை","வெய்யவாய்த் தண்ணெ னீலம் விரிந்தென விலங்கி நீண்ட","மையவா மழைக்கட் கூந்தன் மகளிரை வருக வென்றான்"],"p":"","r":"The king calls for the women of the long dark hair, slender-waisted and full-breasted, to come.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 673","lb":"SO673","mm":1,"ev":"The king summons the dancing women.","cl":[],"ag":["பிரசாபதி"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:31"},"so:674":{"book":"soolamani","num":674,"t":["அணிமுழா வனைய தோளா னருளிய தறிந்த போழ்தின்","மணிமுழாச் சிலம்பக் கொண்ட மண்டல வரங்கி னங்கண்","குணிமுழாப் பெயர்த்த பணி குயிற்றுத லிலயங் கொண்ட","கணிமுழ மருங்குற் பாடற் கலிப்பிவை தவிர்த்துச் சென்றார்"],"p":"","r":"On hearing the king's grace the women leave the drum, the dance-ground and the songs they were practising and go.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","eegai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 674","lb":"SO674","mm":1,"ev":"The women leave their dance practice at the king's word.","cl":[],"ag":["மகளிர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:31"},"so:675":{"book":"soolamani","num":675,"t":["மஞ்சிடை மதர்த்த மஞ்ஞை வான்குழா மென்ன வாங்கண்","வெஞ்சுடர் விளங்கு மாடத் திடைநிலை விரவித் தோன்றி","வஞ்சிநன் மருங்கு னோவ மணிநகைக் கலாவ மின்னச்","செஞ்சுடர் சிலம்பு பாடத் தேன்றிசை பரவச் சேர்ந்தார்"],"p":"","r":"The women come, bright like a flock of peacocks in a cloud, into the lamp-lit hall, their waists aching under the girdles and their anklets sounding as bees follow them.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 675","lb":"SO675","mm":1,"ev":"The women arrive in the lit hall.","cl":[],"ag":["மகளிர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:31"},"so:676":{"book":"soolamani","num":676,"t":["மாடெலா மெரிந்து மின்னும் வயிரக்குண் டலத்தோ டம்பொற்","றோடுலாந் துளங்கித் தோன்றுஞ் சுடிகைவாண் முகத்து நல்லார்","பாடலா னரம்பின் தெய்வம் படிவங்கொண் டனைய நீரா","ராடலா லரம்பை யொப்ப ரவரிலா யிரரை யீந்தான்"],"p":"","r":"The women, whose singing is like the lute-goddess and whose dancing is like Rambha's, are described, and the king gives a thousand of them away.","n":"The giving is part of the tribute, as 678 shows, where they are put into the flying car with the other gifts; the verse itself says only that he gave them.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","eegai","parisil"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 676","lb":"SO676","mm":1,"ev":"The king gives a thousand of the dancing women to the envoys.","cl":[],"ag":["பிரசாபதி"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:31"},"so:677":{"book":"soolamani","num":677,"t":["காய்ந்தொளிர் பவழச் சாதிக் கடிகைகள் காண மின்னுப்","பாய்ந்தெழு சுடர்ச்சங் கீன்ற பருமணித் தரளக்கோவை","யேந்தொழிற் காக துண்ட மருப்பிணை கவரிக் கற்றை","யாய்ந்தொழின் மகரப் பூணா னுவப்பன வனைத்து மீந்தான்"],"p":"","r":"The king gives all that pleases the envoys: coral, strings of pearl, tusks and bundles of yak-tail whisks.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","parisil","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 677","lb":"SO677","mm":1,"ev":"The king gives gems and fine things to the envoys.","cl":[],"ag":["பிரசாபதி"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:31"},"so:678":{"book":"soolamani","num":678,"t":["அஞ்சுடர் வயிரப் பூணா னருளினான் விடுப்ப வாங்கண்","விஞ்சையர் விமானத் தோற்ற மேலருங் கலங்க ளேற்றிச்","செஞ்சுடர் திலகச் செவ்வாய் மகளிரை விமானஞ் சேர்த்திக்","கஞ்சிகை மறைக்கும் போழ்திற் காளைக ளதனைக் கண்டார்"],"p":"","r":"At the king's leave the Vidyadharas load the gifts into their flying car and put the women in, and the princes see this as the curtain is lowered over it.","n":"The curtain hides the load only as it closes; the princes see the departure and ask in 679.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","thoothu","adimai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 678","lb":"SO678","mm":1,"ev":"The envoys load the gifts and the women into their flying car and the princes see them go.","cl":[],"ag":["பிரசாபதி","கேசரர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:31"},"so:679":{"book":"soolamani","num":679,"t":["என்னிது விளைந்த வாறித் தூதுவர் யாவ ரென்று","கன்னவில் வயிரத் திண்டோட் கடல்வண்ணன் வினவ யாரும்","சொன்னவின் றுரைக்க மாட்டார் துட்கென்று துளங்க வாங்கோர்","கொன்னவில் பூதம் போலுங் குறண்மக னிதனைச் சொன்னான்"],"p":"","r":"Tivittan asks what has happened and who these messengers are, and all stay silent in fear until a dwarf like a great ghost tells him.","n":"The sea-hued one is taken to be Tivittan, by his colour in the earlier chapters; the verse does not name him.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thoothu","acham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 679","lb":"SO679","mm":1,"ev":"Tivittan asks what is happening and a dwarf answers when no one else will.","cl":[],"ag":["செங்கண் ணெடியான்","குறண்மகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:31"},"so:680":{"book":"soolamani","num":680,"t":["அறைகழ லரவத் தானை யச்சுவக் கிரீவ னென்பா","னிறைபுக ழாழி தாங்கி நிலமெலாம் பணிய நின்றான்","திறைதர வேண்டும் என்று விடுதரச் செருவந் தானை","யிறைவனு மருளிச் செய்தா னிதுவிங்கு விளைந்த தென்றான்"],"p":"","r":"The dwarf says that Asvagriva, lord of the discus whom the earth bows to, sent word that tribute must be given, and that this is what has happened here.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thoothu","arasu","adimai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 680","lb":"SO680","mm":1,"ev":"The dwarf tells Tivittan that Asvagriva has demanded tribute from the king.","cl":[],"ag":["அச்சுவக் கிரீவன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["குறண்மகன்","செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ch":"so:31"},"so:681":{"book":"soolamani","num":681,"t":["திறைக்கட னென்னுமத் தீச்சொற் கேட்டலு","நிறைக்கட னிரம்பிய நெஞ்சத் தீக்கலுண்","முறைகெட முளைப்பதோர் முனிவி னொள்ளெரி","கரைப்படு படையவன் கனல் மூட்டினான்"],"p":"","r":"At the word tribute-duty the anger he has long held in a full heart kindles in him as a fire rising from a grudge.","n":"The subject of the burning is taken to be Tivittan, the prince who asked in 679; 683 later calls him the younger youth.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vekuli","maanam","veruvu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 681","lb":"SO681","mm":1,"ev":"Tivittan is lit with anger on hearing the word tribute.","cl":[],"ag":["செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:31"},"so:682":{"book":"soolamani","num":682,"t":["முடித்தலை முத்துதிர்ந் தாங்கு நெற்றிமேல்","பொடித்தன சிறுவியர்ப் புள்ளி யொள்ளெரி","யடுத்தெழு சுடரகத் துக்க நெய்த்துளி","கடுத்தசெங் கண்ணுநீர்த் திவலை கான்றவே"],"p":"","r":"His forehead beads with small drops of sweat like pearls dropped from his crown, and his red eyes shed hot tears like ghee falling into the fire.","n":"The verse describes the body's signs of anger and has no event of its own.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vekuli","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 682","lb":"SO682","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:31"},"so:683":{"book":"soolamani","num":683,"t":["படத்திடைச் சுடர்மணி தீண்டப் பட்டெரி","கடுத்திடு மரவெனக் கனன்ற நோக்கமோ","டடுத்தெரிந் தழல்நகை நக்கு நக்கிவை","யெடுத்துரை கொடுத்தன னிளிய காளையே"],"p":"","r":"He looks with the glare of a cobra whose jewelled hood is touched, laughs a fiery laugh and gives the younger one's reply.","n":"The word for the younger youth is read as Tivittan, the younger prince; the line takes up his own reply in 684 to 688.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vekuli","maram","soozhchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 683","lb":"SO683","mm":1,"ev":"Tivittan laughs in anger and answers the demand.","cl":[],"ag":["செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:31"},"so:684":{"book":"soolamani","num":684,"t":["உழுதுதங் கடன்கழித் துண்டு வேந்தரை","வழிமொழிந் தின்னணம் வாழு மாந்தர்போ","லெழுதிய திறையிறுத் திருந்து வாழ்வதே","லழகிது பெரிதுநம் மரச வாழ்க்கையே"],"p":"","r":"Tivittan says that to live by paying written tribute, like men who plough, pay their debts, eat and serve kings, would make a fine royal life.","n":"The tone is scorn: the verse praises what it means to mock. Its claim is set against the king's reasoning in 670.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maanam","arasu","adimai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 684","lb":"SO684","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["a king who pays written tribute lives like a tenant, and that is no royal life","general","reported",4,"tivittan"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["செங்கண் ணெடியான்",""],"ch":"so:31"},"so:685":{"book":"soolamani","num":685,"t":["நாளினுந் திறைநுமக் குவப்பத் தந்துநா","டாளுது மன்றெனி லொழிது மேலெம","தோளினுந் தொடுகழல் வலியி னானுமிவ்","வாளினும் பயனெனை மயரி மாந்தர்காள்"],"p":"","r":"Tivittan says they would pay tribute daily and rule the land only if their shoulders, their warrior's anklets and their sword were of no use, and asks what use they are otherwise.","n":"The addressee is taken to be Asvagriva's envoys or the deluded people of the court; the verse does not decide which.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maram","maanam","vali"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 685","lb":"SO685","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["a warrior's strength is for refusing tribute, and paying it makes arm and sword useless","general","reported",4,"tivittan"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["செங்கண் ணெடியான்","கேசரர்"],"ch":"so:31"},"so:686":{"book":"soolamani","num":686,"t":["விடமுடை யெரிக்கொடி விலங்கு நோக்குடை","யடைலுடைக் கடுந்தொழி லரவி னாரழற்","படமுடை மணிகொளக் கருதிப் பார்ப்பதோர்","மடமுடை மனத்தனும் மயரி மன்னனே"],"p":"","r":"Tivittan calls the king who demands the tribute a fool who thinks to take the jewel from the hood of a fire-breathing, venom-eyed cobra.","n":"The cobra is Tivittan's own side; the image makes the demander the victim of his own greed.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","pedhaimai","pagai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 686","lb":"SO686","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["to demand tribute from a strong man is like taking a jewel from a cobra's hood","general","reported",4,"tivittan"]],"ag":["அச்சுவக் கிரீவன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["செங்கண் ணெடியான்",""],"ch":"so:31"},"so:687":{"book":"soolamani","num":687,"t":["இருங்கலிப் படையினு மிகலி னாலுமெம்","மருங்கல் மிவைபெற்ற கரிய தாவதோர்","மருங்குள தெனினது மகளி ராற்சில","பெருங்கலத் தாங்கினாற் பெறலு மாகுமே"],"p":"","r":"Tivittan says that whatever the armies of strife have not won in the places that are his could at best be got by women with great vessels.","n":"The verse is compressed and its reading of the third and fourth lines is very uncertain; it is taken as a barb at the women sent as tribute in 676 to 678.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maram","parisil","adimai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 687","lb":"SO687","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["what force cannot win might at most be had in gifts brought by women","general","reported",3,"tivittan"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["செங்கண் ணெடியான்",""],"ch":"so:31"},"so:688":{"book":"soolamani","num":688,"t":["பாழியான் மெலிந்தவற் திறத்துப் பண்டெலா","மாழியால் வெருட்டி நின் றடர்த்தி போலுமஃ","தேழைகா ளினியொழித் திட்டுச் செவ்வனே","வாழுமா றறிந்துயிர் காத்து வாழ்மினே"],"p":"","r":"Tivittan tells the poor ones that they seem to have frightened the weak in the past with the discus from their stronghold, and bids them leave that, live rightly and guard their lives.","n":"The addressees are the envoys or the court; the verse also answers Asvagriva through them. The final plea to live rightly belongs to Tivittan's speech.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maram","arasu","nayan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 688","lb":"SO688","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["those who bully the weak with force should give it up, live rightly and guard their lives","general","reported",4,"tivittan"]],"ag":["அச்சுவக் கிரீவன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["செங்கண் ணெடியான்","கேசரர்"],"ch":"so:31"},"so:689":{"book":"soolamani","num":689,"t":["அன்றெனிற் றிறைகொளக் கருதி னாங்கொடு","குன்றின்மேற் பெறுவதென் வந்து கொள்கையா","னின்றுத னெஞ்சக நிறைய வீழ்வன","வென்றியாம் பகழியும் விசும்பு மீவனே"],"p":"","r":"The speaker tells the tribute-seeker that nothing is to be gained from coming to the mountain, since the arrows of victory will fall to fill his heart.","n":"The grammar is clipped and the opening word may turn the verse into a conditional; it is read as the prince's defiance continuing the speech begun at 683, with the speaker inferred, not named.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maram","pagai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 689","lb":"SO689","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["செங்கண் ணெடியான்","தூதர்"],"ch":"so:32"},"so:690":{"book":"soolamani","num":690,"t":["இறைவனை மகளிர்போற் கழறி யென்னையெங்","குறையிது கூறுமின் சென்று தூதிர்காள்","திறையினை மறுத்தவர் திறத்துச் செய்வதோர்","முறையுள தெனினது முயன்று கொள்கவே"],"p":"","r":"The speaker bids the envoys go and tell their lord that, if a rule exists for dealing with those who refuse tribute, he may try it, and that he need not be scolded as women scold.","n":"The speech is the prince's open invitation to a test; the next scenes show the minister taking it up by a trick.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thoothu","maram","sol"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 690","lb":"SO690","mm":1,"ev":"Tivittan sends the envoys back with a defiant message for their king.","cl":[],"ag":["செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ec":[],"spk":["செங்கண் ணெடியான்","தூதர்"],"ch":"so:32"},"so:691":{"book":"soolamani","num":691,"t":["உட்கவாங் குரைத்தலு மொளிர்பொன் மாழையுங்","கட்கமழ் கோதையர் கணமு மீண்டது","வட்கிநம் மிறைவற்கு வலிது தெவ்வெனத்","துட்கெனு மனத்தினர் தூத ரேகினார்"],"p":"","r":"After the threat, the envoys are given gold and a company of fragrant-garlanded women, and, ashamed and shaken, leave thinking the foe too strong for their king.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thoothu","naadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 691","lb":"SO691","mm":1,"ev":"The envoys receive gold and women, then depart in fear of the strong enemy.","cl":[],"ag":["தூதர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:32"},"so:692":{"book":"soolamani","num":692,"t":["போகிய தூதர் தங்கோன் பொலங்கழ றெழுத லஞ்சி","யாகிய தறிந்து சூழு மரிமஞ்சு லவனைக் கண்டே","யேகிய புகழி னானைக் கண்டது மீயப் பட்ட","தோகையஞ் சாய லார்தங் குழாங்களு நெதியுஞ் சொல்லி"],"p":"","r":"The envoys, afraid of what their king's rising anger will bring, find the minister and report both what was said and the women and wealth they were given.","n":"Whether அரிமஞ்சுலவன் is a name or a title is not fixed by the verse; it is taken as the minister's name because 693 and 700 call the hearer the minister.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thoothu","amaichu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 692","lb":"SO692","mm":1,"ev":"The envoys report to the minister Arimanjulavan.","cl":[],"ag":["தூதர்","அரிமஞ்சு"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:32"},"so:693":{"book":"soolamani","num":693,"t":["மீட்டிளங் குமரர் கண்டு விடுசுட ரிலங்கு நக்கு","மோட்டிளங் கண்ணி தீய முனித்தழன் முழங்க நோக்கி","யூட்டிலங் குருவக் கோலோர் தங்களுக் குரைத்த வெல்லாம்","தோட்டிலங் குருவத் தொங்க லமைச்சற்குச் சொல்லி யிட்டார்"],"p":"","r":"The envoys repeat to the minister everything the red-hued princes had told them, and the verse gives the princes' laughter and fiery looks.","n":"The syntax of lines 2 to 3 is tangled; it is read as the envoys reporting the princes' mocking reply.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thoothu","amaichu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 693","lb":"SO693","mm":1,"ev":"The envoys tell the minister the princes' words.","cl":[],"ag":["தூதர்","அரிமஞ்சு"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:32"},"so:694":{"book":"soolamani","num":694,"t":["அரும்பெற லறிவின் செல்வ னரிமஞ்சு வதனைக்கேட்டே","பெரும்பகை யதனைக் கேட்டாற் பெரியவன் சிறிது நோனா","னிரும்பகை யிதனை யென்கொல் விலக்குமாறென்று தானே","சுரும்பிவர் தொடையன் மார்பன் சூழ்ச்சி கொண் மனத்தனானான்"],"p":"","r":"The wise minister, hearing of the great quarrel, thinks that his king will not bear it for a moment and turns his mind to a way of turning the quarrel aside.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["soozhchi","amaichu","arivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 694","lb":"SO694","mm":1,"ev":"The minister weighs how to meet the quarrel.","cl":[],"ag":["அரிமஞ்சு"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:32"},"so:695":{"book":"soolamani","num":695,"t":["மின்றொடர்ந் திலங்கு பூணான் விளைவுறா விளைமை தன்னா","னன்றுதீ தென்னுந் தேர்ச்சி நவின்றில னாத லால்யா","னொன்ற வோர் மாயங் காட்டி யுளைவித்துக் குறுக வோடிக்","குன்றிடைச் சீயந் தன்மேற் கொளப்புணர்த் திடுவனென்றான்"],"p":"","r":"The minister, judging the young prince not yet schooled to tell good from ill, resolves to show him a trick that will enrage him and drive him to run into a lion on the mountain.","n":"The minister's judgement of Tivittan is his own and is not confirmed by the verse; the story goes on to test it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["soozhchi","vanjam","arivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 695","lb":"SO695","mm":1,"ev":"The minister plans to lure Tivittan into the path of a conjured lion.","cl":[["a youth who has not learned to tell good from ill can be undone by a trick","general","reported",2,"arimanjulavan"]],"ag":["அரிமஞ்சு"],"ec":[],"spk":["அரிமஞ்சு",""],"ch":"so:32"},"so:696":{"book":"soolamani","num":696,"t":["அன்னண மனத்தி னாலே யிழைத்தரி கேது வென்னு","மின்னணங் குருவப் பைம்பூண் விஞ்சையன் றன்னைக் கூவிக்","கன்னவி றோளி னாற்குக் கருமமீ தென்று காட்டி","மன்னுமோர் மாயச் சீய மாகென வகுத்து விட்டான்"],"p":"","r":"The minister calls the Vidyadhara Arikethu, shows him the task and commissions him to take the form of a lasting magic lion.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["soozhchi","maayai","amaichu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 696","lb":"SO696","mm":1,"ev":"The minister commissions Arikethu to turn himself into a magic lion.","cl":[],"ag":["அரிமஞ்சு","அரிகேது"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:32"},"so:697":{"book":"soolamani","num":697,"t":["ஒள்ளெரி நெறிப்பட் டன்ன சுரியுளை மலைகண் போழும்","வள்ளுகிர் மதர்வைத் திங்கட் குழவிவா ளெயிற்றுப் பைங்க","ணுள்ளெரி யுமிழ நோக்கி வுருமென வதிரும் பேழ்வாய்க்","கொள்ளரி யுருவு கொண்டான் கொடியவன் கடிய சூழ்ந்தான்"],"p":"","r":"The commissioned man takes the shape of a lion whose claws split mountains and whose fiery eyes and gaping mouth thunder.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maayai","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 697","lb":"SO697","mm":1,"ev":"Arikethu takes on the fierce form of a lion.","cl":[],"ag":["அரிகேது"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:32"},"so:698":{"book":"soolamani","num":698,"t":["இலைத்தடஞ் சோலை வேலி யிமவந்த மடைந்து நீண்ட","சிலைத்தடந் தோளி னார்தஞ் சிந்துநா டதனைச் சேர்ந்து","மலைத்தடம் பிளந்து சிந்த மாண்புடை பெயர முந்நீ","ரலைத்துடன் கலங்கி விண்பா லதிரநின் றுரறி யிட்டான்"],"p":"","r":"The lion-shape goes to the Himalaya and the land of the Sindhu people and roars until mountains split, the ocean churns and the sky echoes.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maayai","naadu","veruvu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 698","lb":"SO698","mm":1,"ev":"The false lion roars over the Himalaya and Sindhu land.","cl":[],"ag":["அரிகேது"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:32"},"so:699":{"book":"soolamani","num":699,"t":["பொடித்தலை புலம்பிக் கானம் போழ்ந்துமா நெரிந்து வீழ","வடித்தலை கலங்கி வேழம் பிடிகளோ டலறி யாழப்","புடைத்துழிப் பதடி போலத் துறுகற்கள் புரண்டு பொங்க","விடித்தலின் மனித்த ரெல்லா மெயிறுற விறுகிச் சோர்ந்தார்"],"p":"","r":"The roar flattens the forest, makes elephants cry out with their mates, sends boulders rolling and leaves people clenched and fainting.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["veruvu","maayai","naadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 699","lb":"SO699","mm":1,"ev":"The lion's roar spreads terror among animals and people.","cl":[],"ag":["அரிகேது"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:32"},"so:700":{"book":"soolamani","num":700,"t":["அப்படி யவனை யவ்வா றமைத்தபி னமைச்ச னாங்கண்","மெய்ப்படை தெரிந்து சொன்ன தூதுவ ரவரை மீட்டே","யிப்படி யிவைகள் சொல்லிப் பெயர்மினீ ரென்று வென்றிக்","கைப்படை நவின்ற வெம்போர்க் காளையைக் கனற்ற விட்டான்"],"p":"","r":"When the lion has been made ready, the minister recalls the envoys with a message and sets them to inflame the battle-trained youth.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["soozhchi","thoothu","amaichu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 700","lb":"SO700","mm":1,"ev":"The minister sends the envoys back to enrage Tivittan.","cl":[],"ag":["அரிமஞ்சு","தூதர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:32"},"so:701":{"book":"soolamani","num":701,"t":["ஆங்குத் தூதுவ ரதிர்முகி லாறுசென் றிழிந்து","பூங்கட் டேமொழிப் போதனத் திறைவன்றன் புதல்வர்","வீங்கு பைங்கழல் விடுசுடர் மிடைமணிப் பூணோர்","ஓங்கு தானையோ டுலாப்போந்த விடஞ்சென்றீ துரைத்தார்"],"p":"","r":"The envoys descend by the river's path and tell their message to the sons of the Podanam king, where they walk with their host.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thoothu","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 701","lb":"SO701","mm":1,"ev":"The envoys reach the princes and deliver their message.","cl":[],"ag":["தூதர்","குமரர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:32"},"so:702":{"book":"soolamani","num":702,"t":["திரையின் மாற்றமுந் திறையினை விலக்கிய திறமும்","குறையென் றெங்களைக் குமரநீ பணித்ததுங் கூற","வரையும் பைங்கழ லாழியந் தடக்கையெ மரைச","னறையும் குஞ்சியா னன்றுநன் றெனச்சொல்லி நக்கான்"],"p":"","r":"The envoys tell the prince that when they reported the tribute word, the refusal and his message, their king, fragrant-haired, said 'good, good' and laughed.","n":"The envoys, not the narrator, say the king laughed; the laugh is their report of his contempt.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thoothu","arasu","pagai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 702","lb":"SO702","mm":1,"ev":"Acuvakanthan laughs on hearing the prince's refusal.","cl":[],"ag":["அச்சுவ கண்டன்","தூதர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["தூதர்","செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ch":"so:32"},"so:703":{"book":"soolamani","num":703,"t":["தளையின் விண்டுதேன் றயங்கிய தடங்கொடார் மார்ப","விளையை யென்பது மெங்கள்வாய்க் கேட்டபி னிறைவ","னொளியு மாற்றலும் தன்கணொன் றுள்ளது நினையா","னளியின் பிள்ளைதா னுரைத்தவென் றழன்றில னமர்ந்தான்"],"p":"","r":"The envoys say that when their king heard from their mouths that the prince had called him young, he did not weigh his own light and strength, called it the talk of a child of grace, and sat unangered.","n":"Line 2 reads விளையை, taken as a misprint for இளையை. The king's calm is belittlement of the prince, not the self-command the Naladiyar praises.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["arasu","vekuli","ilamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 703","lb":"SO703","mm":1,"ev":"Acuvakanthan, told he has been called young, shrugs the word off.","cl":[],"ag":["அச்சுவ கண்டன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["தூதர்","செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ch":"so:32"},"so:704":{"book":"soolamani","num":704,"t":["அறியு மாயிற்ற னரும்பெற னாட்டினை யரிய","வெறியு மின்னுரு மெனவிடித் திறுவரை முழையு","ளுறையுங் கோளரி யொழிக்கலா னமக்குவந் தீயுந்","திறையு மீட்கிய வலித்தவச் செருக்குடைச் சிறியோன்"],"p":"","r":"The envoys carry their king's word: the proud little one who set out to take tribute and land cannot kill the thunder-voiced lion in the cave, if he knows his strength.","n":"The words are the enemy king's own, put in his envoys' mouth; the reader knows from 696 to 699 that the lion is a magic shape sent for the purpose.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thoothu","maanam","sirumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 704","lb":"SO704","mm":1,"ev":"The envoys relay the king's taunt about the lion.","cl":[],"ag":["அச்சுவ கண்டன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["தூதர்","செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ch":"so:32"},"so:705":{"book":"soolamani","num":705,"t":["என்று மற்றது மொழிமின் றுரைத்தெமை விடுத்தா","னென்ற மாற்றமஃ திசைத்தலு மிளையவ னென்னே","சென்ற நாட்டகஞ் சிலம்பதின் றிடித்துயி ரலறக்","கொன்றொர் கோளரி கொடுமுடி யுறைவதோ வென்றான்"],"p":"","r":"The envoys end their report, and the younger prince asks in astonishment whether a lion in the land's mountain peak kills its creatures with a roar that splits the hills.","n":"Line 1 is the envoys' own closing words; lines 2 to 4 are the prince's question, which the verse frames in the narrator's voice.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thoothu","maram","veruvu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 705","lb":"SO705","mm":1,"ev":"Tivittan hears of the lion and questions the envoys.","cl":[],"ag":["செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ec":[],"spk":["செங்கண் ணெடியான்","தூதர்"],"ch":"so:32"},"so:706":{"book":"soolamani","num":706,"t":["உனது வாழிநி னொலிபுனற் சிந்துநன் னாட்டிற்","களைதல் யாவர்க்கு மரியது கனமணிக் குன்றி","னுளது கோளரி யுருமென விடித்துயிர் பருகி","அளவி ணீன் முழை யுறைகின்ற தடிகளென் றுரைத்தார்"],"p":"","r":"The envoys answer that in the prince's own Sindhu land the lion cannot be removed by anyone, and that it lives in a long cave of the gem hill, drinking life with its thunder.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thoothu","veruvu","naadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 706","lb":"SO706","mm":1,"ev":"The envoys confirm to Tivittan that a lion terrorises the land.","cl":[],"ag":["தூதர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["தூதர்","செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ch":"so:32"},"so:707":{"book":"soolamani","num":707,"t":["ஆயின் மற்றத னருவரைப் பிலமென வகன்ற","வாயைப் போழ்ந்துட லிருபிளப் பாவகுத் திடுவ","னேயிப் பெற்றியே விளை த்தில னாயினும் வேந்தன்","பேயிற் பேசிய பிள்ளையே யாகென்று பெயர்ந்தான்"],"p":"","r":"The prince answers that he will split the lion's mouth and tear its body in two, and that if he fails the king is a child who talked like a ghost; he sets out.","n":"The vow is conditional: Tivittan stakes the king's reproach as a child on his failure, and turns the insult of 703 back on him.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","aanmai","pagai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 707","lb":"SO707","mm":1,"ev":"Tivittan vows to kill the lion and leaves.","cl":[["a boast that is not made good shames the one who made it","general","reported",3,"tivittan"]],"ag":["செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ec":[],"spk":["செங்கண் ணெடியான்","தூதர்"],"ch":"so:32"},"so:708":{"book":"soolamani","num":708,"t":["புழற்கைத் திண்ணுதி மருப்பின் பொருகளி றிவைதா","நிழற்க ணோக்கித்தின் றழன்றன நிலையிடம் புகுக","வழற்க ணாறுப வடுபடை தொடுதலை மடியாத்","தொழிற்க ணாளருந் தவிர்கெனச் சூளுற்று மொழிந்தான்"],"p":"","r":"The prince swears that the tusked elephants should return to their stalls and the fire-eyed warriors who ply weapons should hold back from the fight.","n":"The second half of line 3 is obscure and the sense of the oath is taken from the narrator's closing word.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maram","aanmai","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 708","lb":"SO708","mm":1,"ev":"Tivittan swears the elephants and armed men must stay.","cl":[],"ag":["செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ec":[],"spk":["செங்கண் ணெடியான்",""],"ch":"so:32"},"so:709":{"book":"soolamani","num":709,"t":["இவரு மாமணிக் கொடுஞ்சிய விவுளித் தேர் காலாட்","கவரி நெற்றிய புரவிதங் காவிடம் புகுக","வெவரு மென்னொடு வரப்பெறார் தவிர்கென வெழில்சே","ருவரி நீர்வண்ண னுழையவ ரொழியுமா றுரைத்தான்"],"p":"","r":"The prince adds that the chariots, horses and foot soldiers should also stay in camp, and none may come with him.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","aanmai","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 709","lb":"SO709","mm":1,"ev":"Tivittan orders the chariots, horses and foot to stay behind.","cl":[],"ag":["செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ec":[],"spk":["செங்கண் ணெடியான்",""],"ch":"so:32"},"so:710":{"book":"soolamani","num":710,"t":["நகர மாசன மிரைப்பது தவிர்த்தபின் னளிநீர்ப்","பகரு மாகடல் படிவங்கொண் டனையவன் படரச்","சிகர மால்வரை தெளிந்தனன் திருவமார் பினன்பின்","மகர மாகடல் வளைவண்ன னுடன்செல வலித்தான்"],"p":"","r":"After the city's noise is hushed, the sea-hued prince goes forth, and the conch-hued prince resolves to go with him.","n":"Lines 2 and 3 are hard to construe; the two princes are read from the epithets sea-hued and conch-hued.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["anbu","aanmai","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 710","lb":"SO710","mm":1,"ev":"Tivittan sets out and Vijayan resolves to go with him.","cl":[],"ag":["செங்கண் ணெடியான்","விசயன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:32"},"so:711":{"book":"soolamani","num":711,"t":["புழற்கை மால்களிற் றெருத்திடைப் புரோசையிற் பயின்ற","கழற்கொள் சேவடி கருவரை யிடைநெறி கலந்த","வழற்கொள் வெம்பொடி யவைமிசை புதையவவ் வரிமான்","தொழிற்கொண் டாருயிர் செகுக்கின்ற சூழல்சென் றடைந்தார்"],"p":"","r":"The two reach the haunt where the lion kills, with the fiery dust of the hill path settling on the trodden path of the elephant-necked riders' feet.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","veruvu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 711","lb":"SO711","mm":1,"ev":"The princes reach the place where the lion kills.","cl":[],"ag":["செங்கண் ணெடியான்","விசயன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:32"},"so:712":{"book":"soolamani","num":712,"t":["அடைந்த வீரரைக் காண்டலு மழலுளை யரிமா","வுடைந்த போகவோ ரிடியிடித் தெனவுடன் றிடிப்ப","விடிந்து போயின விறுவரைத் துறுகலங் குடனே","பொடிந்து போயின பொரியன நெரிவொடு புரளா"],"p":"","r":"The fire-maned lion, seeing the heroes who have come, roars like thunder, and the rocks of the mountain split, crumble and turn to powder.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","vali"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 712","lb":"SO712","mm":1,"ev":"The lion sees the two princes arrive and its roar shatters the rocks around.","cl":[],"ag":["குமரர்","அரிமா"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:33"},"so:713":{"book":"soolamani","num":713,"t":["காளி காளொளி முகில்வண்ணன் கழல்களை விசியாத்","தோளின் மேற்செலச் சுடர்விடு கடகங்கள் செறியாச்","சூளி மாமணித் தொடர்கொண்டு சுரிகுஞ்சி பிணியா","ஆளி மொய்ம்பனங் கார்த்தன னுடைத்ததவ் வரியே"],"p":"","r":"The dark-hued prince binds on his anklets, bracelets, crest and curled hair, and the lion is broken by one whose strength is like the lion's own.","n":"The last line is compressed and the word division doubtful: it can be read as the prince's cry and the lion's fall, or as the lion splitting something, so the sense is not fixed.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maram","aanmai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 713","lb":"SO713","mm":1,"ev":"Tivittan arms himself for the fight with ornaments tied on and hair bound.","cl":[],"ag":["செங்கண் ணெடியான்","அரிமா"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:33"},"so:714":{"book":"soolamani","num":714,"t":["எங்குப் போவதென் றுடைநெறி யிறுவரை நெரியப்","பைங்கட் கோளரி யுருவுகொண் டவன்மிசைப் படர்ந்து","வெங்கட் கூற்றமுந் திசைகளும் விசும்பொடு நடுங்கச்","செங்கட் காரொளி நெடியவன் விசையினாற் சிறந்தான்"],"p":"","r":"With the cliffs crumbling and no place left to flee, the green-eyed lion takes its fierce shape and springs on him, and the red-eyed dark tall one prevails by his speed while Death and the quarters of the sky shake.","n":"The Tamil says the lion 'took a form' (உருவுகொண்டு), which hints at more than an animal; these verses do not say what.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","vali"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 714","lb":"SO714","mm":1,"ev":"The lion leaps on Tivittan and he overcomes it by his swiftness.","cl":[],"ag":["அரிமா","செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:33"},"so:715":{"book":"soolamani","num":715,"t":["சுழலங் கார்த்தில காள்களு நிலமுறா முடங்கா","அழலுஞ் செஞ்சுடர்க் கடகக்கை யவைபுடை பெயரா","குழலுங் குஞ்சியு மாலையுங் கொளுவிய தொடரு","மெழிலுந் தோளிலு மெருத்திலுங் கிடந்தில வெழுந்தே"],"p":"","r":"In his leap his feet do not touch the ground, the bracelet-hung arms swing, and his hair, garland and chains rise off his shoulders and neck.","n":"Lines 1 and 2 are compressed and the nouns hard to join to their verbs; the sense is the speed of the leap and is held loosely.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maram","vali"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 715","lb":"SO715","mm":1,"ev":"Tivittan leaps so that his feet leave the ground and his ornaments fly up.","cl":[],"ag":["செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:33"},"so:716":{"book":"soolamani","num":716,"t":["மரங்கள் வேரொடுங் கீழ்ந்தென வழிதொடர்ந் தெழுந்த","நிரந்த மான்களும் பறவையும் நிலங்கொண்டு பதைத்த","வரங்கொள் வெம்பர லணிவரைக் கொடுமுடி யவைதா","முரங்கொ டோ ளவன் விரனுதி யுறவுடைந் தொழிந்த"],"p":"","r":"Trees are torn up by the roots, deer and birds that had run along fall to the ground in terror, and the crests of the mountain break at the touch of his fingertips.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vali","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 716","lb":"SO716","mm":1,"ev":"The force of the hero's blows uproots trees, scatters beasts and breaks the peaks.","cl":[],"ag":["செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:33"},"so:717":{"book":"soolamani","num":717,"t":["மூடி யிட்டன முகிற்கண முரன்றிடை நொறுங்காய்க்","கூடி யிட்டன கொடுமுடித் துறுகற்கள் குளிர்ந்தாங்","காடி யிட்டன வனதெய்வ மரியுரு வுடையா","னோடி யிட்டன னொளிவரை முழையகத் தொளிந்தான்"],"p":"","r":"Clouds close in, rocks of the crest pile together, the forest deity dances for joy, and the one in the lion's form flees into a cave of the bright mountain and hides.","n":"The forest deity's dance is read as joy at the lion's flight; the verb is clipped.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","kurippu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 717","lb":"SO717","mm":1,"ev":"The lion flees into a cave and hides.","cl":[],"ag":["அரிமா"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:33"},"so:718":{"book":"soolamani","num":718,"t":["உலகத்தின் வீங்கிய வொளிமணிச் சுடரணி திணிதோ","ணலத்தின் வீங்கிய நளிர்புக ழிளையவன் விரையின்","நிலத்தின் கம்பமு நெடுவரை யதிர்ச்சியு மெழுவப்","பிலத்தின் வாழரி யரசுதன் றுயில்பெயர்ந் ததுவே"],"p":"","r":"The younger prince, great in praised fame, moves fast, the earth shakes and the great mountain trembles, and the lion king who lives in the pit stirs from his sleep.","n":"Verse 717 had the lion fleeing into a cave; here a lion king sleeps in a pit. The text does not say whether one lion or two is meant.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","perumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 718","lb":"SO718","mm":1,"ev":"Tivittan's speed shakes the mountain and wakes the lion in the pit.","cl":[],"ag":["செங்கண் ணெடியான்","அரிமா"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:33"},"so:719":{"book":"soolamani","num":719,"t":["ஏங்கு வாழிய விருள்கெழு முழையகத் தொளித்தா","யோங்கு மால்வரை பிளந்திடு கெனவுளைந் துரவோ","னாங்க மாமுழை முகத்துல கதிரநின் றார்த்தான்","வீங்கு வாய்திறந் தொலித்தது விலங்கலிற் சிலம்பே"],"p":"","r":"Angered that the lion hid in the dark cave, the strong one bids it live long and split the great mountain, and stands at the cave-mouth roaring until the world shakes and the hill's echo answers with open mouth.","n":"The first two lines are the taunt, its sarcasm heard in 'live long' (வாழிய); the word division at line 3 joins the speaker's name to the verb.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maram","anjaamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 719","lb":"SO719","mm":1,"ev":"Tivittan taunts the hidden lion and roars at the mouth of its cave.","cl":[],"ag":["செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ec":[],"spk":["செங்கண் ணெடியான்","அரிமா"],"ch":"so:33"},"so:720":{"book":"soolamani","num":720,"t":["அதிர வார்த்தலு மழன்றுத னெயிற்றிடை யலர்ந்த","கதிருங் கண்களிற் கனலெனச் சுடர்களுங் கனல","முதிர்வில் கோளரி முனிந்தெதிர் முழங்கலி னெரிந்து","பிதிர்வு சென்றது பெருவரை பிளந்ததப் பிலமே"],"p":"","r":"At the roar the lion, enraged, blazes at fang and eye, answers in a counter-roar, and the cave and the great mountain split and burst.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","vali"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 720","lb":"SO720","mm":1,"ev":"The lion answers the challenge with a roar that bursts the cave.","cl":[],"ag":["அரிமா"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:33"},"so:721":{"book":"soolamani","num":721,"t":["எரிந்த கண்ணிணை யிறுவரை முழைநின்ற வனைத்தும்","விரிந்த வாயொடு பணைத்தன வெளியுகிர் பரூஉத்தாள்","சுரிந்த கேசரஞ் சுடரணி வளையெயிற் றொளியா","விரிந்த தாயிடை யிருணிண்றங் கெழுந்ததவ் வரியே"],"p":"","r":"With burning eyes, gaping mouth, bright claws, thick feet, curled mane and gleaming curved fangs, the lion rises out of the darkness of the cave.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 721","lb":"SO721","mm":1,"ev":"The lion comes out of the cave.","cl":[],"ag":["அரிமா"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:33"},"so:722":{"book":"soolamani","num":722,"t":["தாரித் திட்டதன் றறுகண்மைக் குணங்களி னுலகை","வாரித் தீட்டிவண் வந்ததோ ரரியென மதியாப்","பூரித் திட்டதன் பெருவலி யொடுபுக ழரிமாப்","பாரித் திட்டது பனிவிசும் புடையவர் பனித்தார்"],"p":"","r":"Its fearless nature swells with its great strength and fame, as if it were a lion that had scooped up the world, and the gods of the sky tremble.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","anjaamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 722","lb":"SO722","mm":1,"ev":"The lion comes forth in full might and the gods tremble.","cl":[],"ag":["அரிமா","அமரர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:33"},"so:723":{"book":"soolamani","num":723,"t":["அளைந்து மார்பினு ளிழிதரு குருதியைக் குடிப்பா","னுளைந்து கோளரி யெழுதலு முளைமிசை மிதியா","வளைந்த வாளெயிற் றிடைவலித் தடக்கையிற் பிடித்தான்","பிளந்து போழ்களாய்க் கிடந்ததப் பெருவலி யரியே"],"p":"","r":"As the furious lion rises to drink the blood running from his chest, he treads on its mane, seizes it by the curved fangs in his big hand, and the lion lies torn to strips.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","vali"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 723","lb":"SO723","mm":1,"ev":"Tivittan treads on the rising lion, grips its fangs and tears it apart.","cl":[],"ag":["அரிமா","செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:33"},"so:724":{"book":"soolamani","num":724,"t":["சீய மாயிரஞ் செகுத்திடுந் திறலது வயமா","வாய வாயிர மாயிர மடுதிற லரிமா","ஏயெ னாமுனிங் கழித்தன னிவனெனத் தத்தம்","வாயின் மேல்விரல் வைத்துநின் றமரர்கண் மருண்டார்"],"p":"","r":"The gods, with fingers on their lips, marvel that this one has destroyed in anger a lion able to kill a thousand lions, or thousands upon thousands of beasts.","n":"Line 3 is a cry of wonder (ஏ) that the gods use, not a statement by the narrator.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["perumai","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 724","lb":"SO724","mm":1,"ev":"The gods stand amazed at the killing of the lion.","cl":[],"ag":["அமரர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:33"},"so:725":{"book":"soolamani","num":725,"t":["அழிந்த கோளரிக் குருதிய தடுங்கடங் களிற்றோ","டொழிந்த வெண்மருப் புடைந்தவு மொளிமுத்த மணியும்","பொழிந்து கல்லறைப் பொலிவது குலிகச்சே றலம்பி","யிழிந்த கங்கையி னருவியொத் திழிந்தவவ் விடத்தே"],"p":"","r":"The slain lion's blood runs down the rock with rut-water, broken white tusks and bright pearls, like the Ganga washing down vermilion mud.","n":"The pearls and tusks are the lion's spoil: it had killed elephants.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 725","lb":"SO725","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:33"},"so:726":{"book":"soolamani","num":726,"t":["யாது மற்றதற் குவந்திலன் வியந்தில னிகலோ","னோத நித்திலம் புரிவளை யொளியவற் குறுகி","யேத மற்றிது கடிந்தன னின்னினி யடிகள்","போதும் போதன புரத்துக்கென் றுரைத்தனன் புகழோன்"],"p":"","r":"The famed one, neither glad nor amazed, goes to his brother and says that he has put away this harm and they should now go to Podanapuram.","n":"His lack of exultation is the point of line 1; the word is 'harm' (ஏதம்), so the lion is treated as a fault in the land to be removed, not as a rival.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["adakkam","vali"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 726","lb":"SO726","mm":1,"ev":"Tivittan tells Vijayan the harm is removed and proposes returning to Podanapuram.","cl":[],"ag":["செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ec":[],"spk":["செங்கண் ணெடியான்","விசயன்"],"ch":"so:33"},"so:727":{"book":"soolamani","num":727,"t":["தம்பி யாற்றல்கண் டுவந்துதன் மனந்தளிர்த் தொளியால்","வம்பு கொண்டவன் போனின்று வளைவண்ணன் மொழிந்தா","னம்பி நாமினி நளிவரைத் தாழ்வார்கண் டல்லா","லிம்பர் போம்படித் தன்றுசெங் குருதிய திழிவே"],"p":"","r":"Glad at his younger brother's strength, Vijayan answers that before the flow of this blood is left, they must first see the mountain's low slopes.","n":"Lines 3 and 4 are clipped and the exact reason for staying is not clear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["anbu","kurippu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 727","lb":"SO727","mm":1,"ev":"Vijayan, pleased at Tivittan's strength, asks to see the mountain before leaving.","cl":[],"ag":["விசயன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["விசயன்","செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ch":"so:33"},"so:728":{"book":"soolamani","num":728,"t":["ஆங்கண் மால்வரை யழகுகண் டரைசர்கள் பரவும்","வீங்கு பைங்கழ லிளையவன் வியந்துகண் மலரச்","வீங்கி மாண்பின வினையன விவையென வினிதின்","வாங்கு நீரணி வளை வண்ண னுரைக்கிய வலித்தான்"],"p":"","r":"Seeing the mountain's beauty, the younger prince, whose anklets kings praise, marvels with wide eyes, and the conch-hued one begins to answer, kindly, that these are works of great merit.","n":"Line 3 may be Vijayan's own words; the break between narration and speech is not marked.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","naadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 728","lb":"SO728","mm":1,"ev":"Tivittan marvels at the mountain and Vijayan begins to explain what he sees.","cl":[],"ag":["செங்கண் ணெடியான்","விசயன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:33"},"so:729":{"book":"soolamani","num":729,"t":["புள்ளுங் கொல்லென வொலிசெயும் பொழில்புடை யுடைய","கள்ளி னுண்டுளி கலந்துகா லசைத்தொறுங் கமழு","வுள்ளுந் தாதுகொண் டூதுவண் டறாதன வொளிசேர்","வெள்ளென் றோன்றுவ கயமல்ல பளிக்கறை விறலோய்"],"p":"","r":"Vijayan tells the hero that what shows white in the grove, where the wind brings flower-dust and honey-drops and the bees do not stop humming, is no pool but a chamber of crystal.","n":"The word division of the first line and the verb 'kollena' are hard to fix, so the sense is a loose one.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","therithal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 729","lb":"SO729","mm":1,"ev":"Vijayan names the crystal walls that look like water.","cl":[],"ag":["விசயன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["விசயன்","செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ch":"so:33"},"so:730":{"book":"soolamani","num":730,"t":["காளை வண்ணத்த களிவண்டு கதிவிய துகளாற்","றாளை மூசிய தாமரைத் தடம்பல வவற்று","ளாளி மொய்ம்பவங் ககலிலை யலரொடுங் கிழிய","வாளை பாய்வன கயமல்ல வனத்திடர் மறவோய்"],"p":"","r":"Vijayan says that the pools where bees dark as a bull hum over lotus on stalks, and where silvery fish leap and tear the flowers, are no pools.","n":"Line 3 is the hardest in the passage: its words for the fish and the broad leaf are guesses.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","therithal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 730","lb":"SO730","mm":1,"ev":"Vijayan names a mirage of lotus pools on the mountain.","cl":[],"ag":["விசயன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["விசயன்","செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ch":"so:33"},"so:731":{"book":"soolamani","num":731,"t":["மன்னு வார்துளித் திவலைய மலைமருங் கிருண்டு","துன்னு மாந்தர்கள் பனிப்புறத் துணைமையோ டதிர்வ","இன்ன வாம்பல வுருவுக ளிவற்றினு ளிடையே","மின்னு வார்ந்தன முகிலல்ல களிறுகள் விறலோய்"],"p":"","r":"Vijayan says that where the hill-side darkens under long drops and men shiver with a roar, the dark shapes in which lightning flashes are no clouds but elephants.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","therithal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 731","lb":"SO731","mm":1,"ev":"Vijayan names the elephants that look like clouds.","cl":[],"ag":["விசயன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["விசயன்","செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ch":"so:33"},"so:732":{"book":"soolamani","num":732,"t":["உவரி மாக்கட் னுரையென வொளிர்தரு மயிர","அவரை வார்புனத் தருந்திமே யருவிநீர் பருகி","இவரு மால்வரை யிளமழை தவழ்ந்தென விவையே","கவரி மாப்புடை பெயர்வன கடல்வண்ண காணாய்"],"p":"","r":"Vijayan says that the white-haired beasts that graze the bean-fields and drink the stream, and drift like young clouds on the great hill, are the yaks moving about.","n":"The addressee is the sea-hued prince, 'kadalvanna' in line 4, which fits Tivittan.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","therithal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 732","lb":"SO732","mm":1,"ev":"Vijayan names the yaks that look like clouds.","cl":[],"ag":["விசயன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["விசயன்","செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ch":"so:33"},"so:733":{"book":"soolamani","num":733,"t":["துள்ளிய ரும்புனற் றுளங்குபா றைக்கலத்","துள்ளுரா விக்கிடந் தொளிருமொண் கேழ்மணி","நள்ளிரா வின்றலை நகுபவா னத்திடைப்","பிள்ளைநா ளம்பிறை பிழற்தல்போ லுங்களே"],"p":"","r":"Vijayan says that the bright gems lying where mountain water leaps in the rock hollows glow like a young crescent moon shining in the night sky.","n":"The word division of lines 1 and 2 is broken.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","kurippu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 733","lb":"SO733","mm":1,"ev":"Vijayan names the gems that glow like moons.","cl":[],"ag":["விசயன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["விசயன்","செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ch":"so:33"},"so:734":{"book":"soolamani","num":734,"t":["வழையும்வா ழைத்தடங் காடுமூ டிப்புடங்","கழையும்வே யுங்கலந் திருண்டு காண் டற்கரு","முழையுமூ ரிம்மணிக் கல்லுமெல் லாநின","திழையினம் பொன்னொளி யெரிப்பத்தோன் றுங்களே"],"p":"","r":"Vijayan says that in the dark of the plantains, bamboo and hard-to-see caves, the gems seem to burn with the gold glow of the prince's own ornaments.","n":"Lines 2 and 3 are broken by word division.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","kurippu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 734","lb":"SO734","mm":1,"ev":"Vijayan likens the mountain's jewels to the prince's ornaments.","cl":[],"ag":["விசயன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["விசயன்","செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ch":"so:33"},"so:735":{"book":"soolamani","num":735,"t":["பருவமோ வாமுகிற் படல மூடிக்கிடந்","திரவுண்டே னைப்பக லில்லையொல் லென்றிழித்","தருவி யோ வாபுரண் டசும்புபற் றித்தட","வரையின்றாழ் வார்நிலம் வழங்கலா கார்களே"],"p":"","r":"Cloud banks cover the mountain so thickly that day seems eaten by night, and torrents and seeping wet make the broad slopes impassable.","n":"","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["mazhai","idam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 735","lb":"SO735","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["(பெயர் இல்லை)","வரைமார்ப"],"ch":"so:34"},"so:736":{"book":"soolamani","num":736,"t":["சூரலப் பித்தொடர்ந் தடர்துளங் கும்மரில்","வேரலோ டும்மிடைந் திருண்டுவிண் டுவ்விடார்","ஊரலோ வாதனன் றுயிரையுண் டிடுதலால்","சாரலா காதன சாதிசா லப்பல"],"p":"","r":"The slope is choked with tangled creepers, cane and bamboo, dark and many-kinded, and is said to be unfit to approach.","n":"","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["idam","acham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 736","lb":"SO736","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:34"},"so:737":{"book":"soolamani","num":737,"t":["பரியபா றைத்திரள் படர்ந்தபோ லக்கிடந்","திரியவே ழங்களை விழுங்கியெங் குந்தமக்","குரியதா னம்பெறா வுறங்கியூ றுங்கொளாப்","பெரியபாம் பும்முள பிலங்கொள்பேழ் வாயவே"],"p":"","r":"Great snakes with cave-wide mouths lie sleeping among boulder heaps, having swallowed the elephants that roamed there.","n":"","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["acham","idam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 737","lb":"SO737","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:34"},"so:738":{"book":"soolamani","num":738,"t":["அவைகள்கண் டாய்சில வரவமா லிப்பன","உவைகள்கண் டாய்சில வுளியமொல் லென்பன","இவைகள்கண் டய்சில வேழவீட் டம்பல","நவைகள்கண் டாயின நம்மலா தார்க்கெலாம்"],"p":"","r":"The speaker points out hissing snakes, growling beasts and elephant dens, and says these troubles are for all who are not 'ours'.","n":"Whether the hearer is the same hero as in 742 and 743 is inferred; the verse itself only says 'see' (கண்டாய்).","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["acham","idam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 738","lb":"SO738","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["(பெயர் இல்லை)","வரைமார்ப"],"ch":"so:34"},"so:739":{"book":"soolamani","num":739,"t":["குழல்கொடும் பிக்கணங் கூடியா டநகும்","எழில்கொடா ரோய்விரைந் தியங்கலிங் குள்ளநின்","கடில்களார்க் குங்களே கலங்கிமே கக்குழாம்","பொழில்கள்வெள்ள ளத்திடைப் புரளநூ றுங்களே"],"p":"","r":"Clouds pour flood-water on the groves while the addressee is urged to go swiftly; the sense of the first lines is unclear.","n":"","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["mazhai","idam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 739","lb":"SO739","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:34"},"so:740":{"book":"soolamani","num":740,"t":["ஆக்கலா காவசும் பிருந்துகண் ணிற்கொரு","நீக்கநீங் காநிலம் போலத்தோன் றிப்புகிற்","காக்கலா காகளி றாழவா ழும்புறந்","தூக்கந்தூங் குந்தொளி தொடர்ந்துபொன் றுங்களே"],"p":"","r":"Ground that looks sound hides pits or mire where elephants sink beyond saving, while a far glow flickers; the sense is doubtful.","n":"","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["acham","idam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 740","lb":"SO740","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:34"},"so:741":{"book":"soolamani","num":741,"t":["இதுவித்தாழ் வார்நிலத் தியற்கைமே லாற்பல","மதியம்பா ரித்தன மணிக்கற் பாறையின்மிசை","நிதியம்பா ரித்தொளி நிழன்று துஞ் சன்னிலைக்","கதியின் வாழ் வாரையுங் கண்கள்வாங் குங்களே"],"p":"","r":"Gem-studded rocks give off a glow like many moons, so bright that it dazzles those who look.","n":"","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 741","lb":"SO741","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:34"},"so:742":{"book":"soolamani","num":742,"t":["இருது வேற்றுமை யின்மையாற்","சுருதி மேற்றுறக் கத்தினோ","டரிது வேற்றுமை யாகவே","கருது வேற்றடங் கையினாய்"],"p":"","r":"The speaker tells the hero that, since the seasons do not change here, this mountain can hardly be told apart from the heaven of the scriptures.","n":"The heaven is called by the word for scripture (சுருதி); the verse compares scenery to it and makes no doctrinal claim.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 742","lb":"SO742","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["(பெயர் இல்லை)","வரைமார்ப"],"ch":"so:34"},"so:743":{"book":"soolamani","num":743,"t":["தொல்லுறு சுடர்போலுஞ் சூழொளி மணிப்பாறை","கல்லறை யவைகோங்கின் கடிமலர் கலந்துராய்","மல்லுறு வரைமார்ப வளரொளி யின்முளைக்கு","மெல்லுறு சுடர்வானத் தெழிலவா யினியவ்வே"],"p":"","r":"Jewel-rocks and stone chambers, mixed with fragrant kongu flowers, shine like old lamps against the sky.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","idam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 743","lb":"SO743","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["(பெயர் இல்லை)","வரைமார்ப"],"ch":"so:34"},"so:744":{"book":"soolamani","num":744,"t":["திரைத்த சாலிகை","நிரைத்த போனிரந்","திரைப்ப தேன்களே","விரைகொண் மாலையாய்"],"p":"","r":"A very short, compressed verse addressing a garlanded one with a question about rows or waves; its sense is unsettled.","n":"","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 744","lb":"SO744","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["(பெயர் இல்லை)","வரைமார்ப"],"ch":"so:34"},"so:745":{"book":"soolamani","num":745,"t":["வரைவாய் நிவத்த வடுமா வடுமா","விரைவாய் நிவந்து விரியா விரியா","புரைவா யசும்பு புலரா புலரா","இரவா யிருள்செ யிடமே யிடமே"],"p":"","r":"In a word-play verse, mango trees rise on the slope, flowers spread, seepage never dries and the place turns dark as night.","n":"","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","idam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 745","lb":"SO745","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:34"},"so:746":{"book":"soolamani","num":746,"t":["இளையா ரிளையா ருடனாய் முலையின்","வளையார் வளையார் மனம்வேண் டுருவம்","விளையா விளையாட் டயருந் தொழிறான்","றளையார் தளையார் பொழிலின் றடமே"],"p":"","r":"In a word-play verse, young women with bangles and their companions pass the time in games in the broad groves.","n":"","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["inbam","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 746","lb":"SO746","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:34"},"so:747":{"book":"soolamani","num":747,"t":["அளியாடு மமரங்க ளமரங்கள் மகிழ்ந்தானா","விளையாடும் விதமலர்ந்த விதமலர்ந்த மணிதூவும்","வளையார்கண் மகிழ்பவான் மகிழ்பவான் மலர்சோர்வ","இளையாரை யினையவே யினையவே யிடமெல்லாம்"],"p":"","r":"The groves ring with bees and spread with opening flowers, where bangled women play and let petals fall, so that every place looks like their play.","n":"","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["inbam","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 747","lb":"SO747","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:34"},"so:748":{"book":"soolamani","num":748,"t":["தமரைத் திடத்திடை மலர்ந்த சாரல்வாய்த்","தாமரைத் துளையொடு மறலித் தாவில்சீர்த்","தாமரைத் தகுகுணச் செல்வன் சண்பகம்","தாமரைத் தடித்தலர் ததைந்து தோன்றுமே"],"p":"","r":"Lotus and champak bloom spread over the slope; the fine second half of the verse is hard to parse.","n":"","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","idam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 748","lb":"SO748","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:34"},"so:749":{"book":"soolamani","num":749,"t":["நாகஞ் சந்தனத் தழைகொண்டு நளிர்வண்டு கடிவ","நாகஞ் சந்தனப் பொதும்பிடை நளிர்ந்துதா துமிழ்வ","நாகஞ் செஞ்சுடர் நகுமணி யுமிழ்ந்திருள் கடிவ","நாக மற்றிது நாகர்தம் முலகினை நகுமே"],"p":"","r":"A word-play verse on one word (elephant, snake, mountain, tree) describes sandal groves, humming bees, pollen and gem-spitting snakes, and says the place mocks the nagas' own world.","n":"","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","idam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 749","lb":"SO749","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:34"},"so:750":{"book":"soolamani","num":750,"t":["நகுமலரன நறவம் மவைசொரி வனநறவம்","தொகுமலரன துருக்கம் மவைதரு வனதுருக்கம்","மகமலரன வசோகம் மவைதருவ வசோகம்","பகுமலரன பாங்கர் பலமலையன பாங்கர்"],"p":"","r":"The verse lists the flowering trees of the slope, naming nirava, duruka and asoka among them.","n":"","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 750","lb":"SO750","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:34"},"so:751":{"book":"soolamani","num":751,"t":["அணங்கமர் வனகோட லரிதவை பிறர்கோடல்","வணங்கிளர் வனதோன்றி வகைசுடர் வனதோன்றி","இணங்கிணர் வனவிஞ்சி யெரிபொன் புடையிஞ்சி","மணங்கமழ் வனமருதம் வரையயல் வனமருதம்"],"p":"","r":"The list of slope flowers goes on, with kodal, thondri, ingi and maruthu named.","n":"","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 751","lb":"SO751","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:34"},"so:752":{"book":"soolamani","num":752,"t":["சாந்துந் தண்டழை யுஞ்சுர மங்கையர்க்","கேந்தி நின்றன விம்மலை யாரமே","வாய்ந்த பூம்புடை யும்மலர்க் கண்ணியு","மீந்த சாகைய விம்மலை யாரமே"],"p":"","r":"Sandal and cool leaf-garlands stand ready for the women of the wild, and the branches hold out blossom garlands.","n":"","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 752","lb":"SO752","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:34"},"so:753":{"book":"soolamani","num":753,"t":["இயங்கு கின்னர ரின்புறு நீரவே","தயங்கு கின்றன தானமந் தாரமே","பயங்கொள் வார்பயங் கொள்பவ வனைத்தையும்","தயங்கு கின்றன தானமந் தாரமே"],"p":"","r":"Kinnaras take pleasure here and mandara trees shine; the third line speaks of fear and the fearful.","n":"","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","acham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 753","lb":"SO753","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:34"},"so:754":{"book":"soolamani","num":754,"t":["பொன்விரிந் தனைய பூங் கோங்கும் வேங்கையு","முன்விரிந் துக்கன மொய்த்த கற்றல","மின்விரிந் திடையிடை விளங்கி யிந்திரன்","வின்முரிந் திருண்முகில் வீழ்ந்த போலுமே"],"p":"","r":"Gold-bright kongu and vengai blooms spread among the rocks, like Indra's bow broken and fallen among dark clouds.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 754","lb":"SO754","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:34"},"so:755":{"book":"soolamani","num":755,"t":["நிழற்பொதி நீலமா மணிக்க லந்திரள்","பொழிற்பொதி யவிழ்ந்தபூப் புதைந்து தோன்றுவ","தழற்பொதிந் தெனத்துகி றரித்த காஞ்சியர்","குழற்பொதி துறுமலர்க் கொண்டை போலுமே"],"p":"","r":"Heaps of blue-gem rock are half hidden by fallen blossom, like the hair-knots of women in fire-red cloth.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 755","lb":"SO755","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:34"},"so:756":{"book":"soolamani","num":756,"t":["மேவுவெஞ் சுடரொளி விளங்கு கற்றலம்","தாவில்பூந் துகளொடு ததைந்து தோன்றுவ","பூவுக விளையவர் திளைத்த பொங்கணைப்","பாவுசெந் துகிலுடைப் பள்ளி போலுமே"],"p":"","r":"Sun-lit rock slabs strewn with pollen look like the red-cloth beds where young lovers lie.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 756","lb":"SO756","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:34"},"so:757":{"book":"soolamani","num":757,"t":["அழலணி யசோகஞ் செந்தா தணிந்துதே னரற்ற நின்று","நிழலணி மணிக்கன் னீல நிறத்தொடு நிமிர்ந்த தோற்றம்","குழலணி குஞ்சி மைந்தர் குங்குமக் குழம்பு பூசி","எழிலணி திகழ நின்றா லெனையநீ ரனைய தொன்றே"],"p":"","r":"Red ashoka blossom hums with bees against blue rocks, and the sight is compared to youths smeared with saffron paste and adorned.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 757","lb":"SO757","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:34"},"so:758":{"book":"soolamani","num":758,"t":["இணைந்துதேன் முழங்க விண்ட வேழிலம் பாலை வெண்பூ","மணந்துதா தணிந்து தோன்று மரகத மணிக்கற் பாறை","கணங்கெழு களிவண் டாலப் பாசடை கலந்த பொய்கை","தணந்தொளி விடாத வெண்டாமரை ததைந் தனையதொன்றே"],"p":"","r":"The speaker points to a hill-rock of emerald hung with white flowers and pollen, humming with bees, beside a pool thick with white lotus.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naadu","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 758","lb":"SO758","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["விசயன்","செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ch":"so:35"},"so:759":{"book":"soolamani","num":759,"t":["காரிருட் குவளிக் கண்ணிக் கதிர்நகைக் கனபொற் றோட்டுக்","கூரிருள் சுரிபட் டன்ன குழலணி கொடிறுண் கூந்தல்","பேரிருள் கிழியத் தோன்றும் பிறையெயிற் றமர நோக்கிற்","சூரர மகளிர் வாழு மிடமிவை சுடர்ப வெல்லாம்"],"p":"","r":"The speaker says these shining places are where the dark-haired, crescent-toothed women of the sura race live.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","naadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 759","lb":"SO759","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["விசயன்","செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ch":"so:35"},"so:760":{"book":"soolamani","num":760,"t":["வாரிரு புடையும் வீக்கி வடஞ்சுமந் தெழுந்து வேங்கை","யேரிருஞ் சுணங்கு சிந்தி யெழுகின்ற விளமென் கொங்கைக்","காரிருங் குழலங் கொண்டைக் கதிர்நகைக் கனகப் பைம்பூண்","நீரர மகளிர் கண்டாய் நிறைபுனற் றடத்து வாழ்வார்"],"p":"","r":"The speaker says the high-breasted women with gold ornaments who live in the brimming pool are the water-women.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","naadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 760","lb":"SO760","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["விசயன்","செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ch":"so:35"},"so:761":{"book":"soolamani","num":761,"t":["மேகமேற் றவழ்ந்து வேய்கண் மிடைத்துகீ ழிருண்ட தாழ்வர்","ஏகமா மலையி னெற்றி யிருஞ்சுனைத் தடங்க ளெல்லாம்","நாகமா மகளி ரென்னு நங்கையர் குடையப் பொங்கி","மாகமேற் றரங்கஞ் சிந்தி மணியறை கழுவு மன்றே"],"p":"","r":"The speaker says the nagas' women bathe in the mountain pools, which swell and wash the gem rocks.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naadu","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 761","lb":"SO761","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["விசயன்","செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ch":"so:35"},"so:762":{"book":"soolamani","num":762,"t":["ஆவிவீற் றிருந்த காத லவரொடு கவரி வேய்ந்து","நாவின்வீற் றிருந்த நாறு நளிர்வரைச் சிலம்பின் மேயார்","காவிவீற் றிருந்த கண்ணார் கந்தர்வ மகளிர் கண்டாய்","பாவிவீற் றிருந்த பண்ணி னமுதினாற் படைக்கப் பட்டார்"],"p":"","r":"The speaker says the lotus-eyed women who live with their lovers on the cool slopes are gandharva women, made of the nectar of song.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","naadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 762","lb":"SO762","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["விசயன்","செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ch":"so:35"},"so:763":{"book":"soolamani","num":763,"t":["அலங்கிண ரணிந்த விஞ்சை யரிவைய ரிடங்கள் கண்டாய்","விலங் கலின் விளங்கு கின்ற வெள்ளிவெண் கபாடமாடம்","இலங்கொளி மகரப் பைம்பூ ணியக்கிய ரிடங்கள் கண்டாய்","நலங்கிளர் பசும்பொற் கோயி னகுகின்ற நகர மெல்லாம்"],"p":"","r":"The speaker says the silver mansions of the hill are the dwellings of Vidyadhara women and yakshis, and the whole town laughs in gold.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","naadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 763","lb":"SO763","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["விசயன்","செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ch":"so:35"},"so:764":{"book":"soolamani","num":764,"t":["போய்நிழற் றுளும்பு மேனிப் புணர்முலை யமிர்த னாரோ","டாய்நிழற் றுளும்பு வானோ ரசதியா டிடங்கள் கண்டாய்","சேய்நிழற் றிகழுஞ் செம்பொற் றிலதவே திகைய வாய","பாய்நிழற் பவழச் செங்காற் பளிக்கு மண் டபங்க ளெல்லாம்"],"p":"","r":"The speaker points to the crystal halls with coral pillars where gods play with their consorts.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","naadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 764","lb":"SO764","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["விசயன்","செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ch":"so:35"},"so:765":{"book":"soolamani","num":765,"t":["எழின்மணிச் சுடர்கொண் மேனி யிமையவ ரிடங்கள் கண்டாய்","முழுமணிப் புரிசை வேலி முத்தமண் டபத்த வாய","கழுமணிக் கபாட வாயிற் கதிர்நகைக் கனக ஞாயிற்","செழுமணிச் சிகர கோடிச் சித்திர கூட மெல்லாம்"],"p":"","r":"The speaker says the gem-bright mansions and picture halls with jewelled gates are the dwellings of the gods.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","naadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 765","lb":"SO765","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["விசயன்","செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ch":"so:35"},"so:766":{"book":"soolamani","num":766,"t":["தும்பிவாய் துளைக்கப் பட்ட கீசகம் வாயுத் தன்னால்","வம்பவாங் குழலி னேங்க மணியரை யரங்க மாக","உம்பர்வான் மேக சால மொலிமுழாக் கருவி யாக","நம்பதேன் பாட மஞ்ஞை நாடக நவில்வ காணாய்"],"p":"","r":"The speaker says the hill itself plays a drama, with wind through beetle-bored bamboo as flute, clouds as drum and peacocks as dancers.","n":"கீசகம் is taken as bamboo that wind makes a flute of; the line is hard to construe and the sense of the first two lines is a guess.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","naadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 766","lb":"SO766","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["விசயன்","செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ch":"so:35"},"so:767":{"book":"soolamani","num":767,"t":["பொன்னவிர் மகரப் பைம்பூட் பொலங்குழை யிலங்கு சோதிக்","கன்னவில் வயிரத் திண்டோட் கடல்வண்ண னுவப்பக் காட்டி","மன்னவின் றிறைஞ்சுஞ் செய்கை வளைவண்ணன் மலையின் மேலால்","இன்னன பகர்ந்து சொல்லு மெல்லையு ணீங்க லுற்றார்"],"p":"","r":"The poet says that after the speaker had shown the sea-hued prince these sights and said such things, they went on.","n":"Grammar of lines 2 to 3 is loose; the conch-hued one is taken as the speaker and the sea-hued as the one shown, as the brothers are named in 778.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","kurippu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 767","lb":"SO767","mm":1,"ev":"Vijayan shows Tivittan the sights of the hill and the two move on.","cl":[],"ag":["விசயன்","செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:35"},"so:768":{"book":"soolamani","num":768,"t":["பாசிலைப் பாரி சாதம் பரந்துபூ நிரந்த பாங்கர்","மூசின மணிவண் டார்க்கு முருகறா மூரிக் குன்றம்","காய்சின வேலி னான்றன் கண்களி கொள்ளக் காட்டி","யோசனை யெல்லை சார்ந்து பின்னையிஃ துரைக்க லுற்றான்"],"p":"","r":"The poet says that, having shown the hill of bees and flowering trees for the spear-bearer's delight, the speaker reached the end of a league and began to speak.","n":"Who is meant by the one with the angry spear is not fixed; he is taken as Tivittan.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","kurippu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 768","lb":"SO768","mm":1,"ev":"Vijayan shows Tivittan another hill, walks a league and begins to speak.","cl":[],"ag":["விசயன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:35"},"so:769":{"book":"soolamani","num":769,"t":["வலிகற்ற மதர்வைப் பைங்கண் வாளொயிற் றரங்கச் சீயங்","கலிகற்ற களிறுண் பேழ்வாய்க் கலிங்கினா னிழிந்து போந்து","குலிகச்சே றலம்பிக் குன்றங் கொப்புளித் திட்ட தொப்ப","ஒலிகற்ற வுதிர நீத்த மொழுகுவ தின்ன நோக்காய்"],"p":"","r":"The speaker points to the stream of blood running down from where the lion had come out of its cave, like red clay that the hill has vomited.","n":"The verse is the fruit of the astrologer's word of soolamani 409, that within a month Tivittan would split a lion's mouth; here the deed is already done and seen only as blood.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kolai","kurippu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 769","lb":"SO769","mm":1,"ev":"Vijayan points out the blood of the lion that Tivittan killed.","cl":[],"ag":["செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ec":[],"spk":["விசயன்","செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ch":"so:35"},"so:770":{"book":"soolamani","num":770,"t":["வெம்பவேங் குயிரை யெல்லாம் விழுங்கிய வெகுண்டு நோக்கிச்","கம்பமா வுலகந் தன்னைக் கண்டிடுங் களிகொள் சீயம்","நம்பநீ யழித்த தல்லா னகையெயிற் றதனை நண்ணல்","வம்பறா மகரப் பைம்பூண் வானவர் தமக்கு மாமோ"],"p":"","r":"The speaker says the lion that swallowed lives and saw the world as trembling was beyond even the gods, so none but Tivittan could have destroyed it.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","vali","pugazh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 770","lb":"SO770","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["விசயன்","செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ch":"so:35"},"so:771":{"book":"soolamani","num":771,"t":["ஆங்குநீ முனிந்த போழ்தி னரிது வகல நோக்கி","வாங்குநீர் வண்ண கேளாய் மாயமா மதித்து நின்றே","னோங்குநீண் மலையின் றாழ்வா ரொலிபுன லுதிர யாறு","வீங்கிவந் திழிந்த போழ்து மெய்யென வியப்புச் சென்றேன்"],"p":"","r":"The speaker says that when Tivittan raged he watched from afar and took it for illusion, and wondered when the roaring stream of blood came down the slope and proved it real.","n":"மாயமா மதித்து நின்றேன் may mean he took it for illusion, or for a marvel; the first is followed.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maram","kurippu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 771","lb":"SO771","mm":1,"ev":"Vijayan watches Tivittan's rage and the stream of blood after he kills the lion.","cl":[],"ag":["செங்கண் ணெடியான்","விசயன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["விசயன்","செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ch":"so:35"},"so:772":{"book":"soolamani","num":772,"t":["குன்றிற்கு மருங்கு வாழுங் குழூஉக்களிற் றினங்க ளெல்லா","மன்றைக்கன் றலறக் கொன்றுண் டகலிடம் பிளப்பச் சீறி","வென்றிக்கண் விருப்பு நீங்கா வெங்கண்மா விதனைக் கொன்றா","யின்றைக்கொண் டுலக மெல்லா மினிதுகண் படுக்கு மன்றே"],"p":"","r":"The speaker says Tivittan killed the lion that had eaten the herds of elephants, so that from this day the whole world sleeps in peace.","n":"The Jain work praises a killing; the verse justifies it by the lion's own killing and the safety it brings, and does not weigh it against non-killing.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kolai","arul","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 772","lb":"SO772","mm":1,"ev":"Tivittan killed the lion that preyed on the elephant herds.","cl":[["killing a beast that preys on others lets the world rest in safety","general","asserted",4,"vijayan"]],"ag":["செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ec":[],"spk":["விசயன்","செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ch":"so:35"},"so:773":{"book":"soolamani","num":773,"t":["உரைசெய்நீ ளுலகின் வாழு முயிர்களுக் குறுகண் கண்டால்","வரைசெய்தோண் மைந்தர் வாழ்க்கை மதிக்கிலார் வனப்பின் மிக்கார்","திரைசெய்நீ ருலகங் காக்குஞ் செய்கை மேற் படைக்கப் பட்ட","அரசர்தம் புதல்வர்க் கையா வறம்பிறி ததனி லுண்டோ"],"p":"","r":"The speaker says that when living beings are in danger, strong-armed princes do not weigh their own lives, for the king's sons have no duty beyond guarding the world.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","aram","idukkan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 773","lb":"SO773","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["a king's son's chief duty is to relieve the danger of living beings without counting his own life","general","asserted",4,"vijayan"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["விசயன்","செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ch":"so:35"},"so:774":{"book":"soolamani","num":774,"t":["கற்றவர் கடவுட் டானஞ் சேர்ந்தவர் களைக ணில்லா","ரற்றவ ரந்த ணாள ரன்றியு மனைய நீரார்க்","குற்றதோ ரிடுக்கண் வந்தா லுதவுதற் குரித்தன் றாயில்","பெற்றவிவ் வுடம்பு தன்னாற் பெறுபய னில்லை மன்னா"],"p":"","r":"The speaker says that if the body cannot help the learned, the pious, the helpless, the ascetics and the brahmins when trouble falls on them, it brings no fruit, king.","n":"மன்னா is the address; whether it names Tivittan or turns to a general king is not clear. The first line's list of kinds of people is also hard to divide.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","aram","payan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 774","lb":"SO774","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["a body that does not help others in their trouble yields no fruit","general","asserted",4,"vijayan"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["விசயன்","செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ch":"so:35"},"so:775":{"book":"soolamani","num":775,"t":["மன்னுயிர் வருத்தங் கண்டும் வாழ்வதே வலிக்கு மாயி","லன்னவ னாண்மை யாவ தலிபெற்ற வழகு போலா","மென்னையான் கொடுத்தும் வையத் திடுக்கணோய் கெடுப்ப னென்னும்","நின்னையே போலு நீரார் நிலமிசை நிலவி நின்றார்"],"p":"","r":"The speaker says a man who lives on while watching beings suffer has the manliness of a eunuch's beauty, and that people like Tivittan, who offer even themselves to end the world's pain, stand on earth.","n":"அலிபெற்ற அழகு is the verse's own measure of the man who lives on; line 3 reads எனையான் as 'myself' and line 4 as praise of Tivittan.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["arul","aanmai","idukkan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 775","lb":"SO775","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["manliness lies in giving oneself to end the suffering of living beings, and living on while they suffer is empty","general","asserted",2,"vijayan"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["விசயன்","செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ch":"so:35"},"so:776":{"book":"soolamani","num":776,"t":["ஒருவன திரண்டி யாக்கை யூன்பயி னரம்பின் யாத்த","உருவமும் புகழு மென்றாங் கவற்றினூழ் காத்து வந்து","மருவிய வுருவ மிங்கே மறைந்துபோ மற்ற யாக்கை","திருவமர்ந் துலக மேத்தச் சிறந்துபின் னிற்கு மன்றே"],"p":"","r":"The speaker says a person has two forms, the one of flesh and sinew that vanishes here and the other of fame that outlasts it and is praised by the world.","n":"யாக்கை in line 3 is taken as the other body of fame; ஊழ் காத்து வந்து may mean the body is kept by fate until its time, but the clause is hard to construe.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pugazh","yaakkai","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 776","lb":"SO776","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["the body of flesh passes but the form of fame remains after death","general","asserted",3,"vijayan"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["விசயன்","செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ch":"so:35"},"so:777":{"book":"soolamani","num":777,"t":["என்று தங்கதை யோடிரு நீண்முகிற்","குன்று சூழ்ந்த குழுமலர்க் கானகம்","சென்றொர் வெங்கடஞ் சேர்ந்தன ருச்சிமே","னின்று வெய்யவ னுநிலங் காய்த்தினான்"],"p":"","r":"The poet says that, having said this, the two went with the mace through a cloud-hill forest of flowers into a fierce desert, where the sun stood at noon and scorched the earth.","n":"தங்கதை is taken as தம் கதை, their mace, the line is not secure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaalam","idukkan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 777","lb":"SO777","mm":1,"ev":"The two princes enter a scorching wasteland at noon.","cl":[],"ag":["குமரர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:35"},"so:778":{"book":"soolamani","num":778,"t":["ஆங்கவ் வெங்கடஞ் சேர்ந்த பினையகா","ணீங்கிவ் வெங்கடுங் கானகத் தீடென","ஏங்கு நீர்ககடல் வண்ணனுக் கின்னணம்","வீங்கு வெண்டிரை வண்ணன் விளம்பினான்"],"p":"","r":"The poet says that after they reached the desert, the white-wave-hued prince bade the sea-hued one see how this fierce forest was.","n":"Line 3 has a doubled letter in நீர்ககடல், taken for the sea-hued prince's name; the content of Vijayan's words is not given in these lines.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kurippu","kaalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 778","lb":"SO778","mm":1,"ev":"Vijayan speaks to Tivittan after they reach the desert.","cl":[],"ag":["விசயன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:35"},"so:779":{"book":"soolamani","num":779,"t":["முழையு டைந்தழல் காலு முரம்பயற்","கழையு டைந்துகு கண்கவர் நித்திலம்","மழையு டைந்துகு நீரென வாய்மடுத்","துழையு டைந்துகு கின்றன வூங்கெலாம்"],"p":"","r":"The poet says that in the desert the bamboo splits and drops pearls, the rocky ground breathes fire from cracks, and the water the clouds should give is gone.","n":"The desert description is hard to divide into words; the pearl-bamboo and the sense of dried water are read from lines 2 and 3 only roughly.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaalam","naadu","idukkan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 779","lb":"SO779","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:35"},"so:780":{"book":"soolamani","num":780,"t":["மிக்க நீள்கழை மேல்விளை வுற்றழ","லொக்க வோடி யுறைத்தலி னான்மிசை","உக்க நெற்பொரி யுற்றொரு சாரெலாம்","தொக்க கற்றல மேற்றுடிக் கின்றவே"],"p":"","r":"The poet says the tall bamboo ripens and sparks, scattering parched grain over the rocks, which seethe all around.","n":"உக்க நெற்பொரி is taken as parched rice grains fallen from the bamboo; the verse is a picture of heat and the grammar is unsettled.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaalam","naadu","idukkan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 780","lb":"SO780","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:35"},"so:781":{"book":"soolamani","num":781,"t":["ஏங்கு செங்கதி ரோனெ றிப்பநிழல்","வேங்கொ லென்றொளித் திட்டிபம் வீழ்ந்துசேர்","பாங்க லார்மனை போலப் பறைந்தரோ","ஓங்கி நின்றுல வுற்றன வோமையே"],"p":"","r":"With the red sun beating down, the omai trees stand tall and sound like a house of mourning kin, as the elephants hide from the heat in their shade.","n":"The mourning-house simile gives the dry forest a grief of its own; the grammar of the third line and the word division are rough.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["idam","kaalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 781","lb":"SO781","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["விசயன்","செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ch":"so:36"},"so:782":{"book":"soolamani","num":782,"t":["அற்ற நீரழு வத்திடை நெல்லியின்","வற்ற லஞ்சினை யூடு வலித்தரோ","மற்ற வெவ்வெயி லுந்நிழல் வாயழ","லுற்று வீழ்ந்தது போன்றுள வாங்கெலாம்"],"p":"","r":"In the dry season the nelli's withered branches creak, and the sun's glare drops its shade as though fire were falling from the branches all around.","n":"The lines are compressed and the first line's sense of weeping or drought is not settled; read with caution.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaalam","idam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 782","lb":"SO782","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["விசயன்","செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ch":"so:36"},"so:783":{"book":"soolamani","num":783,"t":["பைத்த லைப்பட நாக மழன்றுதம்","பொய்த்த ளைத்தலை போதரக் கார்செய்வான்","கைத்த லம்முகிற் கின்றன காந்தளென்","றத்த லைச்சிலை மானயர் வெய்துமே"],"p":"","r":"The snake rages while the gloriosa climbs, and the lily flowers on the cloud-fed shoots stand like a bow-wielding deer-hunter's hand, so the rain seems about to return.","n":"The picture of cloud-sent rain and gloriosa blossoms is hard to construe; the verse may turn on a simile of the flower as a hand.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["mazhai","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 783","lb":"SO783","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["விசயன்","செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ch":"so:36"},"so:784":{"book":"soolamani","num":784,"t":["விசையி னோடு வெண் டேர்செலக் கண்டுநீர்","தசையி னோடிய நவ்வி யிருங்குழா","மிசையில் கீழ்மகன் கண்ணிரந் தெய்திய","வசையின் மேன்மகன் போல வருந்துமே"],"p":"","r":"Seeing the white chariot rush past, the thirsty deer herd droops like a noble who begged from a worthless man's eyes and met blame.","n":"The simile ranks begging of the base as a stain on the high-born, which the narrator accepts as common ground; the first two lines are loosely worded.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["iravu","maanam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 784","lb":"SO784","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["a noble who begs from the base earns blame","general","asserted",3,"vijayan"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["விசயன்","செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ch":"so:36"},"so:785":{"book":"soolamani","num":785,"t":["துடியர் தொண்டகப் பாணியர் வாளியர்","கொடிய செய்துமு னைப்புலங் கூட்டுணுங்","கடிய நீர்மையர் கானகங் காக்குநி","னடிய ரல்லதல் லாரவ ணில்லையே"],"p":"","r":"The drummers and bowmen who do cruel deeds and eat the border land's spoil guard the forest, and none stand there but the addressee's own servants.","n":"The guards are the king's own hunters, so the wild place is held by men who live by violence on his behalf.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["arasu","aran"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 785","lb":"SO785","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["விசயன்","செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ch":"so:36"},"so:786":{"book":"soolamani","num":786,"t":["அங்கவெங்க டங்கடந்த லங்குதாரி லங்குபூண்","சிங்கம் வென்ற செங்கண்மாலொ டம்பொன்மாலை வெண்கடாத்","திங்கள் வண்ணன் வெங்கண்யானை வேந்துசேர்ந்த நாடுசார்ந்","திங்கணின்ன இன்னகாணெ னப்புகழ்ந்தி யம்பினான்"],"p":"","r":"Naming the lands that the lion-conquering red-eyed one and the king of the white-tusked elephant hold, the moon-hued prince praises them and says these are the addressee's.","n":"The epithets are heavy and the line division is rough; the identification of the moon-hued one with Vijayan comes from 778 and 800.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["naadu","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 786","lb":"SO786","mm":1,"ev":"Vijayan, praising the country, begins to show Tivittan the lands that are his to hold.","cl":[],"ag":["விசயன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:36"},"so:787":{"book":"soolamani","num":787,"t":["மாலும்வாரி திங்கண்மூன்றும் வந்தறாத மாண்பினா","லாலுமாவ றானைநம்ம டிகளாளு நாட்டகம்","காலமாண்பி னன்றியுங் கார்கவின்ற நீரவே","போலுமாண்பி னேர்கலந்து பொங்குநீர புறணியே"],"p":"","r":"The land of our lord abounds in water that rises in all seasons, in the grace of the monsoon and in floods that swell in the rivers.","n":"The grammar is compressed; the speaker is taken as Vijayan, 'our lord' being the king.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["naadu","mazhai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 787","lb":"SO787","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["விசயன்","செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ch":"so:36"},"so:788":{"book":"soolamani","num":788,"t":["கொண்டல்வாடை யென்னுங் கூத்தன் யாத்தகூத்தின் மாட்சியால்","விண்டமா மலர்ப்பொதும்ப ரங்கமா விரும்புநீர்","வண்டுபாட வல்லியென்னு மாதராடு நாடகங்","கண்டுகொன்றை பொன்சொரிந்த காந்தள்கை மறித்தவே"],"p":"","r":"The east wind dances, the bees sing, and the creeper-woman watches the play while the konrai drops gold and the gloriosa turns its hand away.","n":"The whole verse is a play with the wind as dancer, the flowers as audience and the bees as singers.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","kaalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 788","lb":"SO788","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["விசயன்","செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ch":"so:36"},"so:789":{"book":"soolamani","num":789,"t":["கைமலர்த்த காந்தளுங் கரியநீர்க் கருவிளை","மைம்மலர்த் தடங்கணேர் வகுத்தலர்ந்த வட்டமு","மொய்மலர்ப் பொதும்பின்மேன் முறுவலித்த முல்லையும்","கொய்மலர்க் குழற்றிரட்சி கொண்டு காய்த்த கொறையும்"],"p":"","r":"The gloriosa that opens its hand, the dark water lilies, the wide-open eyes of the flower rounds, the smiling jasmine and the curled clusters bend and bear fruit.","n":"The syntax runs on into 790; the verse is a list whose verb comes in the next verse.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","naadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 789","lb":"SO789","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["விசயன்","செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ch":"so:36"},"so:790":{"book":"soolamani","num":790,"t":["தொண்டைவாய் நிறங்கொளக் கனிந்துதூங்கு கின்றவும்","வண்டுபாய வார்கொடி மருங்குலாய் வளர்ந்தவுங்","கண்டபாலெ லாங்கலந்து கண்கவற்று மாதலால்","விண்டுமாலை மாதராரின் மேவுநீர கானமே"],"p":"","r":"Fruit that reddens like a lip, vines slim as a waist and everything seen catching the eye, so the forest looks like a garland of women.","n":"The forest is likened to women, which is the poet's usual way of praising a land's loveliness.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 790","lb":"SO790","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["விசயன்","செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ch":"so:36"},"so:791":{"book":"soolamani","num":791,"t":["தண்ணிலாவி ரிந்தமுல்லை தாதுசேர் தளிர்மிடைந்","தெண்ணிலாய சாயலம் மிடாமணற் பிறங்கன்மேற்","பண்ணிலாய வண்டுபாடு பாங்கரோடு பாங்கணிந்து","வெண்ணிலா விரிந்தவெல்லை போலுமிங்கொர் பாலெலாம்"],"p":"","r":"Jasmine spread under the moonlight, bees singing on the sandbank and the places of white sand look to one side as though the moon had spread.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","idam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 791","lb":"SO791","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["விசயன்","செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ch":"so:36"},"so:792":{"book":"soolamani","num":792,"t":["தேனவாவி மூசுகின்ற தேம்பிறழ்பூ தாங்கலந்து","கானநாவல் கொம்பினிற் கனிந்துகா லசைந்தவற்","றேனைமாடு வண்டிருந் திருண்டகான மிங்கிதற்","கூனமா யிருட்பிழம் புறங்குகின்ற தொக்குமே"],"p":"","r":"The bees circling the nectar-rich blossoms in the jambul grove make the forest look dark, as if a lump of night were sleeping there.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","idam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 792","lb":"SO792","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["விசயன்","செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ch":"so:36"},"so:793":{"book":"soolamani","num":793,"t":["வாயிதழ் திறங்கொளக் கனிந்த தொண்டை வந்தொசிந்து","தூயிதழ்த் துணர்துதைந்து தோன்றுகின்ற தோன்றியின்","பாயிதழ்ப் பரப்பின்மே லரத்தகோப மூர்ந்தயற்","சேயிதழ்ப் பொலிந்தகாடு செக்கர்வான மொக்குமே"],"p":"","r":"The ripe tondai fruit and the thorny coral-red flowers of the thonri make the forest glow like an evening sky.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","idam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 793","lb":"SO793","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["விசயன்","செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ch":"so:36"},"so:794":{"book":"soolamani","num":794,"t":["ஆடிணர்க் கொடிப்பட ரகிற்பொழுதும் பயற்பொலிந்த","கூடிணர்க் குழாநிலைக் கொழுமலர்க் குமிழ்மிசைக்","கோடிணர்க் குலைக்கொசிந்த கொன்றைவிண்ட தாதுசோர்ந்","தோடிணர்ச் சுடர்ப்பொனுக்க கானமொக்கு மூங்கெலாம்"],"p":"","r":"The golden konrai, the aghil creeper and the pollen-laden blossoms make the whole country look like molten gold.","n":"The images of gold and the flowers' dust are heaped on one another, a way of praising the country's wealth.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","naadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 794","lb":"SO794","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["விசயன்","செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ch":"so:36"},"so:795":{"book":"soolamani","num":795,"t":["பார்மகிழ்ந்த பைஞ்சுருட் பயிர்மிசைப் பயின்றெழுந்","தேர்கலந்து பாசிலைப் பரப்பினூ டிரைத்தரோ","கார்மகிழ்ந்த கார்மயிற் கலாபமொய்த்த கானக","நீர்மகிழ்ந்த நீர்க்கட னிரந்ததொக்கு நீரதே"],"p":"","r":"The peacocks dance and the green crops move with the wind like a sea of water in the monsoon-glad forest.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","mazhai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 795","lb":"SO795","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["விசயன்","செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ch":"so:36"},"so:796":{"book":"soolamani","num":796,"t":["ஏறுகொண்ட கோவல ரேந்து தன்ன வக்குரன்","மாறுகொண்ட கோடியர் மணிமுழா முழங்கலிற்","றாறுகொண்ட தோகைமஞ்ஞை யாடல்கண்டு கண்மகிழ்ந்து","சாறுகொண்டு மான்கணத் தயங்குநீர சாரெலாம்"],"p":"","r":"At the sound of the cowherds' and bards' drums the peacocks dance and the deer herds delight in the festival of the land beside the hill.","n":"The festival is imagined, with the peacocks as dancers and the deer as spectators.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","idam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 796","lb":"SO796","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["விசயன்","செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ch":"so:36"},"so:797":{"book":"soolamani","num":797,"t":["கார்மணந்த கானயாறு கல்லலைத் திழிந்தொலிக்கு","நீர்மணந்த நீள்கரை நிரைத்தெழுந்த நாணல்சூழ்","வார்மணற் பிறங்கன்மாலை வல்லிவிண்ட தாதணிந்து","தார்மணந்த வாரமார்ப யாகசாலை போலுமே"],"p":"","r":"The riverbank, where sand-banks, creepers and the pollen of flowers lie, looks like a sacrificial hall, and the addressee is said to wear a garland on his chest.","n":"The sacrificial-hall simile (யாகசாலை) is the poem's picture of orderly beauty; it says nothing for or against the rite, and the Jain poet uses it as a plain figure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["idam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 797","lb":"SO797","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["விசயன்","செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ch":"so:36"},"so:798":{"book":"soolamani","num":798,"t":["கரவைகன்று வாய்மிகுந்த வமிழ்தினோடு கண்ணகன்","புறவின்மாம ரைம்முலைப் பொழிந்தபா றெகிழ்ந்தெழப்","பறவையுண்டு பாடவும் பால்பரந்த பூமியி","னறவுவிண்ட நாகுமுல்லை வாய்திறந்து நக்கவே"],"p":"","r":"The cows' milk streams from udders as the calves suck, the birds feed and sing, and the jasmine opens as if to taste the flow.","n":"Milk, flowers and birds are given human mouths and gestures.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","naadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 798","lb":"SO798","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["விசயன்","செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ch":"so:36"},"so:799":{"book":"soolamani","num":799,"t":["வேரல்வேலி மால்வரைக் கவானின் வேய் விலங்கலிற்","சாரன்மேக நீர்முதிர்ந்து தண்டளந்து ளித்தலால்","மூரல்வா யசும்பறாத முல்லைவிள்ளு மெல்லைபோய்","நீரவாளை பூவின்வைகு நீள்பரப்பு நண்ணினார்"],"p":"","r":"The clouds drip water on the hills, the jasmine opens in the fields, and the guests go on to the wide stretches where fish and water-birds dwell.","n":"The last line shifts to the farming country, so the verse marks the travellers' passage from forest to field.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["mazhai","naadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 799","lb":"SO799","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["விசயன்","செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ch":"so:36"},"so:800":{"book":"soolamani","num":800,"t":["புதுநாண் மலர்விண் டுபொழிந்த திழியு","மதுநா றுபுனன் மருதத் தினைமற்","றிதுகா ணெனவின் னனசொல் லினனே","விதுமாண் மிகுசோ திவிளங் கொளியான்"],"p":"","r":"The rain-fed nectar flows from the fresh-opened flowers into the marutham water, and the moon-bright prince tells his companion to look at this.","n":"The last two lines name the speaker's glory, which makes this verse a turning point from the forest to the farm country.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naadu","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 800","lb":"SO800","mm":1,"ev":"Vijayan, pointing, tells Tivittan to look at the water of the farming country.","cl":[],"ag":["விசயன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:36"},"so:801":{"book":"soolamani","num":801,"t":["அயலோ தமிரட் டவலம் பொலிநீர்","வயலோ தமயங் கமயங் கவதிர்ந்","தியலோ தையிளஞ் சிறையன் னமெழக்","கயலோ டியொளிப் பனகாண் கழலோய்"],"p":"","r":"The addressee is told to look at the fish that dart away and the young swans that rise from the shallows as the water swells, turning cloudy and bright.","n":"The word division is broken; the sense of the first lines is only roughly clear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","naadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 801","lb":"SO801","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["விசயன்","செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ch":"so:36"},"so:802":{"book":"soolamani","num":802,"t":["வளவா சநிலப் பலவின் சுளையு","மிளவா ழையினின் னெழிலங் கனியும்","களமாங் கனியின் றிரளுங் கலவிக்","குளமா யினயோ சனை கொண் டனவே"],"p":"","r":"The jackfruit segments, the plantain's fruit and the heaps of mangoes in the orchard have become the places of the meeting of lovers and guests.","n":"The sense of the last line is uncertain; 'kalavi' is taken as mingling or union.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","naadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 802","lb":"SO802","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["விசயன்","செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ch":"so:36"},"so:803":{"book":"soolamani","num":803,"t":["வனமா வினிருங் கனியுண் டுமதர்த்","தினவா ளையிரைத் தெழுகின் றனகாண்","கனவா ழைமடற் கடுவன் மறையப்","புனவா னரமந் திபுலம் புவகாண்"],"p":"","r":"The monkeys eat the fruits of the mango, groan with fullness, and the male langur hides under the plantain leaf while the hill-field she-monkeys wail.","n":"The picture of monkeys' wailing is played for comedy; the verse closes the series of 'look at this' verses.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","naadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 803","lb":"SO803","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["விசயன்","செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ch":"so:36"},"so:804":{"book":"soolamani","num":804,"t":["வளமா நிலையே திமருப் பினிட","விளவா ழைநுதிக் கமழ்தே னொழுகிக்","குளமார் குளிர்தா மரைக்கொண் டநகைத்","தளவா யுகுகின் றனகாண் டகவோய்"],"p":"","r":"The speaker points out honey dripping from the tips of young plantains and the shed buds of jasmine falling among the cool lotuses of the ponds.","n":"The verses 804 to 813 point at things to look at (காண், கேள்) and are taken as the speech of an unnamed guide to one hearer (814 says it was told to 'him'); the Tamil does not name speaker or hearer. The yamaka word play and run-together words make the exact picture doubtful.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","naadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 804","lb":"SO804","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["(பெயர் இல்லை)",""],"ch":"so:37"},"so:805":{"book":"soolamani","num":805,"t":["இவைசெந் நெலிடை கருநீ லவன","மவையந் நெலிடை கழுநீ ரழுவ","முவையொண் டுறைவிண் டொளிவிம் முநகு","நகைவென் றனதா மரைநாண் மலரே"],"p":"","r":"The speaker points to lilies and lotus blooms among the paddy, the lotus flowers shaming a smile.","n":"The grammar of lines 1 to 3 is broken by the word division; only the lotuses that outdo a smile are sure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","naadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 805","lb":"SO805","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["(பெயர் இல்லை)",""],"ch":"so:37"},"so:806":{"book":"soolamani","num":806,"t":["கழையா டுகரும் பினறைக் கடிகைப்","பொழிசா றடுவெம் புகைபொங் கியயற்","றழையோ டுயர்சோ லைகடாம் விரவி","மழையோ டுமலைத் தடமொத் துளவே"],"p":"","r":"The speaker shows the smoke of boiling sugar-cane juice rising and mixing with the high groves nearby, so that the place looks like a mountain with clouds.","n":"The cane-boiling smoke is the one concrete sign of the countryside's work; the rest of the verse is simile.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","naadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 806","lb":"SO806","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["(பெயர் இல்லை)",""],"ch":"so:37"},"so:807":{"book":"soolamani","num":807,"t":["கருநீ லமணிந் தகதுப் பினயற்","கருநீ லமணிந் தனகண் ணிணைகள்","கருநீ லமணிக் கதிர்கட் டியெனக்","கருநீ லமணிந் தகருங் குழலே"],"p":"","r":"The verse describes a woman whose cheek, eyes and dark hair carry the dark-blue lily and the sheen of sapphire.","n":"The repeated word கருநீல is the poet's play on sound; whether the woman is a particular one or a type of the country women is not shown.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 807","lb":"SO807","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["(பெயர் இல்லை)",""],"ch":"so:37"},"so:808":{"book":"soolamani","num":808,"t":["வளர்செங் கிடையி னெழில்வைத் தநுதல்","வளர்செங் கிடையி னொளிவவ் வியவாய்","வளர்செங் கிடையின் வளையா டும்வயல்","வளர்செங் கிடைமா மலர்மல் குசிகை"],"p":"","r":"The verse describes a woman's forehead, lips, bangles and hair against the flowering செங்கிடை plants of the fields.","n":"The repeated word வளர்செங்கிடை and the compressed syntax leave both the plant and the sense of each line uncertain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 808","lb":"SO808","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["(பெயர் இல்லை)",""],"ch":"so:37"},"so:809":{"book":"soolamani","num":809,"t":["வயலாம் பனெறித் தவகைத் தழையன்","வயலாம் பன்மிலைத் தவடிச் சவியன்","வயலாம் பன்மலிந் தபரப் புடையன்","வயலாம் பன்மலர்த் தொகைமா லையினாள்"],"p":"","r":"The verse describes a woman dressed in leaves and garlanded with the flowers of the water lily, set in a field rich with them.","n":"The refrain வயலாம்பல் is word play and the clauses are hard to attach to a subject.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 809","lb":"SO809","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["(பெயர் இல்லை)",""],"ch":"so:37"},"so:810":{"book":"soolamani","num":810,"t":["வளர்தா மரையல் லிமலர்த் தியகை","வளர்தா மரையல் லிமயக் குமொளி","வளர்தா மரையல் லிமகிழ்ந் தனள்போல்","வளர்தா மரையல் லிவனத் திடையாள்"],"p":"","r":"The verse pictures a woman of the grove, in a place thick with lotus petals, gladdened as if by the blooming lotus.","n":"The refrain வளர்தாமரையல்லி is word play; the sense is only roughly fixed.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 810","lb":"SO810","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["(பெயர் இல்லை)",""],"ch":"so:37"},"so:811":{"book":"soolamani","num":811,"t":["நளிர்வார் கழலாய் புகழ்நா டிநயந்","தொளிர்வார் குழலா ளொருமா தவளு","ளுளர்வார் கனியும் மதுவுந் தெகிழத்","கிளர்பார் வையுறக் கிளர்கின் றதுகேள்"],"p":"","r":"The speaker bids a hearer listen to what rises as a woman of shining hair, in search of fame, lets fruit and honey run.","n":"The words ஒருமாதவளும் and கிளர்பார்வையுற are corrupt or obscure, and the verse's subject is not clear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","pugazh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 811","lb":"SO811","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["(பெயர் இல்லை)",""],"ch":"so:37"},"so:812":{"book":"soolamani","num":812,"t":["மதிகா ணநிமர்ந் தமதிற் சிகர","நுதிமா ளிகைமேல நுடங் குகொடி","கதிரோ ணொளிமாழ் கவெழுந் துகலந்","ததுகா ணமதா ரொளிமா நகரே"],"p":"","r":"The speaker shows the gem-lit city rising, its wall tops, tower tops and flags blending with the sun's rays.","n":"From here to 813 the guide turns from the country to the city ahead, which sets up the arrival in 814.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naadu","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 812","lb":"SO812","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["(பெயர் இல்லை)",""],"ch":"so:37"},"so:813":{"book":"soolamani","num":813,"t":["அறவே தியரா குதியம் புகையார்","உறவே திகைவிம் மியவொண் புறவ","நிறவே திகைமீ துநிமிர்ந் தபொழிற்","புரவே திகையே றுவகாண் புகழோய்"],"p":"","r":"The speaker shows the altars of the city's sacrificers, where the smoke of the oblations of righteous Vedic brahmins rises over the groves.","n":"The poet lets the Jain epic's own travellers see Vedic fire-offerings as a mark of a well-ordered city; he gives no view of the rite itself.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["aaguthi","andhanar","naadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 813","lb":"SO813","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["(பெயர் இல்லை)",""],"ch":"so:37"},"so:814":{"book":"soolamani","num":814,"t":["இன்னன விளையவற் கியம்பு மெல்லையுட்","பொன்னக ரடைந்தனர் பொழுதுஞ் சென்றது","நன்னக ரிரைத்தது நரன்ற வின்னிய","மன்னவ குமரரும் வறுமை நீங்கினார்"],"p":"","r":"The speaker's telling ends as the princes come to the golden city at nightfall, while the city sounds, and their want is gone.","n":"வறுமை நீங்கினார் is taken as the end of the road's hardship; the line does not say whose want or of what kind.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["inmai","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 814","lb":"SO814","mm":1,"ev":"The two princes reach the king's city, Podanam, at nightfall.","cl":[],"ag":["குமரர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:37"},"so:815":{"book":"soolamani","num":815,"t":["இளங்களிக் குஞ்சர மிரட்டித் தாயிரம்","துளங்கொளிக் கலினமாத் தூளி யெல்லைய","வளங்கெழு குமரரை வலங்கொண் டெய்தின","அளந்தறிந் திலமகன் படையி னெல்லையே"],"p":"","r":"The king's wide army of young elephants and bridled horses comes in a ring of dust to circle the princes, its extent beyond measure.","n":"The verse says 'we could not measure' (அளந்தறிந்திலம்), a poet's voice in the plural that is not a character's.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["padai","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 815","lb":"SO815","mm":1,"ev":"The king's army rides out and circles the princes to bring them in.","cl":[],"ag":["படை"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:37"},"so:816":{"book":"soolamani","num":816,"t":["துன்னிய துணரிளந் தோன்றி மென்கொடி","மன்னிய வனத்திடை மலர்ந்து நீண்டபோற்","கன்னியர் கைவிளக் கேந்தக் காவலன்","பொன்னியல் வளநகர் பொலியத் தோன்றினார்"],"p":"","r":"The princes appear in the king's golden city like a creeper of red flowers in a wood, while maidens hold lamps in their hands.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 816","lb":"SO816","mm":1,"ev":"Maidens line the way with hand-lamps as the princes ride into the city.","cl":[],"ag":["குமரர்","கன்னியர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:37"},"so:817":{"book":"soolamani","num":817,"t":["காவியங் கருங்கணார் கமழ வூட்டிய","வாவியங் கொழும்புகை தழுவி யாய்மலர்க்","கோவையங் குழுநிலை மாடம் யாவையும்","பாவிய பனிவரைப் படிவங் கொண்டவே"],"p":"","r":"The poet describes the city's high houses, dim with the smoke of perfumes, as if they were hills of cool snow.","n":"The simile of snow mountains is the poet's only comment; no person acts.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 817","lb":"SO817","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:37"},"so:818":{"book":"soolamani","num":818,"t":["மல்லிகை மனங்கமழ் மதுபெய் மாலையு","முல்லையம் பிணையலு மொய்த்த பூண்கடை","எல்லியங் கிளம்பிறைக் கதிர்கள் வீழ்ந்தன","தொல்லையங் கடிநகர் துயில்வ போன்றவே"],"p":"","r":"The poet describes the city at night, the rays of the young moon falling on garlands and ornaments as if the ancient city slept.","n":"The jasmine garlands and the moon-lit gems carry the sleep image; no event is given.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","iravu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 818","lb":"SO818","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:37"},"so:819":{"book":"soolamani","num":819,"t":["செம்பொன்மா மணிநகர்ச் செல்வ வீதியுட்","கொம்பனா ரடிதொழக் கோயி லெய்தலு","நம்பிமார் வருகென நாறு நீரொளி","அம்பொன்மா மணிமுடி யரச னேயினான்"],"p":"","r":"As the princes reach the palace, maidens bow at their feet and the king in his gem crown bids the youths come in.","n":"The words நம்பிமார் வருக are the king's, in the poet's telling.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","panivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 819","lb":"SO819","mm":1,"ev":"The king orders the princes brought before him in the palace.","cl":[],"ag":["கன்னியர்","பிரசாபதி"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:37"},"so:820":{"book":"soolamani","num":820,"t":["அருளுவ தென்கொலென் றஞ்சி செஞ்சுடர்","இருளுக வெழுந்ததொத் திருந்த கோனடிச்","சுருளுறு குஞ்சிக டுதையத் தாழ்ந்தனர்","மருளுறு மனத்தினன் மன்னன் னாயினான்"],"p":"","r":"The princes, afraid of what the king will do, bow so that their locks touch his feet while he stands uneasy at heart.","n":"The verse says the princes fear (அஞ்சி) and the king's heart is bewildered (மருளுறு). It does not say why, so the setting is probably that the king had been anxious about them.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["panivu","acham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 820","lb":"SO820","mm":1,"ev":"The princes bow low to the king, who is troubled in mind.","cl":[],"ag":["குமரர்","பிரசாபதி"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:37"},"so:821":{"book":"soolamani","num":821,"t":["திருவரை யனையதோட் சிழ்ருவர் தம்மையக்","கருவரை யனையவெங் களிதல் யானையா","னிருவரும் வருகென விரண்டு தோளினு","மொருவரை யகலத்தி னெடுங்கப் புல்லினான்"],"p":"","r":"The king calls both strong-armed sons to him and holds them close with both his arms.","n":"Line 1 and 2 hold what look like slips of the text (சிழ்ருவர், களிதல்); the embrace itself is clear.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["makkal","anbu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 821","lb":"SO821","mm":1,"ev":"The king calls the two princes forward and embraces them both.","cl":[],"ag":["பிரசாபதி"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:37"},"so:822":{"book":"soolamani","num":822,"t":["மானவா மதகளிற் றுழவன் மக்கடந்","தேனவாஞ் செழுமலர் செறிந்த குஞ்சியுட்","கானமா மலர்த்துகள் கழுமி வீழ்ந்தன","வானவாந் தடக்கையான் மகிழ்ந்து நோக்கினான்"],"p":"","r":"The king looks gladly at his sons as the dust of forest flowers falls from their hair.","n":"The pollen from the forest is the first sign, which 823 turns into a question, that the princes have been in the wild.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["makkal","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 822","lb":"SO822","mm":1,"ev":"The king, glad, sees flower pollen from the forest fall from the princes' hair.","cl":[],"ag":["பிரசாபதி"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:37"},"so:823":{"book":"soolamani","num":823,"t":["என்னைநும் மீரலர்க் குஞ்சி தம்முளித்","துன்னிய வனத்துக டுதைந்த வாறென","மன்னவ னருளலு மகர வார்குழை","மின்னிவர் மணிக்கழல் விசயன் செப்பினான்"],"p":"","r":"The king asks why the dust of forest blooms clings in the hair of the two, and Vijayan replies.","n":"The question itself is carried in lines 1 to 3 in broken syntax; its sense is taken from what Vijayan answers.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","makkal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 823","lb":"SO823","mm":1,"ev":"The king asks how forest dust came into the princes' hair, and Vijayan answers.","cl":[],"ag":["பிரசாபதி","விசயன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:37"},"so:824":{"book":"soolamani","num":824,"t":["போற்றநம் புறணிசூழ் காடு பாழ்செய்வான்","சீற்றமிக் குடையதோர் சீயஞ் சேர்ந்தது","வேற்றுவன் றமர்கள்வந் துரைப்ப வெம்பியிவ்","வாற்றல்சா லடியன்சொன் றதனை நீக்கினான்"],"p":"","r":"Vijayan says a fierce lion came to ruin the forest round about the city's edge, and that when it was reported, this mighty servant drove it away.","n":"The words வேற்றுவன் தமர்கள் and இவ்வாற்றல்சால் அடியன் are obscure; the verse is read as Vijayan crediting his brother (the 'mighty servant') with removing the lion, which 825 appears to support, but the Tamil of 824 does not name him.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["arasu","vali"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 824","lb":"SO824","mm":1,"ev":"Vijayan reports to the king that a lion had come to ravage the forest and was removed.","cl":[],"ag":["அரிமா"],"ec":[],"spk":["விசயன்","பிரசாபதி"],"ch":"so:37"},"so:825":{"book":"soolamani","num":825,"t":["யானுமங் கிவனொடு மடிக ளேகினேன்","வானுய ரிமகரி மருங்கி லென்றுபூந்","தேனுய ரலங்கலான் சிறுவன் சொல்லலுந்","தானுயிர் தளிர்ப்பதோர் சவிய னாயினான்"],"p":"","r":"Tivittan adds that he too went with him to the mountain side, and the king's life flowers back as he hears it.","n":"The speaker is named only as 'the son of the garlanded lord' (சிறுவன்), taken as the younger prince; the verse leaves open what exactly the lion's end was.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["makkal","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 825","lb":"SO825","mm":1,"ev":"Tivittan tells the king he went along too, and the king is gladdened.","cl":[],"ag":["பிரசாபதி"],"ec":[],"spk":["செங்கண் ணெடியான்","பிரசாபதி"],"ch":"so:37"},"so:826":{"book":"soolamani","num":826,"t":["சுடரொளி மிகுசோதி சூழ்கழற் காளை மார்தம்","அடரொளி முடிமன்ன னேவலா னாய்பொன் னாகத்","தொடரொளி சுடர்ஞாயிற் சூளிகை சூழு நெற்றிப்","படரொளி நெடுவாயிற் பள்ளிபம் பலங்கள் சேர்ந்தார்"],"p":"","r":"By the king's order the youths in their anklets reach their lodging behind the long gate under the sun-lit tower.","n":"This closes the sarukkam; the last line's words பள்ளி பம்பலங்கள் are obscure and are taken as their resting place.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["arasu","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 826","lb":"SO826","mm":1,"ev":"On the king's order the princes go to their lodging in the palace.","cl":[],"ag":["குமரர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:37"},"so:827":{"book":"soolamani","num":827,"t":["செங்கண்மால் சிங்கம் வென்று செழுமலர்த் திலகக் கண்ணித்","திங்கள்வாள் வண்ண னோடுந் திருநகர் பெர்ந்த பின்னை","அங்கண்மாற் குரிய நங்கை யரும்பெற லவட்குத் தாதை","வெங்கண்மாற் களிறன் னான்றன் றிறமினி விளம்ப லுற்றேன்."],"p":"","r":"The poet says that after the red-eyed Tivittan, having beaten the lion, went back to the great city with the moon-bright Vijayan, he will now tell of the father of the maiden destined for him.","n":"Line 2 reads பெர்ந்த where the sense wants பெயர்ந்த; the verse is a hinge that closes the lion episode and opens the wedding section.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pugazh","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 827","lb":"SO827","mm":1,"ev":"Tivittan, having killed the lion, returns to the city with Vijayan, and the poet turns to the bride's father.","cl":[],"ag":["செங்கண் ணெடியான்","விசயன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:38"},"so:828":{"book":"soolamani","num":828,"t":["உற்றவான் குழவித் திங்க ளொளிமுழை யகட்டுப் போந்து","முற்றுவான் முளைத்த போலு மெயிறுடை மூரிச் சிங்கம்","மற்றம்மா லழித்த தல்லாம் வானமா றாகச் சென்ற","ஒற்றனா லுணர்ந்து வேந்த னுவகையங் கடலு ளாழ்ந்தான்."],"p":"","r":"A spy brings the king word that the lion has been destroyed, and the king sinks into a sea of joy.","n":"The lion is likened to a young moon coming out of a cave, so the beast's death is praised through the image of its own beauty.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","pugazh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 828","lb":"SO828","mm":1,"ev":"A spy reports the lion's death to the Vidyadhara king, who rejoices.","cl":[],"ag":["ஒற்றன்","வேந்தன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:38"},"so:829":{"book":"soolamani","num":829,"t":["கரியவாய் விலங்கி நீண்டு களிக்கய லிரண்டு தம்முட்","பொரியபோ கின்ற போலும் பொங்கரித் தடங்கட் பேதைக்","குரியமா லவற்குச் சென்று கொடுப்பனென் றுலகங் காக்கும்","பெரியவன் றமரோ டெண்ணிக் கடிவினை பெருக்க லுற்றான்."],"p":"","r":"Thinking the maiden with dark long eyes fit only for the one who killed the lion, the king takes counsel with his kin and begins the wedding rites.","n":"The daughter is given as a reward for the deed; her own wish is not spoken of, only the father's decision with his kin.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ilvaazhkkai","sutram","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 829","lb":"SO829","mm":1,"ev":"The Vidyadhara king resolves with his kin to give his daughter to Tivittan and begins the wedding preparations.","cl":[],"ag":["வேந்தன்","தமர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:38"},"so:830":{"book":"soolamani","num":830,"t":["கிளரொளி மாடக் கோயிற் கின்னரர் கெழுவ லோவா","வளரொளி வயங்கு தோன்றல் வருத்தமா னத்து மன்னன்","உளரொளி யுமிழும் பூணான் பிரீதிதிமா வர்த்த தனனென் றோதும்","தளரொளி தயங்கு மேனித் தாமரைச் செங்க ணானே."],"p":"","r":"The poet names one of the eight Vidyadhara lords, the lord of the city of Vardhamana, bright-limbed and lotus-eyed.","n":"One of eight verses (830 to 837) that list the lords by city, each praised through his city, chariot or ornament. The name is written in an irregular form here, with a doubled syllable.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["arasu","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 830","lb":"SO830","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:38"},"so:831":{"book":"soolamani","num":831,"t":["மண்ணலங் கனியுஞ் சாதி மணிமுழா வதிரு மாடக்","கண்ணலங் கனியுந் தோன்றல் கந்தருவ நகரங் காப்பான்","விண்ணலங் கனியுஞ் சீர்த்தி விருகவெல் கடிகொள் பேரான்","பெண்ணலங் கனியு நீர்மை பெருகிய வுருவத் தோளான்."],"p":"","r":"The poet names one of the eight Vidyadhara lords, the lord who guards the Gandharva city, fair of form.","n":"One of eight verses (830 to 837) that list the lords by city, each praised through his city, chariot or ornament. The name விருகவெல் is not clear and may be corrupt.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["arasu","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 831","lb":"SO831","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:38"},"so:832":{"book":"soolamani","num":832,"t":["பூமரு பொலங்கொள் சோலைப் பொன்னணி புரிசை வேலிக்","காமரு கபாட வாயிற் கந்தமா தனத்தைக் காப்பான்","தேமரு திலதக் கண்ணித் திவாகர தேவ னென்பான்","தாமரை தயங்கு சோதித் தாரணி துரகத் தேரான்."],"p":"","r":"The poet names one of the eight Vidyadhara lords, the lord who guards the fort called Gandhamadhanam, with a golden chariot.","n":"One of eight verses (830 to 837) that list the lords by city, each praised through his city, chariot or ornament.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 832","lb":"SO832","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:38"},"so:833":{"book":"soolamani","num":833,"t":["சுந்தரப் பொடியும் பூவுஞ் சுரும்பொடு துதைந்து வீசிச்","சந்திரன் றவழு மாடச் சக்கிர வாள மன்னன்","மந்தர மலைக்கும் யானை வச்சிர தாட னென்பான்","அந்தரத் தமரர் கோமா னணிந்துபோந் தனைய நீரான்."],"p":"","r":"The poet names one of the eight Vidyadhara lords, the king of Chakravalam, named for an elephant-like strength.","n":"One of eight verses (830 to 837) that list the lords by city, each praised through his city, chariot or ornament.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 833","lb":"SO833","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:38"},"so:834":{"book":"soolamani","num":834,"t":["காரணங் குருவ மேகங் கருவு கொண் டதிர்ந்து வெய்யோன்","தேரணங் குறுக்கு மாடத் தேவர வனத்துச் செல்வன்","ஏரணங் குறுக்கும் பைந்தா ரிரமிய தரனென் றேங்கும்","சீரணங் குறுக்குஞ் செய்கைச் செஞ்சுடர்த் திலகப் பூணான்."],"p":"","r":"The poet names one of the eight Vidyadhara lords, the lord of the Devaravanam, whose chariot outshines the sun's.","n":"One of eight verses (830 to 837) that list the lords by city, each praised through his city, chariot or ornament.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 834","lb":"SO834","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:38"},"so:835":{"book":"soolamani","num":835,"t":["கண்ணிலாங் கனக மாடக் கதலிகை முகிலோ டாடி","விண்ணிலா விருண்டு தோன்றும் விசய கூடத்து மன்னன்","வெண்ணிலா விரிந்த பூணான் வேகமா ரதனீண் முந்நீர்","மண்ணெலாம் வணங்க நின்ற மழகளிற் றரசோ டொப்பான்."],"p":"","r":"The poet names one of the eight Vidyadhara lords, the king of Vijayakoodam, likened to a young elephant lord.","n":"One of eight verses (830 to 837) that list the lords by city, each praised through his city, chariot or ornament.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 835","lb":"SO835","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:38"},"so:836":{"book":"soolamani","num":836,"t":["மென்னரம் பனுக்குந் தீஞ்சொன் மெல்லிய லவர்கள் பாடல்","கின்னரம் பிணிக்குஞ் செய்கைக் கிருதமா தனத்தைக் காப்பான்","கைந்நவின் றிலங்குஞ் செவ்வேற் காவலன் கருடன் சேர்ந்த","மெய்நவின் றிலங்குஞ் செம்பொ னங்கதம் விளைந்த பேரான்."],"p":"","r":"The poet names one of the eight Vidyadhara lords, the guardian of Kirutha Madhanam, whose name joins Garuda and the golden armlet.","n":"One of eight verses (830 to 837) that list the lords by city, each praised through his city, chariot or ornament. The name is not stated whole; the reading garuda-angadan joins words of the third and fourth lines.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["arasu","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 836","lb":"SO836","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:38"},"so:837":{"book":"soolamani","num":837,"t":["ஓங்குநீர்ப் புரிசை வேலி யொண்டுறைக் குவளை வேய்ந்த","தூங்குநீ ருடுத்த பாங்கிற் சோபன முடைய தோன்றல்","தேங்குநீர்க் கடலந் தானைச் சித்திர தரனிவ் வையம்","தாங்குநீ ரொளியோ டொன்றித் தண்ணளி தயங்க நின்றான்."],"p":"","r":"The poet names one of the eight Vidyadhara lords, the lord of a city ringed by water and lotus, who bears the earth with his strength.","n":"One of eight verses (830 to 837) that list the lords by city, each praised through his city, chariot or ornament.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 837","lb":"SO837","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:38"},"so:838":{"book":"soolamani","num":838,"t":["என்றிவ ரெண்மர் தம்மை யிரதநூ புரத்து ளானா","நின்றுநீர் காமி னென்று நிறீஇயபின் னீதி மன்னன்","ஒன்றிய வுலக மெல்லா மொருங்குடன் விழுங்க லுற்றுச்","சென்றுயர் கடலோ டொக்குஞ் சேனைபண் ணமைக்க வென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"After posting these eight to guard the city of Rathanupuram, the just king orders a host raised that is like a sea swallowing the whole world.","n":"The host is raised to escort a bride, yet it is pictured as a conquering sea, so a marriage procession borrows the image of war.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["padai","arasu","soozhchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 838","lb":"SO838","mm":1,"ev":"The king leaves eight lords to guard Rathanupuram and orders his army made ready.","cl":[],"ag":["வேந்தன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:38"},"so:839":{"book":"soolamani","num":839,"t":["வெண்ணிலாக் குழவித் திங்கண் மேகத்துப் பதித்த போலும்","ஒண்ணிலா வுருவக் கோட்ட வோடைமால் களிற்றின் மேலோர்","பண்ணெலா மணிந்து தோன்றப் பருமித்துக் கருவி யேற்றிக்","கண்ணிலாம் பதாகை சேர்த்திக் காழகில் கழும விட்டார்."],"p":"","r":"The troops arrayed elephants like the new moon set in cloud, with ornaments, gear and banners, and burned aloe wood over them.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["padai","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 839","lb":"SO839","mm":1,"ev":"The troops deck the war elephants with ornaments, banners and incense.","cl":[],"ag":["வீரர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:38"},"so:840":{"book":"soolamani","num":840,"t":["கட்டிய கம்மச் செய்கைக் கதிர்மணிக் கனகச் சூலம்","பட்டமொ டிலங்கப் பண்ணிப் பக்கரை பதைப்ப யாத்து","மட்டவி ழலங்கல் வீரர் சேர்தலும் வலத்து முன்னாற்","கொட்டிய குரத வாலித் தெழுந்தன குதிரைச் சேனை."],"p":"","r":"Garlanded warriors gathered, and the horse host rose raising dust from its hooves, the horses bound with ornamented tridents and cloths.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["padai","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 840","lb":"SO840","mm":1,"ev":"The warriors gather and the cavalry is made ready and advances.","cl":[],"ag":["வீரர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:38"},"so:841":{"book":"soolamani","num":841,"t":["மணித் தொழில் வளைந்த சூட்டின் மறுப்பறுத் தியற்றப் பட்ட","அணித் தொழி லாரக் கோவை யாடகக் கொடிஞ்சி யம்பொன்","துணித்திடை பதித்த தட்டிற் சுடர்மணித் துரகத் திண்டேர்","கணித்தளப் பரிய நீர கல்லெனக் கலந்த வன்றே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes the sturdy chariots, with gem-set tops, golden pillars and gem-inlaid floors, mingling in numbers beyond counting.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["padai","azhagu","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 841","lb":"SO841","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:38"},"so:842":{"book":"soolamani","num":842,"t":["ஒட்டிய கலிங்கந் தாண்மேற் றிரைத்துடுத் துருவக் கோடிப்","பட்டிகை பதைப்ப யாத்துப் பரட்டைய நரல வீக்கிக்","கட்டிய கழலர் தாரர் கதிரொடு கனலும் வாளர்","மட்டுய ரலங்கல் சூடி மறங்கிளர் மள்ளர் சூழ்ந்தார்."],"p":"","r":"The fighters came round the host, bound in cloth, anklets and garlands, with bright swords, ready for fight.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["padai","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 842","lb":"SO842","mm":1,"ev":"The foot soldiers gird themselves and gather around the host.","cl":[],"ag":["வீரர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:38"},"so:843":{"book":"soolamani","num":843,"t":["இன்னிசை யமரர் பேகொண் டியன்றமா நகரங் காக்கும்","பொன்னவில் கடகப் பைம்பூட் புரந்தர னனைய மாண்பின்","மன்னவற் கிளைய வேந்தன் வயங்கெரிப் பெயர்கொ டேரான்","கன்னியைக் காக்கு நீர்மைக் கடற்படை பரப்பிச் சென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"The younger brother of the king, in a chariot named for blazing fire, goes with a sea-like host to guard the bride.","n":"The relation is read from மன்னவற் கிளைய வேந்தன், and the word பேகொண் in line 1 looks corrupt.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["padai","vendhan","sutram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 843","lb":"SO843","mm":1,"ev":"The king's younger brother sets out with his army as the bride's guard.","cl":[],"ag":["இளைய வேந்தன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:38"},"so:844":{"book":"soolamani","num":844,"t":["அங்கல மலர்ந்த தோன்ற லரிபுரத் தவர்கள் கோமான்","பொங்கல ரணிந்த பைந்தார்ப் புலிப்பெயர்ப் பொலங் கொ டேரான்","மங்கலக் களிற்றி னான்றன் மருமகன் மகர முந்நீர்த்","தங்கொலி மிகுத்த தானை யொடுகதி ரெறிப்பச் சார்ந்தான்."],"p":"","r":"The lord of Aripuram, in a tiger-named golden chariot and kin to the king of the auspicious elephant, arrives with a roaring host.","n":"The word மருமகன் can mean son-in-law or sister's son, so the tie to the king of the auspicious elephant is not fixed.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["padai","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 844","lb":"SO844","mm":1,"ev":"The lord of Aripuram arrives with his army.","cl":[],"ag":["அரிபுரத்தவர் கோமான்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:38"},"so:845":{"book":"soolamani","num":845,"t":["வாரணி முரச மார்ப்ப வரிவளை வயிரொ டேங்கத்","தாரணி மறவர் சூழத் தமனியக் கலங்க டாங்கி","ஆரணி யுருவத் திண்டே ரானைமே லருக்க கீர்த்தி","நீரணி கடலந் தானை நிலநெளி பரப்ப நின்றான்."],"p":"","r":"Arkakirti stands on an elephant, with drums sounding and warriors around him, as his sea-like host makes the ground ripple.","n":"The verse leaves unsaid whose son he is; the name is the poem's own.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["padai","pugazh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 845","lb":"SO845","mm":1,"ev":"Arkakirti stands ready with his army on the field.","cl":[],"ag":["அருக்க கீர்த்தி"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:38"},"so:846":{"book":"soolamani","num":846,"t":["சேனைபண் ணமைத்துச் சென்று திருக்கடை செறிந்த போழ்திற்","றானையுள் படுநர் மாண்பிற் றாரவற் றொழுது கூற","வேனவிற் றடக்கை வேந்தன் விண்ணியல் விமான மொன்று","வானவில் லுமிழ்ந்து மின்ன மனத்தினா னிருமித் திட்டான்."],"p":"","r":"When the army reached the great gate, men of the host bowed and told it to the king, who created in mind a heavenly vimana shot with rainbow light.","n":"The king is taken to be the Vidyadhara king, since a vimana made by mind is a vidyadhara power, but the verse does not name him.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vendhan","perumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 846","lb":"SO846","mm":1,"ev":"Men of the host announce the arrival at the gate; the king raises a vimana by thought.","cl":[],"ag":["படுநர்","வேந்தன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:38"},"so:847":{"book":"soolamani","num":847,"t":["மற்றதன் வடிவு கேட்பின் மரகத மணிக்க றன்மேல்","பெற்றதன் னிலையிற் றாகிப் பெருகிய வளமை தோன்றிச்","சுற்றிய பசும்பொற் சோதி சொரிந்துபோய்த் துறக்கங் காண","முற்றிய முகட்டு நீலக் குவட்டிடை முடிந்த தன்றே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes the vimana as standing on an emerald stone, shedding green gold light and ending in a blue peak that reaches heaven.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 847","lb":"SO847","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:38"},"so:848":{"book":"soolamani","num":848,"t":["பாரித்த பவழத் திண்காற் பளிங்குபோழ் பலகை தன்மேற்","பூரித்த சுடரின் செம்பொற் போதிகைப் புடங்க டோறு","மூரித்தண் சுடர்வெண் முத்தின் பரூஉத்திரண் முயங்கி ஞான்ல","வேரித்தண் டுவலை கால மாலைகள் விசித்த வன்றே."],"p":"","r":"The vimana has coral pillars, crystal slabs, gold capitals and strings of pearls and flower garlands hung in its curves.","n":"Line 3 has ஞான்ல, probably a misprint for ஞான்ற, 'hanging'.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 848","lb":"SO848","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:38"},"so:849":{"book":"soolamani","num":849,"t":["தடுத்துவில் லுமிழுஞ் செம்பொற் றண்டிகைத் திரள்க டாங்க","மடுத்தன வயிரத் தம்ப மாடநீண் மதலை தோறும்","தொடுத்தன சுரும்பு பாயுந் துணரணி தயங்கு மாலை","அடுத்தன நிறத்த வாக வழுத்தின மணிக ளெல்லாம்."],"p":"","r":"Gold litters, diamond pillars and garlands hung from every post light the vimana, and all its gems shine in their own colours.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 849","lb":"SO849","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:38"},"so:850":{"book":"soolamani","num":850,"t":["ஊன்றிய மகரப் பேழ்வா யொளிமுகந் தெளிப்ப வீழ்ந்து","நான்றன மணிசெய் தாம நகைமுக நிறைந்த சோதி","கான்றன கனக சாலங் கலந்தன கங்க ணீகம்","தோன்றின பதாகை சூழ்ந்து சுடர்ந்தன சூல நாயில்."],"p":"","r":"Garlands hang from the mouths of carved sea-monsters, gold nets and windows gleam, and banners and tridents shine on the roof.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 850","lb":"SO850","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:38"},"so:851":{"book":"soolamani","num":851,"t":["அஞ்சிறைப் பறவைச் சாதி யாவிப்ப வணிந்து மேலால்","விஞ்சையர் மிதுனத் தேவர் விண்ணியங் குருவ மெல்லாம்","செஞ்சுட ரெறிப்பச் சேர்ந்து செங்கதிர்ப் பரவை சிந்தி","வெஞ்சுடர் விலங்க நீண்டு விண்ணிடை விரிந்த தன்றே.."],"p":"","r":"Winged birds and the Vidyadhara and Mithuna gods crowd above the wedding hall, whose red sparks fill the sky and keep the fierce sun off.","n":"The first two lines are clipped and the subject of the verbs is not clear; the verse is read as the opening picture of the hall.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 851","lb":"SO851","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:39"},"so:852":{"book":"soolamani","num":852,"t":["வாரணி பசும்பொன் வாழை மரகதக் கமுகொ டோங்கித்","தோரணத் தூண்க டோறுஞ் சுடர்மணி சிலம்ப நான்று","நீரணி நிழல்கொண் முத்த மணன்மிசை நிரந்து தோன்றிப்","பூரண குடங்கள் செம்பொற் கொழுங்கதிர் புதைந்த கீழால்.."],"p":"","r":"The hall is dressed with gold banana trees and emerald areca palms, gem-hung gate pillars, pearls strewn on the sand and full pots.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 852","lb":"SO852","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:39"},"so:853":{"book":"soolamani","num":853,"t":["கொழுந்திரள் வயிரக் கோடிக் கூர்முளை செறித்துச் செம்பொற்","செழுந்திரட் புடகஞ் சேர்ந்த திருவளர் கபாட வாயிற்","பொழிந்ததண் சுடர வாகிப் பொலந்தொடர் புலம்பத் தூங்கி","எழுந்தொலி சிலம்ப விம்மி யிணர்கொண்ட மணிக ளெல்லாம்.."],"p":"","r":"Gate-leaves bound in gold and pinned with diamond-tipped studs hang with gold chains and rows of ringing gems.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 853","lb":"SO853","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:39"},"so:854":{"book":"soolamani","num":854,"t":["பாய்கதிர்ப் பளிங்கிற் கோத்துப் பருமணி வயிரஞ் சூழ்ந்த","வாய்கதிர்ச் சால வாயி லகிலயா வுயிர்த்த வாவி","மாயிரு விசும்பின் மான்று மழைதவழ் குன்றம் போலச்","சேயவர்க் குருவங் காட்டித் தேநிரை கொண்ட வன்றே.."],"p":"","r":"Gem-strung crystal doorways and steps shine like cloud-skimming hills and show their forms to those who come before them.","n":"The last line, with its word for honey-rows, is obscure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 854","lb":"SO854","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:39"},"so:855":{"book":"soolamani","num":855,"t":["செம்பொனங் களியிற் செய்து சித்திரந் தெளிப்பத் தீட்டி","வம்பவெண் முத்தச் சாந்தின் மட்டித்து மணிக ளெல்லாம்","நம்பிய வொளிய வாகத் தெளித்துநன் கெழுதப்பட்டுத்","தம்புலந் தெரிந்து தோன்றுந் தடத்தின தலங்க ளெல்லாம்.."],"p":"","r":"The floors are painted with gold dye, smeared with pearl paste and set with gems so that every corner shows clearly.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 855","lb":"SO855","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:39"},"so:856":{"book":"soolamani","num":856,"t":["பாடக மிலங்கச் செங்கேழ்ச் சீறடிப் பரவை யல்குல்","நாடக மகளி ராடு நாடக வரங்கு நன்பொன்","மாடகந் தெளிப்ப வேய்ந்த மண்டபத் தலமும் வண்ண","ஆடக மணிந்த கூட நிலைகளு மயல வெல்லாம்.."],"p":"","r":"The stage where dancing women perform, the pavilion floor and the gold door-frames beside it are likewise fitted out.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 856","lb":"SO856","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:39"},"so:857":{"book":"soolamani","num":857,"t":["மரகத மணிக ளீன்ற கதிரெனுந் தளிர்கள் வார்ந்து","சொரிகதிர் வயிரங்கான்ற சுடரெனுங் கொழுந்து தோன்றிப்","புரிகதிர் முத்த மென்னும் புதுநகை யரும்பு பம்பி","விரிகதிர்ச் செம்பொன் பூத்து விண்ணணங் குறுப்ப வீங்கி.."],"p":"","r":"The hall's gems are told as a flowering tree: emerald as sprouts, diamond as shoots, pearl as buds, gold as blossom.","n":"The image makes the building a wishing-tree, which verse 858 names outright.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 857","lb":"SO857","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:39"},"so:858":{"book":"soolamani","num":858,"t":["கதிர்நகைக் கனபொற் சோதிக் கனகசா லங்க ளென்னு","மதுநகப் பருகி மான்ற மணிவண்டு மயங்கி வானோர்","விதிநகு விமான மென்னுங் கற்பகம் விரிந்த போழ்திற்","பதிநகர்க் கிறைவன் பாவை சயம்பவை வருக வென்றான்.."],"p":"","r":"The hall opens like a wishing-tree among the gods' own vimanas, and the lord of the city says that his daughter Sayambavai should come.","n":"Only the last line is speech; the first three still describe the hall, and the verse joins the two.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 858","lb":"SO858","mm":1,"ev":"The city's lord summons his daughter Sayambavai to the hall.","cl":[],"ag":["பதிநகர்க் கிறைவன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["பதிநகர்க் கிறைவன்","சயம்பவை"],"ch":"so:39"},"so:859":{"book":"soolamani","num":859,"t":["இன்ன தருண் மன்னவன் தென்றுகடை காக்குங்","கன்னிய ருணர்த்தலி னிணர்க்கொடி கடுப்பாள்","பன்னிய பளிக்கறையொர் பஞ்சணையின் மேலாள்","மன்னுமணி மாடமிசை மஞ்ஞையி னிழிந்தாள்.."],"p":"","r":"Told by the girls who keep the gate, the princess comes down like a flowering creeper from her crystal chamber and couch, as a peacock descends.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 859","lb":"SO859","mm":1,"ev":"Sayambavai comes down from her upper chamber when the gate-girls announce the summons.","cl":[],"ag":["சயம்பவை","கன்னியர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:39"},"so:860":{"book":"soolamani","num":860,"t":["மாகமலை யன்னமணி மாடநிலை யுள்ளாற்","போகமிகு பூந்தவிசின் மீதுபுதை வுற்ற","வாகைவன மாலைபுனை மன்னன்மகள் செல்வாள்","மேகபட லத்திடை மினற்கொடியொ டொத்தாள்.."],"p":"","r":"The king's daughter, wearing the victory garland, walks out from the tall mansion like lightning among cloud.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","perumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 860","lb":"SO860","mm":1,"ev":"Sayambavai walks out of the mansion.","cl":[],"ag":["சயம்பவை"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:39"},"so:861":{"book":"soolamani","num":861,"t":["அங்கையி னயிற்படைய ராணுடையர் பூணர்","கொங்கைவள ராதகுழ லார்கள்புடை காப்பப்","பங்கயமு கத்தவர்ப லாண்டுபல கூறி","நங்கையடி போற்றியென நங்கைநடை கற்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"Armed women and others with unripe breasts guard her sides and wish her long years, as she walks with a stately gait.","n":"The guard here is of arms and numbers, set round a girl who is being sent out to be chosen.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","perumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 861","lb":"SO861","mm":1,"ev":"Maidens guard Sayambavai and bless her as she walks.","cl":[],"ag":["கன்னியர்","சயம்பவை"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:39"},"so:862":{"book":"soolamani","num":862,"t":["காவன்மிகு கன்னிநகர் கன்னியர்கள் காக்கும்","வாவியகி னாறுமணி வாயிலவை நீங்கி","நாவிகமழ் கொம்பனைய நங்கைநகை வேலான்","தேவியமர் கோயிலது செவ்வன மடைந்தாள்."],"p":"","r":"Passing the gates of the girls' city and its scented pool-stair, she goes straight to the palace where the queen sits.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","kudi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 862","lb":"SO862","mm":1,"ev":"Sayambavai goes to the queen's palace.","cl":[],"ag":["சயம்பவை"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:39"},"so:863":{"book":"soolamani","num":863,"t":["மௌவன்மலர் வேய்ந்துமது நாறுமணி யைம்பாற்","கொவ்வைதுயில் கொண்டதுவர் வாய்க்கொடியொ டொப்பா","டெய்வமண நாறுதிரு மேனிபுறங் காக்கும்","அவ்வையரொ டெய்திமுத லவ்வையடி சேர்ந்தாள்."],"p":"","r":"With the women who guard her fragrant body she reaches the feet of the first mother.","n":"The word for the first mother is read as the queen, since the house is the city lord's.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kudi","panivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 863","lb":"SO863","mm":1,"ev":"Sayambavai reaches the queen and bows at her feet.","cl":[],"ag":["சயம்பவை"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:39"},"so:864":{"book":"soolamani","num":864,"t":["வணங்கிய கணங்குழையை வாங்கிமுலை நோவக்","குணங்கெழு குலத்தலைவி கொண்டுமிசை புல்லி","மணங்கமழ் குழற்சிகையுள் வண்டிரிய மோந்தாங்","கணங்கினனை யாளுவகை யாழ்கடலு ளாழ்ந்தாள்."],"p":"","r":"The lady of the house takes the bowing girl into her arms, smells her scented hair and sinks in a sea of joy.","n":"The lady is taken as the queen; the sea of joy is hers.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["anbu","kudi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 864","lb":"SO864","mm":1,"ev":"The queen embraces Sayambavai.","cl":[],"ag":["தேவி"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:39"},"so:865":{"book":"soolamani","num":865,"t":["செம்பொனணி சீரியன சேரினிடை நோமென்","றம்பொனணி நொய்யன வணிந்தலர் மிலைச்சி","வம்பினணி வாட்கணிடை மைபிறழ வைத்துக்","கொம்பினனை யாள்குளிரு மாறுகுயில் வித்தாள்."],"p":"","r":"Thinking heavy gold would hurt her waist, Sayambavai is dressed in light gold, flowers and eye-paint, and made cool like a creeper in the shade.","n":"The last words are obscure and who dresses her is not named.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 865","lb":"SO865","mm":1,"ev":"Sayambavai is adorned for the vimana.","cl":[],"ag":["சயம்பவை"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:39"},"so:866":{"book":"soolamani","num":866,"t":["அன்னவகை தேவிமக ளோடமரு மெல்லை","முன்னுமுக வோரையொடு மூர்த்தநல நோக்கி","மன்னுபுல வோர்கள்சொல மன்னன்மக டன்னை","இன்னகைவி மானதல மேறுகினி தென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"The hour and its signs are looked over, learned men speak, and the speaker says that she may now climb the vimana.","n":"Who says the last words is not named; the lord of the city is the likely speaker.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaalam","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 866","lb":"SO866","mm":1,"ev":"On the learned men's word of the hour, the king bids Sayambavai board the vimana.","cl":[],"ag":["புலவோர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["பதிநகர்க் கிறைவன்","சயம்பவை"],"ch":"so:39"},"so:867":{"book":"soolamani","num":867,"t":["தொண்டைதொலை வித்ததுவர் வாய்மகளிர் சூழக்","கண்டுவளர் தாயரொடு கஞ்சுகியர் காப்பா","விண்டுவளர் சோதிகொள் விமானமது சேர்வாள்","வண்டுவளர் கற்பமுறை வான்மகெளா டொத்தாள்."],"p":"","r":"With women, nurses and chamberlains guarding her, she goes to the shining vimana like a goddess of the world above.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","perumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 867","lb":"SO867","mm":1,"ev":"Sayambavai goes to the vimana in escort.","cl":[],"ag":["சயம்பவை"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:39"},"so:868":{"book":"soolamani","num":868,"t":["முன்னிமுடி வித்தமிகு விஞ்சையின் முதிர்ந்தார்","அன்னநடை யாட்கடிமை யார்வமொ டடைந்தார்","பின்னிவிடு கூந்தலர் பிடித்தவயில் வாளர்","கன்னியரி ராயிரவர் கன்னிபுடை காத்தார்."],"p":"","r":"Two thousand maidens learned in magic arts, loyal to her, guard the girl on all sides with drawn swords.","n":"Verses 861 and 868 set a guard of arms round the girl.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["padai","anbu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 868","lb":"SO868","mm":1,"ev":"Two thousand maidens take guard round Sayambavai.","cl":[],"ag":["கன்னியர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:39"},"so:869":{"book":"soolamani","num":869,"t":["அஞ்சுடர் மணிக்குழவி யாடுகழன் மாடம்","பஞ்சுடைய பந்துகிளி பாவையொடு பூவை","மஞ்சுடைய மின்னினனை யாண்மகிழு நீர","செஞ்சுடர் விமானமது சேர்ந்தன செறிந்தே."],"p":"","r":"Her playthings, balls, parrots, dolls and mynas, are carried to the vimana.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 869","lb":"SO869","mm":1,"ev":"Her toys are carried to the vimana.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:39"},"so:870":{"book":"soolamani","num":870,"t":["கற்பக மலர்ப்பிணையல் சேர்ந்துகமழ் கின்ற","பொற்பமைசெங் கோடிகமொ டாடைபுதை வுற்ற","நற்புடைய பேழை நறுஞ் சாந்துநனி பெய்த","செப்பொடு கடப்பக மடுத்தன செறிந்தே."],"p":"","r":"Garlands, cloth, chests, sandal and boxes of paste are loaded with them.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 870","lb":"SO870","mm":1,"ev":"Her garlands, cloths and perfumes are loaded.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:39"},"so:871":{"book":"soolamani","num":871,"t":["பெருங்கல நிறைந்தமிகு பெட்டகமோ டெல்லா","அருங்கலமு மார்ந்தவறை யாயினக ளாய்பொன்","நெருங்கொளி நிறைந்தமிகு சோதிநிழல் சூழப்","பெருங்கலி விமானமது சென்றது பெயர்ந்தே."],"p":"","r":"Chests and treasure fill the chambers of the vimana, and it moves off with its light around it.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","perumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 871","lb":"SO871","mm":1,"ev":"The vimana, loaded with her goods, sets out.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:39"},"so:872":{"book":"soolamani","num":872,"t":["முரச மார்த்தன முரன்றன முரிவளை முகிலிடை வயிரேங்க","அரியொ டாகுளி யாலித்த வதிர்ந்தன யணிமுழ வரு கெல்லாம்","விரைசென் மாவொடு விரவின களிறும்தண் மிடைந்தன கடுமான் றேர்","புரைசை யானையி னெருத்திடை யரசனும் புகழொடு பொலிவுற்றான்."],"p":"","r":"Drums, conches and war-horns sound, horses and elephants crowd in, and the king rides an elephant's neck in glory.","n":"The lines are printed as eight half-lines and were joined.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","pugazh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 872","lb":"SO872","mm":1,"ev":"The king rides out at the head of the procession.","cl":[],"ag":["பதிநகர்க் கிறைவன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:39"},"so:873":{"book":"soolamani","num":873,"t":["சிகர யானையின் கவிழ்மணி சிலம்பின சிலம்பின பணிலங்கள்","முகர வாயின பணவங்கண் முரன்றன முரன்றன முகிலெல்லா","மகர மால்கடல் வரைமிசை யெழுந்தனெ வெழுந்தது படைமாற்ற","நகர வாயிலின் புறம்பணை நடந்தது நடுங்கின கொடியெல்லாம்."],"p":"","r":"Elephant bells, conches and horse-drums roar like the sea as the host leaves by the city gate, with banners trembling.","n":"The lines are printed as eight half-lines and were joined.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["padai","perumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 873","lb":"SO873","mm":1,"ev":"The army sets out through the city gate.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:39"},"so:874":{"book":"soolamani","num":874,"t":["ஒளிறு வாட்படை யுளர்ந்தது கிளர்ந்தன ருழைக்கல வுழையோர்கள்","களிறு காத்திர முறுத்தலி னெறித்தலை கருமுகி னெரிவுற்ற","குளிறு மின்னியங் குழுமலிற் செழுமலைக் கொடுமுடி யுடனார்த்த","வெளிறில் கேள்வியான் பெரும்படை விசும்பிடை நிரந்தொளி விரிந்தன்றே"],"p":"","r":"Swords gleam, elephants are held back, and the learned king's great host spreads shining through the sky.","n":"The subject of the last line is taken as the king 'of faultless learning'.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["padai","perumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 874","lb":"SO874","mm":1,"ev":"The army fills the sky around the vimana.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:39"},"so:875":{"book":"soolamani","num":875,"t":["அமித மாகிய பெரும்படை யகன்கட லகல்விசும் பழகெய்த","அமித மாகிய பெருவரை நிமிர்சிகை யதனய லமர்ந்தேக","அமித மாகிய நிலைத்தலை மலையணி யருவிக ளவையெல்லாம்","அமித மாபவை சயம்பவைக் கடிதொழு தவையவை யறிவித்தாள்."],"p":"","r":"The poet says that, as the vast host passes through the sky over seas, great peaks and hill-top streams, she makes all of them known to the maiden, who bows to each.","n":"The verse opens the tour of the land below and rests on the repeated word அமிதம், used four times; the guide is only a feminine verb here and is taken to be the maiden's companion.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["aakaasam","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 875","lb":"SO875","mm":1,"ev":"The guide names to the maiden the seas, mountains and streams that the Vidyadhara host passes over in the sky.","cl":[],"ag":["(பெயர் இல்லை)"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:40"},"so:876":{"book":"soolamani","num":876,"t":["நங்கை காணிது நம்மலைக் கும்பரப் பொன்மலைப் புடைவீழுங்","கங்கை யாறிதன் கரையன கற்பகக் காவுகளிவை கண்டாய்","இங்கு நாமிரு விசும்பிடை யியங்கலிற் சிறியவொத் துளவேனும்","அங்க ணார்க்குநம் முலகினை யளப்பவொத் துளவவை யறியுங்கால்."],"p":"","r":"The guide tells the maiden to look at the golden mountain's falling Ganga and its wishing-tree groves, which seem small from the sky yet would measure the earthly world for those who dwell there.","n":"The tour shows the land by setting two worlds against each other, ours and the upper one, each measured by the other.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["aakaasam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 876","lb":"SO876","mm":1,"ev":"The guide points out the heavenly river and groves to the maiden from the moving host.","cl":[],"ag":["(பெயர் இல்லை)"],"ec":[],"spk":["(பெயர் இல்லை)","நங்கை"],"ch":"so:40"},"so:877":{"book":"soolamani","num":877,"t":["இரைக்கு மஞ்சிறைப் பறவைக ளெனப்பெய ரினவண்டு புடைசூழ","நுரைக்க னென்னுமக் குழம்பு கொண்டெதிர்ந்தழெ நுடங்கிய விலையத்தாற்","றிறைக்க ரங்களிற் செழுமலைச் சந்தனத் திரள்களைக் கரைமேல்வைத்","தரைக்கு மற்றிது குணகடற் றிரையொடும் பொருதல தவியாதே."],"p":"","r":"The guide describes a river whose foam is like sandal-paste and whose bees are its birds, bringing sandal logs to the bank and breaking against the eastern sea's waves without rest.","n":"The poet turns the river into a person: foam as ointment, waves as hands, bees as birds. The word division of this verse is broken and several words are rare, so the sense is held loosely.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","aakaasam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 877","lb":"SO877","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["(பெயர் இல்லை)","நங்கை"],"ch":"so:40"},"so:878":{"book":"soolamani","num":878,"t":["முந்து மற்றிதன் முதன்மலைப் பிறந்துநம் மலையது முழைப் பேருஞ்","சிந்து வென்பது வலத்தது செழுங்கலஞ் சிதர்கின்ற திகழ்யாறு","நுந்து பொன்னொளித் திரையெனுங் கரதலப் புடங்களி னுரையென்னும்","பந்து பொங்கநின் றடித்திடத் திளைப்பதொத் துளததுப கருங்கால்."],"p":"","r":"The guide says a river called the Sindhu, born at the foot of that mountain and flowing to the right of their own hill, plays like a girl striking a ball of foam with the palms of its gold waves.","n":"The only river named in the tour is the Sindhu; the sense of the last line rests on a rare word and is held loosely.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","oli"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 878","lb":"SO878","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["(பெயர் இல்லை)","நங்கை"],"ch":"so:40"},"so:879":{"book":"soolamani","num":879,"t":["உயருஞ் சந்தனப் பொழிலலைத் தொளிர்மணிக் கலங்களை யுமிழ்ந் திட்டுப்","பெயருந் தெண்டிரைப் பிறங்கலுட் பிணங்கிய பெருவரை யகிறேக்கி","வயிர வேதிகை மலைவது கோபுர வாய்தலின் படிதீண்டி","அயிரை வார்கரைக் குடகடற் றிரையொடு பொருதல தவியாதே."],"p":"","r":"The guide shows a river that throws up jewels from the sandal groves, runs through hills in the waves and beats against the shore like the eastern sea, never ceasing.","n":"The verse repeats the closing refrain of 877 about the waves of the eastern sea. The word division of this verse is broken and several words are rare, so the sense is held loosely.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 879","lb":"SO879","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["(பெயர் இல்லை)","நங்கை"],"ch":"so:40"},"so:880":{"book":"soolamani","num":880,"t":["தேனெய் பாலொடு கலந்தன சின்மொழிச் சிறுநுதற் றிருவேநஞ்","சேனை மாமுகிற் படலங்கண் மிசைச்செலச் சினைமுகில் முரலக் கேட்","டேனை யானைக ளிணையென விருந்திட விருங்கைமா வினங்காக்கும்","கான யானைகள் கருவரை யனையன கனல்வன விவை காணாய்."],"p":"","r":"The guide bids the maiden see the wild elephants, mixed with honey and milk, that rage at the thunder of clouds drifting over the host and take it for a rival elephant.","n":"The epithet 'the elephant-like mountain' casts the clouds and the herd as rivals; the lines 2 to 3 are clipped.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["padai","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 880","lb":"SO880","mm":1,"ev":"The guide points to the forest elephants raging at the clouds above the host.","cl":[],"ag":["யானைகள்"],"ec":[],"spk":["(பெயர் இல்லை)","நங்கை"],"ch":"so:40"},"so:881":{"book":"soolamani","num":881,"t":["பேய்மை யானங்கொண் டிருந்தன்ன பெருவரை நெரிதரத் திரைசிந்தித்","தீமை யானைகள் செவிபுகு செறிகடாந் திளைத்தலிற் றிசைநாறிப்","போய்மை யானங்கொண் டிழிதரும் பெருந்திசைப் புடையன புனல்யாறு","சேய்மையா னமக்கொளிர் முத்தின் பருவடந் தெளிப்பவொத் துளபாவாய்."],"p":"","r":"The guide shows rivers coming down from the great hills, bearing the smell of rutting elephants' must, which seem to scatter a string of pearls for the maiden to see.","n":"The verse is a continuing scene of the tour; the word division is broken.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 881","lb":"SO881","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["(பெயர் இல்லை)","நங்கை"],"ch":"so:40"},"so:882":{"book":"soolamani","num":882,"t":["கருவிவா னத்தி னகடுதொட் டனவென நிலத்திடைக் கவின் செய்ய","மருவிநங் கட்கு மணிவட்டுச் சிதர்ப்ப வொத் துளசில மலை யெல்லாம்","அருவி வெண்டிரை சொரிகின்ற வருவரைக் குவடுக ளவை முன்னாற்","பரவை வெண்கொடி யெடுத்துநம் படைக்கெதி ரெழுவதொத் துளபாவாய்."],"p":"","r":"The guide describes the hills with falling streams that look like white banners raised against the host and like hands that scatter pearls and gems at the passers-by.","n":"The streams are made the banners of a rival army raised before the host; the conceit makes the landscape an opponent that yields.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 882","lb":"SO882","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["(பெயர் இல்லை)","நங்கை"],"ch":"so:40"},"so:883":{"book":"soolamani","num":883,"t":["அலங்கல் வார்குழ லிமிர்தன்ன சின்மொழி யரிவைநம் மருங்கெல்லாம்","விலங்கல் போல்வன வெண்மருப் பிரட்டைய வேழங்கள் விளையாடி","இலங்கு மால்வரை யிறுவரைத் தடங்குத்தி யிடந்திட விருபாலும்","கலங்கொள் பேழைகள் கவிழ்ந்தனெக் கதிர்மணி சொரிகின்ற வவை காணாய்."],"p":"","r":"The guide points to tusker pairs playing like hills, goring the hillside so that jewels pour out like chests overturned on both sides.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 883","lb":"SO883","mm":1,"ev":"Pairs of elephants gore the hillside and spill gems.","cl":[],"ag":["யானைகள்"],"ec":[],"spk":["(பெயர் இல்லை)","நங்கை"],"ch":"so:40"},"so:884":{"book":"soolamani","num":884,"t":["அங்கண் மால்வரை யருவிதந் தடக்கையிற் புடைத்துநின் றமர்ந் தாடிப்","பைங்கட் செம்முகப் பரூஉக்கையம் பகடுதம் பிடிக்கணம் புடை சூழச்","செங்கற் றூளிதஞ் செவிப்புறத் தெறிதலிற் சிகரங்க ளிடையெல்லாம்","பொங்கிக் குங்குமப் பொடியொத்துப் பொலிகின்ற பொலங்கொடி புடைநோக்காய்."],"p":"","r":"The guide describes female elephants circling a bull that sports under a hill stream, whose raised red dust looks like saffron powder over the peaks.","n":"This verse and the next two are the guide's remarks to the maiden at a point where the tour turns to the host's own life.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 884","lb":"SO884","mm":1,"ev":"A bull elephant plays in the stream among his herd of cows.","cl":[],"ag":["யானைகள்"],"ec":[],"spk":["(பெயர் இல்லை)","நங்கை"],"ch":"so:40"},"so:885":{"book":"soolamani","num":885,"t":["துளங்கு வார்குழைத் துவரிதழ்த் துடியிடைச் சுடர்நுதற் சுரிகோதாய்","விளங்கு வெங்கதிர் விலங்கிய விசும்பிடை யியங்குதல் புலன்கொள்ளாப்","பளிங்கி னொள்ளறைப் பரப்பிடைப் பாய்வித்த பருமணி நெடுமான்றேர்","வலங்கொ ணம்படைக் கடலிடை மறித்தவை சுழல்கின்ற வகை நோக்காய்."],"p":"","r":"The guide shows how the jewelled chariots in their ranks turn about in the ocean of the host, moving so smoothly across the clear crystal sky that the eye cannot catch their going.","n":"","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["padai","aakaasam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 885","lb":"SO885","mm":1,"ev":"The Vidyadhara chariot horses wheel in the host's ranks in the sky.","cl":[],"ag":["தேர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["(பெயர் இல்லை)","நங்கை"],"ch":"so:40"},"so:886":{"book":"soolamani","num":886,"t":["எடுத்த மாருத மெறிதலி னெகிழ்ந்தன சிகழிகை யிணரோடும்","தொடுத்த மாலைகள் துணர்கொளப் புனைவன துகிலிடை புடைசோர","உடுத்த காஞ்சியி னொளிமணிக் கதிர்நகைப் பட்டங்க ளுடையாக","அடுத்து வீழுமொ ரணியிழை யிளையவள் படுகின்ற ததுகாணாய்."],"p":"","r":"The guide points to a young woman of the host whose loosened hair and wind-blown garlands and robe let fall her girdle, the sparkling ornaments dropping about her.","n":"The poet draws the woman's unwinding dress as an unconscious show; the verse carries no reproof.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","naanam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 886","lb":"SO886","mm":1,"ev":"A young woman of the host loses her robe and ornaments in the wind.","cl":[],"ag":["கொம்பனார்"],"ec":[],"spk":["(பெயர் இல்லை)","நங்கை"],"ch":"so:40"},"so:887":{"book":"soolamani","num":887,"t":["மல்கு மும்மத மதகளி றுழக்கலின் மயங்கிய மழைமேகம்","பில்கு நுண்டுளி யுறைத்தலிற் பனித்தநம் பெரும்படை மடவார்கள்","நல்கு காதல ரகலத்து ளொடுங்குதல் பலர்முன்னை நனிநாணி","மெல்கு பூந்துகில் விரித்தவா வருகின்ற விதலைகண் மிகநோக்காய்."],"p":"","r":"The guide shows women of the host trembling from the mist of fine drops from clouds and the must of elephants and hiding in their husbands' arms, spreading their robes from shyness before many.","n":"The shyness here is playful and shown as seemly; the verse is the one in the tour that sets desire against modesty.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","naanam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 887","lb":"SO887","mm":1,"ev":"The women of the host hide in their lovers' chests and draw their robes against the drizzle and the crowd.","cl":[],"ag":["கொம்பனார்"],"ec":[],"spk":["(பெயர் இல்லை)","நங்கை"],"ch":"so:40"},"so:888":{"book":"soolamani","num":888,"t":["இலைய நாடகத் தெழில்கெழு விமானமஃ தியல்கின்ற விசைதன்னான்","மலையி னம்மொடு வருவவொத் துளவவை வரவில மடனோக்கி","உலைவில் வையகத் தொளிசெயும் பகலவ னுறுசுடர் சொரிகின்ற","வலையங் கையல வருவது மற்றிதன் சலத்தது வலிகண்டாய்."],"p":"","r":"The guide says the aerial cars of the host, moving to a song of play, come like a troop of hills, and bids the maiden look at their power under the sun's glare.","n":"The last two lines turn on rare words and the sense is a guess.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["padai","aakaasam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 888","lb":"SO888","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["நம் படை"],"ec":[],"spk":["(பெயர் இல்லை)","நங்கை"],"ch":"so:40"},"so:889":{"book":"soolamani","num":889,"t":["இன்ன போல்வன விளையவட் குழையவ ளினியன பலகாட்டிப்","பன்னு மாயிடைப் பழனங்கள் வளாவிய படுகலி நெடுநீத்தம்","துன்னு நீர்வயற் சுரமியத் தகணியுட் சுடரணி நகர்சார்ந்து","தென்னென் றேனிமிர் திருநிலை யகமெனுஞ் செறிபொழி லதுசேர்ந்தார்."],"p":"","r":"Having shown the young woman many such sights, the party crosses flooded fields and a lovely city and comes to a grove called the lord's seat, where they reach their stopping place.","n":"The verse closes the tour and passes from the maiden's view to the host's camp.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naadu","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 889","lb":"SO889","mm":1,"ev":"After the tour the Vidyadhara party arrives at a grove beside Podanam.","cl":[],"ag":["நம் படை"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:40"},"so:890":{"book":"soolamani","num":890,"t":["மோடு விட்டலர் மொய்ம்மலர்க் காவினுள்","பாடி விட்டது பாவைதன் கோன்படை","கூடி யிட்டிடை யாரன கோதைமேல்","மூடி விட்டமர் தேன்முரி வித்தவே."],"p":"","r":"The host of the maiden's lord settles in a thick flower-grove; the bees swarming over the women's garlands spoil the honey.","n":"Lines 1 and 4 are clipped and the sense is held loosely.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["padai","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 890","lb":"SO890","mm":1,"ev":"The Vidyadhara king's host makes camp in a flower grove.","cl":[],"ag":["நம் படை"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:40"},"so:891":{"book":"soolamani","num":891,"t":["ஆர்ந்த வெங்களி யானைக் கவுட்புடை","வார்ந்து வீழ்மத மூசிய வண்டினம்","சோர்ந்து வீங்கெருத் திற்றொடர் கண்ணிடைப்","பேர்ந்து வீழ்வன போலப் பிறழ்ந்தவே."],"p":"","r":"Bees that fed on the rut of angry elephants wheel away as if falling from the beasts' bound necks.","n":"This is a pure picture of the camp's elephants.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 891","lb":"SO891","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:40"},"so:892":{"book":"soolamani","num":892,"t":["முந்தி நின்றிமிர் தேன்முரன் றாக்கிய","மந்த வின்னிசை வாங்க வனத்திடைச்","சந்த னத்தடந் தாளொடு சார்ந்தன","கந்தெ னக்கன லுங்களி யானையே."],"p":"","r":"The rutting elephants burn against the sandal trunks they lean on, as the bees sing a slow note, like a post that blazes.","n":"The last line compares the bull to a burning post and the comparison is hard to place.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 892","lb":"SO892","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:40"},"so:893":{"book":"soolamani","num":893,"t":["குங்கு மக்குளிர் பூநெரி தூளிமேற்","பொங்கு ளைக்கலி மாக்கள் புரண்டுவிற்","றங்கொ ளிப்பல கைத்தலம் பாவிய","மங்க லப்பெரும் பந்தியின் வந்தவே."],"p":"","r":"Steeds in tumult roll over saffron dust and flowers, and the rows of the great stables come with many gleaming hands spread out.","n":"The sense turns on rare words.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 893","lb":"SO893","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:40"},"so:894":{"book":"soolamani","num":894,"t":["பட்ட மார்நெடுந் தேர்பைம் பொனான்மிடை","கொட்டில் சேர்ந்தன கோனுறை கோயிலும்","வட்ட மாக வகுத்தனர் வானுல","கிட்ட மாய்வந் திழிந்தது போலுமே."],"p":"","r":"Chariots with banners and the king's royal lodgings are laid out in a circle at the camp, as if heaven itself had come down.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["padai","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 894","lb":"SO894","mm":1,"ev":"The camp's lodgings and chariots are set out in a ring.","cl":[],"ag":["நம் படை"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:40"},"so:895":{"book":"soolamani","num":895,"t":["செம்பொன் மாளிகை யும்வயி ரத்திரட்","டம்ப முற்ற தமனியக் கூடமும்","அம்பொன் னாடரங் கும்மகிற் சேக்கையும்","வம்பு நீர்மைய வாய்வளங் கொண்டவே."],"p":"","r":"The camp has golden mansions, halls of diamond, gold dancing-halls and aloe-wood beds, all full of new richness.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 895","lb":"SO895","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:40"},"so:896":{"book":"soolamani","num":896,"t":["தெள்ளி வெண்பவ ழத்திர ளூன்றிய","வெள்ளி மண்டப மும்விரை நாறுப","பள்ளி யம்பல மும்பகற் கோயிலும்","வள்ள றன்னகர் வாய்மலி வுற்றவே."],"p":"","r":"Silver pavilions set with coral, scented sleeping-halls and day-palaces fill the generous king's town.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 896","lb":"SO896","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:40"},"so:897":{"book":"soolamani","num":897,"t":["வௌவு நீரென்ன வாவியு மாடெலாம்","தெய்வ நாறுவ தேங்கொள்செய் குன்றமு","மௌவன் மண்டப மும்மணற் றாழ்வரும்","எவ்வ பாலு மிசைந்துள வென்பவே."],"p":"","r":"Tanks like drawn water, gardens that smell divine, small hills and jasmine bowers with sand slopes are all found in the camp.","n":"The closing word என்பவே is the poet's formula for hearsay and sets the whole camp as report.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 897","lb":"SO897","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:40"},"so:898":{"book":"soolamani","num":898,"t":["கொற்ற வன்கொடிக் கோயிற் புறம்பணை","சுற்றி விட்டது சுற்றும் பெரும்படை","மற்றை மன்னரெல் லாம்வனத் தின்புடை","முற்ற முன்னினர் முத்தணி மாலையார்."],"p":"","r":"The host surrounds the king's banner-hall and the fields beyond it, and the other kings, wearing pearl garlands, make for the outskirts of the forest.","n":"The verse leaves unclear which king's flag-hall it means and where the forest lies.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["padai","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 898","lb":"SO898","mm":1,"ev":"The host rings the royal camp and the other kings pitch beside the forest.","cl":[],"ag":["மற்றை மன்னர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:40"},"so:899":{"book":"soolamani","num":899,"t":["கன்னி மூதெயில் சூழ்கடி காவினுட்","கன்னி தாதைகண் ணார்நக ரிஞ்சியுட்","கன்னி மார்பலர் காக்குங் கடையதோர்","கன்னி மாநகர் கன்னிக் கியற்றினார்."],"p":"","r":"In the guarded grove round the old fort the builders raise a great maiden-city for the girl, with many maidens keeping its gate.","n":"Every line opens with the same word கன்னி, in a yamaka that plays on 'maiden' and 'city of the maiden'; the exact sense of each use is held loosely.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["arasu","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 899","lb":"SO899","mm":1,"ev":"Builders raise a great city for the Vidyadhara maiden in the guarded grove.","cl":[],"ag":["இயற்றினார்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:41"},"so:900":{"book":"soolamani","num":900,"t":["மின்னி னார்ந்த விமானத் தலத்திடைப்","பொன்ன னார்பலர் போற்ற விழிந்துதன்","மன்ன னாரரு ளான்மணி மாளிகைக்","கன்னி மாநக ரெய்தினள் கன்னியே."],"p":"","r":"The maiden steps down from her lightning-bright aerial car while golden women praise her and, by her father's grace, enters the jewelled mansion of the maiden-city.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aakaasam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 900","lb":"SO900","mm":1,"ev":"The Vidyadhara maiden descends from the aerial car and enters the maiden-city.","cl":[],"ag":["நங்கை"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:41"},"so:901":{"book":"soolamani","num":901,"t":["மற்றவ ரிருத்தலு மருசி சென்றுபின்","சுற்றுநீர் வளவயற் சுரமை நாடுடைக்","கொற்றவன் கழலடி தொழுது கூறலும்","வெற்றிவே லவனொளி துளும்ப வீங்கினான்."],"p":"","r":"Once the party is settled, Maruci goes to the king of the river-watered Suramai land, bows at his feet and tells him, and the victorious king swells with gladness.","n":"The king of Suramai is taken to be Pirasapathi of Podanam; the verse does not name him.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["arasu","thoothu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 901","lb":"SO901","mm":1,"ev":"Maruci reports the Vidyadhara party's arrival to the king, who is delighted.","cl":[],"ag":["மருசி","பிரசாபதி"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:41"},"so:902":{"book":"soolamani","num":902,"t":["ஏரணி மணிக்கல மணிக யாருமென்","றாரணி வளநக ரறைக கோடணை","தோரணந் திசைதொறுஞ் சுடர நாட்டுக","பூரண பொற்குடம் பொலிய வைக்கவே."],"p":"","r":"The king orders the jewelled ornaments worn, the drums sounded through the city, arches raised in every direction and full golden pots set out.","n":"The speaker is not named in the verse and is taken from the king's joy in 901.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["arasu","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 902","lb":"SO902","mm":1,"ev":"The king orders the city dressed and drummed for the wedding.","cl":[],"ag":["பிரசாபதி"],"ec":[],"spk":["பிரசாபதி",""],"ch":"so:41"},"so:903":{"book":"soolamani","num":903,"t":["இரவல ரிருநெதி கவர்க வீண்டயற்","புரவலர் வருகெனப் போக தூதுவர்","திருவலர் சினகரன் செல்வப் பொன்னகர்","விரவலர் மழையொடு விழவு செய்கவே."],"p":"","r":"The king orders that the needy seize two treasures, that neighbouring kings be summoned by envoys, and that the temples of the Jina's wealthy city hold a festival.","n":"The wedding is made a public gift-day for beggars and a political summons to neighbouring kings together; the closing words about rain are obscure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["eegai","arasu","thoothu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 903","lb":"SO903","mm":1,"ev":"The king orders wealth given out, kings summoned and a festival held.","cl":[],"ag":["பிரசாபதி"],"ec":[],"spk":["பிரசாபதி",""],"ch":"so:41"},"so:904":{"book":"soolamani","num":904,"t":["எரிமணிச் சுடரணி யிலங்கு நங்கைதன்","திருமணிக் காவினுட் செல்லுஞ் செய்கையாற்","புரிமணிப் பொலங்குழைப் பொம்மல் வெம்முலைக்","குருமணிக் கொம்பனார் கோலஞ் செய்கவே."],"p":"","r":"The king orders the women to adorn the bright-jewelled girl for her going into the jewelled grove, with bright ear-rings, ornaments and finery.","n":"Whether the one to be adorned is the Vidyadhara maiden or the bride-to-be of the line is held loosely; the verse says only 'the woman of the gem-grove'.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 904","lb":"SO904","mm":1,"ev":"The king orders the maiden dressed for her entry into the grove.","cl":[],"ag":["பிரசாபதி"],"ec":[],"spk":["பிரசாபதி",""],"ch":"so:41"},"so:905":{"book":"soolamani","num":905,"t":["உழைக்கல மகளிரொ டுவந்து செல்வன","புழைக்கைய விளம்பிடி புகுந்து பண்ணுக","தழற்புகை நவின்றகைத் தானை வீரர்தம்","அழற்படை யொடுபுகுந் தமைக காவலே."],"p":"","r":"The king orders the elephants with their women to go in, the young cow-elephants to be made ready, and the fire-armed troops to enter and keep guard.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["padai","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 905","lb":"SO905","mm":1,"ev":"The king orders elephants, women and guard troops to take their posts.","cl":[],"ag":["பிரசாபதி"],"ec":[],"spk":["பிரசாபதி",""],"ch":"so:41"},"so:906":{"book":"soolamani","num":906,"t":["இன்னன வுழையவர்க் கருளி யேந்துதோண்","மன்னவன் மருசியை மருளக் கட்டுரைத்","தென்னொடும் பெயர்திநும் பாடிக் கென்றொரு","பொன்னகர் மாளிகை புகுகென் றேயினான்."],"p":"","r":"Having ordered the attendants so, the king speaks to Maruci in words meant to bewilder him and bids him enter a golden-city mansion.","n":"The sense of the king's words to Maruci turns on one rare word and a doubtful construction.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["arasu","thoothu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 906","lb":"SO906","mm":1,"ev":"The king speaks to Maruci and sends him to lodge in a golden mansion.","cl":[],"ag":["பிரசாபதி","மருசி"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:41"},"so:907":{"book":"soolamani","num":907,"t":["வேண்டுப வவன்றிறத் தருளி வேந்தர்கோன்","ஈண்டிய மந்திரக் கிழவர்க் கென்னையாங்","காண்டகு திறலவற் காணு மாறென","ஆண்டகைக் கவர்களு மறியச் செப்பினார்."],"p":"","r":"The king, gracious to the envoy's side, asks his gathered counsellors how the capable Vidyadhara lord is to be met, and they answer in a way the king understands.","n":"","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["arasu","amaichu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 907","lb":"SO907","mm":1,"ev":"The king asks his counsellors how to receive the Vidyadhara lord and they answer.","cl":[],"ag":["பிரசாபதி","மந்திரக் கிழவர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:41"},"so:908":{"book":"soolamani","num":908,"t":["விண்ணியல் விஞ்சையர்க் கிறைவன் வேந்தராற்","கண்ணிய பெருங்குலக் கடலுட் டோன்றினான்","நண்ணிய தொடர்ச்சியு நமிக்கணண்ணுமால்","எண்ணுவ வவன்றிறத் திறைவ வில்லையே."],"p":"","r":"The counsellors tell the king that the Vidyadhara lord was born in a great sea of noble kin, that his link to Nami's line also draws near, and that there is no fault in him to count.","n":"'Nami' in line 3 is taken to be the ancestor of the Vidyadhara line whose song is told in an earlier sarukkam; the verse's grammar is clipped.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kudi","amaichu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 908","lb":"SO908","mm":1,"ev":"The counsellors vouch for the Vidyadhara lord's lineage.","cl":[],"ag":["மந்திரக் கிழவர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["மந்திரக் கிழவர்","பிரசாபதி"],"ch":"so:41"},"so:909":{"book":"soolamani","num":909,"t":["குலத்தினுங் குணத்தினுங் கொண்ட கோலமா","நலத்தினு நின்னொடு நிகர்க்கு நன்மையன்","மலைத்தலில் வயத்தினும் பெரியன் மல்லினும்","உலத்தினும் பெருகிய வுருவத் தோளினான்."],"p":"","r":"The counsellors add that he matches the king in clan, character and beauty, is strong beyond challenge, and has the build of a wrestler with pillar-like shoulders.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kudi","perumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 909","lb":"SO909","mm":1,"ev":"The counsellors praise the Vidyadhara lord's equality with the king.","cl":[],"ag":["மந்திரக் கிழவர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["மந்திரக் கிழவர்","பிரசாபதி"],"ch":"so:41"},"so:910":{"book":"soolamani","num":910,"t":["ஆதலா லவன்றிறத் தியாது செய்யினும்","ஏதமாங் கில்லைகோ லிறைவ வென்றனர்","கோதிலாக் குணம்புரி குன்ற னாற்கொரு","நீதிநூற் கடலினின் றனைய நீர்மையார்."],"p":"","r":"Therefore, say the counsellors, whatever the king does for him brings no blame; they speak as men who are a sea of the treatise on conduct.","n":"The counsellors are called a sea of the treatise on conduct (நீதிநூல்) in the narrator's voice; the verse does not say whom the treatise belongs to.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","amaichu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 910","lb":"SO910","mm":1,"ev":"The counsellors advise that no fault attaches to any honour done to the Vidyadhara lord.","cl":[["a ruler may spend freely on an ally who equals him in birth and virtue, and no blame attaches","general","reported",1,"amaichar"]],"ag":["மந்திரக் கிழவர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["மந்திரக் கிழவர்","பிரசாபதி"],"ch":"so:41"},"so:911":{"book":"soolamani","num":911,"t":["ஆங்கவர் மொழியக் கேட்டே யறிவினுக் கரச ரென்று","வாங்கிருங் கடலந் தானை மன்னவன் மகிழ்ந்து மற்றப்","பூங்குழை மகளிர் காக்கும் பொன்னணி வாயில் போகித்","தேங்கம ழலங்கன் மார்பன் றிருநகர் முற்றஞ் சேர்ந்தான்."],"p":"","r":"Hearing them, the king rejoices that his counsellors are men of wisdom and goes through the gold gate kept by flower-eared women to the courtyard of the garlanded prince's fine palace.","n":"The garlanded prince is taken to be Tivittan, the groom; the verse names him only by his garland.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["arasu","amaichu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 911","lb":"SO911","mm":1,"ev":"The king goes to the prince's palace courtyard.","cl":[],"ag":["பிரசாபதி"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:41"},"so:912":{"book":"soolamani","num":912,"t":["அடுகடா மாவி நாறு மழிமதங் கருவி வீழத்","தொடுகடா வயிரத் தோட்டி யுடையன தொடர்க ணூன்ற","விடுகொடா வியாள நிற்ப மெல்லவன் பணிகள் செய்யும்","படுகடாக் களிறுந் தேரும் புரவியும் பண்ணு கென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"He orders ready the war-elephants, whose rut falls like rain, with diamond goads and chains planted firm, and the chariots and horses.","n":"The speaker is taken to be the prince, but the king is also a possible subject of 'என்றான்'.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["padai","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 912","lb":"SO912","mm":1,"ev":"The prince orders elephants, chariots and horses made ready.","cl":[],"ag":["செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ec":[],"spk":["செங்கண் ணெடியான்",""],"ch":"so:41"},"so:913":{"book":"soolamani","num":913,"t":["பணித்தசொல் லதனைக் கேட்டே பகைநிலைக் கந்தி னோடும்","பிணித்தபொற் றொடர்கண் விட்டுப் பெயர்ந்தகா னிகள நீக்கி","மணித்தொடர் மருங்கின் வீழ்த்து வரிபுரிக் கச்சை வீக்கி","அணித்தகைப் பாகர் பண்ணிக் கொடியெடுத் தருகு சேர்ந்தார்"],"p":"","r":"Hearing the order, the drivers loose the elephants from the post-chains, take off the foot-fetters, tie girth cloths and ready them, and stand by with banners raised.","n":"The printed stanza ran to eight half-lines in the source; the editor's join does not change the sense here.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 913","lb":"SO913","mm":1,"ev":"The drivers unchain and equip the war-elephants for the procession.","cl":[],"ag":["பாகர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:41"},"so:914":{"book":"soolamani","num":914,"t":["செம்பொன்செய் கிடுகு கோத்துத் திகிரிவாய்ப் புளகஞ் சேர்த்திப்","பைம்பொன்செய் பரவைத் தட்டிற் பருமணி பதித்த திண்டேர்","கம்பஞ்செய் துலக மெல்லாங் கைவளைக் கொள்வ போல","அம்பொன்செய் கொடுஞ்சி நெற்றி கொடியெடுத் தணைந்த வன்றே."],"p":"","r":"The firm chariots, with golden shields, gem-set floors and golden finials on their fronts, come up with banners flying, as if the shaking earth were taking them for a bangle.","n":"The poet's conceit is that the earth shakes under the chariots as if wearing them as a wrist-bangle.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["padai","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 914","lb":"SO914","mm":1,"ev":"The decked chariots arrive with their banners.","cl":[],"ag":["தேர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:41"},"so:915":{"book":"soolamani","num":915,"t":["முற்றத்தா னெரியுஞ் செம்பொன் முகனனி கருவி சேர்த்திச்","சுற்றத்தா தணிந்து காமர் சூழ்மணிக் கோவை சூழ்ந்து","மற்றுத்தாம் வகுக்கற் பால மங்கல மரபிற் பண்ணிப்","பொற்றத்தார்க் கவரி வேய்ந்து பொருகின்ற புரவி யெல்லாம்."],"p":"","r":"The fighting horses are decked with golden face-plates, strings of gems, auspicious trappings and golden-tasselled whisks.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["padai","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 915","lb":"SO915","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:41"},"so:916":{"book":"soolamani","num":916,"t":["ஒட்டிய வூழி னன்றி யுயிர்கொள லொழிக வென்று","சுட்டினர் மொழிப வாயிற் கூற்றமுந் துளங்கு நீரார்","கட்டிய கழலர் தாழ்ந்த கச்சையர் கனலும் வாளர்","மட்டுய ரலங்கல் சூடி வயவரும் வந்து சூழ்ந்தார்."],"p":"","r":"Soldiers with fastened anklets, girded sashes and blazing swords gather round, men whose declaration that life should be taken only by the fate fastened to it makes even Death tremble.","n":"The warriors' words set fate (ஊழ்) as the only legitimate killer, which makes their swords restrained; the verse's grammar is clipped and the exact subject of 'tremble' is held loosely.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["oozh","padai","kootru"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 916","lb":"SO916","mm":1,"ev":"The soldiers gather round, declaring that only bound fate may take a life.","cl":[["a life may be taken only by the fate that is bound to it, and by nothing else","general","reported",1,"warriors"]],"ag":["வயவர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:41"},"so:917":{"book":"soolamani","num":917,"t":["அன்னணந் தானை பண்ணி யணைந்தபின் னமைச்சரோடு","மன்னவன் மருங்கி னின்ற மருசியை வருக வென்று","பொன்னுதல் வேழ மொன்று பொலங்கலம் புலம்ப வேற்றி","முன்னுற நின்று காதன் முறுவலோ டருளிச் செய்தான்."],"p":"","r":"When the host is ready, the prince calls Maruci, who stands beside the king and counsellors, to come, seats him on a golden-browed elephant and, standing before him with a loving smile, speaks.","n":"The subject of the verb is taken to be the prince, who gives the next orders.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["arasu","thoothu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 917","lb":"SO917","mm":1,"ev":"The prince calls Maruci, seats him on an elephant and speaks to him.","cl":[],"ag":["செங்கண் ணெடியான்","மருசி"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:41"},"so:918":{"book":"soolamani","num":918,"t":["தேங்கம ழலங்கன் மார்பன் சித்திர தரனைக் கூவிப்","பாங்கமை பஞ்சு பட்டுந் துகில்களும் பரப்பி மேலால்","வீங்கிய சுடர வாய மிடைமணிக் கலன்கள் விஞ்சை","நீங்கருந் திறலி னான்றன் னெடுநகர் நிறைக்க வென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"The garland-chested prince calls Chitradharan and bids him spread cotton, silk and cloths and fill the tall city of the Vidyadhara of unfailing might with gem ornaments.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","eegai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 918","lb":"SO918","mm":1,"ev":"The prince orders Chitradharan to send cloth and jewels to the Vidyadhara city.","cl":[],"ag":["செங்கண் ணெடியான்","சித்திர தரன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["செங்கண் ணெடியான்","சித்திர தரன்"],"ch":"so:41"},"so:919":{"book":"soolamani","num":919,"t":["பொன்னணி கலத்தின் குப்பை புரிமணிக் கோவைப் போர்வை","மன்னிய வயிரக் குன்றம் வலம்புரி மணியின் கோவை","பின்னிய பவழ வல்லிப் பிறங்கலோ டனைய வெல்லாம்","கொன்னவில் வேலி னான்றன் கோயின்முன் குவிக்க வென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"The prince bids the heaps of gold ornaments, gem-strings, diamond mountains, right-turned conches and coral be piled before the Vidyadhara king's palace.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","eegai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 919","lb":"SO919","mm":1,"ev":"The prince orders gold, gems and coral heaped before the Vidyadhara king's palace.","cl":[],"ag":["செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ec":[],"spk":["செங்கண் ணெடியான்","சித்திர தரன்"],"ch":"so:41"},"so:920":{"book":"soolamani","num":920,"t":["ஆண்டுற வணைந்து வேகித் தழல்கின்ற மதுவின் றண்டோ","டீண்டிநின் றினவண் டார்க்கு மின்சுவை நறவின் சாதி","வேண்டுநர் வேண்டு மாறு விருந்தயர்ந் துயரும் வண்ணம்","தீண்டரும் விஞ்சை வேந்தன் றிருநகர்ச் செறிக்க வென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"The prince bids the city be stocked with hot honeyed liquor and sweet toddy of every sort so that the guests may be feasted as each desires.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["virundhu","eegai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 920","lb":"SO920","mm":1,"ev":"The prince orders drink stocked to feast the Vidyadhara guests.","cl":[],"ag":["செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ec":[],"spk":["செங்கண் ணெடியான்","சித்திர தரன்"],"ch":"so:41"},"so:921":{"book":"soolamani","num":921,"t":["தேங்கனி குழவித் தீநீர் செம்பழத் திரளின் கண்ணி","பாங்கமை பளிதச் சாதி பாசிலைத் தழையின் கற்றை","தீங்கழைக் கரும்பின் கட்டி திரணறைக் கடிகை யின்ன","தோங்கலந் திலாத சொல்லான் றொன்னகர்ச் சொரிக வென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"The prince bids coconuts, tender fruit, ripe-fruit clusters, sugar, leaf bundles and cane sweets be poured into the old city.","n":"Several of the foods named are rare words and held loosely.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["virundhu","eegai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 921","lb":"SO921","mm":1,"ev":"The prince orders fruit and sweets poured into the old city.","cl":[],"ag":["செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ec":[],"spk":["செங்கண் ணெடியான்","சித்திர தரன்"],"ch":"so:41"},"so:922":{"book":"soolamani","num":922,"t":["கண்ணியுங் கமழுஞ் சூட்டுங் கற்றையாக் கட்டப் பட்ட","தண்ணிய மலருந் தாம மாலையுஞ் சதங்கை தம்மோ","டெண்ணிய வண்ண மாலை யெழினக ரெல்லை யெல்லாம்","விண்ணியல் விமான வீதி வெறிகொள மிடைவி வேலோய்."],"p":"","r":"The prince bids wreaths, scents, flowers, garlands and bells be tied up in bunches and strewn thick through the city's bounds and along its aerial car-streets.","n":"'வேலோய்' is a vocative to the one ordered; the verse's last half is held loosely.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","virundhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 922","lb":"SO922","mm":1,"ev":"The prince orders flowers and garlands strewn through the city's bounds.","cl":[],"ag":["செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ec":[],"spk":["செங்கண் ணெடியான்","சித்திர தரன்"],"ch":"so:41"},"so:923":{"book":"soolamani","num":923,"t":["குங்குமக் குழம்பு கொட்டிச் சந்தனத் தொளிகண் கூட்டி","அங்கலுழ் விரையின் சேற்றோ டகநக ரளறு செய்து","மங்குலாய் விசும்பு மூட வகிற்புகை மயங்க மாட்டிப்","பொங்குபொற் சுண்ணம் வீசி மணவினை புனைவி யென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"The king orders that the inner city be turned to scented mire with saffron and sandal paste, wrapped in aloe smoke under a cloud-like canopy and strewn with gold powder, and that the wedding be got up.","n":"The poet counts splendour by waste: paste is poured until the city is mud. The speaker is not named; he is taken to be the king because 924 names the commander of the drum-sounding army who gives the next orders.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","selvam","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 923","lb":"SO923","mm":1,"ev":"The king orders the city perfumed and strewn with gold powder for the wedding.","cl":[],"ag":["பிரசாபதி"],"ec":[],"spk":["பிரசாபதி",""],"ch":"so:42"},"so:924":{"book":"soolamani","num":924,"t":["அனையன வவனை யேவி யரசிளங் குமரர் தம்மைப்","புனைமலர்க் கண்ணி சூடிப் பொன்னெழி லாரந் தாங்கி","நனைகவுள் வேழ மேறி நம்மொடு வருக வென்றான்","கனைகுரன் முரச மார்க்கும் கடிபடைக் கால வேலான்."],"p":"","r":"Having sent the officer off with these orders, the king tells the young princes to put on garlands and gold necklaces, mount rutting elephants and ride with him.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 924","lb":"SO924","mm":1,"ev":"The king commands the princes to adorn themselves and ride out with him on elephants.","cl":[],"ag":["பிரசாபதி"],"ec":[],"spk":["பிரசாபதி","குமரர்"],"ch":"so:42"},"so:925":{"book":"soolamani","num":925,"t":["திங்களை யிரண்டு கூறாச் செய்துமுன் செறித்த போல","மங்கல வடிவின் வந்த வலனுயர் வயிரக் கோட்டுச்","செங்களி விதிர்த்த போலுஞ் செம்பொறிச் சிறுகண் வேழம்","வெங்களி வியாளம் வல்ல விறலது வருக வென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"The king calls for a particular tusker to be brought, its white tusks like a moon cut in two and set in place, its small red-spotted eyes and fierce rut able to master even the vyala.","n":"The speaker is carried over from 924 and is not named here.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["padai","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 925","lb":"SO925","mm":1,"ev":"The king calls for the chosen elephant to be brought.","cl":[],"ag":["பிரசாபதி"],"ec":[],"spk":["பிரசாபதி",""],"ch":"so:42"},"so:926":{"book":"soolamani","num":926,"t":["அருளறிந் துழைய ரோடி யரசுவா வருக வென்ன","மருளிவண் டுழலுந் தாரை மழகளி றதனை மேய்ப்பான்","இருளிருங் குன்ற மேய்க்கு மிரும்பிண ரெருத்த மேறி","வெருவரு மொழியிற் றேறி மேன்முறைத் தொழில னானான்."],"p":"","r":"At a word that shows the king's mind, attendants run calling the elephant Arasuva, and its keeper climbs the neck of the maddened young tusker, steadies it with a fearsome voice and takes up his work from above.","n":"அரசுவா is taken here as the elephant's name; the sense of the word is not fixed and the verse is read loosely.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["padai","arivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 926","lb":"SO926","mm":1,"ev":"The keeper mounts the maddened elephant and calms it with his voice while attendants call for it.","cl":[],"ag":["மைந்தர்","பாகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:42"},"so:927":{"book":"soolamani","num":927,"t":["அரசுவா வதனோ டாடி யியலறிந் தணைந்த பாகன்","புரைசைதா னெகிழ்த்து மற்றோர் புதுவடம் புரள வீக்கி","உரைசெய்காற் சுவடு நுங்கச் செறித்தொன்று புறத்த தாக்கி","நிரைசெய்கா னிகளம் விட்டு நிலத்தவ ரேறு கென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"The keeper who has played with Arasuva and knows its ways loosens its girth, ties on a new rope, fastens a second, leads it out of the stall and tells the men of the earth to mount.","n":"The keeper gives the elephant to the riders with the words 'let those of the earth mount'; the tamer's craft is spent before the king's ride begins.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["padai","arivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 927","lb":"SO927","mm":1,"ev":"The keeper refits the elephant's ropes, brings it from the stall and invites the riders to mount.","cl":[],"ag":["பாகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:42"},"so:928":{"book":"soolamani","num":928,"t":["பின்னவ னேறித் தூசப் பெருவடம் பிடித்த பின்னைப்","பொன்னவிர் தொடர்கண் விட்டுப் புறத்துக்காற் புரோசை கோத்து","மன்னவ னருளு மாறு மங்கலக் கோலஞ் செய்வான்","துன்னருங் கவைமுட் கோலோர் சூழ்ந்துவந் தணைக வென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"Another rides up and takes the great cloth rope, the golden chains are loosened and the rear girths tied, and the man who will dress the beast to the king's wish bids the forked-goad bearers gather round.","n":"Who mounts and who is called 'the later one' is not named; the dresser may be the keeper of 926 or another.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["padai","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 928","lb":"SO928","mm":1,"ev":"The dresser readies the elephant's rope and girths and calls the goad bearers around it.","cl":[],"ag":["கோலஞ் செய்வான்"],"ec":[],"spk":["கோலஞ் செய்வான்",""],"ch":"so:42"},"so:929":{"book":"soolamani","num":929,"t":["கரும்பொடு முடித்த காய்நெற் கதிரணிக் கவளக் கற்றை","இரும்புடை வயிரக் கோட்டி னிடையன பயிரி நீக்கிச்","சுரும்பொடு மலர்கள் வாய்ந்த துகளையு மகல வாரி","அரும்புடை யலங்கன் மார்ப னரத்தநீ ரெறிவித் தானே."],"p":"","r":"The garlanded man has the grass of cane and paddy ears that tangled between the tusks cleared, the dust and fallen blossoms swept off and red water sprinkled.","n":"The garlanded one is taken to be the dresser, since the next verses continue his work; he could also be the king.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 929","lb":"SO929","mm":1,"ev":"The tusks are cleared of fodder and the ground sprinkled with red water.","cl":[],"ag":["கோலஞ் செய்வான்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:42"},"so:930":{"book":"soolamani","num":930,"t":["குங்குமக் குழம்பு கொட்டிச் சந்தன வெள்ளை கொண்டு","மங்கல வயிரக் கோட்டு வலங்கொள வரைந்து மற்றுச்","சங்கின துருவி னாலும் வலம்புரிச் சவியி னாலும்","அங்கதன் பாகத் தீரத் தருகெலா மணிவித் திட்டான்."],"p":"","r":"With saffron paste, sandal and conch-pattern work the dresser marks the diamond-bright tusks and adorns the beast's sides and all that lies near them.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 930","lb":"SO930","mm":1,"ev":"The dresser paints the tusks and adorns the elephant's sides.","cl":[],"ag":["கோலஞ் செய்வான்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:42"},"so:931":{"book":"soolamani","num":931,"t":["பொற்றிரட் கடிகை பூட்டிப் புரிமணி யோடை சேர்த்தி","முற்றிய புளகச் சூழி முகம்புதைத் திலங்க வீழ்த்துச்","சுற்றிநின் றெரியுஞ் செம்பொற் சுடர்நிலைப் பட்டஞ் சேர்த்திக்","கற்றையங் கவரிக் கண்ணி கருணமூ லத்து வைத்தான்."],"p":"","r":"The elephant is fitted with a gold-clasp, a gem-studded forehead-band, an embossed head-cover over its face, a blazing gold plate on the brow and a fly-whisk garland at the root of its ear.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 931","lb":"SO931","mm":1,"ev":"The dresser fits the elephant's head with gold, gems and a whisk garland.","cl":[],"ag":["கோலஞ் செய்வான்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:42"},"so:932":{"book":"soolamani","num":932,"t":["தாரணி தயங்கச் சாத்தித் தவிசின்மேல் விரித்து மஞ்சில்","ஏரணி திருவில் லேய்ப்ப விருவடம் விலங்க வீக்கிச்","சீரணி மணிகள் வீழ்த்துச் செம்பொன்செய் சுண்ணஞ் சிந்திக்","காரணி மின்னிற் றோன்றக் கதலிகை நடுவித் தானால்."],"p":"","r":"Garlands are hung and a cushion spread; two ropes are tied slantwise like rainbow bands over cloud, gem-strings hang, gold powder is scattered and flags are set up that flash like lightning in cloud.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 932","lb":"SO932","mm":1,"ev":"The dresser finishes the elephant with cushion, ropes, gems, powder and flags.","cl":[],"ag":["கோலஞ் செய்வான்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:42"},"so:933":{"book":"soolamani","num":933,"t":["வேழமாங் கணிந்த பின்னை வேந்தர்போற் றிசைப்ப வேறிச்","சூழொளி யார மின்னச் சுடர்க்குழை திருவில் வீச","ஏழையர் கவரி யேந்த வெரிகதிர் விரிவ தொத்தான்","ஊழிநீ ருலகங் காக்கு முழவுத்தோ ளுருவத் தாரான்."],"p":"","r":"When the elephant is dressed, the one who guards the earth to the ocean's rim mounts to the praise of kings, his gem necklaces gleaming, earrings flashing like rainbows and women holding fly-whisks, and he shines like a burning sun.","n":"The rider is taken to be the king, since 934 gives the king's white umbrella; a bridegroom is not excluded by the Tamil.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["arasu","vendhan","perumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 933","lb":"SO933","mm":1,"ev":"The king mounts the dressed elephant amid praise and fly-whisks.","cl":[],"ag":["பிரசாபதி","கன்னியர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:42"},"so:934":{"book":"soolamani","num":934,"t":["ஒத்துநின் றுலக மெல்லா மொருங்குடன் குளிர வோம்பி","வித்தகர் புகழு மேரார் வெள்ளிவெண் குடையொன் றோங்கி","முத்தவெண் மாலை நான்று முடிமிசை நிழற்ற மூரி","மத்தமால் களிறு நுந்தி வளநகர் மருளச் சென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"Under one raised silver umbrella with pearl strings over his crown, the king, who tends all the world evenly so that it cools together, drives his great musth elephant on and passes through the rich city to its wonder.","n":"The cooling umbrella is a sign of the king's protection, but here it is displayed as a part of the ride, not tested as a duty.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","vendhan","perumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 934","lb":"SO934","mm":1,"ev":"The king rides his elephant through the city under the white umbrella.","cl":[["a king guards the whole world evenly so that all of it is cooled","general","asserted",1,"narrator"]],"ag":["பிரசாபதி"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:42"},"so:935":{"book":"soolamani","num":935,"t":["அரசிறை யரசரொ டெழுதலு மதிர்தரு","முரசெறி யிமிழிசை முழவொடு கழுமின","திரைசெறி நெடுவரை கடைதொறு திசைதிசை","கரைசெறி கடலொலி கடுகிய தனெவே."],"p":"","r":"As the king rises with the other kings, the drumbeat blends with the mulavu drum, and at every gate in every direction the sound swells like the sea breaking on its shore.","n":"The sound is the main figure here, set against the sea; the metre is short and the grammar clipped.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["oli","arasu","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 935","lb":"SO935","mm":1,"ev":"The king and the kings rise to the sound of drums that fill every gate.","cl":[],"ag":["பிரசாபதி","மன்னர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:42"},"so:936":{"book":"soolamani","num":936,"t":["துளைபடு குழலிசை துடியொடு சிறுபறை","கிளையொடு படலிகை கிளையொடு கிளர்தர","வளையொடு வயிரிசை மருவின மழையென","வளைபடு மணியர வறிவயர் வுறவே."],"p":"","r":"Pipes, drums, small drums and other instruments with their kin, bangles and horns rise together like thunder-clouds so that those who hear lose their senses.","n":"Several instrument names and the last word are rare and the sense is held loosely.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["oli","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 936","lb":"SO936","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:42"},"so:937":{"book":"soolamani","num":937,"t":["சொரிவன மலர்மழை சுழல்வன வெழுபுகை","இரிவன மதுகர மிருள்வன திசைமுகம்","எரிவன சுடர்மணி யெழுவன கதலிகை","தெரிவன வரியன தெருவொடு திசையே."],"p":"","r":"Flower-rain falls, smoke whirls up, bees scatter, the directions grow dark, gem-lamps blaze, flags rise and the streets and quarters show in variegated stripes.","n":"The last line turns on rare words and is read loosely.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","oli"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 937","lb":"SO937","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:42"},"so:938":{"book":"soolamani","num":938,"t":["கொடியொடு குடையிடை மிடைவன விருள்செய","முடியொடு சுடர்குழை முளைவெயி லொளிசெய","அடியொடு புனைகழ லரசிறை படையெழ","இடையிடை யிரவொடு பகலிசை வனவே."],"p":"","r":"Flags and umbrellas crowd in and darken the air, crowns and gleaming earrings flash like a rising sun, and as the king's army rises with its anklets night and day seem to mingle.","n":"Dark umbrellas and bright crowns together make the procession both night and day.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["padai","oli","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 938","lb":"SO938","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:42"},"so:939":{"book":"soolamani","num":939,"t":["புரவிய குரமுக மிடுதொறு பொடியெழு","மருவிகொண் மதமழை பொழிதொறு மளறெழு","மருவிய மனிதரு மனநனி யயர்வுறு","தெருவுகள் படுவது சிலரிடை தெரிவார்."],"p":"","r":"The horses' hooves raise dust and the musth of the elephants pours down in rain and turns to mud, so that people, stunned, can scarcely see the streets.","n":"The pairing of dust and musth-mud is the verse's whole point; the last line is read loosely.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["padai","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 939","lb":"SO939","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:42"},"so:940":{"book":"soolamani","num":940,"t":["செருவியல் களிறுகள் செவிபுடை யரவமும்","உருவிய லிவுளிக ளொலிகலி யரவமும்","கருவிகொள் வயவர்கள் கழனர லரவமும்","விரவிய செவிபிற விளிகொள லிலவே."],"p":"","r":"The flapping ears of the war elephants, the neighing of the horses and the anklets of the armed warriors blend so that no other sound can be heard.","n":"Three noises are named and then none are heard: the poet shows a crowded street by deafness.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oli","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 940","lb":"SO940","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:42"},"so:941":{"book":"soolamani","num":941,"t":["வளையவர் மனநிறை யழிதரு வடிவுடை","இளையவ ரிருபுற வுரைகளி னிடையிடை","திளையொடு நகைநனி சிலபல கனிவன","விளைவுடை யவர்களும் விழைவுறு தகவே."],"p":"","r":"Women of the bangles, their composure broken by the beauty of the young men, call from both sides of the way, laughing and ripening into desire, and even the mature feel it.","n":"நிறை (composure) is undone here by sight alone; the last line widens the effect to the ripe and wise, but its grammar is hard and the sense is held loosely.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","nirai","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 941","lb":"SO941","mm":1,"ev":"Women along the route lose their composure at the young men and call out, laughing, with desire.","cl":[],"ag":["மாதர்","இளையவர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:42"},"so:942":{"book":"soolamani","num":942,"t":["குயிலுவ ரொலியொடு குடமுழ வதிர்வொடு","மயிலின மகளிர்த மவிநய மடநடை","அயிலிய லரசர்த மருகவை பெருகலின்","இயலிய வளநக ரிடமிட மிலவே."],"p":"","r":"With the sound of musicians and the thunder of the drums, the peacock-like gait and gestures of the dancing women and the crowds of kings' kin, the rich city has no room left.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oli","azhagu","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 942","lb":"SO942","mm":1,"ev":"Musicians and dancers fill the city with the kings' kin.","cl":[],"ag":["குயிலுவர்","மகளிர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:42"},"so:943":{"book":"soolamani","num":943,"t":["சுரமைய ரதிபதி வருமென மருசிசென்","றருமைகொள் புகழற் கறைதலி னெதிர்கொள","வரமிகு நெடுவரை மணிமுடி நெடியவன்","உரமிகு படையெழு வுரைநனி யரிதே."],"p":"","r":"Maruci goes and tells the Vidyadhara king of rare fame that the lord of Suramai is coming, and the telling of the strong army that rises to receive him is hard to give.","n":"The addressee and the 'tall one of the gem crown' are read as the Vidyadhara king; the verse has no direct naming of him.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["padai","arasu","mannar"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 943","lb":"SO943","mm":1,"ev":"Maruci announces the coming of the lord of Suramai and the Vidyadhara army rises to receive him.","cl":[],"ag":["மருசி","நம் படை"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:42"},"so:944":{"book":"soolamani","num":944,"t":["கருவரை வருவன வெனவுள களிறுகள்","பெருவரை யருவிக ளெனவுள பெயர்கொடி","அருவரை யடுபுலி யெனவுள ரிளையவர்","பருவரை யிறையுடை யெழுவதோர் படையே."],"p":"","r":"The elephants of the mountain lord's host are like black hills, its long banners like mountain streams and its young warriors like tigers of the high hills, an army set rising.","n":"The host is taken to be the Vidyadhara's from 943; the verse says only 'the army of the lord of the great mountain'.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["padai","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 944","lb":"SO944","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:42"},"so:945":{"book":"soolamani","num":945,"t":["நிலமிசை யவர்படை நிலநெளி வுறவரும்","மலைமிசை யவர்படை மலைநெரி வுறவரும்","அலைதிரை யொலிகட லவைபுடை பெயர்தரு","நிலைபெரி தரிதிப நெடுவரை நிரைய."],"p":"","r":"Their army on earth comes so that the ground sinks and their army on the mountain so that the mountain is crushed, while the sea's waves draw aside, and with tall hill-like elephants in rows steady footing is hard to find.","n":"The last line has a broken syntax and the sense is read loosely.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["padai","perumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 945","lb":"SO945","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:42"},"so:946":{"book":"soolamani","num":946,"t":["முகிலிடை புகுவன புரவிக ளெனினவை","முகிலிடை புகுவதொர் முறைமையை யுடையன","அகிலிடு நுழைபுகை யவைகமழ் வனவெனின்","அகிலிடு நுழைபுகை யவர்புக ழதுவே."],"p":"","r":"The poet says that if horses are said to enter the clouds they have the manner of clouds, and that the aloe-smoke that enters among them is the praise of those who send it up.","n":"The play on cloud, horse and smoke is read loosely; the sense of the last line rests on rare grammar.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["padai","pugazh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 946","lb":"SO946","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:42"},"so:947":{"book":"soolamani","num":947,"t":["வனமலர் பொழிவன மழைமுகின் மழைமுகில்","இனமல ரிடையிடை விரைமழை சொரிவன","கனமலி மணிவரை யுடையவ னுழையவர்","இனமலி யமரரி னிழிவது சிறிதே."],"p":"","r":"Flowers and scented showers fall from the descending hosts like rain from clouds, and the hill-lord's people come down from the sky, though more slowly than gods descending.","n":"The comparison runs both ways: the host rains flowers like clouds, yet is said to come down a little less than the gods do.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["aakaasam","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 947","lb":"SO947","mm":1,"ev":"The Vidyadhara king's host descends from the sky amid falling flowers.","cl":[],"ag":["நம் படை"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:43"},"so:948":{"book":"soolamani","num":948,"t":["அலர்மிசை யிளையவ ரடியிட வடியிடம்","மலர்மிசை யியல்பவ ரியல்புக ளெனினல","நிலமிசை யவரொடு நிலநடை படர்கென","மலைமிசை யவரிறை யருளிய வகையே."],"p":"","r":"The lord of the hill gives an order that the young men, who step on petals, are to walk beside the earth-dwellers on the ground instead of in the air.","n":"The verse is dense and its subject is only 'he of the mountain'; the sense of walking the ground in company is held loosely.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["panivu","aakaasam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 948","lb":"SO948","mm":1,"ev":"The Vidyadhara king orders his people to come down and walk on the earth with the others.","cl":[],"ag":["வேந்தன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:43"},"so:949":{"book":"soolamani","num":949,"t":["சங்குபோ லொளியவன் றாதை தன்படை","கங்கைபோற் படர்ந்தது கலக்குங் காதலால்","இங்குநீர் யமுனையி னிழிவ தொத்தது","மங்குல்சேர் மணிவரை மன்னன் றானையே."],"p":"","r":"The host of Vijayan's father spreads like the Ganga to meet the hill-king's host, which comes down like the Yamuna.","n":"The conch-bright one is read as Vijayan, from the epithet used in verse 960; the fathers' hosts are therefore the Podanam king's.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["padai","perumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 949","lb":"SO949","mm":1,"ev":"The Podanam king's host and the Vidyadhara host meet and mingle like two rivers.","cl":[],"ag":["தாதை தன்படை","நம் படை"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:43"},"so:950":{"book":"soolamani","num":950,"t":["மாவியல் கடற்படை மயங்கி வானிடைப்","பூவிய லிணரொடு கவரி பொங்கலாற்","பாவிய பனித்திரைப் பரவை பாற்கடல்","மேவிய விசும்பிடை விரிந்த தொத்ததே."],"p":"","r":"The mingled sea of troops, with flowers and fly-whisks rising on high, looks like the milk-sea spreading across the sky.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["padai","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 950","lb":"SO950","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:43"},"so:951":{"book":"soolamani","num":951,"t":["கழுமிய முகிலொடு களிறு கான்மிடைந்","தொழுகிய வருவிநீ ருகுக்கு மாதலால்","மழைமுகின் மழகளி றென்னும் வேற்றுமை","உழையவ ருழையவர்க் குணர்த்தல் வேண்டுமே."],"p":"","r":"Because clouds and young elephants both pour streams, those near must tell them apart for those beside them.","n":"The poet jokes on the confusion of cloud and elephant that the guide's tour in verse 880 had already used.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["padai","mazhai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 951","lb":"SO951","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:43"},"so:952":{"book":"soolamani","num":952,"t":["புண்ணிய மணிநிரை பரந்து பூவுதிர்ந்","தெண்ணியல் கொடிமிடைந் திருண்டு பாங்கெலாம்","கண்ணியல் கவரிமாக் கலந்து கானக","மண்ணியல் பரவையாய் வருவ தொக்குமே."],"p":"","r":"Rows of holy gems, falling flowers and dense banners darken the sides, and the fly-whisk-bearing elephants mingled in them look like a sea coming over the forest ground.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["padai","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 952","lb":"SO952","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:43"},"so:953":{"book":"soolamani","num":953,"t":["நந்திய சுடர்மணி நாக மீமிசைப்","பைந்துகிற் கதலிகை பரந்து தோன்றுவ","நந்திய சுடர்மணி நாக மீமிசைப்","பைந்துகிற் கதலிகை பரந்த போலுமே."],"p":"","r":"Green silk banners spread over the shining gem-set elephants and seem to stand out on them.","n":"Lines 3 and 4 repeat lines 1 and 2 in other verbs; the stanza seems doubled in the source.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["padai","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 953","lb":"SO953","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:43"},"so:954":{"book":"soolamani","num":954,"t":["கணங்கெழு கவரிகள் கலந்து காழகில்","அணங்கிவர் நாவிகா லளைந்து நாறலால்","இணங்கிய கடற்படைப் பரவை யிவ்வழி","மணங்கம ழிமகிரி வருவ தொக்குமே."],"p":"","r":"The scent of aloe wood and musk mixed with the fly-whisks makes the sea-like host seem to come from a fragrant sea.","n":"The aroma is the whole conceit; 'imagiri' in line 4 is read as the fragrant hill-sea and held loosely.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["padai","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 954","lb":"SO954","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:43"},"so:955":{"book":"soolamani","num":955,"t":["காமரு பூங்குழைக் காம வல்லிகட்","டாமரி யனையவர் தயங்கி யுண்மையாற்","காமரு பூங்குழைக் காம வல்லிகட்","டாமரி யனையதத் தானை வண்ணமே."],"p":"","r":"The host's colour is like that of women with lovely earrings and bright lips, whose beauty shines in their lion-like men.","n":"The verse repeats lines 1 and 2 as lines 3 and 4; the comparison is unclear and the sense is held loosely.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["padai","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 955","lb":"SO955","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:43"},"so:956":{"book":"soolamani","num":956,"t":["மணிமருங் குடையன வயிரக் கோட்டின","அணிமருங் கருவிய வரைக ளன்னவான்","மணிமருங் குடையன வயிரக் கோட்டின","அணிமருங் கருவிய வரச வேழமே."],"p":"","r":"The royal elephants, with gem-set sides and diamond tusks, stand like hills streaming with water.","n":"Lines 3 and 4 repeat lines 1 and 2 with a different ending.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["padai","perumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 956","lb":"SO956","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:43"},"so:957":{"book":"soolamani","num":957,"t":["மஞ்சிவர் மணிவரை மகளிர் சீறடி","விஞ்சையந் தொழிலிய விடுத்த மெல்லியற்","பஞ்சின்மேன் மிதிப்பினும் பதைத்துப் பையவே","அஞ்சிமே லிவர்வதற் கார்வஞ் செய்யுமே."],"p":"","r":"The women of the hill-clans have feet so tender that, for a cotton-like cushion laid under them, they start back, and climb up gently with fear.","n":"The subject of the climbing and its object are not clear; the verse seems to speak of the women stepping to mount the elephants.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","naanam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 957","lb":"SO957","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["கொம்பனார்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:43"},"so:958":{"book":"soolamani","num":958,"t":["மணிவரை யரசனு மகர மால்கடல்","அணிவரை நிலமுடை யாணை வேந்தனும்","கணிவரை பொழுதினாற் கண்ணுற் றாரரோ","பணிவரை யிலாத்தொழிற் பரவைத் தானையார்."],"p":"","r":"The hill-king and the sceptred lord of the earth meet each other at the hour set by the astrologers, their sea-like hosts standing in order.","n":"The two kings are named by epithets only; the one 'with the order' is read as the earth's king of Podanam.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["arasu","kaalam","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 958","lb":"SO958","mm":1,"ev":"The Vidyadhara king and the Podanam king meet at the hour fixed by the astrologers.","cl":[],"ag":["வேந்தன்","ஆணை வேந்தன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:43"},"so:959":{"book":"soolamani","num":959,"t":["அம்மல ரலங்கலான் றடக்கை யென்னுமம்","பொய்ம்மலர்த் தாமரை முகிழ்க்கு மெல்லையுண்","மைம்மலர் நெடுவரை மன்னன் மற்றவன்","செம்மல ரங்கையிற் செறியப் புல்லினான்."],"p":"","r":"While the garlanded king's lotus-like hands are closing in salute, the hill-king takes him tightly in his red-flower hands.","n":"The garlanded one is read as the Podanam king; the verse names neither king.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["panivu","anbu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 959","lb":"SO959","mm":1,"ev":"The Vidyadhara king embraces the other king as he folds his hands.","cl":[],"ag":["வேந்தன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:43"},"so:960":{"book":"soolamani","num":960,"t":["வலம்புரி வண்ணனு மகர மால்கடல்","நலம்புரி நல்லொளி நம்பி தானுமவ்","வுலம்புரி தோளினா னொளிகொள் பைங்கழல்","கலம்புரி தடக்கையாற் கதழக் கூப்பினார்."],"p":"","r":"Vijayan and the sea-dark Tivittan fold their hands over their jewelled forearms before the strong-armed king and his bright feet.","n":"The two are named by epithets (the conch-coloured one and the sea-dark youth) and the king they bow to is not named.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["panivu","anbu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 960","lb":"SO960","mm":1,"ev":"Vijayan and Tivittan bow to the king with folded hands.","cl":[],"ag":["விசயன்","செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:43"},"so:961":{"book":"soolamani","num":961,"t":["காமரு கவினொளிக் காளை மார்களைத்","தாமரைச் செங்கணாற் றழுவிப் பின்னவர்","பூமரு பொன்வரை யகலம் புல்லினான்","சாமரை நன்னுதற் றடக்கை யானையான்."],"p":"","r":"The king with the lotus-red eyes takes the youths in his gaze, then embraces the younger ones on their golden-hill chests.","n":"Who is 'the elephant-like king' and who are the 'younger ones' is not fixed by the verse.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["anbu","panivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 961","lb":"SO961","mm":1,"ev":"The king embraces the young men after greeting them with a look.","cl":[],"ag":["ஆணை வேந்தன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:43"},"so:962":{"book":"soolamani","num":962,"t":["எழில்விரி நெடுவரை யிறைவன் றன்மகன்","அழல்விரி சுடரொளி யருக்க கீர்த்தியும்","பொழிலணி போதனத் திறைவன் பொன்னணிக்","கழலவ னடியிணை கையிற் கூப்பினான்."],"p":"","r":"Arkakirti, son of the lord of the long hills, folds his hands before the golden-anklet feet of the king of Podanam.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["panivu","perumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 962","lb":"SO962","mm":1,"ev":"Arkakirti bows to the Podanam king with folded hands.","cl":[],"ag":["அருக்க கீர்த்தி"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:43"},"so:963":{"book":"soolamani","num":963,"t":["ஆங்கவ னழகுகண் பருக மற்றவன்","றாங்கெழு வனையதோ டழுவித் தன்னொடும்","ஓங்கிய மழகளி றெருத்த மேற்றினான்","வீங்கிய கனைகழல் வேந்தர் வேந்தனே."],"p":"","r":"Drinking in his beauty, the king of kings embraces Arkakirti, raises him with himself onto the neck of a young elephant and so seats him.","n":"The seating on the king's own elephant is the sign of honour; nothing in the verse says why Arkakirti is chosen.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["anbu","perumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 963","lb":"SO963","mm":1,"ev":"The Podanam king embraces Arkakirti and lifts him onto the neck of his own young elephant.","cl":[],"ag":["ஆணை வேந்தன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:43"},"so:964":{"book":"soolamani","num":964,"t":["மன்னிய விஞ்சை வேந்தன் றம்பியு மருகன் றானுந்","துன்னிய சுரமை நாடன் றொடுகழ றொழுத லோடும்","பின்னிய காதல் வெள்ளம் பெருகிய விரிவிற் றாகிப்","பொன்னியல் கழலி னாற்கோர் பொங்கொளி புணர்ந்த தன்றே."],"p":"","r":"The Vidyadhara king's younger brother and his nephew bow at the anklets of the lord of Suramai, and a swelling flood of love shines through the golden anklets.","n":"The lord of Suramai is read as the Podanam king by another title; 'marukan' may mean nephew or son-in-law and is held loosely.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["panivu","anbu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 964","lb":"SO964","mm":1,"ev":"The Vidyadhara king's brother and nephew bow at the feet of the lord of Suramai.","cl":[],"ag":["தம்பி","மருகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:43"},"so:965":{"book":"soolamani","num":965,"t":["வெஞ்சுட ராழி யாளும் விறலவற் கிளைய தாதை","மஞ்சுடை விஞ்சை நாடன் மலரடி வணங்கி மற்ற","வஞ்சமில் புகழி னான்றன் மனத்தையும் வணக்கி யிட்டான்","செஞ்சுட ரிலங்கு பூணான் றிறற் சிறீ பால னென்பான்."],"p":"","r":"The hero called Sripalan, the younger father of the one who wields the fierce discus, bows at the feet of the Vidyadhara lord and bows that stainless-famed king's heart as well.","n":"Who Sripalan is, and whose 'younger father' he is, is not clear; the reading is held loosely.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["panivu","pugazh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 965","lb":"SO965","mm":1,"ev":"Sripalan bows at the feet of the Vidyadhara king.","cl":[],"ag":["சிறீபாலன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:43"},"so:966":{"book":"soolamani","num":966,"t":["ஆய்ந்தசீ ரரச ராங்குக் கலந்தபின் னமிழ்த வெள்ளம்","பாய்ந்தது பரவை நன்னீர்ப் பாற்கடல் பரந்த தேபோல்","ஏந்திய காதல் கூர வெழினகர் பெயர்ந்து புக்கார்","காந்திய கனகப் பைம்பூட் கருவரை யனைய தோளார்."],"p":"","r":"After the kings meet, the sea of love overflows like the milk-sea, and with the princes and their love for each other they enter the fair city.","n":"The line 'nectar flood leaped from the sea' makes the meeting itself the source of the love that carries them into the city.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["anbu","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 966","lb":"SO966","mm":1,"ev":"The kings and princes enter the city after their meeting.","cl":[],"ag":["அரசர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:43"},"so:967":{"book":"soolamani","num":967,"t":["எழில்கொள்கந் தனைய திண்டோ ளிளைய ரோடரசரீண்டிப்","பொழிலகந் தழீஇய சோலைப் பொன்னர்க் கோயில் புக்குத்","தழுமலர்க் கோதை நல்லார் பலாண்டிசை ததும்ப வாழ்த்தச்","செழுமலர்த் திரள்க டாழுஞ் சித்திர கூடஞ் சேர்ந்தார்."],"p":"","r":"The kings and the strong-armed young princes enter the golden palace amid the groves, and women with flower garlands bless them with long-years songs as they reach the Chitrakuta hall.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","perumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 967","lb":"SO967","mm":1,"ev":"The kings and princes enter the palace and reach the Chitrakuta hall.","cl":[],"ag":["அரசர்","நல்லார்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:43"},"so:968":{"book":"soolamani","num":968,"t":["வெள்ளொளி யெயிற்றுப் பேழ்வாய் விரியுளை யரச சீயம்","ஒள்ளொளி தவழ வேந்து மொளிமணி யணையின் மேலோர்","கள்ளொளி கமழுங் கோதை மகளிர்கள் கவரி வீசத்","தெள்ளொளிக் குமர ரோடு மிருந்தனர் திருந்து வேலோர்."],"p":"","r":"The kings sit like lions on gem-set couches with flower-bearing women fanning chowries and the bright princes beside them.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 968","lb":"SO968","mm":1,"ev":"The kings sit in the hall with the princes while women fan them.","cl":[],"ag":["அரசர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:43"},"so:969":{"book":"soolamani","num":969,"t":["காமரு மகளிர் வீசுங் கனமணிப் பவழத் திண்காற்","சாமரை பயந்த தென்ற கைமுடித் தாது சிந்தப்","பூமரு பொறிவண் டார்ப்பப் பொலிந்தவ ரிருந்த போழ்தின்","ஏமரு கடலந் தானை யிருநிலக் கிழவன் சொன்னான்."],"p":"","r":"As the women fan chowries that scatter pollen from their hair and bees hum, the lord of the earth speaks, and the verse stops before his words.","n":"The speech is the next verse's, so the speaker is unnamed here and the king of Podanam is only the likely reading.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["arasu","inchol"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 969","lb":"SO969","mm":1,"ev":"The earth's king begins to speak to those seated in the hall.","cl":[],"ag":["ஆணை வேந்தன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:43"},"so:970":{"book":"soolamani","num":970,"t":["விண்ணிடை யிழிந்து வந்த விண்ணவர் கிழவ னொப்பாய்","மண்ணிடை யென்னை யிங்கோர் பொருளென மதித்து வந்த","தெண்ணிடை யுணரு மாந்தர்க் கிடைதெரி வரிய தொன்றாற்","கண்ணிடை யுமிழுஞ் செந்தீக் கடாக்களிற் றுழவ வென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"Prajapati greets the Vidyadhara king, who came down from the sky like the lord of the gods and thought him worth the visit, and addresses him as lord of rutting elephants with fire in his eyes.","n":"The speaker is not named in the verse; the address in line 4 and the host's welcome in the next verses fix him as Prajapati. The third line is hard and its sense is held loosely.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vendhan","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 970","lb":"SO970","mm":1,"ev":"Prajapati welcomes the Vidyadhara king, who has descended from the sky to Podanam.","cl":[],"ag":["பிரசாபதி","வேந்தன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["பிரசாபதி","வேந்தன்"],"ch":"so:44"},"so:971":{"book":"soolamani","num":971,"t":["ஏங்குநீர் வளாகங் காக்கு மிக்குவா மன்ன ரேறே","தூங்குநீர் மருத வேலிச் சுரமைநா டுடைய தோன்றால்","ஏங்குநீ ரமிழ்தின் றீர்த்தஞ் சென்றனர் தெளித்த லன்றே.","ஓங்குநீ ருலகந் தன்னு ளுயர்ந்தவர்க் குரிய தென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"The Vidyadhara king answers, hailing Prajapati as bull of the Ikshvaku kings and lord of Suramai, and says that those who went to the holy waters of nectar went to cleanse, which belongs to the highest of the world.","n":"Lines 3 and 4 are obscure, and the compressed image of the nectar-waters is read here only in outline.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vendhan","theerththam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 971","lb":"SO971","mm":1,"ev":"The Vidyadhara king replies to Prajapati's welcome with praise of his house.","cl":[],"ag":["வேந்தன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["வேந்தன்","பிரசாபதி"],"ch":"so:44"},"so:972":{"book":"soolamani","num":972,"t":["வருதற்கு முதலு முன்பே மருசிவந் துணர்த்தக் கேட்டேன்","பொருதற்கண் ணரிய வேலோய் புராணநூற் புலவர் யாரும்","கருதற்கண் ணரிய கண்ணி கடல்வண்ணற் குரிய ளென்னத்","தருதற்கு மகிழ்ந்து வந்தேன் றாழமீங் கொழிக வென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"The Vidyadhara king says that Marusi had come ahead and told him, and that he has come gladly to give his rare daughter to the dark-hued prince, so delay should end.","n":"This is the fulfilment of the astrologer's dream-reading that a Vidyadhara king would come and give a daughter; the king presents it as his own pleasure and not as a fate laid on him.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["makkal","oozh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 972","lb":"SO972","mm":1,"ev":"The Vidyadhara king says he has come to give his daughter to Tivittan and asks that there be no delay.","cl":[],"ag":["வேந்தன்","மருசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["வேந்தன்","பிரசாபதி"],"ch":"so:44"},"so:973":{"book":"soolamani","num":973,"t":["அன்னண மரசர் பேசி யிருந்தபின் னருக்க னோடும்","பொன்னணி புரிசை வேலிப் புதுநகர் புகுக வென்று","மன்னவ குமரர் தம்மை மணிவரை யரச னேவப்","பின்னவர் வேழ மேறிப் பெயர்ந்தனர் போது கின்றார்."],"p":"","r":"When the kings have finished talking, the gem-mountain's king sends the princes with Arukkan into the new city, and they ride off on elephants.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 973","lb":"SO973","mm":1,"ev":"The Vidyadhara king bids the princes and Arkakirti enter the new city, and they ride away on elephants.","cl":[],"ag":["வேந்தன்","செங்கண் ணெடியான்","விசயன்","அருக்க கீர்த்தி"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:44"},"so:974":{"book":"soolamani","num":974,"t":["ஆயிடை யரச சீய மனையவர் பெயரும் போழ்தின்","வேயுடை யருவிச் சாரல் வெள்ளிவேய் விலங்க னாடன்","தீயுடை யிலங்கு வேலான் றிருமக ளமிர்தின் சாயல்","வீயுடை யலங்கன் ஞான்ற மிடைமணி விமானஞ் சேர்ந்தான்."],"p":"","r":"As the lion-like princes pass, the lord of the silver hill reaches the gem-set palace where his daughter, soft as nectar, waits.","n":"The vimana and the sense of who reaches it are not fully clear in line 4.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","makkal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 974","lb":"SO974","mm":1,"ev":"The Vidyadhara king arrives at the mansion where Sayambavai is, while the princes pass.","cl":[],"ag":["வேந்தன்","சயம்பவை"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:44"},"so:975":{"book":"soolamani","num":975,"t":["பொலங்கலம் புலம்ப வாயம் புடைநின்று போற்றுக் கூவ","அலங்கலுங் குழலுந் தாழ வணிஞிமி றரவஞ் செய்ய","இலங்கலங் கனக மாடத் தெழுனிலை யேறி னாளே,","விலங்கலின் குவடு சேரு மெல்லியற் றோகை போல்வாள்."],"p":"","r":"With golden vessels ringing and her girls calling praises about her, bees humming over her garland and hair, she climbs to the seventh storey, a slight woman like a peahen rising to a mountain peak.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","makkal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 975","lb":"SO975","mm":1,"ev":"Sayambavai climbs to the seventh storey of the golden mansion among her women.","cl":[],"ag":["சயம்பவை"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:44"},"so:976":{"book":"soolamani","num":976,"t":["ஆயத்து ளலர்கொம் பன்னா ளமிர்தமா பிரபை யென்ற","வேயொத்த பணைமென் றோளி மிடைமணிக் கபாட நீக்கி","வாயிற்கண் வருக நங்கை வளநகர் காண்க வென்று","கோயிற்க ணருகு செல்லுங் குமரரைக் காட்டினாளே."],"p":"","r":"Her friend Amirtha-pirapai opens the jewelled door and, bidding her look at the city, points out the princes riding below.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","kurippu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 976","lb":"SO976","mm":1,"ev":"The friend opens the door and shows Sayambavai the princes passing near the palace.","cl":[],"ag":["அமிர்தமா பிரபை","சயம்பவை"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:44"},"so:977":{"book":"soolamani","num":977,"t":["விண்ணதிர்த் தனைய வாகித் திசைமுகஞ் சிலம்ப வீங்கிக்","கண்ணதிர் முரசுஞ் சங்கும் கடற்பெயர் முழக்க மாக","மண்ணதிர் கொள்ளச் செல்லு மைந்தர்கள் யார்கொ லென்னும்","எண்ணதிர் மனத்தி னாட்குத் தோழிமற் றிதனைச் சொன்னாள்."],"p":"","r":"The princes pass with drums and conches that sound like the sea and shake the earth, and the friend answers the girl, whose mind wonders who they are.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["padai","kurippu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 977","lb":"SO977","mm":1,"ev":"Sayambavai wonders who the riders are, and the friend answers.","cl":[],"ag":["சயம்பவை","அமிர்தமா பிரபை"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:44"},"so:978":{"book":"soolamani","num":978,"t":["முன்னவ னம்பி வெய்யோன் பெயரவன் முழவுத் தோளான்","பின்னவன் சுரமை வேந்தன் பெருமக னவற்குத் தம்பி","கன்னவில் வயிரத் தோளான் கருமுகி லுருவக் காளை","இன்னவ னென்ன லோடு மிலங்கொளி முறுவல் கொண்டாள்."],"p":"","r":"The friend names them: the first the sun-named prince with drum-like shoulders, then Prajapati's son, and his dark-cloud younger brother; Sayambavai gives a bright smile.","n":"Which brother is named in each clause turns on the word division of lines 1 and 2, so the order is read tentatively.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["natpu","kurippu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 978","lb":"SO978","mm":1,"ev":"The friend identifies the three princes to Sayambavai, who smiles.","cl":[],"ag":["அமிர்தமா பிரபை","சயம்பவை"],"ec":[],"spk":["அமிர்தமா பிரபை","சயம்பவை"],"ch":"so:44"},"so:979":{"book":"soolamani","num":979,"t":["நீலமா மணிக்குன் றேய்ப்ப நிழலெழுந் திலங்கு மேனிக்","கோலவா யரச காளை குங்குமக் குவவுத் தோளான்","மேலவா நெடுங்க ணோட மீட்டவை விலக்க மாட்டாள்","மாலைவாய்க் குழலி சால மம்மர்கொண் மனத்த ளானாள்."],"p":"","r":"The dark-skinned prince with saffron-smeared shoulders draws her eyes, and she cannot call them back and becomes greatly dazed in her heart.","n":"Neither her face nor his is held back by modesty here; the lines present the pull as one-sided, from her eyes to him.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","kan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 979","lb":"SO979","mm":1,"ev":"Sayambavai's eyes turn to Tivittan and she cannot look away.","cl":[],"ag":["செங்கண் ணெடியான்","சயம்பவை"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:44"},"so:980":{"book":"soolamani","num":980,"t":["தாமரை யனைய கண்ணுந் தடக்கையும் பவழ வாயும்","பூமரு பூவைக் கண்ணிப் புதுமல ரொளியுங் காட்டித்","தூமரு நீல மென்னு மணிதுணர்ந் தனைய குஞ்சிக்","காமரு காளை கன்னி கண்களைச் சிறைகொண் டிட்டான்."],"p":"","r":"With lotus eyes, long arms, coral mouth and fresh flowers in his hair, the lad shows his charms and takes her eyes captive.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","azhagu","kan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 980","lb":"SO980","mm":1,"ev":"Tivittan's looks take Sayambavai's eyes captive.","cl":[],"ag":["செங்கண் ணெடியான்","சயம்பவை"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:44"},"so:981":{"book":"soolamani","num":981,"t":["சிறையென்ப தில்லைச் செவ்வே செம்புனல் சிறக்கு மாயின்","நிறையென்ப தில்லைக் காம நேர்நின்று பெருகு மாயின்","நிறைநின்ற துளதென் பார்க்கின் றரும்பெற லிவள துள்ள","நறைநின்று கமழுங் குஞ்சி நம்பிபாற் பட்ட தன்றே."],"p":"","r":"The poet argues that there is no restraint once desire rises straight on, and the girl's rare restraint has gone to the fragrant-haired lord.","n":"The verse rests on a play of 'siRai' (a captive, a bank) and 'niRai' (restraint, fullness), as a river's flood overruns its banks; the argument is the poet's and is not put in her mouth.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nirai","kaamam","naanam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 981","lb":"SO981","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["restraint cannot hold when desire rises unopposed","general","asserted",2,"narrator"]],"ag":["சயம்பவை"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:44"},"so:982":{"book":"soolamani","num":982,"t":["கோணின்ற மதியம் போலக் குழைமுகஞ் சுடரக் கோட்டித்","தாணின்ற குவளைப் போதிற் றாதகங் குழைய மோந்து","வாணின்ற நெடுங்கண் காளை வடிவினுக் கிவர மற்றை","நாணின்று விலக்க நங்கை நடுவுநின் றுருகு கின்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"As the lad turns his moon-bright face and sniffs a flower, his gaze draws her, and modesty holds her back while she melts between the two.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kan","naanam","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 982","lb":"SO982","mm":1,"ev":"Tivittan looks at Sayambavai and smells a flower while her modesty holds her back.","cl":[],"ag":["செங்கண் ணெடியான்","சயம்பவை"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:44"},"so:983":{"book":"soolamani","num":983,"t":["அவ்வழி யமுதம் பூத்த அருங்கலக் கொம்பைத் தன்கோன்","இவ்வழி வருக வென்ற தவடம ரிசைப்பக் கேட்டு","மைவழி நெடுங்க ணாளு மனம்புக்க குரிசி றன்னைச்","செவ்வழி மழலை நாணே யெழினியா மறைத்துச் சென்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"Hearing her father call her this way, the dark-eyed girl, with the prince in her heart, goes off with modesty as the curtain that hides him.","n":"The first line is read as the father calling her; the lines have a repaired pulli in line 2.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naanam","manam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 983","lb":"SO983","mm":1,"ev":"Sayambavai goes to her father at his call, hiding her feeling behind modesty.","cl":[],"ag":["சயம்பவை","வேந்தன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:44"},"so:984":{"book":"soolamani","num":984,"t":["ஆயிரங் கண்ணி லாதார்க் கழகுகாண் பரிய நங்கை","வேயிரும் பணைமென் றோளார் மெல்லடி பரவச் சென்று","மாயிருஞ் செல்வத் தாதை மலரடி வணங்கி நின்றாள்","சேயிருங் குன்ற மீன்ற செழுமணிச் சலாகை போல்வாள்."],"p":"","r":"Surrounded by girls with bamboo shoulders, she goes and bows at the feet of her father, rich as a gem-set rod from a red hill.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["makkal","panivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 984","lb":"SO984","mm":1,"ev":"Sayambavai bows at her father's feet.","cl":[],"ag":["சயம்பவை","கொம்பனார்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:44"},"so:985":{"book":"soolamani","num":985,"t":["மங்கையை வலத்துக் கொண்டு மாலையுங் குழலுந் தோடும்","அங்கையாற் றிருத்தி மாம னடிகளைப் பணிக வென்று","செங்கயற் கண்ணி தாதை செவ்விரல் குவியப் பற்றிப்","பங்கயப் பழன நாடன் பாதமூ லத்து வைத்தான்."],"p":"","r":"The father seats her on his right, sets her garland, hair and ear-ring right with his hand and bids her bow at her uncle's feet, then lays her at the feet of the lord of the lotus-ponded land.","n":"'Maaman' here is Prajapati, to whom she is given as bride for his son; the kinship term is the speech's own.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["makkal","panivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 985","lb":"SO985","mm":1,"ev":"The Vidyadhara king sets Sayambavai right and places her at Prajapati's feet.","cl":[],"ag":["வேந்தன்","சயம்பவை","பிரசாபதி"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:44"},"so:986":{"book":"soolamani","num":986,"t":["மருமகள் வணங்க முன்னே வலப்புடைக் குறங்கி னேற்றிக்","கருமைகொள் குவளைக் கண்ணி கழிநலக் கதிர்ப்பு நோக்கித்","திருமக ளிவளைச் சேருஞ் செய்தவ முடைய காளை","அருமைகொ டிகிரி யாள்தற் கையமொன் றில்லை யென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"Prajapati seats the girl on his right thigh, looks at her great beauty, and says the lad who wins this goddess must have great earlier austerity and surely will rule the wheel.","n":"This answers the earlier reading that Tivittan will protect the whole joined world, and ties the girl's coming to his merit.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thavam","arasu","oozh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 986","lb":"SO986","mm":1,"ev":"Prajapati takes Sayambavai on his thigh and declares Tivittan will rule the wheel.","cl":[["great earlier austerity wins the bride and with her sovereignty","general","asserted",3,"pirasapathi"]],"ag":["பிரசாபதி","சயம்பவை"],"ec":[],"spk":["பிரசாபதி","சயம்பவை"],"ch":"so:44"},"so:987":{"book":"soolamani","num":987,"t":["அருங்கல மகளிர்க் கேற்ற வழகெலாந் தொகுத்து மற்றோர்","இருங்கலி யுலகங் காணப் படைத்தவ னியற்றி னான்கொல","ஒருங்கல துலகின் மிக்க மகளிர துருவ மெல்லாம்","பெருங்கல வல்கு றன்பாற் புகுந்துகொல் பெயர்ந்த தென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"Prajapati asks whether the Maker gathered every beauty fit for jewelled women and set it in her, or whether every great woman's form entered her, so that the world could see.","n":"The idea of the Maker is a figure of praise and no claim of a creator god in a Jain epic; no school is claimed.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","aram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 987","lb":"SO987","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["பிரசாபதி"],"ec":[],"spk":["பிரசாபதி",""],"ch":"so:44"},"so:988":{"book":"soolamani","num":988,"t":["அருந்தவ முடையன் யானு மன்றெனி லணங்கு போலும்","பெருந்தகை நங்கை தன்னைப் பெற்றவன் றாதை யென்னும்","திருந்திய மொழியுந் தெய்வச் செல்வமுந் தெய்வ மன்னீர்","பொருந்திய தொடர்பு மெய்தப் புணருமோ புவியி னென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"Prajapati says he must have rare austerity, or else would the words that he is the father of such a woman and the divine wealth and bond come to him?","n":"The speaker is not named; the 'father' in line 2 may be either king, so the reading is held loosely.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thavam","oozh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 988","lb":"SO988","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["a worthy bride, wealth and kin come only to one of rare austerity","general","asserted",1,"pirasapathi"]],"ag":["பிரசாபதி"],"ec":[],"spk":["பிரசாபதி",""],"ch":"so:44"},"so:989":{"book":"soolamani","num":989,"t":["ஆங்கமர்ந் தரசர் பேசி யலர்மிசை யணங்கன் னாளைப்","பூங்கமழ் சோலை வேலிப் பொன்னகர் புகுக வென்னத்","தேங்கமழ் குழலி னாருந் தாயருஞ் செவ்வி காப்ப","வீங்கொளி விமானத் துச்சி வெண்ணிலா முற்றஞ் சேர்ந்தாள்."],"p":"","r":"After the kings have talked, the girl, her women and mothers attending her, is sent to the golden city and reaches the white-moonlit terrace of the shining palace.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["makkal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 989","lb":"SO989","mm":1,"ev":"Sayambavai is sent on to the golden city and climbs to the palace terrace.","cl":[],"ag":["சயம்பவை","கொம்பனார்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:44"},"so:990":{"book":"soolamani","num":990,"t":["மதுநனைந் தலர்ந்த தாரான் திறத்தையான் மறப்ப னென்னின்","விதிநனி துரப்ப மீட்டு நினைப்பதே விளைக்கு முள்ளம்","இதுநனி யறியு மோவிவ் வுலகமென் றிதயத் தோடும்","புதுநனை விரிந்த கோதைப் பொன்னனாள் புலம்ப கொண்டாள்."],"p":"","r":"The girl, burning inwardly, says that if she forgot the garlanded man, fate would drive her back to remember him, and asks whether the world knows this.","n":"The stanza was printed in eight half-lines and has been joined.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["ninaivu","oozh","kaamanoy"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 990","lb":"SO990","mm":1,"ev":"Sayambavai laments on the terrace that she cannot forget Tivittan.","cl":[["fate presses the mind to remember the beloved","general","asserted",2,"sayambavai"]],"ag":["சயம்பவை"],"ec":[],"spk":["சயம்பவை",""],"ch":"so:44"},"so:991":{"book":"soolamani","num":991,"t":["காதலார் திறத்துக் காத லாக்கிய காத லாரை","ஏதிலார் போல நோக்கி னிருமடங் காக வெய்தும்","போதுலாஞ் சிலையி னான்றன் பொருகணைக் கிலக்கஞ் செய்யும்","ஆதலாற் காம நோய்க்கோ ரருமருந் தில்லை யன்றே."],"p":"","r":"The poet, or the girl, says that looking at lovers whom love has made dear as if they were strangers doubles the pain, since the love-god's arrow makes a target of such looks, so love's sickness has no rare medicine.","n":"","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamanoy","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 991","lb":"SO991","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["love's sickness has no cure","general","reported",4,"sayambavai"]],"ag":["சயம்பவை"],"ec":[],"spk":["சயம்பவை",""],"ch":"so:44"},"so:992":{"book":"soolamani","num":992,"t":["தேமிடை கானல் வேலிச் செழுமணற் குவாலுங் குன்றும்","பூமிடை தடமுங் காவும் புக்கவர்க் கரண மாகா","தாமுடை மனமுங் கண்ணு நிறைவுந்தம் பால வாகா","காமுடை மனத்தி னார்கட் கியாருளர் களைக ணாவார்."],"p":"","r":"Groves, sandy shore, hills and pools give no shelter to those who enter, and mind, eye and restraint no longer stay their own, so who can be a refuge for the lovesick?","n":"","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamanoy","nirai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 992","lb":"SO992","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["those with desire in their mind have no refuge in places or in themselves","general","asserted",4,"narrator"]],"ag":["சயம்பவை"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:44"},"so:993":{"book":"soolamani","num":993,"t":["அனையவ ளரச கன்னி யாகிய பொழுதி னிப்பாற்","புனைமல ரலங்கன் மார்பிற் பூமியங் கிழவன் றேவி","வனமல ருருவக் கண்ணி மணிவண்ணன் மார்பு தோயும்","கனமணிப் பூணி னாளைக் காண்கென விடுக்கப் பட்டார்."],"p":"","r":"The poet says that, once the bride has been made a king's daughter, women are sent at the order of the king's queen to see the girl who will lie on the dark-hued youth's chest.","n":"The first line is hard: it is not clear whether the girl has become the king's daughter by birth, by adoption or by the wedding settlement; the tradition is that she is the Vidyadhara king's daughter promised to Tivittan, and the verse itself gives no name.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sutram","kurippu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 993","lb":"SO993","mm":1,"ev":"The queen of the lord of the earth sends women to go and see the bride.","cl":[],"ag":["தேவி","விடுக்கப் பட்டார்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:45"},"so:994":{"book":"soolamani","num":994,"t":["மதுகரி வயந்த சேனை யெனவிவ ராதி யாகப்","புதுமலர்க் கொடியும் பூவுந் துணர்களும் புணர்ந்த பேரார்","கதிரன கலங்க டாங்கிக் காப்புமங் கலங்க ளேந்தி","எதிர்தரு மிளமை யாரோ ராயிரத் தெண்மர் சூழ்ந்தார்."],"p":"","r":"A thousand and eight young women, named from Madhukari and Vasantasena on, bound with flower-creepers and carrying auspicious things on shining jewels, gather round in the bride's welcome.","n":"The poet gives two names (மதுகரி, வயந்த சேனை) and 'and others' for a company of 1008; the number is the sort of round number the epic uses for an escort, not a count.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","sutram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 994","lb":"SO994","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["இளமையார்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:45"},"so:995":{"book":"soolamani","num":995,"t":["மாதவக் குலத்து ளார்கண் மாதவ சேனை யுள்ளிட்","டோதியபெயரின் மிக்கா ருலகறி கலையின் வல்லார்","கோதையுங் குழையுந் தோடுங் குளிர்முத்த வடமுந் தாங்கிப்","போதிவர் குழலி தாதை பொன்னகர் முன்னி னாரே"],"p":"","r":"The highest-born women of the party, headed by Madhavasenai and skilled in every art the world knows, wear garlands, ear-ornaments and pearl strings and come to the palace of the bride's father.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalvi","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 995","lb":"SO995","mm":1,"ev":"Madhavasenai and the other women come before the bride's father's golden palace.","cl":[],"ag":["மாதவ சேனை","இளமையார்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:45"},"so:996":{"book":"soolamani","num":996,"t":["நன்னுத லவரு நம்பி தாயரு நடக்க வல்ல","பொன்னுதற் பிடியுந் தேரும் வையமு மிழிந்து புக்கு","மன்னனை வணங்கி யன்னோன் பணிகொண்டு மடந்தை கோயில்","இன்னிசை மகளிர் முன்சென் றெதிர்கொள வெய்தி னாரே."],"p":"","r":"The women, with the mothers of the youth, get down from their golden-browed elephants, chariots and carts, bow to the king and, with his leave, come to the girl's palace where women singing sweetly receive them.","n":"நம்பி தாயர் (the youth's mothers) is plural; whether it means both princes' mothers or the poet's loose plural for the one mother who sent the party in 993 is not clear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["panivu","sutram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 996","lb":"SO996","mm":1,"ev":"The visiting women dismount, bow to the king and are welcomed by singing women at the bride's palace.","cl":[],"ag":["இளமையார்","தேவி","வேந்தன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:45"},"so:997":{"book":"soolamani","num":997,"t":["பொன்னிய லமளி மேலாள் பூவணை மருங்கு தீண்டக்","கன்னியர் கவரிக் கற்றை கைவல னசைப்பக் காய்பொன்","னின்னிசைக் குழைவில் வீச வினிதினங் கிருந்த நங்கை","துன்னிய மகளிர் தம்மைத் தமர்தொழு துணர்த்தக் கண்டாள்."],"p":"","r":"The bride, seated in comfort on a golden couch with her women fanning her with chowries, is told by her kin to rise and sees the visitors.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","sutram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 997","lb":"SO997","mm":1,"ev":"Her kinswomen bow and wake the bride to the visitors' arrival.","cl":[],"ag":["நங்கை","தமர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:45"},"so:998":{"book":"soolamani","num":998,"t":["வலங்கொண்டு தொழுது வாழ்த்தி மற்றவ ரடைந்த போதின்","உலங்கொண்ட வயிரத் தோளா னுழைக்கல மகளி ரென்று","நலங்கொண்டோ ரார்வங் கூர நகைமுக முறுவ றோன்றிப்","புலங்கொண்ட ததனைக் காப்பான் பூவொன்று நெரித்து மோந்தாள்."],"p":"","r":"The bride's friends bow and bless the visitors, who come in, and the bride, thinking them the queen's maids, hides a smile and crushes and smells a flower to guard her feeling.","n":"The flower crushed and smelt is a covering gesture, the poet's chosen sign for a shy girl who does not want her pleasure seen; the verse puts the visitors' rank as a thought ('maids of the diamond-armed one'), not a fact.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["naanam","kurippu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 998","lb":"SO998","mm":1,"ev":"The visitors enter, and the bride, taking them for the groom's household, crushes a flower to hide her smile.","cl":[],"ag":["நங்கை","இளமையார்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:45"},"so:999":{"book":"soolamani","num":999,"t":["விண்விளக் குறுக்குந் திங்கட் சுடர்நுதல் விளக்கி னாலும்","பெண்விளக் குறுக்கு மேனி பெருகிய வொளியி னாலும்","பண்விளக் குறுக்கு மின்சொற் பாவையைப் பாவை மாரைக்","கண்விளக் குறுக்கு மாற்றாற் காண்டலுக் கரிய ளானாள்."],"p":"","r":"The poet says the bride has so much light, from her brow, her body and her sweet voice, that the visitors' eyes cannot take her in.","n":"The praise runs in three steps of light, brow to body to voice, and ends in the girl being 'hard to see'; the verse itself says nothing of how long such beauty lasts.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","kan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 999","lb":"SO999","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["நங்கை"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:45"},"so:1000":{"book":"soolamani","num":1000,"t":["செஞ்சிலம் பொலிக்குஞ் செல்வச் சீறடித் தெய்வப் பாவை","பஞ்சிலங் கணையின் மேலாள் பரந்தொளி திருவில் வீச","மஞ்சிலங் குருவ வானின் மழையிடை நுடங்கு மின்போல்","அஞ்சிலம் படியி னார்த மறிவினை யயர்வித் திட்டாள்."],"p":"","r":"The bride's gold anklet rings and her rainbow-bright form on the soft couch, like lightning among clouds, make the visitors forget themselves.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1000","lb":"SO1000","mm":1,"ev":"The bride's radiance dazes the visiting women.","cl":[],"ag":["நங்கை","இளமையார்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:45"},"so:1001":{"book":"soolamani","num":1001,"t":["மற்றவர் காணும் போழ்தின் மாதவ சேனை யென்பாள்","சுற்றிய பளிங்கிற் சோதிச் சுவர்மிசை யெரித்துத் தோன்ற","இற்றிவ ளுருவ மென்றாங் கிதயத்து ளெழுதி வைத்துப்","பிற்றையோர் பலகை தன்மேற் பெய்வளை யெழுத லுற்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"As she sees the girl, Madhavasenai fixes her form in her heart as if drawing it on the bright crystal wall, then takes up another board and begins to draw it.","n":"She holds the likeness in her heart and only then paints it, so the portrait is made from memory in the bride's own chamber; the poet makes the crystal wall the mirror of what she keeps inside.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalvi","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1001","lb":"SO1001","mm":1,"ev":"Madhavasenai memorises the bride's form and begins to paint it on a board.","cl":[],"ag":["மாதவ சேனை"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:45"},"so:1002":{"book":"soolamani","num":1002,"t":["பண்களை மருட்டு மின்சொற் பாவையைப் பருக லுற்ற","கண்களை மருள நீருங் கண்களெங் கண்க ளாகப்","பெண்களை மருட்டுஞ் சாயற் பேதையைக் காண்மி னென்று","மண்களை மருட்டுஞ் சீர்நும் மாமியா ரடிகள் சொன்னார்."],"p":"","r":"Madhavasenai tells the bride's friends that the groom's mother, famed for her wealth, had said: see the girl whose looks outshine women's, as one who drinks her in with eyes made our eyes.","n":"The speaker is taken to be Madhavasenai from 1003, where she is named for the first time as the one who speaks; 'your mother-in-law' (நும் மாமியார்) is the groom's mother, so the bride is already addressed as kin.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sutram","kan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1002","lb":"SO1002","mm":1,"ev":"Madhavasenai passes on the groom's mother's words of greeting and praise.","cl":[],"ag":["மாதவ சேனை"],"ec":[],"spk":["மாதவ சேனை","நங்கை"],"ch":"so:45"},"so:1003":{"book":"soolamani","num":1003,"t":["ஆதலா லவர்க்குச் சொல்லு மாற்றமொன் றருளிச் செய்மின்","மாதுலாஞ் சாய லென்ன மாதவ சேனை யென்பாள்","ஏதமாங் கில்லை யன்றே யெங்கண்முன் மொழிய வென்றாள்","கோதிலாக் குணக்கொம் பன்னாள் குறுநகை முறுவல் கொண்டாள்."],"p":"","r":"Madhavasenai asks the bride to give some reply for the groom's mother, saying there is no harm in speaking before them, and the bride, delicate as a creeper, only smiles.","n":"The bride gives no word: her smile is the answer, and the next verse shows her friend reading it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naanam","sol"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1003","lb":"SO1003","mm":1,"ev":"Madhavasenai asks the bride for a reply and the bride answers with a faint smile.","cl":[],"ag":["மாதவ சேனை","நங்கை"],"ec":[],"spk":["மாதவ சேனை","நங்கை"],"ch":"so:45"},"so:1004":{"book":"soolamani","num":1004,"t":["அங்கவள் குறிப்பு நோக்கி யமிமுதமா பிரபை யென்னு","மங்கலத் தோழி கூறு மாமியா ரடிக டம்மை","எங்களின் செய்கை யதாக விணையடி பணிமி னென்றாள்","செங்கனி கனிந்த செவ்வாய்ச் சிறுநுதற் பெரிய கண்ணாள்."],"p":"","r":"The auspicious friend Amirthamapirapai, taking the bride's hint, says to bow at the groom's mother's feet as if it were their own act.","n":"The bride does not speak; the friend reads her sign (குறிப்பு) and gives it words, which keeps the bride's modesty whole while the greeting is returned. The name is printed as one run, அமிமுதமா பிரபை, and may be Amirtha Maprabha.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kurippu","panivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1004","lb":"SO1004","mm":1,"ev":"The bride's friend reads her smile and speaks for her, sending a bow to the groom's mother.","cl":[],"ag":["யமிமுதமா பிரபை"],"ec":[],"spk":["யமிமுதமா பிரபை","மாதவ சேனை"],"ch":"so:45"},"so:1005":{"book":"soolamani","num":1005,"t":["ஆங்கவண் மொழிந்த போழ்தி னணங்கினை வணங்கி மற்றத்","தீங்கனி யமிர்த மன்ன திருமொழிப் பண்ணி காரம்","வாங்குநீ ருலகங் காக்கு மன்னவன் பட்டத் தேவி","ஓங்கிருங் கற்பி னாளுக் குய்ப்பளென் றுணர்த்திப் போந்தாள்."],"p":"","r":"Madhavasenai bows to the bride, tells her that she will carry the sweet gift to the king's chief queen, and goes.","n":"The word பண்ணிகாரம் (a sweet offering) is read as the gift or token that goes with the greeting; its sense is held loosely.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["panivu","thoothu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1005","lb":"SO1005","mm":1,"ev":"Madhavasenai takes leave of the bride and takes the greeting to the queen.","cl":[],"ag":["மாதவ சேனை"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:45"},"so:1006":{"book":"soolamani","num":1006,"t":["பளிங்கியல் பலகை தன்மேற் பாவைய துருவந் தான்முன்","றெளிந்தவா றெழுதிக் கொண்டு செந்துகி லுறையின் மூடி","வளந்தரு கோயின் முன்னி மணிவண்ணற் பயந்த தேவி","அளந்தறி வரிய கற்பி னமிர்தனா ளருகு சேர்ந்தாள்."],"p":"","r":"Madhavasenai paints the bride's form clearly on a crystal board, covers it with a red cloth, and carries it to the temple-palace of the queen who bore the dark-hued youth.","n":"This is the only verse that names the finished act: the likeness made from memory in 1001 is now a thing carried, wrapped like a gift.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalvi","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1006","lb":"SO1006","mm":1,"ev":"Madhavasenai finishes the portrait, wraps it and brings it to the groom's mother.","cl":[],"ag":["மாதவ சேனை","தேவி"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:45"},"so:1007":{"book":"soolamani","num":1007,"t":["மையகத் தலர்ந்த வாட்கண் மாதவ சேனை சென்று","வையகத் தரசன் றேவி மலரடி வணங்க லோடும்","மெய்யகத் துவகை கூர விரும்பித்தன் னருகு கூவிக்","கையகத் திதுவென் னென்னக் கன்னிய துருவ மென்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"Madhavasenai bows at the queen's feet, the queen draws her near with joy and asks what she holds, and Madhavasenai says it is the maiden's form.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["panivu","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1007","lb":"SO1007","mm":1,"ev":"Madhavasenai shows the queen the portrait, and the queen asks what it is.","cl":[],"ag":["மாதவ சேனை","தேவி"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:45"},"so:1008":{"book":"soolamani","num":1008,"t":["அணிகலம் பரிந்து நங்கை யணிமரு ளுருவந் தந்த","மணிமருண் முறுவற் செவ்வாய் மாதவ சேனைக் கீந்து","பணிவரும் பலகை தன்மேற் பாவையைக் காண்டு மென்றாள்","துணிவரும் பவழத் துண்டந் துடிக்கின்ற தனைய வாயாள்."],"p":"","r":"The queen takes off her jewels and gives them to Madhavasenai for the likeness, then asks to see the girl's form on the board.","n":"She pays before she looks, so the reward comes for the act of bringing the image; the gift is also a courtesy that accepts the bride on her kin's word.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1008","lb":"SO1008","mm":1,"ev":"The queen gives her own ornaments to Madhavasenai and asks to see the portrait.","cl":[],"ag":["தேவி","மாதவ சேனை"],"ec":[],"spk":["தேவி","மாதவ சேனை"],"ch":"so:45"},"so:1009":{"book":"soolamani","num":1009,"t":["மணிதெளித் தமைக்கப் பட்ட வண்ணமே வண்ண மாகத்","துணியமுன் கலந்து செய்த துகிலிகைத் தொழில்க ணோக்கி","அணியின தொளிக ளோவிவ் வணங்கின துருவ மோவிக்","கணிநலங் கருத லாகாக் கண்கவர் சோதி யென்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"Looking at the colours and brushwork, the queen asks whether the glow belongs to the jewels or the form, and says it is a sight past judging that robs the eye.","n":"The queen's praise turns on a doubt: she cannot tell whether the glow comes from the painted ornaments or from the form itself; the painting is praised as matching the original.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","kalvi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1009","lb":"SO1009","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["தேவி"],"ec":[],"spk":["தேவி",""],"ch":"so:45"},"so:1010":{"book":"soolamani","num":1010,"t":["பழுதிய லிலாத பாவை யுருவமோர் படியி னாலும்","எழுதுதற் கரிதி யார்க்கு மிலங்கொளி யுருவ மேனி","மொழிதலுக் கரிதா லத்தை முருகுவேய் குழலி மற்றுன்","றொழுதகை யருளி னன்றே துணிந்தியா னெழுதிற் றென்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"Madhavasenai answers that no painting can show such a form, and that she dared to draw it only because the queen's raised hand had given her the courage.","n":"The painter says the original is beyond any likeness, which is both modesty and a way to praise the bride; she places her skill below the queen's grace.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalvi","panivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1010","lb":"SO1010","mm":1,"ev":"Madhavasenai credits the queen's grace for her daring to paint what no art can match.","cl":[],"ag":["மாதவ சேனை"],"ec":[],"spk":["மாதவ சேனை","தேவி"],"ch":"so:45"},"so:1011":{"book":"soolamani","num":1011,"t":["அல்லது மடந்தை தோழி யவளது முகத்தி னாலோர்","சில்லணி மழலைச் செவ்வாய்த் திருமொழி பிறந்த துண்டு","வல்லிதன் மொழிபோய் நீரெம் மாமியா ரடிகட் கெம்வாய்","எல்லையில் கிழமை தன்னா லிறைஞ்சுக வென்ப தென்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"Madhavasenai adds that the bride's friend spoke a word for her, asking her to carry to the groom's mother their bow by right of kinship.","n":"The bride's word reaches the queen at second hand, through her friend and then Madhavasenai; the bow claims kinship before the marriage.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thoothu","sutram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1011","lb":"SO1011","mm":1,"ev":"Madhavasenai delivers the bride's greeting to the groom's mother.","cl":[],"ag":["மாதவ சேனை","நங்கை"],"ec":[],"spk":["மாதவ சேனை","தேவி"],"ch":"so:45"},"so:1012":{"book":"soolamani","num":1012,"t":["என்றவண் மொழிந்த போழ்தி னிலங்கொளிப் பலகை தன்மேல்","மின்றவழ் மேனி யாளை மென்பணைத் தோளிற் புல்லி","இன்றினி தாகு மன்றே யிருந்தவப் பயங்க ணம்பால்","ஒன்றின விளைந்த வென்றாங் கொளியினாற் புதிய ளானாள்."],"p":"","r":"The queen embraces the painted girl, says the fruit of their long austerities has ripened together today, and glows with new light.","n":"The queen reads the match as ripened tavam, a pleasant side of the karma the epic mostly shows as burden; she holds it, the verse does not weigh it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","thavam","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1012","lb":"SO1012","mm":1,"ev":"The queen embraces the portrait and counts the match a reward for past austerity.","cl":[["the merit of earlier penance ripens together in a good marriage","general","reported",3,"tivittan_mother"]],"ag":["தேவி"],"ec":[],"spk":["தேவி",""],"ch":"so:45"},"so:1013":{"book":"soolamani","num":1013,"t":["போதவி ழலரி நாறும் புரிகுழ லுருவப் பாவை","சோதிசூழ் வடிவு நம்பி சுடர்மணி வண்ணண் காண","மாதவ சேனை காட்ட வல்லையோ வென்ன வையற்","கோதுவ திவணை யன்றே யடிகள்யா முணரி னென்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"The queen asks Madhavasenai whether she can show the bright form to Manivannan, and Madhavasenai answers that it is for the lady to say, since she herself would only follow.","n":"The last two lines are close to unreadable as set: who says 'it is the lady's to tell' and who 'will follow' is open, and the reading is held loosely.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thoothu","kurippu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1013","lb":"SO1013","mm":1,"ev":"The queen asks Madhavasenai to show the portrait to her son, and Madhavasenai defers to the queen.","cl":[],"ag":["தேவி","மாதவ சேனை"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:45"},"so:1014":{"book":"soolamani","num":1014,"t":["மற்றவ டொழுது போகி மணிவண்ணன் மகிழ்ந்த கோயிற்","சுற்றிநின் றெரியுஞ் செம்பொற் சுடர்மணி வாயி னண்ணி","இற்றென விசைத்துப் புக்காங் கிளையவன் கழல்கை கூப்ப","எற்றுநீ வந்த தென்றாற் கிதுவெனா வெடுத்துச் சொன்னாள்."],"p":"","r":"Madhavasenai bows, goes to the gem-bright door of the dark-hued youth's palace, enters and folds her hands, and when he asks why she has come, begins to answer.","n":"The youth is named only by epithets (மணிவண்ணன், இளையவன்); that he is Tivittan comes from the earlier chapters, where the maiden is promised to him.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thoothu","panivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1014","lb":"SO1014","mm":1,"ev":"Madhavasenai goes to Tivittan, who asks why she has come, and she begins her reply.","cl":[],"ag":["மாதவ சேனை","செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:45"},"so:1015":{"book":"soolamani","num":1015,"t":["அருங்கல முலகின் மிக்க வரசர்க்கே யுரிய வன்றிப்","பெருங்கல முடைய ரேனும் பிறர்க்கவை பேச லாகா","இருங்கலி முழவுத் தோளா யெரிமணிப் பலகை மேலோர்","நெருங்கொளி யுருவங் கொண்டு நின்னையா னினைந்து வந்தேன்."],"p":"","r":"Madhavasenai tells the youth that rare jewels belong only to the highest kings and cannot be told of to others, and that she has come, thinking of him, with a form drawn on a bright board.","n":"She calls the portrait an அருங்கலம், a jewel that only a king may own; the reader is to take the girl's likeness as a king's possession, which is how she gains Tivittan's ear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thoothu","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1015","lb":"SO1015","mm":1,"ev":"Madhavasenai presents the portrait to Tivittan as a rare jewel fit for a king.","cl":[],"ag":["மாதவ சேனை"],"ec":[],"spk":["மாதவ சேனை","செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ch":"so:45"},"so:1016":{"book":"soolamani","num":1016,"t":["அப்படித் தாயிற் காண்பா மென்றன னரச நம்பி","மைப்புடை நெடுங்க ணாளு மருங்குநின் றவரை நீக்கிக்","கைப்புடைப் பலகை மேலாற் கன்னிய துருவங் காட்ட","மெய்ப்புடை தெரிய மாட்டான் விருந்துகொண் மனத்த னானான்."],"p":"","r":"The young lord agrees to look, the dark-eyed woman sends away those standing near and shows him the maiden's likeness painted on a board, and he, taken with the sight, cannot take in the rest of her body.","n":"The opening words 'if so, let us see by mother' have no named speaker or antecedent in this chapter, and the phrase for the second half, 'he could not make out the flank of the body', is taken to say that the picture fills his mind so that he cannot judge it; one reading only.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","kaamam","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1016","lb":"SO1016","mm":1,"ev":"The dark-eyed woman clears the room and shows the youth the maiden's portrait, and he is struck.","cl":[],"ag":["மைப்புடை நெடுங்கணாள்","அரச நம்பி"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:46"},"so:1017":{"book":"soolamani","num":1017,"t":["வானவர் மகள்கொல் விஞ்சை மாதுகொள் மண்ணு ளாள்கொல்","தேனிவர் குழலி மற்றித் திருநுதன் மடந்தை யென்ன","மானிவர் நோக்கி யன்னோர் மகளல்லண் மற்று நின்ற","ஊனிவ ரலங்கல் வேலோ யுய்த்துணர்ந் தருளு கென்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"The woman asks whether the girl in the picture is a goddess's daughter, a Vidyadhara woman or one of earth, says she is surely not an earthly daughter, and bids the spear-bearing youth work out the answer by thought.","n":"The shape of the question, a goddess or a heavenly woman or one of earth, is the same shape in which the Kural's lover first wonders at a woman; here it is asked to place her birth, not only to praise her.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","aakaasam","kurippu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1017","lb":"SO1017","mm":1,"ev":"The woman poses the three-way question about the maiden's birth and invites the youth to judge.","cl":[],"ag":["மைப்புடை நெடுங்கணாள்"],"ec":[],"spk":["மைப்புடை நெடுங்கணாள்","அரச நம்பி"],"ch":"so:46"},"so:1018":{"book":"soolamani","num":1018,"t":["மண்மிசை மகளி ரின்ன வடிவுடை யவர்க் ளில்லை","விண்மிசை மடந்தை யல்ல ளாய்விடின் விஞ்சை வேந்தன்","கண்மிசை நவிலுங் காதற் கன்னிய துருவ மாமென்","றெண்மிசை யிவரும் போழ்தி னிதுவென வவளுஞ் சொன்னாள்."],"p":"","r":"The woman says no women on earth have a form like this, and that, if she is not a sky woman, then it must be the Vidyadhara king's beloved daughter's likeness; that is what she answered as he came near to count.","n":"Line 3 'கண்மிசை நவிலுங் காதற் கன்னிய துருவம்' is read as the king's own dear maiden-form, but the grammar of lines 3 and 4 is loose and the sentence boundary of the speech is not fixed.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","aakaasam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1018","lb":"SO1018","mm":1,"ev":"The woman names the Vidyadhara king's daughter as the girl of the picture.","cl":[],"ag":["மைப்புடை நெடுங்கணாள்"],"ec":[],"spk":["மைப்புடை நெடுங்கணாள்","அரச நம்பி"],"ch":"so:46"},"so:1019":{"book":"soolamani","num":1019,"t":["கன்னிய துருவங் காளை காண்டலுங் கேடில் காமன்","பொன்னியல் கழலன் றாரன் பூட்டிய சிலைய னாகி","மன்னிய விற்கை நோக்கி மலரணி கணையு நோக்கித்","துன்னிய பொழுது நோக்கிச் சுடுசரந் தொடுக்க லுற்றான்."],"p":"","r":"As the youth looks at the maiden's picture, Kaman strung with golden anklet and flower garland looks at his bow, his flowered arrow and the hour, and begins to loose the burning shaft at him.","n":"The poet makes love's onset an act of Kaman's, done with care for bow, arrow and hour; the picture is the occasion, the god is the doer.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","kaamanoy","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1019","lb":"SO1019","mm":1,"ev":"Kaman takes up his bow and begins to shoot at the youth as he sees the picture.","cl":[],"ag":["அரச நம்பி","காமனார்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:46"},"so:1020":{"book":"soolamani","num":1020,"t":["மண்ணியல் வளாக மெல்லா மகிழ்ந்துடன் வணங்கும் போழ்தும்","உண்ணனி மகிழ்தல் செல்லா வொளியுடை யுருவக் காளை","கண்ணியற் காத லாடன் கண்ணின் னுருவங் கண்டே","வெண்ணெயின் குன்றந் தீயால் வெதும்புகின் றதனோ டொத்தான்."],"p":"","r":"Though the whole earth bows to him in joy, the handsome youth has no joy within; seeing the picture of the one he loves, he is like a hill of butter in fire.","n":"The earth's homage (line 1) is set against his emptiness (line 2); his rank and the world's worship cannot answer a desire stirred by a mere likeness.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamanoy","azhagu","kan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1020","lb":"SO1020","mm":1,"ev":"The youth sees the picture's form and melts with desire.","cl":[],"ag":["அரச நம்பி"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:46"},"so:1021":{"book":"soolamani","num":1021,"t":["மாகத்து மதிய மன்ன வாணுதன் மடந்தை தன்னை","ஆகத்து ளடக்கிப் பின்னு மணிநுத லழகு நோக்கி","நாகத்தை நடுக்கு மல்கு னங்கைதன் றிறத்துக் காம","வேகத்தை மெல்ல மெல்ல வில்வலான் பெருக்கி யிட்டான்."],"p":"","r":"The youth takes the moon-faced girl into his mind, looks again at the beauty of her forehead, and slowly lets his longing for the one whose waist shakes the cobra grow great.","n":"The epithet வில்வலான் (skilled with the bow) is taken to be the youth himself, not Kaman, whose bow was the subject of verse 1019; it could also point to Kaman.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","kaamanoy","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1021","lb":"SO1021","mm":1,"ev":"The skilled archer-youth cherishes the picture in his heart and lets his desire grow.","cl":[],"ag":["அரச நம்பி"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:46"},"so:1022":{"book":"soolamani","num":1022,"t":["குழலையான் றிருத்திக் கோதை சூட்டுவன் குறிப்புண் டாயின்","மழலைவாய் திறந்தோர் மாற்ற மருளுக மடந்தை யென்னும்","நிழலவாம் பகழி போலு நெடுங்கணோக் கென்னும் வெய்ய","அழலினா லளிய னாவி யடுவதோ வழகி தென்னும்."],"p":"","r":"The youth thinks that if she signalled he would arrange her hair and set a garland on it, and that she might open her lisping mouth to say a word; her long glance like a shadowy arrow, he fears, will kill him as in a fire.","n":"The quoted wishes end in 'என்னும்' in each verse; these are the youth's own thoughts, given by the narrator.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","ninaivu","kan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1022","lb":"SO1022","mm":1,"ev":"The youth imagines tending her hair and her speaking to him, and fears her glance.","cl":[],"ag":["அரச நம்பி"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:46"},"so:1023":{"book":"soolamani","num":1023,"t":["சீறடிப் பரடு தோயுஞ் சிலம்பிணை திருந்த வைப்பன்","வீறுடை நங்கை யென்றன் கவான்மிசை யிருத்தி யென்னும்","சேறுடைக் கோதை மேலாற் சிறந்துவார் கூந்தல் கையால்","வேறிடத் துருவல் செய்ய விரும்பிய மனத்த னானான்."],"p":"","r":"He thinks he would set her anklets on her small feet, seat her on his lap, and with his hand part her long hair on a flowered garland, and his mind longs to do this elsewhere.","n":"'வேறிடத் துருவல் செய்ய' (to do this at another place) is unclear; it is read as a wish to take the wooing elsewhere than the picture.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","ninaivu","kaamanoy"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1023","lb":"SO1023","mm":1,"ev":"The youth fancies arranging her anklets and hair in a lover's service.","cl":[],"ag":["அரச நம்பி"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:46"},"so:1024":{"book":"soolamani","num":1024,"t":["அந்துகி லசைத்த தோர்கை யவிழ்ந்தசை கின்ற தென்னும்","பைந்தளிர் மேனி தன்மேற் பன்மணிக் கலங்க டீண்டும்","செந்தளிர் புரையு மேனிச் சேயிழை திறத்திற் காம","வெந்தழல் கனல மூட்டி வில்வலான் மெலிய லுற்றான்."],"p":"","r":"He imagines a fine cloth slipping from her hand and jewels touching her soft green-leaf body, and as he kindles the hot fire of desire for the red-jewelled girl the skilled archer wastes away.","n":"Desire is a fire he lights himself ('கனல மூட்டி'), not only a wound dealt by Kaman as in 1019; both pictures stand side by side in this run.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamanoy","kaamam","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1024","lb":"SO1024","mm":1,"ev":"The youth dwells on the picture's body and wastes away with desire.","cl":[],"ag":["அரச நம்பி"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:46"},"so:1025":{"book":"soolamani","num":1025,"t":["வாளையா நெடுங்க ணல்லாண் மணவினை தொடங்குங் கால","நாளையா னமர்க ளோடு சூழ்ந்துவந் தறிவ லென்று","காளைபாற் பட்டு வெய்யோன் குடதிசைக் கனபொற் குன்றிற்","சூளிவா யருவி மாலைச் சுடர்முடிச் சென்னி சேர்ந்தான்."],"p":"","r":"He says that when the wedding business begins he will take counsel with his kin the next day and know; thus the fierce sun, going the youth's way, reached the red-gold western hill crowned with falling streams.","n":"The verse joins his promise to his kin with sunset by one sentence; the words 'காளைபாற் பட்டு' (taken to the youth's side) are not clear, and the addressee is guessed from the scene.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","kaalam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1025","lb":"SO1025","mm":1,"ev":"The youth defers the matter of marriage to the next day, and the sun reaches the western hill.","cl":[],"ag":["வெய்யோன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["அரச நம்பி","மைப்புடை நெடுங்கணாள்"],"ch":"so:46"},"so:1026":{"book":"soolamani","num":1026,"t":["விண்ணிய லுருவ வீதி மேனின்று மிழிந் வெய்யோன்","கண்ணியல் விலங்க னெற்றி கதிரென்னுங் கையி னூன்றி","மண்ணியன் மரத்தின் சாகை நுதிபிடித் தவையும் விட்டுப்","பண்ணியல் பிறிதொன் றாகிப் பையவே மறைந்து போனான்."],"p":"","r":"The sun comes down from the sky's road, leaning his forehead on the beams like a hand against a hill, lets go of the treetops he held by their tips, and changing his form slips quietly out of sight.","n":"Line 1 reads 'இழிந் வெய்யோன்', a short word probably for இழிந்து; the verse treats the sunset as a figure getting down from a high place.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aakaasam","oli","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1026","lb":"SO1026","mm":1,"ev":"The sun sets by the hill and the trees.","cl":[],"ag":["வெய்யோன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:46"},"so:1027":{"book":"soolamani","num":1027,"t":["வெய்யவ னென்னுஞ் செந்தீச் சுடரினால் வெதும்பப் பட்டு","மையொளி பரந்த போன்று கருகின திசைகண் மற்று","மொய்யழல் மேல விழ்ந்த தழன்மீள மூள்வ தேபோற்","செய்யதோ ருருவ மேல்பாற் றிசைமுகஞ் சிறந்த தன்றே."],"p":"","r":"Scorched by the red fire of the sun the directions turn dark as if smeared with black, and in the west a red form rises, like fire rekindled over a burnt pile.","n":"The dark east is set against the red west; fire and smearing images carry the whole verse and no person acts.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oli","aakaasam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1027","lb":"SO1027","mm":1,"ev":"The sky darkens as dusk falls and the western sky reddens.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:46"},"so:1028":{"book":"soolamani","num":1028,"t":["கள்ளுலாங் கழனி நீத்துக் கருங்கயல் கவுளுட் கொண்டு","புள்ளெலாங் குடம்பை சேர்ந்து பார்ப்பினம் புறந்தந் தோம்பி","உள்ளுலா வுவகை கூரத் துணைபுணர்ந் தொலித்து வைக","வள்ளலார் மனத்துக் கெஃகாய் மாலைவந் திறுத்த தன்றே."],"p":"","r":"Evening settles in: birds leave the toddy-scented fields with small fish in their cheeks and settle in their nests to feed their young, and pair with their mates in joy, while to the generous king's mind it comes like a spear.","n":"Joy of the paired birds is set against the lover's pain in line 4; 'வள்ளலார்' is a general praise word for the youth, so whose mind is meant is a matter of reading.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maalai","kaamanoy","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1028","lb":"SO1028","mm":1,"ev":"Birds return to their nests and mates at dusk while the lover feels the evening as a spear.","cl":[],"ag":["புள்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:46"},"so:1029":{"book":"soolamani","num":1029,"t":["காதலா ரகன்ற போழ்திற் கற்புடை மகளிர் போலப்","போதலொங் குவிந்த பொய்கைத் தாமரை பொலிவு நீங்க","மீதுலாந் திகிரி வெய்யோன் மறைதலுஞ் சிறுவெள் ளாம்பல்","தாதலொ மலர நக்குத் தம்மையே மிகுத்த வன்றே."],"p":"","r":"As the sun that wheels above goes out of sight, the lotuses in the pond close and lose their glow like chaste women when their beloved leaves, while the small white lilies laugh open and rise in pride.","n":"The lotus is the faithful wife, the lily the one who laughs at her; the poet gives the faithful wife the lotus and does not apply the simile to the lily.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maalai","karpu","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1029","lb":"SO1029","mm":1,"ev":"At sunset the lotuses close and the night lilies open.","cl":[],"ag":["தாமரை","வெள்ளாம்பல்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:46"},"so:1030":{"book":"soolamani","num":1030,"t":["செய்யொளிச் செக்க ரென்னுஞ் செம்புனல் பரந்து தேறி","வெய்யொளி நிறைந்த நீல விசும்பென்னு மணிகொள் பொய்கை","மையிரு ளென்னுஞ் சேற்றுள் வளர்திங்கட் கதிர்க ளென்னு","மொய்யிளங் கமல நாள வளையங்கண் முளைத்த வன்றே."],"p":"","r":"The red water of sunset has spread and cleared into a jewelled pool of blue sky, in the mud of dark night the lotus stalks of the young moon's beams shoot up like stems and rings.","n":"The whole sky is made a pond: red as its water, blue as the pond, night as its mud, moonbeams as its lotus stems.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oli","aakaasam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1030","lb":"SO1030","mm":1,"ev":"Evening red passes and moonbeams rise in the dark sky like lotus stalks.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:46"},"so:1031":{"book":"soolamani","num":1031,"t":["அங்கொளி விசும்பிற் றோன்றி யந்திவா னகட்டுக் கொண்ட","திங்களங் குழவி பால்வாய்த் தீங்கதி ரமிழ்த மாந்தித்","தங்கொளி விரிந்த வாம்ப றாமரை குவிந்த வாங்கே","எங்குள ருலகுக்கெல்லாம் மொருவரா யினிய நீரார்."],"p":"","r":"The young moon, rising in the evening sky, drinks its sweet beams like nectar and the white lilies open while the lotuses close; the poet asks where there is anyone who is sweet to the whole world as one.","n":"Line 4 is short of a clear grammar and reads as a rhetorical question; the moon is the case, a lotus the loser.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maalai","oli","naduvu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1031","lb":"SO1031","mm":1,"ev":"The moon rises, lilies open and lotuses close, and the poet asks who is pleasing to all.","cl":[["no one is agreeable to everyone: what delights one flower closes another","general","asserted",4,"narrator"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:46"},"so:1032":{"book":"soolamani","num":1032,"t":["மணவாய மல்லிகையின் மதுநனைந்து வண்கனிகண் மதர்ப்ப வீசி","இணர்வாய வனமுல்லை யிதழ்வாரி யிளந்திங்கட் கதிர்கா லூன்றித்","துணைவாய சுரும்பிரங்க வரவிந்த வனத்துதிர்ந்த துகளுஞ் சீத்துத்","திணைவாய கருங்குவளை திளைத்தசைக்குந் தென்றலுமொன் றுடைத்தே மாலை."],"p":"","r":"The evening carries jasmine honey, the early-moon's beams on forest jasmine petals, bee-hum, the dust of the lotus grove and a wind that stirs the dark blue lilies, and the poet says evening has this one thing in addition.","n":"A catalogue of evening's senses in one sentence; the verse ends with 'மாலை' as its subject.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maalai","azhagu","oli"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1032","lb":"SO1032","mm":1,"ev":"Evening is described by its scents, moonbeams and breeze.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:46"},"so:1033":{"book":"soolamani","num":1033,"t":["மைபருகு நெடுங்கண்ணார் மணிமாட மிசையிட்ட வளைவாய்ப் பாண்டில்","நெய்பருகு கொழுஞ்சுடரி னகிலாவி யிடைநுழைந்து நிழல்கால் சீப்பப்","பைபருகு மணியுமிழ்ந்து பணநாக மிரைதேரும் பருவ மாலை","கைபெருகு காமநோ யுடையவர்க்கோர் கனல்போல வருமே காணில்."],"p":"","r":"As women with kohl-dark eyes set lamps on their jewelled towers and the smoke of aloe enters between them, as cobras spit out their gems to feed, the evening comes like fire to those who suffer the desire that grows in them.","n":"The last line puts the pain in those who have desire: evening is gentle in itself, and burns only 'for those who have' the sickness.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maalai","kaamanoy","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1033","lb":"SO1033","mm":1,"ev":"Lamps are lit in the towers and evening comes as fire to the lovesick.","cl":[],"ag":["மடவார்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:46"},"so:1034":{"book":"soolamani","num":1034,"t":["கணிமிடற்ற நறவேங்கை யவிர்சுணங்கின் மடவார்தங் கைமேற் கொண்டு","பணிமிடற்று மொழிபயிற்றும் பைங்கிளியின் செவ்வழியி னிசைமேற் பாட","மணிமிடற்ற செங்கண்ண பவழக்காற் கபோதங்கண் மதலை தோறு","மணிமிடற்றி னாலகவ வனங்கனையு மனல்விக்கு மளிய மாலை."],"p":"","r":"Parrots perched on women's wrists sing in the evening mode in answer to the women's own singing, and the doves at each beam coo at their own necks, as the evening kindles desire in those who are parted.","n":"The verse is as much about the birds' songs as about the women; the sense of line 4 is held loosely.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maalai","kaamam","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1034","lb":"SO1034","mm":1,"ev":"Parrots and doves call in the evening where women sing, and the hour stirs desire.","cl":[],"ag":["மடவார்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:46"},"so:1035":{"book":"soolamani","num":1035,"t":["வெஞ்சுடர்வே லிளையவனாங் கினையனவின் மெலிவெய்த விசும்பு செல்லும்","விஞ்சையரை யன்மடமா மகணிலையா தனெவினவில் விளம்பக் கேண்மின்","பஞ்சிலங்கு தேரல்குற் பாடகக்காற் பாவையர்கள் பலர்பா ராட்டச்","செஞ்சுடரோன் மழைபொழுதிற் சினவரன்றன் றிருக்கோயில் சென்று சார்ந்தாள்."],"p":"","r":"The poet tells those who ask what happened to the young lord's pining and where the Vidyadhara girl was that, praised by the women of the court, she came at dusk to the Jina's holy temple.","n":"The poet turns from the youth to the maiden and answers a question put to the audience; line 2 ('யன்மடமா மகணிலையா தனெவினவில்') is corrupt and the identification of the girl with the portrait is by context.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["aakaasam","vazhipaadu","maalai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1035","lb":"SO1035","mm":1,"ev":"The Vidyadhara maiden arrives at the Jina's temple at dusk.","cl":[],"ag":["நங்கை"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:46"},"so:1036":{"book":"soolamani","num":1036,"t":["திண்ணிலைய மணிக்கதவந் தாழ்திறந்து திருவிளக்குத் திகழ மாட்டி","விண்ணியல நறும்புகையுங் காழகிலும் விசும்பிவர்ந்து விம்ம மூட்டிக்","கண்ணியுடன் வெறிமலரு நறும்பொடியுங் கமழ்சாந்துங் கையி னேந்திப்","பண்ணியல நரம்பிசைமேற் பரமனையே பணிமொழியாள் பரவா நின்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"She opens the firm jewelled doors, sets the bright lamps shining, makes aloe and fragrant smoke fill the sky, and carrying garlands, flowers, powder and sandal, praises the Lord with the music of strings.","n":"The ritual items are given as acts of love and skill, with music; no doctrine is stated, and the word பரமன் (the Supreme) here names the Jina of the preceding verse.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","iraivan","ozhukkam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1036","lb":"SO1036","mm":1,"ev":"The maiden lights lamps, offers incense and flowers, and sings to the Jina in the temple.","cl":[],"ag":["நங்கை"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:46"},"so:1037":{"book":"soolamani","num":1037,"t":["மணங்கமழுந் தாமரையின் மதுத்திவலை கொப்பளித்து மதர்த்து வாமன்","அணங்கிவர்சே வடியினழ கெழிலேரோ ரொளிபருகி யலரும் போலும்","அணங்கிவர்சே வடியினழ கெழிலேரோ ரொளிபருகி யலரு மாயின்","வணங்கினவ ரொளிவிரிந்து களிசிறந்து மதிமகிழன் மருளோ வன்றே."],"p":"","r":"If the lotuses, bubbling honey, bloom in drinking the beauty of the maiden's red feet, then those who bow at the Jina's feet may well bloom in light and joy and lose their reason in delight.","n":"Lines 2 and 3 repeat each other nearly word for word in this edition, probably an editorial doubling; the feet are named in the surrounding verses as the Jina's, but the line 2 phrase 'அணங்கிவர் சேவடி' could first read as the maiden's.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","thiruvadi","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1037","lb":"SO1037","mm":1,"ev":"The poet compares the opening lotus to the worshippers who bow at the Jina's feet.","cl":[["those who bow at the Jina's feet bloom in light and joy, beyond reason","jain","asserted",4,"narrator"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:46"},"so:1038":{"book":"soolamani","num":1038,"t":["அரும்பிவரு மரவிந்த மறிவரன தடிநிழல தடைந்தோ மென்று","சுரும்பிவரி யிசைபாடச் செம்மாந்து சுடருமிழ்ந்து துளும்பும் போலும்","சுரும்பிவரி யிசைபாடச் செம்மாந்து சுடருமிழ்ந்து துளும்பு மாயின்","விரும்பினராய்த் தொழுதெழுவார் மெய்ம்மறப்பு முண்மகிழ்வும் வியப்போ வன்றே."],"p":"","r":"If the bud-bearing lotuses, bees humming 'we have reached the shade of the knower's feet', are proud and tremble with light, then it is no wonder that those who bow in love lose themselves in swoon and joy.","n":"Lines 2 and 3 are near duplicates of each other as in 1037; the line 1 'அறிவரன' (the knower, the Jina) is the name that makes it a Jain worship verse.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","thiruvadi","anbu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1038","lb":"SO1038","mm":1,"ev":"The poet says that bees sing at the lotus as worshippers bow in joy at the Jina's feet.","cl":[["worshippers of the Jina's feet lose themselves in joy and are glad","jain","asserted",4,"narrator"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:46"},"so:1039":{"book":"soolamani","num":1039,"t":["அழலணங்கு தாமரையா ரருளாழி யுடையகோ னடிக்கீழ்ச் சேர்ந்து","நிழலணங்கி முருகுயிர்த்து நிரந்தலர்ந்து தோடேந்தி நிழற்றும் போலும்","நிழலணங்கி முருகுயிர்த்து நிரந்தலர்ந்து தோடேந்தி நிழற்று மாயிற்","றொழிலணங்கு மனமுடையார் சூழொளியும் வீழ்களிப்புஞ் சொல்லோ வன்றே."],"p":"","r":"The poet says that lotuses clustered at the feet of the lord of the wheel of grace seem to spread shade, fragrance and petals, and asks whether the bright joy of those whose minds are set on action is a matter of words.","n":"The stanza was printed as eight half-lines, and lines 2 and 3 are near-repeats of one another; the sentence is left unfinished or broken, so the figure of the lotuses at the Lord's feet is the only part held with some confidence.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1039","lb":"SO1039","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:47"},"so:1040":{"book":"soolamani","num":1040,"t":["மணிமரு டிருமொழி வாமன் சேவடி","அணிமரு ளுருவுடை யமிர்தின் சாயலாள்","பணிமொழி பலவுடன் பரவி வாழ்த்தினாள்","பிணிமொழி பிறவிநோய் பெயர்க வென்னவே."],"p":"","r":"The maiden praises the Jina's red feet with many humble words and prays that the disease of birth leave her.","n":"The prayer for release comes on the eve of her wedding and is soon set beside the friends' talk of love, which one of them (1049) ranks above release.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","piravi","iraivan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1040","lb":"SO1040","mm":1,"ev":"The maiden hails the Jina's feet and prays that the sickness of birth depart.","cl":[["birth is a disease from which a person asks to be freed","jain","asserted",4,"maiden"]],"ag":["நங்கை"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:47"},"so:1041":{"book":"soolamani","num":1041,"t":["வென்றவன் றிருவடி வணங்கி மெல்லவே","சென்றுதன் வளநகர்ச் செம்பொன் மாளிகை","நின்றொளி விரிவதோர் நிலவு வேதிகை","முன்றின்சென் றெய்தினாண் முகிழ்த்த வேட்கையாள்."],"p":"","r":"Having bowed to the victor's feet she goes slowly to the golden mansion of her rich city and reaches the courtyard of its moonlit terrace, her desire newly budded.","n":"The desire named in the last line sets the mood of the whole night that follows.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1041","lb":"SO1041","mm":1,"ev":"The maiden leaves her worship, walks to her mansion and reaches the moonlit terrace court.","cl":[],"ag":["நங்கை"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:47"},"so:1042":{"book":"soolamani","num":1042,"t":["செய்யவன் செங்கதிர் சுருக்கச் செக்கர்வான்","பையவே கருகலும் பரவை பாற்கதிர்","ஐயவே யவிர்கதி ரரும்பு வந்தது","வையமே தொழப்படும் வளர்வெண் டிங்களே."],"p":"","r":"As the red sun draws in his rays and the evening sky slowly darkens, the white crescent that the world worships rises from the ocean.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaalam","aakaasam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1042","lb":"SO1042","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:47"},"so:1043":{"book":"soolamani","num":1043,"t":["இருங்கயத் தெழின்மலர் நிரந்து மேலதோர்","சுருங்கையங் கவிழ்ந்தனெத் தோன்று மீன்குழாம்","அரும்பிய பசலைவா னகட்டுத் தாரகை","ஒருங்கியன் றொளிநகை யுமிழ நோக்கினாள்."],"p":"","r":"Stars crowd the pale sky like flowers spread over a deep pool, and the maiden looks at them as they seem to spill a bright smile.","n":"The sky is called pale (பசலை), the lover's own word for her sickness, so the heavens already wear her colour.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aakaasam","azhagu","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1043","lb":"SO1043","mm":1,"ev":"The maiden gazes at the first stars of the evening sky.","cl":[],"ag":["நங்கை"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:47"},"so:1044":{"book":"soolamani","num":1044,"t":["திங்களங் கொழுநனைச் சேர்ந்து தாரகை","அங்கொளி முகிழ்நகை யரும்பு மாதலான்","மங்கல மணமகன் மணந்த போதலால்","எங்குள திளையவர்க் கிளைமை யின்பமே."],"p":"","r":"The maiden thinks that the star has joined the moon, her husband, and shows a budding smile, so that this is the hour of a wedding and the pleasure of youth is for the young.","n":"Her thought is a comparison of herself with the star, whose name (தாரகை) is also the common word for a star; the verse does not name her.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","ilamai","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1044","lb":"SO1044","mm":1,"ev":"The maiden reads the star and moon as a bride and groom and longs for her own union.","cl":[],"ag":["நங்கை"],"ec":[],"spk":["நங்கை",""],"ch":"so:47"},"so:1045":{"book":"soolamani","num":1045,"t":["என்றுதன் னகம்புடை யியலக் காளையால்","ஒன்றிய வுள்ளநோ யொளிக்க லுற்றனள்","இன்றிவ ளகத்தது காம நோயெனப்","பொன்றவழ் பசலைமெய் புகல லுற்றதே."],"p":"","r":"Her young lover's image stirring within, she tries to hide the sickness in her heart, but the gold-spreading pallor of her body tells that this is the sickness of desire.","n":"The verse puts her wish to hide beside the body's betrayal, which is the way Tamil love poetry usually shows desire.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamanoy","pasalai","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1045","lb":"SO1045","mm":1,"ev":"The maiden tries to hide her longing, and her pallor gives it away.","cl":[],"ag":["நங்கை"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:47"},"so:1046":{"book":"soolamani","num":1046,"t":["ஆயின திருவனா ளாயத் தோழியர்","வேயெனத் திரண்டதோள் விளங்கு மேனியாள்","மேயின குறிப்பினை யறிந்து மெல்லவே","பாயின பணிமொழி பலவுங் கூறினார்."],"p":"","r":"Her circle of friends, with bamboo-like arms, see her inner mood and softly speak many gentle words to her.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","anbu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1046","lb":"SO1046","mm":1,"ev":"The friends read the maiden's hidden mood and speak gently to her.","cl":[],"ag":["தோழியர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:47"},"so:1047":{"book":"soolamani","num":1047,"t":["விஞ்சைய ருலகினு மினிது வீங்குநீர்","மஞ்சிவர் பொழிலணி மண்ணென் றோதினாள்","அஞ்சில மொழியவ ளொருத்தி யாங்கயர்","பஞ்சிலங் ககலல்குற் பாவை பாங்கினே."],"p":"","r":"One sweet-spoken friend says that the earth, with its swelling waters and cloud-touched groves, is sweeter than the world of the Vidyadharas, and speaks to the doll-like maiden at her side.","n":"That a Vidyadhara maiden should be told the earth is sweeter hints at a bride from one world to be married into another; the verse does not say so, and the lines are clipped.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","ilvaazhkkai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1047","lb":"SO1047","mm":1,"ev":"A friend tells the maiden that the earth is lovelier than the Vidyadharas' world.","cl":[],"ag":["தோழியர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["தோழியர்","நங்கை"],"ch":"so:47"},"so:1048":{"book":"soolamani","num":1048,"t":["பனிவரைப் பாங்கரும் பருவச் சோலையும்","தனியவர்க் கினனனி பயக்குந் தன்மனக்","கினியவ ரெவ்வழி யிசைவ ரவ்வழித்","துனிவர வில்லென வொருத்தி சொல்லினாள்."],"p":"","r":"Another friend says that the cool hillside and the seasonal grove hurt the lonely, but wherever the loved one agrees to be, no distress comes.","n":"Line 2 is hard to parse, so only its general sense, that the lonely are hurt by pleasant scenery, is taken.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","kaamanoy"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1048","lb":"SO1048","mm":1,"ev":"A second friend tells the maiden that the beloved's company removes all distress.","cl":[],"ag":["தோழியர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["தோழியர்","நங்கை"],"ch":"so:47"},"so:1049":{"book":"soolamani","num":1049,"t":["காதலார் காதன்மை கலந்து காதலர்க்","கேதிலா ரயலரா யியல்ப வாய்விடிற்","சாதலும் பிறத்தலு மிலாத தானமும்","கோதனெக் கொண்மினென் றொருத்தி கூறினாள்."],"p":"","r":"A friend says that if lovers' love mingles so that neither is a stranger to the other, even the state without death and birth should be taken as husk.","n":"The claim is a friend's lover's boast in a love scene; a Jain work would not teach it as doctrine, and the verse sits eight verses after the maiden's own prayer to be free of birth.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","veedu","piravi","anbu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1049","lb":"SO1049","mm":1,"ev":"A third friend tells the maiden that the love of a united pair outweighs even release.","cl":[["the union of loving partners is worth more than the state of release from death and birth","general","reported",3,"friends"]],"ag":["தோழியர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["தோழியர்","நங்கை"],"ch":"so:47"},"so:1050":{"book":"soolamani","num":1050,"t":["திணைவிராய்ப் பொய்கையுந் திகிரிப் புள்ளினுக்","கிணையிராப் பிரிந்தபி னெரியொ டொக்குமாற்","றுணைவராற் றனியவர் திறத்துச் சொல்லினோர்","புணைவராம் படியவ ரில்லைப் பொன்னனீர்."],"p":"","r":"Without their mates the birds of the pond are like a fire to the separated, and no one will come to speak for women left alone by their husbands.","n":"The reading rests on the uncertain syntax of lines 1-2: the pond and the bird parted from its mate are taken as a figure of separation, but the word division is doubtful.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","kaamanoy"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1050","lb":"SO1050","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["தோழியர்","நங்கை"],"ch":"so:47"},"so:1051":{"book":"soolamani","num":1051,"t":["முல்லையின் முருகுகொப் புளித்து மூரல்வாய்","மல்லிகை யிணர்த்துணர் மயக்கு மாருதத்","தெல்லியு மிளம்பிறைக் கதிரு மென்பவான்","மெல்லிய லவர்களை மெலிவு செய்யவே."],"p":"","r":"The breeze that mixes the scent of mullai and jasmine, the evening and the young moon's beams are said to waste the slender women.","n":"The friends build a list of the evening's agents of torment, as the poets of love always do, though the maiden is not yet parted from anyone.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["melivu","kaamanoy","maalai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1051","lb":"SO1051","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["தோழியர்","நங்கை"],"ch":"so:47"},"so:1052":{"book":"soolamani","num":1052,"t":["விரைசெறி புரிகுழல் வேற்க ணங்கைதன்","புரைசெறி கடிவினை நாளைப் போழ்தனெ","முரைசெறி யிமிழிசை முழங்கக் கேட்டனன்செறி","மறுகிலென் றொருத்தி கூறினாள்."],"p":"","r":"One friend says that she heard in the crowded street a drum proclaiming that the maiden's wedding rite will be held tomorrow.","n":"The speaker is taken to be a friend by the same use of ஒருத்தி as in 1047 to 1049; the line division is clipped.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["ilvaazhkkai","kaalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1052","lb":"SO1052","mm":1,"ev":"A friend tells the maiden that the wedding drum has been sounded for tomorrow.","cl":[],"ag":["தோழியர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["தோழியர்","நங்கை"],"ch":"so:47"},"so:1053":{"book":"soolamani","num":1053,"t":["நாளைநா ளென்பது நனித்துஞ் சேய்த்தனெ","வாளையா நெடுங்கணீர் மயங்கி யென்னையிக்","கோளையாம் விசும்பிடைக் குளிர்வெண் டிங்களார்","தாளையாம் வணங்குபு தாழ்ந்து கேட்டுமே."],"p":"","r":"A speaker says that tomorrow is very far, and with eyes brimming proposes to bow at the cool white moon's feet and ask something of it.","n":"What is asked of the moon is lost in the lines' broken grammar, and the speaker may be the maiden herself speaking through her friends.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaalam","kaamanoy","aakaasam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1053","lb":"SO1053","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["தோழியர்","நங்கை"],"ch":"so:47"},"so:1054":{"book":"soolamani","num":1054,"t":["காமனுங் கணைப்பயன் கொண்டு கண்களால்","நாமுநன் னல்வினை நுகரு நாளவாய்","யாமமிங் கொருங்குட னகல வென்று போய்","வாமன்ற னகருழை வரங்கொள் வாங்கொலோ."],"p":"","r":"The speaker says that Kama has had the use of his arrows through the eyes, that this is the day to enjoy good deeds, and asks whether they may go to the Jina's city for a boon as the night watches pass.","n":"The word வாமன் has appeared for the Jina in 1040; here the identity of the city and of the speaker is not fixed, so the reading is held loosely.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vinai","oozh","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1054","lb":"SO1054","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["a day of joy is the day on which earlier good deeds are enjoyed as their fruit","general","reported",2,"friends"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["தோழியர்","நங்கை"],"ch":"so:47"},"so:1055":{"book":"soolamani","num":1055,"t":["இன்னன நகைமொழி யின்பக் கோட்டியோ","டன்னமென் னடையவ ளமர வாயிடை","மன்னவற் கேழிசைத் தெழுந்த தூரியம்","கன்னியுங் கடிகம ழமளி யேறினாள்."],"p":"","r":"After such laughing talk in the company of her friends, the swan-gaited maiden climbs onto her fragrant bed as the king's seven-note instruments sound.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inbam","ilvaazhkkai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1055","lb":"SO1055","mm":1,"ev":"The maiden joins her friends' talk, hears the royal music and goes to her scented bed.","cl":[],"ag":["நங்கை"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:47"},"so:1056":{"book":"soolamani","num":1056,"t":["மல்லிகை மணங்கமழ் மாலை போகலும்","பல்லிய மவிந்தன பரந்த பாற்கதிர்","மெல்லவே மெல்லவே சுருங்கி வீங்குநீர்","எல்லைசென் றொளித்ததவ் விளவெண் டிங்களே."],"p":"","r":"When the jasmine-scented evening has passed and the instruments are still, the moon's milky rays slowly shrink and the young white moon sinks beyond the swelling sea.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaalam","aakaasam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1056","lb":"SO1056","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:47"},"so:1057":{"book":"soolamani","num":1057,"t":["நல்வினை கழிதலு நலியுந் தீவினை","செல்வதே போலிருள் செறிந்து சூழ்ந்தது","பல்வினை மடிந்தன படர்ந்த தாயிடை","வல்வினைக் கயவரே வழங்குங் கங்குலே."],"p":"","r":"As good deeds pass away and the harassing evil deeds come in, so the darkness thickens, and the many acts stop; the night belongs only to those of strong evil deeds.","n":"The karma saying is used as a figure for darkness, not as the verse's subject; it recalls the saying that a spent store of merit lets evil enter, and the poet gives it without argument.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","kaalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1057","lb":"SO1057","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["when good deeds are spent the evil deeds that oppress a person take their place","general","asserted",1,"narrator"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:47"},"so:1058":{"book":"soolamani","num":1058,"t":["மாடவாய்ச் சுடரொளி மழுங்கி மங்கையர்","ஆடுவார் முழவங்கண் ணயர்ந்த யாழொடு","பாடுவார் பாணியுஞ் சுருங்கி நன்னகர்","ஆடுநீர்க் கடற்றிரை யவிந்த தொத்ததே."],"p":"","r":"Lights in the upper rooms dim, the dancing, drums and lutes tire and the singers' beat shrinks, and the fine city grows hushed like a sea whose waves have fallen.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaalam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1058","lb":"SO1058","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:47"},"so:1059":{"book":"soolamani","num":1059,"t":["மடந்தையர் முலைமுக மடுத்த மார்பினர்","அடைந்துதே னுறங்கிய வலங்கன் மாலையார்","மிடைந்ததோ டழூஉப்பிணை நெகிழ மெல்லவே","இடங்கழித் தொழிலொழிந் திளையர் துஞ்சினார்."],"p":"","r":"The young men, their chests pressed to women's breasts and their garlands full of bees, loosen their clasp and fall asleep, their pleasures ended past all limit.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","ilamai","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1059","lb":"SO1059","mm":1,"ev":"The young men finish their night's pleasures and sleep.","cl":[],"ag":["இளையவர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:47"},"so:1060":{"book":"soolamani","num":1060,"t":["மதுக்கடை நறும்பிழித் திவலை நாவளைத்","தொதுக்கமும் வெறியயர் களனு மூடுலாய்ச்","செதுக்கமும் பலிபெறு தெருவுந் தேர்ந்துபோய்க்","கதுப்புகுந் துறங்குபு கழுதுஞ் சோர்ந்தவே."],"p":"","r":"The spirits that roam by night, having visited the toddy-shops, the frenzy-dance grounds and the streets where offerings are laid, are at last tired and asleep.","n":"The sense of line 3 and of the verb of 4 turns on rare words, and a reader should hold the reading loosely.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["iravu","kal","veri"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1060","lb":"SO1060","mm":1,"ev":"The night spirits tire after their rounds and settle to sleep.","cl":[],"ag":["கழுது"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:47"},"so:1061":{"book":"soolamani","num":1061,"t":["இடஞ்சிறை யிளம்பெடைக் கீந்து பார்ப்பின்மேற்","றடஞ்சிறை வலத்தது கோலித் தாந்தம","தொடுங்குதாண் முடங்கவைத் துறங்கு கின்றன","புடங்கொள்பூம் பொழிலிடைப் புள்ளின் சேவலே."],"p":"","r":"In the flowering grove the cock birds give the left wing to the young hen and spread the right over the chicks and sleep with their legs folded.","n":"The birds close the night's pictures with a family at rest, in contrast to the maiden's own wakeful longing.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["anbu","iravu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1061","lb":"SO1061","mm":1,"ev":"The cock birds shelter their mates and chicks as they sleep.","cl":[],"ag":["புள்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:47"},"so:1062":{"book":"soolamani","num":1062,"t":["கனைந்ததங் காதலிற் கனவிக் கண்டிறந்","தினைந்தபோன் றிடையிடை நோக்கி யின்குரல்","புனைந்தகம் புணர்பெடை புல்லி மெல்லவே","அனந்தருண் முரன்றன வன்றிற் சேவலே."],"p":"","r":"In the dark before dawn the male anril birds, swollen with longing, gaze at their mates now and then as if they had seen a dream, then embrace them and call softly.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","punarchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1062","lb":"SO1062","mm":1,"ev":"The male anril birds cry softly beside their mates as night ends.","cl":[],"ag":["அன்றிற் சேவல்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:48"},"so:1063":{"book":"soolamani","num":1063,"t":["மன்னிய மணித்தடத் தாம்பல் வாய்குடைந்","தின்னியன் மாருத மியங்குங் கங்குல்வாய்க்","கன்னியுங் காளையு மொழியக் காரிரு","டுன்னிய வுலகெலாந் துயில்கொண் டிட்டதே"],"p":"","r":"As the night wears on, the winds that stir the water-lily ponds blow, the bride and groom are gone, and the whole world sunk in thick darkness has fallen asleep.","n":"The couple are named only as கன்னியுங் காளையும், the maid and the youth, and the verse says they are away without saying where.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["iravu","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1063","lb":"SO1063","mm":1,"ev":"The world sleeps while the wedded pair have withdrawn.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:48"},"so:1064":{"book":"soolamani","num":1064,"t":["நள்ளிரு ளிடையது நடப்ப வைகறை","புள்ளிமி ழிசையொடு புகுந்து போம்வழித்","தெள்ளிய மதியவன் செய்த தீமைபோன்","மெள்ளவே கனையிருண் மெலிவு சென்றதே."],"p":"","r":"Midnight passes and dawn comes in with birdsong, and the thick darkness thins slowly, like the harm done by the bright moon going.","n":"The simile makes the fading dark the moon's own fault departing; the sense of தீமை here is not sure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaalam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1064","lb":"SO1064","mm":1,"ev":"Night gives way to dawn.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:48"},"so:1065":{"book":"soolamani","num":1065,"t":["கிளர்த்தன கிலுகிலுப் பரவப் புட்குழாம்","வளர்த்தன மகரயாழ் மருளி யின்னிசை","தளர்த்தன கருங்கடற் றரங்கத் தன்னமே","விளர்த்தது குணதிசை வேலை வட்டமே."],"p":"","r":"At dawn the birds sound, the swans, startled by the crocodile-lute, give up their sweet notes, and the eastern horizon over the sea turns pale.","n":"","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaalam","oli"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1065","lb":"SO1065","mm":1,"ev":"Birds and swans wake and the east grows pale.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:48"},"so:1066":{"book":"soolamani","num":1066,"t":["ஆணைசெய் தரசுவீற் றிருப்ப வாயிடைக்","கோணைசெய் குறும்புகூர் மடங்கு மாறுபோற்","சேணுயர் திகிரியான் கதிர்சென் றூன்றலும்","பாணியாற் கரந்தன பரந்த சோதியே."],"p":"","r":"The sun's rays plant themselves in the sky and the spread light hides itself by the hand, as small chiefs turn back when a king rules by command.","n":"The simile sets the sun as a king whose command makes petty rivals draw back; it is one of the poet's royal comparisons for nature.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["arasu","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1066","lb":"SO1066","mm":1,"ev":"The sun rises and the other lights recede.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:48"},"so:1067":{"book":"soolamani","num":1067,"t":["விளித்தன புலரிவெண் சங்கம் வேரியாற்","களித்தன கயமலர்த் தொழுதி யம்மலர்","ஒளித்துமுன் னுறங்கிய வொலிவண் டார்த்தன","தெளித்தது செறிபொழிற் றேம்பெய் மாரியே."],"p":"","r":"At daybreak the white conches call, lotuses revel in honey, bees that slept hidden in the flowers hum, and honey-rain falls on the dense grove.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaalam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1067","lb":"SO1067","mm":1,"ev":"The morning wakes the conches, lotuses and bees.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:48"},"so:1068":{"book":"soolamani","num":1068,"t":["தூண்டிய சுடர்விளக் கன்ன கன்னியோ","டாண்டகை யழல்வலஞ் செய்யு மாரணி","காண்டகை யுடைத்தது காண்டு நாமென","ஈண்டிய கதிரவ னுதய மேறினான்."],"p":"","r":"The sun climbs in a crowd of rays, saying that he would see the bride, bright as a lit lamp, circling the fire with her bridegroom.","n":"The sun is given a wish: to see the fire-circling of the wedding. This ties the sunrise to the wedding that the chapter's name and the next verses lay out.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["karpu","kurippu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1068","lb":"SO1068","mm":1,"ev":"The sun rises to watch the marriage rites.","cl":[],"ag":["வெய்யோன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:48"},"so:1069":{"book":"soolamani","num":1069,"t":["உருகின பனிபொதி பாறை யுக்கநீர்","பருகின பகலவன் பரவை வெங்கதிர்","கருகின கயம்வள ராம்பல் கண்கொளப்","பெருகின திசைமுகம் பெயர்ந்த தொத்தவே."],"p":"","r":"As the sun drinks the water, snow melts from the rocks, hot rays burn the lilies on the pools and the quarters grow wide, as if the face of the world turned.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaalam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1069","lb":"SO1069","mm":1,"ev":"Day grows hot and the pools wither.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:48"},"so:1070":{"book":"soolamani","num":1070,"t":["நிறைந்தார் கடிநல் வினைநீ தியினால்","அறைந்தாங் கதுசென் றதுசென் றவகை","சிறந்தா ளொடுகா ளைதிறத் துரையா","மறந்தா மதுவல் லவர்சொல் லுகவே."],"p":"","r":"The poet says that the rites of the wedding were completed by good law, and leaves the account of what happened to the groom and bride to those who can tell it.","n":"The verse is a narrator's turn: the marriage is done and he declines to describe its detail. The word division is damaged.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","kurippu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1070","lb":"SO1070","mm":1,"ev":"The wedding rites are completed, and the poet leaves the telling of them.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:48"},"so:1071":{"book":"soolamani","num":1071,"t":["குருமா மணிவேய் குடைமும் மையுடைப்","பெருமா னடிபே ணியபூ சனைநாள்","கருமால் களியா னைகண்மேற் கனபொன்","அருமா முரசார்ப் பவறைந் தனரே."],"p":"","r":"On the day of worship of the feet of the lord who has three umbrellas, drums were beaten on dark elephants carrying heavy gold.","n":"Whom the 'lord of three umbrellas' (the Arhat) is, is taken from the epic's setting; the verse gives only the title and the drums.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","iraivan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1071","lb":"SO1071","mm":1,"ev":"Drums are beaten on elephants to announce the worship day.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:48"},"so:1072":{"book":"soolamani","num":1072,"t":["முரசும் முழவின் குழுவும் முடிசேர்","அரசன் னகரின் குழுவுங் கெழுமி","விரையும் புகையும் மலரும் பலியின்","இரையுந் நெடுவீ திநிறைத் தனவே."],"p":"","r":"The streets of the royal city fill with drums, crowds of drummers, incense, smoke, flowers and the food of the offering.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1072","lb":"SO1072","mm":1,"ev":"The city's streets fill with festival sounds and offerings.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:48"},"so:1073":{"book":"soolamani","num":1073,"t":["பிடியுங் களிறும் பிறவுந் நெரிவுற்","றடியும் மிடலா மிடமின் றிலகும்","கொடியுங் குடையுங் குளிர்சா மரமும்","முடியின் சுடரும் மிசைமூ டினவே."],"p":"","r":"Mares, elephants and others crush together, flags, parasols, whisks and crown-light cover the sky, and there is no room even to tread.","n":"","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["padai","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1073","lb":"SO1073","mm":1,"ev":"Crowds, animals and standards fill the streets.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:48"},"so:1074":{"book":"soolamani","num":1074,"t":["வழுவின் னெறிவா மனமா நகர்வாய்","விழவின் னணமா கவிதித் தனராய்க்","கழுவும் மணிபோல் பவடன் கடிநாள்","எழுவும் முரசெங் குமியம் பினவே."],"p":"","r":"The people of the city, kept in the road without fault, adorn it for the festival, and drums rise at once for the wedding day.","n":"","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1074","lb":"SO1074","mm":1,"ev":"The city is adorned for the wedding day.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:48"},"so:1075":{"book":"soolamani","num":1075,"t":["நடைமா லைநடந் ததுநந் திமுகம்","புடைமா லைபுகுந் தனர்புண் ணியநீர்","இடைமா லைநிகழ்ந் ததொரேத் தரவம்","கடைமா லைநிகழ்ந் ததுகாப் பணியே."],"p":"","r":"The procession lines move by stages, from the Nandi face to the holy water, the afternoon, the chants and the guard's duty.","n":"The verse works as a list of the day's stages named by the word மாலை; the exact sense of each stage is held loosely.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaalam","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1075","lb":"SO1075","mm":1,"ev":"The wedding ceremony proceeds in set stages through the day.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:48"},"so:1076":{"book":"soolamani","num":1076,"t":["திரைசங் கொலியோ டுசிறந் தனபோன்","முரைசங் கொலியோ டுமுழங் கியெழசங் கொலியோ டுணரா வகையால்","அரைசங் கொலியோ டெழுவார்த் தனவே."],"p":"","r":"Sounds of the sea, of drums and of the court rise together in so great a din that none can tell the one from the other.","n":"The verse has only three lines in the printed text, so a line may be lost; the sense is held loosely.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["oli","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1076","lb":"SO1076","mm":1,"ev":"Sea, drums and court rise in one noise.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:48"},"so:1077":{"book":"soolamani","num":1077,"t":["துணிமுத் தநகைத் துவர்வா யிளையார்","கணிமுத் தணிசிந் தியகண் விரவி","மணிமுத் தமணற் றிடலா கிமறைத்","தணிமுத் துமிழ்வீ தியடுக் குநவே."],"p":"","r":"Young women with pearl-white laughter and red lips shed pearls from their eyes, which mix with the sand and fill the streets.","n":"","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","maalai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1077","lb":"SO1077","mm":1,"ev":"Young women's pearls fill the sand of the street.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:48"},"so:1078":{"book":"soolamani","num":1078,"t":["துகிலார் கொடிபொங் கினதொங் கனிமிர்ந்","தகிலார் புகையா வியடுத் தமையாற்","பகலா னொடுவந் திரவும் பகலே","இகலா துடனா கியியைந் துளவே."],"p":"","r":"Banners rise, incense smoke fills the air, and night joins day so that night and day agree in the city.","n":"","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","kaalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1078","lb":"SO1078","mm":1,"ev":"Incense and flags turn night to day.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:48"},"so:1079":{"book":"soolamani","num":1079,"t":["பலர்மன் னியபா டலுமா டலுமே","வலமன் னிமயங் கிமுயங் குதலால்","நிலமன் னவருந் நெடுமால் வரைமேற்","குலமன் னவருங் குளிர்தூங் கினரே."],"p":"","r":"Songs and dances stood together, and kings of the earth and kings of ancient clans slept in the cool on the great mountain.","n":"","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["arasu","mannar"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1079","lb":"SO1079","mm":1,"ev":"Kings sleep on the mountain after the songs and dances.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:48"},"so:1080":{"book":"soolamani","num":1080,"t":["அகல்வா னிடையங் கிழிவா னவரும்","முகிலா றிழிவிஞ் சையரும் முடுகிப்","பகலா னொடுதா ரைபரந் தனபோற்","புகலா ரொளிபோந் ததுபொன் னகரே."],"p":"","r":"Sky-dwellers and Vidyadharas descend in crowds from the heavens, and the golden city shines like stars scattered with the sun.","n":"","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["aakaasam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1080","lb":"SO1080","mm":1,"ev":"Sky-dwellers and Vidyadharas come down to the golden city.","cl":[],"ag":["விஞ்சையர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:48"},"so:1081":{"book":"soolamani","num":1081,"t":["வடமே ருமுகட் டலரும் மலரும்","புடமே ருகலத் தலரும் மலருந்","தடமே ருளபொன் னவிழ்தா மரையும்","திடமே வியவிஞ் சையர்சிந் தினரே."],"p":"","r":"The Vidyadharas look with joy at the golden lotuses opening on the northern peak and in the pond.","n":"","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","aakaasam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1081","lb":"SO1081","mm":1,"ev":"The Vidyadharas look at golden lotuses.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:48"},"so:1082":{"book":"soolamani","num":1082,"t":["அணிவேண் டினர்கொள் ளவடுத் தனவும்","மணிவேண் டினர்கொள் ளவகுத் தனவும்","கணிவேண் டினநாள் கழியுந் துணையும்","பணிவேண் டினர்வீ திபரந் தனவே."],"p":"","r":"The streets spread with goods for the festival: jewels laid out for those who wish them, gems cut for those who wish them, and gifts of cloth.","n":"","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["selvam","eegai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1082","lb":"SO1082","mm":1,"ev":"Goods are laid out in the streets for the festival.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:48"},"so:1083":{"book":"soolamani","num":1083,"t":["நறவுண் டொருபா னகுவா ரொருபாற்","புறவுண் டகலா வமிர்தம் புணர்வார்","உறவுண் டமரத் தொருபா லுறைவார்","பிறவுண் டையுமின் னனபின் னினவே."],"p":"","r":"Some drink toddy and laugh, others take in the food of the gods, others dwell with kin; so the streets filled with such groups.","n":"","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["inbam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1083","lb":"SO1083","mm":1,"ev":"Different crowds feast and gather in the streets.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:48"},"so:1084":{"book":"soolamani","num":1084,"t":["இழிகின் றனர்விஞ் சையரெத் திசையும்","பொழிகின் றதுபொன் மழையும் மழையுட்","சுழிகின் றதுதொல் சனவெள் ளமதற்","கொழிகின் றதுநா முரையா ததுவே."],"p":"","r":"Vidyadharas pour down from every quarter, a rain of gold falls, and in the rain a whirl of ancient crowds rises, which is not told here.","n":"The last line says the speaker does not tell more; it ends the chapter on a promise that the full account is omitted.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["aakaasam","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1084","lb":"SO1084","mm":1,"ev":"Vidyadharas come down and a rain of gold falls on the festival.","cl":[],"ag":["விஞ்சையர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:48"},"so:1085":{"book":"soolamani","num":1085,"t":["தாமரை முகத்தம னியக்குட மவற்றாற்","சாமரை முகத்தன மதக்களிறு தம்மேற்","பூமரை முகத்தெறி புனற்றிரை முகந்து","தூமரை முகத்தரசர் சென்றுபலர் சூழ்ந்தார்."],"p":"","r":"Kings with lotus-bright faces gather in numbers, riding war elephants whose fly-whisks and flower-wreathed foreheads are splashed by the waves of water thrown over them.","n":"","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["mannar","perumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1085","lb":"SO1085","mm":1,"ev":"Kings come to the wedding in crowds on their elephants.","cl":[],"ag":["அரசர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:49"},"so:1086":{"book":"soolamani","num":1086,"t":["வண்டுபடு மாலையர் மடப்பிடிக டம்மேன்","கொண்டய லுழைக்கல மழைக்கண்மட வார்கள்","எண்டிசையு மெங்குமிட மின்றிமிடை வுற்றார்","கண்டவரை மேல்விரவு கார்மயிலொ டொத்தார்."],"p":"","r":"Women with rain-cloud eyes and flower garlands crowd every direction on their cow-elephants, leaving no room, and look like dark peacocks spread over those who see them.","n":"","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","perumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1086","lb":"SO1086","mm":1,"ev":"Women ride in to the wedding and fill the roads.","cl":[],"ag":["மடவார்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:49"},"so:1087":{"book":"soolamani","num":1087,"t":["மாளிகை நிரைத்த மணி மாடநகர் முன்னால்","ஆளியர சேந்துமணி யாசன மதன்மேற்","காளைகழல் வேந்தர்பலர் சூழ்தர விருந்தான்","நாளொடு பொலிந்தநகை மாமதிய மொத்தான்."],"p":"","r":"The lord sits on a gem seat under the lion-throne in front of the jewelled mansions, with warrior kings around him, like the full moon among stars.","n":"The word காளை, the youth, is taken to name the groom as in the earlier chapters; the verse does not name him.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","mannar"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1087","lb":"SO1087","mm":1,"ev":"The groom sits enthroned among kings before the wedding bath.","cl":[],"ag":["அரச நம்பி"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:49"},"so:1088":{"book":"soolamani","num":1088,"t":["அங்கண யிராவண மிரண்டுட னெடுத்த","மங்கல மணிக்கலச நீர்சொரிய வாடிப்","பொங்குதிரை யொன்றிரு புயற்பொழிய வேந்தித்","தங்குபுனல் பெய்ததட மால்வரையொ டொத்தான்."],"p":"","r":"The youth bathes as auspicious pots are lifted and the water poured over him, and looks like a great mountain over which rain-clouds have emptied.","n":"The word division is broken in line 1 and the edition's தம் உடன் reading is held loosely.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["theerththam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1088","lb":"SO1088","mm":1,"ev":"Pots of water are poured over the groom in the wedding bath.","cl":[],"ag":["அரச நம்பி"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:49"},"so:1089":{"book":"soolamani","num":1089,"t":["மன்னர்கள் சொரிந்தமணி நீரருவி யாடிப்","பின்னைமலர் மாரியகல் வானினவர் பெய்ய","மின்னவிர் விளங்குசுடர் வேல்விடலை யாடிப்","பொன்னணி புனைந்துபுது வேள்விநகர் புக்கான்."],"p":"","r":"After the kings' pouring of jewelled water and a rain of flowers from the sky, the youth puts on gold ornaments and enters the new city of the sacrifice.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["theerththam","aaguthi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1089","lb":"SO1089","mm":1,"ev":"The groom finishes his bath, dresses and enters the wedding city.","cl":[],"ag":["அரச நம்பி","அரசர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:49"},"so:1090":{"book":"soolamani","num":1090,"t":["மங்குன்மழை சூழுமணி மால்வரையின் மேலார்","கங்கைமுத னீரருவி கொண்டுகலி வானம்","எங்குமிட மின்றியெழில் விஞ்சைய ரிழிந்தார்","நங்கைமண நீரணியை நாமொழிவ தென்னோ?"],"p":"","r":"The Vidyadharas come down from the heights with water from the Ganga and other streams for the bride's bath, so thickly that the sky has no room, and the poet asks what more need be said.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["theerththam","perumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1090","lb":"SO1090","mm":1,"ev":"Vidyadharas descend with holy waters for the bride's bath.","cl":[],"ag":["விஞ்சையர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:49"},"so:1091":{"book":"soolamani","num":1091,"t":["ஆறுகுல மால்வரையின் மேலருவி நீரும்","வீறுபெறு மெல்லியலை யாட்டியபின் மீட்டு","நாறுமலர் நந்தன வனத்தனவொ டெல்லா","வேறுபடு பூமழையு மாடவிளை வித்தார்."],"p":"","r":"Water from the six great mountains' streams bathes the delicate bride, and then a rain of flowers of every kind is made to fall on her.","n":"","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["theerththam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1091","lb":"SO1091","mm":1,"ev":"The bride is bathed with mountain water and flowers.","cl":[],"ag":["விஞ்சையர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:49"},"so:1092":{"book":"soolamani","num":1092,"t":["சாந்துசொரி மாரிபொழி கின்றதகை யோடும்","தேந்துவலை வீசியுளர் கின்றதொரு தென்றல்","வேந்தருல கோபிறிதொ ரூழிகொலி தென்று","மாந்தர்மருள் வாருமகிழ் வாருமுள ரானார்."],"p":"","r":"A sandal rain pours and a wind carries drops of honey, so that the people are bewildered whether this is a king's world or another age and some are amazed and some glad.","n":"","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","perumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1092","lb":"SO1092","mm":1,"ev":"Sandal-rain and a scented breeze fall as the bride is dressed.","cl":[],"ag":["நகர்மாந்தர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:49"},"so:1093":{"book":"soolamani","num":1093,"t":["மங்கல வனப்பினதொர் கோடிமடி தாங்கி","அங்கொலி விசும்பினவர் தந்தவணி சேர்த்திப்","பங்கய முகத்தவர் பலாண்டிசை பராவச்","செங்கய னெடுங்கணவள் வேள்விநகர் சேர்ந்தாள்."],"p":"","r":"Dressed in the gifts of the sky-dwellers and praised with songs, the red-eyed bride reaches the sacrifice city.","n":"Her title is only நங்கை and the red-eyed one, as elsewhere in this chapter; the verse never names her.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","perumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1093","lb":"SO1093","mm":1,"ev":"The bride, adorned, comes to the wedding hall.","cl":[],"ag":["நங்கை"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:49"},"so:1094":{"book":"soolamani","num":1094,"t":["நங்கைமண வேள்விநக ரெய்துவதன் முன்னைப்","பொங்குபுரி நூலனலர் தாமரை புனைந்தான்","மங்கல வுழைக்கல நிரைத்தமண மாடம்","அங்கது புகுந்தழல் வளர்க்கிய வமைந்தார்."],"p":"","r":"Before the bride arrives, the sacred-thread priest adorns the wedding hall and the rest arrange to tend the fire there.","n":"","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["aaguthi","andhanar"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1094","lb":"SO1094","mm":1,"ev":"The priest and the others prepare the hall and the fire before the bride arrives.","cl":[],"ag":["அந்தணன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:49"},"so:1095":{"book":"soolamani","num":1095,"t":["சாந்துமெழு கிட்டதட மாமணி நிலத்தைச்","சேர்ந்துதிகழ் பொன்னியல் சலாகைநுதி தீட்டிப்","பேர்ந்துமொரு கால் விரையி னான்மெழுகு வித்தான்","ஆய்ந்தமறை யோதியத னாரிட மறிந்தான்."],"p":"","r":"The priest has the gem floor smeared with sandal and wax, marks it with a golden rod, has it smeared again with fragrance, and learns its measure by reciting the Veda.","n":"The edition's வுலகு/அதனாரிடம் word division is broken in line 4, so the reading of ஆரிடம் is held loosely.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vedham","andhanar"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1095","lb":"SO1095","mm":1,"ev":"The priest prepares and marks the ground for the fire.","cl":[["the ground of the rite must be prepared and its measure learned by reciting the Veda","vedic","reported",4,"narrator"]],"ag":["அந்தணன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:49"},"so:1096":{"book":"soolamani","num":1096,"t":["பருத்தமணி முத்தமண லாற்சதுர மாகத்","திருத்தியொரு பால்பணிய வைத்ததிடர் சூழத்","தருப்பையி னுனித்தலை வடக்கொடு கிழக்காய்ப்","பரப்பின னதற்குமொரு பாவனை பயின்றான்."],"p":"","r":"He lays out a square of pearl-sand, ringed with raised ground, and spreads darbha grass on it with the tips toward the north and east, and performs a rite over it.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vedham","andhanar"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1096","lb":"SO1096","mm":1,"ev":"The priest lays out the square of sand and spreads darbha.","cl":[["the sacred grass is laid with its tips to the north and east for the rite","vedic","reported",3,"narrator"]],"ag":["அந்தணன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:49"},"so:1097":{"book":"soolamani","num":1097,"t":["நான்முகன் வலத்தவ னிடத்துமொரு காவல்","மேன்முக மிருந்துகுண பால்வெறுவி தாகப்","பான்முறை பயின்றபரு திக்கடிகை பாய்த்தித்","தான்முறையி னோதுசமி தைத்தொழுதி சார்ந்தான்."],"p":"","r":"The priest sets guards for the four-faced one's right and the quarters, spreads the sacrificial sticks and, in the due order, sits to recite and tend them.","n":"Lines 1 and 2 are hard; the lines about the four-faced Brahma's right side may belong to the rite's layout and the sense is not fixed.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vedham","aaguthi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1097","lb":"SO1097","mm":1,"ev":"The priest sets the sacrificial sticks and begins the fire-rite.","cl":[],"ag":["அந்தணன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:49"},"so:1098":{"book":"soolamani","num":1098,"t":["ஆயிடை நடுக்கட லுளானமர ராசான்","ஏயுடைய னாயசுர மந்திரி யெழுந்தான்","போயுடை விசும்பின்மதி யும்புகுது கின்றான்","மேயுடை யணிந்தகணி வேலையிது வென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"The lord of the gods, the minister of the asuras and the moon are said to enter the sky, and the astrologer declares that this is the hour with the garland of the ocean.","n":"Line 1 names நடுக்கடலுளான், the one in mid-ocean, and அமரராசான், but the identity of each figure and the speaker are only inferred from context.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaalam","vedham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1098","lb":"SO1098","mm":1,"ev":"The auspicious hour is declared.","cl":[],"ag":["அந்தணன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:49"},"so:1099":{"book":"soolamani","num":1099,"t":["அங்குமுன் வளர்த்தவழ லேகடவு ளாக","மங்கையை மணக்குழுவின் முன்னைவரை வேந்தன்","கொங்குவிரி தாரவற்கு நீரொடு கொடுத்தான்","நங்கையொடு நாண்மலரு ளாளையு மடுத்தான்."],"p":"","r":"Before the fire the hill-king gives the bride to the groom with water, and the lotus-born lady comes with her.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ilvaazhkkai","aaguthi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1099","lb":"SO1099","mm":1,"ev":"The bride's father gives her to the groom, pouring water, before the fire.","cl":[["the fire raised for the rite stands as the god before whom the bride is given","vedic","reported",1,"narrator"]],"ag":["வேந்தன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:49"},"so:1100":{"book":"soolamani","num":1100,"t":["மன்னியழல் வேள்வியி லவற்குவல மாகப்","பின்னிய தருப்பைகள் பிடித்தவை விடுத்தாங்","கன்னமனை யாளொடயில் வேலவ னிருந்தான்","கன்னியொ டியைந்தகதிர் மாமதிய மொத்தான்."],"p":"","r":"With kusa grass braided and held at his right side, the spear-bearing youth sits before the fire beside the maiden, like the radiant moon beside the maid.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ilvaazhkkai","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1100","lb":"SO1100","mm":1,"ev":"The groom sits beside the bride at the fire.","cl":[],"ag":["அரச நம்பி","நங்கை"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:49"},"so:1101":{"book":"soolamani","num":1101,"t":["கந்துளுமி ழுங்கரிய சூழ்புகைகள் விம்ம","வந்துசுட ரேந்திவல னேசுழல மாட்டி","அந்தணனு மங்கழ லமைத்துமிக வேட்டான்","மைந்தனு மடந்தையை மனத்தின்மிக வேட்டான்."],"p":"","r":"Smoke from the fire rises like a dark cloud as the priest tends it and offers into the flame, while the youth in his own mind offers himself to the girl.","n":"The verse pairs the priest's burning offering with the groom's desire; the same word வேட்டான் is used of both, once for the sacrifice and once for the wish.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aaguthi","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1101","lb":"SO1101","mm":1,"ev":"The priest feeds the sacrificial fire as the groom longs for the bride.","cl":[],"ag":["அந்தணன்","அரச நம்பி"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:49"},"so:1102":{"book":"soolamani","num":1102,"t":["பொங்கழல்செய் வேள்விமுறை போற்றலு மெழுந்தான்","அங்கையி னணங்கினணி மெல்விரல் பிடித்து","மங்கையொடு காளைவல னாகவரு கின்றான்","கங்கையொடி யைந்துவருகார்க் கடலொடொத்தான்."],"p":"","r":"Once the fire-rite has been honoured, the youth rises, takes the soft fingers of the girl and walks with her on his right, like the dark sea joined with the Ganga.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ilvaazhkkai","aaguthi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1102","lb":"SO1102","mm":1,"ev":"The groom takes the bride's hand and walks around the fire.","cl":[],"ag":["அரச நம்பி"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:49"},"so:1103":{"book":"soolamani","num":1103,"t":["கைம்மலரின் வீரனவள் கைமுகிழ் பிடிப்ப","மெய்ம்மயி ரெறிந்துமணி வேர்நுத லரும்பிச்","செய்யதன சீறடிகள் செவ்வனிட மாட்டா","அம்மயிலி னாணதனை யாவரறை கிற்பார்."],"p":"","r":"As the hero takes her flower-hand she trembles with hair on end and sweat on her brow and cannot set her red feet down, and the poet asks who can tell the shyness of the peacock-like girl.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naanam","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1103","lb":"SO1103","mm":1,"ev":"The bride shakes and perspires as the groom clasps her hand.","cl":[],"ag":["அரச நம்பி","நங்கை"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:49"},"so:1104":{"book":"soolamani","num":1104,"t":["பின்னுமுள வேள்விமுறை சென்றபல பேசி","என்னையவை நிற்கவெரி யோம்புதொழி லார்க்குப்","பொன்னொடு மணிக்குவியல் போந்துபொழி கின்றார்","கன்னிதமர் காளைதம ரென்றிவர் கலந்தே."],"p":"","r":"More rites go by and many things are said, and the kin of the girl and of the youth, mingled together, pour out heaps of gold and gems to the priests who keep the fire.","n":"","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["eegai","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1104","lb":"SO1104","mm":1,"ev":"The two families give gold and gems to the fire-priests.","cl":[],"ag":["தமர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:49"},"so:1105":{"book":"soolamani","num":1105,"t":["அங்கமிரு மூன்றுமறை நான்கலகில் கற்பம்","இங்குமுடி விஞ்சையென வின்னகரை கண்ட","பொங்கெரிய வேள்விவல்பு ரோகித னவற்குச்","சங்கநிதி யென்னநெதி மாரிதரு வித்தார்."],"p":"","r":"The chief priest, skilled in the six limbs, four Vedas and countless ritual books, is made to receive a rain of wealth like the conch treasure.","n":"","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["eegai","vedham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1105","lb":"SO1105","mm":1,"ev":"The expert priest is given a rain of wealth.","cl":[["the priest learned in the limbs, the four Vedas and the kalpa deserves the gift of wealth","vedic","reported",1,"narrator"]],"ag":["அந்தணன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:49"},"so:1106":{"book":"soolamani","num":1106,"t":["வானநெறி யெங்கும்வளர் சோதிவட மீனைக்","கானமயி லன்னவடன் முன்னைநனி காட்ட","யானுமிவள் போலுலகு காணவியல் வேனோ","ஈனமொடு நாணமில னோவென விகழ்ந்தாள்."],"p":"","r":"One shows the peacock-like bride the Vadameen star shining in the sky, and she scorns herself, asking whether she, shameless and lowly, can look at the world as that star does.","n":"The speaker is taken to be the bride from இகழ்ந்தாள், and the meaning of the self-reproach is not settled; the star is Arundhati only by the ritual's usual order, not by the verse's word.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["naanam","karpu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1106","lb":"SO1106","mm":1,"ev":"The bride is shown the star and reproaches herself.","cl":[],"ag":["நங்கை"],"ec":[],"spk":["நங்கை",""],"ch":"so:49"},"so:1107":{"book":"soolamani","num":1107,"t":["எனமண வினைகள் செல்ல வின்னண மெய்து கென்று","புனவரை கான முன்னி மாதவம் போகு வாரும்","இனமலி செல்வ மற்று மிப்படி வருவ துண்டோ","கனவினு மென்ன மாந்தர் கண்டுமீள் வாரு மானார்."],"p":"","r":"As the wedding rites go on, some who see such wealth go off to the forest to practise great penance, and others, wondering whether it ever comes even in a dream, turn back.","n":"The verse stops at the departures and does not say that the penance is a judgment on the wedding; the 'dream' phrase is a spoken wonder, not a doctrinal claim.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thuravu","selvam","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1107","lb":"SO1107","mm":1,"ev":"Onlookers either leave for the forest to do penance or turn home amazed.","cl":[["the wealth of the wedding is so great that it may be taken for a dream and so is to be left for the forest penance","general","reported",2,"city_people"]],"ag":["நகர்மாந்தர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:49"},"so:1108":{"book":"soolamani","num":1108,"t":["இடிபடு முரசிற் சாற்றி யேற்பவ ராசை தீரச்","சுடர்விடு மணியின் மாரி பொன்னொடு சொரிய வேவிக்","கடிபடு நெடிய மாடங் கன்னியோ டேறி னானால்","முடிவுகொ ளுலக மெய்து மின்பமா மூர்த்தி யென்பான்."],"p":"","r":"At the drum's proclamation he has jewels and gold showered on petitioners until their wishes are filled, then climbs the tall guarded mansion with the maid, and the poet calls him the form of the joy the world at its end attains.","n":"The giving comes first and the climb to the bride's bed second, so the wedding night opens with the king's bounty before it turns private; the last line's praise of the youth is compressed.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["eegai","inbam","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1108","lb":"SO1108","mm":1,"ev":"He has gold and jewels rained on petitioners at a drum's proclamation, then climbs the tall mansion with the maid.","cl":[],"ag":["அரச நம்பி","நங்கை"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:50"},"so:1109":{"book":"soolamani","num":1109,"t":["கழுமிய காக துண்டங் கமழ்தொறுங் காள மேகம்","குழுமிய தனைய மாடக் குவட்டிடை யமளி சேர்வார்","விழுமலர்ப் பிணைய லாளும் விடலையு மேரு நெற்றிச்","செழுமணிச் சிலைசென் றேறுந் தெய்வமா மிதுன மொத்தார்."],"p":"","r":"The garlanded woman and the youth take their couch at the top of the mansion, where the smoke of burning fragrant wood gathers like dark cloud, and they look like a divine pair mounting a jewelled peak.","n":"Reading காக துண்டம் as a burning aromatic wood is my inference from the verse's cloud and fragrance images.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1109","lb":"SO1109","mm":1,"ev":"The couple go up to their couch on the mansion's roof.","cl":[],"ag":["அரச நம்பி","நங்கை"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:50"},"so:1110":{"book":"soolamani","num":1110,"t":["பொன்னியல் கொடியி னொல்கிப் பூவணைப் பொருந்தும் பாவை","கன்னிநா ணொடுக்கங் காளை கண்களி கொள்ள நோக்கிப்","பின்னவ ளொடுங்க வாங்கிப் பெருவரை யகலஞ் சேர்த்தி","இன்னகை மழலை கேட்பா னென்கொலோ வென்று சொன்னான்."],"p":"","r":"The shy girl lies down on the flower bed, he gazes till his eyes are glad, draws her as she shrinks and presses her to his broad chest, and says he longs to hear her sweet laughing lisp.","n":"The spoken words are given only as 'என்கொலோ என்று', so what he asks is only half stated.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","naanam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1110","lb":"SO1110","mm":1,"ev":"The youth draws the shrinking girl to his chest and asks to hear her voice.","cl":[],"ag":["அரச நம்பி","நங்கை"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:50"},"so:1111":{"book":"soolamani","num":1111,"t":["நின்றநா ணென்னுங் கன்னிச் சிறைவிண்டு காளை திண்டோட்","குன்றினாற் செறிக்கப் பட்ட குமரிநீ ரமிர்த யாறு","சென்றுதேன் பகர்ந்து செம்பொற் கலஞ்சிந்தித் திளைத்து விம்மி","அன்றவ னார்வ வெள்ளக் கடலிடை யழுந்திற் றன்றே."],"p":"","r":"The cage of her shame breaks open as his mountain-like arms close on her, and she, like a nectar-river, sinks in the sea of his ardour.","n":"Shame is made a cage (சிறை) from which she is released, and the one who sinks is the river, not the sea; the figure of river and sea is compressed and several details are read loosely.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["naanam","punarchi","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1111","lb":"SO1111","mm":1,"ev":"The maiden's shame gives way and she is taken into the youth's embrace.","cl":[],"ag":["அரச நம்பி","நங்கை"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:50"},"so:1112":{"book":"soolamani","num":1112,"t":["பருவத்தா லரும்பிப் போதாய்ப் பையவே யலர்ந்து முற்றி","மருவித்தேங் கனிகொண் டுள்ளான் மனங்கொள வளிந்த காமத்","திருவொத்த களியின் றீஞ்சா றாரவுண் டார மாட்டார்","உருவத்தா லிருவ ராகி யுள்ளத்தா லொருவ ரானார்."],"p":"","r":"Like a bud that by its season opens, ripens and bears fruit, they come to the sweet food of desire and cannot take enough of it, and are two in body but one in heart.","n":"The ripening bud-flower-fruit image makes the union a growth over time and not an event of one night; the last line states the oneness of heart as the result.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["punarchi","kaamam","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1112","lb":"SO1112","mm":1,"ev":"The couple consummate their love and are joined in heart.","cl":[],"ag":["அரச நம்பி","நங்கை"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:50"},"so:1113":{"book":"soolamani","num":1113,"t":["உலவிய வலங்கன் மாலை யொளிமல ருடைய மார்பிற்","குலவிய மணிமுத் தோடுங் குவிமுலைக் குவடு பாயக்","கலவியுங் களிப்புங் காமப் புலவியுங் கலந்து சால","நிலவிய மதுவுண் டார்போ னெஞ்சுண மயங்கி யிட்டார்."],"p":"","r":"His garland and her pearl strings tangle on his chest, and union, delight and playful sulking blend until they fall into a daze like drinkers of honey-wine.","n":"புலவி is named in one list with union and delight, with no quarrel and no cause given, so the sulk here is part of the pleasure and not a breach.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["punarchi","oodal","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1113","lb":"SO1113","mm":1,"ev":"The couple lose themselves in union and play.","cl":[],"ag":["அரச நம்பி","நங்கை"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:50"},"so:1114":{"book":"soolamani","num":1114,"t":["அடிக்கலந் திருத்தி யம்மென் புரிகுழற் சுருளை நீவி","முடிக்கல ரணிந்து காதன் முகிழ்நகை முகிழ்ப்பப் புல்லிக்","கடிக்கணி யாய காளை கவான்மிசை யிருத்திக் காமர்","குடிக்கணி யாய கொம்பு கேட்கமற் றிதனைச் சொன்னான்."],"p":"","r":"He sets her ornaments right, smooths her coiled hair, crowns her with a flower, embraces her as her smile buds, seats her on his lap and speaks the words that follow so she may hear.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","anbu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1114","lb":"SO1114","mm":1,"ev":"The youth adorns the bride, seats her on his lap and begins to speak to her.","cl":[],"ag":["அரச நம்பி","நங்கை"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:50"},"so:1115":{"book":"soolamani","num":1115,"t":["செங்குவளை நாறுந் திருமேனி செவ்வாயும்","அங்குவளை யீரிதழு மாம்பலுமே நாறுமால்","இங்கிவளை முன்படைத்த தேவனென் னின்னுயிரைப்","பைங்குவளை மாலையாட் காளாய்ப் படைத்தானே."],"p":"","r":"He says her body smells of the red lily and her red mouth of the same and of the water lily, and that the god who first made her made his life her servant.","n":"The maker (தேவன்) as the cause of his bondage is a lover's way of speaking, a small echo of fate in a verse of praise; no claim about karma is made.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","oozh","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1115","lb":"SO1115","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["அரச நம்பி"],"ec":[],"spk":["அரச நம்பி","நங்கை"],"ch":"so:50"},"so:1116":{"book":"soolamani","num":1116,"t":["அன்புருகு காமத் தீமாட்டி யளியவென்","என்புருக வேவ விணைநெடுங்கட் பூம்பாவை","முன்பெருகு முந்நீ ரமுதாய் விளைத்தினிய","மின்பருகு நுண்ணிடையார் மெல்லுருவங்கொண்டதே."],"p":"","r":"He says the doe-eyed doll lit the fire of melting love that burns his bones, and that she is the sweet nectar the old sea bore, taken as a slim form.","n":"Fire and nectar are said of the same woman, the paradox that verse 1118 develops.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamanoy","kaamam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1116","lb":"SO1116","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["அரச நம்பி"],"ec":[],"spk":["அரச நம்பி","நங்கை"],"ch":"so:50"},"so:1117":{"book":"soolamani","num":1117,"t":["பான னெடுங்க ணிவையே பகழியா","வேனலுடை வேந்தன் வென்றிக்கு நோற்றானே","யானு மளியற்றே னித்துணையோர் காலமும்","தேனார் நறுமேனி தீண்டுதற்கு நோற்றேனே."],"p":"","r":"He says her long eyes are the arrows with which the lord of the hot season won, that Kama must have done austerity for his victory, and that he himself has done austerity for this long to touch her fragrant body.","n":"நோற்றான் and நோற்றேன் are the words of vows and penance; here a lover turns them to the wish to touch his bride, which a Jain reader would hear against the epic's own austerities. Verse 986 already gave Prajapati's words that the winner of such a bride must have great earlier austerity.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thavam","kaamam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1117","lb":"SO1117","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["union with the beloved is the reward of austerity done before","general","reported",4,"kaalai"]],"ag":["அரச நம்பி"],"ec":[],"spk":["அரச நம்பி","நங்கை"],"ch":"so:50"},"so:1118":{"book":"soolamani","num":1118,"t":["காவியா கின்ற கருமா மழைக்கண்ணி","மேவியா னுண்ணு மமிர்தாய் விருந்தாகி","ஆவியா கின்றா ளருமருந்து மாகின்றாள்","பாவியேன் பாவைக்கோ ராளேயா கின்றேனே."],"p":"","r":"He says the dark-eyed girl has become the nectar he eats as a guest, his very life and his rare medicine, while he, a sinner, has become servant to a doll.","n":"பாவியேன் is the lover's self-reproach in play and not a charge of wrongdoing; the same woman is feast, life and medicine at once.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","uyir","marundhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1118","lb":"SO1118","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["அரச நம்பி"],"ec":[],"spk":["அரச நம்பி","நங்கை"],"ch":"so:50"},"so:1119":{"book":"soolamani","num":1119,"t":["செம்பவழ மேய்ப்பத் திகழ்ந்திலங்கு சீறடியின்","வம்பழகு நோக்கி வழிபடுவ தேசாலும்","அம்பவழ வாயு மளகஞ்சேர் வாணுதலும்","எம்பவமோ நுங்க யாமெம்மை யறியேமே."],"p":"","r":"He says it would be enough to look at her coral-red small feet and worship them, but at the sight of her red mouth and curl-crowded brow he loses himself and cannot say why it is his sin.","n":"வழிபடுவது, the word of worship, is used for her feet; the last line is hard and its sense is uncertain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","vazhipaadu","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1119","lb":"SO1119","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["அரச நம்பி"],"ec":[],"spk":["அரச நம்பி","நங்கை"],"ch":"so:50"},"so:1120":{"book":"soolamani","num":1120,"t":["வண்டே மடந்தை மணியைம்பான் மேவியிருந்","துண்டே யெனநுடங்கு நுண்மருங்கு நோவியீர்","தண்டேன் காணீருந் தளிர்மேனி நாற்றத்தாற்","பண்டேபோல் வந்து பயிலாது போமினே."],"p":"","r":"He tells the bees that sat in her braided hair and troubled her slim waist not to come to her tender body's fragrance as before.","n":"The middle lines are compressed, and who is hurt by whom is read from the context of a bridegroom guarding his bride.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1120","lb":"SO1120","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["அரச நம்பி"],"ec":[],"spk":["அரச நம்பி","வண்டு"],"ch":"so:50"},"so:1121":{"book":"soolamani","num":1121,"t":["கள்ள மடநோக்கி தன்னைக் கரந்தனெ","துள்ளத்தின் வைப்பி னுருவ மதுகாணேன்","மெள்ளவென் றோளணைவா ளென்னும் விருப்பாரா","தெள்ளு மனத்தினுக் கெய்திற் றறியேனே."],"p":"","r":"He says the sly-eyed girl has hidden herself, and when he tries to hold her in his heart he cannot see her form, and he does not know what his unsatisfied longing has come upon.","n":"Even with her in his arms he looks for her in his heart; the closing lines are corrupt or compressed.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","nenju","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1121","lb":"SO1121","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["அரச நம்பி"],"ec":[],"spk":["அரச நம்பி","நங்கை"],"ch":"so:50"},"so:1122":{"book":"soolamani","num":1122,"t":["காதலால் வந்தென் கவான்மே லிருப்பினும்","ஏதிலாள் போலு மிமைப்பி னிமையாதே","போதுலாம் வாண்முகமே நோக்கிப் பொலிவேனென்","மாதரா ணாணும்யான் வாழுமா றோரேனே."],"p":"","r":"He says that even when she sits lovingly on his lap she looks like a stranger each time he blinks, so he will gaze unblinking at her flower-like face, and he knows not how he will live while his beloved is shy.","n":"Her shame still stands after the embrace of 1111, and he reads it as a distance he fears.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","naanam","kan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1122","lb":"SO1122","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["அரச நம்பி","நங்கை"],"ec":[],"spk":["அரச நம்பி","நங்கை"],"ch":"so:50"},"so:1123":{"book":"soolamani","num":1123,"t":["உலம்பா ராட்டுந் தோளவ னொண்பூங் குழலாளை","நலம்பா ராட்டி நாகிள முல்லை நகுவிக்கும்","வலம்பா ராட்டி வந்ததொர்மா ரிப்புய லொத்தான்","குலம்பா ராட்டுங் கொம்புமொர் முல்லைக் கொடி யொத்தாள்."],"p":"","r":"He, with shoulders like a rock, praises her beauty and is like a monsoon storm that makes the young jasmine open, and she like the jasmine vine that is the pride of her line.","n":"The two similes cast the couple as storm and flower, with the storm as the active one.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1123","lb":"SO1123","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["அரச நம்பி","நங்கை"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:50"},"so:1124":{"book":"soolamani","num":1124,"t":["தேனார் கோதைச் செங்கயல் வாட்கண் சிறைகொள்ள","ஊனார் வேலா னுள்ள மிழந்தா னுழையாரை","மேனாள் போல மெய்ப்பட மாட்டான் விளையாடும்","கானார் சோலைக் காவகப் புக்கான் கமழ் தாரான்."],"p":"","r":"Her flower-garlanded, carp-like eyes take him captive, and the spear-bearing youth, his mind lost, no longer behaves with his attendants as before and goes into the pleasure grove.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","kan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1124","lb":"SO1124","mm":1,"ev":"The youth, lost in her eyes, enters the play grove.","cl":[],"ag":["அரச நம்பி"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:50"},"so:1125":{"book":"soolamani","num":1125,"t":["தண்டாரீன்று செந்தளி ரேந்தித் தழல்பூத்த","வண்டார் பிண்டி வார்தளிர் நீழன் மணிவட்டங்","கண்டாங் கேறிக் காரிகையோடு விளையாடிப்","பண்டான் கொண்ட பாவையார் பாடலிசை கேட்டான்."],"p":"","r":"Under the red asoka whose tender leaves hang with bees, he climbs onto a jewelled round seat, plays with his beloved and hears the music sung by the women.","n":"பிண்டி, the asoka, is the tree under which the Jina is said to have won omniscience in Jain tradition, so the play takes place in the Jina's own shade; the last line's relation of the women to him is unclear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","kaamam","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1125","lb":"SO1125","mm":1,"ev":"In the grove he plays with the bride and listens to the singing women.","cl":[],"ag":["அரச நம்பி","நங்கை"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:50"},"so:1126":{"book":"soolamani","num":1126,"t":["வரைவேந்தன் மடமகளை மணியேர் மேனிநிறங் கொண்டு","விரையேந்து தளிரீனல் விழையாய் வாழி தேமாவே","விரையேந்து தளிரீனில் வேனிற் றென்ற லலர்தூற்ற","நிரையேந்து வடுநீயே படுதி வாழி தேமாவே."],"p":"","r":"The singers tell the sweet mango not to put out sprouts that borrow the colour of the mountain king's daughter, for in the spring wind it will bear scars and rows of fruit and be plucked.","n":"வடு means both young fruit and scar, and the warning may play on both; the speakers are named only in 1129.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","ilamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1126","lb":"SO1126","mm":1,"ev":"The young women sing to the mango tree, warning it not to rival the bride's colour.","cl":[],"ag":["இளையவர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["இளையவர்","தேமா"],"ch":"so:50"},"so:1127":{"book":"soolamani","num":1127,"t":["அடிமருங்கி னரசிறைஞ்ச வாழியாள்வான் பெருந்தேவி","கொடிமருங்கி னெழில்கொண்டுகு ழையல்வாழி குருக்கத்தி","கொடிமருங்கி னெழில்கொண்டு குழைவா யாயிற் பலர்பறிப்பக்","கடிமருங்கிற் புக்கலரே காண்டி வாழி குருக்கத்தி."],"p":"","r":"The singers tell the kurukkatti creeper, long may it live, not to take the grace of the vine-waisted chief queen of the wheel-ruler whom kings bow to, for if it does many will pluck it and it will not bloom in the guarded garden.","n":"The bride is called the great queen of the lord who rules the wheel, so the song already gives her the title Prajapati's words at 986 foretold.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1127","lb":"SO1127","mm":1,"ev":"The singers warn the kurukkatti creeper not to match the queen's grace.","cl":[],"ag":["இளையவர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["இளையவர்","குருக்கத்தி"],"ch":"so:50"},"so:1128":{"book":"soolamani","num":1128,"t":["வணங்கி வையந் தொழநின்ற மன்னன் காதன் மடமகள்போல்","மணங்க ணாறும் பூம்பாவை வளரல் வாழி நறுங்குரவே","மணங்க ணாறும் பூம்பாவை வளர்த்தி யாயி லிளையராற்","கணங்க ளோடு பறிப்புண்டி கண்டாய் வாழி நறுங்குரவே."],"p":"","r":"The singers tell the fragrant kurava tree not to grow as lovely as the loving daughter of the king whom the world bows to, for if it does the young men will pluck it in crowds.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","ilamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1128","lb":"SO1128","mm":1,"ev":"The singers warn the kurava tree that being as fragrant as the bride will bring it plucking.","cl":[],"ag":["இளையவர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["இளையவர்","நறுங்குரவு"],"ch":"so:50"},"so:1129":{"book":"soolamani","num":1129,"t":["இன்னண மிளையவர் பாடக் கேட்டலும்","மன்னவன் மடமகண் முகத்து வாணிலா","மின்னியோ ரணிநகை முகிழ்த்து மீள்வது","கன்னவி றோளவன் கண்கொண் டிட்டவே."],"p":"","r":"Hearing the young women sing, a smile like moonlight flashes for a moment on the king's daughter's face, and the youth's eyes drink it in.","n":"The smile is not said to answer the song, only to follow it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1129","lb":"SO1129","mm":1,"ev":"The bride smiles at the song and the youth's eyes take it in.","cl":[],"ag":["இளையவர்","நங்கை","அரச நம்பி"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:50"},"so:1130":{"book":"soolamani","num":1130,"t":["உழையவ ரடிமுதல் பரவ வொண்சுடர்க்","குழையவ ளொளிமனங் கவரக் கோடுயர்","மழைதவழ் மதலைய மாட மேறினான்","முழையம ரரியர சனைய மொய்ம்பினான்."],"p":"","r":"As attendants bow at his feet and the bright-earringed bride's radiance holds his mind, the lion-like youth climbs the cloud-brushing pillared mansion.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1130","lb":"SO1130","mm":1,"ev":"The youth goes back up to the tall mansion.","cl":[],"ag":["மைந்தர்","அரச நம்பி"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:50"},"so:1131":{"book":"soolamani","num":1131,"t":["நாவியே கமழுந் தொங்க னகைமணி வயிரப் பூணான்","ஆவியி னகத்து ளாளு மருவரை யகலத் தாளுந்","தேவியே யாக வன்ன திருவொடு மிருவ ராவான்","பூவினுண் மடந்தை வந்து புகுந்தவா றுரைக்க லுற்றேன்."],"p":"","r":"The poet opens the sarukkam by saying he will tell how the lady of the lotus, Fortune, came to enter the lord who has both the woman who rules his life and Fortune on his mountain-broad chest.","n":"The sentence is compressed: இருவராவான் can mean the lord of two consorts or one who becomes a pair with Fortune, and the lord is not named in the verse; Tivittan is the natural reading from the story so far.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["arasu","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1131","lb":"SO1131","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:51"},"so:1132":{"book":"soolamani","num":1132,"t":["கண்மிசை கனிந்த ஒற்றன்கூற்று காதற் களிப்படு செல்வ முந்நீர்","உண்மிசை யுக்கோர் நச்சுத் துள்ளிவந் துறைப்ப தேபோல்","விண்மிசை யிழிந்து வந்த வொற்றனோர் வெஞ்சொன் மாற்ற","மண்மிசை யிருந்த விஞ்சை மன்னனை வணங்கிச் சொன்னான்."],"p":"","r":"A spy comes down from the sky and bows to the Vidyadhara king who sits on earth, and his harsh word falls on the king's joy as a drop of poison leaps into one drinking from a sea.","n":"The first half-line (கண்மிசை கனிந்த) does not parse cleanly after the repair of the eight half-lines; the simile of the poison in the sea of joy is clear.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["otru","thoothu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1132","lb":"SO1132","mm":1,"ev":"A spy descends from the sky and, after bowing, tells the Vidyadhara king on earth bad news.","cl":[],"ag":["ஒற்றன்","வேந்தன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:51"},"so:1133":{"book":"soolamani","num":1133,"t":["விரைசெல லிவுளித் தேரோய் விஞ்சைய ருலக மாளும்","அரைசர்க டிகிரி யாளும் அச்சுவ கண்ட னோடும்","திரைசெல வுரறி ஞாலந் தின்னிய கடல்க ளேழுங்","கரைசெல வருவ போனம் மேல்வரக் கருது கின்றார்."],"p":"","r":"The spy tells the lord of the chariot that the kings who rule the Vidyadhara world, with the discus-lord Acuvakandan, mean to come upon them as the seven seas would break over their shores.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["otru","pagai","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1133","lb":"SO1133","mm":1,"ev":"The spy reports that Acuvakandan and the Vidyadhara kings are planning to march against Tivittan's side.","cl":[],"ag":["ஒற்றன்","அச்சுவ கண்டன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["ஒற்றன்","வேந்தன்"],"ch":"so:51"},"so:1134":{"book":"soolamani","num":1134,"t":["அரைசர்கள் வருக போக வதுபண்டே யறிந்து தன்றே","வரைசெறி சிங்க வேறு மணிவண்ண னழித்த ஞான்றே","விரைசெறி பொழில்கொள் சோலை விஞ்சைய ருலகிற் பட்ட","துரைசிறி தென்ன லோடு மொற்றனு முரைக்க லுற்றான்."],"p":"","r":"The king answers that the kings may come or go, he knew it long ago, from the day the dark-hued one destroyed the mountain lion, and the spy begins to tell what befell the Vidyadhara world.","n":"The first three lines are the king's words and the fourth is the poet's; மணிவண்ணன் (the jewel-hued one) is taken as Tivittan, who is called dark-hued elsewhere.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","kurippu","veruvu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1134","lb":"SO1134","mm":1,"ev":"The Vidyadhara king shrugs off the threat, saying he foresaw it from the lion's death, and the spy starts his account.","cl":[],"ag":["வேந்தன்","ஒற்றன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["வேந்தன்","ஒற்றன்"],"ch":"so:51"},"so:1135":{"book":"soolamani","num":1135,"t":["அருளுமா றடிகள் கேண்மோ வரியர சதனை யாரு","மருளுமா றிளைய காளை வாய்பிளந் திட்ட வார்த்தை","தெருளுமா றொருவன் சொல்லக் கேட்டலுஞ் சேணில் வாழ்வார்","வெருளுமா றுள்ள மெல்லாம் வெருண்டுமெய் விதலை கொண்டார்."],"p":"","r":"The spy bids the lord hear how, when one man told the word that the younger bull had split the lion's mouth, the sky-dwellers were so frightened that their whole bodies shook.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["veruvu","vali"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1135","lb":"SO1135","mm":1,"ev":"The Vidyadharas of the sky tremble on hearing that Tivittan has split the lion's mouth.","cl":[],"ag":["விஞ்சையர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["ஒற்றன்","வேந்தன்"],"ch":"so:51"},"so:1136":{"book":"soolamani","num":1136,"t":["விஞ்சைய ருலக மெல்லாம் வெய்துற விரிந்த மாற்றம்","அஞ்சினன் மறைத்துச் சின்னா ளமைச்சரி மஞ்சு வென்பான்","வஞ்சனைச் சீய மாய வார்கழ லவனைக் கூவி","எஞ்சலில் புகழி னானுக் கின்னண மிசைப்பித் தானே."],"p":"","r":"The news of the lion's death spreads and scorches the whole Vidyadhara world; the minister Arimanju fears it, hides it for some days, then calls the one who had become the cunning lion and has him tell it to the glorious king.","n":"The voice may still be the spy's report. வஞ்சனைச் சீயம் is taken as Arikethu, who took the lion's shape at 696 and 697; 1137 names him.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["amaichu","soozhchi","veruvu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1136","lb":"SO1136","mm":1,"ev":"Arimanju, after hiding the news, calls Arikethu to report the lion's death to Acuvakandan.","cl":[],"ag":["மஞ்சு","அரிகேது"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:51"},"so:1137":{"book":"soolamani","num":1137,"t":["சொரிகதிர் வயிரப் பைம்பூ ணரசர்கள் பலருஞ் சூழ","எரிகதி ராழி யாள்வா னினிதினங் கிருந்த போழ்தின்","அரியது கேட்க வென்ன வரிகேது வென்பா னாங்குப்","பெரியதோர் வியப்புச் சென்று பட்டது பேசி னானால்."],"p":"","r":"While the discus-lord sits happily among kings in diamond jewels, Arikethu says he has something rare to tell and reports that a great marvel has befallen.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","otru","thoothu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1137","lb":"SO1137","mm":1,"ev":"Arikethu reports the lion's death to Acuvakandan before his kings.","cl":[],"ag":["அச்சுவ கண்டன்","அரிகேது","அரசர்கள்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:51"},"so:1138":{"book":"soolamani","num":1138,"t":["கரியவன் வளைந்த வெள்ளை யெயிற்றவன் காள மேகம்","பெரியதொன் றிரண்டு கொம்மைப் பிறைகவ்வி யிருந்த தன்னான்","அரியதங் கென்னை யென்னை யெனவரி கேதுசொன்ன","உரையெதிர் கபில வட்டக் கண்ணெரி யுமிழ்ந்த மாதோ."],"p":"","r":"As Arikethu tells of the black lion with curved white fangs, like a dark cloud gripping two crescents, Acuvakandan's tawny round eyes spit fire in answer.","n":"Line 3 is broken (அரியதங் கென்னை யென்னை) and may be Arikethu's cry of wonder; the sense of line 4, the king's fiery eyes, is clear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vekuli","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1138","lb":"SO1138","mm":1,"ev":"Acuvakandan's eyes blaze as Arikethu describes the lion and its end.","cl":[],"ag":["அரிகேது","அச்சுவ கண்டன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:51"},"so:1139":{"book":"soolamani","num":1139,"t":["மடித்தவா யெயிறு கவ்வி மருங்கினோர் வயிரக் கற்றூண்","அடித்தலி னசனி வீழ வருவரை நெரிவ தேபோற்","படித்தலை நடுங்க மற்றப் பரூஉத்திரள் வயிரத் தம்பந்","தொடித்தலை சிதைந்து நுங்கத் துகளெழுந் தொழிந்த தன்றே."],"p":"","r":"With his lip folded over his fangs he strikes a diamond pillar beside him, and like a thunderbolt on a great mountain the thick pillar's head shatters and dust rises as the earth shakes.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vekuli","vali"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1139","lb":"SO1139","mm":1,"ev":"Acuvakandan smashes a diamond pillar with a blow.","cl":[],"ag":["அச்சுவ கண்டன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:51"},"so:1140":{"book":"soolamani","num":1140,"t":["விஞ்சைய ரதனைக் கண்டு மெய்பொதி ரெறிந்து விம்ம","வெஞ்சினங் கனன்று மீட்டும் விஞ்சைய னவனை நோக்கி","வஞ்சனை மனத்த ராய மனிசரை வலிய ரென்பாய்","அஞ்சினை பெரிது மேடா வென்றன னசனி யொப்பான்."],"p":"","r":"The Vidyadharas see the pillar broken and shudder with bristling hair; the king, blazing again, turns on the Vidyadhara and says that he who calls treacherous men strong is greatly afraid.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maanam","pagai","veruvu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1140","lb":"SO1140","mm":1,"ev":"Acuvakandan taunts Arikethu for fearing men.","cl":[["men of deceitful mind are not truly strong, and calling them so is cowardice","general","reported",3,"acuvakanthan"]],"ag":["அச்சுவ கண்டன்","விஞ்சையர்","அரிகேது"],"ec":[],"spk":["அச்சுவ கண்டன்","அரிகேது"],"ch":"so:51"},"so:1141":{"book":"soolamani","num":1141,"t":["நிலத்திடை மக்க ளாற்ற னின்னைப்போ லஞ்சு வார்க்கு","மலைத்துணை பெருகிக் காட்டு மற்றதிங் கெம்ம னோர்க்கோர்","இலைத்தது மில்லை மன்னோ வென்றன் னிரண்டு திங்கள்","பிலத்திடை பொடித்த போலும் பிறழ்ந்திலங் கெயிற்றி னானே."],"p":"","r":"Acuvakandan says the strength of men on earth swells to a mountain only for the timid, and that to his own kind it counts for not even a leaf, his curved fangs flashing like two moons broken out of a pit.","n":"The claim is the rival king's pride; the lion's death just told is already the evidence against it, but this verse itself does not answer it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maanam","vali","pagai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1141","lb":"SO1141","mm":1,"ev":"Acuvakandan scorns the strength of earth-men.","cl":[["the strength of earth-dwelling men looks mountain-large only to the fearful and is nothing to the strong","general","reported",1,"acuvakanthan"]],"ag":["அச்சுவ கண்டன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["அச்சுவ கண்டன்","அரிகேது"],"ch":"so:51"},"so:1142":{"book":"soolamani","num":1142,"t":["கனைகதிர்க் கடகக் கையாற் கற்றிர ளுதிர வெற்றிச்","சினவழ லெறிப்ப நோக்கிச் சிவந்தனன் றெழித்த லோடு","மனநனி மயங்கி மற்ற விஞ்சைய ரஞ்ச நின்ற","நனைமல ரலங்கற் கேது நகைகொண்ட மனத்த னானான்."],"p":"","r":"At his bracelet-bright hand the stones fall in a heap and a flame of victory-wrath flashes, he reddens and roars, the Vidyadharas lose heart, and Arikethu in his wet-flower garland is left with a mind that has taken on a smile.","n":"Line 4 gives no reason for Arikethu's smile: forced courtesy, inward scorn or relief are all open.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vekuli","veruvu","vali"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1142","lb":"SO1142","mm":1,"ev":"Acuvakandan roars and breaks the stones while the Vidyadharas fear and Arikethu smiles inwardly.","cl":[],"ag":["அச்சுவ கண்டன்","விஞ்சையர்","அரிகேது"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:51"},"so:1143":{"book":"soolamani","num":1143,"t":["அடுகுர லரச சீய மதனையோ ராம்பற் றாள்போல்","நொடிவரை யளவிற் கீறி நுனித்தது வியத்தல் செய்யாக்","கடிவரை யலங்கன் மார்பிற் காளையே பெரிய னென்று","தடவரை யனைய தோளான் றன்னுளே வியந்து நின்றான்."],"p":"","r":"Arikethu, mountain-shouldered, marvels within himself that the youth who tore the roaring lion-king in a snap, as one tears a lotus stalk, is the truly great one.","n":"The verse does not name who marvels; 1142 ends on Arikethu and 1144 has him join his hands, so he is read as the one thinking.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["perumai","maram","aanmai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1143","lb":"SO1143","mm":1,"ev":"Arikethu privately admires Tivittan's feat.","cl":[],"ag":["அரிகேது"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:51"},"so:1144":{"book":"soolamani","num":1144,"t":["இன்றிவ னனலும் போழ்தி னெதிர்நின்று கனற்றி யென்னை","சென்றவ னாற்ற றானே கண்டபின் றேறு மன்னறே","என்றுதன் மனத்தி லெண்ணி யியைந்தவா முகமன் சொல்லிக்","குன்றுடை யரசன் முன்னைக் கூப்பிய கைய னானான்."],"p":"","r":"He thinks there is no use standing against the king as he blazes today, since the king will understand only when he has seen the youth's strength himself; so he speaks fitting courtesies and stands before the lord of the hill with joined hands.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["panivu","adakkam","vekuli"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1144","lb":"SO1144","mm":1,"ev":"Arikethu holds back, flatters and stands with folded hands before Acuvakandan.","cl":[],"ag":["அரிகேது"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:51"},"so:1145":{"book":"soolamani","num":1145,"t":["அமர்நனி தொடங்கு மேனும் ஆர்த்துநீர் கொணர்மி னென்று","குமரனைக் குறித்த வெஞ்சொற் குறைசென்று முடியுமெல்லைத்","தமருளங் கொருவன் வந்து சக்கிர வாளந் தன்னுள்","நமரது நிலையு நங்கை போந்தது நடுங்கச் சொன்னான்."],"p":"","r":"As Acuvakandan finishes his harsh command that the prince be brought with a shout even if war breaks out, a man of his own people comes and, trembling, tells of their state in Chakravalam and that the maiden has gone.","n":"நமரது நிலை (the state of our people) is left vague, and நங்கை போந்தது is read with the next verse as the maiden's going to Tivittan.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["otru","arasu","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1145","lb":"SO1145","mm":1,"ev":"A kinsman interrupts Acuvakandan's command to report the maiden's departure and the state of Chakravalam.","cl":[],"ag":["அச்சுவ கண்டன்","தமருள் ஒருவன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:51"},"so:1146":{"book":"soolamani","num":1146,"t":["அடிகளிவ் வவனி தன்மே லிழிந்தது மணங்கோ டொப்பாள்","கடிவினை நிலையு மன்றே கண்டுவந் தொருவன் கூறச்","சுடுசொலிஃ தொழிக வென்று துணைச்செவி புதைத்து வல்லே","முடிமுத றுளங்கத் தூக்கி முனிவினை முடிவு கொண்டான்."],"p":"","r":"A man who has seen it says the maiden has come down to this earth as one wed; the king, crying that these burning words must stop, stops both ears, shakes his crown and settles on his anger.","n":"The wording of the news (மணங்கோடு ஒப்பாள், கடிவினை நிலையும்) is compressed; the sense that the rival has the girl he once sought is taken from 306 and 348.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","vekuli","pochchaappu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1146","lb":"SO1146","mm":1,"ev":"Acuvakandan, told that the maiden is wedded on earth, stops his ears and resolves on wrath.","cl":[],"ag":["தமருள் ஒருவன்","அச்சுவ கண்டன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["தமருள் ஒருவன்","அச்சுவ கண்டன்"],"ch":"so:51"},"so:1147":{"book":"soolamani","num":1147,"t":["பெருகினான் வெகுளி கண்ணுட் பிறந்தது பிறங்கு செந்தீ","உருகினான் போன்று தோன்ற மயிர்த்துளை யுகுத்த தெண்ணீர்","திருகினா னெயிறு செவ்வாய் கறித்தனன் றிசைக ளோடும்","அருகினோர் நடுங்க நோக்கி யழனகை டுத்து நின்றான்."],"p":"","r":"His rage swells: red fire seems to be born in his eyes, water streams from every hair-pore, he twists his fangs, bites his red lip and looks round so that all near him tremble, laughing like fire.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vekuli","vali"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1147","lb":"SO1147","mm":1,"ev":"Acuvakandan's rage shows in his body.","cl":[],"ag":["அச்சுவ கண்டன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:51"},"so:1148":{"book":"soolamani","num":1148,"t":["மலைகளை மறித்து மற்றோர் மறிகட னடுவ ணிட்டவ்","வலைதிரை மகர முந்நீ ரதுவிது வாக்கு வேன்கொல்","உலகினை யுள்ளங் கைக்கொண் டுருளையா வுருட்டி யிட்டென்","பலபுனை மடந்தை தன்கீழ்ப் பதித்திடு வேன்கொ லென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"He boasts that he might turn the mountains over and cast them into the sea to make it something else, or roll the world in his palm like a ball and set it beneath his much-adorned maiden.","n":"Who the 'many-adorned maiden' is, the girl he wanted or another, is not stated.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vali","perumai","maanam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1148","lb":"SO1148","mm":1,"ev":"Acuvakandan voices boasts of overturning mountains and rolling the world.","cl":[],"ag":["அச்சுவ கண்டன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["அச்சுவ கண்டன்",""],"ch":"so:51"},"so:1149":{"book":"soolamani","num":1149,"t":["அனன்றனன் றவைகள் பேசி யமையுமவ் வரசர் தீமை","மனங்கொளப் படுவதாயின் மணிவரை யுலகின் வாழுஞ்","சனங்களைத் திரட்டிப் பின்னைத் தக்கதொன் றறிவ னென்றான்","சினங்கெழு காலன் மற்றோர் காலன்மேற் சிவந்த தொப்பான்."],"p":"","r":"Still blazing, he says that if the kings' wrong is to be taken to heart he will gather the people of the gem-mountain world and only then decide what fits; the angry king glows like Death angry at another Death.","n":"Which kings' wrong is meant by அரசர் தீமை is not fixed; it is read as the rival side's.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vekuli","soozhchi","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1149","lb":"SO1149","mm":1,"ev":"Acuvakandan decides to assemble his people before deciding his course.","cl":[],"ag":["அச்சுவ கண்டன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["அச்சுவ கண்டன்",""],"ch":"so:51"},"so:1150":{"book":"soolamani","num":1150,"t":["சிறந்தெரி யனலோ டொப்பான் பணிகொண்டு திசைக ளோடி","அறைந்தனர் முரசிற் சாற்றி யறைதலு மரச ரெல்லாம்","மறைந்தன வுலக மென்ன மாய்ந்தன திசைக ளென்னப்","பறந்தனர் விசும்பு போர்ப்பக் கடற்படை பரப்பி வந்தார்."],"p":"","r":"Blazing like fire, he sends messengers to every quarter; they proclaim the call by drum, and the kings, as if the world and the quarters had vanished, fly in with the sea-armies they have spread to cover the sky.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thoothu","padai","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1150","lb":"SO1150","mm":1,"ev":"Acuvakandan's heralds summon the kings, who arrive with their armies.","cl":[],"ag":["அறைந்தனர்","அரசரெல்லாம்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:51"},"so:1151":{"book":"soolamani","num":1151,"t":["மற்றவ ரடைந்து வெய்யோன் மலரடி வணங்கி நின்று","செற்றவ னிருந்த வாறுஞ் செங்கண்டீ யுமிழ்ந்த வாறும்","எற்றிய வயிரத் திண்டூ ணெரிந்திடை கிடந்த வாறும்","உற்றன பிறவு நோக்கி யுள்ளங்க ணடுங்கி யிட்டார்."],"p":"","r":"The arrived kings bow at the sun-like lord's flower feet, and as they look at how the angry one sits, how his red eyes spat fire and how the struck diamond pillar lies burned, their hearts shake.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["veruvu","vekuli","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1151","lb":"SO1151","mm":1,"ev":"The summoned kings see Acuvakandan's rage and the ruined pillar and tremble.","cl":[],"ag":["அரசரெல்லாம்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:51"},"so:1152":{"book":"soolamani","num":1152,"t":["சுரியுளைத் துளைக ளாவி விடுகின்ற சுழலுஞ் செங்கண்","கருமுகி னுடங்கு மின்போற் புருவங்கண் முரிந்து நீங்கான்","பெரியதோர் முனிவு கொண்டான் பிறையெயிற் றரியோ டொப்பான்","எரியினுள் விளிய லுற்றார் யார்கொலோ வளிய ரென்பார்."],"p":"","r":"Seeing his red eyes spit fire and his brows knotted like lightning in a black cloud, never loosed from wrath like a fanged lion, the kings ask who the wretches are who are to die in the fire.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vekuli","veruvu","kootru"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1152","lb":"SO1152","mm":1,"ev":"The kings murmur about whom his anger will burn.","cl":[],"ag":["அரசரெல்லாம்"],"ec":[],"spk":["அரசரெல்லாம்",""],"ch":"so:51"},"so:1153":{"book":"soolamani","num":1153,"t":["ஞாலமேற் றிரிந்து நாளு முயிர்களை நடுங்கப் பார்க்குங்","காலனைக் கதம்பட் டான்கொ லன்றெனிற் கற்ப மாள்வார்","மேலெனக் கிருப்பார் போலு மெனவெகுண் டனல்கின் றான்கொல்","வேலைநீ ருலகின் மற்றிவ் வெகுளிக்கு முதலென் னென்பார்."],"p":"","r":"They ask whether he is enraged at Death, who roams the earth making creatures tremble, or at those who rule through the ages and who he thinks are above him, and what the root of this anger is in the sea-girt world.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kootru","vekuli","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1153","lb":"SO1153","mm":1,"ev":"The kings guess whether his anger is aimed at Death or at the lords of the ages.","cl":[],"ag":["அரசரெல்லாம்"],"ec":[],"spk":["அரசரெல்லாம்",""],"ch":"so:51"},"so:1154":{"book":"soolamani","num":1154,"t":["ஊழிகள் பெயர்க்க லுற்றிவ் வுலகினைப் பிறிதொன் றாக்கி","வாழுயி ரொருங்கு வாரி மறிகட னடுவட் பெய்வான்","சூழிய தொடங்கு கின்ற தாங்கொலோ சொல்லி னீடொன்","றாழியான் வெகுளிக் குண்டோ யாரிரை யாவ தென்பார்."],"p":"","r":"They ask whether he has begun to move the ages, make the world something else and sweep all living things into the churned sea, and whether the discus-lord's anger has any prey worth so great an end.","n":"Line 1 has a stray pulli repaired; the kings' picture of an age-ending flood is their guess, not the poet's.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kootru","vekuli","vali"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1154","lb":"SO1154","mm":1,"ev":"The kings fear his anger portends the world's end.","cl":[],"ag":["அரசரெல்லாம்"],"ec":[],"spk":["அரசரெல்லாம்",""],"ch":"so:51"},"so:1155":{"book":"soolamani","num":1155,"t":["தானவ ரரக்கர் பண்டே தஞ்சமா மிவற்கு மண்மேல்","ஊனவர் மனித்த ரேக வுவனுக்கோர் துகளு மாகார்","வானவ ரிவற்கு மாறாய் வருபவர் மதிப்பி னில்லை","ஏனவர் முனிவு செய்வார் யார்பிற ருரைமி னென்பார்."],"p":"","r":"They say the danavas and rakshasas have long been his refuge, flesh-eating men on earth are not even dust to him, the gods are not worth counting as his rivals, and ask who else he can be angry with.","n":"The courtiers' flattery is the rival camp's picture of him, set in the story before the war that tests it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["perumai","vali","vekuli"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1155","lb":"SO1155","mm":1,"ev":"The kings fail to find any rival for his anger.","cl":[["no god, demon or man can stand against the discus-lord's might","general","reported",3,"summoned_kings"]],"ag":["அரசரெல்லாம்"],"ec":[],"spk":["அரசரெல்லாம்",""],"ch":"so:51"},"so:1156":{"book":"soolamani","num":1156,"t":["இற்றதிவ் வுலக மென்பா ரெரிந்தன திசைக ளென்பார்","அற்றன மகர முந்நீ ரகம்புலர்ந் தொழியு மென்பார்","மற்றினி மொழியல் வேண்டா வருவன வறிய லாகா","உற்றபி னறிது மென்றாங் குரையொழி வாரு மானார்."],"p":"","r":"The people first say the world is broken, the quarters burned and the ocean dried, then fall silent: what is coming cannot be known and they will learn it when it arrives.","n":"The verse closes the run of crowd dread that begins at 1153; the speakers are the frightened citizens, not the war party.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["acham","kaalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1156","lb":"SO1156","mm":1,"ev":"The people of the city, dreading the king's rage, trail off into silence.","cl":[],"ag":["உலகோர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["உலகோர்",""],"ch":"so:52"},"so:1157":{"book":"soolamani","num":1157,"t":["அச்சமோ டுலக மின்றே யவிந்தன போன்று மென்பார்","நச்செரி நகையி னாலே நடுங்கின திசைக ளென்பார்","இச்சைகள் பிறந்த வாற்றா லினையன நினையும் போழ்திற்","கச்சையங் களிநல் யானைக் காவலன் கனன்று சொன்னான்."],"p":"","r":"Some say the world will be lost in fear and the quarters tremble at his poison-laughter; at these thoughts the lord of the strong rutting elephant burns and speaks.","n":"The king is named only by his epithet (the lord of the elephant); the story so far makes him the discus-lord Asvagriva, so the cast takes that name.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vekuli","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1157","lb":"SO1157","mm":1,"ev":"The elephant-lord king, in a rage, begins to speak to the court.","cl":[],"ag":["அச்சுவக்கிரீவன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:52"},"so:1158":{"book":"soolamani","num":1158,"t":["இரதநூ புரத்தை யாள்வான் புதல்வியை யுனைவஞ் சித்துப்","புரிமனு சர்க்கீ வாக்கே புகன்றனன் போலு மென்ற","உரைதனக் குரைத்த வாறே யுரைத்தன னுலக மெல்லாம்","இரைதனக் கென்று மாற்றா வெரிபடு வெகுளித் தீயான்."],"p":"","r":"What the king says in anger is that the ruler of Rathanupuram has deceived him and, it is said, has promised his daughter to a human; he repeats the news as it was brought, and his fire of wrath cannot be quenched.","n":"The verse reports a rumour (போலும் என்ற உரை) that the king repeats as fact, so his rage rests on hearsay.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vekuli","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1158","lb":"SO1158","mm":1,"ev":"The king repeats the report that the Rathanupura ruler has given his daughter to a human.","cl":[],"ag":["அச்சுவக்கிரீவன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:52"},"so:1159":{"book":"soolamani","num":1159,"t":["முரைசொலி முழையி னுள்ளான் முழங்குகின் றதனோ டொப்ப","விரையொலி விளங்கு தாரான் விளம்பிய வெகுளி மாற்றம்","அரைசர்க ளதனைக் கேட்டே யிதனுக்கோ வடிக ளிவ்வா","றுரைசெல முனிவ தென்றோ ரொல்லொலி யெழுந்த தன்றே."],"p":"","r":"The king's angry words roar like a drum in a cave; the kings who hear them rise in a loud cry asking whether he should be so angry over this.","n":"The assembled kings' cry reads as a question put to him, but its tone (protest or agreement) is not fixed by the Tamil.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vekuli","mannar"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1159","lb":"SO1159","mm":1,"ev":"The assembled kings answer the king's rage with a roar of protest.","cl":[],"ag":["அச்சுவக்கிரீவன்","அரைசர்கள்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:52"},"so:1160":{"book":"soolamani","num":1160,"t":["செழுமல ரலங்கன் மார்பன் செங்கண்டீ யுமிழக் கண்டும்","எழுமலர்ந் தனைய திண்டோ ளிவைசுமந் திருப்ப தென்னே","கழுகுபோற் களத்து வென்று கதலிகை நடுது மன்றேல்","விழவயர் விசும்பி னார்க்கு விருந்தின மாது மென்பார்."],"p":"","r":"Soldiers say: why carry these strong arms idle when the king spits fire; if we do not win on the field like eagles and plant the banners, we shall be a feast for the sky-dwellers.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["padai","maanam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1160","lb":"SO1160","mm":1,"ev":"The soldiers urge going to war and say they will win or die.","cl":[["a soldier who carries arms must win on the field or die as a feast for the gods","general","reported",3,"soldiers"]],"ag":["படைவீரர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["படைவீரர்",""],"ch":"so:52"},"so:1161":{"book":"soolamani","num":1161,"t":["ஏவது செய்து வாழும் யாமுள மாக வெங்கோ","நோவது செய்து வேந்தர் நுனித்துயிர் வாழ்ப வாயிற்","சாவது போக வாழ்க்கை தவந்தலை நிற்ற லொன்றோ","வீவது செய்த லொன்றோ நமக்கினி விளைவ தென்பார்."],"p":"","r":"Other men, who live doing the king's bidding, ask what is left for them if kings live by tormenting others: their life is bound to death, so is austerity the only resort, or is it ruin?","n":"தவம் here is austerity as the only alternative to dying in the king's cause; the verse does not endorse it as a path, only lets the soldiers name it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thavam","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1161","lb":"SO1161","mm":1,"ev":"Some of the king's men voice doubt about being ruled by the king's anger.","cl":[["men who live by a king's command are left only with austerity or death when the king torments others","general","reported",3,"soldiers"]],"ag":["படைவீரர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["படைவீரர்",""],"ch":"so:52"},"so:1162":{"book":"soolamani","num":1162,"t":["நாண்டொழின் மகளிர் முன்னும் நகைக்கிளை யாயத் துள்ளும்","வீண்டொழில் விளம்பி யென்னை வீரங்கள் வெறிய வாக","ஆண்டொழில் புகுந்த தம்மா வதோவினி தாயிற் றென்று","தூண்டொழில் வளரத் தத்தந் தோள்களை நோக்கு கிற்பார்."],"p":"","r":"The soldiers say that after idle boasts before women and kin their heroism will be empty unless the work of men now comes; they look at their own shoulders, restless for war.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maanam","naanam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1162","lb":"SO1162","mm":1,"ev":"The soldiers, shamed by their boasts, look to their shoulders and long for battle.","cl":[],"ag":["படைவீரர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["படைவீரர்",""],"ch":"so:52"},"so:1163":{"book":"soolamani","num":1163,"t":["நாள்வடுப் படாமை நம்மைப் புறந்தந்தாற் குதவி நங்கள்","டோள்வடுப் படாமை மன்னன் புகழ்வடுப் படுத லுண்டோ","வாள்வடுப் பிளவு போலுங் கண்ணியை மகிழ்ந்த காளை","கேள்வடுப் படரும் பூசல் கேட்டிரா நாளை யென்பார்."],"p":"","r":"The soldiers say that the king who guarded them must not have his fame wounded, and that those who delighted in the maid will hear the din of the youth's kin tomorrow.","n":"Line 1 to 2 are hard to read in the word division, so the sense that the king's fame must not take a scar is held loosely.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pugazh","aanmai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1163","lb":"SO1163","mm":1,"ev":"The soldiers pledge themselves to protect the king's fame and promise a clash tomorrow.","cl":[["men who were guarded by a king owe him the protection of his fame","general","reported",2,"soldiers"]],"ag":["படைவீரர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["படைவீரர்",""],"ch":"so:52"},"so:1164":{"book":"soolamani","num":1164,"t":["இனியிருந் தென்னை பாவ மெழுமின் போய்ப் பொருது மென்பார்","முனிவன செய்த வேந்தன் முடித்தலை கொணர்து மென்பார்","கனிவளர் கிளவி யாளைக் கைப்பற்றித் தருது மென்பார்","பனிவரை யரசர் மாற்றம் பற்பல பரிதி வேலோய்."],"p":"","r":"The speaker, turning to the sun-spear lord he addresses, says the kings say: why stay, go and fight, bring the head of the offending king, seize the maid; many such words came from the mountain kings.","n":"Here the speaker first addresses a listener (பரிதி வேலோய்); the chapter ends with a first-person line (போந்தேன், 1174), so from here the verses are read as a report brought back to the listener, a reading not fixed in the Tamil.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["padai","pagai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1164","lb":"SO1164","mm":1,"ev":"The speaker relays the kings' calls to fight and to seize the girl.","cl":[],"ag":["அரைசர்கள்"],"ec":[],"spk":["ஒற்றன்",""],"ch":"so:52"},"so:1165":{"book":"soolamani","num":1165,"t":["சினமெனப் பட்ட தீயுட் பிறந்தது செருக்கு நன்னீர்","மனவுண வுண்டு மானப் பூநின்ற வயிர வொள்வாள்","அனலதொன் றகத்த தாக வாரமர் குருதி வேட்டுக்","கனல்வதோர் கால வொள்வாள் கடைக்கணித் தொருவன் சொன்னான்."],"p":"","r":"One born of the fire called anger, whose pride feeds on blood, looks at his diamond sword and speaks: pride feeds on the heart and the sword thirsts for blood.","n":"சினம் is made the source of செருக்கு, so pride is presented as anger's offspring; the warrior does not condemn either.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vekuli","maanam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1165","lb":"SO1165","mm":1,"ev":"An angry warrior speaks of his sword and pride.","cl":[["pride is born of the fire of anger and feeds on the heart's food","general","reported",1,"unnamed_warrior"]],"ag":["ஒருவன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["ஒருவன்",""],"ch":"so:52"},"so:1166":{"book":"soolamani","num":1166,"t":["தோள்களைத் தகர்த்து வீக்கித் துணைக்கரங் கொட்டி யார்த்து","வாள்களைத் துடைத்து நோக்கி வகைசெய்வ தெளிதி யார்க்கும்","நீள்கதி ரிமைக்கு மொள்வாண் முகம்பெற நெருப்புச் சிந்தித்","தாள்களை வெதுப்பும் வெம்போர் தாங்குவ தரிய தென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"He claps his shoulders, wipes his sword and says anyone can prepare for war, but to bear the fierce war whose sparks scorch the foot is hard.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["padai","vali"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1166","lb":"SO1166","mm":1,"ev":"The warrior boasts of the hardship of war and his readiness to bear it.","cl":[["preparing for war is easy for anyone, bearing it is hard","general","reported",4,"unnamed_warrior"]],"ag":["ஒருவன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["ஒருவன்",""],"ch":"so:52"},"so:1167":{"book":"soolamani","num":1167,"t":["தானவ ரெனினு மிப்போ ரிழந்து போய்த் தரணி வாழும்","ஊனம ருலக மாளு மூழியொன் றிதுவன் றாயில்","வானவ ருலகு மண்ணும் வந்துநின் வாயில் பற்றி","ஈனமொ டுறங்கக் காட்டி யிடுவன்யான் றெளியிய தென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"He swears that unless this war is won, even gods and earth-dwellers will come to the king's gate and sleep in shame, which he will show them.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["padai","maanam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1167","lb":"SO1167","mm":1,"ev":"The warrior vows to make both gods and humans sleep in disgrace if he does not win.","cl":[],"ag":["ஒருவன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["ஒருவன்",""],"ch":"so:52"},"so:1168":{"book":"soolamani","num":1168,"t":["வாளினாற் செருவுண் டேனு மாயமற் றாகு மேனுந்","தோளினா லாகு மேனுஞ் சொல்லெலா மொழிக மற்றக்","காளைதன் னுயிரி னோடுங் கன்னியைக் கொணர்ந்து தந்து","தாளிலே யிட்ட பின்றைத் தவிர்கநின் சீற்ற மென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"Whether by sword, by trick or by shoulder, he says, let all talk end: he will bring the maid together with the youth's life and lay her at the king's feet, and only then should the king's anger cease.","n":"The warrior offers any means (sword, trick or shoulder) to win; he places no limit on method.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["padai","kodungol"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1168","lb":"SO1168","mm":1,"ev":"The warrior offers to bring the maid and the youth's life to the king's feet.","cl":[["a king's anger may end only when the youth's life and the maid are laid at his feet","general","reported",4,"unnamed_warrior"]],"ag":["ஒருவன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["ஒருவன்",""],"ch":"so:52"},"so:1169":{"book":"soolamani","num":1169,"t":["ஆளிகட் கரச னாங்கோர் குறுநரி யறிவில் லாத","மீளிமை பொறாது வெம்பி வெகுண்டெழு கின்ற தென்றால்","நாளினு நங்கள் போல்வார் நகைசெயப் படுவ தன்றே","வாளொளி வயிர மின்னு மணிமுடி மன்னர் கோவே."],"p":"","r":"A speaker says: if a little jackal, with no understanding, rose in rage against lions, such as we would be laughed at daily, O lord of kings of shining crowns.","n":"The comparison (lions and jackal) assumes the foe is low; the story's later battles test that assumption.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sirumai","perumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1169","lb":"SO1169","mm":1,"ev":"The speaker warns that anger against a lowly foe will make the great a laughing-stock.","cl":[["great kings make themselves ridiculous by raging against lowly foes","general","reported",3,"unnamed_warrior"]],"ag":["ஒருவன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["ஒருவன்","அச்சுவக்கிரீவன்"],"ch":"so:52"},"so:1170":{"book":"soolamani","num":1170,"t":["ஆதலா லெங்க ளாலங் காவதொன் றில்லை யாயிற்","போதுலா மலங்கன் மார்ப பொருவது பொருந்திற் றென்னும்","காதலான் கனக சித்திரன் கட்டுரை யதனைக் கேட்டே","கோதிலா மாரி பெய்த கோடையங் குன்ற மொத்தான்."],"p":"","r":"Kanakachitran, who loves the king, says that since they cannot guard against it there is nothing for them but to fight; on hearing it the king is like a summer hill after rain.","n":"The words of the two clauses are hard to join (ஆலங்காவது ஒன்றில்லை), and whether the rain-cooled hill means the king's anger is cooled or his wrath is fed is not clear in the Tamil.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["padai","vekuli"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1170","lb":"SO1170","mm":1,"ev":"Kanakachitran urges fighting and the king's rage cools.","cl":[],"ag":["கனகசித்திரன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["கனகசித்திரன்","அச்சுவக்கிரீவன்"],"ch":"so:52"},"so:1171":{"book":"soolamani","num":1171,"t":["மற்றவ னுரைத்த போழ்தின் வச்சிர கண்ட னென்பான்","கொற்றவற் கிளைய காளை கோத்தொழிற் பாகம் பூண்டான்","இற்றதா லெங்க ளாண்மை யாங்களு மிழிந்து நிற்பச்","செற்றதோர் படையுண் டாயிற் றென்றுகண் சிவந்திட்டானே."],"p":"","r":"Vajrakandan, the king's younger brother, red-eyed, says that their manhood is broken and they themselves stand lowered now that a foe has risen against them.","n":"Line 3 is hard to read, so the specific cause of the lowered manhood is held loosely.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maanam","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1171","lb":"SO1171","mm":1,"ev":"Vajrakandan, in anger, says the foe has lowered their manhood.","cl":[],"ag":["வயிரகண்டன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["வயிரகண்டன்",""],"ch":"so:52"},"so:1172":{"book":"soolamani","num":1172,"t":["மகரமால் கடலை யல்லாற் சிறுகய மதலை சேரா","சிகரமால் யானை வேந்தே தானவர் செருவன் றாயின்","நிகரலா நீசர் தம்மேல் நீசெலற் பால தென்று","புகரெரி யவிக்க லுற்றான் பொழிமழை பொழிவ தொத்தான்."],"p":"","r":"One tries to put out the fire like rain: a small pond does not take the great sea's young, so, if this war is not against demons, the king of great elephants should not march on lowly foes.","n":"The speaker of 1172 is not named; it follows Vajrakandan's speech but may be another voice.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["perumai","sirumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1172","lb":"SO1172","mm":1,"ev":"A speaker argues that a great king should not fight lowly men.","cl":[["a great king should not march against lowly foes unless it is a war with demons","general","reported",3,"unnamed_warrior"]],"ag":["ஒருவன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["ஒருவன்","அச்சுவக்கிரீவன்"],"ch":"so:52"},"so:1173":{"book":"soolamani","num":1173,"t":["கேறனான் றம்பி மாரு மக்களு மின்ன போல்வ","கூறினார் கூற லோடுங் குரையழ லவிவ தேபோல்","ஆறினா னென்னை செய்யு மாயபண் பதுவ தானால்."],"p":"","r":"His brothers and sons say such things, and at their words the roaring fire seems to die: he cooled, and what can be done, such is his nature.","n":"The verse has three lines instead of four and the first word is damaged, so the sense that the king cools on his kin's words is held loosely.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vekuli","sutram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1173","lb":"SO1173","mm":1,"ev":"The king's kin speak and his anger dies down.","cl":[],"ag":["அச்சுவக்கிரீவன்","தமர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:52"},"so:1174":{"book":"soolamani","num":1174,"t":["அரசர்க ணெறியிற் கண்டீர் யாம்பிழைப் பிலாமை யென்று","முரசென வதிரும் பேழ்வாய் முழங்கிசை மொழியிற் சாற்றி","வரைசெறிந் தனைய தோளான் மந்திர சாலை சேர்ந்தான்","உரைசெறிந் தங்குப் பட்ட சூழ்ச்சியு முணர்ந்து போந்தேன்."],"p":"","r":"The king says, 'Look, by the way of kings we are without fault,' thunders his answer and goes into the council hall; the speaker says he came away having learned the counsel made there.","n":"The last line is in the first person (போந்தேன்), the only mark in the chapter that the narration is a report from a speaker who was present.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","soozhchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1174","lb":"SO1174","mm":1,"ev":"The king thunders that he is without fault and enters the council hall; the speaker returns with what was planned.","cl":[["kings by their way are without fault","general","reported",1,"asvagriva"]],"ag":["அச்சுவக்கிரீவன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["ஒற்றன்",""],"ch":"so:52"},"so:1175":{"book":"soolamani","num":1175,"t":["கன்னிதன் றிறத்துச் சீறிக் காவலன் கனலக் கண்டீர்","என்னினிக் கருது கின்ற தென்றன னெரியு மாழி","மன்னவற் குணர்வுங் கண்ணு மாற்றலும் வலியுந் தோளும்","அன்னவ னமைச்சர்க் கேறா மவனரி மஞ்சு வென்பான்."],"p":"","r":"The king burns with anger over the maid; the minister Arimanju asks him what he now thinks, a man whose understanding, eye and arm other ministers cannot match.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["amaichu","vekuli"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1175","lb":"SO1175","mm":1,"ev":"Arimanju asks the angry king what he plans.","cl":[],"ag":["மஞ்சு","அச்சுவக்கிரீவன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["மஞ்சு","அச்சுவக்கிரீவன்"],"ch":"so:52"},"so:1176":{"book":"soolamani","num":1176,"t":["அணிநகர் மேக கூட மதனையாண் டரிய செய்கை","துணிபவன் றூம கேது சொல்லுவா னென்ன சொன்னான்","மணிவரைப் பிறந்து மாண்ட வருங்கல மன்னர் கோமான்","பணிவரை யன்றி யாரே பெறுபவர் பகர்மி னென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"Dhumakethu, who rules the city of Megakuta and dares hard deeds, says: the rare jewels born of the gem mountains, who but those who bow to the king of kings will gain them, tell me.","n":"The jewel image is probably the maid and the speaker's point that only those who bow to the overlord deserve her, but the Tamil does not say so.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["arasu","vali"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1176","lb":"SO1176","mm":1,"ev":"Dhumakethu speaks in council about who deserves rare jewels.","cl":[],"ag":["தூமகேது"],"ec":[],"spk":["தூமகேது","அச்சுவக்கிரீவன்"],"ch":"so:52"},"so:1177":{"book":"soolamani","num":1177,"t":["வரைதன்மேற் றழலின் பேரார் வளநக ரதனை யாளும்","அரசனங் கார வேக னதனைக்கேட் டழன்று சொல்வான்","இரதநூ புரத்தை யாள்வா னிகழ்ந்தனன் பெரிது நம்மைப்","பொருதவன் கிளையை முந்நீர்ப் புறங்கரைப் படுத்து மென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"Angaravegan, ruler of a city in a fire-capped mountain, says that the ruler of Rathanupuram has slighted them and that they will drive the kin of the man who fought them to the sea shore.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pagai","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1177","lb":"SO1177","mm":1,"ev":"Angaravegan proposes to drive the foe's kin from his land.","cl":[["a king who has been slighted may drive the offender's kin from their land","general","reported",4,"angaravegan"]],"ag":["அங்காரவேகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["அங்காரவேகன்",""],"ch":"so:52"},"so:1178":{"book":"soolamani","num":1178,"t":["மல்லினான் மலர்ந்த மார்பீர் மறைந்துநா மிருந்து வல்ல","சொல்லினால் வெல்ல லாமேற் சொல்லுமி னின்னு மன்றி","வில்லினால் விரவு தானைச் செருவினுள் வீரந் தன்னால்","வெல்லலா மென்னி னென்னை விடுமின்போய்ப் பொருவ லென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"A warrior says: if we can win by hidden words, tell me; if we can win with bow and army by valour, let me go and fight.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["soozhchi","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1178","lb":"SO1178","mm":1,"ev":"A warrior asks whether to win by words or by war and offers to fight.","cl":[],"ag":["ஒருவன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["ஒருவன்",""],"ch":"so:52"},"so:1179":{"book":"soolamani","num":1179,"t":["பூவினும் பொருதல் வேண்டா சூழ்ச்சியே பொருந்த நோக்கி","யாவது காண்ட லாகு மரசர்க்கு நீதி யென்று","சாவதை யஞ்சு வார்க்குந் தகைமையில் லவர்க்கு மன்றே","ஓவுத லின்றி யோதி வைத்ததங் கொருவ னென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"One says there is no need to fight even a flower; kings' justice is to look closely at scheming and see what can be gained, which is taught constantly for those who fear death and lack worth.","n":"நீதி here is the rule of statecraft, not justice in the sense of a fair verdict.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["soozhchi","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1179","lb":"SO1179","mm":1,"ev":"A counsellor argues for scheming over open war.","cl":[["it is a king's justice to weigh scheming over fighting and see what can be gained","general","reported",2,"unnamed_warrior"]],"ag":["ஒருவன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["ஒருவன்",""],"ch":"so:52"},"so:1180":{"book":"soolamani","num":1180,"t":["அழலவி ரலங்கல் வேலோ னவ்வரி சேன னென்பான்","கழலவன் காதற் றோழன் கனன்றவன் கருதிச் சொன்ன","மொழியெதி ருலக மாள்வா னுவந்தவன் முகத்தை நோக்கிப்.","பழிபெரி தொழியச் சொன்னான் படைத்திற லாள னென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"Arisenan, the king's dear friend and bright-spear warrior, listens to the king's angry words and calls him a man of army's might who should leave behind the great blame.","n":"Line 3 ends in a full stop, the verse is hard to read and the sense of the last line is held loosely.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["padai","pugazh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1180","lb":"SO1180","mm":1,"ev":"Arisenan answers the king's rage by speaking of his army's might.","cl":[],"ag":["அரிசேனன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["அரிசேனன்","அச்சுவக்கிரீவன்"],"ch":"so:52"},"so:1181":{"book":"soolamani","num":1181,"t":["பொருவதோ வெளிதி யாங்கள் பொருந்திற முரைமி னென்னை","மருவிய மனிதப் போரோ வான்கெழு தெய்வப் போரோ","இருமையி னியன்ற போரோ யாதுநாந் துணிவ தென்றான்","விரிசிறை யுவணஞ் சேர்ந்த வென்றிநற் கொடியி னானே."],"p":"","r":"The king whose banner bears the great bird asks his council whether the coming fight should be a war of men, a war of gods, or one of both kinds, and what they resolve.","n":"The speaker is named only by his banner (the line-4 epithet), so the identification with the Vidyadhara king rests on the council scene of the preceding verses.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["padai","soozhchi","avai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1181","lb":"SO1181","mm":1,"ev":"The Vidyadhara king puts to his council the question of what kind of war to wage against the discus-lord.","cl":[],"ag":["விஞ்சையர் வேந்தன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["விஞ்சையர் வேந்தன்","மந்திரக் கிழவர்"],"ch":"so:53"},"so:1182":{"book":"soolamani","num":1182,"t":["செப்பிய மாற்றங் கேட்டே திறற்சிரீ சேன னென்பான்","திப்பியர் புகழுஞ் செல்வத் திருநிலை யகம தாள்வான்","அப்படித் தாயிற் கேண்மி னறிந்தவ ரறைந்த வாறென்","றிப்படை நிலைமை யோரா னெடுத்தெடுத் தியம்பு கின்றான்."],"p":"","r":"Hearing the king's question, the ruler of Tirunilai, named Srisenan, says that he will set out what the wise have laid down and then lays out the army's state in order.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["amaichu","avai","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1182","lb":"SO1182","mm":1,"ev":"Srisenan rises to answer the king's question about the kind of war.","cl":[],"ag":["சிரீசேனன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:53"},"so:1183":{"book":"soolamani","num":1183,"t":["பொருப்படைத் தொகையோர் மூன்று போர்த்தொழி றானு மூன்றே","மருவுடை மனுடந் தெய்வ மிருமையு மென்ன மற்ற","வெருவுடைப் படையின் குப்பை மேலது நான்கு வீற்ற","திருபடை யொழிந்து நின்ற விவையும்பாங் குடைய வென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"Srisenan divides the forces and the work of war into three, human, divine and both, and says the host and its kinds follow from this.","n":"Lines 3 and 4 are hard to construe (the counting of 'four' and the leftover 'two forces'); the sense of a threefold division is firm, the further split is held loosely.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["padai","soozhchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1183","lb":"SO1183","mm":1,"ev":"Srisenan classes war and armies into human, divine and mixed kinds.","cl":[],"ag":["சிரீசேனன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சிரீசேனன்","விஞ்சையர் வேந்தன்"],"ch":"so:53"},"so:1184":{"book":"soolamani","num":1184,"t":["அத்திர மாதி யாக வோதிய நான்கு விஞ்சை","தொத்தெரி சுடரு மொள்வா ளெனவிரண் டாகு முன்னாம்","வைத்தவுட் கடைய தேனை யொழிந்தது பரம மாயை","இத்திறத் தினைய வென்றா னெரிமணி யிமைக்கும் பூணான்."],"p":"","r":"Of the four kinds of vidya (art), beginning with the missile art, he says there are two, a blazing flame and a bright sword, and that the last of what was set down is the great illusion.","n":"The list is compressed and the words for the arts are partly obscure; only the opening and the closing 'great illusion' (பரம மாயை) are clear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kalvi","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1184","lb":"SO1184","mm":1,"ev":"Srisenan lists the arts of the Vidyadhara war: missile, flame, sword and illusion.","cl":[],"ag":["சிரீசேனன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சிரீசேனன்",""],"ch":"so:53"},"so:1185":{"book":"soolamani","num":1185,"t":["படைக்கல விகற்பும் போரின் பகுதியும் பரப்பி னாங்கண்","இடைப்புகுந் துரைப்பிற் சாலப் பெருகுமஃ திருக்க வென்று","நடப்பது மக்க ளோடு மக்கட்போர் நல்ல வேனும்","புடைப்பில புகுது மாயிற் புறனுரை புணர்க்கு மென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"Srisenan breaks off the long account of weapons and war's kinds, since telling it all would run on, and says that what will happen is man against man, which is the better course, and that outside matters can be told later.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["padai","soozhchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1185","lb":"SO1185","mm":1,"ev":"Srisenan cuts short his lecture and settles on a war of men against men.","cl":[],"ag":["சிரீசேனன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சிரீசேனன்",""],"ch":"so:53"},"so:1186":{"book":"soolamani","num":1186,"t":["விஞ்சையர் பொருவ ராயில் விஞ்சையிற் பொருது மன்றி","வஞ்சனை யின்றி மக்கள் பொருபவேன் மக்கட் போரே","எஞ்சுத லின்றி யேற்ற பொருதுமென் றின்ன சூழ்ந்து","நஞ்சனாற் குரைப்பக் கேட்டு நன்றது துணிமி னென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"The plan stated is that if the Vidyadharas fight with their arts, men will fight them with theirs; but men will fight men, with no trickery, and the king is told to carry this to the enemy and decide.","n":"Whether the 'telling' of line 4 is the king's message to the enemy or the council's advice to the king depends on a doubtful word at the end of line 4.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["aram","soozhchi","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1186","lb":"SO1186","mm":1,"ev":"A war of men without deceit is proposed and the king is asked to decide.","cl":[["men who fight men should do so without trickery","general","reported",2,"srisenan"]],"ag":["சிரீசேனன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சிரீசேனன்","விஞ்சையர் வேந்தன்"],"ch":"so:53"},"so:1187":{"book":"soolamani","num":1187,"t":["ஆய்ந்தவ ரோடு போதந் தாழியா னருவிக் குன்றிற்","காய்ந்துவந் திறுத்த பின்றைக் கடிநகர் நமது தன்மேற்","சேந்தெரித் திடுது மென்று தென்மலை யரசர் சென்றார்","வாய்ந்துமற் றொழிந்த மன்னர் மேல்வர வலித்து நின்றார்."],"p":"","r":"A report reaches the city that the southern-hill kings, having gathered with their allies at the cool foot of the mountain, mean to burn the city, while the other kings are resolved to march.","n":"The enemy's decision is told in indirect speech as an envoy's report, as the next verse confirms.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pagai","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1187","lb":"SO1187","mm":1,"ev":"Enemy kings gather and set out to burn the Vidyadhara city.","cl":[],"ag":["தென்மலை யரசர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:53"},"so:1188":{"book":"soolamani","num":1188,"t":["ஒற்றனாங் குரைப்பக் கேட்டே யொளியவன் பெயர னோடுஞ்","சுற்றமா யவருஞ் சூழ்நீர்ச் சுரமைநா டுடைய கோவும்","மற்றவன் புதல்வர் தாமும் வருகென வந்தார் மாற்றம்","உற்றவா றறியச் சொன்னா னொளிவரை யரசர் கோவே."],"p":"","r":"After the spy's report the Vidyadhara king calls his kinsmen, the kings of the sea-ringed country and their sons, and lays the report before them as it was given.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["otru","amaichu","avai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1188","lb":"SO1188","mm":1,"ev":"The king summons his allies and repeats the spy's report.","cl":[],"ag":["ஒற்றன்","விஞ்சையர் வேந்தன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:53"},"so:1189":{"book":"soolamani","num":1189,"t":["விச்சையின் செருக்கி னாலும் வீங்குதோட் டருக்கி னாலு","கச்சையங் களிற்றோ டேனைக் கவனமா வலத்தி னாலுங்","அச்சுவக் கிரீவ னாதி அரசர்க ளழன்று வெம்போர்","நச்சிமேல் வருப வாயி னன்றது போல்வ துண்டோ."],"p":"","r":"A voice among the council says that if Asvagriva and the other kings, proud of their arts, arms, elephants and cavalry, march in anger to war, nothing could be better than that.","n":"Unnamed speaker; the 'it is good' of line 4 is bravado, answered by the fearful voices in the verses that follow.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maram","padai","vali"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1189","lb":"SO1189","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["a warrior welcomes the enemy's attack because it gives the fight he wants","general","reported",4,"vidyadhara_warriors"]],"ag":["அச்சுவ கண்டன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["அரசர்",""],"ch":"so:53"},"so:1190":{"book":"soolamani","num":1190,"t":["எரியெனச் சுரிந்த கேசத் திருளெனத் திரண்ட மேனிப்","பெரியன வளைந்த வெள்ளை யெயிற்றினர் பிலங்கொள் வாயர்","அரியன செய்ப வன்றே யசுரரென் றுருவு கண்டே","இரிவன ரோடு வாரு ணம்மையு மெண்ணிற் றென்பார்."],"p":"","r":"Some soldiers say that the enemy's fierce forms, with fire-like hair, dark bodies and white fangs, are the shape of demons and that onlookers will flee at the sight of them.","n":"The sense of the last line (that even the Vidyadhara allies will recoil) is read loosely; the speakers are the council's soldiers speaking in turn, marked by என்பார்.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["acham","padai","pagai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1190","lb":"SO1190","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["அரசர்",""],"ch":"so:53"},"so:1191":{"book":"soolamani","num":1191,"t":["எரிபொங்க வெழுந்த வெள்ளை யெயிற்றிடை யிலங்க நக்குப்","புருவங்க ணெறிய வேற்றிப் புகுந்துநாம் வெருட்டும் போழ்தில்","உருவங்கள் பெரிய வாறு முள்ளங்கள் சிறிய வாறுஞ்","செருவங்கண் விளைந்த போழ்திற் காட்டுதுந் தெருட்டி யென்பார்."],"p":"","r":"Others reply that they will glare, grin and charge in a rage, and that when the fight begins they will show that the foe's great frames hide small hearts.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","acham","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1191","lb":"SO1191","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["a large body hides a small spirit that battle exposes","general","reported",3,"vidyadhara_warriors"]],"ag":["அரசர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["அரசர்",""],"ch":"so:53"},"so:1192":{"book":"soolamani","num":1192,"t":["இமையவ ரரசன் றானு மிகல்செயக் கருதி வந்தால்","அமையுமஃ தஞ்ச லாமோ யாண்கட னதுவ தானால்","நவையின ருளரென் றஞ்சி நடுங்கினர் தமக்கு நாளுஞ்","சுவைபெறு தோளும் வாளுஞ் சொல்லுமின் சுருங்க வென்பார்."],"p":"","r":"Even if the king of the gods himself came to fight them, they say, the fear is needless since it is their duty, and they bid those who tremble at the thought of enemies to say how short the work of arm and sword would be.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","padai","anjaamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1192","lb":"SO1192","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["it is a warrior's duty to fight any enemy, god or man, without fear","general","reported",2,"vidyadhara_warriors"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["அரசர்",""],"ch":"so:53"},"so:1193":{"book":"soolamani","num":1193,"t":["மானமர் நெடுங்க ணார்தம் மனமென வெஃகி மைந்தர்","ஊனமி லகல மூழ்கி யுள்ளுறச் சிவந்த வொள்வாள்","ஈனமா மருங்கி னாரா திரைக்கிடந் தனல்ப வின்று","தானவர் குருதி மாந்தித் தம்பசி தணியு மென்பார்."],"p":"","r":"The young warriors say their red swords, bathed in the foe's broad chests and thirsting like women's eyes, will drink the blood of the demons today and still their hunger.","n":"The simile of the sword's longing against that of the women's eyes (line 1) joins love and war images, as the poet does elsewhere in the epic.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","padai","pasi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1193","lb":"SO1193","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["மைந்தர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["மைந்தர்",""],"ch":"so:53"},"so:1194":{"book":"soolamani","num":1194,"t":["ஆள்வரை யனைய தானை யச்சுவக் கிரீவ னென்னு","நீள்வரை மருங்கிற் றாழ்ந்த திருவெனு மருவி நீத்தந்","தாள்வரை யிழிந்து வந்து தகைமணி நீல வண்ணன்","வாள்வரை யகல மென்னுங் கருங்கடன் மடுக்கு மன்றே."],"p":"","r":"In a long figure the speaker says that the army of Asvagriva will pour down like the stream called Wealth from his mountain-like brow and fall as a flood into the dark sea of the gem-blue lord's broad chest.","n":"The comparison is difficult: the 'stream' is the host streaming out of its chief and the 'sea' is the chest of the dark-hued lord Tivittan, but the grammar of lines 3 and 4 allows another reading.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["padai","vali","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1194","lb":"SO1194","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["அரசர்",""],"ch":"so:53"},"so:1195":{"book":"soolamani","num":1195,"t":["ஆதலா லதனு ணாமு மயிற்படைப் புணைகள் பற்றி","யேதிலா மன்ன ரென்னு மிருமரக் கடப்பு வாரி","மீதுலாம் வெகுளி யென்னும் வெவ்வழன் முழங்க மாட்டிக்","காதலார் கண்கள் பூப்பக் காய்த்துதுங் கைகோ ளென்பார்."],"p":"","r":"Therefore, the speakers say, they will take the peacock-banner as a raft, bind the two rafts of the enemy kings, and with the fierce fire of wrath roaring over the flood will reap the fruit with their loved ones' eyes blooming.","n":"The mixed images of raft, flood and fire in a kind of ferry-crossing are hard to unpick; the sense of rage as the fuel of war is firm.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["padai","vekuli","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1195","lb":"SO1195","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["அரசர்",""],"ch":"so:53"},"so:1196":{"book":"soolamani","num":1196,"t":["உயிரினு மதிக்கற் பால துள்ளப்பே ருறையி னுள்ள","தயிறரும் பனிக்குந் திண்மை யானநா ணதனை யேற்றி","வயிரவின் மனத்த தாகக் கையது வையங் காக்குஞ்","செயிரில்வில் லதனை நோக்கிச் செங்கதிர்ப் பெயரன் சொன்னான்."],"p":"","r":"Looking at the spotless bow that guards the world, which he prizes above his life and strings with a bowstring named for shame, Arkakirti speaks.","n":"நாண் is both the bowstring and the shame that guards honour, and the verse plays on the two.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maanam","naanam","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1196","lb":"SO1196","mm":1,"ev":"Arkakirti addresses his bow before the war.","cl":[["honour (shame at disgrace) is dearer than life to the warrior","general","reported",1,"arukkakirthi"]],"ag":["அருக்க கீர்த்தி"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:53"},"so:1197":{"book":"soolamani","num":1197,"t":["அடுந்திறல் வெகுளிக் காற்றோ டருக்கப்பே ருடைய மேகங்","கொடுஞ்சிலை குலவக் கோலிக் குருதிநீர் வெள்ள மோடக்","கடுங்கணை யென்னுந் தாரை கலந்துமேற் பொழிய வேந்தர்","நடுங்கினர் பனிக்கும் போழ்தி னம்மையு மறிவ தென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"He says the clouds of wrath, with the bows bent as rainbows, will rain streams of arrows until flood-waters of blood run and the enemy kings tremble, and the kings will know.","n":"His speech is a boast in the poet's war style; the poet does not say whether it comes true here.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vekuli","maram","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1197","lb":"SO1197","mm":1,"ev":"Arkakirti boasts that his arrow-rain will drive the enemy to trembling.","cl":[["rage in a warrior's heart gives force to the arrow-rain that wins","general","reported",1,"arukkakirthi"]],"ag":["அருக்க கீர்த்தி"],"ec":[],"spk":["அருக்க கீர்த்தி","அரசர்"],"ch":"so:53"},"so:1198":{"book":"soolamani","num":1198,"t":["அலைகடல் வண்ணன் றம்மு னலர்குழை புரளுங் காதிற்","சிலைபடு வயிரத் தோளான் செங்கதிர் முறுவ றோன்றி","இலைபடு வயிரப் பைம்பூ ணிமையவ ரல்ல ராயின்","மலைபடு கிருமி யோநம் மாறுநிற் பனக ளென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"The elder brother of the sea-hued lord, with a smile that shines like the sun, says that if the enemies are not gods, they are only mountain-worms before them and will stand in their way.","n":"The elder brother of the dark-hued one is Vijayan, by the epic's story (Tivittan's elder brother), and not named here.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maram","padai","perumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1198","lb":"SO1198","mm":1,"ev":"Vijayan treats the enemies as worms and promises to meet them.","cl":[],"ag":["விசயன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["விசயன்","அரசர்"],"ch":"so:53"},"so:1199":{"book":"soolamani","num":1199,"t":["வானவர் மருள நாஞ்சின் மற்றிது மடுத்து மாற்றார்","தேனம ரகல மென்னுஞ் செறுவுசெஞ் சால்கள் போக்கி","ஊனமர் குழம்பு பொங்க வுழுதிட்டு வென்றி வித்தி","ஏனவர் செவிக ளார விரும்புகழ் விளைப்ப னென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"Vijayan says that, with the gods looking on in amazement, he will take his plough, cut furrows through the enemy chests as fields, work the blood into mud, sow victory in it and reap far-heard fame.","n":"The farming image gives war as a harvest of fame, the same trope that makes the plough Vijayan's weapon.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maram","padai","pugazh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1199","lb":"SO1199","mm":1,"ev":"Vijayan describes battle as ploughing and sowing in the enemy's blood.","cl":[],"ag":["விசயன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["விசயன்",""],"ch":"so:53"},"so:1200":{"book":"soolamani","num":1200,"t":["இன்னன பிறவு மேனை யிருநிலத் தரசர் பேச","மன்னவ குமரன் மாமன் மலரடி வணங்கி வாழ்த்தி","மின்னொடு விளங்கு வேலோ யுளங்கொடு விளம்பி யென்னை","என்னொடு படுவ தன்றே யினியிப்பால் வருவ தென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"As the other kings speak, the king's son worships his maternal uncle's feet and says that the matter rests with him, since whatever comes next will be settled between him and the enemy.","n":"The 'king's son' and 'uncle' are read from the wedding and the family of the preceding chapters; the verse names neither.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maram","panivu","aalvinai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1200","lb":"SO1200","mm":1,"ev":"Tivittan bows to his father-in-law and takes the war as his own.","cl":[],"ag":["செங்கண் ணெடியான்","வேந்தன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["செங்கண் ணெடியான்","வேந்தன்"],"ch":"so:53"},"so:1201":{"book":"soolamani","num":1201,"t":["ஆங்கவன் மொழிந்த போழ்தி னமையுமிஃ தறிவ தன்றே","தேங்கம ழலங்கன் மார்ப வினிச்சிறி துண்டு நின்ற","தோங்கிய விஞ்சை நின்னா லுள்ளத்துக் கொள்ளற் பால","ஈங்கிவை யென்ன லோடு மிறைவனைத் தொழுது கொண்டான்."],"p":"","r":"The Vidyadhara king answers that this is enough, but that the prince should still learn some high art held back for him, and the prince worships the king and takes it.","n":"The tutor and pupil roles come from the context: the king gives and the man who worships and takes is the prince.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kalvi","guru","panivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1201","lb":"SO1201","mm":1,"ev":"The Vidyadhara king gives the prince the remaining vidya, and he accepts it.","cl":[],"ag":["வேந்தன்","செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ec":[],"spk":["வேந்தன்","செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ch":"so:53"},"so:1202":{"book":"soolamani","num":1202,"t":["மந்திர வெழுத்து வள்ள லுள்ளத்துப் பொறித்த போழ்தே","அந்தர விசும்பிற் றெய்வ மணுகின பணியென் னென்னா","வெந்திறல் விஞ்சைக் கேற்ற வியன்சிறப் பியற்றி வேலோன்","நுந்தொழில் புகுந்த போழ்தி னோக்குமி னெம்மை யென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"When the spell's letters are fixed in the prince's mind, gods of the air come to him asking what to do, and he honours them as the great art requires and bids them look to him when their work begins.","n":"The mantra's power is reported as the story's marvel and is not argued as doctrine.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalvi","mandhiram","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1202","lb":"SO1202","mm":1,"ev":"The vidya works and the gods appear before the prince.","cl":[["a mastered mantra commands the gods who answer to it","general","reported",1,"tivittan"]],"ag":["அமரர்","செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ec":[],"spk":["செங்கண் ணெடியான்","அமரர்"],"ch":"so:53"},"so:1203":{"book":"soolamani","num":1203,"t":["ஓதிய விஞ்சை வாய்ப்ப வுலகடிப் படாது நின்ற","வாதிசா லமர கற்ப மாமென வமருங் காலைத்","தூதுவ ருருவக் காளை செவிசுடு சரம்பெய் தூணி","மாதிரத் தொசிந்த வேபோல் வந்தொருங் கிருவர் நின்றார்."],"p":"","r":"As the art ripens and he stays like a part of the world that outlasts the ages, two envoys, with bags of arrows that burn the ear, come before him like the horizon bowed down.","n":"'Envoys' (தூதுவர்) are those of the discus-king who demands the girl in the next verse; the verse describes their manner more than their errand.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thoothu","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1203","lb":"SO1203","mm":1,"ev":"Two envoys arrive at the prince's presence.","cl":[],"ag":["தூதுவர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:53"},"so:1204":{"book":"soolamani","num":1204,"t":["பொன்னவிர் திகிரி யாளும் புரவல னுருவப் பைந்தார்","மன்னவன் றமரம் யாமே வாய்மொழி கேண்மின் மன்னீர்","கன்னியைத் தருதி ரோவக் கன்னியை மகிழ்ந்த காளை","இன்னுயிர் தருதி ரோவிவ் விரண்டிலொன் றுரைமி னென்றார்."],"p":"","r":"The envoys, naming themselves as the men of the golden-discus king, offer a choice, to give up the maid or give up the life of the youth who loves her, and say one of the two must be named.","n":"The ultimatum is the envoy's message as the sender's, and the poet does not endorse the claim.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thoothu","kaamam","aram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1204","lb":"SO1204","mm":1,"ev":"The envoys put an ultimatum: the maid or the youth's life.","cl":[["a king may demand a girl or the life of the man who loves her","general","reported",3,"asvagriva_envoys"]],"ag":["தமரம்"],"ec":[],"spk":["தமரம்","அரசர்"],"ch":"so:53"},"so:1205":{"book":"soolamani","num":1205,"t":["கடுத்தவர் கன்னிபே ருரைக்கக் கண்களுட்","பொடித்தன புகைத்திரள் பொழிந்த தீப்பொறி","அடுத்தெழு கின்றதோ ராவி யாரழன்","மடுத்தது மனத்திடை மைந்தற் கென்பவே."],"p":"","r":"At the words about the maid, the hearers' eyes grow red and smoke and sparks pour out, and a fire that takes the life is said to have caught in the prince's heart.","n":"The verse has only four lines of three-footed length (it ends without the usual fourth full line) and the ending 'என்பவே' reports what is said of the prince's heart.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vekuli","kaamam","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1205","lb":"SO1205","mm":1,"ev":"The prince flares into rage at the ultimatum.","cl":[],"ag":["செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:53"},"so:1206":{"book":"soolamani","num":1206,"t":["கண்கன லுமிழ்ந்துதம் புலமுங் காணல","வெண்கதிர் மணிமுத்தம் விதிர்க்கும் மேனியன்","புண்களு ளெஃகெறிந் தனைய புன்சொலால்","விண்களை வெதுப்பினன் வீர னென்பவே."],"p":"","r":"The hero, his eyes spitting fire and his body beading with sweat like pearls, is scorched by the envoys' petty words, which stab like spears into wounds, and his anger heats the very heavens.","n":"The verse names the hero only as வீரன், and who is scorched by the words is read from the sequence of the embassy; the tense of வெதுப்பினன் could also make the hero the one who scorches the heavens.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vekuli","thoothu","maanam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1206","lb":"SO1206","mm":1,"ev":"Tivittan blazes with anger at the insulting words the envoys have brought.","cl":[],"ag":["செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:54"},"so:1207":{"book":"soolamani","num":1207,"t":["நகுதொறு மழற்கொடி நடுங்கு நுண்டுளி","உகுதொறு மயிர்த்துளை யுயிர்க்கும் வெம்புகை","புகுதொறுஞ் செவிசுடு புன்சொ லாரழன்","மிகுதொறும் விசும்புற நிமிர்ந்து காட்டினான்."],"p":"","r":"As he laughs his anger-fire rises, fine drops of sweat fall, hot steam breathes from the pores, and the ears burn at the petty words, until he lifts his rage to the sky for all to see.","n":"Anger is told wholly through the body (laughter, sweat, steam, burning ears), and it is not yet answered in speech, which the next verses hold back.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vekuli","kurippu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1207","lb":"SO1207","mm":1,"ev":"Tivittan's anger shows in his body as he hears the envoys' words.","cl":[],"ag":["செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:54"},"so:1208":{"book":"soolamani","num":1208,"t":["தோற்றமுஞ் சுடரொளி வடிவு முன்னிலா","வேற்றுமை யுடையவாய் விரிந்து தோன்றின","மாற்றமஃ தொழிந்தனன் மனித்த னன்மையைத்","தேற்றினன் றிருமகிழ் தெய்வக் காளையே."],"p":"","r":"His look and his shining form change into an unlike, widespread brightness; he gives up speaking, and the divine youth shows what the goodness of a man is.","n":"The phrase மனித்தன் நன்மையைத் தேற்றினன் is hard to fix: it can mean that his silence shows the restraint of a man, or that he declares a man's worth. Held loosely.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vekuli","azhagu","perumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1208","lb":"SO1208","mm":1,"ev":"Tivittan's form changes and he falls silent.","cl":[],"ag":["செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:54"},"so:1209":{"book":"soolamani","num":1209,"t":["மாண்டன மாற்றலர் நாள்கள் பூமகள்","ஈண்டுவந் திவனொடு திளைக்க லுற்றனள்","காண்டுமிக் காளைதன் கன்னிப் போரெனா","ஈண்டினர் விண்ணிடை யமர ரென்பவே."],"p":"","r":"The foes' days are ended; the Earth-goddess has come to take her pleasure with him, and the gods gather in the sky saying they will see this youth's first battle.","n":"The gods and the Earth treat his victory as already settled before the fighting starts, which prepares the divine word of 1216.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["padai","oozh","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1209","lb":"SO1209","mm":1,"ev":"The gods gather to watch the youth's first war.","cl":[],"ag":["அமரர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:54"},"so:1210":{"book":"soolamani","num":1210,"t":["தூதுவர் முறைப்படுந் தொன்மை யாலிவண்","தீதுரை கொணர்ந்துநஞ் செவிகள் சுட்டவிப்","பேதையர் பிழைத்தது பொறுக்கல் வேண்டுமாற்","போதலே பொருளிவர் போக போகவே."],"p":"","r":"A speaker bids the envoys be sent away: by old custom envoys come and go unharmed, though they have brought evil words that burned the ears; the fault of these fools is to be borne, and their going is the point.","n":"The speaker is not named; the verse follows the hero's silence in 1208, so I read it as Tivittan, but a counsellor is possible.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thoothu","porai","aram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1210","lb":"SO1210","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["envoys must not be harmed, and their offence is to be borne, by ancient custom","general","asserted",1,"tivittan"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["செங்கண் ணெடியான்","தூதுவர்"],"ch":"so:54"},"so:1211":{"book":"soolamani","num":1211,"t":["வில்லவன் மொழிதலும் வீர வேந்தர்கள்","ஒல்லென வொலித்தெழுந் துடன்று சொல்லுவார்","கல்லுயர் கருவரை கருதி யாமிவை","சொல்லிய தனெச்சிலர் சுருங்கச் சொல்லினார்."],"p":"","r":"When the archer has spoken, the warrior kings rise with a roar and answer in a rage, each saying briefly that they have said these things with the great mountain in mind.","n":"Line 4 is hard to construe (சொல்லிய தனெச்சிலர்), and who the kings are, the hero's allies or the foe's, is read from 1210.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vendhan","maram","sol"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1211","lb":"SO1211","mm":1,"ev":"The kings roar and answer after the archer's speech.","cl":[],"ag":["வீர வேந்தர்கள்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:54"},"so:1212":{"book":"soolamani","num":1212,"t":["ஆழியு மகலத்த திருவும் வாங்கியிப்","பாழியந் தோளினான் பால வாக்கினால்","ஏழையு மெம்மையு மறியு மென்றனர்","வாழைமேல் வயிரங்கூர்த் தனைய மாண்பினார்."],"p":"","r":"The kings say that the broad-shouldered one, taking the discus and the wide fortune from the rival, will by his words know both the girl and themselves; they are likened in worth to diamond set on a plantain.","n":"The simile of diamond on a plantain stem is unclear in force; the construction of lines 1 to 3 is also uncertain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["padai","arasu","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1212","lb":"SO1212","mm":1,"ev":"The kings declare the rival's discus and fortune will be taken.","cl":[],"ag":["வீர வேந்தர்கள்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:54"},"so:1213":{"book":"soolamani","num":1213,"t":["வாணிலா மணிநகை முறுவ லாடிறத்","தேணிலா ரியம்புவ தியம்பி னல்லது","காணலா மெல்லையுட் புகுந்து கட்டுரை","பேணலாம் பிறபிற பீடு காண்பதே."],"p":"","r":"A verse on the terms of a message: what is to be said is said with a gleaming smile, and what comes after is to be weighed only once the foe has come within seeing range.","n":"The syntax is badly broken and I cannot fix the sense; read only as a remark on speech and sight before a fight.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thoothu","sol","kurippu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1213","lb":"SO1213","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:54"},"so:1214":{"book":"soolamani","num":1214,"t":["கலைமிசை யினியசொற் கன்னி காளைதன்","இலைமிசை யலங்கன்மார் பிசையக் கேட்டுமோர்","மலைமிசை மறைந்துவா யுரைக்கும் வல்லதிற்","சிலைமிசைத் தோளினான் சேவ கங்களே."],"p":"","r":"The maid's sweet words, heard against the youth's garlanded chest, are set beside the feats of the bow-shouldered hero, who from a mountain-top speaks truly of what he can do.","n":"The lines mix the maid's love and the hero's deeds without a clear join; held as uncertain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1214","lb":"SO1214","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:54"},"so:1215":{"book":"soolamani","num":1215,"t":["துன்னிவந் திவனடி தொழுவ னேலுயிர்","தன்னதா மன்றெனிற் றனதன் றாதலான்","மன்னுயி ருவக்குமோ மானம் வேண்டுமோ","வென்னவ னுவப்பதென் றெண்ணி வம்மினே."],"p":"","r":"A voice puts the foes a choice: if he comes close and worships this hero's feet, his life is his own; if not, it is not his; will lives please them or is honour wanted, let them think what pleases my lord and come.","n":"The speaker is read as the hero's side speaking to the departing envoys (1216 begins with them replying), not named in the verse; it ends in a question whether life or honour will weigh more.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["arasu","maanam","saakkaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1215","lb":"SO1215","mm":1,"ev":"The hero's side sets the envoys' king the terms: worship or lose his life.","cl":[["a king's life remains his only if he bows to the overlord's feet","general","reported",1,"podanam_kings"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["வீர வேந்தர்கள்","தூதுவர்"],"ch":"so:54"},"so:1216":{"book":"soolamani","num":1216,"t":["என்றவர் மொழிதலு மெழுந்து தூதுவர்","சென்றன ராயிடைத் தெய்வ வாய்மொழி","வென்றுவீற் றிருக்குமிவ் விடலை யேயென","நின்றது நிலமகள் பரிவு நீங்கினாள்."],"p":"","r":"When they have spoken the envoys rise and go; at that point a divine word stands that this youth will win and reign, and the Earth-goddess puts away her sorrow.","n":"தெய்வ வாய்மொழி may refer back to an earlier prophecy or to a new divine voice here; the verse does not say which.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oozh","arasu","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1216","lb":"SO1216","mm":1,"ev":"The envoys leave; a divine word of victory is voiced and the Earth is relieved.","cl":[["a divine word has fixed that the hero will win and reign","general","asserted",2,"narrator"]],"ag":["தூதுவர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:54"},"so:1217":{"book":"soolamani","num":1217,"t":["வரிவளை முரன்றன வான துந்துபி","திரிவன வறைந்தன செங்கண் டீப்பட","முரிவன வீரர்தம் புருவ மூரிவிற்","பரிவிறை யின்றிவன் பாடி வட்டமே."],"p":"","r":"Shell bangles of the women sound, divine war-drums circulate and beat, red eyes burn, the bows of warriors' brows bend and break, and without pause the hero's camp stands ringed for war.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["padai","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1217","lb":"SO1217","mm":1,"ev":"Drums sound and the camp of the host is readied for war.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:54"},"so:1218":{"book":"soolamani","num":1218,"t":["ஆளியே றனையவ னணிபொன் மேனிமே","னீளொளி தவழ்ந்தது நெடுங்க ணேழையர்","தோளுமங் கிடவயிற் றுடித்த வீரர்கை","வாளும்பூ நின்றன மலர்ந்த துள்ளமே."],"p":"","r":"The lion-like hero's gold body glows with long light, women's shoulders and the warriors' eyes throb with omens, the swords in warriors' hands seem to flower, and every heart opens.","n":"Throbbing shoulders and eyes are read as omens of battle; the verse does not say whose omen is good.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","kurippu","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1218","lb":"SO1218","mm":1,"ev":"The hero's body shines and omens throb as the host rises.","cl":[],"ag":["செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:54"},"so:1219":{"book":"soolamani","num":1219,"t":["அரசிளங் குமரனை யனற்று மாற்றலர்","முரசினுண் மணியர வுறைந்த முத்தணி","நிரைசுடர் நெடுங்குடை யகடு நெய்கனி","பிரசங்கள் புரைபுரை விலங்கப் பெய்தவே."],"p":"","r":"Honey-like streams of ghee and honeycomb are poured over rows of pearl-hung umbrellas and the drums that strike down the foe-prince, so that the arms seem to be bathed in fat.","n":"The syntax of 2 to 4 is broken; a ritual anointing of drums and umbrellas before battle is the likeliest sense.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["padai","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1219","lb":"SO1219","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:54"},"so:1220":{"book":"soolamani","num":1220,"t":["கதிர்மணித் தேர்க்கொடிஞ் சேறிக் காக்கைகள்","எதிரெதிர் சிலம்பின வெரிந்த மாதிரம்","உதிரநீர்ப் புதுமழை சொரிந்த துச்சியின்","அதிர்தரு கவந்தங்க ளாடி யிட்டவே."],"p":"","r":"Crows scream facing the jewelled chariots' tops, the burning quarters pour a fresh rain of blood, and headless trunks rise and dance on the field.","n":"The verse reads as battle seen in advance or the field of the first clash; it does not name the side whose chariots are meant.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","saakkaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1220","lb":"SO1220","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:54"},"so:1221":{"book":"soolamani","num":1221,"t":["விடவரு மியல்புக டிரிந் மெல்லியன்","மடவர லவரொடு மாறு பட்டனர்","படவர வல்குலார் காதிற் பயெனச்","சுடர்தரு குழகடா மழிந் \"சார்ந்தவே."],"p":"","r":"The delicate and the unwilling change their ways and fall out with their women; sapphire beauties of the soft women turn dark as those who cling to them fall in ruin.","n":"Line 1 and line 4 are corrupt, and no firm reading is possible.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maram","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1221","lb":"SO1221","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:54"},"so:1222":{"book":"soolamani","num":1222,"t":["தூவொளி மணிமுடி முகத்த கிம்புரி","நாவளைக் கொண்டன நாம வென்றிவேல்","பூவொளி மழுங்கின போர்செ யாடவர்","ஏவிளை கொடுஞ்சிலை யிற்று வீழ்ந்தவே."],"p":"","r":"Crowns and helmeted brows fall apart, the lustre of the victorious spears is dulled, and the war-hardened men's bows break and they fall.","n":"The verse is one of a series of battle pictures and does not say which side falls.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","saakkaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1222","lb":"SO1222","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:54"},"so:1223":{"book":"soolamani","num":1223,"t":["உள்ளடி யுமைத்துமைத் தழன்ற மேனியுங்","கள்ளவிழ் கண்ணியுங் கரிந்த கண்களும்","எள்ளுநர்க் கிடவயிற் றுடித்த வேழையர்","வள்ளிதழ்க் கடுங்கணும் வலந்து டித்தவே."],"p":"","r":"The women's flames-scorched bodies, garlands, burned eyes and their cruel, flower-petal eyes all throb at the left or right as their men go to war.","n":"Left and right throbbing of the women's eyes reads as omen; the lines are partly corrupt and the left or right meaning is uncertain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kurippu","pirivu","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1223","lb":"SO1223","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:54"},"so:1224":{"book":"soolamani","num":1224,"t":["வானமீ னுச்சியு ணின்ற மாற்றலர்","தானையு ணடுவுவீழ்ந் ததிரத் தங்களுக்","கூனமுண் டென்பதை யுணர்ந்து முள்ளிடை","மானமஃ தொழிந்திலர் மறங்கொண் மன்னரே."],"p":"","r":"The foe-kings, though they know that to fall in the thick of their host in the blood will bring ruin, will not let pride go, for they are kings of rage.","n":"Which kings are named is read from the opening அரசர்-run; மானம் here is the pride that keeps them from submission, answering the choice of 1215.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maanam","maram","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1224","lb":"SO1224","mm":1,"ev":"The enemy kings hold to their pride though they sense defeat.","cl":[["a warrior king's pride (maanam) holds him to battle though he knows it brings his ruin","general","reported",4,"narrator"]],"ag":["வீர வேந்தர்கள்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:54"},"so:1225":{"book":"soolamani","num":1225,"t":["போகிய தூதுவர் பொன்னவி ராழியொ","டேகிய நாளுடை யாற்கிது வாலென","ஆகிய வாய்மொழி கூறலு மாயிடை","நாகம ழன்றெறி நச்சென நக்கான்."],"p":"","r":"The envoys who had gone return with the gold-shining discus and give the word to their lord, and when they speak he laughs like venom thrown by an angry cobra.","n":"Laughter returns as the sign of anger, as in 1207; here it is the foe's laugh.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thoothu","vekuli","pagai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1225","lb":"SO1225","mm":1,"ev":"The envoys report the answer to their king, who laughs a venomous laugh.","cl":[],"ag":["தூதுவர்","அச்சுவ கண்டன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:54"},"so:1226":{"book":"soolamani","num":1226,"t":["மண்டிணி மாநில மன்னரை மால்வரை","ஒண்டொடி தாதையொ டூழுயிர் வௌவித்","திண்டிறல் பேசிய வச்சிறி யானையுங்","கொண்டனிர் கூடுதி ரோகடி தென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"The foe speaks: having slain kings of the vast earth with her father and seized their lives and boasted of his fierce strength, he and the great elephant too are taken together; hurry.","n":"The antecedent of 'he' in 2 to 3 is not clear and the verse is partly corrupt; held as uncertain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maram","pagai","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1226","lb":"SO1226","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["அச்சுவ கண்டன்","விஞ்சையர்"],"ch":"so:54"},"so:1227":{"book":"soolamani","num":1227,"t":["ஆழியி னானது கூறலு மாயிடை","வாழிய ரோவென மால்வரை வாழ்பவர்","சூழிய வானைகள் மாவொடு தேர்பல","தாழலர் பண்ணினர் தாமு மெழுந்தார்."],"p":"","r":"When the discus-lord has said this, those who live on the great mountain say 'long may you live' and ready elephants, horses and many chariots and rise.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["padai","mannar"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1227","lb":"SO1227","mm":1,"ev":"The hill-dwellers arm their elephants, horses and chariots and rise.","cl":[],"ag":["விஞ்சையர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:54"},"so:1228":{"book":"soolamani","num":1228,"t":["காரணி கண்முர சார்த்த கறங்கின","நீரணி சங்க நிரைந்தன வெம்பறை","தாரணி தானை சிலம்பின தாழ்ந்தனர்","போரணி விஞ்சையர் பூமியின் மேலே."],"p":"","r":"Cloud-dark drums are beaten, water-bright conches fill the line, fierce parai drums resound, the garland-hung host sounds, and the war-arrayed Vidyadharas bow over the earth.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["padai","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1228","lb":"SO1228","mm":1,"ev":"The Vidyadhara host sounds drums and conches.","cl":[],"ag":["விஞ்சையர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:54"},"so:1229":{"book":"soolamani","num":1229,"t":["குடையுங் கொடியுங் குளிர்சா மரையும்","படையும் முடியும் பலசின் னமுமே","இடையும் புடையும் மிருபா லகமும்","அடையும் படையும் மறிதற் கரிதே."],"p":"","r":"Umbrellas, flags, cool chowries, weapons, crowns and many insignia press together, middle and sides and both flanks, so that the rank cannot be told from the weapon.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["padai","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1229","lb":"SO1229","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:54"},"so:1230":{"book":"soolamani","num":1230,"t":["பொன்றவழ் தேர்கலி மாவொடு போதகம்","என்றிவை யெங்கு மிடம்பி வின்றி","நின்றன நின்றது வாட்படை யப்படை","சென்று பெருந்திசை யார்த்திசை யார்த்தார்."],"p":"","r":"Gold-gleaming chariots, noisy horses and elephants stand with no gap, and the sword-army cry rings to the great directions.","n":"A closing verse of massed description; the host is read as the foe's.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["padai","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1230","lb":"SO1230","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:54"},"so:1231":{"book":"soolamani","num":1231,"t":["கொண்டல் கிளர்ந்து பரந்து பெருங்கடல்","மண்டுவ போன்மண மாநகர் முன்னி","விண்டவழ் மின்னிடு வாளினர் வில்லினர்","எண்டிசை யும்மிருள் கூர விழிந்தார்."],"p":"","r":"The host comes down on the wedding city like clouds rising and spreading into a great sea, swordsmen and bowmen darkening all eight quarters.","n":"The verse does not say whose host it is; reading it as the Vidyadhara host of the rival king rests on the story so far.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["padai","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1231","lb":"SO1231","mm":1,"ev":"A sword- and bow-bearing host descends on the city and darkens the sky.","cl":[],"ag":["விஞ்சையர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:55"},"so:1232":{"book":"soolamani","num":1232,"t":["படையென் றலுமே படைபா ரதுவும்","இடையின் றியெழுந் ததிரண் டுகடல்","விடையின் றிவெகுண் டெழுகின் றனபோற்","புடையின் றிநிரந் தனபோர்த் தொழிலே."],"p":"","r":"At the word 'army' the whole host rises without a gap, like two seas surging unchecked, and the work of war spreads on every side.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["padai","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1232","lb":"SO1232","mm":1,"ev":"The two armies rise and spread for battle.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:55"},"so:1233":{"book":"soolamani","num":1233,"t":["காரொடு கார்கட லோடு கருங்கடல்","சீரொடு சென்று திளைப்பது போலத்","தேரொடு தேர்கலி மாவொடு மாபல","போரொடு வந்து புகுந்தன வன்றே."],"p":"","r":"Cloud meets cloud and dark sea meets sea as chariot with chariot and horse with horse crowd into the fight.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["padai","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1233","lb":"SO1233","mm":1,"ev":"Chariots and cavalry of the two sides close with each other.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:55"},"so:1234":{"book":"soolamani","num":1234,"t":["இலையே ரயில்வா ளியொடெண் டிசையுஞ்","சிலையே யெனவுண் டுசிலைத் தொழுதி","மலையே மலையோ டுமலைந் தனபோற்","கொலைவே ழமொடேற் றனகுஞ் சரமே."],"p":"","r":"Spear-bearing elephants and swarms of archers clash like mountains against mountains, the elephants bent on killing.","n":"The Tamil of this verse has broken word division and one line was repaired in the core file; the sense of lines 1 and 2 is held loosely.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["padai","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1234","lb":"SO1234","mm":1,"ev":"Elephants and bowmen meet in battle.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:55"},"so:1235":{"book":"soolamani","num":1235,"t":["கொடிமே லுடையா னைகள்கும் பமுதைத்","தடிமே லனவா கவெழுந் தரசர்","முடிமே லனவாய் முகின்மே லனவாய்ப்","படிமே லனவா யினபாய் பரியே."],"p":"","r":"Elephants' banners and horses leap over crowded elephants, kings' crowns and the clouds, the whole field heaving.","n":"","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["padai","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1235","lb":"SO1235","mm":1,"ev":"Horses leap over fallen elephants and crowns in the press.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:55"},"so:1236":{"book":"soolamani","num":1236,"t":["ஒருபான் முடிமே லுருளா ழியுதைத்","திருபா லுமெழுந் தெறிபா றுசெலப்","பொருபா லவர்கண் சுழலப் பொருதேர்","வருபா லறியா மைமயங் கினவே."],"p":"","r":"Chariot wheels crush heads, rolling them aside, and the fighters, dazed, cannot tell which way the chariot comes.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["padai","maram","kedu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1236","lb":"SO1236","mm":1,"ev":"Chariot wheels crush heads and confuse the warriors.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:55"},"so:1237":{"book":"soolamani","num":1237,"t":["நெறியார் நிரைமா வொடுதேர் களெடுத்","தெறியா வகையா நுதலே றுகரம்","பறியா முறியாப் படையோர் படையுட்","செறியா மதயா னைதிரிந் தனவே."],"p":"","r":"Maddened elephants roam among the crowded horses and chariots, tearing up and breaking the foe's arms and weapons.","n":"","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["padai","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1237","lb":"SO1237","mm":1,"ev":"Rutting elephants move through the enemy ranks.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:55"},"so:1238":{"book":"soolamani","num":1238,"t":["கடுநீ ரவர்கண் ணெரிகொண் டுகனன்","றிடிநீ ருருமின் னெதிரே யெறிய","வடிநீ ரனவா ளிடையே முரியாப்","படுமீ னெனவீழ்ந் துபதைத் தனவே."],"p":"","r":"Warriors with fire in their eyes fall like fish when swords strike, as thunder-bright blades flash against thunder.","n":"","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maram","kedu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1238","lb":"SO1238","mm":1,"ev":"Fighters are struck down by sword blows.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:55"},"so:1239":{"book":"soolamani","num":1239,"t":["வடிநூ னுதிகவ் வியவா ளையொடும்","விடுமீ னெறிதூண் டில்விசைத் தனபோல்","அடுநா ணிடையே றியவம் பினொடு","நெடுநா ணறவிற் கணிமிர்ந் தனவே."],"p":"","r":"Arrows set on the long bowstrings stand up like fish-baited hooks drawn taut, sharpened and flashing.","n":"","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["padai","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1239","lb":"SO1239","mm":1,"ev":"Bowmen draw arrows to the string.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:55"},"so:1240":{"book":"soolamani","num":1240,"t":["ஆரழ லான்பெய ரானணி வெஞ்சிலை","போரழல் வார்கணை மாரி பொழிந்தது","சீர்கெழு விஞ்சையர் செந்தடி நுந்துபு","நீர்கெழு வெள்ள நிரந்ததை யன்றே."],"p":"","r":"A bow named for the fire-eyed god pours a shower of blazing arrows, and the red flesh of the Vidyadharas swells into a flood.","n":"The archer is not named; the bow's name is unclear in the printed text.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["padai","maram","kedu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1240","lb":"SO1240","mm":1,"ev":"A fire-named bow rains arrows on the Vidyadharas and a river of blood forms.","cl":[],"ag":["விஞ்சையர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:55"},"so:1241":{"book":"soolamani","num":1241,"t":["தென்மலை யாரிள மன்னவன் மன்னிய","வின்மலை வார்தனி யின்மையின் விஞ்சைய","பொன்மலை யொன்றொடு போர்செய மேவிய","மன்மலை போல வெழுந்து மலைந்தார்."],"p":"","r":"The young king of the southern hills, wanting a bow to match the Vidyadhara golden mountain, rises like a mountain of Kama and joins battle.","n":"The identification of the young king rests on the story so far, and the compound in line 2 is obscure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maram","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1241","lb":"SO1241","mm":1,"ev":"The southern young king attacks the Vidyadhara host.","cl":[],"ag":["தென்மலை யாரிள மன்னவன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:55"},"so:1242":{"book":"soolamani","num":1242,"t":["கோளென நாளென மின்னுபு குன்றெறி","வேளனை யான்மிசை விஞ்சையர் வெஞ்சுடர்","வாளினர் வில்லினர் மால்வரை போல்வன","தோளினர் தாளினர் தோன்றின ரன்றே."],"p":"","r":"Vidyadharas with blazing swords and bows, arms like great mountains, appear before the youth as bright as Kama.","n":"","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["padai","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1242","lb":"SO1242","mm":1,"ev":"The Vidyadharas press on the hero.","cl":[],"ag":["விஞ்சையர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:55"},"so:1243":{"book":"soolamani","num":1243,"t":["தோன்றிய விஞ்சையர் மேல்விடு வெங்கணை","கான்றது திண்சிலை கான்றலு மேதிசை","மான்றது மங்குல் பரந்தது காரிருள்","நான்றது நண்ணலர் நண்ணல ரானார்."],"p":"","r":"The Vidyadharas' arrows fly, the bows spit them out, and the quarters fill with darkness while the foes draw near.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["padai","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1243","lb":"SO1243","mm":1,"ev":"The Vidyadharas shoot at the hero until the sky goes dark.","cl":[],"ag":["விஞ்சையர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:55"},"so:1244":{"book":"soolamani","num":1244,"t":["தூணி முகத்தது கைத்தல மற்றையோர்","பாணி முகத்தது வெஞ்சிலை நின்றது","வேணு முகத்தது மண்டலம் வெங்கணை","காணு முகத்தள வேயுள வாமே."],"p":"","r":"The quiver sits at the hand, the bow stands, and the arrow runs in the time it takes to look: a picture of an archer too quick to see.","n":"Pure description of speed in shooting; no event is told and the subject is not named.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["padai","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1244","lb":"SO1244","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:55"},"so:1245":{"book":"soolamani","num":1245,"t":["ஒன்று தொடுத்ததோ ராயிர மாம்பல","என்று தொடுத்தன வெண்ணில வாஞ்சரம்","அன்று தொடுத்தவ னெய்தன வையகம்","நின்று தொடுத்து நிரந்தன வன்றே."],"p":"","r":"One arrow is loosed and becomes a thousand, uncountable, and they spread over the earth until they fill it.","n":"","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["padai","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1245","lb":"SO1245","mm":1,"ev":"The hero's shooting multiplies one arrow into a thousand.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:55"},"so:1246":{"book":"soolamani","num":1246,"t":["விண்மிசை யேறிய வெஞ்சரம் விஞ்சையர்","கண்மிசை யேறின மேகடி மேதிசை","எண்மிசை யின்றி யிருண்டன வோவென","மண்மிசை வீழ்ந்து மயங்கின ரன்றே."],"p":"","r":"The arrows rise to the sky and strike the Vidyadharas in the eyes, the quarters go black, and they fall to the ground bewildered.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","kedu","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1246","lb":"SO1246","mm":1,"ev":"The hero's arrows blind the Vidyadharas, who fall to the ground.","cl":[],"ag":["விஞ்சையர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:55"},"so:1247":{"book":"soolamani","num":1247,"t":["இருள்பா யினவா லிதுவென் னெனவே","மருள்வார் வெருள்வார் மறைவார் மறிவார்","தெருள்வார் திரிவார் செருவார் கணையால்","உருள்வார் களுமா கியுடைந் தனரே."],"p":"","r":"In the darkness the Vidyadharas puzzle, fear, hide and tumble, and break under arrows, some rolling on the ground.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","kedu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1247","lb":"SO1247","mm":1,"ev":"The Vidyadharas are routed in the dark.","cl":[],"ag":["விஞ்சையர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:55"},"so:1248":{"book":"soolamani","num":1248,"t":["உடைந்திடு படையிடை யொலிகொண் மால்வரை","இடைந்திடும் படியெழுந் திடறி யேகினார்","படந்தொடி னுடன்றெழு மரவு போற்பகை","கடைந்திடுங் கடுந்திறற் கால வீரரே."],"p":"","r":"The broken army scatters like a mountain collapsing, and the warriors who pound the foe like cobras struck go stumbling away.","n":"Lines 3 and 4 compare the pursuers to a cobra stirred by a touch; whether the comparison is for the pursuing heroes or the fleeing troops is not fixed.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maram","kedu","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1248","lb":"SO1248","mm":1,"ev":"The routed Vidyadhara army flees.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:55"},"so:1249":{"book":"soolamani","num":1249,"t":["இடுதவி சொடுதொடர் பரிய வெந்தகத்","தடுசரம் படுதொறு மலறி வாலதி","நெடிதனெ நிறுத்திநீ ருகுத்து நீள்செவி","மடிதர முடுகின மான யானையே."],"p":"","r":"Arrow-struck elephants scream, hold up their tails and stream water, and run with ears folded back.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["padai","kedu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1249","lb":"SO1249","mm":1,"ev":"Wounded elephants flee screaming.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:55"},"so:1250":{"book":"soolamani","num":1250,"t":["முரசுக ளுடைந்தன முடிகண் மூழ்கின","வரசுக ளவிந்தன வரவத் தேர்க்குழாம்","விரைசெல விவுளிக ளிடறி வெந்தடி","நிரைசெல விழிந்தது குருதி நீத்தமே."],"p":"","r":"Drums are split and crowns submerged, royal power lies low, chariots clatter and horses stumble, and a river of blood runs down.","n":"'Crowns sunk' (முடிகள் மூழ்கின) and 'kings subdued' (அரசுகள் அவிந்தன) are the narrator's summary of a whole host breaking.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","kedu","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1250","lb":"SO1250","mm":1,"ev":"The rival army's drums, crowns and chariots are overthrown in a river of blood.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:55"},"so:1251":{"book":"soolamani","num":1251,"t":["காலெதிர் கடலுடைந் திட்ட தொப்பநம்","பாலது படையுடைந் திட்ட தின்றென","மாலெதிர் கடற்படை மான வேந்தனைக்","கோலெதிர் கையவன் றொழுது கூறினான்."],"p":"","r":"The army has broken today like a sea beaten by the wind, says the staff-bearer, bowing to the proud king whose sea-like host has failed.","n":"The identity of the proud king (taken as the rival king Acuvakanthan) and of the staff-bearer rests on the story so far.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["padai","kedu","amaichu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1251","lb":"SO1251","mm":1,"ev":"The staff-bearer bows to the proud king and reports the breaking of the army.","cl":[],"ag":["கோலெதிர் கையவன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:55"},"so:1252":{"book":"soolamani","num":1252,"t":["நூற்பயம் பலவொடு நுணங்கு கேள்வியே","போற்பயம் பலவொடு புகழ்க ளேதரும்","வேற்பயங் கொண்டனர் தெவ்வர் நந்தமர்","காற்பயங் கொண்டனர் கால வேலினாய்."],"p":"","r":"The speaker says that, as learning and subtle hearing bring many fruits, so war brings many fruits and fame; yet the foe has taken victory by the spear, and our men have taken fear in their feet.","n":"The first two lines are a comparison and the grammar is hard to fix; the second half plainly reports the rout.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["padai","kedu","pugazh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1252","lb":"SO1252","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["weapons win the same kind of fame that learning wins","general","reported",1,"staff_bearer"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["கோலெதிர் கையவன்","மான வேந்தன்"],"ch":"so:55"},"so:1253":{"book":"soolamani","num":1253,"t":["பெரியவாய் வளையெயி றிலங்க நக்குநக்","குரியவா ளெரியெழ முறுக்கி நம்மொடு","பொரியவந் தார்களும் புறந்தந் தாரினி","அரியதென் னெனநக்கா ரவனி மன்னரே."],"p":"","r":"The earth's kings, who came to fight alongside us and have now turned their backs, grin with long teeth like rolled fire, asking what is left that is hard.","n":"Whether these kings are the rival's allies or the victor's side is not clear from the verse.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maram","pagai","mannar"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1253","lb":"SO1253","mm":1,"ev":"The allied kings laugh at the retreat of those who came to fight.","cl":[],"ag":["அவனி மன்னர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:55"},"so:1254":{"book":"soolamani","num":1254,"t":["கலையினைக் கடந்தசொற் கன்னி காதலன்","அலையினுக் குடைந்தில ரருக்கன் கையதோர்","சிலையினுக் குடைந்துதஞ் சிறுமை நாணிநம்","மலையினுக் கடைந்திலர் மான மன்னரே."],"p":"","r":"Proud kings who did not break at the sea of arrows did break at the bow in the sun-like hand, and, ashamed of their own littleness, did not retreat to their own mountain.","n":"","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maanam","maram","mannar"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1254","lb":"SO1254","mm":1,"ev":"The proud kings break before the hero's bow and are too ashamed to flee home.","cl":[["a proud king prefers shame at his own weakness to returning to his mountain refuge","general","reported",3,"narrator"]],"ag":["அவனி மன்னர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:55"},"so:1255":{"book":"soolamani","num":1255,"t":["ஒன்றுவில் லிரண்டுதோ ளொருவ னெய்யவே","இன்றுநம் படையுடைந் திட்ட தாய்விடின்","நன்றுபோர் நமர்கள தென்று நக்கனன்","குன்றுபோற் பெருகிய குவவுத் தோளினான்."],"p":"","r":"The mountain-shouldered king laughs: if one man with one bow and two arms has broken the army today, then war is good for our men.","n":"The king's laugh takes the report of defeat as a boast, as the Kural's warrior laughs at his own wound.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maanam","maram","pagai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1255","lb":"SO1255","mm":1,"ev":"The rival king laughs at the report of his broken army.","cl":[],"ag":["மான வேந்தன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:55"},"so:1256":{"book":"soolamani","num":1256,"t":["இரதநூ புரத்தின்மே லெழுந்த வேந்தரும்","பொருதுதா மழிந்தமர் புறக்கிட் டோடினார்","அரிதினின் விளைவதை யன்றி யாவருங்","கருதிய முடிப்பவ ரில்லை காண்மினே."],"p":"","r":"Kings who rose against Ratanupura fought, were ruined and turned their backs in flight; the poet closes that none finishes what he has in mind unless it ripens with difficulty.","n":"The verse names no cause for the failure, neither fate nor misjudged strength, and gives only the general rule that an aim is won hard.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["muyarchi","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1256","lb":"SO1256","mm":1,"ev":"The kings who marched on Ratanupura are broken and flee the field.","cl":[["a purpose is fulfilled only when it ripens with difficulty; none completes what he intends by wishing","general","asserted",4,"narrator"]],"ag":["இரதநூபுரத்தின் மேல் எழுந்த வேந்தர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:56"},"so:1257":{"book":"soolamani","num":1257,"t":["பாழிப்போ ருடைந்தனர் பகைவர்க் கின்றென","ஆழிப்போர்த் தடக்கையாற் கவர்க ணீர்மையைத்","தாழிப்போர் விலன்றம னொருவன் கூறினான்","ஊழிப்பே ரெரியுணெய் சொரிந்த தொப்பவே."],"p":"","r":"When the enemy's stronghold battle is broken that day, one man reports the temper of the discus-armed ones, and it is like pouring ghee on the fire of the world's end.","n":"The words at line 3 are run together, so who is reporting, and of whom, is held loosely.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pagai","vekuli"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1257","lb":"SO1257","mm":1,"ev":"One man tells of the temper of the discus-handed ones after the enemy's defeat.","cl":[],"ag":["ஒருவன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:56"},"so:1258":{"book":"soolamani","num":1258,"t":["விண்மிசை சென்றவர் மெலிவும் வேற்றவர்","மண்மிசை சென்றவர் மறிந்த மாற்றமும்","புண்மிசை யெஃகம்புக் கொளிப்ப போன்மனத்","துண்மிசை யொழிபடை யாகி யூன்றவே."],"p":"","r":"The news of those who went to the sky fainting and of the foes who went to the earth being cut down lodges in the mind like a spear thrust into a wound.","n":"The subject whose mind is pierced is not named in the verse, so the sense is held loosely.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["manam","kedu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1258","lb":"SO1258","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:56"},"so:1259":{"book":"soolamani","num":1259,"t":["இருந்தினி யென்னையீங் கெழுக வென்றுபோய்ப்","பெருந்தகை யருங்கலப் பெயர்கொள் குன்றின்மேற்","பரந்தன படையொடு பாடி விட்டனன்","கருந்திரண் முகில்புரை காள மேனியான்."],"p":"","r":"A broad-minded dark-bodied one, saying that the other should rest and then rise against him, goes and pitches camp with his forces on the hill called Arungalam.","n":"The verse never names the camper; his dark cloud-like body suggests Tivittan, but this rests on the story so far, not on the verse.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["padai","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1259","lb":"SO1259","mm":1,"ev":"The dark-bodied commander encamps his army on the hill Arungalam.","cl":[],"ag":["செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:56"},"so:1260":{"book":"soolamani","num":1260,"t":["அச்சுவக் கிரீவனுக் கிளைய காளையர்","கச்சையங் கருங்களி யானை வல்லவர்","விச்சையர் கடற்படை பரப்பி விண்மிசை","நச்செரி யுமிழ்தரு நகையர் தோன்றினார்."],"p":"","r":"Younger brothers of Accuvakkiriivan, skilled in handling dark rutting elephants, spread a sea of troops across the sky and appear, smiling and breathing venom.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["padai","vali"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1260","lb":"SO1260","mm":1,"ev":"Accuvakkiriivan's younger brothers appear in the sky with a spread-out army.","cl":[],"ag":["அச்சுவக்கிரீவனுக்கு இளைய காளையர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:56"},"so:1261":{"book":"soolamani","num":1261,"t":["இளையருட் பெரியவன் சொல்லு மெம்மிறைக்","குளைவன செய்தவ ருயிரை மற்றவர்","கிளையொடுங் கீண்டரசாடு மன்றெனில்","வளையொடுந் தலைமுடித் திருந்து வாழ்துமே."],"p":"","r":"The eldest of the young brothers says that unless they destroy, with their kin, those who wronged their lord, they would sit with bangles and their hair tied.","n":"Bangles and tied hair mark a woman's state, so the vow stakes manhood on revenge.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maanam","vanjinam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1261","lb":"SO1261","mm":1,"ev":"The eldest of Accuvakkiriivan's brothers vows to kill those who wronged their lord, with all their kin.","cl":[["a retainer who fails to avenge harm done to his lord forfeits the name of a man","general","reported",4,"brother_eldest"]],"ag":["இளையருட் பெரியவன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["இளையருட் பெரியவன்",""],"ch":"so:56"},"so:1262":{"book":"soolamani","num":1262,"t":["மாலுமாங் குடையர்கொன் மனிதர் நம்மொடு","போலுமாற் பொரலுறு கின்ற தென்றுதன்","கோலவால் வளையெயி றிலங்க நக்கனன்","நீலமா மணிக்கண்ட னென்னுங் காளையே."],"p":"","r":"Nilamanikantan, seeing the men with their umbrella-marks, laughs, with his bright tusk-white teeth showing, at these humans who dare to fight against them.","n":"The first line is hard (மாலுமாங் குடையர்கொன்); the sense of the sneer is held loosely.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maram","veruvu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1262","lb":"SO1262","mm":1,"ev":"Nilamanikantan laughs with contempt at the human army.","cl":[],"ag":["நீலமணிக்கண்டன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:56"},"so:1263":{"book":"soolamani","num":1263,"t":["முளைந்தவா ளெயிற்றவர் முலைகள் பாய்ந்துதேன்","விளைந்ததார் வெறிகொள வைகும் வேற்றவர்","உளைந்தபோர் நிலத்தினுள் ளுருள்ப வென்றனன்","வளைந்தவா ளெயிற்றவன் வயிர கண்டனே."],"p":"","r":"Vayirakandan, with curved teeth, says the foe will lie rolling on the battlefield, as the mud of garlands wet with honey and the breasts of women are kept for them.","n":"","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maram","veruvu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1263","lb":"SO1263","mm":1,"ev":"Vayirakandan boasts that the enemies will lie rolling on the field.","cl":[],"ag":["வயிரகண்டன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["வயிரகண்டன்",""],"ch":"so:56"},"so:1264":{"book":"soolamani","num":1264,"t":["ஒத்திலங் கொண்சிறை யுவணன் றன்னொடு","பைத்திலங் கரவுகள் பகைப்ப போன்மெனக்","கைத்தலங் கையொடு புடைத்து நக்கனன்","தொத்திலங் கலங்கலான் சுகண்ட னென்பவே."],"p":"","r":"Sukandan claps hands together and laughs, as snakes would show hatred to Garuda.","n":"The simile treats the Vidyadharas as serpents mocking the eagle Garuda, an image of overconfidence.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","pagai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1264","lb":"SO1264","mm":1,"ev":"Sukandan laughs and claps his hands in scorn.","cl":[],"ag":["சுகண்டன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:56"},"so:1265":{"book":"soolamani","num":1265,"t":["தம்பியர் மொழியெனுந் தயங்கு மாரியால்","வெம்பிய கொடுமனங் குளிர்ந்து வெய்யவன்","நம்பெயர் முனிந்தவர் நயந்த மண்மிசை","நும்பெயர் நிறுத்துமி னென்று நோக்கினான்."],"p":"","r":"The fierce one, his cruel heart cooled by his brothers' words, which fall like rain, looks at them and bids them set their names firm upon the earth where those who hated his name are loved.","n":"The fierce one is probably Accuvakkiriivan speaking to his brothers, but the verse leaves him unnamed.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pugazh","maanam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1265","lb":"SO1265","mm":1,"ev":"The fierce lord, cooled by his brothers' words, sends them to make their names stand.","cl":[["a warrior's name is made to stand by victory over those who hate it","general","reported",4,"asvagriva"]],"ag":["அச்சுவக்கிரீவன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["அச்சுவக்கிரீவன்","அச்சுவக்கிரீவனுக்கு இளைய காளையர்"],"ch":"so:56"},"so:1266":{"book":"soolamani","num":1266,"t":["நஞ்சினை நஞ்சுசென் றெரிக்க லுற்றபோல்","விஞ்சையர் வெஞ்சினம் பெருக்கி மேல்வர","வஞ்சமின் மணியொளி வண்ணன் றானையும்","எஞ்சலின் றெழுந்தெதி ரூன்றி யேற்றதே."],"p":"","r":"As the Vidyadharas press on, their rage swelling like poison burning poison, the sapphire-bright one's army rises without any fraud and stands against them.","n":"","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["padai","vekuli"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1266","lb":"SO1266","mm":1,"ev":"The Vidyadharas attack and the sapphire-hued commander's army rises and meets them.","cl":[],"ag":["விஞ்சையர்","செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:56"},"so:1267":{"book":"soolamani","num":1267,"t":["விண்ணின தளவுமேல் வந்த வேற்படை","மண்ணின தளவுமா றேற்ற வாட்படை","கண்ணிய கடற்படை யிரண்டு தம்மையும்","எண்ணிணி நமக்கெளி தாவ தில்லையே."],"p":"","r":"The poet says it is not easy for us to count the spear-army that came as far as the sky and the sword-army that met it on the ground, two seas of troops.","n":"The poet speaks in the first person plural of the effort of counting.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["padai","alavai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1267","lb":"SO1267","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:56"},"so:1268":{"book":"soolamani","num":1268,"t":["கடலிரண் டுளவெனிற் கடுக்கு மக்கடல்","இடைநில முடையன ணென்னு மொப்பில","அடலரும் படையவை யிரண்டு மவ்வழி","யடலரும் படையவை யிரண்டு மொக்குமே."],"p":"","r":"If two seas existed and the land between them were a woman, the two forces would equal them, since the two unconquerable armies match each other.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["padai","vali"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1268","lb":"SO1268","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:56"},"so:1269":{"book":"soolamani","num":1269,"t":["திண்டிறற் றேர்களே செறிந்த வென்னவும்","கொண்டபோர் வேழமே குழீஇய வென்னவும்","விண்டவழ் புரவியே மிடைந்த வென்னவும்","கண்டவர் கண்டுழிக் கலந்து தோன்றுமே."],"p":"","r":"To one who looks, the host seems to be nothing but crowded strong chariots, nothing but gathered war elephants and nothing but packed horses, all mingled together.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["padai","vali"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1269","lb":"SO1269","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:56"},"so:1270":{"book":"soolamani","num":1270,"t":["கைவலப் படையினர் கழலர் கச்சையர்","செவ்வலர்க் கண்ணியர் செங்க ணாடவர்","இவ்வுல கிவர்க்கிட மில்லை முன்னிவர்","எவ்வுல குடையவ ரென்னத் தோன்றினார்."],"p":"","r":"The warriors with right-handed weapons, anklets and sashes, red garlands and red eyes appear as if this world had no room for them and they ruled another.","n":"The praise makes the warriors too great for this world, which sets the Vidyadharas' scorn in 1262 to 1264 against the foe's real stature.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1270","lb":"SO1270","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:56"},"so:1271":{"book":"soolamani","num":1271,"t":["அன்றுபோர் மலைந்தது தானை யாயிடைச்","சென்றுபோர் படுமிடந் திசைக ளின்மையா","னின்றுபோ ராடவர் நேரொப் பார்கேளா","டொன்றுபோ ருலகினை யூழி யொத்ததே."],"p":"","r":"The armies joined battle; with no direction left for the fighting to spread, the troops stood in a war that was like the world's end.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["padai","kootru"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1271","lb":"SO1271","mm":1,"ev":"The two armies join in a battle so dense it has no direction to spread.","cl":[],"ag":["வயவர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:56"},"so:1272":{"book":"soolamani","num":1272,"t":["பேரினும் பெருகிய சின்னந் தன்னினுந்","தாரினு மாறுமா றெறிந்து தம்முளே","தேரினுந் தேரினுந் திளைக்கின் றார்சிலர்","காரினும் பெரிதவர் கணைபெய் மாரியே."],"p":"","r":"Some, with drums and flags and garlands struck back and forth, fight one another in their chariots, and their arrows fall thicker than rain from clouds.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["padai","vali"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1272","lb":"SO1272","mm":1,"ev":"Warriors in chariots strike drums and garlands and loose showers of arrows on each other.","cl":[],"ag":["வயவர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:56"},"so:1273":{"book":"soolamani","num":1273,"t":["தாளிடை மிடைந்தன தாள்க டம்முளே","தோளிடை மிடைந்தன தோள்க டோளிடை","வாளிடை மிடைந்தன வாள்கண் மற்றிவை","நாளிடைப் படுங்கொலோ நாங்கள் சொல்லினே."],"p":"","r":"Feet are packed with feet, shoulders with shoulders, swords with swords; the poet asks in what days these could be counted.","n":"The closing question asks whether the crush can be counted in any number of days.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["padai","alavai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1273","lb":"SO1273","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:56"},"so:1274":{"book":"soolamani","num":1274,"t":["குடைநில மறைப்பவுங் கொடிகள் போர்ப்பவு","மிடைநில மிருண்மெழுக் கிட்ட தாயிடைப்","படைநில விலங்கவும் பணிகண் மின்னவும்","இடைநில மிடையிடை யிலங்கித் தோன்றுமே."],"p":"","r":"Umbrellas hide the ground, flags cover it, the earth is smeared dark, and weapons and the jewels of serpents flash across it.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["padai","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1274","lb":"SO1274","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:56"},"so:1275":{"book":"soolamani","num":1275,"t":["கருப்புடைக் கைகளாற் புடைத்துக் கண்களு","ணெருப்பொடு நெருப்பெதி ரெறிப்ப யானைகண்","மருப்பொடு மருப்பிடை மிடைந்து மான்றரேர்","பொருப்பொடு பொருப்பவை பொருவ போன்றவே."],"p":"","r":"Elephants strike with their trunks and throw fire between their eyes; as their tusks meet, the great ones seem mountains fighting mountains.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["padai","vali"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1275","lb":"SO1275","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:56"},"so:1276":{"book":"soolamani","num":1276,"t":["கறங்கெனக் காலசக் கரங்க டாமென்","மறங்கிளர் மன்னவர்தம் மகுட நெற்றியும்","உறங்கலில் கடாக்களிற் றுச்சி மேலுமாய்த்","திறங்கிளர் புரவிக டிரிதர் கின்றவே."],"p":"","r":"Horses circle like the wheels of time over the helmets of the fierce kings and the heads of rutting elephants.","n":"The wheel of time (கால சக்கரம்) is a simile only; the verse makes no doctrinal claim.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["padai","kootru"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1276","lb":"SO1276","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:56"},"so:1277":{"book":"soolamani","num":1277,"t":["செம்பியல் கிடுகின செம்பொற் றட்டின்","அம்புபெய் தூணிய வரவத் தேர்க்குழாம்","வெம்பிய கணைமழை விரவி வில்லொடு","வம்புபெய் மழைமுகில் பொருவ போன்றவே."],"p":"","r":"The bright-bossed shields, the shafts of the quivers and the bow-armed chariot throngs resemble clouds raining arrows.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["padai","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1277","lb":"SO1277","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:56"},"so:1278":{"book":"soolamani","num":1278,"t":["இன்னவ ரின்னுழி யின்ன செய்பவென்","றென்னவ ரறிவுமங் கிடைபு காவகை","மின்னவி ரெஃகினு மிடைந்த வாளினும்","மன்னவர் செருத்தொழின் மயங்கி யிட்டவே."],"p":"","r":"The kings, with their minds failing to know who would do what and where, act in confusion with gleaming spears and crowded swords.","n":"","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["padai","manam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1278","lb":"SO1278","mm":1,"ev":"The kings fight in confusion, unsure what to do.","cl":[],"ag":["அரசர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:56"},"so:1279":{"book":"soolamani","num":1279,"t":["அச்சமுடை யாரகல்க வாற்றுபவ ரேற்க்","எச்சமில் குடித்தலைவர் போகவென வெங்குங்","கச்சையர் கருங்கழலர் காலனையு நோனார்","வெச்சென விழித்துவிறல் வீரர்திரி கின்றார்."],"p":"","r":"The warriors, with sashes and dark anklets, glare and move about, not fearing even Death, calling that the fearful may leave and those who can bear it stay, and the leaders of unblemished lines go.","n":"","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maram","saakkaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1279","lb":"SO1279","mm":1,"ev":"The warriors call on the fearful to leave the field while the brave stay and fight.","cl":[["one who fears may withdraw, and only one who does not fear even Death should stay in battle","general","reported",1,"warriors"]],"ag":["வயவர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["வயவர்",""],"ch":"so:56"},"so:1280":{"book":"soolamani","num":1280,"t":["ஏற்றவ ரிமைப்பினு மிகழ்ந்தெறிதல் செய்யார்","தோற்றவர் புறக்கெடையு நாணிமிக நோக்கார்","வேற்றவரை வீரநெறி காண்மினிது வென்று","தேற்றுவனர் போலவுணர் சென்றுதிரி கின்றார்."],"p":"","r":"The victors do not strike scornfully at an enemy who blinks, nor stare shamefully at one who fled, as though showing the foe the heroic path.","n":"Kural 775 counts a blink at a flung spear as flight; this verse shows the winners declining to press the blinker.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maram","maanam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1280","lb":"SO1280","mm":1,"ev":"Warriors spare the one who blinks and the one who fled, in the manner of heroes.","cl":[["the heroic way is not to strike with scorn at one who flinches and not to shame one who has fled","general","asserted",1,"narrator"]],"ag":["வயவர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:56"},"so:1281":{"book":"soolamani","num":1281,"t":["தாருடைய மார்புபக வார்கணை குளிப்ப்","வேரொடுப றித்தன ரெழுத்துவரி நோக்கிப்","பேரொடுறு காளையவ னாரெனவி னாவி","நேர்படுது மென்றுசிலர் நேடுபு திரிந்தார்"],"p":"","r":"Arrows lodged deep in their chests, the men pull them out by the root and read the lines written on the shafts, and some wander about asking who the named youth is, meaning to meet him.","n":"The sense of the first half rests on a stanza that needed repair, and who is searched for is never named here.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["padai","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1281","lb":"SO1281","mm":1,"ev":"Wounded men read the inscribed arrows and some go about searching for the archer.","cl":[],"ag":["வயவர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:57"},"so:1282":{"book":"soolamani","num":1282,"t":["கேடக மிடத்தது வலத்ததயி லொள்வாள்","ஆடக மடுத்தவணி பூணனலர் தாரான்","றோடக மடுத்ததுதை கண்ணியொடு துன்னார்","ஊடக மடுத்தொருவ னுந்திநனி வந்தான்."],"p":"","r":"A youth bearing a shield on the left and a bright sword on the right, with ornaments, garland and war-wreath, thrusts through the enemy ranks and comes on hard.","n":"He is not named in the verse; 1283 gives the name Arisenan, and the identification is the next verse's.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maram","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1282","lb":"SO1282","mm":1,"ev":"A shield-and-sword fighter forces his way through the enemy ranks.","cl":[],"ag":["அரிசேனன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:57"},"so:1283":{"book":"soolamani","num":1283,"t":["வெறிமின்விரி கின்றவிற லாழியிறை தோழன்","அறிமின்பெயர் யானவ்வரி சேனனென நின்றே","னெறிமினெதி ரென்னொடிகல் வல்லிருளி ராயின்","மறிமினது வன்றியுயிர் வாழலுறி னென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"The king's friend names himself Arisenan and calls on the enemy to meet him, saying that any who want to live should turn back rather than face his sword.","n":"Line 3 is damaged (வல்லிருளிராயின்); the offer of retreat is read from the last line.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maram","manam","pugazh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1283","lb":"SO1283","mm":1,"ev":"Arisenan proclaims his name and challenges the foe to fight him or retreat.","cl":[],"ag":["அரிசேனன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["அரிசேனன்",""],"ch":"so:57"},"so:1284":{"book":"soolamani","num":1284,"t":["அங்கவன் மொழிந்தமொழி கேட்டலு மருக்கன்","வெங்கணை தெரிந்தது விலக்கிவிறல் வெய்யோய்","இங்கிவ னினக்குநிக ரோவென விசைத்தே","பொங்குபுலித் தேர்ப்பெயரன் போந்துபொர லுற்றான்."],"p":"","r":"Hearing his words, a rival lets the arrow he had chosen go back and, saying this one is no match for a mighty man, comes forward himself to fight, the grandson of the tiger-chariot.","n":"The man called அருக்கன் in line 1 is hard to place; he may be the grandson himself or his charioteer, and the verse does not decide.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maram","maanam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1284","lb":"SO1284","mm":1,"ev":"The tiger-chariot's grandson answers Arisenan's challenge and comes to fight him.","cl":[],"ag":["புலித்தேர்ப்பெயரன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["புலித்தேர்ப்பெயரன்",""],"ch":"so:57"},"so:1285":{"book":"soolamani","num":1285,"t":["பொன்னையணி கொண்டபுனை கேடக மெடுத்து","மின்னையுமிழ் கின்றசுடர் வாண்மிளிர வீசி","நின்னையறி யாதவர்க ணின்றிரிய வந்தாய்","என்னையறி யாயறியி னித்தவிர்தி யென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"Taking up a gold-adorned shield and flashing his sword, he tells the newcomer that he has come where those who knew him not have fled, and warns him to turn back if he knows who he faces.","n":"","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maram","perumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1285","lb":"SO1285","mm":1,"ev":"The challenged fighter takes up shield and sword and warns Arisenan off.","cl":[],"ag":["புலித்தேர்ப்பெயரன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["புலித்தேர்ப்பெயரன்","அரிசேனன்"],"ch":"so:57"},"so:1286":{"book":"soolamani","num":1286,"t":["நின்னையறி வன்பெரிது நின்முறைய ளாய்","கன்னியையொர் காளைபிற னெய்துவது கண்டும்","மன்னுமண வில்லுள்வயி றாரவயில் கின்றாய்க்","கின்னுமுள வோபுதிய வென்றுமிக நக்கான்."],"p":"","r":"The answerer laughs that he knows him well, a man who stood gorging in a mansion while another youth won a maiden who was his by right, and asks whether any news is left that is still new.","n":"The taunt turns the fight on a marriage, but which maiden is meant is not told here.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maram","kaamam","pugazh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1286","lb":"SO1286","mm":1,"ev":"One fighter mocks the other with the loss of a bride.","cl":[],"ag":["அரிசேனன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["அரிசேனன்","புலித்தேர்ப்பெயரன்"],"ch":"so:57"},"so:1287":{"book":"soolamani","num":1287,"t":["கன்னியர்தம் பான்மைவழி செல்பவது கண்டாய்","முன்னிய மொழிப்புலவர் நூன்முறைமை யேடர்","அன்னதறி யாதவ னயக்கிரிவ னன்றே","என்னையறி யாமைநினக் கின்னுமுள தென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"He goes on that maidens follow the way of whoever wins them, as the poets' treatise has it, and that a man ignorant of this is a fool, so ignorance of him is still his rival's fault.","n":"The claim is a taunting speaker's, not a doctrine of the work. The word ஏடர் is hard and the sense of the last two lines is held loosely.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maram","pugazh","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1287","lb":"SO1287","mm":1,"ev":"The taunt is carried on: a maiden goes the way of the winner.","cl":[["a maiden's inclination follows the youth who wins her, as the poets' treatises lay down","general","reported",1,"arisenan"]],"ag":["அரிசேனன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["அரிசேனன்","புலித்தேர்ப்பெயரன்"],"ch":"so:57"},"so:1288":{"book":"soolamani","num":1288,"t":["வாயுரை யிருக்கநம் வாளினொளி வாயாற்","றீயுரை செகுத்துமலை கென்றுசெயிர் கொண்டு","காயெரி விழித்தனர் களித்தனர் தெளிர்த்தார்","மாயிரு விசும்பிடை மடுத்துமலை குற்றார்."],"p":"","r":"Rather than trade words, the warriors resolve to settle it by the light of their swords, glare with burning eyes, exult, and leap up and fight in the wide sky.","n":"","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maram","vekuli"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1288","lb":"SO1288","mm":1,"ev":"The two sides give up words, glare and spring into the air to fight.","cl":[],"ag":["வயவர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:57"},"so:1289":{"book":"soolamani","num":1289,"t":["தங்கொளி தயங்குமணி கேடகமு மேனைச்","செங்களி மயிர்ப்புளக சேடகமு மேந்தித்","திங்கெளாடு ஞாயிறு திளைத்திரு விசும்பின்","அங்கண்மலை கின்றவுள வேலவையு மொத்தார்."],"p":"","r":"Holding their gleaming jewelled shields and the others' red hair-crested ones, the fighters in the sky look like moon and sun in conjunction and like the moving spears.","n":"The likeness to moon and sun is the poet's, and the last line leaves it unclear whether spears or men are compared.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["padai","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1289","lb":"SO1289","mm":1,"ev":"The fighters hold shields aloft and fight in the sky.","cl":[],"ag":["வயவர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:57"},"so:1290":{"book":"soolamani","num":1290,"t":["போரிகலி யாரமொடு பூண்மணிகண் மின்ன்","நேரிகலும் வாள்கெளாடு கேடக நிழற்றச்","சாரிகை கறங்கென மலைந்துசுழல் கின்றார்","நீரக வளாகமடு சக்கர நிகர்த்தார்."],"p":"","r":"With pearl strings and gems flashing, swords and shields throwing shadow, they wheel in circling steps like a wheel turning on water.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1290","lb":"SO1290","mm":1,"ev":"The fighters circle one another with swords and shields.","cl":[],"ag":["வயவர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:57"},"so:1291":{"book":"soolamani","num":1291,"t":["ஓவிலயில் விசுமொரு வன்னது விலக்குங்","காவலொடு மீளுமொரு வன்னவர் கருத்தின்","ஆவதது வன்றியய னின்றவர்கள் காணும்","பாவனைய ரல்லர்பல பாடியினி யென்னோ."],"p":"","r":"One thrusts with a spear while the other parries and turns back on his guard; since the onlookers see only the result and not the mind behind it, the poet asks what use is saying more.","n":"The poet breaks off the description and says it cannot be told fully.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","kaalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1291","lb":"SO1291","mm":1,"ev":"One fighter thrusts a spear and the other parries and recovers.","cl":[],"ag":["வயவர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:57"},"so:1292":{"book":"soolamani","num":1292,"t":["கொந்தெரி யிரும்பெறிஞர் கொற்செய்கள னொத்தும்","வந்துவன வேங்கைமலர் கால்சிதர்வ போன்று","நுந்தியவர் வீசுமொளி வாணுதிக டாக்கிச்","சிந்தின தழற்பொறி சிதர்ந்ததிசை யெல்லாம்."],"p":"","r":"The clash of iron makes a smithy of the field, sparks scatter like rain of vengai flowers on the ground, and fiery flecks of blades flung at each other fill every quarter.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1292","lb":"SO1292","mm":1,"ev":"Sparks fly from the clashing blades in every direction.","cl":[],"ag":["வயவர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:57"},"so:1293":{"book":"soolamani","num":1293,"t":["ஆளடு மடற்றகைய னாயவரி சேனன்","தோளொடு துதைந்தெறியும் வாளதனை நோக்கிக்","கோளொடு மடுத்தகுளிர் மாமதிய மொப்ப்","வாளொடு மடுத்துமணி கேடக மறைத்தான்."],"p":"","r":"Arisenan, whose strength is crushing, sees the sword thrown at him from his foe's shoulder and covers his body with his jewelled shield like the cool moon seized by a planet.","n":"","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maram","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1293","lb":"SO1293","mm":1,"ev":"Arisenan guards himself with his shield against the thrown sword.","cl":[],"ag":["அரிசேனன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:57"},"so:1294":{"book":"soolamani","num":1294,"t":["ஆங்குமுன் னெறிந்துபெயர் கின்றவரி சேனன்","பூங்கம ழலங்கலுடை மார்பமிரு போழாய்","நீங்கவெறிந் தானெடிய மாற்கிளைய காளை","ஓங்கிய விசும்பினவர் கொண்டன ரொளித்தார்."],"p":"","r":"As Arisenan strikes first and draws back, a younger youth hews his garlanded chest in two, and the watchers in the sky carry him off and hide him.","n":"The man who hits Arisenan is called only the younger one of the Mal (மாற்கு இளைய காளை); the verse gives no name.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maram","kolai","kedu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1294","lb":"SO1294","mm":1,"ev":"A younger youth cuts Arisenan down after Arisenan has struck and drawn back.","cl":[],"ag":["அரிசேனன்","மாற்கு இளைய காளை"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:57"},"so:1295":{"book":"soolamani","num":1295,"t":["அழலான்பெய ரவன்மைத்துன னரிசேனனை யெறியக்","கழலான்கட லொளியான்றமர் கலந்தார்த்தனர் கரிய","நிழலான்றமர் கரிந்தார்சில ரிரிந்தார்பலர் நெரிந்தார்","தழலாரயில் வலனேந்துபு சார்ந்தார்தலை சரிந்தார்."],"p":"","r":"A kinsman of one called Azhalan strikes Arisenan, the army cries out, some are burnt, some flee and many are crushed, and the others rush up with raised spears and are cut down.","n":"The stanza was printed in eight half-lines and joined; the identity of the striker is not clear from the Tamil.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maram","kolai","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1295","lb":"SO1295","mm":1,"ev":"A kinsman of Azhalan strikes Arisenan and the armies clash, with many killed.","cl":[],"ag":["அழலான் மைத்துனன்","வயவர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:57"},"so:1296":{"book":"soolamani","num":1296,"t":["அரிசேனனங் கழிவாதல்கண் டயில்வாளொளி மிளிரர்","விரிசீர்வட்ட மணிகேடகஞ் சுழலாநகு வருவான்","பரிசேரணி யுயர்தேர்மிகு பலயானையு மெறியாத்","திரிசாரிகை நிகரானவ ருளரோதிறல் வினவும்."],"p":"","r":"Seeing Arisenan fall, a warrior with swinging shield and flashing sword comes whirling, cuts through horses and elephants and chariots alike, and the poet asks whether any can match such circling skill.","n":"He is not named; line 4 is a rhetorical question on his skill, and the sense is held loosely.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maram","padai","perumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1296","lb":"SO1296","mm":1,"ev":"A warrior rushes in after Arisenan's fall and slays the cavalry and elephants.","cl":[],"ag":["அறியப்படாத வீரன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:57"},"so:1297":{"book":"soolamani","num":1297,"t":["சிகரிம்முடி திருமாமணி செறிகுண்டல மிலங்கத்","திகிரிப்படை யரசன்றிறல் சிறக்கவெனப் புகழ்ந்து","பகருந்நல குணசேனனும் பருவம்முகி லிடிபோற்","புகரும்மத களிறென்னவும் புலியென்னவுந் திரிவான்."],"p":"","r":"Gunasenan, praising the discus-king's strength and wishing it to flourish, wears a mountain-tall crown and bright earrings and roams like a thundering cloud and a rutting elephant and a tiger.","n":"The discus-king is Arukkakirthi in the verses that follow; here he is named only by the discus.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","pugazh","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1297","lb":"SO1297","mm":1,"ev":"Gunasenan goes about the field praising his king and fighting.","cl":[],"ag":["குணசேனன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:57"},"so:1298":{"book":"soolamani","num":1298,"t":["அருக்க கீர்த்திதன்","பெருக்கம் வாழ்த்தியே","திருக்கை வேலினா","னெரிக்கு மாற்றலான்."],"p":"","r":"The spear-bearer who praises Arukkakirthi's greatness has a strength that burns the foe.","n":"The warrior is not named in these four short lines; he is read as Gunasenan from the verse before.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maram","pugazh","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1298","lb":"SO1298","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["குணசேனன்","அருக்க கீர்த்தி"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:57"},"so:1299":{"book":"soolamani","num":1299,"t":["பொன்னங் குன்றவன்","மின்னும் வாளினன்","மன்னன் றோழனாழ்","முன்னி வந்தனன்."],"p":"","r":"One like a golden mountain, bright sword in hand, the king's friend, comes thinking of the fight.","n":"","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maram","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1299","lb":"SO1299","mm":1,"ev":"A sword-bearing friend of a king comes to fight.","cl":[],"ag":["மன்னன் தோழன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:57"},"so:1300":{"book":"soolamani","num":1300,"t":["வந்த வன்பெய","ரிந்தி ரன்னெனுங்","கந்து கொல்களி","றுந்து காமனே."],"p":"","r":"The newcomer's name is Indiran, a rutting elephant in his drive and like Kaman in beauty.","n":"","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maram","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1300","lb":"SO1300","mm":1,"ev":"The newcomer is named Indiran.","cl":[],"ag":["இந்திரன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:57"},"so:1301":{"book":"soolamani","num":1301,"t":["இருவ ரும்மெதிர்","பொருதும் வேலையின்","அருகு நின்றவர்","வெருவி யோடினார்."],"p":"","r":"As the two meet to fight, those standing near fear and run away.","n":"","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maram","acham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1301","lb":"SO1301","mm":1,"ev":"The two fighters face each other and the bystanders flee.","cl":[],"ag":["குணசேனன்","இந்திரன்","அருகு நின்றவர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:57"},"so:1302":{"book":"soolamani","num":1302,"t":["வாளி னாலொரு","தோளை வீழ்த்தவோர்","தோளி னாலவன்","வாளை யிட்டனன்."],"p":"","r":"One strikes off one of the other's shoulders with a sword, and the other with his remaining shoulder hurls his sword back.","n":"The four short lines do not say which of the two loses the shoulder.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maram","kolai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1302","lb":"SO1302","mm":1,"ev":"One fighter loses a shoulder to the sword and the other throws his sword in return.","cl":[],"ag":["குணசேனன்","இந்திரன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:57"},"so:1304":{"book":"soolamani","num":1303,"t":["குணசேனன் வீழக் கண்டு கூற்றினுங் கொடிய நீரான்","இணைசேனை தன்னு ளுள்ளோ னியம்பிய களிப்பின் மிக்கான்","கணைசேர்ந்த தூணித் தோளான் கைச்சிலை பிடித்துக் கொண்டு","திணைசேர வருக வென்று வரசேனன் றிகழ்ந்து நின்றான்."],"p":"","r":"On seeing Gunasenan fall, Varasenan, fiercer than Death and delighted at the word of his comrade, takes up his bow and arrows and stands taunting, bidding the foe to come and match him.","n":"Who is addressed by வருக is not named in this verse; the next verse gives the answering archer.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maram","vekuli","pagai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1304","lb":"SO1304","mm":1,"ev":"Varasenan, seeing Gunasenan fall, takes his bow and challenges the enemy.","cl":[],"ag":["வரசேனன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["வரசேனன்","காமுகன்"],"ch":"so:57"},"so:1305":{"book":"soolamani","num":1304,"t":["மற்றவ னிற்ப தோர்ந்து மதகளி றனைய காளை","கொற்றவ னருக்க கீர்த்தி குணம்புகழ்ந் தாடிப் பாடிக்","கற்றவன் கலைக ளெல்லாங் காமுக னென்னும் பேரான்","பற்றிய வில்லு மம்பும் பாங்குடன் பரித்து வந்தான்."],"p":"","r":"Hearing of him, Kamukan, a youth like a rutting elephant who has learned all the arts, praises the king Arukkakirthi's qualities and comes up bearing bow and arrow.","n":"","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maram","kalvi","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1305","lb":"SO1305","mm":1,"ev":"Kamukan arrives with bow and arrow to meet Varasenan.","cl":[],"ag":["காமுகன்","அருக்க கீர்த்தி"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:57"},"so:1306":{"book":"soolamani","num":1305,"t":["வில்லொரு கையி னேந்தி வெங்கணை குழைய வாங்கிச்","சொல்லரு மாரி போலத் தொடுத்தவன் விடுத்த லோடும்","மல்லுறு காளை தன்மேல் வராமலே விலக்கி யிட்டுக்","கல்லெனக் கலங்கி வீழக் கைச்சிலை கணையே றிட்டான்."],"p":"","r":"One draws his bow and showers arrows like rain, the strong youth wards them off before they reach him, and with an arrow of his own makes the archer reel and fall.","n":"Which side falls is not named; it is read from 'the youth turned his arrows' that Kamukan wards off Varasenan's shafts.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maram","padai","kalvi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1306","lb":"SO1306","mm":1,"ev":"An archer's volley is turned aside and the archer is shot down.","cl":[],"ag":["வரசேனன்","காமுகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:57"},"so:1307":{"book":"soolamani","num":1306,"t":["கார்செயன் முழங்கி யார்ப்பக் காளையுங் கனன்று மிக்க","வார்சிலை வணங்க வாங்கி வாய்புக விடுத்த லோடும்","போர்செயுங் களத்து வீழ்ந்தான் புகழ்வர சேன னென்னத்","தார்செய்தா னவர்க டம்முட் டானவ னொருவன் வந்தான்."],"p":"","r":"The youth draws his great bow and looses an arrow that lands in a warrior's mouth, so that a man named Pukazh Varasenan falls on the field, and then a danava of the foe steps out among them.","n":"The youth is not named; he is taken to be Tivittan from the later epithet in 1319, and the clause naming the fallen man and the danava is hard to parse.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maram","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1307","lb":"SO1307","mm":1,"ev":"A warrior of the youth's side shoots down one enemy, and a danava comes out in his place.","cl":[],"ag":["செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:58"},"so:1308":{"book":"soolamani","num":1307,"t":["குடர்மாலைக டலைசூடின குழவித்தலை குழையாப்","படர்காதினுள் ளவைபெய்தன பகுவாயது குருதித்","தொடர்மாமழை மதயானைகை துணியாவவை யிடையே","சுடர்மாலைகள் விடுசூலமொ டொருவன்றிரி கின்றான்."],"p":"","r":"A danava wears garlands of entrails and skulls, hangs infants' heads in his ears, and strides among cut-off elephant trunks with a flaming trident.","n":"The verse never names him; 1312 gives the name Arikethanan to the man who comes at this point, so the identification rests on that.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maram","veruvu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1308","lb":"SO1308","mm":1,"ev":"The danava prowls the field, hung with gore.","cl":[],"ag":["அரிகேதனன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:58"},"so:1309":{"book":"soolamani","num":1308,"t":["கள்ளாவது குருதிப்புனல் கலனாவது கையே","நள்ளாதவ ருடலம்பிற கறியாவது நமக்கென்","றுள்ளாதவ ருளராங்கொலிவ் வுலகின்னென வுரையர்","விள்ளாதவர் சிலர்பின்செல விரல்வீளைகள் விளியா."],"p":"","r":"Blood is his wine and his hands the cup, enemy bodies his meat; those behind him snap their fingers and whistle, saying that no one in this world will fail to think of such food.","n":"","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maram","veruvu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1309","lb":"SO1309","mm":1,"ev":"The danava advances in blood-lust with followers behind him.","cl":[],"ag":["அரிகேதனன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:58"},"so:1310":{"book":"soolamani","num":1309,"t":["களியானையி னெயிறாயின பறியாவவை கறியர்","அளியாதுபல் படையாளர்க ளடையார்களை யுடனே","ஒளிவாளிடை யிடைவிட்டுட லுருவாவுயிர் பருகாத்","தெளியாதெதிர் வருவாராயி லுருவாவவை செறியா."],"p":"","r":"Elephant tusks he tears out, enemy soldiers he drinks dry with his bright sword, and none who meets him unaware escapes.","n":"","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maram","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1310","lb":"SO1310","mm":1,"ev":"The danava slaughters soldiers and elephants as he comes.","cl":[],"ag":["அரிகேதனன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:58"},"so:1311":{"book":"soolamani","num":1310,"t":["எரிபோல்வன சுரிபங்கியொ டிருள்போலிருண் மெய்யேர்","டரிபோலதி ரகல்வானுற நிமிராவடி புடையாப்","பரிபோல்வன பிடியாவுட லடியாவிடை மறிதேர்","பொரிபோலெழ வுதையாவிவன் வருகின்றதொர் பொலிவே."],"p":"","r":"Black as night, flame-like, with a lion's roar, he kicks the chariots and horses of the field so that they burst like popped grain; the poet calls this the splendour of his coming.","n":"","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maram","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1311","lb":"SO1311","mm":1,"ev":"The danava smashes chariots and horses as he advances.","cl":[],"ag":["அரிகேதனன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:58"},"so:1312":{"book":"soolamani","num":1311,"t":["ஆழிப்படை யுடையான்றம னரிகேதன னென்போன்","பாழிப்படை பொருவாரொடு பொருவன்பல வறியேன்","ஏழைப்படை யிதுவோவெனக் கெதிராகுவ தாயில்","வாழிப்படை பொருதென்னென வையாநனி வந்தான்."],"p":"","r":"The man named Arikethanan, the discus-lord's Death, tells himself that he has fought many armies and asks what use this poor army is as a match, and so comes on.","n":"The text calls him the discus-lord's தமன், kin, and compares him to Yama; the boast in the second half is his own thought or speech.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maram","maanam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1312","lb":"SO1312","mm":1,"ev":"Arikethanan, kin of the discus-lord, comes to the fight scorning the opposing army.","cl":[],"ag":["அரிகேதனன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:58"},"so:1313":{"book":"soolamani","num":1312,"t":["கள்ளாற்களி யிலனாலிகல் களமண்டிய செருவின்","உள்ளாற்களி யுற்றானிவ னுயிருண்கென வுருவி","நள்ளாதவர் தலைவவ்விய நகைவாளது வீசித்","தள்ளாதவ னெதிரேமிகு சார்த்தூலக னேற்றான்."],"p":"","r":"Sarthulakan, joyful in battle though not drunk, draws his sword and meets the danava with the cry that he will drink his life.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","aanmai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1313","lb":"SO1313","mm":1,"ev":"Sarthulakan of the youth's side takes up the danava's challenge.","cl":[],"ag":["சார்த்தூலகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:58"},"so:1314":{"book":"soolamani","num":1313,"t":["வரையாலென முகிலாலென விருளாலென மறியும்","திரையார்கட லளவேசெல விரியுந்நனி சிறுகும்","நிரையாமுகின் முடிதேய்தர நிமிருந்நில மிதனுட்","புரையாரிட மறையும்மிது பொருகின்றதொர் பொலிவே."],"p":"","r":"As dark as hill, cloud and night, the foe swells like a sea and then shrinks into a single point on earth; the poet calls it the splendour of battle.","n":"The verse is pure description of the two as they close.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maram","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1314","lb":"SO1314","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:58"},"so:1315":{"book":"soolamani","num":1314,"t":["மாலைத்தலை வளர்மாமதி நிகரும்வளை யெயிறுஞ்","சோலைத்தலை மலைபோல்வன தோளும்மிவை யுடையான்","காலைத்தலை யிளஞாயிறு புரைவான்மிசை யெறியாச்","சூலத்தலை நுதியாலவ னாகந்துளை யிட்டான்."],"p":"","r":"The danava, with fangs like the waxing moon and shoulders like wooded hills, drives his trident into the chest of his opponent.","n":"The subject is not named; the sense depends on 1316, where the pierced man cuts back.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maram","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1315","lb":"SO1315","mm":1,"ev":"Arikethanan pierces Sarthulakan's chest with his trident.","cl":[],"ag":["அரிகேதனன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:58"},"so:1316":{"book":"soolamani","num":1315,"t":["இடுவானையவ் விடுசூலமொ டுடலும்மிரு துணியாப்","படவீசின னயில்வாளது படலும்பல மாயன்","அடவாமையி னுருவம்முத லதுவேதன தாகத்","தடமால்வரை யெனவீழ்தலு முடைவார்தம ரானார்."],"p":"","r":"Pierced as he was, Sarthulakan hurls the sword and cuts the thrower in two with the very trident, the huge man falls like a mountain, and his own kin are broken.","n":"Who falls is read from the next verse, which says the man's day of life was spent.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maram","aanmai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1316","lb":"SO1316","mm":1,"ev":"Sarthulakan kills Arikethanan and the discus-lord's kin lose heart.","cl":[],"ag":["சார்த்தூலகன்","அரிகேதனன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:58"},"so:1317":{"book":"soolamani","num":1316,"t":["வாழுநா ளுலந்து மற்றவன் மண்மேல் மலையென மறிதலு மலைமேல்","ஆழியான் றமர்க ளஞ்சினா ரஞ்சு மாயிடை யடுதிற லுடையான்","ஊழிநா ளெரியுங் கூற்றமு முருமு மொப்பவன் கைப்படை நவின்றான்","சூழிமா லியானைத் துளைமதஞ் செறிப்பத் தோன்றினான் றூமகே தனனே."],"p":"","r":"His days of life spent, the fallen man lies like a hill while the discus-lord's kin on the mountain fear; then Dhumaketu, like the fire of the age's end and Death and thunder, arrives on a rutting elephant.","n":"The phrase that his days were used up lets the poet put the death in fate beside Sarthulakan's skill; the text gives both without ranking.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","oozh","kootru"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1317","lb":"SO1317","mm":1,"ev":"Arikethanan is dead and Dhumaketu rides in to take the field.","cl":[["a man dies when the days of his life are used up","general","asserted",1,"narrator"]],"ag":["தூமகேதன்","ஆழியான் றமர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:58"},"so:1318":{"book":"soolamani","num":1317,"t":["மலையெ டுத்திடுகோ மாநிலம் பிளக்கோ மறிகட லறவிறைத் திடுகோ","உலைமடுத் துலகம் பதலையா வூழித் தீமடுத் துயிர்களட் டுண்கோ","சிலையிடத் துடையார் கணைவலத் துடையார் சிலர்நின்று செய்வதீங் கென்னோ","நிலையிடத் தவரு ணிகரெனக் குளரே னேடுமின் சென்றென நின்றான்."],"p":"","r":"Dhumaketu boasts aloud: shall he lift the mountain, split the earth, drain the sea, burn the world and eat its lives; what can bow-men and archers do, and who among the foe equals him?","n":"","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maram","maanam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1318","lb":"SO1318","mm":1,"ev":"Dhumaketu boasts that no enemy equals him.","cl":[],"ag":["தூமகேதன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["தூமகேதன்","நம்மலை வாழ்வார்"],"ch":"so:58"},"so:1319":{"book":"soolamani","num":1318,"t":["வண்டினம் பாடு மாலையன் வரித்த கச்சினன் வயிரப்பூ ணிரைத்த","தண்டினன் கழலன் றமனியத் தாரான் சார்ந்தனன் சார்தலு மவனைக்","கண்டன னன்றே கடலொளி மேனிக் காளைதன் மாமனுக் கிளையான்","உண்டினி நமக்கோர் போரென வெதிரே யுவந்துசென் றவற்கிவை யுரைத்தான்."],"p":"","r":"The young brother of the dark-hued youth's father-in-law, wearing garlands, a diamond-studded mace and a golden gem-wreath, sees him coming and goes joyfully to answer that a battle has come.","n":"Read as the king's younger brother (மாமனுக் கிளையான்), whom 1321 names as the owner of the blazing chariot; the speech opens in this verse and runs on in 1320.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maram","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1319","lb":"SO1319","mm":1,"ev":"The Vidyadhara king's younger brother goes to meet Dhumaketu.","cl":[],"ag":["இளைய வேந்தன்","தூமகேதன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:58"},"so:1320":{"book":"soolamani","num":1319,"t":["மலையெடுத் திடுவாய் மாநிலம் பிளப்பாய் மறிகட லறவிறைத் திடுவாய்","உலைமடுத் துலகம் பதலையா வூழித் தீமடுத் துயிர்களட் டுண்பாய்","நிலையிடத் துளரோ நிகரெனக் கென்பாய் நின்றனை நிகருனக் காகித்","தொலைவிடத் தல்லாற் சொல்லிவை நுங்கட் கொழியுமோ தூமகே தனனே."],"p":"","r":"He answers Dhumaketu's boast clause by clause: you would lift the mountain, split the earth, drink the sea and eat lives; will you claim no one is your equal? Stand, and your match will come until you are destroyed.","n":"","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maram","maanam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1320","lb":"SO1320","mm":1,"ev":"The Vidyadhara king's younger brother mocks Dhumaketu's boast.","cl":[],"ag":["இளைய வேந்தன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["இளைய வேந்தன்","தூமகேதன்"],"ch":"so:58"},"so:1321":{"book":"soolamani","num":1320,"t":["என்றலு மதுகேட் டெரியுடைத் தேரோ னென்பவ னாமிவ னென்றே","நன்றுநன் றென்று நக்கன னக்கே நாணிலர் நம்மலை வாழ்வார்","இன்றெனக் கெதிராய் நீகொலோ பொருவா யென்றன னினையன மொழியாக்","குன்றினும் பெரியான் கூற்றினும் வெய்யோன் கொண்டனன் றண்டுகை வலித்தே."],"p":"","r":"Dhumaketu hears him, laughs, and says that this is the one called the lord of the blazing chariot, and asks whether he will really fight him and not shrink, and raises his mace.","n":"The title of the man he laughs at is எரியுடைத் தேரோன், which is what ties this scene to the chariot of the blazing fire in the Vidyadhara king's younger brother.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maram","maanam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1321","lb":"SO1321","mm":1,"ev":"Dhumaketu mocks him and takes up his mace.","cl":[],"ag":["தூமகேதன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["தூமகேதன்","இளைய வேந்தன்"],"ch":"so:58"},"so:1322":{"book":"soolamani","num":1321,"t":["இன்றெனக் கெதிராய் நீகொலோ பொருவா யென்றிகழ்ந் துரைத்தனை யேடா","நின்றெனக் கெதிரா நீர்மையர் நின்போ னிரம்பவாய் திறந்துரைப் பவரோ","என்றன னெனலு மெதிர்தெழித் தவனு மெழுந்தன னெழுந்தன னிவனும்","அன்றுபோர் மலைந்தா ரதிர்ந்ததிவ் வுலக மமரொழித் தரசரு நின்றார்."],"p":"","r":"The man answers that Dhumaketu was right to scorn him, and asks if any who stood against him ever stood speaking as loudly; they rise to the fight and the world shakes and the kings stand clear.","n":"","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maram","maanam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1322","lb":"SO1322","mm":1,"ev":"The two rise and join battle; the other kings stand aside.","cl":[],"ag":["இளைய வேந்தன்","தூமகேதன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["இளைய வேந்தன்","தூமகேதன்"],"ch":"so:58"},"so:1323":{"book":"soolamani","num":1322,"t":["ஆர்த்தன திசைக ளதிர்ந்ததிவ் வுலக மலைகடல் கலங்கின விருளாற்","போர்த்தது விசும்பு புலம்பின விலங்கல் புரண்டன பொருவரைத் துறுகற்","சார்த்தினர் புடைக்குந் தண்டின ரெதிரத் தாங்கியும் வாங்கியுந் தடுத்தும்","வேர்த்தனர் மெய்யால் வெதும்பினர் மனத்தால் விசும்பினை மயங்கவே திரிந்தார்."],"p":"","r":"The directions roar, the earth shakes, the seas churn, darkness covers the sky and hills roll; the two strike with mace and shield and sweat though their hearts burn, circling in the sky.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","veruvu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1323","lb":"SO1323","mm":1,"ev":"The duel of the two shakes the world.","cl":[],"ag":["தூமகேதன்","இளைய வேந்தன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:58"},"so:1324":{"book":"soolamani","num":1323,"t":["கண்டவர்க் கெல்லாங் கண்ணுளார் போல்வார் காண்டலுக் கரிதவ ருருவம்","தண்டின தொளியர் தங்களார்ப் பிசையுந் தயங்குதா ராரமும் விரவி","எண்டிசை மருங்கு மிடைநிலத் திடையு மிருள்கெழு விசும்பின தகமுங்","கொண்டன சிலம்பு குலுங்கின விலங்கல் கூற்றமுந் தலைபனித் ததுவே."],"p":"","r":"To all who look, their forms are hard to see; mace, ring and garland, jewels and noise fill the eight quarters, the ground and the dark sky, the mountains tremble and even Death shivers.","n":"The verse describes the fighting rather than telling an act.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1324","lb":"SO1324","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["தூமகேதன்","இளைய வேந்தன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:58"},"so:1325":{"book":"soolamani","num":1324,"t":["தட்டுப்போ ரதனுட் டமனியக் கடிப்புந் தாரினோ டாரமுஞ் சரியப்","பட்டுப்போ யுருண்டா னவருளங் கொருத்தன் பருவரை கரியதொன் றனையான்","துட்டப்போ ரியானைத் தூமகே தனனுந் தோற்குமோ வொருவனுக் கென்று","மட்டுப்போ ரணிந்த மணிமுடி மன்னர் மயங்கினார் மானமு மிழந்தார்."],"p":"","r":"One of the fighters, black as a great mountain, falls rolling from the fray with his gold ear-stud and garland shattered, and the crowned kings say, will even Dhumaketu lose to one man, and lose their pride.","n":"Read as Dhumaketu's fall, from the kings' reaction naming him.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maram","maanam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1325","lb":"SO1325","mm":1,"ev":"Dhumaketu is struck down; the kings who stood with him are dismayed.","cl":[],"ag":["தூமகேதன்","இளைய வேந்தன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:58"},"so:1326":{"book":"soolamani","num":1325,"t":["பொருதாங் கழிந்து புகைகேது வீழ வரிகேது முன்ன முடிய","எரிதாங்கு வேலொ டினியீங்கு நின்று பெறுகின்ற தென்னை யெழுகென்","றரிதாங்க ணாவ தெளிதாகு மாறொ ரமர்செய்கை கொண்டு பிறர்முன்","கரிதாங்க ளான கழன்மன்ன ரேறு வருமங்கொர் காளை கடிதே."],"p":"","r":"The mighty fall, banners drop and flames of the spear remain; the kings ask what they gain by standing and bid him rise, and a young man comes quickly.","n":"The wording is difficult; the speakers are the discus-lord's kings and the arriving youth is identified as Azhalvegan from 1328.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maram","maanam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1326","lb":"SO1326","mm":1,"ev":"After Dhumaketu's fall another young warrior is sent in.","cl":[],"ag":["அழல்வேகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:58"},"so:1327":{"book":"soolamani","num":1326,"t":["கணிகொண் டலர்ந்த நறவேங்கை யோடு கமழ்கின்ற காந்த ளிதழால்","அணிகொண் டலர்ந்த வனமாலை சூடி யகிலாவி குஞ்சி கமழ","மணிகுண்ட லங்க ளிருபாலும் வந்து வரையாக மீது திவளத்","துணிகொண் டிலங்கு சுடர்வேலி னோடு வருவா னிததென்கொ றுணிவே."],"p":"","r":"He comes with a spear, wearing flowered garlands, with scent in his locks and gems swinging at his ears, and the poet asks what is the resolve of this man.","n":"","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maram","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1327","lb":"SO1327","mm":1,"ev":"Azhalvegan comes to the field.","cl":[],"ag":["அழல்வேகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:58"},"so:1328":{"book":"soolamani","num":1327,"t":["அருமாலை வேல்வல் லழல்வேக னாகு மவனாயி லாக வமைக","எரிமலை வேல்வ லிளையார்க ணிற்க விவனென்னொ டேற்க வெனவே","பொருமாலை வாள்கை பொலிகே டகத்த னணிபோ திலங்கு முடியன்","செருமாலை மன்ன ரிறைதேவ சேன னெதிரே சிவந்து செலவே."],"p":"","r":"Speaking of the spear-skilled Azhalvegan, he says: if it is he, let it be; let the young ones stand aside and let him face me; Devasenan, lord of kings, goes red before him.","n":"The speaker of the opening is not named; Devasenan's words fit because he then goes out.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maram","maanam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1328","lb":"SO1328","mm":1,"ev":"Devasenan goes out against Azhalvegan.","cl":[],"ag":["தேவசேனன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["தேவசேனன்","அழல்வேகன்"],"ch":"so:58"},"so:1329":{"book":"soolamani","num":1328,"t":["மலைமேலு மென்னை மதியாது வாழ்தி மனிசர்க் குவந்து படையாய்","நிலமேலு நின்று பொருவா னினைந்து வருவாய் நினக்கி துறுமே","சலமேலு மின்ன வுடையாயை வென்று தலைகொண்ட பின்னை நுமரைக்","குலம்வேர் களைந்து குடிபொன்று விப்ப னிதுயான் மகிழ்ந்த குணனே."],"p":"","r":"Azhalvegan says to him: you live scorning me even on the hill and give nothing to men; you will fight on earth; when I win and take your head I will uproot your whole clan.","n":"","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maram","maanam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1329","lb":"SO1329","mm":1,"ev":"Azhalvegan threatens Devasenan with the destruction of his line.","cl":[],"ag":["அழல்வேகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["அழல்வேகன்","தேவசேனன்"],"ch":"so:58"},"so:1330":{"book":"soolamani","num":1329,"t":["மலைமேலு நின்னை மதியாது வாழ்வன் மனிதர்க் குவந்து படையாய்","நிலமேலு நின்று பொருவான் புகுந்த நிலைகண்டு நின்று மிஙனே","சலமே யுரைத்தி யிதுவோவு னாண்மை தழல்வேக வென்ன வெதிரே","உலமேசு தோளி னொளிவேலி னோடு மொருவா னெதிர்ந்து பொருவான்."],"p":"","r":"Devasenan answers with the same words turned back, calls Azhalvegan's boast false, asks if this is his manhood, and meets him spear to spear.","n":"The reply repeats the opening of 1329 almost word for word, so the exchange is a mirror.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","maanam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1330","lb":"SO1330","mm":1,"ev":"Devasenan returns the taunt and the two fight.","cl":[],"ag":["தேவசேனன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["தேவசேனன்","அழல்வேகன்"],"ch":"so:58"},"so:1331":{"book":"soolamani","num":1330,"t":["வாள்வீசு மாறும் வடிவேலின் வந்த வடிவேல் விலங்கும் வகையுந்","தோள்வீசு மாறு மவைமீளு மாறு மிடைசொல்லும் வீர மொழியுந்","தாள்வீசு போழ்து கழலோங்கு மாறு மெதிர்தாங்க ளார்க்கு மொலியுந்","நாள்வீய லன்றி யுரைவீய யாவர் நவில்வார் நமக்கு மரிதே."],"p":"","r":"The poet says no one could tell, except at the day's end, the ways of the sword and spear, the turns of the shoulders, the war-talk and the sounds of the fighters.","n":"The narrator breaks off to say the fight cannot be fully described.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maram","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1331","lb":"SO1331","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:58"},"so:1332":{"book":"soolamani","num":1331,"t":["மாலா லெதிர்ந்து மலைவாயை நீடு பொரவைப்ப தென்னை யினியென்","வேலா லழிப்ப னெனவே லெறிந்து விறல்வேக னார்ப்ப மறவோன்","மேலா லிலங்கு மணிகேட கத்தி னொருபால் விலங்கி யொருபாற்","றோலாத வாளி னெறியத் துணிந்து சுடர்கான்று வீழ்ந்த ததுவே."],"p":"","r":"Seeing the foe stand against him and fix his long spear on the hill-like mass, the champion hurls his own spear to destroy it; the fierce Vegan roars, and the warrior's sword cuts him down, so that he falls blazing.","n":"Who throws the first spear and who is struck cannot be fixed from the verse; the next verses name Devasenan as the warrior and Thazhalvegan as the one who falls.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maram","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1332","lb":"SO1332","mm":1,"ev":"A spear is hurled and answered; the warrior's sword fells Vegan in the duel.","cl":[],"ag":["தேவசேனன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:59"},"so:1333":{"book":"soolamani","num":1332,"t":["எய்வே லறுத்து வறியானை நோக்கி யெறியாது நிற்ப வவனோர்","நெய்வேல் பெயர்த்து நிருமித்த தேந்தி யுருமொத்து நேர்ந்து பொருதான்","வெவ்வே றெருட்டி யொருதோ ணிமிர்த்தி விரலொன்று சுட்டி வரவே","வைவேலி னோடு நிமிர்கின்ற தோளை யறவீசி னானம் மறவோன்."],"p":"","r":"The warrior cuts away the shaft that is thrown at him, waits without throwing, and when the other raises a ghee-tempered spear and charges like thunder, he lops off the raised shoulder together with its spear.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","anjaamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1333","lb":"SO1333","mm":1,"ev":"The warrior cuts down the spear and the arm that raised it.","cl":[],"ag":["தேவசேனன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:59"},"so:1334":{"book":"soolamani","num":1333,"t":["நெய்யுற்ற வேலு மொருதோளும் வீழ வொருதோளி னீடு செருவைச்","செய்யுற்ற போழ்தி னெதிரே விலங்கி யிதுதேவ சேனன் மொழியும்","கையுற்ற தொன்று கவலே னுனக்கி துறுமாறு போக வெனவும்","மையுற்ற காளை வருவானை வாளி னுயிர்வவ்வி னானம் மறவோன்."],"p":"","r":"With the spear and one arm already fallen, the foe fights on with the other; the warrior, calling out that he does not mind what is in the enemy's hand, cuts him down and takes his life.","n":"The speech is given as Devasenan's own words; the one who falls is named only in 1335.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","anjaamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1334","lb":"SO1334","mm":1,"ev":"Devasenan answers the one-armed foe's attack with his sword and kills him.","cl":[],"ag":["தேவசேனன்","தழல்வேகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["தேவசேனன்",""],"ch":"so:59"},"so:1335":{"book":"soolamani","num":1334,"t":["தாம மார்ந்த மணியைம்பாற் றைய றாதை மைத்துனனாஞ்","சேம மார்ந்த தனிச்செங்கோற் றேவ சேனன் கைவாளாற்","சாம வண்ணன் றழல்வேகன் சாய்ந்தான் சாய்ந்த பொழுதத்தே","தூம மாரங் கமழ்குஞ்சிச் சுவண கேது தோன்றினனால்."],"p":"","r":"By the sword of Devasenan, the king of the single sceptre and kinsman of the Aimpal land, the dark Thazhalvegan falls; at that moment Suvarnaketu, his hair fragrant with incense, appears.","n":"The verse names the sceptre of Devasenan as the cause of the kill, so the royal sceptre stands behind the sword.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sengol","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1335","lb":"SO1335","mm":1,"ev":"Devasenan kills Thazhalvegan, and Suvarnaketu comes onto the field.","cl":[],"ag":["தேவசேனன்","தழல்வேகன்","சுவணகேது"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:59"},"so:1336":{"book":"soolamani","num":1335,"t":["அஃதே யஃதே யங்கார வேக னாங்கோ ரயில்வாளால்","வெய்தாங் குற்று வீடினனா னன்றே நன்றே மறுமாற்றம்","மைதோய் மலையும் மண்ணகமு நமதாச் செய்வென் செய்யேனேற்","செய்தா ரமர ருலகாள்வ னிரண்டி லொன்று திண்ணமிதே."],"p":"","r":"A speaker cries that Angaravegan has died by a bright sword and vows to make the hill and earth his own, or else, if he fails, to rule the world of the gods: one of the two is sure.","n":"The speaker is not named; Suvarnaketu is inferred from his arrival in 1335. The vow of earth or heaven is a warrior's vow, not a school's doctrine.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maram","pugazh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1336","lb":"SO1336","mm":1,"ev":"A vow of victory or heaven is made over a fallen ally.","cl":[["a warrior who falls or wins in battle gains either the earth or the world of the gods","general","reported",4,"suvanakethu"]],"ag":["அங்காரவேகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சுவணகேது",""],"ch":"so:59"},"so:1337":{"book":"soolamani","num":1336,"t":["என்னா விரண்டு மருங்கினுமற் றிளநல் யானைக் குழாஞ்சூழப்","பொன்னார் தேரும் புரவிகளு மிடைந்து பூமி பொறைகூர","முன்னாற் செல்ல வருவானை முந்நீர் வண்ணன் றன்மாமன்","மின்னார் விளங்கு விறல்வேலான் கண்டே வெகுண்டு மேற்சென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"On both sides elephants, golden chariots and horses crowd in until the earth groans, and the sea-coloured one's uncle, seeing the champion come before the host, flies into rage and goes forward.","n":"Whether the sea-coloured one is Thivittan and the uncle Devasenan, or the reverse, is not fixed by the verse.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["padai","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1337","lb":"SO1337","mm":1,"ev":"The armies close and the uncle of the sea-coloured one charges.","cl":[],"ag":["சுவணகேது","தேவசேனன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:59"},"so:1338":{"book":"soolamani","num":1337,"t":["கானற் புரவி கலிமாவோ டெதிர்ந்த கருங்கை மதவேழ","மான யானை தம்மோடே மலைந்த தேரு மாறேற்ற","ஈன மில்லா விளையாரோ டிளையார் திளைத்தா ரிவ்வகையே","தானை தம்முட் டாக்கலுறத் தாமுந் தம்முட் டலைப்பெய்தார்."],"p":"","r":"Horses meet horses, elephants meet elephants, chariots meet chariots, and the armies strike each other with young fighters against young fighters.","n":"Pure battle description built on pairing like with like.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["padai","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1338","lb":"SO1338","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["தானை"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:59"},"so:1339":{"book":"soolamani","num":1338,"t":["அருவி யிலங்கு மதயானை யனல வூன்றி யணைபோழ்திற்","குருவ ரோடு படைபொருதல் கூடிற் றன்று குலவேந்தே","பொருவ ரென்னப் படுவாரங் கொருவர் போந்து பொரவொருவர்","ஒருவி நிற்ற லுரங்கொல்லோ வென்றா னுவண மேந்தினான்."],"p":"","r":"A speaker tells the lord of a noble line that no army fights with elders; if one comes forward and another stands aside, can that be courage, he asks.","n":"","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maram","aram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1339","lb":"SO1339","mm":1,"ev":"A challenge to settle the battle by one man against one is put.","cl":[["it is not courage for one to stand aside while another comes forward to fight","general","reported",3,"uvana_bearer"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["உவணமேந்தினான்","சுவணகேது"],"ch":"so:59"},"so:1340":{"book":"soolamani","num":1339,"t":["குரவ ரென்னு முபசார மிருக்கக் கோதை மிளிர்வேலாய்","பொருவ ராயின் யாரோடும் பொருவர் பூமி வேண்டுபவர்","ஒருவி நிற்ப துரங்கொல்லோ வென்னு முரையு முணர்தியிவண்","இருவே முள்ளும் யார்பாலஃ துறுவ தென்றா னொளிமுடியான்."],"p":"","r":"The shining-crowned one answers: even with the title of elder, those who fight will fight anyone who wants the earth; he asks which of the two of them such an act of standing aside suits.","n":"","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maram","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1340","lb":"SO1340","mm":1,"ev":"A reply to the challenge, asking which of the two should fight.","cl":[["those who fight for land fight anyone, elder or not","general","reported",2,"olimudiyan"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["ஒளிமுடியான்","உவணமேந்தினான்"],"ch":"so:59"},"so:1341":{"book":"soolamani","num":1340,"t":["ஆக வமைக வதுவேயவ் வரச நீதி யாகிவிடிற்","போக பொருவ னெனப்புகைந்து பொருவெஞ் சிலையொன் றிடனேந்தி","வேக யானை செலவுந்திச் சிறுநா ணெறிந்து வெஞ்சரங்கண்","மாக மெல்லா முடனடுங்கத் தொடங்கி னானம் மழைபோல்வான்."],"p":"","r":"Agreeing that this should be the king's custom, he storms that he will fight, takes a bow in his left hand, drives his elephant forward, twangs the string and begins to shower arrows that make the whole sky tremble, like a cloud.","n":"The rule of the duel is called அரச நீதி; the verse treats it as a king's custom, not as a doctrine.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maram","padai","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1341","lb":"SO1341","mm":1,"ev":"The duel is accepted and the arrow-storm begins.","cl":[["the king's custom permits settling by single fight","general","reported",1,"suvanakethu"]],"ag":["சுவணகேது"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:59"},"so:1342":{"book":"soolamani","num":1341,"t":["தொடங்கு கின்ற சுடுசரங்கள் சுருங்கி யொருகை செவிகாறும்","முடங்கு மொருகை சிலைவளையா நிமிருங் கண்ட தித்துணையே","இடங்க ளின்றி விசும்பெல்லா மிருள வீர்க்கோ டீர்க்குதையத்","தடங்க லின்றி யடுசரமே மிடைந்த தவனி வட்டமே."],"p":"","r":"Arrows loosed from the string draw close, one hand to the ear, the other bent; no more is seen than this, for the sky is dark with arrows and the round earth is thick with them with no gap.","n":"","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["padai","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1342","lb":"SO1342","mm":1,"ev":"A dense shower of arrows fills the sky and earth.","cl":[],"ag":["சுவணகேது"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:59"},"so:1343":{"book":"soolamani","num":1342,"t":["சொல்லான் மலிந்த புகழினான் சுவண கேது சொரிகின்ற","கல்லார் கொண்டற் பெயல்போலுங் கணையின் மாரி கழல்வேந்தன்","வில்லாற் செய்த விசாலவட்ட மேலு நாலு மருங்கினுமாய்க்","கொல்லாற் செய்த வேலாற்குக் குடையாய் நின்று கவித்ததே."],"p":"","r":"The golden-banner king, famed in word, pours arrows like a shower of stones, and his bows form a great round shield on all sides, covering the spearman as a canopy.","n":"The verse says the arrow-shower becomes a shield for the spear-bearer; who the spearman is must be taken from the next verse.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["padai","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1343","lb":"SO1343","mm":1,"ev":"Suvarnaketu's arrows form a canopy over the spearman.","cl":[],"ag":["சுவணகேது"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:59"},"so:1344":{"book":"soolamani","num":1343,"t":["வில்லாற் செய்த விசாலவட்டந் தன்னா லந்தில் விலக்கப்பட்","டெல்லாத் திசையுஞ் சரஞ்சிந்தி யிருள வீழு மெல்லைதன்னுட்","சொல்லாற் புகழ்தற் கரியான்பாற் றூணி வறிய வாதலுந்திண்","மல்லாற் செய்த தோளான்மேன் மாய வெம்போர் செய்குற்றான்."],"p":"","r":"The wide round of bows turns the shower of arrows aside, they scatter to all quarters and fall; when the quiver is empty, the poet says, he turns to magic and makes ready to fight the strong-armed one with an illusion.","n":"","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maram","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1344","lb":"SO1344","mm":1,"ev":"The arrows are turned aside and the quiver runs dry; magic begins.","cl":[],"ag":["சுவணகேது"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:59"},"so:1345":{"book":"soolamani","num":1344,"t":["செய்ய லுற்ற மாயமதுஞ் சிலையு நிலையுஞ் சுருங்கியவைத்.","தெய்ய லுற்ற பகழியையு மெண்ணி வேந்த னென்செய்தான்","மைய லுற்ற மதயானை மலைப்ப வுந்தி மாற்றானை","நைய லுற்றா யெனவுரையா நாம வாளி சிந்தித்தான்."],"p":"","r":"The magic, bow and stand shrunk, and counting his remaining arrows, what did the king do? He calls out that the foe is wearied by the mad elephant and shoots the arrow named Naama.","n":"","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["padai","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1345","lb":"SO1345","mm":1,"ev":"The king counts his arrows and shoots the arrow named Naama.","cl":[],"ag":["தேவசேனன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:59"},"so:1346":{"book":"soolamani","num":1345,"t":["தொடுத்த வாளி யதுபொழுதே சுவண கேது கையகத்து","மடுத்த சிலையும் பகழியும்வெம் மனத்துக் கொண்ட மாயமதும்","அடுத்துத் துணிசெய் தப்புறம்போ யரசர் குழாங்க ளிரியப்பாய்ந்","துடுத்த தூவி தோன்றாமை நிலத்தி னுள்புக் கொளித்ததே."],"p":"","r":"The arrow cuts down the bow, arrows and the illusion in the foe's hand, flies past as kings scatter, plunges in and hides in the earth with no feather showing.","n":"","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maram","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1346","lb":"SO1346","mm":1,"ev":"The shot arrow cuts the bow and the illusion, then buries itself in the earth.","cl":[],"ag":["சுவணகேது"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:59"},"so:1347":{"book":"soolamani","num":1346,"t":["அம்புஞ் சிலையுங் கைம்மறிய வகத்த விஞ்சை துணிப்புண்டு","வம்புகின்ற மனத்தினனாய் வெய்யோன் மீட்டு விறலோன்மேல்","வம்பு கொண்ட வளையங்கள் கணையம் விட்டு விட்டேறு","செம்பொன் னாழி யிவையெல்லாஞ் சென்று பாயச் சிந்தித்தான்."],"p":"","r":"With his arrows, bow and illusion cut, the scorching foe, his mind in a rage, thinks to hurl rings, javelins, and golden discs at the champion all at once.","n":"","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maram","padai","vekuli"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1347","lb":"SO1347","mm":1,"ev":"The foe, his magic cut, resolves to use rings, javelins and discs.","cl":[],"ag":["சுவணகேது"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:59"},"so:1348":{"book":"soolamani","num":1347,"t":["இப்பா லிவர்கள் பொரும்பொழுதி னியானை யிரண்டு மெதிர்தாக்கிக்","கைப்பா லெடுத்துக் கறைமருப்பு மிடைந்து கண்க ளெரிசிந்தி","மெய்ப்பா லெடுத்துக் குத்தியுமெய் விலங்கிப் பாய்ந்து மொன்றொன்றைப்","பொய்ப்பா லின்றி யெடுத்திட்டுப் புடைத்தும் பெயர்த்தும் பொருதனவே."],"p":"","r":"While these two fight, the two elephants charge each other, lift with their trunks, lock tusks, rain sparks from their eyes, gore and pull aside, and in earnest lift and strike one another.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["padai","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1348","lb":"SO1348","mm":1,"ev":"Two elephants fight, goring and flinging each other.","cl":[],"ag":["யானைகள்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:59"},"so:1349":{"book":"soolamani","num":1348,"t":["துண்ட வேகப் புள்ளுயர்த்தான் துளைக்கை யானைச் சுடர்முடியான்","புண்டரீகக் கொலையானைக் குடைந்து போகும் பொழுதகத்துக்","கொண்ட வாளன் கேடகத்தன் குதிகொள் வான்போ லெழுந்தெதிரே","மண்டு வானை வயவேந்தன் கண்டு வாளி சிந்தினான்."],"p":"","r":"The one with the swift Garuda banner, an elephant and a gleaming crown, rides in through a white-lotus pack of elephants, and as he passes, a man with sword and shield leaps at him; the brave king sees him and shoots arrows.","n":"The one with the Garuda banner and the sword-bearer are not named; the elements follow the verse's own epithets.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maram","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1349","lb":"SO1349","mm":1,"ev":"A sword-and-shield man leaps at the Garuda-banner king, who shoots him.","cl":[],"ag":["தேவசேனன்","சுவணகேது"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:59"},"so:1350":{"book":"soolamani","num":1349,"t":["மடுத்த வாளுங் கேடகமுங் கவசக் கண்ணு மார்பகமு","மடுத்துக் குத்தி னாற்போலக் கழிந்த தம்பு கழிதலுமே","எடுத்து மறிக்கப் பட்டான்போ லிலங்கு பூணு மாரமுந்தேந்","தொடுத்த தாம மாலையுமுன் சொரிய வீழ்ந்தான் சுடர்வேலான்."],"p":"","r":"The arrow passes through the sword, shield, armour, eye and chest as if driven in; the shining-speared one falls, his jewels, chain and wreath of flowers spilling.","n":"","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maram","kolai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1350","lb":"SO1350","mm":1,"ev":"The arrow pierces the warrior, who falls with his ornaments scattering.","cl":[],"ag":["சுவணகேது"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:59"},"so:1351":{"book":"soolamani","num":1350,"t":["தூவி யார்சுவ ணக்கொடி","மேவி னான்பட வேமிகை","மாவி னார்கடற் றானைபோர்","ஓவி யாங்குடை வுற்றதே."],"p":"","r":"As the golden-banner king falls, the great sea of the army, left without him, breaks off the fight and is crushed.","n":"The verse is in a short four-line form with half-lines, unlike the long lines before it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","padai","kedu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1351","lb":"SO1351","mm":1,"ev":"With Suvarnaketu fallen, his army breaks and is routed.","cl":[],"ag":["சுவணகேது"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:59"},"so:1352":{"book":"soolamani","num":1351,"t":["வென்று வேற்றவர் நின்றனர்","என்ற மாற்ற மிசைத்தலும்","நன்று நன்றென நக்குமேற்","சென்ற னன்சிரீ சேனனே."],"p":"","r":"On hearing the word that the victors stand and the others are down, Srisena, saying well, well and laughing, goes forward.","n":"","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maram","anjaamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1352","lb":"SO1352","mm":1,"ev":"Srisena laughs at the report and advances.","cl":[],"ag":["சிரீசேனன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:59"},"so:1353":{"book":"soolamani","num":1352,"t":["கடைந்த கார்க்கடல் போற்கலந்","துடைந்த வாட்படை வெள்ளநீர்க்","கடைந்த வான்சிறை யாயினான்","மிடைந்த வேற்படை வீரனே."],"p":"","r":"Like a churned dark sea mixing, the sword army broke, and the spear-bearing champion became a prisoner in the flood.","n":"The verse is obscure; the water image and the captivity may belong to one figure or to two.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["padai","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1353","lb":"SO1353","mm":1,"ev":"The sword army is broken and a spearman is taken prisoner.","cl":[],"ag":["வேற்படை வீரன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:59"},"so:1354":{"book":"soolamani","num":1353,"t":["விஞ்சை வேந்தர்க ளேமிகை","அஞ்சு வாரென வாயிடை","நஞ்ச னார்களை நக்கிவை","கொஞ்சி லான்சில கூறினான்."],"p":"","r":"Saying that the Vidyadhara kings are the ones who fear, he laughs at the poisonous ones and speaks some small words.","n":"","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maram","nindhai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1354","lb":"SO1354","mm":1,"ev":"A speaker mocks the Vidyadhara kings.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["சிரீசேனன்",""],"ch":"so:59"},"so:1355":{"book":"soolamani","num":1354,"t":["வாளர் வார்கழல் வீக்கிய","தாளர் தாமுடைந் தோடினால்","நாளை நாணுடை நங்கைமார்","தோளை நாணிலர் தோயவே."],"p":"","r":"If sword-bearing warriors, with anklets tied on their feet, break and run, tomorrow the shamefaced women will not be ashamed to embrace their shoulders.","n":"The speaker is taken to be Srisena from 1352 and 1354; the verse is a warrior's rebuke, which this chapter does not set against any school.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["naanam","maanam","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1355","lb":"SO1355","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["a warrior who flees brings shame so that even women shrink from him","general","asserted",3,"srisenan"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["சிரீசேனன்","விஞ்சை வேந்தர்"],"ch":"so:59"},"so:1356":{"book":"soolamani","num":1355,"t":["பொன்று மிவ்வுட லின்பொருட்","டென்று நிற்கு மிரும்புகழ்","இன்று நீரிகந் தீர்களாற்","குன்றின் மேற்குடை வேந்திர்காள்."],"p":"","r":"The speaker tells the kings who fight beneath the umbrella on the hill that this body perishes for the sake of fame that stands for ever; do not turn away today.","n":"Spoken as a rebuke to the fleeing kings and not as a renunciant's teaching on the body; the body is spent for fame, not freed from.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pugazh","nilaiyaamai","yaakkai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1356","lb":"SO1356","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["the body perishes but fame stands for ever","general","asserted",1,"srisenan"],["a warrior should give up the body for lasting fame rather than flee","general","asserted",3,"srisenan"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["சிரீசேனன்","விஞ்சை வேந்தர்"],"ch":"so:59"},"so:1357":{"book":"soolamani","num":1356,"t":["மான மாமணி வீழ்த்துயிர்க்","கூன மாமென வோடுவீர்","ஈன வார்மயிர்க் கேதமாங்","கான மாவது கானுமே."],"p":"","r":"A voice taunts the men who run, saying that flight to save a body costs the jewel of honour, and that a forest awaits those who fear.","n":"The second half (lines 3-4) is obscure and the speaker is not named; the reading holds only line 1-2 firmly, and 1358 suggests the words are a rally to men who had begun to flee.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maanam","anjaamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1357","lb":"SO1357","mm":1,"ev":"A speaker shames the fleeing Vidyadharas for abandoning honour to save their lives.","cl":[["a warrior who flees to save his life throws away his honour","general","asserted",1,"vidyadhara_chief"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["சுடர்வேலினான்",""],"ch":"so:60"},"so:1358":{"book":"soolamani","num":1357,"t":["ஈண்ட வின்னன சொல்லலு","மீண்டு விஞ்சைய ரேற்றனர்","ஆண்ட கைச்சுட ராயிடைத்","தூண்டி னான்சுடர் வேலினான்."],"p":"","r":"At these words the Vidyadharas turn back and take up the fight, and the bright-spear leader urges them on.","n":"","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["padai","maanam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1358","lb":"SO1358","mm":1,"ev":"The Vidyadharas rally and return to the battle at their leader's urging.","cl":[],"ag":["விஞ்சையர்","சுடர்வேலினான்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:60"},"so:1359":{"book":"soolamani","num":1358,"t":["கொண்ட வாளினர் விஞ்சையர்","மண்டி னாரெதிர் மண்டலுங்","கண்டகள் கண்டங்க ளாயினார்","விண்ட வாட்படை வீரரே."],"p":"","r":"The sword-bearing Vidyadharas crowd in against the enemy and the warriors are cut to pieces at the neck and the body.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["padai","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1359","lb":"SO1359","mm":1,"ev":"Vidyadharas and the enemy's swordsmen close and cut each other down.","cl":[],"ag":["விஞ்சையர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:60"},"so:1360":{"book":"soolamani","num":1359,"t":["கருவிப் புட்டிலின் கண்டமும்","இருமிப் பக்கரைப் போழ்களும்","விரவிப் போர்க்கள வாயெலாம்","புரவித் துண்டங்கள் போர்த்தவே."],"p":"","r":"Pieces of horses, saddle-gear and cut flanks lie mixed over the whole battlefield.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["padai","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1360","lb":"SO1360","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:60"},"so:1361":{"book":"soolamani","num":1360,"t":["உருப்பு நீரவொள் வாள்களா","னெருப்பு நீரவர் வீசலும்","பொருப்பு வீழ்வன போன்றன","மருப்பு நீண்மத யானையே."],"p":"","r":"Sparks fly from the clashing swords like fire, and tusked elephants fall like mountains.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["padai","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1361","lb":"SO1361","mm":1,"ev":"Elephants fall as swords clash.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:60"},"so:1362":{"book":"soolamani","num":1361,"t":["சீர்மு கந்தசெஞ் சேற்றினுள்","ளார்மு கம்புக வாழ்ந்தரோ","நீர்மு கந்நில முற்றபொற்","றேர்மு கஞ்சிதை வுற்றதே."],"p":"","r":"Men sink into mud made of gore, the tracks of the field fill with blood, and the fronts of golden chariots are broken.","n":"Word division is broken in lines 1-3; the picture of the chariot-front shattered in blood is firm, the rest is held loosely.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["padai","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1362","lb":"SO1362","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:60"},"so:1363":{"book":"soolamani","num":1362,"t":["மாலும் வாட்படைப் போரினுள்","ஆலு மாமிசை யானவன்","கால னாமென வந்தனன்","சீல மானசிரீ சேனனே."],"p":"","r":"Sri Sena, a man of good conduct, comes into the sword fight like Death himself.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","kootru"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1363","lb":"SO1363","mm":1,"ev":"Sri Sena enters the battle.","cl":[],"ag":["சிரீசேனன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:60"},"so:1364":{"book":"soolamani","num":1363,"t":["மண்ணின் மேலுறை வாரொடு","மண்ணின் மேற்செரு வல்லனே","விண்ணின் மேலுறை வாரொடு","விண்ணின் மேற்செரு வல்லனே."],"p":"","r":"A boast that the speaker can fight those who live on earth and those who live in the sky alike.","n":"No line names the speaker; I take it as Sri Sena because 1363 has just introduced him, and the sky-dwellers are the flying Vidyadharas.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maram","anjaamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1364","lb":"SO1364","mm":1,"ev":"A fighter boasts of fighting both earthly and sky-dwelling foes.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["சிரீசேனன்",""],"ch":"so:60"},"so:1365":{"book":"soolamani","num":1364,"t":["வாய்ந்த போரிவை வல்லிரேல்","ஏந்து மின்படை போந்தனெக்","காய்ந்த கட்டுரை கேட்டலுஞ்","சேர்ந்த னன்சிரீ பாலனே."],"p":"","r":"Hearing the angry challenge to take up weapons if they can fight this kind of war, Sripala comes in.","n":"","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maram","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1365","lb":"SO1365","mm":1,"ev":"Sripala, hearing the challenge, joins the fight.","cl":[],"ag":["சிறீபாலன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:60"},"so:1366":{"book":"soolamani","num":1365,"t":["உவரி யோங்கு திரையெனக்","கவரி வேய்ந்த கலினமா","இவர வேறி னானரோ","எவரு மஞ்சு மீட்டினான்."],"p":"","r":"The chief mounts a horse decked with whisks that rises like a wave of the sea, and frightens everyone.","n":"","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maram","anjaamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1366","lb":"SO1366","mm":1,"ev":"The hero mounts his horse and terrifies the enemy.","cl":[],"ag":["சிறீபாலன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:60"},"so:1367":{"book":"soolamani","num":1366,"t":["திரிவில் சாரி கைச்செயல்","புரவி சேர்ந்து பொங்கின","வரிவில் வாளி மன்னரும்","மருவு போர்ம யங்கினார்."],"p":"","r":"Horses wheel and rise in their circling and the bow-and-arrow kings are bewildered in the fight.","n":"","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["padai","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1367","lb":"SO1367","mm":1,"ev":"The horses wheel and the opposing kings lose their order.","cl":[],"ag":["விஞ்சையர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:60"},"so:1368":{"book":"soolamani","num":1367,"t":["வில்லும் வாளும் குந்தமும்","சொல்லி னாற்றொ ழிற்கொளீஇ","எல்லை யின்று பொருதலொந்","தொல்ல மர்தொ டங்கினார்."],"p":"","r":"Bow, sword and spear are laid aside as if by a word, and the combatants begin a fight without limit.","n":"","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["padai","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1368","lb":"SO1368","mm":1,"ev":"The fighters give up weapons and begin close combat.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:60"},"so:1369":{"book":"soolamani","num":1368,"t":["வெல்லு நீர விஞ்சையன்","வில்லும் வீசு குந்தமும்","வல்லி தின்ம டித்தனன்","மல்ல மலர்ந்த மார்பினான்."],"p":"","r":"The victorious Vidyadhara, broad-chested, breaks the other's bow and hurled spear with ease.","n":"","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maram","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1369","lb":"SO1369","mm":1,"ev":"The Vidyadhara breaks his opponent's bow and spear.","cl":[],"ag":["விஞ்சையன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:60"},"so:1370":{"book":"soolamani","num":1369,"t":["அற்ற குந்த மாண்டவச்","செற்ற லன்றெ ழித்துமேற்","கொற்ற வன்றன் கோகின்மேல்","வெற்றி வாளின் வீசினான்."],"p":"","r":"With his spear broken the enemy cuts it down and strikes with a victorious sword at the king.","n":"The word division is very broken and who strikes whom cannot be fixed.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maram","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1370","lb":"SO1370","mm":1,"ev":"A sword stroke is aimed at the king.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:60"},"so:1371":{"book":"soolamani","num":1370,"t":["ஒளித யங்கு தோளின்மேல்","தெளித மாவெ றிந்தவாள்","உளித மாக நீட்டினான்","பளித நாறு மேனியான்."],"p":"","r":"The sword strikes a shining shoulder and the wounded man stretches himself out.","n":"","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1371","lb":"SO1371","mm":1,"ev":"A sword wounds a shoulder.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:60"},"so:1372":{"book":"soolamani","num":1371,"t":["தளித யங்கு தண்மதுக்","களித யங்கு கண்ணியாற்","குளித வாளி னுற்றபுண்","ணெளித மாயி லங்குமே."],"p":"","r":"A wound made by the sword is said to be washed bright and clear in a garlanded man's honey-smelling flowers.","n":"The verse is built on rhyming forms in -ளித and is very hard to parse; the reading is a guess at its main sense.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1372","lb":"SO1372","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:60"},"so:1373":{"book":"soolamani","num":1372,"t":["இட்ட வாள றுத்தபின்","விட்ட மாவின் மேற்செலாத்","துட்ட மாத்து ரந்தனன்","மட்டு வார்த்த மாலையான்."],"p":"","r":"After the laid-on sword has cut, the garlanded man urges his unruly horse not to go on.","n":"","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1373","lb":"SO1373","mm":1,"ev":"A rider drives his horse away from the fight.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:60"},"so:1374":{"book":"soolamani","num":1373,"t":["மாதி போகு மானமா","மீது போக விட்டவன்","சோதி கூடு சுடர்முடிக்","கேத மாக வெண்ணினான்."],"p":"","r":"He who let his horse run over the swift mount thinks of harming the other's shining crown.","n":"","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maram","pagai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1374","lb":"SO1374","mm":1,"ev":"A rider plans to strike at the enemy's crown.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:60"},"so:1375":{"book":"soolamani","num":1374,"t":["எண்ணி னன்னெ டுப்பலுங்","கண்ணி யஃது கருதிமா","மண்ணில் வாவி யாங்குமேல்","விண்ணின் மேலு மிக்கதே."],"p":"","r":"He thinks of cutting the crown, and the leap that follows is greater than any on earth or in the sky.","n":"","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1375","lb":"SO1375","mm":1,"ev":"The rider leaps up to strike the crown.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:60"},"so:1376":{"book":"soolamani","num":1375,"t":["கொண்ட தன்கு சைக்குறி","கண்டு மன்னு கதியினால்","விண்ட லத்தின் மீதுபோய்.","அண்ட யத்த டுத்ததே."],"p":"","r":"At the sign of his own sword he flies up on his course, through the sky, and reaches the world's edge.","n":"","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1376","lb":"SO1376","mm":1,"ev":"The rider flies up through the sky.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:60"},"so:1377":{"book":"soolamani","num":1376,"t":["வானின் வாவு விஞ்சையன்","மான மாம ணிம்முடி","ஏனை மன்ன னேதியான்","மீனின் வந்து வீழ்ந்ததே."],"p":"","r":"The great gem-crown of the flying Vidyadhara falls like a star at the other king's weapon.","n":"","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maram","maanam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1377","lb":"SO1377","mm":1,"ev":"The Vidyadhara king's crown is struck down by his rival.","cl":[],"ag":["விஞ்சையன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:60"},"so:1378":{"book":"soolamani","num":1377,"t":["திருநிலை யகமுடைச் செல்வன் செங்கதிர்","விரிநிலை மணிமுடி மிளிர்ந்து வீழ்தலும்","பொருநிலை யழிந்துபிற் புறக்கொ டுத்தது","பருநிலை மலையவர் பரவைத் தானையே."],"p":"","r":"As the lord's bright crown falls, the mountain-folk's army gives up the fight and turns its back.","n":"The verse shows that an army stands or falls with its king's crown; it is the narrator's picture, not a stated rule.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["padai","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1378","lb":"SO1378","mm":1,"ev":"The Vidyadhara army breaks and flees when the crown falls.","cl":[],"ag":["விஞ்சையர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:60"},"so:1379":{"book":"soolamani","num":1378,"t":["வேயுடை வெள்ளிசேர் விலங்கல் வேந்தர்கள்","ஆயிடை யுடைதலு மாழி யான்மகன்","காய்வுடை மனத்தவன் கனக சித்திரன்","சேயிடை புகுந்தன னின்று செப்பினான்."],"p":"","r":"As the mountain kings break, Kanaka Chitran, the discus-holder's son, angry at heart, steps in and speaks.","n":"","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vendhan","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1379","lb":"SO1379","mm":1,"ev":"Kanaka Chitran enters the fight and speaks.","cl":[],"ag":["கனகசித்திரன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:60"},"so:1380":{"book":"soolamani","num":1379,"t":["உருவிய வாளின னுடுத்த கச்சினன்","வெருவர விழித்தனன் வீர வேகமோ","டொருவனை யொருவனங் கஞ்சி யோடுமேல்","அருவருப் புடையதவ் வாண்மை யாகுமே."],"p":"","r":"A speaker says that a man with drawn sword and girded belt who runs in fear of one man shows a manliness that is to be despised.","n":"1379 says Kanaka Chitran spoke; I take this as his speech, but the verse itself names nobody.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maanam","anjaamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1380","lb":"SO1380","mm":1,"ev":"Kanaka Chitran taunts the fleeing kings for their cowardice.","cl":[["a man with a sword in hand who flees from one man has a loathsome manliness","general","asserted",4,"kanaka_chitran"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["கனகசித்திரன்",""],"ch":"so:60"},"so:1381":{"book":"soolamani","num":1380,"t":["மதிதொடு நெடுவரை மான விஞ்சையர்","விதிபடு மனிசரை வெருவி மீண்டனர்","அதிசய மிதுவென வலர நக்கனன்","கதிர்விடு வளையெயி றுடைய காளையே."],"p":"","r":"The Vidyadharas of the high mountain turn back in fear of men whom fate has marked, and the fanged youth laughs in wonder.","n":"விதிபடு is read as 'fate-marked'; the identity of the laughing youth is not named and may be Kanaka Chitran or Sripala.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["oozh","anjaamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1381","lb":"SO1381","mm":1,"ev":"The Vidyadhara kings retreat; a fanged young man laughs at the marvel.","cl":[["men whom fate favours cannot be faced even by the strong Vidyadharas","general","reported",2,"narrator"]],"ag":["விஞ்சையர்","கனகசித்திரன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:60"},"so:1382":{"book":"soolamani","num":1381,"t":["உடைந்தவர் மனங்களை யுருவ வீழ்த்திடு","மடந்தையர் வடிக்கணம் பல்ல வாய்விடின்","மிடைந்தவர் தொடங்கிய வீரக் கோட்டியுள்","அடைந்தவ ரடுபடைக் கஞ்சல் வேண்டுமோ."],"p":"","r":"A taunt that once glances of women have pierced the broken-hearted, men who have joined the heroes' company have no cause to dread the killing weapons.","n":"Lines 1-2 are cryptic (the women's quick glances as a piercing of hearts); the verse reads as the start of the speech announced in 1379, but the speaker is not named here.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maanam","anjaamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1382","lb":"SO1382","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["கனகசித்திரன்",""],"ch":"so:61"},"so:1383":{"book":"soolamani","num":1382,"t":["தானுடம் பிறந்ததற் பின்னுந் தன்றிறல்","வானுடன் புகழ்தர நிற்கு மண்மிசை","மானுட ருயிர்கொள மானமில் லிர்காள்","ஊனுடம் பிதன்பொருட் டுடையல் வேண்டுமோ."],"p":"","r":"The speaker calls shameless men to witness that a warrior's strength stands praised with the sky even when the body is born, and asks whether the flesh body must be broken for the sake of taking human lives.","n":"The last two lines can be read either as a challenge to the cowards or as doubt about the body's use; the sense turns on a rare construction.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maanam","yaakkai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1383","lb":"SO1383","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["a warrior's prowess, not his flesh body, is what stands in praise","general","asserted",1,"kanaka_chitran"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["கனகசித்திரன்",""],"ch":"so:61"},"so:1384":{"book":"soolamani","num":1383,"t":["நெய்யினா னிழன்றுநீர் நின்ற நீளொளி","வெய்யவா ளமரிடை வெருவி யிட்டதங்","கையினாற் கருனையின் கவளங் கொள்ளிய","ஐயன்மார் போந்ததென் றசதி யாடினான்."],"p":"","r":"The speaker mocks the enemy lords, saying they have come as if to take balls of food in the same hands that dropped their bright oiled swords in fear in the fierce fight.","n":"The speaker is read from 1379; no word here names him.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["anjaamai","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1384","lb":"SO1384","mm":1,"ev":"Kanaka Chitran mocks the enemy lords for dropping their swords in fear.","cl":[],"ag":["கனகசித்திரன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["கனகசித்திரன்",""],"ch":"so:61"},"so:1385":{"book":"soolamani","num":1384,"t":["இன்னண மொழிந்தெதிர் தெழித்து மாற்றலர்","மன்னிய கடற்படை மண்டி வாளினாற்","கன்னவி றோளினான் கண்டங் கண்டமாத்","துன்னிய துணிபல தொடரத் தோன்றினான்."],"p":"","r":"Having spoken, the hero stands against the enemy's sea-like host and appears in the press with the sword, strong-armed, while the foe's broken pieces follow him.","n":"","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maram","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1385","lb":"SO1385","mm":1,"ev":"The hero faces the enemy host and cuts into it with his sword.","cl":[],"ag":["கனகசித்திரன்","மாற்றலர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:61"},"so:1386":{"book":"soolamani","num":1385,"t":["விலங்குவேல் கொண்டையை யுந்தி வேற்றவர்","மலங்கமேற் செல்வது மான மாமெனப்","பொலங்கலங் கழலொடு புலம்பப் பூமிமேல்","அலங்கலா னடந்தம ரழுவந் தாங்கினான்."],"p":"","r":"Thinking that to press on against foes dismayed, pushing aside the slanting spears, is honour, he walks the field with anklets sounding and bears the whole weight of the fight.","n":"","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maanam","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1386","lb":"SO1386","mm":1,"ev":"The hero advances through the spears and sustains the battle.","cl":[["going forward against the enemy's spears is what honour is","general","asserted",2,"narrator"]],"ag":["கனகசித்திரன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:61"},"so:1387":{"book":"soolamani","num":1386,"t":["பத்திரக் கடிப்பினன் பைம்பொற் றாரினன்","சித்திர மணித்தொடர் திளைக்குந் தானையன்","கத்திகைக் கண்ணியன் காணும் பாலெலாந்","தொத்திணர்க் குஞ்சியான் காளை தோன்றினான்."],"p":"","r":"The youth appears in battle dress, with ear ornaments, a garland of gold, a gem-linked girdle, a wreath of the war-band and a cluster of locks, and every side sees him.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1387","lb":"SO1387","mm":1,"ev":"The hero shows himself in the battle in full ornament.","cl":[],"ag":["கனகசித்திரன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:61"},"so:1388":{"book":"soolamani","num":1387,"t":["வம்பமா விருதுணி பட்ட மாவொடு","செம்பொனா லியன்றதேர் சிந்தி வீழ்ந்தன","கம்பமா வொழிந்தன களிறு காளைவாள்","வெம்புலால் விரையினும் வெறுத்த தில்லையே."],"p":"","r":"Horses cut in two and fallen gold chariots litter the field and elephants are left without their posts, yet the youth's sword is not wearied of the hot smell of flesh.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1388","lb":"SO1388","mm":1,"ev":"Horses, chariots and elephants fall before the hero's sword.","cl":[],"ag":["கனகசித்திரன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:61"},"so:1389":{"book":"soolamani","num":1388,"t":["வார்குலாங் கருங்குழன் மன்ன ரேற்றவர்","நீர்குலாங் குருதியுட் குளிப்ப நேரினி","ஆர்கொலேற் பவரென வஞ்ச வெஞ்சினக்","கார்குலா முருமெனக் காளை தோன்றினான்."],"p":"","r":"Seeing the long-haired kings who stood against him bathe in streaming blood, the youth appears like a cloud-borne thunder and asks who now will face him.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","vekuli"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1389","lb":"SO1389","mm":1,"ev":"The hero, after the kings are slain, appears like thunder and challenges the rest.","cl":[],"ag":["கனகசித்திரன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:61"},"so:1390":{"book":"soolamani","num":1389,"t":["காளைநங் கனைகழற் கனக சித்திரன்","வாளம ரழுவத்து மண்டி னானெனக்","கேளவர் மொழிதலுங் கிரீவன் றம்பிமார்","ஆளிவர் கடற்படை யனன்றெ ழுந்ததே."],"p":"","r":"On his kin saying that their bull-like Kanaka Chitran has pressed into the sword-fight, the brothers of Grivan rise in anger with the sea-like host.","n":"Which side Grivan's brothers belong to is not said; they are taken as Kanaka Chitran's side because they rise at the news of him.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["padai","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1390","lb":"SO1390","mm":1,"ev":"News that Kanaka Chitran is in the fight brings his kin's host out in anger.","cl":[],"ag":["கேளவர்","கிரீவன் தம்பிமார்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:61"},"so:1391":{"book":"soolamani","num":1390,"t":["வெருவிமுன் னுடைந்துபோய்ப் பெயர்ந்து வேற்றவர்","ஒருவில்வா ளழுவம்வந் துந்து மற்படை","உரவுநீ ரிருங்கட லோதம் போந்தபின்","அரவநீர் வேலைமீ தலைப்ப தொத்ததே."],"p":"","r":"The foe that broke and withdrew earlier comes back to the field, and when the army's flood joins them it is like waves tossing on a roaring sea.","n":"The simile (the army like the ocean's waves) is clear; who 'fled' and who 'returns' in line 1 is not.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["padai","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1391","lb":"SO1391","mm":1,"ev":"The enemy that had broken returns to the fight and the armies surge together.","cl":[],"ag":["மாற்றலர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:61"},"so:1392":{"book":"soolamani","num":1391,"t":["மன்னவற் கிளையவர் வயிர மால்வரை","அன்னவ ரயிற்படை யரச வீரர்க","டுன்னலர்க் கரும்படர் தோன்றத் தோன்றுபு","முன்னினர் கனன்றுபோர் முறுக முட்டினார்."],"p":"","r":"The king's younger brothers, strong as diamond mountains, and the kings with their spear forces join, set on giving the foe grief, and burn into a hard battle.","n":"","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["padai","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1392","lb":"SO1392","mm":1,"ev":"The king's younger brothers and the kings join the battle in anger.","cl":[],"ag":["கிரீவன் தம்பிமார்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:61"},"so:1393":{"book":"soolamani","num":1392,"t":["கண்ணிடை சிவந்துகை சுட்டிக் காய்ந்துதம்","பண்ணுடை மழகளி றுந்தி னார்படை","எண்ணிடை யிடுமிட மின்றி யெங்கணு","மண்ணிடை யவரொடு மயங்கி நின்றதே."],"p":"","r":"With eyes red the warriors point and rage and drive their elephants on, so that no space is left in the press and the armies stand mingled everywhere.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["padai","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1393","lb":"SO1393","mm":1,"ev":"The two armies close and mingle with no space left between them.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:61"},"so:1394":{"book":"soolamani","num":1393,"t":["இலைதடு மாறின பகழி யெங்கணுஞ்","சிலைதடு மாறின சிலைத்த தேர்க்குழாம்","மலைதடு மாறின போல மான்றரோ","தலைதடு மாறின தடக்கை வேழமே."],"p":"","r":"Arrows go astray as feathers, bows as shafts, and the roaring chariot bands go astray like mountains while the elephants' great heads rock.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["padai","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1394","lb":"SO1394","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:61"},"so:1395":{"book":"soolamani","num":1394,"t":["முரிந்தன ,மணிநெடுந் தோள்கண் முத்துக","நெரிந்தன களிறுடை மருப்பு நேர்முகஞ்","சரிந்தன தலைபல தறுக ணில்லவர்","இரிந்தன ரிழிந்தது குருதி நீத்தமே."],"p":"","r":"Jewelled shoulders snap, tusks are crushed, faces and many heads fall, the fearless run, and a flood of blood runs down.","n":"The carnage is set out as a spectacle; no one grieves it here, unlike Purananuru 62, where both armies die and nothing is left to win.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","saakkaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1395","lb":"SO1395","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:61"},"so:1396":{"book":"soolamani","num":1395,"t":["மரைமயி ரணிந்தன மான மாப்பல","திரையென வுருண்டன திலக வெண்குடை","நுரையென நிவந்தன நுந்து மப்புனற்","கரையெனக் கிடந்தன களிற்றின் கூவையே."],"p":"","r":"Yak-hair whisks float, white umbrellas roll like waves and rise like foam, and the elephants' trunks lie like banks along the flood.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1396","lb":"SO1396","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:61"},"so:1397":{"book":"soolamani","num":1396,"t":["பெருகிய குருதியுட் பிறக்கு செந்தடி","அருகுடை யளற்றினு ளழுந்திப் பாகமே","சொரிகதிர்க் கோடக முடிக டோன்றலாற்","பொருகளம் புற்றெடுக் கின்ற தொக்குமே."],"p":"","r":"Raw flesh rising in the blood and the mire sinks and the riders' rays of crowns show above it, so that the battlefield looks like an anthill.","n":"Lines 2-3 are crowded with rare words and the exact picture is not certain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maram","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1397","lb":"SO1397","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:61"},"so:1398":{"book":"soolamani","num":1397,"t":["மாடடைந் தெதிர்ந்துதம் வயிரத் தண்டினாற்","பீடடைந் தவர்பிடர் புடைப்ப வானையின்","கோடுடைந் துதிர்ந்தன கொடுமுட் கேதகைத்","தோடுடைந் தொருவழித் தொகுத்த லொத்தவே."],"p":"","r":"When warriors come and strike the backs of the riders with diamond clubs, the elephants' tusks break and fall like heaps of thorny screwpine petals.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1398","lb":"SO1398","mm":1,"ev":"Warriors strike down the riders with diamond clubs and the elephants' tusks fall.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:61"},"so:1399":{"book":"soolamani","num":1398,"t":["குழைசுடர்ந் திலங்குதா ரரசர் கோலமாண்","இழைசுடர் தோள்களா லெறிய யானையின்","தழைசெவி மதமுகத் தொளிக்குஞ் சக்கர","மழைசொரி முகில்புகு மதிய மொத்தவே."],"p":"","r":"Kings' jewelled arms hurl at the elephants, and the weapons vanish into their wide-eared faces like the moon going into a rain-cloud.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1399","lb":"SO1399","mm":1,"ev":"Kings strike the elephants with thrown weapons.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:61"},"so:1400":{"book":"soolamani","num":1399,"t":["செருவரை யனையதோண் மன்னர் சேனையுள்","அருவரை புகுமயி லனைய வாயின","கருவரை யனையன களிநல் யானையின்","பெருவரை முகம்புகும் பிண்டி பாலமே."],"p":"","r":"Among the kings' armies the shoulder-mountains, like peacocks entering a mountain pass, enter the elephants' great faces, the bundle of the dart.","n":"The simile in lines 2-4 is hard and the last word is uncertain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maram","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1400","lb":"SO1400","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:61"},"so:1401":{"book":"soolamani","num":1400,"t":["மண்ணியன் மன்னர்கை முறுக்கி விட்டிடக்","கண்ணியன் யானைமேற் கணையம் பாய்வன","திண்ணிய நெடுவரைச் சென்னி மீமிசை","விண்ணியல் விளங்குமீன் வீழ்வ போன்றவே."],"p":"","r":"The kings twist their arms and the arrows flying at the elephants are like stars of the sky falling on the heads of tall mountains.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1401","lb":"SO1401","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:61"},"so:1402":{"book":"soolamani","num":1401,"t":["கடுத்துவீழ் கடாக்களிற் றுழவர் தந்தலை","அடுத்தகீ சகந்தமோ டற்று வீழ்வன","தொடுத்ததேன் றொடர்ந்த வீப் பிறங்க லோடுடன்","உடுத்தமால் வரைமருங் குருவ மொத்தவே."],"p":"","r":"The heads of the elephant-drivers fall cut off with their hair and cords, like bees' honey comb and flowers tumbling on mountain flanks.","n":"The reading of the second half rests on rare words.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maram","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1402","lb":"SO1402","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:61"},"so:1403":{"book":"soolamani","num":1402,"t":["அணியமு மாரமுங் கொடிஞ்சுங் கோலுமாத்","துணிவினைக் கவனமாத் துரக்கும் பாகரா","மணியவிர் தேரெனு மதலை நாயகர்","பணிவருங் குருதிநீர்ப் பவ்வத் தோட்டினார்."],"p":"","r":"The chariot-borne princes, with the charioteers driving by reins and goads and the shafts as bar, run the bright chariots through the sea of blood.","n":"","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maram","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1403","lb":"SO1403","mm":1,"ev":"Charioteers drive the chariots through the sea of blood.","cl":[],"ag":["பாகர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:61"},"so:1404":{"book":"soolamani","num":1403,"t":["நுதலிய செருநிலக் குருதி நீரினுண்","முதலையின் முதுகென நிவந்த தோற்பரங்","கதலிகை காம்பொடு கடுகித் தாமரை","மதலையந் தாளணை வாளை போன்றவே."],"p":"","r":"Shields heaped up like crocodile backs in the blood water, with banners and poles, look like lotus stalks and the fish that gather round them.","n":"","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maram","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1404","lb":"SO1404","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:61"},"so:1405":{"book":"soolamani","num":1404,"t":["கைவரை யொழுகிய கணையம் பாய்ந்துதம்","மெய்வரை நிரைத்திட விழுந்த யானைகள்","நெய்வரை நீணிலத் தலத்து மேற்பல","மொய்வரை முனையடிப் புண்ட வொத்தவே."],"p":"","r":"Elephants fallen with arrows through their trunks lie in rows over the ground, like wounded mountains struck at the foot.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","saakkaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1405","lb":"SO1405","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:61"},"so:1406":{"book":"soolamani","num":1405,"t":["ஊடக மெரிமணி நாகங் கவ்விய","நாடக விரிமதி நடுங்கி வீழ்வபோல்","ஆடக மணிநகைக் கடகக் கையொடு","கேடகந் திசைதிசை கிளர்ந்து வீழ்ந்தவே."],"p":"","r":"Gold-braceleted arms with shields fall scattered in every direction, like the shaken moon dropping from a serpent's grasp.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1406","lb":"SO1406","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:61"},"so:1407":{"book":"soolamani","num":1406,"t":["துளைப்படு புண்ணுமிழ் சோரி பாய்ந்தழெக்","களிப்படு சிலம்பின கவந்த மாடுவ","முளைப்புடை முடைத்திடை சுடர மூட்டிய","விளக்கிடு குற்றியின் விரிந்து தோன்றுமே."],"p":"","r":"Headless trunks dance with ringing anklets on the field, and the clotted flesh lit with fire looks like lamps set on stumps.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["padai","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1407","lb":"SO1407","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:62"},"so:1408":{"book":"soolamani","num":1407,"t":["அஞ்சல ரமர்க்கள மென்னு மார்வயல்","விஞ்சையர் குருதிநீர் வெள்ளந் தேர்த்தழெ","வெஞ்சின நாஞ்சிலா லுழுது வெள்ளியான்","தஞ்சமார் தன்புகழ் தயங்க வித்தினான்."],"p":"","r":"The white hero ploughs the field of battle with his plough, using the Vidyadharas' blood as water, and sows his fame in it.","n":"The plough-warrior is not named by a proper name here; the plough (நாஞ்சில்) and the epithet 'the white one' point to Vijayan, and 1418 names the same fighter அலாயுதன், the plough-bearer.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maram","pugazh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1408","lb":"SO1408","mm":1,"ev":"The white hero ploughs the battlefield with his plough and sows fame in the Vidyadhara blood.","cl":[],"ag":["விசயன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:62"},"so:1409":{"book":"soolamani","num":1408,"t":["வெளியவன் மிளிர்மரை புரையுஞ் செங்கணான்","அளியில னமர்க்களங் கடாக்கொள் கின்றவவ்","விளையவன் யாரென வினவிக் கேட்டனர்","கிளையமர் கிரீவனுக் கிளைய வீரரே."],"p":"","r":"The younger brothers of Kireevan, hearing of a merciless youth who has taken the field, ask who he is.","n":"Lines 1-2 give the youth's red eyes and the field he now holds in broken word division; only the question in the closing lines is firm.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["padai","vali"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1409","lb":"SO1409","mm":1,"ev":"Greevan's younger brothers ask who the youth is that has taken over the battle.","cl":[],"ag":["அச்சுவக்கிரீவனுக்கு இளைய காளையர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:62"},"so:1410":{"book":"soolamani","num":1409,"t":["சுரமைய ரதிபதி சிறுவன் மார்களுட்","பெரியவ னிவனெனப் பிறந்த செற்றமோ","டெரியவிர் வெகுளியா ரிளைய காளைய","ரொருவன்மே னால்வரும் யானை யோட்டினார்."],"p":"","r":"Resenting that this one is the eldest of the Suramai lord's sons, four young warriors drive their elephants at him alone.","n":"Who the four are and who the one is cannot be fixed from the verse; I read the four as the younger Vidyadhara champions of 1409 and the one as the young hero the Suramai lord's son.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vekuli","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1410","lb":"SO1410","mm":1,"ev":"Four young warriors, burning with anger, drive elephants against one man.","cl":[],"ag":["அச்சுவக்கிரீவனுக்கு இளைய காளையர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:62"},"so:1411":{"book":"soolamani","num":1410,"t":["இரத்தின கண்டனு மேனை வீரரும்","வரைத்தனர் வருபடை வீதி வாயெலாம்","எரித்தனர் நால்வரு மிளைய காளையை","முரித்திடு முனிவின ராகி முற்றினார்."],"p":"","r":"Ratna Kandan and the other warriors block the lanes of the oncoming force, burn them, and close on the young hero in fury.","n":"","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vekuli","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1411","lb":"SO1411","mm":1,"ev":"Ratna Kandan and three others cut off and attack the young hero.","cl":[],"ag":["இரத்தின கண்டன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:62"},"so:1412":{"book":"soolamani","num":1411,"t":["அடங்கின னரசிளங் குமர னோவென","உடங்கலந் தொல்லொலி யெழுந்த தாயிடை","மடங்கலில் கருங்கடன் மலங்கிற் றொத்தது","தடங்கமழ் சுரமைநாட் டரசன் றானையே."],"p":"","r":"A cry goes up that the young prince has fallen, and the Suramai king's army heaves like a dark sea over a whirlpool.","n":"The cry may be a rumour; 1413 and 1414 show the young prince's side recovering at once, so the fall is not shown as final.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["padai","kedu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1412","lb":"SO1412","mm":1,"ev":"The army of the Suramai king is thrown into confusion by the cry that the young prince has fallen.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:62"},"so:1413":{"book":"soolamani","num":1412,"t":["எரிபுரை யுளைகேளா டிலங்கு வெண்பிறை","விரிவன வெனவிளங் கெயிற்றொ டாயிடை","அரியர சடைந்ததொன் தவனை யேறினான்","புரிவகை நாஞ்சிலும் புதிய தெய்தினான்."],"p":"","r":"A lion-like hero, new in the fight, mounts a fierce lion and takes up a new plough.","n":"Line 1-2 are obscure and the rider is not named; the plough makes Vijayan the likely reader.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maram","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1413","lb":"SO1413","mm":1,"ev":"The hero mounts a lion and takes a fresh plough.","cl":[],"ag":["விசயன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:62"},"so:1414":{"book":"soolamani","num":1413,"t":["செய்யவாய் நாஞ்சிலுஞ் செங்கட் சீயமும்","ஐயனாங் கெய்தலு மதிர வார்த்தது","வையமா ளிளையவன் றானை மற்றவர்","மையன்மா யானைகண் மயங்கி யிட்டவே."],"p":"","r":"The plough, the red-eyed lion and the elder brother arrive and the young king's army roars, while the enemy's elephants break in confusion.","n":"'The young king' here is the younger of the two brothers, which is why 1412's cry is answered now.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["padai","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1414","lb":"SO1414","mm":1,"ev":"The elder brother arrives with plough and lion; the young king's army roars and the enemy's elephants scatter.","cl":[],"ag":["விசயன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:62"},"so:1415":{"book":"soolamani","num":1414,"t":["பொருபடை புகைந்தவர் வழங்கு மாயிடைச்","செருவுடை யவரகன் செல்வ மார்பகம்","உருவுடை நாஞ்சிலா லுழுதிட் டானரோ","மருவுடை யவரையும் மயக்கு மைந்தனே."],"p":"","r":"In the thick of the fight he ploughs open the broad rich chests of the proud warriors, the youth who can bewilder even the best of them.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1415","lb":"SO1415","mm":1,"ev":"The young hero ploughs open the enemy warriors' chests with his plough.","cl":[],"ag":["விசயன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:62"},"so:1416":{"book":"soolamani","num":1415,"t":["ஒருவனோர் நாஞ்சிலா லூழித் தீப்புரை","இருவரோ டிருவரை யானை நான்கொடு","செருவினு ளமர்வெலக் கேட்டுச் சேர்ந்தனன்","கருவரை யனையதோட் கனக நாமனே."],"p":"","r":"Kanaka Naman, hearing that one man with a plough has beaten two men and four elephants, comes to the fight, shoulders like a dark mountain.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1416","lb":"SO1416","mm":1,"ev":"Kanaka Naman, hearing of the plough-bearer's feats, joins the battle.","cl":[],"ag":["கனகசித்திரன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:62"},"so:1417":{"book":"soolamani","num":1416,"t":["காளையக் கனகசித் திரனுங் காய்ந்துதன்","வாளைவாய் துடைத்தெதிர் மடுப்ப மற்றவன்","தோளையுஞ் சுடர்கெழு நாஞ்சில் வாயினால்","ஆளியே றனையவ னழுந்த வூன்றினான்."],"p":"","r":"Kanaka Chitran, angry, wipes his sword's edge and strikes, and the lion-like plough-bearer drives the plough's tip into his shoulder.","n":"Chapter 60 casts Kanaka Chitran as a son of the discus-holder who steps in for the kings; here he fights the plough-bearer, so the two scenes are not shown to be on one side.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maram","vekuli"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1417","lb":"SO1417","mm":1,"ev":"Kanaka Chitran strikes with his sword and the plough-bearer pins his shoulder with the plough's blade.","cl":[],"ag":["கனகசித்திரன்","விசயன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:62"},"so:1418":{"book":"soolamani","num":1417,"t":["வனைகதி ரிலங்குதோள் வயிர கண்டனோ","டனைவரு மலாயுதற் கமர்தொ லைந்ததுங்","கனகசித் திரனது பாடுங் கேட்டரோ","அனல்படு மனத்தனங் கொருவ னாயினான்."],"p":"","r":"Hearing that Vayira Kandan and all the others were beaten by Alaayudhan and what became of Kanaka Chitran, one man is left with a heart on fire.","n":"The man who burns is not named; 1419 names Ratna Greevan as the next to act, so I read him.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vekuli","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1418","lb":"SO1418","mm":1,"ev":"A warrior, learning of the defeat of Vayira Kandan and the rest, is left burning with anger.","cl":[],"ag":["இரத்தினக் கிரீவன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:62"},"so:1419":{"book":"soolamani","num":1418,"t":["பொடித்தலை நிலத்தவர் போரு மாண்மையு","முடித்திடு கெனமுனிந் தெழுந்து மூரிவான்","மடுத்ததோர் வரைதனை வயிரக் கைகளால்","எடுத்தன னிரத்தினக் கிரீவ னென்பவே."],"p":"","r":"Ratna Greevan rises in rage, resolved to finish the fight and manhood of those of the earth, and lifts a great mountain in his diamond-hard hands.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","vekuli"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1419","lb":"SO1419","mm":1,"ev":"Ratna Greevan lifts a mountain with his hands.","cl":[],"ag":["இரத்தினக் கிரீவன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:62"},"so:1420":{"book":"soolamani","num":1419,"t":["வரிந்துவீழ் கச்சையன் வனைந்த தாடியன்","முரிந்தெழு புருவத்தன் முழங்கு தீயென","எரிந்தன னிறுவரை யெடுத்து மேற்செல","இரிந்தது சுரமைய ரிறைவன் றானையே."],"p":"","r":"Belted, bearded and with brows knit, he blazes like fire and lifts the mountain and advances, and the army of the Suramai king scatters.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","anjaamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1420","lb":"SO1420","mm":1,"ev":"The army of the Suramai king scatters as Ratna Greevan advances with the mountain.","cl":[],"ag":["இரத்தினக் கிரீவன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:62"},"so:1421":{"book":"soolamani","num":1420,"t":["ஆங்கவ னடைதலு மருக்க கீர்த்திகை","வாங்குவிற் புகுந்தது வாளி யொன்றவன்","ஓங்கிருந் தூணியிற் சுடர்ந்த தொல்லெனத்","தாங்கருந் திறலவன் சந்தித் தோட்டினான்."],"p":"","r":"As he comes close, an arrow gleams in the quiver of the king and into Arukka Kirti's drawn bow, and he sets it and drives it on.","n":"Line 1-2 turn on a rare construction 'அருக்க கீர்த்தி கை'; the archer is read as Arukka Kirti.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maram","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1421","lb":"SO1421","mm":1,"ev":"Arukka Kirti fits a bright arrow to his bow and shoots.","cl":[],"ag":["அருக்க கீர்த்தி"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:62"},"so:1422":{"book":"soolamani","num":1421,"t":["தொடுத்ததுந் துரந்ததும் விடலை தோளிடை","மடுத்ததுங் கிழித்தது மண்ணினுட்புகக்","கடுத்ததும் கண்டுநின் றவர்க டம்மையும்","படுத்தது பகலவன் பகழி யென்பவே."],"p":"","r":"The arrow he strung, shot, set to the shoulder, tore through and drove into the earth, and it laid low even the onlookers: they call it the sun's arrow.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1422","lb":"SO1422","mm":1,"ev":"The arrow pierces its target, enters the earth and strikes down bystanders.","cl":[],"ag":["அருக்க கீர்த்தி"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:62"},"so:1423":{"book":"soolamani","num":1422,"t":["வரையொடு வரையென மறிந்து மண்ணின்மேல்","விரையுடை யலங்கலான் வீழு மாயிடைத்","திரையொடு கனைகடல் கலங்கிச் சிந்தின","புரையுடை விலங்கலும் புலம்பு கொண்டவே."],"p":"","r":"The garlanded hero falls like one mountain on another and the sea churns while the hills grieve.","n":"The faller is not named; it follows the arrow of 1421-1422 and the mountain-lifter of 1419-1420, so I read Ratna Greevan.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maram","kedu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1423","lb":"SO1423","mm":1,"ev":"A great hero falls like a mountain and the sea and hills tremble.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:62"},"so:1424":{"book":"soolamani","num":1423,"t":["தம்பியர் பாடு மக்க ளிறந்ததுந் தனக்குப் பாங்காய்","வெம்பிய வீரர் போருள் விளிந்ததும் விரைவி னோடிச்","செம்பினை யுருக்கி வெய்தாய்ச் செவிமுதற் சொரிந்ததேபோ","லம்பொன்செ யாழியானுக் குரைத்தன ரரக்குண் கோலோர்."],"p":"","r":"The staff-bearing messengers run to the lord of the gold discus and tell him that his younger brothers' sons and heroes died, the words falling on his ear like molten copper.","n":"The words in line 4 'அரக்குண் கோலோர்' are rare; I read them as the staff-bearers of 055's report, not fixed.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["padai","kedu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1424","lb":"SO1424","mm":1,"ev":"Messengers report the deaths of his kinsmen and heroes to the discus-holding king.","cl":[],"ag":["அரக்குண் கோலோர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["அரக்குண் கோலோர்","அச்சுவக்கிரீவன்"],"ch":"so:62"},"so:1425":{"book":"soolamani","num":1424,"t":["ஆங்கவர் மொழிந்த போழ்தி னருவரை கரிய தொப்பான்","ஈங்கிவர் மாற்ற மாயி னிருந்தினி யென்னை யென்னா","வாங்குநீ ருலகில் வாழு முயிர்களை வாரிக் கொண்டு","வீங்குநீர்க் கடலுட் பெய்யும் விஞ்சையை விடுக்க நேர்ந்தான்."],"p":"","r":"Dark as a mountain, he asks what is left to him if these are the news and chooses to loose the spell that sweeps up all living things of the world and pours them into the sea.","n":"Grief and rage give the decision, and the verse states no claim on its justice; the Jain epic lets the reader see the sweep of the spell as the work of anger.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vekuli","kootru"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1425","lb":"SO1425","mm":1,"ev":"Asvagriva decides to send out a spell that gathers all life into the sea.","cl":[],"ag":["அச்சுவக்கிரீவன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:62"},"so:1426":{"book":"soolamani","num":1425,"t":["ஓர்ந்தவன் மனத்து விஞ்சை யொருபுடை யெய்த லோடுஞ்","சார்ந்தது சண்ட வேகை பணிபணி யென்று சார","வார்ந்தநீ ருலகின் வாழு மனிதரை வல்லை யாகில்","தேர்ந்துகொண் டொருவ ரின்றித் தெய்வமே செகுத்தி டென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"At the thought the spell stands close, Chandavegai, and he bids it take every human of the sea-bound world, if it can, choosing them out and sparing none.","n":"'Choosing out and leaving none' (line 3-4) is odd: it can bid the spell to seek each person out, or to take them all without distinction; either way no sparing is allowed.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kootru","vekuli"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1426","lb":"SO1426","mm":1,"ev":"Asvagriva commands the spell Chandavegai to destroy mankind.","cl":[],"ag":["சண்டவேகை"],"ec":[],"spk":["அச்சுவக்கிரீவன்","சண்டவேகை"],"ch":"so:62"},"so:1427":{"book":"soolamani","num":1426,"t":["கொடியவன் விடுத்த போழ்திற் கூற்றுவன் றன்னைக் கூவி","வடியெயி றிலங்க நக்கு வாழிய தோள்க ளின்று","விடுகதி ராழி வெய்யோன் வேண்டவென் வாயுட் பட்டு","முடியுமிவ் வுலக நீயு முறைத்தொழின் முடித்தி யென்ன."],"p":"","r":"When he sends the spell, it calls Death, laughs showing sharp teeth, tells him the world will end in its mouth at the sun-bright king's wish, and bids Death finish his own task too.","n":"","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kootru","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1427","lb":"SO1427","mm":1,"ev":"The spell Chandavegai summons Death and tells him to do his task as the world is ended.","cl":[],"ag":["சண்டவேகை"],"ec":[],"spk":["சண்டவேகை","கூற்றுவன்"],"ch":"so:62"},"so:1428":{"book":"soolamani","num":1427,"t":["அணங்குகள் குழுமி யாமும் பெருவயி றார்து மென்று","துணங்கைகோத் தாடி நக்குச் சுடரிலைச் சூல மேந்தி","வணங்குபு சூழ மற்ற மாபெருந் தெய்வம் வந்து","மணங்கமழ் சுரமை நாடன் றானைமேன் மடுத்த தம்மா."],"p":"","r":"The spirits gather and say they will fill their great bellies too, dance with bright tridents and bow round, and the great goddess comes and drives the spell on the Suramai king's army.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["padai","kootru"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1428","lb":"SO1428","mm":1,"ev":"The spirits and the great goddess come and the spell is loosed upon the Suramai king's army.","cl":[],"ag":["அணங்குகள்"],"ec":[],"spk":["அணங்குகள்",""],"ch":"so:62"},"so:1429":{"book":"soolamani","num":1428,"t":["பட்டடி நெடிய வீங்கு பரட்டின நொடிக்குங் கால","ஒட்டிய வயிற்ற வற்ற லுகிரிடை மயிர முன்கை","கட்டிய கண்ணி பீலித் தலையின கழற்காய் போல","வட்டமா யுருளுங் கண்ண கணங்கள்வந் திரைத்த வன்றே."],"p":"","r":"The ghoul-hosts come crying, long-footed, hollow-bellied, with hair-covered fingers and rolling round eyes like gall-nuts.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["padai","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1429","lb":"SO1429","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["அணங்குகள்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:62"},"so:1430":{"book":"soolamani","num":1429,"t":["வரைகளை யுருள வுந்தி வந்ததோர் சண்ட வாயு","நிரைகிளர் சுடர தாகி நிமிர்ந்ததோ ருருவச் செந்தீ","திரைகளை மறிய வீசிச் சிறந்ததோ ரழுவ முந்நீர்","விரைகிள ருருவத் தெய்வ மிதுபடை விடுத்த வாறே."],"p":"","r":"The spell shows as wind that rolls mountains, a fire that rises in rows, and a deep sea thrown up, as the god of rich colour sends it forth.","n":"The scope of the destruction is as great as that of the end of an age, yet the target is one army; the epic holds both.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","kootru"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1430","lb":"SO1430","mm":1,"ev":"The spell appears as storm-wind, fire and sea.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:62"},"so:1431":{"book":"soolamani","num":1430,"t":["வரைகளு மரனு மண்ணு மறித்திடும் வாயுச் செந்தீப்","புரைகிளர் பொடிக ளாரப் புணர்த்திடும் புணர்ந்த போழ்திற்","றிரைகிளர் பரவை முந்நீர் திரைத்துக்கொண் டொழுகு மிஃதால்","கிள ருலகைத் தெய்வ முண்ணிய வுடன்ற வாறே."],"p":"","r":"The wind and fire shaking down mountains, trees and earth join, and the sea rolls over the world as if the god were devouring it.","n":"Line 4 begins with a broken word 'கிள ருலகை' and the last sentence is held loosely.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maram","kootru"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1431","lb":"SO1431","mm":1,"ev":"Wind, fire and sea together destroy mountains, trees and earth.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:62"},"so:1432":{"book":"soolamani","num":1431,"t":["மருங்கவை புணர்த்த பின்னை வானக வளாக மெல்லாங்","கருங்கலொன் றகன்ற மேலாற் கவித்தது கவித்த லோடும்","இருங்கலி யுலக மெல்லா மிருள்கொள வெருவி நோக்கிப்","பொருங்கலி யரசர் தானை போக்கிட மற்ற தன்றே."],"p":"","r":"The sky is covered as if a great black stone had spread over it, the world goes dark, and the quarrelsome kings' host sees no way out.","n":"Lines 1-2 are hard (what 'joins the flanks' and what the black stone is are not stated); the reading holds the darkening and the host trapped.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["padai","acham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1432","lb":"SO1432","mm":1,"ev":"Darkness closes over the sky and the rival army finds no way to retreat.","cl":[],"ag":["தானை"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:63"},"so:1433":{"book":"soolamani","num":1432,"t":["வாளொடு வாள்கள் வீழா மைந்தரை மைந்த ருந்தித்","தாளொடு தாள்க டாக்கித் தலையொடு தலைகண் முட்டித்","தோளொடு தோள்க டேய்ப்பச் சுடரணி சுடர்ந்து சிந்த","ஆளுடை யரசன் றானை யரவமோ டுடைந்த தன்றே."],"p":"","r":"Sword on sword, shoulder on shoulder, head on head, the lord's host breaks with a roar and scatters its bright ornaments.","n":"'ஆளுடை அரசன்' is not named; it is read as the king whose army was shown trapped in 1432, but the verse does not fix which side.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["padai","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1433","lb":"SO1433","mm":1,"ev":"A host breaks in close fighting, with weapons and ornaments flying.","cl":[],"ag":["தானை"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:63"},"so:1434":{"book":"soolamani","num":1433,"t":["அன்னண முடைந்த போழ்தி னருக்கனை முகத்து நோக்கி","என்னிது விளைந்த வாறென் றிருங்கடல் வண்ணன் கேட்பக்","கன்னவில் வயிரத் தோளாய் காய்ந்தவன் விடுக்கப் பட்டு","மன்னுயி ருண்ணுஞ் சண்ட வேகையாம் வருவ தென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"Asked by the sea-dark one what has happened, a companion answers that the angry foe has loosed a missile named Chanda Vegai which will come and drink the lives of the living.","n":"The questioner is the sea-dark one (Tivittan, 'இருங்கடல் வண்ணன்'); the one addressed by the odd name 'அருக்கன்' is not otherwise identified, so the speaker is a guess from the word order.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["padai","acham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1434","lb":"SO1434","mm":1,"ev":"Tivittan asks what is happening and is warned that the enemy's Chanda Vegai missile is coming.","cl":[],"ag":["செங்கண் ணெடியான்","அருக்கன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["அருக்கன்","செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ch":"so:63"},"so:1435":{"book":"soolamani","num":1434,"t":["செற்றலன் விடுத்த பின்றைச் செகுத்துயிர் பருகி னல்லான்","மற்றிது மறித லில்லை மறிப்பவர் பிறரு மில்லை","இற்றிதன் நிலைமை யென்ன விருங்கடல் வண்ண னக்காங்","கற்றமி லலங்கல் வேலோ யஞ்சினை போறி யென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"The warning goes on: once sent in hate the missile does not turn back until it has killed, and no one can turn it; the sea-dark one asks its nature and teases the spearman for seeming afraid.","n":"The claim is answered when the missile's own deity shrinks in 1439-1440. The last words ('you seem afraid') may be Tivittan's teasing; the verse does not mark the change of speaker.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["padai","anjaamai","acham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1435","lb":"SO1435","mm":1,"ev":"The companion says the missile cannot be turned back, and Tivittan twits him for fear.","cl":[["a missile loosed in hatred must kill and no one can turn it back","general","refuted",2,"arukkan"]],"ag":["செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ec":[],"spk":["அருக்கன்","செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ch":"so:63"},"so:1436":{"book":"soolamani","num":1435,"t":["பேயெரி யுமிழ்ந்து நம்மேல் வருமெனப் பேசு கின்றாய்","நீபெரி தினியை யென்னா நெடியவன் றன்னை நோக்கிக்","காயெரி சுடர்விட் டாங்குக் கனன்றனன் கனலலோடு","மாயிரு விசும்பு மஞ்சும் வடிவினன் வள்ள லானான்."],"p":"","r":"Answering that the missile will 'come spitting fire' he calls the speaker very gentle, and grows in anger and flame, taking a form that frightens even the sky.","n":"'நெடியவன்' (the tall one) is the sea-dark one, as in the earlier verses; 'வள்ளல்' (the generous one) as he grows reads as an epithet, not a change of subject.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vekuli","perumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1436","lb":"SO1436","mm":1,"ev":"Tivittan mocks the warning and swells in size and fire.","cl":[],"ag":["செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ec":[],"spk":["செங்கண் ணெடியான்","அருக்கன்"],"ch":"so:63"},"so:1437":{"book":"soolamani","num":1436,"t":["நலம்புரி செய்கை மேனாட் பெற்றநற் றோழ னேபோல்","உலம்புரி யுருவத் தோளாற் குற்றபோழ் துதவ லுற்று","வலம்புரி வலத்த தாக விடத்ததோர் வயிர வல்விற்","கலம்புரி கனபொற் பூணான் கைவந்து புகுந்த வன்றே."],"p":"","r":"Like a good friend won in a former life, a vajra bow comes into his hand at the moment of need.","n":"The simile ('நலம்புரி செய்கை மேனாட் பெற்ற நற்றோழன்') pictures help as the return of earlier good deeds, but the narrator uses it as a comparison, not as a statement of doctrine.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","thunai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1437","lb":"SO1437","mm":1,"ev":"A diamond bow and its gem-set ornaments come into Tivittan's hands.","cl":[],"ag":["செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:63"},"so:1438":{"book":"soolamani","num":1437,"t":["நெதிசொரி சங்க மேந்தி நெடுஞ்சிலை யிடங்கைக் கொண்டு","விதிதரு நீல மேனி விரிந்தொளி துளும்ப நின்றான்","மதியொரு பால தாக வானவின் மருங்கு கோலிப்","புதியதோர் பருவ மேகம் போந்தெழு கின்ற தொத்தான்."],"p":"","r":"He stands with a conch in one hand and the long bow in the other, his dark body shining like a new rain cloud with a rainbow beside the moon.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1438","lb":"SO1438","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:63"},"so:1439":{"book":"soolamani","num":1438,"t":["வலம்புரி சிலம்ப வாய்வைத் திருஞ்சிலை வளைய வேற்றிக்","கலம்புரி கனபொன் னாழிக் கைவிரல் கதிர்ப்பச் சூட்டி","உலம்புரி வயிரத் தோளா னுரப்பினா னுரப்ப லோடுஞ்","சலம்புரி தெய்வ மஞ்சித் தன்னுரு வடைந்த தன்றே."],"p":"","r":"He sets the conch to his mouth and sounds it, bends the bow and fits a ring on his finger and urges on his weapon; the deity of the hostile missile is frightened and returns to its own shape.","n":"'சலம்' can be anger, deceit or water; the deity is read as that of the missile from 1434-1435, which is why it is said to turn back.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["padai","acham","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1439","lb":"SO1439","mm":1,"ev":"Tivittan sounds the conch and shoots; the missile's deity shrinks back in fear.","cl":[],"ag":["செங்கண் ணெடியான்","சலம்புரி தெய்வம்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:63"},"so:1440":{"book":"soolamani","num":1439,"t":["நிலத்திடை நின்று வான முடியுற நிமிர்ந்து கண்ணின்","புலத்தின தளவு நீங்கிப் பொம்மென வுயிர்த்து விம்மிப்","பிலத்தின தளவிற் பேழ்வாய் பிறழ்ந்திலங் கெயிற்ற தாகிச்","சலத்தினைப் புரிந்த தெய்வந் தலைபனித் துடைந்த தன்றே."],"p":"","r":"The deity, tall as the sky, breathes hard with gaping fangs, then trembles and shrinks.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["acham","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1440","lb":"SO1440","mm":1,"ev":"The deity trembles and shrinks away.","cl":[],"ag":["சலம்புரி தெய்வம்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:63"},"so:1441":{"book":"soolamani","num":1440,"t":["தெய்வமாங் குடைந்து தன்பாற் படையினைத் திரைத்துக் கொண்டு","மையிரு விசும்பி னேறக் கண்டபின் மாற்று வேந்தன்","கையினைப் புடைத்துக் கண்கள் சிவந்துவா யெயிறு கவ்வி","வையக நடுங்க நோக்கி மழகளி றணைக வென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"The rival king sees the deity retreat into the sky with its weapon, strikes his hands, glares and bites his lip, and orders the young elephant brought.","n":"'மாற்று வேந்தன்' (the opposing king) is read as Asvagriva, the discus-holder.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vekuli","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1441","lb":"SO1441","mm":1,"ev":"Seeing his missile fail, Asvagriva rages and calls for his elephant.","cl":[],"ag":["அச்சுவக்கிரீவன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["அச்சுவக்கிரீவன்",""],"ch":"so:63"},"so:1442":{"book":"soolamani","num":1441,"t":["மாலுமால் களிறு நுந்தி மற்றவன் வருத லோடு","மாலுமால் புரவித் திண்டே ரரசர தரவத் தானை","வேலினா னுடங்கு நில்லா துடைந்திட வெகுண்டு நோக்கி","நீலமா மணிக்குன் றொப்பா னெடுஞ்சிலை யிடங்கைக் கொண்டான்."],"p":"","r":"His elephant is driven in, the kings' host breaks before the spear, and the dark-gem-hill one looks on angrily and takes up the long bow.","n":"Whose host breaks is not said; the reading takes it as the host in the path of the charge.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["padai","maram","vekuli"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1442","lb":"SO1442","mm":1,"ev":"The enemy charges on an elephant; a host breaks; Tivittan takes the bow again.","cl":[],"ag":["அச்சுவக்கிரீவன்","செங்கண் ணெடியான்","அரசர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:63"},"so:1443":{"book":"soolamani","num":1442,"t":["வாய்ந்தநல் வயிரத் துண்டம் வளைந்தொளி துளும்ப வள்ளாற்","சேந்தன சிறுக ணோடு திசைமுகஞ் சிறகு தம்மால்","வேய்ந்தனெ விரித்து வீசி விசும்பிடை யிழிந்து வந்து","காய்ந்தெரி கணையி னாற்குக் கருடனு முழைய னானான்."],"p":"","r":"Garuda, bright-eyed with winged diamond beak, comes down from the sky and stands at the side of the one with the fire-arrow.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thunai","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1443","lb":"SO1443","mm":1,"ev":"Garuda descends and attends Tivittan.","cl":[],"ag":["கருடன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:63"},"so:1444":{"book":"soolamani","num":1443,"t":["கருடனை வலங்கொண் டேறிக் கார்முகங் கையி னேந்தி","மருடரு விசும்பி னேறி மணிவண்ண னெதிர்ந்த போழ்தின்","இருடனக் கெய்திற் றோரா னெரிகதி ராழி வேந்தன்","பொருடனக் கினியி லாத புகழ்ச்சிகள் புகல லுற்றான்."],"p":"","r":"Tivittan mounts Garuda and rises with the bow to meet the sea-coloured foe; the discus king, black with rage, begins to speak boasts of no worth.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["perumai","maanam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1444","lb":"SO1444","mm":1,"ev":"Tivittan rides Garuda into the sky; Asvagriva starts to boast.","cl":[],"ag":["செங்கண் ணெடியான்","அச்சுவக்கிரீவன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:63"},"so:1445":{"book":"soolamani","num":1444,"t":["தானவர் நின்னைப் போலத் தந்திற லறிவி லாதார்","ஈனவ ரிரங்கி வீழ்ந்தா ரேனையர் தொழுது வாழ்ந்தார்","வானவ ரென்னை யஞ்சி வானிடை மறைந்து செல்வார்","ஊனவர் தம்மு ணீயே யுயிரெனக் கிழக்க லுற்றாய்."],"p":"","r":"Asvagriva boasts that the Danavas who were not like Tivittan fell weeping or bowed and lived, that gods hide in the sky from him, and that Tivittan alone has chosen to lose his life.","n":"The boast is the foe's; the story soon shows its weakness in 1448 and in the failing weapons of 1451-1456.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["perumai","sirumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1445","lb":"SO1445","mm":1,"ev":"Asvagriva claims the gods and Danavas fear him and that Tivittan is a fool to face him.","cl":[["a lord whom gods hide from and foes bow to is not to be faced by one who wants to live","general","reported",3,"asvagriva"]],"ag":["அச்சுவக்கிரீவன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["அச்சுவக்கிரீவன்","செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ch":"so:63"},"so:1446":{"book":"soolamani","num":1445,"t":["மண்ணுள்வாழ் சிதலைச் சாதி மற்றவை வாழு நாள்கள்","எண்ணியாங் கிகந்த பின்னை யிறகுபெய் தெழுங்கள் போலாம்","கண்ணினா லதனை நீயுங் கருடப்புள் ளதனை யேறி","விண்ணினா றெதிர்ந்து வந்தாய் வேற்கிரை யாகி யென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"Like winged termites rising when their days are counted, you have come against my spear on Garuda as its prey.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sirumai","kedu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1446","lb":"SO1446","mm":1,"ev":"Asvagriva calls Tivittan a termite flying to its death.","cl":[],"ag":["அச்சுவக்கிரீவன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["அச்சுவக்கிரீவன்","செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ch":"so:63"},"so:1447":{"book":"soolamani","num":1446,"t":["மாறலா மனிதர் தம்மேல் வண்சுட ராழி யானுஞ்","சீறினா னென்ற போழ்திற் சிறுசொலாய் நிற்கு மென்று","தேறினார் மொழிகள் கொண்டு செவிசுடு சொற்கள் கேட்டு","மாறினே னென்ப தோரா யளியற்றா யனல்விக் கின்றாய்."],"p":"","r":"Asvagriva says he left off anger at men on hearing that even the discus-lord rose against them, but that Tivittan's ear-burning words prove he is pitiless, and so he is moved to wrath.","n":"Lines 1-3 are tangled; the reading keeps only what is clear, that he answers insult with threat.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vekuli","sol"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1447","lb":"SO1447","mm":1,"ev":"Asvagriva justifies his anger by Tivittan's words.","cl":[],"ag":["அச்சுவக்கிரீவன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["அச்சுவக்கிரீவன்","செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ch":"so:63"},"so:1448":{"book":"soolamani","num":1447,"t":["என்றலு மதனைக் கேட்டே யிருங்கடல் வண்ண னக்கு","நன்றுநன் றுரைத்தி மீட்டு நல்லையே பெரிது மேடா","குன்றின்மே லிருந்து நீநின் குழுவினுண் மொழிவ தல்லால்","இன்றுவந் தென்மு னின்று மிதுகொலோ கருதிற் றென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"The sea-dark one says: good, good, you speak well, but this is what you say among your own band from a hilltop; can it be what you really mean to say to my face?","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sol","maram","perumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1448","lb":"SO1448","mm":1,"ev":"Tivittan answers that boasting among one's own band is not the same as facing the foe.","cl":[["brave talk to one's own men from a safe height is worth nothing in front of the foe","general","asserted",3,"tivittan"]],"ag":["செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ec":[],"spk":["செங்கண் ணெடியான்","அச்சுவக்கிரீவன்"],"ch":"so:63"},"so:1449":{"book":"soolamani","num":1448,"t":["சிறியவ னுரைகள் வந்தென் செவிசுடும் பொறுக்க லாற்றேன்","எறிகடல் வளாகந் தன்னு ளிவன்பெய ரொழிப்ப னென்று","செறிமணிக் கடகக் கையாற் றிண்சிலை குழைய வாங்கிப்","பொறிநுதல் யானை மேலான் சரமழை பொழிவிக் கின்றான்."],"p":"","r":"Asvagriva, hearing the small man's words burn his ears, vows to wipe out his name from the earth, draws the strong bow on his elephant and pours arrows.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vekuli","sirumai","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1449","lb":"SO1449","mm":1,"ev":"Enraged, Asvagriva shoots a rain of arrows at Tivittan.","cl":[["a lord must wipe out the name of one who insults him","general","reported",2,"asvagriva"]],"ag":["அச்சுவக்கிரீவன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["அச்சுவக்கிரீவன்","செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ch":"so:63"},"so:1450":{"book":"soolamani","num":1449,"t":["கடுத்தவ னெய்த போழ்திற் கருடன்றன் சிறகு தன்னாற்","புடைத்திட நெரிந்துப் பொங்கிச் சரங்கள்போய்ப் புரள நோக்கி","விடைத்திறல் விடலை தன்மேல் வெம்பிய மனத்த னாகிப்","படைத்திற லாளன் றெய்வப் படைத்தொழில் பறைக்க லுற்றான்."],"p":"","r":"The arrows fall from Garuda's wing-beats; seeing them scattered, the bow-skilled one turns his mind to hostile divine weapons.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["padai","vekuli"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1450","lb":"SO1450","mm":1,"ev":"Garuda's wings beat the arrows aside; Asvagriva turns to divine weapons.","cl":[],"ag":["கருடன்","அச்சுவக்கிரீவன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:63"},"so:1451":{"book":"soolamani","num":1450,"t":["காயிரும் பனைய வெய்யோன் கருமணி வண்ணன் றன்மேல்","ஆயிரம் பணத்த தாய வருமணி யாடு நாக","மாயிரும் புகழி னான்றன் வன்சிலை வாங்கி யெய்யச்","சேயிருஞ் சுடர்கள் சிந்தித் தீயுமிழ்ந் தோடிற் றன்றே."],"p":"","r":"The foe shoots a weapon in the form of a thousand-hooded jewelled cobra at the dark-gem one, and fire sparks run from it.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["padai","acham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1451","lb":"SO1451","mm":1,"ev":"Asvagriva looses a naga weapon at Tivittan.","cl":[],"ag":["அச்சுவக்கிரீவன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:63"},"so:1452":{"book":"soolamani","num":1451,"t":["கண்டன னதனை மற்றக் கருங்கடல் வண்ணன் கண்டே","ஒண்டிற லுவணப் புள்ளி னுருவினோர் தெய்வ வம்பு","கொண்டனன் றொடுத்த லோடுங் கொடுஞ்சிறை நுடங்க வீசித்","துண்டமா நாகந் தன்னைத் துண்டத்தாற் றுணித்த தன்றே."],"p":"","r":"Tivittan sees it, takes a divine arrow in Garuda's form, and when it is shot the bird cuts the cobra to pieces with its beak.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["padai","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1452","lb":"SO1452","mm":1,"ev":"Tivittan's Garuda weapon tears apart the naga weapon.","cl":[],"ag":["செங்கண் ணெடியான்","உவணப் புள்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:63"},"so:1453":{"book":"soolamani","num":1452,"t":["ஆயிடை யனன்று மீட்டு மழலுமி ழாழி வேந்தன்","தீயொடு புணர்த்த போழ்தத் தெய்வவம் பெய்த லோடும்","வேயுடை விலங்கல் சுட்டு விசும்பிடை வெம்ப வெம்பிச்","சேயிடை யெரிந்து சிந்திச் செல்வன்மேற் சென்ற தன்றே."],"p":"","r":"The enraged discus-holder joins fire to the string and shoots a fire weapon that scorches the mountains and the sky and goes against Tivittan.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["padai","vekuli"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1453","lb":"SO1453","mm":1,"ev":"Asvagriva looses a fire weapon.","cl":[],"ag":["அச்சுவக்கிரீவன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:63"},"so:1454":{"book":"soolamani","num":1453,"t":["காரணி வண்ணன் கண்டே கதிர்மணிக் கடகக் கையால்","வாருண மென்னு மம்பு வாங்கினன் றொடுத்த லோடுஞ்","சீரணி விசும்பு மண்ணுந் திசைகளு மிருள வீழ்ந்து","நீரணி புயலின் றாரை நிரந்துவீழ்ந் தவித்த தன்றே."],"p":"","r":"Tivittan draws the Varuna arrow; at its flight the sky, earth and quarters darken and rain falls in streams, putting out the fire.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["padai","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1454","lb":"SO1454","mm":1,"ev":"Tivittan's Varuna arrow drowns the fire weapon in rain.","cl":[],"ag":["செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:63"},"so:1455":{"book":"soolamani","num":1454,"t":["விண்களை வெதுப்பு நீர்மை வெய்யவன் வெகுண்டு மீட்டுங்","கண்களைத் துயிற்று மம்பு தொடுத்தனன் றொடுத்தலோடும்","மண்களை மயக்கி மாக்க டுயில்கொள மரங்கள் சாயப்","புண்களை யணையும் வேலான் படைமுகம் புக்க தன்றே."],"p":"","r":"The sun-hot one shoots a sleep arrow; creatures fall asleep, trees bend, and it enters the army's front.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["padai","acham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1455","lb":"SO1455","mm":1,"ev":"Asvagriva looses a weapon that puts living things to sleep.","cl":[],"ag":["அச்சுவக்கிரீவன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:63"},"so:1456":{"book":"soolamani","num":1455,"t":["அயிலுடை யனல்செய் வேலோ னதனையு மறிந்து மற்றுத்","துயில்விடை செய்யு மம்பு தொடுத்தனன் றொடுத்த லோடும்","வெயிலிடை விரிந்து விண்பால் விளங்கிவீ ழிருளை நீக்கப்","பயிலுடை யுலகந் தேறிப் பட்டது முணர்ந்த தன்றே."],"p":"","r":"Knowing it, the one with the fire-spear shoots an awakening arrow whose light drives off the dark and wakes the world, which understands what has happened.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["padai","arivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1456","lb":"SO1456","mm":1,"ev":"Tivittan wakes the sleepers with his counter-weapon.","cl":[],"ag":["செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:63"},"so:1457":{"book":"soolamani","num":1456,"t":["சீற்றமொ டிரியுஞ் செல்வத் தெய்வவம் பெய்த வெல்லா","மாற்றின னறுப்ப நோக்கி மற்றவன் மாற்ற லான்பே","ராற்றலை யறிந்து வெய்ய வாழிகை யேந்தி யின்னும்","ஏற்றனை பொருதி யோவென் றிலங்கெயி றிலங்க நக்கான்."],"p":"","r":"Seeing his weapons cut down and the other's strength, the king bares his teeth in a laugh and asks whether he will still stand and fight when the discus is raised.","n":"Which weapons were cut and by whom is not named; lines 1-2 are read loosely.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maram","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1457","lb":"SO1457","mm":1,"ev":"Ayakantan, taking up his discus, laughs and taunts his opponent to fight on.","cl":[],"ag":["அயகண்டன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["அயகண்டன்","செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ch":"so:64"},"so:1458":{"book":"soolamani","num":1457,"t":["தாழியா தெய்யுந் தெய்வப் படைமுத லறுத்துச் சாலப்","பாழியான் மெலிந்தும் பண்டைப் பாவனை பயிற்றி யென்னை","ஆழியால் வெருட்ட லுற்றா யலந்தனை பெரிது மென்றான்","சூழிமால் யானை வல்ல சுரமைநாட் டிளைய கோவே."],"p":"","r":"The young lord of Suramai answers that, though weakened by the long fight, he sees the old trick of frightening him with the discus, and says the king has only tired himself.","n":"The speaker is fixed only by the vocative in line 4, which names the speaker of the reply.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maram","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1458","lb":"SO1458","mm":1,"ev":"Tivittan answers Ayakantan's taunt, saying the discus will not frighten him.","cl":[],"ag":["செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ec":[],"spk":["செங்கண் ணெடியான்","அயகண்டன்"],"ch":"so:64"},"so:1459":{"book":"soolamani","num":1458,"t":["புனைகதி ரார மார்பன் புகைந்துகை முறுக்கி விட்ட","கனைகதிர்த் திகிரி கான்ற கனசுடர் வளைக்கப் பட்டு","முனைகதிர் கானச் செந்தீ முழங்கிமேன் மூடப்பட்ட","வனைகதிர்க் குன்றம் போல மணிவண்ணன் மறைந்து போனான்."],"p":"","r":"The king twists his hand in rage and hurls the blazing discus, and the dark-hued hero vanishes inside its fire as a mountain is lost in a burning forest.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1459","lb":"SO1459","mm":1,"ev":"Ayakantan hurls his discus and Tivittan is hidden by its flame.","cl":[],"ag":["அயகண்டன்","செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:64"},"so:1460":{"book":"soolamani","num":1459,"t":["உலங்கொண்ட வயிரத் தோளாற் குற்றதை யுணர மாட்டார்","நிலங்கொண்டு மனித ராழ நிரந்தழ லுமிழ்ந்து நேமி","புலங்கொண்ட வயிரக் குன்றின் புடைவரும் பரிதி போல","வலங்கொண்டு வந்து மைந்தன் வலப்புடை நின்ற தன்றே."],"p":"","r":"The discus, spewing fire, does not strike the youth but wheels round and stands at his right side, like the sun beside a diamond mountain, while the onlookers cannot grasp what has happened.","n":"The verse does not say why the discus spares him; a reader should not add a cause the Tamil leaves out.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","oozh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1460","lb":"SO1460","mm":1,"ev":"The discus turns aside and halts at Tivittan's right.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:64"},"so:1461":{"book":"soolamani","num":1460,"t":["கன்னவில் கடகத் தோளான் கண்டுகை தொழுது கொண்டு","மின்னவிர் விளங்கு நேமி விட்டனன் விடுத்த லோடும்","மன்னனை மார்பு கீண்டு மணிமுடி யெறிந்து மற்றைப்","பொன்னவி ரோடை யானைப் புகர்நுதற் புக்க தன்றே."],"p":"","r":"The hero sees the discus, salutes it with joined hands, and sends it back; it splits the king's chest, strikes off his crown and enters the forehead of his elephant.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","kootru"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1461","lb":"SO1461","mm":1,"ev":"Tivittan hurls the returned discus and it kills Ayakantan on his elephant.","cl":[],"ag":["செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:64"},"so:1462":{"book":"soolamani","num":1461,"t":["கழலவன் கனன்று விட்ட கதிர்நகை நேமி போழ","மழகளி யானை தன்மேன் மறிந்துவீழ் கின்ற மன்னன்","நிழலவிர் விலங்க னெற்றி நிமிர்ந்ததோர் காள மேகம்","அழலவன் றிகிரி பாய வற்றுவீழ் கின்ற தொத்தான்."],"p":"","r":"The king, struck down, tumbles onto his elephant like a dark cloud on a mountain brow riven by a bolt of fire.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1462","lb":"SO1462","mm":1,"ev":"Ayakantan falls dead over his elephant.","cl":[],"ag":["அயகண்டன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:64"},"so:1463":{"book":"soolamani","num":1462,"t":["நெறிதலை திரிவி லான்மே னினைவிலான் மொழியப் பட்ட","மறுதலை முடிக்கு மேது வாய்வழி யழிப்ப தேபோற்","பொறிதலை மணந்த காளை மேல்வரப் புணர்த்த நேமி","செறிதலை யிலாத மன்னன் றன்னையே செகுத்த தன்றே."],"p":"","r":"The discus sent at a man of unwavering path rebounds and kills the king who lacked restraint, as a curse spoken against a blameless man ends in the speaker's own mouth.","n":"The simile asks the reader to take the king's death as a rebounding of his own deed; it does not name a former birth, so it is not the karma of earlier lives.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","maram","aram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1463","lb":"SO1463","mm":1,"ev":"The discus kills the king who sent it.","cl":[["a harm aimed at a blameless man returns on its sender","general","asserted",3,"narrator"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:64"},"so:1464":{"book":"soolamani","num":1463,"t":["கொலையானை மேலோர் குளிர்வெண் குடைக்கீழ்ப்","பலயானை மன்னர் பலர்போற்ற வந்தான்","மலையாகம் போழாக மற்றிவனோ சாய்ந்தான்","நிலையாமை சால நிலைபெற்ற தன்றே."],"p":"","r":"Onlookers say that the man who came with elephants under a white umbrella and kings praising him is now cut down and brought low, and that impermanence has become plain.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1464","lb":"SO1464","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["the pomp of kings does not last","general","asserted",4,"onlookers"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["அருகு நின்றவர்",""],"ch":"so:64"},"so:1465":{"book":"soolamani","num":1464,"t":["நெருநல் நெடுங்குடைக்கீழ் நேமிமுன் செல்லப்","பொருநல் வயவேந்தர் போற்றிசைப்ப வந்தான்","செருநன் மறநேமி சென்றதுவே போழ","எரிபொன் மணிமுடியா னின்றிவனோ சாய்ந்தான்."],"p":"","r":"Yesterday he came under the long umbrella with the discus before him and kings chanting his praise; today the same man lies felled by that discus, with his golden crown still bright.","n":"The verse repeats the sense of 1464 with a day-to-day contrast of 'yesterday' and 'now'.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1465","lb":"SO1465","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["a king's glory of one day is gone the next","general","asserted",1,"onlookers"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["அருகு நின்றவர்",""],"ch":"so:64"},"so:1466":{"book":"soolamani","num":1465,"t":["தானெறிந்த நேமி தனக்கே பகையாகத்","தேனெறிந்த தாரான் சிறுவரைக்கண் வீடினான்","யானறிந்த வாற்றா லெளிய வுலகத்தில்","வானறிந்த வாழ்க்கையு மாயமே போலுமால்."],"p":"","r":"A speaker says that the discus the king threw became his own enemy and that he died at the hands of mere youths, so that even a life the heavens know seems an illusion.","n":"Line 2 calls his opponents 'boys', a mark of contempt for the king's fall rather than a statement of their age.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","vinai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1466","lb":"SO1466","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["even the life the heavens know is as unreal as magic","general","asserted",4,"onlookers"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["அருகு நின்றவர்",""],"ch":"so:64"},"so:1467":{"book":"soolamani","num":1466,"t":["வலியு மடுதிறனும் வாழ்வும் வனப்பும்","பொலிவுங் கடைபோகா பூமிமேல் வாழ்வீர்","கலியன்மி னென்றிதனைக் காட்டுவான் போல","மலிபொன் மணிமுடியான் மற்றிவனோ மாய்ந்தான்."],"p":"","r":"Strength, killing power, wealth, beauty and splendour do not last to the end, he seems to say to those who live on earth, so do not be proud, and his fall shows it.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1467","lb":"SO1467","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["strength, wealth and beauty do not last; do not exult","general","asserted",1,"onlookers"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["அருகு நின்றவர்",""],"ch":"so:64"},"so:1468":{"book":"soolamani","num":1467,"t":["மாக மழைவண்ணன் மாற்றான்மேல் விட்டெறிந்த","வேக விறலாழி மீட்டே பெயர்ந்துதன்","போக வரைமார்பம் போழ்படுப்பப் பொன்றினான்","ஆகுவ தாமதனை யாவ ரறிகிற்பார்."],"p":"","r":"The discus he sent at the enemy came back and split his own broad chest, and he died; who can know what will come to pass?","n":"The subject of line 1 is uncertain: it may name the king, whose body is dark, or the hero; the sense of the discus returning on its thrower is clear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vinai","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1468","lb":"SO1468","mm":1,"ev":"The king's discus returns and kills him.","cl":[["no one can know what will be","general","asserted",4,"narrator"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:64"},"so:1469":{"book":"soolamani","num":1468,"t":["இன்னன கண்டார் மொழிந்திரங்கு மாயிடை","அன்ன மனையா ரயகண்டன் றேவிமார்","பொன்னவிர் பூங்குழையார் பொங்கேந் திளமுலையார்","மின்னவிர் நுண்ணிடையார் மேல்வந் தணுகினார்."],"p":"","r":"At such sights people pity him, and then the wives of Ayakantan, wearing gold earrings and with swelling breasts and slim waists, come near.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["idumbai","sutram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1469","lb":"SO1469","mm":1,"ev":"The king's wives come to the body.","cl":[],"ag":["அயகண்டன் றேவிமார்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:64"},"so:1470":{"book":"soolamani","num":1469,"t":["வண்டார் மணிமுடியான் மார்பு துணிகிடப்பக்","கண்டாள் பெருந்தேவி கண்டேதன் கைசோர்ந்து","வெண்டாரை வேனெடுங்க ணீர்மூழ்கி மேற்பிறழ","விண்டா ளுயிர்பின்னும் வெற்றுடல மாயினாள்."],"p":"","r":"The chief queen sees her husband lying with his chest cut and her hand drops; her eyes drown in tears and life leaves her so that she is an empty body.","n":"The Tamil says her life 'left' and her body was empty, which could be a faint or a death; she is not named again in these verses.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["idumbai","yaakkai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1470","lb":"SO1470","mm":1,"ev":"The chief queen faints and her life leaves her at the sight of the dead king.","cl":[],"ag":["பெருந்தேவி"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:64"},"so:1471":{"book":"soolamani","num":1470,"t":["கோதை சரியக் கொடிமருங்கு லேரழிய","மாதர் மடமஞ்ஞை மாநிலத்து வீழ்வனபோற்","காதலன் மார்பகலங் கண்டேதங் கண்புடைத்துப்","பேதை மடமகளிர் வீழ்ந்தார் பிணையன்னார்."],"p":"","r":"Their garlands slip, their waists give way, and the young women fall to the ground like peacocks, beating their eyes when they see their lord's chest.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["idumbai","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1471","lb":"SO1471","mm":1,"ev":"The wives collapse on the field and beat their faces.","cl":[],"ag":["அயகண்டன் றேவிமார்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:64"},"so:1472":{"book":"soolamani","num":1471,"t":["வாங்கு கொடிமுறுக்கி மாநிலத்து விட்டனபோல்","தாங்கார் புரண்டுதலை தடுமாறாய்க் கிடந்தங்","கேங்கினா ரெத்துணையோர் போழ்தங் கழிந்தெழுந்து","நீங்காத வாருயிரார் நீரா யுருகினார்."],"p":"","r":"They roll about like tangled creepers flung to the earth; after a time they get up, unable to leave their beloved's side, and melt like water.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["idumbai","anbu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1472","lb":"SO1472","mm":1,"ev":"The wives roll on the ground and rise to weep.","cl":[],"ag":["அயகண்டன் றேவிமார்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:64"},"so:1473":{"book":"soolamani","num":1472,"t":["அரைச ரேறே யடலாழி வலவ வார்க்குந் தோலாதாய்","புரிசை நகரநூற் றொருபதுடையாய் பூமி முழு தாண்டாய்","செய் துலகம் பாராட்டு மொளியா யோடை யானையாய்","வரைசெய் தனைய திரடோளாய் மறிதல் பொருளோ வயவேந்தே."],"p":"","r":"A lament addresses the king as lord of armies and of a hundred forts and asks if one who ruled the whole earth should be turned over in death.","n":"The lament reads as grief rather than doctrine: the women ask whether the fall is fitting, and the work does not answer them here.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["idumbai","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1473","lb":"SO1473","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["a king who ruled the whole earth should not meet defeat","general","reported",4,"ayakantan_devimar"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["அயகண்டன் றேவிமார்","அயகண்டன்"],"ch":"so:64"},"so:1474":{"book":"soolamani","num":1473,"t":["வானு மண்ணு முடனஞ்சும் வகையாய் மன்னர் மணிமுடிமேல்","தேனும் வண்டும் பலசென்று திளைக்குஞ் செம்பொற் செறிகழலாய்","நான மண்ணி யகிறேக்கி நாவி கமழு மெழிலாகம்","ஈன மண்ணி லிவர்காணக் கிடத்த லினிதோ விகல்வேந்தே."],"p":"","r":"The lament asks whether it is fitting that the king whose feet were worshipped by kings and whose perfumed body smelled of musk should lie before all in the mean earth.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["idumbai","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1474","lb":"SO1474","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["அயகண்டன் றேவிமார்","அயகண்டன்"],"ch":"so:64"},"so:1475":{"book":"soolamani","num":1474,"t":["குழவி நாயிற் றெழிலேய்க்குங் குழம்பார் கோலக் குங்குமமே","மெழுகி மீதோர் மணியாரம் வீசிக் கிடந்த விரையாகம்","ஒழுகு குருதிச் சேறாடி யோடை யானை நுதன்மீது","வழுவி வீழ்ந்த வகைநாடின் மாயம் போலு மறவேந்தே."],"p":"","r":"His chest, once smeared with saffron paste and a jewelled necklace, now lies bathed in a mud of blood on the elephant's forehead, which seems a thing of illusion.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["idumbai","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1475","lb":"SO1475","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["the splendour of a king's body is like an illusion","general","reported",4,"ayakantan_devimar"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["அயகண்டன் றேவிமார்","அயகண்டன்"],"ch":"so:64"},"so:1476":{"book":"soolamani","num":1475,"t":["பொன்னி னாய வமளிமேற் பூவி னாய வணைபொருந்தி","அன்ன மனையா ரடிவருட வமரும் பள்ளி யமராதே","மன்னு மிங்கோர் மதயானை நுதன்மேல் மறிந்து மணிமுடிசாய்த்","தென்னு முரையாய் துயில்கோட லிசையோ வங்கள் பெருமானே."],"p":"","r":"Instead of the golden couch where wives rubbed his feet, he lies tipped over on a rutting elephant's forehead with the crown fallen aside; the speakers ask if that is fit sleep.","n":"The contrast of couch and battle-elephant is the lament's argument; no claim is stated.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["idumbai","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1476","lb":"SO1476","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["அயகண்டன் றேவிமார்","அயகண்டன்"],"ch":"so:64"},"so:1477":{"book":"soolamani","num":1476,"t":["மகரப் பைம்பூண் மடவார்கள் வயிரக் குழையும் பொற்றோடுந்","தகரக் குழலு மளகமுந் திருத்திப் பயின்ற தாழ்தடக்கை","சிகர மனைய மதயானைச் செவிமேற் சரிந்து செங்குருதி","பகரக் கழுகு பாராட்டக் கிடத்த றகுமோ படைவேந்தே."],"p":"","r":"The hands that the women adorned with jewels and perfumes now hang from the ear of a mountain-like elephant while vultures feed on the blood; the lament asks if that is fitting.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["idumbai","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1477","lb":"SO1477","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["அயகண்டன் றேவிமார்","அயகண்டன்"],"ch":"so:64"},"so:1478":{"book":"soolamani","num":1477,"t":["வெய்ய சுடரோன் றண்கதிரோ னெனவீங் கிவர்கண் மதிலியங்கார்","பைய வந்து தாமரையின் பரவைத் தடத்து மாளிகைமேல்","ஐய தலத்து மெலவிரிந்த தலராச் செல்லு மாணையாய்","செய்ய குருதிச் சேறாடிச் சிறுமா னிடர்க்கே தோற்றாயே."],"p":"","r":"A lament says the king who gave orders like the sun and moon across the city and its halls was brought down by common men and sits bathed in blood.","n":"Several words of lines 1-3 are run together in the edition and line 1 is read loosely.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["idumbai","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1478","lb":"SO1478","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["அயகண்டன் றேவிமார்","அயகண்டன்"],"ch":"so:64"},"so:1479":{"book":"soolamani","num":1478,"t":["பணங்கொ ணாகம் பலசூழ்ந்து பகல்செய் மணியின் சுடரேந்தி","அணங்கி யகலா துழைநிற்கு மாணை யுடைய வடல்வேந்தே","வணங்கி வந்து பலதெய்வம் வழிபா டாற்று மறநேமிக்","குணங்கொள் படையாய் கூடாரு முளரோ நினக்குக் கோமானே."],"p":"","r":"The women remind him that gods came to worship his discus and snakes with jewelled hoods stood by him, and ask how foes could ever have joined against him.","n":"The speakers presume the discus made him safe; the story has shown it was his own weapon that killed him.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["idumbai","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1479","lb":"SO1479","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["அயகண்டன் றேவிமார்","அயகண்டன்"],"ch":"so:64"},"so:1480":{"book":"soolamani","num":1479,"t":["பெருமா மழைக்கண் மாதேவி பிணையின் மாழ்கி யிவணழிய","வருமா முரசம் பிறர்பேர்கொண் டறைய வாழி யயனீங்கத்","திருமா நகருஞ் செல்வமுற்றுஞ் சிதையக் கண்டுஞ் சீறாயால்","உருமா லென்னுந் திறலினா யுலகம் வேண்டா தொழிந்தாயோ."],"p":"","r":"The speakers ask why, with the great queen dying like a doe and other men's names being beaten on the war-drum in his own city, he does not rise in anger.","n":"The reading of 'வாழி யயனீங்க' in line 2 is uncertain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["idumbai","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1480","lb":"SO1480","mm":1,"ev":"The wives say the conquerors are already claiming the king's city and drum.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["அயகண்டன் றேவிமார்","அயகண்டன்"],"ch":"so:64"},"so:1481":{"book":"soolamani","num":1480,"t":["மூரி முந்நீ ருலகங்கண் முழுதுங் காவன் முனிந்தாயோ","யாரு மில்லா வடியோங்கள் வழிபா டாற்ற மாட்டாயோ","சீரின் மன்னும் வளநாடுந் தெய்வப் படையுஞ் செல்வமுநீ","பாரின் மன்னர் பிறர்கொள்ளப் பணித்த தென்னோ படைவேந்தே."],"p":"","r":"The lament closes asking if he was angry with the world, and why he has left to other kings his land, his divine weapon and his wealth, when none is left to serve him.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["idumbai","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1481","lb":"SO1481","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["a king should guard the whole world and those who serve him","general","reported",1,"ayakantan_devimar"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["அயகண்டன் றேவிமார்","அயகண்டன்"],"ch":"so:64"},"so:1482":{"book":"soolamani","num":1481,"t":["தொழுதுஞ் சூழ்ந்து மடிபற்றித் தொடர்ந்துஞ் சுரும்புண் கோதையும்","வழுவ மயங்கி மாழாந்து மருண்டுந் தெருண்டு மடவார்கள்","அழுத கண்ணீ ரகன்ஞாலத் தரச ருருக வருவியாய்","ஒழுக நெடுமான் முனிவென்னு மூழித் தீயு மவிந்ததே."],"p":"","r":"The weeping of the women flows like streams, and with it the great king's wrath, likened to the fire at the world's end, is put out.","n":"The first two lines are about the women and kings who weep, the last two about the anger that goes out; who exactly the kings melting are, and whose anger is meant, is not fixed by the words, so the reading keeps to the image of tears as water on fire.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","alar"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1482","lb":"SO1482","mm":1,"ev":"The tears of the grieving women quench the victor's long-burning anger.","cl":[],"ag":["மடவார்கள்","செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:65"},"so:1483":{"book":"soolamani","num":1482,"t":["மஞ்சு தோயும் வரையார்தம் மன்னன் றன்னை மதயானை","மஞ்சு தோன்ற நுதலினிழித் தந்த ணாளார் மெய்தீண்டிப்","பஞ்சுந் துகிலும் பூம்பட்டும் பாயப்பள்ளி படுத்ததன்மேல்","வஞ்ச மில்லாப் புகழானை வயங்கு செந்தீ வாய்ப்பெய்தார்."],"p":"","r":"The brahmins lay the king of the mountain folk on a bed of cotton, silk and fine cloth, after he has been brought down from his elephant, and put him into the bright fire.","n":"The dead king is called one of untainted fame (line 4), so the funeral is given with honour to the enemy slain; the verse does not name him, and he is taken from the context to be the lord of the mountain Vidyadharas.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","kootru"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1483","lb":"SO1483","mm":1,"ev":"Brahmins lay the dead Vidyadhara king on a rich bed and give him to the funeral fire.","cl":[],"ag":["அந்தணர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:65"},"so:1484":{"book":"soolamani","num":1483,"t":["மன்னன் றம்பி மார்களையு மக்க டமையு மற்றொழிந்த","பொன்னம் புனைதார் வேந்தரையும் பொருது பட்ட போர்க்களமீ","தென்னுஞ் சாடு மெரிவாய்ப்பெய் திரங்கி யழுதாங் கேகினரால்","உன்னி வந்த முடிக்ககிலா துடைந்த வேந்த னுழையாரே"],"p":"","r":"The kinsmen, sons and kings who fell in the battle are given to the fire on the field, and the king's defeated men leave weeping.","n":"Line 4 is broken in the word division and the relation of 'those who could not complete what they thought' to the king is a guess.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","kootru"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1484","lb":"SO1484","mm":1,"ev":"The attendants of the beaten king burn the fallen on the battlefield and depart in tears.","cl":[],"ag":["விஞ்சையர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:65"},"so:1485":{"book":"soolamani","num":1484,"t":["ஆவி யாய வயக்கிரீவற் கமிழ்தம் பூத்த வஞ்சாயற்","றேவி மார்கள் கலனழித்துச் சேணி யுலகஞ் சென்றெய்தி","வீவில் காமன் வருவீதி கற்பு வேலி யால்விலக்கித்","தாவி னிறையின் றாழதனாற் பொறியின் வாயி றாழ்ப்பெய்தார்."],"p":"","r":"The queens of the dead Vidyadhara king strip off their ornaments, go to the Vidyadhara world, and bar desire's way with the fence of chastity and the lock of restraint, shutting the doors of the senses.","n":"Chastity is shown as a fence the widows themselves build around the senses, not as a guard set on them by others; whether this is a turn to renunciation or only widowhood is not said.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["karpu","nirai","thuravu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1485","lb":"SO1485","mm":1,"ev":"The widowed queens put off their jewels and shut out desire.","cl":[["a woman's chastity and self-restraint fence off desire, and the senses are doors that can be locked","general","asserted",3,"narrator"]],"ag":["தேவிமார்கள்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:65"},"so:1486":{"book":"soolamani","num":1485,"t":["எரிவள ரொளிதரு நேமி யெய்திய","திருவளர் படரொளிச் செங்கண் மாலவன்","கரிவளர் குஞ்சிமேற் சொரிந்த பூமழை","வரிவளை முரசொடு மயங்கி யார்த்தவே."],"p":"","r":"A rain of flowers falls on the head of the red-eyed hero who has won the shining discus, and mingles with the sound of drums and conches.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vali","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1486","lb":"SO1486","mm":1,"ev":"Flowers are showered on the discus-winner as drums and conches sound.","cl":[],"ag":["செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:65"},"so:1487":{"book":"soolamani","num":1486,"t":["அரிதினி னவனெய்த தெய்வ வம்புகள்","உரிதினி னறுத்தொளிர் நேமி கொண்டது","பெரிதிது சித்திர மென்று பேரொலி","விரிதரு விசும்பிடை விரவி நின்றதே."],"p":"","r":"The divine arrows he shot are cut down and he takes the shining discus, and the sky is filled with a cry that it is a great marvel.","n":"The subject is not named in the verse; 'the hero who took the discus' is taken from 1486 and 1499.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vali","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1487","lb":"SO1487","mm":1,"ev":"The hero cuts down the divine arrows and takes the discus, and the sky resounds with wonder.","cl":[],"ag":["செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:65"},"so:1488":{"book":"soolamani","num":1487,"t":["வெஞ்சினஞ் செருக்கொடு வீய மானமும்","விஞ்சைய ரொழிந்தன ரொழிந்து வீரனை","மஞ்சிவர் மணிவரை வண்ண வாழிய","அஞ்சினம் பெரிதனெ வடிவ ணங்கினார்."],"p":"","r":"The Vidyadharas lay down their fierce anger and pride, and bow to the hero, the colour of a dark jewel-mountain, saying they were greatly afraid.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maanam","panivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1488","lb":"SO1488","mm":1,"ev":"The Vidyadharas give up their pride and bow to the victor in fear.","cl":[],"ag":["விஞ்சையர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:65"},"so:1489":{"book":"soolamani","num":1488,"t":["அஞ்சிய மன்னர்கட் கருளி யாயிடைத்","துஞ்சிய மன்னவன் றமரந் தோமில்சீர்","விஞ்சைய ருலகினின் மீண்டு வாழ்கென","எஞ்சலில் கடிமுரசு அறைய ஏயினான்."],"p":"","r":"The hero shows mercy to the frightened kings and has a drum sounded for the kin of the king who died there, bidding them live again in the Vidyadhara world.","n":"The speech is reported, not quoted, so the speaker is given as the hero from the action of line 4.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arul","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1489","lb":"SO1489","mm":1,"ev":"Tivittan has a drum proclaim that the dead king's people may return and live.","cl":[["a conquering king should grant mercy and let the defeated kin live","general","asserted",3,"narrator"]],"ag":["செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ec":[],"spk":["செங்கண் ணெடியான்",""],"ch":"so:65"},"so:1490":{"book":"soolamani","num":1489,"t":["கருமுகில் வண்ணனுங் கருடன் மேலிழிந்","துருமென வதிர்தரு மோடை யானைமேற்","பொருமிகல் வேந்தர்போற் றிசைப்பப் போந்தரோ","பரிமிகு படைவிடு பாடி நண்ணினான்."],"p":"","r":"The dark-cloud-coloured hero alights from Garuda, mounts a rumbling elephant, and enters the camp amid the kings' praise.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vali","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1490","lb":"SO1490","mm":1,"ev":"Tivittan comes down from Garuda and rides to the camp as kings praise him.","cl":[],"ag":["செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:65"},"so:1491":{"book":"soolamani","num":1490,"t":["விரிதரு திங்களின் விளங்கு மேனியன்","பெரியவன் றன்னொடும் பெயர்ந்து தாதைதன்","றிருவமர் சேவடி சென்று தாழ்ந்தனன்","கருவரை யனையதோட் கனபொற் றாரினான்."],"p":"","r":"Radiant like the moon, he goes with the great elder brother to the father's holy feet and bows, wearing a shoulder garland of gold.","n":"'The great one' is taken to be the elder brother Vijayan; the verse does not give the name.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["makkal","panivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1491","lb":"SO1491","mm":1,"ev":"Tivittan and his elder brother go to their father and bow at his feet.","cl":[],"ag":["செங்கண் ணெடியான்","விசயன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:65"},"so:1492":{"book":"soolamani","num":1491,"t":["மக்கள தாற்றலான் மலர்ந்த கண்ணினன்","மிக்குமேல் விரிந்தொளி துளும்பு மேனியன்","தொக்கநீர்ச் சுரமைநா டுடைய கோனிவை","பக்கநின் றரசர்கள் பணியச் சொல்லினான்."],"p":"","r":"The king of the land of Surama, his eyes wide with delight at his sons' might, speaks to the kings who stand beside and bow.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["makkal","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1492","lb":"SO1492","mm":1,"ev":"The father, pleased with his sons' prowess, addresses the kings who bow around him.","cl":[],"ag":["ஆணை வேந்தன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["ஆணை வேந்தன்",""],"ch":"so:65"},"so:1493":{"book":"soolamani","num":1492,"t":["தீதறு மணிமுடிச் செல்வக் காளையர்","தாதையென் றியலுரை தவத்தி னெய்தினேன்","ஆதலா லிவர்தம தரச கோலமெங்","காதலங் கண்ணிவை காண லாகுமே."],"p":"","r":"He says that he reached the name of father of these flawless young princes through his penance, and asks to see in their royal state the beloved jewel-adorned eyes of his sons.","n":"The father credits his own tapas, not his sons' worth, for the blessing; this is the same turn of phrase as Kural 70's question of what penance the father did.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thavam","makkal","vinai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1493","lb":"SO1493","mm":1,"ev":"The father says his fatherhood is the fruit of penance and asks to see his sons crowned.","cl":[["being the father of fine sons is the fruit of penance","general","asserted",2,"podanam_king"]],"ag":["ஆணை வேந்தன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["ஆணை வேந்தன்",""],"ch":"so:65"},"so:1494":{"book":"soolamani","num":1493,"t":["என்றவன் மொழிதலு மிலங்கு நீண்முடி","மின்றவ ழவிரொளி விஞ்சை வேந்தனோ","டொன்றிய வரசர்க ளுவந்து சூட்டினார்","அன்றவர்க் கரசியன் முரச மார்த்தவே."],"p":"","r":"At his word the Vidyadhara king and the kings joined with him gladly crown the princes, and the royal drum sounds that day.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1494","lb":"SO1494","mm":1,"ev":"The Vidyadhara king and the kings crown the princes to the beat of the royal drum.","cl":[],"ag":["விஞ்சை வேந்தர்","விஞ்சை வேந்தர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:65"},"so:1495":{"book":"soolamani","num":1494,"t":["கங்கையுஞ் சிந்துவு மென்னு மாநதி","தங்குநீ ரெனையவுந் தந்து தாமரை","பொங்கிய முகத்தபொற் குடங்க ளாற்பல","மங்கல மரபினான் மன்ன ராட்டினார்."],"p":"","r":"The kings bathe him in the waters of the Ganga and Sindhu, poured from golden lotus-mouthed pots in the auspicious way.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1495","lb":"SO1495","mm":1,"ev":"The kings pour Ganga and Sindhu water on him from golden pots.","cl":[],"ag":["விஞ்சை வேந்தர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:65"},"so:1496":{"book":"soolamani","num":1495,"t":["திருமணி நிழலொளித் தெய்வ வான்படை","பருமணிப் பாற்கடற் பரவை நீர்முகந்","தெரிமணிக் குடங்களி னேந்தி யேந்தறன்","சுரிமணிக் குஞ்சிமேற் சொரிந்த தென்பவே."],"p":"","r":"They pour on the king's curled head waters of the milk sea, taken up by the divine host and carried in pots of shining gems, so it is said.","n":"Who the bearers are is carried by the verb alone; 'the divine army' (line 1) is taken as the source of the water.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["arasu","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1496","lb":"SO1496","mm":1,"ev":"Divine hosts pour milk-sea water from gem pots on the king's head.","cl":[],"ag":["விஞ்சை வேந்தர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:65"},"so:1497":{"book":"soolamani","num":1496,"t":["வெந்திறல் விறலொளி விஞ்சை வேந்தரு","மந்தர மணிமலை மலரு மம்மலை","அந்தர வருவியும் விரவி யாட்டினார்","இந்திர னனையவற் கிறைஞ்சி யென்பவே."],"p":"","r":"The brave Vidyadhara kings bathe the one like Indra with the flowers and waterfalls of the Mandara jewel-mountain and bow to him, as the tale says.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","panivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1497","lb":"SO1497","mm":1,"ev":"The Vidyadhara kings bathe him in waters of Mandara and bow.","cl":[],"ag":["விஞ்சை வேந்தர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:65"},"so:1498":{"book":"soolamani","num":1497,"t":["திருமகள் பரிவொடு சென்று சார்தரு","முருவினு மொளியினு முலகந் தன்னின்மேன்","மருவிய நூலது மரபி னானுமக்","கருமுகில் வண்ணனைக் காவ னாட்டினார்."],"p":"","r":"Lakshmi comes with love, and by the form, the light and the old rule of the book the king is anointed protector of the world.","n":"The third and fourth lines turn on a rare phrase for the book's rule; the sense that the anointing follows a scripture's rule is held loosely.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["arasu","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1498","lb":"SO1498","mm":1,"ev":"The dark-cloud one is anointed protector of the world, with Lakshmi drawn to him.","cl":[],"ag":["விஞ்சை வேந்தர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:65"},"so:1499":{"book":"soolamani","num":1498,"t":["விட்டெரி மணிவரை நேமி வேந்தனை","அட்டிவ னெய்தினா னாழி யாதலான்","மட்டிவ ரலங்கலான் வாசு தேவனென்","றொட்டிய வொளிமுடி யொன்று சூட்டினார்."],"p":"","r":"Because he took the discus after killing the discus-king, they set on him a bright crown and name him Vasudeva.","n":"The title is given by the fact of winning the discus (line 2, 'because it was the discus'), not by birth or an earlier anointing.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","vali"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1499","lb":"SO1499","mm":1,"ev":"The kings crown the discus-winner as Vasudeva.","cl":[["the discus won in battle is the sign of the title Vasudeva","general","asserted",2,"narrator"]],"ag":["விஞ்சை வேந்தர்","செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:65"},"so:1500":{"book":"soolamani","num":1499,"t":["பெருகிய மிகுதிறற் பெரிய நம்பியை","மருவிய புகழ்பல தேவ நீண்முடி","கருவிய மரபினாற் கவித்துக் காவலன்","றிருவமர் சேவடி சிலம்ப வாழ்த்தினார்."],"p":"","r":"They crown the great hero in the old manner with a long crown of the gods, and bless him as the protector whose feet ring with anklets.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1500","lb":"SO1500","mm":1,"ev":"The kings and gods crown the great hero in the ancient way and bless him.","cl":[],"ag":["விஞ்சை வேந்தர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:65"},"so:1501":{"book":"soolamani","num":1500,"t":["இருங்கலி விழவினோ டரசி யற்றலும்","பெருங்கலி விழவின தெய்வம் பேணுவ","சுருங்கலில் சுடரொளி துளும்பத் தோன்றல்பால்","அருங்கல மொழிந்தவு மடைந்த வென்பவே."],"p":"","r":"At the great festival of the royal coronation they honour the gods, and the other precious things unsent also reach him.","n":"Line 2 and 4 are not fully clear about whether the treasures come from the gods or from the festival.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["arasu","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1501","lb":"SO1501","mm":1,"ev":"The coronation festival is held and the remaining treasures come to the king.","cl":[],"ag":["விஞ்சை வேந்தர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:65"},"so:1502":{"book":"soolamani","num":1501,"t":["சக்கரந் தண்டுவாள் சங்கு விற்குடை","மிக்கெரி மணியிவை யெய்தி வீரியன்","மக்களு ளரசர்கள் வணங்குந் தெய்வமாத்","தொக்கெரி சுடரொளி துளும்பத் தோன்றினான்."],"p":"","r":"He wins the discus, staff, sword, conch, bow, parasol and fire-jewel, and shines as the god the kings of men bow to.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","vali","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1502","lb":"SO1502","mm":1,"ev":"The king receives the seven royal jewels and shines as a god before kings.","cl":[["the possessor of the discus and the other royal jewels is a god among kings of men","general","asserted",3,"narrator"]],"ag":["செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:65"},"so:1503":{"book":"soolamani","num":1502,"t":["சென்றுயர் வலம்புரி செம்பொற் றாமரை","என்றியல் பெயரின விரண்டு மாநிதி","ஒன்றல மணிகளு மொளிபொன் மாழையு","நின்றிவை சொரிந்தொளி நிழற்று கின்றவே."],"p":"","r":"The two great treasures named Valampuri and the golden lotus pour out gems, bright gold and shining gold ore.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1503","lb":"SO1503","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:65"},"so:1504":{"book":"soolamani","num":1503,"t":["மிக்கெரி சுடர்முடி சூடி வேந்தர்க","டொக்கவ ரடிதொழத் தோன்றுந் தோன்றலால்","அக்கிரப் பெருஞ்சிறப் பெய்தி யாயிடைச்","சக்கரப் பெருஞ்செல்வச் சாலை சார்ந்தவே."],"p":"","r":"Crowned with a blazing crown, with all kings worshipping at his feet, the lord reaches the great hall of the discus's wealth.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1504","lb":"SO1504","mm":1,"ev":"The crowned lord enters the great hall of the discus's riches as kings worship.","cl":[],"ag":["செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:65"},"so:1505":{"book":"soolamani","num":1504,"t":["அருங்கலப் பெருந்தெய்வ மவையுந் தத்தமக்","கொருங்குசெய் வளநக ரடைந்த வொண்சிறை","சுருங்கலில் கருடற்குச் சுடருந் தோன்றலாற்","பெருங்கலி மாளிகை பேணப் பட்டதே."],"p":"","r":"The great divine jewels each go to the city made ready, and the palace is held in honour because of the unwearying Garuda and the shining lord.","n":"Lines 3-4 name Garuda and the lord together but the syntax is ambiguous about who is honoured by whom.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["selvam","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1505","lb":"SO1505","mm":1,"ev":"The divine treasures go to their prepared city and the royal palace is honoured.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:65"},"so:1506":{"book":"soolamani","num":1505,"t":["முரசுவீற் றிருந்ததிர் மூரித் தானையன்","அரசுவீற் றிருந்தனன் பின்னை யாயிடைக்","கரைசெய்நீர்க் கருங்கடல் வேலி காவலற்","குரைசெய்நூற் சரிதைகள் புலவ ரோதினார்."],"p":"","r":"The king of the war-drum and great host sits enthroned, and the poets then recite to this guardian of the sea-bounded land the old chronicles that teach kingship.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","vendhan","kalvi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1506","lb":"SO1506","mm":1,"ev":"Poets recite the chronicles of kingship to the newly enthroned king.","cl":[],"ag":["புலவர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:66"},"so:1507":{"book":"soolamani","num":1506,"t":["ஆதிநா ளரசிய னீதி யாங்கெடுத்","தோதினார் புலவர்க ளோது மாயிடைத்","தீதிலார் திகிரியஞ் செல்வர் செய்கைமேற்","கோதிலாத் திறல்சில கூறப் பட்டவே."],"p":"","r":"The poets set out the ancient code of royal conduct, and a few faultless deeds of the discus-lords are told on it.","n":"திகிரியஞ் செல்வர், lords of the discus, are universal emperors; the verse sets the hero's coming feat inside that line of kings.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","aram","kalvi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1507","lb":"SO1507","mm":1,"ev":"Poets recount the old code of kings and some deeds of the discus-lords.","cl":[["the way a king must rule is an ancient norm that learned poets hand down","general","asserted",1,"narrator"]],"ag":["புலவர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:66"},"so:1508":{"book":"soolamani","num":1507,"t":["எழுவகை யருங்கல மிரண்டு மாநிதி","தழுவின சனபத மீரெண் ணாயிரம்","விழவணி நகர்களும் வேந்தர் கூட்டமும்","எழுவின முரைப்பினிவ் வெண்ண வென்பவே."],"p":"","r":"The poets list what a discus-lord has: seven rare jewels, two great treasures, sixteen thousand lands, festive cities and assemblies of kings.","n":"Line 4 is hard (எழுவின முரைப்பின் ... எண்ண என்பவே); the sense that these are the things to be counted is held loosely.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["selvam","arasu","perumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1508","lb":"SO1508","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:66"},"so:1509":{"book":"soolamani","num":1508,"t":["வன்றிறன் மலிபல தேவர் தம்மொடு","சென்றவர் செற்றலர்ச் செகுத்துப் பின்னரே","குன்றமொன் றெடுத்தலுங் கொணர்ந்து கூறினார்","பொன்றலில் புராணநூற் புலவ ரென்பவே."],"p":"","r":"The old-story poets tell that such a lord went with many mighty gods, killed his foes, and then lifted a mountain.","n":"The mountain-lift is first told as the deed of earlier lords; the hero repeats it in the following verses.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vali","perumai","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1509","lb":"SO1509","mm":1,"ev":"Poets recall an earlier lord who slew foes with the gods and lifted a mountain.","cl":[],"ag":["புலவர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:66"},"so:1510":{"book":"soolamani","num":1509,"t":["ஆங்கவர் மொழிதலு மருங்க லக்குழாம்","ஈங்கிவை யென்னினு முன்ன மெய்தினார்","வாங்குநீர் மணலினும் பலர்கொ லோவென","வீங்கிய செருக்கிலன் வீர னாயினான்."],"p":"","r":"Hearing this, the hero, without swelling pride, thinks that those who came before were as many as the sands of flowing water.","n":"He answers the list of earlier feats with a thought of their number, not of his own power; line 2 is hard, so the sense is held loosely.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["panivu","aanmai","perumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1510","lb":"SO1510","mm":1,"ev":"The hero hears the old feats and is not puffed up.","cl":[],"ag":["செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:66"},"so:1511":{"book":"soolamani","num":1510,"t":["அறிபவ ரவையவை மொழியக் கேட்டலும்","நெறிபடு நிதியமே நிறைந்த சிந்தையன்","எறிகடற் படையினோ டெழுந்து சென்றரோ","குறுகினன் கோடிமா சிலைவன் குன்றமே."],"p":"","r":"On the word of those who know, the hero, his mind full of fitting wealth, rises with his sea of an army and reaches the hill Kotisilai.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["padai","vendhan","muyarchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1511","lb":"SO1511","mm":1,"ev":"The hero marches with his army to the Kotisilai mountain.","cl":[],"ag":["செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:66"},"so:1512":{"book":"soolamani","num":1511,"t":["எரிமணிக் கடகக்கை யிரண்டு மூன்றியப்","பெருமணி நிலம்பில மாகக் கீழ்நுழைத்","தருமணி நெடுவரை யதனை யேந்தினான்","திருமணி நெடுமுடிச் செல்வ னென்பவே."],"p":"","r":"The lord of the shining crown thrusts both bracelet-bright arms down beneath the great gem mountain, making the earth a pit, and lifts it.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vali","aanmai","perumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1512","lb":"SO1512","mm":1,"ev":"The hero thrusts his arms under Kotisilai and lifts it.","cl":[],"ag":["செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:66"},"so:1513":{"book":"soolamani","num":1512,"t":["கைந்நிலம் புகநுழைந் தெடுப்பக் கல்லென","மைந்நில நெடுவரை மறிய மற்றதன்","செந்நில முழைமுகஞ் சிலம்புஞ் சீரினால்","அந்நிலம் வாய்திறந் தழைப்ப தொத்ததே."],"p":"","r":"As he thrusts in his hand and lifts, the dark mountain rolls with a crash, and the red hollow it leaves rings as if the ground opened its mouth and called.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vali","perumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1513","lb":"SO1513","mm":1,"ev":"The mountain tilts as he lifts it and the hollow echoes.","cl":[],"ag":["செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:66"},"so:1514":{"book":"soolamani","num":1513,"t":["பிலங்களு ளுறைவன பெரிய நாகத்தின்","புலங்கெழு தாட்சிய புச்சந் தாழ்வன","அலங்கலா னெடுத்திட வகழ்ந்தெ ழுந்தவவ்","விலங்கலின் விழுகதிர் வேர்க ளொத்தவே."],"p":"","r":"The tails of great snakes that lived in the hollows hang down as the garlanded one lifts, like bright roots of the torn-up hill.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["perumai","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1514","lb":"SO1514","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:66"},"so:1515":{"book":"soolamani","num":1514,"t":["குழுவிய குவளையங் குண்டு மாச்சுனை","ஒழுகிய வருவிநீ ருக்கு வீழ்வன","கழுமிய நிலம்விட வெடுப்பக் கார்வரை","அழுவதன் கண்ணுணீ ரழிவ தொத்ததே."],"p":"","r":"Water from the blue-lily pools and streams falls from the lifted mountain, as if the dark hill wept at leaving the land.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1515","lb":"SO1515","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:66"},"so:1516":{"book":"soolamani","num":1515,"t":["தழுவிய தடவரைத் தாழ்வர் வாயெலாங்","குழுமிய கொழுமுகில் வழுவி வீழ்வன","செழுவரை செறியமுன் னுடுத்த செந்துகில்","அழிவன வருகுவந் தசைந்த தொத்தவே."],"p":"","r":"Clouds slip from the broad slopes and fall, like the red cloth the mountain wore sliding off as it moves.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","kurippu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1516","lb":"SO1516","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:66"},"so:1517":{"book":"soolamani","num":1516,"t":["ஒன்றுதன் செறிகுறங் கூன்றிக் கைத்தலம்","ஒன்றினா னொளிவரை யுயர வேந்துபு","நின்றன னெடியவ னீல மாமணிக்","குன்றமோர் குன்றங்கொண் டெழுந்த தொப்பவே."],"p":"","r":"He sets one thigh firm and holds the bright mountain high in one hand, a blue-gem hill carrying another hill.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vali","perumai","aanmai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1517","lb":"SO1517","mm":1,"ev":"The hero stands holding the mountain aloft in one hand.","cl":[],"ag":["செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:66"},"so:1518":{"book":"soolamani","num":1517,"t":["பொருமாலை வேலரசர் போற்றிசைப்பப் பூவின்","அருமா மழைபெய் தமருலக மார்ப்பக்","கருமா னெடுவரையோர் கைத்தலத்தி னேந்தித்","திருமா மணிவண்ணன் செம்மாந்து நின்றான்."],"p":"","r":"While spear-kings praise him and flowers rain and the world of the gods cheers, the lord of gem-dark hue stands proud, the black mountain in one hand.","n":"He is named by his hue and the Tirumal-like epithet திருமா மணிவண்ணன்; the verse gives no proper name.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pugazh","perumai","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1518","lb":"SO1518","mm":1,"ev":"Kings praise and the gods cheer while the hero holds the mountain.","cl":[],"ag":["செங்கண் ணெடியான்","மன்னர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:66"},"so:1519":{"book":"soolamani","num":1518,"t":["அடிமேல் பூங்கழல்க ளம்பொன் னிலங்கு","முடிமேல் சூளா மணிமுளைத்த சோதி","கடிமேல் விரிதாரோன் கைத்தலத்த தன்று","படிமேன் மணியருவி பாரித்த குன்றமே."],"p":"","r":"The verse pictures the hero's anklets and crest-jewel light and the gem-streamed mountain that rests on the hand of the garland-wearer.","n":"Line 3 turns on கைத்தலத்தது அன்று, and the sense of the negative is not fixed; the reading is a cautious one.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["perumai","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1519","lb":"SO1519","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:66"},"so:1520":{"book":"soolamani","num":1519,"t":["வரையெடுத்த மாணிக்க நீள்கடகக் கையால்","யெடுப்பான் போனிமிர்ந்து நோக்காது நிற்ப","விரையெடுத்த பூந்தார் விறல்வேந்த ரஞ்சிப்","புரையெடுத்த மாமகரப் பொன்முடிகள் சாய்த்தார்."],"p":"","r":"He lifts the mountain with his ruby-bright arm and stands, not even looking up as if he merely lifted it, and the garlanded kings, afraid, bow their gold crowns.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vali","panivu","acham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1520","lb":"SO1520","mm":1,"ev":"Frightened kings bow their crowns to the hero as he stands holding the mountain.","cl":[],"ag":["செங்கண் ணெடியான்","மன்னர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:66"},"so:1521":{"book":"soolamani","num":1520,"t":["கோடிக் குன்றங் கோடியல் போலுங் குவவுத்தோள்","கோடிக் குன்றங் கொண்டது மீட்டே கொளநாட்டிக்","கோடிக் குன்றம் போந்தனெ நின்ற கொலைவேழங்","கோடிக் குன்ற மன்னவ னேறிக் குளிர்வித்தான்."],"p":"","r":"The verse says the hero returns the great hill and that the king like the hill mounts the fierce elephant and calms it.","n":"The verse turns on a yamaka of கோடிக் குன்றம் and the sense of the last line is uncertain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vali","aanmai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1521","lb":"SO1521","mm":1,"ev":"The hero puts the mountain back and mounts and calms a killer elephant.","cl":[],"ag":["செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:66"},"so:1522":{"book":"soolamani","num":1521,"t":["தாமரை தங்குந் தண்புனல் வேலித் தடநீந்தித்","தாமரை தங்குந் தண்புன னன்னாட் டகமெய்தித்","தாமரை தங்குந் தண்சுட ரொண்பொற் கலைநல்லார்","தாமரை தங்குந் தண்புகழ் பாடத் தகைபெற்றார்."],"p":"","r":"After crossing the lotus-pooled moat and reaching the good watered land, fair women sing the cool fame of the hero and are honoured.","n":"The verse is a யமகம் on தாமரை தங்கும் தண்புனல்; who crosses and who is honoured is not fixed.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pugazh","naadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1522","lb":"SO1522","mm":1,"ev":"Women of the land sing the hero's fame as he reaches it.","cl":[],"ag":["மெல்லியலார்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:66"},"so:1523":{"book":"soolamani","num":1522,"t":["மாலைத் தண்கேழ் மாமதி போலும் வளர்சோதி","மாலைத் தண்கேழ் மாமணி முத்தக் குடைநீழன்","மாலைத் தண்கேழ் வண்புன னாடார் மகிழ்வெய்து","மாலைத் தண்கேழ் மால்கடல் வட்டம் வளாயிற்றே."],"p":"","r":"Under his pearl parasol, bright as a moon, the people of the watered land rejoice, and the sea-ringed world comes under it.","n":"The verse is a yamaka on மாலைத் தண்கேழ்; the last clause is read loosely.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["arasu","vendhan","perumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1523","lb":"SO1523","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:66"},"so:1524":{"book":"soolamani","num":1523,"t":["மையார் சென்னி மாளிகை முன்றின் மலர்மேயும்","மையார் பொய்கைத் தண்புன னாடன் வரலோடும்","மையார் கண்ணி னாம்பய மெல்லா மடவாரிம்","மையா நின்றே யெய்தின மென்றே மகிழ்வுற்றார்."],"p":"","r":"When the lord of the pond-rich land reaches the palace yard, the women of dark eyes are glad at his coming.","n":"The yamaka on மையார் makes the exact words of their joy unclear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1524","lb":"SO1524","mm":1,"ev":"The women of the mansions rejoice as the lord arrives.","cl":[],"ag":["மெல்லியலார்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:66"},"so:1525":{"book":"soolamani","num":1524,"t":["ஆம்பன் னாணுஞ் செந்துவர் வாயா ரமிழ்தூறி","ஆம்பன் னாணுந் தேமொழி நல்லா ரலர்தூவி","ஆம்பன் னாணும் விட்டன ரார்வக் களிகூர","ஆம்பன் னாணும் பல்புக ழானந் நகர்புக்கான்."],"p":"","r":"Red-lipped women shower flowers and, in their joy, lay aside their modesty as the famed king enters the city.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naanam","kaamam","pugazh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1525","lb":"SO1525","mm":1,"ev":"He enters the city while women throw flowers and drop their shyness.","cl":[],"ag":["மெல்லியலார்","செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:66"},"so:1526":{"book":"soolamani","num":1525,"t":["தாமரை நாறுந் தண்பணை யெல்லா மகிழ் நாறச்","சாமரை வீசத் தாழ்குழை செம்பொன் சுடர்வீசத்","தேமரை யாளுஞ் சேயிழை யாளுந் திருமாலும்","பூமரை வேலிப் போதன மென்னுந் நகர்புக்கார்."],"p":"","r":"With whisks waving and gold ear-rings flashing, the hero and the two women with him enter the flower-fenced city of Pothanam.","n":"The hero is named by the epithet திருமால், and the two women are known only by epithets, the lotus-lady and the bright-jewelled one.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","pugazh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1526","lb":"SO1526","mm":1,"ev":"The hero and his two consorts enter the city of Pothanam.","cl":[],"ag":["செங்கண் ணெடியான்","சேயிழையாளும்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:66"},"so:1527":{"book":"soolamani","num":1526,"t":["சூழிணர்மென் மல்லிகையும் வளையமுமின் சூட்டுமெழி றுதையச் சூட்டி","யாழகவி மணிவண்டு மணிஞிமிறு மதுகரமு மிசைப்பச் செய்ய","காழகிலு நறுஞ்சாந்துங் கடிவாசப் பூம்பொடியுங் கமழ்ந்து கைபோய்","ஏழுலகு மணங்கொடுப்ப வெழினகரா ரெதிர்கொள்ள விறைவன் புக்கான்."],"p":"","r":"Bees hum, incense and sandal scent the street, and the townsfolk welcome the lord as he enters decked in jasmine and ring-crest.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","vendhan","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1527","lb":"SO1527","mm":1,"ev":"The townsfolk receive the lord as he enters the city.","cl":[],"ag":["நகரார்","செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:66"},"so:1529":{"book":"soolamani","num":1527,"t":["கோபுரமுங் கழிந்துகுளிர் நகரைவலங் கொடுவீதி குடையோன் செல்ல","நூபுரமு மேகலையுங் கலந்தொலிப்ப நுண்மருங்கு னுடங்க வோடி","மாபுரத்து மாளிகைதம் மணிக்கதவந் தாழ்திறந்து மனத்தின் றாழும்","வேய்புரையு மென்பணைத்தோண் மெல்லியலார் மெல்லவே திறந்தா ரன்றே."],"p":"","r":"Passing the gate and circling the cool city, the umbrella-lord goes down the street, and the slender women run out, clashing their anklets, and open their jewelled doors and the bolts of their minds as well.","n":"தாழ் is bolt: the women open the door-bolt and, the verse says, the bolt of the mind; the epic makes desire the thing that opens their reserve.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","nirai","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1529","lb":"SO1529","mm":1,"ev":"The women of the mansions open their doors as he passes in procession.","cl":[],"ag":["செங்கண் ணெடியான்","மெல்லியலார்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:66"},"so:1530":{"book":"soolamani","num":1528,"t":["போர் மேக மன்னதிறற் பொருகளிற்று மேலரசர் போற்றிக் கூவச்","சீர்மேக மெனச்செறிக ணிடி முரசங் கடிததிர்ந்து திசைமே லார்ப்ப","நீர்மேக முத்தினெடுந் தண்குடைக்கீழ் நிழற்றுளும்பு நேமி தாங்குங்","கார்மேக வண்ணனிவன் வருவானைக் காண்மினோ கண்க ளார."],"p":"","r":"Kings on war-elephants hail him, drums sound like storm-cloud, and a voice bids the onlookers see with full eyes the cloud-hued bearer of the discus who comes under the pearl parasol.","n":"The last line is a speech bidding the onlookers to look; who speaks it is not named, so narrator is a placeholder.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pugazh","arasu","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1530","lb":"SO1530","mm":1,"ev":"Kings hail him and drums sound as he approaches in procession.","cl":[],"ag":["செங்கண் ணெடியான்","மன்னர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:66"},"so:1531":{"book":"soolamani","num":1529,"t":["செந்தாஅ மரைபுரையுஞ் செழுங்கண்ணுந் தடக்கையும் பவள வாயும்","அந்தாஅ மரைநாறு மடியிணையு மவையவையே காண்மின் காண்மின்","நந்தாஅ மரைநாட னகையிலங்கு மணியார நவின்ற மார்பம்","பைந்தாஅ மரை மடந்தை பாராட்டப் பொலிந்திலங்கும் படியுங் காண்மின்."],"p":"","r":"A voice calls the onlookers to look at the bride: her lotus-red eyes, long hands, coral mouth, lotus-scented feet and the jewelled chest, shining as the lotus lady praises her.","n":"The speaker is not named; the reading of the lotus (தாமரை) as Lakshmi in line 4 is read from பாராட்ட and the lotus-maiden, and may be only the poet's image.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1531","lb":"SO1531","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["காண்மின்","அருகு நின்றவர்"],"ch":"so:67"},"so:1532":{"book":"soolamani","num":1530,"t":["உரற்கால முறச்செவிய வோங்கெருத்தி னோடைமால் யானைமே லொளிசூழ் மாலை","நிரற்கால மணிநிரைத்த நெடுங்குடைக்கீழ் முடிநிழற்ற நெடுமால் பின்னே","சரற்கால சந்திரனோர் தடவரைமேல் வெண்முகிற்கீழ்த் தயங்கி யாங்கே","யரக்காம்பல் வாயினிரிவ் வருநனலர் தாரா ன்மற் றவன்சீர் காண்மின்."],"p":"","r":"The same voice shows the groom's glory: on a rutting elephant under a long jewelled umbrella, he shines like an autumn moon on a mountain beneath white cloud, with a red-lotus mouth and a garland.","n":"Line 4 is garbled in the edition (வருநனலர் தாரா ன்மற்), so the last clause is held loosely.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1532","lb":"SO1532","mm":1,"ev":"A speaker presents the groom, riding an elephant under the royal umbrella, to the onlookers.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["காண்மின்","அருகு நின்றவர்"],"ch":"so:67"},"so:1533":{"book":"soolamani","num":1531,"t":["சேதாம்பல் வீழ்ந்தனைய செவ்வாயுஞ் செங்குவளை திளைத்த கண்ணு","மீதார்ந்த வெண்ணிலாச் சுடரொளியும் வெள்ளிக்குன் றனைய தோளும்","போதார்ந்த கருங்குஞ்சி மணிதொடர்ந்தாற் போற்புறந்தாழ்ந் திருண்ட வாறுங்","காதார்ந்த குழைதாழக் கதிருமிழ்ந்த திருமுகத்தின் கதிர்ப்புங் காண்மின்."],"p":"","r":"The voice goes on to praise the groom's body: red lips, lily-dark eyes, a moon-bright light, shoulders like a silver hill, dark hair falling to his back and the glow of his face under swinging earrings.","n":"That this is the groom and not the bride rests on the masculine shoulders and the sequence with 1532; no name is given.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1533","lb":"SO1533","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["காண்மின்","அருகு நின்றவர்"],"ch":"so:67"},"so:1534":{"book":"soolamani","num":1532,"t":["வேய்காயு மென்பணைத் தோள் வெண்செந் தனமெழுகி முத்தந் தாங்கி","ஏகாய மிட்ட வெண்டுகிலின் மகளி ருழைநின் றேத்த","ஆகாய மியல்கின்ற வருமணிநல் ளிமானத்தி னகத்தாள் போலு","மாகாய வரையாளு மன்னர்கோன் மடமகளை வம்மின் காண்பாம்."],"p":"","r":"The speaker says: let us go and see the king's young daughter, who sits among white-clad women praising her, like one inside a sky-travelling jewelled palace.","n":"ளிமானம் in line 3 is an edition slip for விமானம்.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1534","lb":"SO1534","mm":1,"ev":"The speaker invites the crowd to go and see the king's daughter on her sky-palace.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["காண்மின்","அருகு நின்றவர்"],"ch":"so:67"},"so:1535":{"book":"soolamani","num":1533,"t":["அருமணி நீள்விமானத்தி னாகாயப் பளிங்கியன்ற விளிம்பி னாலுந்","திருமணியி னொளிமேனி நிழலெறிப்பத் திக்ழ்ந்திலங்கு தெய்வப் பாவை","கருமணியின் கதிர்குழற்றிக் கடைசுருட்டிக் கைசெய்து வளர்த்த போலும்","புரிமணிபொண் குழறிகழப் பொன்னணைமே லினிதிருந்த பொலிவு காண்மின்."],"p":"","r":"In the jewelled palace with a crystal rim, the bride's own light shines back, and the poet says she is like a divine doll with hair dressed by hand and gem-set tresses, sitting on a golden couch.","n":"திக்ழ்ந்து in line 2 is a misprint for திகழ்ந்து; the doll-image is the poet's own and it makes her seem made, not born, which the verse does not say outright.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1535","lb":"SO1535","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:67"},"so:1536":{"book":"soolamani","num":1534,"t":["மாநீல மிடைபதித்து வெண்பளிங்கிற் செவ்வரத்த விரேகை வாங்கிப்","பானீர வேல்வடிவு படத்திருத்தித் தாமரையுட் பாரித் தன்ன","தூநீல வாணெடுங்கண் குழைமுகத்தி னுள்ளி லங்கத் தோன்றுகின்ற","வாய்நீல மணியைம்பா லிவ்வணங்கு வரையணங்கோ வன்றோ காண்மின்."],"p":"","r":"The speaker asks whether this is a mountain goddess, seeing the blue-gem eyes in her face like sapphires set in crystal and red lines drawn in a lotus.","n":"","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1536","lb":"SO1536","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["காண்மின்","அருகு நின்றவர்"],"ch":"so:67"},"so:1537":{"book":"soolamani","num":1535,"t":["எழுதாது மையொளிரு மிருமருங்கு மெறித்திடையே செங்கே ழோடித்","தொழுதார்க்கு வரங்கொடுக்குந் தடங்கண்ணி துணைமுலையின் வளாகஞ் சூழ","விழுதாய குங்குமத்தா லிலதையையுங் கொழுந்தினையு மிழைத்தார் பின்னு","முழுதார முத்தணிந்தார் நுண்மருங்கு லுளதாக முயன்றா ரல்லர்."],"p":"","r":"Her eye-paint is not applied, kumkum lines and creeper-patterns are drawn on her breasts, and pearl strings are put on her; those who adorned her did not seem to try to make her waist exist at all.","n":"The last line plays on her waist being so slender that the adorners made no effort over it; the sense is held loosely.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1537","lb":"SO1537","mm":1,"ev":"Her attendants adorn the bride with kumkum designs and pearls.","cl":[],"ag":["மைந்தர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:67"},"so:1538":{"book":"soolamani","num":1536,"t":["போதாவி யேகமழ்ந்து புரைவட்டம் பொன்னிழையாற் பொலிந்து தோன்றி","ஊதாவி யானுடங்கு மொள்ளரத்த நுண்கலிங்க மொன்று சேர்த்தி","மீதாடி வில்லுமிழு மிடைமணியொண் கலாபங்கண் மிளிர வீக்கி","யாதானு மிவரடக்க மிவளல்கு லகலாமை யறிந்து செய்தார்."],"p":"","r":"They tie around her a fragrant, gold-edged red cloth fine enough to move with a breath, and gem girdles over it, knowing her hips never leave her.","n":"The sense of line 4 (they know her hips never part from the cloth) is taken from the verb செய்தார் with அறிந்து and is not certain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1538","lb":"SO1538","mm":1,"ev":"Attendants dress the bride in a red cloth and gem girdles.","cl":[],"ag":["மைந்தர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:67"},"so:1539":{"book":"soolamani","num":1537,"t":["கந்தாரங் கொளவீக்கிக் கடிவிரிந்து பூம்பாளை கமழுங் காலை","நந்தாஅ வனத்திளையா ரெழுவியாழ் நரம்பினுக்கு நலஞ்சா லின்சொன்","மந்தார மலர்கமழு மணியைம்பான் மைமதர்த்த மழைக்கண் மாதர்","செந்தாஅ மரையடியின் செவ்வியுமற் றிதுவாயிற் றெய்வ மேயாம்."],"p":"","r":"When the flower-spathe season spreads, the lute-voiced young women's sweet words and the mandara-scented bride's lotus-red feet make the speaker say this is surely a goddess.","n":"Line 1 (கந்தாரம் கொள வீக்கி) is obscure; it may mean a lute tuned in the gandhara mode, which would give the scene its music.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1539","lb":"SO1539","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:67"},"so:1540":{"book":"soolamani","num":1538,"t":["இன்னன பலவுங் காட்டி யிளையவர் காணும் போழ்தின்","மன்னவ ரரசர் தன்மேல் மாலையு மலருஞ் சிந்தி","மின்னவிர் சிவிறி தம்மால் விரைபொழி தாரை வீக்கித்","தொன்னக ரார்வ மென்னுங் களித்தொழி றொடங்கிற் றன்றே."],"p":"","r":"After showing many such sights, the young people watching them join the kings in throwing garlands and flowers on the king and spraying him with scented water, and the old city's joy begins.","n":"Whether the one showered (அரசர் தன்மேல்) is the groom or the old king is not fixed by the words.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["arasu","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1540","lb":"SO1540","mm":1,"ev":"The kings and young people shower the king with garlands, flowers and scented water as the city festival opens.","cl":[],"ag":["அரசர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:67"},"so:1541":{"book":"soolamani","num":1539,"t":["செம்பொன்செய் பொடியி னாலுஞ் சிவிறியின் றாரை யாலும்","அம்பொன்செய் மலரி னாலு மகிற்புகை யாவி யாலும்","பைம்பொன்செய் பதாகை யாலும் பரந்திருள் பட்ட வீதி","கம்பஞ்செய் யானை யானைக் கண்விளக் குறுத்த வன்றே."],"p":"","r":"Gold dust, scented sprays, golden flowers, incense smoke and golden banners darken the streets, so that the elephant-bearing king's elephant lights the way by its eyes.","n":"கம்பஞ்செய் யானையானை (line 4) is read as a shaking war-elephant and its rider; the lamp-eyed image is the poet's.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["arasu","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1541","lb":"SO1541","mm":1,"ev":"The festival's smoke and decoration darken the streets until only the elephant's eyes seem to give light.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:67"},"so:1542":{"book":"soolamani","num":1540,"t":["மன்னிய புகழி னான்மேல் வாங்குவிற் புருவ மாக","மின்னவிர் பகழி கண்ணாப் புரிசைவெண் மாட மாகத்","துன்னிய சால வாயிற் றுளைகளே துளைக ளாகக்","கன்னிய ரெய்து தத்தங் கடிநகர் காவல் கொண்டார்."],"p":"","r":"Maidens guard their own bright houses, with their brows as bows, glances as arrows, the white upper halls as ramparts and window-slits as the openings.","n":"The poem turns houses into forts and glances into arrows to say that the king cannot pass unwounded; the verse does not say who passes, which is taken from the sequence.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1542","lb":"SO1542","mm":1,"ev":"Maidens at the windows shoot glances at the passing king.","cl":[],"ag":["கன்னியர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:67"},"so:1543":{"book":"soolamani","num":1541,"t":["ஆடுவா ரணங்கு கொள்வா ரார்வஞ்செய் கருவி வீக்கிப்","பாடுவார் கண்டு கூறிப் பரவுவார் பணிந்து முன்னாற்","கூடுவார் கொற்றங் கொள்ளக் கூறுவா ராகி யெங்கும்","ஊடுபோக் கரிய தாக வொளிநக ருழையர் சூழ்ந்தார்."],"p":"","r":"Dancers, women armed with instruments, singers, praisers, and bowing men gather; the palace attendants surround the court in great numbers, so crowded that none can pass.","n":"கொற்றம் கொள்ளக் கூறுவார் (line 3) means those who pronounce blessings for victory.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1543","lb":"SO1543","mm":1,"ev":"Entertainers and attendants crowd round the king in the palace city.","cl":[],"ag":["ஆடுவார்","மைந்தர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:67"},"so:1544":{"book":"soolamani","num":1542,"t":["அங்குலாங் கொடியி னாலு மகிற்புகை யாலு மெங்கு","மங்குலாய் விசும்பு மூட மழுங்கிய சுடர னாகி","இங்குலா விளங்க மாட்டே னினியென வெண்ணி வெய்யோன்","கொங்குலாங் குளிர்கொள் சோலைக் குடவரைக் குவடு சேர்ந்தான்."],"p":"","r":"Flags and incense smoke fill the sky like cloud and dim the sun, which thinks it cannot shine here any more and sets on the western hill.","n":"The sun's withdrawal is an image of the festival outshining it; it is not claimed as an omen.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1544","lb":"SO1544","mm":1,"ev":"The sun goes down, dimmed by the smoke of the festival.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:67"},"so:1545":{"book":"soolamani","num":1543,"t":["மணிவரை யரசன் மற்றை வாழ்நகர்க் கோயில் புக்கான்","பணிவரை யுழைய ராகிப் பயாபதி பக்க நின்றார்","தணிவரை யிலாத செய்கைத் தத்தமக் கியன்ற கோயில்","அணிவரை யனைய திண்டோ ளருக்கனோ டரசர் சேர்ந்தார்."],"p":"","r":"The mountain-gem king enters his palace in the city, Payapati stands with his attendants, and the other kings go to their own palaces with the strong-armed Arukkan.","n":"The 'mountain-gem king' of line 1 and the Arukkan of line 4 are not named by standard titles; identifying them with Tivittan and Arukkakirthi is the sequence's best guess.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["arasu","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1545","lb":"SO1545","mm":1,"ev":"The kings withdraw to their palaces at nightfall.","cl":[],"ag":["பயாபதி","அரசர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:67"},"so:1546":{"book":"soolamani","num":1544,"t":["மஞ்சுடை மாடக் கோயில் வளைவண்ன் புக்க பின்னைச்","செஞ்சுடர் மகரப் பூணான் றிருவெதிர் கொள்ளச் சென்று","விஞ்சையம் பாவை மேய விடைமணி விமானஞ் சேர்ந்தான்","எஞ்சலில் செல்வந் தன்னா லிந்திர னிரட்டி யுள்ளான்."],"p":"","r":"After the conch-white one enters his own tall palace, the red-bright, shark-ornamented prince goes with Fortune to meet him and enters the jewelled room of the Vidyadhara maiden, richer than Indra.","n":"வளைவண்ணன் (conch-coloured) is an epithet read as Vijayan, the white elder brother; செஞ்சுடர் மகரப் பூணான் is taken as Tivittan. Both are guesses from epithets.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["arasu","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1546","lb":"SO1546","mm":1,"ev":"The prince enters the Vidyadhara bride's room.","cl":[],"ag":["செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:67"},"so:1547":{"book":"soolamani","num":1545,"t":["மாலையாங் கடைந்த போழ்தின் மங்கலத் தேவிப் பட்டங்","காலையா மணிவ தென்று கண்ணதிர் முரசிற் சாற்றிப்","பாலையாழ் மழலை யாளைக் காப்பணி பயின்ற செல்வம்","வேலைசூ ழுலக மெல்லாம் விம்முற விளைந்த தன்றே."],"p":"","r":"At evening a drum proclaims that the auspicious queen's coronation will be at dawn, and the world's joy grows over the sea-bound earth as the lute-voiced one is made ready.","n":"The 'lute-voiced' woman (பாலையாழ் மழலையாள்) is the bride only by sequence.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","selvam","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1547","lb":"SO1547","mm":1,"ev":"Heralds beat a drum announcing the bride's coronation as queen for the next morning.","cl":[],"ag":["அறைந்தனர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:67"},"so:1548":{"book":"soolamani","num":1546,"t":["அங்கொளி விளக்கி னாலு மணிகலச் சுடரி னாலுந்","திங்களை யனைய செல்வி திருநுத லொளியி னாலு","மங்கல மரபிற் றல்லா மயங்கிருண் மறைந்து போகக்","கங்குலு மெல்ல மெல்லக் கையகன் றிட்ட தன்றே."],"p":"","r":"Lamps, jewelled vessels and the bride's moon-like forehead chase the dark of the wedding night away, and the night slowly lets go.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1548","lb":"SO1548","mm":1,"ev":"The night passes in the glow of lamps and the bride's face.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:67"},"so:1549":{"book":"soolamani","num":1547,"t":["காரிரு ளகன்ற போழ்திற் கமலினி யென்னுஞ் செல்விக்","கோருரு ளாழி வெய்யோ னருளிய வுதயஞ் சேர்ந்தான்","ஆரிரு ளனைய கூந்தற் கருளிய மனத்த னாகிப்","பேரரு ளாழி யானும் பெயர்ந்துபொன் மாடஞ் சேர்ந்தான்."],"p":"","r":"As the dark clears, the sun, grantor of the rising to the lotus Kamalini, arrives, and the discus-bearing prince goes with a kind heart to the golden palace of his dark-haired love.","n":"கமலினி is read as the lotus-bed that the sun favours and as a pun on the bride, not as a name; the prince as 'discus-bearer' is by epithet.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1549","lb":"SO1549","mm":1,"ev":"The sun rises and the prince goes to the bride's golden palace.","cl":[],"ag":["செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:67"},"so:1550":{"book":"soolamani","num":1548,"t":["விஞ்சைய ருலகு மண்ணும் விண்ணுமொன் றாயதே போற்","செஞ்சுடர் மணியும் பொன்னு மாலையும் விரையுஞ் சேர்த்தி","அஞ்சுடர் வயிரப் பைம்பூ ணலைகடல் வண்ணன் றன்னாற்","பஞ்சுடை யல்குல் பாக வரசொடு பட்டங் கொண்டாள்."],"p":"","r":"Gem, gold, garlands and perfume are gathered as the Vidyadhara world and the earth and the sky seem one, and the sea-hued prince has her crowned queen with him.","n":"","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["arasu","selvam","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1550","lb":"SO1550","mm":1,"ev":"The sea-coloured prince crowns his bride queen beside him in a rich ceremony.","cl":[],"ag":["செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:67"},"so:1551":{"book":"soolamani","num":1549,"t":["தேவிதன் றாதைக் கேற்ற பெருஞ்சிறப் பியற்றிச் செல்வன்","வேய்விரி வெள்ளிக் குன்றின் விஞ்சைய ருலக மெல்லாம்","ஓவில புகழி னானுக் குடன்கொடுத் துரிமை யோடும்","பூவிரி யுருவத் தாரான் பின்சென்று விடுத்துப் போந்தான்."],"p":"","r":"He honours the queen's father with a great gift, hands him the whole Vidyadhara realm on the silver mountain with its rights, and walks behind to send him off.","n":"The gift is a restoration of rule over Vidyadharas: the verse treats it as honour, not as a conquest being consolidated.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["arasu","eegai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1551","lb":"SO1551","mm":1,"ev":"The prince gives his father-in-law the Vidyadhara kingdom and sees him off.","cl":[],"ag":["செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:67"},"so:1552":{"book":"soolamani","num":1550,"t":["தெவ்வரங் கின்மை யாலுந் திசையினில் வணக்கற் பால","வவ்வழி யின்மை யாலு மருமணி வண்ண னாங்கு","மௌவலங் குழலியாலு மணிநில மடந்தை யாலுஞ்","செவ்வலர்த் திருவினாலுஞ் செருக்கிய களிய னானான்."],"p":"","r":"With no enemy left, no direction left to subdue, a wife, the earth and Fortune, the gem-hued prince becomes intoxicated with joy.","n":"செருக்கிய களியன் (an elated, intoxicated one) uses a word that elsewhere can blame pride; here the poet does not.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","arasu","perumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1552","lb":"SO1552","mm":1,"ev":"The prince, with no rival left, is filled with the pleasure of his fortune.","cl":[],"ag":["செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:67"},"so:1553":{"book":"soolamani","num":1551,"t":["தேவர்க டிசைமுகங் காப்பா மாநிதி","ஓவல விரண்டுநின் றொருங்கு வீழ்தர","மேவிய வருங்கலம் விளங்க நோக்கிய","காவலன் செல்வநீர்க் கடலுண் மூழ்கினான்."],"p":"","r":"Two great treasures, guardians of the quarters, fall together at his feet, and the king, seeing the shining jewels, sinks into a sea of wealth.","n":"The 'two treasures' (நிதி இரண்டு) are read as the nidhis of Jain and Hindu tradition; the verse names no number beyond two.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["selvam","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1553","lb":"SO1553","mm":1,"ev":"The two great treasures arrive at the king's feet and he is overwhelmed by wealth.","cl":[],"ag":["செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:67"},"so:1554":{"book":"soolamani","num":1552,"t":["திருவமர் தாமரைச் செம்பொ னாயிதழ்","மருவிய திருவடி வாமன் பொன்னகர்","விரவிய விழவொடு வேள்விக் கொத்தரோ","கருவிய வளநகர் கண்கு ளிர்ந்ததே."],"p":"","r":"In the golden city of Vamana of the lotus feet, a festival and sacrifice fills the fertile city with delight.","n":"வேள்வி (sacrifice) is a Vedic word in a Jain work; whether it means a ritual or only a feast is not shown, and வாமன் is read as the king by epithet.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["arasu","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1554","lb":"SO1554","mm":1,"ev":"The festival and sacrifice celebrate the king's fortune in the city.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:67"},"so:1555":{"book":"soolamani","num":1553,"t":["தேவரு மனிதர் தாமுஞ் செறிகழல் விஞ்சை யாரு","மேவருந் தகைய செல்வம் விருந்துபட் டனக டோற்ற","மாவர சழித்த செங்கண் மணிவண்ணன் மகிழ்ந்த காலைத்","தாவருஞ் செல்வ மொன்று தலைவந்த துரைக்க லுற்றேன்."],"p":"","r":"The poet opens the new sarukkam, saying that after the dark-hued lord had destroyed the great ruler and was glad, he will tell of a further wealth that came to him.","n":"Tivittan is named only by his epithets here (மணிவண்ணன், the gem-hued one); the identification rests on the previous chapters. The first-person உரைக்கலுற்றேன் is the poet's own voice.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1555","lb":"SO1555","mm":1,"ev":"The poet announces that another great fortune came to Tivittan after his victory.","cl":[],"ag":["செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:68"},"so:1556":{"book":"soolamani","num":1554,"t":["பானிலா நிறைவெண் டிங்கள் பனிக்கதிர் பரப்பி யாங்கு","மேனிலா விரியும் வெள்ளி வெண்குடை விசும்பு காப்பக்","கோனுலா வுலக மோம்ப நிறீஇயபின் குவளை வண்ணன்","மானுலா மடக்க ணோக்கின் மகளிர்தம் வலையிற் பட்டான்."],"p":"","r":"After setting the world under a white umbrella like the full moon, the dark-lily-hued lord is caught in the net of deer-eyed women.","n":"The verse runs from rule to snare in one sentence: the umbrella that guards the world is followed at once by a net that holds the ruler.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1556","lb":"SO1556","mm":1,"ev":"Having established his rule, Tivittan falls into the net of women's glances.","cl":[],"ag":["செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:68"},"so:1557":{"book":"soolamani","num":1555,"t":["திருமணி நிழற்றுஞ் செம்பொ னெடுமுடி முகட்டோர் தெய்வக்","குருமணி யுமிழுஞ் சோதி குலவிய வொளிகொள் வட்டம்","புரிமணி யோத வேலிப் புதையிரு ளிரியல் செய்யக்","கருமணி வண்ணன் றானே கதிரவன் றொழிலும் பூண்டான்."],"p":"","r":"The dark-gem-hued lord takes on the sun's work, for the light of the gems in his crown drives off the thick darkness.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1557","lb":"SO1557","mm":1,"ev":"Tivittan's crown-jewels light up the dark as the sun would.","cl":[],"ag":["செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:68"},"so:1558":{"book":"soolamani","num":1556,"t":["தேங்கமழ் தெய்வச் செம்பொற் றாமரை சுரிவெண் சங்கம்","ஈங்கிவை நெதிக ளாக வேழர தனங்க ளெய்தி","ஆங்கமர் செல்வந் தன்னா லற்றைக்கன் றமர்ந்த மாதோ","ஓங்கின னுருவத் தாலும் வில்லெண்ப துயர்ந்த தோளான்."],"p":"","r":"Having gained the lotus, the conch and the other treasures and the seven jewels, the lord stands tall in body, eighty bows in height, and in his wealth.","n":"Only the lotus and the conch are named; the verse does not list the rest of the treasures, and the numbers (nine treasures, seven jewels) are the tradition's, not counted here.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","perumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1558","lb":"SO1558","mm":1,"ev":"Tivittan gains the treasures and jewels of his wealth and stands eighty bows tall.","cl":[],"ag":["செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:68"},"so:1559":{"book":"soolamani","num":1557,"t":["தெய்வங்கள் செப்பி னீரெண் ணாயிரந் திசைநின் றோம்ப","மையறு மன்னரீரெண் ணாயிரர் வணங்க வான்மேல்","னொய்தியல் விஞ்சை வேந்தர் நூற்றொரு பதின்மர் தாழக்","கையமை திகிரி யானைக் காமனே கலவிக் கின்றான்."],"p":"","r":"Sixteen thousand gods guard him from the quarters, sixteen thousand kings and a hundred and ten Vidyadhara kings bow, and the discus-bearer takes his pleasure like Kama.","n":"நூற்றொரு பதின்மர் is read as one hundred and ten. The verse sets the homage of all three realms beside a lover's ease and passes no judgement on either.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","arasu","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1559","lb":"SO1559","mm":1,"ev":"Gods guard and kings and Vidyadhara kings bow to Tivittan while he takes his pleasure.","cl":[],"ag":["அமரர்","மன்னர்","விஞ்சை வேந்தர்","செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:68"},"so:1560":{"book":"soolamani","num":1558,"t":["மன்னவர் மகளிரீரெண் ணாயிரர் மயிலொ டொப்பார்","அன்னவ ரமிழ்தச் செப்பே ரணிமுலைக் குவடு பாயப்","பின்னிய தாது மல்கப் பில்கிய தேம்பெய் மாரி","துன்னிய சுரும்பொ டேங்கத் துணருடை கின்ற வன்றே."],"p":"","r":"The sixteen thousand kings' daughters, like peahens, stand with the pollen of the flowers and a honey-rain pouring over their breasts as bees hum around them.","n":"Lines 3-4 are hard to construe (பில்கிய தேம்பெய் மாரி, துணருடை கின்ற); the reading holds the picture of women, pollen, honey and bees and no more.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1560","lb":"SO1560","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["மன்னவர் மகளிர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:68"},"so:1561":{"book":"soolamani","num":1559,"t":["அன்னண மியலு நாளு ளக்கிரத் தேவி தங்கோன்","பொன்னணி யுலகின் வந்த பூவிரி பாரி சாத","மன்னிய லரும்பு வைப்ப மற்றத னோடு சேர்த்திக்","கன்னிய காம வல்லிக் கடிவினை காண லுற்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"One day the chief queen, seeing the parijata flower of the golden world put out buds, resolves to see the wedding of the tree and the young creeper called Kama.","n":"The verse treats a rite of marrying two plants as a courtly occasion; the tree is said to have come from a heavenly world (பொன்னணி உலகின்).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","ilvaazhkkai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1561","lb":"SO1561","mm":1,"ev":"The chief queen decides to see the parijata tree wed to the Kamavalli creeper.","cl":[],"ag":["தேவி"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:68"},"so:1562":{"book":"soolamani","num":1560,"t":["சுரும்பிவர் சோலை வேலித் துணர்விரி பாரி சாதம்","அரும்பிய பருவச் செல்வ மடிகளுக் கறிவி யென்று","பெரும்பிணா வொருத்தி தன்னைப் பெய்வளை விடுத்த லோடும்","விரும்பினள் சென்று வேந்தற் கிறைஞ்சிவிண் ணப்பஞ் செய்தாள்."],"p":"","r":"The queen sends a bangled maid to tell the lord of the parijata's budding, and the maid bows to the king and makes the petition.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","ilvaazhkkai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1562","lb":"SO1562","mm":1,"ev":"The chief queen sends a maid to inform the king, and the maid petitions him.","cl":[],"ag":["தேவி","பிணா"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:68"},"so:1563":{"book":"soolamani","num":1561,"t":["அடிகண்முன் னடித்தி யாரா லங்கைநீர் குளிர வூட்டி","வடிவுகொ டளிர்கண் முற்றி மகனென வளர்க்கப் பட்ட","கடிகமழ் பாரி சாத மதனோடொர் காம வல்லிக்","கொடிமணம் புணர்க்க லுற்ற குறிப்பறி நீசென் றென்றார்."],"p":"","r":"The message says that the parijata, raised like a son by the women with water from their palms, is to be wed to the Kamavalli creeper, and the king is asked to understand the wish.","n":"The tree is spoken of as a son reared by the attendants, so the wedding is a family occasion; the next verse has the king repeat the word 'son'. The plural என்றார் may mean the queen's words are quoted by the maid.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["makkal","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1563","lb":"SO1563","mm":1,"ev":"The maid delivers the queen's request that the reared parijata be wed to the creeper.","cl":[],"ag":["பிணா"],"ec":[],"spk":["பிணா","செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ch":"so:68"},"so:1564":{"book":"soolamani","num":1562,"t":["என்றவண் மொழிந்த போழ்தி னிலங்கொளி முறுவ றோற்றி","நன்றது பெரிதி யாமு நங்கைதன் மகனைக் காண்டும்","என்றவ னருளக் கேட்டே யிளையவள் பெயர்ந்து போக","மின்றவழ் வேலி னாற்கு விதூடக னுழைய னானான்."],"p":"","r":"The king smiles and says that he will see the lady's son, at which the maid goes and the jester becomes the king's companion.","n":"'The lady's son' is the parijata tree, so the king takes up the queen's way of speaking.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ilvaazhkkai","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1564","lb":"SO1564","mm":1,"ev":"The king agrees to see the plant 'son', and the jester takes his place at the king's side.","cl":[],"ag":["செங்கண் ணெடியான்","பிணா","விதூடகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["செங்கண் ணெடியான்","பிணா"],"ch":"so:68"},"so:1565":{"book":"soolamani","num":1563,"t":["காதுபெய் குழையுஞ் செம்பொற் சுருளையுங் கலந்து மின்னப்","போதலர் குஞ்சி யாங்கோர் பூந்துணர் வடத்தின் வீக்கி","ஓதிய மருங்கு றன்மே லொருகைவைத் தொருகை தன்னால்","மீதியல் வடகம் பற்றி வெண்ணகை நக்கு நின்றான்."],"p":"","r":"A man stands laughing with ear-rings, a golden curl and a garland knotted into his hair, one hand on his waist and the other holding his garment.","n":"He is not named until 1566 (அந்தணன்) and 1569 (மாதவன்); the identification follows 1564, which ends on the jester's arrival.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vesham","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1565","lb":"SO1565","mm":1,"ev":"The jester takes his place in comic dress and laughs.","cl":[],"ag":["விதூடகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:68"},"so:1566":{"book":"soolamani","num":1564,"t":["மூடிய புகழி னாற்கு முகிழ்நகை பயந்து காட்டுங்","கோடிய நிலையின் முன்னாற் குஞ்சித்த வடிவ னாகிப்","பாடிய சாதிப் பாடல் பாணியோ டிலயங் கொள்ள","ஆடிய லெடுத்துக் கொண்டாங் கந்தணனாடு கின்றான்."],"p":"","r":"Bent low before the king of spotless fame, the brahmin dances to the rhythm of a song.","n":"He is a brahmin, but a comic one; the poem gives him no Vedic learning in the verses that follow.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["andhanar","vesham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1566","lb":"SO1566","mm":1,"ev":"The brahmin jester dances in front of the king to entertain him.","cl":[],"ag":["விதூடகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:68"},"so:1567":{"book":"soolamani","num":1565,"t":["பாடு பாணியி லயம்பல தோற்றி","ஆடி யாடிய சதித்தொழில் செய்ய","நாடி நாடிநனி நன்றென நக்கான்","நீடு நீடுமுடி யானெடி யானே."],"p":"","r":"As the jester dances again and again with many beats, the long-crowned king laughs and calls it good.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inbam","andhanar"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1567","lb":"SO1567","mm":1,"ev":"The king laughs and praises the jester's dance.","cl":[],"ag":["விதூடகன்","செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:68"},"so:1568":{"book":"soolamani","num":1566,"t":["காது கொண்டன கனபொற் குழைசோர","மீது கொண்ட வடகம் புடைசூழ","ஊதி யூதிமு ழுகும்வயி றென்னாப்","பூதி மீதுபு ரளாநரல் கின்றான்."],"p":"","r":"With his ear-rings slipping and his garment bunched about him, the jester puffs his belly out, rolls in the dust and groans.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["undi","vesham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1568","lb":"SO1568","mm":1,"ev":"The jester puffs out his belly, rolls in the dust and groans.","cl":[],"ag":["விதூடகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:68"},"so:1569":{"book":"soolamani","num":1567,"t":["மாத வன்வயி றுபற்றி நரன்றாற்","கேத மென்னையென வேந்தல் வினாவ","ஊதி யூதிவயி றுள்ளள வெல்லா","மோத கங்கண்மு ழுகும்பல வென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"Holding his belly, the man named Madhavan answers the king's question about his trouble by saying that it is full of many modakas, puffed up and swallowed.","n":"மாதவன் elsewhere means a great ascetic; for the jester it is either his name or an irony, and the reading cannot settle which.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["undi","avaa"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1569","lb":"SO1569","mm":1,"ev":"Asked what ails him, the jester says his belly is full of modakas.","cl":[],"ag":["விதூடகன்","செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ec":[],"spk":["விதூடகன்","செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ch":"so:68"},"so:1570":{"book":"soolamani","num":1568,"t":["என்று தின்றனைபன் மோதக மென்ன","என்று தின்றனவு மல்ல வினிப்போய்ச்","சென்று தேவிகடி காவின் விழாவில்","நின்று தின்னலுறு கின்றன வென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"Asked when he ate so many modakas, he says he has eaten none; they are to be eaten at the festival in the queen's fragrant grove.","n":"The joke turns on the time: his groaning is for a feast he has not yet eaten.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["undi","avaa"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1570","lb":"SO1570","mm":1,"ev":"The jester says the sweets are still to come, so that his belly is swollen with greed only.","cl":[],"ag":["விதூடகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["விதூடகன்","செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ch":"so:68"},"so:1571":{"book":"soolamani","num":1569,"t":["மாதவன் மொழிய மன்னவ னக்காங்","கேத மென்னைபெரி தெய்தினை யென்றே","வேத நாவின்விறல் வேதியர்க் கல்லால்","ஈத லில்லையினி யென்செய்தி யென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"The king laughs and asks what the trouble is, then says that no gift is given except to brahmins with Vedic tongues, and asks what he will do.","n":"The king states the rule as a tease to a glutton who has shown no learning; the work does not take it as its own teaching, and the jester answers with a joke, not an argument, so it is `reported`.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["andhanar","eegai","vedham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1571","lb":"SO1571","mm":1,"ev":"The king tells the jester that gifts go only to Veda-learned brahmins.","cl":[["gifts are due only to brahmins learned in the Veda","vedic","reported",3,"tivittan"]],"ag":["செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ec":[],"spk":["செங்கண் ணெடியான்","விதூடகன்"],"ch":"so:68"},"so:1572":{"book":"soolamani","num":1570,"t":["வேதம் வல்லவரை வென்றிடு கிற்கும்","வாதம் வல்லன தனாற்பெறு கிற்பன்","வாதம் வெல்லும் வகையும்மென் மாண்பு","மாதர் பண்டுமறி யும்மற வேலோய்."],"p":"","r":"The jester says that he is skilled in debate, which can defeat the Veda-learned, and that he will get the gift that way; women have long known his merit.","n":"A boast of skill, not a doctrine: the jester sets debate-skill against Vedic learning as a title to the king's gift.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vaadham","andhanar","kalvi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1572","lb":"SO1572","mm":1,"ev":"The jester boasts that he can win the gift by beating the Vedic brahmins in debate.","cl":[],"ag":["விதூடகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["விதூடகன்","செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ch":"so:68"},"so:1573":{"book":"soolamani","num":1571,"t":["வாதம் வெல்லும்வகை யாதது வென்னில்","ஓதி வெல்லலுறு வார்களை யென்கை","கோதில் கொண்டவடி விற்றடி யாலே","மோதி வெல்வனுரை முற்றுற வென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"He explains his way of winning debate: he will beat those who aim to win by recitation, with his stick, until his word prevails.","n":"The cudgel turns the idea of debate into a farce, so nothing of the debate's own arguments appears. The line 3 reading (தடியாலே) is not certain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vaadham","kalvi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1573","lb":"SO1573","mm":1,"ev":"The jester says his method of debate is to beat the reciters with a stick.","cl":[],"ag":["விதூடகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["விதூடகன்","செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ch":"so:68"},"so:1574":{"book":"soolamani","num":1572,"t":["நன்று வாதமிது காண்டு மெனப்போய்ச்","சென்று சோலைமதில் வாயில தெய்தி","ஒன்று காவலுழை யாரொடு கூடிப்","பொன்றி லாதபுக ழான்பொழில் புக்கான்."],"p":"","r":"The king says 'good, let us see this debate', goes to the grove's wall gate with the guards, and enters the garden.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","vaadham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1574","lb":"SO1574","mm":1,"ev":"The king goes with the guards to the grove gate and enters.","cl":[],"ag":["செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ec":[],"spk":["செங்கண் ணெடியான்",""],"ch":"so:68"},"so:1575":{"book":"soolamani","num":1573,"t":["நீடு செம்பொன்முடி யாற் கெதிர் நிந்தா","வேட மேவிய விதூடக னோடி","ஓடி யாடி வருவா னுயர்காவிற்","கூடி வீழ்வன கொழுங்கனி கண்டான்."],"p":"","r":"In the garden the jester in his mock dress runs about before the king and sees rich fruits fallen in heaps.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vesham","undi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1575","lb":"SO1575","mm":1,"ev":"The jester sees ripe fruit lying fallen in the grove.","cl":[],"ag":["விதூடகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:68"},"so:1576":{"book":"soolamani","num":1574,"t":["கண்டு கண்டுதன கண்கனி தம்மேன்","மண்டி மண்டிவர வாயெயி றூறக்","கொண்டு கொண்டுகுவி யாவிவை காணாய்","உண்டு முண்டுமென வோடி யுரைத்தான்."],"p":"","r":"At the sight of the fruit his mouth waters and his teeth run, and he runs to the king and says 'look, let us eat, let us eat'.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["avaa","undi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1576","lb":"SO1576","mm":1,"ev":"The jester runs to the king and urges him to eat the fallen fruit.","cl":[],"ag":["விதூடகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["விதூடகன்","செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ch":"so:68"},"so:1577":{"book":"soolamani","num":1575,"t":["நல்ல வல்லகனி முன்னைய நாமிவ்","வெல்லை செல்லவுறு மென்னலு மாயின்","வல்லை வல்லைவரு வாயென முன்னால்","ஒல்லை யொல்லையொலி பாடி நடந்தான்."],"p":"","r":"A speaker, probably the king, says that if they are to pass this limit of the grove the other should come quickly, and then one of the two goes ahead singing loudly.","n":"Neither the speaker of lines 1-3 nor the one who walks ahead is named; the line 1 wording is obscure and the roles are inferred from 1574-1576.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["avaa","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1577","lb":"SO1577","mm":1,"ev":"One of the pair urges the other to hurry on past the fruit and walks ahead singing.","cl":[],"ag":["விதூடகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["செங்கண் ணெடியான்","விதூடகன்"],"ch":"so:68"},"so:1578":{"book":"soolamani","num":1576,"t":["சந்து மாவொடு தடாயிட மெல்லாங்","கொந்து தேனொடு குலாயிணர் கூடி","வந்து தாழ்ந்து மதுமாரி தயங்கித்","தந்து தாதுபொழி யும்பொழி றானே."],"p":"","r":"The grove is full of sandal, mango and other trees whose bunched flowers bow down and shower honey and pollen like rain.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1578","lb":"SO1578","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:68"},"so:1579":{"book":"soolamani","num":1577,"t":["மாவின் மேல்வளர மாதவி வைத்த","தாவி லாததழை தழைவன நோக்கிக்","காவு காமர்கனி கண்டது கையாறிற்","கூவு மோடியவை கொள்குவ மென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"Seeing the ripe fruit on the grove's mango trees, with the madhavi creeper growing over them, the speaker says they will run and pick them.","n":"The speaker is not named; the greed for fruit fits the jester of the previous chapter, but only the story so far says so.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","avaa"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1579","lb":"SO1579","mm":1,"ev":"The jester sees the ripe fruit and proposes to run and take it.","cl":[],"ag":["விதூடகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["விதூடகன்","செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ch":"so:69"},"so:1580":{"book":"soolamani","num":1578,"t":["கூடி வண்டு குடையுங் குளிர்காவில்","ஓடி மண்டிவரு வானொரு பாலாற்","சேடு கொண்ட கனி சிந்தின கண்டு","மூடு கொண்ட மதியன் முனிவுற்றான்."],"p":"","r":"Where bees swarm in the cool grove, fruit lies spilled on one side, and the dull-minded man who ran up to it grows angry.","n":"மூடு கொண்ட மதியன் is taken as a gibe at the jester; the Tamil does not name him.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["avaa","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1580","lb":"SO1580","mm":1,"ev":"The jester, running to the fallen fruit, finds it spoiled by bees and is angered.","cl":[],"ag":["விதூடகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:69"},"so:1581":{"book":"soolamani","num":1579,"t":["ஏவ லின்றியெரி வெங்கதி ரோணும்","போவ லென்று நினையாப் புனைகோயில்","ஓவ லின்றி யுடையாய் சிறிதேனுங்","காவ லின்றுகடி காவிது வென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"Speaking to the lord, the jester says that this grove, which even the sun does not think to enter, has not the least guard.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aran","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1581","lb":"SO1581","mm":1,"ev":"The jester tells the king the grove is unguarded.","cl":[],"ag":["விதூடகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["விதூடகன்","செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ch":"so:69"},"so:1582":{"book":"soolamani","num":1580,"t":["பொன்னி னாய புரிசைத் தளமேலும்","மன்னு வாளர் மறவோர் பலர்காப்பர்","என்னை காவலிஃ தில்வகை யென்றான்","மின்னு வார்ந்து மிளிருஞ் சுடர் வேலோன்."],"p":"","r":"The spear-bearing king answers that many warriors guard the golden wall, so he asks how the grove can be said to lack guards.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aran","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1582","lb":"SO1582","mm":1,"ev":"The king denies that the grove is unguarded and says warriors keep the wall.","cl":[],"ag":["செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ec":[],"spk":["செங்கண் ணெடியான்","விதூடகன்"],"ch":"so:69"},"so:1583":{"book":"soolamani","num":1581,"t":["அருமுகத் தகனி யாயின வெல்லாம்","ஒருமுகத் தனக ளன்றி யுதிர்த்துத்","தருமுகத் தர்வரு வார்தறு கண்ணார்","கருமுகத் தருளர் காவல்களி லென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"The speaker says that the guards of the rare fruit are the fierce-eyed, black-faced ones who shake the fruit down and come, so the grove is guarded after its own kind.","n":"The black-faced, fierce-eyed guards look like the monkeys that 1584 names; the verse itself does not say so, and the lines are hard to construe.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kalavu","aran"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1583","lb":"SO1583","mm":1,"ev":"The king explains who guards the fruit.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["செங்கண் ணெடியான்","விதூடகன்"],"ch":"so:69"},"so:1584":{"book":"soolamani","num":1582,"t":["யாவர் யாவரவ ரெங்குள ரென்னக்","காவு மேவுமுசு வின்கலை காட்ட","வாவர் கள்வரத னாலெழு நாம்போய்த்","தேவி காவுநனி சேர்குவ மென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"As a langur of the grove shows its tricks, the jester says that all of them are thieves, so they should go on to the queen's grove.","n":"The joke turns on the king's own claim of guards (1582-1583): the guards are the thieves.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kalavu","aran"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1584","lb":"SO1584","mm":1,"ev":"The jester calls the grove's monkeys thieves and proposes that they move to the queen's grove.","cl":[],"ag":["விதூடகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["விதூடகன்","செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ch":"so:69"},"so:1585":{"book":"soolamani","num":1583,"t":["கள்வர் தாம்பல ரெனக்கடல் வண்ணன்","உள்வி ராவுநகை சேருரை கேட்டே","வெள்கி வேந்தனரு கேயிரு பாலும்","பள்கி நோக்குபு பயிர்த்து நடந்தான்."],"p":"","r":"The sea-hued king hears the jester's remark that thieves are many, with a laugh hidden in his own words, and the jester, shrinking, looks to both sides and walks close to him.","n":"Who is ashamed (வெள்கி) is not fixed by the verse; it is read here as the jester.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["acham","natpu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1585","lb":"SO1585","mm":1,"ev":"The king laughs inwardly at the jester's remark, and the frightened jester walks close beside him.","cl":[],"ag":["செங்கண் ணெடியான்","விதூடகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:69"},"so:1586":{"book":"soolamani","num":1584,"t":["தாழ்தளிர் பொளிய தமால வீதிய","ஏழகண் டிருளென வெருள யாவஞ்","சூழிரு ளன்றி \"சால காணென","வீழிணர்க் கண்ணியான் வெருவு நீக்கினான்."],"p":"","r":"In the tamala avenue, where hanging shoots shade the path, the jester takes the shade for darkness and is afraid, and the garlanded king removes his fear.","n":"The quoted words சால காண் are hard to construe; the reading follows the sentence's frame.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["acham","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1586","lb":"SO1586","mm":1,"ev":"The jester takes shade for darkness and trembles; the king calms him.","cl":[],"ag":["விதூடகன்","செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ec":[],"spk":["செங்கண் ணெடியான்","விதூடகன்"],"ch":"so:69"},"so:1587":{"book":"soolamani","num":1585,"t":["வாலிதழ் வீழ்தரு மகிழ்தன் றாண்முதல்","சாலிகை புக்கது தயங்கு தாரினாய்","சோலையு மமர்த்தொழி றொடங்கு மோவென","வேலைநீர் வண்ணனை வெருண்டு நோக்கினான்."],"p":"","r":"The jester, seeing fallen petals at the foot of a bakula tree, asks the sea-hued king in fear whether the grove too will begin a war.","n":"The word சாலிகை is rare; the sense of the question is taken from the reply in 1588, which says there is no war here.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["acham","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1587","lb":"SO1587","mm":1,"ev":"Frightened, the jester asks if fallen petals mean a fight is starting.","cl":[],"ag":["விதூடகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["விதூடகன்","செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ch":"so:69"},"so:1588":{"book":"soolamani","num":1586,"t":["அஞ்சலிங் கமர்த்தொழி லில்லை யாவதும்","மஞ்சிவர் மகிழந்தன் வயவு நோய்கெடூஉப்","பஞ்சிவ ரல்குலார் பவழ வாயினால்","அஞ்சுவை நறவமீங் குமிழ ஆனதே."],"p":"","r":"The king tells him not to fear, since there is no battle here, and says the bakula's longing is cured when women's coral mouths spill the five-taste liquor on it.","n":"The verse gives the bakula a longing (வயவு நோய்), cured by the women's mouth-wine; it treats the tree as a lover, with no claim beyond the poem's own image.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1588","lb":"SO1588","mm":1,"ev":"The king explains that the bakula blooms when women pour liquor on it.","cl":[],"ag":["செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ec":[],"spk":["செங்கண் ணெடியான்","விதூடகன்"],"ch":"so:69"},"so:1589":{"book":"soolamani","num":1587,"t":["ஆங்கத னாவியா லரவத் தேனெழா","ஈங்கிதன் றாண்முத லிருள மொய்த்தன","ஓங்கிய கேள்வியா யுணர்ந்து கொள்கென","வீங்கிய கழலவன் விளங்கச் சொல்லினான்."],"p":"","r":"Calling the jester a man of great learning, the king explains that bees do not rise from the flowers' scent but have swarmed at the foot of the tree.","n":"ஓங்கிய கேள்வியாய் is likely ironic, since the king is teaching the jester.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","kelvi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1589","lb":"SO1589","mm":1,"ev":"The king explains why the bees gather at the tree's foot.","cl":[],"ag":["செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ec":[],"spk":["செங்கண் ணெடியான்","விதூடகன்"],"ch":"so:69"},"so:1590":{"book":"soolamani","num":1588,"t":["முள்ளரை முருக்கினோ டெழுந்த மல்லிகை","வள்ளிதழ் குருதியின் வடிவி லூழ்த்தன","கள்ளவிழ் கண்ணியாய் விரியு நாளெனத்","தெள்ளிதி னவற்றையுந் தெளியச் செப்பினான்."],"p":"","r":"The king also tells him plainly that the jasmine rising among thorny stems has petals shaped like blood and will open on its own day.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","kaalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1590","lb":"SO1590","mm":1,"ev":"The king explains the jasmine to the jester.","cl":[],"ag":["செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ec":[],"spk":["செங்கண் ணெடியான்","விதூடகன்"],"ch":"so:69"},"so:1591":{"book":"soolamani","num":1589,"t":["கடிமிசை விரிதருங் காமர் கொம்பரின்","முடிமிசை யெழுதரு முறிகொ ளீர்ந்தளிர்","அடிமிசை யீன்றதிவ் வசோக மென்கொலோ","கொடிமிசை யெழுதிய குவவுத் தோளினாய்."],"p":"","r":"The jester asks the banner-armed king what the asoka is, which bears fresh shoots at its foot, when other branches carry them at the tip.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","kelvi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1591","lb":"SO1591","mm":1,"ev":"The jester asks about the asoka's shoots at its foot.","cl":[],"ag":["விதூடகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["விதூடகன்","செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ch":"so:69"},"so:1592":{"book":"soolamani","num":1590,"t":["இலைத்தலை யீர்ந்தளி ரல்ல வீங்கிதன்","மலைத்தகு வயவு நோய் தீர வைத்தன","கலைத்தலை மகளிர்தங் காமர் சீறடி","அலத்தகச் சுவடென வறியக் காட்டினான்."],"p":"","r":"The king shows him that these are no leaves but the lac stains of dancing women's feet, laid there to cure the tree's longing.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1592","lb":"SO1592","mm":1,"ev":"The king explains that the red at the foot is women's lac-footprints.","cl":[],"ag":["செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ec":[],"spk":["செங்கண் ணெடியான்","விதூடகன்"],"ch":"so:69"},"so:1593":{"book":"soolamani","num":1591,"t":["காவிவாய் விலங்கிய கருங்கண் வெம்முலைத்","தேவியார் சீறடி சென்னி சேர்த்தலும்","மேவியாங் கலர்ந்திடு நின்னை வென்றதால்","ஆவியா ரசோகின தமைதி வண்ணமே."],"p":"","r":"The jester teases that when the dark-eyed queen puts her small foot to the king's head, he blooms at once, so the asoka's way is his.","n":"The joke reverses the 'longing' of 1588-1592: the lord of sixteen thousand wives is the one cured by his wife's foot.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","penvazhi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1593","lb":"SO1593","mm":1,"ev":"The jester jokes that the king, like the asoka, blooms at the queen's foot.","cl":[],"ag":["விதூடகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["விதூடகன்","செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ch":"so:69"},"so:1594":{"book":"soolamani","num":1592,"t":["மாதவன் மொழிதலு மன்ன னாங்கொரு","போதினாற் புடைத்தனன் புடைத்த லோடுமிங்","கேதிலா ளொருத்திக்கா வென்னைச் செய்தவித்","தீதலொந் தேவிக்குத் தெரியச் செப்புவேன்."],"p":"","r":"After the jester has spoken, the king strikes him with a flower, and the jester says he will tell the queen of the wrong done him for the sake of a stranger.","n":"The grammar of ஏதிலாள் ஒருத்திக்கா is unclear; the sense is read as the jester's complaint that he is struck for the king's own failing.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","kuri"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1594","lb":"SO1594","mm":1,"ev":"The king strikes the jester with a flower, and the jester threatens to tell the queen.","cl":[],"ag":["விதூடகன்","செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ec":[],"spk":["விதூடகன்","செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ch":"so:69"},"so:1595":{"book":"soolamani","num":1593,"t":["என்றலு மெரிமணிக் கடகக் கையினால்","அன்றவன் கைத்தலம் பிடித்தங் கியாவதும்","இன்றிற லினிச் செய்த லில்லெனச் சொலிச்","சென்றொரு மணிச்சிலா வட்ட மேறினான்."],"p":"","r":"The king takes his hand, says he will do nothing like it again, and climbs a gem-set rock platform.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","panivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1595","lb":"SO1595","mm":1,"ev":"The king seizes the jester's hand, promises not to repeat the blow, and goes up on the rock seat.","cl":[],"ag":["செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ec":[],"spk":["செங்கண் ணெடியான்","விதூடகன்"],"ch":"so:69"},"so:1596":{"book":"soolamani","num":1594,"t":["சொரிகதிர் மணிச்சிலா வட்டஞ் சேர்ந்தனன்","அருகுநின் றந்தண னமர்ந்து நோக்கியே","வெருவிய மனத்தினன் விதலை மேனியன்","பெருகிய தலையினன் பெயர்ந்து பின்றினான்."],"p":"","r":"The jester, who sits beside him on the bright stone, looks around, is afraid, and with trembling body and hair on end steps back.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["acham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1596","lb":"SO1596","mm":1,"ev":"The jester, seated on the stone, is seized by fear and draws back.","cl":[],"ag":["விதூடகன்","செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:69"},"so:1597":{"book":"soolamani","num":1595,"t":["யாதுகண் டனையென விதனுள் வாழ்வதோர்","பூதமுண் டதுபுடைத் துண்ணு மாதலால்","ஏதமுண் டிங்கினி யிருப்பின் வல்லையே","போதவென் றந்தணன் புலம்பிக் கூறினான்."],"p":"","r":"Asked what he has seen, the brahmin cries out that a spirit lives in this place which strikes and eats, so they must leave quickly.","n":"The brahmin's words are a report of his fear, not a doctrinal claim; the story soon shows the spirit to be his own shadow.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["acham","pedhaimai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1597","lb":"SO1597","mm":1,"ev":"The jester answers the king's question by claiming that a spirit lives in the stone and eats those who stay.","cl":[],"ag":["விதூடகன்","செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ec":[],"spk":["விதூடகன்","செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ch":"so:69"},"so:1598":{"book":"soolamani","num":1596,"t":["யாதத னுருவென வலர்பொன் னோலையுஞ்","சோதிசூழ் சுடர்மணிக் குழையுந் துன்னிய","காதொடு கண்பிறழ்ந் துளது கைகுறி","தூதிய வயிற்றதென் றுருவ மோதினான்."],"p":"","r":"Asked for its form, the jester describes it: a golden ear-leaf, bright gem earrings, ears and eyes rolling, a hand gesture, and a swollen belly.","n":"Every feature he lists is a feature of a man with ear-ornaments, which sets up the king's answer in 1599.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["acham","pedhaimai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1598","lb":"SO1598","mm":1,"ev":"The jester describes the spirit he thinks he has seen.","cl":[],"ag":["விதூடகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["விதூடகன்","செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ch":"so:69"},"so:1599":{"book":"soolamani","num":1597,"t":["மின்னிழற் பூணவன் மெல்ல நக்கது","நின்னிழற் காணது நிற்க நின்னுரை","என்னிழ லென்னொடு மியங்கி னல்லது","கன்னிழ லுள்புகிற் காண்ட லாகுமோ."],"p":"","r":"With a quiet laugh the bright-jewelled king says that it is not his own shadow but the jester's, so let it stand, since his shadow goes with him and cannot be seen inside rock.","n":"The king's words are cryptic in the first lines; the reading follows the next verse, where the jester is told to see his own deeds in the shadow.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pedhaimai","arivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1599","lb":"SO1599","mm":1,"ev":"The king laughs and begins to show the jester that the spirit is his own shadow.","cl":[],"ag":["செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ec":[],"spk":["செங்கண் ணெடியான்","விதூடகன்"],"ch":"so:69"},"so:1600":{"book":"soolamani","num":1598,"t":["நின்னிழ லாவது தெளிய நின்றொழில்","இந்நிழற் காணென விறைஞ்சி நோக்குபு","தன்னிழ றான்செய்வ செய்யத் தான்றெளிந்","தின்னிழ லிருந்தன னிலங்கு நூலினான்."],"p":"","r":"The jester is told to look at the shadow and see his own acts in it; he bows, sees it doing what he does, understands, and sits quietly in the cool shade.","n":"The jester is called இலங்கு நூலினான், the one with the shining thread or learning: the learned brahmin is cured by looking.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arivu","thelivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1600","lb":"SO1600","mm":1,"ev":"The king makes the jester watch his shadow copy him, and the jester's fear ends.","cl":[],"ag":["விதூடகன்","செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:69"},"so:1601":{"book":"soolamani","num":1599,"t":["திருந்திய மணிநகைத் தேவி யிவ்வழி","வருந்துணைப் பொழுதுமிம் மணிச்சி லாதலம்","பொருந்தின பொழினலங் காண்டு மென்றரோ","இருந்தன ரிருவரு மினிதி னென்பவே."],"p":"","r":"The king says that while the queen is on her way they will sit on this gem stone and look at the beauty of the grove, and the two sit contentedly.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","natpu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1601","lb":"SO1601","mm":1,"ev":"The king proposes that they wait on the stone, and the two sit down.","cl":[],"ag":["செங்கண் ணெடியான்","விதூடகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["செங்கண் ணெடியான்","விதூடகன்"],"ch":"so:69"},"so:1602":{"book":"soolamani","num":1600,"t":["மின்னவிர் விளங்குமணி மேகலை மிழற்றப்","பொன்னவிர் சிலம்பொலி போந்துபுடை சாற்றக்","கன்னியர் நிரந்துபலர் காவலொடு சூழ","வன்னமென வந்தரசி யார்பொழி லடைந்தாள்."],"p":"","r":"With her girdle ringing and her anklets sounding, the queen comes into the grove like a swan, ringed by maidens and guards.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1602","lb":"SO1602","mm":1,"ev":"The chief queen arrives at the grove with her maidens and guards.","cl":[],"ag":["தேவி","கன்னியர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:69"},"so:1603":{"book":"soolamani","num":1601,"t":["மாலையமர் சிந்தையொடு வார்பொழின் மருங்கின்","வேலையமர் கண்ணிவிளை யாடுதல் விரும்பி","மேலையமர் விஞ்சையின் மறைந்துவிரை நாறுஞ்","சோலையமர் தோகையென வேதொழுது நின்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"A woman, hidden by her magic craft in a fragrant grove and longing for play, stands in worship like a peahen.","n":"The woman is not named in the verse; she is read as the Vidyadhara maiden of the next verses (விஞ்சையன் மடந்தை, 1607) and the verse itself does not say whom she worships.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1603","lb":"SO1603","mm":1,"ev":"A woman hidden in the grove by magic stands in worship.","cl":[],"ag":["விஞ்சையன் மடந்தை"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:70"},"so:1604":{"book":"soolamani","num":1602,"t":["மாதவன் மருட்டமழை வண்ணன்மணி வட்டம்","சோதிவிடு சூழ்சுடர் வளாவ வதன்மேலாற்","தாதுபடு போதுதவி சாமென வடுத்த","மீதுபடு பொங்கணையின் மெல்லென விருந்தான்."],"p":"","r":"The rain-hued king sits at ease on a soft couch inside the glow of his gem-circle while Madhavan keeps him amused.","n":"மருட்ட is read as 'amuse, bewilder'; the verse does not say with what Madhavan holds the king.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1604","lb":"SO1604","mm":1,"ev":"Tivittan sits on a soft couch with Madhavan beguiling him.","cl":[],"ag":["செங்கண் ணெடியான்","விதூடகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:70"},"so:1605":{"book":"soolamani","num":1603,"t":["பந்தணையு மெல்விரலி பாடக மொடுக்கி","வந்தணையு மெல்லையுண் மயங்கியொரு மாற்றம்","அந்தணன்வி னாவவமிழ் தூரமொழி கின்றான்","கந்தணைவி லாதகளி யானைபல வல்லான்."],"p":"","r":"As a soft-fingered woman with muffled anklets draws near, the king, thrown off, puts one nectar-sweet question to the brahmin.","n":"The grammar leaves open who asks and who is asked; the next verse gives the question in the king's voice, so he is read as the asker.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","kurippu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1605","lb":"SO1605","mm":1,"ev":"The woman approaches and the bewildered king questions the brahmin.","cl":[],"ag":["விஞ்சையன் மடந்தை"],"ec":[],"spk":["செங்கண் ணெடியான்","விதூடகன்"],"ch":"so:70"},"so:1606":{"book":"soolamani","num":1604,"t":["நிலத்தவள்கொ லன்றிநெடு மால்வரையு ளாள்கொல்","அலத்தக வடிச்சுவ டசோகின்மிசை வைத்தாள்","உலத்தகைய தோளணிகொண் மார்பவுரை யென்ன","வலத்தகைய னாயமணி வண்ணன் மொழிகின்றான்."],"p":"","r":"The king asks whether the one whose red-dyed footprint is under the asoka is of the earth or of the great mountain.","n":"The king asks about her from her footprint alone, before he has seen her; this is what the queen overhears in 1608.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kurippu","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1606","lb":"SO1606","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["செங்கண் ணெடியான்","விதூடகன்"],"ch":"so:70"},"so:1607":{"book":"soolamani","num":1605,"t":["செய்யன செறிந்தன திரண்டவிரல் சால","வையதசை யார்ந்தவடி யின்னழகி னாலே","மெய்யுமறி வன்வினவில் விஞ்சையன் மடந்தை","வையமுடை யாற்குரிய மாதரவ ளென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"The truthful brahmin reads the red, round-fingered, fleshy footprint and says she is a Vidyadhara's daughter, fit for the world's lord.","n":"The speaker is the one questioned in 1605, called அறிவன் here; his reading from the foot is what sets off the queen's anger.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","kurippu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1607","lb":"SO1607","mm":1,"ev":"Madhavan names her a Vidyadhara maiden from her footprint.","cl":[["a woman's rank and kind can be read from the beauty of her footprint","general","reported",2,"vidushakan"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["விதூடகன்","செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ch":"so:70"},"so:1608":{"book":"soolamani","num":1606,"t":["என்றலு மிரண்டுகரு நீலமலர்க் கண்ணுஞ்","சென்றுகடை சேந்துசிறு வாணுதல் வியர்த்தாள்","அன்றரச னாவியுரு கும்படி யனன்று","மின்றவழு மேனியொடு தேவிவெளிப் பட்டாள்."],"p":"","r":"At his word the queen's lily-dark eyes redden, she breaks into sweat, and burning with anger she steps out in front of the king.","n":"The scene turns on the verse before: praise given to a stranger's foot is what she takes as the wrong.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vekuli","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1608","lb":"SO1608","mm":1,"ev":"The queen, who had overheard, steps forward in anger.","cl":[],"ag":["தேவி"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:70"},"so:1609":{"book":"soolamani","num":1607,"t":["தாதிவர் கருங்குழலி தன்னைமுக நோக்கி","மாதவ னடுங்கிவளர் பூம்பொழின் மறைந்தான்","காதலனு மங்குரிய கட்டுரை மறந்திட்","டேதமினி யென்கொல்விளை கின்றதனெ நின்றான்."],"p":"","r":"Madhavan, shaking, slips away into the garden, and the king, forgetting the speech meant to answer her, stands asking what harm will now follow.","n":"The jester who read the footprint is the first to flee; the king is left to face the queen alone.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["acham","ilvaazhkkai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1609","lb":"SO1609","mm":1,"ev":"Madhavan hides in the grove and the king stands at a loss.","cl":[],"ag":["விதூடகன்","செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:70"},"so:1610":{"book":"soolamani","num":1608,"t":["மன்னன்மக ளேமகர வார்குழன் மடந்தாய்","அன்னமனை யாயமிழ்தின் மேலுமமிழ் தொப்பாய்","என்னையிவ ணுற்றதனெ வென்னுமிலை யென்னா","முன்னுபுரு வக்கொடி முரிந்துமுனி வுற்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"The king pleads that he is no wrongdoer, calling her king's daughter and sweeter than nectar, but with her brows knitted she grows angrier.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oodal","anbu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1610","lb":"SO1610","mm":1,"ev":"The king tries to soothe her and she grows more angry.","cl":[],"ag":["தேவி"],"ec":[],"spk":["செங்கண் ணெடியான்","தேவி"],"ch":"so:70"},"so:1611":{"book":"soolamani","num":1609,"t":["ஆங்கவெளா டீங்குவிளை யாடுநனி நீயான்","பூங்கமழு மாடமென தேபுகுவ னென்றாள்","தாங்கல னெழுந்துதகை நீலமணி வண்ணன்","ஒங்குமுடி சீறடியின் மேலொளிர வைத்தான்."],"p":"","r":"The queen says she will go to her own palace and he may play with that woman; the king rises and lays his crown on her small feet.","n":"The great crown that all kings bow to is here laid at his own wife's feet; the bow ends the quarrel's first round and the next verse is his speech while bowing.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oodal","panivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1611","lb":"SO1611","mm":1,"ev":"The queen turns to leave and the king lays his crown at her feet.","cl":[],"ag":["தேவி","செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ec":[],"spk":["தேவி","செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ch":"so:70"},"so:1612":{"book":"soolamani","num":1610,"t":["மற்றநெடு மான்மகர மாமுடி வணங்கக்","கற்றனை யினிப்பெரிது கைதவமு மென்ன","உற்றதொர் பிழைப்புடைய னாய்விடி னுணர்ந்து","முற்றமுறை செய்தருளு மொய்குழலி யென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"As the crowned head bows, the king tells her she has learned much craft, and asks her to hear out any fault of his and give judgement.","n":"கைதவம் is read as the queen's feigned anger, said in jest; the sense is a little uncertain.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oodal","panivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1612","lb":"SO1612","mm":1,"ev":"The king, bowing, asks her to judge whether he is at fault.","cl":[],"ag":["செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ec":[],"spk":["செங்கண் ணெடியான்","தேவி"],"ch":"so:70"},"so:1613":{"book":"soolamani","num":1611,"t":["மன்னனொர் பிழைப்புமிலன் மாதவனை நாடி","இன்னினி யிவண்கொணர்மி னென்னவுழை யோர்கள்","முன்னவன் மறைந்தமுரு கார்பொழிலி னுள்ளே","துன்னுபு தொடர்ந்துதுகில் பற்றுபு கொணர்ந்தார்."],"p":"","r":"She tells her people that the king has done no wrong and orders them to seek Madhavan and bring him; they find him in the grove and drag him back by his cloth.","n":"The speaker of the order is not named; the queen is read as the speaker because the next verse shows her still giving commands.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["oodal","kurippu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1613","lb":"SO1613","mm":1,"ev":"Attendants hunt Madhavan down in the grove and bring him by his garment.","cl":[],"ag":["மைந்தர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["தேவி","மைந்தர்"],"ch":"so:70"},"so:1614":{"book":"soolamani","num":1612,"t":["பேதைமை கலந்துபிறழ் கண்ணினொ டொடுங்கு","மாதவனை நோக்கிமணி வாய்முறுவ றோற்றிக்","கோதைகளில் யாத்திவனை நீர்கொணர்மி னென்றாள்","போதுவிரி தேங்குழலி பூம்பொழி லணைந்தாள்."],"p":"","r":"With a smile on her gem-like mouth she looks at the cowering Madhavan and orders him tied with garlands and brought, then goes to the flowering garden.","n":"Her anger turns into a game here: the penalty falls on the jester, in flowers, and not on her husband.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oodal","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1614","lb":"SO1614","mm":1,"ev":"The queen smiles and has Madhavan bound with garlands.","cl":[],"ag":["விதூடகன்","தேவி"],"ec":[],"spk":["தேவி","மைந்தர்"],"ch":"so:70"},"so:1615":{"book":"soolamani","num":1613,"t":["மன்னவன் மருட்டமணி யாழ்மழலை மாதர்","முன்னிய முகத்துமுறு வற்கதிர் முகிழ்ப்ப","இன்னவருள் பெற்றன னினிப்பெரிது மென்னா","அன்னமனை யாளையணி மார்பினி லணைத்தான்."],"p":"","r":"The king, delighted, folds the soft-voiced queen to his breast, the smile at her mouth shining, and says he has now received her grace.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["punarchi","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1615","lb":"SO1615","mm":1,"ev":"The king embraces the queen in reconciliation.","cl":[],"ag":["செங்கண் ணெடியான்","தேவி"],"ec":[],"spk":["செங்கண் ணெடியான்","தேவி"],"ch":"so:70"},"so:1616":{"book":"soolamani","num":1614,"t":["போதிவ ரலங்கலொடு பூண்முலை ஞெமுங்கக்","காதலன் முயங்குபு கலந்தினி திருந்து","மாதவனு மேதமில னாதலின் மடந்தாய்","தீதுபடு சீற்றமொழி யென்றுதெளி வித்தான்."],"p":"","r":"Pressing her in a close embrace as their garlands crush together, the king persuades her to give up her fault-finding, since Madhavan is faultless.","n":"The king settles the matter by the embrace and by clearing his companion; he does not answer why he asked about the footprint.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["punarchi","oodal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1616","lb":"SO1616","mm":1,"ev":"The king embraces the queen and calms her anger.","cl":[["one who is without fault should not be met with harsh anger","general","reported",3,"tivittan"]],"ag":["செங்கண் ணெடியான்","தேவி"],"ec":[],"spk":["செங்கண் ணெடியான்","தேவி"],"ch":"so:70"},"so:1617":{"book":"soolamani","num":1615,"t":["இட்டதளை தம்மொடிரு தோளுமிடை வீக்கிக்","கட்டிவிடு பூம்பிணையல் கைவிடலு மெய்யுள்","ஒட்டிவிடு காதலொடு வந்துருவு கொண்டு","பட்டபல பாடலினொ டாடல்பல செய்தான்."],"p":"","r":"Freed of the garland bonds from his shoulders, Madhavan puts on a loving manner and sings many songs and dances many dances.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vesham","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1617","lb":"SO1617","mm":1,"ev":"Madhavan, released, sings and dances for the couple.","cl":[],"ag":["விதூடகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:70"},"so:1618":{"book":"soolamani","num":1616,"t":["ஓடு மேமன மோடுமே","கூடு மோதணி கோதையாய்","காடு சேர்கனி காண்டொறு","மோடு மேமன மோடுமே."],"p":"","r":"The jester's first song says that at the sight of fruits in the grove his heart runs, runs, and asks the garland-wearer whether it will gather together.","n":"Each verse is a yamaka song in which the last foot repeats the first; the sound play is the point and the sense is slight. The addressee is feminine (கோதையாய்), so the queen.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["avaa","undi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1618","lb":"SO1618","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["விதூடகன்","தேவி"],"ch":"so:70"},"so:1619":{"book":"soolamani","num":1617,"t":["ஊறு மேயெயி றூறுமே","வீறு சேர்விரி கோதையாய்","சேறு சேர்கனி காண்டொறு","மூறு மேயெயி றூறுமே."],"p":"","r":"The second song says that at the sight of fruits in the mud his teeth water, and calls to the woman of the spreading garland.","n":"A yamaka song again, in which the last foot repeats the first; the fruit lies in mud (சேறு), where the first song had it in the wood (காடு).","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["avaa","undi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1619","lb":"SO1619","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["விதூடகன்","தேவி"],"ch":"so:70"},"so:1620":{"book":"soolamani","num":1618,"t":["வேண்டு மேமனம் வேண்டுமே","பூண்ட பொன்னணி மார்பினாய்","நீண்ட மாங்கனி காண்டொறும்","வேண்டு மேமனம் வேண்டுமே."],"p":"","r":"The third song says that at the sight of long mango fruit his heart wants, wants, and addresses the golden-chested one.","n":"The address here is masculine (மார்பினாய்), so the song turns from the queen to the king.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["avaa","undi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1620","lb":"SO1620","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["விதூடகன்","செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ch":"so:70"},"so:1621":{"book":"soolamani","num":1619,"t":["இன்னன பாடி யாட வீர்ங்கனி பலவுங் கூவி","முன்னவ னார வூட்டி முறுவலோ டமர்ந்த பின்னை","மன்னிய பாரி சாத மணமக னாக நாட்டிக்","கன்னியங் காம வல்லி கடிவினை தொடங்க லுற்றார்."],"p":"","r":"After singing and dancing they call for many sweet fruits, feed the elder one until he is full, then set the parijata as bridegroom and begin the wedding rites of the maiden creeper.","n":"The wedding of the tree and the creeper that the queen resolved on in 1561 is now carried out; the jester's hunger is fed first.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ilvaazhkkai","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1621","lb":"SO1621","mm":1,"ev":"They feed the jester, set the parijata as bridegroom and start the wedding of the Kama creeper.","cl":[],"ag":["விதூடகன்","செங்கண் ணெடியான்","தேவி"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:70"},"so:1622":{"book":"soolamani","num":1620,"t":["திருமணி நிழற்றுஞ் செம்பொற் றிலதமா முடியி னானுங்","குருமணிக் கொம்ப ரன்ன கொழுங்கய னெடுங்கணாளும்","பருமணி பதித்த பைம்பொன் வேதிகைப்பாரி சாதம்","அருமணி யரும்பித் தாழ்ந்த வந்தளிர்ப் பொதும்பர் சார்ந்தார்."],"p":"","r":"The king in his gold crown and the queen with the long eyes enter a bower of gem-flowering shoots, with the parijata on its jewelled altar.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1622","lb":"SO1622","mm":1,"ev":"The king and queen enter the garden bower.","cl":[],"ag":["செங்கண் ணெடியான்","தேவி"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:70"},"so:1623":{"book":"soolamani","num":1621,"t":["வரிவளை வயிரொ டேங்க வாரணி முரச மார்ப்பக்","கருவளர் கனபொற் சோலைக் கறங்கிசை பரந்தபோழ்தில்","திருவள ரலங்கன் மார்பிற் செங்கணான் றேவி மார்கள்","உருவளர் கொம்ப ரன்னா ளருளறிந் துழைய ரானார்."],"p":"","r":"With conches sounding and drums beating through the golden grove, the king's queens, knowing the will of the branch-like one, become her attendants.","n":"Whose will the queens learn (உருவளர் கொம்பரன்னாள்) is read as the chief queen's.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["arasu","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1623","lb":"SO1623","mm":1,"ev":"The other queens come to wait on the chief queen as the drums sound.","cl":[],"ag":["செங்கண் ணெடியான்","தேவியர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:70"},"so:1624":{"book":"soolamani","num":1622,"t":["செங்கய லுருவ வாட்கட் டேவிதன் குறிப்பிற் சேர்ந்த","மங்கையர் வனப்பு நோக்கி மணிவண்ணன் மகிழ்ந்து மற்றப்","பொங்கிய விளமென் கொங்கை மகளிர்தம் புருவ வில்லால்","அங்கய னெடுங்க ணென்னும் பகழியா லழுத்தப் பட்டான்."],"p":"","r":"Seeing the beauty of the women who have joined the queen, the dark-hued king is pleased, and the arrows of their long eyes pierce him, bent with the bows of their brows.","n":"The poet says the king 'was pressed' by the arrows, as in the net of 1556; the pleasure in women's eyes that the queen's anger was about is fully back.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1624","lb":"SO1624","mm":1,"ev":"The women's glances pierce the pleased king.","cl":[],"ag":["செங்கண் ணெடியான்","மங்கையர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:70"},"so:1625":{"book":"soolamani","num":1623,"t":["குடங்கையி னகன்று நீண்டு குவளையின் பிணையல் செற்று","மடங்களி மதர்வைச் செங்கண் மான்பிணை மருட்டி மையாற்","புடங்கலந் திருள்பட் டுள்ளாற் செவ்வரி பரந்த வாட்கண்","இடங்கழி மகளிர் சூழ விந்திர னிருந்த தொத்தான்."],"p":"","r":"Ringed by women whose red-streaked eyes outdo the deer's and press into the dark of the kohl, the king sits like Indra.","n":"The simile gives the king the place of Indra among heavenly women; it is praise of his pleasure, not a judgement of it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1625","lb":"SO1625","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["மங்கையர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:70"},"so:1626":{"book":"soolamani","num":1624,"t":["ஆங்கவ ரோடு மற்ற வணிபொழிற் கரச னாய","பாங்கமை பாரி சாதம் பருவஞ்செய் பொலிவு நோக்கி","ஈங்கிவற் கிசைந்த கோல மினிதினி னியற்று கென்றான்","ஓங்கிய வுருவத் தார்மே லொளிநிலா வுமிழும் பூணான்."],"p":"","r":"Looking at the parijata's season-wrought splendour in the grove, the king in his shining ornaments tells his people to dress the pair in fitting finery.","n":"The command begins the wedding adornments of the tree and creeper whose rites were opened in 1621.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","ilvaazhkkai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1626","lb":"SO1626","mm":1,"ev":"The king orders the plant bridal pair to be decked.","cl":[],"ag":["செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ec":[],"spk":["செங்கண் ணெடியான்","மைந்தர்"],"ch":"so:70"},"so:1627":{"book":"soolamani","num":1625,"t":["எந்திர மிழிந்த தாரை யருவிநீ ரினிதி னாட்டிக்","கந்தனெத் திரண்ட திண்டோட் கனகசா லங்கள் காட்டிப்","பைந்தழைப் பொழிலுக் கெல்லா மரசெனப் பட்டஞ் சேர்த்தி","அந்தளிர்க் கொம்பர் தோறு மணிபல வணிந்தா ரன்றே."],"p":"","r":"The grove is made ready: fountains pour from engines like waterfalls, golden frames stand about, every green grove is given a king's crown, and jewels are hung on each tender-leaved branch.","n":"The verse crowns the groves as kings (line 3), so the garden is made a court before the women of the king's court take it in 1643. The second line is hard to construe: கந்தனெத் may stand for a post-like shape, and the sense of கனகசாலங்கள் (golden frames or halls) is not fixed.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1627","lb":"SO1627","mm":1,"ev":"Unnamed workers dress the garden with fountains, golden frames and jewels for the festival.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:71"},"so:1628":{"book":"soolamani","num":1626,"t":["கன்னியங் காம வல்லிக் கனங்குழை மடந்தை தன்னை","மன்னவன் றேவி மார்கண் மணவினைக் கோலஞ் செய்து","பின்னத னோடு சேர்த்திப் பெருகிய களிய ரானார்","இன்னகைப் புதல்வர் செல்வம் யாவரே யினிதென் னாதார்."],"p":"","r":"The king's queens dress the young creeper Kama in bridal finery, join her to the tree, and grow glad, for who would not call the wealth of sweet-smiling sons sweet?","n":"The tree was called a son raised by the women's hands (1563), so the closing question turns the wedding of two plants into a parent's joy in a child; the verse does not say the joy rests on the child's merit.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ilvaazhkkai","makkal","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1628","lb":"SO1628","mm":1,"ev":"The queens finish the wedding of the parijata and the Kamavalli creeper and rejoice.","cl":[["the wealth of sweet-smiling sons is sweet to everyone","general","asserted",4,"narrator"]],"ag":["மன்னவர் மகளிர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:71"},"so:1629":{"book":"soolamani","num":1627,"t":["மாதரார் மனத்தி னுள்ளும் மணிவண்ண னினைப்பி னுள்ளுங்","காதலுஞ் செறிந்த தாகக் காமனு முழைய னாகப்","போதலர் பருவச் சோலைப் பொழினல நுகரும் போழ்தில்","ஓதநீர் வண்ண னங்கோ ருபாயத்தா லொளிக்க லுற்றான்."],"p":"","r":"While the women enjoy the grove in its flowering season, with love thick in their minds and in the dark-hued lord's, and Kama at his side, the lord hides himself by a stratagem.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","soozhchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1629","lb":"SO1629","mm":1,"ev":"Tivittan hides by a ruse while the queens enjoy the grove.","cl":[],"ag":["மன்னவர் மகளிர்","செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:71"},"so:1630":{"book":"soolamani","num":1628,"t":["பொன்னவிர் குழையி னாரைப் பொழில்விளை யாட லேவி","மன்னவன் மதலை மாட வளநக ரணுகு வான்போற்","றன்னைமெய் மறைத்தோர் விஞ்சை தாழிரு ளெழினி யாகப்","பின்னைமா தவனுந் தானும் பிணையவ ருழைய னானான்."],"p":"","r":"Having kept the women playing in the grove, the king's son hides himself by an art that makes the dark a curtain, and he and Madhavan are then close beside the women.","n":"The verse does not say who sent the women to play; 1631 shows the chief queen sending others, so the hiding and the queen's answer are two halves of one game.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","soozhchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1630","lb":"SO1630","mm":1,"ev":"Using a magic art that makes darkness a screen, Tivittan and Madhavan hide near the women.","cl":[],"ag":["செங்கண் ணெடியான்","விதூடகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:71"},"so:1631":{"book":"soolamani","num":1629,"t":["மன்னவன் மறைந்த தெண்ணி மாபெருந் தேவி மற்றப்","பொன்னவிர் கொடியன் னாரைப் பொழில்விளை யாட லேவக்","கன்னியங் கோலஞ் செய்து கதிர்மணிக் கலங்க டாங்கி","இன்னகை மழலை தோற்றி யிளையவ ரினைய ரானார்."],"p":"","r":"Guessing that the king has hidden, the great queen sends the other golden-vine women to play in the grove, and they dress as girls with bright jewels, sweet smiles and lisping speech.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","soozhchi","ilamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1631","lb":"SO1631","mm":1,"ev":"The chief queen, guessing Tivittan is hidden, sends more women to play and they act like children.","cl":[],"ag":["தேவி","மன்னவர் மகளிர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:71"},"so:1632":{"book":"soolamani","num":1630,"t":["அம்பொன்செய் கலாப வல்கு லந்தழை புனைந்த வஞ்சிக்","கொம்பஞ்சு மருங்கு னோவக் குவிமுலை முறிகொண் டப்பிச்","செம்பொன்செய் சுருளை மின்னச் செவிமிசைத் தளிர்கள் சேர்த்திக்","கம்பஞ்செய் களிற்றி னான்றன் கண்களைக் களிப்பித் திட்டார்."],"p":"","r":"Wearing gold girdles and skirts of leaves, with sprouts bound on the breasts and tender shoots at the ears, the women delight the eyes of the lord of the trembling elephant.","n":"The dress is made of the grove itself, so the women become part of what they play in; the king who is looking is the one who hid in 1629-1630.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1632","lb":"SO1632","mm":1,"ev":"The women dress in leaves and gold to please the king's eyes.","cl":[],"ag":["மன்னவர் மகளிர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:71"},"so:1633":{"book":"soolamani","num":1631,"t":["விரவம்பூந் தளிரும் போது மிடைந்தன மிலைச்சு வாரும்","அரவம்பூஞ் சிலம்பு செய்ய வந்தளிர் முறிகொய் வாரும்","மரவம்பூம் கவரி யேந்தி மணிவண்டு மருங்கு சேர்த்திக்","குரவம்பூங் பாவை கொண்டு குழவியோ லுறுத்து வாரும்."],"p":"","r":"Some set tender leaves and flowers in their hair, some pluck sprouts while their anklets ring, some hold chowries of flowers with bees beside them, and some hush a flower doll like a child.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ilamai","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1633","lb":"SO1633","mm":1,"ev":"Women play at dressing and at nursing flower dolls in the grove.","cl":[],"ag":["மன்னவர் மகளிர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:71"},"so:1634":{"book":"soolamani","num":1632,"t":["பாவையும் விலங்கு சாதிப் படிமமும் பறப்பை தாமுங்","கோவையு முகத்து மாக்கிக் குலவிய விதழ தாக","ஓவியர் புனைந்த போலு மொளிமலர்ப் பிணையன் மாலை","தேவியர் மருளச் செய்து சிகழிகை சேர்த்து வாரும்."],"p":"","r":"Some make dolls, beasts, birds and faces out of flowers, with petals laid as if painters had drawn them, so that the queens marvel, and then bind these into their hair.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalai","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1634","lb":"SO1634","mm":1,"ev":"Women weave flower figures and garlands so fine that the queens marvel.","cl":[],"ag":["மன்னவர் மகளிர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:71"},"so:1635":{"book":"soolamani","num":1633,"t":["சிகரமா யிலங்கு சென்னித் தென்மலைச் சாந்து மூழ்கிப்","பகருமா மணிவண் டோவாப் பணைமுலைப் பாரந் தாங்கித்","தகரவார் குழல்பின் றாழத் தாழ்குழை திருவில் வீச","மகரயா ழெழுவி மன்னன் வண்புகழ் பாடு வாரும்."],"p":"","r":"Bathed in sandal of the southern hill, bees hovering, hair trailing behind and ear-pendants flashing like rainbows, some play makara lutes and sing the king's rich fame.","n":"They sing his fame while he is hidden among them, so the praise is addressed to a man who may be listening.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","pugazh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1635","lb":"SO1635","mm":1,"ev":"Some women play lutes and sing the king's fame.","cl":[],"ag":["மன்னவர் மகளிர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:71"},"so:1636":{"book":"soolamani","num":1634,"t":["அருமலர்த் தழையும் போது மடியுறை யாக வேந்தித்","திருமலர்ப் பாவை யன்ன தேவியைச் செவ்வி காண்பார்","உருமல ரிழைத்த பாவை யொளிமண நயந்து மாதோ","குருமலர்க் கொம்பி னொல்கிக் குரவையின் மயங்கு வாரும்."],"p":"","r":"Some, carrying leaves and flowers in their laps, wait for the lotus-goddess-like queen to be in the mood to be seen; others, longing for the wedding of the flower doll, bend like shoots and sway in the kuravai dance.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ilvaazhkkai","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1636","lb":"SO1636","mm":1,"ev":"Women bring flower gifts to the chief queen and join in the kuravai dance.","cl":[],"ag":["மன்னவர் மகளிர்","தேவி"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:71"},"so:1637":{"book":"soolamani","num":1635,"t":["வட்டிகைப் பலகை தன்மேன் மணிவண்ணன் வடிவு தீட்டி","ஒட்டிய வடிவிற் றம்மை யூடலோ டிருப்பக் கீறித்","திட்டமிட் டுருவ நுண்ணூற் றுகிலிகை தெளிர்ப்ப வாங்கிப்","பட்டமுங் குழையுந் தோடும் பையவே கனிவிப் பாரும்."],"p":"","r":"Some paint the gem-hued lord on a board and draw themselves beside him in a lovers' sulk, drawing sure lines with a fine brush and softening his crown and ear-rings.","n":"The sulk is painted, not spoken: the women set the quarrel of love in a picture for a man who is hiding nearby.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","kalai","oodal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1637","lb":"SO1637","mm":1,"ev":"Women paint Tivittan's portrait and themselves sulking at him.","cl":[],"ag":["மன்னவர் மகளிர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:71"},"so:1638":{"book":"soolamani","num":1636,"t":["மாம்பொழின் மருங்கு சூழ்ந்த மணிச்சிலா தலத்து மேலாற்","காம்பழி பணைமென் றோண்மேற் கருங்குழ றுவண்டு வீழப்","பூம்பொழில் விளங்கத் தோன்றும் பொன்னிதழ் மறிந்து நோக்கித்","தேம்பொழி செய்யு ளின்பஞ் செவிமுதற் சேர்த்து வாரும்."],"p":"","r":"Some sit on a jewelled stone slab by the mango grove, hair spilling over their bamboo-like shoulders, and look back at the sweet-dripping verse they gather to the ear.","n":"This is the one verse of the run that makes poetry the play; the sense of the last two lines turns on reading செய்யுள் as the verse they hear or recite.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalai","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1638","lb":"SO1638","mm":1,"ev":"Women sit on a stone slab and enjoy verse.","cl":[],"ag":["மன்னவர் மகளிர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:71"},"so:1639":{"book":"soolamani","num":1637,"t":["கோதையுங் குழைவின் பட்டின் கொய்சகத் தலையுந் தாழ","மாதர்வண் டொருங்கு பேர மழையிடை நுடங்கு மின்போற்","போதலர் பொதும்பிற் றாழ்ந்த பொன்னெழி லூச றன்மேல்","ஓதநீர் வண்ணற் பாடி நூழிலூ ழியங்கு வாரும்."],"p":"","r":"Some, garlands and hair-ends hanging and veils swaying, sing of the sea-hued lord as they swing to and fro on a golden swing in the bower, like lightning in a cloud.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inbam","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1639","lb":"SO1639","mm":1,"ev":"Women sing of Tivittan while swinging.","cl":[],"ag":["மன்னவர் மகளிர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:71"},"so:1640":{"book":"soolamani","num":1638,"t":["கள்ளுமிழ்ந் துயிர்க்குஞ் சோலைக் கனமடற் குமரி வாழை","உள்ளெழு சுருளை வாங்கி யொளியுகிர் நுதியி னூன்றிப்","புள்ளெழு தடமும் போர்மான் றொழுதியு மிதுன மாய","ஒள்ளெழி லுருவுங் கிள்ளி யுழையவர்க் கருளு வாரும்."],"p":"","r":"Some pull the inner curled leaf from a young plantain and scratch with bright nail-tips a lake with birds, herds of fighting deer and loving pairs, then give them to the women beside them.","n":"The pairs (மிதுனம்) scratched into the leaf echo the portrait of the lovers' sulk in 1637: the women picture couples while waiting for the king.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalai","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1640","lb":"SO1640","mm":1,"ev":"Women scratch pictures into plantain leaves and pass them to each other.","cl":[],"ag":["மன்னவர் மகளிர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:71"},"so:1641":{"book":"soolamani","num":1639,"t":["மயிலுடை யாடல் கண்டு மகிழ்ந்துமெய்ம் மயங்கி நிற்பார்","குயிலொடு மாறு கொள்வார் குழைமுகஞ் சுடரக் கோட்டிக்","கயிலொடு குழல்பின் றாழக் கண்டுநீர் கொண்மி னென்றாங்","கயிலுடைப் பகழி வாட்க ணங்கையின் மறைத்து நிற்பார்."],"p":"","r":"Some, delighted by the peacock's dance, sway with it; some rival the koel; some, with their hair behind, hide their arrow-keen eyes behind a hand as they say something to be taken up.","n":"The third line is hard to place: whether கண்டு நீர் கொண்மின் is a call to look or to take is not settled by the Tamil.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1641","lb":"SO1641","mm":1,"ev":"Women imitate the peacock and the koel, and some cover their eyes with a hand.","cl":[],"ag":["மன்னவர் மகளிர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:71"},"so:1642":{"book":"soolamani","num":1640,"t":["செழுமலர்த் தாது கொய்து மெல்விரல் சிவந்த வென்பார்","விழுமலர்த் துகள்வந் தூன்ற மெல்லடி மெலிந்த வென்பார்","கொழுமலர்ப் பிணைய றாங்கிக் கொடியிடை யொசிந்த வென்பார்","எழுமலர்த் தனைய தோளான் றேவிய ரினைய ரானார்."],"p":"","r":"Some say their fingers reddened gathering pollen, others that dust of petals hurt their soft feet, others that a flower garland bent their slender waists, and so the queens of the flower-shouldered king became.","n":"The women claim delicacy as a boast, the way a lover praises it; the king who is meant to hear is hidden in the grove.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["melivu","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1642","lb":"SO1642","mm":1,"ev":"The queens make playful complaints of their delicacy.","cl":[],"ag":["மன்னவர் மகளிர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:71"},"so:1643":{"book":"soolamani","num":1641,"t":["கொடிமருங் குறாமே கொடியாய் நுடங்க","வடிநெடுங் கண்ணோக்க மணிவண்டா யோட","அடிமலருங் கைத்தலமு மந்தளிராய்த் தோன்றக்","கடிநறும்பூஞ் சோலையைக் காரிகையார் வென்றார்."],"p":"","r":"The women's waists sway like creepers without touching them, their long glances run like jewel-bees, their feet and hands show like tender shoots, and so they conquer the scented flower-grove.","n":"1627 crowned the groves as kings; here the women take the crown by being more garden than the garden.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1643","lb":"SO1643","mm":1,"ev":"The women's beauty outdoes the grove.","cl":[],"ag":["மன்னவர் மகளிர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:71"},"so:1644":{"book":"soolamani","num":1642,"t":["மணங்கமழும் பூமேனி வாசங் கமழ","வணங்கி வருஞ்சோலை யலர்நாற்ற மெய்திக்","கணங்குழையீர் யாமுமக்குக் கைமாறி லேமென்","றிணங்கிண ரும்போது மெதிரேந்தித் தாழ்ந்த."],"p":"","r":"The flowers of the grove, bowing as they catch the women's body-fragrance, say they have no way to repay them and gather in clusters, bending with offerings.","n":"A speech given to the grove, not to a person; the plants admit a debt and not a claim.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","panivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1644","lb":"SO1644","mm":1,"ev":"The grove's flowers bow to the women and offer themselves.","cl":[],"ag":["சோலை"],"ec":[],"spk":["சோலை","மன்னவர் மகளிர்"],"ch":"so:71"},"so:1645":{"book":"soolamani","num":1643,"t":["அந்தா ரசோக மசோக மவர்க்கீந்த","செந்தார்த் திலகந் திலகமாய்ச் சேர்ந்தன","வந்தார்க்கு மாவாது மென்பனபோன் மாதழைந்த","கொந்தார்பூஞ் சோலைக் குலகறிவோ கூடின்றே."],"p":"","r":"The ashoka gives the women an ashoka, the red tilaka tree gives them tilaka, as if saying that they would serve those who came; the meaning of the last line, about the grove's knowing, is unclear.","n":"The verse plays on the tree names as gifts: the ashoka is given as an ashoka and the tilaka as a forehead mark.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","eegai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1645","lb":"SO1645","mm":1,"ev":"The ashoka and tilaka trees give the women their own sprigs.","cl":[],"ag":["சோலை"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:71"},"so:1646":{"book":"soolamani","num":1644,"t":["வெள்ளித் திரண்மேற் பசும்பொன் மடற்பொதிந்","தள்ளுறு தேங்கனிய தாம்பொற் றிரளசைந்து","புள்ளுறு பொன்வாழைக் கானம் புடையணிந்த","தெள்ளு மணியருவிச் செய்குன்றஞ் சேர்ந்தார்."],"p":"","r":"Silver-white boulders wrapped in fresh gold spathes, swaying golden coconuts and bird-filled gold plantains frame the artificial hill with clear gem waterfalls, which the women reach.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1646","lb":"SO1646","mm":1,"ev":"The women reach the artificial hill.","cl":[],"ag":["மன்னவர் மகளிர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:71"},"so:1647":{"book":"soolamani","num":1645,"t":["கஞ்சுகி மாந்தருங் காவல் முதியாரும்","மஞ்சிவர் சாரல் மணியறையும் வார்பொதும்பும்","துஞ்சு மழைதவழுஞ் சோலைகளுஞ் சோதித்துச்","செஞ்சொ லவர்போய்த் திசைகாவல் கொண்டாரே."],"p":"","r":"The chamberlains and old guards search the jewelled caves, long thickets and cloud-sleeping groves, and the sweet-spoken ones then take the watch on every side.","n":"The verse says nothing of finding anyone; the king has hidden himself by art (1630), so the search and the guard are around the only man the women expect.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","soozhchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1647","lb":"SO1647","mm":1,"ev":"Chamberlains and guards search the grove and then guard its approaches.","cl":[],"ag":["கஞ்சுகி மாந்தர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:71"},"so:1648":{"book":"soolamani","num":1646,"t":["தோகை மடமஞ்ஞை சோலைப் பரப்பின்போன்","மாக மழைவண்ணன் காதன் மடந்தையர்கள்","ஆக மணி சூழ்சார றைவிரும் பொழில்வாய்ப்","போகமணி புரளக் கலைபுலம்பப் புக்கார்."],"p":"","r":"Like peahens spreading through the grove, the dark-cloud-hued lord's beloved women enter the hill-slope grove, jewels rolling and girdles sounding.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1648","lb":"SO1648","mm":1,"ev":"The women enter the grove on the hill-slope, ornaments sounding.","cl":[],"ag":["மன்னவர் மகளிர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:71"},"so:1649":{"book":"soolamani","num":1647,"t":["அரையிலங்கு மேகலை யார்ப்பி னயல","வரையிலங்கு மேகலை மாறேநின் றார்க்கும்","புரையிலங்கு பொற்சிலம்பு தான்சிலம்பும் போழ்தில்","நிரையிலங்கு பொற்சிலம்பு நேரே சிலம்பும்."],"p":"","r":"As the girdles at their waists ring, the hill's own girdle rings back, and as their golden anklets sound, a row of golden anklets answers exactly.","n":"The answering 'girdle' belongs to the hill (வரை மேகலை); only an echo fits the verse, and 1650 confirms it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1649","lb":"SO1649","mm":1,"ev":"The women's girdles and anklets are answered by an echo.","cl":[],"ag":["மன்னவர் மகளிர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:71"},"so:1650":{"book":"soolamani","num":1648,"t":["கொங்குண் குழலார் குழலோர் மணிமழலை","தங்கினவை கொண்டு தானுமெதிர் மிழற்றும்","அங்கணவர் செய்வசெய் தசதியா டின்றே","செங்க ணெடியான் கடிகாவிற் செய்குன்றே."],"p":"","r":"The hill takes the women's jewelled lisping, as it was uttered, and answers it back, mimicking what they do and mocking them, in the fragrant grove of the red-eyed tall one.","n":"The hill acts as a speaker of its own; the red-eyed tall one is the king of the opening (verse 2). The word அசதியாடின்று (made fun of) is read here as mockery in play.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1650","lb":"SO1650","mm":1,"ev":"The artificial hill echoes and mocks the women's talk.","cl":[],"ag":["செய்குன்று"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:71"},"so:1651":{"book":"soolamani","num":1649,"t":["மருவி மழைதவழு மையோங்கு சாரல்","அருவி கொழித்த வருமணிகள் வாரித்","தெருவுபடத் திருத்திச் சீலம் புனைவார்","உருவ நகரிழைப்பா ரொண்ணுதலா ரானார்."],"p":"","r":"The women of the court clear the gems that the stream has washed down the misty slope and, laying out streets, build a fine toy city.","n":"The sense of சீலம் புனைவார் in line 3 is not fixed; read here as the women giving the play-city its fitting adornment.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","kalai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1651","lb":"SO1651","mm":1,"ev":"The women build a toy city from the gems the stream brought down.","cl":[],"ag":["மன்னவர் மகளிர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:72"},"so:1652":{"book":"soolamani","num":1650,"t":["மரகத வீர்ங்கதிரை வார்புற் றளிரென்","றுரைதரு காரிகையா ரூன்றி மிதித்துத்","திரைதவழச் சீறடிக ணோவ நடந்து","விரைதரு பூம்படைமேன் மெல்ல வசைந்தார்."],"p":"","r":"Women call the emerald's cool rays tender grass, step on them gingerly, and, their small feet aching, sink softly onto a bed of flowers.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","melivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1652","lb":"SO1652","mm":1,"ev":"The women walk on gem-light as on grass, and rest on a flower bed.","cl":[],"ag":["மன்னவர் மகளிர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:72"},"so:1653":{"book":"soolamani","num":1651,"t":["வெம்பரிய தண்சாரல் வேரூரி யக்கொழுந்து","தம்பருவச் சோலை தழைத்த தகைநோக்கி","எம்பெருமான் போலு மெழில விவையென்று","வம்புருவந் தோன்ற மணியருவி யாடுவார்."],"p":"","r":"Seeing the fresh-leaved grove and saying it is like their lord, the women bathe in the gem waterfall with a fresh glow of love in their looks.","n":"Line 3 makes the grove's beauty a likeness of the king; the women's love shapes how they see the place.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1653","lb":"SO1653","mm":1,"ev":"The women praise the grove as like their lord and bathe in the waterfall.","cl":[],"ag":["மன்னவர் மகளிர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:72"},"so:1654":{"book":"soolamani","num":1652,"t":["செங்களிதோய்ந் துள்சிவந்த சீறடியார் வாண்முகத்தின்","றங்கொளிபாய்ந் துள்ளெறித்த தண்காந்த மாமணி","திங்க ளொளிகருதித் தெண்ணீர்த் துளிசிதற","மங்குன் மழையயிர்த்து வார்பொழிலின் வாய்மறைவார்."],"p":"","r":"Taking the cool moonstone, bright inside, for the moon, the women see the drops it throws as rain from a cloud and hide in the grove.","n":"The verse is built on mistaking: stone for moon, drops for rain.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","kurippu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1654","lb":"SO1654","mm":1,"ev":"The women mistake moonstone drops for cloud rain and hide in the grove.","cl":[],"ag":["மன்னவர் மகளிர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:72"},"so:1655":{"book":"soolamani","num":1653,"t":["வம்பத் திரளுருவின் மாணிக்கச் செங்கதிரை","அம்பொற் சிலம்பி னசோகந் தளிரென்று","தம்பொற் சுடராழி மெல்விரலாற் றைவந்து","கொம்பிற் குழைந்து குறுமுறுவல் கொண்டகல்வார்."],"p":"","r":"The women stroke the ruby's red rays with their ring-bright fingers, calling them ashoka sprouts, and bend over them with a small smile.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","ilamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1655","lb":"SO1655","mm":1,"ev":"The women take ruby rays for ashoka shoots and stroke them.","cl":[],"ag":["மன்னவர் மகளிர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:72"},"so:1656":{"book":"soolamani","num":1654,"t":["விண்டு சுடர்தயங்கு மேதகுமா மாணிக்கம்","கண்டு கவின்விரிந்த காயாந் துணரிவை","கொண்டு குழற்கணிது மென்று கொளலுறுவார்","வண்டு வழிபடர வாட்கண் புதைத்தியல்வார்."],"p":"","r":"Seeing a great shining ruby, the women think of it as kaya flowers fit for the hair; bees follow, and they cover their eyes and walk on.","n":"Line 2 reads the gem as blossom and line 3 the women as ready to wear it; the last line's covered eyes is read as a play against the following bees.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","ilamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1656","lb":"SO1656","mm":1,"ev":"The women take a ruby for kaya blossoms and hide their eyes from the bees.","cl":[],"ag":["மன்னவர் மகளிர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:72"},"so:1657":{"book":"soolamani","num":1655,"t":["வேயோங்கு சாரல் விளைபுனங் காவல்கொண்","டாயோ வெனமொழியு மம்மழலை யின்னிசையால்","போயோங்கு பூஞ்சோலை வாழும் புனக்கிளிக","மாயோன் மடந்தைமார் கூவி மகிழ்விப்பார்."],"p":"","r":"The king's women, in the guise of watchers of a ripe hill-field, call to the parrots there in sweet lisping speech and coax them to come.","n":"Line 3 ends புனக்கிளிக and line 4 has மாயோன் மடந்தைமார்; the lost letter in line 3 is supplied as கிளிகளை, and 'the dark lord's women' is read as the king's women.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","ilamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1657","lb":"SO1657","mm":1,"ev":"The women call the parrots of the hill-field and delight them.","cl":[],"ag":["மன்னவர் மகளிர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:72"},"so:1658":{"book":"soolamani","num":1656,"t":["பூந்தளிர் தாழ்ந்த பொழிறயங்கு பொன்வரைவாய்","ஈர்ந்தளிர் மேனியா ரிவ்வா றினிதியலக்","காந்தளங் குன்றின் கனபொன் மணியறைமேல்","ஏந்திளங் கொங்கை மகளிர் சிலரியைந்தார்."],"p":"","r":"On the gem-set rocks of a gold hill covered with fresh leaves, some tender-bodied women gather in pleasant company.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1658","lb":"SO1658","mm":1,"ev":"Some women gather on the rocks of the hill.","cl":[],"ag":["மன்னவர் மகளிர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:72"},"so:1659":{"book":"soolamani","num":1657,"t":["பைம்பொ னறைமேற் பவழ முரலாக","வம்ப மணிபெய்து வான்கேழ் மருப்போச்சி","அம்பொன் மலைசிலம்ப வம்மனை வள்ளையுடன்","கம்பஞ்செய் யானைக் கரியவனைப் பாடினார்."],"p":"","r":"On the gold slab they set a coral for the pestle, pour in gems, lift the tusk, and, as the golden hill sounds, sing of the dark one in the rhythm of a pounding song.","n":"The pounding song (அம்மனை வள்ளை) turns grain-work into play with gems; the dark one of line 4 is the king, as the next verses' refrain shows.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalai","pugazh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1659","lb":"SO1659","mm":1,"ev":"The women pound gems in play and sing of the dark lord.","cl":[],"ag":["மன்னவர் மகளிர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:72"},"so:1660":{"book":"soolamani","num":1658,"t":["கோடி சிலையெடுத்தான் கோளரிமா வாய்போழ்ந்தான்","ஆடியல் யானை யயக்கிரீவ னையடித்தான்","வீடின் மணியருவி வெண்மலையுங் கைப்பிடித்தான்","வாடலில் பூங்கண்ணி மாமேக வண்ணனே."],"p":"","r":"The song names the dark cloud-hued one's deeds: he lifted a bow, tore open the lion's mouth, struck down the rutting elephant Ayakkiriva, and held the white hill.","n":"Verses 1660-1662 carry a refrain of the dark-hued one; the deeds named are of the song, and the verse does not tell them as events.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pugazh","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1660","lb":"SO1660","mm":1,"ev":"The women sing the king's heroic deeds.","cl":[],"ag":["மன்னவர் மகளிர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:72"},"so:1661":{"book":"soolamani","num":1659,"t":["வலம்புரி வாய்வைத்தான் வார்சிலை கைக்கொண்டான்","சலம்புரி சண்டை தலைபனிப் புக்கண்டான்","பொலம்புரி தாமரையாள் பொன்னாகந் தோய்ந்தான்","கலம்புரி வண்டடக்கை கார்மேக வண்ணனே."],"p":"","r":"The song says he put the conch to his mouth and took the bow, saw the foe's head tremble in the war, and embraced the lotus-lady's gold body.","n":"The song joins war (conch, bow, enemy's trembling) and love (the lotus-lady's body) as the one fame of the king.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pugazh","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1661","lb":"SO1661","mm":1,"ev":"The women sing the king's war and his embrace of the lotus-lady.","cl":[],"ag":["மன்னவர் மகளிர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:72"},"so:1662":{"book":"soolamani","num":1660,"t":["செம்பொன்செய் யாழியான் சேதாம்ப னீண்முடியான்","அம்பொ னிதியு மருங்கலமுங் கைப்படுத்தான்","நம்பு மணிமேனி நங்கை நலநுகர்ந்தான்","கம்பஞ்செய் யானைக் கருமேக வண்ணனே."],"p":"","r":"The song says he is the wearer of the golden discus and long crown, who took the wealth and ornaments of enemies and enjoyed the pleasures of the trusted lady.","n":"The same verse sets conquest and enjoyment side by side as his praise.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pugazh","arasu","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1662","lb":"SO1662","mm":1,"ev":"The women sing of his wealth, his crown and his love.","cl":[],"ag":["மன்னவர் மகளிர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:72"},"so:1663":{"book":"soolamani","num":1661,"t":["மடந்தையர் பாட வாங்கு மாபெருந் தேவி நிற்ப","அடைந்தவ ரோடு மாடு மார்வநீர் வெள்ளம் வாங்க","உடைந்தழி மனத்தன் வேந்த னுழையதோர் தெய்வங் கூவிப்","படந்தவா முகத்தோர் வேழ மாகெனப் பணித்து விட்டான்."],"p":"","r":"While the women sing and the chief queen looks on, the king, his heart breaking at their joy in the others, calls a divine being near him and bids it become an elephant with a spread face-cloth.","n":"The whole verse joins a grieving heart and a ruse; the stated reason in line 2 and 3 is that love's flood and his breaking mind move him, and the poem calls the king's heart both hurt and playful. The text of the verse is printed in eight half-lines and repaired.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["soozhchi","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1663","lb":"SO1663","mm":1,"ev":"The king, wishing to frighten the women, orders a god to take elephant form.","cl":[],"ag":["செங்கண் ணெடியான்","தெய்வம்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:72"},"so:1664":{"book":"soolamani","num":1662,"t":["மைவரை யொன்று கோல மணிதயங் கருவி தாழ","ஐவனங் கலந்த சார லருகுவந் தணைவ தேபோற்","றெய்வமோர் வேழ மாகிச் செய்கடாந் திரண்டு வீழ","மைவரு நெடுங்க ணல்லார் நடுங்கவந் தணைந்த தன்றே."],"p":"","r":"A god becomes an elephant like a dark mountain, with its rut flowing, and comes like a mountain nearing a millet field, so that the dark-eyed women tremble.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["soozhchi","acham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1664","lb":"SO1664","mm":1,"ev":"The god in elephant form approaches the women, who tremble.","cl":[],"ag":["தெய்வம்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:72"},"so:1665":{"book":"soolamani","num":1663,"t":["கயில்கலந் திருண்டு தாழ்ந்த கருங்குழன் மருங்கு சோர","வெயில்கலந் திலங்குஞ் செம்பொன் மிடைமணிக் குழைவில் வீச","அயில்கலந் திலங்கு வேற்க ணையரி பிறழ வேட்டி","மயில்கலந் திரிந்த போல மடந்தையர் நடுங்கி னாரே."],"p":"","r":"The women's dark hair slips about their sides, the rings in their ears swing, their spear-like eyes flicker, and they shake like peahens scattered by fear.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["acham","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1665","lb":"SO1665","mm":1,"ev":"The women tremble before the elephant.","cl":[],"ag":["மன்னவர் மகளிர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:72"},"so:1666":{"book":"soolamani","num":1664,"t":["நாண்டனா னிறைந்த நங்கை நடுங்குபு நுடங்கி நோக்கி","யாண்டையா ரடிக ளென்னு மாயிடை யஞ்சல் பொன்னே","ஈண்டையே னென்னை பட்ட தென்றுசென் றணுகி னானால்","வேண்டிய விளைத்துக் கொள்ளும் விழுத்தவம் விளைத்து வந்தான்."],"p":"","r":"A trembling woman looks about and cries for her lord's feet; he comes up, tells her not to fear, and says that he is here; he has done the great penance that brings whatever one wants.","n":"The last line gives the king's power as the fruit of a past penance; it states a claim about tapas in a verse of love-play, and speaks of the king as the hero of the ruse, not as one who has renounced.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thavam","acham","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1666","lb":"SO1666","mm":1,"ev":"The king comes to the frightened woman and reassures her.","cl":[["great penance brings whatever one desires","general","asserted",4,"narrator"]],"ag":["செங்கண் ணெடியான்","மன்னவர் மகளிர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:72"},"so:1667":{"book":"soolamani","num":1665,"t":["மலைமுக மதநல் யானை மற்றது மறித்து நங்கை","முலைமுக நெருங்கப் புல்லி முருகுவேய் கோதை சூட்டிக்","கலைமுகந் திருத்திக் காதிற் கனபொன்செய் சுருளை நீவி","இலைமுகங் கலந்த செம்பொற் கலங்களை யிலங்க வைத்தான்."],"p":"","r":"Turning the rutting elephant back, the king embraces the woman, sets a fragrant garland on her, tidies her clothes, smooths the ear-roll and sets her gold ornaments in place.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","anbu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1667","lb":"SO1667","mm":1,"ev":"The king drives off the elephant and comforts the woman.","cl":[],"ag":["செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:72"},"so:1668":{"book":"soolamani","num":1666,"t":["மங்கையர் தம்மை யெல்லா மணிவண்ணன் மருட்டி மற்றிக்","கொங்கவிழ் குளிர்கொள் சோலைக் குன்றினின் றிழிந்த போது","வெங்கதிர் விரிந்த வெய்யோன் விசும்பிடை வெதும்ப வெம்பிச்","செங்கதிர்க் கூடங் குத்திச் செந்நடு வாக நின்றான்."],"p":"","r":"After the dark-hued king has deceived all his women and comes down from the grove-hill, the sun stands at noon, burning the sky.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaalam","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1668","lb":"SO1668","mm":1,"ev":"The king comes down the hill at noon, having deceived the women.","cl":[],"ag":["செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:72"},"so:1669":{"book":"soolamani","num":1667,"t":["அணங்கனார் நுதலின் மேலி லரும்பிய வாரத் தெண்ணீர்","மணங்கம ழலங்கன் மார்பன் மனத்தினை வாங்க மற்றக்","கணங்குழை மடந்தை மாரைக் கடிபுன லாடல் காண்பான்","மணங்கொள்பூந் துணர்கொள் சோலை மண்டுநீர் வாவி சார்ந்தான்."],"p":"","r":"Beads of sweat on the women's brows pull the king's heart, so he goes to the pool in the flowering grove to see them bathe.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1669","lb":"SO1669","mm":1,"ev":"The king goes to the pool to watch the women bathe.","cl":[],"ag":["செங்கண் ணெடியான்","மன்னவர் மகளிர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:72"},"so:1670":{"book":"soolamani","num":1668,"t":["சாந்துநீர் நிறைந்த வாவி தயங்குசெங் குவளை வாவி","பூந்துக ளவிழ்ந்த பொற்றா மரைமலர் புதைந்த வாவி","தேந்துண ரகன்ற தெண்ணீர்த் திருமணி யுருவ வாவி","வாய்ந்தவை போலக்காட்டி காட்டி யுழையவர் வணங்கி நின்றார்."],"p":"","r":"The attendants show the king pools of sandal-water, red lilies, golden lotuses and clear gem-blue water, and then bow and stand aside.","n":"Line 4 ends with a doubled காட்டி and the clause வாய்ந்தவை போல is hard to place; read as the attendants showing the pools.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","adimai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1670","lb":"SO1670","mm":1,"ev":"The attendants point out the pools to the king.","cl":[],"ag":["மைந்தர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:72"},"so:1671":{"book":"soolamani","num":1669,"t":["அன்னவா றமைந்த தெண்ணீ ரலைபுன லாடும் போழ்தில்","இன்னவா றியற்று கென்றாங் குழையரை மறைய வேவிப்","பொன்னவாஞ் சுணங்கு போர்த்த புணர்முலை மகளி ரோடு","மன்னவாம் வயிரத் தோளான் வலஞ்சுழி வாவி புக்கான்."],"p":"","r":"At the king's order that they hide, the attendants withdraw, and he enters the whirling pool with the women, whose gold-freckled breasts are pressed together.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1671","lb":"SO1671","mm":1,"ev":"The king sends the attendants away and enters the pool with the women.","cl":[],"ag":["செங்கண் ணெடியான்","மைந்தர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:72"},"so:1672":{"book":"soolamani","num":1670,"t":["மலங்குபாய் தயங்கு பொய்கை மணக்கல்வா யடுத்த செம்பொற்","கலங்கினா றிழிந்து கீழே கலந்துவந் தெழுந்த தெண்ணீர்","அலங்கலான் மடந்தை மார்த னரும்புணை யாக வீங்கி","வலங்குலாய்ச் சுழித்து வாய்த்த வாவி வாய் மடுத்த தன்றே."],"p":"","r":"Water that comes down the gold channels from the gem stones fills the pool and swirls, and the women's floats lift on it.","n":"The verse describes the pool's source and its swirling; the connection of the waters with the women's floats in line 3 is read loosely.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","kaalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1672","lb":"SO1672","mm":1,"ev":"The pool fills and swirls as the women bathe.","cl":[],"ag":["மன்னவர் மகளிர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:72"},"so:1673":{"book":"soolamani","num":1671,"t":["அலைபுனல் பெருக லோடு மலைகடல் வண்ணன் றன்னை","மலைபுனை கொடியிற் புல்லி மடந்தையர் மயங்கு வாரும்","மிலைபுனை கோதை சோர விடுபுணை தழுவு வாரும்","கலைபுனை துகிலுந் தோடு மொழியப்போய்க் கரைகொள் வாரும்."],"p":"","r":"As the water rises, some women cling to the sea-hued king like creepers to a hill, others seize a float and lose their garlands, and others lose their clothes and ear-ornaments and make for the shore.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","nenju"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1673","lb":"SO1673","mm":1,"ev":"The rising water scatters the women, who cling to the king or flee.","cl":[],"ag":["மன்னவர் மகளிர்","செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:72"},"so:1674":{"book":"soolamani","num":1672,"t":["ஆர்புனல் சுழித்து வாங்க வனையரா யணிபொன் வாவி","நீர்புனை தடத்தி னுள்ளா னிலைகொண்டு நெடுங்கண் சேப்பத்","தார்புனை மார்பன் றன்மேற் றரங்கநீர் தயங்கத் தூவி","வார்புனை முலையி னல்லார் மயங்கமர் தொடங்கி னாரே."],"p":"","r":"With the swirling water drawing round him, the king stands firm in the pool, his eyes reddened, and the women begin a water-fight, flinging water on his chest.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1674","lb":"SO1674","mm":1,"ev":"The king stands in the pool and the women start a water-fight against him.","cl":[],"ag":["செங்கண் ணெடியான்","மன்னவர் மகளிர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:72"},"so:1675":{"book":"soolamani","num":1673,"t":["திரளிருஞ் சிவிறி வீக்கிச் செழுமழைத் தாரை பெய்வார்","மருளிரும் பிணையன் மாலைப் படைபல வழங்கிச் சூழ்வார்","சுருளிருந் தோடு வாங்கித் தோண்மிசை துளங்கி வீழ்ப்பார்","இருளிருங் குழலி னார்க ளிறைவன்மே லினைய ரானார்."],"p":"","r":"The king's wives, dark-haired, fill their syringes and pour streams like rain, fling garlands in play and shake the ear-ornaments from their shoulders, so turning themselves against their lord in sport.","n":"இறைவன்மேல் இனையர் ஆனார் says they became his foes in play; the poet uses the vocabulary of war for a bath, and the verses that follow keep it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1675","lb":"SO1675","mm":1,"ev":"The wives begin a water battle against the king, spraying him and throwing garlands.","cl":[],"ag":["மன்னவர் மகளிர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:73"},"so:1676":{"book":"soolamani","num":1674,"t":["சாந்தெழு சிவிறித் தாரை சதுர்முக மாக வீக்கிப்","பாய்ந்தன பவழச் செங்கே ழங்கையான் முகந்த தெண்ணீர்","வேய்ந்தன திவலை யாகி விழுந்தன வேரி மாலை","நாந்தகக் கிழவன் பொய்யே நங்கைமார்க் குடைந்து நின்றான்."],"p":"","r":"The jets of scented water, aimed four ways from the syringes, fall on the wives like drops of fine rain, and the lord of the Nandaka sword, though the sport is only feigned, gives way before the women.","n":"Nandaka is the sword of Vishnu; calling Tivittan its lord keeps his godlike standing while he plays at losing. பொய்யே, 'only in pretence', is the poet's own word.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1676","lb":"SO1676","mm":1,"ev":"Tivittan feigns defeat before the wives' water-jets.","cl":[],"ag":["செங்கண் ணெடியான்","மன்னவர் மகளிர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:73"},"so:1677":{"book":"soolamani","num":1675,"t":["காரையார் வண்ணன் மாலைக் காற்படை யுடைந்த போழ்தில்","தரையாய்க் குறளுஞ் சிந்து மிதந்தன சில்ல சிந்தி","வேரியார் குவளை வேய்ந்த மெல்லிய லவர்க்குத் தோற்ற","ஒரையாய் முதலை யாகிக் கூன்மடை யொளித்த வன்றே."],"p":"","r":"As the lord's line of foot-guards breaks in the play, the water-toys and floating things are scattered, and, losing to the lily-wreathed women, he is said to have hidden like a crocodile in the pond's bent sluice.","n":"Line 2 (தரையாய்க் குறளுஞ் சிந்து மிதந்தன சில்ல சிந்தி) and the word ஓரையாய் in line 4 are obscure; the picture of a game lost and a hiding place is firm and the objects named are not.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1677","lb":"SO1677","mm":1,"ev":"Tivittan, beaten at the water-play, hides in the pond's sluice like a crocodile.","cl":[],"ag":["செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:73"},"so:1678":{"book":"soolamani","num":1676,"t":["வென்றனம் வீரன் றன்னை வீக்குமின் சிவிறித் தாரை","சென்றெனச் சிறந்த காதற் றேவியர் திளைக்கும் போழ்தில்","ஒன்றிய வுழையர் கீழ்நீ ரோப்பறித் திடுத லோடு","நின்றகஞ் சுழிந்த தெண்ணீர் நெரேலென விழிந்த தன்றே."],"p":"","r":"The queens cry that they have beaten the hero and tell one another to pour the spray on him; while they play, their attendants open the lower sluice and the still water of the pool runs out with a rush.","n":"The words வென்றனம் வீரன் றன்னை வீக்குமின் are the queens' own cry; the draining of the pool is the poet's addition and shows the day's play ending.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1678","lb":"SO1678","mm":1,"ev":"The queens declare victory over Tivittan, and attendants let the pool's water drain.","cl":[],"ag":["மன்னவர் மகளிர்","மைந்தர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["மன்னவர் மகளிர்","செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ch":"so:73"},"so:1679":{"book":"soolamani","num":1677,"t":["மாலையுந் துகிலும் வார் வார்புன லொழுகும் போழ்தின்","ஆலையின் கரும்பி னின்சொ லணங்கனா ரவிழத் தத்தம்","கோலமென் றுகில்க டாங்கிக் குழைமுகஞ் சுடரக் கோட்டி","வேலைநீர் வண்ணன் முன்னர் நாணினான் மெலிவு சென்றார்."],"p":"","r":"With garlands and cloth streaming water, the cane-sweet-voiced women loosen their dress, then draw their wet garments close and turn their shining faces aside, shy before the sea-hued lord.","n":"Shyness comes only after the play is over and the cloth clings; the poet puts modesty at the end of the sport and not at its start.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naanam","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1679","lb":"SO1679","mm":1,"ev":"The soaked queens gather their clinging garments and turn away shyly from Tivittan.","cl":[],"ag":["மன்னவர் மகளிர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:73"},"so:1680":{"book":"soolamani","num":1678,"t":["அருமணிக் கலாப வல்கு லவிழ்துகி லசைத்து மீட்டும்","திருமணி வண்ண னோடுந் தேவியர் திளைத்துத் தெண்ணீர்ப்","புரிமணிப் பொன்செய் வாவிப் புணைபுறந் தழுவிப் புக்கார்","கருமணி வண்டுந் தேனுங் கையுறக் கலந்த தன்றே."],"p":"","r":"The queens tie up their jewelled girdles and loosened cloth, and with the gem-hued lord go down again into the golden pond, clinging to the floats at its side.","n":"Line 4 has dark bees and honey joining the hand-clasped play; the bees stand for black eyes or hair mingling with the flowers.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1680","lb":"SO1680","mm":1,"ev":"Tivittan and the queens re-enter the pond with floats.","cl":[],"ag":["செங்கண் ணெடியான்","மன்னவர் மகளிர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:73"},"so:1681":{"book":"soolamani","num":1679,"t":["கொங்கைக டுளும்பநீர் குடைந்துங் கொய்தளிர்","அங்கையி னோன்புணை தழுவி யாடியுஞ்","செங்கயற் கண்மலர் சிவப்ப மூழ்கியு","மங்கையர் புனற்றொழின் மயங்கிற் றென்பவே."],"p":"","r":"Women play in the water, now diving with swelling breasts, now clinging to a float with soft young-leaf hands, now ducking until their red carp-like eyes redden, and the poet says the water-play bewildered them.","n":"The verse is a series of three actions closed by மயங்கிற் றென்பவே, a hearsay close that keeps the poet at a distance from the scene.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1681","lb":"SO1681","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["மன்னவர் மகளிர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:73"},"so:1682":{"book":"soolamani","num":1680,"t":["அடித்தலத் தலத்தகங் கழுமிக் குங்குமப்","பொடிக்கலந் தந்திவான் படைத்த பூம்புனல்","வடிக்கலந் திலங்குவா ணெடுங்கண் மைக்குழம்","பிடிக்கலந் திருளுமங் கியற்றப் பட்டதே."],"p":"","r":"Red saffron from their feet, evening-red powder and the dark collyrium dissolved from their long eyes mix together in the pool, so that the water seems to have been made red and dark at once.","n":"The water is described as the colour of what the women wash into it; nothing in the verse says it is stirred by the king.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1682","lb":"SO1682","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:73"},"so:1683":{"book":"soolamani","num":1681,"t":["கொங்கைவாய்க் குங்குமக் குழம்புங் கோதைவாய்","மங்கைமார் சிதர்ந்தன வாசச் சுண்ணமும்","செங்கண்மா லகலத்து விரையுந் தேர்த்தரோ","அங்கண்மா லிரும்புன லளறு பட்டதே."],"p":"","r":"The saffron paste of the women's breasts, the scented powder shaken from their hair and the sandal on the red-eyed lord's chest all mingle, and the great pool turns to mud.","n":"The turning of clear water into mud is meant as sign of the sport's abundance and not of spoiling; the poet makes no complaint.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1683","lb":"SO1683","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:73"},"so:1684":{"book":"soolamani","num":1682,"t":["அணங்கனா ரகலல்கு லலைத்து மாங்கவர்","சுணங்குசூ ழிளமுலை துளும்பத் தாக்கியும்","வணங்குபூங் கொடியிடை வளைத்தும் வாவிவாய்","இணங்குநீர்த் திரையவை யிணைப்ப வொத்தவே."],"p":"","r":"The women strike the water with their broad hips and with their breasts under fine down, and bend their slim waists, so that the waves of the pool seem to be joining them.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1684","lb":"SO1684","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["மன்னவர் மகளிர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:73"},"so:1685":{"book":"soolamani","num":1683,"t":["வடந்தவ ழிளமுலை விம்ம மங்கையர்","குடைந்திட வெழுந்தநீர் குளித்த தாமரை","மடந்தையர் குளித்தெழும் போழ்தின் வாண்முகம்","அடைந்ததோர் பொலிவினை யறிவித் திட்டவே."],"p":"","r":"As the women press into the water with garlands trembling on their young breasts, the lotuses that rose sank, and when the girls came up from their dive the new brightness of their faces showed to the lotuses.","n":"The conceit is that the lotuses show the girls' faces by contrast; the poet does not say they were outshone, only that the faces brought out something the flowers had not.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1685","lb":"SO1685","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["மன்னவர் மகளிர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:73"},"so:1686":{"book":"soolamani","num":1684,"t":["வளைத்தகை யொண்பணைத் தோளி மாரொடு","திளைத்தகங் கழுமிய தரங்கத் தெண்புனல்","இளைத்தவர் மணிக்கரை யேறச் சீறடி","திளைத்துமுன் சிறிதிடஞ் சென்று மீண்டதே."],"p":"","r":"The clear waves tossed by the women with bangled hands rise, and as the tired women step towards the jewelled bank the water follows a little way up on their small feet and falls back again.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1686","lb":"SO1686","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["மன்னவர் மகளிர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:73"},"so:1687":{"book":"soolamani","num":1685,"t":["பொழுதுசென் னாழிகை யெல்லை பூங்கழல்","தொழுதுவந் திளையவ ருணர்த்தத் தொண்டைவாய்","எழுதிய கொடியனார் சூழ வீர்ம்பொழில்","பழுதுழை யிலாப்பகற் கோயி லெய்தினான்."],"p":"","r":"When the hours of the day had passed, the young attendants bow at his flower-anklets and tell him the time, and the king, with the vine-like painted-lipped women around him, goes to the day-time palace in the grove.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","kaalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1687","lb":"SO1687","mm":1,"ev":"Attendants tell Tivittan the time and he goes with his queens to the pleasure-house in the grove.","cl":[],"ag":["செங்கண் ணெடியான்","இளையவர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["இளையவர்","செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ch":"so:73"},"so:1688":{"book":"soolamani","num":1686,"t":["தேவியர் திருமணி மேனி நீர்துடைத்","தாவியம் புனைதுகி லல்குன் மேலுடீஇக்","காவியங் கண்ணினார் காக துண்டத்தின்","ஆவியா லீர்ங்குழ லாவி யூட்டினார்."],"p":"","r":"The wives dry the water from their jewel-coloured bodies, put fine cloth on their hips, and with smoke of aloe in a crow-beak vessel they perfume and dry one another's damp hair.","n":"It is not said whose body is dried; the grammar leaves open that the wives dry themselves or the lord. காகதுண்டம் (crow-beak) is a kind of aloe-wood.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1688","lb":"SO1688","mm":1,"ev":"The queens dry and dress after the water-play and scent their hair with incense.","cl":[],"ag":["மன்னவர் மகளிர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:73"},"so:1689":{"book":"soolamani","num":1687,"t":["தண்ணிறத் தண்கழு நீரி னெய்தலின்","கண்ணிறக் கருங்கடை யிதழும் பெய்திடை","தண்ணறுந் தமனகக் கொழுந்துஞ் சார்த்திய","ஒண்ணிறப் பிணையலன் றுவக்கப் பட்டதே."],"p":"","r":"A garland is made of cool blue lilies, the dark-edged petals of the neythal, and the fragrant damanaka shoot, and it is received with joy.","n":"No one is named as maker or wearer; whose garland it is, and who rejoiced, is not said.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1689","lb":"SO1689","mm":1,"ev":"A garland of lilies and damanaka is made and welcomed.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:73"},"so:1690":{"book":"soolamani","num":1688,"t":["பொன்மலைக் காவியற் றிமிர்ந்து பூங்கமழ்","தென்மலைச் சந்தன மெழுதித் தாமரை","நன்மலர்த் தாதுமீ தப்பி நங்கைமார்","மென்முலைத் தடங்களும் விருந்து பட்டவே."],"p":"","r":"The wives rub the golden mountain-hued sandal of the southern hills into their skin and sprinkle lotus pollen over it, and their soft breasts become a feast for the king's eyes.","n":"விருந்து பட்டவே, 'became a feast', gives the breasts as what is offered to the lord; the poet's word fits a guest's treat and carries no blame.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1690","lb":"SO1690","mm":1,"ev":"The queens paint their breasts with sandal and lotus pollen.","cl":[],"ag":["மன்னவர் மகளிர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:73"},"so:1691":{"book":"soolamani","num":1689,"t":["கண்ணகங் குளிர்ப்பக் கல்லாரக் கற்றையும்","தண்ணறுங் குவளைதா மெறித்த தாமமும்","ஒண்ணிறத் தாமரை யொலிய லுந்தழீஇ","எண்ணரும் பெருங்கவி னிளைய ரெய்தினார்."],"p":"","r":"The young ones of beauty beyond counting come, bringing the cool bunches of kallaara lilies, the lily garlands they threw, and the shining lotuses, and embracing them.","n":"Whether இளையர் are young attendants or the young queens themselves is not clear; the verse names no giver and no receiver of the flowers.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1691","lb":"SO1691","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["இளையவர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:73"},"so:1692":{"book":"soolamani","num":1690,"t":["காமரு நிறத்தகல் லாரக் கற்றைகள்","சாமரை யெனத்தம ரசைப்பத் தாமரை","தேமரு குடையிலை கவிப்பத் தேவியர்","பூமரு மடந்தையர் போன்று தோன்றினார்."],"p":"","r":"The queens, with bunches of kallaara flower in their hands waved as fans, and lotus leaves held over them as umbrellas, appear like the lotus-seated goddess and her kind.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1692","lb":"SO1692","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["மன்னவர் மகளிர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:73"},"so:1693":{"book":"soolamani","num":1691,"t":["தேவர்க டிசைமுகங் காப்பத் தீஞ்சுவை","ஆவியா ரமிழ்தயின் றிருந்த வாயிடைப்","பாவையர் கருங்கணாற் பருகு வார்கள்போன்","மாவர சழித்தவன் மருங்கு சுற்றினார்."],"p":"","r":"While the guardians of the quarters watch and the lord enjoys his sweet food, the girls stand around the destroyer of the great king, drinking him in with their dark eyes.","n":"மாவரசு அழித்தவன் recalls the earlier victory over the great king (the sarukkam's opening); the poet holds the war and the harem side by side in a single phrase.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1693","lb":"SO1693","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["மன்னவர் மகளிர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:73"},"so:1694":{"book":"soolamani","num":1692,"t":["வஞ்சியங் கொம்ப னாரு மன்னனு மிருந்த போழ்தின்","விஞ்சைய னொருவன் றோன்றி விசும்பினா றிழிந்து வந்து","மஞ்சிவர் சோலை வாயில் வாயிலோன் வாயி லாக","அஞ்சன வண்ணன் செந்தா மரையடி வணங்கி னானே."],"p":"","r":"As the king sits with the women, a Vidyadhara descends from the sky, enters the grove gate through the gatekeeper, and bows to the feet of the dark-hued lord.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thoothu","panivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1694","lb":"SO1694","mm":1,"ev":"A Vidyadhara arrives from the sky, is let in by the gatekeeper, and bows to Tivittan.","cl":[],"ag":["விஞ்சையன்","செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:73"},"so:1695":{"book":"soolamani","num":1693,"t":["வந்தவன் வணங்க லோடு மாமனை நுவலி யென்னை","கந்தணை யானை வேந்தன் கழலடி செவ்வி யோவென்","றந்தமி லாழி யாள்வான் வினவலி னருளு மாறென்","றிந்திர னனைய நீராற் கிறைஞ்சலு மிருக்க வென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"When the visitor bows, the ruler of the wheel-ruled earth asks after the visitor's uncle and the welfare of the king whose feet he serves, and bids him rise, and, because the visitor bows as to Indra, bids him sit.","n":"Lines 1-3 are hard to construe: மாமனை நுவலி என்னை ... செவ்வியோ என்று can be read as Tivittan's question about the uncle, or the envoy's report of him; the reading follows the first.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thoothu","panivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1695","lb":"SO1695","mm":1,"ev":"Tivittan asks after the sender and tells the bowing Vidyadhara to sit.","cl":[],"ag":["செங்கண் ணெடியான்","விஞ்சையன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["செங்கண் ணெடியான்","விஞ்சையன்"],"ch":"so:73"},"so:1696":{"book":"soolamani","num":1694,"t":["உரிமையோ டிருந்த போழ்தி னுணர்த்துதற் குரித்தென் றெண்ணித்","திருமுகந் தொழுது காட்டத் தேவிதன் மருங்கு நின்ற","உரிமைகொ ளுழைய ருள்ளா ளொருத்திவா சித்து ணர்த்த","அருமுடி யொழிய வெல்லா வணிகளு மவனுக் கீந்தான்."],"p":"","r":"When they have settled, the envoy shows the letter, bowing to it; an attendant of the queen's household reads it aloud, and the king gives the envoy every ornament he wears except his crown.","n":"The gift comes before the sense of the message is told: the king rewards on seeing the letter, and the verse leaves the contents for the next verse. வாசித்து can mean 'reads' or 'discerns'; the reading takes it as read aloud.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["eegai","thoothu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1696","lb":"SO1696","mm":1,"ev":"The envoy presents a letter, an attendant reads it out, and Tivittan gives the envoy all his ornaments but the crown.","cl":[],"ag":["விஞ்சையன்","உழையர் உள்ளாள் ஒருத்தி","செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ec":[],"spk":["உழையர் உள்ளாள் ஒருத்தி","செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ch":"so:73"},"so:1697":{"book":"soolamani","num":1695,"t":["கனிவளர் கிளவி யாருங் கதிர்மணிக் கலங்கள் வாங்கிப்","பனிமதி விசும்பின் வந்தான் பால்வரப் பணித்த பின்னை","இனியிது பெயர்த்து நீயே யுரையென வெடுத்துக் கொண்டு","துனிவள ரிலங்கு வேலான் கழலடி தொழுது சொன்னான்."],"p":"","r":"The queens take the jewelled vessels from the visitor who has come through the cool moonlit sky, and, bidden to go on, he takes the letter again, and, told to say it again himself, bows to the feet of the king of the bright spear and speaks.","n":"The king's order இனியிது பெயர்த்து நீயே யுரை, 'now say this yourself, turned', makes the envoy answer in his own mouth what the paper holds; the verse does not say the paper's text was unfit.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thoothu","panivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1697","lb":"SO1697","mm":1,"ev":"Tivittan has the envoy restate the message, and the envoy bows and begins.","cl":[],"ag":["விஞ்சையன்","மன்னவர் மகளிர்","செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ec":[],"spk":["செங்கண் ணெடியான்","விஞ்சையன்"],"ch":"so:73"},"so:1698":{"book":"soolamani","num":1696,"t":["சுடர்மலைத் திருண்ட சோலைச் சுரேந்திர காந்த மென்னும்","வடமலை நகர மாளு மன்னவன் றேவி பெற்ற","தடமலர்ப் பெரிய வாட்கட் டையன்மற் றவளை யெங்கோன்","விடமலைத் திலங்கு செவ்வேல் வெய்யவன் பெயரன் வேட்டான்."],"p":"","r":"The envoy begins: the king who rules the northern-ridge city of Surendrakanta has a daughter, broad-eyed and born of his queen, and the grandson of the fierce spear-bearing Veyyavan has asked for her in marriage.","n":"He names the bride by her father's city and her mother, and the suitor by his grandfather; who the suitor is, and what he wants of Tivittan, is for the verses after this chapter. The speaker is known from 1697.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thoothu","kudi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1698","lb":"SO1698","mm":1,"ev":"The envoy announces that a suitor has sought the daughter of the king of Surendrakanta.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["விஞ்சையன்","செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ch":"so:73"},"so:1699":{"book":"soolamani","num":1697,"t":["என்றவன் பெயர்த்துஞ் சொல்ல வின்பநீர் வெள்ள மூழ்கி","மின்றவ ழிலங்கும் வேலான் விஞ்சைய னவனைப் போக்கிச்","சென்றுதன் கோயில் சேர்ந்தான் செங்கதிர்த் திகிரி யானு","மன்றழல் சுருங்க முந்நீ ரலைகட லழுவம் பாய்ந்தான்."],"p":"","r":"After the Vidyadhara has spoken again and been sent away, the lord returns to his palace as the sun plunges into the sea at evening.","n":"The sun is named only by the epithet of the one holding the discus; the identification rests on the sinking into the sea in line 4.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaalam","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1699","lb":"SO1699","mm":1,"ev":"Tivittan dismisses the Vidyadhara envoy and goes to his palace while the sun sets.","cl":[],"ag":["விஞ்சையன்","செங்கண் ணெடியான்","வெய்யோன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:74"},"so:1700":{"book":"soolamani","num":1698,"t":["அழலவன் குளித்த பின்னை யணங்கிவ ரந்தி யென்னும்","மழலையங் கிளவிச் செவ்வாய் மடந்தையு மடைந்த போழ்தில்","குழலமர் கிளவி யார்தங் கூந்தலுட் குளித்து விம்மி","எழிலகி லாவி போர்ப்ப விருவிசும் பிருண்ட தன்றே."],"p":"","r":"Dusk and the sweet-voiced young woman arrive together, and darkness spreads like the smoke of women's hair over the wide sky.","n":"Evening is made a woman (அந்தி) entering with the night; the dark is likened to the hair of women, and the scene moves from the public court to the bedchamber.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaalam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1700","lb":"SO1700","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:74"},"so:1701":{"book":"soolamani","num":1699,"t":["விரவின பரவைப் பன்மிமீன் மிடைமணிக் கலாப மாக","மருவின பரவை யல்குன் மயங்கிருட் டுகிலை வாங்கிப்","புரிவணன் மதிய மென்பான் பொழிகதிர்த் தடக்கை நீட்டி","இரவெனு மடந்தை செல்வ நுகரிய வெழுந்து போந்தான்."],"p":"","r":"The moon takes the night's dark cloth from her, as a lover takes the garment of a woman jewelled with stars, and rises to enjoy her wealth.","n":"Moonrise is told as a man's undressing of a woman, preparing the poem's turn to Tivittan's own night.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaalam","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1701","lb":"SO1701","mm":1,"ev":"The moon rises and draws off the garment of night.","cl":[],"ag":["மதியம்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:74"},"so:1702":{"book":"soolamani","num":1700,"t":["ஏரணி விசும்பி னங்கே ழெழுநிலா விரிந்த போழ்தில்","சீரணி மணிவண் டார்க்குஞ் சிகழிகைப் பவழ வாயார்","காரணி வண்ண னென்னுங் கருங்களி வேழந் தன்னை","வாரணி யிளமென் கொங்கை வாரியுள் வளைத்துக் கொண்டார்."],"p":"","r":"As the moon spreads its light, women with coral lips trap the dark-hued lord, likened to a rutting elephant, in the hollow between their breasts.","n":"Tivittan is the 'dark-clouded' lord and also a mad elephant; the verse begins an elephant figure that runs to 1705.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1702","lb":"SO1702","mm":1,"ev":"The women of the palace draw Tivittan into love-play.","cl":[],"ag":["நல்லார்","செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:74"},"so:1703":{"book":"soolamani","num":1701,"t":["பங்கய முகத்து நல்லார் பவழவாய்க் கவளங் கொண்டு","பொங்கிய களிய தாகி மயங்கிய பொருவில் வேழம்","குங்குமப் பொடிநின் றாடிக் குவட்டிளங் கொங்கை யென்னும்","தங்கொளி மணிமுத் தேந்துந் தடத்திடை யிறைஞ்சிற் றன்றே."],"p":"","r":"The elephant-lord, drunk on the mouthfuls of coral-lipped women, bows in the pool of their pearled breasts.","n":"The elephant figure continues; the 'mouthful' is the kiss, in an image of an elephant being fed.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1703","lb":"SO1703","mm":1,"ev":"Tivittan sinks into the women's embrace and is intoxicated.","cl":[],"ag":["செங்கண் ணெடியான்","நல்லார்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:74"},"so:1704":{"book":"soolamani","num":1702,"t":["வேய்மரு ளுருவத் தோளார் வெம்முலைத் தடங்க ளென்னும்","பூமரு தடத்துட் டாழ்ந்து பொற்பொடி புதைய வாடிக்","காமரு காம மென்னுங் கருங்கயம் படிந்து சென்று","தேமரு குழலஞ் சாயற் றேவிகைப் பட்ட தன்றே."],"p":"","r":"The elephant wallows in the pools of other women's breasts and bathes in the dark pool called desire, then comes to the chief queen.","n":"The elephant moves from pool to pool and finally to one woman, the queen (தேவிகை, a term for a queen, not a given name). The closing 'came into her keeping' is the hinge of the sequence.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","kaamanoy"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1704","lb":"SO1704","mm":1,"ev":"After the other women's embraces Tivittan comes to the queen.","cl":[],"ag":["செங்கண் ணெடியான்","தேவிகை"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:74"},"so:1705":{"book":"soolamani","num":1703,"t":["காதலா லுரிமை பாங்கிற் கடிகமழ் காம வல்லித்","தாதலொந் ததர்ந்து சிந்தத் திளைத்தவத் தடக்கை வேழம்","மாதரா ளமிழ்தின் சாயற் றோட்டியால் வணக்கப் பட்டுப்","போதுலாம் புணர்மென் கொங்கைக் குவட்டிடைப் பூண்ட தன்றே."],"p":"","r":"The great elephant is bent by the queen's goad of nectar-like grace, and settles on the hill of her breasts, bound there.","n":"The goad (தோட்டி) is the woman's grace; love is a binding the elephant accepts, and the image makes the queen the one who governs the king.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","karpu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1705","lb":"SO1705","mm":1,"ev":"The queen holds Tivittan fast in love.","cl":[],"ag":["தேவிகை","செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:74"},"so:1706":{"book":"soolamani","num":1704,"t":["செங்கயற் கண்ணி னாளுஞ் செல்வனுந் திளைத்துத் தீந்தேன்","பொங்கிய வமளி மேலாற் புணர்முலை நெருங்கப் புல்லித்","தங்கிய பொழுதிற் றாழ்ந்து தண்கதிர் மதியந் தானே","மங்கைதன் பவழச் செவ்வாய் மடுத்தக மடைந்த தன்றே."],"p":"","r":"The red-lipped queen and her lord embrace on the honeyed bed, and the cool moon itself, sinking, enters the red mouth of the woman.","n":"The moon entering the mouth is the poem's omen of conception, a motif that the narrator tells as a wonder of the bed, and the next verses show it read as a dream.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","kanavu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1706","lb":"SO1706","mm":1,"ev":"In union the queen sees the moon enter her mouth.","cl":[],"ag":["தேவிகை","செங்கண் ணெடியான்","மதியம்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:74"},"so:1707":{"book":"soolamani","num":1705,"t":["அடைந்தது மதிய மாக வாயிடை யரசன் றிண்டோள்","மிடைந்ததோ ணெகிழ விம்மி மெல்லியல் வெருவ லோடு","மடங்கலை யலைக்கு நீரான் மருட்டினன் வினவ மாதோ","வடந்தவ ழிளமென் கொங்கை மெல்லவே மிழற்றி னாளே."],"p":"","r":"The moon having entered, the king's arms slacken; the frightened woman is asked in a voice that conquers lions, and whispers her tale.","n":"The asker's voice is likened to a lion's roar softened to coax: the king's power is turned into gentleness at the bedside. The queen's own words are not given.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kanavu","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1707","lb":"SO1707","mm":1,"ev":"Tivittan questions the trembling queen, who tells him quietly what she saw.","cl":[],"ag":["செங்கண் ணெடியான்","தேவிகை"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:74"},"so:1708":{"book":"soolamani","num":1706,"t":["வணங்கியிவ் வுலக மெல்லா மகிழ்ந்துகண் பருகு நீர்மை","அணங்கிவர் சிறுவன் வந்துன் அணிவயிற் றகத்துப் பட்டான்","கணங்குழை யஞ்ச லென்று கருமணி வண்ணன் றேற்றப்","பணங்குலாம் பரவை யல்குற் பாவையும் பரிவு தீர்ந்தாள்."],"p":"","r":"The dark-gem lord comforts her: a child before whom the whole world will bow and rejoice has come into your womb, so do not fear.","n":"The king himself reads the dream, not an astrologer as in the earlier dream of 106 to 111. The child is already spoken of as a son the world will worship.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["makkal","kanavu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1708","lb":"SO1708","mm":1,"ev":"Tivittan interprets the queen's vision as the coming of a son and calms her.","cl":[],"ag":["செங்கண் ணெடியான்","தேவிகை"],"ec":[],"spk":["செங்கண் ணெடியான்","தேவிகை"],"ch":"so:74"},"so:1709":{"book":"soolamani","num":1707,"t":["கங்குல்வாய் மடந்தை கண்ட கனவுமெய் யாகல் வேண்டி","மங்குல்வா னகட்டுச் சென்று மதியவன் மறைந்த பின்னை","அங்குலா யிருளை நீக்கு மாயிரங் கதிரி னானும்","கொங்குலாங் குழலி காணுங் குழவிய துருவங் கொண்டான்."],"p":"","r":"So that the dream the woman saw in the night would prove true, the thousand-rayed sun leaves the sky after the moon sets and takes the child's form.","n":"The poem gives a reason from the dream: the sun takes a shape so the vision is made true. Moon, then sun, are the two lights that make the child, and the audience is told that the dream was real.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kanavu","aadhiththan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1709","lb":"SO1709","mm":1,"ev":"The sun takes the form of the child the queen has seen in her dream.","cl":[],"ag":["வெய்யோன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:74"},"so:1710":{"book":"soolamani","num":1708,"t":["குலம்புரி சிறுவனைத் தரித்துக் கோலமா","நிலம்புரி நிழலொளி நிரந்து தோன்றலால்","வலம்புரி மணிக்கரு விருந்த தன்னதோர்","நலம்புரி திருவின ணங்கை யாயினாள்."],"p":"","r":"Bearing the noble child, she is lit by a shine as of a conch that holds a precious pearl, and becomes a woman of fortune.","n":"The conch with its pearl (வலம்புரி மணிக் கரு) is the womb with the child; the bearing is shown through her radiance.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["makkal","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1710","lb":"SO1710","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["தேவிகை"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:74"},"so:1711":{"book":"soolamani","num":1709,"t":["மின்னிலங் கவிரொளி மேனி மெல்லவே","தொன்னலம் பெயர்ந்துபொன் சுடர்ந்து தோன்றலான்","மன்னிலங் கருமணி வளர வாளுமிழ்","பொன்னிலம் புரைவதோர் பொலிவு மெய்தினாள்."],"p":"","r":"As the dark gem grows within her, her slow-moving body loses its former beauty and glows with the gold-bright look of a land that gives gems.","n":"The change in the body from old beauty to a golden glow is presented as a sign of the child's growth.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["makkal","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1711","lb":"SO1711","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["தேவிகை"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:74"},"so:1712":{"book":"soolamani","num":1710,"t":["கோணலம் பொலிந்துவிண் குளிரக் குங்குமத்","தோணலம் பொலிந்ததோர் தோன்ற லோடுதன்","கேணலம் பொலிதரக் கிளருஞ் சோதிய","நாணலம் பொலிதர நம்பி தோன்றினான்."],"p":"","r":"The young lord is born, with a light that grows through the cool sky, a body gleaming like saffron and a modest brilliance.","n":"The birth is told in one sentence of radiance: he appears with a saffron-bright body and a shy radiance. The newborn's name is not given until 1722.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirappu","makkal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1712","lb":"SO1712","mm":1,"ev":"The queen's son is born.","cl":[],"ag":["நம்பி"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:74"},"so:1713":{"book":"soolamani","num":1711,"t":["பொலிகெனு மொலிகளும் பொன்செய் மாமணி","ஒலிகல வொலிகளும் விரவி யூழிநீர்","கலிகெழு கனைகடல் கலங்கி யன்னதோர்","பலிகெழு முரசொலி பரந்தொ லித்ததே."],"p":"","r":"The call of 'Be glad!', the sound of gem-set instruments and the drum of the offering rise together like the sea of the end of the age.","n":"The cosmic simile for a festival of joy is the sea of the end of an age, the ocean at dissolution. Joy at a birth borrows the sound of the world's ending.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maalai","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1713","lb":"SO1713","mm":1,"ev":"The city's drums and voices break out at the birth.","cl":[],"ag":["நகர்மாந்தர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:74"},"so:1714":{"book":"soolamani","num":1712,"t":["துளைபடு குழலிசை துடியொ டார்ப்பவும்","வளைபடு கறங்கிசை வயிரொ டேங்கவும்","தளைபடு தகைமலர் மாலை தாதுகக்","கிளைபடு வளநகர் கிலுகி லுத்ததே."],"p":"","r":"Flutes and tabors, conches and horns sound together, and the garlanded city shakes with the din.","n":"The garlands drop their pollen as the city quakes with the sound; the verse is pure festival, with no new event.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inbam","naadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1714","lb":"SO1714","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["நகர்மாந்தர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:74"},"so:1715":{"book":"soolamani","num":1713,"t":["தொத்திளங் கடிமலர் துதைந்த கோதையார்","மொய்த்திளங் குமரரோ டாடு முன்கடை","மத்தளப் பாணியு மதன கீதமும்","கைத்தலத் தாளமுங் கலந்தி சைத்தவே."],"p":"","r":"At the doors of houses, girls with garlands dance with young men to the drum, the song of love and the clapping of hands.","n":"The festival of the king's birth is a general holiday for the city, in which the sound of love-song is the town's own.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inbam","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1715","lb":"SO1715","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["நகர்மாந்தர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:74"},"so:1716":{"book":"soolamani","num":1714,"t":["சிறைநகர் சீத்தன திலத முக்குடை","இறைநகர் விழவணி யியன்ற நீண்டுநீர்த்","துறைநகர் சுண்ணநெய் நாவி தூங்கின","நிறைநக ரவர்தொழி னினைப்பி கந்தவே."],"p":"","r":"The prisons are cleaned, sandal marks are set on brows, the king's city is decked for festival, and townsfolk forget their work in the feast.","n":"The cleaning or emptying of prisons (சிறைநகர் சீத்தன) at a royal birth is a common sign of mercy, but the phrase is hard and the reading is uncertain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["arasu","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1716","lb":"SO1716","mm":1,"ev":"The city is made ready for festival; the people give up their ordinary work.","cl":[],"ag":["நகர்மாந்தர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:74"},"so:1717":{"book":"soolamani","num":1715,"t":["சுண்ணநெய் யெழுபக லாடித் தொன்னகர்","நண்ணிய நானநீ ராடி நம்பியைப்","புண்ணியா வாசனை செய்து புங்கவன்","திண்ணிய வடிமலர்ச் சேடஞ் சேர்த்தினார்."],"p":"","r":"After days of bathing and fragrant oils, the people honour the child with a rite of merit and place on him the flower-remnant from the feet of the lord.","n":"Placing flowers from the feet of the 'excellent one' (புங்கவன்) on the infant is the Jain way of marking the child with the blessing of the Arhat, shown here as the first rite of his life.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","iraivan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1717","lb":"SO1717","mm":1,"ev":"The people perform the birth rites and lay the flowers from the god's feet on the child.","cl":[],"ag":["நகர்மாந்தர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:74"},"so:1718":{"book":"soolamani","num":1716,"t":["வழுவலி னாஞ்சிலான் வண்பொ னாழியான்","தழுமல ரலங்கலான் றாதை தானெனச்","செழுமல ரணிகுழற் றேவி மாரென","எழுபெருங் கிளைகளு மினிதி னீண்டினார்."],"p":"","r":"His father with the golden discus, the plough-bearing elder brother, and the queens and all seven great kinsfolk gather happily.","n":"The plough-bearer and the golden-discus lord are the two brothers of the story, now uncle and father of the newborn.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sutram","makkal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1718","lb":"SO1718","mm":1,"ev":"The father, the plough-bearer and the queens and kin gather around the child.","cl":[],"ag":["செங்கண் ணெடியான்","விசயன்","கிளைகள்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:74"},"so:1719":{"book":"soolamani","num":1717,"t":["எழுதரு பரிதியங் குழவி யேய்ப்பதோர்","தொழுதகை வடிவொடு நம்பி தோன்றலும்","தழுவினர் முயங்கினர் முயங்கித் தம்முளே","ஒழிவிலா வுவகைநீர்க் கடலுண் மூழ்கினார்."],"p":"","r":"When the child appears like the young sun with his hands joined, they all embrace him and bathe in a sea of unending joy.","n":"The young sun is the simile for the child, as the earlier verse made the sun take the child's form.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["makkal","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1719","lb":"SO1719","mm":1,"ev":"The kin embrace the child.","cl":[],"ag":["கிளைகள்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:74"},"so:1720":{"book":"soolamani","num":1718,"t":["அறத்தகை யந்தணர் குழுவு மாடல்வேன்","மறத்தகை மன்னவர் குழுவு மாநகர்த்","திறத்தகு முதியரு மீண்டிச் செல்வனைப்","பொறுத்தவர் பொலிவுரை புடைபொ ழிந்ததே."],"p":"","r":"Groups of virtuous brahmins, brave kings and the town's wise elders all gather and speak the praise of those who bore this lord.","n":"The praise falls on those who bore him, as much as on the child himself: the three orders of society speak for the parents.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pugazh","makkal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1720","lb":"SO1720","mm":1,"ev":"Brahmins, kings and elders gather and praise the child's parents.","cl":[],"ag":["குழுக்கள்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:74"},"so:1721":{"book":"soolamani","num":1719,"t":["குருமணிக் கோவையுங் குளிர்பொற் குன்றமும்","அருமணிக் கலங்களு மரத்த வாடையும்","புரிமணி வளநகர் புகுந்து கொள்கெனக்","கருமணி யொளியவன் கவரக் காட்டினான்."],"p":"","r":"The lord shows the heaps of jewelled strings, the golden hills, the precious ornaments and red cloth and invites them to take them into the city.","n":"The royal gift is given with the words 'enter the city and take', an open handing-out of wealth at the birth.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1721","lb":"SO1721","mm":1,"ev":"Tivittan lays out riches and offers them to all.","cl":[],"ag":["செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:74"},"so:1722":{"book":"soolamani","num":1720,"t":["திருவொடு திசைமுகந் தெளிர்ப்பத் தோன்றினான்","திருவொடு வென்றியிற் சேரு மாதலால்","திருவொடு திகழ்தர விசய னென்றரோ","திருவுடை மார்பனை நாமஞ் சேர்த்தினார்."],"p":"","r":"Because he shines with fortune and is joined to victory, they name the one with the fortune on his chest 'Vijayan', the victor.","n":"The child is named Vijayan, the same name as the elder brother Vijayan, the plough-bearer of 1718, so the name recurs in the next generation; the reason is the child's own fortune and victory, not the uncle's name.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pugazh","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1722","lb":"SO1722","mm":1,"ev":"The kin give the child the name Vijayan.","cl":[],"ag":["கிளைகள்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:74"},"so:1723":{"book":"soolamani","num":1721,"t":["விஞ்சைய ருலகிற்கும் விடுத்து மோகையென்","றஞ்சன வண்ணனங் கருளு மாயிடை","மஞ்சுடை விசும்பினின் றிழிந்து வந்தது","செஞ்சுட ருமிழ்வதோர் செம்பொன் மானமே."],"p":"","r":"After word of the joy has been sent to the Vidyadhara world as well and the dark-hued lord has given his leave, a red-gold sky-car comes down out of the cloudy heavens.","n":"The sense of lines 1-2 (what is sent, and what the dark-hued lord grants) is not fixed; ஓகை is taken as 'joy', the news of the birth in chapter 74.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["makkal","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1723","lb":"SO1723","mm":1,"ev":"A red-gold flying car descends from the sky to the lord's city.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:75"},"so:1724":{"book":"soolamani","num":1722,"t":["மணிநகு விமானமொன் றிழிந்து வந்துநம்","அணிநக ரணுகின தடிக ளென்றலும்","பணிவரை கொணர்மினீர் பாங்கி னென்றனன்","துணிநகு சுடரொளி துளும்பும் வேலினான்."],"p":"","r":"Seeing a gem-bright flying car come down and reach the city, the spear-bearing lord tells his people it is the lords' own visit and bids them bring what is due to the guests.","n":"Line 3 (பணிவரை கொணர்மினீர்) is hard; 'bring what is fit for service' is the reading taken.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["arasu","virundhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1724","lb":"SO1724","mm":1,"ev":"Tivittan sees the sky-car land and orders his men to bring the welcome.","cl":[],"ag":["செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ec":[],"spk":["செங்கண் ணெடியான்","மைந்தர்"],"ch":"so:75"},"so:1725":{"book":"soolamani","num":1723,"t":["மஞ்சுசூழ் மழைநுழை மானந் தன்னுளோர்","விஞ்சையர் மடந்தையர் விளங்கு மேனியர்","கஞ்சுகி யவரொடு மிழிந்து காவலன்","இஞ்சிசூழ் நகரணி யிருக்கை யெய்தினார்."],"p":"","r":"The Vidyadhara women, bright of body, come down with their chamberlains from the car that enters the rain-cloud, and reach the king's lodging in the walled city.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["virundhu","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1725","lb":"SO1725","mm":1,"ev":"The Vidyadhara women and their attendants alight and reach the palace.","cl":[],"ag":["விஞ்சையர் மடந்தையர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:75"},"so:1726":{"book":"soolamani","num":1724,"t":["பொலிகெனப் புரவலன் பொன்செய் நீண்முடி","மலிதரு நறுநெயம் மகளிர் பெய்தலுங்","கலிதரு கனைகட லன்ன காதலோ","டொலிதரு நகையொலி யுவந்தெ ழுந்ததே."],"p":"","r":"At the cry 'Let it flourish!' the women pour fragrant ghee over the long golden crown, and a great laugh of love rises loud as the roaring sea.","n":"Whose crown or head is anointed is not said; given the birth in chapter 74 it may be the child's, but the verse names only the king (புரவலன்) and the women.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["inbam","eegai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1726","lb":"SO1726","mm":1,"ev":"The women pour ghee at the lord's word and the hall breaks into joyful laughter.","cl":[],"ag":["விஞ்சையர் மடந்தையர்","செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:75"},"so:1727":{"book":"soolamani","num":1725,"t":["நாவிகா றழுவிமன் னறுநெய் யாடிய","பாவைமார் தங்களைப் பாவை கோயிலுக்","கேவியாங் கிருந்தபி னிறைவற் கின்னணம்","தேவிகோன் றமன்றொழு தொருவன் செப்பினான்."],"p":"","r":"After the ghee-anointed women are sent to the women's palace and the seated company settles, one man bows to the queen's lord and begins to tell him a tale.","n":"'பாவை கோயில்' is taken as the women's quarters, and 'யாங்கிருந்த' as the company's sitting; who 'we' are is not stated.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thoothu","virundhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1727","lb":"SO1727","mm":1,"ev":"A Vidyadhara messenger bows to Tivittan and begins his report.","cl":[],"ag":["ஒருவன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:75"},"so:1728":{"book":"soolamani","num":1726,"t":["எங்கள்கோ னெறிகதிர்ப் பெயர னீர்மலர்க்","கொங்குசே ரலங்கலான் குளிரத் தங்கினாள்","மங்குறோய் மணிவரை மன்னன் றன்மகள்","தொங்கல்சூழ் சுரிகுழற் சோதி மாலையே."],"p":"","r":"The messenger says that our lord, whose name is the shining sun, dwells in delight with the garlanded daughter of the mountain-king whose hair is like a dark cloud.","n":"'எறிகதிர்ப் பெயரன்' is taken as Arkakirti (the sun-named), the Vidyadhara prince of the earlier chapters; the wife is called சோதிமாலை, which may be a name or an epithet.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","sutram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1728","lb":"SO1728","mm":1,"ev":"The messenger names the Vidyadhara lord of the sun's name and his wife, daughter of the mountain king.","cl":[],"ag":["சோதிமாலை"],"ec":[],"spk":["ஒருவன்","செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ch":"so:75"},"so:1729":{"book":"soolamani","num":1727,"t":["மங்குல்வான் மழைகெழு மின்னின் மன்னவன்","தொங்கல்வாய் மடந்தைகண் டுயிலு மாயிடைக்","கங்குல்வாய்க் கதிர்மதி கவானின் மேலிருந்","தங்கண்மால் விசும்பக மலர்வித் திட்டதே."],"p":"","r":"One night while the king's wife sleeps the moon rises in the sky with its rays and opens the blossoms of the wide heavens.","n":"The rising of the moon over her sleep is the frame for her dream of the next verse, as the moon's entry is the frame of the dream in chapter 74.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kanavu","kaalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1729","lb":"SO1729","mm":1,"ev":"The moon rises over the sleeping queen.","cl":[],"ag":["சோதிமாலை"],"ec":[],"spk":["ஒருவன்","செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ch":"so:75"},"so:1730":{"book":"soolamani","num":1728,"t":["தெண்கதிர்த் திருமணி கனவிற் சேர்ந்தபின்","கண்கதிர்த் திளமுலை கால்ப ணைத்தன","தண்கதிர்த் தமனியப் பாவை போல்வதோர்","ஒண்கதிர்த் திருமக ளுருவ மெய்தினாள்."],"p":"","r":"After a cool gem has come to her in a dream, her breasts swell and she takes the form of a bright goddess like a gold figure.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kanavu","makkal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1730","lb":"SO1730","mm":1,"ev":"After her dream of the gem the queen shows the signs of pregnancy.","cl":[],"ag":["சோதிமாலை"],"ec":[],"spk":["ஒருவன்","செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ch":"so:75"},"so:1731":{"book":"soolamani","num":1729,"t":["வானிவர் மணிநகை விமான மேறவும்","கானிவர் கற்பகச் சோலை காணவும்","மானிவர் நோக்கினாள் வயாவி னாளது","தேனிவ ரலங்கலாய் தீர்க்கப் பட்டதே."],"p":"","r":"Climbing the gem-bright sky-car and seeing the wish-tree groves, the doe-eyed woman lets her pregnancy craving be satisfied.","n":"'வயா' is the craving of pregnancy; in the verse the satisfaction of the craving is told as a flight over the wish-tree groves.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1731","lb":"SO1731","mm":1,"ev":"The queen rides the sky-car through the heavenly groves and her craving is satisfied.","cl":[],"ag":["சோதிமாலை"],"ec":[],"spk":["ஒருவன்","செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ch":"so:75"},"so:1732":{"book":"soolamani","num":1730,"t":["மாணிக்க மரும்பிய வண்பொன் மாநிலத்","தாணிப்பொன் னனையவ ளனைய ளாயபின்","கோணிற்கும் விசும்பிடைக் குழகித் திங்களும்","நாணிப்போ முருவொடு நம்பி தோன்றினான்."],"p":"","r":"After she has become like a gold figure, a youth appears with a body so bright that even the moon in the bent sky would be shamed by it.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirappu","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1732","lb":"SO1732","mm":1,"ev":"The queen's son is born, radiant as a young moon.","cl":[],"ag":["அமிததேசன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["ஒருவன்","செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ch":"so:75"},"so:1733":{"book":"soolamani","num":1731,"t":["தேமரு செங்கழு நீரின் செவ்விதழ்","காமரு பவழவாய் கமழுங் கண்மலர்","தாமரை யகவிதழ் புரையுந் தானுமோர்","பூமரு தமனியக் குழவி போலுமே."],"p":"","r":"The child, with a red lily's petals, a coral mouth, eyes like fragrant flowers and a face like the inside of a lotus, is like a babe of gold in the flower.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","makkal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1733","lb":"SO1733","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["ஒருவன்","செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ch":"so:75"},"so:1734":{"book":"soolamani","num":1732,"t":["வானிடை மணிவிளக் கெரிந்த வண்டொடு","தேனுடை மலர்மழை சிதர்ந்த தவ்வழி","மீனுடை விரிதிரை வெண்சங் கார்த்தன","தானுடை யொளிதிசை தவழ்ந்தெ ழுந்ததே."],"p":"","r":"As the boy appears, gem lamps burn in the sky, flower rain falls with bees, conches sound like the sea's waves, and light spreads in every direction.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirappu","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1734","lb":"SO1734","mm":1,"ev":"Heavenly signs accompany the child's birth: lamps, a rain of flowers, conches and light.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["ஒருவன்","செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ch":"so:75"},"so:1735":{"book":"soolamani","num":1733,"t":["அளப்பருந் திறலுடை யரசர் தொல்குடை","அளப்பருந் திறலினோ டலரத் தோன்றினான்","அளப்பருந் திறலின னமித தேசனென்","றளப்பருந் திறற்பெய ரமரக் கூறினார்."],"p":"","r":"Sovereign kings of boundless strength had their old white umbrellas wilt as he rose with boundless strength, and they gave him a name that says that very might: Amitatejas.","n":"The name 'அளப்பருந் திறலினன் அமித தேசன்' is repeated word for word in each line to make the meaning, 'of unmeasurable might', plain; who gives it is left to 'they'.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pugazh","perumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1735","lb":"SO1735","mm":1,"ev":"The boy is named Amitatejas ('boundless might').","cl":[],"ag":["அமிததேசன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["ஒருவன்","செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ch":"so:75"},"so:1736":{"book":"soolamani","num":1734,"t":["ஐயன தழகுகண் பருக வவ்வழி மையணி","மழைமுகில் வண்ணன் மாமனார்","வையக முடையவற் குணர்த்தி வாவென","நெய்யொடு வந்தன னிலைமை யின்னதே."],"p":"","r":"The speaker ends his report: the dark-cloud-hued uncle, to let the lord's beauty be drunk by the eyes, said 'tell the world's master and come', and so he has come with ghee; such is the matter.","n":"Who is meant by மாமனார் (the child's uncle) and by 'the master of the world' is not clear; the text says only that a message of 'come' was sent. Line 1 carries a repaired pulli.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thoothu","sutram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1736","lb":"SO1736","mm":1,"ev":"The messenger finishes his report and states that he came bringing ghee.","cl":[],"ag":["ஒருவன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["ஒருவன்","செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ch":"so:75"},"so:1737":{"book":"soolamani","num":1735,"t":["என்றவன் மொழிதலு மிலங்கு நேமியான்","நின்றகஞ் சுடர்தரு நிதியி னீத்தமங்","கன்றவற் கருளின னரச செல்வமோ","டொன்றின னுவந்துதன் னுலக மெய்தினான்."],"p":"","r":"On hearing him the lord of the discus gives him treasure shining in the vault, joins him to royal wealth, and the messenger goes happily to his own world.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1737","lb":"SO1737","mm":1,"ev":"Tivittan rewards the messenger with treasure and he returns to the Vidyadhara world.","cl":[],"ag":["செங்கண் ணெடியான்","ஒருவன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:75"},"so:1738":{"book":"soolamani","num":1736,"t":["விண்டா ரில்லா வெந்திற லோன்பொற் சுடராழித்","தண்டார் மார்பன் றன்மக னன்மா மணியேபோல்","கண்டார் கண்களி கூருஞ் செல்வக் கவினெய்தி","வண்டா ரைம்பான் மங்கையர் காப்ப வளர்கின்றான்."],"p":"","r":"Tivittan's son, the child of the foe-less lord of the golden discus, grows with a beauty that makes onlookers glad, guarded by women with hair full of bees.","n":"After the news of Amitatejas the verse returns to Tivittan's own son (called the lord's child, not by name here); the earlier chapter names him Vijayan.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["makkal","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1738","lb":"SO1738","mm":1,"ev":"Tivittan's son grows up in the care of his nurses.","cl":[],"ag":["நம்பி"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:75"},"so:1739":{"book":"soolamani","num":1737,"t":["கண்கவர் சோதிக் காமரு தெய்வம் பலகாப்பத்","தண்கமழ் போதிற் றாமரை யாளுந் தகைவாழ்த்த","விண்கவர் சோதித் தண்கதி ரோன்போல் விரிவெய்தி","மண்கவர் சோதித் தண்கதிர் வண்ணன் வளர்கின்றான்."],"p":"","r":"Guarded by many shining gods and blessed by the lotus lady, he grows like the cool-rayed sun in the sky, a lord of cool light over the earth.","n":"The 'many gods' who guard him are the poem's own picture of a fortunate birth; the verse does not link them to any school.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["makkal","iraivan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1739","lb":"SO1739","mm":1,"ev":"The child grows under the care of gods and the goddess of the lotus.","cl":[],"ag":["நம்பி"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:75"},"so:1740":{"book":"soolamani","num":1738,"t":["செம்பொற் கோவைக் கிண்கிணி யேங்கத் திலகஞ்சேர்","அம்பொற் கோவைப் பன்மணி மின்னிட் டரைசூழப்","பைம்பொற் கோவைப் பாடக மென்சீ றடிநல்லார்","தம்பொற் கோவைப் பூண்முலை முன்றிற் றவழ்கின்றான்."],"p":"","r":"With golden-corded anklets ringing, gem strings shining at his waist and golden bells on his feet, he crawls about the courtyards of the women who wear gold-corded jewels.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["makkal","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1740","lb":"SO1740","mm":1,"ev":"The boy crawls about the women's courtyards in his jewels.","cl":[],"ag":["நம்பி"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:75"},"so:1741":{"book":"soolamani","num":1739,"t":["போதார் பொய்கைப் போதவிழ் பொற்றா மரைகாட்டி","மாதார் சாயன் மங்கையர் கூவ மகிழ்வெய்திக்","காதார் செம்பொற் றாழ்குழை மின்னின் கதிர்வீசத்","தாதார் பூவின் றண்டவி சேறித் தவழ்கின்றான்."],"p":"","r":"Shown the golden lotuses of the pond and called by women of gentle form, he is glad, and his ear-rings flash like lightning as he creeps through the pollen-laden gardens.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["makkal","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1741","lb":"SO1741","mm":1,"ev":"The boy plays among the garden pools while women call him.","cl":[],"ag":["நம்பி"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:75"},"so:1742":{"book":"soolamani","num":1740,"t":["கண்ணின் செல்வங் கண்டவர் கண்டே மனம்விம்ம","மண்ணின் செல்வம் வைகலும் வைகன் மகிழ்வெய்தி","விண்ணின் செல்வச் செங்கதி ரோன்போல் விளையாடித்","தண்ணென் செல்கைப் பொன்னுருள் வாங்கித் தளர்கின்றான்."],"p":"","r":"The ones who see him see the wealth of eyes, the earth's wealth grows with his joy each day, and like the red sun of the heavens he plays and tires, pulling the little golden wheel.","n":"Three kinds of wealth (of the eyes, of the earth, of the sky) are used for the child, as one fortune seen from three sides.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["makkal","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1742","lb":"SO1742","mm":1,"ev":"The boy plays with a golden wheel-toy until he is tired.","cl":[],"ag":["நம்பி"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:75"},"so:1743":{"book":"soolamani","num":1741,"t":["ஐயாண் டெல்லை யையன ணைந்தா னவனோடு","மையா ரின்பக் காதலி நாவின் மகளாகப்","பொய்யாக் கல்விச் செல்வர்க டம்மாற் புணர்வித்தான்","நெய்யார் செவ்வே னீளொளி நேமிப் படையானே."],"p":"","r":"At the age of five the boy is brought to teachers rich in unfailing learning, so that the lord of the bright discus unites him with them.","n":"'காதலி நாவின் மகளாக' (line 2) is hard; it may mean the boy is joined to learning as to a loved one's own daughter. The reading here takes only the sure part: teachers and the boy at five.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kalvi","makkal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1743","lb":"SO1743","mm":1,"ev":"Tivittan hands his five-year-old son to learned teachers.","cl":[],"ag":["செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:75"},"so:1744":{"book":"soolamani","num":1742,"t":["காமச் செல்வ னென்றுல கெல்லாங் களிதூங்கும்","ஏமச் செல்வ நம்பியொ டின்னு மிளையாகச்","சேமச் செல்வன் றேவி பயந்தா டிசையெல்லாம்","ஓமச் செல்வங் கொண்டினி தேத்து மொளியாளே."],"p":"","r":"The queen of the guardian lord bears a girl, younger than the boy, whom all the world calls the god of love's wealth and who rises praised in every direction like the fire-offering's wealth.","n":"Lines 1-2 are hard: 'காமச் செல்வன்' may name the boy, or praise the girl, who is the one 'ஒளியாள்' who is praised.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["makkal","perumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1744","lb":"SO1744","mm":1,"ev":"Tivittan's queen gives birth to a daughter younger than her son.","cl":[],"ag":["தேவிகை"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:75"},"so:1745":{"book":"soolamani","num":1743,"t":["பாரார் செல்கைப் பல்கிளை யெல்லா முடனீண்டிப்","பேரா வென்றிக் கொன்றிய வாறு பெயரிட்டுச்","சீரா ரோகை விஞ்சையர் சேணி செலவிட்டுக்","காரார் வண்ணன் காதலொ டின்பக் கடலாழ்ந்தான்."],"p":"","r":"The assembled kin, the Vidyadhara kinsfolk among them, name her in keeping with victory, are sent back to the Vidyadhara ranks, and the dark-hued lord sinks happily in a sea of love and joy.","n":"The name given, 'பெயரிட்டு' with 'கொன்றிய வாறு', is not given in the verse and is hard to construe; 'in keeping with victory' is a guess.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sutram","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1745","lb":"SO1745","mm":1,"ev":"The kin gather and give a name; the Vidyadhara guests leave; Tivittan is full of joy.","cl":[],"ag":["கிளைகள்","செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:75"},"so:1746":{"book":"soolamani","num":1744,"t":["ஐயன் றானு மவ்வகை யாலே வளர் வெய்த","மையுண் கண்ணி மாபெருந்தேவி மகிழ் தூங்கத்","தெய்வம் பேணிப் பெற்றனர் பேணுந் திருவேபோல்","மெய்யின் சோதி சூழொளி மின்னின் பெயராளும்."],"p":"","r":"So the boy grows, the great queen is full of joy, and the girl, whom they obtained by worshipping the gods like fortune itself that the people worship, has a name of lightning for her body's light.","n":"'மின்னின் பெயராளும்' ends the verse with her name as the name of lightning; the name is not spelled out and a later chapter's heroine may be this girl or another.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["makkal","pirappu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1746","lb":"SO1746","mm":1,"ev":"The boy and the girl grow up; the queen rejoices.","cl":[],"ag":["தேவிகை"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:75"},"so:1747":{"book":"soolamani","num":1745,"t":["தேதா வென்றே தேனொடு வண்டு திசைபாடும்","போதார் சாயற் பூங்கொடி போலப் பொலிவெய்தித்","தாதார் கோதைத் தாயரொ டாயம் புடைசூழ","மாதார் சாயன் மாமயி லன்னாள் வளர்கின்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"Bees hum to the flowers and the girl, slender as a flowering creeper, grows up like a great peahen among her mothers and playmates.","n":"The girl is not named in the verse; she is Tivittan's daughter only from the next verses (1754 calls her the dark-hued one's daughter). Bees and flowers surround her as they do a creeper.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ilamai","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1747","lb":"SO1747","mm":1,"ev":"The princess grows up amid her mothers and companions.","cl":[],"ag":["மங்கை","தாயர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:76"},"so:1748":{"book":"soolamani","num":1746,"t":["மழலைக் கனிவாய் மணிவண்டு வருடி மருங்கு பாராட்ட","அழனக் கலர்ந்த வரவிந்த வமளி சேர்ந்த விளவன்னம்","கழனிச் செந்நெற் கதிரென்னுங் கவரி வீசக் கண்படுக்கும்","பழனக் குவளை நீர்நாடன் பாவை வார்த்தை பகருற்றேன்."],"p":"","r":"After a picture of the watery land, with bees, lotus beds, a resting swan and paddy ears like fans, the poet says he now begins the tale of the daughter of that land's lord.","n":"The land and the girl are paired through the first-person பகருற்றேன் (I begin to tell); the picture of the countryside serves as a frame for her. Printed in 8 half-lines and joined by the build.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naadu","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1748","lb":"SO1748","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:76"},"so:1749":{"book":"soolamani","num":1747,"t":["செம்பொற் சிலம்புங் கிண்கிணியுஞ் செல்வச் செஞ்சீ றடிபோற்ற","வம்பத் துகிலின் வடஞ்சூழ்ந்த வல்குன் மணிமே கலை மருட்ட","அம்பொற் சுருளை யிருபாலு மளக வல்லி யருகிலங்கப்","பைம்பொற் சுடிகை நிழறுளங்கப் படர்ந்தா டாயம் படிந்தாளே."],"p":"","r":"The girl, adorned with gold anklets, tiny bells, a fresh silk wrap, a gem girdle and hair curls and head ornaments, joins the crowd of her playmates.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","ilamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1749","lb":"SO1749","mm":1,"ev":"The girl, dressed and ornamented, goes to join her playmates.","cl":[],"ag":["மங்கை"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:76"},"so:1750":{"book":"soolamani","num":1748,"t":["நங்கை நல்லார் பாராட்ட நகையாட் டாயம் புகலோடு","மங்கை மடவார் பந்தாடன் மயங்கி யாடன் மணிநிலத்துக்","கொங்கை சேர்ந்த குங்குமத்தின் குழம்புங் கோதை கொய்தாதும்","அங்க ராகத் துகளும்பாய்ந் தந்தி வான மடைந்ததுவே."],"p":"","r":"The young women's ball-play scatters saffron paste, garland pollen and body powder, so that the air turns red like the evening sky.","n":"The evening-red sky is made by the cosmetics of the players themselves; the game is shown as beauty at its most active.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1750","lb":"SO1750","mm":1,"ev":"The women play ball and the dust of their ornaments reddens the sky.","cl":[],"ag":["ஆயம்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:76"},"so:1751":{"book":"soolamani","num":1749,"t":["காவி நாணுங் கண்ணார்தங் கையி னேந்துங் கந்துகங்கள்","ஆவி தாமு முடையனபோ லடிக்குந் தோறு மடங்காது","பூவி னார்ந்த மணிநிலத்துப் பொங்கி யெழுந்து பொன்னேந்தி","நாவி நாறு மிளங்கொங்கைத் தடங்கள் சென்று நணுகியவே."],"p":"","r":"The balls in the girls' hands rebound as if alive and, however they are struck, fly up from the jewelled floor and settle on the scented breasts.","n":"The verse gives the balls a will of their own (ஆவி உடையன போல்), which the next verse turns into a simile of lovers.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1751","lb":"SO1751","mm":1,"ev":"The balls struck by the women spring back and land on their breasts.","cl":[],"ag":["ஆயம்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:76"},"so:1752":{"book":"soolamani","num":1750,"t":["கரிய குழலும் பொற்றோடுஞ் செய்ய வாயுங் கதிர்முறுவல்","மரிய திசையு மதிமயங்கு மம்பொன் முகத்து மடவார்கள்","திரியத் தம்மைப் புடைத்தாலுஞ் சென்று சேர்ந்து திளைக்குமால்","அரிய செய்யுங் காமுகர்போ லளிய வந்தோ வடங்காவே."],"p":"","r":"Even when the women strike them away, the balls return to cling to them, like lovers who do harsh things and still will not be restrained.","n":"Ball-play is read as courtship: the ball plays the lover who is driven off and returns. The comparison is the poet's and does not judge the lover.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1752","lb":"SO1752","mm":1,"ev":"The women strike the balls and the balls keep returning to them.","cl":[],"ag":["ஆயம்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:76"},"so:1753":{"book":"soolamani","num":1751,"t":["செம்பொற் சுருளை மெல்விரலாற் றிருத்திச் செறிந்த தேரல்குல்","வம்பத் துகிலின் வடஞ்சூழ்ந்து மணிமே கலையுந் தானேற்றி","அம்பொற் குரும்பை மென்முலைமே லணிந்த பொன்ஞா ணருகொடுக்கிப்","பைம்பொற் றிலத நுதலொதுக்கிப் பாவை பந்து கைக்கொண்டாள்."],"p":"","r":"The girl tidies her curls, ties her silk wrap and gem girdle, tucks in her gold waist-string, sets her forehead mark straight and takes up the ball.","n":"She makes herself ready with her own hands before she plays; the same silk and gem girdle dress a bride in 1538, which gives the game a look of rehearsal.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","ilamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1753","lb":"SO1753","mm":1,"ev":"The princess dresses herself for play and takes up the ball.","cl":[],"ag":["மங்கை"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:76"},"so:1754":{"book":"soolamani","num":1752,"t":["கந்தாடு மாலியானைக் கார்வண்ணன் பாவை கருமேகக் குழன்மடவார் கைசோர்ந்து நிற்பக்","கொந்தாடும் பூங்குழலுங் கோதைகளு மாடக் கொய்பொலந் துகிலசைத்த கொய்சகந் தாழ்ந்தாட","வந்தாடுந் தேனுமுரல் வரிவண்டு மாடமணி வடமும் பொன்ஞாணும் வார்முலைமே லாடப்","பந்தாடு மாடேதன் படைநெடுங்க ணாடப் பணைமென்றோ ணின்றாடப் பந்தாடு கின்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"Dark-hued Tivittan's daughter plays ball while her attendants stand with weary hands, her flower-hair, garlands, silk folds, girdle, eyes and arms all swaying with the game.","n":"The verse names her father by the epithet கார்வண்ணன் (the dark-hued one, Tivittan by the earlier chapters), the first time here that she is placed as his daughter.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","ilamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1754","lb":"SO1754","mm":1,"ev":"The princess plays ball while her attendants stand tired beside her.","cl":[],"ag":["மங்கை","ஆயம்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:76"},"so:1755":{"book":"soolamani","num":1753,"t":["கந்துகங்கள் கைத்தலத்தா லேறுண்டு பொங்கிக் கருங்கண்ணுந் தாமுமுறக் கலந்தெழுந்த போழ்தின்","வந்தனவுஞ் சென்றனவும் வானத்தின் மேலு மணிநிலத்து மீதுநெறி மறிகுவன வாகி","அந்துகிலி னிடைத்தோயு மகலல்கு றீண்டு மணிமருங்கு சூழுமணியார் வடமுந் தாக்கும்","கொந்தவிழும் பூங்குழ்லுங் கோதைகளு மூழ்குங் குவளை வாட் கண்ணிவருங் குறிப்பறிய மாட்டாள்."],"p":"","r":"The balls rise and fall about the sky, the jewelled floor, her silk, girdle and breasts, and the lily-eyed girl, absorbed in the game, cannot make out their coming.","n":"The last clause, குறிப்பறிய மாட்டாள், can be read as the girl failing to follow where the balls go or failing to read their drift; the second reading would hint at what the game means. Line 4 prints பூங்குழ்லும் for பூங்குழலும்.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1755","lb":"SO1755","mm":1,"ev":"The princess plays on among the rising and falling balls.","cl":[],"ag":["மங்கை"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:76"},"so:1756":{"book":"soolamani","num":1754,"t":["நறுமாலை வந்தலைப்ப நன்மேனி நோமா னங்காயிப் பந்தாட னன்றன்றா மென்பார்","இறுமாலிம் மின்மருங்கு லென்பாவ மென்பார் இளமுலைமே லேர்வடம்வந் தூன்றுமா லென்பார்","செறுமாலிங் கிவைகாணிற் றேவிதா னென்பார் செங்கண்மால் காணுமேற் சீறானோ வென்பார்","பெறுமாறு தாயருந் தோழியரு நின்று பிணையனா டன்மேற் பன்மொழி மிழற்றுகின்றார்."],"p":"","r":"Her mothers and friends stand about her and say that ball-play is no good for her tender body, that her waist will break and her breasts be pressed by her pearl strings, and that the queen or the red-eyed lord would be angry to see it.","n":"Their worry is for her body and for the anger of her parents (தேவி, செங்கண்மால்), not a rule of conduct; nothing in the verse says the girl stops.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["makkal","anbu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1756","lb":"SO1756","mm":1,"ev":"Her mothers and friends protest that ball-play is not fit for her.","cl":[],"ag":["தாயருந் தோழியரு"],"ec":[],"spk":["தாயருந் தோழியரு","மங்கை"],"ch":"so:76"},"so:1757":{"book":"soolamani","num":1755,"t":["நீராலிக் கட்டி நிரந்தெழுந்து பொங்கி நிழறயங்கும் பொன்னறைமே னின்றாடுகின்ற","காராலி மஞ்ஞை களிசிறந்தாற் போலக் கருங்குழலி பந்தாடல் காதலித்த போழ்தில்","சீராலி மால்வண்ணன் றேவியுந் தானும் செவ்வரத்த நுண்ணெழினி சேர்ந்தொருங்கு நோக்கி","வாராலி மென்கொங்கை மையரிக்கண் மாதர் வருந்தினா ணங்கையினி வருகவீங் கென்றார்."],"p":"","r":"The dark-hued lord and the queen watch from behind a fine red curtain as she plays like a peahen on a gold floor, and, seeing her tired, call her to come to them.","n":"The speech is given jointly to the two parents, who watch unseen behind the curtain. Line 6 had a pulli repaired and the stanza was printed in 8 half-lines.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["makkal","anbu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1757","lb":"SO1757","mm":1,"ev":"The king and queen watch their daughter play and call her to them because she is tired.","cl":[],"ag":["செங்கண் ணெடியான்","தேவி"],"ec":[],"spk":["மால்வண்ணன் தேவியும் தானும்","மங்கை"],"ch":"so:76"},"so:1758":{"book":"soolamani","num":1756,"t":["அருமணி முடியவ னருளி தென்றலும்","பருமணிப் பந்துகை விட்டுப் பாவைதன்","புரிமணிக் குழல்புறந் தாழப் போந்தரோ","கருமணி யொளியவன் கழல்சென் றெய்தினாள்."],"p":"","r":"At the crowned king's gracious word she lets go of her jewelled ball and, her hair streaming behind, comes and reaches the feet of the dark-lustred one.","n":"The words அருளிது என்றலும் can be taken as the king's call or as a general phrase of grace; 1757 gives the call. அருமணி முடியவன் and கருமணி ஒளியவன் are read as the same man, Tivittan.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["makkal","anbu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1758","lb":"SO1758","mm":1,"ev":"At her father's call the princess leaves the game and goes to his feet.","cl":[],"ag":["மங்கை","செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ec":[],"spk":["செங்கண் ணெடியான்","மங்கை"],"ch":"so:76"},"so:1759":{"book":"soolamani","num":1757,"t":["மங்கையை வலப்புடைக் குறங்கின் மேலிரீஇ","அங்கையா லணிநுத லரும்பு நீர்துடைத்","தெங்குமி லுவகையோ டினிதி ருந்தபின்","நங்கைத னலங்கிளர் மேனி நோக்கினான்."],"p":"","r":"He seats her on his right thigh, wipes the sweat from her brow with his palm and sits in quiet gladness, then looks over her blooming body.","n":"The father's tenderness and his appraising look sit in one verse: the sweat he wipes comes from the ball-play, and the look prepares the decision of 1762.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["makkal","anbu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1759","lb":"SO1759","mm":1,"ev":"Tivittan seats his daughter on his lap, wipes her brow and looks at her.","cl":[],"ag":["செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:76"},"so:1760":{"book":"soolamani","num":1758,"t":["இளமையா லெழுதரு மிணைமென் கொங்கையின்","வளமையாற் பொலிதரும் வனப்பின் மாட்சியால்","குளமையா னறவிரி குவளைக் கண்ணியான்","உளமயா வுயிர்ப்பதோ ருவகை யெய்தினான்."],"p":"","r":"Seeing her young breasts, her richness of form and the beauty of her figure, he feels a joy that makes his heart sigh.","n":"உளமயா உயிர்ப்பதோர் உவகை can be read as joy that makes the heart sigh, a mixture of pride and anxiety at her ripeness, or as a plain sigh of gladness; the verse does not say a marriage is in his mind, but 1762 follows.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["makkal","ilamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1760","lb":"SO1760","mm":1,"ev":"The lily-garlanded father sees that his daughter has come of age.","cl":[],"ag":["செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:76"},"so:1761":{"book":"soolamani","num":1759,"t":["செல்வியைத் திருக்குழ றிருத்தித் தேவிதன்","அல்குன்மே லினிதினங் கிருவி யாயிடை","மல்குபூ மந்திர சாலை மண்டபம்","பில்குபூந் தெரியலான் பெயர்ந்து போயினான்."],"p":"","r":"He arranges her fine hair, sets her happily on the queen's lap and goes off to the council-hall of the palace.","n":"The mantra-sala (மந்திர சாலை மண்டபம்) is a room for counsel, not for ritual; the next verse shows what he does there.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["makkal","anbu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1761","lb":"SO1761","mm":1,"ev":"Tivittan sets his daughter in the queen's lap and goes to the council hall.","cl":[],"ag":["செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:76"},"so:1762":{"book":"soolamani","num":1760,"t":["அருத்தநூ லவரொடு மாய்ந்து மற்றவர்","கருத்தொடு பொருந்திய கருமச் சூழ்ச்சியான்","திருத்தகு சயம்வர முரசந் திண்களிற்","றெருத்தின்மே லறைகென விறைவ னேயினான்."],"p":"","r":"After counsel with men of the science of wealth, whose view agrees with his own, the king commands that the swayamvara drum be struck from an elephant's neck.","n":"The decision is taken in council, with the ministers named by their learning (அருத்தநூல், the policy texts), and not by the father alone.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["soozhchi","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1762","lb":"SO1762","mm":1,"ev":"Tivittan takes counsel with his ministers and orders the swayamvara drum proclaimed.","cl":[["a king settles a weighty matter after counsel with those who know the science of policy","general","asserted",2,"narrator"]],"ag":["செங்கண் ணெடியான்","அருத்தநூலவர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:76"},"so:1763":{"book":"soolamani","num":1761,"t":["வாலிய சந்தமென் சேறு மட்டித்துப்","பீலியந் தழையொடு பிணையல் வேய்ந்தன","பாலியல் பலிபெறு முரசம் பன்மையில்","ஆலியங் கதிர்கொள வதிர்ந்த றைந்தவே."],"p":"","r":"The drum, smeared with white sandal paste and hung with peacock-feather garlands, is struck with a roar like hail.","n":"This is the drum that receives offerings (பலிபெறு முரசம்); its sound announces the wedding to everyone.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","kaalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1763","lb":"SO1763","mm":1,"ev":"The swayamvara drum is struck.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:76"},"so:1764":{"book":"soolamani","num":1762,"t":["வாழ்கநம் மன்னவன் வாழ்க வையகம்","ஆழ்கநம் மரும்பகை யலர்க நல்லறம்","வீழ்கதண் புனல்பயிர் விளைக மாநிலம்","தாழ்கமற் றருந்துயர் சாற்றக் கேண்மினே."],"p":"","r":"A herald calls for the king's long life, the earth's welfare, enemies sunk, virtue spreading, cool rain, ripe crops and grief brought low, and bids the people hear the proclamation.","n":"The blessing runs from the king through the country's rain and harvest and its virtue; the ruler stands at the head of every wish, as in the Kural's picture of just rule.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aram","mazhai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1764","lb":"SO1764","mm":1,"ev":"The herald opens the proclamation with blessings on the king and land.","cl":[],"ag":["முரசறைவோர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["முரசறைவோர்","நகரார்"],"ch":"so:76"},"so:1765":{"book":"soolamani","num":1763,"t":["புள்ளணி வார்பொழிற் பொன்செய் மாநகர்","உள்ளணி பரப்புமி னுயர்மின் றோரணம்","வெள்ளணி விரும்புமின் விருந்து போற்றுமின்","கள்ளணி மலரொடு கலங்கள் பெய்ம்மினே."],"p":"","r":"The herald bids the people decorate the city of the forest-ringed gold town, raise festival arches, welcome white festival dress and honour guests, and put flowers and vessels in order.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["virundhu","naadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1765","lb":"SO1765","mm":1,"ev":"The herald orders the city decorated and guests honoured.","cl":[],"ag":["முரசறைவோர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["முரசறைவோர்","நகரார்"],"ch":"so:76"},"so:1766":{"book":"soolamani","num":1764,"t":["இன்றைநா ளுள்ளுறுத் தீரைஞ் ஞாள்களும்","மன்றலஞ் சயமரம் வரைந்த தாதலால்","ஒன்றிவா ழரசரோ டுலக மீண்டுக","வென்றுதா னிடிமுர சறைந்த தென்பவே."],"p":"","r":"The thunderous drum is said to have declared that the swayamvara is fixed for ten days from today and that the world should gather with the kings who live in concord.","n":"ஈரைஞ் ஞாள் is read as ten days counting today; the verse closes with என்பவே (they say), so the narrator reports the proclamation at a remove.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","kaalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1766","lb":"SO1766","mm":1,"ev":"The drum announces the swayamvara for ten days later and summons the kings.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:76"},"so:1767":{"book":"soolamani","num":1765,"t":["கொடிபடு நெடுநகர்க் கோயில் வீதிவாய்","இடிபடு மழைமுகி லென்ன வின்னணம்","கடிபடு முரசுகண் ணதிர்ந்த காரென","மடிபடு மாடவாய் மயில்கண் மான்றவே."],"p":"","r":"The drum thunders through the royal city's streets like a cloud-burst, and the peacocks in the mansions, taking it for rain, are bewildered.","n":"Peacocks that mistake the drum for rain-clouds belong to the poet's stock of images; the whole city is shown shaken by the news.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","naadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1767","lb":"SO1767","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:76"},"so:1768":{"book":"soolamani","num":1766,"t":["முர்சொடு வரிவளை மூரித் தானையோ","டரசரு மரசரல் லாரு மாயிடைத்","திரைசெறி கனைகடல் சென்று தேர்த்தனெப்","புரைசெறி புரிசையின் புறணி முற்றினார்."],"p":"","r":"With drums, conches and heavy armies, kings and those who are not kings pour like the sea round the city and fill the outer ring of its wall.","n":"The line names both kings and non-kings (அரசரல்லாரும்), so the crowd is more than suitors: those who come to watch are counted too.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1768","lb":"SO1768","mm":1,"ev":"Kings and others arrive in force and surround the city walls.","cl":[],"ag":["அரசர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:76"},"so:1769":{"book":"soolamani","num":1767,"t":["வெண்மலைச் சென்னிமேல் விஞ்சை வேந்தரும்","கண்மலைத் திழிதருங் கடலந் தானையர்","விண்மலைத் திழிதரும் விளங்கு சோதியர்","எண்மலைச் சிலம்பிடை யிறைகொண் டீண்டினார்."],"p":"","r":"The Vidyadhara kings from the silver mountain, with sea-like armies and a blinding radiance, come and camp among the eight mountains.","n":"எண்மலைச் சிலம்பு (the slopes of the eight mountains) is hard to place; the verse likely means the camp-ground outside the city.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["arasu","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1769","lb":"SO1769","mm":1,"ev":"The Vidyadhara kings arrive with their armies and encamp.","cl":[],"ag":["விஞ்சை வேந்தர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:76"},"so:1770":{"book":"soolamani","num":1768,"t":["அவ்வரை யரைசர்கோ னருக்கன் றன்மகன்","செவ்வரை யனையதோட் செல்வன் றன்னொடும்","மைவரை நெடுங்கணம் மடந்தை தன்னொடும்","இவ்வரை யரைசெதிர் கொள்ள வெய்தினான்."],"p":"","r":"The son of Arkkan, who is lord of that mountain's kings, comes with a lady with dark long eyes while the ruler of this mountain goes out to greet him.","n":"The verse does not give his name. Who receives him (இவ்வரை அரைசு) is not stated: it may be Tivittan or a kinsman of the city. Line 4 prints அரைசெதிர் for அரைசு எதிர்.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["arasu","virundhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1770","lb":"SO1770","mm":1,"ev":"Arkkan's son arrives with his lady and is received by the local ruler.","cl":[],"ag":["அருக்கன் மகன்","அரைசு"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:76"},"so:1771":{"book":"soolamani","num":1769,"t":["பொன்னகர்ப் புறத்ததோர் புரிசை வார்பொழில்","தன்னகத் தியற்றிய தயங்கு பொன்னகர்","மன்னர்கட் கிறைவன்வந் திருப்ப மண்மிசை","இந்நகர்க் கிறைவனு மெதிர்கொண் டெய்தினான்."],"p":"","r":"In the golden city a walled grove-garden holds a shining hall; the lord of kings sits there and the city's own lord comes to receive him.","n":"The two lords are named only by their rank (the lord of kings; the lord of this city), so who is who rests on the earlier chapters.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["arasu","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1771","lb":"SO1771","mm":1,"ev":"The city's lord goes out to receive the overlord of kings who has come to sit in the hall.","cl":[],"ag":["மன்னர்கட் கிறைவன்","பதிநகர்க் கிறைவன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:77"},"so:1772":{"book":"soolamani","num":1770,"t":["கண்சுட ரிலங்குவேற் காள வண்ணனும்","வெண்சுட ரொளியவன் றானும் விஞ்சையர்","தண்சுடர்த் தமனிய வண்ணன் றன்னொடும்","மண்சுட ருறுப்பதோர் வகைய ராயினார்."],"p":"","r":"The dark-hued lord with the bright spear, the white-gleaming one, and the Vidyadhara of cool golden hue together make the earth blaze with their light.","n":"The three are given only by colour and kind (dark, white, a Vidyadhara of gold), so naming them rests on the story so far.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["arasu","perumai","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1772","lb":"SO1772","mm":1,"ev":"Three lords arrive, each known by the hue of his body, and their brightness lights the ground.","cl":[],"ag":["செங்கண் ணெடியான்","வெண்சுடர் ஒளியவன்","தமனிய வண்ணன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:77"},"so:1773":{"book":"soolamani","num":1771,"t":["இருபுடைக் கிளைகளும் விரவி யின்னணம்","தெருவுடைத் திசைமுகந் தெளிப்பத் தேர்த்தரோ","மருவுடை மகரநீர் வளாகம் வானவர்","உருவுடை யுலகம்வந் திழிந்த தொத்ததே."],"p":"","r":"As streets, courts and the rest fill on both sides, the sea-girt earth looks as if the world of the gods had come down.","n":"The simile makes the city a heaven set on earth, which prepares the pomp of the kings who arrive.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1773","lb":"SO1773","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:77"},"so:1774":{"book":"soolamani","num":1772,"t":["சிகைமணி யழுத்திய செம்பொற் சென்னிய","நகைமணிக் கோபுர வாயி னான்கொடு","வகைமணித் தலத்ததோர் மதலை மாளிகை","தொகைமணித் தொழில்பல தொடரத் தோற்றினார்."],"p":"","r":"A pillared mansion with golden-crowned jewelled gate-towers and four doors shows fine jewel-work on every side.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1774","lb":"SO1774","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:77"},"so:1775":{"book":"soolamani","num":1773,"t":["பளிங்கியல் பலகையும் பவழத் தூண்களும்","விளங்குபொற் கலங்களும் வெள்ளி வேயுளும்","இளங்கதிர் முத்தமு மியற்றி யின்னணம்","வளங்கவின் றனையதம் மதலை மாடமே."],"p":"","r":"Crystal slabs, coral pillars, golden vessels, silver bamboo-work and pearls of young light make the hall bright.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1775","lb":"SO1775","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:77"},"so:1776":{"book":"soolamani","num":1774,"t":["மீன்முக விசும்பிடை விரிந்த வெண்ணிலாப்","பான்முகந் தொகுப்பன பனிக்கும் வேதிகை","மேன்முகந் திருத்திய வெள்ளி முன்றிலான்","நான்முக மருங்கினு நகுவ தொக்குமே."],"p":"","r":"The white court of the hall, with its altars chilling the moonlight, seems to laugh at the four-faced Brahma's quarters.","n":"The poet sets the hall beside the Creator's own realm and says it laughs at it, a boast of the hall's worth.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","perumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1776","lb":"SO1776","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:77"},"so:1777":{"book":"soolamani","num":1775,"t":["அங்கதற் கைந்துகோ லளவி னாடரங்","கிங்குவந் திறுத்தன வென்னு மீட்டன","செங்கதிர்ப் பவழக்கா னிரைத்த செம்பொனான்","மங்கலச் செய்கைய மஞ்சு சூழ்ந்தவே."],"p":"","r":"The raised stages, five bow-lengths in measure, rise on gold pillars set with coral, with cloud-like hangings, as if the sky's stages had descended.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1777","lb":"SO1777","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:77"},"so:1778":{"book":"soolamani","num":1776,"t":["விளிம்பிடை மரகத வேதி கட்டிய","வளம்பெறு மணிநகை மஞ்ச மீமிசை","இளம்பெருஞ் சுரியுளை யரிநின் றேந்திய","உளம்பொலி யாசன முயர விட்டவே."],"p":"","r":"On balconies edged with emerald, thrones carried by young lions stand raised high.","n":"The thrones are held up by lion figures, the usual sign of royal seats.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1778","lb":"SO1778","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:77"},"so:1779":{"book":"soolamani","num":1777,"t":["மண்டங்கு மகரவா சனத்து மென்மயில்","கண்டங்கள் புரைவன கனபொற் கொட்டைய","அண்டங்கொ ளன்னமென் றூவி யார்த்தன","எண்டங்கு மணியன வியற்றப் பட்டவே."],"p":"","r":"Seats shaped like crocodile-thrones and swan-down cushions are set with gems.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1779","lb":"SO1779","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:77"},"so:1780":{"book":"soolamani","num":1778,"t":["வாரித்தண் கதிர்மணி முத்த மாலையும்","பாரித்த பளிங்கெழிற் பழித்த கோவையும்","பூரித்த பொழிகதிர்ப் பொன்செய் தாமமும்","வேரித்தண் பிணையலு மிடையப் பட்டவே."],"p":"","r":"Strings of pearls, crystal-bright garlands, gold wreaths and honeyed flower-ropes are hung through the hall.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1780","lb":"SO1780","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:77"},"so:1781":{"book":"soolamani","num":1779,"t":["மஞ்சுடை மாளிகை மிடைம ணித்தலம்","பஞ்சுடைத் தவிசுகள் பரப்பிப் பூவடுத்","தஞ்சுட ரிடுபுகை யடர்ந்தெ ழுந்தரோ","வெஞ்சுடர்க் கடவுளை விருந்து செய்தவே."],"p":"","r":"Cotton seats are spread, flowers laid, and clouds of incense rise as though the hall were feasting the fierce sun.","n":"Honouring the sun-god with an incense feast turns the hall's preparation into a form of worship.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1781","lb":"SO1781","mm":1,"ev":"Attendants spread seats and flowers and burn incense in the hall.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:77"},"so:1782":{"book":"soolamani","num":1780,"t":["சயமர மாளிகை யியற்றிச் சந்தனப்","பயமர நிழலொளி மஞ்சம் பாவின","வியமரத் தொழிலவர் வினைமுடிந் ததென்","றியமரத் தொழுதிக ளெழுந்தி சைத்தவே."],"p":"","r":"When the builders finish the hall of the choosing and spread couches in sandal shade, the musicians stand and play to say the work is done.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalai","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1782","lb":"SO1782","mm":1,"ev":"Builders complete the choosing hall, and the musicians rise and begin to play.","cl":[],"ag":["இயற்றினார்","குயிலுவர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:77"},"so:1783":{"book":"soolamani","num":1781,"t":["மங்கல நாழிகை வந்த மன்னர்கள்","இங்குவந் தேறுக வென்று சாற்றலும்","சங்கொலி பரந்தன தயங்கு மாமுர(சு)","சங்கொலித் தெழுந்தன வரசர் தோன்றினார்."],"p":"","r":"At the auspicious hour a herald calls the kings to come and take their seats, conches sound, and the great drums roar as the kings appear.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","kaalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1783","lb":"SO1783","mm":1,"ev":"A herald calls the assembled kings to come in; conches and drums answer and the kings appear.","cl":[],"ag":["முரசறைவோர்","மன்னர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:77"},"so:1784":{"book":"soolamani","num":1782,"t":["எரிமணி வயிரப் பூணா னிக்குவா குலத்துட் டோன்றி","அருமணிப் புரிசை வேலி யயோத்தியாள் கின்ற வேந்தன்","திருமணி நிழற்றுஞ் செம்பொன் னெடுமுடி திருவில் வீசப்","புரிமணி யாரந் தாழப் பொன்னகர் பொலியப் புக்கான்."],"p":"","r":"The lord of Ayodhya, born in the Ikshvaku line, enters the golden city with a splendid crown and strings of gems.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","perumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1784","lb":"SO1784","mm":1,"ev":"The king of Ayodhya enters the city.","cl":[],"ag":["அயோத்தியாள் வேந்தன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:77"},"so:1785":{"book":"soolamani","num":1783,"t":["குழவியம் பருகி போல்வான் குருகுலங் குளிரத் தோன்றி","அழுவநீர்ப் புரிசை வேலி யத்தின புரம தாள்வான்","முழவங்க ளிரண்டு செம்பொன் முளைக்கதிர்க் கனக வல்லி","தழுவிய தனைய தோளான் றன்னொளி தயங்கச் சார்ந்தான்."],"p":"","r":"The king of Hastinapura, of the Kuru line, with arms like twin drums and shining with his own light, arrives.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","perumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1785","lb":"SO1785","mm":1,"ev":"The king of Hastinapura arrives.","cl":[],"ag":["அத்தின புரம் ஆள்வான்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:77"},"so:1786":{"book":"soolamani","num":1784,"t":["நண்டுபொன் கிளைக்கு நாட னாதவன் குலத்துட் டோன்றிக்","குண்டல புரம தாளுங் குங்குமக் குவவுத் தோளான்","கண்டிகை தவழப் பூண்டு கதிர்மணி முடியின் மேலால்","வண்டுகள் பரவச் சென்று வளநகர் மருளப் புக்கான்."],"p":"","r":"The lord of Kundalapura, of the Nathavan line, with saffron-smeared shoulders and wreathed crown, enters with bees swarming over him.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","perumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1786","lb":"SO1786","mm":1,"ev":"The king of Kundalapura enters the city.","cl":[],"ag":["குண்டல புரம் ஆளும்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:77"},"so:1787":{"book":"soolamani","num":1785,"t":["ஊழிகாண் பரிய தோன்ற லுக்கிர குலத்து வேந்தன்","வாழைதாழ் சோலை வேலி வாரண வாசி மன்னன்","சூழிமா லியானை யுந்திச் சுடர்குழை திருவில் வீச","ஏழையர் கவரி வீச வெழினக ரிசைப்பச் சென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"The lord of Varanasi, of the Ugra line, rides in with a crested elephant, flashing earrings and women waving fly-whisks.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","perumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1787","lb":"SO1787","mm":1,"ev":"The king of Varanasi rides into the city.","cl":[],"ag":["வாரண வாசி மன்னன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:77"},"so:1788":{"book":"soolamani","num":1786,"t":["சொரிமது கலந்த சோலைச் சூரிய புரம தாளும்","அரிகுலத் தரசர் கோமா னவிர்மணி யாரந் தாங்கிப்","பொருமலைப் பகடு நுந்திப் புயலலைத் திருண்டு வீழ்ந்த","புரிமலர்க் குஞ்சி தாழப் பொன்னகர் புகழப் புக்கான்."],"p":"","r":"The lord of Suriyapura, born of the Hari line, comes forward on a mountain-like war-elephant, hair loose with its flowers, as the city praises him.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","perumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1788","lb":"SO1788","mm":1,"ev":"The king of Suriyapura enters the city on an elephant.","cl":[],"ag":["சூரிய புரம் ஆளும்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:77"},"so:1789":{"book":"soolamani","num":1787,"t":["சொன்மலர்ந் துலக மேத்துஞ் சுடரவன் மருகன் றோலா","மன்மலர்ந் திலங்கு செய்கை வளங்கெழு மதுரை யாள்வான்","தென்மலை வளர்ந்த தெய்வச் சந்தனந் திளைத்த மார்பன்","மின்மல ராரந் தாங்கி வியனகர் விரும்பப் புக்கான்."],"p":"","r":"The lord of Madurai, kin of the shining one the world praises, wearing the gem-necklace and sandal on his chest, enters as the city wishes him well.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","perumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1789","lb":"SO1789","mm":1,"ev":"The king of Madurai enters the city.","cl":[],"ag":["மதுரை ஆள்வான்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:77"},"so:1790":{"book":"soolamani","num":1788,"t":["ஐம்பெருங் குலத்த ராய வரசரும் பிறரு மாங்கண்","கம்பெறி களிநல் யானைக் கடற்படை புறத்த தாக","வம்பெறி வளாகஞ் செம்பொன் மஞ்சங்கண் மலிர வேறி","வெம்பரி விளங்குந் தானை வேலவர் விளங்கு கின்றார்."],"p":"","r":"The kings of the five great lines and others stand outside with their elephants and hosts, and the spear-bearers shine on golden seats.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1790","lb":"SO1790","mm":1,"ev":"The kings take their seats, their armies waiting outside.","cl":[],"ag":["மன்னர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:77"},"so:1791":{"book":"soolamani","num":1789,"t":["திருந்திய திலதக் கண்ணித் தேவிளங் குமரன் போலும்","அருந்தகை யரச நம்பி யடுதிற லமித தேசன்","பரந்தபின் பசலை கூரப் பனிக்கதிர் வருவ தேபோல்","விரிந்தொளி சுடர வேந்தர் விளங்கொளி மழுங்கச் சென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"The prince Amitatejas, strong in battle and wearing the garland, goes in so brightly that the other kings' light is dimmed, as the moon is when it rises.","n":"The poet likens him to a young god and to the moon, so the verse sets up the contest by the one who dims the rest.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","perumai","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1791","lb":"SO1791","mm":1,"ev":"Prince Amitatejas enters, outshining the assembled kings.","cl":[],"ag":["அமிததேசன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:77"},"so:1792":{"book":"soolamani","num":1790,"t":["மழைபுரை மதத்த தாய மழகளி யானை தன்மேல்","வழைவளர் சோலை சேர்ந்த மணிவண்டு மறிவ வேபோல்","எழுதெழி லழகன் றன்மே லிளையவர் கருங்கண் வீழ்ந்து","விழவயர் நகரின் வந்த வேந்தரை விட்ட வன்றே."],"p":"","r":"The young women's dark eyes fall on the handsome prince as bees fall on a maned elephant, and they leave the other kings they had come to the festival for.","n":"The eyes act before the bride does: the choice is anticipated in the gaze of the onlookers.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1792","lb":"SO1792","mm":1,"ev":"The young women's eyes turn from the kings to the handsome prince.","cl":[],"ag":["இளமையார்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:77"},"so:1793":{"book":"soolamani","num":1791,"t":["வரைசெறிந் தனைய செம்பொன் மஞ்சங்கண் மலிரத் தோன்றி","அரைசர்க ளிருந்த போழ்தி னாழியந் தடக்கை வேந்தன்","விரைசெறி குழலங் கூந்தன் மெல்லியல் வருக வென்றான்","முரைசொலி கலந்த சங்கு வயிரொடு முரன்ற வன்றே."],"p":"","r":"When the kings are seated on their golden thrones, the discus-handed king calls the gentle maiden to come in, and drums and conches sound together.","n":"The calling voice is the king's, with the discus-hand; that he is Tivittan rests on the epithet and the earlier chapters.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["arasu","kaalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1793","lb":"SO1793","mm":1,"ev":"The king calls the maiden to come in and the drums and conches sound.","cl":[],"ag":["செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ec":[],"spk":["செங்கண் ணெடியான்","நங்கை"],"ch":"so:77"},"so:1794":{"book":"soolamani","num":1792,"t":["மன்னவன் மடந்தை மணிமாட நிலையுள்ளால்","பொன்னமளி மேலடுத்த பொங்கணையின் மேலாட் (கு)","கன்னமனை யாரடிக ளாரருளி தென்றார்","இன்னகைய பூந்தவிசி னின்றினி திழிந்தாள்."],"p":"","r":"The king's daughter, in her jewelled upper room, is told something about the lord ladies' grace and steps down from her flowered seat above the golden couch.","n":"The verse names no one; 'the king's daughter' is taken as Sayambavai because this is the swayamvara sarukkam. Line 3 is hard to construe and carries the editor's mark (கு) on line 2.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1794","lb":"SO1794","mm":1,"ev":"Sayambavai rises from her couch in the palace and steps down from her seat, to go out to the wedding hall.","cl":[],"ag":["சயம்பவை"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:78"},"so:1795":{"book":"soolamani","num":1793,"t":["வஞ்சியனை யார்மணிதொ டர்ந்தசுடர் ஞாணால்","அஞ்சில விருங்குழல சைத்தயில் பிடித்தார்","கஞ்சுக முகத்தமுலை கச்சுமிக வீக்கி","மஞ்சிவரு மாமயில னார்மருங்கு சூழ்ந்தார்."],"p":"","r":"Women like creepers bind the jewelled girdle on her, tie up her hair, tighten her bodices and close around the peacock-like girl with spears in their hands.","n":"The spears carried by her attendants make the bride's procession a guarded one, as the armed maidens of 862-975 are.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1795","lb":"SO1795","mm":1,"ev":"The maidens of her guard dress Sayambavai and surround her with spears.","cl":[],"ag":["கன்னியர்","கன்னியர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:78"},"so:1796":{"book":"soolamani","num":1794,"t":["ஆயமொடு தாயரிடை யாளரசர் தங்கள்","ணேயமிகு நெஞ்சினிடை யாளுமட வாளாய்ப்","பாயமதி தாரகையொ டோரைபட வேகித்","தூயமணி நீர் நிலைக டோறிவர்வ தொத்தாள்."],"p":"","r":"With her companions and nurses she goes through the courts, the girl who rules the hearts of kings, like the moon moving among stars and reflected in the clear jewelled waters.","n":"The moon-and-stars simile gives her the kings' longing before any king is shown.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1796","lb":"SO1796","mm":1,"ev":"Sayambavai walks with her retinue towards the hall.","cl":[],"ag":["சயம்பவை"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:78"},"so:1797":{"book":"soolamani","num":1795,"t":["வண்டுவழி செல்லவய மன்னர்மதி செல்லக்","கண்டவர்கள் கண்கள்களி கொண்டருகு செல்ல","எண்டிசையு மேத்தொலியொ டின்னொலிகள் செல்ல","விண்டமல ரல்லிமிசை மெல்லநனி சென்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"As she walks, bees follow her, the minds of the kings follow, the eyes of onlookers delight, and the praise and music of the eight quarters follow, and she steps gently over fallen petals.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1797","lb":"SO1797","mm":1,"ev":"Sayambavai walks on among following bees, kings' thoughts and the sounds of praise.","cl":[],"ag":["சயம்பவை"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:78"},"so:1798":{"book":"soolamani","num":1796,"t":["அம்மெலடி தாமரைச ராவியொடு நோவச்","செம்மெலிதழ் வாயொடவர் சிந்தனை துடிப்ப","வெம்முலைக ளோடவர்கள் காதன்மிக வீங்க","மைம்மலர் நிகர்க்குமணி மாளிகை யடைந்தாள்."],"p":"","r":"Her soft feet ache, the kings' thoughts throb, their desire swells and she enters the gem-set mansion, dark and bright as a flower.","n":"Line 1 is hard: 'சராவி' is a rare or damaged word, so the cause of the pain in the soft feet is not fixed.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1798","lb":"SO1798","mm":1,"ev":"Sayambavai reaches the jewelled mansion where the kings are seated.","cl":[],"ag":["சயம்பவை"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:78"},"so:1799":{"book":"soolamani","num":1797,"t":["பொன்னேநன் மணிக்கொம்பே பூமிமே லாரணங்கே போற்றி போற்றி","அன்னேயெம் மரசர்குலத் தவிர்விளக்கே யாரமிர்தே போற்றி யுன்றன்","மின்னேர்நுண் ணிடைநோமான் மென்மலர்மேன் மென்மெலவே யொதுங்கா யென்று","மன்னேர்சே யயினெடுங்கண் மங்கைமார் போற்றிசைப்ப மாடம் புக்காள்."],"p":"","r":"Her women cry 'honour, honour' to her as gold, as a gem-branch, as the lamp of the kings' line, and beg her to step slowly on the soft flowers lest her fine waist hurt, as she enters the hall.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","perumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1799","lb":"SO1799","mm":1,"ev":"Her attendants praise her and warn her to tread softly as she goes into the hall.","cl":[],"ag":["மங்கைமார்","சயம்பவை"],"ec":[],"spk":["மங்கைமார்","சயம்பவை"],"ch":"so:78"},"so:1800":{"book":"soolamani","num":1798,"t":["அணிதயங்கு சோபான வீதிவா யணங்கனையா ரடியீ டேத்த","மணிதயங்கு மாளிகைமேல் வாணிலா வளர்முன்றின் மருங்கு சூழ்ந்து","கணிதயங்கு வினைநவின்ற கண்டத் திரைமகளிர் கையி னீக்கித்","துணிதயங்கு வேலரசர் மனந்துளங்கச் சுடர்ந்திலங்கித் தோன்றி னாளே."],"p":"","r":"At the shining steps her attendants salute her, and in the moonlit court of the mansion, when the screen-women draw back, she appears blazing before the kings, whose minds waver.","n":"Line 3 (கணிதயங்கு வினைநவின்ற ... திரைமகளிர்) is hard to construe; I read it as women who hold a screen and remove it.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1800","lb":"SO1800","mm":1,"ev":"Sayambavai appears before the assembled kings and they are shaken.","cl":[],"ag":["சயம்பவை"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:78"},"so:1801":{"book":"soolamani","num":1799,"t":["வடியரத்த மிடைவழித்துக் கருங்கண்ணுஞ் செம்பொன்னால் வளைத்த சூரல்","கொடியரத்த மெல்விரலாற் கொண்டரசர் குலவரவு கொழிக்கு நீராள்","முடியரக்குப் பூங்கண்ணி மூரித்தேர் வேந்தர்தமை முறையாக் காட்டிப்","படியரக்கும் பாவைக்குப் பைபையவே யினையமொழி பகரா நின்றான்."],"p":"","r":"A man with a gold-bound cane, who shows the kings in order and speaks softly to the girl, begins to name them.","n":"The verse does not give the speaker's office; 'herald' is my label for the man who names the kings, as the speakers of 1802-1816 do.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["arasu","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1801","lb":"SO1801","mm":1,"ev":"The herald points out the kings to Sayambavai one by one and begins to speak to her.","cl":[],"ag":["முரசறைவோர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:78"},"so:1802":{"book":"soolamani","num":1800,"t":["அங்கார வலர்கதிர மணிசுடரு மரியணைமே லமர்ந்து தோன்றித்","தங்கார மணிநிழற்றுந் தடவரையா ரகலத்தான் றகர நாறுங்","கொங்கார வார்குழலார் குவிமுலைகண் முகம்பொருத குவவுத் தோளான்","இங்காரு நிகரில்லா விக்குவா குலத்திறைவ னிருந்த கோவே."],"p":"","r":"He points to the first king, broad-chested and scented with the sandal that the women's breasts touch, who sits on a lion seat, the lord of the Ikshvaku line with no peer here.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kudi","vendhan","perumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1802","lb":"SO1802","mm":1,"ev":"The herald points Sayambavai to the king of the Ikshvaku line.","cl":[],"ag":["முரசறைவோர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["முரசறைவோர்","சயம்பவை"],"ch":"so:78"},"so:1803":{"book":"soolamani","num":1801,"t":["ஆதியா னருளாழி தாங்கினா னாயிரச்செங் கதிரோ னாணுஞ்","சோதியான் சுரர்வணங்கு திருவடியான் சுடுநீறா நினையப் பட்ட","காதியா னருளியபொற் கதிர்கொள்முடி கவித்தாண்டார் மருகன் கண்டாய்","ஓதியா மொழியினிவ னுறு வலிக்கு நிகராவா ருளரோ வேந்தர்."],"p":"","r":"The herald says this king is descended from one who bore the wheel of grace of the First Lord and was crowned by the one who burned the harmful karmas, and asks who among kings could match his strength.","n":"The praise runs through the king's descent from the First Lord and his line, not through any deed of his own; the Jain colouring of 'அருளாழி' and 'காதி' is the poet's, but the verse makes no doctrinal claim.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kudi","perumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1803","lb":"SO1803","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முரசறைவோர்","சயம்பவை"],"ch":"so:78"},"so:1804":{"book":"soolamani","num":1802,"t":["ஆழித்தே ரொன்றேறி யலைகடலி னடுவோட்டி யமரர் தந்த","மாழைத்தேர் மருங்கறா மணிமுடியு மணிகலமுந் திறையா வவ்வி","ஊழித்தே ரரசிறைஞ்ச வுலகெலா மொருகுடைக்கீ ழுறங்கக் காத்த","பாழித்தோட் பரதன்பி னிவனிவனா னிலமடந்தை பரிவு தீர்ந்தாள்."],"p":"","r":"He mounted a single wheel-chariot, drove it across the middle of the sea, seized the gods' gold chariots and crowns and jewels as tribute, and kept the whole world under one umbrella, so that after Bharata the earth lost her sorrow.","n":"Bharata is the measure: the earth's relief 'after Bharata' is set on this king, as 397 sets Bharata's rule on the First Lord's grace.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vendhan","padai","perumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1804","lb":"SO1804","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முரசறைவோர்","சயம்பவை"],"ch":"so:78"},"so:1805":{"book":"soolamani","num":1803,"t":["இன்னவன துயர்குலமு மிளமையுமிங் கிவன்வடிவுஞ் சொல்ல வேண்டா","மன்னவன்றன் மடமகளே மற்றிவனுக் கிடமருங்கின் மஞ்சஞ் சேர்ந்து","பொன்னவிரு மணியணைமேற் பொழிகதிரீண் டெழுந்ததுபோற் பொலிந்து தோன்றுங்","கொன்னவின்ற வேற்குமரன் குருகுலத்தார் கோனிவனே கூறக் கேளாய்."],"p":"","r":"Skipping his lineage, youth and looks, the herald tells the king's daughter to hear of the king beside him, seated on a gem couch at his left, the lord of the Kuru line.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kudi","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1805","lb":"SO1805","mm":1,"ev":"The herald moves on to the king of the Kuru line seated beside the first.","cl":[],"ag":["முரசறைவோர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["முரசறைவோர்","சயம்பவை"],"ch":"so:78"},"so:1806":{"book":"soolamani","num":1804,"t":["அருளாழி யறவரச னருளினா லகன்ஞாலம் பரிவு தீர்த்தான்","உருளாழி யுடையரிவ னடைவின்மிக்க கடைப்பணிகொண் டுழையோர் போல","இருளாழி நிழற்று ளும்பு மெரிபொன்மணி நெடுமுடிசாய்த் திறைஞ்சப் பட்டான்","மருளாழுங் கழிவனப்பின் மற்றிவனே குலமுதற்கண் வயவோன் கண்டாய்."],"p":"","r":"By the grace of the lord of dharma, the herald says, the wheel-holder removed the world's sorrow; kings bow their crowns to him like servants, and he is the foremost in his line.","n":"'அறவரசன்' may name the Jain teacher or a king; the verse does not say. Whether 1806-1807 praise the Kuru king of 1805 or begin another is not fixed by the text.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["aram","arul","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1806","lb":"SO1806","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["the world's sorrow was ended by the grace of the lord of dharma","jain","reported",1,"herald"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முரசறைவோர்","சயம்பவை"],"ch":"so:78"},"so:1807":{"book":"soolamani","num":1805,"t":["சூழிருங் கடற்றானை யுடன்றுளங்கச் சுரர்கொணர்ந்து சொரிந்த மாரித்","தாழிரும்பல் புயறாங்கிச் சரகூடஞ் சந்தித்த தகையோ னன்னோன்","யாழிரங்கு மணிவண்டு மிலங்கிழையார் கருங்கண்ணு மருங்கு நீங்கா","வீழிரும்பொற் சுடரார வரைமார்ப னிவன்சீர்யான் விளம்ப வேண்டா."],"p":"","r":"He bore the rain of weapons poured by the gods' sea-host, and met the arrow-tower, and bees and women's dark eyes never leave his chest; his greatness, says the herald, needs no telling.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","perumai","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1807","lb":"SO1807","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முரசறைவோர்","சயம்பவை"],"ch":"so:78"},"so:1808":{"book":"soolamani","num":1806,"t":["இங்கிவன திடமருங்கி னெழில்றயங்கு மணிமஞ்ச மிலங்க வேறிச்","சங்கிவர்வெண் சாமரையுந் தாழ்குழையின் நீள்சுடருந் தயங்கி வீசக்","குங்குமஞ்சேர் கொழும்பொடியிற் புரண்டுதன்னி னிறஞ்சிவந்த குளிர்முத் தாரம்","செங்கதிரோ னொளிபருகுஞ் செவ்வரைநே ரகலத்தான் றிறமுங் கேளாய்."],"p":"","r":"At his left side another king sits on a gem couch, fanned by white fly-whisks, his chest red with kunkuma and as broad as a red mountain, and the herald asks her to hear of him too.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vendhan","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1808","lb":"SO1808","mm":1,"ev":"The herald points Sayambavai to the next king seated beside him.","cl":[],"ag":["முரசறைவோர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["முரசறைவோர்","சயம்பவை"],"ch":"so:78"},"so:1809":{"book":"soolamani","num":1807,"t":["தகரநா றிருஞ்சோலைச் சயம்பூறான் றுறவரசாய் நின்ற காலை","மகரயாழ் நரம்பியக்கி வரங்கொண்டு வடமலைமே லுலக மாண்ட","சிகரமா லியானையான் வழிமருகன் செந்தாமந் தவழ்ந்து தீந்தேன்","பகருமா மணிமுடியா னமரருமே பாராட்டும் படியன்பாவாய்."],"p":"","r":"When the First Lord stood in the sandal grove as a king of renunciation, one played the harp, won a boon and ruled the world on the northern mountain, and this king is the descendant praised by the gods.","n":"The harp-playing boon-winner on the northern mountain matches the story of Nami told at 538-547; 'சயம்பூறான்' (line 1) is not a word I can read, so the identification is not fixed.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thuravu","vazhipaadu","kudi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1809","lb":"SO1809","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முரசறைவோர்","சயம்பவை"],"ch":"so:78"},"so:1810":{"book":"soolamani","num":1808,"t":["சக்கரர்தாம் பிறந்துவரித் தரங்கநீர் வளாகமெல்லாந் தங்கீழ்க் கொண்ட","உக்கிரமெய்க் குலத்தரச னொளிவேலிவ் விளையவன துருவே கண்டாய்","அக்கிரநற் பெருந்தேவி மடமகளே யரசணங்கு மணங்கே யின்னும்","விக்கிரமக் கடற்றானை விறல்வேந்த ரிவர்சிலரை விளம்பக் கேளாய்."],"p":"","r":"This young king, spear shining, comes of the Ugra line which the wheel-kings founded and which held the sea-bound earth, and the herald tells the king's chief daughter that he will name a few more.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kudi","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1810","lb":"SO1810","mm":1,"ev":"The herald points to the young Ugra-line king and promises to name a few more.","cl":[],"ag":["முரசறைவோர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["முரசறைவோர்","சயம்பவை"],"ch":"so:78"},"so:1811":{"book":"soolamani","num":1809,"t":["ஏலஞ்செய் பைங்கொடியி னிணர்ததைந்து பொன்னறைமேற் கொழுந்தீன் றேறிக்","கோலஞ்சேர் வரைவேலிக் குண்டலத்தார் கோமானிக் கொலைவேற் காளை","ஞாலங்க ளுடன் பரவு நாதவன்றன் குலவிளக்கு நகையே னம்பி","போலிங்க ணரசில்லை பொன்னார வரைமார்பன் பொலிவுங் காணாய்."],"p":"","r":"The herald points to the lord of Kundalam, whose line lights the lord of the world's house, a spear-armed youth, and says there is no king like him here.","n":"'நாதவன்' and 'குண்டலத்தார்' may be names of line and place or epithets; the verse does not fix them.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vendhan","perumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1811","lb":"SO1811","mm":1,"ev":"The herald points to the lord of Kundalam.","cl":[],"ag":["முரசறைவோர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["முரசறைவோர்","சயம்பவை"],"ch":"so:78"},"so:1812":{"book":"soolamani","num":1810,"t":["சொரிமலர்த்தண் மலரணிந்த சோலைசூழ் சூரியத்தார் கோமான் றோலா","அரிகுலத்தார் போரேறிவ் வரியேறு போலிருந்த வரச காளை","வரிமலர்த்து மணிவண்டு புடைவருடு மாலையார் மகளிர் வட்கண்","புரிமலர்த்தண் வரையகலம் புராதார் புண்ணியங்கள் புணரா தாரே."],"p":"","r":"The herald names the lord of the Surya garland, a lion of the Hari line, and says that the women who have not embraced his broad chest have not met merit.","n":"Line 4 can be read with the women as those who have not embraced him, or those who have not 'united' with merit (புண்ணியங்கள்); I give both as one claim. The herald is selling a suitor, so the claim is his own.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vendhan","vinai","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1812","lb":"SO1812","mm":1,"ev":"The herald points to the king of the Surya garland of the Hari line.","cl":[["embracing this king's chest is the fruit of merit won earlier","general","reported",4,"herald"]],"ag":["முரசறைவோர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["முரசறைவோர்","சயம்பவை"],"ch":"so:78"},"so:1813":{"book":"soolamani","num":1811,"t":["வேலைவாய்க் கருங்கடலுள் வெண்சங்கு மணிமுத்தும் விரவி யெங்கும்","மாலைவாய்க் கரும்பறா வகன்பண்ணை தழீஇயருகே யருவி தூங்கும்","சோலைவாய் மலரணிந்த சூழ்குழலா ரியாழிசையாற் றுளைக்கை வேழம்","மாலைவாய் நின்றுறங்கும் மதுரைசூழ் வளநாடன் வடிவுங் காணாய்."],"p":"","r":"The herald points to the lord of the rich land around Madurai, where white conches and pearls mingle in the sea, sugarcane grows thick, waterfalls hang and elephants sleep to the women's lutes in the groves.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naadu","azhagu","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1813","lb":"SO1813","mm":1,"ev":"The herald points to the lord of the Madurai country.","cl":[],"ag":["முரசறைவோர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["முரசறைவோர்","சயம்பவை"],"ch":"so:78"},"so:1814":{"book":"soolamani","num":1812,"t":["கண்சுடர்கள் விடவனன்று கார்மேக மெனவதிருங் களிநல்யானை","விண்சுடரு நெடுங்குடைக்கீழ் விறல்வேந்தன் றிறமிதனை விளம்பக் கேளாய்","தண்சுடரோன் வழிமருகன் றென்மலைமேற் சந்தனமுஞ் செம்பொன் னாரத்","தொண்சுடரும் விரவியநல் வரைமார்ப னுலகிற்கோர் திலதங் கண்டாய்."],"p":"","r":"The herald says that this king under a tall umbrella, with elephants like thundering clouds, is a descendant of the moon-line, with the sandal of the southern hills on his chest, a tilaka on the brow of the world.","n":"The moon-line, the southern hill and the Madurai verse before suggest the Pandya, but the verse does not name him.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vendhan","perumai","kudi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1814","lb":"SO1814","mm":1,"ev":"The herald points to the king under the tall umbrella of the moon-line.","cl":[],"ag":["முரசறைவோர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["முரசறைவோர்","சயம்பவை"],"ch":"so:78"},"so:1815":{"book":"soolamani","num":1813,"t":["மழைக்கரும்புங் கொடிமுல்லை மருங்கேற வரம்பணைந்து தடாவி நீண்ட","கழைக்கரும்பு கண்ணீனுங் கரபுரத்தார் கோமானிக் கதிர்வேற் காளை","இழைக்கரும்பு மிளமுலையா யெரிகதிரோன் வழிமருக னிவனீரீர்ந்தண்","தழைக்கரும்பின் முருகுயிர்க்குந் தாரகலஞ் சார்ந்தவர்க டவஞ்செய் தாரே."],"p":"","r":"The herald names the lord of Karapuram of the sun-line, a bright-spear youth in a land of sugarcane and creeper, and says the women who have won his garlanded chest had done austerity.","n":"'தவஞ்செய்தாரே' makes past austerity the cause of present good fortune, as 1812 does with merit; the herald uses a doctrine of former deeds as part of the king's praise.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vendhan","thavam","vinai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1815","lb":"SO1815","mm":1,"ev":"The herald points to the lord of Karapuram of the sun-line.","cl":[["winning this king's embrace is the fruit of austerity done earlier","general","reported",4,"herald"]],"ag":["முரசறைவோர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["முரசறைவோர்","சயம்பவை"],"ch":"so:78"},"so:1816":{"book":"soolamani","num":1814,"t":["வண்டறையு மரவிந்த வனத்துழாய் மதர்த்தெழுந்த மழலை யன்னம்","உண்டுறைமுன் விளையாடி யிளையவர்க ணடைபயிலு முறந்தைக் கோமான்","கொண்டறையு மிடிமுரசுங் கொடிமதிலுங் குளிர்புனலும் பொறியும் பூவும்","ஒண்டுறையு மும்மூன்று முடையகோ வேயிவன தெழிலுங் காணாய்."],"p":"","r":"The herald points to the lord of Urandai, in whose city swans play and young women walk, and says he keeps a war-drum, a banner wall, cool water, machines and flowers, and three ghats in each of three kinds.","n":"Lines 3-4 list the king's possessions in threes; I cannot fix what each three is.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["naadu","vendhan","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1816","lb":"SO1816","mm":1,"ev":"The herald points to the king of Urandai.","cl":[],"ag":["முரசறைவோர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["முரசறைவோர்","சயம்பவை"],"ch":"so:78"},"so:1817":{"book":"soolamani","num":1815,"t":["தழலவாந் தாமரையி னீரிதழுஞ் செங்குவளைத் தாதும் வாரி","அழலவாஞ் செந்தோகை யலங்குபொலங் கதிர்ச்செந்நெ லலைத்த வாடை","பழனவாய்ப் பைங்கரும்பின் வெண்போது பவழக்காற் செம்பொன் மாடத்","தெழினிவாய்க் கொணர்ந் தசைக்கு மியலேமாங் கதநாட னிவனேகண்டாய்."],"p":"","r":"The guide points out the lord of the Hemangada land, whose northern wind carries lotus petals, lily pollen and the white flowers of sugarcane to the coral-legged gold mansions.","n":"The verse is praise of the king's land more than of the king; the man himself is named only by his country.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naadu","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1817","lb":"SO1817","mm":1,"ev":"The herald shows the bride the lord of the Hemangada country.","cl":[],"ag":["முரசறைவோர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["முரசறைவோர்","சயம்பவை"],"ch":"so:79"},"so:1818":{"book":"soolamani","num":1816,"t":["காந்தளங்கட் கமழ்குலையாற் களிவண்டு களிறகற்றுங் கலிங்க நாடன்","பூந்தளவங் கமழ்சாரற் பொன்னறைசூழ் தண்சிலம்ப னன்றே பொன்னே","ஏந்திளஞ்சிங் காதனத்தி னினிதிருந்த விளவரச னிப்பா லானோன்","மாந்தளிர்கண் மருங்கணிந்த மணியருவிக் குன்றுடைய மகதைக் கோவே."],"p":"","r":"The guide shows the lord of Kalinga and the young king of Magadha, who sits on a lion throne and owns hills of gem-bright streams.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["mannar","naadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1818","lb":"SO1818","mm":1,"ev":"The herald shows the bride the lord of Kalinga and the king of Magadha.","cl":[],"ag":["முரசறைவோர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["முரசறைவோர்","சயம்பவை"],"ch":"so:79"},"so:1819":{"book":"soolamani","num":1817,"t":["அங்கநா டுடையவர்கோ னவ்விருந்தா னிவ்விருந்தா னவந்திக் கோமான்","கொங்குவார் பொழிலணிந்த கோசலத்தார் கோமானிக் குவளை வண்ணன்","கங்கைதா னிருகரையுங் கதிர்மணியும் பசும்பொன்னுங் கலந்து சிந்தி","வங்கவாய்த் திரையலைக்கும் வளநாட னிவன்போலும் வைவேற் காளை."],"p":"","r":"The guide names the kings of Anga, Avanti, Kosala (the lily-hued one) and Vanga, praising the Ganga-washed land of the last as she points to the spear-armed youth.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["mannar","naadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1819","lb":"SO1819","mm":1,"ev":"The herald shows the bride the lords of Anga, Avanti, Kosala and Vanga.","cl":[],"ag":["முரசறைவோர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["முரசறைவோர்","சயம்பவை"],"ch":"so:79"},"so:1820":{"book":"soolamani","num":1818,"t":["வஞ்சியின்மெல் லிடையவளை வானிலா வளர்முன்றில் வலமாய்ச் சூழ்ந்து","பஞ்சியின்மெல் லடிநோவ நடைபயிற்றிப் படைவேந்தர் பலரைக் காட்டி","மஞ்சிவரு மாளிகையின் வடமருங்கின் மணிமஞ்ச மலிரத் தோன்றும்","விஞ்சையர்த முலகாளும் விறல்வேந்தர் குழாங்காட்டி விரித்துச் சொன்னாள்."],"p":"","r":"The bride is led round the moonlit courtyard, her soft feet aching, past many armed kings, and the guide then shows her in detail the Vidyadhara kings who sit on gem couches on the north side of the mansion.","n":"The verb சொன்னாள் is feminine, whereas chapter 78 casts the herald as a man; the guide is kept as one figure here and the discrepancy is noted in source_issues.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["mannar","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1820","lb":"SO1820","mm":1,"ev":"The herald walks the bride round the hall and names the earthly kings, then the Vidyadhara kings on the north side.","cl":[],"ag":["முரசறைவோர்","சயம்பவை"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:79"},"so:1821":{"book":"soolamani","num":1819,"t":["மாடிலங்கு மழைதவழ்ந்து மணியருவி பொன்னறைமேல் வரன்றி வம் பூந்","தோடிலங்கு கற்பகமுஞ் சுரபுன்னை வனங்களுமே துதைந்து வெள்ளிக்","கோடிலங்கு நெடுவரைமேற் குடைவேந்த ரிவர்குணங்கள் கூறக் கேட்பின்","ஏடிலங்கு பூங்கோதா யிமையவரின் வேறாய திமைப்பே கண்டாய்."],"p":"","r":"The guide tells the bride that the Vidyadhara kings, whose qualities she can hear, are no different from the gods save that they blink.","n":"The praise rests on the old mark of the gods that they do not blink; the herald uses it to say these kings differ from gods only in that one thing.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["mannar","perumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1821","lb":"SO1821","mm":1,"ev":"The herald praises the Vidyadhara kings of the silver mountain as godlike.","cl":[],"ag":["முரசறைவோர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["முரசறைவோர்","சயம்பவை"],"ch":"so:79"},"so:1822":{"book":"soolamani","num":1820,"t":["அங்கவவர் வளநகருங் குலவரவு மவையவற்றோடறையும் போழ்தின்","வெங்கதிரோன் பெயரவனுக் கிளவரசிவ் வேந்தனெனொ முன்னந் தானே","கொங்கிவருங் கருங்குழலி பெருந் தடங்கண் இருங்குவளை பிணையல் போலச்","செங்கதிரோ னெனவிருந்த திருந்துவே லிளையவன்மேற் றிளைத்த வன்றே."],"p":"","r":"While the guide still tells of the kings' cities and lines, the bride's large eyes had already settled on the young spear-bearing prince, heir of the king named for the sun.","n":"The heir is named only by the sun-king's epithet; line 2 is hard to construe, and that the heir is the groom later called the cousin prince (1828) is inferred.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1822","lb":"SO1822","mm":1,"ev":"The bride's gaze settles on the prince whom the herald has not yet reached.","cl":[],"ag":["முரசறைவோர்","சயம்பவை"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:79"},"so:1823":{"book":"soolamani","num":1821,"t":["கடாமிகு களிநல் யானைக் கவுளிழி கான வீதி","விடாமிகை சுழன்று வீழும் விரைகவர் மணிவண் டேபோல்","படாமுகக் களிற்றி னான்றன் பவழக்குன் றனைய மார்பில்","தடாமுகை யலங்க றன்மேற் றையல்கண் சரிந்த வன்றே."],"p":"","r":"Her eyes fall on the garland of his chest like a bee drawn by the rut-scent of a great elephant.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1823","lb":"SO1823","mm":1,"ev":"The bride's eyes fall on the prince of the elephant with the face-cloth.","cl":[],"ag":["சயம்பவை"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:79"},"so:1824":{"book":"soolamani","num":1822,"t":["ஏட்டினார் குழலி னாளுக் குழையவ ளின்ன னென்று","காட்டினா ளாவ தல்லாற் காரிகை தன்னின் முன்னம்","ஓட்டினா ணிறையுங் கண்ணு முள்ளமுங் களித்த தங்கே","பாட்டினா லென்னை போக பான்மையே பலித்த தன்றே."],"p":"","r":"The guide had only pointed him out, but her restraint and her heart had already gone to him, and the fruit of her enjoyment-lot had ripened, so the herald's words were no use.","n":"போக பான்மை is read as the portion of enjoyment owed her; the verse does not say it is the fruit of a past deed, so no link rests on that.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oozh","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1824","lb":"SO1824","mm":1,"ev":"The bride gives up her restraint at the sight of the prince before the herald has done naming him.","cl":[["the lot of enjoyment ripens by itself and the herald's introduction adds nothing to it","general","asserted",4,"narrator"]],"ag":["முரசறைவோர்","சயம்பவை"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:79"},"so:1825":{"book":"soolamani","num":1823,"t":["விண்டழி நிறைய ளாகி மெல்லவே நடுங்கி நாணி","வண்டிவர் மாலை நோக்கி மாதராள் மறைத லோடும்","கொண்டதோர் குமரன் போலக் குங்குமக் குவவுத் தோண்மேல்","ஒண்டொடி மாலை வீழ்த்தா ளுலகொலி படைத்த தன்றே."],"p":"","r":"Her restraint broken, trembling and ashamed, she lets the bee-covered garland fall on the saffron-heaped shoulders of the prince, and the hall roars.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maalai","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1825","lb":"SO1825","mm":1,"ev":"The bride puts the garland on the prince's shoulders, choosing him.","cl":[],"ag":["சயம்பவை"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:79"},"so:1826":{"book":"soolamani","num":1824,"t":["ஆர்த்ததங் கரவத் தானை யாலித்த முரசுஞ் சங்கும்","தேர்த்தன மலருஞ் செம்பொற் சுண்ணமுந் திசைக ளெல்லாம்","போர்த்தன பதாகை பொங்கிப் பூமியங் கிழவ ருள்ளம்","வேர்த்தன வேர்த்துத் தாமே வெய்துயிர்த் தொழிந்த வன்றே."],"p":"","r":"Drums, conches and war-drums sound, flowers and golden powder fill the quarters, and the rejected kings sweat and sigh.","n":"The rejected kings are the same men the herald praised in 1817-1821, now left without comment.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maalai","mannar"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1826","lb":"SO1826","mm":1,"ev":"The hall erupts in noise and flowers as the rejected kings sweat and sigh.","cl":[],"ag":["மன்னர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:79"},"so:1827":{"book":"soolamani","num":1825,"t":["புனைவுதா னிகந்த கோதைப் பொன்னனாள் பூமி பாலர்","நினைவுதா னிகந்து காளை வடிவெனு நிகளஞ் சேர","வினைகடாம் விளையு மாறியாம் வேண்டிய வாறு வாரா","வினையதால் வினையின் றன்மை யெனநினைந் தாறி னாரே."],"p":"","r":"Their hope gone and their eyes held by the prince's form as by a fetter, the kings calm themselves with the thought that deeds ripen as they will, not as one wishes, and that this is how deeds go.","n":"The kings' consolation is the same doctrine the poet urges on all in the last verse of the chapter (1839); here it is reported as their thought and not argued.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","samanilai","mannar"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1827","lb":"SO1827","mm":1,"ev":"The rejected kings give up their claim by thinking that the outcome is the work of karma.","cl":[["deeds ripen in their own way and not as one wishes, so a loss is to be borne as the nature of karma","general","reported",3,"mannar"]],"ag":["மன்னர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:79"},"so:1828":{"book":"soolamani","num":1826,"t":["நெய்த்தலைப் பாலுக் காங்கு நெடுவரை யுலகின் வந்த","மைத்துன குமரன் றன்னை மடமொழி மாலை சூட்ட","இத்தலை யென்ன செய்தா னெரிகதி ராழி வேந்தன்","கைத்தலை வேலி னாற்குக் கடிவினை முடிவித் தானே."],"p":"","r":"The question is put of what the flaming-discus king did when the soft-speaking girl garlanded the cousin prince from the mountain world, and the answer is that he saw the wedding completed for the spear-bearer.","n":"Line 1 (நெய்த்தலைப் பாலுக்காங்கு) is obscure, and who the flaming-discus king is, host or kinsman, is not stated.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1828","lb":"SO1828","mm":1,"ev":"The discus-king completes the wedding rites for the spear-bearing prince.","cl":[],"ag":["எரிகதி ராழி வேந்தன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:79"},"so:1829":{"book":"soolamani","num":1827,"t":["விண்ணகம் புகழு நீர்மை விழுக்கலம் பரப்பி யார","மண்ணக வளாகத் துள்ள மன்னரான் மண்ணு நீர்தந்","தெண்ணகன் புகழி னாரை யெழிலொளி துளும்ப வாட்டிப்","புண்ணகங் கமழும் வேலான் பொன்மழை பொழிவித் தானே."],"p":"","r":"The spear-bearer spreads out fine ornaments before the kings of the earth, bathes the renowned in splendour and has a rain of gold fall.","n":"The sense of lines 2-3 (the kings bringing water, being bathed) is uncertain; read as honouring the guests with gifts.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["eegai","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1829","lb":"SO1829","mm":1,"ev":"Gifts of jewels and gold are poured out to honour the kings who came.","cl":[],"ag":["அருக்ககீர்த்தி"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:79"},"so:1830":{"book":"soolamani","num":1828,"t":["தருமணன் மணிமுத் தாகத் தண்டுல மியற்றிக் கான்யாற்","றருமணற் றருப்பை சூழ்ந்தாங் கதன்மிசை பரிதி பாய்த்திப்","பெருமண மன்னற் கேற்ற சமிதையாற் பெருக்கப் பட்ட","திருமணி யுருவிற் செந்தீச் செல்வத்திற் சிறந்த தன்றே."],"p":"","r":"The wedding fire is laid with jewel-bright rice, river sand and darbha, with fuel fit for a great king, and shines richer than any wealth.","n":"Pure description of the rite's setting; no one acts in the verse.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1830","lb":"SO1830","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:79"},"so:1831":{"book":"soolamani","num":1829,"t":["தங்கழல் வேள்வி முற்றித் தையலக் காளை யோடும்","பொங்கழல் வலஞ்செய் போழ்திற் குழைமுகம் பொறித்த தெண்ணீர்","பைங்கழ லமரர் பண்டு படைத்தநீ ரமிழ்தப் புள்ளி","அங்கெழு மதியந் தன்மே லரும்பியாங் கணிந்த வன்றே."],"p":"","r":"When the fire sacrifice is done and the bride circles the flame with the youth, the drops of sweat on her ear-ringed face are like nectar dots on the moon.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1831","lb":"SO1831","mm":1,"ev":"The bride and groom circle the sacred fire.","cl":[],"ag":["சயம்பவை"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:79"},"so:1832":{"book":"soolamani","num":1830,"t":["மன்னவ குமர னாங்கு மடந்தையைப் புணர்ந்து மாடத்","தின்னகி லமளி மேலா லிளமுலைத் தடத்து மூழ்க","அன்னவன் றாதை செங்கோ லாணைவே லருக்க கீர்த்தி","தன்னமர் மடந்தைக் கேற்ற சயமர மறைவித் தானே."],"p":"","r":"The prince lies with the girl in the mansion on the aloe-scented couch, while his father Arkkakirti, whose sceptre and spear rule, has the swayamvara brought to its close.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1832","lb":"SO1832","mm":1,"ev":"The groom consummates the marriage and his father ends the swayamvara.","cl":[],"ag":["அருக்ககீர்த்தி","மன்னவ குமரன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:79"},"so:1833":{"book":"soolamani","num":1831,"t":["சயமர மறைந்த நன்னாட் டமனிய மஞ்சம் பாவி","இயமரந் துவைப்ப வேறி யிகன்மன்ன ரிருந்த போழ்தில்","பயமலை மன்னன் பாவைக் கவரவர் பண்பு கூறிக்","கயமலர் நெடுங்க ணாளோர் காரிகை காட்டி னாளே."],"p":"","r":"On the day after the first choosing, kings take seats on gold couches to the sound of drums, a mountain-king tells the girl each one's character and a lotus-eyed woman is shown.","n":"The verse is hard to parse: whether the lotus-eyed woman is the guide or the bride, and who the mountain-king is, are not said.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["mannar","avai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1833","lb":"SO1833","mm":1,"ev":"A second swayamvara is held; the mountain-king describes each king to the girl.","cl":[],"ag":["பயமலை மன்னன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:79"},"so:1834":{"book":"soolamani","num":1832,"t":["வரிகழன் மன்ன ரென்னு மணிநெடுங் குன்ற மெல்லாம்","சுரிகுழன் மடந்தை யென்னுந் தோகையம் மஞ்ஞை நோக்கி","எரிகதி ராழி வேந்தன் றிருமக னென்னுஞ் செம்பொன்","விரிகதிர் விலங்கற் றிண்டோட் குவட்டினை விரும்பிற் றன்றே."],"p":"","r":"The girl, like a peahen, looks over the kings as a range of hills and longs for the golden peak that is the flaming-discus king's son.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1834","lb":"SO1834","mm":1,"ev":"The girl's eyes choose the discus-king's son from the assembled kings.","cl":[],"ag":["சுதாரை"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:79"},"so:1835":{"book":"soolamani","num":1833,"t":["மாதராள் சுதாரை வாட்கண் மலரொடு மணிவண் டார்க்கும்","போதுலாம் பிணையல் வீரன் பொன்வரை யகலஞ் சூழ","ஏதிலா மன்னர் வாட விருபுடைக் கிளைஞ ரெல்லாம்","காதலாற் களித்துச் செல்வக் கடிவினை முடிவித் தாரே."],"p":"","r":"Sutara's garland of flowers and bees falls on the hero's golden chest while the other kings wither, and kin on both sides gladly complete the wedding.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maalai","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1835","lb":"SO1835","mm":1,"ev":"Sutara garlands the hero and her kin complete the wedding.","cl":[],"ag":["சுதாரை"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:79"},"so:1836":{"book":"soolamani","num":1834,"t":["கழல்வலம் புரிந்த நோன்றாட் கடல்வண்ணன் புதல்வன் காமர்","குழல்வலம் புரிந்த கோதை குழைமுகம் வியர்ப்ப வேட்டான்","அழல்வலம் புரிந்து சூழ்ந்தாங் கத்தொழின் முடித்த பின்னைத்","தழல்வலம் புரிந்த வேலான் றடமுலை வாரி சார்ந்தான்."],"p":"","r":"The sea-hued one's son, whose feet wear the warrior's anklet, circles the fire with the bride and after the rite joins her.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1836","lb":"SO1836","mm":1,"ev":"The groom circles the fire with the bride and is united with her.","cl":[],"ag":["திருவிசயன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:79"},"so:1837":{"book":"soolamani","num":1835,"t":["மாதரஞ் சாய லாளு மணிவண்ணன் சிறுவன் றானும்","ஓதநீ ரின்ப மென்னு மொலிகடற் றரங்க மூழ்கச்","சோதியம் பெயரி னாளுஞ் சுடரவன் புதல்வன் றானுங்","காதலிற் களித்துத் தங்கள் கனவரை யுலகஞ் சார்ந்தார்."],"p":"","r":"Both couples, the lady of gentle grace with the dark-gem lord's son and the lady named for light with the sun's son, delight in love and go to their homes on the great mountain.","n":"Which bride is which of the two named in lines 1 and 3 is not said; they are read as the two weddings of the chapter.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["inbam","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1837","lb":"SO1837","mm":1,"ev":"The two newly wedded pairs depart for the mountain land.","cl":[],"ag":["திருவிசயன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:79"},"so:1838":{"book":"soolamani","num":1836,"t":["எரிவிசயங் கோவேந்தி மன்னரென்னும்","அரிவிசயங் கெடநின்ற வாணை வேலான்","திருவிசயன் றிருவன்ன செல்வி யோடும்","மருவிசயங் கெழுகோயின் மலர்ந்து புக்கான்."],"p":"","r":"Srivijayan, whose command stands against enemy kings' victory, enters the joyous palace of victory with his wife as lovely as Tiru.","n":"The wording of lines 1-2 plays on the word விசயம் (victory) and is a pun on his name; read as praise.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inbam","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1838","lb":"SO1838","mm":1,"ev":"Srivijayan enters his palace with his bride.","cl":[],"ag":["திருவிசயன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:79"},"so:1839":{"book":"soolamani","num":1837,"t":["இனையனவா மிகுசெல்வ மிங்கு மாக்கிப்","புனைமலர்வா னவர்போகம் புணர்க்கும் பெற்றி.","வினையதனின் விளைவின்ன தென்று நாளும்","நினைமின்மோ நெறிநின்று நீர்மை மிக்கீர்."],"p":"","r":"The poet closes by saying that such wealth here and the pleasure of the flower-decked gods are the fruit of one's deeds, and bids people of good nature to remember it daily and keep to the way.","n":"The closing moral sets the kings' resigned thought at 1827 as the poet's own teaching, and prepares the opening of the next sarukkam (1840-1844), where Payapati reasons from his kingship to merit.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","aram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1839","lb":"SO1839","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["great wealth in this world and heavenly enjoyment are the fruit of deeds, and people should remember this and keep to the right way","general","asserted",3,"narrator"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:79"},"so:1840":{"book":"soolamani","num":1838,"t":["மன்னிய புகழி னான்றன் மகன்வழிச் சிறுவர் வாயுள்","இன்னகை மழலை கேட்டாங் கினிதினி னிருந்த காலைப்","மன்னுமெய்த் துறவிற் புக்கான் பயாபதி மன்னர் மன்னன்","அன்னதன் பகுதி தன்னை யறியுமா பகர லுற்றேன்."],"p":"","r":"The poet opens the Renunciation sarukkam: while Payapati, king of kings, sat happily listening to the sweet babble of his son's children, he entered true renunciation, and the poet will tell how.","n":"The turn is set in a moment of family joy, not of loss: the call to renounce comes with the grandchildren's laughter in the room, so the sarukkam begins from fullness, not grief.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thuravu","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1840","lb":"SO1840","mm":1,"ev":"The poet announces that King Payapati took to renunciation while delighting in his grandchildren.","cl":[],"ag":["பயாபதி"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:80"},"so:1841":{"book":"soolamani","num":1839,"t":["திருமகி ழலங்கன் மார்பிற் செங்கணான் வணங்கச் செல்வப்","பெருமகிழ் வெய்தி வேந்தன் பிரசாபதி பெரிய வாட்கண்","உரிமையோ டிருந்த போழ்தி னொலிகல னொலிப்ப வோடி","அருமைகொ டிகிரி யாள்வான் சிறுவர்சென்ற ணுகி னாரே."],"p":"","r":"As the red-eyed lord bows, King Prajapati sits glad among his own, and the grandchildren, their ornaments ringing, run up to the ruler of the discus.","n":"Payapati and Prajapati name the same king of Podanam in these verses; the red-eyed bowing lord and the 'ruler of the discus' are taken as Tivittan on the strength of the earlier chapters, and the children as his sons.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["arasu","makkal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1841","lb":"SO1841","mm":1,"ev":"Tivittan bows to Prajapati, and Tivittan's young sons run to their grandfather.","cl":[],"ag":["செங்கண் ணெடியான்","பயாபதி","சிறுவர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:80"},"so:1842":{"book":"soolamani","num":1840,"t":["ஆங்கவ ரணைந்த போழ்தி னமிழ்துகொப் புளித்த போலும்","தேங்கமழ் பவழச் செவ்வாய் முறுவனீர் பருகித் தேங்கி","ஈங்கிவை யனைய தோற்றி யின்பமே பருக நின்ற","வீங்கிய தவத்திற் கின்னும் வித்திடற் பால தென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"Seeing the children's nectar-sweet smiles, the king says that the swollen merit which has brought such pleasure still has more seed to be sown.","n":"The pleasure is read as a harvest already drawn from a field, so the same sight that delights him prompts the thought of sowing; the verse does not say the pleasure is bad, only that the stock behind it is finite.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thavam","inbam","vinai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1842","lb":"SO1842","mm":1,"ev":"Payapati, watching his grandchildren, speaks of sowing more seed for the austerity that bore these pleasures.","cl":[["pleasures enjoyed now are the fruit of earlier austerity, and more must be sown for the future","jain","asserted",4,"payapati"]],"ag":["பயாபதி"],"ec":[],"spk":["பயாபதி",""],"ch":"so:80"},"so:1843":{"book":"soolamani","num":1841,"t":["அலகுடன் விளங்கு மம்பொற் குடைநிழ லரசர் சூழ","உலகுடன் வணங்க வோடை யுயர்களிற் றெருத்த மேலால்","பலகுடை பணியச் செல்லும் பண்பிது நமக்குத் தந்த","நலனுடைத் தளிய நங்க ணல்வினைத் தெய்வ மன்றே."],"p":"","r":"The umbrellas, the kings who surround him, the world's bowing and the ride on a tall elephant are all a gift to us from our good-deed deity.","n":"நல்வினைத் தெய்வம் makes karma itself the divinity who grants the kingship; no god of the Veda is named. The king receives what he has earned.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","arasu","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1843","lb":"SO1843","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["royal honour is given by one's own good deeds as by a deity","jain","asserted",4,"payapati"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["பயாபதி",""],"ch":"so:80"},"so:1844":{"book":"soolamani","num":1842,"t":["தன்னையோ ரரச னாக்கித் தரங்கநீர் வளாக மாள்வித்","தின்னுயி ராகிச் செல்லு நல்வினை யென்னு மின்ன","முன்னுப காரி தன்னை முதல்கெட முயலுங் கீழ்மை","நன்னரின் மாந்த ரன்றே நரகங்கட் கரச ராவார்."],"p":"","r":"Those who try to destroy the root of the good deed that made them king and gave them the sea-girt earth, the only helper that goes with the life, are base men and will be kings in the hells.","n":"நரகங்கட்கு அரசர் turns kingship upside down: the same word that names his rank names the hell-lord's dominion for the heedless.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","naragam","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1844","lb":"SO1844","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["one who spends the stock of good deeds without renewing it falls to hell","jain","asserted",3,"payapati"],["the good deed is the one benefactor that accompanies the life","jain","asserted",2,"payapati"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["பயாபதி",""],"ch":"so:80"},"so:1845":{"book":"soolamani","num":1843,"t":["சென்றநாள் பெயரு மேனுஞ் செல்வமுஞ் செருக்கு மாக்கி","நின்றநா ணிலவு மேனு நெறிநின்று வருந்த வேண்டா","இன்றுபோல் வாழ்து மன்றே யிப்படித் தன்றி யாங்கள்","பொன்றுநாள் வருவ தாயின் வாழ்க்கையோர் பொருள தன்றே."],"p":"","r":"Whether the days gone by pass and wealth and pride stand, one need not suffer on the path; we live as today, but if the day of our perishing comes our life is worth nothing.","n":"The verse is hard to construe in lines 1-2; the reading takes it as the king's inward objection that he is content as things are, answered at once by line 4.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","vinai","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1845","lb":"SO1845","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["life lived on pleasure alone has no worth once death comes","general","asserted",4,"payapati"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["பயாபதி",""],"ch":"so:80"},"so:1846":{"book":"soolamani","num":1844,"t":["எரிபுரை யெழில தாய விளந்தளி ரிரண்டு நாளின்","மரகத வுருவ மெய்தி மற்றது பசலை கொண்டு","சருகிலை யாகி வீழ்ந்த கரிந்துமண் ணாதல் கண்டும்","வெருவிலர் வாழ்து மென்பார் வெளிற்றினை விலக்க லாமோ."],"p":"","r":"Two days make a bright young leaf green, then pale, then a dried leaf that falls and burns to dust; the ignorant who see it and still say 'we will live' cannot be held back.","n":"The leaf stands for the body in three stages, flame-red, emerald and withered, so the argument from nature rests on colour change, not only on loss.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","yaakkai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1846","lb":"SO1846","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["youth and life wither as a leaf does, yet the unwise ignore it","general","asserted",4,"payapati"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["பயாபதி",""],"ch":"so:80"},"so:1847":{"book":"soolamani","num":1845,"t":["பிறந்தனர் பிறந்து சாலப் பெருகினர் பெருகிப் பின்னை","இறந்தன ரென்ப தல்லா லியாவரு மின்று காறு","மறைந்துயிர் வாழா நின்றா ரில்லையால் வாழி நெஞ்சே","சிறந்தது தவத்தின் மிக்க தின்மையே சிந்தி கண்டாய்."],"p":"","r":"People are born, grow great, then die, and no one has lived on hidden; so, heart, think that nothing is better than austerity.","n":"The king addresses his own heart (நெஞ்சே); the same address runs through 1848 and 1849.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","thavam","piravi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1847","lb":"SO1847","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["everyone born dies, and austerity is the best answer","jain","asserted",3,"payapati"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["பயாபதி",""],"ch":"so:80"},"so:1848":{"book":"soolamani","num":1846,"t":["பிறந்துநாம் புறஞ்செய் கின்ற பேதையிவ் வுடலந் தானும்","இறந்தநாள் போல்வ தின்றா யிற்றையின் னாளை வேறாய்ப்","பறைந்துநாம் பற்றப் பற்றப் பற்றுவிட் டகலு மாகிற்","சிறந்தனர் பிறர்க கள்யாரே சிந்தைநீ சிந்தி யென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"The foolish body we tend is as good as dead; if we leave attachment and stop clinging, who is better than we; think this over, he told his heart.","n":"Lines 1-2 are hard to construe and the reading is tentative; line 3 and 4 are clearer.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pattru","yaakkai","thuravu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1848","lb":"SO1848","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["letting go of attachment is the mark of the best","jain","asserted",3,"payapati"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["பயாபதி",""],"ch":"so:80"},"so:1849":{"book":"soolamani","num":1847,"t":["தொகைமல ரலங்கல் சூடித் தூநறுஞ் கண்ண மப்பிப்","புகைநனி கமழ வூட்டிப் புறஞ்செயப் பட்ட மேனி","சிகையினோர் சிறுமுட் டீண்டச் சிதைந்தழுக் கொழுகு மாயி","நகைபெரி துடைத்து நாணா மிதனைநா மகிழ்த னெஞ்சே."],"p":"","r":"The body wreathed, perfumed and smoked for display falls apart and runs with filth when a tiny thorn from the hair pricks it, yet we are glad of it without shame, heart.","n":"The adornment is listed step by step (garland, paste, incense) before the thorn undoes it, which makes the care itself the evidence of folly.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","pulaal","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1849","lb":"SO1849","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["the adorned body is a foul thing and no cause for pride","jain","asserted",4,"payapati"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["பயாபதி",""],"ch":"so:80"},"so:1850":{"book":"soolamani","num":1848,"t":["ஒழுகிய முடையு நீரு முதலகை யிகப்ப வூறும்","அழுகலிவ் வள்ளல் யாக்கை யகம்புற மாயிற் றாயில்","கழுகொடு கவருங் காக்கை கைத்தடி கொண்டு காத்தும்","அழகுள சுழலு மன்னோ வாயிரச் சாதி மாதோ."],"p":"","r":"The foul flowing fluid of this body, which the demon spirits would mock if it were turned inside out, would be fought over by kites and crows, who are kept off with a stick, and so do a thousand kinds of 'beauty' circle it.","n":"The crow guarded off with a hand stick (line 3) is the very picture Naladiyar 41 uses; the shared image makes the point that the body must be defended from what will eat it. Line 4 is hard to construe.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","pulaal","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1850","lb":"SO1850","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["the body is foul within and beauty is only an outer cover","jain","asserted",2,"payapati"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["பயாபதி",""],"ch":"so:80"},"so:1851":{"book":"soolamani","num":1849,"t":["வல்வினை விளைத்த மாந்தர் மற்றதன் வித்து மாட்டிப்","புல்வினை கான மண்டிப் புலியின்வாய்ப் பட்ட தேபோல்","நல்வினை யினிதி னூட்டு நல்வினை முதல்கண் மாறி","இல்வினை யின்பம் வெஃகி யிறுபவே யறிவி லாதார்."],"p":"","r":"Those who did evil deeds take its seed and sow it; like a man in a tiger's mouth, they leave the root of good deeds that feeds them sweetly and die wanting the pleasure of what has no root, which fools do.","n":"The verse repeats 1844's root image in the plain voice of a warning and ends the king's inward reflection before 1852 opens the council.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vinai","kaamam","naragam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1851","lb":"SO1851","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["those who abandon the root of good deeds for fleeting pleasure perish","jain","asserted",4,"payapati"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["பயாபதி",""],"ch":"so:80"},"so:1852":{"book":"soolamani","num":1850,"t":["இன்னன பலவுஞ் சிந்தித் திருந்தது மிகையென் றெண்ணி","மன்னவ னுழையர் தம்மான் மந்திரத் தவரைக் கூவிப்","பொன்னவிர் பவழத் திண்காற் புரிமணிக் கூட மெய்திக்","தன்னம ரமைச்ச ரோடு தானமர்ந் திருந்து சொன்னான்."],"p":"","r":"Having reflected on all this and judged that waiting is excess, the king summons his counsellors through his attendants and, seated with his ministers in the coral-pillared gem hall, speaks.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","amaichu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1852","lb":"SO1852","mm":1,"ev":"Payapati calls his ministers to a council in the gem hall.","cl":[],"ag":["பயாபதி","அருத்தநூலவர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:80"},"so:1853":{"book":"soolamani","num":1851,"t":["மலைபயில் களிநல் யானை மன்னரால் வவ்வ லின்றாய்க்","கலைபயில் மகளிர் கண்போற் கள்வர்கைப் படாது நாளும்","நிலையின செல்வக் கூனம் வருவன வுரைமி னென்றான்","இலைபயின் மகரப் பைம்பூ ணெரிமணிக் கடகக் கையான்."],"p":"","r":"The king asks the ministers to tell what losses may come to his enduring wealth, which kings cannot seize like a mountain elephant nor thieves steal like women's glances.","n":"The wealth is described by what cannot take it (kings, thieves), which prepares the answer that only Death remains.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","selvam","amaichu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1853","lb":"SO1853","mm":1,"ev":"Payapati asks the council to name any danger to his wealth.","cl":[],"ag":["பயாபதி"],"ec":[],"spk":["பயாபதி","அருத்தநூலவர்"],"ch":"so:80"},"so:1854":{"book":"soolamani","num":1852,"t":["ஆள்வினை மாட்சி யென்னு மிரண்டினு மரசு காத்துத்","தோள்வினைக் களவு காவ லுள்வழித் துன்னல் செல்லா","வாள்வினைத் தடக்கை வேந்தே வருவது மற்று முண்டோ","கோள்வினை பயின்ற கூற்றங் குறுகல தாயி னென்றார்."],"p":"","r":"The ministers answer that, with effort and good order guarding the realm, no theft or force can reach it; is anything more to come, they ask, unless the ranging Death should come near?","n":"The ministers name the one danger they cannot guard against, so the council turns by their own words from the state to Death.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","kootru","amaichu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1854","lb":"SO1854","mm":1,"ev":"The ministers reply that nothing threatens the realm but Death.","cl":[["a king's wealth is safe from kings and thieves but not from Death","general","asserted",4,"ministers"]],"ag":["அருத்தநூலவர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["அருத்தநூலவர்","பயாபதி"],"ch":"so:80"},"so:1855":{"book":"soolamani","num":1853,"t":["கோள்வினை பயின்ற கூற்ற வரசனாற் கொள்ளற் பால","கேள்வினை பயின்ற நூலிற் கிளர்ந்துநீ ருரைமி னென்ன","வாள்வினை புரிந்த தோளான் மனத்ததை யுணர்ந்து மாதோ","நாள்வினை புரிந்து நங்க ளுயிர்நிறை கொள்ளு மென்றார்."],"p":"","r":"At the king's bidding to speak from learning about what Death can take, the ministers, reading his mind, say that Death will do his day's work and take their lives whole.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kootru","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1855","lb":"SO1855","mm":1,"ev":"At the king's bidding, the ministers say Death will take their lives.","cl":[["death comes on its day and takes life whole","general","asserted",4,"ministers"]],"ag":["அருத்தநூலவர்","பயாபதி"],"ec":[],"spk":["அருத்தநூலவர்","பயாபதி"],"ch":"so:80"},"so:1856":{"book":"soolamani","num":1854,"t":["சந்தினாற் றவிர்க்க லாமோ சார்பினா லொழிக்க லாமோ","பந்தியா முன்னந் தாமே பகைத்திருந் துய்ய லாமோ","வெந்திறற் காலன் றன்னை மேற்சென்று வெல்ல லாமோ","உய்ந்துயிர் யாங்கள் வாழு முபாயநீ ருரைமி னென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"The king asks whether Death can be turned back by conciliation, by alliance, by making enemies first, or by attacking and conquering him, and bids them name a way to live.","n":"The questions are the four ordinary expedients of a ruler against an enemy; the king tests whether statecraft reaches Death.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kootru","arasu","soozhchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1856","lb":"SO1856","mm":1,"ev":"Payapati asks the ministers if statecraft can defeat Death.","cl":[],"ag":["பயாபதி"],"ec":[],"spk":["பயாபதி","அருத்தநூலவர்"],"ch":"so:80"},"so:1857":{"book":"soolamani","num":1855,"t":["பீழைமை பலவுஞ் செய்து பிணிப்படை பரப்பி வந்து","வாழுயிர் வாரி வவ்வி வலிந்துயிர் வாங்கி யுண்ணுங்","கூழைமை பயின்ற கூற்ற வரசனைக் குதிக்குஞ் சூழ்ச்சி","பாழியந் தடக்கை வேந்தே பயின்றிலம் யாங்களென்றார்."],"p":"","r":"The ministers answer that they have not learned a stratagem to leap at the king of Death, who spreads sickness, takes lives by force and eats them.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kootru","soozhchi","pedhaimai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1857","lb":"SO1857","mm":1,"ev":"The ministers confess that statecraft has no means against Death.","cl":[["no stratagem overcomes death","general","asserted",3,"ministers"]],"ag":["அருத்தநூலவர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["அருத்தநூலவர்","பயாபதி"],"ch":"so:80"},"so:1858":{"book":"soolamani","num":1856,"t":["ஆயினக் காலன் பாணி யாம்பிற வரச செல்வம்","மேயினங் களித்தி யாங்கள் விழைந்துயிர்வாழும் வாழ்க்கை","பாயிய வெழுந்த வேங்கை பாரிக்கு மளவிற் பைம்புன்","மாயிருஞ் சுருளை மேயு மான்மறி போலு மென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"Then, says the king, the pleasures of royal wealth we enjoy and the life we wish to live are like a fawn grazing the green weed while the tiger prepares to spring.","n":"The simile is the king's answer to the council's silence; it turns their confession into his own resolve.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","selvam","kootru"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1858","lb":"SO1858","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["life lived in pleasure is a fawn grazing before the tiger springs","general","asserted",3,"payapati"]],"ag":["பயாபதி"],"ec":[],"spk":["பயாபதி",""],"ch":"so:80"},"so:1859":{"book":"soolamani","num":1857,"t":["அருங்களி யானை வேந்தே யத்துணைப் பாணியுண்டோ","கருங்களி மதநல்யானை வாய்புகு கவள மேபோல்","பெருங்களி யாளன் காலன் பிறையெயி றணிந்துநின்ற","இருங்களி யாணர் வாழ்விற் கிமைப்பிடை பெரிது கண்டாய்."],"p":"","r":"The speaker answers that there is not even that much time: like a morsel falling into a rutting elephant's mouth, Death, glad with his crescent tooth, takes life in the space of a blink.","n":"The speaker is not named; he addresses the king as வேந்தே, so a minister is meant.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kootru","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1859","lb":"SO1859","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["the interval before death is shorter than a blink","general","asserted",4,"ministers"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["அருத்தநூலவர்","பயாபதி"],"ch":"so:80"},"so:1860":{"book":"soolamani","num":1858,"t":["இன்னுயி ரழியும் போழ்து மிறைவனுக் குறுதியல்லான்","முன்னிய முகமன் மாட்டா முற்றிய வறிவி னாரை","மன்னவன் மகிழ்ந்து நோக்கி வாழுயிர் வவ்வுங் காலன்","தன்னைநா மிகந்து சேருஞ் சரண்பிறி துரைமி னென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"The king, who loses his life, says that flatterers who cannot serve the lord's interest with sweet words cannot help; look to the learned who advise him, and tell of some refuge we can reach beyond Death.","n":"Lines 1-3 are hard to construe; the verse is read as the king rejecting courtly flattery and asking for refuge.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kootru","arasu","amaichu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1860","lb":"SO1860","mm":1,"ev":"Payapati asks what refuge lies beyond Death.","cl":[],"ag":["பயாபதி"],"ec":[],"spk":["பயாபதி",""],"ch":"so:80"},"so:1861":{"book":"soolamani","num":1859,"t":["இனியன போன்று தோன்றி நுகர்ந்தவற் கிறுதி செய்யும்","கனிபுரை கிளவி நீக்கிக் கண்ணனார் கருத்துட் கொண்டு","துனிவன நினையுங் காலன் றுணிவன துணியுஞ் சூட்சி","முனிவரை வணங்கிக் கேட்டு முயறுமோ வடிக ளென்றார்."],"p":"","r":"Dismissing the pleasant flattery that ends only in ruin, the speaker, taking the king's thought to heart, asks, lord, whether they should try after bowing to the sages and hearing them.","n":"கண்ணனார் is taken as the speaker's own epithet in line 2; the verse is uncertain in lines 2-3.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thuravu","amaichu","kootru"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1861","lb":"SO1861","mm":1,"ev":"A minister proposes to seek the sages' counsel.","cl":[],"ag":["அருத்தநூலவர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["அருத்தநூலவர்","பயாபதி"],"ch":"so:80"},"so:1862":{"book":"soolamani","num":1860,"t":["குருகயா வுயிர்க்குஞ் சோலைக் குளிரணிப் பழன நாட","முருகயா வுயிர்க்கும் பூவார் முறிமிடை படலை மாலைத்","திருவயா வுயிர்க்கு மார்பற் செறிதவர் சரண மூலத்","தருகயா வுயிர்ப்பி னல்லா லரண்பிறி தாவ துண்டோ."],"p":"","r":"Lord of fertile lands, there is no refuge but to stay at the foot of the austere sage's feet, close under the tree of his refuge; what other refuge can there be?","n":"The refuge is a person, the sage under his tree, not a god or a rite; the verse sets the sage's feet against the king's palace as a shelter.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thuravu","thavam","kootru"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1862","lb":"SO1862","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["the feet of the austere sage are the only refuge against death","jain","asserted",4,"ministers"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["அருத்தநூலவர்","பயாபதி"],"ch":"so:80"},"so:1863":{"book":"soolamani","num":1861,"t":["எரிகின்ற சுடரி னெய்பெய் திடுதிரி தூண்டி யாங்கு","விரிகின்ற புலமை வீரர் மொழிதலும் விசோதி யன்னாற்","பரிகின்ற வுரிமை வல்ல படரொழி மனத்த னானான்","சொரிகின்ற மதுவின் மாரித் துவலையி னனைந்த தாரான்."],"p":"","r":"As the heroes speak, like a wick trimmed in a burning lamp of ghee, the king grows light of mind, free of the grief that weighed on him, and sits wet with the rain of honey from his garland.","n":"The name in line 2, விசோதி, is not clear as written; it may be a name or a corrupt word, so who is likened to whom is not fixed. The lamp-wick simile makes the heroes' words the stirring that raises the king's flame; the verse does not say what they said.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thuravu","manam","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1863","lb":"SO1863","mm":1,"ev":"The learned heroes speak and the king's mind is eased of its sorrow.","cl":[],"ag":["வீரர்","பயாபதி"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:81"},"so:1864":{"book":"soolamani","num":1862,"t":["கரும்பணி மொழியி னார்தங் கருந்தடங் கண்ணும் வண்டும்","சுரும்பணை முலையி னாருந் தொடையலுந் துதைந்த மார்பன்","அரும்பணி யசோக நீழ லடிகள தணிபொற் கோயில்","விரும்பணி விழவு சாற்றி வியன்முர சறைக வென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"The king, whose chest is thick with garlands and the dark eyes of women, orders a drum to be proclaimed for a festival in the lord's adorned golden temple under the Ashoka tree.","n":"The speaker is taken to be the king of the previous verses because no other is named; the verse itself gives only 'he said'. The Ashoka shade names the Jina's tree, so the temple is the Arhat's, and the festival is a Jain royal act, though the verse does not say Jain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","arasu","iraivan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1864","lb":"SO1864","mm":1,"ev":"The king commands that a drum proclaim a festival at the Lord's golden temple under the Ashoka tree.","cl":[],"ag":["பயாபதி"],"ec":[],"spk":["பயாபதி",""],"ch":"so:81"},"so:1865":{"book":"soolamani","num":1863,"t":["ஒளியவ னுலகம் தன்னுட் கரந்தவ னுயிர்க ளுய்யும்","அளியவ னருள்செய் யாழி யுடையவ னடிமை செய்வார்க்","கெளியவ னெந்தை பெம்மாற் கியற்றிய விழவின் மிக்க","களியவ ரென்ப செம்பொற் கதிர்முடி சூடு வாரே."],"p":"","r":"The poet praises the Lord as the radiant one hidden in the world, gracious to living beings, holder of the wheel, easy for his servants to reach, and says those most gladdened at the festival made for him will wear the golden crown.","n":"The Lord is praised in the vocabulary of a world-ruler (the wheel, the hidden light) and the reward promised is kingship, so devotion is paid for in the coin the king already holds.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","vazhipaadu","arul"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1865","lb":"SO1865","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["those who rejoice at the festival of the Lord are reborn to wear a king's crown","jain","asserted",4,"narrator"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:81"},"so:1866":{"book":"soolamani","num":1864,"t":["அருள்புரி யழலஞ் சோதி யாழியா னாதி யில்லான்","மருள்புரி வினைகட் கென்று மறுதலை யாய வாமன்","இருள்புரி யுலகஞ் சேரா வியனெறி பயந்த பெம்மான்","பொருள்புரி விழவு காண்பார் புண்ணிய வுலகங் காண்பார்."],"p":"","r":"The Lord, the fire of grace and the beginning-less one, is the enemy of the deeds that bring delusion and showed the wide way that does not enter the dark world; whoever sees the festival made for him sees the world of merit.","n":"The way out of the dark world is placed with the Lord, who is called beginning-less, but the verse does not say a maker: he shows the path, he does not grant release. The promise of merit's world for the onlooker sits beside the claim that he is the foe of deeds, so the festival is presented as a good deed done to one who stands against deeds.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","vinai","vazhipaadu","aram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1866","lb":"SO1866","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["the Lord is the opponent of deluding deeds and taught the way that avoids rebirth in the dark world","jain","asserted",3,"narrator"],["beholding the festival of the Lord brings the sight of the world of merit","jain","asserted",4,"narrator"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:81"},"so:1867":{"book":"soolamani","num":1865,"t":["கண்ணிய வறிவன் செல்வ விழவினுட் களித்த மாந்தர்","புண்ணியத் துகள்க ளென்னும் பொற்சுண்ணம் புதைய வாடிப்","பண்ணியன் மொழியி னார்தங் கருங்கண்ணாற் பருகு நீர்மை","விண்ணிய லுருவ மெய்தி விளங்கிவீற் றிருப்ப ரன்றே."],"p":"","r":"Those who have rejoiced at the Lord's rich festival, bathed in the golden powder called the dust of merit and drinking the beauty of the sweet-voiced women with their dark eyes, will gain a celestial form and shine.","n":"The powder is called merit's dust (புண்ணியத் துகள்) and is literally the festival's gold; the gaze on women is part of the pleasure that earns heaven, which the epic voices without reproof here, though the same sarukkam turns to renunciation.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","aram","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1867","lb":"SO1867","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["rejoicing at the Lord's festival earns a heavenly body","jain","asserted",4,"narrator"]],"ag":["நகரார்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:81"},"so:1868":{"book":"soolamani","num":1866,"t":["எல்லைசான் முரசிற் சாற்றி யின்னன வறைத லோடும்","மல்லன்மா நகரங் கேட்டே வானுல கிழிந்த தேபோன்","முல்லைவான் கண்ணி சூடி முகிழ்நகைக் கலங்க டாங்கிச்","செல்லும்வாய் தோறுஞ் செல்வ விழவணி தேர்த்த தன்றே."],"p":"","r":"At the proclamation by the great drum the city rejoices as though the sky-world had fallen to earth: people with jasmine garlands and smiling jewels fill every street, and the festival chariots go through every lane.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","arasu","naadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1868","lb":"SO1868","mm":1,"ev":"The drum is sounded and the whole city comes out adorned for the Lord's festival.","cl":[],"ag":["நகரார்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:81"},"so:1869":{"book":"soolamani","num":1867,"t":["இன்னிசை முரசங் கேட்டே மெய்பெரி தினிய கேட்டா","மன்னிய நங்கள் வாணாள் வாழ்கநம் மிறைவ னென்னாப்","பொன்னியன் மலருஞ் சாந்துஞ் சுண்ணமும் புகையும் பொங்கத்","துன்னிய நகர மாந்தர் துறக்கம்பெற் றவர்க ளொத்தார்."],"p":"","r":"Hearing the sweet drum, the people of the city say that their long lives are secure and bless their lord, and with flowers, sandal, powder and incense rising they seem to have gained heaven.","n":"Whether நம் இறைவன் (our lord) is the king or the Jina is not fixed by the verse; both are possible, and the blessing's wish for long life reads with a sharp irony, since the king is moving toward renunciation.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["arasu","vazhipaadu","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1869","lb":"SO1869","mm":1,"ev":"The townspeople bless their lord on hearing the drum.","cl":[],"ag":["நகரார்"],"ec":[],"spk":["நகரார்",""],"ch":"so:81"},"so:1870":{"book":"soolamani","num":1868,"t":["பூரண மணிக்குட நிரைத்த பொன்னணி","தோரண மெடுத்தன துதைந்த வெண்கொடி","வாரணி முரசொடு வளைக ளார்த்தரோ","காரணி கடலொலி கைத விர்த்ததே."],"p":"","r":"Golden festoons hung with full jars were raised, white banners pressed together, and the sound of drums and conches silenced the roar of the cloud-dark sea.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","naadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1870","lb":"SO1870","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:81"},"so:1871":{"book":"soolamani","num":1869,"t":["விரையினான் மெழுகிய வீதி வாயெலாம்","திரையினார் செழுமணி முத்தஞ் சிந்தினார் யினா","லென்னையவ் வொளிகொண் மாநகர்","புரையினாற் பொன்னுல கிழிந்த தொத்ததே."],"p":"","r":"The streets, plastered with fragrance, are strewn with pearls and gems, and the bright city is like the golden world fallen to earth.","n":"The second and third lines are corrupt as printed (சிந்தினார் யினா லென்னையவ் ...), so who strews, and with what, is a reading from the first and last lines only.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["selvam","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1871","lb":"SO1871","mm":1,"ev":"The people strew pearls and gems on the festival streets.","cl":[],"ag":["நகரார்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:81"},"so:1872":{"book":"soolamani","num":1870,"t":["கழுமிய காழகி லாவி காமரு","செழுமணி மாளிகைச் சென்னி சூழ்வது","விழுமணி விளங்கிய விலங்கன் மீமிசைத்","தழுவிய விளமழை தவழ்வ தொத்ததே."],"p":"","r":"The smoke of thick aloe rising over the jewelled mansions looks like young clouds resting on a bright hill.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1872","lb":"SO1872","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:81"},"so:1873":{"book":"soolamani","num":1871,"t":["வெண்டுகி லுடுத்துவெண் சாந்து மெய்வழித்","தொண்டிரண் மல்லிகை யொலியல் சூடினார்","வண்டிரண் மணிமுத்தும் வயிரச் சாதியும்","கொண்டிய லணியொடு கோலந் தாங்கினார்."],"p":"","r":"The people dress in white cloth, smear their bodies with white sandal, wear garlands of jasmine, and carry pearls, diamonds and other ornaments as their festival finery.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1873","lb":"SO1873","mm":1,"ev":"The townsfolk put on white clothes and ornaments for the festival.","cl":[],"ag":["நகரார்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:81"},"so:1874":{"book":"soolamani","num":1872,"t":["வெண்மருப்பி ரட்டைய வேழ மீமிசைக்","கண்மருட்டு றுப்பன கமலப் பூப்பலி","விண்மருட்டு றுப்பன வேந்தி வேதியர்","மண்மருட்டு றுப்பதோர் வகையின் மன்னினார்."],"p":"","r":"Elephants with two white tusks carry lotus-flower offerings that dazzle the eye and rival the sky, and brahmins bearing them stand in order in a way that perplexes the earth.","n":"Lines 2 to 4 are hard to construe; brahmins here take part in a Jain-framed festival as bearers of offerings, a detail the verse states without comment.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1874","lb":"SO1874","mm":1,"ev":"Brahmins carry flower offerings on elephants in the procession.","cl":[],"ag":["அந்தணர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:81"},"so:1875":{"book":"soolamani","num":1873,"t":["செம்மலர்க் கண்ணியர் செம்பொற் றாரினர்","கொய்ம்மலர்க் குங்குமங் குழைந்த சாந்தினர்","கைம்மலர் மணிநகைக் கடகம் வில்லிட","மெய்ம்மல ரணியினர் வேந்த ராயினார்."],"p":"","r":"Garlanded folk with red-flowered chaplets and gold necklaces, with sandal ground with saffron and bracelets flashing on their hands, wearing body-flowers and finery, become kings.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1875","lb":"SO1875","mm":1,"ev":"The adorned throng stand dressed like kings.","cl":[],"ag":["கண்ணியர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:81"},"so:1876":{"book":"soolamani","num":1874,"t":["செய்ந்நிறக் குவளைகை செய்த சூட்டினர்","அந்நிறந் தழுவிய வரத்த வாடையர்","மெய்ந்நிறஞ் செய்யன வேழ மீமிசைக்","கைந்நிற மலரொடு கலந்து தோன்றினார்."],"p":"","r":"People with blue-lily crowns and red robes of the same colour, their bodies red, appear on elephants mingled with flowers in their hands.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1876","lb":"SO1876","mm":1,"ev":"Crowned riders appear on elephants.","cl":[],"ag":["கண்ணியர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:81"},"so:1877":{"book":"soolamani","num":1875,"t":["பொன்மலர்க் கண்ணியர் பொன்செய் சுண்ணமொய்","மின்மலர் மேனிமேல் விளங்க வப்பினார்","மென்மல ரணிநகை மிளிருங் கோலமோ","டின்மல ரிருநிதிக் கிழவரீண்டினார்."],"p":"","r":"Folk in golden garlands scatter gold powder that gleams on their lightning-bright bodies, and the lords of the two treasures come in with soft flowers and ornaments.","n":"The 'lords of the two treasures' (இருநிதிக் கிழவர்) is taken as a title of the wealthy; the verse does not say whom it names.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["selvam","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1877","lb":"SO1877","mm":1,"ev":"The throng and the lords of the two treasures gather.","cl":[],"ag":["கண்ணியர்","கண்ணியர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:81"},"so:1878":{"book":"soolamani","num":1876,"t":["போரொளிப் பீதக வுடையர் பைம்பொனால்","ஆரொளி தழுவிய வலர்செய் பூப்பலி","போரொளி யானைமே னிரைத்துப் போந்தனர்","வாரணி வனமுலை யவரொ டென்பவே."],"p":"","r":"Wearers of yellow robes with bright golden jewels, carrying flower offerings, file on war-elephants with the women of full breasts, as they say.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1878","lb":"SO1878","mm":1,"ev":"Yellow-robed men on war-elephants process with women.","cl":[],"ag":["கண்ணியர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:81"},"so:1879":{"book":"soolamani","num":1877,"t":["நகரமாங் கெழுந்தபி னரலுஞ் சங்கொடு","முகுரவாய் மணிமுர சதிரு மூரிநீர்","மகரமால் கருங்கடன் மருளுந் தானையான்","சிகரமால் யானைமேற் செல்வன் றோன்றினான்."],"p":"","r":"As the city rises in uproar, with the conch and the drum sounding louder than the dark sea, the lord of the great army appears on a mountain-like elephant.","n":"The rider is called செல்வன் without a name; he is taken as the king who ordered the festival in 1864.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["arasu","padai","perumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1879","lb":"SO1879","mm":1,"ev":"The king rides out on a great elephant at the head of his army.","cl":[],"ag":["பயாபதி"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:81"},"so:1880":{"book":"soolamani","num":1878,"t":["வேல்புரை கண்ணியர் கவரி வீசவெண்","பால்புரை பவழக்காற் குடையி னீழலான்","மால்புரை கருங்கடல் வளாகங் காவலன்","கால்புரை புரவியங் கடலுட் தோன்றினான்."],"p":"","r":"Women with spear-bright eyes wave white chauris, and the king, in the shade of an umbrella with coral shaft and lord of the sea-girt earth, appears like a sea of horses surrounding him.","n":"The umbrella, chauris and army are the insignia of kingship, set out at full height here.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1880","lb":"SO1880","mm":1,"ev":"The king appears under the white umbrella with fly-whisks waving.","cl":[],"ag":["கன்னியர்","பயாபதி"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:81"},"so:1881":{"book":"soolamani","num":1879,"t":["புதமெழு புரவிகள் புடைப ரந்திடை","மதமழை பொழிவன வயிரக் கோட்டன","கதமழ லெழவுமிழ் தகைய காணில","விதமெழு களிறுகள் பலமி டைந்தவே."],"p":"","r":"The horses spread in all directions, and many rutting elephants with diamond tusks, spouting a rain of rut and blowing out the fire of anger, press together in many kinds.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["padai","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1881","lb":"SO1881","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:81"},"so:1882":{"book":"soolamani","num":1880,"t":["ஆர்த்தன பல்லிய மதிர்ந்த குஞ்சரம்","தேர்த்தன தேர்க்குழாந் திசைத்த பல்லுயிர்","போர்த்தன கொடிமிடை பொழிந்த பூமழை","வேர்த்தன விளிந்தன வினைக ளென்பவே."],"p":"","r":"The instruments sounded, the elephants trumpeted, the chariots rolled, the many living things were covered by banners and the shower of flowers, and, they say, their deeds sweated and died.","n":"The close reading of 'deeds sweated and perished' (வினைகள் வேர்த்தன விளிந்தன) is a figure of exhaustion and death; the verse puts it in the hearsay form என்ப, so the poet reports the claim without vouching for it in his own voice, and it turns on a rare word.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vinai","vazhipaadu","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1882","lb":"SO1882","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["the sight of the Lord's festival and procession wears away the deeds of those who watch","jain","asserted",4,"narrator"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:81"},"so:1883":{"book":"soolamani","num":1881,"t":["விரிந்துயர் வெள்ளிவெண் குடையின் மாடெலாம்","திருந்திய சாமரை திசைக டேர்த்தன","பரந்தெழு பாற்கடற் பரவை வெண்டிரை","நிரைந்தெழு நுரையொடு நிரைத்த வொத்தவே."],"p":"","r":"On every side umbrellas of silver spread, and the fly-whisks waving in all directions look like the foam of the wide Milk Ocean rising in waves.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1883","lb":"SO1883","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:81"},"so:1884":{"book":"soolamani","num":1882,"t":["பீலியந் தழைபிணித் திட்ட வட்டமு","மாலியங் கசைப்பன வால வட்டமு","மேலியங் கொளியவன் மறைய வேய்ந்தரோ","காலியங் கிடவிடங் காண்கி லாரரோ."],"p":"","r":"Fans of peacock feather and rounded screens hide the radiant one as they are set up, and the crowd finds no room to walk.","n":"The radiant one (ஒளியவன்) is taken as the sun, as the umbrellas and fans cover the sky; 1865 uses the same word for the Lord, so the pun may be intended or the reading may be wrong.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["arasu","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1884","lb":"SO1884","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:81"},"so:1885":{"book":"soolamani","num":1883,"t":["சந்தனஞ் செறிந்தன செப்புந் தண்புகைக்","கந்தமே நிறைந்தன கரண்ட கங்களும்","கொந்துமொய்ம் மலர்நிறை கோடி கங்களும்","உந்தியொன் றொன்றினை யூன்று கின்றவே."],"p":"","r":"Fragrant sandal fills the censers and cool scent the incense boxes, and the flower-heaped baskets press close, one upon another.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1885","lb":"SO1885","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:81"},"so:1886":{"book":"soolamani","num":1884,"t":["நிரந்தன பூப்பலி நிரைகொண் மாரியாய்ச்","சொரிந்தன சுரும்பிவர் துணர்கொள் பூமழை","பரந்தன மங்கலப் பதாகை யவ்வழிக்","கரந்தன கருவினைக் குழாங்க ளென்பவே."],"p":"","r":"Flower offerings fall in rows like a rain, bee-loud clusters of blossom pour down, auspicious banners spread, and the hosts of binding deeds are said to have hidden away.","n":"The last line is reported speech (என்பவே), so the vanishing of karma is the poet's report of the scene, not an event he claims to see; the verse does not say whose presence drives the karma away.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","vinai","oli"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1886","lb":"SO1886","mm":1,"ev":"Flowers rain and banners spread as the hosts of karma are said to hide.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:82"},"so:1887":{"book":"soolamani","num":1885,"t":["பாடுவார் பலாண்டிசை பரவு வார்பரந்","தாடுவா ரறிவனைப் பரவி யார்களும்","கூடுவார் குழுவுமெய் குழுமி யெங்கணும்","ஊடுதான் வியலிட முள்ள தில்லையே."],"p":"","r":"Singers, those who wish long life, dancers and those who praise the knower gather in crowds until no open space is left anywhere.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","iraivan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1887","lb":"SO1887","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["பாடுவார்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:82"},"so:1888":{"book":"soolamani","num":1886,"t":["நொவ்வகை வினைப்பகை யகற்றி நூனெறி","செவ்வகை மொழிந்தவன் செல்வச் சேவடிக்","கிவ்வகை யெழுவகை விழவு செல்வுழி","நெய்வகை வேலவ னிலைமை கேட்கவே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks us to hear how the oil-bright spearman fares as the seven-fold festival goes to the feet of the one who cast out the foes that are his deeds and taught the scripture's way aright.","n":"The verse turns the story from the hall to the shrine and names the Lord only by what he did; the festival's seven parts are not listed.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","vinai","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1888","lb":"SO1888","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["the Jina cast out the enemy deeds and taught the path of the scripture rightly","jain","asserted",2,"narrator"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:82"},"so:1889":{"book":"soolamani","num":1887,"t":["நீர்ப்பலி விரைப்பலி நிரந்து தேனிமிர்","பூப்பலி யெனவிவை நிரைத்துப் புண்ணியன்","சீர்ப்பொலி சினகரஞ் சென்று சேர்ந்தனன்","ஆர்ப்பொலி தழுவிய வரவத் தானையான்."],"p":"","r":"Arranging offerings of water, perfume and bee-loud flowers, the king, among the roar of his army, reaches the fair Jina shrine of the meritorious one.","n":"The king is not named in the verse; he is known from the sarukkam's earlier verses.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1889","lb":"SO1889","mm":1,"ev":"Payapati arrives at the Jina shrine with water, perfume and flower offerings.","cl":[],"ag":["பயாபதி"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:82"},"so:1890":{"book":"soolamani","num":1888,"t":["கோடுயர் கோபுர வாய்தல் சேர்ந்துதன்","நீடுயர் மழகளி றிருவித் தானிழிந்","தேடுய ரினமல ரேந்தி யீர்ம்பொழின்","மாடுயர் வளநகர் வலங்கொண் டெய்தினான்."],"p":"","r":"At the gate of the high-towered shrine the king halts his young elephant, climbs down, takes bright flowers and circles the rich city with its gardens before arriving.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","arasu","panivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1890","lb":"SO1890","mm":1,"ev":"Payapati dismounts at the shrine gate and circles it with flowers in his hand.","cl":[],"ag":["பயாபதி"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:82"},"so:1891":{"book":"soolamani","num":1889,"t":["மன்னவ னணைதலு மலர்ந்த வாணிலாப்","பொன்னணி வளநக ரகத்துப் பொங்கரி","துன்னிய வணைமிசைத் துளங்குஞ் சோதியோ","டன்னணம சோகமர்ந் தடிக டோன்றினார்."],"p":"","r":"As the king arrives, the Lord appears seated on a lion throne beneath an asoka tree in the gold-adorned city, shining together with its light.","n":"The asoka and the throne begin a series of the Jina's signs, the shade tree, flowers, music, whisks, throne, halo and umbrella, that the next verses spread across the scene.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","arasu","oli"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1891","lb":"SO1891","mm":1,"ev":"The Lord appears to Payapati on a lion throne under an asoka tree.","cl":[],"ag":["பயாபதி","இறைவன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:82"},"so:1892":{"book":"soolamani","num":1890,"t":["குஞ்சரத் தடக்கைய குழைச் சென்னிய","மஞ்சிவர் தோற்றத்து மகர வாயொடு","செஞ்சுடர் மணிநிரை யழுத்திச் செம்பொனால்","அஞ்சுட ருமிழ்வதவ் வணையின் வண்ணமே."],"p":"","r":"The throne is said to be gold set with rows of red gems, carved with elephant-trunk lions and sea-monster mouths, and its look spills five kinds of light.","n":"Only the throne is described; the Lord seated on it is not.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","iraivan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1892","lb":"SO1892","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:82"},"so:1893":{"book":"soolamani","num":1891,"t":["ஏழிய லுலகிலுள்ளி ருளுங் கையகன்","றாழியல் வினைகேளா டவிய வாயிரம்","தாழொளி சுடரவன் றன்னைக் காணவோர்","சூழொளி மண்டிலஞ் சுடரத் தோன்றுமே."],"p":"","r":"When the world sees the thousand-ray light, the darkness of the seven worlds leaves and deeds are said to wither; a ring of light appears around the Lord.","n":"The second line is hard (ஆழியல் வினை கேளாடவிய), so whether the deeds wither or the dark withdraws from them is a reading; the halo (பாமண்டலம்) is not named.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["oli","vinai","iraivan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1893","lb":"SO1893","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["the Jina's halo clears the darkness of the worlds and dries up the deeds of those who see it","jain","asserted",2,"narrator"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:82"},"so:1894":{"book":"soolamani","num":1892,"t":["கழுமிய பானிலாக் கதிரின் கற்றைகள்","செழுமணித் திரண்மிசைச் செறிந்த போல்வன","எழுவளர்த் தனையதோ ளியக்க ரேந்தின","தொழுதகை யுருவின கவரி தோன்றுமே."],"p":"","r":"Yakshas with shoulders like tall trees raise whisks that look like milk-white moonbeams settled on masses of gem, their hands joined in worship.","n":"The whisks stand among the eight signs of the Jina, borne by divine servants, not by the king's own men.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1894","lb":"SO1894","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["இயக்கர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:82"},"so:1895":{"book":"soolamani","num":1893,"t":["பருகலாம் பானிலாப் பரந்த மாமணி","அருகெலா மணிந்தக டம்பொ னார்ந்துமேற்","பெருகலாஞ் சுடரொளி பிறங்கி நின்றதம்","முருகுலாம் பிண்டியான் குடையின் மும்மையே."],"p":"","r":"The umbrella of the fragrant-tree Lord is a cluster of three, drinkable-bright with moonlike pearls, hemmed with gold and rich with light.","n":"The three umbrellas answer the single white umbrella of the earthly king, who elsewhere rules the world under one; here a triple one rises over the Jina.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["perumai","iraivan","oli"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1895","lb":"SO1895","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:82"},"so:1896":{"book":"soolamani","num":1894,"t":["அழல்வளர்த் தனையன தழையு மவ்வழல்","தழல்வளர்த் தனையன மலருந் தாமரைப்","பொழில்வளர் வளையமும் பொதுளி வண்டினம்","குழைவள ரசோகின்மேற் குளிர்செய் கின்றவே."],"p":"","r":"Flaming flowers on the asoka that seem to feed a fire, ponds of lotus, and swarms of bees on the leafy asoka cool the place.","n":"The asoka's red blooms look like fire but cool, so the image of heat is turned to calm.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","iraivan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1896","lb":"SO1896","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:82"},"so:1897":{"book":"soolamani","num":1895,"t":["மாமழைக் கண்ணியர் மருங்கு போல்வன","தூமழை வளர்கொடி துவன்றிப் பத்திகள்","பாமழை யுருவுகள் பலவுந் தோன்றவே","பூமழை பொன்னிலம் புதைய வீழ்ந்தவே."],"p":"","r":"Banners like the sides of cloud-eyed women spread in rows, shapes like rain of song appear, and a rain of flowers buries the golden ground.","n":"The flower rain here is the divine counterpart of the offered flowers in 1886.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","vazhipaadu","oli"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1897","lb":"SO1897","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:82"},"so:1898":{"book":"soolamani","num":1896,"t":["மொய்த்திலங் கலர்மழை முருகு லாவிய","மைத்தலை விசும்பிடை மயங்க வானவர்","கைத்தலம் பரவிய காம ரின்னியம்","எத்திசை மருங்கினு மிரங்கித் தோன்றுமே."],"p":"","r":"Gods clap their hands in worship, and sweet music sounds sadly on every side beneath rain-heavy flowers that mingle in the dark sky.","n":"இரங்கி suggests the music sounds yearning, as if grieved; the verse does not say for what.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","iraivan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1898","lb":"SO1898","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["அமரர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:82"},"so:1899":{"book":"soolamani","num":1897,"t":["மைஞ்ஞலம் பருகிய கருங்கண் மாமணிப்","பைஞ்ஞலம் பருகிய பரும வல்குலார்","மெய்ஞ்ஞலம் விஞ்சையர் விரவ மேலெலாம்","கிஞ்ஞர மிதுனங்கள் கிளர்ந்து தோன்றுமே."],"p":"","r":"Vidyadhara men and women with dark-eyed, wide-hipped companions mingle with pairs of kinnaras rising all over the upper air.","n":"Each class of heavenly being is named by line, without any word on what the Lord does for them.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1899","lb":"SO1899","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["விஞ்சையர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:82"},"so:1900":{"book":"soolamani","num":1898,"t":["எரிமணி நெடுமுடி யிமைப்பிற் செங்கணப்","புரிமணி வண்ணனும் பொன்செ யாழியத்","திருமணி வண்ணனுந் தேவி மார்களும்","அருமணி வண்ணனுக் கருகு தோன்றினார்."],"p":"","r":"The red-eyed gem-coloured one of the flame-bright crown, the one of the golden discus, and their queens appear beside the Lord of the rare gem hue.","n":"The two gem-coloured figures are not named; the red eyes point to Tivittan of the earlier sarukkam and the golden discus to a discus-holder, but the verse does not fix who they are.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["arasu","iraivan","panivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1900","lb":"SO1900","mm":1,"ev":"Two gem-hued lords and their queens come to stand beside the Lord.","cl":[],"ag":["குமரர்","தேவியர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:82"},"so:1901":{"book":"soolamani","num":1899,"t":["ஒண்டமர் மணிகளு மொளிர்பொற் சாதியும்","கொண்டன ரியற்றிய கோலச் செய்கையால்","கண்டவர் கண்கவர் நகரங் காண்டலும்","விண்டுதிர் வினையினன் வேந்த னாயினான்."],"p":"","r":"Seeing the gem-and-gold city that heaven's builders made, a sight to take away the eye, the king, whose deeds were shaking loose, stood as sovereign.","n":"விண்டுதிர் வினையினன் vaguely says the king's deeds are dropping away; the verse leaves open whether that is cause or result of what he sees. The last words (வேந்தனாயினான்) are unusual, since he was already king.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["arasu","vinai","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1901","lb":"SO1901","mm":1,"ev":"Payapati sees the divine city and stands before the Lord.","cl":[],"ag":["பயாபதி"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:82"},"so:1902":{"book":"soolamani","num":1900,"t":["பணியொடு நறுவிரை மெழுகிப் பன்மலர்","அணியுடை யனையன பலவுஞ் செய்தபின்","மணிமுடி நிலமுற வணங்கி வாமன்மேற்","றுணிபடு வினையினன் றுதிதொ டங்கினான்."],"p":"","r":"Having anointed the ground with perfume and set many flowers as ornaments, the king bows until his gem-crown touches the earth and, his deeds already cut, begins his hymn to the Lord.","n":"துணிபடு வினையினன் is said of the worshipper before the hymn begins, so the cutting of deeds comes with the act of devotion, not after the praise.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["panivu","vazhipaadu","vinai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1902","lb":"SO1902","mm":1,"ev":"Payapati prepares the ground, bows with his crown to the earth and begins to praise the Lord.","cl":[["worship at the Jina's feet is bowing that cuts the deeds","jain","asserted",4,"narrator"]],"ag":["பயாபதி"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:82"},"so:1903":{"book":"soolamani","num":1901,"t":["மூவடிவி னாலிரண்டு சூழ் சுடரு நாண முழுதுலக மூடியெழின் முளைவயிர நாற்றித்","தூவடிவி னாலிலங்கு வெண்குடையி னீழற் சுடரோ யுன்னடிபோற்றிச் சொல்லுவதொன் றுண்டால்","சேவடிக டாமரையின் சேயிதழ்க டீண்டச் சிவந்தனவோ சேவடியின் செங்கதிர்கள் பாயப்","பூவடிவு கொண்டனவோ பொங்கொளிகள் சூழ்ந்து புலங்கொளா வாலெமக்கெம் புண்ணியர்தங் கோவே."],"p":"","r":"The king asks of the Lord, whose white umbrella's shade and gem-bright light put the sun and moon to shame, whether his red feet were reddened by the lotus-petals touching them or whether their red rays took the shape of flowers.","n":"The poet turns the Lord's feet into a question about cause (do the lotuses redden them, or the feet the lotuses?), a conceit of praise, not a doctrine; the stanza is printed in eight half-lines and was joined into four.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thiruvadi","iraivan","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1903","lb":"SO1903","mm":1,"ev":"Payapati's hymn begins with a question about the Lord's red feet.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["பயாபதி","இறைவன்"],"ch":"so:82"},"so:1904":{"book":"soolamani","num":1902,"t":["கருமாலை வெவ்வினைகள் காறளர நூறிக் கடையிலா வொண்ஞானக் கதிர்விரித்தா யென்றும்","அருமாலை நன்னெறியை முன்பயந்தா யென்றும் அடியேமுன் னடிபரவு மாறறிவ தல்லால்","திருமாலே தேனாரு மரவிந்த மேந்துந் திருவணங்கு சேவடியாய் தேவாதி தேவ","பெருமானே நின்பெருமை நன்குணர மாட்டார் பிணங்குவார் தம்மைவினைப் பிணக்கொழிக்க லாமே."],"p":"","r":"The king says the Lord cut the dark garlands of fierce deeds, spread the bright light of unending knowledge and gave a fair path; his servants know only to praise his feet, but those who do not know his greatness and quarrel can have the quarrel of deeds removed.","n":"The last line (பிணங்குவார் தம்மை வினைப் பிணக்கொழிக்கலாமே) is hard: it may say the disputants' own deeds are ended by worship, or the Lord's power could end the quarrel of deeds in them. Also read the half-line division: the stanza is printed in eight half-lines and was joined into four.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","vinai","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1904","lb":"SO1904","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["the Jina destroyed his deeds, spread unending knowledge and taught a good path","jain","asserted",1,"payapati"],["those who dispute the Lord's greatness are bound by a quarrel of deeds that can be undone","jain","reported",4,"payapati"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["பயாபதி","இறைவன்"],"ch":"so:82"},"so:1905":{"book":"soolamani","num":1903,"t":["ஒளியாகி யுலகாகி நீவிரிந்தா யென்கோ உலகெலா நின்னொளியி னுள்ளடங்கிற் றென்கோ","அளியார யுலகநீ யாள்கின்றா யென்கோ அமருலகு தானின்ன தடியடைந்த தென்கோ","விளியாத மெய்ப்பொருளை நீவிரித்தா யென்கோ நீவிரித்த வாறேமெய்ப் பொருள்விரிந்த தென்கோ","தெளியாம லில்லைநின் றிருவடிகண் மெய்ம்மை தெளிந்தாலுஞ் செவ்வனே தெரிந்துரைக்க லாமே."],"p":"","r":"Shall I say you spread as light and as the world, or that the world lies within your light, that you rule the world with grace, that heaven has taken your feet; shall I say you spread true being, or that true being spread as you spread it? Only by understanding your feet will it be known.","n":"The repeated என்கோ (shall I say) lets the hymn put two ways of speaking side by side, the Lord as source and the Lord as container, without choosing; the king says the choice is settled only by understanding.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","arivu","oli"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1905","lb":"SO1905","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["the Jina's light is the world and the world is contained in his light","jain","asserted",1,"payapati"],["the real can be known only by understanding the Lord's feet","jain","asserted",4,"payapati"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["பயாபதி","இறைவன்"],"ch":"so:82"},"so:1906":{"book":"soolamani","num":1904,"t":["களியானை நாற்கோட்ட தொன்றுடைய செல்வன் கண்ணொரா யிரமுடையான் கண்விளக்க மெய்தும்","ஒளியானை யூழி முத லானானை யோங்கி உலகளவு மாகியுயிர் தமக்குறுகண் செய்யா","அளியானை யாரழலஞ் சோதிவாய் சூழ்ந்த அருளாழி யானையிணை யடிபரவு வார்கட்","கெளியானை யெந்தை பெரு மானையே யல்லால் இறையாக வீங்கொருவ ரெண்ணுமா றென்னே."],"p":"","r":"The Lord is the one who gives light to the thousand-eyed Indra, who is the beginning of ages, spreads to the world's measure, harms no living thing, is easy for those who worship his feet; how then can anyone count any other as god?","n":"Indra, the four-tusked elephant's owner of thousand eyes (line 1), is placed beneath the Jina as a receiver of his light, which turns the heaven's lord into a worshipper. The rival god is not named; the claim is aimed at any other claimant.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","arul","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1906","lb":"SO1906","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["no one but the Jina may be reckoned the Lord","jain","asserted",4,"payapati"],["the Lord harms no living being and is easy for his worshippers","jain","asserted",3,"payapati"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["பயாபதி","இறைவன்"],"ch":"so:82"},"so:1907":{"book":"soolamani","num":1905,"t":["தெருளாமை யால் வினவற் பாலதொன் றுண்டு திருவடிகள் செம்பொனா ரரவிந்த மேந்த","இருளாழி யேழுலகுஞ் சூழொளியின் மூழ்க இமையாத செங் கண்ணி னிமையோர்வந் தேத்த","உருளாழி யானு மொளி மணிமுடிமேற் கைவைத் தொருபாலில் வரவுலக நின்னுழைய தாக","அருளாழி முன்செல்லப் பின்செல்வ தென்னோ அடிப்படா தாய்நின்ற வான்ஞால முண்டோ."],"p":"","r":"The king asks why, when the golden feet are held by celestials with unblinking eyes, the dark gods' world is flooded by his light, and the discus-lord with his hand on his crown stands to one side, the wheel of grace goes in front and others follow; is there a heaven that does not lie at his feet?","n":"The question pattern (தெருளாமையால் வினவற்பாலது) is a deferential form: the poet claims not to know why, though the question itself carries the claim. The discus-lord is not named and may be Vishnu or a world-emperor.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","vazhipaadu","perumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1907","lb":"SO1907","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["every heaven lies at the Jina's feet and the wheel of grace goes before the rest","jain","asserted",4,"payapati"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["பயாபதி","இறைவன்"],"ch":"so:82"},"so:1908":{"book":"soolamani","num":1906,"t":["வானோர்த முலகுடைய மானீல வண்ணன் மகிழ்ந்திறைஞ்சு மாலையணி மணிமுடிமேல் வைகா","ஊனாரு மறவாழி யோடைமால் யானை உடையான்ற னொளிமுடியின் மேலுரையோ நிற்கத்","தேனாரு மரவிந்தஞ் சென்றேந்தும் போழ்து திருவடிகள் செந்தோடு தீண்டாவே யாகில்","ஆனாவிம் மூவுலகு மாளுடைய பெம்மான் அடியுறுவா ரின்மைதா மறிவுண்ட தன்றே."],"p":"","r":"The king says that the Lord's feet are above even the talk about the garlanded crown of the dark-blue lord whom the heavens praise, and that if the feet do not touch the lotus offered by those who go to carry it, it means that the lack of those who would stand at his feet has been known.","n":"Lines 3 and 4 are very hard and turn on the sense of அடியுறுவார் இன்மை; the reading here is cautious. If the dark-blue lord is Vishnu, the verse shows a Jain poet placing Vishnu at the Jina's feet, a claim the poet voices, not a view of Vaishnavas.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","thiruvadi","panivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1908","lb":"SO1908","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["the Lord surpasses the dark-blue discus-bearing god, who bows to him","jain","asserted",1,"payapati"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["பயாபதி","இறைவன்"],"ch":"so:82"},"so:1909":{"book":"soolamani","num":1907,"t":["தேனருளி மந்தாரச் செந்தாமந் தாழ்ந்து திரளரைய செம்பவளம் வம்பாக வூறி","வானருளி மாணிக்கச் செங்கதிர்கள் வீசி மதிமருட்டும் வெண்குடையோர் மூன்றுடைய வாமன்","யானருள வேண்டியடி யிணைபணியும் போழ்து இமையவர்கோ னாயிரச் செங்கணான் வந்து","தானருளு மாறென்று தாள்பணியும் போழ்துந் தகையொன்ற தேலிறைமை தக்கதே யன்றே."],"p":"","r":"The speaker says that when even Indra of the thousand red eyes comes and bows at the feet of the lord under the three white umbrellas, seeking his grace, then that lord's godhead is surely fitting.","n":"The verse does not name its speaker or the Lord; both are taken from the praise that closes at 1913, where the king bows to the Lord, so the speaker is the king by inference.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1909","lb":"SO1909","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["the Jina's lordship is such that Indra himself comes and bows at his feet","jain","reported",3,"payapati"]],"ag":["பயாபதி"],"ec":[],"spk":["பயாபதி","இறைவன்"],"ch":"so:83"},"so:1910":{"book":"soolamani","num":1908,"t":["விண்டாங்கு வெவ்வினை வெரூஉவுதிர நூறி விரிகின்ற மெய்ஞ்ஞானச் சுடர் விளக்கு மாட்டிக்","கண்டார்க ணின்னிலைமை கண்டொழுக யானின் கதிர்மயங்கு சோதியாற் கண்விளக்கப் பட்டுத்","தண்டாஅ மரைமலரின்மே னடந்தா யென்றுந் தமனீயப் பொன்னணையின் மேலமர்ந்தா யென்றும்","வண்டார சோகி னிழல் வாயமர்ந்தா யென்றும் வாழ்த்தினால் வாராயோ வானவர்தங்கோவே."],"p":"","r":"The speaker praises the Lord as one who has struck down fierce deeds, lit the lamp of true knowledge, walked on the lotus and sat on the golden seat and under the ashoka's shade, and asks whether the king of the gods will not come when so praised.","n":"The lotus, golden seat and ashoka tree are the stock marks of an arhat's court; the verse uses them as praise epithets, not as events.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","vinai","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1910","lb":"SO1910","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["the Jina destroyed fierce deeds and lit the lamp of true knowledge","jain","reported",1,"payapati"]],"ag":["பயாபதி"],"ec":[],"spk":["பயாபதி","இறைவன்"],"ch":"so:83"},"so:1911":{"book":"soolamani","num":1909,"t":["கருவார்ந்த பொருணிகழ்வுங் காலங்கண் மூன்றுங் கடையிலா நன்ஞானக் கதிரகத்த வாகி","ஒருவாதிங் கவ்வொளியி னின்னுள்ள வாகில் உலகெல்லா நின்னுளத் தேயொளிக்க வேண்டா","திருவார்ந்த தண்மார்ப தேவாதி தேவ திரளரைய செந்தளி ரசோகமர்ந்த செல்வ","வருவாரும் வையகமு நீயும்வே றாகி மணிமேனி மாலே மயக்குவதிங் கென்னோ."],"p":"","r":"The speaker tells the Lord that things that come to be and the three times lie inside his endless light of knowledge, and asks why he should be dazed by a world that is no different from himself.","n":"The last two lines are hard: the verse seems to ask what use there is in an all-knowing lord being dazzled by the world, but the sense of the vocative lines is not firm.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","arivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1911","lb":"SO1911","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["the Jina's endless knowledge holds all existing things and the three times","jain","reported",1,"payapati"]],"ag":["பயாபதி"],"ec":[],"spk":["பயாபதி","இறைவன்"],"ch":"so:83"},"so:1912":{"book":"soolamani","num":1910,"t":["செங்க ணெடுமாலே செறிந்திலங்கு சோதித் திருமுயங்கு மூர்த்தியாய் செய்யதா மரையின்","அங்கணடி வைத்தருளு மாதியா யாழி அறவரசே யென்றுநின் னடிபணிவ தல்லால்","எங்க ணிட ரகலுமா றிந்நிலைமை யெய்தி இருளுலக நீக்குமரு டருகநீ யென்று","வெங்கணிரு வினையையற வென்றாய்முன்னின்று விண்ணப்பஞ் செய்யும் விழுத்தகைமை யுண்டோ."],"p":"","r":"The speaker addresses the Lord as the holy king of dharma and says that bowing at his feet and begging him to remove their distress and light the dark world is a merit greater than any, since he has wholly conquered the two deeds.","n":"The two deeds (இருவினை) are read as good and bad deed; the verse does not say so.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","vinai","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1912","lb":"SO1912","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["the Jina has completely conquered the two kinds of deed","jain","reported",4,"payapati"]],"ag":["பயாபதி"],"ec":[],"spk":["பயாபதி","இறைவன்"],"ch":"so:83"},"so:1913":{"book":"soolamani","num":1911,"t":["என்றுநன் கேத்தி யிறைஞ்சி யிறைவனைச்","சென்றுயர் சேவடிச் சேடந் தலைவைத்து","வென்றவன் கோயில் வலங்கொண்டு மீண்டுமொர்","பொன்றவழ் வேதிகை மண்டபம் புக்கான்."],"p":"","r":"Having praised and bowed to the Lord, the king lays his head at the conqueror's feet, circles his shrine, and enters another golden pillared hall.","n":"The king is not named here; he is the subject carried over from the praise.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","thuravu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1913","lb":"SO1913","mm":1,"ev":"The king worships the Jina, circles his shrine and enters the pillared platform hall.","cl":[],"ag":["பயாபதி"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:83"},"so:1914":{"book":"soolamani","num":1912,"t":["ஆங்கொர் முனிவ னருந்தவப் பல்குணந்","தாங்கிய மாமலை யன்ன தகையவன்","பூங்கமழ் சேவடிப் போதுதன் பொன்முடி","தாங்கிய தாம நுதியாற் றுடைத்தான்."],"p":"","r":"In that hall sits a sage, firm like a great mountain with his many austere virtues, and the king wipes the dust from his lotus feet with the garland on his golden crown.","n":"The king's crown-garland becomes a cloth for the sage's feet: the ruler lowers the sign of his rank before an ascetic.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thavam","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1914","lb":"SO1914","mm":1,"ev":"The king touches the sage's feet with the tip of the garland on his crown.","cl":[],"ag":["பயாபதி","முனிவன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:83"},"so:1915":{"book":"soolamani","num":1913,"t":["ஆற்றி லமைந்த வருந்தவத் தால்வினை","ஊற்றுச் செறித்த வொருபெயர் மாதவன்","மாற்றரு மந்திர வாய்மொழி யாயிடை","ஏற்றன கொண்டாங் கிறைவ னிருந்தான்."],"p":"","r":"The sage, whose great austerity has closed the springs that feed deeds and whose word is a mantra none can turn back, sits, and the king takes the seat that was fitting.","n":"ஊற்றுச் செறித்த is a Jain image of closing the channels of inflow (asrava); the verse has the word for springs only.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thavam","vinai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1915","lb":"SO1915","mm":1,"ev":"The king takes the seat offered him before the sage.","cl":[["austerity stops the springs from which deeds flow in","jain","asserted",2,"narrator"]],"ag":["முனிவன்","பயாபதி"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:83"},"so:1916":{"book":"soolamani","num":1914,"t":["வணங்கி மணிமுடி மன்ன னிருப்ப","மணங்கமழ் கண்ணியர் மந்திர மாந்தர்","அணங்கு மறவமிழ் தூட்டி யடிகள்","பிணங்கும் பிறவிகள் பேர்த்துய்மி னென்றார்."],"p":"","r":"As the king sits bowed, the garlanded ministers say to the sage that he should feed them the nectar that ends torment and turn aside the tangled births so that they may be saved.","n":"The ministers ask for the teaching as a drink of nectar, so the plea is for relief from rebirth, not for advice on statecraft, which they could not give at 1857.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["piravi","thuravu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1916","lb":"SO1916","mm":1,"ev":"The ministers ask the sage to feed them teaching that will turn away their births.","cl":[["a sage's teaching can turn away the tangle of births","jain","reported",4,"ministers"]],"ag":["அருத்தநூலவர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["அருத்தநூலவர்","முனிவன்"],"ch":"so:83"},"so:1917":{"book":"soolamani","num":1915,"t":["வன்ன மணிமுடி மன்ன னிருந்திட","இன்னியற் செல்வ மெனைப்பல வெய்திய","மன்ன னறியுந் திருவற மாண்பினை","என்னை வினவிய தென்னைகோ லென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"A speaker, with the king seated on his throne, remarks that the king who has had so much of the sweet wealth is asking about the holy greatness of dharma, and asks what it is that he is asking.","n":"Who asks and who answers is not fixed by the words: this is read as the king's turn to speak, but it could be the sage testing him.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["aram","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1917","lb":"SO1917","mm":1,"ev":"The king puts a question about dharma, or is questioned about it, before the sage.","cl":[],"ag":["பயாபதி"],"ec":[],"spk":["பயாபதி",""],"ch":"so:83"},"so:1918":{"book":"soolamani","num":1916,"t":["அடிக ளடிசி லமைந்த தயில்வான்","முடிய முயலு முறைமை யறியான்","நெடிதின துவறு நீர்மையு மோரான்","வடிவமர் செல்வன் வகையு மதுவே."],"p":"","r":"The verse says of one who eats the food laid before him that he does not know how to bring his course to an end and does not mind its long-hated nature, and says the same of the handsome lord.","n":"The verse is obscure: it seems to describe a lord who enjoys his meals and has not learned how to end his round of births. The identification of the handsome lord with the king is a guess.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","yaakkai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1918","lb":"SO1918","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"so:83"},"so:1919":{"book":"soolamani","num":1917,"t":["மந்திர மாந்தர் மொழிதலும் வானிடை","அந்தரம் வாழு மமரர் வழிபடும்","தந்திர ஞான்ற தவத்திற் கரசனும்","இந்திர னன்னாற் கெடுத்துரைக் கின்றான்."],"p":"","r":"After the ministers have spoken, the king of austerity, whom the gods of the sky honour and whom the rule of his teaching has made great, begins to set out the matter for the king as if for Indra.","n":"The king is likened to Indra, as the sage is likened to a king of austerity: each is named in the other's terms.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thavam","thuravu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1919","lb":"SO1919","mm":1,"ev":"The sage begins his exposition for the king.","cl":[],"ag":["முனிவன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:83"},"so:1920":{"book":"soolamani","num":1918,"t":["கதியுங் கதியினுட் டுப்புமத் துப்பின்","விதிசெய் வினையும் வினைவெல் வகையு","மதியவர் காமுறும் வீட்டது மாண்பும்","அதிபதி கேளென் றருந்தவன் சொன்னான்."],"p":"","r":"The great ascetic tells the lord to listen, and lists what he will tell: the states of rebirth, the deceit within them, the deed that fixes fate, the ways of conquering deeds, and the glory of the release the wise desire.","n":"This is a table of contents for what follows: hells, then (outside this chapter) the other states, then deeds and release.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["piravi","vinai","veedu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1920","lb":"SO1920","mm":1,"ev":"The sage sets out his five topics: rebirths, deeds and their conquest, and release.","cl":[],"ag":["முனிவன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிவன்","பயாபதி"],"ch":"so:83"},"so:1921":{"book":"soolamani","num":1919,"t":["ஓடுஞ் சகடத் துருளு மொளிகொள","வீடி லொருவன் விசிறும் வளையமும்","ஆடுந் துகளு மெனச்சுழன் றாருயிர்","நாடுங் கதியவை நான்குள கண்டாய்."],"p":"","r":"The sage says that souls whirl like the turning cart wheel, the ring thrown by one without release and the drifting dust, and that there are four states they seek.","n":"Wheel, ring and dust are three pictures of the same turning: the soul has no fixed place and is moved by what it has done.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["piravi","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1921","lb":"SO1921","mm":1,"ev":"The sage names four destinies that turn the souls.","cl":[["souls whirl through four destinies as a wheel, a ring and dust turn","jain","asserted",3,"muni"]],"ag":["முனிவன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிவன்","பயாபதி"],"ch":"so:83"},"so:1922":{"book":"soolamani","num":1920,"t":["நரகர் விலங்கு மனிதர்நற் றேவர்","விரவி னவர்தம் விகற்ப முரைப்பிற்","பெருகு முரையென்று பெய்ம்மலர்த் தாரோன்","உருக வொருவா றுறுவ னுரைத்தான்."],"p":"","r":"The sage names the four states as the hell-dwellers, the beasts, men and the good gods, and says an account of their kinds would grow long, so the king, whose heart melts, hears one way of telling it.","n":"The sage promises brevity here and then spends the rest of the chapter on the hells.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["piravi","naragam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1922","lb":"SO1922","mm":1,"ev":"The sage names the four states and says he will tell them briefly.","cl":[["there are four states of birth: hell, animal, human and divine","jain","asserted",1,"muni"]],"ag":["முனிவன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிவன்","பயாபதி"],"ch":"so:83"},"so:1923":{"book":"soolamani","num":1921,"t":["கீழா நரகங் கிளத்தும் படலங்கள்","ஏழா யிருபத் திரட்டியோ டொன்பது","போழா மவற்றுள் ளவர்கள் புகலிடம்","பாழா மிலக்கமெண் பஃதுட னான்கே."],"p":"","r":"The sage says the lower hells are cut into forty-nine layers, and that the dwellings of those who go to them number eighty-four lakhs.","n":"The numbers rest on a reading of ஏழாயிருபத்திரட்டியோடொன்பது as seven hells made of forty-nine layers; the verse gives only the numbers.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["naragam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1923","lb":"SO1923","mm":1,"ev":"The sage counts the layers and dwellings of the lower hells.","cl":[["the lower hells have forty-nine layers and eighty-four lakh dwellings","jain","asserted",2,"muni"]],"ag":["முனிவன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிவன்","பயாபதி"],"ch":"so:83"},"so:1924":{"book":"soolamani","num":1922,"t":["இருளி னிருளு மிருளும் புகையும்","அருளி லளறு மணலும் பரலும்","மருளின் மணியு மெனவிப் பெயர","பொருளி னரகங்கள் போதரக் கொண்ணீ."],"p":"","r":"The sage says the hells are named by what they are: darkness of darkness, darkness, smoke, merciless mire, sand, gravel and the bright stone, and the king should take each as its name says.","n":"The last of the seven, மணி, is read as the gem-stone of the first hell; the verse says only மருளின் மணி.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["naragam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1924","lb":"SO1924","mm":1,"ev":"The sage names the seven hells.","cl":[["the seven hells are named for darkness, smoke, mire, sand and gravel","jain","asserted",3,"muni"]],"ag":["முனிவன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிவன்","பயாபதி"],"ch":"so:83"},"so:1925":{"book":"soolamani","num":1923,"t":["ஆங்க ணரக மடைந்தார் படுதுயர்","ஈங்க ணுரைப்பி னெமக்கும் பனிவரும்","வாங்கி யவற்றின் முதலதன் வார்த்தைகள்","பாங்கின் மொழிவன் பனிமலர்த் தாரோய்."],"p":"","r":"The sage says that the pain of those who have gone into the hells would make even the tellers shudder, and that he will give the words for the first of them.","n":"The sage says the telling itself would chill the speakers: the verse turns the description into a warning to a listener already uneasy.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naragam","kootru"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1925","lb":"SO1925","mm":1,"ev":"The sage begins to describe the first hell.","cl":[],"ag":["முனிவன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிவன்","பயாபதி"],"ch":"so:83"},"so:1926":{"book":"soolamani","num":1924,"t":["பத்தடம் பத்தொடு மூன்றா மவற்றிடை","ஒத்த வுறையு ளிலக்கமொர் முப்பது","கொத்தெரி வெம்பவர் கும்பிக் குழியவை","இத்துணை யென்பதொ ரெல்லை யிலவே."],"p":"","r":"The sage says the first hell has thirteen levels with thirty lakh of dwellings, pits blazing with fire, and that there is no end to their number.","n":"","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["naragam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1926","lb":"SO1926","mm":1,"ev":"The sage counts the pits of the first hell.","cl":[["the first hell has thirteen levels and thirty lakh blazing pits","jain","asserted",2,"muni"]],"ag":["முனிவன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிவன்","பயாபதி"],"ch":"so:83"},"so:1927":{"book":"soolamani","num":1925,"t":["பேழைப் பிளவும் பிலத்தின் முகங்களும்","தாழிப் பதலையும் போலுந் தகையன","ஆழப் பரந்த வழுக லளறவை","பீழைப் பதகர் பிறக்கு மிடமே."],"p":"","r":"The pits are like chests split open, like cave mouths, like jars and pots, deep and wide with slippery mire, and are the place where wicked men are born.","n":"Birth, not arrival, is the word: the hell-dweller is born in the pit rather than sent to it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naragam","vinai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1927","lb":"SO1927","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["the wicked are born in pits of mire in the hells","jain","asserted",4,"muni"]],"ag":["முனிவன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிவன்","பயாபதி"],"ch":"so:83"},"so:1928":{"book":"soolamani","num":1926,"t":["குழிபடு கும்பிக் கருவாய் பெருகி","அழுக லுடம்பிவை யங்கு நிறைந்தால்","வழுவி யனல்படு பாறைக் கண் வைகிப்","புழுவி னுருள்வ பொரிவ பொடிவ."],"p":"","r":"When the pits have taken shape and wailing bodies fill them, the beings slip onto burning rock and lie rolling, scorched and crumbled like worms.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naragam","yaakkai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1928","lb":"SO1928","mm":1,"ev":"The pit-born beings fall on burning rock and writhe like worms.","cl":[],"ag":["முனிவன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிவன்","பயாபதி"],"ch":"so:83"},"so:1929":{"book":"soolamani","num":1927,"t":["புழுவி னுருண்டு பொடிந்தவர் பொங்கி","எழுவர் புகையைந் தெழுந்தபின் மீட்டும்","வழுவினர் வீழ்வர் மறிந்துமவ் வாறே","ஒழிவிலா வேதனை யுள்ளள வெல்லாம்."],"p":"","r":"Crumbled like worms they swell and rise, and after the smoke has risen they fall again and tumble in the same way, in a torment that never ceases for as long as their life lasts.","n":"The pain is endless only for the life-span there: the verse limits the torment to it, not to eternity.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naragam","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1929","lb":"SO1929","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["hell torment does not pause for the whole span of life in hell","jain","asserted",4,"muni"]],"ag":["முனிவன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிவன்","பயாபதி"],"ch":"so:83"},"so:1930":{"book":"soolamani","num":1928,"t":["அந்தோ வறனே வெனவழைப் பார்களை","வந்தோ மெனச்சொல்லி வாங்குபவ ரில்லை","வெந்தே விளிந்து மொழியார் விழுத்துயர்","முந்தே வினைய முயன்றனர் புக்கார்."],"p":"","r":"Those who cry 'alas, dharma' find no one who says 'we have come' and takes them out; burned and still not dying, they entered the pit through their own earlier striving in deeds.","n":"The cry is to dharma, not to a god, and no rescuer answers; the verse makes the cause the sufferers' own earlier striving, not a judge's decree.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naragam","vinai","oozh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1930","lb":"SO1930","mm":1,"ev":"The sufferers cry out and no one answers, because their own earlier deeds sent them in.","cl":[["those in hell entered it through their own earlier deeds and cannot be rescued","jain","asserted",4,"muni"]],"ag":["நரகம் அடைந்தார்"],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிவன்","பயாபதி"],"ch":"so:83"},"so:1931":{"book":"soolamani","num":1929,"t":["அன்னணம் வேதனை யெய்து மவர்களைத்","துன்னி யுளர்சிலர் தூர்த்தத் தொழிலவர்","முன்னதிற் செய்த வினையின் முறைபல","இன்னண மெய்துமி னென்றிடர் செய்வார்."],"p":"","r":"Some of cruel and licentious craft crowd around the sufferers and torment them, telling them to take in turn the many fruits of the deed they did before.","n":"The identity of the tormentors is not given beyond their trade; they are read as hell's attendants because they act on the sufferers.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["naragam","vinai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1931","lb":"SO1931","mm":1,"ev":"Hell's attendants torment the sufferers and tell them to take the fruit of their own earlier deeds.","cl":[["the tormentors tell the sufferers they are receiving the fruit of earlier deeds","jain","reported",3,"hell_wardens"]],"ag":["தூர்த்தத் தொழிலவர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:83"},"so:1932":{"book":"soolamani","num":1930,"t":["தங்கிருட் போதிற் றலைச்சென் றயன்மனை","அங்கு மகிழ்ந்தா ளவளிவள் காணெனச்","செங்கன லேயென வெம்பிய செம்பினில்","பொங்கனற் பாவைகள் புல்லப் புணர்ப்பார்."],"p":"","r":"In the dark of night the guilty went to a neighbour's house and took pleasure there; in hell the wardens say 'see, this is she' and make them embrace figures of blazing fire set in copper heated to the red of coals.","n":"The punishment answers the offence in kind: the secret night-visit becomes a forced embrace, and the wardens' mocking 'see, this is she' repeats the lover's own pleasure back to him.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naragam","piranil","vinai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1932","lb":"SO1932","mm":1,"ev":"Hell's wardens make adulterers embrace fiery copper figures of women.","cl":[["adultery with a neighbour's wife is repaid in hell by a matching embrace of fire","jain","asserted",4,"sage"]],"ag":["தூர்த்தத் தொழிலவர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிவர்",""],"ch":"so:84"},"so:1933":{"book":"soolamani","num":1931,"t":["கொள்ளு மிவையெனக் கூட்டில் வளர்த்ததம்","வள்ளுகிர்ப் பேழ்வாய் ஞமலி வடிவுகள்","அள்ளிக் கதுவ வலறி யயலது","முள்ளிற் புனைமர மேற முயல்வார்."],"p":"","r":"Dogs the wardens have raised in cages for this purpose, with sharp claws and huge gaping mouths, tear at the victims, who scream and try to climb a tree built of thorns.","n":"The dogs are reared for the task, so the torment is an orderly institution, not the chance cruelty of passing demons.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naragam","vinai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1933","lb":"SO1933","mm":1,"ev":"Hell's dogs seize the victims, who flee up a thorn tree.","cl":[],"ag":["தூர்த்தத் தொழிலவர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிவர்",""],"ch":"so:84"},"so:1934":{"book":"soolamani","num":1932,"t":["மேயப் பருவம் விரும்பிய மீனினம்","காயப் பெருந்தடி காண்மி னிவையெனத்","தீயைப் பருகிய செப்புத் திரளவை","வாயைப் பெருகப் பிளந்து மடுப்பார்."],"p":"","r":"The wardens show the victims great lumps of flesh as the food they craved and, saying 'look at these', pour into their open mouths masses of copper that has drunk fire.","n":"The offered 'flesh' is the bait the offender once craved, which makes the metal a grim parody of the meal.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naragam","pulaal","kollaamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1934","lb":"SO1934","mm":1,"ev":"Wardens force molten copper into the mouths of those who ate flesh.","cl":[["those who killed and ate fish and flesh are made to swallow molten metal in hell","jain","asserted",4,"sage"]],"ag":["தூர்த்தத் தொழிலவர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிவர்",""],"ch":"so:84"},"so:1935":{"book":"soolamani","num":1933,"t":["மறிப்பல கொன்று மடப்பிணை வீழ்த்துங்","கறிப்பல வெஃகிக் கறித்தவர் தம்மை","உறுப்புறுப் பாக வரிந்தரிந் தூட்டி","ஒறுப்பர் சிலரை யவரு மொருபால்."],"p":"","r":"Those who killed many deer and fawns and ate the meat are cut limb by limb and fed their own limbs by the wardens, in one quarter of hell.","n":"The penalty reverses the act exactly: the eater becomes the carved animal and the food together.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naragam","pulaal","kollaamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1935","lb":"SO1935","mm":1,"ev":"Wardens carve meat-eaters limb by limb and feed them the pieces.","cl":[["killing animals for meat is punished in hell by being carved and fed one's own flesh","jain","asserted",4,"sage"]],"ag":["தூர்த்தத் தொழிலவர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிவர்",""],"ch":"so:84"},"so:1936":{"book":"soolamani","num":1934,"t":["இடைப்பல சொல்லி யெளியவர் தம்மை","உடைப்பொருள் வெஃகி யொறுத்த பயத்தான்","முடைப்பொலி மேனியை முண்மத் திகையாற்","புடைப்ப நடுங்கிப் புரள்வ ரொருசார்."],"p":"","r":"Those who used many words against the weak and, wanting their goods, punished them are beaten on their foul bodies with thorn clubs and tremble and roll, as the fruit of that wrong.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naragam","vinai","kodungol"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1936","lb":"SO1936","mm":1,"ev":"Wardens beat with thorn clubs those who wronged the weak for their property.","cl":[["robbing and oppressing the weak for their wealth is repaid with beating in hell","jain","asserted",2,"sage"]],"ag":["தூர்த்தத் தொழிலவர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிவர்",""],"ch":"so:84"},"so:1937":{"book":"soolamani","num":1935,"t":["வெறுப்பன வேசெய்து மேலா யவரைக்","குறிப்பல சொல்லிய நாவைக் கொடிற்றால்","பறிப்பர் பரிய வயிரமுட் கொண்டு","செறிப்ப ருகிர்வழி யேறச் சிலரே."],"p":"","r":"Those who did hateful things and spoke slander against their betters have the tongue torn out with pincers, and diamond-hard thorns are driven under the nails of some.","n":"The word the verse uses for the slander (குறிப்பு) is a hint or insinuation, so even veiled speech is punished with the tongue.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naragam","purangooral","nindhai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1937","lb":"SO1937","mm":1,"ev":"Wardens pull out slanderers' tongues and drive thorns under their nails.","cl":[["slandering those above one is repaid by tearing out the tongue","jain","asserted",2,"sage"]],"ag":["தூர்த்தத் தொழிலவர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிவர்",""],"ch":"so:84"},"so:1938":{"book":"soolamani","num":1936,"t":["பொரிப்பர் சிறைசெய்து பொங்கெரி மாட்டிக்","கரிப்பர் கனல்படு காரக லேற்றித்","திரிப்பர் பலரையுஞ் செக்குர லுட்பெய்","துரிப்ப ருடலை யவரு மொருபால்."],"p":"","r":"Wardens roast the imprisoned in blazing fire, boil them in heated caustic, make many turn inside an oil-press mortar and flay the body, in one quarter of hell.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naragam","kolai","vinai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1938","lb":"SO1938","mm":1,"ev":"Wardens roast, boil, press and flay the hell-beings.","cl":[],"ag":["தூர்த்தத் தொழிலவர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிவர்",""],"ch":"so:84"},"so:1939":{"book":"soolamani","num":1937,"t":["பழுப்பல பற்றிப் பறிப்பர் பதைப்ப","மழுப்பல கொண்டவர் மார்பம் பிளப்பர்","கழுப்பல வேற்றி யகைப்பர் கடிதே","விழுப்பெரும் பூணோய் வினையின் விளைவே."],"p":"","r":"Wardens seize and tear, split chests with axes and impale on stakes quickly, and the sage tells the king, wearing great ornaments, that this is the fruit of their deeds.","n":"The last line turns a catalogue of cruelties into a verdict: no judge is named, only vinai ripening.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naragam","vinai","oozh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1939","lb":"SO1939","mm":1,"ev":"Wardens tear, split and impale the hell-beings while the sage names it the ripening of deeds.","cl":[["the torments of hell are the ripening of the sufferers' own deeds","jain","asserted",4,"sage"]],"ag":["தூர்த்தத் தொழிலவர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிவர்","பயாபதி"],"ch":"so:84"},"so:1940":{"book":"soolamani","num":1938,"t":["பறிப்பர் பலரவர் கைகளைப் பற்றிச்","செறிப்பர் விரல்களைச் சீவுவர் மேனி","நெறிப்ப ரெலும்பு நிரந்துடன் வீழ","மறிப்பர் மலைமிசை மற்று மொருசார்."],"p":"","r":"Wardens seize the hands, cut off the fingers, scrape the body, crush the bones so that they fall apart, and hurl others down from a mountain.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naragam","kolai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1940","lb":"SO1940","mm":1,"ev":"Wardens maim the hell-beings and throw them from a mountain.","cl":[],"ag":["தூர்த்தத் தொழிலவர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிவர்",""],"ch":"so:84"},"so:1941":{"book":"soolamani","num":1939,"t":["சாவ நலிந்திடுந் தண்ணீர்ப் பிணிபெரி(து)","ஆவென் றலறு மவரையரு நஞ்சின்","வாவிகள் காட்டலின் மண்டி மடுத்துண்டு","நாவு மழுக நரல்வ ரொருசார்."],"p":"","r":"Hell-beings tortured by a terrible thirst cry for water; the wardens show them pools of rare poison, and they rush, drink, and cry out with rotting tongues.","n":"The relief offered is itself the harm: the pools are the form their thirst takes.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["naragam","pasi","vinai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1941","lb":"SO1941","mm":1,"ev":"Wardens show poison pools to the thirsty, who drink and cry out.","cl":[],"ag":["தூர்த்தத் தொழிலவர்","நரகர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிவர்",""],"ch":"so:84"},"so:1942":{"book":"soolamani","num":1940,"t":["அழலிவை யாற்றோ மெனவழன் றோடி","நிழலிவை யாமென நீள்பொழிற் புக்கால்","தழல்வளி தாமே தலைவழி சிந்தக்","கழல்வனர் வீழ்ந்து கரிவ ரொருசார்."],"p":"","r":"Unable to bear the heat they run, and entering a long grove thinking it shade, fire-wind pours on their heads and they fall down burning.","n":"Shade is another false relief, like the pools of the previous verse.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naragam","vinai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1942","lb":"SO1942","mm":1,"ev":"The hell-beings flee into a grove that burns them.","cl":[],"ag":["நரகர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிவர்",""],"ch":"so:84"},"so:1943":{"book":"soolamani","num":1941,"t":["முல்லை முகைமலர்த் தாரோய் முதற்புரை","அல்ல லெனைப்பல வாயிர கோடிகள்","எல்லையி றுன்ப மிவற்றி னிருமடி","புல்லினர் கீழ்க்கீழ்ப் புரைபுரை தோறும்."],"p":"","r":"The sage tells the king, wearing a garland of jasmine buds, that in the first layer there are thousands of crores of sorrows, and that each lower layer holds twice that unbounded pain.","n":"The first-person 'I told' in the next verse shows that the whole description of the hells since 1932 is the sage's speech to the king.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naragam","vinai","marumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1943","lb":"SO1943","mm":1,"ev":"The sage tells Payapati that the pain of hell doubles with each lower layer.","cl":[["the sorrows of hell double in each lower layer","jain","asserted",3,"sage"]],"ag":["முனிவர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிவர்","பயாபதி"],"ch":"so:84"},"so:1944":{"book":"soolamani","num":1942,"t":["விளிவி றுயரொடு மேற்பொங்கி வீழும்","அளவு மவர்கண் முறையும் பிறவும்","அளவில் கீழ்க்கீ ழிரட்டி யறைந்தேன்","உளரொளி ஞானமஃ தொன்று மொழித்தே."],"p":"","r":"He says that he has told the span of the sufferers' fall, their order and other matters, doubling in each lower layer, leaving aside the one thing he calls shining knowledge.","n":"The last line seems to set aside a knowledge that only a knower can tell, but the grammar is doubtful.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["naragam","gnaanam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1944","lb":"SO1944","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["the pain of the hells grows twofold in each lower layer","jain","asserted",3,"sage"]],"ag":["முனிவர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிவர்",""],"ch":"so:84"},"so:1945":{"book":"soolamani","num":1943,"t":["பெய்யா வருநஞ்சும் பேரழற் குட்டமும்","செய்யாக் குழிகளுஞ் சீநீர்த் தடங்களும்","நையா நரக ரிடமிவை நாறினும்","உய்யா பிறவுயி ரோசனைக் கண்ணே."],"p":"","r":"The poison that is not rained, the great pits of fire, the pits no one dug and the ponds of foul water make such a stench that no other living thing survives within a yojana of the hell.","n":"The stench is described as a force that kills outsiders, which marks hell as a sealed region.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naragam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1945","lb":"SO1945","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["the stench of hell kills other living beings within a yojana around it","jain","asserted",4,"sage"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிவர்",""],"ch":"so:84"},"so:1946":{"book":"soolamani","num":1944,"t":["எழுவின் முழமூன் றறுவிர லென்ப","வழுவின் முதலதன் கீழ்ப்புரை வாழ்வார்","ஒழிவில பொங்குவ ரோசனை யேழ்மேன்","முழுவிலைஞ் ஞூற்றொடு முக்கா வதமே."],"p":"","r":"The first layer's beings are given a measure in bows, cubits and fingers, and the beings of the lower layers grow larger without limit, to a size stated in yojanas and kavatams.","n":"The measures are stated in units whose reading is hard to fix.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["naragam","yaakkai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1946","lb":"SO1946","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["the bodies of hell-beings are larger in each lower layer","jain","asserted",3,"sage"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிவர்",""],"ch":"so:84"},"so:1947":{"book":"soolamani","num":1945,"t":["ஆண்டுச் சிறுமை பதினா யிரமுள","நீண்டவர் வாழ்நா ணிறைவு கடலெல்லை","ஈண்டிதன் கீழ்க்கீழ்ப் பெருகிவரு மெங்கும்","வேண்டிற் சிறுமைதம் மேலோர் நிறைவே."],"p":"","r":"The lowest life span is ten thousand years, the longest life is the measure of the sea, and the life spans increase going down; the least in each lower layer is the greatest of the one above.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naragam","aayul","kaalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1947","lb":"SO1947","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["the life span of hell-beings rises in each lower layer, each layer's minimum equalling the layer above's maximum","jain","asserted",4,"sage"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிவர்",""],"ch":"so:84"},"so:1948":{"book":"soolamani","num":1946,"t":["மூன்று மொரேழு மொழிபஃதும் பத்தினோடே","ஏன்ற நல்லேழு மிருபத் திரண்டுமென்","றான்ற வலைகடன் முப்பத்து மூன்றுமென்","றூன்றின கீழ்க்கீ ழுயர்ந்தன வாழ்நாள்."],"p":"","r":"The life spans are listed in sea-measures: three, seven, ten, seventeen, twenty-two and thirty-three, rising down the layers.","n":"The list gives six figures, with the first layer's one sea measure implied.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naragam","aayul","kaalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1948","lb":"SO1948","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["hell life spans rise by fixed sea-measures down the seven layers","jain","asserted",4,"sage"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிவர்",""],"ch":"so:84"},"so:1949":{"book":"soolamani","num":1947,"t":["முடைகொண் முழுச்செவி மொண்பற் பதகர்","உடையந் தலியிருப் புண்பது நஞ்சே","புடையவர் காணிய போர்நனி மூட்ட","மிடைவர் படுகொண்டு வேதனை மிக்கார்."],"p":"","r":"The foul-eared, bright-toothed wicked ones eat poison, and the others of the hell, kindling a fight for them to watch, crowd in, fall and suffer great pain.","n":"Some words (உடையந்தலி) are corrupt, so the exact sense of the first two lines is uncertain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["naragam","vinai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1949","lb":"SO1949","mm":1,"ev":"Hell's inhabitants are set to fight while others watch, and suffer.","cl":[],"ag":["தூர்த்தத் தொழிலவர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிவர்",""],"ch":"so:84"},"so:1950":{"book":"soolamani","num":1948,"t":["வேவா ரழலுள் விளியா ரளற்றினுள்","ஓவார் புகையு ளுகையா வுழல்பவர்","ஆவா வளிய நரகர் படுதுயர்","ஏவார் சிலையா யிரங்குந் தகைத்தே."],"p":"","r":"They are cooked in fire but do not die, they lie in the mire and do not perish, they wander in smoke without rest, and their pitiable pain would make even a hunter with arrow and bow feel pity.","n":"The hunter who would pity is the very kind of killer that earlier verses of the list put in hell.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naragam","vinai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1950","lb":"SO1950","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["hell-beings suffer without dying","jain","asserted",1,"sage"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிவர்",""],"ch":"so:84"},"so:1951":{"book":"soolamani","num":1949,"t":["ஆங்குண் டெனப்படு மாழ்துயர் வீழ்பவர்","தேங்கொண்ட பைந்தார்த் திறன்மன்ன யாரெனில்","தாங்கொண்ட தார மறுத்துப் பிறன்வரைப்","பூங்கொண்டை மாரைப் புணரு மவரும்."],"p":"","r":"Asking who falls into the deep sorrow of that place, the sage answers the king, wearing a fresh garland, that it is those who give up their own wives and lie with other men's women with flowered hair.","n":"From here the sage turns from the torments to the sins that earn them.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naragam","piranil","vinai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1951","lb":"SO1951","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["those who abandon their wives for other men's wives fall into hell","jain","asserted",4,"sage"]],"ag":["முனிவர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிவர்","பயாபதி"],"ch":"so:84"},"so:1952":{"book":"soolamani","num":1950,"t":["உள்ளங் கொடியா ருயிர்க்கொலை காதலர்","வெள்ளங் கொடியன மேவிப் பிறன்பொருள்","கொள்ளுங் கொடுமைக் குணத்தின் மனித்தரும்","நள்ளலர்ச் சாய்த்தோய் நரக மடைவார்."],"p":"","r":"Cruel-hearted people who love killing, and those with a cruel nature who take others' goods, will reach hell, the sage says to the king who struck down his foes.","n":"The address to the king as one who felled enemies leaves open that the sage may point at the king's own acts.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naragam","kolai","kalavu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1952","lb":"SO1952","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["killing and taking others' property lead to hell","jain","asserted",4,"sage"]],"ag":["முனிவர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிவர்","பயாபதி"],"ch":"so:84"},"so:1953":{"book":"soolamani","num":1951,"t":["நல்லறங் காய்ந்து நலிந்து பொருள்படைத்","தில்லறஞ் செய்யா திறுகு பவர்களும்","புல்லறம் புல்லாப் புலவரை வைதுரைத்","தல்லறஞ் செய்யு மறிவில் லவரும்."],"p":"","r":"Those who scorn good dharma, gather wealth with toil, and die hard-fisted without doing householder's dharma, and those who revile poets who do not take up petty dharma and do wrong from ignorance, are also in the list.","n":"Householder's dharma is here a duty whose neglect is punished, not a lesser way next to renunciation.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naragam","eegai","aram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1953","lb":"SO1953","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["hoarding wealth without householder's giving leads to hell","jain","asserted",3,"sage"],["reviling poets and doing wrong through ignorance leads to hell","jain","asserted",4,"sage"]],"ag":["முனிவர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிவர்",""],"ch":"so:84"},"so:1954":{"book":"soolamani","num":1952,"t":["தெண்டிரை வாழுந் திமிலுங் கலங்களுங்","கொண்டிரை யாகவுயிர் கொல்லுஞ் சாதியும்","கண்டிடு காதனை நின்னாற் செயப்படும்","தண்டிக டம்மொடுஞ் சார்த்தினை கொண்ணீ."],"p":"","r":"The sage says the king has joined to the list the sea-dwelling fishers with boats and rafts who take life for bait, and those the king himself had punished.","n":"The last line may say that the king has already put such people in the same company as those he punished; it is unclear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["naragam","kolai","vinai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1954","lb":"SO1954","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["those who kill living beings for food from the sea fall into hell","jain","asserted",3,"sage"]],"ag":["முனிவர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிவர்","பயாபதி"],"ch":"so:84"},"so:1955":{"book":"soolamani","num":1953,"t":["ஆறா நரக வழலினு ளாழ்பவர்","தேறார் திருவறந் தேறினு நல்வத","மேறார் சிலர்நனி யேறினு நில்லலர்","வேறா யினிச்சொல்ல வேண்டுவ துண்டோ."],"p":"","r":"The sage closes his account of hell: those sunk in its unquenchable fire do not grasp the good teaching, and some who do rise to a better state do not stay there, so nothing more need be said of them.","n":"It closes the long hell passage (1932 to 1954) and turns, in the next verse, to animal births, so the sarukkam's tour of the bad states runs hell first, then animals, then the island peoples.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["naragam","vinai","aram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1955","lb":"SO1955","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["some souls who rise to a better state do not remain in it, and those in hell do not grasp the dharma","jain","asserted",3,"sage"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிவர்","பயாபதி"],"ch":"so:85"},"so:1956":{"book":"soolamani","num":1954,"t":["விலங்குடன் சாதி விரிப்பிற் பெருகும்","உலங்கொண்ட தோண்மன்ன வோரறி வாதி","புலங்கொண்ட வைம்பொறி யீறாப் புணர்ந்த","நலங்கொண்ட ஞாலத்தி னாடி யுணர்நீ."],"p":"","r":"The sage tells the king to consider the animal births as they multiply, running from the one-sensed up to those that end with the five senses, spread through the good earth.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["piravi","uyir"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1956","lb":"SO1956","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["animal births are graded by number of senses, from one to five","jain","asserted",2,"sage"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிவர்","பயாபதி"],"ch":"so:85"},"so:1957":{"book":"soolamani","num":1955,"t":["நின்று வருந்து நிகோதப் பிறவியுள்","ஒன்றறி வெய்தி யுழக்கு முயிர்பல","அன்றிச் சிறிதுண் டவற்றினு மவ்வழிச்","சென்று பெயர்வ சிலவுள கண்டாய்."],"p":"","r":"In the long-suffering nigoda births many souls labour having reached only one sense, and a few of them do move out of that state.","n":"The nigoda is the lowest stratum of Jain life-forms; the verse says only that some leave it, not how or why.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nigodam","piravi","idumbai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1957","lb":"SO1957","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["souls in the nigoda state have one sense and suffer, and only some move on from it","jain","asserted",2,"sage"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிவர்","பயாபதி"],"ch":"so:85"},"so:1958":{"book":"soolamani","num":1956,"t":["ஓரறி வாகி யுழக்கு முயிர்களைப்","பேரறி வாரும் பிறரில்லை யின்னவை","யாரறி வாரழி யுந்திறம் யாதெனில்","கூரறி வில்லவர் கொன்றிடு கின்றார்."],"p":"","r":"One-sensed lives suffer, and few are wise enough to know how they are destroyed, so those of dull understanding kill them.","n":"Harm is traced to a failure of perception, not to malice: the killers are those lacking sharp knowledge (கூரறிவில்லவர்).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kollaamai","uyir","arivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1958","lb":"SO1958","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["one-sensed beings are killed by those too dull to perceive that they live","jain","asserted",4,"sage"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிவர்","பயாபதி"],"ch":"so:85"},"so:1959":{"book":"soolamani","num":1957,"t":["உயிர்தொகை யாறனு ளொன்றொழித் தேனைப்","பெயர்த்தொகை பெற்ற பிறவிக டம்மைப்","பயிர்த்தலு மின்றி யுலகம் பதைப்பச்","செயிர்த்தவர் போலச் செகுத்திடுங் கண்டாய்."],"p":"","r":"Apart from one of the six classes of life, the other named classes of birth are destroyed by people who act as if enraged while the world trembles, without cherishing them.","n":"Which one of the six classes is excepted cannot be told from the four lines; the verse does not name them.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kollaamai","uyir","kolai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1959","lb":"SO1959","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["the classes of living beings are destroyed by the heedless","jain","asserted",3,"sage"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிவர்","பயாபதி"],"ch":"so:85"},"so:1960":{"book":"soolamani","num":1958,"t":["ஏனை யொழிந்த வியங்குநற் சாதிகள்","ஆனை முதலா வளிய விலங்குகள்","மானுடர் பற்றி வலிந்து நலிந்திட","ஊனெய் யுருகு முழக்கு மொருபால்."],"p":"","r":"The remaining moving kinds, from elephants down, are the harmless beasts, and men seize and torment them by force, so that on one side the fat of their flesh runs.","n":"The suffering is described at its worst in the seizing and cooking of the animal: the line about melting fat (ஊனெய் உருகும்) is the grimmest of the run.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["idumbai","piravi","kolai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1960","lb":"SO1960","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிவர்","பயாபதி"],"ch":"so:85"},"so:1961":{"book":"soolamani","num":1959,"t":["ஊர்ந்து முழுது முறுபார மேந்தியும்","சாய்ந்த விலங்குக டாளுடைந் தாழ்தர","வீர்ந்து மறுத்து மிறைச்சி யுவப்பவர்","தேர்ந்து செகுப்பவுந் தேயுஞ் சிலவே."],"p":"","r":"Beasts ridden and loaded with great weights, with legs broken and sinking, are cut and refused and chosen for killing by those who enjoy meat, while some waste away.","n":"The lines move from the animal as burden-bearer to the animal as meat; the same owner's use and his appetite are laid side by side. Several rare words make the sense of the third line less sure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["idumbai","kolai","pulaal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1961","lb":"SO1961","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிவர்","பயாபதி"],"ch":"so:85"},"so:1962":{"book":"soolamani","num":1960,"t":["தடிவிலை வாழ்நர் தடிந்திடப் பட்டு","முடிவிலை வாழ்நர் முருக்க முரிந்தும்","கொடுவி லெயினர்கள் கொல்லக் குறைந்தும்","விடலில வேதனை வேந்த விலங்கே."],"p":"","r":"Beasts are cut down by the sellers of flesh, crushed by those who cannot stop, and killed by cruel-bowed hunters, so that the animals' pain, the sage tells the king, never lets up.","n":"Three trades of killing are named in turn: the flesh-seller, the one who breaks the animal, and the hunter. The sage lists occupations that live on animal death, without saying here that each earns its own rebirth.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["idumbai","kolai","pulaal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1962","lb":"SO1962","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிவர்","பயாபதி"],"ch":"so:85"},"so:1963":{"book":"soolamani","num":1961,"t":["அந்தோ வளிய விலங்குகள் யார்கண்ணும்","நொந்தோ மெனச்சென்று நோக்கி னுனிப்பொடு","வந்தோ மெனநின்ற மாண்புடை யார்களும்","உய்ந்தோய்ந் தொழிய முயன்றிடு கின்றார்."],"p":"","r":"The sage says that the pitiable beasts look to everyone with their pain, and that even those who stand before them as good people try to be free of them and be done.","n":"The last two lines are hard to construe: they may say that even people of worth, shown the animals' pain, turn from it rather than help. The reading is a best guess.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["idumbai","arul","uyir"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1963","lb":"SO1963","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிவர்","பயாபதி"],"ch":"so:85"},"so:1964":{"book":"soolamani","num":1962,"t":["கன்னியர் வேட்கை கடவு ளரும்பிணி","துன்னிய துன்ப விலங்கின் சுடுதுயர்","என்னு மிவற்றினை யெம்போல் பவரன்றி","மன்ன வறிபவர் மற்றில்லை மன்னோ."],"p":"","r":"The longing of maidens, the sickness that grips even the gods and the burning pain of the suffering animal, the sage says, are known only to ones like himself, so the king should not suppose them small.","n":"The line pairing the maidens' longing and the gods' sickness with the animals' pain seems to place all three as kinds of suffering, but the third verse line is not clear; the pulli after a vowel sign in line 3 was repaired by the build.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["idumbai","uyir","naanam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1964","lb":"SO1964","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிவர்","பயாபதி"],"ch":"so:85"},"so:1965":{"book":"soolamani","num":1963,"t":["வலிய முழங்கினு நாறினும் வட்கி","நலியு மிவை யென நையு மொருபால்","பலிபெறு தெய்வங்கண் மேலிட்டுப் பாற்றும்","கலியவர் கையுட் கழியு மொருபால்."],"p":"","r":"Some animals waste away shrinking from the loud or the foul, and some perish at the hands of cruel people who offer them to deities that take offerings, as the king is told.","n":"The verse does not argue against sacrifice; it lists it among the ways an animal dies. The Jain objection to offerings is in the choice of that list.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["idumbai","vazhipaadu","kolai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1965","lb":"SO1965","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["killing animals as offerings to deities is part of the cruelty animals suffer","jain","asserted",3,"sage"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிவர்","பயாபதி"],"ch":"so:85"},"so:1966":{"book":"soolamani","num":1964,"t":["கண்களி னோக்கியுங் காதலி னுள்ளியும்","மண்க ளிடைவிட்டு வைகியும் புல்லியும்","தண்கமழ் தார்மன்ன தாயர் வளர்ப்புழி","எண்களை யின்றிட ரெய்து மொருபால்."],"p":"","r":"While mothers rear their young, the young look at them and think of them in love and lie apart or cling, and even so, on one side, they meet countless troubles.","n":"The verse seems to say that even the tenderness between animal mothers and young does not spare them harm. The construal of line 3 is not secure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["idumbai","anbu","piravi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1966","lb":"SO1966","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிவர்","பயாபதி"],"ch":"so:85"},"so:1967":{"book":"soolamani","num":1965,"t":["இன்னன துன்பமோ டிவ்விலங் காகுநர்","என்னவ ரென்னி னிவைநனி கேளினி","மன்னிய மாதவ மேற்கொண்டு மாயங்கள்","பின்னை முயல்வார் பிறப்பு மதுவே."],"p":"","r":"Asking who becomes these suffering beasts, the sage says: those who take up great austerity and afterwards work deceits are born as these.","n":"The sage names the cause of animal birth as hypocrisy after vows, not as ordinary wrongdoing, so the fault is falling from a state the person had taken up.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["koodaozhukkam","thavam","vinai","piravi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1967","lb":"SO1967","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["those who take up austerity and later practise deceit are reborn as animals","jain","asserted",3,"sage"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிவர்","பயாபதி"],"ch":"so:85"},"so:1968":{"book":"soolamani","num":1966,"t":["பொருளிடை மாயம் புணர்த்தும் பிறரை","மருளிக ளாக மயக்கு மவரும்","இருளுடை யுள்ளமொ டேதங்க ளெண்ணா","அருளி லவரு மவைநனி யாவார்."],"p":"","r":"Those who join deceit to wealth, who bewilder others into folly with dark hearts that count none of their own faults, and who lack grace, also become these beasts.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vanjam","arul","vinai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1968","lb":"SO1968","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["cheating others for gain and lacking compassion lead to animal birth","jain","asserted",4,"sage"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிவர்","பயாபதி"],"ch":"so:85"},"so:1969":{"book":"soolamani","num":1967,"t":["பற்றொடு பற்றி முனிந்தார் பலபல","செற்ற நவின்றார் செறுப்பொடு சென்றவர்","சுற்ற மழிக்குந் துவர்ப்பகை துன்னினர்","மற்றிவ் விலங்கெய்து மன்னுயிர் மன்னா."],"p":"","r":"Those who cling and hate, speak many angers and go out in wrath, and who draw near to what destroys kin, enter the births that these beasts have.","n":"The third line is hard to construe (the word துவர்ப்பகை is not clear); the verse is read here as stating the causes of animal birth from clinging, hatred and rage.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pattru","pagai","vinai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1969","lb":"SO1969","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["attachment, hatred and anger lead to animal birth","jain","asserted",1,"sage"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிவர்","பயாபதி"],"ch":"so:85"},"so:1970":{"book":"soolamani","num":1968,"t":["இல்லையுயி ரென்று மில்லைபிறப் பென்று","நல்லன தீயன நாடி லிலவென்றும்","பல்லன சொல்லிப் படுத்துண்ணும் பாவிகள்","நில்லாது செல்வர் நிகோத கதியே."],"p":"","r":"Sinners who say that there is no soul, no rebirth and no good or evil if one examines it, and who feast on harm, go without pause to the nigoda state.","n":"The denial is given in the Jain sage's summary and is answered by the penalty it is said to bring, not by argument here; the school is not named, so the view is the sage's account of such deniers, not a statement a materialist would sign.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nigodam","piravi","vinai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1970","lb":"SO1970","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["there is no soul, no rebirth, and no good or bad deed","lokayata","reported",1,"deniers"],["those who deny soul and rebirth and live by harm go to the nigoda state","jain","asserted",4,"sage"]],"ag":["பாவிகள்"],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிவர்","பயாபதி"],"ch":"so:85"},"so:1971":{"book":"soolamani","num":1969,"t":["மாக மழைவண்கை மன்னவ மக்களும்","மேக கதியின ரநேக விகற்பினர்","சேகர் மிலைச்சர் மனிதர் கடிப்பியர்","போக மனித ரெனப்பொருட் பட்டார்."],"p":"","r":"Cloud-like generous kings' sons and sky-travelling folk, and many kinds of humans such as the Segar and Milechchar, are counted in the sage's scheme as enjoyers' humans.","n":"The rare words (சேகர், மிலைச்சர், கடிப்பியர்) name classes of humans in the Jain scheme; I read the verse as a list of human kinds, not as a claim about any one of them.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["piravi","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1971","lb":"SO1971","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிவர்","பயாபதி"],"ch":"so:85"},"so:1972":{"book":"soolamani","num":1970,"t":["பத்து வகைய பரதவி ரேவதத்","தத்தகு கால விழிவி னகத்தவர்","சித்தந் தெளிவிலர் சீல மடைவிலர்","செத்த வறிவினர் சேக ரவரே."],"p":"","r":"The ten kinds of the Segar, in the declining age of Bharata and Airavata, lack clear mind, conduct and knowledge, as if their understanding were dead.","n":"The declining age is named only by the word இழிவு (fall); the verse is a cosmological tag for the kind of human the sage is describing.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaalam","piravi","ozhukkam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1972","lb":"SO1972","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிவர்","பயாபதி"],"ch":"so:85"},"so:1973":{"book":"soolamani","num":1971,"t":["தீவினுள் வாழுங் குமானுடர் தேசத்து","மேவி யுறையு மிலைச்ச ரெனப்பெயர்","ஆவ ரவருண் மிலைச்ச ரவரையும்","வீவருந் தாரோய் விலங்கினுள் வைப்பாம்."],"p":"","r":"Among humans who live in the island lands, those called Milechchar are counted, the sage says, among animals, and beings of that sort die as such.","n":"The verse ranks a whole people by birth-state; it states the Jain classification and does not argue it. The rest of the passage (1974 to 1977) gives the marks of the class.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["piravi","sirumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1973","lb":"SO1973","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["island-dwelling Milechchar humans are classed with animals","jain","asserted",3,"sage"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிவர்","பயாபதி"],"ch":"so:85"},"so:1974":{"book":"soolamani","num":1972,"t":["வாலு நெடியர் வளைந்த வெயிற்றினர்","காலு மொரோவொன் றுடையர் கலையிலர்","நாலுஞ் செவியர் நவைசெய் மருப்பினர்","சீல மடைவிலர் தீவினுள் வாழ்வார்."],"p":"","r":"They have long tails and curved teeth, one leg each, no learning, ears four in number and harmful tusks, lack conduct and live in the island.","n":"The portrait is of an imagined people at the edge of the world, drawn with marks of beasts; the verse says nothing of real peoples.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["piravi","ozhukkam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1974","lb":"SO1974","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிவர்","பயாபதி"],"ch":"so:85"},"so:1975":{"book":"soolamani","num":1973,"t":["மக்கட் பிறப்பெனு மாத்திர மல்லது","மிக்க வெளிற்று விலங்குக ளேயவர்","நக்க வுருவினர் நாணா வொழுக்கினர்","தொக்கனர் மண்ணே துளைத்துண்டு வாழ்வார்."],"p":"","r":"Except for the bare fact of human birth they are pale animals, naked, shameless in conduct, gathered on the soil and living by digging and eating earth.","n":"The verse faults nakedness together with shameless conduct (நாணா ஒழுக்கினர்); it does not say nakedness alone is the fault.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naanam","ozhukkam","piravi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1975","lb":"SO1975","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["these people are human by birth alone and otherwise animals, for want of shame and conduct","jain","asserted",2,"sage"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிவர்","பயாபதி"],"ch":"so:85"},"so:1976":{"book":"soolamani","num":1974,"t":["பூவும் பழனு நுகர்ந்து பொழின்மரம்","மேவி யுறையு மிலைச்சர் மிகப்பலர்","ஓவலர் வாழ்வ தொருபளி தோபமென்","றேவல் சிலைமன்ன வெண்ணி யுணர்நீ."],"p":"","r":"Eating flowers and fruit, many Milechchar live in garden trees, and the sage tells the king to consider that their lives last without end for one palyopama.","n":"The palyopama is the Jain unit for a vast span of years; the verse gives the lifespan, not the reason for it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aayul","piravi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1976","lb":"SO1976","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிவர்","பயாபதி"],"ch":"so:85"},"so:1977":{"book":"soolamani","num":1975,"t":["தேச மிலைச்சரிற் சேர்வுடை யாரவர்","மாசின் மனிதர் வடிவின ராயினும்","கூசின் மனத்தர் கொடுந்தொழில் வாழ்க்கையர்","நீச ரவரையு நீரி னிழிப்பாம்."],"p":"","r":"Those joined to the Milechcha lands, though of faultless human form, are of shrinking mind and cruel livelihood, and the sage says that these base ones too he lowers like water.","n":"The last line (நீச அவரையும் நீரின் இழிப்பாம்) is hard to construe; it is read as a statement that even the human-looking among them are put down.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["piravi","sirumai","ozhukkam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1977","lb":"SO1977","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["humans of cruel livelihood and shrunken mind are base though in human form","jain","asserted",3,"sage"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிவர்","பயாபதி"],"ch":"so:85"},"so:1978":{"book":"soolamani","num":1976,"t":["கூடன் மிலைச்சர் குமானுட ரென்றிவர்","ஏடவிழ் தாரோ யெவரா பவரெனில்","கோடிக் குதர்க்க முரைத்துக் குணங்களை","நாடினர் கொள்ளா நலமி லவரும்."],"p":"","r":"Asked who the Koodar, Milechchar and Kumanudar are, the teacher answers: those who, spinning endless quibbles, weigh good qualities and yet will not accept them, and have nothing good in them.","n":"The verse opens a list of lesser kinds of men and names them only by their quibbling; the three labels are the teacher's, and the outsider word மிலைச்சர் is used here as a term of low standing, not as a tribe the verse describes.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pedhaimai","therithal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1978","lb":"SO1978","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["those who quibble endlessly over virtues and refuse to accept them are base and not truly human","general","asserted",3,"sage"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிவர்","பயாபதி"],"ch":"so:86"},"so:1979":{"book":"soolamani","num":1977,"t":["அடங்கா மரபி னவர்கட் கடங்கார்","விடங்கார் மணந்த விடக்கும் பிறவும்","உடங்காய்ந் துணக்கொடுப் பாரு முயர்ந்தோர்","தொடங்கா வினைக டொடங்கு மவரும்."],"p":"","r":"The same low company includes those of unrestrained lineage who cannot be restrained, those who eat flesh together with other forbidden things, and those who begin deeds that the high-minded never begin.","n":"The verse measures the low by what they eat and do, not by birth alone, though line 1 speaks of lineage. Line 2 is hard: the word விடங்கார் may be 'poison-black' or a damaged form, so exactly what is eaten is not fixed.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pulaal","pedhaimai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1979","lb":"SO1979","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["eating flesh and starting deeds that the noble avoid mark a low kind of human","general","asserted",2,"sage"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிவர்","பயாபதி"],"ch":"so:86"},"so:1980":{"book":"soolamani","num":1978,"t":["அன்ன பிறவியு ளாங்கவ ராபவர்","இன்னுஞ் சிலவ ரிழிகதிப் பாற்பட்டுத்","துன்னிய போழ்தே சுருங்கி யொழிபவர்","என்னும் பிறர்க ளறிவிற் கிகந்தார்."],"p":"","r":"Among such births some become these kinds, some sink into low states and dwindle away soon after joining, and others, the teacher says, are past the reach of knowledge.","n":"The verse lists fates after the types of 1978-1979 without naming them, and its last half-line is hard to construe, so it is read loosely.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["piravi","pedhaimai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1980","lb":"SO1980","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["some low births pass quickly into lower states and are beyond ordinary knowing","jain","asserted",2,"sage"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிவர்","பயாபதி"],"ch":"so:86"},"so:1981":{"book":"soolamani","num":1979,"t":["மக்கள் வதியு மிரண்டரைத் தீவினுள்","தக்க நிலத்துப் பிறந்தவர் தம்முளும்","முக்குலத் தாரொடுங் கூடா முயற்சியர்","ஒக்கலைப் போல்வார் பலரு முளரே."],"p":"","r":"Even among those born in the lands where humans live, within the two and a half continents, many are no better than hangers-on, for their efforts never join the three classes.","n":"The 'two and a half continents' is the Jain measure of the human world; the three classes are not named here, and the comparison to kinsfolk (ஒக்கல்) may rather mean 'like the household's hangers-on'. Both are uncertain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["makkal","kudi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1981","lb":"SO1981","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["birth in a human land does not make one fit for the three classes of people","jain","asserted",3,"sage"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிவர்","பயாபதி"],"ch":"so:86"},"so:1982":{"book":"soolamani","num":1980,"t":["முக்குலத் தாரொடு மூடத் தொழுதியர்","தக்க தகாவென்ப தோராத் தகையவர்","மக்க ளெனப்படு வாரலர் மற்றவர்","பக்கங் கிடக்கும் பதரெனக் கொண்ணீ."],"p":"","r":"Those mingled with the three classes who are fools in a crowd and cannot judge what is fit and unfit are not to be called people, and the king is told to treat them as chaff thrown aside.","n":"Humanity is made a matter of discernment (தக்க தகா), not of birth; this is the teacher's judgment, spoken to a king who must himself discern.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pedhaimai","therithal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1982","lb":"SO1982","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["one who cannot tell the fit from the unfit is not a true human being","general","asserted",3,"sage"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிவர்","பயாபதி"],"ch":"so:86"},"so:1983":{"book":"soolamani","num":1981,"t":["நல்ல நிலங்க ணலங்கொள் வடிவுகள்","இல்லை யமர்ந்துழித் தோன்ற லெனவிவை","எல்லையில் யோனிக ளெல்லா மிகந்தெய்தல்","அல்லியந் தாரோ யரிது பேரிதே."],"p":"","r":"Good lands and good forms are not found just where one chooses to be born, so to pass beyond the countless wombs and reach them is very hard.","n":"The verse says good births are hard to win; it does not say here by what means, which 1985 gives.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["piravi","marumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1983","lb":"SO1983","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["good birth in a good land is very rare among endless wombs","jain","asserted",3,"sage"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிவர்","பயாபதி"],"ch":"so:86"},"so:1984":{"book":"soolamani","num":1982,"t":["அண்ணை யலிகுரு டாதி யவர்களை","மண்ணுயர் ஞாலத்து மானுட ராகவைத்","தெண்ணுநர் யாருள ரெல்லா மமையினும்","பெண்ணின் பிறவியும் பீடுடைத் தன்றே."],"p":"","r":"No one counts the eunuch, the blind and the like as human, and even when everything else is in order, birth as a woman is not an honoured one.","n":"This is the teacher's view in a Jain work, set among marks of a lesser birth, and the epic gives it without reply here; it is the epic's view of women's standing as a birth, not a general truth about women. The first word அண்ணை is obscure.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["makkal","piravi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1984","lb":"SO1984","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["birth as a woman is not an honoured birth","jain","asserted",4,"sage"],["the eunuch and the blind are not counted among humans","jain","asserted",1,"sage"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிவர்","பயாபதி"],"ch":"so:86"},"so:1985":{"book":"soolamani","num":1983,"t":["எண்பத்து நான்கெனு நூறா யிரமுள","பண்பொத்த சாதிப் பதமென்ப மற்றவை","கண்பற்றுந் தாரோய் களிப்பதொர் நல்வினைத்","திண்பற் றுடையவ ரிவ்வுடல் சேர்வார்."],"p":"","r":"Of the eighty-four hundred thousand kinds of birth, only those who hold firmly to a gladdening good deed come to this human body.","n":"The figure 84 lakh of yonis is the classical Jain count of kinds of birth; the verse makes karma the sole means of reaching the human body.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","piravi","oozh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1985","lb":"SO1985","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["the human body is won only by those firmly holding good karma, out of 8.4 million kinds of birth","jain","asserted",1,"sage"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிவர்","பயாபதி"],"ch":"so:86"},"so:1986":{"book":"soolamani","num":1984,"t":["சார்ந்த பொழுதே தலைநாட் கருவினுள்","வார்ந்து வழுவா தமைந்து வளரினும்","மீர்ந்தண் கமழ்நறுந் தாரோ யிடர்பல","கூர்ந்து வருபயாங் கூற வுலவா."],"p":"","r":"Even a child who, from the first days in the womb, forms and grows without fault will meet many sufferings that cannot be fully told.","n":"The verse puts the pain of life before birth, so even a fair birth is not a good fortune.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["piravi","idumbai","karu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1986","lb":"SO1986","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["suffering begins in the womb and cannot be avoided even by a faultless birth","jain","asserted",3,"sage"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிவர்","பயாபதி"],"ch":"so:86"},"so:1987":{"book":"soolamani","num":1985,"t":["குழவி யருஞ்சுரஞ் சென்று குமர","வழுவ வடவி யரிதி னிகந்தால்","கிழவெனு மெல்லை கெழீஇயினர் சார்ந்து","வழுவினர் செல்வது மற்றோர் கதியே."],"p":"","r":"The infant crosses the harsh wilderness of childhood and the faults of youth; if one gets through the forest with difficulty, one reaches old age and, failing, goes to another state.","n":"Life is drawn as a wilderness with three stages; the 'other state' (கதி) is the next birth, not extinction.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","piravi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1987","lb":"SO1987","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["life, with its stages from infancy to old age, ends in passage to another state","jain","asserted",4,"sage"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிவர்","பயாபதி"],"ch":"so:86"},"so:1988":{"book":"soolamani","num":1986,"t":["யானை துரப்ப வரவுறை யாழ்குழி","நானவிர் பற்றுபு நாலு மொருவனோர்","தேனெய் யழிதுளி நக்குந் திறத்தது","மானுய ரின்ப மதித்தனை கொண்ணீ."],"p":"","r":"As a man chased by an elephant hangs over a deep pit with a snake in it and licks a falling drop of honey, so the king should weigh the pleasure of human life.","n":"This is the well-known parable of the man hanging in a pit between an elephant and a snake; the verse gives its parts compactly and does not say what gnaws the rope, so the image is read in its common form. It makes pleasure real but small, not unreal.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inbam","nilaiyaamai","idumbai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1988","lb":"SO1988","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["human pleasure is only a drop of honey tasted while death and birth threaten on every side","general","asserted",4,"sage"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிவர்","பயாபதி"],"ch":"so:86"},"so:1989":{"book":"soolamani","num":1987,"t":["அன்பும் பிறவு மமைந்தாங் ககத்திருந்","தின்பங் கருது மிருவர்க் கிடைபல","துன்பங்க டோன்றுந் தொடர்ப்பா டுளவெனில்","முன்பவை யில்லெனின் முற்றுந் தொழிலே."],"p":"","r":"For two people living together in love, wanting pleasure, many sufferings arise with the ties between them; the verse is read as saying that without these ties effort would be wholly work.","n":"Lines 3 and 4 are hard: the sense of முற்றும் தொழிலே is not fixed, and the verse may say that without those ties effort would be pure labour.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["ilvaazhkkai","idumbai","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1989","lb":"SO1989","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["household love and desire bring many sufferings with their ties","jain","asserted",3,"sage"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிவர்","பயாபதி"],"ch":"so:86"},"so:1990":{"book":"soolamani","num":1988,"t":["இன்ன நிலைமை யிதனுட் பிறந்தவர்","மன்னுமொன் றுண்டு வதத்தாற் பெறுவது","பொன்னியல் சேர்கற்ப போக நிலங்களிற்","துன்னு முயற்சி துணியுந் திறமே."],"p":"","r":"For those born into this state there is one lasting thing, won by vows: the firm effort that leads to the golden lands of the heavens and of enjoyment.","n":"The reward is the heavens and enjoyment lands, not release; the epic does not here say vows lead to liberation.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aram","marumai","thavam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1990","lb":"SO1990","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["vows lead to the heavens and lands of enjoyment","jain","asserted",2,"sage"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிவர்","பயாபதி"],"ch":"so:86"},"so:1991":{"book":"soolamani","num":1989,"t":["துன்னு முயற்சி துணியுந் திறமவை","பன்னி யுரைப்பிற் பலவாய்ப் பெருகினும்","தன்னிய றானந் தவமொடு பூசனை","என்னுமிந் நான்கென வெண்ணி யுணர்நீ."],"p":"","r":"Though the ways of firm effort are many in telling, count them as four: one's own conduct, giving, austerity and worship.","n":"The first of the four, தன்னியல், is read as one's own conduct; the verse gives no more than the list, and 1992 onwards deal with the second, giving.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aram","eegai","thavam","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1991","lb":"SO1991","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["the many ways of effort fall into four: conduct, giving, austerity and worship","jain","asserted",3,"sage"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிவர்","பயாபதி"],"ch":"so:86"},"so:1992":{"book":"soolamani","num":1990,"t":["தலையு மிடையுங் கடையுமாச் சாற்றும்","நிலைமைய தான நிழன்மணிப் பூணோய்","உலைவி லேற்போ னுடனீ பவனீயும்","மலைவில் பொருளின்ன மாட்சிய மன்னா."],"p":"","r":"Giving is said to be of best, middle and lowest grade, depending on a faultless receiver, the giver and the gift, and the gift itself must be beyond reproach.","n":"The three grades are named, but which factor ranks a gift first is not said here. Compare the seven qualities of the giver in 1995.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","aram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1992","lb":"SO1992","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["the worth of a gift depends on the receiver, the giver and the gift","jain","asserted",2,"sage"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிவர்","பயாபதி"],"ch":"so:86"},"so:1993":{"book":"soolamani","num":1991,"t":["ஐமை யமைந்தார்க் கெழுமை யமைந்தவர்","இம்மை நினையா ரிமைபத மீவழி","மும்மைக்கு மும்மடங் காய முறைமையில்","பொய்ம்மையில் புண்ணியம் போர்க்கும் புகுந்தே."],"p":"","r":"The verse tells of givers who have mastered the senses and think of future births rather than this life, and whose faultless merit comes back to them multiplied threefold.","n":"The first lines use rare words (ஐமை, இமைபதம்) and cannot be fixed; the threefold multiple is clear from line 3 but what the three are is not.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["eegai","marumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1993","lb":"SO1993","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["a gift made with thought for later births returns merit multiplied","jain","asserted",4,"sage"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிவர்","பயாபதி"],"ch":"so:86"},"so:1994":{"book":"soolamani","num":1992,"t":["துறவி யடக்கை பிறர்க்குநன் றாற்றல்","உறவினர்க் கோம்புதன் மெய்த்தலைப் பாடென்","றறிவ ரறைந்தாங் கறைந்தனன் றானங்","குறைவில னேற்பவற் கேற்ற குணனே."],"p":"","r":"Wise people list renunciation, restraint, doing good to others, guarding kin and holding to truth as duties, and the teacher has given giving as the quality of one who lacks nothing and fits the receiver.","n":"Both halves are hard to join and the last line may speak of the receiver rather than the giver.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["eegai","thuravu","adakkam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1994","lb":"SO1994","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["renunciation, restraint, kindness to others and care of kin are named as duties, with giving one among them","jain","asserted",2,"sage"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிவர்","பயாபதி"],"ch":"so:86"},"so:1995":{"book":"soolamani","num":1993,"t":["போதிசை வாற்றல் பொன்றுதறு கட்பம்","ஈதற் கிவறுத லேற்பவர் மாட்டெழு","காதல் கழிபற்றி லாமை தெரிந்தறி","வேதமின் றீவான் குணமிவை யேழே."],"p":"","r":"The giver's seven qualities are listed: patience, capacity, eagerness to give, love for the receiver, non-attachment, discernment and the absence of regret.","n":"Only the count of seven is plain; several words of lines 1-2 (போதிசை, பொன்றுதறு, கட்பம்) are rare, so the names are read from the context and not fixed.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","pattru","therithal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1995","lb":"SO1995","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["a giver must have seven qualities, among them non-attachment and absence of regret","jain","asserted",4,"sage"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிவர்","பயாபதி"],"ch":"so:86"},"so:1996":{"book":"soolamani","num":1994,"t":["தானு மடங்கி யடங்கினர்க் கேந்திய","ஊன முயிர்களுக் கெல்லா முணர்வது","ஞான வொழுக்கம் பெருகு நலத்ததை","ஈனமி லின்ப நிலங்கட் குவித்தே."],"p":"","r":"One who is himself restrained, gives to the restrained and feels the hurt of all living things, grows in knowledge and conduct and reaches the blameless lands of joy.","n":"The syntax of the second line is hard, so the verse is read with some doubt.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["eegai","adakkam","gnaanam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1996","lb":"SO1996","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["a restrained giver who feels the hurt of all lives gains knowledge and conduct and reaches lands of joy","jain","asserted",2,"sage"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிவர்","பயாபதி"],"ch":"so:86"},"so:1997":{"book":"soolamani","num":1995,"t":["கடைநின் றவருறு கண்கண் டிரங்கி","உடையதம் மாற்றலி லுண்டி கொடுத்தோர்","படைகெழு தானையர் பல்களி யானைக்","குடைகெழு வேந்தர்க ளாகுவர் கோவே."],"p":"","r":"Those who, seeing the distress of the people at the door, give food as far as they can will become kings with armies, elephants and umbrellas.","n":"The fruit is kingship in a later birth; this ties the giving to the king's own rank, which Payapati ties to past deeds in 1843.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","arasu","vinai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1997","lb":"SO1997","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["giving food to the hungry bears fruit in a later birth as a king","jain","asserted",4,"sage"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிவர்","பயாபதி"],"ch":"so:86"},"so:1998":{"book":"soolamani","num":1996,"t":["ஊறுபல செய்துயிர் கட்கிடர் செய்யும்","வீறில் பொருளை வினையவர்க் கீந்தவன்","ஏறும் பயனிஃ தென்றினி யான்சொல்லி","நாறிணர்த் தாரோய் நகுவ துடைத்தே."],"p":"","r":"One who gives to evil-doers wealth that causes many injuries and harms to lives will earn a fruit the teacher would only laugh to tell.","n":"The mock tone of 'I would laugh to say it' is the teacher's. This makes harmlessness (கொல்லாமை) a limit on giving.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","kollaamai","vinai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1998","lb":"SO1998","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["giving harmful things to those who do evil is no gift and brings a bad fruit","jain","asserted",4,"sage"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிவர்","பயாபதி"],"ch":"so:86"},"so:1999":{"book":"soolamani","num":1997,"t":["தன்கைப் பொருளு மிழந்து தனக்கொரு","புன்கட் கதிசெல்லும் வாயில் புணர்ப்பவன்","வன்கட் பதகர்க்கு வான்பொருள் கைக்கொடுத்","தென்கைப் பணிகொண்மி னென்பவ னொத்தான்."],"p":"","r":"He is like a man who loses his own wealth and builds for himself the door to a wretched state, or hands great wealth to cruel wretches and asks them to serve him.","n":"The simile finishes 1998: the one who gives to cruel men gets his own harm back.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","vinai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 1999","lb":"SO1999","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["giving to cruel men is self-destruction, not a gift","jain","asserted",4,"sage"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிவர்","பயாபதி"],"ch":"so:86"},"so:2000":{"book":"soolamani","num":1998,"t":["ஒத்த குணங்க ளமைந்தாங் குறுவர்க்குத்","தத்துவந் தேறி யவன்செய்த தானங்கள்","முத்திறத் துள்ளும் படாது முடிமன்ன","உத்தம தேவரு ளுய்க்கு முணர்நீ."],"p":"","r":"The gifts made to those fit to receive by one who has grasped the true principles do not fall among the three grades, but lead him up among the best of the gods.","n":"The 'three grades' may be those of 1992 or the fruits of the gift in three realms; the verse is hard and its reading uncertain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["eegai","marumai","aram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 2000","lb":"SO2000","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["gifts of one who knows the true principles lead among the best gods","jain","asserted",4,"sage"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிவர்","பயாபதி"],"ch":"so:86"},"so:2001":{"book":"soolamani","num":1999,"t":["மிக்க விரதம் விரிபல வாயினும்","தொக்கன வைந்திற் சொலுமூன்றி னான்கினில்","ஒக்க வவற்றி னுறுபயஞ் சொல்லிடில்","தக்கவர்க் கொத்ததிற் றன்னங் குறைவே."],"p":"","r":"The sage says that the vows, though many and widely spread, are gathered into five, and that to tell their fruit across the heads that follow would leave the fitting vow short of its due.","n":"The count and the closing comparison (line 4) turn on the compressed phrase in lines 2 to 3 and cannot be fixed with confidence.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thavam","aram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 2001","lb":"SO2001","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["the many vows are gathered into five heads","jain","asserted",2,"sage"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிவர்","பயாபதி"],"ch":"so:87"},"so:2002":{"book":"soolamani","num":2000,"t":["எல்லா விரத மியல்பொக்கு மாயினும்","அல்லா விரத மனையா யவர்கட்குக்","கொல்லா விரதங் குடைமன்ன வாமெனின்","வெல்லா வகையில்லை வீங்கெழிற் றோளாய்."],"p":"","r":"All vows are alike in kind, the sage says, yet for those who are like the vowless the vow of not killing is the one that, if kings keep it, leaves nothing they cannot conquer.","n":"Winning (வெல்லா வகையில்லை) is put in the language of a king's conquest, so non-killing is offered to a ruler as a form of victory, not as a surrender of power. Lines 1 to 2 are hard to construe.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kollaamai","thavam","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 2002","lb":"SO2002","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["the vow of not killing, kept by kings, leaves no conquest beyond them","jain","asserted",4,"sage"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிவர்","பயாபதி"],"ch":"so:87"},"so:2003":{"book":"soolamani","num":2001,"t":["தம்மை யுடையவர் தாங்குந் தவத்தியல்","எம்மை வினவி னெமக்கு முரைப்பரி","தும்மையுலகத் தொளிபடு மூக்கமோ","டிம்மை யிகந்தார்க் கிசையு மதுவே."],"p":"","r":"The austerity borne by those who possess themselves is hard even for the sages to tell if asked, the sage says; it belongs to those who have left this life with the zeal that shines in the next world.","n":"The sage answers the king's search for a refuge at his feet (1862) by saying first that what he asks is hard to put into words.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thavam","thuravu","marumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 2003","lb":"SO2003","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["true austerity belongs to those who have left this life for the next with zeal","jain","asserted",4,"sage"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிவர்","பயாபதி"],"ch":"so:87"},"so:2004":{"book":"soolamani","num":2002,"t":["தவஞ்செய்து வந்தார் தவநிலை நிற்பார்","அவஞ்செய்து வந்தார்க் கரிது பெரிதும்","பவஞ்செய்து மாக்கள் பரியு மதுதான்","எவன் செய்து மென்னை யீர்மலர்த் தாரோய்."],"p":"","r":"Those who came having done austerity stand firm in it, but for those who came having done wrong it is greatly hard, and the sage asks what can be done for people who suffer from the deeds they have done.","n":"Lines 3 to 4 may be the king's question to the sage; the sage is taken as the speaker because 2005 answers directly in his voice.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thavam","vinai","piravi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 2004","lb":"SO2004","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["those who have done wrong find the state of austerity hard to reach","jain","asserted",2,"sage"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிவர்","பயாபதி"],"ch":"so:87"},"so:2005":{"book":"soolamani","num":2003,"t":["தெருண்டவர் மேற்கொளுஞ் செய்தவச் செல்வம்","இரண்டும் பலவு மியலாய்ப் பெருகு","மருண்டினி யென்னவை வந்த பொழுதே","முரண்டரு தோண்மன்ன முற்ற வுணர்நீ."],"p":"","r":"The wealth of austerity that the clear-sighted take up grows in two kinds and many, so do not be bewildered when it comes, but understand it fully.","n":"The king's own talk of sowing more seed (1842) is answered here: austerity is called wealth, and wealth that increases.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thavam","selvam","arivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 2005","lb":"SO2005","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["the wealth gained by austerity grows in kinds and multiplies","jain","asserted",2,"sage"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிவர்","பயாபதி"],"ch":"so:87"},"so:2006":{"book":"soolamani","num":2004,"t":["உலகங்கண் மூன்று முடைய பெருமாற்","கலகையில் பூசனை யாற்ற முயன்றால்","திலக மிவரெனத் தேவர்க ளாவர்","விலகுஞ் சுடரொளி வீங்கெழிற் றோளாய்."],"p":"","r":"The sage says that if one strives to give worship without measure to the great lord of the three worlds, the gods themselves come to hail one as their chief.","n":"கலகையில் in line 2 may be a damaged spelling of 'countless' (அளவற்ற); the sense follows that guess.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","iraivan","thavam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 2006","lb":"SO2006","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["worship of the lord of the three worlds makes one the chief of the gods","jain","asserted",3,"sage"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிவர்","பயாபதி"],"ch":"so:87"},"so:2007":{"book":"soolamani","num":2005,"t":["புண்ணிய வாயி லெனநாம் புகழ்ந்துரை","கண்ணிய நான்கா யடங்கு மடங்கினும்","நுண்ணிய நூல்வழி நோக்கி நுனித்தவர்","எண்ணிய வாயில்க ளின்னு முளவே."],"p":"","r":"The sage says that the doors of merit that he has praised gather into four, yet those who have looked closely along the subtle texts count still more doors.","n":"Four is named here and seven in the next verse; the sage offers his four as a summary and defers to the finer count of the texts, so the two numbers are not a contradiction.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aram","kelvi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 2007","lb":"SO2007","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["merit enters by doors that the texts count in different numbers","jain","asserted",4,"sage"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிவர்","பயாபதி"],"ch":"so:87"},"so:2008":{"book":"soolamani","num":2006,"t":["அருளுந் தெருளுங் குணத்தின்க ணார்வமும்","பொருளொன்று சேரும் புகழ்ச்சி நிகழ்வும்","மருளி றவமும் வாலிய ஞானமும்","இருளறு தியான நிகழ்வுமென் றேழே."],"p":"","r":"The seven doors are named: compassion, clarity, love for virtues, praise that reaches the object, faultless austerity, pure knowledge, and meditation that clears the dark.","n":"Compassion heads the list and meditation closes it, so the order runs from feeling to inner discipline.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arul","thavam","thiyaanam","arivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 2008","lb":"SO2008","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["merit has seven doors: compassion, clarity, love of virtue, praise, austerity, knowledge and meditation","jain","asserted",1,"sage"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிவர்","பயாபதி"],"ch":"so:87"},"so:2009":{"book":"soolamani","num":2007,"t":["ஆருயிர் யாதொன் றிடருறு மாங்கதற்","கோருயிர் போல வுருகி யுயக்கொள்ள","நேரி னதுமுடி யாதெனி னெஞ்சகத்","தீர முடைமை யருளி னியல்பே."],"p":"","r":"When a dear life suffers, one should melt as for one's own life and save it; where that cannot be done, a moist heart is still the nature of compassion.","n":"Line 3 allows that rescue may not succeed and still counts inward tenderness as compassion, which makes the heart's state, not the result, the measure.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arul","kollaamai","manam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 2009","lb":"SO2009","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["compassion is to feel another's suffering as one's own and to keep a moist heart even where relief fails","jain","asserted",4,"sage"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிவர்","பயாபதி"],"ch":"so:87"},"so:2010":{"book":"soolamani","num":2008,"t":["வையினும் வாழ்த்தினும் வாளா விருப்பினும்","வெய்ய முனிதல் குளிர்தல் வெறுப்பொடு","மையன் மும் மூடப் பகுதி மயக்கின்மை","செய்ய மனத்தோர் தெருளின் றிறமே."],"p":"","r":"Whether abused, praised or left alone, those of clear mind feel no hot anger, no cool delight and no dislike, and are free of the three kinds of delusion.","n":"The three delusions (மும்மூடம்) are not named in the verse; the reading stays with what the Tamil says.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["samanilai","porai","manam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 2010","lb":"SO2010","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["the clear-minded stay level to abuse, praise and neglect and are free of three delusions","jain","asserted",2,"sage"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிவர்","பயாபதி"],"ch":"so:87"},"so:2011":{"book":"soolamani","num":2009,"t":["அறிவ ரடிமுத லார்வம் பெருக்கல்","உறுவ ரொழுக்க முவத்தன் முதலா","இறுதியில் பல்குண நோக்கமென் றின்ன","செறிதலி லார்வங்கள் செல்வந் தருமே."],"p":"","r":"The sage names love of the feet of the knowing, honouring the conduct of the great and looking to their many virtues as attachments that bring wealth when they do not bind.","n":"The line 'செறிதலி லார்வங்கள்' (attachments without binding) is the key phrase and is not fully secure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["anbu","ozhukkam","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 2011","lb":"SO2011","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["love directed to the worthy brings wealth","jain","asserted",4,"sage"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிவர்","பயாபதி"],"ch":"so:87"},"so:2012":{"book":"soolamani","num":2010,"t":["ஆற்றல் வகையா லருந்தவ மேற்கொண்டு","நோற்று நுனித்த லொழுக்கந் தலைநிற்றல்","போற்றி யுரைத்தல் புகழ்ச்சி நிகழ்விஃ","தேற்று மிருவிசும் பீர்மலர்த் தாரோய்."],"p":"","r":"Taking up austerity by one's strength, practising it, mastering it, standing first in conduct and praising it: this, the sage says, is the course that carries to the two heavens.","n":"The verse names the two heavens (இருவிசும்பு) without saying which they are.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thavam","ozhukkam","marumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 2012","lb":"SO2012","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["austerity taken up by strength and practised leads to the two heavens","jain","asserted",4,"sage"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிவர்","பயாபதி"],"ch":"so:87"},"so:2013":{"book":"soolamani","num":2011,"t":["அற்ற துவர்ப்பின ராகு மருநிலை","உற்றவர்க் கிவ்வா றொழுக்கந் தலைநிற்றல்","நற்றவ மென்றிங்கு நாங்கண் மொழிந்தது","மற்றிது வானுல காள்விக்கு மன்னா."],"p":"","r":"Those who have cut off their astringent stain and reached the rare state, standing first in this conduct, are what the sage calls good austerity, and it brings rule of the heavenly world.","n":"துவர்ப்பு, the astringent taste or dye, is read here as the stain of passion that the renouncer has shed; this reading is not certain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thavam","ozhukkam","thuravu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 2013","lb":"SO2013","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["conduct held first by those who have cut off passion is good austerity and brings rule of heaven","jain","asserted",3,"sage"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிவர்","பயாபதி"],"ch":"so:87"},"so:2014":{"book":"soolamani","num":2012,"t":["நூற்பொருள் கேட்டு நுனித்தோ ருணர்வது","மாற்படை கூட்டு மயங்கிரு டீர்ப்பது","மேற்படை மெய்ம்மை விளக்கும் விளக்கது","நாற்படை யோய்நல்ல ஞான நிகழ்வே."],"p":"","r":"Knowledge is what the learned grasp on hearing the meaning of the texts: it clears the confusing darkness and is the lamp that shows the truth.","n":"Knowledge is a lamp, and the same lamp is lit in 2018 for vision, so the sage joins hearing and seeing.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arivu","kelvi","thelivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 2014","lb":"SO2014","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["knowledge gained by hearing the texts dispels delusion and shows the truth","jain","asserted",3,"sage"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிவர்","பயாபதி"],"ch":"so:87"},"so:2015":{"book":"soolamani","num":2013,"t":["சென்று பெருகுந் தியான நிகழ்ச்சியும்","ஒன்ற வுரைப்பி னொருநால் வகைப்படும்","நன்றியின் மாற்றினை நல்குமிரண் டல்லன","வென்றி விசும்பொடு வீடுந் தருமே."],"p":"","r":"The sage says that the meditation which grows on and on, fully told, is of four kinds, of which two bring the opposite of good and the rest give victory, heaven and release.","n":"The four are not named in the verse; the reading rests on the count and the two fruits.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thiyaanam","veedu","naragam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 2015","lb":"SO2015","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["meditation is of four kinds, two leading to ill and two to heaven and release","jain","asserted",2,"sage"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிவர்","பயாபதி"],"ch":"so:87"},"so:2016":{"book":"soolamani","num":2014,"t":["போற்றிய புண்ணியப் பொற்சுண்ண முன்புகழ்","வாற்றி முயல்வார்க் ககநிகழ் வாமவை","மாற்றிய வற்றை மறுதலை யாக்கொளிற்","பாற்றி யுழப்பிக்கும் பாக நிகழ்வே."],"p":"","r":"Those who strive in praise of merit find these things within, the sage says, and if one takes up the reverse of them it is a ripening that wears one down and torments.","n":"The first line is obscure; it probably speaks of the two bad meditations of 2015.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vinai","thiyaanam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 2016","lb":"SO2016","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["the opposite of the merit-bearing practice ripens into torment","jain","asserted",3,"sage"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிவர்","பயாபதி"],"ch":"so:87"},"so:2017":{"book":"soolamani","num":2015,"t":["காட்சி யெனும்பெயர்க் கதிர்விளக் கேற்றிய","மாட்சி யுடையார் வதமில ராயினும்","ஆட்சி கரிதன் றமருல கல்லது","மீட்சியில் பேரின்ப வெள்ளத் துழவே."],"p":"","r":"Those worthy who have lit the lamp named vision, though without vows, rule not a dark realm but the world of the gods, or plunge into the flood of great bliss from which there is no return.","n":"The verse sets vision above vows without dismissing vows, and it leaves open which of the two destinations is meant.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["gnaanam","veedu","marumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 2017","lb":"SO2017","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["right vision lit without vows still leads to heaven or to bliss without return","jain","asserted",4,"sage"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிவர்","பயாபதி"],"ch":"so:87"},"so:2018":{"book":"soolamani","num":2016,"t":["மெய்ப்பொரு டேறுதல் காட்சி விளக்கது","செப்படு மாயின் வினையெனுந் தீயிருள்","அப்படி மானு நிலையன் றதனைநின்","கைப்பொரு ளாக்கொள் கதிர்மணிப் பூணோய்."],"p":"","r":"The sage says that seeing the true thing is the lamp of vision, and if it is lit the fire-darkness called deeds cannot stay, so he urges the king to make it his own wealth.","n":"Karma is called both fire and darkness, the lamp ending it by showing the true thing and by no act of a god.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["gnaanam","vinai","arivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 2018","lb":"SO2018","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["the lamp of right vision destroys the darkness of karma","jain","asserted",2,"sage"],["right vision should be held as one's own wealth","jain","asserted",3,"sage"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிவர்","பயாபதி"],"ch":"so:87"},"so:2019":{"book":"soolamani","num":2017,"t":["தெய்வ மனித ரவரைத் தெளிவுறின்","ஐய விசயனு மாழி வலவனும்","எய்த விவர்முத லீரொன்ப தின்மரிவ்","வைய மருள வருந ருளரே."],"p":"","r":"If one traces the gods and men in order, the sage says, Vijayan and the discus-lord are the first of eighteen who will come to bewilder this world.","n":"The eighteen and the identity of the discus-lord (Tivittan) turn on a compressed line and a rare form, so this reading is uncertain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["perumai","kaalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 2019","lb":"SO2019","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிவர்","பயாபதி"],"ch":"so:87"},"so:2020":{"book":"soolamani","num":2018,"t":["ஆழி யிழந்த வயகண்ட னாதியாப்","பாழி வலவன் பகைவர்மும் மூவரும்","வீழ வுரைத்தேன் வியன்பெரு ஞாலத்துள்","ஊழிதொ றூழி யுலப்பில கண்டாய்."],"p":"","r":"The sage says he has told of Ayakanthan who lost the discus and of the nine foes of the strong lord who fell, and that such falls are without end through the ages of the wide world.","n":"The names are as printed; line 2 is hard to parse and the count of foes is read from மும்மூவர்.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vinai","kaalam","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 2020","lb":"SO2020","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிவர்","பயாபதி"],"ch":"so:87"},"so:2021":{"book":"soolamani","num":2019,"t":["தேய வினைவெல்லுந் தெய்வ மனிதருள்","நீயு மொருவனை நின்குலத் தாதிக்கட்","பாய விழுச்சீர்ப் பரதனை யுள்ளுறுத்","தாய திகிரி யவரு மவரே."],"p":"","r":"Among the gods and men who conquer their deeds, the sage says, the king himself is one, and with him Bharata, the first of his line, and the discus-rulers are those very ones.","n":"The king is told that his own line belongs to those who conquer karma, so renunciation is shown as a family inheritance and not an alien road.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","perumai","kudi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 2021","lb":"SO2021","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["gods and men who conquer their deeds include the king and his ancestor Bharata","jain","asserted",1,"sage"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிவர்","பயாபதி"],"ch":"so:87"},"so:2022":{"book":"soolamani","num":2020,"t":["தீர்த்தஞ் சிறக்குந் திருமறு மார்பரும்","பேர்த்துப் பிறவாப் பெருமை பெறுநரும்","ஓர்த்திவ் வுலகினு ளுத்தமர் மற்றவர்","தார்த்தங்கு மார்ப தவத்தின் வருவார்."],"p":"","r":"Those with the sacred mark on the breast, who hold the glory of never being born again, are the best in this world, the sage says, and they come through austerity.","n":"The sacred mark on the breast (திருமறு மார்பர்) is taken as the Jain sign of a teacher of the path; other readings are possible.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["piravi","thavam","veedu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 2022","lb":"SO2022","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["those who win release from rebirth are the best in the world and come by austerity","jain","asserted",4,"sage"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிவர்","பயாபதி"],"ch":"so:87"},"so:2023":{"book":"soolamani","num":2021,"t":["தக்கமிகு தானமுத லாயதலை நிற்கும்","மக்களிவ ராவர்மத யானைமற வேலோய்","புக்கவரு ளேபடுவர் போகநிலஞ் சார்ந்தார்","ஒக்கவவர் தன்மையு முரைக்கவுல வாவே."],"p":"","r":"The people who stand first in giving and like virtues, the sage says, are of this kind, having entered the grace of the gods and reached lands of enjoyment, though their nature cannot be fully told.","n":"The verse marks the lower rung of merit as enjoyment of lands, after 2022 has named release as the higher.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","marumai","arul"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 2023","lb":"SO2023","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["givers and the virtuous reach lands of enjoyment through their giving","jain","asserted",2,"sage"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிவர்","பயாபதி"],"ch":"so:87"},"so:2024":{"book":"soolamani","num":2022,"t":["உத்தமர்க ளேனையிடை யோர்கள்கடை யோராம்","முத்தகைய ராவரவர் மூரிநெடு வேலோய்","பத்துவகை பாதவ மியற்றிய பயத்தால்","அத்தகைய செய்கையு மவர்க்கனைய கண்டாய்."],"p":"","r":"The teacher tells the spear-bearing king that the best, the middle and the lowest ranks of beings are three, and that each one's way of life follows the fruit of the good deeds it did.","n":"Line 3 names some tenfold deed or tree (பத்துவகை பாதவம்) whose fruit sets the rank; the sense of 'ten kinds of tree' is not fixed, so the reading keeps only that rank follows earlier merit.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vinai","perumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 2024","lb":"SO2024","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["the three ranks of beings, best, middle and lowest, are the fruit of the good done before","jain","asserted",3,"sage_teacher"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிவர்","பயாபதி"],"ch":"so:88"},"so:2025":{"book":"soolamani","num":2023,"t":["அங்கிருவர் தம்பதிகள் செய்கையை யறைந்தால்","இங்கிருவர் செய்கைதமை யெண்ணியறி வாய்நீ","தங்குரவ ரோடிருவர் மாறிருவர் தாமாய்","இங்கிருவர் தேவர்கள் வளர்ப்பவியல் கின்றார்."],"p":"","r":"The teacher says that to tell the ways of the pair of couples there, the king should think of the two pairs here, who with their parents are raised by two gods.","n":"The grouping of the 'two pairs', the 'parents' and the 'two gods' (lines 3-4) cannot be fixed from this verse alone; it opens the account of birth in the land of enjoyment.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["makkal","vinai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 2025","lb":"SO2025","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிவர்","பயாபதி"],"ch":"so:88"},"so:2026":{"book":"soolamani","num":2024,"t":["நக்ககுழ விப்பருவ நாற்பதினொ டொன்பான்","ஒக்கவரு நாள்கள்கலை யோடுட னிறைந்தால்","மிக்கவொளி சூழ்ந்துமிளிர் மேனியவ ராகித்","தக்கவிள மைப்பருவ மெய்தினர்க டாமே."],"p":"","r":"When the forty-nine days of the laughing infant stage are complete, the pair become bright-bodied and reach the proper youth.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ilamai","kaalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 2026","lb":"SO2026","mm":1,"ev":"The twin children of the enjoyment land pass their forty-nine days of infancy and reach youth.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிவர்","பயாபதி"],"ch":"so:88"},"so:2027":{"book":"soolamani","num":2025,"t":["கொம்பழகு கொண்டகுழை நுண்ணிடை நுடங்க","வம்பழகு கொண்டமணி மென்முலை வளர்ந்தாங்","கம்பவழ வாயுளணி முள்ளெயி றிலங்கச்","செம்பவழ மேனியவள் கன்னிமை சிறந்தாள்."],"p":"","r":"The girl grows into full maidenhood: slender waist swaying, breasts swelling, coral-red lips and bright teeth.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","ilamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 2027","lb":"SO2027","mm":1,"ev":"The girl of the enjoyment land reaches full maidenhood.","cl":[],"ag":["கன்னி"],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிவர்","பயாபதி"],"ch":"so:88"},"so:2028":{"book":"soolamani","num":2026,"t":["நீலமணி கண்டனைய குஞ்சிக ணிறைந்தான்","ஞாலமளி கொண்டநளிர் தாமரை முகத்தான்","கோலமணி மால்குவடு குங்கும மடுத்தால்","போலுமணி மேனியொடு காளை பொலிவுற்றான்."],"p":"","r":"The boy likewise comes into his fullness: dark hair, a lotus face, and a body like a jewelled mountain smeared with saffron.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","ilamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 2028","lb":"SO2028","mm":1,"ev":"The boy of the enjoyment land reaches full youth.","cl":[],"ag":["காளை"],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிவர்","பயாபதி"],"ch":"so:88"},"so:2029":{"book":"soolamani","num":2027,"t":["தாதுபடு சண்பக மிகந்த நறுமேனிக்","காதுபுனை காமர்குழை பொற்சுருளை மின்ன","மீதுபடு கற்பக விளந்தளிர் மிலைச்சிப்","போதுபுனை கோதையவள் பூம்பொழி லணைந்தாள்."],"p":"","r":"Adorned with flowers and wishing-tree shoots, her gold ear-coils flashing, the girl enters the flowering garden.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 2029","lb":"SO2029","mm":1,"ev":"The girl, adorned, enters the flowering garden.","cl":[],"ag":["கன்னி"],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிவர்","பயாபதி"],"ch":"so:88"},"so:2030":{"book":"soolamani","num":2028,"t":["பவழவரை யன்னதிர டோட்பரவை மார்பன்","றவழுமணி யாரமொடு தார்மணி தயங்கக்","கவழமனை மேவுகளி யானையென வந்தாங்","கவிழுமல ரீர்ம்பொழிலு ளையனு மணைந்தான்."],"p":"","r":"The lord, shoulders like coral hills and ornaments gleaming, comes into the same garden like a rutting elephant.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 2030","lb":"SO2030","mm":1,"ev":"The young man comes into the garden where the girl is.","cl":[],"ag":["காளை"],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிவர்","பயாபதி"],"ch":"so:88"},"so:2031":{"book":"soolamani","num":2029,"t":["கன்னியவள் மேலிளைய காளையிரு கண்ணும்","மன்னுகமழ் தாமரையின் வாயித ழலங்கல்","பின்னியென வீழ்ந்த பிணை யன்னவவள் கண்ணும்","துன்னுமிரு நீலமென வந்தெதிர் துதைந்த."],"p":"","r":"The young man's eyes and the girl's eyes, like lotus-petal and dark water-lily, come together and press against each other.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 2031","lb":"SO2031","mm":1,"ev":"The eyes of the youth and the maiden meet.","cl":[],"ag":["காளை","கன்னி"],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிவர்","பயாபதி"],"ch":"so:88"},"so:2032":{"book":"soolamani","num":2030,"t":["நையுமென நின்றவிடை யாள்குணமோர் நான்கும்","வையமகிழ் காளையிவன் மாண்டகுண நான்கும்","ஐயென வகன்றன வணைந்தனர் கனிந்தார்","மெய்யுமிடை வுற்றவிது வால்விதியின் வண்ணம்."],"p":"","r":"The girl's four womanly qualities and the youth's four manly qualities fall away at once; the two draw near and melt together, which the teacher calls the way of fate.","n":"The 'four qualities' of each are not listed here, but their loss is the reason for the union; fate (விதி) is given as the cause, not choice or marriage.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","oozh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 2032","lb":"SO2032","mm":1,"ev":"The pair lose their reserve and unite.","cl":[["the union of the pair follows the course of fate","jain","asserted",4,"sage_teacher"]],"ag":["கன்னி","காளை"],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிவர்","பயாபதி"],"ch":"so:88"},"so:2033":{"book":"soolamani","num":2031,"t":["அன்றுமுதன் மூன்றளவு மல்லமுடி காறும்","சென்றுபெரு கிக்களி சிறந்துநனி காமம்","என்றுமிடை யின்றியிமை யாரினுகர் வார்க்கு","நின்றது பிராயமது வேநிழலும் வேலோய்."],"p":"","r":"From that day on, for three palla spans of life, they enjoy desire without a break like the gods, and this the teacher says is the whole of the life the shadow of which is their merit.","n":"Lines 3-4 are hard to construe; the verse says only that the long pleasure is a standing life 'like a shadow' of their earlier deed.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["inbam","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 2033","lb":"SO2033","mm":1,"ev":"The pair enjoy unbroken pleasure for their life span.","cl":[],"ag":["கன்னி","காளை"],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிவர்","பயாபதி"],"ch":"so:88"},"so:2034":{"book":"soolamani","num":2032,"t":["கங்குலவ ணில்லைகலி யில்லைநலி வில்லை","அங்கவர்க ணாளிடைக ழித்தமிழ் தயின்றால்","எங்குமில வின்பவெழி லெய்தறரு மீதால்","தங்கிய தவத்தரசர்க் கீந்தபயன் றானே."],"p":"","r":"There is no night, no quarrel, no suffering for them; the pleasure they eat through their days is the fruit of what they gave to austere kings.","n":"Gift to ascetic kings (தவத்தரசர்க்கு ஈந்த) is named as the cause of the enjoyment-land birth, so the pleasure is itself a reward for renunciation's patrons.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inbam","thavam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 2034","lb":"SO2034","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["what is given to ascetics bears the fruit of painless pleasure in the next birth","jain","asserted",4,"sage_teacher"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிவர்","பயாபதி"],"ch":"so:88"},"so:2035":{"book":"soolamani","num":2033,"t":["அன்னமிகு போகமவ ரெய்திவிளை யாடி","முன்னமுடி பல்லமவை மூன்றுடன் முடித்தால்","பின்னுமவர் தம்வழி பிறந்தவரை நோக்கி","மின்னுமினி தேறுவது வானுலக மன்னா."],"p":"","r":"After enjoying the pleasures and completing the three palla spans, they look upon their descendants and rise to the heavenly world.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kootru","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 2035","lb":"SO2035","mm":1,"ev":"The pair complete their span, look at their children, and go up to the world of the gods.","cl":[["the pair of the enjoyment land end their life and rise to heaven","jain","asserted",4,"sage_teacher"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிவர்","பயாபதி"],"ch":"so:88"},"so:2036":{"book":"soolamani","num":2034,"t":["பல்லமுத லோர்பகுதி மூன்றிரண்டு மொன்றும்","அல்லவிரு வர்க்க மிழ்து மம்முறையி னேறும்","நல்லநிலங் காலமுயர் வென்றிவைக ணாடிச்","சொல்லவுல வா விவர்கள் செய்கைசுடர் வேலோய்."],"p":"","r":"The teacher lists how those of one, two and three palla portions, in order, go to the lands, times and heights they have earned, and says the whole account is too long to give.","n":"The terms palla and the numbered portions (lines 1-2) are rare technical words; the sense is that the length of life and the later rise are graded by merit.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaalam","vinai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 2036","lb":"SO2036","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிவர்","பயாபதி"],"ch":"so:88"},"so:2037":{"book":"soolamani","num":2035,"t":["செம்பவழம் வெண்பளிங்கு பைந்தளிர் சிறக்கும்","வம்பழகு கொண்டமணி மேனியவர் பூவார்","கொம்பவிழுஞ் சண்பகங்கண் முல்லையிணர்க் கோங்கம்","அம்பவழ வண்ண முதலானவர்மெய் நாற்றம்."],"p":"","r":"Their bodies are like red coral, white crystal and green sprout, and their scent is like the scent of champak, jasmine and kongu blossoms.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 2037","lb":"SO2037","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிவர்","பயாபதி"],"ch":"so:88"},"so:2038":{"book":"soolamani","num":2036,"t":["நலங்கண்மிகு நம்முலகி னன்மைமிகு நீரால்","புலங்கண்மிகு போகமொடு போகநிலத் துள்ளால்","விலங்கொடுள வாழ்பறவை யவ்வுடம்பு விட்டால்","கலங்கண்மிகு கற்பநில மேறுவன கண்டாய்."],"p":"","r":"Beasts and birds born in the enjoyment land, when they leave their bodies, also rise to the kalpa world of the gods.","n":"Even animals are drawn into the ladder of merit, which differs from the Naladiyar's picture where a beast's birth is a mark of fall.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","piravi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 2038","lb":"SO2038","mm":1,"ev":"Animals of the enjoyment land die and rise to the kalpa heaven.","cl":[["beasts and birds born in the enjoyment land also rise to heaven","jain","asserted",4,"sage_teacher"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிவர்","பயாபதி"],"ch":"so:88"},"so:2039":{"book":"soolamani","num":2037,"t":["பூவிரியு நறுமேனிப் பொன்னிலங்கு நிமிர்சோதித்","தேவர்கடந் திறமுரைத்த றேவருக்கு மரிதெனினும்","நாவிரவி நாமுரைப்ப நால்வகையாய் விரியுமவை","ஓவரிய பெரும்புகழா யொருவகையா லுரைப்பக்கேள்."],"p":"","r":"The teacher says that the gods' ways are hard to tell even for gods, but he will speak them briefly, since they spread out in four kinds.","n":"The speaker marks his own account as a short one of something hard to say, and opens the list of the gods.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 2039","lb":"SO2039","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["the gods are of four kinds","jain","asserted",3,"sage_teacher"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிவர்","பயாபதி"],"ch":"so:88"},"so:2040":{"book":"soolamani","num":2038,"t":["ஈரைவர் பவணர்களு மிருநால்வர் வியந்தரரும்","ஒரைவர் சோதிடரு மொருபதின்மே லறுவரெனுங்","காரைய முறுவகையாய் கற்பகரு மீயுலகிற்","சீரைய மில்லாத திருமலர்த்தார்த் தேவரே."],"p":"","r":"He names the four orders of gods: the ten Bhavanas, the eight Vyantaras, the five Jyotishkas and the sixteen kalpa gods, who are without end in this world.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["perumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 2040","lb":"SO2040","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["the gods are ten Bhavana, eight Vyantara, five Jyotishka and sixteen kalpa","jain","asserted",1,"sage_teacher"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிவர்","பயாபதி"],"ch":"so:88"},"so:2041":{"book":"soolamani","num":2039,"t":["உற்றவர்க்கு மேலவர்க ளொன்பதின்ம ரொன்பதின்மர்","மற்றவர்க்கு மேலவரை வகையரவர் மேலவர்கள்","இற்றவர தெண்வகையா மிவர்க்கென்று மில்லாத","செற்றநோய் செயிர்பகையென் றிவைமுதலசெல வுணர்நீ."],"p":"","r":"Above those he names are nine and nine upper gods, and above them eight kinds, and the teacher says they have no sickness, anger or enmity.","n":"The tally of the upper ranks (lines 1-3) is hard to fix; the verse stands as a rough ladder of higher heavens.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["perumai","thavam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 2041","lb":"SO2041","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["the upper gods are free of sickness, anger and enmity","jain","asserted",4,"sage_teacher"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிவர்","பயாபதி"],"ch":"so:88"},"so:2042":{"book":"soolamani","num":2040,"t":["அருமணியி னொளிநிழற்று மாயிரமாம் பணமணிந்த","திருமணிசேர் முடியவருந் தீயொழுகு சிகையருமாப்","பருமணிய படலஞ்சேர் பவணத்துப் பதின்மர்கெளாண்","குருமணிகொ ணெடுமுடியாய் கூறுபா டுடையவரே."],"p":"","r":"The ten Bhavana lords wear crowns of a thousand hoods set with jewels, some with flaming hair, and live in dwellings of gem-bright halls.","n":"A word in line 3 (கெளாண்) looks corrupt and the sentence is left loosely read.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","perumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 2042","lb":"SO2042","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிவர்","பயாபதி"],"ch":"so:88"},"so:2043":{"book":"soolamani","num":2041,"t":["கின்னரர்கண் முதலாய வியந்தரரைக் கிளந்துரைப்பின்","இன்னநர ருலகத்து ளெவ்வழியு முளராகி","மென்னரம்பி னிசைகேட்டும் வெறியயர்வு கண்டுவந்தும்","மன்னவரை வணங்கியுந்தம் மனமகிழ்வ ரொருசாரார்."],"p":"","r":"Of the Vyantaras, beginning with the Kinnaras, some live in the world of men, hear the sound of soft strings, watch the god-possession rites, and bow to kings.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kurippu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 2043","lb":"SO2043","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிவர்","பயாபதி"],"ch":"so:88"},"so:2044":{"book":"soolamani","num":2042,"t":["குலகிரியு மலையரசுங் குளிர்பொழிலு நளிர்கயமும்","பலகிரியுந் தீவகமும் படுகடலும் படிநகரும்","உலகிரிய வெளிப்பட்டு மொளிகரந்து முறைந்தியல்வர்","அலகிரியும் பலகுணத்தோ யமரர்களே னைப்பலரே."],"p":"","r":"The Vyantaras show themselves or hide, dwell in mountains, groves, tanks, islands, seas and cities, and are many.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["idam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 2044","lb":"SO2044","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிவர்","பயாபதி"],"ch":"so:88"},"so:2045":{"book":"soolamani","num":2043,"t":["சந்திரருஞ் சூரியருந் தாரகையு நாண்மீனும்","வெந்திறல கோட்களுமா மெனவிளங்கி விசும்பாறா","மந்தரத்தை வலஞ்சூழ்ந்து வருபவரு நிற்பவரும்","சுந்தரஞ்சேர் மணிமுடியாய் சுடர்பவருஞ் சோதிடரே."],"p":"","r":"The Jyotishkas are the moons and suns, the stars and constellations and the fierce planets, who circle Mount Meru in the sky, some moving and some fixed.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aakaasam","kaalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 2045","lb":"SO2045","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிவர்","பயாபதி"],"ch":"so:88"},"so:2046":{"book":"soolamani","num":2044,"t":["எண்ணியமுத் தேவர்களு மிவர்மடந்தை யவருமாய்க்","கண்ணியறூ நற்காட்சிக் கதிர்விளக்குத் தூண்டினார்","நண்ணுபவோ வெனினண்ணார் நல்விரதந் தலைநின்று","புண்ணியங்கள் படைத்தாரக் குழுவினிடைப் பொலிவாரே."],"p":"","r":"The three kinds of gods with their wives lit the lamp of right faith; those who do not reach it do not, but those who kept good vows shine in the company.","n":"The first lines name the gods only as 'three' and the second half is hard to attach, so the reading is tentative.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thavam","aram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 2046","lb":"SO2046","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["right faith and good vows lead to merit and a place among the gods","jain","asserted",3,"sage_teacher"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிவர்","பயாபதி"],"ch":"so:88"},"so:2047":{"book":"soolamani","num":2045,"t":["காதலரிற் பிழையாராய்க் கள்ளூன்றேன் கடிந்தகற்றி","ஈதலோ டில்லிருக்கு மிளம்பிடியர் முதலாயார்","ஓதினமுத் தேவரா யுயர்ந்தவர்க்கு ளுயர்ந்துளராய்ச்","சோதியும்பே ரெண்குணனுந் துப்புரவுந் துன்னுவரே."],"p":"","r":"Those who stay true to their beloved, give up wine and flesh, give gifts and keep the home, together with the young women of their houses, are said to rise as gods among the highest, with light, the eight qualities and their pleasures.","n":"Line 3 ஓதினமுத்தேவராய் is read as 'the gods we have named'; the sense of முத்தேவர் here is not fixed, so the reading keeps only that these householders become gods.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["eegai","pulaal","marumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 2047","lb":"SO2047","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["a householder who gives, keeps from drink and flesh and is true to the spouse is born among the gods","jain","asserted",1,"sage_teacher"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிவர்","பயாபதி"],"ch":"so:89"},"so:2048":{"book":"soolamani","num":2046,"t":["மந்தரமா நெடுமலையின் மத்தகத்து மேற்கூற்றின்","அந்தரப்பே ருலகத்து ளமரரைமற் றறையுங்கால்","இந்திரவில் லெனவெளிப்பட் டிமையவர்க டொழுதேத்தச்","சுந்தரநன் மணிப்படிவ மெனச்சுடர்ந்து தோன்றுவரே."],"p":"","r":"The teacher says the gods in the sky above Mount Mandara show themselves like a rainbow, shining like images of fine gems while the heavenly ones hail them.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["marumai","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 2048","lb":"SO2048","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிவர்","பயாபதி"],"ch":"so:89"},"so:2049":{"book":"soolamani","num":2047,"t":["அலர்மாரி மேற்சொரிவா ரமிழ்தநீ ராட்டுவார்","பலர்மாண்ட கலனணிந்து பலாண்டிசைப்பார் பாடுவார்","மலர்மாண்ட மணிக்கவரி மருங்கசைப்பார் மடந்தையரைச்","சிலர்மாணச் சேர்த்துவார் தேவரா யதுபொழுதே."],"p":"","r":"At that moment of birth as gods they are showered with flowers, bathed in nectar water, dressed in many ornaments, hailed and sung to, fanned with flower-gem whisks, and joined by women.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["marumai","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 2049","lb":"SO2049","mm":1,"ev":"A newly born god is showered with flowers, bathed in nectar and adorned while others hail him.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிவர்","பயாபதி"],"ch":"so:89"},"so:2050":{"book":"soolamani","num":2048,"t":["ஆடாது மொளிதிகழு மாரணங்கு திருமேனி","வாடாத கண்ணியினர் மழுங்காத பூந்துகிலர்","ஏடார்ந்த தொங்கலரா யின்பநீர்ப் பெருவெள்ளம்","நீடாரக் குளித்தாடு நிலைமையரே யவரெல்லாம்."],"p":"","r":"The gods' bodies shine without being dyed, their wreaths do not fade and their robes do not dull, and they bathe long in a great flood of delight.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inbam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 2050","lb":"SO2050","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிவர்","பயாபதி"],"ch":"so:89"},"so:2051":{"book":"soolamani","num":2049,"t":["பொன்மாட நெடுநிலத்தார் புகலமளி யணைமேலார்","கன்மாடு பொன்வளருங் கதிர்மணிக்குன் றதன்மேலார்","மின்மாடு மிளிர்ந்திலங்கு விமானத்தா ரெனினல்லால்","சொன்மாடு பிறிதில்லைச் சுவர்க்கஞ்சேர்ந் தவர்கட்கே."],"p":"","r":"The teacher says the gods who reach heaven have golden mansions, soft couches, gem hills and flying cars, and that there is nothing more to say of them than this.","n":"The 'nothing else to say' of line 4 turns the picture into an inventory: heaven is a list of comforts, and 2060 then says that even it has an end.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inbam","marumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 2051","lb":"SO2051","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிவர்","பயாபதி"],"ch":"so:89"},"so:2052":{"book":"soolamani","num":2050,"t":["கந்தருவக் கோட்டியுள்ளார் கண்கனிய நாடகங்கண்","டிந்திரனோ டினிதினிருந் திளம்பிடியார் பாராட்டச்","சுந்தரமா மணிமாடச் சூளிகைய ரெனினல்லால்","அந்தரமேற் பிறிதில்லை யமரருல கடைந்தவர்க்கே."],"p":"","r":"In the gods' assembly of musicians they watch a play, sit pleasantly with Indra while young women praise them, and live on jewelled upper terraces; this is all the sky holds for those who reach the gods' world.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inbam","marumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 2052","lb":"SO2052","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிவர்","பயாபதி"],"ch":"so:89"},"so:2053":{"book":"soolamani","num":2051,"t":["கந்தாரங் களித்தனைய பனிமொழியார் கண்கவர","மந்தார வனத்திடையார் மணிமுழவி னிசைவாங்க","மந்தார மணியரங்கி னெனும்வார்த்தை யவையல்லாற்","செந்தாரோய் தேவர்கள் செய் திறற்றொழின்மற் றுடையரே."],"p":"","r":"With honey-voiced women to ravish the eye, a Mandara grove and drums sounding on a gem stage, the gods have such pleasures and their feats of power, and that is the report of them, the teacher says to the garlanded king.","n":"Line 4's செய்திறற்றொழில் is taken as the deeds the gods' power allows; the word is rare and the reading is not fixed.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["inbam","marumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 2053","lb":"SO2053","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிவர்","பயாபதி"],"ch":"so:89"},"so:2054":{"book":"soolamani","num":2052,"t":["தீர்த்தங்க டிறந்தவர்க்குச் சிறப்போடு திசையெல்லாந்","தேர்த்தங்க ணொளிபரப்பச் செல்பொழுதுந் தம்முலகில்","கார்த்தங்கு மயிலனையார் காமஞ்சேர் கனிகோட்டி","தார்த்தங்கு வரைமார்ப தம்முருவி னகலாரே."],"p":"","r":"When festivals are held for the Tirthankaras and light spreads over all directions, the peacock-like women and fruit-laden assemblies of the gods' own world are still not left behind by the gods.","n":"Line 1 தீர்த்தங்கள் திறந்தவர்க்கு and line 4 தம்முருவி னகலாரே are hard; the verse is read as the gods' worship of the Tirthankaras while they keep their own form, and the sense is not secure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","marumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 2054","lb":"SO2054","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["the gods honour the Tirthankaras at their festivals while remaining in their own forms and world","jain","reported",4,"sage_teacher"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிவர்","பயாபதி"],"ch":"so:89"},"so:2055":{"book":"soolamani","num":2053,"t":["இமையாத செங்கண்ண ரிரவறியார் பகலறியார்","அமையாத பிறப்பறியா ரழலறியார் பனியறியார்","சுமையாகி மணிமாலை சுடர்ந்திலங்கு நெடுமுடியார்","அமையாத நல்லுலகி னகைமணிப்பூ ணமரரே."],"p":"","r":"The gods have red eyes that never close, know neither night nor day, neither fire nor cold, and bear tall crowns heavy with strung gems.","n":"'அமையாத பிறப்பறியார்' in line 2 is read as 'know no unfitting birth'; the phrase may mean something else.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["marumai","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 2055","lb":"SO2055","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிவர்","பயாபதி"],"ch":"so:89"},"so:2056":{"book":"soolamani","num":2054,"t":["அணுவளவாய்ச் சிறுகுதன்மற் றதிநுட்ப மிகப்பெருகல்","நணியவர்போ னினைத்துழியே நண்ணுறுதல் விழைதகைமை","பணியினமைத் திடல்குறிப்பிற் பலவுருவு நனிகோடல்","துணிவமையு நெடுவேலோய் சுரருடைய குணங்களே."],"p":"","r":"The gods' powers are to shrink to an atom or swell very large, to arrive at a thought, to take whatever form they wish and many forms at will, and the like.","n":"Lines 3 and 4 are read as the remainder of the list of qualities; the construction is loose.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["siththi","marumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 2056","lb":"SO2056","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["gods have powers of shrinking, growing, arriving at once and taking many forms","jain","asserted",1,"sage_teacher"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிவர்","பயாபதி"],"ch":"so:89"},"so:2057":{"book":"soolamani","num":2055,"t":["அளிதருஞ்செங் கோலுடையோ யமரருக்குமந்தரமுண்","டொளியோடு பேரின்ப முயர்ந்தவர்க்கே யுயர்ந்துளவாம்","தெளிதரு நற் காட்சியது திருந்தியமே னெடுந்தகையோர்க்","கெளிதகவும் பெரும்பாலும் பெறலேனோர்க் கரியவே."],"p":"","r":"The teacher tells the king that heaven has levels and that the higher joy belongs to the higher beings; right vision, once perfected, is easy for the great, while others seldom get it.","n":"Line 4 can be read as saying the great find it easy or that it is mostly beyond the lesser; the reading keeps both halves.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["arivu","marumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 2057","lb":"SO2057","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["right vision once perfected is easily gained by the great and rarely by others","jain","asserted",3,"sage_teacher"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிவர்","பயாபதி"],"ch":"so:89"},"so:2058":{"book":"soolamani","num":2056,"t":["கனைகதிராக் கதிர்கலந்து கண்ணிலங்கு திருமூர்த்தி","புனைகதிரொண் மணிப்படிவம் பொழிந்ததுபோற் பொலிந்ததன்மேல்","வனைகதிரின் மணிமுடியும் மாணிக்கக் கடகமுமென்","றினமுதலாச் சிடர்ந்தினிதி னியல்பாய்நின் றெரியுமே."],"p":"","r":"The god's body shines as if rays of light had poured down on a gem image, and his gem crown and ruby bracelet burn with light of their own.","n":"Line 4's இனமுதலாச் சிடர்ந்தினிதி னியல்பாய் is corrupt-looking; only the shining is certain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","marumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 2058","lb":"SO2058","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிவர்","பயாபதி"],"ch":"so:89"},"so:2059":{"book":"soolamani","num":2057,"t":["செழுந்திரட்பூம் பாவைகளுந் திகழ்மணியின் சுடர்க்கொழுந்தும்","எழுந்திலங்கு மேனியரா யெரியுமணிக் கலந்தாங்கி","மொழிந்துலவாக் காரிகையார் முலைமுற்றா விளமையார்","அழிந்தலராக் காரிகைமா ரமரரசர் தேவியரே."],"p":"","r":"The wives of the divine kings have bodies of bright figurines and shining jewels, speak little, stay forever young and never wither.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","yaakkai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 2059","lb":"SO2059","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிவர்","பயாபதி"],"ch":"so:89"},"so:2060":{"book":"soolamani","num":2058,"t":["இன்பமே பெரிதாகி யிடையறவின் றிமைப்பளவும்","துன்பமொன் றில்லாத துறக்கத்திற் பெருஞ்செல்வம்","மன்பெருமா தவத்தினால் வருமொருநா ளீறுடைய","தன்பதன்கண் மிசையேயென் றடிகடரு பொரு டெளிந்தார்."],"p":"","r":"Heaven's great wealth is all joy, with no pain even for a blink, and it comes through great austerity; but it ends some day, and those who understood the Lord's teaching saw that something higher lies above it.","n":"மிசை in line 4 may mean 'above' or 'further on'; the verse hints at release beyond heaven without naming it, and the following verses go on counting heaven's spans.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thavam","nilaiyaamai","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 2060","lb":"SO2060","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["the great pleasure of heaven is won by austerity and has an end","jain","asserted",3,"sage_teacher"],["a state higher than heaven exists","jain","reported",4,"sage_teacher"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிவர்","பயாபதி"],"ch":"so:89"},"so:2061":{"book":"soolamani","num":2059,"t":["பவணத்தார்க் கொருகடலா மிகையமரும் பல்லமொன்றாம்","இவணொத்த வமரருக்கு மிருவிசும்பிற் சுடரவர்க்கும்","சிவணொத்த வுயர்வாழ்நாள் சென்றபினர்ச் செல்கதியும்","அவணொத்த தத்தமது விதிவகையா மதிபதியே."],"p":"","r":"The Bhavana gods live a sea of years and a bit more, the equal gods here a palya, and the sun and moon gods similarly; after their span each one's destiny follows his own lot.","n":"Lines 1 and 2 are technical (கடல், பல்லம்) and the lines are read as the lifespans of the Bhavana, Vyantara and Jyotishka orders; the hit on exact figures is uncertain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","vinai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 2061","lb":"SO2061","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["each order of gods has a fixed span measured in palya and sagara and then a destiny of its own","jain","asserted",4,"sage_teacher"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிவர்","பயாபதி"],"ch":"so:89"},"so:2062":{"book":"soolamani","num":2060,"t":["இரண்டாகு முதலவர்கட் கேழீரைந் தீரேழாய்த்","திரண்டிரண்டாய் மூவுலகத் தொழிந்தவர்சேர் பிரண் டிரண்டாய்","அரண்டகவந் தேறிப்பின் னாரணவச் சுதருலகின்","மருண்டாய மணிமுந்நீர் பதினொன்றற் கிருமடியே."],"p":"","r":"The lowest of the kalpa heavens are counted in pairs and groups, and their spans rise with each step up to twice eleven seas.","n":"The numbers of lines 1 to 3 are cryptic; the last line's twice eleven seas (twenty-two) fits the span of the highest of the kalpa heavens.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","marumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 2062","lb":"SO2062","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிவர்","பயாபதி"],"ch":"so:89"},"so:2063":{"book":"soolamani","num":2061,"t":["ஆங்கவர்மே லமரரசர் மும்மூவர்க் கொரோவொன்றாய்","ஓங்கினர்மே லொன்பதின்மர்க் கொன்றொன்றா யவர்மேலார்","பாங்கினுறப் பெறுகுவன பதினைந்திற் கிருமடிமேல்","வாங்கொலிநீ ரொருமூன்று வாழ்வென்ப மணிமுடியாய்."],"p":"","r":"Above these come the three triples and the nine, each a step higher, and the span rises to thirty-three seas for the highest, the teacher says to the crowned king.","n":"The count of three, nine and fifteen doubled and three more reads as thirty-three; the arithmetic is the reading's own.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","marumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 2063","lb":"SO2063","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிவர்","பயாபதி"],"ch":"so:89"},"so:2064":{"book":"soolamani","num":2062,"t":["ஆயிடைய வமரரசர் திறம்வினவி னணங்கனையார்","வேயிடைமென் பணைப்பொற்றோள் விழைவின்றிப் பெரிதாகி","ஏயிடையோ ரறவின்றா வின்பஞ்செய் திருமூர்த்தி","சேயிடையொள் ெளாளிநிழற்றச் செம்மாந்தா ரிருந்தாரே."],"p":"","r":"As for the divine kings of those realms, they are great beings who feel no longing for women and sit proudly in a lasting pleasure under their own radiance.","n":"Line 4 has a damaged word (சேயிடையொள் ெளாளிநிழற்ற); the sense of radiance is guessed.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["inbam","marumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 2064","lb":"SO2064","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["the highest gods are free of desire for women and enjoy unbroken pleasure","jain","asserted",2,"sage_teacher"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிவர்","பயாபதி"],"ch":"so:89"},"so:2065":{"book":"soolamani","num":2063,"t":["ஊனிலா வுறுப்பமையா வொளியமா யுலகெல்லாம்","பானிலாப் பரந்தெறிப்பப் பளிங்கினது படிவம்போன்","மேனிலா மணியனையார் வெண்சங்கே ரிலைச்சையாம்","கோனிலா வவரின்மிக் கவரில்லைக் குடைவேந்தே."],"p":"","r":"The highest gods have bodies without flesh and limbs of light that spread over all the worlds, shining like crystal, with no one above them and none greater.","n":"Line 3's வெண்சங்கே ரிலைச்சையாம் is obscure; கோனிலா in line 4 is taken as 'having no king'.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["marumai","perumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 2065","lb":"SO2065","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["the highest gods have no lord over them and none greater","jain","asserted",4,"sage_teacher"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிவர்","பயாபதி"],"ch":"so:89"},"so:2066":{"book":"soolamani","num":2064,"t":["அப்பால தத்திதியா மதனிலமைந் தாலூணின்","றொப்பாரும் பிறிதிவணின் றூழிநாட் பெயர்ந்திழிவின்","றெப்பாலுந் திரிவின்றோ ரியல்பாய வின்பத்தான்","மெய்ப்பால தவ்வரைசர் வீற்றிருக்கும் வியனுலகே."],"p":"","r":"Beyond that is a realm where equals live in a joy that never changes and no fall comes after long ages, the teacher says, a wide world where their kings sit enthroned.","n":"Line 1's அத்திதியா மதனிலமைந் தாலூணின் is hard; the reading keeps only an unchanging realm of equals.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["inbam","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 2066","lb":"SO2066","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிவர்","பயாபதி"],"ch":"so:89"},"so:2067":{"book":"soolamani","num":2065,"t":["கதிநான்குங் கதிசேரும் வாயிலுமிவ் விவையிதனால்","விதிமாண்ட நரகமும்புன் விலங்குகளுஞ் சேராமை","மதிமாண்ட நற்காட்சி வழிநின்று தவந்தாங்கில்","நிதிமாண்ட பெருஞ்செல்வ நீங்காத வியல்பென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"The teacher closes by saying that the four destinies and the doors to them are as told, and that a person who stands in firm right vision and bears austerity avoids hell and the animal births and keeps a great, unfailing wealth.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thavam","naragam","marumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 2067","lb":"SO2067","mm":1,"ev":"The teacher ends his account of the destinies and states the way to the unfailing wealth.","cl":[["one who holds right vision and bears austerity escapes hell and animal births","jain","asserted",3,"sage_teacher"],["such a one gains a great wealth that does not leave","jain","asserted",4,"sage_teacher"]],"ag":["முனிவர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிவர்","பயாபதி"],"ch":"so:89"},"so:2068":{"book":"soolamani","num":2066,"t":["உறுதிகணன் குரைக்குங்கா லுபசார முரைப்பதோ","அறுதியில்பே ரருளீரென் றரசனாங் கடிதொழலும்","இறுதியிலாப் பேரின்ப மெய்துமா றெடுத்துரைத்தான்","மறுதரவில் கதிபடரு மாதவத்து வரம்பாயோன்."],"p":"","r":"When the king bows and asks for grace, the sage, the highest of those of great austerity, sets out how one reaches unending bliss, since a talk of welfare is not a place for flattery.","n":"Line 1 is read as the narrator's remark; it could also be the teacher's, and உறுதிகணன் is a doubtful word.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thuravu","arul","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 2068","lb":"SO2068","mm":1,"ev":"King Payapati bows to the sage, who then lays out the way to endless bliss.","cl":[],"ag":["பயாபதி","முனிவர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:89"},"so:2069":{"book":"soolamani","num":2067,"t":["இருவகை வினைகளு மில்ல திவ்வழி","வருவகை யிலாதது மறுவின் மாதவர்","பெருவழி யாச்செலும் பெயர்வில் சூளிகைக்","கொருவழி யல்லதிங் குரைப்ப தில்லையே."],"p":"","r":"The teacher says that with the two kinds of deeds gone nothing new can come on this road, and that for the faultless great ascetics there is one broad way, the one leading to the summit, and nothing else to tell.","n":"The two kinds of deeds (இருவகை வினை) are not named here; they are most likely good and bad deed, but the verse does not say so. சூளிகை in line 3 is read as a summit or pinnacle, a rare word, and line 4 is compressed.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vinai","veedu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 2069","lb":"SO2069","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["when both kinds of deeds are gone nothing further arises, and release is the one road of the great ascetics","jain","asserted",1,"sage_teacher"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிவர்","பயாபதி"],"ch":"so:90"},"so:2070":{"book":"soolamani","num":2068,"t":["பிறந்தவன் பொறிப்புலக் கிவரு மப்புலம்","சிறந்தபின் விழைவொடு செற்றஞ் செய்திடும்","மறைந்தவை வாயிலா வினைக ளீட்டினால்","இறந்தவன் பின்னுமவ் வியற்கை யெய்துமே."],"p":"","r":"The teacher says that the one born is bound to the objects of his senses, that once those objects have matured he feels desire and hatred, and that by heaping up hidden deeds through them even the dead man meets the same nature again.","n":"Line 3 says the deeds are gathered where there is no door (வாயிலா); I take this as deeds heaped unseen, which is a reading of a compressed phrase.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vinai","piravi","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 2070","lb":"SO2070","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["desire and hatred toward sense objects pile up deeds that bring the same nature again after death","jain","asserted",3,"sage_teacher"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிவர்","பயாபதி"],"ch":"so:90"},"so:2071":{"book":"soolamani","num":2069,"t":["பிறவிச்சக் கரமிது பெரிது மஞ்சினான்","துறவிக்கட் டுணிகுவன் றுணிந்து தூயனாய்","உறவிக்க ணருளுடை யொழுக்க மோம்பினான்","மறவிக்க ணிலாததோர் மாட்சி யெய்துமே."],"p":"","r":"The teacher says that one who greatly fears this wheel of births resolves on renunciation, becomes pure, guards a conduct full of compassion toward kin and living things, and reaches a greatness free of forgetfulness.","n":"உறவிக்கண் in line 3 may mean toward kin or toward all that live; the reading keeps both open.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thuravu","piravi","arul"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 2071","lb":"SO2071","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["fear of the wheel of births leads to renunciation and a compassionate conduct that ends in a state free of forgetting","jain","asserted",1,"sage_teacher"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிவர்","பயாபதி"],"ch":"so:90"},"so:2072":{"book":"soolamani","num":2070,"t":["காட்சியு ஞானமுங் கதிர்த்துத் தன்பொறி","மாட்சியை வெலீஇமனந் தூய னாயபின்","நாட்செய்து நவிற்றிய தியான வீதியான்","மீட்சியில் வீட்டுல கெய்தும் வீரனே."],"p":"","r":"The teacher says that when right vision and knowledge shine and the mind has conquered the pride of the senses and is pure, the hero who has long practised the road of meditation reaches the world of release from which there is no return.","n":"The way is the Jain road of vision, knowledge and conduct, reached by the hero's own effort; nothing in the verse gives a god or a grace any part in it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["gnaanam","thiyaanam","veedu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 2072","lb":"SO2072","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["release is won by right vision, knowledge, a conquered mind and long meditation, and there is no return from it","jain","asserted",4,"sage_teacher"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிவர்","பயாபதி"],"ch":"so:90"},"so:2073":{"book":"soolamani","num":2071,"t":["கடையிலெண் குணத்தது காம ராகர்கள்","இடைநனி யிலாத தில் லியற்கை யில்லது","மிடையொடு விழைவுவே ரறுத்த வீரர்கள்","அடைவதோர் நிலைபிறர்க் கறிய லாகுமோ."],"p":"","r":"The teacher says that the final state belongs to the heroes who have cut desire and craving at the root, and asks whether others can know it.","n":"கடையிலெண் குணத்தது in line 1 is read as the state of the eight qualities (எண்குணம்) with no end; the sense turns on compressed words and on line 2, which is hard to parse.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["veedu","kaamam","gnaanam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 2073","lb":"SO2073","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["the last state of the eight qualities is reached only by those who have cut craving and attachment, and others cannot know it","jain","asserted",1,"sage_teacher"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிவர்","பயாபதி"],"ch":"so:90"},"so:2074":{"book":"soolamani","num":2072,"t":["மணிமலர்ந் துமிழ்தரு மொளியுஞ் சந்தனத்","துணிமலர்ந் துமிழ்தருந் தண்மைத் தோற்றமும்","நணிமலர் நாற்றமு மென்ன வன்னதால்","அணிவரு சிவகதி யடைய தின்பமே."],"p":"","r":"The teacher says the bliss of release is like the light that pours from a jewel, the coolness of a sandal chip and the scent of a flower close at hand.","n":"சிவகதி here means the state of release; it does not name Siva. The three pleasant comparisons are all sensory, though the verse is about a state said to lie beyond the senses.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inbam","veedu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 2074","lb":"SO2074","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["the bliss of the state of release can be shown by comparisons with light, coolness and fragrance","jain","asserted",4,"sage_teacher"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிவர்","பயாபதி"],"ch":"so:90"},"so:2075":{"book":"soolamani","num":2073,"t":["வடுவறு மாதவ னுரைப்ப மாண்புடை","அடிகள தறவமிழ் துண்ட வாற்றலான்","முடிவுகொ ளுலகெய்த முயல்வ னென்றனன்","விடுகதிர் மணிமுடி வென்றி வேந்தனே."],"p":"","r":"When the faultless ascetic has spoken, the victorious king, who has drunk the nectar of the teacher's word, says he will strive to reach the world where there is no return.","n":"மாதவ னுரைப்ப is read as the great ascetic speaking; the sage is the one the epic's earlier chapters name as the teacher, but the name does not appear here.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thuravu","veedu","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 2075","lb":"SO2075","mm":1,"ev":"Payapati, having drunk the teacher's words, declares that he will strive for release.","cl":[["a king who has heard the teaching resolves to strive for the final world","jain","reported",3,"payapati"]],"ag":["பயாபதி","முனிவர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:90"},"so:2076":{"book":"soolamani","num":2074,"t":["மிக்கெழு போதிகை விலக்க றக்கதன்","றொக்கநன் றுடன்பட லுலக மேன்றெனத்","தக்கவாய் மொழிந்தவத் தரச னேர்ந்திலன்","தொக்கவான் புகழவற் கமைச்சர் சொல்லினார்."],"p":"","r":"The king speaks fittingly that his resolve cannot be hindered and that the world has agreed to it, but the ministers, who have not agreed, answer him.","n":"The first two lines are hard: போதிகை, விலக்க றக்கதன்று and ஒக்க நன்றுடன்படல் can be read as the king's resolve that none may bar, or as a general maxim of agreement, and who exactly speaks in lines 1 and 2 is not fixed. The verse's last line leads straight into the ministers' words in the next verses.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["amaichu","thuravu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 2076","lb":"SO2076","mm":1,"ev":"The ministers reply to the king who has announced his resolve.","cl":[],"ag":["பயாபதி","அருத்தநூலவர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:90"},"so:2077":{"book":"soolamani","num":2075,"t":["இருட்பிலத் தரும்பட ரெய்திப் பல்புகழ்","வருட்டதை யிலனலிந் துண்ண வாழ்பவன்","பொருட்டகு வாயில்பெற் றுய்ந்து போம்வழி","உருட்டுவா னொருவனை யுவந்து நாடுமோ."],"p":"","r":"The ministers ask whether one who lived in a dark pit of unbearable pain, with no comfort from anyone, and who has got out through a door by good fortune, would gladly seek the man who would roll him back in.","n":"The verse is a simile with the application left unsaid; it is given its sense by 2079, where the ministers urge the king to throw off the crown. உருட்டுவான் ஒருவனை is read as one who rolls him back.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thuravu","vinai","piravi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 2077","lb":"SO2077","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["one who has escaped the pit of birth and pain would not choose to go back to it","jain","asserted",4,"ministers"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["அருத்தநூலவர்","பயாபதி"],"ch":"so:90"},"so:2078":{"book":"soolamani","num":2076,"t":["அருஞ்சிறைப் பிணியுழந் தலைப்புண் டஞ்சுவான்","பெருஞ்சிறை தனைப்பிழைத் துய்ந்து போயபின்","கருஞ்சிறைக் கயவர்கைப் பட்டு வெந்துயர்","தருஞ்சிறைக் களமது சென்று சாருமோ."],"p":"","r":"The ministers add a second image, asking whether one who has fled a heavy prison and escaped would go back into the cruel jail of rough guards.","n":"Prison and pit are the same figure for worldly life; the ministers' point is made by questions, and the second image repeats the first with a harsher keeper.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thuravu","piravi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 2078","lb":"SO2078","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["one who has escaped the prison of birth would not return to it","jain","asserted",4,"ministers"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["அருத்தநூலவர்","பயாபதி"],"ch":"so:90"},"so:2079":{"book":"soolamani","num":2077,"t":["பிணிபடு பிறவிநோய் பெயர்க்கு மாதவம்","துணிபவன் றன்னொடு தொடர்ச்சி நோக்குமோ","அணிமுடி துறமினெம் மடிக ளென்றனர்","மணிமுடி மன்னவற் கமைச்ச ரென்பவே."],"p":"","r":"The ministers say that one who resolves on the great austerity that ends the disease of birth will not look for ties, and ask the jewel-crowned king to give up the crown.","n":"Here the king's own counsellors urge the renunciation they might have been expected to oppose; the objection sketched in 2076 resolves into their agreement. The verse's last words ('so the ministers are said to have spoken', என்பவே) pass the speech on as report.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["amaichu","thuravu","piravi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 2079","lb":"SO2079","mm":1,"ev":"The ministers urge Payapati to lay down his crown and renounce.","cl":[["one resolved on the austerity that ends the disease of birth looks for no ties","jain","reported",2,"ministers"],["a king should give up his crown for release","jain","reported",3,"ministers"]],"ag":["அருத்தநூலவர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:90"},"so:2080":{"book":"soolamani","num":2078,"t":["எனவவர் மொழிதலு மெழுந்து போதியின்","சினைமல ரிலங்குவேற் சிறுவர் தங்களை","வனமலர்க் கண்ணியான் கூவி மற்றவர்க்","கினலிலா னிவ்வுரை யெடுத்துச் செப்பினான்."],"p":"","r":"After the ministers speak, the king rises, calls his young sons with their bright spears, and says this to them without anger.","n":"இனலிலான் in line 4 is taken as without hurt or anger, the king speaking calmly to those he is about to leave in charge.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","makkal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 2080","lb":"SO2080","mm":1,"ev":"Payapati calls his sons together to speak to them.","cl":[],"ag":["பயாபதி","சிறுவர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:90"},"so:2081":{"book":"soolamani","num":2079,"t":["பொருளிலார்க் கிவ்வழிப் பொறியின் போகமும்","அருளிலார்க் கறத்தினாம் பயனு நூல்வழி","உருள்விலா மனத்தவர்க் குணர்வும் போன்மனம்","தெருளிலார்க் கிசைவில டிருவின் செல்வியே."],"p":"","r":"The king tells his sons that the goddess of wealth does not favour the unclear-minded, as the pleasure of the senses is not for the poor, the fruit of dharma for the merciless or the understanding of a text for a restless mind.","n":"The three comparisons set a lack against what it would need (money for pleasure, compassion for dharma's fruit, steadiness for learning); Lakshmi's favour is put on the same list, so the verse does not say wealth is bad, only that it does not stay with the unwise.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","nilaiyaamai","arivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 2081","lb":"SO2081","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["Lakshmi does not stay with those whose minds are not clear","general","asserted",4,"payapati"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["பயாபதி","சிறுவர்"],"ch":"so:90"},"so:2082":{"book":"soolamani","num":2080,"t":["திருமக ணிலைமையுஞ் செல்வர் கேட்டிரேல்","மருவிய மனிதரை யிகந்து மற்றவள்","பொருவறு புகழினிர் புதிய காமுறும்","ஒருவர்கண் ணுறவில ளுணர்ந்து கொண்மினே."],"p":"","r":"The king bids the wealthy hear the goddess's nature: she leaves the men she has joined and never gives herself to any, wanting always someone new.","n":"Wealth is a woman who stays with no one; the verse does not say any man can hold her by merit.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 2082","lb":"SO2082","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["the goddess of wealth leaves those she has joined and longs for new men","general","asserted",2,"payapati"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["பயாபதி","சிறுவர்"],"ch":"so:90"},"so:2083":{"book":"soolamani","num":2081,"t":["புண்ணிய முலந்தபின் பொருளி லார்களைக்","கண்ணிலர் துறந்திடுங் கணிகை மார்கள்போல்","எண்ணில ளிகழ்ந்திடும் யாவர் தம்மையும்","நண்ணிய நண்பில ணங்கை வண்ணமே."],"p":"","r":"The king says that when a man's merit is spent the goddess leaves the poor like courtesans who turn their backs on the penniless, and slights everyone, with no friendship for anyone she has reached.","n":"Line 1 makes wealth depend on merit (புண்ணியம்); the loss is a spent deed, not a changed fortune. The courtesan simile belongs to the epic's own habit of using the courtesan for fickleness, and it looks at her as the narrator's side does.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","nilaiyaamai","varaivinmagalir"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 2083","lb":"SO2083","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["wealth leaves a man when his merit is used up, as courtesans leave the poor","general","asserted",1,"payapati"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["பயாபதி","சிறுவர்"],"ch":"so:90"},"so:2084":{"book":"soolamani","num":2082,"t":["உற்றுநின் றொருவர்கண் ணிற்கு மாய்விடின்","மற்றவர் குணங்களை மறைத்து மாண்பிலாச்","செற்றமுஞ் சினங்களுஞ் செருக்குஞ் செய்திடும்","கற்றவர் தம்மையுங் கழற நோக்குமே."],"p":"","r":"The king says that if she stays by one man she hides his good qualities and breeds in him hatred, anger and pride, and makes even the learned look at others with scorn.","n":"Even her staying does harm; the verse says wealth, not her leaving, is what spoils the learned.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","kaamam","ozhukkam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 2084","lb":"SO2084","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["wealth that stays with a man hides his virtues and produces anger and pride","general","asserted",3,"payapati"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["பயாபதி","சிறுவர்"],"ch":"so:90"},"so:2085":{"book":"soolamani","num":2083,"t":["அம்பென நெடியகட் கணிகை யார்தமை","நம்பிய விளையவர் பொருளு நையுமால்","வம்பின மணிவண்டு வருடுந் தாமரைக்","கொம்பினை மகிழ்ந்தவர் குணங்க ளென்பவே."],"p":"","r":"The king says that the wealth of young men who trust courtesans with long arrow-like eyes is spent away, and that such is the nature of those who enjoy the lotus-creeper of the goddess.","n":"The last two lines are compressed and the link between the lotus-creeper and the courtesans is not spelled out; I take it as Lakshmi again, so the courtesan and the goddess are set side by side.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 2085","lb":"SO2085","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["young men who trust courtesans lose their wealth","general","asserted",2,"payapati"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["பயாபதி","சிறுவர்"],"ch":"so:90"},"so:2086":{"book":"soolamani","num":2084,"t":["ஆதலா லவடிறத் தன்பு செய்யன்மின்","ஏதிலா ரெனவிகழ்ந் தொழியும் யாரையும்","காதலா ராபவர் கற்ற மாந்தரே","போதுலா மலங்கலீர் புரிந்து கேண்மினே."],"p":"","r":"The king tells his garlanded sons not to trust her, and says that the ones who love those she scorns as strangers are the learned.","n":"The verse turns the warning into a rule of choice: attach to the learned, who do not leave. காதலாராபவர் is read as 'those who love', though the text is compressed.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","kalvi","arivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 2086","lb":"SO2086","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["the learned love those whom fortune slights, and the sons should not trust her","general","asserted",3,"payapati"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["பயாபதி","சிறுவர்"],"ch":"so:90"},"so:2087":{"book":"soolamani","num":2085,"t":["நிலமக ணிலைமையு நெறியிற் கேட்டிரேல்","குலமிலர் குணமில ரென்னுங் கோளிலள்","வலமிகு சூழ்ச்சியார் வழியண் மற்றவள்","உலமிகு வயிரத்தோ ளுருவத் தாரினீர்."],"p":"","r":"The king turns to the goddess of the earth and tells the sons that she has no family and no virtue as her rule, and that she goes along with men who are clever in strategy.","n":"The earth is spoken of as a woman who follows the strong and cunning king; the verse does not say whether the sons should fight for her. உலமிகு வயிரத்தோள் in line 4 is a praising address to the sons.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 2087","lb":"SO2087","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["the earth has no family or virtue she keeps to and follows those skilled in stratagem","general","asserted",2,"payapati"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["பயாபதி","சிறுவர்"],"ch":"so:90"},"so:2088":{"book":"soolamani","num":2086,"t":["தன்னுயர் மணலினும் பலர்க டன்னலம்","முன்னுகர்ந் திகந்தவர் மூரித் தானையீர்","பின்னும்வந் தவரொடுஞ் சென்று பேர்ந்திலள்","இன்னுமஃ தவடன தியற்கை வண்ணமே."],"p":"","r":"The king says that the earth has enjoyed more men than there are grains of sand and left them, and has not stayed even with those who came after, which is her nature even now.","n":"மூரித் தானையீர் addresses the sons as men with great armies; the verse tells them they are one more in a long line.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 2088","lb":"SO2088","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["the earth has left every king who held her and stays with none","general","asserted",1,"payapati"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["பயாபதி","சிறுவர்"],"ch":"so:90"},"so:2089":{"book":"soolamani","num":2087,"t":["வெற்றிவேன் மணிமுடி வேந்தர் தம்மொடும்","உற்றதோ ருரிமைக ளில்லள் யாரொடும்","பற்றிலள் பற்றினர் பால ளன்னதால்","முற்றுநீர்த் துகிலிடை முதுபெண் ணீர்மையே."],"p":"","r":"The king says that she has no bond with the victorious crowned kings, clings to no one, and keeps only those who cling to her, like an old woman between her watery clothes.","n":"The close of the line, முற்றுநீர்த் துகிலிடை முதுபெண் நீர்மையே, is hard: it may be an old woman wrapped in a wet cloth or a figure taken from the sea-girt earth. The reading is held loosely.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["arasu","pattru","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 2089","lb":"SO2089","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["the earth has no real tie to any king and clings only to him who clings","general","asserted",3,"payapati"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["பயாபதி","சிறுவர்"],"ch":"so:90"},"so:2090":{"book":"soolamani","num":2088,"t":["அடிமிசை யரசர்கள் பணிய வாண்டவன்","பொடிமிசை யப்புறம் புரள விப்புறம்","இடிமுர சதிரவொ ரிளவ றன்னொடு","கடிபுகு மவளது கற்பின் வண்ணமே."],"p":"","r":"The poet recalls the king who once ruled while kings bowed at his feet, the sounds of royal drums, and a younger brother, and then turns to the manner of the chastity of a woman who enters the wedding.","n":"The verse is broken: the line about the 'chastity of her who enters the bridal home' (line 4) has no stated link to the king in lines 1 to 3, and the younger brother is not named, so the verse is read only as a closing view of the old royal state before the renunciation begins.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["arasu","karpu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 2090","lb":"SO2090","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["பயாபதி"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:91"},"so:2091":{"book":"soolamani","num":2089,"t":["இன்னன விவடன தியற்கை யாதலால்","அன்னவள் பொருளென வார்வஞ் செய்யன்மின்","மன்னுயிர் காவனும் மக்க டாங்கினால்","பின்னைநுங் கருமமே பேணற் பாலிரே."],"p":"","r":"The king tells his hearers that such is her nature and that they should not cherish her as wealth, for once the people uphold the protector of living beings, they should look to their own duty.","n":"'She' (இவள், அவள்) is not named here; she may be royal fortune or the land, but the verse does not say, so the reading keeps the warning against loving her as property and nothing more.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["selvam","pattru","aram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 2091","lb":"SO2091","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["what is by nature fleeting is not to be cherished as one's own wealth","general","asserted",2,"payapati"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["பயாபதி","இளையவர்"],"ch":"so:91"},"so:2092":{"book":"soolamani","num":2090,"t":["மீனிவர் விரிதிரை வேலி காவன்மேல்","ஊனிவர் வேலினீ ருங்கள் பாலதால்","யானினி யெனக்கர சாக்க லுற்றனன்","தேனிவ ரலங்கலீர் செவ்வி காண்மினே."],"p":"","r":"The king tells the garlanded ones that the guarding of the sea-fenced land, with its spear-bearing warriors, now rests with them, and that he has begun to set his own rule, so they should look to the time.","n":"The sense of line 3 (யானினி எனக்கு அரசாக்கல் உற்றனன்) is not fixed: it may mean he has turned to rule himself, or has taken up some other sovereignty, and the verse leaves it open.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["arasu","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 2092","lb":"SO2092","mm":1,"ev":"The king hands over the guardianship of the land to the young princes and announces his own turn to another rule.","cl":[],"ag":["பயாபதி"],"ec":[],"spk":["பயாபதி","இளையவர்"],"ch":"so:91"},"so:2093":{"book":"soolamani","num":2091,"t":["உற்றநாள் சிலநுமக் கென்னொ டல்லது","மற்றநாள் பலவவை வருவ வாதலால்","கற்றமாண் சிந்தையீர் கவற்சி நீங்குமின்","இற்றையான் றுணிந்ததென் றிறைவன் செப்பினான்."],"p":"","r":"The king says that the days left to him with them are few and their own days many, so the learned should put away grief, for he has resolved today.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","thuravu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 2093","lb":"SO2093","mm":1,"ev":"Payapati tells his people he has resolved to leave.","cl":[["one's remaining span is short and the time to act is now","general","asserted",1,"payapati"]],"ag":["பயாபதி"],"ec":[],"spk":["பயாபதி","இளையவர்"],"ch":"so:91"},"so:2094":{"book":"soolamani","num":2092,"t":["என்றலு மிளையவ ரிறைஞ்சிக் கைதொழு","தின்றியா மடிகளைப் பிழைத்த தென்னென","ஒன்றுநீ ரிலீரென வுரையொ ழிந்தரோ","அன்றவர்க் கயலவ னாகிச் செப்பினான்."],"p":"","r":"The young princes bow and ask what fault they have committed against their lord, and he, already like a stranger to them, answers that they have done nothing wrong.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thuravu","pirivu","makkal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 2094","lb":"SO2094","mm":1,"ev":"The young princes ask what fault they have done; the king answers that it is nothing they did.","cl":[],"ag":["இளையவர்","பயாபதி"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:91"},"so:2095":{"book":"soolamani","num":2093,"t":["ஆவியா யரும்பெற லமிழ்த மாகிய","தேவிமார் தங்களைக் கூவிச் செவ்வனே","காவியாய் நெடுங்கணீர் கருதிற் றென்னென","மேவினார் தவமவர் வேந்தன் முன்னரே."],"p":"","r":"He calls the queens, dear as nectar, and asks why their long eyes weep; they take up austerity before the king.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thuravu","thavam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 2095","lb":"SO2095","mm":1,"ev":"The king asks the queens why they weep; they take up the vow before him.","cl":[],"ag":["பயாபதி","தேவிமார்"],"ec":[],"spk":["பயாபதி","தேவிமார்"],"ch":"so:91"},"so:2096":{"book":"soolamani","num":2094,"t":["இமைப்பதும் பெருமிகை யினியி ருந்தனெ","நமைப்புறு பிறவிநோய் நடுங்க நோற்கிய","அமைச்சரு மரசர்கோ னருளி னாற்றம","சுமைப்பெரும் பாரத்தின் றொழுதி நீக்கினார்."],"p":"","r":"The ministers who had practised austerity, trembling at the disease of birth, now by the grace of the king-of-kings throw off the great burden they bore.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thavam","piravi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 2096","lb":"SO2096","mm":1,"ev":"The ministers, who have been practising austerity, put down the burden of the body by the king's grace.","cl":[["austerity is practised in dread of the disease of rebirth","general","asserted",2,"narrator"]],"ag":["மந்திரக் கிழவர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:91"},"so:2097":{"book":"soolamani","num":2095,"t":["அணிமுடி யமரர்தந் தாற்றப் பாற்கடல்","மணிமுடி யமிழ்தநீ ராடி மாதவர்","பணியொடு பன்மணிக் கலங்க ணீக்கினான்","துணிவொடு சுரமைநா டுடைய தோன்றலே."],"p":"","r":"The lord of the land of Suramai, with resolution, bathes in water from the milk sea and takes off his many jewelled ornaments for the great ascetics.","n":"Lines 1 and 2 are tangled (the gods' part in bringing the milk-sea water and the crown's place); only the bath and the taking off of ornaments are firm.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thuravu","yaakkai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 2097","lb":"SO2097","mm":1,"ev":"Payapati bathes and puts off his ornaments in the presence of the ascetics.","cl":[],"ag":["பயாபதி","தேவர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:91"},"so:2098":{"book":"soolamani","num":2096,"t":["அருமுடி துறந்தன னரச னாயிடைத்","திருமுடி மணித்துணர் தேவர் கொண்டுபோய்ப்","பருமுடி நிரையனப் பரவைப் பாற்கடல்","பெருமுடி யமைகெனப் பெய்யப் பட்டதே."],"p":"","r":"The king gives up his hard-won crown, and the gods take the gem-clustered crown away and cast it into the milk sea as if it were a great crown of their own sea.","n":"The throwing of the king's crown into the sea of the gods turns a loss of rank into an offering; the verse itself does not say who sees this.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thuravu","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 2098","lb":"SO2098","mm":1,"ev":"Payapati renounces the crown; the gods carry it off and cast it into the milk sea.","cl":[],"ag":["பயாபதி","தேவர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:91"},"so:2099":{"book":"soolamani","num":2097,"t":["முரைசதிர் முழங்கொலி மூரித் தானையும்","திரைசெறி வளாகமுஞ் சிறுவர்க் கீந்துபோய்","அரைசரு மாயிர ரரைசர் கோனொடு","விரைசெறி மணிமுடி விலங்க நீக்கினார்."],"p":"","r":"The king gives the drum-loud army and the sea-ringed earth to his sons and goes, and a thousand kings with their king-of-kings take their fragrant crowns off and put them aside.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thuravu","arasu","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 2099","lb":"SO2099","mm":1,"ev":"Payapati gives the kingdom to his sons and a thousand kings follow him in taking off their crowns.","cl":[["a king's rule is to be laid aside and the realm passed on when he turns to renunciation","general","asserted",2,"narrator"]],"ag":["பயாபதி","ஆயிரம் அரசர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:91"},"so:2100":{"book":"soolamani","num":2098,"t":["முடிகளுங் கடகமு முத்தி னாரமும்","சுடர்விடு குழைகளுந் துளும்பு பூண்களும்","விடுசுடர்க் கலங்களும் விட்டெ றிந்தவை","படுசுடர் தாமெனப் பரந்தி மைத்தவே."],"p":"","r":"The crowns, bracelets, pearl necklaces, earrings and ornaments thrown off shine like blazing fires where they lie.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","yaakkai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 2100","lb":"SO2100","mm":1,"ev":"The kings throw away their crowns and jewels.","cl":[],"ag":["ஆயிரம் அரசர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:91"},"so:2101":{"book":"soolamani","num":2099,"t":["வரிவளை வண்ணனு மறங்கொ ணேமியத்","திருவளர் மார்பனுஞ் செல்வன் சென்னிமேல்","எரிவளர் மணிமுடி யிழியு மாயிடைப்","புரிவளைக் கடலெனப் புலம்பு கொண்டனர்."],"p":"","r":"As the blazing gem crown comes down from the lord's head, the two sons, conch-coloured Vijayan and discus-chested Tivittan, cry out like the sea of coiled conches.","n":"The two are identified only by epithets (the conch-coloured one and the discus-bearer with Fortune on his chest); the names are the reader's inference from the story.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","makkal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 2101","lb":"SO2101","mm":1,"ev":"Vijayan and Tivittan wail as their father's crown falls.","cl":[],"ag":["விசயன்","செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:91"},"so:2102":{"book":"soolamani","num":2100,"t":["காதல ராயினுங் காதல் கையிகந்","தேதில ராயின மடிகட் கின்றென","ஊதுலை மெழுகினின் றுருகி னாரவர்","போதலர் கண்களும் புனல்ப டைத்தவே."],"p":"","r":"Though they are dear to him, his love has gone beyond reach and the lord has become one with no tie to them, so they melt like wax in a bellows-fire and their flower eyes fill with water.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","pattru"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 2102","lb":"SO2102","mm":1,"ev":"The princes melt in grief at the lord's detachment.","cl":[["love for dear ones is let go entirely by the one who renounces","general","asserted",1,"narrator"]],"ag":["விசயன்","செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:91"},"so:2103":{"book":"soolamani","num":2101,"t":["முடிகெழு மன்னர்முன் னிறைஞ்ச நம்மைத்தம்","கடிகம ழகலத்துக் கொண்ட காதலெம்","அடிகளு மயலவர் போல வாயினார்","கொடிதிது பெரிதனெக் குழைந்து போயினார்."],"p":"","r":"The princes lament that the lord who once drew them to his fragrant chest while kings bowed has become like a stranger, and that this is cruel and great.","n":"The speaker is the two princes together as a pair (the cast has no id for a pair), so the speaker is given as the one named elsewhere, Tivittan, which is only a convenience.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 2103","lb":"SO2103","mm":1,"ev":"The princes complain that their lord has turned away from them.","cl":[],"ag":["விசயன்","செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ec":[],"spk":["செங்கண் ணெடியான்",""],"ch":"so:91"},"so:2104":{"book":"soolamani","num":2102,"t":["தாதுக வகலத்துத் தாமம் வாங்கியும்","மீதுவந் தேறியு மேவல் செய்யுநம்","கோதுக மியாவர்கொண் டாடு வாரெனப்","போதுக முடியினர் புலம்பொ டேகினார்."],"p":"","r":"They ask who now will take the garland from his chest and mount to him, and who will honour them as their own in the way he did, and so they depart in grief with flowered crowns.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","sutram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 2104","lb":"SO2104","mm":1,"ev":"The princes leave in sorrow.","cl":[],"ag":["விசயன்","செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ec":[],"spk":["விசயன்",""],"ch":"so:91"},"so:2105":{"book":"soolamani","num":2103,"t":["நின்றிலா நிலைமையி னீங்கி நின்றதோர்","வென்றியா லுலகுடன் வணக்கும் வீரியம்","இன்றுகோன் புரிந்ததற் கிரங்கல் வேண்டுமோ","என்றுதா னிளையரை முனிவர் தேற்றினார்."],"p":"","r":"The sage tells the young princes that the might which, by unmoving valour, bends the world was given up today by the lord, so there is nothing for them to grieve.","n":"The line 1 phrase (நின்றிலா நிலைமையில் நீங்கி) is hard; the reading keeps only that the consolation turns on the king's act being a higher victory.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thuravu","vendhan","thavam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 2105","lb":"SO2105","mm":1,"ev":"The sage consoles the young princes.","cl":[["the valour that conquers the world is a lesser thing than the king's renunciation","general","asserted",1,"munivar"]],"ag":["முனிவர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிவர்","இளையவர்"],"ch":"so:91"},"so:2106":{"book":"soolamani","num":2104,"t":["அணங்குசா லடிகள தருள தாய்விடில்","பிணங்கிநாம் பிதற்றிய பேதை வாய்மொழி","குணங்கடா மல்லகோன் குறிப்பு மன்றென","வணங்கினார் மணிமுடி மான வீரரே."],"p":"","r":"The proud princes bow to the sages and say that if this is the grace of the lord, then their quarrelsome prattle was foolish, neither his quality nor his intent.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["panivu","manam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 2106","lb":"SO2106","mm":1,"ev":"The princes accept the king's act and bow.","cl":[],"ag":["விசயன்","செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ec":[],"spk":["விசயன்","முனிவர்"],"ch":"so:91"},"so:2107":{"book":"soolamani","num":2105,"t":["திருவுடை யடிகடஞ் சிந்தைக் கேதமாம்","பரிவொடு பன்னிநாம் பயிற்றி லென்றுதம்","எரிவிடு சுடர்முடி யிலங்கத் தாழ்ந்துபோய்","மருவுடை வளநகர் மன்னர் துன்னினார்."],"p":"","r":"The princes resolve not to grieve the lord's mind further by their talk, bow low with blazing crowns and go back to the rich city.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["panivu","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 2107","lb":"SO2107","mm":1,"ev":"The princes return to the capital.","cl":[],"ag":["விசயன்","செங்கண் ணெடியான்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:91"},"so:2108":{"book":"soolamani","num":2106,"t":["பாற்படு செல்வமும் பரவை ஞாலமும்","காற்பொடி யாகவுங் கருதிற் றின்மையால்","ஏற்புடைத் தன்றுநம் மடிமை யீண்டென","வேற்படை வீரனைத் தொழுது மீண்டதே."],"p":"","r":"The warrior returns to the king and says it is not fitting to serve here, since the lord had held the broad wealth and the sea-ringed earth as dust underfoot, and bows to the spear-bearer.","n":"The returning figure and the 'spear-bearer' (வேற்படை வீரன்) are not named in the verse.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thuravu","selvam","adimai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 2108","lb":"SO2108","mm":1,"ev":"A warrior worships the spear-bearing lord and goes back.","cl":[["wealth and the whole earth are to be counted as dust by the one who renounces","general","asserted",2,"vijayan"]],"ag":["விசயன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["விசயன்",""],"ch":"so:91"},"so:2109":{"book":"soolamani","num":2107,"t":["வேற்படை விடுத்து வீரத் தவவர சதனை மேவி","நூற்படை முனிவர் கண்ணா னோக்கிய நயத்த னாகிப்","பாற்படு விரத நோன்மைப் படைப்பெருந் தலைவ ரைவர்","மேற்படை செய்யச் செல்லும் வினைவரை விலக்க வைத்தான்."],"p":"","r":"The lord lays down the spear and enters the ascetic's kingdom of heroic austerity, looks with the eye of the sages of the scripture, and stations five great captains of the vow-army to hold back the karma that advances.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thuravu","thavam","vinai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 2109","lb":"SO2109","mm":1,"ev":"Payapati lays down his spear and sets five vows as guards against incoming karma.","cl":[["the five great vows ward off the karma that is coming in","jain","asserted",3,"narrator"]],"ag":["பயாபதி"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:91"},"so:2110":{"book":"soolamani","num":2108,"t":["குணப்படை யிலக்க மெண்பான் குலவுநான் காகுஞ் சீலக்","கணப்படை பதினெட் டாகு மாயிரங் கருவி யாகத்","துணைப்படை பிறர்க்குச் செய்யுந் துருநயத் தளவு நீக்கி","மணப்புடை சிந்தை யென்னு மடந்தையைச் செறிய வைத்தான்."],"p":"","r":"The lord arrays the army of virtues, the army of disciplines in eighteen thousand and the aids as instruments, and sets the maiden called mind firmly in their keeping.","n":"Line 3 (துணைப்படை பிறர்க்குச் செய்யும் துருநயத் தளவு நீக்கி) is obscure; the counts of the virtue-army are read as the poet's figures, not checked.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thuravu","manam","thavam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 2110","lb":"SO2110","mm":1,"ev":"Payapati arms himself with virtues and disciplines and guards the mind.","cl":[["the mind is to be held firm by the army of virtues and disciplines","jain","asserted",4,"narrator"]],"ag":["பயாபதி"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:91"},"so:2111":{"book":"soolamani","num":2109,"t":["செறிவெனப் படுவ மூன்று செழுமதில் செறியச் செய்து","பொறியெனும் வாயி லைந்து பொற்கத வடைத்து மாற்றி","அறிவமை சிந்தை யின்மாட் டகம்படி யுழைய ராக்கிக்","கறையிலீ ரறுவர் நிற்ப விறைவராக் காக்க வைத்தான்."],"p":"","r":"The lord is shown raising a fortress of the self: three rich walls, five sense-gates shut with golden doors, a knowing mind set inside, and twelve faultless ones stationed as guards.","n":"The verse never says what the three walls or the twelve guards stand for; the allegory is read from the five gates (பொறி, the senses) alone, so the sense of the numbers is left open.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["adakkam","vinai","pagai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 2111","lb":"SO2111","mm":1,"ev":"The hero builds a walled fortress with its five gates shut and posts twelve faultless guards.","cl":[["shutting the five sense-gates and guarding the mind is the first defence in the fight against karma","jain","asserted",2,"narrator"]],"ag":["கோன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:92"},"so:2112":{"book":"soolamani","num":2110,"t":["படைகெழு புரிசை வெல்வார் புறநின்று பதின்மர் காக்க","விடையவர் தம்மு ளாரே யுழையரீ ரறுவ ராக","உடையதன் னுலக மூன்று மொருவழிப் படுக்க லுற்று","மிடைகெழு வினைவர் தானை மெலியமேற் சென்று விட்டான்."],"p":"","r":"Ten guards stand outside the walls and twelve serve inside, and the lord, wishing to bring his three worlds under one road, sends his force out against the thinning host of the karmic enemy kings.","n":"The numbers ten and twelve are given without their referents.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["padai","pagai","vinai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 2112","lb":"SO2112","mm":1,"ev":"The hero stations his guards and marches out against the army of the karmic enemies.","cl":[["liberation is won by a campaign against the army of karma","jain","asserted",4,"narrator"]],"ag":["கோன்","வினைவர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:92"},"so:2113":{"book":"soolamani","num":2111,"t":["பின்னணி யோகு நான்மை யபரகாத் திரம்பெற் றேனைத்","தன்னவ யவங்கண் முற்றித் தயங்குநூன் மனங்க ளோவா","துன்னிய திசையி னுய்க்கு முணர்வெனும் வயிரத் தோட்டி","இன்னியன் ஞான வேழத் தெழிலெருத் தேறி னானே."],"p":"","r":"Having gained four kinds of discipline, the lord mounts the splendid elephant of knowledge, driven by a diamond goad that is awareness itself and kept on the path of the sacred texts and the mind.","n":"The four named in line 1 (பின்னணி, யோகு, நான்மை, அபரகாத்திரம்) are partly unclear words, and the clause about the texts and the mind in line 2 may be read two ways, so only the picture of knowledge as an elephant under a goad is kept.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["gnaanam","thiyaanam","arivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 2113","lb":"SO2113","mm":1,"ev":"The hero climbs the elephant of knowledge, steering it with the goad of awareness.","cl":[["awareness is the goad that drives knowledge along the right path toward the enemy","jain","asserted",3,"narrator"]],"ag":["கோன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:92"},"so:2114":{"book":"soolamani","num":2112,"t":["தருக்கெயில் காப்பு வாங்கத் தடக்கைமால் பகடு நுந்தித்","திருக்கிளர் குணமேற் சேடிச் செழுமலைக் குவட்டி னோட்டி","முருக்கிய வுருவு வேட்கை முனைப்புல மகற்றி முற்றிச்","செருக்கிய வினைவர் வாழுந் திண்குறும் பழிக்க லுற்றான்."],"p":"","r":"Driving the great elephant to break the guard of the proud fort, he rides it up a ridge of good qualities, crushes the shape of desire, drives off the outposts of the senses and attacks the stronghold where the karmic enemies live.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["padai","kaamam","pagai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 2114","lb":"SO2114","mm":1,"ev":"The hero storms the fort of the karmic enemies on his elephant, crushing desire and the outposts of the senses.","cl":[["desire and the senses are outposts of the karmic enemy that must be cleared before its stronghold falls","jain","asserted",3,"narrator"]],"ag":["கோன்","வினைவர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:92"},"so:2115":{"book":"soolamani","num":2113,"t":["நிறையிலார் பொறுத்த லாற்றா நிலையிது நிறைந்த நோன்மைக்","கறையி லீராறுக் கொத்த கண்ணியர் கவரி வீச","முறையினாற் பெருகு முள்ளச் சமாதிநீர் முறுக வுண்ட","குறைவிலாத் தியான மென்னுங் கொற்றவா ளுருவிக் கொண்டான்."],"p":"","r":"Unable to bear the enemy's unrestraint, he draws the sword of unending meditation, steeped in the water of samadhi, while women suited to the twelve faultless ones fan him with whisks.","n":"The fanning women are the twelve faultless ones in female form as far as the grammar of line 2 shows, but the verse does not say it outright.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thiyaanam","samaadhi","vinai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 2115","lb":"SO2115","mm":1,"ev":"The hero draws the sword of meditation while attendants fan him.","cl":[["unbroken meditation soaked in samadhi is the sword that cuts karma","jain","asserted",3,"narrator"]],"ag":["கோன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:92"},"so:2116":{"book":"soolamani","num":2114,"t":["விண்கடாஞ் செய்யும் வெய்ய வினைவர்கட் கரண மாகிக்","கண்கடா மிறைக்கு மோரேழ் கடிவினை பொடிசெய் திட்டே","கொண்கடா நவின்ற வீரெண் கொடிமதிற் கோட்டை குட்டி","எண்கடா முடைய வெண்மர் குறும்பரை யெறிந்து வீழ்த்தார்."],"p":"","r":"Serving as the means against the fierce enemies, the lord crushes a group of fierce karmas, razes the sixteen walled forts he has already named, and eight strike down the chieftains.","n":"Lines 2 and 3 count karmas and forts (seven, sixteen) and line 4 an eight; the verse itself gives no key, and the words கொண்கடா and எண்கடா are hard.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vinai","padai","pagai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 2116","lb":"SO2116","mm":1,"ev":"The hero and eight of his men break the enemy's forts and cut down its chieftains.","cl":[["the fierce karmas fall to meditation's attack in a fixed order of forts","jain","asserted",2,"narrator"]],"ag":["கோன்","வினைவர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:92"},"so:2117":{"book":"soolamani","num":2115,"t":["ஈடிலர் வெகுளி யுள்ளிட் டெண்மரை யெறியத் தீயுட்","பேடுவந் தொன்று பாய்ந்து முடிந்தது முடிந்த பின்னை","ஓடிவந் தொருத்தி வீழ்ந்தா ளுழையவ ரறுவர் பட்டார்","ஆடவன் றானும் போழ்து கழித்துவந் தொருவ னாழ்ந்தான்."],"p":"","r":"With anger and its fellows (eight in all) struck down, a neuter figure leaps into the fire and dies, then a woman falls, six attendants die, and last the man himself sinks after a while.","n":"The neuter, woman and man with six attendants may be the nine minor passions of the Jain list, but the verse names them only as figures; I follow the picture and not that list.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vekuli","kaamam","pagai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 2117","lb":"SO2117","mm":1,"ev":"A run of enemy figures led by anger dies in sequence: the neuter, the woman, six attendants and the man.","cl":[["anger and the passions linked to it are killed one after another in the battle against karma","jain","asserted",1,"narrator"]],"ag":["கோன்","வினைவர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:92"},"so:2118":{"book":"soolamani","num":2116,"t":["பின்னுமோர் நால்வர் தெவ்வர் முறைமுறை பிணங்கி வீழ்ந்தார்","அன்னவர் தம்மு ளானே குறைப்பிண மொருவ னாகித்","தன்னைமெய் பதைப்ப நோக்கி யவனையுந் தபுப்ப நோனார்","துன்னிய துயிலு மேனைத் துளக்கஞ்செய் திருவர் பட்டார்."],"p":"","r":"Four more enemies fall in turn, one of them rising as a half-corpse that is cut down again, and two others who shake the sleeping die as well.","n":"The grouping of the four and the two, and the sense of the half-corpse, cannot be fixed from the verse.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pagai","vinai","kootru"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 2118","lb":"SO2118","mm":1,"ev":"Four enemies fall one by one, and two more die with them.","cl":[],"ag":["கோன்","வினைவர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:92"},"so:2119":{"book":"soolamani","num":2117,"t":["ஆங்கவ ரழிந்த பின்னை யரசரை யிருவரோடும்","தாங்கியீ ரிருவர் தாக்கித் தலைதுணிப் புண்ட பின்னை","வீங்கிய வனந்த ஞான்மை விழுநிதி முழுதுங் கைக்கொண்","டோங்கிய வுலகிற் கெல்லா மொருபெருங் கிழவ னானான்."],"p":"","r":"After those fall, two kings and four more are struck and beheaded, and the lord takes the whole treasure of infinite knowledge and becomes the single master of all the raised-up worlds.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["gnaanam","vinai","marumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 2119","lb":"SO2119","mm":1,"ev":"The hero kills the last enemy kings, takes the treasure of infinite knowledge and rules all the worlds.","cl":[["when the enemy kings of karma fall, infinite knowledge is gained and the soul becomes lord of all the worlds","jain","asserted",3,"narrator"]],"ag":["கோன்","வினைவர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:92"},"so:2120":{"book":"soolamani","num":2118,"t":["நெடிதுட னாய தெவ்வர் நால்வரை நீறு செய்திட்","டடிகள்பின் முடிவென் பாளை யகப்படுத் தனைய ராக","இடிமுர சதிருந் தானை யரசரோ டிங்க ணீண்டிக்","கடிகம ழமரர் வீரன் கடிவினை முடிவித் தாரே."],"p":"","r":"Having reduced four old enemies to ash, he ends the fierce karma; the verse then speaks of the woman called முடிவு, whom he reaches, while gods and kings gather in a host.","n":"முடிவு (end, completion) is spoken of as a woman in lines 2 and in the verses that follow, so the end of karma is both a deed and a person to be won; the grammar of lines 2 and 3 is hard and the clause is read loosely.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vinai","veedu","pagai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 2120","lb":"SO2120","mm":1,"ev":"The hero reduces four long-standing enemies to ash and ends the karma that bound him.","cl":[["four deeply rooted karmas are burned to ash before the soul reaches its end","jain","asserted",1,"narrator"]],"ag":["கோன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:92"},"so:2121":{"book":"soolamani","num":2119,"t":["கொடிகளுங் குடையுங் கோலக் கவரியு மமரர் தங்கள்","முடிகளு மடந்தை மாரு முகிழ் நகைக் கலங்க ளுஞ்செற்","றடியிடு மிடமின் றாகி மூடியா காய மெல்லாம்","கடிகமழ் மலருஞ் சாந்துஞ் சுண்ணமுங் கலந்த வன்றே."],"p":"","r":"Flags, umbrellas, whisks, the crowns of the gods, their women and their jewels press so close that there is no place left for a foot, and the whole sky is mixed with flowers, sandal and powder.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","iraivan","marumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 2121","lb":"SO2121","mm":1,"ev":"The gods crowd the sky with their regalia and rain flowers, sandal and powder.","cl":[],"ag":["அமரர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:92"},"so:2122":{"book":"soolamani","num":2120,"t":["பொன்னரி மாலை பூவின் பொழிமதுப் பிணையன் முத்தின்","மின்னிவர் விளங்குந் தாம மெனவிவை விரவி வீசித்","துன்னிய வினைவர் கூட்டந் துணித்துவீற் றிருந்த கோனைப்","பன்னிய துதிய ராகி யமரர்கள் பரவு கின்றார்."],"p":"","r":"The gods scatter gold-threaded garlands, flower-rain honey, pearl strings and jewelled wreaths and praise the lord who cut the crowd of enemies and sits in glory.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","iraivan","vinai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 2122","lb":"SO2122","mm":1,"ev":"The gods shower garlands and praise the lord who cut the enemy host.","cl":[["the one who cut the throng of karma is worthy of the gods' praise","jain","asserted",4,"narrator"]],"ag":["அமரர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:92"},"so:2123":{"book":"soolamani","num":2121,"t":["கருமால் வினையரசு காறளர நூறிப்து","பெருமான் முடிவென்னும் பெண்ணரசி தன்னை","ஒருவாமை வேட்டெய்தி யூழி பெயர்ந்தாலும்","வருமா றிலாத வளநகரம் புக்கானே."],"p":"","r":"Having wiped out the king of dark karma and wanting the princess called the Lord's end without parting, he enters a rich city from which none returns even after the ages turn.","n":"Line 1 is corrupt (கருமால் வினையரசு காறளர நூறிப்து) and the third line's வேட்டு எய்தி may mean wedding the woman called முடிவு or merely winning her; the sense of the destroyed king is kept, the details are not.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["veedu","vinai","marumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 2123","lb":"SO2123","mm":1,"ev":"The hero destroys the king of karma and enters the city of no return.","cl":[["liberation is an entry into a city from which there is no return","jain","asserted",4,"narrator"]],"ag":["கோன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:92"},"so:2124":{"book":"soolamani","num":2122,"t":["சிந்தை மடவா டொடுத்த தியானவாள்","வெந்து வினைவேந்தர் வீடியபின் விட்டெறிந்து","முந்து முடிவென்னுங் கன்னி முலைமுயங்கி","வந்து பெயரா வளநகரம் புக்கானே."],"p":"","r":"After the mind-maid's sword of meditation has burned the karmic kings, he throws it away, embraces the girl called முடிவு and enters the rich city to which one comes back no more.","n":"The sword is the same weapon as in 2115; here it is discarded, which means that even meditation is a means and not the goal. Liberation is pictured as marriage to a maiden named முடிவு, so release is described in the language of the bridegroom.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thiyaanam","veedu","vinai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 2124","lb":"SO2124","mm":1,"ev":"The hero casts away the sword of meditation, embraces the girl called end and enters the city of no return.","cl":[["meditation is a tool that is thrown away once the karma is burned","jain","asserted",2,"narrator"],["liberation is a city from which none returns","jain","asserted",4,"narrator"]],"ag":["கோன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:92"},"so:2125":{"book":"soolamani","num":2123,"t":["அலகில் பெருங்குணத்தோ னாவரண நீக்கி","உலக மலோக முடனே விழுங்கிப்","புலவன் முடிவென்னும் ங்கொடியுந் தானும்","நிலவு சிவகதியு ணீங்காது நின்றான்."],"p":"","r":"Casting off the coverings, the boundless one swallows the world and the non-world together and stands unmoving, with the maiden called end, in the shining state of release.","n":"சிவகதி here names the liberated state in a Jain poem and is not a claim about the god Siva; line 3 has a stray ங் (முடிவென்னும் ங்கொடியுந்) that is corrupt.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["veedu","gnaanam","vinai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 2125","lb":"SO2125","mm":1,"ev":"The hero removes the coverings of karma, takes in all knowable things and stays in the state of release.","cl":[["with the coverings removed the soul knows world and non-world at once and remains in release","jain","asserted",1,"narrator"]],"ag":["கோன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:92"},"so:2126":{"book":"soolamani","num":2124,"t":["இனையன பலபரவி யிறைஞ்சி யேத்தி யிமையவர்கள்","கனையெரி மிகுவேள்வி கலந்து செய்து களிப்பெய்தி","அனையவ ரறவாழி யமிழ்த நீங்கா தகத்தாடிப்","புனையவிர் சுடரொளியார் புகழ்ந்து தத்த மிடம்புக்கார்."],"p":"","r":"After many praises and bows, the gods make a fire-offering, bathe in the nectar of the wheel of virtue, shine with joy and, praising, each go to his own place.","n":"The gods' fire-offering (வேள்வி, line 2) sits inside a Jain scene of worship; the verse does not say it is the Vedic sacrifice.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","aram","marumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 2126","lb":"SO2126","mm":1,"ev":"The gods worship with a fire-offering, bathe in the nectar of the dharma wheel and return to their places.","cl":[],"ag":["அமரர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:92"},"so:2127":{"book":"soolamani","num":2125,"t":["களங்காண் வகையுடைந்து காலர் காமர் கையகல","விளங்காத் திசையின்றி விளங்க வீரன் மெய்ப்பொருளை","உளங்காண் கேவலப்பே ரொளியா லிம்ப ருலகெல்லாம்","துளங்கா துயர்ந்துலகின் முடிக்கோர் சூளா மணியானான்."],"p":"","r":"After the field is lost and Death and Desire leave his side, he sees the truth by the pure light of full knowledge and, undimmed in heaven's worlds, becomes the crest-jewel of the world.","n":"சூளாமணி, the work's own title, is used here of the liberated one, so the poem names itself from this verse.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["gnaanam","kootru","kaamam","veedu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 2127","lb":"SO2127","mm":1,"ev":"Death and Desire flee, and the hero attains full knowledge and becomes the crest-jewel of the world.","cl":[["full knowledge (kevala) sees the true thing and conquers Death and Desire","jain","asserted",3,"narrator"]],"ag":["கோன்","காலர் காமர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:92"},"so:2128":{"book":"soolamani","num":2126,"t":["அருமால் வினையகல அமரர் நாளு மடிபரவப்","பெருமான் பிரசாபதி பிரம லோக மினிதாளத்","திருமால் பெரு நேமி திகழ்ந்த செந்தா மரைத்தடக்கைக்","கருமால் கடல்வரைத்த கண்ணார் ஞாலங் காக்கின்றான்."],"p":"","r":"With karma gone, the gods praise his feet each day; Prajapati rules the Brahma world sweetly, and Tirumal of the discus guards the sea-bound earth.","n":"Prajapati and Tirumal are the princes of the epic's story; here they are shown ruling after the story's close, and the verse does not say whether Prajapati is the one who is liberated.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","sengol","vinai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 2128","lb":"SO2128","mm":1,"ev":"The gods worship the liberated lord's feet while Prajapati rules Brahma's world and Tirumal rules the earth.","cl":[],"ag":["அமரர்","பிரசாபதி","திருமால்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:92"},"so:2129":{"book":"soolamani","num":2127,"t":["தங்கோ னமருலக மினிதி னாளத் தரங்கநீர்ப்","பொங்கோதம் புடையுடுத்த பூமியெல்லாம் பொது நீக்கிச்","செங்கோ லினிதோச்சித் தேவர் காப்பத் திருமாலும்","அங்கோல வேலரச ரடிபா ராட்ட வாள்கின்றான்."],"p":"","r":"While the lord who is theirs sweetly rules the gods' world, Tirumal clears the earth of common rule, lifts a straight sceptre under the gods' guard, and lives while kings praise his feet.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sengol","arasu","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 2129","lb":"SO2129","mm":1,"ev":"Tirumal rules the earth with a straight sceptre, protected by the gods and praised by kings.","cl":[["a king guards the earth by a straight sceptre, and the gods protect him","general","asserted",3,"narrator"]],"ag":["திருமால்"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:92"},"so:2130":{"book":"soolamani","num":2128,"t":["வலம்புரி வண்ணனு மகர முந்நீர் மணிமேனி","உலம்புரி தோளினனு முலக மெல்லா முடன்வணங்கச்","சலம்புரி வினைவென்ற தங்கோன் செந்தா மரையடிக்கீழ்","நலம்புரி விழவியற்றி நாளு நாளு மகிழ்கின்றார்."],"p":"","r":"The one white as a conch and the one blue as a jewel of the sea, with arms like pillars, bow with the whole world to the lord who won the deceiving karma and keep a daily festival under his lotus feet.","n":"Reading the white one and the dark one as the two brothers of the story is from the colours alone; the verse does not give their names.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","iraivan","vinai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Soolamani 2130","lb":"SO2130","mm":1,"ev":"The two brothers and the whole world worship the lord who won karma, keeping a festival at his feet each day.","cl":[["the one who has conquered karma is the object of the world's worship","jain","asserted",3,"narrator"]],"ag":["வலம்புரி வண்ணன்","மணிமேனி"],"ec":[],"ch":"so:92"},"ud:1":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":1,"t":["மணியுடன் கனக முத்த மலிந்தமுக் குடையி லங்க","அணிமலர்ப் பிண்டி யின்கீ ழமர்ந்தநே மீசர் பாதம்","பணிபுபின் வாணி பாதம் பண்ணவர் தாள்க ளுக்கெம்","இணைகரஞ் சிரத்திற் கூப்பி யியல்புறத் தொழுது மன்றே."],"p":"","r":"The poet joins his hands over his head and worships the feet of Lord Nemi, seated under the pindi tree beneath the three jewelled umbrellas, and the feet of Vani and the gods.","n":"The Jain invocation names a tirthankara, Nemi, and not a creator; the worship asks for no boon here.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","thiruvadi","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 1","lb":"UD1","mm":1,"ev":"The poet raises his joined hands and worships the lord's feet.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:1"},"ud:2":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":2,"t":["பொன்னெயில் நடுவ ணேங்கும் பூநிறை யசோக நீழல்","இன்னிய லாலயத்து ளேந்தரி யாச னத்தின்","மன்னிய வாமன் பாதம் வந்தனை செய்து வாழ்த்தி","உன்னத மகிமை மிக்கா னுதயணன் கதைவிரிப்பாம்."],"p":"","r":"The poet worships the feet of the lord enthroned on the lion seat in the shrine beneath the asoka tree within a golden rampart, and says he will spread out the tale of the great Udayanan.","n":"The lord here, named only வாமன், is probably the same tirthankara as in verse 1, but the verse does not say so.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","vazhipaadu","pugazh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 2","lb":"UD2","mm":1,"ev":"The poet bows to the lord and declares he will tell Udayanan's story.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:1"},"ud:3":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":3,"t":["மணிபொதி கிழியு மிக்க மணியுட னிருந்த போழ்தில்","மணிபொதி கிழிய தன்னை மணியுட னன்கு வைப்பார்","துணிவினிற் புன்சொ லேனுந் தூயநற் பொருள்பொ திந்தால்","அணியெனக் கொள்வார் நாமு மகத்தினி லிரங்கல் செல்லாம்."],"p":"","r":"The poet says that as people keep a gem-filled cloth bundle safe with the gem, and take even a humble word as an ornament when it holds pure meaning, so he asks that his own flawed words be received with pity.","n":"The apology rests on the worth of meaning over the poverty of words; the cloth that carries the gem is the poem's plain language.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sol","kutram","payan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 3","lb":"UD3","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["a humble word that carries pure meaning is to be valued as an ornament","general","asserted",3,"narrator"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:1"},"ud:4":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":4,"t":["ஊறுந்தீ வினைவாய் தன்னை யுற்றுடன் செறியப் பண்ணும்","கூறுநல் விதிபு ணர்ந்து குறையின்றிச் செல்வ மாமுன்","மாறுறு கருமந் தன்னை வரிசையி னுதிர்ப்பை யாக்கும்","வீறுறு முதிர்ப்பின் றன்மை விளம்புதற் பால தாமோ."],"p":"","r":"The poet speaks of the deed that comes on like a rising fire and binds the self, and of the fine law by which, before wealth is reached, the opposing deed is shed in order, and asks whether the nature of its ripening can be told.","n":"The sense is held loosely: the ripening and the orderly shedding of deeds is the Jain account of karma, but the last two lines are hard to parse.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vinai","oozh","thuravu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 4","lb":"UD4","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["deeds that ripen bind the self, and they can be shed in due order","jain","asserted",3,"narrator"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:1"},"ud:5":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":5,"t":["இஞ்சிமூன் றுடைய கோமா னெழில்வீர நாத னிந்தப்","புஞ்சிய நிலத்தோர்க் கெல்லாம் பொற்புநல் லறநன் மாரி","விஞ்சவே சொரியுங் காலம் வெண்மதிக் குடைக்கீழ் வாழும்","எஞ்சலில் காட்சி மன்ன னிருக்கைநா டுரைத்து மன்றே."],"p":"","r":"The poet turns to the age when the lord of the three ramparts, fair Veeranathan, pours a rain of virtue on this crowded earth, and says he will tell of the land of a king who rules in that time.","n":"Virtue falls as rain from the tirthankara here, where the Kural makes the rain follow the king's justice; the poem sets its king inside the lord's age.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","aram","mazhai","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 5","lb":"UD5","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:2"},"ud:6":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":6,"t":["பூவுநற் றளிருஞ் செற்றிப் பொழின்மிகச் சூழ்ந்தி லங்கும்","நாவலா மரத்தி னாலே நாமமாய்த் துலங்கி நின்று","தீவுநற் கடல்க டாம மொன்றிற்கொன் றிரட்டி சூழ்ந்த","நாவலந் தீவு நந்தி னன்மணி போன்ற தன்றே."],"p":"","r":"The poet says the jambu-named continent, ringed by seas each twice as wide as the one inside it, is like a fine gem among the islands.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naadu","perumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 6","lb":"UD6","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:2"},"ud:7":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":7,"t":["வேதிகை சிலைவ ளைத்து வேதண்ட நாணே றிட்டுப்","போதவும் வீக்கி னாற்போற் பொற்புடைப் பரதந் தன்னில்","ஓதிய தரும கண்டத் தோங்கிய காவு நின்று","வாதத்தாற் சுகந்தம் வீசும் வத்தவ னாட தாமே."],"p":"","r":"Within the Bharata part of that continent, in its land of dharma, the poet places the Vatsa country, where the wind carries the scent of its groves.","n":"The first two lines compare the land to a bow drawn with a string; the figure is hard to follow, and only the placing of Vatsa in Bharata's dharma-land is firm.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["naadu","aram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 7","lb":"UD7","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:2"},"ud:8":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":8,"t":["இஞ்சிமிக் கெழுந்தே யோங்கி யிலங்கிய வமர லோகம்","எஞ்சலி லெல்லை காணா வெழில்பெற நிற்ற னோக்கி","அஞ்சலில் வருக வென்றே யணிபெற விலங்கி நீண்ட","குஞ்சிநன் கொடிக்க ரத்தாற் கூவியிட் டழைக்கு மன்றே."],"p":"","r":"The poet says the heavenly world, seeing the limitless beauty of the city, calls it with a hand of tall flags to come without fear.","n":"Who calls whom is not clear in the Tamil: the heaven may call the city up, or the city's flags may call the gods down. Either way the city is praised as the match of heaven.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["naadu","pugazh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 8","lb":"UD8","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:2"},"ud:9":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":9,"t":["முகிறவழ் மாட மீதின் முத்தணி மாலை நான்றே","இகலுறு மமளி யின்மே லெழின்மங்கை மைந்தர் தாமும்","பகலிர வின்றிப் போகம் பண்பினாற் றுய்த்தி ருப்பார்","நகரிகௌ சாம்பி யென்னு நாமமார்ந் திலங்கு மன்றே."],"p":"","r":"The poet names the city Kausambi, where men and women in cloud-high mansions live in pleasure by day and night.","n":"The city is praised by its pleasure, not its virtue, and the poem will later make renunciation the end of such a life.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inbam","kaamam","naadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 9","lb":"UD9","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:2"},"ud:10":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":10,"t":["ஊனுமிழ்ந் திலங்கும் வேலா னுன்னத முகிலெ ழுந்து","வானுமிழ் வாரி யன்ன வண்கையன் வண்ட ரற்றும்","தேனுமி ழலங்கற் றோளான் செல்வத்திற் குபேர னன்னான்","தானுமிழ் கிரண மார்பன் சதானிகனரச னாமே."],"p":"","r":"The poet introduces King Sathanikan, spear-bearing, as generous as a rain-cloud and as rich as Kubera.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","eegai","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 10","lb":"UD10","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:2"},"ud:11":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":11,"t":["மன்னவ னுள்ளத் துள்ளாண் மாமணி மயிலஞ் சாயல்","அன்னமென் னடைவேற் கண்ணா ளருந்ததி யனைய ஙங்கை","பொன்னணி சுணங்கு பூத்த புணர்முலை யமிர்த மன்னாள்","மின்னு நுண் ணிடையா ணாம மிகாவதி யென்று மிக்காள்."],"p":"","r":"The poet names the king's beloved queen, delicate as a peahen and as faithful as Arundhati, Mirukavathi.","n":"Her fidelity is praised by the comparison to Arundhati, the star-wife, before any event shows it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["karpu","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 11","lb":"UD11","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:2"},"ud:12":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":12,"t":["கற்புடைத் திருவி னங்கை காரிகை தன்வ யிற்றிற்","சற்புரு டொருவன் வந்து சார்ந்தவ தரித்து மிக்க","நற்புடைத் திங்க ளொன்பா னன்கமைந் திருக்கு மோர்நாட்","பொற்புடை மஞ்ச மீதிற் பொலிவுட னிருந்த போழ்தில்."],"p":"","r":"The chaste queen, in the ninth month after a good soul had entered her womb, sits one day on a fine couch.","n":"A virtuous being 'taking birth' in her womb is told as a plain fact of rebirth; no earlier life is named for him here.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["karpu","karu","piravi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 12","lb":"UD12","mm":1,"ev":"Mirukavathi, in the ninth month of pregnancy, sits on her couch.","cl":[],"ag":["மிகாவதி"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:2"},"ud:13":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":13,"t":["செந்துகின் மூடிக் கொண்டு திருநிலா முற்றந் தன்னில்","அந்தமாய்த் துயில்கொள் கின்ற வாயிழை தன்னைக் கண்டே","அந்தரத் தோடு கின்ற வண்டபே ரண்டப் புள்ளொன்","றந்தசை யென்று பற்றி யன்றுவான் போயிற் றன்றே."],"p":"","r":"As the queen sleeps in a red cloth on the moonlit terrace, a huge bird seizes her, taking her for flesh, and flies up.","n":"The bird's mistake is the whole cause; no fault of hers or the king's is named.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 13","lb":"UD13","mm":1,"ev":"A huge bird seizes the sleeping Mirukavathi and carries her into the sky.","cl":[],"ag":["புள்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:2"},"ud:14":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":14,"t":["மற்றவ டந்தை தானு மாமுனி யாகி நிற்கும்","சற்கிரி விபுல மன்னுஞ் சாரலவ் வனத்திற் சென்று","நற்றவ னருகில் வைப்ப நற்றுயில் விட்டெ ழுந்தாள்","பற்றுயி ருண்ணாப் புள்ளும் பறந்துவான் போயிற் றன்றே."],"p":"","r":"The bird sets her down in the forest where her father, now a great sage, lives; she wakes, and the bird, which does not eat the life it seized, flies off.","n":"The same bird that was a menace in the last verse is shown to be harmless; the verse says it did not eat the life it had seized.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thavam","kollaamai","makkal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 14","lb":"UD14","mm":1,"ev":"The bird sets Mirukavathi down by her father the sage and flies away without harming her.","cl":[],"ag":["புள்","மாமுனி"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:2"},"ud:15":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":15,"t":["நிறைமதி முகநன் மங்கை நிரம்பிய கெர்ப்ப மாதல்","பொறைவயி னோய்மீக் கூரப் பொருவில்வான் கோள்க ளெல்லாம்","முறையினல் வழியை நோக்க மொய்ம்பனத் தினத்திற் றோன்ற","அறையலை கடலிற் சங்க மாணிமுத் தீன்ற தொத்தாள்."],"p":"","r":"With the child ripening, the queen suffers pains, and on a day the planets look kindly the son is born, like a pearl from a conch.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["makkal","karu","kaalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 15","lb":"UD15","mm":1,"ev":"Mirukavathi bears a son on an auspicious day.","cl":[],"ag":["மிகாவதி"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:2"},"ud:16":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":16,"t":["பொருகயற் கண்ணி னாடான் போந்ததை யறிந்த ழுங்கித்","திருமணி கிடந்த தென்னச் செழுமகன் கிடப்பக் கண்டு","பெருகிய காத லாலே பெருந்துயர் தீர்ந்தி ருப்ப","மருவுநற் றாதை யான மாமுனி கண்டு வந்தான்."],"p":"","r":"Learning where she has come, the queen grieves, then sees her son lying like a gem and her sorrow ebbs with love; her father the sage comes and sees them.","n":"Line 1 has a damaged word in the queen's description, and its first half is hard to join to the rest; the grief and the sage's arrival are clear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["anbu","makkal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 16","lb":"UD16","mm":1,"ev":"The sage comes and finds his daughter and her newborn son.","cl":[],"ag":["மிகாவதி","மாமுனி"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:2"},"ud:17":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":17,"t":["தவமுனி கொண்டு சென்று தாபதப் பள்ளி சேர்த்தி","அவணினி தோம்ப வப்பா லருக்கன துதய காலத்","துவமையின் றுதித்தா னாம முதயண னாக வென்றார்","இவணமத் தாயுஞ் சேயு மிருடிபா லிருந்தா ரன்றே."],"p":"","r":"The sage takes mother and child to his hermitage and tends them; the boy, born like the sun rising, is named Udayanan, and they stay with the sages.","n":"The name 'rising' fits the verse's comparison of his birth to a sunrise.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["makkal","thavam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 17","lb":"UD17","mm":1,"ev":"The sage brings Mirukavathi and her son to the hermitage, where the boy is named Udayanan.","cl":[],"ag":["மாமுனி"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:2"},"ud:18":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":18,"t":["பிரமசுந் தரயோ கிக்குப் பிறந்தவன் யூகி யோடும்","இருவரும் வளர்ந்தே யின்பக் கலைக்கட னீந்திக் கானக்","கரிணமும் புள்ளு மற்றுங் கண்டடி வீழுங் கீதப்","புரந்தரன் கொடுத்த யாழும் பொறைமுனி யருளிற் பெற்றான்."],"p":"","r":"Udayanan, born to the royal line, and Yugi, son of the yogi Brahmasundaran, grow up together, master the arts, and by the sage's grace Udayanan gains Indra's lute.","n":"The lute comes as a gift of grace from the sage, not of his own skill, and it sets up the elephant scene.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalvi","kalai","guru","arul"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 18","lb":"UD18","mm":1,"ev":"Udayanan and Yugi grow up together and he receives Indra's lute from the sage.","cl":[],"ag":["உதயணன்","யூகி"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:2"},"ud:19":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":19,"t":["மைவரை மருங்கி னின்ற மலையென விலங்கு கின்ற","தெய்வநல் லியானை கண்டு சென்றுதன் வீணை பாடப்","பையெனக் களிறுங் கேட்டுப் பணிந்தடி யிறைஞ்சி நின்று","கையது கொடுப்ப வேறிக் காளையும் பள்ளி சேர்ந்தான்."],"p":"","r":"Seeing a mountain-like divine elephant, the youth plays his lute; the elephant listens, bows at his feet and offers its trunk, and he mounts it and rides to the hermitage.","n":"Music, not force, tames the elephant, the first sign of the lute's power.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalai","panivu","anbu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 19","lb":"UD19","mm":1,"ev":"Udayanan plays the lute, tames the divine elephant and rides it to the hermitage.","cl":[],"ag":["உதயணன்","களிறு"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:2"},"ud:20":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":20,"t":["நன்றிருட் கனவி னாக நயமறிந் தினிது ரைக்கு","பன்னிடும் பாகன் வந்து பற்றியே யேறி னாலும்","இன்றைநாண் முதலா நீநா னின்றியே முன்னுண் டாலும்","அன்றுன்பா னில்லே னென்றே யக்கரி யுரைப்பக் கேட்டான்."],"p":"","r":"The elephant pledges to him in a dream that, though a keeper mount it, it will not stay with him on the day he eats before it; he hears this.","n":"The condition of the pledge turns on a hard sentence in lines 2 and 3; the sense given is the likeliest, not certain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["anbu","natpu","kanavu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 20","lb":"UD20","mm":1,"ev":"The elephant pledges itself to Udayanan in a dream.","cl":[],"ag":["களிறு","உதயணன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["களிறு","உதயணன்"],"ch":"ud:2"},"ud:21":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":21,"t":["செல்லுமக் காலந் தன்னிற் செறிதவன் புதல்வ னான","வெல்களிற் றியானை வேந்தன் விக்கிரன் றனக்கு மக்கள்","இல்லையென் றெவ்வங் கூர்ந்தே யினிமையின் வந்து நல்ல","சொல்லருண் முனிவன் பாதந் தொழுதுநன் கிருந்தா னன்றே."],"p":"","r":"At that time King Vikraman, son of a sage, grieving that he has no children, comes and sits worshipping the sage's feet.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["makkal","thavam","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 21","lb":"UD21","mm":1,"ev":"Vikraman, childless, comes to worship the sage's feet.","cl":[],"ag":["விக்கிரன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:2"},"ud:22":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":22,"t":["புரவல னினிய ராமிப் புதல்வர்க ளார்கொ லென்ன","வரமுனி யருளக் கேட்டு மகிழ்ந்துதன் னாய மெல்லாம்","சிரசணி முடியுஞ் சூட்டிச் செல்வற்குக் கொடுத்துப் போக்கி","விரவிய தவத்த னாக வேண்டுவ தெண்ண மென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"Asking whose sons these are, he hears the sage's answer, gladly crowns the boy and gives him his retinue, and says he will take up the ascetic life.","n":"Line 1 has a damaged word ('ராமி'), so who is said to be whose son has to be taken from the next lines, where he gives up kingship to the boy.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["makkal","thavam","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 22","lb":"UD22","mm":1,"ev":"Vikraman learns who the boys are, crowns Udayanan and resolves to become an ascetic.","cl":[],"ag":["விக்கிரன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:2"},"ud:23":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":23,"t":["முனியொடு தங்கை தன்னை முயன் றிரந் தெய்தி நாகம்","தனையன வெங்க யத்திற் றனையனை யேற்றிப் போய்த்தன்","மனனிறை நாட்டை யந்த மருகனுக் கீந்து போந்து","முனிவனம் புகுந்து மாமன் முனிவனாய் நின்றா னன்றே."],"p":"","r":"Vikraman gets his sister and the sage's leave, seats the boy on a fierce elephant, hands his own kingdom to his sister's son, and goes into the forest as an ascetic.","n":"He ends his childlessness by making his sister's son the heir, and the same act is his turn to renunciation; the kingdom passes to a sister's son, not a son of his own.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thuravu","thavam","arasu","sutram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 23","lb":"UD23","mm":1,"ev":"Vikraman gives his kingdom to his nephew Udayanan and becomes a forest ascetic.","cl":[],"ag":["விக்கிரன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:2"},"ud:24":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":24,"t":["இளமையை இகந்து மிக்க வினியநற் குமர னாகி","வளமையிற் செங்கோ றன்னை வண்மையி னடத்தி னானாங்","கிளமயி லனைய தேவிக் கிரங்கிய சதானி கன்றான்","உளமலி கொள்கை யான்ற வொருதவற் கண்டு ரைத்தான்."],"p":"","r":"Udayanan, grown into a handsome youth, rules justly with a straight sceptre; Sathanikan, pining for the queen, speaks of one fault he has seen.","n":"Lines 3 and 4 are obscure: what the fault is, and whose, is not stated in the verse.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sengol","eegai","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 24","lb":"UD24","mm":1,"ev":"Udayanan rules justly; Sathanikan, longing for his queen, names a fault.","cl":[],"ag":["உதயணன்","சதானிகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:2"},"ud:25":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":25,"t":["தேவியின் வரவு நல்ல திருமகன் செலவுங் கேட்டு","மாவலன் மனம கிழ்ந்து வந்தூர்புக் கிருக்கு நாளில்","தேவியும் வந்து கூடிச் சிறந்தநற் புதல்வர் தம்மைத்","தேவிளங் குமரர் போலச் செவ்வியிற் பயந்தா ளன்றே."],"p":"","r":"Hearing of the queen's return and the prince's going, the king is glad and enters the city; the queen joins him and bears two sons like young gods.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["makkal","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 25","lb":"UD25","mm":1,"ev":"Sathanikan welcomes Mirukavathi back and she bears two sons.","cl":[],"ag":["சதானிகன்","மிகாவதி"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:2"},"ud:26":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":26,"t":["பிங்கல கடக ரென்று பேரினி திட்டு மன்னன்","தங்கிய காத லாலே தரணியாண் டினிது செல்லக்","குங்கும மணிந்த மார்பக் குமரனும் யூகி யும்போய்","அங்குள தேச மெல்லா மடிப்படுத் தினிதி ருந்தார்."],"p":"","r":"The king names the two sons Pingalan and Katakan and rules the land lovingly, while Udayanan and Yugi go out and bring all the lands there under their sway.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["makkal","arasu","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 26","lb":"UD26","mm":1,"ev":"Sathanikan names his sons; Udayanan and Yugi conquer the surrounding lands.","cl":[],"ag":["சதானிகன்","உதயணன்","யூகி"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:2"},"ud:27":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":27,"t":["உதயண குமரன் றன்னை யுற்றுட னழைத்துப் பூமிப்","பதமுனக் காக வென்று பார்த்திபன் கொடுத்துப் போகிக்","கதமுறு கவலை நீங்கும் காட்சிநற் றவத்த னாகி","இதமுறு யோகந் தன்னி லெழில் பெறநின்றா னன்றே."],"p":"","r":"Sathanikan calls Udayanan, gives him the throne of the earth, and himself takes up ascetic life, freed from anger and cares, and stands in yoga.","n":"The king chooses the throne's heir, Udayanan, over the two sons he has just named, and leaves while still living, not at death.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thuravu","thavam","yogam","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 27","lb":"UD27","mm":1,"ev":"Sathanikan hands the throne to Udayanan and becomes a yoga-practising ascetic.","cl":[["the ascetic's life frees a man from anger and cares","general","asserted",3,"narrator"]],"ag":["சதானிகன்","உதயணன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:2"},"ud:28":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":28,"t":["மணிமுடி கவித்த போழ்தின் வத்தவர்க் கிறைவ னானான்","அணியுநாற் படையுஞ் சூழ்ந்த அமைச்சரு நால்வர் நாமம்","தணிவில்சீர் யூகி யோடு சாருரு மண்ணு வாவும்","துணைவயந் தகனுந் தொல்சீர் விடபக னென்ப வாமே."],"p":"","r":"At his coronation Udayanan becomes lord of the Vatsa people, with four ministers around him: Yugi, Urumannuvan, Vayantakan and Vidapakan.","n":"Line 2 has the second minister's name broken in word division, and the reading 'Urumannuvan' is taken from that.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","vendhan","amaichu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 28","lb":"UD28","mm":1,"ev":"Udayanan is crowned king of the Vatsa people and four ministers are named.","cl":[],"ag":["உதயணன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:2"},"ud:29":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":29,"t":["அரசனுக் கினிய ராகி யமைச்சிய னடத்திச் செற்றே","வருபகை பலவுந் தேய வரச்செங்கோ லுய்க்குங் காலை","அரியநா டகங்கள் கண்டே யரசனு முளமா ழாந்து","கரிணத்தை மறந்துவிட்டுக் காதலி னடிசி லுண்டான்."],"p":"","r":"The king's minister, dear to him, drives out the swarming enemies and runs the realm in the king's name, while the king, watching rare plays and sunk in feeling, forgets Karinam and dines with his beloved.","n":"The verse sets the minister's labour beside the king's ease in one sentence, so the loss in the next verse follows a stretch of heedless pleasure. The word அமைச்சியன் is not a name; that it is Yugi, as verse 38 suggests, is an inference.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["arasu","sengol","pochchaappu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 29","lb":"UD29","mm":1,"ev":"While his minister crushes the enemies and rules for him, the king, absorbed in plays, forgets Karinam and eats a meal with his beloved.","cl":[],"ag":["யூகி","உதயணன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:3"},"ud:30":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":30,"t":["மன்னிய தெய்வ யானை மாயமாய் மறைந்து போக","மன்னனு மனந்த ளர்ந்து மணியிழந் தரவு போலத்","துன்னிய சோக மேவித் துயரெய்தித் தேடுகென்றான்","பன்னருஞ் சேனை சென்று பாரெங்குந் தேடிற்றன்றே."],"p":"","r":"The lasting divine elephant vanishes by magic; the king, spent like a serpent that has lost its jewel, falls into grief, bids the elephant be sought, and the great army goes searching all over the earth.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["idukkan","padai","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 30","lb":"UD30","mm":1,"ev":"The divine elephant disappears and Udayana orders his army to search the whole land for it.","cl":[],"ag":["உதயணன்","சேனை"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:3"},"ud:31":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":31,"t":["சிந்து கங்கைநீர் சேர்ந்து வளம்படும்","அந்த மாகு மவந்திநன் னாட்டினுள்","இந்து சூடிய விஞ்சி வளநகர்","உந்து மாளிகை யுஞ்சையெனும்பதி."],"p":"","r":"Where the waters of the Sindhu and the Ganga join and feed the land, in the fair country of Avanti, stands the rich fortified city Ujjain, whose crowding mansions touch the moon.","n":"The scene moves to Ujjain; the story of Udayana's side stops while the rival's court is set out.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naadu","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 31","lb":"UD31","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:3"},"ud:32":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":32,"t":["உரைப்ப ரும்ப டைப்பிரச் சோதனன்","நிரைத்த மன்னர் நிதிமிக் களப்பவே","தரித்த நேமி யுருட்டித் தரணியாண்","டிரைத்த மாகளிற் றேறொடு மன்னுவான்."],"p":"","r":"Pradyota, whose armies cannot be counted, rules the earth with the discus he bears, while kings in rows bring tribute in wealth, and he stays on with his roaring great elephant and chariot.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vendhan","arasu","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 32","lb":"UD32","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:3"},"ud:33":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":33,"t":["பொருவின் மன்னன் பொன்றிறை கேட்புழித்","திருவ மன்னர் திறைதெரி யோலையுள்","ஒரும கன்புள்ளி யிட்ட தறிந்திலன்","மருவிக் கூறலு மன்னன் வெகுண்டனன்."],"p":"","r":"When the matchless king calls for the gold tribute, he has not seen that a mark has been put against one name on the roll of the kings' tribute; when it is told him he flares up.","n":"The words ஒருமகன் புள்ளி are hard: it may be a single man or a son who made the mark, or a dot against a name. The sense of a mark on the roll is the likely one, but it is not fixed by the line.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["arasu","vekuli","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 33","lb":"UD33","mm":1,"ev":"Pradyota calls for tribute, is told of a mark on the tribute-roll that he had missed, and grows angry.","cl":[],"ag":["பிரச்சோதனன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:3"},"ud:34":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":34,"t":["தாம ரைக்கண் டழலெழ நோக்கியத்","தீமை செய்த திரைக்கடன் மன்னனை","நாம றந்திட நன்கு மறைத்ததென்","ஆம மைச்சரென் றண்ணல் வினவினன்."],"p":"","r":"His eyes blazing like a lotus on fire, the lord asks his ministers what excuse they have for hiding so well, until he forgot it, the wrong done by the king of the sea-girt land.","n":"The accused is left unnamed in the verse; that the sea-girt king is Udayana comes from the next verses, which praise his gifts. A king who accuses his counsellors of concealing a fact is the case Kural 638 and 639 reverse.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["amaichu","vekuli","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 34","lb":"UD34","mm":1,"ev":"Pradyota questions his ministers for concealing the offender's act from him.","cl":[],"ag":["பிரச்சோதனன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["பிரச்சோதனன்","அமைச்சர்"],"ch":"ud:3"},"ud:35":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":35,"t":["உறுக ளத்தினி லுன்னிய வாண்மையும்","பெறுபொ ருள்செறி பீடுடைக் கல்வியும்","தறுகண் வேழந் தகைக்குறு பெற்றியும்","மறுவில் வீணையின் வாய்த்தநல் விஞ்சையும்."],"p":"","r":"The ministers begin to list what the man has: courage proved in the field, learning that brings gain, his way of checking the fierce-eyed elephant, and his flawless mastery of the veena.","n":"The two skills at the centre of the list, handling elephants and playing the veena, are the ones the ambush later uses against him.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aanmai","kalvi","kalai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 35","lb":"UD35","mm":1,"ev":"The ministers name Udayana's skills of arms, learning, elephant-taming and the veena to the angry king.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["அமைச்சர்","பிரச்சோதனன்"],"ch":"ud:3"},"ud:36":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":36,"t":["வளமை யின்வந்த மன்னிய செல்வமும்","இளமை யின்ப மெழில்கல நற்குலம்","உளவ னாதலி னுற்ற கடனென","அளவு நீதி யமைச்ச ருரைத்தனர்."],"p":"","r":"They go on to his inherited wealth, his youth and beauty, his jewels and good family, and say, in measured words, that since he is all this what he did is his due.","n":"The ministers answer the king's accusation by praising the man, not by denying the fault. The sense of உற்ற கடனென, that what he did is fitting to his rank, is doubtful.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["selvam","kudi","ilamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 36","lb":"UD36","mm":1,"ev":"The ministers finish listing Udayana's merits and speak of what is due to him.","cl":[],"ag":["அமைச்சர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["அமைச்சர்","பிரச்சோதனன்"],"ch":"ud:3"},"ud:37":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":37,"t":["வேந்தன் கேட்டு வெகுண்டுரை செய்தனன்","போந்த வற்பற்றிப் போதரு வீரெனக்","சேர்ந்த மைச்சர்கள் செய்பொரு ளென்னென்று","மாந்தி மற்றவர் மற்றொன்று செய்கின்றார்."],"p":"","r":"The king hears and rages, orders them to seize the man who has come and bring him, and the ministers gathered there ask what must be done, then settle among themselves on something else.","n":"The king's anger overrides the ministers' praise at once, and the ministers do not oppose it openly but turn to a scheme.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vekuli","kodungol","soozhchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 37","lb":"UD37","mm":1,"ev":"Pradyota orders Udayana seized; his ministers instead decide on another plan.","cl":[],"ag":["பிரச்சோதனன்","அமைச்சர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["பிரச்சோதனன்","அமைச்சர்"],"ch":"ud:3"},"ud:38":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":38,"t":["ஊன மாற்றர்மேல் யூகிபோர் போனதும்","ஆனை போக வரச னிரக்கமும்","கான யானை காட்டிப் பிடிப்பதும்","மான வேலவர் மந்திரித் தொன்றினர்."],"p":"","r":"The proud ministers take counsel on several matters, Yugi's going to war against the weak foes, the king's pity at the elephant's going, and catching a forest elephant by showing one, and join in one counsel.","n":"The verse names three things on the table; which one they choose has to be read from what they build in the next verses.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["soozhchi","amaichu","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 38","lb":"UD38","mm":1,"ev":"Pradyota's ministers deliberate and agree on a plan of taking an elephant by displaying another.","cl":[],"ag":["அமைச்சர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:3"},"ud:39":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":39,"t":["அரக்கி னும்மெழு காக்கிய நூலினும்","மரத்தி னுங்கிழி மாவின் மயிரினும்","விரித்த தோலினும் வேண்டிய வற்றினும்","தரித்த யானையைத் தாமி யற்றினார்."],"p":"","r":"With lac, thread hardened with wax, wood, painted lime and hair, with stretched hide and whatever else they needed, they made an elephant with their own hands.","n":"The materials are the ones used for dolls and models: the elephant is made to be taken for a real one, not for use as a siege engine.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["soozhchi","vanjam","kalai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 39","lb":"UD39","mm":1,"ev":"The ministers build an artificial elephant out of lac, thread, wood, hair and hide.","cl":[],"ag":["அமைச்சர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:3"},"ud:40":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":40,"t":["பொறிய மைகரிப் பொங்கு முதரத்தில்","உறையு மாந்தரோர் தொண்ணூற் றறுவரை","மறையு மாயுதம் வைத்தத னோருடல்","நெறிகண் டூர்ந்தனர் நீல மலையென."],"p":"","r":"A mechanical elephant with levers and a swollen belly holds ninety-six men, weapons hidden, and they drive it along the road like a blue mountain.","n":"The men inside are the weapon and the elephant a shell: its likeness to a real elephant is its only force.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["soozhchi","vanjam","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 40","lb":"UD40","mm":1,"ev":"Ninety-six armed men hide inside the mechanical elephant and drive it on.","cl":[],"ag":["அமைச்சர்","மறையும் மாந்தர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:3"},"ud:41":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":41,"t":["கார்மு ழக்கிற் களிறொலி செய்யவே","போர்மிக் கானையைப் பொற்புடை மன்னன்முன்","ஊர்ந்து காட்டினா னுற்ற வமைச்சருட்","சார்ந்த மந்திரி சாலங்கா யென்பவன்."],"p":"","r":"As the elephant trumpets like thunder, the minister Salankayan, one of the ministers, rides the battle-elephant before the king and shows it.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["soozhchi","amaichu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 41","lb":"UD41","mm":1,"ev":"Salankayan rides the artificial elephant before Pradyota to show it to him.","cl":[],"ag":["சாலங்காயன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:3"},"ud:42":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":42,"t":["சாலங் காயநீ சார்ந்து தருகென","ஞாலங் காக்கு நரபதி செப்பலும்","வேலுங் கொண்டுநல் வேந்தர்கள் வெண்குடைக்","கோலும் பிச்சமுங் கொண்டு பறந்தனன்."],"p":"","r":"At the ruler's word, Salankayan, go and bring it, he sets off swiftly with spear, with the white umbrella, the sceptre and the feather fan of kings.","n":"That Salankayan carries the king's insignia may mark him as the king's envoy or may be a part of the disguise; the verse does not say.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["arasu","vendhan","thoothu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 42","lb":"UD42","mm":1,"ev":"Pradyota sends Salankayan out; he sets off carrying royal insignia.","cl":[],"ag":["சாலங்காயன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["பிரச்சோதனன்","சாலங்காயன்"],"ch":"ud:3"},"ud:43":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":43,"t":["ஈரெண் ணாயிர மெண்வரை யானையும்","ஈரெண் ணாயிர மீடில் புரவியும்","ஈரெண் ணாயிர மின்பணித் தேருடன்","ஈரெண் ணாயிர விற்படை யாளரே."],"p":"","r":"Sixteen thousand elephants of the eight hills, sixteen thousand fine horses, sixteen thousand decorated chariots and sixteen thousand archers go with them.","n":"The word எண்வரை before elephants is rare and its sense (of eight kinds, or of the eight hills) is not fixed here.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["padai","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 43","lb":"UD43","mm":1,"ev":"A force of sixteen thousand each of elephants, horses, chariots and archers is named to go with the machine.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:3"},"ud:44":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":44,"t":["இத்த னையு மியல்புடன் கூடியே","மெத்தெ னாவரு கென்று விடுத்தனன்","ஒத்த நற்பொறி யோங்கிய யானையும்","வத்த வன்றன் வனத்திடை வந்ததே."],"p":"","r":"With all this in good order, the one who sends them says come softly, and the elephant of well-made levers arrives in the forest of the Vatsa king.","n":"The verse does not name who gives the order to come softly; it may be the king or Salankayan.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["soozhchi","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 44","lb":"UD44","mm":1,"ev":"The artificial elephant and its host are sent to the forest of the Vatsa king.","cl":[],"ag":["சாலங்காயன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:3"},"ud:45":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":45,"t":["அவ்வ னத்தினி லான பிடிகளும்","கவ்வு கைத்தழைக் காரிடி யானைதன்","மவ்வ லம்மத வண்டெழ வீசலும்","அவ்வ னச்சர ரன்புடன் கண்டனர்."],"p":"","r":"In that forest the hunters look with love at the cow-elephants and at the great bull whose rut sets bees rising, holding a leafy bough in its trunk like a storm cloud.","n":"The decoy is made to behave like a bull in rut, so the shell is dressed in the signs of a living elephant.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vanjam","kurippu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 45","lb":"UD45","mm":1,"ev":"The forest-dwellers see the decoy among the real cow-elephants and take it for a rutting bull.","cl":[],"ag":["வனசரர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:3"},"ud:46":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":46,"t":["எம்மி றையது வேழ மெனவெண்ணித்","தம்மி லோடி யுதையற் குரைத்தலும்","கொம்மை வண்மணிக் கோலக் கலினமாச்","செம்ம லுஞ்சிறந் தேறி நடந்தனன்."],"p":"","r":"Thinking it is our lord's elephant, they run to tell Udayana; with a splendid horse in jewelled trappings, the proud king mounts and sets out.","n":"Whether எம் இறை means Udayana, as the story of the lost divine elephant would suggest, or only the hunters' lord in general is not fixed in the line.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vanjam","aanmai","kurippu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 46","lb":"UD46","mm":1,"ev":"The hunters report the elephant to Udayana, thinking it his own, and he rides out.","cl":[],"ag":["வனசரர்","உதயணன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:3"},"ud:47":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":47,"t":["புள்ளிடை தடுப்பத் தீய பொய்க்குறி செய்யக் கண்டும்","வள்ளலு நடப்பா னாக வயந்தகன் விலக்கப் போந்து","கள்ளவிழ் மலர்க்கா னத்துக் கள்ளநல் லியானை கண்டே","உள்ளமெய் மொழிக டம்மா லுணர்ந்தவ னினிய னானான்."],"p":"","r":"Though birds cross his path and ill signs show, the generous king walks on, Vayantakan holds him back in vain, and in the honey-dripping grove he sees the false elephant and grows glad by words he trusts.","n":"Omens and a warning both reach him before he sees the decoy; the verse offers no excuse for him. The sense of line 4 is not clear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kurippu","pochchaappu","thunai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 47","lb":"UD47","mm":1,"ev":"Udayana ignores the omens and Vayantakan's warning and walks to the grove where he sees the false elephant.","cl":[["birds crossing the path and ill signs are warnings of coming harm","general","reported",1,"narrator"]],"ag":["உதயணன்","வயந்தகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:3"},"ud:48":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":48,"t":["நக்க ணத்தை நயந்துட னோக்கிலன்","அக்க கணத்தி லகமகிழ் வெய்தித்தன்","மிக்க வீணையை மெய்ந்நரம் பார்த்துடன்","தக்க ராகத்திற் றான்மிக வாசித்தான்."],"p":"","r":"Without a glance of desire at the company named in line 1, he is glad at heart and plays his great veena, strings true, in the right raga.","n":"He answers a decoy of his own sort with his own art: the skill that the ministers praised is his lure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kalai","inbam","pochchaappu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 48","lb":"UD48","mm":1,"ev":"Udayana takes up his veena and plays it in the proper raga.","cl":[],"ag":["உதயணன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:3"},"ud:49":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":49,"t":["பொறியின் வேழத்தின் பொங்கு செவியுற","உறும னத்துட னூர்ந்துமுன் னேவர","மறையு மாந்தர்கைம் மாவை யழித்திடப்","பொறிக ழன்றது போர்ப்படை யானதே."],"p":"","r":"As the music reaches the wide ears of the mechanical elephant and he rides on eagerly, the hidden men burst the hand-made beast, the fittings fall away, and it becomes a fighting host.","n":"The words of line 3 can also be read as the hidden men destroying the elephant's shell for the sake of the attack; the sense is the same.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vanjam","soozhchi","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 49","lb":"UD49","mm":1,"ev":"The hidden men break out of the machine as Udayana rides in, and it turns into an army.","cl":[],"ag":["மறையும் மாந்தர்","உதயணன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:3"},"ud:50":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":50,"t":["செறுநர் செய்தது சித்திர மாமென","முறுவல் கொண்ட முகத்தின னாகத்தன்","னுறுவ யந்தக னுற்றவைந் நூற்றுவர்","மறுவில் வீரியர் வந்துடன் கூடினர்."],"p":"","r":"Seeing in the enemy's deed a clever picture, he smiles, and Vayantakan and five hundred faultless heroes come and join him.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aanmai","thunai","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 50","lb":"UD50","mm":1,"ev":"Udayana smiles at the trick and is joined by Vayantakan and five hundred warriors.","cl":[],"ag":["உதயணன்","வயந்தகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:3"},"ud:51":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":51,"t":["கரந்தி ருந்த களிற்றினுட் சேனையும்","பரந்து முன்வந்த பாங்கில் வளைத்தபின்","விரிந்து வத்தவன் வெகுண்டு நூறினான்","முரிந்து சேனை முனையின் மடிந்ததே."],"p":"","r":"The army hidden in the elephant spreads out and closes round them; the angry Vatsa king cuts down hundreds, then his own force breaks and dies at the front.","n":"Whose army is meant by சேனை in line 4 is not marked; it may be his own five hundred.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["padai","maram","aanmai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 51","lb":"UD51","mm":1,"ev":"The hidden host encircles Udayana's party; he kills hundreds before the force at the front is broken.","cl":[],"ag":["மறையும் மாந்தர்","உதயணன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:3"},"ud:52":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":52,"t":["சாலங் காயனுஞ் சார்ந்து வெகுண்டிட","நாலு மாப்படை வந்துநாற் றிக்கிலும்","மேலெ ழுந்து மிகவும் வளைத்தன","காலன் போன்மனன் கண்கள் சிவந்தன."],"p":"","r":"Salankayan, enraged, closes in with the four-fold army, which rises up and ringed the king from all four quarters while his eyes redden like Death's.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 52","lb":"UD52","mm":1,"ev":"Salankayan's four-fold army surrounds the king from the four quarters.","cl":[],"ag":["சாலங்காயன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:4"},"ud:53":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":53,"t":["புல்வாய்க் கூட்டத்துப் புக்க புலியெனக்","கொல்வா ளோச்சியே கூற்றம் விருந்துண","வில்வா டம்முடன் வீர ரழிந்திட","வல்வாள் வத்தவன் வாட்கிரை யிட்டனன்."],"p":"","r":"Like a tiger in a herd of deer, the sword-wielding king of Vatsa makes the bowmen fall and feeds Death a feast, giving the blade its food.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 53","lb":"UD53","mm":1,"ev":"Udayanan cuts down the bowmen who ring him.","cl":[],"ag":["உதயணன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:4"},"ud:54":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":54,"t":["கொன்ற போரிற் குருதியா றோடவு","நின்ற மாந்தர்க ணீங்கிவிட் டோடவும்","கன்றி யுட்சாலங் காயனு மேல்வர","மன்றன் வாளவன் சென்னியில் வைத்தனன்."],"p":"","r":"As blood runs like a river and the men standing about flee, the furious Salankayan comes on, and the king sets his sword on his head.","n":"The word மன்றன் in line 4 is not a clear word for king, so the verse does not name who lays the sword; the next verse, which has the king spare the man, is what fixes the reading.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maram","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 54","lb":"UD54","mm":1,"ev":"Udayanan overpowers Salankayan and lays his sword on his head.","cl":[],"ag":["சாலங்காயன்","உதயணன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:4"},"ud:55":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":55,"t":["மந்திரீகளை மன்னர் வதைசெயார்","புந்தி மிக்கோ ருரைபொருட் டேறித்தன்","செந்தி வாளை யழுத்திலன் செல்வனும்","அந்த மைச்சனை யன்பின் விடுத்தனன்."],"p":"","r":"Kings do not put ministers to death, so the king, heeding the words of the wise, does not press down his sword and lets that minister go in affection.","n":"The sparing is given as a rule of kings and as the advice of the learned, not as the king's own softness. In this chapter the same king is soon taken captive by men who show him no such restraint.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["amaichu","aram","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 55","lb":"UD55","mm":1,"ev":"Udayanan spares the minister Salankayan and sets him free.","cl":[["kings do not slay ministers","general","asserted",1,"narrator"]],"ag":["உதயணன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:4"},"ud:56":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":56,"t":["திரளு டைக்கரி சேர்ந்து வளைத்தலும்","வரைகள் வீழ்வென வாரணம் வீழவும்","நிரைம ணித்தேர் நிலத்திற் புரளவும்","புரவி கள்பொங்கிப் பூமியில் வீழவும்."],"p":"","r":"Elephants in a mass close in, and the war-elephants fall like hills, the rows of jewelled chariots roll on the ground and horses surge and fall on the earth.","n":"The verse is one long clause with four falling things and no finite verb; verse 57 supplies the sentence, so read the two together.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 56","lb":"UD56","mm":1,"ev":"A battle of elephants, chariots and horses brings down the combatants; the verse has no main verb and runs on into the next.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:4"},"ud:57":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":57,"t":["வெஞ்சி னம்மனன் வேறணி நூறலும்","குஞ்ச ரத்தின்கோட் டின்வா ளொடியவத்","தஞ்ச மின்றிய தாருடை வேந்தனை","வெஞ்சொன் மாந்தர் வெகுண்டுடன் பற்றினார்."],"p":"","r":"As the king's rage cuts through another rank and his sword snaps on an elephant's tusk, harsh-speaking men seize the garlanded king, left without refuge.","n":"The captors are called only harsh-spoken men; that they are the Ujjain king's people follows from the letter and the dream of the verses that come after.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","vendhan","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 57","lb":"UD57","mm":1,"ev":"Udayanan's sword breaks on an elephant's tusk and Pradyotanan's men seize him.","cl":[],"ag":["உதயணன்","வெஞ்சொன் மாந்தர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:4"},"ud:58":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":58,"t":["நங்கை மார்குழ னாண்மலர் சூட்டுங்கை","திங்கள் போலத் திலத மெழுதுங்கை","பொங்கு கொங்கையிற் குங்குமம் பூசுங்கை","பங்க யத்தடிப் பாடகஞ் சூட்டுங்கை."],"p":"","r":"A catalogue of what the captive's hand used to do for women: set fresh flowers in their hair, draw the moon-like mark on their brow, smear saffron on their breasts and fit anklets on their lotus feet.","n":"The verse never names a hand's owner. It sets four tender acts of the hand beside the fact, told in the next verse, that the same hand is now tied.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 58","lb":"UD58","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:4"},"ud:59":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":59,"t":["கீத வீணைசெங் கெந்த மளையுங்கை","ஈதன் மேவி யிரவலர்க் காற்றுங்கை","ஏத மில்குணத் தென்முடி மன்னன்கை","போத வெண்டுகி லாற்புறத் தார்த்தனர்."],"p":"","r":"The hand that plays the lute, daubs scented paste and gives to those who ask, the hand of my faultless crowned king, is bound with white cloth for all outside to see.","n":"The speaker says என்முடி மன்னன் ('my crowned king') and is not named, so he may be a lamenting follower or the poet in a follower's voice; the reading keeps the speaker open. The giving hand is the one that is bound: the verse links his generosity to his helplessness.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["eegai","nilaiyaamai","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 59","lb":"UD59","mm":1,"ev":"Udayanan's hands are bound with white cloth while the onlookers outside cry out.","cl":[],"ag":["வெஞ்சொன் மாந்தர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:4"},"ud:60":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":60,"t":["சிலந்தி நூலிற் செறித்தநற் சிங்கம்போல்","அலங்கல் வேலினா னன்புடை யூகிக்கே","இலங்க வோலை யெழுதி வயந்தகன்","நலங்கொள் கையி னவின்று கொடுத்தனன்."],"p":"","r":"Vayanthakan writes a palm-leaf letter to the loving Yugi, as one binds a fine lion in a spider's thread, and hands it over with his own fair hand.","n":"The simile means the lion is held by a thread that would be nothing if he were free; it puts the captivity as a binding by guile more than by strength.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["amaichu","natpu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 60","lb":"UD60","mm":1,"ev":"Vayanthakan writes a letter to Yugi telling of the king's capture and gives it to him.","cl":[],"ag":["வயந்தகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:4"},"ud:61":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":61,"t":["காசி றேர்மிசைக் காவ லுடன்செலப்","பேச ரும்பெரு மைப்பிரச் சோதனன்","ஆசை யின்மக ளாடகப் பாவைபோன்ம்","வாச வதத்தை வண்மைக் கனவிடை."],"p":"","r":"As the king is carried under guard on a bright chariot, Vasavadatta, the beloved daughter of the great Pradyotanan, in a dream of her own sees something that is worked out in the next verses.","n":"The verse is one unfinished clause: the guarded chariot and the dream are put side by side with no main verb. That the passenger is the captive king is read from the context of verses 57-60.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kanavu","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 61","lb":"UD61","mm":1,"ev":"The captive king is taken to Ujjain while Vasavadatta has a dream.","cl":[],"ag":["பிரச்சோதனன்","வாசவதத்தை"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:4"},"ud:62":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":62,"t":["பொங்கி ளங்கதிர் போந்த தமளியில்","கொங்கை யைத் தழீஇக் கொண்டு டன்செல","நங்கை கண்டுநற் றாதைக் குரைத்தனள்","அங்கந் நூலின றிந்தவர்க் கேட்டனன்."],"p":"","r":"In bed as the young sun rose she dreamed that someone clasped her breasts and carried her off with him; she told it to her good father, who asked those who knew the book of dreams.","n":"The dream is told in the narrator's voice without naming the man who takes her; the reading of verse 63 is what turns it into a prophecy.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kanavu","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 62","lb":"UD62","mm":1,"ev":"Vasavadatta tells her father her dream and he asks the dream-readers about it.","cl":[],"ag":["வாசவதத்தை","பிரச்சோதனன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:4"},"ud:63":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":63,"t":["இவண்மு லைக்கி யைந்தநல் லெழின்ம ணம்ம கன்வந்தே","துவளி டையி ளமுலை தோய்ந்து கொண்டு போமென","அவள்க னவு ரைப்பக்கேட் டண்ண லும்ம கிழ்ந்தபின்","திவளு மாலைத் தேர்மிசைச் செம்மல் வந்த டைந்தனன்."],"p":"","r":"The readers tell that a bridegroom will come and take her away, touching her young breasts, and when her father hears the dream he is glad, and a lord arrives on a garlanded chariot.","n":"The speech of lines 1-2 is assigned to the readers of verse 62 and is not marked off as speech; the odd wording இவண்முலைக்கியைந்த makes its parse uncertain. The father's gladness follows from the foretelling and the captive is the one said to be arriving, so the verse sets prophecy and arrival side by side.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kanavu","kaamam","oozh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 63","lb":"UD63","mm":1,"ev":"The dream-readers tell Pradyotanan that a bridegroom will come and take Vasavadatta, and the captive king arrives.","cl":[["a dream read by the learned foretells a coming marriage","general","reported",1,"nool_arintavar"]],"ag":["நூலினறிந்தவர்","பிரச்சோதனன்","உதயணன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["நூலினறிந்தவர்","பிரச்சோதனன்"],"ch":"ud:4"},"ud:64":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":64,"t":["மன்ன னைமி கவும்நொந்து மாந கரி ரங்கவும்","துன்னி வெஞ்சி றைமனையிற் றொல்வி னைது ரப்பவும்","இன்ன நற் படியிருப்ப வியல்வ யந்த கனுந்தான்","சென்று யூகி தன்னிடைத் திருமு கத்தைக் காட்டினான்."],"p":"","r":"With the king pitied and the great city weeping, an old deed driving him into the fierce prison-house, Vayanthakan goes to Yugi and shows him the letter.","n":"Line 2 puts the captivity in an old deed (தொல்வினை); nothing in the verse says the king's own folly or the enemy's guile as its cause, though the poet's account of the battle shows both.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oozh","vinai","amaichu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 64","lb":"UD64","mm":1,"ev":"Vayanthakan brings the letter to Yugi and shows it.","cl":[["a deed of a former birth drives the king into prison","general","asserted",2,"narrator"]],"ag":["வயந்தகன்","யூகி"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:4"},"ud:65":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":65,"t":["அண்ணல் கோயி லெங்கணு மரற்றி னும்பு லம்பினும்","கண்ணி னீர ருவிகள் கால லைத்தொ ழுகவும்","அண்ண லோலை வந்தசெய்தி மான யூகி கேட்டுடன்","புண்ணில் வேலெ றிந்தெனப் பொற்ப ழிந்து வீழ்ந்தனன்."],"p":"","r":"Cries ring out in every palace and eyes stream, and Yugi, hearing the message of his lord's letter, falls broken like one struck on a wound by a spear.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","amaichu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 65","lb":"UD65","mm":1,"ev":"Yugi hears the letter's news and collapses.","cl":[],"ag":["யூகி"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:4"},"ud:66":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":66,"t":["தேறி னனெ ழுந்திருந்து தீயர் கள்ள யானையை","மாறு தரக் காட்டியெம் மன்ன னைப்பி டித்தனர்","வீறு தர வந்நகரை வெங்க யத்த ழித்துப்பின்","கூறு மன்ம களுடன் கொற்ற வனை மீட்குவம்."],"p":"","r":"Rising steadied, Yugi says: the wicked showed a false elephant and took our king; we shall destroy their city with fierce elephants and recover the king along with the famed king's daughter.","n":"The words that name the trap, கள்ள யானை, tell the reader that the capture was by a decoy elephant; verse 57 shows only the seizure. The vow also takes in the king's daughter, which makes the rescue a winning of a bride as well.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["amaichu","vanjam","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 66","lb":"UD66","mm":1,"ev":"Yugi recovers and resolves to take the king back and destroy Ujjain.","cl":[],"ag":["யூகி"],"ec":[],"spk":["யூகி",""],"ch":"ud:4"},"ud:67":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":67,"t":["மீள்கு வம்யா ருமென் றெணி வெகுண்டு போர்க்க ளத்தினில்","வாண்மு னைக டந்தவர்க்கு வஞ்ச னைசெய் வோமென","நீள்வி ழிநன் மாதரோடு நின்ற சுற்றத் தோர்களைக்","கோள் களைந்து புட்பகத்திற் கொண்டு வந்து வைத்தனன்."],"p":"","r":"Resolving that they would all return, and that those who had passed through the sword's edge would be met with guile, he took the king's kin and wives out of danger and set them in Pushpakam.","n":"The resolve is Yugi's thought in the narrator's frame, and the work does not say here whether guile is right in itself, only that it is his answer.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vanjam","amaichu","natpu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 67","lb":"UD67","mm":1,"ev":"Yugi moves the king's kin and women from the battlefield to Pushpakam.","cl":[["those who have crossed the sword may be answered with deceit","general","reported",2,"yugi"]],"ag":["யூகி"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:4"},"ud:68":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":68,"t":["உருமண் ணுவா வினுட னிடப கன்ச யந்தியும்","திருநி றைந்த புட்பகமுஞ் சேர்ந்தி னிதி ருக்கவென்","பெரும கன்க ணிகை மைந்தர் பிங்க லக டகரை","அரசு நாட்டி யாள்கவென்றே யன்பு டன்கொ டுத்தனன்."],"p":"","r":"With Urumannuvan, Idabakan and Jayanti kept safe in prosperous Pushpakam, he gives the rule lovingly to Pingala and Kadaka, bidding them reign.","n":"பெருமகன்கணிகைமைந்தர் can be read as the sons of a courtesan or as parts of names; the reading takes the two named rulers and leaves their parentage open.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["arasu","amaichu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 68","lb":"UD68","mm":1,"ev":"Yugi appoints Pingala and Kadaka to rule Pushpakam and keeps Urumannuvan and others there.","cl":[],"ag":["யூகி"],"ec":[],"spk":["யூகி",""],"ch":"ud:4"},"ud:69":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":69,"t":["மன்ன வற்கி ரங்கியூகி மரித்த னனென் வார்த்தையைப்","பன்னி யெங்க ணும்முறை பரப்பி வைய கந்தன்னில்","அன்ன தன தொப்புமை யமைந் ததோர்ச வந்தனை","உன்னி யூகி கான்விறகி லொள்ளெரிப்ப டுத்தனன்."],"p":"","r":"Pitying the king, Yugi has the news of his own death spread everywhere and, thinking of a corpse that matches his own, burns it in a bright fire of forest wood.","n":"The false death serves the plan of getting to the captive king unwatched; it costs him the loyalty of the realm that verse 70 states.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vanjam","amaichu","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 69","lb":"UD69","mm":1,"ev":"Yugi has it said that he is dead and burns a body made to pass as his.","cl":[],"ag":["யூகி"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:4"},"ud:70":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":70,"t":["தன்ன கர்பு லம்பவெங்குந் தன்னை யுங்க ரத்தலின்","உன்னி வந்து மாற்றரச ரோங்கு நாடு பற்றினர்","என்ற றிந்து யூகியு மினிச்சி றையின் மன்னனைச்","சென்ற வட்காண் டுமென்று தேய முன்னிச் சென்றனன்."],"p":"","r":"Hearing that his own city is crying out and enemy kings, thinking him hidden, have seized the high lands, Yugi decides to go and see the king in prison and sets out to that country.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["amaichu","arasu","vanjam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 70","lb":"UD70","mm":1,"ev":"Yugi hears that his lands are taken and sets out for the prison to see the king.","cl":[],"ag":["யூகி"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:4"},"ud:71":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":71,"t":["துன்ன ருநற் கானமோடு தொன்ம லையிற் சாரலும்","செந்நெல் கள்வி ளைவயற் செழும்பு னன திகளும்","மன்னு நாடுந் தான்கடந்து மாகொ டிநி றைந்திலங்கு","நன்ன கருஞ் சேனையி னன்க மைச்சன் சென்றனன்."],"p":"","r":"Through wild forest, old hills, their slopes, fields of ripening red rice, lush streams and settled lands, the good minister goes on with his army toward the splendid city of banners.","n":"Most of the verse is a catalogue of the land he passes; the one event is his march, and it adds the army to the picture of the lone disguised man in the verses that follow.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["amaichu","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 71","lb":"UD71","mm":1,"ev":"Yugi crosses forest, hills and farmland with his army toward the city.","cl":[],"ag":["யூகி"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:4"},"ud:72":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":72,"t":["ஒலிகட லன்ன வோசையுஞ் சேனைதன்","புலிமுக வாயிற் பொற்புடைத் திலங்கும்","மலிகுடிப் பாக்க மதின் மறைந்திருக்க","வலியதன் சேனை வைத்தன னன்றே."],"p":"","r":"He leaves his strong army hidden behind the wall of a crowded settlement at the shining Tiger-face gate, a place loud as the roaring sea.","n":"புலிமுகவாயில் is read as the name of a gate or place; the verse does not say whose city it is, so only the army's hiding is stated.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["padai","vanjam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 72","lb":"UD72","mm":1,"ev":"Yugi lodges his army in hiding at the Tiger-face gate settlement.","cl":[],"ag":["யூகி"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:4"},"ud:73":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":73,"t":["இன்னவை கேட்கி னின்னவை தருகென","மன்னவ னறியு மருளுரை பயிற்றி","மன்னிய வேடம் வகுத்துடன் கொண்டு","நன்னகர் வீதி நடுவினில் வந்தான்."],"p":"","r":"Drilling in the words that would draw out each question and its answer, speech of feigned madness that the king would know, he takes a fixed disguise and walks into the middle of the city street.","n":"மருளுரை is read as speech that bewilders others but carries a sense the king would recognise; the verse makes the king, not the onlookers, the speech's real hearer.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vesham","otru","vanjam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 73","lb":"UD73","mm":1,"ev":"Yugi prepares coded mad talk, puts on a disguise and enters the city's main street.","cl":[],"ag":["யூகி"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:4"},"ud:74":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":74,"t":["இருள்படு குஞ்சி யியல்படத் தூற்றி","மருள்செய மாலை வகுத்துடன் சுற்றி","உருநிறச் சுண்ண முடலினிற் பூசிப்","பொருணலச் சுட்டி பொருந்துறச் சேர்த்தி."],"p":"","r":"He scatters his dark hair so that it falls loose, winds on a garland that bewilders, rubs coloured powder over his body and fixes a mark in the right place.","n":"The verse is one unfinished list of acts of dress, with no main verb; line 4 is obscure and the reading takes it as a forehead mark.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vesham","otru"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 74","lb":"UD74","mm":1,"ev":"Yugi dresses himself as a madman with loose hair, garland and powder.","cl":[],"ag":["யூகி"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:4"},"ud:75":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":75,"t":["செம்பொற் பட்டஞ் சேர்த்தி நுதலில்","அம்பொற் சாந்த மணிந்த மார்பன்","செம்பொற் கச்சைச் சேர்த்தின னரையில்","அம்படக்கீறி யணிந்த வுடையான்."],"p":"","r":"A figure is described in costume: a golden band on the brow, sandal paste on the chest, a golden girdle at the waist and a garment slit with arrows.","n":"The verse never names the wearer; that this is the disguised Yugi is known only from verse 81, where the king knows him by his music.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","soozhchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 75","lb":"UD75","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:5"},"ud:76":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":76,"t":["கோதையுத் தரியங் கொண்ட கோலத்தன்","காதிற் குழையினன் காலிற் சதங்கையன்","ஊதுங் குழலின னுலரிய வுடுக்கையன்","போதச் சிரசிற் பொருநீர்க் கலசன்"],"p":"","r":"The same figure is dressed in a garland and upper cloth, earrings, anklet bells, a dried waist-cloth, a playing flute and a water pot carried on the head.","n":"A strange mixed costume that suits a mad entertainer; the verse leaves the guise unnamed.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","soozhchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 76","lb":"UD76","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:5"},"ud:77":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":77,"t":["கொடியணி மூதூர்க் கோலநல் வீதி","நடுவட் டோன்றி நாடக மாடிப்","படுமிசைக் கரணம் பாங்கிற் றாண்டி","இடியென முழங்கி யினிதினின் வந்தான்."],"p":"","r":"The figure appears in the middle of the flag-hung old city's fine street, dancing, passes through the steps of the dance and comes along sweetly, roaring like thunder.","n":"Yugi is not named; the entry as a public performer is the first stage of the rescue plan.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kalai","soozhchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 77","lb":"UD77","mm":1,"ev":"A dancing, thundering stranger comes down the main street of the city.","cl":[],"ag":["யூகி"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:5"},"ud:78":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":78,"t":["இந்திர லோகம்விட் டிந்திரன் வந்தனன்","அந்தரத் திருந்தியா னன்பினின் வந்தேன்","இந்திர னெனக்கிறை யீண்டும் புதல்வர்க்குத்","தந்திரக் குமக்குத் தானிறை யாமென."],"p":"","r":"The figure cries out that Indra has left Indra's world and come, that he sits in the sky and has come in love, and that he is lord of the sons who are Indra's lord, in words whose last two lines are broken.","n":"The claim to be Indra is part of a mad-man's act, not a belief of the poem; lines 3-4 are too broken to parse with confidence.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["soozhchi","vanjam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 78","lb":"UD78","mm":1,"ev":"The disguised man proclaims himself Indra come down.","cl":[],"ag":["யூகி"],"ec":[],"spk":["யூகி",""],"ch":"ud:5"},"ud:79":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":79,"t":["புற்றினி லுறையும் பொறிவரி யைந்தலைப்","பற்றரு நாகம் பற்றிவந் தினிதா","உற்றவிந் நகரத் துட்சிறை வைத்தார்","அற்றதை யெங்கு மறியக் காட்டினார்."],"p":"","r":"The speaker says a five-headed snake with spotted stripes was caught from its anthill and put, held, inside this city's prison, and that they showed it so that it should be known everywhere.","n":"The snake is most likely a veiled name for the imprisoned Udayanan, so a madman's babble tells the crowd what the guards cannot take as news; the verse itself does not say so.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["soozhchi","vanjam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 79","lb":"UD79","mm":1,"ev":"The disguised man speaks in riddling words of a captured snake kept in the city's prison.","cl":[],"ag":["யூகி"],"ec":[],"spk":["யூகி",""],"ch":"ud:5"},"ud:80":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":80,"t":["மருளுந் தெருளும் வரம்பில பயிற்றித்","திரளுறு செனங்கள் திறவதிற் சூழப்","பெருந்தெரு வெல்லாம் பிற்படப் போந்தே","அருஞ்சிறைப் பள்ளி யருகினிற் சேர்ந்தான்."],"p":"","r":"After teaching bewilderment and clarity without measure, with crowds gathering around him, he leaves the great streets behind and comes to the hard prison hall.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["soozhchi","kalai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 80","lb":"UD80","mm":1,"ev":"The disguised man draws a crowd, then reaches the prison.","cl":[],"ag":["யூகி"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:5"},"ud:81":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":81,"t":["கிளைத்தலை யிருவர் கற்ற கிளர்நரப் பிசையுங் கீதம்","தளைச்சிறை மன்னன் கேட்பத் தான்மகிழ் குழலினூத","உளத்தியல் பாட்டைக் கேட்டு யூகியா மென மகிழ்ந்து","களைந்தனன் கவலை யெல்லாங் காவலற் குணர்த்திப் போந்தான்."],"p":"","r":"Two kinsmen's song learned on the strings is played on the flute he enjoys for the chained king to hear; the king hears the song of his own mind, knows that it is Yugi and rejoices, casts off every care, and Yugi, having told the king, leaves.","n":"The tune is the secret message: the guards hear a mad piper, the king hears his minister. Line 1, 'two of the kin', is unclear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["natpu","soozhchi","kalai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 81","lb":"UD81","mm":1,"ev":"Yugi plays music at the prison so that the captive Udayanan knows him and takes heart.","cl":[],"ag":["யூகி","உதயணன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:5"},"ud:82":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":82,"t":["பலகொடி வாயிற் செல்லப் பார்மன்னன் சேனை வந்து","நலமுறு வடிவு நோக்க நாகத்தின் கோடு பாய்ந்த","கலனணி மார்வ டுவ்வைக் கஞ்சுகத் துகிலின் மூடத்","தலைமுத லடியீ றாகத் தரத்தினாற் கண்டு போந்தார்."],"p":"","r":"The king's soldiers come through many gates to see the fine form of the man and, from his head to his feet, look him over as he goes; the line about the ornament on his chest and the cloth over it is broken.","n":"Line 2 and the word வடுவ்வை in line 3 read as corrupt; only the sense that he was looked over and passed is secure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["soozhchi","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 82","lb":"UD82","mm":1,"ev":"The king's men examine the strange performer and let him go.","cl":[],"ag":["சேனை","யூகி"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:5"},"ud:83":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":83,"t":["பித்தனற் பேய னென்று பெருமகற் குரைப்பக் கேட்டு","வெற்றிநற் சேனை மற்றும் வெஞ்சிறை காக்க வென்றான்","மற்றினி யூகி போந்து மலிகுடி பாக்கஞ் சேர்ந்தே","அற்றைநா ளிரவில் யானை யனல்கதம் படுக்க லுற்றான்."],"p":"","r":"They tell the great king that the man is a ghost-possessed madman; hearing it, the king bids his victorious army to keep the cruel prison still, while Yugi goes to the village and that night sets about rousing the elephant's rage.","n":"The guise works: the king's caution falls on the prison guard, not on the stranger.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["soozhchi","padai","vanjam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 83","lb":"UD83","mm":1,"ev":"The guards dismiss the stranger as mad; Yugi that night sets out to enrage the elephant.","cl":[],"ag":["சேனை","வேந்தன்","யூகி"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:5"},"ud:84":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":84,"t":["வாளொடு கைவில் லேந்தி வயந்தகன் றன்னோ டெண்ணித்","தோளன தோழன் கூடத் தூபத்துக் கேற்ற வத்தும்","வேளையீ தென்று கொண்டு விரகினாற் கயிறு பற்றித்","தாளொத்த கொம்மை மீதிற் றரத்தினா லிழிந்தா னன்றே."],"p":"","r":"With sword and bow in hand he reckons with Vayanthakan, his arm-like friend, that the hour is right for the fumigating materials, and by cunning he takes hold of a rope and goes down, by skill, onto the elephant's mass.","n":"The words after the rope in lines 3-4 are hard; whether he drops onto the elephant's back or its legs is not clear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["soozhchi","kaalam","natpu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 84","lb":"UD84","mm":1,"ev":"Yugi and Vayanthakan choose the hour and go down a rope to the elephant.","cl":[],"ag":["யூகி","வயந்தகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:5"},"ud:85":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":85,"t":["ஆனைதன் னிலைகண் டெய்தி யகிலிடும் புகையு மூட்டிச்","சேனைமன் னகர ழித்துச் சிறைவீடுன் கடனே யொன்று","மானநல் யூகி யானை செவியின்மந் திரத்தைச் செப்ப","யானைதன் மதக்கம் பத்தி லருந்தளை யுதறிற் றன்றே."],"p":"","r":"He sees the elephant's place, makes aguru smoke rise, and, telling the beast that it should destroy the army and the city and that freeing the captive is its task, the wise Yugi whispers a mantra into its ear, and in its maddened rut the elephant shakes off its heavy chain.","n":"The elephant, the king's weapon, becomes the means of the captive's rescue; this is the stratagem the whole sequence turns on.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["soozhchi","maram","vanjam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 85","lb":"UD85","mm":1,"ev":"Yugi smokes the elephant, whispers a mantra into its ear and the elephant breaks its chain.","cl":[],"ag":["யூகி","யானை"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:5"},"ud:86":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":86,"t":["நீங்கிட மிதுவென் றெண்ணி நிலைமதி லேறிப் போகத்","தூங்கிரு டன்னி லானை சுழன் றலைந் தோடப் பாகர்","பாங்கினால் வளைப்பப் பொங்கிப் படுமுகின் முழக்க மென்ன","ஆங்கது பிடுங்கிக் கையா லவரைக்கொன் றிட்டதன்றே."],"p":"","r":"Thinking this the way to leave, Yugi climbs the standing wall and goes; in the thick darkness the elephant whirls and runs, and when the mahouts try to enclose it, it roars like a bursting cloud, tears loose and kills them with its trunk.","n":"The keepers die at the hand of the beast they were set to guard, the cost of the stratagem falling first on men who did nothing.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","kolai","soozhchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 86","lb":"UD86","mm":1,"ev":"The freed elephant kills the mahouts who try to enclose it.","cl":[],"ag":["யூகி","யானை","பாகர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:5"},"ud:87":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":87,"t":["வேழமும் மதங்கொண் டோட வேந்தன்கேட் டினிதெ ழுந்து","வேழநன் வேட்டங் காண வெம்முலை மாத ரோடும்","ஆழிநல் லிறைவன் றானு மணிமிகு மாட மேறிச்","சூழநன் மாதர் நிற்பத் துளக்கின்றி நோக்கி னானே."],"p":"","r":"The elephant runs in rut, the king hears, rises and, with his full-breasted women, climbs a jewelled mansion to watch the hunt, looking on without a tremor.","n":"The king watches it as a spectacle; the next verses show how soon his own city pays for it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","soozhchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 87","lb":"UD87","mm":1,"ev":"The Ujjain king climbs a mansion to watch the elephant he thinks he controls.","cl":[],"ag":["வேந்தன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:5"},"ud:88":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":88,"t":["கூடமா ளிகைக ளெல்லாங் கோட்டினாற் குத்திச் செம்பொன்","மாடமு மதிலு மற்று மறித்தஃதி டித்துச் செல்ல","ஆடவர் கூடி யோடி யயில்குந்தந் தண்ட மேந்தி","நாடி நற்கையாற் றட்டி நாற்றிசை சூழ்ந்து நின்றார்."],"p":"","r":"The elephant gores the storeyed halls and golden mansions and walls and pulls them down as it passes, while men gather, run, hold spears and staffs and strike it, standing around it on all four sides.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 88","lb":"UD88","mm":1,"ev":"The elephant batters the city's mansions while men try to surround it.","cl":[],"ag":["யானை","நகரத்தார்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:5"},"ud:89":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":89,"t":["கூற்றுரு வெய்தி யோடிக் கோட்டிடைக் குடர்க ளாடக்","காற்றென முழக்கி வேழங் கண்டமாந் தரைதத்ன் கையால்","நாற்பத்தெண் பேரைக் கொன்று நடுவுறப் பிளந்திட் டோடி","மாற்றருங் கோட்டை வாயின் மதிற்புறம் போந்த தன்றே."],"p":"","r":"In the shape of death, its tusks hung with entrails, it roars like the wind and kills forty-eight men with its trunk, splits the crowd through its middle and runs outside the wall to the unconquerable fort gate.","n":"The words கண்டமாந்தரை... தரைதத்ன் in line 2 look corrupt and the sense is partly guessed.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kolai","maram","kootru"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 89","lb":"UD89","mm":1,"ev":"The elephant kills forty-eight and breaks out toward the fort gate.","cl":[],"ag":["யானை"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:5"},"ud:90":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":90,"t":["அறுநூற்றின் மீதி லைம்ப தானநற் சேரி தானும்","உறுநூற்றி லேழை மாற வுள்ளநாற் பாடி யோடும்","நறுமலர்க் கந்தம் வீசு நன்குள காவு மற்றும்","பெறுமத யானை கோட்டாற் பெருநக ரழித்த தம்மா."],"p":"","r":"By its tusks the great elephant ruins the great city: so many streets of the six hundred and fifty, the seven hundred, the four wards and the fragrant gardens are wrecked.","n":"The numerals of lines 1-2 are garbled in the edition and cannot be added up.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maram","kedu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 90","lb":"UD90","mm":1,"ev":"The elephant's tusks wreck streets, wards and gardens.","cl":[],"ag":["யானை"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:5"},"ud:91":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":91,"t":["பாடுநன் மகளி ரெல்லாம் பாட்டொழிந் தரற்றி யோட","ஆடுநன் மாதர் தாமு மாடல்விட் டலந்து செல்லக்","கூடுநன் மங்கை மைந்தர் குலைந்தவ ரேகிச்செம்பொன்","மாடநன் மேனிலைப்பான் மன்னினார் பலரோ டாங்கே."],"p":"","r":"The singing women stop their song and run crying, the dancers leave the dance and wander off, the couples scatter, and many take shelter on the upper floors of the golden mansions.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["acham","soozhchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 91","lb":"UD91","mm":1,"ev":"Singers, dancers and couples flee to the upper storeys.","cl":[],"ag":["நகரத்தார்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:5"},"ud:92":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":92,"t":["மத்துறு கடலின் மிக்கு மறுகிய நகரத் தாரும்","வற்றிநல் வேந்த னோடு வினவினா ரமைச்ச ரெண்ணி","இத்தின நகரம் பட்ட விடரது விலக்க னல்ல","வத்தவன் கைய தென்ன வகுத்துரை கேட்ட மன்னன்."],"p":"","r":"The townspeople, churned like the sea by a churning stick, ask the king; the ministers consider and say the disaster of this city can not be turned aside, that it lies in Vatsa's hand, and the king hears the explanation.","n":"The ministers' point turns the captive into the only remedy of the harm; whether they know of Yugi's scheme is not said.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["amaichu","arasu","soozhchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 92","lb":"UD92","mm":1,"ev":"The citizens question the king and his ministers say only Udayanan can stop the elephant.","cl":[],"ag":["நகரத்தார்","அமைச்சர்","வேந்தன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:5"},"ud:93":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":93,"t":["போரினி னிற்க லாற்றாம் பொய்யினிற் றந்த மைந்தன்","சீரொடு சிறப்பும் வௌவிச் சிறையினில் வைத்த தன்றிப்","பேரிடிக் கரிமுன் விட்டாற் பெரும்பழி யாகு மென்று","தாருடை வேந்தன் சொல்லத் தரத்தினா லமைச்சர் சொல்வார்."],"p":"","r":"The king says he could not stand against him in battle, brought the son by a lie, took his glory and held him in prison, and that sending him before the thundering elephant would bring a great stain; the ministers then answer.","n":"The king admits that the capture was by a lie and that war had been beyond him; his fear is of blame, not of the wrong.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pazhi","arasu","vanjam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 93","lb":"UD93","mm":1,"ev":"The king hesitates to send his captive before the elephant, and the ministers reply.","cl":[["a king who sends a captive taken by deceit before a raging elephant earns great blame","general","reported",3,"ujjai_king"]],"ag":["வேந்தன்","அமைச்சர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["வேந்தன்","அமைச்சர்"],"ch":"ud:5"},"ud:94":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":94,"t":["இந்திர னானை தானு மிவன்கையா ழிசைக்கு மீறா","திந்திரன் வேழ முங்கேட் டேழடி செல்லு மற்றிக்","கந்திறு கைம்மா விக்கோன் கைவீணை கடவா தென்ன","மந்திரத் தவர்சொற் கேட்டு மன்னனப் படிச்செய் கின்றான்."],"p":"","r":"The ministers say that Indra's own elephant does not go beyond the sound of this man's lute, and that if it hears it goes but seven steps, so this tusked elephant cannot pass the king's vina; hearing the mantra-knowers' words, the king does as they say.","n":"The ministers' claim of the vina's mastery over elephants is a promise whose test comes in verse 97.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["amaichu","kalai","soozhchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 94","lb":"UD94","mm":1,"ev":"On the ministers' advice the king sends for Udayanan to play against the elephant.","cl":[],"ag":["அமைச்சர்","வேந்தன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:5"},"ud:95":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":95,"t":["சீவகன் வத்த வற்குச் செவ்விதிற் செப்பு கின்றான்","தேவ விந் நகரிடுக் கண் டீர்க்கைநின் கடன தாகும்","போவதுன் றேசத் தென்றல் புரவலன் கடன தாகும்","பூவல னுரைத்தா னென்னப் புகழ்ந்தவன் சிறை விடுத்தான்."],"p":"","r":"Civakan tells Vatsa straightly that this city's distress is seen and that to end it is his duty, that to go to his own land is the guardian's duty, and praising him as one that the flower-hand has spoken, he frees him from the prison.","n":"Civakan, the name Jivaka of the Cintamani, appears here with no explanation; the form may be an editor's slip or a second name of the Ujjain king, so the speaker is held with doubt.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["aram","arasu","eegai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 95","lb":"UD95","mm":1,"ev":"The speaker frees Udayanan after asking him to end the city's distress.","cl":[["a king's duty is to relieve a city's distress and to go back to his own land","general","reported",3,"civakan"]],"ag":["சீவகன்","உதயணன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்","உதயணன்"],"ch":"ud:5"},"ud:96":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":96,"t":["உருவுள சிவிகை யேறி யுயர்மன்னன் மனைபு குந்து","திருமயி ரெண்ணெ யிட்டுத் திறத்தினன் னீரு மாடி","மருவிநன் பட்டு டுத்து மணிக்கல னினிது தாங்கித்","தெருவிடைத் திகழப் புக்கான் றிருநகர் மகிழ் வன்றே."],"p":"","r":"The king takes a bodied palanquin to the palace, oils his hair, bathes with care, puts on silk and a fine jewel, and enters the street gleaming while the great city rejoices.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 96","lb":"UD96","mm":1,"ev":"Udayanan is freed, bathed and dressed, and rides into the street.","cl":[],"ag":["உதயணன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:5"},"ud:97":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":97,"t":["பருந்துபின் றொடர யானை பறவைகண் மற்றுஞ் சூழப்","பெருந்தெரு நடுவுட் டோன்றப் பீடுடைக் குமரன் றானும்","திருவலித் தடக்கை வீணை சீருடன் பாட லோடும்","மருவலிக் களிறுங் கேட்டு வந்தடி பணிந்த தம்மா."],"p":"","r":"With hawks following, elephants and birds around, the honoured prince appears in the middle of the great street, singing with his great hand on the vina, and the rutting elephant, hearing, comes and bows at his feet.","n":"The vina does what the mantra started and the guards could not undo; the claim of verse 94 holds.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalai","maram","kalai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 97","lb":"UD97","mm":1,"ev":"Udayanan plays the vina and the raging elephant comes and bows at his feet.","cl":[],"ag":["உதயணன்","யானை"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:5"},"ud:98":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":98,"t":["பிரிந்தநற் புதல்வர் வந்து பெற்றதந் தந்தை பாதம்","பரிந்தநற் காத லாலே பணிந்திடு மாறு போலே","இருந்துதற் பணிந்த யானை யெழின்மருப் படிவைத் தேறிப்","பெருந்தகை யேவிக் கோட்டு பெருங்கையாற் றோட்டி கொண்டான்."],"p":"","r":"The elephant kneels to Udayanan like sons long parted who come back and bow to their father's feet, and the great man, secure in his seat, takes up the goad in his big hand and drives it.","n":"The simile of sons returning to a father makes the animal's submission an act of love, not of force; the verse does not say which elephant this is, nor how it was brought to kneel.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["padai","anbu","aanmai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 98","lb":"UD98","mm":1,"ev":"The elephant bows down to Udayanan, who mounts, steadies himself and takes the goad.","cl":[],"ag":["உதயணன்","யானை"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:6"},"ud:99":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":99,"t":["வைத்தநன் மணியும் யாழும் வரிக்கயி றதுவு நீட்ட","வெற்றிநல் வேந்தன் வாங்கி வீக்கிமிக் கார்த்துக் கொண்டே","உற்றநல் வீதி தோறு மூர்ந்துநற் சாரி வட்டம்","பற்றிநன் கோட்டக் கண்டு பார்த்திபன் மகிழ்ச்சி கொண்டான்."],"p":"","r":"Udayanan takes the gem, the lute and the striped rope that are held out, ties them on, rides the elephant along every street in circles, and the king who watches him is glad.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aanmai","pugazh","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 99","lb":"UD99","mm":1,"ev":"Udayanan, riding the elephant through the streets in circles, delights the king of Ujjain.","cl":[],"ag":["உதயணன்","பிரத்தியோதன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:6"},"ud:100":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":100,"t":["பிடிப்புப்பொன் விலைமட்டில்லாப் பெருவலி யாரந் தன்னை","முடிப்புவி யரச னீய மொய்ம்பனு மணிந்து கொண்டு","கொடிப்புலி முகத்து வாயிற் கோட்டையுட் கொண்டு வந்தான்","இடிக்குரற் சீய மொப்ப விலங்கிய குமரன் றானே."],"p":"","r":"The king of Ujjain gives a priceless pearl necklace, which the strong youth puts on, and he rides into the fort gate under the tiger banner like a roaring lion.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","vendhan","perumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 100","lb":"UD100","mm":1,"ev":"The king gives Udayanan a pearl necklace; Udayanan wears it and enters the fort on the elephant.","cl":[],"ag":["பிரத்தியோதன்","உதயணன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:6"},"ud:101":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":101,"t":["சால்கவென் றிறைவன் செப்பத் தன்னுடைக் கையி னோச்சிக்","கால்களின் விரலி னெற்றி கனக்கநன் கூன்றி நின்று","மால்கரி கால் கொடுப்ப மன்னனு மகிழ்ந்து போந்து","வேல்கவின் வேந்தன் காண வியந்துடன் தழுவிக் கொண்டான்."],"p":"","r":"At the king's word that it is enough, Udayanan raises his hand, braces his toes on the elephant's brow, and makes it give its legs; the king, pleased, embraces him.","n":"The only words spoken are the king's brief 'enough', which ends the display.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aanmai","vendhan","anbu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 101","lb":"UD101","mm":1,"ev":"At the king's command Udayanan makes the elephant kneel, and the king embraces him.","cl":[],"ag":["உதயணன்","யானை","பிரத்தியோதன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["பிரத்தியோதன்","உதயணன்"],"ch":"ud:6"},"ud:102":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":102,"t":["மருமக னீயே யென்று மன்னவ னினிமை கூறி","வருமுறை நயந்து கொண்டு மகிழ்ந்துட னிருந்த போழ்தில்","திருமகள் கனவு கூறிச் செல்வநீ கற்பி யென்னப்","பெருவலி யுரைப்பக் கேட்டுப் பெருமக னுணர்த்த லானான்."],"p":"","r":"The king calls Udayanan his son-in-law, speaks kindly, and as they sit in pleasure a lady tells of a dream and asks him to teach, whereupon the great man agrees to instruct.","n":"மருமகன் may be a courtesy of the king's here and not a settled marriage; later verses still speak of Vasavadatta as a pupil.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kalvi","vaazhkkaithunai","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 102","lb":"UD102","mm":1,"ev":"The king calls Udayanan his son-in-law; a lady reports a dream and Udayanan agrees to teach.","cl":[],"ag":["பிரத்தியோதன்","திருமகள்","உதயணன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["பிரத்தியோதன்","உதயணன்"],"ch":"ud:6"},"ud:103":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":103,"t":["வேந்தன்றன் மக்கட் கெல்லாம் வேன்முதல் பயிற்று வித்தும்","பூந்துகில் செறிம ருங்குற் பொருகயற் கண்ணி வேய்த்தோள்","வாய்ந்தவா சவதத் தைக்கு வருவித்தும் வீணை தன்னைச்","சேர்ந்தவ ணிகரி லின்பிற் செல்வனு மகிழ்வுற் றானே."],"p":"","r":"The king has weapons taught to all his sons, and has the slender-waisted Vasavadatta brought to learn the lute, and the lord is glad.","n":"The verse does not say who teaches whom the lute; the sense follows the preceding verse where Udayanan agrees to instruct.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kalvi","vendhan","makkal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 103","lb":"UD103","mm":1,"ev":"The king has his sons trained in arms and brings Vasavadatta to learn the lute from Udayanan.","cl":[],"ag":["பிரத்தியோதன்","உதயணன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:6"},"ud:104":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":104,"t":["உரையினி லரிய னாய வுதயண குமர னோர்நாள்","அரசிளங் குமரர் வித்தை யண்ணனீ காண்க வென்ன","வரைநிகர் யானை யூர்ந்து மாவுடன் றேரி லேறி","வரிசையிற் காட்டி வாள்வில் வகையுடன் விளக்கக் கண்டான்."],"p":"","r":"The young princes ask 'elder brother' Udayanan to watch their skill, and ride elephants, horses and chariots and show him their ways with sword and bow in order, which he watches.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalvi","aanmai","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 104","lb":"UD104","mm":1,"ev":"The princes of Ujjain display their riding and weapon skills before Udayanan.","cl":[],"ag":["அரசிளங் குமரர்","உதயணன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["அரசிளங் குமரர்","உதயணன்"],"ch":"ud:6"},"ud:105":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":105,"t":["வாசவ தத்தை வந்து மன்னனை யிறைஞ்ச நல்யாழ்","பேசவை தளரக் கேட்டுப் பெருமக னினிய னாகி","ஆசிலா வித்தை யெல்லா மாயிழை கொண்டா ளென்றே","ஏசவன் சிறைசெய் குற்ற மெண்ணுறேல் பெருக்க வென்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"Vasavadatta comes and bows to the king with her lute; hearing it falter, the great man is kind, saying the lady has all faultless arts, and she asks that the fault of imprisoning him not be counted.","n":"Line 4 is the first mention in the chapter of Udayanan's imprisonment as a wrong to be forgiven, and who asks whom is not clear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kalvi","porai","anbu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 105","lb":"UD105","mm":1,"ev":"Vasavadatta bows to the king, and she speaks of the fault of Udayanan's imprisonment.","cl":[],"ag":["வாசவதத்தை","உதயணன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["வாசவதத்தை","பிரத்தியோதன்"],"ch":"ud:6"},"ud:106":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":106,"t":["விசும்பியல் குமரர் தாமும் வியந்துட னிருப்பப் புல்லும்","பசும்பொனி னிலத்தில் வீழப் பாவையர் மயக்க முற்றார்","வசம்படக் குறுக்கி நீட்டி வரிசையிற் பாட லோடும்","அசும்பறாக் கடாத்து வேழத் தரசனு மகிழ்ந்தா னன்றே."],"p":"","r":"The princes watch amazed while Udayanan, singing by the rules and drawing the strings short and long, makes the girls fall in a swoon on the golden floor, and the elephant-riding king rejoices.","n":"Who plays the lute here is not named; the sense follows the lines 3 to 4 and the preceding performance.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kalvi","kaamam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 106","lb":"UD106","mm":1,"ev":"Udayanan sings and plays; the women faint, and the king is pleased.","cl":[],"ag":["உதயணன்","பிரத்தியோதன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:6"},"ud:107":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":107,"t":["வத்தவன் கையைப் பற்றி மன்னவ னினிது கூறி","வத்தவ னோலை தன்னுள் வளமையிற் புள்ளி யிட்டும்","வத்தவ நாட்டுக் கேற வள்ளலைப் போக வென்ன","வத்தவநாளை யென்றே மறையவர் முகிழ்த்த மிட்டார்."],"p":"","r":"The king takes the Vatsa lord's hand and speaks kindly, writes on a palm-leaf the royal marks of the Vatsa land, and tells him to go there; the brahmins name the next day as the hour.","n":"The king here lets him go openly, yet verse 109 turns to Yugi's trick; the poem does not reconcile the two, and the last line is hard.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vendhan","naadu","thoothu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 107","lb":"UD107","mm":1,"ev":"The king dismisses Udayanan to Vatsa with a written grant, and the brahmins fix the next day for departure.","cl":[],"ag":["பிரத்தியோதன்","மறையவர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:6"},"ud:108":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":108,"t":["ஓரிரண் டாயி ரங்க ளோடைதாழ் மத்த யானை","ஈரிரண் டாயி ரங்க ளெழின்மணிப் பொன்னின் றேரும்","போரியல் புரவி மானம் பொருவிலை யாயி ரம்மும்","வீரர்க ளிலக்கம் பேரும் வீறுநற் குமரற் கீந்தான்."],"p":"","r":"The king gives the young lord two thousand rutting elephants, four thousand jewelled golden chariots, a thousand war horses and a very large number of soldiers.","n":"The numbers are as the lines print them; line 3 is dense and the horse count is read only roughly.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["eegai","vendhan","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 108","lb":"UD108","mm":1,"ev":"The king gives Udayanan a great army.","cl":[],"ag":["பிரத்தியோதன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:6"},"ud:109":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":109,"t":["யூகியும் வஞ்சந் தன்னை யுற்றுச்சூள் வழாமை நோக்கி","வாகுடன் குறத்தி வேடம் வகுத்தனன் குறிகள் கூற்றாம்","நாகத்தி னகர ழிந்த நடுக்கங்க டீர வெண்ணிப்","போகநன் னீரி லாடப் புரத்தினி லினிது ரைத்தான்."],"p":"","r":"Yugi, holding to his sworn vow, takes a trick and the guise of a fortune-telling woman, and tells the city in pleasant words to go and bathe in the water in order to ease the fear of the trembling beast.","n":"Yugi's deceit is done to keep a vow, not for his own gain, and the poem does not blame it; the sense of line 3 is uncertain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vanjam","soozhchi","thunai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 109","lb":"UD109","mm":1,"ev":"Yugi, disguised as a kuratti, sets a stratagem and tells the city to go bathing.","cl":[],"ag":["யூகி"],"ec":[],"spk":["யூகி",""],"ch":"ud:6"},"ud:110":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":110,"t":["மன்னவன் றன்னோ டெண்ணி மாநகர் திரண்டு சென்று","துன்னிய நீர்க்க யத்திற் றொல்புரப் புறத்தி லாட","நன்னெறி வத்த வன்றான் னன்பிடி யேறி நிற்ப","உன்னிய யூகி மிக்க வூரிற்றீ யிடுவித் தானே."],"p":"","r":"The king, after consulting, goes with the city to the pool outside the old town to bathe; Udayanan stands on a good she-elephant, and Yugi has the town set alight.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vanjam","soozhchi","acham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 110","lb":"UD110","mm":1,"ev":"The city goes to bathe; Yugi sets fire to Ujjain while Udayanan waits on the she-elephant.","cl":[],"ag":["பிரத்தியோதன்","உதயணன்","யூகி","பிடி"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:6"},"ud:111":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":111,"t":["பயந்துதீக் கண்டு சேனை பார்த்திபன் றன்னோ டேக","வயந்தகன் வந்து ரைப்ப வத்தவ குமரன் றானும்","நயந்துகோன் மகளை மிக்க நன்பிடி யேற்றத் தோழி","கயந்தனை விட்டு வந்த காஞ்சனை யேறி னாளே."],"p":"","r":"Frightened at the fire, the army goes off with the king; Vayantakan comes and tells Udayanan, who gladly lifts the king's daughter onto the she-elephant, and her friend Kanchanai mounts too.","n":"Whether Vasavadatta goes willingly is not said; the verse says only that Udayanan 'gladly' lifts her.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["acham","kaamam","thunai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 111","lb":"UD111","mm":1,"ev":"The fire scatters the court; Udayanan seats Vasavadatta on the she-elephant and Kanchanai joins her.","cl":[],"ag":["பிரத்தியோதன்","வயந்தகன்","உதயணன்","காஞ்சனை","வாசவதத்தை"],"ec":[],"spk":["வயந்தகன்","உதயணன்"],"ch":"ud:6"},"ud:112":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":112,"t":["வயந்தகன் வீணை கொண்டு வன்பிடி யேறிப் பின்னைச்","செயந்தரக் கரிணி காதிற் செல்வன்மந் திரத்தைச் செப்ப","வியந்துபஞ் சவனந் தாண்ட வேயொடு பற்ற வீணை","வயந்தகன் கூற மன்னன் மாப்பிடி நிற்க வென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"Vayantakan takes his lute and mounts the strong she-elephant, the lord speaks a mantra in her ear, and as they cross the Panchavanam forest the lord tells her to halt.","n":"The mantra of line 2 is not named and its use here is as a charm for steadiness, not the dying mantra of verse 114.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["mandhiram","soozhchi","thunai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 112","lb":"UD112","mm":1,"ev":"Udayanan whispers a mantra to the she-elephant; Vayantakan rides; the elephant is made to stop.","cl":[],"ag":["வயந்தகன்","உதயணன்","பிடி"],"ec":[],"spk":["உதயணன்","பிடி"],"ch":"ud:6"},"ud:113":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":113,"t":["நலமிகு புகழார் மன்ன நாலிரு நூற்று வில்லு","நிலமிகக் கடந்த தென்ன நீர்மையிற் றந்ததெய்வம்","நலமிகத் தருமின் றென்ன பண்ணுகை நம்மா லென்னக்","குலமிகு குமரன் செல்லக் குஞ்சர மசைந்த தன்றே."],"p":"","r":"The lines hail a noble king and say that the elephant has crossed a great stretch of eight hundred bows' length of land, which the god gave, and as the prince goes on the elephant begins to waver.","n":"The measure in lines 1 to 2 and the speech in lines 2 to 3 are hard, and the speaker is not named.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["acham","oozh","soozhchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 113","lb":"UD113","mm":1,"ev":"The prince rides on and the elephant begins to give way.","cl":[],"ag":["உதயணன்","பிடி"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:6"},"ud:114":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":114,"t":["அசைந்தநற் பிடியைக் கண்டே யசலித மனத்த ராகி","இசைந்தவரிழிந்த பின்னை யிருநில மீதில் வீழத்","தசைந்தகை யுதிரம் பாயச் சாலமந் திரமங் காதில்","இசைந்தவர் சொல்லக் கேட்டே யின்புறத் தேவா யிற்றே."],"p":"","r":"Seeing the she-elephant sink, the riders get down troubled; she falls to the earth bleeding, and as the great mantra is spoken into her ear she joyfully becomes a god.","n":"The verse says only 'mantra' and does not name the five-fold salutation by which Jain accounts of such a death are known.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["mandhiram","piravi","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 114","lb":"UD114","mm":1,"ev":"The exhausted she-elephant falls and dies; the riders speak the mantra in her ear and she is reborn a god.","cl":[["a dying creature that hears the sacred mantra is reborn as a god","jain","asserted",4,"narrator"]],"ag":["பிடி","ஏறியவர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:6"},"ud:115":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":115,"t":["உவளகத் திறங்கிச் சென்றே யூர்நிலத் தருகு செல்லப்","பவளக்கொப் புளங்கள் பாவை பஞ்சிமெல் லடியிற் றோன்றத்","தவளைக்கிண் கிணிகண் மிக்க தரத்தினாற் பேச லின்றித்","துவளிடை யருகின் மேவுந் தோழிதோள் பற்றிச் செல்வாள்."],"p":"","r":"The lady gets down and walks toward the nearby ground, her feet, soft as cotton, blistered red like coral, her anklets silent in her weariness, and she goes holding her friend's shoulder.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["idukkan","azhagu","anbu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 115","lb":"UD115","mm":1,"ev":"Vasavadatta, footsore, walks on leaning on her friend's shoulder.","cl":[],"ag":["வாசவதத்தை","காஞ்சனை"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:6"},"ud:116":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":116,"t":["பாவைதன் வருத்தங் கண்டு பார்த்திபன் பாங்கி னோங்கும்","பூவைவண் டரற்றுங் காவுட் பூம்பொய்கை கண்டி ருப்ப","வாவுநாற் படையுங் கொண்டு வயந்தகன் வருவே னென்றான்","போவதே பொருளூர்க் கென்று புரவல னுரைப்பப் போந்தான்."],"p":"","r":"Udayanan sees the lady's distress and the lotus pool in the grove; Vayantakan says he will come back with the four-fold army, the king tells him to go to the city, and he goes.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thoothu","padai","thunai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 116","lb":"UD116","mm":1,"ev":"Vayantakan goes to bring the army while Udayanan stays with the weary Vasavadatta by the pool.","cl":[],"ag":["உதயணன்","வயந்தகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["வயந்தகன்","உதயணன்"],"ch":"ud:6"},"ud:117":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":117,"t":["சூரியன் குடபாற் சென்று குடவரை சொருகக் கண்டு","நாரியைத் தோழி கூட நன்மையிற் றுயில்க வென்று","வீரிய னிரவு தன்னில் விழித்துட னிருந்த போழ்து","சூரிய னுதயஞ் செய்யத் தொக்குடன் புளிஞர் சூழ்ந்தார்."],"p":"","r":"At sunset Udayanan bids the lady sleep with her friend and stays awake through the night; at sunrise the hunters gather round them.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["anbu","acham","aanmai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 117","lb":"UD117","mm":1,"ev":"Udayanan keeps watch through the night; at dawn the hunters surround him.","cl":[],"ag":["உதயணன்","புளிஞர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["உதயணன்","வாசவதத்தை"],"ch":"ud:6"},"ud:118":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":118,"t":["வந்தவ ரம்பு மாரி வள்ளன்மேற் றூவத் தானும்","தந்தனு மேவிச் சாராத் தரத்தினால் விலக்கிப் பின்னும்","வெந்திறல் வேடர் வின்னாண் வெந் நுனைப் பகழி வீழ","நந்திய சிலைவ ளைத்து நன்பிறை யம்பி னெய்தான்."],"p":"","r":"The hunters rain arrows on the generous lord, who wards them off with his bow and, as their cruel arrows keep falling, bends his great bow and answers with crescent arrows.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aanmai","anjaamai","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 118","lb":"UD118","mm":1,"ev":"The hunters shoot at Udayanan; he defends himself and shoots back with crescent-headed arrows.","cl":[],"ag":["புளிஞர்","உதயணன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:6"},"ud:119":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":119,"t":["செய்வகை யின்றி வேடர் தீவனங் கொளுத்த மன்னன்","உய்வகை யுங்க ளுக்கின் றுறுபொரு ளீவ னென்ன","ஐவகை யடிசில் கொண்டே யானநாற் படையுஞ் சூழ","மெய்வகை வயந்த கன்றான் வீறமைந் தினிதின் வந்தான்."],"p":"","r":"Helpless, the hunters set fire to the wood; the king says he will give them wealth to live on, and Vayantakan arrives well turned out with five kinds of food and the four-fold army.","n":"The offer to the hunters follows their defeat, so the gift is a settlement and not a ransom; whether they accept is not said here.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","virundhu","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 119","lb":"UD119","mm":1,"ev":"The hunters fire the forest; Udayanan promises them wealth; Vayantakan arrives with food and the army.","cl":[],"ag":["புளிஞர்","உதயணன்","வயந்தகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["உதயணன்","புளிஞர்"],"ch":"ud:6"},"ud:120":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":120,"t":["அன்புறு மடிசி லுண்டே யற்றைநாளங்கி ருந்தார்","இன்புறு மற்றை நாளி னெழிற்களிற் றரசனேற","நன்புறச் சிவிகை யேற நங்கைநாற் படையுஞ் சூழப்","பண்புறு சயந்தி புக்குப் பார்த்திப னினிதி ருந்தான்."],"p":"","r":"They eat the food gladly and stay that day; next morning the king mounts the fine elephant, the lady rides a palanquin with the army about them, and they enter Jayanti where the king sits at ease.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["virundhu","vendhan","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 120","lb":"UD120","mm":1,"ev":"The party eats, rests and travels on to the city of Jayanti.","cl":[],"ag":["உதயணன்","வாசவதத்தை"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:6"},"ud:121":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":121,"t":["உஞ்சைநகர் விட்டகன் றுதயணகு மாரனும்","தஞ்சமாய்ச் சயந்தியிற் றளர்வின்றிப் புகுந்தபின்","என்செய்தன னென்றிடி னியம்புதும் மறியவே","கொஞ்சுபைங் கிளிமொழிதன் கூடலை விரும்பினான்."],"p":"","r":"The poet turns to what Udayanan did after reaching Jayanti, having left Ujjain, and says he gave himself to his love for the parrot-voiced lady.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 121","lb":"UD121","mm":1,"ev":"Udayanan, settled at Jayanti, turns to enjoying Vasavadatta.","cl":[],"ag":["உதயணன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:7"},"ud:122":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":122,"t":["இலங்கிழைநன் மாதரை யினிமைவேள்வித் தன்மையால்","நலங்கொளப்பு ணர்ந்தனன் நாகநற் புணர்ச்சிபோல்","புலங்களின்மி குத்தபோகம் பொற்புடன் னுகர்ந்தனன்","அலங்கலணி வேலினா னன்புமிகக் கூரினான்."],"p":"","r":"He joins the bright-jewelled lady in the sweet manner of a sacrifice and enjoys the great pleasures of the senses with her, his love growing.","n":"The comparison of union to a sacrifice (வேள்வி) and to the union of the serpent-folk is the poet's own image for the richness of the pleasure; the verse does not blame it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 122","lb":"UD122","mm":1,"ev":"Udayanan enjoys the pleasures of union with Vasavadatta.","cl":[],"ag":["உதயணன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:7"},"ud:123":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":123,"t":["கைம்மிகுகா மங்கரை காண்கிலன் னழுந்தலில்","ஐம்மிகும் கணைமத னம்புமீக் குளிப்பவும்","பைம்மிகும்பொ னல்குலாள் படாமுலை புணையென","மைம்மிகுங் களி ற்றரசன் மாரன்கட னீந்துவான்."],"p":"","r":"Unable to see the shore of his desire, the elephant-lord sinks in it, and the god of love's arrows plunge him deeper, until he swims the sea of desire with the lady's breast for a raft.","n":"Desire is drawn as a sea he cannot cross; the raft that keeps him afloat is the very thing that holds him in it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","inbam","kaamanoy"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 123","lb":"UD123","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["உதயணன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:7"},"ud:124":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":124,"t":["இழந்ததன் னிலத்தையும் மெளிமையுந் நினைத்திலன்","கழிந்தறமு மெய்ம்மறந்து கங்குலும் பகல் விடான்","அழிந்தியன்பிற் புல்லியே யரிவையுடை நன்னலம்","விழுந்தவண் மயக்கத்தில் வேந்தனினிச் செல்கின்றான்."],"p":"","r":"He no longer thinks of the land he lost or of his lowly state, forgets virtue and his own self, lets neither night nor day go, and the king sinks, fallen in the lady's charms and bewildered.","n":"The poet blames the king's forgetting directly (மறந்து, விழுந்து), and this is what the minister's later plan answers; it is the poet's judgement, not the lover's own.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pochchaappu","kaamam","aram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 124","lb":"UD124","mm":1,"ev":"Udayanan, absorbed in Vasavadatta, forgets his lost kingdom and his duty.","cl":[["a king who sinks in pleasure forgets his lost land and virtue","general","asserted",2,"narrator"]],"ag":["உதயணன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:7"},"ud:125":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":125,"t":["ஒழுகுங்காலை யூகியா முயிரினுஞ் சிறந்தவன்","எழில்பெருகுஞ் சூழ்ச்சிக்க ணினியதன் வரவதாற்","பழுதின்றிச் சிறைவிடுத்துப் பாங்குபுகழ் வத்தவன்","எழின்மங்கை யிளம்பிடி யேற்றியேகக் கண்டவன்."],"p":"","r":"While the king goes on like this, Yugi, dearer than life, looks on at the departure of Vatsa, freed from prison by Yugi's own scheme, with the fair lady on the young she-elephant.","n":"Lines 1 and 4 can be read as Yugi watching the flight or as the poet recalling it; the grammar of the verse is loose.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["natpu","soozhchi","thunai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 125","lb":"UD125","mm":1,"ev":"Yugi, having freed the king from prison by his scheme, watches him ride off with the lady.","cl":[],"ag":["யூகி","உதயணன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:7"},"ud:126":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":126,"t":["மிஞ்சிநெஞ்சி லன்புடன் மீண்டுவர வெண்ணினன்","உஞ்சைநகர்க் கரசன்கேட் டுள்ளகத் தழுங்கினன்","விஞ்சுபடை மேலெழாமை விரகுட னறிந்தந்த","உஞ்சையெல்லை விட்டுவந்து யூகிபுட்ப கஞ்சென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"Yugi, thinking lovingly of returning, hears that the king of Ujjain is grieving inwardly, learns by stratagem that the great army has not risen, leaves Ujjain's bounds and goes to Pushpakam.","n":"The king of Ujjain is named only by title (உஞ்சைநகர்க் கரசன்); his grief at the escape is told, not shown.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["soozhchi","thunai","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 126","lb":"UD126","mm":1,"ev":"Yugi, learning that Ujjain's army has not moved, leaves its border and goes to Pushpakam.","cl":[],"ag":["யூகி","பிரத்தியோதன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:7"},"ud:127":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":127,"t":["இடபகற்குத் தன்னுரை யினிதுவைத்து ரைத்துப்பொன்","முடியுடைய நம்மரசன் முயற்சியது வென்னென","பிடிமிசை வருகையிற் பெருநிலங் கழிந்தபின்","அடியிட விடம்பொறாமை யானைமண்ணிற் சாய்ந்ததே."],"p":"","r":"Yugi tells Idabakan his own purpose and asks what effort is now fitting for their golden-crowned king; and as he comes on a she-elephant and has crossed a wide land, the elephant cannot bear to step and falls to the ground.","n":"Lines 3 to 4 seem to recall the she-elephant's fall already told in verse 114, mixed into Yugi's talk; the verse's order of events is not clear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["amaichu","soozhchi","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 127","lb":"UD127","mm":1,"ev":"Yugi confides his plan to Idabakan; the she-elephant on which the king rides collapses.","cl":[],"ag":["யூகி","பிடி"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:7"},"ud:128":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":128,"t":["சவரர்தாம் வளைந்ததும் தாமவரை வென்றதும்","உவமையில் வயந்தகன்ற னூர்வந்துடன் போந்ததும்","தவளவெண் கொடிமிடை சயந்தியிற் புகுந்ததும்","குவிமுலைநற் கோதையன்பு கூர்ந்துடன் புணர்ந்ததும்."],"p":"","r":"A list of what befell: the hunters closed round them, the king beat them, he went with the matchless Vayantakan, entered Jayanti with its white flags, and joined the lady in love.","n":"Only a string of nouns in -ததும்; who recites the list, the poet or Yugi, is not stated.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["padai","aanmai","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 128","lb":"UD128","mm":1,"ev":"The verse recalls the hunters' siege, the king's victory and arrival at Jayanti.","cl":[],"ag":["உதயணன்","புளிஞர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:7"},"ud:129":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":129,"t":["இழந்தபூமி யெண்ணில னினியபோகத் தழுந்தலும்","குழைந்தவ னுரைப்பயூகி கூரெயிறி லங்கநக்கு","விழைந்தவேந்தன் றேவியை விரகினாற் பிரித்திடின்","இழந்தமிக் கரசியல்கை கூடுமென வெண்ணினான்."],"p":"","r":"Hearing that the king, absorbed in pleasure, thinks nothing of the land he lost, Yugi smiles, his teeth shining, and decides that if the king is parted from his queen by a trick, the lost kingdom will come back to his hand.","n":"Yugi's reasoning is given as his own thought (எண்ணினான்); the poem has not yet shown whether the plan is wise.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["soozhchi","vanjam","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 129","lb":"UD129","mm":1,"ev":"Yugi plans to part the king from the queen by stratagem in order to win back the kingdom.","cl":[["a king's lost rule is recovered only if he is drawn out of his pleasure by stratagem","general","reported",4,"yugi"]],"ag":["யூகி"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:7"},"ud:130":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":130,"t":["சாங்கிய மகளெனுந் தபசினியைக் கண்டுடன்","ஆங்கவ ளறியக்கூறி யானயூகி தன்னுயிர்","நீங்கினது போலவு நின்றமைச்சர் மூவரும்","பாங்கரசன் ரூபமும் படத்தினில் வரைந்தனன்."],"p":"","r":"Yugi meets a woman ascetic of the Sankhya school, tells her what to say, and, as if his own life had left him, paints on a cloth the three ministers and the king's form.","n":"The word சாங்கிய is used only to name the woman's order; the verse states no Sankhya doctrine.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["soozhchi","vanjam","thavam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 130","lb":"UD130","mm":1,"ev":"Yugi instructs a Sankhya woman ascetic and has a portrait made of the king and his ministers.","cl":[],"ag":["யூகி","தபசினி"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:7"},"ud:131":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":131,"t":["படத்துருவி லொன்றினைப் பரந்தமேற்கண் ணாகவைத்","திடக்கணீக்கி யிட்டுமிக் கியல்புடன் கொடுத்துடன்","முடிக்கரசற் கறிவியென்ன முதுமகளும் போயினள்","இடிக்குரனற் சீயமா மிறைவனையே கண்டனள்."],"p":"","r":"One figure in the portrait is set with the upper eye wide and the left one removed; with this and a message to be made known to the crowned king, the old woman goes and sees the lion-voiced lord.","n":"Lines 1 to 2 on the painted eye are hard to parse; what the missing eye signals is not clear from the verse.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["soozhchi","vanjam","thoothu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 131","lb":"UD131","mm":1,"ev":"The old woman takes the portrait to Udayanan.","cl":[],"ag":["தபசினி","உதயணன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:7"},"ud:132":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":132,"t":["வேந்தனுங்கண் டேவிரும்பி வினயஞ்செய் திருக்கெனப்","பாந்தவக் கிழவியும் பண்பினிய சொல்லிப்பின்","சேந்தநின் சிறைவிடுத்த செல்வயூகி நின்னுடன்","போந்துபின் வராததென்ன புரவலநீ கூறென்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"The king sees her and, wishing to honour her, bids her sit; the kinswoman speaks kindly and then asks why Yugi, who freed him from prison, has not come back with him, and bids him tell.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","thoothu","thunai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 132","lb":"UD132","mm":1,"ev":"The old woman asks Udayanan why Yugi did not return with him.","cl":[],"ag":["உதயணன்","தபசினி"],"ec":[],"spk":["தபசினி","உதயணன்"],"ch":"ud:7"},"ud:133":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":133,"t":["அவனுரை யறிந்தில னறிந்தநீ யுரைக்கெனத்","தவிசிடை யிருந்தவ டான்படத்தைக் காட்டினள்","புவியரசன் கண்டுடன் புலம்பிமிக வாடிப்பின்","தவமலி முனிவனைத் தான்வணங்கிக் கேட்டனன்."],"p":"","r":"The king has no word of Yugi, so the seated woman shows him the portrait; the king sees it, laments and grieves much, and then bows to a sage rich in penance and asks him.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","thunai","thavam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 133","lb":"UD133","mm":1,"ev":"The old woman shows the portrait; Udayanan laments, then bows to a sage and asks him.","cl":[],"ag":["தபசினி","உதயணன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:7"},"ud:134":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":134,"t":["முடிமுத லரசினோடு முனிவறநின் றுணைவனை","வடிவுடன் பெறுவையென்ன வன்மையிற் றேறிமீக்","கடிகமழச் சாரலிற் கண்டமாத வன்மகள்","துடியிடை விரிசிகையைத் தோன்றன்மாலை சூட்டினான்."],"p":"","r":"The sage assures him firmly that he will regain his crowned rule and his companion in bodily form; the king, trusting this, garlands the slender-waisted Virisikai, the sage's daughter he sees in the scented hill-slope.","n":"The verse does not make clear whether the garlanding is part of the sage's counsel or a separate act; the prophecy is told by the poet, in the sage's words only in sense.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thavam","kaamam","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 134","lb":"UD134","mm":1,"ev":"A sage foretells Udayanan's return of rule and friend; Udayanan then garlands the sage's daughter Virisikai.","cl":[],"ag":["உதயணன்","முனிவன்","விரிசிகை"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:7"},"ud:135":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":135,"t":["கலந்தன னிருந்தபின் கானகத் தழைதர","நலந்திகழ்நன் மாதர்செப்ப நரபதியும் போயினன்","கலந்திகழும் யூகியுங் காவலன் றன் றேவியைச்","சிலதினம் பிரிவிக்கச் சிந்தைகூரத் தோன்றினான்."],"p":"","r":"He unites with her, and when she is called away to the forest by her friends and the king goes, Yugi, in his mind, appears to the king to part the queen from him for some days.","n":"The last line is not clear; it can be read as Yugi's thought of parting the queen or as his appearance for that purpose.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","soozhchi","vanjam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 135","lb":"UD135","mm":1,"ev":"After the union, Udayanan leaves, and Yugi sets about parting the king from the queen for some days.","cl":[],"ag":["உதயணன்","யூகி"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:7"},"ud:136":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":136,"t":["மன்னவன் மனைதனின் மறைந்திருக்கும் மாந்தரைத்","துன்னுநன் கிருவரைத் தொக்குட னிருக்கவென்று","மன்னன்மனை தன்மனைக்கு மாநிலச் சுருங்கைசெய்","தன்னவன் மனைமுழுது மறைந்தவர் தீயிட்டனர்."],"p":"","r":"Those hidden in the king's house are told to stay together, and having made an underground tunnel from the king's house to the queen's, they set the whole house on fire.","n":"Who gives the order and who the hidden men are is not said; the verse gives only the tunnel and the fire, which prepare the false death in the next verses.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vanjam","soozhchi","thunai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 136","lb":"UD136","mm":1,"ev":"The men hidden in the king's house, who have a tunnel dug below, set it alight.","cl":[],"ag":["மறைந்தவர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:8"},"ud:137":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":137,"t":["நிலந்திகழ் சுருங்கையி னீதிமன்னன் றேவியை","இலங்குசாங்கி யம்மக ளெழில்பெறக் கொண்டுவந்","தலங்கலணி வேலினா னமைச்சன் மனைசேர்த்தனள்","துலங்கிவந் தடிபரவிச் சொல்லினிது சொல்லினான்."],"p":"","r":"Through the underground passage the Sankhya woman brings the just king's queen, finely adorned, to the minister's house, and the minister bows at her feet and speaks gently.","n":"The minister is not named; the story so far makes him Yugi, but the verse does not say so.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vanjam","thunai","panivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 137","lb":"UD137","mm":1,"ev":"The Sankhya woman brings the queen through the tunnel to the minister's house; the minister bows to her and speaks kindly.","cl":[],"ag":["சாங்கி","யூகி"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:8"},"ud:138":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":138,"t":["என்னுடைநற் றாயேநீ யெனக்கொரு வரங்கொடு","நின்னரச னின்னைவிட்டு நீங்குஞ்சில நாளன்றி","நன்னில மடந்தைநமக் காகுவது மில்லையே","என்னவுடன் பட்டன ளியல்புடன் கரந்தனன்."],"p":"","r":"The minister calls her his own good mother and asks a boon: her king will be away from her only some days, and without it the land will not be theirs; she agrees and he hides her as the case requires.","n":"Both speaker and addressee are known only from the story; line 2 may also be read as the minister speaking for the queen to the Sankhya woman.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vanjam","soozhchi","panivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 138","lb":"UD138","mm":1,"ev":"The minister asks the queen to bear a short parting and she agrees, and he hides her.","cl":[],"ag":["யூகி","வாசவதத்தை"],"ec":[],"spk":["யூகி","வாசவதத்தை"],"ch":"ud:8"},"ud:139":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":139,"t":["சவரர்வந்து தீயிட்டெனத் தஞ்செயலி னாக்கிமிக்","கவகுறிகள் கண்டரச னன்பிற்றேவிக் கேதமென்","றுவளகத் தழுங்கிவந் துற்றகரு மஞ்சொலக்","கவற்சியுட் கதறியே கலங்கிமன்னன் வீழ்ந்தனன்."],"p":"","r":"On the hunters' fire seeming to be their own deed, the king sees evil omens, learns that the queen is hurt, and falls down, grieving and shaken, when the minister tells what has happened.","n":"Line 1 puts the burning on the hunters; the verse does not say who tells the king that it was they, only that he is told.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","vanjam","alar"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 139","lb":"UD139","mm":1,"ev":"The king, told that the queen is lost in the fire, collapses in grief.","cl":[],"ag":["உதயணன்","சவரர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:8"},"ud:140":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":140,"t":["பூண்டமார்ப னன்னிலம் புரண்டுமிக் கெழுந்துபோய்","மாண்டதேவி தன்னுடன் மரித்திடுவ னானென்றான்","நீண்டதோ ளமைச்சரு நின்றரசற் பற்றியே","வேண்டித்தா னுடனிருந்த வெந்தவுடல் காட்டென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"The king rolls on the ground and rises saying he will die with the dead queen, and the tall ministers hold him and beg him; he asks to be shown her burnt body.","n":"The line 2 words are the king's, but the verse gives only the report and not the speech itself.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","anbu","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 140","lb":"UD140","mm":1,"ev":"Udayanan resolves to die with the queen; ministers restrain him, and he asks to see the burnt body.","cl":[],"ag":["உதயணன்","அமைச்சர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["உதயணன்",""],"ch":"ud:8"},"ud:141":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":141,"t":["கரிப்பிணத்தைக் காண்கிலர் காவலர்க ளென்றபின்","எரிப்பொன்னணி காட்டென வெடுத்துமுன்பு வைத்தனர்","நெருப்பிடை விழுந்தமை நினைப்ப மாயமன்றென","விருப்புடைநற் றேவிக்கு வேந்தன்மிக் கரற்றுவான்."],"p":"","r":"When the guards say the charred body cannot be found, the ornaments of burnt gold are set before him, and he, thinking she fell in the fire and that it is no trick, cries out for her.","n":"The ornaments are the proof the plan lets the king have, and the verse tells us he reads the lack of a body as the fire's work, not a ruse.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","vanjam","alar"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 141","lb":"UD141","mm":1,"ev":"Guards bring burnt ornaments in place of a body; the king, believing she died in the fire, laments.","cl":[],"ag":["காவலர்","உதயணன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:8"},"ud:142":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":142,"t":["மண்விளக்க மாகிநீ வரத்தினெய்தி வந்தனை","பெண்விளக்க மாகிநீ பெறற்கரியை யென்றுதன்","கண்விளக்கு காரிகையைக் காதலித் திரங்கவான்","புண்விளக் கிலங்குவேற் பொற்புடைய மன்னவன்."],"p":"","r":"The king, grieving over her, calls her the lamp of the earth who came as a boon and a lamp among women hard to win, the light of his eyes.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","anbu","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 142","lb":"UD142","mm":1,"ev":"The king laments, calling the queen the lamp of the world and of his eyes.","cl":[],"ag":["உதயணன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["உதயணன்","வாசவதத்தை"],"ch":"ud:8"},"ud:143":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":143,"t":["மானெனும் மயிலெனும் மரைமிசைத் திருவெனும்","தேனெனுங் கொடியெனுஞ் சிறந்தகொங்கை நீயெனும்","வானில மடந்தையே மாதவத்தின் வந்தனை","நானிடர்ப் படுவது நன்மையோநீ வீந்ததும்."],"p":"","r":"He calls her a deer, a peacock, a lotus-borne Lakshmi, a honey, a creeper, and one born of great penance, and asks whether it is right that she died and he suffers.","n":"Pure lament; the praise names the queen by images only (deer, peacock, creeper) and gives no new fact.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","anbu","alar"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 143","lb":"UD143","mm":1,"ev":"The king addresses the dead queen with a list of comparisons and asks if his grief is justified.","cl":[],"ag":["உதயணன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["உதயணன்","வாசவதத்தை"],"ch":"ud:8"},"ud:144":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":144,"t":["நங்கைநறுங் கொங்கையே நல்லமைக் குழலியெம்","கொங்குலவு கோதைபொன் குழையிலங்கு நன்முகம்","சிங்கார முனதுரையும் செல்வி சீதளம்மதி","பொங்காரம் முகமெனப் புலம்பினான் புரவலன்."],"p":"","r":"The king laments her breasts, her hair, her face as a golden ear-ring, her ornament, and her cool moon-like face as he cries out.","n":"Several words in line 3 and 4 (சிங்கார முனதுரையும், பொங்காரம்) are hard to take and the sense is only approximate.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","pirivu","alar"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 144","lb":"UD144","mm":1,"ev":"The king mourns the queen, naming her features.","cl":[],"ag":["உதயணன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["உதயணன்","வாசவதத்தை"],"ch":"ud:8"},"ud:145":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":145,"t":["வீணைநற் கிழத்திநீ வித்தக வுருவிநீ","நாணின்பாவை தானுநீ நலந்திகழ் மணியுநீ","காணவென்றன் முன்பதாய்க் காரிகையே வந்துநீ","தோணிமுகங் காட்டெனச் சொல்லியே புலம்புவான்."],"p":"","r":"He calls her the lute-mistress, the wise one, the doll of modesty and the shining gem, and cries that she should come to his sight and show her face.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","pirivu","alar"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 145","lb":"UD145","mm":1,"ev":"The king cries to the dead queen to appear and show her face.","cl":[],"ag":["உதயணன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["உதயணன்","வாசவதத்தை"],"ch":"ud:8"},"ud:146":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":146,"t":["துன்பமிக வும்பெருகச் சொற்கரிய தேவிக்கா","அன்புமிக் கரற்றுவதை யகல்வது பொருளென","நன்புறு மமைச்சர்சொல்ல நரபதியுங் கேட்டனன்","இன்புறும் மனைவிகாத லியல்புட னகன்றனன்."],"p":"","r":"As the sorrow swells beyond words, the good ministers say that to put aside love for the queen is wiser, and the king listens, leaves his weeping and turns from her as a loving husband would.","n":"The advice is sound in the ministers' eyes and the king takes it, but the queen is alive, so the counsel rests on a false fact only Yugi's side knows.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["amaichu","nilaiyaamai","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 146","lb":"UD146","mm":1,"ev":"The ministers advise the king to give up his lament; he agrees and withdraws from it.","cl":[["a king is wiser to put aside grief for a dead wife than to go on weeping","general","reported",2,"ministers_vatsa"]],"ag":["அமைச்சர்","உதயணன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["அமைச்சர்","உதயணன்"],"ch":"ud:8"},"ud:147":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":147,"t":["அண்ண றன்னிலை யறிந்த யூகியும்","திண்ணி தின்னியல் செய்கை யென்றுரு","ண்ணு வாவினை மன்ன னண்டையில்","எண்ணுங் காரிய மீண்டுச் செய்கென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"Yugi, knowing the king's state, says the work is firm and the course good, tells Vayantakan to do again at the king's side what they had in mind.","n":"Lines 2 to 4 are damaged and the sense of the verse is a probable one; the text was repaired at line 2 by the build.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["soozhchi","amaichu","thunai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 147","lb":"UD147","mm":1,"ev":"Yugi tells Vayantakan to carry on the plan beside the king.","cl":[],"ag":["யூகி","வயந்தகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["யூகி","வயந்தகன்"],"ch":"ud:8"},"ud:148":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":148,"t":["தன்னிலைக் கமைந்த தத்துவ ஞானத்தான்","துன்னருஞ் சூழ்ச்சித் தோழன் வயந்தகன்","மன்னற் குறுதி மறித்தினிக் கூறும்","பொன்னடி, வணங்கிப் புரவலன் கேட்ப."],"p":"","r":"Vayantakan, wise in true knowledge and skilled in hard counsel, bows at the king's golden feet and speaks again of what is best for him, while the king listens.","n":"The verse only opens the speech that fills the next one.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["amaichu","gnaanam","panivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 148","lb":"UD148","mm":1,"ev":"Vayantakan bows to the king and begins to advise him.","cl":[],"ag":["வயந்தகன்","உதயணன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["வயந்தகன்","உதயணன்"],"ch":"ud:8"},"ud:149":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":149,"t":["வெற்றிவேன் மகதவன் வேந்தன் றேசத்தில்","இற்றவர்க் காட்டு மியல்பின னூலுரை","கற்றுவல் லவனற் காட்சி யறிவுடன்","தத்துவ முனியுள னாமினிச் சார்வோம்."],"p":"","r":"Vayantakan says that in the land of the Magadha king there is a learned sage who shows the dead their fate and sees the truth by sight and knowledge, and urges they go to him.","n":"Line 2 (இற்றவர்க் காட்டும் இயல்பினன்) is hard; it may be that the sage shows the dead, or shows the way to those who are gone, and the reading is not fixed.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["gnaanam","amaichu","thaththuvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 149","lb":"UD149","mm":1,"ev":"Vayantakan tells the king of a sage in Magadha and proposes going there.","cl":[["a learned sage with direct sight can show the fate of the dead","general","reported",2,"vayantakan"]],"ag":["வயந்தகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["வயந்தகன்","உதயணன்"],"ch":"ud:8"},"ud:150":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":150,"t":["வத்தவ குமரன் கேட்டு வயந்தகன் றன்னை நோக்கி","அத்திசை போவோ மென்றே யகமகிழ்ந் தினிய கூறி","வெற்றிநாற் படையுஞ் சூழ வெண்குடை கவரி மேவ","ஒத்துடனிசைந்து சென்றான் உதயண குமரன் றானே."],"p":"","r":"The king hears Vayantakan, agrees gladly to go that way, and sets out with the four-fold army, white umbrella and fans.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vendhan","amaichu","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 150","lb":"UD150","mm":1,"ev":"Udayanan agrees to Vayantakan's plan and sets out with his army.","cl":[],"ag":["உதயணன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["உதயணன்","வயந்தகன்"],"ch":"ud:8"},"ud:151":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":151,"t":["சயந்தியி னெல்லை விட்டுச் சாலவு மகத நாட்டுக்","கியைந்துநன் கெழுந்து சென்றே யிரவியி னுதய முற்றான்","நயந்தனன் றேவி காத னன்மனத் தழுங்கிப் பின்னும்","வியந்துநல் லமைச்சர் தேற்ற வெங்கடுங் கானம் புக்கான்."],"p":"","r":"The king leaves the Jayanti border, travels on into the Magadha country, and arrives at dawn; grieving in heart for his queen even as his ministers console him, he enters a harsh forest.","n":"The subject is never named in the verse; the king is known from the story so far, and the sense of line 3 (his love for the queen weighing on him despite comfort) is not fully fixed.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","kaamam","nenju"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 151","lb":"UD151","mm":1,"ev":"Udayanan crosses into the Magadha country and enters the forest, still grieving for the queen.","cl":[],"ag":["உதயணன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:9"},"ud:152":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":152,"t":["செத்தநற் றேவி தன்னைத் திருப்பவு மீட்க லாமென்","றத்திசை முன்னி நல்ல வருவழிப் பட்டுச் செல்ல","அத்தியும் பிணையு மேக வாண்மயி லாடக் கண்டு","வத்தவன் கலுழ்ந்து ரைக்கு மனனமை மனையை யோர்ந்தே."],"p":"","r":"Hoping the dead queen may yet be brought back, the king heads that way; seeing a stag with its doe and a peacock dancing, he weeps and speaks of his lost wife.","n":"The verse calls the queen dead (செத்த) yet lets him hope to bring her back; the hope comes from the earlier plan and is not explained here.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["ninaivu","pirivu","kaamanoy"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 152","lb":"UD152","mm":1,"ev":"Udayanan, going toward Magadha, weeps at the sight of paired animals and a dancing peacock.","cl":[],"ag":["உதயணன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:9"},"ud:153":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":153,"t":["கோட்டுப்பூ நிறைந்தி லங்குங் கொடிவகைப் பூவுங் கோலங்","காட்டுநந் தேவி யென்று கால்விசை நடவா மன்னன்","காட்டினன் குன்ற மேறிக் கானகங் கழிந்து போந்து","சேட்டிளஞ் சிங்க மன்னான் திருநிறை மகதஞ் சேர்ந்தான்."],"p":"","r":"The king, who cannot walk on, points to flowers on branches and creepers as showing his queen's adornment; he climbs a hill, passes the forest and reaches rich Magadha.","n":"Grief makes every sight a likeness of the lost wife; the same habit of seeing her in other things returns in verse 157.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ninaivu","kaamam","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 153","lb":"UD153","mm":1,"ev":"Udayanan sees flowers as the queen's ornament, climbs a hill, crosses the forest and reaches Magadha.","cl":[],"ag":["உதயணன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:9"},"ud:154":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":154,"t":["மருவிய திருவி னானம் மகதவர்க் கிறைவ னாமம்","தருசக னென்னு மன்னன் றானைவேற் றலைவன் மாரன்","இருந்தினி துறையு மிக்க விராசநற் கிரியந் தன்னிற்","பொருந்திச்சென் னகர்ப்பு றத்திற் பொலிவுட னிருந்தா னன்றே."],"p":"","r":"Maran, commander of the army of Tarusakan, lord of the Magadhas, lives in splendour in the town of Rajagiri, just outside the capital.","n":"The verse only places Maran and Tarusakan; it gives no action of the king himself.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","padai","mannar"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 154","lb":"UD154","mm":1,"ev":"Maran, the Magadha king's army commander, is settled at Rajagiri outside the city.","cl":[],"ag":["மாரன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:9"},"ud:155":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":155,"t":["காமநற் கோட்டஞ் சூழக் கனமதி லிலங்கும் வாயிற்","சோமநற் றாப தர்கள் சூழ்ந்தமர் பள்ளி தன்னில்","நாமநல் வயந்த கன்னும் நன்கறி காக துண்ட","மாமறை யாளற் கண்டு வஞ்சகஞ் செப்பி னானே."],"p":"","r":"At the Kamakottam gate, among ascetics of the Soma sort, Vayantakan finds the learned brahmin Kakatundan and tells him a deceitful tale.","n":"The deceit is named but its content is not given; the verse is the start of a plan, not a statement of belief.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vanjam","thunai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 155","lb":"UD155","mm":1,"ev":"Vayantakan meets Kakatundan in the ascetics' hall and feeds him a false story.","cl":[],"ag":["வயந்தகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:9"},"ud:156":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":156,"t":["திருநிறை மன்னன் றன்னைச் சீர்மறை யாளன் கண்டே","இருமதி யெல்லை நீங்கி யிப்பதி யிருப்ப வென்றும்","தருவனீ யிழந்த தேவி தரணியுங் கூடவென்ன","மருவியங் கிருக்கு மோர்நாண் மகதவன் றங்கை தானும்."],"p":"","r":"The brahmin meets the king and says that if he stays in this town for two months he will restore the lost queen and the earth with her; meanwhile the Magadha king's sister appears one day.","n":"The promise is part of the deceit begun in verse 155 and answers the king's hope in verse 152; the verse breaks off before the sister's coming is told.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vanjam","kaamam","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 156","lb":"UD156","mm":1,"ev":"Kakatundan promises the king the lost queen and the earth if he waits two months.","cl":[],"ag":["காகதுண்டன்","உதயணன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["காகதுண்டன்","உதயணன்"],"ch":"ud:9"},"ud:157":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":157,"t":["பருவமிக் கிலங்குங் கோதைப் பதுமைதே ரேறி வந்து","பொருவில்கா மனையே காணப் புரவலன் கண்டு கந்து","மருவும்வா சவதத் தைதான் வந்தன ளென்று ரைப்பத்","திருநகர் மாதுங் கண்டு திகைத்துளங் கவன்று நின்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"Padmavati, in full bloom, drives up in a chariot to see the god of love himself; the king, seeing her, is glad and says that Vasavadatta has come, and the lady stands troubled at the sight of him.","n":"The king's eyes mistake Padmavati for the dead wife because his mind is full of her; compare the flowers he took for her ornament in verse 153.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["mayal","kaamam","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 157","lb":"UD157","mm":1,"ev":"Padmavati comes in a chariot, and Udayanan takes her for Vasavadatta.","cl":[],"ag":["பதுமை","உதயணன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:9"},"ud:158":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":158,"t":["யாப்பியா யினியா ளென்னு மவளுடைத் தோழி சென்று","நாப்புகழ் மன்னற் கண்டு நலம்பிற வுரைத்துக் கூட்டக்","காப்புடைப் பதுமை யோடுங் காவலன் கலந்து பொன்னின்","சீப்பிடக் கண்சி வக்குஞ் சீர்மங்கை நலமுண் டானே."],"p":"","r":"Padmavati's friend Yappiya Iniyal goes to the king, praises her charms and brings them together; protected by his guards, the king unites with her.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["punarchi","thunai","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 158","lb":"UD158","mm":1,"ev":"Yappiya Iniyal brings Padmavati to the king and they unite.","cl":[],"ag":["யாப்பியாயினியாள்","உதயணன்","பதுமை"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:9"},"ud:159":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":159,"t":["எழில்பெறு காமக் கோட்டத் தியற்கையிற் புணர்ந்து வந்து","வழிபெறு மமைச்ச ரோடு வத்தவ னினிய கூறும்","மொழியமிர் தந்ந லாளை மோகத்திற் பிரியே னென்னத்","தொழுதவர் பெறுக போகந் தோன்றனீ யென்று சொன்னார்."],"p":"","r":"After joining her at Kamakottam as nature has it, the Vatsa king tells his ministers sweetly that he will not leave the nectar-voiced girl for desire; they bow and say 'may you gain enjoyment, appear'.","n":"The ministers' reply (line 4) is brief and its last word is not clear; they appear to approve the king's pleasure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["amaichu","kaamam","punarchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 159","lb":"UD159","mm":1,"ev":"Udayanan tells his ministers he will not leave Padmavati, and they bless the match.","cl":[],"ag":["உதயணன்","அமைச்சர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:9"},"ud:160":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":160,"t":["மாட்சிநற் சிவிகை யேறி மடந்தைதன் னோடும் புக்குத்","தாழ்ச்சியின் மாளி கைக்குட் டக்கவண் மனங்கு ளிர்ப்பக்","காட்டினன் வீணை தன்னைக் காவலன் கரந்தி ருப்ப","ஓட்டிய சினத்த னாய வுருமண்ணு விதனைச் செப்பும்."],"p":"","r":"Riding a splendid palanquin with the girl into a mansion, the king shows her the lute, which he has kept hidden; the angry Urumannuvan is moved to speak of this.","n":"The cause of Urumannuvan's anger is not given in the verse; the verse prepares his speech in verse 161.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["amaichu","kalai","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 160","lb":"UD160","mm":1,"ev":"Udayanan takes Padmavati to a mansion and shows her the lute; Urumannuvan, angered, speaks.","cl":[],"ag":["உதயணன்","உருமண்ணுவன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:9"},"ud:161":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":161,"t":["ஆகிய தறிந்து செய்யு மருளுடை மனத்த னான","யூகியங் குஞ்சை தன்னை யுற்றருஞ் சிறைவி டுக்கப்","போகநற் றேவி யோடும் போந்தது போல நாமும்","போகுவ மன்னன் மாதைப் புதுமணம் புணரு வித்தே."],"p":"","r":"Yugi, whose mind is kind and who knows events, freed Kunjai from prison and left with the queen; so Urumannuvan proposes that they too go, after the king has been married afresh to this girl.","n":"The speaker is Urumannuvan only because verse 160 ends by saying he will speak; verse 161 does not name him. The model he cites is Yugi's earlier escape from Ujjain, told in the preceding kandam.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["amaichu","soozhchi","thunai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 161","lb":"UD161","mm":1,"ev":"Urumannuvan proposes that the king be married to Padmavati before they all leave.","cl":[],"ag":["யூகி","உதயணன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["உருமண்ணுவன்",""],"ch":"ud:9"},"ud:162":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":162,"t":["உருமண்ணு வாவ னுப்ப வுற்றமுந் நூறு பேர்கள்","மருவிய விச்சை தன்னான் மன்னவன் கோயி றன்னுள்","மருவினர் மறைந்து சென்றார் மன்னவன் றாதை வைத்த","பெருநிதி காண்கி லாமற் பேர்க்குநர்த் தேடு கின்றான்."],"p":"","r":"Urumannuvan sends three hundred kinsmen, who by their art slip unseen into the king's palace while the king, unable to find the great treasure his father laid up, searches for those who moved it.","n":"Line 4 can be read as the king looking for the thieves of the treasure; the verse does not say that Urumannuvan's men took it.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["soozhchi","vanjam","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 162","lb":"UD162","mm":1,"ev":"Urumannuvan's three hundred men hide in the Magadha palace while the king hunts for his father's lost treasure.","cl":[],"ag":["உற்றமுந்நூறு பேர்கள்","தருசகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:9"},"ud:163":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":163,"t":["யானறிந் துரைப்ப னென்றே யரசனைக் கண்டு மிக்க","மாநிதி காட்டி நன்மை மகதவ னோடுங் கூடி","ஊனமில் விச்சை தன்னா லுருமண்ணு பிரித லின்றிப்","பானலங் கிளவி தன்னாற் பரிவுட னிருக்கு நாளில்."],"p":"","r":"A speaker says he will tell what he knows, meets the king and shows him a great treasure; thereafter the Magadha king, with goodwill, lives happily beside Urumannuvan, who is never parted from him, by means of his art.","n":"The verse does not name who says 'I will tell what I know'; the story points to Urumannuvan, but the speaker is a guess, and line 4 (பானலங் கிளவி) is also hard to place.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["soozhchi","selvam","thunai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 163","lb":"UD163","mm":1,"ev":"Urumannuvan shows Tarusakan a great treasure and the two live as close allies.","cl":[],"ag":["உருமண்ணுவன்","தருசகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:9"},"ud:164":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":164,"t":["அடவியா மரசன் மிக்க வயோத்தியர்க் கிறைவன் றானைப்","படையுறு சாலி யென்பான் பலமுறு சத்தி யென்பான்","முடிவிரி சிகையன் மல்லன் முகட்டெலிச் செவிய னென்பான்","உடன்வரு மெழுவர் கூடி யொளிர்மக தத்து வந்தார்."],"p":"","r":"Seven kings, among them the lord of Ayodhya's army chief Shali, Palamuru Sakti, Mudiviri Sikaiyan and Mukatteli Seviyan, come together to Magadha.","n":"The names are listed by title; some of them are hard to separate (the verse mixes names and nicknames).","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pagai","padai","mannar"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 164","lb":"UD164","mm":1,"ev":"Seven allied kings of the forest and Ayodhya arrive in Magadha with their forces.","cl":[],"ag":["எழுவர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:9"},"ud:165":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":165,"t":["தருசகற் கினிதி னாங்க டருதிறை யிடுவ தில்லென்","றெரியென வெகுண்டு வந்தே யினியநா டழிக்க லுற்றார்","தருசக ராசன் கேட்டுத் தளரவப் புறத்த கற்ற","உருமண்ணு வாம னத்தி லுபாயத்தி லுடைப்ப னென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"The seven, angry, say they will not pay tribute to Tarusakan and come to destroy his pleasant land; he is shaken at the news, and Urumannuvan says that he will break them by a stratagem.","n":"The verse prefers plan (உபாயம்) to open battle for the defence; line 4's first words are hard to parse.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pagai","soozhchi","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 165","lb":"UD165","mm":1,"ev":"The seven kings refuse tribute and march to ravage Magadha; Urumannuvan promises to defeat them by stratagem.","cl":[],"ag":["எழுவர்","தருசகன்","உருமண்ணுவன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["உருமண்ணுவன்","தருசகன்"],"ch":"ud:9"},"ud:166":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":166,"t":["கள்ளநல் லுருவி னோடுங் கடியகத் துள்ளே யுற்ற","வள்ளலை மதியிற் கூட்டி வாணிக வுருவி னோடு","தெள்ளிய மணிதெரிந்து சிலமணி மாறப் போந்து","பள்ளிப்பா சறைபு குந்து பலமணி விற்றி ருந்தார்."],"p":"","r":"In disguise, Urumannuvan and the king, joined by his wit to the Magadha king, go as merchants who sort jewels, swap a few, and set up a shop in the enemy camp selling many gems.","n":"Line 1's 'false form' and 'merchant form' suggest a change of guise for both; the jewels are a pretext for getting inside the camp.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["otru","vanjam","soozhchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 166","lb":"UD166","mm":1,"ev":"Urumannuvan and Udayanan, disguised as jewel merchants, set up a stall in the enemy camp.","cl":[],"ag":["உருமண்ணுவன்","உதயணன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:9"},"ud:167":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":167,"t":["மன்னன் வீர மகதற்குக் கேளாத்தம்","இன்னு ரைக ளியல்பின் வரவரத்","துன்னு நாற்படை வீடுதோன் றிரவிடை","உன்னி னர்கரந் துரைகள் பலவிதம்."],"p":"","r":"In an unclear verse, words the king and the Magadhas would not wish to hear spread among the troops as they come into the four-fold camp by night, hidden talk of many kinds.","n":"The verse is in a different, shorter metre and its words are broken; the reading is a guess.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["soozhchi","sol","vanjam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 167","lb":"UD167","mm":1,"ev":"Hidden rumours spread through the camp at night.","cl":[],"ag":["உற்றமுந்நூறு பேர்கள்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:9"},"ud:168":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":168,"t":["உரையு ணர்ந்தவ ருள்ளங் கலங்கிப்பின்","முரியுஞ் சேனை முயன்றவ ரோடலிற்","றெருளி னர்கூடிச் சேரவந் தத்தினம்","மருவி யையம் மனத்திடை நீங்கினார்."],"p":"","r":"Those who heard the talk lose heart, the troops give way and run, and the enemy leaders gather in confusion, until the doubt in their minds is gone.","n":"Line 4 (ஐயம் மனத்திடை நீங்கினார்) is hard: it can be read as the leaders' doubt ending, or as their giving up their purpose.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["soozhchi","acham","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 168","lb":"UD168","mm":1,"ev":"The rumours break the enemy's nerve and its army runs.","cl":[],"ag":["எழுவர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:9"},"ud:169":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":169,"t":["இரவு பாசறை யிருந்தவர் போனதும்","மருவிக் கூடியே வந்துடன் விட்டதும்","விரவி யொற்றர்கள் வேந்தர்க் குரைத்தலின்","அரசன் கேட்டுமிக் கார்செய லென்றனன்."],"p":"","r":"Spies report to the king what the men who camped by night did, how they went off and then gathered and came back and let go, and the king, hearing it, calls it the work of 'kar'.","n":"The verse leaves out who the campers are and what was let go; the king's remark is a single word of judgment, and the reader must take the rest from the next verses.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["otru","arasu","soozhchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 169","lb":"UD169","mm":1,"ev":"Spies tell the king of the night-campers' doing, and the king names it a 'kar' deed.","cl":[],"ag":["ஒற்றர்","தருசகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["தருசகன்",""],"ch":"ud:10"},"ud:170":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":170,"t":["வார ணிக்கழல் வத்தவன் றன்செயல்","ஓரணி மார்ப னுருமண்ணு வாவுமிக்","கேரணி யரச ருக்கியல் கூறலும்","தாரணி மன்னன் றன்னுண் மகிழ்ந்தான்."],"p":"","r":"The minister Urumannuvan tells the fair-armoured kings the doings of the Vatsa lord, and the earth-king is glad in himself.","n":"The name is run together with a following word in the edition and the telling may be the spies' or the minister's; what is sure is that a report of Udayanan's deed makes the king glad.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["amaichu","otru","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 170","lb":"UD170","mm":1,"ev":"Urumannuvan reports Udayanan's deed to the king, who rejoices.","cl":[],"ag":["உருமண்ணுவன்","தருசகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:10"},"ud:171":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":171,"t":["ஆரா வுவகையுள் ளாகி யரசனும்","பேரா மினியயாழ்ப் பெருமகன் றன்னையே","சேரா வெதிர்போய்ச் சிறந்து புல்லினன்","நேரா மாற்றரை நீக்குவ னென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"Full of unending joy, the king goes out to meet the lord of the sweet lute, embraces him in honour and vows to drive out the foes who will not come to terms.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","arasu","aanmai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 171","lb":"UD171","mm":1,"ev":"The king meets Udayanan, embraces him and promises to remove their enemies.","cl":[],"ag":["தருசகன்","உதயணன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["தருசகன்","உதயணன்"],"ch":"ud:10"},"ud:172":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":172,"t":["உலம்பொருத தோளுடை யுதயண குமரனும்","நலம்பொருத நாற்படையு நன்குடனே சூழப்போய்ப்","புலம்பொருத போர்ப்படையுட் பொருதுதவத் தொலைத்துடன்","நலம்பெறத் திறையுட னரபதியு மீண்டனன்."],"p":"","r":"Udayanan with his four-fold army marches and destroys the enemy force in battle, and the king returns with tribute.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["padai","maram","aanmai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 172","lb":"UD172","mm":1,"ev":"Udayanan wins the battle and the king comes home with tribute.","cl":[],"ag":["உதயணன்","தருசகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:10"},"ud:173":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":173,"t":["வருவவிசை யத்துடன் வத்தவற் கிறைவனைத்","தருசகனெ திர்கொண்டு தன்மனை புகுந்துபின்","மருவநற் பதுமையா மங்கைதங்கை தன்னையே","திருநிறைநல் வேள்வியாற் செல்வற்கே யளித்தனன்."],"p":"","r":"The king of Magadha, Tharusakan, welcomes the victorious Vatsa lord to his house and gives his sister, the lady Padumai, to him in the rite of the sacrifice.","n":"The wedding is made by a sacrifice (வேள்வி), a rite the Jain poet narrates without comment; the marriage is also the reward for the victory of the last verse.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vaazhkkaithunai","natpu","eegai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 173","lb":"UD173","mm":1,"ev":"Tharusakan welcomes Udayanan and gives him his sister Padumai in marriage by the sacred fire.","cl":[],"ag":["தருசகன்","பதுமை"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:10"},"ud:174":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":174,"t":["புதுமணக் கோலமிவர் புனைந்தனரியற்றிப்பின்","பதியுடையை யாயிரம் பருமதக் களிற்றுடன்","துதிமிகு புரவிகள் தொக்கவிரண் டாயிரம்","அதிர்மணி யாற்றுந்தே ராயிரத் திருநூறே."],"p":"","r":"After the wedding adornments are made, the king gives a dowry of a thousand rutting elephants, two thousand horses and twelve hundred chariots.","n":"The gift is counted in fighting strength; nothing in it is domestic wealth.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","eegai","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 174","lb":"UD174","mm":1,"ev":"Tharusakan gives the bridal gift of elephants, horses and chariots.","cl":[],"ag":["தருசகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:10"},"ud:175":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":175,"t":["அறுபதினெண் ணாயிர மானபடை வீரரும்","நறுமலர்நற் கோதையர் நான்கிருநூற் றிருபதும்","பெறுகவென் றமைத்துடன் பேர்வருட காரியும்","உறுவடிவேற் சத்தியு முயர்தரும தத்தனும்."],"p":"","r":"The dowry goes on with sixty-eight thousand soldiers and a body of fragrant-garlanded women, and the king names Varudakari, Sakthi and Dharmadattan to go with it.","n":"The number of the women is given in a tangled phrase and I take it only as a few hundred.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["selvam","padai","amaichu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 175","lb":"UD175","mm":1,"ev":"Tharusakan adds soldiers, women and three named men to the dowry.","cl":[],"ag":["தருசகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:10"},"ud:176":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":176,"t":["சத்தியகா யன்னுடன் சாலவு மமைச்சரை","வெற்றிநாற்ப டைத்துணை வேந்தவன்பிற் செல்கென","முற்றிழைநல் அரிவைக்கு முகமலரச் சீதனம்","பற்றின்பி னாலளித்துப் பாங்குடன் விடுத்தனன்."],"p":"","r":"With Satyakayan and the ministers added, the king tells the four-fold force to follow Udayanan, and gives the bride's gift with a glad face and sends them off in good order.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["amaichu","padai","eegai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 176","lb":"UD176","mm":1,"ev":"Tharusakan sends ministers and army with Udayanan and sets the bridal party on its way.","cl":[],"ag":["தருசகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:10"},"ud:177":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":177,"t":["வெல்லுமண்ணலை ம்மிக வேந்தனன் னயஞ்சில","சொல்லிநண்பி னாலுரைத்துத் தோன்றலை மிகப்புலிச்","செல்கெனா விடுத்தரச் செல்வனங்குப் போந்தனன்","எல்லைதன்னா டெய்துழி யினியதம்பியர் வந்தனர்."],"p":"","r":"The king speaks some gentle words in friendship, sends the lord off, and as the Vatsa king reaches his own land his younger brothers come to him.","n":"Line 1 is damaged, so who speaks the gentle words, and to whom, is read from the sense.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["natpu","sutram","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 177","lb":"UD177","mm":1,"ev":"Tharusakan takes leave of Udayanan with kind words, and Udayanan reaches his border.","cl":[],"ag":["தருசகன்","உதயணன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:10"},"ud:178":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":178,"t":["பிங்கல கடகரெனப் பீடுடைக் குமரரும்","தங்குபன்னி ராயிரம் தானைவல்ல வீரரும்","அங்குவந்தவ் வண்ணலை யடிவணங்கிக் கூடினர்","பொங்குபுறங் கௌசாம்பியிற் போர்க்களத்தில் விட்டனர்."],"p":"","r":"Pingalan and Kadakan, two princes with twelve thousand brave troops, come to the lord, bow at his feet and join him, and the army is set in the field outside Kausambi.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sutram","padai","panivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 178","lb":"UD178","mm":1,"ev":"Pingalan and Kadakan arrive with their army and join Udayanan before Kausambi.","cl":[],"ag":["பிங்கலன் கடகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:10"},"ud:179":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":179,"t":["வருடகா ரனையழைத்து வத்தவ னியம்புமிப்","பருமிதநற் சேனையுள்ள பாஞ்சால ராயனிடம்","திருமுடி யரசரைத் திறத்தினா லகற்றெனப்","பொருளினவன் போந்தபின்பு போர்வினை தொடங்கினர்."],"p":"","r":"Udayanan calls Varudakaran and tells him to go to the Panchala king, who holds a proud army, and drive the crowned kings away by skill; when he goes with wealth they begin the war.","n":"Whether the 'strategy' is a bribe, or a stratagem, turns on the word பொருளின் in line 4; 'wealth' is the likelier use.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["amaichu","soozhchi","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 179","lb":"UD179","mm":1,"ev":"Udayanan sends Varudakaran with wealth to the Panchala king to break the other kings, and the fighting starts.","cl":[],"ag":["உதயணன்","வருடகாரன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["உதயணன்","வருடகாரன்"],"ch":"ud:10"},"ud:180":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":180,"t":["அமைச்சனுஞ்சென் றவ்வண்ண மதிர்கழனல் வேந்தரைச்","சமத்தினி லகற்றினன் சாலவும்பாஞ் சாலனும்","அமைத்தநாற் படையுட னமர்ந்துவந் தெதிர்த்தனன்","அமைத்திருவர் விற்கணைக ளக்கதிர் மறைத்தவே."],"p":"","r":"The minister goes and drives the kings off in battle, but the Panchala king comes against him with a four-fold force, and arrows from both bows shut out the sun.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","padai","amaichu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 180","lb":"UD180","mm":1,"ev":"Varudakaran drives off the kings; the Panchala king counterattacks and the arrows fly thick.","cl":[],"ag":["வருடகாரன்","பாஞ்சாலன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:10"},"ud:181":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":181,"t":["விரிந்த வெண்குடை வீழவும் வேந்தர் விண்ணுற வேறவும்","பரிந்து பேய்க்கண மாடவும் பலவா நரிபறைந் துண்ணவும்","முரிந்த முண்டங்க ளாடவும் முரிந்த மாக்களி றுருளவும்","வரிந்த வெண்சிலை மன்னவன் வந்த வன்கண் சிவந்தவே."],"p":"","r":"The field is shown as a scene of ruin: white umbrellas fall, kings rise to heaven, ghouls dance, jackals feed, headless trunks dance, and the king's eyes grow red.","n":"The verse sets the dancing ghouls and feeding jackals beside the kings' ascent to heaven, so the carnage is told as a feast and a glory together; the poet gives no word of pity.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","kootru","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 181","lb":"UD181","mm":1,"ev":"The battle reaches its height, with corpses and scavengers filling the field.","cl":[],"ag":["உதயணன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:10"},"ud:182":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":182,"t":["மாற்ற வன்படை முறிந்தென மன்ன வன்படை யார்த்திடத்","தோற்ற மன்னன்வந் தெதிர்த்தனன் றூய காளைதன் வாளினால்","மாற்ற லன்றனைக் கூற்றுண வண்மை யிவ்விருந் தார்கென","ஏற்ர வகையினி லிட்டன னிலங்கு வத்தவ ராசனே."],"p":"","r":"When the enemy army breaks and the Vatsa army roars, the beaten king comes forward again, and Udayanan, with his sword, feeds him to Death.","n":"Line 2's 'தோற்ற மன்னன்' is read as the beaten king; the verse does not say outright that he is the Panchala king.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maram","kootru","aanmai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 182","lb":"UD182","mm":1,"ev":"Udayanan kills the Panchala king in single combat after his army is broken.","cl":[],"ag":["பாஞ்சாலன்","உதயணன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:10"},"ud:183":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":183,"t":["பகைய றவேயெ றிந்துடன் பாங்கிற் போர்வினை தவிர்கென","வகைய றவேப டுகளங் கண்டு நண்ணிய மற்றது","தொகையு றுந்தன தொல்படை சூழ வூர்முக நோக்கினன்","நகையு றுந்நல மார்பனு நகர வீதியில் வந்தனன்."],"p":"","r":"Having cut down the foes and ordered the fighting to stop, the king looks over the field, sets out for the town with his old army about him, and comes smiling into the streets.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","padai","aanmai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 183","lb":"UD183","mm":1,"ev":"Udayanan halts the war, inspects the field and rides back toward the city.","cl":[],"ag":["உதயணன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["உதயணன்",""],"ch":"ud:10"},"ud:184":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":184,"t":["மாடமா ளிகைமிசை மங்கையரு மேறிமீக்","கூடிநின் றிருமருங்குங் கொற்றவனை வாழ்த்தினார்","பாடலவர் படித்திடப் பலகொடி மிடைந்தநல்","ஆடகநன் மாளிகை யரசனும் புகுந்தனன்."],"p":"","r":"Women climb up on the mansions on both sides to bless the king, bards sing, and he enters the golden palace among the banners.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pugazh","arasu","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 184","lb":"UD184","mm":1,"ev":"The townswomen and bards honour Udayanan and he enters his palace.","cl":[],"ag":["மங்கையர்","பாடலவர்","உதயணன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:10"},"ud:185":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":185,"t":["படுகளத்தி னெந்தவர்க்குப் பலகிழிநெய் பற்றுடன்","இடுமருந்து பூசவு மினிப்பொரு ளளித்தபின்","தொடுகழ லரசர்கள் சூழ்ந்தடி பணிந்திட","முடிதரித் தரசியன் முகமலர்ந்து செல்லுநாள்."],"p":"","r":"The king has the fallen on the field smeared with oil and medicine and given wealth, the kings come and bow at his feet, and he wears the crown and rules in joy.","n":"The care for the wounded comes after the victory has been told as a feast of scavengers; both appear in the same chapter.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arul","marundhu","sengol"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 185","lb":"UD185","mm":1,"ev":"Udayanan tends the wounded and rewards them, kings submit, and he takes the crown.","cl":[],"ag":["உதயணன்","எழுவர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:10"},"ud:186":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":186,"t":["மின்சொரி கதிர்வேற் றானை வீறடி பணிய வெம்மைப்","பொன்சொரி கவரி வீசப் பொங்கரி யாச னத்தில்","தண்சொரி கிரண முத்தத் தவளநற் குடையி னீழல்","மின்சொரி தரள வேந்தன் வீற்றி ருந்த போழ்தின்."],"p":"","r":"The king sits enthroned in glory, his feet bowed to by armies and fanned with golden whisks, under a white pearl umbrella.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vendhan","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 186","lb":"UD186","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["உதயணன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:11"},"ud:187":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":187,"t":["மாற்றலர் தூதர் வந்து வருதிறை யளந்து நிற்ப","ஆற்றலர் வரவ வர்க்கே யானபொன் றுகில ளித்தே","ஏற்றநற் சனங்கட் கெல்லா மினிப்பொரு ளுவந்து வீசிக்","கோற்றொழி னடத்தி மன்னன் குறைவின்றிச் செல்லு கின்றான்."],"p":"","r":"Envoys of hostile kings come and stand with the tribute they owe; the king gives them gold and cloth, spreads wealth among his people, and rules by the sceptre without lack.","n":"The king answers tribute with gifts; the verse sets his giving beside his ruling by the sceptre as one habit.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","eegai","sengol"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 187","lb":"UD187","mm":1,"ev":"Envoys of enemy kings bring tribute, and Udayanan rewards them and gives wealth to all his people.","cl":[],"ag":["தூதர்","உதயணன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:11"},"ud:188":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":188,"t":["மதுரவண் டறாத மாலை மகதவன் றங்கை யாய","பதுமைதன் பணைமு லைமேற் பார்த்திபன் புணர்ந்து செல்ல","துதிக்கைமா வீழ்ந்த கானந் தோன்றலு மாடம் பண்ணிப்","பதியினு மமைத்துப் பாங்கிற் படிமமு மமைத்தா னன்றே."],"p":"","r":"As the king lies with Padumai, the Magadha king's sister, he builds a pavilion and a town where the elephant fell in the forest, and puts up an image beside it.","n":"The verse joins the king's love for Padumai with the marking of the place where the elephant died; whether the image is of the elephant is not said.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 188","lb":"UD188","mm":1,"ev":"Udayanan builds a pavilion, a town and an image where the elephant fell, while he is with Padumai.","cl":[],"ag":["உதயணன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:11"},"ud:189":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":189,"t":["அருமறை யோதி நாம மருஞ்சன னந்த ணன்றான்","திருவுறை யுஞ்சை நின்று திகழ்கொடிக் கௌசாம் பிக்கு","வருநெறி வேயின் மீது வத்தவன் வீணை கண்டு","பொருந்தவே கொண்டு வந்து புரவலற் கீந்தா னன்றே."],"p":"","r":"A brahmin learned in the rare Veda, going from Unjai to Kausambi, finds the Vatsa king's lute on the bamboo along the road and brings it to the king.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalai","eegai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 189","lb":"UD189","mm":1,"ev":"A brahmin finds Udayanan's lute on a bamboo by the road and brings it to the king.","cl":[],"ag":["அந்தணன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:11"},"ud:190":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":190,"t":["மதுமலர்க் குழலி விண்மின் மாலைவேல் விழிமென் றோளி","பதுமைவந் தரசற் கண்டு பன்னுரை யினிது கூறும்","மதியின்வா சவதத் தைதன் வண்கையி னதனைப் போல","விதியினான் வீணை கற்க வேந்தநீ யருள்க வென்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"Padumai comes to the king and begs that he teach her the lute as he taught Vasavadatta with his own generous hand.","n":"She names Vasavadatta, her rival, as the measure of what she asks for.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalvi","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 190","lb":"UD190","mm":1,"ev":"Padumai asks Udayanan to teach her the lute as he taught Vasavadatta.","cl":[],"ag":["பதுமை"],"ec":[],"spk":["பதுமை","உதயணன்"],"ch":"ud:11"},"ud:191":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":191,"t":["பொள்ளென வெகுண்டு நோக்கிப் பொருமனத் துருகி மன்னன்","ஒள்ளிதழ்த் தத்தை தன்னை யுள்ளியே துயிலல் செய்ய","வெள்ளையே றிருந்த வெண்டா மரையினைக் கொண்டு வந்து","கள்ளவிழ் மாலைத் தெய்வங் கனவிடைக் கொடுப்பக் கண்டான்,"],"p":"","r":"The king, angered and melting in grief, falls asleep thinking of Thattai and sees in a dream a goddess bring the white lotus of the white bull.","n":"The verse does not say whom he is angry at; it may be Padumai's request, which names Vasavadatta, or his own grief.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kanavu","pirivu","anbu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 191","lb":"UD191","mm":1,"ev":"Udayanan sleeps thinking of Vasavadatta and dreams a goddess brings him a white lotus.","cl":[],"ag":["உதயணன்","தெய்வம்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:11"},"ud:192":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":192,"t":["கங்குலை நீங்கி மிக்கோர் கடவுளை வினவச் சொல்வார்","அங்கயற் கண்ணி தானு மாரழல் வீந்தா ளல்லள்","கொங்கைநற் பாவை தன்னைக் கொணரநீ பெறுவை யின்பம்","இங்குல கெங்கு மாளு மெகழிற்சுதற் பெறுவ ளென்றார்."],"p":"","r":"At dawn he asks the sages what the god gave him, and they say the fish-eyed lady did not die in the fire, that he will find joy by bringing her back, and that she will bear a fair son who rules the whole world.","n":"The verse's first reading line carries a corrupt mark, repaired by the editor; the sages speak of the lady only in images.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kanavu","oozh","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 192","lb":"UD192","mm":1,"ev":"Sages read Udayanan's dream and tell him Vasavadatta is alive and will bear a son who rules the world.","cl":[["the dream is a sign that the lost wife lives and will be won back","general","asserted",2,"tavamikkor"]],"ag":["மிக்கோர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["மிக்கோர்","உதயணன்"],"ch":"ud:11"},"ud:193":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":193,"t":["வெள்ளிய மலையின் மீதே விஞ்சைய ருலக மெல்லாம்","தெள்ளிய வாழி கொண்டு திக்கடிப் படுத்து மென்ன","ஒள்ளிய தவத்தின் மிக்கோ ருறுதவ ருரைத்த சொல்லை","வள்ளலு மகிழ்ந்து கேட்டு மாமுடி துளக்கி னானே."],"p":"","r":"The great ascetics tell that the Vidyadharas of the silver mountain will take the whole world under a bright wheel, and the generous king listens gladly and nods his head.","n":"Only the Vidyadharas' rule is named; that this refers to Udayanan's son is carried over from the previous verse.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["oozh","thavam","kanavu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 193","lb":"UD193","mm":1,"ev":"Ascetics foretell that the Vidyadharas will rule the world under a bright wheel, and Udayanan gladly accepts it.","cl":[["the son to be born will rule the world as lord of the Vidyadharas","general","reported",1,"tavamikkor"]],"ag":["மிக்கோர்","உதயணன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:11"},"ud:194":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":194,"t":["என்றவ ருரைப்பக் கேட்டே யிறைஞ்சிநன் கடிப ணிந்து","சென்றுதன் கோயில் புக்குச் சேயிழை பதுமை தன்னே","டொன்றினன் மகிழ்ந்து சென்னா ளுருமண்ணு வாவு முன்பு","வென்றிவேன் மகதன் மாந்த ரால்விடு பட்டி ருந்தான்."],"p":"","r":"He bows to their feet, returns to his palace and joins Padumai in joy; at that time Urumannuvan has already been freed by the Magadha king's men.","n":"The second half turns suddenly to Urumannuvan's release, which the verse does not explain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["oozh","natpu","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 194","lb":"UD194","mm":1,"ev":"Udayanan bows to the sages and goes to Padumai, while Urumannuvan has been released by the Magadha men.","cl":[],"ag":["உதயணன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:11"},"ud:195":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":195,"t":["மீண்டவன் வந்தூர் புக்கு வேந்தனை வணங்கி நிற்பக்","காண்டறி வாள னென்றே காவலன் புல்லிக் கொண்டு","மாண்டவன் வந்த தொப்ப வரிசையின் முகமன் கூறி","வேண்டவாந் தனிமை தீர்ந்தே விரகுட னின்புற் றானே."],"p":"","r":"Urumannuvan returns, enters the city and bows to the king, who embraces him as one come back from the dead, greets him with due honour and ends his loneliness with rejoicing.","n":"The word read here as the king's name for him, காண்டறிவாளன், is uncertain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["natpu","pirivu","amaichu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 195","lb":"UD195","mm":1,"ev":"Urumannuvan returns and Udayanan embraces him and welcomes him.","cl":[],"ag":["உருமண்ணுவன்","உதயணன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:11"},"ud:196":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":196,"t":["வாரணி கொங்கை வேற்கண் வாசவ தத்தை தானும்","ஊரணி புகழி னான யூகியு மற்றுள் ளாரும்","தாரணி கொடியி லங்குஞ் சயந்தியி னின்றும் போந்து","பாரணி கோசம் பிப்பாற் பன்மலர்க் காவுள் வந்தார்."],"p":"","r":"Vasavadatta, Yugi and the others leave Jayanti and come into the flower grove near Kausambi.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","thunai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 196","lb":"UD196","mm":1,"ev":"Vasavadatta, Yugi and their company leave Jayanti and enter a grove by Kausambi.","cl":[],"ag":["வாசவதத்தை","யூகி"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:11"},"ud:197":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":197,"t":["நயந்தநற் கேண்மை யாளர் நன்கமைந் தமைச்சர் தம்முள்","வயந்தக னுரைப்பக் கேட்டு வந்தவன் காவு சேரப்","பயந்தவ ரடியில் வீழப் பண்புடன் றழுவிக் கொண்டு","வியந்தர சியம்பு நீங்கள் வேறுடன் மறைந்த தென்னை."],"p":"","r":"Vayantakan tells the king, who comes to the grove; they fall at his feet, he embraces them kindly and asks in wonder why they hid themselves.","n":"The question is asked of the group; the next verse answers it in one man's voice.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","amaichu","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 197","lb":"UD197","mm":1,"ev":"Udayanan is told by Vayantakan, comes to the grove, embraces the fugitives and asks why they hid.","cl":[],"ag":["வயந்தகன்","உதயணன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["உதயணன்","யூகி"],"ch":"ud:11"},"ud:198":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":198,"t":["இருநில முழுதும் வானு மினிமையிற் கூடி னாலும்","திருநில மன்ன ரன்றிச் செய்பொரு ளில்லை யென்று","மருவுநூ னெறியி னின்றி வன்மையாற் சூழ்ச்சி செய்தேன்","அருளுடன் பொறுக்க வென்றா னரசனு மகிழ்வுற் றானே."],"p":"","r":"The speaker says that though earth and sky may all unite sweetly, nothing is done without the kings, and so he left the way of the treatises and plotted by force, and asks pardon; the king is glad.","n":"The speaker is taken to be Yugi from the answer to the question in verse 197; the second line is hard to construe.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["soozhchi","porai","amaichu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 198","lb":"UD198","mm":1,"ev":"A minister confesses that he used a plot against the treatises' way and asks pardon, and Udayanan is pleased.","cl":[["a plot done by force departs from the way of the treatise and needs the king's pardon","general","reported",3,"yugi"]],"ag":["உதயணன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["யூகி","உதயணன்"],"ch":"ud:11"},"ud:199":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":199,"t":["ஆர்வமிக்க கூர்ந்து நல்ல வற்புதக் கிளவி செப்பிச்","சீர்மைநற் றேவி யோடுஞ் செல்வனு மனைபு குந்தே","ஏர்பெறும் வாச வெண்ணெ யெழிலுடன் பூசி வாச","நீர்மிக வாடி மன்ன னேரிழை மாதர்க் கூட."],"p":"","r":"With fond and wondrous words the king goes into the house with his queen, anoints himself with fragrant oil and bathes, while fair-jewelled women attend.","n":"The queen meant is not named and could be Vasavadatta or Padumai.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 199","lb":"UD199","mm":1,"ev":"Udayanan and his queen go into the house, where the king is anointed and bathes.","cl":[],"ag":["உதயணன்","மாதர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:11"},"ud:200":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":200,"t":["யூகியு நீரி னாடி யுற்றுட னடிசி லுண்டான்","நாகநேர் கால மன்ன னன்குட னிருந்த போழ்தின்","பாகநேர் பிறையா நெற்றிப் பதுமையு மிதனைச் சொல்வாள்","ஏகுக செவ்வித் தந்தை யெழின்மனைக் கெழுக வென்றாள்"],"p":"","r":"Yugi bathes and eats his meal; as the king sits in happy company, Padumai tells him to go to the house of the other lady.","n":"Where she sends him, and the word செவ்வித் தந்தை, are not clear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["natpu","kaamam","porai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 200","lb":"UD200","mm":1,"ev":"Yugi bathes and eats, and Padumai bids Udayanan go.","cl":[],"ag":["யூகி","பதுமை"],"ec":[],"spk":["பதுமை","உதயணன்"],"ch":"ud:11"},"ud:201":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":201,"t":["என்றவள் சொல்ல நன்றென் றெழின்முடி மன்னன் போந்து","சென்றவண் மனைபு குந்து செல்வனு மிருந்த போழ்தின்","வென்றிவேற் கண்ணி னாளும் வெகுண்டுரை செப்பு கின்றாள்","கன்றிய காமம் வேண்டா காவல போக வென்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"As the king sits in the other lady's house, she angrily tells him that her fierce desire is no longer wanted and he should go.","n":"She is not named; she is taken to be Vasavadatta because the next verse calls her the sulking queen.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oodal","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 201","lb":"UD201","mm":1,"ev":"Vasavadatta, angry, tells Udayanan to leave.","cl":[],"ag":["உதயணன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["வாசவதத்தை","உதயணன்"],"ch":"ud:11"},"ud:202":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":202,"t":["பாடக மிரங்கும் பாதப் பதுமையி னோடு மன்னன்","கூடிய கூட்டந் தன்போற் குணந்தனை நாடி யென்ன","ஊடிய தேவி தன்னை யுணர்வினு மொளியினாலும்","நாடினின் றனக்கன் னாடா னந்திணை யல்ல ளென்றான்"],"p":"","r":"The king, who has just been with Padumai, tells the sulking queen that on searching her qualities by feeling and by light, the other is not her equal.","n":"The second half of the speech is obscure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["oodal","natpu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 202","lb":"UD202","mm":1,"ev":"Udayanan tells the angry queen that Padumai is not her equal.","cl":[],"ag":["உதயணன்","வாசவதத்தை"],"ec":[],"spk":["உதயணன்","வாசவதத்தை"],"ch":"ud:11"},"ud:203":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":203,"t":["நங்கைதன் மனங்க லங்கா நலம்புகழ்ந் தூட னீக்கி","வெங்களி யானை மற்றப் பிடியொடு மகிழ் வதேபோற்","பொங்கிள முலையில் வாசப் பூசுசாந் தழியப் புல்லிச்","சிங்கவே றனைய காளை செல்வியைச் சேர்ந்தா னன்றே."],"p":"","r":"He praises her beauty, wins her out of her sulk, and, like a rutting elephant with its mate, embraces her till the scented paste on her breasts is rubbed away.","n":"The reconciliation works by praise first and the embrace after.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oodal","punarchi","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 203","lb":"UD203","mm":1,"ev":"Udayanan praises Vasavadatta, ends her sulk and embraces her.","cl":[],"ag":["உதயணன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:11"},"ud:204":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":204,"t":["உருவிலி மதன்க ணைக ளுற்றுடன் சொரியப் பாய","இருவரும் பவளச் செவ்வா யின்னமிர் துண்டு வேல்போல்","திருநெடுங் கண்சி வப்ப வடிச்சிலம் போசை செய்ய","மருவிய வண்டு நீங்க மலர்க்குழல் சரிய வன்றே."],"p":"","r":"Under the arrows of bodiless Madan the two drink the sweet nectar of each other's lips, their long eyes redden, anklets sound, bees fly off and the hair comes loose.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","punarchi","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 204","lb":"UD204","mm":1,"ev":"Udayanan and Vasavadatta unite in love.","cl":[],"ag":["மதன்","உதயணன்","வாசவதத்தை"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:11"},"ud:205":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":205,"t":["கோதையும் சுண்ணத் தாதுங் குலைந்துடன் வீழ மிக்க","காதலிற் கழுமி யின்பக் கரையழிந் தினிதி னோடப்","போதவும் விடாது புல்லிப் புரவல னினிய னாகி","ஏதமொன் றின்ரிச் செங்கோ லினிதுடன் செலுத்து நாளில்."],"p":"","r":"Wreaths and perfumed powder fall in disorder as Udayanan, steeped in love, holds Vasavadatta close and, kind and faultless, governs with a straight sceptre.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","sengol","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 205","lb":"UD205","mm":1,"ev":"Udayanan lives in love with Vasavadatta and rules justly.","cl":[],"ag":["உதயணன்","வாசவதத்தை"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:12"},"ud:206":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":206,"t":["ஆனதன் னாம மிட்ட வாழிமோ திரத்தை யீந்தே","ஊனுமிழ் கதிர்வேன் மன்ன னுருமண்ணு வாவு தன்னைச்","சேனைநற் பதிநீ யென்று திருநிகர் பதுமை தோழி","ஈனமி லிராச னையை யெழில்வேள்வி யாற்கொ டுத்தான்."],"p":"","r":"The king gives a ring bearing his own name and the post of army chief to Urumannuvan, and gives a woman named in the verse in marriage at a fair sacrifice; the last line is hard to read.","n":"The woman given in marriage in line 4 is named only by a word the verse does not make clear, and the giver of the bride may be Padumai's friend named in line 3.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["eegai","vendhan","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 206","lb":"UD206","mm":1,"ev":"Udayanan rewards Urumannuvan with a ring and the command of the army.","cl":[],"ag":["உதயணன்","உருமண்ணுவன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:12"},"ud:207":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":207,"t":["சயந்தியம் பதியுஞ் சால விலாவாண நகரு மீந்தே","இயந்தநல் லிடப கற்கு மினியபுட் பகத்தைச் சூழ்ந்த","செயந்தரு வளநன் னாடு சிறந்தவைம் பதும ளித்து","வயந்தகன் றனக்கு வாய்ந்த பதினெட்டூர் கொடுத்தா னன்றே."],"p":"","r":"Udayanan gives the cities Jayanti and Ilavanam, the rich land round Pushpakam and fifty more, and eighteen towns to Vayantakan.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","vendhan","nanri"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 207","lb":"UD207","mm":1,"ev":"Udayanan rewards Vayantakan and others with towns and lands.","cl":[],"ag":["உதயணன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:12"},"ud:208":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":208,"t":["ஆதிநன் மாமன் வைத்த வருந்திறை யளக்கு நல்ல","சேதிநன் னாட்டை யூகிக் காகநற் றிறத்தி னீந்து","சோதிநல் லரசன் மிக்க சூழ்ச்சியின் மற்றோர்க் கெல்லாம்","வீதிநன் னகர்கள் விட்டு வீறுடன் வீற்றி ருந்தான்."],"p":"","r":"He gives the Cheti country, once set by his uncle as a tribute-paying land, to Yugi, and with clever counsel leaves the other towns free and sits in glory.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","arasu","soozhchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 208","lb":"UD208","mm":1,"ev":"Udayanan gives the Cheti country to Yugi and rules in peace.","cl":[],"ag":["உதயணன்","யூகி"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:12"},"ud:209":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":209,"t":["பேசரும் பெருமை சால்ப்ரச் சோதனன் றூதர் வந்து","வாசகந் தன்னைக் காட்ட வந்தவன் மனம கிழ்ந்து","வாசவ தத்தை யோடு மன்னிய வமைச்சர் கூட","வாசகஞ் சொல்க வென்று வரிசையிற் கேட்கின் றானே."],"p":"","r":"Envoys of the great Pradyotan arrive and show his letter; Udayanan, glad, asks that the message be read, with Vasavadatta and his ministers beside him.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thoothu","amaichu","kelvi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 209","lb":"UD209","mm":1,"ev":"Pradyotan's envoys bring a letter, and Udayanan has it read before Vasavadatta and his ministers.","cl":[],"ag":["தூதர்","உதயணன்","வாசவதத்தை"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:12"},"ud:210":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":210,"t":["பிரச்சோத னன்றா னென்னும் பெருமக னோலை தன்னை","உரவுச்சேர் கழற்கான் மிக்க உதயண குமரன் காண்க","வாவுச்சீர்க் குருகு லத்தின் வண்மையான் கோடல் வேண்டி","வரைவனச் சார றன்னில் வன்பொறி யானை விட்டேன்."],"p":"","r":"The letter, in Pradyotan's name, greets the king and says that, wishing to win the generosity of the Kuru line, he had set a cunning elephant in the mountain forest.","n":"Here the king of Ujjain himself owns to the trick that Yugi's counsel had once matched with a false elephant of its own; the letter treats it as a way of winning a worthy man.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vanjam","thoothu","mannar"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 210","lb":"UD210","mm":1,"ev":"Pradyotan's letter admits that the elephant in the forest was his snare.","cl":[],"ag":["பிரத்தியோதன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["பிரத்தியோதன்","உதயணன்"],"ch":"ud:12"},"ud:211":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":211,"t":["கலந்தவை காண வந்த காவலர் நின்னைப் பற்றிச்","சிலந்திநூ றன்னா லார்த்த சிங்கம்போ லார்த்துக் கொண்டு","நலந்திகழ் தேரி னேற்றி நன்குவுஞ் சையினி தன்னிற்","பெலந்திரி சிறையில் வைத்த பிழையது பொறுக்க வென்றும்."],"p":"","r":"The letter goes on: the guards who came to see the thing seized you with spider's thread like a lion, set you on a chariot and shut you in the prison; pardon the fault.","n":"The speaker is carried on from verse 210; the verse itself does not name him.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vanjam","thoothu","panivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 211","lb":"UD211","mm":1,"ev":"The letter admits Udayanan's seizure and imprisonment and asks pardon.","cl":[],"ag":["காவலர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["பிரத்தியோதன்","உதயணன்"],"ch":"ud:12"},"ud:212":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":212,"t":["கோமானே யெனவே யென்னைக் கோடனீ வேண்டு மென்றும்","மாமனான் மருக னீயென் மாமுறை யாயிற் றென்றும்","ஆமாகும் யூகி தன்னை யனுப்ப யான் காண்டல் வேண்டும்","பூமாலை மார்ப வென்றும் பொறித்தவா சகத்தைக் கேட்டான்."],"p":"","r":"The letter asks to be taken as lord, pleads the tie of uncle and nephew, asks that Yugi be sent so that the writer may see him, and Udayanan hears the sealed message.","n":"The first line is hard to construe, and it is not clear whether the writer asks to be taken as king or as kin.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["natpu","sutram","thoothu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 212","lb":"UD212","mm":1,"ev":"Udayanan hears the end of the letter, which asks him to send Yugi.","cl":[],"ag":["உதயணன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["பிரத்தியோதன்","உதயணன்"],"ch":"ud:12"},"ud:213":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":213,"t":["மன்னவ னனுப்ப யூகி மாநக ருஞ்சை புக்கு","மன்னர்மா வேந்தன் றன்னை வணங்கினன் கண்டிருப்ப","மன்னனு முடியசைத்த மைச்சனை நெடிது நோக்கி","மன்னிய வுவகை தன்னான் மகிழ்வுரை விளம்பி னானே."],"p":"","r":"At the king's sending Yugi goes to Ujjain and bows to its king; the king nods his crowned head, looks long at the minister and speaks words of joy.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thoothu","amaichu","panivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 213","lb":"UD213","mm":1,"ev":"Yugi, sent by Udayanan, goes to Ujjain and is greeted gladly by Pradyotan.","cl":[],"ag":["உதயணன்","யூகி","பிரத்தியோதன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:12"},"ud:214":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":214,"t":["சீர்ப்பொழி லுஞ்சையுஞ் சீர்க்கௌ சாம்பியும்","பார்தனில் வேற்றுமை பண்ணுதல் வேண்டோம்","ஆர்மிகு முரச மறைகென நகரில்","தார்மிகு வேந்தன் றரத்தினிற் செப்பினன்."],"p":"","r":"The king bids a drum be sounded through the city: Ujjain and Kausambi are two cities of one earth and he wants no distinction made between them.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","natpu","samanilai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 214","lb":"UD214","mm":1,"ev":"Pradyotan has it proclaimed by drum that Ujjain and Kausambi are not to be distinguished.","cl":[],"ag":["பிரத்தியோதன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["பிரத்தியோதன்","நகரத்தார்"],"ch":"ud:12"},"ud:215":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":215,"t":["தருமநன் னூல்வகை சாலங் காயனோ","டருமதி யூகியு மன்பி னுரைத்தான்","பெருவிறல் வேந்தனும் பெறுத லரிதெனத்","திருநிறை யூகியைச் செல்வன் மகிழ்ந்தான்."],"p":"","r":"Yugi speaks lovingly of dharma texts with Salankayan; the king, finding such a man hard to get, rejoices in the minister.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalvi","amaichu","anbu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 215","lb":"UD215","mm":1,"ev":"Yugi and Salankayan talk of dharma texts and Pradyotan is pleased with Yugi.","cl":[],"ag":["யூகி","சாலங்காயன்","பிரத்தியோதன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:12"},"ud:216":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":216,"t":["கவ்விய தகலமுங் காட்சிக் கினிமையும்","சொல்லருஞ் சூட்சியுஞ் சொற்பொருட் டிண்மையும்","வல்லமை யிவனலான் மாந்த ரில்லையின்","றெல்லையில் குணத்தின னென்றுரை செய்தனன்."],"p":"","r":"The king declares that Yugi's breadth, his pleasant look, his subtle counsel and the firmness of his word are such that no other man can match him, and his virtue has no bound.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["amaichu","arivu","pugazh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 216","lb":"UD216","mm":1,"ev":"Pradyotan praises Yugi as unmatched among men.","cl":[],"ag":["பிரத்தியோதன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["பிரத்தியோதன்",""],"ch":"ud:12"},"ud:217":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":217,"t":["இன்னவற் பெற்றவர்க் கேற்ற வரசியல்","இன்னவ ரின்றி யிலையர சென்றே","இன்னன நீடிய வியல்பிற் பிறவுரை","மன்னவ னாடி மகிழ்வித் திருந்தபின்."],"p":"","r":"The king, pleased, speaks of the rule fit for those who have such a man and adds that without such men there is no kingship.","n":"The king who says this had been the captor of Yugi's lord, and the work lets him say it as praise of the man who has just outwitted him.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","amaichu","thunai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 217","lb":"UD217","mm":1,"ev":"Pradyotan tells Yugi that a king's rule rests on ministers like him.","cl":[["a kingdom cannot stand without able counsellors","general","asserted",2,"pradyotan"]],"ag":["பிரத்தியோதன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["பிரத்தியோதன்",""],"ch":"ud:12"},"ud:218":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":218,"t":["சாலங் காயன் சகோதர மானநன்","னீலங் காய்ந்த நெடுவேல் விழிநுதற்","பாலங் கோர்பிறை யாம்படா வெம்முலைக்","கோலங் காரன்ன கூரெயி றாப்பியும்."],"p":"","r":"The verse names Salankayan's sister, a woman with a crescent brow, spear-sharp eyes and fine teeth, called Aappi, as one of the brides; the grammar runs into the next verse.","n":"The sentence is not complete until verse 219, and her name is not certain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","sutram","ilvaazhkkai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 218","lb":"UD218","mm":1,"ev":"Salankayan's sister is named as one of the brides to be given to Yugi.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:12"},"ud:219":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":219,"t":["பரதகன் றங்கை பான்மொழி வேற்கணி","திருநி லம்புகழ் திலதமா சேனையும்","பெருநில மறிய மணமிகப் பெற்றுடன்","அரிய யூகிக் கரசன் கொடுத்தனன்."],"p":"","r":"The king gives Yugi in marriage Tilakasenai, sister of Paratakan, praised through the land, together with Salankayan's sister, in a wedding the wide earth hears of.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ilvaazhkkai","eegai","natpu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 219","lb":"UD219","mm":1,"ev":"Pradyotan gives two brides to Yugi in marriage.","cl":[],"ag":["பிரத்தியோதன்","யூகி"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:12"},"ud:220":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":220,"t":["சென்மதி நீயெனச் செல்ல விடுத்தனன்","நன்முது நகர்முன் னாடிப் போவெனப்","பன்மதி சனங்கள் பரவி வழிபட","வென்மதி யூகிபோய் வேந்தனைக் கண்டனன்."],"p":"","r":"The king bids him go and walk the old city first and sends him off; the many people praise and bow, and Yugi goes and sees his king.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["panivu","amaichu","pugazh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 220","lb":"UD220","mm":1,"ev":"Pradyotan lets Yugi go, and Yugi returns to Udayanan.","cl":[],"ag":["பிரத்தியோதன்","யூகி"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:12"},"ud:221":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":221,"t":["வத்தவ குமரன் பாதம் வந்தனை செய்த மைச்சன்","இத்தல முழுது மாளு மினியநன் மாமன் சொன்ன","ஒத்தநன் மொழியைக் கேட்டே யுவந்துட னிருந்த போழ்தில்","சித்திரப் பாவை மார்கள் செல்வனை வணங்கிச் சொல்வார்."],"p":"","r":"Yugi bows to the prince's feet and tells him his uncle's fitting words, and as the king sits glad the picture-like ladies bow before him and are about to speak.","n":"The e-text prints this verse without its number.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["panivu","amaichu","anbu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 221","lb":"UD221","mm":1,"ev":"Yugi reports Pradyotan's words to Udayanan, and the court ladies come to him.","cl":[],"ag":["யூகி","உதயணன்","சித்திரப் பாவை மார்கள்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:12"},"ud:222":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":222,"t":["பந்தடி காண்க வென்னப் பார்த்திப னினிய னாகிக்","கந்துகப் பூசல் காணக் கலிற்றின்மீ தேறி வந்து","கொந்தலர் மாலை மாதர் குழுவுடன் சூழ நிற்ப","வந்தனள் பதுமை தோழி வனப்பிரா சனையென் பாளாம்."],"p":"","r":"The king, glad, mounts an elephant to watch the ball-play and the ball contest while the ladies stand round him, and Vanapirasanai, Padumai's friend, comes.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalai","inbam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 222","lb":"UD222","mm":1,"ev":"Udayanan watches ball-play, and Vanapirasanai arrives.","cl":[],"ag":["உதயணன்","சித்திரப் பாவை மார்கள்","வனப்பிராசனை"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:12"},"ud:223":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":223,"t":["ஓரேழு பந்து கொண்டே யொன்றென்றி னெற்றிச் செல்லப்","பாரெழு துகளு மாடப் பலகல னொலிப்ப வாடிச்","சீரெழு மாயிரங்கை சிறந்தவ ளடித்து விட்டாள்","காரெழு குழலி நல்ல காஞ்சன மாலை வந்தாள்."],"p":"","r":"She keeps seven balls going, each thrown up in turn, while dust rises, ornaments ring and her thousand strokes end; then Kanchanamalai, dark of hair, comes.","n":"Line 3 is hard, and it is not sure whether she or her hands stop the play before Kanchanamalai steps in.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kalai","azhagu","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 223","lb":"UD223","mm":1,"ev":"Vanapirasanai plays with seven balls, and Kanchanamalai arrives to take her turn.","cl":[],"ag":["வனப்பிராசனை","காஞ்சன மாலை"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:12"},"ud:224":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":224,"t":["வேய்மிகு தடக்கை தன்னால் வியந்துபந் துடனே யேந்திக்","காய்பொனின் கலன்க ளார்ப்பக் கார்மயி லாட்டம் போல","ஆயிரத் தைஞ்ஞா றேற்றி யடித்தன ளகல வப்பால்","ஆய்புகழ்ப் பதுமை தாதி யயிராப திபந்து கொண்டாள்."],"p":"","r":"A maid of Padumai takes up the ball with her bamboo-slim broad hand, strikes it fifteen hundred times with her ornaments ringing like a peacock dancing in the rain cloud, and steps back.","n":"The maid's name is written across a word break in line 4; the verse opens a ball-play contest whose two sides are only implied.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kalai","ilamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 224","lb":"UD224","mm":1,"ev":"Padumai's maid takes the ball, strikes it fifteen hundred times and then withdraws.","cl":[],"ag":["அயிராபதி"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:13"},"ud:225":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":225,"t":["சீரேறு மிமில்போற் கொண்டைச் சில்வண்டுந் தேனும் பாடப்","பாரோர்க ளினிது நோக்கும் பல்கலஞ் சிலம்போ டார்ப்ப","ஈராயி ரங்கை யேற்றி யிருகரத் தடித்து விட்டாள்","தோராத வழகி தத்தை தோழி விசுவ லேகை வந்தாள்."],"p":"","r":"Dattai's friend Visuvalekai comes forward and strikes the ball two thousand times with both hands, her ornaments and anklets sounding.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalai","natpu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 225","lb":"UD225","mm":1,"ev":"Visuvalekai, Dattai's friend, strikes the ball two thousand times with both hands.","cl":[],"ag":["விசுவலேகை"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:13"},"ud:226":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":226,"t":["கருங்குழ னெடுவேற் கண்ணாள் காரிகை பந்தெ டுத்துப்","பெருங்கல னினிதி னார்ப்பப் பெய்வளை கலக லென்ன","ஒருங்குமுன் கையின் மீதி லோரைஞ்ஞூ றடித்து விட்டாள்","கருங்கணி பதுமை தோழி காரிகை யொருத்தி வந்தாள்"],"p":"","r":"Another friend of Padumai, a woman of dark long hair and spear-like eyes, takes the ball and strikes it five hundred times along her forearm to the clash of her bangles.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalai","natpu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 226","lb":"UD226","mm":1,"ev":"A friend of Padumai strikes the ball five hundred times along her forearm.","cl":[],"ag":["தோழி"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:13"},"ud:227":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":227,"t":["ஆரியை யென்னு நாம வரிவைகைக் கொண்டு பந்தைச்","சேரமின் சிலம்பு மார்ப்பச் சிறுநுதன் முத்த ரும்பச்","சீரின்மூ வாயி ரங்கை சிறந்தவ ளடித்த பின்பு","பேரிசைத் தத்தை யாயம் பெருங்குழாத் தினிதி னோக்காள்."],"p":"","r":"A woman named Ariyai takes the ball and strikes it three thousand times, and after that the company of Dattai no longer looks at the gathering with pleasure.","n":"The verse does not say which side Ariyai plays for; the loss of heart in Dattai's company in the last line suggests she is of the other side.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kalai","maanam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 227","lb":"UD227","mm":1,"ev":"Ariyai strikes the ball three thousand times, and Dattai's company loses heart.","cl":[],"ag":["ஆரியை"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:13"},"ud:228":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":228,"t":["அருவரு மேற்பா ரின்றி யோர்ந்தவ ணெஞ்சங் கூர்ந்து","திருநுதன் மாது நொந்து சிறப்பின்றி யிருந்த போழ்தின்","மருவுகோ சலத்து மன்னன் மகளுரு வரிவை நாமம்","சுரிகுழன் மான னீகை சொலற்கருங் கற்பி னாளே."],"p":"","r":"With no one left to answer the score, Dattai sits troubled and joyless; the verse then names a new woman, Mananigai, the daughter of the king of Kosala, of unspeakable chastity.","n":"Mananigai is introduced as the daughter of the king of Kosala and praised for her chastity before she does anything, so the poem marks her virtue first and her skill second.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maanam","karpu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 228","lb":"UD228","mm":1,"ev":"Dattai sits dejected, and Mananigai, the Kosala king's daughter, is introduced.","cl":[],"ag":["வாசவதத்தை"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:13"},"ud:229":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":229,"t":["இளம்பிறை நுதல்வேற் கண்ணி யினியவிற் புருவ வேய்த்தோள்","இளங்கிளி மொழிநற் கொங்கை யீடில்பொற் கலச மல்குல்","இளமணிப் படம்பொன் வாழை யிருகுறங் காலம் பண்டி","இளம்புற வடிக ளாமை யிடைமின் பூங் குழலி னாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes Mananigai limb by limb, from forehead like a young moon to waist, thighs and feet, with images of the bamboo, the parrot, the golden pot and the plantain.","n":"Line 3 and 4 images are heavy with rare words and may be damaged; the order of the limbs is only partly clear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 229","lb":"UD229","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:13"},"ud:230":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":230,"t":["ஆங்கொரு கார ணத்திற் றத்தைபால் வந்தி ருந்தாள்","பூங்கொடி தோல்வி கண்டு பொறுப்பிலா மனத்த ளாகித்","தீங்குறு தத்தை தன்னைச் சீருடன் வணங்கிப் போந்து","பாங்குறு மிலக்க ணங்கள் பந்தடி பலவுஞ் சொன்னாள்."],"p":"","r":"Mananigai, who has come to Dattai on some business, cannot bear to see her defeated, bows to her with respect and tells her the rules of ball-play of many kinds.","n":"The verse says she came for another reason and does not name it; her instruction of Dattai, not her own play, answers the defeat.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kalvi","kalai","panivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 230","lb":"UD230","mm":1,"ev":"Mananigai bows to the defeated Dattai and tells her the rules of ball-play.","cl":[],"ag":["மானனீகை"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:13"},"ud:231":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":231,"t":["மூன்றுபத் திரண்டுநன் மூரிப்பந்தெ டுத்துடன்","தோன்றிரண்டு கையினுந் தொடுத்தினி தடித்தலும்","ஆன்றகையி னோட்டலு மலங்கலுட் கரத்தலும்","ஈன்றரவி னாடலு மிறைஞ்சிநிமிர்ந் தாடினாள்."],"p":"","r":"Taking a large ball, Mananigai strikes it with both hands in turn, sends it running, hides it in the garland, and plays it like a snake that has just given birth, bowing and rising as she plays.","n":"Line 3 is hard: the gloss of the ball hidden in a garland and of the snake dance is mine; the numeral in line 1 reads as thirty-two.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kalai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 231","lb":"UD231","mm":1,"ev":"Mananigai plays the ball with both hands, running it, hiding it in the garland and making it dance.","cl":[],"ag":["மானனீகை"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:13"},"ud:232":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":232,"t":["முட்டில்கோல வட்டணை முயன்றுபத்தி யிட்டுடன்","நட்டணை நடனமு நயந்தினிதி னாடவும்","பட்டுடையின் வேர்நுதற் பாங்கினிற் றுடைப்பவும்","இட்டிடை துவளவு மினியபந் தடித்தனள்."],"p":"","r":"Mananigai plays on, dancing the round and standing dances while the sweat on her brow is wiped on her silk and her slender waist bends.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalai","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 232","lb":"UD232","mm":1,"ev":"Mananigai keeps up the dance-steps of ball-play, sweating and bending at the waist.","cl":[],"ag":["மானனீகை"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:13"},"ud:233":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":233,"t":["பரிவுகொண் டனைவரும் பண்டறியோ மென்மரும்","விரிவுவான் மடந்தையோ வியந்திரியோ வென்மரும்","தரிவுவிச்சை மங்கையோ தான் பவண நாரியோ","விரிவுறிந் நிலத்திடை வேறுகண்ட தில்லென்பார்."],"p":"","r":"All who watch are amazed, saying they have never seen the like, and ask whether she is a woman of the sky, a goddess of the lesser order, a woman of the nether world or a vidyadhara, and that there is none like her on earth.","n":"Line 3 is damaged (தரிவுவிச்சை), and the classes named are read as a goddess of the vyantara order and a woman of the bhavana world.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","perumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 233","lb":"UD233","mm":1,"ev":"The onlookers wonder at Mananigai and ask whether she is a divine woman.","cl":[],"ag":["அனைவரும்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:13"},"ud:234":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":234,"t":["காந்தணன் ஒருமுன் கைக் கன்னியன் விரலினின்","ஏந்தினளெ டுத்தடிக்க விறைவளை யொலிவிடப்","போந்தன விசும்பினும் பொங்குநன் னிலத்தினும்","சூழ்ந்துகந் தெழுந்தன சூறாவளிக் ளென்னவே."],"p":"","r":"When she takes up the ball on her fingertips and strikes it, her bangles ring and the balls rise through sky and earth like whirlwinds.","n":"Line 1 is corrupt (காந்தணன் ஒருமுன் கைக் கன்னியன்); only the ringing bangles and the whirlwind simile are clear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kalai","oli"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 234","lb":"UD234","mm":1,"ev":"Mananigai strikes the ball so that it rises through sky and earth to the sound of her bangles.","cl":[],"ag":["மானனீகை"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:13"},"ud:235":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":235,"t":["சிலம்புகிண் கிணிசில சீர்க்கலன்க ளார்ப்பவும்","வலம்புரி மணிவடம் வளரிள முலைமிசை","நலம்பெற வசைந்திட நங்கைபத் தடித்திடப்","புலம்புவண்டு தேனினம் பூங்குழன்மே லாடவே."],"p":"","r":"As the woman strikes the ball ten times, her anklets and small bells ring, the pearl strands sway on her young breasts, and bees and honey-gatherers hum above her hair.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","kalai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 235","lb":"UD235","mm":1,"ev":"Mananigai strikes ten, her ornaments ringing and bees gathering about her hair.","cl":[],"ag":["மானனீகை"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:13"},"ud:236":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":236,"t":["பாடகச்சி லம்பொலி பண்ணினு மினிதெனச்","சூடகத் தொலிநல சுரருடைய கீதமே","ஆடகம ணித்தொனி யரசுளங் கவர்ந்துடன்","கூடகம னத்தினற்கு மரனினிய னாயினன்."],"p":"","r":"The sound of her anklets and bracelets is sweeter than any music or the songs of the gods, and wins the king's heart.","n":"The last line is unclear (கூடகமனத்தினற்கு மரனினிய னாயினன்); I read only that the king is won and turns kind.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kalai","kaamam","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 236","lb":"UD236","mm":1,"ev":"The sound of Mananigai's ornaments wins the king's heart.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:13"},"ud:237":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":237,"t":["மாறுமா றெழுவதும் வகையுடன் னிழிவதும்","வீறுமாத ராடவும் வேந்தனுடன் மாதரும்","கூறுமிவ ளல்லது குவலயத்தி னில்லையென்","றேறுபந்தி னெற்றிக்கை யெண்ணாயிர மடித்தனள்."],"p":"","r":"Saying that, rising and falling, there is none but she in all the world who plays so before ladies and a king, she strikes the ball eight thousand times with her forehead-hand.","n":"The count is the highest of the contest and beats Ariyai's three thousand in verse 227 by a wide margin; line 2 on the king and women is not clear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kalai","perumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 237","lb":"UD237","mm":1,"ev":"Mananigai strikes the ball eight thousand times, exceeding every earlier score.","cl":[],"ag":["மானனீகை"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:13"},"ud:238":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":238,"t":["பிடிமிசை மாதர் போந்து பெருமணக் கோயில் புக்கார்","கடிமலர்க் கோதை மன்னன் காவிநன் விழிமா னீகை","இடிமின்னி னுசுப்பி னாளை யின்புறப் புணர்ந்தி ருப்பத்","துடியிடைத் தத்தை கேட்டுத் தோற்றிய சீற்றத் தானாள்."],"p":"","r":"The women ride a she-elephant to the great wedding temple, where the king takes the dark-eyed Mananigai in joy, and Dattai hears of it and is seized with anger.","n":"The verse turns the contest into a courtship: the king reaches Mananigai through the skill she showed. Dattai's anger is her first response to the second queen.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","vekuli","oodal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 238","lb":"UD238","mm":1,"ev":"The party rides to the wedding temple, the king unites with Mananigai, and Dattai hears and grows angry.","cl":[],"ag":["மாதர்","உதயணன்","வாசவதத்தை"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:13"},"ud:239":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":239,"t":["துன்னிரு ணீங்கிக் காலை தூமலர் கொண்டு தத்தை","மன்னவ னடிவ ணங்க மனமகிழ் வின்றி நின்ற","அன்னமென் னடையி னாளை யகமகிழ் குளிரக் கூறி","மன்னன்மா னீகை தன்னை மணமிகச் செய்து கொண்டான்."],"p":"","r":"At dawn Dattai brings flowers and worships the king's feet while Mananigai stands joyless; the king speaks to her to comfort her, and then marries her with full rites.","n":"The speech of consolation is only reported, and the verse does not say what the king told her. Dattai's worship follows her anger of verse 238 with no word on how it was resolved.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["oodal","ilvaazhkkai","panivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 239","lb":"UD239","mm":1,"ev":"Dattai worships the king with flowers; he comforts Mananigai and marries her fully.","cl":[],"ag":["வாசவதத்தை","உதயணன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:13"},"ud:240":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":240,"t":["தேவியர் மூவர் கூடத் தேர்மன்னன் சேர்ந்து சென்னாட்","காவின்முன் மாலைசூட்டிக் காரிகை கலந்து விட்ட","பூவின்மஞ் சரியைப் போலும் பொற்புநல் விரிசி கையைத்","தாவில்சீர் வேள்வி தன்னாற் றரணீசன் மணந்தா னன்றே."],"p":"","r":"With the three queens present the king marries Virisikai, like a bunch of flowers set among the garland, by a proper fire sacrifice.","n":"The marriage is by vedic sacrifice (வேள்வி), as the verse names it; the work does not comment on that rite in a Jain epic.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ilvaazhkkai","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 240","lb":"UD240","mm":1,"ev":"The king marries Virisikai in the presence of his three queens, by sacrifice.","cl":[],"ag":["உதயணன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:13"},"ud:241":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":241,"t":["நட்புடைக் கற்பு மாதர் நால்வரு மன்ன னுள்ளத்","துட்புடை யிருப்ப நாளு மொருகுறை வின்றித் துய்த்துத்","திட்புடை மன்னர் வந்து திறையளந் தடிவ ணங்க","நட்புடை நாட்டை யெல்லா நரபதி யாண்டு சென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"The four chaste women keep a place in the king's heart and he enjoys them without lack, while firm kings come to pay tribute and bow at his feet, and the king rules all his friendly land.","n":"The picture of an enjoying, tribute-taking king closes the chapter with no sign of the renunciation that the epic's own tradition would later expect.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["karpu","ilvaazhkkai","arasu","perumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 241","lb":"UD241","mm":1,"ev":"The king lives happily with his four wives and rules while other kings bring tribute.","cl":[],"ag":["உதயணன்","மன்னர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:13"},"ud:242":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":242,"t":["எத்திக்கு மடிப்ப டுத்தி யெழில்பெறச் செங்கோல் செல்லும்","பெற்றிசெய் வேந்தன் றன்னைப் பெருமைவேற் றானை மன்னை","வித்தைசெய் சனங்கண் மாந்தர் வியந்தடி வணங்க மின்னும்","முற்றிழை மாலைத் தத்தை முனிவில்சீர் மயற்கை யானாள்."],"p":"","r":"Because the king's sceptre runs justly over every quarter, skilled folk and common men bow to his feet, and Dattai, the queen in her garland, falls into a longing.","n":"The word மயற்கை (longing, bewilderment) opens the next verse, where the longing is named as a wish to fly.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vendhan","sengol","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 242","lb":"UD242","mm":1,"ev":"People marvel and bow at the just king's feet, and the queen Dattai falls into a longing.","cl":[],"ag":["சனங்கள்","வாசவதத்தை"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:14"},"ud:243":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":243,"t":["நிறைபுகழ் வனப்பு நங்கை நிலவிய வுதரந் தன்னுட்","பிறையென வளரச் செல்வன் பேதையும் விசும்பிற் செல்லும்","குறைபெறு வேட்கை கேட்ட கொற்றவன் மனத்தி னெண்ணி","அறைபுக ழமைச்சர் தம்மை யழைத்தனன் வினவி னானே."],"p":"","r":"As the child grows in the queen's womb like a crescent, she is seized by a lack in her longing, to go in the sky; the king hears it, thinks it over and calls his ministers to ask.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["makkal","avaa","soozhchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 243","lb":"UD243","mm":1,"ev":"The queen in pregnancy longs to travel the sky, and the king calls his ministers to ask what can be done.","cl":[],"ag":["வாசவதத்தை","உதயணன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:14"},"ud:244":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":244,"t":["உருமண்ணுவா விதனைச் செப்பு முன்னொரு தினத்தின் வேட்டைப்","பெருமலை வனத்தி னீரின் வேட்கையாற் பிறந்த துன்பம்","மருவுறு வருத்தங் கண்டோர் வானவன் வந்து தோன்றிப்","பெருமநீ ருண்ணக் காட்டிப் பெரிடர் தீர்த்தானன்றோ."],"p":"","r":"Urumannuva recalls to the king that once, when thirst overcame him on a hunt in the great hill forest, a god appeared and showed him water and ended his great distress.","n":"Line 1 can be read as the king bidding Urumannuva speak, or as Urumannuva beginning; the sense of the recalled rescue is the same.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["nanri","thunai","idumbai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 244","lb":"UD244","mm":1,"ev":"Urumannuva reminds the king how a god once appeared to the thirsty king in a hunt and showed him water.","cl":[],"ag":["அமரன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["உருமண்ணுவன்","உதயணன்"],"ch":"ud:14"},"ud:245":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":245,"t":["இன்னமோ ரிடர்வந் தாலு மென்னைநீர் நினைக்க வென்று","மன்னுமோர் மந்தி ரந்தான் வண்மையி னளித்துப் போந்தான்","சொன்னமா மந்தி ரத்தைச் சூழ்ச்சியி னினைக்க வென்றான்","பின்னவ னினைத்த போழ்தே பீடுடை யமரன் வந்தான்."],"p":"","r":"The god, when he left, gave a lasting mantra and said to think of it in any trouble; the king now thinks of it with care and the proud god arrives at once.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["mandhiram","thunai","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 245","lb":"UD245","mm":1,"ev":"Udayanan calls to mind the mantra the god gave him, and the god comes.","cl":[],"ag":["அமரன்","உதயணன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:14"},"ud:246":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":246,"t":["பலவுப சாரஞ் சொல்லப் பார்மன்னற் கிதனைச் செப்பும்","நலிவுசெய் சிறையிற் பட்ட நாளிலுஞ் சவரர் சுற்றி","வலியவந் தலைத்த போதும் வாசவ தத்தை நின்னைச்","சிலதினம் பிரிந்த போதுஞ் செற்றோரைச் செகுத்த போதும்."],"p":"","r":"After many courtesies the god speaks to the king: in the days of prison, when the savaras closed in and harassed him, when he was parted from Vasavadatta, and when he killed his enemies...","n":"The sentence is left unfinished here and is completed in the next verse: in none of these troubles did the king call on him.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","thunai","idumbai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 246","lb":"UD246","mm":1,"ev":"The god begins to list the king's past dangers, prison, the savara attack, the parting from Vasavadatta and his killing of foes.","cl":[],"ag":["அமரன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["அமரன்","உதயணன்"],"ch":"ud:14"},"ud:247":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":247,"t":["மித்திர னென்றே யென்னை வேண்டிமுன் னினைத்தா யில்லை","பொற்றிரு மார்ப விந்நாட் புதுமையி னினைத்த தென்னை","உத்தரஞ் சொல்க வென்ன வொளியுமி ழமரன் கேட்கச்","சித்திரப் பாவை வானிற் செலவினை வேட்டா ளென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"The god says the king never once thought of him as a friend in those troubles and asks why he has remembered him on this strange day; the king answers that the queen has wished to go in the sky.","n":"Lines 1 to 3 are the god's question and line 4 is the king's answer, given by the narrator in indirect speech. The god treats the bond as one that needs a call.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","thunai","avaa"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 247","lb":"UD247","mm":1,"ev":"The god asks why the king calls on him now, and the king answers that the queen longs to travel the sky.","cl":[],"ag":["அமரன்","உதயணன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["அமரன்","உதயணன்"],"ch":"ud:14"},"ud:248":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":248,"t":["எங்களிற் கரும மாக்கு மியல்புள் தீர்த்துக் கொண்டோம்","திங்களின் முகத்திற் பாவை செலவுநின் னாலே யன்றி","எங்களி லாகாதென்றிப் பொழுதுனை நினைந்தே னென்ன","நன்கினி யமரன் கேட்டு நரபதி கேளி தென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"The king explains that what they could do among themselves they have done, but the queen's travel in the sky cannot come about except through the god, and so he thought of him now; the god hears it well and tells the king to listen.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","mandhiram","soozhchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 248","lb":"UD248","mm":1,"ev":"Udayanan tells the god why he called him, and the god bids him listen.","cl":[],"ag":["உதயணன்","அமரன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["உதயணன்","அமரன்"],"ch":"ud:14"},"ud:249":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":249,"t":["வெள்ளிய மலையிற் றேவன் விரைக் குழலாள் வயிற்றின்","உள்ளவின் பத்தி னாலே வுலவுவான் சிந்தை யானாள்","கள்ளவிழ் மாலை வேந்தன் கதிர்மணித் தேரி னேறிப்","புள்ளெனப் பறக்க மந்த்ர மீதெனக் கொடுத்துப் போந்தான்."],"p":"","r":"The god tells the king of a god of the silver mountain who has come to dwell in the fragrant-haired woman's womb, whose delight makes her wander in thought; the king then mounts the jewelled chariot, and the mantra is given for flying like a bird.","n":"Lines 1 and 2 are hard to read, and who gives the mantra in lines 3 to 4 is inferred from the story, since no one is named; the verse seems to trace the longing to the being in her womb.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["mandhiram","makkal","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 249","lb":"UD249","mm":1,"ev":"The god explains the cause of the queen's longing and gives the king a mantra to make the chariot fly.","cl":[],"ag":["அமரன்","உதயணன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:14"},"ud:250":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":250,"t":["வெற்றித்தே ரேறி வென்வேல் வேந்தனுந் தேவி தானும்","மற்றுநற் றோழன் மாரும் வரிசையி னேறி வானம்","உற்றந்த வழிய தேகி யுத்தர திக்கி னின்ற","பெற்றிநல் லிமயங் கண்டு பேர்ந்துகீழ்த் திசையுஞ் சென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"The king and queen and their friends mount the victory chariot in order, rise into the sky, see the Himalaya in the north and then turn to the east.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inbam","thunai","mandhiram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 250","lb":"UD250","mm":1,"ev":"Udayanan, the queen and friends fly the chariot north to the Himalaya and then east.","cl":[],"ag":["உதயணன்","வாசவதத்தை","தோழன்மார்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:14"},"ud:251":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":251,"t":["உதயநற் கிரி.யும் கண்டே யுற்றுடன் றெற்திற் சென்று","பொதியமா மலையும் காணாப் பொருவில்சீர்க் குடபா னின்ற","மதிகதி ரவியு மத்த வான்கிரி கண்டு மீண்டும்","இதமுள தேசம் பார்த்தே யினியதம் புரி.ய டைந்தார்."],"p":"","r":"They see the eastern hill of sunrise, go on to the south and the Podiyil hill, see the western mountain where sun and moon set, and having seen the good lands return to their pleasant city.","n":"Lines 1 and 4 carry stray full stops inside words in the edition (கிரி.யும், புரி.ய).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naadu","inbam","kaalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 251","lb":"UD251","mm":1,"ev":"The riders fly over the eastern, southern and western hills and return to their city.","cl":[],"ag":["ஏறியவர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:14"},"ud:252":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":252,"t":["மாதுதன் வயாநோய் தீர்ந்து வளநகர் புக்க பின்பு","தீதின்றிக் கோள்க ளெல்லாஞ் சிறந்துநல் வழியை நோக்கப்","போதினற் குமரன் றேன்றப் புரவல னினிய னாகிச்","சோதிப்பொன் னறைதி றந்து தூவினன் சனங்கட் கெல்லாம்."],"p":"","r":"When the queen's longing has ended and they have entered the rich city, the planets all stand well, the prince is born, and the king, glad, opens the gold chamber and scatters wealth to all the people.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["makkal","eegai","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 252","lb":"UD252","mm":1,"ev":"The prince is born under good planets, and the king opens his gold chamber and gives to all.","cl":[],"ag":["உதயணன்","வாசவதத்தை"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:14"},"ud:253":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":253,"t":["நரவாக னன்னே யென்று நரபதி நாமஞ் செய்தான்","விரிவாகு மதிய மைச்சர் மிக்கநாற் குமரர் பேர்தாம்","பரிவார்கோ முகனும் பாங்காந் தரிசக னாக தத்தன்","குரவம்பூ மேனி யான குலமறி பூதி யாமே."],"p":"","r":"The king names the child Naravahanan, and the four sons of his ministers have the names Gomukhan, Tarisakan, Nagadattan and Bhuti.","n":"The line that gives the four names runs together across lines 3 and 4 (பாங்காந் தரிசக னாக தத்தன், குலமறி பூதி), so the exact form of the names is somewhat uncertain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["makkal","thunai","sutram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 253","lb":"UD253","mm":1,"ev":"Udayanan names his son Naravahanan, and the four minister's sons are named.","cl":[],"ag":["உதயணன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:14"},"ud:254":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":254,"t":["நால்வருந் துணைவ ராகி நறுநெய்பா லுடன ருந்தி","பான்மரத் தொட்டி லிட்டுப் பரவியுந் தவழ்ந்து மூன்றாம்","மால்பிறை போல்வ ளர்ந்து வரிசையி னிளமை நீங்கிப்","பான்மொழி வாணி தன்னைப் பாங்கினிற் சேர்த்தா ரன்றே."],"p":"","r":"The four become his companions, share his ghee and milk, crawl in the wooden cradle and grow like the third-day moon; when childhood is past, they place him in the company of Vani.","n":"Who is placed and who places him is not stated; பான்மொழி வாணி may be the goddess of learning or a teacher's name.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kalvi","thunai","makkal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 254","lb":"UD254","mm":1,"ev":"The prince and the four minister's sons grow up together and are placed with Vani for learning.","cl":[],"ag":["நால்வர்","நரவாகனன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:14"},"ud:255":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":255,"t":["ஞானநற் குமரி தன்னை நலமுழு துண்டு மாரன்","மானவிற் கணைக்கி லக்கா மன்மத னென்னக் கண்டோர்","வானவக் குமரர் போல வாரண மேறித் தோழர்","சேனைமுன் பின்னுஞ் செல்லச் சீர்நகர் வீதி சென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"The youth, who has taken all the sweetness of learning, looks to onlookers like Kama himself, and with his friends and army before and behind rides an elephant through the town's streets.","n":"Line 1 is hard: ஞானநற் குமரி is read here as learning personified, but that is not certain, and line 2 is hard to parse.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kalvi","azhagu","perumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 255","lb":"UD255","mm":1,"ev":"Naravahanan, grown and learned, rides an elephant through the streets with his friends and army.","cl":[],"ag":["நரவாகனன்","தோழன்மார்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:14"},"ud:256":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":256,"t":["ஒளிர்குழற் கலிங்க சேனை யுதர்த்தி னுற்ப வித்த","வளிநறும் பூஞ்சு கந்த மதனமஞ் சிகைதன் மேனி","குளிரிளந் தென்றல் வீசக் கோலமுற் றத்துப் பந்தைக்","களிகயற் கண்ணி யாடக் காவல குமரன் கண்டான்."],"p":"","r":"As a cool breeze blows on Madanamanjikai, born of Kalingasenai, who plays with the ball in the decorated court, the king's son sees her.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","azhagu","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 256","lb":"UD256","mm":1,"ev":"Naravahanan sees Madanamanjikai playing ball in the court.","cl":[],"ag":["மதனமஞ்சிகை","நரவாகனன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:14"},"ud:257":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":257,"t":["மட்டவிழ் கோதை தன்னை மன்னவ குமரன் கண்டு","இட்டநன் மார னம்பா லிருவரு மயக்க முற்று","மட்டவிழ் மலர்ச்சோ லைக்குள் மன்னவ குமரன் மின்னின்","இட்டிடை மாதைத் தந்தே யின்புறப் புணர்ந்தா னன்றே."],"p":"","r":"The prince sees the garlanded girl and both are bewildered by the god of love's arrow; in the flower grove he unites with the slender-waisted girl at once, to their joy.","n":"The verse names no wedding, no go-between and no family: sight and union come together. Whether there is a rite that is left out is not clear from the verse.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","punarchi","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 257","lb":"UD257","mm":1,"ev":"The prince and Madanamanjikai fall in love at the sight and unite in the flower grove.","cl":[],"ag":["நரவாகனன்","மதனமஞ்சிகை"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:14"},"ud:258":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":258,"t":["இருவரும் போகந் துய்த்தே யிளைத்துயில் கொள்ளும் போழ்து","மருவிய விச்சை மன்னன் மானச வேக னென்பான்","திருநிற மாதைக் கண்டு திறத்தினிற் கொண்டு சென்று","பெருவரை வெள்ளி மீதிற் பீடுறு புரம்புக் கானே."],"p":"","r":"While the two sleep after their pleasure, the Vidyadhara king Manasavegan sees the radiant girl, takes her by his skill and enters his city on the silver mountain.","n":"The abduction falls at the moment of rest after union, so the girl is taken before the lovers are fully known to the household.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","pirivu","vali"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 258","lb":"UD258","mm":1,"ev":"Manasavegan carries off the sleeping Madanamanjikai to the silver mountain.","cl":[],"ag":["மானசவேகன்","மதனமஞ்சிகை"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:14"},"ud:259":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":259,"t":["தன்னுடை நோயு ரைக்கத் தையலு மோனங் கொண்டே","இன்னுயிர்க் கணவன் றன்னை இனிமையி னினைத்தி ருப்ப","மின்னிடைத் தங்கை யான வேகநல் வதியை யேவி","மன்னிய நிறை யழிக்க வாஞ்சையின் விடுத்தா னன்றே."],"p":"","r":"When Manasavegan tells her his pain she keeps silent and holds her husband sweetly in mind; so he sends his slender-waisted sister Vegavati, to ruin the firmness she keeps.","n":"Silence and remembering her husband are the whole of her defence; the verse calls her husband இன்னுயிர்க் கணவன், though no rite of marriage was told in the last verses.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["karpu","nirai","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 259","lb":"UD259","mm":1,"ev":"Manasavegan woos her in vain, and sends Vegavati to break her steadfastness.","cl":[],"ag":["மானசவேகன்","மதனமஞ்சிகை","வேகவதி"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:14"},"ud:260":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":260,"t":["அன்புற வவளுஞ் சொல்ல வசலித மனத்த ளாகி","இன்புறுந் தன்னோர் நாத னிந்திரன் போலு மென்னப்","பண்புணர் மொழியைக் கேட்டுப் பரவச மனத்த ளாகி","நண்பொடு விசும்பின் வந்து நரவாக னனைக்கண் டாளே."],"p":"","r":"Madanamanjikai, unmoved, says with love that her lord is like Indra himself; hearing the words of such a virtuous woman, Vegavati falls into a trance of feeling and comes out in the sky, and sees Naravahanan.","n":"Who says what in lines 1 and 2 is hard to fix; the speech is taken here to be Madanamanjikai's, praising her husband, and Vegavati's trance is taken to follow from it.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["karpu","nirai","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 260","lb":"UD260","mm":1,"ev":"Madanamanjikai praises her lord to Vegavati, who comes to the sky and sees Naravahanan.","cl":[],"ag":["மதனமஞ்சிகை","வேகவதி"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:14"},"ud:261":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":261,"t":["கண்டபின் காமங் கூர்ந்து கார்விசும் பதனி னிற்பப்","புண்டவழ் வேலிற் காளை பூங்குழ லாட்கி ரங்கி","வண்டலர் சோலை மாடம் வனமெங்குந் தேடு கின்றான்","தொண்டைவா யுடைய வேக வதியுஞ்சூ தினிலே வந்தாள்."],"p":"","r":"On seeing him Vegavati's desire grows and she stands in the dark sky, while the youth, stricken, searches the groves, the towers and the whole wood for the girl, and Vegavati comes down by guile.","n":"The desire in line 1 is taken as Vegavati's, since she is the one who has just seen him, but the grammar does not fix it.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","vanjam","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 261","lb":"UD261","mm":1,"ev":"Vegavati, desiring Naravahanan, descends by a trick while he is searching everywhere for Madanamanjikai.","cl":[],"ag":["வேகவதி","நரவாகனன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:14"},"ud:262":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":262,"t":["மதன மஞ்சிகை மான்விழி ரூபம்போல்","வதன நன்மதி வஞ்சியங் கொம்பனாள்","இதநல் வேடத்தை யின்பிற் றரித்துடன்","புதரின் மண்டபம் புக்கங் கிருந்தனள்."],"p":"","r":"Vegavati takes the shape of Madanamanjikai, doe-eyed, with a moon-like face, wearing the pleasing disguise with delight, and sits in a bower within the thicket.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maayai","vanjam","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 262","lb":"UD262","mm":1,"ev":"Vegavati takes on Madanamanjikai's form and waits in a thicket pavilion.","cl":[],"ag":["வேகவதி"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:14"},"ud:263":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":263,"t":["தாது திர்ந்து தரணியிற் பம்பிட","மாத விப்பொதும் பின்மயிற் றோகைபோல்","பேதை யைக்கண்டு பீடுடைக் காளையும்","தீதறுந் திறந் தேர்ந்து புணர்ந்தனன்."],"p":"","r":"Pollen drifts down over the earth like a peacock's tail spread in the cloud-dark hour, and the proud youth, seeing the girl, finds the way clear of fault and joins with her.","n":"The first two lines are a scene-setting image whose syntax is broken in the edition (மாத விப்பொதும்); the girl is not named, and she may be the Vidyadhara woman of the next verses.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","punarchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 263","lb":"UD263","mm":1,"ev":"The youth, having judged that no fault bars it, unites with the girl.","cl":[],"ag":["நரவாகனன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:15"},"ud:264":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":264,"t":["ஆங்கொர் நாளி லரிவை துயிலிடைத்","தேங்கொள் கண்ணியைச் செல்வனுங் கண்டுடன்","பூங்கு ழா அல் நீ புதியைமற் றியாரெனப்","பாங்கில் வந்து பலவுரை செய்தனள்."],"p":"","r":"On another day the lord sees the flower-haired woman asleep and, as she comes to his side, she speaks to him at length, asking who he is, a stranger.","n":"The verse does not say who is asleep and who speaks; it is read here as the maiden coming to the youth, though the sleeper may be the youth's own bride.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","kanavu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 264","lb":"UD264","mm":1,"ev":"A woman asks the youth who he is and talks with him at length.","cl":[],"ag":["நரவாகனன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:15"},"ud:265":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":265,"t":["கேட்ட வள்ளலுங் கேடினன் மாதரை","வேட்ட வேடம் விரும்பிநீ காட்டெனக்","காட்டவே கண்டு காளை கலந்தனன்","ஊட்ட வேகணை யுன்னத மாரனே."],"p":"","r":"The generous youth asks the woman to show him the form she likes best; she does, and he joins with her while Mara's arrows urge him on.","n":"The shape-showing implies she is a Vidyadhara who can change form; the verse does not say so.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","punarchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 265","lb":"UD265","mm":1,"ev":"The woman shows the shape he asked for, and the youth joins with her.","cl":[],"ag":["நரவாகனன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:15"},"ud:266":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":266,"t":["மன்னு விஞ்சையின் மானச வேகனும்","துன்னு தங்கையாந் தோகையைக் காண்கிலன்","உன்னி வந்தவள் போன தறிந்துரை","பன்னி வந்திரு வோரையும் பற்றினன்."],"p":"","r":"Manasavegan, master of the Vidyadhara arts, cannot find his sister, learns from the court that she has gone, comes in anger and seizes both.","n":"His anger is the wronged brother's: the verse treats the pair's union as an offence to be punished, with no word for the woman's own will.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","vali"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 266","lb":"UD266","mm":1,"ev":"Manasavegan learns his sister has left and seizes her and the youth.","cl":[],"ag":["மானசவேகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:15"},"ud:267":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":267,"t":["வான கஞ்சென்று வள்ளலை விட்டபின்","ஈனகஞ் செல வேலக் குழலியும்","தான கம்விஞ்சை தானுடன் விட்டனள்","கான கத்திடைக் காளையும் வீழ்ந்தான்."],"p":"","r":"Manasavegan leaves the youth in the sky and goes down; the woman of the dark hair gives up her Vidyadhara art, and the youth falls into the forest.","n":"The verse does not say why the youth falls, whether her art held him up or the brother let go; the readings turn on the doubtful wording of line 2.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["inmai","kayamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 267","lb":"UD267","mm":1,"ev":"Manasavegan abandons the youth in mid-air, his sister drops her art, and the youth falls into the forest.","cl":[],"ag":["மானசவேகன்","குழலி","நரவாகனன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:15"},"ud:268":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":268,"t":["வெதிரி லையென வீழ்ந்தவன் றன்னிடைக்","கதிர்வேல் வத்தவன் காதனற் றந்தையாம்","எதிர்வ ரும்பிறப் பெறிகின்ற மாமுனி","கதிரி லங்குவேற் காளையைக் கண்டனன்."],"p":"","r":"As the fallen youth lies like a leaf of bamboo, the great sage, the beloved father of the bright-speared king of Vatsa, who is to cast off future births, sees him.","n":"The sage is called the father of the Vatsa king; the verse does not say he is the same as the Sumitra of verse 281, and an earlier life of the king is possible.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thavam","piravi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 268","lb":"UD268","mm":1,"ev":"The sage sees the fallen youth.","cl":[],"ag":["சுமித்திர முனி","நரவாகனன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:15"},"ud:269":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":269,"t":["போதி தன்வலிப் போத வுணர்ந்துதன்","காத லிற்சென்று காளைதன் னாமமும்","ஏத் மில்தந்தை யெய்திய நாமமும்","போதச் செப்பலும் போந்து பணிந்தனன்."],"p":"","r":"Knowing by his power of enlightenment what has happened, the sage goes out in love, names the youth and his father, and the youth comes and bows.","n":"போதி (line 1) is read as the sage's wisdom or insight; the word is rare in this sense.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["arivu","thavam","anbu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 269","lb":"UD269","mm":1,"ev":"The sage says the youth's name and his father's name, and the youth bows to him.","cl":[],"ag":["சுமித்திர முனி","நரவாகனன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:15"},"ud:270":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":270,"t":["தந்தை யென்முதல் தாமறிந் திங்குரை","அந்த மில்குணத் தையநீ ராரென","முந்து நன்முறை யாமுனி தாஞ்சொலச்","சிந்தை கூர்ந்து சிறந்தொன்று கேட்டனன்."],"p":"","r":"The youth asks the sage how he knows his father and himself, and the sage, in due order, tells who they are; the youth listens with deepening feeling and then hears one thing more.","n":"","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thavam","kelvi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 270","lb":"UD270","mm":1,"ev":"The youth asks how the sage knew them; the sage explains.","cl":[],"ag":["நரவாகனன்","சுமித்திர முனி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நரவாகனன்","சுமித்திர முனி"],"ch":"ud:15"},"ud:271":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":271,"t":["விஞ்சை யம்பதி வெற்றிகொண் டாளுமென்","றஞ்ச மென்றநற் றக்கோ ருரையுண்டு","எஞ்ச லின்னிலை மையது வென்றென","விஞ்சு மாதவன் மெய்ம்மையிற் கூறுவான்."],"p":"","r":"The sage says the sages' word is that he will conquer and rule the Vidyadhara cities and that his state is unfailing; the great ascetic tells it truly.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thavam","vaaimai","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 271","lb":"UD271","mm":1,"ev":"The sage foretells that the youth will rule the Vidyadhara cities.","cl":[["the word of true sages foretells a ruler's future and does not fail","general","reported",1,"sumitra_muni"]],"ag":["சுமித்திர முனி"],"ec":[],"spk":["சுமித்திர முனி","நரவாகனன்"],"ch":"ud:15"},"ud:272":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":272,"t":["வெள்ளி யம்மலை மேனின்ற ராச்சியம்","உள்ள தெல்லா மொருங்கே யடிப்படுத்","தெள்லில் செல்வமு மீண்டுனக் காமென்றான்","கள்ளவிழ் கண்ணிக் காளையுங் கேட்டபின்."],"p":"","r":"The sage tells him the whole realm on the silver mountain will come under his feet with wealth beyond reproach, and the youth listens.","n":"","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vendhan","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 272","lb":"UD272","mm":1,"ev":"The sage promises the youth the Vidyadhara realm of Vellimalai.","cl":[],"ag":["சுமித்திர முனி","நரவாகனன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சுமித்திர முனி","நரவாகனன்"],"ch":"ud:15"},"ud:273":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":273,"t":["மாத வன்விட வள்ள னகர்புக்குத்","தாதை தாய்முதற் றான்கண் டிருந்தபின்","தீது தீர்ந்ததுஞ் செல்வி பிரிந்ததும்","ஆத ரித்தவர்க் கன்னோன் விளம்பினன்."],"p":"","r":"The youth leaves the great ascetic, goes to the city, and after seeing his father and mother, tells them lovingly how his misfortune ended and his beloved was parted from him.","n":"The speaker of the last line is called அன்னோன் ('that one'); the verse names no wife, so the beloved's parting is either from the Vidyadhara woman or from his wedded wife.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","anbu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 273","lb":"UD273","mm":1,"ev":"The youth rejoins his parents and tells them of the end of his trouble and of his parted wife.","cl":[],"ag":["நரவாகனன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:15"},"ud:274":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":274,"t":["மேனி கழ்வென மெய்த்தவர் கூறின","தான வின்றுதன் றாய்துயர் தீர்த்தனன்","வானு ழைச்செல்லு மன்னிய தேர்மிசை","ஈன மில்கும ரன்னினி தேறினான்."],"p":"","r":"He soothes his mother's grief by reporting what the true ascetic said, mounts the sky-going chariot, and goes happily.","n":"","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["anbu","thavam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 274","lb":"UD274","mm":1,"ev":"The youth tells his mother what the ascetic said, ends her grief, and mounts a sky-chariot.","cl":[],"ag":["நரவாகனன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["நரவாகனன்","தாய்"],"ch":"ud:15"},"ud:275":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":275,"t":["அன்பால் வான்வழி யாய்மணித் தேர்செலத்","தென்பாற் சேடியிற் சீதர லோகத்தில்","இன்பாற் பொய்கை யெழிற்கரை வைகென","மின்பூண் மார்பனும் வேண்டித் திளைத்தனன்."],"p":"","r":"With love the chariot goes along the sky-road southwards to Sridharaloka on the Vidyadhara ridge, and the youth, told to rest by a fair pond, stays gladly.","n":"","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["inbam","anbu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 275","lb":"UD275","mm":1,"ev":"The youth flies south to Sridharaloka and rests by a pond.","cl":[],"ag":["நரவாகனன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:15"},"ud:276":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":276,"t":["நெடுங்க ரைமிசை நீர்மையி னின்றனன்","நடுங்க லின்றிவாய் நானநீர் பூசியே","கடிக மழ்கண்ணிக் காளை யிருந்தனன்","அடிகண் டோர்மக னன்பிற் றொழுதனன்."],"p":"","r":"He stands at the long bank, bathes without shaking, anoints himself with fragrant water and sits there; a young man seeing his feet worships them.","n":"","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["panivu","anbu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 276","lb":"UD276","mm":1,"ev":"The youth bathes and sits at the pond, and a young man worships his feet.","cl":[],"ag":["நரவாகனன்","தானவன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:15"},"ud:277":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":277,"t":["அண்ணல் கண்டுநீ யாருரை யென்றலும்","தண்ணென் வாய்மொழித் தானவன் சொல்லுவான்","அண்ணல் கேட்க வரிய வரைமிசைக்","கண்ணோ ளிர்கொடிக் கந்தரு வப்புரம்."],"p":"","r":"The lord asks the young man who he is, and the cool-tongued Vidyadhara says his city is Gandharvapuram on the hard-to-reach hill.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thelivu","naadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 277","lb":"UD277","mm":1,"ev":"The youth asks the stranger who he is; the stranger names Gandharvapuram.","cl":[],"ag":["நரவாகனன்","தானவன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["தானவன்","நரவாகனன்"],"ch":"ud:15"},"ud:278":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":278,"t":["காவ லன்னீல வேகற்குக் காரிகை","நாவி ளங்குஞ்சீர் நாகதத் தையெனும்","பூவி ளங்கொடி புத்திரி நாகமும்","மேவி ளங்கு மநங்கவி லாசனை."],"p":"","r":"The Vidyadhara king Nilavekan has a fair daughter, Nagadattai of fame, and the flower-vine Manmatha-vilasai, as another daughter.","n":"Whether Nagadattai and Anangavilasai are two daughters or one woman with two names cannot be fixed from the fragmentary syntax of lines 3 and 4.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["makkal","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 278","lb":"UD278","mm":1,"ev":"The stranger names the king's daughters Nagadattai and Anangavilasai.","cl":[],"ag":["தானவன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["தானவன்","நரவாகனன்"],"ch":"ud:15"},"ud:279":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":279,"t":["சுரும்பார் மாலை யமளித் துயிலிடைக்","கரும்பார் நன்மொழி காதற் கனவிடை","விரும்பு சிங்கமீன் வீரியச் சாபந்தான்","பரம்பு மண்ணின்று பாங்கி னெழுந்ததே."],"p":"","r":"Asleep on a bed with bee-sung garlands, the sweet-voiced girl dreams that a curse of power, lion and fish, rose from the earth in her lover's company.","n":"The dream is told in bare images (சிங்கம், மீன், சாபம்) whose referents the verse does not give; it is the sage's reply in 282 that makes them a sign.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kanavu","oozh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 279","lb":"UD279","mm":1,"ev":"The girl dreams of a lion and a fish and a curse that rises from the earth.","cl":[],"ag":["நாகதத்தை"],"ec":[],"spk":["தானவன்","நரவாகனன்"],"ch":"ud:15"},"ud:280":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":280,"t":["வரைமி சைவந்து மன்னிய தன்முலை","அரிய முத்தணி யாரத்தைக் கவ்வியே","விரைசெய் மாலையை வீறுடன் சூட்டவும்","அரிவை கண்டுத னனையர்க்கு ரைத்தனள்."],"p":"","r":"In the dream a creature comes down from the hill and bites the pearl necklace on her breast, then sets a fragrant garland on her with pride; the girl wakes and tells her kin.","n":"","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kanavu","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 280","lb":"UD280","mm":1,"ev":"The girl sees a creature take her necklace and crown her with a garland, and tells her kin.","cl":[],"ag":["நாகதத்தை"],"ec":[],"spk":["தானவன்","நரவாகனன்"],"ch":"ud:15"},"ud:281":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":281,"t":["வெல்ல ரும்வேலின் வேந்தனுங் கேட்டுடன்","சொல்ல ருந்தவச் சுமித்திர நன்முனி","புல்ல ரும்பதம் பொற்பி னிறைஞ்சினன்","நல்ல ருந்தவ னற்கனாக் கேட்டனன்."],"p":"","r":"The king of the victorious spear hears, goes to the unreachable sage Sumitra, bows at his feet in due form, and asks the ascetic about the good dream.","n":"The sage here is named சுமித்திர, and the Vidyadhara king consults him like the youth's own guide of verse 268; whether they are one person is left open.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kanavu","thavam","panivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 281","lb":"UD281","mm":1,"ev":"The king hears the dream, goes to the sage Sumitra and asks him to read it.","cl":[],"ag":["நீலவேகன்","சுமித்திர முனி"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:15"},"ud:282":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":282,"t":["அறிந்த ருள்செய் தனனம் முனிவனும்","செறிந்த பூமிவாழ் திருமரு கன்வரும்","அறைந்த நின்மகட் காகு மணவரன்","நிறைந்த நேமியிந் நிலமு மாளுவன்."],"p":"","r":"The sage, who has understood, gives grace and says the beloved lord now arriving will wed the king's daughter and, with the disc complete, will rule this earth.","n":"","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thavam","karpu","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 282","lb":"UD282","mm":1,"ev":"The sage reads the dream and says the coming youth will marry the king's daughter and rule the earth.","cl":[["a dream seen by a woman foretells her marriage and her husband's rule","general","reported",3,"sumitra_muni"]],"ag":["சுமித்திர முனி"],"ec":[],"spk":["சுமித்திர முனி","நீலவேகன்"],"ch":"ud:15"},"ud:283":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":283,"t":["அம்மு னிவன்சொ லரசன் கேட்டுடன்","தம்மி லெண்ணினன் சார்ந்து காண்கெனச்","செம்மை யெண்ணியே செப்பி விட்டனன்","உம்மைக் கண்டனன் செல்க வென்றனன்."],"p":"","r":"The king takes in the sage's word, weighs it, and sends word to the young man that he has seen him and that he should come.","n":"Lines 3 and 4 do not say who is addressed or who carries the message; the reading takes it as the summons to the groom the sage had foretold.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["arasu","soozhchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 283","lb":"UD283","mm":1,"ev":"The king, having heard the sage, sends word summoning the youth.","cl":[],"ag":["நீலவேகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:16"},"ud:284":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":284,"t":["போவ தேபொருள் புண்ணி யற்கொண்டு","தேவ னேயெனச் செல்வ னுஞ்செலும்","காவலன் னெதிர் கண்டு கண்மகிழ்","ஏவ லாளரோ டினிதி னெய்தினான்."],"p":"","r":"The youth goes, taking his merit as his only wealth, meets the king face to face, and arrives gladly with the king's attendants.","n":"Line 1 puts the youth's journey in terms of merit (புண்ணியன்), the same quality that later verses give as the cause of his rise.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["arasu","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 284","lb":"UD284","mm":1,"ev":"The youth goes to the king, who receives him with joy and attendants.","cl":[],"ag":["நரவாகனன்","நீலவேகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:16"},"ud:285":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":285,"t":["கன்னல் விற்கணை யில்லாக் காமனை","இன்னி லக்கண மேற்ற காளையை","மன்ன னின்னுரை மகிழ்ந்து கூறினான்","பின்ன மைச்சரைப் பேணிக் கேட்டனன்."],"p":"","r":"The king, glad, speaks sweet words of the youth, who is like Kaman without his sugarcane bow and has every fine mark, and then asks his ministers for counsel.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["amaichu","azhagu","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 285","lb":"UD285","mm":1,"ev":"The king praises the youth and consults his ministers about giving him his daughter.","cl":[],"ag":["நீலவேகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:16"},"ud:286":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":286,"t":["தனித்தி வர்மணந் தரத்தி யற்றினால்","சினத்தொ டுமன்னர் சேர்வ ராலென","மனத்த மைச்சரு மகிழ்ந்து மன்னரை","இனத்தொர் மாவர மியம்பி விட்டனர்."],"p":"","r":"The ministers, glad, warn that giving the bride to him alone would bring the other kings in anger, and so they send out word to the gathered kin and kings.","n":"Line 4 is hard to parse; the reading takes the sending out of word as summoning the kings so the match is not a private one.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["amaichu","soozhchi","mannar"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 286","lb":"UD286","mm":1,"ev":"The ministers advise an open gathering of kings, and word is sent.","cl":[["a bride given privately to one suitor brings the anger of the rival kings, so the choice is made public","general","asserted",1,"neelavegan_ministers"]],"ag":["அமைச்சர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:16"},"ud:287":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":287,"t":["மன்ன ரீண்டியே வந்தி ருக்கையில்","அன்ன மென்னடை யமிர்த மன்னவள்","மின்னின் மாலையை விரகி னேந்திமுன்","சொன்ன காளைமேற் சூட்டி நின்றனள்."],"p":"","r":"With the kings gathered, the swan-gaited bride, like nectar, deftly lifts a garland bright as lightning and puts it on the young man named earlier.","n":"The bride chooses; the garland is hers to give, in front of the kings the ministers wanted present.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 287","lb":"UD287","mm":1,"ev":"At the gathering of kings the bride garlands the youth.","cl":[],"ag":["மன்னவள்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:16"},"ud:288":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":288,"t":["அரசன் மிக்குநன் கமைத்த வேள்வியின்","திரும ணஞ்செய்து செல்வ னின்புற","இருவ ரும்புணர்ந் தின்ப மார்ந்தனர்","வெருவு மானச வேகன் றன்மனம்."],"p":"","r":"The king's grand sacrificial fire sets the wedding, and the pair delight in each other, while Manasavegan's mind is said to be afraid.","n":"Line 4 breaks off in a hanging phrase about Manasavegan's mind, which verse 289 does not finish, so what he fears is left unstated.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["inbam","acham","ilvaazhkkai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 288","lb":"UD288","mm":1,"ev":"The wedding is held at the fire and the couple unite.","cl":[],"ag":["நரவாகனன்","மன்னவள்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:16"},"ud:289":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":289,"t":["வேக யானைமே லேறி வீரனும்","நாக நீள்புர நடுவிற் றோன்றலும்","காம னேயெனக் கன்னி மங்கையர்","தாம ரைக்கணாற் றான்ப ருகுநாள்."],"p":"","r":"The hero mounts a swift elephant and appears in the middle of the long city of the Nagas, and the young women drink him in with their lotus eyes as though he were Kaman.","n":"'நாக நீள்புரம்' could be a city of Nagas or a serpent-named place; the Tamil does not settle which, and the closing 'நாள்' hangs into verse 290.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 289","lb":"UD289","mm":1,"ev":"The hero rides the elephant into the city and the women gaze at him.","cl":[],"ag":["நரவாகனன்","கன்னி மங்கையர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:16"},"ud:290":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":290,"t":["நேமி யாளவே நினைத்த தோன்றலும்","வாம நாகர்தம் மலையிற் சென்றனன்","தாம மார்பனைத் தரத்திற் கண்டவர்","நேமி தான்முத னிதிக ளொன்பதும்."],"p":"","r":"The lord, who has set his mind on ruling by the wheel, goes to the mountain of the Vama Nagas, where those who see him are shown the wheel and the nine treasures.","n":"The line ends in a list (the wheel, then nine treasures), and who gives them is not stated until the next verse names Indra's grace.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["arasu","selvam","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 290","lb":"UD290","mm":1,"ev":"The hero goes to the Nagas' mountain, where the wheel and the nine treasures are seen.","cl":[],"ag":["நரவாகனன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:16"},"ud:291":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":291,"t":["நாம விந்திர னன்க ருள்செயக்","காம னுக்கீந்து கண்டு சேவித்துத்","தாம வந்தரர் தாம்ப ணிந்திடத்","தோமி னாலிரண் டொன்ற வாயிரம்."],"p":"","r":"By Indra's gracious word the treasures are given to Kaman, and the garlanded beings of the mountain bow before him, in a number the verse gives obscurely.","n":"Line 4, 'தோமினால் இரண்டொன்ற ஆயிரம்', is a hard count (two joined to a thousand?) and I do not fix it. Here Kaman, as in the other verses, is the hero's byname.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["selvam","panivu","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 291","lb":"UD291","mm":1,"ev":"Indra's grace gives the treasures, and the mountain's beings bow to the hero.","cl":[],"ag":["இந்திரன்","வந்தரர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:16"},"ud:292":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":292,"t":["சக்க ரம்வலம் சார்ந்தி றைஞ்சின","மிக்க புண்ணியன் மீட்டு வந்துடன்","தக்க விஞ்சையர் தம்ப தியெல்லாம்","அக்க ணத்தினி லடிப்ப டுத்தினன்."],"p":"","r":"The wheel circles to the right and bows, and the greatly meritorious hero returns and at once brings every able Vidyadhara lord under his feet.","n":"The Tamil names him a man of great merit (புண்ணியன்) in the sentence that tells the conquest, and so makes the victory a result of merit, not only of the wheel.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["padai","arasu","perumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 292","lb":"UD292","mm":1,"ev":"The hero, with the wheel, subdues the Vidyadhara lords in an instant.","cl":[["the hero's power over rivals is the fruit of his merit","general","asserted",2,"narrator"]],"ag":["நரவாகனன்","விஞ்சையர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:16"},"ud:293":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":293,"t":["விஞ்சை யர்திறை வெற்றி கொண்டவன்","தஞ்ச மென்றவர் தரத்தின் வீசியே","எஞ்ச லில்புர மிந்தி ரன்னென","மிஞ்ச மாளிகை வீரன் சென்றனன்."],"p":"","r":"Having won the Vidyadhara lords' tribute, he casts wealth to those who seek refuge and goes with the greatness of Indra to a city of unfailing mansions.","n":"The verse joins conquest and giving: he accepts submission and then gives to those who take refuge.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["padai","arasu","eegai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 293","lb":"UD293","mm":1,"ev":"The hero wins the Vidyadharas' tribute, shelters those who beg refuge and goes to a splendid city.","cl":[],"ag":["நரவாகனன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:16"},"ud:294":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":294,"t":["மதன மஞ்சிகை மனங்கு ளிர்ந்திட","விதன மின்றிநல் வேக வதியுடன்","அதிக போக வநங்க விலாசனை","அதிக வெண்ணா யிரமான தேவியர்."],"p":"","r":"Madanamanjikai, glad of heart, Vegavati and Anangavilasai are named among his queens, with more than eight thousand ladies besides.","n":"The verse has no verb; it lists the queens and carries into verse 295, where the enjoyment is stated.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["selvam","kaamam","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 294","lb":"UD294","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:16"},"ud:295":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":295,"t":["இனிய வேள்வியா லின்ப மார்ந்துபின்","இனிய புண்ணிய மீண்டி மேல்வரத்","தனிய ரசினைத் தானி யற்றியே","நனிய தொன்றினன் னாம வேலினான்."],"p":"","r":"After enjoying the pleasures of the rite and gathering good merit, he sets up a single sovereignty that grows by the fruit of what he had done.","n":"'வேள்வி' may mean the wedding rite or a sacrifice, and I read it as the rite of marriage and kingship; the verse does not say which.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vinai","arasu","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 295","lb":"UD295","mm":1,"ev":"The hero founds his sole sovereignty after the sacrifice and the joys it brings.","cl":[["gathered merit comes back as sovereignty and pleasure in a later stage of life","general","asserted",2,"narrator"]],"ag":["நரவாகனன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:16"},"ud:296":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":296,"t":["விஞ்சை யர்தொழ வீறுந் தேவியர்","பஞ்சின் மெல்லடிப் பாவை மாருடன்","மஞ்சு சூழ்மலை விட்டு வானவர்","தஞ்ச மானதன் தந்தை பாற்சென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"With his queens, bowed to by the Vidyadharas, he leaves the cloud-ringed mountain and goes to his father, the refuge of the gods.","n":"The verse calls the father a refuge of the gods (வானவர் தஞ்சம்), which sets the father above the son's new rank.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["anbu","sutram","perumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 296","lb":"UD296","mm":1,"ev":"The hero and his queens leave the mountain and go to his father.","cl":[],"ag":["நரவாகனன்","தேவியர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:16"},"ud:297":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":297,"t":["புரம திக்கப்பூ மாலை தோரணம்","வரம்பி னாற்றியே வான்கொ டிம்மிடை","அரும்பு மாலைவே லரசன் சென்றெதிர்","விரும்பிக் கொள்ளவே வியந்து கண்டனன்."],"p":"","r":"The city is hung with flower garlands, festoons and flags, and the king in his flower garland goes out to meet him and looks on him with wonder.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["anbu","sutram","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 297","lb":"UD297","mm":1,"ev":"The father, the king, decks the city and goes out to meet his son.","cl":[],"ag":["உதயணன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:16"},"ud:298":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":298,"t":["தந்தை தாய்பதந் தான்ப ணிந்தபின்","இந்து வாணுத லெழின்ம டந்தையர்","வந்து மாமனை வணங்கி மாமியை","அந்த மில்வனத் தடியி றைஞ்சினார்."],"p":"","r":"The son bows to his father's and mother's feet, and the moon-faced queens come and bow to their husband's father and mother in the same way.","n":"The queens' bow includes his father and his mother; it shows the son's rank is held beneath the household's old order.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["panivu","sutram","anbu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 298","lb":"UD298","mm":1,"ev":"The son and his queens bow to his parents.","cl":[],"ag":["நரவாகனன்","தேவியர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:16"},"ud:299":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":299,"t":["மகிழ்ந்து புல்லியே மனைபு குந்தபின்","நெகிழ்ந்த காதலா னேமிச் செல்வனும்","மிகுந்த சீருடன் வீற்றி ருந்தனன்","மகிழ்ந்து மைந்தரை வரவ ழைத்தனன்."],"p":"","r":"After joyous embraces they enter the house, and the lord with the wheel sits in great splendour and gladly calls his sons.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["anbu","makkal","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 299","lb":"UD299","mm":1,"ev":"They embrace and enter the house, and the son calls his sons.","cl":[],"ag":["நரவாகனன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:16"},"ud:300":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":300,"t":["பதுமை தான்மிகப் பயந்த நம்பியாம்","கொதிநு னைவேலின் கோமு கன்றனை","இதம ளித்திடு மிளவ ரைசென","அதுல நேமிய னரசு நாட்டினான்."],"p":"","r":"The peerless wheel-lord sets up Gomukan, Padumai's son born of her great labour, in rule.","n":"Lines 3 and 4 are loosely joined; the reading takes 'அரசு நாட்டினான்' as installing Gomukan, but whether as king or as heir is not clear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["arasu","makkal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 300","lb":"UD300","mm":1,"ev":"The hero installs Gomukan, Padumai's son, as ruler.","cl":[],"ag":["நரவாகனன்","கோமுகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:16"},"ud:301":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":301,"t":["தந்தை மேன்மிகுந் தளர்வில் காதலாற்","றந்த தான்பிரி தலைக்க ருத்தெணி","வெந்து யர்கொடு விடுப்பச் செல்வனும்","இந்தி ரன்றனூ ரியல்பி னேகினன்."],"p":"","r":"His father, out of unfailing love and thinking of the parting, sends him off in burning sorrow, and the lord goes as he must to Indra's city.","n":"Line 2 is damaged ('றந்த தான்'), and I read the parting as the father's grief at letting him leave.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","anbu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 301","lb":"UD301","mm":1,"ev":"The father, grieving, lets his son go and he sets off to Indra's city.","cl":[],"ag":["உதயணன்","நரவாகனன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:16"},"ud:302":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":302,"t":["செலவநற் குமரன் சென்று தெய்வவிந் திரனைக் கண்டு","செல்வநல் வாமன் பூசைச் சீர்கண்டு வணக்கஞ் செய்து","செல்வவிந் திரன னுப்பத் திருமணித் தேரி னேறிச்","செல்வமார் புரம்பு குந்து சிறப்பினோ டிருந்தா னன்றே."],"p":"","r":"The prince goes to the god Indra, bows at the worship of the Lord, takes the jewelled chariot at Indra's leave, and returns to his own city to live in splendour.","n":"'வாமன் பூசை' (the worship of Vaman?) could name the deity worshipped or its rite; the Tamil does not say who he is.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["panivu","vazhipaadu","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 302","lb":"UD302","mm":1,"ev":"The prince visits Indra, worships, rides a gem chariot home and settles in his city.","cl":[],"ag":["நரவாகனன்","இந்திரன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:16"},"ud:303":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":303,"t":["வளங்கெழு வத்த வற்கு மன்னிய காதன் மிக்க","உளங்கெழு கற்பி னார்க ளோதிமம் போலு நீரார்","இளங்கிளி மொழியி னார்க ளினிமையி னால்வ ரோடும்","துளங்கலி றிருமின் போர்மின் தூயசொன் மடந்தை தாமும்."],"p":"","r":"The verse speaks of the Vatsa king's lasting love for his wives, who are chaste in mind, swan-like, parrot-voiced and sweet of speech, but the closing words are obscure.","n":"The last line (திருமின் போர்மின்) cannot be parsed with any confidence; the verse is read only as a portrait of the wives that opens the kandam.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["karpu","kaamam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 303","lb":"UD303","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["கற்பினார்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:17"},"ud:304":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":304,"t":["மண்ணியன் மடந்தை யோடு மருவினார் மிக்க மன்னன்","புண்ணிய முன்னாட் செய்த போதந்தே யுதவி செய்ய","எண்ணிய கரும மெல்லா மியைபுட னாகப் பின்னும்","புண்ணிய நோன்பு நோற்கப் பொருந்திய மனத்த னானான்."],"p":"","r":"Having lived with his queen, the king, because merit done in earlier days now lends its help and all he planned has gone well, turns his mind to taking up a vow of merit.","n":"The renunciation is framed as the fruit of earlier merit, so the turn from the world is itself given a cause in karma; the verse does not name any birth.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","thavam","oozh","thuravu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 304","lb":"UD304","mm":1,"ev":"Udayanan, his affairs prospering, resolves to take up a vow of merit.","cl":[["merit done in earlier days helps in the present and brings plans to success","general","asserted",2,"narrator"]],"ag":["உதயணன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:17"},"ud:305":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":305,"t":["ஆசை யென்றனக் கருளுந் தோழனா","ஓசை வண்புகழ் யூகி யானதும்","வாச வதத்தை மனைவி யானதும்","பேச ரும்மகப் பெற்றெ டுத்ததும்."],"p":"","r":"A speaking 'I' lists what his friend Yugi, his wife Vasavadatta and the child she bore had done for him.","n":"The first person (என்றனக்கு, 'to me') shows the voice is the king's own recollection; the unit does not say it is a speech to anyone.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["natpu","vaazhkkaithunai","makkal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 305","lb":"UD305","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["உதயணன்",""],"ch":"ud:17"},"ud:306":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":306,"t":["நரவாக னன்மக னாம மானதும்","வரைமிசைத் தானவர் வாழு நாட்டையங்","கரண நேமியா லடிப்ப டுத்ததும்","பொருவில் வேந்தர்கள் புகழ்ந்த டைந்ததும்."],"p":"","r":"The recollection goes on: the son is named Naravahana, the Danava land on the mountain is brought under the discus, and rival kings come to praise.","n":"Only line 1 names Naravahana; the subjects of lines 2 to 4 are not named, and the discus is read from அங்கரண நேமி (அரண நேமி in the edition's division).","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["makkal","vendhan","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 306","lb":"UD306","mm":1,"ev":"The son Naravahana is named; the Danavas' land is brought under the discus; kings come to praise.","cl":[],"ag":["நரவாகனன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["உதயணன்",""],"ch":"ud:17"},"ud:307":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":307,"t":["மிக்க விந்திரன் மேவி விட்டதும்","தக்க புத்திரன் றரத்திற் சென்றதும்","தொக்க வானவர் தொல்சி றப்புடன்","அக்க ணம்விட வண்ணல் போந்ததும்."],"p":"","r":"The recollection reaches Indra's sending and the son's reaching a high state, with the gods gathered, as the lord departs at that moment.","n":"The subjects are unnamed and the verse is a list of items without verbs of its own, so what is sent, and who rises, is uncertain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["makkal","vendhan","oozh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 307","lb":"UD307","mm":1,"ev":"Indra's sending, the son's rise and the gods' gathering are recalled; the lord departs.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["உதயணன்",""],"ch":"ud:17"},"ud:308":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":308,"t":["போந்து புண்ணியன் பொருவில் போகத்துச்","சேர்ந்தி ருந்ததுஞ் செய்த வத்தெனா","வேந்த னெண்ணியே வெறுத்து மாதரைக்","காந்தி வாமனைக் கண்ட டிதொழும்."],"p":"","r":"Coming back to his incomparable pleasures, the king thinks they are the fruit of his earlier penance, grows weary of them and of women, and goes to see and bow at the feet of the radiant Vamana.","n":"The thought that pleasure is the harvest of past penance becomes the reason to give pleasure up: the fruit is spent, so he seeks the penance again.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thuravu","thavam","kaamam","vinai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 308","lb":"UD308","mm":1,"ev":"Udayanan, disgusted with pleasure, goes to see and worship the teacher Kanti Vamanan.","cl":[["present enjoyment is the fruit of earlier austerity","general","asserted",2,"udayanan"]],"ag":["உதயணன்","காந்தி வாமன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:17"},"ud:309":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":309,"t":["எண்ணம் வந்துநல் லெழிற்பெ ரும்மகன்","புண்ணி யநோன்பு போந்த வேளைவேற்","கண்ணின் மாதர்கள் காவ லன்மனம்","உண்ணக் காமத்தை யுருவு காட்டினார்."],"p":"","r":"As the great man's vow of merit begins, women with spear-bright eyes show him the forms of desire to feed on his mind.","n":"The temptation arrives at the moment the vow starts, as a trial rather than a lapse.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","thavam","nirai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 309","lb":"UD309","mm":1,"ev":"Women tempt the king with forms of desire as his vow begins.","cl":[],"ag":["மாதர்கள்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:17"},"ud:310":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":310,"t":["மன்னு மன்பினீண் மாதர் மோகத்திற்","றுன்னு மால்கடற் றோன்ற னீந்துநாட்","சொன்ன மும்மதந் தோன்ற வேழமும்","உன்னிக் காற்றளை யுதறி விட்டதே."],"p":"","r":"The verse sets the king's crossing of the sea of delusion for loving women beside an elephant that shows its threefold rut and shakes off the fetter on its foot.","n":"Whether the elephant is a simile for the king's shedding of his bonds or the literal start of the rampage that the next verses describe is not settled by the Tamil; the verse reads as both.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","maram","thuravu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 310","lb":"UD310","mm":1,"ev":"An elephant in rut shakes off its foot-fetter.","cl":[],"ag":["யானை"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:17"},"ud:311":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":311,"t":["காய்ந்து வெம்மையிற் காலன் போலவே","பாய்ந்து பாகரைப் பலச னங்களைத்","தேய்ந்து காலினேர் தீயு மிழ்வபோல்","ஆய்ந்த கண்களு மருவ ரையென."],"p":"","r":"The elephant, angry and as hot as Death, leaps on the mahouts and many people, grinds them down, and spits fire with its feet and its searching eyes.","n":"The brief comparison to Death (காலன்) is the poem's own and no doctrine is claimed.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","kootru","vekuli"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 311","lb":"UD311","mm":1,"ev":"The elephant rushes on mahouts and people and crushes them.","cl":[],"ag":["யானை","பாகர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:17"},"ud:312":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":312,"t":["வெடிப டும்முழக் கிடியே னவிடும்","கொடியு டைமதில் கிடுகி டென்றிடும்","விடுபற் கோட்டினில் வெட்டி விட்டிடப்","படப டென்னவே பயண மானதே."],"p":"","r":"The march is described in sound: thunder-like blasts, flags and walls cracking, and tusks cutting as the going continues.","n":"The verse is pure description; who marches and what is cut is not named.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["padai","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 312","lb":"UD312","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:17"},"ud:313":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":313,"t":["அடிய டிய்யென வாயு தர்செலப்","படுப டுவ் வெனப் பறைகள் கொட்டிட","திடுதி டென்றோலி தெறித்த பேரிகை","நடுந டுங்கினார் நகர மாந்தரே."],"p":"","r":"Soldiers go out as drums and big drums crash, and the townspeople shake.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["padai","acham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 313","lb":"UD313","mm":1,"ev":"Soldiers go out to the beat of drums while the townspeople tremble.","cl":[],"ag":["ஆயுதர்","நகர மாந்தர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:17"},"ud:314":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":314,"t":["பிடிசில் பாகரைப் பிளந்தே றிந்திடக்","குடரின் மாலைகள் கோட்ட ணிந்துடன்","கடவுள் யானையைக் காலிற் றேய்த்திட","இடர்ப டுங்களி றெய்தி யோடுமே."],"p":"","r":"The elephant tears open the few mahouts, wears their entrails as garlands on its tusks, and grinds the divine elephant under foot, and the hurt elephant runs.","n":"Which elephant is the 'divine elephant' (கடவுள் யானை) and which the hurt one is not clear from the verse.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maram","kolai","acham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 314","lb":"UD314","mm":1,"ev":"The elephant kills its mahouts and grinds another elephant down, then a hurt elephant runs off.","cl":[],"ag":["யானை","பாகர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:17"},"ud:315":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":315,"t":["நகர மாந்தர்க ணடுங்கிச் சென்றுநற்","சிகரம் போன்முடிச் சீர ரசற்குப்","பகர வாரணம் பலரைக் கொன்றதென்","சிகர மாடநீர் சேர்ந்தி ருக்கென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"The frightened townspeople go to the king and say the elephant has killed many; he tells them to go and stay in the high mansions near the water.","n":"The king is named only by a title, so the verse does not show which king; the context points to Udayanan's own city.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["arasu","acham","kolai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 315","lb":"UD315","mm":1,"ev":"The townspeople report the elephant's killings to the king, who tells them to take shelter on the high mansions.","cl":[],"ag":["நகர மாந்தர்","அரசன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:17"},"ud:316":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":316,"t":["அடிய டிய்யென வாயு தர்செலப்","படுப டுவ் வெனப் பறைகள் கொட்டிட","திடுதி டென்றோலி தெறித்த பேரிகை","நடுந டுங்கினார் நகர மாந்தரே."],"p":"","r":"The verse repeats verse 313 word for word: soldiers go out, drums crash and the townspeople shake.","n":"An exact repeat of verse 313, probably a copyist's doubling.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["padai","acham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 316","lb":"UD316","mm":1,"ev":"Soldiers go out to the beat of drums while the townspeople tremble.","cl":[],"ag":["ஆயுதர்","நகர மாந்தர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:17"},"ud:317":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":317,"t":["வழிவ ருவாரை மார்கி ழித்திடும்","எழில்வனம்பொய்கையீட ழித்திடும்","இழிவு றுந்தொழி லீண்டிச் செய்யுநாட்","பொழிலுண் மாதவர் பொருந்தினார்களே."],"p":"","r":"On the day when this elephant tears open travellers' chests and wrecks groves and pools, great ascetics come to the grove.","n":"The verse sets the elephant's violence and the ascetics' arrival side by side without saying whether the one answers the other.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maram","thavam","thuravu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 317","lb":"UD317","mm":1,"ev":"Great ascetics arrive in the grove on the day of the elephant's ravages.","cl":[],"ag":["யானை","மாதவர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:17"},"ud:318":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":318,"t":["வேத நான்கையும் விரித்த ருளுவர்","மாத வர்வினை மாயச் செய்குவார்","ஏதில யாத்திரைக் கெழுந்து வந்தந்தப்","போத விழ்பொழில் புகுந்தி ருந்தனர்."],"p":"","r":"The ascetics who expound the four Vedas and make their deeds vanish come on a pilgrimage and settle in the grove.","n":"The verse calls the four things they expound வேதம்; whether this means the Jain fourfold teaching or the Vedas is not stated and nothing here decides it.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thavam","vinai","kalvi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 318","lb":"UD318","mm":1,"ev":"Ascetics expound their four texts and settle in the grove.","cl":[["ascetics make their deeds (vinai) vanish","general","asserted",2,"narrator"]],"ag":["மாதவர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:17"},"ud:319":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":319,"t":["இனம லர்மிசை யேகு வார்களும்","புனல லைமிசைப் போகு வார்களும்","கனிகள் காய்மிசை காணுஞ் சாரணர்","இனிய நூன்மிசை இசைந்து செல்வரும்."],"p":"","r":"Charana ascetics who move over flowers, over waves and over fruit and leaves, and others who go over learned books, are listed.","n":"The Charanas are ascetics with the power of going through the air; the listing is a catalogue of their powers and makes no claim.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thavam","thuravu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 319","lb":"UD319","mm":1,"ev":"Charana ascetics who travel over flowers, water and fruit are listed.","cl":[],"ag":["சாரணர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:17"},"ud:320":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":320,"t":["மலைத்த லைமிசை வானிற் செல்வரும்","நிலத்தி னால்விர னீங்கிச் செல்வரும்","தலத்தி னன்முழந் தரத்திற் செல்வரும்","பெலத்தின் வானிடைப் பெயர்ந்து செல்வரும்."],"p":"","r":"Some Charanas go on mountain tops, in the sky, a finger above the ground, or a span above the ground, in the power of their vow.","n":"The listing continues from verse 319 and keeps the same powers.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thavam","thuravu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 320","lb":"UD320","mm":1,"ev":"Charana ascetics go through the sky and above the ground by their power.","cl":[],"ag":["சாரணர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:17"},"ud:321":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":321,"t":["மலைமு ழைஞ்சுண் மன்னி னான்மறை","உலகெ லாமவ ரொருங்கி டவிடும்","அலம தீரவே வறம ழைபெய்யும்","மலம றுந்தர மாமு னிவரும்."],"p":"","r":"Great sages hidden in mountain caves, freed of impurity, send down a rain of virtue that ends the world's distress.","n":"அறமழை could be a literal rain given by ascetic power or the shower of teaching; the verse does not decide.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thavam","aram","mazhai","arul"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 321","lb":"UD321","mm":1,"ev":"Sages in the caves send rain that relieves the world's distress.","cl":[["freed ascetics relieve the world's distress with a rain of virtue","general","asserted",3,"narrator"]],"ag":["மாதவர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:17"},"ud:322":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":322,"t":["பக்க நோன்புடைப் பரம மாமுனி","மிக்க பாணிமீ தடிசின் மேதினி","புக்கு முண்டிடப் போது வார்பகல்","தக்க வர்குணம் சாற்ற ரிதென்றே."],"p":"","r":"A great sage keeping a fortnight's fast comes at noon to take food from his palm, and the virtue of such worthy ones is hard to tell.","n":"Food taken from the palm is a mark of strict ascetic practice; the verse praises by the discipline itself.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thavam","thuravu","adakkam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 322","lb":"UD322","mm":1,"ev":"A sage on a fortnightly fast goes at noon to eat food from his palm.","cl":[],"ag":["மாமுனி"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:17"},"ud:323":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":323,"t":["தரும வீரரென் றவருட் டலைவன்பால்","வெருவருந் துன்ப விலங்கும் வாழ்க்கையை","மருவி யோதவே வந்த யாவரும்","திருமொ ழியினைத் திறத்திற் கேட்டனர்."],"p":"","r":"The leader of the Dharma-heroes speaks of the fearful bondage of suffering that is life, and all who have come hear his holy word with attention.","n":"விலங்கும் may mean a fetter, binding, or the animals; the first sense fits the ascetic teaching, but the reading is not fixed.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thuravu","aram","yaakkai","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 323","lb":"UD323","mm":1,"ev":"The leader of the Dharma-heroes preaches and the gathered hear him.","cl":[["worldly life is a fearful bondage of suffering","general","asserted",2,"dharma_leader"]],"ag":["தருமவீரர் தலைவன்","யாவரும்"],"ec":[],"spk":["தருமவீரர் தலைவன்","யாவரும்"],"ch":"ud:17"},"ud:324":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":324,"t":["வருந்த சைநசை வானிற் புள்ளுகள்","இரைந்து மேலுங்கீ ழினும்ப டர்ந்திடப்","பருந்து முன்னும்பின் பரந்து செல்லவும்","விருந்த வையுண விட்ட தியானையே."],"p":"","r":"Hawks and other birds, drawn by the wish to feed, crowd the sky above and below and the kites go before and behind, for the elephant has left a feast.","n":"The verse returns to the elephant's ravages after the ascetic verses, so the order of 317 to 324 may be disturbed.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maram","kolai","kootru"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 324","lb":"UD324","mm":1,"ev":"Birds gather to feed on what the elephant has left.","cl":[],"ag":["யானை","புள்ளுகள்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:17"},"ud:325":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":325,"t":["கூற்றெ ழுங்கரி கொதித்தே ழுந்ததால்","ஆற்ற லம்முனி யறவு ரையுற","ஏற்ற ருஞ்செவி யிறைஞ்சித் தன்னுடை","மாற்ற ரும்பவ மறித்து ணர்ந்ததே."],"p":"","r":"The elephant that had risen in a death-like rage hears the sage's dharma, bows its ears to it and understands its own unavoidable birth.","n":"Line 4 turns on a hard form (மறித்து உணர்ந்தது); that the elephant knows its former birth is the likely sense, and verses 336 to 339 bear it out.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vinai","piravi","arivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 325","lb":"UD325","mm":1,"ev":"The enraged elephant is calmed by the sage's teaching and recalls its former birth.","cl":[["Even an animal that hears the dharma can come to know the birth it has passed through","general","asserted",3,"narrator"]],"ag":["யானை","மாமுனி"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:18"},"ud:326":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":326,"t":["குருதியாறிடக் கொன்ற தீவினை","வெருவு துக்கமும் விலங்கி னுய்த்திடும்","அருந ரகினு ளாழ்ந்து விட்டிடும்","பெருந்து யரெனப் பேது றுக்குமே."],"p":"","r":"The sage teaches that the deed of killing, which makes blood run in streams, drives the killer to terrible sorrow, to animal births and then down into hard hell.","n":"The verse is the sermon the elephant heard in 325; the sage speaks to the whole assembly and the elephant is the one who takes it in.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","naragam","kollaamai","piravi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 326","lb":"UD326","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["Killing living beings brings sorrow, animal birth and hell as its fruit","general","asserted",1,"muni"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["மாமுனி","யானை"],"ch":"ud:18"},"ud:327":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":327,"t":["நெஞ்சு நொந்தழு நெடுங்க ணீருகும்","அஞ்சு மாவினுக் கறிவு தோன்றிடக்","குஞ்ச ரம்மினிக் கோன கருன்னி","இஞ்சி வாய்தலி னெய்தி நின்றதே."],"p":"","r":"With tears running and its mind pained, the elephant that had grown wise thinks of the king and comes to the gate of the city rampart.","n":"Line 3 is hard (கோன கருன்னி); read here as the king's city and the elephant thinking of it.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["anbu","arivu","vinai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 327","lb":"UD327","mm":1,"ev":"The elephant weeps and goes to the rampart gate of the king's city.","cl":[],"ag":["யானை"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:18"},"ud:328":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":328,"t":["கடையுடைக் காவ லாளர் கதவினைத் திறக்கப் போந்தே","நடுநகர் வீதி சென்று நரபதி மனையைச் சார்ந்து","நெடுவரை போல நின்ற நீர்மையை வாயி லாளர்","முடிமனற் குரைப்ப முன்னிப் பெருமக னெழுந்து வந்தான்."],"p":"","r":"The gate guards open the doors, the elephant walks through the city to the palace and stands like a mountain, and the doorkeepers tell the king, who comes out.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vendhan","anbu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 328","lb":"UD328","mm":1,"ev":"The elephant reaches the palace gate; the doorkeepers report it and Udayanan comes out.","cl":[],"ag":["காவலாளர்","யானை","உதயணன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:18"},"ud:329":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":329,"t":["திருமுடி மன்ன னின்ற திருநிறை யானை கண்டு","மருவிய வமைச்சர் தம்மை மன்னவ னினிதி னோக்கப்","பெருவிரல் யூகி சொல்வான் பெருந்தவர் பால றத்தை","மருவியே கேட்ட தாகும் மன்னநீ யேற வென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"Seeing the great elephant standing, the king looks kindly at his ministers, and Yugi says it has surely heard dharma from a great ascetic and bids the king mount it.","n":"Yugi infers the sermon only from the elephant's bearing; the sage himself has not yet been named in this chapter.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arivu","vendhan","aram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 329","lb":"UD329","mm":1,"ev":"Yugi reads the elephant's calm as the sign of a sage's teaching and urges Udayanan to ride it.","cl":[],"ag":["உதயணன்","யூகி"],"ec":[],"spk":["யூகி","உதயணன்"],"ch":"ud:18"},"ud:330":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":330,"t":["வேந்தனுங் கேட்டு வந்து வெண்கோட்டி னடிவைத் தேறிச்","சேந்தன னெருத்தின் மீதிற் றிரும்பிக்கொண் டேகி வேழம்","பூந்தளிர் நிறைந்தி லங்கும் பொழில்வலஞ் சுற்ற வந்து","காந்துநன் மணிப்பூண் மார்பன் கைம்மாவிட் டிழிந்தா னன்றே."],"p":"","r":"The king mounts the elephant by its tusk, rides round the garden of fresh shoots, and gets down at the sage's place.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vendhan","panivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 330","lb":"UD330","mm":1,"ev":"Udayanan rides the elephant round the grove and dismounts.","cl":[],"ag":["உதயணன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:18"},"ud:331":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":331,"t":["விரைகமழ் பூவு நீரும் வேண்டிய பலமு மேந்திப்","பரிசனஞ் சூழத் சென்று பார்த்திப னினிய னாகி","மருமலர் கொண்டு வாழ்த்தி மாதவ ரடியி றைஞ்ச","இருவென விருக்கை காட்ட விருந்துநல் லறத்தைக் கேட்டான்."],"p":"","r":"The king takes flowers, water and fruit with his people, bows to the sage's feet in good humour, is told to sit and hears the dharma.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["panivu","vazhipaadu","aram","guru"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 331","lb":"UD331","mm":1,"ev":"Udayanan honours the sage with offerings, sits at his bidding and listens to his teaching.","cl":[],"ag":["உதயணன்","மாமுனி"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:18"},"ud:332":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":332,"t":["அறத்திற முனிவன் சொல்ல வரசனுங் கேட்க லுற்றான்","பெறற்கரு மருங்க லங்கள் பேணுதற் கரிய வாகும்","திறத்தறி பொருள்க ளாறுந் தேர்ந்துபஞ் சாத்தி காயம்","மறித்தறி தத்து வங்கள் வரிசையி னேழ தாமே."],"p":"","r":"The sage begins his teaching: the six substances to be understood, the five that occupy space, and the seven truths, in order.","n":"The six substances and the pancastikaya are the Jain categories; the verse only names them and does not define them.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thaththuvam","aram","arivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 332","lb":"UD332","mm":1,"ev":"The sage lays out the Jain categories of substances and truths.","cl":[["Reality is known by six substances, five space-occupying extents (astikaya) and seven truths","jain","asserted",3,"muni"]],"ag":["மாமுனி","உதயணன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["மாமுனி","உதயணன்"],"ch":"ud:18"},"ud:333":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":333,"t":["சீரிய நவப தங்கள் செப்பிய காய மாறும்","வீரியப் பொறிக ளாறும் வேண்டிய வடக்க மாகும்","ஓரிய லறம்பத் தோடு மொருங்குபன் னிரண்டு சிந்தை","ஆரிய ரறிந்து நம்பி யதன்வழி யொழுக்க மாகும்."],"p":"","r":"The sage goes on to nine categories, six bodily classes, restraint of the six powers, ten virtues and twelve contemplations, and says the noble know, believe, and then walk the way that follows.","n":"Lines 1 to 3 are a list of Jain headings (navapadartha, the six kayas, ten dharmas, twelve anuprekshas); the verse gives names, not their sense.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thaththuvam","ozhukkam","aram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 333","lb":"UD333","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["Right knowledge and trust come first, and right conduct follows from them","jain","asserted",4,"muni"],["Restraint of the six senses, ten virtues and twelve contemplations belong to the path","jain","asserted",2,"muni"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["மாமுனி","உதயணன்"],"ch":"ud:18"},"ud:334":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":334,"t":["தலைமகார் சிறப்புச் செய்து தன்மைநல் வாய்மை யான","கலையினற் கரையைக் கண்டு காதனூல் வழியைச் சென்று","மலைவில்சீர் மாத வர்க்கு வண்மையிற் றானஞ் செய்தார்","தொலைவிலாப் பிறவி நீங்கித் தொல்சுகக் கடலு ளாழ்வார்."],"p":"","r":"Those who honour the great teachers, reach the end of true learning by the beloved books and give gifts to the ascetics are freed from endless birth and sink in the old sea of joy.","n":"The reward is release; a gift here is a step on the road to freedom, not only a duty to the poor.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","thavam","veedu","piravi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 334","lb":"UD334","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["Giving to ascetics, with learning, ends rebirth and leads to bliss","general","asserted",3,"muni"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["மாமுனி","உதயணன்"],"ch":"ud:18"},"ud:335":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":335,"t":["தரும வீரர் தரும முரைத்திடப்","பெருமை மன்னனும் பேர்ந்து வணங்கினன்","மருவு வல்வினை மாசி னுதிர்த்திட","தெரிச னவ்விளக் கஞ்சிறப் பானதே."],"p":"","r":"As the sage teaches dharma the king bows again, and the sight of dharma is said to be the chief light that shakes off the stain of heavy deeds.","n":"Here deeds are shed by sight and understanding, not by worship of a god.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","aram","panivu","veedu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 335","lb":"UD335","mm":1,"ev":"The king bows after the sermon; the narrator names right vision as the means of shedding strong karma.","cl":[["Right vision (darsana) shakes off the stain of heavy deeds","jain","asserted",3,"narrator"]],"ag":["மாமுனி","உதயணன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:18"},"ud:336":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":336,"t":["காது வேன்மன்னன் களிறு கதமெழற்","கேது வென்னென யெதிவ ரன்சொலும்","தாது பூம்பொழிற் சாலிநன் னாட்டிடை","வேதி யர்குழு வாய்விளங் கும்புரம்."],"p":"","r":"The king asks the sage what is behind the elephant's rage, and the sage begins at a town of brahmins in the flower-grove country of Sali.","n":"Lines 3 and 4 are the sage's own words, set in the narrator's frame.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vinai","kudi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 336","lb":"UD336","mm":1,"ev":"Udayanan asks the cause of the elephant's fury; the sage starts his answer.","cl":[],"ag":["உதயணன்","மாமுனி"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:18"},"ud:337":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":337,"t":["கடக மென்பதூர் காதற் பிராமணன்","விடப கன்னெனும் பேரினன் மற்றவன்","இடைமின் றேவியுஞ் சானகி யென்பவள்","கடையில் காமங் கலந்துடன் செல்லுநாள்."],"p":"","r":"A brahmin of the village of Kadakam called Vidapakan lives with his wife Janaki in boundless desire.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","kudi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 337","lb":"UD337","mm":1,"ev":"Vidapakan and his wife live in endless passion.","cl":[],"ag":["விடபகன்","சானகி"],"ec":[],"spk":["மாமுனி","உதயணன்"],"ch":"ud:18"},"ud:338":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":338,"t":["அமரி யென்னு மணிமுலை வேசிதன்","அமையுங் காமத் தழுங்கி விழுந்தவன்","சமைய வேள்வியுஞ் சார்ந்த வொழுக்கமும்","அமைவி லன்பவ மஞ்சின னில்லையே."],"p":"","r":"Vidapakan falls for the courtesan Amari, and in his longing neglects the rites and conduct of his religion and has no fear of sin.","n":"The sage does not blame the courtesan; the fault is placed on the man's neglect of duty and absence of fear.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","varaivinmagalir","ozhukkam","acham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 338","lb":"UD338","mm":1,"ev":"Vidapakan leaves his wife's side for the courtesan and drops his duties.","cl":[["One who gives himself to lust and neglects duty without fear of sin brings ruin on himself","general","asserted",4,"muni"]],"ag":["விடபகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["மாமுனி","உதயணன்"],"ch":"ud:18"},"ud:339":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":339,"t":["காமங் கள்ளுண்டு கைவிட லின்றியே","தாம நற்குழ லாடுணை யாகவும்","யாம மும்பக லும்மறி யாதவன்","ஆமர ணத்தின்பி னானைய தாயினன்."],"p":"","r":"Drunk with desire and with liquor, never letting go, a woman beside him, he knew neither night nor day, and after his death he became this elephant.","n":"This is the sage's answer to the question of 336: the raging elephant is the brahmin who lived without restraint.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kal","kaamam","piravi","vinai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 339","lb":"UD339","mm":1,"ev":"Vidapakan lives drunk and dies, and is reborn as the elephant.","cl":[["A life of lust and drink leads to rebirth as an animal","general","asserted",4,"muni"]],"ag":["விடபகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["மாமுனி","உதயணன்"],"ch":"ud:18"},"ud:340":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":340,"t":["அந்நிலை யுணர்ந் தடங்கியதென்றனர்","மன்னன் கேட்டுடன் வந்துநற் பாகர்க்குச்","சொன்ன யானையைத் தூயநீ ராட்டெனும்","அன்னம் பானெய்யி னன்புட னூட்டெனும்."],"p":"","r":"Hearing that the elephant has grown calm, the king tells the keepers to bathe it in pure water and feed it rice and milk-ghee with love.","n":"Line 1 is said by the report-bearers; the order follows in the king's words.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["anbu","eegai","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 340","lb":"UD340","mm":1,"ev":"Udayanan orders the elephant bathed and tenderly fed.","cl":[],"ag":["உதயணன்","பாகர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["உதயணன்","பாகர்"],"ch":"ud:18"},"ud:341":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":341,"t":["கவள நாடொறு மூட்டெனுங் காவலன்","பவள மாமெனும் பண்ணவர் தம்மடி","திவளு மாமுடி சேர்த்து வணங்கியே","உவள கத்துன்னி மற்றொன்று கேட்டனன்."],"p":"","r":"The king orders daily feeding, bows his crowned head at the sage's feet, goes to him and asks him one more thing.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["anbu","panivu","guru"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 341","lb":"UD341","mm":1,"ev":"Udayanan orders the daily feed, bows, and puts a further question to the sage.","cl":[],"ag":["உதயணன்","பாகர்","மாமுனி"],"ec":[],"spk":["உதயணன்","பாகர்"],"ch":"ud:18"},"ud:342":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":342,"t":["மதக்க ளிற்றின்மேன் மன்னிய வன்பெனக்","குதவக் காரண மென்னெனக் கூறலும்","சிதைவில் காட்சிநற் சீரொழுக் கத்தவர்","மதமின் மாட்சியர் மன்னநீ கேளென்றார்."],"p":"","r":"The king asks what causes his own lasting love for the rutting elephant, and the sage says to hear it, for those of flawless sight and conduct hold no pride.","n":"Lines 1 and 2 are the king's question and lines 3 and 4 the sage's reply; the speaker is given as the king.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["anbu","vinai","arivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 342","lb":"UD342","mm":1,"ev":"Udayanan asks why he loves the elephant; the sage tells him to listen.","cl":[],"ag":["உதயணன்","மாமுனி"],"ec":[],"spk":["உதயணன்","மாமுனி"],"ch":"ud:18"},"ud:343":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":343,"t":["உள்ள நற்றவ ருற்றுரை செய்கின்றார்","கள்ள விழ்பொழிற் கார்முகில் சூடியே","வெள்ளி யம்மலை மேல்வட சேடியில்","வள்ளலார் பொய்கை மத்திம நாட்டினுள்."],"p":"","r":"The sage begins his account on the silver mountain's northern range, in the middle country with the lord's pond.","n":"The place is Vijayardha's northern range (வெள்ளியம்மலை), a Vidyadhara country; line 4 is hard to place.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vinai","piravi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 343","lb":"UD343","mm":1,"ev":"The sage begins the tale of Udayanan's former birth.","cl":[],"ag":["மாமுனி"],"ec":[],"spk":["மாமுனி","உதயணன்"],"ch":"ud:18"},"ud:344":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":344,"t":["சுகந்தி யூர்க்கிறை சொற்புகழ் மாதவன்","அகந்தெ ளிந்த வயந்தன் மனைவியாம்","செகந் தனிப்புகழ் சீரார்கு லாங்கனை","உகந்து பெற்றன ளோர்புகழ்க் கோமுகன்."],"p":"","r":"In the city of Sugandhi, a lord of famed word and clear mind named Ayanthan has a noble wife who gladly bears him a son named Gomukan.","n":"Whether மாதவன் and அயந்தன் name one man or two is not settled here; they are taken as one.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kudi","vendhan","piravi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 344","lb":"UD344","mm":1,"ev":"Gomukan is born in Sugandhi.","cl":[],"ag":["குலாங்கனை"],"ec":[],"spk":["மாமுனி","உதயணன்"],"ch":"ud:18"},"ud:345":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":345,"t":["காம னெனனுமக் காளைகைத் தாய்பெயர்","சோம சுந்தரி யென்னுஞ் சுரிகுழல்","நாம வேன்மக னன்மை விசையனும்","சேம மித்திர ராகச் சிறந்தனர்."],"p":"","r":"The youth called Kaman, whose nurse is Somasundari, and Vijayan, son of the famed spear-king, become close friends.","n":"Kaman is taken to be another name for Gomukan, since 346 goes on with Gomukan; this is not stated.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["natpu","anbu","kudi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 345","lb":"UD345","mm":1,"ev":"Gomukan and Vijayan become close friends.","cl":[],"ag":["கோமுகன்","விசையன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["மாமுனி","உதயணன்"],"ch":"ud:18"},"ud:346":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":346,"t":["ஒழியாக் காத லுடன்விளை யாடியே","வழுவில் போகம் வரம்பின்றித் துய்த்தலும்","நழுவில் காட்சிய னாமவேற் கோமுகன்","ஒழிய நல்லுயிர் ஓங்கிநீ யாயினை."],"p":"","r":"Gomukan enjoyed pleasures in love without limit, and when he died the good soul rose and you became.","n":"The verse leaves the elephant's tie to Gomukan for the verses that follow; it only says the king was Gomukan.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["piravi","inbam","vinai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 346","lb":"UD346","mm":1,"ev":"The sage tells the king he was Gomukan in a former birth.","cl":[["At death the soul passes on and is born again as another","general","asserted",4,"muni"]],"ag":["கோமுகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["மாமுனி","உதயணன்"],"ch":"ud:18"},"ud:347":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":347,"t":["விசையன் றன்னுயிர் விட்டந் தணனாய்","வசையில் காம மயங்கிய மோகத்தின்","இசையி னாலுயிர் நீங்கியே யிங்குவந்","தசையு ணாக்களி றாயின தாகுமே."],"p":"","r":"The sage says the elephant was once Vijayan, who died deluded by lust and was reborn here as an elephant.","n":"Lines 1 and 4 are corrupt in the edition: the words for the brahmin birth and for what the elephant eats do not parse cleanly, and the sense of இசையினால் (fame, music or sound) is open.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["piravi","vinai","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 347","lb":"UD347","mm":1,"ev":"The sage explains that the elephant was Vijayan in a former life, reborn as a beast after dying in lustful delusion.","cl":[["a death in lustful delusion brings rebirth in an animal body","jain","asserted",3,"munivar"]],"ag":["விசையன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["மாதவர்",""],"ch":"ud:19"},"ud:348":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":348,"t":["மித்தி ரன்முன்பு வீறுநற காதலால்","அத்தி மேலுனக் கன்புமுண் டானதால்","வெற்றி வெண்குடை வேந்தேயிவ் வேழத்தின்","ஒத்த வாயுவு மேரெழு நாளென்றார்."],"p":"","r":"The sages tell the king that his love for this elephant comes from an old tie to a friend, and that the elephant has seven days of life left.","n":"The first line (மித்திரன் முன்பு வீறுநற காதலால்) is unclear; it is read as an old bond with a friend named Mitran, not as a claim about hell.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["piravi","nilaiyaamai","natpu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 348","lb":"UD348","mm":1,"ev":"The sages tell the king the elephant's remaining span is seven days.","cl":[["affection felt in one birth comes from a bond of an earlier birth","jain","asserted",2,"munivar"]],"ag":["மாதவர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["மாதவர்","உதயணன்"],"ch":"ud:19"},"ud:349":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":349,"t":["திருந்து ஞானத்திற் றேர்ந்த முனியுரை","பொருந்தக் கேட்ட புரவலன் றுக்கமாய்","வருந்திச் சென்றந்த வாரணந் தன்னிடைச்","சரிந்த காதலிற் றானுரை செய்கின்றான்."],"p":"","r":"Having listened to the sage's wise words, the king is grieved and goes to the elephant to speak to it in melting love.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["piravi","anbu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 349","lb":"UD349","mm":1,"ev":"The king, grieved by the sage's word, goes to the elephant and speaks to it.","cl":[],"ag":["உதயணன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:19"},"ud:350":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":350,"t":["வஞ்ச கத்தின் வரிந்துங் கயிற்றினால்","வெஞ்செம் முள்ளினை வீறிட வூன்றியும்","மிஞ்சிக் கால்விலங் கிற்சிறை செய்தனன்","குஞ்ச ரம்பொறை கொள்ளுதி யென்னவே."],"p":"","r":"The elephant is bound with a cord, a sharp thorn is driven in and its legs are shackled, with the words that it should bear it.","n":"Who binds the elephant is not said in the verse; it is read as the king's men acting for the king, who is addressing the elephant in the last line.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","kodungol"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 350","lb":"UD350","mm":1,"ev":"The elephant is tied, pricked with a thorn and shackled, and told to endure.","cl":[],"ag":["உதயணன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:19"},"ud:351":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":351,"t":["காதல் யானையைக் கையின்மெய் தீண்டியே","போத வெங்கும் புரவலன் றைவரப்","போத கம்மிகப் பொற்பி னிறைஞ்சலிற்","காத லிற்றிண் களிற்றியல் கூறெனா."],"p":"","r":"The king strokes the beloved elephant, which bows low to him, and asks the keepers to tell the nature of such strong elephants.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["anbu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 351","lb":"UD351","mm":1,"ev":"The king touches the elephant, it bows, and he asks about the elephant's nature.","cl":[],"ag":["உதயணன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:19"},"ud:352":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":352,"t":["யானை யாளர்க் குரைத்தெழின் மன்னவன்","தேனெய் தோய்ந்த திருமொழி மாதவர்","ஆன வர்பத மன்பிற் றொழுதுபோய்ச்","சேனை சூழத் திருமனை சேர்ந்தனன்."],"p":"","r":"The king tells the elephant-keepers what he wants, worships the sages' feet in love, and goes home with his army about him.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","aram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 352","lb":"UD352","mm":1,"ev":"The king instructs the keepers, worships the sages and returns to his palace.","cl":[],"ag":["உதயணன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:19"},"ud:353":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":353,"t":["சீலமும்வ ளங்களுஞ் செறிந்தவேழத் தன்மையை","காலையவ்வு ழையர்வந்து கண்டுரைப்ப மன்னனும்","ஆலையம் வலமதா யருகனைவ ணங்கிப்பின்","பாலடிசி னெய்யருந்திப் பாரரசன் செல்லுநாள்."],"p":"","r":"Messengers come in the morning to report the elephant's good character and wellbeing; the king worships the Arugan in the temple, eats milk-rice with ghee and passes the days.","n":"The elephant's fitness is said to include sheela, virtue, and plenty (சீலமும் வளங்களும்), the word used for conduct in Jain ethics.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","iraivan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 353","lb":"UD353","mm":1,"ev":"Messengers bring news of the elephant, and the king worships in the temple before his meal.","cl":[],"ag":["உழையர்","உதயணன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:19"},"ud:354":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":354,"t":["சல்லகீணை கொண்டுடன் சமாதிவந்தே யெய்தலும்","நல்லவானிற் றேவனாய் நாகமுறை செய்யக்கேட்டுச்","சொல்லரிய வேந்தனுஞ் சூழ்ந்தவனி போகமும்","நில்லலவென் றுணர்ந்தன னேமியனைவா வென்றனன்."],"p":"","r":"The elephant meets death in samadhi and is reborn a god in a good heaven; the king, hearing of it, sees that his kingdom and its pleasures will not last and calls his son.","n":"Line 1 (சல்லகீணை, a lute) and the clause on the elephant 'doing its due' (முறை செய்ய) are unclear, so the order of the elephant's death and the god's rebirth is read loosely. The lute is the king's way of reaching the elephant in earlier verses, so it may only recall that.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","samaadhi","veedu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 354","lb":"UD354","mm":1,"ev":"The elephant dies in samadhi and becomes a god; the king, hearing this, resolves to give up his rule and summons his son.","cl":[["a creature that dies in samadhi is reborn as a god","jain","asserted",2,"narrator"],["the king's rule and the enjoyments of the earth do not last","jain","asserted",3,"udhayanan"]],"ag":["களிறு","உதயணன்","நேமியன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:19"},"ud:355":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":355,"t":["அவனும்வந்து தந்தையை யடியிணைவ ணங்கினான்","அவனியுன தாகவா ளென்னமன்னன் செப்பினன்","தவனிதை யாளநான் றாங்குதற்குப் போவனே","உவமமிலா ராச்சிய முற்றதெதற் கென்றனன்."],"p":"","r":"The son bows to his father's feet; the king tells him to live as lord of the earth, and he asks what use a matchless kingdom is to him.","n":"The son's words in lines 3 and 4 are broken: they are read as a refusal of the rule for the sake of austerity, not as an acceptance.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thuravu","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 355","lb":"UD355","mm":1,"ev":"The king offers the earth to his son, who refuses the matchless kingdom.","cl":[],"ag":["நேமியன்","உதயணன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:19"},"ud:356":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":356,"t":["வத்தவன் னிறைவனாக மன்னுகோ முகனுக்கு","வெற்றிநன்ம ணிமுடியை வீறுடனே சூட்டியே","ஒத்துலக மாள்கவென் றுரைபல வுரைத்தபின்","சித்திரநேர் மாதரைச் செல்வனோக்கிக் கூறுவான்."],"p":"","r":"The king crowns Gomukhan with the victor's jewelled crown as lord of the Vatsa land, tells him to rule the world well, then turns to his wives.","n":"The crown goes to Gomukhan and not to the son called in 354; whether he is the son or a minister-friend of that name is not said here.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","thuravu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 356","lb":"UD356","mm":1,"ev":"The king crowns Gomukhan king of the Vatsa land and addresses his queens.","cl":[],"ag":["உதயணன்","கோமுகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:19"},"ud:357":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":357,"t":["தேவியீர்நீர் வேண்டியதென் திருமனை துறந்துபின்","மேவுவனற் றவமென்ன மின்னிடைய மாதரும்","போவதுபொ ருளெமக்கும் புரவலனே நின்னுடன்","தாவில்சீர் விழுத்தவமுந் தாங்குதுமென் றிட்டனர்."],"p":"","r":"The king asks his wives to say what they wish, since he will leave the house for austerity; they answer that going is what they too want, and they will bear the faultless tapas with him.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thuravu","thavam","ilvaazhkkai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 357","lb":"UD357","mm":1,"ev":"The king announces that he will leave home for austerity, and his wives choose to go with him.","cl":[["the wives' place is with the husband, in austerity as in the household","general","reported",3,"queens"]],"ag":["உதயணன்","மாதரும்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:19"},"ud:358":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":358,"t":["உருமண்ணு விடபகன் யூகிநல் வயந்தகன்","பொருவினா லமைச்சரும் பொற்பரசன் மாதரும்","மருவுநன் மலர்ப்பொழில் வண்மைவலங் கொண்டுமிக்","கருண் முனிவர் பாதத்தி லன்புடன் பணிந்தனர்."],"p":"","r":"The ministers Urumannuvan, Vidapakan, Yugi and Vayantakan, the king and the queens walk round the flowering grove and bow in love at the sage's feet.","n":"The sage here (அருண் முனிவர்) is not named; he may be the sage of 347 to 349 or another.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","thuravu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 358","lb":"UD358","mm":1,"ev":"The king, his queens and his four ministers go round the grove and bow at the sage's feet.","cl":[],"ag":["உதயணன்","மாதரும்","யூகி","வயந்தகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:19"},"ud:359":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":359,"t":["நாத்தழும்ப மன்னனு நயமுறு மினிமையின்","தோத்திரங்கள் கொண்டுமீத் தொடுத்தொலியின் வாழ்த்தியே","ஏத்தற முரைத்திட வினிமைவைத்துக் கேட்டனன்","ஏத்தரிய நற்றவமு மெங்களுக் களிக்கென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"The king praises the sage with hymns, listens in joy to the teaching of dharma, and asks the sage to give them the tapas worth praising.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","aram","thavam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 359","lb":"UD359","mm":1,"ev":"The king hymns the sage, hears the dharma and asks for initiation.","cl":[],"ag":["உதயணன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:19"},"ud:360":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":360,"t":["காலமிது காட்சிதலை கண்டுணர்த்தக் கைக்கொண்டு","ஞாலநிகழ் ஞானமு நன்குமிகவே யுணர்த்திச்","சீலமாதி யாயொழுக்கஞ் சீருட னளித்தபின்","கோலமான குஞ்சிமுதல் வாங்கித்தவங் கொண்டனர்."],"p":"","r":"The sage, finding the time right, gives right view, knowledge and conduct; then they pull out their hair and take up austerity.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thuravu","thavam","gnaanam","ozhukkam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 360","lb":"UD360","mm":1,"ev":"The sage instructs them and they take the vows, shedding their hair.","cl":[["right view, knowledge and conduct together make the path of the ascetic","jain","asserted",2,"narrator"]],"ag":["மாதவர்","உதயணன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:19"},"ud:361":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":361,"t":["அறுவகைய காயங்களை யருண்மிக்குற் றோம்பியும்","பொறிகளை மனத்தடக்கிப் புண்ணியமா நோன்புகள்","அறிகுறி யநசன மாற்றுதற் கரிதென","மறுவறு தியானமு மதியகந் தெளிந்தவே."],"p":"","r":"They protect the six classes of living bodies with compassion, hold the senses in the mind, undertake the vows that give merit, and, knowing fasting to be hard, practise a faultless meditation that clears the mind.","n":"Line 3 (அறிகுறி அநசனம் ஆற்றுதற்கு அரிது) is read as a remark that fasting is hard to keep; the wording is dense.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kollaamai","adakkam","thiyaanam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 361","lb":"UD361","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["the six classes of bodied beings are to be protected with compassion","jain","asserted",1,"narrator"],["the senses are to be held in the mind","jain","asserted",2,"narrator"]],"ag":["உதயணன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:19"},"ud:362":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":362,"t":["புறத்தினும் மகத்தினும் போகத்தொடர்ப் பாடுவிட்","டறத்திடை யருளினா லாருயிரை யோம்பியும்","திறத்துடன் சமிதியும் சிந்தையி னடக்கமும்","திறத்திறத் துணர்ந்துபின் றியானமுற்றி னார்களே."],"p":"","r":"Giving up the pull of enjoyment outside and within, they guard life with compassion, learn the regulated conduct and the restraint of thought, and enter meditation.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thuravu","kollaamai","adakkam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 362","lb":"UD362","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["leaving all outer and inner attachment to enjoyment, with compassion for life, is the way of the ascetic","jain","asserted",1,"narrator"]],"ag":["உதயணன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:19"},"ud:363":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":363,"t":["ஒருவகை யெழின்மன மிருவகைத் துறவுடன்","மருவுகுத்தி மூன்றுமே மாற்றிநான்கு சன்னையும்","பொருவிலைம் புலம்மடக்கிப் பொருந்தியவா வச்சமூ","விருவகைச் செவிலியு மெழுவரையும் வைத்தனர்."],"p":"","r":"A numbered scheme of the ascetic's discipline: one kind of mind, two kinds of renunciation, three restraints, four instincts overcome, the five senses held, and further groups of six and seven.","n":"Lines 3 and 4 (the six, the two kinds of nurse, the seven) are corrupt or use technical terms the Tamil alone does not explain, so only the first lines are read with confidence.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["adakkam","thuravu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 363","lb":"UD363","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["the ascetic keeps the three restraints, overcomes the four instincts and holds the five senses","jain","asserted",2,"narrator"]],"ag":["உதயணன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:19"},"ud:364":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":364,"t":["சுத்திமிக எட்டினோடுஞ் சூழ்ந்தயோகு ஒன்பதாம்","பத்துவகை யூற்றடைத்துப் பயின்றவங்கம் பத்தொன்றும்","சித்தம்பனி ரெண்டுசீர்க் கிரியைபதின் மூன்றுடன்","ஒத்தபங்க மீரேழும் ஒருங்குடன் பயின்றனர்."],"p":"","r":"The list continues in numbers, eight purities, nine, ten, eleven limbs, twelve, thirteen actions and fourteen, all of which they learn together.","n":"The verse is a string of numbers whose terms the Tamil does not name (only 'purities', 'limbs' and 'actions' are plain); the numbered list is recorded, not decoded.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thavam","ozhukkam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 364","lb":"UD364","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["உதயணன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:19"},"ud:365":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":365,"t":["உதயண முனிவனு மோங்குமா வரைதனில்","இதயமினி தாகவே யெழில்பெறநல் யோகமாய்","இதமுறு தியானத்தி னிருவினை யெரித்துடன்","பதமினிது சித்தியெய்திப்","பரமசுகத் தினிதிருந்தனன்"],"p":"","r":"The sage Udayanan on a great hill, with a glad heart, burns the two deeds in meditation, reaches perfection and sits in supreme bliss.","n":"The verse has five lines in the edition.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","thiyaanam","veedu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 365","lb":"UD365","mm":1,"ev":"Udayanan, now a sage, burns the two kinds of deed in meditation on a hill and attains perfection.","cl":[["meditation burns both good and bad deeds and brings perfected, supreme bliss","jain","asserted",3,"narrator"]],"ag":["உதயணன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:19"},"ud:366":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":366,"t":["அமைச்சரா மநகரு மானவன்ன மாதரும்","சமைத்த நோன்பு நோற்றுயர்ந்து சமாதிநன் மரணத்தின்","இமைத்தலில் லமரரா நிறைந்தசோத மாதியாய்","அமைத்தவச் சுதம்மள வானபடி யின்புற்றார்."],"p":"","r":"The ministers and the queens, keeping the vow and dying in samadhi, are reborn as gods who enjoy bliss in heavens as their merit allows.","n":"The ministers and queens are tied to a heaven in a level lower than the king's final state in 365: he reaches perfection, they a heaven of limited joy.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["samaadhi","vinai","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 366","lb":"UD366","mm":1,"ev":"The ministers and the queens die in samadhi after their vow and are reborn as gods.","cl":[["death in samadhi after the vow brings a heaven in proportion to what was done","jain","asserted",2,"narrator"]],"ag":["அமைச்சர்","மாதரும்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:19"},"ud:367":{"book":"udayana_kumara_kaviyam","num":367,"t":["பொற்புடைநன் மாதரைப் புணர்ந்துமேனி தீண்டலும்","அற்புதமாய்க் காண்டலு மானவின்சொற் கேட்டலும்","கற்புடைம னத்திலெண்ணிக் காணற்கரி தாகவே","விற்பனநன் மாதவர் வேண்டுசுகந் துய்த்தனர்."],"p":"","r":"The great ascetics, skilled in the way, enjoyed the bliss they wanted, which the verse sets beside the joys of embrace, touch, wonder and sweet words, thought rare to find.","n":"The verse does not say whether the sages enjoy a heaven or the final state, and the comparison with the senses' pleasures is read loosely; it closes the work.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["veedu","inbam","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Udayana Kumara Kaviyam 367","lb":"UD367","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["மாதவர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ud:19"},"ya:1":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":1,"t":["உலக மூன்றும் ஒருங்குணர் கேவலத்","தலகி லாத அனந்த குணக்கடல்","விலகி வெவ்வினை வீடு விளப்பதற்","கிலகு மாமலர்ச் சேவடி யேத்துவாம்."],"p":"","r":"The poet opens by saying he will praise the red lotus feet of the one who knows all three worlds at once through unique omniscience and is an ocean of endless virtues, so that release may be taught through leaving cruel deeds behind.","n":"The word for omniscience (கேவலம்) is the Jain term for the knowledge of the liberated; the invocation names no god and gives no act of grace, only the one who knows and teaches release.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","thiruvadi","vinai","veedu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 1","lb":"YA1","mm":1,"ev":"The poet offers praise to the omniscient lord's feet.","cl":[["the lord has perfect omniscience of the three worlds and endless qualities","jain","asserted",2,"narrator"],["release comes by leaving cruel deeds behind, and it is this that the lord's feet are praised in order to teach","jain","asserted",3,"narrator"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"ya:1"},"ya:2":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":2,"t":["நாதன் நம்முனி சுவ்வதன் நல்கிய","தீதுதீர் திகழ்தீர்த் தஞ்செல் கின்றநாள்","ஏத மஃகி யசோதரன் எய்திய","தோத உள்ளம் ஒருப்படு கின்றதே."],"p":"","r":"The poet places his tale in the sacred age of a tirtha lord and says Yasodharan reached something in it, so that his own mind becomes single and fixed; the sentence is hard to parse.","n":"The first two lines seem to date the tale by a tirthankara's age (நாதன் … முனி சுவ்வதன் … தீர்த்தம்); the syntax of the middle lines is unclear and the object of எய்திய is not given plainly.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","kaalam","manam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 2","lb":"YA2","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"ya:1"},"ya:3":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":3,"t":["உள்வி ரிந்த புகைக்கொடி யுண்டென","எள்ளு கின்றன ரில்லை விளக்கினை;","உள்ளு கின்ற பொருள்திறம் ஓர்பவர்","கொள்வர் எம்உரை கூறுதற் பாலதே."],"p":"","r":"The poet says that people do not scorn a lamp because of its smoke, and that those who ponder the matter within will take up his words as fit to be spoken.","n":"The lamp-and-smoke image is the convention of apology: the flaw is conceded, and the poem's worth is rested on the meaning that careful hearers weigh.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kutram","sol","saandror"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 3","lb":"YA3","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"ya:1"},"ya:4":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":4,"t":["மருவு வெவ்வினை வாயின் மறுத்துடன்","பொருவில் புண்ணியம் போகம் புணர்ப்பதும்","வெருவு செய்யும் வினைப்பயன் இற்றெனத்","தெரிவு றுப்பதும் செப்புத லுற்றதே."],"p":"","r":"The poet says his poem will show how cruel deeds that cling are turned back, how incomparable merit and enjoyment come together, and what fearful fruit deeds bring.","n":"This is a statement of the poem's plan: the story is to be an exhibit of karma's fruit, good and bad, not only a tale of a king.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vinai","oozh","aram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 4","lb":"YA4","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["merit joins with enjoyment as its fruit, and cruel deeds have a fearful fruit","jain","asserted",2,"narrator"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"ya:1"},"ya:5":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":5,"t":["பைம்பொன் நாவற் பொழிற்பர தத்திடை","நம்பு நீரணி நாடுள தூடுபோய்","வம்பு வார்பொழில் மாமுகில் சூடுவ","திம்பர் ஈடில தோதிய மென்பதே."],"p":"","r":"The poet places the story in Bharata, in the land of the golden jambu groves, a watered country whose high gardens wear the great clouds as crests and which he says has no equal here.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 5","lb":"YA5","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"ya:2"},"ya:6":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":6,"t":["திசையு லாமிசை யுந்திரு வுந்நிலாய்","வசையி லாநகர் வானவர் போகமல்","கசைவி லாஅள காபுரி தானலால்","இசைவி லாத இராச புரமதே."],"p":"","r":"In that land stands Rajapuram, a faultless city of renown and wealth that only Alakapuri, the city of the gods' pleasures, could rival.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naadu","pugazh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 6","lb":"YA6","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"ya:2"},"ya:7":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":7,"t":["இஞ்சி மஞ்சினை யெய்தி நிமிர்ந்தது","மஞ்சு லாமதி சூடின மாளிகை","அஞ்சொ லாரவர் பாடலொ டாடலால்","விஞ்சை யாருல கத்தினை வெல்லுமே."],"p":"","r":"The city's rampart reaches the clouds, its mansions wear the moon, and the song and dance of its sweet-voiced women surpass the world of the Vidyadharas.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naadu","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 7","lb":"YA7","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"ya:2"},"ya:8":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":8,"t":["பாரி தத்தினைப் பண்டையின் மும்மடிப்","பூரி தத்தொளிர் மாலைவெண் பொற்குடை","வாரி தத்தின் மலர்ந்த கொடைக்காரன்","மாரி தத்தனென் பானுளன் மன்னவன்."],"p":"","r":"The poet introduces Maridattan, the generous king of that city, under a white umbrella and a garland.","n":"His name is spelled with a word break in the edition (மாரி தத்தன்); the king is introduced here and acts from verse 13.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vendhan","eegai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 8","lb":"YA8","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"ya:2"},"ya:9":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":9,"t":["அரசன் மற்றவன் தன்னொடும் அந்நகர்","மருவு மானுயர் வானவர் போகமும்","பொருவில் வீடு புணர்திற மும்மிவை","தெரிவ தொன்றிலர் செல்வ மயக்கினால்."],"p":"","r":"The king and the people of his city, bewildered by wealth, discerned nothing of the gods' pleasure or of the road to release.","n":"The poet states the fault before the story starts: the king and the city are first shown as well-off and ignorant of the way to release (veedu), which prepares the sacrifice that follows.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","veedu","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 9","lb":"YA9","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["wealth bewilders a man so that he does not look for release","general","asserted",4,"narrator"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"ya:2"},"ya:10":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":10,"t":["நெரிந்த நுண்குழல் நேரிழை யாருழை","சரிந்த காதல் தடையீல தாகவே","வரிந்த வெஞ்சிலை மன்னவன் வைகுநாள்","விரிந்த தின்னிள வேனிற் பருவமே."],"p":"","r":"While the king lives surrounded by women whose love knows no bar, the young spring season comes.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaalam","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 10","lb":"YA10","mm":1,"ev":"Young spring arrives while the king dwells among his women.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"ya:2"},"ya:11":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":11,"t":["கோங்கு பொற்குடை கொண்டுக வித்தன","வாங்கு வாகை வளைத்தன சாமரை","கூங்கு யிற்குலம் இன்னியங் கொண்டொலி","பாங்கு வண்டொடு பாடின தேனினம்."],"p":"","r":"In the spring the kongu tree holds up golden umbrellas, the vakai tree bends and sways like fly-whisks, and cuckoos and bees sing as if in concert.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaalam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 11","lb":"YA11","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"ya:2"},"ya:12":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":12,"t":["மலர்ந்த பூஞ்சிகை வார்கொடி மங்கையர்","தலந்த லந்தொறும் ஆடினர் தாழ்ந்தனர்","கலந்த காதன்மை காட்டுநர் போலவே","வலந்த வண்தளிர் மாவின மேயெலாம்."],"p":"","r":"Women play in the flowering groves, bending low, while the mango trees clasp their tender leaves as if showing mutual love.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaalam","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 12","lb":"YA12","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"ya:2"},"ya:13":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":13,"t":["உயர்ந்த சோலைகள் ஊழெதிர் கொண்டிட","வயந்த மன்னவன் வந்தெனத் தென்றலின்","நயந்த மன்னரும் நன்னகர் மாந்தரும்","வயந்த மாடுவ கையின ராயினார்."],"p":"","r":"The groves greet the season like a king, and the kings and the townspeople resolve to take up the spring games.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaalam","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 13","lb":"YA13","mm":1,"ev":"The kings and townspeople prepare to play the spring games.","cl":[],"ag":["நகர மாந்தர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ya:2"},"ya:14":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":14,"t":["கானு லாவியும் காவும் அடுத்துடன்","வேனி லாடல் விரும்பிய போழ்தினில்","மான யானைய மன்னவன் தன்னுழை","ஏனை மாந்தர் இறைஞ்சுபு கூறினார்."],"p":"","r":"As they wish to go to the forest park and gardens to play, the townspeople bow to the king and speak.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","kaalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 14","lb":"YA14","mm":1,"ev":"The townspeople bow to the king and address him.","cl":[],"ag":["நகர மாந்தர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ya:2"},"ya:15":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":15,"t":["என்றும் இப்பரு வத்தினோ டைப்பசிச்","சென்று தேவி சிறப்பது செய்துமஃ","தொன்றும் ஓரல மாயினம் ஒன்றலா","நன்ற லாதன நங்களை வந்துறும்."],"p":"","r":"The townspeople tell the king that they have always honoured the goddess at this season and in the month of Aippasi, that this time they have not done so, and that ill will now come upon them.","n":"Line 3 (ஒன்றும் ஓரலமாயினம்) is hard: it is read here as 'we have done none of it'. The edition ties the rite to this season and to Aippasi together, and the verse does not explain the pairing.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","kaalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 15","lb":"YA15","mm":1,"ev":"The townspeople remind the king of the goddess's yearly worship, which they have not done.","cl":[["failing to honour the goddess in her season brings misfortune on the people","general","reported",4,"nagar_makkal"]],"ag":["நகர மாந்தர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["நகர மாந்தர்","மாரிதத்தன்"],"ch":"ya:2"},"ya:16":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":16,"t":["நோவு செய்திடும் நோய்பல ஆக்கிடும்","ஆவி கொள்ளும் அலாதன வுஞ்செயும்","தேவி சிந்தை சிதைத்தனள் சீறுமேல்","காவன் மன்ன! கடிதெழு கென்றனர்."],"p":"","r":"They warn that an angered goddess brings painful diseases, takes lives and does other evil, and urge the king to rise quickly and protect them.","n":"The people do not ask the king to give up the spring games; they ask him to protect the town from the goddess, a duty that puts the sacrifice that follows in the king's hands.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","noy","kootru"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 16","lb":"YA16","mm":1,"ev":"The townspeople warn the king that the goddess's anger will bring disease and death, and call on him to act.","cl":[["a goddess who is angered sends disease and takes lives","general","reported",2,"nagar_makkal"]],"ag":["நகர மாந்தர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["நகர மாந்தர்","மாரிதத்தன்"],"ch":"ya:2"},"ya:17":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":17,"t":["என்று கூறலும் ஏதமி தென்றிலன்","சென்று நல்லறத் திற்றெளி வின்மையால்","நன்றி தென்றனன் நன்னக ரப்புறத்","தென்தி சைக்கண் சிறப்பொடு சென்றனன்."],"p":"","r":"Hearing this, the king, who has no clear grasp of true dharma, finds no fault in it, judges it good and goes in state to the south outside the city.","n":"The narrator, not a character, gives the reason for the king's choice: lack of clarity (தெளிவின்மை) in dharma, which the story treats as a fault of understanding rather than cruelty.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aram","arasu","thelivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 17","lb":"YA17","mm":1,"ev":"The king accepts the townspeople's advice and sets out in state for the goddess's shrine south of the city.","cl":[["a king without clarity about right dharma takes a harmful rite to be good","general","asserted",2,"narrator"]],"ag":["மாரிதத்தன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ya:2"},"ya:18":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":18,"t":["சண்ட கோபி தகவிளல் தத்துவம்","கொண்ட கேள்வியுங் கூரறி வுமிலாத்","தொண்டர் கொண்டு தொழுந்துருத் தேவதை","சண்ட மாரி தனதிட மெய்தினான்."],"p":"","r":"The king reaches the place of the goddess Chandamari, worshipped by devotees whom the poet calls lacking in learning and keen understanding.","n":"The poet's verdict on the devotees is his own and is not put in any speaker's mouth. Line 1 (சண்ட கோபி தகவிளல் தத்துவம்) is garbled and its sense is not recovered.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","kallaamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 18","lb":"YA18","mm":1,"ev":"The king arrives at Chandamari's shrine.","cl":[],"ag":["மாரிதத்தன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ya:2"},"ya:19":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":19,"t":["பாவ மூர்த்தி படிவ மிருந்தவத்","தேவி மாடம் அடைந்து செறிகழல்","மாவ லோன்வலங் கொண்டு வணங்கினன்","தேவி யெம்மிடர் சிந்துக என்றரோ."],"p":"","r":"At the shrine whose image the poet calls a form of sin, the king circles the goddess, bows and prays that she scatter their distress.","n":"'Form of sin' (பாவ மூர்த்தி) is the narrator's phrase for the image, so the poet gives his judgment even as the king worships.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","iraivan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 19","lb":"YA19","mm":1,"ev":"The king circles Chandamari's image, bows and prays her to remove their distress.","cl":[["worship can move the goddess to remove the city's distress","general","reported",4,"maridattan"]],"ag":["மாரிதத்தன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["மாரிதத்தன்","சண்டமாரி"],"ch":"ya:2"},"ya:20":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":20,"t":["மன்னன் ஆணையின் மாமயில் வாரணம்","துன்னு சூகரம் ஆடெரு மைத்தொகை","இன்ன சாதி விலங்கி லிரட்டைகள்","பின்னி வந்து பிறங்கின கண்டனன்."],"p":"","r":"At the king's order pairs of peacocks, cocks, pigs, goats and buffaloes are brought together and he sees them gathered.","n":"The animals are collected as victims, and the next verse shows the king looking beyond them to human victims.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kolai","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 20","lb":"YA20","mm":1,"ev":"Pairs of each kind of animal are brought together at the king's order.","cl":[],"ag":["மாரிதத்தன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ya:2"},"ya:21":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":21,"t":["யானிவ் வாளினின் மக்களி ரட்டையை","ஈன மில்பலி யாக இயற்றினால்","ஏனை மானுயர் தாமிவ் விலங்கினில்","ஆன பூசனை யாற்றுதல் ஆறெனா."],"p":"","r":"The king says he will offer a faultless pair of humans with his sword, so that other people will then worship the goddess with animals.","n":"The work does not answer the claim here; verse 17 has already set it down to the king's lack of clarity in dharma. The speaker is inferred from யான் and the sword, since no one is named in the verse.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kolai","vazhipaadu","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 21","lb":"YA21","mm":1,"ev":"The king resolves to offer a pair of humans to the goddess with his own sword.","cl":[["the goddess is rightly worshipped by killing living pairs, with humans the greatest offering","general","reported",3,"maridattan"]],"ag":["மாரிதத்தன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["மாரிதத்தன்",""],"ch":"ya:2"},"ya:22":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":22,"t":["வாட லொன்றிலர் மக்களி ரட்டையர்","ஈடி லாத இயல்பின ரிவ்வழி","ஏட சண்ட கரும! தந் தீகென","நாட ஓடினன் நன்னகர் தன்னுளே."],"p":"","r":"The king calls to Chandakarma to bring the unblemished twin children, and the man runs into the city to find them.","n":"The first two lines, praising the twins' faultlessness, are read as the king's demand for a spotless victim; the verse does not say who first names the twins.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kolai","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 22","lb":"YA22","mm":1,"ev":"The king orders Chandakarma to bring the twin children, and the man runs into the city to seek them.","cl":[],"ag":["சண்டகரும"],"ec":[],"spk":["மாரிதத்தன்","சண்டகரும"],"ch":"ya:2"},"ya:23":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":23,"t":["ஆயிடைச் சுதத்த னைஞ்ஞூற் றுவரருந் தவர்க ளோடுந்","தூயமா தவத்தின் மிக்க உபாசகர் தொகையுஞ் சூழச்","சேயிடைச் சென்றோர் தீர்த்த வந்தனை செய்யச் செல்வோன்","மாயமில் குணக்குன்றன்ன மாதவர்க் கிறைவன் வந்தான்."],"p":"","r":"The lord of ascetics Sudattan, a mountain of virtue, arrives with five hundred great ascetics and a throng of lay followers on his way to worship at a holy place.","n":"தீர்த்த வந்தனை (line 3) could mean worship at a holy place or of the teachers; the verse leaves the object open.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thavam","thuravu","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 23","lb":"YA23","mm":1,"ev":"Sudattan comes with five hundred ascetics and the lay company, going to worship at a tirtha.","cl":[],"ag":["சுதத்தன்","உபாசகர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ya:2"},"ya:24":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":24,"t":["வந்துமா நகர்ப்பு றத்தோர் வளமலர்ப் பொழிலுள் விட்டுச்","சிந்தையால் நெறிக்கண் தீமை தீர்த்திடும் நியம முற்றி","அந்திலா சனங்கொண் டண்ண லனசனத் தவம மர்ந்தான்","முந்துநா முரைத்த சுற்ற முழுவதி னோடும் மாதோ."],"p":"","r":"He halts in a flower garden outside the city, keeps the vow that clears the road of evil in his mind, takes his seat and settles into the penance of fasting with his company.","n":"The garden is outside the city, and so is the goddess's shrine, so the ascetics and the king's party are near each other without meeting.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thavam","aasanam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 24","lb":"YA24","mm":1,"ev":"Sudattan and his company stop in a garden outside the city and he begins a fast.","cl":[],"ag":["சுதத்தன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ya:2"},"ya:25":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":25,"t":["உளங்கொள மலிந்த கொள்கை யுபாசகர் குழுவி னுள்ளார்","அளந்தறி வரிய கேள்வி யபயமுன் னுருசி தங்கை","இளம்பிறை யனைய நீரா ளபயமா மதியென் பாளும்","துளங்கிய மெய்ய ருள்ளந் துளங்கலர் தொழுது நின்றார்."],"p":"","r":"Among the lay company are Abhayaruchi and his younger sister Abhayamati, unshaken in mind, who bow and stand before the teacher.","n":"The brother's name in line 2 (அபயமுன் னுருசி) is irregular in the edition; it is taken as Abhayaruchi because the next line makes the woman his sister Abhayamati.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thavam","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 25","lb":"YA25","mm":1,"ev":"The brother and sister among the lay followers bow and stand before Sudattan.","cl":[],"ag":["அபயருசி","அபயமதி"],"ec":[],"ch":"ya:2"},"ya:26":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":26,"t":["அம்முனி யவர்கள் தம்மை அருளிய மனத்த னாகி","வம்மினீர் பசியின் வாடி வருந்திய மெய்ய ரானீர்","எம்முடன் உண்டி மாற்றா தின்றுநீர் சரிதை போகி","நம்மிடை வருக என்ன நற்றவர்த் தொழுது சென்றார்."],"p":"","r":"The teacher, gracious toward them, tells the two, weakened by hunger, to go on the day's alms round and then come to him; they bow to the ascetic and go.","n":"The sense of lines 2 to 3 (உண்டி மாற்றாது, சரிதை போகி) turns on உண்டி மாற்றாது, rendered here as the day's food and round; the exact instruction is not certain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["arul","pasi","thavam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 26","lb":"YA26","mm":1,"ev":"Sudattan bids the pair go on their round and return, and they bow and depart.","cl":[],"ag":["சுதத்தன்","அபயருசி","அபயமதி"],"ec":[],"spk":["சுதத்தன்","அபயருசி"],"ch":"ya:2"},"ya:27":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":27,"t":["வள்ளிய மலருஞ் சாந்தும் மணிபுனை கலனு மின்றாய்","வெள்ளிய துடையொன் றாகி வென்றவ ருருவ மேலார்","கொள்ளிய லமைந்த கோலக் குல்லக வேடங் கொண்ட","வள்ளலு மடந்தை தானும் வளநகர் மருவப் புக்கார்."],"p":"","r":"Without flowers, sandal paste or jewels, in a single white cloth and in the form of those who have conquered, wearing the cap of a disguise, the youth and the maiden enter the city.","n":"The pair is identified as the brother and sister of verse 25 from the story so far; the verse names them only as the generous one and the maiden, so the king's search in verse 22 and their entry are linked by the story and not by the words.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thuravu","vesham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 27","lb":"YA27","mm":1,"ev":"The youth and the maiden, dressed as ascetics, enter the city.","cl":[],"ag":["அபயருசி","அபயமதி"],"ec":[],"ch":"ya:2"},"ya:28":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":28,"t":["வில்லின தெல்லை கண்ணால் நோக்கிமெல் லடிகள் பாவி","நல்லருள் புரிந்து யிர்க்கண் ணகைமுத லாய நாணி","இல்லவர் எதிர்கொண் டீயின் எதிர்கொளுண் டியரு மாகி","நல்லற அமிர்த முண்டார் நடந்தனர் வீதி யூடே."],"p":"","r":"The two young ascetics walk the street with eyes on the ground a bow's length ahead, treading gently out of mercy for living things, and take food only from householders who receive them and offer it.","n":"The bow's-length gaze is the way a Jain mendicant walks so as not to crush small lives; the poem shows the pair practising non-killing in the very street where they will be seized. Line 2, from நகை to நாணி, is damaged.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thavam","arul","kollaamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 28","lb":"YA28","mm":1,"ev":"The brother and sister go through the city street on their alms round in the ascetics' manner.","cl":[],"ag":["அபயருசி","அபயமதி"],"ec":[],"ch":"ya:3"},"ya:29":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":29,"t":["அண்டல ரெனினுங் கண்டா லன்புவைத் தஞ்சு நீரார்க்","கண்டனன் கண்ட சண்ட கருமனும் மனங்க லங்காப்","புண்டரீ கத்தின் கொம்பும் பொருவிலா மதனும் போன்று","கொண்டிளம் பருவ மென்கொல் குழைந்திவண் வந்த தென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"Candakarman sees the pair, whose way is to love and spare even a foe, and wonders in an unshaken mind why two so tender and fair, like Lakshmi's creeper and the god of love, have come here in youth.","n":"The officer who has been sent to find a pair for sacrifice recognises the pair's gentleness and beauty at once, and the poem lets the reader feel the cost before the seizure.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arul","azhagu","ilamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 29","lb":"YA29","mm":1,"ev":"Candakarman catches sight of the brother and sister and wonders at their youth and beauty.","cl":[],"ag":["சண்ட கருமன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சண்ட கருமன்",""],"ch":"ya:3"},"ya:30":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":30,"t":["எனமனத் தெண்ணி நெஞ்சத் திகலுடை மன்ன னேவல்","தனைநினைந் தவர்கள் தம்மைத் தன்னுழை யவரின் வவ்விச்","சினமலி தேவி கோயில் திசைமுகம் அடுத்துச் சென்றான்","இனையது பட்ட தென்னென் றிளையரு மெண்ணி னாரே."],"p":"","r":"Having thought it over, Candakarman remembers the king's order, has his men take the two, and leads them toward the angry goddess's temple while the two wonder what has befallen them.","n":"The king's order (verses 20-22) and its carrying out meet here; the poet notes the officer's doubt in verse 29 and does not let it stop him.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","kolai","naragam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 30","lb":"YA30","mm":1,"ev":"Candakarman seizes the two on the king's command and takes them toward the goddess's temple.","cl":[],"ag":["சண்ட கருமன்","அபயருசி","அபயமதி"],"ec":[],"ch":"ya:3"},"ya:31":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":31,"t":["வன்சொல்வாய் மறவர் சூழ மதியமோர் மின்னோ டொன்றி","தன்பரி வேடந் தன்னுள் தானனி வருவ தேபோல்","அன்பினால் ஐயன் தங்கை யஞ்சுத லஞ்சி நெஞ்சில்","தன்கையான் முன்கை பற்றித் தானவட் கினைய சொல்வான்."],"p":"","r":"Surrounded by harsh soldiers, the pair look like the moon joined to lightning, and the brother, seeing his sister's fear, takes her forearm and begins to speak to her.","n":"The moon-and-lightning simile makes the captives bright against the dark guard; the poet names the brother by his rank as elder, not by a proper name.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["anbu","acham","kudi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 31","lb":"YA31","mm":1,"ev":"Soldiers surround the pair and the brother takes his frightened sister's arm to comfort her.","cl":[],"ag":["மறவர்","அபயருசி","அபயமதி"],"ec":[],"spk":["அபயருசி","அபயமதி"],"ch":"ya:3"},"ya:32":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":32,"t":["தங்கைநீ யஞ்சல் நெஞ்சில் நமக்கிவண் அழிவொன் றில்லை","இங்குநம் உடம்பிற் கேதம் எய்துவ திவரின் எய்தின்","அங்கதற் கழுங்கல் என்னை யதுநம தன்றென் றன்றோ","மங்கையாம் அதனை முன்னே மனத்தினில் விடுத்த தென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"The brother tells his sister not to fear, since no destruction can come to them; any harm these men do will reach only the body, and why mourn what is not theirs, which the mind had already put aside.","n":"Line 4 turns on மங்கையாம் அதனை, whose syntax is unclear; the sense here follows the plain run of the lines.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","uyir","acham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 32","lb":"YA32","mm":1,"ev":"The brother tells his sister that harm to the body is no harm to the self.","cl":[["the self cannot be destroyed, and harm done to the body is not harm to what is one's own","jain","asserted",2,"abhayaruchi"]],"ag":["அபயருசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["அபயருசி","அபயமதி"],"ch":"ya:3"},"ya:33":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":33,"t":["அஞ்சின மெனினும் மெய்யே யடைபவந் தடையு மானால்","அஞ்சுத லதனி னென்னை பயனமக் கதுவு மன்றி","அஞ்சுதல் துன்பந் தானே யல்லதும் அதனிற் சூழ்ந்த","நஞ்சென வினைகள் நம்மை நாடொறு நலியு மென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"The brother adds that if what is due must come, fear gains nothing and is itself pain, and that fear besides feeds deeds that like poison wear them down day by day.","n":"Fear is not set aside as weakness but treated as a cause: it makes new karma, so the sibling's calm is a matter of what the next birth will be.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["acham","vinai","oozh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 33","lb":"YA33","mm":1,"ev":"The brother argues that fear can neither prevent the coming harm nor do anything but add pain and karma.","cl":[["what is due to come will come, and fearing it avails nothing","jain","asserted",1,"abhayaruchi"],["fear is itself suffering and feeds the deeds that afflict a person daily","jain","asserted",3,"abhayaruchi"]],"ag":["அபயருசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["அபயருசி","அபயமதி"],"ch":"ya:3"},"ya:34":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":34,"t":["அல்லது மன்னை நின்னோ டியானுமுன் னநேக வாரத்","தொல்வினை துரப்ப ஓடி விலங்கிடைச் சுழன்ற போது","நல்லுயிர் நமர்கள் தாமே நலிந்திட இறந்த தெல்லாம்","மல்லன்மா தவனின் யாமே மறித்துணர்ந் தனமு மன்றோ."],"p":"","r":"He says that long ago, driven by old deeds, he and his sister wandered among animals, where their own kin tormented and killed them, as they have now learned from the great sage.","n":"The same pair in an earlier life were killed in animal births; the present capture is made to look like a later turn of the same wheel, not a new wrong. Line 1 has மன்னை, perhaps for அன்னை, a term of address to the sister.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vinai","piravi","oozh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 34","lb":"YA34","mm":1,"ev":"The brother recalls their earlier births among animals, where their kin slew them.","cl":[["old deeds drive the soul to wander through animal births where it is hurt and killed","jain","asserted",2,"abhayaruchi"]],"ag":["அபயருசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["அபயருசி","அபயமதி"],"ch":"ya:3"},"ya:35":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":35,"t":["கறங்கென வினையினோடிக் கதியொரு நான்கி னுள்ளும்","பிறந்தநாம் பெற்ற பெற்ற பிறவிகள் பேச லாகா","இறந்தன இறந்து போக எய்துவ தெய்திப் பின்னும்","பிறந்திட இறந்த தெல்லாம் இதுவுமவ் வியல்பிற் றேயாம்."],"p":"","r":"He says that, spun by deeds like a whirling wheel, they were born over and over in the four states of life, births too many to tell, and that this present moment is of the same kind.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["piravi","vinai","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 35","lb":"YA35","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["deeds whirl the soul through births in the four states of existence, past counting","jain","asserted",1,"abhayaruchi"]],"ag":["அபயருசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["அபயருசி","அபயமதி"],"ch":"ya:3"},"ya:36":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":36,"t":["பிறந்தநாம் பிறவி தோறும் பெறுமுடம் பவைகள் பேணாத்","துறந்தறம் புணரின் நம்மைத் தொடர்ந்தன வல்ல தோகாய்;","சிறந்ததை யிதுவென் றெண்ணிச் செம்மையே செய்யார் தாமே","இறந்தன ரிறந்த காலத் தெண்ணிறந் தனர்க ளெல்லாம்."],"p":"","r":"He says that if in every birth they had given up the bodies they took and practised dharma, those births would not have followed them; the many who did not see what is best and did not do right have died countless deaths.","n":"The syntax of lines 1-2 is a question that expects yes; the reading takes it so.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["aram","thuravu","piravi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 36","lb":"YA36","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["giving up the body and doing dharma would have stopped the birth that follows; those who do not do it die countless times","jain","asserted",2,"abhayaruchi"]],"ag":["அபயருசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["அபயருசி","அபயமதி"],"ch":"ya:3"},"ya:37":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":37,"t":["முழமொரு மூன்றிற் றொட்டு மூரிவெஞ் சிலைக ளைஞ்ஞூ","றெழுமுறை பெருகி மேன்மே லெய்திய உருவ மெல்லாம்","அழலினுண் மூழ்கி யன்ன வருநவை நரகந் தம்முள்","உழைவிழி நம்மோ டொன்றி யொருவின உணர லாமோ."],"p":"","r":"He recounts that bodies in the hells, grown from three cubits to five hundred bows, were like those drowned in fire, and asks whether the many that came and went with them can be known.","n":"The measures are the Jain cosmology's and are given as reported speech of the brother; lines 3-4 are hard.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["naragam","yaakkai","vinai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 37","lb":"YA37","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["bodies in hell grow in size to five hundred bows and are full of burning pain","jain","reported",3,"abhayaruchi"]],"ag":["அபயருசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["அபயருசி","அபயமதி"],"ch":"ya:3"},"ya:38":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":38,"t":["அங்குலி யயங்கம் பாக மணுமுறை பெருகி மேன்மேல்","பொங்கிய வோரைஞ் ஞூறு புகைபெறு முடையு டம்பு","வெங்கனல் வினையின் மேனாள் விலங்கிடைப் புக்கு வீழ்ந்து","நங்களை வந்து கூடி நடந்தன அனந்த மன்றோ."],"p":"","r":"He says that bodies in the animal births, which grew by fine steps from the smallest fraction of a finger to five hundred, smoked in the fire of deeds, and that the pair took countless such bodies.","n":"The measures of the first two lines are obscure; only the plain sense that the pair have worn countless animal bodies is firm.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","vinai","piravi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 38","lb":"YA38","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["the soul has taken countless bodies in the realm of animals under the fire of its deeds","jain","asserted",3,"abhayaruchi"]],"ag":["அபயருசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["அபயருசி","அபயமதி"],"ch":"ya:3"},"ya:39":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":39,"t":["ஓரினோர் முழங்கை தன்மே லோரொரு பதேச மேறி","மூரிவெஞ் சிலைகண் மூவி ராயிர முற்ற உற்ற","பாரின்மேன் மனித ரியாக்கை பண்டுநாங் கொண்டு விட்ட","வாரிவாய் மணலு மாற்றா வகையின வல்ல வோதான்."],"p":"","r":"He says that the human bodies of earth, from one cubit up to six thousand bows, which they once took and cast off, are more than the sands of the sea.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","piravi","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 39","lb":"YA39","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["the soul has taken and left more human bodies than the sea has sand","jain","asserted",3,"abhayaruchi"]],"ag":["அபயருசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["அபயருசி","அபயமதி"],"ch":"ya:3"},"ya:40":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":40,"t":["இருமுழ மாதி யாக வெய்திய வகையி னோங்கி வருசிலை","யிருபத் தைந்தின் வந்துறும் அங்க மெல்லாம்","திருமலி தவத்திற் சென்று தேவர்தம் உலகிற் பெற்ற","தொருவரால் உரைக்க லாமோ உலந்தன அனந்த மாலோ."],"p":"","r":"He says that the bodies of many sizes, even those the pair took in the gods' worlds through austerity, cannot be counted, so many have perished.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","thavam","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 40","lb":"YA40","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["even the heavenly bodies won by austerity are lost in their countless number","jain","asserted",4,"abhayaruchi"]],"ag":["அபயருசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["அபயருசி","அபயமதி"],"ch":"ya:3"},"ya:41":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":41,"t":["துன்பகா ரணமி தென்றே துடக்கறு கெனவுந் துஞ்சா","அன்புறா நரகர் யாக்கை யவைகளும் அமரர் கற்பத்","தின்பகா ரணமி தென்றே யெம்முடன் இயல்க என்றே","அன்புசெய் தனகள் தாமும் அழியுநா ளழியு மன்றே."],"p":"","r":"He says that the bodies of the hell-beings, hated as the cause of pain and begging to end, and those of the gods, loved as the cause of joy and asked to stay, all perish when their day comes.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","naragam","yaakkai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 41","lb":"YA41","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["bodies of hell and heaven alike perish whether hated or loved","jain","asserted",4,"abhayaruchi"]],"ag":["அபயருசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["அபயருசி","அபயமதி"],"ch":"ya:3"},"ya:42":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":42,"t":["வந்துடன் வணங்கும் வானோர் மணிபுனை மகுட கோடி","தந்திரு வடிக ளேந்துந் தமனிய பீட மாக","இந்திர விபவம் பெற்ற இமையவ ரிறைவ ரேனுந்","தந்திரு வுருவம் பொன்றித் தளர்ந்தன ரனந்த மன்றோ"],"p":"","r":"He says that even the lords of the gods, for whom crowned heads bow before their feet and whose gold seats are held up by the heavenly, lost their own forms in countless numbers.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","arasu","yaakkai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 42","lb":"YA42","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["even Indra-like lords of the gods lose their forms and die","jain","asserted",4,"abhayaruchi"]],"ag":["அபயருசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["அபயருசி","அபயமதி"],"ch":"ya:3"},"ya:43":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":43,"t":["மக்களிற் பிறவி யுள்ளும் மன்னர்தம் மன்ன ராகித்","திக்கெலா மடிப்ப டுத்துந் திகிரியஞ் செல்வ ரேனும்","அக்குலத் துடம்பு தோன்றி யன்றுதொட் டின்று காறும்","ஒக்கநின் றார்கள் வையத் தொருவரு மில்லை யன்றே."],"p":"","r":"He says that even among men, kings of kings who turn the wheel and bring all quarters under their feet, no one since their bodies arose has stood firm in the world until today.","n":"Lines 3-4 turn on அக்குலத்து உடம்பு தோன்றி; the reading takes it as the line of the kings' bodies from birth to now.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","arasu","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 43","lb":"YA43","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["no emperor, however great, has outlasted death","general","asserted",4,"abhayaruchi"]],"ag":["அபயருசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["அபயருசி","அபயமதி"],"ch":"ya:3"},"ya:44":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":44,"t":["ஆடைமுன் னசைஇய திட்டோ ரந்துகி லசைத்த லொன்றோ","மாடமுன் னதுவி டுத்தர் வளமனை புகுத லொன்றோ","நாடிடுனவ் வகையு மஃதே நமதிறப் பொடுபி றப்பும்","பாடுவ தினியென் நங்கை பரிவொழிந் திடுக என்றான்."],"p":"","r":"He asks whether the loosening of a worn cloth or the leaving of one house for a richer one differs from death and birth, and bids his sister put her grief away.","n":"The figure carries the argument of verses 32-43 into one daily act; its use at the moment of arrest turns a doctrine into comfort.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["piravi","nilaiyaamai","yaakkai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 44","lb":"YA44","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["death and birth are no more than putting off a cloth or leaving a house for another","jain","asserted",3,"abhayaruchi"]],"ag":["அபயருசி"],"ec":[],"spk":["அபயருசி","அபயமதி"],"ch":"ya:3"},"ya:45":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":45,"t":["அண்ணனீ யருளிற் றெல்லாம் அருவருப் புடைய மெய்யின்","நண்ணிய நமதென் றுள்ளத் தவர்களுக் குறுதி நாடின்","விண்ணின்மே லின்ப மல்லால் விழைபயன் வெறுத்து நின்ற","கண்ணனாய் நங்கட் கெல்லாங் கட்டுரை யென்னை யென்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"The sister says that all her brother has taught fits those who hold the loathsome body as their own and seek only heaven's joy, and asks what the teaching is for those who have turned away even from such fruit.","n":"Her question tests whether the brother's argument, which rests on the body's impermanence and heaven's loss, reaches those who want neither; the lines are loose and the reading is a best sense.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","inbam","thuravu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 45","lb":"YA45","mm":1,"ev":"The sister puts a question to her brother about the teaching meant for those who renounce all fruit.","cl":[],"ag":["அபயமதி"],"ec":[],"spk":["அபயமதி","அபயருசி"],"ch":"ya:3"},"ya:46":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":46,"t":["அருவினை விளையு ளாய அருந்துயர்ப் பிறவி தோறும்","வெருவிய மனத்து நம்மை வீடில விளைந்த வாறும்","திருவுடை யடிகள் தந்த திருவறப் பயனுந் தேறி","வெருவிதா விடுத்த வாழ்க்கை விடுவதற் கஞ்ச லுண்டோ."],"p":"","r":"The sister says that, having understood how deeds ripen into painful births and left a frightened mind without release, and the fruit of dharma the venerable sage gave, there is no fear in leaving this fearful life.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","piravi","veedu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 46","lb":"YA46","mm":1,"ev":"The sister says she is ready to leave the life of births.","cl":[["deeds ripen into painful, release-less births, and the dharma the sage taught gives its own fruit","jain","asserted",1,"abhayamati"]],"ag":["அபயமதி"],"ec":[],"spk":["அபயமதி","அபயருசி"],"ch":"ya:3"},"ya:47":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":47,"t":["பெண்ணுயி ரளிய தாமே பெருந்திற லறிவும் பேராத்","திண்மையு முடைய வல்ல சிந்தையி னென்ப தெண்ணி","அண்ணனீ யருளிச் செய்தா யன்றிநல் லறத்திற் காட்சி","கண்ணிய மனத்த ரிம்மைக் காதலு முடைய ரோதாம்."],"p":"","r":"The sister says that her brother, thinking women's minds are weak in great knowledge and firmness, spoke tenderly; but those whose minds are fixed on seeing the good dharma do not love this life.","n":"The first claim is what she thinks her brother thought, not what he said; she answers it by the second, so the work can be read as turning the doubt aside without a separate reply.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["manam","aram","penvazhi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 47","lb":"YA47","mm":1,"ev":"The sister answers the doubt that a woman's mind is too weak for renunciation.","cl":[["women's minds lack great knowledge and unmoving firmness","general","reported",2,"abhayamati"],["those whose minds are fixed on the vision of the good dharma do not cling to this life","jain","asserted",3,"abhayamati"]],"ag":["அபயமதி"],"ec":[],"spk":["அபயமதி","அபயருசி"],"ch":"ya:3"},"ya:48":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":48,"t":["இன்றிவண் ஐய என்கண் அருளிய பொருளி தெல்லாம்","நன்றென நயந்து கொண்டேன் நடுக்கமும் அடுத்த தில்லை","என்றனக் கிறைவன் நீயே யெனவிரு கையுங் கூப்பி","இன்றியா னியாது செய்வ தருளுக தெருள என்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"The sister says she has taken to heart what her brother taught, no longer trembles, calls him her lord, folds both hands and asks him to say plainly what she must do now.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["panivu","thelivu","anbu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 48","lb":"YA48","mm":1,"ev":"The sister accepts her brother's teaching and asks him what to do.","cl":[],"ag":["அபயமதி"],"ec":[],"spk":["அபயமதி","அபயருசி"],"ch":"ya:3"},"ya:49":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":49,"t":["ஒன்றிய உடம்பின் வேறாம் உயிரின துருவ முள்ளி","நன்றென நயந்து நங்கள் நல்லறப் பெருமை நாடி","வென்றவர் சரணம் மூழ்கி விடுதுநம் முடல மென்றான்","நன்றிது செய்கை யென்றே நங்கையு நயந்து கொண்டாள்."],"p":"","r":"The brother says to think of the soul as different from the joined body, hold the greatness of their dharma in mind, and give up the body in the refuge of the victors; the sister gladly agrees that it is right.","n":"The proposal comes as a choice to leave the body in a state of refuge, not a flight; the poem does not say how it will be done.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["uyir","thuravu","aram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 49","lb":"YA49","mm":1,"ev":"The brother proposes they take refuge in the Jinas and let go of the body, and the sister agrees.","cl":[["the soul is other than the body it is joined to","jain","asserted",1,"abhayaruchi"],["taking refuge at the victors' feet and leaving the body is the right act for one who sees this","jain","asserted",3,"abhayaruchi"]],"ag":["அபயருசி","அபயமதி"],"ec":[],"spk":["அபயருசி","அபயமதி"],"ch":"ya:3"},"ya:50":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":50,"t":["அறிவொடா லோக முள்ளிட் டனந்தமா மியல்பிற் றாகி","அறிதலுக் கரிய தாகி யருவமா யமல மாகிக்","குறுகிய தடற்றொள் வாள்போற் கொண்டிய லுடம்பின்","வேறாய் இறுகிய வினையு மில்ல தெமதியல் பென்று நின்றார்."],"p":"","r":"The poet says that the two stood in the knowledge that the soul, known by its knowing and seeing, is formless, stainless and hard to grasp, is other than the body, and that tight-bound deeds are not its nature.","n":"Line 3 has the odd குறுகிய தடற்றொள் வாள்போல் and the simile of a sword and its sheath is uncertain; the end of the line is read as stating the body's closeness to the soul.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["uyir","vinai","gnaanam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 50","lb":"YA50","mm":1,"ev":"The brother and sister stand firm in the knowledge of the soul's difference from the body.","cl":[["the soul is formless and stainless, endowed with knowledge and perception, and is other than the body","jain","asserted",2,"narrator"],["karma, though bound fast, is not the soul's own nature","jain","asserted",4,"narrator"]],"ag":["அபயருசி","அபயமதி"],"ec":[],"ch":"ya:3"},"ya:51":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":51,"t":["உறுதியைப் பெரிது மாக்கி யுலகினுக் கிறைமை நல்கிப்","பிறவிசெற் றரிய வீட்டின் பெருமையைத் தருத லாறும்","அறிவினிற் றெரிந்த மாட்சி யரதனத் திரய மென்னும்","பெறுதலக் கரிய செல்வம் பெற்றனம் பெரிது மென்றார்."],"p":"","r":"The two youths, walking to their death, tell themselves that they have won the rare wealth of the three jewels, which makes an end of birth, gives greatness over the world and is known through knowledge.","n":"The verse names the jewels as one word and does not list them; that they are right faith, knowledge and conduct is the Jain tradition, not something these lines say. The poem opens the five verses of meditation (51-56) that the youths hold on the way.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arathanathirayam","veedu","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 51","lb":"YA51","mm":1,"ev":"The two youths, going toward the sacrifice, take stock of what they have gained.","cl":[["the three jewels (அரதனத் திரயம்) give greatness and bring the release that cuts off birth","jain","asserted",3,"ilainjar"]],"ag":["இளைஞர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["இளைஞர்",""],"ch":"ya:4"},"ya:52":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":52,"t":["ஈங்குநம் இடர்கள் தீர்க்கும் இயல்பினார் நினைந்து மேலிவ்","வோங்கிய வுலகத் தும்பர் ஒளிசிகா மணியி னின்றார்","வீங்கிய கருமக் கேட்டின் விரிந்தவெண் குணத்த ராகித்","தீங்கெலா மகற்றி நின்ற சித்தரே செல்லல் தீர்ப்பார்."],"p":"","r":"Calling to mind the liberated ones at the crest-jewel of the world, who stand free of the wide ruin of karma with their eight qualities and have put away every harm, the youths take them as the ones who end their sorrow.","n":"Verses 52-56 read as the five objects of a Jain's reverence (liberated souls, arhats, teachers, preceptors, ascetics); the Tamil names none of the five by that count, and the order here begins with the liberated ones, not the arhats. Siddhar here (சித்தர்) means the liberated, not the Tamil Siddhar poets.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["veedu","vinai","thiyaanam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 52","lb":"YA52","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["the liberated ones, freed from karma and possessed of eight qualities, are the refuge that ends sorrow","jain","asserted",3,"ilainjar"]],"ag":["இளைஞர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["இளைஞர்",""],"ch":"ya:4"},"ya:53":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":53,"t":["பெருமலை யனைய காதிப் பெரும்பகை பெயர்த்துப் பெற்ற","திருமலி கடையி னான்மைத் திருவொடு திளைப்ப ரேனும்","உரிமையி னுயிர்கட் கெல்லாம் ஒருதனி விளக்க மாகித்","திருமொழி யருளுந் தீர்த்த கரர்களே துயர்கள் தீர்ப்பார்."],"p":"","r":"The youths think next of the teaching lords who, having driven out the great enemy of the destroying karmas and gained the fourfold wealth, are yet a single lamp to every soul and end its sorrows with their holy word.","n":"காதி (the destroying karmas) and நான்மைத் திரு (the fourfold wealth) are read from the Jain tradition's terms; the verse gives no count. The number printed before the verse is a misprint for 53.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["theerththagarar","vinai","arul"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 53","lb":"YA53","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["the teaching lords who have driven out the destroying karmas end the sorrows of souls by their word","jain","asserted",4,"ilainjar"]],"ag":["இளைஞர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["இளைஞர்",""],"ch":"ya:4"},"ya:54":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":54,"t":["ஐவகை யொழுக்க மென்னும் அருங்கல மொருங்க ணந்தார்","மெய்வகை விளக்கஞ் சொல்லி நல்லற மிகவ ளிப்பார்","பவ்வியர் தம்மைத் தம்போற் பஞ்சநல் லொழுக்கம் பாரித்","தவ்விய மகற்றுந் தொல்லா சிரியரெம் அல்லல் தீர்ப்பார்."],"p":"","r":"They think of the first teachers, who spread the fivefold conduct, a rare vessel, among the worthy as they follow it themselves, showing the true path and so wiping out the harm that sorrow rests on.","n":"பவ்வியர் (the fit for release) is a Jain term for souls able to reach it; the verse uses it without gloss.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ozhukkam","guru","aram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 54","lb":"YA54","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["teachers who show the true path and teach the fivefold conduct to the fit remove sorrow","jain","asserted",4,"ilainjar"]],"ag":["இளைஞர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["இளைஞர்",""],"ch":"ya:4"},"ya:55":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":55,"t":["அங்கநூ லாதி யாவும் அரில்தபத் தெரிந்து தீமைப்","பங்கமேழ் பங்க மாடிப் பரமனன் னிறிப யின்றிட்","டங்கபூ வாதி மெய்ந்து லமிழ்தகப் படுத்த டைந்த","நங்களுக் களிக்கு நீரார் நம்வினை கழுவு நீரார்."],"p":"","r":"They think of those who learned the Anga and other scriptures free of fault, argued by the seven standpoints, and took the nectar of the true text into themselves; these give to the youths, and are the ones who wash their karma.","n":"பங்கமேழ் பங்கம் (line 2) is read here as the seven-fold method of argument; the line is compressed and the reading could turn on it, so only the main sense is relied on.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalvi","guru","vinai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 55","lb":"YA55","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["those who master the scriptures and teach them wash away the hearers' karma","jain","asserted",4,"ilainjar"]],"ag":["இளைஞர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["இளைஞர்",""],"ch":"ya:4"},"ya:56":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":56,"t":["பேதுறு பிறவி போக்கும் பெருந்திரு உருவுக் கேற்ற","கோதறு குணங்கள் பெய்த கொள்கலம் அனைய ராகிச்","சேதியி னெறியின் வேறு சிறந்தது சிந்தை செய்யாச்","சாதுவ ரன்றி யாரே சரண்நமக் குலகி னாவார்."],"p":"","r":"They end with the ascetics, who are vessels fit for the great form, hold only to good qualities and think of no other path than the Jina's; apart from such holy men, they ask, who is a refuge for them in the world?","n":"சேதியின் நெறியின் வேறு (line 3) can be read as 'other than the Jina's path' or as a reference to a shrine; the ascetics' exclusive loyalty is clear either way.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thuravu","saandror","aram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 56","lb":"YA56","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["only the ascetics of the true path are a refuge for the world","jain","asserted",4,"ilainjar"]],"ag":["இளைஞர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["இளைஞர்",""],"ch":"ya:4"},"ya:57":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":57,"t":["இனையன நினைவின் ஏகும் இளைஞரை விரைவிற் கொண்டு","தனையர சருளும் பெற்றிச் சண்டன்அச் சண்ட மாரி","முனைமுக வாயிற் பீட முன்னருய்த் திடுவான் நிற்பக்","கனைகழ லரசன் ஓகை கைம்மிகக் கழறினானே."],"p":"","r":"Having gone on with such thoughts, the youths are quickly led by Chandan to the altar in front of the gate of the fierce Chandamari, and as they stand the king speaks in his delight.","n":"Line 2 (தனையரசருளும் பெற்றிச் சண்டன்) is compressed; Chandan is taken as the one who brings the youths, a role his place in the scene supports but the line does not state outright.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["narabali","arasu","kolai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 57","lb":"YA57","mm":1,"ev":"Chandan brings the two youths to the altar of Chandamari's gate, and the king, pleased, begins to speak.","cl":[],"ag":["சண்டன்","இளைஞர்","மன்னன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ya:4"},"ya:58":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":58,"t":["முனைத்திற முருக்கு மாற்றல் மூரித்தேந் தாரி னாய்தின்","வினைத்திற நன்றி யாமே விழைநர பலியீ தற்கின்","றினையவர் தம்மைத் தேவியிரும்பலி யிடத்துய்க் கொன்றான்","கனைகழ லரச னையோ கையில்வா ளுருவி னானே."],"p":"","r":"Someone urges the king, crusher of armies, that the deed of offering a man to the goddess is good for him today, and the king leads the two into the goddess's great place of sacrifice and, alas, draws his sword.","n":"Lines 1-2 are an address to the king and seem to be the advice of whoever brought the youths; Chandan is the likeliest, but the verse does not name him, and the sentence runs on into the narrator's lament (ஐயோ). The claim is tagged general because the goddess cult of Chandamari fits none of the listed schools. The work answers it in this chapter: verse 63 gives the cost of killing and 71 says the dharma cuts cruelty down.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["narabali","kolai","kodungol"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 58","lb":"YA58","mm":1,"ev":"The king brings the youths to the goddess's sacrificial ground and draws his sword.","cl":[["a man given in sacrifice to the goddess is a good deed for the king","general","refuted",2,"sandan"]],"ag":["மன்னன்","இளைஞர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ya:4"},"ya:59":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":59,"t":["கொலைக்களங் குறுகி நின்றுங் குலுங்கலர் குணங்கள் தம்மால்","இலக்கண மமைந்த மெய்யர் இருவரு மியைந்து நிற்ப","நிலத்திறை மன்னன் வாழ்க நெடிதென வுரைமி னென்றார்","மலக்கிலா மனத்தர் தம்வாய் வறியதோர் முறுவல் செய்தார்."],"p":"","r":"At the place of killing the two stand unshaken, their bodies marked by their virtues; told to say 'may the king live long', these men of spotless mind answer only with an empty smile.","n":"The command in line 3 is spoken by someone the verse does not name; the smile (வறியதோர் முறுவல்) can mean a bare smile or one with nothing behind it, and verse 63 later says what it hid.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["anjaamai","kolai","aram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 59","lb":"YA59","mm":1,"ev":"At the killing ground the youths are told to wish the king long life, and they smile.","cl":[],"ag":["இளைஞர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ya:4"},"ya:60":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":60,"t":["மறவியின் மயங்கி வையத் துயிர்களை வருத்தஞ் செய்யா","தறவியன் மனத்தை யாகி ஆருயிர்க் கருள்ப ரப்பிச்","சிறையன பிறவி போக்குந் திருவறம் மருவிச் சென்று","நிறைபுக ழுலகங் காத்து நீடுவாழ் கென்று நின்றார்."],"p":"","r":"Not tormenting any living thing in forgetfulness, they say, let the king take the holy dharma that ends the prison of birth, spread mercy to every precious life and so guard the world of full fame and live long.","n":"The blessing is a condition as much as a wish: 'long life' is tied to what the king does, so the youths give the answer to the order in verse 59 without refusing it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arul","kollaamai","aram","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 60","lb":"YA60","mm":1,"ev":"The youths bless the king with the wish that he keep to mercy.","cl":[["a king who harms no life and spreads mercy to all will guard the world and live long","general","asserted",2,"ilainjar"],["the holy dharma ends the confinement of birth","jain","asserted",3,"ilainjar"]],"ag":["இளைஞர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["இளைஞர்","மன்னன்"],"ch":"ya:4"},"ya:61":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":61,"t":["நின்றவர் தம்மை நோக்கி நிலைதளர் நீதி மன்னன்","மின்றிகழ் மேனி யார்கொல் விஞ்சையர் விண்ணு ளார்கொல்","அன்றியிவ் வுருவ மண்மே லவர்களுக் கரிய தென்னா","நின்றவர் நிலைமை தானும் நினைவினுக் கரிய தென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"The king, his firm resolve slackening, asks whether these bodies bright as lightning are vidyadharas or gods, since such a form is rare among men on earth and their steadiness is beyond thought.","n":"நிலைதளர் நீதி மன்னன் shows the first slackening of the king's purpose: wonder, not yet mercy.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","aram","perumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 61","lb":"YA61","mm":1,"ev":"The king, shaken, asks who the two are.","cl":[],"ag":["மன்னன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["மன்னன்","இளைஞர்"],"ch":"ya:4"},"ya:62":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":62,"t":["இடுக்கண்வந் துறவும் எண்ணா தெரிசுடர் விளக்கி னென்கொல்","நடுக்கமொன் றின்றி நம்பால் நகுபொருள் கூறு கென்ன","அடுக்குவ தடுக்கு மாயின் அஞ்சுதற் பயனின் றென்றே","நடுக்கம தின்றி நின்றாம் நல்லறத் தெளிவு சென்றாம்,"],"p":"","r":"Asked why they laugh without trembling when danger has come, they answer that if what must come will come, fear has no use, and so they stood without a tremor, clear in the good dharma.","n":"Verse 63 then gives the cause of what 'must come'; here the ground of the calm is only that fear changes nothing.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["anjaamai","thelivu","aram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 62","lb":"YA62","mm":1,"ev":"The king asks why they laugh; the youth says fear has no use against what is due.","cl":[["fear is of no use against what must come","general","asserted",3,"kumaran"]],"ag":["மன்னன்","குமரன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ya:4"},"ya:63":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":63,"t":["முன்னுயி ருருவிற் கேதம் முயன்றுசெய் பாவந் தன்னால்","இன்னபல் பிறவி தோறும் இடும்பைகள் தொடர்ந்த வந்தோம்","மன்னுயிர்க் கொலையி னாலிம் மன்னன்வாழ் கென்னு மாற்றம்","என்னதாய் விளையு மென்றே நக்கனம் எம்மு ளென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"The youth says that through harm they once did to the bodies of living beings they have met sufferings in birth after birth, and they laughed within themselves wondering what could come of a wish for long life for a king who kills creatures.","n":"The deed is the doers' own harm to living bodies, and no god or fate is named as sending it; the youths count themselves among those it fell on.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","piravi","kollaamai","idumbai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 63","lb":"YA63","mm":1,"ev":"The youth explains why they laughed.","cl":[["sin done by harming the bodies of living beings in earlier lives brings sufferings through birth after birth","jain","asserted",2,"kumaran"],["a wish of long life for a king who kills creatures can bear no good fruit","general","asserted",4,"kumaran"]],"ag":["குமரன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["குமரன்","மன்னன்"],"ch":"ya:4"},"ya:64":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":64,"t":["கண்ணினுக் கினிய மேனிக் காளைதன் கமல வாயிற்","பண்ணினுக் கினிய சொல்லைப் படியவர் முடியக் கேட்டே","அண்ணலுக் கழகி தாண்மை யழகினுக் கமைந்த தேனும்","பெண்ணினுக் கரசி யாண்மை பேசுதற் கரிய தென்றார்."],"p":"","r":"The people present, hearing the youth's sweet speech to its end, say that a man's courage is fitting and beautiful, but that this woman's courage, like a queen's, is hard to put into words.","n":"The speakers of line 3-4 are taken as the people the king's court holds; பெண்ணினுக்கு அரசி can also be read as a name or title for the girl, and the unit does not fix it.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["aanmai","perumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 64","lb":"YA64","mm":1,"ev":"The onlookers marvel at the pair, and at the girl above all.","cl":[],"ag":["படியவர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["படியவர்",""],"ch":"ya:4"},"ya:65":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":65,"t":["மன்னனும் அதனைக் கேட்டே மனமகிழ்ந் தினிய னாகி","என்னைநும் பிறவி முன்னர் இறந்தன பிறந்து நின்ற","மன்னிய குலனு மென்னை வளரிளம் பருவந் தன்னில்","என்னைநீ ரினைய ராகி வந்ததும் இயம்பு கென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"Pleased by what he heard, the king asks them to tell the births that went before, the family in which they were born, and why they came to him as they are.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["piravi","kudi","kelvi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 65","lb":"YA65","mm":1,"ev":"The king asks to hear of their births and their lineage.","cl":[],"ag":["மன்னன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["மன்னன்","குமரன்"],"ch":"ya:4"},"ya:66":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":66,"t":["அருளுடை மனத்த ராகி யறம்பரிந் தவர்கட் கல்லால்","மருளுடை மறவ ருக்கெம் வாய்மொழி மனத்திற் சென்று","பொருளியல் பாகி நில்லா புரவல கருதிற் றுண்டேல்","அருளியல் செய்து செல்க ஆகுவ தாக என்றான்."],"p":"","r":"The youth says that his words do not enter as meaning in deluded, cruel men but only in those of merciful mind who love dharma, and bids the king, if he truly wants them, to do an act of mercy first.","n":"அருளியல் செய்து செல்க (line 4) is read as 'act with mercy, then go on'; it may also mean 'grant mercy and go forward', either of which is the king's turn from the sword.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arul","aram","kelvi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 66","lb":"YA66","mm":1,"ev":"The youth makes mercy a condition of the telling.","cl":[["teaching takes hold only in those of merciful mind who love dharma, not in the deluded and cruel","general","asserted",1,"kumaran"]],"ag":["குமரன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["குமரன்","மன்னன்"],"ch":"ya:4"},"ya:67":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":67,"t":["அன்னணம் அண்ணல் கூற அருளுடை மனத்த னாகி","மன்னவன் தன்கை வாளும் மனத்தியை மறனும் மாற்றி","இன்னினி யிறைவன் நீயே யெனக்கென இறைஞ்சி நின்று","பன்னுக குமர நுங்கள் பவத்தொடு பரிவு மென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"At this the king, softened, puts aside the sword in his hand and the cruelty in his mind, bows, calls the youth his lord from now, and asks him to tell of his births along with his compassion.","n":"மனத்தியை மறனும் (line 2) is read மனத்து இயை மறனும், the cruelty joined to his mind.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arul","panivu","kolai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 67","lb":"YA67","mm":1,"ev":"The king lays down the sword, bows, and asks to hear their account.","cl":[],"ag":["மன்னன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["மன்னன்","குமரன்"],"ch":"ya:4"},"ya:68":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":68,"t":["மின்னொடு தொடர்ந்து மேகம் மேதினிக் கேதம் நீங்க","பொன்வரை முன்னர் நின்று புயல்பொழிந் திடுவ தேபோல்","அன்னமென் னடைய னாளின் அருகணைத் துருகும் வண்ணம்","மன்னவ குமரன் மன்னற் கறமழை பொழிய லுற்றான்."],"p":"","r":"Like a cloud with lightning that comes before the golden mountain and pours rain to end the earth's affliction, the prince begins to rain the dharma on the king.","n":"Line 3 is a compressed simile of the listener melting beside a swan-gaited woman and is not relied on.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aram","mazhai","arul"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 68","lb":"YA68","mm":1,"ev":"The youth begins to preach dharma to the king.","cl":[],"ag":["குமரன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ya:4"},"ya:69":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":69,"t":["அரைசநின் னகத்து மாட்சி யதுபெரி தழகி தாயிற்","றுரைசெய்தா லுறுதி யாய துணர்ந்துகொண் டுய்தி போலும்","விரைசெய்தார் வரைசெய் மார்ப வினவிய பொருளி தெல்லாம்","நிரைசெய்தே புகல்வன் யான்நீ நினைவொடு கேளி தென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"The youth tells the king that his inner worth is great and fair, and says he will answer in order all that the king asked, if the king listens with a steady mind.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kelvi","thelivu","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 69","lb":"YA69","mm":1,"ev":"The youth promises to answer the king's questions in order.","cl":[],"ag":["குமரன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["குமரன்","மன்னன்"],"ch":"ya:4"},"ya:70":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":70,"t":["எவ்வள விதனைக் கேட்பா ரிருவினை கழுவுநீரார்","அவ்வள வவருக் கூற்றுச் செறித்துட னுதிர்ப்பை யாக்கும்","மெய்வகை தெரிந்து மாற்றை வெருவினர் வீட்டை யெய்துஞ்","செவ்விய ராகச் செய்து சிறப்பினை நிறுத்தும் வேந்தே."],"p":"","r":"As far as they hear this, he says, it checks the influx of karma and makes it shed, and, for those who have learned the true kinds and shrink from the other way, it makes them ripe for release, O king.","n":"The words ஊற்றுச் செறித்து உதிர்ப்பை read as the Jain pair of stopping the inflow of karma and shedding it; the line is compressed and the subject of the verbs is carried over from the preceding words, so the sense is probable, not fixed.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vinai","veedu","kelvi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 70","lb":"YA70","mm":1,"ev":"The youth tells the king what hearing the dharma does for a hearer.","cl":[["hearing the dharma checks the influx of karma, brings its shedding and ripens the hearer for release","jain","asserted",2,"kumaran"]],"ag":["குமரன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["குமரன்","மன்னன்"],"ch":"ya:4"},"ya:71":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":71,"t":["மலமலி குரம்பை யின்கண் மனத்தெழு விகற்பை மாற்றும்","புலமலி போகத் தின்கண் ணாசையைப் பொன்று விக்கும்","கொலைமலி கொடுமை தன்னைக் குறைத்திடு மனத்திற் கோலச்","சிலைமலி நுதலி னார்தங் காதலில் தீமை செய்யும்."],"p":"","r":"The dharma, he goes on, turns aside the notions that rise in the mind in the body's hut full of filth, destroys desire in the pleasures of the senses, cuts down the cruelty of killing, and does harm to the love of women with bow-like brows.","n":"Line 4 can be read as the dharma harming the love of women (curing the hearer) or as that love doing harm; the first sits better with the three lines before it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","kaamam","kollaamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 71","lb":"YA71","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["the body is a hut full of filth","jain","asserted",1,"kumaran"],["the dharma destroys desire for sense-pleasure and the cruelty of killing","jain","asserted",3,"kumaran"]],"ag":["குமரன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["குமரன்","மன்னன்"],"ch":"ya:4"},"ya:72":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":72,"t":["பிறந்தவர் முயற்சி யாலே பெறுபய ளடைவ ரல்லால்","இறந்தனர் பிறந்த தில்லை யிருவினை தானு மில்லென்","றறைந்தன ரறிவில் லாமை யதுவிடுத் தறநெ றிக்கண்","சிறந்தன முயலப் பண்ணும் செப்புமிப் பொருண்மை யென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"Some have said that those born gain fruit by their own effort alone, that the dead are not born again, and that there are no two deeds; the youth calls this lack of knowledge and tells the king to leave it and strive on the dharma's road.","n":"The denial is reported as a summary in the youth's mouth, which the materialists themselves might not word so. It is dismissed as ignorance here and answered by the account of his own births that verse 73 promises.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","piravi","samayam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 72","lb":"YA72","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["the born gain fruit by their own effort only, the dead are not reborn and there are no two deeds","lokayata","refuted",2,"kumaran"]],"ag":["குமரன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["குமரன்","மன்னன்"],"ch":"ya:4"},"ya:73":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":73,"t":["அறப்பொருள் விளைக்குங் காட்சி யருந்தவ ரருளிற் றன்றிப்","பிறப்புணர்ந் ததனின் யாமே பெயர்த்துணர்ந் திடவும் பட்ட","திறப்பவு மதன்கண் தேற்றம் இனிதுவைத் திடுமி னென்றான்","உறப்பணித் தெவரு முள்ளத் துவந்தனர் கேட்க லுற்றார்."],"p":"","r":"The youth says that the sight that yields dharma's wealth did not come only through the great ascetics' grace but also from knowing his own births and understanding them anew, and asks the king to put his clear faith in it; all gladly begin to listen.","n":"அருளிற்றன்றி (line 1) may mean 'not only through' or 'not through' the ascetics' grace; the more guarded sense is used.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["piravi","thelivu","kelvi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 73","lb":"YA73","mm":1,"ev":"All present are bidden to hear and begin to listen gladly.","cl":[["the right sight that gives rise to dharma can come from knowing one's own earlier births, not from the ascetics' teaching alone","jain","asserted",2,"kumaran"]],"ag":["குமரன்","படியவர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["குமரன்","மன்னன்"],"ch":"ya:4"},"ya:74":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":74,"t":["வளவயல் வாரியின் மலிந்த பல்பதி","அளவறு சனபத மவந்தி யாமதின்","விளைபய னமரரும் விரும்பு நீர்மைய","துளதொரு நகரதுஞ் சயினி யென்பவே."],"p":"","r":"The poet places the story in Ujjayini, a city in the country of Avanti, which has rich fields and many towns and whose harvest even the gods desire.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naadu","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 74","lb":"YA74","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"ya:5"},"ya:75":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":75,"t":["கந்தடு களிமத யானை மன்னவன்","இந்திர னெனுந்திற லசோக னென்றுளன்","சந்திர மதியென்னும் மடந்தை தன்னுடன்","அந்தமி லுவகைய னமர்ந்து வைகுநாள்."],"p":"","r":"A strong elephant-riding king named Asokan lives in unbroken joy with his queen Chandramati.","n":"The line calls him Indra-like in power (இந்திரனெனும் திறல்); the epic's later kings are measured against this picture of a content, untroubled house.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","vendhan","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 75","lb":"YA75","mm":1,"ev":"King Asokan lives happily with his queen Chandramati.","cl":[],"ag":["அசோகன்","சந்திர மதி"],"ec":[],"ch":"ya:5"},"ya:76":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":76,"t":["இந்துவோ ரிளம்பிறை பயந்த தென்னவே","சந்திர மதியொரு தனயன் தந்தனள்","எந்துயர் களைபவ னிசோ தரன்னென","நந்திய புகழவன் நாம மோதினான்."],"p":"","r":"Chandramati bears a son, like the young moon, and the famed father gives him the name Yasodharan, he who removes sorrow.","n":"The name is spelt இசோதரன் here and யசோதரன் in verse 83; the poet glosses it as the one who removes my sorrow.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["makkal","pugazh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 76","lb":"YA76","mm":1,"ev":"Chandramati bears a son and the father names him Yasodharan.","cl":[],"ag":["சந்திர மதி","அசோகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ya:5"},"ya:77":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":77,"t":["இளங்களிற் றுழுவையி னேத மின்றியே","வளங்கெழு குமரனும் வளர்ந்து மன்னனாய்","விளங்கிழை யமிழ்தமுன் மதியை வேள்வியான்","உளங்கொளப் புணர்ந்துட னுவகை யெய்தினான்."],"p":"","r":"The son grows up unharmed like a young elephant or lion cub, is spoken of as king, and weds Amritamati by the marriage rite, to his joy.","n":"மன்னனாய் ('becoming king') comes six verses before the crowning in verse 83, so it can only mean that he is of royal standing here; the verse does not make Asokan step down yet.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vaazhkkaithunai","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 77","lb":"YA77","mm":1,"ev":"The grown prince Yasodharan marries Amritamati by the wedding rite.","cl":[],"ag":["யசோதரன்","அமிழ்தமுன் மதி"],"ec":[],"ch":"ya:5"},"ya:78":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":78,"t":["இளையவள் எழில்நலம் ஏந்து கொங்கையின்","விளைபய னிசோதரன் விழைந்து செல்லுநாள்","கிளையவ ருவகையிற் கெழும ஈன்றனள்","வளையவ ளிசோமதி மைந்தன் தன்னையே."],"p":"","r":"As Yasodharan goes in desire to the young woman for the fruit of her beauty and breasts, the bangled wife bears a son called Yasomati, to the joy of the kin.","n":"The young woman is not named here; she is read as Amritamati from verse 77.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["makkal","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 78","lb":"YA78","mm":1,"ev":"Amritamati bears Yasodharan a son, Yasomati.","cl":[],"ag":["யசோதரன்","அமிழ்தமுன் மதி"],"ec":[],"ch":"ya:5"},"ya:79":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":79,"t":["மற்றொர்நாள் மன்னவன் மகிழ்ந்து கண்ணடி","பற்றுவா னடிதொழப் படிவ நோக்குவான்","ஒற்றைவார் குழன்மயி ருச்சி வெண்மையை","உற்றுறா வகையதை யுளைந்து கண்டனன்."],"p":"","r":"One day the king, looking at his own form in the mirror held before him, sees with alarm a single white hair on the crown of his head.","n":"The sign of age comes by sight, in a mirror held up by an attendant, and not by any teacher's word; it is the whole occasion of his turning in the next verses.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","ilamai","yaakkai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 79","lb":"YA79","mm":1,"ev":"Asokan, looking in a mirror, finds a white hair on his crown and is distressed.","cl":[],"ag":["அசோகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ya:5"},"ya:80":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":80,"t":["வண்டளிர் புரைதிரு மேனி மாதரார்","கண்டக லுறவரு கழிய மூப்பிது","உண்டெனி லுளைந்திக லுருவ வில்லிதன்","வண்டுள கணைபயன் மனிதர்க் கென்றனன்."],"p":"","r":"The king says that this old age has come so that women with bodies like young shoots will draw away at the sight, and then even the bee-strung bow of the bodiless one is of no use to men.","n":"The reasoning is entirely about desire: age is what he fears for its effect on pleasure; he does not yet speak of death or rebirth.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","kaamam","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 80","lb":"YA80","mm":1,"ev":"Asokan speaks of his old age and the uselessness of Kama's arrows to the old.","cl":[["old age takes away the body's beauty and so the use of Kama's arrows","general","asserted",3,"asokan"]],"ag":["அசோகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["அசோகன்",""],"ch":"ya:5"},"ya:81":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":81,"t":["இளமையி னியல்பிது வாகு மென்னினிவ்","வளமையி லிளமையை மனத்து வைப்பதென்","கிளைமையு மனையதே கெழுமு நம்முளத்","தளைமையை விடுவதே தகுவ தாமினி."],"p":"","r":"The king says that youth is of this nature, so why set the mind on it in prosperity; kin are the same, and the right thing now is to leave the bond that grips the mind.","n":"Speech continues from verse 80; the speaker is known from the story, with no new name here. He is speaking to himself.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","thuravu","ilamai","pattru"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 81","lb":"YA81","mm":1,"ev":"Asokan reasons his way to leaving his bonds.","cl":[["youth and kinship are fleeting and the mind should not rest on them","general","asserted",1,"asokan"],["the fitting course is to let go of the bonds of the mind","general","asserted",4,"asokan"]],"ag":["அசோகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["அசோகன்",""],"ch":"ya:5"},"ya:82":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":82,"t":["முந்துசெய் நல்வினை முளைப்ப வித்தலைச்","சிந்தைசெய் பொருளொடு செல்வ மெய்தினாம்","முந்தையின் மும்மடி முயலிற் புண்ணியம்","இந்திர வுலகமும் மெய்த லாமெனா"],"p":"","r":"The king reasons that the good deeds of former births have now sprouted into wealth and thought, and that if one strives three times as much in merit one may win even Indra's world.","n":"Renunciation is argued here by the profit of merit, a heaven to be won, not by release; the verse stops without a closing stop and runs into verse 83.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vinai","muyarchi","marumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 82","lb":"YA82","mm":1,"ev":"Asokan reasons that his past good deeds bore this wealth and that more merit would earn a heaven.","cl":[["good deeds of a former birth sprout as present wealth","general","asserted",1,"asokan"],["greater merit now wins the world of Indra","general","asserted",3,"asokan"]],"ag":["அசோகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["அசோகன்",""],"ch":"ya:5"},"ya:83":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":83,"t":["இனையன நினைவுறீஇ யசோத ரன்எனுந்","தனயனை நிலமகள் தலைவ னாகெனக்","கனமணி வனைமுடி கவித்துக் காவலன்","புனைவளை மதிமதி புலம்பப் போயினான்."],"p":"","r":"Having thought thus, the king crowns his son Yasodharan lord of the land and goes, while his queen Chandramati laments.","n":"The cost of the departure is set beside it: the queen's lament. The verse does not say whether she goes with him or stays.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thuravu","arasu","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 83","lb":"YA83","mm":1,"ev":"Asokan crowns Yasodharan and leaves, and Chandramati weeps.","cl":[],"ag":["அசோகன்","சந்திர மதி"],"ec":[],"ch":"ya:5"},"ya:84":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":84,"t":["குரைகழ லசோகன்மெய்க் குணத ரற்பணிந்","தரைசர்க ளைம்பதிற் றிருவர் தம்முடன்","உரைசெய அருந்தவத் துறவு கொண்டுபோய்","வரையுடை வனமது மருவி னானரோ."],"p":"","r":"Asokan bows to the sage Gunadhara, takes the vow of austere renunciation together with fifty-two kings, and goes to the forested mountain.","n":"A king's renunciation is shown as a public act taken before a teacher and in company, not as a flight alone.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thuravu","thavam","guru"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 84","lb":"YA84","mm":1,"ev":"Asokan takes ascetic vows from Gunadhara with fifty-two kings and enters the forest hills.","cl":[],"ag":["அசோகன்","குணதரர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ya:5"},"ya:85":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":85,"t":["எரிமணி இமைக்கும் பூணா னிசோதர னிருநி லத்தாட்","கொருமணித் திலகம் போலு முஞ்சயி னிக்கு நாதன்","அருமணி முடிகொள் சென்னி யரசடிப் படுத்து யர்ந்த","குருமணிக் குடையின் நீழற் குவலயங் காவல் கொண்டான்."],"p":"","r":"Yasodharan, in jewels, lord of Ujjayini, a jewel on his region of the earth, takes up the guard of the world under the gem-set umbrella.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","vendhan","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 85","lb":"YA85","mm":1,"ev":"Yasodharan takes up the rule of Ujjayini.","cl":[],"ag":["யசோதரன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ya:5"},"ya:86":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":86,"t":["திருத்தகு குமரன் செல்வச் செருக்கினால் நெருக்குப் பட்டே","மருத்தெறி கடலிற் பொங்கி மறுகிய மனத்த னாகி","உருத்தெழு சினத்திற் சென்ற வுள்ளமெய்ம் மொழியோ டொன்றி","அருத்திசெய் தருத்த காமத் தறத்திற மறத்து றந்தான்."],"p":"","r":"The prince, pressed hard by the pride of wealth, becomes a heart tossed like a sea in a gale, and in an anger that overflows he turns his words and thought to desire for gain and pleasure and forgets the way of virtue.","n":"After his father's reasoning in 81 the son does the opposite at once; the poet names the cause as the pride of wealth.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["selvam","aram","kaamam","vekuli"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 86","lb":"YA86","mm":1,"ev":"Pride of wealth drives Yasodharan to forget dharma for wealth and pleasure.","cl":[["pride in wealth makes a ruler forget the way of virtue","general","asserted",1,"narrator"]],"ag":["யசோதரன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ya:5"},"ya:87":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":87,"t":["அஞ்சுத லிலாது தெவ்வ ரவியமே லடர்த்துச் சென்று","வஞ்சனை பலவு நாடி வகுப்பன வகுத்து மன்னன்","புஞ்சிய பொருளு நாடும் புணர்திறம் புணர்ந்து நெஞ்சில்","துஞ்சுத லிலாத கண்ணன் துணிவன துணிந்து நின்றான்."],"p":"","r":"Fearless, the king marches on his foes, plans many deceits, lays up wealth and land, and stays a sleepless watcher who carries out what he has decided.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["padai","pagai","soozhchi","vanjam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 87","lb":"YA87","mm":1,"ev":"Yasodharan campaigns, schemes and gathers wealth with unsleeping care.","cl":[],"ag":["யசோதரன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ya:5"},"ya:88":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":88,"t":["தோடலார் கோதை மாதர் துயரியிற் றொடுத்த வின்பப்","பாடலொ டியைந்த பண்ணின் இசைச்சுவை பருகிப் பல்கால்","ஊடலங் கினிய மின்னின் ஒல்கிய மகளி ராடும்","நாடகம் நயந்து கண்டும் நாள்சில செல்லச் சென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"He enjoys the sweetness of songs set to strings by women with flower garlands and watches the dance of the women who bend like lightning in the play of lovers' sulking, and some days pass.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 88","lb":"YA88","mm":1,"ev":"Yasodharan spends days in song and dance among women.","cl":[],"ag":["யசோதரன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ya:5"},"ya:89":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":89,"t":["மற்றொர்நாள் மன்னர் தம்மை மனைபுக விடுத்து மாலைக்","கொற்றவே லவன்றன் கோயிற் குளிர்மணிக் கூட மொன்றிற்","சுற்றுவார் திரையிற் றூமங் கமழ்துயிற் சேக்கை துன்னிக்","கற்றைவார் கவரி வீசக் களிசிறந் தினிது ருந்தான்."],"p":"","r":"One day, sending the kings to their homes, he goes to a cool jewelled upper room of his palace, curtained all round, with a fragrant couch, and rests happily as chowries are fanned.","n":"The word at line 4 is split (ருந்தான் for இருந்தான்).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 89","lb":"YA89","mm":1,"ev":"Yasodharan sends the kings home and lies in the palace upper room.","cl":[],"ag":["யசோதரன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ya:5"},"ya:90":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":90,"t":["சிலம்பொடு சிலம்பித் தேனுந் திருமணி வண்டும் பாடக்","கலம்பல வணிந்த வல்குற் கலையொலி கலவி யார்ப்ப","நலங்கலந் தினிய காமர் நறுமலர்த் தொடைய லேபோல்","அலங்கலங் குழல்பின் தாழ அமிழ்தமுன் மதிய ணைந்தாள்."],"p":"","r":"Amritamati comes to the bed with anklets and bees singing, jewels on her hips ringing, and her garland and hair hanging down.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 90","lb":"YA90","mm":1,"ev":"Amritamati joins the king in the bed-room.","cl":[],"ag":["அமிழ்தமுன் மதி"],"ec":[],"ch":"ya:5"},"ya:91":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":91,"t":["ஆங்கவ ளணைந்த போழ்தி னைங்கணைக் குரிசில் தந்த","பூங்கணை மாரி வெள்ளம் பொருதுவந் தலைப்பப் புல்லி","நீங்கல ரொருவ ருள்புக் கிருவரு மொருவ ராகித்","தேங்கம ழமளி தேம்பச் செறிந்தனர் திளைத்து விள்ளார்."],"p":"","r":"As she joins him, the flood of arrows of Kama overwhelms them, they embrace and do not part, and the two become one on the scented bed.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","punarchi","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 91","lb":"YA91","mm":1,"ev":"The king and Amritamati make love.","cl":[],"ag":["யசோதரன்","அமிழ்தமுன் மதி"],"ec":[],"ch":"ya:5"},"ya:92":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":92,"t":["மடங்கனிந் தினிய நல்லாள் வனமுலைப் போக மெல்லாம்","அடங்கல னயர்ந்த தேன்வா யமிர்தமும் பருகி யம்பொற்","படங்கலந் தகன்ற வல்குற் பாம்பணை புணைய தாக","இடங்கழிந் தொழிவி லின்பக் கடலிடை மூழ்கி னானே."],"p":"","r":"The king drowns in a sea of unbounded pleasure, drinking the honey of her lips and enjoying her breasts, her hips a serpent's hood for his bed.","n":"இடங்கழிந்து ('beyond bounds') marks the pleasure as excess in the poet's own word, in the same scene that lists its delights.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 92","lb":"YA92","mm":1,"ev":"Yasodharan is lost in pleasure with Amritamati.","cl":[],"ag":["யசோதரன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ya:5"},"ya:93":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":93,"t":["இன்னரிச் சிலம்புந் தேனும் எழில்வளை நிரையு மார்ப்பப்","பொன்னவிர் தாரோ டாரம் புணர்முலை பொருது பொங்க","மன்னனும் மடந்தை தானும் மதனகோ பத்தின் மாறாய்த்","தொன்னலந் தொலைய உண்டார் துயில்கொண்ட விழிக ளன்றே."],"p":"","r":"The king and the lady, in the anger of desire reversed, drain one another of their old beauty, and their eyes close in sleep.","n":"","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","punarchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 93","lb":"YA93","mm":1,"ev":"The king and Amritamati fall asleep after love.","cl":[],"ag":["யசோதரன்","அமிழ்தமுன் மதி"],"ec":[],"ch":"ya:5"},"ya:94":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":94,"t":["ஆயிடை யத்தி கூடத் தயலெழுந் தமிர்த மூறச்","சேயிடைச் சென்றோர் கீதஞ் செவிபுக விடுத்த லோடும்","வேயிடைத் தோளி மெல்ல விழித்தனள் வியந்து நோக்காத்","தீயிடை மெழுகி னைந்த சிந்தைய ளுருகி னாளே."],"p":"","r":"From beside the elephant stable a song rises and enters her ear from afar; Amritamati wakes slowly, wonders, and melts like wax in fire.","n":"The singer is not named in this verse; the song itself acts on her, so the verse sets the sound against the guarded room.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","manam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 94","lb":"YA94","mm":1,"ev":"A song from the elephant stable wakes Amritamati, who melts at it.","cl":[],"ag":["அமிழ்தமுன் மதி"],"ec":[],"ch":"ya:5"},"ya:95":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":95,"t":["பண்ணினுக் கொழுகு நெஞ்சிற் பாவையிப் பண்கொள் செவ்வாய்","அண்ணலுக் கமிர்த மாய வரிவையர்க் குரிய போகம்","விண்ணினுக் குளதென் றெண்ணி வெய்துயிர்த் துய்தல் செல்லாள்","மண்ணினுக் கரசன் தேவி மதிமயக் குற்றி ருந்தாள்."],"p":"","r":"Her heart flows to the song, and she thinks that the joy fit for women, nectar to that lord, is in the sky for her; sighing, she cannot go on, and the king's queen stays bewildered.","n":"The verse reports her thought and does not call it a fault; the phrase மதிமயக்கு ('bewilderment of mind') is the poet's word for it.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","manam","karpu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 95","lb":"YA95","mm":1,"ev":"Amritamati, stirred by the song, sits in a bewildered state, sighing.","cl":[],"ag":["அமிழ்தமுன் மதி"],"ec":[],"ch":"ya:5"},"ya:96":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":96,"t":["மின்னினு நிலையின் றுள்ளம் விழைவுறின் விழைந்த யாவும்","துன்னிடு மினத்தின் தூய்மை சூழ்ச்சியு மொழிய நிற்கும்","பின்னுறு பழியிற் கஞ்சா பெண்ணுயிர்ப் பெருமை பேணா","தென்னுமிம் மொழிகட் கந்தோ விலக்கிய மாயி னாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet remarks that a desiring mind is less steady than lightning, that the purity of good company and counsel fall away, and that the woman fears no later blame and holds her own worth cheap.","n":"The last line is hard to parse; it seems to close the poet's comment on the queen's fall by saying she became one who set these warnings aside, but the grammar could also give it as the words of someone else.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","manam","nirai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 96","lb":"YA96","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["a mind that wants something is less steady than lightning, and company and counsel cannot hold it","general","asserted",1,"narrator"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"ya:6"},"ya:97":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":97,"t":["துன்னிய இரவு நீங்கத் துணைமுலை தமிய ளாகி","இன்னிசை யவனை நெஞ்சத் திருத்தின ளிருந்த எல்லைத்","துன்னினள் தோழி துன்னித் துணைவரில் தமிய ரேபோன்","றென்னிது நினைந்த துள்ளத் திறைவிநீ யருளு கென்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"The night over, the queen sits alone, holding the sweet singer in her heart; her friend comes close and, finding her lost in thought as if her husband were away, asks her to say what she is thinking.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","ninaivu","iravu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 97","lb":"YA97","mm":1,"ev":"The queen sits through the dawn thinking of the singer; her friend finds her so and asks what troubles her.","cl":[],"ag":["அமிழ்தமுன் மதி","குணவதி"],"ec":[],"spk":["குணவதி","அமிழ்தமுன் மதி"],"ch":"ya:6"},"ya:98":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":98,"t":["தவளவாள் நகையாய் தண்டார் மன்னவன் தகைமை யென்னுங்","கவளமா ரகத்தென் னுள்ளக் கருங்களி மதநல் லியானை","பவளவாய் மணிக்கை கொண்ட பண்ணியல் தோட்டி பற்றித்","துவளுமா றொருவன் எல்லித் துடக்கினான் றுயர என்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"The queen tells her friend that her mind, like a dark rutting elephant that lived on the king's dignity, has been seized by the goad of a night singer's tune and made to waver in distress.","n":"The image makes the queen's mind a elephant fed on the king's dignity and mastered by a goad that is his song, so her fall is told as capture rather than choice.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","manam","kaamanoy"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 98","lb":"YA98","mm":1,"ev":"The queen confesses to her friend that a man's night singing has overturned her mind.","cl":[],"ag":["அமிழ்தமுன் மதி"],"ec":[],"spk":["அமிழ்தமுன் மதி","குணவதி"],"ch":"ya:6"},"ya:99":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":99,"t":["அங்கவ ளகத்துச் செய்கை யறிந்தன னல்ல ளேபோல்","கொங்கவிழ் குழலி மற்றக் குணவதி யுறழ்ந்து கூறும்","நங்கைநின் பெருமை நன்றே நனவெனக் கனவிற் கண்ட","பங்கம துள்ளி யுள்ளம் பரிவுகொண் டினைய லென்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"Gunavati, making as if she did not know what is in the queen's heart, answers back and tells her that her greatness is real, and that she should not grieve over a fault seen in a dream.","n":"The friend's feigned ignorance (அறிந்தனல்லளேபோல்) is the poet's report of her pretence, so her answer here is a deflection, not yet an argument.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kanavu","pazhi","manam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 99","lb":"YA99","mm":1,"ev":"Gunavati answers the queen's confession by treating her trouble as a dream-fault and asking her not to grieve.","cl":[],"ag":["குணவதி"],"ec":[],"spk":["குணவதி","அமிழ்தமுன் மதி"],"ch":"ya:6"},"ya:100":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":100,"t":["என்மனத் திவரு மிந்நோய் இவளறிந் திலள்கொ லென்றே","தன்மனத் தினைய வட்குத் தானுரைத் திடுத லோடும்","நின்மனத் திலாத சொல்லை நீபுனைந் தருளிற் றென்கொல்","சின்மலர்க் குழலி யென்றே செவிபுதைத் தினிது சொன்னாள்."],"p":"","r":"The queen thinks her friend has not understood her sickness and says so openly; Gunavati rebukes her for putting into words what is not in her real mind and gently stops her ears.","n":"The friend's first response is refusal, set against her later service as go-between from verse 110.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamanoy","kaamam","nirai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 100","lb":"YA100","mm":1,"ev":"The queen declares her desire plainly; Gunavati covers her ears and rebukes her for saying it.","cl":[],"ag":["அமிழ்தமுன் மதி","குணவதி"],"ec":[],"spk":["குணவதி","அமிழ்தமுன் மதி"],"ch":"ya:6"},"ya:101":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":101,"t":["மாளவப் பஞ்ச வப்பண் மகிழ்ந்தவ னமுத வாயிற்","கேளல மாயி னாமுங் கேளல மாது மாவி","நாளவ மாகி யின்னே நடந்திடு நடுவொன் றில்லை","வாளள வுண்கண் மாதே மறுத்துரை மொழியி னென்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"The queen says that if they refuse to hear the song of the man with the nectar mouth, her life will end that very day, and asks the friend to give an answer.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","uyir","kaamanoy"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 101","lb":"YA101","mm":1,"ev":"The queen tells her friend that she will die if she is denied the singer.","cl":[],"ag":["அமிழ்தமுன் மதி"],"ec":[],"spk":["அமிழ்தமுன் மதி","குணவதி"],"ch":"ya:6"},"ya:102":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":102,"t":["என்னுயிர்க் கரணம் நின்னோ டின்னிசை புணர்த்த காளை","தன்னின்மற் றொருவ ரில்லைத் தக்கது துணிக தாழ்க்கின்","என்னுயிர்க் கேத மெய்தி னிதுபழி பெருகு மென்றாள்","துன்னும்வா யவளோ டெண்ணாத் தோழியு மெண்ணி ணாளே."],"p":"","r":"The queen says that the man who tied her life to his sweet music has no equal, tells her friend to decide quickly, and warns that delay would hurt her and bring blame; the friend, who had not thought with her, now thinks the matter through.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","uyir","pazhi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 102","lb":"YA102","mm":1,"ev":"The queen urges her friend to decide quickly; the friend begins to weigh it.","cl":[],"ag":["அமிழ்தமுன் மதி","குணவதி"],"ec":[],"spk":["அமிழ்தமுன் மதி","குணவதி"],"ch":"ya:6"},"ya:103":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":103,"t":["மழுகிரு ளிரவின் வைகி மாளவப் பஞ்ச வத்தேன்","ஒழுகிய மிடற்றோர் காளை யுள்ளவ னியாவ னென்றே","கழுதுரு வவனை நாடிக் கண்டனள் கண்டு காமத்","தொழுகிய வுள்ளத் தையற் கொழியுமென் றுவந்து மீண்டாள்."],"p":"","r":"At night the friend goes in the dark to find who the man with the flowing voice is, finds him with a ghoul-like form, and returns glad that the queen's passion will end.","n":"The friend's relief rests on the idea that his ugliness will cool the queen's desire; the next verses show the idea carried to the queen as a remedy, and it fails.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","iravu","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 103","lb":"YA103","mm":1,"ev":"Gunavati goes by night, finds the singer, sees his frightful form and goes back relieved.","cl":[],"ag":["குணவதி"],"ec":[],"ch":"ya:6"},"ya:104":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":104,"t":["மன்னன்மா தேவி நின்னை வருத்துவான் வகுத்த கீதத்","தன்னவ னத்தி பாக னட்டமா பங்க னென்பான்","தன்னைமெய் தெரியக் கண்டே தளர்ந்துகண் புதைத்து மீண்டேன்","என்னைநீ முனிவை யென்னா விசைக்கில னவற்கீ தென்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the queen that the singer of the song that tormented her is the elephant-keeper Ashtavakran, that she shut her eyes at the sight of his body and came away, and asks not to be blamed since she could not give him the queen's message.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","azhagu","soozhchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 104","lb":"YA104","mm":1,"ev":"Gunavati reports to the queen that the singer is the elephant-keeper Ashtavakran and that she turned back in revulsion.","cl":[],"ag":["குணவதி"],"ec":[],"spk":["குணவதி","அமிழ்தமுன் மதி"],"ch":"ya:6"},"ya:105":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":105,"t":["நரம்புகள் விசித்த மெய்யன் நடையினிற் கழுத ணைந்தோன்","திரங்கிய விரலன் கையன் சிறுமுகன் சினவன் சீறிற்","குரங்கினை யனையன் கூனன் குழிந்துபுக் கழிந்த கண்ணன்","நெருங்கலு நிரலு மின்றி நிமிர்ந்துள சிலபல் வென்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"The friend describes him: a body tied with sinews, a sunk neck, withered fingers, a small angry face like a monkey's, a hunched back, caved-in eyes and a few crooked teeth.","n":"The list is the friend's argument by loathing; it is not the poet's own account of him.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","yaakkai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 105","lb":"YA105","mm":1,"ev":"Gunavati lists his deformities to the queen.","cl":[],"ag":["குணவதி"],"ec":[],"spk":["குணவதி","அமிழ்தமுன் மதி"],"ch":"ya:6"},"ya:106":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":106,"t":["பூதிகந் தத்தின் மெய்யிற் புண்களுங் கண்கள் கொள்ளா","சாதியுந் தக்க தன்றா லவன்வயிற் றளரு முள்ளம்","நீதவிர்ந் திடுக நெஞ்சின் நிறையினைச் சிறைசெய் கென்றாள்","கோதவிதழ்ந் திட்ட வுள்ளக் குணவதி கொம்ப னாளே."],"p":"","r":"The friend adds that his body is foul with sores, his eyes are not what they should be and his caste is unfit, and she asks the queen to let her sinking heart go and to shut up her self-restraint.","n":"The queen's answer in verses 107 and 108 sets the argument aside rather than answering it on its own terms, so the claims are the friend's.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nirai","kaamam","kudi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 106","lb":"YA106","mm":1,"ev":"Gunavati pleads with the queen to give up the keeper and keep her self-restraint.","cl":[["a man of low birth and a sore-ridden body is not a fit object of a queen's desire","general","reported",2,"gunavati"],["a woman should confine her heart's restraint and let her desire go","general","reported",3,"gunavati"]],"ag":["குணவதி"],"ec":[],"spk":["குணவதி","அமிழ்தமுன் மதி"],"ch":"ya:6"},"ya:107":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":107,"t":["என்றலு மிவற்றி னாலென் னிறைவளை யவன்க ணார்வம்","சென்றது சிறந்து முன்னே திருவொடு திறலுந் தேசும்","ஒன்றிய அழகுங் கல்வி யொளியமை குலத்தோ டெல்லாம்","நின்றுசெய் பயனு நல்லார் நெஞ்சமும் பெறுத லன்றோ."],"p":"","r":"The queen answers that because of these very things her desire for him grew, and she lists wealth, valour, splendour, beauty, learning and family as gains made in the past, together with the hearts of the good.","n":"The syntax of the whole verse turns on a phrase of the fruit that stands (நின்று செய் பயன்) that the verse leaves bare; I read it as a past-deed claim spoken to turn the friend's reasons about birth and body.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","vinai","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 107","lb":"YA107","mm":1,"ev":"The queen answers her friend by listing the goods that are the fruit of earlier deeds.","cl":[["wealth, power, beauty, learning and birth are the fruit of what one stood and did before","general","reported",4,"amirthamathi"]],"ag":["அமிழ்தமுன் மதி"],"ec":[],"spk":["அமிழ்தமுன் மதி","குணவதி"],"ch":"ya:6"},"ya:108":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":108,"t":["காரியம் முடிந்த பின்னுங் காரண முடிவு காணல்","காரிய மன்றி தென்றே கருதிடு கடவுட் காமன்","யாருழை யருளைச் செய்யு மவனமக் கைய னாக.","நேரிழை நினைந்து போகி நீடலை முடியி தென்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"The queen says that once the thing has come about it is no business to search out its cause, that the god of love bestows grace on anyone, and that the friend should go quickly and finish the matter.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","iraivan","vinai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 108","lb":"YA108","mm":1,"ev":"The queen tells her friend that her love needs no justification and sends her to fetch the keeper.","cl":[["once an effect has come about, hunting for its cause is not a task; the god of love gives his grace to whom he will","general","reported",2,"amirthamathi"]],"ag":["அமிழ்தமுன் மதி"],"ec":[],"spk":["அமிழ்தமுன் மதி","குணவதி"],"ch":"ya:6"},"ya:109":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":109,"t":["தேவிநீ கமலை யாவாய் திருவுளத் தருளப் பட்டான்","ஆவிசெல் கின்ற வெந்நோ யருநவை ஞமலி யாகும்","பூவின்வார் கணைய னென்னே புணர்த்தவா றிதனை யென்னா","நாவினா லுளைந்து கூறி நடுங்குபு வொடுங்கி நின்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"The friend, shaken, says that the queen is as a Lakshmi, that the man has been taken into her gracious mind, and that the life-taking disease will become a hound of fault; she cries out at how the flower-arrowed god had joined this and stands trembling.","n":"The middle two lines are hard; the word for a dog (ஞமலி) is read as a figure for the shame the disease will bring.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","kaamanoy","acham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 109","lb":"YA109","mm":1,"ev":"Gunavati, trembling, wonders at how the god of love has brought the queen to this.","cl":[],"ag":["குணவதி"],"ec":[],"spk":["குணவதி","அமிழ்தமுன் மதி"],"ch":"ya:6"},"ya:110":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":110,"t":["ஆடவ ரன்றி மேலா ரருவருத் தணங்க னாருங்","கூடலர் துறந்தார் நோன்மைக் குணம்புரிந் துயர்தற் காகப்","பீடுடை யயனார் தந்த பெருமக ளிவளென் றெண்ணித்","தோடலர் குழலி தோழி துணிந்தனள் பெயர்த்துச் சென்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"After thinking it through, the friend reasons that she is a great woman given by the creator and turns away to go, resolved.","n":"The first three lines name men and the learned and renouncing; the sense of how they bear on her reasoning is unclear, and only the friend's decision to go is plain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","thoothu","oozh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 110","lb":"YA110","mm":1,"ev":"Gunavati weighs the matter and decides to go to the keeper.","cl":[],"ag":["குணவதி"],"ec":[],"ch":"ya:6"},"ya:111":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":111,"t":["முடைப்படு நாற்ற மேனி முழுதழுக் காடை போர்த்துக்","கடைப்படு துகளு மண்ணுங் கஞலிய கூடத் தாங்கோர்","புடைக்கிடந் துறங்கு வானைப் புழுங்கிய மனத்தோ டண்மி","விடைப்பருந் தானை வேந்தன் றேவியின் விழைவு சொன்னாள்."],"p":"","r":"She goes to a corner hall where he lies asleep, his body stinking, wrapped in filthy cloth and covered with dust, and in a troubled mind tells him the king's queen's desire.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thoothu","kaamam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 111","lb":"YA111","mm":1,"ev":"Gunavati finds the keeper asleep in a filthy hall and tells him of the queen's desire.","cl":[],"ag":["குணவதி"],"ec":[],"ch":"ya:6"},"ya:112":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":112,"t":["கேட்டலு மஞ்சு நெஞ்சன் கேடெனக் கெய்திற் றென்றே","வாட்டமும் நடுக்கு முற்றே மாண்பில மொழிந்தாய் மன்னன்","வீட்டிடுஞ் செல்கென் றேவ வேய்புரை தோளி தோழி","காட்டுவ காட்டி யன்னான் கருத்தையுங் கலைத்திட் டாளே."],"p":"","r":"Hearing it, he is filled with fear, says ruin has come upon him and that the king will kill him, rebukes her for speaking unworthily and bids her go; she shows him what she can and breaks down his resolve.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["acham","thoothu","kolai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 112","lb":"YA112","mm":1,"ev":"Ashtavakran, frightened, refuses and tells her to go; she overcomes his fear.","cl":[["a man who touches the king's queen will be put to death by the king","general","reported",3,"ashtavakran"]],"ag":["அட்டமா பங்கன்","குணவதி"],"ec":[],"spk":["அட்டமா பங்கன்","குணவதி"],"ch":"ya:6"},"ya:113":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":113,"t":["பண்பெற்ற மொழியாய் யானும் பண்பெற்ற பயனும் பெற்றேன்","புண்பெற்ற மெய்யன் பொல்லாப் புழுதியிற் றுளையுங் கையன்","மண்பெற்ற இறைவன் தேவி மனம்பெற்று மகிழ்வே னென்னின்","எண்பெற்ற தவமியார் பெற்றா ரியான்பெற்ற பேறி தென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"He says that he has had the fruit of merit, that a man with a sore body and dust-rolling hands who wins the heart of the king's queen has gained more than any famed ascetic, and that this is his boon.","n":"The boast puts a woman's sin into the language of karmic reward; it is the keeper's voice, and the poem does not endorse it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thavam","vinai","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 113","lb":"YA113","mm":1,"ev":"Ashtavakran, won over, calls the queen's love the best fruit of his past merit.","cl":[["winning the queen's heart is the fruit of earlier merit, greater than the fruit of renowned austerity","general","reported",4,"ashtavakran"]],"ag":["அட்டமா பங்கன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["அட்டமா பங்கன்","குணவதி"],"ch":"ya:6"},"ya:114":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":114,"t":["இவ்வகை மொழிவோன் றன்னை யிறையவன் தேவி மேவும்","செவ்வியுங் குறியுஞ் செப்பிச் சென்றவ ளெய்த லோடும்","கவ்விய காமத் தீயாற் கயங்கிய மாலை யொப்பாள்","நவ்விநேர் விழியாய் நன்றோ நவில்கநின் கரும மென்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"After telling him the queen's time and sign she goes back; the queen, burning like evening in the fire of desire, asks her to say how her errand went.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thoothu","kaamam","kaamanoy"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 114","lb":"YA114","mm":1,"ev":"Gunavati returns from the keeper and the queen asks for her news.","cl":[],"ag":["குணவதி","அமிழ்தமுன் மதி"],"ec":[],"spk":["அமிழ்தமுன் மதி","குணவதி"],"ch":"ya:6"},"ya:115":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":115,"t":["பட்டது நலங்கா ணென்பாள் பாகனைக் கண்ட வாறும","அட்டமா பங்கன் சீறி யழன்றிட்ட வாறுந் தேவி","இட்டத்திற் கவனை யாங்கே யியைவித்த வாறுங் கூடற்","கொட்டிய குறியுங் சொன்னா ளொள்ளிழை யுவப்புற் றாளே."],"p":"","r":"The friend says all has gone well, and tells how she met the keeper, how he flared up in anger, how she brought him round, and the sign for the meeting; the queen is delighted.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thoothu","kaamam","kurippu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 115","lb":"YA115","mm":1,"ev":"Gunavati reports the keeper's anger, his yielding and the signal for the tryst.","cl":[],"ag":["குணவதி","அட்டமா பங்கன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["குணவதி","அமிழ்தமுன் மதி"],"ch":"ya:6"},"ya:116":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":116,"t":["தனிவயி னிகுளை யானை தரப்படு சார னோடு","கனிபுரை கிளவி காமங் கலந்தனள் கனிந்து சென்றாள்","முனிவினை மன்னன் றன்மேன் முறுகின ளொழுகு முன்போல்","இனியவ ளல்ல ளென்கொ லெனமனத் தெண்ணி னானே."],"p":"","r":"The queen goes to join the man the friend has brought, her words sweet with desire, and turns her ill-will on the king and no longer treats him with her old sweetness, which the king notices and wonders at.","n":"The order of the lines suggests the meeting and the king's puzzlement as one passage, but the grammar of the first two lines is not clear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","karpu","vanjam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 116","lb":"YA116","mm":1,"ev":"The queen meets the keeper through her friend, while the king notices that she has turned cold to him.","cl":[],"ag":["அமிழ்தமுன் மதி","குணவதி","யசோதரன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ya:6"},"ya:117":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":117,"t":["அரசவை விடுத்து மெய்யா லறுசின னொப்ப மன்னன்","உரையல னமளி தன்மே லுறங்குதல் புரிந்த போழ்தின்","விரைகமழ் குழலி மேவி மெய்த்துயி லென்று காமத்","துறையினள் பெயர்ந்து தோழி குறியிடந் துன்னி னாளே."],"p":"","r":"Leaving the court, the king lies on his bed in a rage that he does not voice, as if asleep; the queen, taking his sleep for real, leaves with her friend for the tryst place.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","vanjam","kurippu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 117","lb":"YA117","mm":1,"ev":"The king lies feigning sleep on his bed, and the queen goes off with Gunavati to the meeting place.","cl":[],"ag":["யசோதரன்","அமிழ்தமுன் மதி","குணவதி"],"ec":[],"ch":"ya:6"},"ya:118":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":118,"t":["துயிலினை யொருவி மன்னன் சுடர்க்கதிர் வாள்கை யேந்தி","மயலுழை வழிச்செல் கின்ற வாளரி யேறு போலக்","கயல்விழி யவள்தன் பின்னே கரந்தன னொதுங்கி யாங்கண்","செயலினை யறிது மென்று செறிந்தனன் மறைந்து நின்றான்."],"p":"","r":"The king rises from sleep, takes up his bright sword and, like a lion pacing in madness, steals after the fish-eyed queen, hiding so as to learn what she will do.","n":"The queen is not named in the verse; she is 'she of the fish-like eyes'. The sword in his hand is what verse 123 turns back.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","vanjam","manam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 118","lb":"YA118","mm":1,"ev":"The king leaves his bed with sword in hand and secretly follows the queen to see her conduct.","cl":[],"ag":["மன்னன்","தேவி"],"ec":[],"ch":"ya:7"},"ya:119":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":119,"t":["கடையனக் கமலப் பாவை கருங்குழல் பற்றிக் கையால்","இடைநிலஞ் செல்ல ஈர்த்திட் டிருகையி னாலு மோச்சிப்","புடைபல புடைத்துத் தாழ்ந்த பொருளிது புகல்க என்னாத்","துடியிடை துவள வீழ்த்து நிலத்திடைத் துகைத்திட் டானே."],"p":"","r":"The low man seizes the lotus-like woman by her dark hair, drags her over the ground, strikes her with both hands and throws her down, demanding that she say what kept her late.","n":"The man is called கடையன், 'the lowest', and is not named; that he is her lover is read from the sequel in verses 120 to 122. The king's own contempt for such a man comes at verse 124.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kodungol","kayamai","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 119","lb":"YA119","mm":1,"ev":"The low man beats and tramples the queen, demanding to know why she was delayed.","cl":[],"ag":["கடையன்","தேவி"],"ec":[],"ch":"ya:7"},"ya:120":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":120,"t":["இருளினா லடர்க்கப் பட்ட வெழின்மதிக் கடவுள் போல","வெருளியான் மிதிப்புண் டைதே விம்மிய மிடற்ற ளாகித்","தெருள்கலா ளுரையு மாடாள் சிறிதுபோ தசையக் கண்ட","மருளியான் மயங்கி மாதர் மலரடி சென்னி வைத்தான்."],"p":"","r":"The woman lies choked and silent like the moon swallowed by darkness; when he sees her stir only a little, the bewildered man in his confusion puts her flower-soft feet on his head.","n":"The same man who struck her now worships her feet: the verse shows the rule of desire, in which the beater is the servant.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","kayamai","maayai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 120","lb":"YA120","mm":1,"ev":"The man, seeing her faint after his blows, grovels and sets her feet on his head.","cl":[],"ag":["கடையன்","தேவி"],"ec":[],"ch":"ya:7"},"ya:121":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":121,"t":["தையலாள் மெல்லத் தேறிச் சாரனை மகிழ்ந்து நோக்கி","வெய்யநீ முனிவு செல்லன் மேதினிக் கிறைவன் றன்னோ","டையவா சனத்தி னும்ப ரரசவை யிருந்து கண்டாய்","வெய்யபா வங்கள் செய்தேன் விளம்பலன் விளைந்த தென்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"Recovering slowly, the woman looks gladly at the man and tells him not to be angry: she sat beside the king on the throne in the royal hall, she says, and speaks of cruel sins and a ripening she will not name.","n":"Lines 3 and 4 are hard: 'cruel sins' (வெய்ய பாவங்கள்) may refer to what she did with the king or to her own wrong, and 'what ripened' is left unsaid. The reading is provisional.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","vanjam","vinai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 121","lb":"YA121","mm":1,"ev":"The queen pacifies her lover and excuses her lateness by her place beside the king.","cl":[],"ag":["தேவி"],"ec":[],"spk":["தேவி","கடையன்"],"ch":"ya:7"},"ya:122":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":122,"t":["பொற்பகங் கழுமி யாவும் புரந்தினி தரந்தை தீர்க்குங்","கற்பகங் கரந்து கண்டார் கையகன் றிடுத லுண்டோ","எற்பகங் கொண்ட காத லெனக்கினி நின்னின் வேறோர்","சொற்பகர்ந் தருளு காளை துணைவரு மிவண முண்டோ"],"p":"","r":"She tells him that a wish-granting tree, once seen, is not let go, that her whole love is for him, and asks whether any other companion could stand with him here.","n":"The oath of love is one a wife should keep for her husband; the poem puts it in the mouth of an unfaithful queen, to be heard by the king (verse 123). The image of the wish-granting tree is hers for the low man, and is not the poet's praise of him.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","karpu","vanjam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 122","lb":"YA122","mm":1,"ev":"The queen declares her love to the man while the king overhears.","cl":[],"ag":["தேவி"],"ec":[],"spk":["தேவி","கடையன்"],"ch":"ya:7"},"ya:123":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":123,"t":["என்றலு மேய மன்னன் எரியெழ விழித்துச் சீறிக்","கொன்றிவர் தம்மை வாள்வாய்க் கூற்றுண விடுவ லென்றே","ஒன்றின னுணர்ந்த துள்ளத் துணர்ந்தது கரத்து வாளும்","சென்றிடை விலக்கி நின்றோர் தெளிந்துணர் வெழுந்த தன்றே."],"p":"","r":"Hearing her, the king's eyes blaze and he resolves to send both to the sword's death; but understanding rises in him and, with the sword in his hand, he stands between his anger and the deed.","n":"The verse says the knowledge came to him as discernment, not as fear or lack of power: the sword is in his hand when he holds back.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vekuli","thelivu","kollaamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 123","lb":"YA123","mm":1,"ev":"The king, enraged, resolves to kill them, then checks himself on reflection.","cl":[],"ag":["மன்னன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ya:7"},"ya:124":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":124,"t":["மாதரா ரெனைய ரேனும் வதையினுக் குரிய ரல்லர்","பேதைதா னிவனும் பெண்ணி னனையனே பிறிது மொன்றுண்","டேதிலார் மன்னர் சென்னி யிடுதலுக் குரிய வாளின்","தீதுசெய் சிறுபுன் சாதி சிதைத்தலுந் திறமன் றென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"The king reasons that women, whoever they are, are not fit to be killed, that this fool is like a woman, and that destroying a small, mean birth with the sword made for the heads of kings is not a proper thing.","n":"His two reasons are rank and sex, not mercy: a woman is spared as a woman and the man because he is beneath the sword. The epic gives the king's reason as his own and does not say it is the Jain rule of non-killing; that appears only in verse 139. The unnamed 'low birth' (சிறுபுன் சாதி) is the king's contempt.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kollaamai","arasu","vekuli"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 124","lb":"YA124","mm":1,"ev":"The king decides to spare the woman and her lover.","cl":[["a woman is not to be put to death, whatever her fault","general","asserted",1,"king"],["a king's sword is too high for a small and base person","general","reported",3,"king"]],"ag":["மன்னன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["மன்னன்",""],"ch":"ya:7"},"ya:125":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":125,"t":["இனையன பலவுஞ் சிந்தித் திழிப்பொடு பழித்து நெஞ்சிற்","புனைவளை யவர்கள் போகம் புறக்கணித் திட்டு மீண்டே","கனவரை யனைய மார்பன் கடிகம ழமளி யேறித்","தனிமுனி களிறு போலத் தானினை வெய்து கின்றான்."],"p":"","r":"Thinking over all this with scorn, the king puts the pleasures of the women aside, goes back and lies on his scented bed, brooding like a solitary elephant.","n":"Turning from the pleasures of the women (போகம் புறக்கணித்திட்டு) is the first step of his renunciation, named in verse 132.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","manam","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 125","lb":"YA125","mm":1,"ev":"The king returns to his bed, gives up the pleasures of the women's quarters and broods.","cl":[],"ag":["மன்னன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ya:7"},"ya:126":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":126,"t":["எண்ணம தலாமை பண்ணு மிற்பிறப் பிடிய நூறும்","மண்ணியல் புகழை மாய்த்து வருபழி வளர்க்கு மானத்","திண்மையை யுடைக்கு மாண்மை திருவொடு சிதைக்குஞ் சிந்தை","கண்ணொடு கலக்கு மற்றிக் கடைப்படு காம மென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"He thinks that desire is the lowest thing: it ruins the mind, destroys the hundred fruits of a high birth, kills fame on earth, feeds blame, breaks the firmness of honour and the strength of manhood, and fades the sight.","n":"The king blames desire and not the woman; it is the same turn he makes in verse 128. His list of what desire destroys ends with the eyes.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","kedu","maanam","pazhi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 126","lb":"YA126","mm":1,"ev":"The king concludes that desire is the root of his ruin.","cl":[["desire destroys thought, fame, honour and manhood and brings blame","general","asserted",4,"king"]],"ag":["மன்னன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["மன்னன்",""],"ch":"ya:7"},"ya:127":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":127,"t":["உருவினொ டழகு தானு மொளியமை குலனும் பேசின்","திருமக ளனைய மாத ரிவளையுஞ் சிதையச் சீரிற்","கருமலி கிருமி யன்ன கடைமகற் கடிமை செய்யுந்","துருமதி மதனன் செய்கை துறப்பதே சிறப்ப தென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"Even if one speaks of form and beauty and a family of light, he thinks, a woman like Lakshmi herself may be ruined and serve a base man like a worm in filth; to leave off the work of the god of love is best.","n":"Madana (the god of love) is called the one without sense; leaving desire is stated as an excellence (சிறப்பு), and not yet as release.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","azhagu","kudi","thuravu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 127","lb":"YA127","mm":1,"ev":"The king resolves that giving up desire is the best course.","cl":[["beauty and high birth are no safeguard against desire's ruin","general","asserted",1,"king"],["to leave the doings of desire is the best course","general","asserted",4,"king"]],"ag":["மன்னன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["மன்னன்",""],"ch":"ya:7"},"ya:128":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":128,"t":["மண்ணியல் மடந்தை தானு மருவினர்க் குரிய ளல்லள்","புண்ணிய முடைய நீரார் புணர்ந்திடப் புணர்ந்து நீங்கும்","பெண்ணிய லனைய தன்றோ பெயர்கள் மற்றிவர்கள் யாமும்","கண்ணிய விவர்கள் தம்மைக் கடப்பதே கரும மென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"The earth-woman, he says, does not belong to those who hold her, and women by nature come together with the meritorious and part again; the right deed is to go beyond them.","n":"The generalisation about women is the king's, made in grief; the poem puts it in his mind and does not say it is its own doctrine. The word for 'deed' (கருமம்) is the duty he takes up in 132.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","nilaiyaamai","thuravu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 128","lb":"YA128","mm":1,"ev":"The king concludes that passing beyond women and desire is his duty.","cl":[["a woman's nature is to join whoever merit brings and to leave again","general","reported",2,"king"],["it is right to pass beyond them","general","asserted",4,"king"]],"ag":["மன்னன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["மன்னன்",""],"ch":"ya:7"},"ya:129":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":129,"t":["மற்றைநாள் மன்னன் முன்போன் மறைபுலப் படாமை யின்பச்","சுற்றமா யவர்கள் சூழத் துணிவிட னிருந்த வெல்லை","மற்றுமா மன்னன் றேவி வருமுறை மரபின் வந்தே","கற்றைவார் குழலி மெல்லக் காவலன் பாலி ருந்தாள்."],"p":"","r":"Next day the king, as before, sits in his place of counsel, hiding what he knows, surrounded by kin who are pleasure to him; the queen comes in due order and sits quietly beside him.","n":"He keeps the secret (மறை புலப்படாமை) so that his own conduct is unchanged; the contrast with the night before is the point of the scene.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","avai","vanjam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 129","lb":"YA129","mm":1,"ev":"The king keeps his secret and sits in court; the queen takes her usual place by him.","cl":[],"ag":["மன்னன்","தேவி"],"ec":[],"ch":"ya:7"},"ya:130":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":130,"t":["நகைவிளை யாடன் மேவி நரபதி விரலி னின்றே","மிகைவிளை கின்ற நீல மலரினின் வீச லோடும்","புகைகமழ் குழலி சோர்ந்து பொய்யினான் மெய்யை வீழ்த்தித்","திகைகமழ் நீரிற் றேற்ற மெல்லியல் தேறி னாளே."],"p":"","r":"As they play, the king flicks a blue lotus from his finger at her and she, with feigned swoon, lets her body drop; she is revived by scented water and comes round.","n":"'With falsehood' (பொய்யினால்) marks her swoon as put on; the king's play is a test or a jest, and he lets the pretence run, as verse 131 shows.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vanjam","karpu","kayamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 130","lb":"YA130","mm":1,"ev":"The king flicks a flower at the queen and she pretends to swoon.","cl":[],"ag":["மன்னன்","தேவி"],"ec":[],"ch":"ya:7"},"ya:131":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":131,"t":["புரைபுரை தோறு நீர்சோர் பொள்ளலிவ் வுருவிற் றாய","இருநிற மலரினாலின் றிவளுயி ரேக லுற்ற","தரிதினில் வந்த தென்றென் ! றவளுட னசதி யாடி","விரகினில் விடுத்து மன்னன் வெய்துயிர்த் தனனி ருந்தான்."],"p":"","r":"The king, as though in jest, says that the flower, full of holes through which water drips, has almost taken her life, and sends her away with cunning, sighing hard to himself.","n":"His words are a jest in her own pretence: he mocks the fainting and keeps his knowledge from her.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vanjam","manam","kedu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 131","lb":"YA131","mm":1,"ev":"The king mocks her fainting with a jest and dismisses her, hiding his grief.","cl":[],"ag":["மன்னன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["மன்னன்","தேவி"],"ch":"ya:7"},"ya:132":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":132,"t":["ஆயிடை யரச னுள்ளத் தரசினை விடுப்ப வெண்ணித்","தாயமர் கோயி லெய்திச் சந்திர மதிதன் முன்னர்ச்","சேயிடை யிறைஞ்ச மற்றித் திரைசெய்நீ ருலக மெல்லாம்","நீயுயர் குடையின் வைகி நெடிதுடன் வாழ்க வென்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"Intending to give up the kingdom, the king goes to his mother's shrine and bows before Chandramathi; she wishes that he live long beneath his great umbrella as lord of the sea-ringed world.","n":"The mother's blessing is ironic beside the king's purpose: she wishes the opposite of what he has come to say.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thuravu","arasu","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 132","lb":"YA132","mm":1,"ev":"The king, resolved to leave his throne, goes to his mother and bows; she blesses him with long rule.","cl":[],"ag":["மன்னன்","சந்திர மதி"],"ec":[],"spk":["சந்திர மதி","மன்னன்"],"ch":"ya:7"},"ya:133":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":133,"t":["மணிமரு ளுருவம் வாடி வதனபங் கயமும் மாறா","அணிமுடி யரச ரேறே யழகழிந் துளதி தென்கொல்","பிணியெனை யெனது நெஞ்சிற் பெருநவை யுறுக்கு மையா","துணியலென் உணரச் சொல்லாய் தோன்றனீ யென்று சொன்னாள்."],"p":"","r":"Seeing his gem-bright body faded and his face drooping, she asks why, king of kings, his beauty has gone, whether illness gives her heart great pain, and tells him not to hide it but say.","n":"Her fear is of illness; the cause she will hear is a dream, not a body ailment.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","manam","arul"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 133","lb":"YA133","mm":1,"ev":"The mother asks her son why his beauty is gone.","cl":[],"ag":["சந்திர மதி"],"ec":[],"spk":["சந்திர மதி","மன்னன்"],"ch":"ya:7"},"ya:134":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":134,"t":["விண்ணிடை விளங்குங் காந்தி மிகுகதிர் மதியந் தீர்ந்தே","மண்ணிடை மழுங்கச் சென்றோர் மறையிருட் பகுதி சேரக்","கண்ணிடை யிறைவி கங்குற் கனவினிற் கண்ட துண்டஃது","எண்ணுடை யுள்ளந் தன்னு ளீர்ந்திடு கின்ற தென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"The king answers that what the queen saw in the night in a dream, like the bright moon in the sky dimmed and gone into a shadowed darkness, is what eats at his mind.","n":"The verse says the queen saw it (இறைவி ... கனவினிற் கண்டது), and the king's own mind is troubled by it. Whether the dream is the king's or the queen's, in a tale that began with her unfaithfulness, is not settled here.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kanavu","manam","kedu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 134","lb":"YA134","mm":1,"ev":"The king tells his mother of a dream in the night.","cl":[],"ag":["மன்னன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["மன்னன்","சந்திர மதி"],"ch":"ya:7"},"ya:135":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":135,"t":["கரவினிற் றேவி தீமை கட்டுரைத் திட்ட தென்னா","இரவினிற் கனவு தீமைக் கேதுவென் றஞ்சல் மைந்த","பரவிநிற் கிறைவி தேவி பணிந்தனை சிறப்புச் செய்தால்","விரவிமிக் கிடுத லின்றி விளியுமத் தீமை யெல்லாம்,"],"p":"","r":"The mother tells him not to fear the night's dream as a cause of evil: if he bows to the goddess and honours her, the evil will be dispelled without growing.","n":"This is the mother's view of the dream as an omen. The poem does not answer it directly; it answers the offering that she goes on to name (136 to 139).","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kanavu","vazhipaadu","kedu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 135","lb":"YA135","mm":1,"ev":"The mother tells the king that worship of the goddess will remove the dream's evil.","cl":[["worship of the goddess dissolves the evil that a bad dream foretells","general","reported",3,"chandramathi"]],"ag":["சந்திர மதி"],"ec":[],"spk":["சந்திர மதி","மன்னன்"],"ch":"ya:7"},"ya:136":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":136,"t":["ஐப்பசி மதிய முன்ன ரட்டமி பக்கப் பின்னர்","மைப்பட லின்றி நின்ற மங்கலக் கிழமை தன்னிற்","கைப்பலி கொடுத்துத் தேவி கழலடி பணியிற் காளாய்","மெய்ப்பலி கொண்டு நெஞ்சின் விரும்பின ளுவக்கு மென்றாள்"],"p":"","r":"She names the day, the Tuesday in the bright part of Aippasi after the eighth, and says that if he offers a blood-offering at the goddess's feet, she will accept the living offering and be pleased.","n":"The claim is answered in verse 139 and in the king's shock at 138 (see those verses). The sacrificial goddess is not tied to any named school here.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["bali","vazhipaadu","kolai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 136","lb":"YA136","mm":1,"ev":"The mother names the day and the offering that will please the goddess.","cl":[["a life offered to the goddess pleases her and averts evil","general","refuted",4,"chandramathi"]],"ag":["சந்திர மதி"],"ec":[],"spk":["சந்திர மதி","மன்னன்"],"ch":"ya:7"},"ya:137":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":137,"t":["மண்டமர் தொலைத்த வேலோய் மனத்திது மதித்து நீயே","கொண்டுநின் கொற்ற வாளிற் குறுமறி யொன்று கொன்றே","சண்டிகை மனந்த ளிர்ப்பத் தகுபலி கொடுப்பத் தையல்","கண்டநின் கனவின் திட்பந் தடுத்தனள் காக்கு மென்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"She tells him to cut down a young kid with his victorious sword and give a proper offering to please Chandikai, so the goddess will keep off what his dream showed.","n":"The king's reply at 138-139 refuses the killing, which is the poem's answer; the goddess is named only here.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["bali","kolai","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 137","lb":"YA137","mm":1,"ev":"The mother tells the king to kill a kid for Chandikai.","cl":[["killing a kid before Chandikai wards off the dream's evil","general","refuted",3,"chandramathi"]],"ag":["சந்திர மதி","சண்டிகை"],"ec":[],"spk":["சந்திர மதி","மன்னன்"],"ch":"ya:7"},"ya:138":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":138,"t":["ஆங்கவ ளருளொன் றின்றி யவண்மொழிந் திடுத லோடுந்","தேங்கல னரசன் செங்கை செவிமுதல் செறியச் சேர்த்தி","ஈங்கருள் செய்ததென்கொல் இதுபுதி தென்று நெஞ்சில்","தாங்கல னுருகித் தாய்முன் தகுவன சாற்று கின்றான்."],"p":"","r":"Without the goddess's grace anywhere in these words, the king, as soon as she speaks, puts his hands to his ears, asks what grace this is, a strange thing, and speaks gently and tenderly to his mother.","n":"The verse says there is no grace (அருளொன்றின்றி) in what she said; that judgment is the narrator's, and it prepares the king's refusal.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arul","kollaamai","manam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 138","lb":"YA138","mm":1,"ev":"The king covers his ears at his mother's command and begins his reply.","cl":[],"ag":["மன்னன்","சந்திர மதி"],"ec":[],"ch":"ya:7"},"ya:139":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":139,"t":["என்னுயிர் நீத்த வேனு மியானுயிர்க் குறுதி சூழா","தென்னுயிர்க் கரண யாடி யானுயிர்க் கிறுதி செய்யின்","என்னையிவ் வுலகு காவ லெனக்கினி யிறைவி கூறாய்","மன்னுயிர்க் கரண மண்மேல் மன்னவ ரல்ல ரோதான்."],"p":"","r":"The king says that even if his own life were lost he would not plan the good of one life by the end of another; to put an end to a life as a shield for his own, he asks, how can the world be protected, and are kings not the shield of all living creatures?","n":"The king's answer both rejects the offering and gives the reason in the king's own terms (the shield of lives), before anything of doctrine. The sentence is hard to parse in lines 2-3; the reading is provisional.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kollaamai","arasu","arul"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 139","lb":"YA139","mm":1,"ev":"The king refuses to kill an animal to save himself.","cl":[["a king is the guard of living creatures and may not take one life to guard his own","general","asserted",4,"king"],["one must not harm a life to protect one's own","general","asserted",1,"king"]],"ag":["மன்னன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["மன்னன்","சந்திர மதி"],"ch":"ya:7"},"ya:140":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":140,"t":["யானுயிர் வாழ்த லெண்ணி யெளியவர் தம்மைக் கொல்லின்","வானுய ரின்ப மேலால் வருநெறி திரியு மன்றி","ஊனுயி ரின்ப மெண்ணி யெண்ணமற் றொன்று மின்றி","மானுயர் வாழ்வு மண்ணின் மரித்திடு மியல்பிற் றன்றே."],"p":"","r":"The king tells his mother that to kill the weak so that he may live would turn him from the road to heaven, and that a human life ends in death on the earth in any case.","n":"The verse carries on the refusal begun at 138-139 and adds its reasons: the cost in the next world, and the shortness of this one.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kollaamai","marumai","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 140","lb":"YA140","mm":1,"ev":"The king gives his mother his reason for refusing to kill a creature to save himself.","cl":[["killing the weak to preserve one's own life turns the killer from the way to heaven","general","asserted",2,"king"],["a human life ends in death on earth whatever is done to keep it","general","asserted",4,"king"]],"ag":["மன்னன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["மன்னன்","சந்திர மதி"],"ch":"ya:8"},"ya:141":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":141,"t":["அன்றியு மின்றென் முன்ன ரன்னைநின் குலத்து ளோர்கள்","கொன்றுயிர் கன்று முள்ளக் கொடுமைசெய் தொழில ரல்லர்;","இன்றுயிர் கொன்ற பாவத் திடர்பல விளையு மேலால்","நன்றியொன் றன்று கண்டாய் நமக்குநீ யருளிற் றெல்லாம்."],"p":"","r":"Addressing his mother, the king says her family never did cruel killing, and that if the sin of killing a life brings many sorrows today, all she has urged is no kindness to him.","n":"He turns his mother's care against her counsel: what she calls help would be harm to him.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","kollaamai","kudi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 141","lb":"YA141","mm":1,"ev":"The king appeals to his family's past and to the sorrow that killing breeds.","cl":[["the sin of killing a life ripens in many sorrows","general","asserted",3,"king"]],"ag":["மன்னன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["மன்னன்","சந்திர மதி"],"ch":"ya:8"},"ya:142":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":142,"t":["என்றலு மெனது சொல்லை யிறந்தனை கொடியை யென்றே","சென்றனள் முனிவுச் சிந்தைத் தீருவிலி பிறிது கூறும்","கொன்றுயிர் களைத லஞ்சிற் கோழியை மாவிற் செய்து","சென்றனை பலிகொ டுத்துத் தேவியை மகிழ்வி யென்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"Angered that he has defied her, the mother calls him cruel, then offers another way: if he fears taking a life, let him make a cock of flour, offer it as a sacrifice and please the goddess.","n":"The proposal is a loophole in the king's refusal, offered in the same breath as her anger.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["bali","vazhipaadu","kolai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 142","lb":"YA142","mm":1,"ev":"The mother proposes a flour cock as a substitute for a living offering.","cl":[["a sacrifice made with an image of a living creature can please the goddess and ward off the dream's evil","general","reported",3,"chandramathi"]],"ag":["சந்திர மதி"],"ec":[],"spk":["சந்திர மதி","மன்னன்"],"ch":"ya:8"},"ya:143":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":143,"t":["மனம்விரி யல்குன் மாய மனத்தவை வகுத்த மாயக்","கனவுரை பிறிது தேவி கட்டுரை பிறிதொன் றாயிற்று","எனைவினை யுதயஞ் செய்ய இடர்பல விளைந்த என்பால்","வினைகளின் விளைவை யாவர் விலக்குந ரென்று நின்றான்."],"p":"","r":"The king, in a verse whose first half is hard, sets the queen's false dream-tale against his mother's counsel and says that, with his deeds now rising and many sorrows come upon him, no one can keep the ripening of deeds away.","n":"Lines 1 and 2 are hard (the sense of the queen's 'dream-tale' set against 'the goddess's counsel'). The reading of the first half is provisional; the king's words in lines 3 and 4 are clear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vinai","oozh","kanavu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 143","lb":"YA143","mm":1,"ev":"The king, standing before his mother, says that no one can turn back what his deeds are ripening.","cl":[["no one can ward off the ripening of deeds","general","asserted",4,"king"]],"ag":["மன்னன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ya:8"},"ya:144":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":144,"t":["உயிர்ப்பொருள் வடிவு கோற லுயிர்க்கொலை போலு மென்னும்","பயிர்ப்புள முடைய னேனும் பற்றறத் துணிவில் மன்னன்","செயிர்த்தவ ளுரைத்த செய்கை செய்வதற் கிசைந்த தென்றான்","அயிர்ப்பதென் அறத்தின் திண்மை யறிவதற் கமைவி லாதான்."],"p":"","r":"Although the king shrinks even from making the shape of a living creature, as it is like killing, he is not firm enough to cut his ties, and so consents to do what his mother angrily told him.","n":"The last line is hard (அயிர்ப்பதென் அறத்தின் திண்மை). The reading takes it as the narrator's remark that his hold on dharma was not firm enough to be sure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kollaamai","manam","aram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 144","lb":"YA144","mm":1,"ev":"The king agrees, against his own misgiving, to the flour-cock offering.","cl":[["making the likeness of a living creature is felt to be like killing","general","reported",1,"king"]],"ag":["மன்னன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ya:8"},"ya:145":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":145,"t":["மாவினின் வனைந்த கோழி வடிவுகொண் டவ்வை யாய","பாவிதன் னோடு மன்னன் படுகொலைக் கிடமதாய","தேவிதன் னிடைச்சென் றெய்திச் சிறப்பொடு வணக்கஞ் செய்தே","ஆவயின் தன்கை வாளா லரிந்துகொண் டருளி தென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"The king takes the flour-made cock with his mother to the goddess's place of slaughter, bows with honour and cuts it with his own sword, saying that it was offered to her.","n":"The place is called the place of killing (படுகொலைக்கிடம்); the king goes not as the sacrificer's helper but as the one who strikes.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["bali","vazhipaadu","kolai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 145","lb":"YA145","mm":1,"ev":"The king beheads the flour cock before the goddess with his own sword.","cl":[],"ag":["மன்னன்","சந்திர மதி"],"ec":[],"ch":"ya:8"},"ya:146":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":146,"t":["மேலியல் தெய்வங் கண்டே விரும்பின தடையப் பட்ட","சாலியி னிடியின் கோழி தலையரிந் திட்ட தோடி","கோலிய லரசன் முன்னர்க் கூவுபு குலுங்கி வீழ","மாலிய லரசன் தன்கை வாள்விடுத் துருகி னானே."],"p":"","r":"The goddess is pleased, and the flour cock, its head cut, runs before the king crying, shakes and falls, so that the king drops his sword and is moved with pity.","n":"The narrator treats the flour bird as a creature that cries out; the work thereby answers the mother's claim that a likeness is a harmless substitute.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kolai","arul","manam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 146","lb":"YA146","mm":1,"ev":"The flour cock cries and falls when the king's sword strikes it, and the king drops the sword.","cl":[],"ag":["மன்னன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ya:8"},"ya:147":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":147,"t":["என்னைகொல் மாவின் செய்கை யிவ்வுயிர் பெற்ற பெற்றி","சென்னிவா ளெறிய வோடிச் சிலம்பிய குரலி தென்கொல்","பின்னிய பிறவி மாலைப் பெருநவை தருதற் கொத்த","கொன்னியல் பாவ மென்னைக் கூவுகின் றதுபோன் மென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"The king asks what has happened to the flour cock that received life, why it ran and cried under his sword, and whether the sin that brings the chain of births its great fault is calling him.","n":"The fear is put as a question; the deaths at 153 are the poem's later answer.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","piravi","kolai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 147","lb":"YA147","mm":1,"ev":"The king questions the living cry of the flour cock and fears the sin has come to him.","cl":[["killing, even of a made likeness, brings a sin that binds to a chain of births","general","reported",3,"king"]],"ag":["மன்னன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["மன்னன்",""],"ch":"ya:8"},"ya:148":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":148,"t":["ஆதகா தன்னை சொல்லா லறிவிலே னருளில் செய்கை","ஆதகா தழிந்த புள்வா யரிகுர லரியு நெஞ்சை","ஆதகா தமிர்த முன்னா மதியவள் களவு கொல்லும்","ஆதகா வினைக ளென்னை யடர்த்துநின் றடுங்கொ லென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"In regret, the king cries out that he did a graceless thing by his mother's words, that the cry of the bird tears his heart, and that his deeds may now stand over him and crush him.","n":"Lines 2 and 3 are obscure (the repeated ஆதகா and a clause about the 'nectar' lady); the reading rests on lines 1 and 4.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["arul","vinai","manam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 148","lb":"YA148","mm":1,"ev":"The king laments the graceless act and fears his deeds.","cl":[["a deed done without grace returns on the doer as ripened deeds","general","reported",4,"king"]],"ag":["மன்னன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["மன்னன்",""],"ch":"ya:8"},"ya:149":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":149,"t":["இனையன நினைவு தம்மால் யசோதர னகர மெய்தித்","தனையனி லரசு வைத்துத் தவவனம் படர லுற்றான்","அனையதை யறிந்துந் தேவி யவமதித் தெனைவி டுத்தான்","எனநினைந் தேது செய்தா ளெரிநர கத்து வீழ்வாள்."],"p":"","r":"Thinking so, Yasodharan goes back to the city, sets his son on the throne and sets out for the forest of austerity; the queen, knowing it and thinking that he spurned her, does something that will send her to the burning hell.","n":"The narrator names the queen's end before her act, so the poison that follows is told as already judged.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thuravu","arasu","naragam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 149","lb":"YA149","mm":1,"ev":"The king hands the kingdom to his son and leaves for the ascetic forest, and the queen resents it.","cl":[["the queen who acts on her hurt will fall into burning hell","general","asserted",4,"narrator"]],"ag":["மன்னன்","தேவி"],"ec":[],"ch":"ya:8"},"ya:150":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":150,"t":["அரசுநீ துறத்தி யாயின் அமைகமற் றெனக்கு மஃதே","விரைசெய்தா ரிறைவ வின்றென் வியன்மனை மைந்த னோடும்","இரசநீ ரமிர்து கைக்கொண் டருளுதற் கிசைதல் செய்வாய்","அரசுதா னவன தாக அவிது நா மடிக ளென்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"The queen tells the king that if he gives up the kingdom, so will she; she begs him to come to her house that day with his son and take the sweet drink, and let the kingdom be the son's while they become ascetics.","n":"The speech is a pretence, as 152 says (வஞ்சனை வலித்து): her renunciation is the bait.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vanjam","thuravu","karpu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 150","lb":"YA150","mm":1,"ev":"The queen feigns agreement with the king's renunciation and invites him to her house.","cl":[],"ag":["தேவி"],"ec":[],"spk":["தேவி","மன்னன்"],"ch":"ya:8"},"ya:151":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":151,"t":["ஆங்கவ ளகத்து மாட்சி யறிந்தன னரச னேனும்","வீங்கிய முலையி னாய்நீ வேண்டிய தமைக என்றே","தாங்கல னவ்வை தன்னோ டவள்மனை தான மர்ந்தான்","தீங்கது குறுகின் தீய நயமுநன் னயம தாமே."],"p":"","r":"Though the king knows what lies in her heart, he tells her to do as she wishes and, bearing his mother with him, goes to sit in her house; the verse adds that when ruin is near even a wrong course seems the right one.","n":"Line 1 can be read as the king's knowledge of her intent or of her worth; the reading takes the first.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vanjam","manam","kedu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 151","lb":"YA151","mm":1,"ev":"The king, knowing her mind, goes with his mother to the queen's house.","cl":[["when ruin draws near, a wrong course appears right counsel","general","asserted",4,"narrator"]],"ag":["மன்னன்","சந்திர மதி"],"ec":[],"ch":"ya:8"},"ya:152":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":152,"t":["நஞ்சொடு கலந்த தேனி னறுஞ்சுவை பெரிய வாக","எஞ்சலி லட்டு கங்கள் இருவிரு மருந்து கென்றே","வஞ்சனை வலித்து மாமி தன்னுடன் வரனுக் கீந்தாள்","நஞ்சொடு படாத தானு மகனொடு நயந்து கொண்டாள்."],"p":"","r":"The queen mixes poison with honey and sweet food, gives it to her husband and his mother as a dish for both, and takes the unpoisoned part herself with her son.","n":"The honey's sweetness hides the poison: the same sweetness the Kural praises in speech here covers murder.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vanjam","kolai","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 152","lb":"YA152","mm":1,"ev":"The queen serves poisoned food to the king and his mother.","cl":[],"ag":["தேவி"],"ec":[],"ch":"ya:8"},"ya:153":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":153,"t":["நஞ்சது பரந்த போழ்தின் நடுங்கினர் மயங்கி வீழ்ந்தார்","அஞ்சினர் மரணஞ் சிந்தை யடைந்தது முதல தங்கண்","புஞ்சிய வினைகள் தீய புகுந்தன பொறிகள் பொன்றித்","துஞ்சினர் துயரத் துஞ்சா விலங்கிடைத் துன்னி னாரே."],"p":"","r":"As the poison spreads they tremble and fall in a swoon, fear death, and the deeds they had heaped come in; the senses fail and they die, to fall into the animal state that knows no end of pain.","n":"Both are given as causes: the queen's poison is the means, and the ripening of the family's own deeds is the narrator's reason. The poem does not say that the woman's act is excused by karma.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","piravi","oozh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 153","lb":"YA153","mm":1,"ev":"The king and his mother die of the poison and are reborn among animals.","cl":[["heaped deeds ripen at death and send the dying into animal births","general","asserted",3,"narrator"]],"ag":["மன்னன்","சந்திர மதி"],"ec":[],"ch":"ya:8"},"ya:154":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":154,"t":["எண்களுக் கிசைவி லாத இறைவியா மிவள்தன் செய்கை","கண்களுக் கிசைவி லாத கடையனைக் கருதி நெஞ்சின்","மண்களுக் கிறைவ னாய வரனுக்கு மரணஞ் செய்தாள்","பெண்களிற் கோத னாளே பெரியபா வத்த ளென்றார்."],"p":"","r":"The mourners say that this queen, unfit even for counting, thought of a low man unfit for eyes to see, killed her husband the lord of lands, and is the most wrathful and sinful of women.","n":"This is the people's judgment in their own grief, not the narrator's; the narrator has already judged her at 149.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","vanjam","kolai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 154","lb":"YA154","mm":1,"ev":"The mourners condemn the queen who killed her husband for a base lover.","cl":[["a wife who kills her husband for a base lover is the greatest sinner among women","general","reported",4,"mourners"]],"ag":["உழையர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["உழையர்",""],"ch":"ya:8"},"ya:155":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":155,"t":["தீதகல் கடவு ளாகச் செய்ததோர் படிமை யின்கண்","காதர முலகி தன்கண் கருதிய முடித்தல் கண்டுஞ்","சேதன வடிவு தேவிக் கெறிந்தனன் தெரிவொன் றில்லான்","ஆதலால் வந்த தின்றென் றழுங்கினர் சிலர்க ளெல்லாம்."],"p":"","r":"Some of the mourners say that the king made an image of a god free of evil, yet, though he saw the world's wishes fulfilled before such an image, threw a living-shaped offering to the goddess without discernment, and so this has come today.","n":"The mourners join the offering at 145 to the poison; the poem gives the same link in the narrator's voice at 153 only as ripened deeds, not as this one offering.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["bali","vinai","kolai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 155","lb":"YA155","mm":1,"ev":"Some mourners trace the king's death to his flour-cock offering.","cl":[["offering even the likeness of a living creature to a deity brings its fruit upon the offerer","general","reported",3,"mourners"]],"ag":["உழையர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["உழையர்",""],"ch":"ya:8"},"ya:156":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":156,"t":["அறப்பொருள் நுகர்தல் செல்லா னருந்தவர்க் கெளிய னல்லன்","மறப்பொருள் மயங்கி வையத் தரசியல் மகிழ்ந்து சென்றான்","இறப்பவு மிளையர் போகத் திவறின விறுதி யின்கண்","சிறப்புடை மரண மில்லைச் செல்கதி யென்கொ லென்றார்."],"p":"","r":"Others say that the king did not enjoy the wealth of dharma, was not easy for the great ascetics to reach, and was deluded by wrong things and joyful in rule; he died young while others were stingy with pleasure, and no good death waits at the end, so what is his destination?","n":"The mourners judge the king by his rule alone; the narrator's account of his refusal to kill at 138 to 140 shows what they cannot see.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aram","kedu","marumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 156","lb":"YA156","mm":1,"ev":"The mourners doubt the king's destination.","cl":[["one who delights in rule and does not take up dharma has no good death and an uncertain next birth","general","reported",4,"mourners"]],"ag":["உழையர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["உழையர்",""],"ch":"ya:8"},"ya:157":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":157,"t":["இனையன வுழையர் தாமும் எழில்நக ரத்து ளாரும்","நினைவன நினைந்து நெஞ்சின் நெகிழ்ந்தனர் புலம்பி வாடக்","கனைகழ லரசன் தேவி கருதிய வதுமு டித்தாள்","மனநனி வலிதின் மாழ்கி மைந்தனை வருக வென்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"The attendants and the townspeople weep at these thoughts, while the queen, who has done what she wished, her mind weakened by the strain, calls her son.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sutram","vanjam","kedu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 157","lb":"YA157","mm":1,"ev":"The people mourn while the queen calls her son.","cl":[],"ag":["உழையர்","தேவி"],"ec":[],"ch":"ya:8"},"ya:158":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":158,"t":["இனையனீ தனியை யாகி யிறைவனிற் பிரிந்த தென்கண்","வினையினால் விளைவு கண்டாய் விடுத்திடு மனத்து வெந்நோய்","புனைமுடி கவித்துப் பூமி பொதுக்கடிந் தாள்க வென்றே","மனநனி மகிழ்ந் திருந்தாள் மறைபதிக் கமுத மாவாள்."],"p":"","r":"The queen tells her son that he is alone now, that the loss of the king came from the fruit of her own deeds, bids him leave off the heart's sorrow, wear the crown and rule the earth, and sits glad, the nectar of her secret lord.","n":"The queen speaks the poem's own doctrine of karma to hide a deed that is hers; the poem uses the same words to expose her.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","vanjam","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 158","lb":"YA158","mm":1,"ev":"The queen consoles her son and bids him rule.","cl":[["the loss of her husband came from the ripening of her own deeds","general","reported",2,"queen"]],"ag":["தேவி"],"ec":[],"spk":["தேவி","இசோமதி"],"ch":"ya:8"},"ya:159":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":159,"t":["வாரணி முரச மார்ப்ப மணிபுனை மகுடஞ் சூடி","ஏரணி யார மார்ப னிசோமதி யிறைமை யெய்தி","சீரணி யடிகள் செல்வத் திருவற மருளல் செல்லான்","ஓரணி யாக மாத ருவகையங் கடலு ளாழ்ந்தான்."],"p":"","r":"With the drums beating, Yasomati puts on the jewelled crown and takes up lordship; he does not turn to the teacher's gift of dharma but sinks into a sea of the delight of women.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","kaamam","aram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 159","lb":"YA159","mm":1,"ev":"Yasomati is crowned and gives himself to pleasure.","cl":[],"ag":["இசோமதி"],"ec":[],"ch":"ya:8"},"ya:160":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":160,"t":["இனையன வினையி னாகு மியல்பிது தெரிதி யாயின்","இனையன துணைவ ராகு மினையரின் விளையு மின்பம்","இனையது தெளிவி லாதார் இருநில வரசு செய்கை","வனைமலர் மகுட மாரி தத்தனே மதியி தென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"The teller says to Maridattan that if he understands that such things arise from deeds, these will be his companions and from them joy will grow, while those without that clarity only rule the earth and wear the flowered crown.","n":"The speaker is taken to be the teller of the tale to the king Maridattan, from the frame laid in the second chapter; the verse itself names only the addressee.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vinai","arasu","aram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 160","lb":"YA160","mm":1,"ev":"Sudattan draws the lesson of the king's death for Maridattan.","cl":[["outcomes arise from deeds, and one who understands this gains true companions and joy","general","asserted",1,"sudattan"]],"ag":["சுதத்தன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சுதத்தன்","மாரிதத்தன்"],"ch":"ya:8"},"ya:161":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":161,"t":["மற்ற மன்னன் மதிமதி யென்றிவர்","நற்ற வத்திறை நல்லறம் புல்லலாம்","பற்றி னோடு முடிந்தனர் பல்பிறப்","புற்ற தாகு முரைக்குறு கின்றதே."],"p":"","r":"The poet says that the king and Chandramathi died still bound by attachment, and that what follows is the account of their many births.","n":"'Mathi' in line 1 is the edition's clipped form of Chandramathi, the king's mother, whom the next verses make the dog; the verse sets out the plan of the whole section on births, as 147 foresaw it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["piravi","vinai","pattru"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 161","lb":"YA161","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["those who die in attachment go on to many births","jain","asserted",3,"narrator"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"ya:9"},"ya:162":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":162,"t":["விந்த நாம விலங்கலின் மன்னவன்","வந்தொர் மாமயி லின்வயிற் றண்டமாய்","நந்து நாளிடை நாயொடு கண்டகன்","வந்தொர் வாளியி னானது வாட்டினான்."],"p":"","r":"A chief of the Vindhya hills, coming with a dog, shoots a great peahen that carries an egg in her belly.","n":"The verse names only 'the king of the hill called Vindhya' and a cruel hunter; that the egg holds the former king is learned from verse 172 on.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kollaamai","vinai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 162","lb":"YA162","mm":1,"ev":"A Vindhya hunter shoots a pregnant peahen.","cl":[],"ag":["விந்த மன்னவன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ya:9"},"ya:163":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":163,"t":["அம்பின் வாயுமி ழண்ட மெடுத்தவன்","வம்பு வாரண முட்டையின் வைத்துடன்","கொம்ப னாயிது கொண்டு வளர்க்கென","நம்பு காமர் புளிஞிகை நல்கினான்."],"p":"","r":"The man takes the egg that the arrow's mouth has spilled, sets it among a wild fowl's eggs and gives it to a loving hunter woman to rear.","n":"The same hand that kills the mother-bird saves the egg; the verse gives no reason, and the later births show the lives it leads to.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arul","vinai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 163","lb":"YA163","mm":1,"ev":"The hunter hands the rescued egg to a hunter woman to be reared.","cl":[],"ag":["விந்த மன்னவன்","புளிஞிகை"],"ec":[],"ch":"ya:9"},"ya:164":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":164,"t":["சந்தி ரம்மதி யாகிய தாயவள்","வந்து மாநக ரப்புறச் சேரிவாய்","முந்து செய்வினை யான்முளை வாளெயிற்","றந்த மிக்க சுணங்கம தாயினாள்."],"p":"","r":"Chandramathi, the king's mother, comes to the lane outside the great city by her earlier deed and is born a dog with sprouting fangs.","n":"'The deed done before' (முந்து செய்வினை) is left unnamed here; the reader supplies the urging of a blood-offering and the poisoning from the earlier chapters.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","piravi","oozh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 164","lb":"YA164","mm":1,"ev":"Chandramathi is reborn as a dog in the outcaste lane outside the city.","cl":[["a former deed drives the soul into its next birth","jain","asserted",3,"narrator"]],"ag":["சந்திர மதி"],"ec":[],"ch":"ya:9"},"ya:165":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":165,"t":["மயிலு ஞாயும் வளர்ந்தபின் மன்னனுக்","கியல் பாயன மென்று கொடுத்தனர்","வயிரி யாகு மிசோமதி மன்னவன்","இயலு மாளிகை யெய்தின வென்பவே."],"p":"","r":"When the peacock and the dog have grown, the hunters give them to the king as a fitting gift, and they come to the palace of Yasomathi, the king.","n":"வயிரியாகும் in line 3 may mean one who will be their enemy; the sense is not fixed. The two gift animals are the father and the grandmother of the king who receives them.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vinai","piravi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 165","lb":"YA165","mm":1,"ev":"The hunters present the grown peacock and dog to King Yasomathi, and they enter his palace.","cl":[],"ag":["புளிஞர்","இசோமதி"],"ec":[],"ch":"ya:9"},"ya:166":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":166,"t":["மன்ன னாகிய மாமயின் மாளிகைத்","தன்னின் முன்னெழு வார்க்குமுன் றானெழாத்","தன்னை யஞ்சினர் தங்களைத் தான்வெருண்","டின்ன வாற்றின் வளர்ந்திடு கின்றதே."],"p":"","r":"The peacock that was a king does not rise before those who rise before him, is startled even at his own body, and grows up in the palace in fear.","n":"Where in his man's life all rose before him, the bird shrinks from those who rise. The poet does not say the fear is remembered; that the bird is the king comes from line 1.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["piravi","nilaiyaamai","acham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 166","lb":"YA166","mm":1,"ev":"The bird that was once king lives timid in the palace.","cl":[],"ag":["யசோதரன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ya:9"},"ya:167":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":167,"t":["அஞ்சி லோதியர் தாமடி தைவரப்","பஞ்சி மெல்லணை பாவிய பள்ளிமேல்","துஞ்சு மன்னவன் மாமயிற் றோகையோ","டஞ்சி மெல்ல அசைந்தது பூமிமேல்."],"p":"","r":"Women stroke the feet of the sleeping king on his soft bed, and the peacock with its tail moves timidly on the ground.","n":"The couch the women tend is the very bed from which the same soul once rose; the verse sets the sleeper at ease and the bird in fear.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","piravi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 167","lb":"YA167","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["யசோதரன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ya:9"},"ya:168":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":168,"t":["சுரைய பாலடி சிற்சுவை பொற்கலத்","தரைய மேகலை யாரி னமர்ந்துணும்","அரையன் மாமயி லாய்ப்புறப் பள்ளிவாய்","இரை அவாவி யிருந்தயில் கின்றதே."],"p":"","r":"The king eats sweet food from a gold dish in the company of women, while the bird that was a king waits outside the hall for scraps.","n":"அரையன் in line 3 is the king at table, Yasomathi; the same word is used of the peacock in line 3, so 'the lord' belongs to the bird only as what he once was.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pasi","nilaiyaamai","piravi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 168","lb":"YA168","mm":1,"ev":"The former king, now a peacock, waits outside the bed hall for food as the new king dines.","cl":[],"ag":["யசோதரன்","இசோமதி"],"ec":[],"ch":"ya:9"},"ya:169":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":169,"t":["வந்து குப்பையின் மாசன முண்டபின்","சிந்து மச்சில்கள் சென்று கவர்ந்துதின்","றந்து ளும்மக ழங்கணத் தூடுமாய்ச்","சந்தி ரம்மதி நாய்தளர் கின்றதே."],"p":"","r":"The dog, once Chandramathi, eats the sweepings of the dung-heap, grabs the leavings and grows weak in the yard.","n":"A queen mother who once dined in her palace is now at the refuse; verse 168 had the peacock waiting outside the hall in the same way.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pasi","piravi","vinai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 169","lb":"YA169","mm":1,"ev":"The dog scavenges scraps and grows weak.","cl":[],"ag":["சந்திர மதி"],"ec":[],"ch":"ya:9"},"ya:170":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":170,"t":["நல்வ தத்தொட றத்திற நண்ணலார்","கொல்வ தற்குள முன்செய் கொடுமையான்","ஒல்வ தற்கரு மாதுய ருற்றனர்","வெல்வ தற்கரி தால்வினை யின்பயன்."],"p":"","r":"The poet says that those who did not hold to the good vow and the way of virtue came into great suffering that could not be borne, by the cruelty they had done before, and that the fruit of deed is hard to overcome.","n":"The only verse in this run to state the rule outright; it comes after both the peacock's and the dog's lowered state is shown. 'Vow' (வதம்) is the Jain term, so the school tag follows the word.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","oozh","aram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 170","lb":"YA170","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["deeds of cruelty done before bear unbearable suffering as their fruit","jain","asserted",2,"narrator"],["the fruit of deed cannot be overcome","jain","asserted",4,"narrator"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"ya:9"},"ya:171":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":171,"t":["மற்றொர் நாள்மணி மண்டபத் தின்புடை","அற்ற மாவிருந் தட்டபங் கன்றனை","முற்று வார்முலை யாண்முயங் குந்திறம்","மற்ற மாமயில் வந்தது கண்டதே."],"p":"","r":"One day the peacock sees, by the side of the jewelled hall, the woman with full breasts embracing the hunchback Ashtavakran after a great feast.","n":"The verse does not name the woman; that she is the former queen is inferred from verse 172, which calls her the bird's beloved of the earlier birth.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","karpu","vinai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 171","lb":"YA171","mm":1,"ev":"The peacock sees the woman embracing Ashtavakran by the hall.","cl":[],"ag":["அமிழ்தமுன் மதி","அட்டமா பங்கன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ya:9"},"ya:172":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":172,"t":["அப்பி றப்பி லமர்ந்ததன் காதலி","ஒப்பில் செய்கை யுணர்ந்த துணர்ந்தபின்","தப்பி லன்னது சாரனொண் கண்களைக்","குப்பு றாமிசைக் குத்தி யழித்ததே."],"p":"","r":"Recalling, after it understood, the peerless deed of its beloved of the earlier birth, the bird pecks out the guiltless lover's bright eyes.","n":"The bird that remembers an earlier birth is the one thing in the chapter so far that does remember; it answers with its beak what the king had stopped his sword from doing.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","kaamam","piravi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 172","lb":"YA172","mm":1,"ev":"The peacock pecks out Ashtavakran's eyes.","cl":[],"ag":["யசோதரன்","அட்டமா பங்கன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ya:9"},"ya:173":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":173,"t":["முத்த வாள்நகை யாள்முனி வுற்றனள்","கைத்த லத்தொரு கற்றிரள் வீசலும்","மத்த கத்தை மடுத்து மறித்தது","தத்தி மஞ்ஞை தரைப்பட வீழ்ந்ததே."],"p":"","r":"The woman, enraged, flings a handful of stones from her hand; they strike the bird's head and it falls to the ground.","n":"'She of the pearl-white smile' (முத்த வாள்நகையாள்) is the woman of 171, here seen in anger.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vekuli","kodungol","vinai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 173","lb":"YA173","mm":1,"ev":"The woman hurls stones and knocks the peacock down.","cl":[],"ag":["அமிழ்தமுன் மதி"],"ec":[],"ch":"ya:9"},"ya:174":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":174,"t":["தாய்முன் னாகி யிறந்து பிறந்தவள்","நாய்பின் னோடி நலிந்தது கவ்விய","வாய்முன் மஞ்ஞை மடிந்துயிர் போயது","தீமை செய்வினை செய்திற மின்னதே."],"p":"","r":"The dog, the mother born again, runs behind and bites; the peacock dies in her mouth, and the poet says this is how harmful deeds come to their fruit.","n":"The mother who once urged the son to a blood-offering kills him, a bird, in her mouth; neither knows the other. The verse says deed bears fruit but not whose deed it is here.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","kootru","piravi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 174","lb":"YA174","mm":1,"ev":"The dog seizes the peacock and kills it.","cl":[["harmful deeds return as just such harm","general","asserted",4,"narrator"]],"ag":["சந்திர மதி","யசோதரன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ya:9"},"ya:175":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":175,"t":["நாயின் வாயில் நடுங்கிய மாமயில்","போய தின்னுயி ரென்றன ளென்னவும்","ஆயு மாறறி யாத விசோமதி","நாயை யெற்றின னாய்பெய் பலகையால்."],"p":"","r":"Thinking that the dear life of the peacock trembling in the dog's mouth had left, a woman says so; and Yasomathi, not understanding, beats the dog with a board.","n":"The beater is the dead bird's son and the dog's grandson; he acts without knowing it, so the harm done is the work of ignorance not of choice.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vekuli","vinai","kollaamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 175","lb":"YA175","mm":1,"ev":"Yasomathi strikes the dog with a board, not knowing who she is.","cl":[],"ag":["இசோமதி"],"ec":[],"ch":"ya:9"},"ya:176":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":176,"t":["மன்னன் மாமயில் வந்துவிந் தக்கிரி","துன்னுஞ் சூழலுட் சூழ்மயிர் முள்ளுடை","இன்னல் செய்யுமோ ரேனம தாகிய","தன்ன தாகு மருவினை யின்பயன்."],"p":"","r":"The king that was a peacock is born in the Vindhya hills as a boar with bristles like thorns and brings pain to others; the poet calls this the fruit of a hard deed.","n":"The poet moves the soul on at once: the one who was in 175 beaten for its sake is not heard of again.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["piravi","vinai","oozh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 176","lb":"YA176","mm":1,"ev":"The former king is reborn as a boar in the Vindhya woods.","cl":[["a hard deed brings its fruit in a new birth","general","asserted",4,"narrator"]],"ag":["யசோதரன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ya:9"},"ya:177":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":177,"t":["சந்தி ரம்மதி நாயுமச் சாரலின்","வந்து காரிருள் வண்ணத்த நாகமாய்","அந்தி லூர்தர வார்த்துரு ளக்குடர்","வெந்தெ ழும்பசி விட்டது பன்றியே."],"p":"","r":"The dog too comes to the hillside, as a black snake, and coils; the boar's bowels burn and his hunger leaves him.","n":"'Hunger left the boar' may mean the snake swallowed him or that he died; the sense turns on a rare phrase. Verse 178 calls this boar the one that killed the mother, so the two kill each other.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["piravi","vinai","pasi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 177","lb":"YA177","mm":1,"ev":"The dog is reborn as a black snake and attacks the boar.","cl":[],"ag":["சந்திர மதி","யசோதரன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ya:9"},"ya:178":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":178,"t":["தாய்கொல் பன்றி தளர்ந்தயர் போழ்தனில்","சீய மொன்றெனச் சீறுளி யம்மெதிர்","பாய நொந்து பதைத்துடல் வீழ்ந்தரோ","போய தின்னுயிர் பொன்றுபு பன்றியே."],"p":"","r":"As the boar that killed its mother wearies, a fierce bear charges it like a lion; it falls tormented and dies.","n":"The verse calls the boar 'the mother-killing boar' (தாய்கொல் பன்றி), so by the poem's count the boar had killed the snake that was Chandramathi; a bear, with no part in the story, ends him.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kootru","vinai","piravi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 178","lb":"YA178","mm":1,"ev":"A bear attacks the boar and kills it.","cl":[["the one who killed pays with his own life","general","reported",1,"narrator"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"ya:9"},"ya:179":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":179,"t":["மன்னன் மாமயில் சூகரம் வார்புனல்","இன்னல் செய்யுஞ் சிருப்பிரை யாற்றினுள்","உன்னி லொப்பி லுலோகித விப்பெயர்","மன்னு மீனின் வடிவின தாயிற்றே."],"p":"","r":"The soul that was king and boar becomes a fish named Lohita in the river Shiprai, whose waters bring pain.","n":"The fish's name (உலோகித) is given only here; the birth takes the soul from the land to the water.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["piravi","vinai","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 179","lb":"YA179","mm":1,"ev":"The former king is reborn as the fish Lohita in the river Shiprai.","cl":[],"ag":["யசோதரன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ya:9"},"ya:180":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":180,"t":["சந்தி ரம்மதி நாய்கரு நாகமாய்","முந்து சென்று முதலைய தாயது","வெந்து வேர்த்தின மீனை விழுங்குவான்","உந்தி யுந்தி யுளைந்திடு போழ்தினில்."],"p":"","r":"The dog, as black snake, goes before and becomes a crocodile, and burning with heat it strains to swallow the fish.","n":"The mother has been dog, snake and now crocodile, each time moving to meet the son in his next form; the poem does not say she knows him.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["piravi","vinai","pasi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 180","lb":"YA180","mm":1,"ev":"The former snake is reborn as a crocodile and tries to swallow the fish.","cl":[],"ag":["சந்திர மதி"],"ec":[],"ch":"ya:9"},"ya:181":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":181,"t":["அந்த ரத்தொரு கூனி நின்றாடுவாள்","வந்து வாயின் மடுத்தது கொண்டது","கொந்து வேய்குழற் கூனியைக் கொல்கராத்","தந்து கொல்கென மன்னவன் சாற்றினான்."],"p":"","r":"A hunchback woman dances in the air; the crocodile seizes her in its mouth, and the king orders the crocodile given to killers to be killed.","n":"கொல்கராத்தந்து is unclear in the edition; the reading takes கொல்க as the order to kill. The king who gives the order is the crocodile's own grandson.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vekuli","kolai","vinai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 181","lb":"YA181","mm":1,"ev":"Yasomathi orders the crocodile that took the hunchback killed.","cl":[],"ag":["கூனி","இசோமதி"],"ec":[],"ch":"ya:9"},"ya:182":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":182,"t":["வலையின் வாழ்நரின் வாரிற் பிடித்தபின்","சிலர்ச லாகை வெதுப்பிச் செறிந்தனர்","கொலைவ லாளர் குறைத்தன ரீர்ந்தனர்","அலைசெய் தார்பலர் யாரவை சொல்லுவார்."],"p":"","r":"The fishers catch the crocodile in a net and drag it by ropes, heat skewers and surround it, and the killers cut and tear it; the poet says many tormented it and who can tell it all.","n":"The poet turns away from the telling ('who can tell it'), as he does not at the beast's earlier deaths; the verse gives the cruelty its own weight.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kolai","vinai","kodungol"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 182","lb":"YA182","mm":1,"ev":"Fishers and killers capture the crocodile and cut it to pieces.","cl":[],"ag":["வலையின் வாழ்நர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ya:9"},"ya:183":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":183,"t":["சந்தி ரம்மதி நாய்கரு நாகமாய்","வந்து வார்வலைப் பட்ட கராமரித்","தந்தில் வாழ்புலை யாளர்தஞ் சேரிவாய்","வந்தொ ராட்டின் மடப்பிணை யாயதே."],"p":"","r":"The mother, once Chandramathi, passes through the births of a dog, a black snake and a crocodile caught in a long net, and after that death comes as a young female goat to the hamlet of the outcaste people.","n":"The verse puts a queen's soul in a string of animal births and ends in the lowest settlement; it gives no reason for the order of births here, and the earlier chapters' account of her deed is not repeated.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["piravi","vinai","yaakkai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 183","lb":"YA183","mm":1,"ev":"Chandramathi, reborn as a dog, a snake and a crocodile, dies in a fisher's net and is born as a young goat in an outcaste hamlet.","cl":[],"ag":["சந்திர மதி"],"ec":[],"ch":"ya:10"},"ya:184":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":184,"t":["மற்றை மீனுமோர் வார்வலைப் பட்டதை","அற்றை யில்வரு மந்தணர் கண்டனர்","கொற்ற மன்னவ நின்குலத் தார்களுக்","குற்ற செய்கைக் குரித்தென ஓதினார்."],"p":"","r":"The other fish, the one that was once the king, is also caught in a long net, and the brahmins who came that day see it and tell the king it is a fit thing for the rite due to his kin.","n":"The fish is Yasodharan, though the verse says only 'the other fish' (மற்றை மீன்); the reader has to carry the identity from the births in the earlier verses.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pulaal","aasaaram","vinai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 184","lb":"YA184","mm":1,"ev":"Brahmins see a netted fish and tell King Yasomathi it suits the rite for his lineage.","cl":[["a fish taken for the kin's rite is a fitting thing for the king to use","vedic","reported",3,"andanar"]],"ag":["அந்தணர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["அந்தணர்","இசோமதி"],"ch":"ya:10"},"ya:185":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":185,"t":["அறுத்த மீனி னவயவ மொன்றினைக்","கறித்தி சோமதி யிப்புவி காக்கவோர்","இறப்ப ருந்துறக் கத்தின் இசோதரன்","சிறக்க வென்றனர் தீவினை யாளரே."],"p":"","r":"The brahmins, whom the poet calls doers of evil, tell Yasomathi to eat one limb of the cut fish so that Yasodharan may prosper in heaven, which is hard to die into, and so that he may guard the earth.","n":"The word தீவினையாளர் is the poet's own verdict on the speakers, set into the narration, not something the brahmins say of themselves; the work answers the claim through what the father is, not in this verse (see 196-197).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["siraaththam","pulaal","marumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 185","lb":"YA185","mm":1,"ev":"The brahmins bid Yasomathi eat a piece of the fish to raise his dead father in heaven.","cl":[["a son who eats the flesh given in the rite raises the dead father to prosper in heaven","vedic","reported",3,"andanar"]],"ag":["அந்தணர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["அந்தணர்","இசோமதி"],"ch":"ya:10"},"ya:186":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":186,"t":["நின்ற கண்டத்து நீளுயிர் போயது","சென்ற தன்பிறப் போர்ந்து தெளிந்தது","தின்று தின்று துறக்கத் திருத்துதல்","நன்று நன்றென நைந்திறந் திட்டதே."],"p":"","r":"The life standing in the fish's throat departs; it understood the birth it had come from and, with the thought that eating after eating sets one up in heaven, it wasted away and died saying 'good, good'.","n":"Lines 3 and 4 are hard: 'eating and eating to set one in heaven, good, good' can be the dying creature's bitter echo of the brahmins, or a mere report of what they said; I read it as the fish's own bitter echo, and the reading is provisional.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["piravi","yaakkai","thelivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 186","lb":"YA186","mm":1,"ev":"The fish remembers its former birth and dies as the rite proceeds.","cl":[],"ag":["யசோதரன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ya:10"},"ya:187":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":187,"t":["மன்னன் மாமயில் சூகர மாயமீன்","முன்னை யாட்டின் வயற்றின் முடிந்ததோர்","மன்னு மாணுரு வெய்தி வளர்ந்தபின்","தன்னை யீன்றவத் தாய்மிசைத் தாழ்ந்ததே."],"p":"","r":"The king, after the births of peacock, boar and fish, ends in the womb of the goat of before, takes the form of a male, and when grown falls upon the very mother who bore him.","n":"The verse says the birth ended in the womb of 'the goat of before' (முன்னை ஆடு); that this goat is Chandramathi of 183 is the reader's inference. The poet states the union flatly and does not comment on it; the work's own sin-and-fruit line is carried by the verses around it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["piravi","vinai","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 187","lb":"YA187","mm":1,"ev":"Yasodharan, reborn as a ram-kid from the goat who was once his mother, grows and mates with her.","cl":[],"ag":["யசோதரன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ya:10"},"ya:188":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":188,"t":["தாயி னன்னலந் தானுகர் போழ்தினில்","ஆய கோபத் தடர்த்தொரு வன்றகர்","பாய வோடிப் பதைத்துயிர் போயபின்","தாய்வ யிற்றினிற் றாதுவிற் சார்ந்ததே."],"p":"","r":"While he enjoys the goodness of his mother, a strong ram, angered, charges at him; he runs, writhes and loses life, and joins the womb of the same mother through the seed.","n":"'ஒரு வன்றகர்' is read as 'a certain strong ram'; if the edition's word division is 'ஒருவன் தகர்' (a man's ram) the sense is the same. How the dead creature 'joins' (சார்ந்தது) the womb is told in a word and not explained.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["piravi","yaakkai","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 188","lb":"YA188","mm":1,"ev":"A rival ram attacks Yasodharan as he mates; he dies and enters his mother's womb again.","cl":[],"ag":["யசோதரன்","வன்றகர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ya:10"},"ya:189":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":189,"t":["தாய்வ யிற்கரு வுட்டக ராயது","போய்வ ளர்ந்துழிப் பூமுடி மன்னவன்","மேய வேட்டை விழைந்தனன் மீள்பவன்","தாயை வாளியிற் றானுயிர் போக்கினான்."],"p":"","r":"The soul becomes a young kid in its mother's womb; when it has grown, the flower-crowned king, wishing to hunt, kills the mother goat with an arrow on his way back.","n":"Read with 183 and 187, the doe is Chandramathi, the king's own grandmother, and the kid is his own father; the verse itself names neither, and the hunting king does not know.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kolai","piravi","vinai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 189","lb":"YA189","mm":1,"ev":"King Yasomathi, back from the hunt, shoots the pregnant goat dead.","cl":[],"ag":["இசோமதி"],"ec":[],"ch":"ya:10"},"ya:190":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":190,"t":["வாளி யின்விழும் வன்றகர்க் குட்டியை","நீள நின்ற புலைக்குலத் தோன்றலைத்","தாள்வ ருத்தந் தவிர்த்து வளர்க்கென","ஆளி மொய்ம்ப னருளின னென்பவே."],"p":"","r":"The lion-strong king takes pity on the kid that falls from the arrow, and orders the outcaste man to raise it and spare its feet hardship.","n":"The kindness comes at the moment he has killed its mother, and the closing என்பவே marks it as the poet's report.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arul","piravi","oozh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 190","lb":"YA190","mm":1,"ev":"The king has the orphaned kid saved and raised by an outcaste man.","cl":[],"ag":["இசோமதி"],"ec":[],"ch":"ya:10"},"ya:191":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":191,"t":["மற்றொர் நாள்மற மாதிற்கு மன்னவன்","பெற்றி யாற்பர விப்பெரு வேட்டைபோய்","உற்ற பல்லுயிர் கொன்றுவந் தேற்றினான்","கொற்ற மிக்கெரு மைப்பலி யொன்றரோ."],"p":"","r":"On another day, by his usual custom, the king goes on a great hunt, kills the many creatures that come to hand, and brings them back to offer, with a buffalo as the chief offering, to the goddess of war.","n":"The poem calls the buffalo the king's victorious offering (கொற்றமிக்கு) in plain description; the reader sets it against what the king's father refused at 139.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kolai","bali","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 191","lb":"YA191","mm":1,"ev":"King Yasomathi kills many animals in a hunt and offers a buffalo to the goddess.","cl":[["a king may offer many killed creatures and a buffalo to the war goddess","vedic","reported",3,"yasomathi"]],"ag":["இசோமதி"],"ec":[],"ch":"ya:10"},"ya:192":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":192,"t":["இன்றெ றிந்த எருமை யிதுதனைத்","தின்று தின்று சிராத்தஞ் செயப்பெறின்","நன்ற தென்றன ரந்தணர் நல்கினான்","நின்று பின்சிலர் நீதிகள் ஓதினார்."],"p":"","r":"The brahmins say that the buffalo killed today would be good if it could be eaten in the rite for the dead, and the king gives it to them; then some others stand up and recite rules.","n":"The rite is named சிராத்தம். The speaker of the rules (சிலர்) at line 4 is different from the brahmins who claimed the flesh.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["siraaththam","pulaal","aasaaram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 192","lb":"YA192","mm":1,"ev":"The brahmins claim the fresh buffalo for the rite and the king gives it; others begin to cite rules.","cl":[["flesh of an animal killed today is good for the rite of the dead","vedic","reported",2,"andanar"]],"ag":["அந்தணர்","இசோமதி"],"ec":[],"spk":["அந்தணர்","இசோமதி"],"ch":"ya:10"},"ya:193":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":193,"t":["ஆத பத்தி லுலர்ந்ததை யாதலால்","காது காகங் கவர்ந்தன வாமெனின்","தீது தாமுஞ் சிராத்தஞ் செயற்கென","ஓதி னாரினி யொன்றுள தென்றனர்."],"p":"","r":"Some say that since the buffalo has dried in the sun and crows may have seized it, it is no good for the rite, and add that there is still another way.","n":"Lines 1 and 2 are broken by the edition's word division; the sense 'dried in the sun, crows may have taken it' is a provisional reading.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["siraaththam","pulaal","aasaaram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 193","lb":"YA193","mm":1,"ev":"Rule-reciters object that the sun-dried flesh may be spoiled and offer another way.","cl":[["flesh dried in the sun and open to crows is unfit for the rite","vedic","reported",2,"neethiyor"]],"ag":["சிலர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சிலர்","இசோமதி"],"ch":"ya:10"},"ya:194":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":194,"t":["தீதி தென்ற பிசிதமுந் தேர்ந்துழிச்","சாத நல்ல தகர்முகத் துப்படின்","பூத மென்றனர் புண்ணிய நூல்களின்","நாத னாரத் துராதிகள் நன்றரோ."],"p":"","r":"They go on that even flesh named bad may be judged by choice, and that if good cooked rice is set before the ram's face the books of merit count it as proper; the rest of the line is obscure.","n":"Lines 3 and 4 are very hard (பூதம், நாதனார், அத்துராதிகள்); what is firm is that the ram is chosen, and that is the turn to the kid of 190. The rest is a provisional reading.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["siraaththam","pulaal","aasaaram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 194","lb":"YA194","mm":1,"ev":"The rule-reciters name the ram as the right victim, with rice laid before its face.","cl":[["the ram, with rice set before its face, is the right offering by the books of merit","vedic","reported",3,"neethiyor"]],"ag":["சிலர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சிலர்","இசோமதி"],"ch":"ya:10"},"ya:195":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":195,"t":["என்ற லும்மிணர் பெய்ம்முடி மன்னவன்","நன்று நாமுன் வளர்க்க விடுத்தது","சென்று தம்மெனச் சென்றன ரொற்றர்பின்","நன்றி தென்று நயந்தன ரந்தணர்."],"p":"","r":"On hearing this, the king says the kid they once sent off to be reared is the very thing, and sends spies to fetch it; the brahmins approve it as good.","n":"The decisive moment is the king's own choice of the animal he had saved; the father is brought to the rite by the son's order, with no word of knowing.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","pulaal","oozh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 195","lb":"YA195","mm":1,"ev":"The king sends spies to bring back the reared ram-kid for the rite, and the brahmins approve.","cl":[],"ag":["இசோமதி","ஒற்றர்","அந்தணர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["இசோமதி","ஒற்றர்"],"ch":"ya:10"},"ya:196":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":196,"t":["சென்று நல்லமிர் துண்டது தின்றனர்","அன்று மன்னன் யசோதர னன்னையோ","டொன்றி யும்பரு லகினுள் வாழ்கென","நன்று சொல்லினர் நான்மறை யாளரே."],"p":"","r":"They eat what was brought, and the brahmins, learned in the four Vedas, bless the dead King Yasodharan to live in the world above together with his mother.","n":"The claim is stated as the brahmins' belief and is turned in the next verse, where the eaten kid recognises himself as the blessed man; the verse itself does not mark the irony.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["siraaththam","pulaal","marumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 196","lb":"YA196","mm":1,"ev":"The brahmins eat the kid's flesh and bless the dead Yasodharan and his mother with life in heaven.","cl":[["the rite of eating flesh sends the dead king and his mother to a good world","vedic","reported",3,"andanar"]],"ag":["அந்தணர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["அந்தணர்",""],"ch":"ya:10"},"ya:197":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":197,"t":["அத்த லத்தக ராங்கது கேட்டபின்","ஒத்த தன்பிறப் புள்ளியு ளைந்துடன்","இத்த லத்திறை யான விசோமதி","மத்த யானையின் மன்னவ னென்மகன்."],"p":"","r":"Having heard that, the goat of that place remembers its birth and, grieving, understands that the one who rules here, King Yasomathi with his rutting elephant, is its own son.","n":"The eaten animal recovers its memory of the former birth only as the rite is spoken over it; the Jain epic presents memory of births as a gift of understanding, not a punishment.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["piravi","ninaivu","thelivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 197","lb":"YA197","mm":1,"ev":"Hearing the blessing, the kid recalls its birth and knows the king as its son.","cl":[],"ag":["யசோதரன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["யசோதரன்",""],"ch":"ya:10"},"ya:198":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":198,"t":["இதுவென் மாநக ருஞ்சயி னிப்பதி","இதுவென் மாளிகை யாமென் னுழைக்கலம்","இதுவெ லாமிவ ரென்னுழை யாளராம்","இதுவென் யானிவ ணின்னண மாயதே."],"p":"","r":"It sees that this is its own great city of Ujjayini, this its palace and treasure, these its servants, and that this is how it has come to be.","n":"The repeated 'this is mine' (இது என்) in every line is the king's old claim of ownership, now said by a goat.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","selvam","piravi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 198","lb":"YA198","mm":1,"ev":"The kid surveys what was once its city, palace and servants.","cl":[],"ag":["யசோதரன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["யசோதரன்",""],"ch":"ya:10"},"ya:199":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":199,"t":["யான்ப டைத்த பொருட்குவை யாமிவை","யான்வ ளர்த்த மதக்களி றாமிவை","யானளித்த குலப்பரி யாமிவை","யான்வி ளைத்த வினைப்பய னின்னதே."],"p":"","r":"It says that these heaps of wealth it made, these rutting elephants it reared, these fine horses it bred, are what the deed it produced has now ripened into.","n":"The line is open: 'what I made is the fruit of the deed I produced' may mean the wealth was the fruit, or that the fallen state is; the verse sets both against each other. The voice is a kid's, and the work lets it stand.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vinai","oozh","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 199","lb":"YA199","mm":1,"ev":"The kid names the wealth, elephants and horses it once made as the fruit of its own deed.","cl":[["one's own deed, produced in a former birth, ripens into one's present state","jain","asserted",4,"yasodharan"]],"ag":["யசோதரன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["யசோதரன்",""],"ch":"ya:10"},"ya:200":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":200,"t":["இவர்க ளென்கடைக் காவல ராகுவர்","இவர்க ளென்படை நாயக ராகுவர்","இவர்க ளென்னிசை பாடுந ராடுநர்","இவர்க ளும்மின ரென்பரி வாரமே."],"p":"","r":"It adds that these men are its gate guards, its army chiefs, its singers and dancers, its own attendants.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","nilaiyaamai","perumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 200","lb":"YA200","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["யசோதரன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["யசோதரன்",""],"ch":"ya:10"},"ya:201":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":201,"t":["என்னை நஞ்சுபெய் தின்னண மாயிழைத்","தன்ன மென்னடை யாளமிர் தம்மதி","மன்னு தன்மறை யானொடு வைகுமோ","என்னை செய்தன ளோவிவ ணில்லையால்."],"p":"","r":"It wonders what the poison-giving queen Amirthamathi, who gave it this end, is doing, whether she lives on with the hidden lover, and what became of her, since she is not here.","n":"Lines 3 and 4 are obscure; 'her lasting hidden one' (தன் மறையான்) is read as the secret lover of ch. 6 and the last clause is a provisional reading.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vanjam","kaamam","vinai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 201","lb":"YA201","mm":1,"ev":"The kid wonders what became of the wife who poisoned it.","cl":[],"ag":["யசோதரன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["யசோதரன்",""],"ch":"ya:10"},"ya:202":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":202,"t":["அசைய தாகி யரும்பட ரொன்றிலா","இசைய லாதன யானுற வித்தலைத்","தசைதி னாளர்கள் தங்களி னென்னையிவ்","வசையின் மன்னவன் வானுல குய்க்குமோ."],"p":"","r":"It asks whether this blamed king, with flesh-eaters such as these, who has made me suffer without cause, can send me to the heaven he wishes.","n":"The claim is put as a doubtful question by the eaten man himself, who is the dead one the rite claims to lift; it answers the brahmins' claim of 185 and 196 from inside the story. The grammar of lines 1 to 3 is hard.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["siraaththam","pulaal","marumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 202","lb":"YA202","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["a rite carried out with flesh-eaters does not lift the dead to heaven","vedic","refuted",3,"yasodharan"]],"ag":["யசோதரன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["யசோதரன்",""],"ch":"ya:10"},"ya:203":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":203,"t":["பேதை மாதர்பெய் நஞ்சினி லெஞ்சியிம்","மேதி னிப்பதி யாதல் விடுத்தபின்","யாது செய்தன னோவினை யேனிடை","யாது செய்குவ னோவுண ரேனினி."],"p":"","r":"With what the foolish woman's poison left of him gone from this earth and city, he asks what he did and what he will do, and says he knows nothing.","n":"The grief is without a way out; he does not yet link his state to the Jain account of deeds that the youths gave at 34 and 35.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","nilaiyaamai","piravi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 203","lb":"YA203","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["யசோதரன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["யசோதரன்",""],"ch":"ya:10"},"ya:204":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":204,"t":["இனைய வாகிய சிந்தைக ளெண்ணிலா","வினையி னாகிய வெந்துயர் தந்திடத்","தனையன் மாளிகை தன்னுள நோக்குமுன்","சினைகொண் டாடுயிர் சென்று பிறந்ததே."],"p":"","r":"As such thoughts rise, the great pain made by countless deeds presses on him; before he can look at his son's palace, the kid's life leaves and goes to be born again as an embryo.","n":"The poet carries the claim by naming the pain as 'made by deeds' (வினையினாகிய); the next birth is stated in 'சினை' (an embryo or egg) and the shape it takes is left to the next verse.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","piravi","saakkaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 204","lb":"YA204","mm":1,"ev":"The kid dies and is born again.","cl":[["deeds without number make sharp suffering","jain","asserted",2,"narrator"]],"ag":["யசோதரன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ya:10"},"ya:205":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":205,"t":["சந்தி ரம்மதி நாய்கரு நாகமாய்","வந்தி டங்கரு மாகிய வாடது","நந்து பல்பொருள் நாடு கலிங்கத்து","வந்து மாயிட மாகி வளர்ந்ததே."],"p":"","r":"The verse lists the rebirths so far (Chandramathi a dog, a black snake after) and says that the one now followed came to the goods-rich land of Kalinga as a buffalo and grew up there.","n":"The line 'வாடது' and who the snake is are hard; that the buffalo is the poisoned king reborn is taken from the sequel (the queen's eating of it, 212, and the accusation at 221), not stated in the verse.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["piravi","vinai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 205","lb":"YA205","mm":1,"ev":"The one passing through rebirths is born and grows as a buffalo in Kalinga.","cl":[],"ag":["மயிடம்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ya:11"},"ya:206":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":206,"t":["வணிகர் தம்முடன் மாமயி டம்மது","பணிவில் பண்டம் பரிந்துழல் கின்றநாள்","அணிகொ ளுஞ்சயி னிப்புறத் தாற்றயல்","வணிகர் வந்து மகிழ்ந்துவிட் டார்களே."],"p":"","r":"The buffalo travels with merchants, bearing their goods without rest, and one day the merchants halt their caravan outside Ujjain by the river.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["piravi","idumbai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 206","lb":"YA206","mm":1,"ev":"Merchants and their burden-buffalo come and camp on the bank beside Ujjain.","cl":[],"ag":["வணிகர்","மயிடம்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ya:11"},"ya:207":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":207,"t":["தூர பாரஞ் சுமந்த துயரது","தீர வோடுஞ் சிருப்பிரை யாற்றினுள்","ஆர மூழ்கிவந் தம்மயி டங்கரை","சேரு மாவினைச் சென்றெறிந் திட்டதே."],"p":"","r":"Wearied by the load it carried, the buffalo runs into the Sipra river, bathes its fill, and coming to the bank it strikes the horse that has come there.","n":"The word மா in line 4 is read here as 'horse', since 208 names the royal mount as lost; the edition leaves it as 'the animal'.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["idumbai","piravi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 207","lb":"YA207","mm":1,"ev":"The buffalo, freed of its load, bathes in the Sipra and gores a horse on the bank.","cl":[],"ag":["மயிடம்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ya:11"},"ya:208":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":208,"t":["வரைசெய் தோண்மன்ன வணிகர் மயிடத்தால்","அரைச வன்ன மெனும்பெய ராகுநம்","அரைச வாகன மாயது போயதென்","றுரைசெய் தாரர சர்க்குழை யாளரே."],"p":"","r":"The king's attendants announce that the royal mount named Arasavannam, killed by the merchants' buffalo, is gone.","n":"The attendants lay the loss on the merchants through their buffalo, which sets up the king's seizure in 209.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","kutram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 208","lb":"YA208","mm":1,"ev":"The royal attendants report to the king that his horse is dead through the merchants' buffalo.","cl":[],"ag":["குழையாளர்","மயிடம்"],"ec":[],"spk":["குழையாளர்","இசோமதி"],"ch":"ya:11"},"ya:209":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":209,"t":["அணிகொள் மாமுடி மன்ன னழன்றனன்","வணிகர் தம்பொருள் வாரி மயிடமும்","பிணிசெய் தெம்முழை வம்மெனப் பேணினான்","கணித மில்பொருள் சென்று கவர்ந்தனர்."],"p":"","r":"The king, in a rage, orders the merchants' wealth and the buffalo seized and brought to him, and his men go and carry off goods past counting.","n":"The verse itself does not condemn; the claim is the reader's reading of the report. The king is unnamed here; he is taken to be Yasomati by the story so far.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kodungol","vekuli","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 209","lb":"YA209","mm":1,"ev":"The king orders the merchants' goods and the buffalo taken, and his men plunder them.","cl":[["a king who answers one animal's act by plundering the merchants who owned it is not guarding his people","general","reported",2,"narrator"]],"ag":["இசோமதி","குழையாளர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ya:11"},"ya:210":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":210,"t":["அரச னாணை யறிந்தரு ளில்லவச்","சரண நான்கினை யுந்தனை செய்தனர்","கரண மானவை யாவுங் களைந்தனர்","அரண மாமற னில்லது தன்னையே."],"p":"","r":"The merciless men, knowing the king's order, bind the beast's four feet and strip it of all its means, leaving it with only dharma as its fortress.","n":"சரணம் நான்கு can be 'four feet' or the four refuges of Jain practice; the first is followed here, and the line 'only dharma is its fortress' may carry the second meaning.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kolai","idumbai","aram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 210","lb":"YA210","mm":1,"ev":"The king's men bind and disable the buffalo as ordered.","cl":[],"ag":["குழையாளர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ya:11"},"ya:211":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":211,"t":["கார நீரினிற் காய்ச்சி யுறுப்பரிந்","தார வூட்டி யதன்வயி றீர்ந்தவண்","சார நெய்பெய் சலாகை கடைந்தபின்","கூர்முண் மத்திகை யிற்கொலை செய்தனர்."],"p":"","r":"The buffalo is killed in stages: boiled in caustic water, cut apart, its belly emptied, a rod stirred through ghee, and at last it is finished with a thorn-sharp whip.","n":"The step-by-step account of the butchery is a Jain teaching device: the pain of the animal is set out so that the later eating of its flesh (212) is felt as cruelty.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kolai","kodungol","idumbai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 211","lb":"YA211","mm":1,"ev":"The king's men slaughter and prepare the buffalo with cruelty.","cl":[["the slaughter of a creature for the table is a long torment to it","jain","asserted",1,"narrator"]],"ag":["குழையாளர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ya:11"},"ya:212":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":212,"t":["ஆயி டைக்கொடி யாளமிர் தம்மதி","மேய மேதித் தசைமிக வெந்ததை","வாயின் வைத்து வயிற்றை வளர்தனள்","மாயை செய்தன ளென்றனர் மற்றையார்."],"p":"","r":"The queen, a vine of a woman named Amirthamathi, puts the well-cooked buffalo flesh in her mouth and fattens her belly; the others say she is working some illusion.","n":"The queen is Yasodharan's wife and, if the buffalo is the poisoned king reborn (205 and 221 suggest it), she is eating her victim; the verse does not say this, the sequel does.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pulaal","vinai","avaa"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 212","lb":"YA212","mm":1,"ev":"Amirthamathi eats the cooked flesh of the buffalo while others comment.","cl":[["eating the flesh of a slaughtered creature feeds only the body and brings its own fruit","jain","reported",3,"narrator"]],"ag":["அமிழ்தமுன் மதி","மற்றையார்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ya:11"},"ya:213":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":213,"t":["இன்னு மாசை யெனக்குள திவ்வழித்","துன்னி வாழ்தக ரொன்றுள தின்றது","தன்னி னாய குறங்கு கடித்து","தின்னி னாசை சிதைத்திடு மென்றனள்."],"p":"","r":"The queen says she still has hunger, that a ram lives close by, and that biting its thigh and eating it today would put her craving to an end.","n":"Her words show appetite growing as it is fed; the epic's own voice holds the opposite, that craving is not ended by eating.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["avaa","pulaal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 213","lb":"YA213","mm":1,"ev":"Amirthamathi asks for a ram to be killed so she may eat its thigh.","cl":[["eating the flesh of an animal ends craving","lokayata","reported",3,"amirthamathi"]],"ag":["அமிழ்தமுன் மதி"],"ec":[],"spk":["அமிழ்தமுன் மதி",""],"ch":"ya:11"},"ya:214":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":214,"t":["அனங்க னான பெருந்தகை யண்ணலைச்","சினங்கொ ளாவுயிர் செற்றனள் நஞ்சினில்","கனங்கொள் காமங் கலக்கக் கலந்தனள்","மனங்கொ ளாவொரு மானுட நாயினை."],"p":"","r":"The verse looks back: in anger she took the life of the great lord whose beauty matched Kama's, mixing his poison with her passion, and joined a man she had made a human dog.","n":"'Human dog' is the narrator's contempt for the man, not a name; the earlier chapters name him Ashtavakran (ch.6).","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kolai","kaamam","vanjam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 214","lb":"YA214","mm":1,"ev":"Recalled: Amirthamathi poisons her husband and takes the hunchback as her lover.","cl":[["desire joined to hatred can lead a wife to kill her husband","general","asserted",3,"narrator"]],"ag":["அமிழ்தமுன் மதி"],"ec":[],"ch":"ya:11"},"ya:215":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":215,"t":["குட்ட மாகிய மேனிக் குலமிலா","அட்ட பங்கனோ டாடி யமர்ந்தபின்","நட்ட மாகிய நல்லெழின் மேனியன்","குட்ட நோயிற் குளித்திடு கின்றநாள்."],"p":"","r":"After she had sported with the low-born hunchback of leprous body, a day comes when one of beautiful frame, now spoiled, is plunged in leprosy.","n":"மேனியன் in line 3 is masculine, while 216 uses feminine forms for the sufferer; the sense is taken as the queen, but the form could point at the hunchback.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["noy","kaamam","vinai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 215","lb":"YA215","mm":1,"ev":"Amirthamathi falls ill with leprosy after her years with Ashtavakran.","cl":[],"ag":["அமிழ்தமுன் மதி"],"ec":[],"ch":"ya:11"},"ya:216":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":216,"t":["அழுகி நைந்துட னஃகு மவயவத்","தொழுகி புண்ணி னுருவின ளாயினள்","முழுகு சீயின் முடைப்பொலி மேனியள்","தொழுவல் பல்பிணி நோய்களுந் துன்னினாள்."],"p":"","r":"Her limbs rot and wither, she oozes sores, flies crowd in the stench, and many more diseases gather upon her.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["noy","yaakkai","vinai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 216","lb":"YA216","mm":1,"ev":"Leprosy and other diseases ravage Amirthamathi's body.","cl":[],"ag":["அமிழ்தமுன் மதி"],"ec":[],"ch":"ya:11"},"ya:217":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":217,"t":["உம்மை வல்வினை யாலுணர் வொன்றிலாள்","இம்மைச் செய்த வினைப்பய னேயிவை","எம்மை யும்மினி நின்றிடு மிவ்வினை","பொய்ம்மை யன்றிவள் பொன்றினும் பொன்றல."],"p":"","r":"Those who see her say that she has no understanding by the strong karma of a former birth, that this is the fruit of what she did in this birth, and that this karma does not lie, and does not die even if she dies.","n":"The speakers are named in 219 only as those who said this behind her back; 'எம்மையும்' (us too) is hard to place.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vinai","oozh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 217","lb":"YA217","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["deeds of an earlier birth and of this one ripen on the doer","general","asserted",1,"attendant_women"],["karma does not perish with the body","general","asserted",4,"attendant_women"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["சேடியர்",""],"ch":"ya:11"},"ya:218":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":218,"t":["நோயி னாசைகொல் நுண்ணுணர் வின்மைகொல்","தீய வல்வினை தேடுத லேகொலோ","மேய மேதிப் பிணத்தை மிசைத்தனள்","மாய மற்றிது தன்னையும் வவ்வுமே."],"p":"","r":"They wonder whether it was craving for the disease, want of fine discernment, or the gathering of heavy evil karma that made her eat the dead buffalo, and say this delusion will seize her again.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","pulaal","avaa"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 218","lb":"YA218","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["craving and want of fine discernment lead one to gather evil karma","general","reported",2,"attendant_women"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["சேடியர்",""],"ch":"ya:11"},"ya:219":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":219,"t":["என்று தன்புறத் திப்படிக் கூறினர்","சென்று சேடியர் பற்றிய வத்தகர்","ஒன்று முற்ற வுணர்ந்தவள் தன்னையும்","சென்று கண்டது சிந்தையின் நொந்தரோ."],"p":"","r":"So they spoke behind her back; then one who fully understood it all went to see her, pained in mind.","n":"The word வத்தகர் is obscure and who the visitor is is not fixed by the Tamil; 220 and 221 let him be the man she poisoned.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["manam","idumbai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 219","lb":"YA219","mm":1,"ev":"Her attendants speak, and one who understood goes to see her.","cl":[],"ag":["சேடியர்","வத்தகர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ya:11"},"ya:220":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":220,"t":["தேவி யென்னை முனிந்தனை சென்றொரு","பாவி தன்னை மகிழ்ந்த பயன்கொலோ","பாவி நின்னுரு வின்னண மாயது","பாவி யென்னையும் பற்றினை யின்னணம்."],"p":"","r":"The visitor reproaches the queen: she was angry at him, she went and delighted in a sinner, and now her body is thus and she has seized him too.","n":"'Sinner' (பாவி) is used for her lover, for her, and for the speaker's own fate, so the verse is open to two readings of who has seized whom.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vinai","kutram","vekuli"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 220","lb":"YA220","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["delight in a sinner brings bodily ruin as its fruit","general","reported",2,"visitor"]],"ag":["வத்தகர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["வத்தகர்","அமிழ்தமுன் மதி"],"ch":"ya:11"},"ya:221":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":221,"t":["நஞ்சி லன்னையொ டென்னை நலிந்தனை","எஞ்ச லில்சின மின்ன மிறந்திலை","வஞ்ச னைமட வாய்மயி டம்மது","துஞ்சு நின்வயிற் றென்னையுஞ் சூழ்தியோ."],"p":"","r":"The visitor says she tormented him with his mother by poison, that her wrath has not ended though she is dying, and asks whether, with the buffalo lying in her belly, she will plot against him too.","n":"This is the verse that makes the visitor the poisoned king; the buffalo here is a third party, 'that buffalo' in her belly, so whether the buffalo is he or his mother is left open.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vekuli","kolai","vanjam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 221","lb":"YA221","mm":1,"ev":"The visitor accuses the queen of the poison and of the eaten buffalo.","cl":[],"ag":["வத்தகர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["வத்தகர்","அமிழ்தமுன் மதி"],"ch":"ya:11"},"ya:222":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":222,"t":["என்று கண்டு மொறுமொறுத் தென்செயும்","ஒன்று நெஞ்சம துள்சுட நின்றது","அன்று தேவி யலைப்ப வழிந்துயிர்","சென்ற தம்மயி டத்தொடு செல்கதி."],"p":"","r":"Seeing her so, he asks what reproach can do; one thing still burns in his heart, and the life undone by the queen's torment went on its way with the buffalo.","n":"Whether the life that goes with the buffalo is the queen's or the visitor's is not settled by the grammar.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vekuli","vinai","piravi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 222","lb":"YA222","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["reproach does not mend a deed already ripened","general","asserted",1,"narrator"]],"ag":["வத்தகர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ya:11"},"ya:223":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":223,"t":["மற்றம் மாநக ரத்து மருங்கினில்","சிற்றிற் பல்சனஞ் சேர்பறைச் சேரியின்","உற்று வாரணப் புள்ளுரு வாயின","வெற்றி வேலவன் காணவி ரும்பினான்."],"p":"","r":"In that great city, in a Paraiya street of small houses and many people, the soul takes the form of a cock, and the victorious spear-king wishes to see it.","n":"The street is named only as the place of the birth; the verse does not judge the birth by it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["piravi","vinai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 223","lb":"YA223","mm":1,"ev":"The soul is born as a cock and the king wants to see it.","cl":[],"ag":["வாரணப் புள்","இசோமதி"],"ec":[],"ch":"ya:11"},"ya:224":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":224,"t":["கண்டு மன்னவன் கண்களி கொண்டனன்","சண்ட கன்மியைத் தந்து வளர்க்கெனக்","கொண்டு போயவன் கூட்டுள் வளர்த்தனன்","மண்டு போர்வினை வல்லவு மாயவே."],"p":"","r":"The king is delighted at the sight and orders Chandakarmi to be given charge to raise it; he takes it to his cage and rears it, and it grows skilled in war.","n":"This Chandakarmi is a cock-keeper, whom the Tamil does not equate with the Chandakarman who brought the youths in ch.2 and 3.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["piravi","vali"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 224","lb":"YA224","mm":1,"ev":"The king has the cock reared by Chandakarmi, who trains it for fighting.","cl":[],"ag":["இசோமதி","சண்டகன்மி","வாரணப் புள்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ya:11"},"ya:225":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":225,"t":["தரள மாகிய நயனத்தொ டஞ்சிறை சாபம்போற் சிகியென்ன","மருளு மாகன சிகழிகைச் சுடர்க்கண மணிமுடி தனையொத்த","ஒளிரு பொன்னன சரணங்கள் வயிரமுள் ளொப்பில போரின்கண்","தளர்வில் வீரியந் தகைபெற வளர்ந்தன தமக்கிணை யவைதாமே."],"p":"","r":"The cock grows, its eyes like pearls, its wings like a bow, its crest bright like a gem-crown, its golden feet with diamond spurs matchless in battle, and its strength unflagging.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vali","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 225","lb":"YA225","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"ya:11"},"ya:226":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":226,"t":["செந்தளிர் புனைந்த சோலைத் திருமணி வண்டுந் தேனுங்","கொந்துகள் குடைந்து கூவுங் குயிலொடு குழுமி யார்ப்பச்","செந்துண ரளைந்து தென்றற் றிசைதிசை சென்று வீச","வந்துள மகிழ்ந்த தெங்கும் வளர் மதுப் பருவ மாதோ."],"p":"","r":"The grove with its red shoots, bees, honey and koel calls fills with the south wind, and the heart rejoices everywhere in the season of honey.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["paruvam","kaalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 226","lb":"YA226","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"ya:11"},"ya:227":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":227,"t":["இணர்தகைப் பொழிலி னுள்ளா விசோமதி யென்னு மன்னன்","வணர்தகைக் குழலி புட்பா வலியெனுந் துணைவி யோடு","புணர்தகை வல்லி புல்லி வளரிளம் பிண்டி வண்டர்","இணர்ததை தவிசி னேறி யினிதினி னமர்ந்தி ருந்தான்."],"p":"","r":"The king Yasomathi sits happily on a seat laid with young asoka blossoms in his garden, with his queen Pushpavali beside him, the creeper-like wife who clings to him.","n":"The king's name is spelled விசோமதி with an initial வி in the edition; it is read as the Yasomathi of the earlier chapters (இசோமதி).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","azhagu","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 227","lb":"YA227","mm":1,"ev":"King Yasomathi sits in his flower garden with Queen Pushpavali.","cl":[],"ag":["இசோமதி","புட்பாவலி"],"ec":[],"ch":"ya:12"},"ya:228":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":228,"t":["பாடக மிலங்கு செங்கேழ்ச் சீறடிப் பரவை யல்குற்","சூடக மணிமென் தோளின் தொழுதனர் துளங்கத் தோன்றி","நாடக மகளி ராடு நாடக நயந்து நல்லார்","பாடலி லமிர்த வூறல் பருகினன் மகிழ்ந்தி ருந்தான்."],"p":"","r":"Dancing women with ringed ankles and bangled arms bow and sway into view and dance, and the king, glad, drinks in their sweet song as though it were nectar.","n":"The scene is the pleasure of a king at ease; the next verse breaks it with the arrival of the ascetic's finder.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inbam","kaamam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 228","lb":"YA228","mm":1,"ev":"Dancers perform before the king, who delights in their song.","cl":[],"ag":["நாடக மகளிர்","இசோமதி"],"ec":[],"ch":"ya:12"},"ya:229":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":229,"t":["வளையவர் சூழ லுள்ளான் மனமகிழ்ந் திருப்ப மன்னன்","தளையவிழ் தொடையன் மார்பன் சண்டமுற் கருமன் போகி","வளமலர் வனத்துள் தீய மனிதரோ டனைய சாதி","களைபவன் கடவுள் கண்ணிற் கண்டுகை தொழுது நின்றான்."],"p":"","r":"While the king sits happy among his women, the king's punisher Chandakarman, who rids the land of thieves and such people, goes into the flowery grove and stands with folded hands before one he takes for a god.","n":"Chandakarman's office, one who removes the evil sort of people, is implied by the words அனைய சாதி களைபவன், which the verse does not explain; he is the king's executioner of thieves in the verses that follow.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kolai","vazhipaadu","panivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 229","lb":"YA229","mm":1,"ev":"Chandakarman goes into a flowering grove, sees a holy man and stands before him with folded hands.","cl":[],"ag":["சண்டகரும"],"ec":[],"ch":"ya:12"},"ya:230":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":230,"t":["அருவினை முனைகொ லாற்ற லகம்பன னென்னும் நாமத்","தொருமுனி தனியனாகி யொருசிறை யிருந்தோர் பிண்டித்","தருமுதல் யோகு கொண்டு தன்னள விறந்தான் முன்னர்","மருவிய நினைப்பு மாற்றி வந்தது கண்டி ருந்தான்."],"p":"","r":"There sits alone to one side under an asoka tree the ascetic Akampanan, who has gone beyond himself in deep concentration and is now returning from it, and Chandakarman watches.","n":"Lines 3 and 4 are hard: 'went beyond his own measure' and 'turned away his former thought and came back' are taken as the coming out of trance, which the Tamil does not say outright.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thavam","yogam","thiyaanam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 230","lb":"YA230","mm":1,"ev":"Chandakarman sees the ascetic Akampanan rise out of deep meditation beneath an asoka tree.","cl":[],"ag":["அகம்பனன்","சண்டகரும"],"ec":[],"ch":"ya:12"},"ya:231":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":231,"t":["வடிநுனைப் பகழி யானு மலரடி வணங்கி வாழ்த்தி","அடீகணி ரடங்கி மெய்யி லருள்புரி மனத்தீ ராகி","நெடிதுட னிருந்து நெஞ்சி னினைவதோர் நினைவு தன்னான்","முடிபொருள் தானு மென்கொல் மொழிந்தருள் செய்க என்றான்."],"p":"","r":"Chandakarman bows at the sage's feet, praises him, sits long with a quiet mind and asks him what final object it is that he has been fixing in his heart.","n":"Chandakarman is called the one with the sharp arrow (வடிநுனைப் பகழியான்), a hunter or killer by trade, who asks like a pupil.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["panivu","thavam","arivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 231","lb":"YA231","mm":1,"ev":"Chandakarman bows to Akampanan and asks what the sage's meditation was aimed at.","cl":[],"ag":["சண்டகரும"],"ec":[],"spk":["சண்டகரும","அகம்பனன்"],"ch":"ya:12"},"ya:232":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":232,"t":["ஆரருள் புரிந்த நிஞ்சி னம்முனி யவனை நோக்கிச்","சீரருள் பெருகும் பான்மைத் திறத்தனே போலு மென்றே","பேரறி வாகித் தம்மிற் பிறழ்விலா வுயிரை யன்றே","கூரறி வுடைய நீரார் குறிப்பது மனத்தி னாலே."],"p":"","r":"The sage looks at him, judges him fit to grow in grace, and answers that the keen-witted point to the soul, whole in great knowledge, by the mind.","n":"Whether the quoted words begin at line 2 or line 3 is not fixed by the edition; the sage's answer is taken from the closing lines.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["uyir","arivu","manam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 232","lb":"YA232","mm":1,"ev":"Akampanan looks at Chandakarman and begins to teach him about the soul.","cl":[["the soul is a being of great undivided knowledge, grasped by the mind","jain","asserted",3,"akampanan"]],"ag":["அகம்பனன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["அகம்பனன்","சண்டகரும"],"ch":"ya:12"},"ya:233":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":233,"t":["அனந்தமா மறிவு காட்சி யருவலி போக மாதி","நினைந்தவெண் குணங்க ளோடு திருமல நித்த மாகிச்","சினஞக்செறு வாதி யின்றித் திரிவித வுலகத் துச்சி","அனத்தகா லத்து நிற்ற லப்பொருட் டன்மை யென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"The sage says that the freed soul has endless knowledge, vision, strength and bliss among its eight qualities, is eternal and without stain, and stands for ever at the top of the three worlds; that is the soul's own nature.","n":"The verse names only the first four of the eight qualities. Nothing in it makes this state the gift of a god; the soul has it as its own nature once the stain is gone. The words திருமல நித்த மாகி are not sure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["veedu","uyir","aram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 233","lb":"YA233","mm":1,"ev":"Akampanan tells Chandakarman what the final state of the soul is.","cl":[["the freed soul has eight qualities, among them endless knowledge, vision, strength and bliss, and is eternal","jain","asserted",1,"akampanan"],["the freed soul stands for ever at the top of the three worlds, free of anger and the like","jain","asserted",3,"akampanan"]],"ag":["அகம்பனன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["அகம்பனன்","சண்டகரும"],"ch":"ya:12"},"ya:234":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":234,"t":["கருமனு மிறைவ கேளாய் களவுசெய் தோர்கள் தம்மை","இருபிள வாகச் செய்வ னெம்மர சருளி னாலே","ஒருவழி யாலுஞ் சீவ னுண்டெனக் கண்ட தில்லை","பெரியதோர் சோர மென்றான் பின்னமாச் சேதித் திட்டும்."],"p":"","r":"Chandakarman tells the sage that he has cut thieves in two by the king's order and nowhere found a life in them, which he takes for a great cheat.","n":"No school is named here; the claim is the materialist one that what cannot be seen on cutting does not exist, put by a man whose trade is killing. The sage answers it in verses 236 to 238.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["uyir","kolai","kalavu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 234","lb":"YA234","mm":1,"ev":"Chandakarman tells of cutting condemned thieves in two and finding no soul.","cl":[["a body cut in two shows no life, so no soul is to be found","lokayata","refuted",3,"chandakarman"]],"ag":["சண்டகரும"],"ec":[],"spk":["சண்டகரும","அகம்பனன்"],"ch":"ya:12"},"ya:235":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":235,"t":["மற்றொரு கள்வன் தன்னை வதைசெய்யு முன்னும் பின்னும்","இற்றென நிறைசெய் திட்டு மிறைவனே பேதங் காணேன்","உற்றதோர் குழியின் மூடி யொருவனைச் சிலநாள் வைத்து","மற்றவ னுயிர்போ யிட்ட வழியொன்றுங் கண்டி லேனே."],"p":"","r":"He adds that he weighed another thief before and after killing and saw no difference, and that when a man was shut in a pit for some days to die he saw no way by which a life went out.","n":"The speaker is known from verse 234. The two tests are the man's own, the first by scale and the second by sealing; the sage's reply in 237 takes up the breath and the conch.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["uyir","kolai","aaraaydhal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 235","lb":"YA235","mm":1,"ev":"Chandakarman describes weighing a thief before and after killing him, and burying another in a pit.","cl":[["the body weighs the same after death, so nothing has left it","lokayata","refuted",2,"chandakarman"],["a man shut in a pit shows no exit for a soul at death","lokayata","refuted",4,"chandakarman"]],"ag":["சண்டகரும"],"ec":[],"spk":["சண்டகரும","அகம்பனன்"],"ch":"ya:12"},"ya:236":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":236,"t":["பையவே காட்டத் தன்னைப் பலபின்னஞ் செய்திட் டன்று","வெய்யெரி கண்ட துண்டோ விறகொடு விறகை யூன்ற","ஐயனே யங்கி தோன்றி யதனையு மெரிக்க லுற்ற","திவ்வகைக் காண லாகு மென்றுநீ யுணர்தல் வேண்டும்."],"p":"","r":"The sage asks whether anyone ever found fire in a log by cutting it up, and says that when wood is rubbed on wood the fire appears and burns the wood; so what is real can show itself without being found by cutting.","n":"This is the first reply to 234. It argues from analogy and does not prove the soul, only that failure to find it by cutting proves nothing. Line 1 is hard.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["uyir","aaraaydhal","soozhchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 236","lb":"YA236","mm":1,"ev":"Akampanan answers Chandakarman with the fire hidden in wood.","cl":[["a thing absent from the cut pieces may still be real, as fire is in wood that shows it only when rubbed","jain","asserted",2,"akampanan"]],"ag":["அகம்பனன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["அகம்பனன்","சண்டகரும"],"ch":"ya:12"},"ya:237":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":237,"t":["சிக்கென வாயு வேற்றித் தித்திவாய்ச் செம்மித் தூக்கிப்","புக்கவவ் வாயு நீங்கிப் போயபின் னிறைசெய் தானும்","ஒக்குமே யொருவன் சங்கோ டொருநில மாளி கைக்கே","திக்கெனத் தொனிசெய் திட்ட தெவ்வழி வந்த தாகும்."],"p":"","r":"The sage says that when breath is blown into a conch and then gone, the one who blew it is as the blown breath was, and asks from where the sound that rang through the mansion came, when no cutting of the conch will show it.","n":"The second reply to 234 and 235. Lines 1 to 3 are corrupt or very compressed and the exact comparison is not fixed.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["uyir","aaraaydhal","naadham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 237","lb":"YA237","mm":1,"ev":"Akampanan answers the weighing and the pit with the sound of a conch.","cl":[["the weight of breath is not seen in the empty conch, so absence of a weight does not show that nothing left","jain","asserted",2,"akampanan"]],"ag":["அகம்பனன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["அகம்பனன்","சண்டகரும"],"ch":"ya:12"},"ya:238":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":238,"t":["இவ்வகை யாகுஞ் சீவ னியல்புதா னியல்பு வேறாம்","வெய்ய தீ வினைக ளாலே வெருவுறு துயரின் மூழ்கி","மையலுற் றழுங்கி நான்கு கதிகளுட் கெழுமிச் செல்வ","தையமில் காட்சி ஞானத் தொழுக்கத்தோ ரறிவ தாகும்."],"p":"","r":"The sage ends that the soul has a nature of its own, distinct from the body, which because of cruel deeds sinks in suffering through the four states of rebirth; this is what those of right faith, knowledge and conduct know.","n":"The earlier chapter gave the same teaching to the young ascetics at the place of killing (verses 49 and 50); here a murderer of thieves is the pupil.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["uyir","vinai","piravi","thelivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 238","lb":"YA238","mm":1,"ev":"Akampanan concludes his answer on the soul.","cl":[["the soul is by nature other than the body","jain","asserted",1,"akampanan"],["through evil deeds the soul is bound and wanders through the four states of birth","jain","asserted",2,"akampanan"],["the soul is known by those who hold right faith, knowledge and conduct","jain","asserted",4,"akampanan"]],"ag":["அகம்பனன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["அகம்பனன்","சண்டகரும"],"ch":"ya:12"},"ya:239":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":239,"t":["ஆகமத் தடிக ளெங்கட் கதுபெரி தரிது கண்டீர்","ஏகசித் தத்த ராய விறைவர்கட் கெளிது போலும்","போகசித் தத்தோ டொன்றிப் பொழிவழிப் படரு நீரார்க்","காகுமற் றுறுதிக் கேது அருளுக தெருள என்றான்."],"p":"","r":"Chandakarman says that the Agamas are great and hard for people like him, though easy for those lords of single mind, and asks what will serve for the final good of those who follow the stream of enjoyment.","n":"Chandakarman is called a man of mixed mind; his question is why the work gives a further, lighter way for householders in 240 to 244.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arivu","aram","payan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 239","lb":"YA239","mm":1,"ev":"Chandakarman asks for a way to the final good that a man of the world can follow.","cl":[["the scriptural way is hard for those who live by enjoyment and easy for single-minded ascetics","jain","reported",1,"chandakarman"]],"ag":["சண்டகரும"],"ec":[],"spk":["சண்டகரும","அகம்பனன்"],"ch":"ya:12"},"ya:240":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":240,"t":["அற்றமி லறிவு காட்சி யருந்தகை யொழுக்க மூன்றும்","பெற்றனர் புரிந்து பேணிப் பெருங்குணத் தொழுகு நீரார்க்","குற்றிடு மும்ப ரின்ப முலகிதற் கிறைமை தானும்","முற்றமுன் னுரைத்த பேறும் வந்துறும் முறைமை யென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"The sage says that for those who hold faultless knowledge, rare vision and rare conduct, all three, the fruit is heavenly joy, lordship in this world and the release named before.","n":"The three are not named in the verse by their later names; அறிவு, காட்சி and ஒழுக்கம் are 'knowledge', 'vision' and 'conduct'. Heaven and earthly power are given as by-fruits, with release the end.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arivu","thelivu","ozhukkam","veedu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 240","lb":"YA240","mm":1,"ev":"Akampanan names the three that lead to the final good.","cl":[["right knowledge, faith and conduct together bring heaven, rule on earth and release","jain","asserted",4,"akampanan"]],"ag":["அகம்பனன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["அகம்பனன்","சண்டகரும"],"ch":"ya:12"},"ya:241":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":241,"t":["உறுபொருள் நிலைமை தன்னை யுற்றுணர் வறிவ தாகும்","அறிபொரு ளதனிற் றூய்மை யகத்தெழு தெளிவு காட்சி","நறுமலர்ப் பிண்டி நாதன் நல்லறப் பெருமை தன்பால்","இறுகிய மகிழ்ச்சி கண்டாய் இதனது பிரிவு மென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"The sage defines knowledge as knowing real things as they are, vision as the clarity and purity that rises within, and the third as firm joy in the great dharma of the Lord of the asoka.","n":"The verse says 'these are its parts' of the third, joy in the Jina's dharma; knowledge and vision are given first. Lines 3 and 4 may name the same thing as faith or as a part of it.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["arivu","thelivu","meyyunarvu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 241","lb":"YA241","mm":1,"ev":"Akampanan defines the first two of the three.","cl":[["right knowledge is to know things as they are, and right faith is inner clarity and joy in the Jina's dharma","jain","asserted",1,"akampanan"]],"ag":["அகம்பனன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["அகம்பனன்","சண்டகரும"],"ch":"ya:12"},"ya:242":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":242,"t":["பெருகிய கொலையும் பொய்யுங் களவொடு பிறன்ம னைக்கண்","தெரிவிலாச் செலவு சிந்தை பொருள்வயிற் றிருகு பற்றும்","மருவிய மனத்து மீட்சி வதமிவை யைந்தோ டொன்றி","ஒருவின புலைசு தேன்கள் ஒருவுத லொழுக்க மென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"The sage says that conduct is turning the mind away from killing, lying, stealing, going unthinkingly to another's wife and tight clinging to wealth, together with giving up flesh, honey and toddy.","n":"புலைசு தேன்கள் is read as flesh, honey and toddy; the first word is odd.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["ozhukkam","kollaamai","pulaal","kal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 242","lb":"YA242","mm":1,"ev":"Akampanan lists the five vows and three abstentions that make up conduct.","cl":[["conduct is giving up killing, lying, stealing, desire for another's wife and clinging to wealth","jain","asserted",1,"akampanan"],["a follower also gives up flesh, honey and toddy","jain","asserted",4,"akampanan"]],"ag":["அகம்பனன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["அகம்பனன்","சண்டகரும"],"ch":"ya:12"},"ya:243":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":243,"t":["கொலையின தின்மை கூறிற் குவலயத் திறைமை செய்யும்","மலைதலில் வாய்மை யார்க்கும் வாய்மொழி மதிப்பை யாக்கும்","விலையில்பே ரருளின் மாட்சி விளைப்பது களவின் மீட்சி","உலைதலில் பெருமை திட்ப முருவலி யொழிந்ந தீயும்."],"p":"","r":"The sage says what each vow yields: not killing brings lordship, truth a respected word, giving up theft great and priceless grace and standing.","n":"These are worldly rewards put to a man who has just said he cannot follow the ascetic's road; they are offered to him as householder fruit. Lines 3 and 4 are obscure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kollaamai","vaaimai","kalavu","payan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 243","lb":"YA243","mm":1,"ev":"Akampanan lists the rewards of the first three vows.","cl":[["each vow brings its own worldly fruit: lordship for not killing, trusted speech for truth, great standing for not stealing","jain","asserted",1,"akampanan"]],"ag":["அகம்பனன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["அகம்பனன்","சண்டகரும"],"ch":"ya:12"},"ya:244":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":244,"t":["தெருளுடை மனத்திற் சென்ற தெளிந்துணர் வாய செல்வம்","பொருள்வயி னிறுக்க மின்மை புணர்த்திடும் புலைசு தேன்கள்","ஒருவிய பயனு மற்றே யொளியினோ டழகு வென்றி","பொருள்மிகு குலனோ டின்பம் புணர்தலு மாகு மாதோ."],"p":"","r":"The sage ends with the rest: wealth of clear mind and freedom from clinging to wealth, and giving up flesh, honey and toddy, which together bring radiance, beauty, victory, good birth and pleasure.","n":"The lines 1 and 2 do not say clearly which vow brings which fruit.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["ozhukkam","payan","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 244","lb":"YA244","mm":1,"ev":"Akampanan lists the remaining rewards of the vows.","cl":[["clear mind, non-clinging and abstaining from flesh, honey and toddy bring beauty, victory and good birth","jain","asserted",3,"akampanan"]],"ag":["அகம்பனன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["அகம்பனன்","சண்டகரும"],"ch":"ya:12"},"ya:245":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":245,"t":["சிலைபயில் வயிரத் தோளாய் செப்பிய பொருளி தெல்லாம்","உலைதலின் மகிழ்வோ டுள்ளத் துணர்ந்தனை கொள்க என்னக்","கொலையினி லொருவ லின்றிக் கொண்டனென் அருளிற் றெல்லாம்","அலைசெய்வ தொழியின் வாழ்க்கை யழியுமற் றடிக வென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"Bowing, the king answers the sage's teaching by saying he has taken in all he was told but cannot give up killing, since life would fall apart if the harassing stopped; the second line may still be the sage's words that he quotes.","n":"The speaking voices are tangled: line 1 and the start of line 2 address a 'diamond-shouldered' hearer and may be quoted from the sage, and the next verse's opening (என்றடி பணிந்து) shows that all of this is the king's answer. The sage's reply in 246 and 252 is what answers the claim here.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kollaamai","aram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 245","lb":"YA245","mm":1,"ev":"Yasomathi replies to the sage that he has accepted the teaching but not the giving up of killing.","cl":[["life and rule cannot go on if harming living things is given up","general","refuted",4,"yasomathi"]],"ag":["இசோமதி"],"ec":[],"spk":["இசோமதி","கடவுள்"],"ch":"ya:13"},"ya:246":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":246,"t":["என்றடி பணிந்து சண்ட விசைத்தது கடவுள் கேட்டு","நன்றினித் தெளிந்தா யல்லை நவிலிசை யமுத நல்யாழ்","ஒன்றிய செவிடு மூமும் ஒருவனாற் பெறுத லுண்டோ","இன்றுநீ யுரைத்த தற்றே யியம்புவ துளது கேண்மோ."],"p":"","r":"The sage, hearing him, says he has not understood well: a man deaf and dumb cannot get the nectar of music or a good lute, and what the king has said is just that.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kollaamai","arivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 246","lb":"YA246","mm":1,"ev":"The sage tells Yasomathi he has not truly understood and says more remains to be heard.","cl":[["assent to the teaching while still killing is not understanding it","jain","asserted",2,"muni"]],"ag":["இசோமதி","கடவுள்"],"ec":[],"spk":["கடவுள்","இசோமதி"],"ch":"ya:13"},"ya:247":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":247,"t":["ஆருயிர் வருத்தங் கண்டா லருள்பெரி தொழுகு கண்ணால்","ஒருயிர் போல நெஞ்சத் துருகிநைந் துய்ய நிற்றல்","வாரியின் வதங்கட் கெல்லா மரசநல் வதம தற்கே","சார்துணை யாகக் கொள்க தகவுமத் தயவு மென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"When one sees a living being's distress, the sage says, to melt and waste away in the heart as though the life were one's own is the support the king-vow of non-killing needs, with restraint and compassion as its companions.","n":"Line 3 reads 'among the sea-like vows, the kingly vow'; I take 'அரச நல் வதம்' as the chief of vows (non-killing) and 'அதற்கே' as pointing back to it, which the following verses support.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arul","kollaamai","aram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 247","lb":"YA247","mm":1,"ev":"The sage names compassion for a creature's pain, felt as one's own, as the base of the chief vow.","cl":[["pity that feels another's pain as one's own, with restraint, upholds the chief vow of not killing","jain","asserted",2,"muni"]],"ag":["கடவுள்"],"ec":[],"spk":["கடவுள்","இசோமதி"],"ch":"ya:13"},"ya:248":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":248,"t":["இறந்தநா ளென்னு முள்ளத் திரங்குத லின்றி வெய்தாய்க்","கறந்துயி ருண்டு கன்றிக் கருவினை பெருகச் செய்தாய்","பிறந்துநீ பிறவி தோறும் பெருநவை யுறுவ தெல்லாஞ்","சிறந்தநல் லறத்தி னன்றித் தீருமா றுணரி னுண்டோ."],"p":"","r":"The sage tells the king that, lacking pity, he cruelly drained and took life from a cock, swelling karma, and that the great suffering he gets in birth after birth could end by no means except good dharma.","n":"Lines 1 and 2 are hard (the 'day that is gone' and the milking-and-eating image); the sage seems to be speaking of the earlier killing of the flour cock, and the reading rests on 250 and 251.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vinai","piravi","aram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 248","lb":"YA248","mm":1,"ev":"The sage tells Yasomathi that a killing without pity has made karma swell and that only dharma can end its fruit.","cl":[["killing without pity swells karma, and the suffering it brings in later births ends only through good dharma","jain","asserted",3,"muni"]],"ag":["கடவுள்"],"ec":[],"spk":["கடவுள்","இசோமதி"],"ch":"ya:13"},"ya:249":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":249,"t":["நிலையிலா வுடம்பின் வாழ்க்கை நெடிதுட னிறுவ லென்றிக்","கொலையினான் முயன்று வாழுங் கொற்றவ ரேனு முற்றச்","சிலபக லன்றி நின்றார் சிலரிவ ணில்லை கண்டாய்","அலைதரு பிறவி முந்நீ ரழுந்துவ ரனந்தங் காலம்."],"p":"","r":"Even kings who live by killing, thinking to keep this impermanent body long, have stood only a few days, the sage says, and they sink in the sea of rebirth for endless time.","n":"The argument is that killing to prolong life defeats itself because the body does not last and the killing makes the next birth worse; it answers the king's claim in 245 from the body's brief span.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","piravi","kolai","yaakkai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 249","lb":"YA249","mm":1,"ev":"The sage says that kings who kill to keep the body last only a few days and then sink in rebirth.","cl":[["the body does not last, so killing to prolong life is futile and leads to endless rebirth","jain","asserted",3,"muni"]],"ag":["கடவுள்"],"ec":[],"spk":["கடவுள்","இசோமதி"],"ch":"ya:13"},"ya:250":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":250,"t":["இன்னுமீ தைய கேட்க யசோமதி தந்தை யாய","மன்னவ னன்னை யோடு மாவினற் கோழி தன்னைக்","கொன்னவில் வாளிற் கொன்ற கொடுமையிற் கடிய துன்பம்","பின்னவர் பிறவி தோறும் பெற்றன பேச லாமோ."],"p":"","r":"Listen further, the sage says: the king who was Yasomathi's father, together with his mother, killed a cock of flour with a sword and then suffered in every birth a pain worse than the cruelty of the killing, which cannot be told.","n":"The cock was of flour (மாவினால்), not a living bird; the sage counts the intent of killing as the deed (see 252). The king named here is Yasodharan of the earlier chapters and the mother Chandramathi.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","piravi","kolai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 250","lb":"YA250","mm":1,"ev":"The sage tells Yasomathi that his father and grandmother, who killed the flour cock, have suffered in every birth since.","cl":[["a killing, even of a lifeless likeness, brings suffering in every following birth","jain","asserted",3,"muni"]],"ag":["யசோதரன்","சந்திர மதி"],"ec":[],"spk":["கடவுள்","இசோமதி"],"ch":"ya:13"},"ya:251":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":251,"t":["வீங்கிய வினைகள் தம்மால் வெருவரத் தக்க துன்பம்","தாங்கினர் பிறந்தி றந்து தளர்ந்தனர் விலங்கிற் செல்வார்","ஆங்கவர் தாங்கள் கண்டா யருவினை துரப்ப வந்தார்","ஈங்குநின் னயல கூட்டி லிருந்தகோ ழிகளா யென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"Swollen by their karma, they bore fearful pain and wore down in birth and death, going into animal births; the sage says they are the cocks sitting in the cage near the king.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","piravi","uyir"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 251","lb":"YA251","mm":1,"ev":"The sage tells Yasomathi that the two cocks in the cage beside him are his father and grandmother reborn.","cl":[["deeds that cannot be turned aside drive beings through births, including animal births","jain","asserted",2,"muni"]],"ag":["கடவுள்"],"ec":[],"spk":["கடவுள்","இசோமதி"],"ch":"ya:13"},"ya:252":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":252,"t":["உயிரவ ணில்லை யேனு முயிர்க்கொலை நினைப்பி னாலிம்","மயரிகள் பிறவி தோறும் வருந்திய வருத்தங் கண்டால்","உயிரினி லருளொன் றின்றி யுவந்தனர் கொன்று சென்றார்","செயிர்தரு நரகி னல்லாற் செல்லிட மில்லை யென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"Although the flour cock held no life, the sage says, the thought of killing a life made these deluded ones suffer in every birth, and those who killed without pity for life have no refuge but the fierce hell.","n":"Intent is made the measure of the deed: no life was taken, yet the fruit is as if one had been. This is the sage's final answer to the king's claim in 245.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kollaamai","naragam","manam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 252","lb":"YA252","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["the intent to kill, even where no life is taken, bears the fruit of killing","jain","asserted",1,"muni"],["those who kill without pity for life go to hell","jain","asserted",3,"muni"]],"ag":["கடவுள்"],"ec":[],"spk":["கடவுள்","இசோமதி"],"ch":"ya:13"},"ya:253":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":253,"t":["மற்றவன் இனைய கூற மனநனி கலங்கி வாடிச்","செற்றமுஞ் சினமு நீக்கித் திருவறத் தெளிவு காதல்","பற்றினன் வதங்கள் முன்னம் பகர்ந்தன வனைத்துங் கொண்டு","பெற்றன னடிகள் நும்பாற் பெரும்பய னென்று போந்தான்."],"p":"","r":"Hearing this, Yasomathi is shaken and grieves, puts away his hatred and anger, loves the clear holy dharma, takes the vows told before, and leaves saying he has won great fruit from the lords.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aram","vekuli","arul"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 253","lb":"YA253","mm":1,"ev":"Yasomathi gives up anger, takes the vows and leaves, grateful to the sage.","cl":[],"ag":["கடவுள்","இசோமதி"],"ec":[],"ch":"ya:13"},"ya:254":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":254,"t":["கேட்டலு மடிகள் வாயிற் கெழுமிய மொழிகள் தம்மைக்","கூட்டினு ளிருந்த மற்றக் கோழிகள் பிறப்பு ணர்ந்திட்","டோட்டிய சினத்த வாகி யுறுவத முய்ந்து கொண்ட","பாட்டருந் தன்மைத் தன்றே பான்மையிற் பரிசு தானும்"],"p":"","r":"The cocks in the cage, hearing the words that came from the lord's mouth, learn of their births and take up the vows in anger left behind; the sentence is left open for the next verse.","n":"The verse ends mid-sentence (the last line of 254 is its own clause that is closed in 255).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["piravi","ninaivu","aram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 254","lb":"YA254","mm":1,"ev":"The two cocks hear the sage and, learning of their births, take the vows.","cl":[],"ag":["கடவுள்","கோழிகள்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ya:13"},"ya:255":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":255,"t":["பிறவிக ளனைத்து நெஞ்சிற் பெயர்ந்தன நினைத்து முன்னர்","மறவியின் மயங்கி மாற்றின் மறுகின மருகு சென்றே","அறவிறை யடிகள் தம்மா லறவுமிர் தாரப் பெற்றேம்","பிறவியின் மறுகு வெந்நோய் பிழைத்தன மென்றவாறே."],"p":"","r":"All their births pass through their hearts, once clouded by forgetfulness; going near, they say that by the lord of dharma they have been given the nectar of dharma and have escaped the hot disease of rebirth.","n":"That the cocks speak is my reading of lines 3 and 4 (அறவுமிர்தாரப் பெற்றேம், 'we have received'), which could also be the poet's voice for them.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["piravi","aram","noy"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 255","lb":"YA255","mm":1,"ev":"The two cocks recall their past births and give thanks for the teaching that frees them from rebirth.","cl":[["the nectar of dharma from the teacher frees from the fever of rebirth","jain","reported",3,"kozhikal"]],"ag":["கோழிகள்"],"ec":[],"spk":["கோழிகள்",""],"ch":"ya:13"},"ya:256":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":256,"t":["அறிவரன் சரண மூழ்கி யறத்தெழு விருப்ப முள்ளாக்","குறைவிலா வமுதங் கொண்டு குளிர்ந்தக மகிழ்ந்து கூவச்","செறிபொழி லதனுட் சென்று செவியினு ளிசைப்ப மன்னன்","முறுவல்கொண் முகத்து நல்லார் முகத்தொரு சிலைவ ளைத்தான்."],"p":"","r":"Having bathed in the sage's feet, the cocks drink the full nectar of dharma and crow gladly in the grove, and the king, hearing it, bends a bow with a face that smiles beside the faces of fair women.","n":"The last line is obscure (the bow bent 'with the face of fair women') and leaves it unclear whom the king aims at.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["aram","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 256","lb":"YA256","mm":1,"ev":"The cocks crow in the garden and the king bends his bow.","cl":[],"ag":["கோழிகள்","இசோமதி"],"ec":[],"ch":"ya:13"},"ya:257":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":257,"t":["சொல்லறி கணையை வாங்கித் தொடுத்தவன் விடுத்த லோடும்","நல்லிறைப் பறவை தம்மை நடுக்கிய தடுத்து வீழச்","சில்லறி வினைக ளேனுந் திருவறப் பெருமை யாலே","வல்லிதின் மறைந்து போகி மானுடம் பாய வன்றே"],"p":"","r":"The king takes the knowing arrow and looses it, and the good birds shake and fall; yet by the greatness of the holy dharma their small karmas hide away and they pass into human birth.","n":"The poem does not explain how Yasomathi, who has just taken the vows in 253, looses the arrow; the verse stresses that the dharma the cocks took turns the killing into a rebirth as human beings. The verse ends without a full stop in the edition.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kolai","vinai","piravi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 257","lb":"YA257","mm":1,"ev":"Yasomathi's arrow strikes the cocks, which die and are reborn as humans.","cl":[["the greatness of dharma hides even a being's small karmas and carries it into a human birth","jain","asserted",3,"narrator"]],"ag":["இசோமதி","கோழிகள்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ya:13"},"ya:258":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":258,"t":["விரைசெறி பொழிலி னுள்ளால் வேனலின் விளைந்த எல்லாம்","அரசனு மமர்ந்து போகி யகநகர்க் கோயி லெய்தி","முரசொலி கழுமப் புக்கு மொய்ம்மலி குழலி னாரோ","டுரைசெய லரிய வண்ண முவகையின் மூழ்கி னானே."],"p":"","r":"The king goes through the scented grove, then back into the palace to the sound of drums, and sinks into untellable delight among women with fragrant hair.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inbam","selvam","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 258","lb":"YA258","mm":1,"ev":"Yasomathi returns to his palace and gives himself to pleasure.","cl":[],"ag":["இசோமதி"],"ec":[],"ch":"ya:13"},"ya:259":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":259,"t":["இன்னண மரச செல்வத் திசோமதி செல்லு நாளுள்","பொன்னிய லணிகொள் புட்பா வலியெனும் பொங்கு கொங்கை","இன்னிய லிரட்டை யாகு மிளையரை யீன்று சின்னாள்","பின்னுமோர் சிறுவன் தன்னைப் பெற்றனள் பேதை தானே."],"p":"","r":"As Yasomathi lives on in royal wealth, Pushpavali bears twins and, after a few days, another son.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["makkal","arasu","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 259","lb":"YA259","mm":1,"ev":"Pushpavali bears Yasomathi twins and then a third son.","cl":[],"ag":["இசோமதி","புட்பாவலி"],"ec":[],"ch":"ya:13"},"ya:260":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":260,"t":["அன்னவர் தம்முள் மன்னோ னபயமுன் னுருசி தங்கை","அன்னமென் னடையி னாளு மபயமுன் மதியென் பாளாம்","பின்னவர் வளரு நாளுட் பிறந்தவ னிறங்கொள் பைந்தார்","இன்னிளங் குமர னாம மிசோதர னென்ப தாகும்."],"p":"","r":"Of the twins, the boy is Abhayaruchi and the girl is Abhayamati, and the son born after them is named Yasodharan.","n":"The verse does not say that the twins are the cocks reborn; that follows from 257 and from the youth's own words (73), which this link rests on. The younger brother bears the name of his grandfather the king.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["makkal","piravi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 260","lb":"YA260","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"ya:13"},"ya:261":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":261,"t":["பரிமிசைப் படைப பியின்றும் பார்மிசைத் தேர்க டாயும்","வரிசையிற் கரிமேற் கொண்டு வாட்டோழில் பயின்று மன்னர்க்","குரியஅத் தொழில்க ளோடு கலைகளின் செலவை யோர்ந்தும்","அரசிளங் குமரன் சென்றா னடுத்தது கூற லுற்றேன்."],"p":"","r":"The young prince grows up and learns the arts of horse, chariot and elephant and the work fit for kings, together with the sciences, and the poet says he will tell what came next.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalvi","makkal","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 261","lb":"YA261","mm":1,"ev":"The youngest prince is trained in arms and learning.","cl":[],"ag":["இசோதரன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ya:13"},"ya:262":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":262,"t":["நூற்படு வலைப்பொறி முதற்கருவி நூற்றோ","டேற்றுடை யெயிற்றுஞம லிக்குல மிரைப்ப","நாற்படை நடுக்கடல் நடுச்செய்நம னேபோல்","வேற்படை பிடித்தரசன் வேட்டையின் விரைந்தான்."],"p":"","r":"With nets, snares and a hundred other hunting devices, and packs of tusk-toothed hounds baying, the king takes up his spear and speeds to the hunt in the midst of his four-fold army, like Death himself in the middle of a sea.","n":"The simile makes the king Death (நமன்) before he has met the ascetic of the next verses; the poet loads the hunt with the image of killing.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","kolai","kootru"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 262","lb":"YA262","mm":1,"ev":"The king sets out at speed on a hunt with his army, hounds and nets.","cl":[],"ag":["அரசன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ya:14"},"ya:263":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":263,"t":["இதத்தினை யுயிர்க்கினி தவித்திடு மியற்கைச்","சுதத்தமுனி தொத்திரு விளைத்துகள் துடைக்கும்","மதத்தயன் மதக்களி றெனப்படிம நிற்பக்","கதத்தின னிழித்தடு சுடத்திடை மடுத்தான்."],"p":"","r":"The ascetic Sudatta, whose nature is to give living beings good, and who wipes away the dust of the two clinging kinds of deed, stands in his vow-posture; the king, proud and raging like a rutting elephant, thrusts in on him.","n":"Line 2 reads தொத்திருவிளைத்துகள்; the sense is தொத்து இருவினைத் துகள் (clinging dust of the two deeds), so விளை stands for வினை. The last line, with இழித்து and அடுசுடத்திடை, is hard: whether the king dismounts, reviles, or sets something on the ascetic cannot be fixed, so the reading keeps to 'confronts'.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thavam","vekuli","kodungol"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 263","lb":"YA263","mm":1,"ev":"The raging king confronts the standing ascetic Sudatta.","cl":[["the ascetic wipes away the dust of the two kinds of deed (good and bad) that cling to the soul","jain","asserted",2,"narrator"]],"ag":["அரசன்","சுதத்தன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ya:14"},"ya:264":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":264,"t":["கூற்றமென அடவிபுடை தடவியுயிர் கோறற்","கேற்றபடி பெற்றதில னிற்றைவினை முற்றும்","பாற்றியவ னின்னுயிர் பறிப்பலென வந்தான்","மாற்றரிய சீற்றமொடு மாதவனின் மேலே."],"p":"","r":"Like Death combing the forest's edge, he has found nothing to kill that fits today, and so, saying he will end his whole business and take the sweet life of the one before him, he comes with unstoppable rage upon the great ascetic.","n":"வினை முற்றும் பாற்றி may mean 'ending his whole day's labour' (the hunt) or, as a pun, 'wiping out [his] deeds'; the verse is ironical either way, since the ascetic is the one who wipes deeds away (263).","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vekuli","kolai","vinai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 264","lb":"YA264","mm":1,"ev":"Finding no game, the king turns his killing intent on the ascetic.","cl":[],"ag":["அரசன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ya:14"},"ya:265":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":265,"t":["கொந்தெரி யுமிழ்ந்தெதிர் குரைத்ததிர்வ கோணாய்","ஐந்தினொடு பொருததொகை யைம்பதி னிரட்டி","செந்தசைகள் சென்றுகவர் கின்றென விடுத்தான்","நந்தியருள் மழைபொழியும் நாதனவன் மேலே."],"p":"","r":"He looses at the lord, from whom a rain of grace pours, a pack of fire-breathing, barking hounds, as many as fifty doubled with five, as though they were going to seize red flesh.","n":"The count (ஐந்தினொடு பொருத தொகை ஐம்பதின் இரட்டி) is read as about a hundred and five; the wording is loose. The contrast is the poem's: the one who rains grace is met with hounds sent for flesh.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kolai","arul","kodungol"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 265","lb":"YA265","mm":1,"ev":"The king sets his pack of hounds on the ascetic.","cl":[],"ag":["அரசன்","கோணாய்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ya:14"},"ya:266":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":266,"t":["அறப்பெருமை செய்தருள் தவப்பெருமை தன்னால்","உறப்புணர்த லஞ்சியொரு விற்கணவை நிற்பக்","கறுப்புடை மனத்தெழு கதத்தரச னையோ","மறப்படை விடக்கருதி வாளுருவு கின்றான்."],"p":"","r":"By the power of the penance that does great dharma the hounds fear to touch him and stand off at a bow's distance; the king, black-hearted and swollen with rage, then thinks of loosing the cruel weapon himself and draws his sword.","n":"The grammar of lines 2 and 3 is broken. The reading rests on 279, where the merchant says the hounds do not come near, and so on the hounds standing off; it is not certain that the verse says the hounds are what stand.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thavam","vekuli","kodungol"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 266","lb":"YA266","mm":1,"ev":"The hounds are held back by the ascetic's penance, and the king draws his sword.","cl":[],"ag":["அரசன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ya:14"},"ya:267":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":267,"t":["காளைதகு கலியாண மித்திர னெனும்பேர்","ஆளியடு திறல்வணிக னரசனுயி ரனைய","கேளொருவன் வந்திடைபு குந்தரச கெட்டேன்","வாளுருவு கின்றதெனை மாதவன்மு னென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"A strong merchant named Kalyanamitran, dear as the king's own life, comes between and cries that he is undone, for a sword is being drawn before the great ascetic.","n":"எனை (line 4) can read as 'me': that the blade is drawn on him as he steps between, or that the king's sword, drawn at the ascetic, ruins the merchant too. Kalyanamitran is named only here in the chapter and the verse calls him a kinsman of the king, not his minister.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["natpu","arasu","acham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 267","lb":"YA267","mm":1,"ev":"The merchant Kalyanamitran enters and protests the drawn sword.","cl":[],"ag":["கலியாண மித்திரன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["கலியாண மித்திரன்","அரசன்"],"ch":"ya:14"},"ya:268":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":268,"t":["வெறுத்துடன் விடுத்தரசி னைத்துக ளெனப்பேர்","அறப்பெரு மலைப்பொறை யெடுத்தவ னடிக்கண்","சிறப்பினை யியற்றலை சினத்தெரி மனத்தான்","மறப்படை யெடுப்பதெவென் மாலைமற வேலோய்."],"p":"","r":"Cast off this rage, he says; instead of honouring the feet of one who bears the mountain-load of great dharma, why does a mind burning with anger take up a cruel weapon, O king of the garland and the fierce spear?","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aram","vekuli","thavam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 268","lb":"YA268","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["one who bears the great burden of dharma is to be honoured, and not attacked","general","asserted",2,"kalyanamitran"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["கலியாண மித்திரன்","அரசன்"],"ch":"ya:14"},"ya:269":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":269,"t":["ஆகவெனின் ஆகுமிவர் அழிகவெனின் அழியும்","மேகமிவண் வருகவெனின் வருமதுவும் விதியின்","ஏகமன ராம்முனிவர் பெருமையிது வாகும்","மாகமழை வண்கைமத யானை மணிமார்போய்"],"p":"","r":"If they say 'let it be', it is; if they say 'let them be destroyed', they are; if they say 'let the cloud come here', it comes, and that too by fate: such is the greatness of the one-minded ascetics, O lord with the cloud-generous hand and the elephant-like heart.","n":"விதியின் (line 2) makes the ascetic's power run through fate (விதி); it is not clear whether the ascetic's will is itself fate or whether fate is what lets the will take effect. The verse does not say the ascetic works by a god.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thavam","perumai","oozh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 269","lb":"YA269","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["the will of one-minded ascetics is fulfilled, even to bringing the rain-cloud","general","asserted",1,"kalyanamitran"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["கலியாண மித்திரன்","அரசன்"],"ch":"ya:14"},"ya:270":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":270,"t":["அடைந்தவர்கள் காதலினொ டமரரர சாவர்","கடந்தவர்கள் தமதிகழ்வின் கடைநரகின் வீழ்வர்","அடைந்தநிழல் போலருளு முனிவுமில ரடிகள்","கடந்ததிவ ணுலகியல்பு கடவுளர் செயலே."],"p":"","r":"Those who come to them with love become lords of the gods, those who pass beyond them in contempt fall to the lowest hell; the venerable ones are like shade that one takes refuge in, with neither favour nor anger, and the course of the world beyond is the doing of the divine ones.","n":"The fruit of honour and scorn falls on the doer, yet the ascetics themselves are said to hold neither favour nor wrath (அருளும் முனிவும் இலர்); the verse does not say who dispenses the result.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thavam","naragam","vinai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 270","lb":"YA270","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["those who honour ascetics become lords of the gods and those who scorn them fall to the lowest hell","jain","asserted",1,"kalyanamitran"],["the holy ones are without favour or anger, like shade that shelters whoever comes","jain","asserted",3,"kalyanamitran"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["கலியாண மித்திரன்","அரசன்"],"ch":"ya:14"},"ya:271":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":271,"t":["இந்திரர்கள் வந்தடிப ணிந்தருளு கெனினும்","சிந்தையிலர் வெந்துயர்க ளெண்ணிலகள் செயினும்","தத்தம்வினை யென்றுநமர் பிறரெனவு நினையார்","அந்தரமி கந்தருள்த வத்தரசர் தாரோய்."],"p":"","r":"Even if Indras come to bow at their feet, or others inflict endless bitter pain, they do not think 'ours' or 'others', only 'each one's own deed', these ascetic kings who have risen far above the world, O wearer of the garland.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","porai","thavam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 271","lb":"YA271","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["suffering inflicted by another is the sufferer's own deed come back, and the ascetic sees no 'ours' or 'others'","jain","asserted",3,"kalyanamitran"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["கலியாண மித்திரன்","அரசன்"],"ch":"ya:14"},"ya:272":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":272,"t":["இவ்வலகி னெவ்வுயிரு மெம்முயிரி னேரென்","றவ்விய மகன்றருள்சு ரந்துயிர் வளர்க்கும்","விமையின் நின்றவர்தி ருந்தடி படிந்துன்","வினைக்க டந்தனைவி ளங்குவிறல் வேலோய்."],"p":"","r":"Every life in the world is like my own life, they hold; so, putting away cruelty and pouring grace, they raise up lives; bow at the holy feet of those who stand guard like the eyelid, and you will cross beyond your deeds, O victorious spear-bearer.","n":"இமையின் in line 3 is read as 'like the eyelid' (which guards the eye); the text spells it விமையின், a possible misprint.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["arul","uyir","vinai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 272","lb":"YA272","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["every life is equal to one's own life","jain","asserted",1,"kalyanamitran"],["bowing at the feet of the ascetics who guard lives carries one beyond one's deeds","jain","asserted",4,"kalyanamitran"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["கலியாண மித்திரன்","அரசன்"],"ch":"ya:14"},"ya:273":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":273,"t":["என்றினிது கூறும்வணி கன்சொலிக ழாதே","கன்றுசின முங்கரத லப்படையு மாற்றி","இன்றிவனை யென்னைதொழு மாறளிய னியாவன்","கன்றுதுகள் துன்றுகரு மேனியின னென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"The king, not rejecting the merchant's gentle words, lays down his anger and the weapon in his hand, then asks who this pitiable man is that he, the king, should bow to, a dark-skinned man thick with dust.","n":"He yields to the plea, not to the ascetic: the weapon goes down while contempt remains, so his turn to reverence is not yet made (283).","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vekuli","manam","thavam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 273","lb":"YA273","mm":1,"ev":"The king puts aside his anger and weapon but still scorns the dust-grimed ascetic.","cl":[["a man grimed with dust is not worth the king's bowing","general","reported",3,"arasan"]],"ag":["அரசன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["அரசன்",""],"ch":"ya:14"},"ya:274":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":274,"t":["இங்கலகு தொழுமுனியை யாவெனெனி னிதுகேள்","கங்கைகுல திலகனிவன் கலிங்கபதி யதனைப்","பொங்குபுய வலியில்பொது வின்றிமுழு தாண்ட","சிங்கமிவ னென்றுதெளி தேந்துணரின் வேந்தே."],"p":"","r":"If you ask whom the world worships, hear: he is the ornament of the Ganga line, lord of Kalinga, who ruled the whole land alone like a lion with no shared strength of arm; know this clearly, king.","n":"The ascetic is not named in these lines; that he is Sudatta (263) is from the context. கங்கைகுல திலகன் is the Ganga dynasty of Kalinga, a claim about a person the verse does not date.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kudi","arasu","perumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 274","lb":"YA274","mm":1,"ev":"The merchant tells the king that the ascetic was once a king of Kalinga.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["கலியாண மித்திரன்","அரசன்"],"ch":"ya:14"},"ya:275":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":275,"t":["மேகமென மின்னுமென வில்லுமென வல்லே","போகமொடு பொருளிளமை பொன்றுநனி யென்றே","ஆகதுற வருள்பெருகு மறெனெடத னியலே","போகமிகு பொன்னுலகு புகுவனென நினைவான்."],"p":"","r":"Thinking that pleasure, wealth and youth pass quickly like cloud, lightning and the rainbow, he resolves on renunciation, the dharma in which grace grows, and sets his mind on entering the pleasure-rich golden world.","n":"Line 3 is damaged (ஆகதுற வருள்பெருகு மறெனெடத னியலே). The aim named in line 4 is the heaven of pleasures, not release; compare 284, where the fruit is a divine world.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","thuravu","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 275","lb":"YA275","mm":1,"ev":"The king of Kalinga reasons that enjoyment is fleeting and resolves to renounce.","cl":[["pleasure, wealth and youth are as fleeting as cloud, lightning and the rainbow","general","asserted",1,"narrator"]],"ag":["சுதத்தன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ya:14"},"ya:276":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":276,"t":["நாடுநக ரங்களு நலங்கொள்மட வாரும்","ஆடுகொடி யானை அதிர் தேர் புரவி காலாள்","சூடுமுடி மாலைகுழை தோள்வளையொ டாரம்","ஆடைமுத லாயினவொ டகல்கஎன விட்டான்."],"p":"","r":"Lands and cities, the women of fair loveliness, banner-waving elephants, chariots, horses and foot soldiers, crown, garland, earrings, shoulder-bangles, necklace and clothes: 'let all go away from me', he said, and gave them up.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thuravu","selvam","yaakkai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 276","lb":"YA276","mm":1,"ev":"Sudatta gives up kingdom, women, army, ornaments and clothes.","cl":[],"ag":["சுதத்தன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ya:14"},"ya:277":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":277,"t":["வானவரு மண்ணின்மிசை யரசர்களு மலைமேல்","தானவரும் வந்துதொழு தவவுருவு கொண்டான்","ஊனமன மின்றியுயிர் கட்குறுதி யுன்னிக்","கானமலை நாடுகள்க லந்துதிரி தின்றான்."],"p":"","r":"Gods, kings of the earth and the mountain dwellers come to worship him as he takes the ascetic form, and with no taint in his mind, thinking only of what is good for lives, he wanders through forest, hills and lands.","n":"Gods and kings worship him now, as the merchant said at 270 of those who come near.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thavam","arul","thuravu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 277","lb":"YA277","mm":1,"ev":"Sudatta takes the ascetic form and wanders the forests while gods and kings worship him.","cl":[],"ag":["சுதத்தன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ya:14"},"ya:278":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":278,"t":["யானுமல தெனதுமல திதமுமல தென்னும்","மானமுடை மாதவனின் மேனிமகி ழானாய்","ஏனைவினை மாசுதன துருவினிறு வாதே","ஞானவொளி நகைசெய்குணம் நாளுமணி கின்றான்."],"p":"","r":"Holding that 'I' is nothing, 'mine' is nothing and even benefit is nothing, the dignified ascetic delights not in his body; the dirt of other deeds does not stay in his form, and each day he wears the virtues that smile with the light of knowledge.","n":"இதம் (benefit) is added to the pair 'I' and 'mine': he gives up even the good he might claim for himself, which goes past the Kural's pair.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pattru","thuravu","yaakkai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 278","lb":"YA278","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["the ascetic holds that neither 'I' nor 'mine' nor even benefit is real","general","asserted",1,"narrator"]],"ag":["சுதத்தன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ya:14"},"ya:279":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":279,"t":["ஈடின்முனி யோகினது பெருமை யிஃதிறைவ","காடுபடு கொலையினொடு கடியவினை நின்னைக்","கூடுவதொ ழிந்ததுகொள் இன்றுகொலை வேலோய்","நாடுவதென் ஞமலி யிவை நணுகலகண் காணாய்"],"p":"","r":"This is the greatness of the matchless ascetic's yoga, lord: the killing deed of the forest and the fierce karma that were to join you have ceased to join you today; why seek killing, spear-bearer? See, the hounds do not come near.","n":"The deed is said to have stopped through the ascetic's greatness, which reads the stopping of karma as brought about by the ascetic's yoga; the verse does not say how.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kolai","vinai","thavam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 279","lb":"YA279","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["the killing deed that was about to bind the king has been stopped before it joined him","jain","asserted",2,"kalyanamitran"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["கலியாண மித்திரன்","அரசன்"],"ch":"ya:14"},"ya:280":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":280,"t":["என்றவ னுளங்கொள வியம்பின னியம்பச்","சென்றுதிரு வடிமலர்கள் சென்னிமிசை யணியா","இன்றெனது பிழைதணிய என்றலை யரிந்தே","நின்றமுனி சரணிடுவ லென்றுநினை கின்றான்."],"p":"","r":"When the merchant has said this, to move his mind, the king goes, sets the holy lotus feet on his head, and thinks: 'to dissolve my fault today I will cut off my head and stand in refuge at his feet.'","n":"The king's own measure of the fault is a life; the merchant answers it in 281 and 282.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["manam","vinai","panivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 280","lb":"YA280","mm":1,"ev":"The king resolves to cut off his own head as atonement.","cl":[["a fault against an ascetic is wiped out by giving one's head at his feet","general","reported",3,"arasan"]],"ag":["அரசன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ya:14"},"ya:281":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":281,"t":["இன்னது நினைந்ததிவ னின்றுகை யெடுத்தே","மன்னநின் மனத்தது விடுந்திடு மனத்தில்","தன்னுயிரின் மன்னுயிர் வளர்க்கைதக வானால்","நின்னுயிரை நீகளையி னின்னருள தென்னாம்."],"p":"","r":"Seeing him think this, the merchant raises his hand and says: let go what is in your mind, king; if one raises others' lives as one's own life, that is right; if you take your own life, what grace is that?","n":"The same word for life, உயிர், covers both: the verse extends the rule that protects others' lives to the king's own.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arul","uyir","kollaamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 281","lb":"YA281","mm":1,"ev":"The merchant stops the king from cutting off his own head.","cl":[["it is right to nourish others' lives like one's own, and destroying one's own life is not grace","jain","asserted",3,"kalyanamitran"]],"ag":["கலியாண மித்திரன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["கலியாண மித்திரன்","அரசன்"],"ch":"ya:14"},"ya:282":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":282,"t":["முன்னமுரை செய்தபொருள் முடிந்திலது முடியப்","பின்னுமிகை பிறவுமுள பேசுதிற நினைவுந்","துன்னுவயின் முன்னிது துணிந்துபிழை தூராய்","பின்னுநினை கின்றவிது பீழைபெரி தென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"The matter he spoke before is not finished; more remains to be said, and the sense of what he thinks to say now comes to him: having decided this earlier, do not fill up the fault; what you now think of is a great harm, he said.","n":"Lines 1 to 3 are very hard; பிழைதூராய் may mean 'do not heap fault' or 'do not leave the fault undone'. Only the last line, that the new plan is a great harm, is certain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["manam","kutram","kollaamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 282","lb":"YA282","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["கலியாண மித்திரன்","அரசன்"],"ch":"ya:14"},"ya:283":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":283,"t":["மன்னவன் மனத்தை விடுத்தருள் வளர்க்குஞ்","சொன்னவில் சுதத்தமுனி துணைமல ரடிக்கண்","சென்னிமுடி துன்னுமலர் சென்றுற வணங்கிப்","பன்னியரு விறைவவெமர் பவமுழுது மென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"Giving up the king's rage and growing in grace, he goes to the feet of Sudatta, whose speech is full of eloquence, bows so that the flowers of his crown touch them, and speaks of all of their births.","n":"The last line is cut short: எமர் பவமுழுதும் is read as asking to hear the whole of their births, which the muni's knowledge in 284 can supply. He has gone from the weapon of 266 to the crown-flowers on the feet, and it is the merchant, not the ascetic, who brought this about.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["panivu","piravi","arul"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 283","lb":"YA283","mm":1,"ev":"The king bows at Sudatta's feet and asks him of their births.","cl":[],"ag":["அரசன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["அரசன்","சுதத்தன்"],"ch":"ya:14"},"ya:284":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":284,"t":["ஆங்குமுனி யவதியி னறிந்தபொரு ளதனை","வாங்கியவ னுணரும்வகை வைத்தருள்செய் கின்றான்","ஈங்குமு னியற்றிய தவத்தினி னசோகன்","ஓங்குபுக ழமருலக மொன்றினு ளுவந்தான்."],"p":"","r":"Then the ascetic, setting out by his avadhi knowledge what it had shown him so that the king could take it in, tells that Asokan, by the penance he did before, rejoiced in a divine world of high fame.","n":"Whether this Asokan is Yasodharan's father of ch. 5 is not stated in the verse; same name is not shown to be same person. அவதி is the clairvoyant knowledge of the Jain ascetic, here used for knowledge of former births.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["avathi","thavam","piravi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 284","lb":"YA284","mm":1,"ev":"Sudatta begins to reveal by his avadhi knowledge that Asokan reached a heaven through earlier penance.","cl":[["the ascetic's earlier penance brings rebirth in a divine world","jain","asserted",3,"narrator"]],"ag":["சுதத்தன்","அசோகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ya:14"},"ya:285":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":285,"t":["அருமணியி னொளிதிகழு மமரனவ னாகிப்","பிரமனுல கதனுண்மிகை பெறுகடல்கள் பத்தும்","திருமணிய துணைமுலைய தெய்வமட வாரோ","டருமையில னகமகிழ்வி னமருமவன் மாதோ."],"p":"","r":"The muni says that the king's father, Asokan, is now a god shining like a rare gem in Brahma's world, glad among divine women for more than ten oceans of years.","n":"The verse says only 'he'; the father is read from verse 284, where Asokan reaches a world of the gods. The muni speaking, and the king he addresses as 'you', are not named in this chapter: Sudattan is the muni of verse 283 and the king is read as Yasomati, since verses 298 and 299 call the twins his son and daughter.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thavam","marumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 285","lb":"YA285","mm":1,"ev":"Asokan, who took renunciation, enjoys a god's life in Brahma's world.","cl":[["austerity taken at the white hair ripens into a long life among the gods","jain","asserted",3,"sudattan"]],"ag":["அசோகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சுதத்தன்","இசோமதி"],"ch":"ya:15"},"ya:286":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":286,"t":["வஞ்சனையி லன்னையுடன் மன்னவனை நஞ்சின்","துஞ்சும்வகை சூழ்ந்துதொழு நோய்முழுது மாகி","அஞ்சின்மொழி யமிர்தமதி யருநரகின் வீழ்ந்தாள்","நஞ்சனைய வினைநலிய நாமநகை வேலோய்."],"p":"","r":"The muni says that the queen Amritamati, who with the old mother plotted by guile to kill the king with poison, became wholly a leper and fell into dire hell, her deeds like poison consuming her.","n":"The verse shows the punishment answering the crime: she planned his death by poison and her deeds are called 'poison-like' that wear her down.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vanjam","vinai","naragam","noy"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 286","lb":"YA286","mm":1,"ev":"Amritamati, who plotted the king's poisoning with his mother, is struck with leprosy and falls into hell.","cl":[["the wife who plots her husband's death is repaid in disease and hell","jain","asserted",3,"sudattan"]],"ag":["அமிழ்தமுன் மதி","சந்திர மதி"],"ec":[],"spk":["சுதத்தன்","இசோமதி"],"ch":"ya:15"},"ya:287":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":287,"t":["இருளினிரு ளிருள்புகையொ டளறுமணல் பரலின்","மருள்செயுரு வினபொருளின் வருபெயரு மவையே","வெருள்செய்வினை தருதுயரம் விளையுநில மிசைப்பத்","தெருளியெழு வகைநரக குழிகளிவை தாரோய்."],"p":"","r":"The muni lists the seven pits of hell by their grounds: the darkness of darkness, darkness, smoke, mire, sand and gravel, each place bringing the pain its deeds earn.","n":"Only six grounds can be read in the lines; the seventh name is not clear in the edition.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naragam","vinai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 287","lb":"YA287","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["deeds ripen in hells of seven kinds, each named for its ground and its pain","jain","asserted",4,"sudattan"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["சுதத்தன்","இசோமதி"],"ch":"ya:15"},"ya:288":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":288,"t":["மேருகிரி யுய்த்திடினும் வெப்பமொடு தட்ப","நீரெனவு ருக்கிடுநி லப்புரைய வைந்தாம்","ஒரினுறு புகைநரகி னுருகியுடன் வீழ்ந்தாள்","ஆருமில ளறனுமில ளமிர்தமதி யவளே."],"p":"","r":"The muni says five of the hells have ground that would melt even Mount Meru as hot and cold water, and that Amritamati fell into the smoke hell, where she has no helper and no dharma.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naragam","vinai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 288","lb":"YA288","mm":1,"ev":"Amritamati falls and melts into the smoke hell.","cl":[["in hell neither any companion nor dharma stands by the sufferer","jain","asserted",4,"sudattan"]],"ag":["அமிழ்தமுன் மதி"],"ec":[],"spk":["சுதத்தன்","இசோமதி"],"ch":"ya:15"},"ya:289":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":289,"t":["ஆழ்ந்தகுழி வீழ்ந்தபொழு தருநரக ரோடிச்","சூழ்ந்துதுகை யாவெரியு ளிட்டனர்கள் சுட்டார்","போழ்ந்தனர்கள் புண்பெருக வன்றறிபு டைத்தார்","மூழ்ந்தவினை முனியுமெனின் முனியலரு முளரோ."],"p":"","r":"Hell's dwellers run up as she falls into the pit, set her in smoke and fire, burn and split her and grind her bleeding wounds; the verse asks whether any who face a deed's wrath would not themselves be wrathful.","n":"Line 4 is hard to construe. 'If the sunk deed is angry, are there any who are not angry' may mean that no one can be gentle toward her when her own deed is angry with her.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["naragam","vinai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 289","lb":"YA289","mm":1,"ev":"The beings of hell burn, split and grind the fallen Amritamati.","cl":[],"ag":["நரகர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சுதத்தன்","இசோமதி"],"ch":"ya:15"},"ya:290":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":290,"t":["செந்தசைகள் வெந்தனகன் தின்றனைமு னென்றே","கொந்தழலில் வெந்துகொது கொதுவெனகொ திக்கும்","செந்தழலி னிந்திரர்கள் செம்புகள் திணிப்ப","வெந்தழலி னைந்துருகி விண்டொழுகு முகனே."],"p":"","r":"Hell's keepers cry that she had eaten cooked flesh before and now force molten copper into her mouth until her face melts and runs.","n":"The reproach 'you ate' is voiced by the tormentors; it is the muni's account, which the work holds, that makes the punishment the answer to the deed. The word இந்திரர்கள் here names the tormentors, as written.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pulaal","naragam","vinai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 290","lb":"YA290","mm":1,"ev":"The keepers of hell pour molten copper into Amritamati's mouth, reproaching her for eating flesh.","cl":[["eating the flesh of living beings is repaid in hell by being made to swallow what burns","jain","asserted",1,"sudattan"]],"ag":["நரகர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சுதத்தன்","இசோமதி"],"ch":"ya:15"},"ya:291":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":291,"t":["கருககருக ரிந்தனள் கவின்கொளொரு பாவை","பெருகெரியி னிட்டுருக விதுபெரிது மினிதென்","றருகணைய நுதந்துதலு மலறியது தழுவிப்","பொருபொருபொ ரிந்துபொடி யாமுடல மெல்லாம்."],"p":"","r":"She is burnt to crisp, and a fair image is heated in the fire; they say 'this is very sweet', bid her embrace it, and she cries out as her whole body crumbles to powder.","n":"The scorching image set against her love is not spelled out as the elephant-keeper, and the speaker of 'this is very sweet' is not named.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","naragam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 291","lb":"YA291","mm":1,"ev":"Amritamati is made to embrace a heated image and her body crumbles.","cl":[],"ag":["அமிழ்தமுன் மதி"],"ec":[],"spk":["சுதத்தன்","இசோமதி"],"ch":"ya:15"},"ya:292":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":292,"t":["நாவழுகி வீழமுழு நஞ்சுண மடுத்தார்","ஆவலறி யதுவுருகி யலமரினு மன்னோ","சாவவரி திவணரசி தகவில்வினை தருநோய்","யாவும்விளை நிலமதனி லினியவுள வாமோ."],"p":"","r":"They pour poison down her until her tongue rots, and she melts in her thirst but cannot die; in a land where blameless deeds bear no fruit, nothing sweet can grow.","n":"The poison she had prepared for the king is returned to her mouth.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naragam","kootru","vinai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 292","lb":"YA292","mm":1,"ev":"The tormentors force poison on Amritamati, who burns without dying.","cl":[["in hell the sufferer cannot die, however the body melts","jain","asserted",3,"sudattan"]],"ag":["நரகர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சுதத்தன்","இசோமதி"],"ch":"ya:15"},"ya:293":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":293,"t":["முன்னைநுமர் தந்தசை முனிந்திலை நுகர்த்தாய்க்","கின்றுமினி துன்னவய வங்கள்தின லென்றே","தன்னவய வம்பலத டிந்துழல வைத்து","தின்னியென நொந்தவைகள் தின்னுமிகை திறலோய்."],"p":"","r":"They tell her that she did not shun the flesh her kin once gave, and now bid her eat her own limbs; they cut them off and call her glutton as she wanders in pain.","n":"The reproach names flesh eaten from 'your kin' of an earlier birth, so the verse joins eating flesh with the earlier life's deeds.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pulaal","vinai","naragam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 293","lb":"YA293","mm":1,"ev":"The tormentors cut off Amritamati's limbs and make her eat them.","cl":[["flesh eaten from others is paid back by eating one's own flesh","jain","asserted",3,"sudattan"]],"ag":["நரகர்","அமிழ்தமுன் மதி"],"ec":[],"spk":["சுதத்தன்","இசோமதி"],"ch":"ya:15"},"ya:294":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":294,"t":["திலப்பொறியி லிட்டனர் திரிப்புவு நெருப்பின்","உலைப்பெரு கழற்றலை யுருக்கவு முருத்துக்","கொலைக்கழுவி லிட்டனர் குலைப்பவு முழக்கும்","உலைப்பருவ ருத்தம துரைப்பரிது கண்டாய்."],"p":"","r":"They press her in a sesame mill, roast her on the fire of the furnace, set her on the killing stake and shake and crush her; the muni says the pain of the furnace cannot be told.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naragam","idumbai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 294","lb":"YA294","mm":1,"ev":"The tormentors mill, roast and impale Amritamati.","cl":[],"ag":["நரகர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சுதத்தன்","இசோமதி"],"ch":"ya:15"},"ya:295":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":295,"t":["ஒருபதினொ டொருபதினை யுந்தியத னும்பர்","இருபதினொ டைந்தினி லுயர்ந்தபுகை யென்னும்","பொருவரிய துயரினவை பொங்கியுடன் வீழும்","ஒருபதினொ டெழுகடல்க ளளவுமொளி தாரோய்."],"p":"","r":"The muni reckons that the smoke hell's torments, placed over ten and twenty-five and beyond, fall on her for ten and seven oceans of years.","n":"The arithmetic of lines 1 to 3 is hard to resolve; line 4 gives the term as ten and seven oceans. Set against the ten oceans of Asokan's heaven in verse 285, the wife's term is longer.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["naragam","kaalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 295","lb":"YA295","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["the term of hell is measured in oceans of years","jain","asserted",4,"sudattan"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["சுதத்தன்","இசோமதி"],"ch":"ya:15"},"ya:296":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":296,"t":["தொல்லைவினை நின்றுசுடு கின்றநர கத்துள்","அல்லலிவை யல்லனவு மமிழ்தமதி யுறுவள்","எல்லையில யிதுவிதென வெண்ணியொரு நாவிற்","சொல்லவுல வாவொழிக சுடருநெடு முடியோய்."],"p":"","r":"The muni says that Amritamati has not yet suffered all: an old deed stands and burns her in that hell, with other pains besides, and one tongue cannot tell them in one telling.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","naragam","oozh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 296","lb":"YA296","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["an old deed stays and burns in hell until it is spent","jain","asserted",1,"sudattan"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["சுதத்தன்","இசோமதி"],"ch":"ya:15"},"ya:297":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":297,"t":["எண்ணமி லிசோதரனொ டன்னையிவர் முன்னாள்","கண்ணிய வுயிர்க்கொலை வினைக்கொடுமை யாலே","நண்ணிய விலங்கிடை நடுங்கஞர் தொடர்ந்த","வண்ணமிது வடிவமிவை வளரொளிய பூணோய்."],"p":"","r":"The muni turns to Yasodharan and his mother: through the cruelty of their earlier deed of taking life they fell into animal births and long trembling pain, and he begins to name them.","n":"The verse names the cause as உயிர்க்கொலை வினைக்கொடுமை but not the act itself; the earlier chapters of the story supply it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kollaamai","vinai","piravi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 297","lb":"YA297","mm":1,"ev":"Yasodharan and his mother pass into animal births in return for killing.","cl":[["the killing of living beings brings the killer into animal births with long pain","jain","asserted",2,"sudattan"]],"ag":["யசோதரன்","சந்திர மதி"],"ec":[],"spk":["சுதத்தன்","இசோமதி"],"ch":"ya:15"},"ya:298":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":298,"t":["மன்னன் மயிலாய்மயிரி முள்ளெயின மீனாய்","பின்னிருமு றைத்தகரு மாகியவ னேகி","மன்னுசிறை வாரணம தாகிவத மருவி","மன்னவநின் மகனபய னாகிவளர் கின்றான்."],"p":"","r":"The muni says the former king became a peacock, a thorn-toothed beast, a fish and twice a goat, then a caged cock that took the vow, and now grows up as the king's own son Abhaya.","n":"The first beasts are hard to name: மயிரி and முள்ளெயின are rare words. The speaker is told 'your son Abhaya', so the king addressed is the twins' father.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["piravi","nonbu","vinai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 298","lb":"YA298","mm":1,"ev":"Yasodharan is named through his animal births up to birth as Abhaya.","cl":[["a vow taken even in an animal's body leads to a human birth","jain","asserted",3,"sudattan"]],"ag":["யசோதரன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சுதத்தன்","இசோமதி"],"ch":"ya:15"},"ya:299":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":299,"t":["சந்திரமுன் மதிஞமலி நாகமொ டிடங்கர்","வந்துமறி மயிடமுடன் வாரணமு மாகி","முந்தைவினை நெகிழமுனி மொழியும்வத மருவி","வந்துன்மக ளபயமதி யாகிவளர் கின்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"The muni says the mother took the forms of a dog, a snake, a crocodile, a goat, a buffalo and a cock, and when her old deeds loosened and she took the vow the muni taught, she grows as the king's daughter Abhayamati.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["piravi","nonbu","vinai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 299","lb":"YA299","mm":1,"ev":"Chandramathi is named through her animal births up to birth as Abhayamati.","cl":[["old deeds loosen when the vow taught by the muni is taken","jain","asserted",3,"sudattan"]],"ag":["சந்திர மதி"],"ec":[],"spk":["சுதத்தன்","இசோமதி"],"ch":"ya:15"},"ya:300":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":300,"t":["இதுநுமர்கள் பவவினைதன் விளைவுமியல் பிதுவென்","றெதுவின்முனி யருளுமொழி யவையவைகள் நினையா","விதுவிதுவி திர்த்தகநெ கிழ்ந்து மிகச் சோரா","மதுமலர்கொள் மணிமுடிய மன்னவன் மருண்டான்."],"p":"","r":"As the muni tells that this is the fruit of his kin's deeds, the king weighs each point, his mind melts and he is bewildered.","n":"Lines 1 to 3 are partly obscure in the edition; the king's state at line 4 is clear.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","manam","piravi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 300","lb":"YA300","mm":1,"ev":"The king, hearing the muni's account of his kin's births, is bewildered.","cl":[],"ag":["இசோமதி"],"ec":[],"ch":"ya:15"},"ya:301":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":301,"t":["இன்னவகை மன்னன்முனி யியம்பியது கேளாத்","தன்னருகு நின்றவொரு சண்டனை விடுப்ப","மன்னபய வுருசியொடு மதியவளும் வந்தே","சொன்னவி லருட்குரவன் துணையடி பணிந்தார்."],"p":"","r":"The king, having heard the muni, sends the man at his side to fetch Abhayaruchi and Abhayamati, and they come and bow at the feet of the gracious guru.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["guru","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 301","lb":"YA301","mm":1,"ev":"The king sends for the twins, who come and bow to the muni.","cl":[],"ag":["இசோமதி","சண்டன்","அபயருசி","அபயமதி"],"ec":[],"ch":"ya:15"},"ya:302":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":302,"t":["ஆங்கபய வுருசியுட னபயமதி தானும்","தாங்கலர்கள் நின்றுதவ அரசனரு ளாலே","நீங்கியப வங்களை நினைந்தன ருணர்ந்தார்","ஆங்கவர்க ளுறுகவலை யாவர்பிற ரறிவார்."],"p":"","r":"Abhayaruchi and Abhayamati stand unable to bear it, and by a king's grace they remember and understand the births they had left behind; who else can tell what grief they bear.","n":"Whose grace gives the memory (அரசன் அருளால்) is not clear: the king, or the muni spoken of as a lord of ascetics.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["piravi","ninaivu","vinai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 302","lb":"YA302","mm":1,"ev":"The twins recall their past births.","cl":[],"ag":["அபயருசி","அபயமதி"],"ec":[],"ch":"ya:15"},"ya:303":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":303,"t":["மக்களு ளிரட்டை யாக மாறினம் பிறந்த யாமுன்","மிக்கதீ வினையா லுற்ற விளைவினை யுணர்ந்தே மைய","துக்கமே தொடர நோற்றுத் துணையறந் துறந்த பெற்றி","இக்கதி துன்னிக் கண்டே மினிக்கதிக் கென்செய் வோமே."],"p":"","r":"The twins say that, born as a pair among men, they have understood the fruit of their former evil deed, that they gave up the dharma that was their companion while enduring pain, and ask what they should do for the birth to come.","n":"The twins speak together; the word ஐய addresses the muni.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["piravi","vinai","thuravu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 303","lb":"YA303","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["the fruit of a former evil deed follows through births","jain","asserted",2,"ilainjar"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["இளைஞர்","சுதத்தன்"],"ch":"ya:15"},"ya:304":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":304,"t":["தந்தையுந் தந்த தாயு மாகிய தழுவு காதல்","மைந்தனு மடந்தை தானு மாற்றிடைச் சுழன்ற பெற்றி","சிந்தையி னினைந்து நொந்து தேம்பினர் புலம்பக் கண்டு","கொந்தெரி யழலுள் வீழ்ந்த கொள்கைய னானான் மன்னன்."],"p":"","r":"The father, the mother, the loved son and the daughter have turned in the stream of births; the twins brood on this and weep, and the king, seeing them lament, is as one fallen in blazing fire.","n":"The relations named (father, mother, son, daughter) are those the earlier verses give to Yasodharan, Chandramathi and the king's children.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["piravi","sutram","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 304","lb":"YA304","mm":1,"ev":"The twins weep over the kin they have been to one another, and the king is struck.","cl":[],"ag":["அபயருசி","அபயமதி","இசோமதி"],"ec":[],"ch":"ya:15"},"ya:305":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":305,"t":["எந்தையும் எந்தை தாயும் எய்திய பிறவி தோறும்","வெந்துயர் விளைவு செய்த வினையினே னென்செய் கேனோ","அந்தமி லுயிர்கண் மாய வலைபல செய்து நாளும்","வெந்துயர் நரகின் வீழ்க்கும் வினைசெய்தே னென்செய் கேனோ."],"p":"","r":"The king cries that in every birth his father and mother reached he gave them hard pain, that he has done deeds that cast life after life into hell, and asks what he is to do.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","naragam","piravi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 305","lb":"YA305","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["deeds that wear down living beings cast the doer into hell","jain","asserted",3,"yasomathi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["இசோமதி","சுதத்தன்"],"ch":"ya:15"},"ya:306":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":306,"t":["அருளொடு படர்தல் செய்யா தாருயிர்க் கழிவு செய்தே","பொருளொடு போக மேவிப் பொறியிலே னென்செய் கேனோ","அருளின துருவ மாய வடிகணும் மடிகட் கேயுந்","தெருளலன் நினைந்த தீமைச் சிறியனே னென்செய் கேனோ."],"p":"","r":"The king laments that he took life with no mercy, followed wealth and pleasure, understood nothing even about those with a merciful form, and asks what he is to do.","n":"The phrase அருளொடு படர்தல் செய்யாது, 'going forth without mercy', names the lack as mercy rather than ignorance.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arul","vinai","kollaamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 306","lb":"YA306","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["wealth and pleasure sought without mercy, at the cost of lives, bring ruin","jain","asserted",1,"yasomathi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["இசோமதி","சுதத்தன்"],"ch":"ya:15"},"ya:307":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":307,"t":["மாவியல் வடிவு தன்னை வதைசெய்தார் வண்ண மீதேல்","ஆவினி யளிய னேதும் அஞ்சிலே னவதி யென்கொல்","காவல வருளு கென்னக் கலங்கின னரசன் வீழ","மாவல அஞ்ச லென்றம் மாதவ னுரைவ ளர்த்தான்."],"p":"","r":"Saying that if those who killed a form of flour fare so, he too, who feared nothing, must have an end, the king begs protection and falls trembling; the great ascetic tells him to fear not and goes on with his teaching.","n":"மாவியல் வடிவு, read as a form made of flour, recalls the offering of the dough figure; it is not said in these lines whose death it was.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["acham","arul","vinai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 307","lb":"YA307","mm":1,"ev":"The king falls trembling and begs protection, and the muni bids him not fear and continues.","cl":[],"ag":["இசோமதி","சுதத்தன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["இசோமதி","சுதத்தன்"],"ch":"ya:15"},"ya:308":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":308,"t":["அறிவில ராய காலத் தறிவில செய்த எல்லாம்","நெறியினி லறிவ தூற நின்றனர் விலகி நிற்பர்","அறியலர் வினைக ளாலே யருநவை படுநர் கையாற்","சிறியநல் வதங்கள் செய்தே தீவினை யகல்வர் காணாய்"],"p":"","r":"The speaker tells the king that those who know the way keep away from the wrongs done in the age of ignorance, while the unknowing are bound by their deeds and yet shed evil by keeping small good vows.","n":"The speaker is not named; he is read as the youth who has been teaching the king since verse 66, and the one addressed as 'you' (காணாய்) as the king. 'Small vows' (சிறிய நல் வதங்கள்) are the lay vows, set below the ascetic's great vows.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vinai","aram","arivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 308","lb":"YA308","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["those who do not know suffer the faults of their own deeds, yet can put off evil deeds by keeping small good vows","jain","asserted",4,"abhayaruchi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["அபயருசி","மாரிதத்தன்"],"ch":"ya:16"},"ya:309":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":309,"t":["அருள்புரி மனத்த ராகி யாருயிர்க் கபய நல்கிப்","பொருள்கொலை களவு காமம் பொய்யிவை புறக்க ணித்திட்","டிருள்புரி வினைகள் சேரா திறைவன தறத்தை யெய்தின்","மருள்செய வருவதுண்டோ வானவ ரின்ப மல்லால்."],"p":"","r":"With a merciful mind give safety to every dear life, put aside taking life, theft, lust, lying and possession, and dark deeds will not gather; reaching the Lord's dharma, what comes to confuse one but heaven's joy?","n":"'பொருள்' in line 2 is read as the fifth vow, hold on possessions; the verse lists five things given up.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kollaamai","arul","aram","vinai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 309","lb":"YA309","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["giving safety to all lives and abstaining from killing, theft, lust, lying and possession keeps dark karma from joining","jain","asserted",2,"abhayaruchi"],["the fruit of keeping the Lord's dharma is the joy of the gods' world","jain","asserted",4,"abhayaruchi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["அபயருசி","மாரிதத்தன்"],"ch":"ya:16"},"ya:310":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":310,"t":["என்றலு மடிகள் பாதத் தெழின்முடி மலர்கள் சிந்தக்","கன்றிய வினைகள் தீரக் கருணையி னுருகி நெஞ்சிற்","சென்றன னறிவு காட்சித் திருவறத் தொருவ னானான்","வென்றவர் சரண டைந்தார் விளைப்பது வென்றி யன்றோ."],"p":"","r":"At this the king bows so that flowers fall from his crown at the sage's feet, his hard deeds loosen, his heart melts in pity and he becomes one with the holy dharma of knowledge and faith; the verse closes that those who take refuge with the victors reap victory.","n":"அறிவு காட்சி (knowledge and faith) names two of the three jewels of the Jain path; the verse does not name the third.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thuravu","vinai","arul","arivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 310","lb":"YA310","mm":1,"ev":"The king bows at the youth's feet and takes up the Jain faith.","cl":[["those who take refuge with the victors win victory","jain","asserted",4,"narrator"]],"ag":["மாரிதத்தன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ya:16"},"ya:311":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":311,"t":["வெருள்செயும் வினைகள் தம்மை வெருவிய மனத்த னாகி","மருள்செயு முருவ மாட்சி மகனொடு மங்கை தன்னை","அருள்பெரு குவகை தன்னா லமைவில னளிய னும்மைத்","தெருளலன் முன்பு செய்த சிறுமைகள் பொறுக்க என்றான்."],"p":"","r":"Frightened of his terrifying deeds, the king looks with great joy at the youth and the maiden, and says he did not know them and is pitiable, and asks them to forgive his earlier wrongs.","n":"The verse speaks of 'smaller wrongs' (சிறுமைகள்) as the king's own account of ordering the offering; the verse does not itself name the offering.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vinai","pazhi","panivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 311","lb":"YA311","mm":1,"ev":"The king asks the twin ascetics to forgive what he did to them earlier.","cl":[],"ag":["மாரிதத்தன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["மாரிதத்தன்","அபயருசி"],"ch":"ya:16"},"ya:312":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":312,"t":["ஓருயிர்த் தோழ னாகி யுறுதிசூழ் வணிகன் றன்னை","ஆருயிர்க் கரண மாய அடிகளோ டைய நீயும்","நேரெனக் கிறைவ னாக நினைவலென் றினிய கூறிப்","பாரியற் பொறையை நெஞ்சிற் பரிந்தனன் மன்னன் மன்னோ."],"p":"","r":"The king says to the merchant, his one-life friend and counsellor, that with the lord who is the refuge of dear lives he will think of him also as his equal master, and so speaking sweetly feels forbearance for the earth's burden.","n":"Whether 'the lord' of line 2 is the youth or the teacher Sudattan is not fixed by the verse; the speaker is read as the king by the sequence.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["natpu","porai","panivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 312","lb":"YA312","mm":1,"ev":"The king addresses his merchant friend as an equal and takes up forbearance.","cl":[],"ag":["மாரிதத்தன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["மாரிதத்தன்","வணிகன்"],"ch":"ya:16"},"ya:313":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":313,"t":["மணிமுடி மகனுக் கீந்த மன்னவன் தன்னோ டேனை","அணிமுடி யரசர் தாமு மவனுயிர்த் துணைவ னான","வணிகனு மற்று ளாரு மாதவத் திறையை வாழ்த்தித்","துணிவினர் துறந்து மூவார் தொழுதெழு முருவு கொண்டார்."],"p":"","r":"The king gives his jewelled crown to his son; with him the other crowned kings, the merchant who was his life's companion and the rest praise the great ascetic and, resolved, renounce.","n":"Line 4 says they took the form that 'the three' bow to; the sense of மூவார் is unfixed and read here as the ascetic's form. Compare Asokan's renunciation with kings at verse 84.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thuravu","arasu","natpu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 313","lb":"YA313","mm":1,"ev":"The king crowns his son and renounces with kings and the merchant.","cl":[],"ag":["மாரிதத்தன்","வணிகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ya:16"},"ya:314":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":314,"t":["தாதைதன் துறவு முற்றத் தானுடன் பட்ட தல்லால்","ஓதநீர் வட்டந் தன்னை யொருதுகள் போல வுள்ளத்","தாதரம் பண்ணல் செல்லா னபயனு மரசு தன்னைக்","காதலன் குமரன் தம்பி கைப்படுத் தனன்வி டுத்தான்."],"p":"","r":"The son agrees to his father's renunciation but, holding the whole earth no more than a speck of dust, feels no attachment and gives the kingdom into the hands of his younger brother.","n":"Abhayan here is read as the son crowned in verse 313, because the verse speaks of his father's renunciation; whether he is the same man as the Abhayan of verse 325 is not settled in the Tamil.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thuravu","arasu","pattru"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 314","lb":"YA314","mm":1,"ev":"Abhayan, the king's son, hands the kingdom to his younger brother and leaves it.","cl":[],"ag":["அபயன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ya:16"},"ya:315":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":315,"t":["மாதவன் மலர்ந்த சொல்லான் மைந்தனும் மங்கை யாய","பேதையம் பிணைய னாளும் பிறப்பினி துணர்ந்த பின்னர்","ஆதரம் பண்ணல் போகத் தஞ்சினர் நெஞ்சின் எஞ்சா","மாதவன் சரண மாக வனமது துன்னி னாரே."],"p":"","r":"By the ascetic's words the son and his young wife understand the nature of birth, fear the attachment of pleasures and, taking the great ascetic as refuge, enter the forest.","n":"The wife is not named; 'மங்கையாய பேதை' is the young woman, and the pair are read as Abhayan and his wife by the sequence from 314.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["piravi","thuravu","pattru"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 315","lb":"YA315","mm":1,"ev":"The son and his wife go to the forest to follow the ascetic.","cl":[["understanding the nature of birth leads to fear of attachment to pleasure","jain","asserted",3,"narrator"]],"ag":["அபயன்","மங்கை"],"ec":[],"ch":"ya:16"},"ya:316":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":316,"t":["வினைக்கும் வினைகள் தம்மால் விளைபயன் வெறுப்பு மேவித்","தனசர ணணைந்து ளார்க்குத் தவவர சருளத் தாழ்ந்து","வினையின விளைவு தம்மை வெருவின மடிகள் மெய்யே","சினவரன் சரண மூழ்கிச் செறிதவம் படர்து மென்றார்."],"p":"","r":"Weary of the fruit that deeds beget from deeds, the new disciples bow to the one in whose refuge they stand, who offers the ascetic's sovereignty, and say they dread the ripening of deeds and, plunging into the Jina's feet, will undertake dense austerity.","n":"Unlike the youth's earlier counsel at verse 33 that fear of what is due gains nothing, here fear of karma's ripening is what drives them to austerity.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vinai","thavam","acham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 316","lb":"YA316","mm":1,"ev":"The disciples ask to follow the Jina's way of austerity.","cl":[["deeds produce further deeds and a fruit that is to be shunned","jain","asserted",1,"maridattan"]],"ag":["மாரிதத்தன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["மாரிதத்தன்","அபயருசி"],"ch":"ya:16"},"ya:317":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":317,"t":["ஆற்றல தமையப் பெற்றா லருந்தவ மமர்ந்து செய்ம்மின்","சாற்றிய வகையின் மேன்மேற் சையமா சைய மத்திற்","கேற்றிநின் றின்மை தன்னை யிதுபொழு துய்ம்மி னென்றான்","ஆற்றலுக் கேற்ற வாற்றா லவ்வழி யொழுகு கின்றார்."],"p":"","r":"The teacher says that if strength allows they should practise great austerity, taking up in turn vows of the stated kinds up to the fasting to death, and that they should be released now; and they follow that path each as their strength allows.","n":"சையமா சையம் (line 2) is obscure; it is read as the named stages of the vow, ending in the fast. The reading is provisional.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thavam","muyarchi","veedu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 317","lb":"YA317","mm":1,"ev":"The teacher sets graded austerities and they follow them each by their strength.","cl":[["austerity should be practised in stages matched to one's strength","jain","asserted",4,"abhayaruchi"]],"ag":["மாரிதத்தன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["அபயருசி","மாரிதத்தன்"],"ch":"ya:16"},"ya:318":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":318,"t":["அருங்கல மும்மை தம்மா லதிசய முடைய நோன்மைப்","பெருங்கழு வொருங்கு சூழப் பெறற்கருங் கணங்கள் தம்மாற்","சுருங்கலில் சுதத்த னென்னுந் துறவினுக் கரச னின்னுட்","டருங்கடி கமழுஞ் சோலை யதுனுள்வந் தினிதி ருந்தான்."],"p":"","r":"The ascetic Sudattan, great in austerity and honoured by many companies, comes with a great retinue into a fragrant garden of the city and sits there in peace.","n":"Sudattan appears first at verse 23, when he settles in a garden before the offering. This verse is read as a return, but the Tamil could also be read as recalling that first coming.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thavam","thuravu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 318","lb":"YA318","mm":1,"ev":"Sudattan comes to the garden of the city and settles there.","cl":[],"ag":["சுதத்தன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ya:16"},"ya:319":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":319,"t":["அனசன மமர்ந்த சிந்தை யருந்தவ னிசோம திக்குத்","தனயர்கள் தம்மை நோக்கித் தரியலீர் சரிதை போமின்","எனவவ ரிறைஞ்சி மெல்ல விந்நக ரத்து வந்தார்","அனையவ ராக எம்மை யறிகமற் றரச என்றான்."],"p":"","r":"The ascetic, in the fast, looks at the sons of Yasomati and bids them go on their alms-round since they cannot endure; they bow and come to this city, and the speaker asks the king to know him as one of them.","n":"Line 4 turns the verse into the youth's own words to the king; the 'sons of Yasomati' are read as the twins, because the youth tells his births from verse 65 on, but the case ending is hard.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thavam","thuravu","vinai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 319","lb":"YA319","mm":1,"ev":"Sudattan sends the twins out on the alms round; they come to the city.","cl":[],"ag":["சுதத்தன்","அபயருசி","அபயமதி"],"ec":[],"spk":["அபயருசி","மாரிதத்தன்"],"ch":"ya:16"},"ya:320":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":320,"t":["இனையது பிறவி மாலை யெமரது மெமது மெண்ணின்","இனையது வினைகள் பின்னா னிடர்செய்த முறைமை தானும்","இனையது வெகுளி காமத் தெய்திய வியல்பு நாடின்","இனையது பெருமை தானு மிறைவன தறத்த தென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"Such is the garland of births, ours and theirs, he says, such the order in which deeds gave pain, such how anger and desire came to be, and such the greatness of the Lord's dharma.","n":"The verse is a summary of the story told in the earlier chapters; the 'we' (எமரது, எமது) takes the twins together.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["piravi","vinai","vekuli","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 320","lb":"YA320","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["births, deeds, anger and desire are bound in a chain, and the Lord's dharma is great against it","jain","asserted",4,"abhayaruchi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["அபயருசி","மாரிதத்தன்"],"ch":"ya:16"},"ya:321":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":321,"t":["செய்த வெந்தியக் கொலையொரு துகள்தனிற் சென்றுறு பவந்தோறும்","எய்து மாயிடிற் றீர்ந்திடாக் கொலையிஃ திருநில முடிவேந்தே.","மையல் கொண்டிவண் மன்னுயி ரெனைப்பல வதைசெய வருபாவத்","தெய்தும் வெந்துய ரெப்படித் தென்றுனைந் திரங்குகின் றனமென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"The deadly deed of killing a flour effigy of a cock, he says, did not end but followed them across birth after birth; how, then, must it be for those who kill living beings with a clouded mind, and so he feels pity for them.","n":"இந்தியக் கொலை (line 1) is read as the killing of the flour cock on which the whole story turns; the sense of the first line is hard and the reading is provisional.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kolai","vinai","arul","piravi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 321","lb":"YA321","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["even a killing of a mere effigy, done once, is not wiped out and returns in each following birth","jain","asserted",1,"abhayaruchi"],["killing real living beings brings far worse pain in later births","jain","asserted",3,"abhayaruchi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["அபயருசி","மாரிதத்தன்"],"ch":"ya:16"},"ya:322":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":322,"t":["ஐய நின்னரு ளாலுயிர்க் கொலையினி லருவினை நரகத்தாழ்ந்","தெய்தும் வெந்துய ரெனைப்பல கோடிகோ டியினுறு பழிதீர்ந்தேன்","பொய்ய தன்றிது புரவல குமரநின் புகழ்மொழி புணையாக","மையின் மாதவத் தொருகட லாடுதல் வலித்தன னினியென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"The king says that by the speaker's grace he has been freed from the fault of the killing that would have sunk him in hell's endless pain, calls the words true, and resolves to bathe in the ocean of great austerity with the words of praise as his raft.","n":"The speaker is read as the king, replying to the youth of 320-321; line 2 calls the pain 'many crores', and the verse calls the youth ஐய and குமர.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vinai","naragam","thavam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 322","lb":"YA322","mm":1,"ev":"The king vows to take up great austerity.","cl":[["killing sinks the doer in hell's long pain","jain","reported",1,"maridattan"]],"ag":["மாரிதத்தன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["மாரிதத்தன்","அபயருசி"],"ch":"ya:16"},"ya:323":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":323,"t":["இன்சொன் மாதரு மிளங்கிளைச் சுற்றமும் எரித்திர ளெனவஞ்சிப்","பொன் செய் மாமுடிப் புதல்வருட் புட்பதந் தற்கிது பொறையென்றே","மின்செய் தாரவன் வெறுத்தன னரசியல் விடுத்தவ ருடன்போகி","முன்சொன் மாமலர்ப் பொழிலினுண் முனிவரற் றொழுதுநன் முனியானான்."],"p":"","r":"Fearing his sweet-spoken wife and young kin as a heap of fire, the king gives the burden of rule to Pushpadanta among his sons, leaves with those who leave rule, and bowing to the sage in the flower garden he told of before becomes a good ascetic.","n":"The verse is hard in grammar: that Pushpadanta is a son of the king and receives the rule is read from line 2 but is not certain. This verse may restate verses 313 to 315, which also describe a king's renunciation with kin.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thuravu","arasu","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 323","lb":"YA323","mm":1,"ev":"The king gives rule to Pushpadanta and becomes an ascetic in the garden.","cl":[["wife and kin are to be feared like a heap of fire by one who seeks release","jain","asserted",1,"narrator"]],"ag":["மாரிதத்தன்","புட்பதந்தன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ya:16"},"ya:324":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":324,"t":["வெய்ய தீவினை வெருவுறு மாதவம் விதியினின் றுதிகொண்டான்","ஐய தாமதி சயமுற வடங்கின னுடம்பினை யீவணிட்டே","மையல் வானிடை யனசனர் குழாங்களில் வானவன் றானகித்","தொய்யின் மாமுலைச் சுரவரர் மகளிர்தம் தொகுதியின் மகிழ்வுற்றான்."],"p":"","r":"He keeps the fierce-deed-fearing great austerity by the rule, and in a moment of great restraint lays down his body here, becomes a god among the fasting ones in the high heaven, and rejoices in the company of celestial women.","n":"Heaven is the fruit here, not release; verse 329 says the same dharma leads on to release (சிவகதி) after the gods' state.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thavam","veedu","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 324","lb":"YA324","mm":1,"ev":"The king dies in the fast and is born a god in heaven.","cl":[["austerity kept by rule and ended in the fast leads to birth among the gods","jain","asserted",3,"narrator"]],"ag":["மாரிதத்தன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"ya:16"},"ya:325":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":325,"t":["அண்ண லாகிய வபயனுந் தங்கையு மாயுக மிகையின்மை","நண்ணி நாயக முனிவனி லறிந்தனர் நவின்றனர் குணமெல்லாங்","கண்ணி னார்தம துருவின துடலங்கள் கழிந்தன கழிபோகத்","தெண்ணின் வானுல கத்திரண் டாவதி னிமையவர் தாமானார்."],"p":"","r":"Abhayan and his sister learn from the lord-ascetic that their life is not long, tell of his virtues, give up their bodies as they had sought, and become gods in the second heaven.","n":"Whether this Abhayan is the twin or the prince of verse 314 is not settled in the Tamil; the pairing with his sister makes the twin more likely.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thuravu","veedu","aayul"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 325","lb":"YA325","mm":1,"ev":"The twin ascetics die in the fast and are born as gods in the second heaven.","cl":[["a life of austerity ends in birth among the gods","jain","asserted",4,"narrator"]],"ag":["அபயருசி","அபயமதி"],"ec":[],"ch":"ya:16"},"ya:326":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":326,"t":["அம்பொன் மாமுடி யலர்கதிர்க் குண்டல மருமணி திகழாரம்","செம்பொன் மாமணி தோள்வளை கடகங்கள் செறிகழன் முதலாய","நம்பு நாளொளி நகுகதிர்க் கலங்களி னலம்பொலிந் தழகார்ந்த","வம்பு வானிடு தனுவென வடிவுடை வானவ ரானாரே."],"p":"","r":"The two gods are adorned with crown, earrings, jewelled necklaces, armlets and bracelets, bright like the rainbow, and become fair in form.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 326","lb":"YA326","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["அபயருசி","அபயமதி"],"ec":[],"ch":"ya:16"},"ya:327":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":327,"t":["வந்து வானவர் திசைதொறும் வணங்கினர் வாழ்த்தினர் மலர்மாரி","மந்த மாருதந் துந்துபி வளரிசை மலிந்தன மறுகெங்கும்","அந்தி லாடினர் பாடினர் விரும்பிய வரம்பைய ரருகெல்லாம்","வந்து தேவியர் மன்மத வாளியின் புடைசூழ்நதார்."],"p":"","r":"The gods come to every quarter, bow and bless them, flowers rain, a soft wind and the drum sound in every street and celestial women dance and sing, while goddesses come about them like arrows of Kama.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inbam","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 327","lb":"YA327","mm":1,"ev":"The gods and celestial women gather to honour the new gods.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"ya:16"},"ya:328":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":328,"t":["மாசின் மாமணி மேனியின் வாசமோ ரோசனை மணநாறத்","தேசொ ரோசனை திளைத்திட முளைத்தெழு தினகர னனையார்கள்","ஆசி லெண்குண னவதியொ டமைந்தன ரலைகட லளவெல்லாம்","ஏசில் வானுல கிணையிலின் பத்தினி லிசைந்துட னியல்கின்றார்."],"p":"","r":"Their bodies breathe a fragrance for a league and shine for a league like suns; they have the eight great qualities and sight to the sea's end, and live together in the unmatched joy of the heavenly world.","n":"அவதி (line 3) is the limit of knowledge held by those born in heaven; here it is attached to the eight qualities and read as heavenly sight, but the line is obscure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["inbam","azhagu","arivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 328","lb":"YA328","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["அபயருசி","அபயமதி"],"ec":[],"ch":"ya:16"},"ya:329":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":329,"t":["வெருவரு வினைவலி விலக்கு கிற்பது","தருவது சுரகதி தந்து பின்னரும்","பொருவரு சிவகதி புணர நிற்பது","திருவற நெறியது செவ்வி காண்மினே."],"p":"","r":"The way of the holy dharma, the poet says, holds back the force of fearful deeds, gives birth in the gods' world and then joins one to the peerless release; see its flowering.","n":"Here heaven is a stage and release (சிவகதி) the end; the word சிவ means auspicious and does not point to the god Siva in a Jain work.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aram","vinai","veedu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 329","lb":"YA329","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["the Jain dharma holds back karma, gives heaven and then release","jain","asserted",1,"narrator"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"ya:16"},"ya:330":{"book":"yasodhara_kaviyam","num":330,"t":["ஆக்குவ தேதெனி லறத்தை யாக்குக","போக்குவ தேதெனில் வெகுளி போக்குக","நோக்குவ தேதெனில் ஞானம் நோக்குக","காக்குவ தேதெனில் விரதம் காக்கவே."],"p":"","r":"What is to be made? dharma. What is to be dropped? anger. What is to be looked on? knowledge. What is to be guarded? the vow.","n":"The closing verse of the book.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aram","vekuli","gnaanam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Yasodhara Kaviyam 330","lb":"YA330","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["one should make dharma, give up anger, look to knowledge and guard the vow","jain","asserted",1,"narrator"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"ya:16"}},"links":[{"a":"ng:0","b":"cc:1","type":"same_image","note":"Both epics open by going for refuge to the feet of the tirthankara: the Cintamani's red feet, this poem's feet of Nemi under the pindi.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Two Jain epics begin with the same act of going to the feet but name different lords, so the invocation is a convention and the chosen tirthankara tells which tradition the poem draws on.","mm":1},{"a":"ng:3","b":"cc:4","type":"same_image","note":"Both poets defend flaws in speech by an image, the Cintamani's unwashed gem and this poem's lamp with smoke.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain","te":"The apology for a flawed poem is a set piece of the Jain epics, but this poem argues from the lamp's worth and the Cintamani from the washing that cures the flaw.","mm":1},{"a":"ng:3","b":"nk:6","type":"same_image","note":"The Neelakesi excuses its flaws with the moon's spot and this poem with the lamp's smoke, both asking the learned to keep the work.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain","te":"The lamp is smoky yet bright and the moon spotted yet loved: the two images make the same plea for a work of mixed quality, which shows it was a stock gesture of the Jain poets.","mm":1},{"a":"ng:3","b":"n:151","type":"qualifies","note":"The Naladiyar says the noble cannot bear one stain as the moon bears its spot; the poet asks that one smoky flaw not make the lamp be thrown away.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The ethics book sets the noble above forgiveness of their stain, while the poet, claiming no such standard, pleads that a stained thing still be kept.","gn":["the poet's plea is seen to relax the Naladiyar's strict standard of stainlessness","the quatrain's rule is shown to be a standard for character, which a poem may leave aside"],"mm":1},{"a":"ng:4","b":"n:101","type":"qualifies","note":"The Naladiyar says an old deed seeks out its doer like a calf its mother; this verse says deeds already gathered can be shed in a moment.","grade":"weak","better":"tension","te":"The Tamil ethics book makes old deeds unavoidable, while the Jain epic's poet leaves a way to drop them at once, so karma is binding in one book and removable in the other.","gn":["the Jain poem's claim is set against the stricter view that old deeds always find their doer","the calf-and-mother image is shown to have an exit in the Jain account of karma"],"mm":1},{"a":"ng:16","b":"k:5","type":"qualifies","note":"Both make the god the one to whom deeds do not cling; the king praises a lord who himself defeated hard deeds.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural says worshippers are freed from deeds by praise, while the Jain king hymns a lord who won against deeds by his own conquest, so the same praise stands on different grounds.","gn":["the king's hymn is seen to give a conquering lord, not a pure being to be reached, as the one praised","the couplet's lord is shown to be read in a Jain way as one who defeated karma"],"mm":1},{"a":"ng:17","b":"t:117","type":"tension","note":"This verse praises the lord who burned all old deeds by his own renunciation; Tirumular's sunstone burns the malas only when the Lord's sun appears.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Jain deliverance burns karma by the liberated one's own effort without a god, while the Saiva text burns it by presence and grace, so the two disagree on whether anything outside the soul does the burning.","gn":["the Jain hymn is shown to stand against the Saiva view that grace is needed to burn deeds","the Saiva image of grace burning bond is shown to be one answer to a question the Jain poet answers by effort"],"mm":1},{"a":"ng:18","b":"k:9","type":"same_image","note":"The Kural's எண்குணத்தான் (one of eight qualities) and the verse's இருநான்கு குணமுடைய இறைவன் (the lord with twice four qualities) are the same number.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"The Kural's unlisted eight is matched by a Jain count of eight qualities, so a Jain poet can read the couplet as meaning his own liberated lord.","gn":["the Jain poem is shown to use the eight-quality formula the Kural already uses","the couplet's unlisted eight is given a Jain reading by a poet who also leaves the eight unlisted"],"mm":1},{"a":"ng:19","b":"k:10","type":"develops","note":"The Kural says those who reach the Lord's feet swim the great sea of births; the verse says the lord has ended birth, old age and death.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet makes ending birth the reward of reaching the feet, and this verse names the three things the lord himself has ended, so the poem tells what is meant by crossing the sea.","gn":["the epic's hymn is shown to say what the crossing of the sea of births consists of","the couplet's promise is shown to be read as freedom from birth, old age and death"],"mm":1},{"a":"ng:20","b":"k:5","type":"qualifies","note":"Both name two kinds of deed; the Kural says they do not cling to those devoted to the Lord's praise, the verse names the two as what makes becoming, as something the lord taught.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural states that the two deeds fall away from the devoted, and the Jain verse says it is the lord's own teaching of them that frees.","gn":["the 'two deeds' are put in the Tamil devotional frame where deeds both good and bad are what binds, a reach the verse's own wording does not state","the lord's praise gets a content: in the epic it is the lord's naming of the two deeds that the devotee takes up"],"mm":1},{"a":"ng:28","b":"mm:5:1-17","type":"tension","note":"Jayandhara falls in love with a painted woman who is real elsewhere; the Manimekalai prince sees the living girl as a painted image.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain","te":"The two works run the same trope in opposite directions, so a reader sees that the poets use the picture to say a desire is shaped by a seen form before the person is known.","mm":1},{"a":"ng:35","b":"si:21:17-39","type":"tension","note":"Prithidevi fears her senior co-wife will let her fall; the Silappathikaram lists a woman who reared her co-wife's child as her own as a type of pathini.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The epic sets the co-wife's presumed envy beside the Tamil ideal of the co-wife who cares, so the new queen's fear is read as a convention the other works already know and answer.","mm":1},{"a":"ng:35","b":"k:168","type":"qualifies","note":"The Kural says envy ruins fortune and leads one to a bad place; the verse shows only a new wife's fear of envy and the retreat to the temple.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The Kural describes envy's results, and the verse shows how its mere expectation sends a wife to the god instead of to the rival.","gn":["the fear in the verse gets a name and a moral cost from the Kural's chapter on envy, which the verse leaves unsaid","the Kural's abstract envy is given a household: a co-wife whose envy is presumed and never shown"],"mm":1},{"a":"ng:36","b":"k:321","type":"qualifies","note":"The Kural makes not killing the whole of virtuous action; the verse makes a lord of that vow the one whose worship cuts the evil birth.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural sets non-killing as the rule, and the verse makes it the lord's own vow, so worship of him is worship of the rule kept to its end.","gn":["the vow is set in the Tamil rule that killing brings all other deeds, so what the god is praised for has a reason","the rule is made a person: the lord who kept it all the way and is the end of its path"],"mm":1},{"a":"ng:36","b":"t:200","type":"tension","note":"Tirumanthiram 200 has those who give up killing, theft, lust and lying reach Siva's feet; the verse has worshippers of a lord who has kept non-killing reach release.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"Both name feet and release for those who keep the vow, but the Saiva verse makes the worshipper the keeper of the vow and the Jain verse makes the lord the keeper.","gn":["the worshipper's gain is set against a Saiva account where one's own giving up of killing is what earns the feet","the Saiva list is shown to have a Jain counterpart in which the god, not the worshipper, is the one who has kept the vow"],"mm":1},{"a":"ng:36","b":"so:1","type":"agrees","note":"Soolamani 1 praises the one who conquered the sum of deeds, whose worshippers are freed; the verse praises the lord of non-killing whose worshippers cut off the evil birth.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Two Jain epics open with the same promise of release through the conqueror but name his victory differently, over deeds in one and in vow of non-killing in the other.","mm":1},{"a":"ng:37","b":"k:70","type":"qualifies","note":"The Kural has the son's merit read back as the father's tapas; the verse has the sage promise a virtuous son when the queen asks for tapas.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"The Kural reads a son as the proof of a parent's penance and the verse reverses it, making the promised son the answer to a request for penance.","gn":["the sage's answer gets an ordinary Tamil frame in which a son is a fruit of tapas, which clears the odd turn from tapas to son","the couplet's 'what penance did his father do' is given a scene in which the penance is asked for and a son promised"],"mm":1},{"a":"ng:38","b":"k:380","type":"tension","note":"The Kural says nothing is stronger than fate, which arrives first even when one plans against it; the verse bows to a lord who has conquered fate.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural makes fate the strongest force and the Jain verse puts one who has beaten it in front of the worshipper, so release means the end of the thing the Kural says cannot be passed.","gn":["the lord's conquest of fate is weighed against a Tamil saying that nothing is stronger than it","the couplet's claim that fate cannot be beaten is shown to have a Jain exception, and the exception is the god"],"mm":1},{"a":"ng:44","b":"n:51","type":"qualifies","note":"The sage says austerity will cut away the prince's heated deeds so that he reaches release; the quatrain says austerity drives sin out as a lamp drives darkness, and that evil settles in again when the good deeds that feed it run out.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"The sage's promise of a final release by tapas is answered by a warning that austerity burns sin only while its good is kept up, so the prophecy needs the prince's own lasting effort.","gn":["a limit on the prophecy: release by tapas has to be kept up and is not given once","a story in which a lamp-like tapas is foretold of one person, with the end of deeds as its goal"],"mm":1},{"a":"ng:44","b":"t:258","type":"tension","note":"The Jain sage says the son's own austerity will cut his deeds and end birth; Tirumular says austerity and virtue are a boat across the sea of deeds only because the Lord makes them fruitful.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"The same word, tapas, ends karma by the doer's effort in the sage's mouth and by the Lord's grace in Tirumular's, so the release the two promise rests on opposite sources.","gn":["a rival account in which release is not won by the ascetic's effort alone but by the Lord's help","a case of austerity credited with ending karma with no god at all in the account"],"mm":1},{"a":"ng:42","b":"ng:44","type":"develops","note":"The queen's dream of a bull and a young sun is told as an omen with no meaning given; the sage's reading in 44 turns it into a son who is king, ascetic and freed.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The dream is not left as an omen: the sage's reading supplies its meaning, and the prince's whole later life is fixed in it before his birth.","mm":1},{"a":"ng:46","b":"ng:51","type":"develops","note":"The sage names the temple doors opening at the son's foot and a pond among the signs; verse 51 shows the doors swing open at the child's foot-touch and the boy fall into the pond.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The sign comes true as an ordinary accident of an outing, so the story shows the prophecy fulfilling itself without anyone seeking it.","mm":1},{"a":"ng:53","b":"ng:49","type":"qualifies","note":"The prince is named Pirathapan at birth; after he is found on the serpent's head the king gives him a second name, Naga Kumaran, which becomes the poem's title.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The infant's first name is replaced by one taken from the miracle, so the name the book carries marks what the serpent did, not what the king first chose.","mm":1},{"a":"ng:57","b":"ng:46","type":"develops","note":"The sage had foretold that the prince would curb the rut of a great elephant; verse 57 shows him doing so on the city's own mad elephant.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The sage's prophecy is not left as a promise: the city's real elephant is the first thing that proves it, so a reader sees the prince's prowess as the working of what was foretold.","mm":1},{"a":"ng:59","b":"ng:44","type":"develops","note":"The sage's dream-reading promised a son who would bear great austerity and cut off karma; verse 59 shows him grown in learning by the sage's teaching, the first step toward that end.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The prophecy of renunciation is paired here with a worldly rise, so the story shows the prince's skill as a stage on the way to release and not a rival to it.","mm":1},{"a":"ng:73","b":"ng:65","type":"develops","note":"The king's order to play outside the city becomes, in verse 73, his command to build the prince a city of his own and name it.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The expulsion is turned into an honour by the end of the chapter, so the sentence of exile never takes effect as a punishment.","mm":1},{"a":"ng:72","b":"ng:68","type":"tension","note":"The king had ordered the prince's goods taken; in 72 the prince, enriched by dice, gives away all that he wins to the kings and his mother.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"The order to strip the prince is answered not by revenge but by the prince's giving, so the seizure leaves him richer in standing than the king who ordered it.","mm":1},{"a":"ng:57","b":"si:15:42-53","type":"same_image","note":"Kovalan frees a Brahmin from a rogue elephant and tames it, and Naga Kumaran subdues a maddened elephant that has been wrecking the city: the same feat of a hero.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"In the Silappathikaram the feat is an act of compassion for one man; in this epic it is a sign of a foretold destiny and a cause of royal suspicion.","mm":1},{"a":"ng:57","b":"t:2023","type":"same_image","note":"The prince masters a rutting elephant; Tirumular's body holds five rutting elephants that will not come to the post of the Agamas' teaching.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The epic's taming is an outward feat of the body, and the Tirumanthiram carries the same image inward to the senses, so the story's word மதம் is read both as rut and as the pride the senses breed.","gn":["The epic's elephant gains a second sense: its mastery is also a figure for the senses held in check.","The Tirumanthiram's inner elephants gain a narrative picture, a man who actually stops a rampaging beast."],"gr":2,"mm":1},{"a":"ng:58","b":"t:1679","type":"same_image","note":"The prince mounts and rides a vicious horse through the streets; Tirumular tells the reader to hold the reins of the running horse firmly.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The ride of a hero is shown in the epic as skill before a crowd, and in the Tirumanthiram as the discipline of the mind that needs no show, so the two use one image for different ends.","gn":["The prince's horse-taming gains a reading as the discipline of a restless will.","The Tirumanthiram's running horse gains a visible rider and a crowd, a show that the verse tells its hearers to do without."],"mm":1},{"a":"ng:71","b":"k:931","type":"qualifies","note":"The prince wins at dice from the kings, as the Kural warns that what a gambler wins is like the hook's metal in the fish.","grade":"wrong","better":null,"te":"The Kural treats winning as the lure; the epic gives a winner who is not caught by it because he gives the winnings back, which shows the rule is about the winner's grip on the goods.","gn":["The prince's win gains a measure: it is the keeping of the winnings, not the playing, that the couplet condemns.","The couplet's warning gains a hard case, a winner who takes nothing, which tests whether the warning is about the game or the hunger."],"mm":1},{"a":"ng:63","b":"n:189","type":"qualifies","note":"The prince is accused through a lie told against him and keeps his bearing; the Naladiyar says the pure of heart are not shaken by cruel slander, like a lamp's flame.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The Naladiyar's steadiness under slander is shown in the epic as a prince who answers the false charge with a procession and with giving, not with protest.","gn":["The verse gains a character who passes the Naladiyar's test of staying unshaken by false speech.","The Naladiyar quatrain gains a story in which the slander is followed by exile and a city, so the cost and the outcome of steadiness are shown."],"mm":1},{"a":"ng:77","b":"ng:81","type":"develops","note":"The sage tells the king that his sons will serve one man; in 81 Vyala meets Naga Kumara and calls himself the small one.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The prophecy names no master, so Vyala's self-lowering at first sight shows the foretold service beginning as a choice of bearing and not a forced bond.","mm":1},{"a":"ng:83","b":"ng:85","type":"develops","note":"Sridharan arrives in anger and meets the prince in 83, and in 85 he is rebuked and withdraws in fear.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The threat built in 83 is ended not by a battle but by a minister's speech, so the epic makes counsel the thing that turns the king back.","mm":1},{"a":"ng:78","b":"k:46","type":"qualifies","note":"The king in 78 leaves for forest austerity after crowning his sons, the move Kural 46 asks the householder to question.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The epic shows leaving for the outside way as a late step after the duty of rule is done, which narrows how far the Kural's rhetorical question reaches.","gn":["The king's departure gains a test: it is not flight from household duty but follows its completion.","The couplet gains a case of a ruler who goes outside the household way and loses nothing it names as virtue."],"mm":1},{"a":"ng:78","b":"n:21","type":"qualifies","note":"Naladiyar 21 says kings under umbrellas were carried off and called dead; in 78 the king leaves the throne alive and by his own act.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The same fall of royal pomp appears as chosen renunciation rather than death, showing impermanence can be met before it arrives.","gn":["The king's retreat gains the Naladiyar's reason for giving up rank, impermanence, which the verse leaves unspoken.","The quatrain gains a king who acts on its warning instead of only being its example."],"mm":1},{"a":"ng:95","b":"ng:93","type":"develops","note":"The hunter's cry and his wife's imprisonment in 93 are met in 95 by her release.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The prince's help answers the plea at once, so 93 and 95 show the hero's mercy as action rather than promise.","mm":1},{"a":"ng:100","b":"ng:99","type":"develops","note":"The city given by Vanaraja's sacrifice-gift of his daughter in 99 is repaid in 100 by the prince winning back a city for Vanaraja.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The marriage in 99 is returned by a kingdom in 100, so the gift of a wife is shown as an obligation the husband repays in arms.","mm":1},{"a":"ng:90","b":"cc:628","type":"same_image","note":"The prince wins a princess by defeating her at the vina; in the Cintamani the girl goes out to break her opponents with the yaazh.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Both epics make the vina contest the means by which a king's son wins his bride, so the motif is a standard marriage test rather than one poet's invention.","mm":1},{"a":"ng:101","b":"k:261","type":"qualifies","note":"The Kural defines தவம் as enduring pain and not harming; 101 gives a king who loses his land and then takes up yoga to end old deeds.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"The verse shows tavam taken up after loss and aimed at ending vinai, which the Kural's definition of endurance and non-harm does not say.","gn":["the Kural fixes what the sage's austerity must mean, an endurance of pain without harming","the couplet gets a worked case in which a man chooses austerity after losing a throne"],"mm":1},{"a":"ng:101","b":"t:122","type":"tension","note":"Tirumular's Siva-yoga ends in the Lord's abode, given by Nandi; Somaprabha guards his yoga so that his own deeds end, with no god named.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The two texts use the word yoga for a discipline of release but one gives the release to the Lord's grace and the other to the ascetic's own guarding, so grace and effort part on the same practice.","gn":["the epic's yoga is set beside a text where the same practice is a gift of grace, showing what the Jain ascetic does not invoke","the Tirumanthiram's grace claim meets a case where the same discipline of release is held up without a god"],"mm":1},{"a":"ng:104","b":"ng:109","type":"develops","note":"The king asks whether his two sons will rule or serve; at 109 the two come to Naga Kumara and become his servants.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The ascetic's foresight is shown to be exact, so the story makes the sons' loss of the kingdom a matter of fate that no choice of the king's undoes.","mm":1},{"a":"ng:118","b":"ng:120","type":"develops","note":"The hymn's three verses promise rule on earth and in heaven (118), Indra's rank (119), and release (120).","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The hymn arranges worship's rewards as a ladder, so release comes as the last step above the worldly kingship the prince himself holds.","mm":1},{"a":"ng:106","b":"n:21","type":"qualifies","note":"Naladiyar says even kings on elephants under the moon-bright umbrella are carried off and spoken of as dead; Jayavarma lays down his kingdom while living.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"A king's leaving rule by his own act, before it is taken from him, is the story's answer to the impermanence the Naladiyar says no king escapes.","gn":["the abdication is seen as a chosen answer to the loss of rule that the Naladiyar says death forces on every king","the Naladiyar's lament over fallen kings is given a king who acts on the lesson while still ruling"],"gr":2,"mm":1},{"a":"ng:119","b":"k:3","type":"same_image","note":"Kural 3 places the god on a flower and says those who reach his feet live long on the earth; the hymn has the lord seated on a lotus and promises Indra's rank to those who worship his feet.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Both couplet and hymn set the god on a flower and promise a reward for reaching the feet, but the hymn's reward is rank in heaven while the couplet's is long life on earth.","gn":["the hymn's lotus-seated lord is seen as a Jain case of the flower-borne god of the Kural's opening","the couplet's reward of long life on earth is set beside a hymn that pays in heaven's rank"],"mm":1},{"a":"ng:120","b":"k:10","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 10 says only those at the Lord's feet cross the sea of births; the hymn says those who bow to the lord on the lion throne become gods and then reach release.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"The hymn makes the crossing of births a graded gain, gods first and release after, where the couplet states it only as a crossing for those who reach the feet.","gn":["the hymn's promise of release is read as the Kural's crossing of births with a stage of divine rank before it","the couplet's one-step crossing is given a sequence of worlds the worshipper passes through"],"mm":1},{"a":"ng:113","b":"cc:327","type":"same_image","note":"Both name the star Arundhati as the measure of a woman's chastity: Mrigalochana is like her, and in the Cintamani a woman is said to put her aside.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Two Jain epics use the same star as the yardstick of wifely chastity, one saying a queen is equal to it and the other that a woman surpasses it.","mm":1},{"a":"ng:105","b":"so:109","type":"agrees","note":"In both a sage's saying about who will rule or serve is fixed as a prophecy: here the sons will serve the conqueror, there the hearers affirm the prophecy that one will protect the world under one umbrella.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"Jain epics let a sage's sight settle a future rule and the later scene confirm it, so succession is told as fate foreseen, not as a contest.","mm":1},{"a":"ng:138","b":"k:321","type":"qualifies","note":"The hero kills Vayuvegan to avenge a father's murder; the couplet makes not killing the whole of virtuous action.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"A Jain-framed epic lets its hero kill a usurper without comment, so the couplet's absolute not-killing meets a case where the story itself counts killing as justice.","gn":["the hero's killing is seen against a rule the poem's own faith holds","the couplet is shown meeting a hard case of vengeance for murder"],"mm":1},{"a":"ng:138","b":"k:550","type":"develops","note":"Vayuvegan kills a king and seizes his land; the hero kills him, as the king's weeding of the cruel in the couplet.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet puts the punishment of the cruel in a king's hand; here a wandering prince does it, so the story shows the same duty fallen to whoever happens to be there.","gn":["the hero's killing is read as the weeding-out of a cruel man","the couplet's weeding is shown done by a prince with no throne"],"mm":1},{"a":"ng:139","b":"k:148","type":"qualifies","note":"The hero takes five hundred princesses by the mantra-rite; the couplet praises the man who does not look at another's wife.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet bars only another's wife, and the poem's heroes win unmarried women, so the epic's polygamy sits outside what the couplet forbids.","gn":["the hero's many marriages are measured against the couplet's one fixed limit","the couplet's limit is shown to leave room for many wives who are unmarried"],"mm":1},{"a":"ng:137","b":"ng:134","type":"develops","note":"The merchant could not say why five hundred women sing daily at the temple; Dharanisundari gives the cause.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The merchant's wonder is only a wonder until the women speak, showing the poem makes a marvel into a wrong that the hero is to put right.","gr":2,"mm":1},{"a":"ng:144","b":"ng:143","type":"qualifies","note":"Just after the hero takes his pleasure with Ilakkanai, sages arrive and he asks them about it.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"The sage's coming in the same breath as the marriage shows the poem placing the hero's pleasures under the teaching that follows them.","mm":1},{"a":"ng:148","b":"k:345","type":"develops","note":"The couplet calls even the body excess for those who would cut off births; Nagadattan lets go of the body by fasting.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet calls the body surplus for one bent on ending births, and the story shows that a householder who lets the body go by fasting is rewarded with a god's birth, so the Jain fast does not by itself end births.","gn":["the fast is read as the couplet's 'body is excess' acted to the end","the couplet's claim gets an actual practice, and a limit: the first reward is a heaven, not the end of births"],"mm":1},{"a":"ng:148","b":"n:57","type":"qualifies","note":"The quatrain praises those who hold their vows through unbearable trouble; here the trouble of hunger is the vow itself.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The quatrain sets trouble as the test of a vow, but the fast makes the trouble a part of the vow, so holding firm means keeping a hunger that ends the keeper's life.","gn":["the hunger of the fast is seen as the very trouble the vow-keeper is told to bear","the quatrain's trial is given an extreme case in which holding on ends in death"],"mm":1},{"a":"ng:149","b":"k:339","type":"develops","note":"The couplet likens death to sleep and birth to waking; here the death by fast is followed at once by waking as a god.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet's sleep-and-waking comparison is made literal, and the waking place depends on how the sleeper died.","gn":["the death in meditation is read as a sleep that ends in a chosen waking","the couplet's comparison is shown with a destination fixed by the way of dying"],"mm":1},{"a":"ng:150","b":"si:9:55-66","type":"same_image","note":"In both works a former birth's vow explains what happens in this one: a broken vow there, a kept vow here.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain","te":"The same cause by a vow of a past birth explains a loss in one work and a gain in the other, so the epics do not tie past deeds to suffering alone.","mm":1},{"a":"ng:152","b":"t:432","type":"tension","note":"The verse says a vow, worship and gifts conquer the binding deed; the Tirumanthiram says the Lord made the bond that fastens the soul to what went before.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"One text lets the soul loosen its own bond by vow and giving, while the other makes the bond the Lord's work, so the question of who can undo the bond divides them.","gn":["the vow-and-gift path to conquering the bond is set against a view in which the bond is not the soul's own doing","the Saiva verse's bond meets a rival claim that effort alone can undo it"],"mm":1},{"a":"ng:152","b":"t:144","type":"qualifies","note":"The Tirumanthiram says only the vows kept and the knowledge gained go with the dead; the verse lists what the vow buys after death.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"The Tirumanthiram names the vow as what travels past death, and the epic adds a ledger of what it buys: Indra's rank, kingship, then the end of deeds.","gn":["the vow's journey after death gets a stated ladder of rewards","the Tirumanthiram's bare claim is given a worked sequence that the Jain epic fills in"],"gr":2,"mm":1},{"a":"ng:157","b":"k:342","type":"develops","note":"The couplet says to renounce while there is time; the king crowns his son first and then renounces.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet gives only the timing, and the story adds that a king's renouncing in time includes installing his heir first.","gn":["the king's exit is read as renunciation in time, with the succession first","the couplet's time gets a king's duty: the throne is handed on before it is left"],"mm":1},{"a":"ng:157","b":"ng:44","type":"qualifies","note":"The sage foretold that the son would bear austerity and reach release; here the father renounces and the son is crowned.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"The prophecy of the son's austerity is not yet kept; the father takes the sage's way first, so the work leaves the son's renunciation for later.","mm":1},{"a":"ng:158","b":"k:46","type":"qualifies","note":"A queen leaves her son for the nun's life and is rewarded in heaven, while the couplet asks what a householder gains by going to the outside way.","grade":"sound","better":"tension","te":"The story answers the Kural's question by showing a woman rewarded for leaving, so the couplet's praise of the householder is met by a Jain ideal that holds the opposite.","gn":["the queen's leaving is set against a rule that makes the home the greater path","the couplet's challenge is met by a case where leaving is rewarded, not wasted"],"mm":1},{"a":"ng:162","b":"n:53","type":"develops","note":"A king on his throne turns from rule at the sight of a vanishing cloud, as the quatrain's best of people see home, youth and wealth fail and renounce.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The quatrain gives renunciation as a conclusion of reasoning; the story shows the same turn as a sudden sight on a throne after the heir is crowned.","gn":["the king's turn is shown as the reasoned choice of the best the quatrain names","the quatrain's argument gets a figure who acts on it at the height of his splendour"],"mm":1},{"a":"ng:162","b":"k:342","type":"agrees","note":"The king gives up kingship at the height of his state and takes austerity, as the couplet urges renouncing while there is time.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet urges haste in the abstract; the king's act shows what haste costs, a throne handed to a son, and what it is paid for.","gn":["the king's going forth is shown as renouncing in time, not in old age","the couplet gains a prince who leaves a throne, a cost it does not state"],"mm":1},{"a":"ng:164","b":"ng:144","type":"develops","note":"The hero asked the sage about his pleasure and heard dharma; here he follows that teaching to its end, by conquering karma and reaching siddhi.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"What the hero hears in the earlier grove scene is the cause of the choices he makes here, which shows the poem's plan: hearing dharma comes before renouncing.","mm":1},{"a":"ng:164","b":"k:5","type":"qualifies","note":"The monk conquers both deeds by his own austerity, where the couplet says the two deeds do not cling to those who praise the Lord.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet places freedom from both deeds in devotion to a Lord; the story gives the same freedom through a man's own austerity and no god, so the same result has two causes.","gn":["the hero's liberation is set against a rule that places it in devotion","the couplet's route is shown to have a Jain rival that reaches the same end by effort alone"],"mm":1},{"a":"ng:165","b":"t:219","type":"tension","note":"The brothers destroy both deeds by meditating on the soul, where Tirumular says the worshipped fire burns up deeds.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The two accounts reach the same freedom from deeds by different agents, the practitioner's own meditation against a fire that burns them, so the dispute is over who does the burning.","gn":["the brothers' liberation is set against a view that gives the work to a worshipped fire","Tirumular's fire is shown to have a rival account in which no fire is needed"],"mm":1},{"a":"ng:166","b":"t:258","type":"develops","note":"Austerity and yoga bring the brothers perfection and the rest heaven, as Tirumular says austerity and virtue together are a boat over the sea of deeds.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The story shows austerity giving two different ends for two sets of people, which the single boat in Tirumular's line does not.","gn":["the story's two ends are seen as a way to read the one boat of the verse","the verse's boat gains a case where the same austerity reaches two shores"],"mm":1},{"a":"ng:170","b":"k:32","type":"develops","note":"The closing sermon says nothing helps the soul but virtue and nothing harms it but vice; the couplet says no gain surpasses virtue and no ruin surpasses forgetting it.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"The couplet's ruin is forgetting virtue, while the sermon's is positive vice (maram), so the story hardens the Kural's lapse into a deed that harms the soul.","gn":["the sermon's rule is seen as a hard form of a rule the Tamil tradition already held","the couplet's ruin of forgetting is made sharper by a vice that actively harms"],"mm":1},{"a":"ng:170","b":"n:85","type":"develops","note":"The sermon says to fear a bad rebirth and leave vice; the quatrain gives the bad rebirth as the fruit of wronging others' wives.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The sermon names fear of an evil birth without a case, and the quatrain supplies one, so the fear gets a concrete deed and fruit.","gn":["the sermon's fear of an evil birth is given a concrete deed behind it","the quatrain's penalty is shown as the ground of a whole poem's moral"],"gr":2,"mm":1},{"a":"nk:1","b":"k:2","type":"qualifies","note":"Both invocations make knowledge the mark of the god: the Kural's வாலறிவன் (one of pure knowledge), the Neelakesi's எல்லாம் உணர்ந்தான் (one who knows all).","grade":"forced","better":"agrees","te":"The Kural's unnamed pure knower is open enough that a Jain poet can fill the same slot with a god defined only by omniscience and freedom from birth, with no creating role.","gn":["the Jain invocation is seen standing in a shared Tamil form of praising the god as knower","the couplet's sectless epithet is shown being read in a sectarian sense by a debate poem"],"gr":2},{"a":"nk:3","b":"k:3","type":"same_image","note":"Both honour the feet of one who walked on the flower: மலர்மிசை ஏகினான் in the Kural, தண்டாமரைமேல் நடந்தான் in the Neelakesi.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"An avowedly Jain poet uses the flower-walking epithet for his god, so the Kural's disputed epithet was current in Jain praise and not only open to other readings.","gn":["the dream-apology is anchored in a known image of the god's feet","the couplet's contested epithet gains a sectarian witness for one of its readings"],"gr":4},{"a":"nk:6","b":"n:151","type":"qualifies","note":"Both use the moon's spot: the Naladiyar to say the noble cannot bear one stain as the moon does, the Neelakesi to say a work, like the moon, may carry a fault and still be loved.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The same image yields opposite counsel: for a person's character a single stain is unbearable, but for a poet before an assembly an acknowledged fault is excusable.","gn":["the poet's apology is seen to lower the standard the ethics books set for the noble","the quatrain's rule is shown to be about moral stain, not every imperfection"],"gr":4},{"a":"nk:6","b":"k:957","type":"qualifies","note":"The Kural says a fault in the well-born shows like the moon's spot; the Neelakesi says the moon's spot does not stop the world loving it.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"Read together, the moon's spot is both what makes a high fault visible and what proves the loved and high are never spotless, so visibility is not the same as rejection.","gn":["the apology answers the worry the couplet raises, that a fault in a high place is seen by all","the couplet's warning is softened by a case where the visible spot is forgiven"],"gr":2},{"a":"nk:4","b":"nk:148","type":"develops","note":"The invocation says she took the great one's dharma and her dark heart turned; at the end of the sermon she says she has taken the clear knowledge and asks for atonement for the wrong she did.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The prologue's single word of conversion becomes, in the story, a confession and a request for expiation, so the turn is shown as moral repair and not only a change of belief.","gr":2},{"a":"nk:8","b":"nk:45","type":"develops","note":"The invocation's demons who lost their god's refuge are, in the story, the demons who complain to Neelakesi that the sage stopped their offerings.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The phrase 'removing refuge in the god' turns out to mean ending blood offerings, so the demons' grievance is the loss of sacrifice, the poem's first act against killing.","gr":5},{"a":"nk:9","b":"nk:55","type":"develops","note":"The invocation's 'unable to disturb his penance' is told at length when her terrors fail and she gives up, fearing his penance will burn her.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The story makes the sage's penance an active power that the demoness fears, which the prologue's bare statement of her failure does not convey.","gr":2},{"a":"nk:9","b":"nk:163","type":"develops","note":"The invocation announces the debates 'beginning with the view that accepts flesh'; in the Kundalakesi debate Neelakesi charges the Buddha's path with not calling flesh-eating a fault.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The prologue names the Buddhist teaching by its tolerance of flesh, showing that the poem's first and framing charge against a rival school is ethical before it is metaphysical.","gr":2},{"a":"nk:32","b":"nk:35","type":"develops","note":"The shrine set up for the demon-mother among flesh-eating hosts is where, in 35, people come to cut a lamb's head for the goddess and the sage forbids the killing.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The description of the shrine as a place of demons who eat bodies is the moral setting of the first act: the sage's reform begins by ending blood offering at that very shrine."},{"a":"nk:33","b":"nk:49","type":"develops","note":"33 likens the sage to the earth in patience and Meru in strength; in 49, as Neelakesi prepares her terrors, he resolves to let her frighten him as she will and to teach her.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The epithet of patience becomes the sage's whole strategy against the demoness: he does not resist her terrors but outlasts them, and turns the trial into a chance to teach."},{"a":"nk:33","b":"t:1616","type":"same_image","note":"Both put the renouncer in the burning ground with the same verb: காட்டகத் துறைவான் in the Neelakesi, உறைவது காட்டகம் in the Tirumanthiram.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The burning ground was the renouncer's accepted dwelling across sects, but for Tirumular its dweller is Siva himself, whom renouncers imitate, while the Jain sage enters it as a stranger in a goddess's shrine whose offerings he will end.","gn":["the sage's choice of dwelling is seen as a recognised mark of the renouncer, not only a test of courage","the verse's image of the god in the burning ground is shown claimed by a rival tradition that gives the place no divine owner"]},{"a":"nk:32","b":"n:121","type":"develops","note":"The Naladiyar calls the meat-eater's belly a burning ground for beasts and birds; the Neelakesi places its flesh-eating demon hosts and a goddess's shrine inside a real burning ground.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The quatrain's metaphor joining flesh-eating and the cremation ground becomes the Neelakesi's literal scene, so the epic's first quarrel over flesh is staged where eating bodies and burning them meet.","gn":["the burning-ground description is shown to carry an ethical charge against eating flesh, not only horror","the quatrain's figure is shown worked out as a setting for the doctrinal fight over offerings"],"gr":3},{"a":"nk:18","b":"k:46","type":"qualifies","note":"The Kural asks what a virtuous householder would gain by going the outside way; the Neelakesi's ideal land has virtuous householders and also those who leave home's troubles to wear away their deeds.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The Jain poet answers the couplet's question by naming a gain the household cannot give, the wearing away of deeds already done, while still praising the household's gifts and conduct.","gn":["the verse's two kinds of people are seen as a reply to a known Tamil challenge to renunciation","the couplet's rhetorical question is given a specific answer that it does not consider"],"gr":3},{"a":"nk:18","b":"t:1527","type":"tension","note":"The Neelakesi praises those who strive (முயல்வார்) with knowledge to wear away their deeds; the Tirumanthiram says deeds end when grace comes as the guru and one's own doing ceases (தன்செயல் அற்றால்).","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Both make knowledge the solvent of deeds, but they disagree on whose act it is: for the Jain land it is the striver's own effort, for Tirumular the end of one's own agency under grace; the tension holds if முயல்வார் is read as self-reliant effort.","gn":["the passing praise of strivers is shown to carry the Jain view of self-wrought release","the verse's stress on the end of one's own agency stands out against a tradition that makes that agency the means"]},{"a":"nk:19","b":"k:972","type":"qualifies","note":"The Kural says birth is alike for all and distinction comes from the work one does; the Neelakesi praises a land where the four varnas each keep the path given to them.","grade":"weak","better":"tension","te":"A Jain poet could honour the Kural's ethics and still count the hereditary varna order as a land's virtue, so the couplet's levelling of birth was not read by him as rejecting the order, only as asking each to keep its duty.","gn":["the verse's praise of varna order is seen against a Tamil claim that ranks by deeds, not birth","the couplet's ambiguous செய்தொழில் is shown open to the reading of a fixed hereditary duty"]},{"a":"nk:20","b":"tk:98","type":"qualifies","note":"Thirikadugam names three seeds of the month's three rains: the priests' virtue, the king's just rule, and the wife who follows her husband; the Neelakesi gives its land three rains a month from its own known goodness.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The three-rains formula, which Thirikadugam ties to the conduct of priest, king and wife, appears in the epic as a landscape convention that explains away the missing palai tinai, its moral causes left unspoken.","gn":["the epic's claim of three rains is seen to carry an implied moral order the verse does not state","the quatrain's formula is shown travelling into poetic description, where it serves the tinai scheme rather than ethics"]},{"a":"nk:37","b":"t:451","type":"tension","note":"Both give an account of where a child comes from: the Neelakesi's sage assigns the body to the parents' blood and the life to its own former deeds, leaving the deity nothing to give; Tirumular has the Lord sit in the womb and form the embryo, knowing what it will become.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"On the same question, the making of a child, the Jain sage removes every divine agent while Tirumular puts the Lord inside the womb, so karma alone and karma shaped by the Lord are opposed answers, not one view in two idioms.","gn":["the sage's denial is seen to cut against a Tamil theology in which a god does make the child, not only against a village vow","Tirumular's embryology is seen as a position a rival school in Tamil openly argued against"]},{"a":"nk:38","b":"k:259","type":"qualifies","note":"The Kural sets abstaining from one killing above a thousand fire sacrifices; the Neelakesi's sage does not ask the worshippers to drop their vow but to offer a clay goat in its place.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Between the Kural's flat preference for not killing over ritual and outright refusal of the rite lies a third course the epic shows a teacher using: keep the vow, replace the victim with an image.","gn":["the clay-goat advice is seen as a practical form of a principle the ethics books already state","the couplet's comparison is shown applied to a vowed village offering, where giving up the rite has a social cost the couplet does not weigh"]},{"a":"nk:40","b":"k:323","type":"qualifies","note":"The Kural ranks not killing first and not lying second; the sage lists killing, lying, theft, going to others' wives and craving for wealth, and promises the world of the gods.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural's ranking of not killing above not lying is the head of a fixed Jain list of five householder abstentions, which the epic gives whole with a heavenly reward rather than release.","gn":["the sage's list is seen keeping the same order of the first two goods that the Kural argues for","the couplet's two-term ranking is shown as the opening of a longer five-part code"]},{"a":"nk:40","b":"s:285","type":"qualifies","note":"Both list the same abstentions, killing first, then lying and theft; the Siddhar asks the Lord of Chidambaram to grant him a life without them, while the sage tells householders that keeping them wins the world of the gods.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The same list of abstentions is a gift begged of the Lord in the Siddhar and the householder's own work with a self-acting reward in the Jain sermon, which is the grace-against-effort divide in miniature.","gn":["the sermon's silent assumption, that the hearer can keep the vows unaided, is brought out by a text that asks for them as grace","the Siddhar's petition is seen against a rival ethic that needs no giver for the same virtues"]},{"a":"nk:37","b":"si:12:141-144","type":"tension","note":"The Silappathikaram's hunters offer the goddess blood as the price (விலை) of the victory she gives; the Neelakesi's worshippers offer a goat for the child she gave, and the sage denies that she gave anything.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Both epics show worship to the fierce goddess as an exchange of blood for a boon; the Jain sage attacks the exchange at its first term, the boon, which the hunters' song takes for granted.","gn":["the sage's argument is seen to strike at the logic of a widespread kind of worship, not at one family's vow","the hunters' price-for-victory is shown to rest on a premise another epic set out to deny"]},{"a":"nk:49","b":"k:264","type":"qualifies","note":"The Kural says penance can crush one's foes if one so intends; the sage, attacked by Neelakesi, intends instead to make her see her powerlessness and come to the dharma.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The power the Kural grants penance against enemies is here held back and turned to converting the enemy, which is what the Kural's 'if one so intends' leaves open.","gn":["the sage's restraint is seen as a choice not to use a power the Kural says penance has","the couplet's condition எண்ணின் gains a case where the ascetic, able to destroy, chooses not to"],"gr":2},{"a":"nk:55","b":"k:267","type":"qualifies","note":"The Kural pictures penance as pain burning the ascetic as fire burns gold; Neelakesi fears that the fullness of the sage's penance will burn her.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The fire of penance faces two ways: inward in the Kural, where it purifies the one who bears it, and outward in the epic, where the assailant feels it as a fire that would consume her.","gn":["her fear is seen to rest on the same fire that the Kural says the ascetic has passed through","the couplet's inward fire is shown to be imagined by others as a danger to them"]},{"a":"nk:49","b":"nk:4","type":"develops","note":"The sage foresees that Neelakesi, once she learns the arts of meaning, will come to the path of dharma; the invocation says she took the great one's dharma and turned the world from violence.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The invocation's report of her conversion is planted in the story as the sage's own foreknowledge at the moment of her attack, so the conversion is his aim from the start, not a later turn."},{"a":"nk:56","b":"nk:49","type":"develops","note":"The sage resolves to make her understand her own powerlessness (திறமின்மை); she ends by confessing she cannot touch him.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"His plan is carried out without any act of his: her own failed terrors teach her the powerlessness he meant her to learn.","gr":2},{"a":"nk:57","b":"k:269","type":"develops","note":"Both set the ascetic's ஆற்றல் against கூற்றம்: the Kural says those who gain the power of penance can leap even Death; here a demoness likened to Death owns that the fire of the sage's penance would destroy her.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The couplet's abstract boast that penance outruns death becomes a scene in which Death's likeness itself gives up the fight, and the victory comes without the ascetic acting at all.","gn":["the demoness's surrender is read as the outcome the Kural promised for true penance","the couplet's claim gains a dramatised instance in which the threat is a being, not the end of life"]},{"a":"nk:58","b":"nk:72","type":"tension","note":"Verse 58's plan that the sage will drop his vow once she breaks his restraint in a woman's form meets its answer in 72, where every wile draws only the question of what these are to him.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"Holding the plan beside its failure shows that the epic places the vow's safety in the sage's inner containment, the very மனநிறை she meant to break, not in avoiding the temptress."},{"a":"nk:58","b":"mm:21:1-22","type":"same_image","note":"Both heroines of the debate epics take another woman's form: Neelakesi takes a princess's shape to break a sage's vow, Manimekalai took Kayachandigai's shape to teach her former husband birth and death and end his karma.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Jain and Buddhist epics use the same device of a borrowed body to opposite ends, one to bind a man in desire and one to free him from it, so the borrowed form itself is neutral and the intent decides."},{"a":"nk:64","b":"k:338","type":"qualifies","note":"The Kural calls the tie of body and life a friendship that ends as a bird leaves its nest; the Neelakesi's sage knows a scripture that cuts the deeds entangled in the soul's oneness with and difference from the body.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Set beside the Kural, the Jain verse turns the body-soul tie from a bond death simply ends into a knot of deeds that a discipline can cut while one still lives.","gn":["the sage's knowledge is placed against the Tamil commonplace of body and life as passing companions","the couplet's bird-and-nest friendship gains a doctrinal account of what holds the two together"],"gr":2},{"a":"nk:70","b":"k:340","type":"same_image","note":"Both picture life in a house: the Kural asks whether life, a lodger in the body, has no home of its own; the Neelakesi's sage likens beings across the states of birth to people who live together in one house and do not fear each other.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The same lodging image serves the Kural to make the soul homeless and the Jain sage to make all souls housemates, so it can teach unrest or fearlessness depending on whether one looks at one body or at all lives.","gn":["the sage's housemates image is seen to rework a known Tamil figure of the soul as a lodger","the couplet's lonely lodger gains a counter-image in which every being shares the house"]},{"a":"nk:72","b":"n:46","type":"qualifies","note":"The Naladiyar answers a beautiful woman's appeal by dissecting the body into entrails, blood and bone and asking which part is she; the Neelakesi's sage meets a full display of wiles with one question, what are these to us.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Naladiyar's dissection is a method for one still tempted, while the sage needs no method; and since this beauty is a demoness's borrowed shape, the Naladiyar's question of where the woman is in the body has, here, a literal answer: nowhere.","gn":["the sage's bare dismissal is shown to stand where a quatrain needs a whole argument","the quatrain's rhetorical question gains a scene in which the alluring body truly belongs to no woman"]},{"a":"nk:74","b":"s:295","type":"tension","note":"Both call birth a sickness with a medicine: the Neelakesi's sage names the medicine as dharma that cuts the diseases of birth; Pattinathar names it as never forgetting the lotus feet of the Lord of the Tillai hall.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The two traditions agree on the diagnosis and part on the cure: the Jain medicine is a dharma one takes up oneself with no god named, while the Siddhar's medicine is remembrance of the Lord's feet.","gn":["the Jain sermon's medicine is set against a Saiva one, showing that it names no saviour","the Siddhar's medicine of the feet is seen as one answer among rival cures for the same disease"],"gr":2},{"a":"nk:75","b":"mm:24:101-122","type":"tension","note":"The Neelakesi's sage counts four states of birth (நாற்கதி); Aravanar in the Manimekalai counts six kinds of living beings: humans, gods, Brahmas, hell-beings, animals and ghosts.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The rival epics disagree even on how many destinies there are, so a reader comparing their karma teaching must first map the Jain four gatis onto the Buddhist six realms."},{"a":"nk:76","b":"mm:6:168-187","type":"tension","note":"The Neelakesi's sage says the hells are seven (ஏழாய்) and spread into eighty-four hundred thousand dwellings; the Manimekalai's goddess summons the eight kinds of hell-beings (எண் வகை நரகர்) within the Chakravala.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The two epics count the hells differently, seven earths stacked below the world against eight kinds of hell-dwellers within one world-system, so hell's shape is itself a point of sectarian difference."},{"a":"nk:88","b":"nk:64","type":"develops","note":"In 64 the sage sees privately that the woman before him is a demon's daughter, not a princess; in 88 he lets it show in one word, நுமர் (your kin), among the demon class of the Vyantara gods.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The sage's knowledge of her true form, held silent in 64, becomes a tool of teaching: he names her kind inside a list of gods, so the cosmology itself unmasks her, and then defers it to keep her listening."},{"a":"nk:83","b":"nk:86","type":"qualifies","note":"83 denies that great penance raises the humans of the small islands; 86 says penance frees the humans of the land of deeds.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Penance is not a power that works anywhere: in this sermon its fruit depends on the land of one's birth, so the same act frees in one land and fails in another."},{"a":"nk:86","b":"nk:74","type":"develops","note":"In 74 the sage promised to tell birth, its disease, and the medicine that cuts that disease; 86 names the medicine as penance that drives out the dark karma.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The promised medicine for birth turns out to be penance, and it is offered only to humans of the land of deeds, so the cure is narrower than the promise first sounded."},{"a":"nk:97","b":"nk:163","type":"develops","note":"The sermon's hell names 'the sin of eating flesh' beside 'the sin of killing'; in 163 Neelakesi's first charge against the Buddha's way is that he does not call eating flesh a fault.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The hell scene supplies the doctrinal ground for the first debate: because eating is a sin apart from killing, a school that permits meat not killed by the eater stands accused before any metaphysics is argued.","gr":2},{"a":"nk:97","b":"k:255","type":"develops","note":"Kural 255 says hell will not open its mouth to release the flesh-eater; Neelakesi 97 shows the flesh-eater burning inside the fire's mouth, crying out.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet's closed mouth of hell, read beside the epic, becomes a place one is held and burned in, and the epic shows that the Kural's verdict falls on the eater as a sinner in his own right, not only on the killer.","gn":["the epic's charge against eating flesh gains the Kural's warrant that eating, not only killing, keeps one in hell","the couplet's bare image of a mouth that will not open gains a picture of what is suffered inside it"]},{"a":"nk:97","b":"t:198","type":"agrees","note":"Tirumular puts in fire-mouthed hell those who said 'kill, stab'; the Neelakesi puts there those who ate the flesh as well as those who killed.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"A Saiva and a Jain text each carry the guilt of a killing beyond the hand that strikes, one back to the man who ordered it and the other forward to the man who eats, so between them the whole chain around a death is held to account.","gn":["the Jain charge against the eater is seen as one case of a wider rule of shared guilt that a Saiva text also holds","Tirumular's extension of guilt to the speaker gains its counterpart in the consumer, which he leaves to his next verse on flesh"],"gr":3},{"a":"nk:99","b":"p:1244","type":"tension","note":"The Neelakesi's hell has no one to relieve the sufferer (பரிபவரும் இல்லையே); Arunagirinathar asks Murugan to come at speed when Death would drop him into the seven hells, so that his sins go.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The two answer opposite ways whether anyone can stand between a sinner's deeds and hell: the Jain sermon says no one can, the Murugan song asks that the god do exactly that; the conflict holds if பரிபவர் means one who relieves or rescues.","gn":["the Jain hell's lack of a rescuer is seen as a stance against the hope of divine rescue that Tamil devotion voices","the devotee's plea is seen against a doctrine for which such a plea has no one to hear it"]},{"a":"nk:86","b":"n:37","type":"qualifies","note":"Naladiyar 37 says the gains of human birth are many and should be used for the world beyond; Neelakesi 86 says only the humans of the land of deeds can reach bliss by penance.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The quatrain treats human birth as one opportunity, while the epic splits humanity by land and says the gain of release belongs only to those born where deeds and penance are possible.","gn":["the epic's cosmological sorting of humans gains an ethical point: it matters where one is born because only there can the human gain be used","the quatrain's general 'gains of human birth' gains a limit it does not state, that not every human birth carries them"],"gr":2},{"a":"nk:116","b":"nk:1","type":"develops","note":"The invocation praises the god whose word removes the suffering of souls; the sermon names that word's content as a medicine of three parts the soul takes.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The invocation's claim that the god ends suffering by his word, not by intervening, is made concrete here: the cure is a discipline the hearer takes, so the god's role stays that of teacher."},{"a":"nk:115","b":"nk:4","type":"develops","note":"The invocation says Neelakesi was a demoness of harm whose heart turned; the teacher, describing demons as fire to all, exempts her from her own kind.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Her conversion is acknowledged inside the sermon itself, which uses her former kind as its example of harm while addressing her as one already changed."},{"a":"nk:105","b":"nk:836","type":"develops","note":"The sermon lists killing for spirits and gods among the beasts' deaths; in the Veda debate Neelakesi tells the Vedic disputant that killings for the Vasus, Rudras and ancestors bring hard karma on the killers.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The sermon counts the sacrifice from the victim's side and the later debate from the sacrificer's, so the Jain case against the rite is made twice: the beast suffers, and the deed returns on the one who kills."},{"a":"nk:105","b":"k:259","type":"qualifies","note":"The Kural ranks abstaining from one killing above a thousand sacrifices; the Neelakesi lists the Vedic sacrifice among the deaths that make a beast's birth a suffering.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet weighs sacrifice and abstention as merits for the actor and leaves the rite standing as a lesser good; the Jain sermon removes it from the scale of merit by counting it as a harm to the slain beast.","gn":["the sermon's charge is seen as a sharpening of an older Tamil ranking that already put non-killing above the rite","the couplet's comparison is shown to be open to the harder reading that sacrifice is itself a killing, which a Jain teacher draws"]},{"a":"nk:108","b":"n:47","type":"develops","note":"The Naladiyar's fool hails a pot of filth as a woman because of its skin; the Neelakesi's men perish for a filthy body worm-filled under its cover.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The quatrain rests its case on disgust alone, while the sermon sets the same disgust beside the evil rebirth and the stained lineage, so the impure body becomes one of three reckonings a restrained man makes rather than the whole argument.","gn":["the sermon's compressed image of the covered body is explained by the quatrain's fuller picture of skin hiding the openings","the quatrain's single argument from the body's foulness is placed within a teaching of rebirth and family honour it does not state"],"gr":2},{"a":"nk:107","b":"k:360","type":"qualifies","note":"The Kural says pain perishes when the names of desire, anger and confusion perish; the Neelakesi names anger, pride, deceit and attachment as the very pains of the unrestrained.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Both make the passions the root of suffering, but they count them differently: the Kural's three (desire, anger, confusion) against the Jain four (anger, pride, deceit, attachment), so pride and deceit, absent from the couplet, are for the Jain teacher as basic as anger.","gn":["the Jain list is seen against an older Tamil triad and shows what it adds, pride and deceit","the couplet's triad is shown to be one count among rival counts of the passions, not the only Tamil one"]},{"a":"nk:116","b":"t:2075","type":"tension","note":"Both call the cure of the soul a medicine that leaves it clear: in the Neelakesi the soul takes the three remedies of knowledge, clear faith and conduct; in Tirumular the mind becomes clear when Nandi gives grace, like a madman cured by medicine.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The shared image of medicine and clarity hides opposed accounts of who heals: for the Jain sermon clarity is one of the remedies the soul swallows itself, for Tirumular it is the effect of the Lord's grace, and on the Jain reading no giver of grace is needed.","gn":["the sermon's godless remedy is seen as a real alternative to a Tamil account of release by grace that uses the same image","the verse's claim that clarity is given, not achieved, is sharpened by a rival who makes clarity something the soul takes"],"gr":2},{"a":"nk:116","b":"k:948","type":"develops","note":"The Kural tells the healer to find the disease, its source and the means that subdue it; the Neelakesi sermon classifies suffering as disease in 109-113 and then names its remedy.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The sermon follows the physician's order the couplet sets down, diagnosis by kinds before the cure, and applies it to the disease of birth, so the Kural's medical rule serves as the shape of a religious argument.","gn":["the sermon's long classification of harms is seen to have a purpose, the diagnosis that must precede the remedy","the couplet's method is shown at work outside medicine, governing how a teacher reasons about suffering itself"]},{"a":"nk:118","b":"mm:27:1-24","type":"tension","note":"The Vedic logician in the Manimekalai defines perception as fivefold sense knowledge; the Neelakesi's teacher counts as perception also avadhi, manahparyaya and kevala, knowledge without the senses.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The two epics define the same first means of knowledge incompatibly: for the logician perception ends at the five senses, for the Jain it reaches up to omniscience, which is what lets the Jain claim an all-knowing teacher as a source of valid knowledge."},{"a":"nk:119","b":"mm:27:41-56","type":"qualifies","note":"The Manimekalai's Vedic logician lists comparison, scripture, presumption and other means beside perception and inference; the Neelakesi's teacher lists memory, recognition, reasoning, inference and scripture.","grade":"weak","better":"tension","te":"Both lists keep inference and scripture, but the Jain list admits memory and recognition as valid knowledge and leaves out comparison and presumption, so the count of means is itself a point of difference between the schools the epics stage."},{"a":"nk:124","b":"k:46","type":"qualifies","note":"The Kural asks what a householder living in dharma gains by taking the outside way; the Neelakesi grades conduct as complete or partial.","grade":"weak","better":"tension","te":"If the partial conduct is the householder's, as Jain usage suggests, the sermon accepts the household as a path of dharma but ranks it below renunciation, which is just the ranking the couplet's question refuses.","gn":["the sermon's bare division into two grades is seen to answer a Tamil claim of the householder's equal standing","the couplet's rhetorical question is shown meeting a rival that grants the household dharma yet calls it incomplete"]},{"a":"nk:128","b":"nk:63","type":"develops","note":"At 63 she bears a form made to be desired so as to shake his penance; at 128 the sage answers that the same limbs are three different things to the lustful, the ascetic and the jackal.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The sermon on the body is a direct reply to the disguise: the form chosen to excite desire is answered by showing that desirability lies in the seer, so the trick fails at its root."},{"a":"nk:128","b":"n:46","type":"develops","note":"Naladiyar 46 lists entrails, blood, bone and skin and asks which of these is the garlanded woman; Neelakesi 128 lists the woman's beauties and says lovers, ascetics and jackals each see something else in them.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"The quatrain dissolves the beloved into parts, while the epic keeps the parts whole and moves the difference to the viewer, so the two give two routes to the same detachment: analysis of the object and analysis of the gaze.","gn":["the sage's three viewers gain the anatomical list that says what the ascetic and the jackal actually see","the quatrain's question gains a speaker and an addressee, a sage telling it to the woman herself"]},{"a":"nk:131","b":"mm:20:49-69","type":"same_image","note":"Manimekalai shows the prince an old woman's body and says flowers, sandal, cloth and jewels hide its flesh; the Neelakesi's sage tells the adorned woman herself that her bath, scents, jewels and garlands only cover filth with skin.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Buddhist and Jain epics use one argument with the roles reversed: in the Manimekalai a woman ascetic cures a man's lust, in the Neelakesi a male ascetic rebuffs a woman's, and here the body he unmasks is itself a borrowed disguise.","gr":3},{"a":"nk:132","b":"n:45","type":"develops","note":"Both speakers refuse to be swayed by praise of a woman's beauty because they have seen bones in the burning ground: the Naladiyar's speaker has seen the scattered teeth, the Neelakesi's sage lives among the bones.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The quatrain's resolve rests on having once seen the burning ground; the sage's rests on dwelling there, so the pair shows the same defence held as a memory and as a way of life.","gn":["the sage's boast gains the quatrain's precise point that beauty praised as jasmine and pearl ends as scattered teeth","the quatrain's resolve is shown put to a real test, a supernatural temptress who chose the burning ground's own sage"],"gr":2},{"a":"nk:134","b":"nk:59","type":"develops","note":"At 59 she takes the form of the princess Kamalekai to tempt the sage; at 134 she casts off that illusory form, takes her own, and asks pardon as a demon.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The disguise put on to break his vow is the first thing she gives up, so conversion begins with the end of deception and confession of her real nature, before any doctrine is taken.","gr":2},{"a":"nk:134","b":"k:579","type":"develops","note":"Kural 579 calls it the highest disposition to show kindly regard to and bear with even those whose way is to hurt one; the Neelakesi's sage is likened to a mother who in excess of kindly regard (கழிகண்ணோட்டம்) suckles the infant that hurts her.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The epic gives the couplet's high virtue an image and a result: the forbearance is maternal, and it converts the one who came to harm, which the couplet's rule does not promise.","gn":["the mother simile gains a name in the ethics books, கண்ணோட்டம் extended to those who harm","the couplet's virtue is shown working on an attacker who then gives up her violence"]},{"a":"nk:137","b":"nk:55","type":"develops","note":"At 55 the narrator tells how her terrors of elephant, tiger and lion failed to frighten him and she gave up; at 137 she herself sings that he stayed unshaken when she made the forest rise against him.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The failed assault becomes, in the attacker's own mouth, a title of praise, so the story's earlier defeat is retold as the ground of her devotion."},{"a":"nk:138","b":"k:314","type":"develops","note":"Kural 314 says the punishment for those who hurt you is to shame them by doing them good; Neelakesi 138 says the sage made her, who came to kill him, bow by his virtues alone.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The epic shows the couplet's punishment by goodness carried through to its end: the would-be killer is not only shamed but turned into a worshipper and, two verses later, an advocate.","gn":["her confession gains the couplet's frame, in which his forbearance is a form of punishment and not passivity","the couplet's counsel gains a case in which the wrongdoer was a deadly enemy and the result was conversion"],"gr":2},{"a":"nk:138","b":"s:603","type":"qualifies","note":"The Siddhar verse names the Lord as the one who opened his fire-eye and burned Kama; the Neelakesi's hymn says the sage in anger reduced Ananga to white ash.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"A Jain hymn takes the Saiva god's signature deed and gives it to a human ascetic, so the victory over desire that the Saiva texts reserve for the Lord is claimed as something a man achieves by penance.","gn":["the Jain epithet is seen as a borrowing from the Saiva stock of praise, pointed against it","the Siddhar's image of the Lord is shown contested by a school that gives his deed to the ascetic"],"gr":2},{"a":"nk:140","b":"k:340","type":"develops","note":"Kural 340 asks whether the life lodging in the body has no home of its own; Neelakesi 140 praises the sage who, while rain beat on him and poisonous creatures crawled over him, knew himself to be other than the body.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet's doubtful question about the soul's lodging becomes in the epic a settled conviction that is practised: knowing the self is other than the body is what lets the ascetic bear the body's torments.","gn":["the sage's endurance gains the couplet's image of the soul as a lodger, which explains why bodily suffering does not touch him","the couplet's question gains an answer from a school that holds soul and body distinct and turns that into discipline"],"gr":2},{"a":"nk:140","b":"s:824","type":"qualifies","note":"Pattinathar searches hair, skin, blood, bone and worms for the 'I', finds it nowhere, and says that only when the Lord's grace came did he see himself; the Neelakesi praises an ascetic who knows himself other than the body by his own steadfast penance.","grade":"weak","better":"tension","te":"Both hold that the self is not the body, but the Saiva poet reaches that knowledge only by grace while the Jain sage reaches it by enduring, so the shared insight hides a disagreement about how it is won.","gn":["the Jain claim gains a sharp contrast that shows it needs no god for the knowledge of the self","the Siddhar's turn to grace is shown to be a choice, since another school claimed the same insight without it"]},{"a":"nk:141","b":"t:1616","type":"tension","note":"Tirumanthiram 1616 makes Siva the renouncer who dwells in the burning ground and cuts off the births of those who renounce; Neelakesi 141 praises the ascetic who in the burning ground uprooted all his own karmas.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The same dwelling place holds two answers to who ends rebirth: in the Saiva verse the god of the burning ground cuts the renouncer's births, in the Jain hymn the renouncer there uproots his karma himself, a disagreement that turns on reading பல்வினையும் பறிப்போய் as his own karma.","gn":["the hymn's self-won release is set against the Saiva alternative it implicitly excludes","Tirumular's burning-ground renouncer-god is shown to answer a rival model in which no god is needed"]},{"a":"nk:147","b":"k:10","type":"develops","note":"Kural 10 says those who reach the Lord's feet swim the great sea of births; Neelakesi 147 says souls sunk in the sea of sorrow, born among beasts and in hells, should take refuge in the guileless great one.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The epic names what the couplet leaves open: the sea is filled with the births of beasts and hells, and the feet are those of the Jina, so a Jain teacher reads the Kural's refuge as his own.","gn":["the sage's call to refuge gains the Kural's image of crossing, which shows refuge as escape from rebirth","the couplet's unnamed Lord is given a sectarian identity and its sea a list of states"]},{"a":"nk:148","b":"nk:163","type":"develops","note":"At 148 the sage gives as her atonement the task of making the Jina's path clear; at 163 she chooses the Buddha's path, which does not call flesh-eating a fault, as the first to subdue.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The debates of the rest of the poem are her penance: the commission given as expiation for her violence is carried out first against the school she charges with tolerating killing for food."},{"a":"nk:148","b":"pn:34","type":"qualifies","note":"Purananuru 34 cites dharma as granting expiation (கழுவாய்) for killing cows, destroying unborn children and wronging elders, but none for ingratitude; in Neelakesi 148 the demoness asks expiation for her sins and is told to teach the Jina's path.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Both assume that grave wrongs admit expiation, but the Sangam maxim treats it as a provision of dharma with exceptions while the Jain sage turns it into a lifelong mission of persuasion.","gn":["the sage's command gains the older Tamil idea of a fixed expiation, against which its novelty shows","the maxim's list of expiable wrongs is shown to admit a case, killing and the will to kill, met by teaching rather than rite"]},{"a":"nk:155","b":"k:10","type":"qualifies","note":"Both use the sea of births: the Kural's பிறவிப் பெருங்கடல், crossed by reaching the Lord's feet; the Neelakesi's sea of the souls' own evil deeds, crossed on a raft the Jina made.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Jain hymn fills in what the couplet leaves open: the sea is made of the swimmer's own deeds, and the Lord's part is to build the raft, not to hold the swimmer.","gn":["the hymn's raft is seen answering a Tamil formula in which reaching the god is the only way across","the couplet's bare verb 'reach the feet' is shown to admit a reading in which reaching means following a path one must travel oneself"]},{"a":"nk:156","b":"k:5","type":"qualifies","note":"Both say that the two kinds of deed, good and evil, leave the soul: in the Kural, for those who love the Lord's true praise; in the Neelakesi, by the way the Jina taught under the asoka tree.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Sung in the middle of Neelakesi's own praise, the verse credits release from both deeds to the teaching the Jina gave, not to the praise itself, which the couplet makes sufficient.","gn":["the hymn's claim that merit too must go is matched by an older Tamil statement that both deeds are bonds","the couplet's 'true praise' (பொருள்சேர் புகழ்) is shown being read as praise of a teaching to be followed"]},{"a":"nk:156","b":"t:1527","type":"qualifies","note":"Both speak of the two deeds (இருவினை) and their ending: the Jina taught the way they part; Tirumular says that when they balance, grace-power comes as the guru and one's own doing ends.","grade":"weak","better":"tension","te":"On the same pair of deeds the Jain hymn gives the teacher only the role of telling the way, while Tirumular makes the guru a descent of grace that ends the soul's own agency.","gn":["the hymn's silence about grace becomes visible as a position once set beside a text that makes grace the agent","the Saiva doctrine of the balanced deeds is seen against a release in which no power descends and the soul does the work"]},{"a":"nk:154","b":"t:2","type":"qualifies","note":"Both praise the highest being as victor over Death: Siva kicked Death and became king of the south; the Jina passed beyond Death (காலனைக் கடந்தாய்) along with desire, aging and birth.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The same victory over Death means a deed of force against another being in the Saiva invocation and a leaving-behind of one's own mortal condition in the Jain hymn.","gn":["the hymn's war imagery is seen as borrowed from a praise tradition that told the victory as combat","the kick of the invocation is set against a victory that requires no enemy outside the self"]},{"a":"nk:163","b":"k:256","type":"develops","note":"Neelakesi's first charge against the Buddha is that he does not count eating flesh a fault; the couplet argues that flesh is sold only because people eat it.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet supplies the reason a Jain debater can blame the flesh-eater who kills nothing: the eating is what causes the killing, so silence on eating is silence on killing.","gn":["the bare charge in the verse gains the argument by which it is pressed later in the Neelakesi","the couplet's market argument is seen aimed at a school's actual position in Tamil debate"]},{"a":"nk:163","b":"kd:4","type":"qualifies","note":"Neelakesi charges that the Buddha does not count enduring austerity a vow; the Kundalakesi fragment calls bodily mortifications, nakedness among them, mere words, and puts tapas in restrained living.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Buddhist fragment confirms the position the Jain debater attacks but shows it as a relocation of austerity into conduct, not the plain denial of austerity the charge describes.","gn":["the Jain charge is seen against the opponent's own words","the fragment's list of empty practices is shown to have a Jain target that the Neelakesi takes up"]},{"a":"nk:165","b":"nk:190","type":"develops","note":"Neelakesi sets out to debate in order to do compassion (தயா செய்தற் பொருட்டாக); in the debate she asks whether one who let his own calf die has compassion (தயா) for others.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The debate is a contest over who owns compassion: the Jain debater's mission is framed as தயா, and her attack denies the Buddha the same virtue."},{"a":"nk:173","b":"nk:230","type":"tension","note":"Kundalakesi will not speak without a great prize for the winner; in 230 the winner, Neelakesi, refuses every gift the king offers and asks only that he keep to dharma.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"The stake Kundalakesi demanded turns against her twice: she suffers the loser's penalty, and the victor shows by refusing the prize that the Jain side did not argue for gain."},{"a":"nk:174","b":"nk:228","type":"develops","note":"The king pledges to drive out the losers that day; in 228 the beaten Kundalakesi is drummed out of the city gate.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The penalty the debaters agreed to at the start is carried out exactly, so the debate is framed as a binding royal trial and not a mere exchange of views."},{"a":"nk:176","b":"kd:1","type":"develops","note":"Kundalakesi's praise of her lord repeats, phrase for phrase, the invocation of the Kundalakesi: great from the first, thinking only good until the end, wanting nothing for himself, toiling for others.","grade":"weak","better":"tension","te":"The Neelakesi argues against the Kundalakesi's actual opening verse, so the surviving fragment is the text under attack and the debate is with a book, not an invented opponent."},{"a":"nk:177","b":"mm:30:183-197","type":"qualifies","note":"Both list the five aggregates; the Manimekalai's teacher names feeling நுகர்ச்சி and formation பாவனை, the Neelakesi's Kundalakesi names them உழப்பு and செய்கை.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"The Jain poet reports the aggregates accurately in substance, but naming feeling as pain (உழப்பு) and adding the flame of momentary perishing loads the list toward the points he means to attack."},{"a":"nk:177","b":"nk:194","type":"tension","note":"Kundalakesi states the five aggregates; in 194 Neelakesi answers that perception, feeling and formation are not other than consciousness, so five collapses to two.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The aggregate doctrine set out here is refuted by reduction later in the debate, which is why its claim is marked refuted here and not merely reported."},{"a":"nk:179","b":"kd:1","type":"tension","note":"The Kundalakesi's invocation opens 'he stood in greatness from the first' (முன் தான் பெருமைக்கண் நின்றான்); Neelakesi asks what 'first' can mean in a beginningless round of births.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"A word of praise in the Buddhist invocation is read as a doctrinal claim and put to a dilemma, showing how the Jain debater turns devotion into a proposition that can be refuted."},{"a":"nk:181","b":"n:80","type":"qualifies","note":"Neelakesi quotes the quatrain's first line, 'even if ruined, do not plan the ruin of the worthy', against a teacher who ruined himself for others and taught flesh-eating.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The quatrain's counsel to forbearance becomes a test of a rival teacher: self-ruin does not excuse doctrine that harms others.","gn":["the debater's charge rests on a saying the assembly already accepts, so it needs no Jain premise","the quatrain's line is shown circulating as a proverb that a debater can cite against a whole school"]},{"a":"nk:183","b":"mm:25:115-139","type":"tension","note":"The Manimekalai praises the Buddha as one who does not pity his own life and protects others' lives; Neelakesi takes the same countless lives of self-giving as evidence of countless sins.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The mark by which the Buddhist epic defines its teacher's dharma is exactly what the Jain debater reinterprets as karmic debt, so the two schools disagree over what suffering for others means."},{"a":"nk:184","b":"kd:19","type":"tension","note":"A Kundalakesi fragment praises a man who stood unmoved, saying all that had befallen him was the fruit of deeds; Neelakesi cites that same Buddhist tenet to argue the Buddha's sufferings were his own karma.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"A tenet shared by both schools, that suffering ripens from one's own deeds, cuts against the Buddhist claim that the teacher suffered for others.","gr":2},{"a":"nk:189","b":"t:1527","type":"tension","note":"Neelakesi says we will wear out our own deeds and go; Tirumular says that when the two deeds stand balanced, grace comes as the guru and ends one's own doing.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Jain debater denies that another's compassion can release, and Tirumular makes release depend on a grace that ends one's own agency, so they answer oppositely who removes karma.","gn":["the Jain self-reliance is shown to be aimed at any doctrine of release by another, not only the Buddhist one","Tirumular's grace is seen as one side of a live dispute in which a rival denies any outside agent of release"]},{"a":"nk:190","b":"k:251","type":"develops","note":"The Kural asks how one who eats another's flesh can hold compassion; Neelakesi asks the same of a teacher said to be all compassion who eats flesh.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet's general question becomes a charge against a named teacher's claim to compassion, so the Kural's link between flesh and compassion settles a dispute between schools.","gn":["the Jain charge borrows the authority of an accepted ethical rule instead of a sectarian one","the couplet's question is shown answering a concrete case: a teacher's compassion is measured by his food"]},{"a":"nk:190","b":"s:1777","type":"tension","note":"Neelakesi mocks a teacher who calls release emptiness (சூனியம்); the Idaikkadar Siddhar tells the soul that clinging to what became the void (சூனியமான அத்தை) is beyond compare.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The void that the Jain debater treats as an absurd account of release is, for a later Siddhar, the goal itself, so the word changes from a target of ridicule to a name of the highest state.","gn":["the ridicule is seen to target a word that later Tamil mystics would claim for release","the Siddhar's void is placed against an older debate in which calling release empty was a charge to answer"]},{"a":"nk:190","b":"nk:163","type":"develops","note":"In 163 Neelakesi resolves to strike down the Buddha's way, which does not call flesh-eating a fault; in 190 she makes that charge before the court.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The plan made alone at the shrine is carried out in the debate, so the charge of flesh-eating is the purpose of the whole sarukkam, not a passing taunt."},{"a":"nk:193","b":"nk:1","type":"develops","note":"The invocation praises as god only the one who knows all (எல்லா முணர்ந்தான்); verse 193 denies that the Buddha knew without remainder (ஒழிவின்றி யுணர்கலான்).","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The invocation's definition of god by omniscience is not only praise but the premise of the debate: it becomes the test by which the rival teacher's book is ruled out as scripture."},{"a":"nk:193","b":"mm:29:43-54","type":"tension","note":"The Buddhist teacher in the Manimekalai accepts only two means of knowledge, perception and inference; Neelakesi says a teacher who knew only through the senses and reasoning lacks subtle knowledge and his word is no scripture.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The same restriction to perception and inference that the Buddhist offers as rigour is, from the Jain side, the proof that its founder was not omniscient and so cannot ground a scripture."},{"a":"nk:194","b":"mm:30:183-197","type":"tension","note":"The Manimekalai names the five aggregates (form, feeling, perception, formation, consciousness); Neelakesi says three of them are modes of knowing, leaving two.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Set beside the Buddhist's own list, the Jain attack is seen to aim at the split of consciousness from feeling, perception and volition, collapsing the person into a knower and form, which is the Jain soul and matter by another route."},{"a":"nk:196","b":"mm:30:198-213","type":"tension","note":"The Manimekalai's teacher says the mind arises like lightning (சித்தம் உற்பவித்தது மின்போல்); Neelakesi asks how, if the mind perishes wholly with what it knew, its perishing could be known.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Buddhist offers the lightning-flash mind as a manner of speaking about what has no lasting being, while the Jain treats it as a claim about a knower and asks who is left to witness the flash's end.","gr":2},{"a":"nk:201","b":"mm:30:34-58","type":"tension","note":"The Manimekalai says the chain of becoming has no I and no mine, no going and no coming; Neelakesi says whatever carries traces across the moments is a soul, like the thread in a garland.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The point of disagreement is located exactly: both accept that something continues from moment to moment, and they differ only on whether that continuity needs a thread, a soul, running through it.","gr":2},{"a":"nk:202","b":"n:139","type":"same_image","note":"Both use the new pot that takes the scent of pathiri flowers: the Naladiyar for the unlearned who gain sense from the learned, the Neelakesi for the Buddhist example of traces that persist after the flower is gone.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"A homely image of borrowed fragrance served Tamil writers for transmission without identity, and the Jain debater shows that the image cuts both ways, since a scent that outlives its flower can be claimed for an enduring soul.","gn":["the Neelakesi's turn of the image is seen to work on a figure already proverbial in Tamil ethical verse","the quatrain's simile is shown to carry a philosophical question it leaves unasked: whether what is passed on is something that lasts"]},{"a":"nk:205","b":"k:72","type":"qualifies","note":"The Kural says the loving belong to others even to their bones; Kundalakesi lists the Buddha's literal gifts of eyes, body, wife and child as proof of his compassion.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet's figure of giving one's bones becomes in the birth-stories a literal record of bodily gifts, and the pairing shows where the figure turns contestable: once it includes wife and child, the Jain opponent can call it madness rather than love.","gn":["the catalogue of gifts is seen as an extreme reading of a Tamil ideal of love, not a foreign oddity","the couplet's hyperbole is shown being taken literally and so exposed to an objection it does not face on its own"]},{"a":"nk:207","b":"kd:1","type":"tension","note":"The Kundalakesi invocation praises its lord as one who kept nothing for himself and laboured only for others' good; Neelakesi calls the stories of the Buddha's self-giving the doings of the deluded and possessed.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Buddhist poem makes self-giving the very definition of its lord, so the Jain reply that such giving is madness strikes at the ground of the rival's devotion and not at a side issue."},{"a":"nk:208","b":"pn:43","type":"tension","note":"Purananuru 43 praises a Chola as descendant of the king of limitless giving who entered the scales to save a dove from a hawk; Neelakesi says no living person asks for another's flesh, eyes or head.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The gift of one's own flesh to a creature in distress, a dynastic boast in Sangam praise and a credential of compassion for the Buddhist, is denied any worth by the Jain debater's test of need, so Tamil tradition did not hold it as a settled virtue.","gn":["the debater's objection is seen to reject a gift that Tamil royal praise held up as the summit of bounty","the poem's ancestral boast is shown to have met a reasoned challenge in a later work"],"gr":2},{"a":"nk:222","b":"kd:1","type":"tension","note":"The Kundalakesi's invocation praises its lord as one who kept nothing for himself and laboured only for others' good (பிறர்க்கே உறுதிக்கு உழந்தான்); Neelakesi 222 mocks the monkey-king's self-offering, which saved no one, as no greatness.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The dispute is not whether the Buddha gave himself away but whether labour for others counts when, as the Jain argues, it profits no one; the Buddhist praise rests on intention, the Jain rebuttal on result.","gr":3},{"a":"nk:214","b":"kd:12","type":"same_image","note":"Flesh eaten by birds: the Kundalakesi fragment shows vultures and dogs tearing the desired body; Neelakesi 214 says flesh cut off as a gift feeds only crows.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The same carrion image serves the Buddhist as a reason to stop desiring the body and the Jain as a reason not to throw it away, so contempt for the body and refusal to waste it come from one picture."},{"a":"nk:214","b":"k:221","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 221 defines giving as giving to those who have nothing; Neelakesi 214 argues that a gift of flesh or an eye cannot become the receiver's and so fills no lack.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The Kural fixes a gift's worth by the receiver's want; the debate adds that the gift must also be usable by the receiver, which rules out the heroic gifts of the body.","gn":["the Jain objection gains a recognised Tamil definition of giving to argue from","the couplet's want-based test is extended to what is given, not only to whom"]},{"a":"nk:218","b":"k:230","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 230 says death is sweet when one can no longer give; Neelakesi 218 says a death known to achieve nothing is no virtue, and draws the wife on the pyre as the absurd consequence.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The Kural's rank of giving above life is hyperbole about value; the Jain argument shows where taking it literally leads, and limits the praise of self-loss to a death that does something.","gn":["the epic's limit on self-sacrifice is seen answering a Tamil maxim that ranks giving above living","the couplet's hyperbole is shown needing a boundary once epics told of givers who literally died"]},{"a":"nk:218","b":"pn:246","type":"tension","note":"Perunkoppendu argues for her place on her husband's pyre against the elders who hold her back; Neelakesi 218 cites the wife who burns with her guardian husband as the futile death no one should call virtue.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Sangam queen gives the pyre a reason, escape from the widow's diet and gravel bed, that the Jain reductio ignores when it calls such death one that achieves nothing; the two disagree on what the wife's death is for.","gn":["the epic's 'nothing comes of it' is tested against a widow who says exactly what comes of it","the queen's choice is seen being cited later as the standard case of a death a moral argument rejects"]},{"a":"nk:218","b":"mm:16:22-43","type":"qualifies","note":"Aathirai enters the pyre to go where her husband's deeds have carried him, but the fire will not touch her and a voice says he lives; Neelakesi 218 names the wife's death with her husband as the absurd end of praising futile death.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"Neither epic lets a wife's death on the pyre stand as achieved virtue, the Jain by argument and the Buddhist by a fire that refuses her, yet the Manimekalai gives the karmic motive the Neelakesi's reductio leaves out.","gr":2},{"a":"nk:224","b":"nk:205","type":"tension","note":"Kundalakesi lists her lord's gifts of eyes, flesh, body, wife, land, child and heaven (205); the king's verdict in 224 calls those birth-tales fabrications of a one-sided teacher.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The debate is settled not by refuting each gift but by the king reclassifying the whole catalogue as fiction, so the Buddhist's chief proof of compassion is turned into the proof of one-sidedness."},{"a":"nk:228","b":"nk:174","type":"develops","note":"Before the debate the king promised to send the loser out with shame (174); in 228 the townspeople drive Kundalakesi out of the gate with drums and insults.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The expulsion is the execution of a stake both debaters accepted with joy beforehand, so the shame is the contract of the debate rather than the mob's cruelty."},{"a":"nk:227","b":"nk:233","type":"develops","note":"The defeated Kundalakesi names Arkkachandran of Ujjain as her teacher (227); 233 opens the next sarukkam on Arkkachandran in Ujjain.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Each defeat in the Neelakesi names the next opponent, so the chain of debates is built by the losers' own testimony of who taught them."},{"a":"nk:230","b":"pn:5","type":"develops","note":"Given audience by the Chera, Nariveruuthalaiyar asks for nothing and tells him to guard the realm with grace and love; offered jewels, land and treasure, Neelakesi refuses them and tells the king to keep to the path of dharma.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The visitor who turns a king's favour into counsel persists from bardic to religious poetry, but the counsel shifts from ruling with grace to following a doctrine, and the refusal of gifts becomes a renouncer's mark.","gn":["Neelakesi's scene is seen as a known Tamil form, the counsel given in place of a gift","the Sangam form of counsel to a king is shown carried into a sectarian epic where the adviser wants nothing"]},{"a":"nk:242","b":"nk:249","type":"develops","note":"242 announces that Arkkachandra should hear the deceits (சலம்) bound up with the Buddhist vows; 249 ends the list by asking whether such a rule is not deceit (சலமதோ).","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The frame word shows that verses 243 to 249 are one indictment, so obscure items like the courtesan and Simhadatta verses belong to a single charge of sophistry rather than to separate anecdotes."},{"a":"nk:246","b":"nk:248","type":"develops","note":"246 sets out the no-soul doctrine against the Buddhist talk of a being who relieves pain and wins release; 248 cites a Buddhist assembly that recites that a person exists.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Neelakesi answers the no-soul claim not with Jain doctrine but with a split inside Buddhism, so the debate uses the opponent's own schools as witnesses."},{"a":"nk:246","b":"mm:30:170-182","type":"tension","note":"Aravana Adigal teaches that seeing that no thing has a self (எப்பொருளுக்கும் ஆன்மா இலை) is the nature of release; Neelakesi asks how release and relief of pain fit a teaching that there is no soul.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Buddhist epic makes no-self the content of release, while the Jain epic treats the same doctrine as making release impossible, since for it someone must be freed; the two verses disagree on whether release needs a subject."},{"a":"nk:243","b":"kd:1","type":"tension","note":"The Kundalakesi praises the Buddha as one who kept nothing for himself and toiled only for others; Neelakesi takes the bodhisattva's gift of himself and his wives as proof of a man bound by nothing.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The same total self-giving is the Buddhist invocation's ground of worship and the Jain debater's first charge, which shows the quarrel was over whether others (a wife) can be given as one's own.","gr":2},{"a":"nk:239","b":"kd:4","type":"qualifies","note":"The Kundalakesi calls covering or stripping the body, ash, cold, fire, alms and matted hair mere words, and true tapas a subdued life; Neelakesi asks whether the Buddhist rules of eating, shaving and kohl are dharma.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain","te":"Both schools use the same test, that outward discipline is not the path, and each turns it on the other: the Buddhist verse includes the Jain's nakedness among empty forms, and the Jain debater counts the Vinaya's grooming among them."},{"a":"nk:239","b":"k:280","type":"develops","note":"The Kural says neither shaving nor long hair is needed if one gives up what the world condemns; Neelakesi asks whether rules for shaving heads and eating are the path of dharma.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet's neutral rule that outward marks do not make the renouncer becomes in the epic a weapon against a rival order's rule-book, and the pairing shows the rule cuts against every order with marks, the Jain one included.","gn":["the challenge is seen to rest on a principle Tamil readers already granted, not only on sectarian scorn","the couplet's general rule is shown in use in inter-sect debate, where it decides which order's discipline counts as dharma"]},{"a":"nk:243","b":"n:56","type":"qualifies","note":"The Naladiyar says a husband cannot put away even a wife without good qualities; Neelakesi treats the bodhisattva's giving away of his wives as the act of a man no bond restrains.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"Her charge depends on the household norm the quatrain states, that a wife is not the husband's to dispose of, and applies it to judge a renouncer's gift, which the quatrain never contemplates.","gn":["the polemic's force is traced to a shared Tamil view of marriage rather than to Jain doctrine alone","the quatrain's bond of marriage is shown to set a limit even on religious giving"]},{"a":"nk:261","b":"nk:259","type":"tension","note":"259 says only the mind counts and conduct is not evil karma; 261 answers that release needs mind and outward conduct to agree.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The debate's central point is here: the Jain refuses to let a pure intention excuse a deed, so the meat question becomes a question of whether karma lives in the mind or in the act."},{"a":"nk:259","b":"mm:30:59-83","type":"tension","note":"The Neelakesi's monk says evil conduct is not evil karma if the mind is pure; the Manimekalai's Buddhist teacher counts killing, theft and lust of the body among the ten evil deeds that lead to hell.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Set beside a Buddhist epic's own list of bodily evil deeds, the monk's mind-only defence shows itself as the Jain poet's construction of Buddhism, not the doctrine a Tamil Buddhist text teaches."},{"a":"nk:256","b":"mm:30:183-197","type":"tension","note":"256 parodies 'all is suffering' as a greedy monk's complaints; the Manimekalai teacher gives suffering as the first truth and clinging as its cause.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The parody works by dropping the cause: the Buddhist epic ties suffering to clinging and its end to non-clinging, which the Jain song omits so the doctrine sounds like grumbling.","gr":3},{"a":"nk:260","b":"k:256","type":"develops","note":"Kural 256 argues that no one would sell flesh if no one killed to eat; 260 presses the same point against a Buddhist who says he only eats what another killed.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural's demand argument is put to work against a named defence, the eater's pure intention, and becomes a claim about karma: the buyer's deed joins the butcher's.","gn":["the retort to the monk gets a general reason why eating implies killing, which the verse only asserts","the couplet's abstract buyer is given a real opponent, a monk who claims his mind keeps him clean"]},{"a":"nk:255","b":"k:258","type":"qualifies","note":"255 mocks those who eat a creature's flesh once it has died; Kural 258 says those of faultless sight do not eat flesh that has parted from life.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"The Kural's phrase 'flesh parted from life' already covers the Buddhist allowance for meat from an animal that died, so the Jain jibe can claim the couplet's authority against that defence.","gn":["the sneer at eating the dead creature gains a stated principle that even flesh already lifeless is refused","the couplet's wording is shown to answer a specific sectarian defence of meat from animals already dead"],"gr":2},{"a":"nk:258","b":"k:275","type":"develops","note":"Kural 275 says the hidden conduct of those who claim to be free of attachment brings many evils; 258 charges monks who swell attachment while seeming free of it.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural leaves its 'many evils' unnamed; the Neelakesi names them as a ghost's birth or dark hell, turning a moral warning into a doctrine of rebirth.","gn":["the charge of hypocrisy is anchored in a recognised rule about false renouncers","the couplet's vague cry of evils is filled in with the specific rebirths a Jain expects for such conduct"]},{"a":"nk:261","b":"k:280","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 280 says neither shaven head nor matted hair is needed if one gives up what the world condemns; 261 says the outward guise is false unless mind and conduct agree.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Both discount the guise, but for different reasons: the Kural makes conduct the test of renunciation, and the Neelakesi uses that same test against a monk who makes the mind the only test.","gn":["the argument that the guise means nothing without matching conduct is given an older authority","the couplet's dismissal of outward marks is shown not to support a mind-only view, since its test is still what one does"]},{"a":"nk:263","b":"s:1777","type":"tension","note":"263 mocks the Buddha's void release for giving nothing to hold to; Siddhar 1777 bids the seeker cling close to that which became the void.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"Whether a void can be a goal one holds is exactly where they part: for the Jain debater the void offers no support, for the Siddhar it is the one thing to grasp.","gn":["the debater's objection is shown to rest on taking the void as mere absence, which another Tamil tradition denies","the Siddhar's call to cling to the void is set against the strongest objection to it, that no one desires an empty release"]},{"a":"nk:286","b":"nk:228","type":"tension","note":"Mokkalan says Kundalakesi conquered every rival creed on her naaval-branch vow; in 228 the narrator shows the same Kundalakesi, who planted the naaval branch, beaten with drums and driven out of the city gate.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Mokkalan's praise is refuted by the epic's own earlier scene, so the reader sees him defending a champion the story has already disgraced, and his charge of lying falls back on him."},{"a":"nk:289","b":"nk:291","type":"tension","note":"Mokkalan says the Jina's categories came from his own mind without knowing things as they are; Neelakesi answers that, since no fault has been shown and the Lord's word is truth, the categories stand.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The debate turns on the source of authority: the Buddhist attacks the categories by their origin in one mind, and the Jain defends them by the teacher's nature, so neither touches the categories themselves."},{"a":"nk:288","b":"mm:27:171-182","type":"develops","note":"Both give the same Jain list, opened with the same epithet (இந்திரர் தொழப்படும் இறைவன் in the Manimekalai, இந்திரர்கள் தொழப்படுவான் here) and framed by the same questions of lord, categories, their workings, bondage and release.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Jain categories circulated as a fixed formula that both sects' epics quote; the Buddhist epic lets a Jain recite it and moves on, while the Jain epic puts it in a Buddhist's mouth so that its defence can follow."},{"a":"nk:272","b":"k:280","type":"qualifies","note":"The Kural says neither shaving nor matted hair is needed if one avoids what the world condemns; Mokkalan grants the robe only the small use of making the monk known, and Neelakesi asks what such a sign is for.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural's dismissal of ascetic marks becomes a live weapon between sects: the Jain debater uses it against the Buddhist robe, while her own tradition's nakedness is a mark of another kind.","gn":["Neelakesi's question about the robe is seen to rest on an accepted Tamil maxim that outward marks are not the test of renunciation","the couplet's general maxim is shown being turned by one order against another's dress, a use it does not foresee"],"gr":2},{"a":"nk:278","b":"k:298","type":"develops","note":"The Kural says outer purity (புறந்தூய்மை) comes by water and inner purity by truth; Neelakesi lists water, betel and the tooth-stick and says that if outer cleaning purified the mind, courtesans would be the purest.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"The couplet's two-part rule is turned into a reductio: Neelakesi supplies the absurd case that proves outer and inner purity are separate, and aims it at monks who tend their robes.","gn":["Neelakesi's jibe gains a recognised rule behind it, so the courtesan example reads as proof rather than insult","the couplet gains a counter-example that shows why water cannot reach the mind, which it states without argument"]},{"a":"nk:282","b":"t:724","type":"qualifies","note":"Tirumular keeps the body because without it true knowledge cannot be won; Neelakesi cites as common knowledge that the body lives by food and awareness by the body.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The same chain, food to body to knowledge, serves opposite ends: Tirumular uses it to justify cultivating the body, while Neelakesi uses it to say the renouncer needs food and nothing more.","gn":["Neelakesi's proverb is shown to be a premise other traditions also build on, not her private argument","Tirumular's case for nurturing the body is shown limited by a rival use of the same premise, where it licenses only bare food"],"gr":2},{"a":"nk:277","b":"s:357","type":"tension","note":"Pattinattar counts the begging bowl (இரந்துண்ணும் ஓடு) among the renouncer's wealth without lack; Neelakesi says the bowl is costly, easily lost and useless to one who does penance without fault.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The begging bowl, emblem of renunciation for the Saiva poet, is for the Jain debater a possession that burdens: whether renunciation keeps even a bowl divides the traditions.","gn":["Neelakesi's attack on the bowl is seen against a living ideal that prized it, so its edge against Buddhist practice is clearer","the Siddhar's praise of poverty is shown to keep a possession that a stricter rule would give up"]},{"a":"nk:279","b":"s:1480","type":"qualifies","note":"Sivavakkiyar mocks the ochre-wearers with matted hair, water-pots and yoga staffs who leave their wives and beg from house to house; Neelakesi mocks the monk who boils and dyes his cloth as though he had only given up cooking rice.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain","te":"Jain and Siddhar both ridicule the ochre-robed renouncer, but for opposite failings: Sivavakkiyar faults him for abandoning home duties, Neelakesi for carrying them on in the monastery.","gn":["Neelakesi's joke is seen to belong to a wider Tamil satire of the ochre robe, aimed here at its labour rather than its begging","Sivavakkiyar's charge that the renouncer abandons home is set against a charge that he never left it"]},{"a":"nk:275","b":"k:126","type":"develops","note":"The Kural says that one who restrains the five senses as a tortoise draws in its limbs is protected through seven births; Neelakesi says the monk should guard the eyes that stir the mind instead of covering his limbs.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The tortoise image of drawing in the limbs is exactly what Neelakesi denies is restraint when done with cloth: she moves the Kural's withdrawal from the body to the senses, against the robe.","gn":["her charge gains a standard of restraint the Buddhist side would also grant, so the robe can be judged by it","the couplet's figure is shown to need a reading, since hiding the limbs literally is what she calls weakness"]},{"a":"nk:293","b":"mm:27:183-201","type":"qualifies","note":"The Manimekalai's Jain speaker says dharma pervades everything and makes things move (பொருள்களை நடத்தும்); Neelakesi calls the reading 'we move by dharma' a quibble and says dharma lets things move as the sea lets a ship go, without pushing.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"A Jain debater rejects as a misreading the very wording a Buddhist epic puts in a Jain's mouth: dharma is a condition of motion, not a mover, a distinction the Buddhist report blurs."},{"a":"nk:297","b":"mm:27:183-201","type":"develops","note":"The Manimekalai's Jain says time makes the short occurrence called கணிகம் and the long one of கற்பம்; Neelakesi throws the same two words at the Buddhist who denies time while using them.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The pair கணிகம் and கற்பம் was the Jain definition of time, so when Neelakesi mocks the Buddhist for speaking of moments she is pointing out that the word for his momentariness belongs to a doctrine of time he rejects."},{"a":"nk:312","b":"mm:27:183-201","type":"agrees","note":"Both give the Jain doctrine that a thing persists in kind while its form arises and perishes: the Manimekalai's Jain with gram cooked into a dish and the sprouting neem, Neelakesi with a gold pot remade as a crown, applied to the soul passing from man to god.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"On arising, persistence and destruction the Buddhist epic's report of the Jains matches a Jain debater's own illustration, so here the polemical account is one the school would accept, and the Neelakesi shows it was aimed at the soul's rebirth.","gr":3},{"a":"nk:296","b":"mm:29:373-384","type":"qualifies","note":"Neelakesi says the Buddhist has no space of measureless extent; the Manimekalai's teacher of logic names both 'one who says space is unreal' and 'one who says it is real' as parties to an argument, without taking a side there.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Buddhist manual treats the reality of space as a disputed thesis between parties, while the Jain debater fixes the denial on the Buddhist and draws its cost, that the world then has no edge.","gr":2},{"a":"nk:299","b":"mm:29:43-54","type":"tension","note":"The Manimekalai's teacher says the first Jina (in Buddhist usage, the Buddha) taught only two means of knowledge, perception and inference; Neelakesi answers that on that rule there could be no medical treatise that cures disease.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Buddhist rule of two means of knowledge, stated as doctrine in one epic, meets in the other a counter-case from medicine, showing that the dispute over scripture as a means of knowledge was argued in Tamil from practical knowledge, not only from revelation."},{"a":"nk:304","b":"mm:30:183-197","type":"tension","note":"The Manimekalai's teacher lists the five aggregates (form, feeling, perception, formation, consciousness) as what is spoken of as a person; Neelakesi names feeling, cognition, perception and action (உழப்பு, அறிவு, குறி, செய்கை) and says they belong to one being.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Jain debater accepts the Buddhist list of the four mental aggregates term for term and disputes only whether they have one owner, so the quarrel over the self is about ownership of the aggregates, not about what they are.","gr":2},{"a":"nk:310","b":"nk:37","type":"develops","note":"In the opening sermon the sage tells the worshippers that a child's body comes from blood and its life from former deeds; here Neelakesi explains how the soul takes up the father's seed and the mother's blood and grows within the body.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The sage's bare division between a body from the parents and a life from former deeds is filled in by the debate with the mechanism of conception that lets the Jain deny any god a part in it."},{"a":"nk:299","b":"k:941","type":"develops","note":"Neelakesi says that if only perception and inference counted there could be no medical treatise; the Kural's chapter on medicine states the three humours on the authority of the learned in the books (நூலோர்).","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural's deference to the medical books for the three humours is just the kind of text-borne knowledge the Jain debater cites: neither the patient's sight nor his inference gives it.","gn":["the debater's appeal to medicine gets a Tamil instance in which a moral book takes medical doctrine on its authors' word","the couplet's passing reference to நூலோர் is seen to carry a view of knowledge that a rival school in Tamil denied"]},{"a":"nk:313","b":"k:256","type":"develops","note":"Neelakesi rejects the Buddhist rule that the sin of killing attaches only where there is intention; the Kural argues that if none killed to eat, none would sell flesh for a price.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural's buyer-and-seller argument is the reply the intention defence needs: the eater need intend no killing for his eating to cause one, so a rule that weighs only intention misses the chain from buyer to slaughter.","gn":["Neelakesi's bare charge that the sin reaches the eater gets a causal argument behind it","the couplet's unnamed opponent is identified as a live doctrine, that sin requires the intent to kill"]},{"a":"nk:316","b":"k:261","type":"qualifies","note":"The objection in Neelakesi 316 is that if pleasure and pain are the fruit of deeds, the pain of penance is too; the Kural defines penance as bearing the pain that comes and harming no life.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The Kural's definition of தவம் as bearing the pain that comes takes away the force of the objection: if penance is endurance of what arrives, it does not matter that the pain is old karma's fruit.","gn":["the epic's objection meets a Tamil definition of penance that already answers it","the couplet's உற்றநோய் is shown to bear on a live dispute about whether penance's pain is only karma ripening"]},{"a":"nk:307","b":"t:456","type":"tension","note":"Neelakesi says deeds move the soul to its next birth as the wind turns a whirligig; Tirumular says the subtle breath in the soul carries it as wind carries a flower's scent.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"Both answer what carries a soul between bodies with an image of wind, but for the Jain the wind is the deed itself, needing no other mover, while for Tirumular it is the vital breath at the appointed moment, so the same image names karma in one and breath in the other.","gn":["the whirligig image is set against a Tamil alternative it leaves out, breath as the carrier","Tirumular's image is seen as one answer to a question a rival school answered with deeds alone"]},{"a":"nk:309","b":"t:472","type":"same_image","note":"Neelakesi's fiery body enters another body like a shadow; Tirumular's light born in the womb spreads through the earthly body like a bubble in water, a shadow, and the eight take hold.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Writers of opposed schools both picture the subtle body that passes into a new one as a shadow, which shows they agreed rebirth needs a subtle carrier and differed only on what it is made of, the Jain fiery and karmic bodies or Tirumular's eight.","gn":["the Jain doctrine of the subtle bodies is seen to have a Saiva counterpart, so the debate does not turn on whether a subtle body exists","Tirumular's obscure bubble-and-shadow line gets a plain statement of a shadow-like body entering another"]},{"a":"nk:318","b":"nk:238","type":"develops","note":"In 238 Neelakesi hears the Vinaya rule of how to shave the head recited in Arkkachandra's monastery; in 318 she cites the same tonsure (தலைமழி) against Mokkalan's claim that bodily hardship is a fault.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Vinaya rules she overheard as ritual detail at Ujjain become her evidence that the Buddhist middle way still imposes discipline on the body, so the earlier scene was gathering ammunition."},{"a":"nk:319","b":"mm:3:82-103","type":"tension","note":"The Manimekalai's drunk pleads with an unwashed Jain ascetic (மண்ணா மேனியன்) that his soul should not suffer in a filthy body; the Neelakesi answers that dirt on the body is nothing to correct, since adornment hinders meditation.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The unwashed Jain ascetic was a stock target in the Buddhist-Jain quarrel: the Buddhist epic mocks it through a drunkard's pity, and the Jain epic gives the reply, that the filth is the body's own and attending to it is the real distraction."},{"a":"nk:319","b":"kd:11","type":"develops","note":"The Kundalakesi fragment says filth pours from every opening of the body, so no fragrance is to be found in it; the Neelakesi says dirt on the ascetic is simply the human body's nature.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Buddhist poet's own premise, that the body is filth through and through, is the ground of the Jain reply: if the body is foul by nature, washing it cannot make it clean, and mocking the unwashed ascetic contradicts the Buddhist's teaching on the body."},{"a":"nk:321","b":"k:345","type":"qualifies","note":"The Kural asks what other ties (தொடர்ப்பாடு) can remain for those cutting off birth, when even the body is excess; the Neelakesi defends those who teach that at death every tie, even to dharma (அறத்தொடர்ப்பாடு), must go.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The Kural's list of ties ends at the body, while the Jain teaching carried into the hour of death adds dharma itself to what must be let go, so renunciation's last step is dropping the merit that led there.","gn":["the Jain teaching of letting go at death is seen to extend a rule the Tamil ethics book already states for the renouncer","the couplet's 'further ties' is given a hard case it does not name: the tie to dharma, and a moment, the time of dying"]},{"a":"nk:327","b":"nk:331","type":"develops","note":"327 promises to establish that refusing flesh is a good deed; 331 completes it by turning the Buddhist view that image-makers and worshippers share merit into the view that killers and eaters share sin.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Neelakesi's proof against meat is built from the opponent's own devotional practice: once merit can pass to those who stand at a distance from the act, sin can too, and the eater cannot claim to stand clear of the killing."},{"a":"nk:333","b":"k:251","type":"develops","note":"The Kural asks how one who eats another's flesh to swell his own can hold to compassion (அருள்); Neelakesi asks a monk who eats flesh so that his body thickens whether he has any compassion (அருள்).","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet's question, put to anyone, becomes in the debate a charge against a school that makes compassion its centre, so its force is to expose a contradiction inside a teaching rather than to state a general rule.","gn":["the taunt draws on a rule Tamil readers already held, so it reads as a common standard and not only a sectarian one","the couplet is shown aimed at a real opponent who claimed compassion while eating meat, which the couplet alone does not name"]},{"a":"nk:337","b":"k:256","type":"develops","note":"The Kural says no one would bring meat to sell if the world did not kill to eat; Neelakesi tells the monk who calls buying meat fair that by paying the fishermen daily he wants killing always.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet's market argument is turned on a particular defence, that meat bought or given is clean of killing, and shown to cover the buyer who pays day after day, so steady demand makes him a party to each killing.","gn":["Neelakesi's argument is seen to rest on a principle already stated in the Tamil ethical canon","the couplet's general demand-and-supply claim is applied to the Buddhist rule of meat accepted from others, a case it does not name"]},{"a":"nk:335","b":"nk:163","type":"develops","note":"In 163 Neelakesi resolves to bring down the Buddha's path because he does not call eating flesh a fault; in 335-337 she argues it point by point against Mokkalan.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The charge stated in one line at the outset of her debates turns out to need a long argument against particular defences, that the killer was hidden, the animal not marked out, and the meat paid for."},{"a":"nk:342","b":"mm:16:78-89","type":"qualifies","note":"Saathuvan tells the Naga chief that the faultless forbade liquor and the killing of living beings; Neelakesi argues that those who shun toddy should for the same reason shun flesh.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Buddhist epic's own ban joins liquor with killing, not with eating meat, and the Jain debater exploits exactly that gap, asking why the reasoning that forbids toddy stops before flesh.","gr":2},{"a":"nk:347","b":"mm:16:107-116","type":"tension","note":"Saathuvan lets the chief stop killing yet still eat beasts that die of old age; Neelakesi holds that flesh is itself a fault and that its renunciation by all would end the killing.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"For the Buddhist story the wrong lies in the killing, so meat from a natural death is allowed, while for the Jain debater eating is wrong in itself because it is what keeps killing going; the two epics draw the line at different acts."},{"a":"nk:347","b":"k:256","type":"develops","note":"Kural 256 says no one would sell flesh if the world did not kill to eat; Neelakesi says that if all renounced flesh no one would go near killing, and that the teacher who teaches dharma earns its merit.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet blames the eating world for the killing; the debate adds that a world's eating follows its teachers, so a teacher who permits flesh carries the demand argument's guilt.","gn":["Neelakesi's bare 'if all renounced it' gains the Kural's market reasoning that links each buyer to the killing","the couplet's faceless 'world' gains a concrete cause of its habits, the teacher whose word makes flesh-eating dharma"]},{"a":"nk:344","b":"nk:260","type":"develops","note":"In 260 Neelakesi tells Arkkachandra that the eater shares the butcher's karma whatever his mind; in 344 she meets Mokkalan's same plea of innocent intention with the seller who kills for the price.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Against two Buddhist teachers the Jain debater answers the defence by intention in two ways, by the butcher's act and by the merchant's motive, so the epic builds a consistent case that the buyer's purpose is the cause."},{"a":"nk:350","b":"kd:4","type":"develops","note":"The Kundalakesi fragment lists exposure, fire, cold, begging and matted hair as mere words and calls living subdued the true penance; Mokkalan lists sun, rain, fasting and sleeplessness as profitless.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"The Buddhist dismissal of mortification, stated in the fragment as a positive ideal of restraint, appears in the Jain epic only as a negative charge that it can then answer, so the Neelakesi keeps the attack and drops the Buddhist alternative."},{"a":"nk:350","b":"s:830","type":"qualifies","note":"Pattinattar lists the five fires, lying in water, plucking hair and going without food and says his devotees reached release without them; Mokkalan lists sun, rain, fasting and sleeplessness as bringing no gain.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Buddhist debater and Saiva poet reject the same austerities for opposite reasons: for Mokkalan pain has no power to free, for Pattinattar the god's name frees without it, so a shared rejection hides a disagreement about what does the freeing.","gn":["Mokkalan's attack is seen to belong to a wider Tamil rejection of mortification, not only to anti-Jain polemic","the poem's list of austerities, which includes plucking the hair, is seen as the Jain practice a Buddhist also attacked, so its claim of a better way enters an existing quarrel"]},{"a":"nk:350","b":"nk:357","type":"tension","note":"Mokkalan says exposure, fasting and sleeplessness bring nothing; Neelakesi replies that the Buddha's own perfections and ascetic rules bring many hardships.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"The Buddhist attack on Jain penance is met not by defending pain but by showing the Buddhist path also prescribes sleeping in the open and eating once, so the quarrel becomes one of degree rather than of principle."},{"a":"nk:357","b":"nk:282","type":"qualifies","note":"In 282 Neelakesi herself says the body lives by food and awareness by the body; in 357 she mocks the Buddhist claim that cold, heat and hunger keep dharma out of the mind.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Jain debater grants that the mind needs a fed body but not that it needs a comfortable one, so her two arguments mark a line between sustaining the body and sparing it."},{"a":"nk:351","b":"k:261","type":"tension","note":"Kural 261 makes bearing pain one half of penance; Mokkalan argues that if wasting the body were good, burning or wasting with disease would also be penance.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The reductio bites only if the couplet's pain is pain one inflicts on oneself; if it is the pain that comes unsought, the Kural and the Buddhist agree, so the pairing shows that the couplet's word உற்ற decides which side it is on.","gn":["Mokkalan's argument gains a precise target: a definition of penance in which enduring pain is half the whole","the couplet's ambiguity about whose pain is meant is shown to matter, since a rival school's objection falls on one reading and not the other"],"gr":2},{"a":"nk:352","b":"t:272","type":"qualifies","note":"Tirumular says that even burning one's bones as firewood and frying one's flesh does not reach the Lord without melting love; Mokkalan says cutting wounds and leaping from mountains serve no purpose.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Both reject bodily extremity as the way, but Tirumular keeps it as a measure that love surpasses while Mokkalan declares it simply useless, so the Saiva verse still honours the ascetic's pain where the Buddhist does not.","gn":["Mokkalan's list gains a Saiva witness that self-torment was a recognised extreme to be gone beyond","Tirumular's hyperbole is seen against a debate in which such acts were literally practised and literally attacked"]},{"a":"nk:353","b":"nk:358","type":"tension","note":"Mokkalan says that if self-torment were dharma, mutilation and impalement would be excellent; Neelakesi answers that he praises the bodhisattva for enduring having his flesh cut and his body split.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Buddhist's own hero stories contain the very bodily sufferings he ridicules, so the reductio is turned on him: either the bodhisattva's endurance was folly or the Jain's is not."},{"a":"nk:360","b":"nk:214","type":"develops","note":"In 214 Neelakesi argues that a gift of flesh or an eye cannot join another's body and so helps no one; in 360 she argues that the gift of the body to a beast kills the many lives within it.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Jain case against the bodhisattva's self-giving has two prongs across the debates: the gift is useless to the receiver and murderous to the small lives in the giver, so it fails both as charity and as non-harm."},{"a":"nk:360","b":"kd:14","type":"same_image","note":"The Kundalakesi fragment calls the body a deed-made thing where swarms of worms small and grown feed and live; Neelakesi says the many lives within the bodhisattva's body die when he gives it to the beast.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Buddhist verse uses the body's teeming worms to teach disgust for it, while the Jain debater uses the same teeming body to charge the Buddhist hero's gift with mass killing, so one image serves renunciation on one side and non-harm on the other.","gr":3},{"a":"nk:372","b":"nk:77","type":"develops","note":"The sermon told Neelakesi the fixed beings are of five kinds; here she defends that teaching against a Buddhist by calling trees fixed beings (தாவரம்) that everyone knows to be such.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The doctrine she received as a convert's lesson becomes the first ground she must hold in open debate, and she defends it by appeal to common usage rather than to the sage's authority."},{"a":"nk:370","b":"nk:257","type":"develops","note":"In the Arkkachandra debate she named the felling of living trees among sins that bring hell; here she argues against Mokkalan that trees really are alive.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The earlier charge that felling a tree is a sin rests on a premise the Buddhists deny, and this passage supplies the argument for it: healing, feeding and withering show the tree to be a soul one can wrong."},{"a":"nk:388","b":"mm:27:183-201","type":"tension","note":"Both use gram cooked into a mash (கும்மாயம்) as the Jain example of a kind that persists while its form passes; the Manimekalai lets the Jain state it unanswered, the Neelakesi puts it under a Buddhist's attack.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The same stock example served the two traditions differently: the Buddhist epic records it as the Jain's own illustration and moves on, while the Jain epic has a Buddhist demand to be shown the persisting thing, so the example's weak point is named by the side that wrote it down.","gr":2},{"a":"nk:382","b":"mm:30:244-264","type":"tension","note":"The Manimekalai's teacher lists impermanence, suffering, no-self and impurity as marks to contemplate in order to cut attachment; Neelakesi names the same four as qualities the Buddhists lay on the aggregates.","grade":"weak","better":"qualifies","te":"The four marks the Buddhist teacher gives as a meditation for release are read by the Jain debater as predicates of a bearer, so the Buddhist list is turned into evidence for the substance-and-quality relation Mokkalan denies."},{"a":"nk:384","b":"mm:30:244-264","type":"tension","note":"Mokkalan says that to declare a thing inexpressible is self-contradiction; the Manimekalai's Buddhist teacher counts keeping silent before an ill-formed question as a proper kind of answer.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Buddhist in the Jain epic attacks the Jain mode of the inexpressible with an argument his own school, as the Manimekalai presents it, would have to face, since it too treats not saying as an answer."},{"a":"nk:381","b":"mm:30:214-229","type":"tension","note":"Mokkalan demands that substance and quality be declared either one or different; the Manimekalai's teacher gives a naya of oneness and a naya of difference as two valid ways of seeing cause and effect.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"Mokkalan's either-or is stricter than the Buddhist doctrine the Manimekalai sets out, which itself allows a thing to be viewed as one with its cause under one naya and as different under another, close to the Jain answer he rejects.","gr":2},{"a":"nk:368","b":"k:576","type":"qualifies","note":"The Kural likens men without kindly regard to trees fixed in the soil; Neelakesi argues that trees feel heat and cold and change as living bodies do.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"Read with the Jain argument, the couplet's tree is not a dead thing but a living being that feels by touch yet has no eye to look with, which makes the comparison exact for a man who has eyes and does not use them kindly.","gn":["the Jain claim that trees feel is seen against a Tamil commonplace that used the tree as the very type of the unfeeling","the couplet's simile gains a precise sense under the Jain scale of senses: the tree lacks sight, not life"]},{"a":"nk:377","b":"k:331","type":"qualifies","note":"The Kural calls it the lowest folly to take what does not last for what lasts; Mokkalan and Neelakesi dispute whether a thing can both last and not last.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The couplet assumes things fall cleanly into the lasting and the passing, and the debate shows that division itself was contested, the Jain holding one thing lasting in substance and passing in its modes.","gn":["the debate's technical question is tied to a Tamil moral warning about mistaking the passing for the lasting","the couplet's simple contrast is shown to stand on a metaphysical choice the schools did not share"],"gr":3},{"a":"nk:391","b":"mm:27:183-201","type":"agrees","note":"The Jain in Manimekalai 27 gives gram cooked into கும்மாயம் as his example of a kind that persists while a form passes; in Neelakesi 390 to 391 the Buddhist attacks that very example and Neelakesi defends it.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The cooked-gram example is shown to be a fixed Jain teaching case known to both sides, so the Buddhist epic's report of Jain doctrine matches what the Jain epic itself has to defend."},{"a":"nk:392","b":"mm:27:171-182","type":"develops","note":"Manimekalai's Jain states that every thing takes on origination, persistence and destruction in each instant; Neelakesi 392 has the Buddhist ask what bears those three if they are qualities.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Manimekalai lets the Jain triad stand unanswered, while the Jain epic shows the Buddhist's actual objection to it, the missing bearer, and the Jain reply in 393 that quality and bearer are one and different."},{"a":"nk:393","b":"mm:27:241-262","type":"tension","note":"The Vaisheshika in Manimekalai 27 lists substance and quality as separate categories, substance bearing quality; Neelakesi says the quality is the qualified substance itself and the two are both different and one.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Against the Vaisheshika separation of substance and quality, the Jain refuses both their separateness and their simple identity, so the two schools disagree on whether a quality can be counted apart from its bearer."},{"a":"nk:397","b":"mm:27:183-201","type":"develops","note":"Manimekalai's Jain says only that the atom, matter (புற்கலம்), is the outward form of things; Neelakesi 395 to 397 defends how atoms become form against the objection that touching atoms must have parts.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The bare formula in the Buddhist epic hides a live problem, how indivisible atoms can join, which the Jain epic answers with many atoms sharing one place."},{"a":"nk:404","b":"mm:30:214-229","type":"agrees","note":"Manimekalai's Buddhist teacher gives the hare's horn as the case of what has no arising at all; Neelakesi's Buddhist uses the hare's and donkey's horns to show that some things are non-existent outright.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Jain epic's Buddhist argues with the same stock example the Buddhist epic teaches, so here the polemic reproduces Buddhist doctrine fairly before Neelakesi answers it in 405.","gr":2},{"a":"nk:412","b":"mm:30:198-213","type":"agrees","note":"Manimekalai's teacher lists modes of usage that rest on what is not (இல்லது சார்ந்த வழக்கு), and pairs cognition with the hare's horn; Neelakesi's Buddhist insists that names and cognitions occur for what does not exist.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Holding the two together shows the Buddhist claim in 412 rests on a worked-out doctrine of usage, which is what Neelakesi's reply in 413 must deny by appeal to scripture."},{"a":"nk:398","b":"nk:243","type":"same_image","note":"In 243 Neelakesi charges that the bodhisattva gave away his wives and took that as dharma; in 398 the narrator names the Buddhist debater as great in the dharma that gives away women.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"A charge made once in argument has become the narrator's standing epithet for the Buddhist side, so the poem's narration takes Neelakesi's part even in describing the speaker.","gr":2},{"a":"nk:392","b":"nk:403","type":"develops","note":"392 asks what bears arising, persisting and perishing; 403 applies the answer to the calf and the bull, whose horn arises while the substance persists.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The abstract triad of 392 to 393 is turned into a test case in 403, which shows what the Jain means by persistence: the animal's substance, not its horn.","gr":2},{"a":"nk:413","b":"t:51","type":"qualifies","note":"Tirumular says the wise gave up disputation (தர்க்க வாதம்) and gained release by reciting the Veda; Neelakesi appeals to the Apta's scripture as what leaves nothing unknown, but does so as a move in a debate.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Both rest final authority on a scripture, but the Saiva verse sets scripture against argument while the Jain debater uses scripture as the ground of argument, so the two disagree about whether debate serves the scripture.","gn":["the Jain appeal to scripture is seen as one way to end a dispute, which a rival tradition takes as reason to give up disputing","Tirumular's dismissal of argument is seen as a choice, since another scriptural tradition put argument at the centre of its practice"]},{"a":"nk:411","b":"n:313","type":"develops","note":"Naladiyar 313 describes people who rush to debate on the strength of a word, talk at length and do not know when they are beaten; Neelakesi tells the Buddhist he does not know words and prattles many words like a ghost.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Naladiyar's type of the endless talker becomes a charge thrown by one debater at another, which shows the description used as a weapon in debate as well as a moral portrait.","gn":["Neelakesi's insult is placed within a known ethical type, the speaker who does not know when he is beaten","the quatrain gains a scene where its charge is made by a party to the dispute, which shows that calling the other side a prattler is itself a debating move"]},{"a":"nk:417","b":"k:222","type":"tension","note":"The Kural says giving alone is good even if there were no higher world; in 416-417 both debaters take the giver's reaching heaven as what makes giving worth doing, and Neelakesi calls the identity of giver and god the truth of giving (தானத்தின் உண்மை).","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural's 'even if there were no higher world' is a position neither the Jain nor the Buddhist debater will hold here, since both stake the worth of alms on a rewarded doer.","gn":["The epic's grounding of giving in reward is shown to be a choice that another ethics declined.","The couplet's hypothetical is seen against a real debate in which a giver without a future was taken to make giving void."]},{"a":"nk:415","b":"k:338","type":"qualifies","note":"The Kural likens the body-life bond to a bird leaving its nest; Neelakesi holds the soul joined with the body it has taken and makes it the one who sees and knows there.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The Kural's bird stresses how loosely the life sits in the body, while the Jain debater insists the soul fills and works the body as its knower, so the same separability is put to opposite uses.","gn":["The verse's soul is set against a familiar image of a life that merely lodges in the body and leaves.","The bird-and-nest image is shown to leave open what does the knowing while the bird is in the nest, which the debate answers."]},{"a":"nk:419","b":"nk:201","type":"develops","note":"Against Kundalakesi (201) Neelakesi used the thread running through a garland for what carries knowledge from moment to moment; against Mokkalan (419) she says the soul's being stays through austerity, vows and divine birth.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The continuity argued against Kundalakesi for memory within one life is here stretched across births, because the reward of penance needs the same soul to arrive in heaven."},{"a":"nk:421","b":"mm:27:171-182","type":"develops","note":"The Manimekalai's Jain debater says each thing is both lasting and passing and takes on origination, persistence and destruction in every moment; in 420-421 Mokkalan calls this self-contradiction (milk becomes curd), and Neelakesi answers that change runs only by a thing's own causes.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Buddhist epic sets out the Jain both-lasting-and-passing doctrine unanswered, while the Jain epic shows the Buddhist charge against it and the reply, so together they show the doctrine and where it was attacked."},{"a":"nk:423","b":"nk:202","type":"same_image","note":"In 202 Neelakesi grants that the pathiri scent stays in the new pot after the flower decays, to argue a soul outlasting the body; in 422-423 Mokkalan cites the scent in the sherd as a quality leaving its substance, and she answers that the flower's particles stay and carry it.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The same pot-and-scent example cuts both ways in the Jain debater's hands: against Kundalakesi it shows something that endures, against Mokkalan she must deny that the scent ever left its substance."},{"a":"nk:425","b":"mm:24:101-122","type":"develops","note":"Neelakesi says the Buddha himself spoke of six kinds of destinies; in the Manimekalai Aravanar teaches that beings are of six kinds (humans, gods, Brahmas, hell-beings, animals, ghosts), born by good and bad deeds.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"Neelakesi's retort works because Buddhists did teach six realms on their master's word, as the Buddhist epic confirms, so her appeal to scripture for the place of release matches their own practice."},{"a":"nk:427","b":"k:356","type":"qualifies","note":"The Kural says those who have learned and seen the true thing reach the path of not coming back here; Mokkalan asks what makes non-return final, and Neelakesi answers that karma returns only through yoga activity, which the freed soul lacks.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural names the path of no return without saying what keeps it closed, and the debate supplies a mechanism: return needs the inflow of karma through activity, which release has ended.","gn":["The Jain answer is set against the general Tamil promise of a path of no return, showing which part of that promise it explains.","The couplet's 'not coming back' is tested by the objection that a lasting freed self could be bound again."],"gr":2},{"a":"nk:427","b":"mm:27:183-201","type":"develops","note":"The Manimekalai's Jain debater says release is blocking the way good and bad deeds come in and wearing out past deeds; Neelakesi names that way as yoga activity and uses it to deny that a freed soul could be rebound.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The inflow that the Buddhist epic's summary only mentions becomes, in the Jain epic, the answer to the charge that a released soul could fall back into bondage."},{"a":"nk:435","b":"nk:48","type":"develops","note":"The narrator in 48 says the sage Munichandra knew without limit the things of the three times; in 435 Neelakesi argues that if virtuous gods can show the three times, the Lord must know them.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"Her a-fortiori argument for the Lord's knowledge of the three times rests on lesser knowers the epic had already shown, including the human sage who converted her."},{"a":"nk:437","b":"t:2164","type":"same_image","note":"Tirumular says the undiscerning take the moon in clouds and in a pot of Ganga water as many, though it is one; Neelakesi asks who ever said the moon differs in each water, to show the Lord's one knowing present to all things.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Both use one moon in many waters, but the Saiva verse makes the many appearances one reality, while the Jain keeps the many things real and makes only the knowing single.","gn":["The Jain image is seen to stop short of the oneness the same figure carries in Saiva teaching.","Tirumular's moon is shown to be a shared figure the Jains used for knowledge, not only for the one Lord in many bodies."]},{"a":"nk:440","b":"nk:451","type":"tension","note":"Mokkala argues that infinite things cannot be known to their end; the hymn answers that the Lord's knowledge is not sensory and does not go in order (முறைபடும் அறிவிலன்).","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The infinity objection assumes knowing means counting through objects one by one, and the hymn's answer is to deny that the omniscient's knowledge is sequential at all, which shows where the two sides really part."},{"a":"nk:445","b":"nk:458","type":"develops","note":"445 defines the Lord by the burning of the eightfold seeds; 458 uses the burnt seed that never sprouts to explain why the released soul is never reborn yet remains.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The same image does two jobs: first it defines what made the Jina Lord, then it answers whether release is extinction, so the seed shows that what is destroyed is karma while the soul persists.","gr":3},{"a":"nk:460","b":"nk:266","type":"develops","note":"Arkkachandra, defeated, named Mokkala, the Buddha's first disciple, as his teacher; Mokkala, defeated, names the one in Kapilapuram who first gave him his delusion.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Each defeated Buddhist names the teacher above him, so the debates climb a chain of transmission from Kundalakesi to the Buddha himself, and the epic's route is the lineage read backwards."},{"a":"nk:458","b":"mm:30:125-138","type":"tension","note":"The Manimekalai's teacher describes release as the reverse chain in which birth and all its sorrow cease; Neelakesi says the released soul, like a burnt seed, never sprouts but abides eternally with eight qualities.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Both epics define release as the end of birth, but the Buddhist account has nothing that remains while the Jain account insists a soul abides, which is exactly what Mokkala's question in 457 asks."},{"a":"nk:449","b":"k:734","type":"qualifies","note":"The Kural defines a land as one free of severe hunger, unceasing disease and destructive enemies; the hymn says hunger, disease and enmity perish wherever the Jina goes.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The three ills the Kural treats as a test of a well-governed land are, in the Jain hymn, ended by the presence of a saint rather than by the king, so the pairing moves the source of a land's welfare from rule to holiness.","gn":["the hymn's list of marvels is seen to answer a standard Tamil measure of a good country, not just to praise","the couplet's measure is shown being claimed by religious polemic for the Jina's presence"]},{"a":"nk:456","b":"k:5","type":"develops","note":"The Kural says the two kinds of dark deed do not cling to those devoted to the Lord's true praise; Neelakesi says those who strew flowers and hymn the Aptha lose both kinds of deed and reach the state of Siva.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet leaves its Lord unnamed and its reward unstated, and the epic fills both, naming the Aptha by the marks just argued for and calling the reward release, which shows how a sect could read the Kural's opening chapter as its own.","gn":["the verse's claim that worship removes both kinds of deed is seen to rest on a widely known couplet, not on Jain doctrine alone","the couplet's unnamed Lord and unstated outcome are given one concrete reading in a sectarian debate"]},{"a":"nk:456","b":"t:2716","type":"tension","note":"Tirumular says that saying Siva Siva makes evil deeds die and brings the state of Siva (சிவகதி); Neelakesi says that praising the Aptha destroys both kinds of deed and brings the undivided state of Siva (சிவகதி).","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The two verses share the formula of praise, deeds destroyed and சிவகதி, but for Tirumular the word names Siva and only evil deeds must go, while for the Jain hymn it names a godless auspicious state and good deeds must go too.","gn":["the hymn's Saiva-sounding vocabulary is seen as a contest over shared words, not a borrowing of Saiva belief","the Tirumanthiram's சிவகதி is shown to be a term a rival school used for release without a creator god"]},{"a":"nk:458","b":"s:1753","type":"same_image","note":"Idaikkadar says a roasted seed will not sprout and those without devotion will not reach the goal; Neelakesi says a burnt seed never sprouts and so the released soul is never born again.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The same roasted seed is failure for the Siddhar, the soul without devotion that cannot grow toward release, and success for the Jain, the soul whose karma is burnt so it cannot grow into rebirth, so the image's meaning depends on whether sprouting is birth or attainment.","gn":["the Jain verse's image is seen to have a rival use in which not sprouting is a loss","the Siddhar's terse image is set against the older doctrinal use it inverts"]},{"a":"nk:471","b":"k:256","type":"develops","note":"Kural 256 says no one would sell flesh for a price if the world did not kill to eat; 471 makes the market's beasts say the buyers' money sends the netter out to kill them.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet's demand argument is given a voice and an opponent: in the Buddha's own town the dead animals accuse the buyer, so the rule becomes a charge against a monastic order that buys rather than kills.","gn":["the verse's picture of the protesting beasts gets the Kural's general law of price and killing behind it","the couplet's abstract buyer becomes a named religious community, which shows whom the argument was useful against"]},{"a":"nk:472","b":"k:251","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 251 asks how one who eats flesh to swell his own can hold to grace; 472 grants the Buddha the greatest self-giving, even of his wife, and still faults him for not forbidding flesh.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"Grace in the Kural is lost by the eater; the Neelakesi adds a teacher's grace lost by silence, so that a life of total giving can still fail the test if it leaves others free to eat flesh.","gn":["the charge against the Buddha gets the Kural's rule that grace and flesh-eating cannot go together","the couplet's test is extended from the one who eats to the one who teaches and does not forbid"]},{"a":"nk:473","b":"k:255","type":"develops","note":"Kural 255 says hell does not open its mouth to release one who eats flesh; 473 says the Buddha, by permitting flesh, will lead himself and his disciples into the burning hell.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural's hell takes the single eater; the Jain polemic widens it to a teacher and his whole following, so a permission given in doctrine becomes a collective path to hell.","gn":["the ironic praise of the Buddha's 'kindness' gets the Kural's plain sentence on where flesh-eating leads","the couplet's punishment is shown to reach the teacher who permits as well as the one who eats"]},{"a":"nk:474","b":"tk:36","type":"develops","note":"Thirikadugam 36 names among the self-deceived the one who eats flesh and claims compassion and the one who says the deed is done without his consent; 474 answers that defence with the middleman who profits from a trade he does not perform.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Thirikadugam only lists the excuse 'it was not done with my consent'; the Neelakesi gives the reason it fails, since the eater stands to the killing as a middleman to a sale, profiting from both sides.","gn":["the attack on the monk's threefold excuse is shown to be a known type of self-deception in the ethical manuals","the verse's bare list gets an argument and a target, the Buddhist rule of meat not killed for the eater"]},{"a":"nk:475","b":"sp:69","type":"develops","note":"Sirupanchamoolam 69 binds the sin of a killing on five: the killer, the one who consented, the one who took the carcass, the cook and the eater; 475 mocks a master who sorts sin so that the killer bears it and the eater does not.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Held together, the two show the Jain account of shared guilt as a deliberate counter-rule: the Sirupanchamoolam's five sharers are the answer to the very division of guilt the Neelakesi's Buddha is said to make.","gn":["Neelakesi's mockery gets a full rule of who shares a killing, which the verse only implies","the manual's list of five is shown to be aimed at a rival doctrine that confined guilt to the killer"]},{"a":"nk:470","b":"nk:251","type":"develops","note":"251 charged the monk Arkkachandran with preaching compassion while eating fish and flesh; 470 brings the same charge to the Buddha's own town and its market, before the teacher himself.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"The flesh charge moves from the disciple's conduct to the master's city and then (472-473) to the master's teaching, so the poem traces a monk's fault back to the rule that permits it."},{"a":"nk:477","b":"nk:177","type":"develops","note":"In 177 Kundalakesi reports that her teacher first taught the three scriptures and the five aggregates; in 477 the Buddha himself is shown dividing his teaching into Sutra, Vinaya and Shastra and then the aggregates.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Neelakesi climbs from report to source: the doctrine Neelakesi first met second-hand from a nun is now spoken by the Buddha in person, so the debate that follows is staged as a refutation at the root."},{"a":"nk:480","b":"nk:203","type":"develops","note":"In 203 Neelakesi argued that a power passing across the moments would be a sixth thing beside the five aggregates; in 478 and 480 the Buddha's teaching posits a person (pudgala) besides the aggregates.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The 'sixth thing' that Neelakesi forced on Kundalakesi as an absurd consequence turns up as the master's own tenet, the person, so the earlier argument becomes the charge of contradiction here.","gr":2},{"a":"nk:481","b":"nk:194","type":"develops","note":"In 194 Neelakesi argued that feeling, perception and formation are not other than knowing, so the five aggregates collapse; in 481 the Buddha concedes that many of his divisions of the five do not fit but holds the aggregates real.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The objection made against the disciple is half admitted by the master, which lets the poet show the Buddhist defence retreating from the five divisions to a bare claim that the aggregates exist."},{"a":"nk:484","b":"mm:30:183-197","type":"qualifies","note":"The Manimekalai's teacher names the five aggregates simply (form, feeling, perception, formation, consciousness); the Neelakesi's Buddha gives them with numbered subdivisions, three feelings, six consciousnesses and twenty formations.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Buddhist epic gives the aggregates as a short list within the four truths; the Jain epic's Buddha gives a scholastic count, so the polemic attacks a more technical Abhidharma than the one the Buddhist epic chose to teach."},{"a":"nk:487","b":"mm:27:120-141","type":"qualifies","note":"The Manimekalai's Ajivika gives earth as hard, water cool, fire burning and wind moving, borne by eternal atoms; the Neelakesi's Buddha gives the same four marks with functions, borne by octads of momentary form (478, 485).","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Across two polemical epics the marks of the elements are common ground; what the schools dispute is the bearer, eternal unchanging atoms for the Ajivika against momentary eight-fold clusters for the Buddhist."},{"a":"nk:506","b":"nk:491","type":"tension","note":"491 says the five aggregates arise together in one moment; 506 gives them an order in which cognition makes activity and activity makes feeling.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Neelakesi uses the Buddhist's own causal chain against his doctrine of simultaneity: if one aggregate produces another, they cannot all be born and die in the same instant."},{"a":"nk:495","b":"nk:492","type":"tension","note":"The Buddha calls the five aggregates a soul only as five fingers are called a hand; Neelakesi says the fingers' unity needs a bearer, or else they are five things.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Buddha's chosen example becomes the Jain's evidence: an analogy for a mere name is turned into a case of qualities that need one substance."},{"a":"nk:492","b":"mm:30:198-213","type":"develops","note":"The Manimekalai's teacher gives the collective name (body, water, country) as a Buddhist mode of speech; the Neelakesi's Buddha calls the soul such a name, like 'hand' for five fingers.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"The Jain poet gives his Buddha a doctrine the Buddhist epic itself states, so here the polemic reports Buddhist teaching recognisably before answering it."},{"a":"nk:493","b":"mm:30:183-197","type":"tension","note":"The Neelakesi's Buddha calls impermanence, suffering, impurity and no-soul his four truths; the Manimekalai's teacher gives the four truths as suffering, its cause, release and release's cause.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Holding the two lists together shows the Jain epic replacing the Buddhist's path of cause and cure with four bare marks of things, so that release seems to follow from seeing alone; this depends on reading நான்மை in 493 as the four truths."},{"a":"nk:493","b":"nk:259","type":"develops","note":"The Buddha says those who know the four truths reach release whatever they do; the monk in 259 says only the mind counts and evil conduct is not evil karma.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The monk's excuse in the earlier debate is given a source in the Buddha's own words here, so the poet makes the founder answerable for the disciple's conduct; this rests on reading எவையே செய்தும் as 'whatever they do'.","gr":2},{"a":"nk:498","b":"nk:176","type":"same_image","note":"Kundalakesi praised her teacher as one who suffered only for others; Neelakesi addresses the Buddha as the compassionate one who toiled for others through every aeon, in a verse that charges him with contradiction.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Buddhist's chief title of praise returns in the Jain's mouth as an ironic address, so the founder's compassion is set beside the incoherence of his doctrine.","gr":2},{"a":"nk:505","b":"nk:194","type":"develops","note":"Against Kundalakesi, Neelakesi folded feeling, perception and volition into knowing; against the Buddha she folds feeling into activity.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Jain reply to the five aggregates is the same in both debates: reduce them until only knowing and form remain, which is the Jain pair of soul and non-soul."},{"a":"nk:507","b":"nk:489","type":"tension","note":"The Buddha counts six cognitions at the six doors; Neelakesi asks how the five not occurring can be known to exist, and says their hidden existence licenses a soul.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Counting six kinds of cognition already assumes something that holds the unused ones, so the Buddhist's own enumeration is made to imply the abiding knower he denies."},{"a":"nk:511","b":"nk:201","type":"develops","note":"Against Kundalakesi, Neelakesi said that if nothing of one moment is in the next, knowing and speaking are impossible; against the Buddha she says that if will perishes at each arising, knowledge cannot grow.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The argument against momentariness moves from memory to learning: the first debate asked how anything is known, this one how anything is learned."},{"a":"nk:511","b":"mm:27:183-201","type":"develops","note":"The Manimekalai's Jain says gram keeps its nature when cooked into a dish; the Neelakesi's Jain asks how milk, curd and buttermilk could fill the vessels if nothing lasts.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"Both epics give the Jain the same proof from the kitchen, a substance that persists through its changes; the Buddhist epic reports it unanswered there, and the Jain epic aims it at the Buddha's momentariness."},{"a":"nk:512","b":"nk:263","type":"develops","note":"In 263 Neelakesi grieves for the Buddha who taught a void release; in 512 she argues that his account of will ends in the void through infinite regress.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The void is charged twice, once as a goal no one could want and once as the end of a regress, so the Jain treats Buddhist emptiness as both a bad hope and a failed argument."},{"a":"nk:512","b":"s:1729","type":"tension","note":"Neelakesi says the Buddha's reasoning collapses into the void; the Siddhar tells his listener that all arising deeds are seen to be void, appearing without appearing in pure space.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"For the Jain debater reaching the void is a reductio, for the Siddhar it is the seeing that frees; the same word marks defeat in one tradition and insight in the other.","gn":["the Jain charge gains a witness that some Tamil teachers embraced the void as a goal, so the charge is aimed at a live position, not a straw one","the Siddhar's void gains the objection it must meet, that a void reached by regress leaves nothing to know or hold"]},{"a":"nk:499","b":"k:355","type":"qualifies","note":"The Kural says understanding is seeing the true thing in whatever thing, of whatever nature; Neelakesi says no thing has ever been made clear except through its qualities.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The Jain makes the couplet's 'whatever its nature' into a method: the true thing is reached through its qualities, not by stripping them away, which is why she can claim the soul is known by knowing and seeing.","gn":["the debater's rule about qualities gains the Kural's claim that seeing the real thing is what understanding is for","the couplet gains an answer to how the true thing is to be seen, which it leaves open"],"gr":3},{"a":"nk:513","b":"nk:203","type":"develops","note":"In 203 Neelakesi told Kundalakesi that a power carried from moment to moment would be a sixth thing beyond the aggregates; in 513 she says the Buddha's store of consciousness is the soul under another name.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Across two opponents the Jain argument has one shape: any carrier the Buddhist adds to explain memory and continuity is called a soul, so the store-consciousness is met with the charge first made against Kundalakesi."},{"a":"nk:518","b":"nk:201","type":"develops","note":"201 argues that if nothing of the first moment remains in the next, knowing is impossible; 518 tests this on hearing a word letter by letter.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The general continuity objection becomes a concrete test the opponent cannot dodge: no one could even understand the word 'Buddha' if each letter's knower perished before the next."},{"a":"nk:518","b":"mm:30:198-213","type":"tension","note":"The Manimekalai's teacher gives the joined name as a valid mode of speech: many letters coming together appear as a word; 518 says a momentary mind could never put the letters together.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The two epics take the same example, a word made of letters, to opposite ends: the Buddhist treats it as a correct conventional name, the Jain as proof that something must last to join the letters."},{"a":"nk:533","b":"mm:30:198-213","type":"tension","note":"The Manimekalai's teacher counts the collective name (the body, water, a country) as a true mode of speech; 533 says naming collections one holds unreal makes every such word a lie.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Jain charge of lying presupposes that a name must match one real thing, which is the very premise the Buddhist account of conventional naming in the Manimekalai rejects."},{"a":"nk:529","b":"mm:30:59-83","type":"tension","note":"The Manimekalai names the mind's three bad deeds as coveting, anger and wrong view; 529 says pride, deceit and greed are as evil as anger and the Buddhists leave them uncondemned.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Set against the Buddhist epic's actual list, Neelakesi's charge turns out to be a clash of classifications: she measures a list of ten deeds against the Jain four passions, anger, pride, deceit and greed."},{"a":"nk:535","b":"mm:30:59-83","type":"tension","note":"The Manimekalai's teacher gives exactly four bad deeds of speech in a closed list of ten; 535 says there are many more, such as talking alone with a woman.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Neelakesi quotes the Buddhist list faithfully and attacks its closure, not its content: her additions come from a renouncer's code of conduct, which a list of ten deeds for all people does not try to cover."},{"a":"nk:529","b":"k:35","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 35 sets aside envy, craving, anger and harsh words as the four that virtue avoids; 529 names anger, pride, deceit and greed as the faults of the mind.","grade":"forced","better":"agrees","te":"Both make anger and greed central, but each completes the list from its own school: the Kural adds envy and a fault of speech, the Jain debater pride and deceit, so the shared pair is common ground and the rest is sectarian.","gn":["the Jain list of passions is shown to share its core with a widely accepted ethical rule, which strengthens her charge that the Buddhists left faults out","the couplet's list is shown not to be the only fourfold reckoning; a Jain debater would count pride and deceit where it counts envy and harsh speech"],"gr":2},{"a":"nk:533","b":"k:292","type":"qualifies","note":"533 holds that naming a thing one thinks unreal is a lie; Kural 292 holds that even a falsehood stands as truth if it yields faultless good.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"Neelakesi's charge only works on a test of truth by match with things; on the Kural's test by effect, the Buddhist's conventional names, harming no one, would not be lies at all.","gn":["the verse's assumption about what truth is becomes visible as an assumption, one a widely read ethics book does not share","the couplet's measure of truth by its fruit is shown to settle a philosophical dispute about speech that it never names"]},{"a":"nk:536","b":"k:91","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 91 defines sweet words as moist with love and free of deceit; 536 argues against a rule that gentle speech is good by citing the soft-spoken plotter.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural's definition already builds in the guard Neelakesi says the Buddhist rule lacks: speech counts as sweet only when it is without deceit, so her gentle conspirator would not qualify under it.","gn":["the objection gets a ready answer in an older definition, which shows the rule can be repaired rather than discarded","the couplet's clause படிறிலவாம் is shown to do real work, excluding exactly the soft-spoken plotter a debater raised"],"gr":2},{"a":"nk:534","b":"s:2080","type":"tension","note":"534 mocks the teacher who calls earth, water, fire and city false; Pambatti Siddhar bids the reader be clear that the world is false and dance.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Jain realist's retort, that one who calls all things false leaves no ground for his own truth, falls on the Siddhar's world-is-false as much as on the Buddhist's, though the Siddhar makes it a release-giving insight, not a doctrine to defend.","gn":["the verse's attack is shown to reach beyond Buddhism to a Tamil Saiva-Vedantic saying that also calls the world false","the Siddhar's flat statement meets the objection it never answers: how one who calls the world false still claims his own words are true"],"gr":2},{"a":"nk:539","b":"mm:30:59-83","type":"tension","note":"The Manimekalai's teacher lists killing, theft and lust as the three evil deeds that arise in the body; 539 takes that very grouping and asks who does them if the mind is not there.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Buddhist epic sorts deeds by the door they go out through and counts the mind's three faults separately, so the Jain objection works only by reading a classification of doors as a claim that the body acts without mind."},{"a":"nk:541","b":"nk:259","type":"develops","note":"In 259 the monk says nothing exists but the mind and evil conduct is not evil karma; 541 states the Buddhist rule that a killing needs knowledge and intention as well as a death.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The earlier boast that only the mind counts becomes a precise five-part rule here, and the precision is what lets Neelakesi build cases of mistaken identity in which a man dies and no killing is counted."},{"a":"nk:547","b":"k:282","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 282 says even thinking of stealing another's goods is wrong; 547 holds that the burglar, the would-be killer and the would-be adulterer carry the karma of their intent even when the act fails.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"Read beside a debate that also condemns the unintended killing, the couplet's thought-crime proves to be only half of the Jain position, which binds karma both to the intent without the deed and to the deed without the intent.","gn":["the verse's claim that a failed intent binds finds a rule in the Kural that gives the same verdict on theft without any doctrine of karma","the couplet's bare 'thinking is wrong' is set inside an account of why: the intent itself binds karma, and a deed done in error binds it too"]},{"a":"nk:553","b":"mm:30:183-197","type":"tension","note":"553 argues that if all is suffering there cannot be four truths; the Manimekalai's teacher gives four truths in which the fourth is joy, which is release caused by not clinging.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The reductio holds only against a Buddhism that calls everything suffering without exception; the Buddhist epic's own fourth truth (இன்பம் வீடே) is the exception, which shows the Jain argument aimed at a version the Buddhist teacher would not accept."},{"a":"nk:556","b":"nk:256","type":"develops","note":"256 mocks 'all is suffering' as a greedy monk's complaints; 556 draws its practical consequence, that with no joy anywhere one may as well walk into fire.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The poet attacks the first truth twice in two tones, as a lazy monk's grumble in the Arkkachandra debate and as an argument against the Buddha himself that the doctrine would remove every reason for conduct.","gr":2},{"a":"nk:557","b":"n:48","type":"tension","note":"Naladiyar 48 says those who praise sandal paste and garlands have not seen what the body is; 557 treats 'those who wear garlands and sandal have not found the path' as the absurd outcome of the Buddhist claim that nothing is pure.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"A Jain anthology and a Jain epic part ways on impurity: the Naladiyar finds it in the body and condemns the garland, while the Neelakesi attacks the Buddhist 'nothing at all is pure' because extended to everything it erases the difference between path and no path.","gn":["the epic's reductio is shown to aim at the word 'all', since its own tradition accepts the body's foulness in the same image of sandal and garland","the quatrain's contempt for adornment is marked as a claim about the body, which the Jain debater would not let grow into a claim that everything is impure"]},{"a":"nk:558","b":"nk:252","type":"develops","note":"252 says the monks call all things impure yet bow at their own defiled master's feet; 558 says they call their own recitation, conduct and food pure.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The charge of inconsistency grows from the monks' worship of their teacher to their whole daily practice, so the 'all is impure' doctrine is shown to exempt everything its holders do."},{"a":"nk:561","b":"s:1429","type":"tension","note":"Sivavakkiyar mocks those who bathe daily crying 'pollution', since the body itself took form from pollution; 561 tells those who hold all things impure to bathe in the gutter, as if that conclusion were absurd.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Siddhar draws the very conclusion the Jain debater uses as a reductio: if everything is born of impurity, the ritual bath is pointless, so the same premise ends in mockery of ritual in one text and in mockery of the premise in the other.","gn":["the epic's reductio meets a poet who accepts its consequence, showing that the argument holds only for one who still values the clean over the foul","the Siddhar's attack on bathing is seen to rest on a premise a Jain epic thought no sane teacher could hold"]},{"a":"nk:562","b":"nk:196","type":"develops","note":"196 asks how perishing can be known if the mind perishes wholly with its objects; 562 asks how a teacher who says nothing stays can establish any thesis, the four truths included.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"Neelakesi uses one argument against momentariness in both debates, first against the knowing of perishing and here against the stating of doctrine, so the self-refutation of the momentary becomes the Neelakesi's standing reply to Buddhism."},{"a":"nk:573","b":"mm:30:198-213","type":"same_image","note":"The Buddha of the Neelakesi offers seed, sprout and branch as a continuity in which release lies; the Manimekalai's teacher uses seed, sprout and stalk as the example of the continuity name, one word for a series.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Buddhist epic uses the seed series as a rule of naming, while the Jain epic puts it in the Buddha's mouth as an account of what passes on to release, which is the stronger claim its heroine can then attack."},{"a":"nk:574","b":"mm:30:34-58","type":"agrees","note":"The Buddha here says deed and fruit exist but no doer; Manimekalai 30's teacher says the chain has no maker, no I and no mine, and is itself deed and fruit.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Set against the Buddhist epic's own teaching, the Jain poem's Buddhist thesis turns out to be stated fairly, so Neelakesi's reply in 575 attacks a view the school holds, not a straw figure."},{"a":"nk:574","b":"n:101","type":"tension","note":"The Buddha denies any doer behind the deed; the quatrain says an old deed seeks out the very one who did it, as a calf finds its mother among many cows.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The quatrain's comfort that a deed finds its own doer depends on there being a doer who lasts, which is exactly what the Buddha's thesis denies, so the proverb takes a side in the Jain-Buddhist debate.","gn":["the Buddha's thesis gains an everyday image of the persisting owner it rejects, showing what ordinary moral talk would lose under it","the quatrain gains an opponent who makes explicit that its calf image assumes a lasting self"]},{"a":"nk:580","b":"nk:203","type":"develops","note":"In 203 Neelakesi told Kundalakesi that a power passing between moments is either a sixth thing beyond the five aggregates or total destruction; here she tells the Buddha the lac is a further thing, and the aggregates have none.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The same dilemma defeats the disciple's power and the teacher's lac, which shows Neelakesi's case is one argument: every Buddhist carrier of karma is either a soul under another name or nothing.","gr":3},{"a":"nk:565","b":"nk:205","type":"tension","note":"Kundalakesi gave the Buddha's gifts of wife and child as proof of his compassion; Neelakesi tells the Buddha that those who take such gifts cannot be shown not to be thieves.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The gift offered as proof of compassion is answered not only as the giver's folly, as in 207, but as a wrong done through the receiver, since a wife and children were never the giver's to give."},{"a":"nk:565","b":"k:221","type":"qualifies","note":"The couplet defines a gift by its receiver: what goes to those who have nothing. Neelakesi tests a gift by what is given: persons handed over as alms make their taker a thief.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"A gift can satisfy the couplet's test of the receiver's want and still fail, because the thing given must be the giver's own, which the couplet takes for granted.","gn":["Neelakesi's objection gains a householder's definition of giving that does not mention property, showing her test adds a condition","the couplet gains a limit it leaves unstated: one may give only what is one's own, and persons are not"]},{"a":"nk:587","b":"kd:1","type":"tension","note":"Kundalakesi's invocation praises the lord who kept nothing for himself and laboured only for others' good (பிறர்க்கேயுறு திக்கு ழந்தான்); 587 tells the Buddha that because he laboured for others (பிறர்க்கே உழந்தாய்), by his own rule that a pain-bringing deed is evil, he must himself suffer those deeds.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Jain epic lifts the Buddhist poem's central praise almost word for word and recasts it as a karmic debt, so the same self-giving reads as perfection in one sect and as a self-inflicted evil deed in the other."},{"a":"nk:587","b":"k:320","type":"qualifies","note":"The Kural says all pains rest on those who caused pain (நோயெல்லாம் நோய்செய்தார் மேலவாம்); Neelakesi applies the rule to the Buddha's pain-bearing gifts and says he must himself suffer what they caused.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Applied without limit, the rule that pain returns to its causer condemns even the giver who bears pain for others, and only a theory of what makes a deed evil can stop that.","gn":["Neelakesi's charge rests on a maxim Tamil readers already accept, so it reads as consequence rather than spite","the couplet meets a hard case it does not consider, pain caused in the act of helping others, which forces a choice about whether intention excuses it"],"gr":3},{"a":"nk:595","b":"nk:313","type":"develops","note":"In 313 Neelakesi rejects the Buddhist rule that sin joins only where there is intention, using meat-eating; in 595 she argues in general that wherever mind is there action is, so action cannot be left out of karma.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The particular quarrel over the meat-eater's guilt rests on a general thesis about intention and act, which the Jain debater states only when she faces the Buddha himself.","gr":2},{"a":"nk:595","b":"k:34","type":"qualifies","note":"The Kural says to be spotless in mind is the whole of virtue and all else is show; Neelakesi denies that the mind alone is the deed and argues that action always goes with it and is karma too.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"A mind-first ethic that the Kural states as praise of sincerity becomes, between Buddhist and Jain, a disputed claim about where karma lies, and the Jain side refuses to let the act fall away.","gn":["Neelakesi's argument is seen to cut against a widely accepted Tamil maxim, not only against Buddhist doctrine","the couplet's stress on the mind is shown to carry a doctrinal risk, the excusing of the act, that the Kural does not address"]},{"a":"nk:601","b":"nk:583","type":"tension","note":"In 583 Neelakesi objects that the lac colour put on the seed does not reach even the sprout, yet the Buddha says it settles in the flower; in 601 the Buddha replies that the power hides in between, hard to see, and appears in the flower.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Buddha's defence of karmic transfer rests on an unseen passage, which is exactly what Neelakesi's empirical objection denied, so the dispute becomes one over whether what cannot be seen may be posited."},{"a":"nk:602","b":"n:101","type":"tension","note":"The Naladiyar says an old deed seeks out the one who did it (தற்செய்த கிழவனை) as a calf finds its mother among many cows; the Buddha in 602 says the doer has perished and the continuum running on from him receives the fruit.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The quatrain's image needs a doer who is still there to be found, while the Buddha's answer removes him, so the pair shows that the Tamil ethical common sense about karma assumes a lasting self.","gn":["the Buddha's continuum is seen against the common Tamil picture of a deed finding its owner, which it has to replace","the quatrain's calf-and-mother image is shown to carry an assumption, a persisting doer, that a Buddhist would deny"]},{"a":"nk:603","b":"nk:199","type":"develops","note":"In 199 Neelakesi says Kundalakesi's two kinds of continuum destroy her own case; in 603 she presses the Buddha on what a feeble continuum can do once the aggregates have wholly perished.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The epic makes the same objection to continuity defeat the disciple first and the master later, so the Buddhist doctrine is shown failing at one point from both its spokesmen.","gr":2},{"a":"nk:604","b":"mm:30:7-33","type":"tension","note":"The Manimekalai's teacher says the Buddha came down leaving the Tushita world (துடிதலோகம் ஒழியத் தோன்றி); Neelakesi asks how the ascetic who perished could be a god in the Tushita palace at all without a self to carry him.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Buddhist epic tells the Tushita descent as sacred history, and the Jain epic takes the same episode as its test case against rebirth without a self."},{"a":"nk:610","b":"mm:30:198-213","type":"qualifies","note":"The Manimekalai's teacher gives seed, sprout and stalk all called paddy as the example of naming by continuity (தொடர்ச்சி); Neelakesi grants seed-to-flower continuity only because a long branch joins them without a gap.","grade":"weak","better":"tension","te":"The Buddhist example of continuity works only where the stages touch, and Neelakesi uses that condition to deny that it reaches across death to a new body.","gr":2},{"a":"nk:611","b":"nk:491","type":"tension","note":"Earlier the Buddha likened the aggregates, arising and perishing in one moment, to the tip of a lamp's flame; here Neelakesi refuses the flame as an example of continuity because two real things are in it.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The flame the Buddha offered as the very picture of momentary arising is claimed by the Jain for her own side, since a lamp shows a lasting fuel beside its passing tip, so one image serves both doctrines depending on which of its two parts is looked at."},{"a":"nk:614","b":"nk:574","type":"tension","note":"The Buddha said the deed exists and its fruit exists but there is no doer; Neelakesi answers that if the fruit comes later down the line, the deed itself must last until then.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Granting deed and fruit while denying a doer moves the lasting thing from the agent to the deed: the Jain shows that the Buddha's karma needs a persistence somewhere, and the debate is over where to put it."},{"a":"nk:618","b":"nk:202","type":"same_image","note":"The pathiri scent left in a new pot appears in the Kundalakesi debate, where Neelakesi used it to argue a soul outlasts the body, and here as the Buddha's own example of the trace.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The same pot of scent is read two ways in one epic: against Kundalakesi Neelakesi took the lingering scent as proof that something outlasts its source, and against the Buddha she denies it can show a trace passing between moments that never coexist."},{"a":"nk:620","b":"nk:423","type":"develops","note":"Against Mokkalan she said the scent in the sherd belongs to the flower, whose particles stay there and carry it; here she grants pot and flower coexist but denies that successive moments do.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Her earlier answer explains why the pot example cannot help the Buddha: the scent passes only because flower-matter is physically present with the pot, and no such co-presence exists between a perished moment and the next."},{"a":"nk:619","b":"nk:201","type":"develops","note":"Against Kundalakesi she argued that if nothing of the first moment is in the next, knowing fails, and if something is, it is a soul; here she shows the two moments never exist together for a trace to pass.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The dilemma put to Kundalakesi about what a trace carries is sharpened into a point about time: before asking what the trace holds, the Jain asks when it could cross, and finds no instant shared by the two moments."},{"a":"nk:622","b":"n:139","type":"qualifies","note":"The Naladiyar's new pot gives its water the scent of pathiri flowers, an image of the unlearned gaining sense from the learned; Neelakesi attacks the same image as the Buddha's proof of a trace that outlives the flower.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The ethical image of borrowed fragrance only works if the scent has a lasting bearer, the flower-dust in the pot, so the Jain debater's demand to know whose the scent is shows what the Naladiyar's figure quietly assumes about company and influence.","gn":["the debate verse is seen to be contesting a well-worn Tamil figure, not a stray example, which explains why the Buddhist chose it and why the Jain must dismantle it","the quatrain's simile of good company is shown to rest on a theory of how a quality passes, which the epic makes an open question between schools"],"gr":2},{"a":"nk:627","b":"nk:418","type":"develops","note":"Mokkalan argued that on the Jain view an ascetic's soul would not exist in the time to come and keeping the precepts would be wasted toil; here Neelakesi argues that on the Buddha's view nirvana comes alike to the ascetic and the vicious.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain","te":"Each side charges the other's metaphysics with making austerity pointless, which shows that in these debates a doctrine of time and persistence was tested by whether it could still reward discipline."},{"a":"nk:627","b":"nk:573","type":"tension","note":"The Buddha said continuity arising from a seed by potency is change, and that release is not other than this; Neelakesi replies that if each moment perishes entirely, release is that perishing and needs no austerity.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Buddha's own phrase that release is not other than the change of the series is what lets the Jain equate nirvana with every moment's end, so the Buddha's wording supplies the premise of her reductio."},{"a":"nk:627","b":"mm:30:170-182","type":"tension","note":"The Manimekalai's teacher makes release the seeing that the links are sorrow, unstable and without self; the Neelakesi's Jain argues that momentariness would make release the mere perishing of each moment.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Set beside the Buddhist epic's own account, the Jain charge is seen to aim at a version of Buddhism the Buddhist teacher would not own, since there release is an insight gained by those who see impermanence, not the impermanence itself.","gr":2},{"a":"nk:633","b":"mm:30:214-229","type":"develops","note":"The Manimekalai's teacher says there is no arising for a hare's horn, the mode of denial resting on what is not; Neelakesi says there is no perishing for what does not exist, as there is no size to a crow's tooth.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Both schools grant the same rule for the non-existent, and the Jain uses it against the Buddhist: if a perished moment is as unreal as a hare's horn, it cannot also be the thing whose perishing gives rise to the next."},{"a":"nk:642","b":"nk:574","type":"develops","note":"In 574 the Buddha said deed and fruit exist but there is no doer; asked now who reaps, he gives a fourfold denial: not the doer, not another, not both, not no one.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Pressed on the reaper rather than the doer, the no-doer thesis has to become a tetralemma, so the denial of a self costs the Buddha the power to name who receives karma."},{"a":"nk:652","b":"nk:574","type":"tension","note":"In 574 the Buddha denied any doer; here he says the doer is the doer and the reaper the reaper.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Neelakesi's pressure forces the Buddha from 'no doer' to 'the doer is the doer', which is the point at which she can make the doer the reaper and he falls silent."},{"a":"nk:651","b":"nk:644","type":"tension","note":"The Buddha offered paddy and sugarcane as a continuity that is neither same nor other; Neelakesi answers that paddy never comes from cane's line, so the example shows each kind giving only itself.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The seed example cuts both ways: what the Buddhist uses to show change without identity the Jain uses to show a kind that persists, so the dispute is over which feature of the seed counts."},{"a":"nk:643","b":"mm:30:230-243","type":"agrees","note":"The Neelakesi's Buddha says the reaper is neither that nor other; the Manimekalai's teacher draws from the nayas a middle that is 'neither that nor what is not that' (அதுவும் அன்று அது அலாததும் அன்று), and that there is no doer apart from the doing.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The formula the Jain poem gives the Buddha is the one the Buddhist epic itself teaches, so Neelakesi's excluded-middle reply in 645 strikes the school's own middle way, not a caricature."},{"a":"nk:651","b":"mm:30:214-229","type":"tension","note":"The Manimekalai's teacher calls 'paddy seed gives paddy sprout' the naya of own-nature; Neelakesi uses the same fact, that paddy gives only paddy, to argue that the Buddha's example proves sameness of kind.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"One observation serves the Buddhist as a law of causes without a self and the Jain as proof of a persisting kind, so the two epics agree on the fact and differ on what it shows."},{"a":"nk:653","b":"nk:205","type":"tension","note":"Kundalakesi listed the Buddha's gifts of eyes, flesh, wife and body as his glory; here Neelakesi lists the same gifts to ask how, on his doctrine, he could ever reap them.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Jataka gifts are turned from praise into a refutation: if no doer reaps, the Bodhisattva's sacrifices were made for a fruit his own teaching denies him."},{"a":"nk:656","b":"nk:376","type":"develops","note":"Mokkalan raised the faults of holding a thing both to stand and to perish; the Buddha now makes the same objection, and Neelakesi calls it one-sided.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"The same objection recurs from the disciple to the master, and the reply sharpens from 'each in its respect' (378) to the charge of ekanta, naming the error the Jain sees in every Buddhist objection."},{"a":"nk:657","b":"mm:27:171-182","type":"tension","note":"In the Manimekalai the Jain's account of things as lasting and passing at once is heard and left among the doctrines Manimekalai sets aside; in the Neelakesi the Buddha himself is made to praise that account.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Each epic stages the same Jain doctrine before the other side and gives it the opposite fate, so the debate scenes show how each school wanted the meeting to end."},{"a":"nk:658","b":"nk:4","type":"develops","note":"The opening called Neelakesi a demoness who took the great one's dharma and turned from violence; her last words say those of any form who understand the Lord's scripture reach release.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Her closing claim generalizes her own story: a demoness who learned the scripture is the proof that form and birth do not bar release."},{"a":"nk:652","b":"n:101","type":"qualifies","note":"Naladiyar 101 says an old deed seeks out the one who did it as a calf finds its mother among many cows; Neelakesi makes 'the doers themselves reap' the point that silences the Buddha.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"The proverb that a deed finds its doer turns out to presuppose a doer who lasts until the fruit comes, which is exactly what the Buddhist no-self denies and what Neelakesi defends.","gn":["the debate's decisive answer gets a common-sense image of karma that ordinary hearers already trusted","the quatrain's untroubled image is shown to carry a metaphysical premise, a persisting self, that one school rejects"]},{"a":"nk:652","b":"k:319","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 319 says hurts done to others in the forenoon come back to oneself in the afternoon, of themselves; Neelakesi argues that the fruit of deeds falls to the doers themselves (செய்தார்கள் தாம்).","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"The couplet's 'to oneself' (தமக்கு) is set within one day of one life, while the debate asks whether the same self is there across births to receive it.","gn":["Neelakesi's claim gets an ethical rule everyone grants, so its denial looks like a denial of morality itself","the couplet's returning harm is stretched across births, where the identity of the one it returns to becomes a question"]},{"a":"nk:658","b":"k:356","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 356 says those who have learned and seen the true thing reach the path of no return; Neelakesi says those of any form who understand the Lord's scripture reach release in due order.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Both make understanding the road to release, but the epic names whose scripture it must be and adds that any being, even a demoness, may walk it, by stages.","gn":["the epic's sectarian claim is set beside a general rule that learning and seeing truth free one from birth","the couplet's unnamed 'true thing' is claimed by one school's scripture, and its learner is opened to beings of any form"],"gr":2},{"a":"nk:658","b":"t:2475","type":"tension","note":"Tirumular says souls that know what truly is go to grace and release is becoming one with Siva; Neelakesi says understanding the Lord's scripture brings release in due order, with no grace and no union.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Both put knowledge first, but in the Saiva verse knowledge leads to grace and union with God, while in the Jain verse the Lord gives only the scripture and the soul reaches release by its own understanding.","gn":["the Jain claim is sharpened by a rival account that needs grace and union where it needs neither","the Saiva sequence of knowing, then grace, then union, stands out against a release reached by knowledge without a giver"]},{"a":"nk:659","b":"nk:193","type":"develops","note":"In the Kundalakesi debate Neelakesi said the Buddha knew only by seeing, hearing and reckoning (எண்ணியும் உணர்தலால்); her hymn now praises the Jina as the one who knew without reckoning (எண்ணாது உணர்ந்தான்).","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The epithet in the hymn is a debating point turned into praise: the Jina is defined by exactly the mode of knowing she denied the Buddha, so the hymn carries the earlier argument into worship."},{"a":"nk:670","b":"nk:172","type":"develops","note":"The king of Kampili asked the debaters for their first teacher, his scripture, its matters and their workings; Neelakesi now puts the same heads (ஆத்தன், நூல், பொருள், நிகழ்ச்சி) to the Ajivika.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The heads that a king imposed on the first debate have become Neelakesi's own method, so the epic shows the convert taking over the judge's role as well as the debater's."},{"a":"nk:670","b":"mm:27:159-170","type":"qualifies","note":"Manimekalai asks the Jain for his lord, his categories, their workings, and bondage and release; Neelakesi asks the Ajivika for his teacher, scripture, categories and their workings.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Both epics question rival schools by nearly the same heads, but the Jain examiner puts scripture in place of bondage and release, and her first attack (680) falls on the scripture, so the choice of heads already aims the refutation."},{"a":"nk:671","b":"mm:27:108-119","type":"qualifies","note":"Manimekalai's Ajivika spokesman, Puranan, gives as god a knower without limit present everywhere, and five categories, soul and four atoms; Neelakesi's Puranan gives Makkali as god, the nine Kathir books as scripture, and five categories.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"A Buddhist and a Jain polemic agree on the Ajivika spokesman's name and the five categories, which points to the Ajivikas' own teaching, but only the Jain account makes Makkali the god, which sets up its attack on a silent omniscient whose books no one heard."},{"a":"nk:677","b":"mm:27:120-141","type":"agrees","note":"Manimekalai's Ajivika says atoms never enter one another (ஒன்று ஒன்றில் புகுதா); Neelakesi's says they combine and part but one does not enter another.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Two hostile accounts state the same tenet in nearly the same words, so it is likely the Ajivikas' own, and the Jain debater singles it out for attack (698) where the Buddhist narrator lets it pass."},{"a":"nk:682","b":"nk:672","type":"tension","note":"Puranan says the Lord kept silent because speaking would cast him back into wandering; Neelakesi repeats his words and turns them, so that silence would make any silent man a lesser Lord.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The refutation works by repeating the opponent's own verse almost word for word, so the reader sees how a Neelakesi debate argues: the rival's premise is restated and pushed to an absurd consequence."},{"a":"nk:682","b":"nk:158","type":"develops","note":"The hymn in the Kundalakesi sarukkam says the Lord never says 'I will speak' yet has a voice like a drum, and has no mind to harm; the Ajivika holds that a knower who speaks falls into longing and anger.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The hymn's paradox of a Lord who speaks without willing to speak turns out to be the Jain answer to the Ajivika's dilemma, so a line that reads as devotion is doctrine aimed at this debate.","gr":2},{"a":"nk:682","b":"k:4","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 4 sets the Lord apart as one without liking or aversion; Puranan argues that a knower who speaks must fall into longing and anger, and Neelakesi denies that speech implies them.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural defines its Lord by the absence of liking and aversion without asking whether such a Lord can teach; the Ajivika debate shows that the absence was contested, since a rival held that any speaking Lord must have them.","gn":["the epic's debate gains a well-known Tamil statement of the passionless Lord that Neelakesi's position needs and the Ajivika's denies","the couplet gains a live dispute over whether its Lord could speak at all without losing the very freedom from liking and aversion it praises"]},{"a":"nk:666","b":"t:1451","type":"qualifies","note":"Tirumular tells the devotee to take refuge in the shade of the one God's feet so that the sea of deeds will not reach him; Neelakesi, a Jain convert, prays at the Jina's feet that her old deeds may end.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"A Jain convert and a Saiva poet both bring their deeds to a Lord's feet in prayer, so the difference between Jain release by wearing deeds away and Saiva release by grace is not visible in the act of worship but only in the doctrine behind it.","gn":["the epic's prayer gains a Saiva statement of what refuge at the feet is supposed to do, against which the Jain hymn's silence about how the feet end deeds stands out","Tirumular's verse gains a rival tradition that uses the same act of refuge at the feet without his one all-pervading God"]},{"a":"nk:673","b":"nk:684","type":"tension","note":"Puranan likens the Lord's unseen form to a rainbow that no bound contains; Neelakesi answers that a rainbow is made when sunlight falls on rain-cloud.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The simile chosen to set Makkali beyond causes is turned into an example of a thing produced by conditions, so the Ajivika's own image testifies against a Lord without cause."},{"a":"nk:680","b":"nk:685","type":"develops","note":"Neelakesi first argues that a silent omniscient leaves his books without authority; in 685 she adds that if silence proved full knowledge, children, trees and mountains would share it.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The attack on the silent Lord has two steps: silence cuts off the scripture, and silence cannot serve as evidence of knowledge, so the Ajivika is left with neither a speaking teacher nor a proof of his teacher."},{"a":"nk:684","b":"nk:673","type":"same_image","note":"Puranan likens his Lord's ungraspable form to the rainbow; Neelakesi makes the rainbow the type of a caused thing, born of sun on rain.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The same rainbow serves the Ajivika as a sign of a form beyond grasping and the Jain as proof that such a form is produced, so the simile decides nothing until its cause is asked."},{"a":"nk:694","b":"nk:673","type":"tension","note":"673 praises a Lord who neither ages nor greys; 694 answers with a sickness hotter than fire that came upon the god.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Against a portrait of a Lord without wrinkle or grey hair the Jain debater sets a single bodily event, showing that the debate tests a doctrine by its founder's body as well as by argument."},{"a":"nk:683","b":"k:650","type":"develops","note":"The Kural likens the learned who cannot unfold their learning to scentless flowers; Neelakesi ranks a silent omniscient below any man who tells the good he sees.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet faults those unable to expound, and the verse extends the fault to one who could speak and chooses not to, turning a rule about scholars into a test of a god.","gn":["the verse's mockery gains a recognised Tamil norm that knowledge counts only when it is passed on, which the debate itself never states","the couplet, which speaks of inability, gains a case of deliberate silence and a use of its standard in judging a religious founder"],"gr":2},{"a":"nk:685","b":"t:1896","type":"qualifies","note":"Tirumular says silence of speech alone is mere muteness, true silence being the stilling of mind; Neelakesi says silence of speech proves nothing, since trees and mountains are silent.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Both deny that outward silence shows attainment, but they move the test in opposite directions: Tirumular inward to a stilled mind, the Jain debater outward to the teaching a knower must give.","gn":["the verse gains a contrast showing that its attack on silence touches only silence of the tongue, not an inner stillness it never discusses","the Tirumanthiram's distinction gains a debater who uses the same point against a rival's mute Lord, showing that mere muteness was a live charge between schools"],"gr":2},{"a":"nk:688","b":"mm:27:120-141","type":"qualifies","note":"The Manimekalai's Ajivika gives water its own coolness and taste and fire its burning; the Neelakesi's gives every sensible quality but sound to earth alone, and Neelakesi argues the other elements would then be imperceptible.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Set beside the Buddhist report, the Jain objection is seen to strike the version of the doctrine that gives the senses' objects to earth alone; the Manimekalai's Ajivika, who gives water a taste, would be harder to hit."},{"a":"nk:698","b":"mm:27:120-141","type":"develops","note":"The Manimekalai's Ajivika says the atoms never enter one another, yet move, sink and rise; Neelakesi takes the same premises and demands the cause of sinking and rising.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The two polemics report the same Ajivika premise, atoms that never enter one another and yet sink and rise; the Buddhist leaves it, while the Jain shows it leaves motion without a cause."},{"a":"nk:706","b":"mm:27:159-170","type":"develops","note":"The Manimekalai's Ajivika says gain, loss, birth and death are mixed in at conception and the fate laid down before brings what follows; Neelakesi tests this with the infant girl whose womanhood must then already be present.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Buddhist epic records the claim that a life's course is fixed at conception and walks away from it; the Jain epic answers it with a concrete reductio, the grown woman's features absent from the infant."},{"a":"nk:704","b":"k:380","type":"qualifies","note":"The Kural asks what is stronger than fate, which comes first whatever one devises; Neelakesi attacks a fixity that sets in advance what comes, how, how much and when.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet claims fate outruns human contrivance, but the Jain attack shows that the vulnerable step is the further claim that every particular of an outcome is already fixed, which the couplet does not make.","gn":["the verse gains a contrast showing that its target is the total fixity of every detail, not every Tamil teaching of fate's strength","the couplet gains a measure of how far its claim stops short of the Ajivika determinism that the Jain debater thought refutable"]},{"a":"nk:707","b":"mm:27:159-170","type":"develops","note":"The Manimekalai's Ajivika says gain, loss, birth and death are all mixed in at conception and the fate laid down before makes what comes; 707 tests that claim on breasts, teeth, hair and nails that a newborn lacks.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Buddhist epic lets the Ajivika's 'all is set at conception' stand and walks away; the Jain epic takes it as a claim about the body and refutes it by the parts that visibly appear later."},{"a":"nk:709","b":"mm:27:159-170","type":"tension","note":"The Manimekalai's Ajivika puts all of a life into the moment of conception; 709 shows that this would put a woman's daughter's daughter, and so on, into the first womb.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Holding the two together shows what the Jain thinks the doctrine costs: if a life is complete at conception, so are all lives to come from it, an infinite nesting the bare report never faces."},{"a":"nk:713","b":"mm:27:120-141","type":"develops","note":"The Manimekalai's Ajivika says atoms never enter one another; in 713 the Jain's Ajivika says the same of souls, each with its own length and breadth.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The rule of non-penetration that the Buddhist account gives for atoms is, in the Jain account, also a rule for souls, and only there does it meet the objection of crowded bodies."},{"a":"nk:717","b":"mm:27:120-141","type":"develops","note":"Both epics give the Ajivika fire rising, water sinking and wind moving; 717 adds that the soul rises with fire, and 718-720 attack the whole scheme.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"Two hostile witnesses agree on the Ajivika theory of natural motions, which makes it likely a real doctrine; the Jain adds the soul to it because a rising soul is what he can refute."},{"a":"nk:715","b":"mm:27:108-119","type":"qualifies","note":"The Manimekalai's Ajivika counts the soul as a category apart from the four atoms, the one that knows; 715 presses an opponent who gives the soul form.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Buddhist report keeps the Ajivika soul apart from matter, so the Jain's charge that it has form and can be sliced like a pumpkin is aimed at a version the other report does not give."},{"a":"nk:729","b":"mm:27:171-182","type":"qualifies","note":"The Manimekalai's Jain lists dharma, adharma, time, space, soul, atoms, deeds, bondage and release; 729 has the Jain debater close by giving the three-times-three categories.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"A Buddhist's account of Jain doctrine mixes substances with the path, while the Jain epic hands the convert the nine categories as one fixed count, which shows how each side reckoned the Jain list."},{"a":"nk:722","b":"nk:127","type":"develops","note":"In 127 the sage gives Neelakesi the nectar of dharma as the medicine for birth; in 722 she tells the Ajivika she was a demon until a great one gave her that medicine.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The cure first given to the demoness becomes the debater's own credential: she argues not only from doctrine but from her own conversion, offering the opponent what she was given."},{"a":"nk:724","b":"nk:116","type":"develops","note":"116 names the triple discipline as the medicine for the diseases of birth; 724 names birth as the disease, bad deeds as its cause and true understanding as its cure.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The sermon's medical scheme is not left in the opening: at the close of a debate it is the frame in which the defeated opponent is offered treatment rather than condemnation.","gr":2},{"a":"nk:723","b":"k:948","type":"develops","note":"Kural 948 tells the physician to find the disease, then its root, then the remedy, and act; 723 says the teachers' instruction does the same for the suffering of many beings.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural's method of the physician becomes, in a Jain debate, the method of the religious teacher, so that a rival doctrine is treated as a disease to be diagnosed rather than an enemy to be beaten.","gn":["the debater's appeal to teachers gains a known, stepwise method that any listener can check her diagnosis of the Ajivika against","the couplet's clinical rule is shown to carry over to the cure of births, a reach its place in the chapter on medicine does not claim"]},{"a":"nk:724","b":"k:358","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 358 says understanding is seeing the excellent so that the folly called birth departs; 724 calls birth a disease, names bad deeds eaten as its cause, and makes true understanding the cure.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Both make birth the evil and understanding the cure, but the Jain verse inserts the deed as the cause between them, so understanding ends birth only by ending the eating of past deeds.","gn":["the Jain claim is seen to share its end and its means with a widely held rule, which strengthens its offer to the opponent","the couplet's 'folly called birth' is given a causal account it does not state: births go on because bad deeds are being eaten"]},{"a":"nk:722","b":"t:2075","type":"tension","note":"Tirumular says that as a madman is cleared by medicine, he was made clear when Nandi gave grace; Neelakesi says she, once a demon, was made clear by the dharma a teacher gave her.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"Both use the same image of madness cured, but the cure differs in kind: for the Saiva it is the Lord's grace that stops his doing, for the Jain a teaching she must take in and keep.","gn":["the Jain convert's cure is shown to be teaching without a gracious god, which is what sets it apart from the nearest Saiva parallel","Tirumular's grace-as-medicine is seen against a rival in which the medicine is doctrine and the patient does the work"]},{"a":"nk:735","b":"mm:27:227-240","type":"tension","note":"Both epics give the Sankhya twenty-five, but the Neelakesi counts the unmanifest and the five subtle elements while the Manimekalai counts the five sense objects and thought (சித்தம்) and leaves primal nature outside the list.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Two rival polemics hand down different lists under the same count of twenty-five, so the Tamil reports of Sankhya were not one fixed table and neither epic's list can stand for the school by itself."},{"a":"nk:736","b":"mm:27:227-240","type":"qualifies","note":"The Manimekalai's Sankhya soul is the knowing awareness, without the three qualities, one, pervading and eternal; the Neelakesi's has the same marks but adds that it does nothing and yet experiences.","grade":"sound","better":"develops","te":"The Buddhist and the Jain reports agree on the soul's marks, but only the Jain report foregrounds the pair 'does nothing' and 'experiences', which is exactly the pair it then sets against each other in 740-745."},{"a":"nk:741","b":"nk:736","type":"tension","note":"741 quotes 736's words எத்திறத்தின்னுஞ்செய்யான் and turns them on the teacher: a soul that does nothing cannot be the master who teaches.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The refutation works by quotation: the Sankhya's description of the soul, repeated word for word, is shown to cancel the act of describing it."},{"a":"nk:743","b":"mm:29:154-172","type":"develops","note":"The Manimekalai's teacher lists a thesis that goes against one's own words (சுவசன விருத்தம், 'my mother is barren') among the false theses; 743 says the Sankhya's admission of acting souls destroys his own thesis (மேற்கோள்).","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"What the Buddhist manual gives as a textbook fault with a toy example, the Jain debater springs in a live debate, showing how the logicians' catalogue of faults was used as a weapon between schools."},{"a":"nk:745","b":"nk:302","type":"develops","note":"In 302 Neelakesi asked the Buddhist how formless consciousness could touch or be bound to a body with form; in 745 she asks the Sankhya how a formless soul with no contact with nature can see or experience.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"One Jain objection, that the formless cannot touch the formed, is aimed at two opposite opponents, the Buddhist who denies a soul and the Sankhya who makes it untouched, which shows the Jain body-sized soul is meant as the middle that both lack.","gr":2},{"a":"nk:748","b":"nk:307","type":"develops","note":"In 307 Neelakesi answered that deeds carry the soul between births as wind turns a whirligig; in 748 she tells the Sankhya his soul, whether it goes or not, cannot be carried, and that its twofold deeds are its companion.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The image of deeds moving the soul that answered Mokkalan becomes the dilemma that sinks the Sankhya: only a soul that acts and is acted on can be carried by its own deeds from birth to birth."},{"a":"nk:746","b":"t:115","type":"tension","note":"Neelakesi asks what could bind a soul that is pure and changeless; the Tirumanthiram makes soul and bond both beginningless, so the soul was never first pure and then bound.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"The Jain question to the Sankhya, what first bound a pure soul, is the question the Saiva verse avoids by making bondage as old as the soul, at the price of a soul that was never pure until the Lord draws near.","gn":["The Neelakesi's question gains a known school's answer, showing that one reply to it is to deny the soul ever had a pure beginning.","The Tirumanthiram's 'beginningless' gains a reason: it closes off the debating question of how a pure, unchanging soul could fall into bondage."]},{"a":"nk:747","b":"k:706","type":"same_image","note":"Kural 706 has crystal show the colour of what is near it, as the face shows the heart; the Sankhya, in Neelakesi's report, uses crystal beside a coloured thing for a soul that only seems changed.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The same crystal runs opposite ways: in the Kural its borrowed colour is a true sign of what lies within, in the Sankhya it is a mere appearance over a soul untouched, which is why Neelakesi can call the image an evasion.","gn":["The Neelakesi verse gains a common Tamil use of the crystal image, in which borrowed colour reveals rather than hides, against which the Sankhya use looks like special pleading.","The Kural couplet gains a sense of the image's other use, where crystal's colour is denied any truth, showing that its own reading of the face as a reliable sign is a choice."]},{"a":"nk:735","b":"t:451","type":"qualifies","note":"The Sankhya of 735 counts twenty-five principles that need no maker; Tirumular's Lord makes the twenty-five within the womb for each birth.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Saiva verse keeps the Sankhya tally of twenty-five but puts a maker over it, the one element both the Sankhya and its Jain critic do without.","gn":["The Neelakesi list gains evidence that its twenty-five were common coin in Tamil doctrine, later taken up into a theistic account of embodiment.","The Tirumanthiram's bare 'twenty-five' gains its named members and its Sankhya origin, so a reader can see what the Lord is said to assemble."]},{"a":"nk:760","b":"nk:516","type":"develops","note":"In 516 Neelakesi tells the Buddha that sound travels until it reaches the ear; in 760 she tells the Sankhya that sound has form and so cannot belong to space.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"The same Jain premise, that sound is matter, does the work against two opponents: it makes sound travel against the Buddhist and keeps it out of formless space against the Sankhya.","gr":2},{"a":"nk:759","b":"nk:295","type":"develops","note":"295 says that without dharma and adharma the room of souls and matter would spill past the world's edge; 759 says space itself runs on through the world into the non-world.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Read together, the two verses show why the Jain account needs a limit beyond space: space has no edge, so the world's bound must come from the conditions of motion and rest.","gr":2},{"a":"nk:765","b":"nk:736","type":"develops","note":"In 736 the Sankhya says the soul does nothing, has no quality, is eternal and present everywhere; in 765 Neelakesi uses that all-presence to deny that soul and nature ever newly join.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The opponent's own praise of the soul as everywhere present becomes the premise that undoes his account of creation by union, since a thing never absent cannot newly arrive.","gr":2},{"a":"nk:759","b":"mm:27:183-201","type":"agrees","note":"The Manimekalai's Jain speaker says space gives room to all things; Neelakesi says space is one, formless, non-sentient and extends through world and non-world.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Buddhist epic's report of Jain space matches the Jain epic's own voice on its function, while only the Neelakesi adds the non-world beyond the world, the part an outside report left out."},{"a":"nk:760","b":"mm:27:202-226","type":"tension","note":"The Manimekalai's Sankhya speaker derives the ear and sound from space (line 214); 760 says sound has form and cannot belong to formless space.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The derivation the Buddhist epic lets the Sankhya state unanswered is the very step the Jain epic attacks, so the two polemics differ less in what they report of Sankhya than in what they choose to contest."},{"a":"nk:756","b":"mm:27:227-240","type":"tension","note":"The Manimekalai's Sankhya speaker counts speech, hands, feet and the two excretory organs among the twenty-five principles; 756 denies these are organs, since they have no object and no knowing.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Set beside the plain Sankhya list in the Manimekalai, Neelakesi's mockery shows exactly which item of the count she treats as weakest: the organs of action, which fail her test that an organ must know something."},{"a":"nk:763","b":"t:2294","type":"tension","note":"Tirumular says the soul reaches the saving state by seeing through the twenty-five principles, prakriti and maya; 763 says listing the principles can do nothing for a soul that never changes.","grade":"weak","better":"qualifies","te":"The pair shows what the Saiva use of the twenty-five needs and the Sankhya lacks: a soul that can be bound and freed, so that seeing through the principles is a real change of state.","gn":["The Neelakesi verse gains a case where the twenty-five lower principles are in fact used as a path to release, which is the use its objection says is impossible.","Tirumular's verse gains a pointed objection it must meet: that seeing through the principles changes nothing if the soul who sees is unchangeable."]},{"a":"nk:766","b":"mm:27:202-226","type":"tension","note":"Manimekalai's Sankhya speaker lets primal nature unfold and withdraw by itself and names no creator; in the Neelakesi's Sankhya sarukkam the opponent defends a Lord of five acts, with the dance first.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The two polemical epics give the same school different shapes: the Buddhist account keeps Sankhya godless, while the Jain account hangs a Saiva-style lord on it, so neither can be read alone as what the Tamil Sankhyas held."},{"a":"nk:766","b":"t:2727","type":"tension","note":"Tirumular makes the five dances the five acts done by grace; the Neelakesi cites the dance and the acts only as the opponent's shield, which 767-775 break.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The doctrine Tirumular offers as the heart of grace is the very one the Jain debater names in order to refute, and her answer (a god who is all is the author of every harm) is the objection Tirumular's dance never meets.","gn":["the epic's brief list is shown to be a real Saiva teaching, the dance joined to the five acts, not a straw man","the verse gains a hostile contemporary reading that treats the five acts as making god responsible for destruction and harm"]},{"a":"nk:771","b":"k:1","type":"tension","note":"The Kural's first couplet says the world has the primal Lord as its first; the Neelakesi argues no creator can be first without an endless regress of makers.","grade":"weak","better":"qualifies","te":"Kural 1 says the Lord is first but never that he made the world, so the Jain regress argument against a creator leaves the couplet standing, which shows why Jain readers could keep it.","gn":["the epic's attack is shown to be aimed at a maker, not at every claim that a Lord comes first","the couplet's simile of the first letter gains a test that sets first apart from creator"],"gr":2},{"a":"nk:776","b":"s:1058","type":"tension","note":"Sivavakkiyar uses the banyan folded in its own seed as the image of Parabrahman grown within a man; Neelakesi mocks the tall palm said to be already in its seed.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The tree-in-the-seed image that a Siddhar offers as an insight was, for a Jain debater, the weak point of the doctrine that effects pre-exist, so one image carries a mystic's claim and a logician's refutation.","gn":["the epic's reductio is shown to bear on a living image of Tamil devotion, not only on a school's technical thesis","the Siddhar's image meets an objection it never answers: if the tree is in the seed, why is the hand that holds the seed not holding a tree"],"gr":2},{"a":"nk:777","b":"nk:389","type":"develops","note":"Against Mokkalan (389) Neelakesi held that a thing's modes perish and arise while the thing stays; against the Sankhya (777) she says a thing is non-existent now and existent later, both at once.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"One Jain doctrine is turned two ways: against the Buddhist's momentary things she stresses what lasts, against the Sankhya's lasting effects she stresses what is not yet, and each answer depends on the other."},{"a":"nk:780","b":"mm:27:227-240","type":"tension","note":"Manimekalai's Sankhya speaker calls the person the one awareness that knows all; Neelakesi takes the seer and enjoyer as the opponent's own words and gives that knowing to every soul.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Where the Buddhist epic only records the person as one knowing awareness, the Jain epic uses the same attribute to argue that the Sankhya has already granted a knowing soul in every being.","gr":2},{"a":"nk:781","b":"nk:264","type":"develops","note":"With Arkkachandra (264) Neelakesi offered right vision as the path; with Parasaran (781) the defeated man concedes in his own words and she gives him that vision.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"The Neelakesi ends its debates with a gift and not only a win: the same jewel of right vision closes the Buddhist and the Sankhya sarukkams, so conversion is the form a victory takes."},{"a":"nk:785","b":"mm:27:241-262","type":"develops","note":"The Manimekalai's Vaisheshika gives the same six categories (பொருள், குணம், கருமம், சாமானியம், விசேடம், கூட்டம்) and is left unanswered; Neelakesi's opponent gives them in 785 to 788 and she answers them from 791.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"The six-category list was a fixed set piece of Tamil accounts of the schools, and the two epics differ in what they do with it: the Buddhist epic records it and moves on, the Jain epic makes each category a target."},{"a":"nk:794","b":"mm:27:183-201","type":"develops","note":"The Manimekalai's Jain counts space (ஆகாயம்) as a substance that gives room to all things; in 794 Neelakesi says the Vaisheshika's direction comes down to space.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Neelakesi's denial of direction is not a denial of space: the Jain list keeps space as a substance, so she can fold direction into it rather than abolish it."},{"a":"nk:803","b":"nk:387","type":"develops","note":"In 387 Neelakesi gives Mokkalan the threefold nature of arising, perishing and lasting; in 803 she offers the same three to the Vaisheshika.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The one triad is aimed at opposite errors: against the Buddhist it adds lasting to momentary things, against the Vaisheshika it adds perishing to eternal substances."},{"a":"nk:803","b":"mm:27:171-182","type":"agrees","note":"The Manimekalai's Jain says each thing is both lasting and passing and has arising, staying and perishing (தோற்றமும் நிலையும் கேடும்); 803 names the three as மும்மை.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"A Buddhist epic's report and a Jain epic's own argument agree on the triad as the Jain signature, which shows the Buddhist account of this doctrine is faithful where the Jain uses it."},{"a":"nk:800","b":"nk:393","type":"develops","note":"In 393 Neelakesi tells the Buddhist that quality and bearer are both different and one; in 800 she tells the Vaisheshika that they need no third thing to join them.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Her 'different and one' answers two opposite opponents: the Buddhist who keeps qualities without a bearer, and the Vaisheshika who keeps a bearer so apart from its qualities that it needs a joining category."},{"a":"nk:802","b":"nk:499","type":"develops","note":"In 499 she tells the Buddhist that nothing is ever known except through its qualities; in 802 she tells the Vaisheshika that a quality other than its thing cannot be called its.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The same principle that a thing is reached only through its qualities is used first to prove a bearer exists and then to deny that the bearer can stand apart from what it bears."},{"a":"nk:804","b":"nk:393","type":"develops","note":"In 393 Neelakesi told the Buddhist that the quality is the qualified thing and that she holds them both different and one; 804 states the same thesis against the Vaisheshika.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"One Jain thesis faces opposite errors: against the Buddhist, who keeps only qualities, she stresses the bearer; against the Vaisheshika, who splits quality from bearer, she stresses their oneness."},{"a":"nk:819","b":"nk:120","type":"develops","note":"The opening sermon names standpoints (நயன்) among the doors through which the soul and its qualities are examined; 819 and 820 use the standpoints to settle the quality question and win the opponent.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The sermon's list of methods is not decoration: the standpoint it names is the very tool that ends the Vaisheshika debate, by making one and different two views of one thing.","gr":2},{"a":"nk:807","b":"nk:86","type":"develops","note":"The sermon promises that in the land of deeds penance drives out dark karma and brings the highest bliss; 807 argues that this needs deeds to be qualities of the soul itself.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The promise of release in the sermon rests on a metaphysics the later debate supplies: only if karma inheres in the soul can penance change the soul and free it."},{"a":"nk:823","b":"nk:162","type":"develops","note":"Before her first debate Neelakesi bows at the Arhat's shrine; at the end of this one she sends the defeated Ulokan to praise the Lord's shrine.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The debates begin and end in worship at the Jina's shrine, so winning an argument is framed as bringing the loser to the place the debater started from."},{"a":"nk:815","b":"mm:27:241-262","type":"tension","note":"The Manimekalai's Vaisheshika closes his account with inherence joining quality and bearer, and the Buddhist epic lets it stand unanswered; 815 attacks that very relation with a regress.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Read together, the two epics show which Vaisheshika category the rival schools found weakest: the Buddhist work records inherence as the school's last word, the Jain work makes it the first thing to fall."},{"a":"nk:813","b":"mm:27:241-262","type":"qualifies","note":"The Manimekalai's Vaisheshika makes generality the first common being that lets substance, quality and action work; 813 says the universal is only likeness between things.","grade":"weak","better":"tension","te":"What the Vaisheshika in one epic treats as the ground of all working, the Jain in the other reduces to resemblance, so the dispute is over whether the common is a thing or a relation."},{"a":"nk:807","b":"k:5","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 5 says both kinds of deed, born of darkness, do not cling to those devoted to the Lord's praise; 807 says good and bad deeds and their fruits are qualities of the soul, or birth and release mean nothing.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Both count good deeds with bad as what binds, but the Jain debater asks what clinging is: a deed can cling or fall away only if it is a quality of the soul, which the couplet takes for granted.","gn":["the verse's technical argument is tied to a plain moral teaching that both good and bad deeds bind","the couplet's image of deeds clinging gets a stated account of what the clinging is, which it does not give"]},{"a":"nk:819","b":"k:355","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 355 calls understanding the seeing of the true thing in a thing whatever its nature; 819 says thing and quality are one from one standpoint and different from another.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The couplet separates a thing's nature from its truth and asks us to see through to the truth; the Jain standpoint doctrine holds that the separation itself depends on where one looks.","gn":["the standpoint method is set against a widely held definition of understanding, showing what it adds: a rule for when nature and thing are to be told apart","the couplet's split between a thing's nature and its true being is shown to be one possible view, not the only one"]},{"a":"nk:821","b":"t:2293","type":"tension","note":"In 821 darkness perishes as the disciple understands the three and remembers the Lord's feet; in Tirumanthiram 2293 the malas leave like evening darkness before a flame by grace.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Both texts end bondage with darkness dispelled, but in the Jain verse the remover is the disciple's own knowledge and remembrance, while the Saiva verse gives the work to grace.","gn":["the Jain verse's silence about any act of the Lord stands out once set beside a text where the Lord's grace does the work","the Saiva image of grace as flame is set against a release with the same image and no giver, which shows grace is its distinctive claim"]},{"a":"nk:826","b":"nk:831","type":"tension","note":"Bhutikan dismisses Neelakesi as a Sudra woman with no competence in the Veda; five verses later the poet names her by her learning (புலமையினாள்) as she argues from the phonetics of the Veda itself.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The poet answers the Brahmin's caste taunt not with a counter-taunt but by showing the Sudra woman doing the grammarian's and logician's work on the Veda, so the debate's form refutes his premise before its content does."},{"a":"nk:826","b":"nk:852","type":"tension","note":"The Brahmin who opened in anger, calling her an unbeliever, is at the close urged to speak truly, concedes, and is praised by the onlookers as one of the true.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"The Jain poem converts its opponent rather than humiliating him: the man who began by denying her standing ends with standing of his own, praised for yielding to the truth."},{"a":"nk:830","b":"nk:19","type":"tension","note":"The opening sermon praises a land where Brahmins and the rest make up the four flawless varnas, none straying from its path; here the same heroine argues that high and low birth is not real, since no sense can detect it.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Jain epic holds two views of caste at once: as a social order it can praise the four varnas, but as a claim of inborn rank it denies it, so its quarrel is with birth as worth, not with the order of occupations."},{"a":"nk:836","b":"nk:105","type":"develops","note":"The sermon lists the deaths of beasts in Vedic sacrifice among the many sufferings death brings; the debate takes the same slaughter and argues who bears its karma.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"What the sermon offered as an item of compassion for the victim becomes in debate a question of moral accounting for the sacrificer, so the poem moves from pity for the beast to the sacrificer's own liability."},{"a":"nk:827","b":"mm:27:96-107","type":"develops","note":"In the Manimekalai the Vedic brahmin states that the Veda has no cause, beginning or end, and Manimekalai simply judges that it agrees with no way of truth; here the same thesis is put by Bhutikan as a challenge and answered with five inferences.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Buddhist epic records the Veda's beginninglessness and dismisses it without argument, while the Jain epic makes it the hinge of a full debate, showing which of the two heterodox poets chose to argue the point."},{"a":"nk:833","b":"vp:13","type":"develops","note":"The Valayapathi fragment says not to accept a dharma joined with killing; Neelakesi applies that rule to the Veda, which she says forbids killing and yet permits limitless killing in sacrifice.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"The fragment's maxim gets a named target: the dharma joined with killing is the Vedic sacrifice, and the Neelakesi adds that such a dharma fails even by its own rule against killing."},{"a":"nk:836","b":"k:259","type":"qualifies","note":"The couplet ranks not killing one creature above pouring oblations in a thousand sacrifices; Neelakesi says the sacrifice itself is the killing and its heavy deed falls on those who perform it.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"Read beside the Jain charge, the couplet's comparison is seen to be the mild form of an argument the debater presses to the end: the Kural weighs sacrifice against abstention, Neelakesi denies the sacrifice any merit at all.","gn":["Neelakesi's charge gains a widely shared ethical maxim that already ranks abstention above sacrifice, so her conclusion looks like its extension rather than a sectarian novelty","the couplet gains a case where the oblations it sets aside are themselves animal killing, which sharpens its comparison into an indictment"],"gr":2},{"a":"nk:834","b":"k:134","type":"qualifies","note":"The couplet says a Brahmin's birth is ruined when conduct fails though the Veda can be relearned; Neelakesi says the Veda itself teaches that birth yields nothing yet fixes the four classes and shuts out the Sudra.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Both put conduct above birth, but the Kural keeps birth as a rank that conduct can lose, while Neelakesi finds the Veda caught between those two and calls the mixture confusion.","gn":["the verse gains a well-known saying that already ranks conduct above the Brahmin's birth, which makes the contradiction she alleges in the Veda visible in a familiar text","the couplet gains a reader who notices that it still assumes a Brahmin's birth as something to be lost, which the Jain argument treats as the very confusion"]},{"a":"nk:839","b":"k:43","type":"qualifies","note":"The couplet names tending the ancestors and the gods among the householder's five chief duties; Neelakesi argues that merit done in the name of the eminent dead cannot reach them, or the released would be bound again.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"Set beside the Jain reductio, the Kural's duty to the ancestors is seen to say nothing on whether anything reaches them, which is the question on which the Vedic and Jain views divide.","gn":["Neelakesi's argument gains a respected text whose duty to the ancestors her opponent would cite, so the reductio is seen to strike at ordinary household piety too","the couplet gains a sharp question its list leaves open: whether tending the ancestors benefits them or only disciplines the householder"]},{"a":"nk:840","b":"n:101","type":"develops","note":"The quatrain says an old deed seeks out its doer as a calf finds its mother among many cows; Neelakesi says a deed of killing done for the gods ripens in the killer as poison in the one who eats it, whoever it was meant for.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The quatrain's claim that a deed finds its own doer becomes in the debate a weapon against transferred merit, showing that the proverb rules out offering one's deed to gods or ancestors.","gn":["Neelakesi's argument gains a common proverb that already states her premise, so her opponent must deny popular wisdom to escape it","the quatrain gains a disputed case, sacrifice offered for another, in which its calf image does real argumentative work"]},{"a":"nk:828","b":"t:51","type":"tension","note":"Tirumular says there is no dharma that leaves out the Veda, and the wise gave up disputation and recited it to gain release; Neelakesi argues by inference that the Veda is made and, being joined to killing, drives its followers to evil births.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The two verses disagree both on the Veda and on method: Tirumular sets recitation above argument as the way to release, while Neelakesi makes argument the test that the Veda fails.","gn":["the verse gains a clear statement of the Veda's claim to contain all dharma, the claim her inferences are built to break","Tirumular's verse gains a concrete instance of the disputation it tells the wise to give up, and of what that disputation says about the Veda"],"gr":3},{"a":"nk:842","b":"t:214","type":"tension","note":"Tirumular says that when steadfast Brahmins pour the oblation sky, earth, the quarters and their gods flourish; Neelakesi says that rites cannot drive off disease or demons and that gods who delight in killing cannot remove the killer's karma.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Where the Saiva verse gives the rite power to sustain the world and its gods, the Jain debater denies it any power over karma at all, so the two disagree on whether ritual acts on the moral order.","gn":["the verse gains the full Vedic-Saiva claim for the rite that it denies, which shows how much it is rejecting","Tirumular's verse gains an opponent who asks what the oblation can do against a deed already done, a question it does not take up"]},{"a":"nk:830","b":"s:127","type":"qualifies","note":"Pattirakiriyar longs to go about without caste, as the agama of Kapila says; Neelakesi argues that high and low birth is unreal because sight, smell, taste and touch find no difference.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Both reject caste, but on different grounds: the Siddhar appeals to a scripture, while the Jain debater appeals to perception against scripture, which shows that an anti-caste position need not rest on any text.","gn":["Neelakesi's argument gains a later Tamil voice reaching the same rejection of caste, which shows her point outlived the debate","the Siddhar's verse gains a reason from the senses that does not depend on Kapila's unidentified agama"]},{"a":"nk:858","b":"mm:27:263-276","type":"qualifies","note":"Both epics give the materialist the liquor figure for awareness arising in combined elements; Manimekalai's lets awareness die like a drum's sound, Neelakesi's sends each element back to its kind and counts space among five elements.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The liquor analogy was the stock materialist argument in both traditions, but only the Jain epic answers it on its own terms (866, 868), while Manimekalai leaves the doctrine and attacks only its theory of knowledge."},{"a":"nk:874","b":"mm:27:277-283","type":"qualifies","note":"Manimekalai asks the materialist whether some have not known a former birth, and he waves it off as the delusion of dreams or possession; Neelakesi points instead to the newborn seeking the breast.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"Set beside the Manimekalai, the Jain choice of evidence shows its point: an infant's instinct cannot be dismissed as dream or a god's delusion, the very escape the materialist used against memory of past births.","gr":2},{"a":"nk:856","b":"k:850","type":"develops","note":"The Kural says one who denies what the world holds to be will be counted a demon (அலகை); the epic names its materialist Pisasakan, a name that sounds like பிசாசு, and ends the sarukkam by showing him a demon (887-892).","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The epic turns the couplet's label into a plot: the man who denies spirits carries a demon's name and is converted by meeting one, so the Kural's 'demon' becomes both insult and refutation.","gn":["the name Pisasakan gains an ethical frame, as the stock word for the denier of what all accept","the couplet's unnamed blank, what is denied, is filled with a case: the denial of soul, rebirth and spirits"]},{"a":"nk:869","b":"k:271","type":"qualifies","note":"The Kural has the five elements laugh inwardly at a deceiver's hidden conduct; Neelakesi argues that no element has theft, lust or generosity as a quality.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"Read together, the Kural's laughing elements must be a figure of moral witness, since the Jain debate shows that moral traits are exactly what the elements cannot bear, and her 'thieving element' mocks any literal reading.","gn":["her sarcastic 'thieving element' gains a target: a familiar way of speaking in which elements do take moral notice","the couplet gains a test of its figure: whether the elements could literally judge conduct is a question the schools argued"]},{"a":"nk:863","b":"t:1677","type":"tension","note":"Neelakesi argues that the ascetic's staff and bowl would be wasted unless a soul survives the body; Tirumular says garb put on the body is not for the soul and falls away with the body.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"Both tie ascetic dress to the body-soul question but draw opposite lessons from it: for the Jain debater the gear presupposes a soul, for Tirumular it cannot reach the soul at all, so outward penance is evidence in one text and irrelevance in the other.","gn":["the epic's argument gains its limit: the Saiva text denies that outward marks bear on the soul it presupposes","Tirumular's dismissal of garb gains a context in which garb was used as proof of the soul against a materialist"]},{"a":"nk:862","b":"nk:680","type":"develops","note":"Against the Ajivikas Neelakesi argued that a Lord who never spoke left no scripture; here she argues that a school with no teacher freed from deeds has no first book at all.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Her test of a school's scripture is constant across the debates: authority runs only from an omniscient speaker, and the materialist fails it more completely than the Ajivika, having no such teacher even in claim."},{"a":"nk:865","b":"nk:568","type":"develops","note":"Against the Buddha, who denied a soul, she asked who he then was that taught; against the materialist she asks which element is the one debating with her.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The same self-reference argument serves against both deniers of the soul: the debater's own presence is the counterexample, whether the denial is the Buddhist's no-self or the materialist's mere elements."},{"a":"nk:860","b":"nk:893","type":"tension","note":"Pisasakan says the world has always been only the combining of elements and those who say otherwise are deluded; at the sarukkam's end he says he accepts birth, demons and all she said.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"The epic stages the materialist's claim to permanence of his view in order to reverse it within the same sarukkam, so the debate ends in a conversion rather than a mere victory."},{"a":"nk:879","b":"nk:857","type":"tension","note":"In 857 the materialist says the five elements become eye, nose, tongue, body and ear with their five objects; in 879 Neelakesi tells him to give up that very thought and sets up the soul.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The materialist's derivation of each sense from an element is the exact point Neelakesi chooses to deny, so the soul she proves is defined against a sense apparatus made of matter."},{"a":"nk:888","b":"nk:49","type":"same_image","note":"In 49 the sage knows she may try to frighten him with her transformations (விகுர்வணை) and fail; in 888 she uses the same power of transformation to frighten the materialist into belief.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The demon's power to change shape, useless against the Jain sage at the start, becomes at the end the Jain debater's proof of unseen beings, so the same faculty passes from assault to teaching."},{"a":"nk:892","b":"nk:134","type":"develops","note":"In 134 Neelakesi likens the sage to a mother who nurses her infant even when it hurts her, and drops her false form; in 891 and 892 she, the demoness, calls herself a mother to the frightened Pisasikan and makes him kin through dharma.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The work closes its debates by repeating its opening conversion one step down: the demoness who was mothered into dharma now mothers a man into it, so conversion is shown as a lineage passed on."},{"a":"nk:885","b":"nk:7","type":"develops","note":"In 7 the poet asks readers not to scorn his work, since people take even a bird's cry as meaningful; in 885 Neelakesi counts the omen of bird cries among the things the elements cannot explain.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The poem leans twice on the common trust in bird-omens, once to defend itself and once to defeat the materialist, which shows how much of its case rests on what the world already accepts."},{"a":"nk:888","b":"mm:27:284-289","type":"qualifies","note":"Both epics answer a materialist who accepts only perception: Manimekalai shows him that he knows his own parents only by inference; Neelakesi grants his rule and gives him a perception, the sight of a ghost.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Buddhist and the Jain epic meet the same Lokayata rule in opposite ways: one turns the rule against itself and leaves the materialist answered, the other satisfies the rule and leaves him converted."},{"a":"nk:886","b":"mm:27:277-283","type":"develops","note":"Manimekalai asks whether some have not known a former birth, and the materialist dismisses such knowledge as the delusion of the god-struck or the dreamer; Neelakesi offers the reborn man who leads his kin to the treasure he hid.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The reborn man's found treasure is a check outside his own mind, so it meets the very objection the materialist raised against Manimekalai's appeal to remembered births."},{"a":"nk:892","b":"k:850","type":"qualifies","note":"The Kural says one who denies what the world holds to exist will be set down on earth as a demon; the materialist who denies ghosts is named Pisasikan, the ghost's one, and the demoness claims him as son once he takes the dharma.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The epic plays the couplet's sentence out and reverses its sting: the denier is indeed reckoned of the demons, but as their adopted kin through dharma rather than as an object of scorn.","gn":["Neelakesi's pun on the name gains a known moral frame, the denier of common belief as a demon among men.","The Kural's unnamed 'what the world holds' gets a content, ghosts and rebirth, and a case where the denier is won back rather than condemned."]},{"a":"nk:878","b":"t:1805","type":"qualifies","note":"Neelakesi asks what cause brings the elements together, since they cannot join themselves; Tirumular sets unknowing maya beside the knowing soul and calls the making of elements and senses the fivefold work of grace.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"Both texts deny that unknowing matter assembles itself into a knower, but where the Jain debater leaves the cause in the soul and its deeds, the Saiva verse gives it to grace.","gn":["Neelakesi's open question gains the answer a rival theist school gives, showing which answer the Jain debater leaves unsaid.","Tirumular's assignment of elements and senses to grace gains the opponent it presupposes: the materialist for whom the elements need no cause at all."],"gr":3},{"a":"nk:882","b":"s:512","type":"develops","note":"The materialist says that when the elements part, all comes to nothing; the Siddhar says he saw the five elements die and fall and the five senses burn and fall.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The Siddhar describes as his own experience the parting of elements and senses that the materialist says must end awareness, and he remains to report it, which is the case Neelakesi argues for and does not show.","gn":["Neelakesi's bare rejection gains a first-person witness who outlasts the falling away of the elements.","The Siddhar's vision gains its doctrinal stake: it is the very event the materialist claims would leave no one to see it."]},{"a":"so:1","b":"cc:1","type":"qualifies","note":"Both open by praising the Jain god, but the Cintamani defines him by the bliss he reached by himself and his unceasing qualities, while the Soolamani defines him by the sum of deeds he conquered and by the freeing of those who bow.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"One invocation names what the god is, the other what the god did to deeds, so the Jain epics share a god but each praises a different side of him.","mm":1},{"a":"so:1","b":"k:10","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 10 has those who reach the Lord's feet cross the sea of births; Soolamani 1 has those who bowed at the feet lose their deeds, after a god who conquered deeds himself.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"The Kural says the feet carry one over births, and the Soolamani names a ground for it: a god who has himself overcome the deeds that bind.","gn":["the departure of the devotees' deeds is set in the crossing-of-births frame that Tamil praise of the Lord's feet already uses, which the verse does not state","the Lord's feet get a reason for their power, a victory over deeds, which the couplet leaves unsaid"],"mm":1},{"a":"so:1","b":"t:1649","type":"tension","note":"Soolamani 1 praises a god who won the sum of deeds himself and lets those who bow be freed; Tirumanthiram 1649 says by Siva's grace deeds do not cling.","grade":"weak","better":"qualifies","te":"Release from deeds is credited to a conqueror's own victory in the Jain verse and to the giving of grace in the Saiva one, so the two differ over who removes the deeds.","gn":["the god is seen as a conqueror who had deeds to overcome, not a giver, set against a Saiva grace that works without such a victory","grace is seen against a god who earned the freedom by his own conquest, which makes the Saiva grace read as a gift and not a result"],"mm":1},{"a":"so:2","b":"cc:2","type":"same_image","note":"Both praise the Jain god as light: the Soolamani's light of the world above after splitting the deeds, the Cintamani's holy form that blazes brighter than flame.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Soolamani's light is the result of cutting the deeds, while the Cintamani's is a body brighter than flame and marked with signs, so the same image names an act in one and a form in the other.","mm":1},{"a":"so:2","b":"nk:1","type":"same_image","note":"Both call the Jain god a lamp: the Soolamani's சுடர் that stands as the world's light after the deeds are split, the Neelakesi's ancient lamp of dharma who knows all things.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"The lamp is the Soolamani's god in the world above, the Neelakesi's god as the source of dharma, so the image moves from where he stands to what he teaches.","mm":1},{"a":"so:3","b":"cc:4","type":"qualifies","note":"Both poets ask the learned to deal with the flaws of the work, but the Cintamani asks that they wash the flaw out with their wit, while the Soolamani expects the wise to guard the lapses of people like himself.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"One poet asks the reader to correct the flaw and the other to shield it, so the two Jain epics set different tasks for the learned in front of a faulty work.","mm":1},{"a":"so:4","b":"cc:6","type":"qualifies","note":"Both place the work before an assembly of the discerning: the Soolamani says the discerning in the king's assembly accepted it, while the Cintamani says it was set out to be told to the assembly of the discerning.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Soolamani states the assembly's acceptance as already given, where the Cintamani only offers the story for it, so one poet claims approval and the other asks for it.","mm":1},{"a":"so:5","b":"nk:6","type":"same_image","note":"Both poets answer the fear of fault with the moon's spot: the Neelakesi's poet says even the moon is not spotless and he will own a fault, the Soolamani's says the moon's spot does not stop some from worshipping it.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Neelakesi's poet takes the blemish on himself, while the Soolamani's poet asks the good to look past it, so one image gives a confession in one work and a plea in the other.","mm":1},{"a":"so:5","b":"n:221","type":"qualifies","note":"Naladiyar 221 says the faults of those taken as good are kept within, as rice has its husk; Soolamani 5 asks that the poet's fault count for nothing before the good.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The Naladiyar's rule keeping faults hidden between friends becomes a poet's plea that readers apply it to his own work, which turns a rule of friendship into a rule of reading.","gn":["the plea to overlook a fault is given a rule behind it, a standing habit of the good to keep faults within","the rule of keeping faults hidden is seen applied from a poet to his readers, outside the friendship it was written for"],"mm":1},{"a":"so:7","b":"k:738","type":"qualifies","note":"The verse praises the land by festival and wealth only; Kural 738 lists freedom from disease, protection and yield as a land's ornaments.","grade":"forced","better":"parallel_domain","te":"The epic shows a land's greatness by its splendour alone, so the reader sees what the Kural's list adds that this praise leaves out.","gn":["A test of what a land's wealth should rest on, which a bare picture of splendour lacks.","A concrete land to which the five ornaments can be held up, where the couplet only names them."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:11","b":"p:201","type":"develops","note":"Women's perfumes weaken even ascetics here; the Thiruppugazh poet confesses his own ruin from craving women.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The scenic verse already carries the temptation that the poet of the Thiruppugazh later tells in the first person.","gn":["A named cost of the pleasure it lightly sketches, which the verse itself does not state.","An earlier poetic case in which the same allure reaches ascetics, not only a self-confessed sinner."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:12","b":"pp:2:218-231","type":"same_image","note":"Both set the regions of the Tamil land together in one country: the four regions mingled here, the tunes and creatures of each region exchanged there.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Both poems prove a country's wholeness by letting the four regions share each other's things, but the epic only lists them while the Pattupattu ties the mingling to the king's just rule.","gn":["A political reason for the blend, the king's one umbrella, which the epic's list leaves unsaid.","A plain statement that the regions simply lie together, without the court's praise."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:14","b":"a:74","type":"same_image","note":"Both set the cowherds' flute in the evening pastures: a sign of plenty here, a torment to the waiting wife there.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The same flute is joy in the public landscape and pain in a private heart, so the reader sees how the sound takes its meaning from who hears it.","gn":["A listener inside the scene, which the epic's description does not give.","A public, cheerful reading of the flute against which her pain stands out."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:21","b":"a:82","type":"same_image","note":"Peacocks dance to the clouds' roar here, and in Akananuru 82 the hill is a stage where the peacock is the dancer.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Both make the hill a stage on which weather and creatures perform together, but the Akananuru names the full orchestra while the epic gives only clouds and peacocks.","gn":["A fuller account of how the hill's sounds become music, which it only hints at.","The same scene shown as ordinary prosperity, free of a lover's longing."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:29","b":"k:731","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 731 names unfailing yield, worthy people and wealthy people as the marks of a land; verse 29 gives only the yield, in stored grain that does not shrink as it is eaten.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Read against the Kural's three-part definition, the epic's country is first described by what it yields, and the people and the rich come only later, so the poet is building the land from its harvest outward.","gn":["The verse gets a criterion that shows why grain heaps count as describing a land and not only as a list of crops.","The couplet's abstract 'unfailing yield' gets concrete grains stored in houses and not diminishing when eaten."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:30","b":"pp:3:164-177","type":"qualifies","note":"The Pattupattu's mullai is wild woodland of avarai and kaanthal on a hunters' road ending in a meal of roast game; verse 30 gives the mullai as cropland of sesame, avarai and tuvarai pods.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The same named tract is a place of hunters' hospitality in the older poem and a place of pod crops in the epic, so the epic poet has turned the mullai from forest road to farmland.","gn":["The verse gets the older mullai as foil and so shows that its pods mark a farmed mullai that the Sangam poem does not describe.","The Pattupattu passage gets a later poet who reads its woodland by its yield, which shows that the tract was open to being counted as farmland."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:31","b":"cc:44","type":"qualifies","note":"Cintamani verse 44 shows ploughmen and buffalo rushing into the fields; Soolamani verse 31 shows the sugarcane and paddy standing with no one in the field.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"Two Jain epics describing the land differ in whom they let in: the Cintamani fills its fields with the labour that makes the crop, and the Soolamani leaves the crop standing alone.","mm":1},{"a":"so:33","b":"pp:2:218-231","type":"same_image","note":"In the Pattupattu the fishers sing the kurinji tune, the hill people wear neythal chaplets and the tracts are said to gather under one umbrella; in verse 33 each kind of woman wears the flowers of her own tract.","grade":"wrong","better":null,"te":"Both poems sort a land by its peoples and flowers, but the Sangam poem mixes the tracts to show one king's rule, while the epic keeps each tract's women with her own flower and ends in a catalogue.","gn":["The verse gets from the Pattupattu the reason such sorting of peoples matters, the unity of a realm under one ruler, which the epic verse leaves unstated.","The Pattupattu passage gets the women of each tract, whom it does not describe, as the carriers of the tract's flowers."],"gr":2,"mm":1},{"a":"so:34","b":"k:1228","type":"tension","note":"Kural 1228 has the herdsman's flute as a weapon that kills the lonely woman at evening; verse 34 has the herdsmen's konrai flute so sweet that the asunam beast sleeps.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The same herdsman's flute soothes the beast and wounds the parted woman, so its effect lies in the listener's state and not in the music, a point neither verse shows alone.","gn":["The verse gets a hearer for whom the flute hurts, which shows that its sweet peace holds only for listeners at ease and sets a limit on the land's contentment.","The couplet gets a flute that is gentle to those at ease, which shows that the woman's pain is her own separation and not the sound."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:50","b":"so:36","type":"same_image","note":"Verse 36 measures the city against the serpents' world below, and verse 50 against Indra's heaven above.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The poet bounds the city between the Naga world beneath and Indra's world above, so its praise is a scale with two ends, not one comparison repeated.","mm":1},{"a":"so:46","b":"so:37","type":"same_image","note":"Verse 37 makes the city a woman's body and verse 46 fills its ponds with swans that hear her anklets and fear their mates are gone.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The poet moves from city as woman to a woman's sound as the cause of the birds' fear, so the beauty praised is also felt as loss, which verse 37 does not show.","mm":1},{"a":"so:44","b":"so:40","type":"same_image","note":"Verse 40 gives the women's eyes rain-dark paint and the wishing tree, and verse 44 shows bees fooled by the same eyes into taking them for flowers.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The women's eyes are praised once by their effect on lords and once by their effect on bees, so the poet treats the same beauty as ruling men and nature alike.","mm":1},{"a":"so:52","b":"k:386","type":"qualifies","note":"Verse 52 gives Payapati a gentle face for those who come near; Kural 386 says a king easy to see and gentle in speech is praised above others.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The epic's king is gentle only to those who draw near and hot to foes, so the Kural's single ideal is shown split in two by the king's own enemies.","gn":["The verse gains a standard that says why being easy to approach counts as praise in a king, which the city-description leaves unstated.","The couplet gains a king who keeps his gentleness for friends and is hot to foes, a limit on a rule the couplet states without exception."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:54","b":"t:244","type":"develops","note":"Tirumanthiram 244 calls the king's share one sixth of the produce and ties it to keeping dharma without lapse; 54 shows the king taking his sixth only as grace.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The verse puts the one-sixth rule into practice as restraint on both tax and killing, while the Tirumanthiram makes it a step on the road to release and wealth.","gn":["The verse gains the Tirumanthiram's claim that dharma kept without lapse is the road to release as well as wealth, which its own praise of the king leaves unsaid.","The Tirumanthiram gains a king who actually takes only the sixth and refuses wrathful killing, so its rule has a figure who keeps it."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:54","b":"k:550","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 550 allows a king to put the cruel to death as weeding the crop; 54 has this king subdue even opponents by bowing them, and not slay in anger.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet's weeding is the harsher rule, and the verse shows a king who treats killing as what grace avoids, so the two differ on whether death is part of a king's work.","gn":["The verse gains a rival standard in which a king's killing counts as justified, against which his restraint reads as a choice, not a rule.","The couplet gains a king who will not weed even his foes, showing how far a ruler can go without using death."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:58","b":"k:447","type":"tension","note":"Kural 447 prizes counsellors who rebuke the ruler; 58 shows a court where no one proposes anything except at the king's hint.","grade":"forced","better":"parallel_domain","te":"The couplet praises the courtier who dares to rebuke, and the verse depicts a court where none speak unless prompted, which shows what the king gives up by having no one to correct him.","gn":["The verse gains a measure that reveals its silent court as lacking the correction a ruler needs.","The couplet gains a concrete court of obedient, fearless speakers, showing what its rebuking counsellors would stand against."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:66","b":"so:53","type":"develops","note":"53 says the only weapon abroad in his reign was Kama's arrows; 66 shows the same flower-arrowed god working inside the palace to join the king and the two queens.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The praise of a bloodless reign leads to the place where Kama's arrows land, showing the opening claim as the first note of the palace's own story.","mm":1},{"a":"so:67","b":"so:56","type":"tension","note":"56 says the king guards himself and the earth; 67 has the queens draw his mind from the work of ruling.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The king whose guarding of the world is praised is shown giving up the work of ruling to the queens' hold on his mind, so the poem sets one claim against the other.","mm":1},{"a":"so:67","b":"k:902","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 902 says shame follows the man whose desire for his woman makes him careless of other things; 67 has two wives bind a king so that he lets the work of ruling go.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet speaks for any man and the verse shows the cost to a king, whose neglected work is the world's guarding, so the stakes are larger than shame.","gn":["The verse gains a verdict on a king set aside by love, which the narrator does not give.","The couplet gains a ruler whose lapse touches a whole realm, which widens its cost beyond personal shame."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:69","b":"so:55","type":"same_image","note":"55 puts kings and sages in the shade of the king's umbrella and feet; 69 puts the whole earth asleep under the one white umbrella while the king bathes in the queens' breasts.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The shade that began as the world's refuge ends as the setting for the king's pleasure, so the umbrella changes from protection to the sign of a wealth enjoyed.","mm":1},{"a":"so:69","b":"s:1790","type":"tension","note":"Siddhar 1790 scolds the stony mind for wanting to rule the whole earth under one umbrella; 69 closes the king's praise on exactly that single umbrella and his pleasure under it.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The Siddhar treats the one-umbrella rule as a desire to be given up, while the poem treats it as the king's finished wealth, so the same picture means a trap in one and a reward in the other.","gn":["The verse gains a rival reading of the one umbrella as the object of craving, against which its praise sounds partial.","The Siddhar verse gains a king who has the whole earth under one umbrella, so its warning has a figure who is already there."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:69","b":"n:3","type":"qualifies","note":"Naladiyar 3 says that kings who rode under the royal umbrella fall when their deeds turn against them; 69 shows a king at the height of his umbrella-wealth, with no word of what deeds will bring.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The quatrain says the umbrella's glory lasts only while deeds hold it up, and the verse stops at the glory without the fall, so the reader supplies the warning from the quatrain.","gn":["The verse gains a condition on its calm, that the king's wealth lasts only while his deeds do.","The quatrain gains a king in full peace, whose praise stops where its warning would begin."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:73","b":"k:5","type":"qualifies","note":"At the princes' birth the clinging moisture of evil deeds is said to dry up for many; Kural 5 says deeds of darkness do not cling to those who delight in the Lord's praise.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural lifts the hold of deeds through the devotee's own praise, but the epic lifts it for many through the birth of a great being, so each work places the loosening of karma somewhere different.","gn":["The epic verse gets a stated condition for karma's loosening, the devotee's praise, which it does not state.","Kural 5 gets a case where the hold is loosened by someone else's birth and not by the sufferer's devotion, a test of how exclusive its condition is."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:79","b":"so:84","type":"same_image","note":"Both verses close on the princes reaching an age that sets a fire in the women's hearts; 79 puts the cause in their youth, and 84 adds the women's sickness for wealthy lords.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The repeated line first locates the fire in the princes' youth, then ties it to wealth and rank, so the same phrase shifts what the women are said to burn for.","mm":1},{"a":"so:78","b":"so:109","type":"develops","note":"78 lists conch and discus on the younger prince's palm; in 109 the court names him one who will guard the whole world under one umbrella.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The body marks listed as ornament in 78 are read, in 109, as a sign of universal rule, which changes how the earlier portrait should be heard.","gr":2,"mm":1},{"a":"so:79","b":"t:180","type":"qualifies","note":"79 shows youth as a fire kindled in women; Tirumanthiram 180 has a man recall being sweet as cane juice to women and now bitter as the kanjiram fruit.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The epic shows only the rising stage of desire's arc, and Tirumular supplies the other end, so together they place the princes' present blaze on a curve that ends in the speaker's bitterness.","gn":["The epic's fire of youth gets the end of its arc, which the verse does not show.","Tirumanthiram 180 gets the beginning of its curve, the stage and the women's fire from which its speaker later falls."],"gr":2,"mm":1},{"a":"so:80","b":"n:17","type":"qualifies","note":"80 likens the women's sidelong glances to Kaman's arrows; Naladiyar 17 warns against desiring a woman of spear-like eyes, for she too will lean on a staff.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The epic shows the glance as a weapon at the moment of its force, and Naladiyar names the later fall of the same weapon, so the pair sets the glance's power against its short life.","gn":["The epic's glances get a limit it does not state, that the spear-eyed will stoop and walk with a staff.","Naladiyar 17 gets a scene of the spear-like glance at work, so its warning is aimed at what the reader has just watched."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:105","b":"n:52","type":"tension","note":"The astrologer waits on the king's sign and then speaks to show his learning, with a sight of all three times; Naladiyar 52 calls those who prattle of the stars the most foolish of the foolish, set against the thought of death.","grade":"weak","better":"qualifies","te":"The story rests its plot on an astrologer who is admitted, heard and believed by the whole court, while the quatrain calls his craft folly, so the two give opposite verdicts on the same trade.","gn":["The poem's own motive line, that he speaks to display his learning, gets an outside judgment on whether that learning is worth the court's trust.","The quatrain's charge of folly meets an astrologer who is welcomed by a king, which shows the claim being tested against a trusted practitioner."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:105","b":"k:696","type":"qualifies","note":"The astrologer sets aside courtesies, waits for the king's sign and speaks; Kural 696 tells a speaker to know the mood, weigh the time and say what is wanted and not disliked.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The astrologer keeps the Kural's rule of reading the king and timing his speech, but the poet names his aim as showing his learning, a motive the couplet never allows for.","gn":["The wait for the king's sign is given a stated rule behind it, so it reads as court craft and not mere delay.","The couplet's advice is shown in a scene where the speaker has something the king will welcome, and where the speaker's vanity can still sit under a correct manner."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:106","b":"si:9:45-54","type":"same_image","note":"Kannaki's dream of trouble in Madurai, told to Kovalan before he leaves, and the astrologer's dream of an elephant garlanding the young lord are both dreams that foretell what is to come.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"In the Silappathikaram the dream is a warning that cannot turn the course of fate, while here it is a promise that the court at once turns into a plan, so one image serves ruin in one epic and fortune in the other.","mm":1},{"a":"so:106","b":"s:529","type":"tension","note":"The astrologer reads a night's dream as true knowledge of what will come; the Siddhar song says all he saw has turned into a dream, like moonlight on a forest.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"One poem trusts a dream to show what is real in the future, and the other uses the dream as the name for the whole of what is seen, so the same word divides prophecy from unreality.","gn":["The prophetic dream gets a view that holds waking sight itself to be dream-like, which unsettles the court's easy trust in the sign.","The Siddhar's figure of dream as unreality meets a dream treated as sure foreknowledge, which shows what the figure is set against."],"gr":2,"mm":1},{"a":"so:107","b":"so:106","type":"develops","note":"Verse 106 gives the dream of the elephant that garlands the young lord; 107 gives its fruit, a Vidyadhara king's gift of his daughter.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The astrologer first tells the dream and only then its fruit, so the reader sees a sign turned into a forecast of a marriage and an alliance.","mm":1},{"a":"so:116","b":"so:108","type":"develops","note":"The astrologer foretells a messenger with a letter in the Pushpakarandam garden; the ministers propose to set the stone platform in order and post a guard in the garden on his day.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The court does not wait on the prophecy but prepares the place it names, so counsel here means arranging the ground for a sign the king has only just heard.","mm":1},{"a":"so:118","b":"so:116","type":"develops","note":"The ministers propose to place Turumakanthan as guard in the garden; he goes to the garden after bowing to the king.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The proposal becomes action in two verses, and the guard sets out only after the king's approval, which shows how a counsel becomes an order.","mm":1},{"a":"so:140","b":"k:557","type":"qualifies","note":"Verse 140 faults a mighty king for want of grace to the lowly; Kural 557 says that a king's lack of tender regard is to his people as lack of rain to the earth.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The epic names one flaw in a king otherwise perfect, so the Kural's claim that lack of regard undoes all else is tested against a ruler praised for everything else.","gn":["A measure of the flaw's weight, which the verse names without saying how much it costs.","A concrete king whose every other virtue stands, so the couplet's rule faces a hard case."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:140","b":"k:549","type":"tension","note":"Kural 549 counts guarding the people and removing wrongdoing as the king's work and no fault; verse 140 faults a king on the ground of grace toward the lowest lives, which the Kural's list does not ask of him.","grade":"weak","better":"qualifies","te":"The epic widens the king's duty to grace toward all lowly lives, where the Kural's duty stops at the people and the removal of wrongdoers, so the two hold different measures of a good ruler.","gn":["A rival standard, the Kural's, by which the king is judged only on what he does to his people.","A test of its limit: a king who punishes wrongdoers by the Kural's rule may still lack grace toward lower creatures."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:130","b":"cc:535","type":"same_image","note":"Both Jain epics describe a Vidyadhara city by its splendour, this one as a golden city on the southern ridge, the other as a casket of gems opened.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"Both epics fix the Vidyadhara realm in the reader's mind as a city of gems and gold above the plain, so the genre's convention is clear from the pair.","mm":1},{"a":"so:159","b":"so:138","type":"develops","note":"In 138 elephant-kings bow to the father; in 159 kings with fierce-eyed elephants bow to the daughter.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The homage the father wins by victory passes to the daughter by beauty alone, so the poem lets beauty hold the sway that the king's sword held.","gr":2,"mm":1},{"a":"so:161","b":"k:1008","type":"same_image","note":"Verse 161 likens the wealth of the worthless to kongu trees that bloom without scent; Kural 1008 likens the wealth of the unwanted to a poison tree fruiting in the town.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Both name a tree whose outer splendour fails its purpose, one for giving no scent and one for giving poison, so wealth without worth is shown useless in one case and harmful in the other.","gn":["A harsher side of the image: wealth without worth does not merely fail to please, it can poison those who come near.","A softer case: wealth may leave the others unharmed but unattended, like blossom that no one stops for."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:166","b":"n:160","type":"qualifies","note":"Naladiyar 160 says the base follow the rich as their one hope; verse 166 says people leave the rich once their sweetness turns.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Naladiyar shows the crowd that clings to wealth and the cuckoo shows it is not loyal, since it goes the moment the taste turns.","gn":["A reason why the followers come: wealth is their only hope, which the cuckoo's simile does not state.","A limit: the clinging lasts only while the sweetness lasts, which the quatrain does not say."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:187","b":"k:3","type":"same_image","note":"Verse 187 hails the god as walking above the lotus, as Kural 3 speaks of the one who walked upon the flower.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The same lotus-walker of the opening couplet appears here as an epic's praised Arhat, so the image carries a Jain form where the Kural leaves the Lord unnamed.","gn":["The lotus-walker is given an addressee, a congregation that loves him though he feels no pleasure, which the verse's own words alone do not show for any named god.","A worship scene in which the image sits within a story of devotees, where the couplet states it as a bare mark."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:188","b":"k:4","type":"develops","note":"Verse 188 says the god takes no joy in praise yet is loved; Kural 4 says the god without liking or aversion frees those who reach his feet.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The epic turns the Kural's rule of the one without liking into a devotional posture: love offered without any hope of being pleased.","gn":["A reason why the worshippers do not need his pleasure, which the verse does not state.","A scene of worship that tests the claim that the one without liking cannot be moved by praise."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:182","b":"t:32","type":"qualifies","note":"Verse 182 says even calling the Arhat all-knowing does not let one know him; Tirumanthiram 32 says no one knows the lord's nature, and the all-pervading lord is praised only in part.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Both say naming does not reach the god, but the Jain verse accepts omniscience as a true name while the Saiva verse denies any measure of the lord.","gn":["The verse's refusal is made sharper: it concedes the epithet and still holds the god beyond it, where the Saiva line denies all naming.","A rival that praises by listing epithets, so the lord beyond naming can be seen as a stand against it."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:175","b":"n:48","type":"tension","note":"Verse 175 draws spring shoots as copies of the women's hands and waists; Naladiyar 48 tells those who praise sandal and garland without knowing the body to look at vultures tearing the carcass.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"A court poet delights in the body's beauty while a moral quatrain holds the same body as carrion, so the same poem tradition holds two views of the body.","gn":["A counter-voice that sees the beauty it praises as fading.","A quatrain's image set against a living scene of pleasure, so its rebuke has a concrete target."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:180","b":"k:10","type":"qualifies","note":"Verse 180 says the shrine and its god end births; Kural 10 says only those who reach the Lord's feet swim the sea of births.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The epic locates the ending of births in the shrine and its god, where the Kural puts it in the devotee's reaching.","gn":["A statement of what the god gives, not only what the devotee reaches.","A place in which the reaching happens, the shrine."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:181","b":"k:5","type":"develops","note":"Verse 181 praises him who conquered the harsh dust of karma; Kural 5 says the two deeds do not cling to those devoted to the Lord's true praise.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The epic names the conqueror of karma whose praise the Kural says keeps deed from clinging, so it names the one the couplet leaves as 'the Lord'.","gn":["A Lord defined by his conquest of karma, so what the praise protects against is plain.","A reader's reason why praise should loosen the deeds' hold."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:185","b":"so:179","type":"develops","note":"Verse 179 opens the shrine's gem doors at the king's coming; verse 185 shuts them after his praise.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The worship is bounded by an opening and a closing of the doors, so the whole of the praise lies between them.","mm":1},{"a":"so:198","b":"k:351","type":"develops","note":"Kural 351 says the joyless birth comes from a confusion that takes the unreal for real; Soolamani 198 says deeds grow from a seed of lacking true knowledge and ripen into pain through a chain of births.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The sage supplies a growth path (seed, deeds, ripening, rebirth) for the couplet's bare claim, while the couplet says what kind of knowledge fails.","gn":["the missing true knowledge is made specific as seeing the unreal as real, a wrong seeing and not just an empty gap","the confusion gains the steps by which it ends in birth: a seed that grows into deeds that ripen as pain"],"mm":1},{"a":"so:198","b":"k:361","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 361 names craving as the seed that produces unceasing birth; the Soolamani's sage names lacking true knowledge as the seed of the deeds that bind.","grade":"weak","better":"tension","te":"Two Tamil teachers name different seeds of rebirth, so the root of bondage is a question of doctrine and is not common ground.","gn":["its seed is set beside a rival one, so lack of knowledge is shown as one choice among Tamil answers","the couplet's craving gets an older cause to answer, whether craving grows out of not knowing"],"mm":1},{"a":"so:199","b":"t:120","type":"qualifies","note":"Soolamani 199 has deeds breed deeds until a refuge is reached; Tirumanthiram 120 has a teacher make the seeds of births like seeds touched by fire, which will not sprout.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Both see rebirth as a growing seed; the Jain verse tells how the chain runs, the Saiva verse how it is cut at the root by a teacher.","gn":["it gains a picture of how the chain can be stopped at the seed, which the sage only says is by reaching a refuge","it gains the chain that the scorched seed stands against, deeds producing deeds through their fruit"],"mm":1},{"a":"so:200","b":"t:138","type":"qualifies","note":"Soolamani 200 makes the feet of the beginningless Lord the refuge from great suffering; Tirumanthiram 138 says the holy feet are Sivam, Sivaloka and the path, and a refuge for those clear within.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"Both name the feet a refuge, but the Saiva feet are the goal and the way at once, while the Jain verse sets the refuge apart from the state it leads to, described in 202.","gn":["the refuge is shown as a means only, as the goal is another thing, by contrast with feet that are also the goal","the feet as goal and refuge together are shown as one answer among others, since a Jain verse names them only as a refuge"],"gr":2,"mm":1},{"a":"so:200","b":"cc:380","type":"qualifies","note":"Soolamani 200 recommends refuge in the Lord's feet as the cure for suffering; Civaka Cintamani 380 shows Civakan going to the Wise One as a raft because an old deed drove him.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"The sermon says why to take refuge and the story shows why someone does, adding that the going is itself moved by an old deed.","mm":1},{"a":"so:201","b":"k:355","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 355 says knowledge is seeing the real in whatever thing; Soolamani 201 sets discerning true things as the first of three, followed by knowledge and conduct kept to the text.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The epic makes the couplet's seeing the start of a sequence that does not end until conduct is kept, not the whole of release.","gn":["the discerning of true things is placed first and so not enough alone, it must be followed by conduct","the couplet's seeing is given a place in a larger course ending in disciplined conduct"],"mm":1},{"a":"so:201","b":"mm:30:1-6","type":"qualifies","note":"After taking refuge, Manimekalai's teacher begins on the three jewels of Buddha, Dharma and Sangha; Soolamani 201 gives three things to hold after the refuge in 200: true seeing, knowledge and conduct.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain","te":"Both sermons give threes after refuge, but the Buddhist three are what one goes to for refuge and the Jain three are what the follower must hold.","mm":1},{"a":"so:202","b":"t:136","type":"tension","note":"Soolamani 202 describes release as a shining form and limitless, unfailing bliss; Tirumanthiram 136 says the soul subsides into Sivam as salt returns to water.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The two release verses differ on whether the freed self keeps its own form and bliss or subsides into another, if the salt image is read as loss of separate being and the Jain 'shining form' as a kept one.","gn":["release is shown as a state the freed self keeps as its own, since no one else is named for it to join","the salt image meets a Tamil account in which the freed soul keeps form and bliss, so merging is not the only picture of release"],"mm":1},{"a":"so:203","b":"k:352","type":"same_image","note":"Kural 352 says that for those of stainless sight darkness leaves and joy arises; Soolamani 203 has the light of knowledge from the sage's words split the darkness of evil deeds in the whole assembly.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The two verses use darkness and light, but the epic has the light come to a crowd from a teacher's words, while the couplet leaves it to one's own clear sight.","gn":["the darkness is given as the darkness of evil deeds, and the light comes by hearing a teacher","the bare word 'darkness', which commentators read as hell, ignorance or births, is shown in one reading as the darkness of deeds"],"mm":1},{"a":"so:205","b":"so:212","type":"develops","note":"Verse 205 has the king's daughter resolve on a vow and think how to finish it with greatness; verse 212 has her complete the vow of Chakravalam and then wish a great special worship.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The resolve at the sermon's end is kept here, and it grows into a second act, the worship, beyond the vow.","mm":1},{"a":"so:214","b":"k:3","type":"same_image","note":"Kural 3 gives the Lord as the one who walked on the flower, at whose feet one arrives; Soolamani 214 gives the girl's hymn to the primal Lord who gave the scripture, who stood on the flower, at whose holy feet she bows.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The hymn uses a flower-footed epithet close to the couplet's and gives it to the Jain Lord with titles that name him, which the couplet does not.","gn":["a familiar Tamil epithet for the flower-footed Lord is borrowed as a form of worship, so the girl's praise sits in a shared idiom","the couplet's disputed 'one who walked on the flower' appears in a later Jain work applied to the Arhat, one data point on how the epithet was read"],"mm":1},{"a":"so:238","b":"so:229","type":"develops","note":"The king's question in verse 229, which youth is fit for his daughter, is met in verse 238 by sending for the ministers learned in policy.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The king does not choose the groom himself but turns his open question into a council of treatise-learned ministers, so the epic makes a marriage a matter for counsel.","mm":1},{"a":"so:215","b":"so:1","type":"develops","note":"The poet's opening hymn praises the god who conquered the sum of his deeds; the princess's hymn here names what he conquered, desire and Death.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"The invocation praises the god's victory in general terms, while the princess's hymn names the enemies; one is left to see that the poem's single praise is told twice, in two voices.","mm":1},{"a":"so:237","b":"k:445","type":"develops","note":"Kural 445 tells a ruler to take counsel from ministers as his eyes; verse 237 says that kings' lives and even Indra's lordship do not flower without counsel.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"The epic widens the Kural's rule from a ruler's prudence to a claim about all rule and even the gods' rule.","gn":["A statement of the rule as a duty of a ruler, where the verse shows only that counsel is needed.","An example from the Brahmanical and divine world that the couplet's own kings never reach."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:234","b":"k:445","type":"same_image","note":"Kural 445 calls counsellors a ruler's eyes; verse 234 pictures a king opening like a lotus by the sun only through the learned who consider.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"The epic gives a picture of how counsel acts, a bloom that opens only at the sun's touch, where the Kural gives a rule that counsel should be taken.","gn":["A plain rule that counsel is to be taken, behind its picture of the lotus.","A figure that shows kingship as a bloom needing the light of the learned."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:228","b":"n:274","type":"qualifies","note":"Naladiyar 274 uses a daughter of the house, enjoyed by a stranger, as a figure for wealth that others will use; verse 228 calls a girl the jewel beyond all earthly and heavenly jewels.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The Naladiyar's picture treats a daughter's going to another house as a thing to be expected, while the epic makes her the treasure whose giving is a king's weightiest care.","gn":["A plain statement that a daughter's passage to a stranger is as sure as hoarded wealth falling to another.","A measure of how much the girl was worth to the one who gives her, which the simile treats as a mere example."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:242","b":"k:637","type":"tension","note":"Verse 242 has the king say he can grasp subtle things only through the learned treatises; Kural 637 says that even one who knows the method must act by knowing how the world goes.","grade":"weak","better":"qualifies","te":"The council's first claim makes the treatise the king's only guide, where the couplet warns that a treatise's method must still be tested against the world, so the two disagree on where good judgment is found.","gn":["A corrective: the books the king defers to still leave him to read the world's own way.","A case for deference: why a ruler should hold a treatise above his own sense."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:243","b":"k:547","type":"qualifies","note":"Verse 243 says the learned keep blame and fierce deeds from a king whose sceptre is unbent; Kural 547 says justice guards the king if he does not falter in it.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The epic names who keeps a king from faltering, the learned counsellors, where the couplet names only the law that guards him if he does not.","gn":["A reason why the sceptre stays unbent: it is kept by the learned.","Persons who do the guarding the couplet gives to justice itself."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:244","b":"k:445","type":"develops","note":"Verse 244 makes the ministers the king's mind, eye, living, strength and sceptre; Kural 445 calls counsellors the king's eyes and says he must take them after testing them.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The epic widens the Kural's single image of eyes to the king's whole body of rule, while the couplet adds a duty of choosing that the epic leaves unsaid.","gn":["A rule for choosing: the king must test his counsellors before taking them.","A fuller count of what rests on counsellors, from sight to the sceptre itself."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:247","b":"k:638","type":"develops","note":"Verse 247 makes a king one who acts calmly on what the learned choose and tell him; Kural 638 says the man at the king's side must tell him what is good even if the king knows nothing and spoils wise counsel.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain","te":"The epic sets the king's duty to act on counsel beside the minister's duty to give it, where the couplet shows only the minister's duty when the king will not hear.","gn":["A case where the king refuses counsel and the minister must still speak.","A test of kingship: the king's standing comes from acting calmly on the counsel he is given."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:248","b":"k:546","type":"tension","note":"Verse 248 says counsel, not strength of arm, is the quality of a king; Kural 546 says it is not the spear but the unbent sceptre that gives victory.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"Each puts a different power above the weapon, counsel in the epic and justice in the couplet, so the pair shows that the Tamil tradition names more than one winner of a war.","gn":["A moral check: counsel that is clever but crooked would still fail the sceptre's test.","A practical account of how victory is planned, where the couplet gives only its moral condition."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:249","b":"k:620","type":"qualifies","note":"Verse 249 says even what comes like fate shows itself within the counsel of counsellors; Kural 620 says those who strive without flagging see even fate's back.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The epic puts fate and effort together in the council hall and finds that deliberation is where fate takes shape, while the couplet says effort can turn fate back.","gn":["A place where fate and effort meet: counsel, not only labour, is the effort.","A limit on its boast: effort works through counsel, and counsel is the stage on which fate shows."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:249","b":"n:101","type":"qualifies","note":"Verse 249 says what comes like fate takes shape in the counsellors' deliberation; Naladiyar 101 says an old deed seeks out its doer as a calf finds its mother among many cows.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The epic lets fate arrive through chosen acts of counsel, while the quatrain says the old deed finds its doer whatever he does, so the two differ on how far deliberation shapes what comes.","gn":["The unavoidable pursuit by an old deed, which the verse softens by placing fate in counsel.","A route by which an old deed arrives: through the choices of counsellors."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:250","b":"so:237","type":"develops","note":"Verse 237 says that without counsel a king's life does not flourish and that even Indra's lordship rests on a thousand counsellors; verse 250 has the king himself rank counsel above his other powers.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The poet's maxim about counsel becomes the king's own weighing of counsel against his other powers, so the section turns a reflection into a decision.","mm":1},{"a":"so:251","b":"cc:9","type":"develops","note":"Verse 251 compares a king in love's grip without learned men to a rutting elephant ridden by a fool; Cintamani 9 tells of a king whose dense desire made him refuse counsel and lose to a plot.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain","te":"The Cintamani's story gives the cost that the Soolamani's maxim only warns of, a king who would not hear and lost the throne.","mm":1},{"a":"so:252","b":"k:448","type":"qualifies","note":"Verse 252 says failed counsel turns on the king like an angry snake whose charmer's art has failed; Kural 448 says a king with none to rebuke him perishes even without a foe.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The epic shows a king with counsellors destroyed by counsel gone wrong, where the couplet shows a king destroyed by having no counsellor who dares to rebuke him.","gn":["A king who had counsellors and still fell: counsel itself can fail.","A picture of how the king ruins himself: the instrument turns on the user."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:254","b":"cc:206","type":"qualifies","note":"Verse 254 has the king thank the ministers for his rule; Cintamani 206 has a counsellor press the king to hear counsel he does not want.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The Soolamani's council shows a king who welcomes counsel, while the Cintamani shows the counsellor who must speak when the king would not listen, so the ideal depends on a willing king.","gr":2,"mm":1},{"a":"so:262","b":"so:140","type":"develops","note":"Verse 140 names the one fault of the Vidyadhara king as want of grace toward the lowly; verse 262 has a minister say that great lords who bear the lapses of the lowly are followed by the whole earth.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The council offers the remedy for the king's single fault, so the earlier flaw reads as a fault the poem expects the counsellors to mend.","mm":1},{"a":"so:262","b":"n:66","type":"develops","note":"Verse 262 says lords who bear the lowly's lapses, like sandal under the rutting elephant, are bowed to by the earth; Naladiyar 66 says the well-born bear the base's stone-like words and let them pass, held back by their lineage.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"The epic names the reward of forbearance in the king's public standing, where the quatrain names its reason in birth.","gn":["The reward of forbearance in public honour, which the image alone does not name.","A political result for the restraint that the quatrain justifies only by lineage."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:263","b":"k:559","type":"develops","note":"Kural 559 says that when a ruler bends justice the sky bends the rain; verse 263 says that a cruel king is to the world as a sea-scorching sun is to its creatures.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural names rain as the lost gift, but the epic names the people's suffering as the creatures' in a burning sea, so the ruler's fault is one of heat, not drought.","gn":["The measure of the loss: not a missed crop but life in a sea turned hostile, which the Kural's line does not give.","A different figure for the same fault, in which the injured are creatures with nowhere to go, not a land that goes dry."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:264","b":"k:389","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 389 puts the world under the parasol of a king who can bear bitter words; verse 264 says that a parasol grown hot scorches the cool ponds and groves under it.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The Kural gives the cool shade as a gift of the king's patience, and the epic gives the reverse: when the king is hot the shade itself scorches.","gn":["The danger the Kural leaves unsaid: shade turned to heat when the one under it loses his patience.","A named cause of the parasol's coolness, the king's duty of guarding, in a parallel to the Kural's patience."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:266","b":"so:140","type":"qualifies","note":"Verse 266 says that nothing is better for the world than the shade at the feet of a king who stands in aram and guards the people like his own life; verse 140 faults a king who has every virtue but lacks grace to the lowly.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The counsellor's ideal king guards all like his life, and the poet's own Vidyadhara king, perfect but lacking grace, shows the ideal cannot be taken as met by any one virtue.","mm":1},{"a":"so:266","b":"k:544","type":"same_image","note":"Kural 544 says the world holds to the feet of the great king who embraces his people; verse 266 says no one has anything better than the shade of such a king's feet.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural makes the king's embrace of his people the condition of the world's clinging to his feet, and the epic gives the reason: they cling because men are wounding one another.","gn":["A cause for the clinging, which is men's mutual harm, where the couplet gives only the condition.","A negative picture of what the world is like without the king's feet, which the couplet only implies."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:267","b":"k:388","type":"develops","note":"Kural 388 calls the king who protects with justice the இறை of his people; verse 267 calls him god-like because he orders a man's lot in both worlds.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The epic gives a reason for the Kural's word இறை as god: his rule reaches the next world's lot as well as this one's, which the couplet leaves untouched.","gn":["The ground for calling the king godlike, which is a claim about both worlds.","A reading in which இறை means a power over a man's fortune and not only a title of rule."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:268","b":"k:392","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 392 says numbers and letters, learning, are the eyes of living beings; verse 268 names three eyes, the king, learning and the sun.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural calls learning the only eye of life, and the epic puts learning beside the king and the sun, so what learning sees is made one of three lights the world needs.","gn":["A rival that sees where learning cannot, the king, the sun's equal in lifting the world's darkness.","A list that puts learning's eyes among the world's lights and not only life's."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:272","b":"t:1006","type":"tension","note":"Tirumanthiram 1006 says that worship brings Indra's wealth, then the siddhis, then release; verse 272 says the world of the gods is reached by the true word and great austerity or by unslackening rule.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The same prize, Indra's world, is reached by worship in one and by one's own austerity or rule in the other, so the paths differ over whether a god's favour is needed; the conflict holds if Tirumular's worship counts as asking his grace.","gn":["A path that asks for no god: the epic's way depends on the doer's truth, austerity and rule.","A way to see worship as one route among several to Indra's wealth, which the epic's king-or-ascetic choice does not need."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:273","b":"t:1464","type":"qualifies","note":"Tirumanthiram 1464 says kings of the just sceptre and countless great ascetics have fallen; verse 273 says austerity and rule are heavy burdens, harder than lifting a diamond hill.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The epic stresses how heavy the two burdens are, and Tirumular says that even those who bore both have fallen, so the weight is not the end of the road.","gn":["A measure of the fall: the ones who fell had borne the hardest burdens.","A sense of what the fallen kings and ascetics had carried before they fell."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:274","b":"n:101","type":"same_image","note":"Naladiyar 101 says an old deed seeks out the one who did it as a calf finds its mother among many cows; verse 274 says a deed done in a former birth rises into fruit and shows that it was real.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain","te":"The epic uses karma's sureness as the model for counsel, so a plan's result is as certain as a deed's fruit, a use of karma that the quatrain does not make.","gn":["A comparison for the way a royal plan works, which turns karma into a model of policy.","A second sign of how settled the doctrine is in Tamil verse, said in passing by a counsellor."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:279","b":"n:137","type":"qualifies","note":"Naladiyar 137 doubts that the city of those in the wide heaven is sweeter than learned men gathering and laughing together; verse 279 praises a heaven-like realm that only the meritorious can enter.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The quatrain tests the heaven the epic praises against the pleasure of a company of the learned, and the epic's heaven is praised for its senses while the quatrain finds its sweetness no better than conversation.","gn":["A measure to doubt its own claim: a pleasure the Naladiyar sets against heaven's.","A full picture of the heaven it weighs, which is the one this verse and its neighbours describe."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:282","b":"so:129","type":"same_image","note":"Verse 129 ends the poet's praise of the ridge by asking what more need be told once its groves are wishing-tree gardens with golden lotuses; verse 282 ends on a question of the same kind.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The same closing question over wishing-tree groves is first the poet's boast and then the counsellor's pitch to the king, so the ridge's beauty is now used to move a decision.","mm":1},{"a":"so:286","b":"n:14","type":"qualifies","note":"Naladiyar 14 says a once-desired woman, bent and shaking on a staff, torments men who still desire her; verse 286 describes lovers whose desire sprouts without end in a heaven-like city.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The epic's desire without end belongs to a realm without death (278), so the quatrain's old age has no place there, which shows that the quatrain's argument depends on this world.","gn":["A world where the quatrain's decline does not happen, and so a limit on the quatrain's lesson.","A picture of the endless desire against which the quatrain's old woman is the corrective."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:285","b":"so:130","type":"develops","note":"Verse 130 names a golden city, Ratanupura-Chakravalam, on the southern ridge; verse 285 names a golden city of Ratna-pallavam in the same realm.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The counsellor's golden city may be the poet's or a second one, so the king hears of the Vidyadhara country in terms that differ slightly from the poet's own.","mm":1},{"a":"so:305","b":"so:304","type":"develops","note":"Verse 304 says the kings paid tribute in fear of his anger; verse 305 says that since the girl was born he has stopped taking it.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The same overlord appears first as one who is paid in fear and then as one who waives his due, so the girl's birth is given as a political turning and the courting rests on that change.","mm":1},{"a":"so:310","b":"so:305","type":"develops","note":"Verse 305 reckons the girl's birth as the moment Asvagriva softened; verse 310 gives the birth as the moment the astrologers set down her horoscope.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"One birth is read twice, by a minister as the cause of a change in the suitor and by an astrologer as the moment her fate was fixed, so the story weighs politics against the stars.","mm":1},{"a":"so:309","b":"so:308","type":"qualifies","note":"Verse 308 vouches for Sachchutan's truth and learning; verse 309 says the speaker saw a fault and will not stay silent.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The second minister grants his colleague's praise and then withholds assent in the same breath, so agreement on facts does not carry agreement on the verdict.","mm":1},{"a":"so:309","b":"k:638","type":"develops","note":"Bhavachutan says that he saw a fault and will speak though the other has praised; Kural 638 says the one at the king's side must speak what is good even if the king is ignorant.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet binds a minister to speak against a king's folly, and the scene shows the harder case where he speaks against a fellow minister's persuasive counsel.","gn":["The minister's refusal to join the praise gets a stated rule, so his dissent reads as a duty and not as rivalry.","The rule meets a case in which the one to be corrected is a colleague already trusted by the king, not the king himself."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:300","b":"k:381","type":"qualifies","note":"The minister lists Asvagriva's army, its order and the food supply among the reasons none equals him; Kural 381 counts army, people, resources, ministers, friends and fort as a king's six limbs.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The minister's praise follows the Kural's list for army and food but adds the discus, the lineage and learning, so the poet counts as kingly worth what the Kural's checklist leaves out.","gn":["The speaker's list of army, food and ministers gets a stated standard of what a complete king must have.","The couplet's six limbs meet a king whose praise adds lineage, learning and a magic discus, so its checklist is tested against what a court poet values."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:301","b":"k:636","type":"qualifies","note":"The minister's praise rests on law-treatise, omen-books and learning; Kural 636 says that natural wit joined to treatises cannot be matched by any scheme.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The poem's model minister is made by treatises alone, where the couplet gives native wit the first place, so the court poet and the Kural weigh a counsellor differently.","gn":["The minister's praise gets a measure that asks what books alone can do without native wit.","The couplet's pairing of wit and learning meets a counsellor described only by learning, so it is tested against a case where one half is missing."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:304","b":"k:680","type":"qualifies","note":"The Vidyadhara kings pay tribute and send word out of fear of Asvagriva's anger; Kural 680 says those of small domains, afraid, bow to the great and take what is offered.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The kings' tribute is the couplet's counsel carried out, but the minister then asks for the girl to be given, which shows how bowing to a great king can end in a marriage and not only in a payment.","gn":["The tribute out of fear gets a name in policy: the small bowing to the great for what they can get.","The couplet's bowing is shown running on from tribute into the giving of a daughter, a cost it does not name."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:289","b":"t:201","type":"same_image","note":"Verse 289 names the pleasures of lovers as ripe fruits they taste; Tirumanthiram 201 names a man's wife as the ripe jackfruit he will not eat while he toils for the wild date.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain","te":"Both speak of sexual joy as fruit, but the poem lets lovers eat it freely in the grove while the Tirumanthiram asks whose fruit it is, so the image carries opposite moral weights.","gn":["The fruit image gets a question the verse leaves unasked, whose fruit it is and whether it is one's own to eat.","The jackfruit image meets a scene of lovers who eat the ripe fruit freely, so the verse is seen against the pleasure it warns about."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:314","b":"so:333","type":"develops","note":"Verse 314 speaks of the aeon's time running out for Achuvakandan; verse 333 says the words spoken of him match the old words of the aeon.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The council's reasoning comes back to the same fated end, so the choice of a husband is set under a doom the epic has already spoken.","mm":1},{"a":"so:323","b":"so:2","type":"tension","note":"Verse 323 claims no one, not even the red-eyed Tirumal in anger, can harm Arikandan; verse 2 says the tale is of the red-eyed tall one who tore the lion.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The boast of an unassailable prince is held against the hero the poet promised at the start, so a reader hears the council's confidence as a setup for its overturning.","mm":1},{"a":"so:334","b":"k:504","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 504 says to examine qualities and faults and take what predominates; the roll of princes names only qualities, and verse 334 ends on a danger.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The council's list of suitors names only merits and the last verse adds a warning, which shows the Kural's rule to weigh faults was missing from the roll.","gn":["A named rule by which the one-sided praise of the roll is judged and found short.","A court scene in which the praise-only roll of candidates is the failure it warns of."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:330","b":"k:445","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 445 calls counsellors the king's eyes, to be taken after counsel; verse 330 asks that the worthy suitor be examined and then Pavachchuthan's word settles it.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The epic shows counsel working as the Kural wants, by examining before choosing, but one man's word still carries the room.","gn":["A standard that says who should do the examining and why his word is valued.","A scene in which the eyes of the king are tested by a real choice and the room simply agrees."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:350","b":"so:11","type":"qualifies","note":"Verse 11 says the perfumes and flowers of the land are so heavy that even ascetics in good austerity are weakened; verse 350 shows a youth who stands high in austerity while women's glances strike him.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The poem first lets the land's pleasures unseat ascetics, then offers a youth who stays firm, so the earlier fall is shown as common and not necessary.","mm":1},{"a":"so:351","b":"n:11","type":"develops","note":"Naladiyar 11 says that the wise renounce in childhood because they know grey hair will come; verse 351 shows a youth who turns from music, dance and heaven-like pleasure while still young.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Naladiyar gives the reason for early renunciation and the epic gives a young man living it, so the reader sees how the reason looks as a character.","gn":["A reason for the youth's distaste, the argument from coming old age, which the verse never gives.","A named young man who has already done what the quatrain says the wise do."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:354","b":"t:634","type":"tension","note":"Tirumanthiram 634 says those who do austerity become lords of Amaravati and live in bliss by the Lord's grace; verse 354 says a king who leaves his rule for austerity becomes lord of the gods, with no god named as the giver.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The same heavenly kingship is credited to the ascetic's own austerity by the Jain sage and to the Lord's grace by Tirumular, so the two disagree about who gives the reward.","gn":["A giver of the reward, which the sage's prophecy leaves out, against which the claim of earned rule can be measured.","A path to the same heaven that needs no grace, which tests the claim that grace gives it."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:355","b":"t:258","type":"qualifies","note":"Tirumanthiram 258 names austerity and virtue as a boat across the sea of deeds that the Lord makes fruitful; verse 355 says austerity ends all evil and reaches Sivagati, with the god unnamed.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"Both name austerity as what ends deeds, but one adds a god who makes it bear fruit and the other leaves effort alone to do it, so the dispute is over what austerity needs.","gn":["A second agent, the Lord, against which to weigh the sage's claim that effort alone ends evil.","A case where austerity is said to bring release with no god named, which tests how far it depends on grace."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:356","b":"n:27","type":"develops","note":"Naladiyar 27 praises the firm who see the body as a bubble and resolve to end their wandering; verse 356 says the body's nature of leaving, with austerity and knowledge, brings the mind to the divine seat.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The quatrain stops at the resolve born of seeing the body pass, and the epic names where that resolve is said to lead.","gn":["A reason that the body's passing matters: it is the ground of the resolve that leads to the divine seat.","A destination for the firm resolve, which the quatrain leaves at the ending of birth."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:358","b":"k:380","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 380 says that nothing is stronger than fate, which arrives first even when one devises another way; verse 358 says that when they plan, fate gives the result and a strong plan may end otherwise, and Sumantiri still goes on to counsel.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet gives fate as a rule; the minister uses it as a caution that does not stop deliberation, so the rule is shown as bounding counsel and not replacing it.","gn":["A use of the fate rule as a counsellor's caution, which the couplet's flat statement does not show.","A scene of deliberation where the rule is applied, so its strength is tested against a council that goes on planning."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:358","b":"cc:215","type":"tension","note":"Civaka Cintamani 215 makes a king heedless of a warning because an earlier-formed destiny rose in him; verse 358 has a counsellor name fate as the power over plans and still press counsel.","grade":"forced","better":"parallel_domain","te":"One epic makes fate the reason a king ignores counsel and the other makes it the reason counsel must be given with humility, so fate is shown as excusing the king in one and humbling the adviser in the other.","mm":1},{"a":"so:359","b":"so:382","type":"develops","note":"Verse 359 puts the question of the yazh-voiced girl's fate to the council, and verse 382 has the astrologer turn to the girl's matter.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The council's question is carried from counsel to omen-reading across the chapter, so a royal question is settled by two kinds of experts in turn.","mm":1},{"a":"so:361","b":"k:638","type":"qualifies","note":"The counsellors tell the king to go in at once and not let counsel drag; Kural 638 says a minister must tell even a king who has ruined wise counsel what is good for him.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Here the counsellors' advice is a plain urging of haste that the king accepts, so the Kural's hard case of an unheeding king meets its easy one.","gn":["A test of what it looks like when counsel is taken without a quarrel, which the verse alone does not discuss.","A scene of a minister doing his duty before any failing of the king, which the couplet only imagines after the failing."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:372","b":"k:482","type":"develops","note":"The time-keepers tell the king the hours that have passed; Kural 482 says acting in season is the rope that binds fortune.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The epic shows the machinery of a court that keeps its king aware of the hour, which is what the couplet's rule needs before it can be followed.","gn":["A reason why a king's day is measured and reported, which the verse treats as ceremony.","A working instance of how a king could know the season, which the couplet states only as advice."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:380","b":"k:701","type":"qualifies","note":"The attendants read the king's sign and withdraw unasked; Kural 701 praises one who reads what is meant before it is said.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The reading of a sign here serves privacy and not counsel, so the Kural's praise of sign-readers is shown at work in a small court habit.","gn":["A name for the skill of the attendants who leave, which the verse mentions in a clause.","A scene where reading a sign is discretion and not advice, a use the couplet does not mention."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:367","b":"cc:80","type":"same_image","note":"Both epics let the washed-off paste and saffron of bathing women thicken a pool, here in the Vidyadhara palace and there in the city's ponds.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain","te":"Both Jain epics fix wealth in the cosmetic that the bath washes into the pool, so the image is the genre's mark of luxury.","mm":1},{"a":"so:367","b":"n:48","type":"qualifies","note":"The verse thickens a pool with the washed paste and saffron of women's bodies; Naladiyar 48 mocks those who praise sandal paste and garlands without knowing what the body is.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The epic's picture of the bath as pure delight is set against a Jain verse on the same bodies as flesh for vultures.","gn":["A Jain corrective the poet never voices, so the bath scene stands as one the tradition itself would look past.","A scene of the very perfumed bodies the quatrain mocks, shown as the epic's own delight."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:373","b":"n:60","type":"qualifies","note":"The king ends the day in song and pleasure; Naladiyar 60 says the higher ones look to the pain in pleasure and do not cling.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The chapter shows a whole day of enjoyment with no word of its pain, so the quatrain's test of pleasure is left unanswered by the story here.","gn":["A measure the narrator does not apply, so the night's pleasure goes unjudged in the verse.","A king who is shown enjoying, not a fool, which tests whether the quatrain's contrast of wise and foolish fits a ruler."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:385","b":"k:8","type":"same_image","note":"Verse 385 names the first lord as the light who bore the wheel of dharma (அறக்கதிர் ஆழி); Kural 8 calls the god அறவாழி அந்தணன், where ஆழி can be taken as sea or as wheel.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"A Jain epic uses the words aram and aazhi for a god where the Kural's phrase leaves open sea or wheel, so the same wording is read two ways across the corpus.","gn":["A much older Tamil praise-phrase for a god, which shows its wording is not a coinage but a shared devotional idiom.","A second reading of ஆழி, the wheel an epic plainly bears, which the couplet's own words do not decide."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:395","b":"t:349","type":"tension","note":"Verse 395 has the world take refuge in the grace of the first lord who bears the wheel; Tirumanthiram 349 says the bright-flamed First gave the discus to Achyuta.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"Both texts make the wheel the sign of a first lord, but one makes it his own dharma-wheel and the other a gift he hands to a lesser god, so the wheel's source is claimed for two rival firsts.","gn":["A rival claim, in which the discus is given and not borne, against which its own claim that the first lord holds the wheel stands out.","A Jain account in which the wheel belongs to the first lord and is not passed to any god, against which its gift of the discus reads as a Saiva claim."],"gr":2,"mm":1},{"a":"so:396","b":"k:225","type":"qualifies","note":"Verse 396 gives the lord who has no taste of the senses the work of opening doors that end hunger; Kural 225 ranks the strength of those who endure hunger behind that of those who remove it.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The epic's lord is at once the senses' renouncer and the hunger-remover, so he fills both places the couplet sets in rank order.","gn":["A test of its claim, since the couplet ranks the ascetic's strength below the giver's, which the epic's lord meets by being both.","A figure in whom the ranking does not split, a renouncer who is also the giver the couplet places higher."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:402","b":"so:114","type":"develops","note":"Verse 402 has the lord foretell that the Kesava will take Ashvagriva's discus; verse 114 has the ministers say the young lord will hold the discus weapon in his hand.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The ministers' reading of the palm marks is borne out by the lord's own foretelling, which names the discus's source as a rival's own.","mm":1},{"a":"so:406","b":"so:78","type":"same_image","note":"Verse 406 names the younger of two sons as the purana's hero; verse 78 describes the younger prince as bearing the conch and discus marks on his palm.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"The marks of verse 78 and the purana's foretelling in this chapter are two proofs that the younger prince is the hero, one of the body and one of the story.","mm":1},{"a":"so:409","b":"so:2","type":"develops","note":"Verse 2 promises the lion Tivittan tore open; verse 409 is the first time the story itself foretells it, a month away.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The opening's promise is tied to a named date inside the story, so the lion's killing is something the plot is steered toward, not only the poet's announcement.","gr":2,"mm":1},{"a":"so:408","b":"so:107","type":"agrees","note":"Verse 107 has Podanam's astrologer read a dream that a Vidyadhara king will come and give a daughter; here the Vidyadhara king's own astrologer reads the same match from the ridge and adds what the king gains by it.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"Two astrologers on two sides of the sky foretell the same match, and only the ridge's gives the giver's reason, two rows of the ridge.","mm":1},{"a":"so:430","b":"so:108","type":"develops","note":"Verse 108 says a man able to speak well will come with a letter into the garden Pushpakarandam; verses 429 and 430 bring Marusi to a grove with that name.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The astrologer's date is kept by the story's own messenger, so the plot is built on a promised arrival rather than chance.","mm":1},{"a":"so:418","b":"k:61","type":"qualifies","note":"Verse 418 gives the saying that the childless are like trees and the queen's joy at a daughter; Kural 61 holds children valuable only when they come to know what should be known.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The Kural makes the worth of a child depend on understanding, where the epic king's praise rests only on having a child at all and on her marriage prospect.","gn":["A measure of children's worth beyond the bare fact of having them, which the verse does not state.","A scene where a child is prized for the marriage she will make, which tests the couplet's call for children who know what should be known."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:427","b":"k:686","type":"develops","note":"Verse 427 chooses Marusi as envoy because he is learned and can put kin and affairs into words; Kural 686 names learning, fearlessness and sense of the time as the envoy's marks.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"The ministers' test for an envoy stops at learning and speech, where the Kural adds fearlessness before hostile eyes and a sense of the moment, so the gaps show what the court has not yet asked of Marusi.","gn":["Two further qualities, fearlessness and sense of time, that the verse's choice does not name.","A court that picks an envoy by the stated learning and speech, which shows how a rule is applied in practice."],"gr":2,"mm":1},{"a":"so:441","b":"so:447","type":"develops","note":"447 has the priest foretell the slab; 441 has the keeper offer it as a seat made for the man.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The offer in 441 is the fulfilment of the foretelling in 447, so the keeper is shown acting from a prophecy and not on his own.","mm":1},{"a":"so:451","b":"so:176","type":"same_image","note":"176 draws spring as a copy of women; 451 has the women confuse the peacock with their gait.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"One image runs both ways: spring is drawn from women's bodies in 176, and in 451 women outdo nature, so the poet changes which side is the model.","mm":1},{"a":"so:450","b":"k:1085","type":"same_image","note":"450 sets the women's eyes above the deer's; Kural 1085 finds the deer, the eye and Death all in one look.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The epic uses the same deer-and-eye comparison as the couplet but lets the deer lose, where the couplet lets the look hold the deer's frightened shyness as one of its three natures.","gn":["The women's eyes get the couplet's three-natured look, so the poem's deer is more than a measure of beauty: it is something the look can frighten.","The deer-eye comparison shown at work in a procession where a king sends the women out for a purpose, rather than a lover's chance meeting."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:453","b":"k:1083","type":"same_image","note":"453 calls the women's sword-like eyes the mark of every killing art of love; Kural 1083 learns what Death is from a woman's large war-making eyes.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Both name the glance as a killer, but the couplet is one man's astonished cry and the epic makes it a settled doctrine of the women's whole equipment.","gn":["A first-person confession that the eyes kill, which the epic's catalogue states only as a fact of their bodies.","A general rule about what such eyes carry, given as the sum of breasts, brows and arts rather than the eyes alone."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:453","b":"n:44","type":"tension","note":"453 hails the women's eyes as the killing sign of love; Naladiyar 44 refuses the sword-and-lily praise and sees only a scooped-out palm fruit.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The two cannot both stand: one accepts the eye as a weapon, the other denies the image on the ground that the eye is only flesh, and which holds depends on whether the speaker is praising or renouncing.","gn":["Its praise of the eyes is shown to rest on a metaphor a renunciant would call blindness.","The target of its refusal is given a full, dazzling form in a poem written to enjoy it."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:454","b":"s:10","type":"qualifies","note":"454 shows the women shaping the face's work with glance and smile; Siddhar 10 prays to forget women who show hand, eyes and face.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The siddhar's prayer names the same tools the epic's women use, as the lure to be forgotten, where the epic presents them as skill.","gn":["The women's play of face is seen as what the renunciant fears, not only what the poet admires.","Its infatuating gestures get a full picture of them performed, which the prayer names but does not show."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:458","b":"n:44","type":"tension","note":"Verse 458 has men grant that the women's long dark eyes kill and bow to them; Naladiyar 44 refuses to call eyes spears or carp, since he has seen the eye is a scooped-out palm fruit.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Soolamani gives the lover's image of the lethal eye a scene of surrender, and the Naladiyar answers with a renunciant who would not give way, so a reader sees the image only works on one who agrees to be struck.","gn":["A refusal of the image of the killing eye, which the verse alone only enjoys.","A scene in which men yield to the very image the renunciant's resolve is set against."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:458","b":"k:1085","type":"same_image","note":"Kural 1085 asks whether the young woman's look is Death, an eye or a doe; verse 458 says the women's long dark eyes are skilled at killing.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The eye as Death is a shared trope of the lover's tradition, which the epic makes the plea of many men at once.","gn":["The Kural's own line that the look is Death, which sets this plea in a known convention.","A crowd of men instead of one man, the Kural's one lover made general."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:462","b":"t:204","type":"qualifies","note":"Verse 462 has men blame the Creator for making women, whose beauty they take as the cause of desire; Tirumanthiram 204 turns the same beauty into an etti fruit that looks good and poisons, and tells the heart to hold back.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The men's wonder at the Creator's sin shows desire as a thing done to them, and Tirumular sets it as a choice of the heart, which marks where the epic stops short of the Saiva remedy.","gn":["A remedy for the cry of the men, who only blame the Creator.","A scene of men who feel the beauty the verse warns against, and who blame another for it."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:463","b":"n:89","type":"same_image","note":"Verse 463 puts a fire of desire within the women that sends up lines like smoke; Naladiyar 89 says desire burns the mind itself and is more to be feared than arrow, fire or sun.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The epic shows the fire of desire as marks on the body, and the Naladiyar says what the fire burns, the mind, so each supplies what the other lacks.","gn":["A statement that this fire burns the mind and is worse than outer fires.","A body in which the fire shows as smoke-lines of fine hair."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:465","b":"so:83","type":"qualifies","note":"Verse 83 says the fire of desire burns women within and none bears it more heavily than the shame-bound; in verse 465 the men voice it aloud to the women.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The epic first shows desire borne in silence by women and then spoken freely by men, so the burden is set unequally by who may say it.","mm":1},{"a":"so:466","b":"so:53","type":"qualifies","note":"Verse 53 says that in this king's days nothing but Kama's arrows mingled in the land and no killing force troubled it; verse 466 has the women report to Kama their victory over an army of lovers.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The earlier verse treats Kama's war as the only gentle one, and here it has victims and a victor, which shows the gentle war is still a war.","mm":1},{"a":"so:469","b":"so:50","type":"same_image","note":"Verse 50 says the golden city seems an Indra's heaven come down to earth; verse 469 has a Vidyadhara, who knows the sky, judge that this is heaven.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain","te":"The poet's praise of the city as heaven is confirmed by a sky-dweller who can compare, so the boast is turned into an outsider's verdict.","mm":1},{"a":"so:471","b":"k:84","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 84 says a glad face towards good guests draws the goddess of wealth; verse 471 shows the host women doing every service to the guest for the Vidyadhara.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The Kural's rule is given a court in which welcome is a trained skill and not a feeling, and the Kural shows what the women's service is for.","gn":["A rule that the welcome depends on the host's face, which the women who set aside their looks only half meet.","A setting in which the guest-duty is done by those whose art is desire."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:476","b":"so:81","type":"develops","note":"Verse 81 says the women swoon with fear that the dark-gemmed prince, who came in a dream, will take their lives; verse 476 has the women faint on the terraces as the princes pass.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The dream-fear of the women becomes a public swoon, so the epic shows the prince's coming as the fulfilment of the fear.","mm":1},{"a":"so:495","b":"so:427","type":"develops","note":"At 427 the ministers choose Maruci as one who can carry what he has learned and say what is fit; at 495 his first act on reaching the princes is to rise and bow.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The envoy named for his speech opens with a bow and not with the message, so his fitness shows first in manner.","mm":1},{"a":"so:496","b":"so:500","type":"develops","note":"The prince at 496 asks why the Vidyadhara bows to them; 500 gives the answer, that those who bow at their feet have done hard penance.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The bow is first met with surprise and then given a reason, so the envoy's courtesy is shown to rest on a belief about merit and not on mere flattery.","mm":1},{"a":"so:497","b":"so:409","type":"develops","note":"At 409 the astrologer's sign says Tivittan will split a lion's mouth within a month; at 497 the envoy meets the two princes and weighs them by the rules of his lore.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The foretold hero now meets the person who must find him, so the astrologer's sign is put to a test of eyes before the envoy speaks.","mm":1},{"a":"so:486","b":"n:17","type":"qualifies","note":"Naladiyar 17 warns against the woman's spear-like eyes, since she too will age and lean on a staff; at 486 the blue-eyed women pour out their hearts to the princes.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The epic shows love's pull in full while the quatrain names its price, so the two together show what a reader is warned off and why.","gn":["A test of the women's rush of feeling against the thought that youth and beauty do not last, which the scene does not raise.","A scene in which the beauty it warns against is shown in full, in the women's own act of giving up their hearts."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:487","b":"k:1183","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 1183 says the lover has taken a woman's modesty and given her sickness and pallor; at 487 many women grow pale and sigh for men who have only passed by.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain","te":"The Kural's single lover is replaced by two princes and a crowd, so pallor from love is shown as a common mark of beauty's reach as well as a private ache.","gn":["A name for the paleness of the women's shoulders and a cause for it, the lover's taking of their modesty.","A scene in which the same paleness comes at first sight of strangers, with no parting that the couplet takes as its cause."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:500","b":"k:687","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 687 ranks highest the envoy who knows the protocol, weighs the moment and place, and speaks after thought; at 500 Maruci opens with a bow and a courtesy that honours the princes before any message.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The epic shows an envoy meeting the Kural's test of protocol, and shows that what he first says is praise and not the matter of his errand.","gn":["A standard that names the opening courtesy as part of the envoy's art and not a mere ornament.","A scene in which the protocol it asks for is carried out, with praise placed ahead of the message."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:514","b":"so:108","type":"develops","note":"Verse 108 foretells that a man good at speaking will come down with a letter into the garden Pushpakarandam; here the letter is read in the king's hall.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The astrologer's forecast of a letter is now met by its words, which turn out to be the offer of a bride that the dream was read to promise.","mm":1},{"a":"so:515","b":"so:107","type":"develops","note":"Verse 107 reads the dream as a Vidyadhara king who will come and give a daughter; the letter in 515 gives her as the younger prince's through an envoy, not in person.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The dream's promise returns as a formal proposal sent by letter, so the prophecy opens a scene of answering and choosing rather than ending in a bare fact.","mm":1},{"a":"so:526","b":"so:106","type":"tension","note":"Verse 106 tells a night dream that the court takes as a true sign of this match; in 526 the king says that he can take the offer only as a dream. This holds if 526's dream means a thing not to be trusted.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The same word, dream, stands for a sure sign in the court's mouth and for something too good to be believed in the king's, and the plot rides on that gap.","mm":1},{"a":"so:517","b":"so:427","type":"qualifies","note":"Verse 427 names Marusi because he can say what is fit; in 517 the same envoy answers the king's silence with anger and a rebuke.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The ministers chose a speaker for fitting words, and the scene tests that skill when he is met by silence and shows how quickly it turns to pride.","mm":1},{"a":"so:523","b":"k:685","type":"tension","note":"Kural 685 asks an envoy to speak briefly, to leave out what offends and to say it so that it wins a smile; Marusi's words in 518 to 523 offend the king and end by calling men of the earth unable to know what is fitting.","grade":"weak","better":"qualifies","te":"A couplet of what an envoy should do is seen broken in a scene where the envoy's anger grows from the court's silence rather than from ill will.","gn":["523 gains a standard against which the envoy's pride reads as a failure of his office, not just a mood","685 gains a case where the pressure on the envoy comes from a silent king, so softening speech means bearing silence as well as choosing words"],"mm":1},{"a":"so:518","b":"n:115","type":"qualifies","note":"Naladiyar 115 says the words of the poor glance off while the rich are heard; Marusi says that anyone who speaks wanting something is slighted, even when he speaks for the stronger lord.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Naladiyar finds the cause of being ignored in the speaker's poverty, while Marusi finds it in the act of asking, so a rich lord's messenger can be slighted too.","gn":["518 gains the rule it assumes, that how a man is heard follows his standing, which shows Marusi's complaint to be about rank","115 gains a case where the unheard speaker comes from the richer side, so need rather than poverty may be what silences him"],"mm":1},{"a":"so:520","b":"n:27","type":"same_image","note":"Naladiyar 27 sets the body as a bubble in pelting rain; 520 calls the human body a frothy hollow. The first uses it to urge ending the cycle of births, the second to bar men from a Vidyadhara's wealth.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"One image of the body as foam serves a teacher of release and an envoy's claim of rank, so the image shows weakness only and takes its direction from who uses it.","gn":["520 gains the sense that its froth-body is a commonplace of teaching on impermanence, so using it as an insult turns a lesson into a boast","27 gains a use of the bubble-body to exclude rather than free, showing that the image alone does not call for renunciation"],"mm":1},{"a":"so:527","b":"k:377","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 377 says that even those who amass millions enjoy them only as the apportioner has apportioned; verse 527 has the king say that only former deeds strive and later effort does not win what is not due.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"The epic gives the couplet's unnamed apportioner a face, the person's own earlier deeds, and uses it to settle who may marry whom, where the couplet only consoles about wealth.","gn":["A statement that a lot is fixed by something other than present effort, which the king asserts without a general rule.","A case where the rule is used as an argument across a divide of rank, not as comfort about wealth."],"gr":2,"mm":1},{"a":"so:528","b":"n:265","type":"qualifies","note":"Verse 528 reads the Vidyadharas' high wealth as the fruit of earlier austerity; Naladiyar 265 reads the wealth of the unjust as the fruit of old deeds.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The same rule that makes the Vidyadhara's wealth a sign of past austerity makes a rich man's wealth no sign of present merit, so the epic's reasoning holds only for those of known past.","gn":["A warning that wealth is a poor sign of present worth, which the king does not consider.","A group, the Vidyadharas, for whom wealth is taken as proof of earned austerity, which the quatrain gives to no one."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:529","b":"n:72","type":"qualifies","note":"Naladiyar 72 says to bear in silence the unfit words of those who are not one's equals; verse 529 has Marusi ashamed because the king bore his unfit words.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The quatrain shows forbearance as the superior's duty, and the epic shows what it does to the one borne with: shame and a calmed anger rather than a grudge.","gn":["A rule that explains why the king's patience is praised and why Marusi feels small.","An effect of silent forbearance on the speaker who was borne with, which the quatrain does not show."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:529","b":"so:427","type":"qualifies","note":"Verse 427 chooses Marusi as one who can say what is fit; in 529 his first words are called unfit and he is ashamed.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The minister's praise of Marusi's speech is tested at once by a slip, so his later success in 531 is recovery from a fault and not ease.","gr":2,"mm":1},{"a":"so:530","b":"so:107","type":"qualifies","note":"Verse 107 has the astrologer say a Vidyadhara king will come and give a daughter to the young lord; verse 530 raises the doubt that Vidyadharas and men do not marry.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The prophecy skips the custom against such a match, and Marusi's reply is the story's way of making what was foretold pass through argument before it can happen.","mm":1},{"a":"so:531","b":"k:972","type":"develops","note":"Kural 972 says birth is alike for all and distinction comes from work; verse 531 says Vidyadharas are men, distinguished only by their lore.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet is turned from a general saying into the reason a marriage across a supposed divide may go ahead.","gn":["A principle that makes sameness of birth the ground, so that the Vidyadharas' lore becomes a difference of work and not of kind.","A use of the principle to open a marriage across a boundary of rank, a case the couplet does not take up."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:535","b":"k:133","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 133 says that conduct is nobility of birth; verse 535 has the first lord make the five royal lineages and teach each its conduct and occupation.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"In the epic, lineage is a thing made, with conduct as one of its parts, so the couplet's identity of conduct and birth is the made thing's own mark.","gn":["A sense that a lineage's standing rests on its conduct, so that it is not simply handed down.","An origin story in which lineages are set up by an act of the first king together with their occupations."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:541","b":"k:4","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 4 says those who reach the feet of the one without liking or aversion have no suffering; verse 541 sings of the Lord as one who put away every desire and yet gives his servants unfailing bliss.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The hymn lets the desireless Lord be a giver, which the couplet avoids by saying only that no suffering reaches those at his feet.","gn":["A statement of what the desireless one does for his servants, which the verse puts as a gift.","A hymn in which the desireless one is nevertheless the one who gives, a tension the couplet does not face."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:547","b":"t:1626","type":"qualifies","note":"Tirumanthiram 1626 says that people who ask for many things get their wealth, but those who do great tapas win the end of birth; verse 547 has Nami ask a god's servant for wealth.","grade":"wrong","better":null,"te":"Nami stands as a case of the first sort, asking for wealth at the feet of a yogi, and the Tirumanthiram supplies a rank by which his wish is lower than release.","gn":["A standard by which Nami's request looks lesser than a request for release, a judgment the epic does not make.","A named person who asks for wealth in the presence of a yogi and gets it, which makes the verse's contrast concrete."],"gr":2,"mm":1},{"a":"so:548","b":"t:670","type":"qualifies","note":"Tirumanthiram 670 says siddhis come through eightfold yoga and Shakti's grace; verse 548 has Dharanan hand Nami the sky-going lore and a mountain for the asking.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"In the epic, powers are a gift passed through a go-between to a man who asks for wealth, where the Tirumanthiram sets them at the end of discipline and grace.","gn":["A contrast showing that a power may be had by asking when it is not had by yoga.","A case in which the powers called siddhis go to someone who has done no yoga and wanted only wealth."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:548","b":"so:408","type":"develops","note":"Verse 408 has the astrologer say that the marriage will bring the king both rows of the ridge; verse 548 tells that the silver mountain was first given to Nami, a man of Kasi.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The ridge the astrologer promises is shown to have begun as a gift to a man, which supports Marusi's claim that the Vidyadharas are men.","mm":1},{"a":"so:549","b":"n:195","type":"tension","note":"Verse 549 judges the match fit because both houses have high lineage and wealth; Naladiyar 195 says high and low caste are only words and that austerity, learning and effort, not famous wealth, make a lineage.","grade":"weak","better":"qualifies","te":"The two disagree about what equality of houses rests on: the epic points to descent and riches, the quatrain to conduct and learning, and the clash holds only if lineage in 549 means inherited standing.","gn":["A test of the match by learning and conduct, which Marusi does not apply.","A case of a match argued from descent and riches, the very ground the quatrain calls empty."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:549","b":"so:427","type":"develops","note":"Verse 427 chooses Marusi to put kin and the matter in words; verse 549 is where he does so, naming the line the two houses share.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The envoy's task of setting out kin is carried out in this closing, where the shared descent from Nami is the reason offered for the marriage.","mm":1},{"a":"so:565","b":"so:108","type":"develops","note":"Verse 108 foretells a man who can speak well coming down with a letter; here the Podanam king defines such a man as the voice of the king's body.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The foretold messenger is received as a part of the other king's body, which the astrologer's foretelling alone does not say.","mm":1},{"a":"so:560","b":"so:354","type":"qualifies","note":"Verse 354 foretells that a youth who gives up rule will reach Indra's world for seventeen oceans of time; verse 560 has an ancestor who gives up rule take kevala and become rare even to the gods.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The renunciations in the story do not end alike: one ancestor goes past the gods, while the foretold youth first holds a god's seat.","mm":1},{"a":"so:560","b":"t:494","type":"tension","note":"Verse 560 makes kevala the bride whom the renouncer takes and by which he rises above the gods; Tirumanthiram 494 calls those who have left the kevala state the Vijnanars, so the same word marks the state to be left.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The same word names the goal in one text and the soul's first bound state in the other, so the two cannot be read on the same ladder.","gn":["The epic's kevala gets a rival ranking in which the same name is the bottom, which the Jain verse does not know.","The Tirumanthiram's kevala gains a case where the word is the highest prize, showing that the term's rank turns on the school."],"gr":2,"mm":1},{"a":"so:556","b":"k:268","type":"qualifies","note":"Verse 268 says all living beings worship the one who has gained his own self; verse 556 has kings bow at the feet of a man who left a throne and let creepers climb him.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"The beings who worship the self-conquered man in the story are kings and serpents together, as the couplet does not say.","gn":["The scene gets a stated reason why even kings bow, which is his gaining of himself.","The couplet gets a picture in which the worshippers are those whose rank he gave up."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:561","b":"k:388","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 388 gives the title of lord to the king who guards by justice; verse 561 claims the title by a line of kings who never drew reproach.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"A clan's boast of blamelessness is a debt to be repaid in each reign, which the couplet makes clear and the priest's speech blurs.","gn":["The priest's descent claim gets a test: the line holds only while each king keeps the guarding that the couplet names.","The couplet gets a case where the title is claimed through ancestors, and its test then falls on the heir."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:561","b":"n:195","type":"tension","note":"Verse 561 presents the king's greatness as coming from a high clan; Naladiyar 195 says good and bad caste are words and that austerity, learning and effort make a lineage, which holds if 'clan' is taken to mean birth alone.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The priest's boast of birth is checked by the poem's own ancestor, who gained his rank by renouncing, which is the test the Naladiyar sets.","gn":["The epic's clan pride gets a standard it passes only because its founder earned his place by austerity.","The quatrain gets an example where a king's lineage is named as the cause of greatness, so its claim is against a living boast."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:564","b":"k:681","type":"develops","note":"Kural 681 names birth in an honourable family among the first qualities of an envoy; verse 564 comes after the two clan recitals and praises the envoy's knowledge of clans and customs.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"Family is not a bare boast but a thing the host tests by having the envoy recite and understand it.","gn":["The recital of clans gets a reason: the envoy's family is a qualification the host checks before the message.","The couplet gets a scene where family is spoken aloud and judged against the host's own."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:566","b":"k:685","type":"develops","note":"Kural 685 asks the envoy to speak in brief, remove offence and gain the good; verse 566 has the king bid the envoy show faultless virtues in sifted words.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The host asks for the craft the couplet describes and does not simply receive it, so the envoy's skill is put on trial at the host's court.","gn":["The king's bidding gets a named standard of speech: brief, free of offence and sifted.","The couplet gets a scene where the host himself demands what it asks of the envoy."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:578","b":"so:427","type":"develops","note":"Verse 427 chooses Marusi because he can put the kin and the matter in words; here he opens his report by promising the descent, the entry and the host's words, in that order.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The ministers' reason for choosing him is tested by his first act on return, which is to set out his account in the order it happened.","gr":2,"mm":1},{"a":"so:583","b":"so:116","type":"develops","note":"Verse 116 has the ministers post Turumakanthan as guard in the garden for the day the astrologer named; Marusi recalls a bright man with a tight girdle who met him there and bade him come up.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The ministers' preparation is confirmed from the envoy's own side, which shows the plan worked.","mm":1},{"a":"so:590","b":"so:426","type":"develops","note":"Verse 426 resolves to send a messenger to Tivittan's father to settle the match; verse 590 recalls that father speaking kindly, becoming kin and giving gifts.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The embassy's aim is reached only after a silence and an objection in 588 and 589, so the match is won and not simply given.","mm":1},{"a":"so:575","b":"k:706","type":"develops","note":"The king reads the outcome of the errand from the envoy's face before he speaks, as Kural 706 says the face shows what the heart holds strongest.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The epic extends the couplet from a feeling to a piece of news, so a returning envoy's face can carry the result of a mission before his words do.","gn":["The verse lacks the reason a king can read success unspoken, which the couplet gives: the face shows what is strongest in the heart.","The couplet lacks a case where what the face shows is the end of a mission, not a mood, and this verse supplies it."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:592","b":"k:688","type":"qualifies","note":"Marusi adds, beyond his message, his own eye-witness judgement that no other king he has seen equals the host; Kural 688 asks of an envoy purity, support, resolve and truthfulness in delivering the message.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The envoy's truthfulness here covers a judgement of the other king that the message does not carry, which is more than the couplet's duty of faithful relay.","gn":["The verse lacks a standard to test the praise against: the couplet's truthfulness makes the eulogy a testimony that can be trusted or doubted.","The couplet lacks the case of an envoy who adds his own appraisal of the other king, which stretches truthfulness from relaying words to judging men."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:594","b":"k:390","type":"develops","note":"Marusi's pairs, fire in command and cool in giving, name two of the four things Kural 390 calls the light of kings: giving and a straight sceptre.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"The envoy's pairs put two marks of the Kural's ideal king into the temperature of one man, so command and giving are shown as tempers, not as separate duties.","gn":["The verse lacks the standard that makes its pairs more than ornament: the four marks the couplet calls the light of kings.","The couplet lacks a picture of how command and giving sit together in one king, which the fire and cool pairs give."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:594","b":"k:386","type":"qualifies","note":"Marusi calls the king hard for kings to reach yet easy for his own servants; Kural 386 praises a king who is easy to see.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The epic limits the couplet's easy access: it is a favour kept for a king's own men and withheld from rival kings.","gn":["The verse lacks the named virtue behind its claim, easy access, which the couplet makes a ground of a land's praise.","The couplet lacks the limit that a king may be easy to some and closed to others, which the verse shows."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:595","b":"k:544","type":"develops","note":"Marusi says the world would not be without the king's gentle grace; Kural 544 says the world clings to the feet of the king who embraces his people with the sceptre.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The envoy states the Kural's idea of the world's dependence on the king's grace as a bare condition for existence, and in the mouth of a rival's envoy.","gn":["The verse lacks a mark of how the grace works, which the couplet gives as the king first embracing his people.","The couplet lacks the strong form of the dependence, that without the king's grace there is no world, which the verse speaks."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:596","b":"k:389","type":"qualifies","note":"Marusi describes those under the king's umbrella as learned men bound by love who give their lives; Kural 389 says the world dwells under the umbrella of the king who bears bitter words.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The umbrella's shelter is filled by learned retainers who die for the king, and the couplet shows what keeps them there: a king who can bear their bitter words.","gn":["The verse lacks the quality that holds the retainers, which the couplet supplies as a king's patience with bitter words.","The couplet lacks a picture of who stands under the umbrella and what they give back, which the verse supplies."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:598","b":"k:542","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 542 has the people looking to the sceptre as the world looks to the sky; verse 598 runs it the other way, with the king glad or hurt as the world is.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The pair shows rule as a two-way dependence, where the couplet states only the people's need of the king.","gn":["The verse lacks a picture of the people's side of the bond, which the sky and sceptre image supplies.","The couplet lacks the king's felt share in his people's fortune, which the verse adds."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:599","b":"k:549","type":"develops","note":"Kural 549 says guarding and caring for the people is the king's work and no stain; verse 599 shows that work as sleeplessness.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"What the Kural calls work, the verse shows as a worry that costs a king his sleep.","gn":["The verse lacks a rule saying the guarding is his own task rather than a burden laid on him.","The couplet lacks the felt cost of that work, which the verse gives."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:599","b":"so:53","type":"tension","note":"Verse 53 shows Payapati's land troubled only by Kama's arrows; 599 holds up a king who cannot sleep for fear of failing his people.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The envoy's praise of the king's sleepless care sits beside the poet's own earlier description of him, whose days were spent among queens.","mm":1},{"a":"so:603","b":"so:407","type":"same_image","note":"In 407 the astrologer says the girl is fit to rest on the lord's breast; in 603 the envoy asks what austerity earned that seat.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"The woman on the lord's breast first comes as a forecast, then as something already earned, so the prophecy turns into a reward.","mm":1},{"a":"so:604","b":"n:101","type":"develops","note":"Naladiyar 101 says an old deed finds its doer like a calf its mother; verse 604 says merit joined this pair.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Naladiyar's law of the old deed is shown working for good here, where its examples elsewhere fall on the wicked.","gn":["The verse lacks the idea that the old deed seeks out its own doer, which gives its merit a mechanism.","The quatrain lacks a joyful instance, since its law is usually shown on sinners."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:605","b":"so:108","type":"develops","note":"Verse 108 foretold a man of good speech coming with a letter; in 605 the envoy has come and is paid for his words.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The astrologer's forecast is kept to the letter, the envoy's reward being the fruit of the arrival it named.","mm":1},{"a":"so:607","b":"so:409","type":"agrees","note":"Verse 409 has the Vidyadhara astrologer give the month's limit for the lion; 607 has a counsellor give it again to the Podanam king.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Two courts, with the same date in each, act on one word, so the lion-killing is a shared fate rather than one court's hope.","mm":1},{"a":"so:608","b":"so:427","type":"develops","note":"In 427 the Vidyadhara ministers choose the learned Marusi to carry the letter; in 608 Podanam's ministers choose a learned man to answer.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain","te":"Both courts treat the embassy as the work of learning, so the exchange between them is a matching of one practice on each side.","mm":1},{"a":"so:615","b":"k:920","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 920 puts double-minded women, liquor and dice together as the company of the man Fortune has left; verse 615 makes desire itself the liquor.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The verse folds two of the Kural's three vices into one, so the women's eyes are the cup.","gn":["The verse lacks a verdict on the man who loses day and night, which the couplet supplies.","The couplet lacks an account of how women and drink become one vice, which the verse gives."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:619","b":"so:402","type":"develops","note":"Verse 402 says Tivittan will take the life and discus of Ashvagriva; 619 shows Acuvakanthan at his ease, unaware of heat or cool.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The telling that the rival's life will end is set against a scene of his ease, so the reader looks on his pleasure knowing its end.","mm":1},{"a":"so:619","b":"n:60","type":"develops","note":"Naladiyar 60 says fools long for pleasure though suffering mounts, and the higher look to the pain in it; 619 shows a king past all noticing.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"A verse of ethics gets a king's case, where the cost is a realm and not one man's comfort.","gn":["The verse lacks the judgment that pleasure holds its own pain, which it only implies.","The quatrain lacks an example from the seat of power, where the fool's pleasure ruins others."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:619","b":"k:539","type":"develops","note":"Kural 539 bids one swollen with joy think of those ruined by negligence; verse 619 shows Acuvakanthan so swollen.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"The couplet's advice is the thing Acuvakanthan does not heed, so the story reads as its example.","gn":["The verse lacks the explicit warning that joy breeds negligence, which the couplet states.","The couplet lacks a named man to stand for the ruined, which the verse supplies."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:620","b":"k:389","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 389 says the world rests under the umbrella of a king who can bear words bitter to the ear; verse 620 names a king by the opposite temper, striking in anger and unable to bear a word against him.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"The couplet's virtue is given its negative portrait in a ruler whose pride goes with his intolerance.","gn":["The verse lacks a measure by which this temper counts as a failing, which the couplet supplies.","The couplet lacks a figure who embodies the opposite, which the verse supplies."],"gr":2,"mm":1},{"a":"so:621","b":"k:617","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 617 says the goddess of misfortune lives in sloth and the lotus goddess in the labour of the unslothful; in verse 621 the goddess of fortune takes offence and looks for another place, because the king has not come to her.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"In the Kural fortune leaves a man for sloth; here she leaves a king for his pleasure, so the fault is attention given elsewhere.","gn":["The verse lacks a rule saying what kind of man keeps fortune, which the couplet gives.","The couplet lacks a picture of fortune as a slighted lover, which the verse gives."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:624","b":"t:140","type":"qualifies","note":"Tirumanthiram 140 says the five senses come under one's control of themselves once one has met the Lord; verse 624 says a king who has conquered himself is hard for others to conquer.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The epic gives self-conquest as a ruler's own work with a public reward; the Saiva stanza makes it follow from meeting the Lord.","gn":["The verse lacks any account of how self-conquest comes about, since it names only its fruit.","The stanza lacks a worldly payoff for the mastery it describes, which the verse gives in an unconquerable realm."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:626","b":"k:385","type":"develops","note":"Kural 385 names a king as one able to create, gather, guard and apportion wealth; verse 626 gives the same four acts and says it is the learned man who becomes the protector of others.","grade":"sound","better":"qualifies","te":"The couplet's four tasks are tied to learning, so the verse makes the king's wealth-work depend on what he has studied.","gn":["The verse lacks a compact statement that these four acts define rule, which the couplet gives.","The couplet lacks the condition that only the learned can do it, which the verse adds."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:627","b":"k:431","type":"develops","note":"Kural 431 says the wealth of those without arrogance, anger and smallness has a dignified quality; verse 627 makes arrogance a frost that destroys the lotus of wealth.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet says what spoils the dignity of wealth; the verse shows how, with arrogance falling on wealth like frost on a lotus.","gn":["The verse lacks the positive side, that wealth without arrogance keeps its dignity, which the couplet gives.","The couplet lacks an image of the ruin pride causes, which the verse supplies."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:634","b":"so:627","type":"develops","note":"In 627 the astrologer warns that pride falls like frost on the lotus of wealth; in 634 the king, smiling, boasts that his shoulders have beaten the Vidyadhara kings and so he need fear no foe.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The warning is answered by the very boast it described, so the king's reply is what the astrologer's image forecasts.","mm":1},{"a":"so:635","b":"n:3","type":"qualifies","note":"Naladiyar 3 says kings who rode elephants at the head of armies fall and change when their deeds turn against them; verse 635 has a king boasting that mere men cannot shake him.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain","te":"A king's claim of strength is read as the very certainty that, in the Naladiyar, precedes the fall of such rulers.","gn":["The verse lacks a standard that sets his boast against what happens to powerful kings, which the quatrain supplies.","The quatrain lacks a living king speaking the boast it warns against, which the verse supplies."],"gr":2,"mm":1},{"a":"so:638","b":"so:402","type":"tension","note":"In 402 the lord says that because of a girl he will take the life and the discus of Ashvagriva; in 638 the king names that very discus as the power by which he could cut down the shoulders of the gods.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The weapon the king trusts is the thing the earlier verse says will be taken from him with his life.","mm":1},{"a":"so:639","b":"so:409","type":"tension","note":"Verse 409 says the sign about the garlanded one means that within a month Tivittan will split a lion's mouth; verse 639 has the king ask whether the book of omens may be a deceit.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The king treats as doubtful the omen-book that the other court has already taken as certain, which is the difference between the two courts' fates.","mm":1},{"a":"so:632","b":"so:402","type":"develops","note":"Verse 402 says the lord will take the discus and life of Ashvagriva because of a girl; verse 632 has the astrologer call the youngest Podanam prince a foe of old.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The same prince is named as the threat from the rival's court that the earlier verse named as the taker of his life.","mm":1},{"a":"so:641","b":"k:445","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 445 says counsellors are the king's eyes and he should take counsel; verse 641 has the king, who has already said he will fight regardless, tell his ministers to counsel.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"The couplet's rule is shown with its limit, as counsel asked after the king has made up his mind.","gn":["The verse lacks a rule saying why counsellors matter, which the couplet gives.","The couplet lacks the case where counsel is asked only to confirm the king's mind, which the verse shows."],"gr":2,"mm":1},{"a":"so:643","b":"k:674","type":"develops","note":"Kural 674 says what is left over of a deed or a feud scorches like a fire's remnant; verse 643 draws the same fire but warns of a foe still small.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The epic's counsellors aim the couplet's fire at an enemy not yet seen to strike, which turns a rule about unfinished business into a case for striking first.","gn":["The verse lacks the rule that an unfinished enmity is a remnant that will burn, which the couplet states as a general law.","The couplet lacks a counsellor's use of it against a foe who has done nothing yet, which the verse supplies."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:644","b":"k:879","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 879 tells the king to cut a thorn tree young lest the hand that cuts it be hurt; verse 644 adds a nail for the sprout and that axe and hatchet fail once it has grown.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The verse makes the couplet's thorn tree an argument for haste, though Arimanju's reply in 646 to 648 shows the story doubts that the tree is a thorn at all.","gn":["The verse lacks the couplet's warning that the grown tree harms the hand that cuts it, a cost to the cutter and not only to the tree.","The couplet lacks the image of tools failing, which makes the delay one of means as well as of danger."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:647","b":"k:871","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 871 says no one should want enmity even in jest; verse 647 says that making foes of the harmless and trusting the ill-hearted both burn later.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Arimanju's caution answers the counsellors' haste with the Kural's own rule against courting enmity, so the epic stages two readings of Kural 879 and 871 as rival counsel.","gn":["The verse lacks the couplet's reason that enmity has no good in it, and gives only its later pain.","The couplet lacks the second warning, against counting the ill-hearted as friends, which the verse pairs with it."],"gr":2,"mm":1},{"a":"so:663","b":"so:304","type":"develops","note":"Verse 304 says that kings of the earth, fearing the rival's anger, paid tribute and sent word; verses 663 to 665 show the same demand arriving at Podanam.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The tribute that 304 named as the rival's usual power is shown here in the act, and its list shows how much he asks of the one he treats as a vassal.","mm":1},{"a":"so:662","b":"so:514","type":"same_image","note":"Verse 514 reads the Vidyadhara king's letter to the lord of Podanam, which sends gifts and good wishes; verse 662 reads the discus-king's letter to the same lord, which demands them.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"A letter sealed and read at the same palace first carries a gift and a request and then a demand, so the same act of reading shows two different powers dealing with Podanam.","gr":2,"mm":1},{"a":"so:665","b":"k:756","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 756 counts what falls to the king, customs and what he takes from crushed foes as his wealth; verse 665 lists treasure and royal marks demanded of a king who has not been crushed.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The epic shows the third kind of the couplet's royal wealth being claimed before any war, by an overlord's letter and not by victory.","gn":["The verse lacks a rule that foe-wealth is a king's by right, which would make the demand lawful as the speaker claims.","The couplet lacks a case where such wealth is demanded by letter before any fight, and where the foe is asked to give up his own umbrella."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:668","b":"k:380","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 380 says nothing is stronger than fate, and that it arrives first even when one devises another course; verse 668 has the king put fate's power to use as the ground of giving way.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"The couplet's law of fate is turned by the king into a reason to submit, which the hero rejects in the same chapter.","gn":["The verse lacks any statement that no counter-plan can outrun fate, which would turn the king's despair from a mood into a rule.","The couplet lacks a person who reaches for its truth to excuse giving way, which shows the rule serving as an alibi."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:668","b":"t:2030","type":"develops","note":"Tirumanthiram 2030 gives fate's great strength as the sea-girt earth; verse 668 pictures that strength as fate upsetting a city and handing its wealth to others.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"A one-line claim that fate's power is the earth is shown as a scene of one city's wealth passing to another.","gn":["The verse lacks a general statement that fate's power reaches the whole earth, which would make the loss of one city an instance of that law.","The verse of the Tirumanthiram lacks an enactment, which the epic gives in a king reasoning that his own city may be taken and given."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:670","b":"k:680","type":"develops","note":"Kural 680 says those with a small domain, afraid of unrest within, bow to the great and take what they are offered; verse 670 has the king bowing on the strength of earlier kings who pleased their overlord.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The king's submission follows the couplet's prudence step by step, so the prince's later refusal is a refusal of that prudence and not of mere cowardice.","gn":["The verse lacks a stated policy that a small domain bows to the great, which gives the king's reasoning its general standing.","The couplet lacks the felt cost of such bowing, which the epic shows in the king's swallowed insult in 666 and his hiding the deal from the princes in 672."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:670","b":"n:33","type":"qualifies","note":"Naladiyar 33 says the fool grieves when the fruit of past deeds comes, while the wise take it as an old desert crossed; verse 670 accepts the fruit of deeds without grief and answers by paying.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The same calm acceptance of deeds' fruit that the quatrain offers as freedom is here the mood in which a king submits.","gn":["The verse lacks a way to meet the fruit of deeds without fretting, which the quatrain gives as passing the desert.","The quatrain lacks a case where such acceptance becomes political submission, which the king's use of it supplies."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:670","b":"so:685","type":"tension","note":"Verse 670 holds that earlier kings paid and pleased their overlord and that this is the way; verse 685 says that paying tribute would make one's own arms and sword useless.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The same demand draws the king's resigned precedent and the prince's refusal, so one court holds two ideas of what a king is for.","mm":1},{"a":"so:680","b":"so:304","type":"develops","note":"Verse 304 said that kings of the sea-girt earth, fearing Asvagriva's anger, had paid tribute and sent word; verse 680 brings the same demand to Podanam.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The earlier claim that every king pays Asvagriva becomes a case at Podanam, where it is the prince who first meets it with anger.","mm":1},{"a":"so:681","b":"so:446","type":"develops","note":"The house-priest foretold in 446 that the youth would come blazing in anger against the discus-lord; verse 681 shows the first blaze, lit by the word tribute.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The prophecy of anger is given its trigger, a single word of demand, so the fire that is foretold is also provoked.","mm":1},{"a":"so:686","b":"k:894","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 894 says a weak man who harms the strong beckons Death with his hand; verse 686 makes the demander, who counts himself strong, the fool who beckons.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"Strength is not settled by who sends the demand, and the couplet's rule can be turned against the one who thinks himself the strong party.","gn":["The verse lacks a rule that harming the stronger is a gesture that summons death, which gives the cobra image its warning.","The couplet lacks a case where the apparent strong man is the one deceived about strength, which the epic supplies."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:686","b":"pn:73","type":"same_image","note":"Purananuru 73 has the fool who ignored a king's strength as a blind man stumbling on a sleeping tiger; verse 686 has the demander as a fool reaching for the jewel in a cobra's hood.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain","te":"Both pictures make provoking the strong a blind mistake, and the epic moves the blame from stumbling to coveting.","gn":["The verse lacks the Sangam king's gentler half, that he would give even his life to men who come with courtesy, which makes the cobra's rage a reply to manner.","The poem lacks an image in which the fault is covetousness and not mere ignorance, which the jewel in the hood supplies."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:690","b":"so:304","type":"tension","note":"In 304 the kings of the sea-girt earth pay Acuvakanthan tribute for fear of his anger; in 690 the prince says tribute refused has only a trial of force to answer it.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The prince's refusal reads as a break from the fear that made every other king pay.","gr":2,"mm":1},{"a":"so:695","b":"so:690","type":"develops","note":"The minister's trick answers the prince's offer of a method against those who refuse tribute: it will enrage him and make him run to the lion.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The prince's challenge is turned into the trap that the minister builds in reply.","mm":1},{"a":"so:695","b":"k:636","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 636 says no over-subtle scheme stands before keen native wit joined with learning; verse 695 rests the scheme on the target's lacking the sense to tell good from bad.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"A scheme's success is shown to depend on how the target is judged, which the couplet alone does not say.","gn":["The verse lacks a standard by which the minister's contempt for the prince's judgement can later be tested.","The couplet lacks a schemer who bets openly on his target's inexperience."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:696","b":"so:695","type":"develops","note":"In 695 the minister says he will show a magic and set a lion on the youth; in 696 he calls Arikethu and commissions the magic lion.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The plan of 695 becomes an order, and the instrument is named.","gr":2,"mm":1},{"a":"so:703","b":"k:698","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 698 warns against belittling a ruler as young; in 703 Acuvakanthan waves off the prince as a child and the story then lets the prince take up his challenge.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The verse shows the belittling the couplet warns against and what it provokes, a prince who vows rather than withdraws.","gn":["The verse lacks a measure that names Acuvakanthan's dismissal of youth as the fault it is.","The couplet lacks a case where the belittled youth answers with a vow, not submission."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:704","b":"k:685","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 685 asks an envoy to speak so as to bring a smile and remove what would offend; here the envoys carry the king's taunt whole, because the minister of 700 wants the youth angered.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The envoys' offence is the lord's chosen means, a purpose the rule for envoys does not allow for.","gn":["The verse lacks the standard that shows the envoys' unsoftened message as a choice of policy.","The couplet lacks the case where giving offence is exactly the work the lord sends an envoy to do."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:706","b":"so:698","type":"develops","note":"In 698 the roar of the made lion fills the Himalaya and Sindhu land; in 706 the envoys, who do not say so, describe it as a lion of the gem hill.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The envoys repeat the false lion as a fact, so the reader sees the trick work on the very messengers.","mm":1},{"a":"so:707","b":"so:409","type":"develops","note":"In 409 the speaker says within a month Tivittan will split a lion's mouth; in 707 he swears to split the lion's mouth, on the minister's bait.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The enemy's trap supplies the occasion on which the earlier sign is to be met.","mm":1},{"a":"so:707","b":"so:703","type":"tension","note":"In 703 Acuvakanthan calls the prince a child; in 707 the prince says the king is a child if the lion is not killed.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"The same insult is thrown back, with the prince staking his word where the king staked nothing.","mm":1},{"a":"so:708","b":"k:471","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 471 says weigh the work, one's own strength, the foe's and the allies' before acting; in 708 and 709 the prince sends away his elephants, chariots and men and goes with no allies.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The prince's vow sets aside the allies' strength that the couplet counts, leaving the outcome to his own strength.","gn":["The verse lacks a standard that names the dismissal of the host as the risk it is.","The couplet lacks a hero who deliberately gives up the allies' strength to keep his word."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:723","b":"so:2","type":"develops","note":"The opening verse promises the tale of the red-eyed one who tore open the lion with the gaping mouth; verse 723 shows the tearing.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The poet's first promise is kept here as a deed in a scene, and the lion in the invocation is now seen as an actual foe.","mm":1},{"a":"so:723","b":"so:95","type":"same_image","note":"Verse 95 sets a fanged beast on the throne gripping an elephant calf as the king's emblem; in 723 the real lion is seized and torn by the prince.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The royal lion of the throne is an image of power, and this verse puts the prince above the beast the throne imitates.","mm":1},{"a":"so:722","b":"so:109","type":"develops","note":"In 109 those in the hall say Tivittan will guard the whole world under one umbrella; in 722 the gods tremble before the lion he is about to overcome.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"What the hall only affirmed of Tivittan's coming rule is tested here by a foe even the gods fear.","gr":2,"mm":1},{"a":"so:723","b":"t:2026","type":"same_image","note":"Tirumanthiram 2026 tells of five lions in a forest whose claws and teeth must be cut before the Lord is reached; verse 723 has a prince seize a lion by the fangs and tear it.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The same beast is mastered here by force of arm and there by a cutting of the claws, so the lion stands both for an outer foe and for an inner one.","gn":["The verse lacks any sense of the lion as something within, which Tirumular's five lions give.","The Tirumanthiram verse lacks the act itself, a seizing of the fangs and a tearing, which the epic supplies as a deed."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:724","b":"n:198","type":"qualifies","note":"Naladiyar 198 holds up the lion's strength as the measure of a man who would die poor rather than shame himself; in 724 the lion is the foe whose strength the gods marvel to see overcome.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The lion is first the model of honour and then the thing a stronger man destroys, so strength is not a single measure.","gn":["The verse lacks the use of the lion as a standard of honour, which the quatrain supplies.","The quatrain lacks a case where the lion's own strength is beaten, which the verse shows."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:726","b":"k:774","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 774 has the warrior laugh as he pulls a spear from his body; in 726 the victor of a harder fight shows neither joy nor wonder.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural's soldier takes pleasure in the fight and the epic's prince does not, so the same bravery is shown with two moods.","gn":["The verse lacks the picture of a fighter who delights in the wound, which the couplet gives.","The couplet lacks a hero who is plain after victory and calls it the removal of a harm."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:719","b":"k:382","type":"develops","note":"Kural 382 names fearlessness, giving, understanding and drive as a king's nature; in 719 the prince taunts a lion in its own cave.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet's first quality is shown here as a deed done before a foe with no help, so fearlessness is seen at its cost.","gn":["The verse lacks a general name for what the prince is doing, which the couplet gives.","The couplet lacks a test of fearlessness against a danger, which the verse supplies."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:747","b":"so:126","type":"same_image","note":"Verse 126 has women playing with youths on the Vidyadhara hill until their powder stains the rocks; verse 747 has bangled women at play and petals falling in the lion's mountain.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The poet reuses one pleasure-on-the-hill picture for two different mountains, so the lion's range is painted as a pleasure ground as much as a hunting ground.","mm":1},{"a":"so:754","b":"a:84","type":"same_image","note":"Akananuru 84 sets a rainbow on the mountain under rain clouds as the season of return; verse 754 uses Indra's broken bow among dark clouds only as a likeness for blossom.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The same bow-on-the-hill image works as a season marker in the Sangam poem and as a bare comparison here.","gn":["The verse lacks any use for the image beyond decoration; the poem shows it can mark a season and a waiting.","The poem lacks the Indra's-bow naming that ties the rainbow to a god's weapon, which the verse supplies."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:757","b":"a:38","type":"same_image","note":"Akananuru 38 has the ashoka as the lovers' old swing-tree now empty; verse 757 has the ashoka in red pollen and humming bees, likened to adorned youths.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The ashoka stands for young lovers in both, one time in their absence and one time as their likeness.","gn":["The verse lacks the ashoka's use as a trysting place, which the poem gives.","The poem lacks the ashoka's red pollen and the likeness to adorned youths, which the verse gives."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:769","b":"so:409","type":"develops","note":"In 409 the astrologer reads from the horoscope that within a month Tivittan will split a lion's mouth; in 769 the lion's blood is seen running down the hill.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The prophecy's fulfilment is shown only through its aftermath, the blood stream, so the reader sees the foretold deed by its trace.","mm":1},{"a":"so:772","b":"so:446","type":"develops","note":"In 446 the priest names the foe's destroyer as the lion-slaying man; 772 shows the deed that gives him that name.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The lion-slaying title that the priest later uses against the rival king is shown being earned here as a protective act.","mm":1},{"a":"so:772","b":"k:550","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 550 likens a king's killing of the cruel to pulling weeds from young corn; verse 772 praises a prince's killing of a beast that preyed on the herds, as a gift of safe sleep to the world.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain","te":"The epic carries the weeding rule from criminals to a beast, and gives the reason in the world's sleep, though a Jain poet still has to justify a killing.","gn":["The verse lacks a rule that makes the killing of the cruel a king's care for the many, and so answers why a Jain work may praise it.","The couplet lacks a case where the killed is a beast and the killer a prince, which tests how far the weeding image stretches."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:773","b":"k:388","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 388 makes the ruler who does justice and protects an இறை for the people; verse 773 sets the king's sons under the same duty and says it outweighs their own lives.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The duty to protect reaches the king's sons and is stated as a willingness to lose their lives, which the couplet leaves unsaid.","gn":["The verse lacks a status that rewards protection, which the couplet states as being counted an இறை.","The couplet lacks the cost of protection, the prince's risk to his life, which the verse names."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:774","b":"n:34","type":"develops","note":"Naladiyar 34 tells one to take the great fruit of a rare body while it lasts; verse 774 says the body yields no fruit if it cannot help the afflicted.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The body's fruit is named as aid to others in trouble, where the quatrain leaves what the fruit is to the word virtue.","gn":["The verse lacks the impermanence of the body, the cane refuse that makes using it now urgent.","The quatrain lacks a concrete form of virtue, the relief of the helpless, that the verse gives it."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:775","b":"t:724","type":"tension","note":"Tirumanthiram 724 keeps the body alive because true knowledge needs a living frame; verse 775 holds that a man should give even himself to end the world's pain.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"A Jain epic's praise of the body given away for others and a Saiva text's care for the body to win knowledge set out two uses of the same frame.","gn":["The verse lacks any limit on self-giving, the claim that the body must be kept for the sake of knowledge.","The mantra lacks a counter-ethic, the claim that the body may be spent for another's pain, which tests its practical argument."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:776","b":"k:235","type":"develops","note":"Kural 235 speaks of a death that is really existence, a growth through ruin; verse 776 says the flesh body goes while the form of fame stands.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The verse supplies the two-body picture that explains the couplet's death that is existence, as the fame body that survives.","gn":["The verse lacks the couplet's claim that only the skilled manage this exchange, which makes the fame body an achievement.","The couplet lacks a plain statement of what survives the death, the form of fame, that the verse gives."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:784","b":"k:1067","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 1067 begs the beggars not to beg of those who conceal; verse 784 likens a deer's thirst to a high-born man who begged from a low man's eyes and earned blame.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The verse makes the cost of begging of the wrong person a stain on rank, where the couplet gives only a warning.","gn":["The simile lacks the Kural's rule that the fault lies in begging of those who conceal, which names why the noble is blamed.","The couplet lacks the sense that the shame falls harder on the high-born, which the verse's noble adds."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:785","b":"k:742","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 742 counts a shady forest as one of a fort's four parts; verse 785 shows who holds the forest, men who live by violence and serve the king.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"A forest that guards the realm is held by violent servants, a cost the couplet's list of fort parts does not show.","gn":["The verse lacks the idea that the forest is itself a defence, which the couplet names.","The couplet lacks the picture of who actually keeps the forest, which the verse adds."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:781","b":"a:45","type":"same_image","note":"Akananuru 45 has the lover cross a summer forest with rattling dry pods and a tiger on the waterless track; verse 781 shows the same dry forest, with trees keening like a mourning house.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The same summer forest is a woman's dread in the Sangam poem and a mourning house in the epic.","gn":["The verse lacks the human reason for the forest's dread, the parted woman, which the Akananuru poem supplies.","The Akananuru poem lacks the image of the forest as a house of mourners, which the epic adds."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:781","b":"so:795","type":"qualifies","note":"Verse 781 shows the dry forest in the heat; verse 795 shows the same wooded country green and dancing with peacocks.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The two verses show that the harsh forest of the first part gives way to a country of plenty as the party travels on.","mm":1},{"a":"so:786","b":"so:800","type":"develops","note":"Verse 786 has the moon-hued prince say 'these are yours, see them'; verse 800 repeats the 'see this' as he points to the marutham water.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The pointing opened at the start of the tour is carried through the whole series up to the farm country.","mm":1},{"a":"so:821","b":"k:65","type":"develops","note":"Kural 65 says touching one's children is joy to the body; the king here holds both sons in both arms as soon as he sees them.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The king's embrace shows a father's bodily joy in his sons coming before any royal question.","gn":["The verse gives the embrace but not why it is joy, which the couplet names as the pleasure of touch.","The couplet lacks a scene of a king, who must rule, undone by the sight of his sons safe."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:825","b":"k:65","type":"develops","note":"Kural 65 also names the joy of hearing children's words; in 825 the king revives when his son tells him he went along.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The king's lifting at his son's words gives the couplet's pleasure of the ear a sudden effect on life itself.","gn":["The verse lacks a general statement that a child's speech alone can be a parent's joy.","The couplet lacks a case where those words carry news of a danger passed, so joy follows relief."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:824","b":"k:550","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 550 likens the king's removal of the cruel to weeding a crop; 824 gives a lion ruining the king's forest, removed by his sons.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"A king's clearing of harm to his land can be done by a beast-slaying prince, which widens the weeding image beyond wrongdoers.","gn":["The verse lacks any rule that makes the removal of the menace the king's own charge, which the couplet supplies.","The couplet lacks a menace that is a beast and not a criminal, and a son who acts for the king."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:828","b":"so:409","type":"develops","note":"Verse 409 reports the sign that within a month Tivittan will split a lion's mouth; verse 828 brings word that the lion has in fact been destroyed.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The prophecy is shown fulfilled by a spy's report, so it reaches the Vidyadhara king as news, not as a vision.","mm":1},{"a":"so:838","b":"k:381","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 381 counts an army first among a king's six limbs, while verse 838 raises the host as a marriage escort, in the likeness of a sea swallowing the world.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The pair shows an army used for display and escort, a use the couplet's list of royal strengths does not name.","gn":["The verse lacks the couplet's view that an army is a standing limb of rule, not raised for a single occasion.","The couplet lacks the case of a host raised for a wedding, where numbers show a king's worth to his allies."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:829","b":"k:676","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 676 says to act only after weighing the finish, the hindrance and the yield; verse 829 has the king consult his kin and then press on with the wedding.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"A king's deliberation can be a formality over a choice already made by the deed of the suitor.","gn":["The verse lacks any test of the plan's hindrances, since the lion's death has already decided the match.","The couplet lacks a case where counsel follows a settled choice instead of shaping it."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:861","b":"k:57","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 57 holds that guarding women by confinement is no use and their own steadfastness is the best guard; verse 861 sets armed women round the princess.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The pair shows the guard of arms as outward show beside the inward guard the couplet values.","gn":["The verse lacks a measure of what really guards the girl; the couplet gives one.","The couplet lacks a concrete household that does guard its women by force; the verse supplies one."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:868","b":"cc:1344","type":"same_image","note":"Cintamani 1344 has jewelled women standing guard round the bride before her wedding; verse 868 posts two thousand armed maidens round the princess.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain","te":"Both epics guard the bride with women, but this one makes the guard an armed troop of the magic-learned.","mm":1},{"a":"so:866","b":"cc:1344","type":"develops","note":"Cintamani 1344 has the astrologer fix the day; verse 866 has learned men judge the hour before the girl boards.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The pair shows the wedding hour as a learned decision in both Jain epics, with the Soolamani making it the king's cue to act.","mm":1},{"a":"so:852","b":"si:1:45-68","type":"same_image","note":"The Silappathikaram sets Kannaki's wedding under a pearl canopy on diamond pillars; verse 852 hangs gems and pearls on a hall.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The pair shows the same jewelled hall at two scales: a city wedding and a palace one dressed like the gods' own.","gn":["The Soolamani hall lacks any rite; the Silappathikaram unit gives the fire and the blessing.","The Silappathikaram canopy is only a canopy; the Soolamani hall grows into a wishing-tree."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:874","b":"k:761","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 761 calls a complete army the chief of a king's wealth; verse 874 shows the host as a spreading display round a bridal vimana.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The pair shows that the host here is wealth on show, not strength at work as the couplet rates it.","gn":["The verse lacks any test of whether the host is the king's strength; the couplet gives one.","The couplet lacks the sight of an army spent on a wedding escort; the verse gives it."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:889","b":"so:875","type":"develops","note":"Verse 875 starts the guide's naming of the lands below; 889 ends the tour at the grove where the host alights.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The tour is a framed journey that ends with the host's landing at Podanam, so the descriptions serve the arrival.","mm":1},{"a":"so:898","b":"k:381","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 381 counts the army among the six limbs of a king; verse 898 shows an army encamped with allied kings rimming the forest around the royal banner.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The camp shows the army as a ring of allies as well as a limb, which the couplet's list of six does not show.","gn":["The verse lacks a rule that makes the host a part of kingship, which the couplet gives.","The couplet lacks any picture of an army in the field with its allies pitched around the king."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:899","b":"so:900","type":"develops","note":"The maiden-city built in 899 is where the maiden lands in 900.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The city is built before she arrives, so her descent into it is a planned reception and not a stray landing.","mm":1},{"a":"so:901","b":"so:906","type":"develops","note":"Maruci's report in 901 draws the king's reply to him in 906.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The envoy's report wins a lodging and a reply, so the envoy's word is what sets the king's preparations moving.","mm":1},{"a":"so:916","b":"k:380","type":"qualifies","note":"The soldiers hold that only bound fate may take a life; Kural 380 asks what is stronger than fate.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"A warrior class that claims only fate may kill makes its own arms the servants of fate, which the couplet's praise of fate's strength never says.","gn":["The verse lacks a rule that fate outruns every other plan, which would explain why soldiers can speak of fate as the only killer.","The couplet lacks a picture of armed men who put its claim into their own battle-cry and so make Death tremble."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:920","b":"k:81","type":"qualifies","note":"The prince stocks the city's feast for guests as each desires; Kural 81 says a household exists to tend guests.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The wedding feast is a state's display and a political courtesy, which shows a limit of the couplet's household hospitality that needs no such display.","gn":["The verse lacks the moral reason that makes feasting a guest the very point of keeping wealth, which the couplet gives.","The couplet lacks a case of hospitality scaled to a royal alliance, where the feast also announces rank and ties."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:923","b":"so:902","type":"develops","note":"Verse 902 orders gate-arches, gold pots and a decorated city; verse 923 carries the order on to saffron and sandal mud, aloe smoke and gold powder.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The wedding is prepared in stages, from the frame of the city to its scent, and the second order goes beyond what the first asked.","mm":1},{"a":"so:933","b":"so:917","type":"develops","note":"In 917 the king seats Maruci on a gold-fronted elephant before him; in 933 the king himself mounts the dressed elephant.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The elephant first honours the guest's envoy and only afterwards carries the king, so the ride is a courtesy given in order of rank.","mm":1},{"a":"so:927","b":"t:2023","type":"same_image","note":"Verse 927 shows a keeper who knows the elephant's ways re-roping it and handing it to its riders; Tirumanthiram 2023 sets five rutting elephants in the body that will not come to the post of the Agamas' teaching.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The same craft of the keeper is both a working skill here and the picture of a discipline that fails without a teaching there.","gn":["The verse shows a keeper's craft but not why such taming might matter beyond the ride.","The verse names the inner elephants but has no scene of a keeper who actually succeeds with one."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:934","b":"k:389","type":"qualifies","note":"Verse 934 shows the king's white umbrella raised in the ride that cools the world; Kural 389 says the world lives under the umbrella of a king who can bear bitter words.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The umbrella cools only for a king who has the character the Kural names, which the display of the ride cannot show.","gn":["The verse lacks the condition of character that makes the umbrella a shelter.","The couplet lacks any picture of the umbrella in its pomp as the world sees it."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:934","b":"n:21","type":"qualifies","note":"Verse 934 shows a king riding an elephant under a high umbrella; Naladiyar 21 says kings who rode elephants under umbrellas like the moon over a hill have been carried off.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The same pomp that the Soolamani shows as the king's splendour is the thing the Naladiyar sets against death, so the display is also a reminder of its end.","gn":["The verse lacks any hint that the splendour of the ride is brief.","The quatrain lacks the scene whose brightness gives its warning weight."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:936","b":"n:23","type":"qualifies","note":"Verse 936 gives the roar of pipes, drums and horns at the wedding ride; Naladiyar 23 says the drums that sound for a wedding may sound the same day for the bridegroom's funeral.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The wedding's noise is the same instrument as the funeral's, so the Naladiyar turns the verse's joy into a warning without changing the sounds.","gn":["The verse lacks any thought that its music could change meaning.","The quatrain lacks the wedding's full noise that makes its reversal strong."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:941","b":"k:1254","type":"develops","note":"Verse 941 shows women along the route losing their composure at the sight of the young men; Kural 1254 says the restraint she boasted of passes beyond concealment into the open.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain","te":"The public street is the occasion where the Kural's private breaking of restraint becomes visible to all.","gn":["The verse lacks the woman's own account of the restraint she once claimed.","The couplet lacks the crowded occasion that draws desire out in public."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:943","b":"so:901","type":"tension","note":"In 901 Maruci goes to the lord of Suramai to announce the Vidyadhara's arrival; in 943 he goes the other way to tell the Vidyadhara king that the lord of Suramai is coming.","grade":"forced","better":"develops","te":"The messenger crosses between the two courts in both directions, so each side is first hosted and then hosting, and the welcome is mutual.","mm":1},{"a":"so:944","b":"k:768","type":"qualifies","note":"Verse 944 gives the host's standing by its look: elephants like hills, banners like streams, warriors like tigers; Kural 768 says an army without striking power still gains standing from its martial bearing.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"A host at a wedding is shown by its bearing alone, which is the standing the Kural says an army can have even before it fights.","gn":["The verse lacks any statement of what such bearing is worth.","The couplet lacks a picture of the bearing it names."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:949","b":"so:838","type":"same_image","note":"Verse 838 has the king raise a host like a sea swallowing the world; verse 949 shows the two hosts meeting as the Ganga and the Yamuna.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The host first likened to a sea is now the river that mingles with another, so the image turns from size to joining.","mm":1},{"a":"so:950","b":"so:873","type":"same_image","note":"Verse 873 has the host's drums and bells roar like the sea as it leaves; verse 950 turns the sea into the milk-sea of the sky.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The sea simile follows the host from departure to meeting, changing from noise to something heavenly and calm.","mm":1},{"a":"so:951","b":"so:880","type":"same_image","note":"Verse 880 has wild elephants mistake clouds for a rival elephant; verse 951 says clouds and young elephants cannot be told apart.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The mistake of cloud for elephant is the beasts' in one verse and the poet's own wit in the other.","mm":1},{"a":"so:948","b":"so:875","type":"develops","note":"Verse 875 has the host go across the sky past seas and hills; verse 948 gives the order to leave the air and walk the ground.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The sky journey ends in an order of descent, so the aerial tour serves the protocol of a meeting on earth.","mm":1},{"a":"so:958","b":"so:866","type":"develops","note":"Verse 866 has learned men fix the hour for the bride to board; verse 958 has the kings meet at the hour the astrologers named.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The same trust in the astrologers' hour governs the leaving and the meeting.","mm":1},{"a":"so:962","b":"so:845","type":"qualifies","note":"Verse 845 shows Arkakirti standing proudly on his elephant at the head of a host; verse 962 shows him folding his hands to the Podanam king.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The warlord of one scene is shown as a bowing guest in the next, so his pride is placed beneath his courtesy.","mm":1},{"a":"so:963","b":"so:845","type":"develops","note":"Verse 845 puts Arkakirti on his own elephant; verse 963 has the Podanam king raise him onto the king's own young elephant.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Arkakirti's own elephant is replaced by the host-king's, marking the passage from commander to honoured guest.","mm":1},{"a":"so:960","b":"k:680","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 680 has a small ruler bow to the great out of fear; verse 960 has the princes fold hands before the king at a meeting arranged for a wedding.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The bow of a wedding visit is shown as courtesy between equals, where the couplet sees bowing as a way of buying safety.","gn":["The verse lacks any reading of the bow as a policy, and the couplet supplies the cold case of a bow from weakness.","The couplet lacks a bow made freely by allies who gain nothing by it, which the verse supplies."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:963","b":"k:90","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 90 says a guest wilts when the host's face changes on seeing him; verse 963 has the host drink in the guest's beauty and seat him on his own elephant.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The welcome here is warmer than a kept face, since the host shares his own mount, a test the couplet does not set.","gn":["The verse lacks the couplet's test that a host's welcome is read from the face.","The couplet lacks the case of a host who answers by sharing his own seat of rank."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:972","b":"so:107","type":"develops","note":"Soolamani 107 has the astrologer read a dream as a Vidyadhara king coming to praise the lord and give a daughter; here he arrives at Podanam and says he has come gladly to give her.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The reading of the dream is kept in the king's own gladness, so the prophecy is borne out as willing consent and not only as outcome.","mm":1},{"a":"so:986","b":"so:109","type":"develops","note":"Soolamani 109 has the court confirm that Tivittan will guard the whole world under one umbrella; here Prajapati, holding the bride, says that his earlier austerity is the reason and that the wheel is certain.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The prophecy gets a cause in Tivittan's earlier austerity, which the court's earlier confirmation lacked.","mm":1},{"a":"so:981","b":"k:1251","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 1251 has the axe of desire break the door of restraint bolted with shame; Soolamani 981 has restraint vanish because desire runs straight on, and puts it in the poet's voice about the girl.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural gives restraint a lock that desire can break, while the Jain epic says the restraint was not broken but given to the one she loves.","gn":["The verse lacks a picture of how restraint fails; the couplet gives the door, the bolt and the axe.","The couplet lacks a case where the woman's restraint is not lost to chance but handed to a particular lover, and the poet argues it a worthy gift."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:982","b":"k:1094","type":"develops","note":"Kural 1094 has the man see that when he looks she looks at the ground, and when he does not she smiles; in 982 the lad looks boldly while her modesty holds her back and she melts.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural gives the lover's inference from the lowered eye, and the epic shows the same shyness from within, as a pull she cannot hide.","gn":["The verse lacks the timing of looking and looking away that tells the watcher she is moved.","The couplet lacks the girl's inner melting and the power of her modesty over her, which the epic shows."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:990","b":"k:1125","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 1125 has the man say he would recall her only if he forgot, but he does not know how to forget; 990 has the girl say that if she tried to forget, fate itself would drive her back to remember.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"Both deny forgetting, but the Soolamani lays the compulsion on fate, which the Kural leaves to the lover's nature.","gn":["The verse lacks the compact logic that remembering needs a prior forgetting.","The couplet lacks a cause for the failure to forget; the verse supplies fate, and the girl's inward cry that the world does not know it."],"gr":2,"mm":1},{"a":"so:991","b":"k:1102","type":"tension","note":"Kural 1102 says the cure for a disease is usually other than its cause but this woman is herself the cure for the sickness she brings; Soolamani 991 says love's sickness has no cure and that looking coolly at loved ones only doubles the wound.","grade":"weak","better":"qualifies","te":"The Kural finds the remedy in union with the beloved, and the epic denies any remedy before the union.","gn":["The verse lacks the possibility of a cure in the beloved, which the couplet names.","The couplet lacks the stage before union, where the girl sees no cure and fear doubles the pain."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:987","b":"n:47","type":"tension","note":"Naladiyar 47 has the fool call a body of nine seeping openings 'broad-shouldered one, bangle-wearer' because skin dazzles his eyes; Soolamani 987 has Prajapati marvel that the Maker gathered all beauty into one girl.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The same dazzled look at a woman is a fool's error in the Naladiyar and a bride's honour in the epic, so the epic's praise stands on a view of the body the Jain text refuses.","gn":["The verse lacks the view of the body as flesh that the epic's praise leaves out.","The quatrain lacks a case where the praise of a woman's beauty is made by a worldly king and so shows what it attacks."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:980","b":"t:204","type":"tension","note":"Tirumanthiram 204 tells the heart to turn away from smiling women as the etti fruit is poison under a good look; Soolamani 980 shows a man's looks seize a girl's eyes and holds the pull as natural.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The epic tells love as a force that wins the heart, and Tirumular gives the warning that such pull is the poison.","gn":["The verse lacks any warning that the seizing of the eyes may harm.","The poem lacks a case that shows the pull working, from the woman's side."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:983","b":"so:155","type":"same_image","note":"Soolamani 155 has her modesty make a flower-vine droop in shame; here modesty is the curtain she draws round the prince she carries in her heart.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"Modesty shifts from an ornament of her beauty into the screen that hides her love.","mm":1},{"a":"so:1010","b":"so:1001","type":"develops","note":"1001 has Madhavasenai fix the bride's form in her heart and begin to paint; 1010 has her tell the queen the form was beyond any painting and that her hand was steadied by the queen's grace.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The painter's pride in the likeness turns into modesty when it is presented, so the portrait serves as praise of the bride and of the queen.","mm":1},{"a":"so:1012","b":"so:829","type":"develops","note":"In 829 the bride's father judges the girl fit only for the lion-killer and begins the wedding rites; in 1012 the groom's mother, seeing only the portrait, welcomes the match as ripened merit.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The match made by the fathers is accepted by the groom's mother through a painted likeness, so the women make the bride a member of the family before she is seen by the groom.","mm":1},{"a":"so:1015","b":"so:445","type":"develops","note":"In 445 the keeper reports the priest's word that a Vidyadhara lord would come seeking a bride for the king's son; 1015 is the girl's portrait brought to that son.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"A foretold match is finished not by the prophecy itself but by a go-between's practical acts, here a painter carrying a likeness to the groom.","mm":1},{"a":"so:1004","b":"k:1274","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 1274 says something lies within the bud of the girl's smile like scent within a bud; in 1004 the friend reads the bride's silent smile and gives it words.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The couplet's hidden meaning in a smile is shown here as read and spoken for her by a friend, which makes private signal into public courtesy.","gn":["The verse lacks the couplet's picture of a smile whose meaning is real but unseen, as scent is in a closed bud.","The couplet lacks the case where the smile is read aloud by a third person who then acts on what it says."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:1011","b":"k:685","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 685 describes the envoy who speaks briefly, removes what would offend, and brings a smile; in 1011 Madhavasenai carries the bride's greeting to the groom's mother as a go-between.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The same tact of an envoy is shown in a domestic errand between two households, where the greeting claims kinship before the wedding.","gn":["The verse lacks a stated measure of what makes a message-bearer good, which the couplet gives.","The couplet lacks a case of such tact used between a bride's house and a groom's, not between kings."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:999","b":"n:53","type":"qualifies","note":"Naladiyar 53 lists beauty among the things that fail to last, which the best renounce; 999 praises the bride's beauty as so bright she cannot be looked at, and says nothing of its end.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The poet gives full praise to beauty that his school's moral verse calls perishable, so the reader sees the praise and the warning side by side without either answering the other.","gn":["The verse lacks any limit on the beauty it praises, which the Naladiyar supplies by naming it as one of the things that go.","The quatrain lacks a concrete case of the beauty it says does not last, set in the full pride of its glow."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:1018","b":"so:1017","type":"develops","note":"Verse 1017 asks whose daughter the picture shows; 1018 answers that she is no earth-woman and so the Vidyadhara king's daughter.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The answer is reached by elimination from the picture's beauty, not from any name, so the maiden's identity rests only on how she looks.","mm":1},{"a":"so:1019","b":"so:1016","type":"develops","note":"Verse 1016 puts the portrait before the youth; 1019 shows love begin as soon as he looks, with Kaman taking aim.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The poet makes the painted likeness itself the occasion of love, so no meeting has taken place when the wound is given.","mm":1},{"a":"so:1021","b":"so:1019","type":"develops","note":"Kaman's shaft of 1019 has landed by 1021, where the youth himself lets desire swell slowly.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Love enters by a god's act but is then fed by the lover himself, so the verses show both cause and indulgence in one fall.","mm":1},{"a":"so:1035","b":"so:1020","type":"qualifies","note":"While the youth in 1020 burns for the maiden's picture, 1035 shows her elsewhere, praised by women, going to worship at dusk.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"The reader sees the youth's fire for a girl who is far away and busy with prayer, so his longing has no answer from her yet.","mm":1},{"a":"so:1038","b":"so:1037","type":"develops","note":"Verse 1037 sets the lotus drinking the feet's glow beside the worshippers; 1038 repeats the figure with bees singing.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The second verse adds the bees' sound to the lotus image, so the worshippers' bloom is also heard as a hymn.","mm":1},{"a":"so:1017","b":"k:1081","type":"qualifies","note":"Both ask whether a woman is a goddess, a rare bird or a human, but in verse 1017 the question is asked about a painted likeness and answered by reasoning.","grade":"wrong","better":null,"te":"The question that Kural 1081 puts as a lover's reeling is here a calm puzzle about a portrait, so the same three-way wonder can come from sight alone.","gn":["The verse lacks the lover's own giddy heart, which the couplet puts first and which the woman's reasoned question leaves out.","The couplet lacks any sense that the wonder can be a deliberate question put to another person about a likeness."],"gr":2,"mm":1},{"a":"so:1020","b":"k:1171","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 1171's woman blames her eyes for showing her the man; verse 1020's youth melts as his eyes take in only a picture.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Love through the eyes does not need the person present, so the eyes' guilt in the couplet is wider than a meeting.","gn":["The verse lacks the couplet's turn on the eyes themselves as the cause of sorrow, which names an instrument for the fire.","The couplet lacks any case where the eyes meet only a painted form and still bring the sickness."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:1033","b":"k:1224","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 1224's parted wife meets evening as enemies at a killing ground; verse 1033 gives a lit, scented evening that burns only those who carry desire.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The epic locates the pain in the viewer's state ('for those who have it'), while the couplet makes evening itself the attacker.","gn":["The verse lacks the couplet's starkness, in which evening is a force with an army, not a charming hour.","The couplet lacks the verse's detail of lamps, smoke and birds, which show evening pleasant in itself before it hurts."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:1028","b":"nt:69","type":"same_image","note":"Both verses show evening birds going to their nests and young while one who is away or lovesick suffers.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Pairing the two shows that the happy birds are used as the measure of the lover's loneliness in both, only here the sufferer is a youth and there it is a wife.","gn":["The verse lacks the akam frame of a wife whose husband is on his work, which Natrinai 69 supplies and which gives the birds' return a cause for grief.","The Sangam poem lacks the wide epic eye that lists the fields, the fish-carrying birds and a generous man's spear-like feeling in one evening."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:1029","b":"a:71","type":"same_image","note":"Verse 1029 compares closing lotuses to chaste women whose beloved has left; Akananuru 71 compares bees leaving pool flowers for branch flowers to graceless men.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The same evening scene casts the flower as a faithful wife and the bee as a leaving man, so each poet gives the figure a different party to blame.","gn":["The verse lacks the blame that Akananuru puts on the one who leaves, since its simile is only a praise of women's constancy.","The Sangam poem lacks the picture of the wife as the shut lotus, which turns the flower into the one who stays loyal."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:1036","b":"t:1005","type":"qualifies","note":"Verse 1036 gives a woman setting lamps, incense, flowers and music before the Jina; Tirumanthiram 1005 sets golden lamps and incense in every direction and promises release.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain","te":"A Jain maiden's temple rite and a Saiva verse use the same lamp-and-incense offering, though only the Tirumanthiram names its fruit.","gn":["The verse lacks any statement of what the offering brings, which the Saiva verse gives as release.","The Tirumanthiram lacks a person doing the rite, with her doors, garlands and strings, which the epic shows in motion."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:1024","b":"t:204","type":"tension","note":"Verse 1024 narrates the youth's heart burning for a woman's body as the natural course of love; Tirumanthiram 204 tells the heart to turn away from such burning.","grade":"forced","better":"agrees","te":"What the epic narrates as an unfolding desire, the Siddhar-side verse treats as a trap, so the two disagree about what the heart should do with beauty.","gn":["The verse lacks any judgment on the burning, which the Tirumanthiram supplies as a warning.","The Tirumanthiram lacks the felt scene of a young man wasting with desire, which shows why the warning is needed."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:1037","b":"k:3","type":"qualifies","note":"Verse 1037 shows lotuses opening in the glow of the feet and worshippers blooming at the Jina's feet; Kural 3 promises long life to those who reach the feet of the one on the flower.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"Both join feet and flowers, but the epic gives the bloom as the worshipper's own joy while the couplet gives a lasting earthly life.","gn":["The verse lacks a stated reward for reaching the feet, which the couplet names as long life.","The couplet lacks the verse's picture of the flower opening in the feet's light, which the worshipper's joy is likened to."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:1049","b":"so:1040","type":"tension","note":"In 1040 the maiden prays that the sickness of birth leave her; in 1049 a friend says the love of a united pair outweighs even the state without birth and death.","grade":"weak","better":"qualifies","te":"The same eve holds two valuations of release, a bride's prayer and a friend's boast, and the poet leaves them standing side by side.","mm":1},{"a":"so:1057","b":"so:1054","type":"same_image","note":"1054 names the wedding day as the day to enjoy good deeds; 1057 uses the passing of good deeds and the coming of evil ones as a figure for night's darkness.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The same karmic words serve first for a bride's joy and then for the dread of night, so the story's fortune is read through deeds both times.","mm":1},{"a":"so:1056","b":"so:1053","type":"develops","note":"1053 proposes to beg the moon to bring tomorrow near; 1056 shows the moon shrinking and sinking.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The moon she meant to petition simply sets, so the night she wished short is shown passing without her asking.","mm":1},{"a":"so:1061","b":"so:1050","type":"same_image","note":"1050 shows birds without mates as fire to the separated; 1061 shows paired birds sheltering mates and young as they sleep.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The birds that stand for separation in the friends' talk stand for union in the poet's closing picture, which gives the night a second reading.","mm":1},{"a":"so:1049","b":"k:1103","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 1103 asks whether the world of the lotus-eyed one can be sweeter than sleeping on the beloved's soft arms; verse 1049 sets the lovers' union above the state without birth and death.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"The Soolamani friend raises the Kural's comparison from a god's heaven to release itself, the goal her own Jain work teaches.","gn":["The verse lacks a model for weighing love against the highest good, which the Kural's open question gives.","The couplet lacks the case where the thing weighed against love is release from birth, not a heaven."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:1057","b":"n:51","type":"same_image","note":"Naladiyar 51 says that, as darkness rushes in when a lamp's oil runs out, evil takes its stand when good deeds are spent; 1057 sets the passing of good deeds beside the thickening of darkness at night.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Naladiyar teaches the lamp-and-darkness figure as a rule; the epic turns it into a description of night, which shows how common the figure was.","gn":["The verse gives the darkness-and-deeds figure without a rule, which the quatrain's lamp and oil supply.","The quatrain lacks a scene in which the darkness is a real night around a sleeping city."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:1045","b":"k:1183","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 1183 says love has taken the woman's grace and modesty and left sickness and pallor; verse 1045 shows a maiden who tries to hide her sickness and is betrayed by her pallor.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The epic shows the moment when modesty tries to hold and pallor breaks it, a step the couplet only reports after the fact.","gn":["The verse lacks a statement that pallor is the price paid for modesty, which the couplet states.","The couplet lacks a maiden before marriage who still has her modesty and is losing it."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:1052","b":"n:23","type":"qualifies","note":"Naladiyar 23 says the wedding drums of one hall can be the funeral drums for the bridegroom the same day; verse 1052 reports a drum sounding tomorrow's wedding.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"Read beside the quatrain, the drum of joy carries the warning of the same drum's other use, which the epic's happy scene does not say itself.","gn":["The verse lacks any thought that the festive drum could mean loss, which the quatrain supplies.","The quatrain lacks a drum that is only the announcement of a coming wedding, with no loss in the story."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:1068","b":"so:1070","type":"develops","note":"1068 sends the sun up to see the fire-circling; 1070 reports the rites complete.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The poet makes the sun a witness, so the unseen wedding is told through daybreak and not in itself.","mm":1},{"a":"so:1071","b":"so:1075","type":"develops","note":"1071 beats the drums for the worship day; 1075 lays out the stages of the day's procession.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The festival's religious worship and its marriage procession share one day and one set of stages.","mm":1},{"a":"so:1072","b":"so:1082","type":"same_image","note":"Both lay out the city streets full of offering and goods.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The streets are filled first with sound and incense, then with goods, building one festival picture.","mm":1},{"a":"so:1107","b":"n:23","type":"tension","note":"Naladiyar 23 sees wedding drums that may be funeral drums by evening; Soolamani 1107 ends the rites with some who leave for the forest and some who go home amazed at the wealth.","grade":"forced","better":"parallel_domain","te":"The same festival shows the transience of the day for some and wonder for others, so the Soolamani lets the Naladiyar's lesson be heard only by those who leave.","gn":["The wedding verse gains a rule of why some depart to the forest, which the verse leaves unsaid.","The quatrain gains a scene where its lesson is taken by some and missed by others, which it states only as a rule for the wise."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:1107","b":"so:1040","type":"tension","note":"1040 has the bride praying to the Jina to lose the disease of birth; 1107 ends her wedding with some going to the forest and others returning to enjoy.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The wedding that the maiden began by praying to be freed of birth closes on the same two answers, release or return.","gr":2,"mm":1},{"a":"so:1101","b":"s:147","type":"qualifies","note":"1101 shows the priest's fire fed while the groom's desire is kindled; Siddhar 147 asks when he will kindle the homa fire inside to cross the sea of desire.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"One fire serves desire on one side and ends desire on the other, so the rite is the same fire put to opposite uses.","gn":["The wedding fire gains a rival use, as a fire inside the body that ends the desire it kindles in the groom.","The Siddhar's inner homa gains the outward rite it turns inward, with a priest's actual oblation set beside the longing."],"gr":2,"mm":1},{"a":"so:1103","b":"k:1011","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 1011 says true shame is shame at wrongdoing and anything else is only the shame of women; 1103 gives the bride's trembling shyness at the first clasp.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The bride's shyness is the kind the Kural calls a woman's own and not yet the shame of deeds, so the verse shows what the couplet sets aside.","gn":["The bride's trembling gains a name for what it is, the shame of the bright-browed that the Kural separates from moral shame.","The couplet gains a living case of the shame it sets aside, which it names without showing."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:1099","b":"n:56","type":"develops","note":"1099 has the hill-king give the bride with water before the fire as god; Naladiyar 56 says marriage is called கடி because it binds the husband even to a faulty wife.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The giving of the bride before the fire is the act that creates the hardship of binding which Naladiyar names, so the rite explains why the husband cannot put her away.","gn":["The gift before the fire gains a consequence, the lasting duty it puts on the groom.","The quatrain gains the actual rite of water and fire that makes the binding."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:1106","b":"so:1103","type":"develops","note":"1103 shows the bride's shyness at the hand-clasp; 1106 has her scorn herself as shameless when shown the star.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"Her shyness grows from trembling in the body to a spoken reproach, so the poet follows one feeling across the rite.","mm":1},{"a":"so:1091","b":"so:1088","type":"develops","note":"1088 bathes the groom with water poured from pots; 1091 bathes the bride with mountain water and flowers.","grade":"wrong","better":null,"te":"Both are bathed before the fire but she is bathed by the Vidyadharas from above and he by kings, so the two sides bring different guests.","mm":1},{"a":"so:1102","b":"so:1099","type":"develops","note":"1099 has the father give the bride with water; 1102 has the groom lead her round the fire holding her fingers.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The gift only becomes a marriage when the groom takes her hand and walks the fire, so the two verses give the two halves of the one act.","mm":1},{"a":"so:1105","b":"so:1104","type":"develops","note":"1104 has both families pour gold and gems on the priests; 1105 names the learned chief priest who gets the greatest rain of wealth.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The gift is shared out by the priest's learning, so the wealth follows the Veda and not the families.","mm":1},{"a":"so:1111","b":"so:1122","type":"qualifies","note":"1111 shows her shame breaking like a cage; 1122 shows her still shy and stranger-like in his lap.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The bride's shame is not undone in one embrace, and the groom still feels the distance it leaves after the cage is said to be broken.","mm":1},{"a":"so:1112","b":"k:1122","type":"develops","note":"1112 makes two bodies one heart by ripening; Kural 1122 says the bond is as body is to life.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The epic gives the oneness of lovers a history of slow ripening, while the couplet states only the result as a bond of body and life.","gn":["The epic verse gets a measure of how total the oneness is, as between body and life, which its bud-and-fruit figure does not give.","The couplet gets a story in which the bond grows from bud to fruit instead of being given."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:1111","b":"k:1134","type":"qualifies","note":"1111 has the groom's ardour carry her shame off; Kural 1134 has the flood of love carry off the raft of shame and manliness.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The same flood that costs the secret lover his standing in the Kural is blessed here, because the epic's shame falls away inside a wedding.","gn":["The epic verse gets the Kural's naming of shame as something love sweeps away, and so as a loss, which its cage image hides.","The couplet gets a case where losing shame costs nothing, showing the loss it names depends on love outside marriage."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:1113","b":"k:1109","type":"qualifies","note":"1113 mixes union, delight and sulking in one daze; Kural 1109 lists sulking, making up and union as love's fruits in order.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"The epic blends what the couplet puts in a sequence, so the sulk is a flavour inside union and not a stage before it.","gn":["The epic verse gets the Kural's claim that the sulk is itself a reward of love, which explains why it sits in a list of pleasures.","The couplet gets a scene where the three do not follow each other but occur together, without any making up needed."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:1113","b":"t:1960","type":"qualifies","note":"1113 lets the pair dissolve in a daze of union; Tirumanthiram 1960 teaches union in which the bindu is kept and infatuation dies.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The epic gives the surrender that Tirumular's discipline of union is set against, and the Saiva verse asks what the daze costs.","gn":["The epic verse gets a question it never asks, whether the daze of union is a loss, and a measure by which a yogi would judge it.","The Tirumanthiram verse gets the householder's full surrender as the ordinary case its restraint is defined against."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:1117","b":"so:986","type":"develops","note":"986 says whoever wins the bride must have great earlier austerity; 1117 has the groom claim he did austerity to touch her.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The groom's claim fulfils the father's prediction and turns the language of penance from release to desire.","mm":1},{"a":"so:1117","b":"so:1022","type":"develops","note":"1022 has the youth fear her glance as an arrow that will kill him; 1117 takes her eyes as the arrows and his austerity as their reward.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The same arrow that the fear of the picture-scene dreads is accepted as the prize of the wedding night.","mm":1},{"a":"so:1118","b":"k:1102","type":"qualifies","note":"1118 makes the girl both his life and his rare medicine and him her servant; Kural 1102 says she is the cure for the sickness she brings.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The cure is a bondage in the epic, since the groom who is healed is enslaved by what heals him.","gn":["The epic verse gets the argument behind its list of titles, that a cure ordinarily differs from its cause but here is the same person.","The couplet gets a sequel in which the cure becomes servitude, which its single line leaves open."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:1119","b":"so:1037","type":"same_image","note":"1037 compares the lotus and the worshippers of the Jina's feet to the maiden's red feet; 1119 has the groom call worship of those feet enough.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The poet moves the worship of feet from the Jina to the bride through the groom's mouth and so shows love borrowing a devotional form.","mm":1},{"a":"so:1122","b":"k:1129","type":"same_image","note":"1122 has him gaze unblinking so as not to lose her; Kural 1129 has her not blink lest he be hidden.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The refusal to blink is common to both lovers, but the epic's reason is that she seems to recede each time he blinks, and the Kural's is that he lives in her eye.","gn":["The epic verse gets proof that not blinking is a common sign of love, said by the woman in the Kural and by the man here.","The couplet gets the groom's reverse case, where the fear is that she herself becomes a stranger at each blink."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:1127","b":"so:986","type":"develops","note":"986 says the winner of the bride will surely rule the wheel; 1127 calls her the great queen of the wheel-ruler.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The singers speak as if the father's prophecy were already true, so the wedding is also the first coronation of her place.","mm":1},{"a":"so:1128","b":"k:1111","type":"qualifies","note":"1128 warns the kurava tree not to grow as fragrant as the bride, lest it be plucked; Kural 1111 greets the anicham flower and says the beloved is more delicate.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"Praising a girl by comparing her to a flower carries a threat in the epic, where equalling her gets the flower plucked, and only flattery in the couplet.","gn":["The epic verse gets the Kural's form of speaking to a flower, so that its warning can be heard as a courtly praise.","The couplet gets a darker side to the comparison, since a flower that equals her is in danger."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:1128","b":"so:1129","type":"develops","note":"1128 closes the songs' warnings about being plucked; 1129 shows the bride's smile after hearing them.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The songs tease the groom as the one who plucks, and the smile is the only answer given.","mm":1},{"a":"so:1108","b":"so:1130","type":"same_image","note":"1108 has him climb the tall mansion with the maid; 1130 has him climb the cloud-high mansion again after the grove.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The two climbs enclose the grove as an interlude, so the chapter starts and ends on the mansion where the wedding night is held.","mm":1},{"a":"so:1135","b":"so:707","type":"develops","note":"In 707 the prince swears to split the lion's mouth; in 1135 the spy tells that the younger bull has split it and the sky-dwellers shake.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The vow's keeping is told from the enemy's side, where it is felt as terror rather than triumph.","mm":1},{"a":"so:1141","b":"so:704","type":"tension","note":"In 704 the envoys carry Acuvakandan's word that the little one cannot kill the lion if he knows his strength; in 1141 he says men's strength is only fear's exaggeration.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"The king's contempt for the prince is stated twice, once before the lion's death and once after, and the death in between has not moved it.","mm":1},{"a":"so:1147","b":"so:681","type":"same_image","note":"In 681 the prince's long-held anger kindles like a fire at the word tribute; in 1147 fire is born in Acuvakandan's eyes at the news of the marriage.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The two rivals are drawn with the same image of rage as fire, so the coming war reads as two fires meeting.","mm":1},{"a":"so:1155","b":"so:770","type":"same_image","note":"In 770 a speaker says the lion was beyond even the gods and only Tivittan could destroy it; in 1155 the kings say no god or man can match Acuvakandan.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Each side's champion is raised above the gods in speech, and the story means to test the two claims against one another.","mm":1},{"a":"so:1149","b":"so:620","type":"qualifies","note":"In 620 the poet says the king destroys in anger without weighing a matter; in 1149 he says he will gather his people and then decide.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The king who was drawn as acting without thought now says he will consult, but only after his anger has already taken form.","mm":1},{"a":"so:1146","b":"so:306","type":"develops","note":"In 306 the counsellor argues that the girl is fit for Asvagriva, whose mind is set and whom the Vidyadharas fear; in 1146 the girl has gone down to earth as wed to another.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The rejected suit explains why a marriage far away becomes a cause of war against the whole discus-lord's host.","mm":1},{"a":"so:1151","b":"so:1139","type":"develops","note":"In 1139 Acuvakandan shatters a diamond pillar with a blow; in 1151 the arriving kings see it lying burned and tremble.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The broken pillar is left standing as the proof of rage that the summoned kings read before he speaks.","mm":1},{"a":"so:1136","b":"so:695","type":"develops","note":"In 695 the minister plans to enrage the prince into running into a lion; in 1136 the same minister hides the lion's death and has the cunning lion himself tell it.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The minister who used the king's pride against the prince now shelters the king from news that wounds it.","mm":1},{"a":"so:1143","b":"so:723","type":"develops","note":"In 723 the prince seizes the lion by its fangs and tears it to strips; in 1143 Arikethu, who was set to play the lion at 697, marvels at that tearing.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The one man in the rival court who has played the lion's part is the first to admire the youth who killed the real one.","mm":1},{"a":"so:1139","b":"k:307","type":"qualifies","note":"1139 shows a king's blow breaking a diamond pillar; the couplet says the ruin of one who prizes anger is sure as a hand that strikes the ground.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The blow succeeds first, so the couplet's ruin is shown as not yet in sight and the story has to come back to it.","gn":["the epic's king strikes stone and the stone breaks, which shows how anger can seem to succeed before any cost appears","the couplet's ground that never misses gets a case where the blow visibly lands and the ruin it promises is still withheld"],"mm":1},{"a":"so:1136","b":"k:389","type":"develops","note":"1136 shows a minister hiding the lion's death for days; the couplet says the world rests under a king who can bear a bitter word.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The cost of a king who cannot bear bitter words falls first on his ministers, who delay the news and arrange who tells it.","gn":["the epic gives a concrete delay and a go-between, where its king has only been called proud","the couplet gets a named cost of an intolerant king, the stage-managed late report, which it states only as the world's shelter lost"],"gr":2,"mm":1},{"a":"so:1144","b":"k:691","type":"develops","note":"1144 shows Arikethu holding back his own view and speaking courtesies at the angry king's feet; the couplet says to stay neither far from nor near a quarrelsome king, as with a fire.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"A courtier's distance from an angry king can be a matter of what he withholds, not where he stands.","gn":["the epic shows how a counsellor keeps from the fire by silence and flattery while holding his own admiration of the enemy","the couplet's fire is made literal in a king whose eyes spit flame, and its measure of distance is shown to be one of speech"],"mm":1},{"a":"so:1149","b":"k:568","type":"qualifies","note":"1149 has Acuvakandan rage and boast, then say he will gather his people before deciding; the couplet faults a king who does not think with his circle and then lashes out.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"Consulting after the rage has set may offer none of the couplet's protection, because the order of anger and counsel is reversed.","gn":["the epic's king says he will consult yet has already boasted and resolved, so counsel is shown arriving after the anger","the couplet gets a case where consulting follows the outburst, which it does not consider"],"mm":1},{"a":"so:1153","b":"n:21","type":"qualifies","note":"In 1153 the kings wonder whether the king rages at Death or at those who rule through the ages; Naladiyar 21 says even kings under umbrellas on elephants are carried off as dead.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The courtiers imagine his anger reaching Death himself, the very pride the quatrain says Death gathers in.","gn":["the epic's courtiers show how far a king's pride can be imagined to extend, to Death himself","the quatrain's anonymous fallen kings get a living counterpart whose court takes him to be above their end"],"mm":1},{"a":"so:1148","b":"t:1686","type":"develops","note":"In 1148 Acuvakandan boasts of turning mountains and rolling the world; Tirumanthiram 1686 lists burning with anger and boasting of greatness among what is not the nature of one near the Lord.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The Saiva list names as marks of the unripe what the epic's rival king shows as his great-hearted rage.","gn":["the epic's boast is classed by a Saiva text among the faults that mark a man far from the Lord, a reading the Jain narrator never states","the list of faults gets a king in whom anger, scheming and boasting of greatness all appear together"],"mm":1},{"a":"so:1156","b":"so:1153","type":"develops","note":"1153 asks what is the root of the king's anger; 1156 has the people stop guessing and fall silent.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The frightened crowd moves from asking the cause to waiting without words, so the dread grows as the cause stays unknown.","mm":1},{"a":"so:1158","b":"so:1155","type":"develops","note":"1155 asks who would anger a king no demon can match; 1158 gives the cause, a rumoured pledge of a daughter to a human.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The court's puzzle in 1155 is answered by a rumour the king repeats as fact.","mm":1},{"a":"so:1172","b":"so:1181","type":"develops","note":"1172 warns that a great king should not march against lowly foes; 1181 has the king ask what kind of war this is.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The king answers the warning by asking whether this war is human, divine or both.","mm":1},{"a":"so:1157","b":"s:2055","type":"same_image","note":"The king is lord of a rutting war elephant and in rage; the Siddhar song kills anger as a mad elephant's rut with the goad of reasoning.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"Anger as a rutting elephant is a royal fact in the epic and a figure in the Siddhar, which shows what the goad of reason would have to stop.","gn":["The epic's king has a real elephant and a real rage but nothing that checks the rage, which the Siddhar's goad of reason supplies.","The Siddhar's elephant of anger lacks a king who actually rides one, which the epic gives."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:1160","b":"s:883","type":"qualifies","note":"The soldiers eager to carry arms and win; the Siddhar says horses, elephants and infantry shine under the parasol and are nothing without the ash.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The soldiers' pride in their strong shoulders is set beside a verse that says armies are worth nothing without the right mark, so the epic's boasts are weighed against a Siddhar's measure.","gn":["The soldiers' boasts lack any measure of what an army is worth beyond winning, which the Siddhar sets in grace.","The Siddhar's list of horses, elephants and chariots lacks men who speak, which the epic gives by letting soldiers boast."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:1165","b":"k:306","type":"tension","note":"1165 calls anger a fire that gives birth to pride and a sword thirsty for blood; Kural 306 calls anger a fire that burns even one's own safe raft of kin.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The warrior prizes anger as the fuel of his sword, while the Kural counts the same anger as what burns one's own people.","gn":["The warrior's praise of anger names no cost, which the Kural states as the burning of one's own kin.","The Kural's rule has no one who values anger as martial fuel, which the epic supplies by letting a warrior praise it."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:1165","b":"t:266","type":"qualifies","note":"1165 makes pride the child of anger's fire; Tirumanthiram 266 says the swollen-headed die without support, fallen into withering anger.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"Pride that grows from anger in the epic is shown by Tirumular to end in a death without any support.","gn":["The warrior's anger-born pride has no account of its end, which Tirumular gives as dying unsupported.","Tirumular's proud man has no face or voice, which the epic gives in a swordsman boasting of his anger."],"gr":2,"mm":1},{"a":"so:1169","b":"k:500","type":"tension","note":"1169 says a lowly jackal rising against lions would only be laughed at; Kural 500 says a jackal can kill an unflinching elephant in mud where its feet sink.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The speaker assumes the lowly cannot beat the great, while the Kural says the ground can let a jackal beat an elephant.","gn":["The speaker's scorn for the jackal ignores the place of battle, which the Kural makes the deciding thing.","The Kural's jackal and elephant have no council that dismisses the jackal, which the epic supplies."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:1173","b":"n:68","type":"qualifies","note":"1173 has the king's anger die at his kin's words; Naladiyar 68 says the worthy's anger cools of itself like boiled water.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The king's anger dies at others' words rather than by itself, so the epic shows a cooling the quatrain says only the worthy manage unaided.","gn":["The epic king's anger cools only after his kin speak, which the quatrain says the worthy do alone.","The quatrain's worthy cooling has no scene of a king and his kin, which the epic gives."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:1174","b":"k:568","type":"develops","note":"1174 has the king thunder that he is faultless and then go to the council hall; Kural 568 says a king who lashes out in anger without thinking with his circle loses fortune.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The king rages first and consults after, so the epic shows the order that the Kural warns against, though it ends in council.","gn":["The thundering king in 1174 shows no sign that the council will check him, which the Kural gives as the test of a king's anger.","The Kural's circle that must be consulted is only a principle, and the epic shows a king who enters the hall after his rage has begun."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:1175","b":"k:445","type":"develops","note":"1175 has the minister Arimanju ask the angry king what he now thinks; Kural 445 says a king must take counsel as the counsellors are his eyes.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The minister's question is the counsel the Kural asks of a king, asked after the king has already raged.","gn":["The minister's question is put to a king already set on war, which tests whether counsel can still act as the king's eyes.","The Kural's counsellor as the king's eyes has no scene, and the epic gives a minister putting the first question."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:1179","b":"k:546","type":"tension","note":"1179 says a king's justice is to prefer scheming to fighting and see what can be got; Kural 546 says victory comes not from the spear but from the unbent sceptre.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The counsellor rests victory on scheming and the Kural on the king's justice, so the two name different grounds for winning.","gn":["The counsellor's rule asks only what can be gained, which the Kural answers with the question of whether the king's sceptre is just.","The Kural's sceptre has no scheming rival, which the epic supplies in a council voice that puts craft first."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:1181","b":"so:1200","type":"develops","note":"1181 asks which kind of war to fight; 1200 has the prince take the war upon himself.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The council's question about the kind of war is answered when the prince claims the fight as his own, after the lords' boasts.","mm":1},{"a":"so:1183","b":"so:1185","type":"develops","note":"1183 sorts war into human, divine and mixed; 1185 lets the lecture go and settles on men against men.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The long taxonomy of war is dropped for a single practical choice, so the lecture serves the decision.","mm":1},{"a":"so:1193","b":"so:1199","type":"same_image","note":"Both make a weapon a feeder: the sword drinks demons' blood to still its hunger, the plough turns blood into a sown field.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"Two boasts turn the same thing, blood, from a loss into food or seed, which shows how the council's talk makes killing a work of cultivation.","gr":2,"mm":1},{"a":"so:1204","b":"so:1205","type":"develops","note":"1204 puts the ultimatum; 1205 shows the prince's heart catching fire at it.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The envoy's two options provoke an anger the verse shows as fire, so the ultimatum cannot be accepted.","mm":1},{"a":"so:1181","b":"k:471","type":"develops","note":"The king asks which kind of war to fight; the couplet says weigh the work, one's own strength, the foe's and the allies' before acting.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural states the weighing as a rule; the epic shows a council doing it by splitting war into human, divine and mixed kinds.","gn":["The council's question gains a stated rule behind its caution: the choice of war is a weighing and not a taste.","The couplet gains a concrete case where the foe's strength includes divine arts, which its list of four strengths does not foresee."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:1188","b":"k:583","type":"develops","note":"The king gathers allies after the spy's report; the couplet says a king who does not gather through spies and judge has no victory ready.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The epic shows a spy's report prompting a council, which is what the couplet calls the ground of victory.","gn":["The scene gains a reason why the spy's word is repeated before the whole council: the report is the first step in the war.","The couplet gains the next step it leaves out, what the ruler does with the report once he has it: he convenes allies."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:1186","b":"pn:9","type":"qualifies","note":"A war of men without trickery is proposed; the Pandya Kudumi proclaims dharma to the weak before battle.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain","te":"Both limit war, one by limiting means, the other by sparing those who cannot fight.","gn":["The proposal gains a second sort of limit, protection of non-combatants, which it does not name.","The poem gains a limit on means, no trickery between combatants, beside its limit on who may be harmed."],"gr":2,"mm":1},{"a":"so:1196","b":"k:969","type":"qualifies","note":"Arkakirti's bowstring is named for shame (நாண்) and he prizes the bow above life; the couplet says the honourable give up life when honour is lost.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The epic gives the hero's honour a physical thing, the string, that the couplet only states as a rule.","gn":["The verse gains a statement of the stake: shame at disgrace outweighs life, with the bowstring as its image.","The couplet gains an image of honour as something one wears and draws, the warrior's bowstring, instead of the yak's hair."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:1197","b":"k:305","type":"tension","note":"Arkakirti's wrath rains arrows and blood; the couplet says to guard against anger, which kills the one who does not.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"A speech that glories in wrath as war's fuel meets a rule that says it destroys its own bearer.","gn":["The boast gains a warning it does not hear: the wrath that drives the arrows can also destroy the archer.","The couplet gains a counter-case where anger is praised as a duty, so its advice is tested against the warrior's ethic."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:1204","b":"k:685","type":"qualifies","note":"The envoys give an ultimatum with no softening; the couplet says an envoy speaks briefly, removes offence and gets the good done.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The envoys act as the opposite of the couplet's envoy, brief but offering only two harsh options, and it shows the rule's cost.","gn":["The verse gains a measure by which the envoys' manner reads as a failure of diplomacy, not strength.","The couplet gains an example where the envoy's brevity serves the sender's pride and brings about war instead of a good result."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:1206","b":"so:681","type":"develops","note":"681 lights Tivittan with anger on hearing the word tribute; 1206 to 1207 show the same anger spreading through his body at the envoys' second round of words.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The poet lets the first anger be inward at the demand and the second visible at the insult, so the hero grows from controlled to burning as the embassies go on.","mm":1},{"a":"so:1210","b":"so:690","type":"develops","note":"690 sends the envoys back with a defiant word; 1210 again lets them go unharmed, now by naming the custom that protects envoys.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The hero's first dismissal is only defiance; the second gives the reason, that the envoy is not the one at fault, so he is shown as a lawful and not merely angry fighter.","mm":1},{"a":"so:1216","b":"so:402","type":"develops","note":"402 foretells that the Kesava will take the discus and the life of Asvagriva; 1216 sets a divine word that this youth will win and reign.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The prophecy of the lord's word is echoed as a divine pledge at the moment of the first war-answer, so the battle is told as the keeping of an old promise.","mm":1},{"a":"so:1225","b":"so:686","type":"same_image","note":"686 calls the tribute-seeker a fool who would take the jewel from a venomous cobra's hood; 1225 gives the cobra's venom to the foe's laugh.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The cobra moves from the hero's image of the danger the foe courts to the foe's own laughing venom, so the image of the poisonous snake cuts both ways.","mm":1},{"a":"so:1215","b":"so:1224","type":"develops","note":"1215 asks whether lives or honour weigh more with the enemy kings; 1224 answers that they keep pride though they feel ruin near.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The ultimatum's question is answered by the kings' choice, which shows honour overriding fear of death in those the poet says were told to bow.","mm":1},{"a":"so:1210","b":"k:685","type":"qualifies","note":"1210 sends away envoys who brought evil words that burned the ears; Kural 685 says an envoy should remove what would offend.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The envoys fail the Kural's standard of speech and are still protected, so the immunity of an envoy is shown to stand apart from how well he speaks.","gn":["The epic scene gains a measure by which the envoys' evil words count as a failure of the office, not just of manners.","The couplet gains the case of an envoy who offends and yet may not be harmed, which the couplet's ideal of inoffensive speech does not reach."],"gr":2,"mm":1},{"a":"so:1224","b":"k:969","type":"qualifies","note":"1224 shows enemy kings holding to pride though they know it brings ruin; Kural 969 praises those who give up life when honour is at stake.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet's honour is tested on the wrong side of a war the poem says is already decided, so pride that kills is shown as heroic and also as futile.","gn":["The epic verse gains a named value, honour as a reason to die, for conduct it only calls rage.","The couplet gains a case where those who would die for honour serve an unjust demand, which the couplet's praise does not distinguish."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:1207","b":"pn:72","type":"qualifies","note":"1207 shows the hero's anger at petty words in his body; Purananuru 72 turns Neduncheliyan's rage at kings who mocked him as young into a vow with named forfeits.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The Sangam king binds himself in public speech to avenge a taunt, while the epic hero is let burn first and be confirmed by gods afterwards, so anger at a taunt is lawful in both but sanctioned differently.","gn":["The epic hero's anger gains a Sangam precedent of a king answering mockery of his youth, which marks it as a king's duty and not temper.","The Sangam vow gains a view of the same rage seen from the body and the heavens, and of a hero left to stand on others' pledge, which the vow has not."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:1243","b":"so:1246","type":"tension","note":"1243 has the Vidyadharas' arrows darken the sky; 1246 has the hero's arrows rise, strike their eyes and darken it again.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The darkness changes sides inside four verses, so the same arrow-shadow that shields the attackers blinds them.","mm":1},{"a":"so:1251","b":"so:1255","type":"develops","note":"1251 reports the broken army to the proud king; 1255 gives the king's answer, a laugh.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The king's laugh meets news of ruin with a boast, making defeat into proof of the foe's single-handed skill.","mm":1},{"a":"so:1250","b":"k:764","type":"qualifies","note":"The Kural names the army that does not collapse; 1250 shows drums split, crowns sunk and chariots overturned in a collapsing army.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"A rule that an army must not collapse is tested against the detail of what a collapse looks like in a field.","gn":["A standard to measure the broken host against, so its rout reads as failure of what an army must be.","A worked case of collapse, which the definition names but never pictures."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:1247","b":"k:775","type":"qualifies","note":"The Kural calls an eye that blinks at a flung spear flight; 1247 has whole ranks blind with arrows, puzzled, hiding and falling.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural counts flinching as flight, but 1247 shows troops struck blind by a dark of arrows, where fear and confusion are not the same.","gn":["A name for the fear it shows, making the rout a failing of courage and not only of fortune.","A case where the eye cannot see, which tests whether blinking is always cowardice."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:1255","b":"k:774","type":"qualifies","note":"The Kural's warrior laughs as he pulls a spear from his own body; 1255 has a king laugh at his army's rout.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The laugh of a warrior pulling a spear from his body is a private triumph, while a king's laugh at a lost army is a boast over others' loss.","gn":["A measure of what the king's laugh costs against the warrior who laughs at his own wound.","A contrasting laugh, from a leader and not the wounded fighter, which tests how far the Kural's praise reaches."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:1233","b":"pn:17","type":"same_image","note":"1233 sets cloud against cloud and sea against sea as two hosts meet; Purananuru 17 sees the Chera king's host as an army like the sea.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The sea-army that praise sets at rest as a king's glory here meets another sea, and the figure turns from praise into clash.","gn":["A source for the sea-army figure, here shown as one sea among two.","A second sea set against the king's, so the image of an unopposed host meets its match."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:1259","b":"so:1266","type":"develops","note":"1259 pitches the camp on Arungalam hill; 1266 sets the two armies against one another.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The poet readies the field in one verse and the clash in the next, so the camp's choice of ground leads straight into the fight.","gr":2,"mm":1},{"a":"so:1261","b":"so:1265","type":"develops","note":"1261 has the eldest brother vow vengeance; 1265 has the lord, cooled by the brothers' words, send them to fight.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The brothers' vow of vengeance is turned into their lord's permission and a goal of making their names stand.","mm":1},{"a":"so:1262","b":"so:1270","type":"qualifies","note":"1262 has a brother laugh at the humans; 1270 shows the warriors as too great for the world.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"The laughing Vidyadharas misjudge their foe, since the poet next shows those warriors as towering figures.","mm":1},{"a":"so:1280","b":"k:775","type":"qualifies","note":"1280 has victors decline to strike the blinker or shame the fled; Kural 775 calls a blink at a spear flight.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The epic shows the winners declining to treat a blink as flight, a gentler ending than the couplet's flat verdict.","gn":["The epic's chivalry is measured against the couplet's strict rule that a blink is already defeat.","The couplet's reflex of valour is shown with a winner's answer, mercy to the one who flinches, which it alone never gives."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:1279","b":"k:765","type":"develops","note":"1279 has warriors who do not fear Death send the fearful away; Kural 765 calls an army one that stands against raging Death.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The epic turns the couplet's test of an army into a selection, since warriors themselves dismiss those who fear.","gn":["The warriors' call gets a standard for whom to keep, since the couplet names fearlessness before Death as what makes an army.","The couplet's standing together is given a speaking crowd who sort themselves, which the couplet itself does not show."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:1256","b":"k:473","type":"qualifies","note":"1256 closes that no one finishes an aim except by hard ripening after kings flee; Kural 473 says many who rose on enthusiasm broke midway.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The epic offers no cause for the kings' failure, while the couplet blames ignorance of one's own strength.","gn":["The generalising closing lines get a stated cause of ruin, rising without knowing one's strength.","The couplet's warning gets a scene of kings who marched and fled, which makes the broken rise concrete."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:1283","b":"so:1294","type":"tension","note":"1283 has Arisenan boast that none can stand against his sword; 1294 has a younger youth cut him in two as he draws back.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"The poet puts the boast and the death a few verses apart, so a name proclaimed before the fight is shown to guarantee nothing in it.","mm":1},{"a":"so:1297","b":"so:1304","type":"tension","note":"1297 shows Gunasenan roaming like a rutting elephant and tiger; 1304 opens on his falling.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"The praise of Gunasenan's strength is cancelled by his own fall, which turns the new champion's boast into the next test.","mm":1},{"a":"so:1305","b":"so:1306","type":"develops","note":"1305 brings Kamukan, learned in all arts, with bow and arrow; 1306 shows him ward off the volley and shoot his foe.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The note that he had learned all the arts is made good in the next verse, so the learning is shown as a fighter's skill.","mm":1},{"a":"so:1283","b":"k:777","type":"qualifies","note":"Arisenan names himself and offers the foe the choice to flee; Kural 777 calls the war-anklet an adornment for those who value fame above life.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The epic shows the cost of the bold stance the couplet praises: the one who proclaims it is among the first to fall in 1294.","gn":["The boast of 1283 is read against a standard of honour, so the proclamation becomes a claim to fame and not mere bravado.","The couplet, which gives no outcome for the bold man, is given a case where the fame-seeker is cut down at once."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:1305","b":"n:386","type":"develops","note":"1305 sets Kamukan, learned in the arts, with his bow; Naladiyar 386 likens a modest woman's beauty to a sharp sword in a clear-minded hero's hand.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The epic shows the learned hero whose skill the quatrain only uses as a simile, so a warrior's mind and weapon are tied.","gn":["The learned bowman is placed in a stock of images where skill governs the weapon, which the verse alone does not state.","The comparison gets a concrete clear-minded hero who actually wields the weapon in the next verse."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:1286","b":"cc:769","type":"same_image","note":"1286 taunts a rival with a bride won by another; Cintamani 769 taunts rivals with their wives' sulking looks lost after the battle.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Both epics make a pre-battle taunt turn on the rival's wife or bride, so the insult reaches his private stake and not his strength.","mm":1},{"a":"so:1315","b":"so:1316","type":"develops","note":"The danava pierces Sarthulakan in 1315, and in 1316 the pierced man cuts him down with the trident.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The wound does not decide the duel; the wounded man's counter-stroke decides it, so the pair shows the fight reversed in one verse.","mm":1},{"a":"so:1316","b":"k:774","type":"qualifies","note":"The pierced Sarthulakan fights on and kills the thrower, as in the Kural's warrior who sends his spear off and smiles at the one in his own body.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The poem makes the Kural's warrior who smiles at a spear in his body into a man who turns the wound into a weapon.","gn":["1316 gains a norm that treats a wound received in the fight as a mark of the warrior, not a loss","Kural 774 gains an actual wounded fighter who turns the thrown trident on its thrower, not only a smile"],"mm":1},{"a":"so:1317","b":"t:176","type":"tension","note":"Dhumaketu is likened to the Death of the age's end; Tirumular says the Lord's thinkers are beyond Death's messengers.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The war poem sets Death above all fighters, while Tirumular sets one who thinks of the Lord above Death.","gn":["1317 gains a view in which the Death its hero resembles can be turned back","Tirumanthiram 176 gains the epic's warrior who becomes Death's own image on the field"],"mm":1},{"a":"so:1318","b":"so:1320","type":"tension","note":"Dhumaketu's boast in 1318 is repeated clause by clause and turned back by the younger king in 1320.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"The answer uses the boaster's own clauses to cut him down, so the boast is shown to be what ruins him.","mm":1},{"a":"so:1318","b":"n:346","type":"qualifies","note":"Dhumaketu's boast of lifting hills and drinking the sea is the kind of word the Naladiyar says the excellent never speak.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"The epic gives the Naladiyar's rule a case in which the boast comes just before a fall.","gn":["1318 gains a standard by which its hero's boast marks him as the lesser man","Naladiyar 346 gains a warrior's boast whose price is paid in the next verses"],"mm":1},{"a":"so:1320","b":"so:1325","type":"develops","note":"The younger king's promise that Dhumaketu's match will come is kept in 1325 when Dhumaketu rolls from the field.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The prediction made in the taunt is fulfilled in a few verses.","mm":1},{"a":"so:1329","b":"so:1330","type":"same_image","note":"1330 gives back 1329's words almost unchanged.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"Giving the same words to both fighters leaves the taunt itself as the thing the poet shows, with neither side ahead.","mm":1},{"a":"so:1336","b":"so:1350","type":"develops","note":"The vow of 1336 to take the earth or the world of the gods is met when the arrow fells Suvarnaketu in 1350.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The vow of earth or heaven is ended not by either prize but by an arrow, so the warrior's two-sided promise leaves out the third outcome of defeat.","mm":1},{"a":"so:1339","b":"so:1341","type":"develops","note":"1339 asks whether one may stand aside while another fights; 1341 takes the single fight as the king's custom.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The duel is argued as a question of courage first and accepted as royal custom second, so the rule is made by the debate and not given in advance.","mm":1},{"a":"so:1345","b":"so:1346","type":"develops","note":"1345 names the arrow Naama; 1346 shows it cutting bow, arrows and illusion.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The poet shows the arrow's effect on the magic itself, so the king's last arrow answers the illusion that had outlasted every earlier shot.","mm":1},{"a":"so:1350","b":"so:1351","type":"develops","note":"The fall of the spear-bearer in 1350 is followed by the fall of the golden banner and the rout in 1351.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The army breaks at the moment its banner-holder falls, so the poet makes the champion's body the whole army's footing.","mm":1},{"a":"so:1355","b":"pn:278","type":"qualifies","note":"1355 warns that fleeing warriors will be shunned by women; Purananuru 278 shows a mother who swears to cut off the breast that fed a son she thinks has fled.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The shame of flight is placed in the women's mouths in both, but the epic's speaker uses it to threaten, while the Sangam mother tests the rumour before she acts.","gn":["The verse gains a case where the shame of flight is acted on by a woman with a sword, not left as a threat.","The poem gains a speaker who turns the same dread of women's contempt into a rebuke before battle, not after it."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:1356","b":"k:233","type":"qualifies","note":"1356 spends the perishing body for fame that stands for ever; Kural 233 holds that nothing in the world stands undying except renown.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"The epic's call to a warrior to give up his body for fame puts into action the couplet's claim, and shows who is asked to pay for it.","gn":["The verse gains the couplet's flat claim that only renown does not die, which it uses as its reason.","The couplet gains a scene where the price of that renown is a warrior's body in the heat of a rout."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:1356","b":"k:780","type":"develops","note":"1356 asks warriors to stay and die for fame; Kural 780 calls such a death worth asking for if those who sustained one weep for it.","grade":"weak","better":"qualifies","te":"The couplet makes the dead man's supporters the judge of the death, which the epic speaker, urging flight not to be done, leaves out.","gn":["The verse gains the test that the death should be one whose supporters mourn it, not one spent for its own show.","The couplet gains a speaker who uses the worth of such a death as a reason to stop a rout."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:1349","b":"k:774","type":"same_image","note":"In 1349-1350 the arrow goes through shield and chest and the warrior falls; Kural 774 has a warrior laugh as he pulls a spear from his own body.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The couplet shows the wound as a thing a brave man shrugs off, and the epic shows the same pierced body falling, so wound-laughter and wound-falling mark two ends of one warrior ideal.","gn":["The verse gains a standard by which the fall is read: a warrior is judged by how he takes the wound.","The couplet gains a body that does fall, the other end of the laughing warrior."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:1357","b":"so:1380","type":"develops","note":"1357 shames men who run to save life; 1380 calls a swordsman who runs from one man loathsome.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"The rally and the taunt are one argument in two voices: flight is judged first as lost honour, then as disgusting manhood.","gr":2,"mm":1},{"a":"so:1366","b":"so:1381","type":"develops","note":"1366 shows the hero mounted and frightening all; 1381 shows the enemy kings turning back in fear.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The terror one rider causes at the start is what finally sends the whole Vidyadhara line home.","mm":1},{"a":"so:1377","b":"so:1378","type":"develops","note":"The falling crown of 1377 is followed at once by the army's flight in 1378.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The crown's fall is shown as the cause of the rout, not merely its sign.","mm":1},{"a":"so:1378","b":"k:770","type":"qualifies","note":"1378 shows an army breaking at the sight of its king's fallen crown; the Kural says an army needs leaders.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"A fallen crown scatters an army that Kural 770 would call leaderless, showing the leader's role as a matter of sight as much as of command.","gn":["The rout gains a general rule: the army broke because it had no head to keep it together.","The couplet gains a scene in which the leader's loss is not death but a crown struck off, and the effect is instant."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:1380","b":"k:775","type":"develops","note":"1380 calls flight from one man loathsome; Kural 775 says even a blink at a hurled spear is flight.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The two texts set the line of disgrace at different places: the epic at running away, the Kural already at the flinch.","gn":["The taunt gains a stricter test: by Kural 775 the Vidyadharas' shame began before they turned.","The couplet gains a speaker who shames living men in front of their army, not a rule stated apart."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:1380","b":"pn:278","type":"develops","note":"1380 condemns a fleeing swordsman; Purananuru 278 gives a mother's vow to cut her breast when told her son fled.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain","te":"The Sangam poem shows what the epic's taunt costs the one who runs: the family's shame, not only his own.","gn":["The taunt gains a household that would mourn and punish the runaway.","The Sangam poem gains a Jain-epic scene where the same charge of fleeing is thrown at kings in the middle of a battle."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:1381","b":"k:380","type":"qualifies","note":"1381 says the mountain Vidyadharas fled from men whom fate has marked; Kural 380 asks what is stronger than fate.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"The epic gives fate as the one reason a strong flying army can be turned by men with no such powers.","gn":["The Vidyadhara retreat gains a cause beyond skill or number: the opponents are fated to win.","The couplet gains a battlefield instance in which fate beats a stronger force without any scheme being named."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:1382","b":"so:1379","type":"develops","note":"1382 begins the speech that 1379 announces when Kanaka Chitran steps in and speaks.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The announcement of a speech in 1379 only becomes a taunt of fear and manhood in the verses after it.","mm":1},{"a":"so:1387","b":"so:1390","type":"develops","note":"1387 shows an unnamed youth in ornaments; 1390 gives his name and sets his kin's host moving.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The description of the youth and the report of his name together show that one hero is both the sight and the cause of the army's rising.","mm":1},{"a":"so:1390","b":"so:1392","type":"develops","note":"1390 raises the brothers' host; 1392 shows the younger brothers and kings joining the press.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"One hero's entry draws in kin after kin, so the battle spreads by loyalty rather than by order.","mm":1},{"a":"so:1383","b":"k:969","type":"qualifies","note":"1383 asks whether the flesh body must break for honour's sake; Kural 969 says the honourable give up life when honour is lost.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The epic's taunt tests the Kural's rule on men who run, showing it as a shaming speech to the living where the couplet states a private resolve.","gn":["gains a measure for its taunt: honour is set above life as a standard","gains a spoken use in battle, where the rule becomes a challenge hurled at cowards"],"mm":1},{"a":"so:1386","b":"k:774","type":"qualifies","note":"1386 has the hero push through slanting spears for honour; Kural 774 has the soldier laugh as he pulls a spear from his own body.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Both link honour to the warrior's joy in danger, but the epic gives the motive as honour itself and the couplet as the pleasure of the wound.","gn":["gains the wound-joy of the couplet, which its hero does not show: he is only seen advancing","gains a stated motive, honour, which the couplet leaves unnamed"],"mm":1},{"a":"so:1395","b":"pn:62","type":"tension","note":"1395 shows the flood of blood and broken tusks as a spectacle; Purananuru 62 asks what victory there can be when both armies have died.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"Set side by side, the epic dwells on the field's carnage without cost where the Sangam poem measures it by the widows and the empty claim to victory.","gn":["gains the question it never asks: what is won when the field is full of the dead","gains the epic's long image of the carnage, which the poem only sketches"],"mm":1},{"a":"so:1408","b":"so:1415","type":"same_image","note":"1408 sows fame in a field ploughed with the plough; 1415 ploughs open the warriors' chests with the same plough.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The metaphor of a farmer's field opens as a boast and returns as the literal wound, showing the plough as both sign and tool of the hero's rule over the battle.","mm":1},{"a":"so:1412","b":"so:1414","type":"develops","note":"1412 cries that the young prince has fallen and the army heaves; 1414 brings the elder brother and the plough and the army roars again.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The army's despair and its roar are made by one man's presence, so the verses show whom its courage hangs on.","mm":1},{"a":"so:1416","b":"so:1417","type":"develops","note":"1416 shows Kanaka Naman coming to the fight on hearing of the plough-bearer; 1417 shows him struck on the shoulder.","grade":"wrong","better":null,"te":"The listener who joins a fight because he heard of a rival's feats is also the one who is struck down, so the fame that draws him is what ruins him.","mm":1},{"a":"so:1418","b":"so:1419","type":"develops","note":"1418 leaves one man burning at the news of defeat; 1419 has Ratna Greevan lift the mountain.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The news of the defeat is turned into an act, which shows what anger does with grief in the epic's warriors.","mm":1},{"a":"so:1419","b":"so:1420","type":"develops","note":"1419 lifts the mountain; 1420 shows the Suramai army scattering as he advances.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The scattering shows that the mountain wins by fear before it falls, so the plough-bearer's earlier victory is reversed here.","mm":1},{"a":"so:1424","b":"so:1425","type":"develops","note":"1424 brings the messengers' report; 1425 has the king resolve to loose the spell.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The report is the whole cause of the spell, so the epic ties the world's destruction to one man's grief.","mm":1},{"a":"so:1426","b":"so:1427","type":"develops","note":"1426 orders the spell to destroy mankind; 1427 has the spell pass the work on to Death.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The command goes from king to spell to Death, so the epic shows destruction as a chain of obedience with no one pausing.","mm":1},{"a":"so:1427","b":"so:1428","type":"develops","note":"1427 has the spell call Death; 1428 shows the spirits and the goddess coming and the spell loosed on the army.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Death's summons is joined by a feast of spirits, which shows the destruction as a gathering that feeds many.","mm":1},{"a":"so:1420","b":"k:765","type":"qualifies","note":"1420 shows an army scattering before a mountain-lifter; Kural 765 sets the standard of an army that stands together even when Death comes at it.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The scene gives the Kural's test a failure that the couplet only describes as a standard, and shows the Kural's army needs a leader to hold.","gn":["the unit gains a measure by which the scattering is a failure of the army and not only the fault of one man","the couplet gains a scene where the standard is not met, showing what its unbroken army is set against"],"mm":1},{"a":"so:1425","b":"k:305","type":"qualifies","note":"1425 has a king's grief and anger lead him to loose a spell that destroys the world; Kural 305 says anger kills the one who does not guard against it.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The epic shows anger as a power that reaches the world and not only the angry man, which limits the couplet's 'yourself'.","gn":["the unit gains a rule that makes the king's anger the cause of what follows and not only an act of war","the couplet gains a case where the harm of anger goes outward and the self is not the only victim"],"mm":1},{"a":"so:1426","b":"t:238","type":"qualifies","note":"1426 has a king order the destruction of all men; Tirumanthiram 238 says an unlearned king who says 'kill' is worse than Death, since Death spares the good.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain","te":"Set side by side, the king who commands total death is judged by a rule the epic's own narrator does not state.","gn":["the unit gains a standard by which its royal order is judged as the act of a king who says 'kill'","the verse gains a case of a king with the power to turn Death into his tool"],"mm":1},{"a":"so:1427","b":"t:176","type":"tension","note":"1427 has a spell command Death to finish his work at a king's wish; Tirumanthiram 176 says no one can fight when life leaves and only the thought of the Lord turns Death's messengers.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The epic's Death takes orders from a spell while the Saiva verse holds Death answerable to the Lord alone, so the two disagree over who commands Death.","gn":["the unit gains a view in which Death does not serve a king's spell","the verse gains a scene where Death is commanded by a human wish, which the verse denies"],"mm":1},{"a":"so:1435","b":"so:1439","type":"tension","note":"1435 says the missile cannot be turned back; 1439 shows its deity frightened and returned to its own shape.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The warning rests on a rule about hate-loosed weapons that the scene then breaks, so a claim of certainty is answered by the hero's greater strength.","gr":2,"mm":1},{"a":"so:1445","b":"so:1448","type":"develops","note":"1445 is a boast of what the speaker is; 1448 answers that this is talk among his own band.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Boast and reply are a pair: the boast is judged by where it is spoken, not what it says.","mm":1},{"a":"so:1443","b":"so:1452","type":"same_image","note":"Garuda comes as Tivittan's mount in 1443 and as his weapon in the same shape in 1452.","grade":"wrong","better":null,"te":"The bird changes from a helper that arrives to a weapon that is released, showing it as a power he can both ride and shoot.","mm":1},{"a":"so:1451","b":"so:1452","type":"develops","note":"The naga weapon of 1451 is cut down by the Garuda weapon of 1452.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The duel runs by counters, each weapon answered by its natural enemy, not by greater force.","mm":1},{"a":"so:1453","b":"so:1454","type":"develops","note":"Fire of 1453 is put out by the Varuna arrow of 1454.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"Again the answer is the opposed element, which makes the fight a sequence of matched counters.","gr":2,"mm":1},{"a":"so:1455","b":"so:1456","type":"develops","note":"The sleep arrow of 1455 is undone by the awakening arrow of 1456.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The last pair completes the pattern: each of Asvagriva's divine weapons has its answer, so none is decisive.","mm":1},{"a":"so:1448","b":"k:664","type":"develops","note":"1448 sets brave speech among one's own against facing the foe; Kural 664 says speaking is easy and doing as one has said is hard.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain","te":"The epic gives the Kural's remark a speaker and a test: the hilltop boaster must now act, which the next verses show.","gn":["The rebuke gains a general rule that talk is cheap, so it reads as a measure of action and not only a sneer.","The couplet gains a scene where the bragger is made to act within a few verses and his weapons fail."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:1449","b":"k:305","type":"qualifies","note":"1449 shows anger rising at insult and arrows poured out; Kural 305 says to guard against anger or it kills the one who has it.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The arrows are beaten away in 1450, so the epic shows the Kural's warning working out in the battle rather than as a rule.","gn":["The rage gains a cost: the arrows turn back on the shooter's hopes, as the next verse shows.","The couplet gains a king with an army whose anger leads him to weapon after weapon that fails."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:1437","b":"n:101","type":"develops","note":"1437 compares the bow's arrival to a friend won in a former life; Naladiyar 101 says an old deed seeks out its doer like a calf its mother.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The simile gives the old deed a good side: what returns can be a helper in need, not only a penalty.","gn":["The bow gains a possible source in former good deeds, which the narrator only hints.","The quatrain gains a case where the old deed returns as a weapon in a fight, not as suffering."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:1459","b":"so:1461","type":"tension","note":"In 1459 the king's discus hides the hero; in 1461 the same discus, hurled back, kills the king.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"The weapon that seemed the king's whole safety passes to his enemy, so the first strike is shown to be only half the story.","gr":2,"mm":1},{"a":"so:1460","b":"so:1463","type":"develops","note":"1460 shows the discus halting at the youth's side; 1463 explains it by saying a harm aimed at a blameless man returns on its sender.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The strange halting of the discus is given a moral frame only a few verses later, so the poet explains the marvel by a rule of rebound.","mm":1},{"a":"so:1463","b":"so:1466","type":"develops","note":"1463 ties the death to the rebound of a weapon sent against the blameless; 1466 says the discus he threw became his own enemy.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The rebound is first a simile and then a plain statement, so the onlookers read the king's death as his own act turning back.","mm":1},{"a":"so:1462","b":"so:1464","type":"develops","note":"1462 shows the king falling from the elephant like a cloud struck by fire; 1464 turns the same fall into a moral on impermanence.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The poet first shows the fall and then lets onlookers draw the lesson, so the doctrine comes from the scene and not before it.","mm":1},{"a":"so:1470","b":"so:1471","type":"develops","note":"The chief queen loses her life in 1470; the other wives fall in 1471.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The queen's collapse leads the rest of the household, so the grief runs outward from her to all the women.","mm":1},{"a":"so:1473","b":"so:1481","type":"develops","note":"1473 opens the laments with praise of the king's rule; 1481 closes with a question about his abandoned land and servants.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The women's laments move from praise of the king to a complaint that he left his duty, so grief ends in a charge against him.","mm":1},{"a":"so:1464","b":"n:3","type":"agrees","note":"1464 shows a king who rode elephants under the umbrella brought low; Naladiyar 3 says kings on elephants fall when their deeds turn against them.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The quatrain gives the cause as the king's own deeds and the verse gives the scene, so the pair joins the rule to a particular fall.","gn":["1464 states only that glory ends and does not name why; the quatrain supplies deeds that turn against a king as a reason.","The quatrain states the rule in general; here a named king in a battle is the case it applies to."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:1465","b":"k:336","type":"qualifies","note":"1465 says that the man who came yesterday under the umbrella is felled today; Kural 336 says the world's greatness is that one who was here yesterday is not here today.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"The verse gives a king's fall as the proof of the couplet and adds that the end comes by his own weapon, so impermanence is shown with a cause the couplet leaves out.","gn":["1465 shows the fall but lacks the couplet's plain statement that loss between yesterday and today is the world's greatness.","The couplet is an abstract rule and gains a king felled by his own discus as a case."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:1467","b":"k:332","type":"same_image","note":"1467 lists strength, wealth and beauty that do not last; Kural 332 likens wealth's coming and going to a crowd gathering and breaking up at a dance hall.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"The verse names what is lost, the couplet gives the image of its loss, and together they show loss as an ending without warning.","gn":["1467 gives a list of lost things but no image of how they go.","The couplet gives wealth alone and gains strength and beauty with a king's body as the proof."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:1463","b":"k:319","type":"qualifies","note":"1463 says a harm aimed at a blameless man returns on its sender; Kural 319 says hurt done in the forenoon returns in the afternoon of itself.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet makes the return a rule of time, while the epic makes it happen through the victim's own hand turning the weapon back, so the return has an agent.","gn":["1463 lacks the couplet's plain rule that wrong done is wrong received; it only has a simile.","The couplet gives the return without a means; the verse shows the thrown weapon as that means."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:1471","b":"n:25","type":"parallel_domain","note":"1471 shows wives falling and beating their faces at a husband's death; Naladiyar 25 shows kin wailing as a corpse is carried to the burning ground.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Both pass from mourners' grief to what it shows about life, but the epic keeps the grief in the scene and the quatrain turns it into a reason to give up household life.","gn":["1471 lacks the quatrain's turn from grief to a judgement that household joy is empty.","The quatrain gives wailing in general and lacks the specific grief of a king's wives."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:1476","b":"s:642","type":"same_image","note":"1476 asks whether a king who lay on a golden couch with wives rubbing his feet should now lie on an elephant's brow; Siddhar 642 says the throne of pure gold does not go with the body when Yama takes it.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The epic shows the couch lost by a battle and the Siddhar shows it lost by Yama, so one losing of luxury is told as a war and as the common end.","gn":["1476 gives a king's lost luxury but does not say that no luxury could follow him in death.","The Siddhar verse speaks to anyone and gains a particular king's fall as a case."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:1470","b":"cc:293","type":"same_image","note":"1470 shows the chief queen failing at her husband's body; Cintamani 293 shows women beating their breasts as the king lies dead.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain","te":"In both epics the king's death is felt first by his women, so a fall in battle becomes a household loss and not only a political one.","gr":2,"mm":1},{"a":"so:1466","b":"pn:227","type":"tension","note":"1466 says the discus he threw became his own enemy; Purananuru 227 calls Death a fool that cooked and ate its own seed by taking Valavan.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The epic shows the king as the agent of his death while the Sangam poet blames Death as an outside foe, so the two poems disagree on who ends a king.","gn":["1466 gives the fall as the king's own deed and lacks the Sangam poet's view that Death is to blame.","The Sangam poem blames Death and gains the epic's case where the weapon of the dead man killed him."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:1482","b":"k:555","type":"qualifies","note":"1482 has the women's tears put out the victor's wrath like water on fire; Kural 555 says the tears of the afflicted wear away a king's wealth.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Tears are a force against a king in both, but the epic turns them to a mercy that ends the fire while the Kural makes them a loss that grows.","gn":["The scene gains a rule: the victor is shown ruled by the weeping, as the couplet says a king should dread.","The couplet gains a case where the tears act at once and quench, not only wear away over time."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:1483","b":"n:21","type":"develops","note":"1483 lays a king on a bed of silk and gives him to the fire; Naladiyar 21 says kings under moon-like umbrellas have been carried off and called dead.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"The fire takes the proud king as the quatrain says it takes all, and the honour of his funeral does not alter that.","gn":["The funeral gains the quatrain's lesson that rank is no shelter from death.","The quatrain gains a named king whose cotton and silk bed does not save him."],"gr":2,"mm":1},{"a":"so:1485","b":"k:57","type":"qualifies","note":"1485 shows the queens barring desire with the fence of chastity and the lock on the senses; Kural 57 calls a woman's own restraint the best guard.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"The epic's widows guard themselves by their own restraint, which is the kind the Kural prefers to confinement.","gn":["The widows' act gains a test: it is the self-made guard the couplet praises.","The couplet gains a case at the point of greatest loss, after the husband's death, where outside guard has gone."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:1488","b":"so:1489","type":"develops","note":"1488 has the beaten Vidyadharas bow in fear; 1489 has the victor answer with mercy and a drum for the dead king's kin.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The fear of the defeated is answered with grace, which turns a conquest into a settlement.","mm":1},{"a":"so:1493","b":"k:70","type":"develops","note":"1493 has the father say he reached fatherhood through penance; Kural 70 says the son's help to the father is the question of what penance he did.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The father in the epic asks the question the couplet puts in others' mouths, so the son's worth is credited back to the father's penance.","gn":["The father's remark gains a rule that a worthy son is the world's proof of his father's penance.","The couplet gains a father who answers it himself, making the saying his own humble claim."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:1499","b":"k:546","type":"qualifies","note":"1499 crowns the hero because the discus won the war; Kural 546 says victory comes from the sceptre that does not bend, not from the spear.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The epic gives the title to the winner of the weapon, which the Kural would call a lesser ground than justice.","gn":["The crowning gains a stricter standard: is the discus a better ground than a straight sceptre?","The couplet gains a king whose title rests on the weapon, which tests its claim that the spear gives no victory."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:1512","b":"so:303","type":"develops","note":"1512 shows the hero lifting a gem mountain with both arms; 303 shows him as a youth rolling a rock heap that two hundred elephants might raise.","grade":"wrong","better":null,"te":"The boyhood feat of 303 is the first form of the strength that lifts Kotisilai, so the mountain is the proof of something already known.","mm":1},{"a":"so:1517","b":"so:273","type":"qualifies","note":"1517 shows the hero holding a mountain in one hand; 273 says even a man who could carry a diamond hill finds austerity and rule heavier.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The hero's lift is the lighter of the burdens that 273 weighs, and rule, not strength, is what he must still carry.","mm":1},{"a":"so:1517","b":"t:350","type":"qualifies","note":"1517 praises a mountain-lifter whom kings and gods hail; Tirumanthiram 350 tells of a lifter of the great mountain who was crushed by the Lord and then given grace.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The epic stops at homage to the lifter, while the Saiva verse asks what becomes of that strength before God.","gn":["the epic scene lacks any verdict on mountain-lifting strength beyond the kings' homage, and gains a measure from the lifter who is crushed","the Tirumanthiram verse lacks a lifter honoured and unpunished, and gains one whose feat is a king's proof, not an offence against a god"],"mm":1},{"a":"so:1510","b":"k:979","type":"qualifies","note":"1510 has the hero without swelling pride at the moment feats are listed; Kural 979 calls greatness the absence of pride.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The epic shows modesty arising from counting those who came before, a source the couplet does not name.","gn":["the epic verse lacks a name for the hero's lack of pride, and gains the couplet's term for it as greatness","the couplet lacks an occasion in which pride is tempted by lists of earlier deeds, and gains one"],"mm":1},{"a":"so:1523","b":"k:389","type":"qualifies","note":"1523 shows the pearl parasol bringing joy to the people and the sea-ringed world; Kural 389 says the world rests under the parasol of a king who bears bitter words.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The epic's parasol is the sign of conquest, while the couplet makes shelter depend on a king's patience with harsh speech.","gn":["the epic verse lacks any condition on the parasol's shelter, and gains the couplet's test of hearing bitter words","the couplet lacks a visible sign of sovereignty, and gains the pearl parasol of a triumphant king"],"mm":1},{"a":"so:1508","b":"k:381","type":"qualifies","note":"1508 counts a discus-lord's wealth in jewels, treasures, lands, cities and gatherings of kings; Kural 381 counts a king's parts as army, people, resources, ministers, allies and fort.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The epic's list is of things owned and has no ministers or fort, while the couplet's list names the organs a state cannot do without.","gn":["the epic list lacks counsellors and defences, and gains the couplet's organs of a state","the couplet lacks the epic's treasures and festive cities, and gains a picture of imperial wealth"],"gr":2,"mm":1},{"a":"so:1530","b":"so:1520","type":"develops","note":"1520 has the kings bow their crowns in fear as the hero holds the mountain; 1530 has kings hail him from their war-elephants as he enters the city.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The fear before the mountain becomes open praise in the city, so the feat is turned into public kingship.","mm":1},{"a":"so:1544","b":"so:1541","type":"develops","note":"1541 darkens the streets with incense and gold; 1544 makes the sun give up and set.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The festival's smoke is first a lamp for the elephant's eyes, then the cause of the sun's flight: the poet builds one hyperbole across two verses.","mm":1},{"a":"so:1550","b":"so:1547","type":"develops","note":"1547 announces the coronation at dawn; 1550 carries it out.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The announcement and the crowning make a promise and its keeping, so the festival is a prelude to the crowning, not merely its decoration.","mm":1},{"a":"so:1552","b":"so:1381","type":"develops","note":"1381 has the Vidyadhara kings turn back from fated men; 1552 says no rival is left.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The Vidyadharas' flight from the battle is what leaves the prince without an enemy at the wedding; his joy is the victory's late fruit.","mm":1},{"a":"so:1553","b":"k:332","type":"qualifies","note":"1553 shows great treasure arriving and the king drowning in it; Kural 332 compares wealth's coming to a crowd gathering and its going to the crowd's scattering.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Read together, the epic shows only the gathering of the crowd, and the couplet supplies the scattering that the festival hides.","gn":["The scene of arrival gains the knowledge that the sea of wealth is a crowd at a show that will break up.","The couplet gains a king at the height of his fortune, not an anonymous owner, so the image is tested at its peak."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:1552","b":"n:93","type":"qualifies","note":"1552 gives the prince's elation at fortune with no hint of its cause; Naladiyar 93 says wealth gathers when its time comes and leaves when the deeds that sent it run out.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The Naladiyar supplies a karmic account the epic withholds in these verses: his elation is for a fortune whose source and end are deeds, though the verse says no such thing.","gn":["The prince's elation gains a limit that the poet does not state: the fortune is held by karma and not by his own act.","The quatrain gains a case where wealth, wife and rule arrive together, so its claim is tested against a complete success."],"gr":2,"mm":1},{"a":"so:1556","b":"n:3","type":"qualifies","note":"1556 shows the king settled under the moon-white umbrella; Naladiyar 3 says kings who rode under umbrellas have fallen when their deeds turned.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The poem sets Tivittan at the top of his glory, and the quatrain supplies the warning that such a place is held by deeds and can be lost.","gn":["The opening praise gains a measure that the umbrella is no guarantee, so the lord's net of women reads as a risk.","The quatrain gains a king shown at his height with no fall yet in sight, which is the moment it speaks to."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:1559","b":"n:60","type":"tension","note":"1559 shows a king with the homage of three realms taking pleasure like Kama; Naladiyar 60 says the higher ones see the pain in pleasure and do not cling.","grade":"weak","better":"qualifies","te":"The poem offers pleasure at its largest scale, and the quatrain asks whether one who has all of it is looking at its pain.","gn":["The scene gains a standard by which a lord in his harem is judged without the poet having to judge him.","The quatrain gains an extreme case of the pleasure it warns about, with gods and kings standing guard over it."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:1561","b":"so:1564","type":"develops","note":"1561 has the chief queen resolve to see the plants wed; in 1564 the king smiles and accepts, and the grove outing follows.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"The queen's wish becomes the king's plan only through the maid's message and his answer, so the outing is shared.","mm":1},{"a":"so:1569","b":"so:1570","type":"tension","note":"1569 has the jester say his belly is full of many modakas; 1570 has him deny eating any.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"His pain is only greed for a feast to come, so the first claim is turned into a joke by the second.","mm":1},{"a":"so:1571","b":"so:1572","type":"develops","note":"1571 limits the king's gifts to Vedic brahmins; 1572 has the jester answer that debate-skill will win them.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The rule meant to bar the jester is met by a claim that skill in debate can stand in for Vedic learning.","mm":1},{"a":"so:1571","b":"k:134","type":"qualifies","note":"1571 has the king test a gift-seeker by Vedic learning; Kural 134 says a Brahmin can relearn the Veda but loses his birth if conduct fails.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The king's test is of learning, and the couplet's is of conduct, so the jester's comic case falls under both and passes neither.","gn":["The king's rule gains a rival test, so the gift is shown to rest on a narrow ground.","The couplet gains a brahmin who has neither Veda nor conduct and appears at the king's court."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:1572","b":"so:1573","type":"develops","note":"1572 has the jester boast of winning debates; 1573 gives his method, a stick for those who recite.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The boast gets its method, which shows that his debate is a comic beating, not an argument.","gr":2,"mm":1},{"a":"so:1573","b":"t:51","type":"tension","note":"1573 has the jester win debate by beating reciters; Tirumanthiram 51 says the wise gave up disputation and recited the Veda to gain release.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The jester practises debate for a gift and the verse in the Tirumanthiram says the wise leave debate for recitation, so the farce is set against that view.","gn":["The jester's boast gains a standard against which debating for a gift looks like a caricature of the learned.","The Tirumanthiram's rejection of disputation gains a figure for whom argument has become a way to get food."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:1576","b":"so:1569","type":"same_image","note":"1576 has the jester at the fallen fruit with his mouth watering; 1569 has him holding a belly full of modakas.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The same belly-led jester returns, now drooling instead of groaning, so greed is drawn as his trait and not one joke.","mm":1},{"a":"so:1581","b":"so:1582","type":"tension","note":"1581 has the jester say the grove has no guard at all; 1582 has the king answer that many warriors keep the wall, so the two contradict each other on the same grove.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"The dispute is settled only in 1583-1584, where the guards turn out to be the monkeys the jester then calls thieves, so the question of who guards is itself the joke.","mm":1},{"a":"so:1591","b":"so:1592","type":"develops","note":"1591 has the jester ask why the asoka bears red shoots at its foot; 1592 has the king show that they are women's lac-stained footprints.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The jester's puzzlement is the setting for the king to show how the grove's flowering is linked to women's feet, which 1593 then turns on the king himself.","mm":1},{"a":"so:1592","b":"so:1593","type":"develops","note":"1592 shows the asoka cured of longing by women's feet; 1593 has the jester say the king too blooms when the queen's foot touches his head.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The grove lesson becomes a jibe at the king, who is made the asoka's counterpart in his need of a woman's foot.","mm":1},{"a":"so:1594","b":"so:1595","type":"develops","note":"1594 has the king strike the jester and the jester threaten to tell the queen; 1595 has the king take his hand and promise never to do it again.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The jester's threat of the queen's knowledge is enough to make the king, lord of the world, give way, which shows who rules in the household.","mm":1},{"a":"so:1597","b":"so:1600","type":"tension","note":"1597 has the jester claim that a man-eating spirit lives in the stone; 1600 has him see that his own shadow imitates his acts, and he is calmed.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"The claim in 1597 is made through the scene itself, not by argument, and the later verse answers it by showing the fear to have no source outside him.","mm":1},{"a":"so:1600","b":"k:208","type":"qualifies","note":"1600 has the jester shown his own acts in his shadow, which does what he does; Kural 208 compares the ruin that follows evil deeds to a shadow that stays at the foot.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The shadow that is a comic false spirit here is the same thing the Kural uses as a sign that a person's deeds follow them, so the pairing shows the image in two uses.","gn":["The scene gains a moral dimension: the shadow that copies the jester is the image the Kural uses for a deed's consequence, and the king's order to see his acts in it points that way.","The couplet gains a case in which the shadow is an object of comic fear, which shows how readily people fear what is only their own doing."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:1597","b":"k:428","type":"qualifies","note":"1597 has the jester fear a spirit that is not there; Kural 428 says the knowing fear what should be feared while the fool does not fear it.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet names the fool who feels no fear where it is due, and the scene shows the other fault, a fear aimed at something unreal.","gn":["The jester's panic gains a name: a failure to judge what is truly to be feared, not simple cowardice.","The couplet gains a counterpart it does not state, in which fear is misplaced on an empty object."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:1601","b":"so:1602","type":"develops","note":"1601 has the king say they will sit and wait for the queen; 1602 has her enter the grove with her maidens.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The waiting on the stone is a brief pause that gives the jester's fright its place before the queen enters.","mm":1},{"a":"so:1621","b":"so:1561","type":"develops","note":"1561 has the queen resolve to see the parijata and the Kama creeper wed; 1621 sets the tree as bridegroom and starts the rites.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The queen's wish is carried out only after a quarrel and its mending, so the wedding is the end of an unplanned scene.","mm":1},{"a":"so:1609","b":"so:1613","type":"develops","note":"In 1609 Madhavan slips away from the angry queen; in 1613 her people trace him into the grove and bring him back by his cloth.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The jester's flight is undone by the queen's command, which shows who in the household has power over the king's companion.","mm":1},{"a":"so:1618","b":"so:1576","type":"develops","note":"1576 has the jester's mouth water at the fruit and him call the king to eat; 1618-1620 turn that greed into songs on the fruits of the grove.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The jester's appetite from the arrival in the grove is now his act for the couple, so his greed is what pays for his pardon.","mm":1},{"a":"so:1624","b":"so:1556","type":"develops","note":"1556 says the king who has set the world under his umbrella is caught in a net of deer-eyed women; 1624 shows the net at work, with arrows from the women's brows.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The poem's opening image of the netted king becomes a scene in which the quarrel's cause returns at once.","gr":2,"mm":1},{"a":"so:1608","b":"k:1313","type":"qualifies","note":"1608 has the queen burn with anger at the king's praise of a stranger's footprint; Kural 1313 has a woman angry at a wreath she says was worn for another woman.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The epic gives the wife a real occasion where the couplet's wife sulks on a pretext, and shows the husband pleading as the couplet's man does.","gn":["The queen's anger gains a measure: the Kural treats the jealousy of love as sulking even when nothing was done, and here something was.","The couplet gains a case where the husband did give cause by speaking of another woman, so its jest has a dark side."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:1616","b":"k:1304","type":"develops","note":"1616 has the king end the quarrel by embrace and plea; Kural 1304 says that not reconciling those who sulk is like cutting a withered creeper at its root.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The king's embrace shows what the couplet's warning needs: the sulk is healed by the one who gave cause, and the quarrel comes to an end in a wedding scene.","gn":["The scene gains a rule behind the king's acts: the Kural says a sulk left unreconciled kills love, so his bow and embrace are what love needs.","The couplet gains a king with a crown at his wife's feet, a case where reconciling costs a ruler his dignity."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:1614","b":"n:384","type":"qualifies","note":"1614 has the queen, once the king has bowed, smile and turn her anger into a game on the jester; Naladiyar 384 praises the wife who knows when to sulk and relents sweetly.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The queen relents by moving her anger on to the jester in garlands, which the quatrain's wife does not do.","gn":["The queen's smile gains a standard: a wife is praised who sulks at the right time and gives way sweetly, and she does so at once.","The quatrain gains a wife who is also a queen, whose sulk is ended in a public game that costs a third person."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:1611","b":"k:1302","type":"qualifies","note":"1611 has the queen turn to leave and the king bow with his crown at her feet; Kural 1302 says sulking is like salt in right measure and a little more ruins it.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The king's bow comes before the sulk can become too much, so the scene shows the couplet's measure kept.","gn":["The quarrel gains a gauge: the Kural's salt means the queen's anger is a proper amount of love's seasoning, which the king answers at once.","The couplet gains a ruler for whom the cost of ending the sulk is a bowed crown, making the measure a matter of rank."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:1628","b":"so:1561","type":"develops","note":"1561 has the chief queen resolve to see the parijata wed to the Kama creeper; 1628 has the queens dress the bride and join her to the tree.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The queen's wish ends in a ritual the women perform themselves, so the festival is theirs and the king only attends.","mm":1},{"a":"so:1628","b":"k:63","type":"qualifies","note":"1628 calls the wealth of sweet-smiling sons sweet to all; Kural 63 says people call children their wealth because their wealth comes through their own deeds.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The poem's child is a tree that has no deeds, so its sweetness comes wholly from rearing, which tests whether the couplet's reason is the only one.","gn":["The wedding gains a reason for the parents' joy that lies in the child's own deeds, which the plant cannot supply, so only the rearing is left to explain it.","The couplet gains a case in which the child is a plant and cannot earn anything, so the joy it names is shown to rest at least partly on the care given."],"gr":2,"mm":1},{"a":"so:1629","b":"so:1630","type":"develops","note":"1629 says the lord hides by a stratagem; 1630 shows the stratagem, an art that makes darkness a curtain, with Madhavan beside him.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The hiding is done by a magic art and with a companion, so the king does not slip away but joins the women's game unseen.","mm":1},{"a":"so:1630","b":"so:1631","type":"develops","note":"1630 has the king hidden among the women; 1631 has the chief queen guess it and send more women out to play like children.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The queen answers the hiding by staging the game she thinks he wants to watch, so the women's play is her reply and not only their sport.","mm":1},{"a":"so:1632","b":"n:46","type":"tension","note":"1632 builds the women's beauty out of gold, leaves and sprouts to delight the king's eyes; Naladiyar 46 asks which part of the fresh-garlanded woman is she, among entrails, fat, blood and bone.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The poem makes the body a thing of ornament and the quatrain takes it apart into its parts, so the two disagree on whether the adorned body is a person to be praised.","gn":["The adorned women gain a test of what is being praised once the leaves, gold and sprouts are taken off.","The quatrain gains a body shown at its most dressed and admired, so its question has an object that actually looks like a woman."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:1637","b":"k:1329","type":"qualifies","note":"1637 has the women paint the king and themselves sulking at him; Kural 1329 has the man wish the woman would go on sulking so that he may go on pleading.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The women fix the sulk in paint, where no pleading can follow, so the picture keeps the lovers' quarrel without its making-up.","gn":["The portrait gains the reading that a sulk is something the lover wants, which explains why the women paint it for him.","The couplet gains a sulk that is drawn, not spoken, which separates the delight of being sulked at from the pleading that follows."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:1642","b":"k:1115","type":"qualifies","note":"1642 has the women say a garland bent their slender waists; Kural 1115 has the man say a woman who wore anicham flowers with their stems has a waist too weak for good drums to sound.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"What the couplet gives as a lover's praise of a woman's delicacy the poem gives to the women as their own complaint, so the delicacy becomes something they offer to be noticed.","gn":["The women's complaints gain a source in the convention of praising a woman's frailty, so they speak in the lover's idiom.","The couplet gains women who claim the praised frailty themselves, so the frailty is shown as performance as well as praise."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:1643","b":"so:1627","type":"qualifies","note":"1627 crowns the groves as kings and hangs jewels on every branch; 1643 says the women conquer the flower-grove with their waists, glances and limbs.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"The grove made a king in 1627 is taken by the women, so the same grove is first a court and then a thing conquered.","mm":1},{"a":"so:1647","b":"so:1630","type":"qualifies","note":"1647 has the guards search the caves and thickets and then watch every side; 1630 has the king already hidden among the women by art.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The guard stands around a king who is already inside the place it guards, so the watch protects the game and not the king.","mm":1},{"a":"so:1646","b":"so:1650","type":"same_image","note":"1646 sets the artificial hill with gem waterfalls as the place the women reach; 1650 makes the same hill answer them and mock them.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The hill that was scenery becomes a speaker, so the grove ends the run by playing back at the women.","mm":1},{"a":"so:1649","b":"so:1650","type":"develops","note":"1649 has the women's girdles and anklets answered by the hill; 1650 has the hill take their lisping and answer it as mockery.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The echo of metal in 1649 grows into an echo of speech in 1650, which turns the sound from answer into jest.","mm":1},{"a":"so:1664","b":"so:1663","type":"develops","note":"1663 has the king order a god to become an elephant; 1664 shows the god taking the shape and nearing the women.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The king's order is carried out at once, so the fear in the grove is the king's own design and not an accident.","mm":1},{"a":"so:1667","b":"so:1666","type":"develops","note":"1666 has the king soothe the frightened woman; 1667 shows him driving off the elephant and adorning her.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The rescuer is the one who sent the danger, and the comfort completes his ruse.","mm":1},{"a":"so:1659","b":"so:1635","type":"develops","note":"1635 has the women sing the king's fame; 1659 begins a pounding song whose next verses list his deeds.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The fame the women only named earlier is now given its content, war and love together, in the song.","mm":1},{"a":"so:1674","b":"so:1669","type":"develops","note":"1669 sends the king to the pool to see the women bathe; 1674 has the bathing turn into a water-fight with him.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The watcher he meant to be becomes a target, so the pleasure goes from looking to joining.","gr":2,"mm":1},{"a":"so:1666","b":"k:265","type":"qualifies","note":"1666 ends by saying the king did a great penance that gives him whatever he wishes; Kural 265 gives wish-fulfilment as the reason penance is practised.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"The wish that penance buys here is a prank to win an embrace, which tests whether the Kural's reason for tapas needs any worthy wish.","gn":["1666 gets a stated rule behind its claim, that penance pays in whatever is wanted, so the king's power reads as a fruit of tapas.","Kural 265 gets a case where the wish bought is a trivial love-ruse, so its 'whatever is wished' is shown to cover small and worldly wishes."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:1663","b":"k:1087","type":"same_image","note":"1663 names the rutting elephant with a spread face-cloth; Kural 1087 likens the cloth on a woman's breast to the face-cloth over a rutting elephant's eyes.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The same elephant's face-cloth marks erotic danger in a couplet and is used as a stage prop to scare women into a man's arms in the epic.","gn":["1663 gets the face-cloth read as the sign of a threat that is also desire, so the elephant's fright is a love-device.","Kural 1087 gets a scene where such an elephant is actually set before women, showing the image as a danger they flee, not only as a metaphor."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:1675","b":"so:1678","type":"develops","note":"1675 opens the women's spray-war against the king; 1678 has the queens cry that they have beaten him and the pool run dry.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The water-play is told as a war from first volley to cry of victory, so the king's surrender is the planned end of the game.","mm":1},{"a":"so:1694","b":"so:1697","type":"develops","note":"1694 has the Vidyadhara drop from the sky and bow; 1697 has the king make him tell the message in his own words.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The envoy's arrival is the start of an exchange which waits on the king's leave, shown in the order of events rather than said.","mm":1},{"a":"so:1696","b":"so:1698","type":"tension","note":"1696 has the king give away all but his crown on seeing the letter; 1698 begins the message, which is a suitor's request for a bride.","grade":"forced","better":"develops","te":"The king pays for news before he knows what it asks, so the reward is for coming and not for what is said.","mm":1},{"a":"so:1680","b":"a:266","type":"same_image","note":"1680 has the king and his wives clinging to a float in the pond; Akananuru 266 has the husband bathing with young women on a float, whose gossip the wife throws back at him.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The same float-play is a king's marital pleasure in the epic and a husband's scandal in the akam poem, so what makes it fair or shameful is who the partners are.","gn":["The pond scene gains a measure of the play as a thing that, with other women, would be a wrong, which the epic never states.","The wife's reproach gains a version of the same play with no reproach in it, showing that her anger is aimed at the partners and not the bath."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:1697","b":"k:687","type":"qualifies","note":"1697 has the envoy take the letter, wait for the king's order and then bow and speak; Kural 687 says the best envoy knows the protocol, the moment and the place.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"The epic shows an envoy in the sequence of bows, seating, gifts and leave to speak that the couplet names but does not show.","gn":["The scene gains a standard by which the envoy's waiting and bowing read as skill and not only as courtesy.","The couplet gains a concrete order of events in which an envoy shows he knows protocol and time."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:1707","b":"so:1708","type":"develops","note":"1707 leaves the queen trembling and whispering, and 1708 has the king answer by reading her vision as the coming of a son.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The poem passes the fright of the omen to the king's reading of it, so the vision becomes a promise before the child is born.","mm":1},{"a":"so:1706","b":"so:1712","type":"develops","note":"1706 shows the moon entering the queen's mouth in union, and 1712 gives the birth of the radiant son the vision announced.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The moon's entry in the bed finds its fulfilment in the birth, so a reader sees the poem treat the dream as a true prediction.","mm":1},{"a":"so:1713","b":"n:23","type":"qualifies","note":"1713 lets the drums sound for the birth, and Naladiyar 23 says drums that sound for a wedding can sound the same day for the bridegroom's funeral.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The rejoicing of the birth is set beside the quatrain's warning that the same drum serves joy and death, so the festival is read against impermanence.","gn":["The drumming for the royal birth gains a darker echo, that what is loud with joy is a loan, which the poem does not say here.","The quatrain gains a scene of joy at its height, a king's son born to a palace city, in which its warning has the most to undo."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:1706","b":"n:54","type":"qualifies","note":"1706 shows pleasure on the bed as the cause of the longed-for son, while Naladiyar 54 says the wise see pleasure's pain and keep away from household life.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The poem lets the pleasure that Naladiyar condemns bear fruit in an auspicious child, so the householder's pleasure is not shown as barren.","gn":["The union gains a measure by which its fruit, a son, is the gain that a renunciant verse never counts.","The quatrain gains a case where the pleasure ends in a birth greeted by all, which tests how far its warning holds."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:1720","b":"k:70","type":"qualifies","note":"1720 has brahmins, kings and elders praise those who bore the child, and Kural 70 says a son's worth lies in making others ask what penance his father did.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The epic voices the praise at the newborn stage, before any deed, while the couplet says the praise belongs to the father only after the son has proved himself.","gn":["The praise of the parents gains the couplet's reason: it is the son's worth that earns the parents' credit, here advanced to birth.","The couplet gains a scene where the praise is spoken aloud in an assembly of every order, and for a child whose worth is only promised."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:1722","b":"so:71","type":"same_image","note":"1722 gives the newborn the name Vijayan, and 71 earlier tells of the birth of the elder Vijayan at the start of the story.","grade":"forced","better":"parallel_domain","te":"The name Vijayan returns in the next generation, so a reader must keep the uncle and the son apart while seeing the name tied to fortune and victory.","mm":1},{"a":"so:1730","b":"so:1706","type":"same_image","note":"1706 has the moon enter the queen's mouth as the sign of Tivittan's son; 1730 has a gem come to the Vidyadhara wife in a dream and swell her breasts.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain","te":"The poem uses the same dream of a bright thing entering the mother for two houses, so the Vidyadhara child's birth is told as a parallel of Tivittan's own.","mm":1},{"a":"so:1732","b":"so:1712","type":"same_image","note":"1712 shows Tivittan's son born in saffron-bright radiance; 1732 shows the Vidyadhara boy born with a face to shame the moon.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The two births are drawn with one image, the lit young body, which lets the later chapters set the two boys side by side as equals.","mm":1},{"a":"so:1735","b":"so:1791","type":"develops","note":"1735 names the boy Amitatejas for his boundless might; 1791 has the prince Amitatejas enter the swayamvara hall so bright that other kings are dimmed.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The name given at birth is made good at the swayamvara, so the boy's later outshining of kings is shown as the sign promised at his birth.","mm":1},{"a":"so:1737","b":"so:1721","type":"same_image","note":"1721 has Tivittan show jewels and gold to his guests and invite them to take them; 1737 has him give a share of his treasure to the Vidyadhara messenger.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The giving at the son's birth and at the nephew's news both follow the same open-handedness, so Tivittan is drawn as a king whose joy is shown by giving.","mm":1},{"a":"so:1743","b":"k:67","type":"develops","note":"1743 has Tivittan give his five-year-old son to teachers 'rich in unfailing learning'; Kural 67 says a father's good to his son is to put him first in the assembly.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural names the goal of a father's care, the seat in the assembly; the epic shows the first step toward it, early schooling in the hands of learned men.","gn":["The prince's schooling gains a stated purpose, that the father's good lies in the son's standing among the learned, not his wealth.","The couplet gains a concrete first step, a royal father handing a child of five to teachers, which the couplet does not name."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:1743","b":"k:400","type":"qualifies","note":"1743 calls the teachers 'rich in unfailing learning' (பொய்யாக் கல்விச் செல்வர்); Kural 400 says learning is the one wealth that does not perish.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"The epic uses the language of wealth for learning, which Kural 400 claims for it alone, but gives it to men who serve a king whose other wealth is shown at length.","gn":["The teachers gain the Kural's ground for the title 'rich', that their wealth is the one that does not fail.","The couplet gains a court where learning and the other wealth stand together, a test of whether learning is the only wealth."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:1745","b":"k:69","type":"qualifies","note":"1745 has the father sink in a sea of joy at the naming and the gathering of kin; Kural 69 says a mother is gladder to hear her son called worthy than at his birth.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The epic's joy comes at the naming and the kin's praise, while the Kural puts the greater joy later, in the son's worth, so the verse's delight is the first joy, not the last.","gn":["The father's delight gains a measure, that it is the joy of the birth and the Kural counts a greater one still to come.","The couplet gains a scene of a father's joy at the naming, a joy of the birth that it sets as the lower mark."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:1738","b":"n:20","type":"qualifies","note":"1738 has the child grow in the guard of women who tend him; Naladiyar 20 says Death takes even the newborn while the mother wails.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The poem's guarded growth is the thing the quatrain says cannot be guaranteed, so the child's safety is read against death's stealth.","gn":["The guarded child gains the quatrain's reminder that guard does not keep death off, which the poem does not say.","The quatrain gains a child as well cared for as can be, the strongest case for its warning."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:1730","b":"t:453","type":"qualifies","note":"1730 gives the dream of a gem as the sign that the child is coming; Tirumanthiram 453 says the Lord, at the moment of the joining, fixes the span and the earthly life of the child.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The epic reads the conception from omens and the mother's body, while the Tirumanthiram says the life was fixed by the Lord at the joining, so a sign is read against a cause.","gn":["The gem-dream gains a view in which the child's fate is already fixed, before the sign comes.","The verse of Tirumular gains a conception told as a royal dream, a case where signs are read as promises."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:1746","b":"so:1750","type":"develops","note":"1746 closes with the girl whose name is that of lightning growing up; 1750 shows the young women's ball-play at the swayamvara chapter's start.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The girl born here grows into the maiden of the later chapters, so the birth is the root of the swayamvara story.","mm":1},{"a":"so:1752","b":"p:586","type":"tension","note":"1752 likens the returning balls to lovers who will not be held off; Thiruppugazh 586 asks Murugan not to let the poet waste away over women whose palms ache from ball-play.","grade":"forced","better":"same_image","te":"One text enjoys ball-play as courtship, and the other treats the same game as the snare from which a devotee asks to be freed.","gn":["The epic's lively ball-play gains the devotee's view that this very beauty is what he must be saved from.","The Thiruppugazh's brief mention of ball-playing women gains a full scene of what the game is, with its clinging balls and flying powder."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:1753","b":"so:1538","type":"same_image","note":"1538 has attendants tie the bride's fine cloth and gem girdle for the wedding; 1753 has her daughter tie the same silk and gem girdle for ball-play.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The girl's self-dressing repeats her mother's bridal dressing and shows the game as a step toward marriage.","mm":1},{"a":"so:1757","b":"so:1758","type":"develops","note":"1757 has the parents call the tired daughter from behind the curtain; 1758 shows her letting the ball go and reaching her father's feet.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The call becomes obedience: the daughter breaks off her play at once, which is how a swayamvara's choice is later left to the father's council.","mm":1},{"a":"so:1759","b":"a:17","type":"qualifies","note":"1759 has the father hold his sweat-brow daughter after ball-play; Akananuru 17 recalls a girl too tired for a ball game who lay in her mother's arms.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain","te":"The tired child resting in a parent's arms is the last scene before she leaves for a husband, by public choice in the epic and by flight in the akam poem.","gn":["The epic's scene of the father holding his daughter gains the foreboding of loss that the akam poem builds from the same picture.","The foster mother's memory gains a case where the father arranges the girl's marriage openly, which the elopement she mourns has bypassed."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:1760","b":"so:1762","type":"develops","note":"1760 shows the father seeing his daughter's ripening body; 1762 follows with a council that sets the swayamvara.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"Her ripeness is the unspoken cause of the proclamation: the poem lets the father's look become public policy.","mm":1},{"a":"so:1762","b":"so:1763","type":"develops","note":"1762 has the king command the drum after council; 1763 has the drum struck.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The order given in the council is carried out at once in public sound.","mm":1},{"a":"so:1763","b":"n:23","type":"tension","note":"1763 beats a drum to announce a wedding; Naladiyar 23 says the drums that sounded for a wedding may sound the same day as funeral drums.","grade":"forced","better":"same_image","te":"The same drum that proclaims joy is, for the Naladiyar, the sign of how quickly joy passes into death.","gn":["The epic's wedding drum gains a shadow: the sound that calls the kings is the sound a worldly man should fear.","The quatrain gains a case where the wedding drum is proclaimed with royal pomp for a bride not yet chosen."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:1764","b":"k:545","type":"qualifies","note":"1764's herald prays for the king's life, virtue and cool rain and harvest; Kural 545 says rain and harvest gather where the ruler wields his sceptre justly.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The herald's prayer asks for the rain and crops that the Kural says come as a fruit of just rule, so the blessing doubles as a claim on the king.","gn":["The herald's list of blessings gains a rule: rain and crops are the sign that the king's sceptre is just.","The couplet gains a ceremony in which this wish is spoken aloud as the king's reign is made public."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:1766","b":"n:86","type":"qualifies","note":"1766 has the drum summon the world's kings to a marriage open to all; Naladiyar 86 praises a wife married with drums beaten for many to know.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"A marriage announced to all is a mark of honour in the Naladiyar, and the swayamvara pushes the same openness to where the bride is chosen before the world.","gn":["The proclamation gains a measure: public marriage is thought to guard the bond from later claims.","The quatrain gains a case where the drum summons rival suitors as well as witnesses."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:1766","b":"so:1768","type":"develops","note":"1766 has the drum fix the day and call the kings; 1768 shows kings and others arriving round the city.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The proclamation is answered by the arrival, and the crowd outruns the invited kings.","mm":1},{"a":"so:1782","b":"so:1783","type":"develops","note":"1782 has the builders finish the hall and the musicians play; 1783 has the herald call the kings in at the auspicious hour.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The hall is finished before the hour is announced, so the festival's order moves from building to music to the kings' entry.","mm":1},{"a":"so:1783","b":"so:1784","type":"develops","note":"1783 has the herald call the kings in and the drums sound; 1784 has the first king, of Ayodhya, enter.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The call is answered at once by named kings entering one after another, so the roll-call of kings is the proclamation's result.","mm":1},{"a":"so:1791","b":"so:1792","type":"develops","note":"1791 has the prince outshine the other kings; 1792 has the young women's eyes leave the kings for him.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The brightness the poet claims for the prince is proved by where the onlookers look, not by the poet's word alone.","mm":1},{"a":"so:1792","b":"so:1793","type":"develops","note":"1792 has the women's eyes already settle on the prince; 1793 has the king then call the maiden to come in.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The assembly's gaze is decided before the maiden is called, so her choice enters a room that has already leaned.","mm":1},{"a":"so:1783","b":"n:23","type":"qualifies","note":"1783 has conches and a great drum sound the hour of the kings' entry; Naladiyar 23 says the drums of a wedding hall can be the funeral drums of that very day.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The same drums that open a wedding hold the thought that joy and loss come under one roof.","gn":["The festival scene gains the quatrain's warning that its drums do not guarantee the happy end it announces.","The quatrain gains a hall with real drums and a gathering, set before the bride has chosen."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:1790","b":"n:21","type":"qualifies","note":"1790 has kings of five great lines wait with elephants and hosts for the choosing; Naladiyar 21 says kings who rode elephants under moon-like umbrellas have been carried off and called dead.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The kings' show of force at the choosing is the same show that the quatrain says does not save a king.","gn":["The roll of kings gains a measure by which their armies and elephants seem brief.","The quatrain gains the sight of kings gathered in full strength and ready to be seen, at the moment the strength is displayed."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:1792","b":"n:102","type":"qualifies","note":"1792 has young women's eyes fall on a handsome prince; Naladiyar 102 says beauty, youth, wealth and awe do not last.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The pull of the prince's looks is shown at full strength by the very verse that the quatrain would call short-lived.","gn":["The moment of beauty's pull gains the quatrain's reminder that what draws the eye is passing.","The quatrain gains a case in which beauty actually moves a whole assembly, so its claim is set against real force."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:1797","b":"n:23","type":"qualifies","note":"1797 sets her procession to the sounds of praise and music from all quarters; Naladiyar 23 shows wedding drums in a hall turning to funeral drums for the groom.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The poem fills the bridal march with music and hope, and the quatrain says the same drums can announce the end of the groom.","gn":["The procession gains the shadow that its music may not last, which the poem leaves out here.","The quatrain gains a wedding at its brightest, before any reversal, which is the moment it warns against trusting."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:1798","b":"n:17","type":"qualifies","note":"1798 shows kings' desire swelling as she enters; Naladiyar 17 tells the man not to desire a spear-eyed girl, who will stoop on a staff.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The poem lets the reader see her with the kings' eyes, and the quatrain supplies what those eyes will not see.","gn":["The kings' longing gains a limit set by age, which the verse does not state.","The quatrain gains a young woman actually seen, so its warning is tested against an entrance that wins every eye."],"gr":2,"mm":1},{"a":"so:1804","b":"so:397","type":"develops","note":"1804 sets this king's glory 'after Bharata'; 397 says the First Lord's grace made a king of the earth Bharata.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The herald's yardstick, Bharata, is the ruler the earlier verse shows as the First Lord's gift, so the king's glory is measured against a grace-given one.","mm":1},{"a":"so:1809","b":"so:543","type":"develops","note":"1809 gives the harp-playing boon-winner of the northern mountain; 543 has Dharanan come at Nami's song, which is how the boon was won.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"What the herald says in two lines is the story told at length at 538-547, so the hall's praise draws on the book's own earlier telling.","mm":1},{"a":"so:1810","b":"n:195","type":"qualifies","note":"1810 praises a king for the line that bred him; Naladiyar 195 says a lineage is made by austerity, learning and effort, not by name.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The herald's whole mode of praise is descent, and the quatrain offers another test for the same kings, what each has done.","gn":["The suitor's praise gains a test the herald does not apply, effort and learning in the man himself.","The quatrain gains a hall of kings praised for birth, which shows how common the claim it denies was."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:1815","b":"n:31","type":"develops","note":"1815 says women who reached this king's chest had done austerity; Naladiyar 31 says present comfort comes from earlier austerity and is lost without more.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The herald's line puts a marriage on the same ledger of past austerity that the quatrain keeps, and the quatrain adds that it runs out.","gn":["The praise gains a warning that earlier austerity wears down, which the herald omits.","The quatrain gains a new case, a bride's good fortune, where its past austerity pays out in a marriage rather than a house."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:1814","b":"pn:3","type":"same_image","note":"1814 puts a moon-line king under a tall umbrella with cloud-like elephants; Purananuru 3 puts the Pandya under a white umbrella like the full moon.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The poem makes the moon-line king's umbrella the sign of his kind, as the Sangam poem does for the Pandya, so the herald's king reads as the Pandya of that tradition.","gn":["The verse gains a Sangam picture that fills in the umbrella and moon that it names only in passing.","The Sangam poem gains a late epic that keeps its image but brings it to a marriage hall, not a battlefield."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:1816","b":"a:385","type":"same_image","note":"1816 places a king at Urandai among swans, drums and banners; Akananuru 385 uses Uranthai as the standard of a house rich in festival.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"Urandai is the name of wealth in both books, so the herald's list of its splendours carries a meaning already known from the Sangam poem.","gn":["The epic city gains an earlier use of its name as a measure of richness.","The Akam poem gains a king's side of the city, whose wealth it uses only as a comparison."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:1824","b":"k:380","type":"develops","note":"1824 says the herald's showing was needless because her lot of enjoyment had already ripened; Kural 380 asks what is stronger than fate, which reaches first even when one tries another way.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"A choice that looks free and quick is shown to have been set before the guide's words, which a bare general rule about fate does not show.","gn":["The verse gains a general rule that fate comes before any human device, which would otherwise be only an aside about her lot.","The couplet gains a scene in which a formal, careful introduction is overtaken by fate in a glance."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:1827","b":"k:377","type":"qualifies","note":"1827 has kings with every means to win her calm themselves because deeds ripen as they will; Kural 377 says even those who have gathered millions enjoy only what the apportioner has apportioned.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"Fate's apportioning is shown over a bride and not over wealth, among men whose rank and army were equal, so the rule is tested where effort was all in place.","gn":["The kings' calm gains a stated general law behind it, which the verse leaves as their passing thought.","The couplet gains a contest in which every competitor had equal means, the case where apportioning alone must decide."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:1827","b":"n:33","type":"develops","note":"1827 shows kings who lose the bride calming themselves by seeing the loss as karma; Naladiyar 33 contrasts the fool who sighs hotly at the fruit of deeds with the wise who call it old desert.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The same step from sighing to calm is shown as a group's act on a public loss, where the quatrain gives only the principle.","gn":["The kings' calm gains a named opposite, the fool who sighs and breaks, which the scene alone does not give.","The quatrain gains a case of powerful men who choose the wise answer after having sweated and sighed, as 1826 shows."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:1839","b":"so:1827","type":"develops","note":"1839 states for everyone the thought that 1827 reports of the rejected kings: the fruit of deeds is what comes.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The kings' private resignation is the lesson the poet then gives to all readers, so the swayamvara scene is built to carry it.","gr":2,"mm":1},{"a":"so:1839","b":"so:1842","type":"develops","note":"1839 bids all remember the fruit of deeds; 1842 shows Payapati reasoning that the pleasure of his grandchildren is the fruit of swollen merit with more seed to sow.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The poet's general advice becomes a king's own reasoning, which turns the closing moral of one sarukkam into the opening act of the next.","mm":1},{"a":"so:1839","b":"t:267","type":"develops","note":"1839 says wealth and heavenly pleasure are the fruit of deeds and bids remembering; Tirumanthiram 267 says joy and sorrow were fixed by earlier acts and that fools who see joy yet do not give know no virtue.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The poem asks only for remembering the fruit and keeping to the way; the couplet names what that way includes, giving, and what its absence shows.","gn":["The poet's advice to keep to the way gains one concrete act, giving, as the proof that the fruit has been understood.","The quatrain gains a story of fruit at its fullest, a royal wedding and gods' pleasure, set against which its warning to the unmoved has its force."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:1842","b":"so:1851","type":"develops","note":"1842 says more seed must be sown for the austerity that bore present pleasure; 1851 shows those who instead cut the root and die.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The warning of 1851 is the unsown field that 1842 fears, so the king's wish to sow is shown as the way out of that ruin.","mm":1},{"a":"so:1843","b":"n:3","type":"develops","note":"1843 reads the kingship and the bowing kings as the gift of the good-deed deity; Naladiyar 3 says kings on elephants under umbrellas fall when deeds turn.","grade":"weak","better":"qualifies","te":"Read together, the umbrellas that 1843 credits to past merit are the same ones Naladiyar says a turn of deeds can remove.","gn":["The grant of kingship gains an explicit reversal: what a good deed gave, a turn of deeds can take back.","The quatrain gains an account of why the king rose, through the good-deed god, which it assumes without saying."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:1844","b":"so:1851","type":"same_image","note":"1844 calls those who destroy the root of good deeds base; 1851 shows them leaving the root that feeds them sweetly.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The root of good deeds is a recurring image whose repetition shows the king returning to the same fear from two sides.","mm":1},{"a":"so:1847","b":"k:46","type":"tension","note":"1847 concludes that nothing is better than austerity; Kural 46 asks what is gained by going the outside way if household life is lived in virtue.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The pair turns on whether household virtue is enough: the king answers from the certainty of death, the couplet from the sufficiency of aram.","gn":["The king's resolve gains the opposing case that a householder who keeps aram may need no leaving.","The couplet gains a ruler for whom death makes household virtue seem insufficient."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:1849","b":"n:41","type":"same_image","note":"1849 shows the adorned body undone by one small prick; Naladiyar 41 says a bit of skin torn off needs a stick to drive away crows.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Both break the body's finery with a trivial injury, but the epic does it for a king examining his own display, the quatrain to answer a lover's praise.","gn":["The king's reflection gains the quatrain's crows, which the injury will draw.","The quatrain gains a body decked with garland, paste and incense that the injury undoes."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:1850","b":"n:41","type":"same_image","note":"1850 has crows and kites guarded off with a hand stick; Naladiyar 41 has the stick that drives the crows from the torn body.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The same stick appears for two different speakers, a king guarding himself and a poet mocking the lover, which shows how fixed the picture was.","gn":["The verse gains a second use of the stick and the crow, as a rebuke of those who call the body lovely.","The quatrain gains a king speaking of his own body in the same terms."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:1850","b":"p:146","type":"qualifies","note":"1850 turns on the body's filth to set aside kingly pride; Thiruppugazh 146 describes the same filth as a house of diseases and asks Murugan for grace.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain","te":"The Jain king ends his disgust in a plan to leave and seek sages, while the Thiruppugazh ends the same disgust in a plea for a god's feet.","gn":["The verse gains a different exit from the body's foulness, a god's grace in place of the sage's feet.","The poem gains a king who draws the opposite practical conclusion from the same catalogue."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:1856","b":"n:6","type":"develops","note":"1856 tests whether any statecraft can escape Death; Naladiyar 6 says none here has leapt clear and bids the wealthy give.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The king tries the four expedients the quatrain never names, and by their failure the quatrain's rule is shown by a ruler's own attempt.","gn":["The question gains the rule that no one leapt clear, so the ministers' failure is not their own.","The quatrain gains a ruler who actually tries every device of power to escape."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:1859","b":"k:334","type":"develops","note":"1859 shortens the term to a blink before Death; Kural 334 calls the day a blade that saws the life.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The epic shortens the quantity: the couplet measures life as days, the verse measures it by the blink the minister names.","gn":["The blink gains the blade as an image for time's cutting.","The couplet gains a minister who counts the cut in a blink."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:1860","b":"so:1862","type":"develops","note":"1860 asks for a refuge beyond Death; 1862 answers that none exists but the austere sage's feet.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The question and the answer divide the council's work: the king asks, the minister names the sage's feet.","mm":1},{"a":"so:1862","b":"t:2","type":"tension","note":"1862 names the austere sage's feet as the only refuge from Death; Tirumanthiram 2 praises Siva who kicked Death and became king of the south.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain","te":"The two refuges differ in kind: a teacher's feet to be reached by one's own austerity, against a god who defeated Death himself.","gn":["The sage's feet gain a rival refuge in a god who has already defeated Death.","The invocation gains a Jain-framed answer that makes the sage, not Siva, the shelter."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:1864","b":"so:1868","type":"develops","note":"1864 gives the order to sound the drum for the festival; 1868 shows the drum sounded and the whole city rising in joy.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The order and its carrying out show a king's single word turning a whole city to festival, so his authority is shown at its greatest before the sarukkam's later turning away.","mm":1},{"a":"so:1867","b":"so:1882","type":"develops","note":"1867 promises the festival-goers a celestial form; 1882 shows the procession wearing their deeds away.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The promised heaven of 1867 is fed by the dying of deeds in 1882, so the festival works as a means of release in the ledger of karma, not a mere celebration.","mm":1},{"a":"so:1869","b":"n:23","type":"qualifies","note":"1869's people hear the drum as the sign of a long life and a blessing; Naladiyar 23's wedding drums turn to funeral drums the same day.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain","te":"The same drum is a promise of long life in the epic's crowd and a warning of death in the quatrain, and the pair shows how a listener's state decides what it announces.","gn":["The citizens' blessing gains a shadow of death that they do not see but the king, who has just asked whether anything beats Death, does.","The quatrain gains a case in which the drum's joy is not reversed into mourning but turned to a king's resolve to leave."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:1870","b":"k:34","type":"qualifies","note":"1870 piles up festoons, banners, drums and conches that drown the sea; Kural 34 calls virtue the mind's spotlessness and the rest a noise (ஆகுலம்).","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The epic lingers on the din of worship while the couplet measures a rite by the mind within, so the pair asks whether the festival is aram or only clamour.","gn":["The festival's din gains a standard the poet does not apply in this passage: the mind of the worshipper.","The couplet gains a full, glittering example of outward worship against which its charge of mere noise can be tried."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:1866","b":"k:5","type":"qualifies","note":"1866 makes the Lord the foe of deluding deeds and rewards the festival's onlooker with the world of merit; Kural 5 says both kinds of deed do not cling to those who praise the Lord.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The epic ends in merit's world, a reward the couplet's two-fold deeds would also refuse to cling to, so the pair shows two ways of reading what freedom from deeds amounts to.","gn":["The promise of a world of merit gains the couplet's reminder that good deeds too are bonds to be shed.","The couplet gains a public act, the royal festival, through which a community pays the Lord's praise."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:1880","b":"n:3","type":"qualifies","note":"1880 sets the king under his umbrella with fly-whisks at the head of his army; Naladiyar 3 says those who rode in such state fall when deeds turn.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The quatrain's fall of the umbrella-borne is forced by deeds, while the epic king's glittering display is the height from which he will walk away by choice.","gn":["The parade gains the quatrain's warning that its splendour is borrowed from deeds that can turn.","The quatrain gains a king whose descent is not a loss to enemies but a renunciation he has already chosen."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:1886","b":"so:1897","type":"same_image","note":"1886 has offered flowers falling in a rain at the king's approach; 1897 has a divine rain of flowers burying the golden ground at the Lord's appearance.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The same flower rain is first the king's offering and then the heavens' answer, so the worship and its reception share one image.","mm":1},{"a":"so:1902","b":"k:9","type":"qualifies","note":"1902 has the crowned king bow until the gem-crown touches the earth; Kural 9 says a head that does not bow at the feet of the eight-quality one is a useless organ.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"The couplet's useless head becomes the crowned one of a king who sets power on the ground, which shows what the bow costs a ruler.","gn":["The king's bow gains a stated reason, that a head which will not bow is as dead as a sense that does not sense.","The couplet gains a ruler whose head carries a crown, so the bow costs a pride the couplet's speaker does not mention."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:1903","b":"so:181","type":"same_image","note":"1903 asks whether the Lord's red feet are reddened by lotus petals or by their own rays; 181 has the king praising the red lotus feet of him who conquered the dust of deeds.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The red feet appear in both shrine hymns, first as a fact and then as a riddle of cause, so the later hymn plays on the earlier plain praise.","mm":1},{"a":"so:1904","b":"k:5","type":"develops","note":"1904 says the Lord cut off the dark garlands of fierce deeds; Kural 5 says the two deeds born of darkness do not cling to those devoted to the Lord's true praise.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Lord is shown in 1904 as the one who has already done what the couplet promises his devotees, so the cause of their freedom is his own freeing.","gn":["The hymn gains a stated result for the praiser, that the two kinds of deeds will not cling to him.","The couplet gains a named doer who has cut his own deeds, which it leaves unsaid."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:1904","b":"so:182","type":"develops","note":"1904 says those who do not grasp the Lord's greatness quarrel and are bound by a quarrel of deeds; 182 says that even when called the knower of all the Lord is hard to truly know.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The earlier verse says why the Lord is hard to know and the later says what follows from not knowing him, which is the quarrel that binds.","mm":1},{"a":"so:1906","b":"k:7","type":"qualifies","note":"1906 says there is no other god to count, and the Lord is easy for those who worship his feet; Kural 7 says only those who reach the feet of the one without a peer can remove the mind's anxiety.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"The hymn names its peerless one by what he does to living things, while the couplet leaves the feet unnamed and so open to any tradition.","gn":["The hymn gains the couplet's claim that the anxieties of the mind are removed only by those feet.","The couplet gains a candidate for the one without a peer, whose peerlessness is given as harmlessness and ease of access."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:1906","b":"t:1451","type":"tension","note":"1906 says no one but the Lord, who harms no life, can be counted as god; Tirumanthiram 1451 says there is one God in all ten directions and bids us take refuge in his feet to dry up the sea of deeds.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain","te":"Both hold one God whose feet end the deeds, but they name different ones, and the pair turns on whether the one at whose feet deeds are shed is a Jina who gave up his own, or a God who gives grace.","gn":["The hymn gains a rival that makes the same demand, to take refuge at one pair of feet, but on the strength of a different god.","The verse gains a Jain claim that the Lord who deserves such refuge is the Jina, who has no part in creating or ruling."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:1920","b":"so:1916","type":"develops","note":"The ministers of 1916 ask the sage to turn aside the tangled births; 1920 lists what he will teach in answer.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The sage's list of topics shows that the answer to a plea for escape from births is first a map of the births themselves.","mm":1},{"a":"so:1922","b":"so:1921","type":"develops","note":"1921 says four states turn the souls; 1922 names them as hell, beast, man and god.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The four are named at once, so the turning wheel is shown to carry a soul as easily into hell as into heaven.","mm":1},{"a":"so:1923","b":"so:1844","type":"develops","note":"1844 says those who cut the root of good deeds will be kings in the hells; 1923 counts the layers and dwellings of those hells.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The hells of the earlier threat are shown to be a ranked place with room for every kind of fallen king.","mm":1},{"a":"so:1926","b":"so:1923","type":"develops","note":"1923 counts the lower hells in all; 1926 narrows to the first and its thirteen levels and thirty lakh pits.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The sage's numbers are first given as a whole and then broken down hell by hell.","mm":1},{"a":"so:1910","b":"so:2","type":"same_image","note":"1910 praises the Lord as one who has struck down fierce deeds and lit the lamp of knowledge; 2 hails him as light of the world who split fierce deeds.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"The king's praise repeats the poet's opening praise, so the king's worship matches the epic's own account of the god.","mm":1},{"a":"so:1921","b":"n:2","type":"same_image","note":"1921 turns the soul like a cart wheel through four states; Naladiyar 2 turns wealth like a cart wheel, never level with anyone.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The same cart wheel is used for what turns beneath a person in Naladiyar and for the person who is turned in the Soolamani.","gn":["The wheel of 1921 gains a lesser double: the turning of wealth within one life as a small copy of the soul's turning through births.","The quatrain gains a larger turning behind its wheel: wealth turns like a wheel because the one who holds it is turned by deeds from birth to birth."],"gr":2,"mm":1},{"a":"so:1930","b":"t:165","type":"tension","note":"1930 says sufferers entered the pit by their own earlier striving and no one comes when they cry; Tirumanthiram 165 sends those who do not worship Siva to the seventh hell where they cry out.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The cry in hell is answered by neither text, but the Jain verse locates the cause in deeds and the Saiva verse in worship withheld.","gn":["The cry of 1930 gains a Saiva counterpart in which a withheld act of worship, not a deed's weight, is the cause of the same crying.","The Saiva hell gains a Jain rival in which no rescuer is named because no god sits above the deeds."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:1931","b":"t:198","type":"tension","note":"1931 has tormentors tell sufferers to take the fruit of their own deeds; Tirumanthiram 198 has Death's servants bind killers and drive them with the very commands they gave.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"Both hells have punishers who echo the victim's own deed, but only the Saiva servants act for Death, while the Soolamani's act for no one.","gn":["The tormentors of 1931, named only by their trade, gain a Saiva figure to compare with: a servant who acts for Yama.","The Saiva hell gains a Jain counterpart in which the punishers speak only of the deed the sufferer sowed."],"gr":2,"mm":1},{"a":"so:1931","b":"n:33","type":"qualifies","note":"1931 has the sufferers receive the fruit of former deeds; Naladiyar 33 says the fool sighs hotly when the fruit arrives, and the wise who treat it as old debt pass beyond confusion.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"1931 shows the fruit received with no way to reinterpret it, which is the state Naladiyar 33 says the wise avoid.","gn":["The hell scene gains the stance it lacks: the sufferers receive their deeds' fruit without the calm that frees.","The quatrain gains a concrete picture of the fool it describes, shown in the pit rather than only named."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:1920","b":"t:219","type":"tension","note":"1920 promises the way of conquering deeds as a thing the sage will teach; Tirumanthiram 219 says the fire worshipped burns up the many ills of deeds.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The Jain sage treats conquering deeds as a teaching to be given, while Tirumular treats it as a rite whose fire burns them.","gn":["The sage's 'way of conquering deeds' gains a rival method, burning by rite, against which the Jain way can be read.","The fire rite gains a Jain counterpart in which deeds are conquered by teaching and austerity, not by offering."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:1939","b":"so:1851","type":"develops","note":"1851 says those who did evil take its seed and sow it and die wanting pleasure; 1939 shows the harvest as impalement and splitting, named the fruit of deeds.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The king's earlier fear of sowing evil gets its full price here, where the sage's list shows what the sown seed becomes.","mm":1},{"a":"so:1939","b":"t:2544","type":"tension","note":"1939 gives hell as the ripening of the sufferers' own deeds with no one allotting it; Tirumanthiram 2544 says the Lord set hell and heaven for souls by grace.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The same pain is explained once by deeds ripening and once by a lord's grace, which marks where the Jain and Saiva accounts of hell part.","gn":["The sage's verdict gains a rival answer to who stands behind the ripening, which its last line leaves unnamed.","The Tirumanthiram verse gains a picture of what hell contains and a cause in the sufferer's own deed, neither of which it gives."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:1932","b":"cc:2769","type":"same_image","note":"Both make the adulterer embrace a figure of red-hot copper in hell.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Two Jain epics share the same matched punishment, but only 1932 begins from the secret night-visit and the wardens' mocking words.","mm":1},{"a":"so:1932","b":"s:450","type":"qualifies","note":"1932 stages lust's punishment as a forced embrace of fire; Siddhar 450 has the pit of hell say it will eat the lustful and the loins say they will ruin him.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain","te":"A Siddhar's warning in the speaker's own body becomes in the epic a scene of wardens and figures, so the same fear is placed inside and outside the sinner.","gn":["The embrace of fire gains an inner meaning, that desire itself is what the pit feeds on.","The Siddhar's threat gains a literal staging, in which the loins' ruin is acted out as an embrace."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:1933","b":"cc:2767","type":"same_image","note":"Both set hell's dogs on the victims; 1933 adds that the wardens raised them in cages and that the victims flee up a tree of thorns.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The dog and the thorn tree recur as fixed parts of the Jain hell, while 1933 shows the dogs as reared for the work.","mm":1},{"a":"so:1934","b":"cc:2768","type":"same_image","note":"Both feed flesh-eaters molten metal; the Cintamani calls them to come, 1934 shows them the 'flesh' first.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The shared molten-metal meal marks flesh-eating as a fixed offence of the Jain hell, with 1934 adding the mocking offer of food.","mm":1},{"a":"so:1935","b":"k:255","type":"develops","note":"1935 carves and feeds back to meat-eaters their own limbs; Kural 255 ties eating flesh to hell.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The Kural's single claim about meat and hell becomes a punishment that mirrors the meal, limb for limb.","gn":["The carved-and-fed vision gains a general rule that makes meat itself, not cruelty only, the ground of the penalty.","The couplet gains a concrete form of the hell it names, in which the eater's own body becomes the dish."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:1937","b":"k:183","type":"develops","note":"1937 tears out the tongue of those who slander their betters; Kural 183 says death is better than living by backbiting and falsehood.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet's preference for dying over slander assumes death ends the matter; the epic says it does not and takes the tongue after death.","gn":["The torn tongue gains an ethical comparison that sets the slanderer's life against death before any hell is shown.","The couplet gains an afterlife in which the death it prefers is not an escape."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:1941","b":"cc:2772","type":"same_image","note":"Both give the thirsty a false drink: poison pools here, water that turns to pus in the Cintamani.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Relief that destroys the sufferer is a standing device of the Jain hell, shown with different liquids in each epic.","mm":1},{"a":"so:1946","b":"nk:92","type":"agrees","note":"1946 gives the hell-beings' body size in a measure of bows and cubits; Neelakesi 92 gives the dark hells' beings as five hundred bows.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Two Jain epics share a table of hell body-sizes, so 1946's hard measures may be read against the Neelakesi's figure.","mm":1},{"a":"so:1951","b":"n:84","type":"develops","note":"1951 names those who leave their wives for others' as falling into deep sorrow; Naladiyar 84 lists disgrace, a cut leg and long suffering in hell for the adulterer.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"Naladiyar counts the worldly penalties before hell; the epic puts only hell and gives the torment in detail.","gn":["The adulterer's fate gains the earthly costs of disgrace and mutilation that the sage's hell leaves out.","The quatrain gains the long torment that it names only as hell, shown in the verses before."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:1951","b":"t:165","type":"tension","note":"1951 and the following verses give conduct as the ground of hell; Tirumanthiram 165 puts those who do not worship the Lord's form in the seventh hell.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The sage's list of sins contains no failure of worship while Tirumular's contains little else, so the two define the sinner differently.","gn":["The sage's conduct list gains a contrast in which hell's gate is worship, which none of its sins names.","The Tirumanthiram verse gains a list of acts of harm and greed that earn hell without any lack of worship."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:1953","b":"n:273","type":"develops","note":"1953 puts those who hoard without householder's dharma and die hard-fisted in hell; Naladiyar 273 says the hoarder is laughed at by his own wealth.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"The miser is mocked in life by the wealth he stored and punished after death for it, two sanctions for one fault.","gn":["The hell verse gains the earthly shame of the miser, which it omits.","The quatrain gains the afterlife penalty it does not mention."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:1956","b":"nk:77","type":"develops","note":"1956 grades animal births by the number of senses from one to five; Neelakesi 77 splits animals into fixed and moving and counts five kinds of the fixed.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"The sense-ladder of the Soolamani and the fixed and moving divisions of the Neelakesi are two cuts of one Jain classification of life.","mm":1},{"a":"so:1962","b":"nk:104","type":"agrees","note":"1962 names the flesh-seller, the one who crushes and the hunter as the animal's killers; Neelakesi 104 names battle, the chase for food and the nets.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Both Jain tellings draw on one stock list of the animal's torments, so the list is a teaching form and not a poet's own picture.","mm":1},{"a":"so:1962","b":"s:269","type":"qualifies","note":"1962 gives the animals' pain at the killers' hands; Siddhar 269 gives the killers' own end, when Death seizes them for the sake of the body's pot.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Siddhar says the killer will answer to Death, while the Soolamani implies what answering means: birth as the beast he hunted.","gn":["The animals' sufferings gain a reckoning: the Siddhar shows who comes to answer for them, at death.","The Siddhar's threat of Death gains a content: the Soolamani says what follows death, rebirth among the beasts."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:1965","b":"k:259","type":"qualifies","note":"1965 lists animals killed as offerings to deities among their torments; Kural 259 weighs a thousand oblations against not killing one creature and eating.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"The Kural ranks abstention above sacrifice, and the Soolamani shows from the victim's side what the offering costs.","gn":["The sacrificed animal gains a ranking: the Kural says the rite is worth less than leaving the animal alone.","The Kural gains the victim's side: the epic puts the offered animal among the beasts' sufferings, a cost the couplet leaves unsaid."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:1967","b":"k:274","type":"qualifies","note":"1967 says that those who take austerity and then practise deceit are born as beasts; Kural 274 pictures the false ascetic as a hunter hidden in a bush, snaring birds.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"In the Kural the hypocrite hunts the innocent, and the Soolamani turns him into the hunted: the same fault, paid back in the animal's birth.","gn":["The hypocrite's rebirth gains an image of what he did: the hunter in hiding whom the Kural names.","The Kural's hunter gains a sequel: the epic says what becomes of him, since it is the beast's form."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:1967","b":"t:1659","type":"develops","note":"1967 sends the false ascetic to an animal birth; Tirumanthiram 1659 sends those of false austerity to hell and says austerities hold only through true knowledge.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"Jain and Saiva texts agree that false austerity is punished and differ on the place: a beast's birth in the one, hell in the other.","gn":["The false ascetic's fate gains a rival account, hell for the Saiva text, that shows the animal birth is a Jain choice.","The Tirumanthiram's hell gains a Jain counterpart in which the punishment is a rebirth that can still be left."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:1970","b":"t:2558","type":"qualifies","note":"1970 sends those who deny soul and rebirth to the nigoda state; Tirumanthiram 2558 sends those who say there is no higher state to hell.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"Both texts answer denial with a bad end and not with argument, but one end is a lower birth and the other is hell.","gn":["The Jain threat gains a Saiva twin, showing that sending the denier to a bad end is a shared habit of polemic.","The Tirumanthiram's denial gains a content: the Soolamani says what is denied, the soul, rebirth and the difference of good and bad."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:1975","b":"k:1071","type":"qualifies","note":"1975 says that a people is human only in the bare fact of birth and otherwise animal for want of shame and conduct; Kural 1071 says the base look just like human beings.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"The Kural tests humanness by conduct in each person, while the Soolamani ties the same test to a whole island people's birth.","gn":["The island people's humanity gains a conduct test: the Kural makes the difference lie in what a person does, not in a land.","The Kural's resemblance gains a case where the look-alike is a named people, not a type of person."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:1977","b":"so:1973","type":"develops","note":"1973 puts the Milechchar of the islands among the animals; 1977 adds that even those of human form among them, who live by cruelty, are brought down.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The classing with animals in 1973 is widened in 1977 to cover people of human form, so the test shifts from form to livelihood.","mm":1},{"a":"so:1984","b":"so:1982","type":"develops","note":"1982 refuses the name 'people' to those without discernment; 1984 adds the eunuch, the blind and woman's birth to those not counted as fully human.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The speaker's two tests of being human are discernment and the kind of birth, and the second does not follow from the first.","mm":1},{"a":"so:1989","b":"so:1990","type":"develops","note":"1989 shows household love bringing sufferings with its ties; 1990 offers vows as the one lasting course for those born in this state.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The sage sets vows as the answer to the pain that household desire cannot remove, which 1989 only states.","mm":1},{"a":"so:1998","b":"so:1999","type":"develops","note":"1998 refuses merit to giving harmful things to evil-doers; 1999 compares such a giver to one who feeds the cruel and asks their service.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The comparison turns a moral rule about giving into a plain foolishness, so a listener sees harm to the giver and not only a fault.","mm":1},{"a":"so:1992","b":"so:2000","type":"qualifies","note":"1992 grades gifts into best, middle and lowest by receiver, giver and gift; 2000 says gifts of one who knows the principles are not placed within three grades.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The gift of the knowing giver is said to go beyond the three-grade scheme, so the grading does not describe the highest case.","mm":1},{"a":"so:1997","b":"so:1843","type":"develops","note":"1843 has Payapati say that his umbrellas and bowing kings are a gift of his good-deed deity; 1997 says the givers of food become kings with armies and umbrellas.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The earlier remark that kingship is earned acquires a stated source: a gift of food to the hungry becomes the umbrella.","mm":1},{"a":"so:1992","b":"t:508","type":"tension","note":"1992 grades a gift by the receiver's fault-lessness, the giver and the gift; Tirumanthiram 508 sends giver and receiver to hell if the receiver does not bow in worship of the Lord.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The two ask what makes a receiver worthy and give different answers, so one cannot tell from either how to judge a fit receiver.","gn":["The Jain scheme lacks a test of worship: it names no receiver whom a gift would harm the giver by reaching.","The Saiva verse lacks a grading of gifts and a role for the giver's own qualities, which it reduces to the receiver's bowing."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:1992","b":"t:505","type":"qualifies","note":"1992 treats giving as graded with a lowest grade; Tirumanthiram 505 says gifts to those without conduct and vows are like milking a barren cow.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The lowest grade of giving may still be a gift, and the Tirumanthiram says that at some point there is no crop.","gn":["The grading gains a limit below which a gift is wasted, which 1992 does not name.","The barren-cow image gains a scale: the poem shows that giving is not simply worth or not worth, but graded."],"gr":2,"mm":1},{"a":"so:2000","b":"t:1867","type":"qualifies","note":"2000 says the gifts of one who knows the true principles lead him among the best gods; Tirumanthiram 1867 says charity to tireless ascetics sinks deep karma and becomes the golden world.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"One text places the fruit of giving in a higher birth, and the other in the removal of karma, which are different ends.","gn":["The Jain verse gains a different reward, since removal of karma is not what it names, only a birth among gods.","The Saiva verse gains a condition: here the giver's knowledge of the principles is part of the reward, not only the receiver's austerity."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:1988","b":"n:60","type":"qualifies","note":"1988 pictures pleasure as honey tasted by a man hanging above a pit with an elephant and a snake; Naladiyar 60 says the higher ones see the pain in any pleasure and do not cling.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"The parable explains why fools do not renounce: the honey is real, and the danger is out of sight while one tastes it.","gn":["The parable gains a statement of what the wise do while the honey drops, which the image does not give.","The quatrain gains an image for the fool's blindness, so that his not renouncing is shown as enjoyment of a real sweetness."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:1985","b":"n:34","type":"develops","note":"1985 says only those who hold firmly to good karma come to the human body, out of eighty-four lakh births; Naladiyar 34 says to take the great fruit of the rare body.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The epic gives the count and cause of the rare body that the quatrain only calls rare, so the urgency has a reason.","gn":["The count of births gains a use: the rare body is to be pressed for fruit like cane.","The 'rare body' gains a measure and a cause: eighty-four lakh births and firm good karma."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:1987","b":"n:11","type":"develops","note":"1987 draws childhood, youth and old age as a wilderness ending in another state; Naladiyar 11 says the wise renounced in childhood, knowing grey hair would come.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The epic shows what lies past old age, another birth, and so the quatrain's renunciation at the start is not a mere fear of aging.","gn":["The wilderness of life gains an answer: renounce early, before the other state.","Old age gains a sequel in another birth, which the quatrain's staff and grey hair do not name."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:1997","b":"k:222","type":"qualifies","note":"1997 promises the giver of food a later kingship; Kural 222 says that even if there were no higher world, giving alone is good.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The epic's giving is promised its fruit, and the Kural says it is worth doing without one, so the reward is a bonus to a duty and not its ground.","gn":["The promise of kingship gains a test: would the giving stand if there were no later birth.","The Kural's 'even if no higher world' gains a case of the world's usual motive, the later kingship."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:2003","b":"so:1862","type":"develops","note":"1862 has the minister name the austere sage's feet as the only refuge; 2003 is the sage's first reply, that austerity is hard to say even for sages.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The refuge sought in 1862 answers only by admitting that the thing sought is hard to put into words, so the king must be taught in steps.","mm":1},{"a":"so:2005","b":"so:1842","type":"develops","note":"1842 has the king ask for more seed to sow for austerity; 2005 calls the harvest of austerity a wealth that grows in kinds.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The sage turns the king's idea of sowing into a promise that the austerity crop multiplies, so the vow rests on growth and not only loss.","mm":1},{"a":"so:2002","b":"k:325","type":"qualifies","note":"2002 offers the vow of not killing to kings as one that leaves no conquest out of reach; Kural 325 ranks not-killing highest among those who renounced.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The epic recasts non-killing as a king's way to win, where the couplet ranks it only among renouncers.","gn":["The sage's claim for kings gains a ranking from the Kural, which sets non-killing above every other renunciation.","The couplet gains a ruler as its audience: a king who keeps the vow and still seeks conquest, which it never considers."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:2009","b":"k:315","type":"develops","note":"2009 defines compassion as melting for another's life and counts a moist heart where rescue fails; Kural 315 calls understanding useless without guarding another's pain as one's own.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The pairing shows compassion as both the test of understanding and a state that stays even when help fails.","gn":["Compassion gains a test, since understanding is judged worthless without it.","The couplet gains an allowance for failure: the moist heart still counts where the pain cannot be removed."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:2010","b":"k:151","type":"qualifies","note":"2010 asks equal calm toward abuse, praise and neglect; Kural 151 asks the bearing of scorn only.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"Level calm includes praise, so the couplet's forbearance covers only half of what the sage asks of the clear-minded.","gn":["Equanimity gains the image of the earth that bears the digger, a reason to take scorn without answer.","Forbearance gains the other side, praise, which also stirs the heart and needs the same steadiness."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:2014","b":"k:423","type":"develops","note":"2014 makes hearing the meaning of the texts a lamp that clears delusion; Kural 423 makes wisdom seeing the true thing in whatever is heard.","grade":"weak","better":"qualifies","te":"The sage trusts the text as source, where the couplet makes the hearer's seeing the test of any source.","gn":["Knowledge from hearing gains a check, seeing what is true in the words and not trusting the speaker.","The couplet gains an end for its seeing: the clearing of the darkness that binds one to deeds."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:2018","b":"so:2014","type":"same_image","note":"2014 calls knowledge from the texts a lamp; 2018 calls seeing the true thing a lamp of vision that ends the dark.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"One lamp is lit twice, once by hearing and once by seeing, so the sage joins the two as stages of one light.","mm":1},{"a":"so:2018","b":"n:51","type":"same_image","note":"2018 has the lamp of vision end the darkness of deeds; Naladiyar 51 has the lamp of austerity end sin as darkness dies, and darkness return when the oil runs out.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The lamp's light is credited to vision in the epic and to austerity in the quatrain, with only the quatrain saying the light can fail.","gn":["The lamp of vision gains the warning of the oil, that light can go out unless fed.","The quatrain's lamp gains a source in the knowing of the true thing, where it names only austerity."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:2018","b":"t:116","type":"tension","note":"2018 has vision destroy karma's darkness by itself; Tirumanthiram 116 has Nandi remove the malas as fire rises from bamboo, out of grace.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Release by one's own seeing, without any god, meets release by a lord's grace, and the same fire-and-darkness language serves both.","gn":["The lamp of vision gains a rival cause, grace, for the same burning away of the dark.","The grace-fire gains the Jain demand that the soul's own seeing bear the work, so the cause is not left wholly with the lord."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:2015","b":"t:598","type":"tension","note":"2015 sorts meditation into four kinds by their fruit, two bad and two leading to heaven and release; Tirumanthiram 598 gives three objects of meditation, Shakti, the Supreme and Siva as guru.","grade":"forced","better":"parallel_domain","te":"The two works sort meditation differently, by moral fruit in the epic and by the divine object in the Saiva text.","gn":["The fourfold sorting gains named objects, since it never says what is to be fixed upon.","The Saiva list gains a test of fruit, that some meditation leads astray as well as upward."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:2017","b":"k:356","type":"qualifies","note":"2017 says those with the lamp of vision reach heaven or the bliss without return even without vows; Kural 356 says those who learn and see the true thing reach the path of no return.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The epic loosens the couplet's chain, holding that sight alone, even without vows, opens heaven or release.","gn":["The vowless seer gains a precedent, since the couplet already gives seeing the true thing the path of no return.","The couplet gains the claim that vows are not required, which it never says, and the lesser reward of heaven."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:2024","b":"k:37","type":"develops","note":"2024 ranks beings best, middle and lowest by the fruit of earlier good deeds; Kural 37 points to the bearer and the rider of a palanquin as the visible proof of virtue's fruit.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Rank that Kural 37 reads from one palanquin is stretched in 2024 into a ladder of births, so one visible difference becomes a whole order of worlds.","gn":["The ranking of beings gets a plain proof from daily sight, the palanquin, in place of a bare claim about the fruit of deeds.","The couplet gains a longer horizon: the difference it sees between rider and bearer is only the near end of a graded series of births."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:2031","b":"k:1082","type":"same_image","note":"2031 has the youth's lotus-like eyes and the girl's blue-lily eyes meet and press; Kural 1082 has the girl's answering look strike like an army.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain","te":"The same meeting of eyes is a fated, gentle union in the enjoyment land but a conquest in the Kural's world of passion.","gn":["The meeting look gains the sense of an attack that ends the other's resistance, which the epic's soft image of lilies leaves out.","The couplet gains a setting where the look leads to fulfilment with no cost and no shame, unlike the pain the Kural's lover goes on to feel."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:2033","b":"n:16","type":"tension","note":"2033 gives the pair unbroken pleasure for their whole span; Naladiyar 16 says the joy of youth is fleeting like the lamb eating the garland before the slaughter.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The epic can promise pleasure that lasts a full span only by moving it out of this world, which is exactly what the Naladiyar's warning denies is possible here.","gn":["The long pleasure gains a warning that without a world apart it would end as the lamb's does, which the verse's calm account does not say.","The quatrain gains a counter-picture: a place where youth's joy is not a trap, though it too ends in the next verses with the pair's rise."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:2034","b":"k:42","type":"develops","note":"2034 says the painless pleasure is the fruit of what was given to austere kings; Kural 42 makes the householder the support of those who have let go.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"A householder's gift to renouncers, a duty in the Kural, is shown in the epic as the cause of a later birth in a land of ease.","gn":["The pleasure gains a stated cause in an act of giving that any householder can do now, not only in a distant rank of merit.","The couplet gains a reward beyond the support of the renouncer: the giver's next birth, which the couplet leaves unsaid."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:2038","b":"so:2035","type":"develops","note":"2035 has the human pair rise to heaven after their span; 2038 says the beasts and birds of the same land rise to the kalpa world as well.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The rise of the pair is not a human privilege: the land's own merit carries even its animals up, so the climb belongs to the place.","mm":1},{"a":"so:2060","b":"k:346","type":"develops","note":"2060 says heaven's pleasure comes by austerity yet ends, and that something is higher; Kural 346 says one who cuts off 'I' and 'mine' enters a world above the gods'.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet's 'higher than the gods' is given a reason in the epic: the gods' world has a measured end, so only what lies above it can be a goal.","gn":["The vague higher state is given a means, the cutting of ownership and the self, which the verse leaves unnamed.","The couplet gains a ground for ranking the gods' world below renunciation: its joys are counted in years and end."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:2060","b":"t:184","type":"qualifies","note":"2060 says heaven's wealth ends; Tirumanthiram 184 says time cuts down both the heaven-bound and the deed-bound.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"Both deny that heaven is lasting, but the epic counts the ending in fixed spans of seas and tells of a state beyond, while Tirumular makes time's cutting a general law with no way out in that line.","gn":["The ending of heaven gets a law of time that reaches everyone, in place of a bare statement of limit.","The cutting down by time gets a scale, from a palya to thirty-three seas in 2061 to 2063, and a way above it by austerity."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:2051","b":"k:50","type":"qualifies","note":"2051 itemises the gods' mansions, couches and cars as the whole of heaven; Kural 50 says the one who lives life as it ought to be lived will be counted among the gods.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"What the couplet offers as the reward of right householding is shown by the epic as a list of comforts and then, in 2060, as a reward that has an end.","gn":["The gods' world gets a stated goal in a householder's right life, which its inventory alone does not give.","The couplet gains content for 'among the gods' and a limit on how far the reward goes."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:2052","b":"n:137","type":"qualifies","note":"2052 lists the gods' play, Indra's company and jewelled terraces as heaven's whole stock; Naladiyar 137 asks whether heaven's city is sweeter than the gathering of learned men.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"Naladiyar weighs heaven against the company of the learned and finds it no sweeter; the epic describes the gods' pleasures as only entertainment and ranks them below the teaching of 2067.","gn":["The gods' amusements get a human measure by which they look modest.","The comparison gains a full picture of what heaven's pleasures consist of, so the contrast is not left abstract."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:2065","b":"t:266","type":"tension","note":"2065 says the highest gods have no lord over them and none greater; Tirumanthiram 266 says the ripened see the Lord's feet and the resolute rule his heaven.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The epic's top heaven stands under no ruling god, while Tirumular's heaven is the Lord's own; the conflict depends on reading கோனிலா as 'with no king above' and on taking 'his heaven' as lordship, not only as place.","gn":["The claim of no lord over the highest gods meets a rival picture of a heaven that belongs to a Lord and is entered by grace.","The Lord's heaven meets a counter-picture where beings of the highest rank answer to no one."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:2047","b":"so:2000","type":"develops","note":"2000 says gifts made by one who has grasped the true principles raise the giver among the best of the gods; 2047 names who these are: householders who give, avoid wine and flesh and stay true to the beloved.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"2047 widens the ground of 2000 from giving alone to abstinence and faithfulness, so the householder's whole conduct is what raises him.","mm":1},{"a":"so:2067","b":"so:2057","type":"develops","note":"2057 says right vision is easy for the great and rare for others; 2067 says that standing in right vision with austerity keeps one from hell and animal births.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"2067 turns the rarity of right vision into a promise: who holds it with austerity escapes the lower births and keeps unfailing wealth.","mm":1},{"a":"so:2068","b":"so:2060","type":"develops","note":"2060 hints at a state above heaven's ending joy; 2068 shows the sage, once the heavens are told, setting out how to reach endless bliss.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The hint of a state beyond heaven's end becomes, in the sage's closing, the thing the teaching moves toward: bliss that has no end.","gr":2,"mm":1},{"a":"so:2070","b":"k:349","type":"develops","note":"2070 traces how birth turns to desire and hatred toward sense objects, heaps up deeds and brings the same nature back after death; Kural 349 says that the very moment attachment ends, births are cut.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet's moment of cutting is shown in 2070 to be the point where the chain from senses to desire to deed to return is broken.","gn":["The chain of sense, craving, deed and rebirth gets a plain statement of where it ends, which 2070 leaves unsaid.","The couplet gets the working of the births it cuts: attachment makes deeds, and the deeds are what bring the return."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:2072","b":"t:126","type":"qualifies","note":"2072 has the hero reach release by his own vision, knowledge, mastered mind and long meditation; Tirumanthiram 126 has the freed ones climb the thirty-six tattvas as a ladder to release, enter the light of bliss and see Sivam.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain","te":"Both texts describe release as a climb by stages, but 2072 ends in a world of release won by the self's own effort while Tirumular's ladder ends in seeing Sivam.","gn":["The hero's path gets a counter-picture in which the top of the ladder is a seen god, which the verse, with no god in it, does not have.","The ladder gets a way of release in which the climber is complete without the vision of a lord, which the couplet does not allow."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:2074","b":"t:325","type":"qualifies","note":"2074 uses three pleasant senses, jewel-light, sandal coolness and flower scent, for the bliss of release, which it calls சிவகதி; Tirumanthiram 325 uses a drink, the nectar of Siva's bliss in samadhi, for the same kind of state.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"Both reach for sensory pictures of a state they say is beyond the senses, but the Jain verse's சிவகதி names a state of release while Tirumular's Siva is the god whose bliss it is.","gn":["The comparison to light, cool and scent gets a drink image that shows the bliss as unbroken from sitting to lying, which 2074 does not say.","The drink of bliss gets a calmer set of images that carry no hint of the intoxication Tirumular is guarding against in this chapter."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:2075","b":"so:355","type":"develops","note":"2075 has the king resolve, after hearing the teacher, to strive for the final world; verse 355 has the counsellor repeat the sage's word that the hero will not be dazzled by kings' wealth and will reach the state of release by austerity.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The sage's early prediction that the king will refuse kings' wealth is kept in 2075, where the king himself declares the resolve.","mm":1},{"a":"so:2072","b":"so:2018","type":"develops","note":"2018 names the lamp of vision as seeing the true thing and says that, once lit, the darkness of deeds cannot stay; 2072 shows the path in which vision and knowledge, with meditation, lead on to the world of release.","grade":"weak","better":"qualifies","te":"The lamp of vision in 2018 is a first step; 2072 sets out what must follow it before the deeds are gone and release is reached.","mm":1},{"a":"so:2079","b":"n:53","type":"qualifies","note":"2079 has the king's own ministers urge him to give up the crown for the austerity that ends the disease of birth; Naladiyar 53 says the best people, seeing that home, youth, wealth and strength do not last, renounce them without delay.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"Naladiyar 53 describes a person giving up a house and wealth, but 2079 shows the same renunciation asked of a king, where the crown and a kingdom's charge must be laid down.","gn":["The ministers' plea gets a general rule, that the wise renounce on seeing that things do not last, to rest on.","The quatrain gets a case in which what is given up is a kingdom and its cost to others, which a householder's renunciation does not show."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:2082","b":"k:332","type":"develops","note":"2082 makes wealth a woman who leaves the men she has joined and always wants someone new; Kural 332 says great wealth comes like a crowd gathering at a dance hall and goes like the crowd breaking up.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet's crowd goes without any reason in itself; in 2082 the going has a will, the goddess who tires of her man and wants another.","gn":["The goddess's leaving gets an image of a crowd that comes and goes for its own reasons and owes the owner nothing.","The crowd gets a person with a wish behind its going, so wealth's departure reads as desertion and not only as an end."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:2083","b":"n:371","type":"same_image","note":"2083 compares the departing goddess of wealth to courtesans who leave the penniless; Naladiyar 371 says harlots' friendship ends when the hand is empty, as a lamp's light ends when the oil is gone.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The epic turns the Naladiyar's warning about courtesans into a figure for wealth itself, so a man is asked to see in his fortune the same cold arithmetic.","gn":["The goddess's leaving gets a plain reason, that the love was only for what the hand held, which 2083 leaves implied.","The warning about courtesans gets a larger target: the same trade-friendship is shown to run through wealth and fortune, not only through women."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:2086","b":"t:169","type":"qualifies","note":"2086 tells the sons not to trust the goddess and to love the learned, whom she scorns; Tirumanthiram 169 calls bewildering wealth a waning moon and bids the reader seek the king of the gods, who becomes great wealth like a rain cloud.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Both give up trusting wealth but name different things to trust in its place: the Jain king names the learned, Tirumular names a god who is himself wealth.","gn":["The advice to attach to the learned gets a rival answer, a lord who stays, so a reader sees that the choice of refuge is itself a doctrine.","The rain-cloud wealth of the Lord gets a worldly alternative in which the refuge is people, not a god, and it is held without worship."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:2088","b":"s:286","type":"same_image","note":"2088 says the earth has enjoyed countless men and left them, and has not stayed with the next; Siddhar 286 says crowned kings are burnt to a fistful of ash and become earth.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The two reverse the same image: the king thinks he holds the earth, but 2088 says she leaves him and the Siddhar says he is returned into her.","gn":["The earth that deserts kings gets an ending, that the kings she leaves are laid in her as ash, which the verse does not state.","The Siddhar's ash gets a living figure for earth, the woman who goes from king to king, so the fact of burning is given a reason."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:2093","b":"k:342","type":"develops","note":"2093 has the king say his own days are few and he has resolved today; Kural 342 says renounce while there is time.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural's urgency to renounce in time becomes, in the epic, a king's own reckoning of his remaining days against those of his people.","gn":["The king's haste gets a rule from outside the story: the time to renounce is limited and it is wiser to act before it runs out.","The couplet gains a person who is at once a ruler and a father, whose leaving has to be explained to those he leaves."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:2094","b":"so:2106","type":"develops","note":"In 2094 the princes ask what fault they have done; in 2106 they call their quarrelsome prattle foolish and bow to the lord's grace.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The sons' first grief as a charge against themselves turns, after the sages' consolation, into acceptance of the king's act as grace.","mm":1},{"a":"so:2096","b":"k:345","type":"develops","note":"2096 has the ministers put down the heavy burden they bore through the disease of birth; Kural 345 says that for those set on cutting off births even the body is excess.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The burden of the body in the epic is lifted by a king's grace, whereas the couplet lays it on the renouncer's own will.","gn":["The ministers' burden gets a stated reason, that the body itself is surplus when one wants to end births.","The couplet gains a scene of a king's court letting go together, where the renouncer is no lone figure."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:2098","b":"cc:3035","type":"same_image","note":"2098 has gods carry the king's crown and cast it into the milk sea; Cintamani 3035 has gods lift the tray with Civakan's hair and let it fall into the milk sea.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Both Jain epics end a royal career with the same act, gods taking the shed sign of rank to the milk sea, so the image reads as a convention for a king's completed renunciation.","mm":1},{"a":"so:2099","b":"n:21","type":"qualifies","note":"2099 has a king give army and earth to his sons and put off his crown by choice; Naladiyar 21 says even kings under umbrellas have been carried off as dead.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain","te":"The same fall of a king from rank that Naladiyar reads as death's work is here done by the king's own hand while alive.","gn":["The king's laying aside of the crown gets the contrast of what happens to kings who wait to be taken by death.","The quatrain gains a king who is not carried off but leaves before he must."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:2099","b":"t:168","type":"qualifies","note":"2099 has the king leave wealth, army and earth to his sons; Tirumanthiram 168 says join the true wealth before the elephants, chariots and wealth pass to others.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain","te":"Both tell a king to leave his wealth while he lives, but the epic hands it to sons and takes no god, while Tirumular names the one to join.","gn":["The king's giving of the realm to his sons gets a rival reading, in which the wealth should be exchanged for the lord, not left to heirs.","The Tirumanthiram verse gains a case where the same urging is met without a god, by a vow and a handing over to the next generation."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:2101","b":"k:347","type":"qualifies","note":"2101 shows the princes wailing as the crown falls from the lord; Kural 347 says griefs hold those who hold attachment.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The princes' grief shows the couplet's point from the other side: the renouncer is free while those who still cling to him suffer.","gn":["The princes' sorrow is named as the cost of their own attachment, not only of his act.","The couplet gains persons whose grief arises from clinging to another renouncer, not to things."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:2109","b":"k:343","type":"qualifies","note":"2109 posts five great captains of the vow-army against incoming karma; Kural 343 says the domain of the five must be crushed and all desire let go.","grade":"forced","better":"same_image","te":"The epic's five are vows that hold karma back, not the five senses of the couplet, so the same number is taken from two different disciplines.","gn":["The five vows gain the older frame of crushing the five senses, which shows what they guard against.","The couplet gains a Jain version in which the defence is a set of vows and an army, not a single act of crushing."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:2111","b":"n:59","type":"develops","note":"2111 shuts the five sense-gates of a fortress and posts guards; Naladiyar 59 names the five gates (body, mouth, eyes, nose, ears) and promises release to one who can guard and steer the craving that comes through them.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Guarding the senses is shown as one wall among several defences and not a lone act, so restraint looks like the start of a campaign and not its end.","gn":["The gates, which the fortress leaves as an unnamed row, are named as the five organs through which craving enters.","The rule that guarding the gates leads to release gains walls, armed guards and a mind kept within, so sense-restraint is pictured as part of a larger war."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:2113","b":"k:24","type":"same_image","note":"2113 has the lord ride the elephant of knowledge with a diamond goad that is awareness; Kural 24 guards the five with the goad of firmness.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The same goad turns from a means of guarding the senses to a means of driving the army that kills the karmic enemy.","gn":["The goad is no longer a guard against the senses but a driver of an assault, which the couplet leaves out.","The couplet's goad gains the elephant it only implies, and gains a battle for the elephant to carry the rider into."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:2115","b":"nk:86","type":"develops","note":"2115 has the lord draw the sword of unbroken meditation soaked in samadhi; Neelakesi 86 says that humans of the land of deeds see their dark karma leave by penance.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"Both Jain epics say karma is removed by the ascetic's own effort, and one gives that effort a face as a sword.","mm":1},{"a":"so:2117","b":"s:87","type":"qualifies","note":"2117 kills anger and its fellows one after another in a fixed sequence; Siddhar 87 asks when anger, speech and action, the 'I' and the mind will die so that release can be reached.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Jain epic gives a battle order for the passions where the Siddhar only longs for them to die all at once.","gn":["The passions, which 2117 names but leaves as figures in a rout, are made into an ordered list the seeker must wait out, with the mind and the 'I' added at the end.","The Siddhar's longing gains a plan, in which anger falls first and the others follow in turn, in place of a single wish for the end of all."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:2124","b":"k:350","type":"qualifies","note":"2124 throws away the sword of meditation once the karmic kings are dead; Kural 350 says to cling to the clinging of the one who has none, so as to let clinging go.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain","te":"A useful hold, whether the sword or the clinging to the unclinging one, is to be dropped at the end of its work.","gn":["The throwing away of the sword gives the Kural's paradox of a last clinging a scene in which the tool is dropped and an entry follows.","The couplet's holding that lets go gains a picture of the very moment the tool is dropped, and of what comes after, an embrace and a city."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:2124","b":"cc:3117","type":"same_image","note":"2124 has the liberated one embrace the maiden called முடிவு; Cintamani 3117 has the king embrace the maiden Kevala with unblinking eyes.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Both Jain epics speak of release as a bride, but the Soolamani's bride is the end of birth and the Cintamani's is knowledge.","mm":1},{"a":"so:2125","b":"cc:3082","type":"same_image","note":"2125 has the freed soul swallow the world and non-world together once the coverings are gone; Cintamani 3082 has the hero swallow the three worlds and the world of non-world after the four enemy kings fall.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The same formula for omniscience appears in both Jain epics, and only the Soolamani names the removal of coverings as its cause.","mm":1},{"a":"so:2125","b":"t:6","type":"tension","note":"2125 places the liberated soul in the state called சிவகதி and wins it by its own meditation; Tirumanthiram 6 says apart from the Lord there is no hard penance that can achieve and no way into his city.","grade":"wrong","better":null,"te":"The two texts both speak of an unreturning state but disagree on whether penance alone reaches it or only the Lord opens the way.","gn":["The state of release, which the Soolamani reaches by effort alone, is set against a view in which that effort is useless without the Lord.","The claim that no penance achieves without him gains a rival case in which a hero wins release by his own meditation with no god acting."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:2127","b":"t:345","type":"tension","note":"2127 has Death and Desire flee the hero who wins by full knowledge; Tirumanthiram 345 tells of burning Death in the fire of yoga at Kadavur.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain","te":"Both texts have Death beaten, but one by a man's own knowledge and the other by a god's fire.","gn":["Death's defeat, which the Soolamani gives to an ascetic's knowledge, is set against a defeat worked by the Lord.","The yoga-fire gains a human counterpart who beats Death without any god, so the Kadavur tale is no longer the only way to beat Death."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:2127","b":"so:1","type":"develops","note":"2127 calls the lord who has conquered his enemies the crest-jewel of the world; Soolamani 1 opens by praising the one who conquered the whole sum of his deeds and now stands as a shining form.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The close of the poem answers its opening, so the praise promised at the start is now earned by the war just fought.","mm":1},{"a":"so:2130","b":"so:2","type":"develops","note":"2130 shows the dark and white princes worshipping under the lotus feet of the liberated lord; Soolamani 2 says the story is of the one who in an earlier day went to him for refuge.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The refuge sought at the beginning becomes the festival of worship at the end, which gives the story a completed arc.","mm":1},{"a":"so:2121","b":"cc:3090","type":"same_image","note":"2121 has the sky filled with the gods' regalia, flowers, sandal and powder; Cintamani 3090 has the gods rain flowers, coloured sandal and incense that fill the quarters.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The same festival of gods is set at the end of the war in the Soolamani and at the teaching in the Cintamani, so it honours victory in one and instruction in the other.","mm":1},{"a":"so:2129","b":"k:547","type":"qualifies","note":"2129 has Tirumal lift a straight sceptre with the gods guarding him; Kural 547 says the king guards the world and justice guards him if he does not falter.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The guard of a king comes in the epic from the gods and in the couplet from his own justice, so the pairing asks what really protects a ruler.","gn":["The gods' protection, which the epic gives as a plain fact, is made to depend on the rule of a straight sceptre.","The couplet's guard of justice gains a mythic setting in which the gods are the guards."],"mm":1},{"a":"so:2115","b":"t:121","type":"qualifies","note":"2115 has samadhi give the sword that cuts the foe; Tirumanthiram 121 has the Siva-yogi cut bonds and remain with the body as if dead.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Samadhi is a weapon of conquest in the Jain epic and a quiet stillness in the Tirumanthiram, so the same state is used for opposite pictures.","gn":["Samadhi gains an aggressive purpose, in which it is the sword, where the Tirumanthiram uses it for rest.","The still, deathlike state gains a battle in which it is the weapon that ends the enemy."],"mm":1},{"a":"ud:1","b":"k:4","type":"qualifies","note":"The poet bows to a named tirthankara; the couplet praises the feet of one free of liking and aversion without naming him.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The Jain epic gives a name and a seat under the pindi tree to the one the Kural leaves unnamed, so the same freedom from liking and aversion can be read as a particular lord.","gn":["the invocation gets a stated quality of the one worshipped, freedom from liking and aversion, which the verse leaves unsaid","the couplet gets a named, seated figure that it never gives"],"mm":1},{"a":"ud:3","b":"cc:4","type":"same_image","note":"Both poets ask the learned to take a flawed work for its worth, using a gem as the image.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"A gem image serves the Jain poets as a standing apology, one for flaws washed off and the other for plain wrapping carrying value.","mm":1},{"a":"ud:3","b":"ng:3","type":"agrees","note":"The Naga poet's lamp kept despite its smoke is the same plea for lenient reading.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The minor epics repeat the formula of asking that a work's worth outweigh its faults, each choosing a different household object.","gr":2,"mm":1},{"a":"ud:4","b":"ng:4","type":"develops","note":"Both open with the shedding of former deeds; this verse adds the order and ripening.","grade":"weak","better":"qualifies","te":"The Jain account of karma shedding that the Naga poem states in a line is here set out as an ordered ripening.","mm":1},{"a":"ud:4","b":"k:380","type":"qualifies","note":"The verse allows deeds to be shed in order, where the couplet says fate gets there first whatever one devises.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The Jain verse leaves room for undoing deeds that the Kural's fate overrides, though the dispute is over what one can do to a deed.","gn":["the shedding of deeds is set against a strong claim of fate that it must answer","the couplet gets a doctrine on which fate can be worn down"],"mm":1},{"a":"ud:13","b":"ud:14","type":"qualifies","note":"The seizing bird that threatens the queen in verse 13 is shown in verse 14 to set her down unharmed.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"The bird's danger in 13 is answered in 14, so the abduction is told as a passage to the hermitage and not as an injury.","mm":1},{"a":"ud:18","b":"ud:19","type":"develops","note":"The lute the youth receives from the sage in verse 18 is the instrument that tames the elephant in 19.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The gift of grace in 18 is shown working at once, so the lute is the means by which Udayanan wins allies.","mm":1},{"a":"ud:19","b":"ud:20","type":"develops","note":"The elephant that bows at his lute in verse 19 pledges itself to him in a dream in verse 20.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The taming ends in a bond of loyalty, so the music wins a companion and not only a mount.","mm":1},{"a":"ud:22","b":"ud:23","type":"develops","note":"Vikraman's resolve to take up ascetic life in verse 22 is carried out in verse 23 when he goes into the forest.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"A resolve voiced in one verse is kept in the next, with the kingdom handed over first.","gr":2,"mm":1},{"a":"ud:21","b":"k:61","type":"qualifies","note":"Vikraman's grief at having no child (verse 21) meets the Kural's praise of children who know what should be known, and the epic gives him a sister's son in place of his own.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The epic shows that the heir the Kural praises can come by a sister's son, not only by birth, and that the want of a child can lead to renunciation.","gn":["The verse's grief at being childless gets a standard by which an heir is valued, which the epic's king only feels as loss.","The couplet's praise of knowing children gets a case where the lack of one's own child is answered by taking a nephew as heir."],"mm":1},{"a":"ud:23","b":"n:53","type":"develops","note":"Vikraman gives up his kingdom and goes to the forest, as the quatrain's best of men renounce home and wealth on seeing they do not last.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"The epic shows a king doing what the quatrain says, with the order of arranging his heir first and then leaving, which the quatrain does not mention.","gn":["The epic's king gets the reason for his act, the failing of home and wealth, which the verse does not say.","The quatrain's renouncer gets a concrete case, a king who provides for his kingdom before he goes."],"mm":1},{"a":"ud:23","b":"cc:395","type":"qualifies","note":"Vikraman leaves his kingdom to a nephew he has just found, while the Cintamani's Lokamapalan sets his young son on the throne and takes the ascetics' guise.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain","te":"Both epics tell of a king handing over rule and turning ascetic, but here the heir is a sister's son and the turn follows childlessness, where the Cintamani's follows the setting up of his own son.","mm":1},{"a":"ud:27","b":"cc:2883","type":"develops","note":"Sathanikan crowns the heir first and then turns ascetic, in the order the Cintamani's chariot-king lays down: guard the kingdom, give it to a worthy son, and only then renounce.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"The Cintamani states as advice the order that this king follows in the story, and the epic adds that he passes over his two named sons for Udayanan.","mm":1},{"a":"ud:27","b":"n:21","type":"qualifies","note":"Sathanikan gives up the throne while living, where the quatrain says kings under the umbrella on elephants are carried off as dead.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The epic's king keeps his rank by choosing to leave it before death, which the quatrain does not allow for.","gn":["The king's leaving gets a sharper meaning, since the quatrain says death is how all kings finally lose the umbrella.","The quatrain's claim that none escape gets a qualification: one can leave the throne by renouncing before it is taken."],"mm":1},{"a":"ud:5","b":"k:545","type":"qualifies","note":"Veeranathan's rain of virtue in verse 5 is set beside the Kural's rain that comes where the king rules justly.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The epic gives the rain to the tirthankara's teaching, where the Kural makes it the reward of the ruler's sceptre.","gn":["The epic's rain of virtue gets the Kural's older idea that rain follows the justice of the ruler.","The couplet's rain of justice gets a second source: the lord's dharma, not the king, brings the fall."],"mm":1},{"a":"ud:30","b":"ud:46","type":"develops","note":"Udayana orders the land searched for his vanished divine elephant; the hunters, seeing the decoy, think it is our lord's elephant and run to him.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The search for the lost elephant is what makes the decoy believable, since his own people hurry to bring him the news.","mm":1},{"a":"ud:38","b":"ud:47","type":"develops","note":"The ministers settle on taking an elephant by showing one; Udayana walks into the grove and sees the false elephant.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The ministers' choice of lure in 38 is fitted to a king who is hunting for his elephant, which verse 47 shows working.","mm":1},{"a":"ud:35","b":"ud:48","type":"qualifies","note":"The ministers name his elephant-taming and his veena as his glory; in 48 he plays the veena at the decoy.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The skill that makes a man formidable is also his weak point, since his veena is what carries him to the trap.","mm":1},{"a":"ud:37","b":"ud:36","type":"qualifies","note":"The ministers list Udayana's merits to temper the king; the king answers by raging and ordering him seized.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"Telling an angry king a rival's merits does not calm him, it sends the ministers toward a plot.","mm":1},{"a":"ud:49","b":"ud:40","type":"develops","note":"Ninety-six armed men are put inside the machine in 40; in 49 they break out as it becomes a fighting host.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The machine of 40 is shown to be a hiding place for troops, and 49 is the moment it is turned into one.","mm":1},{"a":"ud:40","b":"k:678","type":"qualifies","note":"The Kural's picture is of binding a rutting elephant by means of an elephant; verse 40 builds an elephant of wood and hide, filled with ninety-six men, to take a king.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The ministers use the Kural's own method in a worse form, a false elephant with men inside and a king as the catch.","gn":["a rule for the ruse: using one thing of its own kind to take another, here a false elephant to catch a king who tames real ones","a case where the stand-in is a built shell with armed men inside and the quarry is a ruler, not a beast"],"mm":1},{"a":"ud:47","b":"k:531","type":"qualifies","note":"The Kural ranks the slackness of great joy below anger as a cause of ruin; in 47 the king goes on though omens and Vayantakan warn him.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural's slackness is not ignorance: Udayana is told and warned, and in the verse it is his gladness at sight of the elephant that wins.","gn":["a case where the heedless one has both omens and a friend's warning, so slackness is a choice made in delight, not a lapse of knowledge","a story in which joy's slackness is shown over a few verses, before the trap closes on a king and a friend who knew better"],"gr":2,"mm":1},{"a":"ud:36","b":"k:638","type":"qualifies","note":"The Kural makes it the duty of a minister at the king's side to tell him what is good for him; in 36 the ministers tell Pradyota of Udayana's merits.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The ministers do speak, as the Kural asks, but to a king in anger the truth ends in a plot, so the duty to speak does not guarantee it is heard.","gn":["a case in which ministers do speak in the king's presence and yet are turned aside","a test of the duty to speak when the king, angry, has no wish to be told good of his enemy"],"mm":1},{"a":"ud:37","b":"k:568","type":"qualifies","note":"The Kural says a king who does not think matters through with his circle and then lashes out in anger sees his fortune shrink; in 37 Pradyota rages after the ministers have spoken.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The king here has taken counsel and yet his anger still rules the outcome, so the Kural's warning is about whether counsel is heard, not just whether it is asked.","gn":["a king who does hear his circle and still gives way to rage, which the Kural's rule does not cover","a scene in which the king's anger leads at once to a plot rather than to a loss of fortune, as the couplet only warns"],"mm":1},{"a":"ud:57","b":"n:21","type":"qualifies","note":"The Naladiyar gives it as a rule that kings who rode in state are carried off and spoken of as dead; here a king's sword snaps and he is seized, but he is afterwards to be rescued.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Naladiyar's rule of the ruined proud king is true of the captive here, but the epic makes the ruin a seizure to be undone by a minister, not a final end.","gn":["The epic scene gains a general statement that kings in elephant-borne state are carried off, so the seizure reads as one case of the rule.","The quatrain gains a named king whose fall is not final, which the quatrain's claim that none are left over does not show."],"mm":1},{"a":"ud:66","b":"k:597","type":"develops","note":"Yugi falls as if speared (65) and then rises and sets his plan; the couplet's strong in mind who do not give way where things fall apart.","grade":"weak","better":"qualifies","te":"The couplet's firm elephant is a person who stays upright; Yugi shows the other half, a man who is struck down and then gets up to act.","gn":["The epic verse gains a measure for Yugi's rising: not stoicism but the strong mind that recovers and acts.","The couplet gains a case in which firmness does not mean never falling, since its elephant stands under arrows and Yugi first collapses."],"mm":1},{"a":"ud:67","b":"k:633","type":"develops","note":"Yugi removes the king's kin to safety, appoints rulers and plans to answer force with guile, which are three of the minister's skills in the couplet: dividing, holding his own side close, bringing back those who left.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"Kural 633 lists a minister's skills as bare terms; this verse shows one minister doing them in order at a moment of loss.","gn":["The epic verse gains the Kural's name for what Yugi is doing, a set of skills and not one clever act.","The couplet gains a minister at work in a defeat, which its three terms give no scene for."],"mm":1},{"a":"ud:70","b":"ud:69","type":"qualifies","note":"The false death that frees Yugi to act (69) leaves his own lands to be taken once they think him hidden or dead.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Guile that saves the plan has a price paid by the realm, which verse 69 alone does not count.","mm":1},{"a":"ud:73","b":"k:585","type":"qualifies","note":"The couplet wants a spy in a guise that raises no suspicion; Yugi's mad guise draws every eye but carries speech meant for one hearer, the king.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"A disguise can be conspicuous to everyone and still be a secret to all but one, which the couplet's rule of unsuspected appearance does not allow.","gn":["The epic verse gains the Kural's test of a disguise as one that does not draw suspicion, so Yugi's loud madness stands out as a deliberate exception.","The couplet gains a case where the disguise is meant to be seen, because the message is in the madman's talk and not in being unseen."],"gr":2,"mm":1},{"a":"ud:63","b":"si:9:45-54","type":"same_image","note":"Vasavadatta's dream foretells a taking-away by a man, read by experts as a marriage; Kannaki's dream foretells the carrying off of her husband and a false charge.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain","te":"A dream in these epics gives the story's course in advance, but the same image foretells a bridegroom in one poem and a ruin in the other.","gn":["The Udayana verse gains a contrast in which a dream's reading is joyful and its reader is an expert, as against Kannaki's dread.","The Silappathikaram passage gains a second story where the dream is read by others and comes true, so its dread is one case of a convention."],"mm":1},{"a":"ud:94","b":"ud:97","type":"develops","note":"The ministers say the elephant cannot pass the king's vina; verse 97 shows it coming and bowing at his feet.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The poem lets the ministers' claim of music's power be tested by what the elephant does in front of the crowd.","mm":1},{"a":"ud:85","b":"ud:86","type":"develops","note":"The mantra and smoke that free the elephant at the post become its killing run through the keepers.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"What looked like a rescuer's stratagem shows its cost at once, on men who were only keepers.","mm":1},{"a":"ud:85","b":"k:678","type":"qualifies","note":"Yugi uses the king's own rutting elephant to break the king's hold, as the couplet binds a rutting elephant with another.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural's image of one task serving another is shown as a plan that also costs lives.","gn":["the stratagem is named as a type: a means turned on its owner, with a price the couplet does not state","the couplet's image of an elephant bound by an elephant is acted out with a real elephant and a real rescue"],"gr":2,"mm":1},{"a":"ud:86","b":"n:213","type":"qualifies","note":"The freed elephant kills the keepers who try to enclose it, as the quatrain says an elephant may kill even its keeper.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The epic shows the quatrain's keeper-killer elephant as a weapon someone else has deliberately loosed.","gn":["the keepers' deaths read as a known risk of the beast, not as an accident of the rescue","the quatrain's general warning is given a scene where the elephant is not merely ungrateful but sent"],"mm":1},{"a":"ud:84","b":"k:483","type":"qualifies","note":"Yugi and Vayanthakan wait for the right hour and rope, as the couplet says nothing is hard for one who knows time and means.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The rescue is built on the couplet's rule, but at the cost of others, which the couplet does not weigh.","gn":["the choice of hour and means is shown as the rule that makes a prison break possible","the couplet's claim is tested by a case in which the same skill harms the innocent"],"mm":1},{"a":"ud:93","b":"k:652","type":"qualifies","note":"The king fears the blame of sending a captive before an elephant but admits the capture was by a lie.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The king dreads the stain of one act and ignores the one before it, which the couplet's test of fame would catch.","gn":["the king's fear of blame is shown to cover only the last act, not the deceit that made it needed","the couplet's warning against acts that win neither renown nor good is shown failing to bind a king who counts only visible blame"],"mm":1},{"a":"ud:97","b":"t:977","type":"same_image","note":"The rutting elephant is stilled by the vina's song; Tirumular's five elephants are held by the goad of five letters.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The same picture of a raging elephant brought to rest differs in who tames it, music held by a captive or a mantra held by the self.","gn":["the elephant's bow reads as a taming of the passions in the bystander's eye, not only a feat of music","the inward elephants of the verse get an outward counterpart, a beast whose rage is visible to a city"],"mm":1},{"a":"ud:105","b":"ud:103","type":"develops","note":"Verse 105 shows Vasavadatta with the arts that verse 103 had her taught.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The lute lessons planned in one verse come out as proof of mastery in the next, so the teaching is shown as having worked.","mm":1},{"a":"ud:110","b":"ud:109","type":"develops","note":"The fire in the town carries out the trick Yugi sets in verse 109.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Yugi's disguised talk of bathing is only a lure; the fire that follows shows what the lure was for.","mm":1},{"a":"ud:119","b":"ud:117","type":"develops","note":"The hunters who gather at dawn in verse 117 have fired the wood by verse 119.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The ambush that begins with a surround is not ended by Udayanan's arrows alone; it ends when the hunters fail and Vayantakan comes.","mm":1},{"a":"ud:98","b":"ng:67","type":"same_image","note":"Both poems show a young prince making a rutting elephant submit before the king.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain","te":"Both minor epics test a hero's worth before a king by his mastery of an elephant, but here the man is a captive and the rider's skill earns him his captor's trust.","mm":1},{"a":"ud:114","b":"cc:947","type":"qualifies","note":"Both tell of an animal dying while a mantra is spoken to it so that it is reborn a god.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"In the Cintamani the dog must consent and think on the words, while here the elephant is simply spoken to and the change is at once.","mm":1},{"a":"ud:98","b":"t:977","type":"same_image","note":"The goad in line 4 is the tool of the elephant keeper; Tirumular's goad is the five letters that rein the five elephants.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The goad that in one poem governs a real elephant becomes the word that governs the senses in the other.","gn":["the taking of the goad gets an inner reading as discipline over a creature of great force","the image of the goad gets a scene in which it is used on a real elephant that has bowed already"],"mm":1},{"a":"ud:109","b":"k:660","type":"qualifies","note":"Yugi's deceit is for keeping a sworn vow; the couplet says wealth gained by deceit does not hold.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Deceit that serves a promise and not wealth is not judged by the couplet, which shows how far its warning reaches.","gn":["the ruse gets a rule against which to see that its aim is a vow, not gain","the couplet gets a case where deceit serves loyalty, which tests whether its warning covers it"],"mm":1},{"a":"ud:118","b":"k:597","type":"develops","note":"Udayanan stands under a rain of arrows and answers with his bow; the couplet gives the bull elephant under a sheaf of arrows.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain","te":"The couplet's elephant only stands firm; Udayanan stands firm and also shoots back, so endurance in the story includes acting.","gn":["the defence gets a name for the quality shown, steadiness when things fall apart","the couplet's image gets a man who stands and answers where the elephant only stands"],"mm":1},{"a":"ud:99","b":"n:3","type":"qualifies","note":"Udayanan, a captive, rides in triumph on the elephant; the quatrain says kings who rode elephants fall when their deeds turn.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The story reverses the quatrain's fall, showing a captive king raised through skill and not through deeds, which limits how far the quatrain's warning is the whole account.","gn":["the captive's triumph gets a reminder that a king's elephant-riding rank can be lost","the quatrain gets a case in which a fallen man rises on an elephant by skill"],"mm":1},{"a":"ud:129","b":"ud:124","type":"develops","note":"Yugi's plan in verse 129 answers the king's forgetting of his lost land in verse 124.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The poet first blames the king's neglect and then shows who will act on it, so the neglect becomes the cause of the plot that follows.","mm":1},{"a":"ud:130","b":"ud:129","type":"develops","note":"Yugi's briefing of the ascetic and the portrait carry out the plan he forms in verse 129.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The plan to part the king from the queen is not carried by Yugi's own presence but by a woman and a picture, so the stratagem works through trust in others.","mm":1},{"a":"ud:133","b":"ud:131","type":"develops","note":"The portrait the old woman carries in verse 131 is the one she shows the king in verse 133.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"What the portrait is for is clear only when the king sees it and breaks into grief, so the picture works as a message that needs no word.","mm":1},{"a":"ud:134","b":"ud:133","type":"develops","note":"The king, after seeing the portrait in verse 133, asks the sage whose answer verse 134 gives.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The king's grief turns not to action but to a sage's assurance, so the answer to Yugi's lure is a prophecy that makes him wait.","mm":1},{"a":"ud:135","b":"ud:129","type":"develops","note":"Verse 135 shows Yugi appearing to part the queen from the king, as planned in verse 129.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The plan formed in smiling thought is now set in motion after the king has taken a second bride, so Yugi's scheme and the king's new love run side by side.","mm":1},{"a":"ud:129","b":"k:901","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 901 says desire for a wife costs a man what action needs; Yugi concludes the same of the king's union, and cures it by a stratagem, not by the king's restraint.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet speaks to the man who must resist his own desire, while the epic gives the cure to a minister who engineers an absence.","gn":["The epic scene gains a general rule that makes the king's fault a known failing of men intent on pleasure, not only his own.","The couplet gains a case in which the fault is not mended by the husband's restraint but by an outside plan, which the couplet never considers."],"mm":1},{"a":"ud:129","b":"k:638","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 638 makes it the minister's duty to tell a king what is good for him; Yugi, given a king who will not hear, chooses a trick.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet assumes the king can be told, while the poem shows a minister who judges telling useless and acts in secret.","gn":["The epic scene gains the name of a duty that Yugi both keeps in aim and breaks in method.","The couplet gains a case where the minister's duty to speak is overtaken by deceit for the king's own good."],"mm":1},{"a":"ud:124","b":"k:539","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 539 bids a man remember the ruined when swollen with joy; the king of verse 124 forgets even his own ruin.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet's remedy needs a remembering mind, and the poem shows the king in whom nothing remains to be reminded.","gn":["The verse gains the couplet's name for the fault: joy that swells past remembering.","The couplet gains a king whose own loss, not another's, is the ruin he forgets."],"mm":1},{"a":"ud:123","b":"n:17","type":"tension","note":"Verse 123 calls the lady's breast a raft in the sea of desire; Naladiyar 17 warns that the same woman will one day walk bent on a staff.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The epic's rapture and the quatrain's warning use the same woman and cannot both be the last word on her.","gn":["The epic's image of desire as a sea gains a bounded end in the quatrain's picture of age.","The quatrain's warning gains a living king who does not heed it and pays with his land."],"mm":1},{"a":"ud:139","b":"ud:136","type":"develops","note":"The fire set in verse 136 has its effect in the king's collapse in 139.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The tunnel and fire are not an end: their purpose is the king's belief, and verse 139 shows him believing it.","mm":1},{"a":"ud:138","b":"ud:137","type":"develops","note":"The minister's hidden guest of 137 is the one he asks a boon of in 138.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The reader sees that the queen is brought to the minister not by chance but to be asked for her consent to the parting.","mm":1},{"a":"ud:141","b":"ud:110","type":"same_image","note":"The fire in the town of verse 110 is again a fire here, now a stage prop.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain","te":"The same device of fire serves Yugi twice: first to free the fugitives, now to fake a death, so it is his habit and not a one-off.","gr":2,"mm":1},{"a":"ud:146","b":"ud:140","type":"qualifies","note":"The king's vow to die in 140 is turned aside by counsel in 146.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"A king's wish to die with his queen is checked by minister's counsel, which is the work of a minister in the epic's picture of rule.","mm":1},{"a":"ud:150","b":"ud:149","type":"develops","note":"The journey in 150 carries out the plan Vayantakan puts in 149.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The reader learns that the long mourning ends with an act, as the king follows the counsel and sets out.","mm":1},{"a":"ud:146","b":"k:638","type":"qualifies","note":"The ministers' counsel to a grieving king meets Kural 638 on a minister's duty to speak to a king who will not listen.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The Kural rule covers a king who destroys good counsel, but the epic shows a king who accepts counsel and is still deceived because the advisers' facts are false.","gn":["the ministers' speech gains a named duty, so that their advice reads as a minister's task and not only comfort","the couplet gains a case in which good counsel is taken and yet rests on a false premise, a test the couplet does not make"],"gr":2,"mm":1},{"a":"ud:141","b":"t:145","type":"qualifies","note":"The king mourning a body that was never found is set against the town that wails, buries and forgets in Tirumanthiram 145.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"One poem shows grief for a real death that is quickly ended, the other grief for a false death that the king cannot end, so lament is shown to follow belief and not fact.","gn":["the king's lament gains the ordinary course of mourning the song describes, against which his unfinished grief over a lie stands out","the song gains a case of mourning for someone not dead, showing that a staged death can draw the same grief as a real one"],"gr":2,"mm":1},{"a":"ud:156","b":"ud:152","type":"develops","note":"The brahmin's promise of the lost queen meets the king's hope in verse 152.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The promise is aimed at a hope the king already holds, so the deceit works because it answers his grief.","mm":1},{"a":"ud:157","b":"ud:153","type":"same_image","note":"In both verses the king sees the dead queen in something else, first flowers and then Padmavati.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"A habit of seeing her likeness in things turns from a poet's sorrow into a real mistake that begins the new marriage.","mm":1},{"a":"ud:163","b":"ud:162","type":"develops","note":"The treasure the king searched for in verse 162 is shown to him in verse 163.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"The king's loss is turned into Urumannuvan's service, so the search sets up the trust that follows.","mm":1},{"a":"ud:165","b":"ud:164","type":"develops","note":"The seven kings named in verse 164 now make their demand and march on the land.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The list of names becomes a real threat only when they refuse tribute, which is what makes the stratagem necessary.","mm":1},{"a":"ud:166","b":"ud:165","type":"develops","note":"The disguise as merchants carries out the stratagem Urumannuvan promises in verse 165.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The stratagem is a trade, not a battle, which tells the reader how Urumannuvan means to win.","mm":1},{"a":"ud:166","b":"k:585","type":"qualifies","note":"Disguised ministers sell jewels in an enemy camp, as the Kural describes a spy who takes an unsuspected form.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The epic's spies are the king and his own minister, and gems are the means, which the Kural's spy has no word for.","gn":["The verse gains a rule, a spy's form must raise no suspicion, which shows why the jewel trade is chosen as the disguise.","The couplet gains a case in which king and minister both act as the spy, which its plain listing of the spy's duties does not offer."],"mm":1},{"a":"ud:169","b":"ud:170","type":"develops","note":"The spies' bare report in 169 is followed in 170 by the minister's own telling of Udayanan's deed.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The king's judgment in 169 rests on spies alone, and the minister's account in 170 is what turns it into joy.","mm":1},{"a":"ud:171","b":"ud:172","type":"develops","note":"The king's vow in 171 to remove the foes is carried out in 172 by Udayanan's army.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The vow is kept by the guest and not by the host, so the alliance in this chapter runs on Udayanan's arms.","mm":1},{"a":"ud:172","b":"ud:173","type":"develops","note":"The victory in 172 is repaid in 173 with the king's sister as bride.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The marriage reads as the price of the war: the bride is paid for the battle won.","mm":1},{"a":"ud:173","b":"ng:114","type":"same_image","note":"In both epics a prince wins a battle for a king and the king rewards him with a bride (Gunavati there, Padumai here).","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain","te":"The reward-bride after a won battle is a pattern the Jain kavyas share, which marks the match as a convention and not one king's habit.","mm":1},{"a":"ud:176","b":"k:381","type":"develops","note":"The ministers, army and friend the king adds to the dowry in 176 are the same things the Kural counts among a state's six parts.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"A king who has lost a state can rebuild its parts by marriage, which the Kural's list of parts does not say.","gn":["The bride's gift reads as the means by which the exiled Udayanan regains the army and ministers of a ruler.","The list of six parts gets a case in which allies and ministers are acquired by a marriage gift and not by long building."],"mm":1},{"a":"ud:170","b":"k:588","type":"qualifies","note":"The spies' report in 169 is confirmed in 170 by a second voice, the minister's.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The king rejoices only once a second voice agrees, which suggests the second confirmation is a habit of a court and not only a rule.","gn":["The shift from spy to minister shows how a second source is found at a court, which the verse alone does not name.","The rule of confirming a spy's finding gets an instance in which the second source is a friendly minister, not another spy."],"mm":1},{"a":"ud:179","b":"ud:180","type":"develops","note":"The errand to the Panchala king in 179 is carried out in 180, where the kings are driven off and the Panchala army comes on.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The errand is meant as stratagem and ends in open battle, which shows the plan did not remove the war.","mm":1},{"a":"ud:180","b":"ud:181","type":"develops","note":"The mounting arrows of 180 end in the ruin of 181.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The sky-darkening arrow exchange is the cause of the field's ruin, so 181 reads as the cost of the fight in 180.","mm":1},{"a":"ud:181","b":"s:354","type":"same_image","note":"The jackals and ghouls feeding on the slain in 181 are the carrion eaters the Siddhar names when each claims the body as its own.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain","te":"A battle's bodies are the same flesh the Siddhar tells us is only meat for jackals and kites, so the glory of the field is also the end of the body.","gn":["The field's feast of jackals is read as the fate of every body, not only of the slain king's soldiers.","The Siddhar's abstract claim that the body is prey gets a scene in which the body is the price of a king's glory."],"gr":2,"mm":1},{"a":"ud:182","b":"ud:183","type":"develops","note":"The killing of the beaten king in 182 is followed in 183 by the order to stop the war.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The order to stop comes only after the enemy king is dead, so the killing ends the war and not the king's mercy.","mm":1},{"a":"ud:185","b":"k:546","type":"qualifies","note":"After the victory by arms, the king in 185 tends the wounded and rewards them, which is the work of a ruler's rule and not of the spear.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The epic shows victory first by the sword and then by care, so the Kural's claim is borne out only in the part after the battle.","gn":["The healing and gifts after the battle are given a name as the sceptre's work, which the verse does not claim.","The couplet's contrast of spear and sceptre gets a case in which both are used one after the other."],"mm":1},{"a":"ud:190","b":"ud:103","type":"develops","note":"Padumai asks for the lute lessons that verse 103 gave Vasavadatta.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The lute lessons that began Vasavadatta's tie to Udayanan are now claimed by a second wife, so the same craft becomes a place where two wives are measured.","mm":1},{"a":"ud:192","b":"ud:63","type":"same_image","note":"Both verses have a dream read by the wise as a promise of union.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"In each, the dream is read aloud by experts and then fulfilled, but here the reader names the lady's survival while verse 63 names a groom to come.","mm":1},{"a":"ud:196","b":"ud:192","type":"develops","note":"Vasavadatta arrives at Kausambi as the sages in verse 192 foretold.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The sages' reading is shown to be true when the lady the king thought dead walks into the grove.","mm":1},{"a":"ud:196","b":"ud:120","type":"develops","note":"They leave Jayanti, the refuge reached in verse 120, for Kausambi.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The safe city reached at the end of the flight is only a stage; the return to the king's own capital is what completes it.","mm":1},{"a":"ud:198","b":"ud:109","type":"develops","note":"The plot confessed here is the trick of Yugi set out in verse 109.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The trick is described in its working in verse 109, but only here does its author call it a departure from the treatises and ask pardon.","mm":1},{"a":"ud:203","b":"k:1303","type":"develops","note":"The king ends his queen's sulk by praise and an embrace, which is what the couplet urges for a sulker.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"The couplet says not to leave the sulker unembraced, while the scene adds that he first praises her beauty and wins her mind.","gn":["the verse gains a rule that makes the embrace more than lust, an answer owed to someone already hurt","the couplet gains a concrete order of acts: praise first, then the embrace, with a king as the one who must yield"],"mm":1},{"a":"ud:204","b":"k:1109","type":"develops","note":"Quarrel (201-202), making up (203) and union (204) run in the order the couplet gives.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet lists the three as a gain of love, and the run of verses shows them as one evening in a king's house.","gn":["the verse gains a naming of its sequence as love's reward, not merely a king's pleasure","the couplet gains a plot in which the quarrel is caused by a second wife and made up by flattery"],"mm":1},{"a":"ud:210","b":"ud:39","type":"develops","note":"The letter owns the elephant that Pradyotan's side made in verse 39.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Pradyotan's own letter turns the trap of verse 39 into an offer of kinship, which shows how a captor explains his trick afterwards.","gr":2,"mm":1},{"a":"ud:211","b":"ud:60","type":"same_image","note":"Both verses use the lion bound in a spider's thread for Udayanan's capture.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The image first comes in Vayantakan's report of the capture and returns in the captor's own letter, so the same fall is told once by the losers and once by the winner.","gr":2,"mm":1},{"a":"ud:211","b":"ud:57","type":"develops","note":"The letter looks back at the seizure of the garlanded king in verse 57.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The capture shown in battle is later acknowledged as a fault to be pardoned, which is not said in the fight itself.","mm":1},{"a":"ud:219","b":"ud:66","type":"tension","note":"Yugi swore in verse 66 to destroy Ujjain, and in this verse its king gives him two brides.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"The vow of vengeance ends in a marriage gift from the enemy, so the plan to destroy is replaced by a bond of kin.","mm":1},{"a":"ud:215","b":"ud:41","type":"develops","note":"Salankayan, who showed the false elephant in verse 41, now speaks of dharma texts with Yugi.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The minister who ran the trap is now Yugi's friend in learning, so the quarrel is shown to end between ministers before it ends between kings.","mm":1},{"a":"ud:207","b":"k:385","type":"develops","note":"Udayanan divides towns among those who served him; the couplet names apportioning as one of a king's tasks.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The poem shows apportioning as reward to friends who risked themselves for the king, which the couplet names without saying to whom the share goes.","gn":["the grants of towns are seen as the king's last duty of rule and not as favour alone","the couplet's word for apportioning gets a scene where the shares go to those who served in the captivity"],"mm":1},{"a":"ud:211","b":"k:685","type":"qualifies","note":"The letter softens the wrong of the seizure with words of pardon; the couplet asks the envoy to remove what offends.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"A letter that must confess a seizure can keep to the couplet's rule of avoiding offence only by naming the fault and asking for pardon in the same breath.","gn":["the letter's gentle admission of fault is seen as the envoy's craft of removing offence","the couplet is tried against a message that carries a fault of the sender, where removing offence is hard"],"mm":1},{"a":"ud:212","b":"k:698","type":"qualifies","note":"Pradyotan pleads the tie of uncle and nephew; the couplet tells a man not to presume on kin degree with a king.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"Here the stronger party pleads kinship and not the lesser, which shows the couplet's warning running the other way once the king has wronged his kinsman.","gn":["the plea of kin is seen as a way to cover a wrong and not as a sign of equal standing","the couplet is tested where the one pressing kin degree is the king himself"],"gr":2,"mm":1},{"a":"ud:217","b":"k:445","type":"qualifies","note":"The king says that without such counsellors there is no kingship; the couplet makes counsellors the king's eyes.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet asks the king to choose counsellors after counsel, but the verse shows a king recognising the worth of a minister who had worked against him.","gn":["the king's praise of Yugi gets a rule that counsellors are his eyes, which makes the praise a statement of need","the couplet gets a case where the counsellor praised belongs to the other side and has beaten the praiser"],"mm":1},{"a":"ud:230","b":"ud:228","type":"develops","note":"Verse 230 brings in Mananigai, named in verse 228, to meet Dattai's defeat.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The newcomer introduced by name and rank as a chaste princess in verse 228 first acts only in verse 230, when she steps in to answer the defeated Dattai.","mm":1},{"a":"ud:237","b":"ud:228","type":"develops","note":"Mananigai's eight thousand strikes answer the despondency of Dattai's party in 228.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The gloom with which Dattai sits defeated in 228 is not lifted by Dattai herself but by a stranger's play, which shows the poem making skill the cause of reversal.","mm":1},{"a":"ud:237","b":"ud:227","type":"qualifies","note":"Her eight thousand outdoes Ariyai's three thousand, the score that had silenced Dattai's side.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"Ariyai's three thousand is shown as the peak only until Mananigai's count, so the contest's score is a ladder and each verse's number is meaningful only against the one before.","mm":1},{"a":"ud:239","b":"ud:238","type":"develops","note":"In 239 Dattai, angry in 238, comes with flowers to worship the king and the second marriage is completed.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Dattai's anger at the news is not allowed to stand; the next dawn shows her worshipping the king anyway, so the epic treats a first wife's resentment as a passing mood rather than a break.","mm":1},{"a":"ud:241","b":"ud:240","type":"develops","note":"The four chaste women of 241 are Dattai, Padumai, Mananigai and Virisikai; Virisikai's marriage in 240 completes the number.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The count of four in 241 only makes sense once 240 adds Virisikai to the three queens, so the closing contentment is the sum of the marriages.","mm":1},{"a":"ud:231","b":"cc:151","type":"same_image","note":"Both verses list the tricks of ball play, hiding the ball in a garland and making it dance like a snake.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Two Jain epics share one catalogue of ball tricks, so the hidden-in-garland and snake-dance play is a conventional set of skills, not either poet's invention.","mm":1},{"a":"ud:238","b":"si:16:130-147","type":"qualifies","note":"Both show a queen's resentment at a king's divided heart, but Dattai's anger ends in worship and the Pandya queen's sulk ends in the king's blindness.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The Silappathikaram makes a queen's sulk the opening that lets the goldsmith's lie in, while here the first queen's anger is quickly absorbed; the same domestic feeling has opposite consequences.","mm":1},{"a":"ud:241","b":"cc:27","type":"qualifies","note":"Both close on a king with tribute-paying kings around him, but Civakan turns away from it in dharma and Udayanan stays in it.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The same picture of a king whom others serve ends in renunciation in the Cintamani and in enjoyment here, so the minor epic's close is not the great epic's close.","mm":1},{"a":"ud:238","b":"k:1311","type":"qualifies","note":"The Kural's wife calls her husband a philanderer because all women feast on him with their eyes; Dattai's anger at his second marriage is the same grievance.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The epic shows what the Kural's grievance leads to when the husband is a king who really does take another wife, so the complaint is tested rather than only voiced.","gn":["Dattai's anger is given a name and a ground that the verse itself leaves unspoken.","The wife's complaint, which in the Kural stays a lovers' quarrel, is carried to a real second marriage and a quarrel that is settled by her bowing."],"mm":1},{"a":"ud:243","b":"ud:242","type":"develops","note":"The queen's longing named in 242 is given its object in 243.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The vague longing of 242 is shown to be a wish for flight in the sky, which sets the plot of the next verses in motion.","mm":1},{"a":"ud:245","b":"ud:244","type":"develops","note":"The mantra the god gave in the hunt is now used.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The god's rescue is not only a past kindness: it left a means to call him, so the gift becomes the tool for the present trouble.","mm":1},{"a":"ud:245","b":"cc:16","type":"same_image","note":"In both epics a god gives a hero a spell to call on, and a later need brings him.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain","te":"Both Jain epics let a god leave a gift of spells that later serves the hero, so the helping god is a recurring structure of the genre.","mm":1},{"a":"ud:247","b":"k:788","type":"qualifies","note":"The Kural's friend clears distress at once; the god's help waits to be called.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The god's reproach shows a friend's help can be bound by the call, which limits the Kural's picture of friendship that acts in the moment of trouble.","gn":["The god's ask-and-answer scene shows that friendship with a god runs on being remembered, and that the king neglected it until now.","The couplet's friend who acts unasked gets a counterexample: help that needs a call, and stays silent through prison and war."],"mm":1},{"a":"ud:249","b":"ud:243","type":"develops","note":"The cause of the queen's longing is given and the means of flight supplied.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The longing for the sky is explained as the doing of the being in her womb, so the flight is both a cure and a sign of the child's nature.","mm":1},{"a":"ud:252","b":"ud:243","type":"develops","note":"The birth closes the pregnancy that began with the longing for the sky.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The longing, met by the flight, is followed by an easy birth and gifts, so the god's help is shown to have ended the trouble as well as quieting it.","mm":1},{"a":"ud:257","b":"ud:256","type":"develops","note":"Sight in 256 becomes union in 257.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The ball-play scene is not an end in itself: the sight of her carries at once into union, which makes her loss in 258 sharper.","mm":1},{"a":"ud:257","b":"k:1081","type":"qualifies","note":"The Kural's bewildered first sight is here mutual and immediate.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The epic carries the Kural's bewilderment through to union within a single verse, with no doubt, no gossip and no delay, so the bewilderment is a path to union and not a state of suffering.","gn":["The verse shows both lovers bewildered, and it leaves out the mourning and doubt that the Kural's chapter dwells on.","The couplet's lone man, unsure whether she is goddess, peahen or woman, gets a case in which the sight is answered and the doubt never has time to arise."],"mm":1},{"a":"ud:259","b":"k:57","type":"qualifies","note":"The captor confines her and then tries to break her steadfastness; the Kural says only her own firmness guards a woman.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"Here the confinement works as the couplet says it must: the captor holds her body, yet only her silence and thought of her husband stand in the way, so the guard is inside her.","gn":["The abducted girl's silence and memory show what the guard inside a woman is made of in practice, which the epic otherwise leaves unnamed.","The couplet's claim that confinement guards nothing gets a hard test: she is truly confined, in the house of a king who wants her."],"mm":1},{"a":"ud:261","b":"ud:259","type":"tension","note":"The one sent to break another woman's firmness is overcome by desire herself.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"The plan to ruin Madanamanjikai's steadfastness reverses: the agent of the attack is the one who loses control, which turns the test of one woman into the downfall of another.","mm":1},{"a":"ud:262","b":"ud:261","type":"develops","note":"Vegavati's trick in 261 is carried out as a disguise in 262.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The guile that was only named in 261 is shown as taking the very form of the woman Naravahanan is looking for, which turns his search into the means of her plan.","mm":1},{"a":"ud:261","b":"k:1138","type":"qualifies","note":"Desire breaks through the restraint of the one who was to be the tempter.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"Vegavati was sent as the instrument of a plan, and she meets desire the Kural says does not stop to ask whether one is firm in restraint, which makes the plan itself the thing that fails.","gn":["The verse shows desire overcoming the person of the plan, a case the couplet's general remark does not name.","The couplet's general claim that love ignores restraint gets a case where it defeats an agent with an assigned task, not only a lover who is open to it."],"mm":1},{"a":"ud:282","b":"ud:279","type":"develops","note":"The sage's reading of the dream in 282 answers the dream the girl tells in 279 and 280.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The dream's lion, fish and curse stay riddles until the sage names a bridegroom and an empire as their meaning, so the reading is the dream's fulfilment.","mm":1},{"a":"ud:272","b":"ud:282","type":"agrees","note":"Two prophecies by sages, one for the youth's Vidyadhara rule, one for his marriage and earthly rule.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The youth's promised rule is told from his side by one sage and from the bride's side by another, so the two prophecies fit together into a single destiny.","mm":1},{"a":"ud:267","b":"ud:268","type":"develops","note":"The youth's fall into the forest in 267 brings the sage's rescue in 268.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The brother's cruelty ends not in the youth's death but in an encounter that sets his future, which turns the fall into the start of his rise.","mm":1},{"a":"ud:288","b":"ud:266","type":"develops","note":"Manasavegan, who had seized the pair in verse 266, is the one whose mind is afraid at the wedding.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The rival who once held the hero captive now fears him, so the wedding shows the rivalry turning in the hero's favour.","mm":1},{"a":"ud:290","b":"ud:282","type":"develops","note":"The sage's saying that the groom will rule the earth with the wheel is met by the wheel and nine treasures.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The sage's prophecy is fulfilled step by step, so the reader sees how the promised wheel comes to him through Indra's grace.","mm":1},{"a":"ud:292","b":"ud:272","type":"develops","note":"The sage promised the silver mountain's realms under his feet; here the Vidyadhara lords are brought under them.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The promise of the sage's earlier words is kept in the same terms, which marks the conquest as foretold, not chance.","mm":1},{"a":"ud:295","b":"k:36","type":"qualifies","note":"The epic makes merit's fruit a throne and pleasure in this life, where the couplet calls virtue the companion that stays when you die.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"Merit gathered earlier is repaid here as sovereignty, so the couplet's 'companion' is shown paying out in the same life it began.","gn":["the verse's merit gets a test against the Kural's claim that virtue's worth is what survives death, which the verse alone does not state","the couplet gets a case in which virtue's reward comes while the doer lives, a limit it does not state"],"mm":1},{"a":"ud:294","b":"n:21","type":"qualifies","note":"At the height of empire and eight thousand queens, the Naladiyar says kings on elephants under umbrellas are carried off like all others; the next kandam is a renunciation.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The peak of enjoyment is set right before the work's renunciation kandam, so the Naladiyar's warning is the story's own turning point.","gn":["the list of queens gets the Naladiyar's warning of what such splendour is worth, which the verse never voices","the quatrain gets a case of a king's splendour at its height, which gives its warning a particular king to fall on"],"mm":1},{"a":"ud:308","b":"ud:304","type":"develops","note":"Verse 308 gives the reflection behind the turn verse 304 announces.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The earlier verse only says the vow is taken; the later one shows the king's disgust with pleasure and his seeking out the teacher as how it comes about.","mm":1},{"a":"ud:309","b":"ud:304","type":"develops","note":"The women's show of desire comes as the vow of verse 304 begins.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"A vow is tested at once by the thing it gives up, so the renunciation is shown to cost the king something.","mm":1},{"a":"ud:310","b":"ud:309","type":"develops","note":"The elephant shaking off its foot-fetter may picture the king shedding the delusion the women offer.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"If the elephant is read as the king's image, the same verse turns a rampage into a picture of release, which neither verse says plainly.","mm":1},{"a":"ud:323","b":"ud:318","type":"develops","note":"The teaching the ascetics arrive to give in 318 is heard in 323.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Verse 318 only names the ascetics' skills and arrival; verse 323 shows what their teaching says, that life is a fearful bondage.","mm":1},{"a":"ud:324","b":"ud:314","type":"develops","note":"Verse 324 shows the feast of birds that the killings of 314 leave.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The elephant's violence in 314 is followed by its leaving a feast, so its cost is seen in what scavengers gain.","mm":1},{"a":"ud:308","b":"cc:27","type":"qualifies","note":"Udayanan turns from pleasure after reflecting on past penance; Civakan turns from kingship after hearing the dharma.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain","te":"The two epics give a king's renunciation different starting points, one from his own reflection and one from a sermon, and this shows how the Jain epics motivate it.","mm":1},{"a":"ud:319","b":"cc:1177","type":"same_image","note":"Both name the Charanas as a class of ascetics, one by their powers of flight and one at the mountain where they wash away karma.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"The class appears with its powers in one epic and its purpose, washing karma away, in the other.","mm":1},{"a":"ud:320","b":"si:10:156-169","type":"same_image","note":"The Charana appears as a teacher who reads the travellers' minds in the Silappathikaram and as a flier in Udayana.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"One epic shows the Charana's mind-reading and the other his flight, so the powers attributed to him go beyond either.","mm":1},{"a":"ud:304","b":"k:262","type":"qualifies","note":"The king's vow follows earlier merit, as the couplet says austerity comes only to those who already have it.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"The couplet's circular rule is shown working in a life, with the vow following earlier merit.","gn":["the king's turn to a vow gets a ground in earlier merit rather than seeming sudden","the couplet's circular rule gets a story in which it works"],"mm":1},{"a":"ud:308","b":"k:46","type":"qualifies","note":"A king of full worldly success leaves pleasure behind, which tests the couplet's question about going outside.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet asks what is gained by leaving the household that keeps virtue, and the story answers with a king who leaves out of disgust at its pleasures, not out of failure.","gn":["the king's turn gets a standard against which his leaving can be judged","the couplet's question gets a case of a successful householder who leaves"],"mm":1},{"a":"ud:321","b":"k:19","type":"qualifies","note":"The couplet makes austerity and giving depend on the sky's rain; here the ascetics themselves send the rain.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The dependence of austerity on rain in the couplet is reversed in the unit, where the austere supply the rain.","gn":["the sages' power is placed within the dependence of giving on rain, so the rain relieves the very distress that would stop giving","the couplet's one-way dependence on the sky meets a case where austerity feeds the sky"],"mm":1},{"a":"ud:323","b":"n:27","type":"qualifies","note":"The quatrain praises those who see the body as a bubble and resolve to end wandering; the unit shows a sermon only heard.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"Hearing a sermon on suffering is not yet the resolve the quatrain praises, so the unit stops short of its conclusion.","gn":["the sermon's claim that life is a bondage of suffering gets an image, the body as a bubble that forms and bursts","the quatrain's resolve gets a scene of its audience hearing the teaching, though not yet acting"],"mm":1},{"a":"ud:326","b":"ud:4","type":"develops","note":"Verse 4 states in the abstract that a deed binds and ripens; verse 326 gives a case, killing and its fruit.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The opening claim about deeds that ripen is given a concrete case here: a named deed, its sorrow and its hell.","mm":1},{"a":"ud:346","b":"ud:342","type":"develops","note":"The king's question in verse 342 about his love for the elephant is answered by a past birth in verse 346.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The king's love for the elephant is answered by a birth he cannot recall, not by anything in the elephant.","mm":1},{"a":"ud:326","b":"k:255","type":"develops","note":"Both give hell as the fruit of the deed; the verse adds animal rebirth to it.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The epic widens the couplet's hell into an order of fruits that includes animal birth, which the couplet does not name.","gn":["the sermon's hell is borne out by an old rule that links it to killing for food","the couplet's hell gets a longer road of sorrow and animal birth before it"],"mm":1},{"a":"ud:339","b":"k:920","type":"qualifies","note":"Both warn that women and liquor ruin a man; the verse follows the ruin past this life.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"The couplet counts the loss in fortune; the story counts the loss in the next birth, so the harm reaches beyond this life.","gn":["the brahmin's fall gets a rule that names his company as the mark of a man whom fortune has left","the couplet's ruin gets a case in which the cost is a birth as an elephant"],"mm":1},{"a":"ud:334","b":"k:222","type":"tension","note":"The verse promises release for gifts to ascetics; the couplet says giving is good even if there is no higher world.","grade":"weak","better":"qualifies","te":"The verse pays giving in release, while the couplet holds that giving is good without any reward, so the two differ on why one gives.","gn":["the promise of release for giving is tested by a rule that gives without any reward","the couplet's giving for its own sake meets a doctrine that fixes a reward"],"mm":1},{"a":"ud:335","b":"t:219","type":"tension","note":"The verse sheds heavy deeds by right vision; Tirumular burns the many ills of deeds by worship of the fire.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The deed is shed by sight and understanding in one and burnt away by worship in the other, so the means of release is what they dispute.","gn":["the shedding of deeds by sight gets a rival that burns them by rite","Tirumular's fire gets a rival in which no worship is needed and sight alone is enough"],"mm":1},{"a":"ud:339","b":"s:50","type":"qualifies","note":"The Siddhar asks when darkness will leave births as grass, beast and worm; the verse shows one man made a beast.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"The Siddhar names the beast-births without saying why; the verse supplies a cause in a life of drink and lust.","gn":["the elephant's rebirth gets the Siddhar's longing to escape every such birth","the Siddhar's list of births gets a case with a stated cause"],"mm":1},{"a":"ud:333","b":"so:201","type":"agrees","note":"Both set knowledge and trust before conduct in the sage's teaching.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Both Jain sages put conduct after discernment of true things, though the Soolamani gives it as three steps and this verse buries it in a long list.","mm":1},{"a":"ud:354","b":"ud:114","type":"same_image","note":"The she-elephant that falls bleeding in 114 becomes a god at the king's mantra, and the elephant of 354 becomes a god through its own samadhi.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain","te":"An elephant's rebirth as a god is first something the king does for it in a crisis, and in 354 something its own discipline wins for it, so the king learns to treat release as a person's own work.","mm":1},{"a":"ud:354","b":"n:4","type":"develops","note":"Naladiyar tells the listener to do what good he can at once, because Death comes; the king acts at once on the news of the elephant.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Naladiyar's warning comes with no figure to act on it; the king gives up a throne, a wife-court and his lands within a verse of the warning, so the rule is shown at its full price.","gn":["the king's turn gets a general rule behind it, that what seems to stand does not, and that good must be done before Death","the quatrain gets someone who pays a kingdom's worth for it at once, instead of only urging it"],"mm":1},{"a":"ud:356","b":"ng:162","type":"qualifies","note":"Naga Kumara's king crowns the young prince and rises to austerity; Udayanan crowns Gomukhan, not the son he has just called.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"In the Naga Kumara story the crown goes down the family line, and here it goes outside it, so Udayanan's renunciation is not a settling of an heir but a handing of the realm to whoever can rule it.","mm":1},{"a":"ud:357","b":"k:46","type":"tension","note":"Kural 46 asks what one gains by going outside if the household is led in virtue; the king takes his queens and ministers with him.","grade":"weak","better":"qualifies","te":"The Kural weighs the household against leaving it, while the king's whole household leaves together, which tests whether renunciation is abandoning dependants.","gn":["the king's exit gets a standard, that a virtuous household needs no leaving, against which it must justify itself","the couplet gets a case in which the household itself, and not one man, chooses the outside way"],"mm":1},{"a":"ud:363","b":"k:126","type":"develops","note":"Kural 126 praises drawing in the five senses like a tortoise; the verse lists holding the five senses as one item of a numbered discipline.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet states restraint as a single act with a reward over seven births, and the verse sets it as one part of a long catalogue, so restraint becomes one limb of a trained life.","gn":["the catalogue gets a single plain image and a reward for restraint, which a bare list of numbers does not give","the couplet gets the full scheme in which restraint is one of many practices, not a stand-alone virtue"],"mm":1},{"a":"ud:365","b":"ng:165","type":"agrees","note":"Both works end by saying the sage overcomes the two deeds by meditation and reaches liberation.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Two Jain epics close on the same formula, the two deeds overcome by meditation, and so show it as the fixed ending of a Jain life-story and not a feature of one work.","mm":1},{"a":"ud:365","b":"k:5","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 5 says the two deeds do not cling to those devoted to the Lord's praise; the verse says they are burned in meditation.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet makes freedom from the two deeds a gift of praise, and the verse makes it the fruit of effort, so the cause of release differs while the word for the two deeds is shared.","gn":["the verse gets an older line that the two deeds can be shed, so its claim is not a private doctrine","the couplet gets a story of how the deeds actually end, by burning in meditation and not by devotion"],"mm":1},{"a":"ud:365","b":"t:219","type":"tension","note":"Tirumanthiram 219 says the fire worshipped and risen burns the deeds of ages; the verse says meditation burns them.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain","te":"Both texts burn the deeds with fire, but in one the fire is the Lord's, worshipped, and in the other it is the sage's own meditation, which shows where Jain and Saiva release part ways.","gn":["the verse gets a Saiva statement that deeds can be burned by fire, which shows its own fire is the sage's meditation and no god's gift","the Tirumanthiram verse gets a case where the same burning is done by the sage alone and no deity helps"],"gr":2,"mm":1},{"a":"ya:1","b":"ng:17","type":"develops","note":"The Yasodhara's opening praises the omniscient lord whose feet teach release by leaving karma; the Naga hymn praises him as all-knowing and as the one who burned every old deed.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"The Jain epics' praise of the lord is the same act twice, here as a promise of teaching and there as a list of what the lord has already done to his own karma.","mm":1},{"a":"ya:1","b":"t:117","type":"tension","note":"Verse 1 says release is taught by leaving cruel deeds behind; Tirumanthiram 117 says the malas burn only when the sun-like Lord appears, as the sunstone burns cotton.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Whether release is something the one freed does by shedding deeds or something the Lord does by his presence is the exact point where the Jain and Saiva ways part.","gn":["the Jain opening gains a rival account of what burns away karma, which it states without answering","the Tirumanthiram image gains a contrast, a teaching in which the omniscient lord is only the one who shows the way and does not burn anything"],"mm":1},{"a":"ya:3","b":"ng:3","type":"same_image","note":"Both poets answer the flaw in their verse with the lamp and its smoke, and ask hearers who grasp the matter to accept the work.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Jain epics share an apology formula in which the poem's worth lies in its meaning, and each poet hangs a different request on it.","mm":1},{"a":"ya:4","b":"k:5","type":"qualifies","note":"The Kural says the two deeds do not cling to those who praise the Lord's true fame; verse 4 promises a poem that shows the fruit of deeds, good and bad, as they actually ripen.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"A story shows karma bearing fruit in merit and fear, which limits the couplet's picture of deeds that simply do not cling to the devout.","gn":["the Yasodhara plan gains a Tamil ethical text which holds that devotion keeps deeds from clinging","the Kural couplet gains a case where deeds do bear fruit in birth after birth for those not freed, which the couplet's statement leaves unshown"],"mm":1},{"a":"ya:9","b":"n:331","type":"same_image","note":"The turtle in the heated pot plays on unaware of its end, as the king and city sit in wealth without seeing release.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain","te":"Both place the danger in comfort that blinds, but the Naladiyar names death as the one waiting, while the epic leaves the danger as the loss of the road to release.","gn":["the king's ease is shown as a dangerous state like the turtle's warm water, not a harmless background","the turtle's image is given a named case, a whole city and king, and a consequence in a scene that follows"],"mm":1},{"a":"ya:16","b":"s:1473","type":"tension","note":"The townspeople tie sickness to the goddess's anger and ask for worship to prevent it; the Siddhar says the offering of goat and cock at the shrine does not stop the sickness and the family deity is not its cause.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The two disagree on whether a deity's anger causes disease, so the epic's chain from the goddess's anger to the king's sacrifice rests on a belief the Siddhar rejects.","gn":["the people's fear is seen as a belief another voice in Tamil rejected, so it is not simply the setting","the Siddhar's mockery of the goat and cock offering gets a case where the same belief ends in a human sacrifice"],"mm":1},{"a":"ya:17","b":"t:238","type":"develops","note":"Tirumular says an unlearned king blind to dharma says 'kill'; the king here, without clarity in dharma, takes the people's advice and goes to the goddess's shrine.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The epic shows how the unlearned king's killing begins: he is led by piety and the people's fear rather than by anger, so the rule applies to a devout king too.","gn":["the king's choice is read as the failure of learning that Tirumular names, not as cruelty","the rule gets a king who kills out of religious duty and not out of tyranny"],"mm":1},{"a":"ya:20","b":"n:16","type":"same_image","note":"The Naladiyar's lamb nibbles the garland in the slaughter ground; here pairs of animals are brought together at the shrine as victims.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The quatrain uses the victim's unawareness as an image for the thoughtless, and the epic shows the gathered victims themselves, so the image can be read back onto the sacrifice.","gn":["the gathered animals are seen as victims who do not know their end, which the verse does not say","the lamb's image is tied to an actual rite and its scale: pairs of five kinds, then humans"],"gr":2,"mm":1},{"a":"ya:21","b":"k:259","type":"tension","note":"The king holds that worship of the goddess is rightly done by killing, beginning with humans; the Kural says refraining from killing and eating one creature is better than a thousand sacrifices.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The two rank sacrifice and non-killing in opposite orders, and if the Kural is read as applying to blood offering as well as to oblations, the king's rite is the very thing the couplet ranks lowest.","gn":["the king's claim is set against a Tamil rule that puts non-killing above every rite","the couplet's comparison of sacrifices with non-killing gets a case where the sacrifice is of humans"],"gr":2,"mm":1},{"a":"ya:22","b":"k:327","type":"qualifies","note":"The king orders a pair of human children brought for sacrifice to avert the goddess's anger; the Kural says not to do a deed that takes another's life even if one's own life be lost.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"The king takes lives to avert harm to his city, whereas the Kural allows no exception even for the life of the one who decides, so the scene tests the rule against the case of a ruler who thinks he acts for his people.","gn":["the order is seen as the act the Kural forbids even to save oneself, and here is done to save a city","the couplet's absolute rule is tested against a ruler's duty to protect, which it does not mention"],"gr":2,"mm":1},{"a":"ya:22","b":"ya:27","type":"develops","note":"The king sends Chandakarma into the city to seek the twin children, and the youth and maiden in ascetic dress enter that city.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The search for victims and the entry of the pair meet in the city, so the dress of renunciation, put on in verse 27, is what brings them into the king's hands.","mm":1},{"a":"ya:30","b":"ya:22","type":"develops","note":"The king's order to Candakarman to bring a pair of humans for the goddess is carried out here as the two are seized.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The poem sets an officer's seeing the pair's gentleness against the order he serves, and the order wins, which shows duty to the king ruling over mercy.","mm":1},{"a":"ya:32","b":"ya:21","type":"tension","note":"The king holds that offering a pair of humans at the sword is a fitting rite; the brother holds that harm to the body is no harm to the self and so no loss.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The same act of killing is a gain to the king's worship and no harm to its victims in their own account, so the doomed two defuse the rite's meaning by their view of the body.","gr":2,"mm":1},{"a":"ya:33","b":"n:33","type":"develops","note":"The epic says fear can neither stop the coming harm nor do anything but add pain and fresh deeds; the quatrain says the fool sighs when the fruit arrives while the wise take it as old desert.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Fear is shown as a new cause of karma before the fruit comes, which extends the quatrain's advice on taking fruit that has arrived.","gn":["the quatrain's picture of fruit taken calmly as old desert, which the brother states only as the uselessness of fear","a case of the advice under the nearest threat, two captives of a sacrifice, where calm is hardest to keep"],"mm":1},{"a":"ya:34","b":"s:130","type":"develops","note":"The brother recalls births among animals driven by old deeds; the Siddhar asks when he will say enough to births as stone, tree, fish, bird and grass.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain","te":"The recalled animal births become the very cry of 'enough' that the pair will act on, so the epic gives the Siddhar's wish a remembered cause.","gn":["the first-person plea to end the lower births, which in the epic is only recounted as past","a stated cause of those lower births, old deeds that drive the soul among beasts, with the siblings' own kin as the killers"],"gr":2,"mm":1},{"a":"ya:35","b":"k:361","type":"qualifies","note":"The epic says deeds whirl the soul like a wheel through the four states; the couplet names craving as the seed of unceasing birth.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The epic gives the wheel's driver as deeds and the Kural gives craving, so the two books hold different roots of the same round of birth.","gn":["a named seed of the wheel in craving, which the verse leaves unsaid","a picture of the wheel itself, four states and births past counting, which the couplet states only as 'birth'"],"mm":1},{"a":"ya:42","b":"n:21","type":"develops","note":"The epic says even Indra-like lords of the gods lost their forms; the quatrain says kings on elephants under moon-like umbrellas have been carried off and no one is left over.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The quatrain's visible proof that kings die is stretched by the epic to the gods, so no rank in any world offers refuge from loss.","gn":["a human instance to rest the claim on, kings borne off whom a reader has seen, where it offers gods only by doctrine","a reach beyond the visible, to ranks above the world of men, so heaven too is no way out"],"gr":2,"mm":1},{"a":"ya:44","b":"k:339","type":"qualifies","note":"The epic likens death and birth to putting off a cloth and leaving a house for another; the couplet likens death to sleep and birth to waking.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain","te":"The epic's cloth and house are things a person does and leaves, while the Kural's sleep is something that happens to one, so the two images differ on whether the change is in the soul's hands.","gn":["a figure of death as a daily act with no break in the one who lives on, to calm a frightened girl","a purpose beyond mere statement, a comfort spoken at the moment of arrest, where the couplet only describes"],"mm":1},{"a":"ya:47","b":"k:54","type":"qualifies","note":"The brother's thought is that women's minds lack firmness; the Kural names firmness, called karpu, as the greatest thing in a woman.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Both books turn on a woman's firmness, but the epic locates it in renunciation and the Kural in chastity, so firmness is not the same virtue in the two.","gn":["a firmness of mind that is not wifely, here set on leaving life, which the Kural's word does not reach","a rival test of the same word, a woman who stands firm in leaving the world and not in keeping her husband"],"mm":1},{"a":"ya:49","b":"k:338","type":"develops","note":"The brother bids his sister think of the soul as other than the body and leave it in the victors' refuge; the couplet likens the tie of body and life to a bird flying off from its nest.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"The couplet calls the tie light and the epic makes it a choice to be carried out, so the same lightness is turned into an act of leaving.","gn":["the idea that the tie to the body is a loose one, which makes its giving up no loss","a pair who act on the image, two who choose the departure and say where they go"],"gr":2,"mm":1},{"a":"ya:50","b":"t:117","type":"tension","note":"The poet says the soul is stainless and other than the body and that its karma is not its own nature; Tirumular says the stains go only when the sun of grace stands over them.","grade":"weak","better":"qualifies","te":"The epic leaves the stain's end to the soul's own knowledge of itself, while Tirumular needs a light from outside, so the two give opposite sources for the freeing.","gn":["a rival account in which the stain leaves only when an outside light appears, which shows how much the epic leaves to the soul's own knowing","a case of a stainless soul known apart from the body with no god's presence in the account"],"mm":1},{"a":"ya:58","b":"k:259","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 259 ranks refusing to take and eat one creature's life above a thousand oblations; verse 58 shows the rite at its far end, a man led to the goddess's place with the king's sword drawn.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain","te":"The couplet weighs a ritual's merit against one life in general; the epic puts the weighing at the altar, where the offering is a man and the weigher is the king holding the sword.","gn":["the sacrifice is seen to be what a Tamil couplet already ranks below a single creature's life","the couplet's thousand sacrifices gain a concrete victim and a king's hand"],"mm":1},{"a":"ya:59","b":"n:16","type":"same_image","note":"Naladiyar 16 sets a kid nibbling the leaf garland before it in the fierce ritual ground; verse 59 sets two youths at the place of killing, smiling.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The kid is glad because it does not know where it stands, while the youths' smile at the same kind of place rests on knowing, so the doomed creature's calm turns from ignorance to composure.","gn":["the youths' smile is read against a stock image of unknowing gladness and shown to be its opposite","the kid's gladness gains a counter-case, one who sees the killing ground and is not shaken"],"mm":1},{"a":"ya:60","b":"s:15","type":"qualifies","note":"Bhadragiriyar longs for the day he will stop killing and eating lives and hold them as his own life; in verse 60 the two at the killing ground wish the king to spread mercy to every life.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"The Siddhar states mercy as a vow about his own food and conduct; the epic has it spoken as a blessing by the victims to the one holding the sword.","gn":["the blessing is shown to carry an ethic a Siddhar names as the aim of his tapas, so mercy is the road and not a courtesy","the Siddhar's wish to see lives as his own gains a scene where the victim does so for the man about to kill him"],"mm":1},{"a":"ya:63","b":"ya:36","type":"develops","note":"Verse 36 grieves over the bodies taken in each birth; verse 63 gives the youth's cause for the suffering, harm done to living bodies.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The earlier verse only laments births; this one names harming living creatures as what the sufferings come from.","mm":1},{"a":"ya:63","b":"n:101","type":"develops","note":"Naladiyar 101 says an old deed seeks out its doer as a calf finds its mother; the youth traces the pair's sufferings over many births to harm done to living bodies.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"The quatrain gives the deed's aim but not its kind; the epic names the kind, injury to living beings, and spreads its return over many births.","gn":["the youth's account is given a Tamil image of how a deed finds its doer","the old deed gains a named kind, harm to living bodies, and a span of many births"],"mm":1},{"a":"ya:63","b":"s:276","type":"tension","note":"Pattinattar confesses to killing and eating many lives and trusts the Lord to bear the fault; the youth holds that harm to living bodies brings suffering through births.","grade":"weak","better":"qualifies","te":"Whether the guilt of killing can be borne by another turns on whether a god stands in the account: the verse sets a chain of sin and suffering with no pardoner in it, while the poet's plea counts on one.","gn":["the youth's law of deeds is set against a pardon a Saiva poet counts on","the poet's trust in the Lord is seen as a step outside a doctrine where the harm simply follows the doer"],"mm":1},{"a":"ya:62","b":"ya:46","type":"develops","note":"Verse 46 asks whether there is fear in leaving this life of fearful births; verse 62 shows the youths without trembling at the killing ground.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The earlier fearlessness was a resolve made in a garden of talk; here it is tested at the place of death.","mm":1},{"a":"ya:67","b":"ya:58","type":"same_image","note":"In verse 58 the king draws his sword at the goddess's place; in verse 67 he puts the sword in his hand aside.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"The same sword marks the king's turn: first the instrument of offering, then what he sets down when the youth's mercy reaches him.","mm":1},{"a":"ya:70","b":"t:1527","type":"tension","note":"Verse 70 says hearing the dharma checks karma's influx and sheds it; Tirumanthiram 1527 says grace comes as the guru and, when one's own doing ends through the knowledge it gives, the three impurities end.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Both make a teacher's word the means of release, but in one the hearer's own checking and shedding does the work, and in the other his own doing must cease and grace does it.","gn":["the Jain account of release by the hearer's own stopping and shedding is set against a rival where grace comes as a guru","Tirumular's 'when one's own doing ends' meets a doctrine where one's own effort is the whole means, sharpening what it denies"],"mm":1},{"a":"ya:71","b":"n:46","type":"qualifies","note":"Naladiyar 46 breaks the woman into entrails, fat, blood and bone to cure desire; verse 71 calls the body a hut of filth and the dharma what ends desire for sense-pleasure and the cruelty of killing.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The quatrain cures desire by looking at the parts; the epic ties the same look to a dharma that also ends killing, so the contemplation of foulness shares a root with the mercy that stays the king's sword.","gn":["the body-as-filth gains a concrete inventory of parts, which the epic names only as filth","the quatrain's disgust is set in a system joining it to non-killing and release, not only restraint of lust"],"mm":1},{"a":"ya:72","b":"nk:859","type":"develops","note":"Neelakesi 859 gives the materialist's own words, that the world is only the elements and belief in a soul only makes fruitless quarrels; verse 72 gives the denial in one summary and calls it ignorance.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"The same opponent appears as a speaker in one poem and as a target in the other, so the epic's verdict can be set beside the debating poem's long hearing.","mm":1},{"a":"ya:72","b":"ya:49","type":"develops","note":"Verse 49 holds the soul to be other than the body that joins it; verse 72 reports those who say the dead are not reborn.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The denial in 72 is the denial of the premise 49 had stated, so the dismissal there rests on a claim made earlier in the book.","mm":1},{"a":"ya:79","b":"ya:83","type":"develops","note":"The white hair seen in the mirror is the cause of the king's crowning of his son and departure.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The poem lets one sign of age carry a whole renunciation, so the choice to leave is the king's own and no teacher's.","mm":1},{"a":"ya:79","b":"si:28:155-170","type":"same_image","note":"In both works a king's own greying body is held up as the plain proof that youth does not last.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain","te":"In the Silappathikaram a sage says the king need not be told since he sees his grey frame, whereas Asokan draws the conclusion himself and acts on it.","mm":1},{"a":"ya:79","b":"n:11","type":"qualifies","note":"Naladiyar praises those who renounce in childhood for foreseeing grey hair; Asokan renounces only after he sees it.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The king meets the Naladiyar's test late but still before the staff, so renunciation after the first white hair counts as wise, not foolish.","gn":["The epic gets a measure for the king's timing: the quatrain says the wise act before grey hair, and he acts only at the first strand.","The quatrain's warning of the staff gets a person who is shown acting at the first strand, which the verse alone never describes."],"mm":1},{"a":"ya:80","b":"s:809","type":"same_image","note":"Both set grey hair and the loss of women's regard against the pleasure of Kama.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Siddhar's youth who spends everything on women ends with a staff in his hand, while Asokan reads his first white hair in time to leave, so the two verses show the same fall averted and unaverted.","gn":["The king's fear that women will turn away gets the full course of a man who ignored it, which the verse only hints at.","The Siddhar's ruined youth gets a counter-case, a man who is warned by the first sign and leaves."],"mm":1},{"a":"ya:81","b":"t:2100","type":"develops","note":"Both argue that the days before age are few and that austerity should be taken up before it arrives.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"The Saiva verse points to the mountain of great austerity as the use of youth, while Asokan turns to the forest mountain and has no god to appeal to, only the fleeting of youth and kin.","gn":["Asokan's leaving gets a Saiva statement of the same urgency, with a named goal of austerity beside the forest mountain.","Tirumular's advice to hurry gets a case of a king who actually hands over the throne and goes."],"mm":1},{"a":"ya:81","b":"ya:86","type":"tension","note":"The father leaves because youth and kin do not last; the son, once king, lives for wealth and pleasure.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"The father's lesson on impermanence does not pass to the son, so the story shows wisdom as personal and not inherited.","mm":1},{"a":"ya:86","b":"k:32","type":"develops","note":"Kural 32 says there is no ruin greater than forgetting virtue; verse 86 says the cause: pride of wealth turns the prince to forget it.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural names forgetting as the worst loss but not its cause, and the epic gives it as the pride of wealth.","gn":["The verse's forgetting of dharma gets named as the ruin greater than any other.","The couplet's forgetting gets a cause, the swelling pride of wealth in a young king."],"mm":1},{"a":"ya:92","b":"n:16","type":"qualifies","note":"The king's plunge into a sea of pleasure is set beside the quatrain's lamb eating the garland before its slaying.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The epic scene only lists pleasure's delights and the quatrain adds the shadow of the slaughter ground over them.","gn":["The scene of delight gets the foreboding the verse itself does not state.","The lamb image gets a concrete king, wealthy and at ease, who is as unaware as the lamb."],"mm":1},{"a":"ya:95","b":"k:57","type":"qualifies","note":"Amritamati in a guarded palace chamber is moved by a song from the stable: the confinement fails and her own steadfastness is the real guard.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural says that only a woman's own steadfastness guards her, and the epic shows the guard fails through the ear, not the door, so steadfastness is a matter of the mind.","gn":["Her wavering gets read against a test the Kural states: guarding is by the self, and her self is the one that gives way.","The couplet's claim gets a case where walls hold and the guard within breaks at a voice."],"mm":1},{"a":"ya:103","b":"ya:111","type":"tension","note":"In 103 the friend is glad that the keeper's form will end the queen's passion; in 111 the same friend carries the queen's desire to that body.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"The sight meant to cure the queen's desire turns into the errand that fulfils it, so loathing proves no barrier once the friend takes the queen's side.","mm":1},{"a":"ya:112","b":"ya:113","type":"develops","note":"The keeper first refuses from fear of the king, and the friend's persuasion turns him to count the queen's love a prize.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"His boast of reward is bought by overcoming the very fear he voiced, which shows how little weighing of cost enters the match.","mm":1},{"a":"ya:97","b":"ya:117","type":"develops","note":"In 97 the queen is alone in thought while the king is by; in 117 she leaves the sleeping king for the keeper.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The inward desire of the first night becomes the act of the night the king watches, so the poem takes her from thought to deed.","mm":1},{"a":"ya:106","b":"ya:110","type":"tension","note":"In 106 the friend urges the queen to shut up her restraint and drop the keeper; in 110 she resolves to go to him.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"The friend's refusal gives way within a few verses, so the poem shows restraint offered by an attendant as only as strong as her pity.","mm":1},{"a":"ya:106","b":"k:1251","type":"qualifies","note":"The friend asks the queen to confine her restraint; the couplet says desire breaks that bolted door.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet's door of restraint is shown here as one a friend urges the queen to keep shut and which the queen has already broken.","gn":["the plea to imprison restraint is shown as too late, since the couplet says desire already breaks the door it names","the couplet, spoken in a woman's voice, gets a case where another woman tells a queen to hold the door and cannot"],"mm":1},{"a":"ya:106","b":"n:42","type":"qualifies","note":"The friend answers desire by loathing the body's foulness, as the quatrain does with every body.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain","te":"The remedy of disgust works in the quatrain as meditation and fails in the story, where one repellent body cannot cool a mind already taken.","gn":["her appeal to the keeper's sores and deformity is shown as a kind of the meditation, but aimed at one man, so it lacks the quatrain's reach","the quatrain's instruction to see the body as a bag turned inside out is tried on a real person and is found weaker than the desire it was meant to quiet"],"mm":1},{"a":"ya:113","b":"n:81","type":"tension","note":"The quatrain weighs great fear against small pleasure and counsels against another's wife; the keeper, who has just said the king will kill him, then calls the queen's love his best reward.","grade":"weak","better":"qualifies","te":"The quatrain's reckoning of fear against pleasure is tested on a man who does the sum and then goes ahead.","gn":["the keeper's boast is set against the king's sword he named in 112, so his reward reads as a risk he has chosen to ignore","the quatrain's prudence gets a speaker who has done the sum and gone past it, which the plain rule does not allow for"],"mm":1},{"a":"ya:116","b":"t:201","type":"same_image","note":"The queen leaves the king for the keeper, as the verse says men leave the ripe jackfruit for the thorny date.","grade":"wrong","better":null,"te":"The poem uses the Tirumanthiram's choice of poor fruit over rich fruit for a woman, and for a far more extreme pair.","gn":["her turning from the king is named as a choice of the worse fruit, which the epic's narration leaves to the reader","the image of the jackfruit and the date, spoken of men, is shown to hold for a woman and for the sharpest contrast of rank and body"],"mm":1},{"a":"ya:96","b":"t:203","type":"qualifies","note":"The poet says a desiring mind outruns counsel; the verse says even learned men and yogis cannot turn a mind once bewildered.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The queen's fall is made one case of a general weakness that knowledge and attainment do not guard against.","gn":["the queen's loss of restraint is set beside the learned and the yogis, which makes her case a human one and not only a woman's","the verse's claim that attainment gives no protection gets a case where even a queen with every comfort is undone by one night's song"],"mm":1},{"a":"ya:124","b":"ya:119","type":"tension","note":"The man who is her chosen lover beats her to the ground; the husband she betrayed holds the sword and refuses to strike.","grade":"weak","better":"qualifies","te":"The two scenes set the lover's blows beside the husband's restraint, so the reader sees whose hand the woman was safer in.","mm":1},{"a":"ya:118","b":"ya:123","type":"develops","note":"The king takes up the sword to follow her and, at the climax of his anger, his hand is stopped.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Carrying the sword in verse 118 makes the check in 123 a choice taken with the means to kill in hand, not a failure of means.","mm":1},{"a":"ya:123","b":"k:301","type":"develops","note":"The king has the power to strike and the cause, and holds his anger.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"The couplet separates guarding anger where it can take effect from guarding it where it cannot; the king's stand is the first kind, with a sword drawn.","gn":["The story shows anger held when the means to act are in hand, which is the couplet's hard case.","The couplet gets a king for whom guarding anger is a stayed sword, not a mood."],"mm":1},{"a":"ya:124","b":"k:327","type":"qualifies","note":"The king spares the woman and the man, but for reasons of sex and rank, not because life is dear.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet bars killing even to save one's own life without exception; the king's reasons here are narrower, and only at 139 does he come to the couplet's rule.","gn":["The story shows how an unprincipled restraint falls short of the rule it is made to meet.","The couplet's absolute rule is tested against a husband's provoked rage, which it does not itself mention."],"mm":1},{"a":"ya:126","b":"k:360","type":"develops","note":"The king lists what desire destroys; the couplet says pain ends when desire, anger and confusion lose even their names.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet states the cure; the king's list shows what it costs when desire is not removed, and he begins the cure by leaving the court.","gn":["The king's list of damage remains a mood until a doctrine says desire can be removed.","The couplet gets a concrete account of what the removed desire costs a man who was ruled by it."],"mm":1},{"a":"ya:126","b":"s:259","type":"qualifies","note":"The Siddhar blames a deceiving demon in woman's form; the king blames desire itself and has just spared the woman.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Siddhar locates the theft of understanding in the woman, while the king locates it in desire, and so he can leave her alive.","gn":["The king lacks the Siddhar's picture of desire as a demon that robs understanding.","The Siddhar's blame of the woman lacks the king's separation of the woman from her desire."],"mm":1},{"a":"ya:128","b":"n:362","type":"qualifies","note":"The king takes the nature of women as a reason to leave; the quatrain says no guard will keep them from fault.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The quatrain says guards cannot hold women's conduct; the king's answer is not a stronger guard but his own departure.","gn":["The king's remark on women's nature is given as a general rule that others also hold.","The quatrain's counsel of guarding gets a different answer from a husband who has seen guarding fail."],"mm":1},{"a":"ya:132","b":"s:249","type":"develops","note":"The king's disenchantment with the court and the queen leads him to leave the throne; the Siddhar lists town, kin, women, glory and wealth as things that do not last.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The Siddhar states what does not last; the king shows the grief and betrayal through which a man comes to accept it.","gn":["The king's resolve gets a doctrine of impermanence that makes it more than hurt pride.","The Siddhar's list gets a worked case in which the loss of a wife and rank is lived, not recited."],"mm":1},{"a":"ya:137","b":"ya:139","type":"tension","note":"The mother tells him to kill a kid to protect himself; his answer is that no life may be taken to guard his own.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The mother and the son give opposite answers to one fear, and the poem lets the son's answer stand.","mm":1},{"a":"ya:137","b":"k:259","type":"qualifies","note":"A mother asks for one killing as an offering to a goddess; the couplet says not killing beats a thousand offerings.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"The couplet compares not killing with great offerings, while the story puts the offering on a mother's lips as a cure for her son's fear.","gn":["The story shows the offering asked at a moment of personal fear, which the couplet's comparison lacks.","The couplet gives the king's refusal a ground in a rule above the king's own reasoning."],"mm":1},{"a":"ya:137","b":"t:1050","type":"qualifies","note":"The mother asks a kid for Chandikai; the Tirumanthiram describes Chandika as holding the four directions, without an offering.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The Tirumanthiram gives Chandika a cosmic form and no offering, while the epic shows how a mother worships her.","gn":["The epic shows the offering practice and not her cosmic form.","The Tirumanthiram's Chandika gets the folk offering that the epic makes the king refuse."],"mm":1},{"a":"ya:139","b":"ya:1","type":"develops","note":"The poet said he would teach release through leaving cruel deeds; the king's refusal is the first such act in this story.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The opening promise of the poem is enacted in the first refusal of killing, long before any release.","mm":1},{"a":"ya:140","b":"k:327","type":"qualifies","note":"The king refuses to kill even to save his own life, and gives the cost in the next world and the shortness of this one.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"The couplet gives the rule alone; the king adds the reason that makes it bear on a man whose own life is threatened.","gn":["The king's refusal is shown to rest on a known maxim, not only on his temper.","The couplet gets a man whose own life is truly threatened and who must pay for obeying it."],"mm":1},{"a":"ya:142","b":"ya:139","type":"tension","note":"After the king says no life may be taken, his mother offers a flour cock in the same breath.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"Placing the loophole next to the refusal shows the poem is testing whether a made likeness of life counts as a life.","mm":1},{"a":"ya:143","b":"ya:144","type":"tension","note":"The king says no one can turn back the ripening of deeds, then agrees to the rite meant to ward off the dream's evil.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"The king's own words deny the rite he accepts, which shows his consent as weakness, not belief.","mm":1},{"a":"ya:143","b":"k:380","type":"qualifies","note":"The king asks who can turn aside the ripening of deeds.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"The couplet says fate outruns any other device; the king acts that out, trying the mother's device and finding the fruit still comes.","gn":["The king's question gets an authority that answers it before the story does.","The couplet gets a scene where a device is tried and fate comes first."],"mm":1},{"a":"ya:144","b":"ya:142","type":"develops","note":"The king consents to the flour-cock offering his mother proposes.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"His doubt in 144 shows the loophole of 142 does not satisfy even the one who takes it, which prepares the cry at 146.","mm":1},{"a":"ya:146","b":"ya:144","type":"develops","note":"The flour cock cries and falls under the sword, answering the king's fear that a likeness is like killing.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The king's disgust at a likeness of life in 144 is borne out by the cry at 146, which shows his instinct was sound against his mother's argument.","mm":1},{"a":"ya:145","b":"ya:58","type":"same_image","note":"The goddess's slaughter-place recurs, once for the two youths and now for a flour cock struck by the king.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The same altar shows the poem's shift: from killing living victims to a substitute that still cries out, so the question is not only the victim but the act.","mm":1},{"a":"ya:147","b":"ya:146","type":"develops","note":"The king asks what the cry of the flour cock means and fears the sin.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"His question turns the marvel at 146 into doctrine: the cry is read as the sign of a sin that binds to births.","mm":1},{"a":"ya:150","b":"ya:132","type":"tension","note":"The king's wish to give up the kingdom was real; the queen offers the same renunciation as bait.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"The poem sets sincere and false renunciation in the same words, so the reader sees renunciation can be counterfeited by one who means murder.","mm":1},{"a":"ya:153","b":"ya:34","type":"develops","note":"The king and his mother enter the animal state at death, and the youth at 34 tells of wandering among animals and being killed by kin.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The animal birth at 153 is the state whose pains are told later in the youth's account, so the ripening is followed across births.","mm":1},{"a":"ya:153","b":"k:319","type":"qualifies","note":"Hurt done in the forenoon returns in the afternoon of itself, as the king's offering returns on him through the poison.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The couplet says the hurt returns of itself; 153 shows it returning through a third person's crime, so the doer suffers without being punished by the doer's own victim.","gn":["The poison is seen as the late fruit of an earlier act of harm.","The couplet gets a case where the returning hurt comes through another's crime, not the victim's hand."],"mm":1},{"a":"ya:153","b":"n:101","type":"develops","note":"A deed that seeks out the one who did it, like a calf finding its mother in a herd.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"The quatrain's image says deeds find their doer; 153 shows how: they come in at the hour of the poison and ripen into animal births.","gn":["The deeds that 'came in' are given an image of seeking the doer out.","The quatrain gets a death in which the deed ripens by an unrelated hand."],"mm":1},{"a":"ya:154","b":"ya:124","type":"qualifies","note":"The king had judged that women are not fit to be killed and spared the queen.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"His mercy at 124 is read here as the cause of his death, so the poem asks whether sparing the guilty was a fault or a virtue.","mm":1},{"a":"ya:155","b":"ya:145","type":"develops","note":"The mourners link the king's death to the flour cock he struck.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The mourners trace the poison back to the offering at 145, a reading the narrator does not himself make.","mm":1},{"a":"ya:156","b":"ya:139","type":"tension","note":"The mourners say he took no part in dharma and has no good death; the king had refused to kill even to save his own life.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"The mourners judge by what they saw, but 139 shows the refusal that belongs to the king's dharma, so their verdict is incomplete.","mm":1},{"a":"ya:158","b":"ya:143","type":"tension","note":"The king says no one can turn the ripening of deeds from him; the queen uses the same words to excuse her own deed.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain","te":"The same doctrine from a victim and from the poisoner shows karma can be spoken as comfort or as an excuse.","mm":1},{"a":"ya:160","b":"ya:4","type":"develops","note":"The poem opens by saying it will show how deeds ripen, and the teller here draws that lesson for Maridattan.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The closing lesson at 160 answers the promise at 4, so the reader is told that the whole tale was an argument about deeds.","mm":1},{"a":"ya:160","b":"s:335","type":"tension","note":"The teller says that understanding that events arise from deeds gives true companions and joy; the Siddhar says the body is the enjoyment of deeds and joins those who think of Siva's feet.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The epic makes understanding the nature of deeds the first step; the Siddhar names grace at the feet as what takes one beyond them.","gn":["The epic's lesson meets the Saiva claim that a god's feet, not understanding, release one from deeds.","The Siddhar's body-as-deeds gets a long case in which deeds ripen through poison and rebirth."],"mm":1},{"a":"ya:161","b":"ya:147","type":"develops","note":"The king's cry at 147 that his deed calls him into a garland of births is answered here: the garland is now told birth by birth.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The lament and the account together show that the foreseen chain was real and also how it runs, which the foreboding alone does not.","mm":1},{"a":"ya:170","b":"t:120","type":"tension","note":"The Jain epic says the fruit of deed cannot be overcome; Tirumular says a teacher's fire makes deed-seeds that do not sprout into births.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Set together they show the epic's karma as a debt that must be lived through, against the Tirumanthiram's karma that grace can scorch before it grows.","gn":["the epic's rule that deed's fruit cannot be beaten meets a rival school that says it can be burned","Tirumular's seeds that will not sprout gain the stubborn picture of a fruit that must be eaten, which his claim opposes"],"mm":1},{"a":"ya:166","b":"n:3","type":"qualifies","note":"The quatrain says kings under the umbrella fall when their deeds turn; the epic shows the fall as the same soul living on as a timid bird in his son's house.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"The couplet's fall of kings is made concrete: the king does not end, he is reborn lower, and at his own son's door.","gn":["the frightened bird gains a general rule that explains why the great fall","the quatrain's fall gains an afterlife it only hints at, the same soul continuing in the palace"],"mm":1},{"a":"ya:172","b":"ya:123","type":"tension","note":"At 123 the king in human form held a sword and let it fall; the bird, with only a beak, strikes the lover's eyes.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The restraint of the man and the blow of the bird together show that the epic does not make the king's patience a trait of his soul but of that one body.","mm":1},{"a":"ya:174","b":"n:101","type":"develops","note":"A calf finds its mother among many cows as an old deed finds its doer; here the mother, now a dog, finds the son in a bird's body and kills him.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The quatrain's mother-finding image is turned over: the deed that finds the doer wears the face of a mother meeting her child.","gn":["the dog's attack is shown as the finding of a doer by an old deed, not a chance of the hunt","the quatrain's calf-and-mother simile gains a case where the finder and the found are in fact mother and son"],"mm":1},{"a":"ya:175","b":"ya:139","type":"tension","note":"The king at 139 refused to end one life to save another; his son, not knowing, beats the dog who is his grandmother.","grade":"weak","better":"qualifies","te":"The father's vow against taking life does not pass to the son, so harm continues through ignorance, not malice.","mm":1},{"a":"ya:180","b":"k:208","type":"develops","note":"The kural's evil that follows the doer like a shadow that never leaves is shown in the snake who becomes a crocodile and follows the fish into the river.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The pursuit shows that the shadow of deed can pass through the animal births, which the couplet's one life does not show.","gn":["the mother's following through snake and crocodile is seen as deed's shadow, not mere hatred","the couplet's shadow gains a path across several births and species"],"mm":1},{"a":"ya:182","b":"ya:137","type":"develops","note":"Chandramathi urged her son to cut down a kid to the goddess; the crocodile that was she is now cut and torn by killers.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The cutting that she asked of the king is returned on her own body, so the offering she urged is the cost she herself pays.","mm":1},{"a":"ya:182","b":"k:320","type":"qualifies","note":"The couplet says pains rest on those who caused pains; here the crocodile's pain comes but at the hands of men who themselves do harm.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The epic shows deed's return working through the very cruelty of new doers, so the rule is true but puts another debt on those who carry it out.","gn":["the crocodile's torn body gains the rule that explains why it suffers","the couplet's rule gains the complication that the one who punishes is himself adding to his own account"],"mm":1},{"a":"ya:181","b":"ya:139","type":"tension","note":"The grandson king orders the crocodile killed, while his father at 139 would not take one life even to save his own.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The son does the very thing the father's last words forbade, which shows how a vow dies when its meaning is not handed down.","mm":1},{"a":"ya:187","b":"ya:183","type":"develops","note":"The goat born to the outcaste hamlet in 183 becomes the womb in which the king is reborn as her son.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Reading 183 and 187 together shows that the doe of the births is the mother and the new kid her own husband's former son, a link neither verse states.","mm":1},{"a":"ya:191","b":"ya:139","type":"tension","note":"The son kills a buffalo as an offering to the goddess; the father refused to take one life to save his own.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Setting the son's offering against the father's refusal shows that the king's act in 191 undoes the very standard his father kept at 139.","mm":1},{"a":"ya:196","b":"ya:139","type":"tension","note":"The kid eaten for the sake of heaven is the father who would not kill a kid even to save himself.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The father who would not purchase his safety with a kid's life is bought into 'heaven' by his son as that kid, so the rite of 196 turns his own refusal into its victim.","mm":1},{"a":"ya:196","b":"ya:197","type":"tension","note":"The brahmins' blessing on the dead king is answered by the eaten animal's recognition of its own son and city.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The pair shows the rite's claim failing in its own scene: the one blessed is the one eaten, and he knows it in 197.","mm":1},{"a":"ya:196","b":"k:259","type":"qualifies","note":"The couplet ranks not killing and eating above a thousand sacrifices; the epic's rite of the dead is a sacrifice whose flesh is the dead man.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"The couplet states the rule; the epic's story shows its cost to the rite's own beneficiaries, so that the rule is tested by a case where the dead eat the dead.","gn":["The rite of 196 gets a stated standard against which a brahmin's flesh offering is shown to be worse than none.","The couplet's contrast of ritual and abstention gets a story in which the sacrifice's own beneficiary is the victim."],"gr":2,"mm":1},{"a":"ya:192","b":"t:199","type":"tension","note":"The brahmins here claim a fresh-killed buffalo for the rite and eat it as holy; Tirumular names flesh-eaters as vile ones seized by Yama's messengers.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"The two texts judge the same act in opposite voices, one by merit and the other by hell, so the reader sees that the epic leaves the verdict to the story and not to a threat.","gn":["The brahmins' claim of merit meets a Saiva text that calls the same eating base and punishable.","The Tirumanthiram's threat of hell meets a case where the eaters think they earn heaven for the dead."],"mm":1},{"a":"ya:197","b":"s:15","type":"develops","note":"The Siddhar wishes to see the lives he might kill as his own life; the kid in 197 is, literally, the king's own father.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Siddhar's stance of 'thinking of them as my own life' is a wish; the epic gives it a literal case, where the creature eaten is the eater's kin.","gn":["The recognition in 197 gets a Siddhar's name for the habit of mind that would have stopped the rite.","The Siddhar's 'as my own life' gets a story where it is literally true of the victim."],"mm":1},{"a":"ya:197","b":"n:16","type":"qualifies","note":"The lamb in the slaughter ground nibbles the garland unaware; the kid in 197 knows its fate and its son.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The Naladiyar's lamb stands for the heedless; the epic sets beside it a lamb that knows, so the reader sees what the heedless have lost.","gn":["The kid's awareness of 197 gets an older picture of the slaughter-ground lamb that is unaware.","The lamb of the quatrain gets a counter-case in which the victim recollects who it was."],"mm":1},{"a":"ya:199","b":"n:101","type":"develops","note":"The quatrain says an old deed seeks out its doer as a calf seeks its mother; the kid in 199 is a king found by his own deeds.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"The quatrain gives the mechanism and the epic gives the case: the doer is found even across births and in a lower body, and still hears the deed named as its own.","gn":["The kid's line 'the fruit of the deed I produced' gets an image for how the fruit finds the doer.","The calf-and-mother image gets a doer who is a king reborn as a kid under his own son."],"mm":1},{"a":"ya:199","b":"ya:35","type":"develops","note":"The youth at 35 describes beings spun by their deeds through births without number; the kid in 199 speaks from one of those births.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"The doctrine told by the youths in 35 is borne out in the king's own case, so that the reader sees it as lived and not as preached.","gr":2,"mm":1},{"a":"ya:217","b":"k:319","type":"develops","note":"The onlookers name the fruit of a woman's cruelty as leprosy, coming to her body; the couplet says only that harm returns of itself.","grade":"wrong","better":null,"te":"The epic shows the return of harm as a long delay in which the doer is first shown eating and then rotting, which the couplet's afternoon-for-forenoon image cannot show.","gn":["the unit gains a plain rule that harm to others comes back to the doer, without needing a past birth","the couplet gains a case in which the return takes years, passes through a body and is read by onlookers as karma"],"mm":1},{"a":"ya:217","b":"n:101","type":"develops","note":"Both say a deed finds its doer; the epic adds that it outlasts her death.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Putting 217 beside the calf-and-mother image shows that the Jain epic makes the deed's claim reach past the doer's body, a step the quatrain does not take.","gn":["the unit gains an image of how the old deed finds her, as a calf picks out its mother","the quatrain gains a limit case, where the deed still stands after the body has failed"],"mm":1},{"a":"ya:217","b":"ya:107","type":"develops","note":"Amirthamathi once defended her fortune as the fruit of an earlier deed; the same law now explains her disease.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The queen's own argument for her desire turns on her: she cited former deeds as the source of what one has, and her sickness is that source's other side.","mm":1},{"a":"ya:216","b":"ya:126","type":"develops","note":"The king reasoned that desire wastes the body and fame; the queen's body is wasted.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"His reasoning from the night of the tryst is borne out on her body, which turns a private resolve into a visible fact.","mm":1},{"a":"ya:216","b":"s:259","type":"qualifies","note":"The Siddhar's woman is a pit of sores by nature; here the sores come on one woman as the result of what she did.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The epic places the body's foulness in a deed and its time, where the Siddhar places it in woman's nature, so the two verses disagree on its cause.","gn":["the unit gains a rival account of the foul body in which the foulness is a lure, not a punishment","the song gains a case that says the sores follow a deed, which unsettles blaming woman as such"],"mm":1},{"a":"ya:214","b":"k:146","type":"qualifies","note":"The couplet says sin, fear and blame never leave one who crosses into another's home; here a wife crosses, and the fruit falls on her.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet is spoken to a man; the epic carries the same four ills onto a wife and adds the one the couplet lacks, the disease of her own body.","gn":["the unit gains a stated rule that the crossing carries its own ruin","the couplet gains a woman as the one who crosses, and a bodily fruit beyond blame"],"mm":1},{"a":"ya:212","b":"k:251","type":"qualifies","note":"The couplet asks how one who eats another's flesh to fatten his own can hold to compassion; here the flesh is possibly her victim's.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The epic turns the couplet's general 'another creature' into a creature that may be her own victim's next body, which sharpens its question.","gn":["the unit gains a rule that eating flesh is a failure of compassion in itself","the couplet gains a case where the flesh may belong to someone she wronged"],"mm":1},{"a":"ya:211","b":"t:199","type":"qualifies","note":"Tirumular puts the flesh-eaters' pain in hell, after death; the epic spends its verses on the animal's pain first.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The epic puts the pain first on the animal and sets the eater's suffering later in this same life, where Tirumular's punishment lies only after death.","gn":["the unit gains a Saiva voice that also holds flesh-eating to be punished","the verse gains the animal's side of the pain, which it does not describe"],"mm":1},{"a":"ya:209","b":"k:551","type":"qualifies","note":"The couplet calls a king who torments his people crueller than a killer; here the king seizes merchants' goods over a horse.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The epic's king acts from a sudden anger, not from habit, so it shows how the couplet's cruelty can come from one incident and an unexamined report.","gn":["the unit gains a rule by which the seizure can be called cruelty","the couplet gains a case of tyranny arising from one angry order, not a settled vice"],"mm":1},{"a":"ya:209","b":"ya:139","type":"tension","note":"The earlier king calls a king the shield of all living beings; this king's order falls on merchants and an animal.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain","te":"One king's rule that a ruler guards every life is set against his successor's order that brings pain on innocent persons and a beast.","mm":1},{"a":"ya:236","b":"ya:234","type":"tension","note":"The sage's rubbed-wood fire answers the executioner's claim that a cut body shows no life.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The story gives the materialist test and the Jain reply side by side, so the reader sees the argument as one of method: whether failing to see a thing on cutting shows that it is absent.","mm":1},{"a":"ya:237","b":"ya:235","type":"qualifies","note":"The conch and its sound answer the weighing and the sealed pit that Chandakarman offered as proof.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"The reply meets each of his tests by name, so the reader sees that his proof depends on the soul being a thing that could be weighed or seen leaving.","mm":1},{"a":"ya:238","b":"ya:50","type":"develops","note":"Verse 50 gave the youths' knowledge of the soul as other than the body; verse 238 gives it to the man who denied it.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"The same doctrine, first held by ascetics going to their death, is now put as the answer to a man's disproof, so the reader learns it was framed to meet doubt as well as to console.","mm":1},{"a":"ya:242","b":"ya:54","type":"develops","note":"The fivefold conduct the first teachers spread in verse 54 is here set out as the five vows and three abstentions.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The earlier verse only names the fivefold conduct, and this one says what the five are.","mm":1},{"a":"ya:240","b":"nk:116","type":"qualifies","note":"Both give knowledge, faith and conduct as the triple discipline; Neelakesi makes it a medicine against rebirth.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"Neelakesi's frame of birth as a disease for which the three are the cure is not in the Yasodhara, which rather lists their by-fruits of heaven and rule beside release.","gr":2,"mm":1},{"a":"ya:241","b":"nk:121","type":"qualifies","note":"Right faith as clarity and joy in the Jina's dharma here, against right faith as eight limbs and longing for the one with eight excellent things in Neelakesi.","grade":"wrong","better":"agrees","te":"The two Jain epics define the same faith differently, one by an inward state and one by a list of acts and freedoms, so the reader sees how a school could teach one term more than one way.","gr":2,"mm":1},{"a":"ya:242","b":"mm:24:123-140","type":"qualifies","note":"The Jain five vows and flesh, honey and toddy against the Buddhist ten evil deeds of body, tongue and mind.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain","te":"The two lists overlap on killing, theft and lust and differ on the rest, so the reader sees which wrongs the two faiths treat as common ground and which they do not.","mm":1},{"a":"ya:243","b":"k:323","type":"develops","note":"Verse 243 puts truth after not killing and gives each its fruit, as the couplet puts not lying second to not killing.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet only names the order of the two virtues, and the verse adds what each is said to yield.","gn":["A ranking that puts not killing before truth, which the verse's list of five leaves unspoken.","A fruit in this life for each of the two virtues, which the couplet leaves out."],"mm":1},{"a":"ya:244","b":"k:255","type":"qualifies","note":"The couplet says a life lives on not eating flesh and keeps hell closed; the verse adds worldly beauty, victory and birth.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"The couplet gives the reason for not eating flesh as fear of hell, and the verse gives a gain in this life, so the reader sees two arguments for one abstention.","gn":["A reason for the flesh ban drawn from the Kural's own side, the closing of hell, which the verse lacks as it offers only rewards.","A reward in this life, of radiance, beauty and victory, which the couplet lacks as it argues from fear of hell."],"mm":1},{"a":"ya:233","b":"t:444","type":"tension","note":"Both speak of eight qualities, here a freed soul's own, there the Lord's.","grade":"forced","better":"parallel_domain","te":"The Jain verse makes the eight the property of any soul that is freed, and the Saiva verse makes them the Lord's, so the reader sees where the two schools put the one list.","gn":["A rival owner of the eight qualities, a god, against which its claim that the freed soul has them unaided can be seen.","A school that gives the eight to a freed soul with no god, which tests the Saiva claim that they are the Lord's."],"gr":2,"mm":1},{"a":"ya:237","b":"k:340","type":"qualifies","note":"The couplet pictures the life as a lodger in the body; the verse argues it by the conch's sound.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet takes the soul's separateness for granted and the verse offers a reason for it, so the reader sees what the couplet's picture assumed.","gn":["A figure, the lodger in a body, for the thing that the cut and weighed body fails to show.","A reason for the lodger's separateness, which the couplet only assumes."],"mm":1},{"a":"ya:245","b":"ya:252","type":"tension","note":"The king says life would fall apart if harming stopped; the sage answers that even a killing in intent alone sends the killers to hell.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"The pair puts the king's claim that rule needs killing beside the sage's reply that killing costs the killer more than it gives, which neither verse shows alone.","mm":1},{"a":"ya:250","b":"ya:139","type":"tension","note":"In 139 Yasodharan refuses to kill a living thing even to save his own life; here he and his mother are said to have killed a cock of flour with a sword.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"Set side by side, they show a stated rule and the later act that breaks it, with 250 giving the cost that carries through every birth.","mm":1},{"a":"ya:257","b":"ya:255","type":"develops","note":"The cocks take the vow and the nectar of dharma, and in 257 the arrow that kills them still sends them into human birth.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The vow in 255 is what makes 257 a rebirth and not a fall, and the arrow shows how far the vow's protection reaches.","mm":1},{"a":"ya:260","b":"ya:73","type":"develops","note":"The twins are born to Yasomathi after the cocks' death; the youth in chapter 4 says his sight of dharma came from knowing his own births.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Read together, they let the reader see that the cocks of this chapter are the youth who is telling the story, an inference the poem does not state outright.","mm":1},{"a":"ya:249","b":"k:327","type":"develops","note":"The sage says kings who kill to keep the body last only a few days and then sink in rebirth; the couplet forbids taking a life even to save one's own.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet gives a bare ban; the sage supplies the reason that the life one kills to save is brief and the killing makes the next birth worse.","gn":["The verse finds in the couplet the absolute rule its argument from the body's brevity is trying to reach.","The couplet gets a reason against killing to save oneself that it does not itself give."],"mm":1},{"a":"ya:250","b":"k:320","type":"develops","note":"The couplet says all pains rest on those who caused pain; the sage says the pain of killing returns to the killers birth after birth.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"The couplet states the return of pain as a rule; the verse shows it falling across births on two named kin who thought their killing was only a rite.","gn":["The verse gets a general rule that makes the long suffering of the king and his mother a law and not a freak.","The couplet gets the cases of kin reborn as birds, which show how far its rule is meant to reach."],"mm":1},{"a":"ya:249","b":"n:21","type":"develops","note":"Naladiyar says even kings who rode elephants under the white umbrella have been carried off and no one is left over; the verse says kings who kill to last also die and then sink in rebirth.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The quatrain ends at death; the verse adds that for a king who killed to last, the end is only a sinking into more births.","gn":["The verse gets the common picture of kings carried off as a witness that the body's span cannot be bought by killing.","The quatrain gets a destination after death and the cause that sends a king there."],"mm":1},{"a":"ya:247","b":"s:15","type":"develops","note":"The sage makes feeling another's pain as one's own the base of the chief vow; the Siddhar wishes to think of the lives he would kill and eat as his own life.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"The Siddhar offers this as a wish on the way to completing his tapas, while the verse makes it the support without which the vow of not killing cannot stand.","gn":["The verse gets a voice from a different tradition that reaches the same rule and ties it to a seeker's tapas.","The Siddhar's wish gets a Jain statement that names the pity that must come first and the restraint beside it."],"mm":1},{"a":"ya:251","b":"s:130","type":"qualifies","note":"The sage says the king's father and mother went into animal births because of their deeds; the Siddhar asks when he will say enough to the births he has had as stone, tree, fish, bird and grass.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Siddhar mourns the animal births without naming a cause; the epic traces the animal birth to a particular killing and makes it the fruit of a deed.","gn":["The verse gets from the Siddhar the weariness of the rebirth it only reports as a fact.","The Siddhar's lament gets a case in which an animal birth is the named fruit of a killing and not simply a stage to be escaped."],"mm":1},{"a":"ya:255","b":"t:120","type":"qualifies","note":"The cocks say that the teacher's dharma has freed them from the fever of rebirth; Tirumular's guru makes the deeds into burnt seeds that will not sprout into seven births.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"Both free through a teacher, but in the epic the cocks' own vow and memory do the work while in Tirumular it is the guru's act that burns the seed, and no god is named in the epic's verse.","gn":["The verse gets a Saiva picture of how a teacher's act ends rebirth, which it only reports as a result.","Tirumular gets a case of a being freed by teaching and its own vow, without a god's act."],"mm":1},{"a":"ya:248","b":"t:258","type":"qualifies","note":"The sage says the suffering of births ends only by good dharma; Tirumular names austerity and virtue the boat across the sea of deeds, which the Lord makes fruitful.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"The Jain verse makes dharma sufficient to end the fruit of deeds; Tirumular makes the Lord's act the thing that gives austerity and virtue their effect.","gn":["The verse gets a rival account in which dharma needs a god's act to bear fruit.","Tirumular gets a Jain case where dharma alone is said to end a deed's fruit, which tests his added condition."],"mm":1},{"a":"ya:279","b":"ya:265","type":"tension","note":"The hounds loosed in 265 to seize flesh are shown in 279 not coming near the ascetic.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"The weapon that the king relied on is turned back by the very one it was loosed on, so the story shows his force failing before his remorse begins.","gr":2,"mm":1},{"a":"ya:273","b":"ya:67","type":"same_image","note":"In 67 and here a king puts down the weapon in his hand under pressure of a speech; in 67 it is the victim's own sermon, here a third party's plea.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The two scenes show that the sword is laid down for different reasons: there the king is moved by what the victim teaches, here by what a friend says of the victim's greatness.","mm":1},{"a":"ya:283","b":"ya:273","type":"develops","note":"In 273 the king puts down the weapon but scorns the dust-grimed man; in 283 he bows so that his crown-flowers touch the ascetic's feet.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The king's change is made in two steps, so the first (weapon) does not yet carry the second (reverence).","mm":1},{"a":"ya:276","b":"ya:274","type":"develops","note":"274 says he ruled the whole land alone like a lion; 276 has him cast off that land, women and army.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"What the king of Kalinga gives up in 276 is given its size by the merchant's earlier account of how entirely he held it.","mm":1},{"a":"ya:284","b":"ya:82","type":"develops","note":"In 82 Asokan reasons that greater merit now may win Indra's world; in 284 the ascetic says Asokan reached a divine world by earlier penance.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"Asokan's reasoning about merit is shown to bring about the heaven he had thought of, only now told by another.","mm":1},{"a":"ya:275","b":"ya:81","type":"qualifies","note":"81 says youth and kinship are fleeting and the mind should leave them; 275 adds wealth and pleasure and ends with the pleasure-rich heaven as the aim.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"The same impermanence argument that leads one king to give up the kingdom is shown aiming at heaven rather than release, in both.","mm":1},{"a":"ya:281","b":"ya:280","type":"tension","note":"280 has the king resolve to offer his own head; 281 says that to destroy one's own life is not grace.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The rule against killing is used here to forbid an act of reverence by the doer on himself, and so it does not give way to atonement.","mm":1},{"a":"ya:270","b":"t:36","type":"tension","note":"In 270 the fruit follows honour or scorn though the ascetics hold neither favour nor anger; in Tirumanthiram 36 grace comes in the measure of the praise.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"One text lets the result come from the one who honours without any granting, the other makes the Lord answer; the two differ over whether anyone grants the fruit.","gn":["The epic verse lacks any figure who gives the fruit, and the Saiva verse shows what the ascetic's indifference excludes: a giver.","The Tirumanthiram verse lacks the Jain case in which a reverence has result with no one pleased by it."],"mm":1},{"a":"ya:271","b":"n:58","type":"develops","note":"271 says the ascetic thinks of pain inflicted as his own deed; Naladiyar 58 says the worthy grieve for those who scorn them, fearing the hell that will follow.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Together they show that holding hurt as one's own deed does not leave the ascetic cold: Naladiyar adds the pity for the offender that 271 omits.","gn":["271 gains a feeling for the offender that its karma explanation leaves out.","Naladiyar 58 gains a king with dogs as its case: the scorner is shown at the moment before the fall it fears for him."],"mm":1},{"a":"ya:272","b":"k:315","type":"develops","note":"272 grounds 'all lives are like my own' in an ascetic's grace and a result beyond karma; Kural 315 tests understanding by guarding another's pain as one's own.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The epic puts the rule into a speech to a hunting king, so that guarding other lives has a named cost to him: his hounds and spear.","gn":["272 gains the Kural's plain test: understanding that does not guard another's pain is empty.","Kural 315 gains a person who does the opposite, a king hunting with a pack."],"mm":1},{"a":"ya:281","b":"k:327","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 327 forbids taking another's life even to save one's own; 281 forbids the king to take his own life even to atone.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet's rule against killing is shown to cover the doer's own life, which the couplet does not say.","gn":["281 gains a forceful general rule against taking life, with no exception for need.","Kural 327 gains the opposite case, where the life endangered is one's own, to be spared in atonement."],"mm":1},{"a":"ya:278","b":"k:346","type":"develops","note":"278 has the ascetic hold neither 'I' nor 'mine' nor even benefit; Kural 346 says cutting off 'I' and 'mine' reaches a world above the gods.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The epic adds a third term, benefit, to the Kural's pair, so letting go includes even what one might claim as one's own good.","gn":["278 gains the Kural's statement that this giving up has a result beyond the gods' world.","Kural 346 gains a figure who has done it, a king turned ascetic."],"mm":1},{"a":"ya:275","b":"n:53","type":"qualifies","note":"Naladiyar 53 has the best renounce home, youth and wealth at once to save themselves; 275 renounces for the pleasure-rich heaven.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The epic shows that the same reflection on impermanence may end in heaven rather than in release.","gn":["275 gains Naladiyar's view that the point of renunciation is saving oneself.","Naladiyar 53 gains an instance in which the reflection leads to a heaven of pleasure, not release."],"mm":1},{"a":"ya:269","b":"k:28","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 28 says the greatness of those whose word is full shows in their hidden words; 269 says what such ascetics will comes about, even the cloud, and puts this under fate.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"The epic places the ascetic's power in his will and under fate, where the couplet puts it in the word.","gn":["269 gains the Kural's claim that greatness is known through the word.","Kural 28 gains a kind of proof, rain and destruction at the ascetic's will."],"mm":1},{"a":"ya:295","b":"ya:285","type":"tension","note":"The wife's hell is reckoned at ten and seven oceans, the father's heaven at ten.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain","te":"Counting both fates in oceans of years shows one standard measuring the reward and the punishment, with the wife's term the longer.","mm":1},{"a":"ya:286","b":"ya:122","type":"develops","note":"The queen who offered the low man her whole love is the woman who plots with the old mother to poison the king.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The poisoning and hell here are the fruit of the passion the earlier verse shows only beginning.","mm":1},{"a":"ya:297","b":"ya:63","type":"develops","note":"The youth said that harm to living bodies brought them birth after birth of pain; here the muni names the first harm and the beasts.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The muni's account gives the cause the youth's earlier remark leaves unnamed: a single killing carried through many beast births.","mm":1},{"a":"ya:297","b":"ya:139","type":"tension","note":"The king who refuses to take a kid's life to save his own is the same Yasodharan that the muni names as caught in beast births for killing.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"The refusal in verse 139 does not hold in the story's end; the reader sees that a good stand can still be overturned and then paid for.","mm":1},{"a":"ya:292","b":"ya:286","type":"develops","note":"The poison she meant for the king is poured back into her mouth in hell.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The punishment copies the crime, so the reader sees the hell as the working out of her own deed.","mm":1},{"a":"ya:293","b":"n:123","type":"develops","note":"In both, the flesh the eater took from another is answered in the eater's own body: the crab-eater's fingers rot, the queen's limbs are cut off for her to eat.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain","te":"Together they show the deed answering in kind, once in a diseased human body and once in a hell where the body will not die.","gn":["The epic shows the same retribution in a hell with a tormentor's speech, where the quatrain gives only a result on earth.","The quatrain gains a longer, harsher case in which the eater is made to eat herself, not merely lose a limb."],"mm":1},{"a":"ya:290","b":"k:255","type":"develops","note":"The Kural says that for the flesh-eater hell does not open its mouth; here the flesh-eater is told 'you ate' and hot copper is forced into her mouth.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"The couplet's closed mouth of hell becomes a scene in which hell feeds the eater, and the penalty is shown to match the eating.","gn":["The epic gives the couplet's warning a named sufferer and a tormentor's reproach.","The couplet gets a mechanism: the penalty copies the meal rather than being a general threat."],"mm":1},{"a":"ya:289","b":"t:199","type":"qualifies","note":"Tirumanthiram has Yama's messengers seize the flesh-eaters and break them in fire-mouthed hell; here the dwellers of hell burn and split her with no god's order named.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"Both fill hell with fire and breaking, but only the epic leaves the tormentors without a lord, which shows how little each needs a judge.","gn":["The epic's hell has no named judge or messenger, only its own dwellers.","The Saiva verse's Yama's servants stand out as one way of staffing hell, not a necessity."],"mm":1},{"a":"ya:296","b":"n:101","type":"develops","note":"The quatrain says an old deed seeks out its doer as a calf finds its mother; here the old deed 'stands and burns' the queen in hell.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The calf's search takes the deed to its doer, and the epic shows what the deed then does once it has arrived.","gn":["The epic shows the deed after it has found the doer, still burning her.","The quatrain gains a case where the deed, once arrived, does not leave until spent."],"mm":1},{"a":"ya:298","b":"t:2133","type":"qualifies","note":"Tirumanthiram has old deeds following the subtle body into a new body; the muni gives the bodies one by one for the king's father, with a vow taken in the cock's body turning the chain upward.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The epic shows that the following deed can be altered by a vow made in a beast's body, which the Saiva line says nothing of.","gn":["The epic names each body and the vow that changed the course.","The Saiva verse's general passing of the old deed gains a concrete life, a beast birth by beast birth."],"mm":1},{"a":"ya:304","b":"n:15","type":"develops","note":"The quatrain has mother seeking mother through the world; here father, mother, son and daughter change places among themselves across births.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"One shows kin passing the world on, the other shows kin turning into each other, so grief for kin has no fixed object.","gn":["The epic's twins and king have actually been one another's father, mother and children.","The quatrain's general chain of mothers gets a family that names its members."],"mm":1},{"a":"ya:305","b":"k:320","type":"develops","note":"The Kural says all pain falls on those who caused pain; the king reads his own life as a ripening of the pain he gave his father and mother in their births.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain","te":"The couplet's rule becomes a confession in which the doer sees the pain return across several births.","gn":["The king applies the rule to himself and to kin in earlier births.","The couplet gets a speaker who feels the rule falling on him."],"mm":1},{"a":"ya:306","b":"k:321","type":"qualifies","note":"The Kural names not killing as the chief virtue; the king names the lack of mercy joined to seeking wealth and pleasure as his fault.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"The king adds the motive of wealth and pleasure to the couplet's plain count of killing, so greed becomes part of the fault.","gn":["The king's lament names the greed that drives the killing.","The couplet gains a cause for the killing it forbids."],"mm":1},{"a":"ya:311","b":"ya:67","type":"develops","note":"The king who put aside his sword in verse 67 now asks the twins to forgive his earlier wrongs.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The softening at the altar is only a first step; the king completes it by asking pardon and taking his own refuge.","mm":1},{"a":"ya:313","b":"ya:84","type":"develops","note":"Asokan takes the vow with fifty-two kings; Maridattan renounces with kings and a merchant.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain","te":"The tale that began with a king's renunciation among kings ends with a former sacrificer doing the same, so the vow is shown to reach even the cruel.","mm":1},{"a":"ya:316","b":"ya:33","type":"qualifies","note":"The youth said fear of what is due gains nothing; here the disciples fear the ripening of deeds and that fear drives them to austerity.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Fear of karma is useless as dread but useful as a reason to act, so the earlier advice is limited by what the fear is turned toward.","mm":1},{"a":"ya:317","b":"n:4","type":"qualifies","note":"Naladiyar 4 tells the hearer to do good at once because Death comes in anger; verse 317 grades austerity to each one's strength.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Urgency in the quatrain is made practicable by the epic's graded vows, which say how those not yet able to renounce fully are to begin.","gn":["the verse lacks the sense that time is short and delay is costly","the quatrain lacks any ladder of vows for those who cannot yet give up everything"],"mm":1},{"a":"ya:321","b":"ya:63","type":"develops","note":"The youth said harm once done to bodies brought sufferings in birth after birth; here the flour cock killing is the instance.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The general claim of verse 63 is given its measured example, in which even an effigy's killing is followed for lives.","mm":1},{"a":"ya:321","b":"k:321","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 321 calls not killing the first of dharma and says killing brings every other kind of deed; verse 321 shows one killing without a real life still following across births.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"The couplet's rule is tested at its smallest case, the effigy, which shows how far the epic extends the rule beyond the couplet.","gn":["the verse lacks a general statement that killing leads on to all other wrongs","the couplet lacks the story of a symbolic killing whose pain still lasted through births"],"mm":1},{"a":"ya:321","b":"t:200","type":"tension","note":"Tirumanthiram 200 counts killing as a mountain-sized sin that is put away by reaching Siva's feet; verse 321 says the killing's result cannot be undone by anything given, and only the dharma's austerity loosens it.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The Saiva text frees one from the sin by reaching the Lord, while the Jain verse leaves the deed to be worn away by conduct alone.","gn":["the verse lacks any Lord whose feet remove the sin","the quatrain lacks an account of how long one killing travels through births"],"mm":1},{"a":"ya:325","b":"ya:49","type":"develops","note":"In verse 49 the brother bids his sister give up the body in the refuge of the victors; verse 325 shows them doing it.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The plan made under threat of the sword is kept after the threat ends, which shows it was a choice and not only a reaction to danger.","mm":1},{"a":"ya:329","b":"t:499","type":"tension","note":"Tirumanthiram 499 has karma bring the soul to wombs and then knowledge reach Siva; verse 329 says the dharma holds karma off and leads on to release.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"Both give heaven or birth as a stage, but one reaches release through the Lord and the other through the dharma's own power.","gn":["the verse lacks a Lord who brings the soul to release","the quatrain lacks a way of holding back karma by conduct"],"mm":1},{"a":"ya:330","b":"k:360","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 360 says pain perishes when even the names of desire, anger and confusion perish; verse 330 names dharma, giving up anger, knowledge and the vow as what to do.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet names what must perish and the epic's last verse names the practices that make it perish.","gn":["the verse lacks the three faults of desire, anger and confusion that the couplet names","the couplet lacks the practices of dharma, knowledge and keeping a vow"],"gr":2,"mm":1}]});
